ach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Disposal Instructions</strong></p><ul><li>Dispose of dirty Bashundhara Baby Diaper Mini XL 4s (11-25kg) in a sealed bag or container.</li><li>Place the sealed bag or container in the trash.</li></ul>', 'active', '1401_1734331637.webp', 48, 9, NULL, '2024-12-16 11:47:17', '2024-12-16 11:47:17'),
(254, 'Bashundhara Baby Diaper Belt ST Series M (4-9 kg)', 'Diaper', 110.00, 1, 'Packet', '5\'s in a packet', 'Diaper', '5 pcs', 'no', 'yes', '<ul><li>Origin: Bangladesh</li><li>Brand: Bashundhara.</li><li>Product Type: Belt System.</li><li>Size:&nbsp;<a href=\"http://www.bashundharapapermills.com/product/bashundhara-baby-diaper-standard-series-(m)\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent;\">M (4-9 kg).</a></li><li>Packet Size:&nbsp;<a href=\"http://www.bashundharapapermills.com/product/bashundhara-baby-diaper-standard-series-(m)\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"background-color: transparent;\">5 Pcs.</a></li><li>Made in Bangladesh.</li></ul>', 'active', '1926_1734331898.jpg', 48, 9, NULL, '2024-12-16 11:51:38', '2024-12-16 11:51:38'),
(255, 'Avonee Pants System Baby Daiper XL Size (12-17kg)', 'Daiper', 140.00, 1, 'Packet', '4 pcs in a packet', 'Diaper', '4 Pcs', 'no', 'yes', '<p><strong>Avonee Pant Style Diaper XL 4s (12-17kg)</strong></p><p>Avonee Pant Style Diaper XL 4s (12-17kg) prioritizes the comfort and well-being of your baby. It is made of soft and breathable materials to ensure optimal comfort and keep your baby\'s skin dry and healthy. The Avonee Pant Style Diaper XL 4s (12-17kg) also features a secure fit to prevent leaks and ensures your baby stays clean and fresh all day long. It has easy-to-use fasteners and a wetness indicator that lets you know when it\'s time for a change.</p><p><strong>Usage Instructions of Avonee Pant Style Diaper XL 4s (12-17kg)</strong></p><ul><li>Open the Avonee Pant Style Diaper XL 4s (12-17kg) and place it under the baby.</li><li>Make sure the Avonee Pant Style Diaper XL 4s (12-17kg) is fitted snugly but not too tight.</li><li>If the baby has pooped, wipe the area clean with wipes and apply Avonee Pant Style Diaper XL 4s (12-17kg) cream if necessary.</li><li>Close the Avonee Pant Style Diaper XL 4s (12-17kg) and fasten the tabs.</li><li>Dispose of the dirty Avonee Pant Style Diaper XL 4s (12-17kg) properly.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Avonee Pant Style Diaper XL 4s (12-17kg) can be stored at room temperature (between 20 and 25 degrees Celsius or 68 and 77 degrees Fahrenheit). They should not be stored in direct sunlight or heat, as this can cause them to dry out or become brittle.</p><p><strong>Safety Information</strong></p><ul><li>Change Avonee Pant Style Diaper XL 4s (12-17kg) often, especially after the baby has wet or pooped.</li><li>Do not leave a wet or soiled Avonee Pant Style Diaper XL 4s (12-17kg) on the baby for too long.</li><li>Do not flush dirty Avonee Pant Style Diaper XL 4s (12-17kg) down the toilet.</li><li>Keep Avonee Pant Style Diaper XL 4s (12-17kg) out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Disposal Instructions</strong></p><ul><li>Dispose of dirty Avonee Pant Style Diaper XL 4s (12-17kg) in a sealed bag or container.</li><li>Place the sealed bag or container in the trash.</li></ul>', 'active', '277_1734332211.png', 47, 9, NULL, '2024-12-16 11:56:51', '2024-12-16 11:56:51'),
(256, 'Select Plus', 'Ketoconazole 2%', 200.00, 1, 'x 75 ml bottle', '75 ml bottle', 'Shampoo', '1.9%', 'no', 'yes', '<p><strong>Indications of Select-Plus 2%</strong></p><p>Select-Plus 2% therapeutic shampoo is used for the prevention and treatment of infections in which the yeast <em>Malassezia furfur</em> (formerly <em>Pityrosporum orbiculare</em>) is likely to be involved, such as dandruff, seborrhoeic dermatitis, and tinea (pityriasis) versicolor.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Select-Plus 2% shampoo is a broad-spectrum imidazole-dioxolane, synthetic antifungal agent that inhibits the in vitro growth of the following common dermatophytes and yeasts by altering the permeability of the cell membrane:</p><ul><li><strong>Dermatophytes:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Trichophyton rubrum</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>T. mentagrophytes</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>T. tonsurans</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Microsporum canis</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>M. audouini</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>M. gypseum</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em></li><li><strong>Yeasts:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Candida albicans</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Malassezia ovale</em> (formerly <em>Pityrosporum ovale</em>)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Candida tropicalis</em></li><li><strong>Malassezia furfur</strong> (cause of tinea versicolor)</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Select-Plus 2%</strong></p><p>For topical administration in adults, elderly, and children:</p><ul><li><strong>Treatment:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dandruff and seborrhoeic dermatitis</strong>: Wash hair twice weekly for <strong>2-4 weeks</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea versicolor</strong>: Wash once daily for a maximum of <strong>5 days</strong>.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dandruff and seborrhoeic dermatitis</strong>: Use once every <strong>1-2 weeks</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea versicolor</strong>: Wash once daily for a maximum of <strong>3 days</strong> before exposure to sunlight.</li></ul><p>If clinical improvement is not observed after the treatment period, re-evaluate the diagnosis.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Select-Plus 2%</strong></p><p>No information is available for drug interactions with the shampoo form of Select-Plus 2%.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Select-Plus 2% therapeutic shampoo is contraindicated in persons who have shown <strong>hypersensitivity</strong> to Select-Plus 2% or any of its excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Select-Plus 2%</strong></p><p>In clinical trials:</p><ul><li>Less than <strong>1%</strong> of patients experienced increased normal hair loss and irritation.</li><li>In open-label safety trials (shampooing 4-10 times weekly for six months), the following adverse events each occurred once:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Abnormal hair texture</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Scalp pustules</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mild dryness of the skin</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Itching</li></ul><p>As with other shampoos, <strong>oiliness</strong> and <strong>dryness</strong> of hair and scalp have been reported. In a placebo-controlled trial for tinea versicolor, drug-related adverse events were reported in:</p><ul><li><strong>5%</strong> (single application),</li><li><strong>7%</strong> (daily application for 3 days),</li><li><strong>4%</strong> (placebo) of patients.</li></ul><p>Common events included:</p><ul><li><strong>Pruritus</strong></li><li><strong>Application site reactions</strong></li><li><strong>Dry skin</strong></li></ul><p>Rare reports of <strong>hair discoloration</strong> have been noted.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: No well-controlled studies exist in pregnant or lactating women. Limited data show no adverse effects of topical Select-Plus 2% on pregnancy or fetal health. Animal studies have shown reproductive toxicity at doses irrelevant to topical use.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: No anticipated effects on breastfed infants, as plasma concentrations from topical application are very low.</li></ul><p>Use during <strong>pregnancy</strong> only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In patients who have been on prolonged corticosteroid treatment, gradually withdraw the steroid therapy over <strong>2 to 3 weeks</strong> while using Select-Plus 2% to prevent rebound effects.</li><li><strong>Avoid contact with eyes</strong>. If the shampoo enters the eyes, rinse immediately with plenty of water.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Select-Plus 2%</strong></p><p>No specific information is available on the treatment of overdose. If accidentally ingested, <strong>supportive and symptomatic measures</strong> should be taken. <strong>Do not induce vomiting or perform gastric lavage</strong> to avoid aspiration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at a temperature <strong>below 25°C</strong>, away from heat and light.</li><li>Do <strong>not store in the bathroom</strong>.</li><li>Keep away from <strong>children and pets</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in children have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Normal adult dosage is appropriate for elderly patients.</li></ul><h3><br></h3>', 'active', '1817_1734360211.webp', 4, 10, NULL, '2024-12-16 19:43:31', '2025-04-22 19:45:12'),
(257, 'Select Plus', 'Ketoconazole 2%', 400.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Shampoo', '1.9%', 'no', 'yes', '<p><strong>Indications of Select-Plus 2% (200 mL)</strong></p><p>Select-Plus 2% therapeutic shampoo is used for the prevention and treatment of infections in which the yeast <em>Malassezia furfur</em> (formerly <em>Pityrosporum orbiculare</em>) is likely to be involved, such as dandruff, seborrhoeic dermatitis, and tinea (pityriasis) versicolor.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Select-Plus 2% shampoo is a broad-spectrum imidazole-dioxolane, synthetic antifungal agent that inhibits the in vitro growth of the following common dermatophytes and yeasts by altering the permeability of the cell membrane:</p><ul><li><strong>Dermatophytes:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Trichophyton rubrum</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>T. mentagrophytes</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>T. tonsurans</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Microsporum canis</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>M. audouini</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>M. gypseum</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em></li><li><strong>Yeasts:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Candida albicans</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Malassezia ovale</em> (formerly <em>Pityrosporum ovale</em>)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Candida tropicalis</em></li><li><strong>Malassezia furfur</strong> (cause of tinea versicolor)</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Select-Plus 2% (200 mL)</strong></p><p>For topical administration in adults, elderly, and children:</p><ul><li><strong>Treatment:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dandruff and seborrhoeic dermatitis</strong>: Wash hair twice weekly for <strong>2-4 weeks</strong> using approximately <strong>5-10 mL</strong> per application, depending on hair length.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea versicolor</strong>: Wash once daily for a maximum of <strong>5 days</strong>, using about <strong>5-10 mL</strong> of shampoo per application.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dandruff and seborrhoeic dermatitis</strong>: Use once every <strong>1-2 weeks</strong>, applying <strong>5-10 mL</strong> per use.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea versicolor</strong>: Wash once daily for a maximum of <strong>3 days</strong> before exposure to sunlight, applying <strong>5-10 mL</strong> per use.</li></ul><p>If clinical improvement is not observed after the treatment period, re-evaluate the diagnosis.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Select-Plus 2% (200 mL)</strong></p><p>No information is available for drug interactions with the shampoo form of Select-Plus 2%.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Select-Plus 2% therapeutic shampoo is contraindicated in persons who have shown <strong>hypersensitivity</strong> to Select-Plus 2% or any of its excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Select-Plus 2% (200 mL)</strong></p><p>In clinical trials:</p><ul><li>Less than <strong>1%</strong> of patients experienced increased normal hair loss and irritation.</li><li>In open-label safety trials (shampooing 4-10 times weekly for six months), the following adverse events each occurred once:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Abnormal hair texture</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Scalp pustules</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mild dryness of the skin</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Itching</li></ul><p>As with other shampoos, <strong>oiliness</strong> and <strong>dryness</strong> of hair and scalp have been reported. In a placebo-controlled trial for tinea versicolor, drug-related adverse events were reported in:</p><ul><li><strong>5%</strong> (single application),</li><li><strong>7%</strong> (daily application for 3 days),</li><li><strong>4%</strong> (placebo) of patients.</li></ul><p>Common events included:</p><ul><li><strong>Pruritus</strong></li><li><strong>Application site reactions</strong></li><li><strong>Dry skin</strong></li></ul><p>Rare reports of <strong>hair discoloration</strong> have been noted.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: No well-controlled studies exist in pregnant or lactating women. Limited data show no adverse effects of topical Select-Plus 2% on pregnancy or fetal health. Animal studies have shown reproductive toxicity at doses irrelevant to topical use.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: No anticipated effects on breastfed infants, as plasma concentrations from topical application are very low.</li></ul><p>Use during <strong>pregnancy</strong> only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In patients who have been on prolonged corticosteroid treatment, gradually withdraw the steroid therapy over <strong>2 to 3 weeks</strong> while using Select-Plus 2% to prevent rebound effects.</li><li><strong>Avoid contact with eyes</strong>. If the shampoo enters the eyes, rinse immediately with plenty of water.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Select-Plus 2% (200 mL)</strong></p><p>No specific information is available on the treatment of overdose. If accidentally ingested, <strong>supportive and symptomatic measures</strong> should be taken. <strong>Do not induce vomiting or perform gastric lavage</strong> to avoid aspiration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at a temperature <strong>below 25°C</strong>, away from heat and light.</li><li>Do <strong>not store in the bathroom</strong>.</li><li>Keep away from <strong>children and pets</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in children have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Normal adult dosage is appropriate for elderly patients.</li></ul>', 'active', '1405_1734360567.webp', 4, 10, NULL, '2024-12-16 19:48:12', '2025-04-22 19:46:10'),
(258, 'Moov Cream Indian', 'Pain Relief Specialist', 260.00, 1, 'Tube', '20 gm cream in a tube', 'Cream', '20 gm', 'no', 'yes', '<p>Moov Cream Indian 20 gm provides effective pain relief. It contains potent natural ingredients to provide fast and lasting relief. Whether you are suffering from muscle strains, joint pain, or arthritis, Moov Cream Indian 20 gm Cream is a trusted solution that can help you find comfort and ease discomfort. Its non-greasy formula absorbs quickly into the skin.</p><p><strong>Safety Information:</strong></p><ul><li>Always follow the directions on the label.</li><li>Do not take more than the recommended dose.</li><li>Do not give pain relievers to children unless directed by a doctor.</li><li>Be aware of the side effects of pain relievers.</li><li>Talk to your doctor if you have any concerns about taking pain relievers.</li></ul>', 'active', '1651_1734361002.jpg', 49, 10, NULL, '2024-12-16 19:56:42', '2024-12-16 19:57:37'),
(259, 'Moov Spray Indian', 'Pain Relief Specialist', 420.00, 1, 'Bottle', '50 gm in a bottle', 'Spray', '50 gm', 'no', 'yes', '<p>Moov Spray Indian 50 gm provides effective pain relief. It contains potent natural ingredients to provide fast and lasting relief. Whether you are suffering from muscle strains, joint pain, or arthritis, Moov Spray Indian 50 gm Cream is a trusted solution that can help you find comfort and ease discomfort. Its non-greasy formula absorbs quickly into the skin.</p><p><strong>Safety Information:</strong></p><ul><li>Always follow the directions on the label.</li><li>Do not take more than the recommended dose.</li><li>Do not give pain relievers to children unless directed by a doctor.</li><li>Be aware of the side effects of pain relievers.</li><li>Talk to your doctor if you have any concerns about taking pain relievers.</li></ul>', 'active', '1053_1734361835.jpg', 49, 10, NULL, '2024-12-16 20:10:35', '2024-12-16 20:11:17'),
(260, 'ACCU-CHEK Instant S', 'Blood glucose monitoring device', 2623.00, 1, 'Piece', '1\'s in a box', 'Glucometer', 'small', 'no', 'yes', '<p><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">ACCU-CHEK Instant S Small blood glucose meter packs many powerful features into a smaller size. No coding reduces handling steps,&nbsp;see test result averages up to 90 days, get alerts if your test strips are expired or if you’ve underdosed, re-dose within 10 seconds, set pre-and post-meal reminders to test, and more.</span></p><p><strong style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Key Benefits:</strong></p><ul><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Accurate blood glucose measurement</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Reports up to 90 days of test results</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Convenient testing</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Safe with alerts for expired strips or underdosing</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">easy-to-read display</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">intuitive handling with just 2 buttons</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">10 seconds re-dose option</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Pre- and post meal markers</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">postprandial reminder</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">USB interface for data transfer</span></li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Directions For Use:</strong></p><ul><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Insert the glucose test strip.</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Prick your finger with the lancet to draw out a blood drop.</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Carefully touch the blood drop onto the green field of the test strip.</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Read the blood glucose result.</span></li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Safety Information:</strong></p><ul><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Read the manual carefully before use</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Keep out of reach of children</span></li></ul>', 'active', '1698_1734363014.jpg', 10, 11, NULL, '2024-12-16 20:30:14', '2024-12-16 20:30:57'),
(261, 'Thermometer', 'Digital Thermometer', 265.00, 1, 'Piece', '1 piece in a box', 'Thermometer', 'Digital', 'no', 'yes', '<p>Thermometer Digital<span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\"> is a medical device used to measure body temperature. It includes high-precision sensors to provide accurate readings. The thermometer is easy to use with a simple one-button operation. It has a clear, digital display for easy reading. It\'s suitable for all ages and can be used orally, rectally, or under the arm.&nbsp;</span></p><p><strong style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Key Benefits:</strong></p><ul><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">LCD digital display</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Fast temperature measurement</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Flexible</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Safe and accurate</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Memory function</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Beep prompt</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Automatic turn-off</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Clean and sanitary</span></li><li><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Portable and durable</span></li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong style=\"color: rgb(33, 33, 33);\">Safety Information:</strong></p><ul><li><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">Keep out of the reach of children</span></li></ul>', 'active', '1584_1734363955.webp', 25, 11, NULL, '2024-12-16 20:45:55', '2024-12-16 20:46:30'),
(262, 'Blood Pressure Machine Digital (Jumper)', 'Electronic Blood Pressure Monitor', 2500.00, 1, 'Piece', '1 piece in a box', 'Machine', 'JPD HA 200', 'no', 'yes', '<p><strong>Product details of JUMPER JPD-HA200 Digital Electronic Blood Pressure Monitor Machine(CE &amp; FDA Approved)</strong></p><ul><li>Jumper JPD-HA200 Digital Electronic Blood Pressure Monitor Machine.</li><li>Brand: Jumper.</li><li>Product Type: Blood Pressure Monitor Machine.</li><li>Model: Jumper JPD-HA200.</li><li>CE &amp; FDA Approved.</li><li>Approved by the US FDA (Food and Drug Administration).</li><li>High-quality Product.</li><li>The large number makes it easy to navigate and read on the monitor.</li><li>100% certainty of the correct result.</li><li>Adjustable comfortable high arm cuffs (8.7’-17.7).</li><li>Warranty: 1 Year manufacturer warranty.</li></ul>', 'active', '1645_1734364818.jpg', 50, 11, NULL, '2024-12-16 21:00:18', '2024-12-16 21:09:45'),
(263, 'Pulse Oximeter (Jumper)', 'Pulse Oximeter', 1600.00, 1, 'Piece', '1 piece in a box', 'Machine', 'JPD-500D', 'no', 'yes', '<p>JUMPER Fingertip Pulse Oximeteris a portable non-invasive, spot-check, oxygen saturation of arterial hemoglobin (SPO2) and pulse rate of adult and pediatric user at home, and hospital (including clinical use in internist/surgery, anesthesia, intensive care, etc). Based on full digital technology, the Finger Pulse Oximeter non-invasively measures the actual content (oxygen saturation) of oxyhemoglobin (HbO2) in arterial blood using the optical transmittance method.</p><p>Supporting four-direction display and large-font display,JUMPERpulse oximeter is equipped with a color OLED screen to show measured values, plethysmogram, and bar graphs. Besides, it will automatically shut down 16 seconds later when no signal is detected, effectively saving its energy.</p><p>Certainly, it is applicable to a wide range of fields, such as families, hospitals (including operation rooms of the departments of internal medicine and surgery, the department of anesthesiology, the department of pediatrics, and intensive care rooms), oxygen bars, social medical care institutions, and sports &amp; health.</p><p>Package Content:</p><p>1 x JUMPER Fingertip Pulse Oximeter</p><p>1 x Lanyard</p><p>2 x Batteries</p><p>1 x User manual</p><p><br></p><ul><li>Accurate &amp; Fast Measurement: Accurately determine your SpO2 (blood oxygen saturation level) and Pulse Rate in very few seconds. The ONLY OLED pulse oximeter that can read and display up to 100% for SpO2 value.Sound Alarm Indicator: A smart sound alarm system to monitor your SpO2 and PR situation. If an irregular value (higher or lower value) being detected, it will make an alert to warn you to care for body condition.Perfectly Safe and Hygienic: Manufactured according to CE and FDA standards for pulse oximeters used by doctors and other health professionals. Finger rubber uses medical silicone that no causes discomfort and just feel soft when plugging your finger.Easy to Read: OLED display has adjustable brightness and offers the clearest and most visible display of results. Waveform and bar graph appears the real-time changes of spO2 and pulse rate directly. Four directions for you to choose the best comfortable angle.Mini Fingertip Pulse Oximeter: Very light in weight, and small in volume. High-density ABS material makes firmly. Portable and handy designs are convenient for home health care and outdoor activities. The package also includes Lanyard, AAA Batteries, Carry-Bag.</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><ul><li>Brand: Jumper Model: JPD-500DDisplay: OLED Screen Parameters: Pulse Rate, SpO2, (Perfusion Index)Accuracy: SpO2 : 70%~100%, ±2%Pulse Rate: 25-250 bpm, ±2bpmIndication: Real-Time Battery Status, Value Limited Remind</li></ul>', 'active', '1750_1734365338.png', 25, 11, NULL, '2024-12-16 21:08:58', '2024-12-16 21:08:58'),
(264, 'NIVEA MEN Protect & Care Deep Cleaning Face Wash', 'Face Wash', 930.00, 1, 'Tube', '100 ml tube', 'Cream', '100ml', 'no', 'yes', '<p>About Men +Care Oil Control Face Wash</p><p>DOVE MEN + CARE Oil absorbing white mineral clay in this advanced care face wash helps effectively clean skin’s pores, removing oil and impurities to prepare skin for a closer shave. The mild amino acid formula cleanses thoroughly for skin that feels clean, comfortable and shine-free.</p><p>Features &amp; Benefits</p><p><br></p><ul><li>Oil-absorbing mineral clay.</li><li>&nbsp;Effectively cleans pores, removing oil and impurities.</li><li>Skin feels clean, comfortable and shine-free.</li><li>Mild Amino Acid formula.</li><li>Cleanses for a closer shave</li></ul><p>&nbsp;</p>', 'active', '333_1734366596.webp', 51, 2, NULL, '2024-12-16 21:29:56', '2024-12-16 21:30:24'),
(265, 'Dove Men+Care Extra Fresh Refreshing 3-n-1 Hand and Body + Face + Shave Moisturizing Cream Soap Bar', 'Soap', 300.00, 1, 'Soap', '106 gm soap bar', 'Soap', '106 gm', 'no', 'yes', '<p><span style=\"color: rgb(50, 59, 73);\">Looking for a soap bar for men that leaves skin feeling refreshed, hydrated and invigorated? Try Dove Men+Care Extra Fresh Hand &amp; Body, Face &amp; Shave Bar. Unlike some regular bar soap for men that can dry out your skin, this soap bar is uniquely formulated with ¼ moisturizing cream that helps to maintain skin’s moisture and hydration levels. With an invigorating scent, this all around cleanser leaves skin looking healthy and delivers refreshment and skin comfort. Suitable for use on both the body and face, our Men+Care soap bar rinses off easily and leaves skin feeling healthy and strong. In addition to helping to protect skin from dryness, this soap bar is dermatologically tested and will help your skin feel soft and smooth, with proper levels of hydration. At Dove, we understand the importance of having a simple daily routine for caring for your skin. That\'s why Dove Extra Fresh Hand &amp; Body, Face &amp; Shave Bar can be used across your whole body to gently nourish and clean skin, and is even mild enough for use on your face. Directions for use: moisten the bar with water and rub it in your hands to produce a light lather. Massage over your skin before rinsing thoroughly. For a comfortable, refreshing clean, the light, non-greasy foam rinses away easily and leaves skin with an invigorating scent. Dove does not test on animals. All of our products are certified cruelty-free by PETA.</span></p>', 'active', '205_1734366900.webp', 51, 2, NULL, '2024-12-16 21:35:00', '2024-12-16 21:35:33'),
(266, 'NIVEA Men Protect & Care After Shave Balm', 'Save Balm', 550.00, 1, 'Bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Spray', '100 ml', 'no', 'yes', '<ul><li>The soothing formula for all skin types provides instant relief to just shaved skin.</li><li>Special moisturisers, Vitamins and Aloe Vera ensure your skin looks healthy and well cared-for.</li><li>Skin compatibility dermatologically approved.</li><li>Instantly relieved and moisturised skin that doesn’t dry out.</li></ul>', 'active', '1052_1734367225.webp', 52, 2, NULL, '2024-12-16 21:40:25', '2024-12-16 21:40:58'),
(267, 'NIVEA MEN Protect & Care Shaving Foam', 'Shaving Foam', 480.00, 1, 'Bottle', '200 ml in a bottle', 'Cream', '200 ml', 'no', 'yes', '<p>• Rich and lubricating shaving foam with Aloe Vera and Pro-Vitamin B5&nbsp;</p><p>• Protects skin from nicks, cuts and irritations during shaving&nbsp;</p><p>• Softens your beard hair and gives you a smooth razor glide.</p><p>How To Use:</p><p>Step 1 - Shake the can well</p><p>Step 2 - Dispense product on hand</p><p>Step 3 - Massage on your beard</p><p>Step 4 - Shave your beard with a razor</p>', 'active', '383_1734367426.webp', 52, 2, NULL, '2024-12-16 21:43:46', '2024-12-16 21:43:46');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(268, 'Tofacent', 'Tofacitinib', 35.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Rheumatoid Arthritis (RA)</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Indicated for adults with moderately to severely active RA who have not responded adequately or are intolerant to methotrexate.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Can be used alone or with methotrexate or other nonbiologic DMARDs.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Psoriatic Arthritis (PsA)</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For adults with active PsA inadequately controlled with methotrexate or other DMARDs.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Ulcerative Colitis (UC)</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For adults with moderately to severely active UC.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Rheumatoid arthritis and other autoimmune diseases involve dysregulation of pro-inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6, IL-7, IL-15, IL-21, IFN-α, and IFN-β.</li><li>Tofacent inhibits the JAK-STAT pathway, reducing cytokine signaling and inflammatory responses.</li><li>This inhibition is partial and reversible, affecting immune cell recruitment and activation.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Rheumatoid Arthritis &amp; Psoriatic Arthritis</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5 mg twice daily or 11 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adjust to 5 mg once daily in moderate/severe renal or hepatic impairment.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Ulcerative Colitis</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initial: 10 mg twice daily for 8 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance: 5–10 mg twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Discontinue if no adequate benefit is seen after 16 weeks at 10 mg twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole)</strong>: Reduce dose to 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 and CYP2C19 inhibitors (e.g., fluconazole)</strong>: Use 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 inducers (e.g., rifampin)</strong>: May reduce efficacy.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Upper respiratory tract infections, headaches, diarrhea, nasopharyngitis.</li><li>Serious: Increased risk of infections and gastrointestinal perforations.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Infections</strong>: Avoid during active infections.</li><li><strong>Lab Monitoring</strong>: Regular checks of lymphocyte/neutrophil counts, hemoglobin, liver enzymes, and lipids.</li><li><strong>Vaccination</strong>: Avoid live vaccines during therapy.</li></ol><p><strong>Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety not established.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Higher infection risks; use cautiously.</li><li><strong>Renal/Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Dosage adjustments required.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Category C: Use only if benefits outweigh risks.</li><li>Unknown if excreted in human milk; consider discontinuation if nursing.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p>This summary ensures clarity and avoids directly replicating the original text while covering the essential details comprehensively. Let me know if you\'d like further refinements.</p>', 'active', '169_1734372540.png', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-16 23:09:00', '2024-12-16 23:09:00'),
(269, 'Avas', 'Atorvastatin Calcium', 12.00, 14, 'Tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Avas 10 mg is prescribed as part of a comprehensive approach, including diet, to manage elevated levels of total cholesterol, LDL cholesterol, apolipoprotein B (Apo-B), and triglycerides. It is effective for:</p><ul><li>Heterozygous and homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia.</li><li>Mixed dyslipidemia (Fredrickson Types Ia and Ib).</li><li>Hypertriglyceridemia (Fredrickson Type IV).</li><li>Dysbetalipoproteinemia (Fredrickson Type III).</li><li>Reducing cardiac ischemic events in coronary artery disease with high LDL cholesterol.</li><li>Hypercholesterolemia associated with diabetes or post-renal transplantation.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Statins; Other anti-anginal and anti-ischemic medications.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Atorvastatin, the active component, selectively inhibits HMG-CoA reductase, a key enzyme in cholesterol biosynthesis. This inhibition reduces cholesterol production in the liver, increases LDL receptor activity, and enhances LDL clearance from the bloodstream.</p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Primary hypercholesterolemia and mixed hyperlipidemia:</strong></li><li>Adults: Start with 10 mg once daily, adjustable to a maximum of 80 mg after at least 4-week intervals.</li><li>Children (10–18 years): Start with 10 mg, with adjustments to a usual maximum of 20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Familial hypercholesterolemia:</strong></li><li>Adults: Begin at 10 mg, titrate up to 40 mg or a maximum of 80 mg if required.</li><li>Children: Doses range from 10 mg up to a maximum of 80 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular event prevention:</strong></li><li>Adults: Initial dose of 10 mg, adjusted based on response.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><p>Risk of muscle-related side effects increases with drugs like cyclosporine, erythromycin, azole antifungals, and niacin. Monitoring is advised when combined with digoxin, warfarin, oral contraceptives, colestipol, or antacids.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Avoid in cases of hypersensitivity, active liver disease, or persistent unexplained liver enzyme elevations.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Commonly reported side effects include gastrointestinal discomfort (constipation, flatulence, dyspepsia), headache, myalgia, and fatigue.</p><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Regular liver function tests are necessary.</li><li>Use cautiously in heavy alcohol users or those with a liver disease history.</li><li>Discontinue if significant CPK elevation or myopathy is detected.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics:</strong> Exercise caution due to a higher risk of muscle-related issues.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation:</strong> Contraindicated; ensure effective contraception during use.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Management</strong></p><p>Treatment is symptomatic and supportive. Liver function and serum CK levels should be monitored. Hemodialysis is unlikely to aid clearance.</p><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place, away from direct light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p>This version organizes the details clearly and avoids directly replicating the original content while maintaining accuracy.</p>', 'active', '1056_1734372956.png', 7, 12, NULL, '2024-12-16 23:15:56', '2024-12-16 23:15:56'),
(270, 'Nofiate', 'Fenofibrate', 7.00, 10, 'Capsul', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p><strong>Nofiate 200 mg</strong> is prescribed for managing various forms of hyperlipidemia, including types IIa, IIb, III, IV, and V, especially when dietary adjustments and other measures have not been sufficiently effective.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Fibrates.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p><strong>Nofiate 200 mg</strong> belongs to the fibric acid derivative class of medications. It is a prodrug converted into its active form, fenofibric acid, after ingestion. Fenofibric acid exerts its lipid-lowering effects by activating the peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor-alpha (PPARα). Activation of PPARα enhances lipoprotein lipase activity while reducing apoprotein C-III, resulting in improved breakdown and clearance of triglyceride-rich lipoproteins. This leads to alterations in LDL particle size and composition, favoring larger, less atherogenic particles that are metabolized more efficiently. Additionally, <strong>Nofiate 200 mg</strong> promotes an increase in HDL levels by boosting apoproteins A-I and A-II and helps lower uric acid levels by enhancing urinary excretion.</p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><p>The recommended dosage for adults is one 200 mg capsule daily, taken with meals to improve absorption. Therapy should be personalized based on patient response, with adjustments made at intervals of 4 to 8 weeks as needed. Patients are advised to maintain a lipid-lowering diet during treatment.</p><p><strong>Dosage Details</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypercholesterolemia or Mixed Hyperlipidemia</strong>: Initial dose of 200 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Hypertriglyceridemia</strong>: 67–200 mg daily.</li><li>Dosage adjustments should be guided by clinical response and under the supervision of a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>May enhance the anticoagulant effects of warfarin; careful monitoring is advised.</li><li>Can augment glucose-lowering effects of antidiabetic medications.</li><li>Increases the risk of nephrotoxicity with cyclosporine.</li><li>Caution is necessary when combining with HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors (statins) due to the risk of rhabdomyolysis, though low-dose statins are generally well-tolerated.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p><strong>Nofiate 200 mg</strong> should not be used in individuals with:</p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to the drug.</li><li>Severe kidney or liver impairment.</li><li>Gallbladder disease.</li><li>Photosensitivity reactions to ketoprofen.</li><li>Breastfeeding mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common adverse effects include:</p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal symptoms (nausea, anorexia, stomach pain).</li><li>Skin reactions (itching, rash).</li><li>Dizziness, headache, fatigue.</li><li>Hair loss and muscle toxicity (in rare cases).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Classified as Category C; use only if benefits outweigh potential risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Not recommended due to potential risks to infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor renal function to prevent toxicity.</li><li>Regular liver function tests are advised during the first year of treatment.</li><li>Discontinue use if significant muscle toxicity or increased creatine kinase levels are observed.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Management</strong></p><p>In case of overdose, supportive care and monitoring of vital signs are recommended. Hemodialysis is unlikely to be beneficial due to the drug\'s high plasma protein binding.</p><p><strong>Storage Instructions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and out of children’s reach.</p><p><strong>Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Standard dosage can typically be used without adjustments.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Not indicated for pediatric use.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Dose reduction is required for severe impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Limited data; use with caution.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class and Mechanism</strong></p><p><strong>Nofiate 200 mg</strong> is a fibric acid derivative that lowers cholesterol and triglycerides while raising HDL levels. The micronized formulation enhances absorption compared to non-micronized versions.</p><p>Let me know if you\'d like to adjust or expand any specific section!</p>', 'active', '1679_1734373167.webp', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-16 23:19:27', '2024-12-16 23:19:27'),
(271, 'Rosuva', 'Rosuvastatin', 32.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p><strong>Rosuva 20 mg</strong> is indicated for the treatment and management of:</p><ul><li><strong>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (familial and non-familial).</li><li><strong>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (familial).</li><li><strong>Mixed Dyslipidemia</strong> (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb).</li><li><strong>Primary Prevention of Cardiovascular Disease</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Belongs to the <strong>Statins</strong> class and categorized under <strong>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischemic drugs</strong>.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rosuva 20 mg is a <strong>selective and competitive inhibitor</strong> of <strong>HMG-CoA reductase</strong>, the key enzyme in cholesterol biosynthesis. It modifies lipid levels through two mechanisms:</p><ol><li><strong>Increasing hepatic LDL receptors:</strong> Enhances uptake and breakdown of LDL cholesterol.</li><li><strong>Inhibiting hepatic VLDL synthesis:</strong> Reduces total VLDL and LDL particle levels.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General Dose Range:</strong> 5-40 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The <strong>40 mg dose</strong> is reserved for patients not achieving LDL-C targets with 20 mg.</li><li><strong>Homozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia (HoFH):</strong> Start with 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Dosing:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>HeFH (8–10 years):</strong> 5-10 mg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>HeFH (10–17 years):</strong> 5-20 mg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>HoFH (7–17 years):</strong> 20 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Rosuva 20 mg can be taken <strong>with or without food</strong> at any time of day.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Cyclosporine:</strong> Increases exposure; do not exceed 5 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil:</strong> Avoid combination; limit to 10 mg/day if co-administered.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or Atazanavir/Ritonavir:</strong> Limit dose to 10 mg/day due to elevated exposure.</li><li><strong>Coumarin Anticoagulants:</strong> May increase INR; monitor INR until stable.</li><li><strong>Concomitant Lipid-Lowering Therapies:</strong> Co-administration with fibrates or niacin may elevate the risk of skeletal muscle effects.</li></ol><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Do not use Rosuva 20 mg in the following cases:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the medication.</li><li>Active liver disease or unexplained persistent elevations of hepatic transaminase levels.</li><li>Pregnancy or women who may become pregnant.</li><li>Breastfeeding mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Commonly reported adverse effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia (muscle pain)</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia (weakness)</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Skeletal Muscle Effects:</strong> Risk of myopathy or rhabdomyolysis increases with higher doses, age &gt;65 years, renal impairment, hypothyroidism, or when used with certain other medications. Discontinue if muscle symptoms occur.</li><li><strong>Liver Enzyme Monitoring:</strong> Persistent elevation in hepatic transaminases may occur; monitor liver enzymes before and during treatment.</li></ol><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Contraindicated as safety in pregnant women has not been established.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Unknown if excreted in breast milk; avoid use in nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><ul><li>Store at <strong>temperatures below 30°C</strong>, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and efficacy not fully established for all age groups.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Increased risk of side effects such as myopathy; monitor closely.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1005_1734373420.webp', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-16 23:23:40', '2024-12-16 23:23:40'),
(272, 'Sitagil M', 'Sitagliptin + Metformin Hydrochloride', 16.00, 4, 'Tablet', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg+500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Sitagil M 50 mg+500 mg</strong></p><p>Sitagil M 50 mg+500 mg is a combination medication designed to manage high blood sugar levels in individuals with type 2 diabetes mellitus. By controlling blood sugar, it reduces the risk of severe complications such as kidney damage, vision loss, heart attack, or stroke. This medication can be prescribed on its own or alongside other antidiabetic drugs. For maximum effectiveness, it should be used in conjunction with a balanced diet and regular physical activity.</p><p>The dosage varies based on individual health status, current blood sugar levels, and any concurrent medications. To minimize stomach upset, it is best taken with meals. Consistent, daily usage at the same time each day is crucial for optimal benefit. Avoid discontinuing the medication without consulting your healthcare provider. Lifestyle modifications, as recommended by your doctor, remain essential for managing diabetes effectively.</p><p><strong>Common Side Effects:</strong></p><p>Some individuals may experience side effects such as nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, headache, or sore throat. Low blood sugar (hypoglycemia) can occur if used with insulin or sulfonylureas, so it’s important to recognize and manage symptoms of hypoglycemia.</p><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><p>This medication may not be suitable for everyone. Inform your doctor if you have a history of kidney, liver, or heart issues, or if you consume alcohol regularly. Pregnant or breastfeeding women should use it only under medical advice.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Sitagliptin, a dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitor, enhances the effects of incretin hormones such as GLP-1 and GIP. These hormones regulate glucose levels by increasing insulin release and reducing glucagon secretion, improving glucose homeostasis. Sitagliptin’s selective mechanism avoids the hypoglycemic risks associated with sulfonylureas.</p><p>Metformin, an antihyperglycemic agent, lowers blood glucose by inhibiting hepatic glucose production and improving insulin sensitivity. It also slows glucose absorption in the gut. Unlike other agents, metformin does not trigger hypoglycemia or excessive insulin secretion.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><p>The dose of Sitagil M should be tailored to the patient, with the daily limit set at 100 mg of sitagliptin and 2000 mg of metformin. Generally, it is taken twice daily with meals to reduce gastrointestinal side effects. Gradual dose adjustments may be necessary to enhance tolerability.</p><p>For patients new to metformin, the starting dose is 50 mg sitagliptin/500 mg metformin twice daily. Those already on metformin can continue with a similar dose of metformin alongside 50 mg sitagliptin twice daily. Extended-release tablets should be taken once daily with an evening meal.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Warnings and Precautions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Lactic Acidosis:</strong> Rare but serious, especially in cases of renal impairment, excessive alcohol consumption, or dehydration. Symptoms include fatigue, abdominal discomfort, and rapid breathing.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12 Deficiency:</strong> Long-term use may lead to reduced B12 levels, requiring monitoring.</li><li><strong>Renal Function:</strong> Regular assessment is crucial as both sitagliptin and metformin are excreted via the kidneys.</li><li><strong>Pancreatitis:</strong> There have been postmarketing reports of pancreatitis. Discontinue use immediately if symptoms develop.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>The most common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal issues (diarrhea, nausea, abdominal discomfort)</li><li>Respiratory symptoms (nasopharyngitis, sore throat)</li><li>Hypoglycemia when used with insulin or sulfonylureas</li><li>Headache and fatigue</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Use with caution as kidney function often declines with age.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics:</strong> Not recommended for those under 18, as safety and efficacy are not established.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation:</strong> Should only be used if absolutely necessary. It is unclear whether sitagliptin is excreted in breast milk, so caution is advised.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Instructions</strong></p><p>Store below 25°C in a dry, dark place. Keep out of reach of children. Use before the expiration date and only under a doctor’s prescription.</p><p>This version is entirely rephrased for clarity, originality, and flow.</p>', 'active', '765_1734373636.webp', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-16 23:27:16', '2024-12-16 23:27:16'),
(273, 'Vibose', 'Voglibose', 8.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.3 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Vibose 0.3 mg</strong></p><p>Vibose 0.3 mg is prescribed for managing <strong>diabetes mellitus (DM)</strong>, specifically to reduce <strong>post-prandial hyperglycemia (PPHG)</strong>. This is recommended when lifestyle changes, such as diet and exercise, or the use of oral hypoglycemic agents (OHAs) or insulin fail to achieve adequate glycemic control. It is indicated for use in the following scenarios:</p><ol><li><strong>Non-insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus (NIDDM):</strong> As an adjunct therapy.</li><li><strong>Combination therapy:</strong> With other OHAs.</li><li><strong>Insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus:</strong> As an addition to insulin therapy.</li><li><strong>Impaired glucose tolerance (IGT):</strong> Prevention of type 2 diabetes mellitus progression, provided dietary and exercise measures have not yielded sufficient improvement (specific to 0.2 mg Vibose tablets).</li><li><strong>Special populations:</strong> Including elderly individuals or those with hepatic dysfunction or mild-to-moderate renal impairment, where other OHAs may be contraindicated or require caution.</li><li><strong>Glycogen storage disease type 1b:</strong> Helps prevent hypoglycemia.</li><li><strong>Non-diabetic hyperinsulinemia:</strong> Useful in avoiding hypoglycemic episodes.</li><li><strong>Steroid-induced diabetes:</strong> While data in this area are limited, Vibose 0.3 mg may offer benefits.</li></ol><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p><strong>Alpha-Glucosidase Inhibitor.</strong></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors, including Vibose, act by competitively inhibiting enzymes in the small intestine necessary for carbohydrate digestion. These enzymes, located in the intestinal brush border, typically convert oligosaccharides, trisaccharides, and disaccharides into absorbable glucose and monosaccharides. Vibose also inhibits pancreatic alpha-amylase, an enzyme that breaks down complex starches into smaller sugars.</p><p>By slowing carbohydrate digestion, the drug reduces glucose absorption, thus lowering blood glucose levels post-meal. In the long term, Vibose contributes to a modest reduction in HbA1c levels in diabetic patients.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><h4><strong>Adults:</strong></h4><ul><li>The usual starting dose is <strong>0.2 mg</strong>, taken orally three times a day before meals.</li><li>If necessary, the dose may be increased to <strong>0.3 mg</strong> per meal.</li></ul><h4><strong>Geriatrics:</strong></h4><ul><li>Due to reduced physiological function in elderly patients, it is recommended to start at a lower dose, such as <strong>0.1 mg per meal</strong>, and adjust gradually under medical supervision.</li></ul><h4><strong>Pediatrics:</strong></h4><ul><li>The safety and effectiveness of Vibose in children have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><p>Vibose should be used cautiously with the following:</p><ol><li><strong>Antidiabetic drugs</strong>: Such as sulfonylureas, biguanides, insulin preparations, and agents improving insulin resistance, due to the risk of hypoglycemia.</li><li><strong>Medications affecting blood glucose levels</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Enhancing hypoglycemic effects</strong>: Beta-blockers, salicylates, monoamine oxidase inhibitors, fibrates, and warfarin.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Reducing hypoglycemic effects</strong>: Adrenaline, corticosteroids, and thyroid hormones.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vibose 0.3 mg is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to the drug or its components.</li><li>Diabetic ketoacidosis or pre-coma.</li><li>Severe infections, trauma, or surgical recovery.</li><li>Gastrointestinal obstruction or conditions predisposing to it.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal symptoms: Diarrhea, flatulence, abdominal pain, nausea, constipation, and heartburn.</li><li>Rare side effects: Hepatitis, serious liver dysfunction, jaundice, hypoglycemia (when combined with OHAs), anemia, hyperkalemia, and blurred vision.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Safety in pregnancy has not been fully established, and the use of Vibose should be avoided unless necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Though minimal amounts of Vibose pass into breast milk, its use is not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with a history of laparotomy or ileus, chronic intestinal diseases, or severe hepatic or renal dysfunction.</li><li>Regular monitoring of blood sugar levels and gastrointestinal symptoms is recommended during treatment.</li><li>Adjustments may be needed for elderly patients and those with underlying conditions like Roemheld’s syndrome or intestinal ulceration.</li></ul><p>O<strong>verdose</strong></p><p>Overdosing on Vibose does not typically result in hypoglycemia. Instead, symptoms like flatulence, diarrhea, and abdominal discomfort may occur transiently. No severe systemic effects are expected.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p>This version rephrases and organizes the information for clarity and originality while avoiding direct duplication. Let me know if further adjustments are needed!</p>', 'active', '1841_1734373996.jpg', 3, 12, NULL, '2024-12-16 23:33:16', '2024-12-16 23:33:16'),
(274, 'Biltin', 'Bilastine', 15.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><p>Biltin 20 mg is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of:</p><ul><li>Allergic rhino-conjunctivitis (seasonal and perennial)</li><li>Urticaria</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Biltin 20 mg is a non-sedative, selective H1 receptor antagonist. Its primary effects result from selective inhibition of peripheral H1-receptors, with moderate to high affinity. It does not exhibit affinity for muscarinic, serotonergic, dopaminergic, or noradrenergic receptors.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration:</strong></p><h4><strong>Adults &amp; Adolescents (12 years and older):</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Dose:</strong> 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Administration:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Swallow the tablet with water on an empty stomach for optimal absorption.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not exceed the recommended daily dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If a dose is missed, take the next scheduled dose. Avoid double dosing.</li></ul><h4><strong>Children (6 to 11 years):</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Dose:</strong> 10 mg mouth-dissolving tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Administration:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Place the tablet in the mouth to dissolve rapidly in saliva or disperse it in a teaspoon of water before swallowing.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not exceed the recommended dose.</li></ul><h4><strong>Children (2 to 11 years):</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Dose:</strong> 4 ml once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Increased Plasma Concentration:</strong> Concomitant use with Ketoconazole, Erythromycin, or Diltiazem may increase the maximum concentration (Cmax) of Biltin 20 mg.</li><li><strong>Alcohol Interaction:</strong> Psychomotor effects are similar to placebo when combined with alcohol.</li><li><strong>With Lorazepam:</strong> No potentiation of CNS depressant effects when co-administered for up to 8 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Biltin 20 mg or any of its excipients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Headache, dizziness, somnolence, and fatigue.</li><li>Occurrence: Comparable to placebo in clinical trials.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid co-administration with P-glycoprotein inhibitors (e.g., Ketoconazole, Erythromycin, Ritonavir) in patients with moderate or severe renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Limited data; avoid use unless recommended by a physician.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Dizziness, headache, and nausea (reported at doses 10-11 times the therapeutic dose).</li><li>No serious adverse effects or QTc interval prolongation were reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment required.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No clinical experience, but no adjustments necessary due to non-metabolized clearance route.</li><li><strong>Elderly (&gt;65 years):</strong> No dosage adjustments needed.</li><li><strong>Pediatric (&lt;6 years):</strong> Safety and efficacy not established.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C.</li><li>Protect from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class and Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Class:</strong> Non-sedating antihistamines</li><li><strong>Action:</strong> Long-acting histamine H1 receptor antagonist, selectively inhibiting histamine-induced skin reactions (wheal and flare) for 24 hours post-dose.</li></ul>', 'active', '932_1734374616.webp', 53, 12, NULL, '2024-12-16 23:43:36', '2024-12-16 23:44:14'),
(275, 'Nebita', 'Nebivolol', 7.02, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nebita 2.5 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Nebita 2.5 mg</strong> is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Treatment of essential hypertension.</li><li>Adjunctive therapy for stable mild-to-moderate heart failure in patients over 70 years of age.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Beta-adrenoceptor blocking drugs</li><li>Beta-blockers</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Nebita 2.5 mg is a β-adrenergic receptor blocking agent. It is a racemic mixture of two enantiomers (d-Nebita and l-Nebita) and exhibits selective β1-adrenergic receptor antagonism. It also promotes vasodilation through endothelium-mediated nitric oxide release. Nebita 2.5 mg does not have intrinsic sympathomimetic or membrane-stabilizing activity.</p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 5 mg once daily; maximum dose: 40 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Start with 2.5 mg daily in severe cases, titrate slowly as needed.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Begin with 2.5 mg daily in moderate cases, titrate slowly if required.</li><li><strong>Elderly Patients:</strong> No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients:</strong> Safety and efficacy have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Caution with CYP2D6 inhibitors (e.g., quinidine, propafenone, fluoxetine, paroxetine).</li><li>Avoid concurrent use with other β-blockers or agents that slow atrioventricular conduction, such as digitalis glycosides, verapamil, or diltiazem, as they may increase bradycardia risk.</li><li>Can enhance the effects of myocardial depressants and certain antiarrhythmic agents (e.g., disopyramide).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Nebita 2.5 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Severe bradycardia or heart block beyond first degree.</li><li>Cardiogenic shock.</li><li>Decompensated cardiac failure.</li><li>Sick sinus syndrome.</li><li>Severe hepatic impairment.</li><li>Known hypersensitivity to any component of the medication.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common adverse effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Bradycardia</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category C – Use only if benefits outweigh risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions and Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in patients with well-compensated heart failure or inadequate cardiac function.</li><li>May mask symptoms of hypoglycemia or hyperthyroidism.</li><li>Abrupt discontinuation may worsen angina or precipitate myocardial infarction in patients with coronary artery disease.</li><li>Avoid in patients undergoing major surgery involving general anesthesia.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Management</strong></p><p>Symptoms: Bradycardia, hypotension, cardiac failure, dizziness, fatigue, hypoglycemia, vomiting, bronchospasm, or heart block.</p><p>Management:</p><ul><li><strong>Bradycardia:</strong> IV atropine or isoproterenol.</li><li><strong>Hypotension:</strong> IV fluids and vasopressors.</li><li><strong>Bronchospasm:</strong> β2-agonists and/or aminophylline.</li><li><strong>Hypoglycemia:</strong> IV glucose.</li><li><strong>Heart failure:</strong> IV cardiac glycosides and diuretics.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at 20–25°C. Protect from light and keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '6_1734374952.png', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-16 23:49:12', '2024-12-16 23:49:12'),
(276, 'Betaloc', 'Metoprolol Tartrate', 2.05, 14, 'Tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Betaloc 50 mg (Metoprolol) is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: Helps manage high blood pressure.</li><li><strong>Angina Pectoris</strong>: Reduces chest pain due to heart conditions.</li><li><strong>Cardiac Arrhythmias</strong>: Especially effective for supraventricular tachyarrhythmias.</li><li><strong>Hyperthyroidism</strong>: Used as an adjunctive therapy to manage symptoms.</li><li><strong>Myocardial Infarction (Heart Attack)</strong>: Early intervention can minimize infarct size, reduce ventricular fibrillation risks, and improve pain management, potentially lowering the need for opioids. It has been shown to lower mortality rates when administered during acute myocardial infarction.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Belongs to the <strong>beta-adrenoceptor blocking agents</strong>, specifically <strong>beta1-selective blockers</strong>.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Metoprolol selectively blocks beta1-adrenergic receptors, minimizing the heart\'s response to catecholamines, which are released during stress. This results in:</p><ul><li>Reduced heart rate, cardiac output, contractility, and blood pressure during stress.</li><li>Minimal interference with insulin secretion and carbohydrate metabolism, compared to non-selective beta-blockers.</li><li>Less impact on cardiovascular responses to hypoglycemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><h4><strong>Oral Administration</strong></h4><ol><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Daily dose: 100-400 mg, taken once or in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Starting dose: 100 mg per day, increased by 100 mg weekly if needed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">May combine with diuretics or other antihypertensives.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Angina</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">50-100 mg taken 2-3 times daily.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Cardiac Arrhythmias</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">50 mg 2-3 times daily; dose can increase up to 300 mg daily if needed.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Hyperthyroidism</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">50 mg, four times a day; dose reduction advised upon achieving a euthyroid state.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Myocardial Infarction</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Start 50 mg every 6 hours for 48 hours, transitioning to a maintenance dose of 200 mg daily.</li></ul><h4><strong>Injection Administration</strong></h4><ol><li><strong>Arrhythmias</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5 mg intravenously (1-2 mg/min), repeatable after 5 minutes; max dose: 10-15 mg.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Surgical Use</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2-4 mg intravenously to manage arrhythmias during anesthesia.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Myocardial Infarction</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Administer 5 mg every 2 minutes up to a max of 15 mg, followed by oral therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No adjustments needed in healthy individuals.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dose modifications required.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Dosage reduction may be necessary for severe liver dysfunction.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Not recommended for patients with:</p><ul><li>Severe bradycardia or heart block.</li><li>Cardiogenic shock or uncontrolled heart failure.</li><li>Significant peripheral arterial disease.</li><li>Hypersensitivity to Metoprolol or other beta-blockers.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>May include:</p><ul><li>Fatigue, dizziness, depression, or headache.</li><li>Gastrointestinal issues such as nausea or constipation.</li><li>Shortness of breath, slow heart rate, or rash.</li><li>Rare: Insomnia, blurred vision, tinnitus, or reduced libido.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Bronchospastic Diseases</strong>: Use cautiously in patients with respiratory conditions.</li><li><strong>Diabetes</strong>: May mask symptoms of hypoglycemia (e.g., tachycardia).</li><li><strong>Peripheral Vascular Disease</strong>: Monitor for aggravated arterial insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Surgery</strong>: Beta-blockers may affect reflex adrenergic responses under anesthesia.</li></ol><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Catecholamine-depleting drugs</strong> (e.g., reserpine, MAO inhibitors) may enhance effects.</li><li><strong>CYP2D6 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., fluoxetine, propafenone) increase Metoprolol levels.</li><li><strong>Digitalis glycosides</strong> combined with beta-blockers increase bradycardia risk.</li><li>Withdrawal of clonidine may lead to rebound hypertension.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry location, away from light.</p><p><br></p><p>This version preserves the key details and structure but avoids direct copying. Let me know if you need further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '1006_1734375176.jpg', 9, 12, NULL, '2024-12-16 23:52:56', '2024-12-16 23:52:56'),
(277, 'Lopirel', 'Clopidogrel Bisulphate', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '75 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Lopirel 75 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Acute Coronary Syndrome (ACS):</strong></p><p>Lopirel 75 mg is used to reduce the risk of myocardial infarction (MI) and stroke in patients with non-ST-segment elevation ACS, such as unstable angina (UA) or non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI).</p><p><strong>Recent MI, Stroke, or Peripheral Arterial Disease:</strong></p><p>It is indicated to reduce the incidence of MI and stroke in patients with a history of recent MI, recent stroke, or established peripheral arterial disease.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p><strong>Anti-platelet Drugs</strong></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Clopidogrel, the active ingredient, is a prodrug that inhibits platelet activation and aggregation. Its active metabolite binds irreversibly to P2Y12 ADP receptors on platelets, preventing platelet aggregation.</p><ul><li><strong>Onset of Action:</strong> Dose-dependent inhibition begins within 2 hours of a single oral dose.</li><li><strong>Steady State:</strong> Achieved between Days 3–7 with repeated daily doses of 75 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>For Acute Coronary Syndrome:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Loading Dose:</strong> 300 mg (4 tablets) orally if immediate antiplatelet effect is required.</li><li><strong>Maintenance Dose:</strong> 75 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Note:</strong> Without a loading dose, the antiplatelet effect may take several days to establish.</li></ul><p><strong>For Recent MI, Stroke, or Peripheral Arterial Disease:</strong></p><ul><li>75 mg once daily without a loading dose.</li><li>Can be taken with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>NSAIDs, Warfarin, SSRIs, and SNRIs:</strong> Increased bleeding risk.</li><li><strong>CYP2C19 Inhibitors (e.g., Omeprazole, Esomeprazole):</strong> Avoid use, as they reduce the activation of clopidogrel.</li><li><strong>Repaglinide (CYP2C8 substrates):</strong> Avoid concurrent use to prevent increased plasma concentrations of repaglinide.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to clopidogrel or its components.</li><li>Active bleeding (e.g., peptic ulcer, intracranial hemorrhage).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p><strong>Common:</strong></p><ul><li>Bleeding</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Gastrointestinal discomfort</li><li>Hemorrhage</li><li>Skin reactions</li></ul><p><strong>Rare:</strong></p><ul><li>Acquired hemophilia</li><li>Anemia</li><li>Angioedema</li><li>Bone marrow disorders</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Use only if clearly necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Unknown if excreted in breast milk. Discontinue either the drug or breastfeeding based on clinical need.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CYP2C19 Poor Metabolizers:</strong> Reduced drug activation and efficacy.</li><li><strong>Bleeding Risk:</strong> Increased due to prolonged platelet aggregation inhibition (7–10 days).</li><li><strong>Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura (TTP):</strong> Rare but serious, requires urgent treatment.</li><li><strong>Pre-Surgery:</strong> Discontinue 5 days before elective surgery with significant bleeding risk.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Management</strong></p><p>Overdose may lead to bleeding complications. Platelet transfusion can restore clotting ability if needed.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a dry place, protected from light.</li><li>Do not freeze.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatrics:</strong> Safety and efficacy not established.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> No dose adjustment necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Clopidogrel’s active metabolite irreversibly binds to P2Y12 ADP receptors on platelets, inhibiting their activation and aggregation. Steady-state inhibition of platelet aggregation is achieved after consistent daily dosing.</p>', 'active', '1078_1734375379.webp', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-16 23:56:19', '2024-12-16 23:56:19'),
(278, 'Diamicron MR', 'Gliclazide', 14.00, 30, 'Tablet', '30 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '30 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Sulfonylureas</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Diamicron MR 30 mg works by stimulating insulin release from the pancreas\' β-cells. It facilitates the transport of calcium ions (Ca²⁺) across β-cell membranes, which enhances insulin secretion. Additionally, it reduces hepatic glucose production.</p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial Dose:</strong> 40 to 80 mg daily, which can be increased up to 320 mg daily as needed.</li><li><strong>Administration:</strong> The tablets should be taken before a meal, preferably at breakfast.</li><li><strong>Modified Release:</strong> The recommended daily dose is 30 mg (one to four tablets) taken once daily at breakfast. The dose may vary based on individual blood sugar levels.</li><li><strong>Combination Therapy:</strong> Diamicron MR 30 mg can be used in combination with other antidiabetic medications like metformin, alpha-glucosidase inhibitors, thiazolidinediones, DPP-4 inhibitors, GLP-1 receptor agonists, or insulin.</li></ul><p><strong>Missed Dose:</strong> If a dose is missed, take it at the regular time. Do not double the dose to compensate for a missed one.</p><p><strong>Overdose:</strong> Symptoms of an overdose are typically those of hypoglycemia (low blood sugar), including dizziness, confusion, and unconsciousness. Immediate treatment with sugar or sugary drinks is advised, followed by a substantial snack or meal. Seek emergency medical care if symptoms persist.</p><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Increased Hypoglycemia Risk:</strong> When taken with other antidiabetics, certain antibiotics (e.g., sulfonamides), beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors, antifungal medications, or alcohol.</li><li><strong>Reduced Effectiveness:</strong> Drugs like corticosteroids, salbutamol, or certain antidepressants (e.g., chlorpromazine) may reduce the blood sugar-lowering effect of Diamicron MR.</li><li><strong>Blood Clotting:</strong> Diamicron MR may increase the effects of blood thinners (e.g., warfarin).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Allergy to sulfonylureas or other related hypoglycemic sulfonamides</li><li>Type 1 diabetes (insulin-dependent)</li><li>Diabetic ketoacidosis or coma</li><li>Severe liver or kidney disease</li><li>During breastfeeding</li><li>Pregnancy</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Hypoglycemia (low blood sugar), which can cause symptoms like dizziness, confusion, or unconsciousness if untreated.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Liver dysfunction, skin reactions, blood disorders (e.g., reduced platelets), digestive issues (nausea, diarrhea), and eye disorders (temporary blurred vision).</li><li><strong>Severe:</strong> Allergic reactions like rash, swelling, and severe hypoglycemia. Liver failure is a rare but serious side effect.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Regular blood glucose and HbA1c monitoring is necessary.</li><li>The risk of hypoglycemia increases with irregular meals, fasting, alcohol consumption, or increased physical activity.</li><li>Elderly patients may be more prone to hypoglycemia and should be closely monitored.</li><li>In stressful situations (surgery, infections, etc.), switching to insulin may be necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation:</strong></p><p>Diamicron MR 30 mg should not be used during pregnancy or breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Do not use after the expiry date.</li></ul><p>Always consult with a healthcare provider before making any changes to the treatment or medication regimen.</p>', 'active', '452_1734375594.jpg', 41, 12, NULL, '2024-12-16 23:59:54', '2024-12-16 23:59:54');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(279, 'Bisocor', 'Bisoprolol Fumarate', 11.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Bisocor 5 mg</strong></p><p>Bisocor 5 mg is indicated in- Hypertension Angina Moderate to severe heart failure</p><p><strong>Theropeutic Class</strong></p><p>Anti adrenergic agent (Beta blockers), Beta-adrenoceptor blocking drugs, Beta-blockers</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Bisoprolol Hemifumarate is the most selective ß1 blocker. It displays highest level of affinity for the ß1 receptor than any other beta-blocker available up to now. Selectively blocks ß1 adrenergic receptor in the heart and vascular smooth muscle and reduces heart rate and cardiac output resulting in decrease of arterial hypertension. Lipid metabolism can be adversely affected by ß-blockers, in patients with non-ß1 selective ß1-blocker, but Bisoprolol does not cause any change in the cholesterol fraction including the cardioprotective HDL-cholesterol, in long-term therapy.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Bisocor 5 mg</strong></p><p>Hypertension: The dose of Bisoprolol must be individualized to the needs of the patient. The usual starting dose is 5 mg once daily. In some patients, 2.5 mg may be an appropriate starting dose. If the antihypertensive effect of 5 mg is inadequate, the dose may be increased to 10 mg and then, if necessary, to 20 mg once daily.</p><p>Angina: Usually 10 mg once daily (5 mg may be adequate in some patients) max 20 mg daily.</p><p>Heart Failure: Initially 1.25 mg once daily (in the morning) for 1 week then, if well tolerated, increased to 2.5 mg once daily for 1 week, then 3.75 mg once daily for 1 week, then 5 mg once daily for 4 weeks, then- 7.5 mg once daily for 4 weeks, then 10 mg once daily maximum 10 mg daily.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Bisocor 5 mg</strong></p><p>Bisoprolol should not be combined with other ß-blocking agents.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>In patients with cardiogenic shock, overt heart failure, second or third degree A-V block, right ventricular failure secondary to pulmonary hypertension and sinus bradycardia.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Bisocor 5 mg</strong></p><p>Gastro-intestinal disturbances, bradycardia, hypotension, headache, fatigue, sleep disturbances, dizziness, vertigo, thrombocytopenia, visual disturbances, alopecia may be occurred.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of Bisocor 5 mg during pregnancy has not been established.&nbsp;No data is available for lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Monitoring of renal, hepatic and hematopoietic function should be performed at regular intervals during long-term treatment with bisoprolol.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><br></p>', 'active', '1340_1734375736.webp', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-17 00:02:16', '2024-12-17 00:02:16'),
(280, 'Linatab M', 'Linagliptin + Metformin Hydrochloride', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.5 mg+500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Linatab M 2.5 mg + 500 mg</strong></p><p>Linatab M is used in conjunction with diet and exercise to help improve blood sugar control in adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus. It is prescribed when treatment with both Linagliptin and Metformin Hydrochloride is appropriate.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Combination oral hypoglycemic preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Linagliptin</strong>: A DPP-4 inhibitor that increases the levels of active incretin hormones (GLP-1 and GIP), stimulating insulin release from pancreatic beta cells and reducing glucagon secretion from pancreatic alpha cells in a glucose-dependent manner.</li><li><strong>Metformin Hydrochloride</strong>: A biguanide that reduces blood sugar levels by decreasing glucose production in the liver, lowering glucose absorption in the intestines, and improving insulin sensitivity, without causing hypoglycemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Immediate-release combination</strong>:</li><li>The dose should be tailored to individual needs. The maximum recommended dose is 2.5 mg of Linagliptin and 1000 mg of Metformin Hydrochloride twice daily with meals. Dose escalation should be gradual to minimize gastrointestinal side effects.</li><li><strong>Starting dose</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">For patients not previously on Metformin, start with 2.5 mg of Linagliptin and 500 mg of Metformin Hydrochloride twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For patients already on Metformin, start with 2.5 mg of Linagliptin and their current dose of Metformin Hydrochloride twice daily.</li><li><strong>Extended-release combination</strong>:</li><li>The maximum daily dose should not exceed 5 mg of Linagliptin and 2000 mg of Metformin Hydrochloride. This combination is taken once daily with a meal.</li><li><strong>Starting dose</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">For patients not previously treated with Metformin, initiate with 5 mg of Linagliptin and 1000 mg of Metformin Hydrochloride extended-release once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For those already on Metformin, start with 5 mg of Linagliptin and a similar dose of Metformin.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><p>Cationic drugs (such as amiloride, digoxin, and trimethoprim) may reduce Metformin elimination. Rifampin, a P-glycoprotein/CYP3A4 inducer, may reduce the effectiveness of the combination.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Not recommended for patients with renal impairment, as Metformin is primarily eliminated through the kidneys.</li><li>Contraindicated in individuals with acute or chronic metabolic acidosis (including diabetic ketoacidosis) and hypersensitivity to Linagliptin or Metformin.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common side effects include nasopharyngitis and diarrhea. Hypoglycemia may occur, especially when combined with sulfonylureas.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></p><p>There are no adequate studies on the safety of this combination during pregnancy. It should be used only if absolutely necessary. Caution is also recommended for breastfeeding mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions and Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Discontinue immediately if lactic acidosis develops.</li><li>Discontinue if pancreatitis is suspected.</li><li>Temporarily stop treatment during procedures requiring fasting or contrast materials.</li><li>Monitor Vitamin B12 levels annually, as Metformin may lower them.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose of Metformin may lead to lactic acidosis, and it is dialyzable.</li><li>In case of overdose, general supportive measures should be employed.</li><li>Linagliptin does not appear to be dialyzable and has a wide margin of safety in overdose cases.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place below 30°C, away from light and moisture, and out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Combination oral hypoglycemic preparations.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Linagliptin</strong>: Increases active incretin hormones, promoting insulin secretion and reducing glucagon release.</li><li><strong>Metformin</strong>: Reduces glucose production, lowers absorption from the intestines, and improves insulin sensitivity, without causing hypoglycemia.</li></ul><p>This version condenses and simplifies the information while avoiding direct copying from the original source.</p>', 'active', '1935_1734375941.jpg', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-17 00:05:41', '2024-12-17 00:05:41'),
(281, 'Tofen', 'Ketotifen Fumarate', 4.00, 15, 'Tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '1 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Cromoglycate &amp; related drugs.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Tofen 1 mg is a potent anti-allergic substance with strong, sustained, non-competitive histamine (H1) receptor-blocking properties. It prevents the release of mediators like histamine, leukotrienes, and prostaglandins from cells involved in type-I allergic reactions. Additionally, it stabilizes mast cells, reduces chemotaxis, and inhibits the degranulation of eosinophils. Tofen also stabilizes mast cells and basophils, inhibits platelet-activating factor (PAF), and is effective in preventing asthmatic attacks.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 1 mg twice daily with food; the dose may be increased to 2 mg twice daily in severe cases.</li><li><strong>Children (above 2 years)</strong>: 1 mg twice daily with food.</li><li><strong>For patients prone to sedation</strong>: Start with 0.5 to 1 mg at night for the first few days, or as directed by a physician.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Same as the adult dose, or as advised by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral antidiabetics</strong>: Concomitant use with oral antidiabetics may result in a reversible fall in platelet count. This combination is advised to be avoided.</li><li><strong>CNS depressants</strong>: Tofen may potentiate the effects of CNS depressants like alcohol, antihistamines, hypnotics, and sedatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Combination with oral antidiabetics</strong>: Tofen should not be used with oral antidiabetics due to the risk of a reversible fall in platelet count.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Drowsiness, dry mouth, and slight dizziness may occur initially, but they typically disappear after a few days.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Although no teratogenic effects have been observed, Ketotifen is not recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Continue prior asthma treatment (e.g., systemic corticosteroids or ACTH) for at least two weeks after starting Tofen to avoid exacerbation of asthma.</li><li>If an intercurrent infection occurs, specific antimicrobial therapy should accompany Tofen treatment.</li><li>During the first day of treatment, the patient should avoid operating machinery or driving until the effects are known.</li><li>Avoid alcohol consumption, as Ketotifen can enhance the sedative effects of alcohol, antihistamines, and other CNS depressants.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Drowsiness, confusion, dyspnea, bradycardia or tachycardia, disorientation, convulsions, severe hypotension, reversible coma.</li><li><strong>Management</strong>: Supportive and symptomatic treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p>This version avoids any reference to copyrighted materials, maintaining a factual, clear description of Tofen 1 mg\'s usage and properties.</p>', 'active', '247_1734376132.jpg', 3, 12, NULL, '2024-12-17 00:08:52', '2024-12-17 00:08:52'),
(282, 'Ticarel', 'Ticagrelor', 75.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '90 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ticarel 90 mg</strong></p><p>Ticarel 90 mg is indicated for preventing atherothrombotic events in adult patients with Acute Coronary Syndromes (ACS), including unstable angina, non-ST elevation Myocardial Infarction (NSTEMI), or ST elevation Myocardial Infarction (STEMI). This includes patients managed medically or with percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) or coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG).</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Anti-platelet drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Ticarel 90 mg is a selective P2Y12 ADP-receptor antagonist that prevents ADP-mediated platelet activation and aggregation. It interacts reversibly with the platelet P2Y12 ADP-receptor, preventing signal transduction that leads to platelet activation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Loading Dose</strong>: Initiate with a single 180 mg dose (two 90 mg tablets).</li><li><strong>Maintenance Dose</strong>: Continue with 90 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Aspirin</strong>: Administer with a maintenance dose of 75-100 mg of aspirin daily (avoid doses above 100 mg).</li><li><strong>Missed Dose</strong>: Take one tablet at the scheduled time if a dose is missed.</li><li><strong>Switching</strong>: Patients can switch from Clopidogrel to Ticarel 90 mg directly. The switch from prasugrel has not been studied.</li><li><strong>Duration</strong>: Treatment is recommended for up to 12 months unless clinically indicated to discontinue.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CYP3A Inhibitors</strong>: Avoid strong inhibitors like ketoconazole, clarithromycin, and ritonavir.</li><li><strong>CYP3A Inducers</strong>: Avoid strong inducers such as rifampin and phenytoin.</li><li><strong>Aspirin</strong>: Do not exceed 100 mg of aspirin, as higher doses reduce Ticarel 90 mg\'s effectiveness.</li><li><strong>Simvastatin/Lovastatin</strong>: Avoid doses &gt;40 mg due to increased serum concentrations.</li><li><strong>Digoxin</strong>: Monitor levels when initiating or adjusting Ticarel 90 mg treatment.</li><li><strong>Other Medications</strong>: Ticarel 90 mg can be used with heparin, GPIIb/IIIa inhibitors, proton pump inhibitors, beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors, and ARBs.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Ticarel or excipients</li><li>Active pathological bleeding (e.g., peptic ulcer)</li><li>History of intracranial hemorrhage</li><li>Moderate to severe hepatic impairment</li><li>Co-administration with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Dyspnea, bleeding, headache, cough, dizziness, nausea, atrial fibrillation, chest pain, hypotension, fatigue, and back pain.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C. Use only if necessary.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: Not recommended due to potential risks to the infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Risk of Bleeding</strong>: Drugs inhibiting platelet function increase bleeding risk.</li><li><strong>Aspirin</strong>: Maintain aspirin doses of 75-100 mg for optimal efficacy.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Use cautiously in patients with impaired liver function.</li><li><strong>Discontinuation</strong>: Stopping treatment increases the risk of myocardial infarction, stent thrombosis, and death.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>No known antidote. Expected effects include prolonged bleeding. Manage with supportive care.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 25°C, protect from light and moisture, and keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric</strong>: Safety and effectiveness have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric</strong>: No significant differences in safety or effectiveness.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Not studied in moderate to severe impairment.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1270_1734376382.webp', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-17 00:13:02', '2024-12-17 00:13:02'),
(283, 'Empa', 'Empagliflozin', 50.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>As an adjunct to diet and exercise for improving glycemic control in adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus.</li><li>To reduce the risk of cardiovascular death in adult patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus and established cardiovascular disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Sodium-glucose Cotransporter-2 (SGLT2) Inhibitors</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Empa 25 mg works as an SGLT2 inhibitor. SGLT2 transporters reabsorb glucose from the kidney\'s glomerular filtrate. By inhibiting this transporter, Empa 25 mg reduces glucose reabsorption, lowering the renal threshold for glucose, leading to increased glucose excretion. This results in better control of blood sugar, weight loss, and reduced blood pressure.</p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial Dose:</strong> 10 mg once daily, preferably in the morning, with or without food.</li><li><strong>Increase to 25 mg:</strong> In patients tolerating the 10 mg dose, it may be increased to 25 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Volume Depletion:</strong> Correct volume depletion before initiating Empa 25 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> Co-administration with diuretics increases urine volume.</li><li><strong>Insulin or Insulin Secretagogues:</strong> May increase the risk of hypoglycemia.</li><li><strong>Urine Glucose Tests:</strong> Not recommended, as Empa 25 mg increases urinary glucose excretion.</li><li><strong>1,5-AG Assay:</strong> Not recommended, as the assay is unreliable with SGLT2 inhibitors.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Empa 25 mg or its ingredients.</li><li>Severe renal impairment, end-stage renal disease, or patients on dialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Urinary tract infections, female genital mycotic infections, dehydration, hypotension, dizziness, weakness, and increased thirst.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> No well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Should only be used if the potential benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Unknown if excreted in human milk; not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Assess renal function before starting Empa 25 mg and periodically after.</li><li><strong>eGFR &lt; 45 ml/min/1.73 m²:</strong> Do not initiate treatment.</li><li>No dose adjustment is needed for patients with eGFR ≥ 45 ml/min/1.73 m².</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose:</strong></p><ul><li>Employ usual supportive measures. Removal by hemodialysis has not been studied.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><p>Sodium-glucose Cotransporter-2 (SGLT2) Inhibitors.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Empa 25 mg inhibits the SGLT2 co-transporter, promoting glucose excretion through the urine, reducing blood glucose levels, assisting weight loss, and lowering blood pressure.</p>', 'active', '1789_1734376557.png', 11, 12, NULL, '2024-12-17 00:15:57', '2024-12-17 00:15:57'),
(284, 'Angilock Plus', 'Losartan Potassium + Hydrochlorothiazide', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg+12.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Angilock Plus 50 mg + 12.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment of hypertension</li><li>Reducing the risk of stroke in patients with hypertension and left ventricular hypertrophy</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Combined antihypertensive preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Losartan</strong>: An angiotensin II receptor antagonist that blocks vasoconstricting and aldosterone-secreting effects of angiotensin II by binding selectively to AT1 receptors.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: A thiazide diuretic that inhibits sodium reabsorption in the distal tubules, leading to increased excretion of sodium, water, potassium, and hydrogen ions.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: Start with 50 mg/12.5 mg once daily. Titrate up to 100 mg/25 mg once daily as necessary.</li><li><strong>Hypertensive Patients with Left Ventricular Hypertrophy</strong>: Initiate with 50 mg/12.5 mg, and increase to 100 mg/25 mg if required.</li><li><strong>Severe Hypertension</strong>: Begin with 50 mg/12.5 mg once daily and increase to 100 mg/25 mg if needed.</li><li><strong>Elderly patients</strong>: No initial dosage adjustment required. The maximum dose should be avoided as initial therapy in elderly patients.</li><li>Can be taken with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Losartan</strong>: No significant drug-drug interactions with Hydrochlorothiazide, Digoxin, Warfarin, Cimetidine, or Phenobarbital.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: May interact with alcohol, barbiturates, narcotics (increased orthostatic hypotension), antidiabetic drugs (may require dose adjustment), other antihypertensives (additive effect), and cholestyramine or colestipol resins (impaired absorption).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component.</li><li>Contraindicated in patients with anuria or hypersensitivity to sulfonamide-derived drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Dizziness, hypotension, hyperkalemia, angioedema, vertigo, gastrointestinal disturbances, headache, sleep disorders, malaise, urticaria, pruritus, rash.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Hepatitis, atrial fibrillation, cerebrovascular accident, syncope, pancreatitis, anaphylaxis, erectile dysfunction, anemia, thrombocytopenia, renal impairment, rhabdomyolysis, tinnitus, photosensitivity, vasculitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated during pregnancy.</li><li>Not recommended for breastfeeding, as the components may pass into human milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Regular monitoring of serum electrolytes, particularly potassium.</li><li>Monitor for possible electrolyte imbalances, especially hypokalemia.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with renal impairment or a history of symptomatic hypotension.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Losartan</strong>: Symptoms may include hypotension and tachycardia; treatment is symptomatic.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: Electrolyte imbalances such as hypokalemia, hypochloremia, and dehydration. Excessive diuresis may cause arrhythmias if combined with digitalis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric patients</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: Can be used if creatinine clearance &gt;30 mL/min. Not recommended in severe renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: Not recommended for titration due to inability to use the 25 mg starting dose of Losartan.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Combined antihypertensive preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Losartan</strong>: Selectively blocks the binding of angiotensin II to the AT1 receptor, inhibiting vasoconstriction and aldosterone secretion.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: Increases excretion of sodium and chloride by blocking their reabsorption in the renal tubules, leading to reduced plasma volume and increased renin and aldosterone secretion.</li></ul><p><br></p><p><br></p>', 'active', '1740_1734376730.png', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-17 00:18:50', '2024-12-17 00:18:50'),
(285, 'Zolium', 'Alprazolam', 3.40, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Anxiety disorder</li><li>Short-term relief of anxiety</li><li>Anxiety associated with depression</li><li>Panic disorder, with or without agoraphobia</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Benzodiazepine sedatives</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Zolium 0.5 mg binds with high affinity to the GABA-benzodiazepine receptor complex. Its therapeutic effects are mediated by this interaction, affecting both BNZ1 receptors (which mediate sleep) and BNZ2 receptors (which influence muscle relaxation, anticonvulsant activity, motor coordination, and memory). The drug enhances the effect of GABA by increasing its affinity for the GABA receptor, leading to hyperpolarization of the cell membrane and inhibiting further excitation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: Initial dose of 0.25 mg, taken 2-3 times daily. The dose may be increased by 0.25 mg increments based on response. Evening doses should be adjusted first. In severe anxiety cases, higher doses may be necessary, up to 3 mg daily in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Elderly and debilitated patients</strong>: Start with 0.125 mg 2-3 times daily, with gradual increases as tolerated.</li><li><strong>Panic Disorder</strong>: Start with 0.25-0.5 mg three times daily, with dose adjustments in increments of 1 mg/day. Maximum dose may reach up to 10 mg daily, depending on patient response.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction:</strong></p><ul><li>CNS-depressant effects may be enhanced by the use of other psychotropic drugs, anticonvulsants, antihistamines, alcohol, and oral contraceptives.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Zolium or other benzodiazepines</li><li>Myasthenia gravis</li><li>Acute narrow-angle glaucoma</li><li>Pregnancy</li><li>Infants</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Drowsiness, light-headedness</li><li>Others: Depression, headache, confusion, dry mouth, constipation</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Not recommended due to potential risk of congenital malformations in the first trimester.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Not recommended as Zolium and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Risk of psychological and physical dependence. Dose increments or abrupt discontinuation should only be done under medical supervision.</li><li>Caution in patients with liver/renal disease, chronic pulmonary insufficiency, or sleep apnea.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Somnolence, confusion, impaired coordination, diminished reflexes, and coma.</li><li>Treatment: Supportive care, including gastric lavage.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and efficacy in patients under 18 years have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Benzodiazepine sedatives</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Zolium 0.5 mg is a triazole analog of the 1,4-benzodiazepine class. It has anxiolytic, hypnotic, and anticonvulsive properties. The drug exerts its effects via interaction with the GABA-benzodiazepine receptor complex, leading to dose-dependent CNS depression.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1502_1734377979.webp', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-17 00:39:39', '2024-12-17 00:39:39'),
(286, 'Telmipres', 'Telmisartan', 9.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Telmipres 40 mg:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: Treatment of essential hypertension in adults.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Prevention</strong>: Reduction of cardiovascular morbidity in adults with:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Atherothrombotic cardiovascular disease (history of coronary heart disease, stroke, or peripheral arterial disease).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Type 2 diabetes mellitus with documented target organ damage.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>: Angiotensin-II receptor blocker (ARB).</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><p>Telmipres 40 mg works by blocking the action of angiotensin II at the AT1 receptor, which is found in various tissues like vascular smooth muscle and the adrenal gland. This blockade prevents vasoconstriction, aldosterone secretion, and sodium reabsorption, leading to blood pressure reduction. It does not affect the synthesis of angiotensin II, unlike ACE inhibitors, and does not interfere with bradykinin.</p><p><strong>Dosage</strong>:</p><p>The starting dose is 40 mg once daily. Blood pressure reduction is dose-related and noticeable within 2 weeks, with maximal effect after 4 weeks. The dose can range from 20 to 80 mg. No adjustment is needed for elderly patients or those with renal impairment. Patients on dialysis should be monitored for orthostatic hypotension. Telmipres can be taken with or without food.</p><p><strong>Administration</strong>:</p><p>May be combined with other antihypertensive agents.</p><p><strong>Interactions</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>NSAIDs</strong>: Increased risk of renal impairment and reduced antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>Aliskiren</strong>: Co-administration with Telmipres is contraindicated in diabetic patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Telmipres or its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li>For hypertensive patients: Common side effects include sinusitis, back pain, and diarrhea.</li><li>For cardiovascular risk reduction: Common side effects include intermittent claudication and skin ulcers.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li>Category C (first trimester). Should be discontinued upon pregnancy detection due to potential fetal and neonatal risks (e.g., hypotension, renal failure).</li><li>Caution with breastfeeding as it may pose serious risks to the infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong>:</p><ul><li>Avoid in pregnancy and fetal/neonatal exposure.</li><li>Caution in patients with hepatic or renal impairment.</li><li>Do not use with an ACE inhibitor or another ARB.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose</strong>:</p><p>Symptoms include hypotension, dizziness, tachycardia, bradycardia, renal failure, and elevated serum creatinine levels.</p><p><strong>Storage</strong>:</p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and humidity, and out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Severe cases or on hemodialysis should start with 20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Maximum of 40 mg daily for mild to moderate impairment. Contraindicated in severe hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</p><p>Telmipres blocks the AT1 receptor to counteract the effects of angiotensin II, reducing blood pressure without affecting the synthesis of angiotensin II or the bradykinin pathway.</p>', 'active', '1690_1734378708.jpg', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-17 00:51:48', '2024-12-17 00:51:48'),
(287, 'Lexotanil', 'Bromazepam', 9.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '3 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Emotional disturbances</strong>: Acute tension, anxiety states, interpersonal difficulties, agitation, insomnia, and anxious depressive reactions.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular and respiratory disturbances</strong>: Pseudoangina, pericardial anxiety, tachycardia, hypertension, dyspnea, and hyperventilation.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal disturbances</strong>: Irritable bowel syndrome, epigastric pain, spasm, bloating, and diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract disturbances</strong>: Frequency, irritable bladder, and dysmenorrhea.</li><li><strong>Psychosomatic disorders</strong>: Psychogenic headache, asthma, and ulcers.</li><li><strong>Chronic organic disease-related emotional reactions</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Benzodiazepine sedatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Lexotanil 3 mg is a psychotropic agent. In low doses, it reduces tension and anxiety. In higher doses, it has sedative and muscle-relaxant properties.</li><li>It works by binding to the GABA-A receptor, enhancing its inhibitory effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Standard dosage</strong>: 1.5-3 mg up to three times daily for outpatients. In severe cases or hospital settings, 6-12 mg 2–3 times daily.</li><li><strong>Elderly and debilitated patients</strong>: Require lower doses.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Typically not indicated but can be adjusted based on body weight (0.1–0.3 mg/kg).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><ul><li>For oral use.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li>The sedative effects are enhanced when combined with other centrally active drugs (e.g., antidepressants, hypnotics, narcotics, sedatives, antiepileptic drugs, antihistamines, anesthetics).</li><li>Alcohol should be avoided, as it may intensify sedative effects.</li><li>Co-administration with cimetidine may prolong the elimination half-life.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Lexotanil, severe respiratory or hepatic insufficiency, and sleep apnea syndrome.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common effects include fatigue, drowsiness, muscle weakness, reduced alertness, confusion, headache, and ataxia. These effects usually disappear with prolonged use.</li><li><strong>Anterograde amnesia</strong> may occur at therapeutic doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Safety not established.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Should be avoided as Lexotanil is excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Risk of physical and psychological dependence with prolonged use, especially at high doses or in predisposed individuals.</li><li>Withdrawal symptoms can occur if treatment is discontinued abruptly; it should be tapered gradually.</li><li>Not recommended for primary treatment of insomnia caused by psychotic illness.</li><li>Caution when driving or using machinery.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Reduces tension and anxiety at low doses; sedative and muscle-relaxant at higher doses. It works by enhancing the inhibitory effects of GABA-A receptors.</li></ul><p>This summary offers a general understanding of Lexotanil 3 mg\'s therapeutic use, dosage, side effects, and precautions.</p>', 'active', '953_1734378944.png', 10, 12, NULL, '2024-12-17 00:55:44', '2024-12-17 11:26:23'),
(288, 'Algin', 'Tiemonium Methylsulphate', 8.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Algin 50 mg is an antispasmodic medication designed to alleviate muscle spasms in the intestines, biliary system, bladder, and uterus. It is commonly used to manage pain associated with functional disorders of the digestive tract and biliary system. Additionally, it provides relief from spasms and pain related to urological and gynecological conditions.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Anticholinergics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The active ingredient in Algin 50 mg acts as a competitive antagonist to acetylcholine and histamine. It also enhances calcium binding with membrane phospholipids and proteins, thereby preventing the contraction of visceral smooth muscle cells. This mechanism effectively reduces spasms and alleviates pain.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablets/Syrup:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: Typically, 2–6 tablets or 3–9 teaspoons of syrup daily, divided into multiple doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children: 3 ml per kg or 6 mg per kg of body weight daily, administered in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> Administer 50 mg intravenously (slowly) or intramuscularly three times daily.</li><li><strong>Suppository:</strong> Use 20 mg rectally, two to three times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><p>It is advised not to combine Tiemonium methylsulphate (Algin 50 mg) with other medications without consulting a healthcare provider to avoid potential interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Algin 50 mg should be avoided in cases of:</p><ul><li>Urethroprostatic disorders that may lead to urinary retention.</li><li>Angle-closure glaucoma risk.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Possible side effects include hypotension and tachycardia in some individuals.</p><p><strong>Use During Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Animal studies have not shown teratogenic effects with Algin 50 mg. To date, no deformities have been reported when used appropriately. However, due to limited human data, caution is advised during pregnancy and breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Exercise caution in patients with prostate disorders, chronic bronchitis, coronary insufficiency, renal or hepatic impairment, and those exposed to high environmental temperatures.</li><li>Potential visual disturbances may impair the ability to drive or operate machinery.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose</strong></p><p>Currently, there is no specific data on overdoses related to Tiemonium methylsulphate.</p><p><strong>Storage Instructions</strong></p><p>Store in a dry place, protected from light and heat. Keep out of children’s reach.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> The safety and efficacy of Algin 50 mg in children have not been firmly established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Studies suggest the medication remains effective and safe in older adults.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Anticholinergics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Algin 50 mg works as a competitive antagonist to acetylcholine and histamine while strengthening calcium bonds with cell membranes, thus preventing the contraction of smooth muscle and alleviating spasms and pain.</p><p>Let me know if you\'d like further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '1631_1734460586.jpg', 6, 12, NULL, '2024-12-17 23:36:26', '2024-12-17 23:36:26'),
(289, 'EmpaMet', 'Empagliflozin + Metformin Hydrochloride', 20.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For patients insufficiently controlled with Metformin alone</strong>: When the maximum tolerated dose of Metformin fails to achieve adequate glycemic control.</li><li><strong>In combination with other diabetes treatments</strong>: For those insufficiently controlled on Metformin and other antidiabetic agents.</li><li><strong>In patients already treated with Empagliflozin and Metformin as separate tablets</strong>: This combination simplifies the treatment regimen.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Empagliflozin</strong>: A Sodium-Glucose Co-Transporter 2 (SGLT2) inhibitor, it reduces renal glucose reabsorption and enhances urinary glucose excretion, helping to lower blood glucose levels.</li><li><strong>Metformin Hydrochloride</strong>: A biguanide that lowers glucose levels by decreasing hepatic glucose production, improving insulin sensitivity, and enhancing glucose uptake by peripheral tissues without causing hypoglycemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Standard Dosage</strong>: Taken twice daily with meals. Dose adjustments should be made gradually to minimize gastrointestinal side effects from Metformin.</li><li><strong>Maximum Doses</strong>: Metformin - 2000 mg/day; Empagliflozin - 25 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Special Populations</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Avoid in patients with an eGFR &lt; 45 mL/min/1.73 m².</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Volume Depletion</strong>: Correct fluid imbalances before initiation.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Not recommended for patients under 18 years due to lack of safety data.</li></ul><p><strong>Key Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With Diuretics</strong>: May increase risk of volume depletion.</li><li><strong>With Insulin/Secretagogues</strong>: Enhances risk of hypoglycemia.</li><li><strong>Alcohol</strong>: Potentiates risk of lactic acidosis when combined with Metformin.</li><li><strong>Other Drugs</strong>: Some medications (e.g., ranolazine, dolutegravir) reduce Metformin clearance, increasing its risk of accumulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Severe renal impairment (GFR &lt; 30 mL/min).</li><li>Acute metabolic acidosis, including lactic acidosis or diabetic ketoacidosis.</li><li>Conditions causing tissue hypoxia or altering renal function (e.g., heart failure, dehydration).</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any components of the medication.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Genital infections (vaginal or balanitis), urinary tract infections, gastrointestinal disturbances, headache.</li><li><strong>Rare but Serious</strong>: Diabetic ketoacidosis, lactic acidosis, Fournier\'s gangrene.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor renal function, signs of ketoacidosis, and vitamin B12 levels.</li><li>Adjust insulin doses to prevent hypoglycemia when used in combination.</li><li>Evaluate risks in pregnant and breastfeeding women.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from moisture and light. Keep out of children’s reach.</p><p>This medication should be used under a healthcare provider’s supervision, with regular monitoring of glycemic control and renal function to ensure safety and effectiveness.</p>', 'active', '1464_1734460888.png', 11, 12, NULL, '2024-12-17 23:41:28', '2024-12-17 23:41:28'),
(290, 'Hypophos', 'Calcium Acetate', 6.00, 15, 'Tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '667 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Hypophos 667 mg</strong></p><p>Hypophos 667 mg is prescribed to manage hyperphosphatemia in patients with end-stage renal disease (ESRD). It effectively controls phosphate levels without promoting aluminum absorption.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Drugs for serum phosphorus reduction in ESRD patients</strong></li><li><strong>Minerals involved in bone formation</strong></li><li><strong>Specific mineral preparations</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>When taken with meals, Hypophos 667 mg binds dietary phosphate in the gut to form insoluble calcium phosphate, which is eliminated in the feces. Maintaining serum phosphorus levels below <strong>6.0 mg/dL</strong> is considered a desirable clinical outcome. The medication is highly soluble at neutral pH, facilitating effective calcium-phosphate binding in the proximal small intestine. Studies show systemic absorption of up to <strong>40%</strong> under fasting conditions and approximately <strong>30%</strong> in non-fasting states, based on data from healthy individuals and dialysis patients.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>Initial daily dose: <strong>3-4 grams</strong>.</li><li>Usual range: <strong>6-12 grams daily</strong>.</li><li>For hyperphosphatemia management in chronic renal failure: Start with <strong>1.334 g (338 mg calcium)</strong>. Dosage can be adjusted gradually based on serum phosphate levels, ensuring hypercalcemia does not occur.</li></ul><p>Dialysis Patients:</p><ul><li>Initial dose: <strong>2 tablets</strong> with each meal. Gradually increase to maintain serum phosphate below <strong>6 mg/dL</strong>, ensuring hypercalcemia does not develop. Most patients require <strong>3-4 tablets per meal</strong>.</li></ul><p>Pediatric Use:</p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy not established.</li></ul><p>Geriatric Use:</p><ul><li>Clinical studies show no significant differences in response between older and younger adults. However, increased sensitivity in some older individuals is possible.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>May reduce the absorption of <strong>tetracycline</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with <strong>hypercalcemia</strong> should not use Hypophos 667 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Nausea.</li><li><strong>Hypercalcemia:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mild hypercalcemia (Ca &gt;10.5 mg/dL): Symptoms may include constipation, anorexia, nausea, or vomiting.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe hypercalcemia (Ca &gt;12 mg/dL): Associated with confusion, delirium, stupor, or coma.</li></ul><p>Management:</p><ul><li>Reduce the dose or discontinue therapy for mild cases.</li><li>Severe cases may require <strong>acute hemodialysis</strong> and therapy discontinuation.</li></ul><p>Other rare side effects:</p><ul><li>Pruritus (possible allergic reactions).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Insufficient data on risks during pregnancy or breastfeeding. Use only if clearly necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor <strong>serum calcium</strong> twice weekly during the initial dose adjustment period.</li><li>Avoid in patients on <strong>digitalis</strong>, as hypercalcemia can precipitate cardiac arrhythmias.</li><li>Adhere strictly to dosing, dietary instructions, and avoid non-prescription antacids.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>Excessive doses can lead to <strong>severe hypercalcemia</strong>, requiring immediate medical intervention.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><ul><li>Store between <strong>15°C and 30°C</strong>, in a dry, light-protected area.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Hypophos 667 mg binds dietary phosphate, forming insoluble calcium phosphate that is excreted. It ensures optimal serum phosphorus levels by being highly soluble and effectively binding phosphate in the proximal small intestine.</p>', 'active', '16_1734461161.webp', 17, 12, NULL, '2024-12-17 23:46:01', '2024-12-17 23:46:01');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(291, 'Myolax', 'Tolperisone Hydrochloride', 9.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Myolax 50 mg</strong></p><p>Myolax 50 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ol><li><strong>Neurological Disorders</strong></li></ol><ul><li>Increased skeletal muscle tone due to organic neurological conditions such as:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Injury of the pyramidal tract</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Multiple sclerosis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Myelopathy</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Encephalomyelitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Treatment of muscular spasm, contractures, rigidity, and spinal automatism.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Vascular Diseases</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Obliterative arteriosclerosis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diabetic angiopathy</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Obliterative thromboangiitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Raynaud’s disease</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diffuse scleroderma</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Post-thrombotic venous and lymphatic circulation disorders</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Crural ulcers (in specific cases)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Central depolarizing muscle relaxants</li><li>Centrally acting skeletal muscle relaxants</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Tolperisone, the active ingredient in Myolax 50 mg, is a centrally acting muscle relaxant. It works on the reticular formation in the brainstem, where it blocks voltage-gated sodium and calcium channels, reducing muscle tone and spasticity.</p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">150–450 mg daily in 2 or 3 divided doses (maximum: 450 mg/day).</li><li><strong>Children:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>3 months to 6 years:</strong> 1–2 mg/kg/day in 3 divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>6 to 14 years:</strong> 2–4 mg/kg/day in 3 divided doses.</li><li>Dosages should be adjusted according to individual needs and tolerance. Always follow the physician’s recommendations.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Co-administration with methocarbamol has been reported to cause disturbances in visual accommodation.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the product</li><li>Myasthenia gravis</li><li>Breastfeeding</li></ul><p><strong>Possible Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Muscle weakness, headache, nausea, vomiting</li><li>Rare: Hypersensitivity reactions such as itching, skin redness, or rash</li></ul><p><strong>Use During Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Use during pregnancy should only be under the advice of a physician.</li><li>Not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions and Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In children, strictly adhere to the prescribed dosage and duration.</li><li>Monitor for potential hypersensitivity reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>High doses in children may cause excitability.</li><li>In rare cases, severe overdose has been linked to fatal outcomes.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Instructions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class and Mechanism of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Myolax 50 mg belongs to central depolarizing muscle relaxants and centrally acting skeletal muscle relaxants.</li><li>It reduces elevated muscle tone and tension by acting on the central nervous system and can also improve certain circulation issues in the extremities.</li></ul><p>This overview ensures clarity while maintaining an accurate description of the medication.</p>', 'active', '1461_1734461870.webp', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-17 23:57:50', '2024-12-17 23:57:50'),
(292, 'Cardizem-SR', 'Diltiazem Hydrochloride', 6.05, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '90 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p><p><strong>Indications for Cardizem-SR 90 mg</strong></p><p>Cardizem-SR 90 mg is a calcium channel blocker primarily used for:</p><ol><li><strong>Prophylaxis and treatment of angina pectoris</strong>: Effective for both chronic stable (classical) and vasospastic angina.</li><li><strong>Hypertension management</strong>: Can be used alone or with other medications.</li><li><strong>Other conditions</strong>: Beneficial for myocardial infarction, coronary artery spasm, arrhythmias, Raynaud\'s phenomenon, esophageal motility disorders, and migraine prevention.</li></ol><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Calcium-channel blockers</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Diltiazem hydrochloride, the active ingredient in Cardizem-SR, acts as a calcium channel antagonist. It promotes:</p><ul><li><strong>Antianginal effects</strong>: Dilates coronary arteries to improve oxygen supply and reduces cardiac afterload to lower myocardial oxygen demand.</li><li><strong>Antiarrhythmic effects</strong>: Slows conduction at the sinoatrial (S-A) and atrioventricular (A-V) nodes by inhibiting calcium influx.</li><li><strong>Antihypertensive effects</strong>: Decreases peripheral vascular resistance through vasodilation.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><ol><li><strong>For Hypertension</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Starting dose: 120-240 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum: 540 mg once daily.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>For Angina</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initial dose: 120-180 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Can be titrated to 540 mg once daily over 7-14 days.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Film-coated formulation</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual dose: 60 mg thrice daily, adjustable up to 180-300 mg/day.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Sustained-release formulation</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mild to moderate hypertension: 90-120 mg twice daily, adjustable up to 360 mg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Angina: 90-120 mg twice daily, up to 240 mg/day for elderly patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Do not use Cardizem-SR in patients with:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to diltiazem.</li><li>Severe hypotension, sick sinus syndrome, or second/third-degree A-V block.</li><li>Acute myocardial infarction with pulmonary congestion.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Bradycardia, sinoatrial or atrioventricular block, headache, dizziness, and flushing.</li><li>Gastrointestinal disturbances, peripheral edema, and fatigue.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly or patients with hepatic/renal impairment</strong>: Start with 60 mg twice daily; monitor heart rate.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Use during pregnancy only if the potential benefit outweighs the risk to the fetus.</li><li>Avoid breastfeeding, as diltiazem is secreted in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><p>Caution when combined with:</p><ul><li>Other drugs affecting cardiac conduction or contractility.</li><li>Nitrates, which may be used safely alongside diltiazem.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Management</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdose include hypotension, bradycardia, heart block, and shock. Management involves:</p><ul><li>Symptomatic and supportive care.</li><li>Gastric lavage and activated charcoal administration.</li><li>Fluid replacement, vasopressors for hypotension, and atropine for bradycardia.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place away from children.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Diltiazem inhibits calcium influx into cardiac and vascular smooth muscle cells, leading to reduced cardiac workload and vasodilation. Peak plasma levels occur within 30-60 minutes, with high protein binding and hepatic metabolism.</p>', 'active', '1544_1734462231.jpg', 9, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 00:03:51', '2024-12-18 00:03:51'),
(293, 'Viglimet', 'Vildagliptin + Metformin Hydrochloride', 20.00, 6, 'Tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg+500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Viglimet 50 mg+500 mg</strong></p><p>Viglimet 50 mg+500 mg is prescribed to improve blood sugar control in individuals with type 2 diabetes who cannot achieve adequate control with Metformin Hydrochloride or Vildagliptin alone or for those already using the combination of these two medications as separate tablets. It should be used alongside proper diet and exercise.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Oral hypoglycemic combination preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Vildagliptin works by inhibiting the enzyme DPP-4 (Dipeptidyl Peptidase-4), which increases levels of incretin hormones such as GLP-1 and GIP. These hormones enhance insulin secretion from pancreatic beta cells and suppress glucagon release from alpha cells, leading to improved blood sugar control, particularly by reducing fasting and postprandial glucose levels.</p><p>Metformin Hydrochloride, a biguanide class drug, reduces glucose production in the liver, decreases intestinal glucose absorption, and enhances insulin sensitivity in peripheral tissues without causing hypoglycemia.</p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><p>For adults, the dosage depends on the current Metformin dose. Typically, Viglimet 50 mg+500 mg is taken twice daily, with one tablet in the morning and the other in the evening. It is recommended to take it with or after meals to reduce gastrointestinal side effects associated with Metformin. A dose higher than 100 mg of Vildagliptin daily is not recommended.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>No significant interaction between Vildagliptin and Metformin Hydrochloride.</li><li>Vildagliptin is unlikely to interact with medications metabolized by the CYP450 enzyme system.</li><li>Certain drugs, such as furosemide, nifedipine, and glyburide, can increase the absorption of Metformin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known allergy to Vildagliptin, Metformin Hydrochloride, or their components.</li><li>Renal dysfunction or failure.</li><li>Acute or chronic metabolic acidosis, including diabetic ketoacidosis.</li><li>Severe cardiac conditions like congestive heart failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache, tremors, dizziness, and nausea.</li><li>Potential hypoglycemia in some cases.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></p><p>Not recommended during pregnancy unless clearly necessary, as adequate studies are lacking. It is unclear if the components pass into breast milk; hence, use during breastfeeding is not advised.</p><p><strong>Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Not recommended for patients under 18 years of age.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Should be used cautiously with regular monitoring of renal function.</li><li><strong>Renal or Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Not suitable for patients with significant renal or liver impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Warnings and Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Lactic acidosis is a rare but serious complication due to Metformin accumulation. Regular renal function monitoring is essential, particularly in elderly patients or those on medications affecting kidney function.</li><li>Liver function tests (LFTs) should be monitored before starting treatment and periodically thereafter.</li><li>Discontinue treatment 48 hours before surgery or procedures requiring iodinated contrast media and resume only after ensuring normal renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Instructions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place away from heat and light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p>This summary is intended for informational purposes and should be tailored based on individual patient needs and health provider recommendations.</p>', 'active', '1234_1734464359.webp', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 00:39:19', '2024-12-18 00:39:19'),
(294, 'Secrin', 'Glimepiride', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Secrin 2 mg: Indications and Information</strong></p><p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Secrin 2 mg is indicated as an adjunct to diet and exercise for lowering blood glucose in patients with non-insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus (Type II). It is used in cases where hyperglycemia cannot be managed with diet and exercise alone. It may be combined with:</p><ul><li><strong>Metformin</strong>: When monotherapy with Secrin 2 mg or metformin is inadequate for glycemic control.</li><li><strong>Insulin</strong>: To lower blood glucose in patients where diet, exercise, and an oral hypoglycemic agent alone are insufficient. Note: Combined use may increase the risk of hypoglycemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Sulfonylureas</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Secrin 2 mg stimulates insulin release from pancreatic beta cells and reduces hepatic glucose production. It also enhances peripheral tissue sensitivity to insulin.</p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Dosage</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial Dose</strong>: Typically 1 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Titration</strong>: Gradual increase in dose (1-2 weeks between adjustments) to achieve desired blood sugar levels. Suggested progression: 1 mg → 2 mg → 3 mg → 4 mg → 6 mg.</li><li><strong>Maintenance Dose</strong>: Common range is 1-4 mg daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><ul><li>Take immediately before a substantial breakfast or the first main meal.</li><li>Do not skip meals after taking the medication.</li></ul><p><strong>Dose Adjustments</strong></p><ul><li>Adjust for changes in lifestyle, weight, or glucose control.</li><li>Switch from other oral antidiabetics with an initial dose of 1 mg, regardless of the prior dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Secrin 2 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus (Type I)</li><li>Diabetic ketoacidosis or coma</li><li>Severe hepatic or renal dysfunction</li><li>Hypersensitivity to sulfonylureas or related compounds</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Hypoglycemia, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, headache, dizziness.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Urticaria, visual impairment, hypotension.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Not recommended; switch to insulin.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Discontinue breastfeeding or switch therapy.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: Safety and efficacy not established.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Careful dose selection due to potential renal function decline.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Increased Hypoglycemia Risk</strong>: ACE inhibitors, salicylates, sulfonamides, and certain antifungals (e.g., fluconazole).</li><li><strong>Reduced Effectiveness</strong>: Diuretics, corticosteroids, thyroid hormones, and sympathomimetics.</li><li><strong>Mixed Effects</strong>: Beta-blockers and clonidine may mask hypoglycemia symptoms.</li></ul><p><strong>Warnings and Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Risk of hypoglycemia is higher during initial treatment weeks; monitor blood glucose closely.</li><li>Hypoglycemia can be treated with immediate carbohydrate intake. Severe cases may require hospitalization and IV glucose administration.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C.</li><li>Keep away from light and out of reach of children.</li></ul><p>This structured format ensures clarity while maintaining all essential details. Let me know if you\'d like to refine further!</p>', 'active', '1001_1734465099.png', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 00:51:39', '2024-12-18 00:51:39'),
(295, 'Angilock', 'Losartan Potassium', 5.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Angilock 25 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: Angilock 25 mg is indicated for the treatment of hypertension, either as monotherapy or in combination with other antihypertensive agents (e.g., thiazide diuretics).</li><li><strong>Renal Protection in Type-2 Diabetic Patients with Proteinuria</strong>: It is recommended for hypertensive type-2 diabetic patients with proteinuria (urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio &gt;300 mg/g) to slow the progression of renal disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Angiotensin-II Receptor Blocker (ARB)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Angilock 25 mg is the first orally active, non-peptide angiotensin II receptor blocker. It selectively binds to the AT1 receptor found in tissues such as the heart, kidneys, adrenal glands, and vascular smooth muscle. By blocking AT1 receptor activation, it prevents key processes like vasoconstriction and aldosterone secretion, both of which contribute to hypertension.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial and Maintenance Dose</strong>: 50 mg once daily for most patients.</li><li><strong>Adjustment</strong>: If 50 mg is insufficient, 25 mg twice daily may be considered.</li><li><strong>Special Populations</strong>: In patients with intravascular volume depletion (e.g., high-dose diuretics), a starting dose of 25 mg once daily is recommended.</li><li><strong>Dosage Range</strong>: The total daily dose can range from 25 mg to 100 mg, given as one or two divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Decreased Metabolite Levels</strong>: Rifampicin and fluconazole reduce Angilock 25 mg\'s active metabolite levels.</li><li><strong>Enhanced Effects</strong>: Combination with hydrochlorothiazide may potentiate antihypertensive effects.</li><li><strong>Potassium Risks</strong>: Concurrent use with potassium-sparing diuretics, potassium supplements, or potassium-containing salt substitutes may increase serum potassium levels.</li><li><strong>NSAIDs</strong>: Drugs like indomethacin may reduce Angilock\'s antihypertensive effects.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment Risk</strong>: Co-administration with ACE inhibitors, ARBs, anti-inflammatory drugs, or thiazide diuretics increases renal impairment risk.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of Angilock 25 mg’s components.</li><li>Pregnant women.</li><li>Patients with diabetes when combined with Aliskiren.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Dizziness</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Nasal congestion</li><li>Cough</li><li>Upper respiratory infections</li></ul><p>Less frequent effects may include fatigue, edema, chest pain, nausea, headache, and pharyngitis.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Angilock 25 mg is categorized as <em>Pregnancy Category D</em>. Use during the second or third trimester can harm fetal development by reducing renal function, leading to increased fetal/neonatal morbidity and mortality.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Since it is unclear whether Angilock is excreted in human milk, a decision should be made to either discontinue breastfeeding or the medication, considering its importance to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypotension</strong>: Symptomatic hypotension may occur in volume-depleted patients, such as those on high-dose diuretics.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Patients with cirrhosis may have elevated plasma concentrations.</li><li><strong>Renal Function</strong>: Changes in renal function, including renal failure, have been reported in patients with renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, protected from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Angiotensin-II Receptor Blocker</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Angilock 25 mg binds selectively to the AT1 receptor in various tissues, preventing angiotensin II-mediated vasoconstriction and aldosterone release, both of which contribute to elevated blood pressure.</p>', 'active', '1025_1734472393.png', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 02:53:13', '2024-12-18 02:53:13'),
(296, 'Pevisone', 'Econazole Nitrate + Triamcinolone Acetonide', 70.00, 1, 'Cream', '10 gm in a box', 'Cream', '1percent plus 0.1percents', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pevisone 1%+0.1% Cream</strong></p><p><strong>Econazole Nitrate &amp; Triamcinolone Acetonide</strong> is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Eczematous Mycoses</li><li>Psoriasis</li><li><strong>Tinea Pedis</strong> (Athlete’s foot)</li><li><strong>Tinea Corporis</strong> (Ringworm)</li><li><strong>Tinea Cruris</strong> (Jock itch)</li><li>Inflammatory Intertrigo</li><li>Diaper Dermatitis</li></ul><p>For <strong>Onychomycoses</strong>, local therapy with Econazole/Triamcinolone cream is recommended alongside an oral antifungal agent.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Triamcinolone and combined antifungal preparations.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Econazole Nitrate</strong>: Modifies fungal cell membrane permeability, potentially interfering with RNA and protein synthesis and lipid metabolism.</li><li><strong>Triamcinolone Acetonide</strong>: A glucocorticoid that suppresses the migration of immune cells and reduces capillary permeability, effectively alleviating inflammation.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: Apply a thin layer twice daily (morning and evening) to the affected area.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Treatment duration: Up to <strong>14 days</strong> or as directed by a physician.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Avoid application under occlusive dressings or to large skin areas.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Suitable for short-term use on small areas under medical supervision.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><p>Children may have increased susceptibility to systemic corticosteroid effects (e.g., HPA axis suppression and Cushing’s syndrome). Use cautiously on small areas for a short duration.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Econazole</strong>: Interacts with CYP3A4/2C9 substrates, such as warfarin.</li><li><strong>Triamcinolone</strong>: May interact with NSAIDs, antidiabetics, potassium-depleting diuretics, and drugs like carbamazepine and rifampicin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Do not use in patients with:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component</li><li>Specific skin infections: Tuberculosis, syphilis, herpes simplex, varicella, or fresh vaccination sites</li><li>Rosacea or decubitus ulcers</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Local: Mild irritation, redness, and itching</li><li>Systemic: Rare adrenal suppression with prolonged use, especially in children or under occlusive dressings.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Avoid prolonged use as topical corticosteroids may cross the placenta.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Small amounts of Triamcinolone may be excreted in breast milk. Avoid use or discontinue breastfeeding during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>For <strong>external use only</strong>; avoid contact with eyes and mucous membranes.</li><li>Prolonged use may result in:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adrenal suppression</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin thinning, striae, and purpura</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Increased risk of systemic absorption; use sparingly and for less than 2 weeks.</li><li>Monitor for <strong>visual disturbances</strong> (e.g., blurred vision) as corticosteroids may cause ocular issues.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>Accidental ingestion: Treat symptomatically. In case of eye exposure, rinse with clean water or saline and seek medical attention.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below <strong>30°C</strong> in a cool, dry place. Keep out of children’s reach.</p>', 'active', '1906_1734473905.webp', 45, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 03:18:25', '2024-12-18 03:18:25'),
(297, 'Empatab', 'Empagliflozin', 25.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Empatab 10 mg is used:</p><ol><li>As an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycemic control in adults with <strong>type 2 diabetes mellitus</strong>.</li><li>To reduce the risk of <strong>cardiovascular death</strong> in adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus and <strong>established cardiovascular disease</strong>.</li></ol><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Sodium-glucose Cotransporter-2 (SGLT2) Inhibitors</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Empatab 10 mg works by inhibiting <strong>SGLT2 co-transporters</strong>, which are responsible for reabsorbing glucose in the kidneys. This inhibition:</p><ul><li>Increases <strong>glucose excretion</strong> via urine.</li><li>Reduces <strong>hyperglycemia</strong>.</li><li>Aids in <strong>weight loss</strong> and helps lower <strong>blood pressure</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial Dose:</strong> 10 mg once daily, in the morning, with or without food.</li><li><strong>Titration:</strong> May increase to <strong>25 mg once daily</strong> if tolerated.</li><li>Patients with <strong>volume depletion</strong> should have their condition corrected prior to starting Empatab.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> Increases urine volume.</li><li><strong>Insulin or Insulin Secretagogues:</strong> Raises the risk of <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>.</li><li><strong>Urine Glucose Tests:</strong> Not recommended as SGLT2 inhibitors cause positive results.</li><li><strong>1,5-AG Assay:</strong> Not reliable for glycemic monitoring. Use alternative tests.</li></ol><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Do not use Empatab 10 mg in patients with:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to the drug or its components.</li><li>Severe <strong>renal impairment</strong> (eGFR &lt; 45 ml/min/1.73 m²), <strong>end-stage renal disease</strong>, or on dialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common adverse effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong> and female genital fungal infections.</li><li><strong>Dehydration</strong>, <strong>hypotension</strong>, dizziness, weakness, and increased thirst.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Should only be used during pregnancy if benefits outweigh potential risks.</li><li>Not recommended for breastfeeding as excretion into milk is unknown.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Assess renal function before initiating therapy and periodically afterward.</li><li>Not to be started in patients with <strong>eGFR &lt; 45 ml/min/1.73 m²</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose</strong></p><p>In case of overdose:</p><ul><li>Implement supportive care, including gastrointestinal decontamination and monitoring.</li><li>Hemodialysis for Empatab 10 mg has not been studied.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, in a cool, dry place protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p>This structured format avoids repetition while presenting the critical details concisely. Let me know if you need further refinements!</p>', 'active', '39_1734474058.png', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 03:20:58', '2024-12-18 03:20:58'),
(298, 'NovoRapid Penfill', 'Protamine Crystallised Insulin Aspart', 460.00, 5, 'penfill', '5 piece in a box', 'Penfill', '100 IU per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indication:</strong></p><p>NovoRapid Penfill 100 IU/ml is a rapid-acting insulin analog used to improve glycemic control in patients with diabetes mellitus.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each ml contains 100 IU (3.50 mg) of Insulin Aspart (rDNA origin).</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Rapid-acting insulin.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Insulin Aspart regulates glucose metabolism by binding to insulin receptors on muscle and fat cells. This action promotes glucose uptake into the cells while suppressing glucose output by the liver.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><h4>General Guidelines:</h4><ul><li>Insulin Aspart is faster in onset and shorter in duration than regular human insulin.</li><li>Administer immediately before meals, or in certain cases, shortly after a meal.</li><li>Dosage varies individually based on medical advice and patient needs.</li><li>Typically combined with long-acting insulin given once daily.</li></ul><h4>Typical Dosing:</h4><ul><li><strong>Adults and Children (≥2 years):</strong> Total insulin requirement: 0.5–1.0 IU/kg/day.</li><li><strong>Meal-Related Treatment:</strong> 50–70% of the total dose as Insulin Aspart, with the rest as long-acting insulin.</li><li>Adjust dosage during physical activity or dietary changes.</li></ul><h4>Routes of Administration:</h4><ol><li><strong>Subcutaneous Injection:</strong> Inject in the abdomen, thigh, upper arm, or buttocks within 5–10 minutes before meals.</li><li><strong>Continuous Subcutaneous Insulin Infusion (CSII):</strong> Delivered via an external insulin pump. Do not mix with other insulins in the pump.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Use:</strong> Administer under medical supervision for glycemic control, monitoring blood glucose and potassium levels closely.</li></ol><p><strong>Instructions for Use</strong></p><ol><li>Prepare the insulin pen per the manufacturer’s instructions.</li><li>Mix the insulin gently by turning the pen upside down 8–10 times until uniformly mixed.</li><li>Remove the needle cap, discharge air bubbles, and adjust the dose as prescribed.</li><li>Inject at the recommended site while ensuring sterility to avoid contamination.</li></ol><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>May Decrease Insulin Requirement:</strong></li><li>Oral antidiabetic drugs, ACE inhibitors, fibrates, salicylates, and sulfonamide antibiotics.</li><li><strong>May Increase Insulin Requirement:</strong></li><li>Thiazides, glucocorticoids, thyroid hormones, growth hormone, and beta-sympathomimetics.</li><li><strong>Variable Effects:</strong></li><li>Beta-blockers, alcohol, clonidine, and lithium salts may either potentiate or weaken insulin’s effect.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Hypoglycemia, allergic reactions, injection site reactions, pruritus, rash, lipodystrophy.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> NovoRapid can be used during pregnancy if clinically needed.</li><li>Safe for use during lactation; dosage adjustments may be required.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions and Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Regular blood glucose monitoring is essential.</li><li>Adjust insulin regimens under medical supervision.</li><li>Increased physical activity or changes in diet may require dose adjustments.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Management</strong></p><ul><li>Mild hypoglycemia: Treat with oral glucose or sugary products.</li><li>Severe hypoglycemia: Administer glucagon (0.5–1 mg) subcutaneously or intramuscularly. If unresponsive, administer intravenous glucose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at 2°C to 8°C (refrigerated).</li><li>Do not freeze; protect from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Rapid-acting insulin.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Facilitates glucose uptake into cells and inhibits hepatic glucose output, ensuring effective glycemic control.</p>', 'active', '1202_1734519352.webp', 42, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 15:55:52', '2024-12-18 15:55:52'),
(299, 'Abecab', 'Amlodipine + Olmesartan Medoxomil', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Abecab 5 mg + 20 mg</strong></p><p>Abecab 5 mg + 20 mg is prescribed for managing hypertension, either as a standalone treatment or in combination with other antihypertensive medications. It is also suitable as initial therapy for patients likely to require multiple medications to achieve optimal blood pressure control.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Classified under combined antihypertensive preparations.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Amlodipine</strong>: A dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker that reduces the influx of calcium ions into vascular smooth muscle and cardiac muscle. This action results in peripheral arterial vasodilation, decreased peripheral vascular resistance, and lower blood pressure.</li><li><strong>Olmesartan</strong>: An angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB) that selectively acts on the AT1 receptor subtype. By inhibiting angiotensin II, it dilates blood vessels and lowers blood pressure without affecting the heart rate.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Initial Therapy</strong>:</p><ul><li>Standard starting dose: One tablet (5/20 mg) daily.</li><li>If required, the dosage may be increased after 1–2 weeks to a maximum of two tablets (10/40 mg) daily.</li><li>Can be taken with or without food.</li><li>Not recommended as initial therapy for patients aged 75 years or older or those with hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Replacement Therapy</strong>:</p><ul><li>Abecab 5 mg + 20 mg can replace the individually titrated components of amlodipine and olmesartan. The dosage of one or both components may be adjusted if blood pressure is not adequately controlled.</li></ul><p><strong>Add-On Therapy</strong>:</p><ul><li>Used to enhance blood pressure control in patients not adequately managed with either amlodipine (or another dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker) or olmesartan (or another ARB) alone.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><p>The pharmacokinetics of amlodipine and olmesartan remain unaffected when co-administered. Although no interaction studies have been conducted specifically for the combination, individual studies for both components indicate no significant drug interactions.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any active or inactive ingredient in the combination.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Adverse reactions are generally mild and rarely lead to treatment discontinuation. Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Edema</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Flushing</li><li>Palpitations</li></ul><p>Other potential side effects:</p><ul><li>Vomiting</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Rhabdomyolysis</li><li>Alopecia</li><li>Pruritus</li><li>Urticaria</li></ul><p>This version avoids direct duplication while retaining the key details and professional tone. Let me know if further adjustments are needed!</p>', 'active', '221_1734519562.jpg', 44, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 15:59:22', '2024-12-18 15:59:22'),
(300, 'Ramoril', 'Ramipril', 5.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Used to lower blood pressure either as monotherapy or in combination with other antihypertensive medications.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Congestive Heart Failure</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Often combined with diuretics, it aids in managing heart failure.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Post-Myocardial Infarction</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Administered to patients showing clinical signs of heart failure within a few days after a heart attack.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Nephropathy</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Beneficial for patients with diabetic or non-diabetic nephropathy, particularly those with glomerular involvement.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Cardiovascular Risk Reduction</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Reduces the risk of myocardial infarction, stroke, or cardiovascular death in patients with increased cardiovascular risk factors such as coronary artery disease, diabetes, or a history of stroke.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) Inhibitors</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Ramoril 2.5 mg functions by inhibiting ACE, thereby blocking the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, a potent vasoconstrictor. This results in reduced vasopressor activity, decreased aldosterone secretion, and improved blood pressure control. Its effectiveness extends to heart failure management and cardiovascular event prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: Start with 1.25–2.5 mg once daily, adjusting every two weeks. Maintenance dosage ranges from 2.5–20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Heart Failure Post-MI</strong>: Begin with 2.5 mg twice daily, reducing to 1.25 mg twice daily if hypotension occurs. Gradually titrate to 5 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Prevention</strong>: Start with 2.5 mg daily for a week, increase to 5 mg for three weeks, then maintain 10 mg daily as tolerated.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Initial dose of 1.25 mg daily, with a maximum of 5 mg for hypertension and 2.5 mg for heart failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><ul><li>Swallow tablets whole with liquid. Food does not significantly affect absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: May cause hypotension and hyperkalemia when combined with potassium-sparing diuretics.</li><li><strong>Lithium</strong>: Increases lithium concentration.</li><li><strong>NSAIDs</strong>: May reduce effectiveness and impair renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>History of angioedema.</li><li>Concurrent use with sacubitril/valsartan, certain dialysis membranes, or extracorporeal treatments.</li><li>Significant renal artery stenosis or hypotension.</li><li>Pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Dizziness, headache, fatigue, hypotension.</li><li>Rare: Angioedema, hyperkalemia, anaphylactic reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor patients with renal impairment, hyperkalemia, or hepatic dysfunction closely.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protect from light, and keep out of reach of children. Use only as prescribed by a physician.</li></ul><p>Ramoril 2.5 mg provides a well-tolerated, long-acting option for managing hypertension, heart failure, and reducing cardiovascular risks.</p>', 'active', '936_1734519739.png', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 16:02:19', '2024-12-18 16:02:19'),
(301, 'Angenta', 'Flupentixol + Melitracen', 5.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.5 mg+10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Angenta 0.5 mg + 10 mg Tablet</strong></p><p><strong>Angenta 0.5 mg + 10 mg</strong> is used to manage the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Anxiety and depression</li><li>Apathy</li><li>Psychogenic depression</li><li>Depressive neuroses</li><li>Masked depression</li><li>Psychosomatic disorders accompanied by anxiety and apathy</li><li>Menopausal depression</li><li>Dysphoria and depression in individuals with alcohol or substance use disorders</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>This medication belongs to the <strong>combined anxiolytics and antidepressant drugs</strong> class.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p><strong>Angenta 0.5 mg + 10 mg</strong> contains flupentixol and melitracen, which work synergistically to provide antidepressant and anxiolytic effects:</p><ul><li><strong>Flupentixol</strong>: Acts as a neuroleptic agent, primarily through the mixed blockade of dopamine D1 and D2 receptors, particularly in the brain\'s mesolimbic system. This action contributes to its antipsychotic effects. It also exerts antiemetic effects via dopamine receptor blockade in the chemoreceptor trigger zone.</li><li><strong>Melitracen</strong>: A tricyclic antidepressant that prevents the reuptake of serotonin and norepinephrine in the CNS, alleviating symptoms of depression.</li></ul><p>The pharmacokinetic profile reveals that maximum serum levels are reached approximately 4 hours after oral intake. The half-life is approximately 35 hours for flupentixol and 19 hours for melitracen.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Adults</strong>:</p><ul><li>Standard dose: 2 tablets daily (morning and afternoon).</li><li>Severe cases: Morning dose can be increased to 2 tablets.</li></ul><p><strong>Elderly Patients</strong>:</p><ul><li>Standard dose: 1 tablet in the morning.</li></ul><p><strong>Maintenance Dose</strong>:</p><ul><li>Typically, 1 tablet in the morning or as advised by a physician.</li></ul><p>For insomnia or severe restlessness, additional sedative therapy may be recommended during the acute phase.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>May enhance the effects of alcohol, barbiturates, and other CNS depressants.</li><li>Concurrent use with MAO inhibitors can result in hypertensive crises.</li><li>Neuroleptics and thymoleptics may reduce the efficacy of certain antihypertensives (e.g., guanethidine).</li><li>Thymoleptics can amplify the effects of adrenaline and noradrenaline.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>This medication should not be used in the following situations:</p><ul><li>Recovery phase immediately following a myocardial infarction</li><li>Bundle-branch conduction defects</li><li>Untreated narrow-angle glaucoma</li><li>Acute intoxication with alcohol, barbiturates, or opioids</li><li>Use of MAO inhibitors within the past two weeks</li><li>Patients prone to agitation or hyperactivity due to the activating effects of the drug</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Side effects are uncommon at recommended doses but may include:</p><ul><li>Transient restlessness</li><li>Insomnia</li></ul><p><strong>Use During Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety profile for use during pregnancy and breastfeeding has not been established. Use only if clearly necessary and under medical supervision.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Overdose Management</strong></p><p>Overdose symptoms are generally associated with melitracen’s anticholinergic effects and, less commonly, flupentixol’s extrapyramidal symptoms.</p><p><strong>Management includes</strong>:</p><ul><li>Gastric lavage and administration of activated charcoal</li><li>Symptomatic and supportive care for respiratory and cardiovascular function</li><li>Avoid epinephrine (adrenaline) in these patients</li><li>Use diazepam for convulsions and biperiden for extrapyramidal symptoms</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Instructions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a dry location.</li><li>Protect from light and keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This medication combines flupentixol and melitracen to provide a balanced antidepressant, anxiolytic, and activating effect. It offers a unique synergy:</p><ul><li><strong>Flupentixol</strong>: Anxiolytic and antidepressant at low doses.</li><li><strong>Melitracen</strong>: Bipolar thymoleptic with activating properties.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1046_1734519918.png', 1, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 16:05:18', '2024-12-18 16:05:18'),
(302, 'Amdocal Plus', 'Amlodipine + Atenolol', 8.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Amdocal Plus (5 mg Amlodipine + 50 mg Atenolol)</strong></p><p>Amdocal Plus is used for:</p><ul><li>Hypertension not adequately managed by a single medication.</li><li>Co-existing angina pectoris and hypertension.</li><li>Post-myocardial infarction (MI) patients.</li><li>Refractory angina pectoris when nitrate therapy proves ineffective.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Combined antihypertensive agents.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacological Overview</strong></p><p><strong>Amlodipine</strong>: A calcium channel blocker that reduces calcium ion influx into vascular smooth and cardiac muscles. Primarily acting on vascular smooth muscle, it lowers peripheral resistance and blood pressure by dilating blood vessels, reducing afterload, and decreasing myocardial oxygen demand.</p><p><strong>Atenolol</strong>: A selective beta-blocker that reduces blood pressure through decreased cardiac output, lower plasma renin levels, and reduced CNS sympathetic outflow. It also decreases myocardial oxygen demand through its negative inotropic and chronotropic effects.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li>The standard dose is one tablet daily (Amlodipine 5 mg + Atenolol 50 mg or Amlodipine 5 mg + Atenolol 25 mg).</li><li>Dose adjustments are recommended based on therapeutic response.</li><li>Elderly patients may begin with half a tablet (Amlodipine 2.5 mg + Atenolol 25 mg).</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Amlodipine</strong>: Compatible with thiazide diuretics, beta-blockers, alpha-blockers, ACE inhibitors, nitrates, NSAIDs, antibiotics, and hypoglycemic agents. It does not interfere with protein binding of drugs like Digoxin, Phenytoin, Warfarin, or Indomethacin.</li><li><strong>Atenolol</strong>: May reduce Disopyramide clearance and interact with high-dose Ampicillin (≥1 g). Beta-blockers may affect insulin sensitivity and secretion, but Atenolol has a lower potential for these effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Avoid use in patients with:</p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Amlodipine or Atenolol.</li><li>Sinus bradycardia or significant heart block.</li><li>Cardiogenic shock, severe hypotension, or heart failure.</li><li>Poor left ventricular function.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Commonly reported side effects include:</p><ul><li>Fatigue, headache, and drowsiness.</li><li>Edema, nausea, and anxiety.</li><li>Rarely, symptoms of depression.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Atenolol crosses the placenta and is not recommended during pregnancy.</li><li>Use should also be avoided while breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Atenolol may mask symptoms of hyperthyroidism or hypoglycemia, potentially increasing sensitivity to hypoglycemic agents in diabetic patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Instructions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a cool, dry place, away from light and out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p>This text avoids direct duplication and ensures a clear understanding of the medication’s indications and usage.</p>', 'active', '631_1734520072.jpg', 3, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 16:07:52', '2024-12-18 16:07:52');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(303, 'Ancor', 'Bisoprolol Fumarate', 11.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypertension</li><li>Angina</li><li>Moderate to severe heart failure</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Anti-adrenergic agents (Beta-blockers)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p><strong>Bisoprolol Hemifumarate</strong> is a highly selective ß1-adrenergic receptor blocker. It reduces heart rate and cardiac output, lowering blood pressure without significantly affecting lipid profiles, including HDL cholesterol, during long-term therapy.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension:</strong> Start with 5 mg once daily. Adjust to 10-20 mg as needed.</li><li><strong>Angina:</strong> Typically, 10 mg daily; may range from 5-20 mg.</li><li><strong>Heart Failure:</strong> Begin with 1.25 mg daily, titrate gradually to a maximum of 10 mg daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><p>Avoid combining Bisoprolol with other ß-blockers.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Cardiogenic shock</li><li>Overt heart failure</li><li>Advanced A-V block (second/third degree)</li><li>Right ventricular failure due to pulmonary hypertension</li><li>Sinus bradycardia</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Possible effects include:</p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal disturbances</li><li>Bradycardia</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Fatigue, dizziness, headache</li><li>Sleep disturbances</li><li>Visual disturbances, alopecia</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Use in pregnancy: Safety not established.</li><li>Use during lactation: Insufficient data available.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Regular monitoring of renal, hepatic, and hematopoietic function is advised during prolonged therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a dry, cool place, away from light and heat. Keep out of children’s reach.</p><p><br></p><p>This summary excludes direct copying and provides an easy-to-read format while retaining essential details.</p>', 'active', '1425_1734520257.png', 29, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 16:10:57', '2024-12-18 16:10:57'),
(304, 'Nebita', 'Nebivolol', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nebita 5 mg</strong></p><p>Nebita 5 mg is used to treat essential hypertension. It is also indicated as an adjunctive therapy in stable mild to moderate heart failure in patients aged over 70 years.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Beta-adrenoceptor blocking drugs (Beta-blockers).</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Nebita 5 mg contains a β-adrenergic receptor blocking agent. It consists of two enantiomers, d-Nebita 5 mg and l-Nebita 5 mg. The drug is highly selective for β1-adrenergic receptors and induces vasodilation via nitric oxide release from the endothelium. It does not possess intrinsic sympathomimetic or membrane-stabilizing properties.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 5 mg once daily; maximum recommended dose is 40 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Initial dose of 2.5 mg once daily for severe cases, titrated gradually.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Initial dose of 2.5 mg once daily for moderate cases, titrated gradually.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Patients</strong>: No dose adjustment required.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients are not established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CYP2D6 inhibitors</strong>: Caution when co-administered with drugs like quinidine, propafenone, fluoxetine, or paroxetine.</li><li><strong>Other β-blockers</strong>: Avoid co-administration.</li><li><strong>Digitalis glycosides</strong>: Concomitant use may increase bradycardia risk.</li><li><strong>Calcium channel blockers (e.g., verapamil, diltiazem)</strong>: May exacerbate myocardial depression and AV conduction issues.</li><li><strong>Antiarrhythmic agents (e.g., disopyramide)</strong>: Use with caution.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Severe bradycardia.</li><li>Heart block greater than first degree.</li><li>Cardiogenic shock.</li><li>Decompensated cardiac failure.</li><li>Sick sinus syndrome.</li><li>Severe hepatic impairment.</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the product.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common side effects include headache, nausea, and bradycardia.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Use in Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Classified as category C. Use only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor patients with inadequate cardiac function, well-compensated heart failure, or myasthenia gravis.</li><li>Use caution during major surgeries involving general anesthesia.</li><li>May mask signs of hypoglycemia and hyperthyroidism.</li><li>Avoid abrupt withdrawal, especially in coronary artery disease patients, to prevent angina, myocardial infarction, or arrhythmias.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Management</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Bradycardia, hypotension, cardiac failure, bronchospasm, fatigue, dizziness, and hypoglycemia.</li><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: Supportive care with IV atropine for bradycardia, IV isoproterenol if needed, and fluids or vasopressors for hypotension. Glucagon, β2-agonists, aminophylline, or IV glucose may be administered as required. For CHF, an IV cardiac glycoside and diuretic may be used.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><p>Store at 20-25°C. Protect from light and keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '246_1734520551.png', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 16:15:51', '2024-12-18 16:15:51'),
(305, 'Telmidip', 'Amlodipine + Telmisartan', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p><strong>Telmidip 5 mg + 40 mg</strong> is used to treat essential hypertension in adults.</p><ol><li><strong>Replacement Therapy</strong>: For patients currently taking telmisartan and amlodipine as separate tablets, it may be substituted with a single Telmidip tablet at the same doses for convenience.</li><li><strong>Add-On Therapy</strong>: Suitable for patients whose blood pressure is not adequately controlled with either telmisartan or amlodipine alone.</li><li><strong>Initial Therapy</strong>: Can be used as an initial treatment in patients likely to require multiple medications to meet blood pressure goals, considering patient-specific factors and risk assessments.</li></ol><p>Prompt treatment is essential for patients with moderate or severe hypertension to reduce the risks of cardiovascular events, kidney failure, and vision problems.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Combined antihypertensive preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Amlodipine</strong>: A calcium channel blocker that relaxes vascular smooth muscle, leading to coronary vasodilation and increased myocardial oxygen delivery in vasospastic angina.</li><li><strong>Telmisartan</strong>: An angiotensin II receptor antagonist that prevents vasoconstriction and aldosterone secretion by blocking the binding of angiotensin II to AT1 receptors.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: Take one tablet (40 mg telmisartan + 5 mg amlodipine) once daily. The maximum dose is two tablets per day.</li><li><strong>Replacement Therapy</strong>: Switch from separate components to this combination if doses match.</li><li><strong>Add-On Therapy</strong>: Use for patients inadequately controlled by amlodipine or telmisartan alone.</li><li><strong>Initial Therapy</strong>: Starting dose is 40/5 mg once daily, not recommended for patients aged 75+ or with hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p>Telmidip can be taken with or without food. Individual dose titration with the components is recommended before switching to the fixed-dose combination.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in hepatic/renal impairment, renovascular hypertension, and severe heart failure.</li><li>Correct dehydration or salt depletion before initiating therapy.</li><li>Avoid use in children under 18 due to insufficient safety data.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to telmisartan, amlodipine, or excipients.</li><li>Severe hepatic impairment, biliary obstructive disorders, cardiogenic shock, or hypotension.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common adverse effects include dizziness, peripheral edema, headache, fatigue, hypotension, abdominal pain, nausea, and myalgia.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category D – Use only if the potential benefit justifies the risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Discontinue nursing or therapy due to the potential risk to the infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place away from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p>Let me know if further refinements are needed!</p><p><br></p><p><br></p>', 'active', '683_1734520939.jpg', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 16:22:19', '2024-12-18 16:22:19'),
(306, 'Duovas', 'Amlodipine + Olmesartan Medoxomil', 13.00, 14, 'Tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Duovas 5 mg+20 mg is prescribed for hypertension treatment. It can be used alone or alongside other antihypertensive medications. It is also suitable as initial therapy for patients likely to require multiple drugs to manage blood pressure effectively.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Combination antihypertensive medications.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Amlodipine</strong>: A calcium channel blocker that reduces calcium influx into smooth and cardiac muscle, causing vasodilation and decreased vascular resistance, leading to lower blood pressure.</li><li><strong>Olmesartan</strong>: An angiotensin II receptor blocker (AT1 subtype) that relaxes blood vessels, lowering blood pressure without significantly affecting heart rate.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial Therapy</strong>: Start with one tablet (5/20 mg) daily. If needed, increase to two tablets (10/40 mg) daily after 1–2 weeks. Administer with or without food.</li><li><strong>Replacement Therapy</strong>: Can substitute for individually adjusted doses of its components.</li><li><strong>Add-On Therapy</strong>: Provides additional blood pressure control for patients inadequately managed on Amlodipine or Olmesartan alone.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Not recommended as initial therapy for patients aged ≥75 years or with hepatic impairment.</li><li>Discontinue during pregnancy due to potential harm to the fetus.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><p>No significant interactions reported between Amlodipine and Olmesartan when co-administered. Studies for this combination with other drugs have not revealed major concerns.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common: Swelling (edema), dizziness, flushing, and palpitations.</p><p>Others: Nausea, diarrhea, alopecia, pruritus, or muscle breakdown (rhabdomyolysis) in rare cases.</p><p><strong>Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Discontinue if pregnancy is detected, especially in the second or third trimester.</li><li><strong>Hypotension</strong>: Risk in patients with salt or volume depletion.</li><li><strong>Heart Conditions</strong>: Use cautiously in those with severe aortic stenosis, obstructive coronary artery disease, or heart failure.</li><li><strong>Renal or Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Adjust dosage and monitor carefully.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose</strong></p><p>Potential effects: Severe hypotension, tachycardia, or bradycardia with Amlodipine. Overdose with Olmesartan may result in prolonged hypotension and shock.</p><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><p>Store in a dry, cool place below 30°C. Keep out of reach of children, away from light and moisture.</p><p><strong>Use in Specific Populations</strong></p><p>The safety profile in pediatric populations remains unverified.</p>', 'active', '130_1734521069.png', 10, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 16:24:29', '2024-12-18 16:24:29'),
(307, 'Bisopro', 'Bisoprolol Fumarate', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Bisopro 5 mg</strong></p><p>Bisopro 5 mg is used to manage:</p><ul><li>Hypertension</li><li>Angina</li><li>Moderate to severe heart failure</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Beta-adrenergic blockers (Beta-blockers), anti-adrenergic agents</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Bisoprolol Hemifumarate is a highly selective beta-1 adrenergic receptor blocker, exhibiting greater affinity for beta-1 receptors compared to other beta-blockers. It decreases heart rate and cardiac output, effectively reducing blood pressure. Unlike non-selective beta-blockers, Bisoprolol has minimal impact on lipid profiles, preserving cardioprotective HDL-cholesterol levels during long-term use.</p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension:</strong> Typically, 5 mg once daily. Initial doses of 2.5 mg may be suitable for some patients. If needed, the dose can be increased to 10 mg or a maximum of 20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Angina:</strong> Commonly 10 mg once daily, with 5 mg being sufficient for some individuals. Maximum dose: 20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Heart Failure:</strong> Start with 1.25 mg once daily (morning) for 1 week, then gradually increase:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Week 2: 2.5 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Week 3: 3.75 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Week 5–8: 5 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Week 9–12: 7.5 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum: 10 mg daily</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><p>Avoid combining Bisoprolol with other beta-blockers.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Do not use in patients with:</p><ul><li>Cardiogenic shock</li><li>Overt heart failure</li><li>Second or third-degree A-V block</li><li>Right ventricular failure due to pulmonary hypertension</li><li>Sinus bradycardia</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Possible side effects include:</p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal issues</li><li>Bradycardia</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Headache</li><li>Fatigue</li><li>Sleep disturbances</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Visual changes</li><li>Alopecia</li><li>Rarely, thrombocytopenia</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy: Safety during pregnancy has not been fully established.</li><li>Lactation: Insufficient data available.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><p>Regular monitoring of renal, hepatic, and hematopoietic functions is recommended during prolonged treatment.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p>This rewritten text is original and free of copy-pasted material, while retaining the necessary information.</p><p><br></p><p><br></p>', 'active', '1056_1734521216.png', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 16:26:56', '2024-12-18 16:26:56'),
(308, 'Ambrox', 'Ambroxol Hydrochloride', 50.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '15 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong> Ambroxol is indicated for the treatment of productive cough and in various acute and chronic inflammatory conditions of the respiratory tract that involve thick, sticky mucus. This includes:</p><ul><li>Acute and chronic bronchitis</li><li>Inflammatory diseases of the rhinopharyngeal tract (e.g., laryngitis, pharyngitis, sinusitis, rhinitis)</li><li>Asthmatic bronchitis</li><li>Bronchial asthma with thick mucus</li><li>Bronchiectasis</li><li>Chronic pneumonia</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong> Cough expectorants &amp; mucolytics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Ambroxol is a metabolite of Bromhexine and possesses mucokinetic and secretolytic properties, which help in the improvement of mucus transport and the liquefaction of secretions. It stimulates serous cells in the mucous membrane of the bronchi, increasing mucus secretion. The mucolytic action involves the depolymerization and breakdown of mucoproteins, reducing mucus viscosity and facilitating its expectoration. Ambroxol also stimulates surfactant production by alveolar cells and has anti-inflammatory effects. It has antioxidant properties and can improve airway patency in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). It is generally well-tolerated for long-term use.</p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Drops:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0-6 months: 0.5 ml twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 months: 1 ml twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-2 years: 1.25 ml twice daily</li><li><strong>Syrup:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2-5 years: 2.5 ml (1/2 teaspoon) 2-3 times a day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-10 years: 5 ml (1 teaspoon) 2-3 times a day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 years and adults: 10 ml (2 teaspoons) 3 times a day</li><li><strong>Sustained Release Capsules:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults and children over 12 years: 1 capsule once daily</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction:</strong> Ambroxol should not be used with antitussives (e.g., Codeine), as this could prevent the expectoration of the liquefied mucus.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong> Ambroxol is contraindicated for individuals with known hypersensitivity to Ambroxol or Bromhexine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include mild gastrointestinal issues like epigastric pain or a feeling of stomach fullness.</li><li>Rare allergic reactions, such as skin rashes, hives, or angioneurotic edema, may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong> Although studies have not shown harmful effects on the fetus, it is recommended to avoid Ambroxol during the first trimester of pregnancy. Its safety during lactation has not been established.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with gastric or duodenal ulcers, convulsive disorders, or liver and kidney insufficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from direct light, at temperatures not exceeding 30°C.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong> Cough expectorants &amp; mucolytics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Ambroxol, a more effective metabolite of Bromhexine, helps by liquefying mucus through a hydrating mechanism. This facilitates easier expectoration and reduces shortness of breath. It also supports the production of surfactant, improving lung function and reducing bronchial hyperactivity. Additionally, it has anti-inflammatory effects by inhibiting certain cytokines and metabolites in the body, and improves airway patency in COPD patients.</p>', 'active', '1802_1734521425.webp', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 16:30:25', '2025-04-16 12:33:24'),
(309, 'Dementa', 'Memantine Hydrochloride', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment of all forms of Alzheimer\'s dementia.</li><li>May be indicated in other types of dementia.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Anti-Alzheimer drugs.</li><li>Drugs for Dementia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Memantine acts as an NMDA receptor antagonist, which is involved in the regulation of glutamate, a neurotransmitter associated with Alzheimer\'s disease. It works by reducing excessive stimulation of NMDA receptors, which may help alleviate symptoms.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Recommended Maintenance Dose:</strong> 20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Titration Schedule:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Week 1:</strong> Morning - 5 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Week 2:</strong> Morning - 5 mg, Night - 5 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Week 3:</strong> Morning - 10 mg, Night - 5 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Week 4 onwards:</strong> Morning - 10 mg, Night - 10 mg.</li><li><strong>Missed Dose:</strong> Take the next dose at the usual time; do not double the dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Adjust dose in patients with moderate or severe renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No adjustment needed for mild/moderate impairment.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> Not recommended for those under 18.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>May enhance the effects of L-dopa, dopaminergic agonists, and anticholinergics.</li><li>Potential interactions with drugs like amantadine, ketamine, and others that affect NMDA receptors.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Memantine or any of its excipients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Hallucinations, confusion, dizziness, headache, fatigue.</li><li>Less common: Anxiety, vomiting, bladder infections, increased sexual drive.</li><li>Seizures can be triggered in those with a history of epilepsy.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> Use only if benefits outweigh risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Caution is recommended as it is unknown if Memantine passes into breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor kidney function in patients with kidney dysfunction.</li><li>Caregivers should be trained in proper administration, especially for doses above 5 mg.</li><li>Caution in patients with a history of seizures.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Symptoms:</strong></p><ul><li>Agitation, dizziness, hallucinations, confusion, bradycardia, weakness, etc.</li><li>Management includes symptomatic treatment and possible urinary acidification to increase elimination.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, away from light, and out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Memantine\'s action as an NMDA receptor antagonist helps mitigate the excessive excitatory activity of glutamate, which is thought to contribute to Alzheimer\'s disease symptoms.</li></ul><p>This summary simplifies the information, but I recommend referring to the official product literature for specific details, especially when administering to patients. Let me know if you need further clarification on any aspect.</p>', 'active', '1066_1734521591.png', 27, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 16:33:11', '2024-12-18 16:33:11'),
(310, 'Linatab M', 'Linagliptin + Metformin Hydrochloride', 14.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.5 mg+850 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Adjunct to diet and exercise for improving glycemic control in adults with type 2 diabetes.</li><li>When treatment with both Linagliptin and Metformin Hydrochloride is appropriate.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Combination Oral Hypoglycemic Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Linagliptin</strong>: A DPP-4 inhibitor that increases the levels of active incretin hormones (GLP-1 and GIP), which in turn stimulates insulin release from pancreatic beta cells in a glucose-dependent manner and decreases glucagon secretion from pancreatic alpha cells.</li><li><strong>Metformin Hydrochloride</strong>: A biguanide that lowers both basal and postprandial plasma glucose by decreasing hepatic glucose production, reducing intestinal glucose absorption, and improving insulin sensitivity.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Immediate Release Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Starting dose: 2.5 mg Linagliptin and 500 mg Metformin twice daily with meals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum dose: 2.5 mg Linagliptin and 1000 mg Metformin twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gradual dose escalation to minimize gastrointestinal side effects.</li><li><strong>Extended-Release Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Starting dose: 5 mg Linagliptin and 1000 mg Metformin once daily with a meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum dose: 5 mg Linagliptin and 2000 mg Metformin.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cationic Drugs</strong>: May reduce Metformin elimination (e.g., amiloride, digoxin).</li><li><strong>P-glycoprotein/CYP3A4 Inducers</strong>: Rifampin may reduce efficacy.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Renal impairment (due to Metformin\'s renal excretion).</li><li>Acute or chronic metabolic acidosis, including diabetic ketoacidosis.</li><li>Hypersensitivity to Linagliptin or Metformin Hydrochloride.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Most common: Nasopharyngitis, diarrhea.</li><li>Hypoglycemia risk when used with sulfonylureas.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Use only if clearly needed during pregnancy.</li><li>Caution with lactating mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Discontinue if lactic acidosis occurs.</li><li>Monitor for acute pancreatitis.</li><li>Temporarily discontinue before radiologic studies or surgery.</li><li>Monitor Vitamin B12 levels annually due to potential reduction by Metformin.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>For overdose, supportive treatment is recommended. Linagliptin is not removed by dialysis, but Metformin can be dialyzed.</li><li>Symptoms of overdose: Hypoglycemia (10% of Metformin overdose cases), and lactic acidosis (32% of Metformin overdose cases).</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry place, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Linagliptin</strong>: Increases active incretin hormones, stimulating insulin release and reducing glucagon secretion.</li><li><strong>Metformin</strong>: Reduces glucose production in the liver, decreases glucose absorption in the intestines, and increases insulin sensitivity.</li></ul><p>This combination drug is effective for managing type 2 diabetes, improving blood sugar control with minimal risk of hypoglycemia when used as directed.</p>', 'active', '1163_1734521764.png', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 16:36:04', '2024-12-18 16:36:04'),
(311, 'Zimax', 'Azithromycin', 40.00, 6, 'Tablet', '6tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Upper Respiratory Tract:</strong> Sinusitis, pharyngitis, tonsillitis.</li><li><strong>Lower Respiratory Tract:</strong> Bronchitis, pneumonia.</li><li><strong>Otitis Media:</strong> Middle ear infections.</li><li><strong>Skin and Soft Tissue Infections.</strong></li><li><strong>Sexually Transmitted Infections:</strong> Non-gonococcal urethritis and cervicitis caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Macrolides.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Zimax 500 mg works by inhibiting microbial protein synthesis. It binds to the 50s ribosomal subunit of bacteria, preventing the bacteria from producing the proteins they need to grow. It is effective against both Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria, as well as some anaerobes, atypical bacteria, and other organisms, including <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em>, <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Legionella pneumophila</em>.</p><p><strong>Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">500 mg once daily for 3 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Alternatively, 500 mg on Day 1, followed by 250 mg daily for the next 4 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For sexually transmitted diseases caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em>, a 1 gm single dose or 500 mg on Day 1 followed by 250 mg for 2 more days.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 mg/kg once daily for 3 days for children over 6 months.</li><li>Specific doses for children based on weight:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">200 mg (15-25 kg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">300 mg (26-35 kg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">400 mg (36-45 kg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For typhoid fever, 500 mg once daily for 7-10 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>Shake the bottle to break up the powder.</li><li>Add boiled and cooled water up to the marked line on the label.</li><li>Mix thoroughly before administration.</li><li>Take Zimax 500 mg at least 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Food and antacids may reduce absorption.</li><li>Avoid co-administration with ergot alkaloids due to risk of ergotism.</li><li>Zimax 500 mg can raise the plasma levels of drugs like cyclosporine and digoxin.</li><li>No significant interactions with warfarin, theophylline, carbamazepine, methylprednisolone, or cimetidine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Zimax 500 mg or other macrolide antibiotics.</li><li>Co-administration with ergot derivatives.</li><li>Liver disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, diarrhea, flatulence, headaches, skin rashes.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Reversible after discontinuation of treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category B (no evidence of harm to the fetus in animal studies). Use only if no suitable alternatives are available.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> The presence in breast milk is unknown, so caution is advised when used by nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor for signs of superinfection with non-susceptible organisms, such as fungi.</li><li>No dose adjustment required for renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose may include hearing loss, severe nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea.</li><li>Treatment may involve gastric lavage and supportive care.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul>', 'active', '770_1734521985.png', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 16:39:45', '2024-12-18 16:39:45'),
(312, 'Humulin Vial', 'Insulin Human [rDNA] + Isophane Insulin Human', 996.00, 1, 'vial', '1 piece in a box', 'Vial', '30%+70% in 100 IU perml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment of all type 1 diabetic patients.</li><li>Treatment of type 2 diabetic patients whose blood sugar cannot be controlled by diet or oral medications.</li><li>Initial stabilization for diabetes in conditions like diabetic ketoacidosis, hyperosmolar non-ketotic syndrome, and during stressful conditions (e.g., serious illnesses or major surgery).</li><li>Management of gestational diabetes.</li></ul><p><strong>Composition:</strong></p><ul><li>Isophane Insulin Human 30/70 Injection: 30% Soluble Insulin Human (Regular) + 70% Isophane Insulin Human.</li><li>Each milliliter contains 100 IU (3.47 mg) of Insulin Human (rDNA).</li><li>Also available in a 50/50 combination of Soluble Insulin Human and Isophane Insulin Human.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Medium-acting insulin.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Human insulin, produced via recombinant DNA technology, mirrors natural insulin in structure and function.</li><li>It regulates glucose metabolism by promoting its absorption and use in the liver, muscles, and fat, lowering blood glucose levels.</li><li>Begins working within 30 minutes, peaks between 2–8 hours, and lasts approximately 24 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li>Type 1 diabetes: Average daily dose ranges from 0.5 to 1.0 IU/kg, with higher doses in cases like obesity or puberty.</li><li>Type 2 diabetes: Starting dose is usually between 0.3 to 0.6 IU/kg/day.</li><li>Dosage may vary based on food intake, activity level, and other individual factors.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>Inject 30 minutes before meals. Subcutaneous injections should be administered in the upper arm, thigh, buttock, or abdomen.</li><li>Gently shake or rotate the vial to mix the insulin before use. Ensure no air bubbles are in the syringe.</li><li>Injection site should be rotated, avoiding the same spot for consecutive injections.</li><li>Ensure that insulin is injected subcutaneously, maintaining the needle position for at least 6 seconds.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Medications like oral contraceptives, thyroid hormone, and corticosteroids may require higher insulin doses.</li><li>Antidepressants, salicylates, and sulfonylamides may reduce insulin requirements due to their hypoglycemic effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypoglycemia or allergies to insulin or excipients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Most common side effect: Hypoglycemia.</li><li>Rare allergic reactions, including redness or itching at the injection site, typically resolve with continued use.</li><li>Other rare reactions may be caused by improper injection techniques or disinfectants.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Insulin is safe during pregnancy as it does not cross the placental barrier.</li><li>It poses no harm to the baby when used by the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Inadequate dosing or discontinuation may lead to hyperglycemia, especially in type 1 diabetes.</li><li>Excessive insulin may cause hypoglycemia, which can result from skipping meals or excessive physical activity.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypoglycemia can occur with excessive insulin. Immediate medical attention is required if hypoglycemia occurs.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store between 2–8°C in a refrigerator. Do not freeze.</li><li>Open vials can be kept at room temperature for up to 1 month away from heat and light.</li></ul><p>This information is a summary and should not replace medical advice. Always consult a healthcare professional for personalized guidance.</p>', 'active', '292_1734522363.png', 54, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 16:46:03', '2024-12-18 16:46:03'),
(313, 'Normens', 'Norethisterone', 6.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Spasmodic dysmenorrhea</li><li>Management of perimenopausal symptoms</li><li>Premenstrual tension</li><li>Management of endometriosis</li><li>Dysfunctional uterine bleeding (DUB)</li><li>Prophylaxis against recurrent dysfunctional bleeding</li><li>Treatment of inoperable breast cancer</li><li>Postponement of menstruation</li><li>Menorrhagia (heavy menstrual bleeding)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Hormone replacement therapy</li><li>Female sex hormones</li><li>Oral contraceptive preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Normens 5 mg mimics endogenous progesterone, influencing cervical and endometrial functions and inhibiting pituitary hormones related to ovulation. It is used for contraception or hormone-related conditions.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Spasmodic Dysmenorrhea</strong>: 15 mg daily from days 5 to 26 of the cycle.</li><li><strong>Perimenopausal Symptoms</strong>: 2.5–10 mg daily, depending on the case.</li><li><strong>Premenstrual Tension</strong>: 5–15 mg daily from days 19 to 26 for 2–3 cycles.</li><li><strong>Dysfunctional Uterine Bleeding</strong>: 1 tablet, 2–3 times daily, for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Endometriosis</strong>: 10–20 mg daily for at least 4–6 months.</li><li><strong>Inoperable Breast Cancer</strong>: 30–40 mg daily, adjusted as necessary.</li><li><strong>Postponement of Menstruation</strong>: 5 mg, 3 times daily, 3 days before expected menstruation.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy</li><li>Lactation</li><li>History of certain liver or blood conditions</li><li>Hormone-dependent malignancies</li><li>Unexplained vaginal bleeding</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Breakthrough bleeding, spotting, or changes in menstrual flow</li><li>Weight changes, mood swings, headaches, nausea</li><li>Edema, acne, mental depression, breast tenderness</li><li>Liver enzyme abnormalities, melasma, allergic rash</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with a history of allergies, blood clots, liver disease, cardiovascular issues, diabetes, and asthma.</li><li>Avoid excessive alcohol.</li><li>Caution in those with conditions that may be aggravated by fluid retention.</li><li>Not recommended for use during pregnancy or lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a cool, dry place, away from light and out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>Not for use in children or elderly individuals.</li></ul><p>If overdose occurs, symptoms may include nausea, breast enlargement, or vaginal bleeding. Contact a healthcare provider immediately.</p><p>This summary is intended for informational purposes and should not replace professional medical advice. Always consult your healthcare provider before using this medication.</p>', 'active', '1296_1734522533.png', 6, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 16:48:53', '2024-12-18 16:48:53'),
(314, 'Pase', 'Clonazepam', 6.50, 15, 'Tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pase 0.5 mg</strong></p><p>Pase 0.5 mg is used for the treatment of panic disorder, with or without agoraphobia. Panic disorder is characterized by unexpected panic attacks and persistent concern about future episodes. It is also indicated for treating seizure disorders, including Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome (specifically the petit mal variant), akinetic seizures, and myoclonic seizures. It may be used alone or as an adjunct treatment in patients with absence seizures who have not responded to succinimides.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs, Benzodiazepine hypnotics.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Pase 0.5 mg works by reducing nerve transmission in the motor cortex, effectively suppressing spike and wave discharges associated with absence seizures. It enhances GABA activity, which aids in controlling both focal and generalized seizures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults with Seizure Disorders:</strong> Start with 1.5 mg/day, divided into three doses. Increase by 0.5-1 mg every three days, up to a maximum of 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Adults with Panic Disorder:</strong> Begin with 0.25 mg twice daily, increasing to 1 mg/day after three days.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Children (up to 10 years or 30 kg) should start with 0.01-0.03 mg/kg/day, not exceeding 0.05 mg/kg/day, divided into multiple doses.</li><li><strong>Injection Dosage:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children: 0.5 mg via slow IV injection or infusion.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 1 mg via slow IV injection or infusion, repeat if necessary (typically 1-4 mg).</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><p>Pase 0.5 mg does not appear to affect the pharmacokinetics of phenytoin, carbamazepine, or phenobarbital, but its effect on other drugs has not been fully studied.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Avoid in patients with hypersensitivity to benzodiazepines or significant liver disease. It should be used with caution in patients with open-angle glaucoma, but it is contraindicated in those with acute narrow-angle glaucoma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common side effects include CNS depression (drowsiness, ataxia), and in some patients, behavioral changes. Other potential side effects include abnormal eye movements, aphonia, tremor, confusion, depression, hallucinations, psychosis, insomnia, and palpitations.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Use during pregnancy should be considered only if the benefits outweigh the risks to the fetus. Breastfeeding is not recommended during treatment.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Patients with multiple seizure disorders may experience increased frequency of generalized tonic-clonic seizures. Careful monitoring and adjustment of anticonvulsants may be necessary.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>Overdose can result in drowsiness, ataxia, and respiratory depression. Severe cases may lead to coma. Treatment includes monitoring vital signs, supportive care, and possibly administering activated charcoal or flumazenil in severe cases.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat, and out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p>For IV injection, dilute with 1 ml of water for injection to prevent vein irritation. IV infusion should be diluted with suitable media like sodium chloride.</p><p><br></p><p><br></p>', 'active', '124_1734522694.png', 7, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 16:51:34', '2024-12-18 16:51:34'),
(315, 'Rostatin', 'Rosuvastatin', 11.00, 14, 'Tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Rostatin 10 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial and Non-familial)</li><li><strong>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial)</li><li><strong>Mixed Dyslipidemia</strong> (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li><strong>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs, Statins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rostatin 10 mg acts as a selective, competitive inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase, the enzyme responsible for converting 3-hydroxy-3-methyl glutaryl coenzyme A to mevalonate, a precursor of cholesterol. It reduces lipid levels in two primary ways:</p><ol><li>It increases the number of hepatic LDL receptors, which enhances the uptake and breakdown of LDL.</li><li>It inhibits the synthesis of VLDL in the liver, reducing the overall levels of VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General Dosage Range</strong>: 5-40 mg once daily.</li><li>The 40 mg dose is recommended only for patients who do not reach their LDL-C goal with 20 mg.</li><li><strong>HoFH (Homozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia)</strong>: Starting dose of 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients with HeFH</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">8 to &lt;10 years: 5-10 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 to 17 years: 5-20 mg/day</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients with HoFH</strong>: 20 mg/day for patients 7 to 17 years of age.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Rostatin 10 mg can be taken at any time of day, with or without food.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong>: Increased exposure; maximum dose should not exceed 5 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil</strong>: Avoid combination; limit the dose to 10 mg/day if used together.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or Atazanavir/Ritonavir</strong>: Increased exposure; maximum dose should be limited to 10 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants</strong>: May prolong INR; stabilize INR before starting and monitor frequently.</li><li><strong>Concomitant lipid-lowering therapies</strong>: Increased risk of skeletal muscle effects when used with fibrates or niacin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Rostatin 10 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to any product component</li><li>Liver disease, including unexplained persistent liver enzyme abnormalities</li><li>Pregnant women or women who may become pregnant</li><li>Nursing mothers</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Rostatin 10 mg is generally well tolerated, though some common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia (muscle pain)</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia (weakness)</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Safety in pregnant women has not been established.</li><li>It is unknown whether Rostatin 10 mg is excreted in human milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Skeletal muscle effects</strong>: Risks increase with higher doses (e.g., 40 mg), advanced age, hypothyroidism, renal impairment, and combination use with certain drugs (e.g., cyclosporine, lopinavir/ritonavir, or other lipid-lowering drugs).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Advise patients to report unexplained muscle pain, tenderness, or weakness immediately. Discontinue if symptoms appear.</li><li><strong>Liver enzyme monitoring</strong>: Persistent elevations in hepatic transaminases may occur. Monitor liver enzymes before and during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and effectiveness have not been established in pediatric patients.</li></ul><p>This text can be used for educational or informational purposes, while avoiding any direct claims or promotional language.</p>', 'active', '585_1734522839.jpg', 9, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 16:53:59', '2024-12-18 16:53:59');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(316, 'Comprid XR', 'Gliclazide', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '60 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong> Comprid XR 60 mg is an oral antidiabetic medication belonging to the sulfonylurea group, used to manage type 2 diabetes mellitus in adults. It is prescribed when diet, exercise, and weight loss alone are insufficient to control blood sugar levels.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong> Sulfonylureas.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Comprid XR 60 mg is a second-generation sulfonylurea. It works by stimulating insulin release from pancreatic β-cells and reducing hepatic glucose production, which helps lower blood sugar levels.</p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial Dose:</strong> 40 to 80 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Maximum Dose:</strong> Up to 320 mg daily in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Administration:</strong> The medicine should be taken before meals.</li><li><strong>For Children:</strong> Not recommended, as it is contraindicated for juvenile-onset diabetes.</li></ul><p>For the modified-release (MR) form, the recommended dose is one to four tablets (maximum 120 mg) taken once daily at breakfast. Always take with a glass of water and swallow whole. Do not chew or crush the tablets.</p><p><strong>Overdose:</strong> Symptoms include hypoglycemia (low blood sugar). If overdose occurs, immediately contact a doctor or emergency services. Consume sugar or sugary drinks if conscious, but do not give food or drink to unconscious patients.</p><p><strong>Missed Dose:</strong> If a dose is missed, take the next dose at the usual time. Do not double the dose.</p><p><strong>Discontinuation:</strong> Consult a doctor before stopping, as this could cause hyperglycemia (high blood sugar) and increase the risk of complications.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Increased Hypoglycemia Risk:</strong> Certain antibiotics, beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors, insulin, GLP-1 receptor agonists, alcohol, and others.</li><li><strong>Weakened Effect on Blood Sugar:</strong> Corticosteroids, certain antidepressants, medications for asthma, and St. John\'s Wort.</li><li><strong>Blood Clotting:</strong> May enhance the effects of warfarin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Insulin-dependent diabetes (type 1).</li><li>Diabetic ketoacidosis or diabetic coma.</li><li>Severe kidney or liver disease.</li><li>Allergy to sulfonylureas.</li><li>Breastfeeding women.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Hypoglycemia (low blood sugar), skin reactions (rash, redness), liver disorders (yellow skin/eyes), blood disorders (anemia, reduced platelet count).</li><li><strong>Less Common:</strong> Digestive issues (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea), eye disturbances (temporary blurred vision), allergic reactions (e.g., angioedema).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation:</strong> Contraindicated during pregnancy and breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions and Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Regular monitoring of blood sugar is essential.</li><li>Hypoglycemia risk increases with fasting, irregular meals, increased physical activity, or alcohol consumption.</li><li>May cause impaired ability to drive or operate machinery if blood sugar is too low or high.</li><li>Caution if you have liver or kidney problems, hormone disorders, or a history of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong> Store below 30°C, out of reach of children. Do not use after the expiration date. Dispose of unused medicine properly.</p><p>This summary provides key details about Comprid XR 60 mg, but consult your doctor or pharmacist for personalized medical advice.</p>', 'active', '115_1734523170.png', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 16:59:30', '2024-12-18 16:59:30'),
(317, 'Ecosprin Plus', 'Clopidogrel + Aspirin', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '75 mg+75 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p><p><strong>Ecosprin Plus: Overview and Usage</strong></p><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Acute Coronary Syndrome (ACS):</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Reduces the risk of Myocardial Infarction (MI) and Stroke in patients with non-ST-segment elevation ACS (unstable angina/NSTEMI) and acute ST-segment elevation ACS (STEMI).</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Recent MI, Stroke, or Established Peripheral Arterial Disease:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Indicated to reduce the occurrence of MI and Stroke in patients with a history of MI, Stroke, or established peripheral arterial disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Anti-platelet drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clopidogrel:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">A prodrug that inhibits platelet activation and aggregation by binding irreversibly to P2Y12 ADP receptors on platelets.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Inhibits platelet aggregation within 2 hours of a single dose, reaching a steady state between Day 3 and Day 7 of repeated dosing.</li><li><strong>Aspirin:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Inhibits platelet aggregation through irreversible inhibition of cyclooxygenase, preventing the generation of thromboxane A2, a platelet aggregator and vasoconstrictor.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>The recommended dose is one tablet per day.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Increased bleeding risk with oral anticoagulants, NSAIDs, Metamizole, SSRIs, and CYP2C19 inhibitors.</li><li>Interactions with Tenofovir, Valproic acid, Varicella vaccine, Acetazolamide, and Nicorandil.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component.</li><li>Active pathological bleeding, such as peptic ulcers or intracranial hemorrhage.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Generally well tolerated.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Use during the first and second trimesters if clearly needed.</li><li>Contraindicated in the third trimester.</li><li>It is unknown if Clopidogrel is excreted in breast milk, but aspirin is known to be.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>May prolong bleeding time.</li><li>Risk of Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura (TTP) and Reye’s syndrome (especially in children with chickenpox or flu symptoms).</li><li>Hypersensitivity reactions, including rash and angioedema.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clopidogrel Overdose:</strong> Can lead to bleeding; platelet transfusion may be required.</li><li><strong>Aspirin Overdose:</strong> Can cause dizziness, confusion, gastrointestinal issues (moderate), and more severe symptoms (respiratory/metabolic acidosis, hyperthermia) requiring supportive treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>Not recommended for children under 12 due to the risk of Reye\'s syndrome.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clopidogrel:</strong> Inhibits platelet aggregation by binding to P2Y12 ADP receptors.</li><li><strong>Aspirin:</strong> Inhibits platelet aggregation by blocking thromboxane A2 production.</li></ul><p>This combination drug is used primarily for its anti-platelet effects to reduce the risk of cardiovascular events.</p><p><br></p><p>4o mini</p><p><br></p><p><br></p>', 'active', '399_1734523328.webp', 5, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 17:02:08', '2024-12-18 17:02:08'),
(318, 'Mezest', 'Megestrol Acetate', 90.00, 6, 'Tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '160 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Breast Cancer</strong>: Used in advanced, recurrent, or metastatic cases.</li><li><strong>Endometrial Carcinoma</strong>: For advanced or inoperable cases.</li><li><strong>Anorexia &amp; Cachexia</strong>: Associated with AIDS and cancer, improving weight loss.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Breast Cancer</strong>: 160 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Endometrial Carcinoma</strong>: 40-320 mg/day in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Oral Suspension</strong>: 800 mg/day (20 ml).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Weight gain (due to increased appetite).</li><li>Thromboembolic phenomena (including fatal pulmonary embolism).</li><li>Glucocorticoid-like effects such as new-onset diabetes and Cushing’s syndrome.</li><li>Other effects include nausea, dyspnea, alopecia, hypertension, mood changes, and rash.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Mezest 160 mg or any formulation component.</li><li>Pregnancy (Category D).</li></ul><p><strong>Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in patients with a history of thromboembolic disease.</li><li>Monitor patients for diabetes exacerbation, as increased insulin requirements may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture, and out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Not recommended during the first four months of pregnancy.</li><li>Small amounts are excreted in breast milk; nursing should be avoided during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>Pediatric use: Not established.</li><li>Geriatric use: Begin at lower doses due to age-related organ function changes.</li></ul><p>Mezest 160 mg should be part of a broader treatment regimen involving surgery, radiation, or chemotherapy, and is intended only for palliative care in advanced cancer stages.</p>', 'active', '1532_1734528384.png', 19, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 18:26:24', '2024-12-18 18:26:24'),
(319, 'Pladex', 'Clopidogrel Bisulphate', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '75 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Acute Coronary Syndrome (ACS):</strong> Reduces myocardial infarction (MI) and stroke in patients with non-ST-segment elevation ACS (including unstable angina or non-ST-elevation MI).</li><li><strong>Recent MI, Recent Stroke, or Peripheral Arterial Disease:</strong> Reduces the risk of MI and stroke in patients with a history of recent MI, stroke, or established peripheral arterial disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Anti-platelet drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clopidogrel</strong> is a prodrug that inhibits platelet activation and aggregation by binding irreversibly to the P2Y12 ADP receptor on platelets. It begins showing effects within 2 hours after a single dose, and steady-state inhibition occurs by Day 3-7 with daily doses of 75 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Acute Coronary Syndrome:</strong> Initiate with a 300 mg oral loading dose, followed by 75 mg daily. Avoid initiation without a loading dose as it delays the antiplatelet effect.</li><li><strong>Recent MI, Stroke, or Peripheral Arterial Disease:</strong> 75 mg once daily without a loading dose. Can be taken with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Increased bleeding risk</strong> with NSAIDs, warfarin, SSRIs, and SNRIs.</li><li><strong>Avoid</strong> concomitant use with omeprazole or esomeprazole (CYP2C19 inhibitors).</li><li><strong>Avoid</strong> with repaglinide as it raises plasma levels of the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to clopidogrel or its components</li><li>Active bleeding conditions such as peptic ulcer or intracranial hemorrhage</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Bleeding, gastrointestinal discomfort, diarrhea, hemorrhage, skin reactions.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Anaemia, angioedema, bone marrow disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Use during pregnancy only if necessary. It’s unknown if clopidogrel passes into breast milk, so decisions on nursing should be based on its importance to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Genetic Impairment:</strong> Poor metabolizers of CYP2C19 may have reduced efficacy.</li><li><strong>Increased bleeding risk:</strong> It inhibits platelet aggregation for up to 7-10 days.</li><li><strong>Surgical Procedures:</strong> Discontinue 5 days prior to surgeries with a major bleeding risk.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>May cause bleeding. Platelet transfusion may be needed to reverse clotting ability.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a dry place, away from light, and out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and efficacy in children are not established.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> No dosage adjustment is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Clopidogrel works as a prodrug, inhibiting platelet aggregation via the P2Y12 ADP receptor, providing dose-dependent inhibition of platelet aggregation, with steady-state achieved by Day 3-7.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1195_1734529555.webp', 8, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 18:45:55', '2024-12-18 18:45:55'),
(320, 'Telmipres', 'Telmisartan', 11.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '80 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Telmipres 80 mg:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: Treatment of essential hypertension in adults.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Prevention</strong>: Reduces cardiovascular morbidity in adults with:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Atherothrombotic cardiovascular disease (including a history of coronary heart disease, stroke, or peripheral arterial disease)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Type 2 diabetes with documented target organ damage.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Angiotensin II Receptor Blocker</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Angiotensin II is formed from angiotensin I through ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme). It has various effects, such as vasoconstriction, aldosterone release, and sodium reabsorption. Telmipres 80 mg selectively blocks angiotensin II from binding to the AT1 receptor, blocking its vasoconstrictive and aldosterone-secreting effects.</li><li>Telmipres does not affect ACE or bradykinin, which is involved in ACE inhibitors\' actions. Its action on blood pressure remains unaffected by increased renin activity.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li>The initial dose is typically 40 mg once daily, with a dose range of 20 to 80 mg for blood pressure control. Maximal effect is usually seen within 4 weeks.</li><li>For elderly or those with renal impairment, no initial dose adjustment is necessary. Monitoring is required in dialysis patients due to the potential for orthostatic hypotension.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>Telmipres can be taken with or without food, with or without other antihypertensive agents.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>NSAIDs may reduce the antihypertensive effect and increase the risk of renal impairment.</li><li>Avoid co-administration with aliskiren in diabetic patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Telmipres or its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects: Sinusitis, back pain, diarrhea in hypertensive patients; intermittent claudication, skin ulcers in cardiovascular risk reduction patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Category C during the first trimester. Discontinue if pregnancy occurs or is expected, as it may cause fetal harm.</li><li>Potential for serious adverse effects in nursing infants; consider discontinuing breastfeeding or the medication.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid use during pregnancy. Monitor blood pressure in patients with hepatic or renal impairment. Avoid combining with ACE inhibitors.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>May cause hypotension, dizziness, tachycardia, bradycardia, elevated serum creatinine, and acute renal failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C. Protect from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>Renal Impairment: For severe impairment or those on hemodialysis, start with 20 mg daily.</li><li>Hepatic Impairment: Maximum dose of 40 mg daily for mild to moderate impairment. Contraindicated for severe impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Angiotensin II Receptor Blocker</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Telmipres blocks the effects of angiotensin II at the AT1 receptor to reduce vasoconstriction and aldosterone secretion, without affecting ACE or other hormone receptors.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1002_1734529679.jpg', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 18:47:59', '2024-12-18 18:47:59'),
(321, 'Indever', 'Propranolol Hydrochloride', 1.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Essential Hypertension</strong></li><li><strong>Angina Pectoris</strong></li><li><strong>Cardiac Arrhythmias</strong></li><li><strong>Thyrotoxicosis</strong></li><li><strong>Anxiety</strong></li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of Migraine</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Beta-adrenoceptor blocking drugs (Beta-blockers)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Indever 40 mg is a non-cardioselective beta-blocker that blocks both β1- and β2-receptors. This results in reduced heart rate, myocardial contractility, blood pressure, and myocardial oxygen demand. It also has membrane-stabilizing properties.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><h4>Tablets:</h4><ul><li><strong>Hypertension:</strong> Start with 80 mg twice daily, increasing according to response, with a typical range of 160-320 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Angina, Anxiety, Migraine, Essential Tremor:</strong> Start with 40 mg 2-3 times daily, usually adjusted to 80-160 mg/day for anxiety, migraine, and tremor, and 120-240 mg/day for angina.</li><li><strong>Situational Anxiety:</strong> 40 mg daily for acute relief.</li><li><strong>Dysrhythmias &amp; Hypertrophic Obstructive Cardiomyopathy:</strong> 10-40 mg 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Post Myocardial Infarction:</strong> Start 40 mg four times daily 5-21 days post-infarction, switching to 80 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Phaeochromocytoma:</strong> 60 mg daily pre-surgery or 30 mg daily for inoperable cases.</li></ul><h4>Sustained Release Capsules:</h4><ul><li><strong>Hypertension:</strong> Initial dose 80 mg once daily, with maintenance doses of 120-160 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Angina:</strong> Start with 80 mg once daily, adjusting as necessary.</li><li><strong>Migraine:</strong> Initial dose 80 mg daily, with a typical range of 160-240 mg daily.</li></ul><h4>Injection:</h4><ul><li><strong>Dosage:</strong> 1-3 mg administered slowly under monitoring, not exceeding 1 mg/minute. A second dose may be given after 2 minutes if necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Close monitoring is advised when used with verapamil, salbutamol, levosalbutamol, ergot alkaloids, lidocaine, or phenobarbital.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Bronchospasm</li><li>Cardiogenic shock</li><li>Second or third degree heart block</li><li>Diabetes mellitus</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Cold extremities, dizziness, insomnia, nausea, paresthesia, and fatigue.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>No sufficient controlled studies in pregnancy. It is excreted in human milk, so caution is advised during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in hepatic and renal impairment, pregnancy, and breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms may include bradycardia, hypotension, acute cardiac insufficiency, and bronchospasm. Overdose management includes intensive care, gastric lavage, activated charcoal, and plasma substitutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, away from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Indever 40 mg works by blocking β-adrenergic receptors, decreasing heart rate and myocardial oxygen demand, thus reducing blood pressure and alleviating symptoms of various conditions.</p>', 'active', '1508_1734529851.png', 44, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 18:50:51', '2024-12-18 18:50:51'),
(322, 'Natrilix SR', 'Indapamide', 11.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '1.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Natrilix SR 1.5 mg</strong></p><p>Natrilix SR 1.5 mg is used to treat essential hypertension. It is also effective in managing hypertension in patients with impaired renal function, though its diuretic effect may be reduced. Additionally, it is indicated for managing salt and fluid retention in patients with congestive heart failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Thiazide diuretics &amp; related drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Natrilix SR 1.5 mg is a diuretic antihypertensive. It likely causes vasodilation by inhibiting the movement of calcium, sodium, and potassium ions across cell membranes. Its action helps reduce vascular hyperreactivity and lowers both total peripheral and arteriolar resistance.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p>The typical dosage is one tablet daily, preferably in the morning. In more severe cases, it can be combined with other antihypertensive medications. The safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Other antihypertensive agents</strong>: Natrilix SR 1.5 mg may enhance the effects of other antihypertensive drugs.</li><li><strong>Norepinephrine</strong>: This medication can reduce the response to norepinephrine, similar to other thiazide diuretics.</li><li><strong>Lithium</strong>: Concomitant use with lithium is not recommended, as it may reduce its renal clearance and increase the risk of lithium toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>This medication should not be used in individuals with:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to sulfonamides</li><li>Severe renal failure</li><li>Hepatic encephalopathy or severe hepatic failure</li><li>Hypokalemia</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common side effects may include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Anorexia</li><li>Gastric irritation</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Diarrhea</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>There are no sufficient studies on the effects during pregnancy, so Natrilix SR 1.5 mg is not recommended for pregnant women. It should not be used during breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Regular monitoring of potassium and uric acid serum levels is recommended, particularly for patients prone to hypokalemia or gout.</li><li>Patients with a history of sulfonamide allergies should be monitored closely, despite no reported allergic reactions in clinical trials.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>Possible overdose symptoms include:</p><ul><li>Allergies</li><li>Skin rashes</li><li>Epigastric pain</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Photosensitivity</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Weakness</li><li>Paraesthesia</li><li>Treatment involves supportive care, focusing on correcting any electrolyte imbalances.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place. Protect from light and moisture.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Thiazide diuretics &amp; related drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Natrilix SR 1.5 mg acts as a diuretic and antihypertensive by inhibiting the passage of calcium, sodium, and potassium across membranes, leading to vasodilation and reduced vascular resistance.</p>', 'active', '829_1734529996.webp', 41, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 18:53:16', '2024-12-18 18:53:16'),
(323, 'Prazopress ER', 'Prazosin Hydrochloride', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p><p>Prazopress ER (Prazosin) 2.5 mg is primarily used for the following indications:</p><ol><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: It is effective for both essential and secondary hypertension. It can be used alone or in combination with other antihypertensive agents like diuretics. It lowers blood pressure through selective inhibition of alpha-1 adrenoceptors in vascular smooth muscle, without impairing renal function, making it safe for hypertensive patients with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Left Ventricular Failure</strong>: Prazosin may be added to conventional heart failure treatments in patients not responding adequately to diuretics or cardiac glycosides. It helps by reducing left ventricular filling pressure, cardiac impedance, and augmenting cardiac output.</li><li><strong>Raynaud\'s Phenomenon and Disease</strong>: Prazosin reduces the severity and frequency of attacks in patients with Raynaud\'s disease by decreasing peripheral vascular resistance.</li><li><strong>Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia</strong>: It is used as an adjunct treatment for urinary obstruction due to benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH), improving symptoms like urinary retention.</li></ol><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Alpha-1 adrenoceptor blockers</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage and Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: The starting dose is typically 0.5 mg at bedtime, gradually increasing based on response. The goal is to reach a daily dose of up to 20 mg.</li><li><strong>Left Ventricular Failure</strong>: Start with 0.5 mg, increasing gradually to a daily dose of 4 mg to 20 mg in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Raynaud\'s Disease</strong>: Start with 0.5 mg twice daily, adjusting based on response.</li><li><strong>Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia</strong>: Start with 0.5 mg twice daily, gradually increasing to 2 mg twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Mode of Action</strong>: Prazosin reduces peripheral vascular resistance by selectively blocking postsynaptic alpha-1 adrenoceptors. This lowers blood pressure and improves symptoms in various conditions like heart failure, Raynaud\'s disease, and BPH.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common effects include dizziness, headache, drowsiness, and nausea. Rare effects include orthostatic hypotension, palpitations, and sexual dysfunction. Monitoring for these side effects is recommended, especially during dose escalation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Careful monitoring is required during the initiation of therapy, particularly in patients with heart failure or low blood pressure. It should be used cautiously in elderly patients and those with renal or hepatic dysfunction.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Prazosin is contraindicated in patients with known sensitivity to quinazolines.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Prazosin may interact with antihypertensive drugs, cardiac-glycosides, and sedatives. It should be used cautiously with other blood pressure-lowering agents.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose:</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose may lead to hypotension. In such cases, support of the cardiovascular system is critical. The drug is not dialyzable due to its protein binding properties.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture, and keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Prazosin can be used safely in patients with renal dysfunction but should be started at a lower dose.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Lower initial doses are recommended due to increased sensitivity to hypotension in older adults.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '292_1734530143.webp', 8, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 18:55:43', '2024-12-18 18:55:43'),
(324, 'Glipita M', 'Sitagliptin + Metformin Hydrochloride', 18.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg+1000 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Glipita M 50 mg + 1000 mg</strong></p><p>Glipita M 50 mg + 1000 mg is a combination medication used to manage high blood sugar levels in individuals with type 2 diabetes mellitus. It helps prevent complications such as kidney damage, blindness, and may reduce the risk of heart attack or stroke. This medication can be taken alone or in combination with other diabetes treatments, and it works best when paired with a healthy diet and regular exercise. The dose will be tailored to each individual’s condition, blood sugar levels, and other medications being taken. For optimal results, it is recommended to take this medication consistently with meals to reduce the risk of gastrointestinal discomfort.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Combination Oral Hypoglycemic Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Sitagliptin</strong>: This drug is a DPP-4 inhibitor, which increases the levels of incretin hormones (GLP-1 and GIP). These hormones enhance insulin release from the pancreas in a glucose-dependent manner and lower glucagon secretion, leading to reduced liver glucose production. Unlike sulfonylureas, which stimulate insulin release even when glucose is low, sitagliptin lowers blood glucose levels without causing hypoglycemia.</li><li><strong>Metformin</strong>: Metformin is an antihyperglycemic agent that reduces glucose production in the liver and increases insulin sensitivity in muscle, promoting better glucose uptake. It does not induce hypoglycemia, unlike other treatments.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p>The dosage of Glipita M 50 mg + 1000 mg is individualized based on the patient’s current regimen, effectiveness, and tolerance, with a maximum dose of 100 mg sitagliptin and 2000 mg metformin per day. The tablet should generally be taken twice daily with meals to reduce gastrointestinal side effects from metformin.</p><ul><li>Starting dose for those not on metformin: 50 mg sitagliptin/500 mg metformin twice daily.</li><li>For those already on metformin, the starting dose is typically 50 mg sitagliptin/1000 mg metformin twice daily.</li><li>If used with insulin or insulin secretagogues, doses of those medications may need adjustment to avoid hypoglycemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cationic Drugs</strong>: Caution is advised when used with cationic drugs cleared by renal secretion.</li><li><strong>Phenprocoumon</strong>: Metformin can reduce the anticoagulant effect of phenprocoumon, requiring INR monitoring.</li><li><strong>Levothyroxine</strong>: May reduce the hypoglycemic effect of metformin; blood glucose should be monitored.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Renal dysfunction (creatinine ≥1.5 mg/dL in men, ≥1.4 mg/dL in women).</li><li>Acute or chronic metabolic acidosis (including diabetic ketoacidosis).</li><li>Known hypersensitivity to sitagliptin, metformin, or other components of the tablet.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common side effects include diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, headache, upper respiratory tract infections, and sore throat. Hypoglycemia may occur when used with insulin or sulfonylureas.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B. Should only be used if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether sitagliptin is excreted in breast milk. Use caution in nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Lactic Acidosis</strong>: Risk increases with renal impairment, dehydration, liver disease, or excessive alcohol consumption.</li><li><strong>Pancreatitis</strong>: Monitor for signs of pancreatitis and discontinue if suspected.</li><li><strong>Renal Function</strong>: Regular monitoring is necessary due to the risk of renal failure and other complications.</li><li><strong>Allergic Reactions</strong>: Serious reactions like anaphylaxis and angioedema may occur. Discontinue immediately if these occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Sitagliptin</strong>: Overdose management involves supportive care. Sitagliptin is modestly dialyzable.</li><li><strong>Metformin</strong>: In cases of overdose, metformin can be dialyzed to remove excess drug from the body.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 25°C in a dry, light-protected place, out of reach of children. Do not use past the expiration date.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Caution is needed due to the risk of renal impairment with aging.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and efficacy in children under 18 have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Glipita M combines two antihyperglycemic agents:</p><ul><li><strong>Sitagliptin</strong>: A DPP-4 inhibitor that prolongs the effects of incretin hormones, increasing insulin release and decreasing glucagon production in a glucose-dependent manner.</li><li><strong>Metformin</strong>: Reduces glucose production in the liver, increases insulin sensitivity in muscles, and decreases intestinal glucose absorption.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1654_1734530283.jpg', 3, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 18:58:03', '2024-12-18 18:58:03'),
(325, 'Tenil', 'Bromazepam', 9.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '3 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Emotional Disturbances</strong>: Acute tension, anxiety, agitation, insomnia, anxious and agitated depressive reactions.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular and Respiratory</strong>: Pseudoangina pectoris, pericardial anxiety, tachycardia, emotiogenic hypertension, dyspnea, hyperventilation.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Irritable bowel syndrome, epigastric pain, spasm, bloating, diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Urinary Tract</strong>: Frequency, irritable bladder, dysmenorrhea.</li><li><strong>Psychosomatic Disorders</strong>: Psychogenic headache, asthma, gastric and duodenal ulcer.</li><li><strong>Chronic Organic Disease</strong>: Emotional reactions to chronic conditions.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>: Benzodiazepine sedatives</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><ul><li>Tenil 3 mg is a psychotropic agent. At lower doses, it reduces tension and anxiety. At higher doses, it acts as a sedative and muscle relaxant by binding to the GABA-A receptor, enhancing its inhibitory effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Outpatients</strong>: 1.5–3 mg, up to three times daily. Start with low doses, gradually increasing to the optimal level.</li><li><strong>Severe Cases</strong>: 6–12 mg, two or three times daily (hospitalized patients).</li><li><strong>Elderly and Debilitated Patients</strong>: Require lower doses.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Only if prescribed by a doctor, adjusted to bodyweight (0.1–0.3 mg/kg).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong>: Oral tablets</p><p><strong>Interactions</strong>:</p><ul><li>May enhance sedative effects when combined with antidepressants, hypnotics, narcotics, antipsychotics, antiepileptic drugs, sedative antihistamines, anesthetics, or alcohol.</li><li>Co-administration with <strong>cimetidine</strong> may prolong the elimination half-life.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Tenil, severe respiratory or hepatic insufficiency, sleep apnea syndrome.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li>Common: Fatigue, drowsiness, muscle weakness, reduced alertness, confusion, headache, ataxia.</li><li>Possible amnesia at therapeutic doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li>Not established as safe during pregnancy. <strong>Avoid during lactation</strong> as it is excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Physical and psychological dependence can occur with prolonged use.</li><li>Gradually taper off to avoid withdrawal symptoms.</li><li>Not recommended for primary treatment of insomnia caused by psychosis.</li><li>Caution when driving or operating machinery.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong>: Keep in a dry, cool place away from light and heat. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</p><ul><li>Selectively reduces tension and anxiety at lower doses. At higher doses, it has sedative and muscle relaxant effects, acting on the GABA-A receptor.</li></ul><p>This is a clearer, more concise version of the original information. Let me know if you\'d like any further modifications!</p>', 'active', '239_1734530389.png', 5, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 18:59:49', '2024-12-18 18:59:49'),
(326, 'Syndopa', 'Levodopa + Carbidopa', 7.02, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '110 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Syndopa 110 mg</strong></p><p>Levodopa and carbidopa are indicated in the treatment of Parkinson\'s disease, post-encephalitic Parkinsonism, and symptomatic Parkinsonism that may follow carbon monoxide intoxication or manganese intoxication.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antiparkinson drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The symptoms of Parkinson\'s disease are related to dopamine depletion in the corpus striatum. Levodopa, a metabolic precursor of dopamine, crosses the blood-brain barrier and is converted to dopamine in the brain. Carbidopa inhibits the decarboxylation of peripheral levodopa, ensuring that more levodopa reaches the brain for conversion into dopamine.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Syndopa 110 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Usual Initial Dosage</strong>: Start with one tablet (Levodopa 100 mg + Carbidopa 10 mg) three or four times a day. Gradually increase the dosage by one tablet daily or every other day until reaching a total of eight tablets per day (2 tablets four times daily).</li><li><strong>Maintenance Dose</strong>: Dosage should be adjusted based on the patient\'s therapeutic response. If higher doses of levodopa are required, Levodopa 250 mg and Carbidopa 25 mg can be substituted. This dosage may be increased as needed to a maximum of eight tablets daily. Total daily doses exceeding 200 mg of carbidopa have limited clinical experience.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Syndopa 110 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Pyridoxine (Vitamin B6) reverses the effects of levodopa.</li><li>Type B MAO inhibitors have a synergistic effect.</li><li>Reduced effects with phenothiazines, haloperidol, reserpine, pyridoxine, diazepam, oxazepam, chlordiazepoxide, and phenobarbital.</li><li>Enhanced effects with carbidopa, amantadine, anticholinergics, and amphetamines.</li><li>Sympathomimetic agents can enhance the effects of levodopa.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Nonselective monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitors are contraindicated, and they must be discontinued at least two weeks before starting Levodopa and carbidopa therapy.</li><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the drug.</li><li>Not recommended for patients with narrow-angle glaucoma.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Syndopa 110 mg</strong></p><p>The most common side effects include dyskinesias (e.g., choreiform, dystonic, and other involuntary movements) and nausea.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy Category C. Levodopa has been detected in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Dyskinesias are associated with Levodopa and its combinations, and dosage reductions may be necessary.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with severe cardiovascular or pulmonary diseases, bronchial asthma, and renal, hepatic, or endocrine disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in children and infants have not been established. It is not recommended for use in individuals under 18 years of age.</li></ul><p><br></p><p><br></p>', 'active', '111_1734530536.webp', 16, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:02:16', '2024-12-18 19:02:16'),
(327, 'Tofen', 'Ketotifen Fumarate', 75.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '1 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p><p>Tofen 100 ml is a medication used for the prophylactic treatment of bronchial asthma and the symptomatic treatment of allergic conditions such as rhinitis and conjunctivitis. It belongs to the therapeutic class of cromoglycate and related drugs.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Tofen 100 ml contains a potent anti-allergic substance with a strong and sustained non-competitive histamine (H1) receptor blocking effect. It inhibits the release of mediators (such as histamine, leukotrienes, and prostaglandins) from cells responsible for type I allergic reactions. The medication also stabilizes mast cells, decreases chemotaxis, and reduces eosinophil activation and degranulation. Furthermore, it inhibits platelet-activating factor (PAF) and helps prevent asthmatic attacks.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 1 mg twice daily with food; this may be increased to 2 mg twice daily in severe cases.</li><li><strong>Children (above 2 years)</strong>: 1 mg twice daily with food.</li><li><strong>Patients prone to sedation</strong>: Start with 0.5 to 1 mg at night for the first few days, as directed by a physician.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Same as the adult dose, or as advised by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>A reversible fall in platelet count has been observed in some patients when Ketotifen is taken with oral antidiabetics, so this combination is advised against.</li><li>Ketotifen may enhance the effects of other CNS depressants, such as alcohol, antihistamines, sedatives, and hypnotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid combining Ketotifen with oral antidiabetic agents due to potential effects on platelet count.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Drowsiness, dry mouth, and slight dizziness may occur at the beginning of treatment, but usually disappear after a few days.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Ketotifen is not recommended during pregnancy and lactation, despite no evidence of teratogenic effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Continue prior treatments for at least two weeks after starting Ketotifen to avoid exacerbation of asthma, especially for patients using systemic corticosteroids or ACTH.</li><li>If an intercurrent infection occurs, Ketotifen should be supplemented with specific antimicrobial therapy.</li><li>During the first day of treatment, reactions may be impaired, so patients should avoid driving or operating machinery until they know how the medication affects them.</li><li>Avoid alcohol, as Ketotifen may potentiate the effects of alcohol and sedatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Drowsiness, confusion, difficulty breathing, bradycardia or tachycardia, disorientation, convulsions, severe hypotension, reversible coma.</li><li>Management: Supportive and symptomatic treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul>', 'active', '7575_1745387595.jpg', 3, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:04:49', '2025-04-23 09:53:15'),
(328, 'Dialiptin-M', 'Vildagliptin + Metformin Hydrochloride', 18.00, 7, 'Tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg+500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Dialiptin-M 50 mg + 500 mg</strong></p><p>Dialiptin-M 50 mg + 500 mg tablet is used as an adjunct to diet and exercise to help improve glycemic control in patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus. It is particularly helpful in patients whose diabetes is not adequately controlled on Metformin Hydrochloride or Vildagliptin alone, or in those already using a combination of Vildagliptin and Metformin as separate tablets.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Combination Oral Hypoglycemic Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vildagliptin</strong>: Works by inhibiting DPP-4 (Dipeptidyl Peptidase-4), an enzyme that degrades incretin hormones GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1) and GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide). This inhibition results in increased insulin secretion and reduced glucagon secretion, thus lowering blood glucose levels.</li><li><strong>Metformin Hydrochloride</strong>: A biguanide that lowers blood glucose by decreasing hepatic glucose production, reducing intestinal glucose absorption, and improving insulin sensitivity. It does not cause hypoglycemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: The typical starting dose is one tablet in the morning and one in the evening. If patients are already on separate Metformin and Vildagliptin tablets, they can switch to this combination.</li><li><strong>Note</strong>: Doses higher than 100 mg of Vildagliptin are not recommended. The tablet can be taken with or after food to reduce gastrointestinal discomfort.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Low Potential for Drug Interactions</strong>: Vildagliptin does not significantly interact with enzymes like CYP 450 and does not affect the metabolism of other drugs.</li><li><strong>Notable Interactions</strong>: Medications like furosemide, nifedipine, and glyburide may increase Metformin’s blood concentration.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Vildagliptin, Metformin, or any excipient.</li><li>Renal disease or dysfunction, acute myocardial infarction, sepsis, congestive heart failure, metabolic acidosis (including diabetic ketoacidosis), and during radiologic studies with iodinated contrast materials.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include headache, dizziness, nausea, tremors, and hypoglycemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Should be used only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: The components are not known to be excreted in human milk, so breastfeeding is not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Lactic Acidosis</strong>: A rare but serious side effect due to Metformin accumulation. Treatment should be discontinued if acidosis is suspected.</li><li><strong>Liver Function</strong>: Liver function should be monitored regularly. Treatment should be stopped if liver enzymes become elevated.</li><li><strong>Surgical Considerations</strong>: Discontinue 48 hours before surgery and resume 48 hours post-surgery.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: Not recommended for children under 18.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Use with caution, as kidney function tends to decline with age.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Not recommended for patients with renal failure or significant renal dysfunction.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Not recommended for patients with liver dysfunction.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Combination Oral Hypoglycemic Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vildagliptin</strong>: Inhibits DPP-4, increasing GLP-1 and GIP levels, promoting insulin secretion, and lowering blood glucose.</li><li><strong>Metformin</strong>: Decreases glucose production in the liver, improves glucose uptake and utilization in tissues, and reduces post-meal glucose spikes.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1720_1734530859.jpg', 9, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:07:39', '2024-12-18 19:07:39');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(329, 'Siglimet', 'Sitagliptin + Metformin Hydrochloride', 14.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg+500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Indications:</p><ul><li>Type 2 diabetes mellitus: Helps control high blood sugar levels.</li><li>Prevents complications such as kidney damage, blindness, heart attacks, and strokes.</li><li>Best used alongside a healthy diet and regular exercise.</li></ul><p>Dosage &amp; Administration:</p><ul><li><strong>Initial Dose</strong>: Typically, 50 mg Sitagliptin and 500 mg Metformin twice daily, with meals.</li><li>Gradual dose escalation is recommended to reduce gastrointestinal side effects like nausea and diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Maximum Dosage</strong>: 100 mg Sitagliptin and 2000 mg Metformin per day.</li><li><strong>Switching from Metformin</strong>: Adjust Sitagliptin to 50 mg twice daily with the existing dose of Metformin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal issues</strong>: Avoid in patients with severe kidney disease or abnormal renal function.</li><li><strong>Acidosis</strong>: Contraindicated in those with metabolic acidosis, including diabetic ketoacidosis.</li><li><strong>Allergy</strong>: Do not use if allergic to Sitagliptin or Metformin.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Diarrhea, nausea, headache, sore throat, and upset stomach.</li><li><strong>Serious</strong>: Risk of lactic acidosis (accumulation of metformin), pancreatitis, and hypoglycemia (especially when combined with insulin or sulfonylureas).</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Liver and kidney disease</strong>: Use with caution in patients with liver disease or renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Alcohol</strong>: Limit alcohol consumption as it can increase the risk of hypoglycemia and lactic acidosis.</li><li><strong>Pancreatitis</strong>: Discontinue the drug if pancreatitis is suspected.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Sitagliptin</strong>: Overdose management includes supportive care and monitoring.</li><li><strong>Metformin</strong>: In cases of overdose, dialysis may be needed to remove the drug, as Metformin is dialyzable.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Monitor renal function regularly, as kidney function often declines with age.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Should be used only if clearly necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: The safety in breastfeeding is not established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cationic Drugs</strong>: Use with caution if the patient is on cationic drugs eliminated via renal secretion.</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants</strong>: Metformin may decrease the anticoagulant effect of phenprocoumon.</li></ul><p>This drug combination is designed to help manage blood sugar levels in type 2 diabetes and reduce the risks of serious complications, provided it\'s used alongside appropriate lifestyle changes. Regular monitoring of kidney function and blood glucose levels is important during treatment.</p>', 'active', '290_1734531006.webp', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:10:06', '2024-12-18 19:10:06'),
(330, 'Biofol', 'Calcium Folinate [Folinic Acid]', 9.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Diminish Toxicity:</strong> To reduce the toxicity and counteract the effects of impaired Methotrexate elimination.</li><li><strong>Megaloblastic Anemia:</strong> For treating megaloblastic anemia due to folate deficiency, such as in sprue or nutritional deficiencies.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy and Infancy:</strong> To treat megaloblastic anemia in pregnancy and infancy.</li></ol><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Supportive Care Therapy</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Folinic acid is a derivative of tetrahydrofolic acid, which is easily converted into other reduced folic acid derivatives, providing vitamin activity equivalent to folic acid.</li><li>It works without needing dihydrofolate reductase (which is inhibited by methotrexate), making it effective in counteracting the side effects of methotrexate.</li><li>Folinic acid also reactivates dihydrofolate reductase, allowing normal DNA replication to proceed, particularly in cells unaffected by methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">To reduce the harmful effects of folic acid antagonists: 5-15 mg every 6 hours for 2-3 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For megaloblastic anemia: 5-15 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Intravenous (IV):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For colorectal cancer adjunctive therapy: 200 mg/m² followed by 370 mg/m² of fluorouracil for 5 days, with subsequent cycles every 4-5 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Methotrexate rescue therapy:</strong> 15 mg (or 10 mg/m²) every 6 hours for 10 doses.</li><li><strong>Intramuscular (IM):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For folate-deficient megaloblastic anemia: Up to 1 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Calcium Folinate or any components of the formulation.</li><li>Pernicious anemia or megaloblastic anemia due to Vitamin B12 deficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Allergic reactions, including itchy rashes, swelling, or difficulty in swallowing/breathing.</li><li>Seizures and fainting may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>No adequate studies on pregnant or breastfeeding women. Caution is advised, as it\'s unknown whether the drug is excreted in milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>It may mask pernicious anemia or other megaloblastic anemia caused by Vitamin B12 deficiency.</li><li>Should be used with methotrexate or fluorouracil only under the supervision of a clinician experienced with cancer treatments.</li><li>Can interfere with anti-epileptic drugs, potentially increasing the risk of seizures.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>No significant adverse effects have been reported with overdose, but excessive doses can nullify the effects of folic acid antagonists.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light, and keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Folinic acid acts as a folate source, bypassing the need for dihydrofolate reductase. It helps in the synthesis of purines and pyrimidines, allowing DNA replication to continue, especially in normal cells during methotrexate treatment.</li></ul><p>This information should help provide a comprehensive understanding of Biofol 5 mg.</p><p><br></p><p><br></p>', 'active', '1278_1734531150.png', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:12:30', '2024-12-18 19:12:30'),
(331, 'Actrapid Vial', 'Insulin Human [Fast-Acting]', 415.00, 1, 'vial', '1 piece in a box', 'Vial', '100IU per 10 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Actrapid Vial 100IU/10 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment of all type 1 diabetic patients.</li><li>Treatment for type 2 diabetes when blood sugar cannot be sufficiently controlled by diet or oral medications.</li><li>Initial stabilization of diabetes in cases of diabetic ketoacidosis, hyperosmolar non-ketotic syndrome, and during times of stress like severe illnesses or major surgery.</li><li>Management of gestational diabetes.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Rapid Acting Insulin.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Insulin works by binding to receptors on muscle and fat cells, while simultaneously inhibiting the liver\'s glucose release, resulting in lower blood sugar levels.</li><li>Its action begins within 1.5 hours, peaks in 4 to 12 hours, and lasts for around 24 hours. The half-life of insulin is only a few minutes. The time-action profile is determined by the absorption characteristics, which depend on factors such as dosage, injection site, and fat thickness.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage</strong></p><ul><li>Use as directed by your doctor, with adjustments based on individual needs.</li><li>Consume a meal or snack containing carbohydrates within 30 minutes of injection to prevent hypoglycemia.</li><li>Dosage adjustments may be required if switching insulin types or brands.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><ul><li>Administered subcutaneously, usually in the thigh, but can also be injected in the deltoid, gluteal, or abdominal areas.</li><li>Thigh injections lead to slower and more consistent absorption.</li><li>Maintain the needle under the skin for at least 6 seconds to ensure full dosage.</li><li>Rotate injection sites to avoid lipodystrophy.</li><li>Do not administer intravenously.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effect: Hypoglycemia (low blood sugar).</li><li>Symptoms of low blood sugar: Cold sweat, pale skin, headache, rapid heartbeat, nausea, hunger, changes in vision, fatigue, weakness, anxiety, tremor, confusion, and difficulty focusing.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Safe for use during pregnancy but may require adjustments in dosage.</li><li>Close monitoring is needed to prevent hypoglycemia, which can impact the baby.</li><li>Safe for use during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Consult a doctor if you have adrenal, pituitary, thyroid, or liver issues.</li><li>Changes in diet or physical activity may affect blood sugar levels.</li><li>Continue insulin usage even if you\'re ill.</li><li>Traveling across time zones may alter insulin needs and timing.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a refrigerator at 2°C to 8°C.</li><li>Avoid freezing and contact with cooling devices.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Children and adolescents can use this medication.</li><li>Patients with impaired kidney or liver function or those over 65 should monitor blood sugar more frequently and consult their doctor for potential dosage adjustments.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1376_1734531327.png', 42, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:15:27', '2024-12-18 19:15:27'),
(332, 'Salazine', 'Sulfasalazine', 5.23, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Salazine 500 mg is used to treat several conditions, primarily focusing on rheumatoid arthritis, juvenile arthritis, ulcerative colitis, and Crohn’s disease.</p><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis and juvenile arthritis</strong> (polyarticular-course): For patients who have not responded to salicylates or NSAIDs.</li><li><strong>Ulcerative colitis</strong>: As an adjunct treatment, combined with other supportive measures.</li><li><strong>Crohn’s disease</strong>: Particularly in cases with colonic involvement.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for irritable bowel syndrome</li><li>Drugs for rheumatoid arthritis</li><li>Ulcerative colitis treatments</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> The mode of action of Salazine is not fully understood but is believed to involve anti-inflammatory or immunomodulatory properties. The medication has an affinity for connective tissues and high concentrations in serous fluids, liver, and intestinal walls. Its main therapeutic effect in ulcerative colitis may come from 5-Aminosalyclic Acid, a metabolite.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong> The dose should be individualized based on the patient’s tolerance and condition:</p><ul><li><strong>For Crohn’s disease</strong>: 500 mg 2 to 4 times a day with food.</li><li><strong>For rheumatoid arthritis</strong>: Starting from a low dose of 0.5 to 1.0 g daily, gradually increasing to 2 g daily.</li><li><strong>For pediatric use (6 years or older)</strong>: Initial dose for ulcerative colitis is 40–60 mg/kg/day divided into 3 to 6 doses. For juvenile arthritis, the dose ranges from 30 to 50 mg/kg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Reduced absorption of folic acid and digoxin when used together with Salazine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Salazine or its metabolites, sulfonamides, or salicylates.</li><li>Conditions like intestinal or urinary obstruction and porphyria.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong> Common adverse effects include anorexia, nausea, vomiting, gastric distress, dyspepsia, abdominal pain, and dizziness. Reversible oligospermia has also been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B, should be used only if necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Caution is advised when given to breastfeeding mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in patients with hepatic/renal impairment, G6PD deficiency, allergic bronchial asthma, or during lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong> Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Salazine’s action is thought to be related to its anti-inflammatory properties and its ability to concentrate in areas like the liver and intestinal walls.</p>', 'active', '1852_1734531504.png', 7, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:18:24', '2024-12-18 19:18:24'),
(333, 'Disopan', 'Clonazepam', 8.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Disopan 0.5 mg</strong></p><p>Disopan 0.5 mg is primarily used to treat panic disorder, with or without agoraphobia. Panic disorder is characterized by sudden panic attacks, which are accompanied by anxiety over their recurrence and their potential consequences.</p><p>Disopan 0.5 mg is also indicated for treating Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome, particularly the petit mal variant, as well as akinetic and myoclonic seizures. It may be an option for patients with absence seizures (petit mal) who do not respond to succinimides.</p><p>It\'s important to note that the long-term effectiveness of Disopan 0.5 mg beyond 9 weeks has not been thoroughly studied in controlled trials. Therefore, healthcare providers prescribing it for extended periods should periodically reassess its suitability for the individual patient.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</li><li>Benzodiazepine hypnotics</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Disopan 0.5 mg works by reducing nerve transmission in the motor cortex, helping to suppress spike and wave discharges that occur in absence seizures. Its action is believed to be due to its enhancement of GABA activity. Clinically, it has proven effective in both focal epilepsy and generalized seizures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Seizure Disorders (Adults):</strong></li><li>Start with 1.5 mg per day, divided into three doses. Increase by 0.5-1 mg every three days, up to 20 mg per day.</li><li><strong>Panic Disorder (Adults):</strong></li><li>Start with 0.25 mg twice daily. After three days, increase to 1 mg per day as needed.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients (Up to 10 years or 30 kg):</strong></li><li>0.01-0.03 mg/kg/day, not exceeding 0.05 mg/kg/day, divided into multiple doses.</li><li><strong>Injection Dosage:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong> 0.5 mg via slow IV injection or infusion.</li><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 1 mg via slow IV injection or infusion, with repeat doses as necessary (typically 1-4 mg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum rate for IV administration is 0.25-0.5 mg per minute. Total IV dose should not exceed 10 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><p>Disopan 0.5 mg does not appear to affect the pharmacokinetics of phenytoin, carbamazepine, or phenobarbital. However, its effects on the metabolism of other drugs are not fully explored.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with a history of hypersensitivity to benzodiazepines</li><li>Significant liver disease</li><li>Acute narrow-angle glaucoma (can be used in open-angle glaucoma with appropriate treatment)</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p>Common side effects include CNS depression, with drowsiness seen in 50% of patients and ataxia in about 30%. Other side effects may include behavior problems (25%), abnormal eye movements, aphonia, coma, tremor, vertigo, confusion, depression, amnesia, hallucinations, psychosis, insomnia, and palpitations.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><p>Disopan 0.5 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the benefits outweigh the risks. It is not recommended for breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Can increase the occurrence of generalized tonic-clonic seizures in patients with multiple seizure disorders.</li><li>Should be combined with other anticonvulsants as necessary.</li><li>Avoid using with valproic acid, as it may lead to absence status.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><p>Symptoms may include drowsiness, ataxia, dysarthria, nystagmus, and, in rare cases, areflexia, apnea, hypotension, cardiorespiratory depression, and coma. Treatment involves monitoring vital signs and supporting care, such as using activated charcoal within 1-2 hours of ingestion or administering flumazenil for severe CNS depression.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><p>Store in a dry, cool place away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution:</strong></p><p>For slow IV injection, dilute with 1 ml of water for injection. For IV infusion, dilute 1 vial (1 mg) in at least 85 ml of diluting media (e.g., sodium chloride solutions, glucose). Do not use sodium bicarbonate, as it may cause precipitation.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</li><li>Benzodiazepine hypnotics</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Disopan 0.5 mg enhances GABAergic neurotransmission at inhibitory synapses, resulting in anticonvulsive, sedative, muscle-relaxing, and anxiolytic effects. It rapidly suppresses various forms of paroxysmal activity, including absence seizures and generalized spike waves, making it useful for both generalized and focal epilepsies.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong></p><p>In infants and young children, Disopan may increase saliva and bronchial secretions, so airway clearance should be ensured.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong></p><p>Older adults may experience more pronounced effects due to age-related changes in drug interactions and organ function.</p><p><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is necessary for patients with renal impairment.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong></p><p>In cirrhotic patients, the medication’s pharmacokinetics may be altered, leading to a higher free fraction of the drug. Close monitoring is advised.</p>', 'active', '674_1734531614.jpg', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:20:14', '2024-12-18 19:20:14'),
(334, 'RTV', 'Rosuvastatin', 6.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p><p>Here’s a non-copyrighted summary of RTV 10 mg based on your provided information:</p><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia (both Familial and Non-familial)</li><li>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia (Familial)</li><li>Mixed Dyslipidemia (Fredrickson Types IIa and IIb)</li><li>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs, Statins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> RTV 10 mg works by inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase, the enzyme responsible for converting HMG-CoA to mevalonate, a precursor of cholesterol. This leads to two main effects:</p><ol><li>Increased liver LDL receptors, enhancing LDL uptake and breakdown.</li><li>Decreased synthesis of VLDL, resulting in reduced VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li>5-40 mg once daily, with 40 mg reserved for patients not reaching LDL-C goals with 20 mg.</li><li>For HoFH (Homozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia), starting dose is 20 mg/day.</li><li>Pediatric patients:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">HeFH (Heterozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia): 5-10 mg for ages 8-10, 5-20 mg for ages 10-17.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">HoFH: 20 mg/day for ages 7-17.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong> RTV 10 mg can be taken with or without food at any time of day.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong>: Increases RTV exposure; limit dose to 5 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil</strong>: Avoid combination; if necessary, limit to 10 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir, Atazanavir/Ritonavir</strong>: May increase RTV exposure; limit dose to 10 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Coumarin Anticoagulants</strong>: Can prolong INR; monitor INR closely.</li><li><strong>Concomitant lipid-lowering drugs</strong>: Increased risk of skeletal muscle effects with fibrates or niacin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to RTV components</li><li>Liver disease (including unexplained persistent hepatic enzyme elevations)</li><li>Pregnancy and breastfeeding</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong> Common side effects include headache, myalgia, constipation, asthenia, abdominal pain, and nausea.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety during pregnancy is not established.</li><li>It is unknown if RTV is excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Muscle effects</strong>: Risk of myopathy or rhabdomyolysis, especially at 40 mg doses or with other risk factors like hypothyroidism or renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Liver monitoring</strong>: Regular monitoring of liver enzymes is recommended due to the risk of hepatic abnormalities.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong> Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture, and keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong> Safety and efficacy in children have not been established.</p>', 'active', '54_1734531776.jpg', 33, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:22:56', '2024-12-18 19:22:56'),
(335, 'Consucon', 'Gliclazide', 8.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '80 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Consucon 80 mg</strong> is an oral antidiabetic medication that belongs to the sulfonylurea class. It is used to manage type 2 diabetes mellitus in adults, especially when diet, exercise, and weight loss are insufficient to control blood sugar levels.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</p><p>Sulfonylureas</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><p>Consucon 80 mg is a second-generation sulfonylurea that reduces blood sugar levels by:</p><ul><li>Stimulating insulin release from pancreatic β-cells through enhanced calcium ion transport across the β-cell membranes.</li><li>Decreasing glucose production in the liver.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Initial dose</strong>: 40 to 80 mg per day.</li><li><strong>Maximum dose</strong>: Up to 320 mg daily in divided doses as needed.</li><li>Take the medication before meals.</li><li><strong>For children</strong>: Consucon is contraindicated for juvenile-onset diabetes.</li></ul><p><strong>Modified-release preparation</strong>:</p><ul><li>Usually, one to four tablets (maximum 120 mg) daily at breakfast, based on treatment response.</li><li>Swallow the tablet whole with water, without crushing or chewing. Take it with a meal.</li></ul><p>If combining Consucon 80 mg with other antidiabetic medications (e.g., metformin, insulin), dosage adjustments may be required. If you miss a dose, take the next dose at the scheduled time. Do not double the dose.</p><p><strong>Interactions</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Increased risk of hypoglycemia</strong> when combined with other oral antidiabetics, antibiotics (e.g., sulphonamides), and certain heart and blood pressure medications (e.g., beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors).</li><li><strong>Decreased effect on blood sugar</strong> with certain drugs like corticosteroids, beta-agonists, and diuretics.</li><li>May interact with <strong>warfarin</strong>, increasing its blood-thinning effects.</li><li>Alcohol can unpredictably affect blood sugar control.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Allergy</strong> to sulfonylureas or related hypoglycemic sulfonamides.</li><li><strong>Type 1 diabetes</strong> (insulin-dependent).</li><li><strong>Diabetic ketoacidosis</strong>, diabetic coma, severe liver or kidney disease, or if breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong> (low blood sugar) is the most common side effect, and severe hypoglycemia can lead to unconsciousness or coma.</li><li><strong>Liver disorders</strong>: Abnormal liver function (yellow skin/eyes).</li><li><strong>Skin reactions</strong>: Rash, itching, or blistering.</li><li><strong>Blood disorders</strong>: Reduced cell count leading to paleness, bleeding, or infections.</li><li><strong>Eye disorders</strong>: Temporary vision disturbances due to fluctuating blood sugar.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li>Contraindicated in pregnancy and breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Regular monitoring of blood sugar levels is essential, especially during the early stages of treatment.</li><li>Risk of <strong>hypoglycemia</strong> increases in cases of irregular meals, malnutrition, increased physical activity, and alcohol use.</li><li>Be cautious if you have a history of <strong>glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency</strong>, as it may lead to hemolytic anemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong>:</p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, out of reach of children. Do not use beyond the expiration date.</li></ul><p>This medication should only be used as prescribed, and you should always follow your doctor\'s instructions for managing blood sugar levels and lifestyle adjustments.</p>', 'active', '237_1734531925.png', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:25:25', '2024-12-18 19:25:25'),
(336, 'Consucon', 'Gliclazide', 8.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '80 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Consucon 80 mg</strong> is an oral antidiabetic medication that belongs to the sulfonylurea class. It is used to manage type 2 diabetes mellitus in adults, especially when diet, exercise, and weight loss are insufficient to control blood sugar levels.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</p><p>Sulfonylureas</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><p>Consucon 80 mg is a second-generation sulfonylurea that reduces blood sugar levels by:</p><ul><li>Stimulating insulin release from pancreatic β-cells through enhanced calcium ion transport across the β-cell membranes.</li><li>Decreasing glucose production in the liver.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Initial dose</strong>: 40 to 80 mg per day.</li><li><strong>Maximum dose</strong>: Up to 320 mg daily in divided doses as needed.</li><li>Take the medication before meals.</li><li><strong>For children</strong>: Consucon is contraindicated for juvenile-onset diabetes.</li></ul><p><strong>Modified-release preparation</strong>:</p><ul><li>Usually, one to four tablets (maximum 120 mg) daily at breakfast, based on treatment response.</li><li>Swallow the tablet whole with water, without crushing or chewing. Take it with a meal.</li></ul><p>If combining Consucon 80 mg with other antidiabetic medications (e.g., metformin, insulin), dosage adjustments may be required. If you miss a dose, take the next dose at the scheduled time. Do not double the dose.</p><p><strong>Interactions</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Increased risk of hypoglycemia</strong> when combined with other oral antidiabetics, antibiotics (e.g., sulphonamides), and certain heart and blood pressure medications (e.g., beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors).</li><li><strong>Decreased effect on blood sugar</strong> with certain drugs like corticosteroids, beta-agonists, and diuretics.</li><li>May interact with <strong>warfarin</strong>, increasing its blood-thinning effects.</li><li>Alcohol can unpredictably affect blood sugar control.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Allergy</strong> to sulfonylureas or related hypoglycemic sulfonamides.</li><li><strong>Type 1 diabetes</strong> (insulin-dependent).</li><li><strong>Diabetic ketoacidosis</strong>, diabetic coma, severe liver or kidney disease, or if breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong> (low blood sugar) is the most common side effect, and severe hypoglycemia can lead to unconsciousness or coma.</li><li><strong>Liver disorders</strong>: Abnormal liver function (yellow skin/eyes).</li><li><strong>Skin reactions</strong>: Rash, itching, or blistering.</li><li><strong>Blood disorders</strong>: Reduced cell count leading to paleness, bleeding, or infections.</li><li><strong>Eye disorders</strong>: Temporary vision disturbances due to fluctuating blood sugar.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li>Contraindicated in pregnancy and breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Regular monitoring of blood sugar levels is essential, especially during the early stages of treatment.</li><li>Risk of <strong>hypoglycemia</strong> increases in cases of irregular meals, malnutrition, increased physical activity, and alcohol use.</li><li>Be cautious if you have a history of <strong>glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency</strong>, as it may lead to hemolytic anemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong>:</p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, out of reach of children. Do not use beyond the expiration date.</li></ul><p>This medication should only be used as prescribed, and you should always follow your doctor\'s instructions for managing blood sugar levels and lifestyle adjustments.</p>', 'in-active', '146_1734531926.png', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:25:26', '2025-05-08 14:17:00'),
(337, 'Spirocard', 'Spironolactone', 5.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Spirocard 25 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Congestive heart failure</strong></li><li><strong>Hepatic cirrhosis with ascites and oedema</strong></li><li><strong>Nephrotic syndrome</strong></li><li><strong>Primary hyperaldosteronism</strong></li><li><strong>Essential hypertension</strong></li><li><strong>Treatment of hypokalemia</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics</li><li>Aldosterone antagonists</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Spirocard 25 mg is an aldosterone antagonist that works by binding to the mineralocorticoid receptor in the distal renal tubule. This action prevents aldosterone from promoting sodium retention and potassium excretion. As a result, sodium and water are excreted, while potassium is retained. It is both a diuretic and antihypertensive agent.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Edema (congestive heart failure, hepatic cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome):</strong> Start with 100 mg daily, adjusting between 25 to 200 mg. Combined therapy may be used for rapid diuresis.</li><li><strong>Primary hyperaldosteronism:</strong> 100 to 400 mg daily, often before surgery. For long-term management, use the lowest effective dose.</li><li><strong>Essential hypertension:</strong> Start with 50 to 100 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Hypokalemia:</strong> Dosage between 25 mg to 100 mg daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ACE inhibitors:</strong> Risk of severe hyperkalemia.</li><li><strong>Alcohol, barbiturates, narcotics:</strong> Can enhance orthostatic hypotension.</li><li><strong>Corticosteroids, ACTH:</strong> May cause increased electrolyte depletion, particularly hypokalemia.</li><li><strong>Lithium:</strong> Increases risk of lithium toxicity by reducing renal clearance.</li><li><strong>Digoxin:</strong> May prolong digoxin\'s half-life.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Acute renal insufficiency</li><li>Significant renal impairment</li><li>Anuria</li><li>Hyperkalemia</li><li>Sensitivity to Spirocard 25 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gynaecomastia</strong></li><li>GI symptoms (cramping, diarrhea)</li><li>Drowsiness, lethargy, headache</li><li>Urticaria, mental confusion</li><li>Impotence, irregular menses, amenorrhea, post-menopausal bleeding</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Should not be used during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> The active metabolite (canrenone) is excreted in breast milk. Alternative feeding methods should be considered if the drug is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor for fluid or electrolyte imbalance.</li><li>Hyperkalemia can cause fatal cardiac irregularities, especially in patients with impaired renal function or excessive potassium intake.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Symptoms:</strong></p><ul><li>Drowsiness, mental confusion, dizziness, diarrhea, and vomiting. Inducing vomiting or performing gastric lavage is recommended in case of overdose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, away from light and out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics</li><li>Aldosterone antagonists</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Spirocard 25 mg works by blocking aldosterone\'s effects on sodium and potassium transport in the kidneys, leading to sodium and water excretion while preserving potassium. This mechanism provides its diuretic and antihypertensive effects.</p>', 'active', '1517_1734532035.webp', 17, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:27:15', '2024-12-18 19:27:15'),
(338, 'Esita', 'Escitalopra', 7.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Esita 5 mg - Information</strong></p><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><p>Esita 5 mg is indicated for the treatment of the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Depressive illness</li><li>Generalized anxiety disorder</li><li>Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD)</li><li>Social anxiety disorder</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors (SSRIs) &amp; related antidepressant drugs.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Esita 5 mg functions as a selective inhibitor of serotonin (5-HT) re-uptake. This inhibition is likely the primary mechanism behind its pharmacological and clinical effects. Esita has minimal or no affinity for a variety of receptors, including:</p><ul><li>5-HT1A, 5-HT2</li><li>DA D1 and D2 receptors</li><li>α1-, α2-, β-adrenoceptors</li><li>Histamine H1</li><li>Muscarinic cholinergic</li><li>Benzodiazepine and opioid receptors</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initial dose: 10 mg once daily. After one week, the dose may be increased to 20 mg if necessary.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Panic disorder: Start with 5 mg daily, increase to 10 mg after 7 days, up to a maximum of 20 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Social anxiety disorder: Start with 10 mg daily, adjust after 2-4 weeks; usual dose: 5-20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommended dose: 10 mg/day. A lower dose may be sufficient, and higher doses should be avoided.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Contraindications:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Esita 5 mg should not be used concurrently with monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) or pimozide.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">It is also contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to Esita, citalopram, or any of its inactive ingredients.</li><li><strong>Important:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">There should be a gap of at least 2 weeks between stopping an MAOI and starting Esita, or vice versa.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Nausea, insomnia, drowsiness</li><li>Issues with ejaculation, fatigue, increased sweating</li><li>Generally, side effects are mild and often resolve with continued treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Safety during pregnancy is not established. Esita should be used only if the potential benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li><strong>Nursing mothers:</strong> Esita is excreted in breast milk and should be avoided unless the benefits outweigh the risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Discontinuation may lead to withdrawal symptoms such as mood changes, agitation, dizziness, sensory disturbances, anxiety, and insomnia. A gradual reduction in dose is advised to minimize these effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place below 30ºC, away from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness have not been established in children under 18 years.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> No significant difference in safety or effectiveness has been observed in elderly patients, but they may have increased sensitivity to the drug.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '247_1734532146.jpg', 1, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:29:06', '2024-12-18 19:29:06'),
(339, 'Ucol', 'Tolterodine Tartrate', 5.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p><p>Ucol 2 mg, which contains tolterodine, is used to treat overactive bladder, characterized by urinary urgency, frequency, and/or urge incontinence.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</p><p>It falls under the categories of BPH (Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia), urinary retention, and urinary incontinence.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><p>Tolterodine acts as a selective muscarinic receptor antagonist. It specifically targets the urinary bladder while avoiding salivary glands, as shown in studies. A major metabolite (5-hydroxymethyl derivative) of tolterodine has a similar pharmacological effect to the parent drug. The therapeutic impact is typically seen within 4 weeks.</p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Recommended Dose</strong>: 2 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Adjustment for Side Effects</strong>: If side effects are bothersome, the dose may be reduced to 1 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Impaired Function and Drug Interactions</strong>: For those with impaired liver or renal function, or those using potent CYP3A inhibitors (e.g., macrolide antibiotics like erythromycin or azole antifungals), the dose should be 1 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Duration of Treatment</strong>: After six months, re-evaluate the need for continued treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Tolterodine may interact with drugs metabolized by or inhibiting CYP2D6 and CYP3A4.</li><li>Potent CYP3A inhibitors like ketoconazole significantly raise tolterodine plasma levels, especially in poor metabolisers.</li><li>There are no significant interactions with warfarin or oral contraceptives.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><ul><li>Urinary retention, narrow-angle glaucoma, or hypersensitivity to tolterodine are contraindications.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li>Common side effects include dry mouth, dyspepsia (indigestion), and reduced lacrimation (tear production).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li>Tolterodine should only be used in pregnancy if the potential benefit outweighs the risk.</li><li>It is not recommended during lactation due to insufficient data on excretion into breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions and Warnings</strong>:</p><ul><li>Use cautiously in patients at risk for urinary retention, decreased gastrointestinal motility, or with renal or hepatic impairment.</li><li>Ensure organic causes of urinary urgency and frequency are ruled out before initiating treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li>Overdosage may lead to severe central anticholinergic effects. ECG monitoring is recommended in case of overdose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, away from light, and out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and efficacy in children have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No significant safety differences were noted between older and younger patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</p><ul><li>Tolterodine selectively antagonizes muscarinic receptors, especially in the urinary bladder, and its major active metabolite contributes significantly to its therapeutic effect.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1849_1734532260.webp', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:31:00', '2024-12-18 19:31:00'),
(340, 'Drylief', 'Carboxymethyl Cellulose', 325.00, 1, 'x 10 ml drop', '10 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Drylief 10 ml (Carboxymethylcellulose Sodium) - Product Information</strong></p><p><strong>Indications:</strong> Carboxymethylcellulose Sodium is used as an ocular lubricant for the relief of dry eye symptoms, including temporary relief of burning, irritation, and discomfort due to dryness of the eye (keratoconjunctivitis sicca).</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong> Drugs for Dry Eyes</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Carboxymethylcellulose Sodium acts as an ocular lubricant. It forms a hydrating protective layer on the surface of the eye, providing moisture and relief from irritation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>Instill one or two drops in the affected eye(s) as needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in individuals hypersensitive to any component of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Possible transient visual disturbances.</li><li>Eye discharge may occasionally occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> There are no sufficient well-controlled studies in pregnant women. The product should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefits outweigh the risks to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> There are no well-controlled studies in lactating women. Caution is advised when used by nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Carboxymethylcellulose sodium is pharmacologically inert and not expected to be absorbed systemically. Therefore, systemic effects from an overdose are unlikely. No toxic side effects are anticipated in the event of accidental systemic overdose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 25°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children:</strong> No information available.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> No specific information available.</li></ul>', 'active', '471_1734532403.webp', 29, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:33:23', '2025-04-23 10:26:54'),
(341, 'Progest', 'Dydrogesterone', 40.00, 15, 'Tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p><p>Progest 10 mg is a synthetic progestogen, used for various conditions related to progesterone deficiency and hormone replacement therapy. Below are the key indications, dosage recommendations, interactions, side effects, and precautions associated with Progest 10 mg:</p><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Progesterone Deficiencies:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Threatened miscarriage</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Habitual miscarriage</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dysmenorrhoea (painful menstruation)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Endometriosis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Secondary amenorrhoea (absence of menstruation)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Irregular cycles</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dysfunctional uterine bleeding</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Infertility due to luteal insufficiency</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Luteal support during Assisted Reproductive Technology (ART)</li><li><strong>Hormone Replacement Therapy:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Endometrial protection in women with an intact uterus undergoing hormone replacement therapy for menopause.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Female sex hormones.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Progest 10 mg is a progestogen related to progesterone. It does not increase body temperature or inhibit ovulation, making it suitable when contraceptive effects are not needed. It has no oestrogenic, androgenic, thermogenic, anabolic, or corticoid activity.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Recurrent Miscarriage:</strong> 10 mg twice daily until conception, then continuously until week 20 of pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Infertility &amp; Menstrual Disorders:</strong> 10 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Endometriosis:</strong> 10 mg twice to three times daily, either cyclically or continuously.</li><li><strong>Threatened Miscarriage:</strong> 40 mg initially, then 10 mg every 8 hours until symptoms resolve.</li><li><strong>Hormone Replacement Therapy:</strong> 10 mg once or twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Carbamazepine, griseofulvin, phenobarbital, and rifampicin can enhance the clearance of progestogens, potentially reducing effectiveness.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to the active substance.</li><li>Known or suspected progestogen-dependent neoplasms (e.g., meningioma).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include headaches, nausea, menstrual disorders, and breast tenderness.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>No evidence suggests Progest 10 mg should not be used during pregnancy.</li><li>It is excreted in breast milk, and its use during breastfeeding is not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Clarify the cause of abnormal bleeding before starting treatment.</li><li>Monitoring for conditions like severe headache, increased blood pressure, or venous thromboembolism is recommended.</li><li>In patients with a history of depression, careful monitoring is required.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Progest 10 mg is generally well-tolerated even at higher doses. In the case of a large overdose, gastric lavage may be recommended if detected early.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a dry place, protected from light, and keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p>Progest 10 mg is a key treatment for hormone imbalances and is used in multiple reproductive health scenarios. Always consult a healthcare provider for the proper regimen.</p>', 'active', '30_1734532609.webp', 17, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:36:49', '2024-12-18 19:36:49');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(342, 'Hexinor', 'Trihexyphenidyl Hydrochloride', 5.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Trihexyphenidyl (Hexinor 2 mg) is primarily used as an adjunct treatment for various forms of parkinsonism, including postencephalitic, arteriosclerotic, and idiopathic types. It is commonly employed as an additional therapy when combined with levodopa. It also aids in controlling extrapyramidal disorders induced by central nervous system drugs such as dibenzoxazepines, phenothiazines, thioxanthenes, and butyrophenones.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Trihexyphenidyl is a selective antagonist of M1 muscarinic acetylcholine receptors in the central nervous system, partially blocking cholinergic activity and alleviating symptoms of Parkinson’s disease. It may also enhance dopamine availability, which is critical for smooth and voluntary muscle control.</p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Idiopathic Parkinsonism</strong>: Start with 1 mg on the first day, and gradually increase by 2 mg every 3–5 days, aiming for a daily dose of 6–10 mg. Some patients may require up to 15 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Drug-Induced Parkinsonism</strong>: Start with 1 mg and increase the dose to 5–15 mg as needed.</li><li><strong>Concomitant Use with Levodopa</strong>: The typical dose is 3–6 mg daily, divided into doses.</li><li><strong>Dosage for elderly or sensitive patients</strong>: Start low and adjust slowly, especially for those over 60 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Minor: Dry mouth, blurred vision, dizziness, mild nausea, or nervousness.</li><li>Severe: Cognitive dysfunction, confusion, memory impairment, tachycardia, urinary retention, hyperthermia, and potential neuroleptic malignant syndrome with abrupt withdrawal.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to trihexyphenidyl.</li><li>Narrow-angle glaucoma.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor intraocular pressure before starting therapy, as it can precipitate narrow-angle glaucoma.</li><li>Caution is required in hot weather and with other anticholinergics, as anhidrosis (impaired sweating) may lead to hyperthermia.</li><li>Abrupt discontinuation may cause a severe exacerbation of Parkinsonism or neuroleptic malignant syndrome.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms include dry skin, tachycardia, elevated temperature, hallucinations, seizures, and potential coma or death.</li><li>Treatment is supportive, including gastric lavage, sedation with diazepam, and other measures to control symptoms like hyperthermia.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place at temperatures not exceeding 30°C, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Note</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established. Trihexyphenidyl should be used during pregnancy only if the benefit outweighs the risk. It is unknown whether it is excreted in human milk.</p>', 'active', '779_1734532768.webp', 55, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:39:28', '2024-12-18 19:39:28'),
(343, 'Duralax', 'Bisacodyl', 0.71, 20, 'Tablet', '20 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p><p><strong>Duralax 5 mg: Information Summary</strong></p><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment of constipation.</li><li>Preparation for radiographic or endoscopic examination of the colon.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Stimulant purgatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Duralax 5 mg stimulates enteric nerves, causing colonic contractions and increasing fluid and salt secretion, primarily affecting the colon. Its action on the small intestine is negligible. It promotes evacuation of the colon.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>For Constipation:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 1-2 tablets (5-10 mg) daily; relief in 6-12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric (under 10 years)</strong>: 1 tablet (5 mg) or 1 suppository (5 mg) daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppositories</strong>: 1 suppository (10 mg) daily for adults, relief in 15-30 minutes.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>For Bowel Preparation</strong> (e.g., before radiographic exams):</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults (&gt;10 years)</strong>: 2-4 tablets the night before and 1 adult suppository on the day of the procedure.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (&lt;10 years)</strong>: 1 tablet the night before and 1 pediatric suppository on the day of the procedure.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid antacids or milk within 1 hour of taking Duralax 5 mg (due to its enteric coating).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Ileus, intestinal obstruction, acute abdominal conditions (e.g., appendicitis), acute inflammatory bowel diseases, severe dehydration.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Rare when used at recommended doses.</li><li>Overuse may result in diarrhea, malabsorption, and potassium depletion.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy Category B: Animal studies show no teratogenicity or fetotoxicity. Use only if clearly needed.</li><li>Unknown if excreted in human milk; exercise caution when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Overuse may cause fluid and electrolyte imbalance or dependence.</li><li>Chronic use can lead to dependency.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Diarrhea, dehydration, and potential electrolyte imbalances. Accidental overdoses are common in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Stimulant purgatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Duralax 5 mg, a diphenolic compound similar to phenolphthalein, stimulates peristalsis in the colon and increases intraluminal water and electrolytes, promoting bowel movement.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1055_1734532918.webp', 7, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:41:58', '2024-12-18 19:41:58'),
(344, 'Informet', 'Metformin Hydrochloride', 3.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Informet 500 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment of <strong>type 2 diabetes mellitus</strong>, particularly in overweight patients when dietary management and exercise alone do not result in adequate glycemic control.</li><li>Can be used as <strong>monotherapy</strong> or in combination with other oral antidiabetic agents or with insulin.</li><li>In children from <strong>10 years of age</strong> and adolescents, it may be used as monotherapy or in combination with insulin.</li><li>Demonstrated to reduce diabetic complications in overweight type 2 diabetic adult patients when used as first-line therapy after diet failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Biguanides</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Metformin</strong> is an oral antihyperglycemic agent that works by lowering both <strong>basal</strong> and <strong>postprandial plasma glucose</strong>.</li><li>It acts by:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Decreasing hepatic glucose production</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Decreasing intestinal glucose absorption</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Improving insulin sensitivity</strong> through increased peripheral glucose uptake and utilization.</li><li>Does not produce <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>, unlike sulfonylureas.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult Dosage</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initial dose: <strong>500 mg twice daily</strong> or <strong>850 mg once daily</strong>, taken with meals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dose can be increased in increments of <strong>500 mg weekly</strong> or <strong>850 mg every 2 weeks</strong>, up to a maximum of <strong>2000 mg per day</strong>, in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For additional control, dose may be increased to <strong>2550 mg per day</strong>.</li><li><strong>Child Dosage</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initial dose: <strong>500 mg twice daily</strong>, taken with meals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dose can be increased weekly by <strong>500 mg</strong> up to a maximum of <strong>2000 mg per day</strong>, in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not use if eGFR is below <strong>30 mL/min/1.73 m²</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If eGFR falls below <strong>45 mL/min/1.73 m²</strong>, assess the risk/benefit.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction:</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs that <strong>reduce Metformin clearance</strong> (e.g., Ranolazine, Dolutegravir, Cimetidine) may increase Metformin accumulation.</li><li><strong>Alcohol</strong> can enhance Metformin’s effect on lactate metabolism, increasing the risk of lactic acidosis.</li><li><strong>Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors</strong> (e.g., Topiramate, Zonisamide) may increase the risk of lactic acidosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Metformin or any excipients.</li><li><strong>Acute metabolic acidosis</strong>, including <strong>lactic acidosis</strong> and <strong>diabetic ketoacidosis</strong>.</li><li>Severe <strong>renal failure</strong> (GFR &lt;30 mL/min).</li><li>Conditions that may alter renal function (e.g., dehydration, severe infection, shock).</li><li>Diseases causing <strong>tissue hypoxia</strong> (e.g., cardiac failure, recent myocardial infarction, shock).</li><li><strong>Hepatic insufficiency</strong>, <strong>acute alcohol intoxication</strong>, <strong>alcoholism</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Very Common</strong>: Gastrointestinal issues (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, loss of appetite).</li><li><strong>Common</strong>: Taste disturbance.</li><li><strong>Very Rare</strong>: Liver abnormalities, hepatitis, skin reactions (erythema, pruritus, urticaria).</li><li><strong>Very Rare</strong>: Lactic acidosis, vitamin B12 deficiency (may lead to megaloblastic anemia and peripheral neuropathy).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: No clear association with major birth defects or adverse maternal outcomes when used during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Metformin is excreted in breast milk. It is generally considered safe, but a decision must be made to either <strong>discontinue breastfeeding</strong> or use the drug, based on the benefit-risk assessment.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor renal function as Metformin is excreted by the kidneys and its accumulation increases the risk of <strong>lactic acidosis</strong>.</li><li>May <strong>lower vitamin B12 levels</strong>, particularly with long-term use.</li><li>Increased risk of <strong>hypoglycemia</strong> when used with insulin or insulin secretagogues.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong> is not typically seen with Metformin overdoses up to 85 gm, but <strong>lactic acidosis</strong> may occur.</li><li><strong>Lactic acidosis</strong> is a medical emergency and requires <strong>hospital treatment</strong>, often requiring <strong>hemodialysis</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below <strong>30°C</strong>, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Biguanides</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Metformin</strong> reduces blood glucose by:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Decreasing hepatic glucose production</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Decreasing glucose absorption</strong> from the intestine.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Improving insulin sensitivity</strong>, leading to better peripheral glucose utilization.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1524_1734533101.png', 3, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:45:01', '2024-12-18 19:45:01'),
(345, 'Reelife', 'Amitriptyline + Chlordiazepoxide', 8.00, 14, 'Tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '12.5 mg+5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Reelife 12.5 mg + 5 mg (Amitriptyline &amp; Chlordiazepoxide)</strong></p><p><strong>Indications:</strong> Reelife 12.5 mg + 5 mg is indicated for the treatment of moderate to severe depression associated with moderate to severe anxiety. This combination therapy provides earlier therapeutic responses and fewer treatment failures than when either amitriptyline or chlordiazepoxide is used alone. Symptoms that may improve within the first week include insomnia, feelings of guilt or worthlessness, agitation, anxiety (both psychic and somatic), suicidal ideation, and anorexia.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong> Combined anxiolytics &amp; anti-depressant drugs.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Amitriptyline</strong>: A tricyclic antidepressant that inhibits the reuptake of norepinephrine and serotonin, increasing their concentration in the synaptic cleft, improving mood and alleviating depression. This effect aligns with the monoamine hypothesis, which suggests that deficiencies in serotonin and norepinephrine neurotransmission lead to depressive symptoms.</li><li><strong>Chlordiazepoxide</strong>: A benzodiazepine that enhances GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) activity by binding to benzodiazepine receptors, which increases chloride influx, leading to CNS inhibition and providing sedative, anxiolytic, and muscle relaxant effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial Dose</strong>: 3-4 tablets daily, which may be increased to 6 tablets daily as required.</li><li><strong>Maintenance Dose</strong>: Some patients may respond to lower doses and can maintain on 2 tablets daily.</li><li><strong>Dosing Adjustment</strong>: Once satisfactory symptom improvement is achieved, the dosage should be reduced to the lowest effective dose. The larger portion of the daily dose may be taken at bedtime, or a single bedtime dose may suffice.</li><li><strong>Elderly patients</strong>: Typically require lower dosages.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>May interfere with the antihypertensive action of guanethidine.</li><li>Caution should be used when co-administering with SSRIs or switching between tricyclic antidepressants and SSRIs, particularly with fluoxetine (a 5-week gap may be needed).</li><li>Drugs that inhibit cytochrome P450 2D6 may require dose adjustments.</li><li><strong>Cimetidine</strong> can increase the concentration of tricyclic antidepressants and benzodiazepines.</li><li>Discontinue the combination several days before elective surgery.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to either benzodiazepines or tricyclic antidepressants.</li><li>Co-administration with monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs). A minimum of 14 days should elapse before starting this combination after discontinuing an MAOI.</li><li>Not recommended in the acute recovery phase following myocardial infarction.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Most common: Drowsiness, dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision, dizziness, and bloating.</li><li>Less common: Vivid dreams, impotence, tremor, confusion, nasal congestion.</li><li>Depressive symptoms like anorexia, fatigue, weakness, restlessness, and lethargy may also occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong> Preferably avoid use during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Antidepressants may worsen depression and increase suicidality, especially in children and teenagers.</li><li>Caution in patients with a history of seizures, hyperthyroidism, thyroid medication use, or impaired renal or hepatic function.</li><li>Periodic liver function tests and blood counts are recommended during prolonged treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose can be fatal, especially when combined with alcohol.</li><li>Symptoms include cardiac dysrhythmias, severe hypotension, convulsions, CNS depression, coma, and changes in the electrocardiogram (particularly in QRS axis or width).</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place below 30°C, protected from light.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatrics</strong>: Excreted via the kidney, so elderly patients with renal impairment are at higher risk for toxicity. Dose selection should start low and be adjusted based on tolerance and renal function.</li></ul><p><br></p><p><br></p>', 'active', '1980_1734533235.png', 53, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:47:15', '2024-12-18 19:47:15'),
(346, 'Histacin', 'Chlorpheniramine Maleate', 0.30, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '4 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Histacin 4 mg - Product Overview</strong></p><p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Histacin 4 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Urticaria (hives)</li><li>Sensitivity reactions</li><li>Angioneurotic edema (swelling beneath the skin)</li><li>Vasomotor rhinitis (allergic rhinitis)</li><li>Cough</li><li>Common cold</li><li>Motion sickness</li><li>Other allergic conditions</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Sedating Anti-histamine</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Histacin 4 mg contains Chlorpheniramine, an alkylamine antihistamine that is a potent H1 receptor antagonist. It is effective at relatively low doses and has a lower tendency to cause drowsiness compared to other antihistamines. Chlorpheniramine is well-absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract, metabolized in the liver, and mainly excreted through urine in its metabolized form.</p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 4 mg, 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Up to 1 year: 1 mg, twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-5 years: 1 mg, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years: 2 mg, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maximum adult dose</strong>: 24 mg daily</li><li><strong>Maximum child doses</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">6-12 years: 12 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">2-5 years: 6 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-2 years: 2 mg daily</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><p>Chlorpheniramine may be incompatible with:</p><ul><li>Calcium chloride</li><li>Kanamycin sulfate</li><li>Noradrenaline acid tartrate</li><li>Pentobarbital sodium</li><li>Meglumine adipiodone</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Chlorpheniramine is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients hypersensitive to the drug</li><li>Newborns or premature infants</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Chlorpheniramine is generally well tolerated but may cause:</p><ul><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Muscular weakness</li><li>Gastrointestinal upset</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: Animal studies have not shown fetal risk, but there are no controlled studies in pregnant women. Chlorpheniramine should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Chlorpheniramine may pass into breast milk. Caution is advised when administering to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions and Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with glaucoma or prostatic hypertrophy.</li><li>Patients should be cautious while driving or operating machinery during therapy due to potential drowsiness.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>CNS depression (sedation, apnea, cardiovascular collapse)</li><li>CNS stimulation (insomnia, hallucinations, tremors, convulsions)</li><li>Tinnitus, blurred vision, dizziness, ataxia, hypotension</li><li>Atropine-like symptoms (dry mouth, fixed dilated pupils, flushing, hyperthermia, gastrointestinal symptoms) are more likely in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place below 25°C, protected from light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Sedating Anti-histamine</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Histacin 4 mg blocks histamine H1 receptors competitively, which helps relieve allergic symptoms like itching, swelling, and runny nose. Compared to other antihistamines like promethazine, it typically causes less sedation.</p>', 'active', '1310_1734533341.webp', 28, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:49:01', '2024-12-18 19:49:01'),
(347, 'Amilin', 'Amitriptyline hydrochloride', 0.85, 14, 'Tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Amilin 10 mg (Amitriptyline)</strong></p><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Depressive Illness:</strong> Particularly effective where sedation is required.</li><li><strong>Nocturnal Enuresis in Children:</strong> Enuresis treatment in children aged 6 years and older.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of Migraine:</strong> Prevents migraine attacks.</li><li><strong>Tension Headache:</strong> Helps alleviate tension-type headaches.</li><li><strong>Chronic Pain:</strong> For managing various chronic pain conditions.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Tricyclic Antidepressant (TCA)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Amitriptyline is an antidepressant with sedative properties. Its exact mechanism of action is unclear, but it works by inhibiting the reuptake of norepinephrine and serotonin in neurons, potentially enhancing their activity. This action is believed to contribute to its antidepressant effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Depression:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: Initially 50-70 mg per day, in divided doses or as a single dose at night before bed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Elderly &amp; Adolescents: 25-50 mg daily in divided doses or as a single bedtime dose. Gradually increase as needed up to 150-200 mg. Maintenance dose is typically 50-100 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child under 16 years: Not recommended.</li><li><strong>Nocturnal Enuresis:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-10 years: 10-20 mg at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">11-16 years: 25-50 mg at bedtime for up to 3 months, then gradually withdrawn.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of Migraine:</strong> 100 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Tension Headache:</strong> 10-25 mg, three times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Alcohol &amp; Opioid Analgesics:</strong> Enhance sedative effects.</li><li><strong>Moxifloxacin/Terfenadine:</strong> Increases risk of ventricular arrhythmias.</li><li><strong>Disulfiram &amp; Cimetidine:</strong> Inhibit Amitriptyline metabolism.</li><li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> Increased risk of postural hypotension.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Not recommended for use in patients with recent myocardial infarction, arrhythmias (particularly heart block), mania, or severe liver disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Anticholinergic:</strong> Dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention, excessive sweating, hyperpyrexia.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular:</strong> Hypotension, syncope, tachycardia, palpitations, myocardial infarction.</li><li><strong>CNS &amp; Neuromuscular:</strong> Confusion, disturbed concentration, delusions.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Nausea, vomiting, anorexia, constipation, weight loss.</li><li><strong>Endocrine:</strong> Testicular swelling, breast enlargement, galactorrhoea.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy Category C. Use is not recommended during pregnancy, especially in the first and third trimesters due to insufficient safety data.</li><li>Amitriptyline is detectable in breast milk. Consider discontinuing breastfeeding or the drug, depending on the situation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in patients with a history of epilepsy, glaucoma, urinary retention, cardiac disease, diabetes, hepatic impairment, thyroid disease, or psychoses (can aggravate mania).</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Amitriptyline inhibits the reuptake of serotonin and norepinephrine, increasing their concentrations in the brain, which helps to alleviate depressive symptoms. It may also have an analgesic effect, although its exact mechanism for this is unclear.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1411_1734533457.png', 7, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:50:57', '2024-12-18 19:50:57'),
(348, 'Telma', 'Telmisartan', 12.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p><p>Here\'s a rephrased version of the information about Telma 40 mg, avoiding direct copying:</p><p><strong>Indications of Telma 40 mg</strong></p><p>Telma 40 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: Management of essential hypertension in adults.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular prevention</strong>: To reduce cardiovascular events in adults with a history of atherothrombotic cardiovascular diseases (e.g., coronary heart disease, stroke, peripheral arterial disease) or those with Type 2 diabetes mellitus and documented organ damage.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB).</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Angiotensin II is produced from angiotensin I through the action of angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE). Angiotensin II plays a key role in regulating blood pressure, stimulating aldosterone release, and causing vasoconstriction. Telma 40 mg blocks the action of angiotensin II by selectively inhibiting its binding to the AT1 receptor, found in tissues like vascular smooth muscle and the adrenal gland. Unlike ACE inhibitors, Telma does not interfere with ACE and does not affect bradykinin levels. While blocking the angiotensin II receptor, it also prevents renin feedback, resulting in continued blood pressure lowering despite higher renin and angiotensin II levels.</p><p><strong>Dosage</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Starting dose</strong>: 40 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Blood pressure control</strong>: Dose increases (up to 80 mg/day) for further control, with maximum effect typically seen within 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Renal and elderly considerations</strong>: No initial dose adjustment for renal impairment or elderly patients, though dialysis patients should be closely monitored for orthostatic hypotension.</li><li><strong>Combination therapy</strong>: Can be used with other antihypertensive medications.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Telma 40 mg can be taken with or without food and may be combined with other antihypertensive agents.</p><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>NSAIDs</strong>: Increased risk of renal impairment and diminished antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>Aliskiren</strong>: Not recommended for co-administration in diabetic patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Telma 40 mg or its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension treatment</strong>: Sinus pain, congestion (sinusitis), back pain, and diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular prevention</strong>: Intermittent claudication, skin ulceration.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C for the first trimester. Telma 40 mg should be discontinued if pregnancy occurs. It may cause fetal harm in later stages.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: The drug\'s excretion in breast milk is unknown; nursing should be avoided or Telma 40 mg discontinued.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor for hypotension and renal or hepatic dysfunction.</li><li>Avoid concurrent use with ACE inhibitors or angiotensin receptor blockers.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>Signs of overdose may include hypotension, dizziness, tachycardia, bradycardia, increased serum creatinine, and acute renal failure.</p><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: For severe cases or hemodialysis, start with 20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: Use up to 40 mg daily for mild to moderate impairment. Contraindicated for severe impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Angiotensin II receptor blocker.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Telma 40 mg selectively blocks the AT1 receptor, inhibiting the vasoconstrictor and aldosterone-secreting effects of angiotensin II, without affecting ACE or bradykinin pathways. This selective action helps control blood pressure without affecting other hormone systems.</p><p>Let me know if you need any further revisions or clarifications!</p>', 'active', '1007_1734533577.jpg', 3, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 19:52:57', '2024-12-18 19:52:57'),
(349, 'Tearfresh Liquigel', 'Carboxymethyl Cellulose', 375.00, 1, 'x 15 ml drop', '15 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Tearfresh Liquigel 1% is used to alleviate symptoms of dry eye (keratoconjunctivitis sicca), such as burning, irritation, and discomfort caused by ocular dryness.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for Dry Eyes.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Carboxymethylcellulose Sodium acts as an ocular lubricant, forming a protective and hydrating layer on the ocular surface.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li>Instill 1–2 drops into the affected eye(s) as needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Not to be used by individuals with known hypersensitivity to any ingredients in the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>May cause transient visual disturbances.</li><li>Eye discharge may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Use During Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Insufficient studies exist. Use only if the potential benefits outweigh risks to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Limited data is available. Administer with caution in nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Information</strong></p><ul><li>Systemic absorption of Carboxymethylcellulose Sodium is minimal, making systemic effects from an overdose unlikely. Accidental systemic overdose is not expected to cause toxic effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep at temperatures below 25°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: No specific information is available.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Limited information available.</li></ul><p>This information is provided for educational purposes and should not replace advice from healthcare professionals.</p>', 'active', '1073_1746527592.webp', 37, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 22:16:53', '2025-05-06 14:33:21'),
(350, 'Duopres', 'Amlodipine + Olmesartan Medoxomil', 9.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Duopres 5 mg+20 mg is used to treat hypertension. It may be prescribed alone or in combination with other antihypertensive agents. It can also serve as initial therapy for patients requiring multiple agents to manage blood pressure effectively.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Combined antihypertensive preparations.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Amlodipine</strong>: A calcium channel blocker that reduces calcium influx into vascular and cardiac muscle cells. It acts as a peripheral arterial vasodilator, reducing vascular resistance and blood pressure.</li><li><strong>Olmesartan</strong>: An angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB) that inhibits the AT1 subtype, resulting in vasodilation and blood pressure reduction without affecting pulse rate.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial Therapy</strong>: Begin with one tablet (5/20 mg) once daily. If required, increase dosage after 1–2 weeks to a maximum of two tablets (10/40 mg) once daily. Can be taken with or without food.</li><li><strong>Replacement Therapy</strong>: Substitutes for separately titrated components, allowing dose adjustments for better blood pressure control.</li><li><strong>Add-on Therapy</strong>: Used alongside amlodipine or olmesartan alone for patients needing additional blood pressure reduction.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Notes</strong>:</p><ul><li>Not recommended as initial therapy for patients ≥75 years old or those with hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>No pharmacokinetic alterations observed when amlodipine and olmesartan are co-administered.</li><li>Interaction studies specific to this combination tablet are limited, but the individual components have not demonstrated significant interactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the active or inactive components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common adverse effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Frequent</strong>: Edema, dizziness, flushing, palpitations.</li><li><strong>Others</strong>: Vomiting, diarrhea, alopecia, urticaria, pruritus, and rarely, rhabdomyolysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Use During Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Discontinue immediately upon detection. Drugs affecting the renin-angiotensin system can harm the fetus, particularly during the second and third trimesters.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Potential adverse effects on the nursing infant. Assess the risks and benefits of continuing the drug or breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Warnings and Precautions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Fetal/Neonatal Risks</strong>: Cease use if pregnancy occurs.</li><li><strong>Hypotension</strong>: Monitor volume or salt-depleted patients closely.</li><li><strong>Vasodilation Effects</strong>: Use with caution in patients with severe aortic stenosis.</li><li><strong>Coronary Artery Disease</strong>: Risk of angina or myocardial infarction when starting or increasing dosage.</li><li><strong>Heart Failure</strong>: Use calcium channel blockers cautiously in these patients.</li><li><strong>Renal and Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Adjustments may be necessary; monitor function.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Olmesartan</strong>: Potential hypotension, tachycardia, or bradycardia.</li><li><strong>Amlodipine</strong>: May cause excessive vasodilation, hypotension, or reflex tachycardia. Severe cases can lead to prolonged hypotension or shock.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Instructions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C in original packaging, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Ensure it is out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>The safety and efficacy in pediatric patients remain unestablished.</li></ul><p>This information should be used as a guide only and not as a substitute for professional medical advice.</p>', 'active', '890_1734542558.png', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 22:22:38', '2024-12-18 22:22:38'),
(351, 'Frulac', 'Furosemide + Spironolactone', 9.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg + 50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>This combination is beneficial for treating conditions such as:</p><ul><li>Essential hypertension.</li><li>Chronic congestive heart failure.</li><li>Hepatic cirrhosis with fluid accumulation (ascites).</li><li>Generalized swelling (edema) due to fluid retention.</li><li>Hyperaldosteronism.</li><li>Resistant edema linked to secondary hyperaldosteronism.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Frusemide (a loop diuretic) and Spironolactone (a potassium-sparing diuretic) work synergistically to enhance diuretic efficacy.</p><ul><li><strong>Frusemide</strong> acts in the ascending loop of Henle by inhibiting the Na+/K+/2Cl- co-transporter, increasing the excretion of sodium, potassium, and chloride, leading to water loss. However, it can cause potassium depletion.</li><li><strong>Spironolactone</strong> counters this by acting at the distal tubule, blocking aldosterone’s effects. This reduces sodium reabsorption and conserves potassium, minimizing the risk of hypokalemia.</li></ul><p>Together, they promote effective fluid elimination while maintaining electrolyte balance.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Start with 1–4 tablets daily (20–80 mg Frusemide and 50–200 mg Spironolactone) based on patient response.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In stabilized cases needing higher doses, 40 mg Frusemide + 50 mg Spironolactone tablets may be administered, with a daily dose of 1–2 tablets.</li><li><strong>Special Populations</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly</strong>: Adjust dosage carefully due to slower drug clearance.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: Not recommended for children.</li></ul><p><strong>Potential Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hyperkalemia Risk</strong>: With ACE inhibitors or potassium supplements.</li><li><strong>Increased Digoxin Toxicity</strong>: Due to elevated digoxin levels with Spironolactone.</li><li><strong>Reduced Efficacy</strong>: Furosemide’s effects may diminish with NSAIDs or sucralfate.</li><li><strong>Enhanced Ototoxicity</strong>: When used with aminoglycosides.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Avoid use in:</p><ul><li>Severe renal dysfunction (e.g., creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min).</li><li>Hyperkalemia or Addison\'s disease.</li><li>Hypersensitivity to the active ingredients or related sulfonamides.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Headaches, gastrointestinal upset, drowsiness, and mild electrolyte imbalances.</li><li><strong>Endocrine Issues</strong>: Breast enlargement, menstrual irregularities, and voice changes.</li><li><strong>Severe</strong>: Dehydration, hypotension, or cardiac arrhythmias due to excessive potassium and magnesium depletion.</li></ul><p><strong>Use During Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Use with caution; Frusemide may carry risks of fetal abnormalities, while Spironolactone can cross the placenta.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Avoid breastfeeding due to excretion of both drugs in milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><ul><li>Maintain below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><p>Monitor for:</p><ul><li>Electrolyte imbalances, particularly in at-risk populations (e.g., diabetics, elderly).</li><li>Volume depletion and associated complications, especially in hypotensive or hypovolemic individuals.</li></ul><p>This document provides a comprehensive overview of the Frusemide and Spironolactone combination, ensuring clarity and precision without directly copying the source material.</p>', 'active', '1493_1742317213.jpg', 43, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 22:24:38', '2025-03-18 22:00:13'),
(352, 'Xyril', 'Hydroxyzine Hydrochloride', 2.30, 20, 'Tablet', '20 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Xyril 25 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Provides symptomatic relief of anxiety and tension associated with psychoneurosis.</li><li>Serves as an adjunct treatment in organic disease states where anxiety is present.</li><li>Effective for managing pruritus linked to allergic conditions, including chronic urticaria, atopic and contact dermatitis, and histamine-mediated itching.</li><li>Acts as a sedative for premedication and post-anesthesia, with adjustments required for concurrent use with drugs like meperidine and barbiturates.</li><li>Not known to interfere with digitalis action, permitting concurrent usage.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Sedating Antihistamine</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Hydroxyzine, a piperazine derivative, exerts its effects by blocking histamine H1-receptors in the gastrointestinal tract, blood vessels, and respiratory system. It has sedative, antimuscarinic, bronchodilator, skeletal muscle relaxant, antiemetic, and analgesic properties.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Xyril 25 mg</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Anxiety and tension</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 50–100 mg, 4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children under 6 years: 50 mg daily in divided doses</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children over 6 years: 50–100 mg daily in divided doses</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Pruritus</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 25 mg, 3–4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children under 6 years: 50 mg daily in divided doses</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children over 6 years: 50–100 mg daily in divided doses</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Premedication and post-anesthesia sedation</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 50–100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children: 0.6 mg/kg of body weight</li></ul><p>For intramuscular initiation, subsequent doses can be administered orally. Dosage adjustments are based on individual response.</p><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><ul><li>May be taken with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Potentiates CNS depressants such as opiates, barbiturates, sedatives, and anesthetics.</li><li>MAO inhibitors and tricyclic antidepressants may increase antimuscarinic effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Not to be used in early pregnancy due to potential teratogenic effects observed in animal studies.</li><li>Contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to hydroxyzine.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Dry mouth, drowsiness (usually transient).</li><li>Rare: Tremors, convulsions (with high doses).</li><li>No significant respiratory depression at recommended doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C – Use only if potential benefits justify the risks.</li><li><strong>Nursing</strong>: Use caution as excretion in breast milk is unknown.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in conditions like porphyria, glaucoma, bladder outflow obstruction, decreased gastrointestinal motility, myasthenia gravis, dementia, or cardiac arrhythmias.</li><li>Avoid subcutaneous, intravenous, or intra-arterial injection.</li><li>Adjust dosage for renal and hepatic impairments.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Management</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Excessive sedation, rare hypotension.</li><li>Treatment: Supportive care, gastric lavage, or induced emesis. Administer IV fluids and vasopressors (e.g., norepinephrine) for hypotension.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><ul><li>Store at 15–30°C, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Reduce dose by 50%.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Reduce daily dose by 33%.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Initiate at lower doses due to higher susceptibility to sedative effects. Monitor renal function and adjust dosing as necessary.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '509_1734542791.png', 7, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 22:26:31', '2024-12-18 22:26:31'),
(353, 'Ketoalfa', 'Amino acid keto analogues', 40.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Ketoalfa 600 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Ketoalfa 600 mg</strong> is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Prevention and treatment</strong> of protein metabolism-related damages in chronic kidney disease (CKD).</li><li>Patients with a <strong>glomerular filtration rate (GFR)</strong> of less than 25 mL/min.</li><li>Used in conjunction with a <strong>restricted protein diet</strong> (40 g/day or less in adults).</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each <strong>film-coated tablet</strong> contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Isoleucine USP</strong>: 67 mg</li><li><strong>Leucine USP</strong>: 101 mg</li><li><strong>Phenylalanine USP</strong>: 68 mg</li><li><strong>Valine USP</strong>: 86 mg</li><li><strong>Methionine USP</strong>: 59 mg</li><li><strong>Lysine Hydrochloride USP</strong>: 93.705 mg (equivalent to Lysine 75 mg)</li><li><strong>Threonine USP</strong>: 53 mg</li><li><strong>Tryptophan USP</strong>: 23 mg</li><li><strong>Histidine USP</strong>: 38 mg</li><li><strong>Tyrosine USP</strong>: 30 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Adults (70 kg body weight):</strong></p><ul><li>4 to 8 tablets, <strong>three times daily</strong>, during meals.</li><li>Tablets must not be chewed; taking them with meals aids absorption and conversion to amino acids.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric use:</strong></p><ul><li>No established experience or data for administration in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calcium-containing drugs:</strong> May exacerbate elevated serum calcium levels.</li><li>Drugs forming insoluble compounds with calcium (e.g., tetracyclines, ciprofloxacin, norfloxacin, iron, fluoride, estramustine) should not be co-administered simultaneously. Maintain at least a <strong>two-hour interval</strong> between these medications.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to any active ingredients or excipients.</li><li><strong>Hypercalcaemia.</strong></li><li><strong>Disturbed amino acid metabolism.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypercalcaemia (very rare):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If hypercalcaemia develops, reduce vitamin D intake and other calcium sources.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Regular monitoring of <strong>serum calcium levels</strong> is recommended.</li><li>Ensure an adequate <strong>calorie intake</strong> to avoid malnutrition.</li><li>In cases of hereditary <strong>phenylketonuria</strong>, note that the product contains phenylalanine.</li><li>Monitor <strong>serum phosphate levels</strong> when co-administered with aluminum hydroxide.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong> in a dry place.</li><li>Protect from <strong>light and moisture.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><ul><li>Classified under <strong>Other genito-urinary preparations.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p><strong>Ketoalfa 600 mg</strong> is used for nutritional therapy in chronic kidney disease:</p><ul><li>Supplies <strong>essential amino acids</strong> while minimizing nitrogen intake.</li><li>Reduces nitrogen load and mitigates complications of a low-protein diet.</li><li>Helps prevent protein malnutrition and maintain proper metabolism in CKD patients.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1339_1734542933.png', 1, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 22:28:53', '2024-12-18 22:28:53');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(354, 'Reticap', 'Isotretinoin', 40.00, 10, 'Capsul', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Reticap 20 mg</strong></p><p>Reticap 20 mg is primarily prescribed for managing severe, stubborn nodular acne. Nodules are inflammatory skin lesions typically 5 mm or larger, which can become suppurative or hemorrhagic. Due to the potential for significant side effects, Reticap 20 mg is intended for patients with severe nodular acne who have not responded to conventional treatments, including systemic antibiotics. The medication is contraindicated for use in pregnant women due to the high risk of severe birth defects.</p><p>A typical course of treatment spans 15 to 20 weeks, often achieving prolonged remission. A second course may be considered after a minimum interval of 8 weeks, as improvement can continue after stopping the initial treatment. The timing for re-treatment is uncertain in patients whose skeletal growth is incomplete.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>This medication falls under <strong>Oral Retinoids for Acne</strong>.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Mechanism of Action</strong></p><p>Reticap 20 mg is a synthetic retinoid that works by reducing the size of sebaceous glands and limiting sebum production. It also regulates cell growth and differentiation while providing an anti-inflammatory effect on the skin.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral Administration (Adults)</strong>: The starting dose is typically 0.5 mg/kg daily, administered as a single dose or split into two doses. If necessary, the dose can be increased to 1 mg/kg daily. The usual treatment duration is 16-24 weeks, with a second course possible after an 8-week break if needed.</li><li><strong>Children (≥12 years)</strong>: The dosage is the same as for adults.</li><li><strong>Topical Use</strong>: Apply a 0.05% gel to the affected area once or twice daily. A therapeutic effect may take 6-8 weeks to become evident.</li></ul><p>The medication has not been proven safe or effective in children under 12 or elderly populations, as these age groups typically do not present with acne vulgaris.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Increased Adverse Effects</strong>: When combined with vitamin A or its derivatives.</li><li><strong>Reduced Effectiveness</strong>: Microdosed progesterone (consider using two contraceptive methods).</li><li><strong>Potential Local Irritation</strong>: With topical keratolytic or exfoliative agents.</li><li><strong>Reduced Topical Efficacy</strong>: When used alongside oxidizing agents like benzoyl peroxide.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Conditions such as hypervitaminosis A, hyperlipidemia, and hepatic impairment.</li><li>Pregnancy and breastfeeding.</li><li>Concurrent use of tetracyclines.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Reported side effects include:</p><ul><li>Skin reactions: Erythema, dryness, exfoliation, photosensitivity, and irritation.</li><li>Systemic symptoms: Headache, nausea, vomiting, muscle or joint pain, and elevated intracranial pressure.</li><li>Metabolic effects: Elevated triglycerides, liver enzymes, blood glucose, and ESR.</li><li>Psychiatric effects: Depression, mood changes, and suicidal tendencies.</li><li>Miscellaneous: Corneal opacities, visual disturbances, and skeletal abnormalities.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></p><p><strong>Category X</strong>: Evidence from studies shows a clear risk of severe fetal abnormalities. The medication is strictly contraindicated for pregnant women or those who may become pregnant.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Precautions and Warnings</strong></p><p>Use with caution in:</p><ul><li>Women of childbearing age (strict contraception measures needed).</li><li>Patients with psychiatric disorders, hypertriglyceridemia, or metabolic conditions like diabetes and obesity.</li><li>Individuals with a history of bone metabolism disorders or osteoporosis.</li></ul><p>Not suitable for prepubertal acne treatment or individuals with severe renal impairment without dose adjustment.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Storage Instructions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature between 20-25°C, protected from light.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Start with a lower oral dose (e.g., 10 mg daily) and increase as needed.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Oral use is contraindicated.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '8327_1746505119.webp', 8, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 22:32:02', '2025-05-06 08:18:39'),
(355, 'Alphapress XR', 'Prazosin Hydrochloride', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Used for managing all grades of primary (essential) and secondary hypertension, regardless of etiology.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Can be prescribed as a standalone treatment or in combination with diuretics and/or other antihypertensive medications.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Safe for use in hypertensive patients with impaired renal function as it does not compromise renal blood flow or glomerular filtration rate.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Left Ventricular Failure</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acts as an adjunct therapy for patients not responding adequately to conventional treatments like diuretics or cardiac glycosides.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Raynaud’s Phenomenon and Disease</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Reduces the severity, frequency, and duration of symptoms by improving peripheral blood flow.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH)</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Alleviates urinary symptoms by relaxing the smooth muscles of the prostate and urethra.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Also serves as a temporary measure for patients awaiting prostate surgery.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Classified under <strong>alpha-adrenoceptor blocking agents</strong>, targeting alpha-1 receptors.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacological Mechanism</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Blood Pressure Control</strong>: Reduces peripheral vascular resistance by selectively inhibiting post-synaptic alpha-1 adrenergic receptors. This action lowers blood pressure without triggering rebound hypertension upon discontinuation.</li><li><strong>Congestive Heart Failure (CHF)</strong>: Enhances cardiac output by lowering left ventricular filling pressure and reducing cardiac impedance without causing reflex tachycardia.</li><li><strong>Raynaud’s Disease</strong>: Improves symptoms by relaxing peripheral vasculature.</li><li><strong>BPH</strong>: Improves urinary flow through alpha-1 receptor antagonism in the prostate and urethra, relieving obstruction.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: Start with low doses (0.5 mg at bedtime). Gradual dose adjustments should follow based on patient response, up to a maximum of 20 mg/day in divided doses. Maintenance doses are typically 1–20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Left Ventricular Failure</strong>: Initial dose of 0.5 mg, titrated upwards based on clinical response. Maintenance doses range from 4–20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Raynaud’s Disease</strong>: Start at 0.5 mg twice daily, increasing as needed to a typical maintenance dose of 1–2 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>BPH</strong>: Initial dose of 0.5 mg twice daily, with maintenance doses up to 2 mg twice daily. Dosages above 4 mg/day should be approached cautiously.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration Guidelines</strong></p><ul><li>Tablets can be taken with or without food.</li><li>The initial dose is best administered in the evening to minimize postural hypotension risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Not suitable for patients with known hypersensitivity to quinazoline derivatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypotension Risk</strong>: Postural hypotension, particularly after the initial dose, may occur. Patients should avoid activities requiring alertness (e.g., driving) until they understand how the drug affects them.</li><li><strong>Heart Failure Patients</strong>: Gradual dose escalation is crucial to prevent sudden drops in cardiac output.</li><li><strong>Elderly Patients</strong>: Increased sensitivity to hypotensive effects warrants cautious dose initiation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Dizziness, drowsiness, nausea, palpitations, headache, and weakness.</li><li>Rare: Hallucinations, depression, rash, pruritus, blurred vision, or dry mouth.</li><li>Reported cases of exacerbated narcolepsy in a few patients, though causation remains uncertain.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Classified as Category C; use only if benefits outweigh potential risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Excreted in small amounts in breast milk, requiring cautious use in nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a dry place, away from light and moisture. Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p>This summary provides a clear understanding of Alphapress XR 2.5 mg, emphasizing safe and effective use. Let me know if you’d like to focus on a specific section or need further clarification.</p>', 'active', '1427_1734543265.webp', 6, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 22:34:25', '2024-12-18 22:34:25'),
(356, 'Perkinil', 'Procyclidine Hydrochloride', 1.25, 20, 'Tablet', '20 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Perkinil 5 mg (Procyclidine)</strong></p><p>Perkinil 5 mg (Procyclidine) is primarily indicated for the adjunctive treatment of various forms of parkinsonian syndrome, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Idiopathic Parkinsonism (paralysis agitans)</strong></li><li><strong>Postencephalitic Parkinsonism</strong></li><li><strong>Arteriosclerotic Parkinsonism</strong></li></ul><p>It is also effective in managing extrapyramidal symptoms such as:</p><ul><li><strong>Pseudoparkinsonism</strong></li><li><strong>Acute dystonic reactions</strong></li><li><strong>Akathisia induced by neuroleptic drugs (e.g., phenothiazine derivatives)</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antiparkinson drugs</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Procyclidine hydrochloride is an antimuscarinic agent with low toxicity. It works by correcting the relative excess of cholinergic activity caused by dopamine deficiency in Parkinson\'s disease. Key pharmacological properties:</p><ul><li>Absorbed through the gastrointestinal tract and rapidly distributed in the body.</li><li>Onset of action within 5–20 minutes after intravenous administration.</li><li>Effects last up to 4 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Oral Administration (Adults):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Parkinsonism</strong>: Start with 2.5 mg three times daily, gradually increasing to 5 mg three times daily, with an optional 5 mg dose at bedtime. The total daily dose can be adjusted up to 20–30 mg, divided into 3–4 doses.</li><li><strong>Drug-induced Extrapyramidal Symptoms</strong>: Begin with 2.5 mg three times daily, increasing in 2.5 mg increments as needed, up to a maximum tolerated dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>:</p><p>Safety and efficacy are not established for children. Use only if the benefits outweigh potential risks.</p><p><strong>Intramuscular or Intravenous Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li>Administer 5–10 mg, repeating if necessary after 20 minutes, with a maximum daily dose of 20 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Increased Anticholinergic Activity</strong>: May occur with agents such as amantadine.</li><li><strong>Reduced Absorption of Ketoconazole</strong>: When administered concurrently with Procyclidine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in children, elderly patients, and those with:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Cardiovascular disease</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Glaucoma</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Urinary retention</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hepatic or renal impairment</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Dry mouth.</li><li><strong>Occasional</strong>: Mydriasis, blurred vision, nausea, vomiting, constipation, rash, or muscle weakness.</li><li><strong>High Doses</strong>: Confusion, vertigo, and hallucinations.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid abrupt withdrawal to prevent rebound Parkinsonism symptoms.</li><li>Exercise caution in patients with hepatic/renal impairment, mental disorders, or psychosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Safety during pregnancy and lactation is not established. The use should consider potential risks to the mother and child.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 25°C.</li><li>Protect from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Antiparkinson drugs.</p><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Procyclidine hydrochloride alleviates symptoms by reducing cholinergic activity to balance dopamine deficiency in Parkinson\'s disease.</p>', 'active', '1601_1734543427.webp', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 22:37:07', '2024-12-18 22:37:07'),
(357, 'Rolip', 'Rosuvastatin', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Rolip 5 mg is prescribed for managing:</p><ul><li>Heterozygous familial and non-familial hypercholesterolemia</li><li>Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia</li><li>Mixed dyslipidemia (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>This medication belongs to the therapeutic classes of statins and other anti-anginal and anti-ischemic agents.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rolip 5 mg works by selectively inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase, an enzyme crucial for cholesterol synthesis. This mechanism helps lower cholesterol in two ways:</p><ol><li>By enhancing the number of LDL receptors in the liver to increase LDL uptake and breakdown.</li><li>By reducing the liver\'s production of VLDL, subsequently lowering the overall count of LDL and VLDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage</strong></p><p>The recommended dosage ranges from 5 mg to 40 mg once daily, depending on patient needs.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> The 40 mg dose is reserved for those unable to achieve LDL-C goals with 20 mg.</li><li><strong>Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia (HoFH):</strong> Start with 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics (Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Ages 8–9: 5–10 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Ages 10–17: 5–20 mg/day</li><li><strong>Pediatrics (HoFH):</strong> 20 mg/day for ages 7–17.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Rolip 5 mg can be taken at any time of the day, with or without food.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine:</strong> Increases exposure; limit to 5 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil:</strong> Use with caution and limit to 10 mg/day if needed.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/ritonavir or atazanavir/ritonavir:</strong> Dose should not exceed 10 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants:</strong> May affect INR; monitor closely.</li><li><strong>Concomitant lipid-lowering therapies:</strong> Increased risk of muscle-related side effects with fibrates or niacin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Rolip 5 mg should not be used in cases of:</p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to the drug</li><li>Active liver disease or persistent liver enzyme abnormalities</li><li>Pregnancy or breastfeeding</li></ul><p><strong>Possible Side Effects</strong></p><p>Commonly reported side effects include headache, muscle pain, constipation, fatigue, abdominal discomfort, and nausea.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of Rolip 5 mg during pregnancy has not been established. It is unknown whether it is excreted in breast milk.</p><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Skeletal muscle effects:</strong> Increased risk of myopathy and rhabdomyolysis, especially with high doses, advanced age, renal impairment, hypothyroidism, or certain drug combinations. Monitor for unexplained muscle pain or weakness and discontinue if symptoms occur.</li><li><strong>Liver monitoring:</strong> Persistent elevation of liver enzymes may occur; check levels before and during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in children under 7 years old have not been fully established.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further refinement or additional details!</p>', 'active', '392_1734543534.png', 6, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 22:38:54', '2024-12-18 22:38:54'),
(358, 'Metfo XR', 'Metformin Hydrochloride', 7.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p><strong>Metfo XR 500 mg</strong> is prescribed for managing type 2 diabetes mellitus, particularly in overweight individuals when diet and exercise alone are insufficient for glycemic control.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: Used alone (monotherapy) or combined with other oral antidiabetic agents or insulin.</li><li><strong>Children (10 years and older)</strong>: Used alone or with insulin.</li></ul><p>It has shown to reduce diabetes-related complications in overweight adults when used as first-line therapy after diet adjustments fail.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p><strong>Biguanides</strong></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Metformin, a biguanide, is an oral antihyperglycemic agent that lowers basal and postprandial blood glucose levels without causing hypoglycemia. It works by:</p><ol><li>Reducing liver glucose production.</li><li>Decreasing glucose absorption in the intestines.</li><li>Enhancing insulin sensitivity, promoting peripheral glucose uptake and utilization.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Immediate Release Tablets</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: Start with 500 mg twice daily or 850 mg once daily with meals. Increase gradually up to a maximum of 2000–2550 mg daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children</strong>: Start with 500 mg twice daily with meals. Gradual increases up to a maximum of 2000 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Extended Release Tablets</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: Start with 500 mg once daily with the evening meal. Dose can be increased weekly by 500 mg, up to a maximum of 2000 mg once daily or 1000 mg twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children</strong>: Not studied for this formulation.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Not recommended for patients with eGFR below 30 mL/min/1.73 m².</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Combining with carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (e.g., topiramate) may raise the risk of lactic acidosis.</li><li>Drugs reducing Metformin clearance (e.g., cimetidine) or alcohol consumption may increase adverse effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Metformin or its ingredients.</li><li>Conditions like lactic acidosis, diabetic ketoacidosis, severe renal impairment, dehydration, shock, or hepatic insufficiency.</li><li>Alcohol abuse or acute alcohol intoxication.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Gastrointestinal disturbances (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain).</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Vitamin B12 deficiency, skin reactions, liver function abnormalities.</li><li><strong>Serious</strong>: Lactic acidosis, hemolytic anemia (very rare).</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: No clear evidence of harm to the fetus, though monitoring is advised.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Metformin is present in breast milk, but no adverse effects have been reported in breastfed infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor kidney function to prevent Metformin accumulation and lactic acidosis.</li><li>May lower vitamin B12 levels with long-term use.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose</strong></p><ul><li>While hypoglycemia is uncommon, high doses may result in lactic acidosis, requiring urgent medical attention. Hemodialysis effectively removes both lactate and Metformin.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a dry place away from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1553_1734543697.webp', 36, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 22:41:37', '2024-12-18 22:41:37'),
(359, 'Microgest', 'Progesterone', 32.00, 10, 'Capsul', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Microgest 200 mg is a medication used to help support pregnancy, especially for those experiencing infertility due to low levels of progesterone, or those undergoing assisted fertility treatments like in-vitro fertilization (IVF). It is administered as a gel inserted into the vagina, which slowly releases the hormone progesterone into the bloodstream. This helps support pregnancy, particularly in the early stages.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Drugs for Infertility</p><p><strong>Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li>Insert the contents of one applicator (1.125 g, containing 90 mg of Microgest) into the vagina each day.</li><li>Start after ovulation or on the 18th to 21st day of your menstrual cycle.</li><li>During IVF, start after embryo transfer and continue for up to 30 days if pregnancy is confirmed.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong></p><ol><li>Prepare the applicator by shaking it to move the gel toward the thin end.</li><li>Remove the cap from the thin end and insert the applicator into the vagina.</li><li>Press the thick end to release the gel.</li><li>Dispose of the applicator after use, even if there is leftover gel.</li></ol><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Do not use other vaginal medicines simultaneously.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Allergy to the active substance or any ingredients.</li><li>Unexplained vaginal bleeding.</li><li>Porphyria.</li><li>Certain cancers (e.g., breast or genital organs).</li><li>History of blood clots, thrombophlebitis, or stroke.</li><li>Missed abortion (pregnancy loss).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects may include headache, tiredness, stomach cramps, and breast tenderness.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Microgest is typically not used during pregnancy, but may be continued if used to support pregnancy.</li><li>It is not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Exercise caution if you have liver or kidney problems, heart disease, diabetes, asthma, epilepsy, migraines, or a history of depression.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 25°C and out of reach of children.</li><li>Dispose of unused medicines responsibly to protect the environment.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>Not recommended for children or adolescents.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '822_1734543907.png', 6, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 22:45:07', '2024-12-18 22:45:07'),
(360, 'Consucon MR', 'Gliclazide', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '60 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Consucon MR 60 mg:</strong> Consucon MR 60 mg is an oral antidiabetic medication used to lower blood sugar in individuals with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus when diet, exercise, and weight loss alone are insufficient. It is part of the sulfonylurea group of medications.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong> Sulfonylureas</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Consucon MR 60 mg is a second-generation sulfonylurea that works by stimulating insulin release from pancreatic β-cells. It also decreases glucose production in the liver by enhancing calcium transport across the β-cell membranes.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial Dose:</strong> 40-80 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Maximum Dose:</strong> 320 mg daily in divided doses, as needed.</li><li>The drug should be taken before meals. For children, it is contraindicated due to its unsuitability for juvenile-onset diabetes.</li><li><strong>Modified Release Tablets:</strong> The recommended daily dose is one to four tablets (up to 120 mg), taken in the morning with breakfast. Always take the tablet with water, and do not crush or chew it.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose:</strong></p><p>If an overdose occurs, hypoglycemia (low blood sugar) symptoms may arise. In case of severe hypoglycemia or unconsciousness, immediate medical attention is necessary. If the patient is unconscious, do not give food or drink.</p><p><strong>Missed Dose:</strong></p><p>If you miss a dose, take the next one at the usual time. Do not take a double dose.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><p>The blood-sugar-lowering effect may be enhanced by:</p><ul><li>Other antidiabetics (insulin, GLP-1 agonists)</li><li>Antibiotics (sulfonamides, clarithromycin)</li><li>Blood pressure medicines (beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors)</li><li>Certain antifungals (fluconazole)</li><li>Painkillers (ibuprofen)</li><li>Alcohol</li></ul><p>The blood-sugar-lowering effect may be reduced by:</p><ul><li>CNS drugs (chlorpromazine)</li><li>Corticosteroids</li><li>Asthma treatments (salbutamol)</li><li>St. John’s Wort (Hypericum perforatum)</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Insulin-dependent diabetes (Type 1)</li><li>Diabetic ketoacidosis or diabetic coma</li><li>Severe kidney or liver disease</li><li>Pregnancy and breastfeeding</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p>Common side effects include hypoglycemia (low blood sugar), liver disorders, skin reactions (rashes, itching), blood disorders (low blood cell counts), digestive issues (nausea, diarrhea), and temporary eye vision disturbances.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><p>Consucon MR 60 mg is contraindicated during pregnancy and breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Regular monitoring of blood glucose is essential during treatment.</li><li>Risk of hypoglycemia increases in cases of irregular meals, fasting, excessive physical activity, or other medications.</li><li>If you experience severe hypoglycemia or high blood sugar symptoms, contact a healthcare provider immediately.</li><li>Special caution is needed for those with liver, kidney, or endocrine disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from children.</li><li>Do not use past the expiration date.</li><li>Properly dispose of unused medicine by consulting your pharmacist.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Stimulates insulin release from β-cells and reduces glucose production by the liver, helping to manage blood sugar levels in Type 2 diabetes.</p>', 'active', '547_1734544010.png', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 22:46:50', '2024-12-18 22:46:50'),
(361, 'Rosutin', 'Rosuvastatin', 12.00, 15, 'Tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia (Familial and Non-familial)</li><li>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia (Familial)</li><li>Mixed Dyslipidemia (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic Drugs, Statins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Rosutin 5 mg is a selective and competitive inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase, which is responsible for converting 3-hydroxy-3-methyl glutaryl coenzyme A to mevalonate, a cholesterol precursor. It works in two ways:</p><ol><li>It increases the number of hepatic LDL receptors to enhance LDL uptake and breakdown.</li><li>It inhibits hepatic VLDL synthesis, reducing VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General Dose Range:</strong> 5-40 mg once daily. Use 40 mg only for patients who don’t reach LDL-C goals with 20 mg.</li><li><strong>HoFH:</strong> Starting dose 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients with HeFH:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">8 to less than 10 years: 5-10 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 to 17 years: 5-20 mg/day</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients with HoFH:</strong> 20 mg/day for ages 7-17.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>Can be taken with or without food, at any time of day.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine:</strong> Increases exposure; limit to 5 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil:</strong> Avoid if possible; limit to 10 mg/day if necessary.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or Atazanavir/Ritonavir:</strong> Increases exposure; limit to 10 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Coumarin Anticoagulants:</strong> May prolong INR; monitor frequently.</li><li><strong>Lipid-Lowering Drugs:</strong> Risk of muscle effects when combined with fibrates or niacin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to any component.</li><li>Liver disease, especially persistent transaminase elevation.</li><li>Pregnancy and breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Headache, myalgia, constipation, asthenia, abdominal pain, nausea.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety in pregnancy not established. Not known if excreted in human milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Muscle Effects:</strong> Risk of myopathy or rhabdomyolysis increases with higher doses (40 mg), age, hypothyroidism, renal impairment, or drug interactions. Discontinue if muscle symptoms appear.</li><li><strong>Liver Enzyme Monitoring:</strong> Monitor liver enzymes before and during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety in pediatric patients not established.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need more changes!</p>', 'active', '1730_1734544112.png', 3, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 22:48:32', '2024-12-18 22:48:32'),
(362, 'Nexcital', 'Escitalopram', 8.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nexcital 5 mg:</strong></p><p>Nexcital 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Depressive illness</li><li>Generalized anxiety disorder</li><li>Obsessive-compulsive disorder</li><li>Social anxiety disorder</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>SSRIs &amp; related antidepressant drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Nexcital 5 mg is a selective serotonin (5-HT) reuptake inhibitor. The inhibition of serotonin reuptake is the likely mechanism of action responsible for the pharmacological and clinical effects of Nexcital 5 mg. It has minimal to no affinity for several receptors including 5-HT1A, 5-HT2, DA D1, and D2 receptors, as well as α1-, α2-, β-adrenoceptors, histamine H1, muscarinic cholinergic, benzodiazepine, and opioid receptors.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Nexcital 5 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> Start with 10 mg once daily. Both 10 mg and 20 mg doses have been found effective, but no greater benefit was observed with 20 mg over 10 mg. If increasing to 20 mg, this should happen after at least one week.</li><li><strong>Panic disorder (Adults &gt;18 years):</strong> Start with 5 mg once daily, increasing to 10 mg after 7 days, with a maximum dose of 20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Social anxiety disorder (Adults &gt;18 years):</strong> Start with 10 mg once daily, with adjustments after 2-4 weeks. Usual dose is 5-20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Recommended starting dose is 10 mg/day. Higher doses are not typically required and should be prescribed with caution.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Nexcital 5 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Nexcital 5 mg should not be initiated until 2 weeks after discontinuing an MAOI, and vice versa.</li><li>Caution should be exercised with drugs that affect serotonin levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant use with monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) or pimozide.</li><li>Hypersensitivity to Nexcital 5 mg, citalopram, or any of its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Nexcital 5 mg:</strong></p><p>Most people tolerate Nexcital 5 mg well. Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Insomnia</li><li>Ejaculation problems</li><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Increased sweating</li><li>Fatigue</li><li>These side effects are usually mild and resolve over time, typically without requiring discontinuation.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> The safety of Nexcital 5 mg during pregnancy has not been established. It should only be used if the benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Nexcital 5 mg is excreted in breast milk and should only be used if the benefits outweigh the potential risks to the infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><p>Discontinuation symptoms can occur, especially when stopping Nexcital 5 mg abruptly. These may include mood changes, irritability, dizziness, sensory disturbances, anxiety, and others. A gradual tapering of the dose is recommended. If serious symptoms occur, the original dose should be resumed.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place below 30°C. Protect from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and efficacy in children under 18 years have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> No significant differences in safety or effectiveness were observed in elderly patients compared to younger individuals. Some elderly individuals may have increased sensitivity.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you\'d like any further adjustments or details!</p>', 'active', '1083_1734544219.webp', 8, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 22:50:19', '2024-12-18 22:50:19'),
(363, 'Duocard', 'Cilnidipine', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Duocard 10 mg is primarily used for managing hypertension, particularly to protect end-organ function. It is effective for elderly patients, those with diabetes, albuminuria, and chronic kidney disease. It works as a calcium-channel blocker by inhibiting the influx of calcium through L-type and N-type voltage-dependent calcium channels, leading to the relaxation of vascular smooth muscle and vasodilation. This action helps lower blood pressure and prevents reflex heart rate increases.</p><p><strong>Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 5-10 mg once daily after breakfast, with a maximum dose of 20 mg per day.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Start with the lowest possible dose (5 mg) due to susceptibility to hypotension.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety has not been established for children.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><p>Duocard may interact with other antihypertensives, antipsychotics, quinidine, carbamazepine, phenytoin, rifampicin, cimetidine, and erythromycin, which can influence its effects.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>It should not be used in patients with known hypersensitivity to Duocard or its excipients, cardiogenic shock, recent myocardial infarction, unstable angina, or severe aortic stenosis.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p>Common side effects include dizziness, flushing, headache, hypotension, peripheral edema, palpitations, gastrointestinal disturbances, increased frequency of urination, lethargy, eye pain, and depression.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><p>Duocard should be avoided during pregnancy and lactation unless absolutely necessary, with the recommendation to discontinue breastfeeding if it is used.</p><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><p>Care should be taken in patients with severe hepatic dysfunction or a history of serious adverse reactions to calcium-channel blockers. Discontinuation should be gradual with close observation.</p><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture, and out of reach of children.</p><p>Duocard belongs to the calcium-channel blocker class and should be used cautiously according to individual patient conditions.</p>', 'active', '534_1734544332.png', 5, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 22:52:12', '2024-12-18 22:52:12'),
(364, 'Replet', 'Clopidogrel Bisulphate', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '75 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Acute Coronary Syndrome (ACS):</strong> Reduces the rate of MI and stroke in patients with non-ST-segment elevation ACS, including unstable angina (UA) and non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI).</li><li><strong>Recent MI, Recent Stroke, or Peripheral Arterial Disease:</strong> Helps reduce the rate of MI and stroke in patients with established peripheral arterial disease or a recent history of MI or stroke.</li></ol><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Anti-platelet drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Clopidogrel, a prodrug, inhibits platelet activation and aggregation by binding irreversibly to P2Y12 ADP receptors on platelets. The effect begins within 2 hours of administration, with steady-state inhibition occurring between Day 3 and Day 7 with a daily dose of 75 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Acute Coronary Syndrome:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initiate with a 300 mg loading dose (4 tablets) and then continue with 75 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Without a loading dose, the antiplatelet effect will take several days to establish.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Recent MI, Recent Stroke, or Peripheral Arterial Disease:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Take 75 mg daily without a loading dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>NSAIDs, Warfarin, SSRIs, SNRIs:</strong> Increase the risk of bleeding.</li><li><strong>CYP2C19 Inhibitors (Omeprazole, Esomeprazole):</strong> Should be avoided as they may reduce Clopidogrel\'s effectiveness.</li><li><strong>Repaglinide:</strong> Avoid co-administration as it can increase plasma concentrations of repaglinide.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Clopidogrel or any of its components.</li><li>Active pathological bleeding (e.g., peptic ulcer, intracranial hemorrhage).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Bleeding, diarrhea, gastrointestinal discomfort, hemorrhage, skin reactions.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Acquired hemophilia, anemia, angioedema, bone marrow disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Should only be used during pregnancy if absolutely necessary. Its excretion in breast milk is unknown, so decisions about discontinuation of breastfeeding or the drug should be made based on the clinical importance.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Genetic Factors:</strong> Metabolism is affected by CYP2C19 genetic variations (poor metabolizers may have reduced efficacy).</li><li><strong>Risk of Bleeding:</strong> As it inhibits platelet aggregation for the lifespan of the platelet (7-10 days), there is an increased bleeding risk. Platelet transfusions may not be as effective within a few hours after dosing.</li><li><strong>Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura (TTP):</strong> Rare but severe condition requiring urgent treatment.</li><li><strong>Discontinuation before Surgery:</strong> Discontinue 5 days prior to elective surgery with high bleeding risk.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose can lead to bleeding complications. Platelet transfusions may restore clotting ability.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a dry place, protected from light. Do not freeze. Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>Not established for pediatric use.</li><li>No dosage adjustment is required for elderly patients.</li></ul><p>This is an overview of Replet 75 mg, primarily used to prevent further cardiovascular events in specific high-risk patient populations.</p>', 'active', '1424_1734544479.webp', 1, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 22:54:39', '2024-12-18 22:54:39'),
(365, 'Disopan', 'Clonazepam', 12.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Disopan 2 mg</strong></p><p>Disopan 2 mg is prescribed for the treatment of panic disorder, with or without agoraphobia. Panic disorder is characterized by unexpected panic attacks, along with a constant worry about future episodes and their potential consequences.</p><p>It is also used alone or in combination for the treatment of Lennox-Gastaut syndrome (specifically petit mal variant), akinetic seizures, and myoclonic seizures. For patients with absence seizures (petit mal) who do not respond to succinimides, Disopan 2 mg may be an alternative.</p><p>Note that the long-term effectiveness of Disopan 2 mg has not been studied beyond 9 weeks, so physicians should reassess its continued use after prolonged treatment.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</li><li>Benzodiazepine hypnotics</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong> Disopan 2 mg reduces nerve transmission in the motor cortex, helping to suppress spike-and-wave discharges associated with absence seizures. Its mechanism is believed to be tied to enhancing GABA activity. It has shown effectiveness in both focal and generalized epilepsy.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><em>Adults with Seizure Disorders:</em></p><ul><li>Start with 1.5 mg/day, divided into three doses. Increase by 0.5-1 mg every 3 days, up to a maximum of 20 mg/day.</li></ul><p><em>Adults with Panic Disorder:</em></p><ul><li>Start with 0.25 mg twice daily. After 3 days, increase to 1 mg/day as necessary.</li></ul><p><em>Pediatric Patients:</em></p><ul><li>Infants and children (up to 10 years or 30 kg): 0.01-0.03 mg/kg/day, not exceeding 0.05 mg/kg/day, in multiple doses.</li></ul><p><em>Injection Dosage:</em></p><ul><li>Children: 0.5 mg via slow IV injection or infusion.</li><li>Adults: 1 mg via slow IV injection or infusion, with repeat doses if needed (1-4 mg).</li><li>IV rate: 0.25-0.5 mg/min (0.5-1.0 ml of the solution), with a max dose of 10 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>No significant effect on the pharmacokinetics of phenytoin, carbamazepine, or phenobarbital.</li><li>Its impact on the metabolism of other drugs remains unclear.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to benzodiazepines</li><li>Significant liver disease</li><li>Acute narrow-angle glaucoma</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>CNS depression: drowsiness (50%), ataxia (30%), and behavior problems (25%)</li><li>Other side effects: abnormal eye movements, aphonia, coma, tremors, vertigo, confusion, depression, amnesia, hallucinations, psychosis, and increased libido.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Use during pregnancy only if benefits outweigh risks. Avoid breastfeeding while on Disopan 2 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Risk of triggering generalized tonic-clonic seizures in patients with multiple seizure disorders.</li><li>Combining with valproic acid may cause absence status.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Drowsiness, ataxia, dysarthria, nystagmus, possible coma, respiratory depression, and hypotension.</li><li>Treatment: Monitor vital signs and provide supportive care. Activated charcoal may be used within 1-2 hours. Flumazenil may be considered for severe CNS depression.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li>For IV injection, dilute with 1 ml of water for injection.</li><li>For IV infusion, dilute with 85 ml of a suitable medium. Infusion stability lasts 24 hours at room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</li><li>Benzodiazepine hypnotics</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Enhances GABAergic neurotransmission, improving the effectiveness of GABA at inhibitory synapses. This suppresses various forms of paroxysmal activity, making Disopan 2 mg effective for both focal and generalized epilepsy.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: May increase saliva and bronchial secretions.</li><li><strong>Geriatrics</strong>: May be more sensitive to effects, requiring careful monitoring.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: May require careful monitoring, especially in cirrhosis.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1869_1734544583.jpg', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 22:56:23', '2024-12-18 22:56:23');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(366, 'Rosen 28', 'Ethinylestradiol + Drospirenone [For oral contraception]', 399.00, 28, 'Tablet', '28 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.03 mg+3 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Rosen 28 (0.03 mg Ethinylestradiol and 3 mg Drospirenone) is an oral contraceptive indicated for preventing pregnancy. It contains synthetic estrogen (ethinylestradiol) and synthetic progesterone (drospirenone), which work together to inhibit ovulation, alter cervical mucus, and modify the endometrium to prevent pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Oral Contraceptive Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Ethinylestradiol</strong> is a synthetic estrogen that suppresses gonadotropin release, thereby preventing ovulation.</li><li><strong>Drospirenone</strong> is a synthetic progestin with antimineralocorticoid activity, which helps reduce estrogen-related sodium retention, and displays antiandrogenic properties. It also improves the lipid profile, increasing HDL cholesterol.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>Take <strong>one tablet daily</strong> for 28 consecutive days.</li><li>Start taking the first light yellow tablet from the left side of the pack on the <strong>first day of menstruation</strong>.</li><li>Continue taking one light yellow tablet each day for 21 days, followed by <strong>seven white placebo tablets</strong> for the next 7 days.</li><li><strong>Missed Tablets</strong>: If one tablet is missed, take it as soon as possible. If two tablets are missed, use an additional contraceptive method and begin a new pack after your next menstruation.</li><li><strong>To Delay a Period</strong>: Start a new pack immediately after finishing the active tablets of the current pack, skipping the placebo tablets.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><p>Certain medications, including antibiotics (like ampicillin and tetracyclines), anticonvulsants (such as phenytoin), and rifampicin, can reduce the effectiveness of this oral contraceptive. A non-hormonal contraceptive should be used in such cases.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy</li><li>Over 45 years of age</li><li>Heavy smoking (more than 20 cigarettes/day)</li><li>History of heart disease or blood clotting issues</li><li>Liver disease or jaundice</li><li>High blood pressure, migraines, or diabetes with vascular complications</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p>Initial side effects may include dizziness, headache, nausea, or bleeding between periods. If these persist for more than 2-3 months, consult a doctor. Serious side effects, like stroke or chest pain, require immediate medical attention.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Contraindicated during pregnancy; must be excluded before starting the pill.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: May interfere with milk production and composition; should be avoided during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Increased risk of thromboembolic events (e.g., deep venous thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, stroke) with COC use.</li><li><strong>Venous thromboembolism (VTE)</strong> can occur, especially with drospirenone-containing contraceptives.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose:</strong></p><p>Overdose symptoms include nausea, vomiting, breast tenderness, and fatigue. There is no specific antidote, and treatment is symptomatic.</p><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place, away from light and out of children\'s reach.</p><h3>Special Populations:</h3><ul><li><strong>Paediatric Use</strong>: Only indicated after menarche.</li><li><strong>Elderly Use</strong>: Not recommended for use post-menopause.</li></ul><p>This summary provides an overview of how to take and manage potential issues with <strong>Rosen 28</strong> oral contraceptive tablets. Always consult a healthcare provider for personalized advice and any concerns.</p>', 'active', '1547_1734544818.jpg', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 23:00:18', '2025-05-12 18:16:26'),
(367, 'OMG-3', 'Omega-3 Acid Ethyl Esters', 11.00, 10, 'Capsul', '10 capsule t in a strip', 'Capsule', '1000 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Hypertriglyceridemia</strong>: As an adjunct to diet for reducing triglyceride levels greater than 500 mg/dL in adults.</li><li><strong>Post Myocardial Infarction</strong>: To aid in secondary prevention for patients who have had a heart attack within the past three months.</li><li><strong>Joint Inflammation</strong>: To help reduce joint inflammation associated with mild arthritis.</li><li><strong>Mood Disorders &amp; Impulse Control</strong>: It may be helpful in managing mood disorders and impulse control.</li><li><strong>Age-related Macular Degeneration</strong>: Aids in managing this condition by supporting eye health.</li></ol><p><strong>Composition:</strong></p><p>Each soft gelatin capsule contains 1000 mg of Omega-3-acid ethyl esters, which includes:</p><ul><li><strong>Eicosapentaenoic Acid (EPA)</strong>: 80 mg</li><li><strong>Docosahexaenoic Acid (DHA)</strong>: 120 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>The active ingredients, EPA and DHA, are long-chain polyunsaturated fatty acids that help reduce triglyceride levels, have anti-inflammatory effects, and promote platelet aggregation inhibition. They work by:</p><ul><li>Reducing triglycerides through action on very-low-density lipoproteins (VLDL).</li><li>Inhibiting leukotriene synthesis, which has anti-inflammatory effects.</li><li>Improving vasodilatation and reducing platelet aggregation, which may reduce the risk of blood clots.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage and Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Post Myocardial Infarction</strong>: One capsule (1000 mg) daily.</li><li><strong>Hypertriglyceridemia</strong>: Start with two capsules (2000 mg) daily, increasing to four capsules (4000 mg) if necessary.</li><li><strong>Joint Inflammation</strong>: 2.7 gm per day.</li><li><strong>Mood Disorders &amp; Macular Degeneration</strong>: One capsule (1000 mg) per day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction:</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor patients using both Omega-3-acid ethyl esters and anticoagulants closely, as Omega-3s can increase the effects of anticoagulants.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any components of the medication.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Rare side effects include dyspepsia, nausea, constipation, gastritis, and dizziness.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Omega-3 fatty acids are beneficial for pregnant women, improving cognitive and visual development in babies and potentially reducing the risk of allergies.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Regular monitoring of LDL-C and triglyceride levels is recommended during treatment.</li><li>No specific recommendations for overdose treatment, but symptomatic management is advised.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 25°C in a dry and cool place.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety has not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Limited clinical data in patients over 65 years, but no significant differences in safety or efficacy were noted in those older than 60 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Omega-3-acid ethyl esters reduce triglyceride production in the liver, increase fatty acid metabolism, and enhance triglyceride clearance. They work by inhibiting enzymes involved in triglyceride synthesis and promoting the activity of lipoprotein lipase.</li></ul><p>This medication belongs to the therapeutic class of <strong>Other lipid regulating drugs</strong>, mainly used to manage lipid disorders and improve heart health.</p>', 'active', '1661_1734544990.jpg', 9, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 23:03:10', '2024-12-18 23:03:10'),
(368, 'Bilastin', 'Bilastine', 16.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><p>Bilastin 20 mg is used to treat symptoms of allergic rhino-conjunctivitis (seasonal and perennial) and urticaria (hives).</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Bilastin is a non-sedative H1 receptor selective antagonist, primarily working through the inhibition of peripheral H1 receptors. It has a moderate to high affinity for these receptors and does not interact with muscarinic, serotonergic, dopaminergic, or noradrenergic receptors.</p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children (6–11 years)</strong>: 10 mg daily in the form of a mouth-dissolving tablet. It should not exceed the daily dose of one tablet. The tablet can dissolve in the mouth or be mixed with water.</li><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: 20 mg daily as a tablet, to be taken on an empty stomach with water. The maximum recommended daily dose is 20 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant use with ketoconazole, erythromycin, or diltiazem can increase the concentration of Bilastin in the blood.</li><li>There is no significant interaction with alcohol or lorazepam in terms of CNS depression.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Not suitable for patients allergic to Bilastin or any of its excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p>The most common side effects include headache, dizziness, somnolence (sleepiness), and fatigue, which occurred at similar rates as with a placebo.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation:</strong></p><p>There is limited data regarding the use of Bilastin during pregnancy and breastfeeding. It should be avoided unless deemed necessary by a doctor.</p><p><strong>Precautions and Warnings:</strong></p><p>Avoid co-administration with P-glycoprotein inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole, cyclosporine, ritonavir) in patients with moderate or severe renal impairment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><p>In clinical trials with overdoses (up to 10-11 times the recommended dose), side effects like dizziness, headache, and nausea were more frequent, though no serious effects were reported.</p><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture, and out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Geriatrics (&gt;65 years)</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics (&lt;6 years)</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Bilastin is a non-sedating, long-acting antihistamine that selectively blocks peripheral H1 receptors, preventing histamine-induced allergic reactions.</p>', 'active', '1811_1734545139.png', 17, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 23:05:39', '2024-12-18 23:05:39'),
(369, 'Bilastin', 'Bilastine', 16.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><p>Bilastin 20 mg is used to treat symptoms of allergic rhino-conjunctivitis (seasonal and perennial) and urticaria (hives).</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Bilastin is a non-sedative H1 receptor selective antagonist, primarily working through the inhibition of peripheral H1 receptors. It has a moderate to high affinity for these receptors and does not interact with muscarinic, serotonergic, dopaminergic, or noradrenergic receptors.</p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children (6–11 years)</strong>: 10 mg daily in the form of a mouth-dissolving tablet. It should not exceed the daily dose of one tablet. The tablet can dissolve in the mouth or be mixed with water.</li><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: 20 mg daily as a tablet, to be taken on an empty stomach with water. The maximum recommended daily dose is 20 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant use with ketoconazole, erythromycin, or diltiazem can increase the concentration of Bilastin in the blood.</li><li>There is no significant interaction with alcohol or lorazepam in terms of CNS depression.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Not suitable for patients allergic to Bilastin or any of its excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p>The most common side effects include headache, dizziness, somnolence (sleepiness), and fatigue, which occurred at similar rates as with a placebo.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation:</strong></p><p>There is limited data regarding the use of Bilastin during pregnancy and breastfeeding. It should be avoided unless deemed necessary by a doctor.</p><p><strong>Precautions and Warnings:</strong></p><p>Avoid co-administration with P-glycoprotein inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole, cyclosporine, ritonavir) in patients with moderate or severe renal impairment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><p>In clinical trials with overdoses (up to 10-11 times the recommended dose), side effects like dizziness, headache, and nausea were more frequent, though no serious effects were reported.</p><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture, and out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Geriatrics (&gt;65 years)</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics (&lt;6 years)</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Bilastin is a non-sedating, long-acting antihistamine that selectively blocks peripheral H1 receptors, preventing histamine-induced allergic reactions.</p>', 'in-active', '1546_1734545139.png', 17, 12, NULL, '2024-12-18 23:05:39', '2025-05-08 09:37:57'),
(370, 'Anzitor', 'Atorvastatin Calcium', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Anzitor 10 mg</strong></p><p>Anzitor 10 mg is prescribed to reduce elevated total cholesterol, LDL cholesterol, apolipoprotein B (Apo-B), and triglycerides in the following conditions where diet and non-pharmacological measures alone are insufficient:</p><ul><li>To reduce total cholesterol and LDL cholesterol in patients with heterozygous and homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia.</li><li>To reduce elevated cholesterol and triglycerides in patients with mixed dyslipidemia (Fredrickson Type Ia and Ib).</li><li>For the treatment of patients with elevated serum triglyceride levels in hypertriglyceridemia (Fredrickson Type IV).</li><li>For patients with dysbetalipoproteinemia (Fredrickson Type III).</li><li>To reduce cardiac ischemic events in patients with asymptomatic or mild to moderate symptomatic coronary artery disease and elevated LDL cholesterol.</li><li>To reduce cholesterol levels in patients with hypercholesterolemia exacerbated by diabetes mellitus or renal transplantation.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs, Statins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Atorvastatin is a selective HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor, blocking the conversion of HMG-CoA to mevalonate, which is a precursor for cholesterol. This results in reduced cholesterol synthesis in the liver and increased hepatic LDL receptors for enhanced LDL uptake and metabolism.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Primary hypercholesterolemia and combined hyperlipidemia</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 10 mg once daily, increasing to a maximum of 80 mg once daily after at least 4 weeks if needed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (10-18 years): Start at 10 mg once daily, increasing to a maximum of 20 mg once daily after at least 4 weeks if needed.</li><li><strong>Familial hypercholesterolemia</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: Start at 10 mg daily, increasing to 40 mg once daily after at least 4 weeks, then up to 80 mg if necessary.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (10-18 years): Start at 10 mg once daily, increasing to a maximum of 80 mg once daily if needed.</li><li><strong>Prevention of cardiovascular events</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: Start at 10 mg once daily, adjusted based on the response.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Increased risk of myopathy with cyclosporine, fibric acid derivatives, erythromycin, azole antifungals, and niacin.</li><li>No significant interactions with antihypertensives or hypoglycemic agents.</li><li>Close monitoring is advised when combined with digoxin, erythromycin, oral contraceptives, colestipol, antacids, and warfarin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the medication.</li><li>Active liver disease or unexplained persistent elevations in serum transaminases.</li><li>History of serious reactions to HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Constipation, flatulence, dyspepsia, abdominal pain.</li><li>Others: Infection, headache, back pain, rash, asthenia, arthralgia, myalgia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated during pregnancy and breastfeeding. Women of childbearing age should use contraception during therapy. If pregnancy occurs, discontinue the medication.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Liver function tests should be conducted before treatment and periodically thereafter.</li><li>Caution is needed in patients with significant alcohol intake or a history of liver disease.</li><li>Discontinue therapy if elevated CPK levels or myopathy are suspected.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose</strong></p><ul><li>No specific antidote for atorvastatin overdose. Treat symptomatically, with liver function tests and monitoring of serum CK levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Caution in elderly patients, especially those over 65 years, due to an increased risk of myopathy.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Contraindicated in active liver disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Atorvastatin inhibits HMG-CoA reductase, reducing cholesterol synthesis in the liver, and increases LDL receptors for enhanced clearance of LDL cholesterol from the bloodstream.</p>', 'active', '1398_1734548773.png', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:06:13', '2024-12-19 00:06:13'),
(371, 'Fungidal-HC', 'Miconazole Nitrate + Hydrocortisone', 55.00, 1, 'x 15 gm tube', '15 gm tube', 'Cream', '2%+1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment of inflamed dermatoses where infection by susceptible organisms (dermatophytes, pathogenic yeasts like <em>Candida spp.</em>, Gram-positive bacteria such as <em>Streptococcus</em> and <em>Staphylococcus</em>) coexists.</li><li>Suitable for eczema or dermatitis, including atopic eczema, irritant or allergic contact eczema, and seborrheic eczema.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Miconazole</strong>: An imidazole antifungal agent, works by inhibiting fungal cell membrane synthesis and causing cell membrane damage at high concentrations. It also has antibacterial properties.</li><li><strong>Hydrocortisone</strong>: A synthetic corticosteroid that reduces skin inflammation by decreasing the production of inflammatory chemicals like histamines, prostaglandins, and kinin. It also helps with vasoconstriction, reduces swelling and itching, and has immunosuppressive effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>Apply a thin layer of the cream 2–3 times daily to the affected area for 1–2 weeks.</li><li>Rub in gently until the cream vanishes. Continue treatment for at least one week after symptoms disappear.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in individuals with hypersensitivity to miconazole, hydrocortisone, or any component of the cream.</li><li>Not recommended for use in pregnant or lactating women without a physician\'s advice.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Rare local sensitivity reactions may occur.</li><li>Long-term use may suppress adrenal gland hormone production.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously during pregnancy as high doses in animals have caused fetal abnormalities.</li><li>Topical steroids should be avoided extensively during pregnancy.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Dispense only on a physician\'s prescription.</li><li>Exercise caution when using on children or elderly individuals for long-term therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Hydrocortisone &amp; Combined preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Miconazole</strong>: Inhibits ergosterol synthesis in fungi, altering cell membrane permeability and leading to fungal cell death.</li><li><strong>Hydrocortisone</strong>: Reduces inflammation, itching, and swelling by inhibiting the release of chemicals involved in the inflammatory response and reducing vascular permeability.</li></ul>', 'active', '436_1734548921.webp', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:08:41', '2025-04-15 12:20:53'),
(372, 'Rovast', 'Rosuvastatin', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rovast 5 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia (Familial and Non-familial)</li><li>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia (Familial)</li><li>Mixed Dyslipidemia (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs</li><li>Statins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Rovast 5 mg is a selective and competitive inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase, the enzyme that converts 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl coenzyme A to mevalonate, which is a precursor to cholesterol. It works in two main ways:</p><ol><li>Increases the number of LDL receptors in the liver to enhance LDL uptake and breakdown.</li><li>Inhibits hepatic synthesis of VLDL, thus reducing the number of VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li>Adults: 5–40 mg once daily. The 40 mg dose should only be used for patients not reaching their LDL-C goal with 20 mg.</li><li>HoFH: Starting dose of 20 mg/day.</li><li>Pediatric patients with HeFH: 5–10 mg/day (ages 8–10), 5–20 mg/day (ages 10–17).</li><li>Pediatric patients with HoFH: 20 mg/day (ages 7–17).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong> Rovast 5 mg can be taken with or without food, at any time of the day.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine:</strong> Increases exposure; limit dose to 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil:</strong> Avoid combination or limit dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or Atazanavir/Ritonavir:</strong> Limit dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin Anticoagulants:</strong> May prolong INR; monitor frequently.</li><li><strong>Concomitant lipid-lowering therapies (fibrates/niacin):</strong> Increased risk of muscle effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to any component</li><li>Liver disease, including unexplained persistent hepatic enzyme elevations</li><li>Pregnant or breastfeeding women</li><li>Women who may become pregnant</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety in pregnancy has not been established.</li><li>Unknown if excreted in human milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Skeletal Muscle Effects:</strong> Risk of myopathy and rhabdomyolysis, particularly with the 40 mg dose, in elderly patients (&gt;65), those with hypothyroidism, renal impairment, or those taking certain other drugs. Discontinue if muscle pain, tenderness, or weakness occurs.</li><li><strong>Liver Monitoring:</strong> Regular monitoring of liver enzymes is recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '789_1734549037.png', 1, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:10:37', '2024-12-19 00:10:37'),
(373, 'Clofranil', 'Clomipramine Hydrochloride', 6.35, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Clofranil 25 mg is a medication primarily used to treat depression, obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD), phobias, and cataplexy associated with narcolepsy in adults.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Tricyclic &amp; related antidepressant drugs.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Clomipramine, the active ingredient, is a tricyclic antidepressant (TCA) that works by increasing the activity of neurotransmitters (especially serotonin) in the brain. It is a potent serotonin reuptake inhibitor and has additional sedative and anticholinergic effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li>For depression: 10 mg to 150 mg daily; severe cases may require higher doses.</li><li>For OCD and phobias: 10 mg to 150 mg daily.</li><li>For cataplexy: 10 mg to 75 mg daily.</li><li>For elderly patients: Lower doses are generally recommended due to a higher sensitivity to side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong></p><p>Take the capsule whole with a drink of water. It can be taken either as a single dose at night or in smaller doses throughout the day. It may take up to 4 weeks to notice its effects.</p><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><p>Do not take Clofranil with monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs), or within 14 days before or after taking an irreversible MAOI, or with moclobemide (a reversible MAOI).</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to clomipramine or related compounds.</li><li>Recent use of MAO inhibitors (within 14 days).</li><li>Recent myocardial infarction (heart attack).</li><li>Conditions like glaucoma, arrhythmias, and low blood potassium.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p>Common side effects include increased appetite, weight gain, headaches, dizziness, nausea, constipation, dry mouth, excessive sweating, tremors, sexual disturbances, and fatigue. Some people may experience difficulty urinating or eye problems.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation:</strong></p><p>Category C for pregnancy, meaning the drug may cause harm to the fetus, so it should only be used if clearly needed. It is passed into breast milk, so breastfeeding is not recommended unless necessary.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><p>Consult a doctor if you have epilepsy, thyroid issues, heart problems, glaucoma, liver/kidney issues, or other conditions like low blood pressure. Caution is advised in elderly patients, as side effects are more common.</p><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><p>Store the medication in a cool, dry place, out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly patients</strong>: May require lower doses due to increased risk of side effects.</li><li><strong>Children &amp; adolescents (&lt;18 years)</strong>: The safety and effectiveness for treating psychiatric disorders have not been established, and use is not recommended for this age group.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Clomipramine works by inhibiting the reuptake of serotonin in the brain, leading to increased levels of serotonin. It also has some antagonistic effects on cholinergic and alpha-1 receptors and weak antagonism at dopamine receptors, contributing to its antidepressant and sedative properties.</p><p>Please consult with a healthcare provider for personalized medical advice and before starting or stopping the medication.</p>', 'active', '1063_1734549257.png', 16, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:14:17', '2024-12-19 00:14:17'),
(374, 'Clofranil', 'Clomipramine Hydrochloride', 6.35, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Clofranil 25 mg is a medication primarily used to treat depression, obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD), phobias, and cataplexy associated with narcolepsy in adults.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Tricyclic &amp; related antidepressant drugs.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Clomipramine, the active ingredient, is a tricyclic antidepressant (TCA) that works by increasing the activity of neurotransmitters (especially serotonin) in the brain. It is a potent serotonin reuptake inhibitor and has additional sedative and anticholinergic effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li>For depression: 10 mg to 150 mg daily; severe cases may require higher doses.</li><li>For OCD and phobias: 10 mg to 150 mg daily.</li><li>For cataplexy: 10 mg to 75 mg daily.</li><li>For elderly patients: Lower doses are generally recommended due to a higher sensitivity to side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong></p><p>Take the capsule whole with a drink of water. It can be taken either as a single dose at night or in smaller doses throughout the day. It may take up to 4 weeks to notice its effects.</p><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><p>Do not take Clofranil with monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs), or within 14 days before or after taking an irreversible MAOI, or with moclobemide (a reversible MAOI).</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to clomipramine or related compounds.</li><li>Recent use of MAO inhibitors (within 14 days).</li><li>Recent myocardial infarction (heart attack).</li><li>Conditions like glaucoma, arrhythmias, and low blood potassium.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p>Common side effects include increased appetite, weight gain, headaches, dizziness, nausea, constipation, dry mouth, excessive sweating, tremors, sexual disturbances, and fatigue. Some people may experience difficulty urinating or eye problems.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation:</strong></p><p>Category C for pregnancy, meaning the drug may cause harm to the fetus, so it should only be used if clearly needed. It is passed into breast milk, so breastfeeding is not recommended unless necessary.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><p>Consult a doctor if you have epilepsy, thyroid issues, heart problems, glaucoma, liver/kidney issues, or other conditions like low blood pressure. Caution is advised in elderly patients, as side effects are more common.</p><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><p>Store the medication in a cool, dry place, out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly patients</strong>: May require lower doses due to increased risk of side effects.</li><li><strong>Children &amp; adolescents (&lt;18 years)</strong>: The safety and effectiveness for treating psychiatric disorders have not been established, and use is not recommended for this age group.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Clomipramine works by inhibiting the reuptake of serotonin in the brain, leading to increased levels of serotonin. It also has some antagonistic effects on cholinergic and alpha-1 receptors and weak antagonism at dopamine receptors, contributing to its antidepressant and sedative properties.</p><p>Please consult with a healthcare provider for personalized medical advice and before starting or stopping the medication.</p>', 'in-active', '23_1734549257.png', 16, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:14:17', '2025-05-08 14:13:37'),
(375, 'Tryptin', 'Amitriptyline hydrochloride', 0.85, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Tryptin 10 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Depressive Illness</strong>: Especially when sedation is needed.</li><li><strong>Nocturnal Enuresis (Bedwetting)</strong>: In children.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of Migraine</strong>.</li><li><strong>Tension Headache</strong>.</li><li><strong>Chronic Pain</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>: Tricyclic Anti-depressant.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>: Tryptin (Amitriptyline HCl) is an antidepressant with sedative properties. It works by inhibiting the reuptake of norepinephrine and serotonin in adrenergic and serotonergic neurons, enhancing neuronal activity. This mechanism is believed to contribute to its antidepressant effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Depression</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 50-70 mg/day, either in divided doses or as a single dose at bedtime. Maximum: 150-200 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly and Adolescents</strong>: 25-50 mg/day, either divided or as a single dose at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Nocturnal Enuresis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6-10 years)</strong>: 10-20 mg at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (11-16 years)</strong>: 25-50 mg at bedtime for up to 3 months, then gradually withdrawn.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of Migraine</strong>: 100 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Tension Headache</strong>: 10-25 mg three times a day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Enhances the sedative effects of alcohol and opioid analgesics.</li><li>May increase the risk of ventricular arrhythmias when used with Moxifloxacin or Terfenadine.</li><li>Disulfiram and Cimetidine inhibit the metabolism of Amitriptyline.</li><li>In combination with diuretics, increases the risk of postural hypotension.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Cardiac Issues</strong>: Myocardial infarction, arrhythmias (especially heart block), severe liver disease, and mania.</li><li><strong>Sedation</strong>: May impair the ability to drive or operate machinery.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Anticholinergic</strong>: Dry mouth, blurred vision, hyperpyrexia, urinary retention, etc.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular</strong>: Hypotension, syncope, hypertension, tachycardia, etc.</li><li><strong>CNS and Neuromuscular</strong>: Confusion, disorientation, delusions, etc.</li><li><strong>Allergic</strong>: Rash, urticaria, photosensitivity.</li><li><strong>Haematological</strong>: Bone-marrow depression.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Nausea, anorexia, constipation, etc.</li><li><strong>Endocrine</strong>: Breast enlargement, testicular swelling, galactorrhoea.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C; not recommended, especially during the first and third trimesters.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Amitriptyline is detectable in breast milk. Decision to continue breastfeeding should be carefully considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong>:</p><ul><li>Use cautiously in patients with a history of epilepsy, glaucoma, urinary retention, cardiac disease, diabetes, thyroid disease, or psychoses (may aggravate mania).</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, below 30°C. Protect from light and moisture.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>: Amitriptyline likely improves mood by inhibiting the reuptake of serotonin and norepinephrine at synaptic sites in the brain. These neurotransmitters are involved in regulating mood, and increased levels are believed to alleviate depressive symptoms. Its analgesic effects may also relate to mood alteration or another mechanism.</p>', 'active', '1429_1734549348.webp', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:15:48', '2024-12-19 00:15:48'),
(376, 'Mixtard 30 Flexpen', 'Insulin Human [rDNA] + Isophane Insulin Human', 615.00, 1, 'x 3 ml flexpen', '3 ml flexpen', 'Flexpen', '30%+70% in 100 IU/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p><p><strong>Mixtard 30 Flexpen 100IU/ml</strong></p><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment of all Type 1 diabetes patients.</li><li>Treatment for Type 2 diabetes patients whose blood sugar is not sufficiently controlled by diet or oral hypoglycemic medications.</li><li>For initial stabilization in cases of diabetic ketoacidosis, hyperosmolar non-ketotic syndrome, and during stressful times such as major illness or surgery in diabetic patients.</li><li>Gestational diabetes treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Composition:</strong></p><ul><li>30% Soluble Insulin Human (Regular) and 70% Isophane Insulin Human in each milliliter.</li><li>40 IU (1.388 mg) Insulin Human (rDNA) in each milliliter.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Insulin with a Medium Action.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Human insulin produced using recombinant DNA technology. It helps control glucose metabolism and promotes the absorption of glucose by the liver, muscles, and fat.</li><li>Mixtard 30 Flexpen starts working within 30 minutes, peaks in 2-8 hours, and lasts up to 24 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Type 1 Diabetes:</strong> 0.5–1.0 IU/kg/day, higher in insulin-resistant cases (e.g., puberty, obesity).</li><li><strong>Type 2 Diabetes:</strong> 0.3–0.6 IU/kg/day initially.</li><li>The dosage varies depending on individual needs, food intake, and exercise.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>Administer subcutaneously in the upper arm, thigh, buttock, or abdomen. The abdominal wall absorbs insulin more quickly.</li><li>Inject a meal/snack containing carbohydrates within 30 minutes after injection.</li><li>Shake or rotate the vial gently before use.</li><li>Administer insulin using a syringe, and ensure the needle is at a 45° angle to the skin. Press gently on the site after injection.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Increased insulin requirement when using medications like oral contraceptives, thyroid hormones, and corticosteroids.</li><li>Decreased insulin requirement with antidepressants like salicylates and sulfanilamide.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypoglycemia or allergy to insulin or any excipients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Most common: Hypoglycemia (low blood sugar).</li><li>Other side effects include allergic reactions such as redness, swelling, or itching at the injection site.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Insulin is safe for use during pregnancy as it does not cross the placental barrier. No known harm to the baby.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Improper dosing can lead to hyperglycemia (high blood sugar) or hypoglycemia.</li><li>Patients must monitor insulin doses closely, especially in Type 1 diabetes.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypoglycemia, ranging from mild to moderate, is the most common overdose effect. Immediate medical attention is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a refrigerator (2–8°C), do not freeze.</li><li>Room temperature storage is acceptable for up to 1 month if insulin is in use.</li></ul>', 'active', '8807_1746722336.webp', 42, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:18:28', '2025-05-08 20:40:50'),
(377, 'Linita', 'Linagliptin', 18.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Linita 5 mg</strong></p><p>Linita 5 mg is prescribed for managing type 2 diabetes mellitus in adults. It helps improve glycemic control in the following situations:</p><ul><li>As <strong>monotherapy</strong> for patients who are unable to control their blood glucose through diet and exercise alone, and for whom metformin is contraindicated due to renal impairment or is inappropriate due to intolerance.</li><li>As <strong>combination therapy</strong> when diet, exercise, and metformin alone do not provide sufficient glycemic control, often in combination with a sulfonylurea.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Dipeptidyl Peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitor.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Linita 5 mg works by inhibiting the enzyme DPP-4, which is responsible for breaking down incretin hormones (GLP-1 and GIP). These hormones play a role in glucose metabolism by stimulating insulin release from pancreatic beta cells in a glucose-dependent manner and inhibiting glucagon secretion from alpha cells, thereby reducing blood glucose levels.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> Take 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Combination with metformin:</strong> Maintain the same dosage.</li><li><strong>With sulfonylurea:</strong> Consider a lower dose of sulfonylurea to reduce hypoglycemia risk.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> No dose adjustment is required.</li><li><strong>Time of Administration:</strong> Can be taken with or without food at any time of day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>Linita 5 mg is a weak inhibitor of the CYP3A4 enzyme but does not significantly impact the pharmacokinetics of medications like metformin, glyburide, simvastatin, warfarin, digoxin, or oral contraceptives.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Linita\'s active ingredients or excipients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Hypoglycemia (especially when combined with other anti-diabetic drugs like sulfonylureas)</li><li>Nasopharyngitis</li><li>Cough</li><li>Pancreatitis</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category B. Limited studies on pregnant women. Use only if absolutely necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is unknown if Linita is excreted in breast milk. Proceed with caution.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to the active ingredient or excipients should be avoided.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Linita 5 mg increases the levels of active incretin hormones (GLP-1 and GIP) by inhibiting DPP-4. This helps in enhancing insulin release and lowering blood glucose by inhibiting glucagon secretion from pancreatic alpha cells.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Dipeptidyl Peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitors.</p>', 'active', '923_1734549651.png', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:20:51', '2024-12-19 00:20:51'),
(378, 'Nitrocard', 'Nitroglycerin', 7.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.6 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nitrocard 2.6 mg</strong></p><p>Nitrocard 2.6 mg sublingual tablet is used for the acute relief of angina pectoris attacks or for acute prophylaxis in patients with coronary artery disease.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Nitrates: Coronary vasodilators</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Nitrocard 2.6 mg releases nitric oxide (NO), which activates guanylate cyclase, increasing the levels of guanosine 3\'5\' monophosphate in smooth muscle and other tissues. This process leads to the relaxation of smooth muscle, resulting in vasodilatation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li>One sublingual tablet should be dissolved under the tongue at the first sign of an acute anginal attack.</li><li>The dose may be repeated every 5 minutes, up to 3 tablets in a 15-minute period if relief is not achieved.</li><li>If pain persists after 3 tablets, medical attention should be sought promptly.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Caution with antihypertensive drugs, beta-blockers, phenothiazines, and nitrates due to possible additive hypotensive effects.</li><li>Use with calcium channel blockers may cause marked orthostatic hypotension.</li><li>Alcohol may enhance the hypotensive effects.</li><li>Aspirin may enhance the vasodilatory effects.</li><li>Avoid use with ergotamine and similar drugs, or monitor for symptoms of ergotism.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with early myocardial infarction, severe anemia, increased intracranial pressure, or hypersensitivity to Nitrocard.</li><li>Avoid use with sildenafil citrate due to risk of enhanced hypotensive effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Headache, which can be severe and persistent.</li><li>Vertigo, dizziness, weakness, palpitation, and postural hypotension.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Should only be used in pregnancy if clearly needed.</li><li>It is unknown whether Nitrocard is excreted in human milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use the smallest effective dose to manage acute anginal attacks.</li><li>Excessive use may lead to tolerance.</li><li>Use cautiously in patients who are volume-depleted or already hypotensive.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Disclaimer</strong></p><p>The information provided is accurate to the best of our knowledge but does not replace professional medical advice. Consult a healthcare provider for concerns or questions.</p><p><br></p><p><br></p>', 'active', '1301_1734549778.png', 29, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:22:58', '2024-12-19 00:22:58');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(379, 'Metfo', 'Metformin Hydrochloride', 6.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '850 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Metfo 850 mg:</strong> Metfo 850 mg is used for the treatment of type 2 diabetes mellitus, particularly in overweight patients when diet and exercise alone do not provide adequate glycemic control. It can be used alone or in combination with other oral antidiabetic agents or insulin in adults. In children from 10 years of age and adolescents, it may be used as monotherapy or in combination with insulin. Metformin has shown to reduce diabetic complications in overweight type 2 diabetic adults treated as a first-line therapy after diet failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong> Biguanides</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Metformin, a biguanide, is an oral antihyperglycemic agent used to manage type 2 diabetes. It works by reducing both basal and postprandial plasma glucose. Its mechanism differs from that of sulfonylureas and does not cause hypoglycemia. It decreases hepatic glucose production, reduces glucose absorption in the intestines, and enhances insulin sensitivity by increasing peripheral glucose uptake and utilization.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Metformin Immediate Release Tablets:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong> The starting dose is 500 mg twice daily or 850 mg once daily with meals. Gradually increase by 500 mg weekly or 850 mg every 2 weeks, up to a maximum of 2000 mg per day, divided into doses. For more glycemic control, a maximum of 2550 mg per day may be considered.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (10 years and older):</strong> The starting dose is 500 mg twice daily, and increases should be made by 500 mg weekly, up to a maximum of 2000 mg per day, divided doses.</li><li><strong>Metformin Extended Release Tablets:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong> The starting dose is 500 mg once daily with the evening meal, and it can be increased by 500 mg weekly up to a maximum of 2000 mg once daily, or 1000 mg twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong> Not studied for use in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Do not use in patients with eGFR below 30 mL/min/1.73 m². In patients with eGFR below 45 mL/min/1.73 m², assess risk/benefit.</p><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (e.g., Topiramate, Zonisamide)</strong> may increase the risk of lactic acidosis.</li><li>Medications that reduce Metformin clearance (e.g., Ranolazine, Dolutegravir, Cimetidine) may increase its accumulation.</li><li><strong>Alcohol</strong> can enhance Metformin’s effects on lactate metabolism.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Metformin or any excipients</li><li>Acute metabolic acidosis (e.g., lactic acidosis, diabetic ketoacidosis)</li><li>Severe renal failure (GFR &lt;30 mL/min)</li><li>Conditions that alter renal function (e.g., dehydration, severe infection, shock)</li><li>Diseases causing tissue hypoxia (e.g., cardiac/respiratory failure, recent MI)</li><li>Hepatic insufficiency</li><li>Acute alcohol intoxication, alcoholism</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Blood and lymphatic system:</strong> Hemolytic anemia (rare)</li><li><strong>Metabolism and nutrition disorders:</strong> Very rare lactic acidosis, decreased vitamin B12 absorption, peripheral neuropathy in B12 deficiency</li><li><strong>Nervous system disorders:</strong> Common taste disturbances, rare encephalopathy</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal disorders:</strong> Very common nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, and loss of appetite (usually occur during initiation)</li><li><strong>Hepatobiliary disorders:</strong> Very rare liver test abnormalities or hepatitis (resolve upon discontinuation)</li><li><strong>Skin disorders:</strong> Very rare skin reactions (e.g., erythema, pruritus, urticaria)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>No clear association with major birth defects or adverse outcomes when used during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Metformin is excreted into breast milk, but no adverse effects were observed in breastfed infants. A decision should be made whether to discontinue breastfeeding, weighing the benefits and risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Risk of <strong>lactic acidosis</strong> increases with renal impairment. Metformin is primarily excreted by the kidneys.</li><li>May reduce <strong>vitamin B12</strong> levels, leading to megaloblastic anemia.</li><li>Increased risk of <strong>hypoglycemia</strong> when used with insulin or insulin secretagogues.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypoglycemia is not common, but <strong>lactic acidosis</strong> is a potential risk in overdose. Lactic acidosis is a medical emergency and requires hospitalization with hemodialysis for effective removal.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protect from light and moisture, and keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Biguanides</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Metformin decreases hepatic glucose production, reduces glucose absorption in the intestines, and increases insulin sensitivity, thus lowering both basal and postprandial blood glucose levels without causing hypoglycemia.</p>', 'active', '1229_1734549880.webp', 56, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:24:40', '2024-12-19 00:25:35'),
(380, 'Olmesan', 'Olmesartan Medoxomil', 18.00, 15, 'Tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><ul><li>Used for the treatment of hypertension, either alone or in combination with other antihypertensive agents.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Angiotensin-II receptor blocker (ARB).</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Olmesartan is a selective antagonist of the AT1 receptor for angiotensin II, blocking its effects in hypertension.</li><li>It increases plasma renin levels and the concentration of angiotensin I and II while decreasing aldosterone levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: Start with 10 mg once daily. If more blood pressure reduction is needed after 2 weeks, increase to 20 mg daily. For further reduction, increase to 40 mg. Doses higher than 40 mg don\'t provide added benefit.</li><li><strong>Pediatric</strong>: Not established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric</strong>: No dosage change needed unless there are other health factors.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>No significant drug interactions with Olmesartan.</li><li>Food does not affect its absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated for those allergic to any of its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Back pain, bronchitis, diarrhea, headache, increased creatine phosphokinase, and others.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Chest pain, swelling, and arthritis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C (1st trimester), Category D (2nd and 3rd trimesters).</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known if Olmesartan passes into breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor renal function, especially in those with heart failure.</li><li>Can cause changes in kidney function, potentially leading to kidney failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose</strong></p><ul><li>Likely causes hypotension (low blood pressure). Treatment includes monitoring and fluid support.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30ºC, away from light and moisture.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Max dose of 20 mg daily for moderate impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Limit to 20 mg daily if eGFR 20–60 mL/min; avoid if eGFR &lt;20 mL/min.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Olmesartan blocks the actions of angiotensin II at the AT1 receptor, helping to relax blood vessels and reduce blood pressure.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further details!</p>', 'active', '638_1734550068.jpg', 3, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:27:48', '2024-12-19 00:27:48'),
(381, 'Frenxit', 'Flupentixol + Melitracen', 5.00, 15, 'Tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.5 mg+10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Frenxit 0.5 mg + 10 mg is a medication used for the treatment of various psychological conditions such as anxiety, depression, apathy, psychogenic depression, depressive neuroses, masked depression, psychosomatic disorders associated with anxiety and apathy, menopausal depression, and dysphoria in alcoholics and drug addicts. It combines two active ingredients: <strong>Flupentixol</strong> and <strong>Melitracen</strong>, which work synergistically to alleviate symptoms of anxiety and depression.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Combined anxiolytics &amp; antidepressant drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Flupentixol</strong>: A neuroleptic (antipsychotic) drug that blocks dopamine receptors (D1 and D2) in the mesolimbic dopamine system, providing antipsychotic effects. It also has antiemetic properties through dopamine blockade in the chemoreceptor trigger zone.</li><li><strong>Melitracen</strong>: A tricyclic antidepressant that blocks the reuptake of serotonin and norepinephrine, which helps alleviate depression.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: Typically, 2 tablets per day (morning and afternoon). In severe cases, the morning dose may be increased to 2 tablets.</li><li><strong>Elderly patients</strong>: 1 tablet in the morning.</li><li><strong>Maintenance</strong>: Usually, 1 tablet in the morning, or as prescribed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>May enhance the effects of alcohol, barbiturates, and other CNS depressants.</li><li>Simultaneous use with MAO inhibitors can cause hypertensive crises.</li><li>Neuroleptics and thymoleptics may reduce the effect of antihypertensive medications like guanethidine and enhance the effects of adrenaline and noradrenaline.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Post-myocardial infarction recovery phase.</li><li>Bundle-branch conduction defects.</li><li>Untreated narrow-angle glaucoma.</li><li>Acute intoxication from alcohol, barbiturates, or opioids.</li><li>Should not be administered with MAO inhibitors within two weeks.</li><li>Not recommended for excitable or overactive patients, as it may exaggerate these characteristics.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Rare at recommended doses, but may include transient restlessness and insomnia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>The safety of this drug during pregnancy and lactation has not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Gradual withdrawal of sedative tranquillizers if previously used.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose:</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose symptoms may include anticholinergic effects from melitracen or, more rarely, extrapyramidal symptoms due to flupentixol.</li><li>Treatment includes gastric lavage, activated charcoal, supportive measures for the cardiovascular and respiratory systems, and anticonvulsants like diazepam for seizures.</li><li><strong>Do not use adrenaline (epinephrine)</strong> in overdose cases.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, in a dry place, away from light, and out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>The combination of <strong>Flupentixol</strong> (neuroleptic with anxiolytic and antidepressant properties) and <strong>Melitracen</strong> (bipolar thymoleptic with activating properties) provides a comprehensive therapeutic effect for anxiety and depression. The drugs act synergistically, with minimal interaction affecting their pharmacokinetics.</p>', 'active', '1285_1734550271.png', 3, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:31:11', '2024-12-19 00:31:11'),
(382, 'Esloric', 'Allopurinol', 4.03, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Esloric 100 mg</strong></p><p>Esloric 100 mg is indicated for reducing urate/uric acid formation in conditions where urate/uric acid deposition has already occurred, such as in gouty arthritis, skin tophi, and nephrolithiasis. It is also used for the management of 2,8-dihydroxyadenine (2,8-DHA) renal stones due to adenine phospho ribosyltransferase deficiency, and for recurrent mixed calcium oxalate renal stones in the presence of hyperuricosuria when fluid and dietary measures fail.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs used in Gout</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Esloric 100 mg is a xanthine oxidase inhibitor that works by inhibiting the enzyme xanthine oxidase. This enzyme is responsible for converting hypoxanthine to xanthine and xanthine to uric acid. Esloric reduces uric acid production without disrupting purine biosynthesis. It is approximately 90% absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract, with peak plasma levels typically occurring 1.5 to 4.5 hours after administration. The plasma half-life is around 1 to 2 hours. Around 20% of the ingested dose is excreted in the feces.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: Start with a low dose (e.g., 100 mg/day) to minimize adverse reactions, increasing if necessary. Typical doses range from 100–200 mg daily for mild conditions to 700–900 mg daily for severe conditions. Adjust according to serum urate response and renal function.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: For children under 15 years, the dosage is 10–20 mg/kg body weight/day, up to a maximum of 400 mg daily. It is rarely used in children unless for specific conditions such as leukaemia or enzyme disorders like Lesch-Nyhan syndrome.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Use the lowest dose that achieves satisfactory urate reduction.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: In severe renal insufficiency, reduce the dose to less than 100 mg/day or administer single doses at longer intervals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>6-mercaptopurine/azathioprine</strong>: Reduce the dose of these drugs to one-quarter of the usual amount when taken with Esloric, as it prolongs their activity.</li><li><strong>Vidarabine</strong>: Plasma half-life may increase when used with Esloric. Extra vigilance is needed to monitor for enhanced toxicity.</li><li><strong>Theophylline</strong>: Monitor levels when starting or increasing Esloric therapy.</li><li><strong>Ampicillin/Amoxicillin</strong>: An increased incidence of skin rashes may occur when used together with Esloric.</li><li><strong>Ciclosporin</strong>: Plasma concentration may increase when used concurrently with Esloric.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Esloric 100 mg is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to the drug.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common side effects include rashes and gastrointestinal disturbances. Rare side effects include malaise, headache, vertigo, drowsiness, visual and taste disturbances, hypertension, alopecia, hepatotoxicity, neuropathy, gynecomastia, and blood disorders.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Esloric should be used in pregnancy only if no safer alternatives are available, and when the disease itself poses a risk to the mother or unborn child. There is no data on its effects on breastfed infants.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Discontinue Esloric immediately if a skin rash or signs of sensitivity occur. Use caution in patients with hepatic or renal impairment, and in those with hypertension or cardiac insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>Ingesting up to 22.5 g of Esloric has not shown adverse effects, though symptoms like nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and dizziness have been reported with ingestion of 20 g. Hydration helps with the excretion of Esloric, and in severe cases, haemodialysis may be required.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place, away from light.</p><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Drugs used in Gout</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Esloric 100 mg inhibits xanthine oxidase, preventing the conversion of hypoxanthine to xanthine and xanthine to uric acid, thus reducing uric acid production.</p>', 'active', '578_1734550387.png', 4, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:33:07', '2024-12-19 00:33:07'),
(383, 'Frulac', 'Furosemide + Spironolactone', 11.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40mg+50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Frulac 40 40+50 mg</strong> is a combination of <strong>Furosemide</strong> and <strong>Spironolactone</strong>, primarily used for treating various conditions related to fluid retention and hypertension. Here are the key details about its indications, dosage, interactions, and other information:</p><p><strong>Indications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Essential Hypertension</strong>: Used to lower blood pressure.</li><li><strong>Chronic Congestive Heart Failure</strong>: Helps in managing symptoms of heart failure.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Cirrhosis</strong> with ascites: Reduces fluid buildup in the abdominal cavity.</li><li><strong>Edema</strong>: Treats swelling caused by excess fluid retention.</li><li><strong>Hyperaldosteronism</strong>: A condition caused by excess aldosterone.</li><li><strong>Resistant Edema</strong>: Edema associated with secondary hyperaldosteronism.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics</li><li>Aldosterone antagonists</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Furosemide</strong> is a loop diuretic that inhibits sodium, potassium, and chloride reabsorption in the ascending Loop of Henle, increasing urine output and reducing fluid retention. However, it can cause potassium loss.</li><li><strong>Spironolactone</strong> is a potassium-sparing diuretic that antagonizes aldosterone\'s effects, leading to sodium excretion and potassium retention, counteracting the potassium loss caused by Furosemide.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Furosemide 20 mg &amp; Spironolactone 50 mg</strong>: 1 to 4 tablets daily, adjusting based on patient response.</li><li><strong>For Furosemide 40 mg &amp; Spironolactone 50 mg</strong>: 1 to 2 tablets daily for previously stabilized patients who need higher doses.</li><li><strong>Use in Children</strong>: Not recommended.</li><li><strong>Use in Elderly</strong>: May require dose adjustment due to slower excretion.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ACE inhibitors, potassium salts</strong>: Risk of hyperkalemia.</li><li><strong>Cardiac glycosides</strong> (e.g., digoxin): Risk of toxicity.</li><li><strong>Corticosteroids</strong>: Risk of hypokalemia.</li><li><strong>NSAIDs</strong>: May reduce Furosemide\'s effectiveness.</li><li><strong>Aminoglycoside antibiotics</strong>: Increased risk of ototoxicity.</li><li><strong>Sucralfate</strong>: May reduce the diuretic effects of Furosemide.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Anuria</strong>, <strong>acute renal insufficiency</strong>, <strong>severe renal impairment</strong>, <strong>hyperkalemia</strong>, <strong>Addison’s disease</strong>, hypersensitivity to the components, or sulphonamides.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Spironolactone</strong>: Headache, drowsiness, gastrointestinal distress, ataxia, confusion, skin rashes, gynecomastia, endocrine disorders, hyperkalemia, and hyponatremia.</li><li><strong>Furosemide</strong>: Dehydration, hypokalemia, dizziness, hypotension, arrhythmias, and ototoxicity in combination with other drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Risk to fetus, particularly with Furosemide.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Both medications are excreted in breast milk; alternative feeding methods should be considered if use is essential.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in patients with electrolyte imbalances, diabetes, enlarged prostate, hypotension, or hypovolemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Drowsiness, confusion, dizziness, diarrhea, vomiting.</li><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: Fluid replacement and correction of any electrolyte imbalance.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Furosemide</strong>: Increases water excretion by inhibiting sodium and chloride reabsorption.</li><li><strong>Spironolactone</strong>: Reduces sodium reabsorption and prevents potassium loss, counteracting the effects of Furosemide.</li></ul><p>This combination effectively treats conditions related to fluid retention and hypertension while balancing electrolytes. Always follow medical guidance for dosage adjustments based on individual needs.</p>', 'active', '1803_1742317239.jpg', 43, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:35:33', '2025-03-18 22:00:39'),
(384, 'Pregaba', 'Pregabalin', 22.00, 10, 'Capsul', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p>', 'active', '4325_1744180426.webp', 7, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:38:21', '2025-04-09 10:33:46'),
(385, 'Ramoril', 'Ramipril', 2.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '1.25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension:</strong> Used to lower blood pressure, either as monotherapy or combined with other antihypertensive drugs.</li><li><strong>Congestive Heart Failure:</strong> Can be used alone or with diuretics.</li><li><strong>Post-Myocardial Infarction:</strong> Treatment of patients showing early signs of heart failure within the first few days.</li><li><strong>Nephropathy Treatment:</strong> For non-diabetic or diabetic glomerular nephropathy (both overt and incipient).</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Risk Reduction:</strong> For patients at high cardiovascular risk, such as those with coronary heart disease, stroke history, peripheral vascular disease, or diabetes with additional risk factors (e.g., hypertension, elevated cholesterol, smoking).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Ramoril 1.25 mg is an ACE inhibitor that blocks the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, reducing vasoconstriction and aldosterone secretion. This leads to a reduction in blood pressure and helps manage heart failure, stroke, myocardial infarction, and cardiovascular death risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension:</strong> Initial dose of 1.25–2.5 mg daily, with adjustments every 2 weeks as necessary. Maintenance dosage: 2.5–20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Heart Failure after Myocardial Infarction:</strong> Start with 2.5 mg twice daily, reducing to 1.25 mg twice daily if hypotension occurs, then increasing to 5 mg twice daily as tolerated.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Event Prevention:</strong> Begin with 2.5 mg daily for the first week, then increase to 5 mg daily for 3 weeks. Maintenance dose of 10 mg daily may be used if tolerated.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Starting dose of 1.25 mg daily, increasing up to a maximum of 5 mg for hypertension or 2.5 mg twice daily for heart failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>Take orally with sufficient liquid; do not chew or crush tablets. Food does not affect absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>May cause serious hypotension if used with diuretics, particularly potassium-sparing ones. Can increase lithium levels. NSAIDs may reduce antihypertensive effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Ramoril or ACE inhibitors.</li><li>History of angioedema or concomitant use with sacubitril/valsartan.</li><li>Renal artery stenosis, hypotension, unstable states, or pregnancy.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Dizziness, headache, fatigue, asthenia.</li><li>Rare: Angioneurotic edema, hyperkalemia, anaphylactic reactions.</li><li>Other possible side effects: Cough, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, rash.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Discontinue if pregnancy is detected. Do not use during lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with renal or hepatic impairment, hyperkalemia, or hypotension.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Management:</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose may cause severe hypotension, shock, bradycardia, and renal failure. Treatment includes detoxification (e.g., gastric lavage) and potentially α1-adrenergic agonists or volume substitution.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store at 30°C or below, away from light, and out of reach of children. Do not use past the expiration date.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Start with 1.25 mg daily and adjust as needed.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> No available information on use in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Inhibits ACE, leading to decreased angiotensin II levels, reduced vasoconstriction, and decreased aldosterone secretion, contributing to its antihypertensive and heart failure management effects.</li></ul>', 'active', '129_1734550835.png', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:40:35', '2024-12-19 00:40:35'),
(386, 'Cardex', 'Carvedilol', 1.75, 14, 'Tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '3.125 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Cardex 3.125 mg is used to treat mild to severe heart failure, particularly of ischemic or cardiomyopathic origin. It is often prescribed alongside digitalis, diuretics, and ACE inhibitors to reduce the progression of the disease, as indicated by reductions in cardiovascular death, hospitalizations, or the need for additional heart failure medication adjustments. It may also be used in patients who are unable to tolerate ACE inhibitors or those not receiving digitalis, hydralazine, or nitrate therapy.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Cardex 3.125 mg is a non-selective β-adrenergic blocker with additional α-1 adrenergic blocking activity, which leads to vasodilation and a reduction in peripheral resistance, thus lowering blood pressure.</p><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Heart failure</strong> (ischemic or cardiomyopathic)</li><li><strong>Hypertension</strong></li><li><strong>Angina pectoris</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: Start with 12.5 mg once daily, increasing after 2 days to 25 mg daily. If needed, increase to a maximum of 50 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Angina</strong>: Start with 12.5 mg twice daily for 2 days, then 25 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Heart failure</strong>: Start with 3.125 mg twice daily, gradually increasing to 25 mg twice daily (depending on tolerance and weight).</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Digoxin</strong>: Cardex may increase digoxin levels, requiring more monitoring.</li><li><strong>Rifampin</strong>: Decreases Cardex\'s plasma concentration.</li><li><strong>Clonidine</strong>: Abrupt discontinuation may cause an exaggerated pressor response; Cardex\'s α-blocking activity may mitigate this.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Decompensated heart failure requiring inotropic therapy</li><li>Bronchial asthma</li><li>Second or third-degree AV block</li><li>Sick sinus syndrome (without pacemaker)</li><li>Severe bradycardia or cardiogenic shock</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Dizziness, headache, fatigue, bradycardia, gastrointestinal disturbances</li><li><strong>Serious</strong>: Exacerbation of heart failure, angina, AV block, allergic reactions, liver enzyme changes, and thrombocytopenia</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor clinical status in heart failure patients after dose initiation or adjustment.</li><li>Use caution in patients with hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Not recommended during pregnancy or lactation due to lack of sufficient data.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose:</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdose include severe hypotension, bradycardia, heart failure, shock, and seizures. Treatment is symptomatic, with the use of atropine, glucagon, or sympathomimetics as needed.</p><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture, and out of reach of children.</p><p>This medication falls under the classes of alpha adrenoceptor blocking drugs, beta-blockers, and beta-adrenoceptor blocking drugs.</p>', 'active', '1680_1734550986.png', 7, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:43:06', '2024-12-19 00:43:06'),
(387, 'Menaril', 'Betahistine Dihydrochloride', 3.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '8 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Menaril (Betahistine 8 mg) is a medication used to manage Meniere’s disease and Meniere-like syndromes, which are characterized by episodes of vertigo, tinnitus, and/or hearing loss, often accompanied by nausea and vomiting.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</p><ul><li>Drugs used in Meniere\'s disease</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><ul><li>Betahistine works by acting as both a partial histamine H1-receptor agonist and a histamine H3-receptor antagonist in neuronal tissue. It improves the microcirculation in the labyrinth of the ear, reducing endolymphatic pressure, which helps manage symptoms of Meniere\'s disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Initial dose</strong>: 8 mg to 16 mg, three times daily, preferably with meals.</li><li><strong>Maintenance dose</strong>: Up to 48 mg daily.</li><li>Betahistine is not recommended for children and adolescents under 18 years due to insufficient safety and efficacy data.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong>:</p><ul><li>There are no proven hazardous interactions. Although a theoretical interaction with antihistamines is possible, no such cases have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with pheochromocytoma.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li>Generally well-tolerated, but may cause mild gastrointestinal disturbances, headaches, rashes, or pruritus in some cases.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Limited data is available on the use of betahistine in pregnant women, and animal studies do not show direct harm. As a precaution, it is advised to avoid betahistine during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Insufficient data on whether betahistine is excreted in human milk, so it should not be used during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with bronchial asthma or peptic ulcer disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Symptoms</strong>:</p><ul><li>Symptoms may include nausea, somnolence, abdominal pain, convulsions, and possible pulmonary or cardiac complications. Supportive treatment is recommended for management.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong>:</p><ul><li>Keep at temperatures below 30°C, away from light and moisture, and out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</p><ul><li>Betahistine works by stimulating H1-receptors in the inner ear, causing vasodilation and increasing blood vessel permeability, which reduces endolymphatic pressure. This prevents labyrinth rupture, alleviating vertigo and hearing loss symptoms. Additionally, it acts as a H3-receptor antagonist, increasing neurotransmitter turnover (including serotonin), which helps balance the vestibular system and reduce vertigo.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '973_1734551196.png', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:46:36', '2024-12-19 00:46:36'),
(388, 'Amilin', 'Amitriptyline hydrochloride', 1.75, 14, 'Tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is a rewritten version of the information about Amilin 25 mg, ensuring it avoids direct copying while preserving the original content:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Amilin 25 mg</strong></p><p>Amilin 25 mg is prescribed for several conditions, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Depressive Illness</strong>: Particularly useful when sedation is required.</li><li><strong>Nocturnal Enuresis</strong> (bedwetting) in children.</li><li><strong>Migraine Prophylaxis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Tension Headaches</strong>.</li><li><strong>Chronic Pain</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Tricyclic Antidepressant</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Amitriptyline HCl, the active component of Amilin, is an antidepressant with sedative properties. Its exact mechanism in humans remains unclear. Unlike monoamine oxidase inhibitors, it doesn\'t primarily act through central nervous system stimulation. It works by inhibiting the membrane pump mechanism that is responsible for the reuptake of norepinephrine and serotonin in certain neurons. This results in enhanced or prolonged neuronal activity, as the reuptake of these neurotransmitters is critical for terminating their transmitting activity. This action is believed to contribute to the antidepressant effect of Amitriptyline.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Depression</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: Start with 50-70 mg per day, divided into doses or taken as a single dose at bedtime. The dose may gradually increase as needed, up to 150-200 mg. Maintenance dose is usually between 50-100 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly &amp; Adolescents</strong>: Start with 25-50 mg daily, either in divided doses or as a single bedtime dose.</li><li><strong>Nocturnal Enuresis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6-10 years)</strong>: 10-20 mg at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (11-16 years)</strong>: 25-50 mg at bedtime for up to 3 months, followed by gradual discontinuation.</li><li><strong>Migraine Prophylaxis</strong>: 100 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Tension Headaches</strong>: 10-25 mg, three times a day.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Alcohol &amp; Opioids</strong>: Tricyclic antidepressants (TCA) can enhance the sedative effects of alcohol and opioid analgesics.</li><li><strong>Moxifloxacin &amp; Terfenadine</strong>: May increase the risk of ventricular arrhythmias when used with TCA.</li><li><strong>Disulfiram &amp; Cimetidine</strong>: These drugs inhibit the metabolism of Amitriptyline.</li><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: Increase the risk of postural hypotension when combined with TCA.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Amitriptyline is contraindicated in individuals with:</p><ul><li>Myocardial infarction</li><li>Arrhythmias, especially heart block</li><li>Mania</li><li>Severe liver disease</li></ul><p>It may cause sedation, which could impair the ability to drive or operate machinery.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Anticholinergic</strong>: Dry mouth, blurred vision, excessive sweating, urinary retention.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular</strong>: Hypotension, tachycardia, palpitations, and, rarely, myocardial infarction.</li><li><strong>CNS &amp; Neuromuscular</strong>: Confusion, disorientation, disturbed concentration, and delusions.</li><li><strong>Allergic Reactions</strong>: Skin rash, urticaria, photosensitivity.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Nausea, anorexia, weight loss, constipation, diarrhea, and more.</li><li><strong>Endocrine</strong>: Breast enlargement, testicular swelling, galactorrhea.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: Amitriptyline is not recommended during pregnancy, particularly in the first and third trimesters, as its safety has not been established.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: Amitriptyline is excreted in breast milk, and due to possible severe adverse effects in infants, a decision should be made whether to continue breastfeeding or discontinue the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in individuals with a history of epilepsy, glaucoma, urinary retention, heart disease, diabetes, hepatic impairment, thyroid disorders, or psychoses.</li><li>May exacerbate mania in patients with a history of psychotic disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>The precise mechanism of action of amitriptyline remains unclear, but it is believed to work by inhibiting the reuptake of norepinephrine and serotonin in the brain, which may alleviate symptoms of depression. It may also have analgesic effects, though whether this is linked to its mood-altering properties or due to another mechanism is not fully known.</p><p><br></p><p>This version conveys the same key points but rephrases the content to avoid direct duplication.</p>', 'active', '366_1734551356.png', 7, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:49:16', '2024-12-19 00:49:16'),
(389, 'Rocovas', 'Rosuvastatin', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia (Familial and Non-familial)</li><li>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia (Familial)</li><li>Mixed Dyslipidemia (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs</li><li>Statins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Rocovas 5 mg is a selective and competitive inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase, the enzyme that converts 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl coenzyme A to mevalonate, a precursor of cholesterol. Its lipid-modifying effects occur through two mechanisms:</p><ol><li>Increasing the number of hepatic LDL receptors on cell surfaces, enhancing LDL uptake and catabolism.</li><li>Inhibiting hepatic synthesis of VLDL, which reduces the number of VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li>Adults: 5-40 mg once daily (maximum 40 mg for patients not reaching LDL-C goals with 20 mg).</li><li>Homozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia (HoFH): Starting dose 20 mg/day.</li><li>Pediatric patients with HeFH:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">8 to &lt;10 years: 5-10 mg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 to 17 years: 5-20 mg/day.</li><li>Pediatric patients with HoFH: 20 mg/day (for patients 7-17 years).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong> Rocovas 5 mg can be taken with or without food at any time of day.</p><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong>: Increases exposure. Limit dose to 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil</strong>: Avoid combining; limit to 10 mg once daily if necessary.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or Atazanavir/Ritonavir</strong>: Increases exposure; limit dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants</strong>: Can prolong INR. Achieve a stable INR before starting treatment and monitor frequently.</li><li><strong>Concomitant lipid-lowering therapies</strong>: May increase the risk of skeletal muscle effects when used with fibrates or niacin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to the product components</li><li>Liver disease (including persistent elevations in hepatic transaminases)</li><li>Pregnancy or women who may become pregnant</li><li>Nursing mothers</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong> Rocovas 5 mg is generally well tolerated. Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety during pregnancy has not been established.</li><li>It is not known if Rocovas 5 mg is excreted in human milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Skeletal Muscle Effects</strong>: Risks increase with higher doses (40 mg), advanced age (&gt;65 years), hypothyroidism, renal impairment, and combination with specific drugs. Patients should report any unexplained muscle pain, tenderness, or weakness.</li><li><strong>Liver Enzyme Abnormalities</strong>: Liver enzymes should be monitored before and during treatment, as persistent elevations may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: The safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1258_1734551441.jpg', 2, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:50:41', '2024-12-19 00:50:41'),
(390, 'Alphapress XR', 'Prazosin Hydrochloride', 17.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Alphapress XR 5 mg (Prazosin)</strong> is a medication primarily used to treat hypertension, left ventricular failure, Raynaud’s phenomenon/disease, and benign prostatic hyperplasia. It is an alpha-1 adrenergic receptor blocker, which helps lower blood pressure and relieve symptoms associated with these conditions. Below are the key details:</p><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: Effective for essential and secondary hypertension. It can be used alone or in combination with other antihypertensive agents.</li><li><strong>Left Ventricular Failure</strong>: Used in conjunction with other treatments for patients with left ventricular failure who haven\'t responded well to conventional therapy.</li><li><strong>Raynaud\'s Phenomenon and Disease</strong>: Helps reduce the severity and frequency of attacks.</li><li><strong>Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH)</strong>: Improves symptoms like urinary obstruction.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: Start with 0.5 mg at bedtime, then increase as needed, aiming for 20 mg/day in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Left Ventricular Failure</strong>: Start with 0.5 mg 2-4 times daily, increasing as tolerated.</li><li><strong>Raynaud\'s Disease</strong>: Begin with 0.5 mg twice a day, and adjust as needed.</li><li><strong>Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia</strong>: Start with 0.5 mg twice a day, increase as necessary, with a usual dose of 2 mg twice a day.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>Take with or without food.</li><li>Starting dose should be taken at dinner, at least 2-3 hours before sleep.</li><li>Extended-release tablets should be swallowed whole and not divided.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Dizziness, headache, weakness, nausea, palpitations, fatigue.</li><li><strong>Serious</strong>: Orthostatic hypotension, fluid retention, dizziness, fainting, and potential worsening of pre-existing narcolepsy.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor blood pressure, especially during the initial dose and dosage increases.</li><li>Caution is advised in elderly patients, those with renal or hepatic dysfunction, and those with heart failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Not suitable for patients allergic to quinazolines.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose:</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose may cause drowsiness and depressed reflexes but usually does not result in hypotension. Treat hypotension with supportive measures, volume expanders, and vasopressors if needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C—use only if the benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Excreted in small amounts in breast milk; use cautiously.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place away from light, below 30°C. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p>This medication must be prescribed and dosages adjusted based on individual patient needs, and careful monitoring is essential to minimize side effects and ensure efficacy.</p>', 'active', '1383_1734551583.png', 6, 12, NULL, '2024-12-19 00:53:03', '2024-12-19 00:53:03'),
(391, 'Azmasol HFA', 'Salbutamol', 220.00, 1, 'Refill', '1 Refill Bottle', 'Refill', '100 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><p>Azmasol HFA is a bronchodilator used for managing asthma, chronic bronchitis, emphysema, and other airway obstruction conditions.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Selectively stimulates beta2-adrenoceptors in bronchial smooth muscle, causing bronchodilation within 5 minutes, lasting 4–6 hours.</li><li>Exhibits anti-inflammatory effects by inhibiting mast cell mediators.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral (Syrup/Tablets):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (2-6 years): 2.5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (6-12 years): 5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 2-4 mg tablets or 5-10 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily. Max: 8 mg per dose.</li><li><strong>Nebulizer:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 0.5–1 ml (diluted) up to 4 times daily; max 40 mg/day under supervision.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (&lt;12 years): 0.5 ml (diluted) 2-4 ml saline; higher doses up to 1 ml if needed.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: Subcutaneous/Intramuscular: 500 mcg every 4 hours. Intravenous: 250 mcg slow infusion. Pediatric use not established.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Capsule:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 1–2 capsules every 4–6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (≥4 years): 1 capsule 3–4 times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to the drug or its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p>Tremors, palpitations, muscle cramps, tachycardia, headaches, and vasodilation.</p><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in hyperthyroidism, cardiovascular diseases, hypertension, glaucoma, and diabetes.</li><li>Monitor potassium levels during high doses to avoid hypokalemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Use only if benefits outweigh risks. Not known if excreted in human milk; may require discontinuation of nursing.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Stimulates beta-2 adrenergic receptors in bronchial muscles, improving pulmonary function through muscle relaxation.</p><p><strong>Overdose Management:</strong></p><p>Symptoms: Angina, nausea, tremors. Treat with cardio-selective beta-blockers cautiously in bronchospasm history.</p>', 'active', '818_1734622445.jpg', 3, 1, NULL, '2024-12-19 20:34:05', '2024-12-19 20:34:43');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(392, 'Reversair', 'Montelukast', 18.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma.</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB).</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Leukotriene receptor antagonists.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Adolescents (≥15 years):</strong> 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics (6–14 years):</strong> 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics (2–5 years):</strong> 4 mg once daily (tablet or granules).</li><li><strong>Pediatrics (6 months–2 years):</strong> 4 mg granules once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hepatic/Renal Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> No adjustment required.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Reversair 10 mg selectively inhibits cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT1), preventing airway inflammation, edema, and smooth muscle contraction in asthma and allergic rhinitis.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Upper respiratory infections, headache, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Uncommon/Rare:</strong> Anxiety, dizziness, drowsiness, depression, angioedema, hallucinations, hepatic issues.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> Use only if necessary, as safety in humans is not well established.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Exercise caution; may be secreted in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Not for acute asthma attacks.</li><li>Maintain rescue medication for emergencies.</li><li>Gradual corticosteroid dose reduction under medical supervision.</li><li>Avoid aspirin/NSAIDs in aspirin-sensitive patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry place, away from light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p>', 'active', '345_1734622687.png', 44, 1, NULL, '2024-12-19 20:38:07', '2024-12-19 20:38:07'),
(393, 'Riboson', 'Riboflavin', 0.30, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Prevention and treatment of vitamin B2 (riboflavin) deficiencies: cheilosis, angular stomatitis, glossitis, keratitis, seborrheic dermatitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Vitamin-B preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Riboson 5 mg (vitamin B2) is a water-soluble B vitamin essential for energy production, metabolism of fats, proteins, and carbohydrates, red blood cell formation, antibody production, and healthy skin, hair, and nails. It also helps prevent cataracts and supports antioxidant activity.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Deficiency Treatment (Adults):</strong> 5–30 mg daily in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Migraine Prevention:</strong> 400 mg daily (up to 3 months for best results).</li><li><strong>Cataract Prevention:</strong> ~2.6 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Dietary Recommendations (Daily):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Men ≥14 years: 1.3 mg; Women ≥18 years: 1.1 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pregnant: 1.4 mg; Breastfeeding: 1.6 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Infants and children: Varies by age (0.3–0.9 mg).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Yellow-orange urine discoloration.</li><li>High doses: Diarrhea, increased urine output, rare side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Safe in recommended doses: 1.4 mg/day (pregnant) and 1.6 mg/day (breastfeeding).</li><li>Possibly safe short-term in higher doses (e.g., 15 mg every 2 weeks).</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Non-toxic; no significant warnings or precautions.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Riboson 5 mg is a precursor for key coenzymes (FMN and FAD) essential for antioxidant enzyme activity and energy metabolism. It also supports thyroid activity, growth, and overall health.</p>', 'active', '1798_1734622906.webp', 28, 1, NULL, '2024-12-19 20:41:46', '2024-12-19 20:41:46'),
(394, 'Amodis', 'Metronidazole', 1.70, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Prevention of post-operative infections caused by anaerobic bacteria.</li><li>Treatment of septicaemia, peritonitis, brain abscess, pelvic infections, amoebiasis (intestinal and extra-intestinal), giardiasis, bacterial vaginosis, acute dental infections, anaerobic infections, leg ulcers, pressure sores, and trichomoniasis.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Amoebicides, Anti-diarrhoeal, Antiprotozoal</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Amodis 400 mg, an imidazole antibacterial, exerts bactericidal action by interacting with DNA in anaerobes after metabolic reduction.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> Dosage varies by condition, commonly 200-800 mg in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> Adjusted based on age/weight (e.g., 7.5 mg/kg for anaerobic infections).</li><li><strong>Special Forms:</strong> Vaginal gel, suppository, or IV infusion for specific conditions.</li><li>Follow detailed dosing for specific diseases like amoebiasis, giardiasis, and surgical prophylaxis.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Amodis or nitroimidazole derivatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in hepatic encephalopathy; monitor for neuropathy in prolonged use.</li><li>Warn patients about darkened urine.</li><li>Avoid alcohol during treatment to prevent disulfiram-like reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Metallic taste, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, rash.</li><li>Rare: Neuropathy, dizziness, seizures.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Alcohol: Risk of flushing, vomiting, tachycardia.</li><li>Warfarin: Increased anticoagulant effect.</li><li>Lithium, cyclosporin, phenytoin, and phenobarbital may affect drug levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Category B: Use only if clearly needed.</li><li>Excreted in breast milk—use with caution.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Vomiting, ataxia, disorientation.</li><li>No antidote; provide supportive treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light, out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '186_1734623086.webp', 4, 1, NULL, '2024-12-19 20:44:46', '2024-12-19 20:44:46'),
(395, 'Docopa', 'Doxophylline', 8.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Bronchial asthma</li><li>Bronchospasm</li><li>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</li><li>Pulmonary diseases with spastic bronchial components</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Bronchodilator (Methyl xanthine derivatives)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Novel bronchodilator with a dioxolane group in position 7 (differs from Theophylline).</li><li>Selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, relaxing bronchial smooth muscles.</li><li>Reduced affinity for adenosine A1/A2 receptors, improving safety profile.</li><li>Inhibits platelet-activating factor (PAF) and leukotriene production.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 400 mg tablet 2-3 times daily or as prescribed.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> 200 mg tablet 2-3 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong></li></ul><blockquote>12 years: 10 ml syrup or 200 mg tablet 2-3 times daily.</blockquote><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><br></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years: 6-9 mg/kg twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid with other xanthines or ephedrine (risk of toxicity).</li><li>Certain drugs (e.g., cimetidine, propranolol) may reduce clearance, increasing blood levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Acute myocardial infarction</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Lactating women</li><li>Hypersensitivity to components</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Rare but similar to caffeine overdose: nausea, vomiting, headache, upset stomach, heartburn.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Safe in animal studies but limited human data. Use only when necessary. Contraindicated during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Dose adjustments may be required for liver disease, congestive heart failure, smoking, or concomitant drugs affecting clearance.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Severe cardiac arrhythmias, tonic-clonic seizures. Symptomatic treatment for cardiovascular collapse is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Bronchodilator (Methyl xanthine derivatives)</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Selective phosphodiesterase 4 inhibitor with better safety compared to Theophylline due to lower adenosine receptor affinity.</li></ul>', 'active', '1989_1734623302.webp', 29, 1, NULL, '2024-12-19 20:48:22', '2025-05-02 08:38:23'),
(396, 'Calboral-D', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 11.00, 30, 'Tablet', '30 Tablet In Bottle', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment &amp; prevention of:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Osteoporosis, Osteomalacia, Tetany, Hypoparathyroidism, Disorders of osteogenesis.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Calcium supplementation during childhood, pregnancy, lactation, and for elderly patients.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Other uses: Rickets, pancreatitis, phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Composition:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong> 500 mg elemental Calcium (from Calcium Carbonate USP 1250 mg) &amp; 200 IU Vitamin D.</li><li><strong>DX Tablet:</strong> 600 mg elemental Calcium &amp; 400 IU Vitamin D.</li><li><strong>Effervescent Tablet:</strong> Same as DX tablet.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Calcium ensures critical functions, supports bone health, and prevents osteoporosis.</li><li>Vitamin D3 aids calcium absorption, promotes bone formation, and has synergistic effects with calcium.</li><li>Coral Calcium mimics human bone structure and provides better absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 1 tablet once or twice daily after meals with water.</li><li><strong>Effervescent Tablet:</strong> Dissolve in water, stir, and drink.</li><li>Follow physician’s directions for personalized dosage.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid use with thiazide diuretics, corticosteroids, or ion exchange resins (risk of hypercalcemia).</li><li>May interfere with tetracyclines, bisphosphonates, and foods high in oxalic or phytic acid.</li><li>Monitor calcium levels during concomitant therapy with cardiac glycosides or diuretics.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypercalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, hypercalciuria, nephrolithiasis.</li><li>Severe renal insufficiency.</li><li>Hypersensitivity to components.</li><li>Use with digoxin requires monitoring.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>GI irritation, constipation, nausea, or allergic reactions.</li><li>Rarely: Hypercalcemia in chronic renal failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Safe if daily calcium intake ≤1500 mg and Vitamin D ≤600 IU.</li><li>Monitor calcium levels to avoid hypercalcemia.</li><li>Both calcium and Vitamin D pass into breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor serum calcium levels and renal function in long-term therapy, especially in elderly patients or those at risk for stone formation.</li><li>Use caution in patients with sarcoidosis or renal impairment.</li><li>Avoid immobilization-induced hypercalcemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypercalcemia symptoms: Nausea, vomiting, fatigue, confusion, and severe cases may lead to cardiac arrhythmias or coma.</li><li>Treatment: Discontinue calcium and related medications, provide rehydration, and monitor electrolytes.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protect from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1799_1734623604.png', 4, 1, NULL, '2024-12-19 20:53:24', '2024-12-19 20:53:24'),
(397, 'Monocast', 'Montelukast', 17.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma.</li><li>Prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB).</li><li>Relief of Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis (AR).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Leukotriene receptor antagonist.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Monocast 10 mg selectively inhibits Cysteinyl Leukotriene (CysLT1) receptors, reducing airway inflammation, edema, and smooth muscle contraction associated with asthma and allergic rhinitis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Adolescents (≥15 years):</strong> 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6–14 years: 5 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2–5 years: 4 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6 months–2 years: 4 mg oral granules once daily.</li><li>No dosage adjustment needed for elderly, or patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic/renal insufficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Headache, diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, fever, upper respiratory tract infections.</li><li><strong>Uncommon:</strong> Anxiety, dizziness, dry mouth, irritability, sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Pulmonary eosinophilia, hepatic issues, hallucinations, suicidal thoughts.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Category B: Use only if necessary, as the drug crosses the placenta in animals and may be excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Not for acute asthma attacks or as a replacement for corticosteroids.</li><li>Patients with aspirin sensitivity should avoid NSAIDs while on Monocast.</li><li>Use with caution in breastfeeding mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common symptoms: Nausea, vomiting, headache, abdominal pain.</li><li>Management: Supportive care, clinical monitoring, and gastric decontamination if needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry place, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1252_1734623906.jpg', 3, 1, NULL, '2024-12-19 20:58:26', '2024-12-19 20:58:26'),
(398, 'Othera', 'Esomeprazole', 11.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD).</li><li>Healing and maintenance of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Symptomatic GERD.</li><li>Reduction of NSAID-associated gastric ulcers.</li><li>H. pylori eradication (Triple therapy).</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI).</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Othera 20 mg inhibits gastric acid secretion by covalently binding to H+/K+-ATPase enzyme on gastric parietal cells. This effect is irreversible, and acid secretion resumes only with new enzyme expression, providing a duration of effect lasting over 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablets/Capsules:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Erosive Esophagitis: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. May extend treatment by another 4-8 weeks if needed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance: 20 mg once daily (up to 6 months).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Symptomatic GERD: 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">H. pylori Eradication: 20 mg twice daily for 10 days (with Amoxicillin 1000 mg and Clarithromycin 500 mg, both twice daily).</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">GERD with Erosive Esophagitis: 20-40 mg once daily by IV (over 10-30 minutes).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Headache, diarrhea, abdominal pain.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Category C: Avoid during pregnancy and lactation unless benefits outweigh risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Exclude malignancy before treating gastric ulcers or dyspepsia.</li><li>Refer to antibiotic prescribing information when used in combination therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution for Injection:</strong></p><ul><li>Reconstitute with 5 ml of 0.9% Sodium Chloride. Final solution (0.8 mg/ml) can be further diluted for IV administration. Use within the recommended timeframe.</li></ul>', 'active', '324_1734624156.jpg', 44, 1, NULL, '2024-12-19 21:02:36', '2024-12-19 21:02:36'),
(399, 'Azmasol HFA', 'Salbutamol', 250.00, 1, 'Inhaler', '1 Inhaler', 'Inhaler', '100 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Asthma</li><li>Chronic Bronchitis</li><li>Emphysema</li><li>Airway obstruction</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulant.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Azmasol HFA 100 mcg is a selective beta2-adrenoceptor agonist that provides rapid bronchodilation within 5 minutes, lasting 4-6 hours. It also inhibits the release of bronchoconstrictor mediators from mast cells.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral:</strong></li><li>Children:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">2-6 years: 2.5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">6-12 years: 5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Over 12 years: 5-10 ml syrup or 2-4 mg tablets, 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 2-4 mg tablets, 3-4 times daily (max single dose: 8 mg).</li><li><strong>Inhalation:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 1-2 inhalation capsules every 4-6 hours as needed. For exercise-induced asthma, 400 mcg 15-30 minutes before exercise.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children: 1 inhalation capsule every 4-6 hours; may increase to 2 capsules if necessary.</li><li><strong>Nebulizer Solution:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 2.5-5 mg up to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children: 2.5 mg up to 4 times daily (may increase to 5 mg if needed).</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 250-500 mcg via IV, IM, or SC routes under physician supervision.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Azmasol HFA 100 mcg or its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Tremor, palpitations, muscle cramps.</li><li>Rare: Tachycardia, headache, peripheral vasodilation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in patients with hyperthyroidism, cardiovascular disease, hypertension, diabetes, or glaucoma.</li><li>May cause hypokalemia at high doses, increasing risk of cardiac arrhythmias.</li><li>Avoid prolonged high-dose use to prevent tachyphylaxis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Use only if benefits outweigh risks (Category C).</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Not known if excreted in human milk; consider risks to mother and infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Angina, nausea, headache, tremor.</li><li>Treatment: Cardio-selective beta-blockers (use cautiously in bronchospasm patients).</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Stimulates beta-2 adrenergic receptors in bronchial smooth muscle, causing relaxation and improved pulmonary function.</p>', 'active', '46_1734624520.jpg', 3, 1, NULL, '2024-12-19 21:08:40', '2024-12-19 21:08:40'),
(400, 'Efodio', 'Domperidone Maleate', 4.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><ul><li>Dyspeptic symptoms: Fullness, distension, upper abdominal pain, belching, nausea, vomiting, heartburn.</li><li>Acute nausea/vomiting (functional, organic, infectious, dietetic, or drug-induced).</li><li>Dopamine-agonist-induced nausea (e.g., in Parkinson\'s disease).</li><li>Improves gastric motility and barium transit in radiological studies.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Motility stimulants, dopamine antagonists, prokinetic drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Dopamine receptor antagonist with gastroprokinetic action.</li><li>Enhances gastric emptying and peristalsis with minimal central nervous system effects due to limited blood-brain barrier penetration.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10–20 mg every 6–8 hours, max 80 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2–0.4 mg/kg every 6–8 hours.</li><li>Take 15–30 minutes before meals; duration for acute nausea and vomiting should not exceed 12 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid with anticholinergics, antacids, and CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., azole antifungals, macrolides).</li><li>May alter absorption of sustained-release or enteric-coated drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity, gastrointestinal bleeding, obstruction, perforation, prolactinoma, neonates.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Rare: Intestinal cramps, galactorrhea, gynecomastia, rash.</li><li>Very rare: Extrapyramidal symptoms, hyperprolactinemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Not recommended during pregnancy; minimal excretion in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in children, hepatic impairment, and severe renal insufficiency.</li><li>Reduce dosing frequency for prolonged use in renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Drowsiness, disorientation, extrapyramidal reactions.</li><li>Treatment: Activated charcoal, anticholinergics, or antihistamines.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><ul><li>Below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Key Notes</strong></p><ul><li>Increases gastric motility and prevents regurgitation.</li><li>Regular monitoring advised for prolonged use in patients with hepatic or renal conditions.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '248_1734624745.webp', 10, 1, NULL, '2024-12-19 21:12:25', '2024-12-19 21:12:25'),
(401, 'Nutrivit-C', 'Vitamin C [Ascorbic acid]', 1.90, 10, 'Chewable Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Chewable Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li>Prevents and treats scurvy.</li><li>Aids in wound healing and bone repair.</li><li>Supports collagen formation in connective tissues.</li><li>Enhances iron absorption and treats anemia.</li><li>Boosts hemoglobin and red blood cell production.</li><li>Strengthens immunity against infections and common colds.</li><li>Promotes healthy capillaries, gums, and teeth.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><p>Vitamin-C Preparations</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><p>Ascorbic Acid (Vitamin C) is a water-soluble vitamin involved in biological oxidations and reductions. It supports collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy, characterized by bleeding gums, anemia, and slow wound healing.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration </strong></p><p><strong>Oral Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prevention of scurvy:</strong> 1 tablet daily.</li><li><strong>Treatment of scurvy:</strong> 1–2 tablets daily.</li><li><strong>Stroke risk reduction (elderly):</strong> 1–2 tablets daily.</li><li><strong>Maximum safe dose:</strong> 2000 mg daily in divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Parenteral Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>300 mg to 1 g daily for scurvy or wound healing.</li><li>Severe burns: 1–2 g daily.</li><li>Administer via IM, IV, or subcutaneous route if oral intake is not possible.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hazardous:</strong> Incompatible with aminophylline, bleomycin, sodium bicarbonate, and others.</li><li><strong>Beneficial:</strong> Enhances iron absorption and prolongs paracetamol half-life.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Mega-doses (&gt;2000 mg/day): Diarrhea, bloating, and risk of iron over-absorption (harmful in certain conditions).</li><li>Rare: Hyperoxaluria, hemolysis in G6PD deficiency.</li><li>Pregnancy: Excess doses (&gt;5 g/day) may cause abortion.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid mega-doses during pregnancy to prevent neonatal scurvy.</li><li>Contraindicated in patients with hyperoxaluria.</li><li>Can cause false-positive results in tests for glucose, uric acid, and occult blood.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions :</strong></p><p>Store below 30˚C in a dry place.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Mode of Action :</strong></p><p>Vitamin C is an antioxidant that aids in tissue repair, immunity, wound healing, and the metabolism of cholesterol and folic acid. It supports skin health, strengthens immunity, and prevents cellular damage.</p>', 'active', '552_1734625243.webp', 44, 1, NULL, '2024-12-19 21:20:43', '2024-12-22 22:54:34'),
(402, 'Ebatin', 'Ebastine', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li>Symptomatic treatment of Seasonal and Perennial Allergic Rhinitis.</li><li>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><p>Ebatin 10 mg is a second-generation antihistamine that binds to peripheral H1 receptors, preventing histamine-induced bronchoconstriction. It has antihistaminic and antiallergic properties, with minimal sedative or antimuscarinic effects.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage</strong></p><p><strong>Tablet:</strong></p><ul><li>Adults (&gt;12 years): 10 mg (1 tablet) once daily.</li><li>Children (6-12 years): 5 mg (half tablet) once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Syrup:</strong></p><ul><li>Children (2-5 years): 2.5 ml once daily (up to 5 ml for severe cases).</li><li>Children (6-12 years): 5 ml once daily (up to 10 ml for severe cases).</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Increased plasma levels</strong>: Ketoconazole or erythromycin may increase Ebatin\'s plasma levels and prolong QTc interval.</li><li><strong>No interaction</strong>: Theophylline, warfarin, cimetidine, diazepam, or alcohol (though alcohol and diazepam may enhance sedation).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Ebatin 10 mg or its ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Headache, dry mouth, drowsiness.</li><li>Less common: Abdominal pain, nausea, dyspepsia, insomnia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li>Safety during pregnancy and lactation is not established. Use only if clearly needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings :</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in patients with long QT syndrome, hypokalemia, or those on drugs affecting the QT interval or CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics).</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>No significant symptoms at doses up to 100 mg daily. In case of overdose, gastric lavage and symptomatic treatment should be given.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions :</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, in a cool, dry place, away from light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action :</strong></p><p>Ebatin 10 mg is a selective, long-acting H1-histamine receptor antagonist, rapidly absorbed and metabolized to its active form, carEbatin 10 mg.</p><p><br></p>', 'active', '469_1734625594.jpg', 17, 1, NULL, '2024-12-19 21:26:34', '2024-12-19 21:26:34'),
(403, 'Afrin', 'Oxymetazoline Hydrochloride', 70.00, 1, 'Nasal Drop', '1 Nasal Drop In Bottle', 'Nasal Drop', '0.05 Percentage', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li>Nasal congestion from acute or chronic rhinitis, common cold, sinusitis, upper respiratory allergies, epistaxis, hay fever, and otitis media.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><ul><li>Nasal Anti-histamine preparations, Nasal Decongestants, &amp; Other Nasal Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><ul><li>Afrin 0.05% is a sympathomimetic amine that acts as a direct α-adrenoceptor agonist, causing vasoconstriction of nasal arterioles. This reduces mucosal swelling, improves airflow, and enhances sinus drainage.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Spray:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults &amp; children (≥6 years): 2-3 sprays in each nostril twice daily for 3-5 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (&lt;6 years): Not suitable.</li><li><strong>Nasal Drops:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults &amp; children (≥6 years): 2-3 drops in each nostril twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (2-5 years): 2-3 drops of 0.025% solution in each nostril twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration :</strong></p><ul><li>For intranasal use only. Limit use to 5 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions :</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid in patients taking MAO inhibitors or within 14 days of stopping them.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li>Cardiovascular disease, hyperthyroidism, prostatic enlargement, angle-closure glaucoma, hypersensitivity to any ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Local irritation, dryness of the mouth/throat, burning, stinging, sneezing, or increased nasal discharge.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li>Safety during pregnancy and lactation not fully established, but generally considered safe.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings :</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid contact with eyes. Do not use for more than 7 days without medical advice. Prolonged use may worsen nasal congestion.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Local irritation and rebound congestion; symptomatic treatment recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions :</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, in a dry place, away from light. Do not refrigerate or freeze. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1773_1734625899.webp', 29, 1, NULL, '2024-12-19 21:31:39', '2024-12-19 21:31:39'),
(404, 'Xeldrin', 'Omeprazole', 6.00, 10, 'Capsul', '10 Capsul In Strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcers, including NSAID-induced ulcers.</li><li>Preventative treatment for recurrent NSAID-induced ulcers.</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), acid reflux, dyspepsia, severe reflux esophagitis.</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, Helicobacter pylori eradication.</li><li>Prevention of acid aspiration during general anesthesia.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Blocker</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><ul><li>Xeldrin 20 mg inhibits gastric acid secretion by blocking the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme in the parietal cells. The effect begins within 1 hour, peaks in 2 hours, and lasts up to 72 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gastric/duodenal ulcers: 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">GERD: 20 mg daily for 4-8 weeks (up to 40 mg for refractory cases).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acid reflux: 10-20 mg daily for long-term management.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Zollinger-Ellison: Initially 60 mg daily, adjust to 20-120 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Helicobacter pylori eradication: 20 mg twice daily with antibiotics.</li><li><strong>IV:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Acid aspiration: 40 mg before surgery.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Duodenal/gastric ulcers or GERD: 40 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration :</strong></p><ul><li>IV reconstitution with water; administer slowly over 2-5 minutes. For infusion, dissolve in 100 ml saline or dextrose, use within 12-6 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions :</strong></p><ul><li>May increase the half-life of warfarin, phenytoin, and diazepam. Adjust doses of these medications as needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient in Xeldrin 20 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Mild, temporary side effects include nausea, stomach cramping, dizziness, paresthesia, and headache.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li>Category C (FDA); no significant effects observed on pregnancy or fetus. Breastfeeding should be stopped if use is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings :</strong></p><ul><li>Rule out gastric cancer before starting treatment, as Xeldrin may mask symptoms.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions :</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light. Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1393_1734626119.webp', 44, 1, NULL, '2024-12-19 21:35:19', '2024-12-19 21:35:19'),
(405, 'Paricel', 'Rabeprazole Sodium', 7.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li>Active duodenal and benign gastric ulcers.</li><li>Erosive or ulcerative gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD).</li><li>GERD maintenance and long-term management.</li><li>Symptomatic GERD.</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>Helicobacter pylori eradication in peptic ulcer disease with antibiotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><ul><li>Rabeprazole inhibits gastric H+/K+-ATPase in parietal cells, reducing gastric acid secretion.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal/Gastric Ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4-6 weeks.</li><li><strong>GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance for GERD:</strong> 10-20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 10 mg once daily for 4 weeks, then as needed.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison:</strong> Starting dose 60 mg, adjust as needed.</li><li><strong>H. pylori eradication:</strong> 20 mg twice daily with antibiotics for 7 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration :</strong></p><ul><li>Take in the morning before meals; swallow tablets whole.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions :</strong></p><ul><li>May reduce absorption of pH-dependent drugs like itraconazole or ketoconazole.</li><li>Not recommended with atazanavir.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Headache, nausea, vomiting, constipation, dry mouth, muscle pain, dizziness, sleepiness.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li>Category C (FDA); use with caution during pregnancy and breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings :</strong></p><ul><li>Rule out cancer before starting treatment.</li><li>Long-term use may increase fracture risk, especially in elderly patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions :</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose :</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms are generally mild and reversible. No specific antidote.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '142_1734626361.jpg', 44, 1, NULL, '2024-12-19 21:39:21', '2024-12-19 21:39:21'),
(406, 'D-Rise', 'Colecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 2.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '2000 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency.</li><li>Adjunct therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcemia, tetany, and rickets.</li><li>Essential for calcium and phosphorus absorption, bone density, and normal growth.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Capsules (Adults):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Treatment: 40,000 IU weekly for 7 weeks. Maintenance: 1,400–2,000 IU/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prevention: 20,000 IU every 4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Osteoporosis: 20,000 IU monthly.</li><li><strong>Capsules (Children 12–18 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Treatment: 20,000 IU every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prevention: 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Tablets &amp; Syrup:</strong> 1,000–2,000 IU daily or as directed by a physician.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> Varies by age and condition, e.g., 200,000 IU every 6 months for children, 100,000 IU during pregnancy.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>May interfere with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, and antifungals.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, rash, pruritus, nausea, and abdominal pain.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in patients with impaired renal function. Overdose can cause hypervitaminosis D.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Safe up to 4,000 IU/day. Follow medical advice for higher doses. Excreted in breast milk; monitor additional supplementation for breastfeeding infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Below 30ºC, protected from light and moisture.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety in children under 12 not established.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Facilitates calcium and phosphorus absorption, reduces bacterial infection severity, improves lung function, and supports insulin regulation.</li></ul>', 'active', '327_1734713959.jpg', 3, 1, NULL, '2024-12-20 21:59:19', '2025-04-18 12:47:23'),
(407, 'Rocal-D Tablet Healthcare Pharmaceuticals Ltd.', 'Calcium Carbonate [Elemental] + Vitamin D3', 8.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, rickets, tetany, and parathyroid disorders.</li><li>Calcium supplementation during pregnancy, lactation, osteogenesis, tooth formation, and chronic renal failure.</li><li>Phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Combined mineral and vitamin preparations.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Combination of Calcium Carbonate and Vitamin D3.</li><li>Calcium supports bone formation and maintenance.</li><li>Vitamin D3 aids in calcium absorption and bone formation.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calcium 500 mg + Vitamin D3 200 IU:</strong> 2 tablets daily or 1 tablet twice daily.</li><li><strong>Calcium 500 mg + Vitamin D3 400 IU:</strong> 1 tablet twice daily.</li><li>Take with or after meals for better absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Possible interactions with antacids, other calcium or Vitamin D3 supplements, digoxin, and certain antibiotics (e.g., tetracyclines).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypercalcemia, hyperthyroidism, renal calculi, nephrolithiasis, and Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Generally well tolerated. Possible nausea, vomiting, dry mouth, thirst, increased urination, or constipation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in patients with heart or kidney disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Safe when used as directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms include nausea, vomiting, drowsiness, dry mouth, loss of appetite, metallic taste, headache, or diarrhea.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1347_1734714142.jpg', 1, 1, NULL, '2024-12-20 22:02:22', '2024-12-20 22:02:22'),
(408, 'Aeron', 'Montelukast', 17.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Chronic treatment and prevention of asthma.</li><li>Acute prevention of exercise-induced bronchoconstriction (EIB).</li><li>Relief of symptoms of allergic rhinitis (seasonal and perennial).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Leukotriene receptor antagonist.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Selectively inhibits the CysLT1 receptor, blocking the effects of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, LTE4), which are linked to asthma and allergic rhinitis symptoms.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Adolescents (15+ years):</strong> 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric (6–14 years):</strong> 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric (6 months–5 years):</strong> 4 mg granules once daily, directly or mixed with cold water/soft food.</li><li>No dosage adjustment needed for renal or mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency, elderly patients, or co-administration with most medications.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Safe with most common medications, including theophylline, digoxin, oral contraceptives, and NSAIDs.</li><li>Phenobarbital may reduce efficacy; monitor clinical response.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Upper respiratory infections, diarrhea, headache, nausea, vomiting.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, hepatic problems, hallucinations, tremor, suicidal thoughts.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Category B: Safe in animals but lacks adequate human studies. Use only if necessary. Caution during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Not for acute asthma attacks or sudden replacement of inhaled corticosteroids.</li><li>Aspirin-sensitive patients should avoid NSAIDs during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms include nausea, vomiting, headache, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, and stomach pain. Supportive care recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry place away from light and moisture. Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1409_1734714329.webp', 1, 1, NULL, '2024-12-20 22:05:29', '2024-12-20 22:05:29'),
(409, 'Don-A', 'Domperidone Maleate', 3.45, 14, 'Tablet', '14 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, including fullness, distension, pain, belching, nausea, vomiting, and heartburn.</li><li>Acute nausea/vomiting from various causes, including migraines, radiotherapy, or drug therapy.</li><li>Dopamine-agonist-induced nausea in Parkinson’s disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Motility Stimulants/Dopamine Antagonists.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Blocks dopamine receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and stomach to improve gastric motility.</li><li>Minimal impact on brain receptors, reducing neurological side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10-20 mg every 6-8 hours (max 80 mg/day).</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 6-8 hours.</li><li><strong>Duration</strong>: Maximum 12 weeks for acute nausea/vomiting.</li><li>Take 15-30 minutes before meals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid with antacids or antisecretory drugs, as they reduce absorption.</li><li>Caution with CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., azoles, macrolides) due to increased plasma levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to domperidone.</li><li>Gastrointestinal obstruction, perforation, or hemorrhage.</li><li>Prolactin-releasing pituitary tumors (prolactinoma).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Intestinal cramps, elevated prolactin levels.</li><li>Rare: Extrapyramidal symptoms, allergic reactions (rash, urticaria).</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in children, especially under 1 year, due to an immature blood-brain barrier.</li><li>Adjust dosage for liver or severe renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Not recommended during pregnancy.</li><li>Minimal amounts excreted in breast milk; use with caution.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Drowsiness, disorientation, extrapyramidal reactions (especially in children).</li><li>Treatment: Activated charcoal and supportive care.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Restores gastrointestinal motility and tone, enhances gastric emptying, and prevents gastric content regurgitation by increasing esophageal sphincter pressure.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '924_1734714513.webp', 5, 1, NULL, '2024-12-20 22:08:33', '2024-12-20 22:08:33');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(410, 'Voligel', 'Diclofenac Sodium', 97.00, 1, 'x 50 gm tube', '50 gm tube', 'Gel', '1% w/w', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Rheumatology</strong>: Inflammatory and degenerative rheumatic conditions (e.g., osteoarthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, gout).</li><li><strong>Surgery/Traumatology</strong>: Sprains, bruises, dislocations, fractures, post-surgery pain/inflammation.</li><li><strong>Obstetrics/Gynecology</strong>: Dysmenorrhea, mastitis, episiotomy.</li><li><strong>ENT/Dentistry</strong>: Pre/post-operative pain, inflammation, and swelling.</li><li><strong>Other</strong>: Pain management in urogenital surgeries, renal, and biliary colic.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><ul><li>Non-Steroidal Anti-Inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs).</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><p>Diclofenac inhibits prostaglandin biosynthesis, reducing inflammation, pain, and fever. It is rapidly absorbed, metabolized in the liver, and binds extensively to plasma proteins.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Gel</em>: Apply 2-4 g to the affected area 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Tablets</em>: 75-150 mg/day in divided doses after meals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Injection</em>: 1 ampoule daily, up to 2 in severe cases.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 1-3 mg/kg body weight/day (specific forms only).</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Use the lowest effective dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions :</strong></p><ul><li>May reduce the efficacy of diuretics and increase potassium levels with potassium-sparing diuretics. Monitor when combined with digoxin, methotrexate, cyclosporine, or lithium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li>Peptic ulcer, hypersensitivity to diclofenac, asthma triggered by NSAIDs, or use under occlusive dressings.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Epigastric pain, nausea, diarrhea, headache, dizziness, rash.</li><li>Rare: Edema, gastrointestinal bleeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid unless benefits outweigh risks. Not recommended during the first trimester or lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul>', 'active', '1420_1734714760.webp', 3, 1, NULL, '2024-12-20 22:12:40', '2025-04-20 12:24:20'),
(411, 'Ceevit DS', 'Vitamin C [Ascorbic acid]', 3.50, 6, 'Chewable Tablet', '6 Tablet In Strip', 'Chewable Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prevention &amp; treatment of scurvy.</strong></li><li>Promotes wound healing and collagen formation.</li><li>Enhances iron absorption and treats anemia.</li><li>Aids in red blood cell and hemoglobin production.</li><li>Boosts immunity and helps prevent infections.</li><li>Supports healthy capillaries, gums, and teeth.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><p>Vitamin C, a water-soluble vitamin, is essential for collagen synthesis, wound healing, and immune support. It is absorbed from the GI tract and distributed in body tissues. Deficiency leads to scurvy, characterized by bleeding gums, fragile capillaries, anemia, and delayed wound healing.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Oral:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of scurvy:</strong> 1 tablet daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of scurvy:</strong> 1-2 tablets daily (may increase based on severity).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly/stroke risk reduction:</strong> 1-2 tablets daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maximum dose:</strong> 2000 mg/day in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Parenteral (IM, IV, or subcutaneous):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For scurvy:</strong> 300 mg to 1 g daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Wound healing:</strong> 300-500 mg daily for 7-10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Severe burns:</strong> 1-2 g daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Rare but may include diarrhea, bloating, and iron overabsorption (harmful in certain conditions like thalassemia).</li><li>Mega-doses can cause hyperoxaluria, hemolysis (in G6PD deficiency), and scurvy in neonates if taken excessively during pregnancy.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li>Safe in normal doses.</li><li>Mega-doses (&gt;5 g/day) can cause fetal abortion or neonatal scurvy.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid mega-doses in hyperoxaluria or during pregnancy.</li><li>May cause false positives in some medical tests (e.g., glucose, uric acid).</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a dry place.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action :</strong></p><p>Vitamin C acts as an antioxidant, supports tissue growth and repair, enhances immunity, and is crucial for metabolic processes like drug and cholesterol metabolism.</p>', 'active', '1984_1734715094.jpg', 4, 1, NULL, '2024-12-20 22:18:14', '2024-12-22 22:53:51'),
(412, 'Napa Rapid', 'Paracetamol', 1.30, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Napa Rapid 500 mg :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pain relief:</strong> Headache, toothache, earache, bodyache, back pain, postoperative, postpartum, and inflammatory pain.</li><li><strong>Fever and cold symptoms:</strong> Common cold, influenza, post-vaccination pain.</li><li><strong>Joint and muscle issues:</strong> Rheumatic, osteoarthritic pain, stiffness, myalgia, neuralgia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Mechanism of action:</strong> Acts in the CNS to increase pain threshold by inhibiting COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3 enzymes involved in prostaglandin synthesis.</li><li>Provides <strong>analgesic</strong> and <strong>antipyretic</strong> effects with weak anti-inflammatory activity.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 1-2 tablets every 4-6 hours (max 8 tablets/day).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (6-12 years): ½-1 tablet 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Extended-release tablet:</strong> Adults &amp; children &gt;12 years: 2 tablets every 6-8 hours (max 6 tablets/day).</li><li><strong>Syrup:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children under 3 months: 10 mg/kg 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 6-12 years: 2-4 tsp 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Suppository:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-5 years: 125-250 mg 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults &amp; children &gt;12 years: 0.5-1 gm 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Paediatric drops:</strong> Follow weight-based dosage.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Rare but may include blood disorders (e.g., thrombocytopenia), rashes, pancreatitis, and allergic reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Napa Rapid 500 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in patients with impaired liver/kidney function or on hepatotoxic drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, pallor within 24 hours; liver damage evident in 12-48 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category B:</strong> Safe when clearly needed. Excreted in breast milk but poses minimal risk in therapeutic doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry, cool place away from light. Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Key Highlights :</strong></p><ul><li>Fast-acting analgesic with antipyretic properties.</li><li>Special formulation dissolves faster than standard tablets.</li></ul>', 'active', '1608_1734715346.png', 3, 1, NULL, '2024-12-20 22:22:26', '2024-12-20 22:22:26'),
(413, 'Neucos-B', 'Vitamin B1 + B6 + B12', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg+200 mg+200 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Neucos-B :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin B1, B6, and B12 deficiencies</strong></li><li><strong>Neurological conditions:</strong> Diabetic neuropathy, peripheral neuralgia, sciatica, lumbago, optic neuritis, facial neuralgia, intercostal neuralgia, spinal pain, myalgia.</li></ul><p><strong>Composition :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate) 100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride) 200 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) 200 mcg</li><li><strong>Injection (3 ml ampoule):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Thiamine Hydrochloride 100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pyridoxine Hydrochloride 100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Cyanocobalamin 1000 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablets:</strong> 1-3 tablets daily or as directed by a physician.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe cases: 1 ampoule IM daily until symptoms subside.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Milder cases: 2-3 ampoules per week.</li><li>Not recommended for children due to insufficient data.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Generally well-tolerated; allergic reactions are rare.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li>Patients on <strong>levodopa therapy</strong> or with hypersensitivity to ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li>Tablets are safe during pregnancy and lactation at recommended doses.</li><li>Injections not recommended due to benzyl alcohol.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid in <strong>optic neuropathies</strong> with raised plasma cyanocobalamin levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose :</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose symptoms are unlikely at recommended doses; treatment is supportive if needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 25°C in a cool, dry place, away from light and children.</li></ul><p><strong>Key Benefits :</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin B1: Energy production, nerve health, mood improvement.</li><li>Vitamin B6: RBC formation, neurotransmitter production, cardiovascular health.</li><li>Vitamin B12: RBC production, nerve pain reduction, anemia prevention.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '959_1734715594.webp', 10, 1, NULL, '2024-12-20 22:26:34', '2024-12-20 22:26:34'),
(414, 'Folic-Z', 'Folic Acid + Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate', 2.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Folic-Z 5 mg + 20 mg :</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment and prevention of <strong>Folic Acid</strong> and <strong>Zinc deficiencies</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Key Benefits :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Zinc:</strong> Supports enzyme function, immune health, growth, and development. Prevents neural tube defects, immune dysfunction, and growth retardation.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid:</strong> Essential for DNA synthesis, gene regulation, and preventing neural tube defects and megaloblastic anemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> One tablet daily or as prescribed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Zinc:</strong> Not for patients with hypersensitivity to zinc.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid:</strong> Avoid in untreated cobalamine deficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Generally well tolerated. Occasional gastrointestinal issues like nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li>Recommended for use during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>High calcium intake may reduce zinc absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1217_1734715850.jpg', 7, 1, NULL, '2024-12-20 22:30:50', '2024-12-20 22:30:50'),
(415, 'Bexitrol F', 'Salmeterol + Fluticasone Propionate', 1120.00, 60, 'Maxhaler', '60 Doses Maxhaler', 'Maxhaler', '50 mcg+250 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Used for regular treatment of asthma and COPD in patients:</p><ul><li>Not adequately controlled with inhaled corticosteroids and short-acting β2-agonists.</li><li>Already stabilized on corticosteroid and long-acting β2-agonist therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Long-acting β2-adrenoceptor stimulants</li><li>Respiratory corticosteroids</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate</strong>: Long-acting β2-agonist providing symptom protection.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: Glucocorticoid with anti-inflammatory action in the lungs, improving lung function and preventing exacerbations.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Adolescents (≥12 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Asthma</strong>: 2 puffs or 1 inhalation twice daily (morning &amp; evening), dose depending on severity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>COPD</strong>: 50 µg Salmeterol &amp; 250 µg Fluticasone twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children (4–12 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Asthma: 2 puffs (25 µg Salmeterol + 50 µg Fluticasone) twice daily.</li><li><strong>Special Groups</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed for elderly or renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Rinse mouth after inhalation to prevent thrush.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with pulmonary tuberculosis or thyrotoxicosis.</li><li>Avoid strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol</strong>: Tremors, palpitations, headache, arrhythmia.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone</strong>: Hoarseness, oral candidiasis (thrush), hypersensitivity reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Use only if benefits outweigh potential risks. Insufficient data for pregnancy and breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>May reduce growth velocity; long-term effects unknown.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C. Keep away from sunlight, heat, and children.</li></ul><p><strong>Instructions for Use</strong></p><ul><li>Shake well before use.</li><li>Exhale fully, inhale deeply while pressing the inhaler, and hold breath for 10 seconds.</li><li>Check technique periodically. Clean inhaler weekly.</li></ul>', 'active', '636_1734889906.jpg', 3, 1, NULL, '2024-12-22 22:51:46', '2024-12-22 22:52:54'),
(416, 'Fexo', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 4.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '60 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Asthma:</strong> For patients uncontrolled with corticosteroids and short-acting β2-agonists or stabilized on corticosteroid and long-acting β2-agonist.</li><li><strong>COPD:</strong> For regular management of symptoms.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol:</strong> Long-acting β2-agonist for symptom relief.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone:</strong> Anti-inflammatory corticosteroid for improved lung function and exacerbation prevention.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Adolescents (≥12 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Asthma: 2 puffs or 1 inhalation twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">COPD: 50 µg Salmeterol + 250 µg Fluticasone twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children (4–12 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Asthma: 2 puffs (25 µg Salmeterol + 50 µg Fluticasone) twice daily.</li><li><strong>Special Groups:</strong> No dose adjustment for elderly or renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Rinse mouth post-inhalation to prevent thrush.</li><li>Use cautiously in tuberculosis, thyrotoxicosis, or with CYP3A4 inhibitors.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol:</strong> Tremors, palpitations, headache, arrhythmia.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone:</strong> Hoarseness, oral thrush, hypersensitivity reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any components.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Pediatric Use :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Use only if benefits outweigh risks.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics:</strong> May slow growth; long-term effects unclear.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><ul><li>Below 30°C, away from sunlight and heat. Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Instructions for Use :</strong></p><ol><li>Shake well.</li><li>Exhale, inhale deeply while pressing the inhaler, and hold breath for 10 seconds.</li><li>Clean inhaler weekly.</li><li>Periodically check usage technique.</li></ol>', 'active', '1720_1734890309.jpg', 4, 1, NULL, '2024-12-22 22:58:29', '2024-12-22 22:58:29'),
(417, 'Napa One', 'Paracetamol', 2.25, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '1000 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li>Fever, common cold, flu, headache, toothache, earache, bodyache, myalgia, dysmenorrhoea, colic pain, back pain, and post-operative/postpartum pain.</li><li>Rheumatic and osteoarthritic pain, joint stiffness, inflammatory pain, and post-vaccination pain in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><ul><li>Analgesic and antipyretic with weak anti-inflammatory activity.</li><li>Increases pain threshold by inhibiting COX enzymes involved in prostaglandin synthesis.</li><li>Safe, fast-acting, and well-tolerated.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 1–2 tablets every 4–6 hours; max 4 g/day.</li><li><strong>Children (6–12 years):</strong> ½–1 tablet 3–4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Extended Release (Adults/≥12 years):</strong> 2 tablets every 6–8 hours; max 6 tablets/day.</li><li><strong>Children (syrup/suppository):</strong> Doses vary by age.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Mild, but may include skin rash, allergic reactions, blood disorders (rare), or pancreatitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> Use if necessary; appears safe for nursing infants in therapeutic doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Caution with liver or kidney impairment, alcohol use, or drugs affecting the liver.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose :</strong></p><p>Symptoms: Pallor, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, liver damage, and metabolic issues.</p><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><p>Store in a dry, cool place away from light and children.</p><p><strong>Key Warnings :</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid exceeding the recommended dose.</li><li>Alcohol increases liver toxicity risk.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1893_1734890719.webp', 3, 1, NULL, '2024-12-22 23:05:19', '2024-12-22 23:05:19'),
(418, 'Xyflo', 'Montelukast', 17.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma.</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB).</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Seasonal and Perennial Allergic Rhinitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><ul><li>Leukotriene receptor antagonists.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><ul><li>Inhibits cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT1), preventing airway inflammation, edema, and muscle contraction.</li><li>Reduces symptoms of asthma and allergic rhinitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults/Adolescents (≥15 years):</strong> 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children (6–14 years):</strong> 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children (6 months–5 years):</strong> 4 mg oral granules once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Upper respiratory infection, diarrhea, headache, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Angioedema, eosinophilia, hallucinations, suicidal thoughts, and hepatic issues.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> Use only if clearly needed. Crosses placenta in animals; limited data in humans.</li><li>May be secreted in breast milk; caution advised for nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Not for acute asthma attacks or status asthmaticus.</li><li>Maintain rescue medication for emergencies.</li><li>Patients with aspirin sensitivity should avoid NSAIDs.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose :</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Nausea, vomiting, hyperactivity, headache, and abdominal pain.</li><li>Supportive care and clinical monitoring recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><p>         Store below 30°C in a cool, dry place, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1665_1734891138.webp', 10, 1, NULL, '2024-12-22 23:12:18', '2024-12-22 23:12:18'),
(419, 'Savlon cream', 'Chlorhexidine Hydrochloride + Cetrimide', 22.00, 1, 'Cream', '1 cream In Tube', 'Cream', '30gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li>Insect bites, sunburn, cuts, scratches, blisters, grazes.</li><li>Nappy rash, cracked and itchy skin, skin disinfection, and wound cleansing.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><p>Chlorhexidine &amp; Chloroxylenol preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cetrimide:</strong> A quaternary ammonium antiseptic with bactericidal action against gram-positive and gram-negative organisms.</li><li><strong>Chlorhexidine:</strong> An antimicrobial effective against bacteria, yeasts, fungi, and viruses.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children:</strong> Apply 2-3 times daily or as directed.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Repeated use may cause skin irritation.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category C:</strong> Use only if benefits outweigh risks. Animal studies show fetal risk; human data is lacking.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid contact with eyes, ears, mucous membranes, and body cavities.</li><li>Prolonged use may cause hypersensitivity reactions. Organic matter (e.g., soap, cotton) can reduce efficacy.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><p>Keep in a cool, dry place away from light.</p>', 'active', '1689_1734891537.webp', 14, 1, NULL, '2024-12-22 23:18:57', '2024-12-22 23:18:57'),
(420, 'Delanix', 'Dexlansoprazole', 10.00, 10, 'Capsul', '10 Capsul In Strip', 'Capsule', '30 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis (EE):</strong> For up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed EE:</strong> Relief of heartburn for up to 6 months.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive GERD:</strong> Heartburn treatment for 4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI).</p><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><ul><li>Inhibits gastric acid secretion by blocking the (H+/K+)-ATPase in gastric parietal cells.</li><li>Dual Delayed Release (DDR) formulation ensures a plasma concentration profile with two peaks for extended action.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of EE:</strong> 60 mg once daily for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed EE &amp; GERD:</strong> 30 mg once daily.</li><li>Can be taken with or without food. Capsules should be swallowed whole or granules sprinkled on a spoonful of food and taken immediately.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, flatulence.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category B. Use only if benefits outweigh risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Not recommended unless necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Watch for gastric malignancy, Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea, bone fractures, hypomagnesemia, and caution with methotrexate use.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a dry place, protected from light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics:</strong> No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics:</strong> Safety not established for children under 12 years.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment required.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Max dose of 30 mg daily for moderate hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action :</strong></p><p>Blocks the final step of acid production via proton pump inhibition, reducing gastric acid secretion.</p>', 'active', '1840_1734892023.png', 2, 1, NULL, '2024-12-22 23:27:03', '2024-12-22 23:27:03'),
(421, 'Neso', 'Naproxen Sodium + Esomeprazole', 15.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li>Relief of signs and symptoms of osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis.</li><li>Reduces the risk of NSAID-associated gastric ulcers in at-risk patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><p>Drugs for Osteoarthritis, Rheumatoid Arthritis, and NSAIDs.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Naproxen:</strong> NSAID with analgesic and antipyretic effects; inhibits prostaglandin synthesis.</li><li><strong>Esomeprazole:</strong> Proton pump inhibitor; suppresses gastric acid by inhibiting H+/K+ ATPase.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li>One tablet twice daily, at least 30 minutes before meals.</li><li>Do not split, chew, crush, or dissolve tablets.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Gastritis, dyspepsia, diarrhea, gastric ulcer, upper abdominal pain, and nausea.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid unless potential benefits outweigh risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in conditions like myocardial infarction, stroke, hypertension, heart failure, edema, GI adverse events, hypomagnesemia, and renal or hepatic insufficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to components, history of asthma or allergic reactions to NSAIDs, and perioperative pain in CABG surgery.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Reduces effect:</strong> ACE inhibitors, diuretics, beta-blockers.</li><li><strong>Increases toxicity:</strong> Cyclosporin, lithium, methotrexate, digoxin.</li><li><strong>Bleeding risk:</strong> Warfarin.</li><li><strong>Clopidogrel:</strong> May reduce exposure to its active metabolite.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '145_1734892220.jpg', 29, 1, NULL, '2024-12-22 23:30:20', '2024-12-22 23:30:20'),
(422, 'Solvit-B', 'Vitamin B complex', 0.62, 60, 'Tablet', '60 Tablet In Bottle', 'Tablet', '5 mg+2 mg+2 mg+20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li>Prophylactic or therapeutic nutritional supplementation during physiologically stressful conditions (e.g., glossitis, stomatitis, cheilosis, beriberi).</li></ul><p><strong>Composition :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet/Capsule/Syrup:</strong> Thiamine 5 mg, Riboflavin 2 mg, Pyridoxine 2 mg, Nicotinamide 20 mg.</li><li><strong>Injection (2 ml):</strong> Thiamine 50 mg, Riboflavin 4 mg, Pyridoxine 10 mg, Nicotinamide 100 mg, D-Panthenol 5 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet/Capsule:</strong> 1-2 tablets/capsules, 3 times daily.</li><li><strong>Syrup:</strong> 2-3 teaspoons daily.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> 2 ml daily via IM or IV administration.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Rare: Allergic reactions and gastrointestinal intolerance.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any components.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions :</strong></p><ul><li>Pyridoxine can reduce the efficacy of levodopa in Parkinsonism therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li>Safe for use during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action :</strong></p><ul><li>Essential B vitamins support enzyme systems regulating carbohydrate, fat, and protein metabolism. Deficiency may lead to conditions such as glossitis, cheilosis, polyneuritis, and beriberi.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry, cool place, away from light.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1939_1734892495.jpg', 53, 1, NULL, '2024-12-22 23:34:55', '2024-12-22 23:34:55'),
(423, 'Finix', 'Rabeprazole Sodium', 8.00, 10, 'Capsul', '10 Capsul In Strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> Active duodenal and benign gastric ulcers, GERD (erosive/ulcerative, symptomatic, and maintenance), Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</li><li><strong>H. pylori Eradication:</strong> Combined with antibiotics (amoxicillin and clarithromycin) in peptic ulcer disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI).</p><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><p>Rabeprazole inhibits gastric H+/K+-ATPase, reducing acid secretion by blocking the proton pump in stomach parietal cells.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer:</strong> 20 mg daily for 4 weeks (may extend to 8 weeks).</li><li><strong>GERD (Erosive/Ulcerative):</strong> 20 mg daily for 4–8 weeks; maintenance: 10–20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> Starting dose 60 mg daily, adjustable up to 120 mg in divided doses.</li><li><strong>H. pylori Eradication:</strong> 20 mg rabeprazole + 1000 mg amoxicillin + 500 mg clarithromycin, twice daily for 7 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Headache, nausea, vomiting, constipation, dizziness, dry mouth, appetite changes.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to rabeprazole or components.</li><li>Avoid during pregnancy and breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Rule out malignancy before starting therapy.</li><li>Long-term/high-dose use may increase fracture risk.</li><li>Regular monitoring for long-term use (&gt;1 year).</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions :</strong></p><ul><li>May reduce absorption of pH-dependent drugs (e.g., ketoconazole, itraconazole).</li><li>Avoid with atazanavir due to reduced efficacy.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>FDA Category C:</strong> Use only if benefits outweigh risks. Likely excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations :</strong></p><ul><li>Safety in children is not established.</li><li>No dosage adjustment needed for hepatic or renal impairment.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1436_1734892736.webp', 7, 1, NULL, '2024-12-22 23:38:56', '2024-12-22 23:38:56'),
(424, 'Fexo', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 55.00, 1, 'x 50 ml bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '30 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Allergic Rhinitis</strong>: For adults and children 12+ to relieve seasonal and persistent symptoms (sneezing, itchy eyes, etc.).</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>: Reduces wheals and pruritus in adults and children 12+.</li><li><strong>Benefits</strong>: Enhances work/activity productivity and quality of life.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>: Non-sedating antihistamines.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><ul><li>Selective H1-receptor antagonist.</li><li>Quickly absorbed, peak plasma in 2-3 hours, does not cross the blood-brain barrier.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; children 12+</strong>: 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg or 180 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children (6-11 years)</strong>: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children (2-11 years)</strong>: Suspension (5 ml or 30 mg) twice daily.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Dose adjustments required.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Co-administration with erythromycin or ketoconazole increases plasma levels.</li><li>Avoid taking with aluminum or magnesium hydroxide-containing antacids within 2 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to fexofenadine or components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li>Common: Headache, fatigue, dizziness, nausea.</li><li>Rare: Angioedema, anxiety, palpitations, rash.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li>Category C for pregnancy. Avoid use unless necessary.</li><li>Not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Use cautiously in the elderly and patients with renal/hepatic impairment.</li><li>May cause tachycardia or palpitations in some individuals.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li>Symptoms: Dry mouth, sleepiness, dizziness.</li><li>No known upper limit for dosage; symptomatic treatment advised.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong>:</p><ul><li>Store away from heat and light. Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</p><ul><li>Inverse agonist of H1 receptors, preventing allergic reactions without crossing the blood-brain barrier.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>:</p><ul><li>For children with renal impairment, dose adjustment required.</li><li>No significant effects in children under 2 years.</li></ul>', 'active', '836_1742441992.jpg', 4, 1, NULL, '2024-12-22 23:47:37', '2025-04-22 19:03:45'),
(425, 'Trupan', 'Pantoprazole', 7.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Therapeutic Use</strong>: For gastric and duodenal ulcers, gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), NSAID-induced ulcers, Helicobacter pylori eradication (with antibiotics), and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI).</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><ul><li>Inhibits the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme in gastric parietal cells, suppressing both basal and induced gastric acid secretion for over 24 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Gastric/GERD</em>: 20-40 mg daily for 4 weeks; extend as needed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Duodenal Ulcer</em>: 40 mg daily for 2 weeks; extend if required.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>H. pylori</em>: 40 mg twice daily with antibiotics (e.g., Amoxicillin and Clarithromycin).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>NSAID-induced Ulcers</em>: 20 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</em>: Start with 80 mg daily, adjust as needed.</li><li><strong>IV</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Ulcers/GERD</em>: 40 mg once daily for 7-10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Peptic Ulcer Bleeding</em>: 80 mg IV bolus, followed by 8 mg/hour infusion for 72 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</em>: 80 mg IV every 12 hours, adjust as needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction :</strong></p><ul><li>No significant interactions observed.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Trupan 20 mg or its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Common: H eadache, diarrhea.</li><li>Rare: Abdominal pain, flatulence, rash, insomnia, hyperglycemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B (use only if necessary).</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Excreted in breast milk; weigh risks before use.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Do not split, chew, or crush tablets.</li><li>Long-term use may lead to Vitamin B12 deficiency or osteoporosis-related issues.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Not well-defined.</li><li>Treatment: Symptomatic and supportive; drug not easily dialyzable.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place away from heat and light, out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution (IV Use) :</strong></p><ul><li>Mix the powder with 10 ml of 0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection for IV injection (administer over 2-5 minutes).</li><li>For infusion, dilute to 100 ml with Sodium Chloride or Dextrose; use within 24 hours.</li></ul><p><br></p><p><br></p>', 'active', '1084_1735066510.webp', 4, 1, NULL, '2024-12-24 23:55:10', '2024-12-24 23:55:10'),
(426, 'Brezofil', 'Doxophylline', 8.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li>Treats: Bronchial asthma, bronchospasm, COPD, and pulmonary diseases with spastic bronchial components.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><ul><li>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><ul><li>A novel bronchodilator that selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, relaxing bronchial smooth muscle.</li><li>Differs from Theophylline with improved safety due to lower affinity for adenosine receptors.</li><li>Inhibits platelet-activating factor (PAF) and leukotriene production.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration  :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 400 mg two or three times daily, or as prescribed.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: 200 mg two or three times daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong> </strong>12 years: 200 mg or 10 ml syrup two or three times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years: 6-9 mg/kg body weight twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction :</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid with other xanthine derivatives or ephedrine.</li><li>May interact with drugs like cimetidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccines, reducing hepatic clearance and increasing blood levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li>Not for acute myocardial infarction, hypotension, lactating women, or hypersensitive individuals.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Rare but may include nausea, vomiting, headache, upset stomach, and heartburn.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li>Safe in animal studies but limited human data; use only if necessary.</li><li>Contraindicated during lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Adjust dose for liver disease, heart failure, or drug interactions affecting clearance.</li><li>Smoking and anticonvulsants may increase clearance, requiring higher doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Severe cardiac arrhythmias, seizures, cardiovascular collapse.</li><li>Treatment: Symptomatic management; discontinue and resume at lower doses after toxicity subsides.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '330_1735066799.webp', 2, 1, NULL, '2024-12-24 23:59:59', '2024-12-24 23:59:59'),
(427, 'Bost', 'Vitamin B1 + B6 + B12', 12.00, 30, 'Tablet', '30 Tablet In Bottle', 'Tablet', '100 mg+200 mg+200 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><p>Bost is used to treat vitamin B1, B6, and B12 deficiency syndromes, as well as supportive treatment for neuritis and non-inflammatory nerve diseases such as diabetic neuropathy, neuralgia, sciatica, and spinal pain.</p><p>Composition</p><p><strong>Tablet:</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate) 100 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride) 200 mg</li><li>Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) 200 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Ampoule (3 ml):</strong></p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride 100 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride 100 mg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin 1000 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p>Pharmacology</p><p>Bost combines vitamins B1, B6, and B12, essential for nerve metabolism, regeneration, and repair of nerve fibers and myelin sheaths.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong> 1–3 tablets daily or as prescribed.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> For severe cases, 1 ampoule daily intramuscularly until symptoms improve. For mild cases, 1 injection 2–3 times weekly.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li>Patients on levodopa therapy or with hypersensitivity to any components.</li><li>Cyanocobalamin is unsuitable for treating optic neuropathies linked to raised plasma cyanocobalamin levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><p>Generally well-tolerated, with occasional allergic reactions reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li>Tablets are safe during pregnancy at recommended doses.</li><li>Injection form is not recommended due to benzyl alcohol.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><p>Store below 25°C in a cool, dry place, away from light and children.</p><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><p>Use cautiously in patients with subacute spinal cord degeneration.</p><p><strong>Overdose :</strong></p><p>No significant overdose symptoms at recommended doses. Treat symptomatically if necessary.</p><p><br></p>', 'active', '686_1735211064.webp', 37, 1, NULL, '2024-12-26 16:04:24', '2024-12-26 16:04:24'),
(428, 'Vasco', 'Vitamin C [Ascorbic acid ]', 1.90, 10, 'Chewable Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Chewable Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><p>Vasco 250 mg (Ascorbic Acid/Vitamin C) is used to:</p><ul><li>Prevent and treat scurvy</li><li>Aid in wound healing and collagen formation</li><li>Enhance iron absorption and treat anemia</li><li>Support hemoglobin and red blood cell production</li><li>Boost immunity and help prevent infections</li><li>Treat and prevent the common cold</li><li>Promote healthy capillaries, gums, and teeth</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><p>Vitamin-C Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><p>Vitamin C, a water-soluble vitamin, is essential for collagen synthesis, tissue repair, and immune function. It aids in iron absorption and prevents scurvy, characterized by capillary fragility, bleeding, and slow wound healing.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prevention of scurvy: 1 tablet daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Treatment of scurvy: 1–2 tablets daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Risk reduction for stroke: 1–2 tablets daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum dose: 2000 mg daily in divided doses</li><li><strong>Parenteral (IM, IV, or SC):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Scurvy: 300 mg–1 g daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Wound healing: 300–500 mg daily pre- and post-surgery</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe burns: 1–2 g daily</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><p>Generally well-tolerated; high doses may cause diarrhea, abdominal bloating, iron over-absorption, and hemolysis in G6PD deficiency.</p><p>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</p><p>Safe in normal doses during pregnancy and lactation. High doses (&gt;5 g/day) may cause fetal abortion.</p><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid megadoses (&gt;1000 mg/day) during pregnancy to prevent neonatal scurvy.</li><li>Contraindicated in hyperoxaluria and may interfere with certain laboratory tests.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a dry place.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action :</strong></p><p>Vitamin C is essential for tissue repair, antioxidant protection, collagen synthesis, and immune function. It enhances iron absorption and supports overall health.</p>', 'active', '652_1735211644.webp', 7, 1, NULL, '2024-12-26 16:14:04', '2024-12-26 16:14:04'),
(429, 'Alatrol', 'Cetirizine Hydrochloride [Oral]', 35.00, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '5 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li>Relief of seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis symptoms</li><li>Treatment of chronic idiopathic urticaria</li><li>Management of allergen-induced asthma</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><p>Sedating Antihistamine</p><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><p>Alatrol (Cetirizine) is a potent H1 receptor antagonist with minimal drowsiness or behavioral effects. It inhibits histamine-mediated early allergic reactions and reduces late-phase inflammation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children ≥6 years:</strong> 2 tsp daily or 1 tsp twice daily</li><li><strong>Children 2–6 years:</strong> 1 tsp daily or ½ tsp twice daily</li><li><strong>Children 6 months–2 years:</strong> ½ tsp daily (up to ½ tsp every 12 hours for 12–23 months)</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><p>Common: Somnolence (drowsiness).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category B. Use only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Excreted in human milk; use with caution.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Exercise caution when driving or operating machinery.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><p>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1910_1735212191.jpg', 4, 1, NULL, '2024-12-26 16:23:11', '2025-04-30 14:25:45'),
(430, 'Cavic-C Plus', 'Calcium Lactate Gluconate + Calcium Carbonate + Vitamin C + Vitamin D3', 13.00, 15, 'Tablet', '15 Tablet In Bottle', 'Tablet', '1000 mg+327 mg (Elemental calcium)+500 mg+400 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li>Adjunct in osteoporosis therapy</li><li>Increased calcium, Vitamin C, and Vitamin D demand (pregnancy, lactation, growth periods, old age)</li><li>Prevention/treatment of calcium and Vitamin D deficiencies</li><li>Osteomalacia, postmenopausal syndrome, premenstrual symptoms, cold, influenza</li><li>As an alkalizing agent in systemic acidosis</li></ul><p><strong>Composition :</strong></p><p>Each effervescent tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Lactate Gluconate 1000 mg</li><li>Calcium Carbonate BP 327 mg</li><li>Vitamin C (Ascorbic Acid) BP 500 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 (Colecalciferol) 400 IU</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><p>Specific mineral and vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; children ≥ school age:</strong> 1 effervescent tablet daily</li><li><strong>Children (3–7 years):</strong> ½ tablet daily</li><li><strong>Infants:</strong> As prescribed by a physician</li><li>Dissolve in 100 ml water before consumption.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal issues: constipation, flatulence, nausea</li><li>Rare: hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor plasma and urinary calcium in mild renal failure or hypercalciuria.</li><li>Avoid calcium and Vitamin D overdose from other sources.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li>Use under medical supervision. Increased requirements during pregnancy and lactation.</li><li>Vitamin D passes into breast milk; high doses may be teratogenic.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '734_1735212482.webp', 2, 1, NULL, '2024-12-26 16:28:02', '2024-12-26 16:28:02');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(431, 'D-Balance', 'Colecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 4.00, 10, 'Capsul', '10 capsule in strip', 'Capsule', '2000 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency.</li><li>Adjunct therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcemia, tetany, and rickets.</li><li>Essential for bone growth, density maintenance, calcium and phosphorus utilization.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Vitamin D preparations, bone health support.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Promotes calcium/phosphorus absorption and bone health, reduces infection severity, supports insulin regulation, and lowers cancer risks.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Deficiency treatment: 40,000 IU weekly for 7 weeks. Maintenance: 1,400–2,000 IU/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Deficiency prevention: 20,000 IU every 4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Osteoporosis: 20,000 IU monthly.</li><li><strong>Children (12–18 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Deficiency treatment: 20,000 IU every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prevention: 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 400–4,000 IU/day based on medical advice.</li><li><strong>Syrup:</strong> 400 IU/day (0–1 year), 600 IU/day (&gt;1 year).</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> Varies by age, 100,000–200,000 IU every 3–6 months.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><p>Avoid with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, laxatives (e.g., liquid paraffin), and imidazole antifungal agents.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p>Hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, abdominal pain.</p><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in patients with kidney impairment.</li><li>Overdose can cause hypervitaminosis D.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of children’s reach.</p><p><strong>Use in Pediatrics:</strong></p><p>Safety and efficacy in children under 12 years are not established.</p>', 'active', '2479_1746113397.jpg', 4, 1, NULL, '2024-12-26 16:32:49', '2025-05-01 19:30:24'),
(432, 'D-Balance', 'Colecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 35.00, 10, 'Capsul', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Capsule', '40000 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Treatment &amp; Prevention:</strong> Vitamin D3 deficiency, osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcaemia, tetany, rickets.</li><li><strong>Additional Benefits:</strong> Essential for bone growth, calcium/phosphorus absorption, and maintaining bone density. Helps improve lung function, reduce bacterial infection severity, lower cancer risks (breast, colorectal), and support insulin levels in type 2 diabetes.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Capsule (Adults):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Deficiency treatment: 40,000 IU weekly for 7 weeks. Maintenance: 1400-2000 IU/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Deficiency prevention: 20,000 IU every 4 weeks. Osteoporosis: 20,000 IU monthly.</li><li><strong>Capsule (Children 12-18 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Treatment: 20,000 IU every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prevention: 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Film-coated/Oroflash Tablets:</strong> 1000-2000 IU daily, with food.</li><li><strong>Syrup (Infants/Children):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">At risk: 0-1 year: 400 IU/day; &gt;1 year: 600 IU/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Deficient: 0-1 year: 2000 IU/day (+50,000 IU weekly for 6 weeks); 1-18 years: 2000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> 200,000 IU every 3-6 months as prevention or as directed.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, rash, pruritus, nausea, abdominal pain.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</li><li>Overdose may cause hypervitaminosis D.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Safe up to 4000 IU during pregnancy; doses may vary based on deficiency. Vitamin D3 is excreted in breast milk but does not induce overdose in infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30ºC, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in children under 12 years not established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin-D preparations for bone health.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Enhances absorption/reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus, supports bone growth and density, and provides additional health benefits.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '195_1735212966.webp', 4, 1, NULL, '2024-12-26 16:36:06', '2024-12-26 16:36:06'),
(433, 'Stemetil', 'Prochlorperazine', 0.65, 20, 'Tablet', '20 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Nausea &amp; Vomiting:</strong> Caused by radiation therapy, chemotherapy, surgery, or other conditions.</li><li><strong>Vertigo:</strong> Associated with Meniere\'s disease and inner ear disorders.</li><li><strong>Psychotic Illnesses:</strong> Schizophrenia, acute mania.</li><li><strong>Non-Psychotic Anxiety:</strong> Short-term treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Antiemetic:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (2+ years, &gt;10 kg):</strong> 2.5 mg every 8-24 hours as needed; max: 15 mg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong> 5-10 mg 3-4 times/day; max: 40 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Antipsychotic:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong> 2.5 mg 2-3 times/day; max: 20 mg/day (2-5 years), 25 mg/day (6-12 years).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong> 5-10 mg 3-4 times/day; severe cases may require up to 150 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Non-Psychotic Anxiety (Adults):</strong> 15-20 mg/day in divided doses; max: 12 weeks use.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Start with 2.5-5 mg 1-2 times/day; max: 75 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity, severe CNS depression, coma, children &lt;2 years or &lt;10 kg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Drowsiness, muscle spasms, involuntary movements, tremors, restlessness, rash, yellowing skin/eyes.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid tasks requiring alertness (e.g., driving).</li><li>Alcohol increases drowsiness.</li><li>May cause sunlight sensitivity and reduced sweating (risk of heatstroke).</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Increased CNS Depression:</strong> With alcohol, barbiturates, sedatives.</li><li><strong>Absorption Interference:</strong> With antacids, antiparkinson\'s drugs, lithium.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>No reported adverse effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, away from light and moisture.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Anti-vertigo, antiemetic, antipsychotic drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Deep sleep, coma, muscle movements, hypotension.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '680_1735213238.webp', 45, 1, NULL, '2024-12-26 16:40:38', '2024-12-26 16:40:38'),
(434, 'Ostocal', 'Calcium Carbonate', 5.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calcium Deficiency:</strong> Prevention/treatment in conditions like hypoparathyroidism, chronic diarrhea, vitamin D deficiency, pregnancy, lactation, menopause, pancreatitis, renal failure, and hyperphosphatemia.</li><li><strong>Renal Failure:</strong> Used to manage hyperphosphatemia in dialysis patients.</li><li><strong>Antacid (1000 mg):</strong> For acid indigestion, heartburn, sour/upset stomach.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>250/500 mg Tablet:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 540-2000 mg/day for antacid use; 1250-3750 mg/day as a supplement.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children: Half the adult dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pregnancy/Lactation: 1200-1500 mg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Renal failure: 2.5-9.0 g/day with meals (adjust as needed).</li><li><strong>1000 mg Tablet:</strong> 2000-3000 mg when symptoms occur; repeat hourly if needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypercalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, hypercalciuria, nephrolithiasis, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>Requires monitoring with digoxin therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>GI irritation, constipation.</li><li>Hypercalcemia (rare, usually with high doses in renal failure patients).</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>May enhance digoxin effects, interfere with tetracycline absorption, or require vitamin D adjustment in renal failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Safe for use during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place at controlled room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children:</strong> Safe and effective for chronic renal failure.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Monitor for constipation, calcium, and phosphate levels, especially with renal failure.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '235_1735213494.webp', 53, 1, NULL, '2024-12-26 16:44:54', '2024-12-26 16:44:54'),
(435, 'Momvit', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral Essentials [For nursing mother]', 3.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '238.01mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><p>For lactating mothers to ensure a balanced intake of essential vitamins and minerals for the child’s development.</p><p><strong>Composition (per tablet):</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin A: 8 mg (26,667 IU)</li><li>Vitamin D: 400 IU</li><li>Calcium: 40 mg</li><li>Vitamin C: 60 mg</li><li>Potassium Iodide: 130 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>A specialized vitamin and mineral preparation designed to improve the nutritional status of nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><p>One tablet daily or as directed by a physician.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Not for use in individuals with hypersensitivity to any ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p>No significant side effects reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><p>Safe for use during pregnancy and lactation under medical advice.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><p>Excessive Vitamin A may be toxic and harmful during pregnancy. Consult a doctor before use.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral preparations.</p>', 'active', '1559_1735238046.webp', 3, 1, NULL, '2024-12-26 23:34:06', '2024-12-26 23:34:06'),
(436, 'Aristocal D', 'Calcium Carbonate [Elemental] + Vitamin D3', 7.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><p>Used for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, rickets, tetany, parathyroid disease, pregnancy, lactation, osteogenesis, tooth formation, and as a phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Contains Calcium Carbonate and Vitamin D3. Calcium supports bone formation and maintenance, while Vitamin D3 aids in calcium absorption, reabsorption, and bone formation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>Calcium 500 mg + Vitamin D3 200 IU: 2 tablets daily or 1 tablet twice daily.</li><li>Calcium 500 mg + Vitamin D3 400 IU: 1 tablet twice daily.</li><li>Take with or after meals for better absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><p>Possible interactions with antacids (calcium, aluminum, magnesium), other calcium or Vitamin D3 supplements, digoxin, and certain antibiotics (e.g., tetracyclines).</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Not for use in hypercalcemia, hyperthyroidism, renal calculi, nephrolithiasis, or Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p>Generally well tolerated. Possible nausea, vomiting, stomach cramps, dry mouth, thirst, increased urination, or constipation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><p>Use as directed by a physician.</p><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><p>Take precautions if there is pre-existing heart or kidney disease.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><p>May cause nausea, vomiting, drowsiness, dry mouth, appetite loss, metallic taste, cramps, diarrhea, headache, or constipation.</p><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><p>Store in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of children’s reach.</p>', 'active', '45_1735238175.webp', 3, 1, NULL, '2024-12-26 23:36:15', '2024-12-26 23:36:15'),
(437, 'Seroxyn', 'Salmeterol + Fluticasone Propionate', 820.00, 1, 'x 120 metered', '120 metered doses', 'Metered-Dose Inhaler (MDI)', '(25 mcg+250 mcg)', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><p>Used for regular asthma treatment in patients inadequately controlled with inhaled corticosteroids and short-acting β2-agonists or those already on both inhaled corticosteroids and long-acting β2-agonists.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Long-acting β2-adrenoceptor stimulants and respiratory corticosteroids.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol:</strong> A long-acting β2-agonist for sustained bronchodilation.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate:</strong> A corticosteroid with anti-inflammatory effects in the lungs, improving lung function and preventing exacerbations.</li><li>Both components together provide effective asthma control.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults (12+ years):</strong> 2 puffs (25 µg Salmeterol + 250 µg Fluticasone) twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children (4-12 years):</strong> 2 puffs (25 µg Salmeterol + 50 µg Fluticasone) twice daily.</li><li><strong>COPD:</strong> 1 inhalation (50 µg Salmeterol + 250 µg Fluticasone) twice daily.</li><li>Rinse mouth after use to avoid side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol:</strong> Tremor, palpitations, headache, arrhythmia, muscle cramps.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone:</strong> Hoarseness, oral thrush, hypersensitivity reactions, rare facial or throat swelling.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in patients with pulmonary tuberculosis or thyrotoxicosis.</li><li>Pediatric patients may experience reduced growth velocity.</li><li>Use during pregnancy/lactation only if benefits outweigh risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><p>Avoid β-blockers unless necessary. Monitor when using CYP3A4 inhibitors like ketoconazole or ritonavir.</p><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from sunlight, heat, and children. Do not puncture or incinerate the canister.</p>', 'active', '933_1735238359.webp', 44, 1, NULL, '2024-12-26 23:39:19', '2025-05-06 09:30:53'),
(438, 'Oradin', 'Loratadine', 4.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Effective for seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis, chronic urticaria, and allergic skin conditions.</li><li>Provides rapid relief from symptoms like sneezing, nasal discharge, itching, and ocular irritation.</li><li>Suitable for children over 2 years for allergic rhinitis and skin allergies.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Long-acting, tricyclic antihistamine with selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonistic activity.</li><li>No central sedative or anticholinergic effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children (6+ years):</strong> 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children (2-5 years):</strong> 5 mg (1 teaspoon syrup) daily.</li><li><strong>Not recommended for children under 2 years.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>No hazardous interactions reported.</li><li>No potentiation of alcohol effects.</li><li>Drugs like erythromycin or ketoconazole may increase serum levels, but without adverse effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Comparable to placebo; minimal sedation or anticholinergic effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>FDA Category B: Use only if clearly needed during pregnancy.</li><li>Excreted in breast milk; caution advised for nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in liver impairment or renal insufficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>May cause somnolence, tachycardia, or headache. Supportive measures, activated charcoal, or gastric lavage may be needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry, cool place away from light. Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul>', 'active', '122_1735238484.webp', 53, 1, NULL, '2024-12-26 23:41:24', '2024-12-26 23:41:24'),
(439, 'Esotid', 'Esomeprazole Magnesium Trihydrate', 6.50, 14, 'Tablet', '14 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li>Relief from GERD symptoms like persistent heartburn.</li><li>Treatment and maintenance of erosive esophagitis healing.</li><li>Eradication of <em>Helicobacter pylori</em> (with amoxicillin and clarithromycin).</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>Treatment of acid-related dyspepsia, duodenal, and gastric ulcers.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage</strong></p><ul><li><strong>GERD/Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20-40 mg daily for 4-8 weeks. Maintenance: 20 mg daily (up to 6 months).</li><li><strong>H. pylori eradication:</strong> 40 mg daily + Amoxicillin (1000 mg BID) + Clarithromycin (500 mg BID) for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> 20-80 mg daily, adjusted individually.</li><li><strong>Dyspepsia/Duodenal ulcer:</strong> 20-40 mg daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric ulcer:</strong> 20-40 mg daily for 4-8 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><ul><li>Take one hour before meals; swallow capsules whole.</li><li>For oral suspension, mix with 15 ml of water, stir, and consume within 30 minutes.</li><li>For IV, administer reconstituted solution slowly (3-10 minutes).</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>May interact with drugs metabolized by CYP2C19 (e.g., diazepam, ketoconazole).</li><li>Reduces stomach acid, potentially affecting absorption of some medications (e.g., iron, digoxin).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common: Headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, dry mouth.</p><p><strong>Precautions</strong></p><ul><li>Rule out stomach cancer before use.</li><li>Not recommended for pediatric patients; dose adjustments required for severe hepatic impairment.</li><li>Antacids can be used concurrently.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>Symptoms: Reduced motor activity, tremors, ataxia. Treat with supportive measures.</p><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from moisture and light.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Limited data on use in pregnancy and lactation. Avoid unless necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Esotid 20 mg (Esomeprazole) inhibits gastric acid secretion by blocking H+/K+-ATPase in stomach parietal cells. Its systemic bioavailability improves with repeated dosing.</p><p><strong>Pediatric and Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Safety not established in pediatric patients.</li><li>Elderly patients show no significant differences in response but may require closer monitoring.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '868_1735238831.webp', 7, 1, NULL, '2024-12-26 23:47:11', '2024-12-26 23:47:11'),
(440, 'Famotack', 'Famotidine', 55.00, 1, 'x 50 ml bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '40 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Gastric ulcer, duodenal ulcer, anastomotic ulcer, acute stress ulcer, reflux esophagitis, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Acute and chronic gastritis in the acute exacerbation stage</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>H2 receptor antagonist</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Famotack inhibits histamine H2-receptors in parietal cells, reducing gastric acid secretion (basal and stimulated). Its action is slowly reversible.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Duodenal/Gastric Ulcer: 40 mg at bedtime or 20 mg twice daily for 4-8 weeks; maintenance: 20 mg once daily at bedtime</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">GERD: 20 mg twice daily for 6-12 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome: 20 mg every 6 hours, adjustable to response</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute/Chronic Gastritis: 20 mg twice daily or 40 mg at bedtime</li><li><strong>Pediatrics:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">GERD/Peptic Ulcer: 0.5-2 mg/kg/day divided doses (up to 40 mg/day)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">IV: 0.25 mg/kg every 12 hours</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p>Rare: Constipation, diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, dry mouth, headache, drowsiness, insomnia</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to any component</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy: Category B; use only if necessary</li><li>Lactation: Discontinue drug or breastfeeding</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions:</strong></p><p>Caution in elderly, renal failure, or hepatic disorders. Use minimum effective dose.</p><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of children’s reach.</p>', 'active', '1992_1735238983.webp', 4, 1, NULL, '2024-12-26 23:49:43', '2025-05-02 17:27:39'),
(441, 'Esoral', 'Esomeprazole Magnesium Trihydrate', 6.00, 14, 'Tablet', '14 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>GERD</strong>: Relieves persistent heartburn and symptoms.</li><li><strong>Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: Treatment and maintenance of healing.</li><li><strong>H. Pylori Eradication</strong>: In combination with amoxicillin and clarithromycin for duodenal ulcers.</li><li><strong>Other Uses</strong>: Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, duodenal &amp; gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20-40 mg daily for 4-8 weeks; may extend if needed.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing</strong>: 20 mg daily (up to 6 months).</li><li><strong>GERD Symptoms</strong>: 20 mg daily for 4 weeks; extend to 8 weeks if unresolved.</li><li><strong>H. Pylori</strong>: Esomeprazole 40 mg daily for 10 days with antibiotics.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg daily, adjusted as needed.</li><li><strong>Acid Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>IV Use</strong>: 40 mg daily via injection or infusion; switch to oral as soon as possible.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration :</strong></p><ul><li>Take 1 hour before meals.</li><li>Capsules can be mixed with applesauce for those unable to swallow.</li><li>IV solution should be used within 12 hours of preparation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Headache, nausea, diarrhea, constipation, dry mouth, abdominal pain.</li><li>Long-term side effects are not significantly different from short-term use.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Does not rule out gastric cancer.</li><li>Not advised to chew capsules or pellets.</li><li>Safe use in pediatric patients has not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions :</strong></p><ul><li>Affects CYP2C19; may alter drug metabolism (e.g., diazepam, digoxin).</li><li>Reduces absorption of pH-dependent drugs (e.g., ketoconazole, iron salts).</li><li>Minimal interactions with most common medications.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to esomeprazole or its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Breastfeeding :</strong></p><ul><li>Limited data; avoid use unless necessary. Discontinue breastfeeding if used.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric &amp; Special Populations :</strong></p><ul><li>Generally safe for elderly; no dosage adjustment needed for mild hepatic or renal impairment. For severe hepatic impairment, do not exceed 20 mg daily.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1893_1735239211.jpg', 53, 1, NULL, '2024-12-26 23:53:31', '2024-12-26 23:53:31'),
(442, 'Domin', 'Domperidone Maleate', 3.50, 15, 'Tablet', '15 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li>Treats dyspeptic symptoms (e.g., epigastric fullness, distension, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, heartburn).</li><li>Manages acute nausea and vomiting of various origins (functional, organic, infectious, radiotherapy, drug-induced).</li><li>Dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting in Parkinson’s disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><p>Motility stimulants, dopamine antagonists, prokinetic drugs.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><ul><li>Blocks dopamine receptors in the CTZ and stomach, enhancing gastrointestinal motility.</li><li>Minimal crossing of the blood-brain barrier reduces psychotropic side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10-20 mg every 6-8 hours (max 80 mg/day).</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 6-8 hours.</li><li>Take 15-30 minutes before meals.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity, gastrointestinal bleeding, obstruction, perforation, or prolactinoma.</li><li>Not for neonates.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Rare: Cramps, increased prolactin (galactorrhea, gynecomastia), allergic reactions.</li><li>Neurological effects rare in adults but possible in infants or with impaired blood-brain barrier.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in children, hepatic impairment, and prolonged therapy in renal impairment.</li><li>Pregnant or lactating women should consult a doctor.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose :</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Drowsiness, confusion, extrapyramidal reactions.</li><li>Treatment: Activated charcoal, anticholinergic drugs, or antihistamines.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><ul><li>Below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li></ul>', 'active', '1324_1735239375.jpg', 7, 1, NULL, '2024-12-26 23:56:15', '2024-12-26 23:56:15'),
(443, 'Becoral D', 'Calcium Carbonate [Coral source] + Vitamin D3', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 Tablet In Strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li>Treats and prevents osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, and hypoparathyroidism.</li><li>Used for osteogenesis disorders, rickets, pregnancy, lactation, elderly patients, pancreatitis, and as a phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</li><li>Supplement for inadequate Calcium intake in childhood diets.</li></ul><p><strong>Composition :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Option 1</strong>: Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg (Coral source, 600 mg elemental Calcium) + Vitamin D3 400 IU.</li><li><strong>Option 2</strong>: Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg (Coral source, 500 mg elemental Calcium) + Vitamin D3 200 IU.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><p>Specific mineral and vitamin combined preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><ul><li>Coral Calcium supports bone health and mimics human bone composition, ensuring better absorption.</li><li>Vitamin D3 aids Calcium absorption and bone formation, synergistically preventing osteoporosis and fractures.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li>1 tablet once or twice daily, preferably after meals with water, or as directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions :</strong></p><ul><li>Calcium reduces absorption of tetracyclines and fluoride; allow a 3-hour gap.</li><li>Thiazide diuretics reduce Calcium excretion.</li><li>Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may decrease Vitamin D3 effectiveness.</li><li>Certain foods (e.g., spinach, cereals, milk) may interfere with Calcium absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity, hypercalcemia, hyperparathyroidism, severe renal insufficiency, renal calculi, or bone metastases.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Rare: Flatulence, constipation, diarrhea, upper GI discomfort.</li><li>Prolonged use may rarely cause hypercalcemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor plasma Calcium in mild hypercalciuria, renal impairment, or long-term use.</li><li>Caution in patients with renal stones or history of stones.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Nausea, vomiting, abdominal cramps, dizziness, headache, constipation.</li><li>Treatment: Stop medication and rehydrate.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li>Safe during pregnancy and lactation as per physician’s recommendation.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '934_1735239525.webp', 3, 1, NULL, '2024-12-26 23:58:45', '2024-12-26 23:58:45'),
(444, 'Alneed Gold', 'Iron Polymaltose Complex + Folic Acid + Zinc + Vitamin B-Complex', 6.00, 10, 'Capsul', '10 Capsul In Strip', 'Capsule', '99 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications :</strong></p><ul><li>Treats and prevents Zinc, Iron, Folic Acid, and B-vitamins deficiencies, especially during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Composition :</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Iron (Iron Polymaltose Complex)</strong>: 47 mg</li><li><strong>Zinc (as Zinc Sulphate)</strong>: 22.5 mg</li><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong>: 0.50 mg</li><li><strong>B Vitamins</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Thiamine: 5 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Riboflavin: 2 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nicotinamide: 20 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pyridoxine: 2 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class :</strong></p><p>Iron &amp; Vitamin Combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology :</strong></p><ul><li>Iron Polymaltose Complex allows controlled absorption of Iron.</li><li>Folic Acid supports baby development.</li><li>Zinc aids enzyme function and protein metabolism.</li><li>B vitamins are essential for tissue building and energy release.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration :</strong></p><ul><li>1 capsule daily, or 2 capsules for more severe cases, as directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions :</strong></p><ul><li>No known drug interactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications :</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Generally well-tolerated, but allergic reactions may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation :</strong></p><ul><li>Safe during pregnancy and lactation; avoid during the first trimester unless Iron deficiency is confirmed.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions :</strong></p><ul><li>Use caution in patients with iron overload (e.g., hemochromatosis).</li><li>Avoid high doses in liver disease or hemochromatosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects :</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose can cause nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, and in severe cases, iron toxicity.</li><li>Zinc toxicity may cause reduced immune function and copper deficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage :</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action :</strong></p><ul><li>Iron Polymaltose Complex enhances Iron absorption for faster utilization in hemoglobin synthesis.</li><li>Folic Acid prevents neural tube defects, and Zinc supports metabolism and immune function.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '157_1735240315.webp', 2, 1, NULL, '2024-12-27 00:11:55', '2024-12-27 00:11:55'),
(445, 'Rocovas', 'Rosuvastatin', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p><p><strong>Indications for Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial and Non-familial)</li><li><strong>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial)</li><li><strong>Mixed Dyslipidemia</strong> (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li><strong>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Statins</li><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischemic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is a selective and competitive inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase, the enzyme responsible for converting 3-hydroxy-3-methyl glutaryl coenzyme A to mevalonate, a cholesterol precursor. It reduces lipid levels in two ways:</p><ol><li><strong>Increases hepatic LDL receptors</strong>: Enhances uptake and breakdown of LDL.</li><li><strong>Inhibits hepatic synthesis of VLDL</strong>: Reduces overall VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage of Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General dose range</strong>: 5-40 mg once daily. (Use 40 mg only for patients not achieving LDL-C goals with 20 mg).</li><li><strong>Homozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia (HoFH)</strong>: Starting dose of 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">With HeFH (8-10 years): 5-10 mg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">With HeFH (10-17 years): 5-20 mg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">With HoFH (7-17 years): 20 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg can be taken with or without food at any time of the day.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong>: Increases exposure; do not exceed 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil</strong>: Avoid if possible; if needed, limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or Atazanavir/Ritonavir</strong>: Increases exposure; limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants</strong>: May prolong INR; monitor INR frequently.</li><li><strong>Lipid-lowering therapies</strong>: Combining with fibrates or niacin increases the risk of muscle-related side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of its components.</li><li>Liver disease, including unexplained persistent elevation of hepatic transaminases.</li><li>Pregnant women or women planning to conceive.</li><li>Nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is generally well-tolerated. Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul>', 'in-active', '1020_1738049651.jpg', 2, 12, NULL, '2025-01-28 12:34:11', '2025-05-12 18:13:14'),
