avir/Ritonavir</strong>: Increases exposure; limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants</strong>: May prolong INR; monitor INR frequently.</li><li><strong>Lipid-lowering therapies</strong>: Combining with fibrates or niacin increases the risk of muscle-related side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of its components.</li><li>Liver disease, including unexplained persistent elevation of hepatic transaminases.</li><li>Pregnant women or women planning to conceive.</li><li>Nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is generally well-tolerated. Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1101_1741626841.webp', 5, 12, NULL, '2025-03-10 22:14:01', '2025-03-10 22:14:01');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(2836, 'Valporin CR', 'Sodium Valproate', 12.00, 6, 'Tablet', '6\'s tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Valporin CR 500 mg</strong></p><p> Sodium Valproate oral is indicated for the treatment of all types of epilepsy, e.g.:</p><ul><li>Partial seizures</li><li>Absence seizures (petit mal)</li><li>Generalized tonic-clonic seizures (grand mal)</li><li>Myoclonic seizures</li><li>Atonic seizures</li><li>Mixed seizures that include absence attack</li><li>Prophylaxis of febrile convulsion</li><li>Prophylaxis of post-traumatic epilepsy</li></ul><p>It is also indicated in the treatment of bipolar disorder &amp; prophylaxis of migraine.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Valporin CR 500 mg tablet:</strong> Each enteric-coated tablet contains Valporin CR 500 mg BP 200 mg.</li><li><strong>Valporin CR 500 mg syrup:</strong> Each 5 ml syrup contains Valporin CR 500 mg BP 200 mg.</li><li><strong>Valporin CR 500 mg controlled release 200 tablet:</strong> Each enteric-coated controlled release tablet contains Valporin CR 500 mg 200 mg as Valporin CR 500 mg BP 133.2 mg &amp; Valproic acid BP 58 mg.</li><li><strong>Valporin CR 500 mg controlled release 300 tablet:</strong> Each enteric-coated controlled release tablet contains Valporin CR 500 mg 300 mg as Valporin CR 500 mg BP 199.8 mg &amp; Valproic acid BP 87 mg.</li><li><strong>Valporin CR 500 mg controlled release 500 tablet:</strong> Each enteric-coated controlled release tablet contains Valporin CR 500 mg 500 mg as Valporin CR 500 mg BP 333 mg &amp; Valproic acid BP 145 mg.</li><li><strong>Valporin CR 500 mg injection:</strong> Each 5 ml injectable solution contains Valporin CR 500 mg BP equivalent to Valproic Acid 500 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Primary anti-epileptic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Valporin CR 500 mg shows antiepileptic properties in various types of seizures. The exact mechanism of Valporin CR 500 mg is not yet established. However, it is suggested that its activity is related to increased brain levels of Gamma Amino Butyric Acid (GABA).</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Valporin CR 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Initial dose:</strong> 600 mg/day in 2 divided doses, preferably after food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Increase by</strong> 200 mg/day at 3-day intervals to a maximum of 2.5 gm daily in divided doses until seizure control is achieved.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance dose:</strong> Usually 1-2 gm daily (20-30 mg/kg daily).</li><li><strong>Children (up to 20 kg):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Initial dose:</strong> 20 mg/kg daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Increase dose if necessary with proper monitoring of plasma concentration.</strong></li><li><strong>Children (over 20 kg):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Initial dose:</strong> 400 mg/day (irrespective of weight).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Increase by 20-30 mg/kg if required for seizure control.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Valporin CR 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablets:</strong> May be given once or twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Syrup:</strong> Should be given in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Epilepsy:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Adults:</strong>Initially, 600 mg daily in 2 divided doses (preferably after food), increasing by 200 mg/day at 3-day intervals to a maximum of 2.5 g daily in divided doses until seizure control is achieved.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual maintenance dose: 1-2 g daily (20-30 mg/kg daily).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Children (over 20 kg):</strong>Initially 400 mg daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increase until control is achieved (usually in the range of 20-30 mg/kg daily); maximum 35 mg/kg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (up to 20 kg):</strong>Initially 20 mg/kg daily in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Febrile convulsion:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20-30 mg/kg/day in 3 divided doses.</li><li><strong>Bipolar disorder:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initially 600 mg daily (20-30 mg/kg/day) in 2-3 divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dosage: 1000-2000 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of migraine:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">400-600 mg daily, although some may require 1000-1500 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong> May be given as a slow intravenous injection over 3-5 minutes, usually 400-800 mg depending on body weight (up to 10 mg/kg), followed by continuous or repeated infusion up to a maximum of 2500 mg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Children:Initial dose:</strong> 20-30 mg/kg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Maximum dose:</strong> 40 mg/kg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">May be given by direct slow intravenous injection or infusion using compatible diluents (e.g., 0.9% Sodium Chloride injection, 5% Dextrose injection).</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Valporin CR 500 mg</strong></p><p> Sodium Valproate appears to act as a non-specific inhibitor of drug metabolism. It interacts significantly with:</p><ul><li>Phenobarbital</li><li>Phenytoin</li><li>Warfarin</li><li>Aspirin</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with known hypersensitivity to the drug.</li><li>Patients with liver dysfunction.</li><li>Restricted use during pregnancy and in women of childbearing potential.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Valporin CR 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Anorexia, nausea, vomiting (minimized with enteric-coated tablets).</li><li><strong>CNS effects:</strong> Sedation, ataxia, tremor (usually resolves with dose reduction).</li><li><strong>Others:</strong> Rash, alopecia, stimulation of appetite.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Liver effects:</strong>Elevated liver enzymes in plasma (up to 40% of patients, often asymptomatic in the first few months).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rarely, fulminant hepatitis (potentially fatal).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increased risk in children under 2 years, especially with other medical conditions or multiple antiepileptic agents.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute pancreatitis and hyperammonemia have also been associated with Valporin CR 500 mg use.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pregnancy:</strong>Crosses the placenta and may cause neural tube defects (e.g., anencephaly, spina bifida) if exposed in the first trimester.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pregnant women taking Sodium Valproate should undergo serum alpha-fetoprotein estimation.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Lactation:</strong>Excreted in breast milk.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Likely no significant risk to the child, but caution is advised.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor liver function before therapy and for the first 6 months, especially in high-risk patients.</li><li>Ensure no undue potential for bleeding before starting treatment or major surgery.</li><li>Use with caution in renal impairment, pregnancy, breastfeeding, and systemic lupus erythematosus.</li><li>May cause false-positive results in urine ketone tests.</li><li>Avoid sudden withdrawal of therapy.</li><li>Should not be used during pregnancy and in women of childbearing potential.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Do not store above 30°C.</li><li>Keep away from light and out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Primary anti-epileptic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Valporin CR 500 mg has anti-epileptic activity against various seizures. Its exact mechanism is not fully established, but it is thought to be related to increased brain levels of gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA).</p>', 'active', '1213_1741626886.jpg', 6, 12, NULL, '2025-03-10 22:14:46', '2025-03-10 22:14:46'),
(2837, 'Cerox CV', 'Cefuroxime Axetil + Clavulanic Acid', 35.00, 7, 'Tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg+62.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial and Non-familial)</li><li><strong>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial)</li><li><strong>Mixed Dyslipidemia</strong> (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li><strong>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Statins</li><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischemic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is a selective and competitive inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase, the enzyme responsible for converting 3-hydroxy-3-methyl glutaryl coenzyme A to mevalonate, a cholesterol precursor. It reduces lipid levels in two ways:</p><ol><li><strong>Increases hepatic LDL receptors</strong>: Enhances uptake and breakdown of LDL.</li><li><strong>Inhibits hepatic synthesis of VLDL</strong>: Reduces overall VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage of Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General dose range</strong>: 5-40 mg once daily. (Use 40 mg only for patients not achieving LDL-C goals with 20 mg).</li><li><strong>Homozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia (HoFH)</strong>: Starting dose of 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients</strong>:</li><li>With HeFH (8-10 years): 5-10 mg/day.</li><li>With HeFH (10-17 years): 5-20 mg/day.</li><li>With HoFH (7-17 years): 20 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg can be taken with or without food at any time of the day.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong>: Increases exposure; do not exceed 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil</strong>: Avoid if possible; if needed, limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or Atazanavir/Ritonavir</strong>: Increases exposure; limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants</strong>: May prolong INR; monitor INR frequently.</li><li><strong>Lipid-lowering therapies</strong>: Combining with fibrates or niacin increases the risk of muscle-related side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of its components.</li><li>Liver disease, including unexplained persistent elevation of hepatic transaminases.</li><li>Pregnant women or women planning to conceive.</li><li>Nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is generally well-tolerated. Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1441_1741626923.webp', 44, 12, NULL, '2025-03-10 22:15:23', '2025-03-10 22:15:23'),
