(2867, 'Telisa-A', 'Amlodipine Besilate + Telmisartan', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial and Non-familial)</li><li><strong>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial)</li><li><strong>Mixed Dyslipidemia</strong> (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li><strong>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Statins</li><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischemic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is a selective and competitive inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase, the enzyme responsible for converting 3-hydroxy-3-methyl glutaryl coenzyme A to mevalonate, a cholesterol precursor. It reduces lipid levels in two ways:</p><ol><li><strong>Increases hepatic LDL receptors</strong>: Enhances uptake and breakdown of LDL.</li><li><strong>Inhibits hepatic synthesis of VLDL</strong>: Reduces overall VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage of Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General dose range</strong>: 5-40 mg once daily. (Use 40 mg only for patients not achieving LDL-C goals with 20 mg).</li><li><strong>Homozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia (HoFH)</strong>: Starting dose of 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients</strong>:</li><li>With HeFH (8-10 years): 5-10 mg/day.</li><li>With HeFH (10-17 years): 5-20 mg/day.</li><li>With HoFH (7-17 years): 20 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg can be taken with or without food at any time of the day.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong>: Increases exposure; do not exceed 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil</strong>: Avoid if possible; if needed, limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or Atazanavir/Ritonavir</strong>: Increases exposure; limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants</strong>: May prolong INR; monitor INR frequently.</li><li><strong>Lipid-lowering therapies</strong>: Combining with fibrates or niacin increases the risk of muscle-related side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of its components.</li><li>Liver disease, including unexplained persistent elevation of hepatic transaminases.</li><li>Pregnant women or women planning to conceive.</li><li>Nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is generally well-tolerated. Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1004_1741629088.webp', 5, 12, NULL, '2025-03-10 22:51:28', '2025-03-10 22:51:28'),
(2868, 'Lulizol', 'Luliconazole', 450.00, 1, 'x 50 gm tube', '50 gm tube', 'Lotion', '1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial and Non-familial)</li><li><strong>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial)</li><li><strong>Mixed Dyslipidemia</strong> (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li><strong>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Statins</li><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischemic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is a selective and competitive inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase, the enzyme responsible for converting 3-hydroxy-3-methyl glutaryl coenzyme A to mevalonate, a cholesterol precursor. It reduces lipid levels in two ways:</p><ol><li><strong>Increases hepatic LDL receptors</strong>: Enhances uptake and breakdown of LDL.</li><li><strong>Inhibits hepatic synthesis of VLDL</strong>: Reduces overall VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage of Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General dose range</strong>: 5-40 mg once daily. (Use 40 mg only for patients not achieving LDL-C goals with 20 mg).</li><li><strong>Homozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia (HoFH)</strong>: Starting dose of 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients</strong>:</li><li>With HeFH (8-10 years): 5-10 mg/day.</li><li>With HeFH (10-17 years): 5-20 mg/day.</li><li>With HoFH (7-17 years): 20 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg can be taken with or without food at any time of the day.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong>: Increases exposure; do not exceed 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil</strong>: Avoid if possible; if needed, limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or Atazanavir/Ritonavir</strong>: Increases exposure; limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants</strong>: May prolong INR; monitor INR frequently.</li><li><strong>Lipid-lowering therapies</strong>: Combining with fibrates or niacin increases the risk of muscle-related side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of its components.</li><li>Liver disease, including unexplained persistent elevation of hepatic transaminases.</li><li>Pregnant women or women planning to conceive.</li><li>Nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is generally well-tolerated. Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul>', 'active', '8180_1746371707.webp', 53, 12, NULL, '2025-03-10 22:53:22', '2025-05-04 19:15:07'),
(2869, 'Vorifast', 'Voriconazole', 1000.00, 1, 'x 40 ml bottle', '40 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '200 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>An azole antifungal drug is Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml. The following fungal infections are recommended for use in individuals 12 years of age and older:</p><ul><li>Aspergillosis invasive</li><li>Disseminated candidiasis and candidemia (nonneutropenic) in the skin, abdomen, kidney, bladder wall, and wounds</li><li>Esophageal candidiasis</li><li>Severe infections brought on by Fusarium species include Fusarium solani, Scedosporium apiospermum</li><li>Those who are resistant to or intolerant of alternative treatments.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Other Antifungal preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The major mechanism of action of Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml is the suppression of 14 alpha-lanosterol demethylation, which is mediated by fungal cytochrome P450 and is a crucial step in the manufacture of fungal ergosterol. The buildup of 14 alpha-methyl sterols and subsequent loss of ergosterol from the fungal cell membrane may be what gives Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml its antifungal properties. It has been demonstrated that Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml is more selective for cytochrome P-450 enzymes found in fungi than for other cytochrome P-450 enzyme systems found in mammals.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Oral</strong></p><p>Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml tablet and powder for suspension are to be taken at least one hour before or one hour following a meal.</p><ul><li><strong>At or over 40 kg body weight</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Loading dose regimen is 400 mg or 10 ml every 12 hours (for the first 24 hours)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose (after first 24 hours) is 200 mg or 5 ml twice daily.</li><li><strong>Below 40 kg body weight</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Loading dose regimen is 200 mg or 5 ml every 12 hours (for the first 24 hours)</li><li>Maintenance dose (after first 24 hours) is 100 mg or 2.5 ml twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Or, as directed by the registered physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Injection</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Invasive Aspergillosis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Loading dose: 6 mg/kg 12 hourly for the first 24 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance Dose: 4 mg/kg 12 hourly.</li><li><strong>Candidemia in nonneutropenic patients and other deep tissue Candida infections</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Loading dose: 6 mg/kg 12 hourly for the first 24 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance Dose: 3-4 mg/kg 12 hourly.</li><li><strong>Scedosporiosis and Fusariosis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Loading dose: 6 mg/kg 12 hourly for the first 24 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance Dose: 4 mg/kg 12 hourly.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>It is recommended to take Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml film-coated pills at least one hour before or one hour after a meal. Depending on the patient\'s clinical and mycological response, the length of the treatment should be as brief as possible. Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml exposure longer than 180 days (6 months) must be carefully weighed against the risks and benefits.</p><p>Prophylaxis can be given for up to 100 days, starting from the day of the transplant. Depending on the risk of acquiring invasive fungal infection (IFI), as indicated by neutropenia or immunosuppression, prophylaxis should be as brief as possible. In the event of ongoing immunosuppression or graft against host disease, it may only be continued for up to 180 days following transplantation (GvHD).</p><p><strong>Interaction of Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Inhibitors and inducers of CYP3A4, CYP2C9, and CYP2C19:</p><p>Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml dosage should be changed, and side effects or ineffectiveness should be watched for. Drugs that are CYP3A4, CYP2C9, and CYP2C19 substrates may have higher concentrations and activities when taken with Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml.</p><ul><li>Decrease these other drugs\' dosages and keep an eye out for any negative effects.</li><li>Raise the maintenance dose of oral and intravenous Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml when also taking phenytoin or efavirenz.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Known sensitivity to the drug\'s active ingredient, Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml, or any other ingredient.</p><ul><li>Risk of major adverse effects necessitates avoiding co-administration of sirolimus with terfenadine, astemizole, cisapride, pimozide, or quinidine.</li><li>Rifampin, carbamazepine, long-acting barbiturates, efavirenz, ritonavir, rifabutin, ergot alkaloids, and St. John\'s Wort should not be administered together due to the possibility of efficacy loss.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Abdominal pain, anemia, impaired vision, headaches, chest pain, nausea, and diarrhea are the most frequent adverse effects.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>There aren\'t enough facts about Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml use during pregnancy. If the potential risk to the fetus is not clearly outweighed by the benefit to the mother, Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml should not be administered during pregnancy.</p><p>There hasn\'t been any research done on Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml excretion into breast milk. Following the start of Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml treatment, breastfeeding must be discontinued.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The benefit-risk balance of long-term exposure (treatment or prophylaxis) of more than 180 days must be carefully weighed. Long-term Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml therapy has been linked to reports of squamous cell carcinoma of the skin (SCC).</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>There is no data found about overdose of Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep out of children\'s reach. Keep away from light in a dry area with a temperature below 25°C. Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml powder for suspension should be kept between 2° and 8°C.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Older people</strong>: No dosage change required.</li><li><strong>Pediatric population</strong>: It is unknown whether Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml is safe and effective for use in children under the age of two.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: Renal impairment has no impact on the pharmacokinetics of Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml taken orally. The oral dosage remains unaffected in individuals with mild to severe renal impairment. Hemodialysis clearance for Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml is 121 ml/min. Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml is not sufficiently removed by a 4-hour hemodialysis session to require a dose change.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: In patients receiving Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml who have mild to severe hepatic cirrhosis (Child-Pugh A and B), it is advised to use the regular loading dosage regimens but to reduce the maintenance dose by half. Patients with severe chronic hepatic cirrhosis have not been examined with Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml. In individuals with abnormal liver function tests (aspartate transaminase [AST], alanine transaminase [ALT], alkaline phosphatase [ALP], or total bilirubin &gt;5 times the upper limit of normal), there is minimal information on the safety of Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml. Only use Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml in patients with severe hepatic impairment if the benefit justifies the potential risk because it has been linked to increases in liver function tests and clinical symptoms of liver damage, such as jaundice. Drug toxicity in patients with severe hepatic impairment needs to be closely monitored.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Other Antifungal preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>A triazole antifungal drug called Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml is used to treat severe fungal infections. By preventing CYP450-dependent 14-alpha sterol demethylase from functioning, Vorifast 200 mg/5 ml binds and prevents the formation of ergosterol. Ergosterol levels in the membrane of fungal cells decrease as a result of the suppression of 14-alpha sterol demethylase.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There is no sufficient, carefully regulated research on pregnant women. Only if the possible benefit outweighs the potential risk to the fetus should it be taken during pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>The effectiveness for children under the age of 12 has not been established.</p>', 'active', '93_1741629207.jpg', 1, 12, NULL, '2025-03-10 22:53:27', '2025-05-07 19:51:28'),
