es exposure; limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants</strong>: May prolong INR; monitor INR frequently.</li><li><strong>Lipid-lowering therapies</strong>: Combining with fibrates or niacin increases the risk of muscle-related side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of its components.</li><li>Liver disease, including unexplained persistent elevation of hepatic transaminases.</li><li>Pregnant women or women planning to conceive.</li><li>Nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is generally well-tolerated. Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1799_1741629860.jpg', 6, 12, NULL, '2025-03-10 23:04:20', '2025-03-10 23:04:20'),
(2878, 'Pretor', 'Pregabalin', 12.04, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pretor 50 mg</strong></p><p> Pretor 50 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Neuropathic pain linked to diabetic peripheral neuropathy (DPN)</li><li>Neuralgia following shingles (PHN)</li><li>Treatment of partial-onset seizures in patients older than one month with adjunctive therapy</li><li>Fibromyalgia</li><li>Neuropathic pain brought on by spinal cord damage</li><li>Neuropathic pain linked to diabetic peripheral neuropathy (DPN)</li><li>Neuralgia following shingles (PHN)</li><li>The following conditions are treated with Pretor 50 mg CR tablets:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Neuropathic pain brought on by diabetic peripheral neuropathy (DPN)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Neuralgia following shingles (PHN)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Supplemental antiepileptic medications</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Gamma-amino butyric acid, an inhibitory neurotransmitter, is the structural basis of the drug Pretor 50 mg (GABA). The GABAA, GABAB, or benzodiazepine receptors are not directly bound by it. In the tissues of the central nervous system, Pretor 50 mg binds to the alpha-2-delta site (an auxiliary subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels) with a high affinity. Pretor 50 mg has a 90% oral bioavailability that is dose-independent. In people with normal renal function, it is largely excreted from the body through the kidneys as an unaltered medication with a mean elimination half-life of 6.3 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Pretor 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neuropathic pain associated with diabetic peripheral neuropathy</strong>: The maximum recommended dose of Pretor 50 mg is 100 mg three times a day (300 mg/day) in patients with creatinine clearance of at least 60 mL/min. Dosing should begin at 50 mg three times a day (150 mg/day) and may be increased to 300 mg/day within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability.</li><li><strong>Post-herpetic neuralgia</strong>: The recommended dose of Pretor 50 mg is 75 to 150 mg two times a day, or 50 to 100 mg three times a day (150 to 300 mg/day) in patients with creatinine clearance of at least 60 mL/min. Dosing should begin at 75 mg two times a day, or 50 mg three times a day (150 mg/day) and may be increased to 300 mg/day within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability.</li><li><strong>Adjunctive therapy for adult patients with partial onset seizures</strong>: In general, it is recommended that patients be started on a total daily dose no greater than 150 mg/day (75 mg two times a day, or 50 mg three times a day). Based on individual patient response and tolerability, the dose may be increased to a maximum dose of 600 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Management of Fibromyalgia</strong>: The recommended dose of Pretor 50 mg for fibromyalgia is 300 to 450 mg/day. Dosing should begin at 75 mg two times a day (150 mg/day) and may be increased to 150 mg two times a day (300 mg/day) within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability.</li><li><strong>Neuropathic pain associated with spinal cord injury</strong>: The recommended dose range is 150 to 600 mg/day. The recommended starting dose is 75 mg two times a day (150 mg/day). The dose may be increased to 150 mg two times a day (300 mg/day) within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability. Pretor 50 mg capsules can be taken without regard to meals.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Pretor 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neuropathic pain associated with diabetic peripheral neuropathy in adults (DPN)</strong>: The maximum recommended dose of Pretor 50 mg is 100 mg three times a day (300 mg/day) in patients with creatinine clearance of at least 60 ml/min. Dosing should begin at 50 mg three times a day (150 mg/day). The dose may be increased to 300 mg/day within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability. Begin dosing of Pretor 50 mg CR capsule at 165 mg once daily and increase to 330 mg once daily within 1 week based on individual patient response and tolerability. The maximum recommended dose of Pretor 50 mg CR capsule is 330 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Postherpetic neuralgia in adults (PHN)</strong>: The recommended dose of Pretor 50 mg is 75 to 150 mg two times a day or 50 to 100 mg three times a day (150 to 300 mg/day) in patients with creatinine clearance of at least 60 ml/min. Dosing should begin at 75 mg two times a day or 50 mg three times a day (150 mg/day). The dose may be increased to 300 mg/day within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability. Patients who do not experience sufficient pain relief following 2 to 4 weeks of treatment with 300 mg/day and who are able to tolerate Pretor 50 mg, may be treated with up to 300 mg two times a day or 200 mg three times a day (600 mg/day).</li><li><strong>Management of fibromyalgia in adults</strong>: The recommended dose of Pretor 50 mg is 300 to 450 mg/day. Dosing should begin at 75 mg two times a day (150 mg/day). The dose may be increased to 150 mg two times a day (300 mg/day) within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability. Patients who do not experience sufficient benefit with 300 mg/day may be further increased to 225 mg two times a day (450 mg/day).</li><li><strong>Neuropathic pain associated with spinal cord injury in adults</strong>: The recommended dose range of Pretor 50 mg is 150 to 600 mg/day. The recommended starting dose is 75 mg two times a day (150 mg/day). The dose may be increased to 150 mg two times a day (300 mg/day) within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability. Patients who do not experience sufficient pain relief after 2 to 3 weeks of treatment with 150 mg two times a day and who tolerate Pretor 50 mg may be treated with up to 300 mg two times a day.</li><li><strong>Conversion from Pretor 50 mg capsules to Pretor 50 mg CR capsule tablet</strong>: When switching from Pretor 50 mg capsules to Pretor 50 mg CR capsule tablet on the day of the switch, instruct patients to take their morning dose of Pretor 50 mg capsule as prescribed and initiate Pretor 50 mg CR capsule therapy after an evening meal.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Pretor 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Route of administration</strong>: Pretor 50 mg is administered orally. Taking it with or without food is acceptable. The Pretor 50 mg CR tablet should be taken after dinner. It shouldn\'t be divided, crushed, or chewed; it should be consumed whole.</li><li><strong>Missed Dose</strong>: If patients forget to take their evening dose of Pretor 50 mg CR, they should take their regular dose after breakfast.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Pretor 50 mg</strong></p><p> Drug interaction with medication: There are not anticipated to be many significant pharmacokinetic medication interactions involving Pretor 50 mg. No relevant drug-food or other-drug interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Those with a known hypersensitivity to Pretor 50 mg or any of its ingredients should not take Pretor 50 mg.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Pretor 50 mg</strong></p><p> Dizziness, somnolence, dry mouth, edema, impaired vision, weight gain, and aberrant thinking (mainly difficulties with concentration/attention) are the most frequent adverse effects in adults. Increased weight and increased hunger are the most frequent adverse reactions in children receiving therapy for partial-onset seizures.</p>', 'active', '1318_1741629897.jpg', 45, 12, NULL, '2025-03-10 23:04:57', '2025-03-10 23:04:57');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(2879, 'Esodip', 'Levamlodipine Maleate', 7.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Esodip 2.5 mg</strong></p><p> Esodip 2.5 mg (Levamlodipine) is a calcium channel blocker that is used to treat <strong>hypertension</strong> (high blood pressure). It can be used alone or in combination with other antihypertensive agents. Lowering blood pressure reduces the risk of fatal and nonfatal cardiovascular events, including strokes and myocardial infarctions (heart attacks).</p><p><strong>Dosage of Esodip 2.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Route of administration</strong>: Oral.</li><li><strong>Dosage</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: The usual initial dose for hypertension is 2.5 mg once daily. The maximum dose is 5 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly patients or those with hepatic insufficiency</strong>: Start with 1.25 mg once daily, with possible adjustments based on blood pressure goals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric patients (6-17 years)</strong>: The effective dose is 1.25 mg to 2.5 mg once daily. Doses greater than 2.5 mg daily have not been studied.</li><li><strong>Titration</strong>: Dosage can be adjusted every 7 to 14 days based on blood pressure goals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Esodip 2.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Impact on other drugs</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>CYP3A inhibitors</strong>: Co-administration with moderate or strong CYP3A inhibitors may require dose reduction due to increased systemic exposure to amlodipine.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>CYP3A inducers</strong>: No information available on their effect on amlodipine.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Simvastatin</strong>: Amlodipine increases the systemic exposure of simvastatin. Limit simvastatin doses to 20 mg daily when on amlodipine.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Immunosuppressants</strong>: Amlodipine may increase the systemic exposure of cyclosporine or tacrolimus. Frequent monitoring of blood levels is recommended.</li><li><strong>Food</strong>: Can be taken with or without food. Avoid grapefruit products and natural licorice.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Esodip 2.5 mg (Levamlodipine) is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to amlodipine or any of its ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Esodip 2.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Difficulty in breathing</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dizziness</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Reflex tachycardia (increased heart rate)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypotension (low blood pressure)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Abdominal pain</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Edema (swelling)</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diarrhea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Back pain</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Headache</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fatigue</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypotension</strong>: Symptomatic hypotension may occur, particularly in patients with severe aortic stenosis. Acute hypotension is unlikely due to the gradual onset of action.</li><li><strong>Increased Angina or Myocardial Infarction</strong>: Worsening angina or heart attack may occur, particularly in patients with severe obstructive coronary artery disease.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Failure</strong>: In patients with hepatic impairment, titrate the dose slowly as amlodipine is metabolized in the liver.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Esodip 2.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Overdosage can lead to excessive peripheral vasodilation, causing marked hypotension and possibly reflex tachycardia.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place.</li><li>Protect from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calcium Channel Blockers</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Levamlodipine is a calcium ion antagonist (also known as a slow channel blocker) that inhibits the influx of calcium ions into vascular smooth muscle and cardiac muscle. It primarily affects vascular smooth muscle, leading to vasodilation, which reduces blood pressure. Levamlodipine is the pharmacologically active isomer of amlodipine.</p><ul><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: 64-90% bioavailability, with peak effects within 6-12 hours.</li><li><strong>Metabolism</strong>: 90% is metabolized by the liver, mainly by CYP3A4.</li><li><strong>Excretion</strong>: 60% is eliminated through the urine, with 10% of the drug excreted unmetabolized.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><h3> Limited data on amlodipine use during pregnancy show no major birth defects or miscarriage risks. There have been no observed adverse effects on the breastfed infant.</h3>', 'active', '1908_1741630187.jpg', 6, 12, NULL, '2025-03-10 23:09:47', '2025-03-10 23:09:47'),
(2880, 'Traxon', 'Ceftriaxone', 210.00, 1, 'Unit', '1 gm per vial', 'IV Injection', '1 gm per vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Traxon 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> Traxon 1 gm/vial is indicated for the treatment of the following major infections:</p><ul><li>Lower respiratory tract infections</li><li>Acute bacterial otitis media</li><li>Skin and skin structure infections</li><li>Urinary tract infections</li><li>Gonorrhea</li><li>Bacterial septicemia</li><li>Bone and joint infections</li><li>Meningitis</li><li>Prevention of postoperative infections</li><li>Perioperative prophylaxis of infections associated with surgery</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Third-generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Traxon 1 gm/vial is a 3rd generation broad-spectrum parenteral cephalosporin antibiotic with potent bactericidal activity against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms. It kills bacteria by interfering with the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall and is stable in the presence of beta-lactamases. It has a long plasma elimination half-life of about 6 to 9 hours, allowing for single or once-daily dosing. Traxon 1 gm/vial is not metabolized and is excreted unchanged in the urine (40-65%), with the remainder excreted in bile and feces. The drug is 95% protein-bound.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Traxon 1 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual dose: 1 to 2 gm once a day (or divided into two doses).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pneumonia, bronchitis, acute bacterial otitis media, skin infections, urinary tract infections, bacterial septicemia, bone and joint infections, meningitis</strong>: 1 to 2 gm IV or IM once a day (or in divided doses). Maximum dose: 4 gm/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated gonococcal infections</strong>: 250 mg IM as a single dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Surgical prophylaxis</strong>: 1 gm IV as a single dose 30 to 120 minutes before surgery.</li><li><strong>Infants and Children (1 month or older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual dose: 50 to 75 mg/kg IV or IM once a day (or in divided doses).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pneumonia, bronchitis, skin infections, urinary tract infections, bacterial septicemia, bone and joint infections</strong>: 50 to 75 mg/kg IV or IM once a day (or in divided doses). Maximum dose: 2 gm/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute bacterial otitis media</strong>: 50 mg/kg IM as a single dose. Maximum dose: 1 gm/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Meningitis</strong>: 100 mg/kg IV or IM once a day (or divided doses). Maximum dose: 4 gm/day.</li><li><strong>Duration of therapy</strong>: Continue for more than 2 days after symptoms disappear, typically for 4 to 14 days. Longer therapy may be required for complicated infections.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Traxon 1 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Intramuscular/Intravenous Injections</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For IM Injection: Dissolve 250 mg or 500 mg in 2 ml Lidocaine HCl 1% or 1 gm in 3.5 ml of Lidocaine HCl 1%.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For IV Injection: Dissolve 250 mg or 500 mg in 5 ml Water for Injection, or 1 gm in 10 ml, or 2 gm in 20 ml Water for Injection USP.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Injection should be given over 2-4 minutes, either by IM or IV injection, or via tubing infusion over 30 minutes at a concentration between 10 mg/mL and 40 mg/mL.</li><li><strong>Patient tolerance test</strong> should be conducted before starting treatment.</li><li><strong>Solution storage</strong>: Freshly reconstituted solution is recommended. It maintains potency for 6 hours at room temperature or 24 hours at 5°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Traxon 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> No drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Traxon 1 gm/vial should not be given to patients with a history of hypersensitivity to cephalosporin antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Traxon 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> Traxon 1 gm/vial is generally well tolerated. Possible side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, stomatitis, glossitis</li><li><strong>Skin</strong>: Rash, pruritus, urticaria, edema, erythema multiforme</li><li><strong>Hematologic</strong>: Eosinophilia, thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, anemia, neutropenia</li><li><strong>Hepatic</strong>: Elevated SGOT, SGPT, bilirubinemia</li><li><strong>CNS</strong>: Nervousness, confusion, sleep disturbances, headache, hyperactivity, convulsion, dizziness</li><li><strong>Phlebitis</strong>: Occurs rarely after IV administration; minimized by slow injection (2-4 minutes).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Safety in pregnancy has not been established. Should not be used unless absolutely necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Low concentrations of Traxon 1 gm/vial are excreted in breast milk. Use caution when administered to lactating mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Anaphylactic shock</strong>: May occur even in patients with a thorough history. Immediate treatment with IV epinephrine and glucocorticoids is required.</li><li><strong>Gallbladder issues</strong>: Shadows suggesting sludge may be detected in sonograms; this condition is reversible upon discontinuation of therapy. Conservative management is recommended.</li><li>Regular <strong>blood tests</strong> should be conducted during prolonged treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Traxon 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> There is no specific antidote for overdose. Treatment should be symptomatic.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neonates</strong>: Traxon 1 gm/vial must not be given to premature neonates or newborns (up to 28 days of age).</li></ul>', 'active', '916_1741630450.jpg', 7, 12, NULL, '2025-03-10 23:14:10', '2025-03-10 23:14:10'),
(2881, 'Tigover', 'Flunarizine', 3.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial and Non-familial)</li><li><strong>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial)</li><li><strong>Mixed Dyslipidemia</strong> (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li><strong>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Statins</li><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischemic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is a selective and competitive inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase, the enzyme responsible for converting 3-hydroxy-3-methyl glutaryl coenzyme A to mevalonate, a cholesterol precursor. It reduces lipid levels in two ways:</p><ol><li><strong>Increases hepatic LDL receptors</strong>: Enhances uptake and breakdown of LDL.</li><li><strong>Inhibits hepatic synthesis of VLDL</strong>: Reduces overall VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage of Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General dose range</strong>: 5-40 mg once daily. (Use 40 mg only for patients not achieving LDL-C goals with 20 mg).</li><li><strong>Homozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia (HoFH)</strong>: Starting dose of 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients</strong>:</li><li>With HeFH (8-10 years): 5-10 mg/day.</li><li>With HeFH (10-17 years): 5-20 mg/day.</li><li>With HoFH (7-17 years): 20 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg can be taken with or without food at any time of the day.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong>: Increases exposure; do not exceed 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil</strong>: Avoid if possible; if needed, limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or Atazanavir/Ritonavir</strong>: Increases exposure; limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants</strong>: May prolong INR; monitor INR frequently.</li><li><strong>Lipid-lowering therapies</strong>: Combining with fibrates or niacin increases the risk of muscle-related side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of its components.</li><li>Liver disease, including unexplained persistent elevation of hepatic transaminases.</li><li>Pregnant women or women planning to conceive.</li><li>Nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is generally well-tolerated. Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '540_1741631181.jpg', 6, 12, NULL, '2025-03-10 23:26:21', '2025-03-10 23:26:21'),
(2882, 'Sizodon', 'Risperidone', 6.02, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial and Non-familial)</li><li><strong>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial)</li><li><strong>Mixed Dyslipidemia</strong> (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li><strong>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Statins</li><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischemic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is a selective and competitive inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase, the enzyme responsible for converting 3-hydroxy-3-methyl glutaryl coenzyme A to mevalonate, a cholesterol precursor. It reduces lipid levels in two ways:</p><ol><li><strong>Increases hepatic LDL receptors</strong>: Enhances uptake and breakdown of LDL.</li><li><strong>Inhibits hepatic synthesis of VLDL</strong>: Reduces overall VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage of Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General dose range</strong>: 5-40 mg once daily. (Use 40 mg only for patients not achieving LDL-C goals with 20 mg).</li><li><strong>Homozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia (HoFH)</strong>: Starting dose of 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients</strong>:</li><li>With HeFH (8-10 years): 5-10 mg/day.</li><li>With HeFH (10-17 years): 5-20 mg/day.</li><li>With HoFH (7-17 years): 20 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg can be taken with or without food at any time of the day.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong>: Increases exposure; do not exceed 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil</strong>: Avoid if possible; if needed, limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or Atazanavir/Ritonavir</strong>: Increases exposure; limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants</strong>: May prolong INR; monitor INR frequently.</li><li><strong>Lipid-lowering therapies</strong>: Combining with fibrates or niacin increases the risk of muscle-related side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of its components.</li><li>Liver disease, including unexplained persistent elevation of hepatic transaminases.</li><li>Pregnant women or women planning to conceive.</li><li>Nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is generally well-tolerated. Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '426_1741631237.webp', 16, 12, NULL, '2025-03-10 23:27:17', '2025-03-10 23:27:17'),
(2883, 'Arotril', 'Clonazepam', 13.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial and Non-familial)</li><li><strong>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial)</li><li><strong>Mixed Dyslipidemia</strong> (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li><strong>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Statins</li><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischemic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is a selective and competitive inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase, the enzyme responsible for converting 3-hydroxy-3-methyl glutaryl coenzyme A to mevalonate, a cholesterol precursor. It reduces lipid levels in two ways:</p><ol><li><strong>Increases hepatic LDL receptors</strong>: Enhances uptake and breakdown of LDL.</li><li><strong>Inhibits hepatic synthesis of VLDL</strong>: Reduces overall VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage of Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General dose range</strong>: 5-40 mg once daily. (Use 40 mg only for patients not achieving LDL-C goals with 20 mg).</li><li><strong>Homozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia (HoFH)</strong>: Starting dose of 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients</strong>:</li><li>With HeFH (8-10 years): 5-10 mg/day.</li><li>With HeFH (10-17 years): 5-20 mg/day.</li><li>With HoFH (7-17 years): 20 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg can be taken with or without food at any time of the day.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong>: Increases exposure; do not exceed 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil</strong>: Avoid if possible; if needed, limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or Atazanavir/Ritonavir</strong>: Increases exposure; limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants</strong>: May prolong INR; monitor INR frequently.</li><li><strong>Lipid-lowering therapies</strong>: Combining with fibrates or niacin increases the risk of muscle-related side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of its components.</li><li>Liver disease, including unexplained persistent elevation of hepatic transaminases.</li><li>Pregnant women or women planning to conceive.</li><li>Nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is generally well-tolerated. Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1430_1741631293.jpg', 29, 12, NULL, '2025-03-10 23:28:13', '2025-03-10 23:28:13'),
