es exposure; limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants</strong>: May prolong INR; monitor INR frequently.</li><li><strong>Lipid-lowering therapies</strong>: Combining with fibrates or niacin increases the risk of muscle-related side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of its components.</li><li>Liver disease, including unexplained persistent elevation of hepatic transaminases.</li><li>Pregnant women or women planning to conceive.</li><li>Nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is generally well-tolerated. Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '681_1741948051.webp', 1, 12, NULL, '2025-03-14 15:27:31', '2025-03-14 15:27:31'),
(3049, 'Sitap', 'Sitagliptin', 18.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial and Non-familial)</li><li><strong>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial)</li><li><strong>Mixed Dyslipidemia</strong> (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li><strong>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Statins</li><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischemic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is a selective and competitive inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase, the enzyme responsible for converting 3-hydroxy-3-methyl glutaryl coenzyme A to mevalonate, a cholesterol precursor. It reduces lipid levels in two ways:</p><ol><li><strong>Increases hepatic LDL receptors</strong>: Enhances uptake and breakdown of LDL.</li><li><strong>Inhibits hepatic synthesis of VLDL</strong>: Reduces overall VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage of Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General dose range</strong>: 5-40 mg once daily. (Use 40 mg only for patients not achieving LDL-C goals with 20 mg).</li><li><strong>Homozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia (HoFH)</strong>: Starting dose of 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients</strong>:</li><li>With HeFH (8-10 years): 5-10 mg/day.</li><li>With HeFH (10-17 years): 5-20 mg/day.</li><li>With HoFH (7-17 years): 20 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg can be taken with or without food at any time of the day.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong>: Increases exposure; do not exceed 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil</strong>: Avoid if possible; if needed, limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or Atazanavir/Ritonavir</strong>: Increases exposure; limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants</strong>: May prolong INR; monitor INR frequently.</li><li><strong>Lipid-lowering therapies</strong>: Combining with fibrates or niacin increases the risk of muscle-related side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of its components.</li><li>Liver disease, including unexplained persistent elevation of hepatic transaminases.</li><li>Pregnant women or women planning to conceive.</li><li>Nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is generally well-tolerated. Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1395_1741948102.webp', 44, 12, NULL, '2025-03-14 15:28:22', '2025-03-14 15:28:22'),
(3050, 'Protear', 'Povidone Iodine', 95.00, 1, 'x 10 ml drop', '10 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '5%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications for Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial and Non-familial)</li><li><strong>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial)</li><li><strong>Mixed Dyslipidemia</strong> (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li><strong>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Statins</li><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischemic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is a selective and competitive inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase, the enzyme responsible for converting 3-hydroxy-3-methyl glutaryl coenzyme A to mevalonate, a cholesterol precursor. It reduces lipid levels in two ways:</p><ol><li><strong>Increases hepatic LDL receptors</strong>: Enhances uptake and breakdown of LDL.</li><li><strong>Inhibits hepatic synthesis of VLDL</strong>: Reduces overall VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage of Rocovas 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General dose range</strong>: 5-40 mg once daily. (Use 40 mg only for patients not achieving LDL-C goals with 20 mg).</li><li><strong>Homozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia (HoFH)</strong>: Starting dose of 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients</strong>:</li><li>With HeFH (8-10 years): 5-10 mg/day.</li><li>With HeFH (10-17 years): 5-20 mg/day.</li><li>With HoFH (7-17 years): 20 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg can be taken with or without food at any time of the day.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong>: Increases exposure; do not exceed 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil</strong>: Avoid if possible; if needed, limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or Atazanavir/Ritonavir</strong>: Increases exposure; limit the dose to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants</strong>: May prolong INR; monitor INR frequently.</li><li><strong>Lipid-lowering therapies</strong>: Combining with fibrates or niacin increases the risk of muscle-related side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of its components.</li><li>Liver disease, including unexplained persistent elevation of hepatic transaminases.</li><li>Pregnant women or women planning to conceive.</li><li>Nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Rocovas 5 mg is generally well-tolerated. Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul>', 'active', '170_1741948190.jpg', 29, 12, NULL, '2025-03-14 15:29:50', '2025-04-23 10:33:09'),
(3051, 'Trimazole', 'Clotrimazole', 68.00, 1, 'x 20 ml bottle', '20 ml bottle', 'Topical Solution', '1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Trimazole 1%</strong></p><p>The confirmed indication for Trimazole 1% includes:</p><ul><li>All dermatomycoses due to dermatophytes (e.g. <em>Trichophyton</em> species).</li><li>All dermatomycoses due to yeasts (<em>Candida</em> species).</li><li>Dermatomycoses due to moulds and other fungi.</li><li>Skin diseases showing superinfections with these fungi.</li></ul><p>Examples of these conditions include interdigital mycoses (e.g. athlete’s foot), paronychias (associated with nail mycoses), mycoses in skin folds, <em>Candida</em> vulvitis, <em>Candida</em> balanitis, <em>Pityriasis versicolor</em>, and erythrasma.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses, topical antifungal preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Trimazole 1% is a broad-spectrum antifungal. It also exhibits activity against <em>Trichomonas</em>, <em>Staphylococci</em>, <em>Streptococci</em>, and <em>Bacteroides</em>. Trimazole 1% kills fungi and yeasts by interfering with their cell membranes. It works by stopping the production of ergosterol, an essential component of fungal cell membranes, resulting in increased membrane permeability and leakage of essential constituents of the fungal cells.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Trimazole 1%</strong></p><ul><li>Wash skin with soap and water and dry thoroughly.</li><li>Apply a thin layer of the cream or solution 2-3 times daily and gently massage over the affected area as directed by the doctor.</li><li>For athlete’s foot, pay special attention to the spaces between the toes.</li><li>Best results in athlete\'s foot and ringworm are usually obtained with 4 weeks of use of this product.</li><li>The cream should be applied two or three times daily for one month or for at least two weeks after the disappearance of all signs of infection.</li><li>If satisfactory results have not occurred within these times, consultation with a doctor is essential.</li></ul><p><strong>Recommended Duration of Treatment</strong></p><ul><li>Dermatomycoses: 3-4 weeks</li><li><em>Candida</em> vulvitis and <em>Candida</em> balanitis: 1-2 weeks</li><li>Erythrasma and <em>Pityriasis versicolor</em>: approximately 3 weeks</li><li>In fungal infection of the feet, to prevent relapses: Continue treatment for about 2 weeks beyond the disappearance of all signs of disease.</li></ul><p>Trimazole 1% cream is odorless, can be washed off, and does not stain.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to Trimazole 1%.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Trimazole 1%</strong></p><p>Trimazole 1% is generally well tolerated after local application, but a few side effects have been reported infrequently, including erythema, stinging, blistering, peeling, oedema, pruritus, urticaria, and general irritation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: Trimazole 1% is recommended during pregnancy only after consulting a doctor.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Since systemic absorption of Trimazole 1% following topical application is marginal, there should be no risk for the infant during lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>If local intolerance develops, consider withdrawal of the medicine and institution of appropriate therapy.</li><li>Trimazole 1% solution is not intended for ophthalmic use.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Trimazole 1%</strong></p><p>Supportive measures should be taken in case of accidental oral ingestion.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place. Protect from light. Do not freeze.</p><p><strong>Duration of Treatment</strong></p><p>For successful treatment, reliable and sufficiently long application of Trimazole 1% cream is important. The duration of treatment varies depending on factors such as the extent and localization of the disease.</p>', 'active', '617_1742013954.jpg', 7, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 09:45:54', '2025-05-07 10:09:57'),
(3052, 'Nordette 28', 'Ethinyl Estradiol + Levonorgestrel + Ferrous Fumarate', 13.64, 1, 'x 28\'s pack', '28\'s tablet pack', 'Tablet', '0.03 mg+0.15 mg+75 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nordette 28 (0.15 mg + 0.03 mg)</strong></p><p> This is indicated for the prevention of pregnancy in women who elect to use oral contraceptives as a method of contraception. Oral contraceptives are highly effective. The efficacy of these contraceptive methods, except sterilization and IUDs, depends on the reliability with which they are used. Correct and consistent use of methods can result in lower failure rates.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Female sex hormones.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> The combination of hormonal contraceptives inhibits ovulation by modulating pituitary secretion of gonadotrophins, luteinizing hormone, and follicle-stimulating hormone through a negative feedback system. They reduce sperm penetration if ovulation does occur by altering the cervical mucus and cause changes in the endometrium, reducing the risk of nidation. They may also change the tubal transport of the ova through the fallopian tubes.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Nordette 28 (0.15 mg + 0.03 mg)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Monophasic combined oral contraceptive (COC):</strong> Levonorgestrel 150-250 mcg + Ethinylestradiol 30 mcg once daily.</li><li><strong>Triphasic COC:</strong> Levonorgestrel 50-125 mcg + Ethinylestradiol 30-40 mcg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Nordette 28 (0.15 mg + 0.03 mg)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CYP3A4 inducers</strong> may decrease levels/effects (e.g., aminoglutethimide, carbamazepine, nafcillin, nevirapine, atazanavir, nelfinavir, phenobarbital, phenytoin, lamotrigine, rifamycins, griseofulvin, ritonavir).</li><li><strong>Antibiotics</strong> (e.g., ampicillin, tetracycline) may reduce efficacy.</li><li><strong>Estrogens</strong> may antagonize the anticoagulant effect of coumarins.</li><li>May <strong>inhibit metabolism</strong> of prednisolone and ciclosporin.</li><li>May <strong>reduce clearance</strong> of alprazolam, chlordiazepoxide, diazepam.</li><li>May <strong>increase clearance</strong> of lorazepam, oxazepam, temazepam.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy, undiagnosed vaginal bleeding, severe arterial disease.</li><li>Liver adenoma, porphyria, history of breast cancer, hepatic impairment.</li><li>Thrombophlebitis or thromboembolic disorders, estrogen-dependent tumors.</li><li>Smoking ≥40 cigarettes daily, age &gt;50 years.</li><li>Diabetes complications, BMI &gt;39 kg/m².</li><li>Migraine with typical focal aura, BP &gt;160/100 mmHg.</li><li>History of transient ischemic attacks, SLE, gallstones.</li><li>History of hemolytic uremic syndrome, cholestatic jaundice, chorea.</li><li>Breastfeeding during the first six months postpartum.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Nordette 28 (0.15 mg + 0.03 mg)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Menstrual irregularities, headache, dizziness, breast discomfort, weight changes, nausea, vomiting.</li><li><strong>Hormonal Effects:</strong> Changes in libido, hair loss or hirsutism, genitourinary changes, gynecological disorders.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Risks:</strong> Increased BP, pulmonary thromboembolism, retinal vascular thrombosis.</li><li><strong>Metabolic &amp; Endocrine Effects:</strong> Cholestatic jaundice, glucose intolerance, changes in lipid profile.</li><li><strong>Other Effects:</strong> Depression, fluid retention, skin reactions, chloasma, contact lens intolerance.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category X:</strong> Studies in animals or humans have demonstrated fetal abnormalities or risks, and the drug is contraindicated in pregnant women.</li><li>Contraindicated in women who are or may become pregnant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Sex-steroid dependent cancer, past ectopic pregnancy, malabsorption syndromes.</li><li>Active liver disease, recurrent cholestatic jaundice, cardiovascular or renal impairment history.</li><li>Diabetes, asthma, epilepsy, migraine, depression.</li><li>Conditions exacerbated by fluid retention, hypercalcemia, gallbladder disease.</li><li>Patients at risk of venous thromboembolism, preexisting uterine leiomyomata.</li><li>BP &gt;140/90 mmHg, age &gt;35 years, BMI 30-39 kg/m².</li><li>GI upset (vomiting, diarrhea), missed pills, and interactions with other drugs may require additional contraceptive precautions.</li><li>Should be taken at the same time each day.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Nordette 28 (0.15 mg + 0.03 mg)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms:</strong> Nausea, vomiting, withdrawal bleeding may occur in females.</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> Symptom-specific and supportive; emesis and charcoal administration may be used.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Use with caution and monitor BP.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Contraindicated.</li></ul>', 'active', '1081_1742014247.jpg', 12, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 09:50:48', '2025-03-15 09:50:48'),
(3053, 'Loteba', 'Loteprednol Etabonate + Tobramycin', 200.60, 1, 'Drop', '5 ml drop', 'Drop', '5 ml drop', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Loteba 0.5%+0.3%</strong></p><p> This is indicated for steroid-responsive inflammatory ocular conditions for which a corticosteroid is indicated and where superficial bacterial ocular infection or a risk of bacterial ocular infection exists. Ocular steroids are indicated in inflammatory conditions of the palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva, cornea, and anterior segment of the globe such as allergic conjunctivitis, acne rosacea, superficial punctate keratitis, herpes zoster keratitis, iritis, and cyclitis. It is also indicated in chronic anterior uveitis and corneal injury from chemical, radiation, or thermal burns, or penetration of foreign bodies. This is also indicated for the treatment of post-operative inflammation following ocular surgery.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic steroid - antibiotic combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Loteprednol is a corticosteroid which is thought to act by the induction of phospholipase A2 inhibitory proteins, which control the biosynthesis of potent mediators of inflammation such as prostaglandins and leukotrienes by inhibiting the release of their common precursor, arachidonic acid. Tobramycin is a member of aminoglycosides which shows bactericidal activity by inhibiting the protein synthesis of bacteria.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Loteba 0.5%+0.3%</strong></p><p> Shake well before use. Apply one drop into the conjunctival sac of the affected eye(s) every four to six hours. During the initial 24 to 48 hours, the dosing may be increased to every one to two hours.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Loteba 0.5%+0.3%</strong></p><p> Since Loteprednol Etabonate is not detected in plasma following topical administration, it is not expected to affect the pharmacokinetics of systemically administered medicinal products.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Contraindicated in most viral diseases of the cornea and conjunctiva. Also contraindicated in individuals with known or suspected hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of this preparation and to other corticosteroids.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Loteba 0.5%+0.3%</strong></p><p> Increased intraocular pressure, burning and stinging upon instillation, vision disorders, discharge, itching, lacrimation disorder, photophobia, corneal deposits, ocular discomfort, eyelid disorder, and other unspecified eye disorders may occur. The incidence of non-ocular adverse events (headache) has also been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Use in pregnancy: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Loteprednol Etabonate &amp; Tobramycin should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p> Use in lactation: Caution should be exercised when Loteprednol Etabonate &amp; Tobramycin is administered to a nursing mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> For ophthalmic use only. If this product is used for 10 days or longer, intraocular pressure should be monitored. Fungal infections of the cornea are particularly prone to develop coincidentally with long-term use of topical steroids. Prolonged use may result in the overgrowth of nonsusceptible organisms, including fungi. If superinfection occurs, appropriate therapy should be initiated. Cross-sensitivity to other aminoglycoside antibiotics may occur.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at room temperature &amp; protect from light. Do not touch the dropper tip to any surface. It is desirable that the contents should not be used four weeks after first opening the bottle. Protect from freezing.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> Use in children: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic steroid - antibiotic combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Loteprednol is a corticosteroid which is thought to act by the induction of phospholipase A2 inhibitory proteins, which control the biosynthesis of potent mediators of inflammation such as prostaglandins and leukotrienes by inhibiting the release of their common precursor, arachidonic acid. Tobramycin is a member of aminoglycosides which shows bactericidal activity by inhibiting the protein synthesis of bacteria.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Use in children: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><br></p><p>4o</p>', 'active', '625_1742015394.jpg', 62, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 10:09:54', '2025-03-15 10:09:54'),
(3054, 'Loteflam', 'Loteprednol Etabonate', 176.00, 1, 'Drop', '5 ml drop', 'Drop', '5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Loteflam 0.5%</strong></p><p> Loteprednol ophthalmic gel: This is indicated for the treatment of post-operative inflammation and pain following ocular surgery. It is also effective in steroid-responsive inflammatory conditions of the palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva, cornea, and anterior segment of the eyeball such as allergic conjunctivitis, acne rosacea, superficial punctate keratitis, herpes zoster keratitis, iritis, and cyclitis.</p><p> Loteprednol ophthalmic suspension: This is indicated in steroid-responsive inflammatory conditions of the palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva, cornea, and anterior segment of the eyeball such as allergic conjunctivitis, acne rosacea, superficial punctate keratitis, herpes zoster keratitis, iritis, and cyclitis. It is also effective in post-operative inflammation.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic Steroid preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Corticosteroids inhibit the inflammatory response to a variety of inciting agents and probably delay or slow healing. They inhibit edema, fibrin deposition, capillary dilation, leukocyte migration, capillary proliferation, fibroblast proliferation, deposition of collagen, and scar formation associated with inflammation. There is no generally accepted explanation for the mechanism of action of ocular corticosteroids. However, corticosteroids are thought to act by the induction of phospholipase A2 inhibitory proteins, collectively called lipocortins. These proteins control the biosynthesis of potent mediators of inflammation such as prostaglandins and leukotrienes by inhibiting the release of their common precursor, arachidonic acid. Arachidonic acid is released from membrane phospholipids by phospholipase A2.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Loteflam 0.5%</strong></p><p> Shake the bottle vigorously before using.</p><ul><li><strong>Steroid-responsive disease treatment:</strong> Apply 1 to 2 drops of Loteprednol into the conjunctival sac of the affected eye(s) four times daily. During the initial treatment within the first week, the dosing may be increased to 1 drop every hour, if necessary. Care should be taken not to discontinue therapy prematurely.</li><li><strong>Post-Operative Inflammation:</strong> Apply 1 to 2 drops of Loteprednol into the conjunctival sac of the operated eye(s) four times daily, beginning 24 hours after surgery and continuing throughout the first 2 weeks of the post-operative period.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Loteflam 0.5%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Loteprednol ophthalmic gel:</strong> Instill one to two drops into the conjunctival sac of the affected eye four times daily, beginning the day after surgery and continuing throughout the first 2 weeks of the post-operative period.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Loteprednol ophthalmic suspension:For steroid-responsive ocular inflammation:</strong> Instill 1 drop into the conjunctival sac of the eye(s) four times a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For inflammation after surgery:</strong> Instill 1 drop into the conjunctival sac of the operated eye(s) four times a day, beginning 24 hours after surgery and continuing throughout the first two weeks after surgery.</li><li><strong>Use in children:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Loteflam 0.5%</strong></p><p> Since Loteflam 0.5% is not detected in plasma following topical administration, it is not expected to affect the pharmacokinetics of systemically administered medicinal products.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> It is contraindicated in most viral diseases of the cornea and conjunctiva. It is also contraindicated in individuals with known or suspected hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of this preparation and to other corticosteroids.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Loteflam 0.5%</strong></p><p> Glaucoma with optic nerve damage, visual acuity and field defects, cataract formation, secondary ocular infection following suppression of host response, and perforation of the globe may occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> It is Pregnancy Category C. No clinical data are available for use in pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is not known whether topical ophthalmic administration of corticosteroids could result in sufficient systemic absorption to produce detectable quantities in human milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> For ophthalmic use only. If this product is used for 10 days or longer, intraocular pressure should be monitored. Fungal infections of the cornea are particularly prone to develop coincidentally with the long-term use of topical steroids. Prolonged use of corticosteroids may result in glaucoma with damage to the optic nerve, defects in visual acuity and visual field, and posterior subcapsular cataract formation. Use of a corticosteroid medication in the treatment of patients with a history of herpes simplex requires great caution.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Protect from light. Store in a cool and dry place. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic Steroid preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Loteprednol is a corticosteroid which is thought to act by the induction of phospholipase A2 inhibitory proteins, which control the biosynthesis of potent mediators of inflammation such as prostaglandins and leukotrienes by inhibiting the release of their common precursor, arachidonic acid.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> No clinical data are available for use in pregnancy. It is not known whether Loteflam 0.5% passes into breast milk.</p>', 'active', '1113_1742015591.jpg', 37, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 10:13:11', '2025-03-15 10:13:11'),
(3055, 'Mudiral', 'Sertraline Hydrochloride', 6.02, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Mudiral 50 mg</strong></p><p> Mudiral 50 mg is indicated for the treatment of depressive illness, obsessive-compulsive disorder, post-traumatic stress disorder, and panic disorder.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> SSRIs &amp; related anti-depressant drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Mudiral 50 mg has a potent and selective inhibitory action on CNS neuronal reuptake of 5-HT, resulting in increased 5-HT concentrations at the synaptic clefts, leading to the facilitation of its sustained activity at the postsynaptic receptor sites. This ultimately results in an improvement in depression. Reduction of serotonin turnover in the brain by Mudiral 50 mg is also another contributing factor implicated in its action. Its prolonged elimination half-life offers the benefit of once-daily administration.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Mudiral 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Depressive illness:Adults:</strong> Initially 50 mg daily, increased if necessary by increments of 50 mg over several weeks to a maximum of 200 mg daily. The usual maintenance dose is 50 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children and adolescents under 18 years:</strong> Not recommended.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Obsessive-compulsive disorder:Adults and adolescents over 13 years:</strong> Initially 50 mg daily, increased if necessary in steps of 50 mg over several weeks. The usual dose range is 50-200 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6-12 years):</strong> Initially 25 mg daily, increased to 50 mg daily after 1 week, further increased if necessary in steps of 50 mg at intervals of at least 1 week (maximum 200 mg daily).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Post-traumatic stress disorder:Adults:</strong> Initially 25 mg daily, increased after 1 week to 50 mg daily. If response is partial and if the drug is tolerated, the dose can be increased in steps of 50 mg over several weeks to a maximum of 200 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children and adolescents under 18 years:</strong> Not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Mudiral 50 mg</strong></p><p> Potential effects of co-administration of drugs that are highly bound to plasma proteins: Since Mudiral 50 mg is tightly bound to plasma proteins, administering it to a patient taking another drug that is also tightly bound to proteins (e.g., warfarin, digitoxin) may cause a shift in plasma concentrations, potentially resulting in adverse effects. Conversely, adverse effects may result from the displacement of protein-bound Mudiral 50 mg by other tightly bound drugs. Mudiral 50 mg may also interact with other drugs such as cimetidine, CNS-active drugs like diazepam, hypoglycemic drugs, and atenolol.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Mudiral 50 mg is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to Mudiral 50 mg or any of the excipients of the drug.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Mudiral 50 mg</strong></p><p> Mudiral 50 mg may cause side effects such as upset stomach, diarrhea, constipation, vomiting, dry mouth, loss of appetite, weight changes, drowsiness, dizziness, headache, pain, burning or tingling in the hands or feet, excitement, and sore throat.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Although animal studies did not provide any evidence of teratogenicity, the safety of Mudiral 50 mg during human pregnancy has not been established.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Mudiral 50 mg is known to be excreted in breast milk. Its effects on the nursing infant have not yet been established. If treatment with Mudiral 50 mg is considered necessary, discontinuation of breastfeeding should be considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Precaution should be taken in cases of liver problems, kidney diseases, seizures, heart problems, and any allergies. This drug may cause dizziness or drowsiness; therefore, caution should be taken in activities requiring alertness, such as driving or using machinery. Caution is advised while using this product in the elderly, as they may be more sensitive to the effects of the drug. Do not take this drug if you have taken a monoamine oxidase inhibitor (MAOI) in the last five weeks.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at 25°C.</p>', 'active', '966_1742016097.jpg', 7, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 10:21:37', '2025-03-15 10:21:37'),
(3056, 'Topita SR', 'Tapentadol Hydrochloride', 25.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Topita SR 100 mg</strong></p><p> Tapentadol is indicated for the relief of moderate to severe acute pain in patients 18 years of age or older.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Tapentadol is a centrally-acting synthetic analgesic. It is 18 times less potent than morphine in terms of binding to human mu-opioid receptors. It also increases norepinephrine concentrations in the brains of rats via inhibition of norepinephrine reuptake. Selective mu-opioid antagonists like naloxone can block analgesia from tapentadol. It also has no effect on the QT interval. Tapentadol causes large increases in levels of extracellular norepinephrine (NE) due to a dual mechanism of action involving mu-opioid receptor (MOR) agonism as well as noradrenaline reuptake inhibition.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Topita SR 100 mg</strong></p><p> As with many centrally-acting analgesic medications, the dosing regimen should be individualized according to the severity of pain being treated, the previous experience with similar drugs, and the ability to monitor the patient. The dose is 50 mg, 75 mg, or 100 mg every 4 to 6 hours depending upon pain intensity. On the first day of dosing, the second dose may be administered as soon as one hour after the first dose if adequate pain relief is not attained. Subsequent dosing is 50 mg, 75 mg, or 100 mg every 4 to 6 hours and should be adjusted to maintain adequate analgesia with acceptable tolerability. Daily doses greater than 700 mg on the first day of therapy and 600 mg on subsequent days have not been studied and are not recommended.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Topita SR 100 mg</strong></p><p> Increased risk of serotonin syndrome with other drugs that enhance monoaminergic neurotransmission (e.g. TCAs, triptans, SSRIs, serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors). Enhanced sedative effect with benzodiazepines, barbiturates, antipsychotics, H1-antihistamines, and other opioids. Increased potential for addiction with mixed μ-opioid agonists/antagonists (e.g. nalbuphine, pentazocine) or partial μ-opioid agonists (e.g. buprenorphine). Increased systemic exposure with strong inhibitors of UGT1A6, UGT1A9, and UGT2B7 isoenzymes. Decreased efficacy with strong enzyme inducers (e.g. rifampicin, phenobarbital).</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> This drug is contraindicated in patients with impaired pulmonary function. It is also contraindicated in patients with acute or severe bronchial asthma or hypercapnia in unmonitored settings or the absence of resuscitative equipment. This drug is contraindicated in any patient who has or is suspected of having paralytic ileus.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Topita SR 100 mg</strong></p><p> The following treatment-emergent adverse events may occur: increased heart rate, decreased heart rate, visual disturbances, abdominal discomfort, impaired gastric emptying, irritability, edema, drug withdrawal syndrome, hypersensitivity, involuntary muscle contractions, sensation of heaviness, hypoesthesia, paresthesia, disturbance in attention, sedation, dysarthria, memory impairment, ataxia, presyncope, syncope, coordination abnormality, seizure, urticaria, and decreased blood pressure.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. This preparation should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. Neonates whose mothers have been taking Tapentadol should be monitored for respiratory depression.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Tapentadol should be administered with caution to patients with conditions accompanied by hypoxia, hypercapnia, and respiratory problems such as asthma or chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Patients with sleep apnea syndrome, myxedema, kyphoscoliosis, or central nervous system (CNS) depression should be cautious before using Tapentadol. Patients receiving other mu-opioid agonist analgesics, general anesthetics, phenothiazines, tranquilizers, sedatives, hypnotics, or other CNS depressants (including alcohol) concomitantly with Tapentadol may exhibit additive CNS depression.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Topita SR 100 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Symptoms:</strong> Vomiting, miosis, cardiovascular collapse, consciousness disorders up to coma, convulsions, and respiratory depression leading to respiratory arrest.</p><p> <strong>Management:</strong> Re-establish a patent airway and institute assisted or controlled ventilation. Gastrointestinal decontamination with activated charcoal or by gastric lavage may be considered within 2 hours after intake. Pure opioid receptor antagonists (e.g. naloxone) may be given as an antidote.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light and moisture. Keep the medicine out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> <strong>Pediatric use:</strong> The safety and effectiveness of Tapentadol in pediatric patients less than 18 years of age have not been established.</p><p> <strong>Use in elderly patients:</strong> In general, recommended dosing for elderly patients with normal renal and hepatic function is the same as for younger adult patients with normal renal and hepatic function. Consideration should be given to starting elderly patients with the lower range of recommended doses.</p><p> <strong>Use in renal disease:</strong> In patients with severe renal impairment, the safety and effectiveness of Tapentadol have not been established.</p><p> <strong>Use in hepatic disease:</strong> Tapentadol should be used with caution in patients with moderate hepatic impairment. Tapentadol has not been studied in patients with severe hepatic impairment.</p>', 'active', '1224_1742030519.jpg', 3, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 14:21:59', '2025-03-15 14:21:59');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3057, 'Mezoxia', 'Megestrol Acetate', 2700.00, 1, 'Bottle', '30 tablet in a bottle', 'Tablet', '160 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Mezoxia 160 mg</strong></p><p> Megestrol tablet is indicated for the palliative treatment of advanced carcinoma of the breast or endometrium (i.e., recurrent, inoperable, or metastatic disease). It should not be used instead of currently accepted procedures such as surgery, radiation, or chemotherapy. Megestrol oral suspension is indicated for the treatment of anorexia, cachexia, or an unexplained, significant weight loss in patients with a diagnosis of Acquired Immunodeficiency Syndrome (AIDS) &amp; cancer.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Hormonal Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Mezoxia 160 mg is a synthetic, antineoplastic, and progestational drug. While the precise mechanism by which Mezoxia 160 mg produces its antineoplastic effects against endometrial carcinoma is unknown at the present time, inhibition of pituitary gonadotrophin production and the resultant decrease in estrogen secretion may be factors. The antineoplastic action of Mezoxia 160 mg on carcinoma of the breast is effected by modifying the action of other steroid hormones and by exerting a direct cytotoxic effect on tumor cells.</p><p>In metastatic cancer, hormone receptors may be present in some tissues but not others. The receptor mechanism is a cyclic process whereby estrogen produced by the ovaries enters the target cell, forms a complex with a cytoplasmic receptor, and is transported into the cell nucleus. There it induces gene transcription and leads to the alteration of normal cell functions.</p><p>Pharmacologic doses of Mezoxia 160 mg not only decrease the number of hormone-dependent human breast cancer cells but also are capable of modifying and abolishing the stimulatory effects of estrogen on these cells. Estimates of plasma levels of Mezoxia 160 mg are dependent on the measurement method used. Peak plasma concentrations occur 2 to 3 hours after a single oral dose of 160 mg tablets. The plasma half-life of Mezoxia 160 mg is 33 to 38 hours. Approximately 66% of an administered dose is excreted in the urine and approximately 20% in the feces.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Mezoxia 160 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Breast cancer:</strong> 160 mg/day</li><li><strong>Endometrial carcinoma:</strong> 40-320 mg/day in divided doses</li><li>At least 2 months of continuous treatment is considered an adequate period for determining the efficacy of Megestrol.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Mezoxia 160 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet:Breast cancer:</strong> 160 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Endometrial carcinoma:</strong> 40-320 mg/day in divided doses</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">At least 2 months of continuous treatment is considered an adequate period for determining the efficacy of Megestrol.</li><li><strong>Oral Suspension:</strong> The recommended adult initial dosage of Megestrol Oral Suspension is 800 mg/day (20 ml/day).</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Mezoxia 160 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With medicine:</strong> Possible interactions of Mezoxia 160 mg with concomitant medications have not been investigated.</li><li><strong>With food and others:</strong> Estrogens and Progestogens may cause fluid retention, particularly when given in high dosages or for prolonged periods. Therapy with these agents should be administered cautiously in patients who have preexisting problems with excess fluid.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>History of hypersensitivity to Mezoxia 160 mg or any component of the formulation.</li><li>Known or suspected pregnancy.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Mezoxia 160 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Weight Gain:</strong> A frequent side effect, often associated with increased appetite and not necessarily with fluid retention.</li><li><strong>Thromboembolic Phenomena:</strong> Thrombophlebitis and pulmonary embolism (in some cases fatal) have been reported.</li><li><strong>Glucocorticoid Effects:</strong> New-onset diabetes mellitus, exacerbation of preexisting diabetes mellitus, and overt Cushing’s syndrome have been reported in association with chronic use. Adrenal insufficiency has also been observed in patients receiving or withdrawing from chronic Mezoxia 160 mg therapy.</li><li><strong>Other Side Effects:</strong> Nausea, dyspnea, tumor flare, hyperglycemia, glucose intolerance, alopecia, hypertension, carpal tunnel syndrome, mood changes, hot flashes, malaise, asthenia, lethargy, sweating, and rash.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category D:</strong> The use of progestational agents during the first four months of pregnancy is not recommended.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Very small amounts (approximately 0.1%) are excreted in mother\'s milk. The effect on newborns is unknown, so nursing should be discontinued during treatment with Mezoxia 160 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General:</strong> Close surveillance is required for any patient treated for recurrent or metastatic cancer.</li><li><strong>Use in Diabetics:</strong> Exacerbation of preexisting diabetes with increased insulin requirements has been reported in association with Mezoxia 160 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Mezoxia 160 mg</strong></p><p> No serious unexpected side effects have resulted from studies involving Mezoxia 160 mg administered in dosages as high as 1600 mg/day.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool (below 30°C) and dry place, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep all medicines out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Dose selection for elderly patients should be cautious, usually starting at the low end of the dosing range due to a greater likelihood of decreased hepatic, renal, or cardiac function, as well as other drug therapies.</li></ul>', 'active', '647_1742030891.jpg', 67, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 14:28:11', '2025-03-15 14:28:11'),
(3058, 'Tetrasina', 'Tetracycline Hydrochloride', 1.75, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Tetrasina 250 mg</strong></p><p> Tetracycline is the drug of choice in the following infections:</p><ul><li><strong>Rickettsial infections</strong>: Rocky Mountain spotted fever, endemic and scrub typhus fever, and human ehrlichiosis.</li><li><strong>Mycoplasma pneumoniae infections</strong>: Common in barracks and institutions, mostly affecting children and young adults. Rare complications include maculopapular rashes, hemolytic anemia, and meningoencephalitis.</li><li><strong>Chlamydial infections</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Chlamydia psittaci</em>: Causes psittacosis (ornithosis), contracted from infected birds, leading to pneumonia and systemic illness.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Non-gonococcal or non-specific urethritis</em>: Inflammation of the urethra not caused by gonococcal, chlamydial, or other specific infectious agents.</li><li><strong>Lyme disease</strong></li><li><strong>Brucellosis</strong></li><li><strong>Miscellaneous infections</strong>: Granuloma inguinale, cholera, glanders, relapsing fever, and <em>Vibrio vulnificus</em>.</li><li><strong>Other common uses</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Urinary tract infections (UTIs) with susceptible organisms.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Bronchitis in patients with chronic lung diseases.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pelvic inflammatory disease and other sexually transmitted diseases (STDs).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Traveler’s diarrhea.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acne vulgaris.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prostatitis.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Alternative treatment for syphilis in penicillin-allergic patients.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Anaerobic infections with susceptible organisms.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Tetracycline group of drugs.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Tetracycline primarily acts by inhibiting bacterial protein synthesis. It enters bacterial cells via an energy-dependent active transport system and binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit.</p><ul><li><strong>Activity spectrum</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Effective against many Gram-positive aerobic cocci but less effective against resistant <em>staphylococci, streptococci,</em> and <em>pneumococci</em>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Ineffective against <em>Pseudomonas</em> and many <em>Enterobacteriaceae</em>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Effective against <em>Brucella species, Calymmatobacterium granulomatis, Vibrio cholerae, and Vibrio vulnificus</em>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">High gastrointestinal concentrations lead to alteration of enteric flora.</li><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: 60-80% is absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract.</li><li><strong>Half-life</strong>: About 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Excretion</strong>: Through urine and feces.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Tetrasina 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 1-2 g daily in 2-4 divided doses.</li><li><strong>Children (above 8 years)</strong>: 25-50 mg/kg daily in 2-4 divided doses (or 0.6-1.2 g/m² daily).</li><li><strong>Administration</strong>: Take 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals.</li></ul><p><strong>Specific Dosages for Certain Conditions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Acne vulgaris</strong>: 250 mg four times daily or 500 mg every 12 hours for 1 week, then 125-250 mg for several weeks or months.</li><li><strong>Acute staphylococcal infections</strong>: 1-2 g daily in divided doses for 10-14 days.</li><li><strong>Acute streptococcal infections</strong>: 1-2 g daily in divided doses for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Amoebiasis</strong>: 1 g daily in four divided doses or 500 mg every 12 hours for 7 days (with amoebicidal agents).</li><li><strong>Brucellosis</strong>: 500 mg four times daily plus 1 g streptomycin twice daily for 1 week, then 500 mg four times daily for another week.</li><li><strong>Subacute bacterial endocarditis</strong>: 1-2 g daily in divided doses for 6 weeks (combined with a bactericidal agent).</li><li><strong>Syphilis</strong>: 30-40 g total, given over 10-15 days. Serology and spinal fluid examination should follow treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Tetrasina 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Reduced absorption</strong> with antacids containing divalent and trivalent cations (<em>Al, Ca, Mg</em>), iron, zinc, sodium bicarbonate, kaolin-pectin, bismuth subsalicylate, sucralfate, strontium ranelate, colestipol, and colestyramine.</li><li><strong>Interference with bactericidal action</strong> of penicillin.</li><li><strong>Increased anticoagulant effects</strong> when taken with anticoagulants.</li><li><strong>Decreased oral contraceptive efficacy</strong>.</li><li><strong>Nephrotoxicity risk</strong> increases with diuretics and nephrotoxic drugs.</li><li><strong>Increased hypoglycemic effect</strong> of insulin and sulfonylureas in diabetic patients.</li><li><strong>Enhanced toxicity</strong> of ergot alkaloids and methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to tetracycline or related drugs.</li><li>Should be avoided in <strong>systemic lupus erythematosus</strong>.</li><li>Contraindicated in <strong>severe renal impairment</strong> (dose adjustment required if used).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Tetrasina 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Teeth &amp; bone effects</strong>: May cause bone growth suppression, permanent gray-brown teeth discoloration, and enamel hypoplasia in children and fetuses.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity reactions</strong>: Rare but include anaphylaxis, urticaria, and rashes.</li><li><strong>Photosensitivity</strong>: Sun-exposed areas may develop a red rash.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal effects</strong>: Epigastric distress, nausea, and vomiting (dose-related).</li><li><strong>Renal effects</strong>: Can worsen pre-existing renal failure.</li><li><strong>Superinfections</strong>: Oral and anogenital candidiasis.</li><li><strong>Esophageal ulcerations</strong>: More common when taken without sufficient water before bed.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Not recommended in pregnancy</strong> due to maternal hepatotoxicity and fetal effects (impaired bone growth, teeth discoloration).</li><li><strong>Avoid during lactation</strong>, as it may pass into breast milk and affect the infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in <strong>patients with liver impairment</strong>.</li><li><strong>Avoid high doses</strong> in liver disease.</li><li><strong>Avoid concomitant use</strong> with hepatotoxic drugs (erythromycin, chloramphenicol, isoniazid, sulfonamides).</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store between <strong>20-25°C</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1455_1742031287.jpg', 30, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 14:34:47', '2025-03-15 14:34:47'),
(3059, 'Xinarox CV', 'Cefuroxime Axetil + Clavulanic Acid', 35.00, 6, 'Tablet', '6\'s tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg+62.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xinarox CV 250 mg+62.5 mg</strong></p><p> Xinarox CV 250 mg+62.5 mg is indicated for treating infections caused by sensitive bacteria, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong> due to <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial otitis media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non beta-lactamase producing strains)</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong>, including pneumonia, caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp., Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes,</em> and <em>E. coli</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis and secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains), or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains)</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin-structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E.coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></li><li><strong>Bone and joint infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong> caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase-producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease</strong> (<em>erythema migrans</em>) caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em></li><li><strong>Septicemia</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pneumoniae, E.coli, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), and <em>Klebsiella spp</em></li><li><strong>Meningitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), <em>Neisseria meningitidis,</em> and <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Switch therapy (injectable to oral)</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime is a bactericidal second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic that effectively targets a wide range of susceptible Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including numerous beta-lactamase producing strains. It works by disrupting bacterial cell wall synthesis through interference with the transpeptidation process.</p><p>Clavulanic acid, a naturally derived beta-lactamase inhibitor produced by <em>Streptomyces clavuligerus</em>, shares a similar structure with beta-lactam antibiotics. It irreversibly binds to beta-lactamase enzymes, rendering them inactive. Clavulanic acid provides Cefuroxime with protection against degradation by beta-lactamase enzymes, offering a solution for treating bacterial infections caused by beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Xinarox CV 250 mg+62.5 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Adolescents and adults (13 years and older):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis:</strong> 250 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infections:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections:</strong> 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Gonorrhoea:</strong> 1000 mg b.i.d. Single dose</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>MDR Typhoid Fever:</strong> 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li><strong>Early Lyme disease:</strong> 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis:</strong> 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Acute otitis media:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li><strong>Impetigo:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Xinarox CV 250 mg+62.5 mg</strong></p><p> Xinarox CV 250 mg+62.5 mg tablet may be taken without regard to food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Xinarox CV 250 mg+62.5 mg</strong></p><p> When probenecid is administered concurrently with Cefuroxime-Clavulanic Acid, it significantly increases the area under the serum concentration-time curve by 50%. The use of medications that reduce gastric acidity may result in decreased bioavailability of Cefuroxime, potentially counteracting its postprandial absorption benefits.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime-Clavulanic Acid is contraindicated in patients with a known allergy to cephalosporins and in patients with <em>Pseudomembranous Colitis</em>.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xinarox CV 250 mg+62.5 mg</strong></p><p> Generally, Xinarox CV 250 mg+62.5 mg is well tolerated. However, a few side effects like nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal discomfort, or pain may occur. As with other broad-spectrum antibiotics, prolonged administration of Cefuroxime and Clavulanic acid combination may result in the overgrowth of nonsusceptible microorganisms. Rarely (&lt;0.2%), renal dysfunction, anaphylaxis, angioedema, pruritus, rash, and serum sickness-like urticaria may appear.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> While it\'s generally advisable to avoid antibiotics during the first trimester of pregnancy, Xinarox CV 250 mg+62.5 mg can be used safely in later stages to address urinary and other infections. It is excreted into breast milk in small amounts, but it\'s essential to consider the potential for sensitizing the infant.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime should be given with care to patients receiving concurrent treatment with potent diuretics and those with a history of colitis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime is a bactericidal second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic with activity against a wide range of susceptible Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including many strains that produce beta-lactamase enzymes. Cefuroxime works by disrupting the bacterial cell wall synthesis through interference with the transpeptidation process.</p><p>Clavulanic acid, a naturally derived beta-lactamase inhibitor produced by <em>Streptomyces clavuligerus</em>, shares a structural similarity with beta-lactam antibiotics. It irreversibly binds to beta-lactamase enzymes, rendering them inactive. This protective action of clavulanic acid shields Cefuroxime from degradation by beta-lactamase enzymes, making it an effective solution for treating bacterial infections caused by beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</p>', 'active', '157_1742031670.jpg', 11, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 14:41:10', '2025-03-15 14:41:10'),
(3060, 'Minirin Melt', 'Desmopressin Acetate', 114.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '120 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Minirin Melt 120 mcg</strong></p><p> Minirin Melt 120 mcg sublingual tablet is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Primary Nocturnal Enuresis</li><li>Nocturia</li><li>Central Diabetes Insipidus</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Minirin Melt 120 mcg</strong></p><p><strong>Treatment of Diabetes Insipidus:</strong></p><ul><li>Dosage is individualized but typically ranges from 120 mcg to 720 mcg daily.</li><li>Starting dose: 60 mcg three times daily sublingually.</li><li>Maintenance dose: 60-120 mcg sublingually three times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Post-hypophysectomy Polyuria/Polydipsia:</strong></p><ul><li>Dose should be controlled by measuring urine osmolality.</li></ul><p><strong>Central Diabetes Insipidus Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li>Starting dose: 0.05 mg (half of a 0.1 mg tablet) twice daily.</li><li>Usual dosage range: 0.1 mg to 1.2 mg daily, divided into two or three doses.</li><li>Monitor response by measuring urine volume and osmolality.</li></ul><p><strong>Primary Nocturnal Enuresis Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li>Limit fluid intake from 1 hour before administration until at least 8 hours after.</li><li>Starting dose: 0.2 mg orally at bedtime.</li><li>Maximum dose: 0.6 mg daily at bedtime.</li><li>Desired response is typically achieved with 0.2-0.4 mg daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Concentration Capacity Test Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li>Administer 0.6 mg (three 0.2 mg tablets) at bedtime after bladder emptying.</li><li>Discard any urine passed within one hour.</li><li>Collect a urine sample 1-12 hours after administration for osmolality measurement.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Minirin Melt 120 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>Minirin Melt 120 mcg is for sublingual use.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Minirin Melt 120 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs inducing SIADH (e.g., tricyclic antidepressants, SSRIs, chlorpromazine, carbamazepine) may increase the risk of water retention and hyponatremia.</li><li>NSAIDs may induce water retention and hyponatremia.</li><li>Loperamide may triple desmopressin plasma levels, increasing water retention risks.</li><li>Fatty meals may decrease desmopressin absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in cardiac insufficiency and conditions requiring diuretics.</li><li>Psychogenic polydipsia and alcohol abuse should be ruled out before prescribing.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Minirin Melt 120 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Headache, stomach pain, nausea.</li><li>Rare: Allergic skin reactions, severe allergic responses.</li><li>Very rare: Emotional disorders, aggression in children.</li><li>Prolonged use without fluid restriction may lead to water retention, hyponatremia, headaches, nausea, vomiting, weight gain, decreased serum sodium, and, in severe cases, convulsions.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in patients with reduced renal function or cardiovascular disease.</li><li>Less effective in chronic renal disease.</li><li>Fluid overload precautions for conditions with fluid/electrolyte imbalance and risk of increased intracranial pressure.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Minirin Melt 120 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged action increases water retention and hyponatremia risk.</li><li>Treatment: Discontinue desmopressin, restrict fluid intake, provide symptomatic management.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in the original package to protect from moisture and light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Synthetic analogue of ADH.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Desmopressin acts as a selective V2 receptor agonist in the renal collecting duct, increasing water reabsorption and reducing urine production.</li><li>It is more potent than ADH in increasing plasma levels of factor VIII activity in hemophilia and von Willebrand’s disease.</li><li>Desmopressin has minimal pressor activity.</li><li>Intranasal administration has about one-tenth the antidiuretic effect of an equivalent injected dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Limited data suggests rare malformations in infants exposed to desmopressin. Animal studies show no direct harm. Blood pressure monitoring is recommended due to pre-eclampsia risk.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Minimal transfer to breast milk, unlikely to affect infant diuresis.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '457_1742055499.jpg', 10, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 21:18:19', '2025-03-15 21:18:19'),
(3061, 'Palbocent', 'Palbocent', 400.00, 7, 'Capsule', '7 capsule in a strip', 'Tablet', '125 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Palbocent 125 mg</strong></p><p> Palbocent 125 mg is a kinase inhibitor indicated for the treatment of hormone receptor (HR)-positive, human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2)-negative advanced or metastatic breast cancer in combination with:</p><ul><li>An aromatase inhibitor as initial endocrine-based therapy in postmenopausal women</li><li>Fulvestrant in women with disease progression following endocrine therapy</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Targeted Cancer Therapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Treatment of breast cancer cell lines with the combination of Palbocent 125 mg and antiestrogens leads to decreased retinoblastoma protein (Rb) phosphorylation, resulting in reduced E2F expression and signaling and increased growth arrest compared to treatment with each drug alone. In vitro treatment of ER-positive breast cancer cell lines with the combination of Palbocent 125 mg and antiestrogens leads to increased cell senescence, which was sustained for up to 6 days following drug removal.</p><p>In vivo studies using a patient-derived ER-positive breast cancer xenograft model demonstrated that the combination of Palbocent 125 mg and letrozole increased the inhibition of Rb phosphorylation, downstream signaling, and tumor growth compared to each drug alone. Palbocent 125 mg is an oral, reversible, selective, small-molecule inhibitor of CDK4 and CDK6. CDK4 and CDK6, along with their regulatory partner cyclin D1, play a key role in regulating the G1- to S-phase cell-cycle transition via regulation of phosphorylation of the retinoblastoma (Rb) protein.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Palbocent 125 mg</strong></p><p> Palbocent 125 mg capsules are taken orally with food in combination with an aromatase inhibitor or fulvestrant.</p><ul><li><strong>Recommended starting dose:</strong> 125 mg once daily taken with food for 21 days followed by 7 days off treatment.</li><li>Dosing interruption and/or dose reductions are recommended based on individual safety and tolerability.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Palbocent 125 mg</strong></p><p> The recommended dose of Palbocent 125 mg is a 125 mg capsule taken orally once daily for 21 consecutive days followed by 7 days off treatment to complete a 28-day cycle. Palbocent 125 mg should be taken with food. Patients should be encouraged to take their dose of Palbocent 125 mg at approximately the same time each day.</p><p>For men treated with combination Palbocent 125 mg plus aromatase inhibitor therapy, consider treatment with an LHRH agonist according to current clinical practice standards.</p><p><strong>Recommended Dose Modification for Adverse Reactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Recommended Starting Dose:</strong> 125 mg/day</li><li><strong>First Dose Reduction:</strong> 100 mg/day</li><li><strong>Second Dose Reduction:</strong> 75 mg/day</li><li><strong>If further dose reduction below 75 mg/day is required, discontinue treatment.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Use in Children:</strong></p><p> Palbocent 125 mg is not indicated for use in children.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Palbocent 125 mg</strong></p><p> Palbocent 125 mg is primarily metabolized by CYP3A and Sulfotransferase (SULT) enzyme SULT2A1. It is a time-dependent inhibitor of CYP3A.</p><ul><li><strong>Agents That May Increase Palbocent 125 mg Plasma Concentrations:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Effect of CYP3A Inhibitors:</strong> Coadministration of a strong CYP3A inhibitor (Itraconazole) increased the plasma exposure of Palbocent 125 mg in patients by 87%.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Avoid concomitant use of strong CYP3A inhibitors (e.g., Clarithromycin, Indinavir, Itraconazole, Ketoconazole, Lopinavir/Ritonavir, Nefazodone, Nelfinavir, Posaconazole, Ritonavir, Saquinavir, Telaprevir, Telithromycin, and Voriconazole).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Avoid grapefruit or grapefruit juice during Palbocent 125 mg treatment.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If coadministration with a strong CYP3A inhibitor cannot be avoided, reduce the dose.</li><li><strong>Agents That May Decrease Palbocent 125 mg Plasma Concentrations:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Effect of CYP3A Inducers:</strong> Coadministration of a strong CYP3A inducer (Rifampin) decreased the plasma exposure of Palbocent 125 mg in healthy subjects by 85%.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Avoid concomitant use of strong CYP3A inducers (e.g., Phenytoin, Rifampin, Carbamazepine, Enzalutamide, and St John’s Wort).</li><li><strong>Drugs That May Have Their Plasma Concentrations Altered By Palbocent 125 mg:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Coadministration of Midazolam with multiple doses of Palbocent 125 mg increased Midazolam plasma exposure by 61%.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dose of sensitive CYP3A substrates with a narrow therapeutic index (e.g., Alfentanil, Cyclosporine, Dihydroergotamine, Ergotamine, Everolimus, Fentanyl, Pimozide, Quinidine, Sirolimus, and Tacrolimus) may need to be reduced.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> None</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Palbocent 125 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Decreased WBC, neutrophils, and platelets</li><li>Neutropenia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, anemia</li><li>Infections</li><li>Elevated AST and ALT levels</li><li>Fatigue, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea</li><li>Rash, dry skin</li><li>Hair loss</li><li>Inflammation of the mouth and lips</li><li>Weakness, lethargy</li><li>Fever</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Advise patients not to breastfeed during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neutropenia:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Neutropenia was the most frequently reported adverse reaction.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Monitor complete blood counts before starting treatment, at the beginning of each cycle, on day 15 of the first two cycles, and as clinically indicated.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dose interruption, dose reduction, or treatment delay is recommended for grade 3 or 4 neutropenia.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Febrile neutropenia has been reported in 1.8% of patients.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">One death due to neutropenic sepsis was observed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Physicians should inform patients to report any fever episodes promptly.</li><li><strong>Embryo-Fetal Toxicity:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Palbocent 125 mg can cause fetal harm when administered to a pregnant woman.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Advise pregnant women of the potential risk to a fetus.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Advise females of reproductive potential to use effective contraception during treatment and for at least 3 weeks after the last dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Palbocent 125 mg</strong></p><p> There is no known antidote for Palbocent 125 mg. The treatment of overdose should consist of general supportive measures.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C temperature.</li><li>Store away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Palbocent 125 mg is an inhibitor of Cyclin-dependent kinases (CDK) 4 and 6. Cyclin D1 and CDK4/6 are downstream of signaling pathways that lead to cellular proliferation. Palbocent 125 mg reduces cellular proliferation of estrogen receptor (ER)-positive breast cancer cell lines by blocking progression from the G1 phase to the S phase of the cell cycle.</p><p>Treatment of breast cancer cell lines with Palbocent 125 mg and antiestrogens leads to decreased retinoblastoma (RB) protein phosphorylation, reduced E2F expression, and increased growth arrest. In vivo studies using a patient-derived ER-positive breast cancer xenograft model demonstrated that the combination of Palbocent 125 mg and letrozole enhanced inhibition of RB phosphorylation, downstream signaling, and tumor growth compared to each drug alone.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Palbocent 125 mg can cause fetal harm when administered to a pregnant woman. Advise pregnant women of the potential risk to a fetus. The risk of major birth defects and miscarriage in clinically recognized pregnancies is <strong>2%-4% and 15%-20%</strong>, respectively.</p>', 'active', '1747_1742055775.jpg', 2, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 21:22:55', '2025-03-15 21:22:55'),
(3062, 'Bumecard', 'Bumetanide', 18.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Bumecard 5 mg</strong></p><p> Bumecard 5 mg is indicated for the treatment of edema associated with:</p><ul><li>Congestive heart failure</li><li>Hepatic ascites</li><li>Renal disease, including nephrotic syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Loop diuretics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Bumecard 5 mg is a loop diuretic of the sulfamyl category used to treat heart failure. It is often used in patients in whom high doses of furosemide are ineffective, though it can be used as a first-choice drug. The main difference between Bumecard and furosemide is its bioavailability, making it a more predictable diuretic. Bumecard 5 mg is 40 times more potent than furosemide (for patients with normal renal function). It interferes with renal cAMP and/or inhibits the sodium-potassium ATPase pump. It appears to block the active reabsorption of chloride and possibly sodium in the ascending loop of Henle, leading to diuresis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Bumecard 5 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Oral:</strong></p><ul><li>1 mg in the morning, repeated after 6-8 hours if necessary</li><li>In severe cases, 5 mg daily increased by 5 mg every 12-24 hours based on response</li><li>Elderly: 500 micrograms daily may be sufficient</li></ul><p><strong>Parenteral:</strong></p><p> <strong>By IV Injection:</strong></p><ul><li>1-2 mg, repeated after 20 minutes if necessary</li><li>Elderly: 500 micrograms (1 ml) daily may be sufficient</li></ul><p><strong>By IV Infusion:</strong></p><ul><li>2-5 mg over 30-60 minutes</li><li>Elderly: 500 micrograms (1 ml) daily may be sufficient</li></ul><p><strong>By IM Injection:</strong></p><ul><li>1 mg initially, then adjusted according to response</li><li>Elderly: 500 micrograms (1 ml) daily may be sufficient</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Bumecard 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant use may potentiate the effects of antihypertensive drugs.</li><li>It increases potassium excretion, which can heighten sensitivity to digitalis toxicity.</li><li>It may elevate blood uric acid levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Loop diuretics should be avoided in patients with:</p><ul><li>Severe hypokalemia</li><li>Severe hyponatremia</li><li>Anuria</li><li>Comatose and pre-comatose states related to liver cirrhosis</li><li>Renal failure</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Bumecard 5 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache, dizziness, fatigue, postural hypotension</li><li>Gastrointestinal symptoms</li><li>Skin reactions, photosensitivity reactions</li><li>Metabolic disturbances (including reduced glucose tolerance)</li><li>Electrolyte disturbances, especially with long-term use</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category C. No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Unknown if excreted in human milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor serum potassium levels periodically.</li><li>Potassium supplements or potassium-sparing diuretics may be needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Bumecard 5 mg</strong></p><p> Symptoms result from excessive diuresis. Treatment includes gastric lavage or emesis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place. Protect from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> <strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in patients below 18 years have not been established.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Bumecard 5 mg is a loop diuretic that blocks chloride and possibly sodium reabsorption in the ascending loop of Henle, leading to diuresis. It is considered more predictable in absorption and effect compared to furosemide and is 40 times more potent.</p>', 'active', '84_1742056016.jpg', 2, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 21:26:56', '2025-03-15 21:26:56'),
(3063, 'Depo-Medrol', 'Methylprednisolone Acetate', 388.00, 1, 'Unit', '80 mg vial', 'Injection', '80 mg/2 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Depo Medrol 80 mg/2 ml</strong></p><p>Depo Medrol 80 mg/2 ml is indicated in the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Asthma</li><li>Atopic dermatitis</li><li>Contact dermatitis</li><li>Bullous dermatitis herpetiformis</li><li>Mycosis fungoides</li><li>Pemphigus</li><li>Severe erythema multiforme</li><li>Primary or secondary adrenocortical insufficiency</li><li>Hypercalcemia associated with cancer</li><li>Nonsuppurative thyroiditis</li><li>Ulcerative colitis</li><li>Hematologic disorders</li><li>Palliative management of leukemias and lymphomas</li><li>Sympathetic ophthalmia</li><li>Keratitis</li><li>Allergic conjunctivitis</li><li>Juvenile rheumatoid arthritis</li><li>Ankylosing spondylitis</li><li>Symptomatic sarcoidosis</li><li>Aspiration pneumonitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Glucocorticoids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Methylprednisolone binds to and activates intracellular glucocorticoid receptors. Activated glucocorticoid receptors bind to promoter regions of DNA (which may activate or suppress transcription). Unbound glucocorticoids cross cell membranes and bind with high affinity to specific cytoplasmic receptors, modifying transcription and protein synthesis. By this mechanism, glucocorticoids can:</p><ul><li>Inhibit leukocyte infiltration at the site of inflammation</li><li>Interfere with mediators of inflammatory response</li><li>Suppress humoral immune responses</li><li>The anti-inflammatory actions of corticosteroids are thought to involve phospholipase A2 inhibitory proteins, lipocortins, which control the biosynthesis of potent mediators of inflammation such as prostaglandins and leukotrienes.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Depo Medrol 80 mg/2 ml</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Administration for Local Effect</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Rheumatoid and Osteoarthritis:</strong>Large joints (Knees, Ankles, Shoulders): 20 to 80 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Medium joints (Elbows, Wrists): 10 to 40 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Small joints (Metacarpophalangeal, Interphalangeal, Sternoclavicular, Acromioclavicular): 4 to 10 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Miscellaneous (Ganglion, Tendinitis, Epicondylitis):</strong> 4 to 30 mg depending on condition</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Dermatological Conditions:</strong>20 to 60 mg injected into the lesion</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Large lesions may require doses distributed as 20 to 40 mg with repeated local injections</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Administration for Systemic Effect</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adrenogenital Syndrome:</strong> 40 mg intramuscularly every two weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Rheumatoid Arthritis:</strong> 40 to 120 mg intramuscularly weekly</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dermatologic Lesions:</strong> 40 to 120 mg intramuscularly weekly for 1-4 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute Severe Dermatitis (Poison Ivy):</strong> 80 to 120 mg intramuscularly, relief within 8-12 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Chronic Contact Dermatitis:</strong> Repeated injections at 5 to 10-day intervals may be needed</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Seborrheic Dermatitis:</strong> 80 mg intramuscularly weekly</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Asthma:</strong> 80 to 120 mg intramuscularly, relief within 6-48 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Allergic Rhinitis (Hay Fever):</strong> 80 to 120 mg intramuscularly, relief within six hours</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Multiple Sclerosis Treatment</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute Exacerbations:</strong> 160 mg daily for a week, followed by 64 mg every other day for a month</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">(4 mg of methylprednisolone is equivalent to 5 mg of prednisolone)</li></ul><p>Or as directed by the registered physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Depo Medrol 80 mg/2 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Convulsions have been reported with concurrent use of methylprednisolone and cyclosporine.</li><li>Hepatic enzyme inducers (phenobarbital, phenytoin, rifampicin) may increase methylprednisolone clearance.</li><li>Troleandomycin and ketoconazole may inhibit methylprednisolone metabolism, decreasing its clearance.</li><li>Methylprednisolone may increase clearance of chronic high-dose aspirin.</li><li>Variable effects on anticoagulant therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Intrathecal administration</li><li>Systemic fungal infections</li><li>Hypersensitivity to the product or its constituents</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Depo Medrol 80 mg/2 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Congestive heart failure (in susceptible patients)</li><li>Hypertension</li><li>Sodium retention</li><li>Muscle weakness</li><li>Osteoporosis</li><li>Peptic ulcer (with possible hemorrhage)</li><li>Abdominal distention</li><li>Increased sweating</li><li>Convulsion</li><li>Vertigo</li><li>Headache</li><li>Menstrual irregularities</li><li>Adrenocortical and pituitary unresponsiveness</li><li>Increased intraocular pressure, glaucoma</li><li>Allergic or hypersensitivity reactions (urticaria, hyperpigmentation, or hypopigmentation)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong></li><li>Adequate human reproduction studies have not been done</li><li>Use in pregnancy, nursing mothers, or women of childbearing potential requires weighing benefits against potential risks</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Corticosteroids should be used cautiously in patients with:</p><ul><li>Ocular herpes simplex</li><li>Nonspecific ulcerative colitis</li><li>Renal insufficiency</li><li>Hypertension</li><li>Osteoporosis</li><li>Myasthenia gravis</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store between 20-25°C.</p>', 'active', '387_1742056618.jpg', 65, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 21:36:58', '2025-03-24 17:15:16');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3064, 'Neoclomide', 'Cyclophosphamide', 400.00, 1, 'x 500 mg vial', '500 mg vial', 'IV Infusion', '500 mg/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Neoclomide 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Malignant Diseases: Neoclomide 500 mg/vial is indicated for the treatment of: Malignant lymphomas (Stages III and IV of the Ann Arbor staging system), Hodgkin\'s disease, lymphocytic lymphoma (nodular or diffuse), mixed-cell type lymphoma, histiocytic lymphoma, Burkitt\'s lymphoma, multiple myeloma.</p><p>Leukemias: Chronic lymphocytic leukemia, chronic granulocytic leukemia (it is usually ineffective in acute blastic crisis), acute myelogenous and monocytic leukemia, acute lymphoblastic (stem-cell) leukemia (Neoclomide 500 mg/vial given during remission is effective in prolonging its duration).</p><p>Mycosis fungoides (advanced disease), neuroblastoma (disseminated disease), adenocarcinoma of the ovary, retinoblastoma, carcinoma of the breast. Neoclomide 500 mg/vial, although effective alone in susceptible malignancies, is more frequently used concurrently or sequentially with other antineoplastic drugs.</p><p>Minimal Change Nephrotic Syndrome in Pediatric Patients: Neoclomide 500 mg/vial is indicated for the treatment of biopsy-proven minimal change nephrotic syndrome in pediatric patients who failed to adequately respond to or are unable to tolerate adrenocorticosteroid therapy.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Neoclomide 500 mg/vial is a prodrug that is converted in the body to the active metabolites. It acts at any stage of the cell cycle. It prevents cell division by cross-linking deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) strands and reducing DNA synthesis. It also exerts a potent immunosuppressive effect.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Neoclomide 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p><strong>Intravenous (Adult) - Malignancies:</strong></p><ul><li>Low dose regimen: 2-6 mg/kg weekly as a single dose</li><li>Moderate dose regimen: 10-15 mg/kg weekly as a single dose</li><li>High dose regimen: 20-40 mg/kg as a single dose every 10-20 days</li><li>Alternatively, 80-300 mg/m² daily as a single dose, or 300-600 mg/m² weekly as a single dose, or 600-1,500 mg/m² as a single dose or short infusion at 10- to 20-day intervals.</li></ul><p><strong>Oral (Child) - Nephrotic syndrome:</strong></p><ul><li>2 mg/kg daily for 8-12 weeks</li><li>Maximum cumulative dose: 168 mg/kg</li><li>Maximum duration: 90 days</li></ul><p><strong>Oral (Adult) - Malignancies:</strong></p><ul><li>Low dose regimen: 2-6 mg/kg weekly in divided doses</li><li>Alternatively, 100-300 mg daily in divided doses, or 50-250 mg/m² daily or 80-300 mg/m² daily in divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Neoclomide 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Should be taken on an empty stomach. Preferably taken on an empty stomach, but may be taken with meals to minimize GI irritation. Ensure adequate fluid intake. Swallow whole.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Neoclomide 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Increased risk of cardiotoxicity with doxorubicin or other cardiotoxic drugs. May increase the incidence of mucositis with protease inhibitors. May increase hematotoxicity and/or immunosuppression with ACE inhibitors, natalizumab, paclitaxel, thiazide diuretics, zidovudine.</p><p>May increase pulmonary toxicity with amiodarone. May increase nephrotoxicity with amphotericin B. May result in acute water intoxication with indomethacin. May increase the risk of hepatotoxicity with azathioprine.</p><p>May increase the incidence of hepatic veno-occlusive disease and mucositis with busulfan. May increase the risk of hemorrhagic cystitis with previous or concomitant radiotherapy. May result in acute encephalopathy with metronidazole.</p><p>May increase the risk of thromboembolic complications. May alter the effect of warfarin. May increase the immunosuppressive effect of ciclosporin. May result in prolonged apnea with depolarizing muscle relaxants (e.g., suxamethonium).</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Patients with bone marrow aplasia, urinary outflow obstruction, UTI, acute infection, drug- or radiation-induced urothelial toxicity. Pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Neoclomide 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Alopecia, skin and nails hyperpigmentation, nausea and vomiting, mucositis, inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion, carbohydrate metabolism disturbances, gonadal suppression, interstitial pulmonary fibrosis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p><strong>Pregnancy Category D:</strong> There is positive evidence of human fetal risk, but the benefits from use in pregnant women may be acceptable despite the risk (e.g., if the drug is needed in a life-threatening situation or for a serious disease for which safer drugs cannot be used or are ineffective).</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Patients with diabetes mellitus, severe immunosuppression, acute porphyria, pre-existing cardiovascular disease, or those at risk for cardiotoxicity. Renal and hepatic impairment. Lactation.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Neoclomide 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p><strong>Symptoms:</strong> Urotoxicity, myelosuppression, cardiotoxicity (including cardiac failure), stomatitis, veno-occlusive hepatic disease.</p><p><strong>Management:</strong> Supportive treatment. May consider hemodialysis. Cystitis prophylaxis with mesna may be useful for urotoxicity.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at or below 25°C.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p>Reconstitute with 25 mL for a 500 mg vial, 50 mL for a 1,000 mg vial, or 100 mL for a 2,000 mg vial to a concentration of 20 mg/mL using NaCl 0.9% for direct IV push, or NaCl 0.9% or sterile water for injection for IV infusion. Gently swirl to mix.</p><p>For IV infusion, dilute further with dextrose 5% in water, NaCl 0.45%, or dextrose 5% and NaCl 0.9% injection to a minimum concentration of 2 mg/mL.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Neoclomide 500 mg/vial is a prodrug that is converted in the body to the active metabolites. It acts at any stage of the cell cycle. It prevents cell division by cross-linking deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) strands and reducing DNA synthesis. It also exerts a potent immunosuppressive effect.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p><strong>Pregnancy Category D:</strong> There is positive evidence of human fetal risk, but the benefits from use in pregnant women may be acceptable despite the risk (e.g., if the drug is needed in a life-threatening situation or for a serious disease for which safer drugs cannot be used or are ineffective).</p>', 'active', '1702_1742056868.jpg', 1, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 21:41:08', '2025-05-05 08:22:19'),
(3065, 'Carbiroid', 'Carbimazole', 4.80, 20, 'Tablet', '20 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Carbiroid 5 mg</strong></p><p> Carbiroid 5 mg is indicated in the management of hyperthyroidism, thyrotoxicosis (including thyroid storm), and also for the preparation of patients for thyroidectomy. Carbiroid 5 mg can also be used in combination with radio-active ablative therapy.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-thyroid drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Carbiroid 5 mg is metabolized to thiamazole, which is responsible for its antithyroid action. It blocks the production of thyroid hormones through inhibition of the organification of iodide and the coupling of iodothyronine residues.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Carbiroid 5 mg</strong></p><p> 10 mg to 60 mg daily according to the severity of the disorder. The dose should be gradually reduced to the smallest amount that will control the disease. The daily dosage should be divided.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Carbiroid 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial dose:</strong> 20-60 mg in 2-3 divided doses until the patient is euthyroid.</li><li><strong>Maintenance regimen:</strong> The dose is gradually reduced to maintain a euthyroid state. The final dosage is usually in the range of 5-15 mg/day, which may be taken as a single daily dose.</li><li><strong>Neonates &amp; children below 12 years:</strong> The usual initial dose is 250 mcg/kg/day in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Duration of treatment:</strong> 18 to 24 months.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Carbiroid 5 mg</strong></p><p> Carbiroid 5 mg may interact adversely with other medicines. Iodine or iodine excess may decrease the response to Carbiroid 5 mg, requiring an increase in dosage or longer therapy duration. As thyroid and metabolic status decrease toward normal, response to oral anticoagulants may decrease. However, if thioamide-induced hypoprothrombinemia occurs, anticoagulant effects may be enhanced. Adjustment of oral anticoagulant dosage based on prothrombin time is recommended. Serum concentrations of digoxin and digitoxin may increase as thyroid function normalizes, necessitating a dosage reduction of digitalis glycosides.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to Carbiroid 5 mg or other thiourea antithyroid agents.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Carbiroid 5 mg</strong></p><p> Side effects may include rash, pruritus, skin pigmentation, paresthesias, urticaria, headache, arthralgia, and gastrointestinal disturbances (including nausea, vomiting, and gastric discomfort), as well as abnormal hair loss. Drug fever, a lupus-like syndrome, vasculitis, nephritis, hepatic disorders (most commonly jaundice), and taste disturbances have been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> <strong>Pregnancy Category D:</strong> There is positive evidence of human fetal risk, but the benefits of using the drug in pregnant women may be acceptable despite the risk.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Carbiroid 5 mg should be given with the utmost caution, or not at all, if there is any degree of tracheal obstruction, as high dosages may produce thyroid enlargement and obstructive symptoms may become severe.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Carbiroid 5 mg</strong></p><p> Overdose or accidental poisoning may result in hypothyroidism and goiter. If blood dyscrasias occur, the drug should be immediately withdrawn. Further treatment is symptomatic and supportive.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 25°C.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Anti-thyroid drugs</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Carbiroid 5 mg is an anti-thyroid substance that depresses the formation of thyroid hormones. It reduces the uptake and concentration of inorganic iodine by the thyroid but mainly reduces the formation of di-iodotyrosine and thyroxine. Carbiroid 5 mg is rapidly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and is widely distributed throughout the body. It is completely metabolized to methimazole, which is responsible for its clinical activity. Carbiroid 5 mg readily crosses the placental barrier and is also present in high concentrations in the milk of lactating patients.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Carbiroid 5 mg may be given during pregnancy to a thyrotoxic patient, but the smallest effective dose should be used, as overdose adversely affects the fetus. Carbiroid 5 mg crosses the placenta and is excreted into breast milk, potentially causing fetal or neonatal hypothyroidism and goiter.</p>', 'active', '1068_1742058023.jpg', 59, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 22:00:23', '2025-03-15 22:00:23'),
(3066, 'Ceptiva', 'Ethinyl Estradiol + Desogestrel', 105.00, 1, 'Strip', '21 tablet pack', 'Tablet', '0.03 mg+0.15 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ceptiva 30 mcg+150 mcg</strong></p><p> Desogestrel &amp; Ethinylestradiol is indicated in the prevention of pregnancy or oral contraception.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Oral contraceptive preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Desogestrel is a progestogen structurally related to levonorgestrel, while ethinylestradiol is a synthetic estrogen. Used together, they inhibit ovulation by a negative feedback mechanism on the hypothalamus, altering the normal FSH and LH secretion pattern by the anterior pituitary. It inhibits the follicular phase FSH and midcycle surge of gonadotropins. It also causes changes in the cervical mucus, making it unsuitable for sperm penetration. Additionally, it may alter tubal transport of the ova through the fallopian tubes. Progestational agents may also alter sperm fertility.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ceptiva 30 mcg+150 mcg</strong></p><p> Dosing instructions may vary according to the brand.</p><ul><li><strong>For 21-day pack:</strong> Start dose on the 1st day of the menstrual cycle. Take 1 tablet daily for 21 days, followed by 7 pill-free days. Start a new pack on the 8th day after the last tablet is taken.</li><li><strong>For 28-day pack:</strong> Start dose on the 1st day of the menstrual cycle. Take 1 tablet daily without interruption. The dose should be taken at the same time every day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ceptiva 30 mcg+150 mcg</strong></p><p> Serum levels may be increased when used with paracetamol, ascorbic acid, and atorvastatin. Serum levels may be reduced by aprepitant, griseofulvin, modafinil, troglitazone, rifampicin, topiramate, nevirapine, amprenavir, lopinavir, nelfinavir, and ritonavir. May affect the efficacy of coumarin derivatives.</p><ul><li>Concurrent use with aminoglutethimide, carbamazepine, felbamate, phenobarbital, phenytoin, or topiramate may decrease contraceptive effectiveness.</li><li>May reduce the clearance of alprazolam, chlordiazepoxide, and diazepam.</li><li>May increase the clearance of lorazepam, oxazepam, temazepam, clofibric acid, morphine, and salicylic acid.</li><li>May inhibit the metabolism of theophylline, ciclosporin, and prednisolone.</li><li>May decrease serum levels of lamotrigine.</li><li>May increase serum levels of selegiline and TCAs, e.g., amitriptyline, imipramine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> History of or current thrombophlebitis or venous thromboembolic disorders; active or recent (within 1 year) arterial thromboembolic disease, e.g., stroke, MI; cerebral vascular disease, coronary artery disease, valvular heart disease with complications. Severe hypertension; diabetes mellitus with vascular involvement; severe headache with focal neurological symptoms. Known or suspected breast carcinoma, endometrial cancer, estrogen-dependent neoplasms. Undiagnosed abnormal genital bleeding; hepatic dysfunction or tumor. Cholestatic jaundice of pregnancy, jaundice with prior combined hormonal contraceptive use. Major surgery with prolonged immobilization. Pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ceptiva 30 mcg+150 mcg</strong></p><p> Intermenstrual bleeding, post-medication amenorrhea, changes in cervical secretion, increase in size of uterine fibromyomata, aggravation of endometriosis, and certain vaginal infections. Breast tenderness, pain, enlargement, or secretion. Nausea, vomiting, cholelithiasis, cholestatic jaundice. Headache, migraine, mood changes, depression. Fluid retention, change in body weight, reduced glucose tolerance.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> <strong>Category X:</strong> Studies in animals or humans have demonstrated fetal abnormalities, or there is evidence of fetal risk based on human experience or both, and the risk of using the drug in pregnant women clearly outweighs any possible benefit. The drug is contraindicated in women who are or may become pregnant.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> May increase the risk of breast cancer, glucose intolerance, and thromboembolism. May affect serum levels of triglyceride and lipoprotein. Caution is required in patients with familial defects of lipoprotein metabolism. Discontinue treatment if papilledema or retinal vascular lesions are observed on eye examination. Caution in patients with risk factors for coronary artery disease, history of depression, or migraine. Renal impairment. Increased cardiovascular risk in smoking women, especially those over 35 years. Treatment should be stopped for 4 weeks before and 2 weeks after surgery associated with increased risk of thromboembolism or during periods of prolonged immobilization.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ceptiva 30 mcg+150 mcg</strong></p><p> Treatment is symptomatic.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at 25°C.</p>', 'active', '428_1742058396.jpg', 44, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 22:06:36', '2025-03-15 22:06:36'),
(3067, 'Novirax', 'Acyclovir', 22.10, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Novirax 400 mg</strong></p><p> Novirax 400 mg cream is a herpes simplex virus (HSV) nucleoside analogue DNA polymerase inhibitor indicated for the treatment of recurrent herpes labialis (cold sores) in immunocompetent adults and adolescents 12 years of age and older.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Topical Antiviral preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Novirax 400 mg is an antiviral drug active against herpes simplex virus. Novirax 400 mg is a synthetic purine nucleoside analogue with cell culture and in vivo inhibitory activity against HSV types 1 (HSV-1) and 2 (HSV-2). The inhibitory activity of Novirax 400 mg is highly selective due to its affinity for the enzyme thymidine kinase (TK) encoded by HSV. This viral enzyme converts Novirax 400 mg into Novirax 400 mg monophosphate, a nucleotide analogue. The monophosphate is further converted into diphosphate by cellular guanylate kinase and into triphosphate by a number of cellular enzymes. In cell culture, Novirax 400 mg triphosphate stops replication of herpes viral DNA. This inhibition is accomplished in three ways:</p><ol><li>Competitive inhibition of viral DNA polymerase</li><li>Incorporation into and termination of the growing viral DNA chain</li><li>Inactivation of the viral DNA polymerase</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Novirax 400 mg</strong></p><p> Novirax 400 mg cream should be applied five times per day for four days. Therapy should be initiated as early as possible following the onset of signs or symptoms of herpes labialis, i.e., during the prodrome or when lesions appear. For adolescents 12 years of age and older, the dosage is the same as in adults.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Novirax 400 mg</strong></p><p> Clinical experience has identified no interactions resulting from topical or systemic administration of other drugs concomitantly with Novirax 400 mg cream. Due to minimal systemic absorption of Novirax 400 mg cream, systemic drug interactions are unlikely.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Novirax 400 mg cream is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Novirax 400 mg, valNovirax 400 mg, or any component of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Novirax 400 mg</strong></p><p> The most common adverse reactions at the site of topical application were dry lips, desquamation, dryness of skin, cracked lips, burning skin, pruritus, flakiness of skin, and stinging on skin; each adverse reaction occurred in less than 1% of patients receiving Novirax 400 mg cream and placebo. Three patients on Novirax 400 mg cream and one patient on placebo discontinued treatment due to an adverse event.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies of Novirax 400 mg cream in pregnant women. Novirax 400 mg cream should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether topically applied Novirax 400 mg is excreted in breast milk. Systemic exposure following topical administration is minimal.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Novirax 400 mg cream should only be applied on the affected external aspects of the lips and face in patients with herpes labialis. Because no data are available, application to human mucous membranes is not recommended. Novirax 400 mg cream is intended for cutaneous use only and should not be used in the eye or inside the mouth or nose. Cream has a potential for irritation and contact. The effect of Novirax 400 mg cream has not been established in immunocompromised patients.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store Novirax 400 mg cream at room temperature between 20°C to 25°C.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> <strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> An open-label, uncontrolled trial with Novirax 400 mg cream 5% was conducted in 113 patients aged 12 to 17 years with recurrent herpes labialis. In this trial, therapy was applied using the same dosing regimen as in adults and subjects were followed for adverse events. The safety profile was similar to that observed in adults. Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients less than 12 years of age have not been established.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Clinical studies of Novirax 400 mg cream did not include sufficient numbers of subjects aged 65 and over to determine whether they respond differently from younger subjects. Other reported clinical experience has not identified differences in responses between the elderly and younger patients. Systemic absorption of Novirax 400 mg after topical administration is minimal.</p>', 'active', '182_1742059363.jpg', 9, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 22:22:43', '2025-03-15 22:22:43'),
(3068, 'Eye Relief', 'Irrigating Solution', 250.00, 1, 'Pot', '118 ml drop', 'Drop', '99.05 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Eye Relief 99.05%</strong></p><p> Eye Relief removes loose foreign material, air pollutants (smog or pollen), and chlorinated water for washing the eye to relieve irritation, stinging, discomfort, itching, and burning.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p> <strong>Active Ingredient:</strong> Purified Water INN (99.05%)</p><p> <strong>Inactive Ingredients:</strong> Boric Acid, Borax, Sodium Chloride</p><p> <strong>Preservative:</strong> Disodium Edetate and Sorbic Acid</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Other ophthalmic preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Eye Relief 99.05% for eye wash can be used for daily or emergency eye cleansing. Boric Acid has mild antibiotic properties against fungal or bacterial infections. Boric Acid is used in eye wash to cleanse or irrigate the eyes. It provides soothing relief from eye irritation and helps remove pollutants from the eye, such as smog, chlorine, or other chemicals.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Eye Relief 99.05%</strong></p><p> Instill in the affected eyes as needed or as prescribed by a physician.</p><p><strong>Using without Eye Cup:</strong></p><ul><li>Flush the affected eye(s) as needed.</li><li>Control the rate of flow of the solution by pressure on the bottle.</li><li>Do not touch the dropper tip to the eye.</li></ul><p><strong>Using with Eye Cup:</strong></p><ul><li>Fill the cup up to the 5 ml round mark with Eye Relief eye wash solution and apply the cup to the affected eye(s), pressing tightly to prevent leakage.</li><li>Tilt the head backward, open eyelids wide, and rotate the eyeball to wash the eye thoroughly.</li><li>Rinse the cup with clean water after each use.</li><li>Close the cap after use.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> No data available.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Safe use during pregnancy and lactation has not been established. If pregnant or breastfeeding, ask your doctor before use.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Do not use if you have open wounds in or near the eyes; seek medical help immediately.</li><li>Do not use if the solution changes color or becomes cloudy.</li><li>Remove contact lenses before using.</li><li>Do not touch the tip of the container to any surface to avoid contamination.</li><li>Replace the cap after use.</li><li>Stop use and consult a doctor if you experience eye pain, changes in vision, continued redness or irritation, or if the condition worsens or persists.</li><li>If swallowed, seek medical help or contact a Poison Control Center immediately.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at 15°C to 30°C in a dry place protected from light. The contents should not be used more than one month after first opening the bottle. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Other ophthalmic preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Eye Relief 99.05% for eye wash can be used for daily or emergency eye cleansing. Boric Acid has mild antibiotic properties against fungal or bacterial infections. Boric Acid is used in eye wash to cleanse or irrigate the eyes. It provides soothing relief from eye irritation and helps remove pollutants from the eye, such as smog, chlorine, or other chemicals.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Safe use during pregnancy and lactation has not been established. If pregnant or breastfeeding, ask your doctor before use.</p>', 'active', '61_1742059789.jpg', 17, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 22:29:49', '2025-03-15 22:29:49'),
(3069, 'Mirotiv', 'Mirogabalin Besylate', 35.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Mirotiv tablet is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Neuropathic Pain</li><li>Diabetic peripheral neuropathic pain (DPNP)</li><li>Postherpetic neuralgia (PHN)</li><li>Peripheral neuropathic pain (PNP)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Mirogabalin belongs to the class of gamma amino acids and its derivatives. Mirogabalin selectively binds α2δ subunits of voltage-gated calcium channels (VGCCs). It reduces calcium (Ca2+) influx and neurotransmission, which inhibits neurotransmitter release in presynaptic neuron endings. Due to the inhibition of neurotransmitter release, the hyper-excitability of central nervous system (CNS) neurons decreases.</p><p>Mirogabalin is rapidly absorbed after oral administration; median time to maximum plasma concentration is 0.5-1.5 hours. It has a low plasma protein binding of approximately 25% and a mean apparent volume of distribution of 64-88L after single or multiple doses. The drug is cleared mainly unchanged (61-72%) via renal excretion by filtration and active secretion; however, a slight fraction (13-20%) is metabolized by hepatic uridine 5\'-diphospho-glucuronosyltransferase isoforms. The mean elimination half-life of mirogabalin is observed to be 2-4.9 hours. 99% of mirogabalin is excreted through the kidneys, while only 1% of the dose is excreted through feces.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Adult dose:</strong></p><p> The initial dose for adults is 5 mg of Mirogabalin twice daily. Then the dose is gradually increased by 5 mg at an interval of at least a week to 15 mg twice daily. The dose may be adjusted appropriately between 10 mg and 15 mg twice daily depending on ages and symptoms.</p><p><strong>Use in Children and Adolescents:</strong></p><p> It is not known if Mirogabalin is safe and effective in children and adolescents.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong></p><p> A single 15 mg dose of Mirogabalin does not produce significant adverse reactions in patients with mild to moderate hepatic impairment. No data is available for severe hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Mild renal dysfunction:</strong> Initial dose starts from 5 mg twice a day, slowly increased by 5 mg at an interval of 1 week to 10 mg.</li><li><strong>Moderate renal dysfunction:</strong> Initial dose starts from 2.5 mg twice a day, slowly increased by 2.5 mg at an interval of 1 week to 7.5 mg twice a day.</li><li><strong>Severe renal dysfunction:</strong> Initial dose starts from 2.5 mg once a day, slowly increased by 2.5 mg at an interval of 1 week to 7.5 mg once a day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p><strong>With Medicine:</strong></p><ul><li>Co-administration of Mirotiv with Cimetidine or Probenecid may raise the Mirotiv plasma concentration.</li><li>If Mirotiv is taken with Lorazepam, the depressive effects on the CNS may be potentiated.</li><li>Mirotiv is an OAT1, OAT3, OCT2, MATE1, MATE2-K, and UGT substrate.</li><li>Mirotiv does not inhibit or induce major human CYP molecular species and does not inhibit the activities of drug transporters (including OAT1, OAT3, OCT1, OCT2, OATP1B1, OATP1B3, MATE1, MATE2-K, P-gp, and BCRP).</li><li>Co-administration with OAT1, OAT3, OCT2, MATE1, MATE2-K, or UGT inhibitors may increase mirogabalin exposure, so use with caution.</li></ul><p><strong>With food &amp; others:</strong></p><ul><li>Administration of Mirotiv with food has no clinically relevant effect on its total absorption.</li><li>Avoid consuming alcohol while taking Mirotiv, as it may potentiate the impairment of motor skills and sedating effects of alcohol.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Mirogabalin is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients with hypersensitivity to Mirogabalin.</li><li>Patients with mild to moderate hepatic and renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> The most reported adverse reactions include somnolence, dizziness, edema, and weight gain. The symptoms described below are rarely seen as initial symptoms of the adverse reactions indicated in brackets. If any of these symptoms occur, stop taking this medicine and see your doctor immediately.</p><ul><li>Light-headedness, state close to sleep with impaired consciousness, loss of consciousness (dizziness, somnolence, unconsciousness).</li><li>General malaise, loss of appetite, nausea, vomiting, jaundice (liver dysfunction).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> For pregnant or potentially pregnant women, administer only if the therapeutic benefit outweighs the risks. Presence in placental passage has been reported in animal studies. Consider the therapeutic and breastfeeding benefits before deciding to continue or discontinue breastfeeding. Animal studies have reported that Mirogabalin is transferred into milk.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>This medicine may cause dizziness, somnolence, or loss of consciousness. Avoid operating dangerous machinery, such as driving a car.</li><li>Especially for elderly patients, careful attention should be taken.</li><li>This medicine may cause weight gain.</li><li>This medicine may cause blurred vision and double vision.</li><li>If an allergic reaction occurs, stop taking the medicine and consult a doctor.</li><li>Dose adjustment is needed in patients with renal dysfunction.</li><li>If taking any other medication, please consult a doctor before administering Mirotiv.</li><li>Elderly patients should be aware of falling and fractures.</li><li>The patient should consult a doctor if signs of blurred vision or double vision appear while taking this medication.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs, Primary anti-epileptic drugs.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C in a dry place, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '212_1742060095.jpg', 6, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 22:34:55', '2025-03-15 22:34:55'),
(3070, 'Meget', 'Megestrol Acetate', 80.00, 6, 'Tablet', '6\'s tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '160 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Meget 160 mg is indicated for the palliative treatment of advanced carcinoma of the breast or endometrium (i.e., recurrent, inoperable, or metastatic disease). It should not be used instead of currently accepted procedures such as surgery, radiation, or chemotherapy.</p><p>Megestrol oral suspension is indicated for the treatment of anorexia, cachexia, or unexplained significant weight loss in patients with a diagnosis of Acquired Immunodeficiency Syndrome (AIDS) and cancer.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Hormonal Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Meget 160 mg is a synthetic, antineoplastic, and progestational drug. While the precise mechanism by which Meget 160 mg produces its antineoplastic effects against endometrial carcinoma is unknown at the present time, inhibition of pituitary gonadotrophin production and a resultant decrease in estrogen secretion may be factors. The antineoplastic action of Meget 160 mg on carcinoma of the breast is effected by modifying the action of other steroid hormones and by exerting a direct cytotoxic effect on tumor cells.</p><p>In metastatic cancer, hormone receptors may be present in some tissues but not others. The receptor mechanism is a cyclic process whereby estrogen produced by the ovaries enters the target cell, forms a complex with cytoplasmic receptors, and is transported into the cell nucleus. There it induces gene transcription and leads to the alteration of normal cell functions.</p><p>Pharmacologic doses of Meget 160 mg not only decrease the number of hormone-dependent human breast cancer cells but also are capable of modifying and abolishing the stimulatory effects of estrogen on these cells.</p><p>Estimates of plasma levels of Meget 160 mg are dependent on the measurement method used. Peak plasma concentrations occur 2 to 3 hours after a single oral 160 mg dose. The plasma half-life of Meget 160 mg is 33 to 38 hours. Approximately 66% of an administered dose is excreted in the urine and approximately 20% in the feces.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p> Breast cancer: 160 mg/day</p><p> Endometrial carcinoma: 40-320 mg/day in divided doses. At least 2 months of continuous treatment is considered an adequate period for determining the efficacy of Megestrol.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Meget 160 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Tablet:</strong> Breast cancer: 160 mg/day. Endometrial carcinoma: 40-320 mg/day in divided doses. At least 2 months of continuous treatment is considered an adequate period for determining the efficacy of Megestrol.</p><p><strong>Oral Suspension:</strong> The recommended adult initial dosage of Megestrol oral suspension is 800 mg/day (20 ml/day).</p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> <strong>With Medicine:</strong> Possible interactions of Meget 160 mg with concomitant medications have not been investigated.</p><p> <strong>With Food and Others:</strong> Estrogens and progestogens may cause fluid retention, particularly when given in high dosages or for prolonged periods. Therapy with these agents should be administered cautiously in patients who have preexisting problems with excess fluid.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> History of hypersensitivity to Meget 160 mg or any component of the formulation. Known or suspected pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> <strong>Weight Gain:</strong> Weight gain is a frequent side effect of Meget 160 mg. This gain has been associated with increased appetite and is not necessarily associated with fluid retention.</p><p> <strong>Thromboembolic Phenomena:</strong> Thromboembolic phenomena including thrombophlebitis and pulmonary embolism (in some cases fatal) have been reported.</p><p> <strong>Glucocorticoid Effects:</strong> The glucocorticoid activity of Meget 160 mg has not been fully evaluated. Clinical cases of new-onset diabetes mellitus, exacerbation of preexisting diabetes mellitus, and overt Cushing’s syndrome have been reported in association with the chronic use of Meget 160 mg. In addition, clinical cases of adrenal insufficiency have been observed in patients receiving or being withdrawn from chronic Meget 160 mg therapy in the stressed and non-stressed state.</p><p> <strong>Other:</strong> Nausea, dyspnea, tumor flare, hyperglycemia, glucose intolerance, alopecia, hypertension, carpal tunnel syndrome, mood changes, hot flashes, malaise, asthenia, lethargy, sweating, and rash.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category D. The use of progestational agents during the first four months of pregnancy is not recommended. Very small amounts (approximately 0.1%) are excreted in mother\'s milk. It is, however, not known whether these amounts exert any harmful effect on the newborn. Because of the potential for adverse effects on the newborn, nursing should be discontinued during treatment with Meget 160 mg.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> <strong>General:</strong> Close surveillance is indicated for any patient treated for recurrent or metastatic cancer. Use with caution in patients with a history of thromboembolic disease.</p><p> <strong>Use in Diabetics:</strong> Exacerbation of preexisting diabetes with increased insulin requirements has been reported in association with the use of Meget 160 mg.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p> No serious unexpected side effects have resulted from studies involving Meget 160 mg administered in dosages as high as 1600 mg/day.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool (below 30°C) and dry place, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep all medicines out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> <strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p> <strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> In dose selection for an elderly patient, caution should be exercised, usually starting at the low end of the dosing range, reflecting the greater frequency of decreased hepatic, renal, or cardiac function, and of concomitant disease or other drug therapy.</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Meget 160 mg is a synthetic, antineoplastic, and progestational drug. While the precise mechanism by which Meget 160 mg produces its antineoplastic effects against endometrial carcinoma is unknown at the present time, inhibition of pituitary gonadotrophin production and a resultant decrease in estrogen secretion may be factors. The antineoplastic action of Meget 160 mg on carcinoma of the breast is effected by modifying the action of other steroid hormones and by exerting a direct cytotoxic effect on tumor cells.</p><p>In metastatic cancer, hormone receptors may be present in some tissues but not others. The receptor mechanism is a cyclic process whereby estrogen produced by the ovaries enters the target cell, forms a complex with cytoplasmic receptors, and is transported into the cell nucleus. There it induces gene transcription and leads to the alteration of normal cell functions.</p><p>Pharmacologic doses of Meget 160 mg not only decrease the number of hormone-dependent human breast cancer cells but also are capable of modifying and abolishing the stimulatory effects of estrogen on these cells.</p><p>Estimates of plasma levels of Meget 160 mg are dependent on the measurement method used. Peak plasma concentrations occur 2 to 3 hours after a single oral 160 mg dose. The plasma half-life of Meget 160 mg is 33 to 38 hours. Approximately 66% of an administered dose is excreted in the urine and approximately 20% in the feces.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category D. The use of progestational agents during the first four months of pregnancy is not recommended. Very small amounts (approximately 0.1%) are excreted in mother\'s milk. It is, however, not known whether these amounts exert any harmful effect on the newborn. Because of the potential for adverse effects on the newborn, nursing should be discontinued during treatment with Meget 160 mg.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> <strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p> <strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Insufficient data from clinical studies of Meget 160 mg tablets are available for patients 65 years of age and older to determine whether they respond differently than younger patients.</p>', 'active', '1696_1742060476.jpg', 4, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 22:41:16', '2025-03-15 22:41:16'),
(3071, 'Veratin', 'Ivermectin', 15.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '6 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Veratin 6 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Strongyloidiasis of the intestinal tract:</strong> Veratin 6 mg is indicated for the treatment of intestinal (i.e., nondisseminated) strongyloidiasis due to the nematode parasite <em>Strongyloides stercoralis</em>. This indication is based on clinical studies of both comparative and open-label designs, in which 64-100% of infected patients were cured following a single 200-mcg/kg dose of Veratin 6 mg.</p><p><strong>Onchocerciasis:</strong> Veratin 6 mg is indicated for the treatment of onchocerciasis due to the nematode parasite <em>Onchocerca volvulus</em>. This indication is based on randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, and comparative studies conducted in 1427 patients in onchocerciasis-endemic areas of West Africa. The comparative studies used diethylcarbamazine citrate (DEC-C).</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anthelmintic</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Veratin 6 mg selectively binds with high affinity to glutamate-gated chloride ion channels, which occur in invertebrate nerve and muscle cells. This leads to an increase in the permeability of cell membranes to chloride ions, causing hyperpolarization of the nerve or muscle cell and, ultimately, the death of the parasite.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Veratin 6 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Strongyloidiasis:</strong> The recommended dosage of Veratin 6 mg for the treatment of strongyloidiasis is a single oral dose of approximately 200 mcg/kg of body weight. Patients should take tablets on an empty stomach with water. Additional doses are generally not necessary. However, follow-up stool examinations should be performed to verify eradication of infection.</p><p><strong>Dosage Guidelines for Veratin 6 mg for Strongyloidiasis:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Body Weight (kg) 15-24:</strong> Dose 3 mg</li><li><strong>Body Weight (kg) 25-35:</strong> Dose 6 mg</li><li><strong>Body Weight (kg) 36-50:</strong> Dose 9 mg</li><li><strong>Body Weight (kg) 51-65:</strong> Dose 12 mg</li><li><strong>Body Weight (kg) 66-79:</strong> Dose 15 mg</li><li><strong>Body Weight (kg) &gt;80:</strong> Dose 200 mcg/kg</li></ul><p><strong>Onchocerciasis:</strong> The recommended dosage of Veratin 6 mg is a single oral dose of approximately 150 mcg/kg of body weight on an empty stomach with water. The most commonly used dose interval is 12 months. Retreatment may be considered at intervals as short as 3 months.</p><p><strong>Dosage Guidelines for Veratin 6 mg for Onchocerciasis:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Body Weight (kg) 15-25:</strong> Dose 3 mg</li><li><strong>Body Weight (kg) 26-44:</strong> Dose 6 mg</li><li><strong>Body Weight (kg) 45-64:</strong> Dose 9 mg</li><li><strong>Body Weight (kg) 65-84:</strong> Dose 12 mg</li><li><strong>Body Weight (kg) &gt;85:</strong> Dose 150 mcg/kg</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Veratin 6 mg</strong></p><p> Post-marketing reports of increased INR (International Normalized Ratio) have been rarely reported when Veratin 6 mg was co-administered with warfarin.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Veratin 6 mg is contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to any component of this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Veratin 6 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Strongyloidiasis:</strong> In clinical studies involving 109 patients given one or two doses of 170-200 mcg/kg of Veratin 6 mg, the following adverse reactions were reported:</p><ul><li><strong>Body as a whole:</strong> Asthenia/fatigue (0.9%), abdominal pain (0.9%)</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Anorexia (0.9%), constipation (0.9%), diarrhea (1.8%), nausea (1.8%), vomiting (0.9%)</li><li><strong>Nervous System/Psychiatric:</strong> Dizziness (2.8%), somnolence (0.9%), vertigo (0.9%), tremor (0.9%)</li><li><strong>Skin:</strong> Pruritus (2.8%), rash (0.9%), and urticaria (0.9%)</li></ul><p><strong>Onchocerciasis:</strong> The following adverse reactions were reported:</p><ul><li>Arthralgia/synovitis (19.3%)</li><li>Axillary lymph node enlargement (11.0%) and tenderness (4.4%)</li><li>Cervical lymph node enlargement (5.3%) and tenderness (1.2%)</li><li>Inguinal lymph node enlargement (12.6%) and tenderness (13.9%)</li><li>Other lymph node enlargement (3.0%) and tenderness (1.9%)</li><li>Pruritus (27.5%)</li><li>Skin involvement, including edema, papular and pustular or urticarial rash (22.7%)</li><li>Fever (22.6%)</li><li>Ocular effects such as abnormal sensation, eyelid edema, anterior uveitis, conjunctivitis, limbitis, keratitis, and chorioretinitis/choroiditis (rarely severe)</li></ul><p><strong>Post-marketing reports:</strong> Hypotension (mainly orthostatic hypotension), worsening of bronchial asthma, toxic epidermal necrolysis, and Stevens-Johnson syndrome.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> <strong>Pregnancy Category C:</strong> Veratin 6 mg does not appear to be selectively fetotoxic, but no adequate and well-controlled studies exist in pregnant women. It should not be used during pregnancy unless the potential benefit justifies the potential risk.</p><p><strong>Nursing Mothers:</strong> Veratin 6 mg is excreted in human milk in low concentrations. Treatment of breastfeeding mothers should only be considered if the risk of delayed treatment outweighs the potential risk to the newborn.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Microfilaricidal drugs such as diethylcarbamazine citrate (DEC-C) may cause cutaneous and/or systemic reactions (Mazzotti reaction) and ophthalmological reactions in onchocerciasis patients.</li><li>Patients treated with Veratin 6 mg for onchocerciasis may experience these reactions in addition to drug-related adverse effects.</li><li>Severe Mazzotti reactions are treated with oral hydration, recumbency, intravenous normal saline, and/or corticosteroids. Mild-to-moderate reactions may be managed with antihistamines and aspirin.</li><li>Patients with hyperreactive onchodermatitis (sowda) may experience severe adverse reactions, including edema and worsening skin conditions.</li><li>Patients with <em>Onchocerca volvulus</em> and heavy <em>Loa loa</em> infections may develop serious or fatal encephalopathy either spontaneously or after microfilaricide treatment. Symptoms include back pain, conjunctival hemorrhage, dyspnea, urinary/fecal incontinence, difficulty standing/walking, mental status changes, confusion, lethargy, stupor, or coma.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a dry place below 30°C. Protect from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness have not been established in patients weighing less than 15 kg.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Clinical studies did not include sufficient numbers of patients aged 65 and older to determine if they respond differently from younger subjects.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1514_1742060746.jpg', 44, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 22:45:46', '2025-03-15 22:45:46');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3072, 'Maxolax', 'Baclofen', 5.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Maxolax 5 mg</strong></p><p> Maxolax 5 mg is indicated for the treatment of spasticity, including flexor spasms, associated pain, clonus, muscular rigidity, as well as skeletal muscle spasms resulting from conditions such as multiple sclerosis, rheumatic disorders, spinal cord injuries, other spinal cord diseases, cerebrovascular accidents, neoplastic, or degenerative brain diseases.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Centrally acting Skeletal Muscle Relaxants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Maxolax 5 mg is an effective muscle relaxant and antispastic agent that primarily acts at the spinal level. While its exact mode of action is not fully understood, Maxolax 5 mg works by stimulating GABAB receptors, which inhibits the release of glutamate and aspartate, thereby affecting both monosynaptic and polysynaptic reflexes.</p><p>It may also have central nervous system (CNS) depressant effects at intraspinal sites. Notably, Maxolax 5 mg does not impact neuromuscular transmission. Maxolax 5 mg exhibits an antinociceptive (pain-relieving) effect, although its clinical significance remains uncertain.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Maxolax 5 mg</strong></p><p> Maxolax 5 mg should be taken with a small amount of liquid during meals. The dosing regimen for Maxolax 5 mg varies for adults and children.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> Typically administered in divided doses, preferably three times a day. The aim is to use the lowest effective dose. The therapy usually begins with a low dose and is then increased gradually to achieve the best response, typically falling within the range of 30-80 mg daily. Additional increases may be necessary but should generally not exceed a maximum daily dose of 80 mg.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> Treatment should begin with an extremely low dose, such as 0.3 mg/kg per day, divided into multiple doses. The dose can then be cautiously increased at 1-2 week intervals until it meets the child\'s specific needs. The usual maintenance dose for children typically falls within the range of 0.75 to 2 mg/kg of body weight per day. For children over 10 years of age, a maximum daily dose of 2.5 mg/kg of body weight may be considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Maxolax 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Increased sedation may occur when Maxolax 5 mg is taken alongside agents that affect the central nervous system, alcohol, or synthetic opiates. This also elevates the risk of respiratory depression.</li><li>When combined with antihypertensives, it may amplify the drop in blood pressure, requiring adjustments to the antihypertensive medication dosage.</li><li>Concurrent use with tricyclic antidepressants may intensify Maxolax 5 mg\'s pharmacological effects, leading to pronounced muscular hypotonia.</li><li>In individuals with Parkinson\'s disease receiving Maxolax 5 mg along with levodopa and carbidopa, there have been reports of mental confusion, hallucinations, headaches, nausea, and agitation.</li><li>MAO inhibitors and Maxolax 5 mg together may result in increased CNS depressant effects.</li><li>Care should be taken when using Maxolax 5 mg with magnesium sulfate or neuromuscular blocking agents due to a potential synergistic effect.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Maxolax 5 mg is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to any component of this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Maxolax 5 mg</strong></p><p> Common adverse reactions include drowsiness, dizziness, weakness, and fatigue.</p><p><strong>Serious adverse reactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Central nervous system:</strong> Headaches, insomnia, euphoria, excitement, depression, confusion, hallucinations, paraesthesia, nightmares, muscle pain, tinnitus, slurred speech, coordination disorders, tremors, rigidity, dystonia, ataxia, blurred vision, nystagmus, strabismus, miosis, mydriasis, diplopia, dysarthria, epileptic seizures, and respiratory depression.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular:</strong> Hypotension, dyspnoea, palpitations, chest pain, and syncope.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Nausea, constipation, dry mouth, anorexia, taste disorders, abdominal pain, vomiting, diarrhea, and positive tests for occult blood in stool.</li><li><strong>Genitourinary:</strong> Urinary frequency, enuresis, urinary retention, dysuria, impotence, inability to ejaculate, nocturia, and hematuria.</li><li><strong>Other:</strong> Rash, pruritus, ankle edema, excessive perspiration, weight gain, nasal congestion, visual disturbances, hepatic function disorders, and a paradoxical increase in spasticity.</li></ul><p>Muscular hypotonia (low muscle tone) can occur but is usually relieved by adjusting the dosage.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B3:</strong> The safety of using Maxolax 5 mg during pregnancy has not been established, as it can cross the placental barrier. It should only be administered if the physician concludes that the potential benefits outweigh the possible risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Maxolax 5 mg is excreted in breast milk, but available evidence suggests that the quantities are so minimal that no adverse effects on the infant are expected.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Lower doses (around 5 mg per day) should be considered for patients with impaired renal function or those undergoing chronic hemodialysis.</li><li>Patients with spasticity and psychiatric disorders (such as schizophrenia, depression, or mania) should be monitored closely, as exacerbations may occur.</li><li>In patients with epilepsy, Maxolax 5 mg should be used with caution under proper supervision, as it may lower the convulsion threshold.</li><li>Caution is advised in patients with a history of peptic ulcers, cerebrovascular diseases, hepatic, renal, or respiratory failure.</li><li>Regular monitoring of respiratory and cardiovascular function is crucial in patients with cardiopulmonary disease and respiratory muscle weakness.</li><li>For individuals with hepatic diseases or diabetes mellitus, periodic laboratory tests should be conducted.</li><li>Maxolax 5 mg has not shown significant benefits in patients with stroke, and they generally have poor tolerance to the medication.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Maxolax 5 mg</strong></p><p> In case of severe overdose, gastric lavage is important.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Centrally acting Skeletal Muscle Relaxants</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Maxolax 5 mg inhibits both monosynaptic and polysynaptic reflexes at the spinal level by stimulating the GABAB receptors, which in turn inhibits the release of glutamate and aspartate. Additionally, it may act at intraspinal sites, leading to CNS depression. Maxolax 5 mg also demonstrates an antinociceptive effect.</p>', 'active', '1957_1742061135.jpg', 6, 12, NULL, '2025-03-15 22:52:15', '2025-03-15 22:52:15'),
(3073, 'Olmetic Plus', 'Olmesartan Medoxomil + Hydrochlorothiazide', 9.00, 14, 'Tablet', '14\'s tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg+12.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Olmetic Plus 20 mg+12.5 mg</strong></p><p> Olmesartan Medoxomil &amp; Hydrochlorothiazide combination is indicated for the treatment of hypertension.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Combined antihypertensive preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Hydrochlorothiazide inhibits the reabsorption of Na in the distal tubules, causing increased excretion of Na and water, including K and hydrogen ions. Olmesartan blocks the vasoconstrictor effects of angiotensin II by selectively blocking the binding of angiotensin II to the AT1 receptor in vascular smooth muscle. Its action is independent of the pathways for angiotensin II synthesis. Olmesartan has a 12,500-fold greater affinity for the AT1 receptor than for the AT2 receptor.</p><p>Blockade of the renin-angiotensin system with ACE inhibitors inhibits the biosynthesis of angiotensin II from angiotensin I, but olmesartan does not inhibit ACE and does not affect bradykinin. Blockade of the angiotensin II receptor inhibits the negative regulatory feedback of angiotensin II on renin secretion, but the resulting increased plasma renin activity and circulating angiotensin II levels do not overcome the effect of olmesartan on blood pressure.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Olmetic Plus 20 mg+12.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>The usual recommended starting dose of Olmesartan Medoxomil is 20 mg once daily. If further blood pressure reduction is required after 2 weeks, the dose may be increased to 40 mg.</li><li>Hydrochlorothiazide is effective in doses between 12.5 mg and 50 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Replacement Therapy</strong>: Olmesartan &amp; Hydrochlorothiazide may be substituted for its titrated components.</li><li><strong>Dose Titration by Clinical Effect</strong>: The dose of Olmesartan &amp; Hydrochlorothiazide is one tablet once daily. More than one tablet daily is not recommended.</li><li>If blood pressure is not controlled by Olmesartan alone, Hydrochlorothiazide may be added starting with a dose of 12.5 mg and later titrated to 25 mg once daily.</li><li>If large doses of Hydrochlorothiazide have been used as monotherapy, caution should be used when adding Olmesartan.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Olmetic Plus 20 mg+12.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: The usual starting dose is 20/12.5 mg once daily. The dose may be titrated at intervals of 2-4 weeks to 40/25 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Olmetic Plus 20 mg+12.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Olmesartan</strong>: No significant drug interactions were reported when co-administered with hydrochlorothiazide, digoxin, or warfarin.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: When administered concurrently, the following drugs may interact:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Alcohol, Barbiturates, or Narcotics</strong>: May potentiate orthostatic hypotension.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Antidiabetic drugs (oral agents and insulin)</strong>: Dosage adjustment may be required.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Other antihypertensive drugs</strong>: Additive effect.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Corticosteroids, ACTH, Lithium</strong>: Potential interactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Olmesartan and Hydrochlorothiazide combination is contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to any component of this product. It is also contraindicated in patients with anuria or hypersensitivity to other sulfonamide-derived drugs.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Olmetic Plus 20 mg+12.5 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include nausea, headache, dizziness, hyperuricemia, upper respiratory tract infection, and urinary tract infection. Other adverse effects include chest pain, back pain, peripheral edema, abdominal pain, dyspepsia, gastroenteritis, and diarrhea.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Categories C (first trimester) and D (second and third trimesters)</strong>. This drug should not be used during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether Olmesartan is excreted in human milk. Hydrochlorothiazide appears in human milk. A decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Periodic determination of serum electrolytes should be performed to detect possible imbalances like hypokalemia, hyponatremia, and hypochloremic alkalosis.</li><li>Hyperuricemia may occur in patients receiving thiazide therapy.</li><li>Caution in patients with impaired renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Olmetic Plus 20 mg+12.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Olmesartan</strong>: The most likely manifestation of overdose is hypotension and tachycardia. Supportive treatment should be instituted.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: Overdose may cause electrolyte depletion, hypokalemia, hypochloremia, and dehydration. If digitalis has been administered, hypokalemia may accentuate cardiac arrhythmias.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: If creatinine clearance &gt;30 ml/min, normal regimens may be followed. In severe renal impairment, loop diuretics are preferred, so this combination is not recommended.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment necessary.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and efficacy in children below 18 years have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Dose selection should be cautious in elderly patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Combined antihypertensive preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Olmesartan</strong>: Blocks the vasoconstrictor and aldosterone-secreting effects of angiotensin-II by selectively blocking AT1 receptors. It is a reversible &amp; competitive inhibitor of AT1 receptors and does not inhibit ACE.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: A thiazide diuretic that increases sodium and chloride excretion, leading to reduced plasma volume and increased renin activity. Co-administration with an angiotensin-II receptor antagonist helps counteract potassium loss.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The drug should be discontinued during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Not recommended if creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment necessary.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Not established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Dose selection should be cautious.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '346_1742099783.jpg', 9, 12, NULL, '2025-03-16 09:36:23', '2025-03-16 09:36:23'),
(3074, 'Fluromet', 'Fluorometholone Acetate', 100.00, 1, 'Unit', '5 ml drop', 'Drop', '5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Fluromet 0.1%</strong></p><p> Fluromet 0.1% ophthalmic suspension is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Acute and chronic non-infectious conjunctivitis and keratitis of allergic origin.</li><li>Non-infectious inflammation of the anterior chamber of the eye (including anterior uveitis, episcleritis, and scleritis).</li><li>Post-operative irritative conditions after strabismus, cataract, and glaucoma surgery.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic Steroid Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Fluorometholone is a corticosteroid with an excellent anti-inflammatory action with no significant influence on intraocular pressure. The anti-inflammatory activity of Fluorometholone is at least 40 times stronger than that of Hydrocortisone. Corticosteroids are thought to act by the introduction of phospholipase A2 inhibitory proteins. These proteins control the biosynthesis of potent mediators of inflammation, such as prostaglandins and leukotrienes, by inhibiting the release of their common precursor, arachidonic acid. Arachidonic acid is released from membrane phospholipids by phospholipase A2.</p><p>Compared with other corticosteroids, Fluorometholone is more rapidly degraded in tissue, reducing its effect on intraocular pressure. The immunosuppressive action of Fluorometholone is less pronounced than that of Dexamethasone.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Fluromet 0.1%</strong></p><ul><li>Instill 1 drop into the conjunctival sac 2-4 times daily.</li><li>During the initial 24 to 48 hours, the dosage may be safely increased to 1 drop every hour.</li><li>Care should be taken not to discontinue therapy prematurely.</li><li>Shake well before use.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Fluromet 0.1%</strong></p><ul><li>1 drop instilled into the conjunctival sac 2-4 times daily.</li><li>During the initial 24 to 48 hours, the dosage may be safely increased to 1 drop every hour.</li><li>Shake well before use.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Fluromet 0.1%</strong></p><p> Specific drug interaction studies have not been conducted with Fluorometholone ophthalmic suspension.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient of the formulation.</li><li>Infectious conjunctivitis or keratitis.</li><li>Injuries and ulcerous processes of the cornea, especially infections caused by viruses, bacteria, and fungi.</li><li>Dry eyes, especially keratoconjunctivitis sicca.</li><li>Glaucoma.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Fluromet 0.1%</strong></p><ul><li>Glaucoma with optic nerve damage.</li><li>Visual acuity or field defects.</li><li>Cataract formation.</li><li>Secondary ocular infection following suppression of host immunity.</li><li>Perforation of the globe.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The safety of topical steroids during pregnancy and lactation has not been established. Fluorometholone ophthalmic suspension should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Shake well before use.</li><li>If this product is used for 10 days or longer, intraocular pressure should be monitored.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li><li>Discard the container 4 weeks after opening.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic Steroid Preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Fluorometholone is a corticosteroid with an excellent anti-inflammatory action and minimal influence on intraocular pressure. It has 40 times stronger anti-inflammatory activity than Hydrocortisone. Corticosteroids act by introducing phospholipase A2 inhibitory proteins, controlling the biosynthesis of potent inflammatory mediators such as prostaglandins and leukotrienes by inhibiting arachidonic acid release.</p><p>Fluorometholone is more rapidly degraded in tissue than other corticosteroids, leading to a reduced effect on intraocular pressure. It also has a less pronounced immunosuppressive action than Dexamethasone.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The safety of topical steroids during pregnancy and lactation has not been established. Fluorometholone ophthalmic suspension should be used in pregnancy only if the potential benefit outweighs the risk to the fetus.</p><p>This version keeps the structure clear and easy to read while maintaining bold headings as per your preference. Let me know if you need further modifications!</p>', 'active', '1134_1742099965.jpg', 17, 12, NULL, '2025-03-16 09:39:25', '2025-03-16 09:39:25'),
(3075, 'PEG-Filastin', 'Pegfilgrastim [Pegylated Filgrastim]', 4000.00, 1, 'Unit', '0.6 ml pre-filled syringe', 'Injection', '6 mg per 0.6 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of PEG-Filastin 6 mg/0.6 ml</strong></p><p>Pegfilgrastim is a leukocyte growth factor indicated to decrease the incidence of infection, as manifested by febrile neutropenia, in patients with non-myeloid malignancies receiving myelosuppressive anti-cancer drugs associated with a clinically significant incidence of febrile neutropenia.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Hematopoietic drug</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Pegfilgrastim is a covalent conjugate of filgrastim and monomethoxypolyethylene glycol which binds to specific cell surface receptors of haematopoietic cells, thereby stimulating proliferation, differentiation, commitment, and end cell functional activation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of PEG-Filastin 6 mg/0.6 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Cancer patients receiving Myelosuppressive Chemotherapy:</strong> The recommended dosage of Pegfilgrastim is a single subcutaneous injection of 6 mg administered once per chemotherapy cycle in adults. For pediatric patients weight less than 10 kg is 0.1 mg (0.01 ml)/kg, 10-20 kg is 0.15 ml, 21-30 kg is 0.25 ml &amp; 31-44 kg is 0.4 ml. Do not administer Pegfilgrastim between 14 days before and 24 hours after administration of cytotoxic chemotherapy.</p><p><strong>Hematopoietic Subsyndrome of Acute Radiation Syndrome:</strong> The recommended dose of Pegfilgrastim is two doses of 6 mg each, administer the first dose as soon as possible after suspected or confirmed exposure to radiation levels greater than 2 gray (Gy) &amp; the second dose one week after the first dose. For dosing in pediatric patients weighing less than 45 kg, refer to Myelosuppressive Chemotherapy dosing system.</p><p><strong>Dosage of PEG-Filastin 6 mg/0.6 ml</strong></p><p>The recommended dosage of Pegfilgrastim is a single subcutaneous injection of 6 mg administered once per chemotherapy cycle in adults. Do not administer Pegfilgrastim between 14 days before and 24 hours after administration of cytotoxic chemotherapy. Visually inspect parenteral drug products for particulate matter and discoloration prior to administration, whenever solution and container permit. Do not administer Pegfilgrastim if discoloration or particulates are observed.</p><p><strong>NOTE:</strong> The needle cover on the single-use pre-filled syringe contains dry natural rubber (latex); persons with latex allergies should not administer this product.</p><p><strong>Interaction of PEG-Filastin 6 mg/0.6 ml</strong></p><p>No formal drug interaction studies between Pegfilgrastim and other drugs have been performed. Increased hematopoietic activity of the bone marrow in response to growth factor therapy may result in transiently positive bone-imaging changes. Consider these findings when interpreting bone-imaging results. This medicinal product must not be mixed with other medicinal products, particularly sodium chloride solutions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Do not administer Pegfilgrastim to patients with a history of serious allergic reactions to pegfilgrastim or filgrastim.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of PEG-Filastin 6 mg/0.6 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Splenic Rupture:</strong> Splenic rupture, including fatal cases, can occur following the administration of Pegfilgrastim. Evaluate for an enlarged spleen or splenic rupture in patients who report left upper abdominal or shoulder pain after receiving Pegfilgrastim.</p><p><strong>Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome:</strong> Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) can occur in patients receiving Pegfilgrastim. Evaluate patients who develop fever and lung infiltrates or respiratory distress after receiving Pegfilgrastim for ARDS. Discontinue Pegfilgrastim in patients with ARDS.</p><p><strong>Serious Allergic Reactions:</strong> Serious allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur in patients receiving Pegfilgrastim. The majority of reported events occurred upon initial exposure. Allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, can recur within days after the discontinuation of initial anti-allergic treatment. Permanently discontinue Pegfilgrastim in patients with serious allergic reactions. Do not administer Pegfilgrastim to patients with a history of serious allergic reactions to Pegfilgrastim or Filgrastim.</p><p><strong>Patients with Sickle Cell Disorders:</strong> Severe sickle cell crises can occur in patients with sickle cell disorders receiving Pegfilgrastim. Severe and sometimes fatal sickle cell crises can occur in patients with sickle cell disorders receiving Filgrastim, the parent compound of Pegfilgrastim.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Pregnancy category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Therefore, Pegfilgrastim should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit to the mother justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is not known whether Pegfilgrastim is secreted in human milk. Other recombinant G-CSF products are poorly secreted in breast milk and G-CSF is not orally absorbed by neonates. Caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Fatal splenic rupture can occur. Evaluate for splenomegaly or splenic rupture in patients with left upper abdominal or shoulder pain. Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) can occur. Evaluate for ARDS in patients who develop fever, lung infiltrates, or respiratory distress. Discontinue Pegfilgrastim in patients with ARDS. Serious allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur. Permanently discontinue Pegfilgrastim in patients with serious allergic reactions.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of PEG-Filastin 6 mg/0.6 ml</strong></p><p>The maximum amount of Pegfilgrastim that can be safely administered in single or multiple doses has not been determined. Single subcutaneous doses of 300 mcg/kg have been administered to 8 healthy volunteers and 3 patients with non-small cell lung cancer without serious adverse effects. These patients experienced a mean maximum Absolute Neutrophil Count (ANC) of 55x10^9/L, with a corresponding mean maximum WBC of 67x10^9/L. The absolute maximum ANC observed was 96x10^9/L with a corresponding absolute maximum WBC observed of 120x10^9/L.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Pegfilgrastim should be stored in a refrigerator at 2-8°C. Do not freeze. Do not shake. Keep away from light.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Hematopoietic drug</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Pegfilgrastim, a Granulocyte Colony-Stimulating Factor (G-CSF), is a pegylated glycoprotein that regulates the production and release of functional neutrophils from bone marrow. Pegfilgrastim is a covalent conjugate of recombinant methionyl human G-CSF (Filgrastim) and monomethoxy polyethylene glycol. The average molecular weight of pegfilgrastim is approximately 39 kD.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness of Pegfilgrastim in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Of the 932 patients with cancer who received Pegfilgrastim in clinical studies, 139 (15%) were age 65 and over, and 18 (2%) were age 75 and over. No overall differences in safety or effectiveness were observed between older and younger patients.</p><p><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Renal dysfunction had no effect on the pharmacokinetics of Pegfilgrastim. Therefore, dose adjustment in patients with renal dysfunction is not necessary.</p>', 'active', '1281_1742100756.jpg', 2, 12, NULL, '2025-03-16 09:52:36', '2025-03-16 09:52:36'),
(3076, 'Imigra', 'Flunarizine', 3.50, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> <strong>Imigra is indicated for</strong></p><ul><li>Prophylaxis of classic (with aura) or common (without aura) migraine.</li><li>Symptomatic treatment of vestibular vertigo (due to a diagnosed functional disorder of the vestibular system).</li><li>Peripheral Vascular Disease (PVD).</li><li>Motion sickness.</li><li>Refractory epilepsy resistant to conventional antiepileptic therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Flunarizine is the difluorinated derivative of cinnarizine. It is a selective calcium channel antagonist. By reducing excessive transmembrane influx of calcium, Flunarizine prevents cellular calcium overload. It does not interfere with normal cellular calcium homeostasis. Flunarizine also has some antihistaminic and sedative properties. It binds at an affinity of 99% to plasma protein.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Migraine Prophylaxis:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Starting Dose:</strong> 10 mg at night in patients less than 65 years of age and 5 mg daily in patients older than 65 years. If depressive, extrapyramidal, or other unacceptable symptoms occur, administration should be discontinued. If no significant improvement is observed after 2 months, treatment should be discontinued.</li><li><strong>Maintenance Treatment:</strong> If satisfactory response is achieved and maintenance treatment is required, the dose should be reduced to 5 days per week with two successive medicine-free days. Even if well tolerated, prophylactic maintenance treatment should be interrupted after 6 months and re-initiated only if relapse occurs.</li></ul><p><strong>Peripheral Vascular Disease:</strong> 10 mg twice daily, up to 30 mg per day if required.</p><p><strong>Vertigo &amp; Motion Sickness:</strong> 10-20 mg daily for adults and 5 mg daily for children (&gt;40 kg).</p><p><strong>Epileptic Seizure:</strong> 15-20 mg daily in adults and 5-10 mg daily for children as an add-on therapy.</p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> Galactorrhoea has been reported in some women on oral contraceptives within the first two months of Imigra treatment. Hepatic enzyme inducers such as Carbamazepine and Phenytoin may increase Imigra metabolism, requiring a dosage adjustment.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to Flunarizine. Contraindicated in patients with a history of depressive illness, Parkinson\'s disease, or other extrapyramidal disorders.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Drowsiness and/or fatigue, as well as weight gain and/or increased appetite, may occur. Chronic treatment may lead to depression (especially in female patients with a history of depressive illness) and extrapyramidal symptoms (such as bradykinesia, rigidity, akathisia, orofacial dyskinesia, tremor), with elderly patients being particularly at risk. Other infrequent adverse reactions include heartburn, nausea, gastralgia, insomnia, anxiety, galactorrhoea, dry mouth, muscle ache, and skin rash.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Safety in pregnancy and lactation has not been established.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Imigra may cause drowsiness, which is worsened by alcohol or other central nervous system depressants. Patients should avoid driving or performing hazardous tasks requiring mental alertness. Imigra is not effective in aborting a migraine attack and should not be used for this purpose. This treatment may cause extrapyramidal and depressive symptoms and reveal Parkinsonism, especially in elderly or predisposed patients. Use with caution in such cases.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Miscellaneous prophylactic migraine preparations.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '514_1742101474.jpg', 62, 12, NULL, '2025-03-16 10:04:34', '2025-03-16 10:04:34'),
(3077, 'Imigra', 'Flunarizine', 5.02, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><ul><li>Flunarizine is used to prevent migraine headaches, whether they are classic (with aura) or common (without aura).</li><li>Treatment for vestibular vertigo symptoms (due to a diagnosed functional disorder of the vestibular system).</li><li>Peripheral Vascular Disease (PVD).</li><li>Motion sickness.</li><li>Conventional antiepileptic treatment is ineffective in treating refractory epilepsy.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Flunarizine is the difluorinated derivative of cinnarizine. It is a selective calcium channel antagonist. By reducing excessive calcium transmembrane influx, it prevents calcium overload in cells. It does not affect normal calcium homeostasis in cells. Flunarizine also has antihistaminic and sedative properties. It binds to plasma protein with a 99% affinity.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Migraine Prophylaxis:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Starting Dose:</strong> 10 mg at night for individuals under 65 years and 5 mg daily for patients over 65 years. If depressive, extrapyramidal, or other undesirable symptoms arise, the medication should be discontinued.</li><li><strong>Maintenance Therapy:</strong> If a patient is responding well and maintenance therapy is required, the dose should be reduced to 5 days per week at the same daily dose with two consecutive medicine-free days. Even if preventive maintenance therapy is effective and well tolerated, it should be discontinued after 6 months and resumed only if necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Peripheral Vascular Disease:</strong> 10 mg twice daily, up to 30 mg per day if needed.</p><p><strong>Vertigo &amp; Motion Sickness:</strong> 10-20 mg per day for adults, 5 mg per day for children over 40 kg.</p><p><strong>Epileptic Seizures:</strong> As an add-on medication, 15-20 mg daily for adults and 5-10 mg daily for children.</p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> Galactorrhoea has been reported in some women taking oral contraceptives during the first two months of therapy with Flunarizine. Carbamazepine and Phenytoin, which are hepatic enzyme inducers, may increase Flunarizine metabolism, requiring a dosage adjustment.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to Flunarizine. Not recommended for individuals with a history of depressive illness or symptoms of Parkinson’s disease or other extrapyramidal disorders.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Drowsiness and/or fatigue, weight gain, and/or increased appetite may occur. Chronic treatment with Flunarizine has been associated with depression, particularly in female patients with a history of depressive illness. Extrapyramidal symptoms such as bradykinesia, rigidity, akathisia, orofacial dyskinesia, and tremor have been reported, especially in elderly patients. Infrequent adverse reactions include heartburn, nausea, gastralgia, insomnia, anxiety, galactorrhoea, dry mouth, muscle ache, and skin rash.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Safety in pregnancy and lactation has not been established.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Flunarizine may cause sleepiness, which is worsened by alcohol or other central nervous system depressants. Patients should avoid driving or performing hazardous tasks requiring mental alertness. Flunarizine is not recommended for stopping a migraine attack, and an attack is not a reason to increase the dosage. This treatment may cause extrapyramidal and depressive symptoms and reveal Parkinsonism, particularly in predisposed individuals such as the elderly. Use with caution in such cases.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Miscellaneous prophylactic migraine preparations.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C in a dry place, protected from light. Do not freeze. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Pharmaceutical Name</strong></p><p> Navana Pharmaceuticals Ltd.</p>', 'active', '1294_1742101668.jpg', 62, 12, NULL, '2025-03-16 10:07:48', '2025-03-16 10:07:48'),
(3078, 'Lactu', 'Lactulose', 140.00, 1, 'Bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Oral Solution', '3.35 gm per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Constipation (Chronic Constipation):</strong> The initial treatment for chronic constipation should include a high-fiber diet (vegetables, salads, fruits, etc.), plenty of liquids, and regular physical activity. Lactulose should only be used if these measures are ineffective.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Disturbances in Intestinal Flora:</strong>When the intestinal flora is damaged, it can lead to various problems (e.g., following long-term antibiotic treatment).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Intestinal disorders and gall bladder illnesses (Colitis, Diverticulosis, Megacolon).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increased blood ammonia levels (Hyperammonemia in hepatopathy, portal-systemic encephalopathy).</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Lactulose is a synthetic disaccharide. It is digested in the colon by saccharolytic bacteria, producing low molecular weight organic acids (primarily lactic acid). This lowers the pH of the colon contents, promotes water retention via osmotic action, and increases peristaltic activity. Since lactulose is only slightly absorbed, the pharmacokinetics of the absorbed substance do not affect the primary therapeutic effect.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>For Constipation (Chronic Constipation):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> Initially 3-6 teaspoons daily; in long-term therapy, 1½-6 teaspoons daily.</li><li><strong>Children up to 14 years:</strong> Initially 3 teaspoons daily; in long-term therapy, 1-2 teaspoons daily.</li><li><strong>Infants and Toddlers:</strong> Initially 1-2 teaspoons daily; in long-term therapy, 1 teaspoon daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For Damaged Intestinal Flora:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 1-2 teaspoons daily.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> 1 teaspoon daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For Reduction of Blood Ammonia Level:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hyperammonemia in Hepatopathy:</strong> Maximum of 18-30 teaspoons daily.</li><li><strong>Portal-Systemic Encephalopathy:</strong> Hourly doses of 6-9 teaspoons of Lactulose solution may be used to induce rapid laxation. Once the laxative effect is achieved, the dose may be reduced.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> There are no notable medication interactions with Lactulose. However, in cases of misuse, potassium depletion can amplify the glycosidic action of cardiac glycosides.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to galactose and/or lactose.</li><li>Patients on a galactose-free diet.</li><li>Gastro-cardial symptom complex.</li><li>Possible intestinal obstruction.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Flatulence, cramps, and abdominal discomfort may occur at the start of treatment but usually resolve by lowering the dose.</li><li>Overdosing may cause diarrhea.</li><li>Electrolyte loss (primarily potassium) is a common side effect in cases of drug misuse.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Lactulose is classified as <strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong> by the US FDA. Studies have shown no negative effects. A registered physician should determine its use during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Patients intolerant to lactulose should take the sugar with caution.</li><li>The dose used in treating (pre) coma hepaticum is usually significantly higher, so diabetics should be aware of this.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Purgatives based on osmotic pressure.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '788_1742102034.jpg', 69, 12, NULL, '2025-03-16 10:13:54', '2025-03-16 10:13:54'),
(3079, 'Imnoc', 'Imipramine Hydrochloride', 4.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Depression</strong></li><li><strong>Panic Disorder</strong></li><li><strong>Nocturnal Enuresis</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Tricyclic &amp; Related Anti-Depressant Drugs</li><li>Tricyclic Anti-Depressant</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Imnoc 25 mg works by inhibiting the neuronal reuptake of norepinephrine and serotonin. It binds to sodium-dependent serotonin and norepinephrine transporters, reducing the reuptake of these neurotransmitters. Depression is linked to a lack of stimulation of the post-synaptic neuron by norepinephrine and serotonin. By slowing their reuptake, their concentration in the synaptic cleft increases, leading to changes in neurotransmission and brain physiology that help relieve depression symptoms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>For Depression:</strong></p><ul><li>Initially up to 75 mg daily in divided doses, gradually increased to 150-200 mg (up to 300 mg in hospital).</li><li>Up to 150 mg may be given as a single dose at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Initially 10 mg daily, gradually increased to 30-50 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> Not recommended for depression.</li></ul><p><strong>For Panic Attack:</strong></p><ul><li>Initially 10-25 mg/day, depending on tolerance.</li><li>The dose is gradually increased to 75-150 mg daily, up to 200 mg if necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>For Nocturnal Enuresis (Children):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>7 years:</strong> 25 mg</li><li><strong>8 to 11 years:</strong> 20-50 mg</li><li><strong>Over 11 years:</strong> 50-75 mg at bedtime</li><li><strong>Maximum treatment duration:</strong> 3 months (including gradual withdrawal). Full physical examination required before further treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> Imnoc 25 mg should not be used in combination with:</p><ul><li><strong>Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAOIs)</strong></li><li><strong>Anticholinergic agents</strong></li><li><strong>Antihypertensive agents</strong></li><li><strong>Methylphenidate</strong></li><li><strong>Levodopa</strong></li><li><strong>Antipsychotic drugs</strong></li><li><strong>Cimetidine</strong></li><li><strong>Barbiturates</strong></li><li><strong>Oral contraceptives</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Recent myocardial infarction</li><li>Arrhythmias (particularly heart block)</li><li>Manic phase of bipolar disorder</li><li>Severe liver disease</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Dry mouth, mild sedation, blurred vision (disturbed accommodation, increased intraocular pressure), constipation, nausea, difficulty with urination.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular:</strong> Arrhythmias, sweating, tremors.</li><li><strong>Skin &amp; Allergy:</strong> Rashes, hypersensitivity reactions (including urticaria &amp; photosensitivity).</li><li><strong>Behavioral:</strong> Behavioral disturbances (especially in children), hypomania or mania (especially in the elderly).</li><li><strong>Metabolic:</strong> Blood sugar changes, increased appetite, weight gain (occasionally weight loss).</li><li><strong>Sexual Dysfunction:</strong> Interference with sexual function.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Should be avoided during pregnancy unless the benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li>Passes into breast milk in small amounts, so gradual withdrawal or discontinuation of breastfeeding is advised.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cardiac diseases</strong> (especially arrhythmias)</li><li><strong>History of epilepsy</strong></li><li><strong>Elderly patients</strong></li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong> (avoid in severe cases)</li><li><strong>Thyroid disease</strong></li><li><strong>Psychotic disorders</strong></li><li><strong>Angle-closure glaucoma</strong></li><li><strong>History of urinary retention</strong></li><li><strong>Concurrent electroconvulsive therapy</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Children are more sensitive to acute overdose, which can be serious or fatal.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul>', 'active', '1516_1742102965.jpg', 8, 12, NULL, '2025-03-16 10:29:25', '2025-03-16 10:29:25');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3080, 'Xytrex', 'Olanzapine', 4.53, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Xytrex 10 mg is indicated for the acute and maintenance treatment of schizophrenia and related psychoses where positive symptoms (e.g. delusions, hallucinations, disordered thinking, hostility, and suspiciousness) and/or negative symptoms (e.g. flattened affect, emotional and social withdrawal, poverty of speech) are prominent. Xytrex 10 mg is indicated for the treatment of acute manic or mixed episodes in bipolar disorder, with or without psychotic features and with or without a rapid cycling course.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Atypical neuroleptic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Xytrex 10 mg is an antipsychotic agent and has affinities for serotonin 5HT2A/2C, 5HT3, 5HT6; dopamine D1, D2, D3, D4, D5; cholinergic muscarinic receptors M1-M5; α1 adrenergic; and histamine H1 receptors. The mechanism of action of Xytrex 10 mg, as with other drugs having efficacy in schizophrenia, is unknown. However, it has been proposed that this drug’s efficacy in schizophrenia is mediated through a combination of dopamine and serotonin type 2 (5HT2) antagonism. Xytrex 10 mg is well absorbed after oral administration, reaching peak plasma concentrations within 5 to 8 hours. The absorption is not affected by food. Xytrex 10 mg is not mutagenic, clastogenic, or carcinogenic.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p>The recommended starting dose for Xytrex 10 mg is 10 mg/day, administered as a single daily dose without regard to meals. Daily dosage may subsequently be adjusted on the basis of individual clinical status within the range of 5-20 mg daily. An increase to a dose greater than the routine therapeutic dose of 10 mg/day (i.e. to a dose of 15 mg/day or greater) is recommended only after appropriate clinical reassessment. When more than one factor is present which might result in slower metabolism (female gender, geriatric age, non-smoking status), consideration should be given to decreasing the starting dose. Dose escalation, when indicated, should be conservative in such patients.</p><p><strong>Dosage</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Schizophrenia, combination therapy for Mania, Bipolar Disorder:</strong> The initial dose is 5-10 mg once daily. Daily dosage may subsequently be adjusted on the basis of individual clinical status within the range of 5-20 mg daily. Dosage greater than 10 mg daily only after reassessment. Maximum dose is 20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Monotherapy for Mania:</strong> Initially 15 mg once daily. Daily dosage may subsequently be adjusted on the basis of individual clinical status within the range of 5-20 mg daily. Dosage greater than 15 mg only after reassessment. Maximum dose is 20 mg daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Xytrex 10 mg can be given without regard to meals. Gradual tapering of the dose should be considered while discontinuing Xytrex 10 mg.</p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>Xytrex 10 mg may antagonize the effects of levodopa and dopamine agonists. Drugs that induce CYP1A2 or glucuronyl transferase enzymes (e.g., Omeprazole and Rifampicin) may increase Xytrex 10 mg clearance. Inhibitors of CYP1A2 may potentially inhibit Xytrex 10 mg elimination. Carbamazepine may increase the clearance of Xytrex 10 mg. Concomitant administration of activated charcoal reduces the oral bioavailability of Xytrex 10 mg by 50-60%. Caution should be taken when Xytrex 10 mg is administered with centrally acting drugs and alcohol.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Xytrex 10 mg is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to any ingredient of the product as well as in patients with known risk for narrow-angle glaucoma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Very common undesirable effects are somnolence and weight gain. Other possible side effects include increased appetite, elevated glucose levels, elevated triglyceride levels, dizziness, akathisia, Parkinson\'s disease, dyskinesia, orthostatic hypotension, mild and transient anticholinergic effects (including constipation and dry mouth), asthenia, edema, and photosensitivity reaction.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Xytrex 10 mg should be used in pregnancy only if the potential benefits justify the potential risk to the fetus. Patients should be advised to notify their physician if they become pregnant or intend to become pregnant during treatment with Xytrex 10 mg. There is no report to show teratogenicity. Patients should not breastfeed if they are taking Xytrex 10 mg.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Xytrex 10 mg should be used cautiously in patients who have a history of seizures or have conditions associated with seizures. It should be used cautiously in patients with low leukocyte and/or neutrophil counts for any reason, drug-induced bone marrow depression/toxicity caused by radiation therapy or chemotherapy, hypereosinophilic conditions, impaired hepatic function, and patients using hepatotoxic medicines, centrally acting drugs, and medicines known to increase QT interval, especially in the elderly. Patients should be cautioned about operating hazardous machinery, including motor vehicles.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children:</strong> Xytrex 10 mg has not been studied in subjects under 18 years of age.</li><li><strong>Elderly patients (age 65 and over):</strong> Starting dose 5 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Patients with hepatic, renal impairment:</strong> Starting dose 5 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Atypical neuroleptic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Xytrex 10 mg is an antipsychotic agent and has affinities for serotonin 5HT2A/2C, 5HT3, 5HT6; dopamine D1, D2, D3, D4, D5; cholinergic muscarinic receptors M1-M5; α1 adrenergic; and histamine H1 receptors. The mechanism of action of Xytrex 10 mg, as with other drugs having efficacy in schizophrenia, is unknown. However, it has been proposed that this drug’s efficacy in schizophrenia is mediated through a combination of dopamine and serotonin type 2 (5HT2) antagonism. Xytrex 10 mg is well absorbed after oral administration, reaching peak plasma concentrations within 5 to 8 hours. The absorption is not affected by food. Xytrex 10 mg is not mutagenic, clastogenic, or carcinogenic.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Xytrex 10 mg should be used in pregnancy only if the potential benefits justify the potential risk to the fetus. Patients should be advised to notify their physician if they become pregnant or intend to become pregnant during treatment with Xytrex 10 mg. There is no report to show teratogenicity. Patients should not breastfeed if they are taking Xytrex 10 mg.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children:</strong> Xytrex 10 mg has not been studied in subjects under 18 years of age.</li><li><strong>Elderly patients (age 65 and over):</strong> Starting dose 5 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Patients with hepatic, renal impairment:</strong> Starting dose 5 mg/day.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1896_1742104526.jpg', 44, 12, NULL, '2025-03-16 10:55:26', '2025-03-16 10:55:26'),
(3081, 'Minomal R', 'Aminophylline', 1.23, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '175 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Minomal R 175 mg</strong></p><p> It is indicated for the treatment and prophylaxis of bronchospasm associated with asthma, emphysema, and chronic bronchitis. Also indicated in adults for the treatment of cardiac asthma and left ventricular or congestive cardiac failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives, Respiratory stimulants: analeptics, Theophylline &amp; related drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Minomal R 175 mg is a combination of theophylline and ethylenediamine. Ethylenediamine is inactive; it increases the solubility of theophylline in water. Theophylline relaxes bronchial smooth muscle. Suggested mechanisms are an increase in intracellular cAMP through inhibition of phosphodiesterase; adenosine receptor antagonism, prostaglandin antagonism, and effects on intracellular calcium.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Minomal R 175 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral:</strong>Chronic bronchospasm:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adult: As hydrate: Initially, 225-450 mg bid, increased if necessary.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child: &gt;3 yr: As modified-release hydrate: 12 mg/kg daily increased to 24 mg/kg daily in 2 divided doses after 1 week.</li><li><strong>Intravenous:</strong>Acute severe bronchospasm:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adult: Loading dose: 5 mg/kg (ideal body weight) or 250-500 mg (25 mg/ml) by slow injection or infusion over 20-30 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Maintenance infusion dose: 0.5 mg/kg/hr. Max rate: 25 mg/min.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Child: Loading dose: same as adult dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Maintenance dose: 6 months-9 years: 1 mg/kg/hr and 10-16 years: 0.8 mg/kg/hr.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Elderly: Dose reduction may be necessary.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hepatic impairment: Dose reduction may be necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Minomal R 175 mg</strong></p><p> Tablets should be swallowed whole and not chewed because of the structure of the tablet.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Minomal R 175 mg should not be administered to patients with hypersensitivity to xanthines or ethylenediamine. It should not be administered to patients with active peptic ulcer, since it may increase the volume and acidity of gastric secretions.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Minomal R 175 mg</strong></p><p> The most common adverse effects are gastric irritation, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, hematemesis, epigastric pain, and tremor. These are usually early signs of toxicity; however, with high doses, ventricular arrhythmias or seizures may be the first signs to appear, along with reactivation of peptic ulcer, headache, irritability, restlessness, insomnia, twitching, convulsion, reflex hyperexcitability, palpitation, tachycardia, hypotension, circulatory failure, ventricular arrhythmias, and flushing, albuminuria, diuresis, and hematuria. Also, inappropriate ADH syndrome may occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Use of Minomal R 175 mg in pregnant women should be balanced against the risk of uncontrolled disease.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Minomal R 175 mg should be given with caution to patients with peptic ulceration, hyperthyroidism, hypertension, cardiac arrhythmias or other cardiovascular disease, or epilepsy, as these conditions may be exacerbated. They should also be given with caution to patients with heart failure, hepatic dysfunction, chronic alcoholism, acute febrile illness, and to neonates and the elderly, since in all of these circumstances, theophylline clearance may be decreased, resulting in increases in serum-theophylline concentrations and serum half-life.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives, Respiratory stimulants: analeptics, Theophylline &amp; related drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Minomal R 175 mg is a combination of theophylline and ethylenediamine. Ethylenediamine is inactive; it increases the solubility of theophylline in water. Theophylline relaxes bronchial smooth muscle. Suggested mechanisms are an increase in intracellular cAMP through inhibition of phosphodiesterase; adenosine receptor antagonism, prostaglandin antagonism, and effects on intracellular calcium.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Use of Minomal R 175 mg in pregnant women should be balanced against the risk of uncontrolled disease.</p>', 'active', '1838_1742107087.jpg', 56, 12, NULL, '2025-03-16 11:38:07', '2025-03-16 11:38:07'),
(3082, 'Ninavir', 'Remdesivir', 3500.00, 1, 'Unit', '100 mg vial', 'IV Infusion', '5 mg per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ninavir 5 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Emergency use of Ninavir 5 mg/ml for treatment of suspected or laboratory-confirmed Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID-19). Severe disease is defined as patients with an oxygen saturation (SpO2) &lt;94% on room air or requiring supplemental oxygen, mechanical ventilation, or extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO). Ninavir 5 mg/ml is authorized only for hospitalized adult and pediatric patients for whom use of an intravenous agent is clinically appropriate.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-viral drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Ninavir 5 mg/ml is a nucleoside analog that inhibits RNA polymerase action. It incorporates into RNA, preventing the addition of further nucleotides, thus terminating RNA transcription. Viruses with mutations in RNA polymerase show partial resistance but are less infective.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ninavir 5 mg/ml</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adult and Pediatric Patients:Loading Dose (Day 1):</strong> 200 mg once daily for patients ≥40 kg, or 5 mg/kg once daily for patients weighing 3.5 kg to &lt;40 kg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance Dose:</strong> 100 mg once daily for patients ≥40 kg, or 2.5 mg/kg once daily for patients weighing 3.5 kg to &lt;40 kg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Treatment Duration:</strong>Patients not requiring invasive mechanical ventilation or ECMO: 5 days (including the loading dose), extendable to 10 days if no improvement.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients requiring mechanical ventilation or ECMO: 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric Dosing:Body Weight ≥40 kg:</strong> Same dosage as adult regimen.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Body Weight 3.5 kg to &lt;40 kg:</strong> 5 mg/kg on Day 1 followed by 2.5 mg/kg daily for 9 days, extendable to 10 days if needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Ninavir 5 mg/ml</strong></p><ul><li>The solution should not be mixed with other medications.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Infusion Rate:</strong>250 ml infusion bag: 30 min (8.33 ml/min), 60 min (4.17 ml/min), or 120 min (2.08 ml/min).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">100 ml infusion bag: 30 min (3.33 ml/min), 60 min (1.67 ml/min), or 120 min (0.83 ml/min).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Ninavir 5 mg/ml.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ninavir 5 mg/ml</strong></p><ul><li>Increased liver transaminases.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Use only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the mother and fetus.</li><li>No information on the presence in human milk or its effects on breastfed infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Limited clinical data available. Serious, unexpected adverse events may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Lyophilized Powder:</strong> Store at temperatures ≤30°C in a dry place, away from light.</li><li><strong>After Reconstitution:</strong> Store at room temperature (20°C-25°C) for up to 4 hours or refrigerated (2°C-8°C) for up to 24 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Pharmacokinetics in renal or hepatic impairment have not been evaluated. Use only if the benefit outweighs the risk.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Anti-viral drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Ninavir 5 mg/ml is an adenosine nucleotide prodrug, metabolized to its active triphosphate form, which competes with ATP for incorporation into RNA, causing delayed chain termination during viral RNA replication. It is a weak inhibitor of mammalian DNA and RNA polymerase.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>No adequate studies on use in pregnant women. Use only if the potential benefits justify the risks to the mother and fetus.</li></ul>', 'active', '1960_1742107371.jpg', 2, 12, NULL, '2025-03-16 11:42:51', '2025-03-16 11:42:51'),
(3083, 'Mazic', 'Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate', 35.00, 1, 'Bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '10 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Mazic 100 ml</strong></p><p>Mazic 100 ml is indicated in zinc deficiency and/or zinc-losing conditions. Zinc deficiency can occur due to inadequate diet or malabsorption. Excessive zinc loss can happen in trauma, burns, diarrhea, and protein-losing conditions. A zinc supplement is given until clinical improvement occurs but may need to be continued in severe malabsorption, metabolic disease, or zinc-losing states.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zinc sulfate monohydrate is an essential trace element involved in various body enzyme systems. The body needs zinc for normal growth and health. Zinc is also vital for sexual maturation, reproduction, dark vision adaptation, olfactory and gustatory activity, insulin storage and release, and host immune defenses. Zinc deficiency can lead to impaired immune function, delayed wound healing, a decrease in the sense of taste and smell, a reduced ability to fight infections, poor night vision, increased risk of abortion, alopecia, mental lethargy, skin changes, and poor development of reproductive organs.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Mazic 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Child under 10 kg:</strong> 5 ml (1 teaspoonful) 2 times daily after food.</li><li><strong>Child between 10-30 kg:</strong> 10 ml (2 teaspoonfuls) 1-3 times daily after food.</li><li><strong>Adults and child over 30 kg:</strong> 20 ml (4 teaspoonfuls) 1-3 times daily after food.</li><li>This drug is most effective when taken at least 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals. However, if stomach upset occurs, it may be taken with a meal.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Mazic 100 ml</strong></p><p>For dispersible tablet:</p><ul><li>Place the tablet in a teaspoon.</li><li>Add an adequate amount of water.</li><li>Let the tablet dissolve completely.</li><li>Give the entire spoonful solution.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Mazic 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant intake of a tetracycline and zinc may decrease the absorption of both the tetracycline and zinc.</li><li>Similarly, concomitant administration of zinc and quinolone drugs may also decrease the absorption of both.</li><li>Concomitant intake of penicillamine and zinc may decrease the absorption of zinc.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in those who are hypersensitive to any component of the ingredients of this preparation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Mazic 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Zinc may cause nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, stomach upset, heartburn, and gastritis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The safety of this product during human pregnancy has not been established. Zinc crosses the placenta and is present in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In acute renal failure, zinc accumulation may occur in the body, so dose adjustment is needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul>', 'active', '1330_1742107705.jpg', 6, 12, NULL, '2025-03-16 11:48:25', '2025-03-24 17:14:28'),
(3084, 'Cutin', 'Biotin', 40.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '5000 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p> Cutin 5000 mcg is indicated for hair loss, weak, brittle, splitting or soft nails, eczema, and dermatitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Herbal and Nutraceuticals</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cutin 5000 mcg is an essential vitamin, also known as vitamin H. It is involved in vital physiological functions like fatty acid synthesis, amino acid catabolism, and gluconeogenesis. It acts as a cofactor in the enzymatic carboxylation of pyruvate and acetyl-CoA. Cutin 5000 mcg influences the growth and differentiation of epidermal cells, making it crucial for the formation and renewal of the skin, hair, and nails. It functions as a coenzyme for mitochondrial carboxylases in hair roots and improves keratin structure. Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency may lead to hair loss and a variety of systemic symptoms such as dermatitis and aciduria. Cutin 5000 mcg is one of the most prescribed nutritional supplements for any kind of hair loss.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p> The dosage of Cutin 5000 mcg varies depending on the disease condition and age group. Generally, 1-3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician. In children, Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency affects those between 1 week and 2 years of age, often exhibiting seizures, hypotonia, developmental delay, ataxia, hyperventilation, and coma. In this case, 5-10 tablets daily should be crushed and mixed with water or fruit juice, or as advised by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>There are indications that anticonvulsant drugs may lower the plasma level of Cutin 5000 mcg.</li><li>Antibiotic use may decrease the Cutin 5000 mcg contribution to the body made by the microflora of the large intestine.</li><li>Excessive consumption of raw egg whites interferes with Cutin 5000 mcg absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Sufficient clinical data is not available to use in pregnant women and lactating mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1294_1742113881.jpg', 29, 12, NULL, '2025-03-16 13:31:21', '2025-03-16 13:31:21'),
(3085, 'Glyset Mix Vial', 'Protamine Crystallised Insulin Aspart', 450.00, 1, 'Vial', '1 piece in a box', 'Vial', '100 IU/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>Cutin 5000 mcg is indicated for hair loss, weak, brittle, splitting or soft nails, eczema, and dermatitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Herbal and Nutraceuticals</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cutin 5000 mcg is an essential vitamin, also known as vitamin H. It is involved in vital physiological functions like fatty acid synthesis, amino acid catabolism, and gluconeogenesis. It acts as a cofactor in the enzymatic carboxylation of pyruvate and acetyl-CoA. Cutin 5000 mcg influences the growth and differentiation of epidermal cells, making it crucial for the formation and renewal of the skin, hair, and nails. It functions as a coenzyme for mitochondrial carboxylases in hair roots and improves keratin structure. Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency may lead to hair loss and a variety of systemic symptoms such as dermatitis and aciduria. Cutin 5000 mcg is one of the most prescribed nutritional supplements for any kind of hair loss.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>The dosage of Cutin 5000 mcg varies depending on the disease condition and age group. Generally, 1-3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician. In children, Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency affects those between 1 week and 2 years of age, often exhibiting seizures, hypotonia, developmental delay, ataxia, hyperventilation, and coma. In this case, 5-10 tablets daily should be crushed and mixed with water or fruit juice, or as advised by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>There are indications that anticonvulsant drugs may lower the plasma level of Cutin 5000 mcg.</li><li>Antibiotic use may decrease the Cutin 5000 mcg contribution to the body made by the microflora of the large intestine.</li><li>Excessive consumption of raw egg whites interferes with Cutin 5000 mcg absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Sufficient clinical data is not available to use in pregnant women and lactating mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1335_1742125147.webp', 2, 12, NULL, '2025-03-16 16:39:07', '2025-03-16 16:39:07'),
