(3096, 'Calcin-O DS', 'Calcium Orotate Dihydrate', 12.00, 12, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '740 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>Cutin 5000 mcg is indicated for hair loss, weak, brittle, splitting or soft nails, eczema, and dermatitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Herbal and Nutraceuticals</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cutin 5000 mcg is an essential vitamin, also known as vitamin H. It is involved in vital physiological functions like fatty acid synthesis, amino acid catabolism, and gluconeogenesis. It acts as a cofactor in the enzymatic carboxylation of pyruvate and acetyl-CoA. Cutin 5000 mcg influences the growth and differentiation of epidermal cells, making it crucial for the formation and renewal of the skin, hair, and nails. It functions as a coenzyme for mitochondrial carboxylases in hair roots and improves keratin structure. Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency may lead to hair loss and a variety of systemic symptoms such as dermatitis and aciduria. Cutin 5000 mcg is one of the most prescribed nutritional supplements for any kind of hair loss.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>The dosage of Cutin 5000 mcg varies depending on the disease condition and age group. Generally, 1-3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician. In children, Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency affects those between 1 week and 2 years of age, often exhibiting seizures, hypotonia, developmental delay, ataxia, hyperventilation, and coma. In this case, 5-10 tablets daily should be crushed and mixed with water or fruit juice, or as advised by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>There are indications that anticonvulsant drugs may lower the plasma level of Cutin 5000 mcg.</li><li>Antibiotic use may decrease the Cutin 5000 mcg contribution to the body made by the microflora of the large intestine.</li><li>Excessive consumption of raw egg whites interferes with Cutin 5000 mcg absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Sufficient clinical data is not available to use in pregnant women and lactating mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '548_1742126115.jpg', 6, 12, NULL, '2025-03-16 16:55:15', '2025-03-16 16:55:15'),
(3097, 'Rotacal', 'Calcium Orotate', 8.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rotacal 400 mg</strong></p><p> Calcium Orotate is used:</p><ul><li>To prevent or to treat low blood Calcium levels</li><li>Hypoparathyroidism</li><li>Latent tetany</li><li>Hip joint plastic surgery</li><li>Many inflammatory diseases like Arthritis, Spondylitis, etc.</li><li>Pregnant women &amp; nursing mothers</li><li>Premenstrual syndrome (PMS)</li><li>Helps in controlling weight by suppressing the habit of frequent appetite</li><li>Mood swings</li><li>Cognitive enhancement</li><li>Protects the heart by enhancing the efficiency of cardiac muscles</li><li>To treat conditions caused by low Calcium levels, such as:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Bone loss (Osteoporosis, Postmenopausal Osteoporosis, Juvenile Osteoporosis, Senile Osteoporosis)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Osteomalacia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rickets</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Minerals in bone formation, Specific mineral preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Calcium Orotate is a Calcium supplement with a functional amino acid chelating ligand—Orotic acid. Orotic acid assists the transport of Calcium through cellular membrane structures, facilitating the intracellular uptake of Calcium, particularly in bone. Calcium Orotate also helps in the maintenance of healthy cartilage. Furthermore, Orotate is involved in the synthesis of DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) and RNA (ribonucleic acid). Calcium Orotate has a high affinity to penetrate complex cell membranes.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rotacal 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calcium Orotate 400 mg</strong>: As an addition to the daily diet, 2-3 tablets are usually recommended with a meal, after a meal, or as directed by a physician.</li><li><strong>Calcium Orotate 740 mg</strong>: As an addition to the daily diet, 1-2 tablets are usually recommended with a meal, after a meal, or as directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Rotacal 400 mg</strong></p><p> Calcium can decrease the absorption of the following drugs when taken together:</p><ul><li>Bisphosphonates</li><li>Quinolone antibiotics</li><li>Tetracycline antibiotics</li><li>Levothyroxine</li><li>Phenytoin</li><li>Tiludronate disodium</li></ul><p>Thiazide-type diuretics can interact with Calcium supplements, increasing the risks of hypercalcemia and hypercalciuria.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Calcium Orotate is contraindicated in conditions such as:</p><ul><li>Kidney stones</li><li>Kidney disease</li><li>Increased activity of the parathyroid gland</li><li>High amounts of Calcium in urine &amp; blood</li><li>Extreme loss of body water</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Rotacal 400 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects:</p><ul><li>Bloating and swelling in the abdomen</li></ul><p>Infrequent side effects:</p><ul><li>Constipation</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Headache</li><li>Increased thirst/urination</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> During pregnancy and lactation, treatment with Calcium Orotate should always be under the direction of a physician. Pregnant and breastfeeding women need more Calcium. Pregnancy-related high blood pressure is a common and serious risk for women and their babies. Taking a supplement of Calcium Orotate can help reduce this risk.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Before taking Calcium Orotate, precautions are needed if the patient has any allergies. This product may contain inactive ingredients that can cause allergic reactions or other problems. Precaution is needed before using this product in cases of:</p><ul><li>Kidney disease</li><li>Kidney stones</li><li>Little or no stomach acid (achlorhydria)</li><li>Heart disease</li><li>Disease of the pancreas</li><li>Certain lung diseases (sarcoidosis)</li><li>Difficulty absorbing nutrition from food (malabsorption syndrome)</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rotacal 400 mg</strong></p><p> Symptoms of overdose may include:</p><ul><li>Nausea/Vomiting</li><li>Weight loss</li><li>Loss of appetite</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Headache</li><li>Weakness</li></ul><p>If any of the side effects become serious, consult a physician.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30ºC. Protect from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1649_1742180352.jpg', 44, 11, 'fazlearabbi97@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 07:59:12', '2025-03-17 07:59:12'),
(3098, 'Lopadine', 'Olopatadine Hydrochloride', 110.00, 1, 'Drop', '5 ml drop', 'Drop', '0.1 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Lopadine 0.1%</strong></p><p> Olopatadine is indicated for the treatment of ocular itching associated with allergic conjunctivitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic Non-Steroid drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Olopatadine is an inhibitor of the release of histamine from the mast cell and a relatively selective histamine H1‐antagonist that inhibits the in vivo and in vitro type 1 immediate hypersensitivity reaction including inhibition of histamine-induced effects on human conjunctival epithelial cells. Olopatadine is devoid of effects on alpha adrenergic, dopamine, and muscarinic type 1 and 2 receptors. Following topical ocular administration in humans, olopatadine was shown to have low systemic exposure.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Lopadine 0.1%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>0.1% Sterile Eye Drops</strong>: One drop in each affected eye two times per day at an interval of 6 to 8 hours.</li><li><strong>0.2% Sterile Eye Drops</strong>: One drop in the affected eye once a day.</li><li><strong>0.7% Sterile Eye Drops</strong>: One drop in each affected eye once a day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Lopadine 0.1%</strong></p><p> May result in additive CNS depression with CNS depressants.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Lopadine 0.1% ophthalmic solution is contraindicated in persons with a known hypersensitivity to Lopadine 0.1%.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Lopadine 0.1%</strong></p><p> Headaches have been reported at an incidence of 7%. The following adverse experiences have been reported in less than 5% of patients: asthenia, blurred vision, burning or stinging, cold syndrome, dry eye, foreign body sensation, hyperemia, hypersensitivity, keratitis, lid edema, nausea, pharyngitis, pruritus, rhinitis, sinusitis, and taste perversion.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal studies are not always predictive of human responses, this drug should be used in pregnant women only if the potential benefit to the mother justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether topical ocular administration could result in sufficient systemic absorption to produce detectable quantities in human breast milk. Nevertheless, caution should be exercised when Lopadine 0.1% ophthalmic solution is administered to a nursing mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Olopatadine HCl ophthalmic solution should not be used to treat contact lens-related irritation. Patients who wear soft contact lenses should be instructed to wait at least ten minutes after instilling Lopadine 0.1% ophthalmic solution before they insert their contact lenses.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Lopadine 0.1%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Drowsiness in adults and, initially, agitation and restlessness, followed by drowsiness in children.</li><li><strong>Management</strong>: Symptomatic or supportive treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C in a cool and dry place protected from light. Keep out of reach of children. Do not touch the dropper tip to surfaces since this may contaminate the solution. Do not use after 30 days of first opening.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No overall differences in safety or effectiveness have been observed between elderly and younger patients.</li></ul>', 'active', '1323_1742189647.jpg', 2, 12, 'fazlearabbi97@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 10:34:07', '2025-03-17 10:34:07'),
(3099, 'Luminor', 'Brimonidine Tartrate', 115.00, 1, 'Drop', '5 ml drop', 'Drop', '0.025%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Luminor 0.025%</strong></p><p>Luminor 0.025% 0.2% ophthalmic solution is indicated for lowering intraocular pressure in patients with open-angle glaucoma or ocular hypertension. Luminor 0.025% 0.15% ophthalmic solution is indicated for the control of intraocular pressure in patients with chronic open-angle glaucoma or ocular hypertension. Luminor 0.025% 0.025% ophthalmic solution relieves redness of the eye due to minor eye irritations.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for miotics and glaucoma</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Brimonidine tartrate is a relatively selective alpha-2 adrenergic receptor agonist that, in radioligand binding assays and in functional assays, is approximately 1000 times more selective for the alpha-2 adrenoceptor than the alpha-1 adrenoceptor. This selectivity results in the absence of vasoconstriction in microvessels associated with human retinal xenografts. Topical administration of brimonidine decreases IOP in humans. When used as directed, Luminor 0.025% ophthalmic solutions reduce elevated IOP with minimal effect on cardiovascular parameters. Brimonidine tartrate has a rapid onset of action, with the peak ocular hypotensive effect occurring at approximately two hours post-dosing. The duration of effect is 12 hours or greater. Fluorophotometric studies in animals and humans suggest that Luminor 0.025% has a dual mechanism of action. Luminor 0.025% ophthalmic solution 0.15% lowers IOP by reducing aqueous humor production and increasing uveoscleral outflow.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Luminor 0.025%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>0.2% ophthalmic solution</strong>: One drop in the affected eye(s) three times daily, approximately 8 hours apart. May be used with other topical ophthalmic drugs to lower IOP, ensuring a 5-minute gap between applications.</li><li><strong>0.15% ophthalmic solution</strong>: One drop in the affected eye(s) three times daily, approximately 8 hours apart.</li><li><strong>0.025% ophthalmic solution</strong>: Instill 1 drop in the affected eye(s) every 6-8 hours, not exceeding 4 times daily. Ensure a 5-minute interval if using multiple ophthalmic products.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Luminor 0.025%</strong></p><p>Avoid contact between the dropper tip and the eye or surrounding structures to prevent contamination and potential infections. Contaminated solutions can cause serious eye damage and vision loss. If experiencing any eye condition or undergoing surgery, consult a doctor before continuing use.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Luminor 0.025%</strong></p><p>Although specific drug interaction studies have not been conducted, caution is advised when using Luminor 0.025% ophthalmic solution with:</p><ul><li><strong>CNS depressants</strong> (alcohol, barbiturates, opiates, sedatives, anesthetics) due to potential additive effects.</li><li><strong>Alpha-agonists</strong>, which may reduce pulse and blood pressure; caution is advised with beta-blockers (ophthalmic and systemic), antihypertensives, and cardiac glycosides.</li><li><strong>Tricyclic antidepressants</strong>, which may blunt the hypotensive effect of systemic clonidine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Luminor 0.025% ophthalmic solution is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to Luminor 0.025% and in those receiving monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitor therapy.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Luminor 0.025%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common (10-30%)</strong>: Oral dryness, ocular hyperemia, burning/stinging, headache, blurred vision, foreign body sensation, fatigue/drowsiness, conjunctival follicles, ocular allergies, ocular pruritus.</li><li><strong>Less Common (3-9%)</strong>: Corneal staining/erosion, photophobia, eyelid erythema, ocular pain, dry eyes, tearing, respiratory symptoms, eyelid edema, conjunctival edema, dizziness, blepharitis, gastrointestinal symptoms, asthenia, abnormal vision, muscular pain.</li><li><strong>Rare (&lt;3%)</strong>: Lid crusting, conjunctival hemorrhage, abnormal taste, insomnia, conjunctival discharge, depression, hypertension, anxiety, palpitations, nasal dryness, syncope.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>There are no adequate studies in pregnant women. Animal studies suggest that brimonidine crosses the placenta but in limited amounts. Luminor 0.025% should only be used during pregnancy if the potential benefit outweighs the risk. Since it is unknown whether this drug is excreted in human milk, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised when treating patients with severe cardiovascular disease, depression, cerebral or coronary insufficiency, Raynaud\'s phenomenon, orthostatic hypotension, or thromboangiitis obliterans.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry place protected from light. Keep out of reach of children. Avoid touching the dropper tip to surfaces to prevent contamination. Do not use after 30 days of first opening.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatric patients (&gt;65 years old)</strong>: No significant differences in safety and efficacy.</li><li><strong>Children under 2 years</strong>: Contraindicated.</li><li><strong>Children (2-18 years)</strong>: Not recommended for use.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Drugs for miotics and glaucoma</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Brimonidine is an <strong>α-2 adrenoreceptor agonist</strong> that selectively targets α-2 over α-1 receptors. It reduces intraocular pressure (IOP) by decreasing aqueous humor production and increasing uveoscleral outflow. The peak effect occurs around <strong>2 hours post-dosing</strong>, with effects lasting <strong>12 hours or more</strong>.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>No well-controlled studies exist for pregnant women. Animal studies suggest minimal fetal exposure. Luminor 0.025% ophthalmic solution 0.2% should be used during pregnancy only if the benefits outweigh the potential risks. In animal studies, Luminor 0.025% was found in breast milk, so a decision should be made whether to discontinue breastfeeding or the drug.</p>', 'active', '96_1742190201.jpg', 17, 12, 'fazlearabbi97@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 10:43:21', '2025-03-24 17:12:02'),
(3100, 'Ramipro', 'Ramipril', 5.04, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ramipro 2.5 mg</strong></p><p> Ramipro 2.5 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: To lower blood pressure, either as monotherapy or in combination with other antihypertensive agents.</li><li><strong>Congestive Heart Failure</strong>: Can be used alone or with diuretics.</li><li><strong>Post-Myocardial Infarction Therapy</strong>: For patients with clinical signs of congestive heart failure within the first few days after an acute myocardial infarction.</li><li><strong>Nephropathy Treatment</strong>: Suitable for both diabetic and non-diabetic nephropathy.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Risk Reduction</strong>: Helps reduce the risk of myocardial infarction, stroke, or cardiovascular death in high-risk patients, including those with coronary heart disease, a history of stroke, peripheral vascular disease, or diabetes mellitus with additional cardiovascular risk factors.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Ramipro 2.5 mg is an ACE inhibitor that, after hydrolysis to its active form, blocks the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, a vasoconstrictor. This leads to decreased vasopressor activity and aldosterone secretion, resulting in lower blood pressure and improved management of heart failure. It is effective in reducing the risk of stroke, myocardial infarction, and cardiovascular-related deaths.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ramipro 2.5 mg</strong></p><p> Dosage should be adjusted based on patient tolerance and response:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initial dose: 1.25 - 2.5 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 2.5 - 20 mg daily as a single dose or in two divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If BP is uncontrolled, a diuretic may be added.</li><li><strong>Congestive Heart Failure (Post-Myocardial Infarction)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Start 2 days after infarction with 2.5 mg twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If hypotension occurs, reduce to 1.25 mg twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Target dose: 5 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Prevention of Cardiovascular Events</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Week 1: 2.5 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Weeks 2-4: 5 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: Up to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initial dose: 1.25 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum dose: 5 mg daily (hypertension) or 2.5 mg twice daily (heart failure).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Ramipro 2.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Swallow tablets with sufficient liquid without crushing or chewing.</li><li>Can be taken before, during, or after meals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ramipro 2.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: May cause serious hypotension and hyperkalemia (with potassium-sparing diuretics).</li><li><strong>Lithium</strong>: May increase serum lithium concentration.</li><li><strong>NSAIDs</strong>: Can reduce antihypertensive effects and worsen renal function.</li><li><strong>Alcohol</strong>: May enhance the BP-lowering effect, affecting alertness and coordination.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Ramipro 2.5 mg should not be used in:</p><ul><li>Patients allergic to Ramipro 2.5 mg or other ACE inhibitors.</li><li>History of angioedema.</li><li>Concomitant use with sacubitril/valsartan.</li><li>Patients with renal artery stenosis or severe hypotension.</li><li>Combination with aliskiren in diabetic patients or those with severe renal impairment.</li><li>Combination with angiotensin II receptor antagonists in diabetic nephropathy.</li><li>Pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ramipro 2.5 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include dizziness, headache, fatigue, hypotension, cough, nausea, diarrhea, rash, and anxiety. Rare side effects include angioedema, anaphylactic reactions, and hyperkalemia.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>If pregnancy is detected, discontinue Ramipro 2.5 mg immediately unless deemed life-saving.</li><li>Not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Use with caution in patients with renal impairment, hyperkalemia, hypotension, and liver dysfunction.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ramipro 2.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Severe hypotension, shock, bradycardia, electrolyte imbalance, renal failure.</li><li><strong>Management</strong>: Gastric lavage, adsorbents, fluid/salt replacement, and α1-adrenergic agonists (e.g., norepinephrine).</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Use only on prescription.</li></ul><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Start with a lower dose (1.25 mg daily).</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Close medical supervision required; max dose 2.5 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Not established.</li><li><strong>Patients on Diuretics</strong>: Consider reducing or stopping diuretics 2-3 days before starting Ramipro 2.5 mg.</li></ul>', 'active', '993_1742192798.jpg', 37, 12, 'fazlearabbi97@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 11:26:38', '2025-03-17 11:26:38'),
(3101, 'Uptiva', 'Armodafinil', 15.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '150 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Uptiva 150 mg</strong></p><p> Uptiva 150 mg is indicated to improve wakefulness in adult patients with:</p><ul><li>Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA)</li><li>Narcolepsy</li><li>Shift work disorder (SWD)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> CNS stimulant drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Uptiva 150 mg is an indirect dopamine receptor agonist. This is the R-enantiomer of Modafinil, which is a 1:1 mixture of the R- and S-enantiomers. Uptiva 150 mg binds to the dopamine transporter and inhibits dopamine reuptake. As a result, it increases neuronal activity in the hypothalamus and enhances activity in the hypothalamic wakefulness center (TMN, tuberomammillary nucleus) within the hypothalamic sleep-wake switch.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Uptiva 150 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA) &amp; Narcolepsy:</strong> 150 mg to 250 mg as a single dose in the morning.</li><li><strong>Shift Work Disorder (SWD):</strong> 150 mg as a single dose approximately 1 hour before the start of the work shift.</li></ul><p><strong>Children:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients under 17 years of age have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Elderly:</strong></p><ul><li>In elderly patients, elimination of Uptiva 150 mg and its metabolites may be reduced due to aging. Therefore, lower doses and close monitoring should be considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Uptiva 150 mg</strong></p><ul><li>The clearance of drugs that are substrates for CYP3A4 or CYP3A5 (e.g., steroidal contraceptives, Cyclosporine, Midazolam, and Triazolam) may be increased, leading to lower systemic exposure. Dosage adjustments should be considered when used with Uptiva 150 mg.</li><li>Elimination of drugs that are substrates for CYP2C19 (e.g., Phenytoin, Diazepam, Propranolol, Omeprazole, and Clomipramine) may be prolonged, leading to higher systemic exposure. Dosage adjustments should be considered.</li><li>More frequent monitoring of prothrombin times/International Normalized Ratio (INR) should be considered when co-administering Uptiva 150 mg with Warfarin.</li><li>Caution is advised when administering Monoamine Oxidase (MAO) inhibitors with Uptiva 150 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Uptiva 150 mg or any of its excipients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Uptiva 150 mg</strong></p><p> The most common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Serious rash, including Stevens-Johnson syndrome</li><li>Angioedema and anaphylaxis reactions</li><li>Multi-organ hypersensitivity reactions</li><li>Persistent sleepiness</li><li>Psychiatric symptoms</li><li>Cardiovascular events</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Uptiva 150 mg should be used only if the potential benefit justifies the risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is unknown whether Uptiva 150 mg or its metabolites are excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administering Uptiva 150 mg to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Patients should be cautious while operating vehicles or hazardous machinery until they are certain Uptiva 150 mg does not impair their abilities.</li><li>Caution should be exercised in patients with a history of psychosis, depression, or mania. Treatment should be discontinued if psychiatric symptoms develop.</li><li>Heart rate and blood pressure should be monitored regularly.</li><li>Patients with known cardiovascular disease should take Uptiva 150 mg with caution.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Uptiva 150 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No overdoses were reported in clinical studies.</li><li>Symptoms of overdose may be similar to those of Modafinil, including excitation, agitation, insomnia, and mild to moderate elevations in hemodynamic parameters.</li><li>There is no specific antidote; treatment should focus on supportive care.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool (below 25°C) and dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Patients with hepatic impairment:</strong> In severe cases, the dose should be reduced.</li><li><strong>Patients with renal impairment:</strong> Safety and efficacy in severe renal impairment are not well established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> CNS stimulant drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Uptiva 150 mg is an indirect dopamine receptor agonist. It binds to the dopamine transporter, inhibiting dopamine reuptake. This increases neuronal activity in the hypothalamus and enhances wakefulness by stimulating the hypothalamic wakefulness center (TMN, tuberomammillary nucleus).</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hepatic impairment:</strong> Uptiva 150 mg should be administered at a reduced dose in severe cases.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> There is inadequate information on safety and efficacy in patients with severe renal impairment.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '965_1742193883.jpeg', 2, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 11:44:43', '2025-03-17 11:44:43'),
(3102, 'Simplovir', 'Acyclovir', 14.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Simplovir 200 mg</strong></p><p> Simplovir 200 mg cream is a herpes simplex virus (HSV) nucleoside analogue DNA polymerase inhibitor indicated for the treatment of recurrent herpes labialis (cold sores) in immunocompetent adults and adolescents aged 12 years and older.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Topical Antiviral Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Simplovir 200 mg is an antiviral drug active against herpes simplex virus. It is a synthetic purine nucleoside analogue with in vitro and in vivo inhibitory activity against HSV types 1 (HSV-1) and 2 (HSV-2). The inhibitory activity of Simplovir 200 mg is highly selective due to its affinity for the enzyme thymidine kinase (TK) encoded by HSV. This viral enzyme converts Simplovir 200 mg into its monophosphate form, which is further converted into diphosphate and triphosphate by cellular enzymes. In cell culture, Simplovir 200 mg triphosphate inhibits herpes viral DNA replication through:</p><ol><li>Competitive inhibition of viral DNA polymerase</li><li>Incorporation into and termination of the growing viral DNA chain</li><li>Inactivation of viral DNA polymerase</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Simplovir 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Simplovir 200 mg cream should be applied five times per day for four days.</li><li>Therapy should be initiated as early as possible following the onset of signs or symptoms of herpes labialis (during the prodrome or when lesions appear).</li><li><strong>Adolescents (12 years and older):</strong> The dosage is the same as in adults.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Simplovir 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Clinical experience has not identified any drug interactions with Simplovir 200 mg cream when administered topically or systemically.</li><li>Due to minimal systemic absorption, systemic drug interactions are unlikely.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Simplovir 200 mg, valacyclovir, or any component of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Simplovir 200 mg</strong></p><p> The most common adverse reactions at the application site include:</p><ul><li>Dry lips</li><li>Skin desquamation</li><li>Dryness of skin</li><li>Cracked lips</li><li>Burning sensation on the skin</li><li>Pruritus</li><li>Flakiness of skin</li><li>Stinging sensation</li></ul><p>Each adverse reaction occurred in less than 1% of patients receiving Simplovir 200 mg cream or placebo. Three patients on Simplovir 200 mg cream and one patient on placebo discontinued treatment due to adverse events.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy (Category B):</strong> No adequate and well-controlled studies are available in pregnant women. Use during pregnancy should be considered only if the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is unknown whether topically applied Simplovir 200 mg is excreted in breast milk. However, systemic exposure following topical administration is minimal.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Simplovir 200 mg cream should be applied only to the affected external areas of the lips and face.</li><li>Application to human mucous membranes is not recommended due to a lack of data.</li><li>Intended for <strong>cutaneous use only</strong>; do not use in the eyes, inside the mouth, or inside the nose.</li><li>The cream has the potential for irritation and contact reactions.</li><li>The effectiveness of Simplovir 200 mg cream has not been established in immunocompromised patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at room temperature between <strong>20°C to 25°C</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li>An open-label, uncontrolled trial was conducted with Simplovir 200 mg cream (5%) in 113 patients aged <strong>12 to 17 years</strong> with recurrent herpes labialis.</li><li>The dosage regimen was the same as in adults, and the safety profile was similar to that observed in adults.</li><li>Safety and effectiveness in <strong>pediatric patients under 12 years of age have not been established</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li>Clinical studies did not include sufficient subjects aged <strong>65 and older</strong> to determine whether they respond differently from younger patients.</li><li>Other reported clinical experience has not identified significant differences in responses between elderly and younger patients.</li><li>Systemic absorption after topical administration is minimal.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1880_1742194278.jpg', 2, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 11:51:18', '2025-03-17 11:51:18'),
(3103, 'Galfin', 'Fluconazole', 9.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong><span class=\"ql-cursor\">﻿</span>Galfin 50 mg Overview</strong></p><p> Galfin 50 mg is a member of the triazole class of antifungal agents. It is a potent and selective inhibitor of fungal enzymes necessary for the synthesis of ergosterol. Galfin 50 mg is indicated for vaginal candidiasis (acute or recurrent) and candidal balanitis. The treatment of partners who present with symptomatic genital candidiasis should be considered.</p><p><strong>Mucosal Candidiasis</strong></p><p> Includes oropharyngeal, esophageal, noninvasive bronchopulmonary infections, candiduria, mucocutaneous, and chronic oral atrophic candidiasis (denture sore mouth). Normal hosts and patients with compromised immune function may be treated.</p><p><strong>Dermatophytic and Systemic Fungal Infections</strong></p><p> Galfin 50 mg is also indicated for tinea pedis, tinea cruris, tinea versicolor, and dermal candidal infections, including nail fungal infections. It is used for systemic candidiasis, including candidaemia, disseminated candidiasis, and other forms of invasive candidal infections of the peritoneum, endocardium, and pulmonary and urinary tracts. Candidal infections in patients with malignancy, in intensive care units, or those receiving cytotoxic or immunosuppressive therapy may be treated.</p><p><strong>Cryptococcosis</strong></p><p> Including cryptococcal meningitis and infections of other sites (e.g., pulmonary, cutaneous), may be treated in normal hosts and patients with acquired immune deficiency syndrome (AIDS), organ transplants, or other causes of immunosuppression. Galfin 50 mg can be used as maintenance therapy to prevent relapse of cryptococcal disease in patients with AIDS.</p><p><strong>Prevention of Fungal Infections</strong></p><p> For the prevention of fungal infections in immunocompromised patients considered at risk due to neutropenia following cytotoxic chemotherapy or radiotherapy, including bone marrow transplant patients, Galfin 50 mg is used. Other uses include fungal urinary tract infections, disseminated candidiasis, prophylaxis for fungal infection in neutropenic cancer patients, and acute treatment of other systemic fungal infections such as coccidioidomycosis and histoplasmosis.</p><p><strong>Mechanism of Action</strong></p><p> Galfin 50 mg belongs to the therapeutic class of drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses. It is a triazole antifungal agent that inhibits fungal cytochrome P-450 dependent enzymes. The cytochrome P-450 enzyme system is an essential component of the fungal cell membrane responsible for the synthesis of ergosterol.</p><p><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults (Oral):</strong></p><ul><li>150 mg as a single dose for vaginal candidiasis.</li><li>For oropharyngeal candidiasis, 200 mg is given on the first day, followed by 100 mg once daily for at least two weeks.</li><li>For esophageal candidiasis, 200 mg is given on the first day, followed by 100 mg once daily, with doses up to 400 mg/day if necessary. Treatment should last a minimum of three weeks and continue for at least two weeks after symptom resolution.</li><li>For systemic candida infections, the optimal dosage and duration have not been established, but doses of up to 400 mg daily have been used.</li><li>Urinary tract infections caused by candida and peritonitis are treated with 50-200 mg daily.</li><li>Cryptococcal meningitis is treated with 400 mg on the first day, followed by 200 mg once daily.</li><li>For prophylaxis in patients undergoing bone marrow transplantation, 400 mg once daily is administered.</li></ul><p><strong>Children (Oral):</strong></p><ul><li>Doses of 3-6 mg/kg daily have been used, with doses up to 12 mg/kg recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Intravenous Administration</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>Invasive candidal infections and cryptococcal infections, including meningitis, are treated with 400 mg initially, then 200 mg daily, increasing if necessary to 400 mg daily. Treatment should continue for at least 6-8 weeks for cryptococcal meningitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Children:</strong></p><ul><li>Intravenous dosage is 6-12 mg/kg daily (every 72 hours for neonates up to two weeks old, every 48 hours for neonates 2-4 weeks old), with a maximum of 400 mg daily.</li><li>For prevention of relapse of cryptococcal meningitis, intravenous administration is 100-200 mg daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Increases prothrombin time after warfarin administration, so careful monitoring is recommended.</li><li>Prolongs the serum half-life of oral sulfonylureas (chlorpropamide, glibenclamide, glipizide, and tolbutamide), so hypoglycemia should be considered.</li><li>Co-administration with hydrochlorothiazide increases fluconazole plasma concentration by 40%.</li><li>When administered with phenytoin, fluconazole may increase phenytoin levels significantly.</li><li>Rifampicin decreases the AUC and half-life of fluconazole.</li><li>Co-administration with oral contraceptives at 50 mg daily has no effect.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '76_1742194734.jpg', 37, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 11:58:54', '2025-03-17 11:58:54'),
(3104, 'Galfin', 'Fluconazole', 22.15, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '150 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Galfin 150 mg</strong></p><p> Galfin 150 mg, a member of the triazole class of antifungal agents, is a potent and selective inhibitor of fungal enzymes necessary for the synthesis of ergosterol. Galfin 150 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Vaginal candidiasis</strong> (acute or recurrent)</li><li><strong>Candidal balanitis</strong>: The treatment of partners who present with symptomatic genital candidiasis should be considered.</li><li><strong>Mucosal candidiasis</strong>: Includes oropharyngeal, esophageal, noninvasive bronchopulmonary infections, candiduria, mucocutaneous, and chronic oral atrophic candidiasis (denture sore mouth). Both normal hosts and patients with compromised immune function may be treated.</li><li><strong>Tinea pedis, tinea cruris, tinea versicolor, and dermal candidal infections</strong>: Galfin 150 mg is also indicated for nail fungal infections.</li><li><strong>Systemic candidiasis</strong>: Includes candidaemia, disseminated candidiasis, and other invasive candidal infections of the peritoneum, endocardium, and pulmonary and urinary tracts. Candidal infections in patients with malignancy, in intensive care units, or those receiving cytotoxic or immunosuppressive therapy may be treated.</li><li><strong>Cryptococcosis</strong>: Includes cryptococcal meningitis and infections of other sites (e.g., pulmonary, cutaneous). Normal hosts and patients with acquired immune deficiency syndrome (AIDS), organ transplants, or other causes of immunosuppression may be treated.</li><li><strong>Prevention of fungal infections</strong> in immunocompromised patients at risk due to neutropenia following cytotoxic chemotherapy or radiotherapy, including bone marrow transplant patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Galfin 150 mg is a triazole antifungal agent. It is a potent inhibitor of fungal cytochrome P-450-dependent enzymes. The cytochrome P-450 enzyme system is an essential component of the fungal cell membrane responsible for ergosterol synthesis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Galfin 150 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults (oral)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vaginal candidiasis</strong>: 150 mg as a single dose.</li><li><strong>Oropharyngeal candidiasis</strong>: 200 mg on the first day, followed by 100 mg once daily. Treatment should continue for at least two weeks to decrease the likelihood of relapse.</li><li><strong>Esophageal candidiasis</strong>: 200 mg on the first day, followed by 100 mg once daily. Doses up to 400 mg/day may be used. Patients should be treated for a minimum of three weeks and at least two weeks following the resolution of symptoms.</li><li><strong>Systemic candida infections</strong>: Dosage varies, with doses of up to 400 mg daily being used.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections caused by candida and peritonitis</strong>: 50-200 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Cryptococcal meningitis</strong>: 400 mg on the first day, followed by 200 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis in patients undergoing bone marrow transplantation</strong>: 400 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Children (oral)</strong></p><ul><li>Doses of 3-6 mg/kg daily have been used, with up to 12 mg/kg recommended in some cases.</li></ul><p><strong>Intravenous Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: Invasive candidal infections, candidaemia, disseminated candidiasis, and cryptococcal infections including meningitis: 400 mg initially, then 200 mg daily, increased if necessary to 400 mg daily. Treatment should continue according to response (at least 6-8 weeks for cryptococcal meningitis).</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 6-12 mg/kg daily (every 72 hours in neonates up to 2 weeks old, every 48 hours in neonates 2-4 weeks old); maximum 400 mg daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Galfin 150 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Fluconazole increases <strong>prothrombin time</strong> when administered with <strong>warfarin</strong>.</li><li>Fluconazole may prolong the serum half-life of <strong>oral sulfonylureas</strong> (e.g., chlorpropamide, glibenclamide).</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong> increases plasma concentrations of fluconazole by 40%.</li><li><strong>Phenytoin</strong> levels may increase significantly when co-administered with fluconazole.</li><li><strong>Rifampicin</strong> decreases fluconazole\'s bioavailability.</li><li>Fluconazole at 200 mg/day increases <strong>ethinyl estradiol and levonorgestrel</strong> levels slightly.</li><li>Fluconazole may slowly increase <strong>cyclosporin concentrations</strong> in renal transplant patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Galfin 150 mg should not be used in patients with known hypersensitivity to fluconazole or related triazole compounds.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Galfin 150 mg</strong></p><p> Galfin 150 mg is generally well tolerated. Common side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal symptoms</strong>: Nausea, abdominal discomfort, diarrhea, and flatulence.</li><li><strong>Rashes</strong> (rare, incidence &lt;1%).</li><li>Rare cases of <strong>anaphylaxis</strong> have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: FDA Pregnancy Category C. Avoid unless potential benefits outweigh risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Present in breast milk; use is not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with <strong>AIDS or cancer</strong> may develop abnormalities in hepatic, renal, and hematological functions.</li><li><strong>Liver enzyme elevations</strong> should be monitored.</li><li>Rare cases of <strong>hepatic necrosis</strong> have been reported.</li><li>Discontinue if severe <strong>skin reactions</strong> (e.g., Stevens-Johnson syndrome) occur.</li><li><strong>Not recommended in nursing mothers</strong> due to presence in breast milk.</li><li><strong>Does not impair driving ability</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Galfin 150 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: Symptomatic support and gastric lavage if necessary.</li><li><strong>Hemodialysis</strong> reduces plasma levels by approximately 50% in three hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a <strong>dry place away from light and heat</strong>.</li><li><strong>Keep out of reach of children</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Galfin 150 mg is a triazole antifungal agent that inhibits fungal cytochrome P-450-dependent enzymes, essential for ergosterol synthesis.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Normal doses unless renal impairment is present.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Adjust dosage based on <strong>creatinine clearance (CrCl)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl &gt;40: Normal dosage interval.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 21-40: Dosage every <strong>48 hours</strong> or <strong>half normal daily dose</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 10-20: Dosage every <strong>72 hours</strong> or <strong>one-third normal daily dose</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients receiving <strong>regular hemodialysis</strong>: Administer <strong>one dose after every dialysis session</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1688_1742195040.jpg', 37, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 12:04:00', '2025-03-17 12:04:00');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3105, 'Venesa', 'Dienogest', 50.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Venesa 2 mg</strong></p><p> Dienoaest is indicated for the treatment of endometriosis. This is the preparation of Venesa 2 mg, which belongs to the class of medications called progestins. Progestins reduce the effects of estrogen on tissues such as the endometrium (lining of the uterus) and the breast. By reducing the growth effect of estrogen on the endometrium, Venesa 2 mg helps to reduce the pelvic pain experienced by women with endometriosis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Female Sex Hormones</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Venesa 2 mg acts as an agonist at the progesterone receptor (PR) with weak affinity that is comparable to that of progesterone but has a very potent progestagenic effect in the endometrium, causing endometrial atrophy after prolonged use. It promotes antiproliferative, immunologic, and antiangiogenic effects on endometrial tissue. Venesa 2 mg reduces the level of endogenous production of oestradiol and thereby suppresses the trophic effects of oestradiol on both the eutopic and ectopic endometrium. Continuous administration of Venesa 2 mg results in a hyperprogestogenic and moderately hypoestrogenic endocrine environment, which causes initial decidualization of endometrial tissue. It is an antagonist at androgen receptors, improving androgenic symptoms such as acne and hirsutism.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Venesa 2 mg</strong></p><p> Tablet-taking can be started on any day of the menstrual cycle. The dosage of Venesa 2 mg is 2 mg daily without any break, taken preferably at the same time each day with some liquid as needed. The tablet must be taken continuously without regard to vaginal bleeding. When a pack is finished, the next one should be started without interruption. In the event of missed tablet(s), the woman should take 2 mg only as soon as she remembers and should then continue the next day to take the tablet at her usual time. A tablet not absorbed due to vomiting or diarrhea should likewise be replaced by 2 mg.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Venesa 2 mg</strong></p><p> Tablet-taking can be started on any day of the menstrual cycle. The dosage of Venesa 2 mg is 2 mg daily without any break, taken preferably at the same time each day with some liquid as needed. The tablet must be taken continuously without regard to vaginal bleeding. When a pack is finished, the next one should be started without interruption. In the event of missed tablet(s), the woman should take 2 mg only as soon as she remembers and should then continue the next day to take the tablet at her usual time. A tablet not absorbed due to vomiting or diarrhea should likewise be replaced by 2 mg.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Venesa 2 mg</strong></p><p> Progestogens, including Venesa 2 mg, are metabolized mainly by the cytochrome P450 3A4 system. Therefore, inducers or inhibitors of CYP3A4 may affect the progestogen drug metabolism. Known CYP3A4 inhibitors like azole antifungals (e.g., ketoconazole, itraconazole, fluconazole), cimetidine, verapamil, macrolides (e.g., erythromycin, clarithromycin, and roxithromycin), diltiazem, protease inhibitors (e.g., ritonavir, saquinavir, indinavir, nelfinavir), and antidepressants (e.g., nefazodone, fluvoxamine, fluoxetine) may increase plasma levels of progestogens and result in adverse reactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to Venesa 2 mg or any of its excipients. Venesa 2 mg should not be used in the presence of any of the following conditions: active venous thromboembolic disorder, arterial and cardiovascular disease (e.g., myocardial infarction, cerebrovascular accident, ischemic heart disease), diabetes mellitus with vascular involvement, presence or history of severe hepatic disease as long as liver function values have not returned to normal, presence or history of liver tumors (benign or malignant), known or suspected sex hormone-dependent malignancies, and undiagnosed vaginal bleeding.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Venesa 2 mg</strong></p><p> Undesirable effects are more common during the first months after starting Venesa 2 mg but subside with continued treatment. The most frequently reported side effects are:</p><ul><li><strong>Nervous System Disorders</strong>: Headache (9%), migraine</li><li><strong>Cardiac Disorders</strong>: Uncommon: unspecified circulatory system disorder, palpitations</li><li><strong>Vascular Disorders</strong>: Uncommon: hypotension</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal Disorders</strong>: Common: nausea, abdominal pain, flatulence</li><li><strong>Metabolism and Nutrition Disorders</strong>: Weight increase (3.6%)</li><li><strong>Psychiatric Disorders</strong>: Depressed mood (5.1%), irritability, nervousness, altered mood</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There are limited data on the use of Venesa 2 mg in pregnant women. Animal studies and data from women exposed to Venesa 2 mg during pregnancy reveal no special risks on pregnancy, embryonic/fetal development, birth, or postnatal development in humans. However, Venesa 2 mg should not be administered to pregnant women as there is no need to treat endometriosis during pregnancy. Treatment with Venesa 2 mg during lactation is not recommended. Physiochemical properties and animal data indicate excretion of Venesa 2 mg in breast milk. A decision must be made whether to discontinue breastfeeding or to abstain from Venesa 2 mg therapy, taking into account the benefit of breastfeeding for the child and the benefit of therapy for the woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Before starting Venesa 2 mg treatment, pregnancy must be excluded. During treatment, patients are advised to use nonhormonal methods of contraception (e.g., barrier method) if contraception is required. As Venesa 2 mg is a progestogen-only preparation, it can be assumed that special warnings and special precautions for use of other progestogen-only preparations are also valid for the use of Venesa 2 mg.</p><ul><li><strong>Changes in Bleeding Pattern</strong>: Venesa 2 mg treatment affects the menstrual bleeding pattern in most women.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Venesa 2 mg is contraindicated in patients with present or past severe hepatic disease.</li><li><strong>Impairment of Fertility</strong>: Ovulation is inhibited in most patients during treatment with Venesa 2 mg. However, Venesa 2 mg is not a contraceptive. If contraception is required, a nonhormonal method should be used.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Venesa 2 mg</strong></p><p> Acute toxicity studies performed with Venesa 2 mg did not indicate a risk of acute adverse effects in case of inadvertent intake of a multiple of the daily therapeutic dose. There is no specific antidote. Venesa 2 mg at 20-30 mg/day (10-15 times higher dose than the therapeutic dose) over 24 weeks was well tolerated.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a cool and dry place. Protect from light. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in Children</strong>: Venesa 2 mg is not indicated in children before menarche. The safety and efficacy of Venesa 2 mg in adolescents (menarche to 18 years) have not yet been established.</li><li><strong>Use in the Elderly</strong>: There is no relevant indication for the use of Venesa 2 mg in the geriatric population.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Female Sex Hormones</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Venesa 2 mg acts as an agonist at the progesterone receptor (PR) with weak affinity that is comparable to that of progesterone but has a very potent progestagenic effect in the endometrium, causing endometrial atrophy after prolonged use. It promotes antiproliferative, immunologic, and antiangiogenic effects on endometrial tissue. Venesa 2 mg reduces endogenous oestradiol production, suppressing the trophic effects of oestradiol on both the eutopic and ectopic endometrium.</p>', 'active', '1698_1742195388.jpg', 5, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 12:09:48', '2025-03-17 12:09:48'),
(3106, 'Imanix', 'Imatinib Mesylate', 320.00, 6, 'Tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Imanix 400 mg</strong></p><p> Newly diagnosed adult and pediatric patients with Philadelphia chromosome positive chronic myeloid leukemia (Ph+CML) in chronic phase. Patients with Philadelphia chromosome positive chronic myeloid leukemia in blast crisis, accelerated phase, or in chronic phase after failure of interferon-alpha therapy. Adult patients with relapsed or refractory Philadelphia chromosome positive acute lymphoblastic leukemia (Ph+ ALL). Pediatric patients with newly diagnosed Philadelphia chromosome positive acute lymphoblastic leukemia (Ph+ ALL) in combination with chemotherapy. Adult patients with myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative diseases associated with platelet-derived growth factor receptor (PDGFR) gene re-arrangements. Adult patients with aggressive systemic mastocytosis without the D816V c-Kit mutation or with c-Kit mutational status unknown. Adult patients with hypereosinophilic syndrome and/or chronic eosinophilic leukemia who have the FIP1L1-PDGFR a fusion kinase (mutational analysis or fluorescence in situ hybridization [FISH] demonstration of CHIC2 allele deletion) and for patients with HES and/or CEL who are FIP1L1-PDGFRa fusion kinase negative or unknown. Adult patients with unresectable, recurrent and/or metastatic dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans. Patients with Kit (CD117) positive unresectable and/or metastatic malignant gastrointestinal stromal tumors. Adjuvant treatment of adult patients following complete gross resection of Kit (CD117) positive GIST.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Targeted Cancer Therapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Imatinib is a tyrosine kinase inhibitor that inhibits the BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase created by the Philadelphia chromosome abnormality in chronic myeloid leukemia (CML). It blocks proliferation and induces apoptosis in BCR-ABL positive cell lines, as well as fresh leukemic cells from Philadelphia chromosome positive CML. Imatinib also inhibits receptor kinases for platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF) and stem cell factor (SCF), c-kit, PDGF- and SCF-mediated cellular events.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Imanix 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults with Ph+ CML CP:</strong> 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adults with Ph+ CML AP or BC:</strong> 600 mg/day</li><li><strong>Pediatrics with Ph+ CML CP:</strong> 340 mg/m²/day</li><li><strong>Adults with Ph+ ALL:</strong> 600 mg/day</li><li><strong>Pediatrics with Ph+ ALL:</strong> 340 mg/m²/day</li><li><strong>Adults with MDS/MPD:</strong> 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adults with ASM:</strong> 100 mg/day or 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adults with HES/CEL:</strong> 100 mg/day or 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adults with DFSP:</strong> 800 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adults with metastatic and/or unresectable GIST:</strong> 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adjuvant treatment of adults with GIST:</strong> 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Patients with mild to moderate hepatic impairment:</strong> 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Patients with severe hepatic impairment:</strong> 300 mg/day</li></ul><p>All doses of Imatinib should be taken with a meal and a large glass of water. Doses of 400 mg or 600 mg should be administered once daily, whereas a dose of 800 mg should be administered as 400 mg twice a day. Imatinib can be dissolved in water or apple juice for patients having difficulty swallowing. Daily dosing of 800 mg and above should be accomplished using the 400-mg tablet to reduce exposure to iron.</p><p><strong>Administration of Imanix 400 mg</strong></p><p> Should be taken with food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Imanix 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Agents Inducing CYP3A Metabolism:</strong> Concomitant administration of Imatinib and strong CYP3A4 inducers may reduce total exposure of imatinib; consider alternative agents.</li><li><strong>Agents Inhibiting CYP3A Metabolism:</strong> Concomitant administration of Imatinib and strong CYP3A4 inhibitors may result in a significant imatinib exposure increase. Grapefruit juice may also increase plasma concentrations of imatinib; avoid grapefruit juice.</li><li><strong>Interactions with Drugs Metabolized by CYP3A4:</strong> Imatinib will increase plasma concentration of CYP3A4 metabolized drugs (e.g., triazolo-benzodiazepines, dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, certain HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors, etc.). Use caution when administering Imatinib with CYP3A4 substrates that have a narrow therapeutic window.</li><li><strong>Interactions with Drugs Metabolized by CYP2D6:</strong> Use caution when administering Imatinib with CYP2D6 substrates that have a narrow therapeutic window.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity. Lactation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Imanix 400 mg</strong></p><p> The following serious adverse reactions are described elsewhere in the labeling:</p><ul><li>Fluid Retention and Edema</li><li>Hematologic Toxicity</li><li>Congestive Heart Failure and Left Ventricular Dysfunction</li><li>Hepatotoxicity</li><li>Hemorrhage</li><li>Gastrointestinal Disorders</li><li>Hypereosinophilic Cardiac Toxicity</li><li>Dermatologic Toxicities</li><li>Hypothyroidism</li><li>Growth Retardation in Children and Adolescents</li><li>Tumor Lysis Syndrome</li><li>Impairments Related to Driving and Using Machinery</li><li>Renal Toxicity</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> <strong>Category D:</strong> There is positive evidence of human fetal risk, but the benefits from use in pregnant women may be acceptable despite the risk (e.g., if the drug is needed in a life-threatening situation or for a serious disease for which safer drugs cannot be used or are ineffective).</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Edema and severe fluid retention have occurred. Weigh patients regularly and manage unexpected rapid weight gain by drug interruption and diuretics.</li><li>Cytopenias, particularly anemia, neutropenia, and thrombocytopenia, have occurred. Manage with dose reduction, dose interruption, or discontinuation of treatment.</li><li>Severe congestive heart failure and left ventricular dysfunction have been reported, particularly in patients with comorbidities and risk factors.</li><li>Severe hepatotoxicity, including fatalities, may occur. Assess liver function before initiation of treatment and monthly thereafter or as clinically indicated.</li><li>Grade 3/4 hemorrhage has been reported in clinical studies.</li><li>Cardiogenic shock/left ventricular dysfunction has been associated with the initiation of Imatinib in patients with conditions associated with high eosinophil levels.</li><li>Bullous dermatologic reactions (e.g., erythema multiforme and Stevens-Johnson syndrome) have been reported.</li><li>Hypothyroidism has been reported in thyroidectomy patients undergoing levothyroxine replacement.</li><li>Growth retardation occurring in children under Imatinib treatment has been reported.</li><li>Tumor Lysis Syndrome. Close monitoring is recommended.</li><li>Reports of motor vehicle accidents have been received in patients receiving Imatinib.</li><li>Renal Toxicity. A decline in renal function may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Imanix 400 mg</strong></p><p> Experience with doses higher than the recommended therapeutic dose is limited. In the event of overdose, the patient should be observed, and appropriate symptomatic treatment given.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C, in a cool and dry place. Keep away from light. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Patients on potent CYP3A4 inducers:</strong> Increase dose by 50% and carefully monitor clinical response.</li><li><strong>Severe Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Reduce dose by 25%.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Targeted Cancer Therapy, Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Imatinib is a small molecule protein-tyrosine kinase inhibitor that potently inhibits the activity of the Bcr-Abl tyrosine kinase (TK), as well as several receptor TKs.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Women of childbearing potential:</strong> Must use effective contraception during treatment and for at least 15 days after stopping treatment.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Limited data; should not be used unless clearly necessary.</li><li><strong>Breast-feeding:</strong> Should not breast-feed during treatment and for at least 15 days after stopping Imatinib.</li><li><strong>Fertility:</strong> Patients concerned about fertility should consult their physician.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '414_1742195729.jpg', 55, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 12:15:29', '2025-03-17 12:15:29'),
(3107, 'Iprex', 'Ipratropium Bromide', 250.00, 1, 'x 200 metered doses', '200 metered doses', 'Syrup', '20 mcg/puff', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Iprex 20 mcg/puff</strong></p><p>Nebuliser Solution: It is indicated for the treatment of reversible bronchospasm associated with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). It is also indicated, when used concomitantly with inhaled beta 2-agonists, for treatment of reversible airway obstruction as in acute and chronic asthma.</p><p>Inhaler: As bronchodilator in the treatment of chronic reversible airway obstruction as in asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease including chronic bronchitis and emphysema. Treatment of acute reversible airway obstruction.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Anticholinergic bronchodilators</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Iprex 20 mcg/puff is a quaternary ammonium compound with anticholinergic properties. It appears to inhibit vagal reflexes by antagonising the action of acetylcholine (the transmitter agent released from the vagus nerve). Anticholinergics prevent the increase in intracellular concentration of cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cyclic GMP) caused by interaction of acetylcholine with the muscarinic receptor on bronchial smooth muscle.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Iprex 20 mcg/puff</strong></p><p>Children over 3 years: The usual dose is 0.4-2.0 ml Iprex 20 mcg/puff Solution (100-500 micrograms) up to three times daily.</p><p>Adults (including elderly and adolescents) over 12 years of age: The usual dose is 0.4-2.0 ml Iprex 20 mcg/puff Solution (100-500 micrograms) up to four times daily.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Iprex 20 mcg/puff</strong></p><p>Nebuliser Solution- Adults (including the elderly) and children over 12 years of age: 250-500 micrograms (i.e. 1 ml or 2 ml) 3 to 4 times daily.</p><p>For treatment of acute bronchospasm: 500 micrograms/2 ml. It is advisable not to exceed the recommended daily dose during either acute or maintenance treatment.</p><p>Daily doses exceeding 2 mg in adults and children over 12 years of age should only be given under medical supervision.</p><p>Children 6-12 years of age: 250 micrograms (i.e. 1 ml) up to a total daily dose of 1 mg (4 ml). The time interval between doses may be determined by the physician.</p><p>Children 0-5 years of age (for treatment of acute asthma only): 125-250 micrograms (i.e. 0.5 ml-1 ml) up to a total daily dose of 1 mg (4 ml). Ipratropium bromide should be administered no more frequently than 6 hourly in children under 5 years of age.</p><p>Repeated doses can be administered until the patient is stable. The time interval between the doses may be determined by the physician.</p><p>Ipratropium Nebuliser Solution may be combined with a short-acting beta 2-agonist in the same nebulizer chamber. The solution should be used as soon as possible after mixing and any unused solution should be discarded.</p><p>Ipratropium Nebuliser Solution can be administered using a range of commercially available nebulizing devices. The dose of nebulizer solution may need to be diluted in order to obtain a final volume suitable for the particular nebulizer being used (usually 2-4 ml); if dilution is necessary use only sterile sodium chloride 0.9% solution.</p><p>The unit dose Ipratropium Nebulizer Solution is intended only for inhalation with suitable nebulizing devices and should not be taken orally or administered parenterally.</p><p><strong>Inhaler-</strong></p><p>Adults: The usual dose is 1-2 puffs (20 gm/spray) three or four times daily.</p><p>Single dose up to 80 gm (4 puffs) may be required to obtain maximum benefit during early treatment.</p><p>Patients may take additional inhalations as required; however, the total number of inhalations should not exceed 12 in 24 hours.</p><p>Children: 6-12 years: Usually 1-2 puffs two to three times daily.</p><p>Below 6 years: The usual dose is 1 puff (20 gm) three times daily.</p><p>In order to ensure that the inhaler is used correctly, the administration should be supervised by an adult.</p><p>No specific information on the use of the product in the elderly is available. Clinical trials have included patients over 65 years and no adverse reactions specific to this age group has been reported.</p><p><strong>Administration of Iprex 20 mcg/puff</strong></p><p>Using an Inhaler seems simple, but most patients do not know how to use it in the right way. If the Inhaler is used in the wrong way, less medicine can reach the lungs. Correct and regular use of the Inhaler will prevent or lessen the severity of asthma attacks.</p><p>Following simple steps can help to use Inhaler effectively (According to \"National Asthma Guidelines for Medical Practitioners\" published by Asthma Association):</p><ol><li>Take off the cap. Shake the inhaler (at least six times) vigorously before each use.</li><li>If the inhaler is new or if it has not been used for a week or more, shake it well and release one puff into the air to make sure that it works.</li><li>Breathe out as full as comfortably possible &amp; hold the inhaler upright.</li><li>Place the actuator into mouth between the teeth and close lips around the mouthpiece.</li><li>While breathing deeply and slowly through the mouth, press down firmly and fully on the canister to release medicine.</li><li>Remove the inhaler from mouth. Continue holding breath for at least 10 seconds or as long as it is comfortable.</li><li>If doctor has prescribed more than one inhalation per treatment, wait 1 minute between puffs (inhalations). Shake the inhaler well and repeat steps 4 to 7.</li><li>After use, replace the cap on the mouthpiece. After each treatment, rinse mouth with water.</li><li>Check your technique in front of a mirror from time to time, if you see a white mist during the inhalation, you may not have closed your lips properly around the mouthpiece, or you may not be breathing in as you press the can. This indicates failure of technique. If this happens, repeat the procedure from step 4 carefully.</li></ol><p><strong>Instructions for Cleaning Inhaler:</strong></p><p>Clean your Inhaler at least once a week. Remove canister and rinse the plastic actuator and cap in warm water but do not put the metal canister into water.</p><p>Dry the actuator and cap thoroughly and gently replace the metal canister into the actuator with a twisting motion. Put the cap on the mouthpiece.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Iprex 20 mcg/puff</strong></p><p>Inhaler: Ipratropium has been used concomitantly with other drugs, including sympathomimetic bronchodilators, methylxanthines, steroids, and cromolyn sodium, commonly used in the treatment of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, without adverse drug reactions.</p><p>There are no studies fully evaluating the interaction effects of Ipratropium and these drugs with respect to effectiveness.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Nebuliser Solution: Known hypersensitivity to atropine or its derivatives, or to any other component of the product.</p><p>Inhaler: Known hypersensitivity to Ipratropium, Atropine or its derivative. Also contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to soya lecithin or related food products such as soybean, lecithin, and peanut.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Iprex 20 mcg/puff</strong></p><p>Nebuliser Solution:</p><p>Common: Headache, Dizziness, Cough, Inhalation induced bronchospasm, Dryness of mouth, Vomiting.</p><p>Uncommon: Urticaria, Tachycardia, Skin rash, Pruritus.</p><p>Rare: Anaphylactic reaction, Eye pain, Mydriasis, Intraocular pressure increased, Palpitations, Atrial fibrillation, Nausea.</p><p>Inhaler:</p><p>Potentially life-threatening effects- Idiosyncratic reactions to Ipratropium bromide are rare.</p><p>Severe adverse effects due to the inhibition of muscarinic receptors and ganglion blockade are theoretically possible but unlikely with the metered-dose aerosol.</p><p>Severe/Irreversible adverse effects- No effects of this kind are reported.</p><p>Symptomatic adverse effects- Regular use of Ipratropium can lead to a dry mouth through inhibition of salivary flow.</p><p>Observed during clinical practice- The most common adverse reactions reported are dryness of the oropharynx (5%); cough, exacerbation of symptoms, &amp; irritation from aerosol (3%); headache (2%); nausea, dizziness, blurred vision/difficulty in accommodation &amp; drying of secretions (1%).</p><p>Less frequently reported adverse reactions include tachycardia, nervousness, paresthesias, drowsiness, coordination difficulty, itching, hives, flushing, alopecia, constipation, tremor &amp; mucosal ulceration.</p><p>Cases of precipitation or worsening of narrow-angle glaucoma, acute eye pain &amp; hypotension have been reported.</p><p>Allergic-type reactions such as skin rash, angioedema of the tongue, lips &amp; face, urticaria (including giant urticaria), laryngospasm, and anaphylactic reactions have also been reported; with positive rechallenges in some cases.</p><p>Ipratropium bromide does not produce adverse effects on mucociliary clearance, in contrast to atropine and other muscarinic antagonists. There is no evidence that in the therapeutic dose range, Ipratropium has any adverse effect on bronchial secretion.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of Iprex 20 mcg/puff during human pregnancy has not been established.</p><p>The benefits of using Iprex 20 mcg/puff during a confirmed or suspected pregnancy must be weighed against possible hazards to the unborn child.</p><p>It is not known whether Iprex 20 mcg/puff is excreted into breast milk.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Nebuliser Solution: Use of the nebulizer solution should be subject to close medical supervision during initial dosing.</p><p>Caution is advised in the use of anticholinergic agents in patients with narrow-angle glaucoma, or with prostatic hyperplasia or bladder-outflow obstruction or cystic fibrosis.</p><p>Immediate hypersensitivity reactions have been demonstrated by rare cases of urticaria, angioedema, rash, bronchospasm, oropharyngeal edema, and anaphylaxis.</p><p>There have been isolated reports of ocular complications (i.e., mydriasis, increased intraocular pressure, narrow-angle glaucoma, eye pain) when aerosolized Iprex 20 mcg/puff has come into contact with the eyes during nebulizer therapy.</p><p>Patients must be instructed in the correct administration of the Ipratropium Nebulizer Solution.</p><p>Care must be taken not to allow the solution or mist to enter the eyes.</p><p>It is recommended that the nebulizer solution is administered via a mouthpiece. If a mouthpiece is not available and a nebulizer mask is used, it must fit properly.</p><p><strong>Inhaler:</strong></p><p>Patients should be advised that temporary blurring of vision precipitation or worsening of narrow-angle glaucoma or eye pain may result if the aerosol is sprayed into the eyes.</p><p>If the recommended dosage does not provide relief or symptoms become worse, patients should seek immediate medical attention.</p><p>While taking Ipratropium inhalation aerosol, other inhaled drugs should not be used unless prescribed.</p><p>Ipratropium inhalation aerosol is not indicated for the initial treatment of acute episodes of bronchospasm where rapid response is required.</p><p>Drugs with a faster onset may be preferable as initial therapy in this situation.</p><p>Immediate hypersensitivity reactions may occur after administration of Ipratropium bromide, as demonstrated by rare cases of urticaria, angio-oedema, rash, bronchospasm, and oropharyngeal oedema.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Iprex 20 mcg/puff</strong></p><p>Acute overdosage by inhalation is unlikely since Ipratropium bromide is not well absorbed systematically after aerosol administration.</p><p>Inhaled dosage of 5 mg produces an increase in heart rate and palpitation.</p><p>Single doses of Ipratropium bromide 30 mg by mouth caused anticholinergic side effects but which were not considered severe enough to require specific reversal.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>As the product contains no preservative, a fresh ampoule should be used for each dose and the ampoule should be opened immediately before administration.</p><p>Any solution left in the ampoule should be discarded.</p><p>Do not store above 25°C. Keep the ampoule in the outer carton.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Anticholinergic bronchodilators</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Iprex 20 mcg/puff is a quaternary ammonium compound with anticholinergic properties. It appears to inhibit vagal reflexes by antagonising the action of acetylcholine (the transmitter agent released from the vagus nerve).</p><p>Anticholinergics prevent the increase in intracellular concentration of cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cyclic GMP) caused by interaction of acetylcholine with the muscarinic receptor on bronchial smooth muscle.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The safety of Ipratropium Nebulizer Solution during human pregnancy has not been established.</p><p>The benefits of using Ipratropium Nebuliser Solution during pregnancy must be weighed against the possible hazards to the fetus.</p><p>It is not known whether Iprex 20 mcg/puff is excreted into breast milk.</p><p>Caution should be exercised when Ipratropium Nebulizer Solution is administered to nursing mothers.</p>', 'active', '1583_1742196268.jpg', 4, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 12:24:28', '2025-05-04 15:58:11'),
(3108, 'Olmepres', 'Olmesartan Medoxomil', 9.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Olmepres 20 mg</strong></p><p> Olmepres 20 mg is indicated for the treatment of hypertension. It may be used alone or in combination with other antihypertensive agents.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Angiotensin-II receptor blocker</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Olmesartan medoxomil is a potent, orally active, selective angiotensin II receptor (type AT1) antagonist. It is expected to block all actions of angiotensin II mediated by the AT1 receptor, regardless of the source or route of synthesis of angiotensin II. The selective antagonism of the angiotensin II (AT1) receptors results in increases in plasma renin levels and angiotensin I and II concentrations, and some decrease in plasma aldosterone concentrations. Angiotensin II is the primary vasoactive hormone of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) and plays a significant role in the pathophysiology of hypertension via the type 1 (AT1) receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Olmepres 20 mg</strong></p><p> Adult: Dosage must be individualized. The usual initial dose is 10 mg once daily. In patients whose blood pressure is not adequately controlled at this dose, the dose may be increased to 20 mg once daily as the optimal dose. For patients requiring further reduction in blood pressure after 2 weeks of therapy, the dose of Olmesartan may be increased to 40 mg. Doses above 40 mg do not appear to have greater effect.</p><p> Pediatric Use: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p> Geriatric Use: Of the total number of hypertensive patients receiving Olmepres 20 mg in clinical studies, more than 20% were 65 years of age and over, while more than 5% were 75 years of age and older. No overall differences in effectiveness or safety were observed between elderly patients and younger patients.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Olmepres 20 mg</strong></p><p> Dosage must be individualized. The usual recommended starting dose of Olmesartan is 20 mg once daily when used as monotherapy in patients who are not volume-contracted. For patients requiring further reduction in blood pressure after 2 weeks of therapy, the dose of Olmesartan may be increased to 40 mg. Doses above 40 mg do not appear to have a greater effect. Twice-daily dosing offers no advantage over the same total dose given once daily. No initial dosage adjustment is recommended for elderly patients, for patients with moderate to marked renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;40 ml/min) or with moderate to marked hepatic dysfunction. For patients with possible depletion of intravascular volume (e.g. patients treated with diuretics, particularly those with impaired renal function), Olmesartan should be initiated under close medical supervision and consideration should be given to use of a lower starting dose. Olmesartan may be administered with or without food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Olmepres 20 mg</strong></p><p> With medicine: No significant drug interactions were reported in which Olmesartan was co-administered.</p><p> With food &amp; others: Food does not affect the bioavailability of Olmesartan.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Olmesartan is contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to any component of this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Olmepres 20 mg</strong></p><p> Common: The most common side effects include Back pain, bronchitis, creatine phosphokinase increased, diarrhea, headache, hematuria, hyperglycemia, hypertriglyceridemia, influenza-like symptoms, pharyngitis, rhinitis, and sinusitis.</p><p> Rare: Chest pain, peripheral edema, arthritis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Categories C (first trimester) and D (second and third trimesters).</p><p> Nursing Mothers: It is not known whether Olmesartan is excreted in human milk, but Olmesartan is secreted at low concentration in the milk of lactating rats. Because of the potential for adverse effects on the nursing infant, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> As a consequence of inhibiting the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, changes in renal function may be anticipated in susceptible individuals treated with Olmepres 20 mg. In patients whose renal function may depend upon the activity of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (e.g. patients with severe congestive heart failure), treatment with angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors and angiotensin receptor antagonists has been associated with oliguria and/or progressive azotemia and (rarely) with acute renal failure and/or death. Similar results may be anticipated in patients treated with Olmepres 20 mg.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Olmepres 20 mg</strong></p><p> Symptoms: There is no experience of overdose with Olmesartan. The most likely effects of Olmepres 20 mg overdosage are hypotension and tachycardia; bradycardia could be encountered if parasympathetic (vagal) stimulation occurred.</p><p> Treatment: If intake is recent, gastric lavage or induction of emesis may be considered. Clinically significant hypotension due to an overdose of Olmesartan requires the active support of the cardiovascular system, including close monitoring of heart and lung function, the elevation of the extremities, and attention to circulating fluid volume and urine output.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in cool &amp; dry place below 30ºC, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> Hepatic Impairment: Dose should not exceed 20 mg daily in moderate impairment.</p><p> Renal Impairment: Max. 20 mg daily if eGFR 20–60 mL/minute/1.73 m2. Avoid if eGFR less than 20 mL/minute/1.73 m2.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Angiotensin-II receptor blocker</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Angiotensin-II formed from angiotensin-I in a reaction catalyzed by angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE), is a potent vasoconstrictor, the primary vasoactive hormone of the renin-angiotensin system and an important component in the pathophysiology of hypertension. It also stimulates aldosterone secretion by the adrenal cortex. Olmesartan blocks the vasoconstrictor and aldosterone-secreting effects of angiotensin-II by selectively blocking the binding of angiotensin-II to the AT 1 receptor found in many tissues (e.g. vascular smooth muscle, adrenal gland). In-vitro-binding studies indicate that Olmesartan is a reversible &amp; competitive inhibitor of AT 1 receptor. Olmesartan does not inhibit ACE (kinase-I, the enzyme that converts angiotensin-I to angiotensin-II and degrades bradykinin).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy: When pregnancy is detected, discontinue this product as soon as possible. When used in pregnancy during the second and third trimesters, drugs that act directly on the renin-angiotensin system can cause injury and even death to the developing fetus.</p><p> Nursing Mothers: It is not known whether Olmesartan is excreted in human milk, but Olmesartan is secreted at low concentration in the milk of lactating rats. Because of the potential for adverse effects on the nursing infant, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Pediatric use: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p>', 'active', '1344_1742196443.jpg', 37, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 12:27:23', '2025-03-17 12:27:23'),
(3109, 'Levox TS', 'Levofloxacin', 130.00, 1, 'Bottle', '1 bottle', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '1.5 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Levox TS 1.5%</strong></p><p>Levox TS 1.5% is indicated for the treatment of respiratory tract infections including acute bacterial sinusitis, acute exacerbations of chronic bronchitis, community-acquired pneumonia and nosocomial pneumonia. It is also used in the treatment of uncomplicated and complicated skin &amp; soft tissue infections, chronic bacterial prostatitis and uncomplicated &amp; complicated urinary tract infections (mild to moderate). It is indicated for the treatment of adults (18 years of age).</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>4-Quinolone preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Levox TS 1.5% is a synthetic, broad-spectrum, third generation fluoroquinolone antibiotic. Chemically, Levox TS 1.5% is a chiral fluorinated carboxyquinolone. Levox TS 1.5% exerts antibacterial action by inhibiting bacterial topoisomerase IV and DNA gyrase, the enzymes required for DNA replication, transcription repair and recombination. It has in vitro activity against a wide range of gm-ve and gm+ve microorganisms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Levox TS 1.5%</strong></p><p>Acute sinusitis: 500 mg daily for 10-14 days.</p><p>Acute exacerbations of chronic bronchitis: 250-500 mg daily for 7-10 days.</p><p>Community-acquired pneumonia: 500 mg once or twice daily for 7-14 days.</p><p>Nosocomial pneumonia: 750 mg once daily for 7-14 days.</p><p>Skin &amp; soft tissue infections: 500 mg once or twice daily for 7-14 days.</p><p>Chronic prostatitis: 500 mg once daily for 28 days.</p><p>Urinary tract infections: 250 mg daily for 7-10 days (for 3 days in uncomplicated infections).</p><p><strong>Interaction of Levox TS 1.5%</strong></p><p>Antacids, iron and adsorbents reduce absorption of Levox TS 1.5%. NSAIDs may increase the risk of CNS stimulation. Warfarin may increase the risk of bleeding.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Levox TS 1.5% is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Levox TS 1.5% and other quinolone antimicrobial agents or any other components of this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Levox TS 1.5%</strong></p><p>Levox TS 1.5% is generally well tolerated. However, a few side effects can usually be seen. Side effects include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, flatulence and rare occurrence of phototoxicity (0.1%). Side effects that may be seen very rarely include tremors, depression, anxiety, confusion, etc.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>US FDA pregnancy category of Levox TS 1.5% is C. So, Levox TS 1.5% should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Adequate amount of water should be taken during treatment period to avoid concentrated form of urine. Dose adjustment is required for patients with impaired renal function.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '22_1742196720.jpeg', 7, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 12:32:00', '2025-03-17 12:48:26'),
(3110, 'Rehydril', 'Racecadotril', 6.50, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rehydril 100 mg</strong></p><p> Rehydril 100 mg is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of acute diarrhea.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-diarrhoeal</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Rehydril 100 mg increases the availability of endogenous opioids (enkephalins) by inhibiting the membrane-bound enkephalinase. The enkephalins in turn mediate their effect through δ receptor (delta opioid receptor) activation that induces a selective increase in Cl absorption by inhibiting adenylate cyclase.</p><p> Onset: 30 min (plasma enkephalinase inhibition).</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rehydril 100 mg</strong></p><p> Adults: One capsule initially, regardless of the time of day. Then one capsule three times daily, preferably before the main meals.</p><p> Children (from 3 months of age): Rehydril 100 mg granules should be administered together with oral rehydration. The recommended dose is determined according to body weight: 1.5 mg/kg per administration, three times daily.</p><ul><li>Infants less than 9 kg: one 10 mg sachet 3 times daily.</li><li>Infants from 9 kg to 13 kg: two 10 mg sachets 3 times daily.</li><li>Children from 13 kg to 27 kg: one 30 mg sachet 3 times daily.</li><li>Children of more than 27 kg: two 30 mg sachets 3 times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Rehydril 100 mg</strong></p><p> Adults: One capsule initially, regardless of the time of day. Then one capsule three times daily, preferably before the main meals.</p><p> Children (from 3 months of age): Rehydril 100 mg granules should be administered together with oral rehydration. The recommended dose is determined according to body weight: 1.5 mg/kg per administration, three times daily.</p><ul><li>Infants less than 9 kg: one 10 mg sachet 3 times daily.</li><li>Infants from 9 kg to 13 kg: two 10 mg sachets 3 times daily.</li><li>Children from 13 kg to 27 kg: one 30 mg sachet 3 times daily.</li><li>Children of more than 27 kg: two 30 mg sachets 3 times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Rehydril 100 mg</strong></p><p> No drug interactions found. Joint treatment with Rehydril 100 mg and Loperamide or Nifuroxazide does not modify the kinetics of Rehydril 100 mg.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to Rehydril 100 mg, or to any of the excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Rehydril 100 mg</strong></p><p> Headache, erythema multiforme, urticaria, angioedema may be seen.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Due to a lack of clinical data, Rehydril 100 mg should not be administered to pregnant or breastfeeding women.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> The administration of Rehydril 100 mg does not modify the usual rehydration regimens. The presence of bloody stools and fever may indicate the presence of invasive bacteria as a reason for diarrhea or the presence of other severe diseases. Rehydril 100 mg has not been tested in antibiotic-associated diarrhea and should therefore not be administered under these conditions. Caution should be taken in patients with renal or liver impairment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rehydril 100 mg</strong></p><p> No cases of overdose have been reported. In adults, single doses above 2 g, which is equivalent to 20 times the therapeutic dose, have been administered, and no harmful effects have been described.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Anti-diarrhoeal</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Rehydril 100 mg increases the availability of endogenous opioids (enkephalins) by inhibiting the membrane-bound enkephalinase. The enkephalins in turn mediate their effect through δ receptor (delta opioid receptor) activation that induces a selective increase in Cl absorption by inhibiting adenylate cyclase.</p><p> Onset: 30 min (plasma enkephalinase inhibition).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Due to a lack of clinical data, Rehydril 100 mg should not be administered to pregnant or breastfeeding women.</p>', 'active', '929_1742196926.jpg', 30, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 12:35:26', '2025-03-17 12:35:26');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3111, 'Humalog 200', 'Insulin Lispro', 1600.00, 1, 'Injection', '3 ml KwikPen', 'Injection', '200 unit  per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen</strong></p><p>Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen is an insulin analogue used to treat patients with diabetes mellitus for the control of hyperglycemia. It has a more rapid onset and shorter duration of action than human regular insulin. In patients with type 1 diabetes, it should be used alongside a longer-acting insulin. However, in type 2 diabetes, it may be used without a longer-acting insulin when combined with sulfonylurea agents.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Rapid Acting Insulin</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen is a human insulin analogue that rapidly lowers blood glucose. It is created by reversing amino acids at positions 28 and 29 on the insulin B-chain and synthesized using genetically modified Escherichia coli bacteria. The blood glucose-lowering effect results from enhanced glucose uptake by muscle and fat cells and decreased glucose production by the liver.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults (Subcutaneous Injection):</strong>Individualized dosing based on glycemic response.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual range: <strong>0.5-1 unit/kg/day</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Type 1 Diabetes:</strong> About one-third of total insulin needs should come from Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen, with the rest from longer-acting insulin.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Type 2 Diabetes:</strong> If oral medication is inadequate, start with <strong>10 units/day SC (or 0.1-0.2 unit/kg/day)</strong> of intermediate- or long-acting insulin at bedtime. Alternatively, rapid-acting insulin before meals may be used.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Doses should be carefully adjusted based on blood glucose levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Increased insulin requirements:</strong> Corticosteroids, isoniazid, certain lipid-lowering drugs (niacin), estrogens, oral contraceptives, phenothiazines, thyroid hormone therapy.</li><li><strong>Decreased insulin requirements:</strong> Oral antidiabetics, salicylates, sulfa antibiotics, ACE inhibitors, angiotensin II receptor blockers, beta-blockers, alcohol.</li><li><strong>Beta-blockers may mask hypoglycemia symptoms.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen or its excipients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Hypoglycemia, injection site reactions, lipodystrophy, rash, pruritus.</li><li><strong>Serious (Rare):</strong> Allergic reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> Maintaining good glucose control is crucial during pregnancy to reduce risks. Insulin requirements change throughout pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Unknown if excreted in breast milk; dose adjustments may be necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in <strong>renal/hepatic impairment</strong>, pregnancy, lactation, and when switching from other insulin types.</li><li>Monitor <strong>blood glucose, potassium, electrolytes, HbA1c, and lipid profile.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms:</strong> Hypoglycemia, hypokalemia.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Management:Mild cases:</strong> Oral glucose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Severe cases:</strong> IM/subcutaneous glucagon or IV glucose, with continued monitoring.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at <strong>2°C - 8°C</strong> in a refrigerator.</li><li><strong>Do not freeze.</strong></li><li>If in use, it can be stored at room temperature for up to one month, away from heat and light.</li></ul><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Approved for children (daily SC injections). U-100 formulation is also approved for insulin pumps. Not studied in children under 3 years or in type 2 diabetes.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> No significant difference in efficacy or safety in older adults.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Higher risk of <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>; requires frequent dose adjustments.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Higher risk of <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>; requires close monitoring.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Rapid Acting Insulin</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen is a rapid-acting human insulin analogue that facilitates glucose uptake in muscle and fat while reducing hepatic glucose production.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category B:</strong> Essential to maintain glucose control during pregnancy. Insulin needs may fluctuate.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Unknown if excreted in breast milk; caution advised.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Approved for <strong>children</strong> using daily subcutaneous injections.</li><li><strong>U-100 formulation</strong> is approved for insulin pumps.</li><li>Not studied in <strong>children under 3 years</strong> or those with <strong>type 2 diabetes</strong>.</li><li>Dosage must be individualized based on <strong>frequent blood glucose monitoring</strong>.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '1967_1742201471.jpg', 1, 12, 'fazlearabbi97@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 13:51:11', '2025-05-08 20:06:58'),
(3112, 'Zunami', 'Azithromycin Dihydrate', 45.00, 5, 'Tablet', '5 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><p> Zunami 500 mg is prescribed for infections caused by susceptible organisms in the following areas:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper respiratory tract</strong> (e.g., sinusitis, pharyngitis/tonsillitis)</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract</strong> (e.g., bronchitis, pneumonia)</li><li><strong>Otitis media</strong></li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong></li><li><strong>Sexually transmitted infections</strong>: Non-gonococcal urethritis and cervicitis caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> in both men and women.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Macrolides</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Zunami 500 mg belongs to the macrolide subclass of antibiotics known as azalide. It works by binding to the 50s ribosomal subunit of susceptible species, preventing microbial protein synthesis. It has activity against a wide range of organisms, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Moraxella catarrhalis</strong></li><li><strong>Anaerobes</strong> like <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em></li><li><strong>Gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria</strong>, including <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Escherichia coli</em></li><li><strong>Other organisms</strong>: <em>Bordetella pertussis</em>, <em>Bordetella parapertussis</em>, <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em>, <em>Haemophilus ducreyi</em>, <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>, <em>Campylobacter sp.</em>, <em>Toxoplasma gondii</em>, <em>Treponema pallidum</em>, <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>, <em>Legionella pneumophila</em>, and hominins.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">500 mg once daily for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Alternatively, 500 mg on Day 1, followed by 250 mg once daily for the next 4 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For sexually transmitted diseases caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em>, the dose is 1 gm as a single dose or 500 mg on Day 1, followed by 250 mg for 2 more days.</li><li><strong>Children</strong> (Over 6 months of age):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 mg/kg body weight once daily for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">200 mg (1 teaspoonful) for 15-25 kg body weight</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">300 mg (1½ teaspoonfuls) for 26-35 kg body weight</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">400 mg (2 teaspoonfuls) for 36-45 kg body weight</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For typhoid fever, 500 mg once daily for 7-10 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>For suspension, shake the bottle thoroughly.</li><li>Fill the bottle with boiled and cooled water up to the watermark on the label.</li><li>Mix the powder and water well.</li><li>Take Zunami 500 mg at least one hour before or two hours after meals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Food &amp; Antacids</strong>: Reduce absorption of Zunami 500 mg.</li><li><strong>Ergot Alkaloids</strong>: Avoid co-administration due to the risk of ergotism.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporine and Digoxin</strong>: Macrolides may increase their plasma levels.</li><li><strong>Other drugs</strong> (Warfarin, Theophylline, Carbamazepine, Methylprednisolone, Cimetidine): No significant interactions with Zunami 500 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Zunami 500 mg or other macrolides.</li><li>Co-administration with ergot derivatives.</li><li>Patients with liver disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Low-frequency side effects include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vomiting</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Abdominal pain or cramps</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Flatulence</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diarrhea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Headaches</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin rashes</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category</strong>: B.</li><li>No harm to the fetus has been observed in animal studies, but there is limited research on pregnant women. Use only if necessary.</li><li>The presence in breast milk is unknown. Caution should be exercised when administering to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor for signs of superinfection with non-susceptible organisms (e.g., fungi).</li><li>No dose adjustment required for renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No specific information on overdose.</li><li>Symptoms may include hearing loss, severe nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea.</li><li>Gastric lavage and supportive treatments are recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '993_1742203141.jpg', 58, 12, 'emugp959@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 14:19:01', '2025-03-17 14:19:01'),
(3113, 'Zunami', 'Azithromycin Dihydrate', 45.00, 5, 'Tablet', '5 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><p>Zunami 500 mg is prescribed for infections caused by susceptible organisms in the following areas:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper respiratory tract</strong> (e.g., sinusitis, pharyngitis/tonsillitis)</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract</strong> (e.g., bronchitis, pneumonia)</li><li><strong>Otitis media</strong></li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong></li><li><strong>Sexually transmitted infections</strong>: Non-gonococcal urethritis and cervicitis caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> in both men and women.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Macrolides</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zunami 500 mg belongs to the macrolide subclass of antibiotics known as azalide. It works by binding to the 50s ribosomal subunit of susceptible species, preventing microbial protein synthesis. It has activity against a wide range of organisms, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Moraxella catarrhalis</strong></li><li><strong>Anaerobes</strong> like <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em></li><li><strong>Gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria</strong>, including <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Escherichia coli</em></li><li><strong>Other organisms</strong>: <em>Bordetella pertussis</em>, <em>Bordetella parapertussis</em>, <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em>, <em>Haemophilus ducreyi</em>, <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>, <em>Campylobacter sp.</em>, <em>Toxoplasma gondii</em>, <em>Treponema pallidum</em>, <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>, <em>Legionella pneumophila</em>, and hominins.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">500 mg once daily for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Alternatively, 500 mg on Day 1, followed by 250 mg once daily for the next 4 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For sexually transmitted diseases caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em>, the dose is 1 gm as a single dose or 500 mg on Day 1, followed by 250 mg for 2 more days.</li><li><strong>Children</strong> (Over 6 months of age):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 mg/kg body weight once daily for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">200 mg (1 teaspoonful) for 15-25 kg body weight</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">300 mg (1½ teaspoonfuls) for 26-35 kg body weight</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">400 mg (2 teaspoonfuls) for 36-45 kg body weight</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For typhoid fever, 500 mg once daily for 7-10 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>For suspension, shake the bottle thoroughly.</li><li>Fill the bottle with boiled and cooled water up to the watermark on the label.</li><li>Mix the powder and water well.</li><li>Take Zunami 500 mg at least one hour before or two hours after meals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Food &amp; Antacids</strong>: Reduce absorption of Zunami 500 mg.</li><li><strong>Ergot Alkaloids</strong>: Avoid co-administration due to the risk of ergotism.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporine and Digoxin</strong>: Macrolides may increase their plasma levels.</li><li><strong>Other drugs</strong> (Warfarin, Theophylline, Carbamazepine, Methylprednisolone, Cimetidine): No significant interactions with Zunami 500 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Zunami 500 mg or other macrolides.</li><li>Co-administration with ergot derivatives.</li><li>Patients with liver disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Low-frequency side effects include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vomiting</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Abdominal pain or cramps</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Flatulence</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diarrhea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Headaches</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin rashes</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category</strong>: B.</li><li>No harm to the fetus has been observed in animal studies, but there is limited research on pregnant women. Use only if necessary.</li><li>The presence in breast milk is unknown. Caution should be exercised when administering to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor for signs of superinfection with non-susceptible organisms (e.g., fungi).</li><li>No dose adjustment required for renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No specific information on overdose.</li><li>Symptoms may include hearing loss, severe nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea.</li><li>Gastric lavage and supportive treatments are recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '476_1742203142.jpg', 58, 12, 'emugp959@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 14:19:02', '2025-05-13 07:31:40'),
(3114, 'Zunami', 'Azithromycin Dihydrate', 45.00, 5, 'Tablet', '5 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><p>Zunami 500 mg is prescribed for infections caused by susceptible organisms in the following areas:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper respiratory tract</strong> (e.g., sinusitis, pharyngitis/tonsillitis)</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract</strong> (e.g., bronchitis, pneumonia)</li><li><strong>Otitis media</strong></li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong></li><li><strong>Sexually transmitted infections</strong>: Non-gonococcal urethritis and cervicitis caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> in both men and women.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Macrolides</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zunami 500 mg belongs to the macrolide subclass of antibiotics known as azalide. It works by binding to the 50s ribosomal subunit of susceptible species, preventing microbial protein synthesis. It has activity against a wide range of organisms, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Moraxella catarrhalis</strong></li><li><strong>Anaerobes</strong> like <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em></li><li><strong>Gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria</strong>, including <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Escherichia coli</em></li><li><strong>Other organisms</strong>: <em>Bordetella pertussis</em>, <em>Bordetella parapertussis</em>, <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em>, <em>Haemophilus ducreyi</em>, <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>, <em>Campylobacter sp.</em>, <em>Toxoplasma gondii</em>, <em>Treponema pallidum</em>, <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>, <em>Legionella pneumophila</em>, and hominins.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">500 mg once daily for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Alternatively, 500 mg on Day 1, followed by 250 mg once daily for the next 4 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For sexually transmitted diseases caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em>, the dose is 1 gm as a single dose or 500 mg on Day 1, followed by 250 mg for 2 more days.</li><li><strong>Children</strong> (Over 6 months of age):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 mg/kg body weight once daily for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">200 mg (1 teaspoonful) for 15-25 kg body weight</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">300 mg (1½ teaspoonfuls) for 26-35 kg body weight</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">400 mg (2 teaspoonfuls) for 36-45 kg body weight</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For typhoid fever, 500 mg once daily for 7-10 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>For suspension, shake the bottle thoroughly.</li><li>Fill the bottle with boiled and cooled water up to the watermark on the label.</li><li>Mix the powder and water well.</li><li>Take Zunami 500 mg at least one hour before or two hours after meals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Food &amp; Antacids</strong>: Reduce absorption of Zunami 500 mg.</li><li><strong>Ergot Alkaloids</strong>: Avoid co-administration due to the risk of ergotism.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporine and Digoxin</strong>: Macrolides may increase their plasma levels.</li><li><strong>Other drugs</strong> (Warfarin, Theophylline, Carbamazepine, Methylprednisolone, Cimetidine): No significant interactions with Zunami 500 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Zunami 500 mg or other macrolides.</li><li>Co-administration with ergot derivatives.</li><li>Patients with liver disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Low-frequency side effects include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vomiting</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Abdominal pain or cramps</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Flatulence</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diarrhea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Headaches</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin rashes</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category</strong>: B.</li><li>No harm to the fetus has been observed in animal studies, but there is limited research on pregnant women. Use only if necessary.</li><li>The presence in breast milk is unknown. Caution should be exercised when administering to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor for signs of superinfection with non-susceptible organisms (e.g., fungi).</li><li>No dose adjustment required for renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No specific information on overdose.</li><li>Symptoms may include hearing loss, severe nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea.</li><li>Gastric lavage and supportive treatments are recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '1843_1742203142.jpg', 58, 12, 'emugp959@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 14:19:02', '2025-05-13 07:31:46'),
(3115, 'Zunami', 'Azithromycin Dihydrate', 45.00, 5, 'Tablet', '5 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><p>Zunami 500 mg is prescribed for infections caused by susceptible organisms in the following areas:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper respiratory tract</strong> (e.g., sinusitis, pharyngitis/tonsillitis)</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract</strong> (e.g., bronchitis, pneumonia)</li><li><strong>Otitis media</strong></li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong></li><li><strong>Sexually transmitted infections</strong>: Non-gonococcal urethritis and cervicitis caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> in both men and women.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Macrolides</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zunami 500 mg belongs to the macrolide subclass of antibiotics known as azalide. It works by binding to the 50s ribosomal subunit of susceptible species, preventing microbial protein synthesis. It has activity against a wide range of organisms, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Moraxella catarrhalis</strong></li><li><strong>Anaerobes</strong> like <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em></li><li><strong>Gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria</strong>, including <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Escherichia coli</em></li><li><strong>Other organisms</strong>: <em>Bordetella pertussis</em>, <em>Bordetella parapertussis</em>, <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em>, <em>Haemophilus ducreyi</em>, <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>, <em>Campylobacter sp.</em>, <em>Toxoplasma gondii</em>, <em>Treponema pallidum</em>, <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>, <em>Legionella pneumophila</em>, and hominins.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">500 mg once daily for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Alternatively, 500 mg on Day 1, followed by 250 mg once daily for the next 4 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For sexually transmitted diseases caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em>, the dose is 1 gm as a single dose or 500 mg on Day 1, followed by 250 mg for 2 more days.</li><li><strong>Children</strong> (Over 6 months of age):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 mg/kg body weight once daily for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">200 mg (1 teaspoonful) for 15-25 kg body weight</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">300 mg (1½ teaspoonfuls) for 26-35 kg body weight</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">400 mg (2 teaspoonfuls) for 36-45 kg body weight</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For typhoid fever, 500 mg once daily for 7-10 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>For suspension, shake the bottle thoroughly.</li><li>Fill the bottle with boiled and cooled water up to the watermark on the label.</li><li>Mix the powder and water well.</li><li>Take Zunami 500 mg at least one hour before or two hours after meals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Food &amp; Antacids</strong>: Reduce absorption of Zunami 500 mg.</li><li><strong>Ergot Alkaloids</strong>: Avoid co-administration due to the risk of ergotism.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporine and Digoxin</strong>: Macrolides may increase their plasma levels.</li><li><strong>Other drugs</strong> (Warfarin, Theophylline, Carbamazepine, Methylprednisolone, Cimetidine): No significant interactions with Zunami 500 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Zunami 500 mg or other macrolides.</li><li>Co-administration with ergot derivatives.</li><li>Patients with liver disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Low-frequency side effects include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vomiting</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Abdominal pain or cramps</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Flatulence</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diarrhea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Headaches</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin rashes</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category</strong>: B.</li><li>No harm to the fetus has been observed in animal studies, but there is limited research on pregnant women. Use only if necessary.</li><li>The presence in breast milk is unknown. Caution should be exercised when administering to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor for signs of superinfection with non-susceptible organisms (e.g., fungi).</li><li>No dose adjustment required for renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zunami 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No specific information on overdose.</li><li>Symptoms may include hearing loss, severe nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea.</li><li>Gastric lavage and supportive treatments are recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '493_1742203143.jpg', 58, 12, 'emugp959@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 14:19:03', '2025-05-13 07:31:54'),
(3116, 'Nacsol', 'Acetylcysteine', 15.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nacsol 600 mg</strong></p><p> Nacsol 600 mg is indicated as an adjuvant treatment in certain clinical conditions characterized by the presence of thick and viscous mucoid or mucopurulent secretions such as: Chronic bronchopulmonary diseases (chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, emphysema with bronchitis, chronic asthmatic bronchitis, bronchiectasis); Acute bronchopulmonary diseases (asthma with bronchial mucus plugging, bronchitis, bronchopneumonia, tracheobronchitis, bronchiolitis, pulmonary complications of cystic fibrosis, pulmonary complications associated with surgery).</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Antidote preparations, Cough expectorants &amp; mucolytics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Nacsol 600 mg is a mucolytic agent that reduces the viscosity of secretions probably by the splitting of disulphide bonds in mucoproteins. Moreover, it gives an antisecretory effect. These results in clearing of respiratory ducts and facilitate breathing. Nacsol 600 mg also has anti-oxidant properties by reacting with free radicals and also by serving as a precursor to glutathione, which is an important intra and extra-cellular antioxidant. By providing antioxidant action, it neutralizes exogenous and endogenous oxidants, which in fact act as pathogens in respiratory inflammations.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Nacsol 600 mg</strong></p><p> Effervescent tablet or Dispersible tablet:</p><ul><li>Children above 6 years of age or adults: 1 tablet daily (preferably in the evening). Dissolve it in ½ glass of water and mix properly. After dissolving, take the whole content of the glass.</li><li> Granules:</li><li>Adults and children above 6 years: 1 sachet Nacsol 600 mg 200 mg or 2 sachets Nacsol 600 mg 100 mg, 2-3 times a day.</li><li>Children (2-6 years of age): 1 sachet Nacsol 600 mg 100 mg from 2 to 4 times a day, according to the age.</li><li> The duration of treatment should be 5 to 10 days in the acute treatment, whereas it may be continued in the chronic states for several months, according to the advice of the physician.</li><li> Dissolve the contents of the sachets in a glass containing a small quantity of water; mixing it, if necessary, with a spoon. A palatable solution is thus obtained, which can be drunk directly from the glass.</li></ul><p>Nebulizer Solution:</p><ul><li>The 20% solution may be diluted to a lesser concentration with either Sodium Chloride for injection, Sodium Chloride for inhalation, sterile water for injection, or sterile water for inhalation.</li><li>Adult: 5-10 ml of 10% or 20% solution by Nebulizer every 6-8 hr PRN.</li><li>Children:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-11 months: 1-2 ml of 20% or 2-4 ml of 10% solution by Nebulizer every 6-8 hr PRN.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">12 months-11 years: 3-5 ml of 20% or 6-10 ml of 10% solution by Nebulizer every 6-8 hr PRN.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Below 12 years: 5-10 ml of 10/20% solution by Nebulizer every 6-8 hr PRN.</li><li>Diagnostic Bronchograms: 1-2 ml of 20% or 2-4 ml of 10% solution 2-3 times by Nebulization or by instillation intratracheally prior to procedure.</li><li>Nebulization tent or croupette: This form of administration requires very large volumes of the solution, occasionally as much as 300 ml during a single treatment period. If a tent or croupette must be used, the recommended dose is the volume of Nacsol 600 mg (using 20%) that will maintain a very heavy mist in the tent or croupette for the desired period. Administration for intermittent or continuous prolonged periods, including overnight, may be desirable.</li><li>Direct Instillation: When used by direct instillation, 1-2 ml of a 20% solution may be given as often as every hour. When used for the routine nursing care of patients with tracheostomy, 1-2 ml of a 20% solution may be given every 1-4 hours by instillation into the tracheostomy.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Nacsol 600 mg</strong></p><p> Effervescent tablet or Dispersible tablet:</p><ul><li>Adults and children above 6 years: One effervescent tablet of Nacsol 600 mg 600 mg a day (preferably in the evening). The duration of treatment should be 5 to 10 days in the acute treatment, whereas it may be continued in the chronic states for several months, according to the advice of the physician. Dissolve the tablets in a glass containing a small quantity of water, mixing it, if necessary, with a spoon. A palatable solution is thus obtained, which can be drunk directly from the glass.</li></ul><p>Effervescent Granules:</p><ul><li>Adults and children above 6 years: 1 sachet Nacsol 600 mg 200 mg or 2 sachets Nacsol 600 mg 100 mg, 2-3 times a day.</li><li>Children (2-6 years of age): 1 sachet Nacsol 600 mg 100 mg from 2 to 4 times a day, according to the age.</li><li> The duration of treatment should be 5 to 10 days in the acute treatment, whereas it may be continued in the chronic states for several months, according to the advice of the physician.</li><li> Dissolve the contents of the sachets in a glass containing a small quantity of water; mixing it, if necessary, with a spoon. A palatable solution is thus obtained, which can be drunk directly from the glass.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Nacsol 600 mg</strong></p><p> The thiol group of Nacsol 600 mg can reduce the efficacy of certain antibiotics such as ampicillin, tetracycline, macrolides, cephalosporins, aminoglycosides, and amphotericin. Concomitant use of Nacsol 600 mg and amoxicillin will increase the level of the antibiotic in tissues. It is, therefore, advisable to use the two medicines 2 hours apart from each other. Nacsol 600 mg can increase the inhibitory effect of thrombocyte aggregation and vasodilation by nitroglycerine.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Known hypersensitivity to Nacsol 600 mg. Nacsol 600 mg contains aspartame, thus it is contraindicated in patients suffering from phenylketonuria.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Nacsol 600 mg</strong></p><p> Like all medicines, Nacsol 600 mg can cause side effects, although not everybody gets them. In very rare cases, severe immune reactions may occur such as anaphylactic shock and severe skin reaction. In rare cases, the oral administration can be followed by shortness of breath, upset stomach, and bronchospasm. The most frequent side effects are headache, increased heart rate, stomatitis, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, fever, decrease in blood pressure, diarrhea, and noises in the ears.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The US Food and Drug Administration has classified Nacsol 600 mg as category B medicine for pregnant women. Nacsol 600 mg should only be used in pregnant women when clearly needed.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Patients suffering from bronchial asthma must be strictly controlled during the therapy; should bronchospasm occur, the treatment must immediately be suspended. Caution should be taken in patients suffering from or with a history of peptic ulcer. As Nacsol contains sodium (156.9 mg per dose), this has to be taken into consideration by patients on a controlled sodium diet. The possible presence of a sulfurous odor does not indicate an alteration of the product but is a characteristic of the active ingredient contained in this preparation. It is preferable not to mix other drugs with the Nacsol solution.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Nacsol 600 mg</strong></p><p> Overdose of Nacsol 600 mg may cause nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Antidote preparations, Cough expectorants &amp; mucolytics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Nacsol 600 mg is a mucolytic agent that reduces the viscosity of secretions probably by the splitting of disulphide bonds in mucoproteins. Moreover, it gives an antisecretory effect. These results in clearing of respiratory ducts and facilitate breathing. Nacsol 600 mg also has anti-oxidant properties by reacting with free radicals and also by serving as a precursor to glutathione, which is an important intra and extra-cellular antioxidant. By providing antioxidant action, it neutralizes exogenous and endogenous oxidants, which in fact act as pathogens in respiratory inflammations.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> In case of pregnancy &amp; lactation, the medicine should be taken consulting physician or pharmacist.</p>', 'active', '740_1742204569.jpg', 11, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 14:42:49', '2025-03-17 14:42:49'),
(3117, 'Zycin', 'Azithromycin Dihydrate', 35.00, 6, 'Tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zycin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Zycin 200 mg/5 ml is prescribed for infections caused by susceptible organisms in the following areas:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper respiratory tract</strong>: Including sinusitis and pharyngitis/tonsillitis</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract</strong>: Including bronchitis and pneumonia</li><li><strong>Otitis media</strong></li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong></li><li><strong>Sexually transmitted diseases</strong>: Non-gonococcal urethritis and cervicitis caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> in both men and women.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Macrolides</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Zycin 200 mg/5 ml belongs to the macrolide subclass of antibiotics known as azalides. It prevents microbial protein synthesis by binding to the 50s ribosomal subunit of susceptible organisms. It is effective against various bacteria and other organisms, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Moraxella catarrhalis</strong></li><li><strong>Anaerobes</strong> like <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em></li><li><strong>Gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria</strong>, such as <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Escherichia coli</em></li><li><strong>Other organisms</strong>: <em>Bordetella pertussis</em>, <em>Bordetella parapertussis</em>, <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em>, <em>Haemophilus ducreyi</em>, <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>, <em>Campylobacter sp.</em>, <em>Toxoplasma gondii</em>, <em>Treponema pallidum</em>, <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>, <em>Legionella pneumophila</em>, and hominins.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Zycin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">500 mg once daily for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Alternatively, 500 mg on Day 1, followed by 250 mg once daily for the next 4 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For sexually transmitted diseases caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em>, the dose is 1 gm as a single dose or 500 mg on Day 1, followed by 250 mg for 2 more days.</li><li><strong>Children</strong> (Over 6 months of age):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 mg/kg body weight once daily for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">200 mg (1 teaspoonful) for 15-25 kg body weight</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">300 mg (1½ teaspoonfuls) for 26-35 kg body weight</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">400 mg (2 teaspoonfuls) for 36-45 kg body weight</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For typhoid fever, 500 mg once daily for 7-10 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zycin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Shake the bottle thoroughly to break up the powder.</li><li>Fill the bottle with boiled and cooled water to the watermark on the label.</li><li>Mix the powder and water well.</li><li>Take Zycin 200 mg/5 ml at least one hour before or two hours after meals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zycin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Food &amp; Antacids</strong>: May reduce absorption.</li><li><strong>Ergot Alkaloids</strong>: Avoid co-administration due to the risk of ergotism.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporine and Digoxin</strong>: Co-administration may increase their plasma levels.</li><li><strong>Other drugs</strong> (Warfarin, Theophylline, Carbamazepine, Methylprednisolone, Cimetidine): No significant interactions with Zycin 200 mg/5 ml.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Zycin 200 mg/5 ml or other macrolide antibiotics.</li><li>Co-administration with ergot derivatives.</li><li>Liver disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Zycin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Low-frequency side effects may include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vomiting</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Abdominal pain or cramps</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Flatulence</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diarrhea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Headaches</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin rashes</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category</strong>: B.</li><li>Animal studies have shown no harm to the fetus, but there is limited data on human pregnancy. Use only when necessary.</li><li>The presence in breast milk is unknown. Caution is advised when administering to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Watch for signs of superinfection with non-susceptible organisms (e.g., fungi).</li><li>No dose adjustment required for renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zycin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose may include hearing loss, severe nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea.</li><li>Gastric lavage and supportive treatments are recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '341_1742204703.jpg', 64, 12, 'emugp959@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 14:45:03', '2025-03-17 14:45:03'),
(3118, 'Lubrigel', 'Polyethylene Glycol + Propylene Glycol', 200.00, 1, 'drop', '1 drop', 'Solution', '10 ml drop', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Lubrigel 1%</strong></p><p> Carboxymethylcellulose Sodium is indicated for use as a lubricant in dry eye (keratoconjunctivitis sicca), including temporary relief of burning, irritation, and/or discomfort due to dryness of the eye.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for Dry eyes</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Carboxymethylcellulose Sodium acts as an ocular lubricant. It provides a lubricating and hydrating protective shield on the ocular surface.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Lubrigel 1%</strong></p><p> Instill one or two drops in the affected eye(s) as needed.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to any of the components of this preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Lubrigel 1%</strong></p><p> Transient visual disturbance and eye discharge may occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Use in pregnancy: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. This should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefits justify the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p> Use in lactation: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in lactating women. Caution should be exercised when this is administered to a nursing mother.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Lubrigel 1%</strong></p><p> Since CMC is pharmacologically inert and not expected to be absorbed systemically, systemic effects from topical overdose are not expected from the administration of Carboxymethylcellulose sodium (Cellufresh) 0.5%. Additionally, no toxic side effects are expected should accidental systemic overdose occur.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at temperatures not exceeding 25°C.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> Use in children: No information available.</p><p> Use in elderly patients: No information available.</p>', 'active', '842_1742205286.jpeg', 7, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 14:54:46', '2025-03-17 14:54:46'),
(3119, 'Milam', 'Midazolam', 22.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '15 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Milam 15 mg</strong></p><p> Milam 15 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Short-term treatment of insomnia.</li><li>Sedation in premedication before surgical or diagnostic procedures.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Benzodiazepine hypnotics, Benzodiazepine sedatives</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Milam 15 mg binds to stereospecific benzodiazepine receptors on the postsynaptic GABA neuron at several sites within the CNS, including the limbic system and reticular formation. Enhancement of the inhibitory effect of GABA on neuronal excitability results in increased neuronal membrane permeability to Cl ions, which causes hyperpolarization (a less excitable state) and stabilization. Benzodiazepine receptors and effects appear to be linked to the GABA-A receptors.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Milam 15 mg</strong></p><p> The duration of treatment with oral Milam 15 mg should not exceed 2 weeks. In certain cases, extension beyond the maximum treatment period may be necessary.</p><ul><li><strong>Insomnia:</strong></li><li> Adults: 7.5 mg to 15 mg daily (Oral)</li><li> Elderly: 7.5 mg daily (Oral)</li><li><strong>Premedication:</strong></li><li> 7.5 mg to 15 mg, should be given 30-60 minutes before the procedure (Oral)</li><li><strong>Endoscopic or Cardiovascular procedures:</strong></li><li> Adult: Initial dose is 2.5 mg (IV)</li><li> Elderly &amp; debilitated patients: 1-1.5 mg (IV)</li><li><strong>Induction of Anesthesia:</strong></li><li> Adult: 10-15 mg (IV) or 0.07-0.1 mg/kg body weight, usually 5 mg (IM)</li><li> Children: 0.15-0.20 mg/kg (IM)</li><li> Elderly &amp; debilitated patients: 0.025-0.05 mg/kg (IM)</li><li><strong>Rectal administration in children (preoperative sedation):</strong></li><li> 0.35-0.45 mg/kg, 20-30 minutes before induction of general anesthesia</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Milam 15 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral dosage:</strong></li><li> Adults: 7.5-15 mg daily</li><li> Elderly and debilitated patients: 7.5 mg</li><li> In premedication: 15 mg should be given 30-60 minutes before the procedure.</li><li><strong>Intravenous administration (Endoscopic or Cardiovascular procedures):</strong></li><li> Healthy adults: Initial dose is 2.5 mg</li><li> Severe illness/elderly: Reduce dose to 1-1.5 mg</li><li><strong>Induction of Anesthesia (Intravenous):</strong></li><li> 10-15 mg</li><li><strong>Intramuscular administration:</strong></li><li> Adult: 0.07-0.1 mg/kg body weight (about 5 mg)</li><li> Children: 0.15-0.20 mg/kg</li><li> Elderly &amp; debilitated patients: 0.025-0.05 mg/kg</li><li><strong>Rectal administration in children (preoperative sedation):</strong></li><li> 0.35-0.45 mg/kg, 20-30 minutes before induction of general anesthesia</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Milam 15 mg</strong></p><p> Milam 15 mg can enhance the central sedative effect of neuroleptics, tranquillizers, antidepressants, sleep-inducing drugs, analgesics, anaesthetics, antipsychotics, anxiolytics, antiepileptic drugs, and sedative antihistamines.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Milam 15 mg must not be given to patients with:</p><ul><li>Severe respiratory insufficiency</li><li>Severe hepatic insufficiency</li><li>Myasthenia gravis</li><li>Sleep apnea syndrome</li><li>Known hypersensitivity to benzodiazepines or any component of the product</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Milam 15 mg</strong></p><p> At the start of therapy, drowsiness during daytime, confusion, fatigue, headache, and muscle weakness may occur, which usually disappear with repeated administration. Following parenteral (IV or IM) administration, fluctuations in vital signs may occur, including respiratory depression, apnea, variations in blood pressure and pulse rate.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Insufficient data are available to assess Milam 15 mg\'s safety during pregnancy, but benzodiazepines adversely affect the human fetus. Its use should be avoided if a safer alternative is available.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Milam 15 mg is excreted through breast milk, and should not be used by nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Milam 15 mg IV should be administered very slowly.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Milam 15 mg</strong></p><p> Extreme overdosage may lead to coma, areflexia, cardiorespiratory depression, and apnea. The effects of overdosage can be controlled with the benzodiazepine antagonist flumazenil.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Protect from light and moisture. Store in a cool and dry place. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Benzodiazepine hypnotics, Benzodiazepine sedatives</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> The actions of benzodiazepines such as Milam 15 mg are mediated through the inhibitory neurotransmitter gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA), which is one of the major inhibitory neurotransmitters in the central nervous system. Benzodiazepines increase the activity of GABA, thereby producing a sedating effect, relaxing skeletal muscles, and inducing sleep, anesthesia, and amnesia. Benzodiazepines bind to the benzodiazepine site on GABA-A receptors, which potentiates the effects of GABA by increasing the frequency of chloride channel opening. These receptors are present in different body tissues, including the heart and skeletal muscle, but mainly appear to be located in the central nervous system.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Milam 15 mg should be avoided during pregnancy unless there is no safer alternative. Since Milam 15 mg passes into breast milk, it should not be administered to breastfeeding mothers.</p>', 'active', '1153_1742205452.jpg', 26, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 14:57:32', '2025-03-17 14:57:32');
