(3120, 'Nitanid', 'Nitazoxanide', 10.05, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nitanid 500 mg</strong></p><p> Nitanid 500 mg is indicated for the treatment of diarrhea caused by <em>Cryptosporidium parvum, Giardia lamblia,</em> and <em>Entamoeba histolytica.</em></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-diarrhoeal Antiprotozoal</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Nitanid 500 mg is a synthetic antiprotozoal agent for oral administration. The antiprotozoal activity of Nitanid 500 mg is believed to be interference with the Pyruvate Ferredoxin Oxido Reductase (PFOR) enzyme-dependent electron transfer reaction. This reaction is essential for anaerobic energy metabolism of the protozoa. Nitanid 500 mg and its metabolite, tizoxanid, are active <em>in vitro</em> in inhibiting the growth of sporozoites and oocysts of <em>Cryptosporidium parvum</em> and trophozoites of <em>Giardia lamblia.</em></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Nitanid 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Age 1-3 years:</strong> 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) suspension every 12 hours for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Age 4-11 years:</strong> 2 teaspoonfuls (10 ml) suspension every 12 hours for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Age 12 years or above:</strong> 5 teaspoonfuls (25 ml) suspension or 1 tablet every 12 hours for 3 days.</li></ul><p>It is recommended to be administered with food.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Nitanid 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Age 1-3 years:</strong> 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) suspension every 12 hours for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Age 4-11 years:</strong> 2 teaspoonfuls (10 ml) suspension every 12 hours for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Age 12 years or above:</strong> 5 teaspoonfuls (25 ml) suspension or 1 tablet every 12 hours for 3 days.</li></ul><p>It is recommended to be administered with food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Nitanid 500 mg</strong></p><p> It is highly bound to plasma protein. Therefore, caution should be exercised when administering Nitanid 500 mg concurrently with other highly plasma protein-bound drugs with a narrow therapeutic index.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Nitanid 500 mg or any components of the preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Nitanid 500 mg</strong></p><p> The most frequent side effects reported for Nitanid 500 mg are abdominal pain, vomiting, and headache. These side effects are typically mild and transient in nature. Very rare side effects include nausea, anorexia, flatulence, increased appetite, enlarged salivary glands, increased creatinine &amp; SGPT levels, pruritus, rhinitis, sweating, dizziness, and discolored urine.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> US FDA pregnancy category of Nitanid 500 mg is <strong>B</strong>. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Nitanid 500 mg has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Nitanid 500 mg is administered during lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Nitanid 500 mg must be administered with caution to patients with hepatic &amp; biliary disease and to patients with renal disease.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Anti-diarrhoeal Antiprotozoal</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Nitanid 500 mg is a synthetic antiprotozoal agent for oral administration. The antiprotozoal activity of Nitanid 500 mg is believed to be interference with the Pyruvate Ferredoxin Oxido Reductase (PFOR) enzyme-dependent electron transfer reaction. This reaction is essential for anaerobic energy metabolism of the protozoa. Nitanid 500 mg and its metabolite, tizoxanid, are active <em>in vitro</em> in inhibiting the growth of sporozoites and oocysts of <em>Cryptosporidium parvum</em> and trophozoites of <em>Giardia lamblia.</em></p><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> US FDA pregnancy category of Nitanid 500 mg is <strong>B</strong>. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Nitanid 500 mg has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Nitanid 500 mg is administered during lactation.</p>', 'active', '127_1742216718.jpg', 9, 12, 'fazlearabbi97@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 18:05:18', '2025-03-17 18:05:18'),
(3121, 'Sylhep', 'Silymarin [Dried extract of milk thistle fruits]', 11.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '140 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sylhep 140 mg</strong></p><p> For the treatment of jaundice, chronic inflammatory liver conditions, i.e., hepatitis, alcoholic liver damage, and hepatic cirrhosis. It has antioxidant properties.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Cholagogues, Cholelitholytics &amp; Hepatic Protectors, Herbal and Nutraceuticals</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sylhep 140 mg possesses hepatoprotective and antioxidant activity. The hepatoprotective effect is due to stimulation of the synthesis of structural and functional proteins and phospholipids, as well as acceleration of the regeneration of hepatocytes. The antioxidant effect is determined by the interaction of bioflavones with free radicals in the liver and its detoxification. In this manner, the process of lipid peroxidation is interrupted, and further liver destruction is prevented. Clinically, these effects are manifested by improvement of the signs and symptoms and normalization of liver variables (serum level of transaminases, gamma-globulins, and bilirubin).</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sylhep 140 mg</strong></p><p> 70 mg, 140 mg, or 500 mg capsules should be taken 3 times daily as per the instruction of a physician. The medication should be continued until symptom relief, according to the advice of a physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Sylhep 140 mg</strong></p><p> Not known.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> No adequate data of investigations are available about the use of this drug in children. Therefore, it should be used in children under 12 years of age with caution under the direct supervision of a physician.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sylhep 140 mg</strong></p><p> A mild laxative effect has occasionally been observed.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> No experience is available about the use of Sylhep 140 mg, 70 mg, or 140 mg during pregnancy and lactation. Therefore, if needed, Sylhep 140 mg, 70 mg, or 140 mg should be taken with caution according to the physician’s advice.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> To be stored in a cool and dry place, away from direct sunlight. Keep the medicine out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Disclaimer:</strong> The information provided here is for reference only and should not replace professional medical advice. Always consult your physician or healthcare provider before taking any medication.</p>', 'active', '1135_1742219198.jpg', 8, 12, 'fazlearabbi97@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 18:46:38', '2025-03-17 18:46:38'),
(3122, 'Nitrofur SR', 'Nitrofurantoin', 4.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nitrofur SR 50 mg</strong></p><p> Nitrofur SR 50 mg is specifically indicated for the treatment &amp; prophylaxis of urinary tract infections caused by susceptible strains of <em>Escherichia coli, Enterococci, Staphylococcus aureus, Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em>, and certain susceptible strains of <em>Klebsiella</em> and <em>Enterobacter</em> species.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Systemic Urinary Anti-infective</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Nitrofur SR 50 mg interferes with cell metabolism and cell wall synthesis by inhibiting several enzyme systems, including acetyl coenzyme A. It is bactericidal to most gram-positive and gram-negative urinary tract pathogens.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Nitrofur SR 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Nitrofur SR 50 mg tablet (Adults)</strong><em>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections:</em> 50-100 mg four times a day. The lower dosage level is recommended. Therapy should be continued for one week or for at least 3 days after sterility of the urine is obtained.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Long-term suppressive therapy:</em> 50-100 mg at bedtime may be adequate.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Nitrofur SR 50 mg capsule (Adults)</strong><em>Acute uncomplicated UTIs:</em> 50 mg four times daily for 7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Long-term suppression:</em> 50-100 mg once a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Prophylaxis:</em> 50 mg four times daily for the duration of procedure and for three days thereafter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Nitrofur SR 50 mg SR capsule (Adults &amp; children over 12 years)</strong>One 100 mg capsule every 12 hours for seven days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Genito-urinary surgical prophylaxis:</em> One capsule twice daily on the day of the procedure and for the next 3 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Nitrofur SR 50 mg suspension (Children)</strong>5-7 mg/kg/day in four divided doses (<em>contraindicated under one month of age</em>).</li><li>Average pediatric doses:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">7 to 11 kg: 2.5 ml (½ teaspoon) 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">12 to 21 kg: 5 ml (1 teaspoon) 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">22 to 30 kg: 7.5 ml (1½ teaspoons) 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">31 to 41 kg: 10 ml (2 teaspoons) 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Therapy should continue for one week or at least 3 days after urine sterility.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>For long-term suppressive therapy in children:</em> 1 mg/kg per 24 hours, given in a single dose or two divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Nitrofur SR 50 mg</strong></p><p> Nitrofur SR 50 mg should be taken with food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Nitrofur SR 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Antacids containing Magnesium Trisilicate reduce both the rate and extent of absorption.</li><li>Uricosuric drugs (Probenecid, Sulfinpyrazone) can inhibit renal tubular secretion of Nitrofur SR 50 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with anuria, oliguria, or significant renal impairment.</li><li>Not recommended for pregnant patients at 38-42 weeks, during labor, or delivery.</li><li>Contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to Nitrofur SR 50 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Nitrofur SR 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Nausea, headache, flatulence.</li><li>Less frequent: Diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain, constipation, emesis, dizziness, drowsiness.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> No adequate studies in pregnant women. Use only if clearly needed.</li><li>Detected in human breast milk in trace amounts.</li><li>If nursing an infant under one month, consider discontinuing either the drug or breastfeeding based on its importance to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Discontinue use if acute, sub-acute, or chronic pulmonary reactions occur.</li><li>Avoid taking antacids containing magnesium trisilicate while using Nitrofur SR 50 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Nitrofur SR 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose incidents are rare, with vomiting as the primary symptom.</li><li>Emesis induction is recommended in case of overdose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C.</li><li>Keep away from light and out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Systemic Urinary Anti-infective</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Nitrofur SR 50 mg is an antibacterial agent specific for urinary tract infections.</li><li>Highly soluble in urine, giving it a brownish color.</li><li>It inactivates or alters bacterial ribosomal proteins, affecting bacterial macromolecules.</li><li>Active against:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gram-positive aerobes:</strong> <em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus, Coagulase-negative staphylococci (including Staphylococcus epidermidis), Enterococcus faecalis, Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus agalactiae, Group D streptococci, Viridans group streptococci.</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gram-negative aerobes:</strong> <em>Escherichia coli, Citrobacter amalonaticus, Citrobacter diversus, Citrobacter freundii, Klebsiella oxytoca, Klebsiella ozaenae.</em></li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Nitrofur SR 50 mg has been detected in human breast milk in trace amounts. Because of the potential for serious adverse reactions from Nitrofur SR 50 mg in nursing infants under one month of age, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or to discontinue the drug, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</p>', 'active', '1979_1742220645.jpg', 44, 12, 'fazlearabbi97@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 19:10:45', '2025-03-17 19:10:45'),
(3123, 'Mitadip', 'Mirtazapine', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '15 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Mitadip 15 mg</strong></p><p> Mitadip 15 mg Tablets are indicated for the treatment of <strong>major depressive disorder (MDD)</strong>.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Atypical anti-depressant drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Mitadip 15 mg, a piperazinoazepine tetracyclic antidepressant, enhances noradrenergic and serotonergic activity through blockade of central presynaptic adrenergic α2-receptors.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Mitadip 15 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult:</strong> Initially, 15 mg daily; may be increased gradually depending on clinical response. Change dose at intervals of at least 1-2 weeks. Usual effective dose: 15-45 mg daily given as a single dose, preferably at bedtime, or in 2 divided doses.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> Safety and efficacy not established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric:</strong> Use with caution.</li></ul><p><strong>Missed Dose:</strong></p><p> If anyone misses a dose of Mitadip 15 mg, take it as soon as remembered unless it is close to when the next dose is due. If the missed dose is close to the next scheduled dose, skip the missed dose and take the next dose at the usual time. Do not take a double dose.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Mitadip 15 mg</strong></p><p> Mitadip 15 mg has clinically significant drug-drug interactions with <strong>Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAOI)</strong> and other serotonergic drugs such as <strong>triptophan, triptans, linezolid, serotonin reuptake inhibitors, venlafaxine, lithium, tramadol,</strong> or <strong>St. John\'s wort</strong>. Mitadip 15 mg may interrupt the metabolism or activity of <strong>Carbamazepine, Phenytoin,</strong> or <strong>Cimetidine</strong>. Patients should avoid <strong>Alcohol</strong> and <strong>Diazepam</strong> while taking Mitadip 15 mg.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity:</strong> Mitadip 15 mg is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to Mitadip 15 mg or to any of the excipients.</li><li><strong>Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors:</strong> The concomitant use of Mitadip 15 mg and a monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitor is contraindicated. Mitadip 15 mg should not be used within 14 days of initiating or discontinuing therapy with an MAOI.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Mitadip 15 mg</strong></p><p> The most common side effects of Mitadip 15 mg include <strong>dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, increased appetite,</strong> and <strong>weight gain</strong>.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Mitadip 15 mg should <strong>not</strong> be used during <strong>pregnancy</strong> and <strong>lactation</strong>.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Patients, their families, and caregivers should be vigilant for the emergence of <strong>anxiety, agitation, panic attacks, insomnia, irritability, hostility, aggressiveness, impulsivity, akathisia (psychomotor restlessness), hypomania, mania, other unusual changes in behavior, worsening of depression,</strong> and <strong>suicidal ideation</strong>, especially early during antidepressant treatment and when the dose is adjusted.</li><li>Mitadip 15 mg may impair judgment, thinking, and particularly, motor skills due to its prominent sedative effect.</li><li>Patients should be warned about the risk of developing <strong>agranulocytosis</strong>.</li><li>Clinically significant ALT (SGPT) elevations (≥3 times the upper limit of the normal range) may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Mitadip 15 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms:</strong> Disorientation, drowsiness, impaired memory, tachycardia.</li><li><strong>Management:</strong> Ensure an adequate airway, oxygenation, and ventilation. Monitor cardiac functions. General supportive and symptomatic measures are recommended. Do not induce emesis. Gastric lavage may be used if done soon after ingestion, or in symptomatic patients. Administer activated charcoal. No specific antidotes are known.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep away from light and moisture. Store below 30º C. Keep all medicine out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Atypical anti-depressant drugs</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> <strong>Pharmacodynamics:</strong> The mechanism of action of Mitadip 15 mg, like other drugs effective in the treatment of major depressive disorder, is unknown. Evidence from preclinical studies suggests that Mitadip 15 mg enhances central noradrenergic and serotonergic activity by acting as an antagonist at central presynaptic α2-adrenergic inhibitory autoreceptors and heteroreceptors. This action is believed to increase central noradrenergic and serotonergic activity. Mitadip 15 mg is a potent antagonist of 5-HT2 and 5-HT3 receptors.</p><p> Mitadip 15 mg is a potent antagonist of <strong>histamine (H1) receptors</strong>, which may explain its prominent sedative effects.</p><p> Mitadip 15 mg also acts as a <strong>moderate antagonist</strong> at <strong>muscarinic receptors</strong>, contributing to its relatively low incidence of anticholinergic side effects.</p><p><strong>Pharmacokinetics:</strong></p><p> After oral administration, Mitadip 15 mg is rapidly and well-absorbed, reaching peak plasma levels after about 2 hours. It is approximately <strong>85%</strong> bound to plasma proteins. The mean half-life of elimination is <strong>20-40 hours</strong>, with a slight difference between males and females (26 hours for males, 37 hours for females). This half-life supports once-a-day dosing. Mitadip 15 mg undergoes extensive metabolism and is eliminated via the urine and feces over four days. Major biotransformation pathways include demethylation and oxidation followed by conjugation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category <strong>C</strong>. Patients should inform their physician if they become pregnant or intend to become pregnant during Mitadip 15 mg therapy. Patients should also notify their physician if they are breastfeeding.</p>', 'active', '1473_1742226118.png', 6, 12, 'fazlearabbi97@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 20:41:58', '2025-03-17 20:41:58'),
(3124, 'LK Plus', 'Losartan Potassium + Hydrochlorothiazide', 9.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg+12.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Description.........</p>', 'active', '46_1742227551.jpg', 56, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:05:51', '2025-03-17 21:05:51'),
