(3129, 'Lubric D', 'Dextran + Hypromellose', 90.00, 1, 'drop', '1 drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '10 ml drop', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Description</p>', 'active', '1970_1742228089.jpg', 2, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:14:49', '2025-03-17 21:14:49'),
(3130, 'Ticoma DS', 'Timolol Maleate', 70.26, 1, 'drop', '1 drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '5 ml drop', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Description</p>', 'active', '304_1742228309.jpg', 7, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:18:29', '2025-03-17 21:18:29'),
(3131, 'Ormin', 'Metformin Hydrochloride', 5.01, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '850 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the requested information with the headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Ormin 850 mg</strong></p><p> Treatment of type 2 diabetes mellitus, particularly in overweight patients when dietary management and exercise alone does not result in adequate glycaemic control. In adults: Metformin may be used as monotherapy or in combination with other oral antidiabetic agents or with insulin. In children from 10 years of age and adolescents: Metformin may be used as monotherapy or in combination with insulin. A reduction of diabetic complications has been shown in overweight type 2 diabetic adult patients treated with metformin as first-line therapy after diet failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Biguanides</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Metformin is a biguanide type oral antihyperglycemic drug used in the management of type 2 diabetes. It lowers both basal and postprandial plasma glucose. Its mechanism of action is different from those of sulfonylureas and it does not produce hypoglycemia. Metformin decreases hepatic glucose production, decreases intestinal absorption of glucose, and improves insulin sensitivity by an increase in peripheral glucose uptake and utilization.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ormin 850 mg</strong></p><p> Metformin immediate release tablet: Dosage of Ormin 850 mg must be individualized on the basis of both effectiveness and tolerance, while not exceeding the maximum recommended daily doses.</p><p><strong>Adult</strong>: The usual starting dose of Metformin is 500 mg twice a day or 850 mg once a day, given with meals. Dosage increases should be made in increments of 500 mg weekly or 850 mg every 2 weeks, up to a total of 2000 mg per day, given in divided doses. For those patients requiring additional glycemic control, Glucomin may be given to a maximum daily dose of 2550 mg per day. Doses above 2000 mg may be better tolerated given three times a day with meals.</p><p><strong>Children</strong>: The usual starting dose of Metformin is 500 mg twice a day, given with meals. Dosage increases should be made in increments of 500 mg weekly up to a maximum of 2000 mg per day, given in divided doses.</p><p><strong>Metformin extended release tablet</strong>: Swallow Metformin XR tablet whole and never crush, cut or chew.</p><p><strong>Adult</strong>: The usual starting dose of Metformin XR is 500 mg once daily with the evening meal. Dose should be increased in increments of 500 mg weekly, up to a maximum of 2000 mg once daily with the evening meal, alternatively increased to 1000 mg twice daily taken with meals. Patient receiving Metformin immediate release tablet may be switched to Metformin extended release tablet up to a maximum recommended daily dose.</p><p><strong>Children</strong>: Metformin extended release tablet has not been studied in children.</p><p><strong>Renal impaired patient</strong>: Do not use Metformin in patients with eGFR below 30 mL/min/1.73 m². Assess risk/benefit of continuing if eGFR falls below 45 mL/min/1.73 m².</p><p><strong>Interaction of Ormin 850 mg</strong></p><p> Co-administration with Carbonic anhydrase (Topiramate, Zonisamide) may increase the risk of lactic acidosis. Drugs (Ranolazine, Dolutegravir, Cimetidine) that reduce Metformin clearance may increase the accumulation of Metformin. Alcohol can potentiate the effect of Metformin on lactate metabolism.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to the active substance or to any of the excipients. Any type of acute metabolic acidosis (such as lactic acidosis, diabetic ketoacidosis). Severe renal failure (GFR &lt;30 mL/min). Acute conditions with the potential to alter renal function such as: dehydration, severe infection, shock. Acute or chronic disease, which may cause tissue hypoxia such as: cardiac or respiratory failure, recent myocardial infarction, shock, Hepatic insufficiency, acute alcohol intoxication, alcoholism.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ormin 850 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Blood and lymphatic system disorders</strong>: Not known: Hemolytic anemia</li><li><strong>Metabolism and nutrition disorders</strong>: Very rare: Lactic acidosis. Decrease of vitamin B12 absorption with a decrease of serum levels during long-term use of metformin. Consideration of such etiology is recommended if a patient presents with megaloblastic anemia. Cases of peripheral neuropathy in patients with vitamin B12 deficiency have been reported in post-marketing experience (frequency not known).</li><li><strong>Nervous system disorders</strong>: Common: Taste disturbance. Not known: Encephalopathy</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal disorders</strong>: Very common: Gastrointestinal disorders, such as nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, and loss of appetite. These undesirable effects occur most frequently during the initiation of therapy and resolve spontaneously in most cases. To prevent them, it is recommended that metformin be taken in 2 or 3 daily doses during or after meals. A slow increase of the dose may also improve gastrointestinal tolerability.</li><li><strong>Hepatobiliary disorders</strong>: Very rare: Isolated reports of liver function test abnormalities or hepatitis resolving upon metformin discontinuation.</li><li><strong>Skin and subcutaneous tissue disorders</strong>: Very rare: Skin reactions, such as erythema, pruritus, urticaria.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Published studies have not reported a clear association with Metformin and major birth defects, miscarriage, or adverse maternal or fetal outcomes when Metformin was used during pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Lactation</strong>: Metformin is excreted into human breast milk. No adverse effects were observed in breastfed newborns/infants. However, a decision on whether to discontinue breastfeeding should be made or taking into account the benefit of breastfeeding and the potential risk to adverse effects on the child.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Ormin 850 mg is known to be substantially excreted by the kidney and the risk of Metformin accumulation and lactic acidosis increases with the degree of impairment of renal function. Metformin may lower vitamin B12 level. It also increases the risk of hypoglycemia when used in combination with insulin or insulin secretagogue.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ormin 850 mg</strong></p><p> Hypoglycemia has not been seen with Metformin doses up to 85 gm, although lactic acidosis has occurred in such circumstances. Lactic acidosis is a medical emergency and must be treated in hospital. The most effective method to remove lactate and Metformin is hemodialysis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C temperature, protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Biguanides</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Metformin is a biguanide type oral antihyperglycemic drug used in the management of type 2 diabetes. It lowers both basal and postprandial plasma glucose. Its mechanism of action is different from those of sulfonylureas and it does not produce hypoglycemia. Metformin decreases hepatic glucose production, decreases intestinal absorption of glucose, and improves insulin sensitivity by an increase in peripheral glucose uptake and utilization.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Uncontrolled diabetes during pregnancy (gestational or permanent) is associated with an increased risk of congenital abnormalities and perinatal mortality. When the patient plans to become pregnant and during pregnancy, it is recommended that diabetes is not treated with metformin but insulin be used to maintain blood glucose levels as close to normal as possible, to reduce the risk of malformations of the fetus.</p><p><strong>Breastfeeding</strong></p><p> Metformin is excreted into human breast milk. No adverse effects were observed in breastfed newborns/infants. However, as only limited data are available, breastfeeding is not recommended during metformin treatment. A decision on whether to discontinue breastfeeding should be made, taking into account the benefit of breastfeeding and the potential risk to adverse effects on the child.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Due to the potential for decreased renal function in elderly subjects, the metformin dosage should be adjusted based on renal function. Regular assessment of renal function is necessary.</li><li><strong>Pediatric population</strong>: The diagnosis of type 2 diabetes mellitus should be confirmed before treatment with metformin is initiated. No effect of metformin on growth and puberty has been detected during controlled clinical studies of one-year duration, but no long-term data on these specific points are available. Therefore, a careful follow-up of the effect of metformin on these parameters in metformin-treated children, especially prepubescent children, is recommended.</li><li><strong>Children aged between 10 and 12 years</strong>: Particular caution is recommended when prescribing to children aged between 10 and 12 years.</li><li><strong>Renal function</strong>: As metformin is excreted by the kidney, creatinine clearance (this can be estimated from serum creatinine levels by using the Cockcroft-Gault formula) should be determined before initiating treatment and regularly thereafter:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">At least annually in patients with normal renal function.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">At least two to four times a year in patients with creatinine clearance at the lower limit of normal and in elderly subjects.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Decreased renal function in elderly subjects is frequent and asymptomatic. Special caution should be exercised in situations where renal function may become impaired, for example when initiating antihypertensive therapy or diuretic therapy and when starting therapy with a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">GFR should be assessed before treatment initiation and regularly thereafter. Metformin is contraindicated in patients with GFR &lt;30 ml/min and should be temporarily discontinued in the presence of conditions that alter renal function.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '540_1742228321.jpeg', 43, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:18:41', '2025-03-17 21:18:41'),
(3132, 'Salpac', 'Sodium Chloride', 47.87, 1, 'bag', '1bag', 'IV Infusion', '100 ml bag', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Salpac 0.9%</strong></h3><p>Salpac 0.9% intravenous solutions are indicated for use in adults and pediatric patients as sources of electrolytes and water for hydration. It is used for:</p><ul><li><strong>Extracellular fluid replacement</strong></li><li><strong>Treatment of metabolic alkalosis</strong> (in the presence of fluid loss)</li><li><strong>Mild sodium depletion</strong></li><li><strong>Hemodialysis procedures</strong> (used as a priming solution)</li><li><strong>Blood transfusions</strong> (used to initiate and terminate transfusions without hemolyzing red blood cells)</li><li><strong>Pharmaceutical aid and diluent</strong> for the infusion of compatible drug additives (refer to prescribing information for specific drugs).</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p>Intravenous fluid preparations</p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Sodium chloride is the major extracellular cation responsible for:</p><ul><li>Maintaining <strong>electrolyte and fluid balance</strong></li><li>Regulating <strong>osmotic pressure and water distribution</strong></li><li>Treating <strong>metabolic acidosis, hyperosmolar diabetes, and fluid loss</strong></li><li>Acting as a <strong>diluent for compatible drug additives</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Salpac 0.9%</strong></h3><p><strong>Route:</strong> Intravenous (IV) use only. The dosage is determined by a physician based on:</p><ul><li><strong>Age and weight</strong></li><li><strong>Clinical condition</strong></li><li><strong>Laboratory findings</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Dosage Guidelines:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> Daily requirements of sodium and chloride are typically met with <strong>one liter of Salpac 0.9%</strong> (contains 154 mEq of sodium and chloride each).</li><li><strong>Pediatrics:</strong> No fixed dose—determined by weight, condition, and laboratory results. Follow pediatric reference guidelines.</li><li><strong>Hemodialysis:</strong> Used intravascularly as a <strong>priming fluid</strong>.</li><li><strong>Diluent for compatible drugs:</strong> Follow the <strong>prescribing information</strong> of the additive drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Important Administration Guidelines:</strong></p><ul><li>Always use <strong>aseptic techniques</strong> when introducing additives.</li><li><strong>Mix thoroughly</strong> and do not store after mixing.</li><li><strong>Visually inspect</strong> for particulate matter or discoloration before administration.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><p>This solution is contraindicated in patients where the administration of sodium or chloride could be <strong>clinically detrimental</strong>.</p><h3><strong>Side Effects of Salpac 0.9%</strong></h3><p>Possible reactions due to the solution or administration technique include:</p><ul><li><strong>Febrile response</strong></li><li><strong>Injection site infection</strong></li><li><strong>Venous thrombosis or phlebitis</strong></li><li><strong>Extravasation (leakage into surrounding tissue)</strong></li><li><strong>Hypervolemia (fluid overload)</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Additional Risks:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypernatremia</strong>: May lead to <strong>edema and worsening of congestive heart failure</strong> due to fluid retention.</li><li><strong>Excess chloride ions</strong>: May cause <strong>bicarbonate ion loss</strong>, resulting in <strong>acidosis</strong>.</li><li>If adverse reactions occur, <strong>discontinue infusion, evaluate the patient, and take appropriate measures</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><p><strong>Pregnancy (Category C):</strong></p><ul><li>No animal studies have been conducted.</li><li>It is unknown if Salpac 0.9% Injection can harm a fetus.</li><li>Should only be used during pregnancy <strong>if clearly needed</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>It is unknown whether this drug is <strong>excreted in human milk</strong>.</li><li>Caution is advised when administering to nursing mothers.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Monitor fluid balance, electrolytes, and acid-base levels</strong> during prolonged IV therapy.</li><li>Use <strong>cautiously in patients with</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypervolemia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Renal insufficiency</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Urinary tract obstruction</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Cardiac decompensation</li><li><strong>Electrolyte losses</strong> from prolonged <strong>vomiting, diarrhea, or nasogastric suctioning</strong> may require additional supplementation.</li><li><strong>Corticosteroid &amp; corticotropin users:</strong> Monitor for sodium retention risks.</li><li><strong>Elderly &amp; post-operative patients:</strong> Require careful sodium administration to avoid <strong>hypernatremia and acidosis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Additive Compatibility:</strong> Inspect for <strong>cloudiness or precipitation</strong> when mixing with other solutions.</li><li><strong>Avoid air embolism:</strong> Do <strong>not connect plastic containers in series</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Salpac 0.9%</strong></h3><ul><li>Excessive administration may lead to <strong>fluid or solute overload</strong>.</li><li>If this occurs, <strong>reassess the patient’s condition and initiate corrective treatment</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><p><strong>Pediatrics:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and effectiveness have not been established in <strong>clinical trials</strong>.</li><li>Pediatric use is referenced in medical literature.</li><li><strong>Monitor electrolyte levels carefully</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatrics:</strong></p><ul><li>No significant clinical differences in response between elderly and younger patients.</li><li><strong>Elderly patients</strong> are more likely to have <strong>renal impairment</strong>, requiring cautious dosing.</li><li>Monitor <strong>renal function</strong> to reduce the risk of toxicity.</li></ul><h1><br></h1><p><br></p>', 'active', '347_1742228590.jpeg', 43, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:23:10', '2025-03-17 21:23:10'),
(3133, 'ZincFast', 'Zinc Orotate', 5.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '60 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of ZincFast 60 mg</h3><p>Oral zinc therapy is indicated for zinc deficiency and zinc-losing conditions, such as:</p><ul><li>Hair loss</li><li>Skin rashes</li><li>Major depression</li><li>Loss of smell, taste, and memory</li><li>White spots on the fingernails</li><li>Increased susceptibility to infection</li><li>Loss of activity of growth hormone (GH)</li><li>Visual impairment</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Male infertility</li><li>Sickle cell anemia</li><li>Delayed wound healing</li><li>Type-2 diabetes</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Specific mineral preparations</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Zinc, a major mineral, is involved in nearly all cellular and enzymatic processes in the body. Its bioavailability and digestion improve significantly when bound to orotic acid. As an essential component of over 80 enzymes, zinc plays a crucial role in metabolism and has antioxidant and anti-free radical properties.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of ZincFast 60 mg</h3><p><strong>Adults:</strong> One tablet <strong>twice daily</strong> with a meal.</p><h3>Interaction of ZincFast 60 mg</h3><p>Taking zinc with <strong>tetracyclines, quinolones, or penicillamine</strong> may reduce the absorption of both zinc and these medications.</p><h3>Side Effects of ZincFast 60 mg</h3><p>Possible side effects include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Metallic taste</li><li>Stomach upset</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><p>Use during pregnancy or lactation <strong>only after consulting a healthcare professional</strong>.</p><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Patients with <strong>diabetes</strong> and <strong>acute renal failure</strong> should use zinc products with caution.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of ZincFast 60 mg</h3><p>Doses higher than <strong>40 mg (elemental zinc) daily</strong> may cause:</p><ul><li>Fever</li><li>Cough</li><li>Upset stomach</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><p>Store in a <strong>cool and dry place</strong>, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1814_1742228789.jpg', 11, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:26:29', '2025-03-17 21:26:29');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3134, 'Neofloxin', 'Ciprofloxacin', 18.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '750 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Neofloxin 750 mg</h3><p>Neofloxin 750 mg is used for treating single or mixed infections caused by two or more susceptible organisms. It is also effective against infections caused by organisms resistant to other antibiotics, including <strong>Aminoglycosides, Penicillins, and Cephalosporins</strong>. Due to its extensive tissue penetration and antibacterial activity (including <strong>antipseudomonal activity</strong>), Neofloxin 750 mg can be used alone or in combination with other antibiotics in severe cases.</p><p><strong>Indications include:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Severe systemic infections</strong>: Septicemia, bacteremia, peritonitis, infections in immunosuppressed patients, intensive care unit infections.</li><li><strong>Respiratory tract infections</strong>: Lobar and bronchopneumonia, acute and chronic bronchitis, cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis, empyema.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: Urethritis, cystitis, pyelonephritis, prostatitis, epididymitis.</li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: Infected ulcers, wound infections, abscesses, cellulitis, otitis externa, erysipelas, infected burns.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal infections</strong>: Enteric fever, infective diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Biliary tract infections</strong>: Cholangitis, cholecystitis, gallbladder empyema.</li><li><strong>Intra-abdominal infections</strong>: Peritonitis, intra-abdominal abscesses.</li><li><strong>Bone and joint infections</strong>: Osteomyelitis, septic arthritis.</li><li><strong>Pelvic infections</strong>: Salpingitis, endometritis, pelvic inflammatory disease.</li><li><strong>Eye, ear, nose, and throat infections</strong>: Otitis media, sinusitis, mastoiditis, tonsillitis.</li><li><strong>Gonorrhea</strong>: Urethral, rectal, and pharyngeal gonorrhea caused by beta-lactamase-producing organisms or penicillin-resistant strains.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p><strong>4-Quinolone preparations, Anti-diarrhoeal Antimicrobial drugs</strong></p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Neofloxin 750 mg is a <strong>synthetic fluoroquinolone</strong> with bactericidal activity against a broad range of gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria. It inhibits <strong>bacterial DNA synthesis</strong> by targeting DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV, enzymes responsible for DNA supercoiling.</p><h3>Dosage of Neofloxin 750 mg</h3><p>The dosage depends on infection severity, organism sensitivity, patient age, weight, and renal function.</p><p><strong>Adults:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: 250-500 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Respiratory tract infections</strong>: 250-500 mg twice daily, or 750 mg twice daily for <strong>Streptococcus pneumoniae</strong> infections.</li><li><strong>Gonorrhea</strong>: Single dose of 250 or 500 mg.</li><li><strong>Other infections</strong>: 500-750 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Cystic fibrosis with pseudomonal infections</strong>: 750 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: Adjust dose for <strong>serum creatinine &gt;265 µmol/L</strong> or <strong>creatinine clearance &lt;20 mL/min</strong> (reduce daily dose by half).</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dose adjustment required.</li><li><strong>Children/Adolescents</strong>: Not recommended unless benefits outweigh risks; if used, <strong>7.5-15 mg/kg/day in two divided doses</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Duration of treatment:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Acute infections</strong>: <strong>5-10 days</strong>, or three days after symptoms disappear.</li><li><strong>Extended-release tablets (UTI)</strong>: <strong>1000 mg once daily for three days</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Intravenous (IV) infusion doses:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>UTI</strong>: 200 mg <strong>12-hourly</strong> (mild to moderate) or 400 mg <strong>12-hourly</strong> (severe).</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong>: 400 mg <strong>12-hourly</strong> (mild) or <strong>8-hourly</strong> (severe).</li><li><strong>Nosocomial pneumonia</strong>: 400 mg <strong>8-hourly</strong> for <strong>10-14 days</strong>.</li><li><strong>Skin &amp; soft tissue infections</strong>: 400 mg <strong>12-hourly</strong> (mild) or <strong>8-hourly</strong> (severe).</li><li><strong>Bone &amp; joint infections</strong>: 400 mg <strong>12-hourly</strong> (mild) or <strong>8-hourly</strong> (severe, &gt;4-6 weeks).</li><li><strong>Intra-abdominal infections</strong>: 400 mg <strong>12-hourly</strong> for <strong>7-14 days</strong>.</li><li><strong>Acute sinusitis</strong>: 400 mg <strong>12-hourly</strong> for <strong>10 days</strong>.</li><li><strong>Chronic bacterial prostatitis</strong>: 400 mg <strong>12-hourly</strong> for <strong>28 days</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Administration of Neofloxin 750 mg</h3><p>For <strong>IV infusion</strong>, ensure there are no leaks, cloudiness, or precipitates before use. Do not use in <strong>series connections</strong>. Follow standard <strong>IV administration</strong> procedures.</p><h3>Interaction of Neofloxin 750 mg</h3><ul><li>Avoid concurrent use with <strong>magnesium/aluminum-containing antacids, sucralfate, or products containing calcium, iron, or zinc</strong>, as they <strong>reduce absorption</strong>. Take them <strong>two hours after or six hours before Neofloxin 750 mg</strong>.</li><li><strong>Do not take with milk or dairy products alone</strong>, as they reduce absorption. However, dietary calcium in meals does not significantly affect absorption.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to <strong>Neofloxin 750 mg</strong> or other quinolones.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Neofloxin 750 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Nausea, diarrhea, vomiting, dyspepsia, abdominal pain.</li><li><strong>CNS</strong>: Headache, dizziness, fatigue, confusion, convulsions, risk of <strong>retinal detachment</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Rash (rarely <strong>Stevens-Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis</strong>), pruritus, systemic reactions.</li><li><strong>Other</strong>: Joint pain, <strong>photosensitivity</strong>, transient <strong>liver enzyme elevation</strong>, changes in <strong>bilirubin, urea, or creatinine levels</strong>.</li><li><strong>Metabolic effects</strong>: Risk of <strong>hypoglycemia, mental health effects</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Use <strong>only if benefits outweigh risks</strong>.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Excreted in human milk; <strong>not recommended due to risk of articular damage</strong> in infants.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>CNS disorders (e.g., epilepsy, arteriosclerosis)</strong>: Use with caution due to seizure risk.</li><li>Drink <strong>plenty of fluids</strong> during treatment.</li><li><strong>Avoid antacids, sucralfate, calcium, iron, and zinc</strong> supplements close to dosing times.</li><li><strong>Avoid milk/yogurt alone</strong>, as they reduce absorption.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Neofloxin 750 mg</h3><p>Overdose may cause:</p><ul><li><strong>Seizures, hallucinations, confusion</strong></li><li><strong>Abdominal discomfort, renal &amp; hepatic impairment</strong></li><li><strong>Crystalluria, hematuria, reversible renal toxicity</strong></li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li><strong>Store below 30°C</strong>, protected from <strong>light and moisture</strong>.</li><li><strong>Keep out of reach of children</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Not a first-choice drug for children</strong> due to the risk of <strong>joint damage</strong>.</li><li><strong>Pediatric use only if benefits outweigh risks</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Drug Class</h3><p><strong>4-Quinolone preparations, Anti-diarrhoeal Antimicrobial drugs</strong></p><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>Synthetic <strong>quinolone anti-infective agent</strong>.</li><li><strong>Broad-spectrum activity</strong> against gram-negative and gram-positive bacteria.</li><li>Inhibits <strong>DNA gyrase</strong>, preventing bacterial <strong>DNA replication</strong>.</li><li>Rapid <strong>oral absorption</strong>, widely distributed in tissues.</li><li><strong>Half-life</strong>: ~3.5 hours.</li><li><strong>30-50% excreted unchanged in urine</strong> within <strong>24 hours</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy Considerations</h3><ul><li>Animal studies show <strong>no teratogenic effects</strong>, but <strong>arthropathy in immature animals</strong>.</li><li><strong>Not recommended</strong> during pregnancy or lactation.</li></ul><h3>Pediatric Uses</h3><ul><li>Effective in trials but <strong>not a first-choice drug for children</strong>.</li><li>Use <strong>only if benefits outweigh risks</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1681_1742228964.jpg', 3, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:29:24', '2025-03-17 21:29:24'),
(3135, 'Vortiox', 'Vortioxetine Hydrobromide', 25.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Description</p>', 'active', '1482_1742229113.png', 45, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:31:53', '2025-03-17 21:31:53'),
(3136, 'Sinacort', 'Betamethasone Valerate', 20.00, 1, 'x 10 gm tube', '10 gm tube', 'Cream', '0.1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sinacort 0.1%</strong></p><p>Sinacort 0.1% cream or ointment is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Eczema in children and adults, including atopic, infantile, and discoid eczema</li><li>Prurigo nodularis</li><li>Psoriasis (excluding widespread plaque psoriasis)</li><li>Neurodermatoses, including lichen simplex and lichen planus</li><li>Seborrheic dermatitis</li><li>Contact sensitivity reaction</li><li>Discoid lupus erythematosus</li><li>May be used as an adjunct to systemic steroid therapy in generalized erythroderma</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Corticosteroid</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Betamethasone valerate is a potent topical corticosteroid. Topical corticosteroids have anti-inflammatory, antipruritic, and vasoconstrictive actions. Betamethasone acts by inhibiting phospholipase A2 activity and reduces the formation of prostaglandins and leukotrienes in local tissues.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sinacort 0.1%</strong></p><p>Apply sparingly to the affected area two or three times daily until improvement occurs, then twice daily or less. The usual maximum duration of therapy is three weeks.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Sinacort 0.1%</strong></p><p>Apply sparingly to the affected area two or three times daily until improvement occurs, then twice daily or less. The usual maximum duration of therapy is three weeks.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Sinacort 0.1%</strong></p><p>There are no significant drug interactions reported with Betamethasone Cream/Ointment.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Sinacort 0.1% cream or ointment is contraindicated in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients in the preparation</li><li>Rosacea &amp; acne vulgaris</li><li>Perioral dermatitis, perianal &amp; genital pruritus</li><li>Viral infections of the skin, e.g., herpes simplex &amp; chickenpox</li><li>Primary infected skin lesions caused by fungi or bacteria</li><li>Dermatoses in children under one year of age</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Sinacort 0.1%</strong></p><p>The following local adverse reactions are more common with the use of high doses, long-term use, and the use of occlusive dressings:</p><ul><li>Dryness, itching, burning</li><li>Skin thinning, local irritation, features of hypercortisolism</li><li>Telangiectasia, striae, skin atrophy</li><li>Hypertrichosis, changes in pigmentation</li><li>Secondary infection, perioral dermatitis, allergic contact dermatitis</li><li>Maceration of the skin, acneiform eruption, exacerbation of symptoms</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It should not be used extensively in pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Betamethasone Cream/Ointment is usually well tolerated, but if signs of hypersensitivity appear, application should be stopped. Long-term continuous topical therapy should be avoided where possible, particularly in children, as adrenal suppression may occur even without occlusion.</p><ul><li>When extensive areas are treated, sufficient systemic absorption may occur to produce symptoms of hypercortisolism.</li><li>This effect is more likely if occlusive dressings are used or if treatment is prolonged.</li><li>Prolonged treatment on the face or other areas of the body may cause atrophic changes.</li><li>If applied to the eyelids, care should be taken to prevent it from entering the eyes, as glaucoma might result.</li><li>Any spread of infection requires the withdrawal of topical corticosteroid therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Corticosteroid</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Sinacort 0.1% BP is a synthetic adrenocorticosteroid, glucocorticoid in nature. It is an analog of prednisolone with slight mineralocorticoid activity. Due to its anti-inflammatory, antipruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties, it is highly effective for dermatological use.</p><ul><li>It is absorbed from the skin, and inflammation or other disease processes can increase percutaneous absorption.</li><li>Occlusive dressings significantly increase percutaneous absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>It should not be used extensively in pregnancy.</p>', 'active', '4329_1746510120.jpg', 2, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:35:46', '2025-05-06 09:42:00'),
(3137, 'Orocal Kit', 'Ibandronic Acid & Calcium Orotate', 800.00, 1, 'Kit tablet', '1 Kit tablet', 'Tablet', '150 mg and 400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Orocal Kit 150 mg &amp; 400 mg</strong></p><p> Ibandronic acid is indicated for the treatment of postmenopausal osteoporosis, to reduce the risk of fractures. Treatment of Osteoporosis: Osteoporosis may be confirmed by the finding of low bone mass (T-score &lt;-2.0 SD) and the presence or history of osteoporotic fracture, or a low bone mass (T-score &lt;-2.5 SD) in the absence of documented pre-existing osteoporotic fracture.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Bisphosphonate preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Osteoporosis causes the body to remove more bone than it replaces, leading to weaker bones, which are more likely to break. Osteoporosis is a common bone disease in women after menopause but also affects men. Initially, osteoporosis has no symptoms, but it can lead to loss of height and increased risk of fractures, especially in the spine, wrist, and hip. Osteoporosis can be prevented, and with proper therapy, it can be treated. Ibandronic acid inhibits osteoclast activity, reducing bone resorption and turnover. In postmenopausal women, it reduces the elevated rate of bone turnover, leading to a progressive gain in bone mass. After absorption, ibandronic acid rapidly binds to bone or is excreted unchanged into the urine.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Orocal Kit 150 mg &amp; 400 mg</strong></p><p> The recommended dose for treatment is one 150 mg film-coated tablet once a month. The tablet should preferably be taken on the same date each month. Ibandronic acid should be taken 60 minutes before the first food or drink (other than water) of the day or any oral medication or supplementation (including calcium). The tablet should be swallowed whole with a full glass of plain water (180-240 ml) while the patient is sitting or standing in an upright position. Patients should not lie down for 60 minutes after taking the tablet. If a once-monthly dose is missed, patients should take one 150 mg tablet the morning after remembering, unless the next scheduled dose is within 7 days. If the next dose is within 7 days, patients should wait until the next dose and continue as scheduled.</p><p><strong>Administration of Orocal Kit 150 mg &amp; 400 mg</strong></p><p> To maximize absorption, ibandronic acid should be taken at least 60 minutes before food, drink, or other medications. The tablet should be swallowed whole with a full glass of plain water. Patients should not lie down for 60 minutes after taking ibandronic acid, but they can engage in regular activities.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Orocal Kit 150 mg &amp; 400 mg</strong></p><p> Calcium supplements, antacids, and oral medications containing multivalent cations (e.g., aluminum, magnesium, iron) may interfere with the absorption of ibandronic acid. Patients should wait 60 minutes after taking ibandronic acid before taking other oral medications. There are no significant drug interactions with tamoxifen or hormone replacement therapy. No interaction is observed with melphalan/prednisolone or H2-antagonists.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Ibandronic acid is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to ibandronic acid or excipients, uncorrected hypocalcemia, esophageal abnormalities (e.g., stricture or achalasia), and in patients unable to sit or stand upright for at least 60 minutes.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Orocal Kit 150 mg &amp; 400 mg</strong></p><p> The main side effects include dyspepsia, nausea, diarrhea, abdominal pain, muscle aches, headaches, and dizziness.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy: Category C. Ibandronic acid should only be used during pregnancy if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk.</p><p> Lactation: Ibandronic acid is not known to be excreted in human milk, but caution should be exercised during breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Patients should ensure adequate calcium and vitamin D intake. Ibandronic acid may cause gastrointestinal irritation. Caution is needed in patients with active upper gastrointestinal issues. Patients should discontinue the medication and seek medical attention if they experience dysphagia, odynophagia, or retrosternal pain. Osteonecrosis of the jaw (ONJ) has been reported, especially in cancer patients, but some cases have also been observed in postmenopausal osteoporosis patients.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Orocal Kit 150 mg &amp; 400 mg</strong></p><p> Overdose may result in gastrointestinal issues such as upset stomach, heartburn, or esophagitis. Milk or antacids should be given to bind ibandronic acid. Inducing vomiting should be avoided, and patients should remain upright.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture, and out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> Pediatric use: Safety and efficacy have not been established in children.</p><p> Geriatric Patients: No dose adjustment is necessary for elderly patients.</p><p> Patients with Hepatic Impairment: No dose adjustment is necessary.</p><p> Patients with Renal Impairment: No dose adjustment is necessary for patients with mild or moderate renal impairment (creatinine clearance ≥30 ml/min).</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Bisphosphonate preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Ibandronic acid inhibits bone resorption by targeting osteoclasts. It reduces bone turnover, leading to an increase in bone mass. In postmenopausal women, it helps reduce bone resorption and fracture risk by increasing bone mass.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Ibandronic acid should not be used during pregnancy. It may affect implantation and delivery in animal studies, but there is no clinical experience with ibandronic acid in pregnant women.</p><p> <strong>Nursing Mothers:</strong> Ibandronic acid should not be used during lactation.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Safety and efficacy in patients under 18 years have not been established. For patients with renal impairment, dosing should be carefully considered, especially if creatinine clearance is &lt;30 ml/min.</p>', 'active', '1463_1742229418.jpeg', 27, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:36:58', '2025-03-17 21:36:58'),
(3138, 'Pednisol', 'Prednisolone Acetate', 90.30, 1, 'x 5 ml drop', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Suspension', '1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pednisol 1%</strong></p><p>Prednisolone eye drops are indicated for the treatment of steroid-responsive inflammation of the palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva, cornea, and anterior segment of the globe.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Glucocorticoids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Prednisolone decreases inflammation by inhibiting the migration of polymorphonuclear leukocytes and reversing increased capillary permeability. It suppresses the immune system by reducing the activity and production of lymphocytes and eosinophils.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Pednisol 1%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> Instill one drop into the conjunctival sac two to four times daily. During the initial 24 to 48 hours, the dosing frequency may be increased if necessary. Care should be taken not to discontinue the therapy before completing the course.</li><li><strong>Pediatric:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Pednisol 1%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> Instill one drop into the conjunctival sac two to four times daily. During the initial 24 to 48 hours, the dosing frequency may be increased if necessary. Care should be taken not to discontinue the therapy before completing the course.</li><li><strong>Pediatric:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Pednisol 1%</strong></p><p>No significant drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Pednisol 1% is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Viral diseases of the cornea and conjunctiva</li><li>Known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients in this preparation or other corticosteroids</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Pednisol 1%</strong></p><ul><li>Elevation of intraocular pressure (IOP) with possible development of glaucoma and infrequent optic nerve damage</li><li>Posterior subcapsular cataract formation</li><li>Delayed wound healing</li><li>Fungal and viral infections of the cornea, which may develop coincidentally with long-term steroid applications</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pednisol 1% is classified as pregnancy category C. This drug should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed. It is not known whether topical administration of corticosteroids results in systemic absorption sufficient to produce detectable quantities in human milk. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk, caution should be exercised when administering Pednisol 1% to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Shake the bottle well before use.</li><li>Prolonged use of corticosteroids may result in damage to the optic nerve and defects in vision.</li><li>If used for an extended period, intraocular pressure should be routinely monitored.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Pednisol 1%</strong></p><p>A one-time accidental overdose of Prednisolone Ophthalmic Suspension generally will not cause acute problems. Long-term overdose may cause general side effects. In case of accidental overdose, sufficient water should be taken to dilute the medication.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place and protect from light.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li><li>Protect from freezing.</li><li>Shake well before using.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Glucocorticoids</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Prednisolone decreases inflammation by inhibiting the migration of polymorphonuclear leukocytes and reversing increased capillary permeability. It suppresses the immune system by reducing the activity and production of lymphocytes and eosinophils.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Pednisol 1% is classified as pregnancy category C. This drug should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed. It is not known whether topical administration of corticosteroids results in systemic absorption sufficient to produce detectable quantities in human milk. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk, caution should be exercised when administering Pednisol 1% to a nursing woman.</p>', 'active', '885_1742229519.jpg', 9, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:38:39', '2025-05-05 14:46:16'),
(3139, 'Tegretol', 'Carbamazepine', 379.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '100 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Tegretol 100 ml</strong></p><p>Tegretol 100 ml is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Partial and secondary generalized tonic-clonic seizures</li><li>Primary generalized tonic-clonic seizures</li><li>Trigeminal neuralgia</li><li>Prophylaxis of bipolar disorder</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Primary anti-epileptic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Tegretol 100 ml depresses activity in the nucleus ventralis of the thalamus, reduces synaptic propagation of excitatory impulses, or decreases summation of temporal stimulation leading to neural discharge by limiting the influx of Na ions across the cell membrane or other unknown mechanisms. It stimulates the release of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) and potentiates its action in promoting the reabsorption of water.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Tegretol 100 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Epilepsy:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initial: 200 mg <strong>b.i.d.</strong> (tablets/controlled-release tablets) or 1 teaspoon <strong>q.i.d.</strong> (suspension) (400 mg/day).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increase weekly by up to 200 mg/day using <strong>b.i.d., t.i.d.,</strong> or <strong>q.i.d.</strong> regimen until the optimal response is obtained.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 12-15 years:</strong> Maximum dose should not exceed 1000 mg daily (1200 mg in patients over 15 years).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong> Doses up to 1600 mg daily have been used in rare instances.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance dose:</strong> Usually 800-1200 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6-12 years:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initial: 100 mg <strong>b.i.d.</strong> (tablets/controlled-release tablets) or ½ teaspoon <strong>q.i.d.</strong> (suspension) (200 mg/day).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increase weekly by up to 100 mg/day until the optimal response is obtained.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum dose: 1000 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance dose:</strong> 400-800 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Children under 6 years:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initial: 10-20 mg/kg/day <strong>b.i.d.</strong> or <strong>t.i.d.</strong> (tablets) or <strong>q.i.d.</strong> (suspension).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increase weekly to achieve the optimal clinical response.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance dose:</strong> Below 35 mg/kg daily. If the clinical response is inadequate, plasma levels should be measured to determine if they are within the therapeutic range.</li></ul><p><strong>Combination Therapy:</strong></p><ul><li>Tegretol 100 ml can be used alone or with other anticonvulsants.</li><li>When added to an existing anticonvulsant regimen, it should be introduced gradually while maintaining or decreasing other anticonvulsants, except <strong>phenytoin</strong>, which may require an increase.</li></ul><p><strong>Trigeminal Neuralgia:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial dose:</strong> 100 mg <strong>b.i.d.</strong> (tablets/controlled-release tablets) or ½ teaspoon <strong>q.i.d.</strong> (suspension) (200 mg/day).</li><li>Increase by up to 200 mg/day in 100 mg increments every 12 hours (tablets) or 50 mg (½ teaspoon) <strong>q.i.d.</strong> (suspension) as needed.</li><li><strong>Maximum dose:</strong> 1200 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Maintenance dose:</strong> Typically 400-800 mg daily, though some patients may require 200 mg or up to 1200 mg daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Tegretol 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Galactorrhoea</strong> has been reported in some women taking oral contraceptives during the first two months of Tegretol 100 ml treatment.</li><li>Hepatic enzyme inducers such as <strong>Tegretol 100 ml and phenytoin</strong> may interact, increasing its metabolism, which may require an increased dose of Tegretol 100 ml.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Tegretol 100 ml should not be used in individuals with:</p><ul><li>Allergy to any of its ingredients</li><li>Atrioventricular block (heart conduction issues)</li><li>History of bone marrow suppression</li><li>Hereditary blood disorders (porphyrias)</li><li>Allergy to tricyclic antidepressants</li><li>Use of monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) within the last 14 days</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Tegretol 100 ml</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Neurological:</strong> Dizziness, drowsiness, ataxia, unsteadiness</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Dry mouth, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, anorexia</li><li><strong>Hematological:</strong> Leucopenia, aplastic anemia</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular:</strong> Bradycardia, hypotension, heart failure</li><li><strong>Dermatological:</strong> Erythematous skin rash, increased sensitivity to sunlight</li></ul><p>Severe adverse effects have been observed in the <strong>hemopoietic system, skin, and cardiovascular system</strong>.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category D:</strong> Tegretol 100 ml and its metabolite can transfer to breast milk. Due to potential serious effects, a decision should be made to either discontinue the drug or stop breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>May cause <strong>dizziness and drowsiness</strong>; avoid hazardous activities such as driving.</li><li>May cause <strong>serious skin reactions</strong> (rash, skin peeling, itching). Seek medical advice if these occur.</li><li><strong>Liver toxicity:</strong> If symptoms such as <strong>jaundice, dark urine, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, or loss of appetite</strong> occur, consult a doctor immediately.</li><li><strong>Effect on contraception:</strong> Reduces the efficacy of hormonal contraceptives; women should use a contraceptive with <strong>≥50 mcg estrogen</strong> or <strong>non-hormonal methods (e.g., condoms)</strong>.</li><li><strong>Gradual withdrawal:</strong> Sudden discontinuation may cause symptoms to return; tapering under medical supervision is advised.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Use caution in:</strong>Mixed seizures, including <strong>absence seizures</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Elderly patients</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">History of <strong>heart, kidney, or liver disease</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">History of <strong>psychotic illness</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Glaucoma</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">History of <strong>blood disorders from previous medication use</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Primary anti-epileptic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Tegretol 100 ml works by:</p><ul><li><strong>Depressing</strong> activity in the <strong>nucleus ventralis of the thalamus</strong></li><li><strong>Reducing synaptic propagation</strong> of excitatory impulses</li><li><strong>Limiting Na+ ion influx</strong> across cell membranes</li><li><strong>Stimulating ADH release</strong>, enhancing water reabsorption</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category D:</strong> Tegretol 100 ml and its epoxide metabolite are transferred to <strong>breast milk</strong>. Due to potential serious side effects, a decision should be made whether to <strong>discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug</strong>.</li></ul>', 'active', '1172_1742229670.jpg', 40, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:41:10', '2025-05-06 14:34:34'),
(3140, 'Serotal', 'Escitalopram Oxalate', 5.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Serotal 5 mg</strong></p><p> Serotal 5 mg is indicated for the treatment of depressive illness, generalized anxiety disorder, obsessive-compulsive disorder, and social anxiety disorder.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> SSRIs &amp; related antidepressant drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Serotal 5 mg is a selective serotonin (5-HT) reuptake inhibitor. The inhibition of 5-HT reuptake is the likely mechanism behind its pharmacological and clinical effects. Serotal 5 mg has minimal to no affinity for several other receptors, including 5-HT1A, 5-HT2, DA D1 and D2 receptors, α1-, α2-, β-adrenoceptors, histamine H1, muscarinic cholinergic, benzodiazepine, and opioid receptors.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Serotal 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: The initial dose of Serotal 5 mg is 10 mg once daily. The dose may be increased to 20 mg after a minimum of one week if necessary.</li><li><strong>Panic disorder</strong>: For adults over 18 years, the initial dose is 5 mg once daily, increased to 10 mg daily after 7 days, with a maximum dose of 20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Social anxiety disorder</strong>: For adults over 18 years, the initial dose is 10 mg once daily, adjusted after 2-4 weeks. The usual dose is 5-20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: A single oral dose of 10 mg/day is recommended for most elderly patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Serotal 5 mg</strong></p><p> Serotal 5 mg should not be started until at least 2 weeks after stopping an MAOI, and conversely, an MAOI should not be started until at least a week after stopping Serotal 5 mg or a related antidepressant.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant use of Serotal 5 mg with monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) is contraindicated.</li><li>Concomitant use with pimozide is contraindicated.</li><li>Serotal 5 mg is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to citalopram, Serotal 5 mg, or any of the inactive ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Serotal 5 mg</strong></p><p> Serotal 5 mg is generally well-tolerated, but some common side effects include nausea, insomnia, problems with ejaculation, drowsiness, increased sweating, and fatigue. Most side effects are mild and tend to resolve with continued treatment.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: The safety of Serotal 5 mg during pregnancy has not been established. It should not be used during pregnancy unless the expected benefits to the mother outweigh the potential risks to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Nursing Mothers</strong>: Serotal 5 mg is excreted in human milk and should not be given to nursing mothers unless the expected benefits to the mother outweigh the potential risks to the child.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Discontinuation of Serotal 5 mg and other SSRIs or SNRIs may lead to adverse events, especially if stopped abruptly. Symptoms may include dysphoric mood, irritability, agitation, dizziness, sensory disturbances, anxiety, confusion, headache, lethargy, emotional lability, insomnia, and hypomania. These events are generally self-limiting but may require a gradual dose reduction to minimize the risk. If intolerable symptoms occur, the dose may be returned to the previous level, and the dose can be gradually reduced again.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place below 30°C. Protect from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in children under 18 years have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Patients</strong>: No significant differences in safety or effectiveness were observed between elderly patients (65+) and younger subjects. However, some elderly individuals may experience greater sensitivity to the drug.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '692_1742229722.jpeg', 64, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:42:02', '2025-03-17 21:42:02'),
(3141, 'Tirzep', 'Tirzepatide', 3500.00, 1, 'x 7.5 mg pre-filled syringe', '7.5 mg pre-filled syringe', 'Injection', '7.5 mg/0.5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Description</p>', 'active', '116_1742230058.jpg', 44, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:47:38', '2025-05-13 05:23:02'),
(3142, 'Tirzep', 'Tirzepatide', 3500.00, 1, 'Injection', '7.5 mg pre-filled syringe', 'Injection', '7.5 mg per 0.5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Description</p>', 'in-active', '1070_1742230060.jpg', 44, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:47:40', '2025-05-13 05:23:16'),
(3143, 'Osimert', 'Osimertinib', 425.00, 30, 'Tablet', '30 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '80 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the revised text with headings changed to normal bold text:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Osimert 80 mg</strong></p><p> Osimert 80 mg is indicated for the treatment of patients with metastatic epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) T790M mutation-positive non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), as detected by an FDA-approved test, whose disease has progressed on or after EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitor (TKI) therapy.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Osimert 80 mg is an epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) tyrosine kinase inhibitor (TKI) that binds to certain mutant forms of EGFR (T790M, L858R, and exon 19 deletion) that predominate in non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) tumours following treatment with first-line EGFR-TKIs. As a third-generation tyrosine kinase inhibitor, Osimert 80 mg is specific for the gate-keeper T790M mutation, which increases ATP binding activity to EGFR and results in poor prognosis for late-stage disease. Furthermore, Osimert 80 mg has been shown to spare wild-type EGFR during therapy, thereby reducing non-specific binding and limiting toxicity.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Osimert 80 mg</strong></p><p> The recommended dose of Osimert 80 mg is 80 mg once a day until disease progression or unacceptable toxicity. Osimert 80 mg can be taken with or without food. If a dose of Osimert 80 mg is missed, do not make up the missed dose and take the next dose as scheduled.</p><p><strong>Administration of Osimert 80 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Administration To Patients Who Have Difficulty Swallowing Solids:</strong> Disperse tablet in 60 ml (2 ounces) of non-carbonated water only. Stir until the tablet is dispersed into small pieces (the tablet will not completely dissolve) and swallow immediately. Do not crush, heat, or ultrasonicate during preparation. Rinse the container with 120 ml to 240 ml (4 to 8 ounces) of water and immediately drink.</p><p>If administration via nasogastric tube is required, disperse the tablet as above in 15 ml of non-carbonated water, and then use an additional 15 ml of water to transfer any residues to the syringe. The resulting 30 ml liquid should be administered as per the nasogastric tube instructions with appropriate water flushes (approximately 30 ml).</p><p><strong>Interaction of Osimert 80 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Strong CYP3A4 Inducers:</strong> If concurrent use is unavoidable, increase Osimert 80 mg dosage to 160 mg daily when coadministering with a strong CYP3A inducer. Resume Osimert 80 mg at 80 mg 3 weeks after discontinuation of the strong CYP3A4 inducer.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Osimert 80 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects are Interstitial Lung Disease or Pneumonitis, QTc Interval Prolongation, Cardiomyopathy, and Keratitis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> <strong>Use in Pregnancy:</strong> There are no or limited amount of data from the use of Osimert 80 mg in pregnant women. Studies in animals have shown reproductive toxicity. Based on its mechanism of action and preclinical data, Osimert 80 mg may cause fetal harm when administered to a pregnant woman. Administration of Osimert 80 mg to pregnant rats was associated with embryolethality, reduced fetal growth, and neonatal death at exposures similar to what is expected in humans. Osimert 80 mg is not recommended during pregnancy and in women of childbearing potential not using contraception.</p><p><strong>Use in Lactation:</strong> It is not known whether Osimert 80 mg or its metabolites are excreted in human milk. Administration to rats during gestation and early lactation was associated with adverse effects, including reduced growth rates and neonatal death. There is insufficient information on the excretion of Osimert 80 mg or its metabolites in animal milk. A risk to the suckling child cannot be excluded. Breast-feeding should be discontinued during treatment with Osimert 80 mg.</p><p><strong>Fertility:</strong> There are no data on the effect of Osimert 80 mg on human fertility. Results from animal studies have shown that Osimert 80 mg has effects on male and female reproductive organs and could impair fertility.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store Osimert 80 mg at room temperature between 20°C to 25°C. Safely throw away medicine that is out of date or that you no longer need. Keep Osimert 80 mg and all medicines out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Osimert 80 mg is an epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) tyrosine kinase inhibitor (TKI) that binds to certain mutant forms of EGFR (T790M, L858R, and exon 19 deletion) that predominate in non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) tumours following treatment with first-line EGFR-TKIs. As a third-generation tyrosine kinase inhibitor, Osimert 80 mg is specific for the gate-keeper T790M mutation, which increases ATP binding activity to EGFR and results in poor prognosis for late-stage disease. Furthermore, Osimert 80 mg has been shown to spare wild-type EGFR during therapy, thereby reducing non-specific binding and limiting toxicity.</p><p>Let me know if you need any further formatting or modifications!</p>', 'active', '675_1742230108.jpeg', 67, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:48:28', '2025-03-17 21:48:28'),
(3144, 'Nalphin', 'Nalbuphine', 54.36, 1, 'x 1 ml ampoule', '1 ml ampoule', 'IV Injection', '10 mg/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nalphin 10 mg/ml</strong></p><p>Nalphin 10 mg/ml Hydrochloride is indicated for the relief of moderate to severe pain. It is used for relief of moderate to severe pain associated with myocardial infarction (MI). Nalphin 10 mg/ml Hydrochloride can also be used as a supplement to balanced anesthesia, for preoperative and postoperative analgesia, and for obstetrical analgesia during labor and delivery.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Nalphin 10 mg/ml Hydrochloride is a synthetic potent analgesic. Its analgesic potency is essentially equivalent to that of morphine on a milligram basis. Receptor studies show that Nalphin 10 mg/ml binds to mu, kappa, and delta receptors, but not to sigma receptors. Nalphin 10 mg/ml is primarily a kappa agonist/partial mu antagonist analgesic.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Nalphin 10 mg/ml</strong></p><p>The usual recommended adult dose is 10 mg for a 70 kg individual, administered subcutaneously, intramuscularly, or intravenously; this dose may be repeated every 3 to 6 hours as necessary. Dosage should be adjusted according to the severity of the pain, physical status of the patient, and other medications which the patient may be receiving.</p><ul><li><strong>Moderate to severe pain:</strong> By intravenous or intramuscular injection 10-20 mg for a 70 kg patient, adjusted as required; child up to 0.3 mg/kg repeated once or twice as necessary.</li><li><strong>Preoperative anesthesia:</strong> By intravenous or intramuscular injection 0.1-0.2 mg/kg.</li><li><strong>Obstetrical analgesia during labor &amp; delivery:</strong> By intravenous injection 0.3-1 mg/kg over 10-15 minutes with maintenance doses of 0.25-0.5 mg/kg in single intravenous administration as required.</li><li><strong>Intraoperative analgesia:</strong> By intravenous injection 0.25-0.5 mg/kg at 30-minute intervals.</li><li><strong>Myocardial infarction:</strong> By slow intravenous injection 10-20 mg, repeated after 30 minutes if necessary. A larger dose is required when used as a supplement of anesthesia than that required for analgesia.</li><li><strong>Children (18 months to 15 years old):</strong> Usually 0.2 mg/kg body weight, given preferably by intravenous or intramuscular injection. Maintenance doses may be given at intervals of 4 to 6 hours or the dose must be determined by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Nalphin 10 mg/ml</strong></p><p>No hazardous interactions have been identified with Nalphin 10 mg/ml; however, interactions described with other opioids may be anticipated. Patients receiving a narcotic analgesic, general anesthesia, phenothiazines, or other tranquilizers, sedatives, hypnotics, or other CNS depressants (including alcohol) concomitantly with Nalphin 10 mg/ml may exhibit an additive effect.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Known hypersensitivity to Nalphin 10 mg/ml Hydrochloride.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Nalphin 10 mg/ml</strong></p><p>Generally, Nalphin 10 mg/ml is well tolerated. However, a few side effects like sedation, sweating, nausea, vomiting, dizziness, vertigo, dry mouth, headache, respiratory depression, dyspnea, and asthma may be seen.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy category B. The placental transfer of Nalphin 10 mg/ml is high and rapid. There are no well-controlled studies in pregnant women. This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Limited data suggest that Nalphin 10 mg/ml is excreted in maternal milk but only in a small amount (less than 1% of the administered dose) and with a clinically insignificant effect. Caution should be exercised when Nalphin 10 mg/ml is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be taken in the following conditions: impaired respiration, impaired renal or hepatic function, biliary tract surgery, myocardial infarction, and hypotension.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Nalphin 10 mg/ml</strong></p><p>Sleepiness and mild dyspnea may occur due to overdose. The immediate intravenous administration of an opiate antagonist such as Naloxone or Nalmefene is a specific antidote. Oxygen, intravenous fluids, vasopressors, and other supportive measures should be used as indicated.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep it in a cool and dry place, protected from light and moisture.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Nalphin 10 mg/ml Hydrochloride is a synthetic potent analgesic. Its analgesic potency is essentially equivalent to that of morphine on a milligram basis. Receptor studies show that Nalphin 10 mg/ml binds to mu, kappa, and delta receptors, but not to sigma receptors. Nalphin 10 mg/ml is primarily a kappa agonist/partial mu antagonist analgesic.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Pregnancy category B. The placental transfer of Nalphin 10 mg/ml is high and rapid. There are no well-controlled studies in pregnant women. This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Limited data suggest that Nalphin 10 mg/ml is excreted in maternal milk but only in a small amount (less than 1% of the administered dose) and with a clinically insignificant effect. Caution should be exercised when Nalphin 10 mg/ml is administered to a nursing woman.</p>', 'active', '950_1742230319.jpg', 5, 12, 'mdnayemmia443@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:51:59', '2025-05-04 21:14:57');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3145, 'Ovacon Gold', 'Ethinyl Estradiol plus Lynestrenol', 65.00, 1, 'x 22 Tablet Pack', '22 Tablet pack', 'Tablet', '0.0375 mg+0.75 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the revised text with headings changed to normal bold text:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Ovacon Gold 37.5 mcg + 0.75 mg</strong></p><p>Ethinylestradiol &amp; Lynestrenol is indicated in oral contraception and the prevention of pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Oral contraceptive preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Estradiol is the major oestrogen in pre-menopausal women. Ethinylestradiol has similar actions as oestradiol. It is responsible for the development and maintenance of the female reproductive system and secondary sexual characteristics. It also inhibits the anterior pituitary by negative feedback effect and causes capillary dilation, fluid retention, and protein anabolism. Lynestrenol is a progestogen structurally related to norethisterone. It may be used alone or as the progestogenic component of some oral contraceptives.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ovacon Gold 37.5 mcg + 0.75 mg</strong></p><p>The pill is to be taken daily. Women should be instructed to take the pills at about the same time every day, preferably after the evening meal or at bedtime. Each subsequent pack is started on the day after the current pack is completed. Continue taking one pill each day.</p><p>It is most likely that menstruation will start while taking the brown pills. Do not discontinue taking the brown pills. In addition to providing iron supplementation, regularly taking the brown tablets for 7 days will help maintain the pill-taking routine.</p><p>When a woman wishes for her period to begin, she should stop taking the tablet. While using the second pack, some breakthrough bleeding or spotting may occur. Start the next pack after the usual 7-day red inactive pill interval. The advice concerning the management of missed pills is applicable. The woman must take the extra active pill(s) needed from a backup pack after vomiting.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Ovacon Gold 37.5 mcg + 0.75 mg</strong></p><p>CYP1A2 and CYP3A4 inducers such as aminoglutethimide, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, and rifampin may decrease the effects of estradiol. It may enhance the effects of hydrocortisone and prednisolone when used together. Altered anticoagulant effects may occur when used with dicoumarol.</p><p><strong>Potentially Fatal Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Antibiotics (ampicillin, tetracycline, sulphonamides, and chloramphenicol) can cause intermenstrual bleeding or failure of contraception.</li><li>Reduced efficacy of antihypertensives or hypoglycaemic drugs.</li><li>Increased metabolism and subsequent reduction in efficacy with enzyme-inducing agents (e.g., carbamazepine, griseofulvin, phenobarbital, phenytoin, and rifampicin).</li><li>Adjustment in antidiabetic dose may be required.</li><li>May increase plasma levels and toxicity of ciclosporin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Pregnancy, cardiovascular diseases, thromboembolic disorders, severe hypertension, severe liver disease, known or suspected estrogen-dependent tumors, endometrial hyperplasia.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ovacon Gold 37.5 mcg + 0.75 mg</strong></p><p>Nausea, vomiting, cholestatic jaundice, thrombosis, blood pressure rise, rash, headache, migraine, mood change, breast tenderness, pain, enlargement, and secretion.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p><strong>Pregnancy Category X:</strong> Studies in animals or humans have demonstrated fetal abnormalities and/or there is positive evidence of human fetal risk based on adverse reaction data from investigational or marketing experience, and the risks involved in the use of the drug in pregnant women clearly outweigh potential benefits.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Some medications may interfere with the efficacy of the pill. Contact your doctor if you are taking any drugs such as antibiotics, rifampicin, or medicines for seizures. You may have to use another method of contraception during this time.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p>Let me know if you need further modifications!</p>', 'active', '1342_1742230571.jpeg', 12, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 21:56:11', '2025-04-19 07:23:56'),
(3146, 'Sanfix', 'Cefixime Trihydrate', 50.00, 7, 'Capsule', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the revised text with headings changed to normal bold text:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Sanfix 400 mg</strong></p><p> Cefixime is an orally active cephalosporin antibiotic with marked in-vitro bactericidal activity against a wide variety of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms. Sanfix 400 mg is indicated for the treatment of the following acute infections when caused by susceptible microorganisms:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper Respiratory Tract Infections (URTI):</strong> e.g., otitis media and other URTIs where the causative organism is known or suspected to be resistant to other commonly used antibiotics, or where treatment failure may carry significant risk.</li><li><strong>Lower Respiratory Tract Infections:</strong> e.g., bronchitis.</li><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections:</strong> e.g., cystitis, cystourethritis, pyelonephritis.</li></ul><p>Clinical efficacy has been demonstrated in infections caused by commonly occurring pathogens, including <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Streptococcus pyogenes, Escherichia coli, Proteus mirabilis, Klebsiella species, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase positive and negative), <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase positive and negative), and <em>Enterobacter</em> species. Cefixime is highly stable in the presence of beta-lactamase enzymes.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Third-generation cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cefixime is a third-generation semisynthetic cephalosporin antibiotic for oral administration. It is bactericidal against a broad spectrum of gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria at easily achievable plasma concentrations. It kills bacteria by interfering with bacterial cell wall synthesis. It is highly stable in the presence of beta-lactamase enzymes, making it effective against many organisms resistant to penicillins and some cephalosporins. The absorption of cefixime is about 40% to 50% whether administered with or without food.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sanfix 400 mg</strong></p><p> The usual course of treatment is 7 days, which may be extended up to 14 days depending on the severity of the infection.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years:</strong> The recommended dose is 200-400 mg (1 to 2 capsules) daily, given either as a single dose or in two divided doses.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated cervical/urethral gonococcal infections:</strong> A single oral dose of Cefixime 400 mg is recommended.</li><li><strong>Children (6 months or older):</strong> Usually 8 mg/kg/day given as a single dose or in two divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">½-1 year: 75 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-4 years: 100 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-10 years: 200 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">11-12 years: 300 mg daily</li><li><strong>Typhoid fever:</strong> 10 mg/kg/day for 14 days.</li><li><strong>Children under 6 months:</strong> The safety and efficacy of Cefixime have not been established in this age group.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage in Renal Impairment:</strong></p><p> Cefixime may be administered in the presence of impaired renal function.</p><ul><li><strong>Creatinine clearance ≥ 20 ml/min:</strong> Normal dose and schedule.</li><li><strong>Creatinine clearance &lt; 20 ml/min:</strong> A dose of 200 mg once daily should not be exceeded.</li><li><strong>Patients on chronic ambulatory peritoneal dialysis or hemodialysis:</strong> Should follow the same recommendation as patients with creatinine clearance &lt; 20 ml/min.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sanfix 400 mg</strong></p><p> Like other cephalosporins, cefixime may cause an increase in prothrombin time in some patients. Caution is advised for patients on anticoagulant therapy.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Patients with known hypersensitivity to cephalosporin antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sanfix 400 mg</strong></p><p> Cefixime is generally well tolerated, with most adverse reactions being mild and self-limiting.</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal disturbances:</strong> Diarrhea, stool changes, nausea, abdominal pain, dyspepsia, vomiting, flatulence, and pseudomembranous colitis (rare).</li><li><strong>Central nervous system:</strong> Headache, dizziness.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity reactions:</strong> Rash, pruritus, urticaria, drug fever, arthralgia, usually subsiding upon discontinuation.</li><li><strong>Hematological effects:</strong> Thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, eosinophilia (infrequent and reversible).</li><li><strong>Liver and renal function:</strong> Mild transient changes in liver and renal function tests.</li><li><strong>Miscellaneous:</strong> Genital pruritus, vaginitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Since animal studies are not always predictive of human response, cefixime should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. It is unknown whether cefixime is excreted in human milk, so caution is advised for nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Cefixime should be used with caution in patients with known hypersensitivity to other drugs.</li><li>Cephalosporins should be given with caution to penicillin-sensitive patients due to partial cross-allergenicity.</li><li>Severe reactions, including anaphylaxis, may occur in sensitive individuals.</li><li>If an allergic reaction occurs, discontinue the drug and initiate appropriate treatment.</li><li>Cefixime should be used cautiously in patients with markedly impaired renal function.</li><li>Broad-spectrum antibiotics can alter normal colon flora, potentially leading to overgrowth of <em>Clostridium difficile</em> and antibiotic-associated diarrhea.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sanfix 400 mg</strong></p><p> In cases of overdose, gastric lavage may be considered. There is no specific antidote. Cefixime is not significantly removed from circulation by hemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis. Healthy adult volunteers receiving single doses up to 2 g did not experience adverse effects different from those seen at recommended doses.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Third-generation cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Cefixime is a third-generation semisynthetic cephalosporin antibiotic for oral administration. It exerts bactericidal activity by interfering with bacterial cell wall synthesis. It is highly stable in the presence of beta-lactamase enzymes, making it effective against many penicillin- and cephalosporin-resistant organisms. The absorption of cefixime is approximately 40% to 50%, whether taken with or without food.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Cefixime should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. It is unknown whether cefixime is excreted in human milk; therefore, caution is advised for nursing mothers.</p><p>Let me know if you need any further modifications!</p>', 'active', '1815_1742230852.jpeg', 58, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 22:00:52', '2025-03-17 22:00:52'),
(3147, 'Sabitar', 'Sacubitril plus Valsartan', 120.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sabitar 200 mg</strong></p><p> This is indicated: To reduce the risk of cardiovascular death and hospitalization for heart failure in patients with chronic heart failure (NYHA Class ll-IV) and reduced ejection fraction. For the treatment of symptomatic heart failure with systemic left ventricular systolic dysfunction in pediatric patients aged one year and older. This is usually administered in conjunction with other heart failure therapies, in place of an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor (ACEi) or other ARB.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Combined antihypertensive preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sacubitril\'s active metabolite, LBQ657, inhibits neprilysin, a neutral endopeptidase that would typically cleave natriuretic peptides, which include: atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP), brain natriuretic peptide (BNP), and c-type natriuretic peptide (CNP). ANP and BNP are released under atrial and ventricle stress, which activate downstream receptors leading to vasodilation, natriuresis, and diuresis. Under normal conditions, neprilysin breaks down other vasodilating peptides and also vasoconstrictors such as angiotensin I and II, endothelin-1, and peptide amyloid beta-protein. Therefore, the inhibition of neprilysin leads to reduced breakdown and increased concentration of endogenous natriuretic peptides in addition to increased levels of vasoconstricting hormones such as angiotensin II.</p><p>(However, when combined with valsartan, it would result in blocking angiotensin II from binding to its receptor, preventing the vasoconstrictive effects and resulting in a decrease in vascular resistance and blood pressure.) Cardiovascular and renal effects of sacubitril are a result of the increased levels of peptides that are normally degraded by neprilysin.</p><p>Valsartan is an oral medication that belongs to a class of drugs called angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs). It is orally active and a specific angiotensin II antagonist acting on the AT1 subtype. Angiotensin\'s attachment to the receptors causes the blood vessels to narrow (vasoconstrict), which leads to an increase in blood pressure (hypertension). Valsartan blocks the angiotensin II receptor. By blocking the action of angiotensin, valsartan dilates blood vessels and reduces blood pressure without affecting pulse rate. Valsartan has a much greater affinity (about 20,000-fold) for the AT1 receptor than for the AT2 receptor. It does not bind or block other hormone receptors or ion channels known to be important in cardiovascular regulation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sabitar 200 mg</strong></p><p> The recommended starting dose of this combination is 49/51 mg twice daily. Double the dose after 2 to 4 weeks to the target maintenance dose of 97/103 mg twice daily, as tolerated by the patient.</p><p><strong>Dose Adjustment For Patients Not Taking An ACE inhibitor Or ARB Or Previously Taking Low Doses Of These Agents</strong></p><p> A starting dose of 24/26 mg twice daily is recommended for patients not currently taking an ACE inhibitor or an angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB) and for patients previously taking low doses of these agents. Double the dose of this combination every 2 to 4 weeks to the target maintenance dose of 97/103 mg twice daily, as tolerated by the patient.</p><p>Sacubitril &amp; Valsartan is contraindicated with concomitant use of an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor. If switching from an ACE inhibitor to Sacubitril &amp; Valsartan, allow a washout period of 36 hours between administration of the two drugs.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Sabitar 200 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Adult Heart Failure:</strong> The recommended starting dose is 49/51 mg orally twice daily. Double the dose after 2 to 4 weeks to the target maintenance dose of 97/103 mg twice daily, as tolerated by the patient. Reduce the starting dose to 24/26 mg twice daily for:</p><ul><li>Patients not currently taking an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor (ACEi) or an angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB) or previously taking a low dose of these agents.</li><li>Patients with severe renal impairment.</li><li>Patients with moderate hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Heart Failure:</strong> Refer to Table 1 for the recommended dose for pediatric patients aged one year and older. Take the recommended dose orally twice daily. Adjust pediatric patient doses every 2 weeks, as tolerated by the patient.</p><p><strong>Recommended Dose Titration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Patients Less than 40 kg:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Starting: 1.6 mg/kg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Second: 2.3 mg/kg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Final: 3.1 mg/kg</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients At least 40 kg, less than 50 kg:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Starting: 24/26 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Second: 49/51 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Final: 49/51 mg</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients At least 50 kg:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Starting: 49/51 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Second: 72/78 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Final: 97/103 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sabitar 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dual Blockade of the Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System:</strong> Should not be used with an ACEi, aliskiren in patients with diabetes, and use with an ARB should be avoided.</li><li><strong>Potassium-sparing Diuretics:</strong> Serum potassium level may be increased.</li><li><strong>NSAIDs:</strong> Risk of renal impairment may be increased.</li><li><strong>Lithium:</strong> Increased risk of lithium toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> This combination is contraindicated:</p><ul><li>In patients with hypersensitivity to any component</li><li>In patients with a history of angioedema related to previous ACE inhibitor or ARB therapy</li><li>With concomitant use of ACE inhibitors. Do not administer within 36 hours of switching from or to an ACE inhibitor</li><li>With concomitant use of aliskiren in patients with diabetes</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Sabitar 200 mg</strong></p><p> The most common side effects are:</p><ul><li>Angioedema</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Impaired Renal Function</li><li>Hyperkalemia</li><li>Cough</li><li>Dizziness</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> <strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Advise female patients of childbearing age about the consequences of exposure to this combination during pregnancy. Discuss treatment options with women planning to become pregnant. Ask patients to report pregnancies to their physicians as soon as possible.</p><p><strong>Lactation:</strong> There is no information regarding the presence of sacubitril/valsartan in human milk, the effects on the breastfed infant, or the effects on milk production. Because of the potential for serious adverse reactions in breastfed infants from exposure to sacubitril/valsartan, advise a nursing woman that breastfeeding is not recommended during treatment.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>This tablet may cause angioedema and must not be used in patients with a known history of angioedema related to previous ACEi or ARB therapy and in patients with hereditary angioedema.</li><li>This tablet lowers blood pressure and may cause symptomatic hypotension. Closely monitor serum creatinine and adjust dosage accordingly.</li><li>Monitor serum potassium periodically, especially in patients with risk factors for hyperkalemia such as severe renal impairment, diabetes, or a high potassium diet.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sabitar 200 mg</strong></p><p> Limited data are available on overdosage. Hypotension is the most likely result. Symptomatic treatment should be provided.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place and store below 30°C. Protect from moisture and keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1187_1742231862.jpeg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 22:17:42', '2025-03-17 22:17:42'),
(3148, 'Oxi-Q', 'Coenzyme Q10 [Ubidecarenone]', 12.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '30 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Oxi-Q 30 mg</strong></p><p> It is indicated for Co-enzyme Q10 deficiency and mitochondrial disorders. It is also used in congestive heart failure, myocardial infarction, high blood pressure (hypertension) in combination with other medications, preventing migraine headaches, Parkinson\'s disease, improving the immune system, infertility in men, and muscular dystrophy.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Supplements &amp; adjuvant therapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Co-enzyme Q10 is a vitamin-like substance found throughout the body, especially in the heart, liver, kidneys, and pancreas. It is required for the proper function of many organs and chemical reactions in the body. It helps provide energy to cells and has antioxidant activity.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Oxi-Q 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Co-enzyme Q10 deficiency:</strong> 150 mg daily.</li><li><strong>For mitochondrial disorders:</strong> 150-160 mg, or 2 mg/kg/day.</li><li><strong>For heart failure in adults:</strong> 100 mg per day divided into 2 or 3 doses.</li><li><strong>For recent myocardial infarction:</strong> 120 mg daily in 2 divided doses.</li><li><strong>For high blood pressure:</strong> 120-200 mg per day divided into 2 doses.</li><li><strong>For isolated systolic hypertension:</strong> 60 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>For preventing migraine headaches:</strong> 100 mg three times daily.</li><li><strong>For Parkinson\'s disease:</strong> 300 mg, 600 mg, 1200 mg, and 2400 mg per day in 3-4 divided doses.</li><li><strong>For infertility in men:</strong> 200-300 mg per day.</li><li><strong>For muscular dystrophy:</strong> 100 mg per day.</li><li> Dividing the total daily dose into smaller amounts taken two or three times a day instead of a large dose at once can help reduce side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Oxi-Q 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Antihypertensive drugs:</strong> May cause blood pressure to drop too low.</li><li><strong>Anticancer drugs:</strong> May decrease the effectiveness of some anticancer drugs.</li><li><strong>Warfarin:</strong> May decrease the effectiveness of warfarin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Co-enzyme Q10 is contraindicated in patients who have hypersensitivity to the active substance or any of the excipients. It is also contraindicated in patients scheduled for surgery in the next two weeks.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Oxi-Q 30 mg</strong></p><p> Co-enzyme Q10 is safe for most adults. It can cause mild side effects, including stomach upset, loss of appetite, nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. Some people may experience allergic skin rashes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Co-enzyme Q10 should not be used during pregnancy and breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Co-enzyme Q10 may lower blood sugar levels, so caution should be taken in patients with diabetes or hypoglycemia.</li><li>Co-enzyme Q10 may decrease blood pressure, so caution is advised in patients with low blood pressure or those taking blood pressure medications.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1525_1742232047.jpeg', 11, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 22:20:47', '2025-03-17 22:20:47'),
(3149, 'Oxyton', 'Oxytocin', 10.00, 1, 'Injection', '5 Injection', 'Injection', '5 IU per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Oxyton 5 IU/ml</strong></p><p> Oxyton 5 IU/ml is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Induction of labor</strong> and stimulation of uterine contractions.</li><li><strong>Post-delivery period</strong>, for the prevention or treatment of secondary hemorrhage and adherent placenta.</li><li><strong>Stimulation of lactation</strong>, only if advised by a registered gynecologist/physician.</li><li><strong>Treatment of incomplete abortion</strong> to obtain more rapid expulsion in order to prevent secondary hemorrhage.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs acting on the uterus</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Uterine motility depends on the formation of the contractile protein actomyosin under the influence of the <strong>Ca²⁺-dependent phosphorylating enzyme myosin light-chain kinase</strong>. Oxyton 5 IU/ml promotes contractions by increasing intracellular <strong>Ca²⁺</strong>, which in turn activates myosin\'s light-chain kinase. It has specific receptors in the muscle lining of the uterus, with receptor concentration increasing significantly during pregnancy, reaching a <strong>maximum in early labor at term</strong>.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Oxyton 5 IU/ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Induction or Stimulation of Labor</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The standard solution is prepared by adding <strong>10 units of Oxyton 5 IU/ml</strong> (from a 1 ml vial) to <strong>1000 ml of infusion fluid</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The initial dose should be <strong>0.5-1 mU/min</strong> (3-6 ml/hour of the dilute solution).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Every <strong>30-60 minutes</strong>, the dose may be <strong>gradually increased</strong> by <strong>1-2 mU/min</strong> until the desired contraction pattern is established.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Once labor progresses to <strong>5-6 cm dilation</strong>, the dose may be reduced.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Infusion rates exceeding <strong>9-10 mU/min are rarely required</strong> at term.</li><li><strong>Control of Postpartum Uterine Bleeding</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV Infusion:</strong> <strong>10-40 units</strong> of Oxyton 5 IU/ml may be added to <strong>1000 ml of infusion fluid</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adjust infusion rate to <strong>sustain uterine contraction</strong> and control atony.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IM Injection:</strong> <strong>1 ml (10 units)</strong> after delivery of the placenta.</li><li><strong>Treatment of Incomplete or Inevitable Abortion</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV infusion:</strong> <strong>10 units</strong> of Oxyton 5 IU/ml in <strong>500 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride solution</strong> may help contract the uterus.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For <strong>mid-trimester elective abortion</strong>, infusion at <strong>10-20 mU/min</strong> (20-40 drops per minute) may shorten the abortion time.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Total dose should not exceed 30 units in 12 hours</strong> due to the risk of water intoxication.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Oxyton 5 IU/ml</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Induction of labor, stimulation of uterine contractions:IV infusion:</strong> <strong>1-5 IU</strong> in <strong>500 ml of infusion fluid</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Start at <strong>1 mU/min</strong>, doubling every <strong>30-45 minutes</strong> until the uterus threshold is reached.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IM administration is not preferred</strong> but, if unavoidable, the dose should not exceed <strong>0.5-1 IU per injection</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Post-delivery hemorrhage and adherent placenta:IM injection:</strong> <strong>2-5 IU</strong>, or <strong>IV infusion</strong> in adequate dosage.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>To stimulate lactation (only if advised by a doctor):IM injection:</strong> <strong>2 IU</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of incomplete abortion:IM injection:</strong> <strong>2-5 IU</strong> every <strong>30-60 minutes</strong>, or <strong>IV infusion</strong> in adequate dosage.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Oxyton 5 IU/ml</strong></p><ul><li>Severe <strong>hypertension</strong> may occur if given <strong>3-4 hours after vasoconstrictor administration</strong> during caudal-block anesthesia.</li><li><strong>Cyclopropane anesthesia</strong> may modify cardiovascular effects, leading to unexpected <strong>hypotension, maternal bradycardia, or abnormal atrioventricular rhythms</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Oxyton 5 IU/ml should not be used in:</p><ul><li><strong>Cephalopelvic disproportion</strong></li><li><strong>Abnormal fetal position</strong> (e.g., transverse lie, breech)</li><li><strong>Overdistended lower uterine segment</strong></li><li><strong>Severe toxemia</strong></li><li><strong>Hypertonic uterine dysfunction</strong></li><li><strong>Fetal distress</strong> (unless delivery is imminent)</li><li><strong>Cord presentation and prolapse</strong></li><li><strong>Total placenta previa</strong></li><li><strong>Vasa previa</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Oxyton 5 IU/ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity reactions</strong> (e.g., uterine hypertonicity, spasm, rupture).</li><li><strong>Increased blood loss and afibrinogenemia</strong> should be considered.</li><li><strong>Severe water intoxication</strong> may occur, leading to <strong>convulsions and coma</strong>.</li><li>Other possible reactions:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Nausea, vomiting</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Postpartum hemorrhage</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cardiac arrhythmias</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Anaphylactic reaction</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Jaundice in newborns (icterus neonatorum)</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong> – Risk is not ruled out.</li><li>It is <strong>unknown if Oxyton 5 IU/ml is excreted in human milk</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Uterine sensitivity to Oxyton 5 IU/ml varies widely</strong>, so careful dose adjustments are required.</li><li><strong>Must be administered under adequate medical supervision</strong>.</li><li><strong>Monitor fetal heart rate and maternal blood pressure</strong>.</li><li>Use <strong>cautiously</strong> in patients with <strong>previous uterine or cervical scars</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Oxyton 5 IU/ml</strong></p><p> Excessive Oxyton 5 IU/ml may cause:</p><ul><li><strong>Violent uterine contractions</strong>, leading to <strong>fetal bradycardia, arrhythmias, soft tissue laceration, or uterine rupture</strong>.</li><li>In severe cases, it may result in <strong>fetal or maternal death</strong>.</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> Immediately <strong>discontinue infusion</strong> and administer <strong>tocolytics</strong> to relax the uterus.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store between <strong>2-8°C</strong>, in a <strong>dark and frost-free place</strong>.</li><li><strong>Keep out of reach of children</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p> Can be diluted in:</p><ul><li><strong>0.9% Sodium Chloride solution</strong></li><li><strong>5% Dextrose-in-water solution</strong></li><li><strong>Ringer’s solution</strong></li><li><strong>Hartmann’s solution (Ringer-lactate)</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Drugs acting on the uterus</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Oxyton 5 IU/ml increases <strong>intracellular Ca²⁺ levels</strong>, activating <strong>myosin\'s light-chain kinase</strong>, which promotes <strong>uterine contractions</strong>. Receptor concentration is highest at <strong>term labor</strong>, maximizing its effect.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> <strong>Category C</strong> – It is unknown if Oxyton 5 IU/ml is excreted in human milk.</p>', 'active', '327_1742232429.jpeg', 6, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 22:27:09', '2025-03-17 22:27:09'),
(3150, 'Urilit', 'Potassium Citrate', 10.30, 15, 'Tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '1080 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Urilit 1080 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Renal Tubular Acidosis (RTA) With Calcium Stones:</strong> Potassium citrate is indicated for the management of renal tubular acidosis.</p><p><strong>Hypocitraturic Calcium Oxalate Nephrolithiasis Of Any Etiology:</strong> Potassium citrate is indicated for the management of Hypocitraturic calcium oxalate nephrolithiasis.</p><p><strong>Uric Acid Lithiasis With Or Without Calcium Stones:</strong> Potassium citrate is indicated for the management of Uric acid lithiasis with or without calcium stones.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Prevention of repeated kidney stone formation.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>When Urilit 1080 mg is given orally, the metabolism of absorbed citrate produces an alkaline load. The induced alkaline load in turn increases urinary pH and raises urinary citrate by augmenting citrate clearance without measurably altering ultrafilterable serum citrate... <em>(rest of the text remains unchanged).</em></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Urilit 1080 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Dosing Instructions:</strong> Treatment with extended-release Urilit 1080 mg should be added to a regimen that limits salt intake... <em>(continue with existing text).</em></p><p><strong>Dosage of Urilit 1080 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Dosing Instructions:</strong> Treatment with extended-release Urilit 1080 mg should be added to a regimen that limits salt intake... <em>(continue with existing text).</em></p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Urilit 1080 mg is contraindicated in patients with hyperkalemia, chronic renal failure, uncontrolled diabetes mellitus, acute dehydration... <em>(continue with existing text).</em></p><p><strong>Side Effects of Urilit 1080 mg</strong></p><p>Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and stomach pain may occur. Taking it after meals will help prevent these side effects... <em>(continue with existing text).</em></p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p><strong>Pregnancy Category C.</strong> Animal reproduction studies have not been conducted... <em>(continue with existing text).</em></p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>This medication should not be used if the patient has Addison’s disease, current bladder infection, uncontrolled diabetes, severe heart disease... <em>(continue with existing text).</em></p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Urilit 1080 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Treatment Of Overdosage:</strong> The administration of potassium salts to persons without predisposing conditions for hyperkalemia rarely causes serious hyperkalemia... <em>(continue with existing text).</em></p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in children have not been established.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Prevention of repeated kidney stone formation.</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>When Urilit 1080 mg is given orally, the metabolism of absorbed citrate produces an alkaline load... <em>(continue with existing text).</em></p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p><strong>Pregnancy Category C.</strong> Animal reproduction studies have not been conducted... <em>(continue with existing text).</em></p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in children have not been established.</p>', 'active', '1334_1742233372.jpeg', 7, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 22:42:52', '2025-03-17 23:04:13'),
(3151, 'Solina', 'Solifenacin Succinate', 14.00, 14, 'Tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Solina 5 mg</strong></p><p>Symptomatic treatment of urge incontinence and/or increased urinary frequency and urgency as may occur in patients with overactive bladder syndrome.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Anticholinergics (antimuscarinics)/ Anti-spasmodics, BPH/ Urinary retention/ Urinary incontinence</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Solifenacin is a competitive muscarinic receptor antagonist. It has the highest affinity for M3, M1, and M2 muscarinic receptors. 80% of the muscarinic receptors in the bladder are M2, while 20% are M3. Solifenacin\'s antagonism of the M3 receptor prevents contraction of the detrusor muscle, while antagonism of the M2 receptor may prevent contraction of smooth muscle in the bladder.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Solina 5 mg</strong></p><p>The recommended dose for adults and elderly is Solina 5 mg once daily. If needed, the dose may be increased to Solina 10 mg once daily.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Solina 5 mg</strong></p><p>The recommended dose for adults and the elderly: Solina 5 mg once daily. If needed, the dose may be increased to Solina 10 mg once daily.</p><p><strong>Use in children:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in children have not yet been established. Therefore, Solina 5 mg should not be used in children.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Solina 5 mg</strong></p><p>Concomitant medication with other medicinal products with anticholinergic properties may result in more pronounced therapeutic effects and undesirable effects. An interval of approximately one week should be allowed after stopping treatment with Solina 5 mg before commencing other anticholinergic therapy. The therapeutic effect of Solifenacin may be reduced by concomitant administration of cholinergic receptor agonists. Solifenacin can reduce the effect of medicinal products that stimulate the motility of the gastrointestinal tract, such as Metoclopramide and Cisapride.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Solifenacin is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to solifenacin or any of the excipients. It is also contraindicated in myasthenia gravis, urinary retention, uncontrolled narrow-angle glaucoma, severe gastrointestinal conditions (including toxic megacolon), patients undergoing haemodialysis, patients with severe hepatic impairment, and patients with severe renal impairment or moderate hepatic impairment who are on treatment with a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor (e.g. Ketoconazole).</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Solina 5 mg</strong></p><p>Due to the pharmacological effect of Solifenacin, it may cause anticholinergic undesirable effects of mild or moderate severity. The frequency of anticholinergic undesirable effects is dose-related.</p><ul><li><strong>Very common:</strong> Dry mouth</li><li><strong>Common:</strong> Constipation, nausea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain, blurred vision</li><li><strong>Uncommon:</strong> Gastroesophageal reflux disease, dry throat, urinary tract infection, somnolence, dysgeusia, fatigue, peripheral oedema, nasal dryness, dry skin</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Colonic obstruction, faecal impaction, urinary retention</li><li><strong>Very rare:</strong> Vomiting, hallucinations, dizziness, headache, pruritus, rash, urticaria</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>No clinical data are available from women who became pregnant while taking Solifenacin. Animal studies do not indicate direct harmful effects on fertility, embryonal/foetal development, or parturition. The potential risk for humans is unknown. Caution should be exercised when prescribing to pregnant women. The use of Solifenacin should be avoided during breastfeeding, as animal studies indicate it may be excreted in milk and impact neonatal development.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Other causes of frequent urination (heart failure or renal disease) should be assessed before treatment with Solina 5 mg. If urinary tract infection is present, an appropriate antibacterial therapy should be started.</p><p>Solina 5 mg should be used with caution in patients with:</p><ul><li>Clinically significant bladder outflow obstruction at risk of urinary retention</li><li>Gastrointestinal obstructive disorders</li><li>Severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min)</li><li>Moderate hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh score of 7 to 9) – dose should not exceed 5 mg</li><li>Patients using potent CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., Ketoconazole)</li><li>Hiatus hernia/gastroesophageal reflux, especially those on medications like bisphosphonates that can cause or worsen oesophagitis</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Solina 5 mg</strong></p><p>Overdose with Solina 5 mg can potentially result in severe anticholinergic effects. The highest reported accidental dose was 280 mg in 5 hours, leading to mental status changes without hospitalization. Treatment options include:</p><ul><li><strong>Activated charcoal</strong> (if within 1 hour)</li><li><strong>Gastric lavage</strong> (if within 1 hour, without inducing vomiting)</li><li><strong>Physostigmine or carbachol</strong> for severe central anticholinergic effects</li><li><strong>Benzodiazepines</strong> for convulsions or excitation</li><li><strong>Beta-blockers</strong> for tachycardia</li><li><strong>Artificial respiration</strong> for respiratory insufficiency</li><li><strong>Catheterization</strong> for urinary retention</li><li><strong>Pilocarpine eye drops</strong> for mydriasis</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children:</strong> Safety and effectiveness have not been established.</li><li><strong>Patients with renal impairment:</strong> No dose adjustment is necessary for mild to moderate renal impairment. Severe renal impairment requires caution, with a maximum of 5 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Patients with hepatic impairment:</strong> No dose adjustment is needed for mild hepatic impairment. Moderate hepatic impairment requires caution, with a maximum of 5 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Potent inhibitors of cytochrome P450 3A4:</strong> The maximum dose of Solina 5 mg should be limited to 5 mg when used with CYP3A4 inhibitors such as Ketoconazole, Ritonavir, Nelfinavir, or Itraconazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Anticholinergics (antimuscarinics)/ Anti-spasmodics, BPH/ Urinary retention/ Urinary incontinence</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Solifenacin is a competitive muscarinic receptor antagonist. It has the highest affinity for M3, M1, and M2 muscarinic receptors. 80% of the muscarinic receptors in the bladder are M2, while 20% are M3. Solifenacin\'s antagonism of the M3 receptor prevents contraction of the detrusor muscle, while antagonism of the M2 receptor may prevent contraction of smooth muscle in the bladder.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>No clinical data are available from women who became pregnant while taking Solifenacin. Animal studies do not indicate direct harmful effects on fertility, embryonal/foetal development, or parturition. The potential risk for humans is unknown. Caution should be exercised when prescribing to pregnant women. No data exist on the excretion of Solifenacin in human milk, but animal studies show it may cause neonatal developmental issues. The use of Solifenacin should therefore be avoided during breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Patients with renal impairment:</strong> No dose adjustment for mild to moderate cases. Severe cases should receive no more than 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Patients with hepatic impairment:</strong> No dose adjustment for mild cases. Moderate cases should receive no more than 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Potent inhibitors of cytochrome P450 3A4:</strong> The maximum dose should be limited to 5 mg when used with CYP3A4 inhibitors.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Solina 5 mg tablet should be taken orally, swallowed whole with liquids, and can be taken with or without food.</p>', 'active', '841_1742234874.jpg', 9, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 22:55:18', '2025-03-17 23:07:54'),
(3152, 'C-Berry', 'Cranberry [Vaccinium macrocarpon]', 20.00, 6, 'Capsule', '6 Tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of C-Berry 400 mg</strong></p><p>Cranberry is indicated in the treatment and prevention of urinary tract infection (UTI) and kidney stones.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Herbal and Nutraceuticals</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cranberry (Vaccinium macrocarpon) is a native North American plant commonly used for reducing urinary tract infections (UTIs) occurrence and managing kidney stones. Cranberry reduces the need for repeated antibiotic use in the treatment of recurrent UTIs. Bacterial adherence to mucosal surfaces is generally considered the initial event in the pathogenesis of most bacterial infectious diseases, such as UTIs. Cranberry inhibits the adherence of bacteria to the lining of the urinary bladder and urethra. At higher doses, it also works as a urinary antiseptic and reduces the incidence of stone formation. Recent publications show as much as a 65% reduction in UTI occurrences with cranberry consumption, leading to reduced use of antibiotics and fewer incidences of recurrent cystitis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of C-Berry 400 mg</strong></p><p>1-3 capsules daily or as advised by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of C-Berry 400 mg</strong></p><p>No known interactions with antibiotics or other drugs. Cranberry enhances vitamin B12 absorption, which is useful for patients taking omeprazole, a drug used to treat ulcers.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Potential contraindications of cranberry may be present with renal insufficiency and in persons with the potential for developing uric acid or calcium oxalate stones.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of C-Berry 400 mg</strong></p><p>Well tolerated at the recommended dose. Occasionally, diarrhea or mild gastrointestinal upset may occur at high doses.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>No known restrictions during pregnancy or lactation.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '76_1742234976.jpg', 53, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 23:00:45', '2025-03-17 23:09:36');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3153, 'Cipran', 'Levomilnacipran Hydrochloride', 18.00, 6, 'Capsule', '6 Tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cipran 40 mg</strong></p><p> Levomilnacipran is a serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SNRI) indicated for the treatment of Major Depressive Disorder (MDD) in adults.</p><p> <strong>Limitation of Use</strong>: Levomilnacipran is not approved for the management of fibromyalgia. The efficacy and safety of Levomilnacipran for the management of fibromyalgia have not been established.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Cipran 40 mg</strong></p><p> Recommended dose: 40 mg to 120 mg once daily with or without food. Initiate dose at 20 mg once daily for 2 days and then increase to 40 mg once daily. Based on efficacy and tolerability, increase dose in increments of 40 mg at intervals of 2 or more days. The maximum recommended dose is 120 mg once daily. Take capsules whole; do not open, chew or crush.</p><p> <strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Do not exceed 80 mg once daily for moderate impairment. Do not exceed 40 mg once daily for severe renal impairment.</p><p> <strong>Discontinuation</strong>: Reduce dose gradually whenever possible.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Cipran 40 mg</strong></p><p> Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ketoconazole: Do not exceed 80 mg once daily.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to levomilnacipran, milnacipran HCl, or to any excipient in the formulation.</p><p> The use of MAOIs intended to treat psychiatric disorders with Levomilnacipran or within 7 days of stopping treatment with Levomilnacipran is contraindicated because of an increased risk of serotonin syndrome. The use of Levomilnacipran within 14 days of stopping an MAOI intended to treat psychiatric disorders is also contraindicated.</p><p> Starting Levomilnacipran in a patient who is being treated with MAOIs such as linezolid or intravenous methylene blue is also contraindicated because of an increased risk of serotonin syndrome.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Cipran 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity</li><li>Suicidal Thoughts and Behaviors in Adolescents and Young Adults</li><li>Serotonin Syndrome</li><li>Elevated Blood Pressure</li><li>Elevated Heart Rate</li><li>Increased Risk of Bleeding</li><li>Angle Closure Glaucoma</li><li>Urinary Hesitation or Retention</li><li>Activation of Mania/Hypomania</li><li>Seizure</li><li>Discontinuation Syndrome</li><li>Hyponatremia</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SNRI)</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> The exact mechanism of the antidepressant action of levomilnacipran is unknown, but is thought to be related to the potentiation of serotonin and norepinephrine in the central nervous system, through inhibition of reuptake at serotonin and norepinephrine transporters. Non-clinical studies have shown that levomilnacipran is a potent and selective serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SNRI).</p><p> Levomilnacipran binds with high affinity to the human serotonin (5-HT) and norepinephrine (NE) transporters (Ki = 11 and 91 nM, respectively) and potently inhibits 5-HT and NE reuptake (IC50 = 16-19 and 11 nM, respectively). Levomilnacipran lacks significant affinity for any other receptors, ion channels or transporters tested in vitro, including serotonergic (5HT1-7), α- and β-adrenergic, muscarinic, or histaminergic receptors and Ca2+, Na+, K+ or Cl- channels. Levomilnacipran did not inhibit monoamine oxidase (MAO).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy: Third trimester use may increase risk for symptoms of poor adaptation (respiratory distress, temperature instability, feeding difficulty, hypotonia, tremor, irritability) in the neonate.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Pediatric Use: Clinical studies on the use of Levomilnacipran in pediatric patients have not been conducted; therefore, the safety and effectiveness of Levomilnacipran in the pediatric population have not been established. Levomilnacipran is not approved for use in pediatric patients.</p><p> <strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No dose adjustment is recommended on the basis of age. In a multiple-dose clinical pharmacokinetic study, elderly subjects (&gt; 65 years) had a slightly higher exposure (C max by 24% and AUC by 26%) of levomilnacipran than younger subjects (18-45 years). Of the total number of subjects in the 8-week clinical studies of Levomilnacipran, 2.8% of patients were age 65 or older. Because levomilnacipran is predominately excreted by the kidney, renal clearance of levomilnacipran should be considered when determining the dose. SSRIs and SNRIs, including Levomilnacipran, have been associated with cases of clinically significant hyponatremia in elderly patients, who may be at greater risk for this adverse event.</p><p> <strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Hepatic elimination of levomilnacipran is low. Dose adjustment is not recommended in subjects with mild (Child-Pugh score of 1-6), moderate (Child-Pugh score of 7-9), or severe (Child-Pugh score of 10-13) hepatic impairment.</p><p> <strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Renal excretion plays a predominant role in the elimination of levomilnacipran. Dose adjustment is not recommended for patients with mild (creatinine clearance of 60-89 ml/min) renal impairment. Dosing adjustment is recommended for patients with moderate (creatinine clearance of 30-59 ml/min) or severe (creatinine clearance of 15-29 ml/min) renal impairment. Levomilnacipran is not recommended for patients with end stage renal disease.</p>', 'active', '34_1742234704.jpeg', 6, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 23:05:04', '2025-03-17 23:05:04'),
(3154, 'Terbiderm', 'Terbinafine Hydrochloride', 50.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Terbiderm 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablet</strong>: This tablet is indicated for the treatment of onychomycosis of the toenail or fingernail due to dermatophytes (tinea unguium).</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Granules</strong>: This is indicated in Tinea Capitis.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Cream</strong>: Fungal infections of the skin caused by Trichophyton (e.g., T. rubrum, T. mentagrophytes, T. verrucosum, T. violaceum), Microsporum canis, and Epidermophyton floccosum. Yeast infections of the skin, principally those caused by the genus Candida (e.g., C. albicans). Pityriasis (tinea) versicolor due to Pityrosporum orbicular (also known as Malassezia furfur).</li><li><strong>Terbinafine 1% Spray</strong>: This spray is indicated in the treatment of tinea infections of the skin. It is also indicated in the treatment of pityriasis (tinea) versicolor due to Malassezia furfur.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses, Topical Antifungal preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Terbinafine is an allylamine with a range of antifungal activity. It is fungicidal against dermatophytes, moulds, and certain dimorphic fungi. Terbinafine is either fungicidal or fungistatic against yeasts, depending on the species. Terbinafine interferes with fungal ergosterol biosynthesis by inhibiting squalene epoxidase in the fungal cell membrane at an early stage. This leads to a deficiency in ergosterol and to intracellular accumulation of squalene, resulting in fungal cell death. Terbinafine is highly effective in fungal infections of the skin, hair, and nails caused by Trichophyton spp., Microsporum spp., and Epidermophyton floccosum. It is also effective against yeast infections of the skin, principally those caused by the genus Candida. Topical terbinafine appears to be effective in pityriasis versicolor due to Pityrosporum orbicular.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Terbiderm 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Topical Application</strong>: Terbinafine cream to affected areas once or twice daily for 1-2 weeks may be adequate for fungal infections of the skin, but certain infections may require oral Terbinafine tablet therapy.</li><li><strong>Oral Administration</strong>: Terbinafine tablet is essential for hair or nail infections. The usual oral dose is Terbinafine 250 mg daily for 2 to 12 weeks depending upon the infection.</li><li><strong>Finger Nail Onychomycosis</strong>: Terbinafine 250 mg once daily for 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Toe Nail Onychomycosis</strong>: Terbinafine 250 mg once daily for 12 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Terbiderm 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fingernail Onychomycosis: 250 mg (one tablet), once daily for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Toenail Onychomycosis: 250 mg (one tablet), once daily for 12 weeks.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Granules</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Body Weight: &lt;25 kg: 125 mg/day up to 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Body Weight: 25-35 kg: 187.5 mg/day up to 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Body Weight: &gt;35 kg: 250 mg/day up to 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Cream</strong>: Apply once or twice daily. Cleanse and dry the affected areas before application. Apply the cream to the affected skin and the surrounding area in a thin layer and rub in lightly.</li><li><strong>Duration of Treatment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Tinea corporis, cruris: 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Tinea pedis: 2-4 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Cutaneous candidiasis: 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pityriasis versicolor: 2 weeks</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Terbiderm 250 mg</strong></p><p> Terbinafine is an inhibitor of the CYP450 2D6 isozyme. Co-administration of terbinafine with drugs metabolized by CYP450 2D6 (e.g., tricyclic antidepressants, beta-blockers) should be done with careful monitoring and may require a dose reduction of the other drug.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Terbinafine tablet and cream are contraindicated in individuals with hypersensitivity to terbinafine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Terbiderm 250 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include gastrointestinal symptoms (diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain), liver test abnormalities, rashes, urticaria, pruritus, and taste disturbances. Rare but serious adverse events include liver failure, severe skin reactions, and allergic reactions.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablet</strong>: Not recommended during pregnancy unless the benefit outweighs the risk. Terbinafine is excreted in breast milk and should not be used in nursing mothers.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Cream</strong>: Should not be used during pregnancy unless the potential benefits outweigh the risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablets</strong>: Rare cases of liver failure have occurred. Treatment should be discontinued if there is evidence of liver injury.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Cream</strong>: For external use only. Avoid contact with eyes.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Terbiderm 250 mg</strong></p><p> Symptoms of overdose include nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dizziness, rash, frequent urination, and headache. Doses up to 5 gm (20 times the therapeutic daily dose) have been taken without serious adverse effects.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, below 30°C, and protect from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in Children</strong>: Terbinafine cream is effective and well-tolerated for treating tinea corporis and tinea cruris in children.</li><li><strong>Use in Elderly</strong>: Terbinafine is generally safe for elderly patients, but dose adjustments may be necessary in cases of significant hepatic or renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Other Antifungal preparations, Topical Antifungal preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Terbinafine inhibits the biosynthesis of ergosterol, an essential component of fungal cell membranes, by inhibiting the enzyme squalene epoxidase. This results in fungal cell death due to increased membrane permeability.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablet</strong>: Should not be initiated during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Cream</strong>: Should not be administered unless the benefits outweigh the risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> The safety and efficacy of terbinafine have not been established in pediatric patients.</p><p><strong>Use in the Elderly</strong></p><p> There is no evidence suggesting that elderly patients require different dosages or experience different side effects compared to younger patients.</p>', 'active', '1621_1742235235.jpg', 43, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 23:13:55', '2025-03-17 23:13:55'),
(3155, 'Neoral', 'Cyclosporine', 234.00, 5, 'Capsule', '5 Tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sandimmum Neoral 100 mg</strong></p><p>Sandimmum Neoral 100 mg Ophthalmic Emulsion is indicated to increase tear production in patients whose tear production is presumed to be suppressed due to ocular inflammation associated with dry eye syndrome (keratoconjunctivitis sicca). In addition to this, several clinical studies have shown that Sandimmum Neoral 100 mg is effective in the management of different eye diseases like vernal keratoconjunctivitis, posterior blepharitis, non-infectious uveitis, superior limbic keratoconjunctivitis, phlyctenular keratoconjunctivitis, and meibomian gland diseases.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for Dry eyes</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Sandimmum Neoral 100 mg is an immunosuppressive agent when administered systemically. In patients whose tear production is presumed to be suppressed due to ocular inflammation associated with keratoconjunctivitis sicca, topical Sandimmum Neoral 100 mg is thought to act as a partial immunomodulator.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sandimmum Neoral 100 mg</strong></p><p>One drop of Sandimmum Neoral 100 mg eye drop should be instilled twice a day in each eye, approximately 12 hours apart. Sandimmum Neoral 100 mg eye drop can be used concomitantly with artificial tears, allowing a 15-minute interval between products.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Sandimmum Neoral 100 mg is contraindicated in patients with active ocular infections and in patients with known or suspected hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients in the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sandimmum Neoral 100 mg</strong></p><p>The most common adverse event was ocular burning. Other events reported included conjunctival hyperemia, discharge, epiphora, eye pain, foreign body sensation, pruritus, stinging, and visual disturbance (most often blurring).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies of Sandimmum Neoral 100 mg Ophthalmic Emulsion in pregnant women. It should be administered to pregnant women if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Sandimmum Neoral 100 mg is known to be excreted in human milk following systemic administration, but excretion in human milk after topical treatment has not been investigated. Although blood concentrations are undetectable after topical administration of Sandimmum Neoral 100 mg eye drop, caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Sandimmum Neoral 100 mg eye drop should not be administered while wearing contact lenses. Patients with decreased tear production typically should not wear contact lenses. If contact lenses are worn, they should be removed prior to the administration of this formulation. Lenses may be reinserted 15 minutes following administration of Sandimmum Neoral 100 mg eye drop.</p>', 'active', '148_1742235525.jpg', 40, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 23:18:45', '2025-03-17 23:31:36'),
(3156, 'Xablin', 'Pregabalin', 17.15, 10, 'Capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '75 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xablin 75 mg</strong></p><p> Xablin 75 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Neuropathic pain linked to diabetic peripheral neuropathy (DPN)</li><li>Neuralgia following shingles (PHN)</li><li>Treatment of partial-onset seizures in patients older than one month with adjunctive therapy</li><li>Fibromyalgia</li><li>Neuropathic pain brought on by spinal cord damage</li></ul><p>The following conditions are treated with Xablin 75 mg CR tablets:</p><ul><li>Neuropathic pain brought on by diabetic peripheral neuropathy (DPN)</li><li>Neuralgia following shingles (PHN)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Supplemental antiepileptic medications</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Gamma-amino butyric acid, an inhibitory neurotransmitter, is the structural basis of the drug Xablin 75 mg (GABA). The GABAA, GABAB, or benzodiazepine receptors are not directly bound by it. In the tissues of the central nervous system, Xablin 75 mg binds to the alpha-2-delta site (an auxiliary subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels) with a high affinity. Xablin 75 mg has a 90% oral bioavailability that is dose-independent. In people with normal renal function, it is largely excreted from the body through the kidneys as an unaltered medication with a mean elimination half-life of 6.3 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xablin 75 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neuropathic pain associated with diabetic peripheral neuropathy</strong>: The maximum recommended dose of Xablin 75 mg is 100 mg three times a day (300 mg/day) in patients with creatinine clearance of at least 60 mL/min. Dosing should begin at 50 mg three times a day (150 mg/day) and may be increased to 300 mg/day within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability.</li><li><strong>Post-herpetic neuralgia</strong>: The recommended dose of Xablin 75 mg is 75 to 150 mg two times a day, or 50 to 100 mg three times a day (150 to 300 mg/day) in patients with creatinine clearance of at least 60 mL/min. Dosing should begin at 75 mg two times a day, or 50 mg three times a day (150 mg/day) and may be increased to 300 mg/day within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability.</li><li><strong>Adjunctive therapy for adult patients with partial onset seizures</strong>: In general, it is recommended that patients be started on a total daily dose no greater than 150 mg/day (75 mg two times a day, or 50 mg three times a day). Based on individual patient response and tolerability, the dose may be increased to a maximum dose of 600 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Management of Fibromyalgia</strong>: The recommended dose of Xablin 75 mg for fibromyalgia is 300 to 450 mg/day. Dosing should begin at 75 mg two times a day (150 mg/day) and may be increased to 150 mg two times a day (300 mg/day) within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability.</li><li><strong>Neuropathic pain associated with spinal cord injury</strong>: The recommended dose range is 150 to 600 mg/day. The recommended starting dose is 75 mg two times a day (150 mg/day). The dose may be increased to 150 mg two times a day (300 mg/day) within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability. Xablin 75 mg capsules can be taken without regards to meals.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Xablin 75 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neuropathic pain associated with diabetic peripheral neuropathy in adults (DPN)</strong>: The maximum recommended dose of Xablin 75 mg is 100 mg three times a day (300 mg/day) in patients with creatinine clearance of at least 60 ml/min. Dosing should begin at 50 mg three times a day (150 mg/day). The dose may be increased to 300 mg/day within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability.</li><li><strong>Postherpetic neuralgia in adults (PHN)</strong>: The recommended dose of Xablin 75 mg is 75 to 150 mg two times a day or 50 to 100 mg three times a day (150 to 300 mg/day) in patients with creatinine clearance of at least 60 ml/min. Dosing should begin at 75 mg two times a day or 50 mg three times a day (150 mg/day). The dose may be increased to 300 mg/day within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability.</li><li><strong>Management of fibromyalgia in adults</strong>: The recommended dose of Xablin 75 mg is 300 to 450 mg/day. Dosing should begin at 75 mg two times a day (150 mg/day). The dose may be increased to 150 mg two times a day (300 mg/day) within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability.</li><li><strong>Neuropathic pain associated with spinal cord injury in adults</strong>: The recommended dose range of Xablin 75 mg is 150 to 600 mg/day. The recommended starting dose is 75 mg two times a day (150 mg/day). The dose may be increased to 150 mg two times a day (300 mg/day) within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Xablin 75 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Route of administration</strong>: Xablin 75 mg is administered orally. Taking it with or without food is acceptable. The Xablin 75 mg CR tablet should be taken after dinner. It shouldn\'t be divided, crushed, or chewed; it should be consumed whole.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xablin 75 mg</strong></p><p> Drug interaction with medication: There are not anticipated to be many significant pharmacokinetic medication interactions involving Xablin 75 mg. No relevant drug-food or other-drug interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Those with a known hypersensitivity to Xablin 75 mg or any of its ingredients should not take Xablin 75 mg.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xablin 75 mg</strong></p><p> Dizziness, somnolence, dry mouth, edema, impaired vision, weight gain, and aberrant thinking (mainly difficulties with concentration/attention) are the most frequent adverse effects in adults. Increased weight and increased hunger are the most frequent adverse reactions in children receiving therapy for partial-onset seizures.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Xablin 75 mg is classified as a C pregnancy drug. Hence, it should only be used if the potential benefits outweigh the hazards to the fetus. Xablin 75 mg should only be administered to nursing mothers if the risks are clearly outweighed by the potential benefits. Xablin 75 mg may be released through breast milk like other medications.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Angioedema (swelling of the throat, head, and neck, for example) can happen and is sometimes linked to respiratory compromise that requires immediate medical attention. In these circumstances, Xablin 75 mg should be stopped promptly. Also, if hypersensitivity reactions like hives, dyspnea, or wheezing manifest, Xablin 75 mg should be stopped right away. Xablin 75 mg and other antiepileptic medications raise the chance of having suicidal ideas or actions. Xablin 75 mg may cause respiratory depression when used concurrently with CNS depressants or when there is underlying respiratory impairment. Patients must be watched closely, and dosage needs to be modified as necessary. Xablin 75 mg may make a patient drowsy and woozy, making it difficult for them to drive or operate machinery. Xablin 75 mg may become more frequent in seizures or have other negative effects if it is abruptly stopped. Xablin 75 mg should be discontinued gradually over at least two weeks.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xablin 75 mg</strong></p><p> Reduced consciousness, melancholy or anxiety, confusional mood, agitation, and restlessness are signs and symptoms of a Xablin 75 mg overdose. As well as heart block and seizures, there have been reports. There isn\'t a particular remedy. If necessary, emesis or stomach lavage may be used to try to eliminate any unabsorbed medication; normal safety procedures should be followed to preserve the airway. It is necessary to provide the patient with general supportive care, which includes keeping track of their vital signs and assessing their clinical condition.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Maintain in a cool, dry location (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> Xablin 75 mg\'s efficacy and safety in pediatric patients have not been shown.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs, Primary anti-epileptic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Gamma-aminobutyric acid, an inhibitory neurotransmitter, is the structural basis of the drug Xablin 75 mg (GABA). It doesn\'t directly bind to benzodiazepine, GABAA, or GABAB receptors. In the tissues of the central nervous system, Xablin 75 mg binds to the alpha2-delta site (an auxiliary subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels) with a high affinity. Xablin 75 mg\'s mechanism of action is still unknown, although findings from animal research point to a potential role for binding to the alpha2-delta subunit in the drug\'s anti-nociceptive and anti-seizure properties.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Xablin 75 mg use in expectant women has not been adequately and carefully studied. The potential harm to a fetus should be disclosed to expectant mothers. Xablin 75 mg has been found in trace levels in the milk of nursing women. Breastfeeding is not advised while using Xablin 75 mg due to the potential risk of tumorigenicity.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Usage in children and adolescents: Safety and efficacy for the treatment of fibromyalgia, postherpetic neuralgia, neuropathic pain associated with spinal cord injury, and neuropathic pain associated with diabetic peripheral neuropathy in pediatric patients have not been proven. The safety and efficacy of supplementary therapy for partial onset seizures in pediatric children younger than one month of age have not been established. Xablin 75 mg extended-release tablet safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been shown.</p>', 'active', '505_1742235849.jpg', 57, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 23:24:09', '2025-03-17 23:24:09'),
(3157, 'Vastocor', 'Simvastatin', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vastocor 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Primary hypercholesterolemia (type IIa and IIb) in patients who have not responded adequately to diet and other appropriate measures.</li><li>Coronary heart disease and elevated plasma cholesterol level.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs, Statins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Vastocor 10 mg acts as a cholesterol-lowering agent. It works by inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase activity, which increases the LDL receptor density on liver cells. This leads to an increased removal of LDL cholesterol from the plasma and increased catabolism of LDL cholesterol. Additionally, it reduces very low-density lipoprotein (VLDL) cholesterol and reduces the formation of LDL from VLDL. Vastocor 10 mg is extensively metabolized in the liver, which is also the main site of action of the drug.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Vastocor 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>The patient should be placed on a standard cholesterol-lowering diet before receiving Vastocor 10 mg and continue this during treatment.</li><li>The usual starting dose is 10 mg/day given as a single dose in the evening.</li><li>Adjust dosage, if needed, at intervals of not less than four weeks, to a maximum of 40 mg daily.</li><li>In hypercholesterolemia, the recommended starting dose is 5-10 mg once a day in the evening, with a dosing range of 5-40 mg per day as a single dose in the evening.</li><li>In patients with coronary heart disease and hypercholesterolemia, the starting dose should be 20 mg once a day in the evening.</li><li>Since Vastocor 10 mg does not undergo significant renal excretion, dosage modification is not necessary in patients with renal insufficiency.</li><li>Safety and effectiveness in children and adolescents have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Vastocor 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Same as Dosage &amp; Administration.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Vastocor 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Digoxin:</strong> Concomitant administration of Vastocor 10 mg and Digoxin results in a slight elevation (less than 0.3 µgm/ml) in plasma drug concentrations.</li><li><strong>Coumarin derivatives:</strong> Slightly enhance the anticoagulant effect of Warfarin (mean changes in prothrombin time less than two seconds).</li><li><strong>Others:</strong> Vastocor 10 mg has been used with ACE inhibitors, beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, diuretics, and NSAIDs without significant adverse interactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Active liver disease</li><li>Pregnant and breastfeeding women</li><li>Women of childbearing age unless adequately protected by contraception</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the preparation</li><li>Patients with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia who have a complete absence of LDL receptors</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Vastocor 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Headache, fatigue, insomnia</li><li>Gastrointestinal effects like nausea, constipation, diarrhea, flatulence, dyspepsia, abdominal cramps</li><li>Muscular effects like myalgia, myositis, and myopathy</li><li>Rare cases of rhabdomyolysis with acute renal failure secondary to myoglobinuria</li><li>Hepatitis, pancreatitis, rash, angioedema</li><li>No life-threatening effects reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category X:</strong> Studies in animals or humans have demonstrated fetal abnormalities or evidence of fetal risk, and the risk of using the drug in pregnant women clearly outweighs any possible benefit.</li><li>Contraindicated in women who are or may become pregnant.</li><li>The drug is contraindicated for breastfeeding mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>History of liver disease or high alcohol consumption: Liver function tests should be done before and during treatment.</li><li>If serum transaminase rises three times the upper limit of normal, treatment should be discontinued.</li><li>Avoid pregnancy during and for one month after treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Vastocor 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>There are no specific data on overdose.</li><li>No antidote is available.</li><li>General measures should be adopted, and liver function should be monitored.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, away from light.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs, Statins</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Vastocor 10 mg is a cholesterol-lowering agent that works by inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase activity, increasing LDL receptor density on liver cells. This leads to increased removal of LDL cholesterol from the plasma, as well as reduced VLDL cholesterol and LDL formation from VLDL. It is metabolized in the liver, which is the main site of action.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Category X: The drug is contraindicated in pregnant women as it may cause fetal abnormalities.</li><li>Contraindicated for breastfeeding mothers.</li></ul>', 'active', '856_1742236163.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-17 23:29:23', '2025-03-17 23:29:23'),
(3158, 'Zentixol D', 'Zuclopenthixol', 400.00, 1, 'x 200 mg ampoule', '200 mg ampoule', 'Injection', '200 mg per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zentixol D 200 mg/ml</strong></p><p>Zentixol D 200 mg/ml is indicated for the management of the manifestations of schizophrenia. This is intended for the initial treatment of acute psychotic episodes or exacerbation of psychosis associated with schizophrenia.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Phenothiazine related drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zentixol D 200 mg/ml, a thioxanthene derivative, has high affinity for both dopamine D1 receptors and dopamine D2 receptors. Zentixol D 200 mg/ml also has high affinity for α1-adrenergic and 5 HT2 receptors. It has weaker histamine H1 receptor blocking activity and even lower affinity for muscarinic cholinergic and α2-adrenergic receptors.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zentixol D 200 mg/ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Zentixol D 200 mg/ml Tablets:</strong></li><li>When initiating treatment with Zentixol D 200 mg/ml tablets, it is recommended that the drug be given in divided doses (BID or TID). During the maintenance phase of treatment, Zentixol D 200 mg/ml tablets may be given as a single nighttime dose. For acute psychosis, the usual starting dose is 10-50 mg/day, which may be increased by 10-20 mg every 2-3 days, according to the patient’s response. The usual therapeutic range is 20 mg to 60 mg daily. However, as with other antipsychotic drugs, some patients may require lower, while others may require higher dosage in order to obtain optimal benefit. Daily dosage higher than 100 mg is not recommended. For maintenance therapy, dosage should be reduced to the lowest level compatible with symptom control. The usual maintenance dose is 20-40 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Zentixol D 200 mg/ml Acuphase:</strong></li><li>Dosage should be individually adjusted according to the patient’s condition. The usual dose is 50-150 mg (1-3 ml) administered intramuscularly and repeated if necessary, at intervals of 2-3 days. Some patients may need an additional injection 1 or 2 days after the first injection.</li><li><strong>Zentixol D 200 mg/ml Depot:</strong></li><li>Close supervision is required during the period following initiation of Zentixol D 200 mg/ml Depot treatment, in order to minimize the risk of over-medication or insufficient suppression of psychotic symptoms. Supplemental oral antipsychotic medication may be required in diminishing dosage during this period. The usual maintenance dose is 150-300 mg intramuscularly, every 2-4 weeks. Some patients may require higher or lower doses, or shorter intervals between doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zentixol D 200 mg/ml</strong></p><p>Drugs that may interact with Zentixol D 200 mg/ml include:</p><ul><li>Tricyclic antidepressant medicines</li><li>Guanethidine and similar medicines (used to lower blood pressure)</li><li>Barbiturates and similar medicines</li><li>Levodopa and similar medicines (used to treat Parkinson’s disease)</li><li>Metoclopramide (used in the treatment of gastro-intestinal disorders)</li><li>Other antipsychotic medicines (e.g., thioridazine)</li><li>Medicines that change the heartbeat (e.g., quinidine, amiodarone, sotalol, erythromycin, moxifloxacin, lithium)</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Acute alcohol, barbiturate, or opiate intoxication.</li><li>CNS depression due to any cause, comatose states, suspected or established subcortical brain damage, or circulatory collapse.</li><li>Patients with known hypersensitivity to thioxanthenes, Zentixol D 200 mg/ml, or to any ingredient in the formulation or component of the container.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Zentixol D 200 mg/ml</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Racing heart (tachycardia), a sensation of a rapid, forceful, or irregular beating of the heart (palpitations)</li><li>Tremor, twisting or repetitive movements, or abnormal postures due to sustained muscle contractions (dystonia)</li><li>Increased muscle stiffness (hypertonia)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The safe use of Zentixol D 200 mg/ml during pregnancy has not been established.</li><li>Zentixol D 200 mg/ml is excreted in human milk. The safe use of Zentixol D 200 mg/ml during lactation has not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be taken if:</p><ul><li>You have used Zentixol D 200 mg/ml or any other treatment for schizophrenia before and had any problems.</li><li>You are taking any other prescription or non-prescription medicines.</li><li>You are pregnant or thinking of becoming pregnant, or if you are breast-feeding.</li><li>You regularly drink a lot of alcohol.</li><li>You have any liver problems, Parkinson’s disease, or have ever had seizures.</li><li>You have dementia, glaucoma, or any kidney problems.</li><li>You have risk factors for stroke (e.g., smoking, high blood pressure).</li><li>You have hypokalemia or hypomagnesia (too little potassium or magnesium in your blood).</li><li>You have a history of cardiovascular disorders.</li><li>You or someone in your family has a history of blood clots.</li><li>You are being treated for cancer (applies to Zentixol D 200 mg/ml Depot only).</li><li>You use any other antipsychotic medicine.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zentixol D 200 mg/ml</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdose may include:</p><ul><li>Somnolence, coma</li><li>Unusual movements, convulsions, shock</li><li>High or low body temperature</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Should be stored between 15°C and 25°C. Protect from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Zentixol D 200 mg/ml in children under the age of 18 years have not been established; therefore, its use is not recommended.</p>', 'active', '1385_1742239251.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 00:20:51', '2025-05-05 22:19:39'),
(3159, 'Sinaceph', 'Cephradine', 55.00, 1, 'x 15 ml bottle', '15 ml bottle', 'Pediatric Drops', '125 mg/1.25 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>It looks like you\'ve provided a detailed description of <strong>Sinaceph 125 mg/1.25 ml</strong> (a cephalosporin antibiotic). Here\'s a summary of the key points from your text in normal text:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Sinaceph 125 mg/1.25 ml Overview</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: If a patient is hypersensitive to penicillins, there\'s a risk of cross-sensitivity with beta-lactam antibiotics. Cephalosporins may also cause a positive Coombs\' test, particularly in neonates whose mothers received cephalosporins before labor. This is important to note, as it may be due to the drug and not the condition.</li><li><strong>Urine Glucose Testing</strong>: Sinaceph can cause a false positive in urine glucose tests when using Benedict’s or Fehling’s solutions, or tests like Clinitest. However, enzyme-based tests (such as Clinistix or Diastix) are not affected by this.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Dosage adjustments are required in patients with kidney impairment.</li><li><strong>Lactose</strong>: This product contains lactose, so individuals with certain rare hereditary issues like galactose intolerance or glucose-galactose malabsorption should avoid it.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose are non-specific and generally include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and stomach discomfort. Treatment is mainly supportive, and gastric lavage may be needed if a large quantity is ingested.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Suspension</strong>: Should be freshly prepared. If kept at room temperature, it should be used within 7 days; if refrigerated, within 14 days.</li><li><strong>Injection Solutions</strong>: Should be used within 2 hours at room temperature or within 12 hours if stored at 5°C.</li><li><strong>Color Variations</strong>: Reconstituted solutions may vary in color from light to straw yellow, but this does not affect their potency.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>First-generation Cephalosporins</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</p><ul><li>Sinaceph is a semisynthetic, broad-spectrum, bactericidal antibiotic effective against both gram-positive and gram-negative microorganisms. It inhibits the cross-linking of peptidoglycan in the bacterial cell wall, leading to bacterial lysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Breastfeeding</strong>:</p><ul><li>While animal studies haven\'t shown teratogenicity, the safety of Sinaceph during pregnancy is not established. It is excreted in breast milk, so caution is advised for lactating mothers. Patients should also be cautious about dizziness and avoid operating heavy machinery.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment Dosing</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl &gt;20 ml/min: 500 mg every 6 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 5-20 ml/min: 250 mg every 6 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl &lt;5 ml/min: 250 mg every 50-70 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For patients on chronic intermittent hemodialysis: specific dosing schedules are provided.</li><li><strong>Additional Considerations</strong>: Higher doses (up to 1 gm four times daily) may be required for chronic or severe infections. Treatment should continue for at least 2-3 days after symptoms resolve, and in the case of streptococcal infections, for at least 10 days to prevent complications like rheumatic fever or glomerulonephritis.</li></ul><p><strong>Note for All Patients</strong>:</p><ul><li>Doses should not exceed the recommended levels for pediatric patients, regardless of infection severity. Persistent infections may require prolonged therapy, and patients may be switched from IV/IM therapy to oral therapy at the same dosage.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you\'d like further clarification or any specific points expanded!</p>', 'active', '702_1742239406.jpg', 30, 12, 'rukai.mallick@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 00:23:26', '2025-05-06 09:32:31'),
(3160, 'Telmipres Plus', 'Telmisartan + Hydrochlorothiazide', 11.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '80 mg+12.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Telmipres Plus 80 mg + 12.5 mg</h3><p><strong>Zuclopenthixol</strong> is indicated for the management of the manifestations of schizophrenia. This is intended for the initial treatment of acute psychotic episodes or exacerbation of psychosis associated with schizophrenia.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Phenothiazine-related drugs</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Zuclopenthixol, a thioxanthene derivative, has high affinity for both dopamine D1 and D2 receptors. It also has a high affinity for α1-adrenergic and 5-HT2 receptors. Zuclopenthixol exhibits weaker histamine H1 receptor blocking activity and even lower affinity for muscarinic cholinergic and α2-adrenergic receptors.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Telmipres Plus 80 mg + 12.5 mg</h3><p><strong>Zuclopenthixol Tablets:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial treatment for acute psychosis</strong>: The usual starting dose is 10-50 mg/day, which can be increased by 10-20 mg every 2-3 days according to the patient’s response.</li><li><strong>Therapeutic range</strong>: The typical dose is between 20 mg to 60 mg daily, with some patients requiring higher or lower doses.</li><li><strong>Maintenance phase</strong>: Once symptoms are controlled, the dose can be reduced to the lowest level compatible with symptom control. The usual maintenance dose is 20-40 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Maximum dose</strong>: Daily dosages higher than 100 mg are not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Zuclopenthixol Acuphase</strong> (Injection):</p><ul><li>Usual dose: 50-150 mg (1-3 ml), administered intramuscularly and repeated if necessary at intervals of 2-3 days.</li><li>Some patients may require an additional injection 1 or 2 days after the first injection.</li></ul><p><strong>Zuclopenthixol Depot</strong> (Long-acting Injection):</p><ul><li>Usual maintenance dose: 150-300 mg intramuscularly every 2-4 weeks.</li><li>Some patients may require higher or lower doses, or shorter intervals between doses.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Telmipres Plus 80 mg + 12.5 mg</h3><p>Drugs that may interact with Zuclopenthixol include:</p><ul><li>Tricyclic antidepressants</li><li>Guanethidine and similar medicines (used to lower blood pressure)</li><li>Barbiturates</li><li>Levodopa and similar drugs (used for Parkinson\'s disease)</li><li>Metoclopramide (for gastrointestinal disorders)</li><li>Other antipsychotic medicines (e.g., thioridazine)</li><li>Medicines that affect the heartbeat (e.g., quinidine, amiodarone, sotalol, erythromycin, moxifloxacin, lithium)</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Acute alcohol, barbiturate, or opiate intoxication</li><li>CNS depression from any cause, comatose states, suspected or established subcortical brain damage, or circulatory collapse</li><li>Hypersensitivity to thioxanthenes, zuclopenthixol, or any ingredient in the formulation</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Telmipres Plus 80 mg + 12.5 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Common side effects</strong>: Racing heart (tachycardia), sensation of rapid or irregular heartbeat (palpitations), tremor, dystonia, hypertonia.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>The safe use of zuclopenthixol during pregnancy has not been established.</li><li>Zuclopenthixol is excreted in human milk, and its safe use during lactation has not been established.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><p>Caution should be taken if:</p><ul><li>You have previously used zuclopenthixol or other treatments for schizophrenia and had issues.</li><li>You are pregnant, planning to become pregnant, or breastfeeding.</li><li>You regularly drink alcohol or have liver, kidney, or Parkinson’s disease.</li><li>You have seizures, dementia, glaucoma, or cardiovascular problems.</li><li>You have risk factors for stroke (e.g., smoking, high blood pressure), hypokalemia, or hypomagnesia.</li><li>You have a family history of blood clots or are being treated for cancer (applies to zuclopenthixol Depot only).</li><li>You are using any other antipsychotic medication.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Telmipres Plus 80 mg + 12.5 mg</h3><p>Symptoms of overdose may include:</p><ul><li>Somnolence, coma</li><li>Unusual movements, convulsions</li><li>Shock, high or low body temperature</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store between 15°C and 25°C.</li><li>Protect from light.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of zuclopenthixol in children under the age of 18 years have not been established, so its use is not recommended in this population.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '965_1742240341.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 00:34:21', '2025-05-12 21:20:34');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3161, 'Sinaceph', 'Cephradine', 55.00, 1, 'x 500 mg vial', '500 mg vial', 'IV Injection', '500 mg/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s your text converted to normal bold formatting:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Sinaceph 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Sinaceph 500 mg/vial is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. These include:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper respiratory tract infections</strong>: sinusitis, pharyngitis, tonsillitis, laryngo-tracheo bronchitis, otitis media</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong>: bronchitis (acute and chronic), lobar pneumonia, bronchopneumonia</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: cystitis, urethritis, pyelonephritis</li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: abscess, cellulitis, furunculosis, impetigo</li></ul><p>The following microorganisms are susceptible, in vitro, to Sinaceph 500 mg/vial:</p><ul><li><strong>Gram-positive</strong>: Staphylococci (both penicillin-sensitive and resistant strains, and penicillinase-producing species), Streptococci, Streptococcus pyogenes (beta-hemolytic), Streptococcus pneumoniae</li><li><strong>Gram-negative</strong>: Escherichia coli, Klebsiella spp, Proteus mirabilis, Haemophilus influenzae, Shigella spp, Salmonella spp (including Salmonella typhi), Neisseria spp</li></ul><p>Many strains of <strong>E. coli</strong> and <strong>Staphylococcus aureus</strong> that produce penicillinase (making them ampicillin-resistant) are susceptible to Sinaceph 500 mg/vial, as it is unaffected by this enzyme.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>First generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Sinaceph 500 mg/vial is a cephalosporin antibiotic with broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against both gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria. Sinaceph interferes with bacterial cell wall synthesis by inhibiting the transpeptidase enzyme. This weakens the bacterial cell wall, causing it to swell and eventually rupture.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sinaceph 500 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For respiratory tract infections (other than lobar pneumonia) and skin and skin structure infections: 250 mg every 6 hours or 500 mg every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For lobar pneumonia: 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 g every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For uncomplicated urinary tract infections: 500 mg every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For more serious urinary tract infections (including prostatitis): 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 g every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Children</strong> (over 9 months of age):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual dose: 25 to 50 mg/kg/day in equally divided doses every 6 or 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For otitis media due to <em>H. influenzae</em>: 75 to 100 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 6 or 12 hours (maximum daily dose: 4 g).</li></ul><p><strong>Oral Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Urinary tract infections: 500 mg four times daily or 1 g twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Respiratory tract infections: 250 to 500 mg four times daily or 500 mg to 1 g twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin and soft tissue infections: 250 to 500 mg four times daily or 500 mg to 1 g twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Total daily dose: 25 to 50 mg/kg in two or four equally divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Otitis media: 75 to 100 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 6 or 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum daily dosage: 4 g.</li></ul><p><strong>Elderly</strong>:</p><ul><li>Normal adult dose is appropriate.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal or hepatic impairment</strong>:</p><ul><li>Patients with impaired function should be monitored during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Injectable Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 2-4 gm daily in four equally divided doses, up to 8 gm daily.</li><li>For prophylaxis: a single preoperative dose of 1-2 gm IM or IV.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 50-100 mg/kg daily in four equally divided doses, up to 300 mg/kg daily for severe infections.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sinaceph 500 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li>The concomitant use of nephrotoxic drugs (such as aminoglycosides) may increase the risk of kidney damage.</li><li>Diuretics (e.g., frusemide, ethacrynic acid) and probenecid may enhance the possibility of renal toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Sinaceph 500 mg/vial should not be used in patients with known or suspected hypersensitivity to cephalosporins.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Sinaceph 500 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Gastrointestinal disturbances, hypersensitivity reactions (especially in individuals with a history of allergies, asthma, hay fever, or urticaria).</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Glossitis, heartburn, dizziness, tightness in the chest, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, vaginitis, candida overgrowth.</li><li><strong>Skin and hypersensitivity reactions</strong>: Urticaria, skin rashes, joint pains, edema.</li><li><strong>Blood disorders</strong>: Thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, agranulocytosis, aplastic anemia, hemolytic anemia.</li><li><strong>Immune system disorders</strong>: Fever, serum sickness-like reactions, anaphylaxis.</li><li><strong>Psychiatric</strong>: Confusion, sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Nervous system</strong>: Hyperactivity, dizziness, nervousness, headaches.</li><li><strong>Hepatic</strong>: Liver enzyme disturbances, transient hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice.</li><li><strong>Renal</strong>: Reversible interstitial nephritis.</li><li><strong>Investigations</strong>: Elevation of blood urea nitrogen, serum creatinine, liver enzymes, bilirubin, and alkaline phosphatase.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: US FDA Category B. While animal studies have not shown teratogenicity, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Sinaceph is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administering to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged use may lead to superinfection due to the emergence of resistant organisms.</li><li>Use with caution in patients hypersensitive to penicillins due to cross-sensitivity risks.</li><li>Cephalosporins can cause a positive Coombs\' test.</li><li>False positive urine glucose results may occur with Benedict’s or Fehling’s solutions or Clinitest tablets, but not with enzyme-based tests.</li><li>Dosage adjustment is necessary in renal impairment.</li><li>Contains lactose: should be avoided by patients with galactose intolerance, Lapp lactase deficiency, or glucose-galactose malabsorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sinaceph 500 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and gastric upset. Treatment is mainly supportive, and gastric lavage may be necessary if a large amount is ingested.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Suspension</strong>: Freshly prepared. Reconstituted suspension should be used within 7 days at room temperature or within 14 days when refrigerated.</li><li><strong>Injection Solutions</strong>: Use within 2 hours at room temperature or within 12 hours at 5°C.</li><li><strong>Color</strong>: Reconstituted solutions may vary from light to straw yellow, but this does not affect potency.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>First-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Sinaceph 500 mg/vial is a semisynthetic, broad-spectrum bactericidal antibiotic. It acts by inhibiting bacterial cell wall synthesis, particularly affecting organisms that contain peptidoglycan in their cell wall. This inhibition causes bacterial lysis due to external osmotic pressure.</p><p>Let me know if you need any other edits or clarifications!</p>', 'active', '567_1742240104.jpg', 30, 12, 'rukai.mallick@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 00:35:04', '2025-05-06 09:33:10'),
(3162, 'Ornopain', 'Lornoxicam', 25.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '8 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the revised version of your text with the heading in normal bold text:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Ornopain is indicated in</strong></p><ul><li>Short-term relief of acute mild to moderate pain</li><li>Symptomatic relief of pain and inflammation in osteoarthritis</li><li>Symptomatic relief of pain and inflammation in rheumatoid arthritis.</li><li>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন*</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Lornoxicam is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) and antirheumatic drug of the oxicam class. It is intended for short-term treatment of acute mild to moderate pain and symptoms of rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis such as pain and inflammation of joints. Lornoxicam\'s mode of action is mainly related to the inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis (inhibition of the cyclooxygenase enzyme), leading to desensitization of peripheral nociceptors and consequently inhibition of inflammation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Acute Pain</strong>: 8-16 mg Lornoxicam daily, divided into 2 or 3 doses. Maximum recommended daily dose is 16 mg.</li><li><strong>Osteoarthritis and Rheumatoid Arthritis</strong>: Initial recommended dose is 12 mg Lornoxicam daily, divided into 2 or 3 doses. Maintenance dose should not exceed 16 mg Lornoxicam daily.</li><li><strong>Use in Children &amp; Adolescents</strong>: Lornoxicam is not recommended for use in children and adolescents below age 18 due to lack of data on safety and efficacy.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No special dosage modification is required for elderly patients above age 65, but Lornoxicam should be administered with caution as gastrointestinal adverse effects are less well tolerated in this group.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: For patients with mild to moderate renal impairment, the maximum recommended daily dose is 12 mg, divided into 2 or 3 doses.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: For patients with moderate hepatic impairment, the maximum recommended daily dose is 12 mg, divided into 2 or 3 doses. Undesirable effects may be minimized by using the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration necessary to control symptoms.</li><li>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন*</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> Concomitant administration of Lornoxicam and combination with Vitamin K antagonists like warfarin increases the risk of bleeding. Combination with Cyclosporin can lead to reduced kidney function and to acute kidney injury in rare cases. Lornoxicam can also increase the adverse effects of lithium, methotrexate, and digoxin and its derivatives. The effect of diuretics, ACE inhibitors, and angiotensin II receptor antagonists can be reduced in patients with heart failure.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to Lornoxicam or to any of the excipients, peptic ulcer, severe hepatic impairment, severe renal impairment (Serum creatinine &gt; 700 pmol/l), thrombocytopenia, and the third trimester of pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> The most commonly observed adverse events of NSAIDs are gastrointestinal in nature. Peptic ulcers, perforation, or GI bleeding, sometimes fatal, particularly in the elderly, may occur. Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, flatulence, constipation, dyspepsia, abdominal pain, melaena, hematemesis, ulcerative stomatitis, exacerbation of colitis, and Crohn’s disease have been reported following the administration of NSAIDs.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Lornoxicam is contraindicated in the third trimester of pregnancy and should not be used during pregnancy in the first and second trimesters and delivery, as no clinical data on exposed pregnancies are available. There are no data on the excretion of Lornoxicam in human breast milk. Lornoxicam is excreted in the milk of lactating rats in relatively high concentrations. Therefore, Lornoxicam should not be used in breastfeeding women.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Lornoxicam should be taken carefully when someone has impaired kidney function, a history of high blood pressure or heart failure, suffers from ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s diseases, has a history of bleeding tendency, has a history of asthma, or suffers from SLE (Systemic Lupus Erythematosus), a rare immunological disease, and women who are taking treatment for infertility.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p> At this time, there is no experience of overdose to permit definition of the consequences of an overdose or to suggest specific managements. However, it can be expected that after an overdose with Lornoxicam, the following symptoms may be observed: Nausea, vomiting, cerebral symptoms (dizziness, disturbances in vision). In the case of a real or suspected overdose, the medicinal product should be withdrawn. Due to its short half-life, Lornoxicam is rapidly excreted. Lornoxicam is not dialyzable. No specific antidote is known to date. The usual emergency measures, including gastric lavage, should be considered.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs).</p><p>Let me know if you\'d like any more changes or adjustments!</p>', 'active', '1068_1742240721.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 00:45:21', '2025-03-18 00:45:21'),
(3163, 'Teripen', 'Teriparatide', 9500.00, 1, 'x 2.24 ml pre-filled pen', '2.24 ml pre-filled pen', 'Injection', '20 mcg per 80 mcl', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Teripen 20 mcg/80 mcl Overview</h3><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Postmenopausal women with osteoporosis at high risk for fracture:</strong> Teripen is indicated to reduce the risk of fractures.</li><li><strong>Men with primary or hypogonadal osteoporosis at high risk for fracture:</strong> It helps in increasing bone mass in men.</li><li><strong>Osteoporosis associated with sustained systemic glucocorticoid therapy:</strong> Teripen is used in men and women at high risk of fractures due to glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class:</h3><ul><li><strong>Thyroid Drugs &amp; Hormones</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology:</h3><ul><li>Teripen is a <strong>parathyroid hormone analog</strong> (PTH 1-34), mimicking the action of the endogenous PTH in regulating calcium and phosphate metabolism in bones and kidneys.</li><li>It <strong>stimulates bone formation</strong> by promoting osteoblastic activity (bone-forming cells) over osteoclastic activity (bone-resorbing cells), which leads to an increase in bone mass and strength.</li><li>Teripen has <strong>anabolic</strong> effects on both trabecular (spongy) and cortical (dense) bone, stimulating new bone formation and improving microarchitecture.</li></ul><h3>Dosage:</h3><ul><li><strong>For all indications (postmenopausal women, men with primary/hypogonadal osteoporosis, and glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis):</strong></li><li>The recommended dose is <strong>20 mcg subcutaneously once a day</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Administration:</h3><ul><li>Teripen should be <strong>administered subcutaneously</strong> into the thigh or abdominal wall.</li><li>It should be visually inspected for any <strong>particulate matter</strong> or discoloration before use.</li><li>Patients and caregivers should be trained on how to use the delivery device properly.</li></ul><h3>Interactions:</h3><ul><li><strong>Digoxin:</strong> Teripen may transiently increase serum calcium levels, so caution is needed when used with digoxin due to the potential risk of digoxin toxicity.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide:</strong> No significant interaction with a 25 mg dose; higher doses have not been studied.</li><li><strong>Furosemide:</strong> May cause slight increases in serum calcium and urinary calcium but are not clinically significant.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications:</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity to Teripen or its excipients</strong> (e.g., angioedema or anaphylaxis).</li><li><strong>Patients with bone metastases, skeletal malignancies, metabolic bone diseases other than osteoporosis, or hypercalcemic disorders</strong> should not use Teripen.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects:</h3><ul><li><strong>Most common:</strong> Arthralgia (joint pain), pain, and nausea.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> No available data to assess the risk of birth defects or other pregnancy outcomes. Consider discontinuing Teripen if pregnancy occurs.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Unknown whether Teripen is excreted in breast milk, so caution is advised when breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</h3><ul><li><strong>Paget’s disease of bone, children/young adults with open epiphyses, or prior radiation to the skeleton:</strong> Should not be treated with Teripen.</li><li><strong>Long-term use:</strong> Use for more than <strong>2 years</strong> is not recommended.</li><li><strong>Hypercalcemia:</strong> Can increase serum calcium levels; use with caution in patients with a history of urolithiasis or kidney issues.</li><li><strong>Orthostatic hypotension:</strong> May occur with initial doses.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions:</h3><ul><li>Store the <strong>delivery device under refrigeration</strong> at 2-8°C. Do not freeze.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use:</strong> Safety and efficacy not established.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment:</strong> No studies available.</li></ul><p>If you need more specific details or have further questions on any of the sections, feel free to ask!</p>', 'active', '733_1742241463.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 00:57:43', '2025-05-04 22:21:58'),
(3164, 'Patalon Max', 'Olopatadine Hydrochloride', 230.00, 1, 'x 5 ml drop', '5 ml drop', 'Drop', '5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Patalon Max 0.7%</strong></p><p>Olopatadine is indicated for the treatment of ocular itching associated with allergic conjunctivitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Ophthalmic Non-Steroid Drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Olopatadine is an inhibitor of the release of histamine from the mast cell and a relatively selective histamine H1-antagonist. It inhibits the in vivo and in vitro type 1 immediate hypersensitivity reaction, including the inhibition of histamine-induced effects on human conjunctival epithelial cells. Olopatadine does not affect alpha-adrenergic, dopamine, or muscarinic type 1 and 2 receptors. Following topical ocular administration in humans, olopatadine was shown to have low systemic exposure.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Patalon Max 0.7%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>0.1% Sterile Eye Drops</strong>: One drop in each affected eye, two times per day, at an interval of 6 to 8 hours.</li><li><strong>0.2% Sterile Eye Drops</strong>: One drop in the affected eye, once a day.</li><li><strong>0.7% Sterile Eye Drops</strong>: One drop in each affected eye, once a day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Patalon Max 0.7%</strong></p><p>May result in additive CNS depression with CNS depressants.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Patalon Max 0.7% ophthalmic solution is contraindicated in persons with a known hypersensitivity to olopatadine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Patalon Max 0.7%</strong></p><ul><li>Headaches (incidence: 7%)</li><li>Asthenia, blurred vision, burning or stinging, cold syndrome, dry eye, foreign body sensation, hyperemia, hypersensitivity, keratitis, lid edema, nausea, pharyngitis, pruritus, rhinitis, sinusitis, and taste perversion (less than 5%)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Animal studies are not always predictive of human responses. This drug should be used in pregnant women only if the potential benefit to the mother justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li>It is not known whether topical ocular administration could result in sufficient systemic absorption to produce detectable quantities in human breast milk. Caution should be exercised when administering Patalon Max 0.7% ophthalmic solution to a nursing mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Olopatadine HCl ophthalmic solution should not be used to treat contact lens-related irritation.</li><li>Patients who wear soft contact lenses should wait at least 10 minutes after instilling Patalon Max 0.7% ophthalmic solution before inserting their contact lenses.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Patalon Max 0.7%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Drowsiness in adults, agitation and restlessness followed by drowsiness in children.</li><li><strong>Management</strong>: Symptomatic or supportive treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Do not touch the dropper tip to surfaces as this may contaminate the solution.</li><li>Do not use after 30 days of first opening.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No overall differences in safety or effectiveness have been observed between geriatric and younger patients.</li></ul>', 'active', '1410_1742241795.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 01:03:15', '2025-05-04 10:19:24'),
(3165, 'Sinaceph', 'Cephradine', 85.00, 1, 'x 1 gm vial', '1 gm vial', 'IV Injection', '1 gm/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sinaceph 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Sinaceph 1 gm/vial is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. These include:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper respiratory tract infections</strong>: sinusitis, pharyngitis, tonsillitis, laryngo-tracheo bronchitis, otitis media</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong>: bronchitis (acute and chronic), lobar pneumonia, bronchopneumonia</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: cystitis, urethritis, pyelonephritis</li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: abscess, cellulitis, furunculosis, impetigo</li></ul><p><strong>Microorganisms susceptible to Sinaceph 1 gm/vial:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gram-positive</strong>: Staphylococci (both penicillin-sensitive and resistant strains, penicillinase-producing species), Streptococci, Streptococcus pyogenes (beta-haemolytic), Streptococcus pneumoniae</li><li><strong>Gram-negative</strong>: Escherichia coli, Klebsiella spp., Proteus mirabilis, Haemophilus influenzae, Shigella spp., Salmonella spp. (including Salmonella typhi), Neisseria spp.</li></ul><p>Many strains of E. coli and Staphylococcus aureus that produce penicillinase (and thus are resistant to ampicillin) are still susceptible to Sinaceph 1 gm/vial, as it is unaffected by this enzyme.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>First-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Sinaceph 1 gm/vial is a cephalosporin antibiotic with broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against both Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. It works by interfering with the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall, inhibiting the transpeptidase enzyme. This weakens the cell wall, causing the cell to swell and rupture.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sinaceph 1 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Respiratory tract infections (other than lobar pneumonia) and skin/skin structure infections: 250 mg every 6 hours or 500 mg every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Lobar pneumonia: 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 gm every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated urinary tract infections: 500 mg every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">More serious urinary tract infections (including prostatitis): 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 gm every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Over nine months of age: 25 to 50 mg/kg/day, divided equally every 6 or 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For otitis media due to <em>H. influenzae</em>: 75 to 100 mg/kg/day, divided equally every 6 or 12 hours, not exceeding 4 gm per day.</li></ul><p><strong>For Oral Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Urinary tract infections: 500 mg four times daily or 1 gm twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Respiratory tract infections: 250 to 500 mg four times daily or 500 mg to 1 gm twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin and soft tissue infections: 250 to 500 mg four times daily or 500 mg to 1 gm twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Total daily dose: 25 to 50 mg/kg in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For otitis media: 75 to 100 mg/kg in divided doses every 6 or 12 hours (maximum daily dosage: 4 gm).</li></ul><p><strong>For Injectable Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 2-4 gm daily in four equally divided doses (up to 8 gm daily). For prophylaxis: a single preoperative dose of 1-2 gm, intramuscularly or intravenously.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 50-100 mg/kg daily in four equally divided doses, up to 300 mg/kg daily for severe infections.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><p>The concomitant use of nephrotoxic drugs like aminoglycosides with Sinaceph 1 gm/vial may increase the risk of kidney damage. Diuretics and probenecid may enhance the possibility of renal toxicity.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Sinaceph 1 gm/vial should not be used in patients with known or suspected hypersensitivity to cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sinaceph 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Common side effects are mostly related to gastrointestinal disturbances and hypersensitivity reactions. Rare side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Urticaria, skin rash, joint pain, edema</li><li><strong>Blood and lymphatic disorders</strong>: Thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, agranulocytosis, aplastic anemia</li><li><strong>Immune system</strong>: Anaphylaxis, serum sickness-like reactions</li><li><strong>Nervous system</strong>: Dizziness, confusion, sleep disturbances</li><li><strong>Renal</strong>: Reversible interstitial nephritis</li><li><strong>Hepatic</strong>: Liver enzyme disturbances, transient hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Sinaceph 1 gm/vial falls under <strong>US FDA Pregnancy Category B</strong>. While animal studies have not shown teratogenic effects, safety in pregnancy has not been fully established. It is excreted in breast milk, so it should be used with caution in nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged use may result in superinfection due to resistant organisms.</li><li>Sinaceph 1 gm/vial should be used with care in patients allergic to penicillins due to potential cross-sensitivity.</li><li>It may interfere with Coombs\' testing or cause false-positive urine glucose results with non-enzyme-based tests.</li><li><strong>Dosage adjustments</strong> are necessary for patients with renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>Symptoms include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and gastric upset. Treatment is generally supportive, but gastric lavage may be needed if a large amount has been ingested.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Reconstituted suspension</strong>: Use within 7 days at room temperature or 14 days if refrigerated.</li><li><strong>Injection solutions</strong>: Use within 2 hours at room temperature or 12 hours at 5°C. Do not use past the expiration date.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Sinaceph 1 gm/vial inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by interfering with the cross-linking process, leading to bacterial lysis due to external osmotic pressure.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Dosing adjustments for patients with reduced renal function or those on hemodialysis are required.</li><li><strong>For severe infections</strong>, higher doses of up to 1 gm four times daily may be required. Treatment should continue for at least 2-3 days after symptoms have resolved.</li></ul>', 'active', '1376_1742242269.jpg', 30, 12, 'rukai.mallick@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 01:11:09', '2025-05-06 09:33:43'),
(3166, 'SPLAC', 'Sodium Picosulfate', 85.00, 1, 'x 50 ml bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '5 mg per 5ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you provided, with the headings and important sections in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of SPLAC 50 ml</strong></p><p>SPLAC 50 ml is indicated in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Constipation of any etiology</li><li>Relief from prolonged &amp; recurrent constipation</li><li>Bowel clearance before surgery, childbirth, or radiological investigations</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Osmotic purgatives</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Sodium picosulfate is a stimulant laxative that, in conjunction with magnesium citrate, produces a purgative effect on stools. In a multicenter, observational study comprising patients undergoing colonoscopy, more than 93% of patients receiving SPLAC 50 ml-containing preparations reported the colon cleansing effect to be effective.</p><p>Sodium picosulfate is a contact laxative. It inhibits the absorption of water and electrolytes and increases their secretion into the intestinal lumen. It is hydrolyzed by colonic bacterial enzyme, sulfatase, to form an active metabolite bis-(p-hydroxy-phenyl)-pyridyl-2-methane (BHPM), which acts directly on the colonic mucosa to stimulate colonic peristalsis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of SPLAC 50 ml</strong></p><p>For oral administration. The following dosages are recommended to be taken at night to produce evacuation the following morning. It is recommended to start with the lowest dose. The dose may be adjusted up to the maximum recommended dose to produce regular stools. The maximum recommended daily dose should not be exceeded:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 10 years of age</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tablet: 5-10 mg per day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oral Solution: 5-10 ml or one to two teaspoonfuls per day</li><li><strong>Children aged 4-10 years</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tablet: 2.5-5 mg per day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oral Solution: 2.5-5 ml or Half to one teaspoonful per day</li><li><strong>Children under 4 years of age</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oral Solution: 0.25 ml/kg body weight per day</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of SPLAC 50 ml</strong></p><ul><li>The concomitant use of diuretics or adreno-corticosteroids may increase the risk of electrolyte imbalance if excessive doses are taken.</li><li>Concurrent administration of antibiotics may reduce the laxative action of this product.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Ileus or intestinal obstruction</li><li>Severe painful and/or feverish acute abdominal conditions (e.g. appendicitis) potentially associated with nausea and vomiting</li><li>Acute inflammatory bowel diseases</li><li>Severe dehydration</li><li>Known hypersensitivity to SPLAC 50 ml or any other component of the product</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of SPLAC 50 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity</li><li>Dizziness, syncope, vasovagal response</li><li>Gastrointestinal disorders (diarrhea, abdominal pain, and abdominal cramps)</li><li>Nausea, vomiting</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Long experience has shown no evidence of undesirable or damaging effects during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Clinical data show that neither the active moiety of sodium Picosulfate (BHPM or bis-(p hydroxyphenyl)-pyridyl-2-methane) nor its glucuronides are excreted into the milk of healthy lactating females.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged excessive use may lead to fluid and electrolyte imbalance and hypokalemia.</li><li>Harmful for those suffering from alcoholism.</li><li>To be taken into account in pregnant or breast-feeding women, children, and high-risk groups such as patients with liver disease or epilepsy.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of SPLAC 50 ml</strong></p><p>Laxatives, when taken in chronic overdosage, may cause chronic diarrhea, abdominal pain, hypokalemia, secondary hyperaldosteronism, and renal calculi. Renal tubular damage, metabolic alkalosis, and muscle weakness secondary to hypokalemia have also been described in association with chronic laxative abuse.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Osmotic purgatives</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>SPLAC 50 ml is a triarylmethane group derivative stimulant laxative. After oral administration, it is activated by the colonic bacteria and acts locally in the colon. The active form then stimulates the nerve endings of the intestinal wall and results in colonic peristalsis with promotion of accumulation of water and electrolytes in the colonic lumen. This results in stimulation of defecation, reduction of transit time, and softening of the stool. Stimulation of the rectum causes increased motility and a feeling of rectal fullness. The rectal effect may help to restore the \"call to stool.\"</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There are no reports of undesirable or damaging effects during pregnancy or to the fetus attributable to the use of this product. Use of the drug should be avoided during the first trimester. Clinical data show that neither the active moiety of sodium Picosulfate (BHPM or bis-(p hydroxyphenyl)-pyridyl-2-methane) nor its glucuronides are excreted into the milk of healthy lactating females.</p>', 'active', '1175_1742242607.webp', 9, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 01:16:47', '2025-05-04 21:22:33'),
(3167, 'Othera', 'Esomeprazole', 11.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Othera 20 mg</strong></p><p> Sodium Picosulfate is indicated in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Constipation of any etiology</li><li>Relief from prolonged &amp; recurrent constipation</li><li>Bowel clearance before surgery, childbirth, or radiological investigations</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Osmotic purgatives</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sodium Picosulfate is a stimulant laxative that, in conjunction with magnesium citrate, produces a purgative effect on stools. In a multicenter, observational study comprising of patients undergoing colonoscopy, more than 93% of the patients receiving sodium picosulfate-containing preparations reported the colon cleansing effect to be effective. Sodium picosulfate is a contact laxative. It inhibits the absorption of water and electrolytes, and increases their secretion into the intestinal lumen. It is hydrolyzed by the colonic bacterial enzyme sulfatase to form an active metabolite, bis-(p-hydroxy-phenyl)-pyridyl-2-methane (BHPM), which acts directly on the colonic mucosa to stimulate colonic peristalsis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Othera 20 mg</strong></p><p> For oral administration. The following dosages are recommended to be taken at night to produce evacuation the following morning. It is recommended to start with the lowest dose. The dose may be adjusted up to the maximum recommended dose to produce regular stools. The maximum recommended daily dose should not be exceeded:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 10 years of age</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tablet: 5-10 mg per day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oral Solution: 5-10 ml or one to two teaspoonfuls per day</li><li><strong>Children aged 4-10 years</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tablet: 2.5-5 mg per day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oral Solution: 2.5-5 ml or half to one teaspoonful per day</li><li><strong>Children under 4 years of age</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oral Solution: 0.25 ml/kg body weight per day</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Othera 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>The concomitant use of diuretics or adreno-corticosteroids may increase the risk of electrolyte imbalance if excessive doses are taken.</li><li>Concurrent administration of antibiotics may reduce the laxative action of this product.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Ileus or intestinal obstruction</li><li>Severe painful and/or feverish acute abdominal conditions (e.g., appendicitis) potentially associated with nausea and vomiting</li><li>Acute inflammatory bowel diseases</li><li>Severe dehydration</li><li>Known hypersensitivity to Sodium Picosulfate or any other component of the product</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Othera 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Syncope</li><li>Vasovagal response</li><li>Gastrointestinal disorders</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Abdominal pain and abdominal cramps</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Long experience has shown no evidence of undesirable or damaging effects during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Clinical data show that neither the active moiety of sodium Picosulfate (BHPM or bis-(p hydroxyphenyl)-pyridyl-2-methane) nor its glucuronides are excreted into the milk of healthy lactating females.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged excessive use may lead to fluid and electrolyte imbalance and hypokalemia.</li><li>Harmful for those suffering from alcoholism.</li><li>To be taken into account in pregnant or breastfeeding women, children, and high-risk groups such as patients with liver disease or epilepsy.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Othera 20 mg</strong></p><p> Laxatives when taken in chronic overdosage may cause chronic diarrhea, abdominal pain, hypokalemia, secondary hyperaldosteronism, and renal calculi. Renal tubular damage, metabolic alkalosis, and muscle weakness secondary to hypokalemia have also been described in association with chronic laxative abuse.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Osmotic purgatives</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Sodium Picosulfate is a triarylmethane group derivative stimulant laxative. After oral administration, it is activated by the colonic bacteria and acts locally in the colon. The active form then stimulates the nerve endings of the intestinal wall and results in colonic peristalsis with promotion of the accumulation of water and electrolytes in the colonic lumen. This results in the stimulation of defecation, reduction of transit time, and softening of the stool. Stimulation of the rectum causes increased motility and a feeling of rectal fullness. The rectal effect may help to restore the \"call to stool.\"</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> There are no reports of undesirable or damaging effects during pregnancy or to the fetus attributable to the use of this product. Use of the drug should be avoided during the first trimester. Clinical data show that neither the active moiety of sodium Picosulfate (BHPM or bis-(p hydroxyphenyl)-pyridyl-2-methane) nor its glucuronides are excreted into the milk of healthy lactating females.</p>', 'in-active', '1692_1742242879.jpg', 44, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 01:21:19', '2025-03-18 01:21:19'),
(3168, 'Sinaclox', 'Cloxacillin Sodium', 5.75, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sinaclox 500 mg</strong></p><p>Sinaclox 500 mg is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by Gram-positive organisms, including those caused by β-lactamase-producing Staphylococci, such as:</p><ul><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: Boils, abscesses, carbuncles, furunculosis, cellulitis, infected wounds, infected burns, otitis media and externa, protection of skin grafts, and skin infections like ulcers, eczema, acne, etc.</li><li><strong>Respiratory tract infections</strong>: Pneumonia, lung abscess, empyema, sinusitis, pharyngitis, tonsillitis.</li><li><strong>Other infections caused by sensitive organisms</strong>: Osteomyelitis, enteritis, endocarditis, urinary tract infections, meningitis, and septicaemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Penicillinase-resistant penicillins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sinaclox 500 mg works by inhibiting bacterial cell wall synthesis. It binds to one or more penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), which inhibits the final transpeptidation step of peptidoglycan synthesis in bacterial cell walls. This results in bacterial lysis due to the activity of cell wall autolytic enzymes (autolysins and murein hydrolases), while cell wall assembly is arrested.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sinaclox 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Upper Respiratory Tract Infection (Adult)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual Dose: 250 mg orally every 6 hours for 7 to 14 days, depending on the infection\'s nature and severity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum Dose: 4 g/day.</li><li><strong>Upper Respiratory Tract Infection (Pediatric)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The safety and efficacy in children &lt; 1 year have not been established.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">≥ 1 year to 18 years: 50 to 100 mg/kg/day, divided every 6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum Dose: 4 g/day.</li><li><strong>Pneumonia (Adult)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual Dose: 500 mg orally every 6 hours for up to 21 days, depending on infection severity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum Dose: 4 g/day.</li><li><strong>Pneumonia (Pediatric)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The safety and efficacy in children &lt; 1 year have not been established.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">≥ 1 year to 18 years: 50 to 100 mg/kg/day, divided every 6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum Dose: 4 g/day.</li><li><strong>Skin and Structure Infection (Adult)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual Dose: 500 mg orally every 6 hours for 7 days, or until 3 days after acute inflammation resolves, depending on infection severity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum Dose: 4 g/day.</li><li><strong>Skin and Structure Infection (Pediatric)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The safety and efficacy in children &lt; 1 year have not been established.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">≥ 1 year to 18 years: 50 to 100 mg/kg/day, divided every 6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum Dose: 4 g/day.</li><li><strong>Cystitis (Adult)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual Dose: 250 mg orally every 6 hours for 3 to 7 days, depending on infection severity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sinaclox 500 mg is rarely indicated for the treatment of cystitis.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum Dose: 4 g/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Sinaclox 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>May diminish the effect of BCG and typhoid vaccine.</li><li>May increase the risk of methotrexate toxicity.</li><li>May reduce the therapeutic effect of Na picosulfate.</li><li>May decrease serum concentrations of mycophenolate.</li><li>May prolong bleeding time with anticoagulants.</li><li>Increased serum concentrations with probenecid.</li><li>Tetracycline may antagonize the bactericidal effect of Sinaclox 500 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Sinaclox 500 mg should not be administered to patients who are hypersensitive to penicillin.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sinaclox 500 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Skin rash</li><li>Transient diarrhea</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Heartburn</li><li>Pruritus</li><li>Disturbance of blood electrolytes</li></ul><p>Rare side effects include anaphylactic shock.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Sinaclox 500 mg is classified under <strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>. This means animal reproduction studies have not demonstrated a fetal risk, but there are no controlled studies in pregnant women. Animal studies have shown an adverse effect (other than a decrease in fertility) that was not confirmed in controlled studies in women in the 1st trimester. No evidence of risk exists in later trimesters.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Sinaclox 500 mg should be administered with caution to patients with a known history of allergy.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dry Powder</strong>: Store below 25°C.</li><li><strong>Reconstituted Solution</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Store between 2-8°C (stable for 4 days).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Store at 23°C (stable for 24 hours).</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '638_1742243057.jpg', 30, 12, 'rukai.mallick@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 01:24:17', '2025-03-18 01:24:17');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3169, 'Humalog 200', 'Insulin Lispro', 1600.00, 1, 'x 3 ml KwikPen', '5 injection in a pack', 'Injection', '200 unit/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen</strong></p><p>Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen is an insulin analogue that is indicated in the treatment of patients with diabetes mellitus for the control of hyperglycemia. Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen has a more rapid onset and a shorter duration of action than human regular insulin. Therefore, in patients with type 1 diabetes, Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen should be used in regimens that include a longer-acting insulin. In patients with type 2 diabetes, Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen may be used without a longer-acting insulin when used in combination therapy with sulfonylurea agents.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Rapid Acting Insulin</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen (Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen, rDNA origin) is a human insulin analogue that is a rapid-acting, parenteral blood glucose-lowering agent. Chemically, it is Lys(B28), Pro(B29) human insulin analogue, created when the amino acids at positions 28 and 29 on the insulin B-chain are reversed. Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen is synthesized in a special non-pathogenic laboratory strain of <em>Escherichia coli</em> bacteria that has been genetically altered by the addition of the gene for Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen. The blood glucose-lowering effect of Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen is due to the facilitated uptake of glucose following binding of insulin to receptors on muscle and fat cells and the simultaneous inhibition of glucose output from the liver.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen</strong></p><p><strong>Adult:</strong></p><ul><li>SC dosing regimen should be individualized and adjusted based on the patient\'s glycaemic response.</li><li>Usual range is 0.5-1 unit/kg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus:</strong></p><ul><li>Approximately one third of the total daily insulin requirements should be rapid-acting or short-acting, premeal insulin.</li><li>Usual daily maintenance range: 0.5-1 unit/kg/day in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nonobese may require 0.4-0.6 unit/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Obese may require 0.8-1.2 units/kg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus:</strong></p><ul><li>If inadequately controlled with oral medication: 10 units/day SC (or 0.1 -0.2 unit/kg/day) of intermediate- or long-acting insulin given at bedtime is generally recommended.</li><li>As an alternative, rapid-acting formulations, such as Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen, given before meals have also been used. The dose must be adjusted carefully.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen</strong></p><p>Insulin requirements may be increased by medications with hyperglycemic activity such as corticosteroids, isoniazid, certain lipid-lowering drugs (e.g., niacin), estrogens, oral contraceptives, phenothiazines, and thyroid replacement therapy. Insulin requirements may be decreased in the presence of drugs that increase insulin sensitivity or have hypoglycemic activity, such as oral antidiabetic agents, salicylates, sulfa antibiotics, certain antidepressants (monoamine oxidase inhibitors), angiotensin-converting-enzyme inhibitors, Angiotensin II receptor blocking agents, beta-adrenergic blockers, inhibitors of pancreatic function (e.g., octreotide), and alcohol. Beta-adrenergic blockers may mask the symptoms of hypoglycemia in some patients.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen is contraindicated in patients hypersensitive to Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen or the other excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen</strong></p><ul><li>Clinical studies comparing Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen with Regular human insulin did not demonstrate a difference in the frequency of adverse events between the two treatments.</li><li>Adverse events commonly associated with human insulin therapy include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Body as a Whole</strong>: allergic reactions.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Skin and Appendages</strong>: injection site reaction, lipodystrophy, pruritus, rash.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Other</strong>: hypoglycemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: All pregnancies have a background risk of birth defects, loss, or other adverse outcomes regardless of drug exposure. This background risk is increased in pregnancies complicated by hyperglycemia and may be decreased with good metabolic control. It is essential for patients with diabetes or a history of gestational diabetes to maintain good metabolic control before conception and throughout pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Nursing Mothers</strong>: It is unknown whether Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen is administered to a nursing woman. The use of Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen is compatible with breastfeeding, but women with diabetes who are lactating may require adjustments of their insulin doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Renal or hepatic impairment; pregnancy, lactation; transferring from other insulin.</li><li>Monitor serum glucose, potassium, electrolytes, HbA1c, and lipid profile.</li><li>Concomitant illness, especially infections.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen</strong></p><p>Excess insulin administration may cause hypoglycemia and hypokalemia. Mild episodes of hypoglycemia usually can be treated with oral glucose. Adjustments in drug dosage, meal patterns, or exercise may be needed. More severe episodes with coma, seizure, or neurologic impairment may be treated with intramuscular/subcutaneous glucagon or concentrated intravenous glucose. Hypokalemia must be corrected appropriately.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at 2°C - 8°C in a refrigerator. Do not freeze.</li><li>In case of insulin for recent use, it need not be refrigerated. It should be kept in a cool place and kept away from heat and light.</li><li>The insulin in use can be kept under room temperature for up to one month.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen is approved for use in children for subcutaneous daily injections. Only the U-100 formulation of Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen is approved for use in children by continuous subcutaneous infusion in insulin pumps. Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen has not been studied in pediatric patients younger than 3 years of age or with type 2 diabetes.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Twelve percent of subjects in clinical studies were 65 years of age or older, with no significant differences in HbA1c values and hypoglycemia rates between age groups.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Patients with renal impairment may be at increased risk of hypoglycemia and may require more frequent dose adjustments and blood glucose monitoring.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Patients with hepatic impairment may also be at increased risk of hypoglycemia and require more frequent dose adjustments and monitoring.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Rapid Acting Insulin</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Humalog 200 3 ml KwikPen is a human insulin analogue with a rapid-acting, blood glucose-lowering effect. It facilitates glucose uptake in muscle and fat cells while inhibiting glucose production from the liver.</p>', 'active', '1536_1742243265.webp', 1, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 01:27:45', '2025-05-03 21:04:15'),
(3170, 'Sinaclox', 'Cloxacillin Sodium', 43.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '125 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sinaclox 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Sinaclox 125 mg/5 ml is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by Gram-positive organisms, including those caused by β-lactamase-producing Staphylococci, such as:</p><ul><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: Boils, abscesses, carbuncles, furunculosis, cellulitis, infected wounds, infected burns, otitis media and externa, protection of skin grafts, and skin infections like ulcers, eczema, acne, etc.</li><li><strong>Respiratory tract infections</strong>: Pneumonia, lung abscess, empyema, sinusitis, pharyngitis, tonsillitis.</li><li><strong>Other infections caused by sensitive organisms</strong>: Osteomyelitis, enteritis, endocarditis, urinary tract infections, meningitis, and septicaemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Penicillinase-resistant penicillins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Sinaclox 125 mg/5 ml inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to one or more penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs). This action inhibits the final transpeptidation step of peptidoglycan synthesis in bacterial cell walls. As a result, bacteria eventually lyse due to the ongoing activity of cell wall autolytic enzymes (autolysins and murein hydrolases), while cell wall assembly is arrested.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sinaclox 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Upper Respiratory Tract Infection (Adult)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual Dose: 250 mg orally every 6 hours for 7 to 14 days, depending on infection severity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum Dose: 4 g/day.</li><li><strong>Upper Respiratory Tract Infection (Pediatric)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Safety and efficacy in children &lt; 1 year have not been established.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">≥ 1 year to 18 years: 50 to 100 mg/kg/day, divided every 6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum Dose: 4 g/day.</li><li><strong>Pneumonia (Adult)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual Dose: 500 mg orally every 6 hours for up to 21 days, depending on infection severity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum Dose: 4 g/day.</li><li><strong>Pneumonia (Pediatric)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Safety and efficacy in children &lt; 1 year have not been established.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">≥ 1 year to 18 years: 50 to 100 mg/kg/day, divided every 6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum Dose: 4 g/day.</li><li><strong>Skin and Structure Infection (Adult)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual Dose: 500 mg orally every 6 hours for 7 days, or until 3 days after acute inflammation resolves, depending on infection severity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum Dose: 4 g/day.</li><li><strong>Skin and Structure Infection (Pediatric)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Safety and efficacy in children &lt; 1 year have not been established.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">≥ 1 year to 18 years: 50 to 100 mg/kg/day, divided every 6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum Dose: 4 g/day.</li><li><strong>Cystitis (Adult)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual Dose: 250 mg orally every 6 hours for 3 to 7 days, depending on infection severity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sinaclox 125 mg/5 ml is rarely indicated for the treatment of cystitis.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum Dose: 4 g/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Sinaclox 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>May diminish the effect of BCG and typhoid vaccine.</li><li>May increase the risk of methotrexate toxicity.</li><li>May reduce the therapeutic effect of Na picosulfate.</li><li>May decrease serum concentrations of mycophenolate.</li><li>May prolong bleeding time with anticoagulants.</li><li>Increased serum concentrations with probenecid.</li><li>Tetracycline may antagonize the bactericidal effect of Sinaclox 125 mg/5 ml.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Sinaclox 125 mg/5 ml should not be administered to patients known to be hypersensitive to penicillin.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sinaclox 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Skin rash</li><li>Transient diarrhea</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Heartburn</li><li>Pruritus</li><li>Disturbance of blood electrolytes</li></ul><p>Rare side effects include anaphylactic shock.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Sinaclox 125 mg/5 ml is classified under <strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>. Animal reproduction studies have not demonstrated a fetal risk, but there are no controlled studies in pregnant women. Some animal studies have shown an adverse effect (other than a decrease in fertility) that was not confirmed in controlled studies in women during the first trimester. There is no evidence of risk in later trimesters.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Sinaclox 125 mg/5 ml should be given with caution to patients with a known history of allergy.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dry Powder</strong>: Store below 25°C.</li><li><strong>Reconstituted Solution</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Store between 2-8°C (stable for 4 days).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Store at 23°C (stable for 24 hours).</li></ul>', 'active', '1930_1742243385.jpg', 30, 12, 'rukai.mallick@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 01:29:45', '2025-05-06 09:40:53'),
(3171, 'Orofit', 'Benzydamine + Chlorhexidine gluconate', 150.00, 1, 'x 120 ml bottle', '120 ml bottle', 'Solution', '120 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Orofit anti-inflammatory antiseptic solution can be used for the relief of pain and inflammation that may occur in conditions of the mouth and throat such as tonsillitis, sore throat, and mouth ulcers. This solution may also be used to relieve pain following mouth or throat surgery, or following dental procedures.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Benzydamine belongs to a family of medicines known as non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). They work to reduce pain and inflammation in the body by reducing the production of hormone-like substances that promote the inflammatory response. Benzydamine does not treat the underlying cause of the inflammation, however, and should not be used for extended periods of time without medical advice. Benzydamine also possesses local anaesthetic activity to further enhance pain relief.</p><p>Chlorhexidine is an antiseptic agent that kills or prevents the growth of a wide range of bacteria that may be involved in causing pain and inflammation in conditions of the mouth and throat. Chlorhexidine also helps to treat and prevent gingivitis and reduces the accumulation of dental plaque.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p>This Anti-inflammatory Antiseptic Solution may be used as either a gargle (for throat conditions) or a mouth rinse (for oral lesions such as mouth ulcers, or as a short-term alternative to oral hygiene procedures).</p><p>Can be used every 1.5-3 hours as needed (refer to packaging for dose instructions).</p><p>Best used undiluted, however, may be diluted with water if stinging or burning occurs.</p><p>Do not use for more than 7 days without medical or dental advice.</p><p>In the case of sore throats, if symptoms do not improve after a few days of treatment, consult your doctor as the condition may require treatment with antibiotics.</p><p>This Anti-inflammatory Antiseptic Solution should not be swallowed, but expelled from the mouth after each use.</p><p>Thoroughly brush teeth before use to minimise the risk of discolouring the teeth.</p>', 'active', '1222_1742243519.webp', 37, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 01:31:59', '2025-05-04 01:24:28'),
(3172, 'Xylistin', 'Colistimethate Sodium', 1000.00, 1, 'x 4.5 MIU vial', '4.5 MIU vial', 'IV Injection', '4.5 MIU (150 mg)', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xylistin 4.5 MIU (150 mg):</strong></p><p>Colistimethate is indicated for the treatment of acute or chronic infections caused by sensitive strains of certain gram-negative bacilli. It is particularly indicated in infections caused by sensitive strains of <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>. Colistimethate is not indicated for infections due to <em>Proteus</em> or <em>Neisseria</em>. It is very effective in treating infections due to the following gram-negative organisms:</p><ul><li><em>Enterobacter aerogenes</em></li><li><em>Escherichia coli</em></li><li><em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></li><li><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em></li></ul><p><strong>Composition:</strong></p><p>Each vial contains:</p><ul><li>Xylistin 4.5 MIU (150 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 MIU or 34 mg Colistin</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 MIU or 68 mg Colistin</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">3 MIU or 102 mg Colistin</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">4.5 MIU or 150 mg Colistin</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Other antibacterial preparation</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Colistimethate is a surface-active agent that penetrates and disrupts the bacterial cell membrane. It is polycationic and contains both hydrophobic and lipophilic moieties. Colistimethate interacts with the bacterial cytoplasmic membrane, altering its permeability, resulting in bactericidal activity. There is also evidence that polymyxins enter the cell and precipitate cytoplasmic components, primarily ribosomes.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Xylistin 4.5 MIU (150 mg):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Maintenance dose:</strong> 9 MIU/day in 2-3 divided doses.</li><li>In critically ill patients, a loading dose of 9 MIU should be administered.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal impairment:</strong>Creatinine clearance &lt;50 ml/min: daily dose 5.5-7.5 MIU</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Creatinine clearance &lt;30-10 ml/min: daily dose 4.5-5.5 MIU</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Creatinine clearance &lt;10 ml/min: daily dose 3.5 MIU</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Haemodialysis (HD) patients: No-HD days: 2.25 MIU/day; HD days: 3 MIU/day (administered after HD session).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric population:</strong>Children ≤40 kg: 75,000-150,000 IU/kg/day in 3 doses</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children &gt;40 kg: &gt;150,000 IU/kg/day in cystic fibrosis cases</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Xylistin 4.5 MIU (150 mg):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Direct Intermittent Administration:</strong> Inject one-half of the total daily dose over 3-5 minutes every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Continuous Infusion:</strong> Inject one-half of the total daily dose over 3-5 minutes. Add the remaining dose to infusion solutions like 0.9% NaCl, 5% dextrose in water, or Lactated Ringer\'s. Administer the second dose by slow infusion 1-2 hours after the initial dose. In impaired renal function, reduce infusion rate as necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xylistin 4.5 MIU (150 mg):</strong></p><p>Colistimethate should not be administered with certain antibiotics (e.g., aminoglycosides and polymyxin) or muscle relaxants (e.g., tubocurarine, succinylcholine). The concomitant use of Sodium Cephalothin should also be avoided.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in patients with a history of sensitivity to Colistimethate or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xylistin 4.5 MIU (150 mg):</strong></p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal upset</li><li>Tingling in extremities and tongue</li><li>Slurred speech, dizziness, vertigo, paresthesia</li><li>Generalized itching, rash, urticaria</li><li>Fever, elevated BUN and creatinine, decreased urine output</li><li>Respiratory distress, nephrotoxicity, apnea</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><p>Colistimethate may cross the placental barrier; it should be used during pregnancy only if the benefits outweigh the risks. It is unknown whether Colistimethate is excreted in breast milk, so caution is advised when administered to nursing women.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired renal function. Dose adjustments are necessary in the presence of renal impairment.</li><li>Use with caution in neonates, infants, and children.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xylistin 4.5 MIU (150 mg):</strong></p><p>Overdose can cause neuromuscular blockade with symptoms like paresthesia, lethargy, confusion, dizziness, ataxia, nystagmus, and speech disorders. Respiratory muscle paralysis may lead to apnea and respiratory arrest. Overdose may also cause acute renal failure. Discontinue therapy and use supportive measures in case of overdose.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Before reconstitution:</strong> Store below 30°C.</li><li><strong>After reconstitution:</strong> Store at 2°C to 8°C (Do not freeze) and use within 24 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution:</strong></p><ul><li>For IM injection: Reconstitute with 2 ml of water for injection.</li><li>For bolus injection: Reconstitute with 5 ml of water for injection.</li><li>For infusion: Dilute with 50-100 ml 0.9% NaCl.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><p>Other antibacterial preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Colistimethate disrupts the bacterial cell membrane, altering its permeability and causing bactericidal activity. It can also precipitate cytoplasmic components within the bacterial cell.</p>', 'active', '1168_1742244065.webp', 5, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 01:41:05', '2025-05-05 21:54:49'),
(3173, 'Cefopar', 'Cefoperazone Sodium', 400.00, 1, 'x 2 gm vial', '2 gm vial', 'IV Injection', '2 gm per vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the text with the necessary headings in <strong>bold</strong>:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Cefopar 2 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Cefoperazone is indicated for the treatment of the following infections when caused by susceptible organisms:</p><ul><li>Respiratory Tract Infections</li><li>Peritonitis &amp; Other Intra-abdominal Infections</li><li>Bacterial Septicemia</li><li>Skin and Skin Structures Infections</li><li>Pelvic Inflammatory Disease</li><li>Endometritis &amp; Other Infections of the Female Genital Tract</li><li>Urinary Tract Infections</li><li>Enterococcal Infections etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Cefopar 2 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Sterile Cefopar 2 gm/vial can be administered by IM or IV injection (following dilution).</p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: 2 to 4 grams per day administered in equally divided doses every 12 hours.</li><li>In severe infections or infections caused by less sensitive organisms, the total daily dose and/or frequency may be increased.</li><li>Patients have been successfully treated with a total daily dosage of 6-12 grams divided into 2, 3, or 4 administrations ranging from 1.5 to 4 grams per dose.</li><li>When treating infections caused by <strong>Streptococcus pyogenes</strong>, therapy should be continued for at least 10 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Cefoperazone is contraindicated in patients with known allergy to the Cephalosporin-class of antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Cefopar 2 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: As with all Cephalosporins, hypersensitivity manifested by skin reactions (1 patient in 45), drug fever (1 in 260), or a change in Coombs’ test (1 in 60) has been reported. These reactions are more likely to occur in patients with a history of allergies, particularly to Penicillin.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Cefoperazone is extensively excreted in bile. The serum half-life of Cefoperazone is increased 2-4 fold in patients with hepatic disease and/or biliary obstruction. In general, total daily dosage above 4 gm should not be necessary in such patients. If higher dosages are used, serum concentrations should be monitored.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Cefoperazone is to be stored in a dry place, below 25°C, and protected from light prior to reconstitution. The reconstituted solution may be stored for 24 hours if kept at room temperature (below 25°C).</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Cefoperazone is a bactericidal Cephalosporin antibiotic that is resistant to most beta-lactamases and active against a wide range of aerobic &amp; anaerobic, Gram-positive &amp; Gram-negative bacteria. The bactericidal action of Cefoperazone results from the inhibition of bacterial cell wall synthesis. Cefoperazone has a high degree of stability in the presence of beta-lactamases produced by most Gram-negative pathogens. Cefoperazone is usually active against organisms that are resistant to other beta-lactam antibiotics because of beta-lactamase production.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>. This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Only low concentrations of Cefoperazone are excreted in human milk. Although Cefoperazone passes poorly into the breast milk of nursing mothers, caution should be exercised when Cefoperazone is administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Safety and effectiveness in children have not been established.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><p>Reported clinical experience has not identified differences in responses between the elderly and younger patients. In general, dose selection for an elderly patient should be cautious, usually starting at the low end of the dosing range, reflecting the greater frequency of decreased hepatic, renal, or cardiac function, and of concomitant disease or other drug therapy.</p><p>Let me know if you need further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '902_1742244393.webp', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 01:46:33', '2025-05-03 01:17:47'),
(3174, 'Reluren', 'Relugolix + Estradiol + Norethindrone', 100.00, 5, 'tablet', '5 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg+1 mg+0.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Reluren 40 mg+1 mg+0.5 mg</strong></h3><p>Reluren is indicated for the management of heavy menstrual bleeding associated with uterine leiomyomas (fibroids) in premenopausal women.</p><h3><strong>Dosage of Reluren 40 mg+1 mg+0.5 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Exclusion of Pregnancy &amp; Hormonal Contraceptives</strong>: Ensure pregnancy is excluded and discontinue hormonal contraceptives prior to starting treatment.</li><li><strong>Administration</strong>: Take one tablet orally once daily.</li><li><strong>Missed Dose</strong>: If a dose is missed, take it as soon as possible the same day, and then resume regular dosing the next day at the usual time.</li><li><strong>Concomitant Use of Oral P-gp Inhibitors</strong>: If taking oral P-gp inhibitors, take Reluren at least 6 hours before the P-gp inhibitor.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness have not been established in pediatric patients.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction of Reluren 40 mg+1 mg+0.5 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>P-gp Inhibitors</strong>: Co-administration increases the AUC and Cmax of relugolix, possibly increasing the risk of adverse reactions.</li><li><strong>P-gp and Strong CYP3A Inducers</strong>: Co-administration may decrease the AUC and Cmax of relugolix, estradiol, and/or norethindrone, reducing the therapeutic effects.</li><li><strong>Grapefruit Juice</strong>: May increase plasma concentrations of drugs that are CYP450 3A4 substrates.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>High risk of arterial, venous thrombotic or thromboembolic disorders.</li><li>Pregnancy.</li><li>Known osteoporosis.</li><li>History of breast cancer or other hormone-sensitive malignancies.</li><li>Known hepatic impairment or disease.</li><li>Undiagnosed abnormal uterine bleeding.</li><li>Known hypersensitivity to relugolix, estradiol, or norethindrone acetate.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Reluren 40 mg+1 mg+0.5 mg</strong></h3><p>Most common adverse reactions (incidence &gt;3%):</p><ul><li>Hot flushes</li><li>Hyperhidrosis or night sweats</li><li>Uterine bleeding</li><li>Alopecia</li><li>Decreased libido</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Thromboembolic Disorders</strong>: Discontinue if an arterial or venous thrombotic, cardiovascular, or cerebrovascular event occurs.</li><li><strong>Bone Loss</strong>: May cause decreases in Bone Mineral Density (BMD) that might not be reversible.</li><li><strong>Mood Changes</strong>: Seek medical attention for new or worsening depression, anxiety, or mood changes.</li><li><strong>Risk of Early Pregnancy Loss</strong>: Can cause early pregnancy loss; effective non-hormonal contraception is recommended.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Reluren 40 mg+1 mg+0.5 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Nausea, vomiting, breast tenderness, abdominal pain, drowsiness, fatigue, and withdrawal bleeding.</li><li><strong>Management</strong>: Supportive care is recommended. The removal of relugolix, estradiol, or norethindrone by hemodialysis is unknown.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry place, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Gonadotropin-Releasing Hormone (GnRH) Antagonist</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Reluren is a combination of:</p><ul><li><strong>Relugolix</strong>: A GnRH receptor antagonist that reduces the release of LH and FSH, leading to decreased ovarian sex hormones (estradiol and progesterone) and reduced bleeding associated with uterine fibroids.</li><li><strong>Estradiol (E2)</strong>: Reduces bone resorption and bone loss due to decreased estrogen from relugolix.</li><li><strong>Norethindrone Acetate (NETA)</strong>: Protects the uterus from adverse endometrial effects of unopposed estrogen.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Risk of Early Pregnancy Loss</strong>: Discontinue if pregnancy occurs during treatment.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: There is no data on the presence of relugolix or its metabolites in human milk, but it has been detected in lactating rats.</li></ul>', 'active', '1458_1742244674.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 01:51:14', '2025-05-04 15:09:03'),
(3175, 'Genib', 'Gefitinib', 250.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Genib 250 mg</strong></p><p>Genib 250 mg is a tyrosine kinase inhibitor indicated for the first-line treatment of patients with metastatic non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) whose tumors have epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) exon 19 deletions or exon 21 (L858R) substitution mutations as detected by an FDA-approved test.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Targeted Cancer Therapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Genib 250 mg inhibits the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) tyrosine kinase by binding to the adenosine triphosphate (ATP)-binding site of the enzyme. This inhibits the EGFR tyrosine kinase\'s function in activating the Ras signal transduction cascade, resulting in the inhibition of malignant cells. Genib 250 mg is the first selective inhibitor of the EGFR tyrosine kinase, also known as Her1 or ErbB-1. EGFR is overexpressed in the cells of certain human carcinomas, such as lung and breast cancers. Overexpression leads to inappropriate activation of the Ras signal transduction cascade, resulting in uncontrolled cell proliferation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Genib 250 mg</strong></p><p>The recommended dose of Genib 250 mg is 250 mg orally once daily with or without food, until disease progression or unacceptable toxicity occurs. Do not take a missed dose within 12 hours of the next dose.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Genib 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant use with CYP3A4 inducers (e.g., rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine, barbiturates) may reduce serum Genib 250 mg levels.</li><li>Plasma concentrations may increase with potent CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., itraconazole, ketoconazole).</li><li>May increase INR or bleeding events with warfarin.</li><li>May increase plasma levels of metoprolol.</li><li>May exacerbate vinorelbine-induced neutropenia.</li><li>Decreased plasma levels and potential reduction in efficacy with drugs that affect gastric pH (e.g., PPIs, H2-receptor antagonists).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity</li><li>Lactation</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Genib 250 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Pruritus, rash, angioedema, urticaria, epistaxis, haematuria</li><li>Alopecia, dry mouth and skin, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, stomatitis</li><li>Diarrhea, nail disorders, asthenia, pyrexia, proteinuria</li><li>Eye pain, corneal erosion or ulcer, aberrant eyelash growth, and elevations in blood creatinine</li></ul><p>Rare side effects include:</p><ul><li>Pancreatitis, erythema multiforme, toxic epidermal necrolysis</li><li>Corneal membrane sloughing, ocular ischemia, ocular hemorrhage</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy category D: There is positive evidence of human fetal risk, but the benefits from use in pregnant women may be acceptable in life-threatening situations or when safer drugs cannot be used or are ineffective.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Interstitial Lung Disease (ILD)</strong>: ILD occurred in patients taking Genib 250 mg. It should be withheld for worsening respiratory symptoms and discontinued if ILD is confirmed.</li><li><strong>Hepatotoxicity</strong>: Periodic liver function testing should be performed. Withhold Genib 250 mg for Grade 2 or higher ALT/AST elevations. Discontinue in case of severe hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal Perforation</strong>: Discontinue Genib 250 mg in case of gastrointestinal perforation.</li><li><strong>Diarrhea</strong>: Withhold Genib 250 mg for Grade 3 or higher diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Ocular Disorders including Keratitis</strong>: Withhold for severe or worsening ocular disorders, and discontinue for persistent ulcerative keratitis.</li><li><strong>Bullous and Exfoliative Skin Disorders</strong>: Withhold Genib 250 mg for Grade 3 or higher skin reactions or exfoliative conditions.</li><li><strong>Embryo-Fetal Toxicity</strong>: Genib 250 mg can cause fetal harm. Effective contraception should be used, and the potential risk to a fetus should be advised.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store between 20-25°C.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: The safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No significant differences in safety were observed between patients 65 years and older and those younger than 65. There is insufficient data to assess differences in efficacy between older and younger patients.</li><li><strong>Administration to Patients with Difficulty Swallowing Solids</strong>: Immerse Genib 250 mg tablets in 4 to 8 ounces of water for approximately 15 minutes, then drink the liquid or administer through a naso-gastric tube. Rinse the container with 4 to 8 ounces of water and immediately drink or administer the solution through the tube.</li></ul>', 'active', '371_1742244957.webp', 1, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 01:55:57', '2025-05-03 18:21:05'),
(3176, 'Renocare', 'Gohkshura', 8.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?sca_esv=539099fe38e3365b&amp;sxsrf=AHTn8zqOnNNyPv0gX31peJx2pIkaTY0gig:1742245026854&amp;q=Description&amp;spell=1&amp;sa=X&amp;ved=2ahUKEwjzyb-_gJKMAxXmxTgGHV4dLgoQBSgAegQIDRAB\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: var(--JKqx2);\"><strong><em>Description</em></strong></a></p>', 'active', '1433_1742245199.jpg', 5, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 01:59:59', '2025-05-04 15:10:11'),
(3177, 'Linax-E', 'Empagliflozin + Linagliptin', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg+5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Linax-E 10 mg + 5 mg</strong></p><p>This combination of empagliflozin (an SGLT2 inhibitor) and linagliptin (a DPP-4 inhibitor) is indicated as an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycemic control in adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus. Empagliflozin is also indicated to reduce the risk of cardiovascular death in adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus and established cardiovascular disease.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Linax-E 10 mg + 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial Dose</strong>: The recommended dose is 10 mg empagliflozin and 5 mg linagliptin once daily, taken in the morning, with or without food.</li><li><strong>Dose Adjustment</strong>: The dose may be increased to 25 mg empagliflozin and 5 mg linagliptin once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established in pediatric patients.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Patients</strong>: Elderly patients have a higher incidence of adverse reactions related to volume depletion and reduced renal function.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Higher incidence of adverse reactions related to reduced renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Linax-E 10 mg + 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>The most common side effects include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Urinary tract infections</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Stuffy or runny nose and sore throat</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Upper respiratory tract infections</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pancreatitis</strong>: Discontinue immediately if acute pancreatitis, including fatal cases, is suspected.</li><li><strong>Ketoacidosis</strong>: Monitor for signs of metabolic acidosis or ketoacidosis, regardless of blood glucose levels, and discontinue if suspected.</li><li><strong>Volume Depletion</strong>: Evaluate volume status and renal function before starting therapy, particularly in patients with impaired renal function, elderly patients, or those on loop diuretics.</li><li><strong>Urosepsis and Pyelonephritis</strong>: Prompt treatment of urinary tract infections is necessary.</li><li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong>: Adjust insulin secretagogue or insulin doses to reduce hypoglycemia risk when starting this therapy.</li><li><strong>Necrotizing Fasciitis of the Perineum (Fournier\'s Gangrene)</strong>: This serious, life-threatening condition can occur. Discontinue therapy if suspected and initiate treatment immediately.</li><li><strong>Genital Mycotic Infections</strong>: Monitor and treat as needed.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity Reactions</strong>: Includes anaphylaxis, angioedema, and skin conditions; discontinue if hypersensitivity occurs.</li><li><strong>Arthralgia</strong>: Severe joint pain may result from DPP-4 inhibitors; discontinue therapy if necessary.</li><li><strong>Bullous Pemphigoid</strong>: Blisters or erosions may indicate this condition, requiring discontinuation of the medication.</li><li><strong>Heart Failure</strong>: Use with caution in patients with risk factors for heart failure. Monitor for symptoms.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Empagliflozin</strong>: Inhibits the SGLT2 transporter, reducing renal glucose reabsorption and increasing urinary glucose excretion.</li><li><strong>Linagliptin</strong>: Inhibits the DPP-4 enzyme, increasing active incretin hormones (GLP-1 and GIP). This enhances insulin secretion in a glucose-dependent manner and lowers glucagon levels, supporting better glucose homeostasis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This medication should be avoided during pregnancy, especially in the second and third trimesters, due to potential risks to the fetus. It is not recommended for use while breastfeeding.</p>', 'active', '53_1742245377.webp', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 02:02:57', '2025-05-03 23:30:01'),
(3178, 'Bobcare', 'Rowghan Zeenat', 300.00, 1, 'x 20 gm tube', '20 gm tube', 'Ointment', '20 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Description</p>', 'active', '67_1742245619.png', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 02:06:59', '2025-05-03 00:59:27'),
(3179, 'Linax-E', 'Empagliflozin + Linagliptin', 30.00, 10, 'tablet', '10', 'Tablet', '25 mg+5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Linax-E 25 mg+5 mg</strong></p><p>Linax-E is a combination of <strong>empagliflozin</strong> (a sodium-glucose co-transporter 2 [SGLT2] inhibitor) and <strong>linagliptin</strong> (a dipeptidyl peptidase-4 [DPP-4] inhibitor). It is indicated as an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycemic control in adults with <strong>type 2 diabetes mellitus</strong>. Empagliflozin is also indicated to reduce the risk of <strong>cardiovascular death</strong> in adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus and established cardiovascular disease.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Linax-E 25 mg+5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Function Assessment</strong>: Assess renal function before initiating and periodically thereafter as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Recommended Dose</strong>: 10 mg empagliflozin and 5 mg linagliptin once daily in the morning, with or without food. The dose may be increased to 25 mg empagliflozin and 5 mg linagliptin once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established in pediatric patients.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Patients</strong>: Increased risk of adverse reactions related to volume depletion and reduced renal function.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Higher risk of adverse reactions related to reduced renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Linax-E 25 mg+5 mg</strong></p><p>The most common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Urinary tract infections</li><li>Stuffy or runny nose</li><li>Sore throat</li><li>Upper respiratory tract infections</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pancreatitis</strong>: Discontinue immediately if pancreatitis is suspected, as there have been reports of acute pancreatitis, including fatal cases.</li><li><strong>Ketoacidosis</strong>: Monitor for ketoacidosis, even with normal blood glucose levels. Discontinue therapy and treat promptly if ketoacidosis is suspected.</li><li><strong>Volume Depletion</strong>: Assess volume status and renal function before initiating therapy, especially in patients with impaired renal function, the elderly, or those on loop diuretics.</li><li><strong>Urosepsis and Pyelonephritis</strong>: Evaluate for signs of urinary tract infections and treat promptly.</li><li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong>: Consider lowering the dose of insulin or insulin secretagogues when starting this medication to reduce the risk of hypoglycemia.</li><li><strong>Necrotizing Fasciitis of the Perineum (Fournier\'s Gangrene)</strong>: Discontinue therapy and seek immediate treatment if suspected.</li><li><strong>Genital Mycotic Infections</strong>: Monitor and treat as appropriate.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity Reactions</strong>: Discontinue the tablet if any severe allergic reactions (e.g., anaphylaxis, angioedema) occur.</li><li><strong>Arthralgia</strong>: Discontinue the tablet if severe joint pain occurs.</li><li><strong>Bullous Pemphigoid</strong>: If blisters or erosions appear, discontinue the tablet.</li><li><strong>Heart Failure</strong>: Exercise caution in patients with a history or risk factors for heart failure. Monitor for symptoms.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Empagliflozin</strong>: Inhibits SGLT2, reducing the renal reabsorption of glucose, leading to increased urinary glucose excretion.</li><li><strong>Linagliptin</strong>: Inhibits DPP-4, increasing active incretin hormone levels (GLP-1 and GIP), stimulating insulin release in a glucose-dependent manner, and reducing glucagon levels, thus helping regulate glucose homeostasis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Advise women of the potential risks to a fetus, particularly in the second and third trimesters. This medication is not recommended for use during breastfeeding.</p>', 'active', '823_1742245803.webp', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 02:10:03', '2025-05-04 18:42:15'),
(3180, 'Emoli', 'Liquid Paraffin + Benzalkonium Chloride + Chlorhexidine + Isopropyl Myristate', 240.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Lotion', '2.5%+0.1%+0.1%+2.5%', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Description</p>', 'active', '1787_1742246301.webp', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 02:18:21', '2025-05-03 10:06:16');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3181, 'Renvista', 'Roxadustat', 200.00, 3, 'tablet', '3 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Renvista 50 mg</h3><p>Renvista 50 mg is indicated for the treatment of adult patients with <strong>symptomatic anemia</strong> associated with <strong>chronic kidney disease (CKD)</strong>.</p><p><br></p><h3>Dosage of Renvista 50 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Frequency</strong>: Take Renvista 50 mg <strong>orally three times per week</strong>, but <strong>not on consecutive days</strong>.</li><li><strong>Target Hemoglobin (Hb) Levels</strong>: Maintain Hb levels between <strong>10 to 12 g/dL</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Patients not on erythropoiesis-stimulating agent treatment</strong>:</p><ul><li>For adults, the usual starting dose is <strong>50 mg</strong> three times weekly.</li><li>For patients weighing:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Less than 100 kg: <strong>70 mg</strong> three times per week.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">100 kg and over: <strong>100 mg</strong> three times per week.</li></ul><p><strong>Patients switching from erythropoiesis-stimulating agents</strong>:</p><ul><li>The usual starting dose is <strong>70 or 100 mg</strong> three times weekly. Adjust dosage as needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Dose Adjustment</strong>:</p><ul><li>Increase or decrease dosage as per the \"Dose increase/decrease table\" and \"Stepwise Dose Adjustment Sequence\".</li><li>Maintain the adjusted dose for at least <strong>4 weeks</strong>.</li><li>If Hb increases too rapidly (&gt;2.0 g/dL in 4 weeks), reduce the dose or suspend treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Missed Dose</strong>:</p><ul><li>If there is ≥ 24 hours until the next dose, take the missed dose immediately.</li><li>If &lt;24 hours remain, skip the missed dose and continue the regular schedule. <strong>Do not take 2 doses on the same day</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Method of Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li>Take <strong>orally with or without food</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Renvista 50 mg</h3><ol><li><strong>With Phosphate Binders and Multivalent Cations</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Risk: Reduced Renvista 50 mg absorption.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommendation: Take Renvista at least <strong>1 hour after</strong> phosphate binders or products containing multivalent cations.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>With Gemfibrozil (CYP2C8 and OATP1B1 inhibitor) or Probenecid (UGT and OAT1/OAT3 inhibitor)</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Risk: Increased Renvista AUC and Cmax.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommendation: Adjust the dose based on Hb monitoring.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>With OATP1B1 or BCRP Substrates (e.g., Simvastatin, Rosuvastatin, Atorvastatin)</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Risk: Increased AUC and Cmax.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommendation: Adjust the dose based on Hb monitoring.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Renvista 50 mg</h3><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong></li><li><strong>Vascular access thrombosis</strong></li><li><strong>Diarrhea</strong></li><li><strong>Peripheral edema</strong></li><li><strong>Hyperkalemia</strong></li><li><strong>Nausea</strong></li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Thrombotic Vascular Events (TVEs)</strong>: Use with caution in patients with <strong>pre-existing risk factors</strong> (e.g., obesity, history of TVEs).</li><li><strong>Seizure History</strong>: Use cautiously in patients with a history of seizures.</li><li><strong>Infections</strong>: Do not administer if there are serious signs and symptoms of infection.</li><li><strong>Liver Disorder</strong>: Not recommended for patients with liver dysfunction.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Not to be administered to <strong>pregnant women</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Renvista 50 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Increased heart rate and higher-than-required hemoglobin levels.</li><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: Dose reduction or interruption as needed. The drug is <strong>not removed by dialysis</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool (below 30°C)</strong>, <strong>dry place</strong>, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li><strong>Drugs for Hemolytic Hypoplastic &amp; Renal Anemia</strong></li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Renvista 50 mg is a <strong>HIF (Hypoxia-Inducible Factor) prolyl hydroxylase inhibitor</strong>. It:</p><ul><li><strong>Prevents HIF breakdown</strong>, leading to increased HIF activity.</li><li><strong>Stimulates endogenous erythropoietin production</strong>, boosting erythropoiesis.</li><li>Reduces <strong>hepcidin</strong> levels, improving iron availability.</li><li>Increases <strong>hemoglobin (Hb) levels</strong> and enhances <strong>iron metabolism</strong> in CKD patients.</li></ul><p>Renvista has been shown to increase RBC count while keeping EPO levels within a <strong>normal physiological range</strong> without the need for <strong>intravenous iron</strong> supplementation.</p><p><br></p><h3>Pregnancy and Breastfeeding</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: <strong>Contraindicated</strong> during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: <strong>Contraindicated</strong> during breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3>Pediatric Use</h3><ul><li><strong>Not indicated for children</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Liver Dysfunction</h3><ul><li>Not recommended for use in patients with <strong>severe hepatic impairment</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1208_1742246518.jpg', 29, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 02:21:58', '2025-03-18 02:21:58'),
(3182, 'Freezy', 'Methyl Salicylate + Menthol + Camphor', 80.00, 6, 'x 6 nasal inhaler stick', '6 nasal inhaler stick', 'Inhaler', '(122.70 mg+415.40 mg+415.40 mg) per gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p>It seems like you\'re requesting to format the headings in the text to normal bold. Here\'s your text with the headings bolded:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Freezy (122.70 mg+415.40 mg+415.40 mg)/gm</strong></p><p>This cream is indicated for the fast relief of minor aches and pains of muscles and joints e.g., Simple Backache, Arthritis, Strains, Bruises, and Sprains.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Topical anti-inflammatory preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This cream is a specially formulated Methyl salicylate, Menthol, and Camphor cream. It penetrates into the skin to provide fast relief from pain and stiffness of minor arthritis and muscle aches. It alleviates pain and inflammation by inhibiting the synthesis of prostaglandins that occur in inflamed tissues. Methyl salicylate has been shown that first-pass metabolism exists in the skin and rapidly hydrolyzes the salicylate ester to release the active salicylate in both epidermis and dermis. Menthol increases the penetration of drugs when applied on the skin to give a faster onset of action. It dilates the blood vessels causing a sensation of coldness followed by an analgesic effect. Camphor is a stimulant, used topically to increase local blood flow and as a \'counterirritant,\' which reduces pain &amp; swelling. When in combination with other ingredients like menthol and methyl salicylate, it becomes ideal for neuralgia and other painful areas.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Freezy (122.70 mg+415.40 mg+415.40 mg)/gm</strong></p><p>Adults and children (12 years of age and older): Apply to the affected area not more than 3 to 4 times daily.</p><p>Children under 12 years of age: Use on advice of a physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Freezy (122.70 mg+415.40 mg+415.40 mg)/gm</strong></p><p>There are no known drug interactions and none well documented.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to salicylate or any of its ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Freezy (122.70 mg+415.40 mg+415.40 mg)/gm</strong></p><p>Redness or irritation may occur, especially in persons with sensitive skin.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This medication should be used only if clearly needed during pregnancy or while breast-feeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>For external use only. Do not use on wounds or damaged skin with a heating pad, on a child under 12 years of age, or with arthritis-like conditions.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Topical anti-inflammatory preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This cream is a specially formulated Methyl salicylate, Menthol, and Camphor cream. It penetrates into the skin to provide fast relief from pain and stiffness of minor arthritis and muscle aches. It alleviates pain and inflammation by inhibiting the synthesis of prostaglandins that occur in inflamed tissues. Methyl salicylate has been shown that first-pass metabolism exists in the skin and rapidly hydrolyzes the salicylate ester to release the active salicylate in both epidermis and dermis. Menthol increases the penetration of drugs when applied on the skin to give a faster onset of action. It dilates the blood vessels causing a sensation of coldness followed by an analgesic effect. Camphor is a stimulant, used topically to increase local blood flow and as a \'counterirritant,\' which reduces pain &amp; swelling. When in combination with other ingredients like menthol and methyl salicylate, it becomes ideal for neuralgia and other painful areas.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This medication should be used only if clearly needed during pregnancy or while breast-feeding.</p><p>Let me know if you need any further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '549_1742246854.webp', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 02:27:34', '2025-05-03 10:53:54'),
(3183, 'Nabumet', 'Nabumetone', 30.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '1000 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nabumet 1000 mg</strong></p><p> Nabumet 1000 mg is indicated for relief of signs and symptoms of osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis. Carefully consider the potential benefits and risks of Nabumet 1000 mg and other treatment options before deciding to use Nabumet 1000 mg. Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration consistent with individual patient treatment goals.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Nabumet 1000 mg is a naphthylalkanone. It is a non-selective prostaglandin G/H synthase (a.k.a. cyclooxygenase or COX) inhibitor that acts on both prostaglandin G/H synthase 1 and 2 (COX-1 and -2). Prostaglandin G/H synthase catalyzes the conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandin G2 and prostaglandin G2 to prostaglandin H2. Prostaglandin H2 is the precursor to a number of prostaglandins involved in fever, pain, swelling, inflammation, and platelet aggregation. The parent compound is a prodrug that undergoes hepatic biotransformation to the active compound, <strong>6-methoxy-2-naphthylacetic acid (6MNA)</strong>. The analgesic, antipyretic, and anti-inflammatory effects of NSAIDs occur as a result of decreased prostaglandin synthesis. The parent compound is a prodrug, which undergoes hepatic biotransformation to 6MNA, a potent inhibitor of prostaglandin synthesis, likely through binding to the COX-2 and COX-1 receptors.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Nabumet 1000 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Osteoarthritis and Rheumatoid Arthritis:</strong> The recommended starting dose is <strong>1,000 mg</strong> taken as a single dose with or without food. Some patients may obtain more symptomatic relief from <strong>1,500 mg to 2,000 mg</strong> per day. Nabumet 1000 mg can be given in either a single or twice-daily dose. Dosages greater than 2,000 mg per day have not been studied. The lowest effective dose should be used for chronic treatment.</li><li><strong>Patients weighing under 50 kg</strong> may be less likely to require dosages beyond 1,000 mg. After observing the response to initial therapy, the dose should be adjusted to meet individual patient requirements.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Nabumet 1000 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Nabumet 1000 mg 500 mg &amp; 750 mg Tablet:</strong> Oral</li><li><strong>Nabumet 1000 mg 1000 mg Tablet:</strong> Mix 1 tablet thoroughly in a glass (250 ml) of pure water and drink it completely.</li><li><strong>Moderate or Severe Renal Insufficiency:</strong> Caution should be taken in prescribing Nabumet 1000 mg to patients with moderate or severe renal insufficiency. The maximum starting doses of Nabumet 1000 mg in patients with moderate or severe renal insufficiency should not exceed <strong>750 mg</strong> or <strong>500 mg</strong>, respectively, once daily. After careful monitoring of renal function, daily doses may be increased to a maximum of <strong>1,500 mg</strong> and <strong>1,000 mg</strong>, respectively.</li><li><strong>Use in Pediatric Patients:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Nabumet 1000 mg</strong></p><p> Reports suggest that NSAIDs may diminish the antihypertensive effect of ACE-inhibitors. This interaction should be considered in patients taking NSAIDs concomitantly with ACE-inhibitors.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Nabumet 1000 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Nabumet 1000 mg or its excipients. Nabumet 1000 mg should not be given to patients who have experienced asthma, urticaria, or allergic-type reactions after taking aspirin or other NSAIDs. Severe, rarely fatal, anaphylactic-like reactions to NSAIDs have been reported in such patients. Nabumet 1000 mg is contraindicated for the treatment of peri-operative pain in the setting of coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Nabumet 1000 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain, constipation, flatulence, nausea, positive stool guaiac, dry mouth, gastritis, stomatitis, vomiting.</li><li><strong>Central Nervous System:</strong> Dizziness, headache, fatigue, increased sweating, insomnia, nervousness, somnolence.</li><li><strong>Dermatologic:</strong> Pruritus, rash.</li><li><strong>Special Senses:</strong> Tinnitus.</li><li><strong>Miscellaneous:</strong> Edema.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C:</strong> Reproductive studies conducted in rats and rabbits have not demonstrated evidence of developmental abnormalities. However, animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response. There are no adequate, well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Nabumet 1000 mg should be used in pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Nursing Mothers:</strong> It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk; however, 6MNA is excreted in the milk of lactating rats. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk and because of the potential for serious adverse reactions in nursing infants from Nabumet 1000 mg, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or to discontinue the drug, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</li><li><strong>Labor and Delivery:</strong> In rat studies with NSAIDs, as with other drugs known to inhibit prostaglandin synthesis, an increased incidence of dystocia, delayed parturition, and decreased pup survival occurred. The effects of Nabumet 1000 mg on labor and delivery in pregnant women are unknown.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> NSAIDs, including Nabumet 1000 mg, have been associated with renal papillary necrosis and other abnormal renal pathology during long-term administration to animals. They can also cause a dose-dependent decrease in prostaglandin synthesis, which can lead to a reduction in renal blood flow, especially in patients with impaired renal function, heart failure, liver dysfunction, those taking diuretics, and the elderly.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Nabumet 1000 mg</strong></p><p> Symptoms following acute NSAIDs overdoses are usually limited to lethargy, drowsiness, nausea, vomiting, and epigastric pain, which is generally reversible with supportive care. Gastrointestinal bleeding can occur. Rarely, hypertension, acute renal failure, respiratory depression, and coma may occur. Anaphylactoid reactions have been reported with therapeutic ingestion of NSAIDs and may occur following an overdose. Management includes symptomatic and supportive care.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, away from light and moisture. Keep all medicines out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> As with any NSAIDs, caution should be exercised in treating the elderly (65 years and older).</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Nabumet 1000 mg is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) that exhibits anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic properties. The exact mechanism is not known, but it likely involves the inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis. The parent compound is a prodrug that undergoes hepatic biotransformation to the active component, <strong>6-methoxy-2-naphthylacetic acid (6MNA)</strong>, which is a potent inhibitor of prostaglandin synthesis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> <strong>Pregnancy Category C.</strong> There are no adequate, well-controlled studies in pregnant women. This drug should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed. Due to its potential effects on the fetal cardiovascular system (closure of ductus arteriosus), Nabumet 1000 mg is not recommended during the third trimester of pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Lactation</strong></p><p> Nabumet 1000 mg is not recommended for use in nursing mothers due to potential adverse effects on neonates from prostaglandin-synthesis-inhibiting drugs.</p>', 'active', '814_1742247220.webp', 53, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 02:33:40', '2025-03-18 02:33:40'),
(3184, 'Luzinta kids', 'Lutein + Zeaxanthin', 30.00, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg plus 2 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Luzinta Kids 10 mg + 2 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Age-related macular degeneration (AMD)</li><li>Cataracts</li><li>Retinitis pigmentosa</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Herbal and Nutraceuticals</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Lutein and Zeaxanthin are nutritionally important carotenoids that protect healthy cells from oxidative stress and free radical damage. These carotenoids have immunostimulant and photoprotectant properties. Recent research has shown that Lutein and Zeaxanthin are major eye pigments of the retina, particularly concentrated in rods, which are specialized light-sensitive cells responsible for black and white vision in the dark. They play a key role in protecting the retina from high-energy blue spectrum light. Free radicals and harmful light are believed to contribute to age-related macular degeneration (AMD), cataracts, and retinitis pigmentosa. Lutein and Zeaxanthin may also benefit skin health and exhibit anti-aging properties.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Luzinta Kids 10 mg + 2 mg</strong></p><ul><li>1 capsule daily or as advised by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Luzinta Kids 10 mg + 2 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Certain drugs, such as proton-pump inhibitors and lipid-lowering drugs, may decrease the absorption of Lutein and Zeaxanthin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>No toxicities have been reported in the scientific literature for Lutein and Zeaxanthin at doses up to 40 mg for 2 months.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Luzinta Kids 10 mg + 2 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No adverse side effects have been reported. Ames testing has demonstrated an absence of any mutagenic effects for purified Lutein and Zeaxanthin.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Studies on Lutein and Zeaxanthin in pregnant and nursing women have not been conducted.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '790_1742247506.jpg', 17, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 02:38:26', '2025-03-18 02:38:26'),
(3185, 'Mygest', 'Progesterone Micronized', 15.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Description</p>', 'active', '1353_1742247651.jpg', 9, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 02:40:51', '2025-03-18 02:40:51'),
(3186, 'Voricare', 'Voriconazole', 50.00, 50, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Voricare 200 mg</strong></p><p>Voricare 200 mg is an azole antifungal medication recommended for treating the following fungal infections in individuals aged 12 years and older:</p><ul><li>Invasive Aspergillosis</li><li>Disseminated candidiasis and candidemia (nonneutropenic) in the skin, abdomen, kidney, bladder wall, and wounds</li><li>Esophageal candidiasis</li><li>Severe infections caused by Fusarium species, including <em>Fusarium solani</em></li><li>Infections caused by <em>Scedosporium apiospermum</em></li><li>For patients who are resistant to or intolerant of alternative treatments.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Other Antifungal preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> The primary mechanism of action of Voricare 200 mg involves the suppression of 14 alpha-lanosterol demethylation. This process, mediated by fungal cytochrome P450, is crucial in the production of fungal ergosterol. The accumulation of 14 alpha-methyl sterols and the subsequent loss of ergosterol from the fungal cell membrane contribute to its antifungal properties. Voricare 200 mg is more selective for fungal cytochrome P450 enzymes compared to those found in mammals.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Voricare 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Oral:Body weight ≥ 40 kg:</strong>Loading dose: 400 mg or 10 ml every 12 hours (for the first 24 hours)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose (after 24 hours): 200 mg or 5 ml twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Body weight &lt; 40 kg:</strong>Loading dose: 200 mg or 5 ml every 12 hours (for the first 24 hours)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose (after 24 hours): 100 mg or 2.5 ml twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Injection (for invasive infections):Invasive Aspergillosis:</strong>Loading dose: 6 mg/kg every 12 hours for the first 24 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 4 mg/kg every 12 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Candidemia and deep tissue Candida infections:</strong>Loading dose: 6 mg/kg every 12 hours for the first 24 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 3-4 mg/kg every 12 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Scedosporiosis and Fusariosis:</strong>Loading dose: 6 mg/kg every 12 hours for the first 24 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 4 mg/kg every 12 hours</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Voricare 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>It should be taken at least one hour before or one hour after a meal.</li><li>The duration of treatment should be as short as possible, depending on clinical and mycological response.</li><li>Long-term use (&gt;180 days) should be carefully considered in terms of risks and benefits.</li><li>For prophylaxis (e.g., post-transplant), it can be used for up to 100 days, with careful consideration of the patient\'s risk factors.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Voricare 200 mg interacts with CYP3A4, CYP2C9, and CYP2C19 inhibitors and inducers. Dosage adjustments are required for drugs that are substrates of these enzymes.</li><li>The dosage of Voricare 200 mg may need to be increased when used with phenytoin or efavirenz.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Voricare 200 mg or any of its components.</li><li>Co-administration with certain drugs (sirolimus, terfenadine, astemizole, cisapride, pimozide, quinidine, rifampin, carbamazepine, efavirenz, etc.) is contraindicated due to risk of severe interactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Voricare 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects: Abdominal pain, anemia, impaired vision, headaches, chest pain, nausea, diarrhea.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> No sufficient controlled studies in pregnant women. It should only be used if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> There is no data on the excretion of Voricare 200 mg into breast milk. Breastfeeding should be discontinued after starting treatment with Voricare 200 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Long-term exposure (over 180 days) requires careful monitoring, as there have been reports of squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) of the skin.</li><li>Monitor liver function closely, especially in patients with liver impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Voricare 200 mg</strong></p><p> There is no specific data available regarding an overdose of Voricare 200 mg.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Store in a dry area, away from light, below 25°C.</li><li>Store the powder for suspension between 2°C and 8°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Older Adults:</strong> No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics:</strong> Safety and efficacy have not been established in children under 12 years of age.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> No impact on the pharmacokinetics of Voricare 200 mg in individuals with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> For patients with mild to severe hepatic cirrhosis, reduce the maintenance dose by half. Use with caution in severe hepatic impairment due to the potential for liver toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Other Antifungal preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Voricare 200 mg is a triazole antifungal. It inhibits the enzyme 14-alpha sterol demethylase, preventing the synthesis of ergosterol, a key component of fungal cell membranes, thereby leading to cell membrane dysfunction and antifungal effects.</p>', 'active', '665_1742247934.jpg', 33, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 02:45:34', '2025-03-18 02:45:34'),
(3187, 'Tems A', 'Amlodipine Besilate + Telmisartan', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+80 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Tems A 5 mg + 80 mg</strong></p><p>Tems A 5 mg + 80 mg is indicated for the treatment of hypertension, either alone or in combination with other antihypertensive agents. It may also be used as an initial therapy for patients who are likely to need multiple drugs to achieve their blood pressure goals.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Tems A 5 mg + 80 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial Therapy:</strong> This tablet may be started if it is unlikely that blood pressure control can be achieved with a single agent. The usual starting dose is 40/5 mg once daily. For patients requiring greater reductions in blood pressure, the starting dose may be 80/5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Add-on Therapy:</strong> For patients not adequately controlled with amlodipine (or another dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker) or telmisartan (or another angiotensin receptor blocker) alone, this combination can be used. Patients on 10 mg amlodipine who experience adverse effects like edema may switch to the 40/5 mg tablet once daily, reducing the amlodipine dose without compromising the overall antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>Replacement Therapy:</strong> Patients on separate amlodipine and telmisartan tablets may switch to this combined tablet with the same doses, taken once daily. The dosage should be individualized and can be increased after at least two weeks. The maximum recommended dose is 80/10 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Tems A 5 mg + 80 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Telmisartan does not cause clinically significant interactions with acetaminophen, amlodipine, glyburide, simvastatin, hydrochlorothiazide, warfarin, or ibuprofen.</li><li>Telmisartan is not metabolized by the cytochrome P450 system and does not significantly affect cytochrome P450 enzymes, except for minor inhibition of CYP2C19.</li><li>Amlodipine has been safely used with various medications, including thiazide diuretics, beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors, nitrates, digoxin, warfarin, NSAIDs, antibiotics, and oral hypoglycemics.</li><li>There are no significant effects on the pharmacokinetics of amlodipine from drugs like cimetidine, grapefruit juice, and sildenafil.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to this product or any of its components.</li><li>Pregnancy and lactation.</li><li>Biliary obstructive disorders, severe hepatic impairment, hypotension, cardiogenic shock, and left ventricle outflow tract obstruction.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Tems A 5 mg + 80 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects: dizziness, peripheral edema, migraine, headache, paraesthesia, vertigo, bradycardia, palpitations, hypotension, cough, abdominal pain, diarrhea, nausea, pruritus, myalgia, spasm, erectile dysfunction, chest pain, fatigue, and edema.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Fetal or Neonatal Exposure:</strong> Avoid during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Hypotension:</strong> Correct any volume or salt depletion before starting therapy. Monitor for symptoms of hypotension.</li><li><strong>Titration:</strong> Titrate slowly in patients with hepatic or severe renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Heart Failure:</strong> Monitor for worsening symptoms.</li><li><strong>ACE Inhibitor and ARB Use:</strong> Avoid concomitant use of an ACE inhibitor and angiotensin receptor blocker.</li><li><strong>Myocardial Infarction:</strong> In patients with severe coronary artery disease, initiating a calcium channel blocker (CCB) may precipitate myocardial infarction or angina.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Tems A 5 mg + 80 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Telmisartan Overdose:</strong> Symptoms may include hypotension, dizziness, and tachycardia. If symptomatic hypotension occurs, supportive treatment should be initiated. Telmisartan is not removed by hemodialysis.</li><li><strong>Amlodipine Overdose:</strong> May cause excessive peripheral vasodilation, marked hypotension, and possible reflex tachycardia. In case of massive overdose, active cardiac and respiratory monitoring is necessary. If hypotension persists, vasopressors may be administered. Amlodipine is not removed by hemodialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protect from light and humidity. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Combined antihypertensive preparations.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Telmisartan:</strong> A non-peptide angiotensin receptor blocker (ARB) that selectively blocks the binding of angiotensin II to the AT1 receptor, leading to vasodilation and reduced blood pressure.</li><li><strong>Amlodipine:</strong> A dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker (CCB) that inhibits calcium ion influx into vascular smooth muscle and cardiac muscle, leading to peripheral arterial vasodilation and reduced blood pressure.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Category C in the first trimester and D in the second and third trimesters. Not known whether telmisartan and amlodipine are excreted in human milk. Due to potential risks to the infant, nursing should be discontinued or the drug should be stopped after considering its importance to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Initial therapy with this combination is not recommended for patients ≥75 years old.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong></p><ul><li>Initial therapy with this combination is not recommended for patients with hepatic impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '672_1742248229.webp', 26, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 02:50:29', '2025-04-16 20:47:12'),
(3188, 'NuvaCare', 'White Soft Paraffin + Light Liquid Paraffin + Lanolin Anhydrous', 220.00, 1, 'x 50 gm tube', '50 gm tube', 'Cream', '14.5% + 12.6% + 1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1093_1742248569.webp', 58, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 02:56:09', '2025-05-04 01:04:22'),
(3189, 'Osify', 'Ossein Hydroyapatite', 9.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1162_1742248676.jpg', 37, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 02:57:56', '2025-03-18 02:57:56'),
(3190, 'Renesis', 'Roxadustat', 800.00, 3, 'tablet', '3 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '150 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Renesis 150 gm</strong></p><p> Renesis 150 gm is indicated for the treatment of adult patients with symptomatic anemia associated with chronic kidney disease (CKD).</p><p><strong>Dosage of Renesis 150 gm</strong></p><p> The appropriate dose of Renesis 150 gm must be taken orally three times per week and not on consecutive days. The dose should be individualized to achieve and maintain target Hb levels of 10 to 12 g/dL as described below:</p><ul><li><strong>Patients not on erythropoiesis-stimulating agent treatment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For adults, the usual starting dose is 50 mg three times weekly.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The recommended starting dose of Renesis 150 gm is 70 mg three times per week in patients weighing less than 100 kg and 100 mg three times per week in patients weighing 100 kg and over.</li><li><strong>Patients switching from erythropoiesis-stimulating agents</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For adults, the usual starting dose is 70 or 100 mg three times weekly.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage thereafter should be adjusted according to the patient\'s condition.</li></ul><p><strong>Dose Adjustment</strong></p><p> When dose adjustments are required, increase or decrease the dose according to the \"Dose increase/decrease table\" and \"Stepwise Dose adjustment sequence\" below. Once adjusted, maintain the dose level for ≥4 weeks.</p><p> If the hemoglobin concentration increases rapidly (&gt;2.0 g/dL) within 4 weeks of a dose increase, decrease the dose or suspend the treatment immediately.</p><p><strong>Stepwise Dose Adjustment Sequence</strong>:</p><ul><li>20 mg - 40 mg - 50 mg - 70 mg - 100 mg - 150 mg - 200 mg - 250 mg - 300 mg - 400 mg (only for CKD patients on dialysis).</li></ul><p><strong>Missed Dose</strong></p><ul><li>When there is ≥ 24-hour interval until the next scheduled dosing time, take the missed dose immediately and follow the prescribed schedule for subsequent doses.</li><li>If there is &lt;24 hours until the next scheduled dosing time, skip the missed dose, and take the next dose as scheduled. Do not take 2 doses on the same day.</li></ul><p><strong>Method of Administration</strong></p><p> Renesis 150 gm tablets are to be taken orally with or without food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Renesis 150 gm</strong></p><p> Renesis 150 gm in combination with other medications may have drug-drug interactions.</p><ul><li><strong>Renesis 150 gm with Phosphate binders and other products containing multivalent cations (EXCEPT lanthanum carbonate)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Risk: Decreased Renesis 150 gm AUC by 67% and 46% and Cmax by 66% and 52%.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommendation: Renesis 150 gm should be taken at least 1 hour after administration of phosphate binders or other medicinal products or supplements containing multivalent cations.</li><li><strong>Renesis 150 gm with gemfibrozil (CYP2C8 and OATP1B1 inhibitor) or probenecid (UGT and OAT1/OAT3 inhibitor)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Risk: Increased Renesis 150 gm AUC by 2.3-fold and Cmax by 1.4-fold.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommendation: Adjust the dose of Renesis 150 gm following dose adjustment rules based on Hb monitoring.</li><li><strong>Renesis 150 gm with OATP1B1 or BCRP Substrates (simvastatin, rosuvastatin, &amp; atorvastatin)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Risk: Increased AUC and Cmax.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommendation: Adjust the dose of Renesis 150 gm following dose adjustment rules based on Hb monitoring.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Renesis 150 gm</strong></p><p> Common adverse reactions associated with Renesis 150 gm include:</p><ul><li>Hypertension</li><li>Vascular access thrombosis</li><li>Diarrhoea</li><li>Peripheral oedema</li><li>Hyperkalaemia</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Renesis 150 gm tablets should be used with caution as it may initiate thrombotic vascular events (TVEs), particularly in patients with pre-existing risk factors such as obesity and prior history of TVEs.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with a history of seizures.</li><li>Do not administer if the patient has serious signs and symptoms of infection.</li><li>Not recommended for use in patients with liver disorders.</li><li>Not to be initiated in pregnant women.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Renesis 150 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: When this drug was administered at 5 mg/kg (510 mg) to a single healthy adult, transient increased heart rate was reported. Overdosage is likely to increase hemoglobin concentration more than necessary.</li><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: Appropriate measures such as dose reduction or interruption should be taken. This drug is not removed by dialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool (below 30°C), dry place, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Hemolytic Hypoplastic &amp; Renal Anemia</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Renesis 150 gm is an orally bioavailable, hypoxia-inducible factor prolyl hydroxylase inhibitor (HIF-PHI) with potential anti-anemic activity. Upon administration, Renesis 150 gm binds to and inhibits HIF-PH, an enzyme responsible for the degradation of transcription factors in the HIF family under normal oxygen conditions. This prevents HIF breakdown and promotes HIF activity, leading to increased endogenous erythropoietin production and enhanced erythropoiesis. It also reduces the expression of hepcidin, improves iron availability, and boosts hemoglobin (Hb) levels. HIF regulates the expression of genes involved in erythropoiesis and iron metabolism.</p><p>Renesis 150 gm has been shown to induce coordinated erythropoiesis, increasing red blood cell count while maintaining plasma erythropoietin levels near normal in CKD patients, without the need for supplemental intravenous iron. It also improves iron metabolism by increasing serum transferrin and intestinal iron absorption, and releases stored iron in a dose-dependent manner.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Do not administer to women that may be pregnant or are pregnant. Renesis 150 gm is contraindicated during breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><p> Renesis 150 gm is not indicated for use in children.</p><p><strong>Liver Dysfunction</strong></p><p> Renesis 150 gm is not recommended for use in patients with severe hepatic impairment.</p>', 'active', '748_1742248856.jpg', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 03:00:56', '2025-03-18 03:00:56'),
(3191, 'Xalcort', 'Deflazacort', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '18 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xalcort 18 mg</strong></p><p> Xalcort 18 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li><strong>Anaphylaxis</strong></li><li><strong>Asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Severe hypersensitivity reactions</strong></li><li><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis</strong></li><li><strong>Juvenile chronic arthritis</strong></li><li><strong>Polymyalgia rheumatica</strong></li><li><strong>Systemic lupus erythematosus</strong></li><li><strong>Dermatomyositis</strong></li><li><strong>Mixed connective tissue disease</strong> (excluding systemic sclerosis)</li><li><strong>Polyarthritis nodosa</strong></li><li><strong>Sarcoidosis</strong></li><li><strong>Pemphigus</strong></li><li><strong>Bullous pemphigoid</strong></li><li><strong>Pyoderma gangrenosum</strong></li><li><strong>Minimal change nephrotic syndrome</strong></li><li><strong>Acute interstitial nephritis</strong></li><li><strong>Rheumatic carditis</strong></li><li><strong>Ulcerative colitis</strong></li><li><strong>Crohn\'s disease</strong></li><li><strong>Uveitis</strong></li><li><strong>Optic neuritis</strong></li><li><strong>Autoimmune hemolytic anemia</strong></li><li><strong>Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura</strong></li><li><strong>Acute and lymphatic leukemia</strong></li><li><strong>Malignant lymphoma</strong></li><li><strong>Multiple myeloma</strong></li><li><strong>Immune suppression in transplantation</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p> Glucocorticoids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p> Xalcort 18 mg provides anti-inflammatory action by inhibiting the Phospholipase A2 enzyme, which is responsible for prostaglandin synthesis. Additionally, it decreases the release of certain chemicals important in the immune system, thus providing immunosuppressive effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xalcort 18 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For acute disorders: Initial dose up to 120 mg/day may be necessary. Maintenance doses are typically in the range of 3-18 mg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis:</strong> Maintenance dose usually falls within 3-18 mg/day. The smallest effective dose should be used, adjusting as needed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Bronchial asthma:</strong> For acute attacks, high doses (48-72 mg/day) may be required, reducing gradually once the attack is controlled. Maintenance doses should be titrated to the lowest effective dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Other conditions:</strong> Doses are adjusted based on clinical need, titrated to the lowest effective maintenance dose. Initial dose may be calculated based on a 5 mg prednisone to 6 mg Xalcort 18 mg ratio.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Doses typically range from 0.25-1.5 mg/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Juvenile chronic arthritis:</strong> Maintenance dose ranges from 0.25-1.0 mg/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Nephrotic syndrome:</strong> Initial dose around 1.5 mg/kg/day, followed by gradual down titration.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Bronchial asthma:</strong> Initial dose on alternate days ranges between 0.25-1.0 mg/kg.</li></ul><p><strong>Xalcort 18 mg withdrawal:</strong></p><p> For patients receiving more than physiological doses for longer than 3 weeks, gradual withdrawal is essential to prevent relapse.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Xalcort 18 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Liver enzyme inducers</strong> (e.g., rifampicin, carbamazepine, phenytoin) may necessitate an increased maintenance dose.</li><li><strong>Liver enzyme inhibitors</strong> (e.g., ketoconazole) may require dose reduction.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Xalcort 18 mg or any of its ingredients</li><li>Patients receiving live virus immunization</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xalcort 18 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>GI disturbances</li><li>Musculoskeletal issues</li><li>Endocrine disturbances</li><li>Neuropsychiatric effects</li><li>Ophthalmic problems</li><li>Fluid and electrolyte disturbances</li><li>Increased susceptibility to infection</li><li>Impaired healing</li><li>Hypersensitivity reactions</li><li>Skin issues (atrophy, striae, acne)</li><li>Myocardial rupture post-MI</li><li>Thromboembolic disorders</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Xalcort 18 mg crosses the placenta and may increase the risk of intra-uterine growth retardation when administered for prolonged periods. Should only be used if the benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Xalcort 18 mg is excreted in breast milk. Doses up to 50 mg/day are unlikely to affect the infant systemically. Higher doses may lead to adrenal suppression in the infant, but the benefits of breastfeeding usually outweigh the risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><p> Special caution and frequent monitoring are required in patients with the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Cardiac disease or congestive heart failure</li><li>Hypertension</li><li>Thromboembolic disorders</li><li>Gastric issues (e.g., gastritis, ulcerative colitis)</li><li>Diabetes mellitus or family history</li><li>Osteoporosis</li><li>Epilepsy</li><li>Liver failure</li><li>Hypothyroidism and cirrhosis</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xalcort 18 mg:</strong></p><p> Abrupt withdrawal of corticosteroids, especially after long-term use, can cause serious complications. Dose reduction should be done gradually.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 25°C in a cool, dry place, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> No specific precautions other than those normally taken for glucocorticoid therapy.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment:</strong> Blood levels of Xalcort 18 mg may increase, requiring careful dose adjustment.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> No special precautions necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><p> Glucocorticoids</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p> Inhibits Phospholipase A2, reducing the synthesis of prostaglandins and offering anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive effects.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy (Revisited):</strong></p><p> Xalcort 18 mg crosses the placenta, and prolonged or repeated use during pregnancy may increase the risk of intrauterine growth retardation. It should only be prescribed if the benefits to the mother and fetus outweigh the risks.</p><p><strong>Nursing Mothers:</strong></p><p> Xalcort 18 mg is excreted in breast milk. Doses up to 50 mg daily are unlikely to cause systemic effects in the infant. Higher doses may lead to adrenal suppression in the infant.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hepatic impairment:</strong> Monitor carefully for increased blood levels.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> No special precautions necessary.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Similar precautions as for adults receiving glucocorticoid therapy. Common side effects may have more severe consequences in older patients.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '184_1742249060.jpg', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 03:04:20', '2025-03-18 03:04:20'),
(3192, 'Herbisol', 'Sharbat Atfal', 90.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '200 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Description</p>', 'active', '915_1742249268.png', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 03:07:48', '2025-05-03 19:00:04');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3193, 'Bevicort', 'Formoterol Fumarate + Glycopyrrolate + Budesonide', 1200.00, 1, 'x 120 metered sprays', '120 metered sprays', 'Inhaler', '(5.5 mcg+10.4 mcg+182 mcg) per actuation', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you provided, with headings formatted in bold for better clarity:</p><p><strong>Indications of Bevicort (5.5 mcg+10.4 mcg+182 mcg)/actuation</strong></p><p>This inhaler is indicated for the maintenance treatment of patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). This inhaler is not indicated for the relief of acute bronchospasm or for the treatment of asthma.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Bevicort (5.5 mcg+10.4 mcg+182 mcg)/actuation</strong></p><p>The recommended dosage is two inhalations twice daily, once in the morning and again in the evening, by oral inhalation. Do not take more than two inhalations twice daily. After inhalation, rinse mouth with water without swallowing.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Bevicort (5.5 mcg+10.4 mcg+182 mcg)/actuation</strong></p><ul><li>Strong cytochrome P450 3A4 inhibitors (e.g., ritonavir): Use with caution. May cause systemic corticosteroid effects.</li><li>Other adrenergic drugs may potentiate the effect: Use with caution.</li><li>Diuretics, xanthine derivatives, or steroids may potentiate hypokalemia or ECG changes. Use with caution.</li><li>Monoamine oxidase inhibitors and tricyclic antidepressants: Use with extreme caution. May potentiate the effect of formoterol fumarate on the cardiovascular system.</li><li>Beta-blockers: Use with caution. May block bronchodilatory effects of beta-agonists and produce severe bronchospasm.</li><li>Anticholinergics: May interact additively with concomitantly used anticholinergic medications. Avoid administration of this spray with other anticholinergic-containing drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>This inhaler is contraindicated in patients who have demonstrated hypersensitivity to budesonide, glycopyrrolate, formoterol, or any of the excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Bevicort (5.5 mcg+10.4 mcg+182 mcg)/actuation</strong></p><ul><li>Serious asthma-related events (hospitalizations, intubations, death)</li><li>Candida albicans infection</li><li>Increased risk of pneumonia</li><li>Immunosuppression and risk of infections</li><li>Hypercorticism and adrenal suppression</li><li>Paradoxical bronchospasm</li><li>Hypersensitivity reactions including anaphylaxis</li><li>Cardiovascular effects</li><li>Reduction in bone mineral density</li><li>Worsening of narrow-angle glaucoma and cataracts</li><li>Worsening of urinary retention</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>This inhaler is not indicated for the treatment of asthma or acute bronchospasm.</li><li>It has not been studied in patients with acutely deteriorating COPD and should not be used for rescue therapy.</li><li>Patients who use this inhaler should be prescribed a short-acting beta-agonist for symptomatic relief of acute symptoms.</li><li>The daily dosage should not exceed the recommended dose.</li><li>Overuse or combining with other LABA medications can lead to clinically significant cardiovascular effects.</li><li>This inhaler can cause localized Candida infections, and appropriate antifungal therapy should be used if such infections occur.</li><li>Particular care should be taken when transitioning patients from systemic corticosteroids to ICS.</li><li>Potential for paradoxical bronchospasm or severe allergic reactions, including angioedema.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Bevicort (5.5 mcg+10.4 mcg+182 mcg)/actuation</strong></p><p>No cases of overdose have been reported. However, the risks associated with overdose for the individual components are:</p><ul><li><strong>Budesonide</strong>: If used at excessive doses for prolonged periods, systemic corticosteroid effects like hypercorticism may occur.</li><li><strong>Glycopyrrolate</strong>: High doses may cause anticholinergic symptoms, such as nausea, dizziness, blurred vision, or increased intraocular pressure.</li><li><strong>Formoterol Fumarate</strong>: An overdose may lead to typical beta2-agonist effects like seizures, hypertension, tachycardia, arrhythmias, tremors, and potentially fatal outcomes.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Pressurized canister, do not puncture, break, or incinerate, even when empty, as it may explode.</li><li>Avoid exposure to direct sunlight or heat.</li><li>Clean the inhaler regularly as per the instructions.</li><li>Do not store above 30°C.</li><li>Keep in a dry place, protect from light, and keep out of the reach of children.</li><li>Keep away from eyes.</li><li>Discard within three months after removing from the foil pouch.</li><li>Shake well before use.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Combined bronchodilators</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This inhaler delivers a combination of micronized budesonide (an inhaled corticosteroid), micronized glycopyrrolate (an anticholinergic), and micronized formoterol fumarate (an inhaled long-acting beta2-adrenergic agonist). These components help reduce inflammation, relax airway muscles, and prevent symptoms like wheezing and shortness of breath.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. The potential risks should be considered based on the maternal need and any adverse effects on the fetus. There are no data on the effect of Glycopyrrolate or Formoterol Fumarate on the breastfed child.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>This inhaler is not indicated for children, and the safety and effectiveness have not been established for pediatric patients.</p><p><strong>Hepatic and Renal Impairment</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: Use with caution, as systemic exposure to budesonide and formoterol fumarate may increase in patients with severe hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: Use only if the potential benefit outweighs the risks in patients with severe renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustments are necessary for geriatric patients, though greater sensitivity in some older individuals cannot be ruled out.</p><p>This information is crucial for proper use and monitoring of the inhaler. Let me know if you need further details or have additional questions!</p>', 'active', '709_1742249501.jpg', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 03:11:41', '2025-05-03 00:31:32'),
(3194, 'Fenatrol', 'Fenofibrate', 8.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '145 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Fenatrol 200 mg</strong></p><p>Fenatrol 200 mg is indicated for hyperlipidemias of type IIa, IIb, III, IV &amp; V in patients who have not responded adequately to diet &amp; other appropriate measures.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Fibrates</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Fenatrol 200 mg is a fibric acid derivative, a prodrug comprising fenofibric acid linked to an isopropyl ester. Fenatrol 200 mg is rapidly hydrolyzed after oral ingestion to its pharmacologically active form, fenofibric acid. The effects of fenofibric acid seen in clinical practice have been explained in vivo in transgenic mice and in vitro in human hepatocyte cultures by the activation of peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor α (PPARα).</p><p>It lowers lipid levels by activating PPARα, which in turn activates lipoprotein lipase and reduces apoprotein CIII (an inhibitor of lipoprotein lipase activity). This process increases lipolysis and eliminates triglyceride-rich particles from plasma. The resulting fall in triglycerides alters the size and composition of LDL from small, dense particles (which are more susceptible to oxidation and considered atherogenic) to larger, buoyant particles. These larger particles have a greater affinity for cholesterol receptors and are catabolized rapidly.</p><p>PPARα also increases apoproteins AI and AII, reduces VLDL- and LDL-containing apoprotein B, and increases HDL-containing apoprotein AI and AII. Fenatrol 200 mg also reduces serum uric acid levels in hyperuricemic and normal individuals by increasing the urinary excretion of uric acid.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Fenatrol 200 mg</strong></p><p>The dose is one 200 mg capsule per day. Dosage should be individualized according to patient response and increased sequentially if necessary, following repeat serum triglyceride estimations at 4 to 8-week intervals.</p><p>Patients should be placed on an appropriate triglyceride-lowering diet before receiving Fenatrol 200 mg and should continue this diet during treatment. Fenatrol 200 mg should be given with meals to optimize bioavailability.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Fenatrol 200 mg</strong></p><p>For the treatment of adult patients with hypercholesterolemia or mixed hyperlipidemia, the initial dose of Fenatrol 200 mg is 200 mg per day. For adult patients with hypertriglyceridemia, the initial dose ranges from 67 to 200 mg per day.</p><p>The dose should be individualized according to patient response. Fenatrol 200 mg should be taken with meals to optimize bioavailability. Or, as directed by a registered physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Fenatrol 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Potentiates the anticoagulant effects of warfarin.</li><li>May improve glucose tolerance when administered with antidiabetic drugs.</li><li>May increase the nephrotoxicity of cyclosporine.</li><li>Caution should be taken when used with HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors due to an increased risk of rhabdomyolysis. However, the use of low-dose statins with Fenatrol 200 mg appears to be well tolerated.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Fenatrol 200 mg is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Fenatrol 200 mg</li><li>Severe renal or hepatic impairment</li><li>Existing gall bladder disease</li><li>Breastfeeding mothers</li><li>Photosensitivity to ketoprofen</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Fenatrol 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal: nausea, anorexia, gastric pain</li><li>Skin: pruritus, urticaria</li><li>Others: impotence, headache, dizziness, vertigo, fatigue, hair loss, myotoxicity</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C:</strong> Should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Nursing mothers:</strong> Should not be used due to the potential for tumorigenicity seen in animal studies. A decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or to discontinue the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Special care is needed in patients with renal disease, as progressive increases in serum creatinine concentration or failure to follow dosage guidelines may result in myotoxicity.</li><li>Discontinue use if myotoxicity is suspected or if creatinine kinase concentration increases significantly.</li><li>Liver function tests are recommended every 3 months for the first year.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Fenatrol 200 mg</strong></p><p>There is no specific treatment for overdose with Fenatrol 200 mg. General supportive care, including monitoring of vital signs and clinical observation, is indicated. Since Fenatrol 200 mg is highly bound to plasma proteins, hemodialysis should not be considered.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Do not store above 30°C.</li><li>Keep away from light and out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics:</strong> Similar dosage regimen can be used in elderly patients without increasing drug or metabolite accumulation.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics:</strong> Not indicated for use in pediatric patients due to lack of data.</li><li><strong>Gender:</strong> No pharmacokinetic difference observed between males and females.</li><li><strong>Renal insufficiency:</strong> Dosage should be minimized in patients with severe renal impairment; no modification required for moderate renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic insufficiency:</strong> No pharmacokinetic study has been conducted in patients with hepatic insufficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Fibrates</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Fenatrol 200 mg is a fibric acid derivative that is rapidly hydrolyzed after oral ingestion into its active form, fenofibric acid. It reduces total cholesterol, LDL cholesterol, apolipoprotein B, total triglycerides, and VLDL while increasing HDL and apoproteins apoAI and apoAII.</p><p>Fenatrol 200 mg also reduces serum uric acid levels by increasing its urinary excretion. The micronized form of Fenatrol 200 mg has enhanced absorption over the non-micronized formulation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Fenatrol 200 mg is not recommended for pregnant women.</p>', 'in-active', '616_1742252496.jpg', 9, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 04:01:36', '2025-05-08 14:55:58'),
(3195, 'Starcef', 'Cefixime Trihydrate', 45.00, 7, 'Capsule', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Starcef 200 mg</strong></p><p> Cefixime is an orally active cephalosporin antibiotic with marked in-vitro bactericidal activity against a wide variety of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms. Starcef 200 mg is indicated for the treatment of the following acute infections caused by susceptible microorganisms:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper Respiratory Tract Infections (URTI):</strong> Otitis media and other URTI where the causative organism is known or suspected to be resistant to other commonly used antibiotics, or where treatment failure may carry significant risk.</li><li><strong>Lower Respiratory Tract Infections:</strong> Bronchitis.</li><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections:</strong> Cystitis, cystourethritis, pyelonephritis.</li></ul><p>Clinical efficacy has been demonstrated in infections caused by commonly occurring pathogens, including <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Streptococcus pyogenes, Escherichia coli, Proteus mirabilis, Klebsiella species, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase positive and negative), <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase positive and negative), and <em>Enterobacter species.</em> Cefixime is highly stable in the presence of beta-lactamase enzymes.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Third-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cefixime is a third-generation semisynthetic cephalosporin antibiotic for oral administration. It is bactericidal against a broad spectrum of Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria by interfering with bacterial cell wall synthesis. Cefixime is highly stable in the presence of beta-lactamase enzymes, making it effective against beta-lactamase-producing bacteria. Absorption is about 40% to 50%, whether administered with or without food.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Starcef 200 mg</strong></p><p> The usual course of treatment is 7 days, which may be extended up to 14 days depending on the severity of the infection.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years:</strong> 200-400 mg (1 to 2 capsules) daily, either as a single dose or in two divided doses.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated cervical/urethral gonococcal infections:</strong> Single oral dose of 400 mg.</li><li><strong>Children (6 months or older):</strong> Usually 8 mg/kg/day, given as a single dose or in two divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">½-1 year: 75 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-4 years: 100 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-10 years: 200 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">11-12 years: 300 mg daily</li><li><strong>Typhoid fever:</strong> 10 mg/kg/day for 14 days.</li><li><strong>Children under 6 months:</strong> Safety and efficacy have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Starcef 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 10 years:</strong> 200-400 mg daily, either as a single dose or in two divided doses.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> The same dose as adults, but renal function should be assessed, and dosage adjusted in severe renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> 8 mg/kg/day, given as a single dose or in two divided doses. Suggested daily doses:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6 months up to 1 year: 3.75 ml daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-4 years: 5 ml daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-10 years: 10 ml daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Typhoid fever: 5 mg/kg body weight twice daily for 10-14 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children over 50 kg or older than 10 years: 200-400 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal impairment:</strong>Creatinine clearance ≥ 20 ml/min: Normal dose and schedule.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Creatinine clearance &lt; 20 ml/min: Do not exceed 200 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients on chronic ambulatory peritoneal dialysis or hemodialysis should follow the same regimen as those with creatinine clearance &lt; 20 ml/min.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Starcef 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Increases in prothrombin times have been observed in a few patients, requiring caution in those receiving anticoagulant therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with known hypersensitivity to cephalosporin antibiotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Starcef 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Diarrhea, stool changes, nausea, abdominal pain, dyspepsia, vomiting, flatulence, pseudomembranous colitis.</li><li><strong>Central nervous system:</strong> Headache, dizziness.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity reactions:</strong> Rash, pruritus, urticaria, drug fever, arthralgia (usually subsides upon discontinuation).</li><li><strong>Hematological effects:</strong> Thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, eosinophilia (infrequent and reversible).</li><li><strong>Miscellaneous:</strong> Mild transient changes in liver and renal function tests, genital pruritus, vaginitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>No adequate, well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Use only if clearly needed.</li><li>Unknown if excreted in human milk, so caution is advised when administering to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in penicillin-sensitive patients due to partial cross-allergenicity.</li><li>If allergic reactions occur, discontinue use and treat as necessary.</li><li>Use cautiously in patients with severe renal impairment.</li><li>Broad-spectrum antibiotics can alter gut flora, potentially leading to Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Starcef 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Gastric lavage may be indicated.</li><li>No specific antidote exists.</li><li>Cefixime is not removed in significant quantities by hemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30ºC, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Third-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Cefixime is a third-generation cephalosporin that interferes with bacterial cell wall synthesis, leading to bacterial death. It is stable against beta-lactamase enzymes, making it effective against resistant strains. Absorption is about 40% to 50%, with or without food.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Use only if clearly needed, as there are no well-controlled studies in pregnant women.</li><li>Exercise caution when administering to nursing mothers, as excretion in breast milk is unknown.</li></ul>', 'active', '1580_1742252743.jpg', 55, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 04:05:43', '2025-03-18 04:05:43'),
(3196, 'Pivalo', 'Pitavastatin Calcium', 10.03, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pivalo 2 mg</strong></p><p> Pitavastatin is indicated as an adjunctive therapy to diet to reduce elevated total cholesterol (TC), low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-C), apolipoprotein B (Apo B), triglycerides (TG), and to increase HDL-C in adult patients with primary hyperlipidemia or mixed dyslipidemia.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs, Statins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Pitavastatin lowers plasma cholesterol and lipoprotein levels by inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase and cholesterol synthesis in the liver and increases the number of hepatic LDL receptors on the cell surface for enhanced uptake and catabolism of LDL.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Pivalo 2 mg</strong></p><p> The dose range for Pitavastatin is 1 to 4 mg orally once daily at any time of the day with or without food. The recommended starting dose is 2 mg, and the maximum dose is 4 mg. After initiation or upon titration of Pitavastatin, lipid levels should be analyzed after 4 weeks, and the dosage adjusted accordingly.</p><p><strong>Dosage in Patients with Renal Impairment:</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with moderate renal impairment and end–stage renal disease receiving hemodialysis should receive a starting dose of Pitavastatin 1 mg once daily and a maximum dose of Pitavastatin 2 mg once daily.</li><li>Pitavastatin should not be used in patients with severe renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Pivalo 2 mg</strong></p><p> The dose range for Pitavastatin is 1 to 4 mg orally once daily at any time of the day with or without food. The recommended starting dose is 2 mg, and the maximum dose is 4 mg. After initiation or upon titration of Pitavastatin, lipid levels should be analyzed after 4 weeks, and the dosage adjusted accordingly.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Pivalo 2 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine:</strong> Co-administration of cyclosporine with Pitavastatin is contraindicated.</li><li><strong>Erythromycin:</strong> Erythromycin significantly increased Pitavastatin exposure. In patients taking erythromycin, a dose of Pitavastatin 1 mg once daily should not be exceeded.</li><li><strong>Rifampin:</strong> Rifampin significantly increased Pitavastatin exposure. In patients taking rifampin, a dose of Pitavastatin 2 mg once daily should not be exceeded.</li><li><strong>Fibrates:</strong> Pitavastatin should be administered with caution when used concomitantly with gemfibrozil or other fibrates.</li><li><strong>Warfarin:</strong> Pitavastatin had no significant pharmacokinetic interaction with warfarin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with a known hypersensitivity to any component of this product. Hypersensitivity reactions, including rash, pruritus, and urticaria, have been reported with Pitavastatin.</li><li>Patients with active liver disease, which may include unexplained persistent elevations of hepatic transaminase levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Pivalo 2 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Muscle-related:</strong> Rhabdomyolysis, myopathy</li><li><strong>Liver-related:</strong> Liver enzyme abnormalities</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category X:</strong> Pitavastatin is contraindicated in women who are or may become pregnant.</li><li>It is not known whether Pitavastatin is excreted in human milk; however, it has been shown that a small amount of another drug in this class passes into human milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Pitavastatin should be prescribed with caution in patients with predisposing factors for myopathy, such as advanced age (&gt;65 years), renal impairment, and inadequately treated hypothyroidism.</li><li>Increase in serum transaminases has been reported with HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors, including Pitavastatin. In most cases, the elevations were transient and resolved or improved on continued therapy or after a brief interruption in therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Pivalo 2 mg</strong></p><p> There is no known specific treatment in the event of an overdose of Pitavastatin. In case of overdose, the patient should be treated symptomatically and supportive measures instituted as required. Hemodialysis is unlikely to be of benefit due to the high protein-binding ratio of Pitavastatin.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs, Statins</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Pitavastatin lowers plasma cholesterol and lipoprotein levels by inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase and cholesterol synthesis in the liver and increases the number of hepatic LDL receptors on the cell surface for enhanced uptake and catabolism of LDL.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category X:</strong> Pitavastatin is contraindicated in women who are or may become pregnant.</li><li>It is not known whether Pitavastatin is excreted in human milk; however, it has been shown that a small amount of another drug in this class passes into human milk.</li></ul>', 'active', '717_1742253016.jpg', 4, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 04:10:16', '2025-03-18 04:10:16'),
(3197, 'Rantoin', 'Nitrofurantoin', 6.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rantoin 100 mg</strong></p><p> Rantoin 100 mg is specifically indicated for the treatment &amp; prophylaxis of urinary tract infections caused by susceptible strains of <em>Escherichia coli, Enterococci, Staphylococcus aureus, Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em> and certain susceptible strains of <em>Klebsiella</em> and <em>Enterobacter</em> species.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Systemic Urinary Anti-infective</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Rantoin 100 mg interferes with cell metabolism and cell wall synthesis by inhibiting several enzyme systems, including acetyl coenzyme A. It is bactericidal to most gram-positive and gram-negative urinary tract pathogens.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Rantoin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Rantoin 100 mg tablet (In adults)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated urinary tract infections: 50-100 mg four times a day—lower dosage level is recommended. Therapy should be continued for one week or for at least 3 days after sterility of the urine is obtained.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Long-term suppressive therapy: 50-100 mg at bedtime may be adequate.</li><li><strong>Rantoin 100 mg capsule (In adults)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute uncomplicated urinary tract infections (UTIs): 50 mg four times daily for 7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Long-term suppression: 50-100 mg once a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prophylaxis: 50 mg four times daily for the duration of procedure and for three days thereafter.</li><li><strong>Rantoin 100 mg SR capsule</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults and children over 12 years: One 100 mg capsule every 12 hours for seven days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Genito-urinary surgical prophylaxis: One capsule twice daily on the day of procedure and for the next 3 days.</li><li><strong>Rantoin 100 mg suspension (Children)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-7 mg/kg/day in four divided doses (contraindicated under one month of age).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Average pediatric doses:</strong>7 to 11 kg: ½ (2.5 ml) teaspoonfuls 4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">12 to 21 kg: 1 (5 ml) teaspoonful 4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">22 to 30 kg: 1½ (7.5 ml) teaspoonfuls 4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">31 to 41 kg: 2 (10 ml) teaspoonfuls 4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Therapy should be continued for one week or for at least 3 days after sterility of the urine is obtained.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Long-term suppressive therapy in children:</strong> 1 mg/kg per 24 hours, given in a single dose or in two divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Rantoin 100 mg</strong></p><p> Rantoin 100 mg should be taken with food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Rantoin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Antacids containing magnesium trisilicate, when administered concomitantly with Rantoin 100 mg, reduce both the rate and extent of absorption.</li><li>Uricosuric drugs, such as Probenecid and Sulfinpyrazone, can inhibit renal tubular secretion of Rantoin 100 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Anuria, oliguria, or significant impairment of renal function.</li><li>Contraindicated in pregnant patients at 38-42 weeks, during labor, and delivery.</li><li>Patients with known hypersensitivity to Rantoin 100 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Rantoin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Most frequent: nausea, headache, and flatulence.</li><li>Less common: diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain, constipation, emesis, dizziness, and drowsiness.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B.</strong> No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Use only if clearly needed.</li><li>Rantoin 100 mg is detected in trace amounts in human breast milk. Due to the potential for serious adverse reactions in nursing infants under one month of age, a decision should be made to either discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, considering its importance to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>If acute, sub-acute, or chronic pulmonary reactions occur, discontinue Rantoin 100 mg.</li><li>Antacid preparations containing magnesium trisilicate should not be taken with Rantoin 100 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rantoin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Acute overdosage has not resulted in specific symptoms other than vomiting.</li><li>Induction of emesis is recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Do not store above 30°C.</li><li>Keep away from light and out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Systemic Urinary Anti-infective</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Rantoin 100 mg is an antibacterial agent specific for urinary tract infections. It is highly soluble in urine, to which it may impart a brown color. It inactivates or alters bacterial ribosomal proteins and other macromolecules.</p><p><strong>Active against the following bacteria:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gram-Positive Aerobes:</strong><em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus, Coagulase-negative staphylococci (including Staphylococcus epidermidis), Enterococcus faecalis, Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus agalactiae, Group D streptococci, Viridans group streptococci.</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gram-Negative Aerobes:</strong><em>Escherichia coli, Citrobacter amalonaticus, Citrobacter diversus, Citrobacter freundii, Klebsiella oxytoca, Klebsiella ozaenae.</em></li></ul>', 'active', '1199_1742253382.jpg', 9, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 04:16:22', '2025-03-18 04:16:22'),
(3198, 'Parinox', 'Enoxaparin Sodium', 575.00, 1, 'Injection', '0.6 ml pre-filled syringe', 'Injection', '6000 Anti-Xa IU per 0.6 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Parinox 6000 Anti-Xa IU/0.6 ml</strong></p><p> Enoxaparin is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Treatment of deep vein thrombosis, with or without pulmonary embolism.</li><li>Treatment of unstable angina and non-Q-wave myocardial infarction, administered concurrently with aspirin.</li><li>Prevention of thrombus formation in the extra-corporal circulation during haemodialysis.</li><li>Prophylaxis of venous thromboembolic disease (prevention of blood clot formation in the veins), particularly in orthopedic or general surgery.</li><li>Prophylaxis of venous thromboembolic disease in bedridden medical patients due to acute illness, including cardiac insufficiency, respiratory failure, severe infections, and rheumatic diseases.</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p> Each 0.2 ml pre-filled syringe contains Parinox 6000 Anti-Xa IU/0.6 ml BP 20 mg equivalent to 2000 anti-Xa III.</p><p> Each 0.4 ml pre-filled syringe contains Parinox 6000 Anti-Xa IU/0.6 ml BP 40 mg equivalent to 4000 anti-Xa III.</p><p> Each 0.6 ml pre-filled syringe contains Parinox 6000 Anti-Xa IU/0.6 ml BP 60 mg equivalent to 6000 anti-Xa IU.</p><p> Each 0.8 ml pre-filled syringe contains Parinox 6000 Anti-Xa IU/0.6 ml BP 80 mg equivalent to 8000 anti-Xa IU.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Parenteral anti-coagulants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Parinox 6000 Anti-Xa IU/0.6 ml is a low molecular weight heparin with high anti-Xa activity and low anti-IIa (antithrombin) activity. At therapeutic doses, it does not increase bleeding time. At preventive doses, it does not significantly modify activated Partial Thromboplastin Time (aPTT). It does not influence platelet aggregation or fibrinogen binding to platelets. It is primarily metabolized in the liver.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Deep vein thrombosis (with or without pulmonary embolism):</strong> Subcutaneously 100 anti-Xa IU/kg twice daily for 10 days or 150 anti-Xa IU/kg once daily for 10 days. Oral anticoagulant therapy should be initiated when appropriate.</li><li><strong>Unstable angina and non-Q-wave myocardial infarction:</strong> Subcutaneously 100 anti-Xa IU/kg twice daily for 2–8 days, with oral aspirin (100–325 mg once daily).</li><li><strong>Thrombus prevention in extra-corporeal circulation (hemodialysis):</strong> Recommended dose is 100 anti-Xa IU/kg. For high hemorrhage risk, reduce to 50 anti-Xa IU/kg (double vascular access) or 75 anti-Xa IU/kg (single vascular access). Administer into the arterial line at dialysis session start.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of venous thromboembolic disease:</strong>General surgery (moderate risk): Subcutaneously 2000–4000 anti-Xa IU once daily for 7–10 days. First dose given 2 hours before surgery.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Orthopedic surgery (high risk): Subcutaneously 4000 anti-Xa IU once daily for 7–10 days, first dose given 12 hours before surgery. Extended therapy may be required (4000 anti-Xa IU once daily for 3 weeks).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Medical patients: Subcutaneously 4000 anti-Xa IU once daily for 6–14 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><ul><li>Inject in the right or left abdomen, at least 5 cm from the belly button, alternating sides.</li><li>Clean the area with alcohol or soap and water.</li><li>Do not press the plunger before injecting to remove air bubbles.</li><li>Hold the syringe like a pencil and insert the needle at a 90° angle into a pinched skin fold.</li><li>Inject the entire dose and do not rub the site afterward.</li><li>Dispose of the syringe in a sharps container.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><p> Parinox 6000 Anti-Xa IU/0.6 ml should not be used with medications affecting hemostasis unless strictly indicated. These include:</p><ul><li>Acetylsalicylic acid and derivatives</li><li>NSAIDs (including ketorolac)</li><li>Ticlopidine, clopidogrel</li><li>Dextran 40</li><li>Glucocorticoids</li><li>Thrombolytics and anticoagulants</li><li>Antiplatelet agents (e.g., glycoprotein IIb/IIIa antagonists)</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Enoxaparin, heparin, or low molecular weight heparins.</li><li>Active major bleeding or high risk of uncontrolled hemorrhage (e.g., recent hemorrhagic stroke).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Hemorrhage (bleeding)</li><li>Thrombocytopenia</li><li>Increased serum aminotransferase levels</li><li>Pain or bruising at injection sites</li><li>Neuraxial hematomas with spinal/epidural anesthesia, potentially leading to neurological complications</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> No evidence of crossing the placental barrier, but should only be used if clearly needed.</li><li>Pregnant women with mechanical heart valves may have increased thromboembolism risk.</li><li>Unknown if excreted in human milk; consider stopping breastfeeding or discontinuing the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Administer subcutaneously for prophylaxis and curative treatment; intravascular for hemodialysis.</li><li>Do not administer intramuscularly.</li><li>Use cautiously in patients with increased bleeding risk (e.g., peptic ulcers, recent ischemic stroke, severe hypertension, diabetic retinopathy).</li><li>Monitor platelet counts before and during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose may cause hemorrhagic complications.</li><li>Can be neutralized by IV protamine sulfate (1 mg protamine neutralizes 1 mg Enoxaparin).</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 25°C in a cool, dry place, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Do not refrigerate or freeze.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> No dosage adjustment unless kidney function is impaired.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> Monitor for bleeding. Severe impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min) requires dose adjustment:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prophylaxis: 2000 anti-Xa IU once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Therapeutic: 100 anti-Xa IU/kg once daily</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment:</strong> Use with caution.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Parenteral anti-coagulants</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Parinox 6000 Anti-Xa IU/0.6 ml is a low molecular weight heparin that inhibits factor Xa, reducing thrombus formation without significantly affecting clotting time or platelet function. It is metabolized in the liver.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> No dose adjustment unless renal function is impaired.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> Moderate/mild impairment does not require dose adjustment, but close monitoring is necessary.</li><li><strong>Severe renal impairment:</strong> Adjust dosage accordingly.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment:</strong> Use with caution.</li></ul>', 'active', '1722_1742253996.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 04:26:36', '2025-03-18 04:26:36'),
(3199, 'Parkifen', 'Trihexyphenidyl Hydrochloride', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Parkifen 5 mg</strong></p><p> Trihexyphenidyl is indicated as an adjunct in the treatment of all forms of parkinsonism (postencephalitic, arteriosclerotic, and idiopathic). It is often useful as adjuvant therapy when treating these forms of Parkinsonism with levodopa. Additionally, it is indicated for the control of extrapyramidal disorders caused by central nervous system drugs such as the dibenzoxazepines, phenothiazines, thioxanthenes, and butyrophenones.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Antiparkinson drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Trihexyphenidyl is a selective M1 muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist. It is able to discriminate between the M1 (cortical or neuronal) and the peripheral muscarinic subtypes (cardiac and glandular). Trihexyphenidyl partially blocks cholinergic activity in the CNS, which is responsible for the symptoms of Parkinson\'s disease. It is also thought to increase the availability of dopamine, a brain chemical that is critical in the initiation and smooth control of voluntary muscle movement.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Parkifen 5 mg</strong></p><p> Dosage should be individualized. The initial dose should be low and then increased gradually, especially in patients over 60 years of age. Whether Trihexyphenidyl may best be given before or after meals should be determined by the way the patient reacts.</p><ul><li><strong>Idiopathic Parkinsonism:</strong> 1 mg of Trihexyphenidyl may be administered the first day. The dose may then be increased by 2 mg increments at intervals of three to five days.</li><li><strong>Drug-Induced Parkinsonism:</strong> Commence therapy with a single 1 mg dose. Increase the total daily dosage to the 5-15 mg range if extrapyramidal manifestations are not controlled.</li><li><strong>Concomitant Use with Levodopa:</strong> When used with levodopa, the usual dose is 3-6 mg daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Parkifen 5 mg</strong></p><p> Dosage should be individualized. The initial dose should be low and then increased gradually, especially in patients over 60 years of age. The timing of administration (before or after meals) depends on patient response.</p><ul><li><strong>Idiopathic Parkinsonism:</strong> As initial therapy, 1 mg may be administered the first day, increasing by 2 mg increments at intervals of three to five days. The total daily dose is typically 6-10 mg but may go up to 12-15 mg in some cases.</li><li><strong>Drug-Induced Parkinsonism:</strong> The total daily dosage usually ranges between 5 and 15 mg. The dose should be determined empirically.</li><li><strong>Concomitant Use with Levodopa:</strong> The usual dosage is 3-6 mg daily in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Use in Children:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Parkifen 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Cannabinoids, barbiturates, opiates, and alcohol may have additive effects, leading to potential abuse.</li><li>Concurrent use of alcohol or other CNS depressants may cause increased sedative effects.</li><li>Monoamine oxidase inhibitors and tricyclic antidepressants may intensify anticholinergic effects.</li><li>Concomitant administration with neuroleptics may increase the risk of tardive dyskinesia.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Trihexyphenidyl</li><li>Narrow-angle glaucoma (blindness has been reported due to long-term use)</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Parkifen 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Dry mouth, blurred vision, dizziness, mild nausea, nervousness</li><li>Rare: Skin rashes, cognitive dysfunction, urinary retention, tachycardia, hallucinations, and delusions</li><li>Serious: Exacerbation of parkinsonism with abrupt withdrawal, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, blindness due to glaucoma</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C:</strong> Should only be used if the benefit outweighs the potential risk.</li><li>It is unknown if Trihexyphenidyl is excreted in human milk; therefore, caution should be exercised.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Patients should undergo gonioscopic evaluation before therapy initiation to monitor intraocular pressure.</li><li>Anticholinergic drugs may precipitate angle closure and lead to increased intraocular pressure.</li><li>Use caution in hot weather to prevent anhidrosis and hyperthermia.</li><li>Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS) has been reported in association with dose reduction or discontinuation.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Parkifen 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Delirium, hallucinations, agitation, dry skin, tachycardia, seizures, and coma</li><li>Treatment: Supportive therapy, gastric lavage, diazepam for CNS excitation, respiratory support if needed</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at temperatures not exceeding 30ºC in a dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Antiparkinson drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Trihexyphenidyl is a non-selective muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist with a higher affinity for the M1 subtype. It modifies nicotinic acetylcholine receptor neurotransmission, indirectly enhancing dopamine release in the striatum.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>There are no controlled studies in pregnancy; it should only be used if necessary.</li><li>May suppress lactation; use with caution in breastfeeding women.</li></ul>', 'active', '1233_1742254308.jpg', 8, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 04:31:48', '2025-03-18 04:31:48');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3200, 'Stafoxin', 'Flucloxacillin Sodium', 5.50, 6, 'Capsule', '6 Tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Stafoxin 250 mg</strong></p><p> Stafoxin 250 mg is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by Gram-positive organisms, including those resulting from penicillinase-producing staphylococci. These indications encompass a wide range of conditions:</p><ul><li><strong>Skin and Soft Tissue Infections:</strong> Includes boils, abscesses, carbuncles, and various infected skin conditions such as ulcers, eczema, acne, furunculosis, cellulitis, as well as infected wounds and burns.</li><li><strong>Respiratory Tract Infections:</strong> Effective against pneumonia, lung abscesses, empyema, sinusitis, pharyngitis, tonsillitis, and quinsy.</li><li><strong>Other Infections:</strong> Used for treating osteomyelitis, enteritis, endocarditis, urinary tract infections, meningitis, and septicaemia caused by organisms sensitive to Stafoxin 250 mg.</li><li><strong>Prophylactic Use:</strong> Serves as a prophylactic agent during major surgical procedures, particularly in cardiothoracic and orthopedic surgery when deemed appropriate.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Penicillinase-resistant penicillins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Flucloxacillin is effective against Gram-positive organisms, including those that produce penicillinase, with limited activity against Gram-negative bacilli. It inhibits bacterial cell wall formation by disrupting peptidoglycan synthesis, an essential component for bacterial stability. This bactericidal action ultimately leads to cell swelling and rupture. Its resistance to bacterial penicillinase arises from steric hindrance induced by its acyl side chain, preventing the opening of the β-lactam ring.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Stafoxin 250 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Oral Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 250 mg four times a day. The dosage may be doubled in severe infections.</li><li><strong>Osteomyelitis and Endocarditis:</strong> Up to 8 grams daily, administered in 6-8 hourly divided doses.</li><li><strong>Children (2-10 years):</strong> Half of the adult dose.</li><li><strong>Children (Under 2 years):</strong> Quarter of the adult dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Parenteral Administration:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults or Elderly:Intramuscular Injection:</strong> 250 mg four times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intravenous Injection:</strong> 250 mg to 1 gram four times daily (slow injection over 3-4 minutes or IV infusion). In severe infections, doses may be doubled, up to 8 grams daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:2-10 years:</strong> Half of the adult dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Under 2 years:</strong> Quarter of the adult dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Stafoxin 250 mg</strong></p><p> Oral doses should be administered 1 hour before meals.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Stafoxin 250 mg</strong></p><p> For severe renal failure (creatinine clearance &lt;10 ml/min), a dose reduction or an extension of the dosing interval should be considered.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Stafoxin 250 mg is contraindicated in patients with penicillin hypersensitivity.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Stafoxin 250 mg</strong></p><p> Common gastrointestinal side effects include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, dyspepsia, and minor disturbances. Other reported side effects include rashes, urticaria, purpura, fever, interstitial nephritis, hepatitis, and cholestatic jaundice.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Exercise caution in patients with liver dysfunction and those with an allergic predisposition.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> For severe renal failure (creatinine clearance &lt;10 ml/min), consider a dose reduction or an extended dosing interval.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p> Stafoxin 250 mg has been administered through intra-articular and intrapleural injections at doses of 250 mg to 500 mg per day. It has also been used via nebulizer at doses of 125 mg to 250 mg dissolved in 3 ml of sterile water, four times daily.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Penicillinase-resistant penicillins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Flucloxacillin inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by interfering with peptidoglycan production. It is particularly effective against penicillinase-producing staphylococci, leading to bacterial cell wall weakening, swelling, and rupture.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category B (US FDA):</strong> No adequate, well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Use only if clearly necessary.</li><li>Excreted in human milk; caution is advised when administering to lactating mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> For severe renal failure (creatinine clearance &lt;10 ml/min), consider dose reduction or extended dose interval.</p>', 'active', '1834_1742254630.jpg', 29, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 04:37:10', '2025-03-18 04:37:10'),
(3201, 'Spanil', 'Hyoscine Butylbromide', 6.90, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Spanil 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li> হায়োসিন বিউটাইলব্রোমাইড একটি এন্টিকোলিনার্জিক ওষুধ, যা এবডোমিনাল ক্যাভিটির বিভিন্ন অংশের নরম মাংসপেশীর (smooth muscle) উপর খিঁচুনিবিরোধী (antispasmodic) ক্রিয়া রাখে। এটি গ্যাসট্রো ইন্টেসটিনাল ট্র্যাক্ট বা জেনিটো-ইউরিনারী ট্র্যাক্টের ভিসেরাল খিঁচুনি জনিত লক্ষণসমূহে নির্দেশিত।<strong>স্পাসমোডিক ডিসমেনোরিয়া:</strong> স্পাসমোডিক ডিসমেনোরিয়ার চিকিৎসায় ব্যবহৃত হয়।</li><li><strong>ইনজেকশন ব্যবহার:</strong> গ্যাসট্রো-ইন্টেসটিনাল খিঁচুনি, রেনাল বা পিত্তের (biliary) তীব্র ব্যথার চিকিৎসায় কার্যকর।</li><li><strong>রেডিওলজিতে ব্যবহার:</strong> প্রতিবন্ধকতার পার্থক্যমূলক রোগ নির্ণয়ে, পাইলোগ্রাফীতে খিঁচুনি ও ব্যথা কমাতে এবং গ্যাসট্রো-ডিওডেনাল এনডোস্কোপীর মত রোগ নির্ণয়ের অন্যান্য পদ্ধতিতে ব্যবহৃত হয়।</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anticholinergics (antimuscarinics) / Anti-spasmodics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> হায়োসিন মুসকারিনিক রিসেপ্টর ব্লক করে এবং কেন্দ্রীয় ও পার্শ্বীয়ভাবে কাজ করে। এটি স্মুথ মাংসপেশী শিথিল করে এবং গ্যাস্ট্রিক ও অন্ত্রের গতিশীলতা কমায়।<strong>Dosage of Spanil 10 mg</strong></p><p><strong>খাওয়ার জন্য:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>প্রাপ্ত বয়স্ক:</strong> ২০ মিগ্রা দিনে চার বার।</li><li><strong>স্পাসমোডিক ডিসমেনোরিয়া:</strong> ঋতুস্রাব শুরুর সম্ভাব্য তারিখের কমপক্ষে দুদিন আগে থেকে চিকিৎসা শুরু করতে হবে এবং স্রাব শুরুর তিনদিন পরে পর্যন্ত তা চালিয়ে যেতে হবে।</li><li><strong>শিশু (৬-১২ বছর):</strong> ১০ মিগ্রা দিনে তিনবার বা চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ অনুযায়ী।</li></ul><p><strong>ইনজেকশন হিসেবে:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>প্রাপ্তবয়স্ক:মাংসপেশীতে বা শিরাপথে:</strong> সাধারণ মাত্রা ২০ মিগ্রা (১টি অ্যাম্পুল), যা ৩০ মিনিট পর পুনরায় প্রয়োগ করা যেতে পারে।</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>এনডোস্কোপীর ক্ষেত্রে:</strong> আরও ঘন ঘন প্রয়োগের প্রয়োজন হতে পারে।</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Spanil 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>ওষুধের মাত্রা ব্যক্তির ওজন, অন্যান্য শারীরিক অবস্থা ও অন্যান্য ওষুধের উপর নির্ভর করে।</li><li>ট্যাবলেট সম্পূর্ণ গিলে ফেলতে হবে, চিবানো বা গুঁড়ো করা যাবে না।</li><li>কোনো ডোজ মিস হলে পরবর্তী নির্ধারিত সময়ে ওষুধ সেবন করুন। দ্বিগুণ ডোজ গ্রহণ করবেন না।</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Spanil 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>অ্যালকোহল বা অন্যান্য সিএনএস ডিপ্রেস্যান্টের সাথে:</strong> সংযোজিত প্রশমনমূলক (sedative) প্রভাব বাড়তে পারে।</li><li><strong>অ্যাসিটাইলকোলিনেস্টেরেজ ইনহিবিটর (যেমন: ডোনেপেজিল, গ্যালান্টামিন, রিভাস্টিগমিন, টাকরিন):</strong> এর সাথে গ্রহণ করলে কার্যকারিতা হ্রাস পেতে পারে।</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>প্রস্টেটিক বৃদ্ধি:</strong> প্রস্টেট বড় হলে ব্যবহার নিষিদ্ধ।</li><li><strong>বৃদ্ধদের ক্ষেত্রে:</strong> সতর্কতার সাথে ব্যবহার করতে হবে।</li><li><strong>প্যারালাইটিক ইলিয়াস বা পাইলোরিক স্টেনোসিস:</strong> এই অবস্থায় ব্যবহার নিষিদ্ধ।</li><li><strong>গ্লুকোমা:</strong> মাইড্রিয়াটিক ক্ষতির সম্ভাবনার কারণে ব্যবহার নিষিদ্ধ।</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Spanil 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>মুখ শুকিয়ে যাওয়া</li><li>পিউপিলের প্রসারণ (dilatation)</li><li>ইন্ট্রা-অকুলার প্রেসার বৃদ্ধি</li><li>ফ্লাশিং, ত্বক শুকিয়ে যাওয়া</li><li>ব্র্যাডিকার্ডিয়া এবং পরে ট্যাকিকার্ডিয়া ও এরিমিয়া</li><li>ক্লান্তি, বমি, মাথা ঝিমঝিম করা (giddiness), এবং ষ্ট্যাগারিং</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C:</strong> পশু পরীক্ষায় ভ্রূণের উপর বিরূপ প্রভাব দেখা গেছে, তবে মানব পরীক্ষায় পর্যাপ্ত তথ্য নেই। সম্ভাব্য ঝুঁকি সত্ত্বেও উপকারিতা থাকলে গর্ভবতী নারীদের জন্য ব্যবহার করা যেতে পারে।</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ঝিমুনি সৃষ্টি করতে পারে:</strong> হায়োসিন গ্রহণকারী রোগীদের গাড়ি চালানো বা মানসিক সতর্কতার প্রয়োজনীয় কাজ থেকে বিরত থাকা উচিত।</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>অন্য ওষুধের সাথে মিথস্ক্রিয়া:</strong>থাইরোটক্সিকোসিস, কার্ডিয়াক ফেলিওরের ক্ষেত্রে এটি হৃদযন্ত্রের গতি বাড়িয়ে দিতে পারে।</li></ul><p>এন্টিকোলিনার্জিক ওষুধ (যেমন: এমানটাডিন, এন্টিহিস্টামিন, বিউটাইরোফেনন, ফেনোথায়াজিন, ট্রাইসাইক্লিক এন্টিডিপ্রেসেন্ট) এর সাথে গ্রহণ করলে হায়োসিনের কার্যকারিতা বেড়ে যেতে পারে, তাই মাত্রা কমিয়ে নিতে হতে পারে।<strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>মেয়াদ উত্তীর্ণের পর ব্যবহার করবেন না।</li><li>সকল ওষুধ শিশুদের নাগালের বাইরে রাখুন।</li></ul><p>শুধুমাত্র রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের ব্যবস্থাপত্র অনুযায়ী বিতরণযোগ্য।<strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Anticholinergics (antimuscarinics) / Anti-spasmodics</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> গর্ভাবস্থার প্রথম তিন মাসে অন্যান্য ওষুধের মতো একেও সতর্কতার সাথে ব্যবহার করতে হবে।</p>', 'active', '1920_1742254844.jpg', 3, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 04:40:44', '2025-03-18 04:40:44'),
(3202, 'Thenxet', 'Flupentixol plus Melitracen', 5.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.5 mg plus 10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Thenxet 0.5 mg+10 mg</strong></p><p> Thenxet 0.5 mg+10 mg tablet is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Anxiety</li><li>Depression</li><li>Apathy</li><li>Psychogenic depression</li><li>Depressive neuroses</li><li>Masked depression</li><li>Psychosomatic affections accompanied by anxiety and apathy</li><li>Menopausal depressions</li><li>Dysphoria and depression in alcoholics and drug addicts</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Combined anxiolytics &amp; anti-depressant drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Maximum serum concentration is reached in about 4 hours after oral administration. The half-life of Flupentixol is about 35 hours, and that of Melitracen is about 19 hours.</p><ul><li><strong>Flupentixol:</strong> Primarily acts as a neuroleptic drug. It blocks dopamine D1 and D2 receptors, leading to its antipsychotic effect in the mesolimbic dopamine system. It also has an antiemetic effect due to dopamine blockade in the chemoreceptor trigger zone.</li><li><strong>Melitracen:</strong> A tricyclic antidepressant that blocks the neuronal reuptake of both serotonin and norepinephrine in the central nervous system, reducing depressive symptoms.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Thenxet 0.5 mg+10 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>Usually 2 tablets daily, in the morning and afternoon.</li><li>In severe cases, the morning dose may be increased to 2 tablets.</li></ul><p><strong>Elderly patients:</strong></p><ul><li>1 tablet in the morning.</li></ul><p><strong>Maintenance dose:</strong></p><ul><li>Usually 1 tablet in the morning or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Thenxet 0.5 mg+10 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>Usually 2 tablets orally daily in the morning and noon.</li><li>In severe cases, the morning dose may be increased to 2 tablets.</li></ul><p><strong>Elderly patients:</strong></p><ul><li>1 tablet in the morning.</li></ul><p><strong>Maintenance dose:</strong></p><ul><li>Usually 1 tablet orally in the morning.</li><li>In cases of insomnia or severe restlessness, additional treatment with a sedative in the acute phase is recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Thenxet 0.5 mg+10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>May enhance the response to alcohol, barbiturates, and other CNS depressants.</li><li>Simultaneous administration of MAO-inhibitors may cause hypertensive crises.</li><li>Neuroleptics and thymoleptics reduce the antihypertensive effect of guanethidine and similar compounds.</li><li>Thymoleptics enhance the effects of adrenaline and noradrenaline.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Immediate recovery phase after myocardial infarction.</li><li>Defects in bundle-branch conduction.</li><li>Untreated narrow-angle glaucoma.</li><li>Acute alcohol, barbiturate, and opiate intoxications.</li><li>Should not be given to patients who have received an MAO-inhibitor within two weeks.</li><li>Not recommended for excitable or overactive patients, as its activating effect may exaggerate these characteristics.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Thenxet 0.5 mg+10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>At recommended doses, side effects are rare.</li><li>Possible transient restlessness and insomnia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The safety of this drug has not been established in pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>If the patient has previously been treated with tranquillizers with sedative effects, these should be withdrawn gradually.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Thenxet 0.5 mg+10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose are mainly related to Melitracen, especially of anticholinergic nature.</li><li>Rarely, extrapyramidal symptoms due to Flupentixol may occur.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Management:</strong>Symptomatic and supportive care.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gastric lavage should be performed as soon as possible, and activated charcoal may be administered.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Support for respiratory and cardiovascular systems.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Epinephrine (adrenaline) should not be used.</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Convulsions may be treated with diazepam, and extrapyramidal symptoms with biperiden.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at a temperature not exceeding 30°C in a dry place.</li><li>Protect from light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Combined anxiolytics &amp; anti-depressant drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> This tablet contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Flupentixol:</strong> A neuroleptic with anxiolytic and antidepressant properties in small doses.</li><li><strong>Melitracen:</strong> A bipolar thymoleptic with activating properties in low doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Combination Effect:</strong></p><ul><li>Provides antidepressant, anxiolytic, and activating properties.</li><li>Maximum serum concentration is reached in about 4 hours for both Flupentixol and Melitracen.</li><li>The combination does not affect the pharmacokinetic properties of the individual compounds.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>This tablet should preferably not be given during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul>', 'active', '681_1742255174.jpg', 56, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 04:46:14', '2025-03-18 04:46:14'),
(3203, 'Vesanoid', 'Tretinoin', 288.66, 10, 'Capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vesanoid 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment of acne vulgaris with comedones, papules, and pustules.</li><li>Treatment of hyperpigmentation, roughness, and fine wrinkling of photodamaged skin due to chronic sun exposure.</li><li>Treatment of acute promyelocytic leukemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Topical retinoid and related preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Vesanoid 10 mg is structurally and pharmacologically related to vitamin A. It decreases the cohesiveness of follicular epithelial cells, reducing microcomedone formation. It also stimulates mitotic activity and increases follicular epithelial cell turnover, helping to extrude comedones.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Vesanoid 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cream:</strong> Apply sparingly to the affected area once or twice daily. Skin should be cleaned and dried before application. A therapeutic effect may take 6-8 weeks. Avoid applying moisturizers and cosmetics at the same time as Vesanoid 10 mg. Avoid astringent toiletries.</li><li><strong>Gel:</strong> Apply once or twice daily, preferably before bedtime, covering the entire affected area lightly. If excessive application causes redness, peeling, or discomfort, discontinue use for a few days. Therapeutic effects may take 6-8 weeks. Avoid astringent toiletries.</li><li><strong>Capsule:</strong> The recommended dose is 45 mg/m²/day in two divided doses until complete remission. Therapy should be discontinued 30 days after remission or after 90 days, whichever occurs first. If the presence of translocation is not confirmed and the patient has not responded, consider alternative therapy for acute myelogenous leukemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Vesanoid 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Caution when using with peeling agents like sulfur, resorcinol, benzoyl peroxide, or salicylic acid.</li><li>Avoid topical products with high concentrations of alcohol, menthol, spices, or lime (e.g., shaving lotions, astringents, perfumes).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Allergy to Vesanoid 10 mg.</li><li>Pregnancy or suspected pregnancy.</li><li>Personal or familial history of cutaneous epithelioma.</li><li>Severe local irritation (e.g., erythema, peeling, pruritus, sunburn) should resolve before initiating or resuming treatment.</li><li>Severe irritation on eczematous skin; use with caution in such patients.</li><li>Avoid or minimize exposure to sunlight, including UV lamps.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Cleanse treatment areas thoroughly before application. Avoid excessive washing; twice daily with mild soap is sufficient.</li><li>Dry the skin gently without rubbing.</li><li>Avoid contact with eyes, eyelids, nostrils, mouth, and mucous membranes. If contact occurs, rinse with water.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Vesanoid 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Irritation, erythema, peeling, and warmth. Slight stinging may occur but usually subsides with continued use or reduced application frequency.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> True allergic contact dermatitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in pregnancy or suspected pregnancy.</li><li>Avoid use during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place away from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '156_1742255422.jpg', 10, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 04:50:22', '2025-03-18 04:50:22'),
(3204, 'Ximeclav', 'Cefuroxime Axetil plus Clavulanic Acid', 57.50, 6, 'Tablet', '6 Tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg +125 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ximeclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p>Ximeclav 500 mg+125 mg is indicated for treating infections caused by sensitive bacteria, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong> due to <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial otitis media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non beta-lactamase producing strains)</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections, including pneumonia</strong>, caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp., Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, and <em>E. coli</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis and secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains), or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains)</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin-structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></li><li><strong>Bone and joint infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong> caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase-producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease (erythema migrans)</strong> caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em></li><li><strong>Septicemia</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pneumoniae, E. coli, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), and <em>Klebsiella spp.</em></li><li><strong>Meningitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), <em>Neisseria meningitidis</em>, and <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Switch therapy (injectable to oral)</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime is a bactericidal second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic that targets a broad spectrum of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including many beta-lactamase-producing strains. It inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by interfering with the transpeptidation process.</p><p>Clavulanic acid is a beta-lactamase inhibitor derived from <em>Streptomyces clavuligerus</em>. It irreversibly binds to beta-lactamase enzymes, inactivating them and protecting cefuroxime from degradation. This combination enhances the drug’s efficacy against beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Ximeclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adolescents and Adults (13 years and older):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis:</strong> 250 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infections:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections:</strong> 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea:</strong> 1000 mg as a single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>MDR Typhoid Fever:</strong> 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Early Lyme disease:</strong> 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis:</strong> 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute otitis media:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Impetigo:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Ximeclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p>Ximeclav 500 mg+125 mg tablet may be taken with or without food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Ximeclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Concurrent use of <strong>probenecid</strong> significantly increases cefuroxime-clavulanic acid serum levels by 50%.</li><li>Medications that reduce <strong>gastric acidity</strong> may lower cefuroxime bioavailability, potentially reducing its efficacy.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with a <strong>known allergy to cephalosporins</strong>.</li><li>Patients with <strong>pseudomembranous colitis</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ximeclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p>Generally well tolerated, but potential side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal discomfort.</li><li><strong>Prolonged use:</strong> Overgrowth of nonsusceptible microorganisms.</li><li><strong>Rare (&lt;0.2%):</strong> Renal dysfunction, anaphylaxis, angioedema, pruritus, rash, urticaria (serum sickness-like reaction).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid use in the <strong>first trimester</strong> of pregnancy.</li><li>Can be used in later stages for urinary and other infections.</li><li>Excreted in <strong>small amounts in breast milk</strong>; consider potential infant sensitization.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use <strong>caution in patients receiving potent diuretics</strong>.</li><li>Patients with a <strong>history of colitis</strong> should use with care.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cefuroxime:</strong> A bactericidal cephalosporin that disrupts bacterial cell wall synthesis by interfering with the transpeptidation process.</li><li><strong>Clavulanic Acid:</strong> A beta-lactamase inhibitor that prevents degradation of cefuroxime, enhancing its effectiveness against beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</li></ul>', 'active', '1902_1742255831.jpg', 64, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 04:57:11', '2025-05-07 20:12:28'),
(3205, 'Winop', 'Ketorolac Tromethamine', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Winop 10 mg</strong></p><p> Winop 10 mg is indicated for the short-term management of moderate to severe acute post-operative pain.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-Opioid Analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Winop 10 mg is a potent analgesic of the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). It acts by inhibiting the cyclooxygenase enzyme system and hence inhibits prostaglandin synthesis. It demonstrates a minimal anti-inflammatory effect at its analgesic dose.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Winop 10 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Tablet:</strong> The recommended dose is 10 mg every 4-6 hours. It should be used short-term only (up to 7 days) and is not recommended for chronic use. Doses exceeding 40 mg/day are not recommended.</p><p><strong>Injection:</strong> Ketorolac injection may be used as a single or multiple doses, on a regular or as-needed schedule for the management of moderately severe, acute pain that requires analgesia at the opioid level, usually in a postoperative setting.</p><ul><li>When administering Ketorolac injection, the IV bolus must be given over no less than 15 seconds.</li><li>The IM administration should be given slowly and deeply into the muscle.</li><li>The analgesic effect begins within 30 minutes, with maximum effect in 1 to 2 hours after dosing IV or IM.</li><li>Duration of analgesic effect is usually 4 to 6 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Single-Dose Treatment</strong></p><p><strong>IM Dosing (Adult):</strong></p><ul><li>Patients &lt;65 years of age: One dose of 60 mg.</li><li>Patients &gt;65 years of age, renally impaired, and/or less than 50 kg body weight: One dose of 30 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>IV Dosing (Adult):</strong></p><ul><li>Patients &lt;65 years of age: One dose of 30 mg.</li><li>Patients &gt;65 years of age, renally impaired, and/or less than 50 kg body weight: One dose of 15 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>IV or IM Dosing (2 to 16 years of age):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IM Dosing:</strong> One dose of 1 mg/kg up to a maximum of 30 mg.</li><li><strong>IV Dosing:</strong> One dose of 0.5 mg/kg up to a maximum of 15 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Multiple-Dose Treatment (IV or IM)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Patients &lt;65 years of age:</strong> The recommended dose is 30 mg Ketorolac injection every 6 hours. The maximum daily dose should not exceed 120 mg.</li><li><strong>Patients &gt;65 years of age, renally impaired patients, and patients less than 50 kg:</strong> The recommended dose is 15 mg Ketorolac injection every 6 hours. The maximum daily dose for these populations should not exceed 60 mg.</li></ul><p>For breakthrough pain, do not increase the dose or the frequency of Winop 10 mg.</p><p><strong>Conversion from Parenteral to Oral Therapy</strong></p><p> Ketorolac tablets may be used either as monotherapy or as follow-on therapy to parenteral Ketorolac.</p><ul><li>When switching from parenteral to oral Ketorolac, the total combined daily dose (oral + parenteral) should not exceed 120 mg in younger adults or 60 mg in elderly patients on the day of formulation change.</li><li>On subsequent days, oral dosing should not exceed the recommended daily maximum of 40 mg.</li><li>Ketorolac IM should be replaced by Ketorolac tablet as soon as feasible.</li><li>The total duration of combined parenteral and oral treatment should not exceed 5 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Winop 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Other NSAIDs or aspirin:</strong> Increases the side effects of Ketorolac Tromethamine.</li><li><strong>Anti-coagulants:</strong> Enhances anti-coagulant effect.</li><li><strong>Beta Blockers:</strong> Reduces the anti-hypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>ACE Inhibitors:</strong> Increases the risk of renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate:</strong> Enhances the toxicity of Methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Ketorolac is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to this drug or other NSAIDs. It should not be used in children under 16 years of age. It is also contraindicated as a prophylactic analgesic before surgery.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Winop 10 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include nausea, vomiting, gastrointestinal bleeding, melena, peptic ulcer, pancreatitis, anxiety, drowsiness, headache, excessive thirst, fatigue, bradycardia, hypertension, palpitation, chest pain, infertility in females, and pulmonary edema.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> US FDA Pregnancy Category C. Winop 10 mg should be avoided during pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be exercised in patients over the age of 65 years.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with active or suspected peptic ulcer, gastrointestinal bleeding, asthma, and liver dysfunction.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1482_1742256034.jpg', 5, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 05:00:34', '2025-03-18 05:00:34'),
(3206, 'Velpacee', 'Sofosbuvir plus Velpatasvir', 930.00, 7, 'Tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '400 mg plus 100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Velpacee 400 mg + 100 mg</strong></p><p> Sofosbuvir and Velpatasvir combination is indicated for the treatment of adult patients with chronic hepatitis C virus (HCV) genotype 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, or 6 infection:</p><ul><li>Without cirrhosis or with compensated cirrhosis</li><li>With decompensated cirrhosis for use in combination with ribavirin</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Hepatic viral infections (Hepatitis C)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Velpacee is a fixed-dose combination tablet containing Sofosbuvir and Velpatasvir for oral administration.</p><ul><li><strong>Sofosbuvir</strong> is a nucleotide analog HCV NS5B polymerase inhibitor.</li><li><strong>Velpatasvir</strong> is an NS5A inhibitor.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Velpacee 400 mg + 100 mg</strong></p><p> The recommended dosage is one tablet (400 mg of Sofosbuvir and 100 mg of Velpatasvir) taken orally once daily.</p><p><strong>Recommended treatment regimen:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Patients without cirrhosis and patients with compensated cirrhosis (Child-Pugh A):</strong> One tablet once daily for 12 weeks.</li><li><strong>Patients with decompensated cirrhosis (Child-Pugh B or C):</strong> One tablet once daily and Ribavirin for 12 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Ribavirin dosage based on body weight:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>&lt;75 kg:</strong> 1000 mg/day in two divided doses.</li><li><strong>≥75 kg:</strong> 1200 mg/day in two divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Velpacee 400 mg + 100 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Drugs that may decrease the concentrations of Sofosbuvir and/or Velpatasvir:</strong></p><ul><li>Antacids</li><li>H2-receptor antagonists</li><li>Proton-pump inhibitors</li></ul><p><strong>Coadministration is not recommended with:</strong></p><ul><li>Topotecan</li><li>Carbamazepine</li><li>Phenytoin</li><li>Phenobarbital</li><li>Oxcarbazepine</li><li>Rifabutin</li><li>Rifampin</li><li>Rifapentine</li><li>Efavirenz</li><li>Tipranavir</li><li>Ritonavir</li><li>Hypericum perforatum</li></ul><p><strong>Coadministration with Rosuvastatin and Atorvastatin</strong> may significantly increase the concentration of these statins.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Sofosbuvir, Velpatasvir, and Ribavirin combination regimen is contraindicated in patients for whom Ribavirin is contraindicated.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Velpacee 400 mg + 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li>The most common side effects include headache and tiredness.</li><li>Treatment may result in slowing of the heart rate along with other symptoms when taken with amiodarone (a medicine used to treat certain heart problems).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>No adequate human data are available to establish whether Sofosbuvir and Velpatasvir combination poses a risk to pregnancy outcomes.</li><li>If administered with Ribavirin, the combination regimen is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in pregnant women and in men whose female partner is pregnant or planning pregnancy in the next six months.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Serious symptomatic bradycardia may occur in patients taking amiodarone, particularly in those also receiving beta blockers, or those with underlying cardiac comorbidities and/or advanced liver disease.</li><li>Coadministration of amiodarone with Sofosbuvir and Velpatasvir combination is <strong>not recommended</strong>.</li><li>In patients without alternative viable treatment options, cardiac monitoring is recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Velpacee 400 mg + 100 mg</strong></p><p> If overdose occurs, the patient must be monitored for evidence of toxicity. Treatment of overdose includes monitoring of vital signs and observation of the clinical status of the patient.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place (preferably below 30°C). Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment patients:</strong> No dosage recommendation can be given for patients with severe renal impairment (eGFR ≤30 mL/min/1.73 m²) or ESRD, due to higher exposures of the predominant Sofosbuvir metabolite.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Hepatic viral infections (Hepatitis C)</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Velpacee is a fixed-dose combination tablet containing Sofosbuvir and Velpatasvir for oral administration.</p><ul><li><strong>Sofosbuvir</strong> is a nucleotide analog HCV NS5B polymerase inhibitor.</li><li><strong>Velpatasvir</strong> is an NS5A inhibitor.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment patients:</strong> No dosage recommendation can be given for patients with severe renal impairment (eGFR ≤30 mL/min/1.73 m²) or ESRD, due to higher exposures of the predominant Sofosbuvir metabolite.</li></ul>', 'active', '422_1742256365.jpg', 63, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 05:06:05', '2025-03-18 05:06:05'),
(3207, 'Unifarin', 'Warfarin Sodium', 2.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Unifarin 2.5 mg</strong></p><p> Unifarin 2.5 mg is indicated in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and/or treatment of thromboembolic complications associated with atrial fibrillation and/or cardiac valve replacement</li><li>Indicated to reduce the risk of death, recurrent myocardial infarction, and thromboembolic events such as stroke or systemic embolization after myocardial infarction</li><li>Prophylaxis and treatment of venous thrombosis and pulmonary embolism</li><li>Transient ischaemic attacks</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-coagulants, Oral Anti-coagulants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Unifarin 2.5 mg inhibits synthesis of vit K-dependent coagulation factors II, VII, IX, and X as well as the anticoagulant protein C and its cofactor protein S. These clotting factors are biologically activated by the addition of carboxyl groups to key glutamic acid residues within the proteins\' structure. Unifarin 2.5 mg competitively inhibits the C1 subunit of the multi-unit vit K epoxide reductase (VKORC1) enzyme complex, thus depleting functional vit K reserves and reducing synthesis of active clotting factors.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Unifarin 2.5 mg</strong></p><p> Whenever possible, the baseline prothrombin time should be determined, but the initial dose should not be delayed whilst awaiting the result. The usual adult induction dose of Unifarin 2.5 mg is 10 mg daily for 2 days. The subsequent maintenance dose depends upon the prothrombin time, reported as INR (international normalized ratio). The daily maintenance dose of Unifarin 2.5 mg is usually 3 to 9 mg (taken at the same time each day). The maintenance dose is omitted if the prothrombin time is excessively prolonged. Once the maintenance dose is established in the therapeutic range, it is rarely necessary to alter. In emergencies, anticoagulant therapy should be initiated with heparin and Unifarin 2.5 mg together. Where there is less urgency, as in patients disposed to or at special risk of thromboembolism, anticoagulant therapy may be initiated with Unifarin 2.5 mg alone. Control tests must be made at regular intervals and maintenance dosage further adjusted according to the results obtained.</p><p> <strong>Use in children:</strong> Safety and efficacy in children &lt;18 years old have not been established. However, there is evidence of use, and the initial dose is usually 0.1 mg.kg-1.d-1 adjusted subsequently to aim for an INR range the same as in adults.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Unifarin 2.5 mg</strong></p><p> Oral anticoagulants have a greater potential for clinically significant drug interactions. Unifarin 2.5 mg all patients about potential hazards and instruct against taking any drug, including non-prescription products, without the advice of a physician.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Actual or potential haemorrhagic conditions, e.g. Peptic ulcer, or to patients with uncontrolled hypertension</li><li>Severe hepatic or renal disease</li><li>Pregnancy</li><li>Known hypersensitivity to Unifarin 2.5 mg</li><li>Bacterial endocarditis</li><li>Its use within 24 hours following surgery or labour should be undertaken with caution, if at all.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Unifarin 2.5 mg</strong></p><p> Hemorrhage is the principal adverse effect of oral anticoagulants. Other adverse reactions include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Hypersensitivity</li><li>Rash</li><li>Alopecia</li><li>Unexplained drop in hematocrit</li><li>\"Purple toes\"</li><li>Skin necrosis</li><li>Jaundice</li><li>Hepatic dysfunction</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Unifarin 2.5 mg is contraindicated in the first trimester of pregnancy because of the risk of teratogenicity. It should not be used in women who are or may become pregnant because the drug passes through the placental barrier and may cause fatal hemorrhage to the fetus. Unifarin 2.5 mg appears in the milk of nursing mothers in an inactive form. Infants nursed by mothers treated with Unifarin 2.5 mg had no change in prothrombin times. Effects in premature infants have not been evaluated.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Periodic determination of prothrombin time (PT)/international normalized ratio (INR) or other suitable coagulation test is essential. Numerous factors, alone or in combination, including travel, changes in diet, environment, physical state, and medication may influence response of the patient to anticoagulants. It is generally good practice to monitor the patient\'s response with additional PT/INR determination in the period immediately after discharge from the hospital, and whenever other medications are initiated, discontinued, or taken irregularly. The following factors may exaggerate the effects of Unifarin 2.5 mg and necessitate a reduction in dosage:</p><ul><li>Loss of weight</li><li>Elderly subject</li><li>Acute illness</li><li>Deficient renal function</li><li>Decreased dietary intake of vitamin K</li><li>Administration of certain drugs (see drug interaction)</li></ul><p>Factors which may call for an increase in maintenance dosage include:</p><ul><li>Weight gain</li><li>Diarrhea and vomiting</li><li>Increased intake of vitamin K, fats, and oils</li><li>The administration of certain drugs (see drug interaction)</li></ul><p>Careful additional laboratory control is necessary if the patient is to be changed from one formulation to another. Reversal of Unifarin 2.5 mg anticoagulation by vitamin K takes several days. In emergency situations, fresh frozen plasma should be given.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Unifarin 2.5 mg</strong></p><p> If hemorrhage occurs or a potential bleeding state arises, excessive depression of the coagulation activity can be corrected by temporary withdrawal of Unifarin 2.5 mg, accompanied, if necessary, by infusion of fresh-frozen plasma or whole blood. Vitamin K, 5 to 10 mg orally or intravenously, may be required to supplement specific treatment with factor concentrates.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Replace cap securely and protect from light.</p>', 'active', '866_1742256741.jpg', 8, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 05:12:21', '2025-03-18 05:12:21');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3208, 'Ranocor ER', 'Ranolazine', 16.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ranocor ER 500 mg</strong></p><p> Ranocor ER 500 mg is indicated for the treatment of chronic angina. It may be used with beta-blockers, nitrates, calcium channel blockers, anti-platelet therapy, lipid-lowering therapy, ACE inhibitors, and angiotensin receptor blockers. It has been shown to decrease angina episodes in patients with coronary artery disease on maximal doses of amlodipine. Because Ranocor ER 500 mg prolongs the QT interval, it should be reserved for patients who have not achieved an adequate response with other antianginal drugs. The effect on angina rate or exercise tolerance appeared to be smaller in women than men.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Ranocor ER 500 mg has anti-ischemic and antianginal effects that do not depend upon reductions in heart rate or blood pressure. The exact mechanism of action of Ranocor ER 500 mg is unknown. At therapeutic levels, it can inhibit the cardiac late sodium current (INa), though the relationship of this inhibition to angina symptoms is uncertain. The QT prolongation effect on the electrocardiogram results from inhibition of IKr, which prolongs the ventricular action potential.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ranocor ER 500 mg</strong></p><p> Initiate Ranocor ER 500 mg dosing at 500 mg twice daily and increase to 1000 mg twice daily, if needed, based on clinical symptoms. Take Ranocor ER 500 mg with or without meals. Swallow the tablets whole; do not crush, break, or chew. The maximum recommended daily dose is 1000 mg twice daily. If a dose is missed, take the prescribed dose at the next scheduled time; do not double the next dose.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Ranocor ER 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CYP 3A Inhibitors:</strong> Do not use Ranocor ER 500 mg with strong CYP 3A inhibitors. With moderate CYP 3A inhibitors (e.g., diltiazem, verapamil, erythromycin), limit the maximum dose to 500 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>CYP 3A Inducers:</strong> Do not use Ranocor ER 500 mg with inducers.</li><li><strong>P-gp Inhibitors (e.g., Cyclosporin):</strong> May need to lower the Ranocor ER 500 mg dose based on clinical response.</li><li><strong>Drugs transported by P-gp or metabolized by CYP2D6 (e.g., digoxin, TCA):</strong> May need reduced doses of these drugs when used with Ranocor ER 500 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Ranocor ER 500 mg is contraindicated in patients:</p><ul><li>With pre-existing QT prolongation</li><li>With hepatic impairment</li><li>Taking QT prolonging drugs</li><li>Taking potent and moderately potent CYP3A inhibitors such as ketoconazole, itraconazole, clarithromycin, nefazodone, nelfinavir, ritonavir, indinavir, saquinavir, and diltiazem</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ranocor ER 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cardiac Disorders:</strong> Bradycardia, palpitations</li><li><strong>Ear and Labyrinth Disorders:</strong> Tinnitus, vertigo</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal Disorders:</strong> Abdominal pain, dry mouth, vomiting</li><li><strong>General Disorders and Administration Site Reactions:</strong> Peripheral edema</li><li><strong>Respiratory, Thoracic, and Mediastinal Disorders:</strong> Dyspnea</li><li><strong>Vascular Disorders:</strong> Hypotension, orthostatic hypotension</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> <strong>Pregnancy Category C:</strong> There are no adequate studies assessing the effect of Ranocor ER 500 mg on the developing fetus. It should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk. It is not known whether Ranocor ER 500 mg is excreted in human milk. Because of the risk of serious adverse reactions in nursing infants, a decision should be made to either discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, considering its importance to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Ranocor ER 500 mg blocks QTc and prolongs the QTc interval in a dose-related manner. Clinical experience in an acute coronary syndrome population did not show an increased risk of proarrhythmia or sudden death. Co-administration with digoxin increases plasma concentrations of digoxin by approximately 1.5-fold, and the digoxin dose may need to be reduced accordingly. The dose of other P-gp substrates may also need to be reduced when co-administered with Ranocor ER 500 mg. Caution should be exercised when co-administering Ranocor ER 500 mg with P-gp inhibitors such as ritonavir or cyclosporine.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ranocor ER 500 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Symptoms:</strong> Dizziness, nausea, vomiting, diplopia, lethargy, syncope, severe tremor, incoordination, dysplasia, hallucination.</p><p> <strong>Management:</strong> Symptomatic and supportive treatment.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store Ranocor ER 500 mg tablets at 25°C with excursions permitted to 15° to 30°C. Protect from light and moisture.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong>Mild to moderate (CrCl 30-80 mL/min): Dose titration needed</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe (CrCl &lt;30 mL/min): Contraindicated</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong>Mild: Dose titration needed</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Moderate to severe: Contraindicated</li></ul>', 'active', '1590_1742256974.jpg', 17, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 05:16:14', '2025-03-18 05:16:14'),
(3209, 'Periset', 'Ondansetron', 45.00, 1, 'Bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Solution', '4 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Periset 4 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Periset 4 mg/5 ml is a serotonin subtype 3 (5-HT3) receptor antagonist indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prevention of nausea and vomiting associated with initial and repeat courses of emetogenic cancer chemotherapy.</li><li>Prevention and treatment of post-operative nausea and vomiting.</li><li>Prevention of radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-emetic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Periset 4 mg/5 ml is a selective 5-HT3 receptor antagonist. While its mechanism of action has not been fully characterized, Periset 4 mg/5 ml is not a dopamine-receptor antagonist. Serotonin receptors of the 5-HT3 type are present both peripherally on vagal nerve terminals and centrally in the chemoreceptor trigger zone of the area postrema. It is not certain whether Periset 4 mg/5 ml’s antiemetic action is mediated centrally, peripherally, or in both sites. However, cytotoxic chemotherapy appears to be associated with the release of serotonin from the enterochromaffin cells of the small intestine.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Periset 4 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Prevention of chemotherapy-induced nausea &amp; vomiting (CINV)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet and oral solution:</strong> The recommended adult oral dosage is 24 mg given as three 8 mg tablets in highly emetogenic chemotherapy. For moderately emetogenic chemotherapy, the oral dose is one 8 mg tablet or 10 ml of the oral solution given twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection:</strong> A single 32 mg dose or three 0.15 mg/kg doses. A single 32 mg dose is infused over 15 minutes, starting 30 minutes before chemotherapy. Subsequent doses (0.15 mg/kg) are administered 4 and 8 hours after the first dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppository:</strong> One 16 mg suppository 1-2 hours before treatment, continued for up to 5 days.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients (4-11 years)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet and oral solution:</strong> One 4 mg tablet or 5 ml of solution administered 3 times a day for 1-2 days after chemotherapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection:</strong> Three 0.15 mg/kg doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppository:</strong> Not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> One 8 mg tablet or 10 ml oral solution given 3 times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Post-operative nausea &amp; vomiting</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet and oral solution:</strong> 16 mg (two 8 mg tablets or 20 ml solution) 1 hour before anesthesia.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection:</strong> 4 mg undiluted intravenously over 30 seconds to 5 minutes before anesthesia or postoperatively if needed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppository:</strong> One 16 mg suppository 1-2 hours before treatment, continued for up to 5 days.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients (2-12 years)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection:</strong> 0.1 mg/kg IV for patients ≤40 kg or a single 4 mg IV dose for those &gt;40 kg.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Periset 4 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Chemotherapy-Induced Nausea and Vomiting</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults, Pediatric (6 months-18 years): Three 0.15 mg/kg doses, up to 16 mg per dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Injection: Three 0.15 mg/kg doses, up to 16 mg per dose, infused over 15 minutes.</li><li><strong>Radiotherapy-Induced Nausea and Vomiting</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 8 mg orally 1-2 hours before radiotherapy, followed by 8 mg every 8 hours for up to 5 days.</li><li><strong>Postoperative Nausea and Vomiting</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 16 mg (two 8 mg tablets) orally.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pediatric (&gt;40 kg): 4 mg injection; ≤40 kg: 0.1 mg/kg injection.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Periset 4 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ol><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Oral Soluble Film Administration:</strong>Tear the pouch carefully.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Place the film on the tongue; it dissolves within 20 seconds.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not chew or swallow the film whole.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Swallow after dissolution, with or without liquid.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Wash hands after taking the film.</li></ol><p><strong>Interaction of Periset 4 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Periset 4 mg/5 ml does not induce or inhibit liver cytochrome P-450 enzymes. However, inducers or inhibitors of these enzymes may affect its clearance and half-life. No dosage adjustment is recommended.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Periset 4 mg/5 ml or any of its components.</li><li>Concomitant use with apomorphine.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Periset 4 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Common side effects include headache, constipation, diarrhea, rash, flushing, warmth sensation, hiccups, and liver enzyme abnormalities. Rare effects include anaphylaxis, bronchospasm, tachycardia, angina, hypokalemia, and shortness of breath. No evidence of extrapyramidal reactions except for rare oculogyric crises.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy (Category B):</strong> No evidence of fetal harm in animal studies, but should be used only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Excreted in animal milk; use with caution in nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity reactions may occur in patients allergic to other 5-HT3 receptor antagonists.</li><li>Does not stimulate gastric or intestinal peristalsis; should not replace nasogastric suction.</li><li>May mask ileus or gastric distension in post-abdominal surgery or chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Periset 4 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> No specific antidote. Overdose symptoms include hypotension and faintness. Events typically resolve completely.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30ºC in a dry place. Protect from light and moisture.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Can be given to children ≥1 month old.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Patients with Hepatic Impairment:Tablet/Oral Solution:</strong> Do not exceed 8 mg per day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection:</strong> A single maximal dose of 8 mg infused over 15 minutes before chemotherapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppository:</strong> Not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p> Dilute in 50 ml dextrose 5% injection or normal saline before IV infusion.</p><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Anti-emetic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Periset 4 mg/5 ml is a potent, selective 5HT3 receptor antagonist. It blocks serotonin-induced activation of vagal afferents, preventing nausea and vomiting triggered by chemotherapy, radiotherapy, or surgery.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> No carcinogenic effects were observed in 2-year animal studies. No evidence of fetal harm in animal studies, but human data is insufficient. Use with caution in pregnant women and nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Maximum daily dose of 8 mg recommended.</li><li><strong>Children &lt;4 years:</strong> Limited data available.</li><li><strong>Elderly (&gt;65 years):</strong> No dosage adjustment needed.</li></ul>', 'active', '1172_1742257331.jpg', 5, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 05:22:11', '2025-03-18 05:22:11'),
(3210, 'Orili', 'Elagolix Sodium', 170.00, 6, 'Tablet', '6 Tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '150 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Orili 150 mg</strong></p><p> Elagolix is indicated for the management of moderate to severe pain associated with endometriosis.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Orili 150 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Route of Administration:</strong> Should be taken orally.</p><p> <strong>Dosage:</strong> Pregnancy should be excluded before starting Elagolix and starting Elagolix within 7 days from the onset of menses. Elagolix should be taken at approximately the same time each day, with or without food. Lowest effective dose, taking into account the severity of symptoms and treatment objectives. Initial treatment with Elagolix 150 mg once daily up to 24 months. But in case of moderate hepatic impairment up to 6 months.</p><p> <strong>Use in children and adolescents:</strong> &lt;18 years not established.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Orili 150 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>With medicine:</strong> May potentiate P-gp substrates (e.g. digoxin), CYP2C19 substrates (e.g. omeprazole; limit doses to &lt;40 mg daily). May antagonize CYP3A substrates. Antagonizes oral midazolam, rosuvastatin; consider increasing their doses. May be antagonized by CYP3A inducers. Reduced efficacy with estrogen-containing contraceptives.</p><p> <strong>With food and others:</strong> No known food interaction.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> It is contraindicated in pregnant women because exposure to Elagolix early in pregnancy may increase the risk of early pregnancy loss. With known osteoporosis, it can increase the risk of further bone loss. It is also contraindicated for severe hepatic impairment and also with concomitant use of strong organic anion transporting polypeptide OATP1B1 inhibitors (e.g. cyclosporine and gemfibrozil).</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Orili 150 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Common:</strong> The most common side effects include hot flushes, night sweats, headache, nausea, amenorrhea, arthralgia, mood changes &amp; bone loss.</p><p> <strong>Rare:</strong> Insomnia, anxiety &amp; elevated hepatic transaminase.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Bone Loss:</strong> Dose and duration-dependent decreases in bone mineral density (BMD) that may not be completely reversible. Assess BMD in women with additional risk factors for bone loss.</li><li><strong>Reduced Ability to Recognize Pregnancy:</strong> Elagolix may alter menstrual bleeding, which may reduce the ability to recognize pregnancy. Perform testing if pregnancy is suspected. Discontinue if pregnancy is confirmed.</li><li><strong>Suicidal Ideation and Mood Disorders:</strong> Advise patients to seek medical attention for suicidal ideation, suicidal behavior, new onset or worsening depression, anxiety, or other mood changes.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Transaminase Elevations:</strong> Dose-dependent elevations in serum alanine aminotransferase (ALT). Counsel patients on signs and symptoms of liver injury.</li><li><strong>Potential for Reduced Efficacy with Estrogen-Containing Contraceptives:</strong> Use non-hormonal contraception during treatment and for one week after discontinuing Elagolix.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Orili 150 mg</strong></p><p> In case of overdose, patients should be monitored for any signs or symptoms of adverse reactions and initiate appropriate symptomatic treatment, as needed.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool &amp; dry place, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep all medicines out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) antagonist</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Elagolix is a GnRH receptor antagonist that inhibits endogenous GnRH signaling by binding competitively to GnRH receptors in the pituitary gland. Administration of Elagolix results in dose-dependent suppression of luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), leading to decreased blood concentrations of the ovarian sex hormones, estradiol and progesterone.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The limited human data with the use of Elagolix in pregnant women are insufficient to determine whether there is a risk for major birth defects or miscarriage. Exposure to Elagolix early in pregnancy may increase the risk of early pregnancy loss. Use of it is contraindicated in pregnant women. Elagolix has to be discontinued if pregnancy occurs during treatment. There is no information on the presence of elagolix or its metabolites in human milk, the effects on the breastfed child or the effects on milk production.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> No dose adjustment of Elagolix is required in women with any degree of renal impairment or end-stage renal disease (including women on dialysis). No dosage adjustment of Elagolix is required in women with mild hepatic impairment. Safety and effectiveness of Elagolix in patients less than 18 years of age have not been established.</p>', 'active', '848_1742257548.jpg', 4, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 05:25:48', '2025-03-18 05:25:48'),
(3211, 'Phenadryl', 'Diphenhydramine Hydrochloride', 45.00, 1, 'Bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '10 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Phenadryl 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Diphenhydramine is indicated for the treatment of the following:</p><ul><li>Seasonal, perennial, vasomotor rhinitis</li><li>Urticaria, angioneurotic oedema, anaphylaxis</li><li>Pruritic conditions</li><li>Premedication for emesis and motion sickness</li><li>Miscellaneous conditions like Meniere\'s disease and Parkinsonism</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Sedating Antihistamine</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Diphenhydramine is an antihistamine with anticholinergic and sedative effects. It competes with histamine for H1-receptor sites on effector cells in the GI tract, blood vessels, and respiratory tract.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Phenadryl 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults:</strong> Most allergic conditions: 25-50 mg three times a day with a further 50 mg at night.</p><p> <strong>Children:</strong></p><ul><li>1 to 5 years: 5 mg (2.5 ml of elixir) 4 times a day</li><li>More than 6 years: 10 mg (5 ml of elixir) 4 times a day</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Phenadryl 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Diphenhydramine administration significantly reduces the absorption of the antituberculous agent para-aminosalicyclic acid (PAS) from the gastrointestinal tract.</p><ul><li>CNS depressants may potentiate the sedative action of Diphenhydramine.</li><li>Anticholinergic drugs may potentiate Diphenhydramine’s anticholinergic side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Known hypersensitivity to Phenadryl 10 mg/5 ml. Ammonium chloride is contraindicated in the presence of impaired hepatic or renal function.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Phenadryl 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Side effects include sedation, dizziness, tinnitus, fatigue, ataxia, blurred vision, diplopia, euphoria, and epigastric discomfort.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> <strong>Category B:</strong> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women using Phenadryl 10 mg/5 ml. Therefore, Phenadryl 10 mg/5 ml should be used in pregnancy only if clearly needed. Diphenhydramine hydrochloride has been reported to be excreted in breast milk, and thus, use in lactating mothers is not recommended.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Caution should be exercised in patients where drowsiness is undesirable, e.g., drivers and machine operators. Concomitant consumption of alcohol or central nervous system (CNS) depressants will potentiate drowsiness.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Phenadryl 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Symptoms:</strong> Impaired consciousness, psychosis, seizures, antimuscarinic symptoms (e.g., mydriasis, tachycardia, tachyarrhythmias), respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis, and acute delirium with visual and auditory hallucinations (topical).</p><p> <strong>Management:</strong> Supportive and symptomatic treatment. Convulsions and marked CNS stimulation may be treated with IV diazepam.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store between 15-30°C. Protect from moisture.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Sedating Antihistamine</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Diphenhydramine is an antihistamine with anticholinergic and sedative effects. It competes with histamine for H1-receptor sites on effector cells in the GI tract, blood vessels, and respiratory tract.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> <strong>Category B:</strong> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women using Phenadryl 10 mg/5 ml. Therefore, Phenadryl 10 mg/5 ml should be used in pregnancy only if clearly needed. Diphenhydramine hydrochloride has been reported to be excreted in breast milk, and thus, use in lactating mothers is not recommended.</p>', 'active', '743_1742257828.jpg', 5, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 05:30:28', '2025-03-18 05:30:28'),
(3212, 'Cranmax', 'Cranberry [Vaccinium macrocarpon]', 25.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cranmax 500 mg</strong></p><p>Cranberry is indicated in the treatment and prevention of urinary tract infection (UTI) and kidney stones.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Herbal and Nutraceuticals</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cranberry (Vaccinium macrocarpon) is a native North American plant commonly used for reducing the occurrence of urinary tract infections (UTIs) and in kidney stone management. Cranberry reduces the need for repeated antibiotic use in the treatment of recurrent UTIs. Bacterial adherence to mucosal surfaces is generally considered to be the initial event in the pathogenesis of most infectious diseases caused by bacteria in humans, such as UTIs. Cranberry inhibits the adherence of bacteria to the lining of the urinary bladder and urethra. At higher doses, it also works as a urinary antiseptic and reduces the incidence of stone formation. Recent studies show as much as a 65% reduction in UTIs with cranberry consumption, leading to reduced use of antibiotics and a decrease in the number of recurrent cystitis incidences.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cranmax 500 mg</strong></p><p>1-3 capsules daily or as advised by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Cranmax 500 mg</strong></p><p>No known interactions with antibiotics or other drugs. Cranberry enhances vitamin B12 absorption, which is useful for patients taking omeprazole, a drug used to treat ulcers.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Potential contraindications of cranberry may be present with renal insufficiency and in persons with the potential for developing uric acid or calcium oxalate stones.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Cranmax 500 mg</strong></p><p>Well tolerated at the recommended dose. Occasionally, diarrhea or mild gastrointestinal upset may occur at high doses.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>No known restrictions during pregnancy or lactation.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1861_1742272695.jpg', 50, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 09:38:15', '2025-05-03 01:42:49'),
(3213, 'Alzacare', 'Memantine + Donepezil', 20.00, 6, 'capsule', '6 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '7 mg plus 10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Alzacare 7 mg + 10 mg:</strong></p><p>Alzacare (memantine and donepezil hydrochlorides extended-release capsules) is indicated for the treatment of moderate to severe dementia of the Alzheimer’s type in patients who are stabilized on 10 mg of donepezil hydrochloride once daily.</p><p><strong>Composition:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>7/10 Capsule</strong>: Contains Memantine Hydrochloride (7 mg) and Donepezil Hydrochloride (10 mg).</li><li><strong>14/10 Capsule</strong>: Contains Memantine Hydrochloride (14 mg) and Donepezil Hydrochloride (10 mg).</li><li><strong>21/10 Capsule</strong>: Contains Memantine Hydrochloride (21 mg) and Donepezil Hydrochloride (10 mg).</li><li><strong>28/10 Capsule</strong>: Contains Memantine Hydrochloride (28 mg) and Donepezil Hydrochloride (10 mg).</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Starting dose</strong>: For patients stabilized on 10 mg of donepezil, the starting dose is 7 mg/10 mg, taken once daily in the evening.</li><li><strong>Maintenance dose</strong>: The dose can be increased by 7 mg increments to the recommended maintenance dose of 28 mg/10 mg, with a minimum of one week between dose increases.</li><li><strong>Switching</strong>: Patients on 10 mg twice daily of memantine or 28 mg extended-release once daily, and 10 mg of donepezil, can switch to 28 mg/10 mg of the extended-release capsule.</li><li><strong>Severe renal impairment</strong>: For patients with severe renal impairment, the recommended maintenance dose is 14 mg/10 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Combined use with NMDA antagonists</strong>: Use with caution.</li><li><strong>Anticholinergic medications</strong>: May interfere with memantine and donepezil hydrochlorides extended-release capsules.</li><li><strong>Succinylcholine or neuromuscular blocking agents</strong>: Caution is advised when used together, as synergistic effects may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to memantine hydrochloride, donepezil hydrochloride, piperidine derivatives, or any excipients in the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects (≥5% of patients): Headache, diarrhea, dizziness, anorexia, vomiting, nausea, and ecchymosis.</li><li><strong>More severe effects</strong>: Gastrointestinal bleeding, bradycardia, heart block, diarrhea, bladder outflow obstruction, and nausea/vomiting.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Can exaggerate succinylcholine-type muscle relaxation during anesthesia.</li><li>May cause vagotonic effects on the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes (bradycardia, heart block).</li><li>Monitor for gastrointestinal bleeding, especially in high-risk individuals.</li><li>Can cause bladder outflow obstructions, nausea, diarrhea, and vomiting.</li><li>Conditions that increase urine pH can reduce the elimination of memantine, leading to higher plasma levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture, and keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Anti-Alzheimer drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Memantine</strong>: Works as a low to moderate affinity uncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonist, which is thought to reduce the pathological effects of excessive glutamate in Alzheimer’s disease.</li><li><strong>Donepezil</strong>: Enhances cholinergic neurotransmission by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase, which breaks down acetylcholine, thereby improving cognition in Alzheimer’s patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy and Breastfeeding:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: No adequate data on the developmental risk associated with the use of memantine and donepezil hydrochlorides in pregnant women.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: No data available on the presence of memantine or donepezil in human milk or their effects on the infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal and Hepatic Impairment:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed for mild or moderate renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed for mild or moderate hepatic impairment. Not studied in patients with severe hepatic impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '3712_1746124003.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 09:42:18', '2025-05-01 22:26:43'),
(3214, 'Mexclav', 'Cefuroxime Axetil + Clavulanic Acid', 60.00, 7, 'tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+125 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p> Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg is indicated for treating infections caused by sensitive bacteria, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong> due to <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial otitis media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non beta-lactamase producing strains)</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong>, including pneumonia, caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp., Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes,</em> and <em>E. coli</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis and secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains), or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains)</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin-structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></li><li><strong>Bone and joint infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong> caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase-producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease (erythema migrans)</strong> caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em></li><li><strong>Septicemia</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pneumoniae, E. coli, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), and <em>Klebsiella spp.</em></li><li><strong>Meningitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), <em>Neisseria meningitidis,</em> and <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Switch therapy (injectable to oral)</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime is a bactericidal second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic that effectively targets a wide range of susceptible Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including numerous beta-lactamase-producing strains. It works by disrupting bacterial cell wall synthesis through interference with the transpeptidation process.</p><p>Clavulanic acid, a naturally derived beta-lactamase inhibitor produced by <em>Streptomyces clavuligerus</em>, shares a similar structure with beta-lactam antibiotics. It irreversibly binds to beta-lactamase enzymes, rendering them inactive. Clavulanic acid provides Cefuroxime with protection against degradation by beta-lactamase enzymes, offering a solution for treating bacterial infections caused by beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Adolescents and adults (13 years and older)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis:</strong> 250 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin-structure infections:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections:</strong> 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Gonorrhea:</strong> 1000 mg b.i.d. Single dose</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>MDR Typhoid Fever:</strong> 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li><strong>Early Lyme disease:</strong> 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis:</strong> 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Acute otitis media:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li><strong>Impetigo:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p> Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg tablet may be taken without regard to food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p> When probenecid is administered concurrently with Cefuroxime-Clavulanic Acid, it significantly increases the area under the serum concentration-time curve by 50%. The use of medications that reduce gastric acidity may result in decreased bioavailability of Cefuroxime, potentially counteracting its postprandial absorption benefits.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime-Clavulanic Acid is contraindicated in patients with a known allergy to cephalosporins and in patients with Pseudomembranous Colitis.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p> Generally, Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg is well tolerated. However, a few side effects like nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal discomfort, or pain may occur. As with other broad-spectrum antibiotics, prolonged administration of Cefuroxime and Clavulanic acid combination may result in overgrowth of nonsusceptible microorganisms. Rarely (&lt;0.2%), renal dysfunction, anaphylaxis, angioedema, pruritus, rash, and serum sickness-like urticaria may appear.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> While it\'s generally advisable to avoid antibiotics during the first trimester of pregnancy, Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg can be used safely in later stages to address urinary and other infections. It is excreted into breast milk in small amounts, but it\'s essential to consider the potential for sensitizing the infant.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime should be given with care to patients receiving concurrent treatment with potent diuretics &amp; those who have a history of colitis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime is a bactericidal second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic with activity against a wide range of susceptible Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including many strains that produce beta-lactamase enzymes. Cefuroxime works by disrupting bacterial cell wall synthesis through interference with the transpeptidation process.</p><p>Clavulanic acid, a naturally derived beta-lactamase inhibitor produced by <em>Streptomyces clavuligerus</em>, shares a structural similarity with beta-lactam antibiotics. It irreversibly binds to beta-lactamase enzymes, rendering them inactive. This protective action of clavulanic acid shields Cefuroxime from degradation by beta-lactamase enzymes, making it an effective solution for treating bacterial infections caused by beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</p>', 'active', '595_1742273264.png', 69, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 09:47:44', '2025-03-18 09:47:44'),
(3215, 'Mexclav', 'Cefuroxime Axetil + Clavulanic Acid', 60.00, 7, 'tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+125 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p>Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg is indicated for treating infections caused by sensitive bacteria, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong> due to <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial otitis media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non beta-lactamase producing strains)</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong>, including pneumonia, caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp., Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes,</em> and <em>E. coli</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis and secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains), or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains)</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin-structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></li><li><strong>Bone and joint infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong> caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase-producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease (erythema migrans)</strong> caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em></li><li><strong>Septicemia</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pneumoniae, E. coli, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), and <em>Klebsiella spp.</em></li><li><strong>Meningitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), <em>Neisseria meningitidis,</em> and <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Switch therapy (injectable to oral)</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime is a bactericidal second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic that effectively targets a wide range of susceptible Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including numerous beta-lactamase-producing strains. It works by disrupting bacterial cell wall synthesis through interference with the transpeptidation process.</p><p>Clavulanic acid, a naturally derived beta-lactamase inhibitor produced by <em>Streptomyces clavuligerus</em>, shares a similar structure with beta-lactam antibiotics. It irreversibly binds to beta-lactamase enzymes, rendering them inactive. Clavulanic acid provides Cefuroxime with protection against degradation by beta-lactamase enzymes, offering a solution for treating bacterial infections caused by beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Adolescents and adults (13 years and older)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis:</strong> 250 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin-structure infections:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections:</strong> 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Gonorrhea:</strong> 1000 mg b.i.d. Single dose</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>MDR Typhoid Fever:</strong> 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li><strong>Early Lyme disease:</strong> 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis:</strong> 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Acute otitis media:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li><strong>Impetigo:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p>Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg tablet may be taken without regard to food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p>When probenecid is administered concurrently with Cefuroxime-Clavulanic Acid, it significantly increases the area under the serum concentration-time curve by 50%. The use of medications that reduce gastric acidity may result in decreased bioavailability of Cefuroxime, potentially counteracting its postprandial absorption benefits.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime-Clavulanic Acid is contraindicated in patients with a known allergy to cephalosporins and in patients with Pseudomembranous Colitis.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p>Generally, Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg is well tolerated. However, a few side effects like nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal discomfort, or pain may occur. As with other broad-spectrum antibiotics, prolonged administration of Cefuroxime and Clavulanic acid combination may result in overgrowth of nonsusceptible microorganisms. Rarely (&lt;0.2%), renal dysfunction, anaphylaxis, angioedema, pruritus, rash, and serum sickness-like urticaria may appear.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>While it\'s generally advisable to avoid antibiotics during the first trimester of pregnancy, Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg can be used safely in later stages to address urinary and other infections. It is excreted into breast milk in small amounts, but it\'s essential to consider the potential for sensitizing the infant.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime should be given with care to patients receiving concurrent treatment with potent diuretics &amp; those who have a history of colitis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime is a bactericidal second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic with activity against a wide range of susceptible Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including many strains that produce beta-lactamase enzymes. Cefuroxime works by disrupting bacterial cell wall synthesis through interference with the transpeptidation process.</p><p>Clavulanic acid, a naturally derived beta-lactamase inhibitor produced by <em>Streptomyces clavuligerus</em>, shares a structural similarity with beta-lactam antibiotics. It irreversibly binds to beta-lactamase enzymes, rendering them inactive. This protective action of clavulanic acid shields Cefuroxime from degradation by beta-lactamase enzymes, making it an effective solution for treating bacterial infections caused by beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</p>', 'in-active', '934_1742273265.png', 69, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 09:47:45', '2025-05-08 20:28:40');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3216, 'Mexclav', 'Cefuroxime Axetil + Clavulanic Acid', 60.00, 7, 'tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+125 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p>Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg is indicated for treating infections caused by sensitive bacteria, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong> due to <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial otitis media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non beta-lactamase producing strains)</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong>, including pneumonia, caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp., Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes,</em> and <em>E. coli</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis and secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains), or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains)</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin-structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></li><li><strong>Bone and joint infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong> caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase-producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease (erythema migrans)</strong> caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em></li><li><strong>Septicemia</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pneumoniae, E. coli, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), and <em>Klebsiella spp.</em></li><li><strong>Meningitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), <em>Neisseria meningitidis,</em> and <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Switch therapy (injectable to oral)</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime is a bactericidal second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic that effectively targets a wide range of susceptible Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including numerous beta-lactamase-producing strains. It works by disrupting bacterial cell wall synthesis through interference with the transpeptidation process.</p><p>Clavulanic acid, a naturally derived beta-lactamase inhibitor produced by <em>Streptomyces clavuligerus</em>, shares a similar structure with beta-lactam antibiotics. It irreversibly binds to beta-lactamase enzymes, rendering them inactive. Clavulanic acid provides Cefuroxime with protection against degradation by beta-lactamase enzymes, offering a solution for treating bacterial infections caused by beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Adolescents and adults (13 years and older)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis:</strong> 250 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin-structure infections:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections:</strong> 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Gonorrhea:</strong> 1000 mg b.i.d. Single dose</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>MDR Typhoid Fever:</strong> 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li><strong>Early Lyme disease:</strong> 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis:</strong> 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Acute otitis media:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li><strong>Impetigo:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p>Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg tablet may be taken without regard to food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p>When probenecid is administered concurrently with Cefuroxime-Clavulanic Acid, it significantly increases the area under the serum concentration-time curve by 50%. The use of medications that reduce gastric acidity may result in decreased bioavailability of Cefuroxime, potentially counteracting its postprandial absorption benefits.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime-Clavulanic Acid is contraindicated in patients with a known allergy to cephalosporins and in patients with Pseudomembranous Colitis.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p>Generally, Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg is well tolerated. However, a few side effects like nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal discomfort, or pain may occur. As with other broad-spectrum antibiotics, prolonged administration of Cefuroxime and Clavulanic acid combination may result in overgrowth of nonsusceptible microorganisms. Rarely (&lt;0.2%), renal dysfunction, anaphylaxis, angioedema, pruritus, rash, and serum sickness-like urticaria may appear.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>While it\'s generally advisable to avoid antibiotics during the first trimester of pregnancy, Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg can be used safely in later stages to address urinary and other infections. It is excreted into breast milk in small amounts, but it\'s essential to consider the potential for sensitizing the infant.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime should be given with care to patients receiving concurrent treatment with potent diuretics &amp; those who have a history of colitis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime is a bactericidal second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic with activity against a wide range of susceptible Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including many strains that produce beta-lactamase enzymes. Cefuroxime works by disrupting bacterial cell wall synthesis through interference with the transpeptidation process.</p><p>Clavulanic acid, a naturally derived beta-lactamase inhibitor produced by <em>Streptomyces clavuligerus</em>, shares a structural similarity with beta-lactam antibiotics. It irreversibly binds to beta-lactamase enzymes, rendering them inactive. This protective action of clavulanic acid shields Cefuroxime from degradation by beta-lactamase enzymes, making it an effective solution for treating bacterial infections caused by beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</p>', 'in-active', '59_1742273265.png', 69, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 09:47:45', '2025-05-08 20:28:51'),
