50-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin-structure infections:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections:</strong> 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Gonorrhea:</strong> 1000 mg b.i.d. Single dose</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia:</strong> 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>MDR Typhoid Fever:</strong> 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li><strong>Early Lyme disease:</strong> 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis:</strong> 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Acute otitis media:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li><strong>Impetigo:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p>Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg tablet may be taken without regard to food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p>When probenecid is administered concurrently with Cefuroxime-Clavulanic Acid, it significantly increases the area under the serum concentration-time curve by 50%. The use of medications that reduce gastric acidity may result in decreased bioavailability of Cefuroxime, potentially counteracting its postprandial absorption benefits.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime-Clavulanic Acid is contraindicated in patients with a known allergy to cephalosporins and in patients with Pseudomembranous Colitis.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg</strong></p><p>Generally, Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg is well tolerated. However, a few side effects like nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal discomfort, or pain may occur. As with other broad-spectrum antibiotics, prolonged administration of Cefuroxime and Clavulanic acid combination may result in overgrowth of nonsusceptible microorganisms. Rarely (&lt;0.2%), renal dysfunction, anaphylaxis, angioedema, pruritus, rash, and serum sickness-like urticaria may appear.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>While it\'s generally advisable to avoid antibiotics during the first trimester of pregnancy, Mexclav 500 mg+125 mg can be used safely in later stages to address urinary and other infections. It is excreted into breast milk in small amounts, but it\'s essential to consider the potential for sensitizing the infant.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime should be given with care to patients receiving concurrent treatment with potent diuretics &amp; those who have a history of colitis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime is a bactericidal second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic with activity against a wide range of susceptible Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including many strains that produce beta-lactamase enzymes. Cefuroxime works by disrupting bacterial cell wall synthesis through interference with the transpeptidation process.</p><p>Clavulanic acid, a naturally derived beta-lactamase inhibitor produced by <em>Streptomyces clavuligerus</em>, shares a structural similarity with beta-lactam antibiotics. It irreversibly binds to beta-lactamase enzymes, rendering them inactive. This protective action of clavulanic acid shields Cefuroxime from degradation by beta-lactamase enzymes, making it an effective solution for treating bacterial infections caused by beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</p>', 'in-active', '910_1742273267.png', 69, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 09:47:47', '2025-05-08 20:29:01'),
(3218, 'GensuPen2', 'Insulin device & needle', 700.00, 1, 'x Each device', 'Each device', 'Insulin Device', 'niddle', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Use the injection technique as recommended by a physician and/or healthcare professional. Press the push-button fully for injection. Hold the push-button pressed down during medication discharge. After the medication has been completely discharged, wait for 6 seconds and then pull out the pen needle slowly. You may either hold the push-button pressed or release it during the 6 seconds.</p><p><strong>Description</strong></p><p><strong>Pen device:</strong></p><p>This is an insulin delivery device developed for diabetes patients to self-administer insulin conveniently. It looks like a pen with a push, twist, and lock cartridge holder that allows users to inject insulin, read and correct dosages easily, and change cartridges in a fast and simple way. This device allows the injection of 1 to 60 units of insulin or any 3 mL cartridge. If too many units are set, the dose can be corrected without wasting insulin.</p><p><strong>Pen needle:</strong></p><ul><li>4 mm needles are associated with less risk of intramuscular injections compared to 6 mm needles.</li><li>Unique needle design to reduce the risk of bending or breaking.</li><li>4 mm length is suitable for subcutaneous delivery, regardless of BMI.</li><li>Compatible with most pen devices to simplify use.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Pen preparation or changing cartridge:</strong></p><ul><li>Remove the pen cap.</li><li>Remove the cartridge sleeve by turning it.</li><li>Remove the empty cartridge if necessary.</li><li>Insert the new cartridge into the cartridge sleeve.</li><li>Push the threaded rod back completely.</li><li>Attach the cartridge sleeve to the housing by turning it.</li></ul><p><strong>Attach pen needle:</strong></p><ul><li>Pull off the peel foil.</li><li>Twist on the pen needle to the cartridge sleeve. Check that the pen needle is attached correctly.</li><li>Remove the outer needle protective cap. Keep the outer needle protective cap to safely remove and dispose of the pen needle after use.</li><li>Remove and dispose of the inner needle protective cap.</li></ul><p><strong>Priming / function check:</strong></p><ul><li>Dial the test dose by turning the dosage knob clockwise (not less than 2 units).</li><li>For the function check, hold the pen pointing upwards and gently tap the cartridge sleeve so that the air can rise to the top.</li><li>Press the push-button.</li><li>A few drops of insulin must emerge from the pen needle tip.</li></ul><p><strong>Remove pen needle after each injection:</strong></p><ul><li>Carefully attach the outer needle protective cap on the pen needle.</li><li>Twist off the pen needle and dispose of it correctly, according to the advice of your physician and/or healthcare professional.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage conditions</strong></p><p>Please store and handle your needles and cartridges according to the respective instructions for use. After each use, attach the pen cap. Always keep your pen in the pen pouch without a pen needle attached. Use and store your pen at room temperature below or equal to 30°C. Please handle your pen with care. Protect it from water, dust, and moisture. A damp cloth is sufficient to clean your pen. Please do not use alcohol, other solvents, or cleaning agents. Never immerse your pen in water, as this could damage the pen.</p>', 'active', '1666_1742273814.jpg', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 09:53:56', '2025-05-03 18:22:27'),
(3219, 'Ralect', 'Rasagiline', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you provided with headings formatted in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Ralect 0.5 mg</strong></p><p> Ralect 0.5 mg is indicated for the treatment of the signs and symptoms of idiopathic Parkinson\'s disease as initial monotherapy, and as adjunct therapy to dopamine agonists or to levodopa.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Antiparkinson drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Ralect 0.5 mg is a potent, irreversible, selective MAO-B inhibitor that effectively crosses the blood-brain barrier after oral administration. The precise mechanisms of action of Ralect 0.5 mg are unknown. One mechanism is believed to be related to its MAO-B inhibitory activity, which causes an increase in extracellular levels of dopamine in the brain. The elevated dopamine level and subsequent increased dopaminergic activity are likely to mediate Ralect 0.5 mg\'s beneficial effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ralect 0.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Monotherapy</strong>: Ralect 0.5 mg 1 mg once daily</li><li><strong>As adjunct without Levodopa</strong>: Ralect 0.5 mg 1 mg once daily</li><li><strong>As adjunct to Levodopa</strong>: Ralect 0.5 mg 0.5 mg once daily</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ralect 0.5 mg</strong></p><p> Concomitant use of Ralect 0.5 mg with meperidine, dextromethorphan, antidepressants is not recommended.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Ralect 0.5 mg is contraindicated for use with meperidine, tramadol, methadone, propoxyphene, and MAO inhibitors (MAOIs), including other selective MAO-B inhibitors, because of the risk of serotonin syndrome.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ralect 0.5 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects of Ralect 0.5 mg include: dizziness, spinning sensation, joint pain, headache, heartburn, nausea, muscle pain, etc.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. Ralect 0.5 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Exacerbation of hypertension may occur during treatment with Ralect 0.5 mg. Medication adjustment may be necessary if elevation of blood pressure is sustained. Dose should not exceed 0.5 mg once daily for patients with mild hepatic impairment or taking concomitant Ciprofloxacin or other CYP1A2 inhibitors.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep all medicines out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use</strong>: The safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Antiparkinson drugs</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Ralect 0.5 mg is a potent, irreversible, selective MAO-B inhibitor that effectively crosses the blood-brain barrier after oral administration. The precise mechanisms of action of Ralect 0.5 mg are unknown. One mechanism is believed to be related to its MAO-B inhibitory activity, which causes an increase in extracellular levels of dopamine in the brain. The elevated dopamine level and subsequent increased dopaminergic activity are likely to mediate Ralect 0.5 mg\'s beneficial effects.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. Ralect 0.5 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Pediatric use: The safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p>', 'active', '8_1742273895.png', 16, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 09:58:15', '2025-03-18 09:58:15'),
(3220, 'Esolok MUPS', 'Esomeprazole', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Esolok MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><p> Esolok MUPS 20 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</li><li>Healing of erosive esophagitis</li><li>Maintenance of healing of erosive esophagitis</li><li>Symptomatic Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</li><li>Reduction of NSAID-associated gastric ulcer</li><li>H. pylori eradication (Triple therapy)</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Esolok MUPS 20 mg exerts its stomach acid-suppressing effects by preventing the final step in gastric acid production. It covalently binds to sulfhydryl groups of cysteines found on the H+/K+-ATPase enzyme at the secretory surface of gastric parietal cells. This leads to the inhibition of both basal and stimulated gastric acid secretion, irrespective of the stimulus. Since the binding of Esolok MUPS 20 mg to the H+/K+-ATPase enzyme is irreversible, new enzyme needs to be expressed to resume acid secretion. This results in an antisecretory effect lasting longer than 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Esolok MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Tablet or Capsule:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of erosive esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4 to 8 weeks. Most patients heal within this period. If not, an additional 4-8 weeks may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of healing of erosive esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg once daily (controlled studies did not extend beyond six months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms persist, an additional 4 weeks may be considered.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>H. pylori eradication (Triple therapy):</strong>Esolok MUPS 20 mg: 20 mg twice daily for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Amoxicillin: 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clarithromycin: 500 mg twice daily for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Injection:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>GERD with Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20 or 40 mg given once daily by intravenous injection (no less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Esolok MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><p> A solution for intravenous infusion is prepared by first reconstituting the contents of one vial with 5 ml of 0.9% Sodium Chloride, then further diluting to a final volume of 50 ml. The final concentration is 0.8 mg/ml.</p><ul><li><strong>20 mg dose:</strong> Withdraw 25 ml of the final solution and administer as an intravenous infusion over 10 to 30 minutes.</li><li><strong>10 mg dose:</strong> Withdraw 12.5 ml of the final solution and administer as an intravenous infusion over 10 to 30 minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Esolok MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><p> Esolok MUPS 20 mg appears to selectively inhibit the cytochrome P450 mono-oxygenase system, possibly affecting hepatic clearance. However, no clinically relevant interactions have been reported.</p><ul><li>The effect on oral combined contraceptive pills is uncertain.</li><li>Reduction in gastric acid may influence bacterial colonization in the stomach and duodenum and affect vitamin B12 absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Esolok MUPS 20 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any components of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Esolok MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include headache, diarrhea, and abdominal pain.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C (US FDA):</strong> Esolok MUPS 20 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Exclude the possibility of malignancy when gastric ulcer is suspected and before treating dyspepsia.</li><li>When using in combination with an antibiotic, refer to the prescribing information of the respective antibiotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p> <strong>Directions for reconstitution of solution:</strong> Solution for injection is prepared by adding 5 ml of 0.9% Sodium Chloride into the vial containing the dry powder. The reconstituted solution for injection is clear and colorless to very slightly yellow.</p>', 'active', '1682_1742273954.jpg', 30, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 09:59:14', '2025-03-18 09:59:14'),
(3221, 'Mexiderm', 'Betamethasone Valerate', 30.00, 1, 'cream', NULL, 'Cream', '15 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Mexiderm cream or ointment is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li><strong>Eczema</strong> in children and adults, including atopic</li><li><strong>Infantile and discoid eczema</strong></li><li><strong>Prurigo nodularis</strong></li><li><strong>Psoriasis</strong> (excluding widespread plaque psoriasis)</li><li><strong>Neurodermatoses</strong>, including <em>lichen simplex</em> and <em>lichen planus</em></li><li><strong>Seborrheic dermatitis</strong></li><li><strong>Contact sensitivity reaction</strong></li><li><strong>Discoid lupus erythematosus</strong></li><li>May be used as an adjunct to systemic steroid therapy in generalized erythroderma</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Betamethasone Valerate BP is a synthetic adrenocorticosteroid with glucocorticoid properties. It is an analog of prednisolone and possesses a slight degree of mineral corticosteroid activity. Due to its <strong>anti-inflammatory, antipruritic, and vasoconstrictive</strong> properties, it is highly effective for dermatological use.</p><ul><li>It is absorbed from the skin, and inflammation or other skin diseases can increase percutaneous absorption.</li><li>Occlusive dressings significantly enhance percutaneous absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p> Apply sparingly to the affected area <strong>two or three times daily</strong> until improvement occurs, then reduce to <strong>twice daily or less</strong>. The usual <strong>maximum duration of therapy is three weeks</strong>.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> No significant drug interactions have been reported with Mexiderm Cream/Ointment.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Mexiderm Cream/Ointment is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients</li><li><strong>Rosacea &amp; acne vulgaris</strong></li><li><strong>Perioral dermatitis, perianal &amp; genital pruritus</strong></li><li><strong>Viral infections</strong> of the skin (e.g., herpes simplex, chickenpox)</li><li><strong>Primary infected skin lesions</strong> caused by fungi or bacteria</li><li><strong>Dermatoses in children under one year of age</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> The following local adverse reactions are more common with <strong>high doses, long-term use, or occlusive dressings</strong>:</p><ul><li>Dryness, itching, burning</li><li>Skin thinning, local irritation</li><li>Features of hypercortisolism</li><li>Telangiectasia, striae, skin atrophy</li><li>Hypertrichosis, change in pigmentation</li><li>Secondary infection</li><li>Perioral dermatitis, allergic contact dermatitis</li><li>Maceration of the skin, acneiform eruption</li><li>Exacerbation of symptoms</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Mexiderm Cream/Ointment <strong>should not be used extensively during pregnancy</strong>.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Usually well tolerated, but <strong>discontinue use if signs of hypersensitivity appear</strong>.</li><li>Avoid <strong>long-term continuous topical therapy</strong>, particularly in children, as adrenal suppression may occur even without occlusion.</li><li><strong>Systemic absorption may cause symptoms of hypercortisolism</strong>, especially if large areas are treated, occlusive dressings are used, or treatment is prolonged.</li><li><strong>Prolonged treatment on the face or other areas may cause atrophic changes</strong>.</li><li><strong>Avoid contact with eyes</strong> if applied to the eyelids, as it may cause glaucoma.</li><li>Any <strong>signs of spreading infection</strong> require discontinuation of topical corticosteroid therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Corticosteroid</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1025_1742274196.jpg', 69, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:03:16', '2025-03-18 10:03:16'),
(3222, 'Pegaron', 'Pregabalin', 14.00, 6, 'Capsule', '6 Tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pegaron 50 mg</strong></p><p> Pegaron 50 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Neuropathic pain linked to diabetic peripheral neuropathy (DPN)</li><li>Neuralgia following shingles (PHN)</li><li>Treatment of partial-onset seizures in patients older than one month with adjunctive therapy</li><li>Fibromyalgia</li><li>Neuropathic pain brought on by spinal cord damage</li><li>Pegaron 50 mg CR tablets are also used for neuropathic pain caused by diabetic peripheral neuropathy (DPN) and postherpetic neuralgia (PHN)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Supplemental antiepileptic medications</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Pegaron 50 mg is structurally similar to gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA), an inhibitory neurotransmitter. However, it does not directly bind to GABAA, GABAB, or benzodiazepine receptors. Instead, it binds with high affinity to the alpha-2-delta site, an auxiliary subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels in the central nervous system. Pegaron 50 mg has a 90% oral bioavailability that is dose-independent. It is primarily excreted through the kidneys as an unchanged drug, with a mean elimination half-life of 6.3 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Pegaron 50 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Neuropathic Pain Associated with Diabetic Peripheral Neuropathy (DPN):</strong></p><ul><li>Initial dose: 50 mg three times a day (150 mg/day)</li><li>Maximum dose: 100 mg three times a day (300 mg/day) in patients with creatinine clearance ≥ 60 mL/min</li><li>CR capsule: Start with 165 mg once daily and increase to 330 mg once daily based on efficacy and tolerability</li></ul><p><strong>Post-Herpetic Neuralgia (PHN):</strong></p><ul><li>Recommended dose: 75 to 150 mg two times a day, or 50 to 100 mg three times a day (150 to 300 mg/day)</li><li>Maximum dose: 600 mg/day for patients who do not experience sufficient relief at lower doses</li><li>CR capsule: Start with 165 mg once daily and increase up to 660 mg once daily</li></ul><p><strong>Adjunctive Therapy for Partial-Onset Seizures (Adults):</strong></p><ul><li>Initial dose: 50 mg three times a day or 75 mg twice a day</li><li>Maximum dose: 600 mg/day based on patient response and tolerability</li></ul><p><strong>Management of Fibromyalgia:</strong></p><ul><li>Initial dose: 75 mg twice a day (150 mg/day)</li><li>May increase to 150 mg twice a day (300 mg/day) within 1 week</li><li>Maximum dose: 450 mg/day</li></ul><p><strong>Neuropathic Pain Associated with Spinal Cord Injury:</strong></p><ul><li>Initial dose: 75 mg twice a day (150 mg/day)</li><li>May increase to 150 mg twice a day (300 mg/day) within 1 week</li><li>Maximum dose: 600 mg/day</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Pegaron 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Pegaron 50 mg is administered orally and can be taken with or without food.</li><li>The CR tablet should be taken after dinner and must be swallowed whole without crushing, chewing, or splitting.</li><li>If a dose is missed, take it with the next meal or snack but do not double doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Pegaron 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No significant pharmacokinetic drug interactions are anticipated.</li><li>No relevant food-drug interactions have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Pegaron 50 mg or any of its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Pegaron 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include dizziness, somnolence, dry mouth, edema, blurred vision, weight gain, and difficulty concentrating.</li><li>In pediatric patients, increased appetite and weight gain may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy Category C: Should be used only if the benefits outweigh potential risks to the fetus.</li><li>Pegaron 50 mg may be excreted in breast milk; breastfeeding is not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Angioedema:</strong> May occur, requiring immediate discontinuation.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity Reactions:</strong> Discontinue if symptoms like hives, wheezing, or difficulty breathing occur.</li><li><strong>Suicidal Thoughts/Behavior:</strong> Increased risk with Pegaron 50 mg and other antiepileptic drugs.</li><li><strong>Respiratory Depression:</strong> Monitor when used with CNS depressants or in patients with respiratory issues.</li><li><strong>Drowsiness &amp; Dizziness:</strong> May impair driving or machinery operation.</li><li><strong>Withdrawal Symptoms:</strong> Should be discontinued gradually over at least two weeks to avoid adverse effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Pegaron 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms include drowsiness, confusion, agitation, restlessness, heart block, and seizures.</li><li>No specific antidote; supportive care and gastric lavage may be required.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients for fibromyalgia, PHN, and neuropathic pain have not been established.</li><li>The safety of Pegaron 50 mg extended-release tablets in children has not been determined.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</li><li>Primary anti-epileptic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Pegaron 50 mg is structurally related to GABA but does not directly bind to GABA receptors.</li><li>It binds to the alpha2-delta subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels, contributing to its anti-nociceptive and anti-seizure properties.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>The potential risks should be discussed with pregnant women.</li><li>Trace amounts of Pegaron 50 mg have been found in breast milk; breastfeeding is not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy for pediatric patients under one month for partial-onset seizures have not been established.</li><li>Not recommended for children in the treatment of fibromyalgia, PHN, or neuropathic pain.</li></ul>', 'active', '285_1742274269.jpg', 63, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:04:29', '2025-03-18 10:04:29'),
(3223, 'Gavisus DX', 'Sodium Alginate + Sodium Bicarbonate + Calcium Carbonate', 270.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Suspension', '500 mg plus 213 mg plus 325 mg per10 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Gavista DX (500 mg+213 mg+325 mg)/10 ml</h3><p>Gavista DX (500 mg+213 mg+325 mg)/10 ml is indicated for individuals experiencing:</p><ul><li>Gastric reflux</li><li>Heartburn</li><li>Flatulence associated with gastric reflux</li><li>Heartburn during pregnancy</li><li>Epigastric and retrosternal discomfort where the underlying cause is gastric reflux</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Antacids</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>The action of Gavista DX is physical and does not rely on absorption into the systemic circulation. Upon ingestion, it rapidly reacts with gastric acid to form a protective Alginic acid gel raft with a nearly neutral pH. This raft floats on the stomach contents, preventing gastro-esophageal reflux for up to 4 hours. In severe cases, the raft may reflux into the esophagus, providing a soothing effect instead of stomach contents.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><p><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: Take 10-20 mL after meals and at bedtime, up to four times a day.</p><p><strong>Children 6 to 12 years</strong>: Take 5-10 mL after meals and at bedtime, up to four times a day.</p><p><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed.</p><p><strong>Children under 6 years</strong>: Not recommended.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dose modification required.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: Use caution if a highly restricted salt diet is required.</p><h3>Interaction</h3><p>A 2-hour interval should be observed between taking Gavista DX and other medications, particularly:</p><ul><li>Tetracyclines</li><li>Digoxin</li><li>Fluoroquinolones</li><li>Iron salts</li><li>Ketoconazole</li><li>Neuroleptics</li><li>Thyroid hormones</li><li>Penicillamine</li><li>Beta-blockers</li><li>Glucocorticoids</li><li>Bisphosphonates</li><li>Estramustine</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Gavista DX should not be used in patients with known or suspected hypersensitivity to the active ingredients or any excipients.</p><h3>Side Effects</h3><p>Possible side effects include:</p><ul><li>Constipation</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Belching</li></ul><p>If any of these occur, consult a physician. In case of an overdose, a sensation of swelling may occur. Seek medical advice if this happens.</p><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: No malformation or fetal/neonatal toxicity observed in clinical studies involving over 500 pregnant women. Safe for use during pregnancy when clinically necessary.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: No adverse effects on breastfed newborns or infants observed.</li><li><strong>Fertility</strong>: No impact on fertility observed in pre-clinical or clinical investigations.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>If symptoms persist for more than 7 days, reassess the clinical condition.</li><li>Each 10 mL dose contains 141 mg of sodium (6.2 mmol), which should be considered for patients on a restricted salt diet.</li><li>Each 10 mL dose contains 160 mg of calcium carbonate (1.6 mmol), caution is advised for patients with hypercalcemia, nephrocalcinosis, or recurrent calcium-containing renal calculi.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><p>In case of overdose, symptomatic treatment should be given. Abdominal distension may occur.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><p>Store in a cool (below 30°C), dry place, away from light. Keep out of reach of children. Do not refrigerate or freeze.</p><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Paediatric Use</strong>: Not recommended for children under 6 years of age.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No dose modification required.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><p>Antacids</p><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Gavista DX works through a physical mechanism by forming a gel raft in the stomach after reacting with gastric acid. This raft helps reduce gastro-esophageal reflux and protects the esophagus from gastric contents like acid, pepsin, and bile.</p>', 'active', '243_1742274304.webp', 5, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:05:04', '2025-05-03 18:13:58'),
(3224, 'Gavinil', 'Sodium Alginate + Sodium Bicarbonate + Calcium Carbonate', 200.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '(500 mg+267 mg+160 mg)/10 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Gavista DX (500 mg+213 mg+325 mg)/10 ml</h3><p>Gavista DX (500 mg+213 mg+325 mg)/10 ml is indicated for individuals experiencing:</p><ul><li>Gastric reflux</li><li>Heartburn</li><li>Flatulence associated with gastric reflux</li><li>Heartburn during pregnancy</li><li>Epigastric and retrosternal discomfort where the underlying cause is gastric reflux</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Antacids</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>The action of Gavista DX is physical and does not rely on absorption into the systemic circulation. Upon ingestion, it rapidly reacts with gastric acid to form a protective Alginic acid gel raft with a nearly neutral pH. This raft floats on the stomach contents, preventing gastro-esophageal reflux for up to 4 hours. In severe cases, the raft may reflux into the esophagus, providing a soothing effect instead of stomach contents.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><p><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: Take 10-20 mL after meals and at bedtime, up to four times a day.</p><p><strong>Children 6 to 12 years</strong>: Take 5-10 mL after meals and at bedtime, up to four times a day.</p><p><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed.</p><p><strong>Children under 6 years</strong>: Not recommended.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dose modification required.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: Use caution if a highly restricted salt diet is required.</p><h3>Interaction</h3><p>A 2-hour interval should be observed between taking Gavista DX and other medications, particularly:</p><ul><li>Tetracyclines</li><li>Digoxin</li><li>Fluoroquinolones</li><li>Iron salts</li><li>Ketoconazole</li><li>Neuroleptics</li><li>Thyroid hormones</li><li>Penicillamine</li><li>Beta-blockers</li><li>Glucocorticoids</li><li>Bisphosphonates</li><li>Estramustine</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Gavista DX should not be used in patients with known or suspected hypersensitivity to the active ingredients or any excipients.</p><h3>Side Effects</h3><p>Possible side effects include:</p><ul><li>Constipation</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Belching</li></ul><p>If any of these occur, consult a physician. In case of an overdose, a sensation of swelling may occur. Seek medical advice if this happens.</p><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: No malformation or fetal/neonatal toxicity observed in clinical studies involving over 500 pregnant women. Safe for use during pregnancy when clinically necessary.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: No adverse effects on breastfed newborns or infants observed.</li><li><strong>Fertility</strong>: No impact on fertility observed in pre-clinical or clinical investigations.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>If symptoms persist for more than 7 days, reassess the clinical condition.</li><li>Each 10 mL dose contains 141 mg of sodium (6.2 mmol), which should be considered for patients on a restricted salt diet.</li><li>Each 10 mL dose contains 160 mg of calcium carbonate (1.6 mmol), caution is advised for patients with hypercalcemia, nephrocalcinosis, or recurrent calcium-containing renal calculi.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><p>In case of overdose, symptomatic treatment should be given. Abdominal distension may occur.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><p>Store in a cool (below 30°C), dry place, away from light. Keep out of reach of children. Do not refrigerate or freeze.</p><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Paediatric Use</strong>: Not recommended for children under 6 years of age.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No dose modification required.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><p>Antacids</p><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Gavista DX works through a physical mechanism by forming a gel raft in the stomach after reacting with gastric acid. This raft helps reduce gastro-esophageal reflux and protects the esophagus from gastric contents like acid, pepsin, and bile.</p>', 'active', '5407_1746281965.webp', 33, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:05:06', '2025-05-03 18:19:25');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3225, 'Minocin', 'Minocycline Hydrochloride', 45.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Minocin 100 mg</strong></p><p> Minocycline is indicated for oral administration for the treatment of infections caused by tetracycline-sensitive organisms and some tetracycline-resistant strains of staphylococci. Indications include:</p><ul><li><strong>Acne</strong></li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong></li><li><strong>Ophthalmological infections</strong></li><li><strong>Acute and chronic bronchitis</strong></li><li><strong>Bronchiectasis</strong></li><li><strong>Lung abscess</strong></li><li><strong>Ear, nose, and throat infections</strong></li><li><strong>Pelvic inflammatory disease</strong></li><li><strong>Nocardiosis</strong></li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong></li><li><strong>Gonorrhoea</strong></li><li><strong>Non-gonococcal urethritis and prostatitis</strong></li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of asymptomatic meningococcal carriers</strong></li><li><strong>Pre- and post-operative prophylaxis of infection</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Tetracycline group of drugs.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Minocin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Routine antibiotic use:</strong> 200 mg daily in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Acne:</strong> 50 mg twice daily for at least 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gonorrhoea:Males:</strong> 200 mg initially, followed by 100 mg every 12 hours for at least 4 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Females:</strong> May require a more prolonged therapy.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of asymptomatic meningococcal carriers:</strong> 100 mg twice daily for 5 days, followed by rifampicin treatment.</li><li>If there is no satisfactory response after six months, discontinue minocycline and consider alternative therapies.</li><li>If continued beyond six months, monitor for signs of hepatitis or systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) every three months.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Minocin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Anticoagulants:</strong> May require reduced doses of anticoagulants due to depression of plasma prothrombin activity.</li><li><strong>ACE inhibitors, antacids, and adsorbents:</strong> May impair absorption when taken together.</li><li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> May aggravate nephrotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Antibacterials:</strong> Minocycline should not be used with penicillins.</li><li><strong>Ergotamine and ergometrine:</strong> Increased risk of ergotism.</li><li><strong>Oral contraceptives:</strong> Can induce hyperpigmentation.</li><li><strong>Retinoids:</strong> Should not be used shortly before, during, or shortly after minocycline therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to tetracyclines</li><li>Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)</li><li>Pregnancy and lactation</li><li>Complete renal failure</li><li>Children under 12 years</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Minocin 100 mg</strong></p><p> Common and severe side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Oral and anogenital candidiasis</strong></li><li><strong>Hematologic issues</strong> (e.g., leucopenia, neutropenia, thrombocytopenia)</li><li><strong>Hepatic side effects</strong> (e.g., hepatitis, jaundice, hepatic failure)</li><li><strong>Neurological issues</strong> (e.g., dizziness, headache, tinnitus, convulsions)</li><li><strong>Pulmonary issues</strong> (e.g., bronchospasm, pulmonary eosinophilia)</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal issues</strong> (e.g., nausea, diarrhea, pseudomembranous colitis)</li><li><strong>Skin reactions</strong> (e.g., hyperpigmentation, photosensitivity, rash, erythema)</li><li><strong>Renal failure</strong></li><li><strong>Lupus-like syndrome</strong></li><li><strong>Hyperpigmentation</strong> (can occur in skin, nails, teeth, and other body parts)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Minocycline is contraindicated during pregnancy and lactation. It crosses the placenta and can cause fetal skeletal developmental retardation. It may also cause permanent tooth discoloration and enamel hypoplasia when used in pregnancy. Minocycline is found in breast milk and can cause tooth discoloration in infants.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Breathing difficulties:</strong> Includes dyspnoea, bronchospasm, and pulmonary issues.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment:</strong> Caution in patients with liver dysfunction.</li><li><strong>Autoimmune disorders:</strong> Rare cases of autoimmune hepatotoxicity and SLE.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> Caution in severe renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Intracranial hypertension:</strong> Discontinue if symptoms like headache or blurred vision occur.</li><li><strong>Hyperpigmentation:</strong> Can develop at various body sites and may be reversible upon discontinuation.</li><li><strong>Photosensitivity:</strong> Patients should avoid sun exposure.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Minocin 100 mg</strong></p><p> Overdose symptoms include dizziness, nausea, and vomiting. Treatment involves gastric lavage and supportive care. There is no specific antidote. Minocycline is not significantly removed by dialysis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 25°C in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> No significant drug accumulation in mild to moderate renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Paediatric use:</strong> Not recommended for children under 12 years. For children over 12, use 50 mg every 12 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Tetracycline group of drugs.</p>', 'active', '1981_1742274477.jpg', 8, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:07:57', '2025-03-18 10:07:57'),
(3226, 'Momelo', 'Mometasone Furoate + Olopatadine Hydrochloride', 550.00, 1, 'x 120 metered spray', '120 metered spray', 'Nasal Spray', '(25 mcg+600 mcg)/Spray', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p>', 'active', '403_1742274628.png', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:10:28', '2025-04-23 08:03:01'),
(3227, 'Pralidox', 'Pralidoxime Chloride', 500.00, 1, 'Injection', '1 ml vial injection', 'Injection', '1000 mg per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pralidox 1000 mg/ml</strong> Pralidoxime chloride is indicated as an antidote: In the treatment of poisoning due to those pesticides and chemicals (e.g., nerve agents) of the organophosphate class which have anticholinesterase activity and in the control of overdosage by anticholinesterase drugs used in the treatment of myasthenia gravis. The principal indications for the use of Pralidoxime chloride are muscle weakness and respiratory depression. In severe poisoning, respiratory depression may be due to muscle weakness.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong> Antidote preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong> Pralidoxime reactivates cholinesterase outside the CNS that had been inactivated by phosphorylation due to exposure to certain organophosphates by displacing the enzyme from its receptor sites. It removes the phosphoryl group from the active site of the inactivated enzyme by nucleophilic attack, regenerating active cholinesterase and forming an oxime complex. It also detoxifies certain organophosphates by direct chemical reaction.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Pralidox 1000 mg/ml</strong> <strong>Intramuscular - Organophosphorus poisoning:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult:</strong> Mild: 600 mg, repeat 1-2 times at 15 min intervals as needed. Severe: 1.8 g given as 3 inj of 600 mg in rapid succession. Persistent: May repeat the entire series (1.8 g) beginning 1 hr after admin of the last inj.</li><li><strong>Child (&lt;40 kg):</strong> Mild: 15 mg/kg, repeat as needed every 15 min. Max: 45 mg/kg. Severe: 15 mg/kg, repeat twice in rapid succession to a total dose of 45 mg/kg. Persistent: May repeat the entire series (45 mg/kg) beginning 1 hr after admin of the last inj.</li><li><strong>Child (≥40 kg):</strong> Same as adult dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Intravenous - Organophosphorus poisoning:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult:</strong> In combination with atropine: Loading dose: 1-2 g by infusion over 15-30 min or slow inj over at least 5 min, may repeat dose after 1 hr then 10-12 hrly, as needed. Administer as soon as the effects of atropine are observed. Maintain atropinisation for at least 48 hr.</li><li><strong>Child (≤16 yr):</strong> Loading dose: 20-50 mg/kg (max: 2 g/dose) by inj over 15-30 min followed by 10-20 mg/kg/hr as continuous infusion. Alternatively, a repeat bolus of 20-50 mg/kg after 1 hr and repeated 10-12 hrly as needed.</li><li><strong>Child (&gt;16 yr):</strong> Same as adult dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Poisoning or overdosage with compounds having muscarinic actions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult:</strong> Initially, 1-2 g, followed by 0.5-1 g/hr as infusion. Alternatively, the initial dose may be repeated after 1 hr and then 3-8 hrly as needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong> There are no known absolute contraindications for the use of Pralidox 1000 mg/ml. Relative contraindications include known hypersensitivity to the drug and other situations in which the risk of its use clearly outweighs possible benefit.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Pralidox 1000 mg/ml</strong> Drowsiness, dizziness, visual disturbances, nausea, HTN, tachycardia, headache, hyperventilation, muscle weakness, impaired renal function, elevated liver enzymes, transient increase in creatine phosphokinase, transient neuromuscular blockade; mild to moderate pain at inj site.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong> Pregnancy Category C. Either studies in animals have revealed adverse effects on the foetus (teratogenic or embryocidal or other) and there are no controlled studies in women or studies in women and animals are not available. Drugs should be given only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the foetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong> Patient with myasthenia gravis receiving anticholinesterase agents. Not indicated in the treatment of poisoning due to phosphorous, inorganic phosphates, or organophosphates without anticholinesterase activity and carbamate pesticides. Renal impairment. Pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Pralidox 1000 mg/ml</strong> Manifestations of Overdosage: Observed in normal subjects only: dizziness, blurred vision, diplopia, headache, impaired accommodation, nausea, slight tachycardia. In therapy it has been difficult to differentiate side effects due to the drug from those due to the effects of the poison.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong> Store between 20-25° C.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Intramuscular:</strong> Dilute 1 g with 3.3 mL sterile water for inj to a final concentration of 300 mg/mL.</li><li><strong>Intravenous:</strong> Dilute 1 g with 20 mL of sterile water for inj to make a concentration of 50 mg/mL to be administered in fluid-restricted patients or when rapid admin is required; for all other patients, further dilute with normal saline to a final concentration of 20 mg/mL.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong> Antidote preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong> Pralidoxime reactivates cholinesterase outside the CNS that had been inactivated by phosphorylation due to exposure to certain organophosphates by displacing the enzyme from its receptor sites. It removes the phosphoryl group from the active site of the inactivated enzyme by nucleophilic attack, regenerating active cholinesterase and forming an oxime complex. It also detoxifies certain organophosphates by direct chemical reaction.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong> Pregnancy Category C. Either studies in animals have revealed adverse effects on the foetus (teratogenic or embryocidal or other) and there are no controlled studies in women or studies in women and animals are not available. Drugs should be given only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the foetus.</p>', 'active', '1129_1742274646.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:10:46', '2025-03-18 10:10:46'),
(3228, 'Posac', 'Posaconazole', 200.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Posac 100 mg</h3><p>Posac 100 mg is indicated in patients aged 13 years and above for the treatment of the following fungal infections:</p><ul><li><strong>Invasive aspergillosis</strong> in patients with disease that is refractory or intolerant to Amphotericin B or Itraconazole</li><li><strong>Fusariosis</strong> in patients with disease that is refractory or intolerant to Amphotericin B</li><li><strong>Chromoblastomycosis</strong> and <strong>mycetoma</strong> in patients with disease that is refractory or intolerant to Itraconazole</li><li><strong>Coccidioidomycosis</strong> in patients with disease that is refractory or intolerant to Amphotericin B, Itraconazole, or Fluconazole, or in patients who are intolerant of these medicinal products.</li></ul><p>Also indicated for <strong>prophylaxis</strong> of invasive fungal infections in the following patients:</p><ul><li>Patients receiving remission-induction chemotherapy for <strong>Acute Myelogenous Leukemia (AML)</strong> or <strong>Myelodysplastic Syndromes (MDS)</strong> expected to result in prolonged neutropenia and who are at high risk of developing invasive fungal infections</li><li><strong>Hematopoietic Stem Cell Transplant (HSCT)</strong> recipients who are undergoing high-dose immunosuppressive therapy for graft versus host disease and who are at high risk of developing invasive fungal infections</li></ul><p><strong>Refractoriness</strong> is defined as progression of infection or failure to improve after a minimum of 7 days of prior therapeutic doses of effective antifungal therapy.</p><h3>Dosage of Posac 100 mg</h3><p>Posac 100 mg tablets can be taken with or without food. The tablets should be swallowed whole with water and should not be crushed, chewed, or broken.</p><ul><li><strong>Loading Dose</strong>: 300 mg (3 tablets) twice a day on the first day.</li><li><strong>Maintenance Dose</strong>: 300 mg (3 tablets) once a day from the second day.</li></ul><p>Duration of therapy should be based on the severity of the underlying disease, recovery from immunosuppression, and clinical response.</p><h3>Interaction of Posac 100 mg</h3><p>Posac 100 mg is an inhibitor of <strong>CYP3A4</strong> and should only be used under specific circumstances during treatment with other medicinal products that are metabolized by <strong>CYP3A4</strong> (e.g., <strong>Midazolam</strong>, <strong>Triazolam</strong>, <strong>Alprazolam</strong>). Posac 100 mg concentrations may be significantly lowered in combination with:</p><ul><li>Rifamycin antibacterials (<strong>Rifampicin</strong>, <strong>Rifabutin</strong>)</li><li>Certain anticonvulsants (<strong>Phenytoin</strong>, <strong>Carbamazepine</strong>, <strong>Phenobarbital</strong>, <strong>Primidone</strong>, <strong>Efavirenz</strong>)</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Posac 100 mg is contraindicated in persons with known hypersensitivity to Posac 100 mg or other azole antifungal agents. It should not be co-administered with:</p><ul><li>Ergot alkaloids, <strong>CYP3A4 substrates</strong>, <strong>Terfenadine</strong>, <strong>Astemizole</strong>, <strong>Cisapride</strong>, <strong>Pimozide</strong>, <strong>Halofantrine</strong>, or <strong>Quinidine</strong> (due to risk of increased plasma concentrations leading to QTc prolongation and torsades de pointes)</li><li><strong>HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors</strong> (<strong>Simvastatin</strong>, <strong>Lovastatin</strong>, <strong>Atorvastatin</strong>)</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Posac 100 mg</h3><p>Common treatment-emergent adverse reactions in studies with Posac 100 mg include:</p><ul><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Fever</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Headache</li><li>Coughing</li><li>Hypokalemia</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><p>Store below <strong>25°C</strong>. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li>Aural Anti-fungal preparations</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Posac 100 mg blocks the synthesis of <strong>ergosterol</strong>, a key component of the fungal cell membrane. It inhibits the cytochrome P-450 dependent enzyme <strong>Lanosterol 14-α-demethylase</strong>, responsible for the conversion of lanosterol to ergosterol in the fungal cell membrane. This results in an accumulation of methylated sterol precursors and a depletion of ergosterol, weakening the structure and function of the fungal cell membrane.</p><h3>Pregnancy</h3><p>Posac 100 mg must not be used during pregnancy unless the benefit to the mother clearly outweighs the potential risk to the fetus. <strong>Breast-feeding</strong> must be stopped on initiation of treatment with this drug.</p><h3>Pediatric Uses</h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients below the age of 13 years have not been established. Therefore, Posac 100 mg is not recommended for pediatric patients under 13 years of age.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No dose adjustment is necessary for geriatric patients.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Posac 100 mg should be used with caution in patients with hepatic impairment due to limited clinical experience. Liver function tests should be monitored.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment is required for patients with mild to moderate renal impairment. Patients with severe renal impairment should be monitored closely for breakthrough fungal infections.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1247_1742274896.webp', 44, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:14:56', '2025-03-18 10:14:56'),
(3229, 'Mexiderm-N', 'Betamethasone + Neomycin Sulphate', 22.00, 1, 'cream', '1 cream box', 'Cream', '10 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> This cream is typically used for treating <strong>superficial infections</strong> caused by gram-positive and gram-negative microorganisms. It is also effective in:</p><ul><li><strong>Burns</strong></li><li><strong>Regeneration of new skin</strong> in wounds and ulceration</li><li><strong>Otitis externa</strong></li><li><strong>Postoperative infections</strong></li><li><strong>Neurodermatitis and eczema</strong></li><li>May be used as an <strong>adjunct to systemic steroid therapy</strong> in special cases of pyoderma</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Betamethasone</strong> is an active topical corticosteroid that provides a rapid response in <strong>inflammatory dermatoses</strong> and <strong>psoriasis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Neomycin Sulfate</strong> is a broad-spectrum bactericidal <strong>aminoglycoside</strong> effective against gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria like <em>Proteus vulgaris</em> and <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>.</li><li>This preparation ensures <strong>effective penetration and uniform distribution</strong>, making it suitable for <strong>hairy and intertriginous areas</strong>.</li><li>It is highly effective in treating <strong>wet ulcerations of the skin</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Use in adults:</strong></p><ul><li>Apply <strong>a thin layer</strong> to the affected area <strong>3 to 4 times daily</strong>.</li><li>For chronic conditions, <strong>gradually reduce the frequency</strong> until the cream is used <strong>once a week</strong>.</li><li>Or, use as directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in children:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Not recommended for children under 1 year of age</strong>.</li><li>Treatment duration should <strong>not exceed 5 days</strong> unless advised by a physician.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> No significant drug interactions have been reported with <strong>Betamethasone Valerate BP and Neomycin Sulphate BP</strong> Cream/Ointment.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> This preparation is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to any of its components</li><li><strong>Otitis externa with a perforated eardrum</strong></li><li><strong>Skin lesions caused by viral or fungal infections</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Mexiderm-N is generally well tolerated. However, prolonged use or high doses may cause:</p><ul><li><strong>Cushing’s syndrome</strong></li><li><strong>Acne</strong></li><li><strong>Thinning and dilatation of blood vessels</strong></li><li>Risk increases when <strong>occlusive dressings</strong> are used</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> This preparation <strong>should not be used extensively during pregnancy</strong>.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Avoid long-term continuous use</strong>, as it may lead to the development of <strong>resistant microorganisms</strong>.</li><li><strong>Apply only to infected areas</strong> to ensure accurate dosing.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Aural steroid &amp; antibiotic combined preparations</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from <strong>light and moisture</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '806_1742275080.jpg', 69, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:18:00', '2025-03-18 10:18:00'),
(3230, 'Montair ODT', 'Montelukast Sodium', 8.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '674_1742275156.webp', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:19:16', '2025-03-18 10:19:16'),
(3231, 'Pen-G', 'Benzyl Penicillin', 16.73, 1, 'Injection', '10 pieces box', 'Injection', '10 lac unit per vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pen-G 10 lac unit/vial</strong> For use in the treatment of severe infections caused by penicillin G-susceptible microorganisms when rapid and high penicillin levels are required such as in the treatment of septicemia, meningitis, pericarditis, endocarditis, and severe pneumonia.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong> Benzylpenicillin &amp; Phenoxymethyl penicillin</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong> Benzylpenicillin has a bactericidal action against gram-positive bacteria, gram-negative cocci, and some other gram-negative organisms. It inhibits the cross-linking stage of peptidoglycan production by binding and inactivating transpeptidases on the inner surface of the bacterial cell membrane, thereby inhibiting bacterial cell wall synthesis. It is inactivated by penicillinase and other β-lactamases.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Pen-G 10 lac unit/vial</strong> <strong>Adult:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral:</strong> Susceptible infections: 125-312 mg every 4-6 hours.</li><li><strong>IM/IV:</strong> Susceptible infections: 0.6-3.6 g/day in 4-6 divided doses.</li><li><strong>IV:</strong> Bacterial endocarditis: 7.2-12 g/day in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Intrapartum prophylaxis against Group B Streptococcal infection in neonates:</strong> Initial: 3 g, followed by 1.5 g every 4 hours until delivery.</li><li><strong>Meningococcal meningitis, Pneumococcal meningitis:</strong> 2.4 g every 4 hours. Max: 18 g/day in meningococcal meningitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Child:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV (Meningococcal meningitis, Pneumococcal meningitis):Newborn infants:</strong> 100 mg/kg daily in 2 divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1-4 weeks:</strong> 150 mg/kg daily in 3 divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>&gt;1 month to 12 years:</strong> 180-300 mg/kg daily in 4-6 divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Parenteral (Susceptible infections):Newborn infants:</strong> 50 mg/kg daily in 2 divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1-4 weeks:</strong> 75 mg/kg daily in 3 divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>&gt;1 month to 12 years:</strong> 100 mg/kg daily in divided doses, not exceeding 4 g/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Pen-G 10 lac unit/vial</strong> Should be taken on an empty stomach. Take with a full glass of water 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals. Avoid acidic beverages within 1 hour of a dose.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Pen-G 10 lac unit/vial</strong></p><ul><li>May increase the risk of methotrexate toxicity.</li><li>Increased plasma concentration with probenecid.</li><li>Antagonism of bactericidal effect with bacteriostatic antibacterials (e.g., erythromycin, tetracyclines).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong> Hypersensitivity to benzylpenicillin and other penicillins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Pen-G 10 lac unit/vial</strong> Nausea, vomiting, stomatitis, black or hairy tongue, rash, fever, serum-like sickness, convulsions, interstitial nephritis, haemolytic anaemia, granulocytopenia, agranulocytosis, leucopenia, thrombocytopenia.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> Animal reproduction studies have not demonstrated fetal risk, but there are no controlled studies in pregnant women.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Monitor electrolyte, hepatic, renal, cardiac, and hematologic function; watch for signs of anaphylaxis during the first dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with a history of allergy, especially β-lactam allergy and/or asthma.</li><li>Seizure disorders.</li><li>Diabetic patients.</li><li>Renal impairment.</li><li>Pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Pen-G 10 lac unit/vial</strong> <strong>Symptoms:</strong> Agitation, confusion, asterixis, hallucinations, stupor, coma, multifocal myoclonus, seizures, encephalopathy, and hyperkalemia. <strong>Management:</strong> Symptomatic and supportive treatment.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong> Store between 20-25°C. Reconstituted solution: Store between 2-8°C.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong> Loosen the powder, then hold the vial horizontally and rotate it while slowly directing the stream of diluent against the wall of the vial. Shake the vial vigorously after all the diluent has been added. Depending on the route of administration, reconstitute with sterile water for injection, NaCl 0.9% injection, or dextrose 5% injection.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong> Benzylpenicillin &amp; Phenoxymethyl penicillin</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong> Benzylpenicillin has a bactericidal action against gram-positive bacteria, gram-negative cocci, and some other gram-negative organisms. It inhibits the cross-linking stage of peptidoglycan production by binding and inactivating transpeptidases on the inner surface of the bacterial cell membrane, thereby inhibiting bacterial cell wall synthesis. It is inactivated by penicillinase and other β-lactamases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong> <strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> Animal reproduction studies have not demonstrated fetal risk, but there are no controlled studies in pregnant women. <strong>Lactation:</strong> Monitor electrolyte, hepatic, renal, cardiac, and hematologic function; watch for signs of anaphylaxis during the first dose.</p>', 'active', '1983_1742275174.jpg', 7, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:19:34', '2025-03-18 10:19:34'),
(3232, 'Ajardy', 'Empagliflozin', 25.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you provided with the headings formatted in bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Ajardy 10 mg</strong></p><p> Ajardy 10 mg is indicated in:</p><ul><li>As an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycemic control in adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus.</li><li>To reduce the risk of cardiovascular death in adult patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus and established cardiovascular disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Sodium-glucose Cotransporter-2 (SGLT2) Inhibitors</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Ajardy 10 mg is a sodium glucose co-transporter-2 (SGLT-2) inhibitor. SGLT2 co-transporters are responsible for reabsorption of glucose from the glomerular filtrate in the kidney. The glucuretic effect resulting from SGLT2 inhibition reduces renal absorption and lowers the renal threshold for glucose, resulting in increased glucose excretion. Additionally, it contributes to reduced hyperglycaemia, assists weight loss, and reduces blood pressure.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ajardy 10 mg</strong></p><p> The recommended dose of Ajardy 10 mg is 10 mg once daily, taken in the morning, with or without food. In patients tolerating Ajardy 10 mg, the dose may be increased to 25 mg once daily. In patients with volume depletion, correcting this condition prior to initiation of Ajardy 10 mg is recommended.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Ajardy 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: Co-administration of Ajardy 10 mg with diuretics resulted in increased urine volume.</li><li><strong>Insulin or Insulin Secretagogues</strong>: Co-administration of Ajardy 10 mg with insulin or insulin secretagogues increases the risk for hypoglycemia.</li><li><strong>Positive Urine Glucose Test</strong>: Monitoring glycemic control with urine glucose tests is not recommended in patients taking SGLT2 inhibitors as SGLT2 inhibitors increase urinary glucose excretion and will lead to positive urine glucose tests. Use alternative methods to monitor glycemic control.</li><li><strong>Interference with 1,5-anhydroglucitol (1,5-AG) Assay</strong>: Monitoring glycemic control with 1,5-AG assay is not recommended as measurements of 1,5-AG are unreliable in assessing glycemic control in patients taking SGLT2 inhibitors. Use alternative methods to monitor glycemic control.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Ajardy 10 mg is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>History of serious hypersensitivity reaction to Ajardy 10 mg or any of its ingredients.</li><li>Severe renal impairment, end-stage renal disease, or dialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ajardy 10 mg</strong></p><p> The most common adverse reactions associated with Ajardy 10 mg are urinary tract infections and female genital mycotic infections. Other common side effects include dehydration, hypotension, weakness, dizziness, and increased thirstiness.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>There are no adequate and well-controlled studies of Ajardy 10 mg in pregnant women. Ajardy 10 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li>It is not known if Ajardy 10 mg is excreted in human milk. It is not recommended when breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Assessment of renal function is recommended prior to initiation of Ajardy 10 mg and periodically thereafter. Ajardy 10 mg should not be initiated in patients with an eGFR less than 45 ml/min/1.73 m². No dose adjustment is needed in patients with an eGFR greater than or equal to 45 ml/min/1.73 m².</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ajardy 10 mg</strong></p><p> In the event of an overdose with Ajardy 10 mg, the usual supportive measures (e.g., remove unabsorbed material from the gastrointestinal tract, perform clinical monitoring, and institute supportive treatment) should be employed. Removal of Ajardy 10 mg by hemodialysis has not been studied.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a cool &amp; dry place (below 30°C), protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Sodium-glucose Cotransporter-2 (SGLT2) Inhibitors</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Ajardy 10 mg is a sodium glucose co-transporter-2 (SGLT-2) inhibitor. SGLT2 co-transporters are responsible for reabsorption of glucose from the glomerular filtrate in the kidney. The glucuretic effect resulting from SGLT2 inhibition reduces renal absorption and lowers the renal threshold for glucose, resulting in increased glucose excretion. Additionally, it contributes to reduced hyperglycaemia, assists weight loss, and reduces blood pressure.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies of Ajardy 10 mg in pregnant women. Ajardy 10 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known if Ajardy 10 mg is excreted in human milk. It is not recommended when breastfeeding.</p>', 'active', '834_1742275282.jpg', 27, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:21:22', '2025-03-18 10:21:22'),
(3233, 'Dotfix', 'Dotinurad', 24.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1804_1742275430.jpg', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:23:50', '2025-03-18 10:23:50'),
(3234, 'Carbate', 'Carbetocin', 150.00, 1, 'Injection', '1 ml ampoule', 'Injection', '100 mcg per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Carbate 100 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> Carbate 100 mcg/ml is indicated for the prevention of uterine atony and postpartum haemorrhage (excessive bleeding) following delivery of the infant by elective caesarean section under epidural or spinal anaesthesia.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs acting on the Uterus</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Carbate 100 mcg/ml is a drug used to control postpartum hemorrhage, bleeding after giving birth. It is an analogue of oxytocin, and its action is similar to that of oxytocin; it causes contraction of the uterus. Carbate 100 mcg/ml binds to oxytocin receptors present on the smooth musculature of the uterus, resulting in rhythmic contractions of the uterus, increased frequency of existing contractions, and increased uterine tone. The oxytocin receptor content of the uterus is very low in the non-pregnant state, and increases during pregnancy, reaching a peak at the time of delivery.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Carbate 100 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> A single intravenous dose of 100 mcg of Carbate 100 mcg/ml injection is administered by bolus injection, slowly over 1 minute, only when infant delivery has been completed by caesarean section under epidural or spinal anaesthesia. Carbate 100 mcg/ml is to be used as a single dose only.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Carbate 100 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> A single intravenous dose of 100 mcg of Carbate 100 mcg/ml injection is administered by bolus injection, slowly over 1 minute, only when infant delivery has been completed by caesarean section under epidural or spinal anaesthesia. Carbate 100 mcg/ml is to be used as a single dose only.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Carbate 100 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> No specific drug interactions have been reported with Carbate 100 mcg/ml.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Because of its long duration of action relative to oxytocin, uterine contractions produced by Carbate 100 mcg/ml cannot be stopped by simply discontinuing the medication. Therefore, Carbate 100 mcg/ml should not be administered prior to delivery of the infant for any reason, including elective or medical induction of labour. Inappropriate use of Carbate 100 mcg/ml during pregnancy could theoretically mimic the symptoms of oxytocin overdosage, including hyperstimulation of the uterus with strong (hypertonic) or prolonged (tetanic) contractions, tumultuous labour, uterine rupture, cervical and vaginal lacerations, postpartum haemorrhage, utero-placental hypoperfusion and variable deceleration of foetal heart, foetal hypoxia, hypercapnia, or death.</p><p>Carbate 100 mcg/ml should not be used in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to oxytocin or Carbate 100 mcg/ml. Carbate 100 mcg/ml should not be used in patients with cardiovascular disease, especially coronary artery disease, valvular heart disease, cardiomyopathy and heart failure. Carbate 100 mcg/ml is not intended for use in children.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Carbate 100 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> 10-40% of patients experienced nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, itching skin, increased body temperature, trembling and weakness. Infrequent adverse events (1-5% of patients) included back pain, dizziness, metallic taste, anaemia, sweating, chest pain, dyspnoea, chills, tachycardia and anxiety.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Use of Carbate 100 mcg/ml injection is contraindicated during pregnancy. Small amounts of Carbate 100 mcg/ml have been shown to cross over from plasma into the breast milk of nursing women. The small amount of Carbate 100 mcg/ml ingested by an infant would not be expected to present a significant safety concern.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> In patients who may not have an adequate uterine contraction after a single injection of Carbate 100 mcg/ml, more aggressive treatment with additional doses of oxytocin or ergometrine is warranted. As Carbate 100 mcg/ml is closely related in structure to oxytocin, hyponatraemia may occur.</p><p>Carbate 100 mcg/ml should be used cautiously in the presence of epilepsy, migraine, asthma or any state in which a rapid addition to extracellular water may produce hazard for an already overburdened system. Patients with eclampsia and pre-eclampsia should be monitored for changes in blood pressure. Carbate 100 mcg/ml is not recommended for use in elderly patients.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Carbate 100 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> Overdosage of Carbate 100 mcg/ml can be expected to produce enhanced pharmacological effects associated with uterine hyperactivity and pain. Treatment consists of symptomatic and supportive management.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at 2-8°C. Do not freeze. Keep away from light. Once the ampoule has been opened, the product should be used immediately.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Drugs acting on the Uterus</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Carbate 100 mcg/ml is a drug used to control postpartum hemorrhage, bleeding after giving birth. It is an analogue of oxytocin, and its action is similar to that of oxytocin; it causes contraction of the uterus. Carbate 100 mcg/ml binds to oxytocin receptors present on the smooth musculature of the uterus, resulting in rhythmic contractions of the uterus, increased frequency of existing contractions, and increased uterine tone. The oxytocin receptor content of the uterus is very low in the non-pregnant state, and increases during pregnancy, reaching a peak at the time of delivery.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Use of Carbate 100 mcg/ml injection is contraindicated during pregnancy. Small amounts of Carbate 100 mcg/ml have been shown to cross over from plasma into the breast milk of nursing women. The small amount of Carbate 100 mcg/ml ingested by an infant would not be expected to present a significant safety concern.</p>', 'active', '1316_1742275489.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:24:49', '2025-03-18 10:24:49'),
(3235, 'UDCA', 'Ursodeoxycholic Acid', 25.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '300 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p>UDCA is used to treat:</p><ul><li><strong>Cholestasis (Jaundice)</strong></li><li><strong>Viral Hepatitis</strong></li><li><strong>Alcoholic Fatty Liver</strong></li><li><strong>Primary Biliary Cirrhosis (PBC)</strong></li><li><strong>Primary Sclerosing Cholangitis (PSC)</strong></li><li><strong>Dissolution of Gallstones</strong></li><li><strong>Non-Alcoholic Steatohepatitis (NASH)</strong></li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Description</strong></h3><p>UDCA helps <strong>reduce cholesterol saturation</strong> in bile and <strong>dissolve gallstones</strong>. It works by:</p><ul><li><strong>Lowering cholesterol secretion</strong> into bile</li><li><strong>Increasing bile acid secretion rate</strong></li><li>Maintaining <strong>phospholipid levels</strong> without reducing them</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p><strong>Ursodeoxycholic Acid (UDCA)</strong> is a naturally occurring <strong>bile acid</strong> that helps treat hepatobiliary disorders.</p><h4><strong>Mechanism of Action:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Reduces cholesterol absorption</strong></li><li><strong>Suppresses liver cholesterol synthesis</strong></li><li><strong>Alters bile composition</strong> from supersaturated to unsaturated</li><li><strong>Promotes removal of cholesterol</strong> from the gallbladder</li></ul><h4><strong>Effects on Cholestatic Liver Diseases:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Protects cholangiocytes</strong> from cytotoxic bile acids</li><li><strong>Stimulates hepatobiliary secretion</strong></li><li><strong>Prevents bile acid-induced hepatocyte apoptosis</strong></li></ul><h4><strong>Absorption &amp; Metabolism:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Completely absorbed</strong> in the upper intestine (60-80%)</li><li><strong>Peak concentration</strong> reached within <strong>30-150 minutes</strong></li><li><strong>Metabolized in the liver</strong> and conjugated with <strong>glycine or taurine</strong></li><li><strong>Eliminated mainly through bile</strong>, with minimal excretion in urine</li><li><strong>Biological half-life</strong>: <strong>3.5-5.8 days</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Dissolution of Gallstones</strong>: <strong>8-12 mg/kg/day</strong> (single nighttime dose or divided doses)</li><li><strong>Primary Biliary Cirrhosis</strong>: <strong>10-15 mg/kg/day</strong> (divided into 2-4 doses)</li><li><strong>Acute Viral Hepatitis</strong>: <strong>600 mg/day</strong></li><li><strong>Alcoholic Fatty Liver</strong>: <strong>300 mg/day</strong></li><li><strong>Primary Sclerosing Cholangitis</strong>: <strong>25-30 mg/kg/day</strong></li><li><strong>Non-Alcoholic Steatohepatitis (NASH)</strong>: <strong>13-15 mg/kg/day</strong></li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Interaction</strong></h3><p>Avoid using UDCA with:</p><ul><li><strong>Estrogenic hormones</strong> (increase bile cholesterol)</li><li><strong>Bile-acid binding drugs</strong> (e.g., antacids, charcoal, cholestyramine)</li></ul><p>These may <strong>reduce the effectiveness</strong> of UDCA therapy.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><p>UDCA is not recommended for patients with:</p><ul><li><strong>Non-functioning gallbladder</strong></li><li><strong>Calcified and pigmented gallstones</strong></li><li><strong>Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD)</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p>Commonly reported side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea</strong></li><li><strong>Gallstone opacification</strong></li><li><strong>Pruritus (itching)</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: No evidence of harm in pregnancy</li><li>Used safely for <strong>cholestasis of pregnancy</strong> in the <strong>last trimester</strong></li><li>No reported issues with <strong>breastfeeding</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Use with caution</strong> in patients with <strong>liver disease</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p>Anti-gallstone drugs: <strong>Bile Acids</strong></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Store below 25°C</strong>, protected from <strong>light and moisture</strong></li><li><strong>Keep out of reach of children</strong></li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1584_1742275514.jpg', 69, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:25:14', '2025-03-18 10:25:14');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3236, 'Clonil', 'Clonazepam', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Clonil 2 mg</strong></p><p> Clonil 2 mg serves as a treatment for panic disorder, whether with or without agoraphobia. Panic disorder manifests through unexpected panic attacks and the accompanying worry about experiencing more of these episodes, along with concerns about their implications or consequences.</p><p>Additionally, it is indicated for use on its own or as an adjunct in treating Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome (specifically the petit mal variant), akinetic seizures, and myoclonic seizures. It may also be considered for patients with absence seizures (petit mal) who have not responded to succinimides.</p><p>It\'s important to note that the long-term effectiveness of the medicine, specifically for durations exceeding 9 weeks, hasn\'t been comprehensively studied in controlled clinical trials. Therefore, physicians who choose to prescribe Clonil 2 mg for extended periods should periodically reassess its long-term suitability for each patient.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs, Benzodiazepine hypnotics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Clonil 2 mg works by reducing nerve transmission in the motor cortex, effectively suppressing the spike and wave discharges associated with absence seizures. This mechanism is thought to be linked to its ability to enhance the activity of GABA. In clinical applications, it has proven effective in improving both focal epilepsy and generalized seizures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Clonil 2 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Oral Dosage for Adults with Seizure Disorders:</strong></p><p> Begin with 1.5 mg per day, divided into three doses. Adjust by 0.5-1 mg every three days, up to a maximum of 20 mg per day.</p><p><strong>Oral Dosage for Adults with Panic Disorder:</strong></p><p> Start with 0.25 mg twice daily. After three days, increase to 1 mg per day as needed.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Patients:</strong></p><p> To minimize drowsiness, infants and children (up to 10 years or 30 kg) should take 0.01-0.03 mg/kg/day, not exceeding 0.05 mg/kg/day, split into multiple doses.</p><p><strong>Injection Dosage:</strong></p><p> <strong>Children:</strong> Administer 0.5 mg via slow IV injection or infusion.</p><p> <strong>Adults:</strong> Use 1 mg via slow IV injection or infusion, with the option to repeat if necessary (typically 1-4 mg).</p><p> Ensure a controlled IV rate of 0.25 - 0.5 mg per minute (equivalent to 0.5-1.0 ml of the solution), and do not exceed a total dose of 10 mg.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Clonil 2 mg</strong></p><p> Clonil 2 mg does not seem to impact the pharmacokinetics of phenytoin, carbamazepine, or phenobarbital. However, its effect on the metabolism of other drugs remains unexplored.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Avoid prescribing it to patients with a history of hypersensitivity to benzodiazepines or those showing clinical or biochemical signs of significant liver disease. It can be administered to patients with open-angle glaucoma who are receiving appropriate treatment but should not be used in cases of acute narrow-angle glaucoma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Clonil 2 mg</strong></p><p> The most commonly observed side effects of Clonil 2 mg are related to central nervous system (CNS) depression. Experience in treating seizures has revealed that drowsiness occurs in around 50% of patients, while approximately 30% experience ataxia. In some instances, these effects may diminish over time.</p><p>Additionally, behavior problems have been reported in about 25% of patients. Other potential side effects include abnormal eye movements, aphonia, coma, tremor, vertigo, confusion, depression, amnesia, hallucinations, hysteria, increased libido, insomnia, psychosis, and palpitations.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Clonil 2 mg should be considered during pregnancy only when the potential benefits outweigh the risks to the fetus. It is recommended that women taking the medicine should avoid breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> When administered to patients with multiple coexisting seizure disorders, this medication can potentially raise the occurrence or trigger the onset of generalized tonic-clonic seizures. In such cases, it may be necessary to supplement with suitable anticonvulsants or adjust their dosages accordingly. Combining valproic acid with Clonil 2 mg may lead to the development of absence status.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Clonil 2 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Symptoms:</strong> Benzodiazepines commonly result in drowsiness, ataxia, dysarthria, and nystagmus. In cases of Clonil 2 mg overdose taken alone, it is rarely life-threatening but may lead to areflexia, apnea, hypotension, cardiorespiratory depression, and coma. Coma, if it occurs, typically lasts a few hours, but it may be more prolonged and cyclical in elderly patients.</p><p>Elevated seizure frequency can occur with supratherapeutic plasma concentrations. It\'s important to note that benzodiazepine-induced respiratory depression poses a greater risk for individuals with respiratory conditions. Additionally, benzodiazepines can amplify the effects of other central nervous system depressants, including alcohol.</p><p><strong>Treatment:</strong> Monitor the patient\'s vital signs and provide appropriate supportive care based on their clinical condition. Symptomatic treatment for cardiorespiratory and central nervous system effects may be necessary. To prevent further drug absorption, consider timely administration of activated charcoal within 1-2 hours. When using activated charcoal, ensure airway protection for drowsy patients.</p><p>Gastric lavage may be considered in cases of mixed ingestion but should not be routine. In cases of severe CNS depression, flumazenil, a benzodiazepine antagonist, may be an option. However, it should be administered under close monitoring due to its short half-life (approximately one hour). Patients given flumazenil will require monitoring after its effects wear off. Use flumazenil with extreme caution when other drugs that lower the seizure threshold (e.g., tricyclic antidepressants) are present. For detailed information on the correct use of flumazenil, refer to the prescribing information.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p> For slow intravenous injection, it\'s crucial to dilute the vial\'s contents with 1 ml of water for injection before administering to prevent irritation of the veins. This injection solution should be prepared just before use. During IV injection, it should be administered slowly while continuously monitoring EEG, respiration, and blood pressure.</p><p>When opting for intravenous infusion, Clonil 2 mg (from the vial) can be diluted for infusion at a ratio of 1 vial (1 mg) to at least 85 ml of diluting media. Suitable diluting media options include sodium chloride 0.9%, sodium chloride 0.45% + glucose 2.5%, glucose 5%, or glucose 10%. These mixtures remain stable for 24 hours at room temperature.</p><p>If PVC infusion bags are used, the mixture should be infused immediately or within 4 hours. The infusion should not exceed 8 hours. Avoid preparing Clonil 2 mg infusions using sodium bicarbonate solution, as it may cause solution precipitation.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs, Benzodiazepine hypnotics</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Clonil 2 mg shares pharmacological properties common to benzodiazepines, encompassing anticonvulsive, sedative, muscle-relaxing, and anxiolytic effects. The central actions of benzodiazepines involve enhancing GABAergic neurotransmission at inhibitory synapses.</p>', 'active', '1409_1742275692.jpg', 63, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:28:12', '2025-03-18 10:28:12'),
(3237, 'Salburen', 'Salbutamol', 250.00, 1, 'x 200 metered doses', '200 metered doses', 'Inhaler', '100 mcg per puff', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Salburen 100 mcg/puff</strong></h3><p>Salburen 100 mcg/puff is indicated as a bronchodilator for use in:</p><ul><li><strong>Asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Chronic Bronchitis</strong></li><li><strong>Emphysema</strong></li><li><strong>Other conditions associated with airways obstruction</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Salburen 100 mcg/puff is a selective beta2-adrenoceptor agonist. At therapeutic doses, it acts on the beta-2-adrenoceptors of bronchial smooth muscle, with little or no action on the ß1-adrenoceptors of cardiac muscle. It provides short-acting (4-6 hours) bronchodilatation with a fast onset (within 5 minutes) in reversible airways obstruction. It also has an anti-inflammatory effect on mast cells, causing inhibition of the release of bronchoconstrictor mediators including histamine, neutrophil chemotactic factor (NCF), and prostaglandin D2.</p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Salburen 100 mcg/puff</strong></h3><h4><strong>Oral</strong></h4><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong>2-6 years: 2.5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years: 5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Over 12 years: 5-10 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily (or 2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong>2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily (Maximum single dose is 8 mg tablet)</li></ul><h4><strong>Injection</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Subcutaneous route: 500 mcg (8 mcg/kg), repeated every four hours as required</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Intramuscular route: 500 mcg (8 mcg/kg), repeated every four hours as required</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Slow intravenous injection: 250 mcg (4 mcg/kg) injected slowly (may be repeated if necessary)</li><li><strong>Pediatric Population:</strong></li><li>The safety and efficacy in children under the age of 12 has not been established. For children aged 12 years and over, dose as per adults.</li></ul><h4><strong>Respirator Solution</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Elderly:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0.5 ml-1.0 ml Salburen 100 mcg/puff up to four times a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Up to 40mg per day may be given under strict medical direction in the hospital.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dilute to 2-4 ml with sterile normal saline solution (10-minute time).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">May be used undiluted for intermittent administration.</li><li><strong>Children under 12 years:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0.5 ml solution diluted to 2.0-4.0 ml with normal saline.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Some children may require higher doses up to 1.0 ml solution.</li></ul><h4><strong>Nebulizer Solution (Intermittent Administration)</strong></h4><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; Elderly:</strong>2.5 mg to 5 mg Salburen 100 mcg/puff, up to 4 times a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Up to 40 mg/day may be given in a hospital setting.</li></ul><h4><strong>Injection</strong></h4><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong>Infusion solution diluted to 10 mcg/ml. Infusion rates from 5 mcg/min to 20 mcg/min are typical, adjusted at 15-30 minute intervals.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>History of hypersensitivity to Salburen 100 mcg/puff or any of its components.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Salburen 100 mcg/puff</strong></h3><ul><li>Fine tremor of skeletal muscles (particularly the hands), palpitations, muscle cramps, tachycardia, tenseness, headaches, peripheral vasodilatation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> The drug should only be used during pregnancy if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is unknown if the drug is excreted in human milk. Given the potential tumorigenicity shown in some animal studies, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or the drug.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with hyperthyroidism, cardiovascular disease, occlusive vascular disorders, hypertension, and aneurysms.</li><li>Hypokalaemia may occur with high doses, leading to increased susceptibility to digitalis-induced cardiac arrhythmias.</li><li>Tachyphylaxis may occur with prolonged use of high dosages.</li><li>Caution is necessary when treating patients with diabetes mellitus, closed-angle glaucoma, and those receiving antihypertensive therapy.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Salburen 100 mcg/puff</strong></h3><p>Symptoms include angina, headache, nausea, vomiting, and tremor. The preferred antidote for overdose is a cardio-selective beta-blocking agent, though beta-blocking drugs should be used with caution in patients with a history of bronchospasm.</p><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Salburen 100 mcg/puff is a synthetic sympathomimetic agent with predominant beta-2 adrenergic activity. It produces bronchodilatation through stimulation of beta-2 adrenergic receptors in bronchial smooth muscles, leading to relaxation of bronchial muscle fibers. This action improves pulmonary function, as demonstrated by spirometric measurements.</p><h3><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Use only if the potential benefit justifies the risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> The drug\'s excretion in human milk is not known. Decisions regarding nursing should be made considering the importance of the drug to the mother.</li></ul>', 'active', '278_1742275896.png', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:31:36', '2025-05-04 20:42:56'),
(3238, 'Ferintus', 'Ferric Carboxymaltose', 700.00, 1, 'Injection', '10 ml vial injection', 'Injection', '500 mg per 10 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ferintus 500 mg</strong></p><p> Ferintus 500 mg is indicated for the treatment of iron deficiency anemia in adult patients:</p><ul><li>Who have intolerance to oral iron or have had an unsatisfactory response to oral iron</li><li>Who have non-dialysis dependent chronic kidney disease</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Parenteral Iron Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Non-dextran, IV is a colloidal iron hydroxide in complex with carboxymaltose, a carbohydrate polymer that releases iron; replaces iron stores found in hemoglobin, myoglobin, and enzymes; works to transport oxygen via hemoglobin. Macrophages engulf FCM from blood and control iron release. Transferrin saturates and transports iron into the liver, spleen, and bone marrow.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ferintus 500 mg</strong></p><p> For patients weighing 50 kg or more: Give Ferintus 500 mg in two doses separated by at least 7 days. Each dose should be 750 mg, with a total cumulative dose not exceeding 1500 mg of iron per course.</p><p> For patients weighing less than 50 kg: Give Ferintus 500 mg in two doses separated by at least 7 days. Each dose should be 15 mg/kg body weight, with a total cumulative dose not exceeding 1500 mg of iron per course.</p><p> The dosage of Ferintus 500 mg is expressed in mg of elemental iron. Each mL of Ferintus 500 mg contains 50 mg of elemental iron. Treatment may be repeated if iron deficiency anemia reoccurs.</p><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><ul><li>Administer Ferintus 500 mg intravenously, either as an undiluted slow intravenous push or by infusion.</li><li>When administering as a slow intravenous push, give at a rate of approximately 100 mg (2 mL) per minute.</li><li>When administered via infusion, dilute up to 750 mg of iron in no more than 250 mL of sterile 0.9% sodium chloride injection, USP, ensuring the concentration is not less than 2 mg of iron per mL and administer over at least 15 minutes.</li><li>When added to an infusion bag containing 0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection, USP, at concentrations ranging from 2 mg to 4 mg of iron per mL, Ferintus 500 mg solution remains physically and chemically stable for 72 hours when stored at room temperature.</li><li>Do not dilute to concentrations less than 2 mg iron/mL.</li><li>Inspect parenteral drug products visually for particulate matter and discoloration prior to administration.</li><li>Each vial of Ferintus 500 mg is intended for single use only. Discard any unused drug remaining after injection.</li><li>Avoid extravasation of Ferintus 500 mg since brown discoloration of the extravasation site may be long-lasting. Monitor for extravasation. If extravasation occurs, discontinue the administration at that site.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ferintus 500 mg</strong></p><p> There are no known drug interactions and none well documented.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ferintus 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Hypertension</li><li>Flushing</li><li>Decreased blood phosphorus</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Pruritus</li><li>Rash</li><li>Urticaria</li><li>Wheezing</li><li>Injection site discoloration</li><li>Headache</li><li>Increased alanine aminotransferase</li><li>Dysgeusia</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Serious anaphylactic/anaphylactoid reactions</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C: Animal reproduction studies have shown an adverse effect on the fetus. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in humans, but potential benefits may warrant use of the drug in pregnant women despite potential risks.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity Reactions:</strong> Serious hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylactic-type reactions, some of which have been life-threatening and fatal, have been reported. Patients may present with shock, clinically significant hypotension, loss of consciousness, and/or collapse.</li><li>Monitor patients for signs and symptoms of hypersensitivity during and after Ferric carboxymaltose administration for at least 30 minutes and until clinically stable following completion of the infusion.</li><li>Only administer when personnel and therapies are immediately available for the treatment of serious hypersensitivity reactions.</li><li>Other severe adverse reactions, including pruritus, rash, urticaria, wheezing, or hypotension, may occur.</li><li><strong>Hypertension:</strong> Transient elevations in systolic blood pressure, sometimes occurring with facial flushing, dizziness, or nausea, were observed. These elevations generally occurred immediately after dosing and resolved within 30 minutes. Monitor patients for signs and symptoms of hypertension following each administration.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Do not store above 30°C. Do not freeze.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Parenteral Iron Preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Non-dextran, IV is a colloidal iron hydroxide in complex with carboxymaltose, a carbohydrate polymer that releases iron; replaces iron stores found in hemoglobin, myoglobin, and enzymes; works to transport oxygen via hemoglobin. Macrophages engulf FCM from blood and control iron release. Transferrin saturates and transports iron into the liver, spleen, and bone marrow.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C: Animal reproduction studies have shown an adverse effect on the fetus. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in humans, but potential benefits may warrant use of the drug in pregnant women despite potential risks.</p>', 'active', '165_1742275937.jpg', 3, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:32:17', '2025-03-18 10:32:17'),
(3239, 'Minomal R', 'Aminophylline', 1.23, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '175 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p>This medication is used for the <strong>treatment and prevention</strong> of bronchospasm associated with:</p><ul><li><strong>Asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Emphysema</strong></li><li><strong>Chronic bronchitis</strong></li></ul><p>It is also indicated in <strong>adults</strong> for the treatment of:</p><ul><li><strong>Cardiac asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Left ventricular or congestive cardiac failure</strong></li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p><strong>Aminophylline</strong> is a combination of <strong>theophylline</strong> and <strong>ethylenediamine</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>Ethylenediamine</strong>: Increases the <strong>solubility</strong> of theophylline in water (inactive component).</li><li><strong>Theophylline</strong>: Relaxes <strong>bronchial smooth muscle</strong> through multiple mechanisms:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Inhibiting phosphodiesterase</strong>, increasing intracellular <strong>cAMP</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adenosine receptor antagonism</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prostaglandin antagonism</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Effects on intracellular calcium</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><h4><strong>Oral (Chronic Bronchospasm)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 225-450 mg <strong>twice daily</strong> (may increase if necessary)</li><li><strong>Children (&gt;3 years old)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Modified-release form</strong>: <strong>12 mg/kg/day</strong>, can increase to <strong>24 mg/kg/day</strong> in <strong>2 divided doses</strong> after 1 week</li></ul><h4><strong>Intravenous (Acute Severe Bronchospasm)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Loading dose</strong>: 5 mg/kg (ideal body weight) or <strong>250-500 mg</strong> (25 mg/ml) by <strong>slow injection/infusion</strong> over <strong>20-30 minutes</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance dose</strong>: <strong>0.5 mg/kg/hour</strong> (Max: 25 mg/min)</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Loading dose</strong>: Same as adults</li><li><strong>Maintenance dose</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>6 months - 9 years</strong>: <strong>1 mg/kg/hour</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>10-16 years</strong>: <strong>0.8 mg/kg/hour</strong></li><li><strong>Elderly &amp; Hepatic Impairment</strong>: <strong>Dose reduction may be necessary</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Administration</strong></h3><ul><li>Tablets <strong>should be swallowed whole</strong>—do not chew, as it may affect drug release.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><p><strong>Aminophylline should not be used in patients with:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity to xanthines or ethylenediamine</strong></li><li><strong>Active peptic ulcer</strong> (may increase gastric secretion volume and acidity)</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p>The most common adverse effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastric irritation, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, hematemesis, epigastric pain, and tremor</strong> (early signs of toxicity)</li></ul><p>High doses may cause:</p><ul><li><strong>Ventricular arrhythmias or seizures</strong> (first signs in severe cases)</li><li><strong>Peptic ulcer reactivation</strong></li><li><strong>Headache, irritability, restlessness, insomnia, twitching, convulsions, and reflex hyperexcitability</strong></li><li><strong>Palpitations, tachycardia, hypotension, circulatory failure, and flushing</strong></li><li><strong>Albuminuria, diuresis, and hematuria</strong></li><li><strong>Inappropriate ADH syndrome</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Use during pregnancy</strong> should be <strong>carefully considered</strong> based on risks vs. benefits.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><p>Aminophylline should be used with <strong>caution</strong> in patients with:</p><ul><li><strong>Peptic ulcer disease</strong></li><li><strong>Hyperthyroidism</strong></li><li><strong>Hypertension</strong></li><li><strong>Cardiac arrhythmias or cardiovascular disease</strong></li><li><strong>Epilepsy</strong></li></ul><p>It should also be used cautiously in patients with:</p><ul><li><strong>Heart failure</strong></li><li><strong>Liver dysfunction</strong></li><li><strong>Chronic alcoholism</strong></li><li><strong>Acute febrile illness</strong></li><li><strong>Neonates and elderly patients</strong> (as <strong>theophylline clearance</strong> may be <strong>reduced</strong>, leading to <strong>higher serum levels</strong> and increased drug half-life)</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Bronchodilator</strong></li><li><strong>Methyl xanthine derivatives</strong></li><li><strong>Respiratory stimulants (Analeptics)</strong></li><li><strong>Theophylline &amp; related drugs</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store <strong>below 30°C</strong>, protected from <strong>light and moisture</strong></li><li>Keep <strong>out of reach of children</strong></li></ul>', 'in-active', '1478_1742276028.jpg', 56, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:33:48', '2025-05-08 20:30:45'),
(3240, 'Salburen Refill', 'Salbutamol', 220.00, 1, 'x 200 metered doses', '200 metered doses', 'Inhaler', '100 mcg per puff', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is your text with headings converted to normal bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff</strong></p><p>Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff is indicated as a bronchodilator for use in Asthma, Chronic Bronchitis, Emphysema, and other conditions associated with airway obstruction.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Short-acting selective &amp; β2-adrenoceptor stimulants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff is a selective beta2-adrenoceptor agonist. At therapeutic doses, it acts on the beta 2-adrenoceptors of bronchial smooth muscle, with little or no action on the ß1-adrenoceptors of cardiac muscle. Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff provides short acting (4-6 hours) bronchodilatation with a fast onset (within 5 minutes) in reversible airways obstruction. It also has an anti-inflammatory effect on mast cells, causing inhibition of release of bronchoconstrictor mediators including histamine, neutrophil chemotactic factor (NCF), and prostaglandin D2.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff</strong></p><p><strong>Oral:</strong></p><p>Children:</p><ul><li>2-6 years: 2.5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li>6-12 years: 5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li>Over 12 years: 5-10 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily (2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily)</li><li>Adults: 2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily. Maximum single dose is 8 mg tablet.</li></ul><p><strong>Injection:</strong></p><p>Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff Injection may be administered by the subcutaneous, intramuscular, or intravenous route, under the direction of a physician.</p><p>Adults:</p><ul><li>Subcutaneous route: 500 mcg (8 mcg/kg) and repeated every four hours as required.</li><li>Intramuscular route: 500 mcg (8 mcg/kg) and repeated every four hours as required.</li><li>Slow intravenous injection: 250 mcg (4 mcg/kg) injected slowly. If necessary, the dose may be repeated.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Population:</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff Injection in children under the age of 12 has not been established. From the available data, no recommendation on posology can be made.</p><p>Children aged 12 years and over: Dose as per adult population.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff</strong></p><p><strong>Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff tablet or syrup</strong></p><p>Children:</p><ul><li>2-6 years: 2.5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li>6-12 years: 5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li>Over 12 years: 5-10 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily (2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily)</li><li>Adults: 2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily. Maximum single dose is 8 mg tablet.</li></ul><p><strong>Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff Respirator Solution:</strong></p><p>To be used with a suitable nebulizer device under the direction of a physician. The solution must not be injected or ingested.</p><p><strong>Method-1 (Intermittent Administration):</strong></p><p>Adults (and the elderly): 0.5 ml-1.0 ml Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff up to four times a day. Up to 40mg per day can be given under strict medical direction in the hospital.</p><p>Children under 12 years: 0.5 ml of the solution diluted to 2.0-4.0 ml with normal saline. Some children may, however, require higher doses of up to 1.0 ml of the solution. Intermittent treatment may be repeated four times a day.</p><p><strong>Method-2 (Continuous administration):</strong></p><p>1-2 ml solution is diluted to make up to 100 ml with normal saline solution. The diluted solution is administered by a suitable nebulizer device.</p><p><strong>Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff Nebulizer Solution:</strong></p><p>To be used with a suitable nebulizer device under the direction of a physician. The solution must not be injected or ingested.</p><p>Adults &amp; Elderly: 2.5 mg to 5 mg Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff up to 4 times a day. Up to 40 mg/day may be given under strict medical direction in the hospital.</p><p>Children under 12 years: 2.5 mg up to 4 times a day. A higher dose up to 5 mg four times a day may be used if required.</p><p><strong>Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff Injection:</strong></p><p>Adults: Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff I.V. infusion solution is used to prepare a solution for continuous intravenous infusion. It should not be injected undiluted. A suitable solution for infusion may be prepared by diluting 5 mL of Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff I.V. infusion solution (1000 mcg/mL) in 500 mL of a chosen i.v. solution to provide a Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff concentration of 10 mcg/mL.</p><p>Infusion rates providing 3 to 20 micrograms Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff/minute (0.3 to 2ml/minute of the above infusion solution) are usually adequate.</p><p><strong>Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff Inhalation Capsule:</strong></p><p>Adults: For the relief of bronchospasm and for managing intermittent episodes of asthma, one or two inhalation capsules may be administered as a single dose.</p><p>The usual recommended dosage for maintenance or prophylactic therapy is the contents of one 200 microgram capsule every 4 to 6 hours using a device. In some patients, two 200 microgram capsules inhaled every 4 to 6 hours may be required.</p><p><strong>Exercise-induced Asthma:</strong></p><p>Adults: 400 micrograms</p><p>Children: 200 micrograms, 15-30 minutes prior to any physical exertion.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff</strong></p><p>Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff may cause fine tremor of skeletal muscles (particularly the hands), palpitations, and muscle cramps. Tachycardia, tenseness, headaches, and peripheral vasodilatation have been reported after large doses.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy: The drug should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p>Lactation: It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Because of the potential for tumorigenicity shown for Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff in some animal studies, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, considering the importance of the drug to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff should be used with caution in patients with hyperthyroidism, cardiovascular disease, occlusive vascular disorders, hypertension, and aneurysms.</p><p>Hypokalaemia associated with high doses of Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff may result in increased susceptibility to digitalis-induced cardiac arrhythmia.</p><p>Tachyphylaxis with resistance may occur with prolonged use of high dosage. Care is necessary when treating patients with diabetes mellitus or closed-angle glaucoma, and in those receiving antihypertensive therapy.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff</strong></p><p>The symptoms of overdosage include angina, headache, nausea, vomiting, tremor, etc.</p><p>The preferred antidote for overdosage with Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff is a cardio-selective beta-blocking agent, but beta-blocking drugs should be used with caution in patients with a history of bronchospasm.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Short-acting selective &amp; β2-adrenoceptor stimulants</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff is a synthetic sympathomimetic agent with predominant beta-2 adrenergic activity. Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff produces bronchodilatation through stimulation of beta-2-adrenergic receptors in bronchial smooth muscles, thereby causing relaxation of bronchial muscle fibers. This action improves pulmonary function as demonstrated by spirometric measurements.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The drug should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Because of the potential for tumorigenicity shown for Salburen Refill 100 mcg/puff in some animal studies, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, considering the importance of the drug to the mother.</p>', 'active', '528_1742276243.png', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:37:23', '2025-05-04 20:43:42'),
(3241, 'Informet XR', 'Metformin Hydrochloride', 9.00, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '750 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Informet XR 750 mg</strong></h3><p>Informet XR 750 mg is used for the <strong>treatment of type 2 diabetes mellitus</strong>, particularly in <strong>overweight patients</strong> when dietary management and exercise alone do not result in adequate glycemic control.</p><ul><li><strong>In adults:</strong> It can be used as monotherapy or in combination with other oral antidiabetic agents or insulin.</li><li><strong>In children (10 years and older) and adolescents:</strong> Metformin may be used as monotherapy or in combination with insulin.</li><li><strong>In overweight type 2 diabetic adults:</strong> Metformin as a first-line therapy after diet failure has shown a reduction in diabetic complications.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Biguanides</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></h3><p>Metformin is a <strong>biguanide</strong> oral antihyperglycemic drug used to manage type 2 diabetes. It works by:</p><ul><li><strong>Decreasing hepatic glucose production</strong></li><li><strong>Decreasing intestinal glucose absorption</strong></li><li><strong>Improving insulin sensitivity</strong> by increasing peripheral glucose uptake and utilization.</li></ul><p>Metformin does not cause hypoglycemia, unlike sulfonylureas.</p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></h3><h4><em>Metformin Immediate Release Tablet:</em></h4><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong>Start with <strong>500 mg twice a day</strong> or <strong>850 mg once a day</strong> with meals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increase by <strong>500 mg weekly</strong> or <strong>850 mg every 2 weeks</strong>, up to a maximum of <strong>2000 mg per day</strong>, in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For additional glycemic control, up to <strong>2550 mg per day</strong> can be administered.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong>Start with <strong>500 mg twice a day</strong>, increasing by <strong>500 mg weekly</strong> up to <strong>2000 mg per day</strong> in divided doses.</li></ul><h4><em>Metformin Extended Release Tablet:</em></h4><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong>Start with <strong>500 mg once daily</strong> with the evening meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increase by <strong>500 mg weekly</strong>, up to a maximum of <strong>2000 mg once daily</strong>, or <strong>1000 mg twice daily</strong> with meals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong>Not studied in children.</li></ul><h4><em>Renal Impairment:</em></h4><ul><li><strong>Contraindicated</strong> in patients with an eGFR below <strong>30 mL/min/1.73 m²</strong>.</li><li>Evaluate risk/benefit if eGFR is below <strong>45 mL/min/1.73 m²</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></h3><ul><li>Co-administration with <strong>carbonic anhydrase inhibitors</strong> (e.g., Topiramate, Zonisamide) may increase the risk of lactic acidosis.</li><li>Drugs like <strong>Ranolazine, Dolutegravir, Cimetidine</strong> that reduce Metformin clearance may lead to its accumulation.</li><li><strong>Alcohol</strong> potentiates Metformin\'s effect on lactate metabolism.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to Metformin or excipients.</li><li><strong>Acute metabolic acidosis</strong> (e.g., lactic acidosis, diabetic ketoacidosis).</li><li><strong>Severe renal failure</strong> (GFR &lt;30 mL/min).</li><li>Acute conditions that can alter renal function (dehydration, severe infection, shock).</li><li>Conditions causing tissue hypoxia (e.g., cardiac failure, respiratory failure, recent myocardial infarction, shock, hepatic insufficiency, alcohol intoxication).</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Blood disorders:</strong> Rare cases of <strong>hemolytic anemia</strong>.</li><li><strong>Metabolism/nutrition disorders:</strong> Very rare cases of <strong>lactic acidosis</strong>, <strong>decreased vitamin B12 absorption</strong>, which could lead to megaloblastic anemia.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal issues:</strong> <strong>Very common</strong> (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, loss of appetite).</li><li><strong>Skin reactions:</strong> Very rare cases of <strong>erythema, pruritus, urticaria</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> No clear association with birth defects or adverse outcomes during pregnancy, but <strong>insulin</strong> is preferred for managing diabetes during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Metformin is excreted in breast milk. No adverse effects observed in infants, but caution is advised. The decision to discontinue breastfeeding should weigh the benefits and risks.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Renal function monitoring</strong> is necessary, as Metformin is excreted by the kidneys.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12 deficiency</strong> can occur with long-term use.</li><li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong> risk increases when used with insulin or insulin secretagogues.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong> has not been observed with doses up to <strong>85 gm</strong>, but <strong>lactic acidosis</strong> can occur.</li><li><strong>Lactic acidosis</strong> is a medical emergency and must be treated in a hospital. The most effective removal method is <strong>hemodialysis</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></h3><ul><li>Keep below <strong>30°C</strong>, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Class:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Biguanides</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></h3><p>Metformin lowers <strong>basal</strong> and <strong>postprandial glucose</strong> levels by:</p><ul><li>Reducing <strong>hepatic glucose production</strong>.</li><li>Reducing <strong>intestinal glucose absorption</strong>.</li><li>Enhancing <strong>insulin sensitivity</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '447_1742276797.webp', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:46:37', '2025-03-18 10:46:37'),
(3242, 'Levox', 'Levofloxacin Hemihydrate', 75.00, 1, 'Bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Oral Solution', '125 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Levox 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Levox 125 mg/5 ml is indicated for the treatment of respiratory tract infections including acute bacterial sinusitis, acute exacerbations of chronic bronchitis, community-acquired pneumonia, and nosocomial pneumonia. It is also used in the treatment of uncomplicated and complicated skin &amp; soft tissue infections, chronic bacterial prostatitis, and uncomplicated &amp; complicated urinary tract infections (mild to moderate). It is indicated for the treatment of adults (18 years of age).</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> 4-Quinolone preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Levox 125 mg/5 ml is a synthetic, broad-spectrum, third-generation fluoroquinolone antibiotic. Chemically, Levox 125 mg/5 ml is a chiral fluorinated carboxyquinolone. Levox 125 mg/5 ml exerts antibacterial action by inhibiting bacterial topoisomerase IV and DNA gyrase, the enzymes required for DNA replication, transcription repair, and recombination. It has in vitro activity against a wide range of gram-negative and gram-positive microorganisms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Levox 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Acute sinusitis: 500 mg daily for 10-14 days.</li><li>Acute exacerbations of chronic bronchitis: 250-500 mg daily for 7-10 days.</li><li>Community-acquired pneumonia: 500 mg once or twice daily for 7-14 days.</li><li>Nosocomial pneumonia: 750 mg once daily for 7-14 days.</li><li>Skin &amp; soft tissue infections: 500 mg once or twice daily for 7-14 days.</li><li>Chronic prostatitis: 500 mg once daily for 28 days.</li><li>Urinary tract infections: 250 mg daily for 7-10 days (for 3 days in uncomplicated infections).</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Levox 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Antacids, iron, and adsorbents reduce the absorption of Levox 125 mg/5 ml. NSAIDs may increase the risk of CNS stimulation. Warfarin may increase the risk of bleeding.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Levox 125 mg/5 ml is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Levox 125 mg/5 ml and other quinolone antimicrobial agents or any other components of this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Levox 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Levox 125 mg/5 ml is generally well tolerated. However, a few side effects can usually be seen. Side effects include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, flatulence, and rare occurrence of phototoxicity (0.1%). Side effects that may be seen very rarely include tremors, depression, anxiety, confusion, etc.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> US FDA pregnancy category of Levox 125 mg/5 ml is C. So, Levox 125 mg/5 ml should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Adequate amounts of water should be taken during the treatment period to avoid concentrated urine. Dose adjustment is required for patients with impaired renal function.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1859_1742276914.jpg', 7, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:48:34', '2025-03-18 10:48:34'),
(3243, 'Chirolin', 'D-chiro-inositol', 85.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Chirolin 500 mg</strong></p><p> Chirolin 500 mg capsule is indicated for the treatment and prevention of:</p><ul><li>Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS)</li><li>Anovulation</li><li>Menstrual disorders such as amenorrhea</li><li>Hyperandrogenism complaints such as:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hirsutism</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Alopecia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acanthosis nigricans</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acne</li><li>Ovarian hyperthecosis</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p> 1 capsule (500 mg) contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Energy Value</strong>: 1.9 Cal</li><li><strong>Proteins</strong>: 0 mg</li><li><strong>Carbohydrates</strong>: ≥ 0.475 mg</li><li><strong>Sugars</strong>: ≥ 0.475 mg</li><li><strong>Total Fat</strong>: 0 mg</li><li><strong>Saturated Fat</strong>: 0 mg</li><li><strong>Trans Fat</strong>: 0 mg</li><li><strong>Fiber</strong>: 0 mg</li><li><strong>Mineral (Sodium)</strong>: ≤ 0.01 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Chirolin 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take 1-2 capsules orally daily, or as advised by the physician.</li><li>The duration of treatment should be a minimum of 2 months.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Chirolin 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Concurrent use with digoxin may result in increased digoxin toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Chirolin 500 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Chirolin 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Well tolerated when taken at the recommended dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at a temperature below 30˚C, protect from light &amp; moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Trophic Hormones &amp; Related Synthetic Drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Chirolin 500 mg is extracted through a patented process from the Carob (Ceratonia siliqua) pod, which is predominantly found in Southern Europe. The deficiency of Chirolin 500 mg in peripheral tissue causes insulin resistance, which leads to hyperandrogenism in the ovaries, resulting in PCOS.</p><p>Chirolin 500 mg helps regulate the balance between androgen and estrogen by decreasing luteinizing hormone and serum testosterone levels. It improves glucose tolerance, reduces circulating insulin, and decreases oxidative stress on the follicle. Chirolin 500 mg also lowers total cholesterol and triglyceride levels.</p><p>Clinically, Chirolin 500 mg has been proven to be an effective treatment for ovarian cysts and PCOS-associated metabolic disorders. It helps regulate normal ovulation within two weeks of administration. Additionally, it reduces hyperandrogenism-related skin disorders, including hirsutism, alopecia, acanthosis nigricans, and acne. Furthermore, it lowers cardiovascular risk in women suffering from PCOS by controlling arterial pressure.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Several human studies have been conducted with inositol and have shown no clinically relevant adverse effects.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1612_1742276934.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:48:54', '2025-03-18 10:48:54');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3244, 'Renafor-B', 'Budesonide + Formoterol Fumarate', 11.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mcg plus 6 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Renafor-B 200 mcg+6 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>This medication is indicated for the <strong>regular treatment of asthma</strong>.</li><li>It is also used for the <strong>symptomatic treatment of severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</strong> in patients with a history of repeated exacerbations despite regular therapy with long-acting bronchodilators.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Renafor-B 200 mcg+6 mcg</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Inhaler (For Asthma)</strong></li></ol><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Recommended maintenance dose: <strong>1 puff twice daily</strong> or <strong>2 puffs once daily</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">For some patients, a maintenance dose of <strong>2 puffs twice daily</strong> may be appropriate (for the 160/4.5 mcg/inhalation formulation only).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>1 additional puff</strong> can be taken as needed in response to symptoms. If symptoms persist, take the additional puff after a few minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maximum dose:</strong> <strong>6 puffs</strong> on any single occasion.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Usual maintenance dose: <strong>1-2 puffs once or twice daily</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>1 additional puff</strong> can be taken as needed for symptoms.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maximum dose:</strong> <strong>4 puffs</strong> on any single occasion.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Inhalation Capsule (For Asthma)</strong></li></ol><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Maintenance dose:</strong> Budesonide and Formoterol 100 &amp; 200 Inhalation Capsule, twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Reliever dose:</strong> <strong>1 additional Inhalation Capsule</strong> as needed for symptoms.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Maximum dose:</strong> <strong>6 Inhalation Capsules</strong> on any single occasion.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Total daily dose:</strong> No more than <strong>8 Inhalation Capsules</strong>, although <strong>12</strong> may be used temporarily.</li><li><strong>Children (4 years and older):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Maintenance dose:</strong> Budesonide and Formoterol 100 Inhalation Capsule, once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Reliever dose:</strong> <strong>1 additional Inhalation Capsule</strong> as needed for symptoms.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Maximum dose:</strong> <strong>4 Inhalation Capsules</strong> on any single occasion.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Total daily dose:</strong> No more than <strong>4 Inhalation Capsules</strong>, though up to <strong>8</strong> may be used temporarily.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Inhalation Capsule (For COPD)</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Adults (40 years and older):</strong>For the 200 mcg capsule: <strong>2 Inhalation Capsules twice daily</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Maximum daily maintenance dose:</strong> <strong>4 Inhalation Capsules</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">For the 400 mcg capsule: <strong>1 Inhalation Capsule twice daily</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maximum daily maintenance dose:</strong> <strong>2 Inhalation Capsules</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Renafor-B 200 mcg+6 mcg</strong></p><p><strong>Using an Inhaler:</strong></p><ol><li>Remove the cap.</li><li>Shake the inhaler vigorously at least <strong>six times</strong> before each use.</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If the inhaler is new or hasn\'t been used for a week, release one puff into the air to ensure it works.</li></ul><ol><li>Exhale fully.</li><li>Hold the inhaler upright and place the mouthpiece between your teeth.</li><li>Close lips around the mouthpiece.</li><li>Inhale deeply and slowly through your mouth while pressing down on the canister to release the medicine.</li><li>Remove the inhaler from your mouth and hold your breath for at least <strong>10 seconds</strong> or as long as comfortable.</li><li>If you need more than one puff, wait <strong>1 minute</strong> between puffs and repeat steps 4 to 7.</li><li>After use, replace the cap.</li><li>Rinse your mouth with water after each treatment.</li></ol><p><strong>Cleaning Instructions:</strong></p><ul><li>Clean the inhaler <strong>once a week</strong>.</li><li>Remove the canister and rinse the plastic actuator and cap in <strong>warm water</strong> (do not put the metal canister in water).</li><li>Dry thoroughly and replace the metal canister into the actuator with a twisting motion.</li><li>Ensure the cap is placed on the mouthpiece.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Renafor-B 200 mcg+6 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Avoid</strong> concomitant treatment with <strong>Ritonavir</strong>, <strong>Itraconazole</strong>, <strong>Ketoconazole</strong>, or other potent <strong>CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to <strong>Budesonide</strong>, <strong>Formoterol</strong>, or <strong>Lactose</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Renafor-B 200 mcg+6 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Budesonide</strong>: Hoarseness, candidiasis (thrush) of the mouth and throat, cutaneous hypersensitivity reactions.</li><li><strong>Formoterol Fumarate Dihydrate</strong>: Tremor, palpitations, headache, cardiac arrhythmias, muscle cramps, hypersensitivity reactions (rash, oedema, and angio-oedema).</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Do not initiate treatment with <strong>Budesonide and Formoterol</strong> combination during a <strong>severe exacerbation</strong> or if asthma is significantly worsening or acutely deteriorating.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool, dry place</strong>.</li><li><strong>Do not store above 30°C</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li><li>Protect from freezing and light.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Budesonide</strong>: Anti-inflammatory corticosteroid with potent glucocorticoid activity, inhibits multiple cell types and mediators involved in inflammation.</li><li><strong>Formoterol Fumarate Dihydrate</strong>: Long-acting <strong>β2 - adrenergic agonist</strong> with a rapid onset of action. It relaxes bronchial smooth muscle by increasing <strong>cyclic AMP</strong>, inhibiting the release of mediators from mast cells.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use during pregnancy</strong> only if the benefit to the mother outweighs any potential risk to the fetus.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Regularly monitor the <strong>height</strong> of children receiving prolonged treatment with inhaled corticosteroids.</li><li>If growth slows, consider re-evaluating therapy and reducing the corticosteroid dose.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1711_1742277346.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:55:46', '2025-03-18 10:55:46'),
(3245, 'Teslar-M', 'Amlodipine Besilate + Telmisartan', 20.00, 7, 'tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+80 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a clearer, formatted version of the information you provided about <strong>Teslar-M 5 mg + 80 mg</strong>:</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Indications of Teslar-M 5 mg + 80 mg</strong></h3><p>Teslar-M is indicated for the treatment of <strong>hypertension</strong>, either alone or in combination with other antihypertensive agents. It may also be used as <strong>initial therapy</strong> in patients who are likely to need multiple drugs to achieve their blood pressure goals.</p><h3><strong>Dosage of Teslar-M 5 mg + 80 mg</strong></h3><p><strong>1. Initial Therapy:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Starting dose</strong>: 40/5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>For patients requiring larger blood pressure reductions</strong>: Start with 80/5 mg once daily.</li><li>Initial therapy is not recommended for patients ≥75 years old or with hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>2. Add-on Therapy:</strong></p><p>For patients who are not adequately controlled with amlodipine (or another dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker) alone or with telmisartan (or another angiotensin receptor blocker) alone.</p><ul><li><strong>For patients with edema</strong> due to amlodipine, a switch to 40/5 mg once daily may be made without reducing the overall expected antihypertensive response.</li></ul><p><strong>3. Replacement Therapy:</strong></p><p>Patients already on amlodipine and telmisartan from separate tablets can switch to Teslar-M (same doses) once daily.</p><p><strong>4. Maximum Dose:</strong></p><p>The maximum recommended dose is <strong>80/10 mg once daily</strong>.</p><h3><strong>Interactions of Teslar-M 5 mg + 80 mg</strong></h3><ul><li>Co-administration of <strong>telmisartan</strong> does not result in clinically significant interactions with acetaminophen, amlodipine, glyburide, simvastatin, hydrochlorothiazide, warfarin, or ibuprofen.</li><li><strong>Telmisartan</strong> is not metabolized by the cytochrome P450 system, except for possible mild inhibition of CYP2C19.</li><li><strong>Amiodipine</strong> has been safely used with thiazide diuretics, beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors, nitrates, digoxin, warfarin, NSAIDs, and oral hypoglycemic drugs.</li><li>Grapefruit juice, sildenafil, cimetidine, and amlodipine do not significantly affect the pharmacokinetics of amlodipine.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to this product or any of its components.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy &amp; lactation</strong>.</li><li><strong>Biliary obstructive disorders</strong>, <strong>severe hepatic impairment</strong>, <strong>hypotension</strong>, <strong>cardiogenic shock</strong>, and <strong>left ventricle outflow tract obstruction</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Teslar-M 5 mg + 80 mg</strong></h3><ul><li>Dizziness, peripheral edema, migraine, headache, paraesthesia, vertigo, bradycardia, palpitations, hypotension, cough, abdominal pain, diarrhea, nausea, pruritus, myalgia, spasm, erectile dysfunction, chest pain, fatigue, edema, etc.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Fetal or neonatal exposure</strong>: Avoid during pregnancy due to potential harm.</li><li><strong>Hypotension</strong>: Correct any volume or salt depletion before starting therapy.</li><li>Monitor for signs of hypotension and adjust dose slowly in patients with <strong>hepatic or severe renal impairment</strong>.</li><li><strong>Heart failure</strong>: Monitor for worsening conditions.</li><li>Avoid using an <strong>ACE inhibitor</strong> and <strong>angiotensin receptor blocker</strong> together.</li><li><strong>Myocardial infarction</strong>: In patients with severe coronary artery disease, initiating a CCB could precipitate myocardial infarction or worsen angina.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Telmisartan</strong>: Overdose may cause <strong>hypotension</strong>, dizziness, and tachycardia. If symptomatic hypotension occurs, supportive treatment should be provided. Telmisartan is not removed by hemodialysis.</li><li><strong>Amiodipine</strong>: Overdose may cause excessive <strong>peripheral vasodilation</strong>, marked <strong>hypotension</strong>, and possibly reflex <strong>tachycardia</strong>. Monitoring and supportive measures like fluid administration and, if needed, vasopressors are advised. Amiodipine is not removed by hemodialysis.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Store below 30°C</strong>.</li><li>Protect from light and high humidity.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Combined antihypertensive preparations</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Teslar-M is a <strong>fixed-dose combination</strong> of <strong>Telmisartan</strong> (an angiotensin receptor blocker) and <strong>Amiodipine</strong> (a calcium channel blocker).</p><ul><li><strong>Telmisartan</strong> works by blocking the effects of <strong>angiotensin II</strong>, causing blood vessels to dilate and lowering blood pressure.</li><li><strong>Amiodipine</strong> inhibits calcium influx into smooth and cardiac muscle cells, leading to vasodilation and reduced blood pressure.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Category C (first trimester)</strong> and <strong>Category D (second and third trimesters)</strong>.</li><li>Use during <strong>pregnancy</strong> is not recommended due to potential risks to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is unknown if the drug is excreted in breast milk; nursing mothers should discontinue either the drug or breastfeeding based on the benefits of treatment.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></h3><ul><li>The safety and effectiveness of Teslar-M in <strong>pediatric patients</strong> have not been established.</li></ul><h3><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></h3><ul><li>Initial therapy with Teslar-M is <strong>not recommended</strong> for patients <strong>≥75 years old</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Initial therapy</strong> with Teslar-M is <strong>not recommended</strong> for <strong>hepatically impaired</strong> patients.</li></ul><p>This summary makes the information more accessible and organized for better understanding!</p>', 'active', '1612_1742277513.webp', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 10:58:33', '2025-05-06 20:43:40'),
(3246, 'Herbisol', 'Sharbat Atfal', 65.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Spray', '100 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '89_1742277646.png', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 11:00:46', '2025-05-03 20:52:43'),
(3247, 'Minomal-R SR', 'Aminophylline', 3.59, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p>This medication is indicated for the <strong>treatment and prevention</strong> of bronchospasm associated with:</p><ul><li><strong>Asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Emphysema</strong></li><li><strong>Chronic bronchitis</strong></li></ul><p>It is also indicated in <strong>adults</strong> for the treatment of:</p><ul><li><strong>Cardiac asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Left ventricular or congestive cardiac failure</strong></li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p><strong>Aminophylline</strong> is a combination of <strong>theophylline</strong> and <strong>ethylenediamine</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>Ethylenediamine</strong>: Increases the <strong>solubility</strong> of theophylline in water (inactive component).</li><li><strong>Theophylline</strong>: Relaxes <strong>bronchial smooth muscle</strong> by:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Inhibiting phosphodiesterase</strong>, leading to an increase in <strong>intracellular cAMP</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adenosine receptor antagonism</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prostaglandin antagonism</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Effects on intracellular calcium</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><h4><strong>Oral (Chronic Bronchospasm)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: <strong>225-450 mg</strong> twice daily (<strong>bid</strong>), may increase if necessary.</li><li><strong>Children (&gt;3 years old)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Modified-release form</strong>: <strong>12 mg/kg/day</strong>, can increase to <strong>24 mg/kg/day</strong> in <strong>2 divided doses</strong> after <strong>1 week</strong>.</li></ul><h4><strong>Intravenous (Acute Severe Bronchospasm)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Loading dose</strong>: <strong>5 mg/kg (ideal body weight)</strong> or <strong>250-500 mg</strong> (25 mg/ml) by <strong>slow injection/infusion</strong> over <strong>20-30 minutes</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance dose</strong>: <strong>0.5 mg/kg/hour</strong> (Max rate: <strong>25 mg/min</strong>).</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Loading dose</strong>: Same as adults.</li><li><strong>Maintenance dose</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>6 months - 9 years</strong>: <strong>1 mg/kg/hour</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>10-16 years</strong>: <strong>0.8 mg/kg/hour</strong></li><li><strong>Elderly &amp; Hepatic Impairment</strong>: <strong>Dose reduction may be necessary</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Administration</strong></h3><ul><li>Tablets <strong>should be swallowed whole</strong>—do <strong>not</strong> chew, as it may affect drug release.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><p><strong>Aminophylline should not be used in patients with:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity to xanthines or ethylenediamine</strong></li><li><strong>Active peptic ulcer disease</strong> (as it may increase gastric secretion volume and acidity)</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p>The most common adverse effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastric irritation, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, hematemesis, epigastric pain, and tremor</strong> (early signs of toxicity)</li></ul><p>High doses may cause:</p><ul><li><strong>Ventricular arrhythmias or seizures</strong> (initial signs in severe cases)</li><li><strong>Peptic ulcer reactivation</strong></li><li><strong>Headache, irritability, restlessness, insomnia, twitching, convulsions, and reflex hyperexcitability</strong></li><li><strong>Palpitations, tachycardia, hypotension, circulatory failure, and flushing</strong></li><li><strong>Albuminuria, diuresis, and hematuria</strong></li><li><strong>Inappropriate ADH syndrome</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Use in pregnancy should be carefully considered</strong> based on risk vs. benefit.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><p>Aminophylline should be used <strong>with caution</strong> in patients with:</p><ul><li><strong>Peptic ulcer disease</strong></li><li><strong>Hyperthyroidism</strong></li><li><strong>Hypertension</strong></li><li><strong>Cardiac arrhythmias or cardiovascular disease</strong></li><li><strong>Epilepsy</strong></li></ul><p>It should also be used cautiously in patients with:</p><ul><li><strong>Heart failure</strong></li><li><strong>Liver dysfunction</strong></li><li><strong>Chronic alcoholism</strong></li><li><strong>Acute febrile illness</strong></li><li><strong>Neonates and elderly patients</strong> (as <strong>theophylline clearance</strong> may be <strong>reduced</strong>, leading to <strong>higher serum levels</strong> and increased drug half-life)</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Bronchodilator</strong></li><li><strong>Methyl xanthine derivatives</strong></li><li><strong>Respiratory stimulants (Analeptics)</strong></li><li><strong>Theophylline &amp; related drugs</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store <strong>below 30°C</strong>, protected from <strong>light and moisture</strong></li><li>Keep <strong>out of reach of children</strong></li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '515_1742278458.jpg', 56, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 11:14:18', '2025-03-18 11:14:18'),
(3248, 'Bihale', 'Vilanterol Trifenatate + Fluticasone Furoate', 30.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Dry Powder Inhalation Capsule', '25 mcg+100 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Bihale 25 mcg+100 mcg:</strong></p><p>This medication is indicated for:</p><ol><li><strong>Long-term, once-daily, maintenance treatment</strong> of airflow obstruction and reducing exacerbations in patients with <strong>Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)</strong>.</li><li><strong>Once-daily treatment of asthma</strong> in patients aged 18 years and older.</li></ol><p><strong>Important limitation of use:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Not indicated</strong> for relief of acute bronchospasm.</li></ul><p><strong>Composition:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>25/100 inhalation capsule</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vilanterol 25 mcg (as Vilanterol Trifenatate INN)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fluticasone Furoate INN 100 mcg</li><li><strong>25/200 inhalation capsule</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vilanterol 25 mcg (as Vilanterol Trifenatate INN)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fluticasone Furoate INN 200 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Bihale 25 mcg+100 mcg:</strong></p><ul><li>This inhalation capsule must <strong>not</strong> be swallowed; it is to be used with the device only.</li><li>Remove the capsule from the blister pack immediately before using it in the inhalation device.</li><li>After inhalation, rinse your mouth with water without swallowing to reduce the risk of oropharyngeal candidiasis.</li></ul><p><strong>Route of administration:</strong> Oral inhalation</p><ul><li><strong>For COPD</strong>: 1 inhalation of <strong>25/100 mcg</strong> once daily.</li><li><strong>For Asthma</strong>: 1 inhalation of <strong>25/100 mcg</strong> or <strong>25/200 mcg</strong> once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Bihale 25 mcg+100 mcg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Strong cytochrome P450 3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., ketoconazole): Use with caution due to potential systemic corticosteroid and cardiovascular effects.</li><li><strong>Monoamine oxidase inhibitors and tricyclic antidepressants</strong>: Use with extreme caution due to the risk of potentiating the effect of vilanterol on the vascular system.</li><li><strong>Beta-blockers</strong>: Use with caution; may block the bronchodilatory effects of beta-agonists and cause severe bronchospasm.</li><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: Use with caution, as they may worsen electrocardiographic changes and/or hypokalemia when used with beta-agonists.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Primary treatment of <strong>status asthmaticus</strong> or acute episodes of <strong>COPD</strong> or <strong>asthma</strong> requiring intensive measures.</li><li>Severe hypersensitivity to <strong>milk proteins</strong> or any ingredients of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Bihale 25 mcg+100 mcg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>COPD</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Most common adverse reactions (incidence ≥3%) include nasopharyngitis, upper respiratory tract infection, headache, oral candidiasis, back pain, pneumonia, bronchitis, sinusitis, cough, oropharyngeal pain, arthralgia, hypertension, influenza, pharyngitis, and pyrexia.</li><li><strong>Asthma</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Most common adverse reactions (incidence ≥2%) include nasopharyngitis, oral candidiasis, headache, influenza, upper respiratory tract infection, bronchitis, sinusitis, oropharyngeal pain, dysphonia, and cough.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>LABA monotherapy</strong> increases the risk of serious asthma-related events.</li><li>Do not initiate in acutely deteriorating <strong>COPD</strong> or <strong>asthma</strong>.</li><li>Do not use to treat acute symptoms.</li><li><strong>Candida albicans infection</strong> of the mouth and pharynx may occur.</li><li><strong>Increased risk of pneumonia</strong> in COPD patients. Monitor for signs and symptoms of pneumonia.</li><li>Risk of impaired adrenal function when transferring from systemic corticosteroids.</li><li><strong>Paradoxical bronchospasm</strong> may occur; discontinue and initiate alternative therapy if this occurs.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with cardiovascular disorders, <strong>diabetes</strong>, <strong>thyrotoxicosis</strong>, and <strong>convulsive disorders</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hypercorticism</strong> and adrenal suppression can occur, especially at high dosages or in susceptible individuals.</li><li><strong>Glaucoma and cataracts</strong> may develop with long-term use of ICS. Consider referral to an ophthalmologist for long-term users.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Bihale 25 mcg+100 mcg:</strong></p><ul><li>No clinical trial data is available regarding overdose effects for this inhalation capsule.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool &amp; dry place</strong>.</li><li>Avoid exposure to <strong>direct sunlight</strong> or heat.</li><li>Keep away from eyes and out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Respiratory corticosteroids</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vilanterol</strong> is a <strong>beta 2-adrenoceptor agonist</strong> that works by increasing cyclic AMP levels, causing <strong>bronchodilation</strong> and inhibiting the release of hypersensitivity mediators.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Furoate</strong> is a corticosteroid with <strong>anti-inflammatory</strong> properties that affect multiple cell types involved in inflammation (mast cells, eosinophils, neutrophils, etc.) in COPD and asthma.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><ul><li>Insufficient data is available regarding the use of this preparation in pregnant women and lactating mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li>Not indicated for use in <strong>children</strong> and <strong>adolescents</strong> (aged 17 years and younger). Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Fluticasone furoate</strong> may have increased systemic exposure in patients with <strong>moderate or severe hepatic impairment</strong>. Monitor for systemic corticosteroid effects.</li></ul>', 'active', '1625_1742278802.webp', 5, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 11:20:02', '2025-04-30 20:42:40'),
(3249, 'Mirapin', 'Mirtazapine', 9.03, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '15 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p><strong>Mirapin Tablets</strong> are indicated for the <strong>treatment of major depressive disorder (MDD).</strong></p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><h4><strong>Pharmacodynamics</strong></h4><p>The <strong>mechanism of action</strong> of <strong>Mirtazapine</strong> in treating <strong>major depressive disorder</strong> is not fully understood. However, <strong>preclinical studies</strong> suggest that:</p><ul><li>Mirtazapine <strong>enhances central noradrenergic and serotonergic activity</strong>.</li><li>It acts as an <strong>antagonist at central presynaptic α2-adrenergic autoreceptors and heteroreceptors</strong>, leading to an increase in <strong>noradrenaline and serotonin</strong> levels.</li><li>It <strong>potently antagonizes 5-HT2 and 5-HT3 receptors</strong>, but has <strong>no significant affinity for 5-HT1A and 5-HT1B receptors</strong>.</li><li>Mirtazapine is a <strong>potent H1 histamine receptor antagonist</strong>, contributing to its <strong>sedative effects</strong>.</li><li>It has <strong>moderate α1-adrenergic receptor antagonism</strong>, which may explain <strong>occasional orthostatic hypotension</strong>.</li><li>It also has <strong>moderate muscarinic receptor antagonism</strong>, but <strong>low anticholinergic side effects</strong>.</li></ul><h4><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: Rapid and well-absorbed after oral administration; <strong>peak plasma levels</strong> occur after <strong>~2 hours</strong>.</li><li><strong>Protein Binding</strong>: ~<strong>85%</strong> bound to plasma proteins.</li><li><strong>Half-life</strong>: ~<strong>20-40 hours</strong> (<strong>26 hours in males, 37 hours in females</strong>), allowing <strong>once-daily dosing</strong>.</li><li><strong>Metabolism</strong>: <strong>Extensively metabolized</strong> via <strong>demethylation, oxidation, and conjugation</strong>.</li><li><strong>Elimination</strong>: Excreted via <strong>urine and feces</strong> over <strong>four days</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><h4><strong>Adults</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Starting dose</strong>: <strong>15 mg/day</strong>, taken <strong>once daily</strong> in the <strong>evening</strong> or <strong>before sleep</strong>.</li><li><strong>Effective dose range</strong>: <strong>15-45 mg/day</strong>.</li><li><strong>Dose adjustments</strong>: If <strong>no response at 15 mg</strong>, increase up to <strong>30 mg</strong>, with a <strong>maximum of 45 mg/day</strong>.</li><li><strong>Dose changes</strong> should be made at <strong>intervals of 1-2 weeks</strong> to allow for <strong>therapeutic response</strong>.</li></ul><h4><strong>Use in Children</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Not recommended</strong>.</li></ul><h4><strong>Missed Dose</strong></h4><ul><li>If a dose is missed, take it <strong>as soon as remembered</strong>, unless it is <strong>close to the next dose</strong>.</li><li>If it is almost time for the next dose, <strong>skip the missed dose</strong>—do <strong>not</strong> double the next dose.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></h3><p>Mirapin has <strong>clinically significant drug interactions</strong> with:</p><ul><li><strong>Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAOI)</strong> &amp; other <strong>serotonergic drugs</strong> such as:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tryptophan, Triptans, Linezolid, SSRIs, Venlafaxine, Lithium, Tramadol, St. John\'s Wort</strong></li><li><strong>Carbamazepine, Phenytoin, or Cimetidine</strong> may alter Mirapin metabolism.</li><li><strong>Avoid Alcohol &amp; Diazepam</strong> while taking Mirapin.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><p>Mirapin is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in patients with:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to <strong>Mirtazapine</strong> or its <strong>excipients</strong>.</li><li><strong>Concomitant use with MAO inhibitors (MAOIs)</strong>.</li><li><strong>Mirtazapine should not be used within 14 days</strong> of starting or stopping an <strong>MAOI</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p><strong>Common side effects</strong> include:</p><ul><li><strong>Dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, increased appetite, and weight gain</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: Use only if <strong>benefits outweigh risks</strong>.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: Consult a physician before use.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Patients and caregivers should monitor for:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Anxiety, agitation, panic attacks, insomnia, irritability, aggression, impulsivity, hypomania, mania, unusual behavior changes, worsening depression, suicidal ideation</strong>.</li><li><strong>Agranulocytosis risk</strong>: Monitor for <strong>infection signs</strong>.</li><li><strong>Sedative effects</strong> may impair <strong>judgment, thinking, and motor skills</strong>.</li><li><strong>Liver enzyme elevation (ALT/SGPT)</strong> may occur.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Atypical Antidepressant Drugs</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store <strong>below 30°C</strong>, <strong>away from light and moisture</strong>.</li><li><strong>Keep out of reach of children</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1105_1742278833.webp', 55, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 11:20:33', '2025-03-18 11:20:33'),
(3250, 'Lulima', 'Luliconazole', 100.00, 1, 'x 10 gm tube', '10 gm tube', 'Cream', '10 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Lulima 10 gm</strong></p><p>Lulima 10 gm cream is indicated for the topical treatment of interdigital tinea pedis, tinea cruris, and tinea corporis caused by the organisms <em>Trichophyton rubrum</em> and <em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em>.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Miscellaneous topical agents</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Lulima 10 gm is an antifungal that belongs to the azole class. Although the exact mechanism of action against dermatophytes is unknown, Lulima 10 gm appears to inhibit ergosterol synthesis by inhibiting the enzyme lanosterol demethylase. Inhibition of this enzyme’s activity by azoles results in decreased amounts of ergosterol, a constituent of fungal cell membranes, and a corresponding accumulation of lanosterol.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Lulima 10 gm</strong></p><p>For topical use only. Lulima 10 gm Cream, 1% is not for ophthalmic, oral, or intravaginal use.</p><ul><li><strong>Interdigital tinea pedis</strong>: Apply a thin layer of Lulima 10 gm Cream, 1% to the affected area and approximately 1 inch of the immediate surrounding area once daily for two weeks.</li><li><strong>Tinea cruris or tinea corporis</strong>: Apply a thin layer of Lulima 10 gm Cream, 1% to the affected area and approximately 1 inch of the immediate surrounding area once daily for one week.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Lulima 10 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult &amp; Geriatric</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Interdigital tinea pedis</strong>: Apply to the affected area and surrounding areas once daily for two weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea cruris and tinea corporis</strong>: Apply to the affected area and surrounding areas once daily for one week.</li><li><strong>Pediatric</strong>: Safety and effectiveness have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Lulima 10 gm</strong></p><p>Lulima 10 gm has the potential to inhibit cytochrome P-450 (CYP) enzymes 1A2, 2C9, 2C19, 2D6, and 3A4.</p><ul><li>Based on in vitro assessment, it may inhibit the activity of CYP2C19 and CYP3A4, particularly when applied to patients with moderate to severe tinea cruris.</li><li>It is not expected to inhibit CYPs 1A2, 2C9, or 2D6.</li><li>The induction potential of Lulima 10 gm on CYP enzymes has not been evaluated.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Lulima 10 gm cream is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Lulima 10 gm.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Lulima 10 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Contact dermatitis</li><li>Cellulitis</li><li>Application site reactions (observed in less than 1% of subjects in clinical trials)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Lulima 10 gm Cream should only be used during pregnancy if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Nursing Mothers</strong>: It is not known whether Lulima 10 gm is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when using Lulima 10 gm Cream in breastfeeding women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>If any unusual allergic reaction occurs, treatment should be discontinued, and appropriate therapy should be instituted. Lulima 10 gm is for topical use only and not intended for ophthalmic, oral, or intravaginal use.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C.</li><li>Keep away from light and out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness have not been established in pediatric patients.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No overall differences in safety or effectiveness were observed between older and younger subjects in clinical studies. However, greater sensitivity in older individuals cannot be ruled out.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Miscellaneous topical agents</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Lulima 10 gm is a broad-spectrum antifungal agent that belongs to the azole group. It works by inhibiting the enzyme lanosterol demethylase, blocking ergosterol synthesis, which is an important constituent of the fungal cell membrane. This leads to fungicidal activity against dermatophytes, <em>Candida albicans</em>, <em>Malassezia</em> spp., <em>Trichophyton</em>, and <em>Epidermophyton</em> spp.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>There are no adequate and well-controlled studies of Lulima 10 gm cream in pregnant women.</li><li>It should only be used during pregnancy if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li>It is not known whether Lulima 10 gm is excreted in human milk. Caution is advised when administering to breastfeeding women.</li></ul>', 'active', '903_1742278973.webp', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 11:22:53', '2025-05-03 23:35:11'),
(3251, 'Uroben XR', 'Mirabegron', 30.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Uroben XR 25 mg</strong></p><p>Uroben XR 25 mg is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of urgency, increased micturition frequency, and urgency incontinence as may occur in adult patients with overactive bladder (OAB) syndrome.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>BPH/ Urinary retention/ Urinary incontinence</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Uroben XR 25 mg is a potent and selective beta 3-adrenoceptor agonist. It showed relaxation of bladder smooth muscle in rat and human isolated tissue, increased cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) concentrations in rat bladder tissue, and demonstrated a bladder relaxant effect in rat urinary bladder function models. Uroben XR 25 mg increased mean voided volume per micturition and decreased the frequency of non-voiding contractions without affecting voiding pressure or residual urine in rat models of bladder overactivity. In a monkey model, Uroben XR 25 mg showed decreased voiding frequency, indicating that Uroben XR 25 mg enhances urine storage function by stimulating beta 3-adrenoceptors in the bladder.</p><p>During the urine storage phase, sympathetic nerve stimulation predominates, leading to the activation of beta-adrenoceptors in the bladder musculature and causing relaxation. During the voiding phase, the bladder is controlled by the parasympathetic nervous system, where cholinergic M2 and M3 receptors mediate contraction. Beta 3-adrenoceptor stimulation does not interfere with the voiding process, as shown in rat models of partial urethral obstruction.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Uroben XR 25 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Recommended starting dose:</strong> 25 mg once daily, alone or in combination with solifenacin succinate 5 mg, once daily.</li><li>Based on individual efficacy and tolerability, the dose may be increased to 50 mg once daily, alone or in combination with solifenacin succinate 5 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal or Hepatic Impairment:</strong>For patients with severe renal impairment or moderate hepatic impairment, the maximum dose is 25 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uroben XR 25 mg is not recommended for patients with end-stage renal disease (ESRD) or severe hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Uroben XR 25 mg tablet should be swallowed whole with water, with or without food. It should not be chewed, divided, or crushed.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Uroben XR 25 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Enzyme Inhibitors:</strong> Uroben XR 25 mg exposure is increased 1.8-fold in the presence of strong CYP3A/P-gp inhibitors like ketoconazole. No dose adjustment is needed, except for patients with renal or hepatic impairments.</li><li><strong>Enzyme Inducers:</strong> CYP3A or P-gp inducers, such as rifampicin, decrease Uroben XR 25 mg plasma concentrations. No dose adjustment is needed.</li><li><strong>CYP2D6 Substrates:</strong> Caution is advised when co-administering Uroben XR 25 mg with drugs metabolized by CYP2D6, such as metoprolol and desipramine, due to increased Cmax and AUC.</li><li><strong>Transporters:</strong> Uroben XR 25 mg is a weak P-gp inhibitor. For patients starting a combination of Uroben XR 25 mg and digoxin, digoxin dose should be initiated at the lowest level and monitored.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Uroben XR 25 mg is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to the active substance or any excipients</li><li>Severe uncontrolled hypertension (systolic BP ≥ 180 mm Hg and/or diastolic BP ≥ 110 mm Hg)</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Uroben XR 25 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Tachycardia (1.2% of patients)</li><li>Urinary tract infections (2.9% of patients)</li></ul><p>Serious adverse reactions include atrial fibrillation (0.2%).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Uroben XR 25 mg is not recommended during pregnancy or for women planning to become pregnant, as animal studies have shown reproductive toxicity.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Uroben XR 25 mg is excreted in rodent milk and is predicted to be in human milk. Therefore, it should not be used during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Uroben XR 25 mg is not recommended for patients with end-stage renal disease (GFR &lt; 15 mL/min/1.73 m²) or severe renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Not recommended for patients with severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh Class C).</li><li><strong>Hypertension:</strong> Uroben XR 25 mg may increase blood pressure. Regular monitoring is advised, especially in hypertensive patients.</li><li><strong>Bladder Outlet Obstruction:</strong> Use with caution in patients with clinically significant bladder outlet obstruction or those taking antimuscarinics for OAB.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Uroben XR 25 mg</strong></p><p>In overdose, adverse events like palpitations and increased pulse rate may occur. Treatment should be symptomatic and supportive, with monitoring of pulse rate, blood pressure, and ECG.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal and Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Uroben XR 25 mg has not been studied in patients with end-stage renal disease or severe hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Gender:</strong> No dose adjustment is necessary based on gender.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> Safety and efficacy in children under 18 years of age have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>BPH/Urinary retention/Urinary incontinence</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Uroben XR 25 mg is a beta-3 adrenoceptor agonist that increases cyclic AMP levels in the bladder, leading to relaxation of the detrusor smooth muscle during the bladder\'s storage phase.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Limited data are available on the use of Uroben XR 25 mg in pregnant women. Reproductive toxicity has been observed in animal studies, and Uroben XR 25 mg is not recommended during pregnancy or for women planning to become pregnant.</p>', 'active', '1428_1742279183.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 11:26:23', '2025-03-18 11:26:23'),
(3252, 'Folimax', 'Folinic Acid', 9.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1801_1742279373.jpg', 53, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 11:29:33', '2025-03-18 11:29:33');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3253, 'Ultraclav', 'Amoxicillin + Clavulanic Acid', 28.75, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+125 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Co-amoxiclav is indicated for short-term treatment of bacterial infections at the following sites:</p><ul><li>Upper respiratory tract infections (including ENT) e.g. tonsillitis, sinusitis, otitis media.</li><li>Lower respiratory tract infections e.g. acute and chronic bronchitis, lobar and bronchopneumonia.</li><li>Genito-urinary tract infections e.g. cystitis, urethritis, pyelonephritis.</li><li>Skin and soft tissue infections.</li><li>Bone and joint infections e.g. osteomyelitis.</li><li>Other infections e.g. septic abortion, puerperal sepsis, intra-abdominal sepsis etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><ul><li><strong>375 mg tablet</strong>: Each film-coated tablet contains Amoxicillin 250 mg as Amoxicillin Trihydrate BP and Clavulanic Acid 125 mg as Diluted Potassium Clavulanate BP.</li><li><strong>625 mg tablet</strong>: Each film-coated tablet contains Amoxicillin 500 mg as Amoxicillin Trihydrate BP and Clavulanic Acid 125 mg as Diluted Potassium Clavulanate BP.</li><li><strong>1 gm tablet</strong>: Each film-coated tablet contains Amoxicillin 875 mg as Amoxicillin Trihydrate BP and Clavulanic Acid 125 mg as Diluted Potassium Clavulanate BP.</li><li><strong>Powder for Suspension</strong>: Each 5 ml reconstituted suspension contains Amoxicillin 125 mg as Amoxicillin Trihydrate BP and Clavulanic Acid 31.25 mg as Diluted Potassium Clavulanate BP.</li><li><strong>Powder for Suspension (Forte)</strong>: Each 5 ml reconstituted suspension contains Amoxicillin 400 mg as Amoxicillin Trihydrate BP and Clavulanic Acid 57.5 mg as Diluted Potassium Clavulanate BP.</li><li><strong>1.2 gm Injection</strong>: Each vial contains a sterile mixture of Amoxicillin Sodium BP (equivalent to Amoxicillin 1 gm) and Clavulanate Potassium USP (equivalent to Clavulanic Acid 200 mg).</li><li><strong>0.6 gm injection</strong>: Each vial contains a sterile mixture of Amoxicillin Sodium BP (equivalent to Amoxicillin 500 mg) and Clavulanate Potassium USP (equivalent to Clavulanic Acid 100 mg).</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> <strong>Pharmacodynamic properties</strong>: Co-amoxiclav is an antibacterial combination consisting of the antibiotic Amoxicillin and the β-lactamase inhibitor Clavulanic Acid. Amoxicillin has a broad spectrum of bactericidal activity against many Gram-positive and Gram-negative microorganisms but is susceptible to degradation by β-lactamases. Clavulanic Acid inactivates a wide range of β-lactamase enzymes, thereby extending the antibiotic spectrum.</p><p><strong>Pharmacokinetic properties</strong>: Peak serum levels occur about one hour after oral administration. Absorption is optimized when taken at the start of a meal. Both Clavulanate and Amoxicillin have low serum binding, with about 70% remaining free in the serum. Doubling the dosage approximately doubles the serum levels.</p><p><strong>Dosage</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults and children over 12 years:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablets</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual dose: One 625 mg tablet every 12 hours or one 375 mg tablet every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For severe infections: One 1 gm tablet every 12 hours or one 625 mg tablet every 8 hours.</li><li><strong>Suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6-12 years</strong>: 2 teaspoonfuls every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 1-6 years</strong>: 1 teaspoonful every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children below 1 year</strong>: 25 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 8 hours.</li><li><strong>Forte suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Mild to moderate infections</strong>: 25/3.6 mg/kg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Serious infections</strong>: 45/6.4 mg/kg/day</li><li><strong>IV Injection</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 1.2 gm every 8 hours (in severe infections, every 6 hours).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Surgical prophylaxis</strong>: 1.2 gm at induction (up to 2-3 gm for high-risk procedures).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 0-3 months</strong>: 30 mg/kg every 8 hours (every 12 hours in premature infants).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 3 months to 12 years</strong>: 30 mg/kg every 8 hours (or every 6 hours for severe infections).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral</strong>: Should be taken at the start of a meal to enhance absorption.</li><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>: Not for intramuscular or subcutaneous administration. Should be used within 20 minutes of reconstitution.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li>May prolong bleeding and prothrombin time.</li><li>May reduce the efficacy of oral contraceptives.</li><li>Concomitant use with allopurinol increases the risk of allergic skin reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>History of Penicillin hypersensitivity.</li><li>Cross-sensitivity with other beta-lactam antibiotics.</li><li>History of cholestatic jaundice associated with Co-amoxiclav or Penicillin.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, indigestion, candidiasis.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice, urticaria, erythematous rashes, Stevens-Johnson Syndrome, anaphylaxis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Animal studies show no teratogenic effects. However, use during pregnancy is not recommended unless essential.</li><li>Amoxicillin is excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients on anticoagulation therapy or with severe hepatic dysfunction.</li><li>Dosage adjustments required for renal impairment.</li><li>Adequate fluid intake should be maintained to prevent crystalluria.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Mild</strong> (Creatinine clearance &gt;30 ml/min): No change.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Moderate</strong> (10-30 ml/min): Reduce dosage.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Severe</strong> (&lt;10 ml/min): Max 375 mg tablet every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dialysis patients</strong>: May require an additional dose after dialysis.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: Use with caution, monitor liver function regularly.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose symptoms are unlikely but may include gastrointestinal disturbances and fluid/electrolyte imbalances.</li><li>Hemodialysis may be required in severe cases.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Broad-spectrum penicillins.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>: Reconstitute 1.2 gm IV injection with 20 ml Water for Injection BP.</li><li>Should not be mixed with dextrose solutions, sodium bicarbonate, protein hydrolysates, or blood products.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 25°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Reconstituted suspension should be refrigerated and used within 7 days.</li><li>Reconstituted vials should be used within 20 minutes.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1084_1742279483.jpg', 64, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 11:31:23', '2025-03-18 11:31:23'),
(3254, 'Targaba', 'Mirogabalin Besylate', 12.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1731_1742279525.jpg', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 11:32:05', '2025-03-18 11:32:05'),
(3255, 'Razosin ER', 'Prazosin Hydrochloride', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Razosin ER 5 mg (Prazosin)</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>:</li><li> Prazosin is indicated for the treatment of all grades of essential (primary) hypertension and secondary hypertension of various etiologies. It can be used alone or in combination with a diuretic and/or other antihypertensive drugs to achieve the desired patient response. It is safe for hypertensive patients with impaired renal function since renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate are not adversely affected.</li><li><strong>Left Ventricular Failure</strong>:</li><li> Prazosin is useful in the treatment of left ventricular failure, especially for patients who have not responded adequately or have become refractory to conventional therapies such as diuretics or cardiac glycosides.</li><li><strong>Raynaud\'s Phenomenon and Raynaud\'s Disease</strong>:</li><li> Prazosin is indicated for the treatment of Raynaud’s phenomenon and Raynaud’s disease by reducing the frequency and severity of attacks.</li><li><strong>Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia</strong>:</li><li> Prazosin can be used as an adjunct in treating urinary obstruction caused by benign prostatic hyperplasia, and it can help patients awaiting prostatic surgery.</li></ol><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</p><p> Alpha adrenoceptor blocking drugs.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><p> Prazosin works by decreasing total peripheral vascular resistance through selective inhibition of alpha-1-adrenoreceptors in vascular smooth muscle, thus lowering blood pressure. It does not cause rebound hypertension after cessation of therapy. It is beneficial for patients with left ventricular failure by reducing left ventricular filling pressure, cardiac impedance, and increasing cardiac output. Additionally, it helps improve urinary flow in men with benign prostatic hyperplasia.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Razosin ER 5 mg</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: Start at 0.5 mg in the evening, and increase to 1 mg or more gradually, up to a daily total of 20 mg. Additional medications like diuretics or beta-blockers can be added.</li><li><strong>Left Ventricular Failure</strong>: Start at 0.5 mg two to four times daily, gradually increasing to 4 mg to 20 mg in divided doses as clinically needed.</li><li><strong>Raynaud\'s Phenomenon/Disease</strong>: Start at 0.5 mg twice daily, with potential increases depending on response.</li><li><strong>Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia</strong>: Start at 0.5 mg twice daily, with potential increases based on response. The typical maintenance dose is 2 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Extended Release (XR)</strong>: Start at 2.5 mg once daily and increase gradually, with a maintenance dose up to 20 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong>:</p><p> Prazosin ER can be taken with or without food. It’s recommended to start the dosage at dinner, at least 2-3 hours before bedtime. Maintenance doses can be taken with or without meals.</p><p><strong>Interactions</strong>:</p><p> No significant adverse interactions have been reported with common medications such as digitalis, insulin, tranquilizers, antiarrhythmics, and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><p> Prazosin is contraindicated in patients with a known sensitivity to quinazolines.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</p><p> Common side effects include dizziness, headache, drowsiness, nausea, and weakness. Other side effects may include orthostatic hypotension, tachycardia, nausea, vomiting, and liver function abnormalities. In rare cases, it may cause hallucinations, depression, and priapism.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong>:</p><p> Prazosin is a pregnancy category C drug. It should only be used during pregnancy if the benefits outweigh the risks. Prazosin is excreted in small amounts in breast milk, so caution is advised when administered to nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions and Warnings</strong>:</p><ul><li>Careful monitoring is required during the initiation of therapy to prevent postural hypotension.</li><li>Patients with left ventricular failure should be closely monitored as too rapid titration could result in a significant fall in cardiac output and blood pressure.</li><li>Elderly patients may require lower doses due to increased sensitivity to hypotension.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose</strong>:</p><p> If overdose occurs, hypotension may be managed by keeping the patient supine and using volume expanders or vasopressors if necessary. Prazosin is not dialyzable.</p><p><strong>Storage</strong>:</p><p> Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture, and out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Special Populations</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Prazosin does not worsen renal function in patients with renal impairment. Start with 0.5 mg daily and adjust carefully.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Dysfunction</strong>: Initiate treatment with 0.5 mg daily and increase cautiously.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Begin with the lowest possible dose due to increased susceptibility to hypotension.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '430_1742279676.jpg', 17, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 11:34:36', '2025-03-18 11:34:36'),
(3256, 'Loxodol', 'Lornoxicam', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '8 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p>Loxodol is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Short-term relief of <strong>acute mild to moderate pain</strong></li><li><strong>Symptomatic relief</strong> of pain and inflammation in <strong>osteoarthritis</strong></li><li><strong>Symptomatic relief</strong> of pain and inflammation in <strong>rheumatoid arthritis</strong></li><li> 🚨 <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Lornoxicam is a <strong>non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID)</strong> and <strong>antirheumatic</strong> drug of the <strong>oxicam class</strong>. It is intended for <strong>short-term treatment</strong> of acute mild to moderate pain and symptoms of <strong>rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis</strong>, such as <strong>pain and inflammation of joints</strong>.</p><p>Its <strong>mode of action</strong> is mainly due to the <strong>inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis</strong> (by inhibiting the <strong>cyclooxygenase enzyme</strong>), leading to <strong>desensitization of peripheral nociceptors</strong> and <strong>inhibition of inflammation</strong>.</p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Acute Pain:</strong> <strong>8-16 mg</strong> Lornoxicam daily, divided into <strong>2 or 3 doses</strong> (Max: <strong>16 mg/day</strong>)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Osteoarthritis &amp; Rheumatoid Arthritis:Initial dose:</strong> <strong>12 mg/day</strong>, divided into <strong>2 or 3 doses</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance dose:</strong> <strong>Not to exceed 16 mg/day</strong></li><li><strong>Use in Children &amp; Adolescents:</strong> <strong>Not recommended</strong> (below 18 years) due to lack of safety data.</li><li><strong>Elderly Patients (&gt;65 years):</strong> No special dose adjustment, but caution advised due to <strong>gastrointestinal risks</strong>.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Max <strong>12 mg/day</strong>, divided into <strong>2 or 3 doses</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Max <strong>12 mg/day</strong>, divided into <strong>2 or 3 doses</strong>.</li></ul><p>🚨 <em>Undesirable effects may be minimized by using the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration necessary to control symptoms.</em></p><p> 🚨 <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><h3><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Increased bleeding risk</strong> with <strong>Vitamin K antagonists</strong> (e.g., Warfarin).</li><li><strong>Kidney function issues</strong> when combined with <strong>Cyclosporin</strong>.</li><li><strong>Increased toxicity</strong> with <strong>Lithium, Methotrexate, and Digoxin</strong>.</li><li><strong>Reduced efficacy</strong> of <strong>Diuretics, ACE inhibitors, and Angiotensin II receptor antagonists</strong> in heart failure patients.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to Lornoxicam or its excipients</li><li><strong>Peptic ulcer disease</strong></li><li><strong>Severe hepatic impairment</strong></li><li><strong>Severe renal impairment</strong> (Serum creatinine &gt;700 pmol/L)</li><li><strong>Thrombocytopenia</strong></li><li><strong>Third trimester of pregnancy</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p>Common NSAID-related <strong>gastrointestinal side effects</strong>, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Peptic ulcers, GI bleeding, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, constipation, and abdominal pain</strong></li><li><strong>Severe cases:</strong> Perforation, <strong>hematemesis, ulcerative stomatitis, or exacerbation of colitis &amp; Crohn’s disease</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Contraindicated in the third trimester</strong> of pregnancy</li><li><strong>Not recommended during the first and second trimesters</strong></li><li><strong>Avoid in breastfeeding mothers</strong>, as Lornoxicam is excreted in rat milk (no human data available).</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><p>Lornoxicam should be used with caution in:</p><ul><li><strong>Impaired kidney function</strong></li><li><strong>History of high blood pressure or heart failure</strong></li><li><strong>Ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease</strong></li><li><strong>Bleeding disorders</strong></li><li><strong>History of asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE)</strong></li><li><strong>Women undergoing infertility treatment</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><p>Currently, no specific antidote for Lornoxicam overdose. Possible symptoms include:</p><ul><li><strong>Nausea, vomiting, dizziness, visual disturbances</strong></li><li><strong>Gastric lavage</strong> and <strong>symptomatic treatment</strong> recommended</li><li><strong>Lornoxicam is not dialyzable</strong> due to its short half-life</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Store below 30°C</strong>, protect from <strong>light &amp; moisture</strong>.</li><li><strong>Keep out of reach of children.</strong></li><li><strong>Do not use after expiration date.</strong></li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '261_1742279870.jpg', 62, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 11:37:50', '2025-03-18 11:37:50'),
(3257, 'Kidopia', 'Atropine Sulfate', 280.00, 1, 'x 5 ml drop', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '0.01%', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a clear, structured summary with normal bold text for Kidopia 5 ml (Atropine):</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications</strong></p><ul><li>Non-ulcer dyspepsia</li><li>Irritable bowel syndrome</li><li>Diverticular disease</li><li>Bradycardia</li><li>Organophosphorus poisoning</li><li>Premedication in anesthesia</li><li>Poisoning or overdosage with compounds having muscarinic actions</li><li>Ophthalmic inflammatory eye disorders</li><li>Eye refraction</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Anticholinergics (antimuscarinics)/Anti-spasmodics</li><li>Mydriatic and Cycloplegic agents</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Atropine binds to and inhibits muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, producing a range of anticholinergic effects. It competitively blocks muscarinic receptors in peripheral tissues (heart, intestines, bronchial muscles, iris, and secretory glands) and can cause some central stimulation. It abolishes bradycardia, reduces heart block due to vagal activity, relaxes smooth muscles in the bronchi and gut, reduces glandular secretions, and has mydriatic and cycloplegic effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">IV: Bradycardia: 500 mcg every 3-5 minutes (max 3 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">IV/IM: Organophosphorus poisoning: 2 mg every 10-30 minutes until muscarinic effects disappear or atropine toxicity appears</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">IM/SC: Premedication in anesthesia: 300-600 mcg 30-60 minutes before anesthesia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">IV/IM/SC: Poisoning/overdosage with muscarinic compounds: 0.6-1 mg, repeat every 2 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Ophthalmic (inflammatory eye disorders): 1-2 drops (0.5-1% solution), 4 times/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Ophthalmic (refraction): 1 drop (1% solution), twice daily for 1-2 days before procedure</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oral: Non-ulcer dyspepsia, IBS, Diverticular disease: 0.6-1.2 mg as a single dose at bedtime</li><li><strong>Pediatric</strong> (Anesthesia):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">7 to 16 pounds: 0.1 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">17 to 24 pounds: 0.15 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">24 to 40 pounds: 0.2 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">40 to 65 pounds: 0.3 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">65 to 90 pounds: 0.4 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Over 90 pounds: 0.4-0.6 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Additive anticholinergic effects with quinidine, antidepressants, and some antihistamines.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Glaucoma</li><li>Chronic respiratory disease</li><li>Sick sinus syndrome</li><li>Thyrotoxicosis</li><li>Cardiac failure</li><li>Pyloric stenosis</li><li>Prostatic hypertrophy</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Injection</strong>: Dry mouth, dysphagia, constipation, flushing, tachycardia, arrhythmias, mydriasis, photophobia, cycloplegia, raised intraocular pressure.</li><li><strong>Toxic doses</strong>: Tachycardia, hyperpyrexia, restlessness, confusion, excitement, hallucinations, delirium, circulatory failure, respiratory depression.</li><li><strong>Eye drops</strong>: Irritation, hyperemia, edema, conjunctivitis, increased intraocular pressure.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: Animal reproduction studies not conducted. It is unknown whether atropine can harm a fetus or affect reproduction capacity. Should be used only if clearly needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Reflux esophagitis</li><li>Elderly</li><li>Infants and children</li><li>Pregnancy</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Hyperthermia, hypertension, increased respiratory rate, nausea, vomiting, CNS stimulation.</li><li>Severe intoxication may cause CNS depression, coma, respiratory failure, and death.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at room temperature (20-25°C), away from heat, moisture, and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Anticholinergics (antimuscarinics)/Anti-spasmodics</li><li>Mydriatic and Cycloplegic agents</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Atropine blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, causing a range of anticholinergic effects, including abolishing bradycardia, relaxing smooth muscles, reducing glandular secretions, and having mydriatic and cycloplegic effects.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category C</strong>: Should be used only if clearly needed during pregnancy.</li></ul><p>This summary provides a concise view of Kidopia (Atropine) 5 ml. Let me know if you need more details!</p>', 'active', '888_1742280127.jpg', 17, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 11:42:07', '2025-05-03 23:15:33'),
(3258, 'Methsolon', 'Methylprednisolone', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '16 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p><strong>Endocrine Disorders:</strong></p><ul><li>Primary or Secondary <strong>Adrenocortical Insufficiency</strong></li><li><strong>Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia</strong></li><li><strong>Nonsuppurative Thyroiditis</strong></li><li><strong>Hypercalcemia</strong> associated with <strong>Cancer</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Rheumatic Disorders:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Juvenile Rheumatoid Arthritis</strong></li><li><strong>Ankylosing Spondylitis</strong></li><li><strong>Acute and Subacute Bursitis</strong></li><li><strong>Synovitis of Osteoarthritis</strong></li><li><strong>Acute nonspecific Tenosynovitis</strong></li><li><strong>Post-traumatic Osteoarthritis</strong></li><li><strong>Psoriatic Arthritis</strong></li><li><strong>Epicondylitis</strong></li><li><strong>Acute Gouty Arthritis</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Collagen Diseases:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Systemic lupus Erythematosus</strong></li><li><strong>Systemic Dermatomyositis</strong></li><li><strong>Acute Rheumatic Carditis</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Dermatologic Diseases:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Bullous Dermatitis Herpetiformis</strong></li><li><strong>Severe Erythema Multiforme</strong> (Stevens-Johnson syndrome)</li><li><strong>Severe Seborrheic Dermatitis</strong></li><li><strong>Exfoliative Dermatitis</strong></li><li><strong>Mycosis Fungoides</strong></li><li><strong>Pemphigus</strong></li><li><strong>Severe Psoriasis</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Allergy:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Seasonal or Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</strong></li><li><strong>Drug hypersensitivity reactions</strong></li><li><strong>Serum Sickness</strong></li><li><strong>Contact Dermatitis</strong></li><li><strong>Bronchial Asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Atopic Dermatitis</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Ophthalmic Diseases:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Allergic Corneal Ulcers</strong></li><li><strong>Herpes Zoster Ophthalmicus</strong></li><li><strong>Anterior segment inflammation</strong></li><li><strong>Sympathetic Ophthalmia</strong></li><li><strong>Keratitis</strong></li><li><strong>Optic Neuritis</strong></li><li><strong>Allergic Conjunctivitis</strong></li><li><strong>Chorioretinitis, Iritis, and Iridocyclitis</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Respiratory Diseases:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptomatic sarcoidosis</strong></li><li><strong>Loeffler\'s syndrome</strong> not manageable by other means</li><li><strong>Berylliosis</strong></li><li><strong>Aspiration Pneumonitis</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Hematological Disorders:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Idiopathic Thrombocytopenic Purpura</strong> in adults</li><li><strong>Secondary Thrombocytopenia</strong> in adults</li><li><strong>Acquired (Autoimmune) Hemolytic Anemia</strong></li><li><strong>Erythroblastopenia</strong></li><li><strong>Congenital (Erythroid) Hypoplastic Anemia</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Neoplastic Diseases:</strong></p><ul><li>Palliative management of <strong>Leukemias</strong> and <strong>Lymphomas</strong> in adults</li><li><strong>Acute leukemia of childhood</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Edematous States:</strong></p><ul><li>To induce a <strong>diuresis</strong> or remission of <strong>Proteinuria</strong> in <strong>Nephrotic Syndrome</strong>, without <strong>Uremia</strong>, of the <strong>idiopathic type</strong> or due to <strong>Lupus Erythematosus</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Gastrointestinal Disease:</strong></p><ul><li>To tide over a critical period of the disease in <strong>Ulcerative Colitis</strong> and <strong>Regional Enteritis</strong></li></ul><p><strong>CNS Disease:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Acute Exacerbations of Multiple Sclerosis</strong></li></ul><p>🚨 <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><h3><strong>Description</strong></h3><p><strong>Methsolon</strong> is a <strong>potent anti-inflammatory steroid</strong>. It has greater anti-inflammatory potency than <strong>Prednisolone</strong>, with <strong>less tendency</strong> than Prednisolone to induce <strong>sodium and water retention</strong>. Its relative potency compared to <strong>Hydrocortisone</strong> is at least <strong>4:1</strong>.</p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p><strong>Pharmacodynamic Properties:</strong></p><p> <strong>Methylprednisolone</strong> is a potent <strong>anti-inflammatory agent</strong> with a significant capacity to <strong>inhibit the immune system</strong>. Glucocorticoids primarily bind to and activate intracellular <strong>glucocorticoid receptors</strong>, which then bind to promoter regions of <strong>DNA</strong> (activating or suppressing transcription), resulting in the inactivation of genes via <strong>deacetylation of histones</strong>.</p><p><strong>Pharmacokinetic Properties:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Bioavailability:</strong> <strong>82-89%</strong> following oral administration.</li><li><strong>Absorption:</strong> Rapid absorption; maximum plasma concentration is reached in <strong>1.5 to 2.3 hours</strong>.</li><li><strong>Volume of Distribution:</strong> <strong>41-61.5 liters</strong></li><li><strong>Crosses:</strong> Blood-brain barrier and placental barrier.</li><li><strong>Excretion:</strong> Secreted in breast milk.</li><li><strong>Plasma Protein Binding:</strong> <strong>77%</strong></li><li><strong>Metabolism:</strong> Primarily in the liver to inactive metabolites.</li><li><strong>Renal Failure:</strong> No adjustment necessary.</li><li><strong>Haemodialyzable:</strong> Yes</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Usual Range:</strong> <strong>2-48 mg daily</strong> in divided doses, depending on the disease.</li><li><strong>Anti-inflammatory/Immunosuppressive Dosage:</strong> Initial dosage varies from <strong>4-48 mg/day</strong>, depending on the disease.</li><li><strong>Maintenance Dosage:</strong> After a favorable response, reduce the dosage until the lowest level that maintains an adequate response is reached.</li></ul><p><strong>Multiple Sclerosis Treatment:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>160 mg/day</strong> for one week, followed by <strong>64 mg every other day</strong> for 1 month.</li></ul><p><strong>For Severe Allergy and Dermatitis Treatment (Using 4 mg tablets):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Day 1:</strong> 2 tablets before breakfast + 1 tablet after lunch + 1 tablet after dinner + 2 tablets at bedtime</li><li><strong>Day 2:</strong> 1 tablet before breakfast + 1 tablet after lunch + 1 tablet after dinner + 2 tablets at bedtime</li><li><strong>Day 3:</strong> 1 tablet before breakfast + 1 tablet after lunch + 1 tablet after dinner + 1 tablet at bedtime</li><li><strong>Day 4:</strong> 1 tablet before breakfast + 1 tablet after lunch + 1 tablet at bedtime</li><li><strong>Day 5:</strong> 1 tablet before breakfast + 1 tablet at bedtime</li><li><strong>Day 6:</strong> 1 tablet before breakfast</li></ul><p><strong>Alternate-Day Therapy (ADT):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Principles:</strong> ADT uses <strong>twice the usual daily dose</strong> every other morning. This method minimizes side effects like <strong>pituitary-adrenal suppression</strong> and <strong>Cushingoid symptoms</strong>.</li></ul><p>🚨 <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><h3><strong>Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Erythromycin, Clarithromycin, Phenobarbital, Phenytoin, Rifampin, and Ketoconazole</strong> can inhibit <strong>Methsolon</strong> metabolism.</li><li><strong>Estrogens</strong> (including birth control pills) can <strong>increase</strong> the effect of corticosteroids by <strong>50%</strong>.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin</strong> reduces <strong>Methsolon</strong> metabolism, while <strong>Methsolon</strong> reduces <strong>Cyclosporin</strong> metabolism.</li><li><strong>Blood thinners</strong> (e.g., Warfarin) may have their effects <strong>increased or decreased</strong> by Methsolon.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Systemic fungal infections</strong></li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to components</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p>Short courses of <strong>Methsolon</strong> are typically well-tolerated, with few mild side effects. However, long-term, high doses may result in <strong>serious side effects</strong>, such as:</p><ul><li><strong>Fluid retention, weight gain, high blood pressure, potassium loss</strong></li><li><strong>Headache, muscle weakness, facial hair growth, glaucoma, cataracts</strong></li><li><strong>Peptic ulceration, growth retardation in children</strong></li><li><strong>Convulsions, psychiatric disturbances</strong> (e.g., depression, insomnia)</li><li>Prolonged use can lead to <strong>adrenal insufficiency</strong>.</li><li><strong>Withdrawal symptoms</strong> can occur when abruptly discontinuing; <strong>gradual tapering</strong> is recommended.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: Use only if the potential benefit justifies the risk.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Insufficient data on excretion in human breast milk.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Adrenocortical insufficiency</strong> may persist for months after stopping therapy; reinstitute hormone therapy in stressful situations.</li><li><strong>Caution</strong> in patients with <strong>ocular herpes simplex</strong>, <strong>hypothyroidism</strong>, or <strong>cirrhosis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Taper doses gradually</strong> to avoid withdrawal symptoms.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><p>Acute toxicity and death from overdose are rare. Treatment is <strong>supportive and symptomatic</strong>, with monitoring of <strong>serum electrolytes</strong>.</p><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool, dry place</strong>, away from light.</li><li><strong>Keep out of reach of children.</strong></li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1582_1742280151.jpg', 2, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 11:42:31', '2025-03-18 11:42:31'),
(3259, 'Algisum DX', 'Sodium Alginate + Sodium Bicarbonate + Calcium Carbonate', 290.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '(500 mg+213 mg+325 mg)/10 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of <strong>Algisum DX 200 ml</strong></h3><p>Algisum DX 200 ml is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Gastric reflux</li><li>Heartburn</li><li>Flatulence associated with gastric reflux</li><li>Heartburn during pregnancy</li><li>Epigastric and retrosternal discomfort due to gastric reflux</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p><strong>Antacids</strong></p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Algisum DX 200 ml acts physically by forming a protective gel raft (Alginic acid) after ingestion. This raft neutralizes gastric acid, helping to prevent gastro-esophageal reflux for up to 4 hours. In severe cases, the raft may reflux into the esophagus, providing soothing relief instead of stomach contents.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; children over 12 years:</strong> 10-20 mL after meals and at bedtime, up to 4 times a day.</li><li><strong>Children (6-12 years):</strong> 5-10 mL after meals and at bedtime, up to 4 times a day.</li><li><strong>Children under 6 years:</strong> Not recommended.</li><li><strong>Elderly &amp; Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustments necessary.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong> Caution if a highly restricted salt diet is required.</li></ul><h3>Interaction</h3><p>A <strong>2-hour</strong> time interval should be maintained between Algisum DX and the following medications:</p><ul><li>Tetracyclines</li><li>Digoxin</li><li>Fluoroquinolones</li><li>Iron salts</li><li>Ketoconazole</li><li>Neuroleptics</li><li>Thyroid hormones</li><li>Penicillamine</li><li>Beta-blockers (e.g., atenolol, metoprolol)</li><li>Glucocorticoids</li><li>Chloroquine</li><li>Bisphosphonates (e.g., diphosphonates)</li><li>Estramustine</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Do not use in patients with hypersensitivity to any of the active ingredients or excipients.</p><h3>Side Effects</h3><p>Common side effects may include:</p><ul><li>Constipation</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Belching</li></ul><p>In case of excessive dosing, symptoms may include a sensation of swelling. Seek medical advice if needed.</p><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Safe for use during pregnancy when clinically necessary.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong> No adverse effects observed in breastfed infants.</li><li><strong>Fertility:</strong> No adverse effects on fertility or reproduction have been noted.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Dietary considerations:</strong> Each 10 mL dose contains 141 mg of sodium (6.2 mmol) and 160 mg of calcium carbonate (1.6 mmol), so consider these when prescribing to patients on a restricted salt or calcium diet.</li><li>If symptoms don\'t improve within 7 days, reassess the patient\'s condition.</li><li>Use caution with patients having hypercalcemia or recurrent calcium-containing kidney stones.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><p>In case of overdose, symptomatic treatment should be administered. The patient may experience abdominal distension.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong> in a cool, dry place.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li><li>Do not refrigerate or freeze.</li></ul><h3>Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Paediatric Use:</strong> Not recommended for children under 6 years.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> No modification required.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Algisum DX 200 ml works by reacting with gastric acid to form a protective gel raft, which neutralizes acid and prevents gastro-esophageal reflux. The raft floats on the stomach contents, protecting the esophagus from acid, pepsin, and bile. This effect is further enhanced by Calcium Carbonate and Sodium Bicarbonate.</p>', 'active', '1120_1742280517.webp', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 11:48:37', '2025-04-30 14:31:18'),
(3260, 'Abc Derm Hydratant', 'Bioderma Topical Preparation', 2500.00, 1, 'x 200 ml tube', '200 ml tube', 'Lotion', '200 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1548_1742280784.jpg', 8, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 11:53:04', '2025-05-02 22:39:09'),
(3261, 'Mexclav', 'Cefuroxime Axetil + Clavulanic Acid', 35.00, 7, 'tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg+62.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Mexclav 250 mg + 62.50 mg</strong></h3><p>Mexclav 250 mg + 62.50 mg is indicated for treating infections caused by sensitive bacteria, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong> due to <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial otitis media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non-beta-lactamase producing strains)</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong>, including pneumonia, caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp.</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, and <em>E. coli</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong> and secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains), or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains)</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin-structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></li><li><strong>Bone and joint infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong> caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase-producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease</strong> (erythema migrans) caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em></li><li><strong>Septicemia</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), and <em>Klebsiella spp.</em></li><li><strong>Meningitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), <em>Neisseria meningitidis</em>, and <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Switch therapy</strong> (injectable to oral)</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Second-generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Cefuroxime</strong>: A bactericidal second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic that disrupts bacterial cell wall synthesis, specifically by interfering with the transpeptidation process. It is effective against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including beta-lactamase-producing strains.</li><li><strong>Clavulanic Acid</strong>: A beta-lactamase inhibitor that irreversibly binds to beta-lactamase enzymes, protecting Cefuroxime from degradation by these enzymes. It provides an effective solution for treating infections caused by beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage of Mexclav 250 mg + 62.50 mg</strong></h3><h4><strong>Adolescents and Adults (13 years and older)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong>: 250 mg twice daily (b.i.d.) for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis</strong>: 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infections</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong>: 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Gonorrhoea</strong>: 1000 mg b.i.d. Single dose</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>MDR Typhoid Fever</strong>: 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li><strong>Early Lyme disease</strong>: 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li></ul><h4><strong>Paediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong>: 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Acute otitis media</strong>: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis</strong>: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li><strong>Impetigo</strong>: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li></ul><h3><strong>Administration of Mexclav 250 mg + 62.50 mg</strong></h3><ul><li>The tablet may be taken with or without food.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction of Mexclav 250 mg + 62.50 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Probenecid</strong>: Co-administration may increase serum concentration of Cefuroxime by 50%.</li><li><strong>Medications reducing gastric acidity</strong>: May reduce the bioavailability of Cefuroxime.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known allergies to cephalosporins and those with <strong>Pseudomembranous Colitis</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Generally well-tolerated, but potential side effects include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal discomfort, and pain.</li><li>Rarely (&lt;0.2%): renal dysfunction, anaphylaxis, angioedema, pruritis, rash, and serum sickness-like urticaria.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>Mexclav is typically avoided in the first trimester but can be used safely in later stages for urinary and other infections. It is excreted in breast milk in small amounts, so caution is advised during lactation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients receiving potent diuretics or with a history of colitis.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><ul><li>Second-generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Cefuroxime</strong>: Bactericidal action against Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria by disrupting cell wall synthesis.</li><li><strong>Clavulanic Acid</strong>: Beta-lactamase inhibitor that protects Cefuroxime from degradation, enhancing its effectiveness against resistant bacteria.</li></ul><p><br></p><p>4o mini</p>', 'active', '1976_1742315069.jpg', 69, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 11:55:43', '2025-03-18 21:24:29');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3262, 'Fertinatal', 'Dehydroepiandrosterone', 30.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information regarding <strong>Fertinatal 25 mg</strong> (DHEA) in a more structured and bold format:</p><h3><strong>Indications of Fertinatal 25 mg</strong></h3><p>Adequately powered, long-term clinical trials are lacking to support the therapeutic use of Fertinatal 25 mg (DHEA) and Fertinatal 25 mg sulfate (DHEAS) supplementation (hereafter jointly referred to as DHEA/S). Reviews of clinical trials found no convincing evidence to support a place in therapy for DHEA in improving cognitive function or physical strength in elderly patients, or in treating postmenopausal symptoms in women, hyperlipidemia or insulin resistance, schizophrenia, or cancer. Some evidence exists to support the use of DHEA/S supplementation in:</p><ul><li>Women with diminished ovarian reserves.</li><li>Subpopulations of elderly women with osteoporosis.</li><li>Mild systemic lupus erythematosus.</li></ul><p>DHEA is recommended as third-line monotherapy or adjunctive therapy for the treatment of major depressive disorder (MDD) by Canadian Network for Mood and Anxiety Treatments (CANMAT) clinical guidelines. Limited data suggest a potential role for DHEA as an <strong>anxiolytic</strong>.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Dosage of Fertinatal 25 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Adrenal Insufficiency</strong>: 50 mg/day for 3 months is considered a replacement dose, while 200 mg/day achieves supraphysiological circulating levels and is considered a pharmacological dose.</li><li><strong>Anorexia Nervosa</strong>: 100 mg/day for 6 months was used in a pilot study.</li><li><strong>Diminished Ovarian Reserve</strong>: 50 to 75 mg/day (in divided doses) has been used in clinical studies of assisted reproduction.</li><li><strong>Exercise Training–Induced Muscle Damage</strong>: 100 mg/day of Fertinatal 25 mg supplementation was administered over 5 days in a study in young men undergoing exercise training.</li><li><strong>Major Depressive Disorder</strong>: Doses ranging from 30 to 450 mg/day for 6 to 8 weeks have been used in clinical studies.</li><li><strong>Metabolic Syndrome</strong>: 100 mg/day for 3 months has been used in a study evaluating effects against metabolic syndrome in pre- and postmenopausal women.</li><li><strong>Postmenopausal Women</strong>: 25 mg/day has been suggested because this dose minimizes androgenic adverse effects; however, only studies in which at least 50 mg/day was used demonstrated positive outcomes as hormonal replacement therapy.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction of Fertinatal 25 mg</strong></h3><p>Supraphysiologic serum DHEAS levels due to DHEA supplementation have been documented to interfere with commercially available progesterone assays, yielding false-positive increases in serum progesterone.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><p>Use of Fertinatal 25 mg is <strong>not recommended</strong> in:</p><ul><li><strong>Breast cancer</strong>.</li><li><strong>Prostate cancer</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Fertinatal 25 mg</strong></h3><p>Studies in adrenal insufficiency suggest DHEA is generally well tolerated. However, data from long-term studies are lacking. Observed adverse effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Mania and hypomania</strong></li><li><strong>Acne</strong></li><li><strong>Hirsutism</strong></li><li><strong>Gynecomastia</strong></li><li><strong>Testicular changes</strong></li><li><strong>Increased blood pressure</strong></li><li><strong>Decreased high-density lipoprotein (HDL) levels</strong></li></ul><p>Use caution in individuals with:</p><ul><li><strong>Psychiatric disorders</strong> (agitation, confusion, anxiety, paranoia, and suicidal thoughts have been reported).</li><li><strong>Diabetes</strong> (DHEA may increase insulin resistance or sensitivity).</li><li><strong>Liver dysfunction</strong> (DHEA may exacerbate this condition).</li><li><strong>Polycystic ovarian syndrome</strong> (DHEA may worsen this condition).</li><li><strong>Erythrocytosis</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Keep below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from light &amp; moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>The sulfate derivative of DHEA has been shown to act as a:</p><ul><li><strong>Noncompetitive antagonist</strong> of gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) receptors.</li><li><strong>Positive allosteric modulator</strong> of N-methyl-D-aspartate receptors.</li></ul><p>In humans, neuroprotective, antioxidant, antihypertensive, and anti-inflammatory effects have also been reported. Serum DHEA levels peak in <strong>60 to 480 minutes</strong> after administration.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h3><p>Information regarding safety and efficacy in pregnancy and lactation is lacking. Fertinatal 25 mg supplementation has been evaluated for use in improving oocyte production in infertility.</p>', 'active', '1697_1742281019.jpg', 50, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 11:56:59', '2025-03-18 11:56:59'),
(3263, 'Neumir', 'Mirogabalin Besylate', 12.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '293_1742281268.webp', 62, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 12:01:08', '2025-03-18 12:01:08'),
(3264, 'Decaren', 'Coenzyme Q10 [Ubidecarenone]', 75.00, 5, 'capsule', '5 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Decaren 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>It is indicated for Co-enzyme Q10 deficiency and mitochondrial disorders.</li><li>It is also used in congestive heart failure, myocardial infarction, high blood pressure (hypertension) in combination with other medications, preventing migraine headache, Parkinson\'s disease, improving the immune system, infertility in men, and muscular dystrophy.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Supplements &amp; adjuvant therapy</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Co-enzyme Q10 is a vitamin-like substance found throughout the body, especially in the heart, liver, kidneys, and pancreas. It is required for the proper function of many organs and chemical reactions in the body. It helps to provide energy to cells and has antioxidant activity.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Decaren 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Co-enzyme Q10 deficiency</strong>: 150 mg daily.</li><li><strong>For mitochondrial disorders</strong>: 150-160 mg, or 2 mg/kg/day.</li><li><strong>For heart failure in adults</strong>: 100 mg per day divided into 2 or 3 doses.</li><li><strong>For recent myocardial infarction</strong>: 120 mg daily in 2 divided doses.</li><li><strong>For high blood pressure</strong>: 120-200 mg per day divided into 2 doses.</li><li><strong>For isolated systolic hypertension</strong>: 60 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>For preventing migraine headache</strong>: 100 mg three times daily.</li><li><strong>For Parkinson\'s disease</strong>: 300 mg, 600 mg, 1200 mg, and 2400 mg per day in 3-4 divided doses.</li><li><strong>For infertility in men</strong>: 200-300 mg per day.</li><li><strong>For muscular dystrophy</strong>: 100 mg per day.</li></ul><p>Dividing the total daily dose by taking smaller amounts two or three times a day instead of a large amount all at once can help to reduce side effects.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Decaren 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Antihypertensive drugs</strong>: May cause blood pressure to go too low.</li><li><strong>Anticancer drugs</strong>: May decrease the effectiveness of some anticancer drugs.</li><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>: May decrease the effectiveness of warfarin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Co-enzyme Q10 is contraindicated in patients who have hypersensitivity to the active substance or to any of the excipients. It is also contraindicated in patients scheduled for surgery in the next two weeks.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Decaren 200 mg</strong></p><p>Co-enzyme Q10 is safe for most adults. It can cause some mild side effects, including:</p><ul><li>Stomach upset</li><li>Loss of appetite</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>It can cause allergic skin rashes in some people.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Co-enzyme Q10 should not be used during pregnancy or breast-feeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Co-enzyme Q10 may lower blood sugar levels, so caution should be taken in patients with diabetes or hypoglycemia.</li><li>Co-enzyme Q10 may decrease blood pressure, so caution is advised in patients with low blood pressure or those taking blood pressure medications.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '994_1742281597.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 12:06:37', '2025-05-01 20:04:34'),
(3265, 'Bliss', 'Silymarin [Dried extract of milk thistle fruits]', 20.00, 6, 'capsule', '6 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '140 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1317_1742281742.webp', 50, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 12:09:02', '2025-05-01 22:59:58'),
(3266, 'Ursoren', 'Ursodeoxycholic Acid', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '150 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ursoren 150 mg</strong></p><p> Ursoren 150 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Cholestasis (Jaundice)</li><li>Viral Hepatitis</li><li>Alcoholic Fatty Liver</li><li>Primary Biliary Cirrhosis (PBC)</li><li>Primary Sclerosing Cholangitis (PSC)</li><li>Dissolution of Gallstones</li><li>Non-Alcoholic Steato Hepatitis (NASH)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Anti-gallstones drugs: Bile Acids</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Ursoren 150 mg is a naturally occurring bile acid used to treat different hepatobiliary disorders. Its activity is achieved through a decrease in the secretion of cholesterol in bile. Ursoren 150 mg works via several mechanisms:</p><ul><li>It reduces cholesterol absorption</li><li>Suppresses liver cholesterol synthesis</li><li>It does not inhibit bile acid synthesis, which alters bile composition from supersaturated to unsaturated</li><li>Promotes the formation of liquid cholesterol crystal complexes that enhance the removal of cholesterol from the gallbladder into the intestine for expulsion</li></ul><p>Ursoren 150 mg improves cholestatic liver diseases by:</p><ul><li>Protecting cholangiocytes against cytotoxicity of hydrophobic bile acids</li><li>Stimulating hepatobiliary secretion</li><li>Protecting hepatocytes against bile acid-induced apoptosis</li></ul><p>Ursoren 150 mg is completely absorbed in the upper intestine. The time to peak serum concentration ranges from 30 to 150 minutes, with an absorption rate of 60-80%. After absorption, it enters the portal vein and undergoes extraction from portal blood by the liver, where it is conjugated with amino acids (either glycine or taurine) and secreted into the hepatic bile ducts. Small quantities of Ursoren 150 mg appear in the circulation, and very small amounts are excreted into urine. The biologic half-life ranges from 3.5 to 5.8 days.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ursoren 150 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dissolution of Gallstones</strong>: 8-12 mg/kg/day, either as a single night-time dose or in divided doses</li><li><strong>PBC</strong>: 10-15 mg/kg/day in 2-4 divided doses</li><li><strong>Acute Viral Hepatitis</strong>: 600 mg/day</li><li><strong>Alcoholic Fatty Liver</strong>: 300 mg/day</li><li><strong>PSC</strong>: 25-30 mg/kg/day</li><li><strong>NASH</strong>: 13-15 mg/kg/day</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ursoren 150 mg</strong></p><p> Ursoren 150 mg should not be used with drugs such as oestrogenic hormones that increase bile cholesterol. Concomitant administration with bile-acid binding drugs, including antacids, charcoal, and cholestyramine, should be avoided as this may reduce the effectiveness of therapy with Ursodeoxycholic acid.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Non-functioning gall-bladder</li><li>Calcified and pigmented gallstones</li><li>Inflammatory bowel disease</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ursoren 150 mg</strong></p><p> Commonly reported side effects include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Diarrhoea</li><li>Gallstone opacification</li><li>Pruritus</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy category B</strong>: No evidence of harm has been reported in pregnancy. It has been effectively used for the treatment of cholestasis of pregnancy during the last trimester without any side effects.</li><li>Problems have not been documented in humans regarding breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Should be used cautiously in those with liver disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 25°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep the medicine out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1260_1742281934.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 12:12:14', '2025-03-18 12:12:14'),
(3267, 'Denixil', 'Clonazepam', 4.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Denixil 0.25</h3><p>Denixil 0.25 is primarily used for treating the following conditions:</p><ol><li><strong>Panic Disorder</strong> (with or without agoraphobia): Denixil 0.25 helps manage panic disorder, which is characterized by unexpected panic attacks and the subsequent fear of further episodes. This includes excessive worry about the consequences of panic attacks.</li><li><strong>Seizure Disorders</strong>: Denixil 0.25 is also used for managing:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome</strong> (especially the petit mal variant)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Akinetic Seizures</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Myoclonic Seizures</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Absence Seizures</strong> (petit mal) in patients who have not responded to other treatments like succinimides.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</strong></li><li><strong>Benzodiazepine hypnotics</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Denixil 0.25 works by enhancing the activity of GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) in the central nervous system, leading to a reduction in nerve transmission in the motor cortex. This suppression helps in controlling spike and wave discharges associated with absence seizures, and it also has a general anticonvulsive effect.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><h4>For Adults with Seizure Disorders:</h4><ul><li><strong>Initial Dose</strong>: 1.5 mg/day, divided into 3 doses.</li><li><strong>Adjustment</strong>: Increase by 0.5-1 mg every 3 days, up to a maximum of 20 mg/day.</li></ul><h4>For Adults with Panic Disorder:</h4><ul><li><strong>Initial Dose</strong>: 0.25 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>After 3 days</strong>: Increase to 1 mg/day as needed.</li></ul><h4>Pediatric Patients (up to 10 years or 30 kg):</h4><ul><li><strong>Dose</strong>: 0.01-0.03 mg/kg/day, not exceeding 0.05 mg/kg/day, divided into multiple doses.</li></ul><h4>Injection Dosage:</h4><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.5 mg via slow IV injection or infusion.</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 1 mg via slow IV injection or infusion, repeat as necessary (typically 1-4 mg).</li></ul><p><strong>Note</strong>: Ensure slow administration to avoid complications like respiratory depression.</p><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to benzodiazepines.</li><li><strong>Severe liver disease</strong> or signs of significant liver impairment.</li><li><strong>Acute narrow-angle glaucoma</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><p>Common side effects include CNS depression-related issues like drowsiness (50% of patients), ataxia (30%), and behavioral changes (25%). Other side effects may include:</p><ul><li>Abnormal eye movements</li><li>Aphonia</li><li>Coma</li><li>Tremor</li><li>Confusion, depression, amnesia, hallucinations</li><li>Psychosis and palpitations</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>Denixil 0.25 should only be used during pregnancy if the benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: Not recommended.</li></ul><h3>Warnings &amp; Precautions</h3><ul><li>May trigger generalized tonic-clonic seizures in patients with multiple coexisting seizure disorders.</li><li><strong>Interaction with Valproic Acid</strong>: Combining Denixil 0.25 with valproic acid may result in absence status.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Higher sensitivity to the drug.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><p>Symptoms of overdose include:</p><ul><li>Drowsiness, ataxia, dysarthria, nystagmus</li><li>Severe overdose can lead to coma, hypotension, and respiratory depression.</li></ul><p><strong>Treatment</strong> for overdose may include:</p><ul><li><strong>Supportive care</strong> based on clinical condition.</li><li><strong>Activated charcoal</strong> within 1-2 hours to prevent further absorption.</li><li><strong>Flumazenil</strong> may be used for severe CNS depression but should be monitored carefully.</li></ul><h3>Storage &amp; Reconstitution</h3><ul><li><strong>Storage</strong>: Keep in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li><strong>Reconstitution for IV use</strong>: Dilute with 1 ml of water for injection. The infusion should not exceed 8 hours and must be prepared just before use.</li></ul><h3>Drug Interactions</h3><ul><li><strong>No effect on phenytoin, carbamazepine, or phenobarbital</strong>.</li><li><strong>Unexplored impact</strong> on the metabolism of other drugs.</li></ul><h3>Geriatric Use</h3><ul><li>Elderly individuals may experience more pronounced effects due to age-related changes in organ function and drug-receptor interactions.</li></ul><h3>Hepatic &amp; Renal Impairment</h3><ul><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: Caution is needed in cirrhotic patients.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment required.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further details on any aspect of Denixil 0.25.</p>', 'active', '1285_1742282153.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 12:15:53', '2025-03-18 12:15:53'),
(3268, 'Ulticort Plus', 'Halobetasol Propionate + Tazarotene', 250.00, 1, 'x 30 gm tube', '30 gm tube', 'Lotion', '0.01% + 0.045%', 'yes', 'no', '<h2>Indications of Ulticort Plus 30 gm</h2><p>This Lotion is a combination of halobetasol propionate and tazarotene indicated for the topical treatment of plaque psoriasis in adults.</p><h2>Theropeutic Class</h2><p>Other Topical corticosteroids</p><h2>Pharmacology</h2><p>Corticosteroids play a role in cellular signaling, immune function, inflammation, and protein regulation; however, the precise mechanism of action in plaque psoriasis is unknown. Tazarotene is a retinoid prodrug which is convert d to its active form, tazarotenic acid, the carboxylic acid of tazarotene, by deesterification. Tazarotenic acid binds to all three members of the retinoic acid receptor (RAR) family: RARα, RARβ and RARγ, but shows relative selectivity for RARβ, and RARγ and may modify gene expression. The clinical significance of these findings for the treatment of plaque psoriasis is unknown.A vasoconstrictor assay in healthy subjects with this lotion indicated that it is in the high to super-high range of potency as compared to other topical corticosteroids; however, similar blanching scores do not necessarily imply therapeutic equivalence. The potential for hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis suppression was evaluated in a study in adult subjects with moderate to severe plaque psoriasis. A median dose of 8.2 grams this lotion was applied once daily for 8 weeks and 20 subjects were assessed for HPA axis suppression at Weeks 4 and 8. HPA axis suppression was observed in 3 out of 20 (15%) subjects at Week 4. None of the 20 (0%) subjects had HPA axis suppression at Week 8. In this study, the criteria for HPA axis suppression was a serum cortisol level of less than or equal to 18 micrograms per deciliter 30 minutes after stimulation with cosyntropin (adrenocorticotropic hormone).</p><h2>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ulticort Plus 30 gm</h2><p>Apply a thin layer of this lotion once daily to cover only affected areas and rub in gently. If a bath or shower is taken prior to application, the skin should be dry before applying the lotion. The total dosage should not exceed approximately 50 g per week because of the potential for the drug to suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis. Do not use with occlusive dressings unless directed by a physician. Discontinue treatment when control is achieved. Avoid the application of this lotion on the face, groin, or in the axillae. This lotion is not for oral, ophthalmic, or intravaginal use.</p><h2>Dosage of Ulticort Plus 30 gm</h2><p>Apply a thin layer of this lotion once daily to cover only affected areas and rub in gently. If a bath or shower is taken prior to application, the skin should be dry before applying the lotion. The total dosage should not exceed approximately 50 g per week because of the potential for the drug to suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis. Do not use with occlusive dressings unless directed by a physician. Discontinue treatment when control is achieved. Avoid the application of this lotion on the face, groin, or in the axillae. This lotion is not for oral, ophthalmic, or intravaginal use.</p><h2>Contraindications</h2><p>This lotion is contraindicated in pregnancy.</p><h2>Side Effects of Ulticort Plus 30 gm</h2><p>The most common side effects are redness, itching, swelling, burning, stinging, application site pain, inflamed hair follicles (folliculitis), thinning of the skin (atrophy), peeling and rash.</p><h2>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h2><p>Based on data from animal reproduction studies, retinoid pharmacology, and the potential for systemic absorption, This Lotion may cause fetal harm when administered to a pregnant female and is contraindicated during pregnancy. Safety in pregnant females has not been established. There are no data on the presence of tazarotene, halobetasol propionate or its metabolites in human milk, the effects on the breastfed infant, or the effects on milk production after treatment with This Lotion.</p><h2>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h2><p>This lotion contains tazarotene, which is a teratogenic substance. In females of reproductive potential, obtain a negative pregnancy test within 2 weeks prior to initiating treatment and advise patients to use an effective method of contraception during treatment. Reversible hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis suppression may occur, with the potential for glucocorticosteroid insufficiency during or after treatment. Systemic effects of topical corticosteroids may also include Cushing’s syndrome, hyperglycemia, and glucosuria. Systemic absorption may require evaluation for HPA axis suppression. Use of potent corticosteroids on large areas, for prolonged durations, under occlusive dressings, or on an altered skin barrier may increase systemic exposure. Local Adverse Reactions: Local adverse reactions may include atrophy, striae, telangiectasias, and folliculitis. If these effects occur, discontinue at least until the integrity of the skin has been restored. DUOBRII Lotion should not be used on eczematous skin, as it may cause severe irritation. Photosensitivity and Risk for Sunburn: Avoid exposure to sunlight, sunlamps, and weather extremes. DUOBRII Lotion should be administered with caution if the patient is also taking drugs known to be photosensitizers. Ophthalmic Adverse Reactions: Use of topical corticosteroids may increase the risk of posterior subcapsular cataracts and glaucoma. If visual symptoms occur, consider referral to an ophthalmologist</p><h2>Storage Conditions</h2><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><h2>Use In Special Populations</h2><p>Safety and effectiveness of this lotion in pediatric patients under the age of 18 years have not been evaluated.</p><h2>Drug Classes</h2><p>Other Topical corticosteroids</p><h2>Mode Of Action</h2><p>Corticosteroids play a role in cellular signaling, immune function, inflammation, and protein regulation; however, the precise mechanism of action in plaque psoriasis is unknown. Tazarotene is a retinoid prodrug which is convert d to its active form, tazarotenic acid, the carboxylic acid of tazarotene, by deesterification. Tazarotenic acid binds to all three members of the retinoic acid receptor (RAR) family: RARα, RARβ and RARγ, but shows relative selectivity for RARβ, and RARγ and may modify gene expression. The clinical significance of these findings for the treatment of plaque psoriasis is unknown.A vasoconstrictor assay in healthy subjects with this lotion indicated that it is in the high to super-high range of potency as compared to other topical corticosteroids; however, similar blanching scores do not necessarily imply therapeutic equivalence. The potential for hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis suppression was evaluated in a study in adult subjects with moderate to severe plaque psoriasis. A median dose of 8.2 grams this lotion was applied once daily for 8 weeks and 20 subjects were assessed for HPA axis suppression at Weeks 4 and 8. HPA axis suppression was observed in 3 out of 20 (15%) subjects at Week 4. None of the 20 (0%) subjects had HPA axis suppression at Week 8. In this study, the criteria for HPA axis suppression was a serum cortisol level of less than or equal to 18 micrograms per deciliter 30 minutes after stimulation with cosyntropin (adrenocorticotropic hormone).</p><h2>Pregnancy</h2><p>Based on data from animal reproduction studies, retinoid pharmacology, and the potential for systemic absorption, This Lotion may cause fetal harm when administered to a pregnant female and is contraindicated during pregnancy. Safety in pregnant females has not been established. There are no data on the presence of tazarotene, halobetasol propionate or its metabolites in human milk, the effects on the breastfed infant, or the effects on milk production after treatment with This Lotion.</p><h2>Pediatric Uses</h2><p>Safety and effectiveness of this lotion in pediatric patients under the age of 18 years have not been evaluated.</p>', 'active', '6100_1744699404.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 12:18:30', '2025-04-15 10:43:24'),
(3269, 'Biotin', 'Biotin', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '1000 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Biotin 1000 mcg</strong></p><p> Biotin 1000 mcg is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Hair loss</li><li>Weak, brittle, splitting, or soft nails</li><li>Eczema &amp; dermatitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Herbal and Nutraceuticals</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Biotin 1000 mcg is an essential vitamin also known as vitamin H. It plays a role in vital physiological functions like fatty acid synthesis, amino acid catabolism, and gluconeogenesis. It acts as a cofactor in the enzymatic carboxylation of pyruvate and acetyl-CoA. Biotin 1000 mcg influences the growth and differentiation of epidermal cells and is crucial for the formation and renewal of the skin, hair, and nails. It functions as a coenzyme for mitochondrial carboxylases in hair roots, improving keratin structure. Biotin deficiency may result in hair loss and a variety of systemic symptoms such as dermatitis and aciduria. Biotin is one of the most prescribed nutritional supplements for hair loss.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Biotin 1000 mcg</strong></p><p> The dose of Biotin 1000 mcg varies depending on the disease condition and age group. Generally, 1-3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician.</p><p> In children, Biotin 1000 mcg deficiency, particularly between 1 week and 2 years of age, may result in seizures, hypotonia, developmental delay, ataxia, hyperventilation, and coma. In this case, 5-10 tablets daily, crushed and mixed with water or fruit juice, or as advised by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Biotin 1000 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>Anticonvulsant drugs may lower the plasma level of Biotin 1000 mcg.</li><li>Antibiotic use may decrease the Biotin 1000 mcg contribution made by the microflora of the large intestine.</li><li>Excessive consumption of raw egg whites interferes with Biotin 1000 mcg absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Sufficient clinical data is not available for use in pregnant women and lactating mothers.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1275_1742282447.jpg', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 12:20:47', '2025-03-18 12:20:47'),
(3270, 'Nim-A', 'Nimbadi Churna', 5.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><span style=\"background-color: rgb(245, 245, 245); color: rgb(63, 82, 100);\">This is indicated in skin diseases like Acne vulgaris, Eczema, Psoriasis and Boils.</span></p>', 'active', '1820_1742284483.jpg', 5, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 12:54:43', '2025-03-18 12:54:43'),
(3271, 'Fluracil', 'Fluorouracil', 75.00, 1, 'Injection', '250 mg vial', 'Injection', '25 mg per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Fluracil 25 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Fluorouracil is indicated alone or in combination for:</p><ul><li>Carcinoma of the colon or rectum</li><li>Carcinoma of the stomach and exocrine pancreas</li><li>Carcinoma of the liver</li><li>Carcinoma of the breast</li><li>Carcinoma of the bladder</li><li>Carcinoma of the lung</li><li>Epithelial ovarian carcinoma</li><li>Cervical carcinoma</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Fluorouracil is inactive as such in mammalian cells but is converted into the active 5-fluorodeoxyuridine monophosphate (FdUMP) by a variety of different metabolic pathways. The drug works by inhibiting the enzyme thymidylate kinase, which results in reduced formation of thymidine and thus of DNA. The active metabolite FdUMP appears to form a stable complex with the folate cofactor N-5, 10-methylene tetrahydrofolate which inactivates thymidylate kinase. Fluorouracil as FdUMP is also incorporated into RNA which results in fluorination of RNA. The effect of fluorouracil on living cells is limited mainly to those in the proliferative phase but while cells in the G2 and S phase are most affected, there may be effects at any stage of the cell cycle.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Fluracil 25 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Intravenous 5-fluorouracil can be delivered by rapid intravenous bolus injection or slow infusion. The vial contents can rapidly be injected directly into a peripheral vein, the commonest schedules being:</p><ul><li>12-13.5 mg/Kg (500 mg/m²) daily for 5 days, repeated at 4-weekly intervals.</li><li>Slow intravenous infusion requires the drug to be diluted in 500 ml of dextrose 5% solution, then infused over 2-3 hours on 5 successive days.</li></ul><p>For palliative management of cancer:</p><ul><li>Initial Dose: 12 mg/kg intravenously once daily for 4 successive days.</li><li>Maximum Dose: 800 mg/day.</li><li>If no toxicity is observed, 6 mg/kg may be administered on the 6th, 8th, 10th, and 12th day (No therapy is given on days 5, 7, 9, or 11). Discontinue at the end of day 12, even with no apparent toxicity.</li><li>Poor risk patients and those who are not in an adequate nutritional state: Initial Dose: 6 mg/kg/day for 3 days.</li><li>Maximum Dose: 400 mg/day.</li><li>If no toxicity is observed, 3 mg/kg may be administered on days 5, 7, and 9 (No therapy is to be administered on days 4, 6, or 8). Discontinue at the end of day 9, even with no apparent toxicity.</li><li>Maintenance therapy: In instances where toxicity has not been a problem, it is recommended that therapy be continued using either of the following schedules:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Repeat the dosage of the first course every 30 days after the last day of the previous course.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">When the toxic signs resulting from the initial course of therapy have subsided, administer a maintenance dose of 10 to 15 mg/kg/week as a single dose. Maximum Dose: 1g/week.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The reaction by the patient to the previous course of therapy should be taken into account, and the dosage should be adjusted accordingly.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Fluracil 25 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Pre-treatment with cimetidine for 4 weeks led to increased plasma concentrations of fluorouracil following intravenous and oral administration in 6 patients. The effect was probably due to a combination of hepatic enzyme inhibition and reduced hepatic blood flow.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> It is contraindicated in patients who are severely debilitated or in patients with bone marrow suppression due to either radiotherapy or chemotherapy. It is likewise contraindicated in pregnant or breastfeeding women. It should also be avoided in patients that do not have malignant illnesses.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Fluracil 25 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Potentially life-threatening effects: Severe effects from 5-fluorouracil are related to the dosage and duration of therapy.</p><ul><li><strong>Cardiac effects</strong>: Occasional case reports associating 5-fluorouracil therapy with ischemic cardiac events; evidence against the autoimmune phenomenon is the fact that in several cases, cardiotoxicity occurred within several hours of the first dose.</li><li><strong>Hematological effects</strong>: Potentially lethal effects caused by severe hematological toxicity may develop within the first 10 days of treatment being instituted but generally resolves within 3 weeks. At the recommended dose and schedule, it is rather uncommon for hematological toxicity to be severe.</li><li><strong>Neurological effects</strong>: Effects on the central nervous system have been occasionally reported, and cerebral ataxia is dose-dependent with an incidence of between 3.1 and 7%. Acute cerebellar syndromes and myelopathy have been described following intrathecal 5-fluorouracil.</li><li><strong>Other effects</strong>: Allergic reactions (including difficulty in breathing, closing of the throat, swelling of the lips, tongue, or face, or hives), decreased bone marrow function and blood problems (extreme fatigue, easy bruising or bleeding, black, bloody or tarry stools, or fever, chills, or signs of infection), hand-foot syndrome (tingling, pain, redness, swelling or tenderness of the hands and feet), severe vomiting, diarrhea, frequent bowel movements or watery stools, or sores in the mouth or throat, or stomach pain or heartburn, or black, bloody or tarry stools.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Fluorouracil is contraindicated throughout pregnancy. The literature pertaining to pregnancy and cytotoxic drugs is necessarily limited, but it appears in general that the risk of teratogenesis diminishes with the advancement of pregnancy. Therefore, most cytotoxic drugs are absolutely contraindicated in the first trimester, and 5-fluorouracil, used in the first trimester, has been reported to cause multiple congenital abnormalities. There are many case reports, however, of pregnancy being conducted successfully with combination chemotherapy being given to the mother during the second and third trimesters. It is not known whether fluorouracil is excreted in human milk. Because fluorouracil inhibits DNA, RNA, and protein synthesis, mothers should not nurse while receiving this drug.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> 5-fluorouracil is a highly toxic drug with a narrow margin of safety. Therefore, patients should be carefully supervised, since therapeutic response is unlikely to occur without some evidence of toxicity. The daily dose should not exceed 1 gram. Treatment should be discontinued promptly when one of the following signs of toxicity appears:</p><ul><li>Leucopenia (WBC under 3500/mm³)</li><li>Thrombocytopenia (platelet under 100,000/mm³)</li><li>Stomatitis (the first small ulceration at the inner margin of the lips is a signal for stopping treatment)</li><li>Severe diarrhea (frequent bowel movements and watery stools)</li><li>Gastro-intestinal ulceration and bleeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Fluracil 25 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Cases of deliberate overdose are unknown, but excessive toxicity due to the hematological effects as described above can occur. There is no specific antidote to 5-fluorouracil toxicity; treatment consists in supportive care. In addition to the hematological toxicity, other toxicities will occur with overdose. Signs and symptoms are qualitatively similar to the side effects. Treatment should be performed promptly, and appropriate drugs are given to control symptoms of overdose.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store the vial in its original carton not exceeding 25℃. Do not refrigerate. Protect from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neonates</strong>: No dosage recommendations are made for neonates.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in children have not been established.</li><li><strong>The elderly</strong>: No special precautions are required, doses being adjusted for the patient\'s weight and height.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p> 5-Fluorouracil is a cytotoxic anticancer drug, and caution should be exercised in handling 5-Fluorouracil. Special, trained personnel should reconstitute the drug. Preparation requires a room reserved for this purpose. The work surface should be covered with disposable plastic-backed absorbent paper. Smoking, eating, and drinking are prohibited in the room. The handling staff must have a set of appropriate equipment, particularly long-sleeved coats, protective masks, caps, protective goggles, sterile disposable gloves, worktop protection sheets, and waste collection containers and bags. If 5-Fluorouracil solution contacts the skin, wash the skin immediately and thoroughly with soap and water. Following topical exposure, events have included tingling, burning, and redness. If 5-Fluorouracil contacts mucous membranes, the membranes should be flushed thoroughly with water. Pregnant women must be warned and avoid handling cytotoxic agents. All broken containers must be treated with the same precautions and regarded as contaminated waste. Contaminated waste is to be disposed of by incineration in rigid containers labeled for this purpose or must be destroyed as per the government rules.</p>', 'active', '1621_1742285022.jpg', 9, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:03:42', '2025-03-18 13:03:42');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3272, 'Sefril', 'Cephradine', 8.03, 4, 'Capsule', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sefril 250 mg</strong></p><p>Sefril 250 mg is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. These include:</p><ul><li><strong>Undesirable Upper respiratory tract infections</strong>: sinusitis, pharyngitis, tonsillitis, laryngo-tracheo bronchitis, and otitis media.</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong>: bronchitis (acute and chronic), lobar pneumonia, and bronchopneumonia.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: cystitis, urethritis, and pyelonephritis.</li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: abscess, cellulitis, furunculosis, and impetigo.</li></ul><p>The following microorganisms are susceptible, in vitro to Sefril 250 mg:</p><ul><li><strong>Gram-positive</strong>: Staphylococci (both penicillin sensitive and resistant strains and penicillinase-producing species), Streptococci, Streptococci pyogenes (beta haemolytic), Streptococcus pneumonia.</li><li><strong>Gram-negative</strong>: Escherichia coli, Klebsiella spp, Proteus mirabilis, Haemophilus influenza, Shigella spp, Salmonella spp (including Salmonella typhi), Neisseria spp.</li></ul><p>Many strains of E.coli and Staphylococcus aureus that produce the enzyme penicillinase and are ampicillin-resistant are susceptible to Sefril 250 mg, which is unaffected by this enzyme.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>First generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Sefril 250 mg is a cephalosporin antibiotic with broad spectrum bactericidal activity against both gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria. Sefril 250 mg interferes with the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall by inhibiting the transpeptidase enzyme. As a result, the bacterial cell wall is weakened, the cell swells, and then ruptures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sefril 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: For respiratory tract infections (other than lobar pneumonia) and skin and skin structure infections, the usual dose is 250 mg every 6 hours or 500 mg every 12 hours. For lobar pneumonia, the usual dose is 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 g every 12 hours. For uncomplicated urinary tract infections, the usual dose is 500 mg every 12 hours. In more serious urinary tract infections, including prostatitis, 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 g every 12 hours may be administered.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: The usual dose in children over nine months of age is 25 to 50 mg/kg/day administered in equally divided doses every 6 or 12 hours. For otitis media due to H. influenzae, doses are from 75 to 100 mg/kg/day administered in equally divided doses every 6 or 12 hours, but should not exceed 4 g per day.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Sefril 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For oral administration</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Urinary tract infections: 500 mg four times daily or 1 g twice daily. Infections that are severe or chronic may necessitate the administration of higher doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Respiratory tract infections: 250 to 500 mg four times daily or 500 mg to 1 g twice daily, depending on the site and severity of the infection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin and soft tissue infections: 250 to 500 mg four times daily or 500 mg to 1 g twice daily, depending on the site and severity of the infection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children</strong>: Total daily dose of 25 to 50 mg/kg given in two or four equally divided doses. Otitis media: Total daily dose of 75 to 100 mg/kg given in divided doses every 6 or 12 hours. Maximum daily dosage: 4 gm.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly</strong>: The normal adult dose is appropriate. Patients with impaired renal or hepatic function should be monitored during treatment.</li><li><strong>For injectable administration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adult</strong>: The usual dose is 2-4 gm daily in four equally divided doses, up to 8 gm daily. For prophylaxis, a single preoperative dose of 1-2 gm intramuscularly or intravenously is given.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children</strong>: The dose is 50-100 mg/kg daily in four equally divided doses, up to 300 mg/kg daily in severe infection.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sefril 250 mg</strong></p><p>The concomitant use of nephrotoxic drugs such as aminoglycosides with Cefradine may increase the risk of kidney damage. Diuretics (e.g. frusemide, ethacrynic acid) and probenecid enhance the possibility of renal toxicity.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Sefril 250 mg should not be used in patients with known or suspected hypersensitivity to cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sefril 250 mg</strong></p><p>Limited essentially to gastro-intestinal disturbances and, on occasion, hypersensitivity phenomena. These are more likely to occur in individuals who have previously demonstrated hypersensitivity and those with a history of allergy, asthma, hay fever, or urticaria. Skin reactions have occasionally been reported. Rare side effects include:</p><ul><li>Glossitis, heartburn, dizziness, tightness in the chest, nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, abdominal pain, vaginitis, candida overgrowth.</li><li>Skin and hypersensitivity reactions: urticaria, skin rashes, joint pains, oedema.</li><li>Blood and lymphatic system disorders: blood disorders (e.g., thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, agranulocytosis, aplastic anaemia, haemolytic anaemia).</li><li>Immune system disorders: fever, serum sickness-like reactions, anaphylaxis.</li><li>Psychiatric disorders: confusion, sleep disturbances.</li><li>Nervous system disorders: hyperactivity, hypertonia, dizziness, nervousness; rarely, headache.</li><li>Hepatobiliary disorders: liver enzyme disturbances, transient hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice.</li><li>Renal and urinary disorders: reversible interstitial nephritis.</li><li>Investigations: Elevation of blood urea nitrogen, serum creatinine, alanine aminotransferase, aspartate aminotransferase, total bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>US FDA Pregnancy Category of Cefradine is B. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Cefradine has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Cefradine is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Prolonged use of an anti-infective may result in the development of superinfection due to the emergence of resistant organisms. Sefril 250 mg should be administered with care to patients hypersensitive to penicillins because of the risk of cross-sensitivity between beta-lactam antibiotics. Cephalosporin antibiotics may cause a positive result in Coombs’ testing. When Coombs testing is performed on neonates whose mothers received cephalosporins prior to labour, it should be noted that a positive result may be due to the drug. Sefril 250 mg may cause a false positive urine glucose result when Benedict’s or Fehling’s solutions or tablets such as Clinitest are used in the testing. This does not occur with enzyme-based tests (e.g., Clinistix, Diastix). Dosage adjustment is necessary in renal impairment. This product contains lactose. Patients with rare hereditary problems of galactose intolerance, Lapp lactase deficiency, or glucose-galactose malabsorption should not take this medicine.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sefril 250 mg</strong></p><p>The symptoms of Sefril overdose are non-specific and generally include nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, and gastric upsets. Treatment is mainly supportive, although gastric lavage may be necessary if a large amount has been ingested.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Sefril 250 mg Suspension should be freshly prepared. Reconstituted Suspension should be used within 7 days if kept at room temperature or within 14 days if kept in a refrigerator. Sefril 250 mg Injection solutions should be used within 2 hours when kept at room temperature. When stored at 5°C, solutions retain potency for 12 hours. Reconstituted solutions may vary in colour from light to straw yellow; however, this does not affect the potency. Do not use later than the date of expiry. Keep all medicines out of the reach of children. To be dispensed only on the prescription of a registered physician.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>First generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Sefril 250 mg is a semisynthetic broad-spectrum bactericidal antibiotic. It is active against infections caused by both gram-positive and gram-negative microorganisms. Both penicillinase-producing and non-producing staphylococci are sensitive to Sefril 250 mg. The main site of action of Sefril 250 mg is the cell wall of bacteria. The cell wall of sensitive organisms contains peptidoglycan. Sefril 250 mg inhibits the cross-linking process, and as a result, cell walls with many pores are formed, leading to bacterial lysis due to external osmotic pressure.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Although animal studies have not demonstrated any teratogenicity, safety in pregnancy has not been established. Sefril 250 mg is excreted in breast milk and should be used with caution in lactating mothers. Since the medicine may cause dizziness, patients should be cautioned about operating hazardous machinery, including automobiles.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: The following doses are recommended for patients not on haemodialysis:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl &gt;20 ml/min: 500 mg every 6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 5-20 ml/min: 250 mg every 6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl &lt;5 ml/min: 250 mg every 50-70 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For patients on chronic, intermittent haemod</li></ul>', 'active', '1470_1742285217.jpg', 5, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:06:57', '2025-03-18 13:06:57'),
(3273, 'Imanix', 'Imatinib Mesylate', 100.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Imanix 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Newly diagnosed adult and pediatric patients with Philadelphia chromosome positive chronic myeloid leukemia (Ph+CML) in chronic phase.</li><li>Patients with Philadelphia chromosome positive chronic myeloid leukemia in blast crisis, accelerated phase, or in chronic phase after failure of interferon-alpha therapy.</li><li>Newly diagnosed adult and pediatric patients with Philadelphia chromosome positive chronic myeloid leukemia (Ph+CML) in chronic phase.</li><li>Adult patients with relapsed or refractory Philadelphia chromosome positive acute lymphoblastic leukemia (Ph+ ALL).</li><li>Pediatric patients with newly diagnosed Philadelphia chromosome positive acute lymphoblastic leukemia (Ph+ ALL) in combination with chemotherapy.</li><li>Adult patients with myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative diseases associated with platelet-derived growth factor receptor (PDGFR) gene re-arrangements.</li><li>Adult patients with aggressive systemic mastocytosis without the D816V c-Kit mutation or with c-Kit mutational status unknown.</li><li>Adult patients with hypereosinophilic syndrome and/or chronic eosinophilic leukemia who have the FIP1L1-PDGFRa fusion kinase (mutational analysis or fluorescence in situ hybridization [FISH] demonstration of CHIC2 allele deletion) and for patients with HES and/or CEL who are FIP1L1-PDGFRa fusion kinase negative or unknown.</li><li>Adult patients with unresectable, recurrent and/or metastatic dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans.</li><li>Patients with Kit (CD117) positive unresectable and/or metastatic malignant gastrointestinal stromal tumors.</li><li>Adjuvant treatment of adult patients following complete gross resection of Kit (CD117) positive GIST.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Targeted Cancer Therapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Imatinib is a tyrosine kinase inhibitor that inhibits the BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase created by the Philadelphia chromosome abnormality in chronic myeloid leukaemia (CML). It blocks proliferation and induces apoptosis in BCR-ABL positive cell lines, as well as fresh leukaemic cells from Philadelphia chromosome positive CML. Imatinib also inhibits receptor kinases for platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF) and stem cell factor (SCF), c-kit, PDGF- and SCF-mediated cellular events.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Imanix 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Adults with Ph+ CML CP: 400 mg/day</li><li>Adults with Ph+ CML AP or BC: 600 mg/day</li><li>Pediatrics with Ph+ CML CP: 340 mg/m²/day</li><li>Adults with Ph+ ALL: 600 mg/day</li><li>Pediatrics with Ph+ ALL: 340 mg/m²/day</li><li>Adults with MDS/MPD: 400 mg/day</li><li>Adults with ASM: 100 mg/day or 400 mg/day</li><li>Adults with HES/CEL: 100 mg/day or 400 mg/day</li><li>Adults with DFSP: 800 mg/day</li><li>Adults with metastatic and/or unresectable GIST: 400 mg/day</li><li>Adjuvant treatment of adults with GIST: 400 mg/day</li><li>Patients with mild to moderate hepatic impairment: 400 mg/day</li><li>Patients with severe hepatic impairment: 300 mg/day</li></ul><p>All doses of Imatinib should be taken with a meal and a large glass of water. Doses of 400 mg or 600 mg should be administered once daily, whereas a dose of 800 mg should be administered as 400 mg twice a day. Imatinib can be dissolved in water or apple juice for patients having difficulty swallowing. Daily dosing of 800 mg and above should be accomplished using the 400-mg tablet to reduce exposure to iron.</p><p><strong>Administration of Imanix 100 mg</strong></p><p> Should be taken with food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Imanix 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Agents Inducing CYP3A Metabolism:</strong> Concomitant administration of Imatinib and strong CYP3A4 inducers may reduce total exposure of imatinib; consider alternative agents.</li><li><strong>Agents Inhibiting CYP3A Metabolism:</strong> Concomitant administration of Imatinib and strong CYP3A4 inhibitors may result in a significant imatinib exposure increase. Grapefruit juice may also increase plasma concentrations of imatinib; avoid grapefruit juice.</li><li><strong>Interactions with Drugs Metabolized by CYP3A4:</strong> Imatinib will increase plasma concentration of CYP3A4 metabolized drugs (e.g., triazolo-benzodiazepines, dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, certain HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors, etc.). Use caution when administering Imatinib with CYP3A4 substrates that have a narrow therapeutic window.</li><li><strong>Interactions with Drugs Metabolized by CYP2D6:</strong> Use caution when administering Imatinib with CYP2D6 substrates that have a narrow therapeutic window.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity</li><li>Lactation</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Imanix 100 mg</strong></p><p> The following serious adverse reactions are described elsewhere in the labeling:</p><ul><li>Fluid Retention and Edema</li><li>Hematologic Toxicity</li><li>Congestive Heart Failure and Left Ventricular Dysfunction</li><li>Hepatotoxicity</li><li>Hemorrhage</li><li>Gastrointestinal Disorders</li><li>Hypereosinophilic Cardiac Toxicity</li><li>Dermatologic Toxicities</li><li>Hypothyroidism</li><li>Growth Retardation in Children and Adolescents</li><li>Tumor Lysis Syndrome</li><li>Impairments Related to Driving and Using Machinery</li><li>Renal Toxicity</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category D:</strong> There is positive evidence of human fetal risk, but the benefits from use in pregnant women may be acceptable despite the risk (e.g., if the drug is needed in a life-threatening situation or for a serious disease for which safer drugs cannot be used or are ineffective).</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Edema and severe fluid retention have occurred. Weigh patients regularly and manage unexpected rapid weight gain by drug interruption and diuretics.</li><li>Cytopenias, particularly anemia, neutropenia, and thrombocytopenia, have occurred. Manage with dose reduction, dose interruption, or discontinuation of treatment.</li><li>Severe congestive heart failure and left ventricular dysfunction have been reported, particularly in patients with comorbidities and risk factors. Monitor and treat patients with cardiac disease or risk factors for cardiac failure.</li><li>Severe hepatotoxicity, including fatalities, may occur. Assess liver function before initiation of treatment and monthly thereafter or as clinically indicated.</li><li>Grade 3/4 hemorrhage has been reported in clinical studies in patients with newly diagnosed CML and with GIST. Gastrointestinal tumor sites may be the source of gastrointestinal bleeds in GIST.</li><li>Gastrointestinal (GI) perforations, some fatal, have been reported.</li><li>Cardiogenic shock/left ventricular dysfunction has been associated with the initiation of Imatinib in patients with conditions associated with high eosinophil levels (e.g., HES, MDS/MPD, and ASM).</li><li>Bullous dermatologic reactions (e.g., erythema multiforme and Stevens-Johnson syndrome) have been reported with the use of Imatinib.</li><li>Hypothyroidism has been reported in thyroidectomy patients undergoing levothyroxine replacement. Closely monitor TSH levels in such patients.</li><li>Fetal harm can occur when administered to a pregnant woman. Apprise women of the potential harm to the fetus, and to avoid pregnancy when taking Imatinib.</li><li>Growth retardation occurring in children and pre-adolescents receiving Imatinib has been reported. Close monitoring of growth in children under Imatinib treatment is recommended.</li><li>Tumor Lysis Syndrome: Close monitoring is recommended.</li><li>Reports of motor vehicle accidents have been received in patients receiving Imatinib. Caution patients about driving a car or operating machinery.</li><li>Renal Toxicity: A decline in renal function may occur in patients receiving Imatinib. Evaluate renal function at baseline and during therapy, with attention to risk factors for renal dysfunction.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Imanix 100 mg</strong></p><p> Experience with doses higher than the recommended therapeutic dose is limited. In the event of overdose, the patient should be observed, and appropriate symptomatic treatment given. Generally, the reported outcome in these cases was \"improved\" or \"recovered.\" Events that have been reported at different dose ranges are as follows:</p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adult Population:</strong>1200 to 1600 mg (duration varying between 1 to 10 days): Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, rash, erythema, edema, swelling, fatigue, muscle spasms, thrombocytopenia, pancytopenia, abdominal pain, headache, decreased appetite.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1800 to 3200 mg (as high as 3200 mg daily for 6 days): Weakness, myalgia, increased creatine phosphokinase, increased bilirubin, gastrointestinal pain.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6400 mg (single dose): One case reported in the literature of one patient who experienced nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, pyrexia, facial swelling, decreased neutrophil count, and increased transaminases.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">8 to 10 g (single dose): Vomiting and gastrointestinal pain have been reported.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric population:</strong>One 3-year-old male exposed to a single dose: 400 mg experienced vomiting, diarrhea, and anorexia.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Another 3-year-old male exposed to a single dose: 980 mg experienced decreased white blood cell count and diarrhea.</li></ul><p>In the event of overdose, the patient should be observed and appropriate supportive treatment given.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C, in a cool and dry place. Keep away from light. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Patients on potent CYP3A4 inducers:</strong> Increase dose by 50% and carefully monitor clinical response.</li><li><strong>Severe Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Reduce dose by 25%.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Targeted Cancer Therapy, Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Imatinib is a small molecule protein-tyrosine kinase inhibitor that potently inhibits the activity of the Bcr-Abl tyrosine kinase (TK), as well as several receptor TKs: Kit, the receptor for stem cell factor (SCF) coded for by the c-Kit proto-oncogene, the discoidin domain receptors (DDR1 and DDR2), the colony stimulating factor receptor (CSF-1R), and the platelet-derived growth factor receptors alpha and beta (PDGFR-alpha and PDGFR-beta). Imatinib can also inhibit cellular events mediated by activation of these receptor kinases.</p><p><strong>Absorption and Distribution:</strong></p><p> Imatinib is well absorbed after oral administration with Cmax achieved within 2-4 hours post-dose. Mean absolute bioavailability is 98%. Mean Imatinib AUC increases proportionally with increasing doses ranging from 25 mg to 1,000 mg. There is no significant change in the pharmacokinetics of Imatinib on repeated dosing, and accumulation is 1.5- to 2.5-fold at steady state when Imatinib is dosed once-daily. At clinically relevant concentrations of Imatinib, binding to plasma proteins in in vitro experiments is approximately 95%, mostly to albumin and 1-acid glycoprotein.</p><p><strong>Metabolism:</strong></p><p> CYP3A4 is the major enzyme responsible for metabolism of Imatinib. Other cytochrome P450 enzymes, such as CYP1A2, CYP2D6, CYP2C9, and CYP2C19, play a minor role in its metabolism. The main circulating active metabolite in humans is the N-demethylated piperazine derivative, formed predominantly by CYP3A4. It shows in vitro potency similar to the parent Imatinib. The plasma AUC for this metabolite is about 15% of the AUC for Imatinib. The plasma protein binding of N-demethylated metabolite CGP74588 is similar to that of the parent compound.</p><p><strong>Excretion:</strong></p><p> Imatinib elimination is predominately in the feces, mostly as metabolites. Based on the recovery of compound(s) after an oral 14C-labeled dose of Imatinib, approximately 81% of the dose was eliminated within 7 days, in feces (68% of dose) and urine (13% of dose). Unchanged Imatinib accounted for 25% of the dose (5% urine, 20% feces), the remainder being metabolites. Following oral administration in healthy volunteers, the elimination half-lives of Imatinib and its major active metabolite, the N-demethyl derivative (CGP74588), are approximately 18 and 40 hours, respectively.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Women of childbearing potential must be advised to use effective contraception during treatment and for at least 15 days after stopping treatment with Imatinib.</p><p> <strong>Pregnancy:</strong> There are limited data on the use of imatinib in pregnant women. There have been post-marketing reports of spontaneous abortions and infant congenital anomalies from women who have taken Imatinib. Studies in animals have, however, shown reproductive toxicity, and the potential risk for the fetus is unknown. Imatinib should not be used during pregnancy unless clearly necessary. If it is used during pregnancy, the patient must be informed of the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p> <strong>Breast-feeding:</strong> There is limited information on imatinib distribution in human milk. Studies in two breastfeeding women revealed that both imatinib and its active metabolite can be distributed into human milk. The milk plasma ratio studied in a single patient was determined to be 0.5 for imatinib and 0.9 for the metabolite, suggesting greater distribution of the metabolite into the milk. Considering the combined concentration of imatinib and the metabolite and the maximum daily milk intake by infants, the total exposure would be expected to be low (-10% of a therapeutic dose). However, since the effects of low-dose exposure of the infant to imatinib are unknown, women should not breast-feed during treatment and for at least 15 days after stopping treatment with Imatinib.</p><p> <strong>Fertility:</strong> In non-clinical studies, the fertility of male and female rats was not affected, although effects on reproductive parameters were observed. Studies on patients receiving Imatinib and its effect on fertility and gametogenesis have not been performed. Patients concerned about their fertility on Imatinib treatment should consult with their physician.</p>', 'active', '1202_1742285499.jpg', 55, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:11:39', '2025-03-18 13:11:39'),
(3274, 'Maxnor', 'Norepinephrine Tartrate', 480.00, 1, 'IV Infusion', '4 ml ampoule', 'IV Infusion', '4 mg per 4 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Maxnor 4 mg/4 ml</strong></p><p> For blood pressure control in certain acute hypotensive states (e.g., pheochromocytomectomy, sympathectomy, poliomyelitis, spinal anesthesia, myocardial infarction, septicemia, blood transfusion, and drug reactions). As an adjunct in the treatment of cardiac arrest and profound hypotension.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Alpha and Beta-adrenergic agonist</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Maxnor 4 mg/4 ml is a direct-acting sympathomimetic which stimulates β1- and α-adrenergic receptors. Its α-agonist effects cause vasoconstriction, thereby raising systolic and diastolic BP with reflex slowing of heart rate.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Maxnor 4 mg/4 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Upper gastrointestinal haemorrhage:</strong></li><li> Adult: Intraperitoneal admin: 8 mg in 250 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride inj. Alternatively, instill 8 mg in 100 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride solution through a nasogastric tube every hr for 6–8 hr, then every 2 hr for 4–6 hr. Withdraw drug gradually.</li><li><strong>Acute hypotensive states:</strong></li><li> Adult: Initially, 8-12 mcg/minute, up to 8-30 mcg/minute in refractory shock. Infuse using a solution of 4 mcg/ml in glucose 5%, or sodium chloride 0.9% and glucose 5% at a rate of 2-3 ml/minute. Adjust according to BP response. Average maintenance dose: 0.5-1 ml/minute (2-4 mcg/minute). Infuse via a central venous catheter or into a large vein.</li><li> Child: Administer at a rate of 2 mcg/minute. Alternatively, 2 mcg/m²/minute. Adjust rate according to BP response and perfusion.</li><li> Elderly: Initial dose should be at low end of dose range.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Maxnor 4 mg/4 ml</strong></p><p> Guanethidine, methyldopa, reserpine, TCAs may increase pressor response to Maxnor 4 mg/4 ml.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypertension, pregnancy, and patients with peripheral or mesenteric vascular thrombosis unless necessary as a life-saving procedure.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Maxnor 4 mg/4 ml</strong></p><p> Hypertension, headache, peripheral ischemia, bradycardia, arrhythmias, anxiety, skin necrosis (with extravasation), dyspnea, respiratory difficulty.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. Either studies in animals have revealed adverse effects on the fetus (teratogenic or embryocidal or other) and there are no controlled studies in women or studies in women and animals are not available. Drugs should be given only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Not a substitute for replacement of blood, plasma, fluids, and/or electrolytes; correct volume depletion prior to admin.</li><li>Identify and correct hypoxia, hypercapnia, and acidosis prior to or during admin.</li><li>Avoid extravasation as tissue necrosis may occur.</li><li>Avoid injection into leg veins, especially in elderly or those with occlusive vascular diseases, arteriosclerosis, diabetes, or Buerger\'s disease.</li><li>Hypertensive or hyperthyroid patients.</li><li>In conjunction with local anesthetics, do not use in fingers, toes, ears, nose, or genitalia.</li><li>Lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Maxnor 4 mg/4 ml</strong></p><p> Symptoms: Hypertension, sweating, cerebral hemorrhage, convulsions.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at room temperature (25°C) and protect from light.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p> Intravenous: Dilute with 5% glucose inj, with or without sodium chloride; dilution with sodium chloride inj alone is not recommended.</p>', 'active', '994_1742285851.jpg', 1, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:17:31', '2025-03-18 13:17:31'),
(3275, 'Movilax', 'Polyethylene Glycol 3350 plus Electrolytes', 35.00, 10, 'Packs', '10 Packs', 'Oral Powder', '7.5 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Movilax</strong></p><p> For use in adults and children over 12 years of age for effective relief from constipation and treatment of chronic constipation. Also effective in resolving fecal impaction, defined as refractory constipation with fecal loading of the rectum and colon.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p> Each 25 ml concentrated oral solution contains:</p><ul><li>Polyethylene Glycol 3350: 13.125 gm</li><li>Sodium Chloride: 350.7 mg</li><li>Sodium Bicarbonate: 178.5 mg</li><li>Potassium Chloride: 46.6 mg</li></ul><p>Each sachet (Powder for Suspension) contains:</p><ul><li>Polyethylene Glycol 3350: 13.125 gm</li><li>Sodium chloride: 350.7 mg</li><li>Sodium Bicarbonate: 178.5 mg</li><li>Potassium Chloride BP: 46.6 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Osmotic purgatives</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Polyethylene Glycol 3350 exerts an osmotic action in the gut, which induces a laxative effect. Polyethylene Glycol 3350 increases the stool volume, which triggers colon motility via neuromuscular pathways. Electrolytes combined with Polyethylene Glycol 3350 are exchanged across the intestinal barrier (mucosa) with serum electrolytes and excreted in fecal water without net gain or loss of sodium, potassium, and water.</p><ul><li>Sodium chloride is the major extracellular cation, important in electrolyte and fluid balance, osmotic pressure control, and water distribution.</li><li>Sodium bicarbonate raises blood and urinary pH by dissociation to provide bicarbonate ions, neutralizing hydrogen ion concentration and gastric acid.</li><li>Potassium chloride plays an active role in nerve impulse conduction in the heart, brain, and skeletal muscle; muscle contraction; maintenance of normal renal function, acid-base balance, carbohydrate metabolism, and gastric secretion.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Movilax</strong></p><p> For adults and children aged 12 years and above:</p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Powder for SuspensionConstipation:</strong> The dose is 1 sachet daily. This may be increased to 2-3 sachets daily, if required.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Fecal Impaction:</strong> 8 sachets daily, consumed within 6 hours. A course of treatment for fecal impaction does not normally exceed 3 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Patients with renal insufficiency:</strong> No dosage change is necessary for treatment of either constipation or fecal impaction.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Direction of use:</strong> Each sachet should be dissolved in 125 ml of water. For fecal impaction, 8 sachets may be dissolved in 1 liter of water. Store the solution refrigerated and discard any solution not used within 6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Oral SolutionConstipation:</strong> 25 ml of Polyethylene Glycol 3350 and electrolytes oral solution added to 100 ml of water once daily (to make a total volume of 125 ml). This may be increased to 2-3 doses of 25 ml daily (each 25 ml dose added to 100 ml of water), if required according to individual response.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Fecal Impaction:</strong> 8 doses of 25 ml daily (each 25 ml dose added to 100 ml of water). A course of treatment for fecal impaction does not normally exceed 3 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Direction of use:</strong> 25 ml of oral solution added to 100 ml of water (to make a total volume of 125 ml). Any unused diluted solution should be discarded within 24 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Movilax</strong></p><p> There is a possibility that the absorption of other medicinal products could be transiently reduced during use with this preparation. There have been isolated reports of decreased efficacy with some concomitantly administered medicinal products, e.g. anti-epileptics.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Intestinal perforation or obstruction due to structural or functional disorder of the gut wall, ileus, severe inflammatory conditions of the intestinal tract, such as Crohn\'s disease and ulcerative colitis and toxic megacolon. Hypersensitivity to the active substances.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Movilax</strong></p><p> Generally well tolerated. However, side effects like allergic reactions, electrolyte disturbances particularly hyperkalemia and hypokalemia, abdominal pain, diarrhea, headache, and peripheral edema may appear.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in Pregnancy:</strong> There is no experience of the use of Polyethylene Glycol 3350 during pregnancy and lactation. It should only be used if considered essential by the physician.</li><li><strong>Use in Lactation:</strong> It is unknown whether Polyethylene Glycol 3350 is excreted in human breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Patients with impaired cardiovascular function:</strong> For the treatment of fecal impaction, the dose should be divided so that no more than two sachets are taken in any one hour.</li><li>If patients develop any symptoms indicating shifts of fluid/electrolytes (e.g., edema, shortness of breath, increasing fatigue, dehydration, cardiac failure), Movilax should be stopped immediately and any abnormality should be treated appropriately.</li><li>Prolonged use with all laxatives is undesirable and may lead to dependence.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Movilax</strong></p><p> Severe pain or distention can be treated by nasogastric aspiration. Extensive fluid loss by diarrhea or vomiting may require correction of electrolyte disturbances.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C and in a place protected from light. Do not refrigerate.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Osmotic purgatives</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Macrogol, also known as polyethylene glycol, exerts an osmotic action in the gut, which induces a laxative effect. Macrogol increases stool volume, which triggers colon motility via neuromuscular pathways. The physiological consequence is an improved propulsive colonic transportation of the softened stools and a facilitation of defecation. Electrolytes combined with Macrogol are exchanged across the intestinal barrier (mucosa) with serum electrolytes and excreted without net gain or loss of sodium, potassium, and water. Macrogol is unchanged along the gut and is virtually unabsorbed from the gastrointestinal tract.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Clinically, no effects during pregnancy are anticipated, since systemic exposure to Macrogol (3350) is negligible. This oral solution can be used during pregnancy. No effects on the breastfed newborn/infant are anticipated since the systemic exposure of the breastfeeding woman to Macrogol (3350) is negligible. This oral solution can be used during breastfeeding.</p>', 'active', '1488_1742286536.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:28:56', '2025-03-18 13:28:56'),
(3276, 'Olmepres', 'Olmesartan Medoxomil', 17.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Olmepres 40 mg</strong></p><p> Olmepres 40 mg is indicated for the treatment of hypertension. It may be used alone or in combination with other antihypertensive agents.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Angiotensin-II receptor blocker</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Olmesartan medoxomil is a potent, orally active, selective angiotensin II receptor (type AT1) antagonist. It is expected to block all actions of angiotensin II mediated by the AT1 receptor, regardless of the source or route of synthesis of angiotensin II. The selective antagonism of the angiotensin II (AT1) receptors results in increases in plasma renin levels and angiotensin I and II concentrations, and some decrease in plasma aldosterone concentrations. Angiotensin II is the primary vasoactive hormone of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) and plays a significant role in the pathophysiology of hypertension via the type 1 (AT1) receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Olmepres 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult:</strong> Dosage must be individualized. The usual initial dose is 10 mg once daily. In patients whose blood pressure is not adequately controlled at this dose, the dose may be increased to 20 mg once daily as the optimal dose. For patients requiring further reduction in blood pressure after 2 weeks of therapy, the dose of Olmesartan may be increased to 40 mg. Doses above 40 mg do not appear to have greater effect.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Of the total number of hypertensive patients receiving Olmepres 40 mg in clinical studies, more than 20% were 65 years of age and over, while more than 5% were 75 years of age and older. No overall differences in effectiveness or safety were observed between elderly patients and younger patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Olmepres 40 mg</strong></p><p> Dosage must be individualized. The usual recommended starting dose of Olmesartan is 20 mg once daily when used as monotherapy in patients who are not volume-contracted. For patients requiring further reduction in blood pressure after 2 weeks of therapy, the dose of Olmesartan may be increased to 40 mg. Doses above 40 mg do not appear to have a greater effect. Twice-daily dosing offers no advantage over the same total dose given once daily. No initial dosage adjustment is recommended for elderly patients, for patients with moderate to marked renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;40 ml/min) or with moderate to marked hepatic dysfunction. For patients with possible depletion of intravascular volume (e.g. patients treated with diuretics, particularly those with impaired renal function), Olmesartan should be initiated under close medical supervision and consideration should be given to use of a lower starting dose. Olmesartan may be administered with or without food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Olmepres 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With medicine:</strong> No significant drug interactions were reported in which Olmesartan was co-administered.</li><li><strong>With food &amp; others:</strong> Food does not affect the bioavailability of Olmesartan.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Olmesartan is contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to any component of this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Olmepres 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> The most common side effects include back pain, bronchitis, creatine phosphokinase increased, diarrhea, headache, hematuria, hyperglycemia, hypertriglyceridemia, influenza-like symptoms, pharyngitis, rhinitis, and sinusitis.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Chest pain, peripheral edema, arthritis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Categories C (first trimester) and D (second and third trimesters).</strong></li><li><strong>Nursing Mothers:</strong> It is not known whether Olmesartan is excreted in human milk, but Olmesartan is secreted at low concentration in the milk of lactating rats. Because of the potential for adverse effects on the nursing infant, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> As a consequence of inhibiting the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, changes in renal function may be anticipated in susceptible individuals treated with Olmepres 40 mg. In patients whose renal function may depend upon the activity of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (e.g. patients with severe congestive heart failure), treatment with angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors and angiotensin receptor antagonists has been associated with oliguria and/or progressive azotemia and (rarely) with acute renal failure and/or death. Similar results may be anticipated in patients treated with Olmepres 40 mg.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Olmepres 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms:</strong> There is no experience of overdose with Olmesartan. The most likely effects of Olmepres 40 mg overdosage are hypotension and tachycardia; bradycardia could be encountered if parasympathetic (vagal) stimulation occurred.</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> If intake is recent, gastric lavage or induction of emesis may be considered. Clinically significant hypotension due to an overdose of Olmesartan requires the active support of the cardiovascular system, including close monitoring of heart and lung function, the elevation of the extremities, and attention to circulating fluid volume and urine output.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool &amp; dry place below 30ºC, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Dose should not exceed 20 mg daily in moderate impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Max. 20 mg daily if eGFR 20–60 mL/minute/1.73 m². Avoid if eGFR less than 20 mL/minute/1.73 m².</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Angiotensin-II receptor blocker</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Angiotensin-II formed from angiotensin-I in a reaction catalyzed by angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE), is a potent vasoconstrictor, the primary vasoactive hormone of the renin-angiotensin system and an important component in the pathophysiology of hypertension. It also stimulates aldosterone secretion by the adrenal cortex. Olmesartan blocks the vasoconstrictor and aldosterone-secreting effects of angiotensin-II by selectively blocking the binding of angiotensin-II to the AT1 receptor found in many tissues (e.g., vascular smooth muscle, adrenal gland). In-vitro binding studies indicate that Olmesartan is a reversible &amp; competitive inhibitor of AT1 receptor. Olmesartan does not inhibit ACE (kinase-I, the enzyme that converts angiotensin-I to angiotensin-II and degrades bradykinin).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> When pregnancy is detected, discontinue this product as soon as possible. When used in pregnancy during the second and third trimesters, drugs that act directly on the renin-angiotensin system can cause injury and even death to the developing fetus.</p><p><strong>Nursing Mothers</strong></p><p> It is not known whether Olmesartan is excreted in human milk, but Olmesartan is secreted at low concentration in the milk of lactating rats. Because of the potential for adverse effects on the nursing infant, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p>', 'active', '1994_1742286796.jpg', 37, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:33:16', '2025-03-18 13:33:16'),
(3277, 'Rosu', 'Rosuvastatin Calcium', 50.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rosu 40 mg</strong></p><p>Rosu 40 mg is indicated in:</p><ul><li><strong>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial and Non-familial)</li><li><strong>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia</strong> (Familial)</li><li><strong>Mixed Dyslipidemia</strong> (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li><strong>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs</li><li>Statins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Rosu 40 mg is a selective and competitive inhibitor of <strong>HMG-CoA reductase</strong>, the rate-limiting enzyme that converts 3-hydroxy-3-methyl glutaryl coenzyme A to mevalonate, a precursor of cholesterol. It produces its lipid-modifying effects in two ways:</p><ol><li>Increases the number of hepatic LDL receptors on the cell surface to enhance the uptake and catabolism of LDL.</li><li>Inhibits hepatic synthesis of VLDL, which reduces the total number of VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage of Rosu 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dose range</strong>: 5-40 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Use 40 mg dose only for patients not reaching LDL-C goal with 20 mg</strong></li><li><strong>HoFH (Homozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia)</strong>: Starting dose 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with HeFH (Heterozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-10 mg/day for patients aged 8 to &lt;10 years</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-20 mg/day for patients aged 10 to 17 years</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with HoFH</strong>: 20 mg/day for patients aged 7 to 17 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Rosu 40 mg</strong></p><p> Rosu 40 mg can be taken with or without food, at any time of the day.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Rosu 40 mg</strong></p><p> Remarkable drug interactions of Rosu 40 mg are:</p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong>: Combining these medications increases its exposure. The recommended dose should not exceed 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrosil</strong>: It is advisable to avoid combining these drugs. If necessary, limit the dosage to 10 mg once daily when used together.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or Atazanavir/Ritonavir</strong>: When used in combination, these medications can elevate its exposure. The recommended dose should be limited to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants</strong>: Combining with coumarin anticoagulants can prolong the international normalized ratio (INR). Achieve a stable INR before initiating treatment and monitor it frequently during therapy.</li><li><strong>Concomitant lipid-lowering therapies</strong>: When used with fibrates and niacin products, there may be an increased risk of skeletal muscle effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Rosu 40 mg is contraindicated if:</p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to product components</li><li>Liver disease, which may include unexplained persistent elevations in hepatic transaminase levels</li><li>Pregnant women and women who may become pregnant</li><li>Nursing mothers</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Rosu 40 mg</strong></p><p> Rosu 40 mg is generally well tolerated. The most frequent adverse events thought to be related to the medicine were:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The safety in pregnant women has not been established. It is not known whether Rosu 40 mg is excreted in human milk.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Skeletal muscle effects (e.g., myopathy and rhabdomyolysis)</strong>: Risks increase with use of 40 mg dose, advanced age (&gt;65 years), hypothyroidism, renal impairment, and combination use with cyclosporine, lopinavir/ritonavir, atazanavir/ritonavir, or certain other lipid-lowering drugs. Patients should be advised to promptly report unexplained muscle pain, tenderness, or weakness. Rosu 40 mg can be discontinued if signs or symptoms appear.</li><li><strong>Liver enzyme abnormalities and monitoring</strong>: Persistent elevations in hepatic transaminases can occur. Liver enzymes should be monitored before and during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below <strong>30°C</strong>, protected from light &amp; moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children</strong>: The safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1267_1742286870.webp', 17, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:34:30', '2025-03-18 13:34:30'),
(3278, 'Apremig ODT', 'Rimegepant', 150.00, 4, 'tablet', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '75 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1141_1742286974.webp', 44, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:36:14', '2025-03-18 13:36:14');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3279, 'Netis', 'Levocetirizine Dihydrochloride', 4.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Netis 5 mg:</strong></p><p>Levocetirizine is indicated in the treatment of symptoms associated with allergic conditions such as:</p><ul><li>Perennial allergic rhinitis</li><li>Seasonal allergic rhinitis</li><li>Chronic idiopathic urticaria</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Netis 5 mg is a preparation of Levocetirizine. Levocetirizine is the active (levo) isomer of Cetirizine. It is a new, highly effective, and well-tolerated non-sedating antihistamine with potent antiallergic properties. It has a two-fold higher affinity for H1-receptors than Cetirizine. Levocetirizine has a rapid and long-lasting action, allowing for once-a-day administration.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Netis 5 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children 12 years of age and older:</strong> 5 mg once daily in the evening</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 11 years of age:</strong> 2.5 mg once daily in the evening</li><li><strong>Children 6 months to 5 years of age:</strong> 1.25 mg (½ teaspoon oral solution) once daily in the evening</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Adjust the dose in patients 12 years of age and older with decreased renal function</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Netis 5 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult and children 12 years of age and older:</strong> The recommended dose is 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 11 years of age:</strong> The recommended dose is 2.5 mg (½ tablet or 1 teaspoon oral solution) once daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6 months to 5 years of age:</strong> The recommended dose is 1.25 mg (½ teaspoon oral solution) once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric use:</strong></p><ul><li>The recommended dose for the treatment of the uncomplicated skin manifestations of chronic idiopathic urticaria in patients 6 months to 17 years of age is based on extrapolation of efficacy from adults aged 18 years and older.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric use:</strong></p><ul><li>In clinical studies, each approved indication did not include sufficient numbers of patients aged 65 years and older to determine whether they respond differently than younger patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic impairment:</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage adjustment is required in patients with solely hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal impairment:</strong></p><ul><li>Mild renal impairment (creatinine clearance: 50-80 ml/min): a dose of 2.5 mg once daily is recommended.</li><li>Moderate renal impairment (creatinine clearance: 30-50 ml/min): a dose of 2.5 mg once every other day is recommended.</li><li>Severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance: 10-30 ml/min): a dose of 2.5 mg twice weekly (administered once every 3-4 days) is recommended.</li><li>End-stage renal disease patients (creatinine clearance: &lt;10 ml/min) and patients undergoing hemodialysis: should not receive Levocetirizine.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Netis 5 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Levocetirizine is not known to have any interactions with other drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to levocetirizine, cetirizine, or its parent compound hydroxyzine.</li><li>Patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance: &lt;10 ml/min) should not be administered levocetirizine.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Netis 5 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Generally, levocetirizine is well tolerated. However, a few side effects like headache, dry mouth, fatigue, and skin rash have been reported rarely.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> The safety of Levocetirizine in pregnancy has not been established. Therefore, it should be used with caution during pregnancy and only if the potential benefits to the mother outweigh any risks to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Levocetirizine passes into breast milk. It should be used with caution in nursing mothers and only if the expected benefit to the mother is greater than the risk to the nursing infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Epileptic patients and patients at risk of convulsions.</li><li>Renal impairment.</li><li>Pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Netis 5 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>No clinically relevant adverse events have been reported in case of overdose. However, in the event of overdosage, symptomatic and supportive treatment is recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store at a temperature not exceeding 30°C in a dry place. Protect from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use:</strong> Not recommended for use in children less than 6 years of age.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>This preparation contains Netis 5 mg. Levocetirizine is the active (Levo) isomer of cetirizine. It is a new, highly effective, and well-tolerated nonsedating antihistamine with potent antiallergic properties. It has a two-fold higher affinity for H1 receptors than cetirizine. Levocetirizine has a rapid and long-acting action, allowing for once-a-day administration.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy Category B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, levocetirizine should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Since levocetirizine is excreted in breast milk, it is not recommended for use by nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '966_1742287151.jpg', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:39:11', '2025-03-18 13:39:11'),
(3280, 'Lubric Extra', 'Carboxymethylcellulose Sodium plus Hypromellose', 210.00, 1, 'Ophthalmic Gel', '10 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Gel', '0.25 percent plus 0.3 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Lubric Extra 0.25%+0.3%</strong></p><p> This medication is used to relieve the dryness and pain associated with reduced or abnormal tear production.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for Dry eyes</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Carboxymethylcellulose Sodium &amp; Hypromellose are both polymers in nature. It is a clear and colorless aqueous solution. This medication lubricates the surface of the eye in the same way as natural tears. It can therefore be used to relieve the dryness and pain associated with reduced or abnormal tear production.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Lubric Extra 0.25%+0.3%</strong></p><p> For Adults and Children: Instill 1 or 2 drops in the affected eye(s) as needed.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Known sensitivity or allergy to any ingredient of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Lubric Extra 0.25%+0.3%</strong></p><p> Common side effects are blurred vision, matting or stickiness of eyelashes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Carboxymethylcellulose Sodium &amp; Hypromellose have not been shown to cause birth defects or other problems during pregnancy and breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> If you experience eye pain, changes in vision, continued redness or irritation of the eye or if your symptoms continue for more than 3 days or become worse, check with your doctor.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place and protected from light. Keep out of the reach of children. Do not use more than 4 weeks after opening.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> This drug does not cause different side effects or problems in children than it does in adults.</p>', 'active', '37_1742287152.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:39:12', '2025-03-18 13:39:12'),
(3281, 'Fericit', 'Ferric Citrate', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '210 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Fericit 210 mg</strong></p><p> Fericit 210 mg is a phosphate binder indicated for the control of serum phosphorus levels in adult patients with chronic kidney disease on dialysis. It is also an iron replacement product indicated for the treatment of iron deficiency anemia in adult patients with chronic kidney disease not on dialysis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Oral Iron Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hyperphosphatemia in Chronic Kidney Disease on Dialysis</strong>: Ferric iron binds dietary phosphate in the GI tract and precipitates as ferric phosphate. This compound is insoluble and is excreted in the stool, thereby decreasing phosphate absorption and lowering serum phosphate levels.</li><li><strong>Iron Deficiency Anemia in Chronic Kidney Disease Not on Dialysis</strong>: Ferric iron is reduced from the ferric to the ferrous form by ferric reductase in the GI tract. After transport into the blood, ferric iron circulates bound to plasma protein transferrin and can be incorporated into hemoglobin.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Fericit 210 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hyperphosphatemia in Chronic Kidney Disease on Dialysis</strong>: Starting dose is 2 tablets orally 3 times per day with meals. The dose can be adjusted by 1 to 2 tablets as needed, up to a maximum of 12 tablets daily. Dose adjustments can occur at 1-week or longer intervals.</li><li><strong>Iron Deficiency Anemia in Chronic Kidney Disease Not on Dialysis</strong>: Starting dose is 1 tablet orally 3 times per day with meals. The dose can be adjusted as needed to achieve and maintain the hemoglobin goal, up to a maximum of 12 tablets daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Fericit 210 mg</strong></p><p> When clinically significant drug interactions are expected, consider separating the timing of administration. It is recommended to monitor clinical responses or blood levels of the concomitant medication.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Fericit 210 mg is contraindicated in patients with iron overload syndromes.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Fericit 210 mg</strong></p><p> Common adverse reactions (≥5% incidence) include:</p><ul><li>Discolored feces</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Cough</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Hyperkalemia</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>There are no available data on Fericit 210 mg use in pregnant women to inform a drug-associated risk of major birth defects and miscarriage.</li><li>There are no human data regarding the effects of Fericit 210 mg in human milk, the effects on the breastfed child, or the effects on milk production.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Iron absorption from Fericit 210 mg may lead to excessive elevations in iron stores. Increases in serum ferritin and transferrin saturation (TSAT) levels have been observed in clinical trials.</li><li>Assess iron parameters (e.g., serum ferritin and TSAT) prior to initiating Fericit 210 mg and monitor while on therapy.</li><li>Patients receiving intravenous iron may require a reduction in dose or discontinuation of intravenous iron therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Fericit 210 mg</strong></p><p> No data are available regarding overdose of Fericit 210 mg in patients. In patients with chronic kidney disease, the maximum dose studied was 2,520 mg of ferric iron (12 tablets of Fericit 210 mg) per day. Excessive iron absorption may lead to elevated iron stores, especially when intravenous iron is also used.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: The safety and efficacy of Fericit 210 mg have not been established in pediatric patients.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Clinical studies included 292 subjects aged 65 years and older, with no identified differences in response between elderly and younger patients in tolerability or efficacy.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Oral Iron Preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hyperphosphatemia in Chronic Kidney Disease on Dialysis</strong>: Ferric iron binds dietary phosphate in the GI tract and precipitates as ferric phosphate. This insoluble compound is excreted in the stool, reducing phosphate absorption.</li><li><strong>Iron Deficiency Anemia in Chronic Kidney Disease Not on Dialysis</strong>: Ferric iron is reduced to the ferrous form in the GI tract and binds to transferrin, circulating in the blood to be incorporated into hemoglobin.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> There are no available data on Fericit 210 mg use in pregnant women to inform the risk of birth defects or miscarriage. There are no human data regarding its effect on breast milk or breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><p> The safety and efficacy of Fericit 210 mg have not been established in pediatric patients.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><p> Clinical studies included 292 subjects aged 65 years and older, including 104 aged 75 years and older, without identifying differences in the drug\'s effectiveness or tolerability in older adults compared to younger patients.</p>', 'active', '321_1742287286.webp', 53, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:41:26', '2025-03-18 13:41:26'),
(3282, 'Vista', 'Ofloxacin', 85.00, 1, 'Ophthalmic Solution', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '0.3 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vista 0.3%</strong></p><p> Vista 0.3% tablets are indicated for the treatment of adults with mild to moderate infections caused by susceptible strains.</p><ul><li><strong>Lower Respiratory Tract</strong>: Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis, lung abscess, pneumonia.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal Tract</strong>: Enteric fever, shigellosis.</li><li><strong>Multi-drug-resistant Tuberculosis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Skin and Skin Structures</strong>: Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infections.</li><li><strong>Sexually Transmitted Diseases</strong>: Acute, uncomplicated urethral and cervical gonorrhoea, nongonococcal urethritis and cervicitis, mixed infections of the urethra and cervix.</li><li><strong>Urinary Tract</strong>: Uncomplicated urinary tract infections, complicated urinary tract infections.</li></ul><p>Vista 0.3% Eye &amp; Ear Drops is indicated in:</p><ul><li><strong>Eye</strong>: External ocular infections such as acute &amp; sub-acute conjunctivitis, keratitis, kerato-conjunctivitis, blepharo-conjunctivitis, blepharitis, corneal ulcer, and pre-operative prophylaxis in ocular surgery.</li><li><strong>Ear</strong>: External ear infections (otitis externa) and certain middle ear infections (otitis media).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> 4-Quinolone preparations, Aural Anti-bacterial preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Vista 0.3% is a synthetic 4-fluoroquinolone antibacterial agent with bactericidal activity against a wide range of Gram-negative and Gram-positive organisms. Vista 0.3% is thought to exert bactericidal effects by inhibiting DNA gyrase, an essential enzyme that is critical for the duplication, transcription, and repair of bacterial DNA.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Vista 0.3%</strong></p><p> <strong>General dosage recommendations</strong>: The dose of Vista 0.3% is determined by the type and severity of the infection. The dosage range for adults is 200 mg to 800 mg daily. Up to 400 mg may be given as a single dose, preferably in the morning. Larger doses should be given as two divided doses.</p><p> Vista 0.3% tablets should be swallowed with liquid; they should not be taken within two hours of intake of magnesium/aluminium containing antacids or iron preparations since reduction of absorption of Vista 0.3% can occur.</p><ul><li><strong>Enteric fever</strong>: For adults 200 mg, every 12 hours, for 5 days. For children, 15 mg/kg/day in 2 divided doses for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Shigellosis</strong>: 400 mg single dose.</li><li><strong>Multi-drug-resistant tuberculosis</strong>: 400 mg twice daily along with conventional anti-tuberculosis drugs.</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infection</strong>: 400 mg daily, increasing, if necessary, to 400 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong>: A single dose of 200/400 mg.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urethral and cervical gonorrhoea</strong>: A single dose of 400 mg.</li><li><strong>Non-gonococcal urethral and cervicitis</strong>: 400 mg daily in single or divided doses.</li><li><strong>Complicated urinary tract infection</strong>: 200/400 mg/day for 7 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Impaired liver function</strong>: The excretion of Vista 0.3% may be reduced in patients with severe hepatic dysfunction.</p><p> <strong>Children</strong>: Vista 0.3% is usually not indicated for use in children or growing adolescents.</p><p> <strong>Elderly</strong>: No adjustment of dosage is required in the elderly.</p><p><strong>Vista 0.3% Eye &amp; Ear Drops</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Eye</strong>: Instill 1 drop in the affected eye(s) every 2 to 4 hours for the first two days and then 4 times daily. The length of treatment should not exceed ten days.</li><li><strong>Ear</strong>: Instill 1-2 drops in the affected ear(s) twice daily or as needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Vista 0.3%</strong></p><p> May be taken with or without food. Avoid antacids or supplements containing Fe or Zn within 2 hours before or after Vista 0.3%. Ensure adequate hydration.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Vista 0.3%</strong></p><p> Antacids containing magnesium, aluminium, or calcium may decrease absorption of Vista 0.3%. Iron or Zinc may decrease oral absorption of Vista 0.3%.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Vista 0.3% should not be used in patients with known hypersensitivity to 4-fluoroquinolone antibacterials. It is contraindicated in patients with a history of epilepsy or with a lowered seizure threshold. Vista 0.3% is usually contraindicated in children or growing adolescents and in pregnant or breastfeeding women.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Vista 0.3%</strong></p><p> Vista 0.3% tablet is generally well tolerated, and clinical side effects are quite low.</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Nausea, rash, vomiting, abdominal pain, diarrhoea, and gastrointestinal distress.</li><li><strong>Central Nervous System</strong>: Headache, dizziness, and insomnia.</li><li><strong>Eye</strong>: Transient ocular irritation, burning, stinging, redness, itching, or photophobia.</li><li><strong>Ear</strong>: Mild irritation or mild discomfort in the ear may occur. Symptoms of an allergic reaction include rash, itching, swelling, or trouble breathing.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The safety of Vista 0.3% during pregnancy has not been established. Vista 0.3% may enter breast milk, but data are not available.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Patients being treated with Vista 0.3% should not expose themselves unnecessarily to strong sunlight and should avoid UV rays. Caution is recommended if the drug is to be used in psychotic patients or in patients with a history of psychiatric disease. Prolonged use of eye drops &amp; ear drops may result in overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms and secondary infections respectively.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Vista 0.3%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Confusion, dizziness, impairment of consciousness, convulsive seizures, GI reactions (e.g., nausea, mucosal erosions).</li><li><strong>Management</strong>: Symptomatic and supportive treatment. Remove any unabsorbed drug by gastric lavage or administration of adsorbents and Na sulfate. Antacids are recommended for protection of gastric mucosa. Elimination may be increased by forced diuresis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store between 15-30°C. Protect from light.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl &lt;20 and patients on haemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis: 100 mg 24 hrly following the usual initial dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 20-50: Reduce dose by half 24 hrly following the usual initial dose.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Severe: Reduce dose. Max: 400 mg daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> 4-Quinolone preparations, Aural Anti-bacterial preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Vista 0.3% containing Vista 0.3% is a synthetic 4-fluoroquinolone antibacterial agent with bactericidal activity against a wide range of Gram-negative and Gram-positive organisms. Vista 0.3% is thought to exert bactericidal effects by inhibiting DNA gyrase, an essential enzyme that is critical for the duplication, transcription, and repair of bacterial DNA. Following oral administration, Vista 0.3% is rapidly and well absorbed from the G.I. tract. The bioavailability of Vista 0.3% in the tablet formulation is almost 100%. It is widely distributed into body tissues and fluid. The half-life is about 4.6 to 6.9 hours. Up to 80% of an oral dose of Vista 0.3% is eliminated in the urine as the parent compound within 48 hours.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The safety of Vista 0.3% during pregnancy has not been established. Vista 0.3% may enter breast milk, but data are not available.</p>', 'active', '1285_1742287557.jpg', 29, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:45:57', '2025-03-18 13:45:57'),
(3283, 'Manforce Strawberry Flavour Condoms', 'Condom', 32.00, 10, 'condom', '10 condom in a pack', 'Condom', 'condom', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Description:</strong></p><p>Manforce Strawberry Flavour Condoms ensure your peace of mind and let you enjoy intimate moments. These condoms are designed to provide maximum protection while balancing safety and sensitivity. Made with high-grade, durable latex, they guarantee protection against unwanted pregnancies and sexually transmitted diseases. Both partners experience comfort and pleasure, thanks to the natural-feeling lubrication. They are rigorously tested to meet international safety standards.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Usage Instructions of Manforce Strawberry Flavour Condoms:</strong></p><ol><li>Place the Manforce Strawberry Flavour Condoms on the erect genital just before sexual intercourse.</li><li>Ensure that the roll of the condom is on the outside.</li><li>Squeeze the end of the condom to release any trapped air before putting it on.</li><li>With one hand, put the condom on top of the genital while still squeezing the end, and roll it down with the other hand.</li><li>After ejaculation, while the genital is still erect, hold the condom firmly at the base and carefully remove it.</li></ol><p><strong>Safety Information:</strong></p><ul><li>Ensure the Manforce Strawberry Flavour Condoms are within their expiry date for optimal effectiveness.</li><li>The condoms are designed for single use only. Never reuse them.</li><li>Avoid using sharp objects to open the packaging, as it can damage the condom.</li><li>Dispose of used condoms responsibly. Do not flush them down the toilet.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Open the package carefully, avoiding sharp objects that could potentially damage the condom.</li><li>Always check the expiry date before use. Expired condoms may not provide effective protection.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store the condoms in a cool (below 30°C), dry place, shielded from sunlight.</li></ul>', 'active', '1521_1742287683.jpg', 74, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:48:03', '2025-09-20 09:41:55'),
(3284, 'Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condoms', 'Condoms', 32.00, 10, 'codom', '10 condom in a pack', 'Condom', '0 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condoms</strong></p><p>Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condoms ensures your peace of mind and lets you enjoy intimate moments. This Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condom is designed to provide maximum protection while balancing safety and sensitivity. They are made with high-grade, durable latex to guarantee protection against unwanted pregnancies and sexually transmitted diseases. Both partners experience comfort and pleasure thanks to the natural-feeling lubrication. Rigorously tested to meet international safety standards.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Usage Instructions Of Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condoms:</strong></h3><ol><li>Place the Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condom on the erect genital just before sexual intercourse.</li><li>Ensure that the roll of the Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condom is on the outside.</li><li>Squeeze the end of the Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condom to release any trapped air before putting it on.</li><li>With one hand, put the Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condom on top of the genital while still squeezing the end, and roll it down with the other hand.</li><li>After ejaculation, while the genital is still erect, hold the Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condom firmly at the base and carefully remove it.</li></ol><h3><strong>Safety Information:</strong></h3><ul><li>Ensure the Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condom is within its expiry date for optimal effectiveness.</li><li>The Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condom is designed for single use only. Never reuse a Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condom.</li><li>Avoid using sharp objects to open the packaging as it can damage the Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condom.</li><li>Dispose of used Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condoms responsibly. Do not flush them down the toilet.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></h3><ul><li>Open the package carefully, avoiding the use of sharp objects that could potentially damage the Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condom.</li><li>Always check the expiry date before use. Expired Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condoms may not provide effective protection.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></h3><ul><li>Ensure Manforce Chocolate Flavour Condoms are stored in a cool (below 30°C), dry place, shielded from sunlight.</li></ul>', 'active', '1951_1742287847.jpg', 74, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:50:47', '2025-09-20 09:41:44'),
(3285, 'Asynta Max', 'Sodium Alginate + Sodium Bicarbonate + Calcium Carbonate', 6.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg plus 106.5 mg plus 187.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is a structured overview of the Asynta Max 250 mg + 106.5 mg + 187.5 mg medication, highlighting the key sections and details:</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Indications:</strong></h3><p>Asynta Max 250 mg + 106.5 mg + 187.5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastric reflux</strong></li><li><strong>Heartburn</strong></li><li><strong>Flatulence associated with gastric reflux</strong></li><li><strong>Heartburn during pregnancy</strong></li><li><strong>Epigastric and retrosternal discomfort</strong> (where the underlying cause is gastric reflux)</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Antacids</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></h3><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Mode of Action:</strong>The action is physical, not dependent on absorption into the bloodstream.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">It forms an <strong>Alginic acid gel raft</strong> that floats on the stomach contents, effectively preventing gastro-esophageal reflux for up to 4 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In severe cases, the raft may be refluxed into the esophagus, providing a soothing effect instead of stomach contents.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Calcium Carbonate</strong> neutralizes gastric acid, and <strong>Sodium Bicarbonate</strong> enhances this neutralizing effect.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">This combination protects the esophagus from acid, pepsin, and bile exposure.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></h3><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years:</strong>10-20 mL after meals and at bedtime, up to <strong>4 times a day</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6 to 12 years):</strong>5-10 mL after meals and at bedtime, up to <strong>4 times a day</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children under 6 years:</strong>Not recommended.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly:</strong>No dosage modification needed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong>No modification necessary.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong>Use caution if on a highly restricted salt diet.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></h3><ul><li>Wait <strong>2 hours</strong> between Asynta Max and the following medications:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tetracyclines, digoxin, fluoroquinolones, iron salts, ketoconazole, neuroleptics, thyroid hormones, penicillamine, beta-blockers, glucocorticoids, chloroquine, bisphosphonates, estramustine.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to any of the active ingredients or excipients.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects:</strong></h3><ul><li>Common side effects include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Constipation</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Flatulence</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Stomach cramps</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Belching</li><li>In case of overdose, a sensation of swelling may occur. Seek medical advice if this happens.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></h3><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pregnancy:</strong>Safe to use during pregnancy based on clinical studies.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong>No adverse effects observed in breastfed infants.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Fertility:</strong>No detrimental effects on fertility have been reported.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></h3><ul><li>If no improvement is noticed within <strong>7 days</strong>, reassess the clinical condition.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Sodium content:</strong>Each 10 mL dose contains 141 mg sodium (6.2 mmol), so caution is needed in patients on a restricted salt diet, especially with heart failure or renal impairment.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Calcium content:</strong>Each 10 mL dose contains 160 mg calcium carbonate (1.6 mmol), caution in hypercalcemia, nephrocalcinosis, and recurrent calcium-containing kidney stones.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></h3><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose may include abdominal distension. Symptomatic treatment should be given.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong> in a cool, dry place, away from light.</li><li><strong>Keep out of reach of children</strong>.</li><li>Do not refrigerate or freeze.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></h3><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Paediatric Use:</strong>Not recommended for children under 6 years.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong>No dose adjustment needed for the elderly.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Class:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Antacids</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action Summary:</strong></h3><ul><li>Asynta Max operates through a physical mechanism, combining <strong>Calcium Carbonate</strong> and <strong>Sodium Bicarbonate</strong> with <strong>Alginate</strong>. The medication quickly reacts with gastric acid to form a protective raft with a nearly neutral pH. This raft prevents reflux for up to 4 hours and offers protection to the esophagus from stomach acid, pepsin, and bile.</li></ul><p>This summary includes all the key information regarding Asynta Max\'s indications, usage, pharmacology, and safety considerations.</p>', 'active', '790_1742288010.webp', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:53:30', '2025-03-18 13:53:30'),
(3286, 'Fobunid', 'Budesonide + Formoterol Fumarate', 10.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '200 mcg plus 6 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Fobunid 200 mcg + 6 mcg</strong></p><p>This is indicated in the regular treatment of asthma. It is also indicated in the symptomatic treatment of severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), with a history of repeated exacerbations despite regular therapy with long-acting bronchodilators.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants</li><li>Respiratory corticosteroids</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Budesonide</strong>: It is an anti-inflammatory corticosteroid that exhibits potent glucocorticoid activity and weak mineralocorticoid activity. Inflammation plays a key role in the pathogenesis of asthma. Corticosteroids inhibit various cell types (e.g., mast cells, eosinophils, neutrophils, macrophages, and lymphocytes) and mediators (e.g., histamine, eicosanoids, leukotrienes, and cytokines) involved in allergic and non-allergic mediated inflammation. These anti-inflammatory effects contribute to their efficacy in asthma.</li><li><strong>Formoterol Fumarate Dihydrate</strong>: A long-acting, selective β2 - adrenergic agonist with rapid onset. It acts locally in the lungs as a bronchodilator. The pharmacological effects are due to the stimulation of intracellular adenyl cyclase, which catalyzes the conversion of ATP to cyclic AMP, leading to bronchial smooth muscle relaxation and inhibition of mediator release from mast cells.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Fobunid 200 mcg + 6 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Inhaler (For Asthma)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: The recommended maintenance dose is 1 puff twice daily or 2 puffs once daily. In some cases, 2 puffs twice daily may be appropriate. For 160/4.5 mcg/inhalation only. One additional puff may be taken as needed for symptoms. Do not exceed 6 puffs on any single occasion.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children</strong>: The usual maintenance dose is 1-2 puffs once or twice daily. One additional puff may be taken as needed for symptoms. Do not exceed 4 puffs on any single occasion.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Capsule (For Asthma)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: Maintenance dose: 1 inhalation capsule twice daily. Reliever dose: 1 additional inhalation capsule as needed for symptoms. Do not exceed 6 inhalation capsules on any single occasion. A total daily dose of more than 8 inhalation capsules is typically not needed, but up to 12 can be used temporarily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4 years and older)</strong>: Maintenance dose: 1 inhalation capsule once daily. Reliever dose: 1 additional inhalation capsule as needed. Do not exceed 4 inhalation capsules on any single occasion, and up to 8 inhalation capsules can be used temporarily.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Capsule (For COPD)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults (40 years and older)</strong>: 200 mcg inhalation capsule: 2 inhalation capsules twice daily. Maximum daily dose: 4 inhalation capsules. 400 mcg inhalation capsule: 1 inhalation capsule twice daily. Maximum daily dose: 2 inhalation capsules.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Fobunid 200 mcg + 6 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant treatment with Ritonavir, Itraconazole, Ketoconazole, or other potent CYP3A4 inhibitors should be avoided.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Budesonide, Formoterol, or Lactose.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Fobunid 200 mcg + 6 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Budesonide</strong>: Hoarseness, candidiasis (thrush) of the mouth and throat, cutaneous hypersensitivity reactions.</li><li><strong>Formoterol Fumarate Dihydrate</strong>: Tremor, palpitations, headache, cardiac arrhythmias, muscle cramps, and hypersensitivity reactions, including rash, edema, and angioedema.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Administration should only be considered if the expected benefit to the mother is greater than any possible risk to the fetus or infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Budesonide and Formoterol combination should not be initiated to treat a severe exacerbation or in cases of significantly worsening or acutely deteriorating asthma.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Protect from light, store in a cool and dry place. Do not store above 30°C. Keep out of the reach of children. Protect from freezing.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Regular monitoring of height in children receiving prolonged treatment with inhaled corticosteroids is recommended. If growth is slowed, therapy should be reevaluated with the aim of reducing the dose of inhaled corticosteroid. The benefits of corticosteroid therapy should be weighed against potential risks of growth suppression. Referring to a pediatric respiratory specialist may also be considered.</li></ul>', 'active', '1260_1742288168.webp', 53, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:56:08', '2025-05-03 10:50:49'),
(3287, 'Meropen', 'Meropenem Trihydrate', 400.00, 1, 'IV injection', '250 mg vial iv injection', 'IV Injection', '250 mg/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Meropen 250 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Meropenem is indicated for treatment in adults and children for the following infections caused by single or multiple bacteria sensitive to Meropenem:</p><ul><li>Pneumonia and Nosocomial Pneumonia</li><li>Urinary Tract Infections</li><li>Intra-abdominal Infections</li><li>Gynaecological Infections, such as endometritis and pelvic inflammatory disease</li><li>Skin and Skin Structure Infections</li><li>Meningitis</li><li>Septicaemia</li><li>Pulmonary infections in cystic fibrosis</li><li>Empiric treatment for presumed infections in patients with febrile neutropenia</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Other beta-lactam Antibiotics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Meropenem is a carbapenem antibiotic for parenteral use. It exerts its bactericidal action by interfering with bacterial cell wall synthesis. It penetrates bacterial cell walls, is highly stable to all serine beta-lactamases, and has a marked affinity for the Penicillin Binding Proteins (PBPs.). It shows potent bactericidal activity against a broad spectrum of Gram-positive and Gram-negative, aerobic and anaerobic bacteria.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Meropen 250 mg/vial</strong></p><p>The dosage and duration of therapy depend on the type and severity of infection and the condition of the patient. The recommended daily dosage is as follows:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The usual dose is 500 mg to 1 gm by intravenous administration every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pneumonia, urinary tract infections, gynaecological infections, skin and skin structure infections: 500 mg IV every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nosocomial pneumonias, peritonitis, presumed infections in neutropenic patients, and septicaemia: 1 g IV every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Intra-abdominal infections: 500 mg to 1 gm every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Cystic fibrosis: Up to 2 gm every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Meningitis: 2 gm IV every 8 hours.</li><li><strong>Children (3 months to 12 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 to 40 mg/kg intravenously every 8 hours depending on the type and severity of infection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Intra-abdominal infections: 20 mg/kg every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Cystic fibrosis (4-18 years): 25-40 mg/kg every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Meningitis: 40 mg/kg IV every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Febrile neutropenia: 20 mg/kg every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children over 50 kg: Use adult dosage.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">No experience in children with hepatic or renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Meropen 250 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Meropenem should be administered by intravenous infusion over approximately 15-30 minutes or as intravenous bolus (5 to 20 ml) over approximately 3-5 minutes.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Meropen 250 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li>Probenecid competes with Meropenem for active tubular secretion, inhibiting renal excretion, which increases the elimination half-life and plasma concentration of Meropenem.</li><li>Meropenem may reduce serum valproic acid levels, potentially leading to sub-therapeutic levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Meropenem is contraindicated in patients who have demonstrated hypersensitivity to this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Meropen 250 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Meropenem is generally well tolerated. However, side effects such as inflammation, thrombophlebitis, pain at the injection site, skin reactions (rash, pruritus, urticaria), abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and headache may occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women, so this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Caution should be exercised when Meropenem is administered to a nursing woman because many drugs are excreted in human milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>If an allergic reaction to Meropenem occurs, the drug should be discontinued, and appropriate measures should be taken.</li><li>Use of Meropenem in patients with hepatic disease should be made with careful monitoring of transaminase and bilirubin levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Meropen 250 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Accidental overdose could occur, particularly in patients with renal impairment. Treatment should be symptomatic. In normal individuals, rapid renal elimination will occur. In subjects with renal impairment, haemodialysis will remove Meropenem and its metabolite.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Dosage should be reduced in patients with creatinine clearance less than 51 ml/min.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustments are necessary with liver function impairment. Hemodialysis patients should receive Meropenem after dialysis has been completed.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustments are necessary unless creatinine clearance is &lt;51 ml/min.</li><li><strong>Use in Children</strong>: Efficacy and tolerability in infants under 3 months have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Intravenous bolus administration</strong>: Reconstitute Meropenem (500 mg or 1 g) with sterile water for injection. Shake to dissolve and obtain a solution that is clear and colorless or pale yellow.</li><li><strong>Intravenous infusion administration</strong>: Meropenem for intravenous infusion may be directly constituted with a compatible infusion fluid and then further diluted (50 to 200 ml) with the compatible infusion fluid, as needed.</li><li>Meropenem is compatible with the following infusion fluids:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0.9% sodium chloride intravenous infusion</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5% or 10% glucose intravenous infusion</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5% glucose intravenous infusion with 0.02% sodium bicarbonate</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5% glucose and 0.9% sodium chloride intravenous infusion</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5% glucose with 0.225% sodium chloride intravenous infusion</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5% glucose with 0.15% potassium chloride intravenous infusion</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2.5% and 10% mannitol intravenous infusion</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Normosol-M in 5% glucose intravenous infusion</li><li>The freshly reconstituted solution is recommended for use, but it maintains potency for up to 3 hours at 25°C or 13 hours at 5°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Other beta-lactam Antibiotics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Meropenem is a carbapenem antibiotic for parenteral use. It exerts its bactericidal action by interfering with bacterial cell wall synthesis. It penetrates bacterial cell walls, is highly stable to all serine beta-lactamases, and has a marked affinity for the Penicillin Binding Proteins (PBPs.). It shows potent bactericidal activity against a broad spectrum of Gram-positive and Gram-negative, aerobic and anaerobic bacteria.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Category B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women, so this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk, caution should be exercised when Meropenem is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: Dosage should be reduced in patients with creatinine clearance less than 51 ml/min.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustments are necessary with liver function impairment. Hemodialysis patients should receive Meropenem after dialysis has been completed.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustments are necessary unless creatinine clearance is &lt;51 ml/min.</li><li><strong>Use in Children</strong>: Efficacy and tolerability in infants under 3 months have not been established.</li></ul>', 'active', '60_1742288240.jpg', 6, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:57:20', '2025-05-04 20:30:42');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3288, 'Evania-M', 'Empagliflozin + Metformin Hydrochloride', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg plus 500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here\'s the text with the key headings formatted in bold for better readability:</p><p><strong>Indications of Evania-M 5 mg+500 mg</strong></p><p> This tablet is indicated for the treatment of adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus as an adjunct to diet and exercise:</p><ul><li>In patients insufficiently controlled on their maximally tolerated dose of Metformin alone.</li><li>In combination with other medicinal products for the treatment of diabetes, in patients insufficiently controlled with Metformin and these medicinal products.</li><li>In patients already being treated with the combination of Empagliflozin and Metformin as separate tablets.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Combination Oral hypoglycemic preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Empagliflozin is an inhibitor of Sodium-Glucose Co-Transporter 2 (SGLT2). SGLT2 is the predominant transporter responsible for reabsorption of glucose from the kidney back into the circulation. By inhibiting SGLT2, Empagliflozin reduces renal reabsorption of filtered glucose, lowers the renal threshold for glucose, and increases urinary glucose excretion.</p><p> Metformin Hydrochloride is a biguanide type oral antihyperglycemic drug used in the management of type 2 diabetes. It lowers both basal and postprandial plasma glucose. It does not produce hypoglycemia. Metformin Hydrochloride decreases hepatic glucose production, decreases intestinal absorption of glucose, and improves insulin sensitivity by increasing peripheral glucose uptake and utilization.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Evania-M 5 mg+500 mg</strong></p><p> The dosage should be individualized based on effectiveness and tolerability. Take this combination twice daily with meals. Dose escalation should be gradual to reduce gastrointestinal side effects due to Metformin Hydrochloride.</p><p> Maximum recommended daily dose:</p><ul><li>Metformin Hydrochloride: 2000 mg</li><li>Empagliflozin: 25 mg</li><li> Recommended individualized starting dose:</li><li>In patients on Metformin Hydrochloride, switch to this combination containing Empagliflozin 5 mg with a similar total daily dose of Metformin Hydrochloride.</li><li>In patients on Empagliflozin, switch to this combination containing Metformin Hydrochloride 500 mg with a similar total daily dose of Empagliflozin.</li><li>In patients already treated with Empagliflozin and Metformin Hydrochloride separately, switch to this combination containing the same total daily doses of each component.</li><li>In patients with volume depletion not previously treated with Empagliflozin, correct this condition before initiating this combination.</li><li> Renal impaired patients: Assess renal function before initiating this combination. It is contraindicated in patients with an eGFR below 45 mL/min/1.73 m².</li><li> Pediatric patients under 18 years of age: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients under 18 years of age have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Evania-M 5 mg+500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: Co-administration of Empagliflozin with diuretics increased urine volume and frequency, which may enhance the potential for volume depletion.</li><li><strong>Insulin or Insulin Secretagogues</strong>: Co-administration of Empagliflozin with insulin or insulin secretagogues increases the risk for hypoglycemia.</li><li><strong>Positive Urine Glucose Test</strong>: Monitoring glycemic control with urine glucose tests is not recommended in patients taking SGLT2 inhibitors. Use alternative methods to monitor glycemic control.</li><li><strong>Drugs that Reduce Metformin Clearance</strong>: Drugs that reduce Metformin clearance (e.g., ranolazine, vandetanib, dolutegravir, and cimetidine) may increase the accumulation of Metformin.</li><li><strong>Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors</strong>: Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors may increase the risk of lactic acidosis.</li><li><strong>Drugs Affecting Glycemic Control</strong>: Thiazides, diuretics, corticosteroids, phenothiazines, thyroid products, estrogens, oral contraceptives, phenytoin, nicotinic acid, sympathomimetics, calcium channel blockers, and isoniazid can cause hyperglycemia. Close monitoring is needed when such drugs are used with this combination.</li><li><strong>Alcohol</strong>: Alcohol can potentiate the effect of Metformin on lactate metabolism. Warn patients against excessive alcohol intake.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Empagliflozin and Metformin</li><li>Any type of acute metabolic acidosis (e.g., lactic acidosis, diabetic ketoacidosis)</li><li>Diabetic pre-coma</li><li>Severe renal failure (GFR &lt;30 ml/min)</li><li>Acute conditions that may alter renal function such as dehydration, severe infection, shock</li><li>Diseases that may cause tissue hypoxia (e.g., decompensated heart failure, respiratory failure, recent myocardial infarction, shock)</li><li>Hepatic impairment, acute alcohol intoxication, alcoholism</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Evania-M 5 mg+500 mg</strong></p><p> Most common adverse reactions associated with Empagliflozin (≥5%) were urinary tract infection and female genital mycotic infections.</p><p> Most common adverse reactions associated with Metformin (&gt;5%) include diarrhea, nausea/vomiting, flatulence, abdominal discomfort, indigestion, asthenia, and headache.</p><p> Important adverse reactions include:</p><ul><li><strong>Very common</strong>: Hypoglycemia (when used with sulphonylurea or insulin), gastrointestinal symptoms</li><li><strong>Common</strong>: Vaginal moniliasis, vulvovaginitis, balanitis and other genital infections, urinary tract infections (including pyelonephritis and urosepsis), thirst, taste disturbance, pruritus (generalized), rash, increased urination, increased serum lipids</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Volume depletion, urticaria, dysuria, increased blood creatinine/ decreased glomerular filtration rate, increased hematocrit</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Diabetic ketoacidosis</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Advise females of the potential risk to a fetus, especially during the second and third trimesters. This medication is not recommended during breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Lactic Acidosis</strong>: Postmarketing cases of Metformin-associated lactic acidosis. If suspected, discontinue immediately and provide general supportive care.</li><li><strong>Hypotension</strong>: Correct volume status before initiating therapy in patients with renal impairment, the elderly, or those on diuretics.</li><li><strong>Ketoacidosis</strong>: Assess patients for ketoacidosis before initiating this combination.</li><li><strong>Acute Kidney Injury &amp; Impaired Renal Function</strong>: Consider temporarily discontinuing this combination in settings of reduced oral intake or fluid losses.</li><li><strong>Urosepsis, Pyelonephritis, Fournier’s Gangrene &amp; Genital Mycotic Infections</strong>: Treatment with SGLT2 inhibitors increases the risk of urinary tract infections.</li><li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong>: Consider lowering the dose of insulin secretagogue or insulin when initiating this combination.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12 Deficiency</strong>: Metformin may lower vitamin B12 levels. Monitor hematologic parameters annually.</li><li><strong>Increased LDL-C</strong>: Monitor and treat as appropriate.</li><li><strong>Macrovascular Outcomes</strong>: There is no conclusive evidence of macrovascular risk reduction with this combination.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Evania-M 5 mg+500 mg</strong></p><p> In clinical studies, single doses of up to 800 mg Empagliflozin and multiple daily doses of up to 100 mg Empagliflozin did not show toxicity.</p><p> Lactic acidosis has occurred with Metformin doses up to 85 g. In the event of an overdose, treatment should be initiated as appropriate. Hemodialysis is the most effective method to remove lactate and Metformin. Removal of Empagliflozin by hemodialysis has not been studied.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients under 18 years of age have not been established.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Combination Oral hypoglycemic preparations.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Empagliflozin</strong>: An SGLT2 inhibitor that reduces renal reabsorption of glucose.</li><li><strong>Metformin Hydrochloride</strong>: A biguanide that decreases hepatic glucose production, intestinal glucose absorption, and improves insulin sensitivity.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Advise females of the potential risk to a fetus, particularly during the second and third trimesters. This medication is not recommended during breastfeeding.</p>', 'active', '672_1742288360.png', 1, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 13:59:20', '2025-03-18 13:59:20'),
(3289, 'Diampa', 'Empagliflozin', 50.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Diampa 25 mg</strong></p><p> Diampa 25 mg is indicated in:</p><ul><li>As an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycemic control in adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus.</li><li>To reduce the risk of cardiovascular death in adult patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus and established cardiovascular disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Sodium-glucose Cotransporter-2 (SGLT2) Inhibitors</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Diampa 25 mg is a sodium glucose co-transporter-2 (SGLT-2) inhibitor. SGLT2 co-transporters are responsible for reabsorption of glucose from the glomerular filtrate in the kidney. The glucuretic effect resulting from SGLT2 inhibition reduces renal absorption and lowers the renal threshold for glucose, resulting in increased glucose excretion. Additionally, it contributes to reduced hyperglycaemia, assists weight loss, and reduces blood pressure.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Diampa 25 mg</strong></p><p> The recommended dose of Diampa 25 mg is 10 mg once daily, taken in the morning, with or without food. In patients tolerating Diampa 25 mg, the dose may be increased to 25 mg once daily. In patients with volume depletion, correcting this condition prior to initiation of Diampa 25 mg is recommended.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Diampa 25 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> Co-administration of Diampa 25 mg with diuretics resulted in increased urine volume.</li><li><strong>Insulin or Insulin Secretagogues:</strong> Co-administration of Diampa 25 mg with insulin or insulin secretagogues increases the risk for hypoglycemia.</li><li><strong>Positive Urine Glucose Test:</strong> Monitoring glycemic control with urine glucose tests is not recommended in patients taking SGLT2 inhibitors as SGLT2 inhibitors increase urinary glucose excretion and will lead to positive urine glucose tests. Use alternative methods to monitor glycemic control.</li><li><strong>Interference with 1,5-anhydroglucitol (1,5-AG) Assay:</strong> Monitoring glycemic control with 1,5-AG assay is not recommended as measurements of 1,5-AG are unreliable in assessing glycemic control in patients taking SGLT2 inhibitors. Use alternative methods to monitor glycemic control.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Diampa 25 mg is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>History of serious hypersensitivity reaction to Diampa 25 mg or any of its ingredients</li><li>Severe renal impairment, end-stage renal disease, or dialysis</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Diampa 25 mg</strong></p><p> The most common adverse reactions associated with Diampa 25 mg are:</p><ul><li>Urinary tract infections</li><li>Female genital mycotic infections</li><li> Other common side effects include:</li><li>Dehydration</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Weakness</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Increased thirstiness</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies of Diampa 25 mg in pregnant women. Diampa 25 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known if Diampa 25 mg is excreted in human milk. It is not recommended when breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Assessment of renal function is recommended prior to initiation of Diampa 25 mg and periodically thereafter.</li><li>Diampa 25 mg should not be initiated in patients with an eGFR less than 45 ml/min/1.73 m².</li><li>No dose adjustment is needed in patients with an eGFR greater than or equal to 45 ml/min/1.73 m².</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Diampa 25 mg</strong></p><p> In the event of an overdose with Diampa 25 mg, the usual supportive measures (e.g., remove unabsorbed material from the gastrointestinal tract, perform clinical monitoring, and institute supportive treatment) should be employed. Removal of Diampa 25 mg by hemodialysis has not been studied.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a cool &amp; dry place (below 30°C), protected from light &amp; moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Sodium-glucose Cotransporter-2 (SGLT2) Inhibitors</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Diampa 25 mg is a sodium glucose co-transporter-2 (SGLT-2) inhibitor. SGLT2 co-transporters are responsible for reabsorption of glucose from the glomerular filtrate in the kidney. The glucuretic effect resulting from SGLT2 inhibition reduces renal absorption and lowers the renal threshold for glucose, resulting in increased glucose excretion. Additionally, it contributes to reduced hyperglycaemia, assists weight loss, and reduces blood pressure.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies of Diampa 25 mg in pregnant women. Diampa 25 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known if Diampa 25 mg is excreted in human milk. It is not recommended when breastfeeding.</p>', 'active', '1673_1742288495.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 14:01:35', '2025-03-18 14:01:35'),
(3290, 'Palker', 'Palbociclib', 400.00, 21, 'Capsule', '21 Tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '125 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Palker 125 mg</strong></p><p> Palker 125 mg is a kinase inhibitor indicated for the treatment of hormone receptor (HR)-positive, human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2)-negative advanced or metastatic breast cancer in combination with:</p><ul><li>An aromatase inhibitor as initial endocrine-based therapy in postmenopausal women; or</li><li>Fulvestrant in women with disease progression following endocrine therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Targeted Cancer Therapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Treatment of breast cancer cell lines with the combination of Palker 125 mg and antiestrogens leads to decreased retinoblastoma protein (Rb) phosphorylation resulting in reduced E2F expression and signaling and increased growth arrest compared to treatment with each drug alone. In vitro treatment of ER-positive breast cancer cell lines with the combination of Palker 125 mg and antiestrogens leads to increased cell senescence, which was sustained for up to 6 days following drug removal. In vivo studies using a patient-derived ER-positive breast cancer xenograft model demonstrated that the combination of Palker 125 mg and letrozole increased the inhibition of Rb phosphorylation, downstream signaling, and tumor growth compared to each drug alone.</p><p> Palker 125 mg is an oral, reversible, selective, small-molecule inhibitor of CDK4 and CDK6. CDK4 and CDK6 along with their regulatory partner cyclin D1 play a key role in regulating the G1- to S-phase cell-cycle transition via regulation of phosphorylation of the retinoblastoma (Rb) protein.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Palker 125 mg</strong></p><p> Palker 125 mg capsules are taken orally with food in combination with an aromatase inhibitor or fulvestrant.</p><p> Recommended starting dose: 125 mg once daily taken with food for 21 days followed by 7 days off treatment. Dosing interruption and/or dose reductions are recommended based on individual safety and tolerability.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Palker 125 mg</strong></p><p> The recommended dose of Palker 125 mg is a 125 mg capsule taken orally once daily for 21 consecutive days followed by 7 days off treatment to comprise a complete cycle of 28 days. Palker 125 mg should be taken with food. Patients should be encouraged to take their dose of Palker 125 mg at approximately the same time each day. For men treated with combination Palker 125 mg plus aromatase inhibitor therapy, consider treatment with an LHRH agonist according to current clinical practice standards.</p><p> Recommended Dose Modification for Adverse Reactions:</p><ul><li>Recommended Starting Dose: 125 mg/day</li><li>First Dose Reduction: 100 mg/day</li><li>Second Dose Reduction: 75 mg/day</li><li> If further dose reduction below 75 mg/day is required, discontinue. Or, as directed by the registered physician.</li><li> <strong>Use in Children</strong>: Palker 125 mg is not indicated for use in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Palker 125 mg</strong></p><p> Palker 125 mg is primarily metabolized by CYP3A and Sulfotransferase (SULT) enzyme SULT2A1. Palker 125 mg is a time-dependent inhibitor of CYP3A.</p><p><strong>Agents That May Increase Palker 125 mg Plasma Concentrations</strong></p><ul><li>Effect of CYP3A-Inhibitors: Coadministration of a strong CYP3A inhibitor (Itraconazole) increased the plasma exposure of Palker 125 mg in patients by 87%. Avoid concomitant use of strong CYP3A inhibitors (e.g., Clarithromycin, Indinavir, Itraconazole, Ketoconazole, Lopinavir/Ritonavir, Nefazodone, Nelfinavir, Posaconazole, Ritonavir, Saquinavir, Telaprevir, Telithromycin, and Voriconazole). Avoid grapefruit or grapefruit juice during Palker 125 mg treatment. If coadministration of Palker 125 mg with a strong CYP3A inhibitor cannot be avoided, reduce the dose of Palker 125 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Agents That May Decrease Palker 125 mg Plasma Concentrations</strong></p><ul><li>Effect of CYP3A Inducers: Coadministration of a strong CYP3A Inducer (Rifampin) decreased the plasma exposure of Palker 125 mg in healthy subjects by 85%. Avoid concomitant use of strong CYP3A inducers (e.g., Phenytoin, Rifampin, Carbamazepine, Enzalutamide, and St John’s Wort).</li></ul><p><strong>Drugs That May Have Their Plasma Concentrations Altered By Palker 125 mg</strong></p><p> Coadministration of Midazolam with multiple doses of Palker 125 mg increased the Midazolam plasma exposure by 61%, in patients, compared to administration of Midazolam alone. The dose of the sensitive CYP3A substrate with a narrow therapeutic index (e.g., Alfentanil, Cyclosporine, Dihydroergotamine, Ergotamine, Everolimus, Fentanyl, Pimozide, Quinidine, Sirolimus, and Tacrolimus) may need to be reduced, as Palker 125 mg may increase its exposure.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> None</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Palker 125 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects of Palker 125 mg include:</p><ul><li>WBC decreased</li><li>Neutrophils decreased</li><li>Neutropenia</li><li>Platelets decreased</li><li>Infections</li><li>AST increased</li><li>ALT increased</li><li>Leukopenia</li><li>Fatigue</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Hair loss</li><li>Inflammation of the mouth and lips</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Anemia</li><li>Rash</li><li>Weakness/lethargy</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Thrombocytopenia</li><li>Dry skin</li><li>Fever</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> <strong>Lactation</strong>: Advise not to breastfeed.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neutropenia</strong>: Neutropenia was the most frequently reported adverse reaction. Monitor complete blood counts prior to starting Palker 125 mg therapy and at the beginning of each cycle, as well as on day 15 of the first 2 cycles, and as clinically indicated. Dose interruption, dose reduction, or delay in starting treatment cycles is recommended for patients who develop grade 3 or 4 neutropenia. Febrile neutropenia has been reported in 1.8% of patients exposed to Palker 125 mg. One death due to neutropenic sepsis was observed. Physicians should inform patients to promptly report any episodes of fever.</li><li><strong>Embryo-Fetal Toxicity</strong>: Palker 125 mg can cause fetal harm when administered to a pregnant woman. Advise pregnant women of the potential risk to a fetus. Advise females of reproductive potential to use effective contraception during treatment with Palker 125 mg and for at least 3 weeks after the last dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Palker 125 mg</strong></p><p> There is no known antidote for Palker 125 mg. The treatment of overdose of Palker 125 mg should consist of general supportive measures.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Palker 125 mg is an inhibitor of Cyclin-dependent kinases (CDK) 4 and 6. Cyclin D1 and CDK4/6 are downstream of signaling pathways that lead to cellular proliferation. Palker 125 mg reduced cellular proliferation of Estrogen receptor (ER)-positive breast cancer cell lines by blocking progression of the cell from G1 into s phase of the cell cycle. Treatment of breast cancer cell lines with the combination of Palker 125 mg and antiestrogens leads to decreased retinoblastoma (RB) protein phosphorylation resulting in reduced E2F expression and signaling, and increased growth arrest compared to treatment with each drug alone. Treatment of ER-positive breast cancer cell lines with the combination of Palker 125 mg and antiestrogens led to increased cell senescence compared to each drug alone, which was sustained for up to 6 days following Palker 125 mg removal and was greater if antiestrogen treatment was continued. In vivo studies using a patient-derived ER-positive breast cancer xenograft model demonstrated that the combination of Palker 125 mg and letrozole increased the inhibition of RB phosphorylation, downstream signaling, and tumor growth compared to each drug alone.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Palker 125 mg can cause fetal harm when administered to a pregnant woman. Advise pregnant women of the potential risk to a fetus. Risk of major birth defects and miscarriage in clinically recognized pregnancies is 2%-4% and 15%-20%, respectively.</p>', 'active', '1284_1742289697.jpg', 55, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 14:21:37', '2025-03-18 14:21:37'),
(3291, 'Simvatin', 'Simvastatin', 11.04, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Simvatin 10 mg</strong></p><p> Primary hypercholesterolemia (type IIa and IIb) in patients who have not responded adequately to diet and other appropriate measures. Coronary heart disease and elevated plasma cholesterol level.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs, Statins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Simvatin 10 mg is a preparation of Simvatin 10 mg which acts as a Cholesterol lowering agent. The main mechanism of reduction of low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol is that following inhibition of HMG-CoA reductase activity, the LDL receptor density on the liver cells is increased and this leads to an increased removal of LDL cholesterol from the plasma and increased catabolism of LDL cholesterol. In addition, there is a reduction in the very low-density lipoprotein (VLDL) cholesterol and reduced formation of LDL from VLDL. Simvatin 10 mg is extensively metabolized in the liver; which is also the main site of action of the drug.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Simvatin 10 mg</strong></p><p> The patient should be placed on a standard cholesterol-lowering diet before receiving Simvatin 10 mg and should continue on this during treatment with Simvatin 10 mg. The usual starting dose is 10 mg/day given as a single dose in the evening. Adjustment of dosage, if required, should be made at intervals of not less than four weeks, to a maximum of 40 mg daily given as a single dose in the evening. If LDL-cholesterol levels fall below 2 mmol/L or total plasma cholesterol levels fall below 3.5 mmol/L, consideration should be given to reducing the dose of Simvatin 10 mg. In hypercholesterolemia, the recommended starting dose is 5-10 mg once a day in the evening and the recommended dosing range is 5-40 mg per day as a single dose in the evening. In patients with coronary heart disease and hypercholesterolemia, the starting dose should be 20 mg once a day in the evening. Because Simvatin 10 mg does not undergo significant renal excretion, modification of dosage should not be necessary in patients with renal insufficiency. Safety and effectiveness in children and adolescents have not been established.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Simvatin 10 mg</strong></p><p> The patient should be placed on a standard cholesterol-lowering diet before receiving Simvatin 10 mg and should continue on this during treatment with Simvatin 10 mg. The usual starting dose is 10 mg/day given as a single dose in the evening. Adjustment of dosage, if required, should be made at intervals of not less than four weeks, to a maximum of 40 mg daily given as a single dose in the evening. If LDL-cholesterol levels fall below 2 mmol/L or total plasma cholesterol levels fall below 3.5 mmol/L, consideration should be given to reducing the dose of Simvatin 10 mg. In hypercholesterolemia, the recommended starting dose is 5-10 mg once a day in the evening and the recommended dosing range is 5-40 mg per day as a single dose in the evening. In patients with coronary heart disease and hypercholesterolemia, the starting dose should be 20 mg once a day in the evening. Because Simvatin 10 mg does not undergo significant renal excretion, modification of dosage should not be necessary in patients with renal insufficiency. Safety and effectiveness in children and adolescents have not been established.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Simvatin 10 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Digoxin</strong>: Concomitant administration of Simvatin 10 mg and Digoxin in normal volunteers resulted in a slight elevation (less than 0.3 µgm/ml) in drug concentrations in plasma compared to concomitant administration of placebo and Digoxin.</p><p> <strong>Coumarin derivatives</strong>: Slightly enhance the anticoagulant effect of Warfarin (mean changes in prothrombin time less than two seconds) in normal volunteers maintained in a state of low therapeutic anticoagulation.</p><p> <strong>Others</strong>: In clinical studies, Simvatin 10 mg was used concomitantly with ACE inhibitors, beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, diuretics, and NSAIDs without evidence of clinically significant adverse interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Simvatin 10 mg should not be used in:</p><ul><li>Active liver disease</li><li>Pregnant and breastfeeding mothers</li><li>Women of childbearing age unless they have been adequately protected by contraception</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the preparation</li><li>Patients with the homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia who have a complete absence of LDL receptors</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Simvatin 10 mg</strong></p><p> Simvatin 10 mg is generally well tolerated. Headache, fatigue, insomnia, gastrointestinal effects like nausea, constipation or diarrhea, flatulence, dyspepsia, abdominal cramps, and muscular effects like myalgia, myositis, and myopathy have been reported. Rare cases of rhabdomyolysis with acute renal failure secondary to myoglobinuria have been associated with Simvatin 10 mg therapy. Hepatitis, pancreatitis, rash, and Angio-oedema have also been reported. No potentially life-threatening effects have been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> <strong>Category X</strong>: Studies in animals or human beings have demonstrated fetal abnormalities or there is evidence of fetal risk based on human experience or both, and the risk of the use of the drug in pregnant women clearly outweighs any possible benefit. The drug is contraindicated in women who are or may become pregnant.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> If there is a history of liver disease or high alcohol consumption, liver function tests should be done before and during treatment. If serum transaminase rises three times the upper limit of normal, treatment should be discontinued. Avoid pregnancy during and for one month after treatment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Simvatin 10 mg</strong></p><p> There are no data available on overdose. No antidote is available. General measures should be adopted and liver function should be monitored.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place, away from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs, Statins</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Simvatin 10 mg is a preparation of Simvatin 10 mg which acts as a cholesterol-lowering agent. The main mechanism of reduction of low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol is that following inhibition of HMG-CoA reductase activity, the LDL receptor density on the liver cells is increased and this leads to an increased removal of LDL cholesterol from the plasma and increased catabolism of LDL cholesterol. In addition, there is a reduction in the very low-density lipoprotein (VLDL) cholesterol and reduced formation of LDL from VLDL. Simvatin 10 mg is extensively metabolized in the liver; which is also the main site of action of the drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Category X: Studies in animals or human beings have demonstrated fetal abnormalities or there is evidence of fetal risk based on human experience or both, and the risk of the use of the drug in pregnant women clearly outweighs any possible benefit. The drug is contraindicated in women who are or may become pregnant.</p>', 'active', '1551_1742290038.jpeg', 5, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 14:27:18', '2025-03-18 14:27:18'),
(3292, 'Natoph', 'Natamycin', 100.00, 1, 'bottle', '1 bottle', 'Drop', '5 parcent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Natoph 5%</strong></p><p> Natoph 5% sterile ophthalmic suspension is indicated for the treatment of fungal blepharitis, conjunctivitis, and keratitis caused by susceptible organisms, including <em>Fusarium solani</em>.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic antibacterial drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Natoph 5% is a tetraene polyene antibiotic derived from <em>Streptomyces natalensis</em>. It probably exerts its antifungal effects by binding to sterols in the fungal cell membrane, causing a change in membrane permeability that allows the loss of essential cellular constituents. It possesses <em>in vitro</em> activity against a variety of yeast and filamentous fungi, including <em>Candida, Aspergillus, Cephalosporium, Fusarium,</em> and <em>Penicillium</em>. Although its antifungal activity is dose-related, Natoph 5% is predominantly fungicidal. Topical administration produces effective concentrations of Natoph 5% within the corneal stroma but not in intraocular fluid.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Natoph 5%</strong></p><p> For <strong>fungal keratitis</strong>: The preferred initial dosage is 1 drop instilled in the conjunctival sac(s) at 1-2 hour intervals. The frequency of application can usually be reduced to 1 drop 6-8 times daily after the first 3-4 days. Therapy should generally be continued for 14 to 21 days or until active fungal keratitis resolves. In many cases, it may be helpful to gradually reduce the dosage at 4 to 7-day intervals to ensure the replicating fungus has been eliminated.</p><p>For <strong>fungal blepharitis &amp; conjunctivitis</strong>: A less frequent initial dosage of 1 drop 4-6 times daily may be sufficient.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Natoph 5%</strong></p><p> May increase the spread of fungal eye infection when used with topical corticosteroids.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Natoph 5% ophthalmic suspension is contraindicated in individuals with a history of hypersensitivity to any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Natoph 5%</strong></p><p> Eye irritation, eye discomfort, eye edema, conjunctival chemosis, and hyperemia have been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> <strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: Natoph 5% sterile ophthalmic suspension should be given to a pregnant woman only if clearly needed.</p><p> <strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Natoph 5% is administered to a nursing mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> For topical eye use only. Failure to improve keratitis after 7-10 days of administration suggests that the infection may be caused by a microorganism not susceptible to Natoph 5%.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place. Protect from light. Do not freeze. Do not use for longer than one month after first opening the bottle.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> <strong>Pediatric</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic antibacterial drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Natoph 5% is a tetraene polyene antibiotic derived from <em>Streptomyces natalensis</em>. It probably exerts its antifungal effects by binding to sterols in the fungal cell membrane, causing a change in membrane permeability that allows the loss of essential cellular constituents. It possesses <em>in vitro</em> activity against a variety of yeast and filamentous fungi, including <em>Candida, Aspergillus, Cephalosporium, Fusarium,</em> and <em>Penicillium</em>. Although its antifungal activity is dose-related, Natoph 5% is predominantly fungicidal. Topical administration produces effective concentrations of Natoph 5% within the corneal stroma but not in intraocular fluid.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p>', 'active', '193_1742293073.jpg', 30, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 15:17:53', '2025-03-18 15:17:53'),
(3293, 'Olanap ODT', 'Olanzapine', 4.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Olanap ODT 10 mg</strong></p><p> Olanap ODT 10 mg is indicated for the acute and maintenance treatment of schizophrenia and related psychoses, where positive symptoms (e.g., delusions, hallucinations, disordered thinking, hostility, and suspiciousness) and/or negative symptoms (e.g., flattened affect, emotional and social withdrawal, poverty of speech) are prominent.</p><p>Olanap ODT 10 mg is also indicated for the treatment of acute manic or mixed episodes in bipolar disorder, with or without psychotic features and with or without a rapid cycling course.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Atypical neuroleptic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Olanap ODT 10 mg is an antipsychotic agent with affinities for serotonin (5HT2A/2C, 5HT3, 5HT6), dopamine (D1, D2, D3, D4, D5), cholinergic muscarinic receptors (M1-M5), α1-adrenergic, and histamine H1 receptors. The exact mechanism of action in schizophrenia is unknown, but it is believed to work through a combination of dopamine and serotonin type 2 (5HT2) antagonism.</p><p>Olanap ODT 10 mg is well absorbed after oral administration, reaching peak plasma concentrations within 5 to 8 hours. Absorption is not affected by food. It is not mutagenic, clastogenic, or carcinogenic.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Olanap ODT 10 mg</strong></p><p> The recommended starting dose is <strong>10 mg/day</strong>, administered as a single daily dose without regard to meals. Dosage may be adjusted based on individual clinical response within a range of <strong>5-20 mg daily</strong>. An increase beyond <strong>10 mg/day</strong> (to <strong>15 mg/day or greater</strong>) should only be done after clinical reassessment.</p><p>For patients with factors that may result in slower metabolism (e.g., female gender, geriatric age, non-smoking status), a lower starting dose should be considered, and dose escalation should be conservative.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Olanap ODT 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Schizophrenia, combination therapy for mania, bipolar disorder</strong>: Initial dose <strong>5-10 mg once daily</strong>. Dosage may be adjusted up to <strong>20 mg daily</strong>, with increases beyond <strong>10 mg/day</strong> only after reassessment.</li><li><strong>Monotherapy for mania</strong>: Initially <strong>15 mg once daily</strong>. Dosage may be adjusted within <strong>5-20 mg daily</strong>, but doses beyond <strong>15 mg/day</strong> should only be considered after reassessment.</li><li><strong>Maximum dose</strong>: <strong>20 mg daily</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Olanap ODT 10 mg</strong></p><p> Olanap ODT 10 mg can be taken with or without food. Gradual dose tapering should be considered when discontinuing the medication.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Olanap ODT 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>May <strong>antagonize the effects of levodopa and dopamine agonists</strong>.</li><li><strong>CYP1A2 or glucuronyl transferase inducers</strong> (e.g., omeprazole, rifampicin) may increase Olanap ODT 10 mg clearance.</li><li><strong>CYP1A2 inhibitors</strong> may reduce Olanap ODT 10 mg clearance.</li><li><strong>Carbamazepine</strong> may increase the clearance of Olanap ODT 10 mg.</li><li><strong>Activated charcoal</strong> reduces oral bioavailability by <strong>50-60%</strong>.</li><li>Caution is needed when combining Olanap ODT 10 mg with <strong>centrally acting drugs and alcohol</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Olanap ODT 10 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients with <strong>hypersensitivity</strong> to any ingredient of the product.</li><li>Patients at risk for <strong>narrow-angle glaucoma</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Olanap ODT 10 mg</strong></p><p> Very common side effects:</p><ul><li><strong>Somnolence</strong></li><li><strong>Weight gain</strong></li></ul><p>Other side effects:</p><ul><li><strong>Increased appetite, elevated glucose and triglyceride levels</strong></li><li><strong>Dizziness, akathisia, Parkinsonism, dyskinesia</strong></li><li><strong>Orthostatic hypotension</strong></li><li><strong>Mild and transient anticholinergic effects</strong> (e.g., constipation, dry mouth)</li><li><strong>Asthenia, edema, photosensitivity reactions</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Olanap ODT 10 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefits outweigh the risks. Patients should notify their physician if they become pregnant or plan to conceive during treatment. No reports of teratogenicity exist.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Patients should <strong>not breastfeed</strong> while taking Olanap ODT 10 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in <strong>patients with a history of seizures</strong> or conditions associated with seizures.</li><li>Caution is required in patients with:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Low leukocyte and/or neutrophil counts</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Drug-induced bone marrow depression</strong> (including those undergoing radiation therapy or chemotherapy)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Hypereosinophilic conditions</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Impaired hepatic function</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Use of hepatotoxic drugs</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Use of drugs that prolong the QT interval</strong> (especially in elderly patients)</li><li>Patients should be <strong>cautioned against operating hazardous machinery, including motor vehicles</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of <strong>children</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Not studied in individuals under <strong>18 years of age</strong>.</li><li><strong>Elderly (65 and over)</strong>: <strong>Starting dose: 5 mg/day</strong>.</li><li><strong>Patients with hepatic/renal impairment</strong>: <strong>Starting dose: 5 mg/day</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Atypical neuroleptic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Olanap ODT 10 mg is an antipsychotic agent with affinities for multiple receptors, including serotonin (5HT2A/2C, 5HT3, 5HT6), dopamine (D1-D5), cholinergic muscarinic (M1-M5), α1-adrenergic, and histamine H1. Its mechanism of action in schizophrenia is believed to involve <strong>dopamine and serotonin type 2 (5HT2) antagonism</strong>.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Olanap ODT 10 mg has <strong>not</strong> been studied in subjects under <strong>18 years of age</strong>.</li><li><strong>Elderly patients (65 and over)</strong>: <strong>Starting dose: 5 mg/day</strong>.</li><li><strong>Patients with hepatic or renal impairment</strong>: <strong>Starting dose: 5 mg/day</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '348_1742293543.jpg', 2, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 15:25:43', '2025-03-18 15:25:43'),
(3294, 'Ziton', 'Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate', 30.00, 1, 'bottle', '1 bottle', 'Drop', '10 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p>Ziton is indicated for zinc deficiency and/or conditions that result in excessive zinc loss. Zinc deficiency may occur due to:</p><ul><li><strong>Inadequate diet</strong></li><li><strong>Malabsorption</strong></li></ul><p>Excessive zinc loss may occur in:</p><ul><li><strong>Trauma</strong></li><li><strong>Burns</strong></li><li><strong>Diarrhea</strong></li><li><strong>Protein-losing conditions</strong></li></ul><p>A zinc supplement should be continued until clinical improvement occurs. In cases of <strong>severe malabsorption, metabolic disease, or persistent zinc-losing conditions</strong>, long-term supplementation may be necessary.</p><blockquote><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></blockquote><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Zinc sulfate monohydrate is an <strong>essential trace element</strong> involved in numerous enzyme systems. The body requires zinc for:</p><ul><li><strong>Normal growth and development</strong></li><li><strong>Sexual maturation and reproduction</strong></li><li><strong>Dark vision adaptation</strong></li><li><strong>Olfactory (smell) and gustatory (taste) activity</strong></li><li><strong>Insulin storage and release</strong></li><li><strong>Immune system function</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Zinc deficiency</strong> may lead to:</p><ul><li>Impaired immune function</li><li>Delayed wound healing</li><li>Reduced sense of taste and smell</li><li>Increased susceptibility to infections</li><li>Poor night vision</li><li>Increased risk of miscarriage</li><li>Hair loss (alopecia)</li><li>Mental lethargy</li><li>Skin changes</li><li>Poor reproductive organ development</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage</strong></h3><h4><strong>Children</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Under 10 kg</strong>: <strong>5 ml (1 teaspoonful) twice daily after food</strong></li><li><strong>10-30 kg</strong>: <strong>10 ml (2 teaspoonfuls) 1-3 times daily after food</strong></li></ul><h4><strong>Adults &amp; Children Over 30 kg</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>20 ml (4 teaspoonfuls) 1-3 times daily after food</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Best taken on an empty stomach</strong> (<strong>at least 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals</strong>) for optimal absorption. However, if it causes stomach upset, it may be taken with food.</p><blockquote><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></blockquote><h3><strong>Administration</strong></h3><h4><strong>For Dispersible Tablet:</strong></h4><ol><li>Place the tablet in a teaspoon.</li><li>Add an adequate amount of water.</li><li>Allow the tablet to dissolve completely.</li><li>Give the entire spoonful solution.</li></ol><blockquote><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></blockquote><h3><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Tetracyclines &amp; Zinc</strong>: May decrease absorption of both.</li><li><strong>Quinolone antibiotics &amp; Zinc</strong>: May decrease absorption of both.</li><li><strong>Penicillamine &amp; Zinc</strong>: May decrease zinc absorption.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><p>Ziton is contraindicated in individuals with <strong>hypersensitivity</strong> to any of its ingredients.</p><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p>Possible side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Nausea</strong></li><li><strong>Vomiting</strong></li><li><strong>Diarrhea</strong></li><li><strong>Stomach upset</strong></li><li><strong>Heartburn</strong></li><li><strong>Gastritis</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>The safety of Ziton in pregnancy has <strong>not been established</strong>.</li><li><strong>Zinc crosses the placenta</strong> and is present in breast milk.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>In <strong>acute renal failure</strong>, zinc may <strong>accumulate in the body</strong>, requiring <strong>dose adjustment</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Specific mineral preparations</strong></li><li><strong>Vaccines, Anti-sera &amp; Immunoglobulin</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Keep in a <strong>dry place</strong>, away from <strong>light and heat</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of reach of <strong>children</strong>.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '1342_1742293865.webp', 9, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 15:31:05', '2025-05-13 07:27:08');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3295, 'Lucon', 'Fluconazole', 8.06, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Lucon is indicated in:</p><ul><li><strong>Vaginal Candidiasis</strong></li><li><strong>Oropharyngeal Candidiasis</strong></li><li><strong>Esophageal Candidiasis</strong></li><li><strong>Tinea corporis/Tinea cruris/Tinea pedis/Other Tinea</strong></li><li><strong>Kerion</strong></li><li><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong></li><li><strong>Onychomycosis</strong></li><li><strong>Invasive candidal infections and cryptococcal infections (including meningitis)</strong></li><li><strong>Prevention of cryptococcal meningitis</strong></li><li><strong>Prevention of fungal infections in immunocompromised patients</strong></li><li><strong>Systemic Candidiasis and Cryptococcal infection</strong></li><li><strong>Superficial Candidiasis</strong></li><li><strong>Systemic Candidiasis &amp; Cryptococcal infection</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Other indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Fungal urinary tract infections</li><li>Disseminated candidiasis</li><li>Prophylaxis for fungal infection in neutropenic cancer patients</li><li>Acute treatment of other systemic fungal infections such as coccidioidomycosis and histoplasmosis</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Fluconazole is a triazole antifungal agent. It is a potent inhibitor of fungal cytochrome P-450-dependent enzymes. Cytochrome P-450 enzyme system is an essential component of the fungal cell membrane, responsible for the synthesis of ergosterol.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vaginal Candidiasis:</strong> 150 mg as a single dose.</li><li><strong>Oropharyngeal Candidiasis:</strong> 200 mg on the 1st day followed by 100 mg daily for 14 days.</li><li><strong>Esophageal Candidiasis:</strong> 200 mg on the 1st day followed by 100 mg daily for 14-30 days.</li><li><strong>Tinea corporis/Tinea cruris/Tinea pedis/Other Tinea:</strong> 150 mg weekly for 4-6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Kerion:</strong> 50 mg daily for 20 days.</li><li><strong>Pityriasis versicolor:</strong> 400 mg as a single dose.</li><li><strong>Onychomycosis:</strong> 150 mg weekly for 12 months.</li><li><strong>Invasive candidal infections and cryptococcal infections (including meningitis):</strong> Orally or by IV infusion, 400 mg on the first day then 200-400 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Prevention of cryptococcal meningitis:</strong> Orally or by IV infusion 200 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Prevention of fungal infections in immunocompromised patients:</strong> Orally or by IV infusion, 50-400 mg daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Children (over 1 year):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Superficial Candidiasis:</strong> 1-2 mg/kg daily.</li><li><strong>Systemic Candidiasis &amp; Cryptococcal infection:</strong> 3-6 mg/kg daily.</li><li><strong>Serious life-threatening infections:</strong> Up to 12 mg/kg daily for children aged 5-13 years (Maximum 400 mg daily).</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage based on weight:</strong></p><ul><li>1 year (9 kg): ½ measuring spoonful</li><li>1-2 years (12 kg): 1 measuring spoonful</li><li>2-3 years (14 kg): 1½ measuring spoonful</li><li>3-4 years (16 kg): 2 measuring spoonful</li><li>4-6 years (20 kg): 2½ measuring spoonful</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Specific Populations</strong></p><p> <strong>Elderly patients:</strong> The normal dose should be used if there is no evidence of renal impairment.</p><p><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> No adjustment in single-dose therapy is required. In multiple-dose therapy, normal doses should be given on days 1 and 2, and thereafter dosage intervals should be modified:</p><ul><li><strong>Creatinine clearance (&gt;41 ml/min):</strong> Dosage interval 24 hours</li><li><strong>Creatinine clearance (21-40 ml/min):</strong> Dosage interval 48 hours</li><li><strong>Creatinine clearance (10-20 ml/min):</strong> Dosage interval 72 hours</li><li><strong>Patients receiving regular dialysis:</strong> One dose after every dialysis session</li></ul><p>Children may have more rapid renal clearance than adults, and doses up to 12 mg/kg are recommended.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li>Fluconazole may increase <strong>prothrombin time</strong> after warfarin administration. Monitoring is recommended.</li><li>It may <strong>prolong the half-life</strong> of sulfonylureas (chlorpropamide, glibenclamide, glipizide, tolbutamide), increasing the risk of hypoglycemia.</li><li>Hydrochlorothiazide increases plasma concentrations of fluconazole by <strong>40%</strong>, but no dosage change is necessary.</li><li><strong>Phenytoin levels</strong> may increase when taken with fluconazole.</li><li><strong>Rifampicin</strong> decreases fluconazole\'s AUC by <strong>25%</strong> and its half-life by <strong>20%</strong>, requiring a possible dose increase.</li><li><strong>Oral contraceptives</strong> (ethinyl estradiol, levonorgestrel) may have slight increases in levels, but no effect on efficacy.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin concentrations</strong> may increase with fluconazole, requiring monitoring.</li><li><strong>No significant absorption issues</strong> occur with food, cimetidine, antacids, or irradiation.</li><li>Fluconazole decreases <strong>theophylline clearance by 18%</strong>, increasing toxicity risk.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to <strong>fluconazole</strong> or related triazole compounds.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Nausea, abdominal discomfort, diarrhea, flatulence.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Rash (&lt;1% incidence).</li><li><strong>Very rare:</strong> Anaphylaxis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Fluconazole has shown adverse fetal effects in animals at high doses. <strong>Not recommended in pregnancy</strong> unless contraception is used.</li><li>It is excreted in breast milk, <strong>so it should not be used in nursing mothers</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with <strong>AIDS and cancer</strong> may have abnormal liver, renal, or blood function tests during treatment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic necrosis</strong> has been reported in patients with multiple medications or severe disease. If liver enzymes rise significantly, assess risks.</li><li><strong>Severe skin reactions</strong> (Stevens-Johnson Syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis) are rare but require discontinuation of fluconazole if serious rashes develop.</li><li><strong>Driving &amp; machinery use:</strong> No impairment is expected.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Normal dose unless renal impairment is present.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal impairment:CrCl &gt;40:</strong> Normal dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>CrCl 21-40:</strong> 48-hour interval or half normal daily dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>CrCl 10-20:</strong> 72-hour interval or one-third normal daily dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dialysis patients:</strong> One dose after every dialysis session</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Supportive measures and <strong>gastric lavage</strong> may be needed.</li><li><strong>Forced diuresis</strong> can help eliminate the drug faster.</li><li><strong>Hemodialysis (3-hour session)</strong> reduces plasma levels by <strong>50%</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for subcutaneous and systemic mycoses.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place away from <strong>light and heat</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of reach of <strong>children</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '288_1742294336.jpg', 62, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 15:38:56', '2025-03-18 15:38:56'),
(3296, 'Maxtra', 'amsulosin Hydrochloride', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.4 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Maxtra 0.4 mg</strong></h3><p>Maxtra 0.4 mg is indicated for the treatment of the signs and symptoms of <strong>Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH).</strong></p><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p>BPH / Urinary retention / Urinary incontinence</p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Tamsulosin is an <strong>antagonist of α1A-adrenoreceptors</strong> in the prostate. It binds selectively and competitively to <strong>postsynaptic α1A-adrenoreceptors</strong>, which convey smooth muscle contraction, thereby relaxing <strong>prostatic and urethral smooth muscle.</strong></p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li>The recommended dose of <strong>Tamsulosin</strong> is <strong>0.4 mg once daily.</strong></li><li>It should be administered <strong>approximately half an hour after the same meal each day.</strong></li><li>If patients <strong>fail to respond</strong> to the <strong>0.4 mg dose after two to four weeks</strong>, the dose can be <strong>increased to 0.8 mg once daily.</strong></li><li>If Tamsulosin administration is <strong>discontinued or interrupted</strong> for several days, therapy should be <strong>restarted with the 0.4 mg once daily dose.</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Increased plasma concentration</strong> with strong <strong>CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g. ketoconazole).</li><li><strong>Moderate CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g. erythromycin) and <strong>strong/moderate CYP2D6 inhibitors</strong> (e.g. paroxetine, terbinafine) <strong>may increase exposure</strong> of Tamsulosin.</li><li><strong>Increased plasma concentration</strong> with <strong>cimetidine.</strong></li><li><strong>Additive effect</strong> with other <strong>α-adrenergic blocking agents.</strong></li><li><strong>Concomitant use with PDE5 inhibitors</strong> may lead to <strong>symptomatic hypotension.</strong></li><li><strong>Decreased plasma concentration</strong> with <strong>furosemide.</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>History of orthostatic hypotension.</strong></li><li><strong>Severe hepatic impairment.</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Dizziness, abnormal ejaculation</strong></li><li>Less frequently (1-2%): <strong>headache, asthenia, postural hypotension, palpitations, and rhinitis</strong></li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal reactions</strong>: nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, constipation</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity reactions</strong>: rash, pruritus, urticaria</li><li><strong>Other rare effects</strong>: drowsiness, blurred vision, dry mouth, edema, syncope, angioedema, and priapism</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><p>Maxtra 0.4 mg is <strong>not indicated for use in women.</strong></p><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Not for use in women.</strong></li><li>Use with caution in <strong>severe renal impairment (CrCl &lt;10 mL/min).</strong></li><li><strong>Not recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Overdose may lead to <strong>hypotension.</strong></li><li><strong>Supportive cardiovascular measures</strong> are necessary.</li><li><strong>Restoration of blood pressure</strong> and <strong>normalization of heart rate</strong> may be achieved by keeping the patient <strong>in a supine position.</strong></li><li>If necessary, <strong>IV fluids</strong> may be administered.</li><li><strong>Gastric lavage</strong> and administration of <strong>activated charcoal</strong> may help reduce absorption in cases of significant overdose.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><p>Store between <strong>20-25°C.</strong></p><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Not indicated for pediatric use.</strong></li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1203_1742294568.jpg', 55, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 15:42:48', '2025-03-18 15:42:48'),
(3297, 'MB 12', 'Mecobalamin', 4.03, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of MB 12 500 mcg</strong></h3><p>MB 12 500 mcg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li><strong>Peripheral Neuropathies</strong></li><li><strong>Diabetic Neuropathy</strong></li><li><strong>Vertebral Syndrome</strong></li><li><strong>Nerve Compression Syndrome</strong></li><li><strong>Multiple Sclerosis</strong></li><li><strong>Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS)</strong></li><li><strong>Parkinson’s Disease</strong></li><li><strong>Alzheimer’s Disease</strong></li><li><strong>Diabetic Retinopathy</strong></li><li><strong>Entrapment Neuropathy</strong></li><li><strong>Drug-Induced Neuropathy</strong></li><li><strong>Megaloblastic Anemia due to Vitamin B12 Deficiency</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p>Drugs for <strong>Megaloblastic Anemia</strong></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>MB 12 500 mcg is the <strong>neurologically active form of Vitamin B12</strong>, a water-soluble vitamin.</p><ul><li>It acts as a <strong>cofactor in the enzyme methionine synthase</strong>, which facilitates the transfer of <strong>methyl groups</strong> for the regeneration of <strong>methionine from homocysteine</strong>.</li><li>In <strong>anemia</strong>, it increases <strong>erythrocyte production</strong> by promoting <strong>nucleic acid synthesis in the bone marrow</strong>, aiding in the <strong>maturation and division of erythrocytes</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><h4><strong>Tablet Form</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Usual adult dose</strong>: <strong>500 mcg, three times daily</strong>.</li><li><strong>Dosage adjustment</strong>: Based on <strong>age and severity of symptoms</strong>.</li></ul><h4><strong>Injection Form</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Peripheral Neuropathies</strong>: <strong>500 mcg intramuscularly (IM) or intravenously (IV), three times a week</strong>.</li><li><strong>Megaloblastic Anemia</strong>: <strong>500 mcg IM or IV, three times a week</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance Therapy</strong>: After <strong>two months</strong>, the dosage should be <strong>reduced to one ampoule (500 mcg) every 1-3 months</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Reduced GI absorption</strong> with <strong>neomycin, aminosalicylic acid, H2-blockers, and colchicine</strong>.</li><li><strong>Reduced serum concentration</strong> with <strong>oral contraceptives</strong>.</li><li><strong>Reduced effectiveness in anemia</strong> when used with <strong>parenteral chloramphenicol</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to any component of this product.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p>MB 12 500 mcg is <strong>generally well tolerated</strong>, but some side effects may include:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal discomfort</strong> (anorexia, nausea, diarrhea)</li><li><strong>Skin reactions</strong> (rash)</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Not recommended</strong> during <strong>pregnancy</strong> and <strong>lactation</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>If there is <strong>no response</strong> after <strong>prolonged use</strong>, discontinuation should be considered.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Oral Form</strong>: Store at <strong>room temperature</strong>, protected from <strong>moisture and light</strong>.</li><li><strong>Parenteral Form</strong>: Store at <strong>room temperature</strong>. <strong>Avoid direct light exposure</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: <strong>Not recommended</strong> for children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Class</strong></h3><p><strong>Drugs for Megaloblastic Anemia</strong></p><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Neurologically active</strong> form of Vitamin B12.</li><li>Acts as a <strong>cofactor</strong> in methionine synthase, aiding in <strong>homocysteine metabolism</strong>.</li><li><strong>Enhances erythrocyte production</strong> by stimulating <strong>nucleic acid synthesis</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '310_1742294815.webp', 5, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 15:46:55', '2025-03-18 15:46:55'),
(3298, 'Merotrax', 'Meropenem Trihydrate', 1300.00, 1, 'bottle', '1 bottle', 'Injection', '1 gm per vial', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p>Merotrax Injection is indicated for the treatment of the following infections in adults and children caused by bacteria sensitive to Merotrax:</p><ul><li><strong>Pneumonia</strong> and <strong>Nosocomial Pneumonia</strong></li><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections</strong></li><li><strong>Intra-abdominal Infections</strong></li><li><strong>Gynaecological Infections</strong> (e.g., endometritis, pelvic inflammatory disease)</li><li><strong>Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong></li><li><strong>Meningitis</strong></li><li><strong>Septicaemia</strong></li><li><strong>Pulmonary Infections</strong> in cystic fibrosis</li><li><strong>Empiric Treatment</strong> for presumed infections in patients with febrile neutropenia</li></ul><p><em>Note: Use as prescribed by a registered physician.</em></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Meropenem is a <strong>carbapenem antibiotic</strong> for parenteral use.</p><ul><li>It works by <strong>inhibiting bacterial cell wall synthesis</strong>, exerting <strong>bactericidal activity</strong>.</li><li>Meropenem is <strong>highly stable</strong> to serine beta-lactamases and has a strong affinity for <strong>Penicillin Binding Proteins (PBPs)</strong>.</li><li>It is effective against a <strong>broad spectrum of Gram-positive, Gram-negative, aerobic, and anaerobic bacteria</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><p><strong>Adults</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Usual dose</strong>: 500 mg to 1 g via <strong>intravenous (IV) administration every 8 hours</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pneumonia, UTIs, Gynaecological Infections (e.g., endometritis, pelvic inflammatory disease), Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>: 500 mg IV every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Nosocomial Pneumonias, Peritonitis, Presumed Infections in Neutropenic Patients, Septicaemia</strong>: 1 g IV every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intra-abdominal Infections</strong>: 500 mg to 1 g every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cystic Fibrosis</strong>: Up to <strong>2 g every 8 hours</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Meningitis</strong>: <strong>2 g IV every 8 hours</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Children (3 months to 12 years)</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>10 to 40 mg/kg IV every 8 hours</strong> depending on the infection type, pathogen susceptibility, and patient condition.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intra-abdominal Infections</strong>: 20 mg/kg every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cystic Fibrosis (4-18 years)</strong>: 25-40 mg/kg every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Meningitis</strong>: 40 mg/kg IV every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Febrile Neutropenia</strong>: 20 mg/kg every 8 hours.</li><li><strong>Children over 50 kg</strong>: Use adult dosage.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li>Merotrax should be administered via <strong>IV infusion over 15-30 minutes</strong> or <strong>IV bolus (5-20 ml) over 3-5 minutes</strong>.</li><li><em>Use as prescribed by a registered physician.</em></li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Probenecid</strong>: Competes with Merotrax for <strong>renal tubular secretion</strong>, increasing the elimination half-life and plasma concentration of Meropenem.</li><li><strong>Valproic Acid</strong>: Merotrax may reduce serum levels of <strong>valproic acid</strong>, potentially leading to sub-therapeutic levels.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to any component of this product.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p>Merotrax is generally well tolerated, but side effects may include:</p><ul><li><strong>Injection site reactions</strong>: Inflammation, thrombophlebitis, pain.</li><li><strong>Skin reactions</strong>: Rash, pruritus, urticaria.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal symptoms</strong>: Abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Neurological</strong>: Headache.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: There are no adequate studies in pregnant women, so use only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Meropenem may be excreted in breast milk, so caution is advised when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Discontinue Merotrax and take appropriate measures if an <strong>allergic reaction</strong> occurs.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Disease</strong>: Monitor <strong>transaminase</strong> and <strong>bilirubin levels</strong> in patients with liver conditions.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Dose adjustment is necessary for patients with <strong>creatinine clearance</strong> less than 51 ml/min.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment required for liver function impairment.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dose adjustment needed unless creatinine clearance is &lt;51 ml/min.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and efficacy in <strong>infants under 3 months</strong> have not been established.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>In case of overdose, <strong>symptomatic treatment</strong> should be administered.</li><li>In patients with <strong>renal impairment</strong>, hemodialysis can remove Merotrax and its metabolites.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Other Beta-lactam Antibiotics</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Reconstitution</strong></h3><p><strong>For IV Bolus Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li>Reconstitute <strong>500 mg or 1 g Merotrax</strong> with <strong>sterile water for injection</strong>. Shake well to obtain a <strong>clear and colorless or pale yellow</strong> solution.</li></ul><p><strong>For IV Infusion Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li>Merotrax can be directly constituted with a compatible infusion fluid and then diluted with <strong>50 to 200 ml</strong> of a compatible fluid.</li></ul><p><strong>Compatible Infusion Fluids</strong>:</p><ul><li>0.9% sodium chloride, 5% or 10% glucose, 5% glucose with sodium bicarbonate, mannitol, and several others.</li></ul><p><strong>Solution Stability</strong>:</p><ul><li>The freshly reconstituted solution is stable for <strong>up to 3 hours at room temperature</strong> or <strong>up to 13 hours at 5°C</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool, dry place</strong> at <strong>below 30°C</strong>, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '163_1742295272.jpg', 30, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 15:54:32', '2025-05-08 20:26:28'),
(3299, 'Moxaclav', 'Amoxicillin + Clavulanic Acid', 300.00, 1, 'x 1.2 gm vial', '1.2 gm vial', 'Injection', '(1 gm+200 mg)/20 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p>Moxaclav (co-amoxiclav) is indicated for short-term treatment of bacterial infections such as:</p><ul><li>Upper respiratory tract infections (tonsillitis, sinusitis, otitis media)</li><li>Lower respiratory tract infections (bronchitis, pneumonia)</li><li>Genito-urinary tract infections (cystitis, urethritis, pyelonephritis)</li><li>Skin and soft tissue infections</li><li>Bone and joint infections (osteomyelitis)</li><li>Other infections (septic abortion, puerperal sepsis, intra-abdominal sepsis)</li></ul><h3><strong>Composition</strong></h3><p>Moxaclav (1 gm + 200 mg)/20 ml contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Amoxicillin Sodium (equivalent to Amoxicillin 1 gm)</strong></li><li><strong>Clavulanate Potassium (equivalent to Clavulanic Acid 200 mg)</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Amoxicillin</strong> is a broad-spectrum antibiotic with activity against Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria.</li><li><strong>Clavulanic Acid</strong> inactivates beta-lactamase enzymes that could degrade amoxicillin, thereby extending its spectrum of activity.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></h3><h4>Oral Dosage:</h4><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 x 625 mg tablet every 12 hours or 1 x 375 mg tablet every 8 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe infections: 1 x 1 gm tablet every 12 hours or 1 x 625 mg tablet every 8 hours</li><li><strong>Children (6-12 years)</strong>: 2 teaspoonfuls of suspension every 8 hours</li><li><strong>Children (1-6 years)</strong>: 1 teaspoonful of suspension every 8 hours</li><li><strong>Children below 1 year</strong>: 25 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 8 hours</li></ul><h4>IV Injection:</h4><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: 1.2 gm every 6-8 hours</li><li><strong>Children (0-3 months)</strong>: 30 mg/kg every 8 hours</li><li><strong>Children (3 months to 12 years)</strong>: 30 mg/kg every 6-8 hours</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Common: Diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, rash, and candidiasis</li><li>Rare: Hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice, anaphylaxis, angioedema, and severe skin reactions (e.g., Stevens-Johnson Syndrome)</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to penicillin or any components of the product</li><li>History of penicillin-associated cholestatic jaundice</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>Pregnancy: Use only if necessary and prescribed by the physician</li><li>Lactation: Amoxicillin is detectable in breast milk, so caution is advised</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions</strong></h3><ul><li>Use cautiously in patients with renal or hepatic impairment</li><li>Monitor for crystalluria with high doses</li><li>Possible reduction in effectiveness of oral contraceptives</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below 25°C, away from light and moisture</li><li>After reconstitution, store in the refrigerator and use within 7 days</li></ul><h3><strong>Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Adjust dosage based on creatinine clearance</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Use with caution and monitor hepatic function regularly</li></ul><p>If you need further clarification on specific aspects, feel free to ask!</p><p>I prefer this response</p><p>ChatGPT</p><p>Response 2</p><h3><strong>Indications of Moxaclav (1 gm + 200 mg) / 20 ml</strong></h3><p>Moxaclav (Co-amoxiclav) is indicated for the short-term treatment of bacterial infections in the following sites:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper Respiratory Tract Infections (ENT)</strong>: Tonsillitis, sinusitis, otitis media</li><li><strong>Lower Respiratory Tract Infections</strong>: Acute and chronic bronchitis, lobar and bronchopneumonia</li><li><strong>Genito-Urinary Tract Infections</strong>: Cystitis, urethritis, pyelonephritis</li><li><strong>Skin and Soft Tissue Infections</strong></li><li><strong>Bone and Joint Infections</strong>: Osteomyelitis</li><li><strong>Other Infections</strong>: Septic abortion, puerperal sepsis, intra-abdominal sepsis</li></ul><h3><strong>Composition</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>375 mg tablet</strong>: Amoxicillin 250 mg and Clavulanic Acid 125 mg</li><li><strong>625 mg tablet</strong>: Amoxicillin 500 mg and Clavulanic Acid 125 mg</li><li><strong>1 gm tablet</strong>: Amoxicillin 875 mg and Clavulanic Acid 125 mg</li><li><strong>Powder for Suspension (Forte)</strong>: Amoxicillin 400 mg and Clavulanic Acid 57.5 mg per 5 ml reconstituted suspension</li><li><strong>Injection (1.2 gm)</strong>: Amoxicillin 1 gm and Clavulanic Acid 200 mg</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Co-amoxiclav is a combination of <strong>Amoxicillin</strong> and <strong>Clavulanic Acid</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>Amoxicillin</strong>: A broad-spectrum antibiotic effective against many Gram-positive and Gram-negative microorganisms. However, it is susceptible to degradation by beta-lactamases.</li><li><strong>Clavulanic Acid</strong>: A beta-lactamase inhibitor that protects Amoxicillin from degradation by beta-lactamase-producing bacteria, thus extending its spectrum of activity.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Oral Dosage</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: One 375 mg tablet 3 times a day. For severe infections, one 625 mg tablet 3 times a day or one 1 gm tablet twice a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6-12 years)</strong>: 2 teaspoonfuls of powder for suspension every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (1-6 years)</strong>: 1 teaspoonful every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children under 1 year</strong>: 25 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 8 hours.</li><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: 1.2 g every 6-8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (0-3 months)</strong>: 30 mg/kg every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (3 months to 12 years)</strong>: 30 mg/kg every 6-8 hours.</li></ul><h3><strong>Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Oral</strong>: Can be taken with or without food; however, it is recommended to take it at the start of meals to enhance absorption and reduce gastrointestinal intolerance.</li><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>: Administer over <strong>2 minutes</strong> for injection or via slow infusion over <strong>30 minutes</strong>. The reconstituted vial should be used within <strong>20 minutes</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Prolonged Bleeding Time</strong>: May be observed in some patients.</li><li><strong>Oral Contraceptives</strong>: May reduce efficacy.</li><li><strong>Allopurinol</strong>: Increases the risk of allergic skin reactions when used with Amoxicillin.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Penicillin Hypersensitivity</strong>: Should not be used in patients with a history of allergy to penicillin or any other beta-lactam antibiotics (e.g., cephalosporins).</li><li><strong>Previous History of Cholestatic Jaundice</strong>: Caused by Co-amoxiclav.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Diarrhoea, nausea, vomiting, candidiasis.</li><li><strong>Serious (rare)</strong>: Hepatitis, jaundice, skin reactions like urticaria, and severe reactions such as <strong>Stevens-Johnson syndrome</strong>, <strong>erythema multiforme</strong>, and <strong>exfoliative dermatitis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Allergic Reactions</strong>: Angioedema and anaphylaxis may occur, although rarely.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Use only if considered essential by the physician, as there are no major effects observed in humans, but limited data exist.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Trace amounts of Amoxicillin are excreted in breast milk, so caution is advised.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Anticoagulation Therapy</strong>: Use with care in patients on anticoagulants.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Dysfunction</strong>: Monitor hepatic function regularly.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Adjust dosage for patients with renal issues.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose may include gastrointestinal disturbances and electrolyte imbalance.</li><li>Can be removed from circulation by <strong>haemodialysis</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>25°C</strong>. Protect from light and moisture.</li><li><strong>Reconstituted suspension</strong>: Store in the refrigerator for up to <strong>7 days</strong>.</li><li><strong>Reconstituted vial</strong>: Use within <strong>20 minutes</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Adjust dosage for patients with impaired kidney function.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Caution is needed, and hepatic function should be monitored regularly.</li></ul>', 'active', '1416_1742295791.webp', 4, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 16:03:11', '2025-05-04 21:06:36'),
(3300, 'Prosol', 'Amino Acid, D-Sorbitol and Electrolytes', 500.00, 1, 'Bottle', '500 ml bottle', 'IV Infusion', '5 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Prosol 5%</strong></p><p> Amino acid is indicated as a source of amino acids for protein synthesis in patients needing intravenous nutrition. It is particularly suitable for patients with basal amino acid requirements. Amino acid is also indicated for faster recovery in surgery, burns, renal insufficiency, hepatic insufficiency, and effective management of cancer.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p> Each 100 ml solution (5%) contains:</p><p><strong>Essential Amino Acids</strong></p><ul><li>L-Isoleucine 0.352 gm</li><li>L-Leucine 0.490 gm</li><li>L-Lysine Hydrochloride 0.430 gm</li><li>L-Methionine 0.225 gm</li><li>L-Phenylalanine 0.533 gm</li><li>L-Threonine 0.250 gm</li><li>L-Tryptophan 0.090 gm</li><li>L-Valine 0.360 gm</li><li>L-Histidine 0.250 gm</li><li>L-Tyrosine 0.025 gm</li></ul><p><strong>Non-Essential Amino Acids</strong></p><ul><li>L-Arginine 0.500 gm</li><li>L-Aspartic Acid 0.250 gm</li><li>L-Glutamic Acid 0.075 gm</li><li>L-Alanine 0.200 gm</li><li>L-Cystine 0.010 gm</li><li>Glycine (Aminoacetic Acid) 0.760 gm</li><li>L-Proline 0.100 gm</li><li>L-Serine 0.100 gm</li></ul><p><strong>Excipients</strong></p><ul><li>D-Sorbitol 5.000 gm</li></ul><p><strong>Electrolytes</strong></p><ul><li>Sodium (Na+) 57 mmol/L</li><li>Potassium (K+) 25 mmol/L</li><li>Magnesium (Mg++) 1.2 mmol/L</li><li>Chloride (Cl-) 87 mmol/L</li><li>Acetate (CH3COO-) 25 mmol/L</li></ul><p><strong>Total Nitrogen Content</strong>: 7.25 gm/L</p><p> <strong>Total Energy Content</strong>: 1551 kj/L (371.14 Kcal/L)</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Parenteral nutritional preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> This IV solution is a sterile aqueous solution of crystalline Amino Acid and D-Sorbitol with electrolytes, necessary as the nitrogen source for parenteral nutrition. Nitrogen is provided in the form of essential and non-essential amino acids. This IV solution contains all 18 essential and non-essential amino acids needed for protein synthesis. The amino acid composition allows for a positive nitrogen balance in the postoperative period and during extended intravenous nutrition. The solution is clear, colorless to pale yellow, with a pH range of 5.0 to 7.0.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Prosol 5%</strong></p><p> The nitrogen requirement for maintaining body protein mass depends on the patient\'s condition (nutritional state and degree of metabolic stress):</p><ul><li><strong>No or minor metabolic stress and normal nutritional state</strong>: 0.10-0.15 g nitrogen/kg/day</li><li><strong>Moderate metabolic stress with or without malnutrition</strong>: 0.15-0.20 g nitrogen/kg/day</li><li><strong>Severe catabolism (e.g., burns, sepsis, trauma)</strong>: up to 0.20-0.25 g nitrogen/kg/day</li></ul><p>This dosage range corresponds to <strong>15-35 ml Amino acids IV/kg/day</strong>. In obese patients, the dose should be based on the estimated ideal weight. Depending on patient requirements, <strong>1000-2000 ml Amino acids IV may be infused per 24 hours</strong>. Amino acids IV should be infused slowly at rates of <strong>1.4-2.8 ml (30-60 drops) per minute</strong>.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Prosol 5%</strong></p><p> At the recommended dosage, amino acids have no pharmacological effects and are not expected to interact with other medications.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Amino acid is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Inborn errors of amino acid metabolism</li><li>Irreversible liver damage</li><li>Severe uremia when dialysis facilities are unavailable</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Prosol 5%</strong></p><p> Amino acid is usually well tolerated. Rarely, nausea may occur. Other possible side effects include:</p><ul><li>Vomiting, flushing, and sweating (at high infusion rates)</li><li>Transient liver test increases during intravenous nutrition</li><li>Hypersensitivity reactions</li><li>Thrombophlebitis when peripheral veins are used (risk reduced by simultaneous infusion of a 10% fat emulsion)</li><li>Hyperphenylalaninemia in severely ill, premature infants</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Successful and safe administration of amino acid solutions during pregnancy has been reported. However, animal reproduction studies have not been conducted. Amino acids should not be mixed with other preparations due to the risk of microbial contamination and incompatibility.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Hyperphenylalaninemia has been observed in severely ill, premature infants. Phenylalanine levels should be monitored, and the infusion rate adjusted as needed.</li><li>Do not use if the solution is turbid or contains particles.</li><li>Discard any unused portion.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Protect from light and store between <strong>15°C to 25°C</strong>. Avoid freezing. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> For children and infants, the recommended infusion rate is <strong>28-35 ml/kg body weight per day</strong>, with a stepwise increase in administration rate during the first week.</p>', 'active', '631_1742295909.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 16:05:09', '2025-03-18 16:05:09'),
(3301, 'Rivadel', 'Rivaroxaban', 15.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rivadel 10 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Rivadel 10 mg (2.5 mg):</strong> For the prevention of atherothrombotic events in adult patients after an Acute Coronary Syndrome (ACS) with elevated cardiac biomarkers (Troponin or CK-MB). It is co-administered with Aspirin alone or with Aspirin plus Clopidogrel or Ticlopidine.</p><p><strong>Rivadel 10 mg (10-20 mg):</strong></p><ul><li>To reduce the risk of stroke and systemic embolism in patients with nonvalvular atrial fibrillation</li><li>Treatment of Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) &amp; Pulmonary Embolism (PE) and reduction in the risk of recurrence of DVT and PE</li><li>Prophylaxis of DVT, which may lead to PE in patients undergoing knee or hip replacement surgery</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Oral Anti-coagulants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Rivadel 10 mg is a highly selective direct factor Xa inhibitor. Inhibition of factor Xa interrupts the intrinsic and extrinsic pathways of the blood coagulation cascade, thereby inhibiting thrombin formation. Rivadel 10 mg does not inhibit thrombin (activated factor II), and no effects on platelets have been demonstrated.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Rivadel 10 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Rivadel 10 mg (2.5 mg):</strong> The recommended dose is <strong>2.5 mg twice daily</strong>. Patients should also take:</p><ul><li><strong>Aspirin (75-100 mg daily)</strong> OR</li><li><strong>Aspirin (75-100 mg daily) plus Clopidogrel (75 mg daily) or Ticlopidine (standard daily dose)</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Rivadel 10 mg (10-20 mg):</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Nonvalvular Atrial Fibrillation:</strong>Creatinine Clearance &gt;50 mL/min: <strong>20 mg once daily with evening meal</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Creatinine Clearance 15-50 mL/min: <strong>15 mg once daily with evening meal</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of DVT &amp; PE:15 mg twice daily with food</strong> for the first 21 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">After the initial treatment, <strong>20 mg once daily with food</strong></li><li><strong>Prevention of recurrent DVT &amp; PE:</strong> <strong>20 mg once daily with food</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of DVT:Hip replacement surgery:</strong> 10 mg once daily for 35 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Knee replacement surgery:</strong> 10 mg once daily for 12 days</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Rivadel 10 mg</strong></p><p> May be taken with or without food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Rivadel 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant use with <strong>P-gp and CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., ketoconazole, ritonavir, clarithromycin, erythromycin, fluconazole, diltiazem, verapamil, dronedarone) increases Rivadel 10 mg exposure and pharmacodynamic effects, so it should be avoided.</li><li>Co-administration with <strong>P-gp and strong CYP3A4 inducers</strong> (e.g., rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine) decreases Rivadel 10 mg efficacy and should be avoided.</li><li>The use of <strong>anti-platelet agents, heparin, fibrinolytic therapy, and NSAIDs</strong> may increase the risk of bleeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Rivadel 10 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients with hypersensitivity to Rivadel 10 mg or its excipients</li><li>Patients with active pathological bleeding</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Rivadel 10 mg</strong></p><p> The most common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Increased risk of bleeding</li><li>Spinal or epidural hematoma</li><li>Increased risk of stroke after discontinuation in nonvalvular atrial fibrillation</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>There are no adequate or well-controlled studies of Rivadel 10 mg in pregnant women, and dosing for pregnant women has not been established.</li><li>Safety and efficacy in breastfeeding women have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Early discontinuation of Rivadel 10 mg, without adequate alternative anticoagulation, increases the risk of thrombotic events.</li><li>Rivadel 10 mg increases the risk of fatal bleeding, especially in patients with:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Bleeding disorders</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncontrolled severe arterial hypertension</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gastrointestinal diseases (e.g., inflammatory bowel disease, esophagitis, gastritis, GERD)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vascular retinopathy, bronchiectasis, history of pulmonary bleeding</li><li>In neuraxial anesthesia (spinal/epidural anesthesia) or spinal puncture, <strong>epidural or spinal hematoma</strong> can occur, requiring monitoring for neurological impairment.</li><li>Not recommended for patients with <strong>pulmonary embolism with hemodynamic instability or requiring thrombolysis or pulmonary embolectomy</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rivadel 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose may lead to <strong>hemorrhage</strong>.</li><li><strong>Absorption is limited at doses &gt;50 mg</strong>, so systemic exposure does not increase further.</li><li><strong>No specific antidote</strong> is available.</li><li><strong>Activated charcoal</strong> may be considered to reduce absorption in case of overdose.</li><li>Partial reversal of anticoagulation may be achieved with plasma products.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a <strong>cool (below 30°C) &amp; dry place</strong>, protected from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Oral Anti-coagulants</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Rivadel 10 mg is a highly selective direct <strong>factor Xa inhibitor</strong>. By inhibiting factor Xa, it disrupts the <strong>intrinsic and extrinsic coagulation pathways</strong>, preventing thrombin formation. Rivadel 10 mg does <strong>not</strong> inhibit thrombin (activated factor II) and has no effect on platelets.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Rivadel 10 mg is classified as a <strong>pregnancy category C drug</strong>.</li><li>There are no adequate studies in pregnant women.</li><li>It is <strong>unknown</strong> whether Rivadel 10 mg is excreted in human milk.</li><li>Safety and efficacy in breastfeeding women have not been established.</li></ul>', 'active', '879_1742296166.jpg', 33, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 16:09:26', '2025-03-18 16:09:26');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3302, 'Lucon', 'Fluconazole', 22.15, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '150 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Lucon 150 mg</strong></h3><p>Lucon 150 mg, a member of the triazole class of antifungal agents, is a potent and selective inhibitor of fungal enzymes necessary for the synthesis of ergosterol. It is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Vaginal candidiasis</strong> (acute or recurrent)</li><li><strong>Candidal balanitis</strong> (treatment of symptomatic partners)</li><li><strong>Mucosal candidiasis</strong>, including:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oropharyngeal</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Esophageal</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Noninvasive bronchopulmonary infections</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Candiduria</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mucocutaneous and chronic oral atrophic candidiasis (denture sore mouth)</li><li><strong>Tinea infections</strong>, including:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tinea pedis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tinea cruris</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tinea versicolor</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dermal candidiasis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nail fungal infections</li><li><strong>Systemic candidiasis</strong>, including:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Candidaemia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Disseminated candidiasis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Invasive candidal infections (peritoneum, endocardium, pulmonary, and urinary tracts)</li><li><strong>Cryptococcosis</strong>, including:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Cryptococcal meningitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pulmonary and cutaneous infections</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance therapy for AIDS patients to prevent relapse</li><li><strong>Prevention of fungal infections</strong> in immunocompromised patients, such as those undergoing bone marrow transplantation, chemotherapy, or radiotherapy</li><li><strong>Other uses</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fungal urinary tract infections</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Disseminated candidiasis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prophylaxis for fungal infections in neutropenic cancer patients</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute treatment of systemic fungal infections like coccidioidomycosis and histoplasmosis</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses</p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Lucon 150 mg is a triazole antifungal agent that inhibits fungal cytochrome P-450 enzymes, which are essential for the synthesis of ergosterol in fungal cell membranes.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><h4><strong>Adults (Oral)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Vaginal candidiasis:</strong> 150 mg as a single dose</li><li><strong>Oropharyngeal candidiasis:</strong> 200 mg on the first day, followed by 100 mg once daily for at least 2 weeks</li><li><strong>Esophageal candidiasis:</strong> 200 mg on the first day, followed by 100 mg once daily for at least 3 weeks</li><li><strong>Systemic candida infections:</strong> Up to 400 mg daily</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections and peritonitis caused by candida:</strong> 50-200 mg daily</li><li><strong>Cryptococcal meningitis:</strong> 400 mg on the first day, followed by 200 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis in bone marrow transplant patients:</strong> 400 mg once daily</li></ul><h4><strong>Children (Oral)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Mucosal candidiasis:</strong> 3-6 mg/kg daily</li><li><strong>Systemic candidiasis &amp; cryptococcal infections:</strong> 6-12 mg/kg daily</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis in immunocompromised patients:</strong> 3-12 mg/kg daily</li></ul><h4><strong>Intravenous (IV) Administration</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 400 mg initially, then 200 mg daily (may increase to 400 mg if necessary)</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> 6-12 mg/kg daily (adjusted based on neonate age)</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li>Increases <strong>prothrombin time</strong> when taken with <strong>warfarin</strong> (monitor closely)</li><li>Prolongs <strong>oral sulfonylurea half-life</strong> (monitor for hypoglycemia in diabetic patients)</li><li>Increases plasma levels of <strong>phenytoin</strong></li><li>Decreases <strong>fluconazole AUC</strong> when taken with <strong>rifampicin</strong></li><li>May increase <strong>cyclosporin concentrations</strong> (monitor levels)</li><li>No significant effects with <strong>food, cimetidine, antacids, or bone marrow irradiation</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to <strong>fluconazole</strong> or other <strong>triazole antifungals</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Nausea, abdominal discomfort, diarrhea, flatulence</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Rash, anaphylaxis</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category C – should be avoided unless benefits outweigh risks</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Found in breast milk; not recommended for nursing mothers</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Monitor <strong>liver enzymes</strong>, especially in AIDS or cancer patients</li><li>Rare cases of <strong>hepatic necrosis</strong> in patients with multiple medications</li><li>Risk of <strong>Stevens-Johnson Syndrome</strong> and <strong>toxic epidermal necrolysis</strong> in AIDS patients</li><li><strong>Driving &amp; machinery use</strong>: No known impairment</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Management:</strong> Symptomatic treatment, gastric lavage if necessary</li><li><strong>Elimination:</strong> Forced diuresis and hemodialysis can reduce plasma levels</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>dry place</strong> away from <strong>light and heat</strong></li><li>Keep out of reach of <strong>children</strong></li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '728_1742296208.jpg', 62, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 16:10:08', '2025-03-18 16:10:08'),
(3303, 'Toroaid', 'Ketorolac Tromethamine', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Toroaid 10 mg</strong></p><p> Toroaid 10 mg is indicated for the short-term management of moderate to severe acute post-operative pain.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-Opioid Analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Toroaid 10 mg is a potent analgesic of the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). It acts by inhibiting the cyclooxygenase enzyme system, thereby inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis. It demonstrates minimal anti-inflammatory effects at its analgesic dose.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Toroaid 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong> Recommended dose is <strong>10 mg every 4-6 hours</strong>. It should be used <strong>short-term only (up to 7 days)</strong> and is not recommended for chronic use. Doses exceeding <strong>40 mg/day</strong> are not recommended.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> Ketorolac injection may be used as a single or multiple doses on a regular or as-needed schedule for <strong>moderately severe, acute pain</strong> requiring opioid-level analgesia, usually in a postoperative setting.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV bolus:</strong> Must be given <strong>over no less than 15 seconds</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IM administration:</strong> Should be given <strong>slowly and deeply into the muscle</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Onset of analgesia:</strong> Within <strong>30 minutes</strong>, with peak effect in <strong>1 to 2 hours</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duration of effect:</strong> Usually <strong>4 to 6 hours</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Single-Dose Treatment</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IM Dosing (Adults):Patients &lt;65 years:</strong> 60 mg single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Patients &gt;65 years, renally impaired, or &lt;50 kg:</strong> 30 mg single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV Dosing (Adults):Patients &lt;65 years:</strong> 30 mg single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Patients &gt;65 years, renally impaired, or &lt;50 kg:</strong> 15 mg single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV or IM Dosing (Children 2-16 years):IM:</strong> 1 mg/kg (max 30 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV:</strong> 0.5 mg/kg (max 15 mg)</li></ul><p><strong>Multiple-Dose Treatment (IV or IM)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Patients &lt;65 years:</strong> 30 mg every 6 hours (max <strong>120 mg/day</strong>)</li><li><strong>Patients &gt;65 years, renally impaired, or &lt;50 kg:</strong> 15 mg every 6 hours (max <strong>60 mg/day</strong>)</li><li>For breakthrough pain, <strong>do not increase dose or frequency</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Conversion from Parenteral to Oral Therapy</strong></p><ul><li>Ketorolac tablets can be used alone or as a follow-on to parenteral Ketorolac.</li><li>The total combined daily dose (oral + parenteral) should not exceed:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>120 mg in younger adults</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>60 mg in elderly patients</strong></li><li>On subsequent days, oral dosing should <strong>not exceed 40 mg/day</strong>.</li><li><strong>Total duration of combined therapy (parenteral + oral) should not exceed 5 days</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Toroaid 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Other NSAIDs or Aspirin:</strong> Increases side effects of Ketorolac.</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants:</strong> Enhances anticoagulant effect.</li><li><strong>Beta Blockers:</strong> Reduces antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>ACE Inhibitors:</strong> Increases risk of renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate:</strong> Enhances toxicity of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Toroaid 10 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients with hypersensitivity to Ketorolac or other NSAIDs.</li><li>Children under <strong>16 years of age</strong>.</li><li>Use as a <strong>prophylactic analgesic before surgery</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Toroaid 10 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Nausea, vomiting, GI bleeding, melena, peptic ulcer, pancreatitis</li><li><strong>Neurological:</strong> Anxiety, drowsiness, headache</li><li><strong>General:</strong> Excessive thirst, fatigue</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular:</strong> Bradycardia, hypertension, palpitation, chest pain</li><li><strong>Reproductive:</strong> Infertility in females</li><li><strong>Respiratory:</strong> Pulmonary edema</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>US FDA Pregnancy Category C</strong>: Should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the <strong>potential benefits outweigh the risks</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use with caution in patients over 65 years</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Caution in patients with:</strong>Active or suspected peptic ulcer or GI bleeding</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Asthma</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Liver dysfunction</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a <strong>dry place</strong>, away from <strong>light and heat</strong>. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1980_1742296346.jpg', 37, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 16:12:26', '2025-03-18 16:12:26'),
(3304, 'Pifen', 'Pizotifen', 3.01, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pifen 0.5 mg</strong></p><p> Pifen 0.5 mg is indicated as a prophylactic treatment for vascular headaches of the migraine type, including classical migraine, common migraine, and cluster headaches. It is not effective in relieving migraine attacks once they have started.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-histamine Preparations, Other drugs for migraine</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Pifen 0.5 mg works by inhibiting the peripheral actions of serotonin and histamine, which increases the membrane permeability of cranial vessels and the transudation of plasmakinin while altering pain thresholds in migraines. By blocking 5-HT receptors, Pifen 0.5 mg reduces serotonin-induced cranial vasoconstriction, platelet function, and aggregation. It may also inhibit the peripheral actions of bradykinin. Additionally, Pifen 0.5 mg may inhibit serotonin reuptake by blood platelets, reducing extracranial artery distension. The appetite-stimulating effect of Pifen 0.5 mg may be due to its metabolic action rather than direct stimulation of the appetite center.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Pifen 0.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> Usually <strong>1.5 mg daily</strong>, taken as a single dose at night or in three divided doses. Dosage should be adjusted based on individual patient needs, up to a <strong>maximum of 4.5 mg daily</strong>. A single dose of up to <strong>3 mg</strong> may be given.</li><li><strong>Children (over 2 years):</strong> Up to <strong>1.5 mg daily</strong>, usually as a divided dose, though up to <strong>1 mg</strong> has been given as a single dose at night.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Pifen 0.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Enhances the central effects of <strong>sedatives, hypnotics, antihistamines (including cold preparations), and alcohol</strong>.</li><li><strong>Antagonizes the hypotensive effect</strong> of adrenergic neuron blockers.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Pifen 0.5 mg is contraindicated in patients with known <strong>hypersensitivity to any of its ingredients</strong>.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Pifen 0.5 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Appetite stimulation and weight gain</strong></li><li><strong>Drowsiness</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Limited data is available on the use of Pifen 0.5 mg in pregnancy, although no adverse effects have been reported. It should only be used if the <strong>potential benefit to the mother outweighs the potential risk to the fetus</strong>.</li><li>The safety of Pifen 0.5 mg during <strong>lactation</strong> has not been established, so <strong>breastfeeding mothers should avoid it</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in <strong>patients with urinary retention or renal impairment</strong>.</li><li><strong>Drowsiness may affect the ability to drive vehicles or operate machinery</strong>.</li><li><strong>Enhances the effects of alcohol</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a <strong>dry place</strong>, away from <strong>light and heat</strong>. Keep out of the <strong>reach of children</strong>.</p>', 'active', '1218_1742296552.jpg', 7, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 16:15:52', '2025-03-18 16:15:52'),
(3305, 'Novirax', 'Acyclovir', 14.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Novirax 200 mg</strong></p><p> Novirax 200 mg cream is a <strong>herpes simplex virus (HSV) nucleoside analogue DNA polymerase inhibitor</strong> indicated for the treatment of <strong>recurrent herpes labialis (cold sores)</strong> in immunocompetent adults and adolescents <strong>12 years of age and older</strong>.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Topical Antiviral Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Novirax 200 mg is an <strong>antiviral drug</strong> active against <strong>herpes simplex virus (HSV-1 and HSV-2)</strong>. It is a synthetic purine nucleoside analogue that selectively inhibits <strong>HSV thymidine kinase (TK)</strong>, leading to the formation of Novirax 200 mg triphosphate. This active form <strong>inhibits viral DNA replication</strong> through:</p><ol><li><strong>Competitive inhibition of viral DNA polymerase</strong></li><li><strong>Incorporation into and termination of the growing viral DNA chain</strong></li><li><strong>Inactivation of viral DNA polymerase</strong></li></ol><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Novirax 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Apply five times per day for four days.</strong></li><li>Treatment should begin as soon as possible at the <strong>first sign of herpes labialis symptoms</strong> (prodrome stage or lesion appearance).</li><li><strong>For adolescents (12 years and older), the dosage is the same as in adults.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Novirax 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No known drug interactions have been identified from <strong>topical or systemic administration</strong>.</li><li>Due to <strong>minimal systemic absorption</strong>, systemic drug interactions are <strong>unlikely</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Novirax 200 mg cream is contraindicated in patients with <strong>known hypersensitivity</strong> to <strong>Novirax 200 mg, valNovirax 200 mg, or any component of the formulation</strong>.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Novirax 200 mg</strong></p><p> The most common adverse reactions at the <strong>site of application</strong> include:</p><ul><li><strong>Dry lips, cracked lips, skin dryness, desquamation, burning, pruritus, flakiness, and stinging</strong>.</li><li>Less than <strong>1% of patients</strong> reported these side effects.</li><li><strong>Discontinuation due to adverse effects</strong>: 3 patients on Novirax 200 mg cream vs. 1 on placebo.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Should only be used if the <strong>benefits outweigh potential risks</strong>.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is <strong>unknown</strong> whether topically applied Novirax 200 mg is excreted in <strong>breast milk</strong>, but <strong>systemic absorption is minimal</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Apply <strong>only to the affected external lips and face</strong> in patients with herpes labialis.</li><li><strong>Do not apply to mucous membranes</strong> (inside the mouth, nose, or eyes).</li><li><strong>Not recommended for immunocompromised patients</strong> due to <strong>lack of data</strong>.</li><li><strong>May cause irritation upon contact with skin</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at <strong>room temperature (20°C to 25°C)</strong>.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safe for adolescents <strong>12 to 17 years</strong> (same dosage as adults). <strong>Not established</strong> for children under <strong>12 years</strong>.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Insufficient data to determine differences in response, but no identified age-related differences in clinical experience.</li><li><strong>Systemic absorption is minimal</strong> after topical administration.</li></ul>', 'active', '1737_1742296732.jpg', 9, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 16:18:52', '2025-03-18 16:18:52'),
(3306, 'Osartan-HZ', 'Losartan Potassium plus Hydrochlorothiazide', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg plus 12.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Osartan-HZ 50 mg + 12.5 mg</strong></p><p> This is indicated for the treatment of <strong>hypertension</strong>. It is also indicated to <strong>reduce the risk of stroke</strong> in patients with <strong>hypertension and left ventricular hypertrophy</strong>.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Combined Antihypertensive Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Losartan</strong> is an <strong>angiotensin II receptor antagonist</strong> that selectively blocks the vasoconstricting and aldosterone-secreting effects of angiotensin II by antagonizing its binding to <strong>AT1 receptors</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong> is a <strong>thiazide diuretic</strong> that inhibits the <strong>reabsorption of sodium (Na) in the distal tubules</strong>, leading to increased excretion of <strong>Na, water, potassium (K), and hydrogen ions</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Osartan-HZ 50 mg + 12.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension:</strong> Initial dose <strong>50/12.5 mg once daily</strong>. Titrate as needed up to <strong>100/25 mg once daily</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hypertensive Patients with Left Ventricular Hypertrophy:</strong> Start with <strong>50/12.5 mg</strong>. Increase to <strong>100/12.5 mg</strong> or <strong>100/25 mg</strong> if needed.</li><li><strong>Severe Hypertension:</strong> Start with <strong>50/12.5 mg once daily</strong>. Increase to <strong>100/25 mg</strong> if blood pressure is not adequately controlled after 2–4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Administration:</strong> Can be taken with or without food and in combination with other antihypertensive agents.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Osartan-HZ 50 mg + 12.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Losartan:</strong> No significant interactions with <strong>Hydrochlorothiazide, Digoxin, Warfarin, Cimetidine, or Phenobarbital</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Concomitant use of potassium-sparing diuretics</strong> (e.g., <strong>Spironolactone, Triamterene, Amiloride</strong>), <strong>potassium supplements</strong>, or <strong>salt substitutes</strong> may increase serum potassium levels.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>NSAIDs (e.g., Indomethacin)</strong> may reduce the antihypertensive effect of Losartan.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide:Alcohol, barbiturates, or narcotics</strong> may potentiate <strong>orthostatic hypotension</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Antidiabetic drugs</strong> may require dosage adjustments.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cholestyramine and colestipol resins</strong> impair Hydrochlorothiazide absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to <strong>Losartan, Hydrochlorothiazide, or sulfonamide-derived drugs</strong>.</li><li><strong>Anuria</strong> (severe kidney dysfunction).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Osartan-HZ 50 mg + 12.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Dizziness, symptomatic hypotension, hyperkalemia, vertigo, headache, sleep disturbances, rash, and gastrointestinal disturbances.</li><li><strong>Less Common:</strong> Angioedema, edema, dyspnea, palpitations, syncope, hepatitis, anemia, thrombocytopenia, myalgia, renal impairment, photosensitivity, vasculitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Should be <strong>discontinued immediately</strong> upon pregnancy detection due to risk of <strong>fetal toxicity</strong>.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> <strong>Thiazides appear in breast milk</strong>, and <strong>Losartan\'s excretion in milk is unknown</strong>. Either discontinue breastfeeding or the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Monitor serum electrolytes</strong> periodically to detect imbalances.</li><li><strong>Hypokalemia risk</strong> is higher with <strong>brisk diuresis, severe cirrhosis, or prolonged therapy</strong>.</li><li><strong>Use caution in renal impairment</strong> and <strong>symptomatic hypotension</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Osartan-HZ 50 mg + 12.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Losartan:</strong> Overdose symptoms include <strong>hypotension, tachycardia, or bradycardia (due to vagal stimulation)</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide:</strong> Overdose symptoms include <strong>electrolyte depletion (hypokalemia, hypochloremia, dehydration)</strong>, and <strong>cardiac arrhythmias (if digitalis is administered)</strong>. <strong>Hemodialysis is not effective for Losartan removal</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Store below 30°C</strong>. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness <strong>not established</strong> in children.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Can be used <strong>if creatinine clearance &gt;30 mL/min</strong>. For <strong>severe renal impairment</strong>, loop diuretics are preferred.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Not recommended as <strong>Losartan 25 mg titration is not possible in this combination</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Combined Antihypertensive Preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Losartan:</strong> Blocks <strong>angiotensin II\'s vasoconstrictive and aldosterone-secreting effects</strong> by selectively binding <strong>AT1 receptors</strong> in vascular smooth muscle and adrenal glands.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide:</strong> Inhibits sodium and chloride reabsorption, reducing plasma volume and increasing renin-angiotensin activity, which <strong>Losartan helps counteract</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Angiotensin II receptor antagonists should be <strong>avoided unless essential</strong>.</li><li>May cause <strong>fetal hypotension, renal dysfunction, and skull defects</strong>.</li><li><strong>Not recommended during breastfeeding</strong> due to limited safety data.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Can be used <strong>if creatinine clearance &gt;30 mL/min</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Not recommended as <strong>Losartan 25 mg titration is not possible</strong>.</li><li><strong>Safety in pediatric patients is not established</strong>.</li></ul>', 'active', '599_1742296957.jpg', 29, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 16:22:37', '2025-03-18 16:22:37'),
(3307, 'Peflacine', 'Pefloxacin Mesylate Dihydrate', 15.28, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Peflacine 400 mg</strong></p><p> Peflacine 400 mg is indicated for the treatment of single or mixed infections caused by two or more susceptible organisms. It can also be used for infections caused by organisms resistant to other antibiotics including aminoglycosides, penicillins, and cephalosporins.</p><p>Peflacine 400 mg is indicated for the treatment of the following infections caused by sensitive bacteria:</p><ul><li><strong>Severe systemic infection:</strong> Septicemia, bacteremia, peritonitis, infections in immunosuppressed patients with hematological or solid tumors, and in patients in intensive care units with specific problems such as infected burns.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infection:</strong> Uncomplicated and complicated urethritis, cystitis, pyelonephritis, prostatitis, epididymitis.</li><li><strong>Respiratory tract infection:</strong> Lobar and bronchopneumonia, acute and chronic bronchitis, acute exacerbation of cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis, empyema.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal infection:</strong> Enteric fever, infective diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Infections of the biliary system:</strong> Cholangitis, cholecystitis, empyema of the gall bladder.</li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infection:</strong> Infected ulcers, wound infections, abscesses, cellulitis, otitis externa, erysipelas, infected burns.</li><li><strong>Eye, ear, nose, and throat infection:</strong> Otitis media, sinusitis, mastoiditis, tonsillitis.</li><li><strong>Intra-abdominal infection:</strong> Peritonitis, intra-abdominal abscesses.</li><li><strong>Bone and joint infection:</strong> Osteomyelitis, septic arthritis.</li><li><strong>Pelvic infection:</strong> Salpingitis, endometritis, pelvic inflammatory diseases.</li><li><strong>Gonorrhea:</strong> Including urethral, rectal, and pharyngeal gonorrhea caused by β-lactamase producing organisms or organisms moderately sensitive to penicillin.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> 4-Quinolone preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> The bactericidal action of Peflacine 400 mg results from interference with the activity of the bacterial enzymes DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV, which are needed for the transcription and replication of bacterial DNA. DNA gyrase appears to be the primary quinolone target for gram-negative bacteria, while topoisomerase IV appears to be the preferential target in gram-positive organisms. Interference with these two topoisomerases results in strand breakage of the bacterial chromosome, supercoiling, and resealing, leading to inhibition of DNA replication and transcription.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Peflacine 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral:</strong> The usual dose is 400 mg twice daily (morning and evening) by mouth in most infections. Dosage should be adjusted for adults with hepatic insufficiency. Patients should take the drug with meals to avoid gastrointestinal disturbances.</li><li><strong>IV Infusion:</strong> 400 mg in 100 ml of 5% dextrose slow infusion over 1 hour twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Peflacine 400 mg</strong></p><p> Antacids may interfere with the absorption of Peflacine 400 mg, resulting in serum and urine levels lower than desired. Therefore, concurrent administration of these agents with Peflacine 400 mg should be avoided.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Peflacine 400 mg is contraindicated in patients who have known hypersensitivity to Peflacine 400 mg or other quinolones. It is also contraindicated in children and growing adolescents except where the benefits of treatment exceed the risks, in pregnant women, nursing mothers, and in patients with glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Peflacine 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal system:</strong> Nausea, diarrhea, vomiting, dyspepsia, abdominal pain.</li><li><strong>Central nervous system (CNS):</strong> Dizziness, headache, tiredness, confusion, convulsions.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity reactions:</strong> Skin rashes, pruritus.</li><li>Other reported reactions include joint pain, mild photosensitivity, and thrombocytopenia (at doses of 1600 mg daily).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy category:</strong> Not classified.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Avoid exposure to sunlight and ultraviolet radiation during treatment with Peflacine 400 mg because of the risk of photosensitivity reactions. Dosage readjustment is required in severe hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at room temperature. Keep away from excess heat and moisture.</p>', 'active', '1625_1742297128.jpg', 45, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 16:25:28', '2025-03-18 16:25:28'),
(3308, 'Veracal', 'Verapamil Hydrochloride', 30.00, 1, 'x 2 ml ampoule', '2 ml ampoule', 'IV Infusion', '5 mg/2 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Veracal 5 mg/2 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Verapamil Tablet:</strong> Essential hypertension, angina pectoris, prevention of re-infarction, supraventricular arrhythmias.</li><li><strong>Verapamil Injection:</strong> Tachycardias such as paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardias, atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response (except WPWS), atrial flutter with rapid conduction, extrasystoles, acute hypertension, acute coronary insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Other uses:</strong> Prophylaxis and/or therapy of ectopic arrhythmias (predominantly ventricular extrasystoles) in halothane anesthesia and in the application of adrenaline in halothane anesthesia.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Calcium-channel blockers</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Verapamil inhibits the entry of calcium ions into the slow channels or select voltage-sensitive areas of vascular smooth muscle and myocardium during depolarization. It relaxes coronary vascular smooth muscle, causing coronary vasodilation, increases myocardial oxygen delivery, and slows automaticity and AV node conduction.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Veracal 5 mg/2 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Verapamil Tablet:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dose should be individualized by titration and administered with food.</li><li><strong>For essential hypertension:</strong> Initial dose of 180 mg in the morning. If inadequate response, titration as follows:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">240 mg each morning.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">180 mg each morning plus 180 mg each evening.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">240 mg every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For angina:</strong> 80-120 mg three times a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For arrhythmias in digitalized patients:</strong> 240-360 mg in divided doses, depending on severity. Up to 180 mg/day in divided doses may occasionally be needed.</li><li><strong>Verapamil Injection:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong> 5 mg slowly IV in tachycardias and hypertensive crises, repeat after 5-10 minutes if necessary.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Drip infusion for maintenance:</strong> 5-10 mg/hour in physiological saline, glucose, laevulose, or similar solutions. Total dose up to 100 mg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Children:Newborns:</strong> 0.75-1 mg (0.3-0.4 ml)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Infants:</strong> 0.75-2 mg (0.3-0.8 ml)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Children (1-5 years):</strong> 2-3 mg (0.8-1.2 ml)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6-14 years):</strong> 2.5-5 mg (1-2 ml)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intravenous infusion in hypertensive crises:</strong> Initially 0.05-0.1 mg/kg/hour. If insufficient, increase dose at 30-60 min intervals, up to 1.5 mg/kg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Veracal 5 mg/2 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Increased plasma levels with CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., erythromycin, ritonavir), cimetidine.</li><li>Decreased plasma levels with CYP3A4 inducers (e.g., rifampicin), phenobarbital, sulfinpyrazone.</li><li>Increased risk of bleeding with aspirin.</li><li>Increased bradycardic and hypotensive effect with telithromycin.</li><li>Increased AV blocking effect with clonidine.</li><li>May increase plasma levels of cardiac glycosides (e.g., digoxin, digitoxin), β-blockers, α-blockers, immunosuppressants, lipid-lowering agents, colchicine, quinidine, carbamazepine, imipramine, glibenclamide, doxorubicin, midazolam, buspirone, almotriptan, theophylline.</li><li>May potentiate hypotensive effects with diuretics, antihypertensives, vasodilators.</li><li>Increased neurotoxic effect of lithium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Severe left ventricular dysfunction</li><li>Hypotension or cardiogenic shock</li><li>Sick sinus syndrome (except in patients with a functioning artificial ventricular pacemaker)</li><li>Second or third-degree AV block (except in patients with a functioning artificial pacemaker)</li><li>Atrial flutter or atrial fibrillation with an accessory bypass tract (e.g., Wolff-Parkinson-White, Lown-Ganong-Levine syndrome)</li><li>Hypersensitivity to Veracal 5 mg/2 ml</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Veracal 5 mg/2 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Digestive system:</strong> Constipation, nausea</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular system:</strong> Hypotension, edema, CHF, pulmonary edema, bradycardia, AV block</li><li><strong>Respiratory system:</strong> Upper respiratory tract infections</li><li><strong>Nervous system:</strong> Dizziness, headache, fatigue</li><li><strong>Skin:</strong> Rash, flushing</li><li><strong>Hepatic:</strong> Elevated liver enzymes</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Verapamil may cause fetal hypoxia associated with maternal hypotension.</li><li>Should not be administered IV during the first six months of pregnancy.</li><li>No data available for use in the first and second trimesters.</li><li>Should only be used in the final trimester if benefits outweigh risks.</li><li>Should not be administered IV during lactation. If needed, breastfeeding should be discontinued during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in 1st-degree AV block, bradycardia (&lt;50 beats/min), hypotension (&lt;90 mmHg systolic), atrial fibrillation/flutter with WPW syndrome, heart failure (compensate with cardiac glycosides/diuretics first).</li><li>May impair ability to drive or operate machinery, especially in the initial treatment stages and with alcohol consumption.</li><li>Slows alcohol elimination and prolongs its effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Veracal 5 mg/2 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Supportive treatment required.</li><li>Beta-adrenergic stimulation or parenteral calcium solution may increase calcium ion flux and help counteract overdose effects.</li><li>Verapamil cannot be removed by hemodialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Do not store above 30°C.</li><li>Keep away from light and out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Calcium-channel blockers</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Verapamil is an L-type calcium channel blocker with antiarrhythmic, antianginal, and antihypertensive properties. Immediate-release verapamil has a short duration of action requiring 3-4 doses daily, whereas extended-release formulations allow once-daily dosing. Due to its negative inotropic effects (decreasing myocardial contraction strength), it should not be used in patients with severe left ventricular dysfunction or hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, as it may exacerbate these conditions.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women.</li><li>Crosses the placental barrier and can be detected in umbilical vein blood during delivery.</li><li>Excreted in breast milk—breastfeeding should be discontinued if Verapamil is required.</li></ul>', 'active', '1173_1742297467.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 16:31:07', '2025-05-07 16:29:47'),
(3309, 'Naid', 'Naproxen Sodium', 7.52, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Naid 500 mg</strong></p><p> Naproxen is indicated for the relief of signs and symptoms of rheumatoid arthritis, osteoarthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, juvenile arthritis, tendonitis, bursitis, and acute gout. It is also indicated for the management of primary dysmenorrhea and pain.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for Osteoarthritis, Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Naproxen is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) with anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic properties. It is rapidly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and achieves 95% bioavailability.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Naid 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Naproxen TabletRheumatoid arthritis, osteoarthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis:</strong> The usual dose is 500-1000 mg daily in two divided doses after meals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Management of pain, primary dysmenorrhea, acute tendonitis &amp; bursitis:</strong> Recommended starting dose is 500 mg followed by 500 mg every 12 hours or 250 mg every 6-8 hours. The initial total daily dose should not exceed 1250 mg and thereafter, the total daily dose should not exceed 1000 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute gout:</strong> Recommended starting dose is 750 mg followed by 250 mg every 8 hours until the attack has subsided.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Naproxen SuspensionFor Juvenile rheumatoid arthritis:</strong> The usual dose for children over 2 years is 10 mg/kg/day given as two divided doses at 12-hour intervals. Therapy in children under 2 years of age is not recommended.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Naproxen Gel</strong>Is to be applied 2-6 times a day as required and is not recommended for use in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Naid 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ACE inhibitors:</strong> Diminish the antihypertensive effect of ACE inhibitors.</li><li><strong>Antacids &amp; Sucralfate:</strong> Delay the absorption of Naproxen.</li><li><strong>Aspirin:</strong> Increases adverse effects.</li><li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> Reduce the natriuretic effect of Furosemide and Thiazides.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate:</strong> Enhances the toxicity of Methotrexate.</li><li><strong>Warfarin:</strong> Increases the risk of GI bleeding.</li><li><strong>Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors (SSRI):</strong> Increase the risk of GI bleeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Naproxen is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Naproxen. It should not be given to patients who have experienced asthma, urticaria, or allergic-type reactions after taking aspirin or other NSAIDs. It is contraindicated for the treatment of perioperative pain in the setting of coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Naid 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Heartburn, abdominal pain, nausea, diarrhea, dyspepsia.</li><li><strong>Central Nervous System:</strong> Headache, vertigo, drowsiness.</li><li><strong>Dermatological:</strong> Pruritus (itching), purpura.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular:</strong> Edema, palpitation.</li><li><strong>Others:</strong> Visual disturbances, hearing disturbances.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> US FDA pregnancy category of Naproxen is C. So, Naproxen should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C temperature, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Drugs for Osteoarthritis, Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Naproxen is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) with anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic properties. It is rapidly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and achieves 95% bioavailability.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> US FDA pregnancy category of Naproxen is C. So, Naproxen should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p>', 'active', '543_1742297664.jpg', 56, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 16:34:24', '2025-03-18 16:34:24'),
(3310, 'Peflox', 'Pefloxacin Mesylate Dihydrate', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Peflox 400 mg</strong></p><p> Peflox 400 mg is indicated for the treatment of single or mixed infections caused by two or more susceptible organisms. It can also be used for infections caused by organisms resistant to other antibiotics, including aminoglycoside, penicillin, and cephalosporin. Peflox 400 mg is indicated for the treatment of the following infections caused by sensitive bacteria:</p><ul><li><strong>Severe systemic infection</strong>: Septicaemia, bacteraemia, peritonitis, infections in immunosuppressed patients with haematological or solid tumors, and in patients in intensive care units with specific problems such as infected burns.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infection</strong>: Uncomplicated and complicated urethritis, cystitis, pyelonephritis, prostatitis, epididymitis.</li><li><strong>Respiratory tract infection</strong>: Lobar and bronchopneumonia, acute and chronic bronchitis, acute exacerbation of cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis, empyema.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal infection</strong>: Enteric fever, infective diarrhoea.</li><li><strong>Infections of the biliary system</strong>: Cholangitis, cholecystitis, empyema of the gall bladder.</li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infection</strong>: Infected ulcers, wound infections, abscesses, cellulitis, otitis externa, erysipelas, infected burns.</li><li><strong>Eye, ear, nose and throat infection</strong>: Otitis media, sinusitis, mastoiditis, tonsillitis.</li><li><strong>Intra-abdominal infection</strong>: Peritonitis, intra-abdominal abscesses.</li><li><strong>Bone and joint infection</strong>: Osteomyelitis, septic arthritis.</li><li><strong>Pelvic infection</strong>: Salpingitis, endometritis, pelvic inflammatory diseases.</li><li><strong>Gonorrhoea</strong>: Including urethral, rectal, and pharyngeal gonorrhoea caused by β-lactamase producing organisms or organisms moderately sensitive to penicillin.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> 4-Quinolone preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> The bactericidal action of Peflox 400 mg results from interference with the activity of the bacterial enzymes DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV, which are needed for the transcription and replication of bacterial DNA. DNA gyrase appears to be the primary quinolone target for gram-negative bacteria. Topoisomerase IV appears to be the preferential target in gram-positive organisms. Interference with these two topoisomerases results in strand breakage of the bacterial chromosome, supercoiling, and resealing. As a result, DNA replication and transcription are inhibited.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Peflox 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral</strong>: The usual dose is 400 mg twice daily (morning and evening) by mouth in most infections. Dosage should be adjusted for adults with hepatic insufficiency. Patients should take the drug with meals to avoid gastrointestinal disturbances.</li><li><strong>IV Infusion</strong>: 400 mg in 100 ml of 5% dextrose slow infusion over 1 hour twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Peflox 400 mg</strong></p><p> Antacids may interfere with absorption of Peflox 400 mg, resulting in serum and urine levels lower than desired, so concurrent administration of these agents with Peflox 400 mg should be avoided.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Peflox 400 mg is contraindicated in patients who have known hypersensitivity to Peflox 400 mg or other quinolones. Peflox 400 mg is also contraindicated in children and growing adolescents except where the benefits of treatment exceed the risks, in pregnant women, nursing mothers, and in patients with glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Peflox 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal system</strong>: Nausea, diarrhoea, vomiting, dyspepsia, abdominal pain.</li><li><strong>Central nervous system (CNS)</strong>: Dizziness, headache, tiredness, confusion, convulsions.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity reactions</strong>: Skin rashes, pruritus.</li><li> Other reactions that have been reported include joint pain, mild photosensitivity, and thrombocytopenia (at doses of 1600 mg daily).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy category - not classified.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Avoid exposure to sunlight and ultraviolet radiation during treatment with Peflox 400 mg because of the risk of photosensitivity reactions. Dosage readjustment is required in severe hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at room temperature. Keep away from excess heat and moisture.</p>', 'active', '255_1742298000.jpg', 9, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 16:40:00', '2025-03-18 16:40:00');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3311, 'PEG-Epo', 'Pegylated Erythropoietin', 4000.00, 1, 'IV injection', '0.3 ml pre-filled syringe', 'IV Injection', '50 mcg per 0.3 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of PEG-Epo 50 mcg/0.3 ml</strong></p><p> Pegylated erythropoietin is indicated for the treatment of anemia associated with chronic kidney disease (CKD) in adult patients on dialysis and patients not on dialysis. It is not indicated for use:</p><ul><li>In the treatment of anemia due to cancer chemotherapy.</li><li>As a substitute for RBC transfusions in patients who require immediate correction of anemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of PEG-Epo 50 mcg/0.3 ml</strong></p><p> Initiate PEG-Epo 50 mcg/0.3 ml treatment when the hemoglobin level is less than 10 g/dL. If the hemoglobin level approaches or exceeds 11 g/dL, reduce or interrupt the dose of PEG-Epo 50 mcg/0.3 ml. If the hemoglobin rises rapidly (e.g., more than 1 g/dL in any 2-week period), reduce the dose of PEG-Epo 50 mcg/0.3 ml by 25% or more as needed to reduce rapid responses. For patients who do not respond adequately, if the hemoglobin has not increased by more than 1 g/dL after 4 weeks of therapy, increase the dose by 25%.</p><p> For patients who do not respond adequately over a 12-week escalation period, increasing the PEG-Epo 50 mcg/0.3 ml dose further is unlikely to improve response and may increase risks.</p><ul><li><strong>For patients with CKD on dialysis</strong>: The recommended starting dose of PEG-Epo 50 mcg/0.3 ml for the treatment of anemia in adult CKD patients who are not currently treated with an ESA is 0.6 mcg/kg body weight administered as a single IV or SC injection once every two weeks. Once the hemoglobin has been stabilized, it may be administered once monthly using a dose that is twice that of the every two-week dose and subsequently titrated as necessary.</li><li><strong>For patients with CKD not on dialysis</strong>: The recommended starting dose of Pegylated erythropoietin for the treatment of anemia in adult CKD patients who are not currently treated with an ESA is 0.6 mcg/kg body weight administered as a single IV or SC injection once every two weeks. Once the hemoglobin has been stabilized, it may be administered once monthly using a dose that is twice that of the every two-week dose and subsequently titrated as necessary.</li><li><strong>Conversion from another ESA to Pegylated erythropoietin</strong>: It can be administered once every two weeks or once monthly to patients whose hemoglobin has been stabilized by treatment with an ESA.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of PEG-Epo 50 mcg/0.3 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Increased mortality, myocardial infarction, stroke, and thromboembolism.</li><li>Increased mortality and/or tumor progression in patients with cancer.</li><li>Hypertension.</li><li>Seizures.</li><li>Pure red cell aplasia.</li><li>Serious allergic reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Using ESA to target a hemoglobin level of &gt;11 g/dL increases the rate of serious adverse cardiovascular reactions. It should be used with caution in patients with co-existent cardiovascular disease, stroke, seizure, etc.</p><ul><li>Control hypertension prior to initiating and during treatment with PEG-Epo 50 mcg/0.3 ml.</li><li>Monitoring of patients for changes in seizure frequency or premonitory symptoms.</li><li>If severe anemia and low reticulocyte count develop during treatment, withhold it and evaluate patients for neutralizing antibodies to erythropoietin.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of PEG-Epo 50 mcg/0.3 ml</strong></p><p> PEG-Epo 50 mcg/0.3 ml overdosage can elevate hemoglobin levels above the desired level, which should be managed with discontinuation or reduction of PEG-Epo 50 mcg/0.3 ml dosage and/or with phlebotomy. Cases of severe hypertension have been observed following overdose with ESAs.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at 2°C to 8°C in the refrigerator. Protect from light. Do not shake the syringe. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Drugs for Haemolytic Hypoplastic &amp; Renal Anemia</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Pegylated erythropoietin is an erythropoietin receptor activator with greater activity in vivo as well as an increased half-life, in contrast to erythropoietin. It differs from erythropoietin through the integration of an amide bond between the N-terminal amino group or the ε-amino group of any lysine present in erythropoietin, predominantly Lys52 and Lys45, and PEG-Epo 50 mcg/0.3 ml (PEG).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. It is not known whether it is excreted into human breast milk. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk, caution should be exercised when it is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Evaluation of Iron stores</strong></p><p> Supplementary iron therapy is recommended for all patients with serum ferritin values below 100 mcg/L or with transferrin saturation below 20%. To ensure effective erythropoiesis, iron status has to be evaluated for all patients prior to and during treatment.</p>', 'active', '957_1742299633.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 17:07:13', '2025-03-18 17:07:13'),
(3312, 'Xynofen SR', 'Ketoprofen', 7.54, 10, 'Capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xynofen SR 100 mg</strong></p><p> Xynofen SR 100 mg is indicated in musculoskeletal and joint disorders such as ankylosing spondylitis, osteoarthritis, and rheumatoid arthritis, in periarticular disorders such as bursitis and tendinitis, in mild to moderate pain such as dysmenorrhoea or postoperative pain, and in other painful and inflammatory conditions such as acute gout or soft tissue disorders.</p><p> Xynofen SR 100 mg is used for symptomatic relief of mild to moderate pain, such as postoperative (including that associated with dental surgery), postpartum and orthopedic (including musculoskeletal strains or sprains) pain, and visceral pain associated with cancer.</p><p> Xynofen SR 100 mg IM injection is indicated for management of acute exacerbations of:</p><ul><li>Rheumatoid arthritis, osteoarthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, periarticular conditions such as fibrositis, bursitis, capsulitis, tendinitis, and tenosynovitis.</li><li>Low back pain of musculoskeletal origin, sciatica, other musculoskeletal conditions, acute gout, and control of pain and inflammation following orthopedic surgery.</li><li> Xynofen SR 100 mg 2.5% Gel is an anti-inflammatory and analgesic preparation to be applied topically to the painful area. It is indicated as a short-term treatment for traumatic lesions (sprains, tendinitis, edema, bruises) and pain.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Xynofen SR 100 mg is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) with analgesic and antipyretic actions. In addition to the inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis, it stabilizes lysosomal membranes in vitro and in vivo, inhibits leukotriene synthesis in vitro at high concentrations, and also exhibits antibradykinin activity in vivo.</p><p> Xynofen SR 100 mg produces analgesia by inhibiting the synthesis of prostaglandins peripherally and centrally. It has also been suggested that Xynofen SR 100 mg causes the suppression of prostaglandin synthesis in the CNS (probably in the hypothalamus) leading to its antipyretic effect.</p><p> Xynofen SR 100 mg is rapidly and almost completely absorbed from the GI tract. It is approximately 99% bound to plasma protein, mainly albumin. Following single or multiple oral doses in healthy adults, the elimination half-life of the drug has averaged 1.1-4 hours. It is rapidly and extensively metabolized in the liver, principally via conjugation with glucoronic acid. Following a single oral dose of Xynofen SR 100 mg in healthy adults, about 50-90% of the drug is excreted in urine and about 1-8% in faeces within 1-5 days; most urinary excretion occurs within 12-24 hours and most faecal excretion occurs within 24-48 hours.</p><p> In case of IM injection, peak concentration of approximately 10 mg/L is reached at about 0.5-0.75 hour after a 100 mg dose. The elimination half-life is approximately 1.88 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xynofen SR 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Xynofen SR 100 mg Tablet</strong>: Oral treatment with Xynofen SR 100 mg is 50-100 mg daily, taken with food to minimize gastrointestinal disturbance. For rheumatic disease, 100-200 mg daily in 2-4 divided doses with food. For pain and dysmenorrhoea, 50 mg up to 3 times daily.</li><li><strong>Xynofen SR 100 mg SR Capsule</strong>: 100-200 mg once daily, depending on the patient’s weight and on severity of symptoms.</li><li><strong>Xynofen SR 100 mg IM injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 50 to 100 mg every four hours, repeated up to a maximum of 200 mg in twenty-four hours for up to 3 days. Following a satisfactory response, oral therapy should be instituted with Xynofen SR 100 mg tablet. It is recommended that the injection should not normally be continued for longer than three days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Elderly: As with other medications, it is generally advisable in the elderly to begin Xynofen SR 100 mg therapy at the lower end of the dose range and to maintain such patients on the lowest effective dosage.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Paediatric dosage: Not established for intramuscular use. It is not used as intravenous injection.</li><li><strong>Xynofen SR 100 mg 2.5% Gel</strong>: This is to be applied to the painful area twice daily. The gel can be used with an occlusive dressing. Rub gently into the skin to ensure penetration.</li><li><strong>Suppository</strong>: 1 suppository (100 mg) late at night, supplemented as required with Xynofen SR 100 mg capsules during daytime. Xynofen SR 100 mg suppositories are especially appropriate for controlling overnight symptoms (severity of night and morning pain; duration and severity of morning stiffness). Suppositories administered late at night provide more consistent effective control of overnight symptoms than oral medication.</li><li> <strong>Dosage of Xynofen SR 100 mg must be carefully adjusted according to individual requirements and response, using the lowest possible effective dosage. Dosage exceeding 300 mg daily has not been adequately studied and is not recommended.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xynofen SR 100 mg</strong></p><p> As Xynofen SR 100 mg may cause GI bleeding, inhibit platelet aggregation, and prolong bleeding time, the drug should be used with caution, and the patient should be carefully observed if the drug is used concomitantly with any anticoagulant or thrombolytic agent.</p><ul><li>Concomitant administration of Xynofen SR 100 mg and hydrochlorothiazide has resulted in decreased urinary excretion of potassium and chloride compared with hydrochlorothiazide alone.</li><li>Xynofen SR 100 mg and salicylates appear to interact in a complex manner and should not be used concomitantly.</li><li>Concomitant use of Xynofen SR 100 mg and probenecid is also not recommended.</li><li>Xynofen SR 100 mg should be avoided in patients receiving methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Xynofen SR 100 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to the drug.</p><p> Xynofen SR 100 mg is contraindicated in patients in whom asthma, urticaria, or other sensitivity reactions are precipitated by aspirin or other NSAIDs, since severe, rarely fatal, anaphylactic reactions to Xynofen SR 100 mg have been reported in these patients.</p><p> The risk of potentially serious adverse GI effects should be considered in patients receiving Xynofen SR 100 mg, particularly in patients receiving chronic therapy with the drug. Xynofen SR 100 mg should be used in patients with GI bleeding or active peptic ulceration only when the potential benefits justify the possible risks.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xynofen SR 100 mg</strong></p><p> Adverse reactions to Xynofen SR 100 mg are usually mild and mainly involve the GI tract, particularly the upper GI tract. Most Xynofen SR 100 mg-induced adverse effects occur during the first month of treatment, and the frequency of adverse effects generally decreases with continued therapy.</p><ul><li><strong>Digestive system reactions</strong>: Dyspepsia, nausea, abdominal pain, diarrhea, constipation, flatulence, anorexia, vomiting, stomatitis.</li><li><strong>Nervous system reactions</strong>: Headache, dizziness, malaise, depression, nervousness, dreams, etc.</li><li><strong>Other reactions</strong>: Tinnitus, visual disturbance, rash, impairment of renal function, signs or symptoms of urinary-tract irritation.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Embryopathic effects have not been recorded with Xynofen SR 100 mg, but it is recommended to avoid medication during pregnancy.</p><p> Trace amounts of the drug appear in breast milk, and it should not be used during breastfeeding unless unavoidable.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Xynofen SR 100 mg should be used with caution in patients who may be adversely affected by a prolongation of bleeding time (e.g., patients receiving anticoagulant therapy), since the drug may inhibit platelet function.</p><p> Xynofen SR 100 mg should be used with caution in patients with heart failure, hypertension, or other conditions associated with fluid retention, since peripheral edema has been observed in some patients receiving the drug.</p><p> Liver function should be monitored periodically during long-term Xynofen SR 100 mg therapy.</p><p> Xynofen SR 100 mg injection must not be given intravenously.</p><p> Xynofen SR 100 mg gel should not be applied to patients who have an allergy to Xynofen SR 100 mg, other anti-inflammatory agents, and aspirin.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For children</strong>: There is no contraindication to the use of this drug in children, but the appropriate dosage has not yet been established.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '216_1742299902.jpg', 3, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 17:11:42', '2025-03-18 17:11:42'),
(3313, 'Terbex', 'Terbinafine Hydrochloride', 50.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Terbex 250 mg (Terbinafine)</h3><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablets</strong>: For the treatment of onychomycosis (nail infections) of the toenail or fingernail caused by dermatophytes (e.g., tinea unguium).</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Granules</strong>: Indicated for Tinea Capitis (scalp fungal infections).</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Cream</strong>: Used for skin fungal infections caused by <em>Trichophyton</em> species (e.g., <em>T. rubrum, T. mentagrophytes</em>), <em>Microsporum canis</em>, and <em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em>. Also effective against yeast infections caused by <em>Candida</em> species (<em>C. albicans</em>) and pityriasis versicolor caused by <em>Malassezia furfur</em>.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine 1% Spray</strong>: Indicated for tinea infections of the skin and pityriasis versicolor due to <em>Malassezia furfur</em>.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses</li><li>Topical Antifungal preparations</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li>Terbinafine is an <strong>allylamine</strong> antifungal that interferes with fungal ergosterol biosynthesis by inhibiting squalene epoxidase in the fungal cell membrane. This leads to a deficiency in ergosterol and an accumulation of squalene, resulting in fungal cell death.</li><li>It is <strong>fungicidal</strong> against dermatophytes, molds, and dimorphic fungi and may be either <strong>fungicidal</strong> or <strong>fungistatic</strong> against yeasts, depending on the species.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Topical Application</strong>: Apply <strong>terbinafine cream</strong> once or twice daily to the affected areas for 1-2 weeks, depending on the infection type.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea corporis &amp; Tinea cruris</strong>: 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea pedis</strong>: 2-4 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cutaneous candidiasis</strong>: 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong>: 2 weeks</li><li><strong>Oral Administration</strong>:</li><li><strong>Terbinafine 250 mg tablet</strong>: Used for nail and hair infections.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Fingernail onychomycosis</strong>: 250 mg daily for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Toenail onychomycosis</strong>: 250 mg daily for 12 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Optimal results may be seen months after treatment, as nail growth takes time.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li>Common side effects include gastrointestinal symptoms (diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain), liver test abnormalities, rashes, and taste disturbances.</li><li>Serious side effects may include liver failure (in rare cases), skin reactions like Stevens-Johnson syndrome, neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, and allergic reactions such as anaphylaxis.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablets</strong>: Not recommended during pregnancy, unless essential, due to lack of well-controlled studies. Terbinafine is excreted in breast milk, so it is not recommended for nursing mothers.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Cream</strong>: Studies in animals show no significant fetal toxicity; however, it should be used only when the benefits outweigh risks, as it is excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to terbinafine.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Terbinafine tablets can cause rare and severe liver issues, including liver failure, and should be discontinued if liver injury is detected. It is not recommended for patients with active or chronic liver disease.</li><li>Serious skin reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome may occur, requiring discontinuation of the drug.</li><li>Terbinafine cream is for external use only and should not be applied to the eyes.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li>Limited clinical experience with overdose, but doses up to 5 gm (20 times the daily dose) have been tolerated without serious effects. Symptoms of overdose include nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dizziness, rash, frequent urination, and headache.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place below 30°C, and protect from light.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Terbinafine cream is effective for tinea corporis and tinea cruris in children. Safety and efficacy of oral terbinafine have not been established in pediatric patients.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Terbinafine is generally safe for elderly patients, but the dose should be reduced by half in cases of significant hepatic or renal impairment.</li></ul><h3>Drug Interactions</h3><ul><li>Terbinafine inhibits the CYP450 2D6 enzyme, which can interact with drugs metabolized by this enzyme (e.g., tricyclic antidepressants, beta-blockers, certain antiarrhythmics). Careful monitoring and dose adjustments may be required for such drugs.</li></ul><p>If you need more specific details on dosage or interactions for a particular case, feel free to ask!</p>', 'in-active', '819_1742302849.png', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 18:00:49', '2025-05-13 04:51:18'),
(3314, 'Terbitac', 'Terbinafine Hydrochloride', 50.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Terbitac 250 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine tablet</strong>: Used for treating onychomycosis (fungal infections of the toenail or fingernail) caused by dermatophytes (e.g., <em>Tinea unguium</em>).</li><li><strong>Terbinafine granules</strong>: Used for treating Tinea Capitis (fungal infections of the scalp).</li><li><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>: Treats fungal infections of the skin caused by dermatophytes such as <em>Trichophyton</em> (e.g., <em>T. rubrum</em>, <em>T. mentagrophytes</em>, <em>T. verrucosum</em>), <em>Microsporum canis</em>, and <em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em>, as well as yeast infections like those caused by <em>Candida</em> species (e.g., <em>C. albicans</em>) and <em>Pityrosporum orbicular</em> (also known as <em>Malassezia furfur</em>), which causes Pityriasis (Tinea) versicolor.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine 1% Spray</strong>: Used for treating tinea infections of the skin and Pityriasis versicolor due to <em>Malassezia furfur</em>.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Drugs for Subcutaneous and Mycoses</strong>, <strong>Topical Antifungal Preparations</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li>Terbinafine is an <strong>allylamine antifungal</strong> that inhibits the enzyme <strong>squalene epoxidase</strong> in fungal cell membranes. This leads to a deficiency in <strong>ergosterol</strong>, a key component of the cell membrane, resulting in the accumulation of <strong>squalene</strong>, which ultimately causes fungal cell death.</li><li>It is effective against <strong>dermatophytes</strong>, <strong>moulds</strong>, certain <strong>dimorphic fungi</strong>, and <strong>yeasts</strong>, depending on the species.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Topical Application (Cream)</strong>: Apply once or twice daily for 1-2 weeks depending on the infection (e.g., 1-2 weeks for Tinea corporis and Tinea cruris, 2-4 weeks for Tinea pedis).</li><li><strong>Oral Administration (Tablet)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For <strong>finger nail onychomycosis</strong>: 250 mg daily for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For <strong>toe nail onychomycosis</strong>: 250 mg daily for 12 weeks.</li><li><strong>Granules</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Body weight &lt;25 kg: 125 mg/day for up to 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Body weight 25-35 kg: 187.5 mg/day for up to 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Body weight &gt;35 kg: 250 mg/day for up to 6 weeks.</li></ul><h3>Interactions</h3><ul><li>Terbinafine inhibits <strong>CYP450 2D6</strong>, affecting drugs metabolized by this isozyme (e.g., <strong>tricyclic antidepressants</strong>, <strong>beta-blockers</strong>, <strong>antiarrhythmics</strong>, <strong>SSRIs</strong>). Close monitoring and potential dose adjustments are necessary.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Contraindicated in individuals who are hypersensitive to <strong>terbinafine</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Gastrointestinal symptoms (diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain), liver test abnormalities, rashes, pruritus, and taste disturbances.</li><li><strong>Serious</strong>: Liver injury (including liver failure), serious skin reactions (e.g., Stevens-Johnson syndrome), neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, angioedema, anaphylaxis.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine tablet</strong>: Not recommended during pregnancy, as there are no adequate human studies. It is excreted in breast milk and should be avoided in nursing mothers.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>: Use with caution, as there is no clinical experience in pregnant women, and it is also excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Liver Issues</strong>: Terbinafine tablets should be used cautiously in patients with liver disease. <strong>Liver tests</strong> should be conducted before starting treatment. Discontinue if there are signs of liver injury.</li><li><strong>Skin Reactions</strong>: Serious reactions like <strong>Stevens-Johnson syndrome</strong> or <strong>toxic epidermal necrolysis</strong> can occur; discontinue use if such symptoms develop.</li><li><strong>Eye Contact</strong>: Avoid contact with eyes when using the cream.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li>Overdose symptoms may include nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dizziness, rash, frequent urination, and headache. No serious reactions have been reported from doses up to 5 grams.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool, dry place</strong>, below 30°C, and protect from light.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Terbinafine cream is well tolerated in children for treating Tinea corporis and Tinea cruris.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Safe for use, but dose adjustments are recommended for patients with hepatic or renal impairment.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>Terbinafine acts by inhibiting <strong>squalene epoxidase</strong>, leading to fungal cell death due to increased membrane permeability from squalene accumulation.</li></ul><p>This information is essential for effective and safe use of Terbitac 250 mg and related forms. Always consult a healthcare provider for personalized advice.</p>', 'in-active', '1858_1742303009.webp', 27, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 18:03:29', '2025-05-13 05:16:03'),
(3315, 'Xfin', 'Terbinafine Hydrochloride', 40.00, 7, 'tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xfin 250 mg (Terbinafine)</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Terbinafine Tablet:</strong>Treatment of <strong>onychomycosis</strong> of the toenail or fingernail due to dermatophytes (tinea unguium).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Terbinafine Granules:</strong>Treatment of <strong>Tinea Capitis</strong> (scalp fungal infection).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Terbinafine Cream:</strong>Treatment of <strong>fungal infections of the skin</strong> caused by dermatophytes, including <em>Trichophyton</em>, <em>Microsporum canis</em>, <em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em>, and yeasts like <em>Candida</em>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Also used for <strong>Pityriasis (tinea) versicolor</strong> caused by <em>Malassezia furfur</em>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Terbinafine 1% Spray:</strong>Treatment of <strong>tinea infections of the skin</strong>, including <strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong> caused by <em>Malassezia furfur</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Drugs for Subcutaneous and Mycoses</strong></li><li><strong>Topical Antifungal Preparations</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Terbinafine is an <strong>allylamine</strong> with broad antifungal activity. It acts by inhibiting <strong>squalene epoxidase</strong>, an enzyme critical in the synthesis of <strong>ergosterol</strong>, a key component of fungal cell membranes. This leads to a <strong>fungicidal</strong> effect, especially against <strong>dermatophytes</strong>, <strong>yeasts</strong>, and certain molds.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Topical Application (Terbinafine Cream)</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Apply <strong>once or twice daily</strong> for 1-2 weeks for fungal infections of the skin, depending on the condition.</li><li>Duration:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Tinea corporis/cruris:</strong> 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Tinea pedis:</strong> 2-4 weeks (usually 1 week if applied twice daily)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Cutaneous candidiasis:</strong> 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pityriasis versicolor:</strong> 2 weeks</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Oral Administration (Terbinafine Tablet)</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Fingernail onychomycosis:</strong> 250 mg daily for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Toenail onychomycosis:</strong> 250 mg daily for 12 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Terbinafine Granules (for children):</strong>Weight &lt; 25 kg: 125 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Weight 25-35 kg: 187.5 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Weight &gt; 35 kg: 250 mg/day</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CYP450 2D6 Inhibition:</strong> Terbinafine can interact with drugs metabolized by this enzyme, such as tricyclic antidepressants, SSRIs, beta-blockers, and antiarrhythmics (e.g., <strong>flecainide</strong>, <strong>propafenone</strong>). Monitor closely and adjust dosages as necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to terbinafine or any of its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common: <strong>Gastrointestinal issues</strong> (diarrhea, abdominal pain), <strong>liver enzyme changes</strong>, <strong>skin reactions</strong> (rashes, pruritus).</li><li>Serious: <strong>Liver injury</strong> (rare but can be severe), <strong>severe skin reactions</strong> (Stevens-Johnson syndrome), <strong>allergic reactions</strong> (anaphylaxis, angioedema), <strong>neutropenia</strong> and <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablet:</strong> Not recommended during pregnancy. It is also present in breast milk and should not be used by nursing mothers.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Cream:</strong> No clear evidence of harm, but should be used during pregnancy only if the benefit outweighs the risk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Liver function:</strong> Monitor liver enzymes before starting treatment and during therapy, particularly in patients with pre-existing liver conditions.</li><li>Discontinue if signs of liver dysfunction or <strong>serious skin reactions</strong> (like rash or blisters) appear.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablet Overdose:</strong> Clinical experience is limited, but <strong>5 gm</strong> (20 times the therapeutic dose) has been taken without serious effects. Symptoms may include <strong>nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dizziness, and rash</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at <strong>below 30°C</strong>, in a <strong>cool, dry place</strong>, and protect from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children:</strong> Safety and efficacy of terbinafine have not been established for pediatric patients.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Safe in elderly, but dosage should be adjusted in the presence of liver or kidney impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Terbinafine inhibits <strong>squalene epoxidase</strong>, disrupting fungal cell membrane synthesis, leading to fungal cell death.</li></ul><p>Would you like more details or have any specific questions on the usage of this medication?</p>', 'active', '940_1742303148.webp', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 18:05:48', '2025-03-18 18:05:48'),
(3316, 'Xfin', 'Terbinafine Hydrochloride', 80.00, 1, 'x 10 gm tube', '10 gm tube', 'Cream', '1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you requested with key headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Xfin 10 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablet</strong>: This tablet is indicated for the treatment of onychomycosis (fungal infections of the toenail or fingernail) due to dermatophytes (tinea unguium).</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Granules</strong>: Indicated for Tinea Capitis.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Cream</strong>: Indicated for fungal infections of the skin caused by <em>Trichophyton</em> (e.g., <em>T. rubrum, T. mentagrophytes, T. verrucosum, T. violaceum</em>), <em>Microsporum canis</em>, and <em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em>. It is also effective against yeast infections caused by <em>Candida</em> species (e.g., <em>C. albicans</em>) and pityriasis (tinea) versicolor caused by <em>Pityrosporum orbicular</em> (Malassezia furfur).</li><li><strong>Terbinafine 1% Spray</strong>: This spray is indicated for treating tinea infections of the skin and pityriasis (tinea) versicolor caused by <em>Malassezia furfur</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses</li><li>Topical antifungal preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Terbinafine is an allylamine antifungal that works by inhibiting the enzyme squalene epoxidase in the fungal cell membrane. This disrupts ergosterol biosynthesis, leading to fungal cell death due to the accumulation of squalene. Terbinafine is effective against dermatophytes, moulds, dimorphic fungi, and some yeasts.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xfin 10 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Topical Application</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Terbinafine cream is applied once or twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In <strong>Tinea corporis</strong> and <strong>Tinea cruris</strong>: 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In <strong>Tinea pedis</strong>: 2-4 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In <strong>Cutaneous candidiasis</strong>: 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In <strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong>: 2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For <strong>intertriginous infections</strong>, a gauze strip may be used for application.</li><li><strong>Oral Administration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For <strong>onychomycosis of fingernail</strong>: 250 mg once daily for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For <strong>onychomycosis of toenail</strong>: 250 mg once daily for 12 weeks</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xfin 10 gm</strong></p><p>Terbinafine is an inhibitor of the CYP450 2D6 isozyme. Drugs metabolized by this enzyme, such as tricyclic antidepressants, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, beta-blockers, and antiarrhythmics, should be used with caution and may require dose adjustments.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Terbinafine is contraindicated in individuals with hypersensitivity to the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xfin 10 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain, liver test abnormalities, rashes, pruritus, taste disturbances.</li><li><strong>Severe</strong>: Hepatic injury (including liver failure), serious skin reactions (e.g., Stevens-Johnson syndrome), neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, angioedema, anaphylaxis, malaise, fatigue, vomiting, arthralgia, myalgia, and hair loss.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablet</strong>: Should not be used during pregnancy unless essential. Not recommended for nursing mothers as it is excreted in breast milk.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Cream</strong>: Should be avoided unless the potential benefits outweigh the risks, as it is excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Rare cases of liver failure, some requiring transplants or resulting in death, have been reported.</li><li>Treatment should be discontinued if there is any evidence of liver injury.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong> should be used with caution in individuals with active or chronic liver disease.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine tablets</strong> should be avoided in patients with pre-existing liver disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xfin 10 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Clinical experience with overdose is limited. Symptoms of overdose include nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dizziness, rash, frequent urination, and headache. No serious adverse reactions have been reported with doses up to 5 gm.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place, below 30°C, and protect from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Terbinafine cream has been found effective for treating tinea corporis and tinea cruris in children.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Generally safe; however, the dose should be reduced if there is significant hepatic or renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Other antifungal preparations</li><li>Topical antifungal preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Terbinafine inhibits the enzyme squalene epoxidase, leading to the accumulation of squalene and increased membrane permeability, resulting in fungal cell death.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established for terbinafine tablets.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Terbinafine is not recommended during pregnancy unless the potential benefit outweighs the risk. It is excreted in breast milk, and use should be avoided during breastfeeding.</p><p>This format organizes the key information clearly. Let me know if you need further assistance!</p>', 'active', '1261_1742303528.webp', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 18:12:08', '2025-04-23 08:32:51'),
(3317, 'Dermafin', 'Terbinafine Hydrochloride', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Dermafin 250 mg</h3><p><strong>Terbinafine tablet</strong>:</p><ul><li>Treatment of <strong>onychomycosis</strong> (fungal infection of the toenail or fingernail) caused by dermatophytes (e.g., <em>Tinea unguium</em>).</li></ul><p><strong>Terbinafine granules</strong>:</p><ul><li>Indicated for <strong>Tinea Capitis</strong> (fungal infection of the scalp).</li></ul><p><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>:</p><ul><li>Treatment of <strong>fungal infections of the skin</strong>, including those caused by <em>Trichophyton</em> species (e.g., <em>T. rubrum, T. mentagrophytes, T. verrucosum, T. violaceum</em>), <em>Microsporum canis</em>, and <em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em>.</li><li><strong>Yeast infections</strong> of the skin, mainly caused by <em>Candida</em> species (e.g., <em>C. albicans</em>).</li><li>Treatment of <strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong> caused by <em>Pityrosporum orbiculare</em> (also known as <em>Malassezia furfur</em>).</li></ul><p><strong>Terbinafine 1% Spray</strong>:</p><ul><li>For treatment of <strong>tinea infections</strong> of the skin, including <strong>pityriasis versicolor</strong> due to <em>Malassezia furfur</em>.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses</strong></li><li><strong>Topical Antifungal preparations</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li>Terbinafine is an <strong>allylamine antifungal</strong> with broad antifungal activity.</li><li>It is <strong>fungicidal</strong> against dermatophytes, moulds, and certain dimorphic fungi.</li><li>Terbinafine works by inhibiting <strong>squalene epoxidase</strong> in the fungal cell membrane, disrupting ergosterol biosynthesis, causing a deficiency of ergosterol, and accumulation of squalene, leading to fungal cell death.</li><li>It is effective against a variety of fungal species, including <strong>Trichophyton</strong>, <strong>Microsporum</strong>, and <strong>Epidermophyton</strong>. It is also effective against <strong>Candida</strong> species and <strong>Pityrosporum</strong> (now known as <em>Malassezia</em>).</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Dermafin 250 mg</h3><h4>Topical Application:</h4><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>:</li><li>Apply to affected areas <strong>once or twice daily</strong> for <strong>1-2 weeks</strong> for fungal infections of the skin.</li><li>Duration:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea corporis</strong> and <strong>Tinea cruris</strong>: 1-2 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea pedis</strong>: 2-4 weeks (usually 1 week if applied twice daily).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cutaneous candidiasis</strong>: 1-2 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong>: 2 weeks.</li><li>For best results, clean and dry affected areas thoroughly before applying a thin layer of cream to the skin.</li></ul><h4>Oral Administration:</h4><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine tablet</strong> is essential for <strong>hair or nail infections</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Fingernail onychomycosis</strong>: 250 mg once daily for <strong>6 weeks</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Toenail onychomycosis</strong>: 250 mg once daily for <strong>12 weeks</strong>.</li></ul><h4>Dosage for Terbinafine Granules:</h4><ul><li><strong>&lt;25 kg body weight</strong>: 125 mg/day for up to <strong>6 weeks</strong>.</li><li><strong>25-35 kg body weight</strong>: 187.5 mg/day for up to <strong>6 weeks</strong>.</li><li><strong>&gt;35 kg body weight</strong>: 250 mg/day for up to <strong>6 weeks</strong>.</li></ul><h4>Terbinafine 1% Spray:</h4><ul><li><strong>Tinea pedis</strong>: Apply once daily for <strong>1 week</strong>.</li><li><strong>Tinea corporis</strong>/<strong>cruris</strong>: Apply once daily for <strong>1 week</strong>.</li><li><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong>: Apply twice daily for <strong>1 week</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Interactions of Dermafin 250 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine is a CYP450 2D6 inhibitor</strong>, which can affect the metabolism of drugs processed by this enzyme.</li><li><strong>Drugs affected by CYP450 2D6</strong> include <strong>tricyclic antidepressants</strong>, <strong>SSRIs</strong>, <strong>beta-blockers</strong>, <strong>antiarrhythmic drugs</strong> (like flecainide and propafenone), and <strong>MAO-B inhibitors</strong>.</li><li>Monitor for potential interactions, and dose adjustments may be necessary for drugs metabolized by CYP450 2D6.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to terbinafine or any of its components.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Dermafin 250 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Gastrointestinal symptoms (diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain), liver test abnormalities, rashes, pruritus, taste disturbances.</li><li><strong>Severe (rare)</strong>: Hepatic injury, liver failure (in some cases leading to death or liver transplant), serious skin reactions (e.g., Stevens-Johnson Syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis), neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, angioedema, allergic reactions (including anaphylaxis).</li><li>Other possible side effects: malaise, fatigue, vomiting, arthralgia, myalgia, and hair loss.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine tablet</strong>:</li><li>Not recommended during pregnancy due to insufficient human studies. It is advised to wait until after pregnancy to start treatment. Terbinafine is excreted in breast milk and should not be used by nursing mothers.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>:</li><li>Limited clinical data in pregnancy, and it should only be used if benefits outweigh potential risks. It is also excreted in breast milk, so avoid use in breastfeeding mothers.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Liver failure</strong> has been reported with terbinafine tablets, some leading to death or transplant, especially in patients with preexisting liver conditions.</li><li><strong>Discontinue</strong> treatment if liver injury is suspected.</li><li><strong>Serious skin reactions</strong>: If a rash develops or worsens, discontinue treatment.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>: External use only. Avoid eye contact.</li><li><strong>Patients with chronic or active liver disease</strong> should not use terbinafine. Serum transaminase tests should be conducted before starting treatment with terbinafine tablets.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Dermafin 250 mg</h3><ul><li>Limited clinical experience with overdose.</li><li>Symptoms of overdose include <strong>nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dizziness, rash, headache</strong>, and <strong>frequent urination</strong>.</li><li>Doses up to <strong>5 gm</strong> (20 times the therapeutic dose) have been ingested without serious adverse reactions.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool, dry place</strong>, below <strong>30°C</strong>, and protect from light.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Terbinafine cream is generally well-tolerated in children, especially for <strong>Tinea corporis</strong> and <strong>Tinea cruris</strong>.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No special dosage adjustment is required unless there is significant liver or renal impairment.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine</strong> inhibits the <strong>squalene epoxidase enzyme</strong>, disrupting the biosynthesis of <strong>ergosterol</strong>, a key component of the fungal cell membrane. This leads to <strong>fungal cell death</strong> by disrupting membrane permeability.</li></ul><h3>Pediatric Uses</h3><ul><li>Safety and efficacy have not been established in pediatric patients for terbinafine tablets. It is safe for use in children when applied topically.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further clarification on any part of this!</p>', 'in-active', '102_1742303862.jpg', 33, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 18:17:42', '2025-05-08 14:31:02');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3318, 'Mycofree', 'Terbinafine Hydrochloride', 38.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Mycofree 250 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablet</strong>: Indicated for the treatment of onychomycosis of the toenail or fingernail caused by dermatophytes (tinea unguium).</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Granules</strong>: Indicated for Tinea Capitis.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Cream</strong>: Indicated for fungal skin infections caused by Trichophyton species (e.g., <em>T. rubrum</em>, <em>T. mentagrophytes</em>, <em>T. verrucosum</em>, <em>T. violaceum</em>), <em>Microsporum canis</em>, and <em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em>. Also effective against yeast infections caused by <em>Candida</em> species (e.g., <em>C. albicans</em>), and Pityriasis (tinea) versicolor due to <em>Pityrosporum orbiculare</em> (Malassezia furfur).</li><li><strong>Terbinafine 1% Spray</strong>: Indicated for the treatment of tinea infections of the skin and Pityriasis versicolor due to <em>Malassezia furfur</em>.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Drugs for Subcutaneous and Mycoses</strong>, <strong>Topical Antifungal Preparations</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Terbinafine is an allylamine with antifungal activity. It is fungicidal against dermatophytes, moulds, and certain dimorphic fungi. It works by inhibiting squalene epoxidase in the fungal cell membrane, leading to a deficiency in ergosterol and the accumulation of squalene, resulting in fungal cell death.</p><p><br></p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Mycofree 250 mg</h3><h4><strong>Topical Application</strong>:</h4><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Cream</strong>: Apply to affected areas once or twice daily for 1-2 weeks for skin infections. Treatment duration varies depending on the condition:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea corporis/cruris</strong>: 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea pedis</strong>: 2-4 weeks (one week if applied twice daily)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cutaneous candidiasis</strong>: 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong>: 2 weeks</li><li><strong>Application Instructions</strong>: Clean and dry affected areas before applying a thin layer of cream. For intertriginous infections, cover with gauze at night.</li></ul><h4><strong>Oral Administration</strong>:</h4><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Fingernail onychomycosis</strong>: 250 mg once daily for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Toenail onychomycosis</strong>: 250 mg once daily for 12 weeks.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Mycofree 250 mg</h3><ul><li>Terbinafine inhibits the <strong>CYP450 2D6 isozyme</strong>, affecting the metabolism of certain drugs, including tricyclic antidepressants, SSRIs, beta-blockers, antiarrhythmics, and monoamine oxidase inhibitors. Caution and monitoring are advised when co-administering these drugs.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity to Terbinafine</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Mycofree 250 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Common side effects</strong>: Gastrointestinal symptoms (diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain), liver test abnormalities, rashes, urticaria, pruritus, and taste disturbances.</li><li><strong>Serious side effects</strong>: Liver failure (rare), skin reactions (e.g., Stevens-Johnson Syndrome), neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, angioedema, and anaphylaxis.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablet</strong>: Not recommended during pregnancy. Terbinafine is excreted in breast milk and should not be used in nursing mothers.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Cream</strong>: Limited clinical experience in pregnancy. Should only be used if benefits outweigh risks. Excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablet</strong>: Monitor liver function, especially in patients with pre-existing liver disease. Discontinue treatment if liver injury is suspected.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Cream</strong>: For external use only. Avoid eye contact. Not recommended for patients with chronic liver disease.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Mycofree 250 mg</h3><ul><li>Limited experience with overdose. Symptoms include nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dizziness, rash, frequent urination, and headache. No serious adverse reactions reported with doses up to 5 g (20 times the therapeutic dose).</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place below 30°C. Protect from light.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Terbinafine cream is effective and well-tolerated in children for tinea corporis and tinea cruris.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No specific dosage adjustments are necessary unless hepatic or renal impairment is present.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li><strong>Other Antifungal Preparations</strong>, <strong>Topical Antifungal Preparations</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Terbinafine inhibits ergosterol biosynthesis in fungal cells by blocking the enzyme squalene epoxidase. This results in fungal cell death through increased membrane permeability caused by squalene accumulation.</p><p><br></p><p>This summary provides essential information about <strong>Mycofree 250 mg</strong> and its different forms of administration for treating fungal infections.</p>', 'active', '27_1742304013.webp', 9, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 18:20:13', '2025-03-18 18:20:13'),
(3319, 'Telfin', 'Terbinafine Hydrochloride', 50.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Telfin 250 mg (Terbinafine)</h3><p><strong>Terbinafine tablet</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Onychomycosis of the toenail or fingernail</strong> due to dermatophytes (tinea unguium).</li></ul><p><strong>Terbinafine granules</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Tinea Capitis</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>:</p><ul><li>Fungal infections of the skin caused by <em>Trichophyton</em> species (e.g., <em>T. rubrum, T. mentagrophytes, T. verrucosum, T. violaceum</em>), <em>Microsporum canis</em>, and <em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em>.</li><li>Yeast infections of the skin, primarily caused by <em>Candida</em> species (e.g., <em>C. albicans</em>).</li><li><strong>Pityriasis (tinea) versicolor</strong> caused by <em>Pityrosporum orbiculare</em> (also known as <em>Malassezia furfur</em>).</li></ul><p><strong>Terbinafine 1% Spray</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Tinea infections</strong> of the skin.</li><li><strong>Pityriasis (tinea) versicolor</strong> due to <em>Malassezia furfur</em>.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Drugs for <strong>subcutaneous and mycoses</strong>.</li><li><strong>Topical Antifungal preparations</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine</strong> is an <strong>allylamine</strong> with a broad spectrum of antifungal activity. It is <strong>fungicidal</strong> against dermatophytes, moulds, and some dimorphic fungi. It is either <strong>fungicidal</strong> or <strong>fungistatic</strong> against yeasts, depending on the species.</li><li>It inhibits <strong>squalene epoxidase</strong>, a key enzyme in the biosynthesis of ergosterol, disrupting the fungal cell membrane.</li><li>Effective against <strong>dermatophytes</strong>, <strong>yeasts</strong>, and <strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><p><strong>Topical application (Terbinafine cream)</strong>:</p><ul><li>Apply to affected areas once or twice daily for 1-2 weeks for fungal skin infections.</li><li><strong>Tinea corporis</strong> and <strong>Tinea cruris</strong>: 1-2 weeks.</li><li><strong>Tinea pedis</strong>: 2-4 weeks (1 week if applied twice daily).</li><li><strong>Cutaneous candidiasis</strong>: 1-2 weeks.</li><li><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong>: 2 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Oral administration (Terbinafine tablet)</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Fingernail onychomycosis</strong>: 250 mg daily for 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Toenail onychomycosis</strong>: 250 mg daily for 12 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Terbinafine granules</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>&lt;25 kg</strong>: 125 mg/day up to 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>25-35 kg</strong>: 187.5 mg/day up to 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>&gt;35 kg</strong>: 250 mg/day up to 6 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Terbinafine 1% Spray</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Tinea pedis</strong>: Once daily for 1 week.</li><li><strong>Tinea corporis/cruris</strong>: Once daily for 1 week.</li><li><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong>: Twice daily for 1 week.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Telfin 250 mg</h3><ul><li>Terbinafine inhibits the <strong>CYP450 2D6</strong> enzyme, which can interact with drugs metabolized by this isozyme, such as <strong>tricyclic antidepressants</strong>, <strong>SSRIs</strong>, <strong>beta-blockers</strong>, <strong>antiarrhythmics</strong>, and <strong>monoamine oxidase inhibitors</strong>. Co-administration requires careful monitoring and potential dose adjustments.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to terbinafine.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Telfin 250 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Common side effects</strong>: Gastrointestinal symptoms (diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain), liver test abnormalities, skin reactions (rash, pruritus), taste disturbances.</li><li><strong>Serious side effects</strong>: Hepatic injury (including liver failure), serious skin reactions (e.g., Stevens-Johnson Syndrome), neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, angioedema, anaphylaxis.</li><li><strong>Other side effects</strong>: Malaise, fatigue, vomiting, arthralgia, myalgia, hair loss.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine tablet</strong>: Not recommended during pregnancy. It is excreted in breast milk, so it is not recommended for breastfeeding mothers.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>: No significant adverse effects in animal studies, but should be used cautiously during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine tablets</strong>: Monitor for <strong>liver toxicity</strong>. Discontinue if signs of liver injury appear. Use caution in patients with preexisting liver disease.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>: For external use only. Avoid contact with eyes.</li><li><strong>Pre-treatment liver function tests</strong> are recommended for all patients on terbinafine tablets.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li>Overdose is rarely serious, but symptoms may include <strong>nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dizziness, rash, headache</strong>, and <strong>frequent urination</strong>. Doses up to <strong>5 gm</strong> have been tolerated without significant effects.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place below <strong>30°C</strong>, and protect from light.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Terbinafine cream is effective for <strong>tinea corporis</strong> and <strong>tinea cruris</strong> in children.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No specific dosage adjustments required, but reduce the dose if <strong>hepatic or renal impairment</strong> is present.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>Terbinafine inhibits <strong>ergosterol biosynthesis</strong> by blocking <strong>squalene epoxidase</strong>, leading to fungal cell death due to increased <strong>squalene</strong> and disrupted membrane function.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '1050_1742304248.webp', 8, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 18:24:08', '2025-05-12 21:18:24'),
(3320, 'Terbin', 'Terbinafine Hydrochloride', 60.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Terbin 250 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Terbinafine tablet</strong>: This tablet is indicated for the treatment of onychomycosis of the toenail or fingernail due to dermatophytes (tinea unguium).</p><p><strong>Terbinafine granules</strong>: This is indicated for Tinea Capitis.</p><p><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>: Fungal infection of the skin caused by Trichophyton (e.g., T. rubrum, T. mentagrophytes, T. verrucosum, T. violaceum), Microsporum canis, and Epidermophyton floccosum. Yeast infections of the skin, principally those caused by the genus <em>Candida</em> (e.g., <em>C. albicans</em>). Pityriasis (tinea) versicolor due to <em>Pityrosporum orbicular</em> (also known as <em>Malassezia furfur</em>).</p><p><strong>Terbinafine 1% Spray</strong>: This spray is indicated in the treatment of tinea infections of the skin. This spray is also indicated in the treatment of pityriasis (tinea) versicolor due to <em>Malassezia furfur</em>.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses, Topical Antifungal preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Terbinafine is an allylamine with a range of antifungal activity. It is fungicidal against dermatophytes, moulds, and certain dimorphic fungi. Terbinafine is either fungicidal or fungistatic against yeasts, depending on the species. Terbinafine interferes with fungal ergosterol biosynthesis by inhibiting squalene epoxidase in the fungal cell membrane at an early stage. This leads to a deficiency in ergosterol and intracellular accumulation of squalene, resulting in fungal cell death. Terbinafine is highly effective in fungal infections of the skin, hair, and nails caused by <em>Trichophyton spp.</em>, <em>Microsporum spp.</em>, and <em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em>. It is also effective against yeast infections of the skin, principally those caused by the genus <em>Candida</em>. Topical terbinafine appears to be effective in pityriasis versicolor due to <em>Pityrosporum orbiculare</em>.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Terbin 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Topical application</strong>: Terbinafine cream should be applied to affected areas once or twice daily for 1-2 weeks for skin infections. Some infections may require oral terbinafine tablet therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Tinea corporis and Tinea cruris</em>: 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Tinea pedis</em>: 2-4 weeks (1 week if applied twice daily)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Cutaneous candidiasis</em>: 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Pityriasis versicolor</em>: 2 weeks</li></ul><p>To apply Terbinafine cream, clean and dry the affected areas thoroughly, apply the cream once or twice a day in a thin layer, and rub in lightly. For intertriginous infections, a gauze strip may be used, especially at night.</p><ul><li><strong>Oral administration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For <em>finger nail onychomycosis</em>: 250 mg once daily for 6 weeks</li><li>For <em>toe nail onychomycosis</em>: 250 mg once daily for 12 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The optimal clinical effect is seen some months after mycological cure and cessation of treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Terbin 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fingernail onychomycosis: 250 mg (one tablet), once daily for 6 weeks</li><li>Toenail onychomycosis: 250 mg (one tablet), once daily for 12 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The clinical effect is observed after a few months as healthy nail growth resumes.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine granules</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>&lt;25 kg</em>: 125 mg/day for up to 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>25-35 kg</em>: 187.5 mg/day for up to 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>&gt;35 kg</em>: 250 mg/day for up to 6 weeks</li><li><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>: Apply once or twice daily. The likely duration of treatment is as follows:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Tinea corporis/cruris</em>: 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Tinea pedis</em>: 1 week</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Cutaneous candidiasis</em>: 2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Pityriasis versicolor</em>: 2 weeks</li><li><strong>Terbinafine 1% Spray</strong>: Apply once or twice daily depending on the indication. The affected areas should be cleansed and dried thoroughly before application.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Tinea pedis</em>: Once a day for 1 week</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Tinea corporis/cruris</em>: Once a day for 1 week</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Pityriasis versicolor</em>: Twice a day for 1 week</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Terbin 250 mg</strong></p><p>Terbinafine is an inhibitor of the CYP450 2D6 isozyme. Co-administration with drugs metabolized by CYP450 2D6 (e.g., tricyclic antidepressants, SSRIs, beta-blockers, antiarrhythmics) should be done with careful monitoring, and dose adjustments may be required.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Terbinafine tablet and cream are contraindicated in individuals hypersensitive to terbinafine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Terbin 250 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include gastrointestinal symptoms (diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain), liver test abnormalities, rashes, urticaria, pruritus, and taste disturbances. Rare adverse reactions include liver failure, severe skin reactions, neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, and allergic reactions like anaphylaxis. Other side effects may include fatigue, vomiting, arthralgia, myalgia, and hair loss.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine tablet</strong>: Not recommended during pregnancy unless necessary. Terbinafine is excreted in breast milk, so it is also not recommended for nursing mothers.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>: Limited clinical experience, so it should only be used during pregnancy if the benefits outweigh risks. It is also excreted in breast milk, so it should not be used by breastfeeding mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Liver failure, some leading to death or transplant, has been reported, especially in individuals with pre-existing liver disease. Regular liver function tests are recommended before starting terbinafine.</li><li>Serious skin reactions like Stevens-Johnson Syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis have been reported. Discontinue use if severe skin reactions occur.</li><li>For Terbinafine cream: Avoid contact with eyes.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Terbin 250 mg</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdose may include nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dizziness, rash, frequent urination, and headache. There is limited clinical experience with overdose, and doses up to 5 gm have been taken without serious adverse effects.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place below 30°C, away from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and efficacy of terbinafine in children have not been established.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No significant difference in safety or dosing compared to younger patients. However, dose reduction may be required for patients with hepatic or renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Other Antifungal preparations, Topical Antifungal preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Terbinafine inhibits ergosterol biosynthesis in fungal cells by inhibiting squalene epoxidase, resulting in fungal cell death due to increased membrane permeability and accumulation of squalene. It has demonstrated activity against <em>Trichophyton mentagrophytes</em>, <em>Trichophyton rubrum</em>, and other fungi.</p>', 'in-active', '1924_1742304505.jpg', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 18:28:25', '2025-05-13 05:10:45'),
(3321, 'Mycofree', 'Terbinafine Hydrochloride', 100.00, 1, 'x 15 gm tube', '15 gm tube', 'Cream', '1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Mycofree 1%:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablet</strong>: For the treatment of onychomycosis of the toenail or fingernail due to dermatophytes (tinea unguium).</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Granules</strong>: For the treatment of Tinea Capitis.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Cream</strong>: For fungal skin infections caused by Trichophyton spp. (e.g., T. rubrum, T. mentagrophytes, T. verrucosum, T. violaceum), Microsporum canis, Epidermophyton floccosum, and yeast infections (mainly Candida spp., like C. albicans). It is also used for <strong>Pityriasis (tinea) versicolor</strong> due to <em>Pityrosporum orbiculare</em> (also known as <em>Malassezia furfur</em>).</li><li><strong>Terbinafine 1% Spray</strong>: For treating tinea infections of the skin, including pityriasis versicolor due to <em>Malassezia furfur</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</p><p>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses, <strong>Topical Antifungal preparations</strong></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><p>Terbinafine is an allylamine antifungal agent with fungicidal activity against dermatophytes, molds, and some dimorphic fungi. It works by inhibiting <strong>squalene epoxidase</strong>, leading to a deficiency in ergosterol and a buildup of squalene, causing fungal cell death.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Mycofree 1%:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Topical Application</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Apply <strong>Terbinafine Cream</strong> to affected areas once or twice daily for 1-2 weeks for skin fungal infections, though some infections may require oral therapy.</li><li>Usual treatment duration:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Tinea corporis and Tinea cruris: 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Tinea pedis: 2-4 weeks (typically 1 week with twice-daily use)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Cutaneous candidiasis: 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pityriasis versicolor: 2 weeks</li><li><strong>Oral Administration</strong>:</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablet</strong> for nail infections:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Finger nail onychomycosis: 250 mg daily for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Toe nail onychomycosis: 250 mg daily for 12 weeks.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Granules</strong> (for children):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">&lt;25 kg: 125 mg/day up to 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">25-35 kg: 187.5 mg/day up to 6 weeks</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">35 kg: 250 mg/day up to 6 weeks</blockquote><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine 1% Spray</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tinea pedis: once a day for 1 week</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tinea corporis/cruris: once a day for 1 week</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pityriasis versicolor: twice a day for 1 week</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Mycofree 1%</strong>:</p><p>Terbinafine is an inhibitor of the <strong>CYP450 2D6</strong> isozyme. Caution is required when used with drugs metabolized by this enzyme, such as tricyclic antidepressants, SSRIs, beta-blockers, and antiarrhythmic drugs (e.g., flecainide).</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to terbinafine</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Mycofree 1%</strong>:</p><ul><li>Common: Gastrointestinal symptoms (e.g., diarrhea, abdominal pain), liver test abnormalities, rashes, pruritus, taste disturbances.</li><li>Severe reactions (rare): Hepatic injury, severe skin reactions (e.g., Stevens-Johnson Syndrome), neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, angioedema, anaphylaxis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablets</strong>: Not recommended during pregnancy unless necessary, as there are no adequate studies in pregnant women. It is also present in breast milk, so not recommended during breastfeeding.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Cream</strong>: Animal studies show no adverse effects, but clinical experience in pregnant women is lacking. Should be used only if the benefits outweigh risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Not recommended for nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine Tablets</strong>: Rare liver failure cases (including death or liver transplant) have been reported. Discontinue if there is evidence of liver injury. Severe skin reactions like <strong>Stevens-Johnson Syndrome</strong> may occur.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Cream</strong>: For external use only; avoid contact with eyes.</li><li><strong>Pre-existing Liver Disease</strong>: Not recommended for patients with chronic liver disease. Pre-treatment liver tests are advised for those taking terbinafine tablets.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Mycofree 1%</strong>:</p><p>Limited clinical experience with overdose. High doses (e.g., 5 gm) have been taken without serious effects. Symptoms may include nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dizziness, and headache.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>:</p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Terbinafine cream is effective and well-tolerated for treating tinea corporis and cruris.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Generally safe, but dose adjustments may be necessary for significant hepatic or renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong>:</p><p>Other <strong>Antifungal preparations</strong>, Topical <strong>Antifungal preparations</strong></p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</p><p>Terbinafine inhibits the enzyme <strong>squalene epoxidase</strong>, disrupting ergosterol biosynthesis in fungal membranes, leading to cell death due to increased membrane permeability caused by squalene accumulation.</p>', 'active', '1000_1742304720.webp', 9, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 18:32:00', '2025-05-04 00:11:22'),
(3322, 'Trophen', 'Tropicamide plus Phenylephrine Hydrochloride', 90.00, 1, 'Ophthalmic Solution', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '0.8 percent plus 5 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Trophen 0.8%+5%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Therapeutic purpose</strong>: Inflammatory conditions of the uveal tract. May be used in temporary lowering of intraocular pressure in glaucoma.</li><li><strong>Diagnostic purpose</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Retinal photography</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Refractive errors</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fundus examination/photography</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Slit lamp examination</li><li><strong>Pre-operative use</strong>: In order to undergo a surgical procedure that requires the visualization of structures behind the iris, such as cataract extraction, vitrectomy, and retinal detachment surgery.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Preparations for Ophthalmic diagnosis</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tropicamide</strong> binds to and blocks the receptors in the muscles of the eye (muscarinic receptor M4). Tropicamide acts by blocking the responses of the iris sphincter muscle to the iris and ciliary muscles to cholinergic stimulation, producing dilation of the pupil and paralysis of the ciliary muscle.</li><li><strong>Phenylephrine Hydrochloride</strong> is a selective alpha-1 agonist, which causes mydriasis without cycloplegia. It tends to reduce intraocular lesion by vasoconstrictor action.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Trophen 0.8%+5%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Uveitis</strong>: 1-2 drops bid-qid or as required.</li><li><strong>Diagnostic purpose/Pre-operative purpose</strong>: Instill 1-2 drops in the eye(s) 15-20 minutes before examination. If examination is not conducted within 20-30 minutes, an additional drop may be placed in the eye(s) to prolong the effect.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Trophen 0.8%+5%</strong></p><p> Tropicamide may interfere with the antihypertensive action of carbachol, pilocarpine, or ophthalmic cholinesterase inhibitors.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> This medication is contraindicated in the following cases:</p><ul><li>Patients with hypersensitivity to any of the components</li><li>Narrow angles or narrow-angle glaucoma</li><li>Low birth weight infants and some elderly adults with severe arteriosclerotic cardiovascular or cerebrovascular disease</li><li>During intraocular operative procedures when the corneal epithelial barrier has been disturbed.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Trophen 0.8%+5%</strong></p><ul><li>Elevated IOP (occasionally), stinging, blurred vision, tachycardia, photophobia, headache, parasympathetic stimulation, and allergic reactions.</li><li>Systemic effects include arrhythmias, hypertension, and coronary artery spasm.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The safety of this medication during pregnancy or lactation has not been established.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be taken when administered with, or up to 21 days after administration of monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitors as exaggerated adrenergic effects may result.</li><li>The possibility of psychotic reactions and behavioral disturbances should be considered in patients who are hypersensitive to anticholinergic drugs.</li><li>Use with extreme caution in infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Trophen 0.8%+5%</strong></p><p> Excessive use in children and in certain susceptible individuals may produce systemic toxic symptoms.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep out of the reach of children. Store in a cool, dry place, away from heat and direct light. Do not use more than 4 weeks after opening.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Preparations for Ophthalmic diagnosis</li></ul><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Tropicamide binds to and blocks the receptors in the muscles of the eye (muscarinic receptor M4). Tropicamide acts by blocking the responses of the iris sphincter muscle to the iris and ciliary muscles to cholinergic stimulation, producing dilation of the pupil and paralysis of the ciliary muscle.</li><li>Phenylephrine Hydrochloride is a selective alpha-1 agonist, which causes mydriasis without cycloplegia. It tends to reduce intraocular lesion by vasoconstrictor action.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The safety of this medication during pregnancy or lactation has not been established.</p>', 'active', '786_1742305518.jpg', 29, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 18:45:18', '2025-03-18 18:45:18'),
(3323, 'Terbin', 'Terbinafine Hydrochloride', 90.00, 1, 'x 15 gm tube', '15 gm tube', 'Cream', '1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Terbin 15 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine tablet</strong>: This tablet is indicated for the treatment of onychomycosis of the toenail or fingernail due to dermatophytes (tinea unguium).</li><li><strong>Terbinafine granules</strong>: This is indicated in Tinea Capitis.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>: Fungal infection of the skin caused by Trichophyton (e.g., T. rubrum, T. mentagrophytes, T. verrucosum, T. violaceum), Microsporum canis, and Epidermophyton floccosum. Yeast infections of the skin, principally those caused by the genus Candida (e.g., C. albicans). Pityriasis (tinea) versicolor due to Pityrosporum orbiculare (also known as Malassezia furfur).</li><li><strong>Terbinafine 1% Spray</strong>: This spray is indicated in the treatment of tinea infections of the skin. This spray is also indicated in the treatment of pityriasis (tinea) versicolor due to Malassezia furfur.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</p><ul><li>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses, Topical Antifungal preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>: Terbinafine is an allylamine with a range of antifungal activity. It is fungicidal against dermatophytes, moulds, and certain dimorphic fungi. Terbinafine interferes with fungal ergosterol biosynthesis by inhibiting squalene epoxidase in the fungal cell membrane at an early stage. This leads to a deficiency in ergosterol and intracellular accumulation of squalene, resulting in fungal cell death.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Terbin 15 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Topical application</strong>: Terbinafine cream should be applied to affected areas once or twice daily for 1-2 weeks for fungal infections of the skin. Specific infection durations may vary:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea corporis and Tinea cruris</strong>: 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea pedis</strong>: 2-4 weeks (One week of treatment will normally suffice if the cream is applied twice daily.)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cutaneous candidiasis</strong>: 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong>: 2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Application</strong>: Clean and dry the affected area, then apply a thin layer of cream to the skin and rub in lightly. In intertriginous infections, a gauze strip may be used, especially at night.</li><li><strong>Oral administration</strong>: Terbinafine tablet is essential for hair or nail infections. Usual oral dose:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Fingernail onychomycosis</strong>: Terbinafine 250 mg daily for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Toenail onychomycosis</strong>: Terbinafine 250 mg daily for 12 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong>: Terbinafine is an inhibitor of the CYP450 2D6 isozyme. Drugs predominantly metabolized by this isozyme, including tricyclic antidepressants, beta-blockers, and antiarrhythmics, may require dose adjustments.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>: Terbinafine tablets and cream are contraindicated in individuals hypersensitive to terbinafine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>: Adverse effects include gastrointestinal symptoms, liver abnormalities, rashes, urticaria, taste disturbances, and more serious side effects like liver failure, skin reactions (e.g., Stevens-Johnson Syndrome), and allergic reactions.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine tablet</strong>: Not recommended during pregnancy. It is also excreted in breast milk, and treatment is not recommended for nursing mothers.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>: There are no clinical experiences, so it should only be used if the potential benefits outweigh risks. Terbinafine is excreted in breast milk, so it should not be used while breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine tablets</strong>: Liver failure has been reported in some cases. Discontinue if liver injury is detected. Use caution in patients with active or chronic liver disease.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>: For external use only. Avoid contact with eyes.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong>: Symptoms of overdose include nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dizziness, rash, and frequent urination. No serious reactions are reported for doses up to 5 gm.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>: Store in a cool and dry place, below 30°C, and protect from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Safe when used as prescribed. Dose reduction may be necessary in cases of hepatic or renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong>:</p><ul><li>Other Antifungal preparations, Topical Antifungal preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>: Terbinafine inhibits ergosterol biosynthesis via the inhibition of the Squalene Epoxidase enzyme. This results in fungal cell death due to increased membrane permeability caused by squalene accumulation.</p>', 'in-active', '1425_1742305645.jpg', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 18:47:25', '2025-05-13 05:12:34'),
(3324, 'Palorex', 'Palonosetron', 23.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Palorex 0.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Acute and delayed nausea and vomiting</li><li>Uncontrolled nausea and vomiting</li><li>Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute CINV resulting in on the day of treatment with certain types of chemotherapy</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Delayed CINV resulting on days later with certain types of chemotherapy</li><li>Radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (RINV)</li><li>Post-operative &amp; Post-discharge nausea and vomiting (PONV &amp; PDNV)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Anti-emetic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Nausea and vomiting are usually produced by chemotherapeutic agents by releasing serotonin from the enterochromaffin cells of the small intestine. The released serotonin (5-HT) activates 5-HT3 receptors located on vagal afferents to initiate the vomiting reflex. Postoperative nausea and vomiting is influenced by multiple patient, surgical, and anesthesia-related factors and is triggered by the release of serotonin (5-HT) in a cascade of neuronal events involving both the central nervous system and the gastrointestinal tract. The 5-HT3 receptor has been demonstrated to selectively participate in the emetic response.</p><p> Palorex 0.5 mg is a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist with a strong binding affinity for this receptor and little or no affinity for other receptors. By binding with this receptor, Palorex 0.5 mg inhibits the binding of serotonin to this receptor and also inhibits the vomiting reflex.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Palorex 0.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Usual dosage</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adult tablet dosage: 0.5 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adult IV dosage: A single IV dose of 0.075 mg should be administered over 10 seconds</li><li><strong>Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adult tablet dosage: 0.5 mg administered approximately 1 hour prior to the start of chemotherapy</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adult IV dosage: A single IV dose of 0.25 mg should be administered over 30 seconds approximately 30 minutes before the start of chemotherapy</li><li><strong>Radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting</strong>:</li><li> A single IV dose of 0.25 mg should be administered over 30 seconds approximately 30 minutes before each week of radiation fraction</li><li><strong>Post-operative nausea and vomiting</strong>:</li><li> A single IV dose of 0.075 mg should be administered over 10 seconds immediately before induction of anesthesia</li><li><strong>Children dosage</strong> (1 month to 17 years): A single IV dose at 20 mcg/kg body weight. The maximum dose is 1.5 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Palorex 0.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Instructions for I.V. Administration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">It should not be mixed with other drugs</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Flush the infusion line with normal saline before and after administration</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Parenteral drug products should be inspected visually for particulate matter and discoloration before administration</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Palorex 0.5 mg</strong></p><p> In controlled clinical trials, Palorex 0.5 mg injection has been safely administered with corticosteroids, analgesics, antiemetics/antinauseants, antispasmodics, and anticholinergic agents. Palorex 0.5 mg did not inhibit the antitumor activity of cisplatin, cyclophosphamide, cytarabine, doxorubicin, and mitomycin C in murine tumor models. Concomitant administration of Palorex 0.5 mg and metoclopramide has no significant pharmacokinetic interactions. In vitro studies indicated that Palorex 0.5 mg is not an inhibitor of CYP1A2, CYP2A6, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2D6, CYP2E1 &amp; CYP3A4/5 (CYP2C19 was not investigated), nor does it induce the activity of CYP1A2, CYP2D6, or CYP3A4/5. Therefore, the potential for clinically significant drug interactions with Palorex 0.5 mg appears to be low.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Palorex 0.5 mg is contraindicated in patients known to have hypersensitivity to the drug or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Palorex 0.5 mg</strong></p><p> The most common adverse reactions are headaches and constipation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>US FDA Pregnancy category B.</li><li>It is not known whether Palorex 0.5 mg is excreted in human milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>For IV administration only. Not for intradermal, subcutaneous, or IM administration.</li><li>Do not administer if particulate matter, cloudiness, or discoloration is noted.</li><li>Discard any unused solution. Do not save unused solution for later administration.</li><li>Do not mix with other medications.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Palorex 0.5 mg</strong></p><p> There is no known antidote to Palorex 0.5 mg. Overdose should be managed with supportive care.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool &amp; dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in patients below the age of 18 years have not been established. However, clinical trials show Palorex 0.5 mg is well-tolerated and effective from one month of age.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Pharmacokinetics analysis did not reveal any differences in Palorex 0.5 mg pharmacokinetics between patients ≥ 65 years of age and younger patients (18 to 64 years).</li><li><strong>Renal Function Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustments are needed with any degree of renal function impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Function Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustments are needed with any degree of hepatic function impairment.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustments or special monitoring are needed in elderly patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Intravenous</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea and vomiting associated with cancer chemotherapy: Physically and chemically stable at concentrations of 5 and 30 mcg/ml in glucose 5%, sodium chloride 0.9%, glucose 5% in lactated Ringer\'s for at least 48 hours at room temperature, exposed to light, and for 14 days under refrigeration.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Anti-emetic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Palorex 0.5 mg is a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist with a strong binding affinity for this receptor and little or no affinity for other receptors. It works by blocking the actions of serotonin, associated with nausea and vomiting, at 5-HT3 receptors. Palorex 0.5 mg may work both in the small intestine and the brain.</p><p> Pharmacokinetics: Palorex 0.5 mg exhibits linear dose-proportional pharmacokinetics over the dose range 1-90 mcg/kg in healthy subjects and cancer patients. It has a volume of distribution of approximately 6.9-7.9 L/kg, with approximately 62% bound to plasma proteins. Approximately 50% of the drug is metabolized into two inactive metabolites, and around 40% is metabolized via the kidney and liver enzymes CYP2D6, CYP3A4, and CYP1A2. About 50% of the drug undergoes metabolism. After a single intravenous dose, approximately 40% is excreted as unchanged drug in the urine after 144 hours. Palorex 0.5 mg has a longer half-life (40 hours) and greater 5-HT3 receptor binding affinity.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy category \'B\'.</li><li>It is not known whether Palorex 0.5 mg is excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Use in elderly patients: No dosage adjustment is recommended for elderly patients &gt;65 years of age.</li><li>Use in Children (1 month to 10 years): A single IV dose at 20 mcg/kg body weight, with a maximum dose of 1.5 mg.</li><li>Use in patients with impaired renal and hepatic function: No dosage adjustment is recommended in patients with renal and hepatic dysfunction.</li></ul>', 'active', '1206_1742305739.jpg', 63, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 18:48:59', '2025-03-18 18:48:59'),
(3325, 'Rosulip', 'Rosuvastatin Calcium', 18.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rosulip 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia (Familial and Non-familial)</li><li>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia (Familial)</li><li>Mixed Dyslipidemia (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs, Statins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Rosulip 10 mg is a selective and competitive inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme that converts 3-hydroxy-3-methyl glutaryl coenzyme A to mevalonate, a precursor of cholesterol. It produces its lipid-modifying effects in two ways:</p><ol><li>It increases the number of hepatic LDL receptors on the cell surface to enhance the uptake and catabolism of LDL.</li><li>It inhibits hepatic synthesis of VLDL, which reduces the total number of VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage of Rosulip 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dose range</strong>: 5-40 mg once daily. Use 40 mg dose only for patients not reaching LDL-C goal with 20 mg.</li><li><strong>HoFH</strong>: Starting dose 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with HeFH</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-10 mg/day for patients aged 8 to less than 10 years</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-20 mg/day for patients aged 10 to 17 years</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with HoFH</strong>: 20 mg/day for patients aged 7 to 17 years</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Rosulip 10 mg</strong></p><p> Rosulip 10 mg can be taken with or without food, at any time of day.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Rosulip 10 mg</strong></p><p> Remarkable drug interactions of Rosulip 10 mg include:</p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong>: Combining these medications increases its exposure. The recommended dose should not exceed 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrosil</strong>: It is advisable to avoid combining these drugs. If necessary, limit the dosage to 10 mg once daily when used together.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or atazanavir/ritonavir</strong>: When used in combination, these medications can elevate its exposure. The recommended dose should be limited to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants</strong>: Combining with coumarin anticoagulants can prolong the international normalized ratio (INR). Achieve a stable INR before initiating treatment and monitor it frequently until it remains stable during therapy.</li><li><strong>Concomitant lipid-lowering therapies</strong>: When used with fibrates and niacin products, there may be an increased risk of skeletal muscle effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Rosulip 10 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to product components</li><li>Liver disease, which may include unexplained persistent elevations in hepatic transaminase levels</li><li>Pregnant women and women who may become pregnant</li><li>Nursing mothers</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Rosulip 10 mg</strong></p><p> Rosulip 10 mg is generally well tolerated. The most frequent adverse events thought to be related to the medicine were:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The safety in pregnant women has not been established.</li><li>It is not known whether Rosulip 10 mg is excreted in human milk or not.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Skeletal muscle effects</strong> (e.g., myopathy and rhabdomyolysis): Risks increase with the use of the 40 mg dose, advanced age (&gt;65 years), hypothyroidism, renal impairment, and combination use with cyclosporine, lopinavir/ritonavir, atazanavir/ritonavir, or certain other lipid-lowering drugs.</li><li> Patients should be advised to promptly report unexplained muscle pain, tenderness, or weakness. Rosulip 10 mg can be discontinued if signs or symptoms appear.</li><li><strong>Liver enzyme abnormalities and monitoring</strong>: Persistent elevations in hepatic transaminases can occur. Liver enzymes should be monitored before and during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C temperature, protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children</strong>: The safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul>', 'active', '11_1742306050.jpg', 64, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 18:54:10', '2025-03-18 18:54:10');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3326, 'Daprosis', 'Daprodustat', 100.00, 7, 'Tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><span style=\"background-color: rgb(245, 245, 245); color: rgb(63, 82, 100);\">Daprosis is indicated for the treatment of anemia due to chronic kidney disease (CKD) in adults who have been receiving dialysis for at least four months.</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"background-color: rgb(245, 245, 245); color: rgb(63, 82, 100);\">Daprosis is not indicated for use:</span></p><ul><li>As a substitute for red blood cell transfusions in patients who require immediate correction of anemia.</li><li>For treatment of anemia of chronic kidney disease in patients who are not on dialysis.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1784_1742306262.jpg', 55, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 18:57:42', '2025-03-18 18:57:42'),
(3327, 'Trova', 'Atorvastatin Calcium', 20.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Trova 20 mg</strong></p><p> Trova 20 mg is indicated as an adjunct to diet to reduce elevated total cholesterol, LDL cholesterol, apolipoprotein B (Apo-B), and triglycerides levels in the following diseases when response to diet and other non-pharmacological measures is inadequate:</p><ul><li>To reduce total cholesterol and LDL cholesterol in patients with heterozygous and homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia.</li><li>To reduce elevated cholesterol and triglycerides in patients with mixed dyslipidemia (Fredrickson Type Ia and Ib).</li><li>For the treatment of patients with elevated serum triglyceride levels in hypertriglyceridemia (Fredrickson Type IV).</li><li>For the treatment of patients with dysbetalipoproteinemia (Fredrickson Type III).</li><li>To reduce cardiac ischemic events in patients with asymptomatic or mild to moderate symptomatic coronary artery disease with elevated LDL-cholesterol levels.</li><li>To reduce total and LDL-cholesterol concentrations in patients with hypercholesterolemia associated with or exacerbated by diabetes mellitus or renal transplantation.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs, Statins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Atorvastatin is a selective inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase. This enzyme is the rate-limiting enzyme responsible for the conversion of HMG-CoA to mevalonate, a precursor of sterols, including cholesterol. Atorvastatin lowers plasma cholesterol and lipoprotein levels by inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase and cholesterol synthesis in the liver and increases the number of hepatic LDL receptors on the cell surface for enhanced uptake and catabolism of LDL.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Trova 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Primary hypercholesterolemia and combined hyperlipidemia</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: Usually 10 mg once daily; if necessary, may be increased at intervals of at least 4 weeks to a maximum of 80 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Child (10-18 years)</strong>: Initially 10 mg once daily, increased if necessary at intervals of at least 4 weeks to a usual maximum of 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Familial hypercholesterolemia</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: Initially 10 mg daily, increased at intervals of at least 4 weeks to 40 mg once daily; if necessary, further increased to a maximum of 80 mg once daily (or 40 mg once daily combined with anion-exchange resin in heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Child (10-18 years)</strong>: Initially 10 mg once daily, increased if necessary at intervals of at least 4 weeks to a usual maximum of 80 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Prevention of cardiovascular events</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: Initially 10 mg once daily adjusted according to response.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Trova 20 mg</strong></p><p> The risk of myopathy during treatment with Atorvastatin is increased with concurrent administration of:</p><ul><li>Cyclosporine, fibric acid derivatives, erythromycin, azole antifungals, and niacin.</li><li> No clinically significant interactions were seen when Atorvastatin was administered with antihypertensives or hypoglycemic agents. Patients should be closely monitored if Atorvastatin is added to digoxin, erythromycin, oral contraceptives, colestipol, antacid, and warfarin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Trova 20 mg should not be used in patients with:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of this medication.</li><li>Active liver disease or unexplained persistent elevations of serum transaminases.</li><li>A history of serious adverse reactions to prior administration of HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Trova 20 mg</strong></p><p> Trova 20 mg is generally well-tolerated. The most frequent side effects related to Trova 20 mg are:</p><ul><li>Constipation, flatulence, dyspepsia, abdominal pain.</li><li> Other side effects include:</li><li>Infection, headache, back pain, rash, asthenia, arthralgia, myalgia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Trova 20 mg is contraindicated during pregnancy. Safety in pregnant women has not been established. No controlled clinical trials with atorvastatin have been conducted in pregnant women. Rare reports of congenital anomalies following intrauterine exposure to HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors have been received. Animal studies have shown toxicity to reproduction. Maternal treatment with atorvastatin may reduce fetal levels of mevalonate, which is a precursor of cholesterol biosynthesis. Atorvastatin should not be used in women who are pregnant, trying to become pregnant, or suspect they are pregnant. Treatment with atorvastatin should be suspended for the duration of pregnancy or until it has been determined that the woman is not pregnant.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether atorvastatin or its metabolites are excreted in human milk. In rats, plasma concentrations of atorvastatin and its active metabolites are similar to those in milk. Because of the potential for serious adverse reactions, women taking atorvastatin should not breastfeed their infants. Atorvastatin is contraindicated during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Liver effects</strong>: Liver function tests should be performed before the initiation of treatment and periodically thereafter. Atorvastatin should be used with caution in patients who consume substantial quantities of alcohol or have a history of liver disease. Atorvastatin therapy should be discontinued if markedly elevated CPK levels occur or myopathy is diagnosed or suspected.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Trova 20 mg</strong></p><p> Specific treatment is not available for atorvastatin overdose. The patient should be treated symptomatically and supportive measures instituted, as required. Liver function tests should be performed and serum CK levels should be monitored. Due to extensive atorvastatin binding to plasma proteins, hemodialysis is not expected to significantly enhance atorvastatin clearance.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Of the 39,828 patients who received Atorvastatin in clinical studies, 15,813 (40%) were ≥65 years old and 2,800 (7%) were ≥75 years old. No overall differences in safety or effectiveness were observed between these subjects and younger subjects. Other clinical experience has not identified differences in responses between the elderly and younger patients, but greater sensitivity of some older adults cannot be ruled out. Since advanced age (≥65 years) is a predisposing factor for myopathy, Atorvastatin should be prescribed with caution in the elderly.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Atorvastatin is contraindicated in patients with active liver disease, which may include unexplained persistent elevations in hepatic transaminase levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs, Statins</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Atorvastatin is a selective inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase. This enzyme is the rate-limiting enzyme responsible for the conversion of HMG-CoA to mevalonate, a precursor of sterols, including cholesterol. Atorvastatin lowers plasma cholesterol and lipoprotein levels by inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase and cholesterol synthesis in the liver and increases the number of hepatic LDL receptors on the cell surface for enhanced uptake and catabolism of LDL.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: Trova 20 mg should be used with caution in patients with hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Pediatric use</strong>: For patients aged 10 years and above, the recommended starting dose of atorvastatin is 10 mg per day with titration up to 20 mg per day. Trova 20 mg is not indicated in the treatment of patients below the age of 10 years.</li></ul>', 'active', '1218_1742306552.jpg', 69, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 19:02:32', '2025-03-18 19:02:32'),
(3328, 'Fitaro', 'Semaglutide', 1500.00, 1, 'Injection', '2.4 mg pre-filled syringe', 'Injection', '2.4 mg per 0.75 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml</strong></p><p> Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml is a glucagon-like peptide 1 (GLP-1) receptor agonist indicated as an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycemic control in adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus and to reduce the risk of major adverse cardiovascular events in adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus and established cardiovascular disease. It is also an adjunct to a reduced calorie diet and increased physical activity for chronic weight management in adult patients with an initial body mass index (BMI) of 30 kg/m² or greater (Obesity) or 27 kg/m² or greater (Overweight) in the presence of at least one weight-related comorbid condition (e.g., hypertension, type 2 diabetes mellitus, or dyslipidemia).</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><ul><li>0.25 mg injection: Each pre-filled syringe contains Semoglutido INN 0.25 mg in 0.5 ml solution for injection</li><li>0.50 mg injection: Each pre-filled syringe contains Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml INN 0.50 mg in 0.5 ml solution for injection</li><li>1 mg injection: Each pre-filled syringe contains Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml INN 1 mg in 0.5 ml solution for injection</li><li>1.7 mg injection: Each pre-filled syringe contains Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml INN 1.7 mg in 0.75 ml solution for injection</li><li>2.4 mg injection: Each pre-filled syringe contains Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml INN 2.4 mg in 0.75 ml solution for injection</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Subcutaneous injection</strong>:</p><ul><li>The starting dose: 0.25 mg Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml once weekly for 4 weeks subcutaneously.</li><li>Dose escalation schedule:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Week 1 through 4: 0.25 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Week 5 through 8: 0.5 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Week 9 through 12: 1 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Week 13 through 16: 1.7 mg</li><li><strong>Maintenance dose</strong> (Week 17 and onwards): 2.4 mg</li><li>If patients do not tolerate a dose during dose escalation, consider delaying dose escalation for 4 weeks.</li><li>If patients do not tolerate the maintenance dose of 2.4 mg, the dose can be temporarily decreased to 1.7 mg once weekly for a maximum of 4 weeks.</li><li>Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml is to be administered once weekly at any time of the day with or without meals.</li><li>Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml is to be injected subcutaneously in the abdomen, thigh, or upper arm.</li><li><strong>Missed dose</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If one dose is missed and the next scheduled dose is more than 2 days away (48 hours), administer Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml as soon as possible.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If one dose is missed and the next scheduled dose is less than 2 days away (48 hours), do not administer the dose. Resume dosing on the regular scheduled day of the week.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml</strong></p><p> Initiating Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml concomitant with an Insulin Secretagogue (e.g., Sulfonylurea) or with Insulin, consider reducing the dose of concomitantly administered insulin secretagogue (such as sulfonylureas) or insulin to reduce the risk of hypoglycemia. In clinical pharmacology trials, Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml did not affect the absorption of orally administered medications to any clinically relevant degree. Nonetheless, caution should be exercised when oral medications are concomitantly administered with Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml is contraindicated in patients with a personal or family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC) or in patients with Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia syndrome type 2 (MEN 2) and hypersensitivity to Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml. Serious hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis and angioedema, have been reported with Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml</strong></p><p> It is not known if Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml will cause thyroid tumors or medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC) in people. Tell your healthcare provider if you get a lump or swelling in your neck, hoarseness, trouble swallowing, or shortness of breath.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Risk of Thyroid C-Cell Tumors</strong>: It is unknown whether Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml causes thyroid C-cell tumors, including medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC), in humans.</li><li><strong>Pancreatitis</strong>: After initiation of Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml, observe patients carefully for signs and symptoms of pancreatitis (including persistent severe abdominal pain, sometimes radiating to the back and which may or may not be accompanied by vomiting). If pancreatitis is suspected, Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml should be discontinued and appropriate management initiated; if confirmed, Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml should not be restarted.</li><li><strong>Diabetic Retinopathy Complications</strong>: The effect of long-term glycemic control with Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml on diabetic retinopathy complications has not been studied. Patients with a history of diabetic retinopathy should be monitored for the progression of diabetic retinopathy.</li><li><strong>Never Share Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml Pen Between Patients</strong>: Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml pens must never be shared between patients, even if the needle is changed. Pen-sharing poses a risk for the transmission of blood-borne pathogens.</li><li><strong>Hypoglycemia with Concomitant Use of Insulin</strong>: Secretagogues or Insulin: Patients receiving Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml in combination with an insulin secretagogue (e.g., sulfonylurea) or insulin may have an increased risk of hypoglycemia. The risk of hypoglycemia may be lowered by a reduction in the dose of sulfonylurea (or another concomitantly administered insulin secretagogue) or insulin.</li><li><strong>Acute Kidney Injury</strong>: There have been post-marketing reports of acute kidney injury and worsening of chronic renal failure, which may sometimes require hemodialysis, in patients treated with GLP-1 receptor agonists.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Do not use in patients with previous hypersensitivity to Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml. If hypersensitivity reactions occur, discontinue the use of Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml; treat promptly per standard of care, and monitor until signs and symptoms resolve.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml</strong></p><p> In the event of overdose, appropriate supportive treatment should be initiated according to the patient’s clinical signs and symptoms.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store your Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml in the refrigerator at 2°C to 8°C. After the first use of the Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml, the pen can be stored for 56 days in a refrigerator (2°C to 8°C). Do not freeze Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml. Do not use Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml if it has been frozen. Unused Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml cartridges may be used until the expiration date printed on the label, if kept in the refrigerator (2°C to 8°C). Keep Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml away from heat and out of the light. Keep the pen cap on when not in use.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> GLP-1 receptor agonists</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml is a GLP-1 analogue with 94% sequence homology to human GLP-1. Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml acts as a GLP-1 receptor agonist that selectively binds to and activates the GLP-1 receptor, the target for native GLP-1. GLP-1 is a physiological hormone that has multiple actions on glucose, mediated by the GLP-1 receptors. The principal mechanism of protraction resulting in the long half-life of Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml is albumin binding, which results in decreased renal clearance and protection from metabolic degradation. Furthermore, Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml is stabilized against degradation by the DPP-4 enzyme. Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml reduces blood glucose through a mechanism where it stimulates insulin secretion and lowers glucagon secretion, both in a glucose-dependent manner. Thus, when blood glucose is high, insulin secretion is stimulated and glucagon secretion is inhibited. The mechanism of blood glucose lowering also involves a minor delay in gastric emptying in the early postprandial phase.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> There are limited data with Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml use in pregnant women. Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. There are no data on the presence of Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml in human milk, the effects on the breastfed infant, or the effects on milk production. Discontinue Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml in women at least 2 months before a planned pregnancy due to the long washout period for Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: The safety and efficacy of Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml have not been established in pediatric patients (younger than 18 years).</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No overall differences in safety or efficacy were detected between geriatric patients and younger patients, but greater sensitivity of some older individuals cannot be ruled out.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment of Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml is recommended for patients with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment of Fitaro 2.4 mg/0.75 ml is recommended for patients with hepatic impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '1466_1742306857.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 19:07:37', '2025-03-18 19:07:37'),
(3329, 'Topirva XR', 'Topiramate', 7.00, 8, 'Capsule', '8 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Topirva XR 25 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Epilepsy</strong>: Topirva XR 25 mg is indicated as monotherapy in adults and children aged 6 years and above with newly diagnosed epilepsy who have generalised tonic-clonic seizures or partial seizures with or without secondarily generalised seizures. Topirva XR 25 mg is indicated as adjunctive therapy for adults and children over 2 years of age who are inadequately controlled on conventional first-line antiepileptic drugs for partial seizures with or without secondarily generalised seizures; seizures associated with Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome, and primary generalised tonic-clonic seizures.</p><p> <strong>Migraine</strong>: Topirva XR 25 mg is indicated in adults for the prophylaxis of migraine headache. Prophylactic treatment of migraine may be considered in situations such as: adults experiencing three or more migraine attacks per month; frequent migraine attacks that significantly interfere with the patient\'s daily routine. Continuing therapy should be reviewed every six months.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Topirva XR 25 mg is a sulfamate-substituted monosaccharide, related to fructose. Topirva XR 25 mg is quickly absorbed after oral use. It enhances GABA-activated chloride channels and inhibits excitatory neurotransmission through actions on kainate and AMPA receptors. It has a specific effect on GluR5 kainate receptors and inhibits carbonic anhydrase, particularly subtypes II and IV. This weak inhibition is unlikely to be related to its anticonvulsant actions but may account for the bad taste and development of renal stones during treatment.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Topirva XR 25 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Epilepsy: Monotherapy</strong></p><p> Adults and children over 16 years: Titration should begin at 25 mg nightly for 1 week. The dosage should then be increased at 1- or 2-week intervals by increments of 25 or 50 mg/day, administered in two divided doses. The recommended initial target dose is 100 mg/day, and the maximum daily dose is 400 mg.</p><p> Children aged 6-16 years: Treatment should begin at 0.5 to 1 mg/kg nightly for the first week. The dosage should then be increased at 1- or 2-week intervals by increments of 0.5 to 1 mg/kg/day, administered in two divided doses. The recommended initial target dose range is 3 to 6 mg/kg/day, with higher doses up to 16 mg/kg/day rarely given.</p><p><strong>Epilepsy: Adjunctive Therapy</strong></p><p> Adults and children over 16 years: The minimal effective dose as adjunctive therapy is 200 mg/day. The usual dose is 200 mg to 400 mg, administered in two divided doses. Some patients may require doses up to 800 mg/day, the maximum recommended dose.</p><p> Children aged 2-16 years: The recommended daily dose is approximately 5 to 9 mg/kg/day, administered in two divided doses. Titration should begin at 25 mg nightly for the first week and then be increased at 1- or 2-week intervals by increments of 1 to 3 mg/kg/day.</p><p><strong>Migraine</strong></p><p> Adults and children over 16 years: Titration should begin at 25 mg nightly for 1 week. The dose should be increased in increments of 25 mg/day at 1-week intervals. The recommended daily dose is 100 mg/day, administered in two divided doses.</p><p> Children: Topirva XR 25 mg has not been studied in children under 16 years for migraine prophylaxis.</p><p><strong>Administration of Topirva XR 25 mg</strong></p><p> Topirva XR 25 mg tablets can be taken without regard to meals.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Topirva XR 25 mg</strong></p><p> Topirva XR 25 mg may increase the plasma clearance of estrogenic components in oral contraceptives.</p><p> Caution is needed when used with alcohol and other CNS depressants.</p><p> Patients on lithium should have their levels monitored, as Topirva XR 25 mg may increase systemic exposure at higher doses.</p><p> Co-administration with metformin, pioglitazone, or glibenclamide requires careful monitoring of diabetic control.</p><p> Topirva XR 25 mg can increase the risk of nephrolithiasis when used with agents that predispose to kidney stones.</p><p> Concomitant administration with valproic acid may result in hyperammonemia and requires careful monitoring.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to any component of this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Topirva XR 25 mg</strong></p><p> Common: Nausea, abdominal pain, dyspepsia, diarrhea, dry mouth, taste disturbance, weight loss, anorexia, paraesthesia, headache, fatigue, dizziness, speech disorder, drowsiness, insomnia, impaired memory and concentration, anxiety, depression, visual disturbances.</p><p> Less common: Suicidal ideation, metabolic acidosis, alopecia.</p><p> Very rare: Leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, serious skin reactions.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Topirva XR 25 mg is pregnancy category C. It should be used only if the benefit outweighs the risk to the fetus. The excretion of Topirva XR 25 mg through breast milk is not known, so caution is advised when prescribing to breastfeeding mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Gradual withdrawal is advised to minimize the potential for seizures or increased frequency during discontinuation. CNS-related side effects, including drowsiness and visual disturbances, may occur. Patients should be advised not to drive or operate machinery if affected.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Topirva XR 25 mg</strong></p><p> Overdose can result in severe metabolic acidosis. If the ingestion is recent, stomach lavage or emesis should be induced. Activated charcoal may help absorb the drug. Hemodialysis can effectively remove Topirva XR 25 mg from the body.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> <strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: In patients with renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt; 70 ml/min/1.73 m2), a reduced dose is recommended.</p><p> <strong>Hemodialysis</strong>: Topirva XR 25 mg is cleared more rapidly in patients undergoing hemodialysis, and supplemental doses may be required to maintain efficacy.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Topirva XR 25 mg blocks neuronal excitability through actions on GABA and glutamate receptors, enhancing GABA activity and inhibiting glutamate activity. It also blocks voltage-dependent sodium channels to prevent seizures and migraines.</p>', 'active', '528_1742307080.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 19:11:20', '2025-03-18 19:11:20'),
(3330, 'Optichlor', 'Chloramphenicol', 12.90, 1, 'Ophthalmic Ointment', '5 gm tube', 'Ophthalmic Ointment', '1 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Optichlor 1%</strong></p><p> Optichlor 1% is indicated in ocular infections, bacterial meningitis, anaerobic bacterial infections, anthrax, brain abscess, ehrlichiosis, gas gangrene, granuloma inguinale, infections caused by <em>H. influenzae</em>, listeriosis, plague, psittacosis, Q fever, severe gastroenteritis, severe melioidosis, severe systemic infections with <em>Campylobacter fetus</em>, tularaemia, Whipple\'s disease, otitis externa.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Ear Anti-Infectives &amp; Antiseptics, Eye Anti-Infectives &amp; Antiseptics, Macrolides.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Optichlor 1% inhibits bacterial protein synthesis by binding to the 50s subunit of the bacterial ribosome, thus preventing peptide bond formation by peptidyl transferase. It has both bacteriostatic and bactericidal action against <em>H. influenzae</em>, <em>N. meningitidis</em>, and <em>S. pneumoniae</em>.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Optichlor 1%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Eye</strong>:</li><li> Adults, children, and infants (all age groups): One or two drops 4 to 6 times a day should be placed in the infected eyes. If necessary, the frequency of dose can be increased. Treatment should be continued for approximately 7 days but should not be continued for more than three weeks without re-evaluation by the prescribing physician.</li><li><strong>For Ear</strong>:</li><li> 2 to 3 drops into the ear canal thrice or four times daily.</li><li><strong>Oral (for Bacterial meningitis, Anaerobic bacterial infections, Anthrax, Brain abscess, Ehrlichiosis, Gas gangrene, Granuloma inguinale, Infections caused by <em>H. influenzae</em>, Listeriosis, Plague, Psittacosis, Q fever, Severe gastroenteritis, Severe melioidosis, Severe systemic infections with <em>Campylobacter fetus</em>, Tularaemia, Whipple\'s disease)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adult: 50 mg/kg/day in 4 divided doses increased to 100 mg/kg/day for meningitis or severe infections due to moderately resistant organisms. Continue treatment after the patient\'s temperature has normalized for a further 4 days in rickettsial disease and 8-10 days in typhoid fever.</li><li>Child:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Premature and full-term neonates: 25 mg/kg/day in 4 divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Full-term infants &gt;2 weeks: 50 mg/kg/day in 4 divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children: 50 mg/kg/day in 4 divided doses increased to 100 mg/kg/day for meningitis or severe infections.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Optichlor 1%</strong></p><p> Decreased effects of iron and vitamin B12 in anaemic patients. Phenobarbitone and rifampin reduce the efficacy of Optichlor 1%. Impairs the action of oral contraceptives.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> History of hypersensitivity or toxic reaction to the drug; pregnancy, lactation; porphyria; parenteral administration for minor infections or as prophylaxis; preexisting bone marrow depression or blood dyscrasias.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Optichlor 1%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral</strong>: GI symptoms; bleeding; peripheral and optic neuritis, visual impairment, blindness; encephalopathy, confusion, delirium, mental depression, headache. Haemolysis in patients with G6PD deficiency.</li><li><strong>Ophthalmic application</strong>: Hypersensitivity reactions including rashes, fever, and angioedema.</li><li><strong>Ear drops</strong>: Ototoxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. Either studies in animals have revealed adverse effects on the fetus (teratogenic or embryocidal or other) and there are no controlled studies in women or studies in women and animals are not available. Drugs should be given only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Impaired renal or hepatic function; premature and full-term neonates. Monitor plasma concentrations to avoid toxicity.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Cap/susp: Store at temperature not exceeding 30°C.</li><li>Ophthalmic/otic preparation: Store between 2-8°C. Do not freeze. Protect from light.</li></ul>', 'active', '289_1742307358.jpg', 28, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 19:15:58', '2025-03-18 19:15:58'),
(3331, 'Zoclast', 'Zoledronic Acid [For hypercalcemia]', 6500.00, 1, 'IV Infusion', '4 mg vial', 'IV Infusion', '4 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zoclast 4 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Zoledronic Acid is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Hypercalcaemia of malignancy</li><li>Bone metastases associated with solid tumours</li><li>Osteolytic lesions associated with multiple myeloma</li><li>Corticosteroid-induced osteoporosis</li><li>Increased bone mass in men with osteoporosis</li><li>Osteoporosis in postmenopausal women</li><li>Paget\'s disease of bone</li><li>Prophylaxis of postmenopausal osteoporosis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Bisphosphonate preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Zoledronic acid belongs to the class of nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates and acts primarily on bone. It is an inhibitor of osteoclast-mediated bone resorption. The action of bisphosphonates on bone is based on their high affinity for mineralized bone. Intravenously administered Zoledronic acid is rapidly distributed to bone. The main molecular target of Zoledronic acid in the osteoclast is the enzyme farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase, but this does not exclude other mechanisms. Clinical studies in tumour-induced hypercalcemia demonstrated that the effect of Zoledronic acid is characterized by decreases in serum calcium and urinary calcium excretion. In addition to its inhibitory activity against bone resorption, Zoledronic acid also possesses anti-tumour activity, anti-angiogenic activity, anti-pain activity, cytostatic and pro-apoptotic activity on tumour cells, and synergistic cytostatic effect with other anti-cancer drugs. There was no accumulation of the active substance in plasma after multiple doses given every 28 days. Zoledronic acid is not metabolized and is excreted unchanged via the kidney.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Zoclast 4 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypercalcemia of malignancy</strong>: The maximum recommended dose of Zoledronic Acid in hypercalcemia of malignancy (serum calcium 12 mg/dl or 3.0 mmol/l) is 4 mg. The 4 mg dose must be given as a single-dose intravenous infusion. Dose adjustment of Zoledronic Acid is not necessary in treating patients for hypercalcemia of malignancy presenting with mild-to-moderate renal impairment. Re-treatment with Zoledronic Acid may be considered if serum calcium does not return to normal after initial treatment. It is recommended that a minimum of 7 days elapse before re-treatment, to allow for full response to the initial dose.</li><li><strong>Multiple myeloma and bone metastases of solid tumors</strong>: The recommended dose of Zoledronic Acid in patients with multiple myeloma and metastatic bone lesions from solid tumors is 4 mg infused every 3-4 weeks. Patients should also be administered an oral calcium supplement of 500 mg and 400 IU of Vitamin D daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zoclast 4 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Prior to administration, the required amount of concentrate from one vial must be further diluted with 100 ml of calcium-free infusion solution (0.9% w/v sodium chloride solution or 5% w/v glucose solution). The duration of infusion must not be less than 15 minutes. After addition of the solution to the infusion media, the infusion solution should be used as soon as possible. If storage of the infusion solution is necessary, hold at 2-8º C for not more than 24 hours. If refrigerated, the solution must be allowed to reach room temperature before administration. Zoledronic Acid must not be mixed with calcium or other divalent cation-containing infusion solutions, such as Lactated Ringer\'s solution, and should be administered as a single intravenous solution in a line separate from all other drugs.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Zoclast 4 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> In clinical studies, Zoledronic acid has been administered concomitantly with commonly used anticancer agents, diuretics, antibiotics, and analgesics without interactions. Caution is advised when Zoledronic acid is administered with aminoglycosides, since these agents may have an additive effect to lower serum calcium level for prolonged periods. In multiple myeloma patients, the risk of renal dysfunction may be increased when Zoledronic acid is used in combination with thalidomide. Concomitant use of loop diuretics with Zoledronic acid increases the risk of hypocalcemia. Caution is indicated when Zoledronic acid is used with other potentially nephrotoxic drugs.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> The drug is contraindicated if patients have hypersensitivity to the active substance or to any of the excipients or to any bisphosphonates; severe renal impairment (Creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min); pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zoclast 4 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> The post-dose side effects are:</p><ul><li>Headache, nausea, anorexia, fatigue, osteonecrosis of the jaw, anemia, bone pain, constipation, fever, vomiting, flu-like syndrome, hypocalcemia, myalgia, arthralgia, and hypophosphatemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Zoledronic acid is contraindicated during pregnancy and in breastfeeding women. It is also not recommended for use in children and adolescents below 18 years of age.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Patients must be appropriately hydrated prior to administration of Zoledronic Acid. This is especially important in the elderly and for patients receiving diuretic therapy. Adequate hydration can be achieved by the patient drinking two glasses of fluid (such as water) before and after the infusion. Serum levels of calcium, phosphate, magnesium, and potassium, as well as serum creatinine should be carefully monitored after initiating Zoledronic Acid therapy. If hypocalcemia, hypophosphatemia, or hypomagnesemia occurs, short-term supplemental therapy may be necessary. Moreover, careful renal function monitoring should be considered.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zoclast 4 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Clinical experience with acute overdose is limited. Overdose may cause hypocalcemia, hypophosphatemia, and hypomagnesemia. In such cases, reduction in serum levels of calcium, phosphorus, and magnesium should be corrected by intravenous administration of calcium gluconate, potassium or sodium phosphate, and magnesium sulfate respectively.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C prior to opening. Protect from moisture and light. Zoledronic Acid must be kept out of the reach and sight of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Patients with renal impairment</strong>: The use of Zoledronic Acid is not recommended in patients with severe renal impairment (Creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min). No dose adjustment is necessary in patients with creatinine clearance &gt;60 ml/min. Based on creatinine clearance, the following dose should be used in patients with impaired renal function:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl &gt; 60 ml/min: 4 mg (5 ml)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 50-60 ml/min: 3.5 mg (4.4 ml)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 40-49 ml/min: 3.3 mg (4.1 ml)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 30-39 ml/min: 3 mg (3.8 ml)</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p> Dilute the required amount in 100 mL of NaCl 0.9% or dextrose 5%.</p>', 'active', '841_1742307590.jpg', 1, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 19:19:50', '2025-03-18 19:19:50'),
(3332, 'Sinaflox', 'Flucloxacillin Sodium', 5.60, 4, 'Capsule', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sinaflox 250 mg</strong></p><p> Sinaflox 250 mg is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by Gram-positive organisms, including those resulting from penicillinase-producing staphylococci. These indications encompass a wide range of conditions:</p><ul><li><strong>Skin and Soft Tissue Infections</strong>: This includes boils, abscesses, carbuncles, and various infected skin conditions such as ulcers, eczema, acne, furunculosis, cellulitis, as well as infected wounds and burns.</li><li><strong>Respiratory Tract Infections</strong>: Flucloxacillin is effective against pneumonia, lung abscesses, empyema, sinusitis, pharyngitis, tonsillitis, and quinsy.</li><li><strong>Other Infections</strong>: It is also utilized for treating osteomyelitis, enteritis, endocarditis, urinary tract infections, meningitis, and septicaemia caused by organisms sensitive to Sinaflox 250 mg.</li><li><strong>Prophylactic Use</strong>: In addition, it serves as a prophylactic agent during major surgical procedures, particularly in cardiothoracic and orthopedic surgery when deemed appropriate.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Penicillinase-resistant penicillins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Flucloxacillin is effective against Gram-positive organisms, including those that produce penicillinase. It exhibits limited activity against Gram-negative bacilli. The mode of action of Flucloxacillin involves the inhibition of bacterial cell wall formation. As an isoxazolyl penicillin, Flucloxacillin combines key properties such as resistance to penicillinase hydrolysis, stability in gastric acid conditions, and activity against Gram-positive bacteria.</p><p>This bactericidal antibiotic is particularly valuable in combating penicillinase-producing staphylococci. Flucloxacillin works by disrupting peptidoglycan synthesis, a vital component of the bacterial cell wall that provides mechanical stability. In the final step of peptidoglycan synthesis, Flucloxacillin inhibits the transpeptidase enzyme responsible for cross-linking, specifically preventing the linkage of the terminal glycine residue to the fourth residue of the pentapeptide (D-alanine).</p><p>The bacterial cell wall weakens, leading to swelling and eventual rupture. Flucloxacillin\'s resistance to bacterial penicillinase likely arises from steric hindrance induced by its acyl side chain, preventing the opening of the β-lactam ring.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Sinaflox 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral Administration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: Take 250 mg four times a day. The dosage may be doubled in severe infections. For osteomyelitis and endocarditis, a daily dose of up to 8 grams is administered in 6-8 hourly divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (2-10 years)</strong>: Use half of the adult dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (Under 2 years)</strong>: Use a quarter of the adult dose.</li><li><strong>Parenteral Administration</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults or Elderly</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Intramuscular Injection</strong>: Take 250 mg four times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong>: Administer 250 mg to 1 gram four times daily, either through slow injection over 3 to 4 minutes or by intravenous infusion. In severe infections, all systemic doses can be doubled, with doses of up to 8 grams daily suggested for endocarditis or osteomyelitis.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">For children aged 2-10 years, use half of the adult dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For children under 2 years, use a quarter of the adult dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Sinaflox 250 mg</strong></p><p> Oral doses should be administered 1 hour before meals.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Sinaflox 250 mg</strong></p><p> For severe renal failure (creatinine clearance &lt;10 ml/min), it is advisable to contemplate a dose reduction or an extension of the dosing interval.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Sinaflox 250 mg is contraindicated in penicillin hypersensitive patients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sinaflox 250 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects affecting the gastrointestinal tract include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, dyspepsia, and other minor gastrointestinal disturbances. Reports have indicated occurrences of rashes, urticaria, purpura, fever, interstitial nephritis, hepatitis, and cholestatic jaundice.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Exercise caution when administering Sinaflox 250 mg to patients exhibiting signs of liver dysfunction. Additionally, be cautious when treating patients with an allergic predisposition.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> For severe renal failure (creatinine clearance &lt;10 ml/min), it is advisable to contemplate a dose reduction or an extension of the dosing interval.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p> Sinaflox 250 mg has been employed through alternative administration routes in combination with systemic therapy. It has been dosed at 250 mg to 500 mg per day via intra-articular injection, potentially dissolved in a 0.5% solution of lignocaine hydrochloride, as well as through intrapleural injection at a daily dosage of 250 mg. Using the powder for injection, 125 mg to 250 mg has been dissolved in 3 ml of sterile water and administered via nebulizer four times daily.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Penicillinase-resistant penicillins</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Flucloxacillin exhibits activity against Gram-positive organisms, including strains that produce penicillinase. Its efficacy against Gram-negative bacilli is limited. The mechanism of action involves the inhibition of bacterial cell wall formation. Flucloxacillin, classified as an isoxazolyl penicillin, possesses unique qualities such as resistance to hydrolysis by penicillinase, stability in gastric acid conditions, and effectiveness against Gram-positive bacteria.</p><p>This bactericidal antibiotic is particularly valuable in combatting penicillinase-producing staphylococci. Flucloxacillin disrupts bacterial cell wall synthesis, specifically interfering with peptidoglycan production. Peptidoglycan is a complex structure that provides mechanical stability to the cell wall. The final step in peptidoglycan synthesis, which involves cross-linking with the terminal glycine residue of the pentaglycin bridge connecting to the fourth residue of the pentapeptide (D-alanine), is hindered by Flucloxacillin. Consequently, the bacterial cell wall weakens, leading to cell swelling and eventual rupture. Flucloxacillin\'s resistance to bacterial penicillinase is likely attributed to steric hindrance caused by its acyl side chain, preventing the opening of the β-lactam ring.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The US FDA Pregnancy Category for Sinaflox 250 mg is B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies conducted in pregnant women. Since animal reproduction studies may not always accurately predict human responses, it is advisable to use this medication during pregnancy only when it is clearly necessary. Sinaflox 250 mg has been demonstrated to be excreted in human milk, so caution should be exercised when administering it to a lactating mother.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> In severe renal failure (creatinine clearance &lt;10 ml/min), a reduction in dose or an extension of dose interval should be considered.</p>', 'active', '1613_1742307995.jpg', 30, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 19:26:35', '2025-03-18 19:26:35');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3333, 'Riboclin', 'Clindamycin', 15.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '300 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Riboclin 300 mg</strong></p><p> Clindamycin has been shown to be effective in the treatment of the following infections when caused by susceptible anaerobic bacteria or susceptible strains of gram-positive bacteria such as Streptococci, Staphylococci, and Pneumococci:</p><ul><li>Upper respiratory tract infections</li><li>Lower respiratory tract infections</li><li>Skin and soft tissue infections</li><li>Bone and joint infections</li><li>Pelvic infections</li><li>Intra-abdominal infections</li><li>Septicemia and endocarditis</li><li>Dental infections</li></ul><p>As an alternative therapy, it is used in combination with quinine or amodiaquine for the treatment of multi-drug-resistant <em>Plasmodium falciparum</em> infection.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Macrolides</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Clindamycin is a lincosamide antibiotic. Clindamycin inhibits bacterial protein synthesis by binding to the 50S subunit of the ribosome. It has activity against aerobic gram-positive cocci, including:</p><ul><li>Staphylococcus aureus</li><li>Staphylococcus epidermidis (penicillinase and non-penicillinase producing strains)</li><li>Streptococci</li><li>Pneumococci</li></ul><p>It also acts on anaerobic gram-negative bacilli, including:</p><ul><li><em>Bacteroides</em> species</li><li><em>Fusobacterium</em> species</li><li>Anaerobic gram-positive non-spore forming bacilli, including:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Propionibacterium</em> species</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Eubacterium</em> species</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Actinomyces</em> species</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Peptostreptococcus</em> species</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Microaerophilic streptococci</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>C. perfringens</em></li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Riboclin 300 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Capsule &amp; Suspension Dosage (Adults)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Serious infections</strong>: 150 mg-300 mg every six hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>More severe infections</strong>: 300 mg-450 mg every six hours</li><li><strong>Pediatric Dosage</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Serious infections</strong>: 8-16 mg/kg/day, divided into three or four equal doses</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>More severe infections</strong>: 16-20 mg/kg/day, divided into three or four equal doses</li><li><strong>IM/IV Injection (Adults)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Serious infections</strong>: 600-1200 mg/day in 2-4 equal doses</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>More severe infections</strong>: 1200-2700 mg/day in 2-4 equal doses</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Life-threatening infections</strong>: Doses as high as 4800 mg daily may be used intravenously.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Single intramuscular injections of greater than 600 mg are not recommended.</li><li><strong>Neonates (less than 1 month)</strong>: 15 to 20 mg/kg/day in 3 to 4 equal doses</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients (1 month of age to 16 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Parenteral (IV/IM) administration: 20 to 40 mg/kg/day in 3 or 4 equal doses</li><li><strong>For β-hemolytic streptococcal infections</strong>, treatment should continue for at least 10 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Riboclin 300 mg</strong></p><p> Clindamycin enhances the action of other neuromuscular blocking agents. Therefore, it should be used with caution in patients receiving such agents. Antagonism has been demonstrated between Clindamycin and Erythromycin in vitro. Due to possible clinical significance, these two drugs should not be administered concurrently.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Clindamycin is contraindicated in patients previously found to be sensitive to Clindamycin or lincomycin or any of the ingredients of this medicine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Riboclin 300 mg</strong></p><p> The adverse effects reported with the use of Clindamycin include:</p><ul><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Oesophagitis and oesophageal ulcer</li><li>Nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea</li><li>Pruritus, skin rashes, and urticaria</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: Clindamycin crosses the placenta in humans. After multiple doses, amniotic fluid concentrations were approximately 30% of maternal concentrations. Clindamycin should be used in pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Clindamycin has been reported to appear in breast milk. Therefore, it is not recommended for nursing mothers unless clearly needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Clindamycin should be prescribed with caution in individuals with a history of gastrointestinal disease, particularly colitis.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Riboclin 300 mg</strong></p><p> Overdosage with orally administered Clindamycin has been rare. Adverse reactions similar to those seen with normal doses can be expected, but unexpected reactions may also occur. Haemodialysis and peritoneal dialysis are not effective in removing Clindamycin from the serum. Overdosage should be treated with simple gastric lavage. No specific antidote is known.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> For newborns and infants (birth to 16 years), appropriate monitoring of organ system functions is desirable when Clindamycin is administered.</p>', 'active', '1961_1742308460.jpg', 69, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 19:34:20', '2025-03-18 19:34:20'),
(3334, 'Trumen', 'Tramadol Hydrochloride', 22.14, 1, 'IV injection', '5 Injections', 'IV Injection', '100 mg per 2 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Trumen 100 mg/2 ml</strong></p><p> Tramadol is used for the treatment of moderate to severe painful conditions. These include:</p><ul><li>Postoperative pain</li><li>Colic and spastic pain</li><li>Cancer pain</li><li>Joint pain</li><li>Neck and back pain</li><li>Pain associated with osteoporosis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Tramadol is a centrally acting synthetic analgesic compound. It inhibits the reuptake of neurotransmitters—serotonin and noradrenaline—modifying the transmission of pain impulses by activating both descending serotonergic pathways and noradrenergic pathways involved in analgesia. The analgesic effects of Tramadol are mediated via stimulation of mu-opioid receptors and indirect modulation of central monoaminergic inhibitory pathways.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Trumen 100 mg/2 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Capsule or Tablet</strong>: Usual doses are 50 to 100 mg every four to six hours. For acute pain, an initial dose of 100 mg is required. For chronic painful conditions, an initial dose of 50 mg is recommended. Subsequent doses should be 50 to 100 mg administered every 4-6 hours. The total daily dosage should not exceed 400 mg.</li><li><strong>Sustained Release Capsule or Tablet</strong>: One SR capsule or tablet every 12 hours. The number of capsules will depend upon the severity of pain, but it should not be taken more frequently than every 12 hours. The total daily dosage should not exceed 400 mg.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: A dose of 50-100 mg may be given every 4 to 6 hours by intramuscular or intravenous infusion. For postoperative pain, the initial dose is 100 mg, followed by 50 mg every 10 to 20 minutes if necessary to a maximum of 250 mg in the first hour. Thereafter, doses are 50 to 100 mg every 4 to 6 hours up to a total daily dose of 600 mg.</li><li><strong>Suppository</strong>: Tramadol suppository should be administered rectally. For adults, the usual dose is 100 mg every 6 hours. In general, 400 mg per day (4 suppositories) is sufficient. However, higher doses may be used for cancer pain or severe pain after operations.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Trumen 100 mg/2 ml</strong></p><p> In general, physicians need not be concerned about drugs interacting with Tramadol. The monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitors represent the only drug class not recommended for combination with Tramadol. Concomitant administration of carbamazepine with Tramadol causes a significant increase in Tramadol metabolism, requiring a dose increase.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Tramadol is contraindicated in persons having hypersensitivity to this drug. It is also contraindicated in acute intoxication with alcohol, hypnotics, centrally acting analgesics, opioids, or psychotropic drugs.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Trumen 100 mg/2 ml</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Dizziness/vertigo</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Headache</li><li>Somnolence</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Pruritus</li><li>CNS stimulation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Sweating</li><li>Dyspepsia</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Diarrhea</li></ul><p>Less common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Malaise</li><li>Allergic reaction</li><li>Weight loss</li><li>Vasodilatation</li><li>Palpitations</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Anorexia</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>GI bleeding</li><li>Hepatitis</li><li>Stomatitis</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Safe use of Tramadol in pregnancy has not been established. Tramadol has been shown to cross the placenta. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Therefore, Tramadol should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the risk to the fetus.</li><li>Trumen 100 mg/2 ml should not be administered during breastfeeding as Tramadol and its metabolites have been detected in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Respiratory depression</strong>: Large doses of Tramadol administered with anaesthetic medications or alcohol may cause respiratory depression. Caution is needed in patients at risk for respiratory depression.</li><li><strong>Opioid dependence</strong>: Tramadol is not recommended for patients who are dependent on opioids.</li><li><strong>Concomitant CNS depressants</strong>: Tramadol should be used with caution and in reduced dosages when administering to patients receiving CNS depressants such as alcohol, opioids, anesthetic agents, phenothiazines, tranquilizers, or sedative hypnotics.</li><li><strong>Concomitant MAO inhibitors</strong>: Tramadol should be used with great caution in patients taking MAO inhibitors, as Tramadol inhibits the uptake of norepinephrine and serotonin.</li><li><strong>Intracranial pressure</strong>: Tramadol should be used with caution in patients with increased intracranial pressure, head injury, or acute abdominal conditions.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>In children from the age of 1 year, Trumen 100 mg/2 ml can be given in a dose of 1-2 mg/kg body weight.</li><li>Suppositories (100 mg Trumen 100 mg/2 ml) should not be administered in children and adolescents below the age of 14 years.</li><li>Trumen 100 mg/2 ml 100 mg SR Capsules have not been studied in children. Therefore, safety and efficacy have not been established, and the product should not be used in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Tramadol is a centrally acting synthetic analgesic compound. It inhibits the reuptake of neurotransmitters—serotonin and noradrenaline—modifying the transmission of pain impulses by activating both descending serotonergic pathways and noradrenergic pathways involved in analgesia. The analgesic effects of Tramadol are mediated via stimulation of mu-opioid receptors and indirect modulation of central monoaminergic inhibitory pathways.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Safe use of Tramadol in pregnancy has not been established. Tramadol has been shown to cross the placenta. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Therefore, Tramadol should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the risk to the fetus. Trumen 100 mg/2 ml should not be administered during breastfeeding as Tramadol and its metabolites have been detected in breast milk.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> In children from the age of 1 year, Trumen 100 mg/2 ml can be given in a dose of 1-2 mg/kg body weight. However, suppository (100 mg Trumen 100 mg/2 ml) should not be administered in children and adolescents below the age of 14 years. Trumen 100 mg/2 ml 100 mg SR Capsules have not been studied in children. Therefore, safety and efficacy have not been established and the product should not be used in children.</p>', 'active', '881_1742308735.jpg', 37, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 19:38:55', '2025-03-18 19:38:55'),
(3335, 'Piomin', 'Pioglitazone plus Metformin Hydrochloride', 11.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '15 mg plus 850 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Piomin 15 mg+850 mg</strong></p><p> Pioglitazone &amp; Metformin combination is indicated as an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycemic control in patients with type 2 diabetes who are already treated with a combination of Pioglitazone and Metformin or whose diabetes is not adequately controlled with Metformin alone or for those patients who have initially responded to Pioglitazone and require additional glycemic control.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Combination Oral hypoglycemic preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Pioglitazone depends on the presence of insulin for its mechanism of action. Pioglitazone decreases insulin resistance in the periphery and in the liver resulting in increased insulin-dependent glucose disposal and decreased hepatic glucose output. Pioglitazone is a potent and highly selective agonist for peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor-gamma (PPARg). Activation of PPARg nuclear receptors modulates the transcription of a number of insulin-responsive genes involved in the control of glucose and lipid metabolism.</p><p> Metformin hydrochloride improves glucose tolerance in patients with type 2 diabetes, lowering both basal and postprandial plasma glucose. Metformin decreases hepatic glucose production, decreases intestinal absorption of glucose, and improves insulin sensitivity by increasing peripheral glucose uptake and utilization. Unlike sulfonylureas, metformin does not produce hypoglycemia in either patients with type 2 diabetes or normal subjects and does not cause hyperinsulinemia.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Piomin 15 mg+850 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>General</strong>: The use of antihyperglycemic therapy in the management of type 2 diabetes should be individualized on the basis of effectiveness and tolerability while not exceeding the maximum recommended daily dose of Pioglitazone 45 mg and Metformin 2550 mg.</p><p> <strong>Dosage Recommendations</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Starting dose for patients inadequately controlled on Metformin monotherapy</strong>: Based on the usual starting dose of Pioglitazone (15-30 mg daily), Pioglitazone &amp; Metformin may be initiated at either the 15 mg/500 mg or 15 mg/850 mg tablet strength once or twice daily, and gradually titrated after assessing adequacy of therapeutic response.</li><li><strong>Starting dose for patients who initially responded to Pioglitazone monotherapy and require additional glycemic control</strong>: Based on the usual starting doses of Metformin (500 mg twice daily or 850 mg daily), Pioglitazone &amp; Metformin may be initiated at either the 15 mg/500 mg twice daily or 15 mg/850 mg tablet strength once daily, and gradually titrated after assessing adequacy of therapeutic response.</li><li><strong>Starting dose for patients switching from combination therapy of Pioglitazone plus Metformin as separate tablets</strong>: Pioglitazone &amp; Metformin may be initiated with either the 15 mg/500 mg or 15 mg/850 mg tablet strengths based on the dose of Pioglitazone and Metformin already being taken.</li><li> <strong>Maximum Recommended Dose</strong>: Pioglitazone &amp; Metformin tablets are available as a 15 mg Pioglitazone plus 500 mg Metformin or a 15 mg Pioglitazone plus 850 mg Metformin formulation for oral administration. The maximum recommended dose for Pioglitazone is 45 mg daily. The maximum recommended daily dose for Metformin is 2550 mg in adults.</li><li> <strong>Special Patient Populations</strong>: The initial and maintenance dosing of the combination of Pioglitazone and Metformin should be conservative in patients with advanced age, due to the potential for decreased renal function in this population. Generally, elderly, debilitated, and malnourished patients should not be titrated to the maximum dose of the combination of Pioglitazone and Metformin. Monitoring of renal function is necessary to aid in the prevention of Metformin-associated lactic acidosis, particularly in the elderly. Therapy with the combination of Pioglitazone and Metformin should not be initiated if the patient exhibits clinical evidence of active liver disease or increased serum transaminase levels (ALT greater than 2.5 times the upper limit of normal) at the start of therapy. Liver enzyme monitoring is recommended in all patients prior to initiation of therapy with the combination of Pioglitazone and Metformin and periodically thereafter.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Piomin 15 mg+850 mg</strong></p><p> (Same as above)</p><p><strong>Interaction of Piomin 15 mg+850 mg</strong></p><p> May impair vitamin B12 absorption. Increased plasma levels with strong CYP2C8 inhibitors (e.g., gemfibrozil). Decreased plasma levels with CYP2C8 inducers (e.g., rifampicin). Reduced metabolism with cationic drugs eliminated by renal tubular secretion.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Renal disease or renal dysfunction (e.g., as suggested by serum creatinine levels &gt;1.5 mg/dl in males, &gt;1.4 mg/dl in females, or abnormal creatinine clearance), which may also result from conditions such as cardiovascular collapse (shock), acute myocardial infarction, and septicemia.</li><li>Known hypersensitivity to Pioglitazone, Metformin, or any other component of this combination.</li><li>Acute or chronic metabolic acidosis including diabetic ketoacidosis with or without coma.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Piomin 15 mg+850 mg</strong></p><p> Generally, this combined preparation is well tolerated. However, the most common side effects are upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, peripheral edema, and headache, respectively. These are mild in severity.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women with this combination or its individual components. So, it should only be used if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Nursing mother</strong>: It is not known whether Pioglitazone and/or Metformin are secreted in human milk. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk, this combination should not be administered to a breastfeeding woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Pioglitazone exerts its antihyperglycemic effect only in the presence of insulin. Therefore, Pioglitazone should not be used in patients with type 1 diabetes or for the treatment of diabetic ketoacidosis.</li><li>Pioglitazone should be used with caution in combination, especially with insulin, hepatic insufficiency, and heart diseases.</li><li>Metformin is known to be substantially excreted by the kidney, and the risk of Metformin accumulation and lactic acidosis increases with the degree of impairment of renal function. Thus, patients with serum creatinine levels above the upper limit of normal for their age should not receive this combination.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Piomin 15 mg+850 mg</strong></p><p> In the event of Overdosage, appropriate supportive treatment should be initiated according to the patient\'s clinical signs and symptoms.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place. Protect from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> The initial and maintenance dose of Pioglitazone &amp; Metformin should be carefully selected in patients with advanced age due to the potential for decreased renal function in these populations. Monitoring of renal function is necessary to aid in the prevention of Metformin-associated lactic acidosis, particularly in the elderly. Pioglitazone &amp; Metformin should not be initiated if the patients exhibit clinical evidence of active liver disease. Liver enzyme monitoring is recommended in all patients prior to initiation of therapy with the combination of Pioglitazone and Metformin and periodically thereafter.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Combination Oral hypoglycemic preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Pioglitazone depends on the presence of insulin for its mechanism of action. Pioglitazone decreases insulin resistance in the periphery and in the liver resulting in increased insulin-dependent glucose disposal and decreased hepatic glucose output. Pioglitazone is a potent and highly selective agonist for peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor-gamma (PPARg). Activation of PPARg nuclear receptors modulates the transcription of a number of insulin-responsive genes involved in the control of glucose and lipid metabolism.</p><p> Metformin hydrochloride improves glucose tolerance in patients with type 2 diabetes, lowering both basal and postprandial plasma glucose. Metformin decreases hepatic glucose production, decreases intestinal absorption of glucose, and improves insulin sensitivity by increasing peripheral glucose uptake and utilization. Unlike sulfonylureas, metformin does not produce hypoglycemia in either patients with type 2 diabetes or normal subjects and does not cause hyperinsulinemia.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women with this combination or its individual components. So, it should only be used if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Nursing mother</strong>: It is not known whether Pioglitazone and/or Metformin are secreted in human milk. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk, this combination should not be administered to a breastfeeding woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> The initial and maintenance dose of Pioglitazone &amp; Metformin should be carefully selected in patients with advanced age due to the potential for decreased renal function in these populations. Monitoring of renal function is necessary to aid in the prevention of Metformin-associated lactic acidosis, particularly in the elderly. Pioglitazone &amp; Metformin should not be initiated if the patients exhibit clinical evidence of active liver disease. Liver enzyme monitoring is recommended in all patients prior to initiation of therapy with the combination of Pioglitazone and Metformin and periodically thereafter.</p>', 'active', '645_1742309055.jpg', 56, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 19:44:15', '2025-03-18 19:44:15'),
(3336, 'Sinaceph DS', 'Cephradine', 125.00, 1, 'Bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '250 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. These include:</p><ul><li>Upper respiratory tract infections: sinusitis, pharyngitis, tonsillitis, laryngo-tracheo bronchitis, and otitis media.</li><li>Lower respiratory tract infections: bronchitis (acute and chronic), lobar pneumonia, and bronchopneumonia.</li><li>Urinary tract infections: cystitis, urethritis, and pyelonephritis.</li><li>Skin and soft tissue infections: abscess, cellulitis, furunculosis, and impetigo.</li></ul><p>The following microorganisms are susceptible, in vitro, to Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml:</p><p> <strong>Gram-positive:</strong> Staphylococci (both penicillin-sensitive and resistant strains and penicillinase-producing species), Streptococci, Streptococcus pyogenes (beta haemolytic), Streptococcus pneumonia.</p><p> <strong>Gram-negative:</strong> Escherichia coli, Klebsiella spp, Proteus mirabilis, Haemophilus influenza, Shigella spp, Salmonella spp (including Salmonella typhi), Neisseria spp.</p><p>Many strains of E. coli and Staphylococcus aureus that produce the enzyme penicillinase and are thus ampicillin resistant are susceptible to Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml, which is unaffected by this enzyme.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> First generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml is a cephalosporin antibiotic with broad spectrum bactericidal activity against both gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria. It interferes with the synthesis of bacterial cell walls by inhibiting the transpeptidase enzyme. This weakens the bacterial cell wall, causing the cell to swell and rupture.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>For respiratory tract infections (other than lobar pneumonia) and skin and skin structure infections, the usual dose is 250 mg every 6 hours or 500 mg every 12 hours.</li><li>For lobar pneumonia, the usual dose is 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 g every 12 hours.</li><li>For uncomplicated urinary tract infections, the usual dose is 500 mg every 12 hours.</li><li>For more serious urinary tract infections, including prostatitis, the usual dose is 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 g every 12 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Children:</strong></p><p> The usual dose for children over nine months of age is 25 to 50 mg/kg/day administered in equally divided doses every 6 or 12 hours.</p><p> For otitis media due to H. influenzae, doses are from 75 to 100 mg/kg/day, administered in equally divided doses every 6 or 12 hours, but should not exceed 4 g per day.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> <strong>For oral administration</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>Urinary tract infections: 500 mg four times daily or 1 g twice daily.</li><li>Respiratory tract infections: 250 to 500 mg four times daily or 500 mg to 1 g twice daily.</li><li>Skin and soft tissue infections: 250 to 500 mg four times daily or 500 mg to 1 g twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Children:</strong></p><ul><li>Total daily dose of 25 to 50 mg/kg given in two or four equally divided doses.</li><li>Otitis media: Total daily dose of 75 to 100 mg/kg given in divided doses every 6 or 12 hours (maximum daily dosage: 4 gm).</li></ul><p><strong>Elderly:</strong></p><p> The normal adult dose is appropriate. Patients with impaired renal or hepatic function should be monitored during treatment.</p><p><strong>For injectable administration</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults:</strong> The usual dose is 2-4 gm daily in four equally divided doses, up to 8 gm daily. For prophylaxis, a single preoperative dose of 1-2 gm intramuscularly or intravenously is given.</p><p> <strong>Children:</strong> The dose is 50-100 mg/kg daily in four equally divided doses, up to 300 mg/kg daily in severe infection.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> The concomitant use of nephrotoxic drugs such as aminoglycosides with Cefradine may increase the risk of kidney damage. Diuretics (e.g. frusemide, ethacrynic acid) and probenecid enhance the possibility of renal toxicity.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml should not be used in patients with known or suspected hypersensitivity to cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Limited essentially to gastrointestinal disturbances and, on occasion, hypersensitivity phenomena. The latter are more likely to occur in individuals who have previously demonstrated hypersensitivity and those with a history of allergy, asthma, hay fever, or urticaria. Skin reactions have occasionally been reported.</p><p> Rare side effects include:</p><ul><li>Glossitis, heartburn, dizziness, tightness in the chest, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, vaginitis, candida overgrowth.</li><li>Skin and hypersensitivity reactions include urticaria, skin rashes, joint pains, and edema.</li><li>Blood and lymphatic system disorders: thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, agranulocytosis, aplastic anemia, and hemolytic anemia.</li><li>Immune system disorders: fever, serum sickness-like reactions, anaphylaxis.</li><li>Psychiatric disorders: confusion, sleep disturbances.</li><li>Nervous system disorders: hyperactivity, hypertonia, dizziness, nervousness; rarely headache.</li><li>Hepatobiliary disorders: liver enzyme disturbances, transient hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice.</li><li>Renal and urinary disorders: reversible interstitial nephritis.</li><li>Investigations: elevation of blood urea nitrogen, serum creatinine, alanine aminotransferase, aspartate aminotransferase, total bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> US FDA Pregnancy Category of Cefradine is B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</p><p> Cefradine has been shown to be excreted in human milk, so caution should be exercised when Cefradine is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Prolonged use of an anti-infective may result in the development of superinfection due to the emergence of resistant organisms. Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml should be administered with care to patients hypersensitive to penicillins because of the risk of cross-sensitivity between beta-lactam antibiotics.</p><p> Cephalosporin antibiotics may cause a positive result in Coombs’ testing. When Coombs testing is performed on neonates whose mothers received cephalosporins prior to labor, it should be noted that a positive result may be due to the drug.</p><p> Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml may cause a false positive urine glucose result when Benedict’s or Fehling’s solutions or tablets such as Clinitest are used in the testing. This does not occur with enzyme-based tests (e.g. Clinistix, Diastix).</p><p> Dosage adjustment is necessary in renal impairment. This product contains lactose. Patients with rare hereditary problems of galactose intolerance, Lapp lactase deficiency, or glucose-galactose malabsorption should not take this medicine.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> The symptoms of Cefradine overdose are non-specific and generally include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and gastric upsets. Treatment is mainly supportive, although gastric lavage may be necessary if a large amount has been ingested.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml Suspension should be freshly prepared. Reconstituted Suspension should be used within 7 days if kept at room temperature or within 14 days if kept in a refrigerator.</p><p> Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml Injection solutions should be used within 2 hours when kept at room temperature. When stored at 5°C, solutions retain potency for 12 hours.</p><p> Reconstituted solutions may vary in color from light to straw yellow; however, this does not affect the potency.</p><p> Do not use later than the date of expiry. Keep all medicines out of the reach of children. To be dispensed only on the prescription of a registered physician.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> First generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml is a semisynthetic broad spectrum bactericidal antibiotic, active against infections caused by both gram-positive and gram-negative microorganisms. Both penicillinase-producing and non-producing staphylococci are sensitive to Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml.</p><p> The main site of action is the bacterial cell wall. The cell wall of sensitive organisms contains peptidoglycan. Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml inhibits the cross-linking process, resulting in a cell wall with many pores, leading to bacterial lysis due to external osmotic pressure.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Although animal studies have not demonstrated any teratogenicity, safety in pregnancy has not been established. Sinaceph DS 250 mg/5 ml is excreted in breast milk and should be used with caution in lactating mothers. Since the medicine may cause dizziness, patients should be cautioned about operating hazardous machinery, including automobiles.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> <strong>Renal Impairment:</strong></p><ul><li>CrCl &gt;20 ml/min: 500 mg every 6 hours</li><li>CrCl 5-20 ml/min: 250 mg every 6 hours</li><li>CrCl &lt;5 ml/min: 250 mg every 50-70 hours</li><li> Recommendations for patients on chronic, intermittent hemodialysis:</li><li>250 mg at the start of hemodialysis</li><li>250 mg 6 to 12 hours after the start</li><li>250 mg 36 to 48 hours after the start</li><li>250 mg at the start of the next hemodialysis session if more than 30 hours have elapsed since the last dose.</li></ul><p>Additional Information for all patients:</p><p> Regardless of patient age or weight, higher doses of up to 1 gm four times daily may be required for chronic or severe infections.</p><p> Treatment should continue for at least 2 to 3 days after symptoms have resolved or bacteria have been eradicated. For preventing rheumatic fever or glomerulonephritis from infections with hemolytic streptococci, treatment     </p><p> should last for at least 10 days. Regular monitoring is required for chronic urinary tract infections, both clinically and bacteriologically.</p>', 'active', '83_1742309394.jpg', 30, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 19:49:54', '2025-03-18 19:49:54'),
(3337, 'Leroid', 'Levothyroxine Sodium', 1.10, 15, 'tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Leroid 25 mcg: Overview</strong></p><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypothyroidism:</strong> Used as replacement therapy in hypothyroidism of any etiology. It should not be used in transient hypothyroidism during the recovery phase of subacute thyroiditis.</li><li><strong>Thyroid conditions:</strong> Used to suppress Thyroid Stimulating Hormone (TSH) levels in goitres, nodules, and after treatment for thyroid cancer (radiological or surgical).</li><li><strong>Goitrogenic effects:</strong> For the suppression of thyroid-stimulating effects of other drugs, like Lithium.</li><li><strong>Diagnostic aid:</strong> Used in suppression tests.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong> Thyroid drugs &amp; hormones.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Leroid 25 mcg contains synthetic Levothyroxine (T4), identical to the natural thyroid hormone produced by the thyroid gland. T4 is converted to the active form, Triiodothyronine (T3), in peripheral tissues. T4 binds to Thyroxine Binding Globulin (TBG), prolonging its half-life in circulation (6-7 days). In conditions like hypothyroidism, T4\'s half-life can be extended. Levothyroxine has a steady effect with once-daily dosing due to its long half-life.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><p><strong>Adults:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial dose:</strong> 25-50 mcg/day, with gradual increases at 6-8 week intervals.</li><li><strong>Severe hypothyroidism:</strong> Start with 12.5-25 mcg/day and increase by 25 mcg/day every 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Secondary or tertiary hypothyroidism:</strong> Adjust until the patient is euthyroid with serum free T4 levels in the upper normal range.</li><li><strong>Cardiac disease or older patients:</strong> 1.7 mcg/kg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Newborns:</strong> 10-15 mcg/kg/day, with lower doses for cardiac risk infants.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Infants and Children:0-3 months:</strong> 10-15 mcg/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>3-6 months:</strong> 8-10 mcg/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>6-12 months:</strong> 6-8 mcg/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1-5 years:</strong> 5-6 mcg/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>6-12 years:</strong> 4-5 mcg/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>&gt;12 years (incomplete puberty):</strong> 2-3 mcg/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Growth/puberty complete:</strong> 1.7 mcg/kg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>For goitres and thyroid cancer suppression:</strong> Typically 2.6 mcg/kg/day.</p><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Tricyclic antidepressants with Levothyroxine may increase both therapeutic and toxic effects.</li><li>Sertraline may increase Levothyroxine requirements.</li><li>Diabetic agents or insulin may require higher doses when starting Levothyroxine therapy.</li><li>Digitalis glycosides may be less effective when transitioning from hypothyroid to euthyroid.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Untreated thyrotoxicosis, acute myocardial infarction, or uncorrected adrenal failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General:</strong> Fatigue, weight loss, heat intolerance, excessive sweating.</li><li><strong>CNS:</strong> Anxiety, irritability, insomnia, headache.</li><li><strong>Musculoskeletal:</strong> Tremors, muscle weakness.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular:</strong> Palpitations, arrhythmias, tachycardia.</li><li><strong>Respiratory:</strong> Dyspnea.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Diarrhea, abdominal cramps, vomiting.</li><li><strong>Dermatologic:</strong> Hair loss.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category A, but may increase Levothyroxine needs.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Levothyroxine is minimally excreted in milk, but caution is advised.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adrenal insufficiency:</strong> Should be treated before starting Levothyroxine if hypothyroidism is due to pituitary dysfunction.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular concerns:</strong> Start doses cautiously in those with cardiovascular disease, as high doses can worsen angina, arrhythmias, or lead to myocardial infarction.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Agitation, tachycardia, arrhythmias, confusion, hyperactivity, vomiting.</li><li>Severe effects: Coma, shock, and even death.</li><li>Treatment: Symptomatic, reduce or temporarily discontinue dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong> Store below 30°C, in a dry place, and protect from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong> Thyroid drugs &amp; hormones.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Levothyroxine (T4) mimics the body\'s natural thyroid hormone, supporting metabolism and energy regulation. It helps normalize thyroid function in hypothyroidism, with a long half-life ensuring steady T3 levels from a single daily dose.</p>', 'active', '982_1742309711.jpg', 9, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 19:55:11', '2025-03-18 19:55:11'),
(3338, 'Leroid', 'Levothyroxine Sodium', 2.00, 15, 'tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p>', 'active', '1855_1746361585.jpg', 9, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 19:55:12', '2025-05-04 16:26:25'),
(3339, 'Leroid', 'Levothyroxine Sodium', 1.10, 15, 'tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Leroid 25 mcg: Overview</strong></p><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypothyroidism:</strong> Used as replacement therapy in hypothyroidism of any etiology. It should not be used in transient hypothyroidism during the recovery phase of subacute thyroiditis.</li><li><strong>Thyroid conditions:</strong> Used to suppress Thyroid Stimulating Hormone (TSH) levels in goitres, nodules, and after treatment for thyroid cancer (radiological or surgical).</li><li><strong>Goitrogenic effects:</strong> For the suppression of thyroid-stimulating effects of other drugs, like Lithium.</li><li><strong>Diagnostic aid:</strong> Used in suppression tests.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong> Thyroid drugs &amp; hormones.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Leroid 25 mcg contains synthetic Levothyroxine (T4), identical to the natural thyroid hormone produced by the thyroid gland. T4 is converted to the active form, Triiodothyronine (T3), in peripheral tissues. T4 binds to Thyroxine Binding Globulin (TBG), prolonging its half-life in circulation (6-7 days). In conditions like hypothyroidism, T4\'s half-life can be extended. Levothyroxine has a steady effect with once-daily dosing due to its long half-life.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><p><strong>Adults:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial dose:</strong> 25-50 mcg/day, with gradual increases at 6-8 week intervals.</li><li><strong>Severe hypothyroidism:</strong> Start with 12.5-25 mcg/day and increase by 25 mcg/day every 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Secondary or tertiary hypothyroidism:</strong> Adjust until the patient is euthyroid with serum free T4 levels in the upper normal range.</li><li><strong>Cardiac disease or older patients:</strong> 1.7 mcg/kg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Newborns:</strong> 10-15 mcg/kg/day, with lower doses for cardiac risk infants.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Infants and Children:0-3 months:</strong> 10-15 mcg/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>3-6 months:</strong> 8-10 mcg/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>6-12 months:</strong> 6-8 mcg/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1-5 years:</strong> 5-6 mcg/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>6-12 years:</strong> 4-5 mcg/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>&gt;12 years (incomplete puberty):</strong> 2-3 mcg/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Growth/puberty complete:</strong> 1.7 mcg/kg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>For goitres and thyroid cancer suppression:</strong> Typically 2.6 mcg/kg/day.</p><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Tricyclic antidepressants with Levothyroxine may increase both therapeutic and toxic effects.</li><li>Sertraline may increase Levothyroxine requirements.</li><li>Diabetic agents or insulin may require higher doses when starting Levothyroxine therapy.</li><li>Digitalis glycosides may be less effective when transitioning from hypothyroid to euthyroid.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Untreated thyrotoxicosis, acute myocardial infarction, or uncorrected adrenal failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General:</strong> Fatigue, weight loss, heat intolerance, excessive sweating.</li><li><strong>CNS:</strong> Anxiety, irritability, insomnia, headache.</li><li><strong>Musculoskeletal:</strong> Tremors, muscle weakness.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular:</strong> Palpitations, arrhythmias, tachycardia.</li><li><strong>Respiratory:</strong> Dyspnea.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Diarrhea, abdominal cramps, vomiting.</li><li><strong>Dermatologic:</strong> Hair loss.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category A, but may increase Levothyroxine needs.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Levothyroxine is minimally excreted in milk, but caution is advised.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adrenal insufficiency:</strong> Should be treated before starting Levothyroxine if hypothyroidism is due to pituitary dysfunction.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular concerns:</strong> Start doses cautiously in those with cardiovascular disease, as high doses can worsen angina, arrhythmias, or lead to myocardial infarction.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Agitation, tachycardia, arrhythmias, confusion, hyperactivity, vomiting.</li><li>Severe effects: Coma, shock, and even death.</li><li>Treatment: Symptomatic, reduce or temporarily discontinue dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong> Store below 30°C, in a dry place, and protect from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong> Thyroid drugs &amp; hormones.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Levothyroxine (T4) mimics the body\'s natural thyroid hormone, supporting metabolism and energy regulation. It helps normalize thyroid function in hypothyroidism, with a long half-life ensuring steady T3 levels from a single daily dose.</p>', 'in-active', '1782_1742309713.jpg', 9, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 19:55:13', '2025-05-08 20:15:38'),
(3340, 'Zixol', 'Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate', 30.00, 1, 'Syrup', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '10 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zixol 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Zixol 10 mg/5 ml is indicated in zinc deficiency and/or zinc losing conditions. Zinc deficiency can occur as a result of inadequate diet or malabsorption. Excessive loss of zinc can occur in trauma, burns, diarrhoea, and protein-losing conditions. A zinc supplement is given until clinical improvement occurs, but it may need to be continued in severe malabsorption, metabolic disease, or in zinc-losing states.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Specific mineral preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Zinc sulphate monohydrate is an essential trace element and is involved in a number of body enzyme systems. The body needs zinc for normal growth and health. Zinc is also vital for sexual maturation and reproduction, dark vision adaptation, olfactory and gustatory activity, insulin storage &amp; release, and for a variety of host immune defenses. Zinc deficiency may lead to impaired immune function, delayed wound healing, a decrease in sense of taste and smell, a reduced ability to fight infections, poor night vision, increased risk of abortion, alopecia, mental lethargy, skin changes, and poor development of reproductive organs.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Zixol 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Child under 10 kg: 5 ml (1 teaspoonful) 2 times daily after food.</li><li>Child between 10-30 kg: 10 ml (2 teaspoonfuls) 1-3 times daily after food.</li><li>Adults and child over 30 kg: 20 ml (4 teaspoonfuls) 1-3 times daily after food.</li><li> This drug is most effective if taken at least 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals. However, if it causes stomach upset, it may be taken with a meal.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zixol 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> For dispersible tablet:</p><ul><li>Place the tablet in a teaspoon.</li><li>Add an adequate amount of water.</li><li>Let the tablet dissolve completely.</li><li>Give the entire spoonful solution.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zixol 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Concomitant intake of a tetracycline and zinc may decrease the absorption of both the tetracycline and zinc. Similarly, concomitant administration of zinc and quinolone drugs may also decrease the absorption of both. Concomitant intake of penicillamine and zinc may decrease absorption of zinc.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> It is contraindicated in those who are hypersensitive to any component of the ingredients of this preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zixol 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Zinc may cause nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, stomach upset, heartburn, and gastritis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The safety of this product in human pregnancy has not been established. Zinc crosses the placenta and is present in breast milk.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> In acute renal failure, zinc accumulation may occur in the body; so dose adjustment is needed.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '475_1742309770.jpg', 8, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 19:56:10', '2025-03-18 19:56:10');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3341, 'Aristopin', 'Atropine Sulfate', 150.00, 1, 'x 5 ml drop', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '0.01 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Aristopin 5 ml</strong></h3><p>Atropine is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Non-ulcer dyspepsia</li><li>Irritable bowel syndrome</li><li>Diverticular disease</li><li>Bradycardia</li><li>Organophosphorus poisoning</li><li>Premedication in anesthesia</li><li>Poisoning or overdosage with compounds having muscarinic actions</li><li>Ophthalmic inflammatory eye disorders</li><li>Eye refraction</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Anticholinergics (antimuscarinics)/Anti-spasmodics</li><li>Mydriatic and Cycloplegic agents</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Atropine binds to and inhibits muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, producing a range of anticholinergic effects. It competitively blocks muscarinic receptors in tissues such as the heart, intestines, bronchial muscles, iris, and secretory glands, with some central nervous system stimulation. It:</p><ul><li>Abolishes bradycardia</li><li>Reduces heart block due to vagal activity</li><li>Relaxes smooth muscles in the bronchi and gut</li><li>Reduces glandular secretions</li><li>Has mydriatic (pupil dilation) and cycloplegic (paralysis of accommodation) effects.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Aristopin 5 ml</strong></h3><h4><strong>Adult Dosing:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Bradycardia (IV)</strong>: 500 mcg every 3-5 minutes (Total: 3 mg).</li><li><strong>Organophosphorus poisoning (IV/IM)</strong>: 2 mg every 10-30 minutes until muscarinic effects disappear or atropine toxicity appears.</li><li><strong>Premedication in anesthesia (IM/SC)</strong>: 300-600 mcg 30-60 minutes before anesthesia.</li><li><strong>Poisoning/Overdosage with muscarinic agents (IV/IM/SC)</strong>: 0.6-1 mg, repeat every 2 hours.</li><li><strong>Ophthalmic Inflammatory eye disorders</strong> (0.5-1% solution): 1-2 drops, 4 times/day.</li><li><strong>Ophthalmic refraction</strong> (1% solution): 1 drop twice daily for 1-2 days before the procedure.</li><li><strong>Non-ulcer dyspepsia, IBS, Diverticular disease (oral)</strong>: 0.6-1.2 mg as a single dose at bedtime.</li></ul><h4><strong>Pediatric Dosing for Anesthesia:</strong></h4><ul><li>7 to 16 lbs: 0.1 mg (IV/IM/SC)</li><li>17 to 24 lbs: 0.15 mg (IV/IM/SC)</li><li>24 to 40 lbs: 0.2 mg (IV/IM/SC)</li><li>40 to 65 lbs: 0.3 mg (IV/IM/SC)</li><li>65 to 90 lbs: 0.4 mg (IV/IM/SC)</li><li>Over 90 lbs: 0.4-0.6 mg (IV/IM/SC)</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction of Aristopin 5 ml</strong></h3><p>Additive anticholinergic effects may occur with:</p><ul><li>Quinidine</li><li>Antidepressants</li><li>Some antihistamines</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Glaucoma</li><li>Chronic respiratory disease</li><li>Sick sinus syndrome</li><li>Thyrotoxicosis</li><li>Cardiac failure</li><li>Pyloric stenosis</li><li>Prostatic hypertrophy</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Aristopin 5 ml</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Injection</strong>: Dry mouth, dysphagia, constipation, flushing, dryness of skin, tachycardia, palpitations, arrhythmias, mydriasis, photophobia, cycloplegia, raised intraocular pressure. Toxic doses can cause:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tachycardia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hyperpyrexia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Restlessness</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Confusion</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Excitement</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hallucinations</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Delirium, progressing to circulatory failure and respiratory depression.</li><li><strong>Eye Drops/Ointment</strong>: Systemic toxicity (especially in children), irritation, hyperemia, edema, conjunctivitis, increased intraocular pressure.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: Animal studies have not been conducted with atropine. It is unknown whether atropine can harm the fetus or affect reproduction capacity. Atropine should only be given to pregnant women if clearly needed.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Reflux esophagitis</li><li>Elderly patients</li><li>Infants and children</li><li>Pregnancy</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Aristopin 5 ml</strong></h3><p>Overdose may cause:</p><ul><li>Hyperthermia</li><li>Hypertension</li><li>Increased respiratory rate</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>CNS stimulation (may progress to CNS depression, coma, respiratory failure, and death).</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><p>Store atropine at room temperature (20 to 25°C). Keep away from heat, moisture, and light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><ul><li>Anticholinergics (antimuscarinics)/Anti-spasmodics</li><li>Mydriatic and Cycloplegic agents</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Atropine competitively blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, leading to:</p><ul><li>Relaxation of smooth muscles</li><li>Reduction in glandular secretions</li><li>Mydriasis and cycloplegia effects</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h3><p>Atropine falls under <strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>. There is insufficient data to confirm its safety for pregnant women. It should only be used if the potential benefits outweigh the risks.</p>', 'active', '1752_1742309960.jpg', 29, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 19:59:20', '2025-05-02 23:50:00'),
(3342, 'TPS', 'Thiopental Sodium', 100.67, 1, 'IV injection', '1 gm vial injection', 'IV Injection', '1 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of TPS 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> TPS 1 gm/vial for Injection is indicated:</p><ul><li>As the sole anesthetic agent for brief (15 minutes) procedures</li><li>For induction of anesthesia prior to administration of other anesthetic agents</li><li>To supplement regional anesthesia</li><li>To provide hypnosis during balanced anesthesia with other agents for analgesia or muscle relaxation</li><li>For the control of convulsive states during or following inhalation anesthesia, local anesthesia, or other causes</li><li>In neurosurgical patients with increased intracranial pressure, if adequate ventilation is provided</li><li>For narcoanalysis and narcosynthesis in psychiatric disorders</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p> Thiopental 500 mg injection: Each box contains 1 vial of 500 mg TPS 1 gm/vial for Injection (a sterile mixture of TPS 1 gm/vial and anhydrous Sodium Carbonate as a buffer) &amp; 1 ampoule of 10 ml Water for Injection.</p><p> Thiopental 1 gm injection: Each box contains 1 vial of 1 gm TPS 1 gm/vial for Injection (a sterile mixture of TPS 1 gm/vial and anhydrous Sodium Carbonate as a buffer) &amp; 2 ampoules of 10 ml Water for Injection.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> General (Intravenous) anesthetics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Thiopental, a barbiturate, is used for the induction of anesthesia prior to the use of other general anesthetic agents and for induction of anesthesia for short surgical, diagnostic, or therapeutic procedures associated with minimal painful stimuli. Thiopental is an ultrashort-acting depressant of the central nervous system which induces hypnosis and anesthesia, but not analgesia. It produces hypnosis within 30 to 40 seconds of intravenous injection. Recovery after a small dose is rapid, with some somnolence and retrograde amnesia. Repeated intravenous doses lead to prolonged anesthesia because fatty tissues act as a reservoir; they accumulate Pentothal in concentrations 6 to 12 times greater than the plasma concentration, and then release the drug slowly to cause prolonged anesthesia. Thiopental binds at a distinct binding site associated with a Cl- ionopore at the GABAA receptor, increasing the duration of time for which the Cl- ionopore is open. The post-synaptic inhibitory effect of GABA in the thalamus is, therefore, prolonged.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of TPS 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> Thiopental Injection is administered intravenously normally as a 2.5% w/v solution. On occasions, it may be administered as a 5% w/v solution (500 mg in 10 ml). The intravenous injection preparation should be used after reconstitution of the sterile powder with Water for Injections, usually to produce a 2.5% w/v solution.</p><ul><li><strong>Premedication</strong>: Premedication usually consists of atropine or scopolamine to suppress vagal reflexes and inhibit secretions. In addition, a barbiturate or an opiate is often given. Ideally, the peak effect of these medications should be reached shortly before the time of induction.</li><li><strong>Test dose</strong>: It is advisable to inject a small \"test\" dose of 25 mg to 75 mg of TPS 1 gm/vial for Injection to assess tolerance or unusual sensitivity to TPS 1 gm/vial for Injection, and pausing to observe patient reaction for at least 60 seconds. If unexpectedly deep anesthesia develops or if respiratory depression occurs, consider these possibilities:</li></ul><ol><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The patient may be unusually sensitive to TPS 1 gm/vial for Injection</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The solution may be more concentrated than had been assumed</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The patient may have received too much</li></ol><ul><li><strong>Use in anesthesia</strong>: Normal dosage for the induction of anesthesia is 100 mg to 150 mg injected over 10 to 15 seconds. If necessary, a repeat dose of 100 mg to 150 mg may be given after one minute. No fixed dosage recommendations for the intravenous injection can be given, since the dosage will need to be carefully adjusted according to the patient\'s response. Factors such as age, sex, and weight of the patient should be taken into consideration. TPS 1 gm/vial reaches effective concentrations in the brain within 30 seconds and anesthesia is normally produced within one minute of an intravenous dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adult</strong>: 100 mg to 150 mg intravenously over 10 to 15 seconds, normally as a 2.5% w/v solution. A repeat dose of 100 mg to 150 mg may be given after one minute. The intravenous injection should be given slowly and the amount given titrated against the patient\'s response to minimize the risk of respiratory depression or the possibility of overdosage. The average dose for an adult of 70 kg is roughly 200 mg to 300 mg (8 mls to 12 mls of a 2.5% w/v solution) with a maximum of 500 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children</strong>: 2 mg/kg to 7 mg/kg bodyweight, intravenously over 10 to 15 seconds, normally as a 2.5% w/v solution. A repeat dose of 2 mg/kg to 7 mg/kg may be given after one minute. The dose is 2 mg/kg to 7 mg/kg based on the patient\'s response. The dose for children should not exceed 7 mg/kg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly</strong>: Smaller adult doses are advisable.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Use in convulsive states</strong>: 75 mg to 125 mg (3 mls to 5 mls of a 2.5% w/v solution) should be given as soon as possible after the convulsion begins. Further doses may be required to control convulsion following the use of a local anesthetic. Other regimens, such as the use of intravenous or rectal diazepam, may be used to control convulsive states.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of TPS 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> Possible increase in difficulty in producing anesthesia in patients taking alcohol or CNS depressants. Additive action with other CNS depressants including sedatives, hypnotics, nitrous oxide, or alcohol. Increased hypotension and excitatory effects with phenothiazine antipsychotics. Increased hypnotic effect with antipsychotic. Decreased requirement of TPS 1 gm/vial with metoclopramide, sulfisoxazole, aspirin, meprobamate, probenecid, and other highly protein-bound drugs.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Absolute Contraindication</strong>: Absence of suitable veins for intravenous administration, Hypersensitivity (allergy) to barbiturates, Variegate porphyria (South African) or acute intermittent porphyria, Status asthmaticus</li><li><strong>Relative Contraindication</strong>: Severe cardiovascular disease, Hypotension or shock, Conditions in which the hypnotic effect may be prolonged or potentiated, eg, excessive premedication, Addison\'s disease, hepatic or renal dysfunction, myxedema, increased blood urea, severe anemia, asthma, myasthenia gravis</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of TPS 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity reactions have been reported. Other adverse reactions to TPS 1 gm/vial include the following: respiratory depression, myocardial depression, cardiac arrhythmias, prolonged somnolence and recovery, hypotension, tachycardia, sneezing, coughing, bronchospasm, laryngospasm, and shivering. Anaphylactic reactions have been reported. Symptoms, e.g., urticaria, bronchospasm, vasodilation, and edema.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. Animal reproduction studies have not been conducted with Thiopental. It is also not known whether Thiopental can cause fetal harm when administered to a pregnant woman or can affect reproduction capacity. Thiopental should be given to a pregnant woman only if clearly needed. Thiopental sodium readily crosses the placental barrier and small amounts may appear in the milk of nursing mothers following administration of large doses.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> A person competent in anesthesia management should be in constant attendance and adequate facilities for support of respiration and circulation should be available when TPS 1 gm/vial for injection is being used. TPS 1 gm/vial for Injection should be administered with caution to patients with preexisting hypotension or in conditions where the hypnotic effect may be prolonged or intensified, such as in the presence of liver disease and renal disease. This product may be habit forming. Keep resuscitative and endotracheal intubation equipment and oxygen readily available. Maintain patency of the airway at all times. Only persons qualified in the use of anesthetics should administer this drug. Avoid extravasations or intra-arterial injection.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of TPS 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> Overdosage may occur from too rapid or repeated administration. Too rapid injection may be followed by an alarming fall in blood pressure even to shock levels. Apnoea, occasional laryngospasm, coughing, and other respiratory difficulties with excessive or too rapid injections may occur. In the event of suspected or apparent overdosage, the agent should be discontinued.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at controlled room temperature of 15°C to 30°C. Solutions should be freshly prepared and used immediately. Any portion of the contents remaining should be discarded.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> General (Intravenous) anesthetics</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Thiopental, a barbiturate, is used for the induction of anesthesia prior to the use of other general anesthetic agents and for induction of anesthesia for short surgical, diagnostic, or therapeutic procedures associated with minimal painful stimuli. Thiopental is an ultrashort-acting depressant of the central nervous system which induces hypnosis and anesthesia, but not analgesia. It produces hypnosis within 30 to 40 seconds of intravenous injection. Recovery after a small dose is rapid, with some somnolence and retrograde amnesia. Repeated intravenous doses lead to prolonged anesthesia because fatty tissues act as a reservoir; they accumulate Pentothal in concentrations 6 to 12 times greater than the plasma concentration, and then release the drug slowly to cause prolonged anesthesia. Thiopental binds at a distinct binding site associated with a Cl- ionopore at the GABAA receptor, increasing the duration of time for which the Cl- ionopore is open. The post-synaptic inhibitory effect of GABA in the thalamus is, therefore, prolonged.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. Animal reproduction studies have not been conducted with Thiopental. It is also not known whether Thiopental can cause fetal harm when administered to a pregnant woman or can affect reproduction capacity. Thiopental should be given to a pregnant woman only if clearly needed. Thiopental sodium readily crosses the placental barrier and small amounts may appear in the milk of nursing mothers following administration of large doses.</p>', 'active', '597_1742309974.jpg', 17, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 19:59:34', '2025-03-18 19:59:34'),
(3343, 'Hanrhoid-B', 'Guggul + Barberry + Chebulic Myrobalan', 4.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg plus 33.33 mg plus 16.67 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '742_1742310078.jpg', 66, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 20:01:18', '2025-03-18 20:01:18'),
(3344, 'Zadit', 'Ketotifen Fumarate', 100.00, 1, 'Drop', '5ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '0.025 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zadit 0.025%</strong></p><p> Zadit 0.025% is indicated in the prophylactic treatment of bronchial asthma, symptomatic treatment of allergic conditions including rhinitis and conjunctivitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Cromoglycate &amp; related drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Zadit 0.025% is a potent anti-allergic substance possessing a powerful and sustained non-competitive histamine (H1) receptor blocking property. It inhibits the release of mediators (e.g., histamine, leukotrienes, and prostaglandins) from cells responsible for type-I allergic reactions. Zadit 0.025% also stabilizes mast cells, decreases chemotaxis, activation of degranulation of eosinophils. Additionally, it stabilizes mast cells &amp; basophils and inhibits PAF (platelet-activating factor) and is effective in preventing asthmatic attacks.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zadit 0.025%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 1 mg twice daily with food. If necessary, the dose may be increased to 2 mg twice daily in severe cases.</li><li><strong>Children above 2 years</strong>: 1 mg twice daily with food. Patients known to be easily sedated should begin treatment with 0.5 to 1 mg at night for the first few days or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Use in elderly</strong>: Same as adult dose or as advised by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zadit 0.025%</strong></p><p> A reversible fall in the platelet count has been observed in a few patients receiving Ketotifen concomitantly with oral antidiabetics, and it has been suggested that this combination should therefore be avoided. Since Ketotifen has the properties of antihistamines, it may potentiate the effects of other CNS depressant drugs such as alcohol, antihistamines, hypnotics, and sedatives.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> A reversible fall in the platelet count has been observed in a few patients receiving Ketotifen concomitantly with oral antidiabetic agents, and it has been suggested that this combination should therefore be avoided.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zadit 0.025%</strong></p><p> Drowsiness and, in isolated cases, dry mouth and slight dizziness may occur at the beginning of treatment, but usually disappear spontaneously after a few days.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Although there is no evidence of any teratogenic effect, Ketotifen in pregnancy and lactation is not recommended.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Zadit 0.025% is important to continue the previous treatment for a minimum of two weeks after starting Ketotifen to avoid the possibility of exacerbation of asthma. This applies especially to systemic corticosteroids and ACTH because of the possible existence of adrenocortical insufficiency in steroid-dependent patients. If intercurrent infection occurs, Ketotifen treatment must be supplemented by specific antimicrobial therapy. During the first day of treatment with Ketotifen, reactions may be impaired, and patients should be warned not to take charge of vehicles or machinery until the effect of Ketotifen treatment on the individual is known. Patients should be advised to avoid alcoholic drinks. Ketotifen may potentiate the effects of sedatives, hypnotics, antihistamines, and alcohol.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zadit 0.025%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Drowsiness, confusion, dyspnoea, bradycardia or tachycardia, disorientation, convulsions, severe hypotension, reversible coma.</li><li><strong>Management</strong>: Supportive and symptomatic.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '859_1742310288.jpg', 17, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 20:04:48', '2025-03-18 20:04:48'),
(3345, 'MagVital', 'Magnesium Oxide', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '365 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of MagVital 365 mg</h3><p>MagVital 365 mg is indicated for the treatment of the following conditions:</p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Relieving the symptoms of magnesium deficiencyCardiovascular system</strong>: Rapid heartbeat, heart rate irregularity, heart attack, angina pectoris (chest pain caused by narrowing/obstruction of heart-feeding vessels), mild to severe hypertension.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Nervous system and muscles</strong>: Sudden and excessive muscle contractions (tetany), muscle cramps, gastrointestinal cramps, increased stimulability of muscles and nerves, calf cramps, cramp conditions in newborns and young children, and stress.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gynecological diseases, birth, and pregnancy</strong>: Pre-term contractions, cervical insufficiency, premature membrane rupture, contractions during pregnancy (eclampsia/preeclampsia), tocolysis requiring the use of beta mimetics, dysmenorrhea.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Orthopedics</strong>: Calcification and ossification, prevention of kidney stone formation (prevention of recurrence of calcium oxalate urolithiasis).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Diabetes treatment and migraine (headaches)</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Dosage of MagVital 365 mg</h3><p>Magnesium supplements should be taken with meals to reduce stomach upset and diarrhea.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (12-17 years)</strong>: 1-2 tablets daily.</li><li><strong>Kidney failure</strong>: Should not be used in patients with severe kidney insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Liver failure</strong>: No data available for patients with liver insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Elderly usage</strong>: No data available for use in elderly patients.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of MagVital 365 mg</h3><p>MagVital 365 mg may interact with other medications. If you are using any of the following, inform your doctor:</p><ul><li>Muscle relaxants (non-depolarizing neuromuscular blockers)</li><li>Aminoquinolones</li><li>Nitrofurantoin</li><li>Penicillamine</li><li>Tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones (antibiotics)</li><li>Digoxin (for heart failure)</li><li>Lithium (for emotional changes and depression)</li><li>Sodium polystyrene sulfonate (for potassium removal)</li><li>Cellulose sodium phosphate (for kidney stones)</li><li>Other medicines containing magnesium (including magnesium enemas)</li><li>Barbiturates (insomnia treatment)</li><li>Opioids (morphine-like substances)</li><li>Hypnotics (soporific)</li><li>Nifedipine (used for high blood pressure and heart problems)</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to magnesium or any component of the product.</li><li><strong>Severe renal impairment</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of MagVital 365 mg</h3><h4>Mild side effects may include:</h4><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Cramps</li><li>Tiredness</li><li>Weakness</li><li>Confusion</li></ul><p>These side effects generally disappear when the dose is reduced or treatment is discontinued.</p><h4>Serious side effects may include:</h4><ul><li>Low blood pressure</li><li>Changes in the heart\'s electrical activity (ECG)</li><li>Depression</li><li>Severe allergic reactions (swelling in mouth and throat, itching, rash, redness)</li><li>Respiratory depression</li><li>Coma</li></ul><p>These serious side effects are rare but may require emergency medical treatment.</p><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><p>This product may contain inactive ingredients, which can cause allergic reactions or other problems. Consult your doctor before using this product if you have:</p><ul><li>Kidney disease</li><li>Alcohol dependence</li><li>Liver disease</li><li>Phenylketonuria (PKU)</li><li>Any other condition requiring dietary restrictions or limitations.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of MagVital 365 mg</h3><p>Symptoms of overdose may include:</p><ul><li>Slow heartbeat</li><li>Severe dizziness</li><li>Confusion</li><li>Muscle weakness</li><li>Loss of consciousness</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Hypermagnesemia</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 25°C.</li><li>Protect from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep all medicines out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li>Antacids</li><li>Electrolyte preparations</li><li>Oral electrolyte preparations</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>: Magnesium is a cofactor for over 300 metabolic reactions in the body, including protein synthesis, cellular energy production, and nerve transmission. It also plays a significant role in glucose and insulin metabolism. Magnesium helps maintain bone health, muscle function, and blood pressure regulation.</p><p><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong>: Magnesium is primarily intracellular and is filtered and reabsorbed by the kidneys. Only a small fraction circulates in the blood, and a minor percentage is excreted in the urine.</p><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li>The recommended daily dose during pregnancy and lactation is 1-2 tablets.</li><li><strong>Use only when clearly needed</strong> during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '108_1742310310.webp', 8, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 20:05:10', '2025-03-18 20:05:10'),
(3346, 'Piramil', 'Ramipril', 7.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Piramil 2.5 mg</strong></p><p> Piramil 2.5 mg is indicated in the following cases:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: To lower blood pressure, as single-drug therapy or in combination with other antihypertensive agents.</li><li><strong>Congestive heart failure</strong>: Also in combination with diuretics.</li><li><strong>Treatment of patients</strong>: Within the first few days after an acute myocardial infarction who have demonstrated clinical signs of congestive heart failure.</li><li><strong>Treatment of non-diabetic or diabetic overt glomerular or incipient nephropathy</strong>.</li><li><strong>Reduction in the risk of myocardial infarction, stroke, or cardiovascular death</strong>: In patients with an increased cardiovascular risk, such as manifest coronary heart disease (with or without a history of myocardial infarction), a history of stroke, a history of peripheral vascular disease, or diabetes mellitus that is accompanied by at least one other cardiovascular risk factor (microalbuminuria, hypertension, elevated total cholesterol levels, low high-density lipoprotein cholesterol levels, smoking).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Piramil 2.5 mg is an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor, which after hydrolysis, blocks the conversion of angiotensin I to the vasoconstrictor substance, angiotensin II. Inhibition of ACE by Piramil 2.5 mg results in decreased plasma angiotensin II, leading to decreased vasopressor activity and decreased aldosterone secretion. Thus, Piramil 2.5 mg exerts its antihypertensive activity. It is also effective in the management of heart failure and the reduction of the risk of stroke, myocardial infarction, and death from cardiovascular events. It is long-acting and well tolerated, making it suitable for long-term therapy.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Piramil 2.5 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Hypertension</strong>: For the management of hypertension in adults not receiving a diuretic, the usual initial dose of Piramil 2.5 mg is 1.25 - 2.5 mg once daily. Dosage generally is adjusted no more rapidly than at 2-week intervals. The usual maintenance dosage in adults is 2.5 - 20 mg daily, given as a single dose or in 2 divided doses daily. If BP is not controlled with Piramil 2.5 mg alone, a diuretic may be added.</p><p> <strong>Congestive heart failure after myocardial infarction</strong>: Piramil 2.5 mg therapy may be initiated as early as 2 days after myocardial infarction. An initial dose of 2.5 mg twice daily is recommended, but if hypotension occurs, the dose should be reduced to 1.25 mg twice daily. Therapy is then titrated to a target daily dose of 5 mg twice daily.</p><p> <strong>Prevention of major cardiovascular events</strong>: The recommended dose is 2.5 mg once daily for the first week of therapy and 5 mg once daily for the following 3 weeks; dosage may then be increased, as tolerated, to a maintenance dosage of 10 mg once daily.</p><p><strong>Dosage in renal impairment</strong>:</p><ul><li>For patients with hypertension and renal impairment, the recommended initial dose is 1.25 mg Piramil 2.5 mg once daily. Subsequent dosage should be titrated according to individual tolerance and BP response, up to a maximum of 5 mg daily.</li><li>For patients with heart failure and renal impairment, the recommended dose is 1.25 mg once daily. The dose may be increased to 1.25 mg twice daily and up to a maximum dose of 2.5 mg twice daily depending upon clinical response and tolerability.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Piramil 2.5 mg</strong></p><p> Piramil 2.5 mg tablets must be swallowed with sufficient amounts of liquid. The tablets must not be chewed or crushed. Absorption of Piramil 2.5 mg is not significantly affected by food, so it may be taken before, during, or after a meal.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Piramil 2.5 mg</strong></p><p> Concomitant administration with diuretics may lead to serious hypotension and dangerous hyperkalemia with potassium-sparing diuretics. Concomitant therapy with lithium may increase the serum lithium concentration. Reduction in BP may affect the ability to drive and operate machinery, which may be exacerbated by alcohol. NSAIDs may reduce the antihypertensive effect of Piramil 2.5 mg and cause deterioration of renal function.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Piramil 2.5 mg must not be used in:</p><ul><li>Patients with hypersensitivity to Piramil 2.5 mg, any other ACE inhibitor, or any of the excipients.</li><li>Patients with a history of angioedema.</li><li>Concomitant use with sacubitril/valsartan therapy. Do not initiate Piramil 2.5 mg until sacubitril/valsartan is eliminated from the body.</li><li>In patients with hemodynamically relevant renal artery stenosis, bilateral or unilateral in the single kidney.</li><li>In patients with hypotensive or hemodynamically unstable states.</li><li>With aliskiren-containing medicines in patients with diabetes or with moderate to severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;60 ml/min).</li><li>With angiotensin II receptor antagonists (AIIRAs) in patients with diabetic nephropathy.</li><li>During pregnancy.</li><li>Concomitant use of ACE inhibitors and extracorporeal treatments leading to contact of blood with negatively charged surfaces must be avoided, as this may lead to severe anaphylactoid reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Piramil 2.5 mg</strong></p><p> Piramil 2.5 mg is generally well tolerated. Common side effects include dizziness, headache, fatigue, and asthenia. Other side effects that occur less frequently include symptomatic hypotension, cough, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, rash, urticaria, oliguria, anxiety, and amnesia. Rarely, angioneurotic edema, anaphylactic reactions, and hyperkalemia have been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> If pregnancy is detected, Piramil 2.5 mg should be discontinued as early as possible unless continued use is considered life-saving. Piramil 2.5 mg should not be used during lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Piramil 2.5 mg should be used with caution in patients with impaired renal function, hyperkalemia, hypotension, and impaired hepatic function.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Piramil 2.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Signs and symptoms</strong>: Overdosage may cause excessive peripheral vasodilatation (with marked hypotension, shock), bradycardia, electrolyte disturbances, and renal failure.</li><li><strong>Management</strong>: Primary detoxification through gastric lavage, administration of adsorbents, sodium sulfate (if possible during the first 30 minutes). In case of hypotension, administration of α1-adrenergic agonists (e.g., norepinephrine, dopamine) or angiotensin II (angiotensinamide) may be considered, in addition to volume and salt substitution.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at 30°C or below, protect from light. Keep out of the reach of children. Do not use later than the date of expiry. To be dispensed only on the prescription of a registered physician.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> <strong>Dosage in renal impairment</strong>:</p><p> For patients with hypertension and renal impairment, the recommended initial dose is 1.25 mg Piramil 2.5 mg once daily. The maximum permitted daily dose is 5 mg Piramil 2.5 mg.</p><p> For patients with heart failure and renal impairment, the recommended dose is 1.25 mg once daily, which may be increased to 1.25 mg twice daily and up to 2.5 mg twice daily, depending upon clinical response and tolerability.</p><p> <strong>Use in children</strong>: No information is yet available on the use of Piramil 2.5 mg in children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Piramil 2.5 mg blocks the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, leading to decreased vasopressor activity and decreased aldosterone secretion, thereby exerting its antihypertensive activity. It also helps manage heart failure and reduces the risk of stroke, myocardial infarction, and cardiovascular death.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Piramil 2.5 mg must not be taken during pregnancy. Therefore, pregnancy must be excluded before starting treatment. If the patient intends to become pregnant, treatment with ACE inhibitors must be discontinued and replaced by another form of treatment.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> For the elderly, a reduced initial dose of 1.25 mg Piramil 2.5 mg daily must be considered.</p><p> <strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: Treatment in these patients must be initiated under close medical supervision. The maximum permitted daily dose is 2.5 mg Piramil 2.5 mg.</p><p> <strong>Renal impairment</strong>: For creatinine clearance between 50 and 20 ml/min per 1.73 m² body surface area, the initial daily dose is generally 1.25 mg Piramil 2.5 mg, with a maximum dose of 5 mg.</p>', 'active', '72_1742310520.jpg', 57, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 20:08:40', '2025-03-18 20:08:40'),
(3347, 'Octreotide', 'Octreotide Acetate', 560.00, 5, 'x 1 ml ampoule', '1 ml ampoule', 'Injection', '50 mcg per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Octreotide 50 mcg/ml</h3><ul><li><strong>Acromegaly</strong>: Indicated to reduce blood levels of growth hormone (GH) and IGF-I (somatomedin C) in patients with acromegaly who have had inadequate response to or cannot be treated with surgical resection, pituitary irradiation, and bromocriptine mesylate at maximally tolerated doses. The goal is to achieve normalization of GH and IGF-I levels. Improvement in clinical signs and symptoms or reduction in tumor size was not shown in clinical trials.</li><li><strong>Carcinoid Tumors</strong>: Indicated for the symptomatic treatment of patients with metastatic carcinoid tumors, suppressing severe diarrhea and flushing episodes associated with the disease.</li><li><strong>Vasoactive Intestinal Peptide Tumors (VIPomas)</strong>: Indicated for the treatment of profuse watery diarrhea associated with VIP-secreting tumors.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Growth hormone antagonist.</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li>Octreotide is a synthetic analogue of somatostatin. It suppresses basal and stimulated secretion of growth hormone, and also reduces the secretion of several hormones such as insulin, glucagon, secretin, motilin, and pancreatic polypeptide.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Intramuscular (Acromegaly)</strong>: Initially, 20 mg every 4 weeks as a depot preparation. After 3 months, adjust to 10-30 mg every 4 weeks (max: 40 mg every 4 weeks).</li><li><strong>Intravenous (Variceal Hemorrhage)</strong>: Continuous IV infusion at 25 mcg/hr for up to 48 hours (up to 5 days for high-risk patients).</li><li><strong>Subcutaneous (Acromegaly)</strong>: Initially, 50 mcg three times daily, increased to 100-200 mcg three times daily as needed. Max: 500 mcg three times daily.</li><li><strong>Subcutaneous (Secretory Neoplasms)</strong>: Initially, 50 mcg 1-2 times daily, increased gradually to up to 600 mcg daily in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Subcutaneous (HIV-associated Diarrhea)</strong>: Initially, 100 mcg three times daily, may increase to 250 mcg three times daily if symptoms are not controlled.</li></ul><h3>Interaction</h3><ul><li>Dosage adjustment may be required with calcium channel blockers, oral hypoglycemics, β-blockers, diuretics.</li><li>May increase the concentration of bromocriptine.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Octreotide or any of its components.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Local pain, stinging, tingling at the site of injection, anorexia, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, bloating, flatulence, loose stools, steatorrhoea, biliary tract abnormalities.</li><li><strong>Less common</strong>: Hypoglycemia, hyperglycemia, hypothyroidism, cardiac conduction abnormalities, pancreatitis.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Category B</strong>: Animal studies have not shown fetal risk, but there are no controlled studies in pregnant women.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with <strong>renal disease</strong>, <strong>gall bladder disease</strong>, <strong>diabetes</strong>, and <strong>hypothyroidism</strong>.</li><li>Monitor <strong>vitamin B12</strong> levels during long-term therapy.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store at <strong>2-8°C</strong>. It is stable at room temperature for up to 14 days.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Dosage may need to be reduced in severe renal impairment requiring dialysis.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>Octreotide works by suppressing the secretion of growth hormone (GH) and other hormones such as insulin, glucagon, and others, which helps manage conditions like acromegaly and certain tumors.</li></ul><p>This summary provides a comprehensive overview of the use, dosing, interactions, and precautions for Octreotide. Let me know if you need further details on any specific section!</p>', 'active', '599_1742310581.webp', 16, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 20:09:41', '2025-05-04 01:14:06'),
(3348, 'Susten', 'Dapoxetine Hydrochloride', 50.00, 5, 'Tablet', '5 Tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '60 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Susten 60 mg</strong></p><p> Indicated for the treatment of premature ejaculation (PE) in men 18 to 64 years of age, who have all of the following: Persistent or recurrent ejaculation with minimal sexual stimulation before, on, or shortly after penetration and before the patient wishes. Marked personal distress or interpersonal difficulty as a consequence of PE and poor control over ejaculation.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for Erectile Dysfunction</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> The mechanism of action is thought to be related to inhibition of neuronal reuptake of serotonin and subsequent potentiation of serotonin activity. The central ejaculatory neural circuit comprises spinal and cerebral areas that form a highly interconnected network. The sympathetic, parasympathetic, and somatic spinal centers, under the influence of sensory genital and cerebral stimuli integrated and processed at the spinal cord level, act in synergy to command physiological events occurring during ejaculation. Experimental evidence indicates that serotonin (5-HT), throughout brain descending pathways, exerts an inhibitory role on ejaculation. To date, three 5-HT receptor subtypes 5-HT(1A), 5-HT(1B), and 5-HT(2C) have been postulated to mediate 5-HT\'s modulating activity on ejaculation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Susten 60 mg</strong></p><p> Adult (18 to 64 years of age): The recommended starting dose for all patients is 30 mg, taken as needed approximately 1 to 3 hours prior to sexual activity. If the effect of 30 mg is insufficient and the side effects are acceptable, the dose may be increased to the maximum recommended dose of 60 mg. The maximum recommended dosing frequency is one dose every 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Susten 60 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CNS active medicinal products</strong>: The use of Dapoxetine in combination with CNS active medicinal products has not been systematically evaluated in patients with premature ejaculation. Caution is advised if the concomitant administration of Dapoxetine and such medicinal products is required.</li><li><strong>PDE5 inhibitors</strong>: Tadalafil did not affect the pharmacokinetics of Dapoxetine. Sildenafil caused slight changes in Dapoxetine pharmacokinetics, which are not expected to be clinically significant. However, Dapoxetine should be prescribed with caution in patients who use PDE5 inhibitors due to possible reduced orthostatic tolerance.</li><li><strong>Tamsulosin</strong>: Concomitant administration of single or multiple doses of 30 mg or 60 mg Dapoxetine to patients receiving daily doses of Tamsulosin did not result in changes in the pharmacokinetics of Tamsulosin. However, Dapoxetine should be prescribed with caution in patients who use alpha adrenergic receptor antagonists due to possible reduced orthostatic tolerance.</li><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>: There are no data evaluating the effect of chronic use of Warfarin with Dapoxetine; therefore, caution is advised when Dapoxetine is used in patients taking Warfarin chronically.</li><li><strong>Ethanol</strong>: Concomitant use of alcohol and Dapoxetine could increase the chance or severity of adverse reactions such as dizziness, drowsiness, slow reflexes, or altered judgment. Combining alcohol with Dapoxetine may increase these alcohol-related effects and may also enhance neurocardiogenic adverse events such as syncope, thereby increasing the risk of accidental injury; therefore, patients should be advised to avoid alcohol while taking Dapoxetine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with known hypersensitivity to Susten 60 mg.</li><li>Patients with significant pathological cardiac conditions such as heart failure (NYHA class II-IV), conduction abnormalities (second or third degree AV block or sick sinus syndrome) not treated with a permanent pacemaker, significant ischemic heart disease, or significant valvular disease.</li><li>Concomitant treatment with monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs), thioridazine. Similarly, MAOIs or thioridazine should not be administered within 7 days after Dapoxetine has been discontinued.</li><li>Concomitant treatment with serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs), serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors (SNRIs), tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs) or other medicinal/herbal products with serotonergic effects or within 14 days of discontinuing treatment with these medicinal/herbal products.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Susten 60 mg</strong></p><p> Dizziness, Headache, Somnolence, Tremor, Blurred vision, Tinnitus, Sinus congestion, Nausea, Diarrhea, Abdominal pain, Dry mouth, Fatigue, Insomnia, Hypertension.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Dapoxetine is not indicated for use by women. It is not known whether dapoxetine or its metabolites are excreted through human breast milk.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with bleeding disorders, epilepsy, susceptibility to angle-closure glaucoma or raised intraocular pressure.</li><li>Not intended for use in women.</li><li>Known CYP2D6 poor metabolisers.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Susten 60 mg</strong></p><p> There were no unexpected adverse events in a clinical pharmacology study of Dapoxetine with daily doses up to 240 mg. In general, symptoms of overdose with SSRIs include serotonin-mediated adverse reactions such as somnolence, gastrointestinal disturbances such as nausea and vomiting, tachycardia, tremor, agitation, and dizziness. In cases of overdose, standard supportive measures should be adopted as required.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C. Protect from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Drugs for Erectile Dysfunction</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> The mechanism of action is thought to be related to inhibition of neuronal reuptake of serotonin and subsequent potentiation of serotonin activity. The central ejaculatory neural circuit comprises spinal and cerebral areas that form a highly interconnected network. The sympathetic, parasympathetic, and somatic spinal centers, under the influence of sensory genital and cerebral stimuli integrated and processed at the spinal cord level, act in synergy to command physiological events occurring during ejaculation. Experimental evidence indicates that serotonin (5-HT), throughout brain descending pathways, exerts an inhibitory role on ejaculation. To date, three 5-HT receptor subtypes 5-HT(1A), 5-HT(1B), and 5-HT(2C) have been postulated to mediate 5-HT\'s modulating activity on ejaculation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Dapoxetine is not indicated for use by women. It is not known whether dapoxetine or its metabolites are excreted through human breast milk.</p>', 'active', '1785_1742310864.jpg', 4, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 20:14:24', '2025-03-18 20:14:24'),
(3349, 'XeroSoft', 'White Soft Paraffin + Liquid Paraffin', 180.00, 1, 'x 50 gm tube', '50 gm tube', 'Ointment', '50%+50%', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Sure, here\'s your text with the headings in bold as requested:</p><p><strong>Indications of XeroSoft 50 gm</strong></p><p>This lotion or cream is used for the treatment of dry skin conditions.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Emollients &amp; combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Light liquid paraffin and White soft paraffin exert an emollient effect by forming an occlusive film which reduces trans-epidermal water loss, thus helping to maintain normal skin humidity levels.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of XeroSoft 50 gm</strong></p><p>Light Liquid Paraffin and White Soft Paraffin lotion is used when required. Apply to the affected area and rub in well. It is especially effective after washing when the sebum content of the stratum corneum may be depleted, resulting in excessive moisture loss.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of this cream.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of XeroSoft 50 gm</strong></p><p>No remarkable adverse effects or side effects have been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>There are no restrictions on the use during pregnancy or lactation.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 25°C and protect from light. Do not refrigerate. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1189_1744700452.jpg', 8, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 20:14:55', '2025-04-15 11:00:52');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3350, 'Piravix', 'Favipiravir', 200.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Piravix 200 mg</strong></p><p> Treatment of novel or re-emerging pandemic influenza virus infections (limited to cases in which other influenza antiviral drugs are ineffective or not sufficiently effective).</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-viral drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Piravix 200 mg is a new antiviral drug against influenza. It is metabolized into Piravix 200 mg ribosyl triphosphate (Piravix 200 mg RTP) by an intracellular enzyme, and Piravix 200 mg RTP selectively inhibits RNA polymerase (RNA-dependent RNA polymerase) of the influenza virus, preventing replication of the influenza virus. It is a drug with a mechanism of action different from that of the existing influenza antiviral drugs and effective against all types and sub-types of human influenza A, B, and C viruses in vitro, showing a wide range of anti-viral activity against various influenza virus strains including avian and swine viruses.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Piravix 200 mg</strong></p><p> The usual adult dosage is 1600 mg of Piravix 200 mg administered orally twice daily on Day 1, followed by 600 mg orally twice daily from Day 2 to Day 5 or as directed by physicians. The total treatment duration should be 5 days.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Piravix 200 mg</strong></p><p> In animal studies, decreased RBC production, and increases in liver function parameters such as AST, ALP, ALT, and total bilirubin, and increased vacuolization in hepatocytes. Toxicity information regarding Piravix 200 mg in humans is not readily available.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Piravix 200 mg is contraindicated for pregnant women and women who may possibly be pregnant.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Piravix 200 mg</strong></p><p> Most common side effects are diarrhea and an increase in blood uric acid levels.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Piravix 200 mg may cause delayed development or death of embryos during the early stage of pregnancy. Should not be given during pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Piravix 200 mg should not be given to pregnant women. Confirmation of non-pregnancy in women of childbearing potential before use is required, and thorough contraception measures should be taken from the start of the treatment to 7 days after the end of the treatment. Caution should be exercised for patients with hepatic and renal impairment, or Piravix 200 mg should be used according to the direction of a registered physician.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Piravix 200 mg</strong></p><p> In animal studies, decreased RBC production, and increases in liver function parameters such as AST, ALP, ALT, and total bilirubin, and increased vacuolization in hepatocytes. Toxicity information regarding Piravix 200 mg in humans is not readily available.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> This drug is only approved as an experimental drug, and further studies are needed about its efficacy, toxic reactions, and use in children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Anti-viral drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Piravix 200 mg is a new antiviral drug against influenza. It is metabolized into Piravix 200 mg ribosyl triphosphate (Piravix 200 mg RTP) by an intracellular enzyme, and Piravix 200 mg RTP selectively inhibits RNA polymerase (RNA-dependent RNA polymerase) of the influenza virus, preventing replication of the influenza virus. It is a drug with a mechanism of action different from that of the existing influenza antiviral drugs and effective against all types and sub-types of human influenza A, B, and C viruses in vitro, showing a wide range of anti-viral activity against various influenza virus strains including avian and swine viruses.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Piravix 200 mg may cause delayed development or death of embryos during the early stage of pregnancy. Should not be given during pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> This drug is only approved as an experimental drug, and further studies are needed about its efficacy, toxic reactions, and use in children.</p>', 'active', '588_1742311349.jpg', 10, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 20:22:29', '2025-03-18 20:22:29'),
(3351, 'Spadyl', 'Tiemonium Methylsulfate', 5.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Spadyl 50 mg</strong></p><p> Spadyl 50 mg is an antispasmodic drug that reduces muscle spasm of the intestine, biliary system, bladder, and uterus. It is used in the symptomatic treatment of pain related to functional disorders of the digestive tract and biliary system. It is also indicated for the treatment of spasm and pain in urological and gynecological diseases.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anticholinergics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Spadyl 50 mg is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine and histamine and strengthens the calcium bond with membrane phospholipids and proteins. Thus, it inhibits intracellular contractile proteins of visceral cells, which causes inhibition of visceral spasm and pain.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Spadyl 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet/Syrup</strong></li><li> Adult: Usual dose is 2-6 tablets or 3-9 teaspoonfuls syrup daily in divided doses.</li><li> Children: 3 ml/kg or 6 mg/kg body weight daily in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong></li><li> 1 Spadyl 50 mg injection 3 times daily, through intravenous route slowly or intramuscular route.</li><li><strong>Suppository</strong></li><li> 20 mg Spadyl 50 mg suppository two or three times daily, through the rectal route.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Spadyl 50 mg</strong></p><p> Tiemonium methylsulphate tablets should not be used with other drugs without prior consultation with a registered physician to avoid possible drug interaction.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> It should not be used in urethroprostatic disorders involving a risk of urine retention. It is contraindicated in patients with a risk of angle-closure glaucoma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Spadyl 50 mg</strong></p><p> Spadyl 50 mg may cause hypotension and tachycardia in certain individuals.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The results of animal studies of Spadyl 50 mg did not reveal any teratogenic effects; no deformities have been reported up until now with normal use. In the absence of sufficient data, caution should be exercised for nursing mothers, although no problems have been reported with normal use.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Caution should be taken during treatment of patients with prostate disorders. Caution should also be taken in cases of chronic bronchitis, coronary insufficiency, ambient hyperthermia, renal, and hepatic insufficiency. The risks of visual disturbances can make it dangerous to drive or use machines.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Spadyl 50 mg</strong></p><p> There is no available data regarding the overdose of Tiemonium methylsulphate tablets.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness of Tiemonium methylsulphate in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Efficacy and safety were maintained with increasing age.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Anticholinergics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Spadyl 50 mg is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine and histamine and strengthens the calcium bond with membrane phospholipids and proteins. It inhibits intracellular contractile proteins of visceral cells, which causes inhibition of visceral spasm and pain.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The results of animal studies of Spadyl 50 mg did not reveal any teratogenic effects; no deformities have been reported up until now with normal use. In the absence of sufficient data, caution should be exercised for nursing mothers, although no problems have been reported with normal use.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Pediatric use: Safety and effectiveness of Tiemonium methylsulphate in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p> Geriatric use: Efficacy and safety were maintained with increasing age.</p>', 'active', '289_1742311557.jpg', 12, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 20:25:57', '2025-03-18 20:25:57'),
(3352, 'Vitalens', 'Adenosine plus Cytochrome-C plus Sodium Succinate plus Nicotinamide', 125.00, 1, 'Drop', '5ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '2 mg plus 0.5 mg plus 0.6 mg plus 10 mg per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vitalens</strong></p><p> This eye drop is used for the treatment of lens opacification.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p> Each ml contains:</p><ul><li>Anhydrous sodium succinate 0.6 mg</li><li>Cytochrome C 0.5 mg</li><li>Adenosine 2 mg</li><li>Nicotinamide 10 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for lens opacification</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Anhydrous Sodium Succinate</strong>: Promotes the production of ATP.</li><li><strong>Cytochrome C</strong>: Involved in oxidative phosphorylation for synthesizing ATP from ADP.</li><li><strong>Adenosine</strong>: Plays an essential role in producing energy required for the vital function of the lens, such as the biosynthesis of glutathione, intermembrane active transport of ions and amino acids, and the synthesis of DNA, RNA, and nucleic acids.</li><li><strong>Nicotinamide</strong>: Involved in the process of creating ATP. NADPH plays a major role in protecting cells against oxidizing agents and free radicals by maintaining glutathione in its reduced form.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Vitalens</strong></p><p> One drop into the affected eye twice daily.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Vitalens</strong></p><p> One drop into the affected eye twice daily.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Vitalens</strong></p><p> To avoid interaction with other drugs, it is recommended to inform your doctor of any concomitant treatment, particularly with other eye drops.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> This eye drop is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any ingredient of the product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Vitalens</strong></p><p> Like any active ingredient, this drug may cause more or less discomfort in some patients.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> It is recommended to take advice from a doctor before using this medicine in pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Do not inject or swallow. In case of concomitant treatment with another ophthalmic solution, wait 15 minutes between each instillation.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Vitalens</strong></p><p> Accidental ingestion of the medicine is unlikely to cause any toxicity due to the low content of ingredients. The medication should be kept out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at room temperature and protect from light. It is desirable that the content should not be used more than 4 weeks after first opening the bottle.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Drugs for lens opacification</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Anhydrous Sodium Succinate</strong>: Promotes the production of ATP.</li><li><strong>Cytochrome C</strong>: Involved in oxidative phosphorylation for synthesizing ATP from ADP.</li><li><strong>Adenosine</strong>: Plays an essential role in producing energy required for the vital function of the lens, such as the biosynthesis of glutathione, intermembrane active transport of ions and amino acids, and the synthesis of DNA, RNA, and nucleic acids.</li><li><strong>Nicotinamide</strong>: Involved in the process of creating ATP. NADPH plays a major role in protecting cells against oxidizing agents and free radicals by maintaining glutathione in its reduced form.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> It is recommended to take advice from a doctor before using this medicine in pregnancy and lactation.</p>', 'active', '1426_1742311806.jpg', 37, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 20:30:06', '2025-03-18 20:30:06'),
(3353, 'Arotide', 'Salmeterol plus Fluticasone Propionate', 12.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '50 mcg plus 250 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Arotide 50 mcg+250 mcg</strong></p><p> This is indicated in the regular treatment of asthma where the use of a combination product (long-acting β2-agonist and inhaled corticosteroid) is appropriate: patients not adequately controlled with inhaled corticosteroids and \'as needed\' inhaled short-acting β2-agonist or patients already adequately controlled on both inhaled corticosteroid and long-acting β2-agonist.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants, Respiratory corticosteroids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate</strong>: A selective, long-acting beta-2 agonist used in the treatment of asthma and other forms of diffuse airways obstruction.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: A corticosteroid with mainly glucocorticoid activity. It is stated to exert a topical effect on the lungs without systemic effects at usual doses. Salmeterol protects against symptoms, and Fluticasone Propionate improves lung function and prevents exacerbations.</li><li><strong>Salmeterol</strong>: A selective long-acting (12-hour) beta-2-adrenoceptor agonist that binds to the exo-site of the receptor.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: Given by inhalation at recommended doses, it has a potent glucocorticoid anti-inflammatory action within the lungs, resulting in reduced symptoms and exacerbations of asthma, without the adverse effects observed when corticosteroids are administered systemically.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Arotide 50 mcg+250 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Inhalation Aerosol</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily, or 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 125 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily, or 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 250 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4-12 years)</strong>: 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Powder in Capsule (For Asthma)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg or Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 250 µg twice daily (morning and evening, approximately 12 hours apart).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric Patients (4 to 11 years)</strong>: Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg twice daily (morning and evening, approximately 12 hours apart).</li><li><strong>Inhalation Powder in Maxhaler (For Asthma)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: 50/100 Maxhaler: One Inhalation twice daily, 50/250 Maxhaler: One Inhalation twice daily, 50/500 Maxhaler: One Inhalation twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4 years and older)</strong>: 50/100 Maxhaler: One Inhalation twice daily.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Powder in Maxhaler (For COPD)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maxhaler: One Inhalation twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Arotide 50 mcg+250 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Inhaler</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily, or 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 125 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily, or 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 250 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4-12 years)</strong>: 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Capsule (For Asthma)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg or Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 250 µg twice daily (morning and evening, approximately 12 hours apart).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric Patients (4 to 11 years)</strong>: Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Capsule (For COPD)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 250 µg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Maxhaler (For Asthma)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: 50/100 inhaler: One Inhalation twice daily, 50/250 inhaler: One Inhalation twice daily, 50/500 inhaler: One Inhalation twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4 years and older)</strong>: 50/100 inhaler: One Inhalation twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Arotide 50 mcg+250 mcg</strong></p><p> Using an Inhaler may seem simple, but proper technique is crucial to ensure the medication reaches the lungs. Follow these steps for effective use:</p><ol><li>Take off the cap.</li><li>Shake the inhaler vigorously (at least six times) before each use.</li><li>If the inhaler has not been used for a week or more, shake it well and release one puff into the air.</li><li>Breathe out fully, then place the actuator in your mouth, close lips around the mouthpiece.</li><li>While breathing deeply and slowly, press down on the canister to release the medicine.</li><li>Remove the inhaler from your mouth and hold your breath for at least 10 seconds.</li><li>If prescribed more than one inhalation, wait 1 minute between puffs and repeat steps.</li><li>After use, replace the cap and rinse your mouth with water.</li></ol><p><strong>Interaction of Arotide 50 mcg+250 mcg</strong></p><p> Both non-selective and selective β-blockers should be avoided in patients with asthma, unless absolutely necessary. There is a potential for increased systemic exposure to Fluticasone Propionate when co-administered with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole, ritonavir).</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> This is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Arotide 50 mcg+250 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol</strong>: Side effects may include tremor, palpitations, headache, arrhythmia, rash, oedema, angioedema, oropharyngeal irritation, and muscle cramps.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: Side effects may include hoarseness, candidiasis (thrush) in the mouth and throat, cutaneous hypersensitivity reactions, and rare cases of facial and oropharyngeal oedema.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Use during pregnancy and lactation should only be considered if the expected benefit to the mother outweighs any potential risk to the fetus or child. There is insufficient experience with Salmeterol Xinafoate and Fluticasone Propionate during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> This preparation should be used with caution in patients with active or quiescent pulmonary tuberculosis and in patients with thyrotoxicosis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C. Do not puncture, break, or incinerate even when empty. Keep away from sunlight, heat, and children. Avoid contact with eyes.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> In pediatric patients, orally inhaled corticosteroids may reduce growth velocity, and the long-term impact on adult height is unknown.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants, Respiratory corticosteroids</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate</strong>: A long-acting beta-2 agonist used to treat asthma and airway obstruction.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: A glucocorticoid with potent anti-inflammatory effects when inhaled, reducing asthma symptoms and exacerbations.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> As mentioned earlier, the use of this medication during pregnancy should be considered only if the benefit to the mother outweighs any risks to the fetus or child.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Long-term use of corticosteroids in pediatric patients may reduce growth velocity, though the long-term effects on adult height are not known.</p>', 'active', '1190_1742312176.jpg', 53, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 20:36:16', '2025-03-18 20:36:16'),
(3354, 'Spakem', 'Baclofen', 4.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Spakem 5 mg</strong></p><p> Spakem 5 mg is indicated for the treatment of spasticity, including flexor spasms, associated pain, clonus, muscular rigidity, as well as skeletal muscle spasms resulting from conditions such as multiple sclerosis, rheumatic disorders, spinal cord injuries, other spinal cord diseases, cerebrovascular accidents, neoplastic, or degenerative brain diseases.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Centrally acting Skeletal Muscle Relaxants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Spakem 5 mg is an effective muscle relaxant and antispastic agent that primarily acts at the spinal level. While its exact mode of action is not fully understood, Spakem 5 mg works by stimulating GABAB receptors, which inhibits the release of glutamate and aspartate, thereby affecting both monosynaptic and polysynaptic reflexes.</p><p>It may also have central nervous system (CNS) depressant effects at intraspinal sites. Notably, Spakem 5 mg does not impact neuromuscular transmission. Spakem 5 mg exhibits an antinociceptive (pain-relieving) effect, although its clinical significance remains uncertain.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Spakem 5 mg</strong></p><p> Spakem 5 mg should be taken with a small amount of liquid during meals. The dosing regimen for Spakem 5 mg varies for adults and children.</p><p>In adults, it\'s typically administered in divided doses, preferably three times a day. The aim is to use the lowest effective dose.</p><p>For adults, the therapy usually begins with a low dose and is then increased gradually to achieve the best response, typically falling within the range of 30-80 mg daily. Additional increases may be necessary but should generally not exceed a maximum daily dose of 80 mg.</p><p>For children, treatment should begin with an extremely low dose, such as 0.3 mg/kg per day, divided into multiple doses. The dose can then be cautiously increased at 1-2 week intervals until it meets the child\'s specific needs.</p><p>The usual maintenance dose for children typically falls within the range of 0.75 to 2 mg/kg of body weight per day.</p><p>For children over 10 years of age, a maximum daily dose of 2.5 mg/kg of body weight may be considered.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Spakem 5 mg</strong></p><p> Increased sedation may occur when Spakem 5 mg is taken alongside agents that affect the central nervous system, alcohol, or synthetic opiates. This also elevates the risk of respiratory depression. When Spakem 5 mg is combined with antihypertensives, it\'s likely to amplify the drop in blood pressure. Adjustments to the antihypertensive medication dosage may be necessary.</p><p>Concurrently administering Spakem 5 mg with tricyclic antidepressants may intensify Spakem 5 mg\'s pharmacological effects, leading to pronounced muscular hypotonia. In individuals with Parkinson\'s disease receiving Spakem 5 mg along with levodopa and carbidopa, there have been reports of mental confusion, hallucinations, headaches, nausea, and agitation.</p><p>When MAO inhibitors and Spakem 5 mg are used together, it may result in increased CNS depressant effects. Caution is advised, and the dosage of one or both agents should be adjusted accordingly. Care should also be taken when administering Spakem 5 mg in conjunction with magnesium sulfate or other neuromuscular blocking agents, as there is a theoretical potential for a synergistic effect.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Spakem 5 mg is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to any component of this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Spakem 5 mg</strong></p><p> The common adverse reactions associated with Spakem 5 mg include drowsiness, dizziness, weakness, and fatigue.</p><p>More serious adverse reactions are less common, but can occur. These include:</p><ul><li><strong>Central nervous system effects</strong>: headaches, insomnia, euphoria, excitement, depression, confusion, hallucinations, paraesthesia, nightmares, muscle pain, tinnitus, slurred speech, coordination disorders, tremors, rigidity, dystonia, ataxia, blurred vision, nystagmus, strabismus, miosis, mydriasis, diplopia, dysarthria, epileptic seizures, and respiratory depression.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular reactions</strong>: hypotension, dyspnoea, palpitations, chest pain, and syncope.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal reactions</strong>: nausea, constipation, dry mouth, anorexia, taste disorders, abdominal pain, vomiting, diarrhea, and positive tests for occult blood in stool.</li><li><strong>Genitourinary reactions</strong>: urinary frequency, enuresis, urinary retention, dysuria, impotence, inability to ejaculate, nocturia, and hematuria.</li><li><strong>Other potential reactions</strong>: rash, pruritus, ankle edema, excessive perspiration, weight gain, nasal congestion, visual disturbances, hepatic function disorders, and a paradoxical increase in spasticity.</li><li>Muscular hypotonia, or low muscle tone, can also occur with Spakem 5 mg. This is usually relieved by adjusting the dosage.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy category B3. The safety of using Spakem 5 mg during pregnancy has not been established, as it can cross the placental barrier. Administration to pregnant women should only occur when the physician concludes that the potential benefits outweigh the possible risks. Spakem 5 mg is excreted in breast milk, but available evidence suggests that the quantities are so minimal that no adverse effects on the infant are expected.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Lower doses (approximately 5 mg per day) should be considered for patients with impaired renal function or those undergoing chronic hemodialysis. Patients who not only have spasticity but also suffer from psychotic disorders, schizophrenia, depressive or manic disorders, or confusional states should be treated cautiously and closely monitored, as exacerbations of these conditions may occur. In patients with epilepsy and muscle spasticity, Spakem 5 mg may be used under appropriate supervision, provided that adequate anticonvulsive therapy is continued. It\'s important to note that lowering the convulsion threshold may occur, and seizures have been reported after the cessation of Spakem 5 mg therapy or in cases of overdose.</p><p>Caution should be exercised in patients with a history of peptic ulcers, cerebrovascular diseases, or hepatic, renal, or respiratory failure. Vigilant monitoring of respiratory and cardiovascular function is essential, especially in those with cardiopulmonary disease and respiratory muscle weakness. While Spakem 5 mg treatment may improve neurogenic disturbances affecting bladder emptying, individuals with preexisting sphincter hypertonia may experience acute retention of urine in these circumstances. It\'s important to note that Spakem 5 mg has not significantly benefited patients with stroke, and they have shown poor tolerance to the medication. For individuals with hepatic diseases or diabetes mellitus, appropriate laboratory tests should be conducted periodically to ensure that there are no medication-induced changes in these underlying conditions.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Spakem 5 mg</strong></p><p> Gastric lavage is important in case of severe overdose.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Centrally acting Skeletal Muscle Relaxants</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Spakem 5 mg inhibits both monosynaptic and polysynaptic reflexes at the spinal level by stimulating the GABAB receptors, which in turn inhibits the release of glutamate and aspartate. Additionally, it may act at intraspinal sites, leading to CNS depression. Spakem 5 mg also demonstrates an antinociceptive effect.</p>', 'active', '1372_1742312411.jpg', 8, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 20:40:11', '2025-03-18 20:40:11'),
(3355, 'Urobak', 'Nitrofurantoin', 60.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '25 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Urobak 100 ml</strong></p><p>Urobak 100 ml is specifically indicated for the treatment &amp; prophylaxis of urinary tract infections caused by susceptible strains of <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Enterococci</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em>, and certain susceptible strains of <em>Klebsiella</em> and <em>Enterobacter</em> species.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Systemic Urinary Anti-infective</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Urobak 100 ml interferes with cell metabolism and cell wall synthesis by inhibiting several enzyme systems including acetyl coenzyme A. It is bactericidal to most gram-positive and gram-negative urinary tract pathogens.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Urobak 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Urobak 100 ml tablet (In adults)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated urinary tract infections: 50-100 mg four times a day— the lower dosage level is recommended. Therapy should be continued for one week or for at least 3 days after sterility of the urine is obtained.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For long-term suppressive therapy: In adults, a reduction of dosage to 50-100 mg at bedtime may be adequate.</li><li><strong>Urobak 100 ml capsule (In adults)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute Uncomplicated Urinary Tract Infections (UTIs): 50 mg four times daily for 7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Long-term suppression: 50-100 mg once a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prophylaxis: 50 mg four times daily for the duration of the procedure and for three days thereafter.</li><li><strong>Urobak 100 ml SR capsule</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults and children over 12 years: One 100 mg capsule every 12 hours for seven days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Genito-urinary surgical prophylaxis: One capsule twice daily on the day of procedure and for the next 3 days.</li><li><strong>Urobak 100 ml suspension (Children)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-7 mg/kg/day in four divided doses (contraindicated under one month of age).</li><li>The average dose of Urobak suspension for pediatric patients is as follows:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">7 to 11 kg: ½ (2.5 ml) teaspoonfuls 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">12 to 21 kg: 1 (5 ml) teaspoonfuls 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">22 to 30 kg: 1½ (7.5 ml) teaspoonfuls 4 times daily.</li><li>31 to 41 kg: 2 (10 ml) teaspoonfuls 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Therapy should be continued for one week or for at least 3 days after sterility of the urine is obtained. For long-term suppressive therapy in children, doses as low as 1 mg/kg per 24 hours, given in a single dose or in two divided doses, may be adequate.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Urobak 100 ml</strong></p><p>Urobak 100 ml should be taken with food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Urobak 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Antacids containing Magnesium Trisilicate, when administered concomitantly with Urobak 100 ml, reduce both the rate and extent of absorption.</li><li>Uricosuric drugs, such as Probenecid and Sulfinpyrazone, can inhibit renal tubular secretion of Urobak 100 ml.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Anuria, oliguria, or significant impairment of renal function are contraindications.</li><li>This drug is contraindicated in pregnant patients at 38-42 weeks, during labor and delivery.</li><li>Urobak 100 ml is also contraindicated in those patients with known hypersensitivity to Urobak 100 ml.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Urobak 100 ml</strong></p><p>The most frequent clinical adverse events are nausea, headache, and flatulence. Other less occurred adverse events are diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain, constipation, emesis, dizziness, and drowsiness.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy Category B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Urobak 100 ml has been detected in human breast milk in trace amounts. Because of the potential for serious adverse reactions from Urobak 100 ml in nursing infants under one month of age, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or to discontinue the drug, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>If acute, sub-acute, or chronic pulmonary reactions occur, Urobak 100 ml should be discontinued. Antacid preparations containing magnesium trisilicate should not be taken while taking Urobak 100 ml.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Urobak 100 ml</strong></p><p>Occasional incidents of acute overdosage of Urobak 100 ml have not resulted in any specific symptoms other than vomiting. Induction of emesis is recommended.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Do not store above 30°C. Keep away from light and out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Systemic Urinary Anti-infective</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Urobak 100 ml is an antibacterial agent specific for urinary tract infections. Urobak 100 ml is highly soluble in urine, to which it may impart a brown color. Urobak 100 ml inactivates or alters bacterial ribosomal proteins and other macromolecules. Urobak 100 ml has been shown to be active against the following bacteria:</p><ul><li><strong>Gram-Positive Aerobes</strong>: <em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em>, Coagulase-negative staphylococci (including <em>Staphylococcus epidermidis</em>), <em>Enterococcus faecalis</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus agalactiae</em>, Group D streptococci, Viridans group streptococci.</li><li><strong>Gram-Negative Aerobes</strong>: <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Citrobacter amalonaticus</em>, <em>Citrobacter diversus</em>, <em>Citrobacter freundii</em>, <em>Klebsiella oxytoca</em>, <em>Klebsiella ozaenae</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Category B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Urobak 100 ml has been detected in human breast milk in trace amounts. Because of the potential for serious adverse reactions from Urobak 100 ml in nursing infants under one month of age, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or to discontinue the drug, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</p>', 'active', '247_1742312629.jpg', 7, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 20:43:49', '2025-05-07 10:27:18'),
(3356, 'Sweet Drop', 'Sucralose', 130.00, 1, 'Drop', '10ml drop', 'Drop', '25 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sweet Drop 25%</strong></p><p> Sweet Drop 25% is a sugar substitute that can be used as a sweetener in various foods such as tea, coffee, pudding, milk products, jelly, fruit juices, desserts, and hot or cold beverages. Due to its zero-calorie nature, it is an ideal choice for health-conscious individuals. Sweet Drop 25% is also a beneficial option for diabetic patients who need to manage their blood glucose levels. The FDA has confirmed that Sweet Drop 25% is suitable for everyone, including children, pregnant women, and individuals looking to reduce calorie intake.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Oral nutritional preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sweet Drop 25% is an artificial sweetener and sugar substitute. The majority of ingested Sweet Drop 25% is not broken down by the body, making it noncaloric. In the European Union, it is recognized under the E number E955. It is approximately 320 to 1,000 times sweeter than sucrose, three times as sweet as aspartame, twice as sweet as saccharin, and three times as sweet as acesulfame potassium. It remains stable under heat and across a broad pH range, making it suitable for baking and long-shelf-life products. The commercial success of Sweet Drop 25% is attributed to its favorable taste, stability, and safety compared to other low-calorie sweeteners.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sweet Drop 25%</strong></p><ul><li>The acceptable daily intake (ADI) of Sweet Drop 25% is 5-15 mg/kg of body weight.</li><li>Normally, <strong>1 tablet (8 mg)</strong> is sufficient to sweeten a cup of tea or coffee.</li><li>Each drop of Sweet Drop 25% provides sweetness equivalent to <strong>one teaspoon of sugar</strong>.</li><li>Typically, <strong>one drop</strong> in a cup of tea or coffee is enough, or as directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sweet Drop 25%</strong></p><p> There are no known drug interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> No known contraindications.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sweet Drop 25%</strong></p><p> No known side effects.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category:</strong> Not Classified.</li><li>The FDA has not yet assigned Sweet Drop 25% to a specific pregnancy category.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at a temperature not exceeding <strong>30°C</strong>.</li><li>Keep in a <strong>cool and dry place</strong>, protected from light.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Oral nutritional preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Sweet Drop 25% is a <strong>zero-calorie artificial sweetener</strong> that is about <strong>600 times sweeter than sugar</strong>. It is derived from sucrose by replacing three hydroxyl groups with three chlorine atoms. Because it is made from sugar, Sweet Drop 25% closely mimics the taste of sugar.</p><ul><li>The <strong>carbon-chlorine bonds</strong> in Sweet Drop 25% are highly stable, preventing metabolism for energy, making it <strong>calorie-free</strong>.</li><li>The chlorine atoms contribute to its <strong>heat stability</strong>, allowing it to retain sweetness during <strong>cooking and baking</strong>.</li><li>Sweet Drop 25% <strong>does not cause tooth decay</strong>, <strong>does not affect carbohydrate metabolism</strong>, and <strong>does not interfere with insulin secretion</strong>.</li><li>It does not cross the <strong>blood-brain barrier, placental barrier, or mammary gland</strong>, ensuring <strong>no impact on fetal or neonatal development</strong>.</li></ul><p>Because of these properties, Sweet Drop 25% is considered <strong>safe for diabetics</strong> and people looking to manage their calorie intake.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The FDA has not yet classified Sweet Drop 25% under a specific pregnancy category.</p>', 'active', '1768_1742312929.jpg', 61, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 20:48:49', '2025-03-18 20:48:49'),
(3357, 'Pancreon 10000', 'Pancreatin [High Lipase]', 60.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '150 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><span style=\"background-color: rgb(245, 245, 245); color: rgb(63, 82, 100);\">Pancreon 10000 is indicated for the treatment of-</span></p><ul><li>Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency due to cystic fibrosis</li><li>Chronic pancreatitis</li><li>Pancreatectomy or other conditions</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '436_1742313098.png', 55, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 20:51:38', '2025-03-18 20:51:38'),
(3358, 'Rabivax-IG', 'Rabies Immunoglobulin', 1000.00, 1, 'Injection', '1000 IU vial injection', 'Injection', '1000 IU per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rabivax-IG 1000 IU/5 ml</strong></p><p> Rabivax-IG 1000 IU/5 ml is used for the <strong>seroprophylaxis (prevention by injecting antibodies) of rabies</strong> in individuals suspected to have been exposed to the rabies virus, particularly in cases of severe exposure such as:</p><ul><li>Multiple transdermal bites or scratches</li><li>Contamination of mucous membranes with saliva</li><li>Severe bites on the face, head, neck, and hands</li><li>When the responsible animal cannot be examined or is suspected to be rabid</li><li>Bites to young children</li></ul><p>Rabivax-IG 1000 IU/5 ml provides <strong>passive immunization</strong> against rabies for prevention after contact with a rabid or potentially rabid animal. However, it <strong>must always be used in conjunction with a rabies vaccine</strong> and does not constitute a standalone anti-rabies treatment.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Rabivax-IG 1000 IU/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>First-aid treatment</strong>: Immediate flushing and washing of bite wounds with <strong>soap and water, detergent, or another rabies-virus-killing agent</strong> is essential, regardless of the time elapsed since exposure.</li><li><strong>Injection timing</strong>: Rabies immune globulin should be injected <strong>as soon as possible after exposure</strong>.</li><li><strong>Recommended dose</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children</strong>: 40 IU/kg of body weight</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If possible, the dose should be <strong>infiltrated around and into the wound(s)</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The remainder should be given <strong>intramuscularly (gluteal region) in a single injection</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In <strong>children with multiple wounds</strong>, the dose may be diluted <strong>2-3 times</strong> with a 0.9% sodium chloride solution to ensure proper wound infiltration.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Caution</strong> should be exercised while infiltrating wounds in sensitive areas like fingertips to prevent increased local tissue pressure.</li></ul><p><strong>Combination with rabies vaccine</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Rabies vaccine and immune globulin should be administered together</strong> but at different injection sites.</li><li>If <strong>rabies immune globulin is not available</strong> at the time of the first rabies vaccine dose, it <strong>can still be administered up to the 7th or 8th day</strong> after vaccination.</li><li><strong>Additional precautions</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Anti-tetanus treatment</strong> should be considered when indicated.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Antimicrobial drugs</strong> may be prescribed to prevent secondary infections.</li></ul><p><strong>WHO Recommendations for Rabies Exposure Categories</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category I</strong> (Touching, feeding, licks on intact skin): <strong>No treatment required</strong> if a reliable history is available.</li><li><strong>Category II</strong> (Minor scratches, superficial bites not on high-risk areas, licks on broken skin):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Administer rabies vaccine</strong> on Day 0, D3, D7, D14, and D28.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Stop treatment</strong> if the animal remains healthy for 10 days or is tested negative in a lab.</li><li><strong>Category III</strong> (Severe bites, transdermal wounds, contamination of mucous membranes with saliva):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Administer rabies vaccine immediately</strong> on <strong>Day 0, D3, D7, D14, and D28 (or D90, optional)</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Give rabies immune globulin immediately on Day 0</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Stop treatment</strong> if the animal is confirmed healthy or tests negative.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known allergy to horse proteins.</li><li>However, <strong>the life-threatening risk of rabies outweighs any contraindication</strong>, meaning it should still be administered if necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Rabivax-IG 1000 IU/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Immediate reactions</strong>: Hypotension, difficulty breathing (dyspnea), and urticaria (hives).</li><li><strong>Delayed reactions</strong>: Inflammatory response, fever, itching, rash, swollen lymph nodes (adenopathy), and joint pain (arthralgia).</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Do not administer intravenously</strong> due to the risk of <strong>shock (sudden collapse with low blood pressure)</strong>.</li><li>Ensure <strong>the needle has not penetrated a blood vessel</strong> before injection.</li><li>Rabies immunoglobulin <strong>should not be given in repeated doses</strong>.</li><li>Repeated injections <strong>may reduce vaccine efficacy</strong>.</li><li><strong>Shake well before use</strong>, but <strong>do not shake vigorously</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rabivax-IG 1000 IU/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Excessive dosage can lead to <strong>immunosuppressive interference</strong> with the rabies vaccine, reducing its protective effect.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep <strong>out of reach of children</strong>.</li><li>Store at <strong>2°C to 8°C</strong> (refrigerated).</li><li>Transportation should also be maintained at <strong>2°C to 8°C</strong>.</li><li><strong>Protect from light</strong> and <strong>do not freeze</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Vaccines, Anti-sera &amp; Immunoglobulin</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>The safety of rabies immune globulin <strong>has not been established</strong> in human pregnancy trials.</li><li>However, given the <strong>lethal risk of rabies</strong>, pregnancy <strong>is not considered a contraindication</strong> to its administration after exposure.</li></ul>', 'active', '63_1742313432.jpg', 17, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 20:57:12', '2025-03-18 20:57:12'),
(3359, 'Omari', 'Omarigliptin', 140.00, 4, 'Tablet', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><span style=\"background-color: rgb(245, 245, 245); color: rgb(63, 82, 100);\">Omarigliptin is a long-acting inhibitor of the enzyme dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP- 4). DPP-4 rapidly degrades incretin hormones. Incretins lower blood glucose concentrations in response to a meal by increasing insulin and decreasing glucagon levels in a glucose-dependent manner. Thus, Omarigliptin\'s mechanism of action augments the action of incretin hormones.</span></p>', 'active', '909_1742313626.jpg', 5, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 21:00:26', '2025-03-18 21:00:26');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3360, 'Premelos', 'Ginkgo Biloba', 7.00, 30, 'Capsule', '30 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '60 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Premelos 60 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Primary uses:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cerebral insufficiency:</strong> Memory deficit, depression, attention and memory loss associated with <strong>Alzheimer\'s disease and multi-infarct dementia</strong>.</li><li><strong>Vertigo and tinnitus</strong> (ringing in the ears) of vascular and involutional origin.</li><li><strong>Peripheral vascular disease:</strong> Improves pain-free walking distance in <strong>Peripheral Arterial Occlusive Disease (Stage II, Fontaine classification)</strong> as part of a regimen including physical therapy, particularly walking exercises.</li></ul><p><strong>Other potential uses:</strong></p><ul><li>Protective action in <strong>hypoxia</strong>.</li><li><strong>Acute cochlear deafness</strong>.</li><li><strong>Sexual dysfunction associated with SSRI use</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Herbal and Nutraceuticals</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Ginkgo biloba works by:</p><ul><li><strong>Inhibiting platelet aggregation</strong>, reducing thrombosis, and improving <strong>blood circulation</strong>.</li><li><strong>Enhancing cognitive functions</strong>, including working memory, short-term visual memory, and attention in individuals with dementia or cerebral insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Improving social functioning and activities of daily living (ADL) scores</strong> in dementia patients.</li><li><strong>Reducing tinnitus symptoms</strong> in people with dementia.</li><li><strong>Enhancing mood and sleep quality</strong> in older individuals.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Premelos 60 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>1 or 2 capsules</strong>, <strong>2 to 3 times daily</strong>, or as advised by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Use <strong>with caution in patients taking anticoagulants or antiplatelet agents</strong> (e.g., warfarin, heparin, aspirin) due to <strong>increased bleeding risk</strong>.</li><li><strong>Contraindicated in bleeding disorders</strong>, especially with long-term use (6-12 months) or before <strong>elective surgery</strong>.</li><li><strong>Contraindicated in patients at risk for intracranial hemorrhage</strong>.</li><li>There is <strong>no known restriction</strong> for use in pregnancy and lactation, but caution is advised.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Premelos 60 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No significant side effects <strong>when taken at recommended doses</strong>.</li><li>In clinical trials with 10,000 patients, side effects were <strong>rare</strong>, including:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Headaches</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dizziness</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Palpitations</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gastrointestinal disturbances</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Bleeding disorders</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin hypersensitivity reactions</li><li><strong>High doses</strong> may cause:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diarrhea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vomiting</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Restlessness</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Weakness</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Not recommended during pregnancy</strong> due to potential risks.</li><li><strong>Unknown if it passes into breast milk</strong>; breastfeeding is not recommended while using this product.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Inform your doctor if you have:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Allergies to Ginkgo biloba</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Bleeding disorders</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Seizures, convulsions, or epilepsy</strong></li><li>Ginkgo biloba may <strong>reduce blood clotting ability</strong>.</li><li><strong>Stop taking at least 2 weeks before surgery</strong> to minimize bleeding risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep <strong>out of reach of children</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Not recommended for children</strong>.</li></ul>', 'active', '1891_1742313930.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 21:05:30', '2025-03-18 21:05:30'),
(3361, 'Timocare', 'Timolol Maleate', 80.00, 1, 'Drop', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '0.5 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Timocare 0.5%</strong></p><p> Timocare 0.5% Ophthalmic Solution is indicated for the treatment of <strong>elevated intraocular pressure</strong> in patients with <strong>ocular hypertension</strong> or <strong>open-angle glaucoma</strong>.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for miotics and glaucoma</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Timolol is a <strong>non-selective β-adrenergic receptor blocker</strong>. It does not have significant <strong>intrinsic sympathomimetic activity</strong>, <strong>direct myocardial depressant activity</strong>, or <strong>local anesthetic activity</strong>. The exact mechanism of its <strong>ocular hypotensive effect</strong> is unclear but is believed to be due to <strong>reduction of aqueous humor formation</strong>. <strong>β-blockade also causes a lowering of blood pressure (BP).</strong></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Timocare 0.5%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Eye drops solution:</strong> Initially, instill <strong>1 drop of 0.25% solution twice daily (bid)</strong> into the affected eye(s). If the response is inadequate, increase to <strong>1 drop of 0.5% solution bid</strong>. If controlled, reduce to <strong>1 drop once daily</strong>. Do not exceed <strong>1 drop bid of 0.5% solution</strong>.</li><li><strong>Gel-forming eye drops:</strong> Instill <strong>1 drop of 0.25% or 0.5% solution into the affected eye(s) once daily</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Timocare 0.5%</strong></p><ul><li>Although <strong>Timolol alone</strong> has little or no effect on pupil size, <strong>mydriasis (pupil dilation)</strong> has been reported when used <strong>with epinephrine</strong>.</li><li>Drug interactions have been noticed with:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Beta-adrenergic blocking agents</strong> (both oral and topical)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Calcium antagonists</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Catecholamine-depleting drugs</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Digitalis</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Quinidine</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Clonidine</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injectable epinephrine</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Timolol is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in patients with:</p><ul><li><strong>Bronchial asthma</strong> or a history of bronchial asthma</li><li><strong>Severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</strong></li><li><strong>Sinus bradycardia</strong></li><li><strong>Second- or third-degree atrioventricular (AV) block</strong></li><li><strong>Overt cardiac failure</strong></li><li><strong>Cardiogenic shock</strong></li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity to any component of the product</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Timocare 0.5%</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Ophthalmic side effects:</strong>Burning and stinging sensation in the eyes</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Systemic side effects:</strong>Bradycardia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypotension</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Arrhythmia and AV or SA nodal block</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Congestive heart failure (CHF)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pulmonary edema</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Raynaud\'s phenomenon</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Headache, dizziness, fatigue, asthenia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Abdominal discomfort, nausea, constipation</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypoglycemia</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. <strong>Use only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus</strong>.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Timolol has been detected in <strong>breast milk</strong> following <strong>oral and ophthalmic administration</strong>. <strong>A decision should be made to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug</strong>, considering its importance to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Use with caution in patients with:</strong>Inadequate cardiac function</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diabetes mellitus</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Myasthenia gravis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Cerebrovascular insufficiency</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">History of atopy</li><li><strong>Avoid abrupt withdrawal</strong>, as it may:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Exacerbate <strong>angina symptoms</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Precipitate <strong>myocardial infarction (MI)</strong> in coronary artery disease patients</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Trigger <strong>thyroid crisis</strong> in thyrotoxicosis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Other precautions:</strong>Patients undergoing <strong>major surgery</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">May <strong>mask signs of hyperthyroidism and hypoglycemia</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Not to be used as monotherapy for angle-closure glaucoma</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Use with caution in <strong>renal and hepatic impairment</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Use cautiously in pregnancy and lactation</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Timocare 0.5%</strong></p><ul><li>Reports of inadvertent <strong>overdosage</strong> have led to <strong>systemic effects</strong> similar to <strong>systemic beta-blockers</strong>, including:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dizziness</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Headache</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Shortness of breath</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Bradycardia</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Bronchospasm</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cardiac arrest</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store between <strong>15-30°C</strong>.</li><li><strong>Avoid freezing</strong> and <strong>protect from light</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Drugs for miotics and glaucoma</p>', 'active', '357_1742314140.jpg', 1, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 21:09:00', '2025-03-18 21:09:00'),
(3362, 'Segorin', 'Cefuroxime Axetil', 45.00, 6, 'Tablet', '6 Tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Segorin 500 mg</strong></p><p> Segorin 500 mg is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive bacteria, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (β-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Maxillary Sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non-β-lactamase producing strains)</li><li><strong>Lower Respiratory Tract Infections</strong>, including <strong>pneumonia</strong>, caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including β-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp., Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes, E. coli</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong> and <strong>secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (β-lactamase-negative strains) or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (β-lactamase-negative strains)</li><li><strong>Skin and skin-structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including β-lactamase-producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></li><li><strong>Bone and Joint Infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Gonorrhea</strong> caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase-producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease (erythema migrans)</strong> caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime is a broad-spectrum <strong>bactericidal agent</strong> with activity against a wide range of common pathogens, including β-lactamase-producing strains. It has <strong>good stability to bacterial β-lactamase</strong>, making it effective against <strong>ampicillin-resistant and amoxicillin-resistant strains</strong>.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Segorin 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet or Suspension (Adolescents &amp; Adults - 13 years and older)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis:</strong> 250 mg <strong>b.i.d.</strong> for <strong>5-10 days</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 250 mg <strong>b.i.d.</strong> for <strong>10 days</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis:</strong> 250-500 mg <strong>b.i.d.</strong> for <strong>10 days</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis:</strong> 250-500 mg <strong>b.i.d.</strong> for <strong>5-10 days</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infections:</strong> 250-500 mg <strong>b.i.d.</strong> for <strong>10 days</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections:</strong> 250 mg <strong>b.i.d.</strong> for <strong>7-10 days</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea:</strong> 1000 mg <strong>single dose</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia:</strong> 250-500 mg <strong>b.i.d.</strong> for <strong>5-10 days</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>MDR Typhoid Fever:</strong> 500 mg <strong>b.i.d.</strong> for <strong>10-14 days</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Early Lyme Disease:</strong> 500 mg <strong>b.i.d.</strong> for <strong>20 days</strong></li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis:</strong> 20 mg/kg/day <strong>b.i.d.</strong> for <strong>5-10 days</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute otitis media:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day <strong>b.i.d.</strong> for <strong>10 days</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day <strong>b.i.d.</strong> for <strong>10 days</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Impetigo:</strong> 30 mg/kg/day <strong>b.i.d.</strong> for <strong>10 days</strong></li><li><strong>Parenteral (Injectable) Dosage</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong> 750 mg <strong>three times daily</strong> via IM or IV injection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Severe infections:</strong> Up to 1.5 g <strong>three times daily</strong> via IV injection. Can be increased to <strong>four times daily</strong>, total daily dose <strong>3-6 g</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (above 3 months):</strong> 30-100 mg/kg/day <strong>in 3 or 4 divided doses</strong> (60 mg/kg/day is typical for most infections).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Neonates:</strong> 30-100 mg/kg/day <strong>in 2 or 3 divided doses</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Surgical prophylaxis:</strong> 1.5 g IV at <strong>induction of anesthesia</strong>, followed by <strong>750 mg IV/IM every 8 hours</strong> for high-risk procedures.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pneumonia:</strong> 1.5 g IV <strong>twice daily for 2-3 days</strong>, followed by <strong>500 mg twice daily (oral) for 7-10 days</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute exacerbations of chronic bronchitis:</strong> 750 mg <strong>twice daily (IM/IV) for 2-3 days</strong>, then <strong>500 mg twice daily (oral) for 5-10 days</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gonorrhea:</strong> 1.5 g <strong>single dose</strong> (as <strong>two 750 mg injections</strong> at different sites).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Meningitis:Adults:</strong> 3 g IV <strong>three times daily</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Children (above 3 months):</strong> 200-240 mg/kg/day <strong>IV in 3-4 divided doses</strong>, reduced to <strong>100 mg/kg/day after 3 days</strong> or upon clinical improvement.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Neonates:</strong> 100 mg/kg/day IV initially, then <strong>reduced to 50 mg/kg/day</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Bone and Joint Infections:Adults:</strong> 1.5 g IV <strong>four times daily</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong> 150 mg/kg/day <strong>divided every 8 hours</strong> (not exceeding the adult dose).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Segorin 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Freshly reconstituted solutions are recommended.</strong></li><li>Remains <strong>potent for 24 hours at room temperature</strong> or <strong>48 hours at 5°C</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Segorin 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No <strong>potentially hazardous</strong> interactions reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cefuroxime is contraindicated</strong> in patients with <strong>known allergy to cephalosporins</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Segorin 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Mild and transient effects</strong>, including:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Rashes</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastrointestinal disturbances</strong></li><li><strong>Prolonged use may result in overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms (e.g., Candida).</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>FDA Pregnancy Category B:</strong> No adequate, well-controlled studies in pregnant women. <strong>Use only if clearly needed.</strong></li><li><strong>Cefuroxime is excreted in breast milk.</strong> Use with caution in <strong>nursing mothers</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use with caution in patients receiving potent diuretics</strong> or with a <strong>history of colitis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Cephalosporins may be safely given to penicillin-sensitive patients,</strong> though <strong>cross-reactions have been reported</strong>.</li><li><strong>No significant toxicity observed at recommended dose levels.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Store in a cool, dry place</strong> (below 30°C), away from <strong>light and moisture</strong>.</li><li><strong>Keep out of reach of children.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>750 mg IM injection:</strong> Add <strong>3 ml water for injection</strong> and shake gently.</li><li><strong>750 mg IV injection:</strong> Add <strong>8 ml water for injection</strong> and shake gently.</li><li><strong>1.5 g IV injection:</strong> Add <strong>16 ml water for injection</strong>, shake gently, and inject slowly over <strong>3-5 minutes</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Second-generation Cephalosporins</p>', 'active', '1969_1742314455.jpg', 56, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 21:14:15', '2025-03-18 21:14:15'),
(3363, 'Xelobine', 'Capecitabine', 138.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xelobine 500 mg</strong></p><p> Xelobine 500 mg is a nucleoside metabolic inhibitor with antineoplastic activity indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Adjuvant Colon Cancer:</strong> Patients with Dukes\' C colon cancer.</li><li><strong>Metastatic Colorectal Cancer:</strong> First-line as monotherapy when treatment with fluoropyrimidine therapy alone is preferred.</li><li><strong>Metastatic Breast Cancer:</strong> In combination with docetaxel after failure of prior anthracycline-containing therapy. As monotherapy in patients resistant to both paclitaxel and an anthracycline-containing regimen.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Xelobine 500 mg is a prodrug of fluorouracil, a pyrimidine antimetabolite, which is metabolized into 5-fluoro-2′-deoxyuridine-5′monophosphate (FdUMP) and 5-fluorouridine triphosphate (FUTP). FdUMP covalently binds to thymidylate synthase, inhibiting the formation of thymidilate, thus interfering with DNA synthesis. Additionally, FUTP interferes with RNA synthesis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xelobine 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Stage-III colon cancer and locally advanced or metastatic breast cancer:</strong> 1.25 g/m² twice daily for 14 days, followed by a 7-day interval, given as 3-week cycles for a total of 8 cycles.</li><li><strong>Metastatic colorectal cancer (in combination therapy):</strong> 0.8-1 g/m² twice daily for 14 days, repeated after a 7-day interval.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Xelobine 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Monotherapy:</strong> 1250 mg/m² twice daily orally for 2 weeks followed by a one-week rest period in 3-week cycles.</li><li><strong>Adjuvant treatment:</strong> Recommended for a total of 6 months (8 cycles).</li><li><strong>In combination with docetaxel:</strong> 1250 mg/m² twice daily for 2 weeks followed by a 7-day rest period, combined with docetaxel at 75 mg/m² as a 1-hour IV infusion every 3 weeks.</li><li><strong>Patients with moderate renal impairment:</strong> Dosage should be reduced by 25%.</li><li><strong>Administration:</strong> Tablets should be taken in the morning and evening within 30 minutes after a meal (breakfast and dinner) and swallowed whole with water. Tablets should not be cut or crushed.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xelobine 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Anticoagulants:</strong> Anticoagulant response (INR or prothrombin time) should be monitored frequently to adjust the anticoagulant dose as needed.</li><li><strong>Phenytoin:</strong> Levels should be monitored, and dose adjustments may be required.</li><li><strong>Leucovorin:</strong> Increases the concentration of 5-fluorouracil and may enhance its toxicity.</li><li><strong>CYP2C9 substrates:</strong> Caution is required when co-administered.</li><li><strong>Food:</strong> Reduces both the rate and extent of absorption of Xelobine 500 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Severe renal impairment</li><li>Hypersensitivity</li><li>Leukopenia, neutropenia, or thrombocytopenia</li><li>Severe reactions to fluoropyrimidine therapy</li><li>Complete DPD deficiency</li><li>Pregnancy or breastfeeding</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xelobine 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Rash, dry or itchy skin</li><li>Tiredness, loss of appetite (anorexia)</li><li>Diarrhea, vomiting, nausea, stomatitis</li><li>Hand-and-foot skin reaction</li><li>Fever, infection, chest pain</li><li>Steven-Johnson syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category D:</strong> There is positive evidence of human fetal risk. However, benefits may outweigh risks in life-threatening conditions.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong> It is unknown whether the drug is excreted in human milk. Breastfeeding should be discontinued during treatment and for 2 weeks after the final dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Coagulopathy:</strong> Anticoagulant response should be monitored to prevent bleeding or death.</li><li><strong>Diarrhea:</strong> Treatment should be stopped until diarrhea resolves or decreases to grade 1.</li><li><strong>Cardiotoxicity:</strong> Common in patients with a prior history of coronary artery disease.</li><li><strong>DPD Deficiency:</strong> Increased risk of severe or fatal adverse reactions. Treatment should be withheld or discontinued if severe toxicity occurs.</li><li><strong>Dehydration &amp; Renal Failure:</strong> Treatment should be stopped until dehydration is corrected.</li><li><strong>Mucocutaneous &amp; Dermatologic Toxicity:</strong> Severe mucocutaneous reactions (e.g., Steven-Johnson Syndrome, Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis). Treatment should be discontinued if such reactions occur.</li><li><strong>Hyperbilirubinemia:</strong> Treatment should be interrupted if hyperbilirubinemia develops.</li><li><strong>Hematologic Effects:</strong> Should not be used in patients with neutrophil counts &lt;1.5×10⁹/L or thrombocyte counts &lt;100×10⁹/L.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xelobine 500 mg</strong></p><p> Symptoms of overdose include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, mucositis, gastrointestinal irritation, bleeding, and bone marrow depression. Treatment should focus on managing symptoms and preventing complications.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a dry place below 30°C. Protect from light and keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Xelobine 500 mg is an orally-administered chemotherapeutic agent used in cancer treatment. It is a prodrug converted to fluorouracil, which inhibits DNA synthesis and slows tumor growth. Fluorouracil is further metabolized into active forms (FdUMP and FUTP), which interfere with DNA and RNA synthesis, leading to cell death.</p>', 'active', '1688_1742314673.jpg', 1, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 21:17:53', '2025-03-18 21:17:53'),
(3364, 'Wikijet', 'Tirzepatide', 2100.00, 1, 'x 5 mg pre-filled syringe', '5 mg pre-filled syringe', 'Injection', '5 mg per 0.5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '692_1742317999.jpg', 49, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 22:13:19', '2025-05-05 18:00:56'),
(3365, 'Respimax Pro', 'Vilanterol Trifenatate + Fluticasone Furoate + Umeclidinium Bromide', 60.00, 6, 'capsule', '6 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '25 mcg plus 200 mcg plus 62.5 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Respimax Pro 25 mcg+200 mcg+62.5 mcg</h3><p>Respimax Pro is indicated for:</p><ul><li>The <strong>maintenance treatment</strong> of <strong>chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</strong>.</li><li>The <strong>maintenance treatment</strong> of <strong>asthma</strong> in patients aged <strong>18 years and older</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Limitation of use</strong>: Not indicated for relief of acute bronchospasm.</p><h3>Composition</h3><p>Each dry powder inhaler capsule contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Vilanterol</strong> 25 mcg (as Vilanterol Trifenatate INN)</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Furoate</strong> 100 mcg (INN)</li><li><strong>Umeclidinium</strong> 62.5 mcg (as Umeclidinium Bromide INN)</li></ul><p>Another formulation contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Vilanterol</strong> 25 mcg (as Vilanterol Trifenatate INN)</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Furoate</strong> 200 mcg (INN)</li><li><strong>Umeclidinium</strong> 62.5 mcg (as Umeclidinium Bromide INN)</li></ul><h3>Dosage of Respimax Pro 25 mcg+200 mcg+62.5 mcg</h3><ul><li>This inhalation capsule <strong>must not be swallowed</strong>. It is intended for use only with the inhalation device.</li><li><strong>Adults (18 years or older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For <strong>COPD</strong>: <strong>1 inhalation capsule</strong> once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For <strong>Asthma</strong>: <strong>1 inhalation capsule</strong> once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Use at the same time every day</strong>, and <strong>not more than once every 24 hours</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If shortness of breath or other asthma symptoms arise between doses, an <strong>inhaled SABA (Short Acting β-Agonist)</strong> should be used for immediate relief.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Respimax Pro 25 mcg+200 mcg+62.5 mcg</h3><ul><li><strong>Strong cytochrome P450 3A4 inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole)</strong>: Use with caution. May cause systemic corticosteroid and cardiovascular effects.</li><li><strong>Monoamine oxidase inhibitors and tricyclic antidepressants</strong>: Use with extreme caution, as they may potentiate the effect of vilanterol on the vascular system.</li><li><strong>Beta-blockers</strong>: Use with caution as they may block the bronchodilatory effects of beta-agonists and produce severe bronchospasm.</li><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: Use with caution, as non–potassium-sparing diuretics may worsen hypokalemia when used with beta-agonists.</li><li><strong>Anticholinergics</strong>: May interact additively with concomitant use of anticholinergic medications. Avoid using with other anticholinergic-containing drugs.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Primary treatment</strong> of status asthmaticus or acute episodes of COPD or asthma requiring intensive measures.</li><li><strong>Severe hypersensitivity</strong> to milk proteins or any ingredients.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Respimax Pro 25 mcg+200 mcg+62.5 mcg</h3><ul><li><strong>COPD</strong>: Most common adverse reactions (incidence ≥1%) include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Upper respiratory tract infection, pneumonia, bronchitis, oral candidiasis, headache, back pain, influenza, sinusitis, pharyngitis, rhinitis, dysgeusia, constipation, urinary tract infection, diarrhea, gastroenteritis, oropharyngeal pain, cough, and dysphonia.</li><li><strong>Asthma</strong>: Most common adverse reactions (incidence ≥2%) include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/nasopharyngitis, upper respiratory tract infection, bronchitis, respiratory tract infection, sinusitis, urinary tract infection, rhinitis, influenza, headache, and back pain.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>LABA monotherapy</strong> increases the risk of serious asthma-related events.</li><li>Do not initiate in <strong>acutely deteriorating COPD</strong> or asthma.</li><li><strong>Not for use in treating acute symptoms</strong>.</li><li><strong>Candida albicans</strong> infection of the mouth and pharynx may occur; patients should rinse their mouth after inhalation.</li><li>Increased <strong>risk of pneumonia</strong> in COPD patients. Monitor for symptoms of pneumonia.</li><li><strong>Paradoxical bronchospasm</strong>: Discontinue and institute alternative therapy if it occurs.</li><li>Use with caution in <strong>cardiovascular disorders</strong> due to beta-adrenergic stimulation.</li><li><strong>Urinary retention</strong> may worsen in patients with prostatic hyperplasia or bladder-neck obstruction.</li><li><strong>Caution</strong> in patients with convulsive disorders, thyrotoxicosis, diabetes mellitus, and ketoacidosis.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Respimax Pro 25 mcg+200 mcg+62.5 mcg</h3><p>There are no data available from clinical trials regarding overdose with this inhalation capsule.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li><strong>Avoid storage</strong> in direct sunlight or heat.</li><li><strong>Store</strong> in a cool and dry place.</li><li><strong>Keep away from eyes</strong>.</li><li><strong>Keep out of reach of children</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li><strong>Combined bronchodilators</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Vilanterol</strong> (LABA) stimulates β2-adrenoceptors, increasing cyclic AMP and causing smooth muscle relaxation.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Furoate</strong> (ICS) acts by modulating inflammation, reducing mast cell activation, eosinophils, and neutrophils.</li><li><strong>Umeclidinium</strong> (anticholinergic) inhibits M3 muscarinic receptors, leading to bronchodilation.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy and Pediatric Use</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Insufficient data on use in pregnant women and lactating mothers.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Not indicated for children and adolescents under 18 years. Safety and efficacy have not been established in pediatric patients.</li><li><strong>Elderly population</strong>: No dosage adjustment necessary, but greater sensitivity cannot be ruled out.</li><li><strong>Renal and Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Has not been studied in patients with renal or hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you\'d like further clarification!</p>', 'active', '1981_1742318180.png', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 22:16:20', '2025-05-04 16:40:30'),
(3366, 'Pancreon 25000', 'Pancreatin [High Lipase]', 70.00, 6, 'capsule', '6 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '300 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '215_1742318568.png', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 22:22:48', '2025-05-04 10:12:24'),
(3367, 'Femotol', 'Ferric Maltol', 11.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '30 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1730_1742318695.jpg', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 22:24:55', '2025-05-03 10:33:49'),
(3368, 'Ciprocin', 'Ciprofloxacin', 18.12, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '750 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a structured summary of the information you provided for <strong>Ciprocin 750 mg</strong>:</p><h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p>Ciprocin 750 mg is used to treat a variety of infections caused by susceptible organisms, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Single or mixed infections</strong> involving two or more organisms.</li><li>Infections resistant to other antibiotics like Aminoglycosides, Penicillins, and Cephalosporins.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong> (e.g., urethritis, cystitis, pyelonephritis, prostatitis, epididymitis).</li><li><strong>Respiratory infections</strong> (e.g., pneumonia, bronchitis, bronchiectasis, cystic fibrosis exacerbation).</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal infections</strong> (e.g., enteric fever, infective diarrhea).</li><li><strong>Infections of the skin and soft tissues</strong> (e.g., infected ulcers, abscesses, cellulitis, otitis externa).</li><li><strong>Bone and joint infections</strong> (e.g., osteomyelitis, septic arthritis).</li><li><strong>Pelvic infections</strong> (e.g., salpingitis, pelvic inflammatory diseases).</li><li><strong>Eye, ear, nose, and throat infections</strong> (e.g., otitis media, sinusitis, tonsillitis).</li><li><strong>Gonorrhea</strong> (including infections resistant to penicillin).</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li>4-Quinolone preparations</li><li>Anti-diarrhoeal antimicrobial drugs</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li>Ciprocin 750 mg is a <strong>fluoroquinolone</strong> with <strong>bactericidal activity</strong> against a wide range of gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria.</li><li>It inhibits bacterial <strong>DNA synthesis</strong> by binding to DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV, enzymes responsible for DNA supercoiling.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 100-750 mg twice daily, depending on infection severity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: 250-500 mg twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Respiratory tract infections</strong>: 250-500 mg twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Streptococcus pneumonia</strong>: 750 mg twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gonorrhea</strong>: 250-500 mg as a single dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cystic fibrosis</strong>: 750 mg twice daily for pseudomonal infections.</li><li><strong>Impaired renal function</strong>: Adjust dosage based on creatinine clearance (e.g., reduce the dose by half in severe renal impairment).</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustment necessary.</li><li><strong>Children/Adolescents</strong>: Not recommended unless benefits outweigh risks (7.5-15 mg/kg/day).</li></ul><h3><strong>Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Oral</strong>: Take with or without meals, avoid dairy products around dosing time.</li><li><strong>IV Infusion</strong>: Properly prepare the infusion, check for leaks, and regulate the flow during administration.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li>Avoid concurrent use with <strong>Magnesium/Aluminum-containing antacids</strong>, <strong>sucralfate</strong>, and products with <strong>Calcium, Iron, or Zinc</strong>.</li><li>Avoid milk and dairy products around the time of administration as it can reduce absorption.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>History of hypersensitivity to Ciprocin 750 mg or other quinolones.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Gastrointestinal disturbances (nausea, diarrhea), headache, dizziness, and fatigue.</li><li><strong>Serious</strong>: Seizures, hallucinations, confusion, skin rash, Stevens-Johnson syndrome (rare), liver enzyme changes, joint pain, hypoglycemia.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Use only if benefits outweigh risks due to potential effects on fetal development.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Not recommended, as it is excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions and Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with <strong>CNS disorders</strong> (e.g., epilepsy, arteriosclerosis).</li><li>Ensure adequate fluid intake during treatment to avoid crystalluria.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Overdose may cause <strong>seizures</strong>, confusion, <strong>renal and hepatic impairment</strong>, and <strong>hematuria</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture, and out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>4-Quinolone preparations</strong>: Anti-infective agent with a broad spectrum of activity.</li></ul><p>This is a concise overview of the key details of Ciprocin 750 mg, useful for understanding its applications, dosage, and precautions.</p>', 'active', '1856_1742318840.webp', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 22:27:20', '2025-03-18 22:27:20'),
(3369, 'Lorno', 'Lornoxicam', 25.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '8 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you provided, organized with headings in normal bold text:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Lorno 8 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Short-term relief of acute mild to moderate pain</li><li>Symptomatic relief of pain and inflammation in osteoarthritis</li><li>Symptomatic relief of pain and inflammation in rheumatoid arthritis</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Lorno 8 mg</strong></p><ul><li>For all patients, the appropriate dosing regimen should be based upon individual response to treatment.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pain</strong>: 8-16 mg Lorno 8 mg daily, divided into 2 or 3 doses. The maximum recommended daily dose is 16 mg.</li><li><strong>Osteoarthritis and Rheumatoid arthritis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Initial recommended dose: 12 mg Lorno 8 mg daily, divided into 2 or 3 doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: Should not exceed 16 mg Lorno 8 mg daily.</li><li>Lorno 8 mg film-coated tablets are supplied for oral use and should be taken with a sufficient quantity of liquid.</li><li><strong>Children and adolescents</strong>:</li><li> Lorno 8 mg is not recommended for use in children and adolescents below age 18 due to a lack of data on safety and efficacy.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>:</li><li> No special dosage modification is required for elderly patients above age 65, but Lorno 8 mg should be administered with precaution, as gastrointestinal adverse effects are less well tolerated in this group.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>:</li><li> For patients with mild to moderate renal impairment, the maximum recommended daily dose is 12 mg divided into 2 or 3 doses.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>:</li><li> For patients with moderate hepatic impairment, the maximum recommended daily dose is 12 mg divided into 2 or 3 doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Lorno 8 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cimetidine</strong>: Increased plasma concentrations of Lorno 8 mg. (No interaction between Lorno 8 mg and ranitidine, or Lorno 8 mg and antacids has been demonstrated).</li><li><strong>Anti-coagulants</strong>: NSAIDs may enhance the effects of anti-coagulants, such as warfarin (careful monitoring of INR should be undertaken).</li><li><strong>Phenprocoumon</strong>: Decreased effect of phenprocoumon treatment.</li><li><strong>Heparin</strong>: NSAIDs increase the risk of spinal or epidural haematoma when given concomitantly with heparin in the context of spinal or epidural anaesthesia.</li><li><strong>ACE inhibitors</strong>: The antihypertensive effect of the ACE inhibitor may decrease.</li><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: Decreased diuretic and antihypertensive effect of loop diuretics, thiazide diuretics, and potassium-sparing diuretics.</li><li><strong>Beta-adrenergic blockers</strong>: Decreased antihypertensive efficacy.</li><li><strong>Angiotensin II receptor blockers</strong>: Decreased antihypertensive efficacy.</li><li><strong>Digoxin</strong>: Decreased renal clearance of digoxin.</li><li><strong>Corticosteroids</strong>: Increased risk of gastrointestinal ulceration or bleeding.</li><li><strong>Quinolone antibiotics</strong>: Increased risk of seizures.</li><li><strong>Anti-platelet agents</strong>: Increased risk of gastrointestinal bleeding.</li><li><strong>Other NSAIDs</strong>: Increased risk of gastrointestinal bleeding.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: Increased serum concentration of methotrexate, potentially leading to increased toxicity.</li><li><strong>Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs)</strong>: Increased risk of gastrointestinal bleeding.</li><li><strong>Lithium</strong>: NSAIDs inhibit renal clearance of lithium, leading to an increase in lithium serum concentration, which requires monitoring.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong>: Increased serum concentration of cyclosporine, potentially enhancing nephrotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Sulphonylureas (e.g., glibenclamide)</strong>: Increased risk of hypoglycaemia.</li><li><strong>Known inducers and inhibitors of CYP2C9 isoenzymes</strong>: Lorno 8 mg may interact with known inducers and inhibitors of CYP2C9 isoenzymes.</li><li><strong>Tacrolimus</strong>: Increased risk of nephrotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Pemetrexed</strong>: NSAIDs may reduce the renal clearance of pemetrexed, resulting in increased renal and gastrointestinal toxicity and myelosuppression.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Lorno 8 mg or to any of the excipients.</li><li>Thrombocytopenia.</li><li>Hypersensitivity (symptoms like asthma, rhinitis, angioedema, or urticaria) to other NSAIDs, including acetylsalicylic acid.</li><li>Severe heart failure.</li><li>Gastro-intestinal bleeding, cerebrovascular bleeding, or other bleeding disorders.</li><li>History of gastrointestinal bleeding or perforation related to previous NSAID therapy.</li><li>Active or history of recurrent peptic ulcer/haemorrhage (two or more distinct episodes of proven ulceration or bleeding).</li><li>Severe hepatic impairment.</li><li>Severe renal impairment (Serum creatinine &gt; 700 µmol/l).</li><li>The third trimester of pregnancy.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: Lorno 8 mg should be used cautiously in patients with mild to moderate renal impairment (serum creatinine 150-700 µmol/l). Renal functions should be monitored, and treatment should be discontinued if renal function deteriorates.</li><li><strong>Blood coagulation disorders</strong>: Careful clinical monitoring and laboratory assessment is recommended (e.g., APTT).</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: Clinical monitoring and regular laboratory assessments are recommended in patients with hepatic impairment. Accumulation of Lorno 8 mg may occur after daily doses of 12-16 mg.</li><li><strong>Long-term treatment (longer than 3 months)</strong>: Regular laboratory assessments of haematology (haemoglobin), renal functions (creatinine), and liver enzymes are recommended.</li><li><strong>Elderly patients above 65 years</strong>: Monitoring of renal and hepatic function is recommended, and caution is advised in elderly postoperative patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Lorno 8 mg is an NSAID that belongs to the oxicam class. It inhibits the cyclooxygenase enzymes (COX-1 and COX-2), responsible for the formation of prostaglandins and thromboxane, which mediate inflammation and pain. Lorno 8 mg’s inhibition of COX enzymes reduces inflammation, pain, fever, and swelling. Unlike some other NSAIDs, it does not increase leukotriene formation, thus minimizing the risk of certain adverse events.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Lorno 8 mg is contraindicated in the third trimester of pregnancy and should not be used during pregnancy in the first and second trimesters or during delivery, as there is no clinical data on exposed pregnancies.</li><li>Studies in animals have shown reproductive toxicity.</li><li>There are no data on the excretion of Lorno 8 mg in human breast milk, but it is excreted in the milk of lactating rats in relatively high concentrations. Therefore, Lorno 8 mg should not be used in breastfeeding women.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further information or details!</p>', 'active', '888_1742319050.jpg', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 22:30:50', '2025-03-18 22:30:50');
