(3370, 'Lorno', 'Lornoxicam', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '4 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Lorno 4 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Short-term relief of acute mild to moderate pain.</li><li>Symptomatic relief of pain and inflammation in osteoarthritis.</li><li>Symptomatic relief of pain and inflammation in rheumatoid arthritis.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Lorno 4 mg</strong></p><ul><li>For all patients, the appropriate dosing regimen should be based upon individual response to treatment.</li><li><strong>Pain</strong>: 8-16 mg Lorno 4 mg daily, divided into 2 or 3 doses. The maximum recommended daily dose is 16 mg.</li><li><strong>Osteoarthritis and Rheumatoid arthritis</strong>: Initial recommended dose is 12 mg Lorno 4 mg daily, divided into 2 or 3 doses. The maintenance dose should not exceed 16 mg Lorno 4 mg daily.</li><li>Lorno 4 mg film-coated tablets are for oral use and should be taken with a sufficient quantity of liquid.</li><li><strong>Children and Adolescents</strong>: Lorno 4 mg is not recommended for use in children and adolescents below age 18 due to a lack of data on safety and efficacy.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No special dosage modification is required for elderly patients above age 65, but Lorno 4 mg should be administered with caution as gastrointestinal adverse effects are less well tolerated in this group.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: For patients with mild to moderate renal impairment, the maximum recommended daily dose is 12 mg, divided into 2 or 3 doses.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: For patients with moderate hepatic impairment, the maximum recommended daily dose is 12 mg, divided into 2 or 3 doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Lorno 4 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cimetidine</strong>: Increased plasma concentrations of Lorno 4 mg.</li><li><strong>Anti-coagulants</strong>: NSAIDs may enhance the effects of anti-coagulants, such as warfarin. Careful monitoring of INR should be undertaken.</li><li><strong>Phenprocoumon</strong>: Decreased effect of phenprocoumon treatment.</li><li><strong>Heparin</strong>: Increased risk of spinal or epidural haematoma when given with heparin in the context of spinal or epidural anaesthesia.</li><li><strong>ACE inhibitors</strong>: Decreased antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: Decreased diuretic and antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>Beta-adrenergic blockers</strong>: Decreased antihypertensive efficacy.</li><li><strong>Angiotensin II receptor blocker</strong>: Decreased antihypertensive efficacy.</li><li><strong>Digoxin</strong>: Decreased renal clearance of digoxin.</li><li><strong>Corticosteroids</strong>: Increased risk of gastrointestinal ulceration or bleeding.</li><li><strong>Quinolone antibiotics</strong>: Increased risk of seizures.</li><li><strong>Anti-platelet agents</strong>: Increased risk of gastrointestinal bleeding.</li><li><strong>Other NSAIDs</strong>: Increased risk of gastrointestinal bleeding.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: Increased serum concentration of methotrexate.</li><li><strong>Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs)</strong>: Increased risk of gastrointestinal bleeding.</li><li><strong>Lithium</strong>: NSAIDs inhibit renal clearance of lithium, increasing the serum concentration of lithium.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong>: Increased serum concentration of cyclosporine.</li><li><strong>Sulphonylureas</strong>: Increased risk of hypoglycaemia.</li><li><strong>Known inducers and inhibitors of CYP2C9 isoenzymes</strong>: Lorno 4 mg interacts with inducers and inhibitors of CYP2C9 isoenzymes.</li><li><strong>Tacrolimus</strong>: Increased risk of nephrotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Pemetrexed</strong>: NSAIDs may reduce renal clearance of pemetrexed, increasing toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Lorno 4 mg or any of the excipients.</li><li>Thrombocytopenia.</li><li>Hypersensitivity to other NSAIDs including acetylsalicylic acid.</li><li>Severe heart failure.</li><li>Gastrointestinal bleeding, cerebrovascular bleeding, or other bleeding disorders.</li><li>History of gastrointestinal bleeding or perforation related to previous NSAIDs therapy.</li><li>Active or history of recurrent peptic ulcer/haemorrhage.</li><li>Severe hepatic impairment.</li><li>Severe renal impairment (Serum creatinine &gt; 700 µmol/l).</li><li>The third trimester of pregnancy.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: Administer with caution in patients with renal impairment due to dependence on renal prostaglandins. Discontinue if renal function deteriorates.</li><li><strong>Blood coagulation disorders</strong>: Careful clinical monitoring and laboratory assessment are recommended.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: Clinical monitoring is advised for patients with liver cirrhosis, as accumulation of Lorno 4 mg may occur.</li><li><strong>Long-term treatment</strong>: Regular monitoring of haematology, renal functions, and liver enzymes is recommended for treatments longer than 3 months.</li><li><strong>Elderly patients</strong>: Monitoring of renal and hepatic function is recommended, especially in postoperative patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs).</li></ul><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Lorno 4 mg is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) that inhibits cyclooxygenase enzymes (COX-1 and COX-2), reducing the formation of prostaglandins and thromboxane, which mediate inflammation, pain, fever, and swelling.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Lorno 4 mg is contraindicated in the third trimester of pregnancy and should not be used during pregnancy in the first and second trimesters due to a lack of clinical data. It should not be used during delivery. Animal studies have shown reproductive toxicity, and there is no data on excretion in human breast milk. Therefore, Lorno 4 mg should not be used in breastfeeding women.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '787_1742321477.jpg', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 23:11:17', '2025-03-18 23:11:17'),
(3371, 'Combair', 'Salbutamol + Ipratropium', 250.75, 1, 'x 200 metered doses', '200 metered doses', 'Metered-Dose Inhaler (MDI)', '(100 mcg+20 mcg)/puff', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1920_1742321884.webp', 44, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 23:18:04', '2025-04-23 07:42:07'),
(3372, 'Sertacon VS', 'Sertaconazole Nitrate [Vaginal]', 750.00, 1, 'suppository', '1 Suppository', 'Suppository', '300 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '988_1742322087.webp', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 23:21:27', '2025-05-04 21:06:41'),
(3373, 'Epastat', 'Epalrestat', 10.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you requested with headings in bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Epastat 50 mg</strong></p><p> Epastat 50 mg consequently improves peripheral neuropathy due to sorbitol accumulation. It is usually used to improve numbness, pain, and abnormality of vibration sensation/heart rate variability associated with diabetic peripheral neuropathy.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Aldose reductase inhibitor</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Epastat 50 mg is an aldose reductase inhibitor. The aldose reductase inhibitors have a distinct mechanism of action, affecting the underlying disease process in diabetic neuropathy, although the extent of the polyol pathway involvement is yet to be determined. This medicine suppresses the accumulation of sorbitol in cells by inhibiting the action of aldose reductase, which converts glucose (sugar) to sorbitol. Epastat 50 mg may improve motor and sensory nerve conduction velocity and subjective neuropathy symptoms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Epastat 50 mg</strong></p><p> In general, for adults, take 1 tablet (50 mg of the active ingredient) at a time, 3 times a day before meals. The dosage may be adjusted according to your age and symptoms.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Epastat 50 mg</strong></p><p> There are no known drug interactions and none well documented.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>If you have previously experienced any allergic reactions (itch, rash, etc.) to any medicines.</li><li>If you are pregnant or breastfeeding.</li><li>If you are taking any other medicinal products. (Some medicines may interact to enhance or diminish medicinal effects. Beware of over-the-counter medicines and dietary supplements as well as other prescription medicines.)</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Epastat 50 mg</strong></p><p> The most commonly reported adverse reactions include abdominal pain, nausea, rash, itch, erythema, and blisters.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The pregnancy category is not classified. The FDA has not yet classified the drug into a specified pregnancy category.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Your urinary color may turn yellowish-brown or red due to the color of the components of this medicine. Do not worry about the change.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep out of the reach of children. Store away from direct sunlight, heat, and moisture.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Aldose reductase inhibitor</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Epastat 50 mg is an aldose reductase inhibitor. The aldose reductase inhibitors have a distinct mechanism of action, affecting the underlying disease process in diabetic neuropathy, although the extent of the polyol pathway involvement is yet to be determined. This medicine suppresses the accumulation of sorbitol in cells by inhibiting the action of aldose reductase, which converts glucose (sugar) to sorbitol. Epastat 50 mg may improve motor and sensory nerve conduction velocity and subjective neuropathy symptoms.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The pregnancy category is not classified. The FDA has not yet classified the drug into a specified pregnancy category.</p>', 'active', '554_1742322232.jpg', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 23:23:52', '2025-03-18 23:23:52'),
(3374, 'Biotab', 'Biotin', 25.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5000 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Biotab 5000 mcg</strong></p><p> Biotab 5000 mcg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Hair loss</li><li>Weak, brittle, splitting, or soft nails</li><li>Eczema &amp; dermatitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Herbal and Nutraceuticals</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Biotab 5000 mcg is an essential vitamin, also known as vitamin H. It plays a crucial role in vital physiological functions such as fatty acid synthesis, amino acid catabolism, and gluconeogenesis. It acts as a cofactor in the enzymatic carboxylation of pyruvate and acetyl-CoA. Biotab 5000 mcg influences the growth and differentiation of epidermal cells and is essential for the formation and renewal of skin, hair, and nails. It functions as a coenzyme for mitochondrial carboxylases in hair roots and improves the keratin structure. Biotab 5000 mcg deficiency may lead to hair loss and various systemic symptoms such as dermatitis and aciduria. It is one of the most prescribed nutritional supplements for any form of hair loss.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Biotab 5000 mcg</strong></p><p> The dosage of Biotab 5000 mcg varies depending on the disease condition and age group. Generally, 1-3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician.</p><ul><li>In children, Biotab 5000 mcg deficiency often occurs between 1 week and 2 years of age and may present with seizures, hypotonia, developmental delay, ataxia, hyperventilation, and coma. In such cases, 5-10 tablets daily, crushed and mixed with water or fruit juice, or as advised by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Biotab 5000 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>Anticonvulsant drugs may lower the plasma level of Biotab 5000 mcg.</li><li>Antibiotics may reduce the contribution of Biotab 5000 mcg from the microflora of the large intestine.</li><li>Excessive consumption of raw egg whites interferes with Biotab 5000 mcg absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Sufficient clinical data is not available for use in pregnant women and lactating mothers.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1630_1742322379.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 23:26:19', '2025-03-18 23:26:19'),
(3375, 'Limaryl M', 'Glimepiride + Metformin', 9.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '1 mg plus 500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Limaryl M 1 mg + 500 mg:</strong></p><p>This tablet is indicated as an adjunct to diet and exercise for patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus, particularly when monotherapy with glimepiride or metformin does not provide adequate glycemic control. It may also serve as a replacement for combination therapy using glimepiride and metformin.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p> Combination Oral Hypoglycemic Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Glimepiride:</strong> A sulfonylurea that stimulates insulin release from functioning pancreatic beta-cells. It also increases insulin sensitivity in peripheral tissues, improving glucose uptake.</li><li><strong>Metformin:</strong> An antihyperglycemic agent that lowers both basal and postprandial plasma glucose. It reduces hepatic glucose production, stimulates intracellular glycogen synthesis, and enhances insulin sensitivity, thereby improving peripheral glucose uptake and utilization.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>Take once or twice a day with meals.</li><li>Maximum single dose of metformin is 1000 mg and the daily maximum is 2000 mg.</li><li>Maximum daily dose for glimepiride is 8 mg.</li><li>The initial dose should not exceed the current daily doses of glimepiride and metformin to avoid hypoglycemia.</li><li>Regular blood glucose and glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c) monitoring is required.</li><li>Incorrect administration (e.g., skipping doses) should never be corrected by taking larger doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>Swallow the tablet whole. Do not crush or chew.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Glimepiride:</strong> It is metabolized by CYP2C9, so be cautious when coadministering with drugs that induce or inhibit this enzyme. Certain drugs, like insulin, other oral antidiabetic agents, ACE inhibitors, and some antibiotics, can enhance the hypoglycemic effect, leading to potential hypoglycemia. Conversely, drugs like corticosteroids, diuretics, and sympathomimetic agents can raise blood glucose levels.</li><li><strong>Metformin:</strong> Alcohol increases the risk of lactic acidosis. Metformin should be discontinued before imaging procedures involving iodinated contrast agents. It is also contraindicated in cases of renal dysfunction (GFR &lt; 30 mL/min) and in patients with severe metabolic acidosis or dehydration.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Glimepiride:</strong> Hypersensitivity to glimepiride, metformin, other sulfonylureas, and sulfonamides. It is contraindicated in pregnant and breastfeeding women, as well as in those with severe hepatic impairment or in dialysis patients.</li><li><strong>Metformin:</strong> Contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to metformin, severe renal failure, diabetic ketoacidosis, lactic acidosis, and during lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Glimepiride:</strong> Common side effects include hypoglycemia, gastrointestinal symptoms (nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain), and visual disturbances. Severe side effects may include liver failure, thrombocytopenia, or allergic reactions.</li><li><strong>Metformin:</strong> Most common side effects include gastrointestinal symptoms like nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. Rare side effects include lactic acidosis, hepatobiliary disorders, and B12 deficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> This combination is contraindicated during pregnancy. Insulin therapy should be used to maintain normal blood glucose levels during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong> The use of glimepiride is contraindicated during breastfeeding. If needed, the patient should be switched to insulin therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Glimepiride:</strong> Increased risk of hypoglycemia, especially during the initial weeks of treatment. Regular monitoring is necessary. Caution should be used in patients with G6PD deficiency.</li><li><strong>Metformin:</strong> Regular monitoring of thyroid function and vitamin B12 levels is recommended during long-term use.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Glimepiride:</strong> Overdose may result in severe hypoglycemia. Immediate action with sugar or glucose is necessary. In serious cases, hospitalization and intravenous glucose or glucagon administration may be required.</li><li><strong>Metformin:</strong> Overdose may lead to lactic acidosis, a medical emergency requiring immediate hospital treatment. Hemodialysis is the most effective method to remove metformin.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place at temperatures not exceeding 25°C, and protect from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly Patients:</strong> Metformin should be used with caution, especially in those with impaired renal function. Regular renal function monitoring is essential.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients:</strong> Safety and efficacy in children have not been established.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Dosing adjustments may be necessary for patients with impaired renal function. In patients with a GFR of less than 30 mL/min, metformin is contraindicated.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><p> Combination Oral Hypoglycemic Preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Glimepiride:</strong> Stimulates insulin release from pancreatic beta cells and increases peripheral insulin sensitivity.</li><li><strong>Metformin:</strong> Reduces hepatic glucose production, decreases intestinal glucose absorption, and improves peripheral insulin sensitivity.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '664_1742322521.webp', 17, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 23:28:41', '2025-03-18 23:28:41'),
(3376, 'Xiomox', 'Moxifloxacin Hydrochloride', 40.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xiomox 400 mg</strong></p><p> The ophthalmic solution is indicated for the treatment of bacterial conjunctivitis caused by susceptible strains of the following organisms:</p><ul><li><strong>Aerobic Gram-positive microorganisms</strong>: <em>Corynebacterium</em> species, <em>Micrococcus luteus</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Staphylococcus epidermidis</em>, <em>Staphylococcus haemolyticus</em>, <em>Staphylococcus hominis</em>, <em>Staphylococcus warneri</em>, <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Streptococcus viridans</em> group.</li><li><strong>Aerobic Gram-negative microorganisms</strong>: <em>Acinetobacter lwoffii</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em>.</li><li><strong>Other microorganisms</strong>: <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic antibacterial drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Moxifloxacin is a synthetic broad-spectrum, fluoroquinolone derivative antibacterial agent. Moxifloxacin has in vitro activity against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative microorganisms. Its bactericidal action results from inhibition of topoisomerase II (DNA gyrase) and topoisomerase IV, which are required for bacterial DNA replication, transcription, repair, and recombination.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xiomox 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Eye Drops</strong>: One drop in the affected eye 3 times per day for 7 days.</li><li><strong>Eye Ointment</strong>: Apply thinly and evenly to the affected eye three times a day for the first two days. For the next five days, apply two times a day, or as advised by the registered physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xiomox 400 mg</strong></p><p> Drug-drug interaction studies have not been conducted with Xiomox 400 mg ophthalmic solution. In vitro studies indicate that Moxifloxacin does not inhibit CYP3A4, CYP2D6, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, or CYP1A2, indicating that Moxifloxacin is unlikely to alter the pharmacokinetics of drugs metabolized by these cytochrome P450 isozymes.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Xiomox 400 mg ophthalmic solution is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Moxifloxacin, other quinolones, or any of the components in this medication.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xiomox 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li>The most frequently reported ocular adverse events were conjunctivitis, decreased visual acuity, dry eye, keratitis, ocular discomfort, ocular hyperemia, ocular pain, ocular pruritus, subconjunctival hemorrhage, and tearing. These occurred in approximately 1-6% of patients.</li><li>Nonocular adverse events reported at a rate of 1-4% included fever, increased cough, infection, otitis media, pharyngitis, rash, and rhinitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Since there are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women, Xiomox 400 mg ophthalmic solution should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li>Moxifloxacin has not been measured in human milk, although it can be presumed to be excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Moxifloxacin hydrochloride ophthalmic solution is administered to a nursing mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged use may result in the overgrowth of nonsusceptible organisms, including fungi. If superinfection occurs, discontinue use and institute alternative therapy.</li><li>Patients should be examined with the aid of magnification, such as slit-lamp biomicroscopy, and, where appropriate, fluorescein staining.</li><li>Patients should be advised not to wear contact lenses if they have signs and symptoms of bacterial conjunctivitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Do not touch the dropper tip to surfaces since this may contaminate the solution.</li><li>Do not use after 30 days of first opening.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '430_1742322687.webp', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 23:31:27', '2025-03-18 23:31:27'),
(3377, 'Teslar-M', 'Amlodipine Besilate + Telmisartan', 12.50, 7, 'tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Teslar-M 5 mg+40 mg</strong></p><p>Teslar-M is indicated for the treatment of hypertension, either alone or in combination with other antihypertensive agents. It may also be used as initial therapy in patients likely to need multiple drugs to achieve their blood pressure goals.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Teslar-M 5 mg+40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial Therapy</strong>: This tablet may be started if it is unlikely that blood pressure control would be achieved with a single agent. The usual starting dose is 40/5 mg once daily. Patients requiring greater blood pressure reduction may start with 80/5 mg once daily. Initial therapy with this combination is not recommended for patients 75 years or older or those with hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Add-on Therapy</strong>: For patients inadequately controlled with amlodipine alone or telmisartan alone. For patients on 10 mg amlodipine experiencing side effects like edema, this 40/5 mg tablet may be used once daily, reducing amlodipine without affecting the overall expected antihypertensive response.</li><li><strong>Replacement Therapy</strong>: Patients taking amlodipine and telmisartan separately may switch to this combination tablet containing the same component doses, taken once daily. Dosage must be individualized, and increases can be made after at least 2 weeks. The maximum recommended dose is 80/10 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Teslar-M 5 mg+40 mg</strong></p><p>Co-administration of telmisartan with acetaminophen, amlodipine, glyburide, simvastatin, hydrochlorothiazide, warfarin, or ibuprofen did not result in clinically significant interactions. Telmisartan is not metabolized by the cytochrome P450 system and has minimal effects on cytochrome P450 enzymes, except for slight inhibition of CYP2C19.</p><p>Amlodipine has been safely administered with various drugs, including thiazide diuretics, beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors, nitrates, digoxin, warfarin, NSAIDs, and oral hypoglycemic drugs. There are no clinically significant interactions with cimetidine, grapefruit juice, or sildenafil. Amlodipine does not affect the pharmacokinetics or pharmacodynamics of atorvastatin, digoxin, or warfarin.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to this product or any of its components.</li><li>Pregnancy and lactation.</li><li>Biliary obstructive disorders, severe hepatic impairment, hypotension, cardiogenic shock, or left ventricular outflow tract obstruction.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Teslar-M 5 mg+40 mg</strong></p><p>Possible side effects include:</p><ul><li>Dizziness, peripheral edema, migraine, headache, paraesthesia, vertigo, bradycardia, palpitations, hypotension, cough, abdominal pain, diarrhea, nausea, pruritus, myalgia, spasm, erectile dysfunction, chest pain, fatigue, and edema.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Avoid fetal or neonatal exposure</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hypotension</strong>: Correct any volume or salt depletion before starting therapy. Monitor for signs of hypotension.</li><li><strong>Titrate slowly</strong> in patients with hepatic or severe renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Heart failure</strong>: Monitor for worsening symptoms.</li><li><strong>Avoid concomitant use</strong> of ACE inhibitors and angiotensin receptor blockers.</li><li><strong>Myocardial infarction</strong>: In patients with severe coronary artery disease, initiation of a calcium channel blocker may increase the risk of myocardial infarction or worsen angina.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Teslar-M 5 mg+40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Telmisartan</strong>: Overdose may cause hypotension, dizziness, and tachycardia. In case of symptomatic hypotension, supportive treatment is recommended, and telmisartan is not removed by hemodialysis.</li><li><strong>Amlodipine</strong>: Overdose may cause excessive peripheral vasodilation, marked hypotension, and possible reflex tachycardia. In the event of massive overdose, cardiovascular and respiratory monitoring is essential, with fluid administration and vasopressors (e.g., phenylephrine) considered if hypotension persists. Amlodipine is not removed by hemodialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C. Protect from light and high humidity. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Combined antihypertensive preparations.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Teslar-M is a fixed-dose combination of Telmisartan (an angiotensin receptor blocker) and Amlodipine (a calcium channel blocker).</p><ul><li><strong>Telmisartan</strong> works by blocking the action of angiotensin II, which is responsible for blood vessel constriction, helping to dilate blood vessels and reduce blood pressure.</li><li><strong>Amlodipine</strong> reduces peripheral vascular resistance by inhibiting calcium influx into vascular smooth muscle and cardiac muscle, which leads to a decrease in blood pressure.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category C (first trimester)</strong> and <strong>Category D (second and third trimesters)</strong>.</li><li>It is unknown whether Telmisartan and Amlodipine are excreted in human milk. Due to potential adverse effects on the nursing infant, either discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug based on its importance to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><p>Initial therapy with this combination is not recommended for patients aged 75 years or older.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong></p><p>Initial therapy with this combination is not recommended for patients with hepatic impairment.</p>', 'active', '1446_1742323008.webp', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 23:36:48', '2025-05-06 20:43:28'),
(3378, 'Xyclon', 'Clonazepam', 6.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xyclon 0.5 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Panic Disorder</strong>: Treats panic disorder, with or without agoraphobia, characterized by unexpected panic attacks and the fear of future episodes.</li><li><strong>Seizure Disorders</strong>: Used alone or as an adjunct for treating Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome (petit mal variant), akinetic seizures, myoclonic seizures, and absence seizures (if unresponsive to succinimides).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</p><ul><li>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</li><li>Benzodiazepine hypnotics</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><ul><li>Xyclon 0.5 mg works by reducing nerve transmission in the motor cortex and enhancing GABA activity, thus controlling spike and wave discharges, especially in absence seizures. It is effective in focal and generalized seizures.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>For Adults with Seizure Disorders</strong>:</li><li> Start at 1.5 mg/day (divided into three doses). Increase by 0.5-1 mg every 3 days, with a maximum of 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>For Adults with Panic Disorder</strong>:</li><li> Begin with 0.25 mg twice daily. After 3 days, increase to 1 mg/day as needed.</li><li><strong>For Pediatric Patients</strong>:</li><li> Infants and children (up to 10 years or 30 kg): 0.01-0.03 mg/kg/day, not exceeding 0.05 mg/kg/day, split into multiple doses.</li><li><strong>Injection Dosage</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children</strong>: 0.5 mg slow IV injection or infusion.</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 1 mg slow IV injection or infusion (1-4 mg if necessary).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"> IV rate should be controlled at 0.25-0.5 mg/min.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong>:</p><ul><li>No impact on the pharmacokinetics of phenytoin, carbamazepine, or phenobarbital.</li><li>The effect on other drug metabolism is not fully explored.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><ul><li>Avoid in patients with hypersensitivity to benzodiazepines or significant liver disease.</li><li>Not recommended for acute narrow-angle glaucoma but can be used with open-angle glaucoma under treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>CNS-related</strong>: Drowsiness (50% of patients), ataxia (30%), and other potential effects like abnormal eye movements, aphonia, coma, tremor, depression, hallucinations, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li>Consider during pregnancy only if benefits outweigh risks.</li><li>Avoid breastfeeding while on Xyclon 0.5 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong>:</p><ul><li>May exacerbate generalized tonic-clonic seizures in patients with multiple seizure disorders.</li><li>Co-administration with valproic acid may result in absence status.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose</strong>:</p><ul><li>Symptoms: Drowsiness, ataxia, dysarthria, nystagmus, and in severe cases, apnea, hypotension, and coma.</li><li>Treatment: Supportive care, activated charcoal (if within 1-2 hours), flumazenil (with caution).</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat, out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong>:</p><ul><li>For IV injection, dilute with 1 ml of water before administration.</li><li>For infusion, dilute with at least 85 ml of appropriate diluent.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</p><ul><li>Xyclon 0.5 mg enhances GABAergic neurotransmission, leading to anticonvulsant, sedative, muscle-relaxing, and anxiolytic effects. It rapidly suppresses paroxysmal activity in various seizure types, including absence seizures.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric and Geriatric Use</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric</strong>: May increase saliva and bronchial secretions. Ensure clear airways.</li><li><strong>Geriatric</strong>: May experience heightened sensitivity to the medication.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Plasma binding and clearance may differ in cirrhosis patients.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need more details or any specific section to be expanded!</p>', 'active', '1482_1742323126.jpg', 27, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 23:38:46', '2025-03-18 23:38:46'),
(3379, 'Tapisis', 'Tapinarof', 1000.00, 1, 'x 20 gm tube', '20 gm tube', 'Cream', '1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1431_1742323329.jpg', 37, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 23:42:09', '2025-05-04 22:16:59'),
(3380, 'Sacuva', 'Sacubitril + Valsartan', 75.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '49 mg plus 51 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Sacuva 49 mg + 51 mg - Indications, Dosage, and Key Information</strong></h3><p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Sacuva 49 mg + 51 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Reduction of the risk of cardiovascular death</strong> and <strong>hospitalization for heart failure</strong> in patients with chronic heart failure (NYHA Class II-IV) and reduced ejection fraction.</li><li><strong>Treatment of symptomatic heart failure</strong> with systemic left ventricular systolic dysfunction in pediatric patients aged <strong>one year and older</strong>.</li><li>It is typically administered <strong>in conjunction with other heart failure therapies</strong>, replacing angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors (ACE inhibitors) or other angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Combined antihypertensive preparations</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Sacubitril</strong>: Its active metabolite, LBQ657, inhibits neprilysin, an enzyme that breaks down natriuretic peptides (ANP, BNP, CNP). By preventing this breakdown, sacubitril increases the concentration of natriuretic peptides, leading to vasodilation, natriuresis, and diuresis.</li><li><strong>Valsartan</strong>: An angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB), valsartan selectively blocks the AT1 receptor, reducing vasoconstriction and lowering blood pressure without affecting the pulse rate.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults (Heart Failure)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Starting dose: 49/51 mg orally <strong>twice daily</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">After 2 to 4 weeks, the dose may be doubled to the target maintenance dose of 97/103 mg twice daily, as tolerated.</li><li><strong>Dose Adjustments</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Low-dose ACEi/ARB users</strong>: Start with 24/26 mg twice daily, and then increase the dose as tolerated.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Adjust the dose based on severity, with a starting dose of 24/26 mg twice daily for severe renal impairment.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: A starting dose of 24/26 mg twice daily is recommended for moderate hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Heart Failure (aged 1 year and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Starting doses are weight-based and adjusted over time.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Less than 40 kg</strong>: Starting at 1.6 mg/kg, with dose escalation as tolerated.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>At least 40 kg but less than 50 kg</strong>: Start at 24/26 mg, increase as tolerated.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Sacuva 49 mg + 51 mg is contraindicated:</p><ul><li>In patients with a <strong>history of angioedema</strong> related to previous ACE inhibitors or ARB therapy.</li><li>In patients <strong>taking ACE inhibitors</strong> (allow a 36-hour washout period before switching).</li><li>With <strong>aliskiren</strong> in diabetic patients.</li><li>In <strong>pregnant</strong> women (may harm the fetus).</li></ul><p><strong>Common Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Angioedema</strong></li><li><strong>Hypotension</strong></li><li><strong>Impaired renal function</strong></li><li><strong>Hyperkalemia</strong></li><li><strong>Cough</strong></li><li><strong>Dizziness</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Not recommended due to potential harm to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Breastfeeding is not recommended due to unknown effects on the infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>May cause <strong>angioedema</strong>.</li><li>Close monitoring of <strong>serum creatinine</strong> and <strong>potassium</strong> is required.</li><li>Avoid in <strong>patients with severe renal impairment</strong> or <strong>hepatic impairment</strong> (Child-Pugh C).</li><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong> and <strong>lactation</strong> warnings apply.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose</strong></p><ul><li>The most likely symptom of an overdose is <strong>hypotension</strong>. Symptomatic treatment should be provided.</li><li><strong>Hemodialysis</strong> is unlikely to remove the drug due to its high protein binding.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a <strong>dry place</strong> at <strong>below 30°C</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established for children under 1 year of age.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No significant pharmacokinetic differences observed in elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Adjust starting dose in severe renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Adjust starting dose for moderate hepatic impairment; avoid in severe cases.</li></ul><p>This information summarizes the key points for the safe and effective use of Sacuva 49 mg + 51 mg. Always consult with a healthcare provider for personalized advice.</p>', 'active', '884_1742323503.jpg', 5, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 23:45:03', '2025-03-18 23:45:03'),
(3381, 'Febout', 'Febuxostat', 12.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Febout 40 mg</strong></p><p> Febout 40 mg is indicated for the chronic management of hyperuricemia in patients with gout. Febout 40 mg is not recommended for the treatment of asymptomatic hyperuricemia.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs used in Gout</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Febout 40 mg is a non-purine, selective xanthine oxidase (XO) inhibitor. It decreases serum uric acid levels by inhibiting xanthine oxidase, which is responsible for uric acid production. Xanthine oxidase breaks down hypoxanthine to xanthine and then to uric acid. Febout 40 mg is not expected to inhibit other enzymes involved in purine and pyrimidine synthesis and metabolism at therapeutic concentrations.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Febout 40 mg</strong></p><p> Febout 40 mg is recommended at 40 mg or 80 mg once daily. The recommended starting dose of Febout 40 mg is 40 mg once daily. For patients who do not achieve a serum uric acid level of less than 6 mg/dL after 2 weeks with 40 mg, Febout 40 mg 80 mg is recommended. Febout 40 mg can be administered without regard to food or antacid use. No dose adjustment is necessary when administering Febout 40 mg to patients with mild to moderate renal or hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Febout 40 mg</strong></p><p> Concomitant administration of Febout 40 mg with azathioprine, mercaptopurine, or theophylline could increase plasma concentrations of these drugs, resulting in severe toxicity.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Febout 40 mg is contraindicated in patients being treated with azathioprine, mercaptopurine, or theophylline.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Febout 40 mg</strong></p><p> The most common adverse events associated with the use of Febout 40 mg may include liver function abnormalities, nausea, arthralgia, and rash.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Febout 40 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Febout 40 mg is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gout Flare</strong>: An increase in gout flares is frequently observed during initiation of anti-hyperuricemic agents, including Febout 40 mg. If a gout flare occurs during treatment, Febout 40 mg need not be discontinued. Prophylactic therapy (i.e., non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) or colchicine upon initiation of treatment) may be beneficial for up to six months.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Events</strong>: A higher rate of cardiovascular thromboembolic events was observed in patients treated with Febout 40 mg than with allopurinol in clinical trials. Monitor for signs and symptoms of MI and stroke.</li><li><strong>Liver Enzyme Elevation</strong>: Transaminase elevations have been observed in Febout 40 mg-treated patients. Monitor liver function tests periodically.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Febout 40 mg</strong></p><p> Febout was studied in healthy subjects in doses up to 300 mg daily for seven days without evidence of dose-limiting toxicities.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients under 18 years of age have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Drugs used in Gout</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Febout 40 mg is a non-purine, selective xanthine oxidase (XO) inhibitor. It decreases serum uric acid levels by inhibiting xanthine oxidase, which is responsible for uric acid production. Xanthine oxidase breaks down hypoxanthine to xanthine and then to uric acid. Febout 40 mg is not expected to inhibit other enzymes involved in purine and pyrimidine synthesis and metabolism at therapeutic concentrations.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Febout 40 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Febout 40 mg is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients under 18 years of age have not been established.</p>', 'active', '582_1742323639.webp', 10, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 23:47:19', '2025-03-18 23:47:19'),
(3382, 'Farobest', 'Faropenem', 88.00, 7, 'tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1577_1742323764.jpg', 43, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 23:49:24', '2025-03-18 23:49:24'),
(3383, 'Cetrolix', 'Cetrorelix Acetate', 2500.00, 1, 'x 250 mcg vial', '250 mcg vial', 'Injection', '250 mcg per vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the provided information with headings converted to normal bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Cetrolix 250 mcg/vial</strong></p><p>Cetrorelix is indicated for assisted reproductive technologies.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs affecting (inhibiting) gonadotrophin.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cetrorelix, a synthetic decapeptide analog of naturally occurring gonadorelin, is a gonadorelin antagonist. It competitively blocks gonadorelin receptors on the anterior pituitary gonadotroph and the subsequent transduction pathway, inducing a rapid, reversible suppression of gonadotrophin secretion.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cetrolix 250 mcg/vial</strong></p><p>Adult: 250 mcg/day, given either in the morning beginning on day 5 or 6 of ovarian stimulation or in the evening beginning on day 5, and continued until ovulation induction.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity. Moderate to severe renal or hepatic impairment. Women with severe allergic conditions. Pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Cetrolix 250 mcg/vial</strong></p><p>Mild and transient reactions at injection site, nausea, headache, ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome, systemic hypersensitivity reactions.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Category X. Studies in animals or human beings have demonstrated fetal abnormalities or there is evidence of fetal risk based on human experience or both, and the risk of the use of the drug in pregnant women clearly outweighs any possible benefit. The drug is contraindicated in women who are or may become pregnant.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Women with active allergic conditions or a history of allergies; children; elderly &gt;65 years.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store Cetrolix 250 mcg/vial at room temperature, between 15 to 30°C.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Drugs affecting (inhibiting) gonadotrophin.</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Cetrorelix, a synthetic decapeptide analog of naturally occurring gonadorelin, is a gonadorelin antagonist. It competitively blocks gonadorelin receptors on the anterior pituitary gonadotroph and the subsequent transduction pathway, inducing a rapid, reversible suppression of gonadotrophin secretion.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Category X. Studies in animals or human beings have demonstrated fetal abnormalities or there is evidence of fetal risk based on human experience or both, and the risk of the use of the drug in pregnant women clearly outweighs any possible benefit. The drug is contraindicated in women who are or may become pregnant.</p>', 'active', '1267_1742323981.webp', 53, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 23:53:01', '2025-05-03 01:20:28');
