(3384, 'Merotrax', 'Meropenem Trihydrate', 700.00, 1, 'x 500 mg vial', '500 mg vial', 'IV Infusion', '500 mg/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Merotrax 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Meropenem is indicated for the treatment of the following infections caused by single or multiple bacteria sensitive to Meropenem in adults and children:</p><ul><li>Pneumonia and Nosocomial Pneumonia</li><li>Urinary Tract Infections</li><li>Intra-abdominal Infections</li><li>Gynaecological Infections, such as endometritis and pelvic inflammatory disease</li><li>Skin and Skin Structure Infections</li><li>Meningitis</li><li>Septicaemia</li><li>Pulmonary infections in cystic fibrosis</li><li>Empiric treatment for presumed infections in patients with febrile neutropenia</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Other Beta-lactam Antibiotics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Meropenem is a carbapenem antibiotic for parenteral use. It exerts its bactericidal action by interfering with bacterial cell wall synthesis. It penetrates bacterial cell walls, is highly stable to all serine beta-lactamases, and has a marked affinity for Penicillin Binding Proteins (PBPs). It demonstrates potent bactericidal activity against a broad spectrum of Gram-positive and Gram-negative, aerobic and anaerobic bacteria.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Merotrax 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p><strong>Adults</strong></p><ul><li>Usual dose: 500 mg to 1 gm by intravenous administration every 8 hours.</li><li>Pneumonia, urinary tract infections, gynaecological infections (endometritis, pelvic inflammatory disease), skin and skin structure infections: 500 mg IV every 8 hours.</li><li>Nosocomial pneumonias, peritonitis, presumed infections in neutropenic patients, and septicaemia: 1 gm IV every 8 hours.</li><li>Intra-abdominal infections: 500 mg to 1 gm every 8 hours.</li><li>Cystic fibrosis: Up to 2 gm every 8 hours.</li><li>Meningitis: 2 gm IV every 8 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Children (3 months to 12 years)</strong></p><ul><li>10 to 40 mg/kg intravenously every 8 hours, depending on infection type, pathogen susceptibility, and patient condition.</li><li>Intra-abdominal infections: 20 mg/kg every 8 hours.</li><li>Cystic fibrosis (4-18 years): 25-40 mg/kg every 8 hours.</li><li>Meningitis: 40 mg/kg IV every 8 hours.</li><li>Febrile neutropenia: 20 mg/kg every 8 hours.</li><li>Children over 50 kg: use adult dosage.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Merotrax 500 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li>Intravenous Infusion: Administer over approximately 15-30 minutes.</li><li>Intravenous Bolus: Administer 5 to 20 ml over approximately 3-5 minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Merotrax 500 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Probenecid</strong>: Competes with Meropenem for active tubular secretion, inhibiting renal excretion and increasing the elimination half-life and plasma concentration of Meropenem.</li><li><strong>Valproic Acid</strong>: Meropenem may reduce serum valproic acid levels, potentially reaching sub-therapeutic levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Meropenem is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Merotrax 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Meropenem is generally well-tolerated, but side effects may include:</p><ul><li>Inflammation, thrombophlebitis, and pain at the site of injection</li><li>Skin reactions such as rash, pruritus, and urticaria</li><li>Abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea</li><li>Headache</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Use during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Meropenem may be excreted in human milk. Caution is advised when administering to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>If an allergic reaction occurs, discontinue Meropenem and take appropriate measures.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with hepatic disease, monitoring transaminase and bilirubin levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Merotrax 500 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose could occur, especially in patients with renal impairment.</li><li>Treatment of overdose should be symptomatic.</li><li>In patients with renal impairment, hemodialysis will remove Meropenem and its metabolites.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Dosage should be reduced in patients with creatinine clearance less than 51 ml/min.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustments are necessary.</li><li><strong>Hemodialysis</strong>: Administer Meropenem after dialysis.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustments are necessary unless creatinine clearance is &lt;51 ml/min.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Efficacy and tolerability in infants under 3 months have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Intravenous Bolus Administration</strong>: Reconstitute Meropenem (500 mg or 1 g) with sterile water for injection. Shake to dissolve and obtain a clear or pale yellow solution.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Infusion Administration</strong>: Meropenem for intravenous infusion may be directly constituted with a compatible infusion fluid and further diluted (50-200 ml) as needed. Compatible infusion fluids include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0.9% sodium chloride intravenous infusion</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5% or 10% glucose intravenous infusion</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5% glucose with 0.02% sodium bicarbonate</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5% glucose with 0.9% sodium chloride</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5% glucose with 0.225% sodium chloride</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5% glucose with 0.15% potassium chloride</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2.5% and 10% mannitol intravenous infusion</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Normosol-M in 5% glucose intravenous infusion</li><li><strong>Potency</strong>: The freshly reconstituted solution is recommended for use, maintaining potency for up to 3 hours at up to 25°C or 13 hours at up to 5°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Other Beta-lactam Antibiotics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Meropenem is a carbapenem antibiotic that acts bactericidally by interfering with bacterial cell wall synthesis. It has a high level of stability to all serine beta-lactamases and a strong affinity for Penicillin Binding Proteins (PBPs), demonstrating potent activity against a broad spectrum of Gram-positive and Gram-negative, aerobic, and anaerobic bacteria.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Category B. Use during pregnancy only if clearly needed, as there are no adequate studies. Caution is advised during lactation due to possible excretion in milk.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Adjust dosage in patients with creatinine clearance &lt;51 ml/min.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustments needed.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustments unless creatinine clearance &lt;51 ml/min.</li><li><strong>Use in Children</strong>: Safety and efficacy in infants under 3 months have not been established.</li></ul>', 'active', '672_1742324173.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 23:56:13', '2025-05-04 20:31:40'),
(3385, 'Tapinor', 'Tapinarof', 1500.00, 1, 'x 30 gm tube', '30 gm tube', 'Cream', '1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '220_1742324356.jpg', 27, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-18 23:59:16', '2025-05-04 22:16:35'),
