(3387, 'Helax', 'Aloe + Black Pepper + Henbane', 3.20, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '130.43 mg plus 86.96 mg plus18.12mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '614_1742324919.jpg', 66, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 00:08:39', '2025-03-19 00:08:39'),
(3388, 'Colasa', 'Mesalazine [5-aminosalicylic acid]', 60.00, 5, 'Suppository', '5 Suppository', 'Suppository', '1 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a more organized and highlighted summary with the requested normal and bold text formatting:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Colasa 1 gm</strong></p><p>Mesalazine is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Treatment of mild to moderately active Ulcerative Colitis</strong></li><li><strong>Crohn’s Disease</strong></li><li><strong>Maintenance of remission of Ulcerative Colitis</strong></li><li><strong>Maintenance of remission of Crohn’s disease</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Anti-Tubercular Chemotherapeutics</li><li>Ulcerative Colitis</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Although the mechanism of action of mesalazine is not fully understood, it appears to be topical rather than systemic. Mucosal production of arachidonic acid metabolites, both through the cyclooxygenase pathways (i.e., prostanoids), and through the lipoxygenase pathways (i.e., leukotrienes and hydroxyeicosatetraenoic acids), is increased in patients with chronic inflammatory bowel disease. Mesalazine may reduce inflammation by blocking cyclooxygenase and inhibiting prostaglandin production in the colon.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Colasa 1 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Ulcerative Colitis</strong></li><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Active disease: Individual dosage, up to 4 gm mesalazine once daily or divided into 2-4 doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance treatment: Individual dosage, recommended dosage of 2 gm mesalazine once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Safety and efficacy in children below 6 years of age have not been established.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Limited documentation for children (6-18 years).</li><li><strong>Children 6 years and older</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Active disease: Start with 30-50 mg/kg/day in divided doses. Maximum dose: 75 mg/kg/day in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance treatment: Start with 15-30 mg/kg/day in divided doses. The total dose should not exceed 2 gm/day (recommended adult dose).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Colasa 1 gm</strong></p><ul><li>The granules must not be chewed.</li><li>The contents of the sachet should be emptied onto the tongue and washed down with some water or orange juice. Alternatively, it can be mixed with yogurt and consumed immediately.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Colasa 1 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Concurrent use with other nephrotoxic agents such as NSAIDs and Azathioprine may increase the risk of renal reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to salicylates or any other component of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Colasa 1 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Headache</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Nausea</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dizziness</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Asthenia</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dyspepsia</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Vomiting</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pruritus</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Either animal-reproduction studies have not demonstrated a fetal risk, but there are no controlled studies in pregnant women, or animal-reproduction studies have shown an adverse effect (other than a decrease in fertility) that was not confirmed in controlled studies in women in the 1st trimester. No evidence of risk in later trimesters.</li><li><strong>Excreted in breast milk</strong>: Caution is advised when administered to a nursing mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be exercised in:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pyloric stenosis</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: Mesalazine may cause nephropathy or interstitial nephritis. Renal function should be monitored before and during treatment.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Hepatic failure</strong>: Caution in patients with pre-existing liver disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Colasa 1 gm</strong></p><ul><li>There is no specific antidote for mesalazine overdose. Treatment should be symptomatic and supportive. This includes preventing further gastrointestinal absorption, correcting fluid-electrolyte imbalances, and maintaining renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>.</li><li>Protect from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Aminosalicylates</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Mesalamine’s mechanism is largely topical. It may reduce inflammation by blocking cyclooxygenase and inhibiting prostaglandin production in the colon.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Mesalamine should be used in pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. Caution is advised for nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric &amp; Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Caution with monitoring blood cell counts and renal function due to possible decreased renal function in older patients.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: Safety and effectiveness have not been established for children under 6 years of age.</li></ul><p>This format should make it easier to reference key information. Let me know if you\'d like to adjust or add anything!</p>', 'active', '352_1742325244.jpg', 8, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 00:14:04', '2025-05-03 01:32:38'),
(3389, 'Viltica-U', 'Vilanterol Trifenatate + Fluticasone Furoate + Umeclidinium Bromide', 90.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mcg plus 100 mcg plus 62.5 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Viltica-U 25 mcg + 100 mcg + 62.5 mcg</strong></h3><p>This combination medication is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Maintenance treatment of patients with Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)</strong></li><li><strong>Maintenance treatment of asthma in patients aged 18 years and older</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Limitation of use</strong>:</p><ul><li>Not indicated for relief of acute bronchospasm.</li></ul><h3><strong>Composition</strong></h3><p>Each dry powder inhaler capsule contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Vilanterol</strong> (as Vilanterol Trifenatate INN) - 25 mcg</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Furoate</strong> (INN) - 100 mcg</li><li><strong>Umeclidinium</strong> (as Umeclidinium Bromide INN) - 62.5 mcg</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage of Viltica-U 25 mcg + 100 mcg + 62.5 mcg</strong></h3><ul><li>This inhalation capsule <strong>must not be swallowed</strong> and should only be used with the provided device.</li><li><strong>Capsule removal</strong>: Remove the capsule from the blister pack immediately before use.</li><li>After inhalation, rinse your mouth with water <strong>without swallowing</strong> to reduce the risk of <strong>oropharyngeal candidiasis</strong>.</li></ul><h4><strong>Adults (18 years or older)</strong>:</h4><ul><li>Use once daily, at the same time every day, <strong>not more than once every 24 hours</strong>.</li><li>If shortness of breath or other asthma symptoms occur between doses, use an <strong>inhaled Short-Acting Beta-Agonist (SABA)</strong> for immediate relief.</li></ul><h4><strong>COPD</strong>:</h4><ul><li>1 inhalation capsule once daily.</li></ul><h4><strong>Asthma</strong>:</h4><ul><li>1 inhalation capsule once daily.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction of Viltica-U 25 mcg + 100 mcg + 62.5 mcg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Cytochrome P450 3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., ketoconazole): Use with caution. May increase systemic corticosteroid and cardiovascular effects.</li><li><strong>Monoamine oxidase inhibitors and tricyclic antidepressants</strong>: Use with extreme caution. May potentiate the effect of vilanterol on the vascular system.</li><li><strong>Beta-blockers</strong>: Use with caution as they may block the bronchodilatory effects of beta-agonists, leading to severe bronchospasm.</li><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: Use with caution. Electrocardiographic changes and/or hypokalemia associated with non–potassium-sparing diuretics may worsen with concomitant beta-agonists.</li><li><strong>Anticholinergics</strong>: May interact additively with concomitant anticholinergic medications. Avoid use with other anticholinergic drugs.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Primary treatment of status asthmaticus</strong> or <strong>acute episodes of COPD</strong> or asthma requiring intensive measures.</li><li><strong>Severe hypersensitivity to milk proteins</strong> or any of the ingredients.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Viltica-U 25 mcg + 100 mcg + 62.5 mcg</strong></h3><h4><strong>COPD</strong> (Common Adverse Reactions - ≥1% incidence):</h4><ul><li>Upper respiratory tract infection, pneumonia, bronchitis, oral candidiasis, headache, back pain, arthralgia, influenza, sinusitis, pharyngitis, rhinitis, dysgeusia, constipation, urinary tract infection, diarrhea, gastroenteritis, oropharyngeal pain, cough, and dysphonia.</li></ul><h4><strong>Asthma</strong> (Common Adverse Reactions - ≥2% incidence):</h4><ul><li>Pharyngitis/nasopharyngitis, upper respiratory tract infection/viral upper respiratory tract infection, bronchitis, respiratory tract infection/viral respiratory tract infection, sinusitis/acute sinusitis, urinary tract infection, rhinitis, influenza, headache, and back pain.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>LABA monotherapy</strong> increases the risk of serious asthma-related events.</li><li>Do <strong>not initiate</strong> in acutely deteriorating COPD or asthma.</li><li>Do <strong>not use</strong> for treating acute symptoms.</li><li><strong>Candida albicans</strong> infection of the mouth and pharynx may occur. Monitor patients periodically.</li><li><strong>Increased risk of pneumonia</strong> in patients with COPD.</li><li><strong>Paradoxical bronchospasm</strong> may occur, discontinue if this occurs.</li><li>Use with caution in <strong>patients with cardiovascular disorders</strong>, <strong>urinary retention</strong>, <strong>prostatic hyperplasia</strong>, <strong>convulsive disorders</strong>, <strong>thyrotoxicosis</strong>, <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong>, and <strong>ketoacidosis</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Viltica-U 25 mcg + 100 mcg + 62.5 mcg</strong></h3><ul><li>There are <strong>no data</strong> available from clinical trials on overdose with this inhalation capsule.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Avoid direct sunlight or heat.</li><li>Store in a cool, dry place.</li><li>Keep away from eyes.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><ul><li>Combined <strong>bronchodilators</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>This inhalation powder combines:</p><ol><li><strong>Fluticasone Furoate</strong> (ICS): Acts on inflammation in the lungs.</li><li><strong>Umeclidinium</strong> (Anticholinergic): Inhibits the M3 muscarinic receptor, leading to bronchodilation.</li><li><strong>Vilanterol</strong> (LABA): Stimulates the <strong>beta-2 adrenergic receptors</strong> leading to relaxation of bronchial smooth muscle and inhibition of mediator release from mast cells.</li></ol><h3><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h3><ul><li>Insufficient data on the use of this medication during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Not indicated for use</strong> in children and adolescents (under 18 years).</li><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients (aged 17 years and younger) have <strong>not been established</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Elderly Population</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>No dosage adjustment</strong> necessary in geriatric patients, but greater sensitivity may be observed.</li></ul><h3><strong>Renal &amp; Hepatic Impairment</strong></h3><ul><li>It has <strong>not been studied</strong> in patients with renal or hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1298_1742325436.png', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 00:17:16', '2025-03-19 00:17:16'),
(3390, 'Renofix', 'Roxadustat', 350.00, 3, 'tablet', '3 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1952_1742325591.jpg', 67, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 00:19:51', '2025-03-19 00:19:51'),
(3391, 'Dermacom', 'Clobetasol Propionate + Ofloxacin + Ornidazole + Terbinafine', 150.00, 1, 'x 15 gm tube', '15 gm tube', 'Cream', '0.05%+0.75%+2%+1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Dermacom 15 gm</strong></p><p>This cream is indicated in the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Mixed and complicated fungal, protozoal &amp; bacterial infections</li><li>Mild to moderate vaginitis</li><li>Candidiasis and trichomoniasis</li><li>Pruritus vulvae</li><li>Dermatitis</li><li>Eczema</li><li>Genito-urinary infections</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each gram of cream contains:</p><ul><li>Clobetasol Propionate USP 0.50 mg</li><li>Ofloxacin USP 7.50 mg</li><li>Ornidazole INN 20 mg</li><li>Terbinafine Hydrochloride BP 10 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This cream is a combination product exhibiting anti-bacterial, anti-fungal, anti-protozoal, and anti-inflammatory actions:</p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol</strong> is a potent corticosteroid with anti-inflammatory, anti-pruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties.</li><li><strong>Ofloxacin</strong> is a broad-spectrum antibiotic that acts against many gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria.</li><li><strong>Ornidazole</strong> belongs to the nitroimidazole group of antibiotics, used to treat amoeba and trichomonas infections.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine</strong> is an Allylamine antifungal that inhibits ergosterol biosynthesis (an essential component of fungal cell membranes), leading to fungal cell death.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Dermacom 15 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Apply by gently rubbing onto the affected area twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Dermacom 15 gm</strong></p><ul><li>No hazardous interactions have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients hypersensitive to any of the components of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Dermacom 15 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Burning, itching, irritation, dry skin</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The safe use of this preparation during pregnancy and lactation has not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>For external use only.</li><li>Do not swallow.</li><li>Keep away from light and out of reach of children.</li><li>Do not freeze.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C. Keep away from light and out of the reach of children. Do not freeze.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This cream exhibits anti-bacterial, anti-fungal, anti-protozoal, and anti-inflammatory actions. Ofloxacin is a broad-spectrum antibiotic effective against many gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria. Ornidazole treats amoeba and trichomonas infections, while Terbinafine is an antifungal that disrupts ergosterol biosynthesis in fungal cell membranes. Clobetasol is a potent corticosteroid with anti-inflammatory, anti-pruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>The safe use of this preparation during pregnancy and lactation has not been established.</li></ul>', 'active', '3_1742325826.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 00:23:46', '2025-04-15 12:08:14'),
(3392, 'Olmevas', 'Olmesartan Medoxomil', 18.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Olmevas 40 mg</strong></p><p> Olmevas 40 mg is indicated for the treatment of hypertension. It may be used alone or in combination with other antihypertensive agents.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Angiotensin-II receptor blocker</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Olmesartan medoxomil is a potent, orally active, selective angiotensin II receptor (type AT1) antagonist. It blocks all actions of angiotensin II mediated by the AT1 receptor, regardless of the source or route of synthesis of angiotensin II. Selective antagonism of the AT1 receptors increases plasma renin levels and angiotensin I and II concentrations, with a decrease in plasma aldosterone concentrations. Angiotensin II is the primary vasoactive hormone of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) and plays a significant role in the pathophysiology of hypertension via the AT1 receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Olmevas 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult:</strong> Dosage must be individualized. The usual initial dose is 10 mg once daily. In patients whose blood pressure is not adequately controlled at this dose, the dose may be increased to 20 mg once daily. For further blood pressure reduction after 2 weeks of therapy, the dose may be increased to 40 mg. Doses above 40 mg do not appear to have a greater effect.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> No overall differences in effectiveness or safety were observed between elderly patients (65 years and over) and younger patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Olmevas 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>The usual recommended starting dose of Olmesartan is 20 mg once daily for monotherapy in patients who are not volume-contracted. If further reduction in blood pressure is needed after 2 weeks, the dose may be increased to 40 mg. Doses above 40 mg do not provide additional benefit.</li><li><strong>Twice-daily dosing</strong> does not offer an advantage over the same total dose given once daily.</li><li>No initial dosage adjustment is recommended for elderly patients or those with moderate renal or hepatic dysfunction.</li><li><strong>For patients with volume depletion (e.g., diuretics use),</strong> Olmesartan should be initiated under close medical supervision, and a lower starting dose may be considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Olmevas 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With medicine:</strong> No significant drug interactions have been reported when Olmesartan is co-administered with other medications.</li><li><strong>With food &amp; others:</strong> Food does not affect the bioavailability of Olmesartan.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Olmesartan is contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to any component of this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Olmevas 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Back pain, bronchitis, increased creatine phosphokinase, diarrhea, headache, hematuria, hyperglycemia, hypertriglyceridemia, influenza-like symptoms, pharyngitis, rhinitis, sinusitis.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Chest pain, peripheral edema, arthritis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category C (first trimester) and D (second and third trimesters). Olmesartan can cause injury or death to the developing fetus if used during the second and third trimesters.</li><li><strong>Nursing Mothers:</strong> It is not known whether Olmesartan is excreted in human milk, but it is secreted at low concentrations in lactating rats. A decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, considering the importance of the drug to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Olmesartan may cause changes in renal function, particularly in susceptible individuals. In patients with severe heart failure, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors and angiotensin receptor antagonists have been associated with oliguria, progressive azotemia, acute renal failure, and rarely death. Similar results may occur with Olmesartan.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Olmevas 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms:</strong> The most likely effects of overdose are hypotension and tachycardia. Bradycardia may occur if parasympathetic (vagal) stimulation happens.</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> If intake is recent, gastric lavage or emesis induction may be considered. Clinically significant hypotension requires cardiovascular system support, including monitoring heart and lung function, elevating extremities, and ensuring adequate circulating fluid volume and urine output.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place below 30ºC, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Dose should not exceed 20 mg daily in moderate impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Max dose of 20 mg daily if eGFR is between 20-60 mL/min/1.73 m². Avoid if eGFR is less than 20 mL/min/1.73 m².</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Angiotensin-II receptor blocker</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Olmesartan blocks the effects of angiotensin-II by selectively binding to the AT1 receptor, which is present in many tissues (e.g., vascular smooth muscle, adrenal gland). Olmesartan does not inhibit ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme), which is involved in converting angiotensin-I to angiotensin-II.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation (cont’d)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Discontinue if pregnancy is detected. Drugs acting on the renin-angiotensin system can cause injury or death to the fetus in the second and third trimesters.</li><li><strong>Nursing Mothers:</strong> Not known if Olmesartan is excreted in human milk, but it is secreted in low concentrations in rats. Consider discontinuing breastfeeding or the drug depending on its importance to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><p> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p>', 'active', '1159_1742325981.jpg', 17, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 00:26:21', '2025-03-19 00:26:21'),
(3393, 'Bygerd', 'Vonoprazan', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>descriptio</p>', 'active', '1375_1742326101.webp', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 00:28:21', '2025-03-19 00:28:21'),
(3394, 'Ticaflow', 'Ticagrelor', 35.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '60 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the revised text with normal headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Ticaflow 60 mg</strong></p><p> Ticaflow 60 mg is indicated for the prevention of atherothrombotic events in adult patients with Acute Coronary Syndromes (unstable angina, non ST elevation Myocardial Infarction [NSTEMI] or ST elevation Myocardial Infarction [STEMI]); including patients managed medically, and those who are managed with percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) or coronary artery by-pass grafting (CABG).</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-platelet drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Ticaflow 60 mg is a selective adenosine diphosphate (ADP) receptor antagonist acting on the P2Y12 ADP-receptor that can prevent ADP-mediated platelet activation and aggregation. Ticaflow 60 mg reversibly interacts with the platelet P2Y12 ADP-receptor. Ticaflow 60 mg does not interact with the ADP binding site itself, but interacts with platelet P2Y12 ADP-receptor to prevent signal transduction. Thus, it prevents platelet activation &amp; aggregation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ticaflow 60 mg</strong></p><p> Ticaflow 60 mg treatment should be initiated with a single 180 mg loading dose (two tablets of 90 mg) and then continued at 90 mg twice daily. Patients taking Ticaflow 60 mg should also take aspirin daily, unless specifically contraindicated. Following an initial dose of aspirin (usually 325 mg), Ticaflow 60 mg should be used with a maintenance dose of aspirin of 75-100 mg. Maintenance dose of aspirin above 100 mg decreased the efficacy of Ticaflow 60 mg. So, maintenance dose of aspirin above 100 mg should be avoided. A patient who misses a dose of Ticaflow 60 mg should take only one 90 mg tablet (the next dose) at its scheduled time. Patients treated with Clopidogrel can be directly switched to Ticaflow 60 mg if needed. Switching from prasugrel to Ticaflow 60 mg has not been investigated. Treatment is recommended for up to 12 months unless discontinuation of Ticaflow 60 mg is clinically indicated. Ticaflow 60 mg can be administered with or without food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Ticaflow 60 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CYP3A inhibitors</strong>: Avoid use of strong inhibitors of CYP3A (e.g., ketoconazole, itraconazole, voriconazole, clarithromycin, nefazodone, ritonavir, saquinavir, nelfinavir, indinavir, atazanavir, and telithromycin).</li><li><strong>CYP3A inducers</strong>: Avoid use with potent inducers of CYP3A (e.g., rifampin, dexamethasone, phenytoin, carbamazepine, and phenobarbital).</li><li><strong>Aspirin</strong>: Use of Ticaflow 60 mg with aspirin maintenance doses above 100 mg reduced the effectiveness of Ticaflow 60 mg.</li><li><strong>Simvastatin, Lovastatin</strong>: Ticaflow 60 mg will result in higher serum concentrations of simvastatin and lovastatin because these drugs are metabolized by CYP3A4. Avoid simvastatin and lovastatin doses greater than 40 mg.</li><li><strong>Digoxin</strong>: Because of inhibition of the P-glycoprotein transporter, monitor digoxin levels with initiation of or any change in Ticaflow 60 mg therapy.</li><li><strong>Other Concomitant Therapy</strong>: Ticaflow 60 mg can be administered with unfractionated or low-molecular-weight heparin, GPIIb/IIa inhibitors, proton pump inhibitors, beta-blockers, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, and angiotensin receptor blockers.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Ticaflow 60 mg is contraindicated in case of:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Ticaflow 60 mg or to any of the excipients</li><li>Active pathological bleeding (peptic ulcer)</li><li>History of intracranial hemorrhage</li><li>Moderate to severe hepatic impairment</li><li>Co-administration of Ticaflow 60 mg with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole, clarithromycin, nefazodone, ritonavir, and atazanavir)</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ticaflow 60 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Dyspnea, bleeding, headache, cough, dizziness, nausea, atrial fibrillation, hypertension, non-cardiac chest pain, diarrhea, back pain, hypotension, fatigue, chest pain.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy category C</strong>. There are no or limited amounts of data from the use of Ticaflow 60 mg in pregnant women. Ticaflow 60 mg is not recommended during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Nursing mothers</strong>: Available pharmacodynamic/toxicological data in animals have shown excretion of Ticaflow 60 mg and its active metabolites in milk. A risk to newborns/infants cannot be excluded. A decision must be made whether to discontinue breastfeeding or to discontinue/abstain from Ticaflow 60 mg therapy taking into account the benefit of breastfeeding for the child and the benefit of therapy for the woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General Risk of Bleeding</strong>: Drugs that inhibit platelet function including Ticaflow 60 mg increase the risk of bleeding.</li><li><strong>Concomitant Aspirin Maintenance Dose</strong>: Use of Ticaflow 60 mg with maintenance doses of aspirin above 100 mg decreased the effectiveness of Ticaflow 60 mg. Therefore, after the initial loading dose of aspirin (usually 325 mg), use Ticaflow 60 mg with a maintenance dose of aspirin of 75-100 mg.</li><li><strong>Moderate Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Ticaflow 60 mg has not been studied in patients with moderate hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Discontinuation of Ticaflow 60 mg</strong>: Discontinuation of Ticaflow 60 mg will increase the risk of myocardial infarction, stent thrombosis, and death.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ticaflow 60 mg</strong></p><p> There is currently no known antidote to reverse the effects of Ticaflow 60 mg and it is not expected to be dialysable. Treatment of overdose should follow local standard medical practice. The expected effect of excessive Ticaflow 60 mg dosing is prolonged duration of bleeding risk associated with platelet inhibition. If bleeding occurs, appropriate supportive measures should be taken.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Protect from light &amp; moisture. Store below 25°C. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: The safety and effectiveness of Ticaflow 60 mg in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No overall differences in safety or effectiveness were observed in geriatric patients.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Ticaflow 60 mg has not been studied in patients with moderate or severe hepatic impairment. Ticaflow 60 mg is metabolized by the liver and impaired hepatic function can increase risks for bleeding and other adverse events.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is needed in patients with renal impairment. Patients receiving dialysis have not been studied.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Anti-platelet drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Ticaflow 60 mg is a selective adenosine diphosphate (ADP) receptor antagonist acting on the P2Y12 ADP-receptor that can prevent ADP-mediated platelet activation and aggregation. Ticaflow 60 mg reversibly interacts with the platelet P2Y12 ADP-receptor. Ticaflow 60 mg does not interact with the ADP binding site itself, but interacts with platelet P2Y12 ADP-receptor to prevent signal transduction. Thus, it prevents platelet activation &amp; aggregation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy category C. There are no or limited amounts of data from the use of Ticaflow 60 mg in pregnant women. Ticaflow 60 mg is not recommended during pregnancy.</p><ul><li><strong>Nursing mothers</strong>: Available pharmacodynamic/toxicological data in animals have shown excretion of Ticaflow 60 mg and its active metabolites in milk. A risk to newborns/infants cannot be excluded. A decision must be made whether to discontinue breastfeeding or to discontinue/abstain from Ticaflow 60 mg therapy taking into account the benefit of breastfeeding for the child and the benefit of therapy for the women.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> The safety and effectiveness of Ticaflow 60 mg in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p> <strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No overall differences in safety or effectiveness were observed in geriatric patients.</p><p> <strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Ticaflow 60 mg has not been studied in the patients with moderate or severe hepatic impairment. Ticaflow 60 mg is metabolized by the liver and impaired hepatic function can increase risks for bleeding and other adverse events. Hence,</p><p> <strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is needed in patients with renal impairment. Patients receiving dialysis have not been studied.</p>', 'active', '1170_1742326290.webp', 5, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 00:31:30', '2025-03-19 00:31:30'),
(3395, 'Nasovap', 'Menthol + Eucalyptus Oil', 130.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Suspension', '2 percent plus 10 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nasovap 100 ml</strong></p><p>This is indicated for relief of the symptoms of coughs, colds, and blocked noses.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Nasovap 100 ml</strong></p><p>Inhaled via the mouth and nasal passages.</p><p>Adults, children over 3 months of age, and the elderly: Add one 5 ml spoonful to a pint of hot, but not boiling water. The dose may be repeated after 4 hours if required.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Not suitable for children under 3 months. Contra-indicated in patients with hypersensitivity to menthol, eucalyptus, or any of the other ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Nasovap 100 ml</strong></p><p>Menthol may give rise to hypersensitivity reactions, including contact dermatitis. There have been reports of apnoea and instant collapse in infants following the local application of menthol to their nostrils.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Not suitable for children under 3 months. For external use only. Keep all medicines away from children. This medicine contains 1.25mg benzalkonium chloride in each 5ml dose, which is equivalent to 0.025% w/v. Benzalkonium chloride may cause wheezing and breathing difficulties (bronchospasm), especially if you have asthma.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Nasovap 100 ml</strong></p><p>Symptoms of poisoning with swallowed menthol and eucalyptus inhalation include epigastric burning, severe abdominal pain, central nervous system depression, pulmonary damage, nausea and vomiting, drowsiness, dizziness and muscular weakness, ataxia, miosis, coma, and a feeling of suffocation. Cyanosis, delirium, and convulsions may occur. Deaths have been recorded from doses as low as 3.5ml of eucalyptus oil (equivalent to 35ml of the inhalation). The fatal dose of menthol in humans has been estimated to be about 2g (equivalent to 100ml of the inhalation). Treatment should consist of emptying the stomach by gastric lavage and aspiration. Administer a saline laxative by mouth, such as sodium sulphate, 30g in 250ml of water, or a dilute solution of sodium phosphate. Convulsions may be controlled by the use of parenteral anticonvulsant agents. The latter treatment would require hospitalization.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Herbal and Nutraceuticals</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Use of this product in the above conditions is not considered likely to cause any harmful effects.</p>', 'active', '876_1742326651.webp', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 00:37:31', '2025-05-04 00:24:21'),
(3396, 'Hazmi', 'Hazmina', 190.00, 1, 'x 450 ml bottle', '450 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '450 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1580_1742326835.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 00:40:35', '2025-05-03 18:48:05'),
(3397, 'Bel', 'Belgiri [Bael fruit + Connessi bark]', 60.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '(1 gm+0.5 gm)/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '4801_1746029578.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 00:43:18', '2025-04-30 20:12:58'),
(3398, 'Bel', 'Belgiri [Bael fruit + Connessi bark]', 100.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '(1 gm+0.5 gm)/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1855_1742327120.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 00:45:20', '2025-04-30 20:16:30'),
(3399, 'Bolardi', 'Saccharomyces Boulardii', 10.00, 6, 'capsule', '6 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '250 mg 5 billion CFU', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1623_1742327271.webp', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 00:47:51', '2025-05-03 00:59:49'),
(3400, 'Flamfix', 'Nabumetone', 15.00, 10, 'Tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><br></h3>', 'active', '4363_1746244945.png', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 00:50:53', '2025-05-03 08:02:25'),
(3401, 'Ecozol', 'Econazole Nitrate', 32.12, 1, 'x 10 gm tube', '10 gm tube', 'Cream', '1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ecozol 10 gm</strong></p><p>Ecozol 10 gm is indicated for topical application in the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Tinea pedis</li><li>Tinea cruris</li><li>Tinea corporis caused by the following fungi:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Trichophyton rubrum</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Trichophyton mentagrophytes</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Trichophyton tonsurans</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Microsporum canis</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Microsporum audouini</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Microsporum gypseum</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em></li><li>Cutaneous candidiasis</li><li>Tinea versicolor</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs used in Vaginal and Vulval conditions</li><li>Topical Antifungal preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Econazole modifies the permeability of the fungal cell wall membrane, interfering with RNA and protein synthesis as well as lipid metabolism.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ecozol 10 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Econazole Cream</strong>: Apply to the affected skin areas once or twice daily (morning and/or evening) and gently rub in with clean fingers. For mycoses of the hands, apply after each hand wash.</li><li><strong>For onychomycosis</strong>: Occlusive dressings are recommended.</li><li><strong>Econazole Vaginal Tablet</strong>: One tablet should be inserted deep into the vagina at bedtime for three consecutive nights.</li><li><strong>Econazole 30 gm Cream</strong>: Insert the supplied applicator deep into the vagina once or twice daily at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Econazole 10 gm Cream</strong>: Apply sparingly to the affected area once or twice daily until improvement occurs. Treatment duration may vary:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea pedis, tinea cruris, tinea corporis, and tinea versicolor</strong>: Apply once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cutaneous candidiasis</strong>: Apply twice daily (morning and evening).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Tinea Pedis</strong>: Continue treatment for one month to prevent recurrence.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea Cruris &amp; Tinea Corporis</strong>: Treat for two weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea Versicolor</strong>: Clinical and mycological improvement may be seen within two weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>: Concomitant administration of econazole with warfarin can enhance anticoagulation. Monitor INR/prothrombin time, especially when applied under occlusion or on large areas.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Econazole is contraindicated in individuals with hypersensitivity to any of its ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common side effects include burning, itching, stinging, and erythema. A pruritic rash has also been reported in rare cases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: No known adverse effects have been reported in neonates with the use of Econazole cream during pregnancy. It is classified as Pregnancy Category C, and should be used during the first trimester only if considered essential by the physician.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Since systemic absorption is low, excretion into breast milk is negligible, and it is considered safe for use during lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Not for ophthalmic use.</li><li>Discontinue use if sensitivity or chemical irritation occurs.</li><li>For external use only. Avoid contact with the eyes.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>Overdosage has not been reported to date.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Do not use after the expiry date.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Ecozol 10 gm interferes with fungal oxidative enzymes, leading to lethal accumulation of H2O2 and reduction in ergosterol formation, which is crucial for the fungal cell wall.</p><p>This summary highlights the primary indications, dosage, and safety considerations for Ecozol 10 gm. If you have more specific questions, feel free to ask!</p>', 'active', '134_1742327642.webp', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 00:54:02', '2025-04-23 08:16:51');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3402, 'Ecozol', 'Econazole Nitrate', 70.48, 1, 'x 30 gm tube', '30 gm tube', 'Cream', '1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information for <strong>Ecozol 30 gm</strong> with the relevant details highlighted in bold as requested:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Ecozol 30 gm</strong></p><p>Ecozol 30 gm is indicated for topical application in the treatment of:</p><ul><li><strong>Tinea pedis</strong>, <strong>tinea cruris</strong>, and <strong>tinea corporis</strong> caused by <em>Trichophyton rubrum</em>, <em>Trichophyton mentagrophytes</em>, <em>Trichophyton tonsurans</em>, <em>Microsporum canis</em>, <em>Microsporum audouini</em>, <em>Microsporum gypseum</em>, and <em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em></li><li><strong>Cutaneous candidiasis</strong></li><li><strong>Tinea versicolor</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Drugs used in Vaginal and Vulval conditions</strong></li><li><strong>Topical Antifungal preparations</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Econazole modifies the permeability of the cell wall membrane in fungi; it may interfere with RNA and protein synthesis, and lipid metabolism.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ecozol 30 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Econazole Cream</strong> is applied to the affected skin areas once or twice daily (morning and/or evening) and gently rubbed in with a clean finger.</li><li>For <strong>mycoses of the hands</strong>, application is recommended after each hand wash.</li><li>For <strong>onychomycosis</strong>, occlusive dressings are recommended.</li><li><strong>Econazole-G Cream</strong> is applied with the applicator vaginally once or twice daily.</li><li><strong>Econazole VT</strong> is applied with the applicator vaginally, one tablet once or twice daily.</li><li>Treatment should continue for <strong>2 weeks after clinical cure</strong> to prevent relapse.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Ecozol 30 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Econazole vaginal tablet</strong>: One tablet inserted deep into the vagina at bedtime for three consecutive nights.</li><li><strong>Econazole 30 gm cream</strong>: Applied sparingly to the affected area once or twice daily until improvement occurs. For <strong>tinea pedis</strong>, <strong>tinea cruris</strong>, <strong>tinea corporis</strong>, and <strong>tinea versicolor</strong>, sufficient cream should be applied once daily, and for <strong>cutaneous candidiasis</strong>, it should be applied twice daily.</li><li><strong>Tinea pedis</strong> should be treated for <strong>one month</strong>, while <strong>tinea cruris</strong> and <strong>tinea corporis</strong> should be treated for <strong>two weeks</strong> to prevent recurrence. <strong>Tinea versicolor</strong> usually clears in <strong>two weeks</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ecozol 30 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>: Concomitant administration of econazole and warfarin may enhance anticoagulation effects. Monitoring of <strong>International Normalized Ratio (INR)</strong> and/or <strong>prothrombin time</strong> may be necessary, especially for patients applying econazole to large areas or under occlusion.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ecozol 30 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Approximately 3% of patients reported side effects, including <strong>burning</strong>, <strong>itching</strong>, <strong>stinging</strong>, and <strong>erythema</strong>.</li><li>A <strong>pruritic rash</strong> has also been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>During pregnancy</strong>: No known abnormalities in neonates. It should be used during the <strong>first trimester</strong> only when essential.</li><li><strong>During lactation</strong>: As systemic absorption is extremely low, it is unlikely to be excreted in breast milk. It can be used topically/vaginally during lactation if necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Not for ophthalmic use</strong>.</li><li>If <strong>sensitivity or chemical irritation</strong> occurs, discontinue use.</li><li><strong>For external use only</strong>. Avoid contact with eyes.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ecozol 30 gm</strong></p><ul><li>No reported overdose cases to date.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Do not use after the expiry date. Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Dispensed only with a prescription from a registered physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Drugs used in Vaginal and Vulval conditions</strong></li><li><strong>Topical Antifungal preparations</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Ecozol interferes with fungal <strong>oxidative enzymes</strong>, causing lethal accumulation of <strong>H2O2</strong>.</li><li>It reduces the formation of <strong>ergosterol</strong>, an important constituent of the fungal cell wall.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category C</strong>: Should be used during the <strong>first trimester</strong> only when essential.</li><li>Not known whether excreted in human milk; caution is advised during lactation.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you\'d like any additional information!</p>', 'active', '1970_1742327845.webp', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 00:57:25', '2025-04-23 08:16:25'),
(3403, 'Itrazen SB', 'Itraconazole', 20.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '65 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Itrazen SB 65 mg</strong></p><p>Itrazen SB 65 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Tinea corporis</li><li>Tinea cruris</li><li>Tinea pedis</li><li>Tinea manus</li><li>Vulvovaginal candidiasis</li><li>Fungal keratitis</li><li>Pityriasis versicolor</li><li>Oropharyngeal candidiasis</li><li>Onychomycosis</li><li>Histoplasmosis</li><li>Systemic Infections: Aspergillosis, Candidiasis, and Cryptococcus (when other drugs are effective)</li><li><strong>Antifungal prophylaxis</strong>: To prevent systemic fungal infections in severely neutropenic patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Itrazen SB 65 mg is an orally active triazole antifungal drug that demonstrates a broad spectrum of activity. It inhibits cytochrome P-450 dependent enzymes, impairing the biosynthesis of ergosterol, a key component of the cell membrane of yeast and fungal cells. This disruption leads to abnormal cell wall formation, inhibition of growth, and accumulation of intracellular lipids and vesicles.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Itrazen SB 65 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tinea corporis, Tinea cruris</strong>: 100 mg daily for 2 weeks.</li><li><strong>Tinea pedis, Tinea manus</strong>: 100 mg daily for 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Vulvovaginal candidiasis</strong>: 200 mg twice daily for 1 day or 200 mg daily for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Fungal keratitis</strong>: 200 mg daily for 3 weeks.</li><li><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong>: 200 mg daily for 1 week.</li><li><strong>Oropharyngeal candidiasis</strong>: 100 mg daily (200 mg for AIDS patients) for 15 days.</li><li><strong>Onychomycosis</strong>: 200 mg daily for 3 months; or 200 mg twice daily for 7 days (repeat courses as needed).</li><li><strong>Histoplasmosis</strong>: 200 mg 1-2 times daily.</li><li><strong>Systemic Infections</strong>: 200 mg once daily, increased in invasive or cryptococcal meningitis to 200 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Antifungal prophylaxis</strong>: 100 mg daily for severely neutropenic patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Itrazen SB 65 mg</strong></p><p>Should be taken with food, immediately after a full meal.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Itrazen SB 65 mg</strong></p><p>Itrazen SB 65 mg inhibits the metabolism of Cyclosporine and Warfarin. Interactions with Terfenadine, Phenobarbitone, and Midazolam have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Itrazen SB 65 mg</li><li>Pregnant women</li><li>Patients with severe hepatic disease</li><li>Patients undergoing Rifampicin therapy</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Itrazen SB 65 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Dyspepsia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Headache</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Raised liver enzymes</li><li>Hepatitis</li><li>Cholestatic jaundice</li><li>Peripheral neuropathy</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Itrazen SB 65 mg is contraindicated during pregnancy. Breastfeeding while receiving this medication is not recommended.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Liver function should be monitored in patients with preexisting hepatic abnormalities, and periodically in all patients receiving long-term treatment (more than one month).</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Itrazen SB 65 mg</strong></p><p>There is no known experience with overdosage.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light, and in a dry place. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Itrazen SB 65 mg is a triazole antifungal agent that inhibits cytochrome P-450 dependent enzymes, leading to impaired ergosterol biosynthesis in fungal cell membranes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Itrazen SB 65 mg is not recommended during pregnancy and lactation, unless otherwise advised by a physician.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>The efficacy and safety of Itrazen SB 65 mg have not been established in pediatric patients.</p>', 'active', '1405_1742327961.webp', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 00:59:21', '2025-04-18 22:11:49'),
(3404, 'Proston', 'Dinoprostone', 200.00, 1, 'x 3 gm tube', '3 gm tube', 'Gel', '0.5 mg/3 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '361_1742328317.webp', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:05:17', '2025-05-04 10:34:30'),
(3405, 'Linax Plus', 'Linagliptin + Metformin Hydrochloride', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+1000 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Linax Plus 5 mg+1000 mg</strong></p><p>Linax Plus 5 mg+1000 mg is indicated as an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycemic control in adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus when treatment with both Linagliptin and Metformin Hydrochloride is appropriate.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Combination Oral Hypoglycemic Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Linagliptin</strong>: An inhibitor of DPP-4 (dipeptidyl peptidase-4), an enzyme that degrades the incretin hormones GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1) and GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide). Linagliptin increases the concentrations of active incretin hormones, which stimulates insulin release from pancreatic beta cells in a glucose-dependent manner and decreases glucagon secretion from pancreatic alpha cells.</li><li><strong>Metformin Hydrochloride</strong>: A biguanide that reduces both basal and postprandial plasma glucose levels. It works by decreasing hepatic glucose production, reducing intestinal glucose absorption, and improving insulin sensitivity.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Linagliptin &amp; Metformin Immediate Release Tablets</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum dose: 2.5 mg Linagliptin and 1000 mg Metformin Hydrochloride twice daily with meals. Gradual dose escalation is recommended to reduce gastrointestinal (GI) side effects associated with Metformin.</li><li><strong>Starting Dose</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">For patients not treated with Metformin: 2.5 mg Linagliptin and 500 mg Metformin Hydrochloride twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For patients already on Metformin: 2.5 mg Linagliptin with the current dose of Metformin twice daily.</li><li><strong>Linagliptin &amp; Metformin Extended-Release Tablets</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum dose: 5 mg Linagliptin and 2000 mg Metformin Hydrochloride daily.</li><li><strong>Starting Dose</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">For patients not treated with Metformin: 5 mg Linagliptin/1000 mg Metformin Hydrochloride extended-release once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For patients already on Metformin: 5 mg Linagliptin with the same total daily dose of Metformin once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cationic drugs</strong> (amiloride, digoxin, morphine, ranitidine, trimethoprim, etc.) may reduce Metformin elimination.</li><li><strong>P-glycoprotein/CYP3A4 inducers</strong> (e.g., rifampin) may reduce the efficacy of this combination.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Renal impairment (due to Metformin accumulation risk)</li><li>Acute or chronic metabolic acidosis (including diabetic ketoacidosis)</li><li>Hypersensitivity to either Linagliptin or Metformin Hydrochloride</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Most common: Nasopharyngitis and diarrhea.</li><li>Hypoglycemia may occur, especially when combined with sulfonylureas.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Use during pregnancy only if clearly needed due to the lack of adequate studies.</li><li>Exercise caution when administered to a lactating mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Discontinue if <strong>lactic acidosis</strong> occurs.</li><li><strong>Acute pancreatitis</strong> has been reported; discontinue immediately if suspected.</li><li>Temporary discontinuation may be needed before radiologic procedures with contrast or surgeries.</li><li><strong>Metformin</strong> can lower Vitamin B12 levels; monitor annually.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Metformin</strong> overdose can result in lactic acidosis; hemodialysis is effective for Metformin removal.</li><li><strong>Linagliptin</strong> overdose did not show dose-related adverse effects in clinical trials.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Combination Oral Hypoglycemic Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Linagliptin</strong>: Increases incretin hormones to stimulate insulin release and decrease glucagon secretion.</li><li><strong>Metformin</strong>: Decreases hepatic glucose production and improves insulin sensitivity.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><ul><li>No adequate studies on its use in pregnant women. Use only if absolutely necessary. Caution is advised when administered to lactating mothers.</li></ul>', 'active', '323_1742328464.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:07:44', '2025-05-04 18:41:51'),
(3406, 'Beltas', 'Bilastine', 15.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Beltas 20 mg</strong></p><p>Beltas 20 mg is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of allergic rhino-conjunctivitis (seasonal and perennial) and urticaria.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Beltas 20 mg is a non-sedative H1 receptor selective antagonist. Its principal effects are mediated via selective inhibition of peripheral H1-receptors. It shows moderate to high affinity for histamine H1-receptors and no affinity for muscarinic, serotonergic, dopaminergic, and noradrenergic receptors.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Beltas 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children between 6 to 11 years</strong>: 10 mg mouth dissolving tablet for the symptomatic relief of allergic rhinitis, allergic rhinoconjunctivitis, and urticaria. The mouth dissolving tablet is for oral use only. It should be placed in the mouth, where it will disperse rapidly in saliva and can be easily swallowed. Alternatively, the tablet can be dispersed in a teaspoon of water before being swallowed by the child. The maximum recommended daily dose for children aged 6 to 11 years is 10 mg (1 tablet) and should not be exceeded. If a dose is missed, the next scheduled dose should be taken. An extra dose should not be taken.</li><li><strong>Adults &amp; adolescents (12 years and over)</strong>: 20 mg tablet once daily for symptomatic relief of allergic rhinitis, urticaria, and allergic rhinoconjunctivitis. The maximum recommended daily dose is 20 mg (1 tablet) and should not be exceeded. If a dose is missed, the next scheduled dose should be taken. An extra dose should not be taken. The 20 mg tablet (1 tablet) should be swallowed with water on an empty stomach to achieve optimal exposure.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Beltas 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant intake of Beltas 20 mg and Ketoconazole, Erythromycin, or Diltiazem increases Cmax of Beltas 20 mg.</li><li>The psychomotor performance after concomitant intake of alcohol and Beltas 20 mg was similar to that observed after intake of alcohol and placebo.</li><li>Concomitant intake of Beltas 20 mg and Lorazepam 3 mg for 8 days did not potentiate the depressant CNS effects of Lorazepam.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Beltas 20 mg is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to the active substance or to any of the excipients of the tablet.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Beltas 20 mg</strong></p><p> The most commonly reported side effects in clinical trials are headache, dizziness, somnolence, and fatigue. These adverse events occurred with a comparable frequency in patients receiving placebo.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There is no adequate or limited amount of data for the use of Beltas 20 mg in pregnant women and during breastfeeding and on the effects on fertility. In case of pregnancy or breastfeeding, Beltas 20 mg should be avoided unless advised otherwise by a physician.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Co-administration of Beltas 20 mg and P-glycoprotein inhibitors (e.g., Ketoconazole, Erythromycin, Cyclosporine, Ritonavir, or Diltiazem) should be avoided in patients with moderate or severe renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Beltas 20 mg</strong></p><p> In clinical trials, after administration of Beltas 20 mg at doses 10 to 11 times the therapeutic dose (220 mg as a single dose; or 200 mg/day for 7 days), the frequency of treatment-emergent adverse events was two times higher than with placebo. The adverse reactions most frequently reported were dizziness, headache, and nausea. No serious adverse events and no significant prolongation in the QTc interval were reported.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C temperature, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in patients with impaired hepatic &amp; renal function</strong>: There is no clinical experience in patients with hepatic impairment. Since Beltas 20 mg is not metabolized and renal clearance is its major elimination route, hepatic impairment is not expected to increase the systemic exposure above the safety margin. Therefore, no dosage adjustment is required in patients with hepatic impairment. No dosage adjustment is required in patients with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Geriatrics (&gt;65 years of age)</strong>: No dosage adjustments are necessary in patients over 65 years.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics (&lt;6 years of age)</strong>: The safety and efficacy of Beltas 20 mg in children under 6 years of age have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Beltas 20 mg is a non-sedating, long-acting histamine antagonist with selective peripheral H1 receptor antagonist affinity and no affinity for muscarinic receptors. Beltas 20 mg inhibits histamine-induced wheal and flare skin reactions for 24 hours following single doses.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> There are no or limited data from the use of Beltas 20 mg in pregnant women. Animal studies do not indicate direct or indirect harmful effects with respect to reproductive toxicity, parturition, or postnatal development. As a precautionary measure, it is preferable to avoid the use of Beltas 20 mg during pregnancy. The excretion of Beltas 20 mg in milk has not been studied in humans. A decision must be made taking into account the benefit of breastfeeding for the child and the benefit of Beltas 20 mg therapy for the mother.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Efficacy and safety of Beltas 20 mg in children under 2 years of age have not been established, and there is little clinical experience in children aged 2 to 5 years. Therefore, Beltas 20 mg should not be used in these age groups.</p>', 'active', '439_1742328762.jpg', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:12:42', '2025-03-19 01:12:42'),
(3407, 'Resight', 'Pilocarpine Hydrochloride', 175.00, 1, 'x 2.5 ml drop', '2.5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '1.25%', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the revised text with the headings in bold and formatted for clarity:</p><p><strong>Indications of Resight 1.25% 2.5 ml</strong></p><p>Pilocarpine is indicated for chronic open-angle glaucoma, acute or chronic narrow-angle glaucoma, suspected glaucoma, and ocular hypertension.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs affecting exocrine secretions.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Resight 1.25% 2.5 ml directly stimulates cholinergic receptors. It produces contraction of the iris sphincter muscle, resulting in pupillary constriction (miosis); constriction of the ciliary muscle, resulting in increased accommodation; and reduction in intraocular pressure associated with an increase in the outflow and a decrease in the inflow of aqueous humor. In chronic open-angle glaucoma, the exact mechanism by which miotics lower intraocular pressure is not exactly known; however, contraction of the ciliary muscle apparently opens the intertrabecular spaces and facilitates aqueous humor outflow. There is also a decrease in the rate of inflow of aqueous humor. In angle-closure glaucoma, constriction of the pupil apparently pulls the iris away from the trabeculum, thereby relieving blockage of the trabecular meshwork.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Resight 1.25% 2.5 ml</strong></p><p>Instill 1 drop 2-4 times daily. To minimize systemic absorption of the active ingredient of the eye drops, pressure should be applied for one minute on the tear duct after application.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Resight 1.25% 2.5 ml</strong></p><p>Belladonna alkaloids or cyclopentolate used ophthalmically may interfere with the miotic effects of pilocarpine and may have their own mydriatic effects dampened. This latter may be used to therapeutic advantage.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient including excipients. Conditions where pupillary constriction is undesirable (e.g., acute iritis). Retinal detachment; past history of retinal detachment or conditions that predispose to retinal detachment.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Resight 1.25% 2.5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Temporary blurred vision, burning, stinging, redness, watering of the eyes, decrease in night vision, eye irritation, headache.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Eye pain, increased sweating, muscle tremors, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, watering of the mouth, troubled breathing or wheezing.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Safe use during pregnancy has not been established. Problems in human pregnancy have not been documented. However, ophthalmic Pilocarpine is systemically absorbed.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>If blurred vision or changes in near or far sight occur, especially at night, patients should exercise caution when involved in night driving or hazardous work in poor light. It is recommended that intraocular pressure measurements be performed regularly during therapy.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Resight 1.25% 2.5 ml</strong></p><p>Dilution with water and other fluids is the usual response to accidental or deliberate overdose.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at room temperature and protect from light. It is desirable that the contents should not be used more than 4 weeks after first opening of the bottle.</p>', 'active', '1433_1742329073.webp', 53, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:17:53', '2025-05-06 08:14:42'),
(3408, 'Xorel MUPS', 'Rabeprazole Sodium', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xorel MUPS 20 mg (Rabeprazole)</strong></p><p> Gastro-resistant tablets of rabeprazole are recommended for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Active duodenal ulcer</li><li>Active benign stomach ulcer</li><li>Symptomatic gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) that is erosive or ulcerative</li><li>GERD maintenance (long-term management)</li><li>Moderate to severe symptomatic GERD</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Helicobacter pylori eradication in patients with peptic ulcer disease, in combination with suitable antibacterial therapy</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Rabeprazole works by inhibiting the gastric H+/K+-ATPase enzyme located at the secretory surface of the stomach parietal cells, which reduces gastric acid secretion. It functions as an inhibitor of the gastric proton pump.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xorel MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer:</strong> 20 mg in the morning for 4 weeks; most patients heal within this period.</li><li><strong>Erosive or ulcerative GERD:</strong> 20 mg daily for 4 to 8 weeks; if not healed after 8 weeks, an additional 8-week course may be considered.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 20 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks; if symptoms persist, consider an additional course.</li><li><strong>GERD maintenance (long-term management):</strong> 20 mg once daily (studies do not extend beyond 12 months).</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> Starting dose of 60 mg once daily, titrated based on individual needs; up to 120 mg daily in some cases.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication (duodenal ulcer):</strong> 20 mg rabeprazole, 1000 mg amoxicillin, and 500 mg clarithromycin twice daily with meals for 7 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Xorel MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Active Duodenal Ulcer and Active Benign Gastric Ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily in the morning.</li><li><strong>Erosive or Ulcerative GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>GERD Maintenance:</strong> 10 mg or 20 mg once daily depending on response.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 10 mg once daily for 4 weeks; can continue with an on-demand regimen as symptoms resolve.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric GERD (1 to 11 years):</strong>Less than 15 kg: 5 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">15 kg or more: 10 mg once daily for 12 weeks.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> Starting dose of 60 mg once daily, adjustable up to 120 mg/day based on patient needs.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication:</strong> 20 mg rabeprazole, 500 mg clarithromycin, and 1g amoxicillin twice daily for 7 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Xorel MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take once daily, preferably in the morning before meals.</li><li>Do not chew or crush the tablets, swallow them whole.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Xorel MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>May reduce absorption of pH-dependent substances like itraconazole or ketoconazole.</li><li>Avoid co-administration with atazanavir (a drug that requires acidic pH for absorption).</li><li>No significant interaction with liquid antacids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</li><li>Not advised during pregnancy or breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xorel MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Generally well tolerated; may cause headache, nausea, vomiting, constipation, dry mouth, muscle pain, dizziness, or sleepiness.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>FDA pregnancy category \"C.\"</li><li>No clear evidence of harm to the fetus in animal studies, but no controlled studies in humans.</li><li>Likely excreted in breast milk; consider discontinuing breastfeeding or the medication based on the importance to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Ruling out malignancy before initiating therapy is advised, as symptomatic response does not rule out gastric or esophageal cancer.</li><li>Long-term use, especially for more than a year, requires regular monitoring.</li><li>Long-term PPIs may slightly increase fracture risk, particularly in elderly patients or those with other risk factors.</li><li>Ensure adequate calcium and vitamin D intake in individuals at risk for osteoporosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms typically mild and reversible without medical intervention.</li><li>No specific antidote; treatment should be symptomatic.</li><li>Rabeprazole is highly protein-bound and not dialyzable.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in children have not been established.</li><li>No dose adjustments required for hepatic or renal impairment.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '465_1742329197.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:19:57', '2025-03-19 01:19:57'),
(3409, 'Semaglo', 'Semaglutide', 455.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '3 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Semaglo 3 mg:</strong></p><p>Semaglo 3 mg is a glucagon-like peptide 1 (GLP-1) receptor agonist indicated for:</p><ol><li><strong>Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">As an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycemic control in adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">To reduce the risk of major adverse cardiovascular events in adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus and established cardiovascular disease.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Chronic Weight Management:</strong></li></ol><ul><li>As an adjunct to a reduced-calorie diet and increased physical activity for chronic weight management in adult patients with:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">An initial body mass index (BMI) of 30 kg/m² or greater (obesity), or</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">BMI of 27 kg/m² or greater (overweight) with at least one weight-related comorbid condition (e.g., hypertension, type 2 diabetes mellitus, or dyslipidemia).</li></ul><p><strong>Composition:</strong></p><ul><li>0.25 mg injection: Each pre-filled syringe contains 0.25 mg of Semaglo 3 mg in 0.5 ml solution for injection.</li><li>0.5 mg injection: Each pre-filled syringe contains 0.50 mg of Semaglo 3 mg in 0.5 ml solution for injection.</li><li>1 mg injection: Each pre-filled syringe contains 1 mg of Semaglo 3 mg in 0.5 ml solution for injection.</li><li>1.7 mg injection: Each pre-filled syringe contains 1.7 mg of Semaglo 3 mg in 0.75 ml solution for injection.</li><li>2.4 mg injection: Each pre-filled syringe contains 2.4 mg of Semaglo 3 mg in 0.75 ml solution for injection.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Semaglo 3 mg:</strong></p><p><strong>Subcutaneous Injection:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Starting Dose:</strong> 0.25 mg once weekly for 4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dose Escalation Schedule:</strong>Weeks 1-4: 0.25 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Weeks 5-8: 0.5 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Weeks 9-12: 1 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Weeks 13-16: 1.7 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance Dose (Week 17 and onwards):</strong> 2.4 mg</li></ul><p>If the patient does not tolerate a dose, the dose escalation may be delayed for up to 4 weeks, or the maintenance dose may be reduced to 1.7 mg once weekly for up to 4 weeks.</p><p>Semaglo 3 mg should be administered subcutaneously in the abdomen, thigh, or upper arm, once weekly at any time of the day, with or without meals.</p><p><strong>Missed Dose:</strong></p><ul><li>If the missed dose is more than 2 days (48 hours) away, administer it as soon as possible.</li><li>If the missed dose is less than 2 days away, skip it and resume on the next scheduled dose day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Semaglo 3 mg:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>With Insulin or Insulin Secretagogues (e.g., Sulfonylurea):</strong>May increase the risk of hypoglycemia. Consider reducing the dose of the insulin secretagogue or insulin.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Oral Medications:</strong>No significant effect on the absorption of orally administered medications, but caution should be exercised.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Personal or family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC).</li><li>Patients with Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia syndrome type 2 (MEN 2).</li><li>Hypersensitivity to Semaglo 3 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Potential for <strong>thyroid tumors</strong> or MTC (unconfirmed in humans).</li><li>Monitor for <strong>pancreatitis</strong> symptoms (severe abdominal pain).</li><li>Risk of <strong>diabetic retinopathy complications</strong>.</li><li>Risk of <strong>hypoglycemia</strong> when used with insulin or insulin secretagogues.</li><li>Post-marketing reports of <strong>acute kidney injury</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity reactions</strong> like anaphylaxis and angioedema.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Thyroid C-Cell Tumors:</strong> The risk of thyroid tumors is unknown.</li><li><strong>Pancreatitis:</strong> Monitor for symptoms; discontinue if suspected.</li><li><strong>Retinopathy:</strong> Monitor patients with a history of diabetic retinopathy.</li><li><strong>Pen Sharing:</strong> Never share the pen between patients due to the risk of blood-borne pathogens.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>In case of overdose, provide appropriate supportive treatment as per clinical symptoms.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in the refrigerator at 2°C to 8°C.</li><li>After the first use, the pen can be stored in the refrigerator for up to 56 days.</li><li>Do not freeze. Do not use if frozen.</li><li>Keep away from heat and light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>GLP-1 receptor agonists.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Semaglo 3 mg is a GLP-1 analogue that binds to GLP-1 receptors, promoting insulin secretion and inhibiting glucagon secretion in a glucose-dependent manner. It also slows gastric emptying, contributing to its blood glucose-lowering effects.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Pediatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Use only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Not established for children under 18 years.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> No significant differences in safety or efficacy between geriatric and younger patients, though caution is advised.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal &amp; Hepatic Impairment:</strong></p><ul><li>No dose adjustment is recommended for renal or hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '796_1742329320.jpg', 27, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:22:00', '2025-03-19 01:22:00'),
(3410, 'Liposem', 'Saroglitazar Magnesium', 70.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1757_1742329442.jpg', 67, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:24:02', '2025-03-19 01:24:02'),
(3411, 'Resva', 'Rosuvastatin Calcium', 20.00, 7, 'tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Resva 10 mg</strong></p><p> Rosuvastatin is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia (Familial and Non-familial)</li><li>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia (Familial)</li><li>Mixed Dyslipidemia (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Resva 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Dose range: 5-40 mg once daily.</li><li>Use 40 mg dose only for patients not reaching LDL-C goal with 20 mg.</li><li><strong>HoFH</strong>: Starting dose 20 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with HeFH</strong>: 5-10 mg/day for patients 8 to less than 10 years of age, and 5-20 mg/day for patients 10 to 17 years of age.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with HoFH</strong>: 20 mg/day for patients 7 to 17 years of age.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Resva 10 mg</strong></p><p> Rosuvastatin can be taken with or without food, at any time of day.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Resva 10 mg</strong></p><p> Remarkable drug interactions of Rosuvastatin include:</p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong>: Combination increases Rosuvastatin exposure. Rosuvastatin dose should be limited to 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil</strong>: Combination should be avoided. If used together, Rosuvastatin dose should be limited to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or atazanavir/ritonavir</strong>: Combination increases Rosuvastatin exposure. Rosuvastatin dose should be limited to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants</strong>: Combination prolongs international normalized ratio (INR). Stable INR should be achieved prior to starting Rosuvastatin. INR should be monitored frequently until stable upon initiation or alteration of Rosuvastatin therapy.</li><li><strong>Concomitant lipid-lowering therapies</strong>: Use with fibrates and niacin products may increase the risk of skeletal muscle effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Rosuvastatin is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to product components</li><li>Liver disease, which may include unexplained persistent elevations in hepatic transaminase levels</li><li>Pregnant women and women who may become pregnant</li><li>Nursing mothers</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Resva 10 mg</strong></p><p> Rosuvastatin is generally well tolerated. The most frequent adverse events thought to be related to Rosuvastatin include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Skeletal muscle effects</strong> (e.g., myopathy and rhabdomyolysis): Risks increase with use of 40 mg dose, advanced age (&gt;65 years), hypothyroidism, renal impairment, and combination use with cyclosporine, lopinavir/ritonavir, atazanavir/ritonavir, or certain other lipid-lowering drugs. Patients should be advised to promptly report unexplained muscle pain, tenderness, or weakness. Rosuvastatin can be discontinued if signs or symptoms appear.</li><li><strong>Liver enzyme abnormalities and monitoring</strong>: Persistent elevations in hepatic transaminases can occur. Liver enzymes should be monitored before and during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C temperature, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs</li><li>Statins</li></ul><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Rosuvastatin is a selective and competitive inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme that converts 3-hydroxy-3-methyl glutaryl coenzyme A to mevalonate, a precursor of cholesterol. Rosuvastatin produces its lipid-modifying effects in two ways:</p><ol><li>It increases the number of hepatic LDL receptors on the cell surface to enhance uptake and catabolism of LDL.</li><li>It inhibits hepatic synthesis of VLDL, which reduces the total number of VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The safety in pregnant women has not been established. It is not known whether Rosuvastatin is excreted in human milk or not.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> The safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p>', 'active', '398_1742329560.webp', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:26:00', '2025-03-19 01:26:00'),
(3412, 'Itogut', 'Itopride Hydrochloride', 6.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '262_1742329655.webp', 44, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:27:35', '2025-03-19 01:27:35'),
(3413, 'Cystosan', 'Pentosan Polysulfate Sodium', 40.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information about <strong>Cystosan 100 mg</strong> formatted as per your request:</p><p><strong>Indications of Cystosan 100 mg:</strong></p><p> Pentosan polysulfate sodium is indicated for the relief of bladder pain or discomfort associated with interstitial cystitis.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Cystosan 100 mg:</strong></p><p> The recommended dose of Cystosan 100 mg is <strong>300 mg/day</strong>, taken as one <strong>100 mg capsule orally three times daily</strong>. The capsules should be taken with water <strong>at least 1 hour before meals or 2 hours after meals</strong>. Patients receiving Cystosan 100 mg should be reassessed after 3 months. If improvement has not occurred and if limiting adverse events are not present, Cystosan 100 mg may be continued for another 3 months. The clinical value and risks of continued treatment in patients whose pain has not improved by 6 months is not known.</p><p> <strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients below the age of 16 years have not been established.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Cystosan 100 mg:</strong></p><p> In a study in which healthy subjects received Cystosan 100 mg (100 mg capsule or placebo) every 8 hours for 7 days, and were titrated with warfarin to an INR of 1.4 to 1.8, the pharmacokinetic parameters of R-warfarin and S-warfarin were similar in the absence and presence of Cystosan 100 mg. <strong>INR for warfarin + placebo</strong> and <strong>warfarin + Cystosan 100 mg</strong> were comparable.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p> Cystosan 100 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to the drug, structurally related compounds, or excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Cystosan 100 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Alopecia (4%)</li><li>Diarrhea (4%)</li><li>Nausea (4%)</li><li>Headache (3%)</li><li>Rash (3%)</li><li>Dyspepsia (2%)</li><li>Abdominal pain (2%)</li><li>Liver function abnormalities (1%)</li><li>Dizziness (1%)</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><p> Cystosan 100 mg is a weak anticoagulant (1/15 the activity of heparin). At a daily dose of 300 mg (n=128), rectal hemorrhage was reported as an adverse event in 6.3% of patients. Bleeding complications of ecchymosis, epistaxis, and gum hemorrhage have been reported. Patients undergoing invasive procedures or having signs/symptoms of underlying coagulopathy or other increased risk of bleeding (due to other therapies such as coumarin anticoagulants, heparin, t-PA, streptokinase, high dose aspirin, or nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) should be evaluated for hemorrhage. Patients with diseases such as aneurysms, thrombocytopenia, hemophilia, gastrointestinal ulcerations, polyps, or diverticula should be carefully evaluated before starting Cystosan 100 mg.</p><p> A similar product that was given subcutaneously, sublingually, or intramuscularly (and not initially metabolized by the liver) is associated with delayed immunoallergic thrombocytopenia with symptoms of thrombosis and hemorrhage. Caution should be exercised when using Cystosan 100 mg in patients who have a history of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia.</p><p> Alopecia is associated with pentosan polysulfate and with heparin products. In clinical trials of Cystosan 100 mg, alopecia began within the first 4 weeks of treatment. Ninety-seven percent (97%) of the cases of alopecia reported were alopecia areata, limited to a single area on the scalp.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><p> Keep below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><p> Other genito-urinary preparations.</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action:</strong></p><p> Pentosan polysulfate is a polymer of xylose hydrogen sulfate and contains two sulfate groups per carbohydrate monomer. It binds Fibroblast growth factors (FGFs) as well as other heparin-binding growth factors. It has been shown to interact also with the heparin-binding site of FGFR-1. It inhibits the growth of SW13 adrenocortical cells transfected with FGF-4 and tumorigenicity of MCF-7 breast carcinoma cells transfected with FGF-1 or FGF-4.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><p> Reproduction studies have been performed in mice and rats with intravenous daily doses of 15 mg/kg, and in rabbits with 7.5 mg/kg. These doses are <strong>0.42 and 0.14 times</strong> the daily oral human doses of Cystosan 100 mg when normalized to body surface area. These studies did not reveal evidence of impaired fertility or harm to the fetus from Cystosan 100 mg. Direct in vitro bathing of cultured mouse embryos with Cystosan 100 mg (PPS) at a concentration of <strong>1 mg/mL</strong> may cause reversible limb bud abnormalities. Adequate and well-controlled studies have not been performed in pregnant women. Because animal studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used in pregnancy only if clearly needed.</p><p> <strong>Nursing Mothers:</strong> It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk, caution should be exercised when Cystosan 100 mg is administered to a nursing woman.</p>', 'active', '167_1742329761.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:29:21', '2025-03-19 01:29:21'),
(3414, 'Hazmi', 'Hazmina', 80.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '100 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '734_1742329887.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:31:27', '2025-05-03 18:48:29'),
(3415, 'Hazmi', 'Hazmina', 115.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '200 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '615_1742329998.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:33:18', '2025-05-03 18:48:46');
