(3416, 'Ursoren', 'Ursodeoxycholic Acid', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '150 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Ursoren 150 mg</h3><p>Ursoren 150 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Cholestasis (Jaundice)</li><li>Viral Hepatitis</li><li>Alcoholic Fatty Liver</li><li>Primary Biliary Cirrhosis (PBC)</li><li>Primary Sclerosing Cholangitis (PSC)</li><li>Dissolution of Gallstones</li><li>Non-Alcoholic Steato Hepatitis (NASH)</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Anti-gallstones drugs: Bile Acids</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Ursoren 150 mg is a naturally occurring bile acid used to treat different hepatobiliary disorders. Its activity is achieved through a decrease in the secretion of cholesterol in bile. This is accomplished via several mechanisms:</p><ul><li>Reducing cholesterol absorption</li><li>Suppressing liver cholesterol synthesis</li><li>Not inhibiting bile acid synthesis, thereby altering bile composition from supersaturated to unsaturated.</li><li>Promoting the formation of liquid cholesterol crystal complexes, which help in the removal of cholesterol from the gallbladder into the intestine to be expelled.</li></ul><p>Ursoren 150 mg improves cholestatic liver diseases by:</p><ul><li>Protecting cholangiocytes against the cytotoxicity of hydrophobic bile acids</li><li>Stimulating hepatobiliary secretion</li><li>Protecting hepatocytes against bile acid-induced apoptosis</li></ul><p>Ursoren 150 mg is completely absorbed in the upper intestine, with peak serum concentration occurring within 30 to 150 minutes. The absorption rate ranges from 60-80%. After absorption, it enters the portal vein and undergoes extraction by the liver, where it is conjugated with either glycine or taurine and then secreted into the hepatic bile ducts. Only small amounts of Ursoren 150 mg appear in circulation, with very small quantities excreted in urine. Its biologic half-life ranges from 3.5-5.8 days.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ursoren 150 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Dissolution of Gallstones:</strong> 8-12 mg/kg/day, either as a single night-time dose or in divided doses</li><li><strong>PBC (Primary Biliary Cirrhosis):</strong> 10-15 mg/kg/day in 2-4 divided doses</li><li><strong>Acute Viral Hepatitis:</strong> 600 mg/day</li><li><strong>Alcoholic Fatty Liver:</strong> 300 mg/day</li><li><strong>PSC (Primary Sclerosing Cholangitis):</strong> 25-30 mg/kg/day</li><li><strong>NASH (Non-Alcoholic Steato Hepatitis):</strong> 13-15 mg/kg/day</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Ursoren 150 mg</h3><ul><li>Ursoren 150 mg should not be used with drugs that increase bile cholesterol, such as oestrogenic hormones.</li><li>Concomitant administration with bile-acid binding drugs, including antacids, charcoal, and cholestyramine, should be avoided, as they may reduce the effectiveness of Ursodeoxycholic acid therapy.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Non-functioning gall-bladder</li><li>Calcified and pigmented gallstones</li><li>Inflammatory bowel disease</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Ursoren 150 mg</h3><p>Commonly reported side effects include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Gallstone opacification</li><li>Pruritus</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> No evidence of harm has been reported in pregnancy. It has been effectively used for the treatment of cholestasis during the last trimester without side effects.</li><li>No documented problems regarding breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Use cautiously in individuals with liver disease.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 25°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '64_1742330119.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:35:19', '2025-05-13 05:42:30'),
(3417, 'Neosten', 'Clotrimazole', 45.00, 1, 'x 20 gm tube', '20 gm tube', 'Cream', '1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p>', 'in-active', '3581_1746419265.png', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:40:21', '2025-05-12 17:33:58'),
(3418, 'Ciprocin', 'Ciprofloxacin', 18.12, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '750 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Ciprocin 750 mg</h3><p>Ciprocin 750 mg is indicated for the treatment of both single and mixed infections caused by susceptible organisms. It is also effective against infections caused by organisms resistant to other antibiotics such as Aminoglycosides, Penicillins, and Cephalosporins.</p><p>Due to its ability to reach high antibacterial concentrations in serum, body tissues, and urine, Ciprocin 750 mg is used for treating a wide range of infections, including those of the urinary, respiratory, and gastrointestinal tracts, gonorrhea, and sepsis. Its tissue penetration and enhanced antibacterial activity, including against <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, make it suitable for severe or complicated infections. It can be used alone or in combination with other antibiotics (e.g., Aminoglycosides, Beta-lactams) depending on the severity of the infection.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class:</h3><ul><li>4-Quinolone preparations</li><li>Anti-diarrhoeal Antimicrobial drugs</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology:</h3><p>Ciprocin 750 mg is a synthetic fluoroquinolone that exhibits bactericidal activity against a wide range of gram-positive and gram-negative organisms. It works by inhibiting bacterial DNA synthesis through binding with DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV, enzymes responsible for DNA supercoiling.</p><h3>Dosage of Ciprocin 750 mg:</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 100-750 mg twice daily, based on infection type and severity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Urinary Tract Infections</strong>: 250-500 mg twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Respiratory Tract Infections</strong>: 250-500 mg twice daily; 750 mg for Streptococcus pneumonia.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gonorrhea</strong>: Single dose of 250-500 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cystic Fibrosis</strong>: 750 mg twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Reduce dose if serum creatinine &gt;265 µmol/L or creatinine clearance &lt;20 ml/min.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed despite higher serum levels.</li><li><strong>Children &amp; Adolescents</strong>: Not recommended for use in growing children, due to potential risk of joint damage. If necessary, dosage is 7.5-15 mg/kg/day, administered in two divided doses.</li><li><strong>Duration of Treatment</strong>: Typically 5-10 days, continuing for 3 days after symptoms resolve.</li><li><strong>Extended-release tablet</strong> (for uncomplicated urinary tract infections): 1000 mg once daily for 3 days.</li></ul><h3>Administration of Ciprocin 750 mg (IV infusion):</h3><ul><li>Ensure the IV solution is clear and free of leaks.</li><li>Administer using an IV set; regulate the rate of infusion.</li><li>Do not administer with series connections or if the solution is cloudy.</li></ul><h3>Drug Interactions:</h3><p>Avoid concurrent administration with:</p><ul><li><strong>Magnesium or Aluminum antacids</strong>, <strong>sucralfate</strong>, <strong>products containing Calcium, Iron, or Zinc</strong>.</li><li><strong>Milk or dairy products</strong>, as they significantly reduce absorption.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications:</h3><ul><li>History of hypersensitivity to Ciprocin 750 mg or other quinolones.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects:</h3><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Nausea, diarrhea, vomiting, dyspepsia.</li><li><strong>CNS</strong>: Headache, dizziness, confusion, seizures.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Skin rash, rare Stevens-Johnson syndrome, pruritus.</li><li><strong>Other</strong>: Joint pain, photosensitivity, liver enzyme increase, renal impairment, hypoglycemia.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Use only if the benefit justifies the risk.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Avoid due to potential risk of articular damage in infants.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</h3><ul><li>Caution in patients with CNS disorders, epilepsy, or those predisposed to seizures.</li><li><strong>Ciprocin 750 mg</strong> should not be taken concurrently with certain antacids, sucralfate, and dairy products.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects:</h3><ul><li>Symptoms include seizures, confusion, renal and hepatic impairment, crystalluria, and hematuria.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions:</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action:</h3><ul><li><strong>Ciprocin 750 mg</strong> is a synthetic quinolone with broad-spectrum antibacterial activity, particularly effective against <strong>gram-negative</strong> bacteria (e.g., <em>Enterobacteriaceae</em>, <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>) and <strong>gram-positive</strong> bacteria, including methicillin-resistant staphylococci.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Not a first-choice drug for pediatric populations due to potential adverse effects on joints.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '911_1742330548.webp', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:42:28', '2025-05-08 14:07:53'),
(3419, 'Xyclon', 'Clonazepam', 6.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xyclon 0.5 mg</strong></p><p>Xyclon 0.5 mg is used to treat panic disorder, including cases with or without agoraphobia. Panic disorder is characterized by unexpected panic attacks and the ongoing worry about future episodes, often with concerns about their implications.</p><p>It is also indicated for the treatment of Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome (specifically the petit mal variant), akinetic seizures, and myoclonic seizures. It may be considered for patients with absence seizures (petit mal) who have not responded to succinimides.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</li><li>Benzodiazepine hypnotics</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Xyclon 0.5 mg reduces nerve transmission in the motor cortex, suppressing spike and wave discharges linked to absence seizures. This action is likely connected to its enhancement of GABA activity. It has shown efficacy in both focal epilepsy and generalized seizures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xyclon 0.5 mg</strong></p><p><em>Oral Dosage for Adults with Seizure Disorders</em>:</p><ul><li>Start with 1.5 mg per day, divided into three doses.</li><li>Increase by 0.5–1 mg every three days, up to 20 mg per day.</li></ul><p><em>Oral Dosage for Adults with Panic Disorder</em>:</p><ul><li>Begin with 0.25 mg twice daily.</li><li>Increase to 1 mg per day after three days, as needed.</li></ul><p><em>Pediatric Patients</em>:</p><ul><li>Infants and children (up to 10 years or 30 kg): 0.01–0.03 mg/kg/day, not exceeding 0.05 mg/kg/day, split into multiple doses.</li></ul><p><em>Injection Dosage</em>:</p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.5 mg via slow IV injection or infusion.</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 1 mg via slow IV injection or infusion (repeat if needed, typically 1–4 mg).</li><li>IV rate: 0.25–0.5 mg per minute, max total dose 10 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xyclon 0.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>It does not appear to impact the pharmacokinetics of phenytoin, carbamazepine, or phenobarbital.</li><li>The effect on other drugs’ metabolism is not well understood.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>History of hypersensitivity to benzodiazepines or severe liver disease.</li><li>Should be avoided in acute narrow-angle glaucoma; however, it can be used for open-angle glaucoma under treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xyclon 0.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common CNS-related side effects: drowsiness (50%), ataxia (30%), behavioral problems (25%).</li><li>Other effects include abnormal eye movements, aphonia, coma, tremor, vertigo, confusion, depression, amnesia, hallucinations, psychosis, insomnia, increased libido, and palpitations.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Consider during pregnancy only if the benefits outweigh the risks to the fetus.</li><li>Avoid breastfeeding during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Can raise the occurrence of generalized tonic-clonic seizures in patients with multiple coexisting seizure disorders.</li><li>Combining with valproic acid may lead to absence status.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xyclon 0.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Drowsiness, ataxia, dysarthria, nystagmus.</li><li>Rarely life-threatening; however, it can lead to coma, apnea, hypotension, and cardiorespiratory depression.</li><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: Symptomatic care, activated charcoal (within 1–2 hours), or flumazenil (with caution).</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li>Dilute the vial contents with 1 ml of water for injection before administering IV.</li><li>For infusion, dilute at a ratio of 1 vial (1 mg) to at least 85 ml of diluting media.</li><li>Infusion stability: 24 hours at room temperature, with a maximum 8-hour infusion duration.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</li><li>Benzodiazepine hypnotics</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Enhances GABAergic neurotransmission, leading to anticonvulsive, sedative, and anxiolytic effects.</li><li>Suppresses paroxysmal activity such as spike and wave discharges in absence seizures.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>In infants and young children, may increase saliva and bronchial secretions; ensure clear airways.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Elderly may experience heightened sensitivity to benzodiazepine effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Impairment</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage adjustment required for kidney issues.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong></p><ul><li>Liver cirrhosis may reduce the clearance of unbound Xyclon 0.5 mg.</li></ul><p>This summary provides a detailed overview of Xyclon 0.5 mg\'s uses, dosage, interactions, side effects, and more. Always consult a healthcare provider for personalized guidance.</p>', 'in-active', '399_1742330670.jpg', 27, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:44:30', '2025-05-13 05:54:11'),
(3420, 'Flamfix', 'Nabumetone', 22.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '750 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Flamfix 750 mg</strong></p><p> Flamfix 750 mg is indicated for the relief of signs and symptoms of osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis. Carefully consider the potential benefits and risks of Flamfix 750 mg and other treatment options before deciding to use Flamfix 750 mg. Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration consistent with individual patient treatment goals.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for Osteoarthritis, Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Flamfix 750 mg is a naphthylalkanone. It is a non-selective prostaglandin G/H synthase (a.k.a. cyclooxygenase or COX) inhibitor that acts on both prostaglandin G/H synthase 1 and 2 (COX-1 and -2). Prostaglandin G/H synthase catalyzes the conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandin G2 and prostaglandin G2 to prostaglandin H2. Prostaglandin H2 is the precursor to a number of prostaglandins involved in fever, pain, swelling, inflammation, and platelet aggregation. The parent compound is a prodrug that undergoes hepatic biotransformation to the active compound, 6-methoxy-2-naphthylacetic acid (6MNA). The analgesic, antipyretic, and anti-inflammatory effects of NSAIDs occur as a result of decreased prostaglandin synthesis. The parent compound is a prodrug, which undergoes hepatic biotransformation to the active component, 6-methoxy-2-naphthylacetic acid (6MNA), which is a potent inhibitor of prostaglandin synthesis, most likely through binding to the COX-2 and COX-1 receptors.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Flamfix 750 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Osteoarthritis and Rheumatoid Arthritis</strong>: The recommended starting dose is 1,000 mg taken as a single dose with or without food. Some patients may obtain more symptomatic relief from 1,500 mg to 2,000 mg per day. Flamfix 750 mg can be given in either a single or twice-daily dose. Dosages greater than 2,000 mg per day have not been studied. The lowest effective dose should be used for chronic treatment. Patients weighing under 50 kg may be less likely to require dosages beyond 1,000 mg. Therefore, after observing the response to initial therapy, the dose should be adjusted to meet individual patient requirements.</li><li><strong>Moderate or Severe Renal Insufficiency</strong>: Caution should be taken in prescribing Flamfix 750 mg to patients with moderate or severe renal insufficiency. The maximum starting doses of Flamfix 750 mg in patients with moderate or severe renal insufficiency should not exceed 750 mg or 500 mg, respectively, once daily. After careful monitoring of renal function in patients with moderate or severe renal insufficiency, daily doses may be increased to a maximum of 1,500 mg and 1,000 mg, respectively.</li><li><strong>Use in Pediatric Patients</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Flamfix 750 mg</strong></p><p> Reports suggest that NSAIDs may diminish the antihypertensive effect of ACE inhibitors. This interaction should be considered in patients taking NSAIDs concomitantly with ACE inhibitors.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Flamfix 750 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Flamfix 750 mg or its excipients. Flamfix 750 mg should not be given to patients who have experienced asthma, urticaria, or allergic-type reactions after taking aspirin or other NSAIDs. Severe, rarely fatal, anaphylactic-like reactions to NSAIDs have been reported in such patients. Flamfix 750 mg is contraindicated for the treatment of peri-operative pain in the setting of coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Flamfix 750 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain, constipation, flatulence, nausea, positive stool guaiac, dry mouth, gastritis, stomatitis, vomiting.</li><li><strong>Central Nervous System</strong>: Dizziness, headache, fatigue, increased sweating, insomnia, nervousness, somnolence.</li><li><strong>Dermatologic</strong>: Pruritus, rash</li><li><strong>Special Senses</strong>: Tinnitus</li><li><strong>Miscellaneous</strong>: Edema</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>. Reproductive studies conducted in rats and rabbits have not demonstrated evidence of developmental abnormalities. However, animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response. There are no adequate, well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Flamfix 750 mg should be used in pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Nursing Mothers</strong>: It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk, but 6MNA is excreted in the milk of lactating rats. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk and because of the potential for serious adverse reactions in nursing infants from Flamfix 750 mg, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or to discontinue the drug, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</li><li><strong>Labor and Delivery</strong>: In rat studies with NSAIDs, as with other drugs known to inhibit prostaglandin synthesis, an increased incidence of dystocia, delayed parturition, and decreased pup survival occurred. The effects of Flamfix 750 mg on labor and delivery in pregnant women are unknown.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> As a class, NSAIDs have been associated with renal papillary necrosis and other abnormal renal pathology during long-term administration to animals. Renal toxicity is often seen in patients with conditions leading to a reduction in renal blood flow or blood volume, where renal prostaglandins have a supportive role in maintaining renal perfusion. The administration of an NSAID results in a dose-dependent decrease in prostaglandin synthesis, which may precipitate renal decompensation.</p><p> Patients at greatest risk of this reaction are those with impaired renal function, heart failure, liver dysfunction, those taking diuretics, and the elderly. Discontinuation of NSAID therapy is typically followed by recovery to the pretreatment state.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Flamfix 750 mg</strong></p><p> Symptoms following acute NSAIDs overdoses are usually limited to lethargy, drowsiness, nausea, vomiting, and epigastric pain, which is generally reversible with supportive care. Gastrointestinal bleeding can occur. Hypertension, acute renal failure, respiratory depression, and coma may occur but are rare. Anaphylactoid reactions have been reported with therapeutic ingestion of NSAIDs and may occur following an overdose.</p><p> Patients should be managed by symptomatic and supportive care following an NSAID overdose. There are no specific antidotes. Emesis and/or activated charcoal (60 to 100 grams in adults, 1 to 2 g/kg in children) and/or osmotic cathartic may be indicated in patients seen within 4 hours of ingestion with symptoms or following a large overdose (5 to 10 times the usual dose). Forced diuresis, alkalinization of urine, hemodialysis, or hemoperfusion may not be useful due to high protein binding. There have been overdoses of up to 25 grams of Flamfix 750 mg reported with no long-term sequelae following standard emergency treatment (i.e., activated charcoal, gastric lavage, IV H2-blockers, etc.).</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep all medicines out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: As with any NSAIDs, caution should be exercised in treating the elderly (65 years and older).</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Drugs for Osteoarthritis, Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Flamfix 750 mg is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) that exhibits anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic properties. Its mode of action is not fully known; however, the ability to inhibit prostaglandin synthesis may be involved in the anti-inflammatory effect. The parent compound is a prodrug, which undergoes hepatic biotransformation to the active component, 6-methoxy-2-naphthylacetic acid (6MNA), which is a potent inhibitor of prostaglandin synthesis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. There are no adequate, well-controlled studies in pregnant women. This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Because of the known effect of prostaglandin-synthesis-inhibiting drugs on the human fetal cardiovascular system (closure of ductus arteriosus), use of Flamfix 750 mg during the third trimester of pregnancy is not recommended.</p><p> <strong>Lactation</strong>: Flamfix 750 mg is not recommended for use in nursing mothers because of the possible adverse effects of prostaglandin-synthesis-inhibiting drugs on neonates.</p>', 'active', '573_1742330818.jpg', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:46:58', '2025-03-19 01:46:58'),
(3421, 'Resight 1.25%', 'Pilocarpine Hydrochloride', 175.00, 1, 'x 2.5 ml drop', '2.5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '2.5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Resight 1.25% 2.5 ml</strong></p><p>Pilocarpine is indicated for chronic open-angle glaucoma, acute or chronic narrow-angle glaucoma, suspected glaucoma, and ocular hypertension.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs affecting exocrine secretions</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Resight 1.25% 2.5 ml directly stimulates cholinergic receptors. It produces contraction of the iris sphincter muscle, resulting in pupillary constriction (miosis); constriction of the ciliary muscle, resulting in increased accommodation; and reduction in intraocular pressure associated with an increase in the outflow and a decrease in the inflow of aqueous humor. In chronic open-angle glaucoma, the exact mechanism by which miotics lower intraocular pressure is not exactly known; however, contraction of the ciliary muscle apparently opens the intertrabecular spaces and facilitates aqueous humor outflow. There is also a decrease in the rate of inflow of aqueous humor. In angle-closure glaucoma, constriction of the pupil apparently pulls the iris away from the trabeculum, thereby relieving blockage of the trabecular meshwork.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Resight 1.25% 2.5 ml</strong></p><p>Instill 1 drop 2-4 times daily. To minimize systemic absorption of the active ingredient of the eye drops, pressure should be applied for one minute on the tear duct after application.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Resight 1.25% 2.5 ml</strong></p><p>Belladonna alkaloids or cyclopentolate used ophthalmically may interfere with the miotic effects of pilocarpine and may have their own mydriatic effects dampened. This latter may be used to therapeutic advantage.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient including excipients.</li><li>Conditions where pupillary constriction is undesirable (e.g. acute iritis).</li><li>Retinal detachment; past history of retinal detachment or conditions that predispose to retinal detachment.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Resight 1.25% 2.5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Temporary blurred vision, burning, stinging, redness, watering of the eyes, decrease in night vision, eye irritation, headache.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Eye pain, increased sweating, muscle tremors, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, watering of the mouth, troubled breathing or wheezing.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Safe use during pregnancy has not been established. Problems in human pregnancy have not been documented. However, ophthalmic Pilocarpine is systemically absorbed.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>If blurred vision or changes in near or far sight occur, especially at night, patients should exercise caution when involved in night driving or hazardous work in poor light. It is recommended that intraocular pressure measurements be performed regularly during therapy.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Resight 1.25% 2.5 ml</strong></p><p>Dilution with water and other fluids is the usual response to accidental or deliberate overdose.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at room temperature and protect from light. It is desirable that the contents should not be used more than 4 weeks after first opening of the bottle.</p>', 'in-active', '1371_1742330989.webp', 67, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:49:49', '2025-05-12 18:07:19'),
(3422, 'Mirovan', 'Mirogabalin Besylate', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '502_1742331145.jpg', 49, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:52:25', '2025-03-19 01:52:25'),
(3423, 'Mirovan', 'Mirogabalin Besylate', 35.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '37_1742331262.jpg', 29, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:54:22', '2025-03-19 01:54:22'),
(3424, 'Tapina', 'Tapinarof', 1500.00, 1, 'x 30 gm tube', '30 gm tube', 'Cream', '1 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '631_1742331415.jpg', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 01:56:55', '2025-05-04 21:32:42'),
(3425, 'Tedibac', 'Tedizolid Phosphate', 500.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Tedibac 200 mg</strong></p><p>Tedizolid is an oxazolidinone-class antibacterial drug indicated in adults for the treatment of acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections (ABSSSI) caused by designated susceptible bacteria. To reduce the development of drug-resistant bacteria and maintain the effectiveness of Tedizolid and other antibacterial drugs, Tedizolid should be used only to treat or prevent infections that are proven or strongly suspected to be caused by susceptible bacteria.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong> Other antibiotic</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p> After conversion to its active form by phosphatases, tedizolid exerts its antibacterial activity through inhibition of protein synthesis by binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit of susceptible Gram-positive bacteria. Cross-resistance between other non-oxazolidinone antibacterial drugs is unlikely as it inhibits bacterial protein synthesis through a different mechanism.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Tedibac 200 mg:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute Bacterial Skin and Skin Structure Infection (ABSSSI):Intravenous:</strong> 200 mg once daily, infused over 1 hour for 6 days</li><li><strong>Oral:</strong> 200 mg once daily for 6 days</li><li> No dose adjustment is necessary when changing from intravenous to oral Tedizolid.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"> If a dose is missed, it should be taken as soon as possible, up to 8 hours before the next dose. If less than 8 hours remain before the next dose, wait until the next scheduled dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Tedibac 200 mg:</strong></p><p> Tedizolid (when administered orally) can increase the plasma concentrations of orally administered Breast Cancer Resistance Protein (BCRP) substrates. Monitor for adverse reactions related to concomitant BCRP substrates if coadministration cannot be avoided.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p> None</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Tedibac 200 mg:</strong></p><p> The most common adverse reactions (&gt;2%) are nausea, headache, diarrhea, vomiting, and dizziness.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies of Tedizolid in pregnant women. Tedizolid should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Nursing Mothers:</strong> Tedizolid is excreted in the breast milk of rats. It is not known whether tedizolid is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Tedizolid is administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Patients with neutropenia:</strong> The safety and efficacy of Tedizolid in patients with neutropenia (neutrophil counts &lt;1000 cells/mm³) have not been adequately evaluated. Consider alternative therapies in neutropenic patients.</li><li><strong>Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea:</strong> Evaluate if diarrhea occurs.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Tedibac 200 mg:</strong></p><p> In the event of overdosage, Tedizolid should be discontinued, and general supportive treatment should be provided. Hemodialysis does not result in meaningful removal of tedizolid from systemic circulation.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><p> Tablets and injection should be stored at 20°C to 25°C.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients below the age of 18 have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Clinical studies of Tedizolid did not include sufficient numbers of subjects aged 65 and over to determine whether they respond differently from younger subjects. No overall differences in pharmacokinetics were observed between elderly subjects and younger subjects.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong> Other antibiotic</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p> After conversion to its active form by phosphatases, tedizolid exerts its antibacterial activity through inhibition of protein synthesis by binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit of susceptible Gram-positive bacteria. Cross-resistance between other non-oxazolidinone antibacterial drugs is unlikely as it inhibits bacterial protein synthesis through a different mechanism.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><p> Category C. Tedizolid should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses:</strong></p><p> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients below the age of 18 have not been established.</p><p> <strong>Geriatric Use:</strong></p><p> No significant differences observed in pharmacokinetics between elderly and younger subjects.</p>', 'active', '1960_1742331689.jpg', 50, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:01:29', '2025-03-19 02:01:29'),
(3426, 'Azonox', 'Itraconazole', 28.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Azonox 200 mg</strong></p><p> Azonox 200 mg is indicated in the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Tinea corporis</li><li>Tinea cruris</li><li>Tinea pedis</li><li>Tinea manus</li><li>Vulvovaginal candidiasis</li><li>Fungal keratitis</li><li>Pityriasis versicolor</li><li>Oropharyngeal candidiasis</li><li>Onychomycosis</li><li>Histoplasmosis</li><li>Systemic infections (Aspergillosis, Candidiasis, Cryptococcus when other drugs are ineffective)</li><li>Antifungal prophylaxis: To prevent systemic fungal infections in severely neutropenic patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Azonox 200 mg is an orally active triazole antifungal drug with a broad spectrum of activity and favorable pharmacokinetics. It inhibits Cytochrome P-450 dependent enzymes, which results in the impairment of ergosterol biosynthesis, a crucial component of the fungal cell membrane. This inhibition causes abnormal cell growth, formation of the cell wall, and accumulation of intracellular lipids.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Azonox 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tinea corporis, Tinea cruris</strong>: 100 mg daily for 2 weeks</li><li><strong>Tinea pedis, Tinea manus</strong>: 100 mg daily for 4 weeks</li><li><strong>Vulvovaginal candidiasis</strong>: 200 mg twice daily for 1 day, or 200 mg daily for 3 days</li><li><strong>Fungal keratitis</strong>: 200 mg daily for 3 weeks</li><li><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong>: 200 mg daily for 1 week</li><li><strong>Oropharyngeal candidiasis</strong>: 100 mg daily (200 mg for AIDS patients) for 15 days</li><li><strong>Onychomycosis</strong>: 200 mg daily for 3 months, or 200 mg twice daily for 7 days (repeated courses after 21-day interval)</li><li><strong>Histoplasmosis</strong>: 200 mg 1-2 times daily</li><li><strong>Systemic Infections (Aspergillosis, Candidiasis, Cryptococcus)</strong>: 200 mg once daily (increased to 200 mg twice daily for invasive or cryptococcal meningitis)</li><li><strong>Antifungal prophylaxis</strong>: 100 mg daily for severely neutropenic patients, or as directed by a physician</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p> Azonox 200 mg should be taken with food, immediately after a full meal.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Azonox 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Inhibits the metabolism of Cyclosporine and Warfarin</li><li>Reported interactions with Terfenadine, Phenobarbitone, and Midazolam</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Azonox</li><li>Pregnant women</li><li>Severe hepatic disease</li><li>Rifampicin therapy</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Azonox 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Nausea, abdominal pain, dyspepsia, constipation</li><li>Headache, dizziness</li><li>Raised liver enzymes, hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice</li><li>Peripheral neuropathy</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated during pregnancy</li><li>Not recommended for breastfeeding mothers</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor hepatic enzyme values in patients with pre-existing liver issues</li><li>Regular monitoring is recommended for patients undergoing long-term treatment (more than one month) or those showing signs of liver dysfunction.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Azonox 200 mg</strong></p><p> There is no known experience with overdose of Azonox 200 mg.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C, away from light and in a dry place. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Azonox 200 mg inhibits cytochrome P-450 dependent enzymes, disrupting the biosynthesis of ergosterol, which is critical for the yeast and fungal cell membrane.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Not recommended during pregnancy or lactation, unless specifically prescribed by a physician.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> The efficacy and safety of Azonox 200 mg have not been established in pediatric patients.</p>', 'active', '1603_1742331839.webp', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:03:59', '2025-03-19 02:03:59'),
(3427, 'Bilfast', 'Bilastine', 90.00, 1, 'x 40 ml bottle', '40 ml bottle', 'Oral Solution', '40 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Bilfast 40 ml</strong></p><p>Bilfast 40 ml is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of allergic rhino-conjunctivitis (seasonal and perennial) and urticaria.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Bilfast 40 ml is a non-sedative H1 receptor selective antagonist. Its principal effects are mediated via selective inhibition of peripheral H1-receptors. It shows moderate to high affinity for histamine H1-receptors and no affinity for muscarinic, serotonergic, dopaminergic, and noradrenergic receptors.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Bilfast 40 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children between 6 to 11 years:</strong></li><li>10 mg mouth dissolving tablet for the symptomatic relief of allergic rhinitis, allergic rhinoconjunctivitis, and urticaria. The mouth dissolving tablet is for oral use only. It should be placed in the mouth, where it will disperse rapidly in saliva and can be easily swallowed. Alternatively, the mouth dissolving tablet can be dispersed in a teaspoon of water before being swallowed. The maximum recommended daily dose for children between 6 to 11 years is 10 mg (1 tablet). An extra dose should not be taken if a dose is missed.</li><li><strong>Adults &amp; Adolescents (12 years and over):</strong></li><li>20 mg tablet once daily for symptomatic relief of allergic rhinitis, urticaria, and allergic rhinoconjunctivitis. The maximum recommended daily dose is 20 mg (1 tablet). It should be swallowed with water on an empty stomach to achieve optimal exposure to Bilfast 40 ml.</li><li><strong>Children between 2 to 11 years:</strong></li><li>4 ml once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Bilfast 40 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant intake of Bilfast 40 ml with Ketoconazole, Erythromycin, or Diltiazem increased the C max of Bilfast 40 ml.</li><li>Psychomotor performance after concomitant intake of alcohol and Bilfast 40 ml was similar to that observed after intake of alcohol and placebo.</li><li>Concomitant intake of Bilfast 40 ml and Lorazepam 3 mg for 8 days did not potentiate the depressant CNS effects of Lorazepam.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Bilfast 40 ml is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to the active substance or any of the excipients of the tablet.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Bilfast 40 ml</strong></p><p>The most commonly reported side effects in clinical trials are headache, dizziness, somnolence, and fatigue. These adverse events occurred with a comparable frequency in patients receiving placebo.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>There are no adequate or limited data for the use of Bilfast 40 ml in pregnant women and during breastfeeding. Bilfast 40 ml should be avoided during pregnancy unless advised otherwise by a physician.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Co-administration of Bilfast 40 ml and P-glycoprotein inhibitors (e.g., Ketoconazole, Erythromycin, Cyclosporine, Ritonavir, or Diltiazem) should be avoided in patients with moderate or severe renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Bilfast 40 ml</strong></p><p>In clinical trials, administration of Bilfast 40 ml at doses 10 to 11 times the therapeutic dose (220 mg as a single dose or 200 mg/day for 7 days) showed a frequency of treatment-emergent adverse events that was two times higher than with placebo. The adverse reactions most frequently reported were dizziness, headache, and nausea. No serious adverse events or significant prolongation in the QTc interval were reported.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Impaired Hepatic &amp; Renal Function:</strong> No dosage adjustment is required for hepatic or renal impairment, as Bilfast 40 ml is not metabolized and renal clearance is its major elimination route.</li><li><strong>Geriatrics (&gt;65 years):</strong> No dosage adjustments are necessary for patients over 65 years.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics (&lt;6 years):</strong> The safety and efficacy of Bilfast 40 ml in children under 6 years have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Bilfast 40 ml is a non-sedating, long-acting histamine antagonist with selective peripheral H1 receptor antagonist affinity and no affinity for muscarinic receptors. It inhibits histamine-induced wheal and flare skin reactions for 24 hours following a single dose.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There are no or limited data from the use of Bilfast 40 ml in pregnant women. Animal studies do not indicate direct or indirect harmful effects with respect to reproductive toxicity, parturition, or postnatal development. It is preferable to avoid the use of Bilfast 40 ml during pregnancy as a precaution.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Efficacy and safety of Bilfast 40 ml in children under 2 years of age have not been established, and there is little clinical experience in children aged 2 to 5 years. Therefore, Bilfast 40 ml should not be used in these age groups.</p>', 'active', '317_1742332034.webp', 11, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:07:14', '2025-05-03 00:50:48'),
(3428, 'Grincal DX', 'Algae Calcium + Vitamin D3', 16.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '922_1742332148.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:09:08', '2025-04-19 07:25:03'),
(3429, 'Itra', 'Itraconazole', 580.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Oral Solution', '50 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Itra 100 ml</strong></p><p>Itra 100 ml is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Tinea corporis</li><li>Tinea cruris</li><li>Tinea pedis</li><li>Tinea manus</li><li>Vulvovaginal candidiasis</li><li>Fungal keratitis</li><li>Pityriasis versicolor</li><li>Oropharyngeal candidiasis</li><li>Onychomycosis</li><li>Histoplasmosis</li><li>Systemic infections: Aspergillosis, Candidiasis, Cryptococcus (when other drugs are ineffective)</li><li>Antifungal prophylaxis: To prevent systemic fungal infection in severely neutropenic patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Itra 100 ml is an orally active triazole antifungal drug with a broad spectrum of activity. It inhibits cytochrome P-450 dependent enzymes, leading to impairment in the biosynthesis of ergosterol, a major component of the fungal cell membrane. This results in abnormalities in cell membrane permeability, enzyme activity, and chitin synthesis, ultimately inhibiting fungal growth and cell wall formation.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Itra 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tinea corporis, Tinea cruris</strong>: 100 mg daily for 2 weeks.</li><li><strong>Tinea pedis, Tinea manus</strong>: 100 mg daily for 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Vulvovaginal candidiasis</strong>: 200 mg twice daily for 1 day or 200 mg daily for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Fungal keratitis</strong>: 200 mg daily for 3 weeks.</li><li><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong>: 200 mg daily for 1 week.</li><li><strong>Oropharyngeal candidiasis</strong>: 100 mg daily (200 mg for AIDS patients) for 15 days.</li><li><strong>Onychomycosis</strong>: 200 mg daily for 3 months; or 200 mg twice daily for 7 days (course), subsequent courses repeated after a 21-day interval; fingernails (2 courses), toenails (3 courses).</li><li><strong>Histoplasmosis</strong>: 200 mg 1-2 times daily.</li><li><strong>Systemic Infections</strong>: 200 mg once daily, increased to 200 mg twice daily in invasive or cryptococcal meningitis.</li><li><strong>Antifungal prophylaxis</strong>: 100 mg daily for severely neutropenic patients or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Itra 100 ml</strong></p><p>Take with food, immediately after a full meal.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Itra 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Inhibits the metabolism of Cyclosporine and Warfarin.</li><li>Interaction with Terfenadine, Phenobarbitone, and Midazolam has been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Itra 100 ml.</li><li>Pregnancy (contraindicated).</li><li>Severe hepatic disease.</li><li>Rifampicin therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Itra 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Nausea, abdominal pain, dyspepsia, constipation</li><li>Headache, dizziness</li><li>Raised liver enzymes, hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice</li><li>Peripheral neuropathy</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Itra 100 ml is contraindicated in pregnancy.</li><li>Breastfeeding while receiving Itra 100 ml is not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor hepatic enzyme values in patients with preexisting liver conditions.</li><li>Periodic liver function tests are recommended for continuous treatment beyond one month.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Itra 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li>No known experience of overdose with Itra 100 ml.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Itra 100 ml is an orally active triazole antifungal that inhibits cytochrome P-450 dependent enzymes, impairing the biosynthesis of ergosterol, which is essential for the fungal cell membrane structure.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Not recommended during pregnancy and lactation unless otherwise directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>The efficacy and safety of Itra 100 ml in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul>', 'active', '437_1742332372.webp', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:12:52', '2025-04-16 12:37:47'),
(3430, 'Tirza', 'Tirzepatide', 3500.00, 1, 'x 7.5 mg pre-filled syringe', '7.5 mg pre-filled syringe', 'Injection', '7.5 mg/0.5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1199_1742332511.webp', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:15:11', '2025-05-07 09:58:12'),
(3431, 'Tirza', 'Tirzepatide', 2800.00, 1, 'x 5 mg pre-filled syringe', '5 mg pre-filled syringe', 'Injection', '5 mg/0.5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1118_1742332624.jpg', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:17:04', '2025-05-07 09:58:32');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3432, 'Kidopia', 'Atropine Sulfate', 280.00, 1, 'x 5 ml drop', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '0.01%', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the provided information with headings formatted in bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Kidopia 5 ml</strong></p><p>Atropine is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Non-ulcer dyspepsia</li><li>Irritable bowel syndrome</li><li>Diverticular disease</li><li>Bradycardia</li><li>Organophosphorus poisoning</li><li>Premedication in anesthesia</li><li>Poisoning or overdosage with compounds having muscarinic actions</li><li>Ophthalmic inflammatory eye disorders</li><li>Eye refraction</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Anticholinergics (antimuscarinics)/Anti-spasmodics</li><li>Mydriatic and Cycloplegic agents</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Atropine binds to and inhibits muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, producing a wide range of anticholinergic effects. It is an anticholinergic agent that competitively blocks the muscarinic receptors in peripheral tissues such as the heart, intestines, bronchial muscles, iris, and secretory glands. Some central stimulation may occur. Atropine abolishes bradycardia and reduces heart block due to vagal activity. Smooth muscles in the bronchi and gut are relaxed, while glandular secretions are reduced. It also has a mydriatic and cycloplegic effect.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Kidopia 5 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Adult:</strong></p><ul><li>IV: Bradycardia: 500 mcg every 3-5 mins. Total: 3 mg.</li><li>IV/IM: Organophosphorus poisoning: 2 mg every 10-30 mins until muscarinic effects disappear or atropine toxicity appears.</li><li>IM/SC: Premedication in anesthesia: 300-600 mcg 30-60 mins before anesthesia.</li><li>IV/IM/SC: Poisoning or overdosage with compound having muscarinic actions: 0.6-1 mg, repeat every 2 hours.</li><li>Ophthalmic: Inflammatory eye disorders: As 0.5-1% solution: 1-2 drops 4 times/day.</li><li>Ophthalmic: Refraction: 1% solution, 1 drop twice daily for 1-2 days before procedure.</li><li>Oral: Non-ulcer dyspepsia, Irritable bowel syndrome, Diverticular disease: 0.6-1.2 mg as a single dose at bedtime.</li></ul><p><strong>Usual Pediatric Dose for Anesthesia:</strong></p><ul><li>7 to 16 pounds: 0.1 mg, IV, IM, or subcutaneously</li><li>17 to 24 pounds: 0.15 mg, IV, IM, or subcutaneously</li><li>24 to 40 pounds: 0.2 mg, IV, IM, or subcutaneously</li><li>40 to 65 pounds: 0.3 mg, IV, IM, or subcutaneously</li><li>65 to 90 pounds: 0.4 mg, IV, IM, or subcutaneously</li><li>Over 90 pounds: 0.4 to 0.6 mg, IV, IM, or subcutaneously</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Kidopia 5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Additive anticholinergic effects with quinidine, antidepressants, and some antihistamines.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Glaucoma</li><li>Chronic respiratory disease</li><li>Sick sinus syndrome</li><li>Thyrotoxicosis</li><li>Cardiac failure</li><li>Pyloric stenosis</li><li>Prostatic hypertrophy</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Kidopia 5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Injection:</strong> Dry mouth, dysphagia, constipation, flushing and dryness of skin, tachycardia, palpitations, arrhythmias, mydriasis, photophobia, cycloplegia, raised intraocular pressure.</li><li><strong>Toxic doses:</strong> Tachycardia, hyperpyrexia, restlessness, confusion, excitement, hallucinations, delirium, and may progress to circulatory failure and respiratory depression.</li><li><strong>Eye drops or ointment:</strong> Systemic toxicity, especially in children; prolonged use may lead to irritation, hyperemia, edema, and conjunctivitis. Increased intraocular pressure.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy Category C. Animal reproduction studies have not been conducted with atropine. It is also not known whether atropine can cause fetal harm when given to a pregnant woman or affect reproduction capacity. Atropine should be given to a pregnant woman only if clearly needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Reflux oesophagitis</li><li>Elderly</li><li>Infants and children</li><li>Pregnancy</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Kidopia 5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>May cause hyperthermia, hypertension, increased respiratory rate, nausea, and vomiting. May also lead to CNS stimulation.</li><li>Severe intoxication may lead to CNS depression, coma, respiratory failure, and death.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store atropine at room temperature between 20 to 25°C. Store away from heat, moisture, and light. Keep atropine out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Anticholinergics (antimuscarinics)/Anti-spasmodics</li><li>Mydriatic and Cycloplegic agents</li></ul><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Atropine binds to and inhibits muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, producing a wide range of anticholinergic effects. It is an anticholinergic agent that competitively blocks the muscarinic receptors in peripheral tissues such as the heart, intestines, bronchial muscles, iris, and secretory glands. Some central stimulation may occur. Atropine abolishes bradycardia and reduces heart block due to vagal activity. Smooth muscles in the bronchi and gut are relaxed, while glandular secretions are reduced. It also has a mydriatic and cycloplegic effect.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy Category C. Animal reproduction studies have not been conducted with atropine. It is not known whether atropine can cause fetal harm when given to a pregnant woman or affect reproduction capacity. Atropine should be given to a pregnant woman only if clearly needed.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '407_1742332801.jpg', 17, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:20:01', '2025-05-08 20:12:49'),
(3433, 'Denixil', 'Clonazepam', 4.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Denixil 0.25</strong></p><p>Denixil 0.25 is indicated for the treatment of panic disorder, with or without agoraphobia. Panic disorder is characterized by unexpected panic attacks and a persistent fear of future attacks, along with concerns about their implications. Additionally, Denixil 0.25 is used for the treatment of Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome (particularly the petit mal variant), akinetic seizures, myoclonic seizures, and may be considered for patients with absence seizures who have not responded to succinimides.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</li><li>Benzodiazepine hypnotics</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Denixil 0.25 works by reducing nerve transmission in the motor cortex, effectively suppressing spike and wave discharges associated with absence seizures. This is linked to its ability to enhance GABA activity, which contributes to its efficacy in treating focal and generalized seizures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Seizure Disorders (Adults)</strong>: Start with 1.5 mg daily (divided into three doses), increasing by 0.5-1 mg every three days, up to a maximum of 20 mg per day.</li><li><strong>Panic Disorder (Adults)</strong>: Start with 0.25 mg twice daily, increasing after three days to 1 mg per day as needed.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients</strong>: For children up to 10 years or 30 kg, the recommended dosage is 0.01-0.03 mg/kg/day, not exceeding 0.05 mg/kg/day, divided into multiple doses.</li><li><strong>Injection Dosage</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children</strong>: Administer 0.5 mg via slow IV injection or infusion.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: Administer 1 mg via slow IV injection or infusion, repeating as necessary (typically 1-4 mg). The IV rate should be controlled at 0.25-0.5 mg per minute, with a total dose not exceeding 10 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>Denixil 0.25 does not appear to affect the pharmacokinetics of phenytoin, carbamazepine, or phenobarbital. However, its effect on the metabolism of other drugs has not been fully studied.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Denixil 0.25 should not be prescribed to patients with a history of hypersensitivity to benzodiazepines or significant liver disease. It can be given to patients with open-angle glaucoma receiving appropriate treatment but should not be used for acute narrow-angle glaucoma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common side effects include CNS depression, with approximately 50% of patients experiencing drowsiness and 30% experiencing ataxia. Other potential side effects include:</p><ul><li>Abnormal eye movements, aphonia, coma, tremor, vertigo, confusion, depression, hallucinations, hysteria, insomnia, psychosis, increased libido, palpitations.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Denixil 0.25 should only be used during pregnancy when the potential benefits outweigh the risks. It is not recommended for breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In patients with multiple seizure disorders, the use of Denixil 0.25 may increase the occurrence of generalized tonic-clonic seizures. Combining with valproic acid may lead to absence status. Regular monitoring is recommended for long-term use.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdose may include drowsiness, ataxia, dysarthria, and nystagmus. Severe cases can lead to coma, hypotension, respiratory depression, and cardiorespiratory depression. Treatment includes monitoring vital signs, administering activated charcoal, and considering flumazenil in cases of severe CNS depression.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store Denixil 0.25 in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p>For slow intravenous injection, dilute the vial with 1 ml of water for injection. The solution should be prepared just before use and administered slowly while monitoring vital signs and EEG.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</li><li>Benzodiazepine hypnotics</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Denixil 0.25 shares the common properties of benzodiazepines, enhancing GABAergic neurotransmission, which results in anticonvulsant, sedative, muscle-relaxing, and anxiolytic effects.</p><p><strong>Pediatric and Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: Increased secretion of saliva and bronchial secretions in infants and children may require airway management.</li><li><strong>Geriatrics</strong>: Elderly patients may experience more pronounced effects from the drug due to age-related changes in drug metabolism and receptor sensitivity.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal and Hepatic Impairment</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is needed for patients with renal issues.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: In patients with liver cirrhosis, the plasma binding of Denixil 0.25 may differ from healthy individuals, potentially leading to reduced clearance.</li></ul><p>This medication should be used under the guidance of a healthcare provider who can assess the risks and benefits for each individual.</p>', 'in-active', '1391_1742332962.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:22:42', '2025-05-08 14:29:59'),
(3434, 'Recodep', 'Amitriptyline Hydrochloride + Chlordiazepoxide', 8.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '12.5 mg+5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Recodep 12.5 mg + 5 mg</strong></p><p>This medication is indicated for the treatment of patients with moderate to severe depression associated with moderate to severe anxiety. Symptoms likely to respond in the first week of treatment include insomnia, feelings of guilt or worthlessness, agitation, psychic and somatic anxiety, suicidal ideation, and anorexia.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Recodep 12.5 mg + 5 mg</strong></p><p>The optimum dosage varies depending on the severity of the symptoms and the patient\'s response. When a satisfactory response is obtained, the dosage should be reduced to the smallest amount necessary to maintain remission. The larger portion of the total daily dose may be taken at bedtime. In some patients, a single dose at bedtime may be sufficient. The typical initial dosage is 3 or 4 tablets daily in divided doses. However, the dosage may be adjusted as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Recodep 12.5 mg + 5 mg</strong></p><p>Due to the Amitriptyline component, this medication may block the antihypertensive action of guanethidine or other compounds with a similar mechanism of action.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>This medication is contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to either benzodiazepines or tricyclic antidepressants. It should not be used in conjunction with monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs).</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Recodep 12.5 mg + 5 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include symptoms like anorexia, fatigue, weakness, restlessness, and lethargy.</p><p>The most frequently reported adverse reactions are:</p><ul><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Blurred vision</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Bloating</li></ul><p>Less common adverse effects include:</p><ul><li>Vivid dreams</li><li>Impotence</li><li>Tremor</li><li>Confusion</li><li>Nasal congestion</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Use with caution in patients with a history of seizures. Close supervision is required when administering this medication to hyperthyroid patients or those on thyroid medication. The usual precautions should be observed when treating patients with impaired renal or hepatic function. Pediatric patients receiving antidepressants for any indication should be closely monitored for clinical worsening, suicidality, and unusual changes in behavior.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Recodep 12.5 mg + 5 mg</strong></p><p>Overdose can lead to serious consequences, including death. Critical manifestations of Amitriptyline overdose include:</p><ul><li>Cardiac dysrhythmias</li><li>Severe hypotension</li><li>Convulsions</li><li>CNS depression, including coma</li></ul><p>Benzodiazepine overdose symptoms include:</p><ul><li>Somnolence</li><li>Confusion</li><li>Coma</li><li>Diminished reflexes</li></ul><p>Withdrawal symptoms from abrupt discontinuation of Chlordiazepoxide may include:</p><ul><li>Convulsions</li><li>Tremor</li><li>Abdominal and muscle cramps</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Sweating</li></ul><p>Abrupt discontinuation of Amitriptyline may cause withdrawal symptoms such as:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Headache</li><li>Malaise</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>This medication belongs to the combined anxiolytics and antidepressant drug classes.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Amitriptyline is a tricyclic antidepressant that inhibits the reuptake of norepinephrine and serotonin in the brain, contributing to its antidepressant effects.</p><p>Chlordiazepoxide is an anxiolytic that works by enhancing GABA-mediated chloride influx through GABA receptor channels, causing membrane hyperpolarization, leading to sedative and anxiolytic effects.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The safety of this medication during pregnancy and lactation has not been established.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>The safety and effectiveness of this medication in the pediatric population have not been established. When considering the use of Chlordiazepoxide and Amitriptyline Hydrochloride Tablets in children or adolescents, the potential risks must be carefully weighed against the clinical need.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><p>In elderly and debilitated patients, it is recommended that the dosage be limited to the smallest effective amount to prevent the development of ataxia, over-sedation, confusion, or anticholinergic effects.</p>', 'active', '647_1742333076.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:24:36', '2025-04-17 21:30:17'),
(3435, 'Itonorm', 'Itopride Hydrochloride', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1012_1742333198.jpg', 58, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:26:38', '2025-03-19 02:26:38'),
(3436, 'Xerova EZ', 'Atorvastatin + Ezetimibe', 24.00, 7, 'tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg plus 10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xerova EZ 20 mg + 10 mg:</strong></p><p>Therapy with lipid-altering agents should be only one component of multiple risk factor intervention in individuals at significantly increased risk for atherosclerotic vascular disease due to hypercholesterolemia. Drug therapy is indicated as an adjunct to diet when the response to a diet restricted in saturated fat and cholesterol and other nonpharmacologic measures alone has been inadequate.</p><ol><li><strong>Primary Hyperlipidemia</strong>: This tablet is indicated for the reduction of elevated total cholesterol (total-C), low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-C), apolipoprotein B (Apo B), triglycerides (TG), and non-high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (non-HDL-C), and to increase high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-C) in patients with primary (heterozygous familial and non-familial) hyperlipidemia or mixed hyperlipidemia.</li><li><strong>Homozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia (HoFH)</strong>: This tablet is indicated for the reduction of elevated total-C and LDL-C in patients with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia, as an adjunct to other lipid-lowering treatments (e.g., LDL apheresis) or if such treatments are unavailable.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage of Xerova EZ 20 mg + 10 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dosage Range</strong>: 10/10 mg to 80/20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Starting Dose</strong>: The recommended starting dose is 10/10 mg or 20/10 mg daily. This can be administered as a single dose at any time of the day, with or without food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For patients who require a larger reduction in LDL-C (greater than 55%), the starting dose is 40/10 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Homozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia</strong>: The dosage is 40/10 mg or 80/10 mg daily, used as an adjunct to other lipid-lowering treatments (e.g., LDL apheresis) in these patients or if such treatments are unavailable.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Contraindicated in patients with active liver disease or unexplained persistent elevations in hepatic transaminase levels.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary, but closer monitoring for skeletal muscle effects is advised due to an increased risk of statin-associated myopathy.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xerova EZ 20 mg + 10 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>The risk of myopathy is increased with concurrent administration of fibric acid derivatives, lipid-modifying doses of niacin, cyclosporine, or strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., clarithromycin, HIV protease inhibitors, and itraconazole).</li><li><strong>Avoid</strong> coadministration with cyclosporine and gemfibrozil due to the risk of myopathy/rhabdomyolysis.</li><li><strong>Monitor</strong> patients on digoxin.</li><li><strong>Caution</strong> should be exercised when prescribing this combination with colchicine, niacin, or other medications that could interact.</li><li>If combined with <strong>warfarin</strong>, the International Normalized Ratio (INR) should be monitored.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Active liver disease or unexplained persistent elevations of hepatic transaminase levels.</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of this preparation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xerova EZ 20 mg + 10 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include <strong>rhabdomyolysis</strong>, <strong>myopathy</strong>, <strong>liver enzyme abnormalities</strong>, <strong>myalgia</strong>, <strong>abdominal pain</strong>, and <strong>increased hepatic enzymes</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Myopathy/Rhabdomyolysis</strong>: Exercise caution when prescribing with colchicine, and discontinue therapy if any signs of myopathy or risk factors for renal failure due to rhabdomyolysis occur.</li><li><strong>Liver Enzymes</strong>: Liver enzyme tests should be performed before starting therapy, and if liver injury or jaundice occurs, therapy should be interrupted immediately. If no alternative cause is identified, do not restart therapy.</li><li><strong>Endocrine Function</strong>: Be cautious when administering this drug with drugs that may affect steroid hormones (e.g., ketoconazole, spironolactone, and cimetidine).</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xerova EZ 20 mg + 10 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>No specific treatment for overdose is recommended. Symptomatic and supportive measures should be used as required.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong> in a dry place. Protect from light and keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Other lipid-regulating drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Atorvastatin</strong>: Lowers plasma cholesterol and lipoprotein levels by inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase in the liver, enhancing LDL receptor activity, and reducing LDL production.</li><li><strong>Ezetimibe</strong>: Inhibits cholesterol absorption in the small intestine, reducing the delivery of cholesterol to the liver, which lowers hepatic cholesterol stores and increases cholesterol clearance from the blood.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category X</strong>: Contraindicated in pregnant women or those who may become pregnant, as lipid-lowering drugs are not recommended during pregnancy.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric and Geriatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established for pediatric patients.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary for geriatric patients.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '46_1742333385.jpg', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:29:45', '2025-03-19 02:29:45'),
(3437, 'MomCare', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral', 3.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0 mg/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'in-active', '759_1742333492.jpg', 44, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:31:32', '2025-05-08 20:41:22'),
(3438, 'Caspogin', 'Caspofungin Acetate', 4500.00, 1, 'x 50 mg vial', '50 mg vial', 'IV Injection', '50 mg per vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Caspogin 50 mg/vial:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Empirical Therapy for Presumed Fungal Infections in Febrile, Neutropenic Patients:</strong></li><li>Caspofungin acetate for injection is indicated as empirical therapy for presumed fungal infections in febrile, neutropenic adult and pediatric patients (3 months of age and older).</li><li><strong>Treatment of Candidemia and Other Candida Infections:</strong></li><li>Caspofungin acetate for injection is indicated for the treatment of candidemia and the following Candida infections:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Intra-abdominal abscesses</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Peritonitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pleural space infections in adult and pediatric patients (3 months of age and older).</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Treatment of Esophageal Candidiasis:</strong></li><li>Caspofungin acetate for injection is indicated for the treatment of esophageal candidiasis in adult and pediatric patients (3 months of age and older).</li><li><strong>Treatment of Invasive Aspergillosis in Patients Who Are Refractory to or Intolerant of Other Therapies:</strong></li><li>Caspofungin acetate for injection is indicated for the treatment of invasive aspergillosis in adult and pediatric patients (3 months of age and older) who are refractory to or intolerant of other therapies.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage of Caspogin 50 mg/vial:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Administration Instructions for All Patients:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Administer by slow intravenous (IV) infusion over approximately 1 hour.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not administer by IV bolus.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not mix or co-infuse with other medications.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Avoid using diluents containing dextrose (α-D-glucose).</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Dosage in Adults (18 years and older):</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">A single 70 mg loading dose on Day 1, followed by 50 mg once daily for all indications except esophageal candidiasis.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For esophageal candidiasis, use 50 mg once daily with no loading dose.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Dosage in Pediatric Patients (3 months to 17 years):</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dosing based on body surface area.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">A single 70 mg/m² loading dose on Day 1, followed by 50 mg/m² once daily thereafter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum loading dose and daily maintenance dose should not exceed 70 mg, regardless of calculated dose.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Dosage Adjustments in Patients with Hepatic Impairment:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Reduce dosage for adult patients with moderate hepatic impairment to 35 mg once daily, with a 70 mg loading dose on Day 1 if appropriate.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Dosage Adjustments in Patients Receiving Concomitant Inducers of Hepatic CYP Enzymes:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increase dosage to 70 mg once daily for adult patients on rifampin.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pediatric patients on rifampin should receive 70 mg/m² once daily (not exceeding 70 mg).</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Caspogin 50 mg/vial:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Cyclosporine:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increases AUC of caspofungin but does not affect cyclosporine plasma levels.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Monitor liver enzymes and evaluate the risk/benefit of continued therapy.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Tacrolimus:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Monitor tacrolimus blood concentrations and adjust dosage accordingly.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Inducers of Hepatic CYP Enzymes (e.g., Rifampin, Nevirapine, Efavirenz, Carbamazepine):</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rifampin decreases caspofungin levels. Dose adjustment to 70 mg daily for adults and 70 mg/m² daily for pediatric patients may be necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity:</strong> Caspofungin is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of this product.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Caspogin 50 mg/vial:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Most common (≥10%): Diarrhea, pyrexia, ALT/AST increased, blood alkaline phosphatase increased, blood potassium decreased.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Pediatric Patients:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Most common (≥10%): Pyrexia, diarrhea, rash, ALT/AST increased, blood potassium decreased, hypotension, chills.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Hypersensitivity:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Anaphylaxis has been reported. Discontinue caspofungin if this occurs and provide appropriate treatment. Monitor for histamine-mediated reactions (rash, angioedema, pruritus, bronchospasm).</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Hepatic Effects:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Monitor for liver abnormalities, including cases of hepatic dysfunction, hepatitis, or failure. Limit use in patients with abnormal liver enzymes, especially with cyclosporine co-administration.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Storage:</strong> Store the lyophilized vials at 20° to 25°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Other Antifungal Preparations</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Caspofungin inhibits beta-(1,3)-D-glucan synthesis, an essential component of the cell wall in Aspergillus and Candida species, through the enzyme 1,3-beta glucan synthase. This mechanism is specific to fungi, as beta-(1,3)-D-glucan is absent in mammalian cells.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category C:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">There are no adequate studies in pregnant women, but animal studies showed embryofetal toxicity. Caspofungin should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">It is unknown whether caspofungin is excreted in human milk, though it was found in the milk of lactating rats. Caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul>', 'active', '1193_1742333639.webp', 1, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:33:59', '2025-05-03 01:14:45'),
(3439, 'Merolin', 'Mirogabalin Besylate', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1876_1742333737.jpg', 27, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:35:37', '2025-03-19 02:35:37'),
(3440, 'Buflex', 'Nabumetone', 22.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '750 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Buflex 750 mg</h3><p>Buflex 750 mg is indicated for the relief of signs and symptoms of osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis. The decision to use Buflex 750 mg should be based on carefully considering the potential benefits and risks, along with other treatment options. It is recommended to use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration consistent with individual patient treatment goals.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Buflex 750 mg is a naphthylalkanone, a non-selective prostaglandin G/H synthase (COX) inhibitor, affecting both COX-1 and COX-2. It works by inhibiting the conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandins, which play a role in fever, pain, swelling, inflammation, and platelet aggregation. Buflex is a prodrug that undergoes hepatic biotransformation to the active component, <strong>6-methoxy-2-naphthylacetic acid (6MNA)</strong>, which inhibits prostaglandin synthesis.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><p><strong>For Osteoarthritis and Rheumatoid Arthritis</strong>:</p><ul><li>Starting dose: 1,000 mg daily, which can be taken as a single dose, with or without food.</li><li>Some patients may require a higher dose (1,500 mg to 2,000 mg daily).</li><li>Buflex 750 mg can be administered once or twice daily.</li><li>Doses greater than 2,000 mg per day have not been studied.</li><li>The lowest effective dose should be used for chronic treatment.</li><li>For patients weighing under 50 kg, 1,000 mg may be sufficient. Dosage can be adjusted as per the patient\'s response.</li></ul><p><strong>For Patients with Renal Insufficiency</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Moderate renal insufficiency</strong>: Starting dose should not exceed 750 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Severe renal insufficiency</strong>: Starting dose should not exceed 500 mg once daily. After monitoring, the dose may be increased to a maximum of 1,500 mg (moderate) or 1,000 mg (severe).</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Patients</strong>:</p><p> Safety and effectiveness in children have not been established.</p><h3>Interaction</h3><p>NSAIDs, like Buflex, may reduce the antihypertensive effect of ACE inhibitors. This interaction should be considered when taking both drugs simultaneously.</p><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Buflex 750 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients with known hypersensitivity to Buflex or its components.</li><li>Patients with a history of asthma, urticaria, or allergic-type reactions after taking aspirin or other NSAIDs.</li><li>Treatment of peri-operative pain following coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><p><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>:</p><ul><li>Diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain, constipation, flatulence, nausea, gastritis, stomatitis, vomiting.</li></ul><p><strong>Central Nervous System</strong>:</p><ul><li>Dizziness, headache, fatigue, increased sweating, insomnia, nervousness, somnolence.</li></ul><p><strong>Dermatologic</strong>:</p><ul><li>Pruritus, rash.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Senses</strong>:</p><ul><li>Tinnitus.</li></ul><p><strong>Miscellaneous</strong>:</p><ul><li>Edema.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><p><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>:</p><ul><li>Animal studies have not demonstrated developmental abnormalities, but human studies are insufficient. Buflex should be used during pregnancy only if the benefits justify the potential risks to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Third trimester</strong>: Buflex is not recommended due to the risk of affecting the fetal cardiovascular system (closure of ductus arteriosus).</li></ul><p><strong>Lactation</strong>:</p><p> It is not known whether Buflex is excreted in human milk. Since the active component is excreted in the milk of lactating rats, the drug should not be used in nursing mothers unless necessary, considering the potential risks to the infant.</p><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Long-term use of NSAIDs, including Buflex, may cause renal toxicity, especially in patients with impaired renal function, heart failure, or liver dysfunction.</li><li>Discontinuation of the drug typically leads to recovery of kidney function.</li><li>Special caution is advised in elderly patients (65 years and older).</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><p>Symptoms of overdose may include:</p><ul><li>Lethargy, drowsiness, nausea, vomiting, epigastric pain.</li><li>Gastrointestinal bleeding, hypertension, acute renal failure, respiratory depression, and coma (rare).</li><li>Anaphylactoid reactions can occur.</li></ul><p>Management involves symptomatic and supportive care. Activated charcoal or osmotic cathartic may be used if the patient is seen within 4 hours of ingestion, especially in cases of large overdose (5 to 10 times the usual dose).</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep all medicines out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in children have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Caution should be exercised in elderly patients.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Buflex 750 mg is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) with anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic properties. Its mechanism of action is not fully understood, but it is believed to inhibit prostaglandin synthesis by blocking COX-1 and COX-2 receptors.</p>', 'active', '1340_1742333867.jpg', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:37:47', '2025-03-19 02:37:47'),
(3441, 'FlexiRun Plus', 'Undenatured Type II Collagen + Boswellia Serrata', 40.00, 6, 'capsule', '6 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '40 mg plus 100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '287_1742333991.webp', 8, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:39:51', '2025-05-03 10:47:34'),
(3442, 'Aloglip', 'Alogliptin Benzoate', 12.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '12.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Aloglip 12.5 mg</strong></p><p> Alogliptin is indicated as an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycaemic control in adults with type-2 diabetes mellitus.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Aloglip 12.5 mg</strong></p><p> The recommended dose in patients with normal renal function or mild renal impairment is 25 mg once daily or as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Aloglip 12.5 mg</strong></p><p> Alogliptin is primarily renally excreted. Cytochrome (CYP) P450-related metabolism is negligible. No significant drug-drug interactions are observed with the CYP-substrates or inhibitors tested or with renally excreted drugs.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> History of a serious hypersensitivity reaction to Alogliptin-containing products, such as anaphylaxis, angioedema, or severe cutaneous adverse reactions.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Aloglip 12.5 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include nasopharyngitis, headache, and upper respiratory tract infection.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Acute pancreatitis</strong>: If pancreatitis is suspected, promptly discontinue Alogliptin.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Serious hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, angioedema, and severe cutaneous adverse reactions, have been reported. Discontinue Alogliptin immediately in such cases.</li><li><strong>Hepatic effects</strong>: There have been postmarketing reports of hepatic failure, sometimes fatal. If liver injury is detected, promptly interrupt Alogliptin and assess the cause. Do not restart Alogliptin if liver injury is confirmed and no alternative etiology can be found.</li><li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong>: When combined with insulin secretagogues (e.g., sulfonylurea) or insulin, a lower dose of the insulin secretagogue or insulin may be required to minimize the risk of hypoglycaemia.</li><li><strong>Macrovascular outcomes</strong>: No clinical studies have conclusively established macrovascular risk reduction with Alogliptin or any other antidiabetic drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Aloglip 12.5 mg</strong></p><p> The highest doses in clinical trials were 800 mg (single dose) and 400 mg once daily for 14 days (equivalent to 32 times and 16 times the maximum recommended clinical dose of 25 mg). No serious adverse events were observed at these doses. In the event of an overdose, clinical monitoring and supportive therapy should be administered. Alogliptin is minimally dialyzable, so hemodialysis is unlikely to be beneficial.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Dipeptidyl Peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitor.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Alogliptin is a DPP-4 inhibitor that increases the bloodstream concentrations of incretin hormones (GLP-1 and GIP), which enhances insulin secretion and reduces fasting and postprandial glucose concentrations in a glucose-dependent manner.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category B. Alogliptin should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. It is unknown whether Alogliptin is excreted in human milk, so caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><p> No overall differences in safety or effectiveness were observed between patients aged 65 and over and younger patients. However, greater sensitivity in some elderly individuals cannot be ruled out.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong></p><p> No dose adjustments are needed for mild to moderate hepatic impairment. Caution is advised when administering Alogliptin to patients with liver disease.</p><p><strong>Renal Impairment</strong></p><ul><li>Mild renal impairment (creatinine clearance ≥60 mL/min): No dose adjustment needed.</li><li>Moderate renal impairment (creatinine clearance ≥30 to &lt;60 mL/min): 12.5 mg once daily.</li><li>Severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance ≥15 to &lt;30 mL/min) or end-stage renal disease (ESRD): 6.25 mg once daily.</li><li> Patients requiring hemodialysis can receive their dose of Alogliptin without regard to the timing of dialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Disclaimer</strong></p><p> The information provided here is intended for educational purposes only and should not be used as a substitute for professional medical advice. Always consult with a healthcare provider for guidance on your specific health condition.</p>', 'active', '1004_1742334099.jpg', 62, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:41:39', '2025-03-19 02:41:39'),
(3443, 'Oxcarb XR', 'Oxcarbazepine', 18.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '300 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Oxcarb XR 300 mg</h3><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong>Monotherapy or adjunctive therapy in the treatment of partial seizures.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatrics:</strong>Monotherapy in the treatment of partial seizures in children aged 4–16 years.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adjunctive therapy in the treatment of partial seizures in children aged 2–16 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>The pharmacological activity of Oxcarb XR 300 mg is primarily exerted through the metabolite derivative (the monohydroxy derivative, MHD) of Oxcarb XR 300 mg.</li><li>Its mechanism of action is thought to be based on the blockade of voltage-sensitive sodium channels, stabilizing hyperexcited neural membranes, inhibiting repetitive neuronal firing, and diminishing the propagation of synaptic impulses.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:Initial Dose:</strong> 600 mg/day, divided twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adjunctive Therapy:</strong> Increase by 600 mg/day at weekly intervals, reaching a recommended daily dose of 1200 mg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Conversion to Monotherapy:</strong> Gradually withdraw concomitant medication over 3 to 6 weeks, increasing Oxcarb XR 300 mg by 600 mg/day at weekly intervals to a maximum of 2400 mg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Initiation of Monotherapy:</strong> Increase by 300 mg/day every third day to reach 1200 mg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Creatinine Clearance &lt;30 mL/min:</strong> Start at half the usual dose and titrate slowly.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatrics:Initial Dose:</strong> 8 to 10 mg/kg/day, divided twice daily. For patients aged 2 to &lt;4 years and under 20 kg, consider a starting dose of 16 to 20 mg/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adjunctive Therapy (Aged 2–16 Years):</strong> Target maintenance dose should be reached in 2 weeks for children aged 4–16 years, and in 2–4 weeks for children aged 2–4 years. The maximum dose should not exceed 60 mg/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Conversion to Monotherapy (Aged 4–16 Years):</strong> Increase by 10 mg/kg/day at weekly intervals, withdrawing concomitant antiepileptic drugs over 3 to 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Initiation of Monotherapy (Aged 4–16 Years):</strong> Increase by 5 mg/kg/day every third day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CYP2C19 Inhibition:</strong> Oxcarb XR 300 mg and its metabolite inhibit CYP2C19, which may interact with drugs metabolized by this enzyme (e.g., phenobarbital, phenytoin).</li><li><strong>Hormonal Contraceptives:</strong> Concurrent use may render some contraceptives ineffective (e.g., Ethinylestradiol and Levonorgestrel).</li><li><strong>Felodipine and Verapamil:</strong> Oxcarb XR decreases the bioavailability of these drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to the active substance or any excipients in the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Somnolence, headache, dizziness, diplopia, nausea, vomiting, fatigue.</li><li>Rare: Clinically significant hyponatraemia, hypersensitivity reactions including rash, pruritus, urticaria, angioedema, and anaphylaxis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Oxcarb XR may cause serious birth defects (e.g., cleft palate). It crosses the placenta, and both maternal and neonatal plasma concentrations are similar. Vitamin K1 should be administered as a preventive measure during pregnancy and to the newborn.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Excreted in human breast milk, therefore, Oxcarb XR should not be used during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Alcohol:</strong> Caution should be exercised when combining Oxcarb XR with alcohol due to the possibility of an additive sedative effect.</li><li><strong>Withdrawal:</strong> Gradual withdrawal is recommended to minimize the risk of increased seizure frequency.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>The primary mechanism of Oxcarb XR 300 mg and its active metabolite (MHD) is the blockade of voltage-sensitive sodium channels, leading to stabilization of neural membranes, inhibition of repetitive firing, and reduction in the propagation of synaptic impulses.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '202_1742334223.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:43:43', '2025-03-19 02:43:43');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3444, 'Alergix MR', 'Mizolastine', 6.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Alergix MR 10 mg</strong></p><p> Alergix MR 10 mg is indicated for the symptomatic relief of the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Seasonal allergic rhinoconjunctivitis (hay fever)</li><li>Perennial allergic rhinoconjunctivitis</li><li>Urticaria</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Alergix MR 10 mg, a non-sedating antihistamine, blocks histamine H1-receptors on effector cells of the GI tract, blood vessels, and respiratory tract. It also has mast-cell stabilizing properties.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Alergix MR 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult and children above 12 years</strong>: The usual recommended dose is one 10 mg tablet daily.</li><li><strong>Children below 12 years</strong>: Not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Alergix MR 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Systemically administered <strong>Ketoconazole</strong> and <strong>Erythromycin</strong>, antiarrhythmics (e.g., <strong>Amiodarone</strong>) moderately increase the plasma concentration of Alergix MR 10 mg. This could increase the risk of arrhythmias.</li><li>Concurrent use of other potent inhibitors of the cytochrome P450 3A4 enzyme (e.g., <strong>Ciclosporin</strong>) should be approached with caution.</li><li>No potentiation of the sedation and alteration in performance caused by alcohol with Alergix MR 10 mg has been observed.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Alergix MR 10 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients with clinically significant cardiac disease or a history of symptomatic arrhythmias.</li><li>Patients with known or suspected QT prolongation.</li><li>Patients with electrolyte imbalance (particularly hypokalemia).</li><li>Patients with clinically significant bradycardia.</li><li>Patients taking other drugs that decrease its metabolism.</li><li>Patients with significantly impaired liver function.</li><li>Patients who are hypersensitive to the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Alergix MR 10 mg</strong></p><p> Alergix MR 10 mg is generally well tolerated at recommended doses. The usual side effects include:</p><ul><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Diarrhoea</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Headache</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Raised liver enzymes</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Tachycardia and palpitations</li><li>Bronchospasm and aggravation of asthma have been reported, but causality remains uncertain due to the high frequency of asthma in the treated patient population.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The safety of Alergix MR 10 mg during human pregnancy has not been established. Animal studies show no direct or indirect harmful effects on the development of the embryo or fetus, gestation, or post-natal development.</li><li><strong>Alergix MR 10 mg should be avoided during pregnancy, particularly in the 1st trimester.</strong></li><li>Alergix MR 10 mg is excreted into breast milk and is therefore not recommended during lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>A small number of individuals may experience sedation. It is advisable to assess individual response before performing activities requiring concentration, such as driving.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Alergix MR 10 mg</strong></p><p> In cases of overdose, general symptomatic surveillance with cardiac monitoring, including QT interval and cardiac rhythm, for at least 24 hours is recommended. Standard measures should be used to remove any unabsorbed drug. Studies suggest that <strong>hemodialysis</strong> does not increase the clearance of the drug in patients with renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place.</li><li>Protect from light.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Alergix MR 10 mg blocks histamine H1-receptors on effector cells of the GI tract, blood vessels, and respiratory tract. It also has mast-cell stabilizing properties.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>The safety of Alergix MR 10 mg during pregnancy has not been established. Animal studies show no harmful effects on embryo or fetus development, gestation, or post-natal development. However, it should be avoided in pregnancy (especially in the first trimester) and is not recommended during lactation.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1430_1742334355.webp', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:45:55', '2025-03-19 02:45:55'),
(3445, 'Myfocin', 'Mefenamic acid', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Myfocin 500 mg</strong></p><p> Mefenamic Acid is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Fever</li><li>Toothache</li><li>Headache</li><li>Migraine</li><li>Primary dysmenorrhoea</li><li>Menorrhagia</li><li>Mild to moderate pain in rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis</li><li>Traumatic pain</li><li>Low-back pain</li><li>Juvenile chronic arthritis in children, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Myfocin 500 mg, an anthranilic acid derivative, is a prototypical NSAID. It reversibly inhibits the cyclooxygenase-1 and cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-1 and COX-2) enzymes, thus resulting in reduced synthesis of prostaglandin precursors. It has analgesic and antipyretic properties with minor anti-inflammatory activity.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Myfocin 500 mg</strong></p><p> As with other NSAIDs, the lowest dose should be sought for each patient. Therefore, after observing the response to initial therapy with Myfocin 500 mg, the dose and frequency should be adjusted to suit an individual patient\'s needs. Administration is by the oral route, preferably with food.</p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: A 500 mg dose should be given to adults up to three times (1.5 g total) per day.</li><li><strong>Infants over 6 months</strong>: 25 mg/kg of body weight daily in divided doses for not longer than 7 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Myfocin 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: 500 mg dose should be given to adults up to three times (1.5 gm total) per day with meals. The dosage may be reduced to 1 tablet (250 mg) three times daily. In case of menorrhagia: 500 mg three times daily with meals and from the onset of menses and continued according to the judgment of the physician.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6 months to 1 year: 5 ml three times/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2-4 years: 10 ml three times/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-8 years: 10 ml four times/day.</li><li>9-12 years: 15 ml four times/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"> Treatment should not be continued for more than 7 days except on the advice of a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Myfocin 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Aspirin</strong>: As with other NSAIDs, concomitant administration of Mefenamic Acid and Aspirin is not generally recommended because of the potential of increased adverse effects.</li><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>: Mefenamic Acid can inhibit platelet aggregation and may prolong the prothrombin time in patients on warfarin therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Mefenamic Acid. It should not be used in patients with:</p><ul><li>Peptic ulcer</li><li>Inflammatory bowel diseases</li><li>Hepatic or renal impairment</li><li>Patients in whom aspirin and/or other NSAIDs have induced symptoms of bronchospasm, skin rashes, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Myfocin 500 mg</strong></p><p> The most frequently reported adverse experiences occurring in approximately 1-10% of patients are gastrointestinal:</p><ul><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Diarrhoea</li><li>Dyspepsia</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Nausea</li><li>GI ulcers</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Abnormal renal function</li><li>Pruritus</li><li>Rashes</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: In late pregnancy, as with other NSAIDs, Myfocin 500 mg should be avoided because it may cause premature closure of the ductus arteriosus. In general, there are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Myfocin 500 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. Rated as Pregnancy Category C.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Trace amounts of Myfocin 500 mg may be present in breast milk. Taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or to discontinue the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> It should be administered with caution in allergic diseases, especially in asthma. The treatment should be withdrawn in:</p><ul><li>Diarrhoea</li><li>Rashes</li><li>Cholestatic jaundice</li><li>Thrombocytopenia</li><li>Hemolytic anaemia</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Myfocin 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Headache</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vomiting</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Epigastric pain</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">GI bleeding</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rarely: Diarrhoea, disorientation, excitation, coma, drowsiness, tinnitus, fainting, and occasionally convulsions.</li><li><strong>Management</strong>: Symptomatic and supportive treatment. In acute overdosage, empty the stomach immediately by inducing emesis or by gastric lavage, followed by administration of activated charcoal.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep away from light, store in a cool and dry place. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Myfocin 500 mg contains Mefenamic Acid, which is a nonsteroidal agent with demonstrated analgesic, anti-inflammatory, and antipyretic activity due to its dual action on prostaglandins. It inhibits the enzymes of prostaglandin synthetase and also antagonizes the actions of prostaglandin at the receptor sites.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The safety of Myfocin 500 mg on reproductive capacity and pregnancy has not been established. Thus, Myfocin 500 mg should be used in women of childbearing potential and during pregnancy only when the potential benefits are expected to outweigh the potential risks. Trace amounts of Myfocin 500 mg may be present in breast milk and transmitted to the nursing infant. Thus, Myfocin 500 mg should not be taken by the nursing mother because of the effects of this class of drugs on the infant\'s cardiovascular system.</p>', 'active', '1358_1742334480.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:48:00', '2025-03-19 02:48:00'),
(3446, 'Cibrate', 'Ciprofibrate', 15.00, 15, 'tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cibrate 100 mg</strong></p><p> Ciprofbrate is indicated as an adjunct to diet, exercise, and weight reduction for the following:</p><ul><li>Treatment of severe hypertriglyceridemia with or without low HDL cholesterol.</li><li>Mixed hyperlipidemia when a statin is contraindicated or not tolerated.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Cibrate 100 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults:</strong> The recommended dose is Ciprofbrate 100 mg per day. This dose should not be exceeded.</p><p> <strong>Patients with renal insufficiency:</strong></p><ul><li>In moderate renal impairment (creatinine clearance 30-80 ml/min/1.73 m²), it is recommended that the dosage be reduced to one tablet every other day. Patients should be carefully monitored.</li><li>Ciprofbrate should not be used in severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min/1.73 m²).</li><li> <strong>Patients with hepatic insufficiency:</strong> Use with caution in patients with impaired hepatic function. Ciprofbrate treatment should be discontinued in case of increased AST and ALT levels to more than 3 times the upper limit of normal or if cholestatic liver injury is evidenced.</li><li> <strong>Elderly:</strong> As for adults, but precautions should be taken for age over 70 years.</li><li> <strong>Pediatric population:</strong> Not recommended since safety and efficacy in children have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Cibrate 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Other fibrates &amp; HMG CoA reductase inhibitors:</strong> Risk of myopathy, rhabdomyolysis, and myoglobinuria may be increased if Ciprofbrate is used in combination with other fibrates and HMG CoA reductase inhibitors.</li><li><strong>Oral anticoagulant therapy:</strong> Caution should be exercised when Ciprofbrate is taken with oral anticoagulants. Concomitant oral anticoagulant therapy should be given at a reduced dosage and adjusted according to INR.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to the active substance or to any of the excipients.</li><li>Severe hepatic impairment.</li><li>Severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min/1.73 m²).</li><li>Pregnancy and lactation or when pregnancy is suspected.</li><li>Concurrent use with another fibrate.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Cibrate 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Headache, Dizziness, Somnolence, Vertigo, Nausea, Vomiting, Diarrhea, Dyspepsia, Abdominal pain, Rash, Alopecia, Myalgia, Fatigue.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Special warnings:</strong> Patients with rare hereditary problems of galactose intolerance, lactose deficiency, or glucose-galactose malabsorption should not take this medicine.</li><li><strong>Myalgia/myopathy:</strong> Patients should be advised to report unexplained muscle pain, tenderness, or weakness immediately.</li><li><strong>Patients with impaired hepatic function:</strong> Periodic hepatic function tests are recommended (every 3 months for the first 12 months of treatment). Ciprofbrate treatment should be discontinued in case of increased AST and ALT levels to more than 3 times the upper limit of normal or if cholestatic liver injury is evidenced.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C. Protect from light and moisture. Keep the medicine out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Ciprofbrate reduces both LDL &amp; VLDL and hence the levels of triglyceride and cholesterol associated with these lipoprotein fractions. It also increases levels of HDL cholesterol. Ciprofbrate is effective in the treatment of hyperlipidemia associated with high plasma concentrations of LDL and VLDL. There is evidence that treatment with fibrates may reduce coronary heart disease events.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> There is no evidence that Ciprofbrate is teratogenic, but signs of toxicity at high doses were observed in teratogenicity tests in animals. As there are no data on its use in human pregnancy, Ciprofbrate is contraindicated during pregnancy. As there are no data on its use in lactation, Ciprofbrate is contraindicated in nursing mothers.</p>', 'active', '1221_1742334597.jpg', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:49:57', '2025-03-19 02:49:57'),
(3447, 'Bilastin', 'Bilastine', 130.00, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Oral Solution', '12.5 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Bilastin 60 ml</strong></p><p>Bilastin 60 ml is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of allergic rhino-conjunctivitis (seasonal and perennial) and urticaria.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Bilastin 60 ml is a non-sedative H1 receptor selective antagonist. Its principal effects are mediated via selective inhibition of peripheral H1-receptors. It shows moderate to high affinity for histamine H1-receptors and no affinity for muscarinic, serotonergic, dopaminergic, and noradrenergic receptors.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Bilastin 60 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children between 6 to 11 years</strong>:</li><li>10 mg mouth dissolving tablet for the symptomatic relief of allergic rhinitis, allergic rhinoconjunctivitis, and urticaria. The Mouth dissolving tablet is for oral use only. It should be placed in the mouth, where it will disperse rapidly in saliva and can be easily swallowed. Alternatively, the mouth dissolving tablet can be dispersed in a teaspoon of water before being swallowed. The maximum recommended daily dose is 10 mg (1 tablet), and it should not be exceeded. If a dose is missed, the next scheduled dose should be taken. An extra dose should not be taken.</li><li><strong>Adults &amp; adolescents (12 years of age and over)</strong>:</li><li>20 mg tablet once daily for symptomatic relief of allergic rhinitis, urticaria, and allergic rhinoconjunctivitis. The maximum recommended daily dose is 20 mg (1 tablet), and it should not be exceeded. If a dose is missed, the next scheduled dose should be taken. An extra dose should not be taken. The tablet should be swallowed with water on an empty stomach for optimal exposure to Bilastin 60 ml.</li><li><strong>Children between 2 to 11 years</strong>:</li><li>4 ml once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Bilastin 60 ml</strong></p><p>Concomitant intake of Bilastin 60 ml and Ketoconazole, Erythromycin, or Diltiazem increases the C max of Bilastin 60 ml. The psychomotor performance after concomitant intake of alcohol and Bilastin 60 ml was similar to that observed after intake of alcohol and placebo. Concomitant intake of Bilastin 60 ml and Lorazepam 3 mg for 8 days did not potentiate the depressant CNS effects of Lorazepam.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Bilastin 60 ml is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to the active substance or to any of the excipients of the tablet.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Bilastin 60 ml</strong></p><p>The most commonly reported side effects in clinical trials are headache, dizziness, somnolence, and fatigue. These adverse events occurred with a comparable frequency in patients receiving placebo.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>There are no adequate or limited data for the use of Bilastin 60 ml in pregnant women and during breastfeeding. In the case of pregnancy or breastfeeding, Bilastin 60 ml should be avoided unless advised otherwise by a physician.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Co-administration of Bilastin 60 ml and P-glycoprotein inhibitors (e.g., Ketoconazole, Erythromycin, Cyclosporine, Ritonavir, or Diltiazem) should be avoided in patients with moderate or severe renal impairment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Bilastin 60 ml</strong></p><p>In clinical trials, after administration of Bilastin 60 ml at doses 10 to 11 times the therapeutic dose (220 mg as a single dose or 200 mg/day for 7 days), the frequency of treatment-emergent adverse events was two times higher than with placebo. The most frequent adverse reactions were dizziness, headache, and nausea. No serious adverse events or significant prolongation in the QTc interval were reported.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in patients with impaired hepatic &amp; renal function</strong>:</li><li>There is no clinical experience in patients with hepatic impairment. Since Bilastin 60 ml is not metabolized and renal clearance is its major elimination route, hepatic impairment is not expected to increase systemic exposure above the safety margin. Therefore, no dosage adjustment is required in patients with hepatic impairment. No dosage adjustment is required in patients with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Geriatrics (&gt;65 years of age)</strong>:</li><li>No dosage adjustments are necessary in patients over 65 years.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics (&lt;6 years of age)</strong>:</li><li>The safety and efficacy of Bilastin 60 ml in children under 6 years of age have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Bilastin 60 ml is a non-sedating, long-acting histamine antagonist with selective peripheral H1 receptor antagonist affinity and no affinity for muscarinic receptors. Bilastin 60 ml inhibits histamine-induced wheal and flare skin reactions for 24 hours following single doses.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There are no or limited data on the use of Bilastin 60 ml in pregnant women. Animal studies do not indicate direct or indirect harmful effects with respect to reproductive toxicity, parturition, or postnatal development. As a precautionary measure, it is preferable to avoid the use of Bilastin 60 ml during pregnancy. The excretion of Bilastin 60 ml in milk has not been studied in humans. A decision must be made taking into account the benefit of breastfeeding for the child and the benefit of Bilastin 60 ml therapy for the mother.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Efficacy and safety of Bilastin 60 ml in children under 2 years of age have not been established, and there is little clinical experience in children aged 2 to 5 years. Therefore, Bilastin 60 ml should not be used in these age groups.</p>', 'active', '81_1742334791.webp', 17, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:53:11', '2025-05-03 00:50:17'),
(3448, 'L-Amlo', 'Levamlodipine Maleate', 4.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '1.25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the formatted text with the headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of L-Amlo 1.25 mg</strong></p><p> Levamlodipine is a calcium channel blocker and may be used alone or in combination with other antihypertensive agents for the treatment of hypertension, to lower blood pressure. Lowering blood pressure reduces the risk of fatal and nonfatal cardiovascular events, primarily strokes and myocardial infarctions.</p><p><strong>Dosage of L-Amlo 1.25 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Route of administration</strong>: Should be taken orally.</li><li><strong>Dosage</strong>: The usual initial antihypertensive oral dose of Levamlodipine is 2.5 mg once daily, and the maximum dose is 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Adult and elderly patients</strong>: Elderly patients, or patients with hepatic insufficiency may be started on 1.25 mg once daily, and this dose may be used when adding Levamlodipine to other antihypertensive therapy. Dosage needs to be adjusted according to blood pressure goals. 7 to 14 days waiting period between titration steps is needed. Titration needs to be done more rapidly, however, if clinically warranted, provided the patient is assessed frequently.</li><li><strong>Use in children and adolescents</strong>: The effective antihypertensive oral dose in pediatric patients ages 6-17 years is 1.25 mg to 2.5 mg once daily. Doses in excess of 2.5 mg daily have not been studied in pediatric patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of L-Amlo 1.25 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Impact of other drugs on Amlodipine</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>CYP3A Inhibitors</strong>: Co-administration with CYP3A inhibitors (moderate and strong) results in increased systemic exposure to amlodipine and may require dose reduction.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>CYP3A Inducers</strong>: No information is available on the quantitative effects of CYP3A inducers on amlodipine.</li><li><strong>Impact of Amlodipine on other drugs</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Simvastatin</strong>: Co-administration of simvastatin with amlodipine increases the systemic exposure of simvastatin. Limit the dose of simvastatin in patients on amlodipine to 20 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Immunosuppressants</strong>: Amlodipine may increase the systemic exposure of cyclosporine or tacrolimus when co-administered. Frequent monitoring of trough blood levels of cyclosporine and tacrolimus is recommended and adjust the dose when appropriate.</li><li><strong>With food and others</strong>: Can be taken with or without food. The absorption is unaffected by food. Avoid grapefruit products &amp; natural licorice.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> S-Amlodipine is contraindicated in patients with known sensitivity to amlodipine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of L-Amlo 1.25 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Difficulty in breathing, dizziness, reflex tachycardia, hypotension, abdominal pain &amp; edema.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Diarrhea, back pain, headache, fatigue &amp; nausea.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypotension</strong>: Symptomatic hypotension is possible, particularly in patients with severe aortic stenosis. Because of the gradual onset of action, acute hypotension is unlikely.</li><li><strong>Increased Angina or Myocardial Infarction</strong>: Worsening angina and acute myocardial infarction can develop after starting or increasing the dose of amlodipine, particularly in patients with severe obstructive coronary artery disease.</li><li><strong>Patients with Hepatic Failure</strong>: Because amlodipine is extensively metabolized by the liver and the plasma elimination half-life (t½) is 56 hours in patients with impaired hepatic function, titrate slowly when administering amlodipine to patients with severe hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of L-Amlo 1.25 mg</strong></p><p> Overdosage might be expected to cause excessive peripheral vasodilation with marked hypotension and possibly a reflex tachycardia.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool &amp; dry place, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep all medicines out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Calcium-channel blockers.</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Levamlodipine is indicated for the treatment of hypertension in adults and pediatric patients 6 years and older, to lower blood pressure. Amlodipine is a dihydropyridine calcium antagonist (calcium ion antagonist or slow channel blocker) that inhibits the transmembrane influx of calcium ions into vascular smooth muscle and cardiac muscle. Amlodipine inhibits calcium ion influx across cell membranes selectively, with a greater effect on vascular smooth muscle cells than on cardiac muscle cells. Amlodipine is a 1:1 racemic mixture of levamlodipine and dextro amlodipine, it has been demonstrated that levamlodipine is the pharmacologically active, anti-hypertensive isomer.</p><ul><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: Oral Levamlodipine has a Tmax of 6-12 h and a bioavailability of 64-90%. Absorption of Levamlodipine is not significantly affected by food.</li><li><strong>Distribution</strong>: The volume of distribution of Levamlodipine is similar to amlodipine. The volume of distribution of amlodipine is 21 L/kg.</li><li><strong>Metabolism and Excretion</strong>: Levamlodipine is 90% metabolized to inactive metabolites. Incubation with liver microsomes has shown that this metabolism is primarily mediated by CYP3A4. This derivative can be further oxidatively deaminated or O-dealkylated, but does not appear to undergo O-demethylation like racemic amlodipine. Levamlodipine is 60% eliminated in urine with 10% eliminated as the unmetabolized drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The limited available data based on post-marketing reports with amlodipine use in pregnant women are not sufficient to inform a drug-associated risk for major birth defects and miscarriage. No adverse effects of amlodipine on the breastfed infant have been observed.</p>', 'active', '1990_1742334900.jpg', 62, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:55:00', '2025-03-19 02:55:00'),
(3449, 'XeroSoft Junior', 'White Soft Paraffin + Liquid Paraffin', 350.00, 1, 'x 180 ml container', '180 ml container', 'Lotion', '8%+ 5%', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Sure! Here\'s the information you provided, with headings in normal bold text as requested:</p><p><strong>Indications of XeroSoft Junior 8%+3% 180 ml</strong></p><p>For general use as an emollient in the symptomatic relief of dry skin conditions.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Emollients &amp; combined preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>White Soft Paraffin and Liquid Paraffin are emollients with occlusive properties for topical application. There is no significant systemic absorption of the active ingredients but it has substantial penetration into the stratum corneum.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of XeroSoft Junior 8%+3% 180 ml</strong></p><p>Apply a thin film of the ointment to the affected area of the skin, in the direction of hair growth, as required. Repeat as necessary. Ideally, the product should be applied three or four times a day or at least twice a day. In adults, where a large area of the body is affected, up to 500 g a week may be used. For topical administration only.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of XeroSoft Junior 8%+3% 180 ml</strong></p><p>Prolonged use may cause folliculitis. Should this occur, use of the product should be discontinued.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of White Soft Paraffin and Liquid Paraffin during pregnancy and lactation has not been established, but use during these periods is not considered to constitute a hazard.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The ointment should be applied in the direction of hair growth to reduce the incidence of folliculitis.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of XeroSoft Junior 8%+3% 180 ml</strong></p><p>Emosoft ointment has low toxicity. If ingested, treatment should be supportive and symptomatic. Gastric lavage should not be attempted following ingestion of paraffin-based products due to the risk of inhalation or aspiration into the lungs.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Do not store above 30° C. Keep away from light and out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1286_1742335093.webp', 8, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 02:58:13', '2025-05-05 18:21:33'),
(3450, 'XeroSoft Junior 15% + 6%', 'White Soft Paraffin + Liquid Paraffin', 180.00, 1, 'x 50 gm tube', '50 gm tube', 'Cream', '50 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the text with the heading in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of XeroSoft Junior 15%+6% 50 gm</strong></p><p>For general use as an emollient in the symptomatic relief of dry skin conditions.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Emollients &amp; combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>White Soft Paraffin and Liquid Paraffin are emollients with occlusive properties for topical application. There is no significant systemic absorption of the active ingredients but has substantial penetration into the stratum corneum.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of XeroSoft Junior 15%+6% 50 gm</strong></p><p>Apply a thin film of the ointment to the affected area of the skin, in the direction of hair growth, as required. Repeat as necessary. Ideally, the product should be applied three or four times a day or at least twice a day. In adults, where a large area of the body is affected, up to 500 g a week may be used. For topical administration only.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of XeroSoft Junior 15%+6% 50 gm</strong></p><p>Prolonged use may cause folliculitis. Should this occur, the use of the product should be discontinued.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of White Soft Paraffin and Liquid Paraffin during pregnancy and lactation has not been established, but use during these periods is not considered to constitute a hazard.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The ointment should be applied in the direction of hair growth to reduce the incidence of folliculitis.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of XeroSoft Junior 15%+6% 50 gm</strong></p><p>Emosoft ointment has low toxicity, and if ingested, treatment should be supportive and symptomatic. Gastric lavage should not be attempted following ingestion of paraffin-based products, due to the risk of inhalation or aspiration into the lungs.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Do not store above 30°C. Keep away from light and out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1971_1742335250.webp', 8, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 03:00:50', '2025-05-05 18:22:43'),
(3451, 'Tems', 'Telmisartan', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '80 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information for Tems 80 mg in a clearer, bold format for the key headings:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Tems 80 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: Treatment of essential hypertension in adults.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular prevention</strong>: Reduction of cardiovascular morbidity in adults with:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Atherothrombotic cardiovascular disease (history of coronary heart disease, stroke, or peripheral arterial disease)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Type 2 diabetes mellitus with documented target organ damage.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Angiotensin-II receptor blocker</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Tems 80 mg blocks the vasoconstrictor and aldosterone-secreting effects of angiotensin II by selectively blocking the binding of angiotensin II to the AT1 receptor. It has a much greater affinity for the AT1 receptor than for the AT2 receptor, which is not associated with cardiovascular homeostasis.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Tems 80 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Starting dose: <strong>40 mg once daily</strong>.</li><li>Dosage range: 20 to 80 mg.</li><li>Blood pressure response is dose-related and visible within 2 weeks, with maximal reduction after 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment or elderly</strong>: No initial dosage adjustment required for these populations, but dialysis patients should be closely monitored for orthostatic hypotension.</li><li><strong>Patients ≥75 years old or with hepatic impairment</strong>: Initial therapy not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Tems 80 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Can be administered with or without food.</li><li>Can be taken with other antihypertensive agents.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Tems 80 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>NSAIDs</strong>: Increased risk of renal impairment and loss of antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>Aliskiren</strong>: Co-administration with Tems 80 mg should be avoided in patients with diabetes.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Tems 80 mg or any of its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Tems 80 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypertensive patients</strong>: Sinus pain and congestion (sinusitis), back pain, diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular risk reduction</strong>: Intermittent claudication, skin ulcer.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong> (first trimester), <strong>Category D</strong> (second and third trimesters).</li><li>Discontinue if pregnancy is detected.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Discontinue nursing or the drug due to potential risks to the infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid fetal or neonatal exposure.</li><li>Monitor carefully in patients with hepatic or renal impairment.</li><li>Avoid concomitant use with an ACE inhibitor.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Tems 80 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Most likely manifestation: <strong>Hypotension</strong>, dizziness, tachycardia.</li><li>Other effects: Bradycardia, increase in serum creatinine, acute renal failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Do not store above <strong>30°C</strong>.</li><li>Protect from light and high humidity.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: Severe impairment or on hemodialysis: <strong>20 mg once daily</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mild to moderate: Max <strong>40 mg once daily</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe: <strong>Contraindicated</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Angiotensin-II receptor blocker</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Tems 80 mg selectively blocks the AT1 receptor of angiotensin II, blocking the vasoconstrictor and aldosterone-secreting effects, independent of angiotensin II synthesis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category C</strong> (first trimester), <strong>Category D</strong> (second and third trimester).</li><li>Use during pregnancy can cause fetal and neonatal injury, including hypotension, neonatal skull hypoplasia, anuria, renal failure, and death.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>No overall differences in safety and effectiveness compared to younger patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong></p><ul><li>Carefully monitor and titrate slowly in patients with hepatic insufficiency.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you\'d like further details or clarification!</p>', 'active', '161_1742335396.webp', 26, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 03:03:16', '2025-03-19 03:03:16'),
(3452, 'Progency', 'Dydrogesterone', 30.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '742_1742335552.jpg', 1, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 03:05:52', '2025-03-19 03:05:52'),
(3453, 'Azobrin 1%', 'Brinzolamide', 500.00, 1, 'x 5 ml drop', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Suspension', '5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Azobrin 1% 5 ml Ophthalmic Suspension</h3><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><p>Azobrin 1% 5 ml is indicated as <strong>monotherapy</strong>, or as <strong>adjunctive therapy</strong> to beta-blockers in the treatment of <strong>elevated intraocular pressure</strong> in <strong>ocular hypertension</strong> or <strong>open-angle glaucoma</strong>.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for <strong>miotics</strong> and <strong>glaucoma</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Carbonic anhydrase (CA) is an enzyme found in many tissues of the body, including the eye. It catalyzes the reversible reaction involving the hydration of carbon dioxide and the dehydration of carbonic acid. The most active isoenzyme, CA-II, is found primarily in red blood cells but also in other tissues.</p><p>Inhibition of carbonic anhydrase in the ciliary processes of the eye reduces aqueous humor secretion by slowing the formation of bicarbonate ions, which reduces sodium and fluid transport. This results in a decrease in intraocular pressure. Elevated intraocular pressure is a significant risk factor for optic nerve damage and glaucomatous visual field loss.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Recommended Dose:</strong> One drop in the conjunctival sac of the affected eye(s) <strong>twice daily</strong>. Some patients may respond better with <strong>three times daily</strong> administration.</li><li><strong>Instructions:</strong> Shake well before use.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors:</strong> Rare drug interactions have occurred with high-dose salicylate therapy in patients receiving oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitors. Potential interactions should be considered when prescribing Azobrin 1%.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to any component of this product.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common side effects</strong> include blurred vision, bitter/sour/unusual taste.</li><li>Other side effects: <strong>blepharitis, dermatitis, dry eye, foreign body sensation, headache, hyperemia, ocular discharge, ocular discomfort, ocular keratitis, ocular pain, ocular pruritus, rhinitis.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C:</strong> No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Use only if the benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is unknown whether Azobrin 1% 5 ml is excreted in human milk. Lactating mothers should either discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, based on the drug\'s importance to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Azobrin 1% 5 ml is excreted predominantly by the kidney. It is not recommended for <strong>severe renal impairment</strong>.</li><li>Not studied in <strong>hepatic impairment</strong>. Use with caution in these patients.</li><li>Concomitant administration with <strong>oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitors</strong> is not recommended.</li><li>If hypersensitivity reactions occur after use, discontinue the drug and consult a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Although no human data are available, overdose may cause <strong>electrolyte imbalances</strong>, acidosis, and nervous system effects.</li><li><strong>Monitoring:</strong> Serum electrolyte (especially potassium) and blood pH levels should be monitored.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store at <strong>room temperature</strong> and <strong>protect from light</strong>.</li><li>Do not touch the dropper tip to any surface.</li><li>Discard the contents after <strong>four weeks</strong> of opening.</li><li>Protect from freezing.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Drugs for miotics and glaucoma</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Inhibition of carbonic anhydrase in the ciliary processes of the eye decreases aqueous humor formation, thus reducing elevated intraocular pressure, a major risk factor in glaucoma and optic nerve damage.</p><p>Let me know if you need further details!</p>', 'active', '1806_1742335730.jpg', 1, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 03:08:50', '2025-05-03 00:24:37'),
(3454, 'Ternilla SR', 'Aceclofenac', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information with the headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Ternilla SR 200 mg</strong></p><p> Ternilla SR 200 mg is indicated for the relief of pain and inflammation in osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, toothache, trauma, and lumbago.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for Osteoarthritis, Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Ternilla SR 200 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ternilla SR 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Extended release tablet</strong>: The recommended dose in adults is one 200 mg Ternilla SR 200 mg tablet daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Film-coated tablet</strong>: The recommended dose in adults is 100 mg, twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ternilla SR 200 mg</strong></p><p> No significant drug interactions have been observed, but close monitoring of patients is required when it is used with:</p><ul><li><strong>Lithium and Digoxin</strong>: May increase plasma concentration of lithium and digoxin.</li><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: May interact with the activity of diuretics.</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants</strong>: May enhance the activity of anticoagulants.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: May increase the plasma level of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Ternilla SR 200 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to it or in whom aspirin or NSAIDs precipitate attacks of asthma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ternilla SR 200 mg</strong></p><p> Ternilla SR 200 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The use of Ternilla SR 200 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Caution should be exercised with patients who have active or suspected peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding, moderate to severe hepatic impairment, and cardiac or renal impairment. Caution should also be exercised in patients suffering from dizziness or urticaria.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> There are no clinical data on the use of Ternilla SR 200 mg in children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Drugs for Osteoarthritis, Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Ternilla SR 200 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The use of Ternilla SR 200 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> There are no clinical data on the use of Ternilla SR 200 mg in children.</p>', 'active', '1427_1742335914.jpg', 1, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 03:11:54', '2025-03-19 03:11:54'),
(3455, 'Freshfil Mono', 'Carboxymethylcellulose Sodium', 15.00, 5, 'Ophthalmic Solution', '5 Ophthalmic Solution in a pack', 'Ophthalmic Suspension', '0.5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Freshfil Mono 1%</strong></p><p>It is used as a lubricant to relieve irritation and discomfort due to dryness of the eye or due to exposure to wind or sun.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Freshfil Mono 1%</strong></p><p>Instill 1 drop in the affected eye(s) 4 times a day or as needed.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Freshfil Mono 1%</strong></p><p>Not known.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>This eye drop is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any ingredient of the product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Freshfil Mono 1%</strong></p><p>Burning, eye irritation or pruritus, visual disturbance, and ocular discharge have been reported with this eye drop.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Concomitant ocular medication should be administered 15 minutes prior to the instillation of this eye drop.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>The drug is to be used within 30 days after the first opening. Store at a temperature not exceeding 30°C in a dry place. Protect from light. The bottle is to be closed strongly immediately after use. Keep away from the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Carboxymethylcellulose binds to the surface of corneal epithelial cells via its glucopyranose subunits binding to glucose receptors GLUT-1. The residence time of carboxymethylcellulose bound to corneal cells is approximately 2 hours as indicated by a short-term binding assay. Binding of carboxymethylcellulose to the matrix proteins stimulated corneal epithelial cell attachment, migration, and re-epithelialization of corneal wounds. This eye drop contains Freshfil Mono 1%, similar to normal tears, which acts as an ocular lubricant. It provides a lubricating and hydrating protective shield on the ocular surface.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Safe use during pregnancy and lactation has not been established.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>This eye drop should not be used in infants and small children under 3 years.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><p>No overall differences in safety or effectiveness have been observed between elderly and other adult patients.</p>', 'active', '9_1742336119.webp', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 03:15:19', '2025-05-03 10:56:45');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3456, 'Lofos', 'Calcium Acetate', 6.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '667 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Lofos 667 mg</strong></p><p>Lofos 667 mg is indicated for the control of hyperphosphatemia in end-stage renal disease (ESRD) and does not promote aluminum absorption.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for reduction of serum phosphorus in patients with ESRD</li><li>Minerals in bone formation</li><li>Specific mineral preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Lofos 667 mg, when taken with meals, combines with dietary phosphate to form insoluble Calcium Phosphate, which is excreted in the feces. Maintaining serum phosphorus below 6.0 mg/dL is generally considered a clinically acceptable outcome with phosphate binders. Lofos 667 mg is highly soluble at neutral pH, making the calcium readily available for binding to phosphate in the proximal small intestine. Orally administered Lofos 667 mg from pharmaceutical dosage forms has been demonstrated to be systemically absorbed up to approximately 40% under fasting conditions and up to approximately 30% under non-fasting conditions. This range represents data from both healthy subjects and renal dialysis patients under various conditions.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Lofos 667 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: Initially 3-4 g daily. Usual range: 6-12 g daily. To control hyperphosphatemia in chronic renal failure: Initially 1.334 g (338 mg of calcium). May increase dosage gradually according to serum phosphate concentration, provided hypercalcemia does not occur.</li><li><strong>Recommended Initial Dose for Dialysis Patients</strong>: 2 tablets with each meal. The dosage may be increased gradually to bring the serum phosphate level below 6 mg/dL, as long as hypercalcemia does not develop. Most patients require 3-4 tablets with each meal.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: The safety and efficacy of Lofos 667 mg have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: In clinical studies, no overall differences in safety or effectiveness were observed between geriatric and younger subjects, and other clinical experience has not identified differences in responses between elderly and younger patients. However, greater sensitivity in some older individuals cannot be ruled out.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Lofos 667 mg</strong></p><p>Lofos 667 mg may decrease the bioavailability of tetracycline.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Lofos 667 mg is contraindicated in patients with hypercalcemia.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Lofos 667 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Patients may occasionally experience nausea during therapy.</li><li>Hypercalcemia may occur.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Mild hypercalcemia (Ca &gt;10.5 mg/dL)</strong> may be asymptomatic or manifest as constipation, anorexia, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Severe hypercalcemia (Ca &gt;12 mg/dL)</strong> may be associated with confusion, delirium, stupor, and coma.</li><li>Mild hypercalcemia is easily controlled by reducing the dose or temporarily discontinuing therapy. Severe hypercalcemia can be treated by acute hemodialysis and discontinuing therapy.</li><li>Decreasing dialysate calcium concentration may reduce the incidence and severity of hypercalcemia induced by Lofos 667 mg.</li><li>Long-term effects of Lofos 667 mg on vascular or soft-tissue calcification have not been determined.</li><li>Isolated cases of pruritus have been reported, possibly indicating allergic reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Animal reproduction studies have not been conducted with Lofos 667 mg. It is not known whether Lofos 667 mg can cause fetal harm or affect reproduction capacity. It should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: There are no adequate studies in women for determining infant risk during breastfeeding. Weigh the potential benefits against the potential risks before using Lofos 667 mg while breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Excessive dosage of Lofos 667 mg can induce hypercalcemia. Therefore, during early treatment and dosage adjustment, serum calcium should be monitored twice weekly.</li><li>Should hypercalcemia develop, the dosage should be reduced or treatment discontinued based on severity.</li><li>Lofos 667 mg should not be given to patients on digitalis, as hypercalcemia may precipitate cardiac arrhythmias.</li><li>Therapy should always start with a low dose and should not be increased without careful monitoring of serum calcium.</li><li>Patients should be informed about compliance with dosage instructions, adherence to diet, and avoiding nonprescription antacids. They should also be aware of hypercalcemia symptoms.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Lofos 667 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Administration in excess of the appropriate daily dosage can cause severe hypercalcemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at 15°C to 30°C in a dry place protected from light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Lofos 667 mg, when taken with meals, combines with dietary phosphate to form insoluble Calcium Phosphate, which is excreted in the feces. It is highly soluble at neutral pH, allowing calcium to bind to phosphate in the proximal small intestine. Orally administered Lofos 667 mg is absorbed systemically up to approximately 40% under fasting conditions and up to approximately 30% under non-fasting conditions.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Teratogenic Effects</strong>: Category C. Animal reproduction studies have not been conducted with Lofos 667 mg. It is unknown whether Lofos 667 mg can cause fetal harm or affect reproduction capacity when administered to a pregnant woman. It should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '1854_1742336245.webp', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 03:17:25', '2025-05-08 20:20:57'),
(3457, 'DLP', 'Dexlansoprazole', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '30 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the text with normal bold formatting applied to the headings:</p><p><strong>Indications of DLP 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: DLP 30 mg is indicated for the healing of all grades of erosive esophagitis (EE) for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: DLP 30 mg is indicated to maintain healing of EE and relief of heartburn for up to 6 months.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease</strong>: DLP 30 mg is indicated for the treatment of heartburn associated with symptomatic non-erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD) for 4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> DLP 30 mg delayed-release capsule is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI), that inhibits gastric acid secretion. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole (A racemic mixture of the R- and S-enantiomers). This is supplied as a Dual Delayed Release (DDR) formulation in a capsule for oral administration. DLP 30 mg capsule contains a mixture of two types of enteric-coated granules with different pH-dependent dissolution profiles. DLP 30 mg is a PPI that suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell. By acting specifically on the proton pump, DLP 30 mg blocks the final step of acid production.</p><p><strong>Dosage of DLP 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis and Relief of Heartburn</strong>: 30 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive GERD</strong>: 30 mg once daily for 4 weeks</li><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 60 mg once daily for up to 8 weeks</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of DLP 30 mg</strong></p><p> DLP 30 mg can be taken without regard to food. It should be swallowed whole. Alternatively, DLP 30 mg capsules can be administered as follows:</p><ul><li>Open capsule and sprinkle intact granules on one tablespoon of food. Swallow immediately. Granules should not be chewed.</li><li>If a capsule is missed at its usual time, it should be taken as soon as possible. But if it is too close to the time of the next dose, only the prescribed dose should be taken at the appointed time. A double dose should not be taken.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of DLP 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With Medicine</strong>: Atazanavir, Warfarin, Tacrolimus, Clopidogrel, Methotrexate.</li><li><strong>With Food &amp; Others</strong>: No data available.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> DLP 30 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of DLP 30 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and flatulence.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There are no adequate or well-controlled studies in pregnant women with DLP 30 mg. Exposure in clinical trials was very limited. DLP 30 mg should not be administered to pregnant women unless the expected benefits outweigh the potential risks. It is not known whether DLP 30 mg is excreted in human milk. However, DLP 30 mg and its metabolites are excreted in the milk of rats. As many drugs are excreted in human milk, DLP 30 mg should not be given to nursing mothers unless its use is considered essential.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Gastric Malignancy</li><li>Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea</li><li>Bone fracture</li><li>Hypomagnesemia</li><li>Concomitant use of DLP 30 mg with Methotrexate</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of DLP 30 mg</strong></p><p> There have been no reports of a significant overdose of DLP 30 mg. Multiple doses of DLP 30 mg (120 mg) and a single dose of DLP 30 mg (300 mg) did not result in death or other severe adverse events.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C temperature and in a dry place, protected from light. Keep all medicines out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary for elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: Safety and effectiveness of DLP 30 mg in patients below 12 years of age have not been established yet.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary for patients with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No adjustment of DLP 30 mg is necessary for patients with mild hepatic impairment. A maximum daily dose of 30 mg for patients with moderate hepatic impairment may be considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> DLP 30 mg delayed-release capsule is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI) which suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell. By acting specifically on the proton pump, DLP 30 mg blocks the final step of acid production. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole (A racemic mixture of the R- and S-enantiomers). DLP 30 mg is supplied as a Dual Delayed Release (DDR) formulation in a capsule for oral administration. This capsule contains a mixture of two types of enteric-coated granules with different pH-dependent dissolution profiles. The dual delayed-release formulation in DLP 30 mg gives a plasma concentration-time profile with two distinct peaks; the first peak occurs 1 to 2 hours after administration, followed by a second peak within 4 to 5 hours. After oral administration, mean Cmax and AUC values of DLP 30 mg increased approximately dose proportionally. DLP 30 mg is extensively metabolized in the liver and excreted by urine.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category B. There is no adequate and well-controlled studies with DLP 30 mg in pregnant women. There is no adequate and well-controlled studies with DLP 30 mg in lactating mothers.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in Children &amp; Adolescents</strong>: Safety and effectiveness of DLP 30 mg in patients below 12 years of age have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No dose adjustment is necessary for elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment of DLP 30 mg is necessary for patients with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment for DLP 30 mg is necessary for patients with mild hepatic impairment. A maximum daily dose of 30 mg should be considered for patients with moderate hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further modifications or clarifications!</p>', 'active', '1560_1742336446.jpg', 37, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 03:20:46', '2025-03-19 03:20:46'),
(3458, 'Pixan', 'Apixaban', 25.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pixan 5 mg</strong></p><p> Pixan 5 mg is a factor Xa inhibitor indicated:</p><ul><li>To reduce the risk of stroke and systemic embolism in patients with nonvalvular atrial fibrillation</li><li>For the prophylaxis of deep vein thrombosis (DVT), which may lead to pulmonary embolism (PE), in patients who have undergone hip or knee replacement surgery</li><li>For the treatment of DVT and PE, and for the reduction in the risk of recurrent DVT and PE following initial therapy</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-coagulants, Anti-platelet drugs, Fibrinolytics (Thrombolytics), Oral Anti-coagulants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Pixan 5 mg acts by inhibiting coagulation, thus preventing the development of blood clots. As a result of FXa inhibition, Pixan 5 mg prolongs clotting tests such as prothrombin time (PT), INR, and activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT). However, changes observed in these clotting tests at the expected therapeutic dose are small, subject to high variability, and not useful in monitoring the anticoagulation effect of Pixan 5 mg.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Pixan 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Reduction of risk of stroke and systemic embolism in nonvalvular atrial fibrillation:</strong> The recommended dose is 5 mg orally twice daily. In patients with at least 2 of the following characteristics (age ≥80 years, body weight ≤60 kg, or serum creatinine ≥1.5 mg/dL), the recommended dose is 2.5 mg orally twice daily.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of DVT following hip or knee replacement surgery:</strong> The recommended dose is 2.5 mg orally twice daily.</li><li><strong>Treatment of DVT and PE:</strong> The recommended dose is 10 mg taken orally twice daily for 7 days, followed by 5 mg taken orally twice daily.</li><li><strong>Reduction in the risk of recurrent DVT and PE following initial therapy:</strong> The recommended dose is 2.5 mg taken orally twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Pixan 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Recommended Dose:</strong> The recommended dose for most patients is 5 mg taken orally twice daily.</li><li><strong>Dosage Adjustments:</strong> The recommended dose is 2.5 mg twice daily in patients with any 2 of the following characteristics: age ≥80 years, body weight ≤60 kg, serum creatinine ≥1.5 mg/dL.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 and P-gp inhibitors:</strong> When coadministered with strong dual inhibitors of CYP3A4 and P-gp (e.g., ketoconazole, itraconazole, ritonavir, clarithromycin), the recommended dose is 2.5 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Missed Dose:</strong> If a dose is not taken at the scheduled time, it should be taken as soon as possible on the same day. The twice-daily administration should then be resumed. The dose should not be doubled to make up for a missed dose.</li><li><strong>Discontinuation for Surgery and Other Interventions:</strong> Pixan 5 mg should be discontinued at least 48 hours before elective surgery or invasive procedures with a moderate or high risk of significant bleeding. For procedures with a low risk of bleeding, discontinuation at least 24 hours prior is recommended.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Switching from or to Pixan 5 mg:From warfarin to Pixan 5 mg:</strong> Discontinue warfarin and start Pixan 5 mg when the INR is below 2.0.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>From Pixan 5 mg to warfarin:</strong> Since Pixan 5 mg affects INR, discontinue Pixan 5 mg and start both a parenteral anticoagulant and warfarin at the next scheduled dose. The parenteral anticoagulant should be discontinued when INR reaches an acceptable range.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Between Pixan 5 mg and anticoagulants other than warfarin:</strong> Discontinue one and start the other at the next scheduled dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Pixan 5 mg</strong></p><p> Pixan 5 mg is a substrate of both CYP3A4 and P-gp.</p><ul><li><strong>Inhibitors of CYP3A4 and P-gp</strong> increase exposure to Pixan 5 mg, raising the risk of bleeding.</li><li><strong>Inducers of CYP3A4 and P-gp</strong> decrease exposure to Pixan 5 mg, increasing the risk of stroke.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Pixan 5 mg is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Active pathological bleeding</li><li>Severe hypersensitivity reaction to Pixan 5 mg (i.e., anaphylactic reactions)</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Pixan 5 mg</strong></p><p> Pixan 5 mg can cause skin rash or severe allergic reactions.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Treatment may increase the risk of hemorrhage during pregnancy and delivery. Pixan 5 mg should only be used if the potential benefit outweighs the risk.</li><li><strong>Nursing Mothers:</strong> It is unknown whether Pixan 5 mg or its metabolites are excreted in human milk. Rats excrete Pixan 5 mg in milk (12% of the maternal dose). Women should either discontinue breastfeeding or discontinue Pixan 5 mg therapy, based on its importance to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Increased Risk of Stroke with Discontinuation:</strong> Stopping Pixan 5 mg without adequate alternative anticoagulation increases the risk of thrombotic events. During transition to warfarin, increased stroke risk has been observed. If discontinuation is necessary, consider coverage with another anticoagulant.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Pixan 5 mg</strong></p><p> There is no antidote for Pixan 5 mg overdose, which increases the risk of bleeding. Activated charcoal may help in overdose management.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a dry place below 30°C. Protect from light and keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No dose adjustment is required for mild impairment. Dosing recommendations cannot be provided for moderate impairment, and Pixan 5 mg is not recommended for severe hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Adjustments for moderate impairment are described above. No data is available for patients with creatinine clearance &lt;15 ml/min or on dialysis.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> In clinical studies, &gt;69% of subjects were 65 or older, and &gt;31% were 75 or older. The effects of Pixan 5 mg on stroke risk and major bleeding were maintained in geriatric subjects.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Anti-coagulants, Anti-platelet drugs, Fibrinolytics (Thrombolytics), Oral Anti-coagulants</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Pixan 5 mg acts by inhibiting coagulation and preventing blood clot formation. It prolongs clotting tests (PT, INR, aPTT), but these changes are small and not useful for monitoring its anticoagulation effect.</p>', 'active', '128_1742336592.jpg', 64, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 03:23:12', '2025-03-19 03:23:12'),
(3459, 'Veripel SR', 'Mebeverine Hydrochloride', 9.03, 8, 'Capsule', '8 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Veripel SR 200 mg</strong></p><p> Mebeverine is indicated for the:</p><ul><li>Symptomatic treatment of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS)</li><li>Chronic irritable colon</li><li>Spastic constipation</li><li>Mucous colitis</li><li>Colicky abdominal pain</li><li>Persistent non-specific diarrhoea</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anticholinergics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Mebeverine is a musculotropic antispasmodic agent used to relieve cramps or spasms of the stomach and intestine (gut). It is particularly useful in treating Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) and similar conditions. It works by relaxing the muscles and helping to restore the normal movement of the gut.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Veripel SR 200 mg</strong></p><p> For adults, elderly, and children over 10 years:</p><ul><li><strong>Mebeverine Tablet:</strong> 1 tablet 3 times daily.</li><li><strong>Mebeverine 200 SR Capsule:</strong> 1 capsule 2 times daily.</li></ul><p>This is most effective when taken 20 minutes before meals. After several weeks, when the desired effect has been obtained, the dosage may be gradually reduced.</p><p><strong>Missed Dose:</strong> If a dose of this medicine is missed, it should be taken as soon as possible. However, if it is almost time for the next dose, skip the missed dose and maintain the regular dosing schedule. The dose should not be doubled to compensate for the missed dose.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Veripel SR 200 mg</strong></p><p> For adults, elderly, and children over 10 years:</p><ul><li><strong>Veripel SR 200 mg 135 mg tablet:</strong> 1 tablet 3 times daily.</li><li><strong>Veripel SR 200 mg 200 mg capsule:</strong> 1 capsule 2 times daily.</li></ul><p>This is most effective when taken 20 minutes before meals. After several weeks, when the desired effect has been obtained, the dosage may be gradually reduced.</p><p><strong>Missed Dose:</strong> If a dose is missed, it should be taken as soon as possible. However, if it is almost time for the next dose, skip the missed dose and maintain the regular dosing schedule. The dose should not be doubled to compensate for the missed dose.</p><p><strong>Use in Children:</strong> Mebeverine is not recommended for children under 10 years.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Veripel SR 200 mg</strong></p><p> Not known.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to the drug or any other ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Veripel SR 200 mg</strong></p><p> Generally, Mebeverine is well tolerated. However, a few side effects like skin rash, urticaria, and angioedema may appear.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> No teratogenicity has been shown in animal experiments. However, the usual precautions concerning the administration of any drug during pregnancy should be exercised. Mebeverine does not excrete in breast milk after administering a therapeutic dose.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Caution should be exercised in porphyria or allergic reactions to this or any other medicine of this group.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Veripel SR 200 mg</strong></p><p> On theoretical grounds, it may be predicted that CNS excitability will occur in case of overdosage. No specific antidote is known; gastric lavage and symptomatic treatment are recommended.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Do not store above 30°C. Keep away from light and out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> <strong>Use in Children:</strong> Mebeverine is not recommended in children under 10 years.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Anticholinergics</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> No teratogenicity has been shown in animal experiments. However, the usual precautions concerning the administration of any drug during pregnancy should be exercised. Mebeverine does not excrete in breast milk after administering a therapeutic dose.</p>', 'active', '278_1742336842.jpg', 55, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 03:27:22', '2025-03-19 03:27:22'),
(3460, 'Pixorel', 'Apixaban', 25.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pixorel 5 mg</strong></p><p> Pixorel 5 mg is a factor Xa inhibitor indicated:</p><ul><li>To reduce the risk of stroke and systemic embolism in patients with nonvalvular atrial fibrillation.</li><li>For the prophylaxis of deep vein thrombosis (DVT), which may lead to pulmonary embolism (PE), in patients who have undergone hip or knee replacement surgery.</li><li>For the treatment of DVT and PE, and for the reduction in the risk of recurrent DVT and PE following initial therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-coagulants, Anti-platelet drugs, Fibrinolytics (Thrombolytics), Oral Anti-coagulants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Pixorel 5 mg acts by inhibiting coagulation, preventing the development of blood clots. As a result of FXa inhibition, Pixorel 5 mg prolongs clotting tests such as prothrombin time (PT), INR, and activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT). However, changes in these clotting tests at the expected therapeutic dose are small, subject to variability, and not useful in monitoring the anticoagulation effect of Pixorel 5 mg.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Pixorel 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Reduction of risk of stroke and systemic embolism in nonvalvular atrial fibrillation:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommended dose: 5 mg orally twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For patients with at least 2 of the following characteristics (age ≥80 years, body weight ≤60 kg, or serum creatinine ≥1.5 mg/dL), the recommended dose is 2.5 mg orally twice daily.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of DVT following hip or knee replacement surgery:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommended dose: 2.5 mg orally twice daily.</li><li><strong>Treatment of DVT and PE:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 mg orally twice daily for 7 days, followed by 5 mg orally twice daily.</li><li><strong>Reduction in the risk of recurrent DVT and PE following initial therapy:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2.5 mg orally twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Pixorel 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Recommended Dose:</strong> 5 mg orally twice daily.</li><li><strong>Dosage Adjustments:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2.5 mg twice daily for patients with any 2 of the following: age ≥80 years, body weight ≤60 kg, serum creatinine ≥1.5 mg/dL.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">When coadministered with strong CYP3A4 and P-gp inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole, itraconazole, ritonavir, clarithromycin), the recommended dose is 2.5 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Missed Dose:</strong> If a dose is missed, it should be taken as soon as possible on the same day, and twice-daily administration should be resumed. The dose should not be doubled to make up for a missed dose.</li><li><strong>Discontinuation for Surgery and Other Interventions:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Discontinue at least 48 hours before elective surgery or invasive procedures with a moderate or high risk of significant bleeding.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Discontinue at least 24 hours before elective surgery with a low risk of bleeding or where bleeding would be non-critical and easily controlled.</li><li><strong>Switching from or to Pixorel 5 mg:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>From warfarin to Pixorel 5 mg:</strong> Discontinue warfarin and start Pixorel 5 mg when INR is below 2.0.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>From Pixorel 5 mg to warfarin:</strong> Discontinue Pixorel 5 mg and begin both a parenteral anticoagulant and warfarin at the next scheduled dose, discontinuing the parenteral anticoagulant when INR reaches an acceptable range.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Between Pixorel 5 mg and other anticoagulants:</strong> Discontinue one and begin the other at the next scheduled dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Pixorel 5 mg</strong></p><p> Pixorel 5 mg is a substrate of both CYP3A4 and P-gp.</p><ul><li><strong>Inhibitors of CYP3A4 and P-gp</strong> increase exposure to Pixorel 5 mg, raising the risk of bleeding.</li><li><strong>Inducers of CYP3A4 and P-gp</strong> decrease exposure to Pixorel 5 mg, increasing the risk of stroke.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Pixorel 5 mg is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Active pathological bleeding.</li><li>Severe hypersensitivity reactions (e.g., anaphylactic reactions).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Pixorel 5 mg</strong></p><p> Pixorel 5 mg may cause skin rash or severe allergic reactions.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> No adequate and well-controlled studies exist in pregnant women. Pixorel 5 mg may increase the risk of hemorrhage during pregnancy and delivery. It should only be used if the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk.</li><li><strong>Nursing Mothers:</strong> It is unknown whether Pixorel 5 mg or its metabolites are excreted in human milk. Women should either discontinue breastfeeding or discontinue Pixorel 5 mg therapy, considering the importance of the drug to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Increased Risk of Stroke with Discontinuation:</strong> Discontinuing Pixorel 5 mg without adequate alternative anticoagulation increases the risk of thrombotic events.</li><li><strong>Transition from Pixorel 5 mg to warfarin:</strong> An increased rate of stroke was observed during the transition. If Pixorel 5 mg must be discontinued for reasons other than pathological bleeding, consider alternative anticoagulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Pixorel 5 mg</strong></p><p> There is no antidote for Pixorel 5 mg. Overdose increases the risk of bleeding. Activated charcoal may be useful in managing overdose.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place and store below 30°C. Protect from light and keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No dose adjustment is required for mild hepatic impairment. Pixorel 5 mg is not recommended in severe hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> No data are available for patients with creatinine clearance &lt;15 ml/min or those on dialysis.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> More than 69% of clinical study subjects were 65 and older, and more than 31% were 75 and older. The effects of Pixorel 5 mg on the risk of stroke and major bleeding compared to warfarin were maintained in geriatric patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Anti-coagulants, Anti-platelet drugs, Fibrinolytics (Thrombolytics), Oral Anti-coagulants</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Pixorel 5 mg inhibits coagulation, preventing blood clot formation. FXa inhibition prolongs clotting tests such as PT, INR, and aPTT, but changes are small, variable, and not useful for monitoring the anticoagulation effect.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Pixorel 5 mg may increase the risk of hemorrhage. Use only if the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk.</li><li><strong>Labor and Delivery:</strong> Safety and effectiveness have not been studied in clinical trials. Consider the risks of bleeding and stroke.</li><li><strong>Nursing Mothers:</strong> It is unknown whether Pixorel 5 mg is excreted in human milk. Women should either discontinue breastfeeding or discontinue Pixorel 5 mg therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Not recommended for patients with severe hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> No data for patients with creatinine clearance &lt;15 ml/min or those on dialysis.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> More than 69% of clinical study subjects were 65 and older, and more than 31% were 75 and older. The risk of stroke and major bleeding compared to warfarin remained consistent in geriatric patients.</li></ul>', 'active', '1293_1742336991.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 03:29:51', '2025-03-19 03:29:51'),
(3461, 'Tirzema', 'Tirzepatide', 1400.00, 1, 'x 2.5 mg pre-filled syringe', '2.5 mg pre-filled syringe', 'Injection', '2.5 mg plus 0.5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '268_1742338028.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 03:47:08', '2025-05-04 22:27:57'),
(3462, 'Hepatolin', 'Rohitakarista', 120.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '200 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '278_1742338197.webp', 5, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 03:49:57', '2025-05-03 18:54:48'),
(3463, 'Asha', 'Dehydroepiandrosterone', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Asha 25 mg</h3><p>Asha 25 mg, containing DHEA (Dehydroepiandrosterone) and DHEAS (Dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate), has not been supported by adequately powered, long-term clinical trials for therapeutic use. Reviews of clinical trials show no convincing evidence for its effectiveness in treating conditions such as:</p><ul><li>Cognitive function or physical strength improvement in elderly patients.</li><li>Postmenopausal symptoms in women.</li><li>Hyperlipidemia or insulin resistance.</li><li>Schizophrenia or cancer.</li></ul><p>However, some evidence suggests it may be helpful in:</p><ul><li>Women with diminished ovarian reserves.</li><li>Elderly women with osteoporosis (specific subpopulations).</li><li>Mild systemic lupus erythematosus.</li></ul><p>DHEA is recommended as third-line monotherapy or adjunctive therapy for <strong>major depressive disorder (MDD)</strong> according to the Canadian Network for Mood and Anxiety Treatments (CANMAT) clinical guidelines. There is also limited data suggesting it may act as an anxiolytic.</p><h3>Dosage of Asha 25 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Adrenal insufficiency</strong>: 50 mg/day for 3 months (replacement dose) or 200 mg/day for supraphysiological levels (pharmacological dose).</li><li><strong>Anorexia nervosa</strong>: 100 mg/day for 6 months (pilot study).</li><li><strong>Diminished ovarian reserve</strong>: 50-75 mg/day (divided doses) for assisted reproduction studies.</li><li><strong>Exercise training-induced muscle damage</strong>: 100 mg/day for 5 days in young men.</li><li><strong>Major depressive disorder (MDD)</strong>: 30-450 mg/day for 6-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Metabolic syndrome</strong>: 100 mg/day for 3 months in pre- and postmenopausal women.</li><li><strong>Postmenopausal women</strong>: 25 mg/day (minimizes androgenic side effects), although studies with 50 mg/day or more showed positive outcomes as hormonal replacement therapy.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Asha 25 mg</h3><p>DHEA supplementation can interfere with progesterone assays, causing false-positive increases in serum progesterone levels.</p><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Asha 25 mg should <strong>not</strong> be used in individuals with:</p><ul><li><strong>Breast or prostate cancer</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Asha 25 mg</h3><p>While DHEA is generally well tolerated in adrenal insufficiency, long-term studies are lacking. Possible side effects include:</p><ul><li>Mania and hypomania.</li><li>Acne, hirsutism, gynecomastia, testicular changes.</li><li>Increased blood pressure and decreased HDL (good cholesterol) levels.</li></ul><p>Other noted effects:</p><ul><li>Psychiatric symptoms like agitation, confusion, anxiety, paranoia, and suicidal thoughts.</li><li>Potential erythrocytosis (increased red blood cells).</li><li>Worsening of diabetes (may increase insulin resistance or sensitivity).</li><li>Exacerbation of liver dysfunction.</li><li>Worsening of polycystic ovarian syndrome (PCOS).</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>The sulfate derivative of DHEA acts as:</p><ul><li>A <strong>noncompetitive antagonist</strong> of GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) receptors.</li><li>A <strong>positive allosteric modulator</strong> of N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptors.</li></ul><p>In humans, DHEA has demonstrated:</p><ul><li>Neuroprotective, antioxidant, antihypertensive, and anti-inflammatory effects.</li><li>Peak serum DHEA levels occur 60 to 480 minutes after administration.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '235_1742338291.jpg', 17, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 03:51:31', '2025-03-19 03:51:31'),
(3464, 'Metazem', 'Coenzyme Q10 [Ubidecarenone]', 25.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Metazem 100 mg</strong></p><p>Metazem 100 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Co-enzyme Q10 deficiency and mitochondrial disorders</li><li>Congestive heart failure</li><li>Myocardial infarction</li><li>High blood pressure (hypertension) in combination with other medications</li><li>Preventing migraine headaches</li><li>Parkinson\'s disease</li><li>Improving the immune system</li><li>Infertility in men</li><li>Muscular dystrophy</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Supplements &amp; adjuvant therapy</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Co-enzyme Q10 is a vitamin-like substance found throughout the body, especially in the heart, liver, kidney, and pancreas. It is essential for the proper function of many organs and chemical reactions in the body. Co-enzyme Q10 helps provide energy to cells and has antioxidant activity.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Metazem 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Co-enzyme Q10 deficiency:</strong> 150 mg daily</li><li><strong>Mitochondrial disorders:</strong> 150-160 mg, or 2 mg/kg/day</li><li><strong>Heart failure in adults:</strong> 100 mg per day, divided into 2 or 3 doses</li><li><strong>Recent myocardial infarction:</strong> 120 mg daily in 2 divided doses</li><li><strong>High blood pressure:</strong> 120-200 mg per day, divided into 2 doses</li><li><strong>Isolated systolic hypertension:</strong> 60 mg twice daily</li><li><strong>Preventing migraine headache:</strong> 100 mg three times daily</li><li><strong>Parkinson\'s disease:</strong> 300 mg, 600 mg, 1200 mg, and 2400 mg per day in 3-4 divided doses</li><li><strong>Infertility in men:</strong> 200-300 mg per day</li><li><strong>Muscular dystrophy:</strong> 100 mg per day</li></ul><p>Dividing the total daily dose into smaller amounts taken two or three times a day, instead of a large dose all at once, can help reduce side effects.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Metazem 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Antihypertensive drugs:</strong> May cause blood pressure to go too low.</li><li><strong>Anticancer drugs:</strong> May decrease the effectiveness of some anticancer drugs.</li><li><strong>Warfarin:</strong> May decrease the effectiveness of warfarin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Metazem 100 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients with hypersensitivity to the active substance or to any of the excipients.</li><li>Patients scheduled for surgery in the next two weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Metazem 100 mg</strong></p><p>Co-enzyme Q10 is generally safe for most adults. Mild side effects can include:</p><ul><li>Stomach upset</li><li>Loss of appetite</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Allergic skin rashes in some people</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Co-enzyme Q10 should not be used during pregnancy or breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Co-enzyme Q10 may lower blood sugar levels, so caution should be taken in patients with diabetes or hypoglycemia.</li><li>Co-enzyme Q10 may decrease blood pressure, so caution is advised in patients with low blood pressure or those taking blood pressure medications.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul>', 'active', '1978_1742338476.jpg', 50, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 03:54:36', '2025-05-04 00:01:31'),
(3465, 'Qfol', '6s-5-Methyltetrahydrofolate [Bio-Active Folate]', 200.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '1000 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1853_1742338588.jpg', 17, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 03:56:28', '2025-05-04 15:00:04'),
(3466, 'Topirva XR', 'Topiramate', 7.00, 8, 'Capsule', '8 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Topirva XR 25 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Epilepsy:</strong> Topirva XR 25 mg is indicated as monotherapy in adults and children aged 6 years and above with newly diagnosed epilepsy who have generalized tonic-clonic seizures or partial seizures with or without secondarily generalized seizures. Topirva XR 25 mg is indicated as adjunctive therapy for adults and children over 2 years of age who are inadequately controlled on conventional first-line antiepileptic drugs for partial seizures with or without secondarily generalized seizures; seizures associated with Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome and primary generalized tonic-clonic seizures.</p><p><strong>Migraine:</strong> Topirva XR 25 mg is indicated in adults for the prophylaxis of migraine headaches. Prophylactic treatment of migraine may be considered in situations such as adults experiencing three or more migraine attacks per month and frequent migraine attacks that significantly interfere with the patient\'s daily routine. Continuing therapy should be reviewed every six months.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Topirva XR 25 mg is a sulfamate-substituted monosaccharide, related to fructose. It is quickly absorbed after oral use and enhances GABA-activated chloride channels. Additionally, Topirva XR 25 mg inhibits excitatory neurotransmission through actions on kainate and AMPA receptors, with a specific effect on GluR5 kainate receptors. It is also an inhibitor of carbonic anhydrase, particularly subtypes II and IV, but this action is weak and unlikely to be related to its anticonvulsant properties. This may account for the bad taste and the development of renal stones during treatment. Its potential mood-stabilizing effect appears to occur before its anticonvulsant properties at lower dosages.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Topirva XR 25 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Epilepsy: Monotherapy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 16 years:</strong> Start with 25 mg nightly for 1 week. Increase at 1- or 2-week intervals by increments of 25 or 50 mg/day, administered in two divided doses. The initial target dose is 100 mg/day, with a maximum recommended dose of 400 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Children aged 6-16 years:</strong> Start at 0.5 to 1 mg/kg nightly for the first week. Increase at 1- or 2-week intervals by 0.5 to 1 mg/kg/day, administered in two divided doses. The recommended target dose is 3 to 6 mg/kg/day, with rare cases up to 16 mg/kg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Epilepsy: Adjunctive Therapy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 16 years:</strong> Minimum effective dose is 200 mg/day. Usual total daily dose is 200-400 mg in two divided doses, with some patients requiring up to 800 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Children aged 2-16 years:</strong> Recommended daily dose is 5 to 9 mg/kg/day in two divided doses. Start at 25 mg nightly for the first week, then increase at 1- or 2-week intervals by 1 to 3 mg/kg/day (in two divided doses). Maximum studied dose is 30 mg/kg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Migraine</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 16 years:</strong> Start at 25 mg nightly for 1 week, then increase by 25 mg/day at 1-week intervals. Recommended daily dose is 100 mg/day in two divided doses, but some patients benefit at 50 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> Not studied in children under 16 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Topirva XR 25 mg</strong></p><p>Can be taken without regard to meals.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Topirva XR 25 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Antiepileptic drugs:</strong> No significant effect on plasma concentrations except in some cases where phenytoin levels may increase.</li><li><strong>CNS depressants:</strong> Use caution when combined with alcohol or other CNS depressants.</li><li><strong>Oral contraceptives:</strong> May reduce the effectiveness of oral contraceptives. Use alternative contraception if necessary.</li><li><strong>Lithium:</strong> Monitor lithium levels, as exposure may increase with Topirva XR 25 mg doses up to 600 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide (HCTZ):</strong> May lead to serum potassium reduction when combined with Topirva XR 25 mg.</li><li><strong>Metformin, pioglitazone, and glibenclamide:</strong> Monitor diabetic patients carefully, as Topirva XR 25 mg may affect glucose control.</li><li><strong>Valproic acid:</strong> Combined use may lead to hyperammonemia with or without encephalopathy.</li><li><strong>Nephrolithiasis risk:</strong> Avoid concurrent use with other agents that predispose to kidney stones.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to any component of this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Topirva XR 25 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Nausea, abdominal pain, dyspepsia, diarrhea, dry mouth, taste disturbance, weight loss, anorexia, paresthesia, hypoaesthesia, headache, fatigue, dizziness, speech disorder, drowsiness, insomnia, impaired memory, anxiety, depression, visual disturbances.</li><li><strong>Less common:</strong> Suicidal ideation.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Reduced sweetening perception (mainly in children), metabolic acidosis, alopecia.</li><li><strong>Very rare:</strong> Leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, serious skin reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Topirva XR 25 mg is classified as pregnancy category C and should be used only if the benefits outweigh the risks. It is not known if it is excreted in breast milk, so caution is advised in breastfeeding mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Gradual withdrawal is recommended to avoid seizures or increased seizure frequency.</li><li>May cause CNS-related side effects such as drowsiness, dizziness, and visual disturbances.</li><li>Patients should avoid driving or operating machinery if affected.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Topirva XR 25 mg</strong></p><p>Overdose can result in severe metabolic acidosis. Treatment includes gastric lavage, activated charcoal, supportive care, and possibly hemodialysis. Adequate hydration is essential.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> Reduce dose by half for patients with creatinine clearance &lt;70 ml/min.</li><li><strong>Hemodialysis patients:</strong> Additional dosing may be required during dialysis to maintain therapeutic levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Topirva XR 25 mg modulates voltage-dependent sodium channels, enhances GABA-A receptor activity, and reduces glutamate activity at AMPA and kainate receptors. These actions reduce neuronal excitability, preventing seizures and migraines. It also inhibits carbonic anhydrase, though the clinical significance is uncertain.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Should only be used if the benefits outweigh the risks to the fetus. Not recommended during breastfeeding.</p>', 'in-active', '960_1742339016.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 04:03:36', '2025-05-13 05:28:39');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3467, 'Zolamid Plus', 'Brinzolamide plus Brimonidine Tartrate', 495.00, 1, 'Drop', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Suspension', '1 percent plus 0.2 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zolamid Plus 1%+0.2%</strong></p><p> Zolamid Plus 1%+0.2% is indicated for the reduction of elevated intraocular pressure in patients with open-angle glaucoma or ocular hypertension.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for miotics and glaucoma</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Brinzolamide is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor that reduces intraocular pressure (IOP) by decreasing aqueous humor secretion in the ciliary processes of the eye. Brimonidine Tartrate is an alpha-2 adrenergic receptor agonist that reduces IOP by decreasing aqueous humor production and increasing uveoscleral outflow.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zolamid Plus 1%+0.2%</strong></p><p> Instill one drop of Zolamid Plus 1%+0.2% in the affected eye(s) three times daily. If more than one topical ophthalmic drug is being used, the drugs should be administered at least five minutes apart. Shake well before use.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Zolamid Plus 1%+0.2%</strong></p><p> In patients treated with Zolamid Plus 1%+0.2%, rare instances of drug interactions have occurred with high-dose salicylate therapy, CNS depressants, antihypertensives/cardiac glycosides, tricyclic antidepressants, and monoamine oxidase inhibitors. The potential for such drug interactions should be considered in patients receiving Zolamid Plus 1%+0.2%.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Zolamid Plus 1%+0.2% is contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to Brinzolamide, Brimonidine Tartrate, or any ingredient in the formulation. It is also contraindicated in neonates and infants (under the age of 2 years).</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zolamid Plus 1%+0.2%</strong></p><p> The most commonly reported side effects include blurred vision, eye irritation, bad taste, and dry mouth.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Brinzolamide and Brimonidine Tartrate ophthalmic suspension should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether Brinzolamide or Brimonidine Tartrate is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised while giving this ophthalmic suspension to a nursing mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Shake well before use. For ophthalmic use only. Contact lenses should be removed during instillation of Brinzolamide and Brimonidine Tartrate ophthalmic suspension but may be reinserted 15 minutes after instillation.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zolamid Plus 1%+0.2%</strong></p><p> Although no human data are available, electrolyte imbalance, development of an acidotic state, and possible nervous system effects may occur following an oral overdose of Brinzolamide. Serum electrolyte levels (particularly potassium) and blood pH levels should be monitored.</p><p>Very limited information exists on accidental ingestion of Brimonidine Tartrate in adults; the only adverse event reported to date has been hypotension. Symptoms of Brimonidine Tartrate overdose have been reported in neonates, infants, and children receiving Brimonidine Tartrate as part of medical treatment of congenital glaucoma or by accidental oral ingestion. Treatment of an oral overdose includes supportive and symptomatic therapy; a patent airway should be maintained.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at room temperature and protect from light. Do not touch the dropper tip to any surface. The contents should not be used more than one month after first opening the bottle. Shake well before use and do not freeze.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in children below the age of 2 years have not been established.</li><li><strong>Use in elderly patients:</strong> No overall differences in safety and effectiveness have been observed between elderly and other adult patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Drugs for miotics and glaucoma</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Brinzolamide is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor that reduces intraocular pressure (IOP) by decreasing aqueous humor secretion in the ciliary processes of the eye. Brimonidine Tartrate is an alpha-2 adrenergic receptor agonist that reduces IOP by decreasing aqueous humor production and increasing uveoscleral outflow.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Brinzolamide and Brimonidine Tartrate ophthalmic suspension should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether Brinzolamide or Brimonidine Tartrate is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised while giving this ophthalmic suspension to a nursing mother.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in children below the age of 2 years have not been established.</li><li><strong>Use in elderly patients:</strong> No overall differences in safety and effectiveness have been observed between elderly and other adult patients.</li></ul>', 'active', '1140_1742339415.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 04:10:15', '2025-03-19 04:10:15'),
(3468, 'Sefurox', 'Cefuroxime Axetil', 45.31, 4, 'Tablet', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sefurox 500 mg</strong></p><p> It is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive bacteria, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Maxillary Sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non-beta-lactamase producing strains)</li><li><strong>Lower Respiratory Tract Infections</strong>, including pneumonia, caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp., Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>E. coli</em></li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Exacerbation of Chronic Bronchitis</strong> and <strong>Secondary Bacterial Infections of Acute Bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains), or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains)</li><li><strong>Skin and Skin-Structure Infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></li><li><strong>Bone and Joint Infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Gonorrhoea</strong> caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease (erythema migrans)</strong> caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime is a well-characterized and effective antibacterial agent with broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against a wide range of common pathogens, including β-lactamase producing strains. It has good stability against bacterial β-lactamase and is consequently active against many ampicillin-resistant and amoxicillin-resistant strains.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Sefurox 500 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Tablet or Suspension</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adolescents and Adults (13 years and older):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis: 250 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis: 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infections: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated urinary tract infections: 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated gonorrhoea: 1000 mg single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Community-acquired pneumonia: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">MDR Typhoid Fever: 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Early Lyme disease: 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li><li><strong>Paediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis: 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute otitis media: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Impetigo: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Parenteral</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">750 mg three times daily by IM or IV injection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In severe infections, dose can be increased up to 1.5 g three times daily by IV injection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In very severe cases, the frequency may be increased to four times daily, giving total daily doses of 3 to 6 g.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pneumonia: 1.5 g IV injection twice daily for 2-3 days, followed by 500 mg twice daily (oral) for 7-10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gonorrhoea: 1.5 g as a single dose (as 2 x 750 mg IM injections at different sites).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Meningitis: 3 g IV injection three times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Bone and joint infections: 1.5 g IV injection four times daily.</li><li><strong>Children (above 3 months of age):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">30-100 mg/kg/day given in 3 or 4 divided doses. A dose of 60 mg/kg/day is appropriate for most infections.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Meningitis: 200-240 mg/kg/day IV in 3 or 4 divided doses, reduced to 100 mg/kg/day after 3 days or upon clinical improvement.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Bone and joint infections: 150 mg/kg/day (not exceeding the adult dose), divided every 8 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Sefurox 500 mg</strong></p><p> The use of freshly reconstituted solution is recommended. However, it maintains potency for at least 24 hours at room temperature or 48 hours at 5°C.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Sefurox 500 mg</strong></p><p> No potentially hazardous interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime is contraindicated in patients with a known allergy to Cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sefurox 500 mg</strong></p><p> Adverse effects occur infrequently and are generally mild and transient. Reported effects include rashes and gastrointestinal disturbances. Prolonged use may lead to overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms (e.g., <em>Candida</em>).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> US FDA pregnancy category B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Cefuroxime is excreted in human milk, so caution should be exercised when administering it to nursing women.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients receiving concurrent treatment with potent diuretics or those with a history of colitis.</li><li>Cephalosporins may be given safely to penicillin-hypersensitive patients, although cross-reactions have been reported.</li><li>At recommended doses, Cefuroxime is unlikely to pose a problem.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For 750 mg intramuscular injection:</strong> Add 3 ml of water for injection to the vial and shake gently.</li><li><strong>For 750 mg intravenous injection:</strong> Add 8 ml of water for injection and shake gently. The solution should be injected slowly into a vein over 3-5 minutes.</li><li><strong>For 1.5 g intravenous injection:</strong> Add 16 ml of water for injection and shake gently. Inject slowly into a vein over 3-5 minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime has broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against a wide range of common pathogens, including β-lactamase producing strains. It is stable against bacterial β-lactamase and is effective against many ampicillin-resistant and amoxicillin-resistant strains.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> US FDA pregnancy category B. It should only be used in pregnancy if clearly needed. Cefuroxime is excreted in human milk, so caution is advised when administering it to nursing mothers.</p>', 'active', '1583_1742339593.jpg', 45, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 04:13:13', '2025-03-19 04:13:13'),
(3469, 'Spaflex', 'Baclofen', 4.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Spaflex 5 mg</strong></p><p> Spaflex 5 mg is indicated for the treatment of spasticity, including flexor spasms, associated pain, clonus, muscular rigidity, as well as skeletal muscle spasms resulting from conditions such as multiple sclerosis, rheumatic disorders, spinal cord injuries, other spinal cord diseases, cerebrovascular accidents, neoplastic, or degenerative brain diseases.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Centrally acting Skeletal Muscle Relaxants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Spaflex 5 mg is an effective muscle relaxant and antispastic agent that primarily acts at the spinal level. While its exact mode of action is not fully understood, Spaflex 5 mg works by stimulating GABAB receptors, which inhibits the release of glutamate and aspartate, thereby affecting both monosynaptic and polysynaptic reflexes.</p><p>It may also have central nervous system (CNS) depressant effects at intraspinal sites. Notably, Spaflex 5 mg does not impact neuromuscular transmission. Spaflex 5 mg exhibits an antinociceptive (pain-relieving) effect, although its clinical significance remains uncertain.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Spaflex 5 mg</strong></p><p> Spaflex 5 mg should be taken with a small amount of liquid during meals. The dosing regimen for Spaflex 5 mg varies for adults and children.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> Typically administered in divided doses, preferably three times a day. The aim is to use the lowest effective dose. Therapy usually begins with a low dose and is then increased gradually to achieve the best response, typically within the range of 30-80 mg daily. Additional increases may be necessary but should generally not exceed a maximum daily dose of 80 mg.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> Treatment should begin with an extremely low dose, such as 0.3 mg/kg per day, divided into multiple doses. The dose can then be cautiously increased at 1-2 week intervals until it meets the child\'s specific needs. The usual maintenance dose typically falls within the range of 0.75 to 2 mg/kg of body weight per day.</li><li><strong>Children over 10 years of age:</strong> A maximum daily dose of 2.5 mg/kg of body weight may be considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Spaflex 5 mg</strong></p><p> Increased sedation may occur when Spaflex 5 mg is taken alongside agents that affect the central nervous system, alcohol, or synthetic opiates. This also elevates the risk of respiratory depression. When Spaflex 5 mg is combined with antihypertensives, it\'s likely to amplify the drop in blood pressure. Adjustments to the antihypertensive medication dosage may be necessary.</p><p>Concurrently administering Spaflex 5 mg with tricyclic antidepressants may intensify Spaflex 5 mg\'s pharmacological effects, leading to pronounced muscular hypotonia. In individuals with Parkinson\'s disease receiving Spaflex 5 mg along with levodopa and carbidopa, there have been reports of mental confusion, hallucinations, headaches, nausea, and agitation.</p><p>When MAO inhibitors and Spaflex 5 mg are used together, it may result in increased CNS depressant effects. Caution is advised, and the dosage of one or both agents should be adjusted accordingly. Care should also be taken when administering Spaflex 5 mg in conjunction with magnesium sulfate or other neuromuscular blocking agents, as there is a theoretical potential for a synergistic effect.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Spaflex 5 mg is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to any component of this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Spaflex 5 mg</strong></p><p> The common adverse reactions associated with Spaflex 5 mg include drowsiness, dizziness, weakness, and fatigue.</p><p>More serious adverse reactions include:</p><ul><li><strong>Central nervous system effects:</strong> Headaches, insomnia, euphoria, excitement, depression, confusion, hallucinations, paraesthesia, nightmares, muscle pain, tinnitus, slurred speech, coordination disorders, tremors, rigidity, dystonia, ataxia, blurred vision, nystagmus, strabismus, miosis, mydriasis, diplopia, dysarthria, epileptic seizures, and respiratory depression.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular reactions:</strong> Hypotension, dyspnoea, palpitations, chest pain, and syncope.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal reactions:</strong> Nausea, constipation, dry mouth, anorexia, taste disorders, abdominal pain, vomiting, diarrhea, and positive tests for occult blood in stool.</li><li><strong>Genitourinary reactions:</strong> Urinary frequency, enuresis, urinary retention, dysuria, impotence, inability to ejaculate, nocturia, and hematuria.</li><li><strong>Other reactions:</strong> Rash, pruritus, ankle edema, excessive perspiration, weight gain, nasal congestion, visual disturbances, hepatic function disorders, and a paradoxical increase in spasticity.</li></ul><p>Muscular hypotonia, or low muscle tone, can also occur with Spaflex 5 mg. This is usually relieved by adjusting the dosage.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy category B3:</strong> The safety of using Spaflex 5 mg during pregnancy has not been established, as it can cross the placental barrier. Administration to pregnant women should only occur when the physician concludes that the potential benefits outweigh the possible risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Spaflex 5 mg is excreted in breast milk, but available evidence suggests that the quantities are so minimal that no adverse effects on the infant are expected.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Lower doses (approximately 5 mg per day) should be considered for patients with impaired renal function or those undergoing chronic hemodialysis.</li><li>Patients with spasticity who also suffer from psychotic disorders, schizophrenia, depressive or manic disorders, or confusional states should be treated cautiously and closely monitored.</li><li>In patients with epilepsy and muscle spasticity, Spaflex 5 mg may be used under appropriate supervision, provided that adequate anticonvulsive therapy is continued. Lowering the convulsion threshold may occur, and seizures have been reported after the cessation of Spaflex 5 mg therapy or in cases of overdose.</li><li>Caution should be exercised in patients with a history of peptic ulcers, cerebrovascular diseases, or hepatic, renal, or respiratory failure.</li><li>Vigilant monitoring of respiratory and cardiovascular function is essential, especially in those with cardiopulmonary disease and respiratory muscle weakness.</li><li>While Spaflex 5 mg treatment may improve neurogenic disturbances affecting bladder emptying, individuals with preexisting sphincter hypertonia may experience acute retention of urine in these circumstances.</li><li>Spaflex 5 mg has not significantly benefited patients with stroke, and they have shown poor tolerance to the medication.</li><li>For individuals with hepatic diseases or diabetes mellitus, appropriate laboratory tests should be conducted periodically to ensure that there are no medication-induced changes in these underlying conditions.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Spaflex 5 mg</strong></p><p> Gastric lavage is important in case of severe overdose.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Centrally acting Skeletal Muscle Relaxants</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Spaflex 5 mg inhibits both monosynaptic and polysynaptic reflexes at the spinal level by stimulating the GABAB receptors, which in turn inhibits the release of glutamate and aspartate. Additionally, it may act at intraspinal sites, leading to CNS depression. Spaflex 5 mg also demonstrates an antinociceptive effect.</p>', 'active', '1281_1742339813.jpeg', 58, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 04:16:53', '2025-03-19 04:16:53'),
(3470, 'Citra-K', 'Potassium Citrate', 10.00, 30, 'Tablet', '30 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '1080 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Citra-K 1080 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Tubular Acidosis (RTA) With Calcium Stones:</strong> Potassium citrate is indicated for the management of renal tubular acidosis.</li><li><strong>Hypocitraturic Calcium Oxalate Nephrolithiasis of Any Etiology:</strong> Potassium citrate is indicated for the management of hypocitraturic calcium oxalate nephrolithiasis.</li><li><strong>Uric Acid Lithiasis With or Without Calcium Stones:</strong> Potassium citrate is indicated for the management of uric acid lithiasis with or without calcium stones.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Prevention of repeated kidney stone formation.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> When Citra-K 1080 mg is given orally, the metabolism of absorbed citrate produces an alkaline load. The induced alkaline load increases urinary pH and raises urinary citrate by augmenting citrate clearance without significantly altering ultrafilterable serum citrate. This leads to urine that is less conducive to the crystallization of stone-forming salts such as calcium oxalate, calcium phosphate, and uric acid.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Citra-K 1080 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Severe Hypocitraturia:</strong> Initial dosage of 60 mEq/day (30 mEq twice daily or 20 mEq three times daily with meals or within 30 minutes after meals or bedtime snack).</li><li><strong>Mild to Moderate Hypocitraturia:</strong> Initial dosage of 30 mEq/day (15 mEq twice daily or 10 mEq three times daily within 30 minutes after meals or bedtime snack).</li><li>Monitor serum electrolytes, creatinine, and complete blood counts every four months. Treatment should be discontinued if there is hyperkalemia, a significant rise in serum creatinine, or a significant drop in hemoglobin/hematocrit.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Citra-K 1080 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients with hyperkalemia or conditions predisposing to hyperkalemia.</li><li>Patients with delayed gastric emptying, esophageal compression, intestinal obstruction, or stricture.</li><li>Patients with peptic ulcer disease.</li><li>Patients with active urinary tract infections.</li><li>Patients with renal insufficiency (GFR &lt;0.7 ml/kg/min).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Citra-K 1080 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and stomach pain. Taking the medication after meals may help reduce these effects. Serious side effects include gastrointestinal issues (bleeding, blockage, perforation) and hyperkalemia.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C:</strong> There are no adequate animal studies or well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Use only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Nursing Mothers:</strong> It is unknown if Citra-K 1080 mg affects potassium ion content in breast milk. Use only if clearly needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid use in patients with Addison’s disease, uncontrolled diabetes, severe heart disease, peptic ulcers, or severe kidney disease.</li><li>Use cautiously in patients with severe diarrhea, heart conditions, kidney disease, and gastrointestinal disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Citra-K 1080 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose may lead to hyperkalemia, which can cause muscle paralysis and cardiac arrest.</li><li>Treatment includes monitoring electrolytes, eliminating potassium-rich foods and medications, administering calcium gluconate, insulin with dextrose, and in severe cases, hemodialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in children have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Prevention of repeated kidney stone formation.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Citra-K 1080 mg increases urinary citrate and pH, reducing calcium oxalate and uric acid stone formation. It achieves this by modifying renal citrate handling rather than simply increasing citrate filtration.</p>', 'active', '168_1742340080.jpg', 8, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 04:21:20', '2025-03-19 04:21:20'),
(3471, 'GensuPen', 'Insulin Human [rDNA] plus Isophane Insulin Human', 700.00, 1, 'Flexpen', '3 ml flexpen', 'Flexpen', '30 percent  plus 70 percent in 100 IU per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of GensuPen 30%+70% in 100 IU/ml</strong></p><p> Treatment of all type 1 diabetic patients. Treatment for those with type 2 diabetes whose blood sugar cannot be sufficiently controlled by diet or oral hypoglycemic medications. For the initial stabilization of diabetes in people with diabetic ketoacidosis, hyperosmolar non-ketotic syndrome, and during stressful times like serious illnesses and major surgery for people with diabetes. Gestational diabetes treatment.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p> Isophane Insulin Human 30/70 Injection with Insulin Human (rDNA) Insulin Human (rDNA) USP 40 IU (equal to 1.388 mg) is present in each milliliter of solution as 30% Soluble Insulin Human (Regular) and 70% Isophane Insulin Human. Isophane Insulin Human 30/70 Injection with Insulin Human (rDNA) Insulin Human (rDNA) USP 100 IU (equal to 3.47 mg) is present in each milliliter of solution as 30% Soluble Insulin Human (Regular) and 70% Isophane Insulin Human. 50/50 Injection of Insulin Human (rDNA) and Isophane Insulin Human (rDNA) USP 100 IU (equal to 3.47 mg) is present in each milliliter of solution as 50% Soluble Insulin Human (Regular) and 50% Isophane Insulin Human.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Insulin with a Medium Action.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Human insulin is produced using recombinant DNA technology as Insulin Human (rDNA). It works and is structurally identical to natural insulin. Insulin controls how glucose is metabolized and promotes the absorption and use of glucose by the liver, muscles, and fat. By promoting glycogenesis and preventing gluconeogenesis, it also reduces blood sugar levels. After injection, insulin human (rDNA) 30/70 and insulin human (rDNA) 50/50 begin to work within 30 minutes, reach their peak levels between 2 and 8 hours later, and persist for roughly 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage of GensuPen 30%+70% in 100 IU/ml</strong></p><p> The average range of total daily insulin requirement for maintenance therapy in type 1 diabetic patients lies between 0.5 and 1.0 IU/kg. In pre-pubertal children, it usually varies from 0.7 to 1.0 IU/kg, whereas in insulin-resistant cases, e.g., during puberty or due to obesity, the daily insulin requirement may be substantially higher. Initial dosages for type 2 diabetic patients are often lower, e.g., 0.3 to 0.6 IU/kg/day. The dosage form, the dosage, and the administration time of the insulin are different due to the individual differences of each patient. In addition, the dosage is also affected by food, working style, and exercising intensity. Therefore, patients should use the insulin under a doctor\'s instruction.</p><p><strong>Administration of GensuPen 30%+70% in 100 IU/ml</strong></p><p> Within 30 minutes of an injection, a meal or snack containing carbs should be had. Subcutaneous injections are given in the upper arm, thigh, buttock, or abdominal wall. More quickly than from other injection locations, the abdominal wall receives a subcutaneous injection. Never inject insulin human (rDNA) 30/70 or insulin human (rDNA) 50/50 intravenously.</p><p><strong>Getting ready for use:</strong></p><ul><li>Sanitize your hands.</li><li>To evenly mix the solution and see if the insulin looks normal, gently shake or rotate the vial.</li><li>When using a brand-new vial, remove the plastic protective cap and use an alcohol swab to clean the rubber plug.</li><li>Add air to your syringe in an amount equal to the required amount of insulin. Inject air by sticking the needle into the vial.</li><li>Draw the appropriate amount of insulin into the syringe while turning the bottle and syringe upside down.</li><li>Check to see if there are any bubbles left in the syringe before removing the needle. In that case, hold the syringe upright and tap it to release the air bubbles.</li></ul><p><strong>Injection site:</strong></p><p> Choose the region of the body with looser-fitting skin, such as the upper arm, thigh, buttock, or abdomen. Choose a location for each injection that is at least 1 cm away from the location of the preceding injection to prevent tissue injury.</p><p><strong>Injection method:</strong></p><ul><li>Wash the area of the skin where the injection will be made with alcohol.</li><li>Place the needle so that it forms a 45° angle with the skin. Injecting insulin requires puncturing the skin with a needle.</li><li>To ensure that the entire dosage is injected, keep the needle subcutaneous for at least 6 seconds.</li><li>After removing the needle, place mild pressure on the injection site for several seconds.</li><li>Avoid scratching the injection site.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of GensuPen 30%+70% in 100 IU/ml</strong></p><p> When taking oral contraceptives, thyroid hormone, adrenal cortical hormone, and other medications that can cause blood sugar levels to rise, you may need to take more insulin. Reduced insulin dosage is necessary when using antidepressants such as salicylate, sulfanilamide, and others that have hypoglycemic effects and cause a drop in blood sugar levels.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Patients with hypoglycemia or those who are allergic to insulin or any of the excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of GensuPen 30%+70% in 100 IU/ml</strong></p><p> The most frequent unfavorable consequence of insulin therapy is hypoglycemia, and its symptoms might strike rapidly. There aren\'t many occurrences of allergic reactions that cause red, puffy, or itchy skin. It typically goes away after a few days. In rare cases, factors other than insulin, such as disinfectant and improper injection technique, may be to blame for the allergy.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Because insulin cannot cross the placental barrier, there are no limits on using it to treat diabetes during pregnancy. Baby safety is unaffected by the mother\'s insulin medication.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Hyperglycemia may result from improper dosing or stopping the medication, particularly in type 1 diabetes. If the insulin dose is excessively high compared to the amount of insulin needed, hypoglycemia could result. Hypoglycemia can result from skipping meals or unexpectedly doing hard physical activity.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of GensuPen 30%+70% in 100 IU/ml</strong></p><p> Hypoglycemia during treatment may result from using too much insulin. Hypoglycemia between mild and moderate may strike suddenly. When hypoglycemia strikes, it is crucial to seek immediate medical attention. If you have hypoglycemia frequently, speak with your doctor about any potential adjustments to your therapy, diet, and/or exercise regimen that may be necessary to help you stay hypoglycemia-free.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keeping in a refrigerator, store between 2 and 8 degrees Celsius. Never freeze. If insulin is being used recently and does not need to be refrigerated, try to keep it in a cool area away from heat and light. The currently-in-use insulin can be stored at room temperature for a month.</p>', 'active', '1791_1742340469.jpg', 3, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 04:27:49', '2025-03-19 04:27:49'),
(3472, 'Spectra', 'Ciprofloxacin', 40.12, 1, 'Drop', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '0.3 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Spectra 0.3%</strong></p><p> Spectra 0.3% is indicated for the treatment of single infections or mixed infections caused by two or more susceptible organisms. It can also be used for infections caused by organisms resistant to other antibiotics, including Aminoglycosides, Penicillins, and Cephalosporins.</p><p>As antibacterial concentrations of Spectra 0.3% are obtained in serum, body tissues, and urine following oral administration, it has been suggested for use in treating a wide range of infections, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Severe systemic infections</strong>: Septicaemia, bacteraemia, peritonitis, infections in immunosuppressed patients with haematological or solid tumors, and infections in ICU patients (e.g., infected burns).</li><li><strong>Respiratory tract infections</strong>: Lobar and bronchopneumonia, acute and chronic bronchitis, acute exacerbation of cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis, empyema.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: Uncomplicated and complicated urethritis, cystitis, pyelonephritis, prostatitis, epididymitis.</li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: Infected ulcers, wound infections, abscesses, cellulitis, otitis externa, erysipelas, infected burns.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal infections</strong>: Enteric fever, infective diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Infections of the biliary tract</strong>: Cholangitis, cholecystitis, empyema of the gall bladder.</li><li><strong>Intra-abdominal infections</strong>: Peritonitis, intra-abdominal abscesses.</li><li><strong>Bone and joint infections</strong>: Osteomyelitis, septic arthritis.</li><li><strong>Pelvic infections</strong>: Salpingitis, endometritis, pelvic inflammatory diseases.</li><li><strong>Eye, ear, nose, and throat infections</strong>: Otitis media, sinusitis, mastoiditis, tonsillitis.</li><li><strong>Gonorrhoea</strong>: Including urethral, rectal, and pharyngeal gonorrhoea caused by beta-lactamase-producing organisms or organisms moderately sensitive to penicillin.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> 4-Quinolone preparations, Anti-diarrhoeal Antimicrobial drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Spectra 0.3% is a synthetic fluoroquinolone with bactericidal activity against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms. It inhibits bacterial DNA synthesis by binding to bacterial enzyme-DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV, which are responsible for DNA supercoiling.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Spectra 0.3%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 100-750 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: 250-500 mg twice daily, depending on severity.</li><li><strong>Respiratory tract infections</strong>: 250-500 mg twice daily (750 mg twice daily for <em>Streptococcus pneumonia</em>).</li><li><strong>Gonorrhoea</strong>: Single dose of 250 or 500 mg.</li><li><strong>Other infections</strong>: 500-750 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Cystic fibrosis</strong>: 750 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Impaired renal function</strong>: Adjust dose by reducing total daily dose by half in severe cases.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Children &amp; Adolescents</strong>: Use not recommended; if needed, 7.5-15 mg/kg/day in divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Duration of Treatment</strong></p><ul><li>Acute infections: 5-10 days. Continue treatment for three days after symptoms disappear.</li><li><strong>Extended-release tablet</strong>: 1000 mg once daily for three days in uncomplicated urinary tract infections.</li><li><strong>IV Infusion</strong>: Dosage varies based on infection type and severity.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Spectra 0.3%</strong></p><ul><li>Check for leaks before use.</li><li>Do not use if solution is cloudy or contains precipitate.</li><li>Follow standard IV administration guidelines.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Spectra 0.3%</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid concurrent use with magnesium/aluminum-containing antacids, sucralfate, calcium, iron, or zinc. These products may be taken two hours after or six hours before Spectra 0.3%.</li><li>Do not take with milk or dairy products alone, as absorption may be reduced. Dietary calcium in a meal does not significantly affect absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Spectra 0.3% or other quinolones.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Spectra 0.3%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Nausea, diarrhea, vomiting, dyspepsia, abdominal pain.</li><li><strong>CNS</strong>: Headache, dizziness, confusion, convulsions, risk of retinal detachment.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Rash, pruritus (rarely Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis).</li><li><strong>Others</strong>: Joint pain, mild photosensitivity, transient liver enzyme increase, risk of hypoglycemia, mental health effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Use only if potential benefits outweigh risks.</li><li>Excreted in human milk; not recommended for breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in patients with CNS disorders (arteriosclerosis, epilepsy).</li><li>Drink fluids liberally.</li><li>Avoid concurrent use with certain antacids, sucralfate, calcium, iron, and zinc.</li><li>Do not take with milk or yogurt alone.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Spectra 0.3%</strong></p><ul><li>May cause seizures, hallucinations, confusion, renal and hepatic impairment, crystalluria, hematuria, and reversible renal toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Not a first-choice drug for pediatric patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> 4-Quinolone preparations, Anti-diarrhoeal Antimicrobial drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Synthetic quinolone anti-infective agent.</li><li>Broad-spectrum activity against Gram-negative and some Gram-positive bacteria.</li><li>Bactericidal effect through DNA gyrase inhibition.</li><li>Rapid absorption from G.I. tract with a half-life of ~3.5 hours.</li><li>30-50% excreted unchanged in urine within 24 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Animal studies show no teratogenicity, but use in pregnancy is not recommended due to potential arthropathy.</li><li>Excreted in breast milk; not recommended for nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Not a first-choice drug for pediatric patients despite effectiveness in clinical trials.</li></ul>', 'active', '1569_1742340683.jpg', 28, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 04:31:23', '2025-03-19 04:31:23'),
(3473, 'Vertex', 'Ceftriaxone Sodium', 302.04, 1, 'x 2 gm vial', '2 gm vial', 'IV Injection', '2 gm/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vertex 2 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Vertex 2 gm/vial is indicated for the treatment of the following major infections:</p><ul><li>Lower respiratory tract infections</li><li>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media</li><li>Skin and skin structure infections</li><li>Urinary tract infections</li><li>Gonorrhea</li><li>Bacterial Septicemia</li><li>Bone and joint infections</li><li>Meningitis</li><li>Prevention of postoperative infections</li><li>Perioperative prophylaxis of infections associated with surgery</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Vertex 2 gm/vial is a 3rd generation broad-spectrum parenteral cephalosporin antibiotic. It has potent bactericidal activity against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms. Like other cephalosporins and penicillins, Vertex 2 gm/vial kills bacteria by interfering with the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall. Vertex 2 gm/vial has a high degree of stability in the presence of beta lactamases. A remarkable feature of Vertex 2 gm/vial is its relatively long plasma elimination half-life of about 6 to 9 hours, which makes single or once-daily dosage of the drug appropriate for most patients. Vertex 2 gm/vial is not metabolized in the body. About 40-65% of a dose of Vertex 2 gm/vial is excreted unchanged in the urine; the remainder is excreted in the bile and ultimately found in the feces as unchanged drug and microbiologically inactive compound. The drug is highly protein bound (95%).</p><p><strong>Dosage of Vertex 2 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: The usual dose is 1 to 2 gm by intravenous or intramuscular administration once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pneumonia, Bronchitis, Acute bacterial otitis media, Skin and skin structure infection, Urinary tract infections, Bacterial Septicemia, Bone and joint infections, Meningitis: 1 to 2 g IV or IM once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 4 gm/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated gonococcal infections: 250 mg IM as a single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Surgical prophylaxis: 1 g IV as a single dose 30 to 120 minutes before surgery</li><li><strong>Infants and Children (01 month or older)</strong>: The usual dose is 50 to 75 mg/kg intravenous or intramuscular administration once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pneumonia, Bronchitis, Skin and skin structure infection, Urinary tract infections, Bacterial Septicemia, Bone and joint infections: 50 to 75 mg/kg IV or IM once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 2 gm/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bacterial otitis media: 50 mg/kg IM in single dose; Maximum dose: 1 gm/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Meningitis: 100 mg/kg IV or IM in single daily dose or (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 4 gm/day</li><li><strong>Duration of therapy</strong>: Continue for more than 2 days after signs and symptoms of infection have disappeared. Usual duration is 4 to 14 days; in complicated infections, longer therapy may be required.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Vertex 2 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Preparation of Solutions for Intramuscular / Intravenous Injections</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Intramuscular Injection</strong>: 250 mg or 500 mg Vertex 2 gm/vial should be dissolved in 2 ml Lidocaine HCI 1% injection or 1 g Vertex 2 gm/vial in 3.5 ml of Lidocaine HCI 1% injection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Intravenous Injection</strong>: 250 mg or 500 mg Vertex 2 gm/vial should be dissolved in 5 ml of Water for injection or 1 g Vertex 2 gm/vial in 10 ml of Water for injection USP or 2 g Vertex 2 gm/vial in 20 ml of Water for injection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The injection should be administered over 2-4 minutes, by Intramuscular or Intravenous injection or by tubing infusion over a period of 30 minutes at concentration between 10 mg/mL and 40 mg/mL.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Before starting treatment through Vertex 2 gm/vial injection, patient tolerance test should be checked by administration of a test dose. (The use of freshly reconstituted solution is recommended. However, it maintains potency for at least 6 hours at room temperature or 24 hours at 5°C).</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Vertex 2 gm/vial</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vertex 2 gm/vial should not be given to patients with a history of hypersensitivity to cephalosporin antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Vertex 2 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Vertex 2 gm/vial is generally well tolerated. A few side effects such as gastro-intestinal effects including diarrhea, nausea and vomiting, stomatitis and glossitis; cutaneous reactions including rash, pruritus, urticaria, edema and erythema multiforme; hematologic reactions including eosinophilia, thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, anemia and neutropenia; hepatic reactions including elevations of SGOT or SGPT, bilirubinemia; CNS reactions including nervousness, confusion, sleep disturbances, headache, hyperactivity, convulsion, hypertonia and dizziness were reported. Local phlebitis occurs rarely following intravenous administration but can be minimized by slow injections over 2-4 minutes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Its safety in human pregnancy has not been established. Therefore, it should not be used in pregnancy unless absolutely indicated. Low concentrations of Vertex 2 gm/vial are excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Vertex 2 gm/vial is administered to a lactating mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>As with other cephalosporins, anaphylactic shock cannot be ruled out even if a thorough patient history is taken. Anaphylactic shock requires immediate countermeasures such as intravenous epinephrine followed by a glucocorticoid. In rare cases, shadows suggesting sludge have been detected by sonograms of the gallbladder. This condition was reversible on discontinuation or completion of Vertex 2 gm/vial therapy. Even if such findings are associated with pain, conservative, nonsurgical management is recommended. During prolonged treatment the blood picture should be checked at regular intervals.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Vertex 2 gm/vial</strong></p><p>There is no specific antidote. Treatment of overdosage should be symptomatic.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Vial store in a cool, dry place (below 30° C), away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p>Vertex 2 gm/vial must not be given to neonates if the neonates is premature and newborn (up to 28 days of age).</p>', 'active', '644_1742341311.jpg', 43, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 04:41:51', '2025-05-07 16:33:51');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3474, 'Ticstop', 'Tetrabenazine', 35.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ticstop 25 mg</strong></p><p> Ticstop 25 mg is indicated for the treatment of chorea associated with Huntington\'s disease.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Atypical neuroleptic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Prolongation of the QTc interval has been observed at doses of 50 mg. In rats, it has been observed that Ticstop 25 mg or its metabolites bind to melanin-containing tissues such as the eyes and skin. After a single oral dose of radiolabeled Ticstop 25 mg, radioactivity was still detected in eye and fur at 21 days post-dosing. Ticstop 25 mg is a reversible human vesicular monoamine transporter type 2 inhibitor (Ki = 100 nM). It acts within the basal ganglia and promotes depletion of monoamine neurotransmitters serotonin, norepinephrine, and dopamine from stores. It also decreases uptake into synaptic vesicles. Dopamine is required for fine motor movement, so the inhibition of its transmission is efficacious for hyperkinetic movement. Ticstop 25 mg exhibits weak in vitro binding affinity at the dopamine D2 receptor (Ki = 2100 nM).</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ticstop 25 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>General Dosing Considerations</strong>: The chronic daily dose of Ticstop 25 mg used to treat chorea associated with Huntington\'s disease (HD) is determined individually for each patient. When first prescribed, Ticstop 25 mg therapy should be titrated slowly over several weeks to identify a dose of Ticstop 25 mg that reduces chorea and is tolerated. Ticstop 25 mg can be administered without regard to food.</p><p><strong>Individualization of Dose</strong></p><p> <strong>Dosing Recommendations Up to 50 mg per day</strong>: The starting dose should be 12.5 mg per day given once in the morning. After one week, the dose should be increased to 25 mg per day given as 12.5 mg twice a day. Ticstop 25 mg should be titrated up slowly at weekly intervals by 12.5 mg daily, to allow the identification of a tolerated dose that reduces chorea. If a dose of 37.5 to 50 mg per day is needed, it should be given in a three times a day regimen. The maximum recommended single dose is 25 mg. If adverse reactions such as akathisia, restlessness, parkinsonism, depression, insomnia, anxiety, or sedation occur, titration should be stopped, and the dose should be reduced. If the adverse reaction does not resolve, consideration should be given to withdrawing Ticstop 25 mg treatment or initiating other specific treatment (e.g., antidepressants).</p><p><strong>Dosing Recommendations Above 50 mg per day</strong>: Patients who require doses of Ticstop 25 mg greater than 50 mg per day should be first tested and genotyped to determine if they are poor metabolizers (PMs) or extensive metabolizers (EMs) by their ability to express the drug-metabolizing enzyme, CYP2D6. The dose of Ticstop 25 mg should then be individualized accordingly to their status as PMs or EMs.</p><p> <strong>Extensive and Intermediate CYP2D6 Metabolizers</strong>: Genotyped patients who are identified as extensive (EMs) or intermediate metabolizers (IMs) of CYP2D6, who need doses of Ticstop 25 mg above 50 mg per day, should be titrated up slowly at weekly intervals by 12.5 mg daily, to allow the identification of a tolerated dose that reduces chorea. Doses above 50 mg per day should be given in a three times a day regimen. The maximum recommended daily dose is 100 mg, and the maximum recommended single dose is 37.5 mg. If adverse reactions such as akathisia, parkinsonism, depression, insomnia, anxiety, or sedation occur, titration should be stopped, and the dose should be reduced. If the adverse reaction does not resolve, consideration should be given to withdrawing Ticstop 25 mg treatment or initiating other specific treatment (e.g., antidepressants).</p><p><strong>Dosage of Ticstop 25 mg</strong></p><p> Ticstop 25 mg can be taken with or without food. Individualization of dose with careful weekly titration is required.</p><p> <strong>1st week\'s starting dose</strong>: 12.5 mg daily.</p><p> <strong>2nd week</strong>: 25 mg (12.5 mg twice daily).</p><p> Then slowly titrate at weekly intervals by 12.5 mg to a tolerated dose that reduces chorea.</p><p> Doses of 37.5 mg and up to 50 mg per day should be administered in three divided doses per day with a maximum recommended single dose not to exceed 25 mg.</p><p> Patients requiring doses above 50 mg per day should be genotyped for the drug-metabolizing enzyme CYP2D6 to determine if the patient is a poor metabolizer or an extensive metabolizer.</p><p> <strong>Maximum daily dose in poor metabolizers</strong>: 50 mg with a maximum single dose of 25 mg.</p><p> <strong>Maximum daily dose in extensive and intermediate metabolizers</strong>: 100 mg with a maximum single dose of 37.5 mg.</p><p> If serious adverse reactions occur, titration should be stopped, and the dose should be reduced. If the adverse reactions do not resolve, consider withdrawal of Ticstop 25 mg.</p><p> <strong>Pediatric &amp; geriatric use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established. The pharmacokinetics of Ticstop 25 mg and its primary metabolites have not been formally studied in geriatric subjects.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Ticstop 25 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>With medicine</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Strong CYP2D6 Inhibitors</strong>: A reduction in Ticstop 25 mg dose may be necessary when adding a strong CYP2D6 inhibitor (e.g., fluoxetine, paroxetine, quinidine) in patients maintained on a stable dose of Ticstop 25 mg.</li><li><strong>Reserpine</strong>: Reserpine binds irreversibly to VMAT2, and the duration of its effect is several days. At least 20 days should elapse after stopping reserpine before starting Ticstop 25 mg. Ticstop 25 mg and Reserpine should not be used concomitantly.</li></ul><p><strong>With food &amp; others</strong>: No interaction with food, can be taken with or without food.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Ticstop 25 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Actively suicidal, or those who have depression which is untreated or undertreated</li><li>Hepatic impairment</li><li>Concurrent use of monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) or reserpine</li><li>Concurrent use of Ticstop 25 mg or valbenazine</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ticstop 25 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Common</strong>: Most common adverse reactions (&gt;10% and at least 5% greater than placebo) were: Sedation/somnolence, fatigue, insomnia, depression, akathisia, anxiety/anxiety aggravated, and nausea.</p><p> <strong>Rare</strong>: Suicidal thoughts, neuroleptic malignant syndrome (a reaction characterized by fever, muscle rigidity, and confusion), and pneumonia.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> <strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Ticstop 25 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p> <strong>Nursing Mothers</strong>: It is not known whether Ticstop 25 mg or its metabolites are excreted in human milk. Since many drugs are excreted into human milk and because of the potential for serious adverse reactions in nursing infants from Ticstop 25 mg, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or to discontinue Ticstop 25 mg, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> The benefit and potential for adverse effects such as worsening mood, cognition, rigidity, and functional capacity should be periodically re-evaluated. The maximum daily dose should not exceed 50 mg/day, and the maximum single dose should not exceed 25 mg if administered in conjunction with a strong CYP2D6 inhibitor (e.g., fluoxetine, paroxetine). In case of neuroleptic malignant syndrome, the treatment should be discontinued. The dose should be reduced or discontinued if the patient experiences restlessness, agitation, akathisia, and parkinsonism. The treatment may impair the patient\'s ability to drive or operate complex machinery. Tetrabenazine is not recommended in combination with other drugs that prolong QTc.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ticstop 25 mg</strong></p><p> Signs and symptoms of overdosage of Ticstop 25 mg may include drowsiness, sweating, and hypothermia.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool &amp; dry place. Protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep all medicines out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: The safety and efficacy of Ticstop 25 mg in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: The pharmacokinetics of Ticstop 25 mg and its primary metabolites have not been formally studied in geriatric subjects.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Because the safety and efficacy of the increased exposure to Ticstop 25 mg and other circulating metabolites are unknown, it is not possible to adjust the dosage of Ticstop 25 mg in hepatic impairment to ensure safe use. The use of Ticstop 25 mg in patients with hepatic impairment is contraindicated.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Atypical neuroleptic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Ticstop 25 mg is a drug formerly used as an antipsychotic but now used primarily in the symptomatic treatment of various hyperkinetic disorders. It is a vesicular monoamine transporter-2 (VMAT-2) inhibitor that is used as symptomatic treatment of chorea associated with Huntington\'s disease.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> There are no adequate data on the developmental risk associated with the use of Ticstop 25 mg in pregnant women. There are no data on the presence of Ticstop 25 mg or its metabolites in human milk.</p>', 'active', '455_1742341513.jpg', 4, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 04:45:13', '2025-03-19 04:45:13'),
(3475, 'Zopime', 'Cefepime Hydrochloride', 550.00, 1, 'IV injection', '1 gm vial injection', 'IV Injection', '1 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zopime 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> Cefepime is indicated for the treatment of the following infections caused by susceptible strains of the microorganisms:</p><ul><li><strong>Pneumonia (moderate to severe)</strong>: caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, including cases associated with concurrent bacteremia, <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Enterobacter</em> species.</li><li><strong>Febrile Neutropenia</strong>: Cefepime as monotherapy is indicated for empiric treatment of febrile neutropenic patients.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated and Complicated Urinary Tract Infections (including pyelonephritis)</strong>: caused by <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>: caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (methicillin-susceptible strains only) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections</strong> (used in combination with metronidazole): caused by <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>viridans group streptococci</em>, <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, <em>Enterobacter</em> species, or <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Fourth generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cephalosporins are bactericidal and have the same mode of action as other beta-lactam antibiotics (such as penicillins). Cephalosporins disrupt the synthesis of the peptidoglycan layer of bacterial cell walls. The peptidoglycan layer is important for cell wall structural integrity, especially in Gram-positive organisms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zopime 1 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Moderate to Severe Pneumonia</strong>: 1-2 gm IV 12 hourly for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Empiric therapy for febrile neutropenic patients</strong>: 2 gm IV 8 hourly for 7 days.</li><li><strong>Mild to Moderate Uncomplicated or Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</strong>: 0.5-1 gm IV/IM 12 hourly for 7-10 days.</li><li><strong>Severe Uncomplicated or Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</strong>: 2 gm IV 12 hourly for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Moderate to Severe Uncomplicated Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>: 2 gm IV 12 hourly for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections (used with metronidazole)</strong>: 2 gm IV 12 hourly for 7-10 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zopime 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> Renal function should be monitored carefully if high doses of aminoglycosides are administered with Cefepime due to increased potential for nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity. Concomitant administration with potent diuretics like furosemide may also increase nephrotoxicity.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Cefepime is contraindicated in patients who have shown immediate hypersensitivity reactions to cefepime, cephalosporins, penicillin, or other beta-lactam antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zopime 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> Common side effects include hypersensitivity reactions, gastrointestinal disturbances, and hematological abnormalities.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B. Should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Excreted in human breast milk in very low concentrations. Caution should be exercised when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Prolonged use may result in overgrowth of non-susceptible microorganisms. Cephalosporins may cause a fall in prothrombin activity, especially in patients with renal or hepatic impairment, or poor nutritional states.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zopime 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> Symptoms of overdose include encephalopathy, myoclonus, seizures, and neuromuscular excitability. In case of overdose, hemodialysis (not peritoneal) is recommended.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Do not use after the expiry date. Keep all medicines out of reach of children. To be dispensed only on the prescription of a registered physician.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: The maximum dose for pediatric patients should not exceed the recommended adult dose.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Careful dose selection is required, and renal function should be monitored.</li><li><strong>Impaired Renal Function</strong>: Dose adjustments are necessary for patients with renal insufficiency (creatinine clearance &lt;60 ml/min).</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p> For IV administration, Cefepime is compatible with Sterile Water for Injection and several IV infusion fluids, such as 0.9% Sodium Chloride, 5% Dextrose, and Lactated Ringer’s solution.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Fourth generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Cephalosporins disrupt the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall by inhibiting the final transpeptidation step, facilitated by penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category B. Should be used only if clearly needed.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Recommended for patients up to 40 kg in weight. For febrile neutropenic patients, it is administered every 8 hours.</p>', 'active', '387_1742341711.jpg', 1, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 04:48:31', '2025-03-19 04:48:31'),
(3476, 'Plasmex', 'Hydroxyethyl Starch plus Sodium Chloride', 654.41, 1, 'Bottle', '500 ml bottle', 'IV Infusion', '6 percent plus 0.9 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Plasmex 6%+0.9%</strong></p><p> Hydroxyethyl starch (HES 130/0.4) in an isotonic electrolyte solution for intravenous infusion. It belongs to a group of medicines known as plasma volume substitutes. They work by increasing and maintaining the circulating blood volume for several hours. This helps to keep your blood pressure stable. This solution is used for the treatment and prevention of low blood volumes (hypovolaemia) in adults and children.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p> 1000 ml solution for infusion contains the active substances:</p><ul><li>Poly (O-2-hydroxyethyl) starch: 60.00 g</li><li>Sodium acetate trihydrate: 4.63 g</li><li>Sodium chloride: 6.02 g</li><li>Potassium chloride: 0.30 g</li><li>Magnesium chloride hexahydrate: 0.30 g</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Plasma expanders</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Colloidal solution volume expander that contains hydroxyethyl starch.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Plasmex 6%+0.9%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult:</strong> Dose up to 50 mL of (hydroxyethyl starch in sodium chloride injection) per kg of body weight per day (equivalent to 3 g hydroxyethyl starch and 7.7 mEq sodium per kg of body weight). This dose is equivalent to 3500 mL of (hydroxyethyl starch in sodium chloride injection) for a 70 kg patient.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Dose:</strong> Dosage in children should be adapted to the individual patient colloid needs, taking into account the disease state, as well as the hemodynamic and hydration status.</li><li><strong>Newborns and infants (2-12 years):</strong> Mean dose of 36 mL/kg IV.</li><li><strong>Initial dose:</strong> 10-20 mL by slow IV infusion and monitor for anaphylactoid reaction.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Plasmex 6%+0.9%</strong></p><p> This solution will be given by, or under the direct supervision of, your physician, who will closely control the amount of this solution given to you.</p><ul><li><strong>Mode of administration:</strong> You will receive this medicine by infusion into a vein (intravenous drip). The speed of infusion and the amount of solution infused will depend on your specific requirements, the disease for which the product is being used, and reference to the maximum daily dose.</li><li><strong>Dosage:</strong> Your doctor will decide on the correct dose for you to receive. The recommended maximum daily dose is up to 50 mL of this solution per kg of body weight.</li><li><strong>Use in children and adolescents:</strong> Your doctor may give the product to children after careful evaluation of advantages and disadvantages.</li><li><strong>If you have received more than the recommended dose:</strong> Your doctor will ensure that you receive the right amount. If the dose proves too much for you, your doctor may stop this solution immediately and, if necessary, administer diuretics to remove excess water from the body.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Plasmex 6%+0.9%</strong></p><p> The safety and compatibility of other additives, other than citrate anticoagulant, have not been established. Incompatibilities: In the absence of incompatibility studies, this medicinal product must not be mixed with other medicinal products.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Do not use this solution if you:</p><ul><li>Suffer from severe generalized infection (sepsis)</li><li>Have severe liver disease</li><li>Have a known allergy (hypersensitivity) to hydroxyethyl starch</li><li>Have a clinical condition where too much fluid in your body is a potential problem, especially if you have too much fluid in your lungs (pulmonary edema) or if you suffer from a condition where your heart cannot pump enough blood to other organs of your body (congestive heart failure)</li><li>Have pre-existing blood clotting (coagulation) or bleeding disorders</li><li>Suffer from kidney failure and produce little or no urine (unless caused by low blood volumes)</li><li>Receive dialysis treatment</li><li>Have severe hyperkalemia, hypernatremia, or hyperchloremia</li><li>Suffer from bleeding in the brain (intracranial bleeding)</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Plasmex 6%+0.9%</strong></p><p> Common (may affect up to 1 in 10 people):</p><ul><li>Itching is a known side effect of hydroxyethyl starches when used over long periods of time and at high doses.</li><li>Other effects are associated with blood dilution, such as prolonged blood clotting time.</li><li>Serum amylase levels may rise, interfering with pancreatitis diagnosis (though it does not cause pancreatitis).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in pregnancy:</strong> Animal reproduction studies have not been conducted with Hydroxyethyl Starch in Sodium Chloride infusion. The potential for fetal harm is unknown. It should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Use during lactation:</strong> It is unknown whether Hydroxyethyl starch is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Tell your doctor if you have:</p><ul><li>Liver impairment</li><li>Heart or circulation problems</li><li>Blood clotting disorders</li><li>Kidney problems</li><li> In certain serious diseases, your doctor may use salt solutions instead. Avoid using this solution if you have kidney disease or are undergoing dialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep this medicine out of the sight and reach of children.</li><li>Do not freeze.</li><li>Do not store above 25°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Plasma expanders</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> For this solution, no clinical data on exposed pregnancies are available. Your doctor will only give this solution after weighing the benefits versus the potential risks. It is unknown if this drug is excreted in human milk. Consult your doctor regarding breastfeeding.</p>', 'active', '1713_1742341964.jpg', 55, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 04:52:44', '2025-03-19 04:52:44'),
(3477, 'Trucef', 'Cefpodoxime Proxetil', 98.37, 1, 'Bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '40 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Trucef 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Cefpodoxime is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by susceptible microorganisms, listed below:</p><ul><li>Acute otitis media caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenza</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains).</li><li>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li>Acute maxillary sinusitis caused by <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>.</li><li>Community-acquired pneumonia caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em> or <em>H. influenza</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains).</li><li>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em>, <em>H. influenzae</em> (non-beta-lactamase-producing strains only), or <em>M. catarrhalis</em>.</li><li>Skin and skin structure infections caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections caused by <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, or <em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em>.</li><li>Uncomplicated gonorrhea caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li><li>Rectal gonococcal infections in women due to <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cefpodoxime is an oral 3rd generation cephalosporin, which has good stability to beta-lactamases and activity against Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. It is indicated for the treatment of infections either before the infecting organism has been identified. It is a prodrug; its active metabolite is Cefpodoxime. Approximately 29 to 33% of Cefpodoxime is excreted unchanged in the urine within 12 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Trucef 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (13 years and older):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/tonsillitis: 100 mg 12 hourly, 5 to 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute maxillary sinusitis: 200 mg 12 hourly, 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Community-acquired pneumonia: 200 mg 12 hourly, 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bacterial exacerbations of chronic bronchitis: 200 mg 12 hourly, 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin and skin structure: 400 mg 12 hourly, 7 to 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: 100 mg 12 hourly, 7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated gonorrhea: single dose of 200 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rectal gonococcal infections in women: single dose of 200 mg</li><li><strong>Infants and Pediatric Patients (2 months to 12 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute otitis media: 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly, 5 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/tonsillitis: 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly, 5 to 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute maxillary sinusitis: 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly, 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Trucef 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (13 years and older):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/tonsillitis: 100 mg 12 hourly, 5 to 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute maxillary sinusitis: 200 mg 12 hourly, 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Community-acquired pneumonia: 200 mg 12 hourly, 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bacterial exacerbations of chronic bronchitis: 200 mg 12 hourly, 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin and skin structure: 400 mg 12 hourly, 7 to 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: 100 mg 12 hourly, 7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated gonorrhea: single dose of 200 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rectal gonococcal infections in women: single dose of 200 mg</li><li><strong>Infants and Pediatric Patients (2 months to 12 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute otitis media: 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly, 5 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/tonsillitis: 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly, 5 to 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute maxillary sinusitis: 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly, 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Trucef 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Cefpodoxime:</p><ul><li>Concomitant administration of high doses of antacids (sodium bicarbonate and aluminum hydroxide) or H2 blockers reduces peak plasma levels and the extent of absorption, respectively.</li><li>Renal excretion of Cefpodoxime is inhibited by probenecid.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Cefpodoxime is contraindicated in patients with known allergy to cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Trucef 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Cefpodoxime has very few side effects. Possible side effects include gastrointestinal disorders (such as diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain), rash, urticaria, and itching.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> US FDA pregnancy category of Cefpodoxime is B. There is, however, no adequate and well-controlled study in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Cefpodoxime has been shown to be excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Cefpodoxime is administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> In patients with transient or persistent reduction in urinary output due to renal insufficiency, the total daily dose of Cefpodoxime should be reduced. Cefpodoxime should be administered with caution to patients receiving concurrent treatment with potent diuretics. As with other antibiotics, prolonged use of Cefpodoxime may result in overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Severe Renal Impairment:</strong> In patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min), the dosing intervals should be increased to 24 hourly.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment is required in cases of hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ol><li>Shake the bottle well to loosen the powder.</li><li>Add boiled and cooled water to the bottle.</li><li>Shake until the powder is completely mixed with water.</li></ol><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Cefpodoxime is an oral 3rd generation cephalosporin, which has good stability to beta-lactamases and activity against Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. It is indicated for the treatment of infections either before the infecting organism has been identified. It is a prodrug; its active metabolite is Cefpodoxime. Approximately 29 to 33% of Cefpodoxime is excreted unchanged in the urine within 12 hours.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> For this solution, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women are available. This drug should only be used if clearly needed. Cefpodoxime has been shown to be excreted in human milk, so caution should be exercised when it is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Severe Renal Impairment:</strong> In patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min), the dosing intervals should be increased to 24 hourly.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment is required in cases of hepatic impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '261_1742342258.jpg', 6, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 04:57:38', '2025-03-19 04:57:38'),
(3478, 'Preslo-L', 'Losartan Potassium', 8.02, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Preslo-L 50 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Hypertension:</strong> It is recommended to use Preslo-L 50 mg to treat hypertension. It could be used with other antihypertensive medications or taken alone (e.g., thiazide diuretics).</p><p> <strong>Renal Protection in Type-2 Diabetic Patients with Proteinuria:</strong> In hypertensive type-2 diabetics with proteinuria, which is defined as urine albumin to creatinine ratio &gt;300 mg/g, Preslo-L 50 mg is advised to postpone the advancement of renal disease.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Angiotensin-II receptor blocker</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> The first angiotensin II receptor blocker that is orally active without a peptide is Preslo-L 50 mg. It binds to the AT1 receptor, which is present in numerous tissues (such as the heart, kidneys, adrenal glands, and vascular smooth muscle), and inhibits several critical biological processes, such as vasoconstriction and the production of the hormone aldosterone that causes hypertension.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Preslo-L 50 mg</strong></p><p> For the majority of patients, the beginning and maintenance dose is 50 mg once daily. Prior to raising the dose, 25 mg twice daily is advised if the antihypertensive impact of 50 mg once daily is insufficient. A starting dose of 25 mg once daily should be taken into consideration for individuals with intravascular volume depletion (such as those on high-dose diuretics). One or two doses of Preslo-L 50 mg can be given each day. From 25 mg to 100 mg is the range for the total daily dose.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Preslo-L 50 mg</strong></p><p> For the majority of patients, the beginning and maintenance dose is 50 mg once daily. Prior to raising the dose, 25 mg twice daily is advised if the antihypertensive impact of 50 mg once daily is insufficient. A starting dose of 25 mg once daily should be taken into consideration for individuals with intravascular volume depletion (such as those on high-dose diuretics). One or two doses of Preslo-L 50 mg can be given each day. From 25 mg to 100 mg is the range for the total daily dose.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Preslo-L 50 mg</strong></p><p> Levels of the Preslo-L 50 mg active metabolite are decreased by rifampicin and fluconazole. The antihypertensive effects of hydrochlorothiazide and Preslo-L 50 mg used together may be potentiated. Increases in serum potassium may result from the concurrent use of potassium-sparing diuretics (such as spironolactone, triamterene, and amiloride), potassium supplements, or salt substitutes containing potassium. The non-steroidal anti-inflammatory medicine indomethacin may lessen the antihypertensive effects of losartan. The risk of renal impairment is increased by the concurrent use of an ACE inhibitor, an angiotensin receptor antagonist, an anti-inflammatory medication, and a thiazide diuretic.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Pregnant women and individuals who are hypersensitive to any ingredient in this medication should not use Preslo-L 50 mg. Those with diabetes shouldn\'t take Aliskiren and Preslo-L 50 mg together.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Preslo-L 50 mg</strong></p><p> Preslo-L 50 mg side effects are minor and short-lived in nature. Dizziness, diarrhea, nasal congestion, coughing, and upper respiratory infections are the most frequent adverse effects. Fatigue, oedema, chest pain, nausea, headaches, and pharyngitis are other adverse effects.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Category D for pregnancies. If Preslo-L 50 mg is given during the second or third trimester of pregnancy, the risk to the fetus increases. Given that numerous medicines are excreted in human milk and that it is unknown whether Preslo-L 50 mg is one of them, a choice should be taken regarding whether to stop breastfeeding or stop taking the medication, taking into account the significance of the medication to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> In addition to increasing fetal and neonatal morbidity and mortality, the use of Preslo-L 50 mg throughout the second and third trimesters of pregnancy decreases fetal renal function. Symptomatic hypotension may happen in people who are intravascularly volume-depleted (such as those taking high-dose diuretics). Patients with cirrhosis have a considerably higher plasma concentration of Preslo-L 50 mg. Patients with renal impairment have experienced changes in renal function, including renal failure.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep dry and away from heat and light. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Angiotensin-II receptor blocker</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> The first angiotensin II receptor blocker that is orally active without a peptide is Preslo-L 50 mg. It binds to the AT1 receptor, which is present in numerous tissues (such as the heart, kidneys, adrenal glands, and vascular smooth muscle), and inhibits several critical biological processes, such as vasoconstriction and the production of the hormone aldosterone that causes hypertension.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Category D for pregnancies. If Preslo-L 50 mg is given during the second or third trimester of pregnancy, the risk to the fetus increases. Given that numerous medicines are excreted in human milk and that it is unknown whether Preslo-L 50 mg is one of them, a choice should be taken regarding whether to stop breastfeeding or stop taking the medication, taking into account the significance of the medication to the mother.</p>', 'active', '1603_1742347598.jpg', 68, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 06:26:38', '2025-03-19 06:26:38'),
(3479, 'Cefim-3', 'Cefixime Trihydrate', 230.00, 1, 'x 50 ml bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '50 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cefim-3 50 ml</strong></p><p>Cefixime is an orally active cephalosporin antibiotic which has marked in-vitro bactericidal activity against a wide variety of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms. Cefim-3 50 ml is indicated for the treatment of the following acute infections when caused by susceptible microorganisms.</p><p><strong>Upper Respiratory Tract Infections (URTI):</strong> e.g., otitis media; and other URTI where the causative organism is known or suspected to be resistant to other commonly used antibiotics, or where treatment failure may carry significant risk.</p><p><strong>Lower Respiratory Tract Infections:</strong> e.g., bronchitis.</p><p><strong>Urinary Tract Infections:</strong> e.g., cystitis, cystourethritis, pyelonephritis.</p><p>Clinical efficacy has been demonstrated in infections caused by commonly occurring pathogens including <em>Streptococcus pneumonia</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, <em>Klebsiella</em> species, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase positive and negative), <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase positive and negative), and <em>Enterobacter</em> species. Cefixime is highly stable in the presence of beta-lactamase enzymes.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefixime is a third generation semisynthetic cephalosporin antibiotic for oral administration. It is bactericidal against a broad spectrum of gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria at easily achievable plasma concentrations. It kills bacteria by interfering in the synthesis of bacterial cell walls. It is highly stable in the presence of Beta-lactamase enzymes. As a result, many organisms resistant to penicillins and some cephalosporins due to the presence of beta-lactamases may be susceptible to Cefixime. Absorption is about 40% to 50% whether administered with or without food.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cefim-3 50 ml</strong></p><p>The usual course of treatment is 7 days. This may be continued for up to 14 days depending on the severity of the infection.</p><p><strong>Adults and children over 12 years:</strong> The recommended adult dose is 200-400 mg (1 to 2 capsules) daily, given either as a single dose or in two divided doses. For the treatment of uncomplicated cervical/urethral gonococcal infections, a single oral dose of Cefixime 400 mg is recommended.</p><p><strong>Children (6 months or older):</strong> Usually 8 mg/kg/day given as a single dose or in two divided doses or may be given as follows:</p><p>½-1 year: 75 mg daily.</p><p>1-4 years: 100 mg daily.</p><p>5-10 years: 200 mg daily.</p><p>11-12 years: 300 mg daily.</p><p>In typhoid fever, dosage should be 10 mg/kg/day for 14 days.</p><p><strong>Children (under 6 months):</strong> The safety and efficacy of Cefixime have not been established in children aged less than 6 months.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Cefim-3 50 ml</strong></p><p>Absorption of Cefixime is not significantly modified by the presence of food. The usual course of treatment is 7 days. This may be continued for up to 14 days if required.</p><p><strong>Adults and children over 10 years:</strong> The recommended adult dosage is 200-400 mg daily according to the severity of the infection, given either as a single dose or in two divided doses.</p><p><strong>Elderly:</strong> Elderly patients may be given the same dose as recommended for adults. Renal function should be assessed, and dosage should be adjusted in severe renal impairment.</p><p><strong>Children:</strong> The recommended dosage for children is 8 mg/kg/day administered as a single dose or in two divided doses. As a general guide for prescribing in children, the following daily doses in terms of volume of suspension are suggested:</p><p>6 months up to 1 year: 3.75 ml daily</p><p>Children 1-4 years: 5 ml daily</p><p>Children 5-10 years: 10 ml daily</p><p>In typhoid: the recommended children dose is 5 mg/kg body weight twice daily for 10-14 days.</p><p><strong>Children weighing more than 50 kg or older than 10 years:</strong> Should be treated with the recommended adult dose, 200-400 mg daily depending on the severity of infection.</p><p><strong>Children aged less than 6 months:</strong> The safety and efficacy of Cefixime have not been established in children aged less than 6 months.</p><p><strong>Dosage in Renal Impairment:</strong> Cefixime may be administered in the presence of impaired renal function. Normal dose and schedule may be given in patients with creatinine clearances of 20 ml/min or greater. In patients whose creatinine clearance is less than 20 ml/min, it is recommended that a dose of 200 mg once daily should not be exceeded. The dose and regimen for patients who are maintained on chronic ambulatory peritoneal dialysis or haemodialysis should follow the same recommendation as that for patients with creatinine clearances of less than 20 ml/min.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Cefim-3 50 ml</strong></p><p>In common with other cephalosporins, increases in prothrombin times have been noted in a few patients. Care should therefore be taken in patients receiving anticoagulant therapy.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Patients with known hypersensitivity to cephalosporin antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Cefim-3 50 ml</strong></p><p>Cefixime is generally well tolerated. The majority of adverse reactions observed in clinical trials were mild and self-limiting in nature.</p><p><strong>Gastrointestinal disturbances:</strong> The most frequent side effects seen with Cefixime are diarrhea and stool changes; diarrhea has been more commonly associated with higher doses. Other gastrointestinal side effects seen less frequently are nausea, abdominal pain, dyspepsia, vomiting, and flatulence. Pseudomembranous colitis has been reported.</p><p><strong>Central nervous system:</strong> Headache and dizziness.</p><p><strong>Hypersensitivity reactions:</strong> Allergies in the form of rash, pruritus, urticaria, drug fever, and arthralgia have been observed. These reactions usually subsided upon discontinuation of therapy.</p><p><strong>Hematological and clinical chemistry:</strong> Thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, and eosinophilia have been reported. These reactions were infrequent and reversible. Mild transient changes in liver and renal function tests have been observed.</p><p><strong>Miscellaneous:</strong> Other possible reactions include genital pruritis and vaginitis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. It is not known whether Cefixime is excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Cefixime is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Cefixime should be given with caution to patients who have shown hypersensitivity to other drugs. Cephalosporins should be given with caution to penicillin-sensitive patients, as there is some evidence of partial cross-allergenicity between the penicillins and the cephalosporins. Patients have had severe reactions (including anaphylaxis) to both classes of drugs. If an allergic effect occurs with Cefixime, the drug should be discontinued and the patient treated with appropriate agents if necessary. Cefixime should be administered with caution in patients with markedly impaired renal function. Treatment with broad-spectrum antibiotics alters the normal flora of the colon and may permit overgrowth of clostridia. Studies indicate that a toxin produced by <em>Clostridium difficile</em> is a primary cause of antibiotic-associated diarrhea.</p><p><strong>Use in pregnancy and lactation:</strong> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Cefixime should therefore not be used in pregnancy or in nursing mothers unless considered essential by the physician.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Cefim-3 50 ml</strong></p><p>Gastric lavage may be indicated; otherwise, no specific antidote exists. Cefixime is not removed in significant quantities from the circulation by hemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis. Adverse reactions in small numbers of healthy adult volunteers receiving single doses up to 2 g of Cefixime did not differ from the profile seen in patients treated at the recommended doses.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30ºC temperature, protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Cefixime is a third generation semisynthetic cephalosporin antibiotic for oral administration. It is bactericidal against a broad spectrum of gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria at easily achievable plasma concentrations. It kills bacteria by interfering in the synthesis of bacterial cell walls. It is highly stable in the presence of Beta-lactamase enzymes. As a result, many organisms resistant to penicillins and some cephalosporins due to the presence of beta-lactamases may be susceptible to Cefixime. Absorption is about 40% to 50% whether administered with or without food.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. It is not known whether Cefixime is excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Cefixime is administered to a nursing woman.</p>', 'active', '5833_1746079698.webp', 44, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 06:32:42', '2025-05-01 10:08:18');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3480, 'PrevaHAV', 'Inactivated Hepatitis A vaccine', 600.00, 1, 'Injection', '0.5 ml vial sc injection', 'Injection', '250 Unit per 0.5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of PrevaHAV 250 U/0.5 ml</strong></p><p> The vaccine is indicated for the active immunization of adults and children from 12 months of age against infection caused by Hepatitis A virus.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Vaccines, Anti-sera &amp; Immunoglobulin</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> The hepatitis A virus belongs to the picornavirus family. It is one of several hepatitis viruses that cause systemic disease with pathology in the liver. The incubation period for hepatitis A averages 28 days (range: 15 to 50 days). The course of hepatitis A infection is extremely variable, ranging from asymptomatic infection to icteric hepatitis and death. The presence of antibodies to HAV confers protection against hepatitis A infection. However, the lowest titer needed to confer protection has not been determined.</p><p><strong>Dosage of PrevaHAV 250 U/0.5 ml</strong></p><p> Hepatitis A vaccine should be given intramuscularly only. Do not inject it intravenously. The primary immunization consists of two doses, the first dose is at the selected date, and the second dose will be 6 months later.</p><ul><li><strong>Children 12 months to 15 years</strong>:</li><li>The recommended dose is 0.5 ml</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>First dose</strong>: At the elected date</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Second dose</strong>: 6 months after the first dose</li><li><strong>Children 16 years and above</strong>:</li><li>The recommended dose is 1 ml</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>First dose</strong>: At the elected date</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Second dose</strong>: 6 months after the first dose</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of PrevaHAV 250 U/0.5 ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Method of administration</strong>: Hepatitis A vaccine should be injected intramuscularly in the deltoid region in adults and children and in the anterolateral part of the thigh in young children under 1 year. The vaccine should not be administered intramuscularly in the gluteal region or subcutaneously/intradermally since administration by these routes may result in less antibody response than optimal. Hepatitis A vaccine should be inspected visually for any foreign particulate matter and/or discoloration prior to administration. Before use, the vial should be well shaken to obtain a slightly milky-white suspension. The vaccine must be used as supplied.</p><p> <strong>Co-administration</strong>: Since Hepatitis A vaccine is an inactivated vaccine, its concomitant use with other inactivated vaccines is unlikely to result in interference with immune responses. When concomitant administration of other vaccines is considered necessary, the vaccines must be given with different syringes and at different injection sites. Concomitant administration of Hepatitis B, typhoid, yellow fever, cholera (injectable), or tetanus vaccine does not interfere with Hepatitis A vaccine immune response. Concomitant administration of Hepatitis A vaccine and human immune globulin may be considered when a subject is at risk of being exposed to Hepatitis A before adequate antibody titre can be reached. Hepatitis A vaccine and human immune globulin should be administered at separate injection sites.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> People who are allergic to any component of the vaccine. People who suffer from serious diseases, fever, and any immune disease.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of PrevaHAV 250 U/0.5 ml</strong></p><p> Reactions at the site of injection are common but can be recovered within 72 hours without any treatment. Some of the mild and temporary adverse reactions are: pain and redness at the site of injection, fever after vaccination.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The vaccine should be given to a pregnant woman only if clearly needed. The vaccine should be used with caution for lactating women.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Shake well before use</li><li>The product should be used with caution for people who or whose family has a history of convulsion or epilepsy, chronic diseases, and allergic symptoms</li><li>Do not use the product if any crack of the container, foreign particulate matter, or out of expiry date</li><li>This product should be used immediately after opening</li><li>Adrenaline and other first aid medicines should be prepared at the vaccination place in case of serious allergic reaction</li><li>The people after vaccination should remain under medical supervision for 30 minutes</li><li>The vaccine must not be frozen</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep out of the reach and sight of children. Store and transport at 2°C to 8°C. Protect from light.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Vaccines, Anti-sera &amp; Immunoglobulin</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> The hepatitis A virus belongs to the picornavirus family. It is one of several hepatitis viruses that cause systemic disease with pathology in the liver. The incubation period for hepatitis A averages 28 days (range: 15 to 50 days). The course of hepatitis A infection is extremely variable, ranging from asymptomatic infection to icteric hepatitis and death. The presence of antibodies to HAV confers protection against hepatitis A infection. However, the lowest titer needed to confer protection has not been determined.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The vaccine should be given to a pregnant woman only if clearly needed. The vaccine should be used with caution for lactating women.</p>', 'active', '711_1742348370.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 06:39:30', '2025-03-19 06:39:30'),
(3481, 'Trado', 'Tramadol Hydrochloride', 25.00, 1, 'x 2 ml ampoule', '2 ml ampoule', 'IV Injection', '100 mg/2 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Trado 100 mg/2 ml</strong></p><p>Tramadol is used for the treatment of moderate to severe painful conditions. These include:</p><ul><li>Postoperative pain</li><li>Colic and spastic pain</li><li>Cancer pain</li><li>Joint pain</li><li>Neck and back pain</li><li>Pain associated with osteoporosis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Tramadol is a centrally acting synthetic analgesic compound. It inhibits the reuptake of neurotransmitters—serotonin and noradrenaline. Thus, it modifies the transmission of pain impulses by activating both descending serotonergic pathways and noradrenergic pathways involved in analgesia. The analgesic effects of Tramadol are mediated via stimulation of mu-opioid receptors and indirect modulation of central monoaminergic inhibitory pathways.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Trado 100 mg/2 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Capsule or Tablet</strong>:</p><ul><li>Usual doses are 50 to 100 mg every four to six hours. For acute pain, an initial dose of 100 mg is required. For chronic painful conditions, an initial dose of 50 mg is recommended. Subsequent doses should be 50 to 100 mg administered 4-6 hourly. The dose level and frequency of dosing will depend on the severity of the pain.</li><li>The total daily dosage by mouth should not exceed 400 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Sustained Release Capsule or Tablet</strong>:</p><ul><li>One SR capsule or tablet every 12 hours, for example, first one in the morning and then at the same time in the evening. The number of capsules taken at a time will depend upon the severity of pain, but it should not be taken more frequently than every 12 hours.</li><li>The total daily dosage by mouth should not exceed 400 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Injection</strong>:</p><ul><li>A dose of 50-100 mg may be given every 4 to 6 hours by intramuscular or intravenous infusion.</li><li>For the treatment of postoperative pain, the initial dose is 100 mg followed by 50 mg every 10 to 20 minutes if necessary, to a maximum of 250 mg in the first hour.</li><li>Thereafter, doses are 50 to 100 mg every 4 to 6 hours up to a total daily dose of 600 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Suppository</strong>:</p><ul><li>Tramadol suppository should be administered rectally.</li><li>For adults, the usual dose is 100 mg Trado 100 mg/2 ml every 6 hours. In general, 400 mg Trado 100 mg/2 ml (4 Tramadol suppositories) per day is sufficient. However, for the treatment of cancer pain and severe pain after operations, much higher daily doses can be used.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Trado 100 mg/2 ml</strong></p><p>In general, physicians need not be concerned about drugs interacting with Tramadol. The monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitors represent the only drug class not recommended for combination with Tramadol. Concomitant administration of carbamazepine with Tramadol causes a significant increase in Tramadol metabolism and it requires an increase in the dose of Tramadol.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Tramadol is contraindicated in persons having hypersensitivity to this drug. It is also contraindicated in acute intoxication with alcohol, hypnotics, centrally acting analgesics, opioids, or psychotropic drugs.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Trado 100 mg/2 ml</strong></p><p>Commonly occurring side-effects include dizziness/vertigo, nausea, constipation, headache, somnolence, vomiting, pruritus, CNS stimulation, asthenia, sweating, dyspepsia, dry mouth, diarrhea.</p><p>Less commonly occurring side-effects include malaise, allergic reaction, weight loss, vasodilatation, palpitations, abdominal pain, anorexia, flatulence, GI bleeding, hepatitis, stomatitis, etc.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Safe use of Tramadol in pregnancy has not been established. Tramadol has been shown to cross the placenta. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Therefore, Tramadol should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the risk to the fetus.</p><p>Trado 100 mg/2 ml should not be administered during breastfeeding as Tramadol and its metabolites have been detected in breast milk.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Respiratory depression</strong>: When large doses of tramadol are administered with anesthetic medications or alcohol, respiratory depression may result. Therefore, tramadol should be administered cautiously in patients at risk for respiratory depression.</li><li><strong>Opioid dependence</strong>: Tramadol is not recommended for patients who are dependent on opioids.</li><li><strong>Concomitant CNS depressants</strong>: Tramadol should be used with caution and in reduced dosages when administering to patients receiving CNS depressants such as alcohol, opioids, anesthetic agents, phenothiazines, tranquilizers, or sedative hypnotics.</li><li><strong>Concomitant MAO inhibitors</strong>: Tramadol should be used with great caution in patients taking MAO inhibitors, since tramadol inhibits the uptake of norepinephrine and serotonin.</li><li>Tramadol should be used with caution in patients with increased intracranial pressure or head injury and patients with acute abdominal conditions.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>In children from the age of 1 year, Trado 100 mg/2 ml can be given in a dose of 1-2 mg/kg body weight.</li><li>However, suppository (100 mg Trado 100 mg/2 ml) should not be administered in children and adolescents below the age of 14 years.</li><li>Trado 100 mg/2 ml 100 mg SR Capsules have not been studied in children. Therefore, safety and efficacy have not been established and the product should not be used in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Tramadol is a centrally acting synthetic analgesic compound. It inhibits the reuptake of neurotransmitters—serotonin and noradrenaline. Thus, it modifies the transmission of pain impulses by activating both descending serotonergic pathways and noradrenergic pathways involved in analgesia. The analgesic effects of Tramadol are mediated via stimulation of mu-opioid receptors and indirect modulation of central monoaminergic inhibitory pathways.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Safe use of Tramadol in pregnancy has not been established. Tramadol has been shown to cross the placenta. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Therefore, Tramadol should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the risk to the fetus.</p><p>Trado 100 mg/2 ml should not be administered during breastfeeding as Tramadol and its metabolites have been detected in breast milk.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>In children from the age of 1 year, Trado 100 mg/2 ml can be given in a dose of 1-2 mg/kg body weight.</li><li>However, suppository (100 mg Trado 100 mg/2 ml) should not be administered in children and adolescents below the age of 14 years.</li><li>Trado 100 mg/2 ml 100 mg SR Capsules have not been studied in children. Therefore, safety and efficacy have not been established and the product should not be used in children.</li></ul>', 'active', '488_1742348614.jpeg', 56, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 06:43:34', '2025-05-07 10:04:39'),
(3482, 'Rezor Plus', 'Olmesartan Medoxomil plus Hydrochlorothiazide', 15.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg+12.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rezor Plus 40 mg+12.5 mg</strong></p><p>Olmesartan Medoxomil &amp; Hydrochlorothiazide combination is indicated for the treatment of hypertension.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Combined antihypertensive preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Hydrochlorothiazide inhibits the reabsorption of Na in the distal tubules causing increased excretion of Na and water including K and hydrogen ions. Olmesartan blocks the vasoconstrictor effects of angiotensin II by selectively blocking the binding of angiotensin II to the AT1 receptor in vascular smooth muscle. Its action is therefore independent of the pathways for angiotensin II synthesis. An AT2 receptor is also found in many tissues, but this receptor is not known to be associated with cardiovascular homeostasis. Olmesartan has more than a 12,500-fold greater affinity for the AT1 receptor than for the AT2 receptor. Blockade of the renin-angiotensin system with ACE inhibitors, which inhibit the biosynthesis of angiotensin II from angiotensin I, is a mechanism of many drugs used to treat hypertension. ACE inhibitors also inhibit the degradation of bradykinin, a reaction also catalyzed by ACE. Because olmesartan medoxomil does not inhibit ACE, it does not affect the response to bradykinin. Whether this difference has clinical relevance is not yet known. Blockade of the angiotensin II receptor inhibits the negative regulatory feedback of angiotensin II on renin secretion, but the resulting increased plasma renin activity and circulating angiotensin II levels do not overcome the effect of olmesartan on blood pressure.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rezor Plus 40 mg+12.5 mg</strong></p><p>The usual recommended starting dose of Olmesartan Medoxomil is 20 mg once daily when used as monotherapy in patients who are not volume-contracted. For patients requiring further reduction in blood pressure after 2 weeks of therapy, the dose may be increased to 40 mg. No initial dosage adjustment is recommended for elderly patients, for patients with moderate to marked renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;40 ml/min), or with moderate to marked hepatic dysfunction. Hydrochlorothiazide is effective in doses between 12.5 mg and 50 mg once daily.</p><p>Replacement Therapy: Olmesartan &amp; Hydrochlorothiazide may be substituted for its titrated components.</p><p>Dose Titration by Clinical Effect: The dose of Olmesartan &amp; Hydrochlorothiazide tablet is one tablet once daily. More than one tablet daily is not recommended. Olmesartan &amp; Hydrochlorothiazide tablet may be administered with other antihypertensive agents. A patient whose blood pressure is inadequately controlled by Olmesartan or Hydrochlorothiazide alone may be switched to once daily Olmesartan &amp; Hydrochlorothiazide tablet. Dosing should be individualized. Depending on the blood pressure response, the dose may be titrated at intervals of 2-4 weeks. If blood pressure is not controlled by Olmesartan alone, Hydrochlorothiazide may be added starting with a dose of 12.5 mg and later titrated to 25 mg once daily. If a patient is taking Hydrochlorothiazide, Olmesartan may be added starting with a dose of 20 mg once daily and titrated to 40 mg, for inadequate blood pressure control. If large doses of hydrochlorothiazide have been used as monotherapy and volume depletion or hyponatremia is present, caution should be used when adding Olmesartan or switching to Olmesartan &amp; Hydrochlorothiazide tablet, as marked decreases in blood pressure may occur. Consideration should be given to reducing the dose of Hydrochlorothiazide to 12.5 mg before adding Olmesartan. The antihypertensive effect of Olmesartan &amp; Hydrochlorothiazide is related to the dose of both components over the range of 10 mg/12.5 mg to 40 mg/25 mg.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Rezor Plus 40 mg+12.5 mg</strong></p><p>Hypertension: The usual starting dose is 20/12.5 mg one tablet once daily. Dosing should be individualized. Depending on the blood pressure response, the dose may be titrated at intervals of 2-4 weeks to two tablets 40/25 once daily.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Rezor Plus 40 mg+12.5 mg</strong></p><p>Olmesartan: No significant drug interactions were reported in studies in which Olmesartan Medoxomil was co-administered with hydrochlorothiazide, digoxin, or warfarin in healthy volunteers. Olmesartan Medoxomil is not metabolized by the cytochrome P450 system and has no effects on P450 enzymes; thus, interactions with drugs that inhibit, induce, or are metabolized by those enzymes are not expected.</p><p>Hydrochlorothiazide: When administered concurrently, the following drugs may interact with Thiazide diuretics: Alcohol, Barbiturates or Narcotics: Potentiation of orthostatic hypotension may occur. Antidiabetic drugs (oral agents and Insulin): Dosage adjustment of the antidiabetic drug may be required. Other antihypertensive drugs: Additive effect. Corticosteroids, ACTH. Lithium.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>The combination of Olmesartan and Hydrochlorothiazide is contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to any component of this product. Because of the Hydrochlorothiazide component, this product is contraindicated in patients with anuria or hypersensitivity to other sulfonamide-derived drugs.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Rezor Plus 40 mg+12.5 mg</strong></p><p>The common side-effects are nausea, headache, dizziness, hyperuricemia, upper respiratory tract infection, and urinary tract infection. Other adverse effects are chest pain, back pain, peripheral edema, abdominal pain, dyspepsia, gastroenteritis, and diarrhea.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Categories C (first trimester) and D (second and third trimesters). This combination drug should not be used during pregnancy.</p><p>Lactation: It is not known whether Olmesartan is excreted in human milk, but Olmesartan is secreted at low concentration in the milk of lactating rats. Thiazides appear in human milk. Because of the potential for adverse effects on the nursing infant, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Periodic determination of serum electrolytes should be performed at appropriate intervals to detect possible electrolyte imbalance like hypokalemia, hyponatremia, and hypochloremic alkalosis. Hyperuricemia may occur in certain patients receiving thiazide therapy. Impaired renal function.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rezor Plus 40 mg+12.5 mg</strong></p><p>Olmesartan: Limited data are available in regard to overdosage in humans. The most likely manifestation of overdosage would be hypotension and tachycardia. Supportive treatment should be instituted.</p><p>Hydrochlorothiazide: The most common signs and symptoms observed are those caused by electrolyte depletion (hypokalemia, hypochloremia, and dehydration) resulting from excessive diuresis. If digitalis has also been administered, hypokalemia may accentuate cardiac arrhythmias.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place, protect from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p>Renal impairment: The usual regimens of therapy with Olmesartan &amp; Hydrochlorothiazide tablet may be followed provided the patient\'s creatinine clearance &gt;30 ml/min. In patients with more severe renal impairment, loop diuretics are preferred to thiazides, so this combination tablet is not recommended.</p><p>Patients with Hepatic Impairment: No dosage adjustment is necessary with hepatic impairment.</p><p>Paediatric population: The safety and efficacy of Olmesartan &amp; Hydrochlorothiazide in children and adolescents below 18 years have not been established. No data are available.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Combined antihypertensive preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Angiotensin-II formed from angiotensin-I in a reaction catalyzed by angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE), is a potent vasoconstrictor, the primary vasoactive hormone of the renin-angiotensin system and an important component in the pathophysiology of hypertension. It also stimulates aldosterone secretion by the adrenal cortex. Olmesartan blocks the vasoconstrictor and aldosterone-secreting effects of angiotensin-II by selectively blocking the binding of angiotensin-II to the AT 1 receptor found in many tissues (e.g. vascular smooth muscle, adrenal gland). In-vitro-binding studies indicate that Olmesartan is a reversible &amp; competitive inhibitor of AT 1 receptor. Olmesartan does not inhibit ACE (kinase-I, the enzyme that converts angiotensin-I to angiotensin-II and degrades bradykinin).</p><p>Hydrochlorothiazide is a thiazide diuretic. Thiazides affect the renal tubular mechanisms of electrolyte reabsorption, directly increasing the excretion of Sodium and Chloride in approximately equivalent amounts. Indirectly, the diuretic action of Hydrochlorothiazide reduces plasma volume with consequent increases in plasma renin activity, increases Aldosterone secretion &amp; urinary Potassium loss and decreases serum Potassium. The renin-aldosterone link is mediated by angiotensin-II. So, co-administration of an angiotensin-II receptor antagonist tends to reverse the potassium loss associated with these diuretics.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Safety and effectiveness in nursing mothers &amp; pregnancy have not been established. The drug should be discontinued during these conditions.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Renal Impairment Patients: The usual regimens of therapy with this may be followed provided the patient\'s creatinine clearance is &gt;30 ml/min. In patients with more severe renal impairment, loop diuretics are preferred to thiazides. So, this preparation is not recommended.</p><p>Hepatic Impairment Patients: No dosage adjustment is necessary with hepatic impairment.</p><p>Paediatric use: Safety and effectiveness in paediatric patients have not been established.</p><p>Geriatric use: Clinical studies of Olmesartan and Hydrochlorothiazide combination did not include sufficient numbers of subjects aged 65 and over to determine whether they respond differently from younger subjects. In general, dose selection for an elderly patient should be cautious.</p>', 'active', '786_1742348865.jpg', 6, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 06:47:45', '2025-03-21 21:33:32'),
(3483, 'Roxim', 'Cefixime Trihydrate', 250.00, 1, 'x 75 ml bottle', '75 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '100 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Roxim 75 ml</strong></p><p>Cefixime is an orally active cephalosporin antibiotic that exhibits significant in-vitro bactericidal activity against a broad range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms. Roxim 75 ml is indicated for the treatment of the following acute infections when caused by susceptible microorganisms:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper Respiratory Tract Infections (URTI)</strong>: e.g. otitis media and other URTI where the causative organism is known or suspected to be resistant to other commonly used antibiotics, or where treatment failure could carry significant risk.</li><li><strong>Lower Respiratory Tract Infections</strong>: e.g. bronchitis.</li><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections</strong>: e.g. cystitis, cystourethritis, pyelonephritis.</li></ul><p>Clinical efficacy has been demonstrated in infections caused by commonly occurring pathogens including <strong>Streptococcus pneumonia</strong>, <strong>Streptococcus pyogenes</strong>, <strong>Escherichia coli</strong>, <strong>Proteus mirabilis</strong>, <strong>Klebsiella species</strong>, <strong>Haemophilus influenzae</strong> (both beta-lactamase positive and negative), <strong>Moraxella catarrhalis</strong> (both beta-lactamase positive and negative), and <strong>Enterobacter species</strong>. Cefixime is highly stable in the presence of beta-lactamase enzymes.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Third-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefixime is a third-generation, semi-synthetic cephalosporin antibiotic for oral administration. It is bactericidal against a broad spectrum of Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria at easily achievable plasma concentrations. Cefixime kills bacteria by interfering with the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall. It is highly stable in the presence of beta-lactamase enzymes, allowing it to target organisms resistant to penicillins and some cephalosporins.</p><ul><li>Absorption is about 40% to 50%, whether administered with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Roxim 75 ml</strong></p><p>The usual course of treatment is 7 days, which may be extended for up to 14 days depending on the severity of the infection.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Children over 12 years</strong>: The recommended dose is <strong>200-400 mg</strong> daily (1 to 2 capsules), given as a single dose or in two divided doses. For uncomplicated cervical/urethral gonococcal infections, a single oral dose of <strong>Cefixime 400 mg</strong> is recommended.</li><li><strong>Children (6 months or older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">½-1 year: 75 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-4 years: 100 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-10 years: 200 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">11-12 years: 300 mg daily.</li><li>In typhoid fever, the dosage should be <strong>10 mg/kg/day</strong> for 14 days.</li><li><strong>Children under 6 months</strong>: The safety and efficacy of Cefixime have not been established.</li><li><strong>Dosage in Renal Impairment</strong>: In patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;20 ml/min), it is recommended that a dose of 200 mg once daily should not be exceeded.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Roxim 75 ml</strong></p><p>Like other cephalosporins, increased prothrombin times have been observed in some patients. Caution should be exercised when Cefixime is co-administered with anticoagulant therapy.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to cephalosporin antibiotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Roxim 75 ml</strong></p><p>Most adverse reactions observed in clinical trials were mild and self-limiting. Common side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Diarrhoea (more common with higher doses), nausea, abdominal pain, dyspepsia, vomiting, flatulence, and rare pseudomembranous colitis.</li><li><strong>Central Nervous System</strong>: Headache, dizziness.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Rash, pruritis, urticaria, drug fever, and arthralgia.</li><li><strong>Hematological</strong>: Thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, and eosinophilia (infrequent and reversible).</li><li><strong>Miscellaneous</strong>: Genital pruritis, vaginitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Cefixime should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed, as there are no well-controlled studies in pregnant women.</li><li>It is not known whether Cefixime is excreted in human milk, so caution is advised when administering it to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Cefixime should be used with caution in patients with hypersensitivity to other drugs, especially penicillins, as there is a possibility of cross-allergenicity between penicillins and cephalosporins.</li><li>It should be administered with caution to patients with impaired renal function.</li><li>Broad-spectrum antibiotics may alter the normal flora of the colon, increasing the risk of Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Roxim 75 ml</strong></p><p>There is no specific antidote for Cefixime overdose. Gastric lavage may be indicated. Cefixime is not significantly removed from circulation by hemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below <strong>30ºC</strong>, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Third-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Cefixime is bactericidal against a broad spectrum of bacteria by interfering with the bacterial cell wall synthesis. It is stable in the presence of beta-lactamase enzymes, making it effective against organisms resistant to other antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Cefixime should be used during pregnancy only when clearly needed and with caution in nursing mothers due to unknown effects on breast milk.</li></ul>', 'active', '385_1742357416.webp', 26, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 09:10:16', '2025-05-04 17:05:34'),
(3484, 'Cabanib', 'Cabozantinib', 450.00, 30, 'Capsule', '30 capsule in a pot', 'Capsule', '60 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the provided information in a more structured format with headings and bolded text where appropriate:</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Indications of Cabanib 60 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC):</strong> Cabanib 60 mg is indicated for the treatment of patients with advanced renal cell carcinoma.</li><li><strong>Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC):</strong> Cabanib 60 mg is indicated for the treatment of patients with hepatocellular carcinoma who have been previously treated with Sorafenib.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>In vitro biochemical and/or cellular assays have shown that Cabanib 60 mg inhibits the tyrosine kinase activity of:</p><ul><li><strong>MET, VEGFR-1, -2, and -3, AXL, RET, ROS1, TYRO3, MER, KIT, TRKB, FLT-3, and TIE-2.</strong></li><li>These receptor tyrosine kinases are involved in both normal cellular function and pathologic processes such as oncogenesis, metastasis, tumor angiogenesis, drug resistance, and maintenance of the tumor microenvironment.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage of Cabanib 60 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC):</strong> 60 mg once daily without food until the patient no longer experiences clinical benefit or experiences unacceptable toxicity.</li><li><strong>Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC):</strong> 60 mg once daily without food until disease progression or unacceptable toxicity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Additional instructions:Do not substitute Cabanib 60 mg tablets with Cabanib 60 mg capsules.</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Administer at least 1 hour before or at least 2 hours after eating.</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Swallow tablets whole. Do not crush.</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Do not take a missed dose within 12 hours of the next dose.</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adjust the dose for hepatic impairment or when taking drugs known to strongly induce or inhibit CYP450.</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><h3><strong>Administration of Cabanib 60 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pre-Surgery Instructions:</strong> Stop treatment at least 28 days before scheduled surgery, including dental surgery.</li><li><strong>Do not administer with food.</strong></li><li><strong>Missed dose:</strong> Do not take a missed dose within 12 hours of the next dose.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions of Cabanib 60 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Strong CYP3A4 Inhibitors:</strong> Avoid coadministration with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors. If unavoidable, reduce the dosage.</li><li><strong>Strong CYP3A4 Inducers:</strong> Avoid coadministration with strong CYP3A4 inducers. If unavoidable, increase the dosage.</li><li><strong>Avoid grapefruit or grapefruit juice</strong> due to its potential to increase exposure to Cabanib 60 mg.</li><li><strong>Avoid St. John\'s Wort</strong> due to its potential to decrease exposure to Cabanib 60 mg.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity:</strong> Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Cabanib 60 mg or any of its components.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Cabanib 60 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hemorrhage</strong></li><li><strong>Perforations and Fistulas</strong></li><li><strong>Thrombotic Events</strong></li><li><strong>Hypertension and Hypertensive Crisis</strong></li><li><strong>Diarrhea</strong></li><li><strong>Palmar-plantar Erythrodysesthesia (PPE)</strong></li><li><strong>Proteinuria</strong></li><li><strong>Osteonecrosis of the Jaw</strong></li><li><strong>Wound Complications</strong></li><li><strong>Reversible Posterior Leukoencephalopathy Syndrome (RPLS)</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Cabanib 60 mg can cause fetal harm.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> No information regarding its presence in human milk, but due to potential adverse effects, women should not breastfeed during treatment and for 4 months after the last dose.</li><li><strong>Contraception:</strong> Females of reproductive potential should use effective contraception during treatment and for 4 months post-treatment.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hemorrhage:</strong> Discontinue for Grade 3 or 4 hemorrhage.</li><li><strong>Perforations and Fistulas:</strong> Discontinue if perforation or fistula occurs.</li><li><strong>Thrombotic Events:</strong> Discontinue for acute myocardial infarction or serious thromboembolic events.</li><li><strong>Hypertension:</strong> Monitor blood pressure; withhold if not controlled.</li><li><strong>Diarrhea:</strong> Withhold until Grade 1 improvement and resume at a reduced dose.</li><li><strong>PPE:</strong> Withhold until Grade 1 improvement and resume at a reduced dose.</li><li><strong>Proteinuria:</strong> Monitor urine protein and discontinue for nephrotic syndrome.</li><li><strong>Osteonecrosis of the Jaw (ONJ):</strong> Advise good oral hygiene and withhold for dental procedures.</li><li><strong>Wound Complications:</strong> Withhold treatment at least 28 days before surgery.</li><li><strong>RPLS:</strong> Discontinue if diagnosed.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Cabanib 60 mg</strong></h3><ul><li>One case of overdosage was reported with a patient taking twice the intended dose for 9 days. Symptoms included memory impairment, mental status changes, cognitive disturbance, weight loss, and increased BUN. The extent of recovery was not documented.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Store below 30°C</strong> in a cool, dry place away from sunlight.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> No overall differences in safety or effectiveness observed.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment recommended for mild to moderate renal impairment.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitor</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Cabanib 60 mg inhibits the tyrosine kinase activity of MET, VEGFR-1, -2, and -3, AXL, RET, ROS1, TYRO3, MER, KIT, TRKB, FLT-3, and TIE-2, involved in oncogenesis, metastasis, tumor angiogenesis, drug resistance, and maintenance of the tumor microenvironment.</p><h3><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Absorption:</strong> Peak concentrations at 3–4 hours post-dose.</li><li><strong>Distribution:</strong> Volume of distribution (Vz/F) approximately 319 L. Over 99.7% protein-bound.</li><li><strong>Elimination:</strong> Terminal half-life of approximately 99 hours, clearance estimated at 2.2 L/hr.</li><li><strong>Metabolism:</strong> Cabanib 60 mg is a substrate of CYP3A4.</li><li><strong>Excretion:</strong> 81% of total radioactivity recovered (54% in feces, 27% in urine).</li></ul><h3><strong>Embryo-Fetal Toxicity</strong></h3><p>Cabanib 60 mg can cause fetal harm based on animal studies. Advise pregnant women of the potential risks and advise females of reproductive potential to use effective contraception during treatment and for 4 months after the last dose.</p><p><br></p><p>Let me know if you\'d like further clarification on any of the sections!</p>', 'active', '1929_1742357549.png', 9, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 09:12:29', '2025-05-01 09:26:33'),
(3485, 'Vorinox', 'Voriconazole', 1000.00, 1, 'x 40 ml bottle', '40 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '200 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Vorinox 40 ml</strong></h3><p>Vorinox 40 ml is an azole antifungal drug indicated for the following fungal infections in individuals aged 12 years and older:</p><ul><li><strong>Invasive Aspergillosis</strong></li><li><strong>Disseminated candidiasis and candidemia (non-neutropenic)</strong>, including infections in the skin, abdomen, kidneys, bladder wall, and wounds</li><li><strong>Esophageal candidiasis</strong></li><li><strong>Severe infections caused by Fusarium species</strong>, including <em>Fusarium solani</em></li><li><strong>Infections caused by Scedosporium apiospermum</strong></li><li><strong>Fungal infections resistant or intolerant to alternative treatments</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Other Antifungal preparations</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Vorinox 40 ml works by inhibiting the enzyme <strong>14 alpha-lanosterol demethylase</strong>, which is mediated by fungal <strong>cytochrome P450</strong>. This step is crucial for the production of <strong>ergosterol</strong>, a key component of the fungal cell membrane. Inhibition leads to the accumulation of 14 alpha-methyl sterols and the loss of ergosterol, disrupting fungal cell membrane integrity. Vorinox is more selective for fungal P450 enzymes than for mammalian enzymes.</p><h3><strong>Dosage of Vorinox 40 ml</strong></h3><h4><strong>Oral Dosage (Tablets or Powder for Suspension)</strong></h4><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For individuals weighing 40 kg or more:Loading dose</strong>: 400 mg (10 ml) every 12 hours for the first 24 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance dose</strong>: 200 mg (5 ml) twice daily after the first 24 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For individuals weighing less than 40 kg:Loading dose</strong>: 200 mg (5 ml) every 12 hours for the first 24 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance dose</strong>: 100 mg (2.5 ml) twice daily after the first 24 hours</li></ul><h4><strong>Injection Dosage</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Invasive Aspergillosis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Loading dose</strong>: 6 mg/kg every 12 hours for the first 24 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance dose</strong>: 4 mg/kg every 12 hours</li><li><strong>Candidemia and other deep tissue Candida infections</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Loading dose</strong>: 6 mg/kg every 12 hours for the first 24 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance dose</strong>: 3-4 mg/kg every 12 hours</li><li><strong>Scedosporiosis and Fusariosis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Loading dose</strong>: 6 mg/kg every 12 hours for the first 24 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance dose</strong>: 4 mg/kg every 12 hours</li></ul><h3><strong>Administration</strong></h3><ul><li>Vorinox 40 ml should be taken <strong>at least one hour before or one hour after a meal</strong>.</li><li>The treatment duration should be as short as possible, based on clinical and mycological response.</li><li>Prophylaxis can begin the day of a transplant and continue for up to <strong>100 days</strong>, depending on the risk of <strong>invasive fungal infections (IFI)</strong>.</li><li><strong>Prolonged exposure</strong> (beyond 180 days) should be carefully considered, especially in immunosuppressed patients.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Inhibitors and inducers of CYP3A4, CYP2C9, and CYP2C19</strong> may alter Vorinox levels. Caution should be used, and adjustments may be required.</li><li><strong>Phenytoin</strong> and <strong>efavirenz</strong> may necessitate <strong>higher maintenance doses</strong> of Vorinox.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Known <strong>hypersensitivity</strong> to Vorinox or any of its components.</li><li>Avoid co-administration with <strong>sirolimus</strong>, <strong>terfenadine</strong>, <strong>astemizole</strong>, <strong>cisapride</strong>, <strong>pimozide</strong>, or <strong>quinidine</strong> due to risk of significant adverse effects.</li><li>Do not use with <strong>rifampin</strong>, <strong>carbamazepine</strong>, <strong>long-acting barbiturates</strong>, <strong>efavirenz</strong>, <strong>ritonavir</strong>, <strong>rifabutin</strong>, <strong>ergot alkaloids</strong>, or <strong>St. John’s Wort</strong> due to the risk of reduced efficacy.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Abdominal pain, anemia, impaired vision, headaches, chest pain, nausea, diarrhea.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>Limited data on the use of Vorinox 40 ml during <strong>pregnancy</strong>. It should only be used if the potential benefit outweighs the risk.</li><li>It is unknown if Vorinox 40 ml is excreted into <strong>breast milk</strong>, so <strong>breastfeeding should be discontinued</strong> once treatment begins.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions and Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Long-term use (over 180 days) is associated with the risk of <strong>squamous cell carcinoma (SCC)</strong> of the skin.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong> requires dose adjustments, particularly in <strong>mild to severe cirrhosis</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose</strong></h3><ul><li>No specific data is available on overdose.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Tablets</strong>: Store at temperatures below 25°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li><strong>Suspension powder</strong>: Store between <strong>2° and 8°C</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustment required.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established for children under the age of <strong>two</strong>.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment is needed.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Dose reduction may be required in <strong>mild to severe liver cirrhosis</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Other Antifungal preparations</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Vorinox is a <strong>triazole antifungal</strong> that inhibits the <strong>CYP450-dependent 14-alpha sterol demethylase</strong>, disrupting ergosterol synthesis and impairing fungal cell membrane integrity.</p>', 'active', '1574_1742357965.png', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 09:19:25', '2025-05-05 17:59:20');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3486, 'Sporagin', 'Itraconazole', 15.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sporagin 100 mg</strong></p><p>Sporagin 100 mg is indicated in the following conditions:</p><ul><li><strong>Tinea corporis</strong></li><li><strong>Tinea cruris</strong></li><li><strong>Tinea pedis</strong></li><li><strong>Tinea manus</strong></li><li><strong>Vulvovaginal candidiasis</strong></li><li><strong>Fungal keratitis</strong></li><li><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong></li><li><strong>Oropharyngeal candidiasis</strong></li><li><strong>Onychomycosis</strong></li><li><strong>Histoplasmosis</strong></li><li><strong>Systemic Infections</strong>: Aspergillosis, Candidiasis, and Cryptococcus (when other drugs are ineffective)</li><li><strong>Antifungal Prophylaxis</strong>: To prevent systemic fungal infection in severely neutropenic patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Sporagin 100 mg is an orally active triazole antifungal drug with broad-spectrum activity. It inhibits cytochrome P-450 dependent enzymes, leading to the impairment of ergosterol biosynthesis, a vital component of the cell membrane in yeast and fungal cells. This disruption affects cell wall formation, growth, and membrane-bound enzyme activity, ultimately inhibiting the growth of the fungi.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Sporagin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tinea corporis, Tinea cruris</strong>: 100 mg daily for 2 weeks</li><li><strong>Tinea pedis, Tinea manus</strong>: 100 mg daily for 4 weeks</li><li><strong>Vulvovaginal candidiasis</strong>: 200 mg twice daily for 1 day or 200 mg daily for 3 days</li><li><strong>Fungal keratitis</strong>: 200 mg daily for 3 weeks</li><li><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong>: 200 mg daily for 1 week</li><li><strong>Oropharyngeal candidiasis</strong>: 100 mg daily (200 mg for AIDS patients) for 15 days</li><li><strong>Onychomycosis</strong>: 200 mg daily for 3 months; or 200 mg twice daily for 7 days (repeat courses with 21-day interval)</li><li><strong>Histoplasmosis</strong>: 200 mg 1-2 times daily</li><li><strong>Systemic Infections (Aspergillosis, Candidiasis, Cryptococcus)</strong>: 200 mg once daily, increased to 200 mg twice daily for invasive or cryptococcal meningitis</li><li><strong>Antifungal Prophylaxis</strong>: 100 mg daily in severely neutropenic patients</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Sporagin 100 mg</strong></p><p>Take with food, immediately after a full meal.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Sporagin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Inhibits the metabolism of <strong>Cyclosporine</strong> and <strong>Warfarin</strong></li><li>Interaction with <strong>Terfenadine</strong>, <strong>Phenobarbitone</strong>, <strong>Midazolam</strong> has been reported</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to the drug</li><li>Pregnant women</li><li>Patients with severe hepatic disease</li><li>Rifampicin therapy</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Sporagin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Nausea, abdominal pain, dyspepsia, constipation</li><li>Headache, dizziness</li><li>Raised liver enzymes, hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice</li><li>Peripheral neuropathy</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated during pregnancy</li><li>Not recommended during breastfeeding</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor hepatic enzyme values in patients with preexisting liver conditions</li><li>Periodic liver function tests for patients on long-term treatment (over 1 month)</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sporagin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No reported experience of overdose</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light, and in a dry place. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Sporagin 100 mg is an orally active triazole antifungal agent with broad-spectrum activity. It inhibits cytochrome P-450 dependent enzymes, impairing ergosterol biosynthesis and disrupting fungal cell membrane integrity.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Not recommended during pregnancy or lactation unless prescribed by a physician.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><p>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</p>', 'active', '1065_1742358087.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 09:21:27', '2025-05-04 21:23:03'),
(3487, 'Linez', 'Linezolid', 85.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Linez 600 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vancomycin-Resistant Enterococcus faecium</strong> infections, including cases with concurrent bacteremia.</li><li><strong>Nosocomial pneumonia</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (methicillin-susceptible and -resistant strains) or <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> (including multi-drug resistant strains). Combination therapy may be clinically indicated if the documented or presumptive pathogens include Gram-negative organisms.</li><li><strong>Complicated skin and skin structure infections</strong>, including diabetic foot infections (without concomitant osteomyelitis), caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (methicillin-susceptible and methicillin-resistant strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, or <em>Streptococcus agalactiae</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (methicillin-susceptible only) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> (including multi-drug resistant strains), including cases with concurrent bacteremia or <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (methicillin-susceptible strains only).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Macrolides</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Linez 600 mg is a synthetic antibacterial agent belonging to the oxazolidinone class, with activity against Gram-positive aerobic bacteria, some Gram-positive anaerobic bacteria, and certain Gram-negative bacteria. It selectively inhibits bacterial protein synthesis by binding to the 23S ribosomal RNA of the 50S subunit of the bacterial ribosome, preventing the formation of the 70S initiation complex, which is essential for bacterial translation.</p><ul><li><strong>Bacteriostatic</strong> against enterococci and staphylococci.</li><li><strong>Bactericidal</strong> for the majority of <em>Streptococcus</em> strains.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Linez 600 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Complicated skin and skin structure infections, Community-acquired pneumonia, including concurrent bacteremia</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric Patients (Birth through 11 Years of Age)</strong>: 10 mg/kg IV or oral, 3 times a day (t.i.d.).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 Years and Older)</strong>: 600 mg IV or oral, 2 times a day (b.i.d.).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duration</strong>: 10 to 14 days.</li><li><strong>Nosocomial pneumonia, Vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus faecium infections, including concurrent bacteremia</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric Patients (Birth through 11 Years of Age)</strong>: 10 mg/kg IV or oral, 3 times a day (t.i.d.).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 Years and Older)</strong>: 600 mg IV or oral, 2 times a day (b.i.d.).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duration</strong>: 14 to 28 days.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infections</strong>:</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients (Birth through 11 Years of Age)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">&lt;5 years: 10 mg/kg oral, 3 times a day (t.i.d.).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-11 years: 10 mg/kg oral, 2 times a day (b.i.d.).</li><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 Years and Older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adults: 400 mg oral, 2 times a day (b.i.d.).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adolescents: 600 mg oral, 2 times a day (b.i.d.).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duration</strong>: 10 to 14 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Linez 600 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral Suspension</strong>:</li></ul><ol><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Shake the bottle to loosen powder.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Add 75 ml of boiled and cooled water to the bottle.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Shake well after each addition until powder is in suspension.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Shake the suspension well before use and store tightly closed. Use within 21 days after reconstitution.</li></ol><ul><li><strong>Intravenous (IV) Administration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Administer over 30 to 120 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not use in series connections or introduce additives.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Store at room temperature and protect from freezing.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">May exhibit a yellow color that does not affect potency.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Linez 600 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Monoamine Oxidase Inhibition</strong>: Reversible, nonselective inhibitor of monoamine oxidase.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adrenergic Agents</strong>: Can enhance the pressor response to sympathomimetic agents.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Serotonergic Agents</strong>: Risk of serotonin syndrome when used with serotonergic drugs (e.g., SSRIs, SNRIs, triptans, meperidine).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Linez 600 mg or components.</li><li>Use with monoamine oxidase inhibitors (e.g., Phenelzine, Isocarboxazid) or within 2 weeks of their use.</li><li>Contraindicated in patients with uncontrolled hypertension, pheochromocytoma, thyrotoxicosis, carcinoid syndrome, or those taking sympathomimetic agents.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Linez 600 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Diarrhea, headache, nausea.</li><li>Other: Oral moniliasis, vaginal moniliasis, hypertension, dyspepsia, abdominal pain, pruritus, tongue discoloration.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: Should only be used if the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li>Caution in breastfeeding, as it is not known if excreted in human milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Watch for <strong>serotonin syndrome</strong> when using with serotonergic agents.</li><li><strong>Visual impairment</strong>: If symptoms occur, prompt ophthalmic evaluation is recommended.</li><li>Seizures have been reported; use cautiously in patients with a history of seizures.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptomatic and supportive care.</li><li><strong>Hemodialysis</strong> removes about 30% of the dose in 3 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at room temperature (15°C-30°C), away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li>Oral suspension: Add 75 ml of boiled and cooled water to the bottle, shake well after each addition. Use within 21 days.</li><li>IV injection: Administer over 30 to 120 minutes, and do not use in series connections.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Macrolides</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Linez 600 mg inhibits bacterial protein synthesis by binding to the 23S rRNA of the 50S ribosomal subunit, disrupting bacterial translation.</p><p>Let me know if you need more details!</p>', 'active', '1892_1742358266.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 09:24:26', '2025-03-19 09:24:26'),
(3488, 'Voriderm', 'Voriconazole', 110.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Voriderm 200 mg</h3><p>Voriderm 200 mg is an azole antifungal drug recommended for individuals 12 years of age and older for the treatment of various fungal infections, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Invasive Aspergillosis</strong></li><li><strong>Disseminated Candidiasis and Candidemia</strong> (non-neutropenic) affecting the skin, abdomen, kidneys, bladder wall, and wounds</li><li><strong>Esophageal Candidiasis</strong></li><li><strong>Severe Fusarium infections</strong> (e.g., <em>Fusarium solani</em>)</li><li><strong>Scedosporium infections</strong> (<em>Scedosporium apiospermum</em>)</li><li>For patients <strong>resistant or intolerant</strong> to alternative treatments.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Other Antifungal Preparations</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Voriderm 200 mg primarily works by inhibiting the enzyme <strong>14 alpha-lanosterol demethylase</strong>, which is crucial for the production of fungal ergosterol. This inhibition causes the accumulation of 14 alpha-methyl sterols, leading to a reduction of ergosterol in the fungal cell membrane, which results in the antifungal effect. The drug is more selective for fungal cytochrome P450 enzymes than for mammalian cytochrome P450 enzymes.</p><h3>Dosage of Voriderm 200 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Oral Administration:For patients ≥ 40 kg body weight</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Loading dose: 400 mg (10 ml) every 12 hours for the first 24 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose (after 24 hours): 200 mg (5 ml) twice daily.</li><li><strong>For patients &lt; 40 kg body weight</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Loading dose: 200 mg (5 ml) every 12 hours for the first 24 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose (after 24 hours): 100 mg (2.5 ml) twice daily.</li><li><strong>Injection (for invasive infections):Invasive Aspergillosis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Loading dose: 6 mg/kg every 12 hours for the first 24 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 4 mg/kg every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Candidemia and other deep tissue Candida infections</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Loading dose: 6 mg/kg every 12 hours for the first 24 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 3-4 mg/kg every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Scedosporiosis and Fusariosis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Loading dose: 6 mg/kg every 12 hours for the first 24 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 4 mg/kg every 12 hours.</li></ul><h3>Administration</h3><ul><li>Take Voriderm 200 mg tablets or suspension at least <strong>one hour before or one hour after a meal</strong>.</li><li>The length of treatment should be as brief as possible depending on clinical and mycological response.</li><li>Prophylaxis can begin the day of transplantation, lasting up to 100 days, depending on the risk of fungal infection.</li></ul><h3>Drug Interactions</h3><ul><li><strong>CYP3A4, CYP2C9, and CYP2C19 inhibitors or inducers</strong>: Adjust dosages and monitor for side effects or ineffectiveness.</li><li>Drugs such as <strong>phenytoin</strong> or <strong>efavirenz</strong> may require dose adjustments of Voriderm 200 mg.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Voriderm 200 mg or any of its ingredients.</li><li>Avoid co-administration with <strong>sirolimus</strong>, <strong>terfenadine</strong>, <strong>astemizole</strong>, <strong>cisapride</strong>, <strong>pimozide</strong>, or <strong>quinidine</strong> due to the risk of serious adverse effects.</li><li>Avoid use with <strong>rifampin</strong>, <strong>carbamazepine</strong>, <strong>long-acting barbiturates</strong>, <strong>efavirenz</strong>, <strong>ritonavir</strong>, <strong>rifabutin</strong>, <strong>ergot alkaloids</strong>, and <strong>St. John\'s Wort</strong> as these may reduce efficacy.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Anemia</li><li>Impaired vision</li><li>Headaches</li><li>Chest pain</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Diarrhea</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy and Lactation</h3><ul><li>Insufficient data on the use of Voriderm 200 mg during pregnancy.</li><li>Should only be used during pregnancy if the potential benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li>It is unknown if Voriderm 200 mg is excreted in breast milk; therefore, breastfeeding should be discontinued during treatment.</li></ul><h3>Precautions and Warnings</h3><ul><li>Long-term treatment (beyond 180 days) may be linked to <strong>squamous cell carcinoma of the skin (SCC)</strong>.</li><li>Careful consideration of the benefit-risk balance is needed for extended use.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li>There is no specific data available for overdose of Voriderm 200 mg.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Store below <strong>25°C</strong>, away from light.</li><li><strong>Powder for suspension</strong> should be stored between <strong>2°C and 8°C</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Older adults</strong>: No dosage change required.</li><li><strong>Children under 12</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No adjustment necessary for oral doses in patients with mild to severe renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: For patients with mild to moderate hepatic cirrhosis, use the normal loading dose and reduce the maintenance dose by 50%. Use with caution in severe hepatic impairment.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Voriderm 200 mg is a <strong>triazole antifungal</strong> that inhibits <strong>CYP450-dependent 14-alpha sterol demethylase</strong>, reducing ergosterol synthesis and disrupting the fungal cell membrane.</p>', 'active', '1151_1742358372.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 09:26:12', '2025-03-19 09:26:12'),
(3489, 'Padi-D', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 150.00, 1, 'x 30 ml bottle', '30 ml bottle', 'Oral Solution', '30 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1471_1742358566.webp', 9, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 09:29:26', '2025-05-04 10:10:37'),
(3490, 'Duodart', 'Tamsulosin Hydrochloride + Dutasteride', 20.00, 10, 'packet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '0.4 mg+0.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Duodart 0.4 mg+0.5 mg</strong></p><p>Duodart 0.4 mg+0.5 mg capsule is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Treatment of moderate to severe symptoms of benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH).</li><li>Reduction in the risk of acute urinary retention and surgery in patients with moderate to severe symptoms of BPH.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>BPH</li><li>Urinary retention</li><li>Urinary incontinence</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Dutasteride and Tamsulosin combination capsules contain:</p><ul><li><strong>Dutasteride</strong>: A selective inhibitor of both type 1 and type 2 isoforms of steroid 5 alpha-reductase, an enzyme that converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT).</li><li><strong>Tamsulosin</strong>: An antagonist of alpha1A-adrenoceptors in the prostate.</li></ul><p><strong>Dutasteride</strong> inhibits the androgen responsible for the development and enlargement of the prostate gland. It is a competitive and specific inhibitor of both type 1 and type 2 5 alpha-reductase isoenzymes.</p><p><strong>Tamsulosin</strong> blocks alpha1-adrenoceptors in the prostate, leading to relaxation of the smooth muscles in the bladder neck and prostate, improving urine flow and reducing symptoms of BPH.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Recommended dose</strong>: 1 capsule (0.5 mg Dutasteride and 0.4 mg Tamsulosin Hydrochloride) taken once daily, approximately 30 minutes after the same meal each day.</li><li>The capsule should be swallowed whole and not chewed or opened.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage for Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed for patients with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: Caution should be used in patients with mild to moderate hepatic impairment. The capsule is contraindicated in patients with severe hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Effects of other drugs on the pharmacokinetics of Dutasteride</strong>: Dutasteride is mainly metabolized via CYP3A4 and CYP3A5. Use with CYP3A4 inhibitors (like ritonavir or itraconazole) may increase serum concentrations of Dutasteride.</li><li><strong>Effects of Dutasteride on the pharmacokinetics of other drugs</strong>: Dutasteride has no significant effect on the pharmacokinetics of Tamsulosin, warfarin, or digoxin.</li><li><strong>Tamsulosin interactions</strong>: Concomitant administration with drugs that reduce blood pressure, such as PDE5 inhibitors or other alpha-1 adrenergic blockers, may enhance hypotensive effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Not for use by women and children.</li><li>Contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to Dutasteride, Tamsulosin, or other components of the formulation.</li><li>Contraindicated in patients with a history of orthostatic hypotension and severe hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include: impotence, decreased libido, breast disorders, ejaculation disorders, and dizziness.</li><li>The incidence of these effects may be higher in combination therapy compared to monotherapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Contraindicated for use by women, especially those who are or may become pregnant. Dutasteride may affect fetal development in male fetuses.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether Tamsulosin or Dutasteride are excreted in human milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cardiac failure</strong>: Higher incidence of cardiac failure observed in some studies.</li><li><strong>Prostate-specific antigen (PSA)</strong>: Serum PSA levels are reduced by approximately 50%, so a new baseline must be established after 6 months of treatment.</li><li><strong>Hypotension</strong>: Caution in patients taking PDE5 inhibitors or experiencing orthostatic hypotension.</li><li><strong>Intraoperative Floppy Iris Syndrome</strong>: A potential complication in cataract surgery for patients on Tamsulosin.</li><li><strong>Leaking Capsules</strong>: Women, children, and adolescents must avoid contact with leaking capsules.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dutasteride</strong>: No specific antidote; symptomatic and supportive treatment should be provided.</li><li><strong>Tamsulosin</strong>: Acute overdose may cause hypotension, which can be managed with fluid replacement and possibly vasopressors. Dialysis is unlikely to be beneficial.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment required for renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: Caution should be used in patients with mild to moderate hepatic impairment. The use of Duodart is contraindicated in severe hepatic impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '1483_1742358731.jpg', 58, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 09:32:11', '2025-03-21 21:42:40'),
(3491, 'Uroblock', 'Mirabegron', 30.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Uroblock 25 mg</strong></p><p>Uroblock 25 mg is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of urgency, increased micturition frequency, and urgency incontinence as may occur in adult patients with overactive bladder (OAB) syndrome.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>BPH/ Urinary retention/ Urinary incontinence</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Uroblock 25 mg is a potent and selective beta 3-adrenoceptor agonist. Uroblock 25 mg showed relaxation of bladder smooth muscle in rat and human isolated tissue, increased cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) concentrations in rat bladder tissue, and showed a bladder relaxant effect in rat urinary bladder function models. Uroblock 25 mg increased mean voided volume per micturition and decreased the frequency of non-voiding contractions, without affecting voiding pressure or residual urine in rat models of bladder overactivity. In a monkey model, Uroblock 25 mg showed decreased voiding frequency. These results indicate that Uroblock 25 mg enhances urine storage function by stimulating beta 3-adrenoceptors in the bladder. During the urine storage phase, when urine accumulates in the bladder, sympathetic nerve stimulation predominates. Noradrenaline is released from nerve terminals, leading predominantly to beta-adrenoceptor activation in the bladder musculature and hence bladder smooth muscle relaxation. During the urine voiding phase, the bladder is predominantly under parasympathetic nervous system control. Acetylcholine, released from pelvic nerve terminals, stimulates cholinergic M2 and M3 receptors, inducing bladder contraction. The activation of the M2 pathway also inhibits beta 3-adrenoceptor-induced increases in cAMP. Therefore, beta 3-adrenoceptor stimulation should not interfere with the voiding process. This was confirmed in rats with partial urethral obstruction, where Uroblock 25 mg decreased the frequency of non-voiding contractions without affecting the voided volume per micturition, voiding pressure, or residual urine volume.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Uroblock 25 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Recommended starting dose: 25 mg once daily, alone or in combination with solifenacin succinate 5 mg, once daily.</li><li>Based on individual efficacy and tolerability, the dose may be increased to 50 mg once daily, alone or in combination with solifenacin succinate 5 mg, once daily.</li><li>Swallow whole with water, with or without food. Do not chew, divide, or crush.</li><li><strong>Patients with Severe Renal Impairment or Patients with Moderate Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Maximum dose is 25 mg Uroblock 25 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Patients with End Stage Renal Disease (ESRD) or Patients with Severe Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Uroblock 25 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults including elderly:</strong> The recommended starting dose is Uroblock 25 mg tablet once daily with or without food. Based on individual patient efficacy and tolerability, the dose may be increased to Uroblock 25 mg 50 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal or hepatic impairment:</strong>Patients with severe renal impairment (ClCr 15 to 29 mL/min or eGFR 15 to 29 mL/min/1.73 m2) or moderate hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh Class B), the daily dose of Uroblock 25 mg should not exceed 25 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uroblock 25 mg has not been studied in patients with end-stage renal disease (GFR &lt;15 mL/min/1.73 m2 or patients requiring haemodialysis) or severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh Class C) and it is therefore not recommended for use in these patient populations.</li><li><strong>Gender:</strong> No dose adjustment is necessary according to gender.</li><li><strong>Pediatric population:</strong> The safety and efficacy of Uroblock 25 mg in children below 18 years of age have not yet been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Uroblock 25 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Uroblock 25 mg tablet is to be taken once daily, with liquids, swallowed whole and is not to be chewed, divided, or crushed.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Uroblock 25 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Effect of enzyme inhibitors:</strong> Uroblock 25 mg exposure (AUC) was increased 1.8-fold in the presence of the strong inhibitor of CYP3A/P-gp ketoconazole in healthy volunteers. No dose adjustment is needed when Uroblock 25 mg is combined with inhibitors of CYP3A and/or P-gp.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">However, in patients with mild to moderate renal impairment or mild hepatic impairment concomitantly receiving strong CYP3A inhibitors, such as itraconazole, ketoconazole, ritonavir, and clarithromycin, the recommended dose is 25 mg once daily with or without food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uroblock 25 mg is not recommended in patients with severe renal impairment or patients with moderate hepatic impairment concomitantly receiving strong CYP3A inhibitors.</li><li><strong>Effect of enzyme inducers:</strong> Substances that are inducers of CYP3A or P-gp decrease the plasma concentrations of Uroblock 25 mg. No dose adjustment is needed for Uroblock 25 mg when administered with therapeutic doses of rifampicin or other CYP3A or P-gp inducers.</li><li><strong>Effect of Uroblock 25 mg on CYP2D6 substrates:</strong> Caution is advised if Uroblock 25 mg is co-administered with medicinal products with a narrow therapeutic index and significantly metabolized by CYP2D6, such as thioridazine, Type 1 C antiarrhythmics (e.g., flecainide, propafenone), and tricyclic antidepressants (e.g., imipramine, desipramine).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Uroblock 25 mg is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to the active substance or to any of the excipients and severe uncontrolled hypertension defined as systolic blood pressure 180 mm Hg and/or diastolic blood pressure 110 mm Hg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Uroblock 25 mg</strong></p><ul><li>The most common side effects reported for patients treated with Uroblock 25 mg 50 mg during the three 12-week phase 3 double-blind, placebo-controlled studies are tachycardia and urinary tract infections.</li><li>Serious adverse reactions included atrial fibrillation (0.2%).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in Pregnancy:</strong> There are limited data from the use of Uroblock 25 mg in pregnant women. Studies in animals have shown reproductive toxicity. Uroblock 25 mg is not recommended during pregnancy and in women of childbearing potential not using contraception.</li><li><strong>Use in Lactation:</strong> Uroblock 25 mg is excreted in the milk of rodents and therefore, is predicted to be present in human milk. No studies have been conducted to assess the impact of Uroblock 25 mg on milk production in humans, its presence in human breast milk, or its effects on the breast-fed child. Uroblock 25 mg should not be administered during breast-feeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> Uroblock 25 mg has not been studied in patients with end-stage renal disease (GFR &lt;15 mL/min/1.73 m2 or patients requiring haemodialysis) and therefore, it is not recommended for use in this patient population.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment:</strong> Uroblock 25 mg has not been studied in patients with severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh Class C) and, therefore, it is not recommended for use in these patient populations.</li><li><strong>Hypertension:</strong> Uroblock 25 mg can increase blood pressure. Blood pressure should be measured at baseline and periodically during treatment with Uroblock 25 mg, especially in hypertensive patients.</li><li><strong>QT prolongation:</strong> Caution should be exercised when administering Uroblock 25 mg in patients with congenital or acquired QT prolongation.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Uroblock 25 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Uroblock 25 mg has been administered to healthy volunteers at single doses up to 400 mg. At this dose, adverse events reported included palpitations and increased pulse rate exceeding 100 beats per minute (bpm).</li><li>Treatment for overdose should be symptomatic and supportive. In the event of overdose, pulse rate, blood pressure, and ECG monitoring is recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal and Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Uroblock 25 mg has not been studied in patients with end-stage renal disease.</li><li><strong>Gender:</strong> No dose adjustment is necessary according to gender.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> The safety and efficacy of Uroblock 25 mg in children below 18 years of age have not yet been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>BPH/ Urinary retention/ Urinary incontinence</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Uroblock 25 mg is the first beta-3 adrenoceptor agonist. Uroblock 25 mg exerts its effect via a dual mechanism, both directly acting on the bladder smooth muscle and also via the sensory nervous system, it increases the levels of cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cyclic AMP) and leads to relaxation of the detrusor smooth muscle during storage phase of the urinary bladder fill-void cycle by activation of beta-3 adrenoceptors which increase bladder capacity.</p>', 'active', '242_1742358917.jpg', 58, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 09:35:17', '2025-03-21 20:52:22'),
(3492, 'Ternilla SR', 'Aceclofenac', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the text with the appropriate headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Ternilla SR 200 mg</strong></p><p>Ternilla SR 200 mg is indicated for the relief of pain and inflammation in osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, toothache, trauma, and lumbago.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for Osteoarthritis, Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Ternilla SR 200 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ternilla SR 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Extended release tablet:</strong> The recommended dose in adults is one 200 mg Ternilla SR 200 mg tablet daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Film-coated tablet:</strong> The recommended dose in adults is 100 mg, twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ternilla SR 200 mg</strong></p><p>No significant drug interactions have been observed, but close monitoring of patients is required when it is used with:</p><ul><li><strong>Lithium and Digoxin:</strong> May increase plasma concentration of lithium and digoxin.</li><li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> May interact with the activity of diuretics.</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants:</strong> May enhance the activity of anticoagulants.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate:</strong> May increase the plasma level of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Ternilla SR 200 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to it or in whom aspirin or NSAIDs precipitate attacks of asthma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ternilla SR 200 mg</strong></p><p>Ternilla SR 200 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of Ternilla SR 200 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in patients with active or suspected peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding, moderate to severe hepatic impairment, and cardiac or renal impairment. Caution should also be exercised in patients suffering from dizziness or urticaria.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Ternilla SR 200 mg in children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Drugs for Osteoarthritis, Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Ternilla SR 200 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The use of Ternilla SR 200 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Ternilla SR 200 mg in children.</p>', 'in-active', '372_1742359096.jpg', 1, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 09:38:16', '2025-05-13 05:16:41'),
(3493, 'Mefoglip', 'Glipizide + Metformin Hydrochloride', 2.80, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.5 mg+500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Mefoglip 2.5 mg + 500 mg</strong></h3><p>Glipizide &amp; Metformin is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Initial therapy</strong>, as an adjunct to diet and exercise, to improve glycemic control in patients with type 2 diabetes whose hyperglycemia cannot be satisfactorily managed with diet and exercise alone.</li><li><strong>Second-line therapy</strong> when diet, exercise, and initial treatment with a Sulfonylurea or Metformin do not result in adequate glycemic control in patients with type 2 diabetes.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Combination Oral Hypoglycemic Preparations</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Metformin</strong>: A biguanide antihyperglycemic agent that improves glucose tolerance in patients with type 2 diabetes by lowering both basal and postprandial plasma glucose. It works by:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Decreasing hepatic glucose production.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Decreasing intestinal absorption of glucose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Improving insulin sensitivity by increasing peripheral glucose uptake and utilization.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Unlike sulfonylureas, metformin does not cause hypoglycemia or hyperinsulinemia.</li><li><strong>Glipizide</strong>: A sulfonylurea that lowers blood glucose by stimulating the release of insulin from the pancreas, dependent upon functioning beta cells in the pancreatic islets. Extrapancreatic effects may also play a role in its mechanism of action.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Glipizide &amp; Metformin should be taken with meals.</strong></li><li><strong>Initial Therapy</strong>: Recommended starting dose is <strong>250 mg Metformin / 2.5 mg Glipizide</strong> once a day with a meal.</li><li><strong>Second-Line Therapy</strong>: Recommended starting dose is <strong>500 mg Metformin / 2.5 mg Glipizide</strong> or <strong>500 mg Metformin / 5 mg Glipizide</strong>, taken twice daily with morning and evening meals.</li><li><strong>Maximum recommended dose</strong>: <strong>2000 mg Metformin / 20 mg Glipizide</strong> per day.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Cimetidine</strong>: Reduces the renal clearance of Metformin.</li><li><strong>Alcohol</strong>: Potentiates the antihyperglycemic and hyperlactataemic effects of Metformin.</li><li><strong>Nifedipine</strong>: Enhances Metformin absorption but has minimal effect on Nifedipine.</li><li><strong>Other Drugs</strong>: Certain drugs like NSAIDs, azoles, salicylates, sulfonamides, and beta blockers may potentiate the hypoglycemic action of sulfonylureas.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to the drug</li><li>Renal impairment</li><li>Diabetic coma and ketoacidosis</li><li>Chronic renal diseases</li><li>Congestive heart failure</li><li>Trauma, dehydration, alcohol dependence</li><li>Pregnancy and breastfeeding</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Common side effects: Hypoglycemia, diarrhea, nausea/vomiting, abdominal pain, headache, musculoskeletal pain.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Contraindicated during pregnancy and lactation.</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Renal function</strong>: Metformin is excreted by the kidney, and the risk of lactic acidosis increases in patients with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Elderly patients</strong>: Should be carefully titrated to avoid complications due to reduced renal function with aging.</li><li><strong>Surgical procedures</strong>: Metformin therapy should be temporarily suspended for any surgical procedure and resumed once renal function is evaluated.</li><li><strong>Concomitant therapy</strong>: Blood glucose should be monitored during combination therapy with sulfonylureas and insulin, especially in hospitalized patients.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>25ºC</strong> in a cool, dry place.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Combination Oral Hypoglycemic Preparations</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Metformin</strong>: Decreases hepatic glucose production, reduces intestinal glucose absorption, and improves insulin sensitivity, reducing basal and postprandial blood glucose.</li><li><strong>Glipizide</strong>: Stimulates insulin release from the pancreas, helping to lower blood glucose levels.</li></ul>', 'active', '1920_1742359323.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 09:42:03', '2025-05-04 20:23:09'),
(3494, 'Cardocal', 'Cilnidipine', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cardocal 5 mg</strong></p><p> Cardocal 5 mg is indicated for the management of hypertension, particularly for end-organ protection. It is beneficial in elderly patients and those with diabetes and albuminuria. The drug has increasingly been used in patients with chronic kidney disease. Hypertension refers to high blood pressure, which is caused by excessive pressure on the blood vessels. This elevated pressure can damage the blood vessels over time.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Calcium-channel blockers</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cardocal 5 mg is a dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker. It binds to the dihydropyridine binding sites of the L-type voltage-dependent calcium channel and inhibits Ca²⁺ influx across the cell membranes of vascular smooth muscle cells, leading to muscle relaxation and vasodilation. Cardocal also inhibits Ca²⁺ influx via N-type voltage-dependent calcium channels in sympathetic nerve cell membranes. This reduces the release of norepinephrine from sympathetic nerve terminals, thus suppressing reflex heart rate increases after blood pressure reduction.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 5-10 mg once daily after breakfast. Maximum dose: 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric use:</strong> The safety of Cardocal 5 mg in pediatric patients has not been established.</li><li><strong>Use in the elderly:</strong> Since elderly individuals may be more susceptible to hypotension, therapy should begin with the lowest possible dose (5 mg).</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><p> Cardocal 5 mg may interact with:</p><ul><li>Other antihypertensive medications</li><li>Antipsychotics that cause hypotension</li><li>Quinidine</li><li>Carbamazepine</li><li>Phenytoin</li><li>Rifampicin</li><li>Cimetidine</li><li>Erythromycin</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Cardocal 5 mg is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Known sensitivity to Cardocal 5 mg or its excipients</li><li>Cardiogenic shock</li><li>Recent myocardial infarction (MI) or acute unstable angina</li><li>Severe aortic stenosis</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Dizziness</li><li>Flushing</li><li>Headache</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Peripheral edema</li><li>Palpitations</li><li>Gastrointestinal disturbances</li><li>Increased micturition frequency</li><li>Lethargy</li><li>Eye pain</li><li>Depression</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Cardocal 5 mg should not be administered to pregnant women or those who might become pregnant. It is also not advisable for nursing mothers. If its use is necessary, the patient should be instructed to discontinue lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Cardocal 5 mg should be used with caution in:</p><ul><li>Patients with serious hepatic dysfunction</li><li>Patients with a history of severe adverse reactions to calcium antagonists</li><li>During discontinuation, the dosage should be gradually reduced under close observation.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Calcium-channel blockers</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Cardocal 5 mg binds to the dihydropyridine binding sites of the L-type voltage-dependent calcium channel, inhibiting Ca²⁺ influx into vascular smooth muscle cells, which causes vasodilation. It also inhibits Ca²⁺ influx via N-type voltage-dependent calcium channels in sympathetic nerve cell membranes, reducing the release of norepinephrine. This leads to suppressed reflex heart rate increases after blood pressure reduction.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Cardocal 5 mg should not be administered to pregnant women or those who might become pregnant. If it is necessary, the patient should stop breastfeeding.</p>', 'active', '1442_1742359430.webp', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 09:43:50', '2025-03-19 09:43:50');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3495, 'Tezo', 'Tedizolid Phosphate', 300.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Tezo (Tedizolid) 200 mg:</h3><h4>Indications:</h4><p>Tedizolid is an oxazolidinone-class antibacterial drug used in adults for the treatment of acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections (ABSSSI) caused by designated susceptible bacteria. To reduce the development of drug-resistant bacteria and maintain the effectiveness of Tedizolid, it should be used only to treat or prevent infections that are proven or strongly suspected to be caused by susceptible bacteria.</p><h4>Therapeutic Class:</h4><p>Other antibiotic</p><h4>Pharmacology:</h4><p>After conversion to its active form by phosphatases, Tedizolid exerts its antibacterial activity through inhibition of protein synthesis by binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit of susceptible Gram-positive bacteria. Cross-resistance between other non-oxazolidinone antibacterial drugs is unlikely because Tedizolid inhibits bacterial protein synthesis through a different mechanism.</p><h4>Dosage &amp; Administration:</h4><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute Bacterial Skin and Skin Structure Infection (ABSSSI):Intravenous (IV):</strong> 200 mg once daily for 6 days (1-hour infusion time)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Oral:</strong> 200 mg once daily for 6 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">No dose adjustment is necessary when switching between IV and oral Tedizolid.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If a dose is missed, patients should take it as soon as possible if it\'s within 8 hours of the next dose. If less than 8 hours remain before the next dose, they should wait until the next scheduled dose.</li></ul><h4>Drug Interactions:</h4><p>Tedizolid can increase plasma concentrations of orally administered Breast Cancer Resistance Protein (BCRP) substrates. If coadministration cannot be avoided, monitor for adverse reactions related to these substrates.</p><h4>Contraindications:</h4><p>None.</p><h4>Side Effects:</h4><p>The most common adverse reactions (&gt;2%) include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Headache</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Dizziness</li></ul><h4>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</h4><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy category C:</strong> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Tedizolid should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Nursing mothers:</strong> Tedizolid is excreted in the breast milk of rats, but it is not known if it is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administering Tedizolid to nursing women.</li></ul><h4>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</h4><ul><li><strong>Neutropenia:</strong> The safety and efficacy of Tedizolid in patients with neutropenia (neutrophil count &lt;1000 cells/mm³) have not been adequately studied. Consider alternative therapies in neutropenic patients.</li><li><strong>Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea:</strong> Evaluate if diarrhea occurs during treatment.</li></ul><h4>Overdose Effects:</h4><p>In case of overdose, Tedizolid should be discontinued, and general supportive treatment should be provided. Hemodialysis does not result in meaningful removal of Tedizolid from the systemic circulation.</p><h4>Storage Conditions:</h4><p>Tablets and injection should be stored at 20°C to 25°C.</p><h4>Use in Special Populations:</h4><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients below 18 years of age have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Clinical studies did not include sufficient numbers of subjects aged 65 and older to determine whether they respond differently from younger subjects. No significant pharmacokinetic differences were observed between elderly and younger subjects.</li></ul><h4>Drug Classes:</h4><p>Other antibiotics</p><h4>Mode of Action:</h4><p>Tedizolid exerts antibacterial activity by binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit of susceptible Gram-positive bacteria, inhibiting protein synthesis. Its mechanism differs from that of other non-oxazolidinone antibacterial drugs, reducing the likelihood of cross-resistance.</p><p><br></p><p>Let me know if you need further clarification or additional information!</p>', 'active', '1558_1742359533.jpg', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 09:45:33', '2025-03-19 09:45:33'),
(3496, 'Roxatat', 'Roxadustat', 600.00, 3, 'tablet', '3 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is a more structured version of the provided information:</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Indications of Roxatat 100 mg</strong></h3><p>Roxatat 100 mg is indicated for the treatment of <strong>symptomatic anemia</strong> associated with <strong>chronic kidney disease (CKD)</strong> in adult patients.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Dosage of Roxatat 100 mg</strong></h3><p>The appropriate dose of Roxatat 100 mg should be taken <strong>orally three times per week</strong>, not on consecutive days. The dose should be individualized to achieve and maintain target <strong>Hb levels of 10 to 12 g/dL</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>For patients not on erythropoiesis-stimulating agents:</strong></li><li><strong>Starting dose</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">50 mg three times weekly for adults.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">70 mg three times per week for patients weighing <strong>less than 100 kg</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">100 mg three times per week for patients weighing <strong>100 kg and over</strong>.</li><li><strong>For patients switching from erythropoiesis-stimulating agents:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Starting dose</strong>: 70 or 100 mg three times per week.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dosage adjustment</strong>: The dosage should be adjusted based on the patient\'s condition.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dose Adjustment</strong></h3><p>When dose adjustments are required:</p><ul><li>Increase or decrease the dose according to the <strong>\"Dose increase/decrease table\"</strong> and <strong>\"stepwise dose adjustment sequence\"</strong>.</li><li>Once adjusted, <strong>maintain the dose level for ≥4 weeks</strong>.</li><li>If hemoglobin increases rapidly (<strong>&gt;2.0 g/dL</strong>) within 4 weeks of a dose increase, reduce the dose or suspend treatment immediately.</li></ul><p><strong>Stepwise Dose Adjustment Sequence</strong>:</p><ul><li>20 mg → 40 mg → 50 mg → 70 mg → 100 mg → 150 mg → 200 mg → 250 mg → 300 mg → 400 mg (only for CKD patients on dialysis).</li></ul><p><strong>Missed dose</strong>:</p><ul><li>If there is a ≥24-hour interval until the next dose, take the missed dose immediately and resume the prescribed schedule.</li><li>If &lt;24 hours until the next dose, skip the missed dose and take the next dose as scheduled. <strong>Do not take 2 doses on the same day.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Method of administration</strong>:</p><ul><li>Take Roxatat 100 mg <strong>with or without food</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction of Roxatat 100 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>With Phosphate binders and multivalent cation products (except lanthanum carbonate)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Risk</strong>: Decreased Roxatat AUC by 67% and Cmax by 66%.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Take Roxatat at least 1 hour after administration of these products.</li><li><strong>With Gemfibrozil (CYP2C8 inhibitor) or Probenecid (UGT and OAT1/OAT3 inhibitor)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Risk</strong>: Increased Roxatat AUC by 2.3-fold and Cmax by 1.4-fold.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Adjust the dose of Roxatat based on Hb monitoring.</li><li><strong>With OATP1B1 or BCRP Substrates (e.g., Simvastatin, Rosuvastatin, Atorvastatin)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Risk</strong>: Increased AUC and Cmax.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Adjust the dose of Roxatat based on Hb monitoring.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Roxatat 100 mg</strong></h3><p>Common adverse reactions include:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong></li><li><strong>Vascular access thrombosis</strong></li><li><strong>Diarrhea</strong></li><li><strong>Peripheral edema</strong></li><li><strong>Hyperkalemia</strong></li><li><strong>Nausea</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with a <strong>history of thrombotic vascular events (TVEs)</strong>, particularly those with <strong>obesity</strong> or a prior history of TVEs.</li><li>Caution is required in patients with a <strong>history of seizures</strong>.</li><li><strong>Not to be administered</strong> in patients with <strong>serious infection</strong> or <strong>liver disorders</strong>.</li><li><strong>Not to be initiated</strong> in <strong>pregnant women</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Roxatat 100 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Increased heart rate, transient increase in hemoglobin.</li><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: Dose reduction or interruption. This drug is <strong>not removed by dialysis</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool place</strong> (below 30°C), <strong>dry</strong>, away from light and moisture.</li><li><strong>Keep out of the reach of children</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Drugs for <strong>Haemolytic Hypoplastic &amp; Renal Anemia</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Roxatat 100 mg is an orally bioavailable <strong>hypoxia-inducible factor prolyl hydroxylase inhibitor (HIF-PHI)</strong>. It inhibits HIF-PH enzymes, preventing the degradation of HIF transcription factors. This promotes increased erythropoietin production, enhances erythropoiesis, and improves iron availability. It also reduces hepcidin expression, boosts hemoglobin levels, and coordinates erythropoiesis without requiring intravenous iron supplementation.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Roxatat 100 mg is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in pregnant women.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: <strong>Contraindicated</strong> during breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Not indicated for use in children</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Liver Dysfunction</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Not recommended</strong> for use in patients with <strong>severe hepatic impairment</strong>.</li></ul><p>This version maintains the core content while making it easier to navigate and read through key points. Let me know if you\'d like further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '783_1742359658.jpg', 1, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 09:47:38', '2025-03-19 09:47:38'),
(3497, 'ConviPen', 'Insulin device & needle', 700.00, 1, 'x 1 Insulin device', '1 Insulin device', 'Insulin Device', '0 mg/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '74_1742359913.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 09:51:53', '2025-05-03 01:35:14'),
(3498, 'D-Cap', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 7.00, 15, 'Capsule', '15 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '2000 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '14_1742360010.webp', 9, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 09:53:30', '2025-05-01 19:34:43'),
(3499, 'Roxim', 'Cefixime Trihydrate', 220.00, 1, 'x 50 ml bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '100 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><br></h3>', 'active', '1845_1746506355.webp', 26, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 09:58:41', '2025-05-06 08:39:15'),
(3500, 'Cabanib', 'Cabozantinib', 120.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p>', 'active', '2651_1746077336.jpg', 9, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 10:00:52', '2025-05-01 09:28:56'),
(3501, 'Vorinox', 'Voriconazole', 1000.00, 1, 'x 40 ml bottle', '40 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '200 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vorinox 40 ml</strong></p><p>Vorinox 40 ml is an azole antifungal drug recommended for use in individuals 12 years of age and older to treat the following fungal infections:</p><ul><li>Invasive Aspergillosis</li><li>Disseminated candidiasis and candidemia (nonneutropenic) in the skin, abdomen, kidney, bladder wall, and wounds</li><li>Esophageal candidiasis</li><li>Severe infections caused by Fusarium species (including Fusarium solani)</li><li>Scedosporium apiospermum</li><li>Infections resistant to or intolerant of alternative treatments</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Other Antifungal Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Vorinox 40 ml works by inhibiting 14 alpha-lanosterol demethylation, mediated by fungal cytochrome P450, a key step in the synthesis of ergosterol, an essential component of the fungal cell membrane. This results in a build-up of 14 alpha-methyl sterols and loss of ergosterol, leading to fungal cell damage. Vorinox 40 ml is more selective for fungal cytochrome P450 enzymes than those in mammals, contributing to its antifungal action.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Vorinox 40 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral Administration</strong></li><li><strong>For patients over 40 kg body weight</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Loading dose: 400 mg (10 ml) every 12 hours for the first 24 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 200 mg (5 ml) twice daily after the first 24 hours</li><li><strong>For patients under 40 kg body weight</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Loading dose: 200 mg (5 ml) every 12 hours for the first 24 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 100 mg (2.5 ml) twice daily after the first 24 hours</li><li><strong>Injection</strong></li><li><strong>Invasive Aspergillosis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Loading dose: 6 mg/kg every 12 hours for the first 24 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 4 mg/kg every 12 hours</li><li><strong>Candidemia (nonneutropenic)</strong> and <strong>deep tissue Candida infections</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Loading dose: 6 mg/kg every 12 hours for the first 24 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 3-4 mg/kg every 12 hours</li><li><strong>Scedosporiosis and Fusariosis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Loading dose: 6 mg/kg every 12 hours for the first 24 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 4 mg/kg every 12 hours</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Vorinox 40 ml tablets or powder for suspension should be taken at least one hour before or one hour after a meal. The treatment duration should be as short as possible, depending on the clinical response. Long-term treatment over 180 days must be carefully considered due to potential risks. For prophylaxis, it can be administered for up to 100 days after a transplant, depending on the patient\'s risk of fungal infections.</p><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CYP3A4, CYP2C9, and CYP2C19 Inhibitors and Inducers</strong>: Vorinox 40 ml may require dosage adjustments if used with drugs that affect these enzymes.</li><li><strong>Drugs like phenytoin or efavirenz</strong>: May require higher maintenance doses of Vorinox 40 ml.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Avoid co-administration with certain drugs due to the risk of severe adverse effects, such as:</p><ul><li>Sirolimus, terfenadine, astemizole, cisapride, pimozide, quinidine</li><li>Rifampin, carbamazepine, long-acting barbiturates, efavirenz, ritonavir, rifabutin, ergot alkaloids, St. John\'s Wort</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Anemia</li><li>Impaired vision</li><li>Headaches</li><li>Chest pain</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Diarrhea</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Insufficient data is available on the use of Vorinox 40 ml during pregnancy. It should only be used if the benefit to the mother outweighs the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li>Vorinox 40 ml has not been studied in breast milk. Discontinue breastfeeding after starting treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Long-term therapy (&gt;180 days) is associated with the risk of squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) of the skin.</li><li>Regular monitoring is necessary for patients on prolonged therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>No data is available on overdose effects.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Store in a dry place below 25°C and protect from light.</li><li>Store the suspension powder between 2° and 8°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Older Adults</strong>: No dosage change required.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Population</strong>: Safety and efficacy for children under 12 years have not been established.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment required for oral use.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: For mild to severe hepatic cirrhosis, use regular loading doses but halve the maintenance dose. Avoid use in severe hepatic impairment unless the benefit outweighs the risk.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Other Antifungal Preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Vorinox 40 ml is a triazole antifungal that inhibits fungal CYP450-dependent 14-alpha sterol demethylase, preventing ergosterol formation, thereby disrupting the fungal cell membrane.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Use during pregnancy is not recommended unless the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><p>Vorinox 40 ml has not been established as safe or effective for children under 12 years.</p>', 'active', '850_1742360658.webp', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 10:04:18', '2025-05-05 17:59:06'),
(3502, 'Sporagin', 'Itraconazole', 15.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sporagin 100 mg:</strong> Sporagin 100 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Tinea corporis</li><li>Tinea cruris</li><li>Tinea pedis</li><li>Tinea manus</li><li>Vulvovaginal candidiasis</li><li>Fungal keratitis</li><li>Pityriasis versicolor</li><li>Oropharyngeal candidiasis</li><li>Onychomycosis</li><li>Histoplasmosis</li><li>Systemic Infections (Aspergillosis, Candidiasis, and Cryptococcus when other drugs are ineffective)</li><li><strong>Antifungal prophylaxis</strong> to prevent systemic fungal infection in severely neutropenic patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Sporagin 100 mg is an orally active triazole antifungal drug with a broad spectrum of activity and favorable pharmacokinetic profile. It works by inhibiting cytochrome P-450 dependent enzymes, impairing the biosynthesis of ergosterol, which is a major component of the cell membrane of yeast and fungal cells. This inhibition disrupts cell membrane function, leading to abnormal cell growth and accumulation of intracellular lipids.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Sporagin 100 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tinea corporis, Tinea cruris:</strong> 100 mg daily for 2 weeks.</li><li><strong>Tinea pedis, Tinea manus:</strong> 100 mg daily for 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Vulvovaginal candidiasis:</strong> 200 mg twice daily for 1 day, or 200 mg daily for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Fungal keratitis:</strong> 200 mg daily for 3 weeks.</li><li><strong>Pityriasis versicolor:</strong> 200 mg daily for 1 week.</li><li><strong>Oropharyngeal candidiasis:</strong> 100 mg daily (200 mg for AIDS patients) for 15 days.</li><li><strong>Onychomycosis:</strong> 200 mg daily for 3 months, or 200 mg twice daily for 7 days (repeated courses after 21-day intervals).</li><li><strong>Histoplasmosis:</strong> 200 mg 1-2 times daily.</li><li><strong>Systemic Infections (Aspergillosis, Candidiasis, and Cryptococcus):</strong> 200 mg once daily, increased to 200 mg twice daily for invasive infections or cryptococcal meningitis.</li><li><strong>Antifungal prophylaxis:</strong> 100 mg daily for severely neutropenic patients or as directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Sporagin 100 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Should be taken with food, immediately after a full meal.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine</strong> and <strong>Warfarin</strong> metabolism may be inhibited.</li><li>Interactions with <strong>Terfenadine</strong>, <strong>Phenobarbitone</strong>, and <strong>Midazolam</strong> have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to the drug.</li><li>Pregnancy and severe hepatic disease.</li><li>Rifampicin therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Dyspepsia</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Headache</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Raised liver enzymes</li><li>Hepatitis</li><li>Cholestatic jaundice</li><li>Peripheral neuropathy</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated during pregnancy.</li><li>Breastfeeding is not recommended while on Sporagin 100 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor hepatic enzyme levels in patients with preexisting liver conditions or those on long-term treatment (more than one month).</li><li>Liver function should also be monitored if symptoms of liver dysfunction appear.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>There is no known experience of overdosage with Sporagin 100 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Sporagin 100 mg is an orally active triazole antifungal agent that inhibits cytochrome P-450 dependent enzymes, leading to the disruption of ergosterol biosynthesis in fungal cells.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><ul><li>Not recommended during pregnancy and lactation unless directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses:</strong></p><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Sporagin 100 mg have not been established for pediatric patients.</li></ul>', 'active', '329_1742360778.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 10:06:18', '2025-05-04 21:23:27'),
(3503, 'EPO-EC', 'Dietary Supplement', 30.67, 30, 'Capsule', '30 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '800 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '249_1742361810.png', 72, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 10:23:30', '2025-05-02 10:12:24'),
(3504, 'Myofolic', 'Myo-Inositol + Ascorbic Acid + Folic Acid + Cranberry Extract', 159.50, 10, 'x 1 Sachet box', '1 Sachet box', 'Sachet', '4 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1471_1742362132.png', 72, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 10:28:52', '2025-05-04 00:15:07'),
(3505, 'Exelon Patch 10', 'Rivastigmine Tartrate', 224.00, 30, 'capsule', '30 capsule in a box', 'Capsule', '9.5 mg per 24 hr', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you\'ve provided, formatted with bold headings for clarity:</p><p><strong>Indications of Exelon Patch 10 9.5 mg/24 hr</strong></p><ul><li>Symptomatic treatment of mild to moderately severe Alzheimer\'s dementia.</li><li>Symptomatic treatment of mild to moderately severe dementia in patients with idiopathic Parkinson\'s disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Dementia</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Rivastigmine is a carbamate derivative, structurally related to physostigmine, but not to donepezil and tacrine. The precise mechanism of rivastigmine has not been fully determined, but it is suggested that rivastigmine binds reversibly with and inactivates cholinesterase (e.g., acetylcholinesterase, butyrylcholinesterase), preventing the hydrolysis of acetylcholine and leading to an increased concentration of acetylcholine at cholinergic synapses. Its anticholinesterase activity is relatively specific for brain acetylcholinesterase and butyrylcholinesterase compared with peripheral tissues.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Exelon Patch 10 9.5 mg/24 hr</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Rivastigmine Capsule:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initial dose: Rivastigmine 1.5 mg twice a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dose titration: If well tolerated after a minimum of two weeks, increase to 3 mg twice a day. Subsequently increase to 4.5 mg and 6 mg twice a day, based on tolerance.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 3 mg to 6 mg twice a day, with the highest well-tolerated dose recommended. The maximum daily dose is 6 mg twice a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Re-initiation of therapy: If treatment is interrupted for more than several days, re-initiate at 1.5 mg twice daily, with titration as described above.</li><li><strong>Rivastigmine Transdermal Patch:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Apply patch on intact skin for 24 hours; replace with a new patch every 24 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initial dose: 4.6 mg/24 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dose titration: After a minimum of 4 weeks, increase to 9.5 mg/24 hours if tolerated. After another 4 weeks, increase to a maximum of 13.3 mg/24 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Mild to Moderate Alzheimer\'s Disease and Parkinson’s Disease Dementia:</strong> 9.5 mg/24 hours or 13.3 mg/24 hours once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Severe Alzheimer\'s Disease:</strong> 13.3 mg/24 hours once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For treatment interruption longer than 3 days, retitrate starting at 4.6 mg/24 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Exelon Patch 10 9.5 mg/24 hr</strong></p><ul><li>Rivastigmine should be administered twice a day with morning and evening meals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Exelon Patch 10 9.5 mg/24 hr</strong></p><ul><li>As a cholinesterase inhibitor, Rivastigmine may exaggerate the effects of succinylcholine-type muscle relaxants during anesthesia.</li><li>Rivastigmine should not be given concomitantly with other cholinomimetic substances.</li><li>No pharmacokinetic interaction was observed between rivastigmine and digoxin, warfarin, diazepam, or fluoxetine in healthy volunteers.</li><li>The prothrombin time induced by warfarin is not affected by rivastigmine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to the active substance or other carbamate derivatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Exelon Patch 10 9.5 mg/24 hr</strong></p><ul><li>The most common adverse reactions are gastrointestinal (e.g., nausea 38%, vomiting 23%), especially during titration.</li><li>Female patients are more susceptible than male patients to gastrointestinal adverse reactions and weight loss.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Rivastigmine should not be used during pregnancy unless clearly necessary.</li><li>Rivastigmine is excreted into animal milk, and it is not known if it is excreted into human milk. Women on rivastigmine should not breast-feed.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in patients with sick sinus syndrome, conduction defects, respiratory diseases, asthma, or obstructive pulmonary disease.</li><li>Rivastigmine may exacerbate urinary obstruction and seizures.</li><li>It may worsen extrapyramidal symptoms, and patients should be monitored for body weight.</li><li>Caution in renal and hepatic impairment (mild to moderate).</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Exelon Patch 10 9.5 mg/24 hr</strong></p><ul><li>Most accidental overdose cases have not shown clinical signs or symptoms.</li><li>Symptoms of overdose may include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, hypertension, hallucinations, bradycardia, and syncope.</li><li>In cases of massive overdose, atropine (0.03 mg/kg) may be used, and symptomatic treatment should be provided.</li><li>In asymptomatic overdose, no further dose should be administered for 24 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Dementia</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Rivastigmine binds reversibly to acetylcholinesterase and butyrylcholinesterase, increasing acetylcholine concentrations at cholinergic synapses, particularly in the brain.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Rivastigmine should not be used during pregnancy unless necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Rivastigmine is not recommended for use in children.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you\'d like further clarification or have additional questions!</p>', 'active', '1308_1742362400.webp', 40, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 10:33:20', '2025-05-03 10:19:13'),
(3506, 'Hemocrit', 'Erythropoietin Alfa', 2100.00, 1, 'x 5000 IU pre-filled syringe', '5000 IU pre-filled syringe', 'IV Injection', '5000 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Hemocrit 5000 IU Pre-Filled Syringe</strong></p><p>Erythropoietin alfa is an erythropoiesis-stimulating agent (ESA) indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Treatment of anemia due to</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) in patients on dialysis and not on dialysis.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Zidovudine use in HIV-infected patients.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The effects of concomitant myelosuppressive chemotherapy, with a minimum of two additional months of planned chemotherapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Reduction of allogeneic RBC transfusions in patients undergoing elective, noncardiac, nonvascular surgery.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prevention of anemia of prematurity in infants with a birth weight of 750 to 1500 grams and gestational age of less than 34 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Hemocrit 5000 IU Pre-Filled Syringe</strong></p><p><strong>Evaluation of Iron Stores and Nutritional Factors</strong>:</p><p>Evaluate iron status in all patients before and during treatment and ensure iron repletion. Correct or exclude other causes of anemia (e.g., vitamin deficiency, metabolic or chronic inflammatory conditions, bleeding, etc.) before initiating Erythropoietin alfa.</p><ul><li><strong>For all patients with CKD</strong>:</li><li>Monitor hemoglobin levels at least weekly until stable, then monitor at least monthly. Adjust dose based on hemoglobin rate of rise, decline, ESA responsiveness, and variability.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not increase dose more frequently than once every 4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If hemoglobin rises rapidly (&gt;1 g/dL in any 2-week period), reduce the dose by 25% or more.</li><li><strong>For patients with CKD on dialysis</strong>:</li><li>Initiate treatment when hemoglobin level is less than 10 g/dL. If hemoglobin level approaches or exceeds 11 g/dL, reduce or interrupt the dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The recommended starting dose is <strong>50 to 100 units/kg</strong> 3 times weekly intravenously or subcutaneously.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The intravenous route is recommended for hemodialysis patients.</li><li><strong>For patients with CKD not on dialysis</strong>:</li><li>Initiate treatment only when hemoglobin level is less than 10 g/dL, and the risk of RBC transfusion is high.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The recommended starting dose is <strong>50 to 100 units/kg</strong> 3 times weekly intravenously or subcutaneously.</li><li><strong>Zidovudine-treated HIV-infected patients</strong>:</li><li>The recommended starting dose is <strong>100 units/kg</strong> 3 times per week intravenously or subcutaneously. Adjust the dose if needed after 4-8 weeks of therapy.</li><li><strong>Patients on Cancer Chemotherapy</strong>:</li><li>Start Erythropoietin alfa only if hemoglobin is less than 10 g/dL, with a minimum of two additional months of planned chemotherapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: <strong>150 units/kg</strong> subcutaneously 3 times per week or <strong>40,000 units</strong> weekly until chemotherapy completion.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric Patients (5-18 years)</strong>: <strong>600 units/kg</strong> intravenously weekly until chemotherapy completion.</li><li><strong>Prevention of anemia of prematurity</strong>:</li><li><strong>3x250 IU/kg</strong> subcutaneously per week, starting preferably by day 3 of life for premature infants with a birth weight of 750 to 1500 grams and gestational age of less than 34 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Hemocrit 5000 IU Pre-Filled Syringe</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Preparation and Administration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not shake.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not use if shaken or frozen.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Protect syringe from light.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Inspect visually for particulate matter and discoloration before administration. Discard any syringe with particulate matter or discoloration.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Discard unused portions of the syringe.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Hemocrit 5000 IU Pre-Filled Syringe</strong></p><ul><li>There are no known clinically significant drug interactions.</li><li>The effect of Erythropoietin alfa may be potentiated by the simultaneous therapeutic administration of a haematinic agent (e.g., ferrous sulphate) in the case of deficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Uncontrolled hypertension</strong>.</li><li><strong>Serious allergic reactions</strong> to Erythropoietin alfa.</li><li>Patients who develop <strong>Pure Red Cell Aplasia (PRCA)</strong> following erythropoietin treatment should not receive Erythropoietin alfa or any other erythropoietin.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Hemocrit 5000 IU Pre-Filled Syringe</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common Adverse Reactions (5%)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Patients with CKD</strong>: Hypertension, arthralgia, muscle spasm, pyrexia, dizziness, device malfunction, vascular occlusion, upper respiratory tract infection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zidovudine-treated HIV-infected Patients</strong>: Pyrexia, cough, rash, injection site irritation.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cancer Patients on Chemotherapy</strong>: Nausea, vomiting, myalgia, arthralgia, stomatitis, cough, weight loss, leukopenia, bone pain, rash, hyperglycemia, headache, depression, dysphagia, hypokalemia, thrombosis.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Surgical Patients</strong>: Nausea, vomiting, pruritus, headache, injection site pain, chills, deep vein thrombosis, cough, hypertension.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Premature Infants</strong>: A fall in serum ferritin values is common (&gt;10%).</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Hemocrit 5000 IU Pre-Filled Syringe</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Overdosage</strong> can cause hemoglobin levels above the desired level, managed with dose reduction, discontinuation of Erythropoietin alfa, or phlebotomy if needed.</li><li>Severe hypertension has been observed following overdose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at <strong>2ºC to 8ºC</strong>.</li><li><strong>Do not freeze or shake</strong>.</li><li>Keep the syringe in its original packaging to protect from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Drugs for Hemolytic Hypoplastic &amp; Renal Anemia</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>No adequate studies in pregnant women. Animal studies show reproduction toxicity.</li><li>Use in <strong>pregnancy</strong> should only be if the potential benefit outweighs the risk to the fetus.</li><li>It is not known whether Erythropoietin alfa is excreted in human milk. Use with caution in nursing women, considering the benefits of breastfeeding and Erythropoietin alfa therapy.</li></ul>', 'active', '1028_1742362629.webp', 26, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 10:37:09', '2025-05-03 18:54:26'),
(3507, 'Trihexy', 'Trihexyphenidyl Hydrochloride', 125.00, 1, 'x 50 ml bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '5 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Trihexy 50 ml</strong></p><p>Trihexyphenidyl is indicated as an adjunct in the treatment of all forms of parkinsonism (postencephalitic, arteriosclerotic, and idiopathic). It is often useful as adjuvant therapy when treating these forms of Parkinsonism with levodopa. Additionally, it is indicated for the control of extrapyramidal disorders caused by central nervous system drugs such as the dibenzoxazepines, phenothiazines, thioxanthenes, and butyrophenones.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antiparkinson drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Trihexyphenidyl is a selective M1 muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist. It discriminates between the M1 (cortical or neuronal) and the peripheral muscarinic subtypes (cardiac and glandular). Trihexyphenidyl partially blocks cholinergic activity in the CNS, which is responsible for the symptoms of Parkinson\'s disease. It is also thought to increase the availability of dopamine, a brain chemical that is critical in the initiation and smooth control of voluntary muscle movement.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Trihexy 50 ml</strong></p><p>Dosage should be individualized. The initial dose should be low and gradually increased, especially in patients over 60 years of age. Whether Trihexyphenidyl may best be given before or after meals should be determined by the patient\'s reaction.</p><ul><li><strong>Idiopathic Parkinsonism</strong>: Start with 1 mg on the first day. Increase by 2 mg increments every 3 to 5 days.</li><li><strong>Drug-Induced Parkinsonism</strong>: Start with a 1 mg dose. Increase to 5-15 mg daily if extrapyramidal manifestations are not controlled.</li><li><strong>Concomitant Use with Levodopa</strong>: Use 3-6 mg daily in divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Trihexy 50 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cannabinoids, barbiturates, opiates, and alcohol</strong> may have additive effects and abuse potential.</li><li><strong>Monoamine oxidase inhibitors and tricyclic antidepressants</strong> may intensify the anticholinergic effects.</li><li><strong>Prophylactic administration of anticholinergic agents</strong> is not recommended for drug-induced parkinsonism during neuroleptic therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Trihexyphenidyl is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to the drug and those with narrow-angle glaucoma. Blindness has been reported in some cases due to this condition.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Trihexy 50 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Minor side effects</strong>: Dryness of the mouth, blurred vision, dizziness, nausea, nervousness.</li><li><strong>Serious side effects</strong>: Suppurative parotitis, skin rashes, constipation, urinary retention, hallucinations, memory impairment, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, and angle-closure glaucoma (which may lead to blindness).</li><li><strong>Pediatric side effects</strong>: Hyperkinesia, psychosis, weight loss, and sleep alterations.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Category C. The safety and effectiveness in pregnant women are not well established. Use during pregnancy should be based on the potential benefit to the mother outweighing the risk to the infant. It is not known whether Trihexyphenidyl is excreted in human milk, so it should be used cautiously in nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Patients should undergo a gonioscope evaluation before starting treatment. Trihexyphenidyl can precipitate angle closure with increased intraocular pressure, and blindness has been reported due to this condition. It should be administered cautiously in hot weather and in patients with CNS diseases or those doing manual labor in hot environments. Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS) may occur with abrupt treatment withdrawal, with symptoms like hyperpyrexia, muscle rigidity, and autonomic instability.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Trihexy 50 ml</strong></p><p>Overdosage can lead to atropine intoxication with symptoms like dilated pupils, dry skin, tachycardia, elevated temperature, confusion, hallucinations, and seizures. Severe overdose may lead to coma or death.</p><p><strong>Treatment of Overdose</strong>: Symptomatic and supportive therapy, gastric lavage, or activated charcoal. Use diazepam or short-acting barbiturates for CNS excitation. Respiratory and cardiovascular support may be necessary.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature not exceeding 30ºC in a dry place, and protect from light.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Antiparkinson drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Trihexyphenidyl is a non-selective muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist, with a higher affinity for central M1 receptors. It may modify nicotinic acetylcholine receptor neurotransmission and enhance dopamine release in the striatum.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Trihexyphenidyl should only be used during pregnancy if the benefits outweigh the risks. It may suppress lactation and caution should be exercised when using it in nursing women.</p>', 'active', '1215_1742362759.webp', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 10:39:19', '2025-04-16 13:00:29'),
(3508, 'Renvista', 'Roxadustat', 600.00, 3, 'tablet', '3 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a summary of the key information on <strong>Renvista 100 mg</strong> formatted with headings:</p><h3><strong>Indications of Renvista 100 mg</strong></h3><p>Renvista 100 mg is indicated for the treatment of adult patients with symptomatic anemia associated with chronic kidney disease (CKD).</p><h3><strong>Dosage of Renvista 100 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Frequency</strong>: Take orally three times per week, not on consecutive days.</li><li><strong>Target Hb Levels</strong>: 10 to 12 g/dL.</li><li><strong>For patients not on erythropoiesis-stimulating agents</strong>:</li><li><strong>Starting dose</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">50 mg three times weekly for adults.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">70 mg for patients weighing &lt;100 kg, and 100 mg for those weighing ≥100 kg.</li><li><strong>For patients switching from erythropoiesis-stimulating agents</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Starting dose</strong>: 70 mg or 100 mg three times weekly.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adjust dosage based on patient\'s condition.</li><li><strong>Dose Adjustments</strong>: Adjust based on the \"Dose increase/decrease table\" and \"Stepwise Dose adjustment sequence\".</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If Hb concentration increases rapidly (&gt;2.0 g/dL), reduce the dose or suspend treatment immediately.</li><li><strong>Missed Dose</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If ≥ 24 hours until the next dose, take the missed dose immediately.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If &lt;24 hours, skip the missed dose and follow the regular schedule.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Do not take 2 doses on the same day.</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Method of Administration</strong></h3><ul><li>Take Renvista 100 mg tablets orally with or without food.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction of Renvista 100 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Phosphate binders &amp; products with multivalent cations</strong>: Risk of reduced AUC and Cmax of Renvista. <strong>Recommendation</strong>: Take Renvista 100 mg at least 1 hour after these medications.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil or probenecid</strong>: Risk of increased AUC and Cmax of Renvista. <strong>Recommendation</strong>: Adjust dose based on Hb monitoring.</li><li><strong>OATP1B1 or BCRP substrates</strong> (e.g., simvastatin, rosuvastatin): Risk of increased AUC and Cmax. <strong>Recommendation</strong>: Adjust dose based on Hb monitoring.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Renvista 100 mg</strong></h3><ul><li>Common adverse reactions include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypertension</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vascular access thrombosis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diarrhea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Peripheral edema</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hyperkalemia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pre-existing thrombotic vascular events (TVE) risk factors (e.g., obesity, prior TVEs).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">History of seizures.</li><li><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Serious infections.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Liver disorders.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pregnant women (not to be initiated during pregnancy).</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Renvista 100 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Overdose may lead to increased heart rate and excessive hemoglobin concentration.</li><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: Dose reduction or interruption. Dialysis does not remove Renvista.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, in a cool, dry place, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><ul><li>Drugs for Hemolytic Hypoplastic &amp; Renal Anemia.</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Renvista 100 mg</strong> is a HIF-PHI (hypoxia-inducible factor prolyl hydroxylase inhibitor).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Mechanism</strong>: Inhibits HIF-PH to increase HIF activity, leading to increased erythropoietin production and improved erythropoiesis.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Effect</strong>: Reduces hepcidin expression, improves iron availability, and increases hemoglobin levels.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">It also promotes iron absorption, transport, and hemoglobin synthesis.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy and Breastfeeding</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Contraindicated for pregnant women.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: Not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Not indicated for use in children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Liver Dysfunction</strong></h3><ul><li>Not recommended for patients with severe hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '461_1742362888.jpg', 29, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 10:41:28', '2025-03-19 10:41:28');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3509, 'Mefoglip', 'Glipizide + Metformin Hydrochloride', 2.80, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.5 mg plus 500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Mefoglip 2.5 mg + 500 mg (Glipizide &amp; Metformin)</strong></p><p>Mefoglip 2.5 mg + 500 mg is indicated as:</p><ul><li><strong>Initial therapy</strong>, as an adjunct to diet and exercise, to improve glycemic control in patients with type 2 diabetes whose hyperglycemia cannot be satisfactorily managed with diet and exercise alone.</li><li><strong>Second-line therapy</strong>, when diet, exercise, and initial treatment with a Sulfonylurea or Metformin do not result in adequate glycemic control in patients with type 2 diabetes.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</p><p>Combination Oral Hypoglycemic Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Metformin</strong>: A biguanide antihyperglycemic agent that improves glucose tolerance in type 2 diabetes patients, lowering both basal and postprandial plasma glucose. It decreases hepatic glucose production, decreases intestinal glucose absorption, and improves insulin sensitivity. Unlike sulfonylureas, metformin does not cause hypoglycemia or hyperinsulinemia.</li><li><strong>Glipizide</strong>: A sulfonylurea that lowers blood glucose by stimulating the release of insulin from the pancreas. It depends on functioning beta cells in the pancreatic islets, and may also have extrapancreatic effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Mefoglip 2.5 mg + 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>As Initial Therapy</strong>: The recommended starting dose is <strong>250 mg Metformin / 2.5 mg Glipizide</strong> once daily with a meal.</li><li><strong>As Second-Line Therapy</strong>: The recommended starting dose is <strong>500 mg Metformin / 2.5 mg Glipizide</strong> or <strong>500 mg Metformin / 5 mg Glipizide</strong> twice daily with the morning and evening meals.</li><li><strong>Maximum Dose</strong>: <strong>2000 mg Metformin / 20 mg Glipizide</strong> per day.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Cimetidine</strong> reduces the renal clearance of Metformin.</li><li><strong>Alcohol</strong> potentiates the antihyperglycemic &amp; hyperlactataemic effects of Metformin.</li><li><strong>Nifedipine</strong> enhances Metformin absorption.</li><li><strong>Sulfonylureas</strong> may have enhanced hypoglycemic effects when combined with certain drugs, including NSAIDs, azoles, salicylates, sulfonamides, and others.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to the drug</li><li>Renal impairment</li><li>Diabetic coma and ketoacidosis</li><li>Chronic renal diseases</li><li>Congestive heart failure</li><li>Trauma, dehydration, alcohol dependence</li><li>Pregnancy &amp; Breast-feeding</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Mefoglip 2.5 mg + 500 mg</strong>:</p><ul><li>Common side effects include hypoglycemia, diarrhea, nausea/vomiting, abdominal pain, headache, and musculoskeletal pain.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Contraindicated during pregnancy and lactation.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Renal Function</strong>: Metformin is excreted by the kidneys, so renal function should be carefully monitored, especially in elderly patients or those with serum creatinine levels above normal.</li><li><strong>Surgical Procedures</strong>: Metformin should be suspended temporarily for any surgical procedures until oral intake resumes, and renal function is evaluated.</li><li><strong>Sulfonylurea Therapy</strong>: Blood glucose should be monitored when used with sulfonylurea drugs.</li><li><strong>Impaired Metabolism/Excretion of Glipizide</strong>: In patients with impaired renal or hepatic function, Glipizide metabolism may slow, and if hypoglycemia occurs, it may be prolonged.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Store below 25°C in a cool and dry place.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong>:</p><p>Combination Oral Hypoglycemic Preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Metformin</strong>: Decreases hepatic glucose production, reduces intestinal glucose absorption, and improves insulin sensitivity.</li><li><strong>Glipizide</strong>: Stimulates insulin release from the pancreas, dependent on functional beta cells.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong>:</p><ul><li>Contraindicated during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '1808_1742364457.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 11:07:37', '2025-05-08 20:24:57'),
(3510, 'Cardocal', 'Cilnidipine', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Cardocal 5 mg</h3><p>Cardocal 5 mg is indicated for the management of hypertension for end-organ protection. It is reported to be useful in elderly patients and in those with diabetes and albuminuria. Cardocal 5 mg has been increasingly used in patients with chronic kidney disease. Hypertension, commonly known as high blood pressure, occurs when there is too much pressure on the blood vessels, potentially damaging them.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Calcium-channel blockers</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Cardocal 5 mg is a dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker. It binds to the dihydropyridine binding sites of the L-type voltage-dependent calcium channel, inhibiting Ca²⁺ influx across the cell membranes of vascular smooth muscle cells. This leads to the relaxation of vascular smooth muscle and vasodilation. Cardocal 5 mg also inhibits Ca²⁺ influx via N-type voltage-dependent calcium channels in the sympathetic nerve cell membrane. This reduces the release of norepinephrine from sympathetic nerve terminals. It is considered to suppress the reflex increase in heart rate following blood pressure reduction.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 5-10 mg once daily after breakfast. The maximum dose is 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> The safety of Cardocal 5 mg in pediatric patients has not been established.</li><li><strong>Use in the Elderly:</strong> Since elderly patients may be more susceptible to hypotension, therapy should begin with the lowest possible dose (5 mg).</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Cardocal 5 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Other anti-hypertensives</strong>, antipsychotics that cause hypotension</li><li><strong>Drugs like quinidine, carbamazepine, phenytoin, rifampicin, cimetidine, erythromycin</strong> may interact.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Cardocal 5 mg is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Known sensitivity to Cardocal 5 mg or any excipients</li><li>Cardiogenic shock</li><li>Recent myocardial infarction (MI) or acute unstable angina</li><li>Severe aortic stenosis</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Cardocal 5 mg</h3><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Dizziness</li><li>Flushing</li><li>Headache</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Peripheral edema</li><li>Palpitations</li><li>Gastrointestinal disturbances</li><li>Increased micturition frequency</li><li>Lethargy</li><li>Eye pain</li><li>Depression</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><p>Cardocal 5 mg should <strong>not</strong> be administered to pregnant women or those who might become pregnant. It is also advisable to avoid using it in nursing mothers. If it is deemed necessary, the patient should be instructed to discontinue lactation.</p><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with serious hepatic dysfunction.</li><li>Use with care in patients with a history of serious adverse reactions to calcium antagonists.</li><li>Gradually reduce the dosage during discontinuation under close observation.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><p>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><h3>Drug Class</h3><p>Calcium-channel blockers</p><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Cardocal 5 mg is a dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker that inhibits Ca²⁺ influx through L-type and N-type voltage-dependent calcium channels. This causes relaxation of vascular smooth muscle and vasodilation, while also inhibiting norepinephrine release from sympathetic nerve terminals.</p>', 'in-active', '1449_1742364819.webp', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 11:13:39', '2025-05-08 14:04:52'),
(3511, 'Tezo', 'Tedizolid Phosphate', 300.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a summary of the information for <strong>Tezo (Tedizolid) 200 mg</strong> in a more organized format:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Tezo (Tedizolid 200 mg)</strong></p><p>Tedizolid is an oxazolidinone-class antibacterial drug indicated in adults for the treatment of <strong>acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections (ABSSSI)</strong> caused by susceptible bacteria. It is important to use Tedizolid only for infections that are <strong>proven or strongly suspected</strong> to be caused by susceptible bacteria, in order to reduce the development of drug-resistant bacteria.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Other Antibiotic</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Tedizolid is converted to its active form by phosphatases.</li><li>It exerts its antibacterial activity by inhibiting protein synthesis, binding to the <strong>50S ribosomal subunit</strong> of susceptible Gram-positive bacteria.</li><li>It is unlikely to cross-resist with other non-oxazolidinone antibiotics due to its unique mechanism of action.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Skin and Skin Structure Infection (ABSSSI):</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intravenous (IV):</strong> 200 mg once daily for 6 days (1-hour infusion time).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Oral:</strong> 200 mg once daily for 6 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Switching between IV and oral:</strong> No dose adjustment required.</li></ul><ol><li>If a dose is missed:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Take it as soon as possible, up to 8 hours before the next scheduled dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If less than 8 hours remain, wait for the next scheduled dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Tedizolid (oral form) can increase plasma concentrations of <strong>Breast Cancer Resistance Protein (BCRP)</strong> substrates.</li><li>Monitor for adverse reactions related to co-administered BCRP substrates if this combination cannot be avoided.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>None</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects (&gt;2%): <strong>nausea</strong>, <strong>headache</strong>, <strong>diarrhea</strong>, <strong>vomiting</strong>, <strong>dizziness</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: No adequate studies in pregnant women. Use only if the potential benefit justifies the risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is unknown whether Tedizolid is excreted in human milk. Caution is advised when administering to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neutropenia</strong>: Tedizolid\'s safety and efficacy in patients with neutropenia have not been fully studied. Consider alternative therapies in neutropenic patients.</li><li><strong>Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea</strong>: Evaluate if diarrhea occurs during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>In case of overdose, discontinue Tedizolid and provide supportive treatment.</li><li>Hemodialysis does not significantly remove Tedizolid from circulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store tablets and injections between <strong>20°C to 25°C</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness not established in patients below 18 years.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Insufficient data for those aged 65 and older, but no significant differences in pharmacokinetics between elderly and younger patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Tedizolid inhibits bacterial protein synthesis by binding to the <strong>50S ribosomal subunit</strong> of Gram-positive bacteria, reducing the likelihood of cross-resistance with other antibiotics.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further details!</p>', 'in-active', '117_1742364932.jpg', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 11:15:32', '2025-05-13 05:17:32'),
(3512, 'Roxatat', 'Roxadustat', 600.00, 3, 'tablet', '3 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Roxatat 100 mg</strong></p><p>Roxatat 100 mg is indicated for the treatment of adult patients with symptomatic anemia associated with chronic kidney disease (CKD).</p><p><strong>Dosage of Roxatat 100 mg</strong></p><p>The appropriate dose of Roxatat 100 mg must be taken orally three times per week and not on consecutive days. The dose should be individualized to achieve and maintain target Hb levels of 10 to 12 g/dL, as described below:</p><ul><li><strong>Patients not on erythropoiesis-stimulating agent treatment:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For adults, the usual starting dose is 50 mg three times weekly.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The recommended starting dose of Roxatat 100 mg is 70 mg three times per week in patients weighing less than 100 kg and 100 mg three times per week in patients weighing 100 kg and over.</li><li><strong>Patients switching from erythropoiesis-stimulating agents:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For adults, the usual starting dose is 70 or 100 mg three times weekly.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage thereafter should be adjusted according to the patient\'s condition.</li></ul><p><strong>Dose adjustment:</strong></p><p>When dose adjustments are required, increase or decrease the dose according to the \"Dose increase/decrease table\" and \"stepwise Dose adjustment sequence\" below. Once adjusted, maintain the dose level for ≥4 weeks. If the hemoglobin concentration increases rapidly (&gt;2.0 g/dL) within 4 weeks of a dose increase, decrease the dose or suspend the treatment immediately.</p><p>The stepwise dose adjustments up or down should follow the sequence of available doses:</p><p>20 mg - 40 mg - 50 mg - 70 mg - 100 mg - 150 mg - 200 mg - 250 mg - 300 mg - 400 mg (only for CKD patients on dialysis).</p><p><strong>Missed dose:</strong></p><ul><li>When there is ≥ 24-hour interval until the next scheduled dosing time, take the missed dose immediately and follow the prescribed schedule for subsequent doses.</li><li>If there is &lt;24 hours until the next scheduled dosing time, skip the missed dose, and take the next dose as scheduled. Do not take 2 doses on the same day.</li></ul><p><strong>Method of administration:</strong></p><p>Roxatat 100 mg tablets are to be taken orally with or without food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Roxatat 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Roxatat 100 mg with Phosphate binders and other products containing multivalent cations (except lanthanum carbonate):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Risk:</strong> Decreased Roxatat 100 mg AUC by 67% and 46% and Cmax by 66% and 52%.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Roxatat 100 mg should be taken at least 1 hour after administration of phosphate binders or other medicinal products or supplements containing multivalent cations.</li><li><strong>Roxatat 100 mg with gemfibrozil (CYP2C8 and OATP1B1 inhibitor) or probenecid (UGT and OAT1/OAT3 inhibitor):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Risk:</strong> Increased Roxatat 100 mg AUC by 2.3-fold and Cmax by 1.4-fold.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Adjust the dose of Roxatat 100 mg following dose adjustment rules based on Hb monitoring.</li><li><strong>Roxatat 100 mg with OATP1B1 or BCRP Substrates (simvastatin, rosuvastatin &amp; atorvastatin):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Risk:</strong> AUC and Cmax increased.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Adjust the dose of Roxatat 100 mg following dose adjustment rules based on Hb monitoring.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Roxatat 100 mg</strong></p><p>Common adverse reactions associated with Roxatat 100 mg include:</p><ul><li>Hypertension</li><li>Vascular access thrombosis</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Peripheral edema</li><li>Hyperkalemia</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Roxatat 100 mg tablets should be used with caution in the following cases:</p><ul><li>It may initiate thrombotic vascular events (TVEs), particularly in patients with pre-existing risk factors for TVE, including obesity and a prior history of TVEs.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with a history of seizures.</li><li>Should not be administered if the patient has serious signs and symptoms of infection.</li><li>Should not be administered if the patient has a liver disorder.</li><li>Should not be initiated in pregnant women.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Roxatat 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms:</strong> Increased heart rate transiently, and an increase in hemoglobin concentration.</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> Appropriate measures such as dose reduction or interruption should be taken. This drug is not removed by dialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool (below 30°C), dry place, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Drugs for Hemolytic Hypoplastic &amp; Renal Anemia</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Roxatat 100 mg is an orally bioavailable, hypoxia-inducible factor prolyl hydroxylase inhibitor (HIF-PHI) with potential anti-anemic activity. It binds to and inhibits HIF-PH, an enzyme responsible for the degradation of transcription factors in the HIF family under normal oxygen conditions. This promotes HIF activity and leads to increased erythropoiesis, reduced expression of hepcidin, improved iron availability, and boosted hemoglobin (Hb) levels.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Roxatat 100 mg should not be administered to pregnant women. It is contraindicated during breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Roxatat 100 mg is not indicated for use in children.</p><p><strong>Liver Dysfunction</strong></p><p>Roxatat 100 mg is not recommended for use in patients with severe hepatic impairment.</p>', 'in-active', '1250_1742365059.jpg', 1, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 11:17:39', '2025-05-12 19:17:35'),
(3513, 'VonoKit', 'Vonoprazan + Amoxicillin + Clarithromycin', 60.00, 1, 'x 4 tablet strip', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg+1000 mg+500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '345_1742365427.webp', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 11:23:47', '2025-05-07 19:50:23'),
(3514, 'CM Care', 'Herbal Medicine', 67.22, 18, 'capsule', '18 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '1000 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1825_1742365754.png', 72, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 11:29:14', '2025-05-03 01:28:37'),
(3515, 'EPO-EC', 'Dietary Supplement', 920.00, 30, 'capsule', '30 capsule in a pack', 'Capsule', '800 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'in-active', '1941_1742366712.png', 72, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 11:45:12', '2025-05-03 10:10:27'),
(3516, 'Zodef', 'Deflazacort', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '18 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a revised version of the provided information with proper formatting, based on your request for heading and bold text:</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Indications of Zodef 18 mg</strong></h3><p>Zodef 18 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li><strong>Anaphylaxis</strong></li><li><strong>Asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Severe hypersensitivity reactions</strong></li><li><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis</strong></li><li><strong>Juvenile chronic arthritis</strong></li><li><strong>Polymyalgia rheumatica</strong></li><li><strong>Systemic lupus erythematosus</strong></li><li><strong>Dermatomyositis</strong></li><li><strong>Mixed connective tissue disease</strong> (other than systemic sclerosis)</li><li><strong>Polyartritis nodosa</strong></li><li><strong>Sarcoidosis</strong></li><li><strong>Pemphigus</strong></li><li><strong>Bullous pemphigoid</strong></li><li><strong>Pyoderma gangrenosum</strong></li><li><strong>Minimal change nephrotic syndrome</strong></li><li><strong>Acute interstitial nephritis</strong></li><li><strong>Rheumatic carditis</strong></li><li><strong>Ulcerative colitis</strong></li><li><strong>Crohn\'s disease</strong></li><li><strong>Uveitis</strong></li><li><strong>Optic neuritis</strong></li><li><strong>Autoimmune hemolytic anemia</strong></li><li><strong>Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura</strong></li><li><strong>Acute and lymphatic leukaemia, malignant lymphoma, multiple myeloma</strong></li><li><strong>Immune suppression in transplantation</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Zodef 18 mg provides anti-inflammatory action by inhibiting the <strong>Phospholipase A2 enzyme</strong>, which is responsible for prostaglandin synthesis. In addition, Zodef 18 mg decreases the release of certain chemicals important in the immune system, providing <strong>immunosuppressive action</strong>.</p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><h4><strong>Adults</strong>:</h4><ul><li><strong>Acute disorders</strong>: Up to <strong>120 mg/day</strong> may be needed initially.</li><li><strong>Maintenance doses</strong>: Typically range from <strong>3-18 mg/day</strong> for most conditions.</li><li><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis</strong>: Maintenance doses usually range from <strong>3-18 mg/day</strong>. The smallest effective dose should be used and increased if necessary.</li><li><strong>Bronchial asthma</strong>: For acute attacks, <strong>48-72 mg/day</strong> may be required, depending on severity, gradually reducing once controlled. For chronic asthma, the dose should be titrated to the lowest effective dose.</li><li><strong>Other conditions</strong>: Dose depends on clinical need, titrated to the lowest effective dose for maintenance.</li></ul><h4><strong>Children</strong>:</h4><p>Doses usually range from <strong>0.25-1.5 mg/kg/day</strong> depending on the condition. Some general guidance includes:</p><ul><li><strong>Juvenile chronic arthritis</strong>: The usual maintenance dose is <strong>0.25-1.0 mg/kg/day</strong>.</li><li><strong>Nephrotic syndrome</strong>: Initial dose of <strong>1.5 mg/kg/day</strong>, followed by down titration according to clinical need.</li><li><strong>Bronchial asthma</strong>: Initial dose should be between <strong>0.25 - 1.0 mg/kg</strong>, administered on alternate days.</li></ul><h3><strong>Zodef 18 mg Withdrawal</strong></h3><p>For patients who have been on higher doses for more than <strong>3 weeks</strong>, withdrawal should not be abrupt. The dose reduction should be gradual to avoid relapse of the underlying disease.</p><h3><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Liver enzyme inducers</strong> (e.g., rifampicin, carbamazepine) may require an increased maintenance dose of Zodef 18 mg.</li><li><strong>Liver enzyme inhibitors</strong> (e.g., ketoconazole) may require a reduced maintenance dose.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to Zodef 18 mg or any of its ingredients</li><li><strong>Patients receiving live virus immunization</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>GI disturbances</strong></li><li><strong>Musculoskeletal</strong></li><li><strong>Endocrine issues</strong></li><li><strong>Neuropsychiatric</strong></li><li><strong>Ophthalmic issues</strong></li><li><strong>Fluid and electrolyte disturbances</strong></li><li><strong>Susceptible to infections</strong></li><li><strong>Impaired healing</strong></li><li><strong>Skin issues</strong> (e.g., atrophy, striae, telangiectasia, acne)</li><li><strong>Myocardial rupture</strong> after recent MI</li><li><strong>Thromboembolism</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Zodef 18 mg crosses the placenta and may increase the risk of intra-uterine growth retardation when used for prolonged periods.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Excreted in breast milk. Doses up to <strong>50 mg/day</strong> are unlikely to cause significant effects on the infant.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><p>Special monitoring is needed in patients with:</p><ul><li><strong>Cardiac disease or congestive heart failure</strong></li><li><strong>Hypertension</strong></li><li><strong>Thromboembolic disorders</strong></li><li><strong>Gastritis, peptic ulcers, or active infections</strong></li><li><strong>Diabetes mellitus</strong></li><li><strong>Osteoporosis</strong></li><li><strong>Renal insufficiency</strong></li><li><strong>Psychotic tendencies</strong></li><li><strong>Epilepsy</strong></li><li><strong>Liver failure</strong></li><li><strong>Ocular herpes simplex</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><p>Withdrawal should not be abrupt in patients who have been receiving <strong>higher-than-physiological doses</strong> for more than <strong>3 weeks</strong>.</p><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool, dry place</strong> (below <strong>25°C</strong>), protected from light and moisture.</li><li><strong>Keep out of the reach of children.</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No specific precautions other than usual glucocorticoid therapy.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: Blood levels may be increased, so doses should be carefully monitored.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: No special precautions necessary.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Zodef 18 mg works by inhibiting the <strong>Phospholipase A2 enzyme</strong>, thus preventing prostaglandin synthesis, and decreasing immune system chemicals, leading to <strong>anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive</strong> effects.</p><p><br></p><p>If you need further information or clarification, feel free to ask!</p>', 'active', '1143_1742367000.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 11:50:00', '2025-03-19 11:50:00'),
(3517, 'Rulicent', 'Ruxolitinib', 300.00, 10, 'tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Rulicent 10 mg</h3><p><strong>Myelofibrosis (MF):</strong></p><p> Rulicent 10 mg is indicated for the treatment of intermediate or high-risk myelofibrosis (MF), including primary MF, post-polycythemia vera MF, and post-essential thrombocythemia MF in adults.</p><p><strong>Polycythemia Vera (PV):</strong></p><p> Rulicent 10 mg is indicated for the treatment of polycythemia vera (PV) in adults who have had an inadequate response to or are intolerant of hydroxyurea.</p><p><strong>Acute Graft-Versus-Host Disease (GVHD):</strong></p><p> Rulicent 10 mg is indicated for the treatment of steroid-refractory acute GVHD in adult and pediatric patients aged 12 years and older.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Pyrrolopyrimidines</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Rulicent 10 mg is a selective inhibitor of Janus Associated Kinases (JAK1 and JAK2). These enzymes mediate the signaling of cytokines and growth factors essential for hematopoiesis and immune function. Rulicent inhibits JAK-STAT signaling and cell proliferation in cytokine-dependent cellular models of hematological malignancies, including those with the JAK2V617F mutation.</p><h3>Dosage of Rulicent 10 mg</h3><p><strong>Myelofibrosis:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Platelet count &gt; 200 × 10⁹/L:</strong> 20 mg orally twice daily</li><li><strong>Platelet count 100–200 × 10⁹/L:</strong> 15 mg orally twice daily</li><li><strong>Platelet count 50–100 × 10⁹/L:</strong> 5 mg orally twice daily</li></ul><p><strong>Polycythemia Vera:</strong></p><ul><li>Starting dose: 10 mg orally twice daily. May be titrated based on safety and efficacy. Consider reducing to 5 mg twice daily if hemoglobin is between 8 and 12 g/dL, and platelet count is 50–75 × 10⁹/L.</li></ul><p><strong>Acute GVHD:</strong></p><ul><li>Starting dose: 5 mg orally twice daily. Can be increased to 10 mg twice daily if necessary after 3 days of treatment.</li></ul><h3>Administration</h3><ul><li>Take orally, with or without food.</li><li>If a dose is missed, skip the missed dose and take the next prescribed dose at the usual time.</li></ul><h3>Drug Interactions</h3><ul><li><strong>Fluconazole:</strong> Concomitant use with doses &gt;200 mg may increase Rulicent exposure, increasing the risk of adverse reactions.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 Inhibitors/Inducers:</strong> Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors may increase Rulicent exposure. Strong CYP3A4 inducers may decrease exposure.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Rulicent 10 mg or any component of the product.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Thrombocytopenia, anemia, neutropenia, risk of infection, bruising, dizziness, headache.</li><li><strong>Serious:</strong> Non-melanoma skin cancers, progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy, elevated lipid levels.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>Contraindicated during pregnancy and lactation. Animal studies show embryotoxicity and foetotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong> Discontinue breastfeeding during treatment and for two weeks after the final dose.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Blood disorders:</strong> Monitor CBC regularly due to the risk of thrombocytopenia, anemia, and neutropenia.</li><li><strong>Infections:</strong> Increased risk of serious infections, including bacterial, viral, and fungal. Monitor patients closely.</li><li><strong>Tuberculosis:</strong> Screen for TB before treatment; manage TB infections appropriately.</li><li><strong>Non-melanoma skin cancers:</strong> Regular skin checks are advised.</li><li><strong>Lipid elevations:</strong> Monitor lipid levels and treat hyperlipidemia as necessary.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li>No known antidote. Supportive treatment should be provided in case of overdose.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a dry place, away from sunlight. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> No dose adjustment for mild to moderate renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment:</strong> Dose should be reduced by about 50% for patients with hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> No additional adjustments needed for elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Pediatric use:</strong> Safety and efficacy in children under 18 have not been established.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Rulicent 10 mg inhibits JAK1 and JAK2, interfering with the JAK-STAT pathway that regulates hematopoiesis and immune function. It decreases splenomegaly and inflammatory cytokines, particularly in patients with MF and PV.</p><h3>Absorption, Distribution, and Excretion</h3><ul><li><strong>Absorption:</strong> Rapidly absorbed with peak plasma concentration within 1–2 hours.</li><li><strong>Distribution:</strong> Volume of distribution is 72 L in MF and PV patients.</li><li><strong>Excretion:</strong> Predominantly through metabolism (74% via urine), with minor excretion via feces. Less than 1% excreted unchanged.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '941_1742367122.webp', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 11:52:02', '2025-03-19 11:52:02'),
(3518, 'Spusoft', 'Erdosteine', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '300 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Spusoft 300 mg</strong></p><p> Spusoft 300 mg is a mucolytic drug used in the treatment of acute and chronic respiratory diseases. It also improves the symptoms associated with short episodes of chronic bronchitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Cough expectorants &amp; mucolytics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Spusoft 300 mg is a prodrug that is metabolized into N-thiodiglycolyl-homocysteine. This metabolite forms free thiol groups that lead to the opening of the disulfide bonds of the bronchial mucoproteins, thereby reducing the viscosity of mucus and purulent sputum. It also antagonizes the local formation of free radicals, inhibits elastase enzyme activity, and reduces bacterial adhesion to epithelial cells.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Spusoft 300 mg</strong></p><p> The usual dose for elderly patients and adults above 18 years is 1 capsule twice daily for 10 days. However, in chronic cases, Spusoft 300 mg can be used for up to 12 months or as directed by the physician. The capsule may be taken with or without food.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Spusoft 300 mg is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to the drug. It should not be used in patients with creatinine clearance (CrCl) &lt;25 ml/min or in cases of severe liver failure.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Spusoft 300 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include epigastric pain.</li><li>Other side effects may include headache, cold, taste alterations, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, angioedema, and other skin allergic reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The use of Spusoft 300 mg in pregnant or breastfeeding women is not recommended.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Spusoft 300 mg should be used with caution in cases of mild liver failure. In such cases, the dose should not exceed 300 mg per day.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Contraindicated in patients with CrCl &lt;25 ml/min.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong>Mild to moderate impairment: Max dose 300 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe impairment: Contraindicated.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Cough expectorants &amp; mucolytics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Spusoft 300 mg is a prodrug that is metabolized into N-thiodiglycolyl-homocysteine, leading to the formation of free thiol groups that open the disulfide bonds of bronchial mucoproteins. This reduces mucus viscosity and purulent sputum. It also inhibits the formation of free radicals, elastase enzyme activity, and bacterial adhesion to epithelial cells.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The use of Spusoft 300 mg in pregnant or breastfeeding women is not recommended.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Contraindicated in patients with CrCl &lt;25 ml/min.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong>Mild to moderate impairment: Max dose 300 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe impairment: Contraindicated.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '264_1742367216.webp', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 11:53:36', '2025-03-19 11:53:36'),
(3519, 'VonoKit', 'Vonoprazan + Amoxicillin + Clarithromycin', 60.00, 14, 'tablet', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg plus 1000 mg plus 500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'in-active', '936_1742367403.webp', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 11:56:43', '2025-05-13 05:49:03'),
(3520, 'Empaglif M', 'Empagliflozin + Metformin Hydrochloride', 20.00, 4, 'Tablet', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+1000 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1122_1742367653.jpg', 29, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 12:00:53', '2025-05-02 09:46:17'),
(3521, 'Qupet', 'Ozenoxacin', 250.00, 1, 'cream', '1 cream box', 'Cream', '10gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Qupet is indicated for the topical treatment of impetigo due to <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em> in adult and pediatric patients 2 months of age and older.</p><p> <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p><strong>Mechanism of Action:</strong> Ozenoxacin is a quinolone antimicrobial drug. The mechanism of action involves the inhibition of bacterial DNA replication enzymes, DNA gyrase A, and topoisomerase IV. Ozenoxacin has been shown to be bactericidal against <em>S. aureus</em> and <em>S. pyogenes</em> organisms.</p><p><strong>Absorption:</strong> Four pharmacokinetic studies were conducted in 110 patients utilizing varying strengths of ozenoxacin cream, up to 2% (twice the concentration of the marketed formulation). Three of these studies assessed systemic absorption in healthy subjects and in subjects with impetigo. These studies were conducted with either single or repeated application of up to 1 g ozenoxacin cream to intact or abraded skin (up to 200 cm² surface area). No systemic absorption was observed in 84 of 86 subjects, and negligible systemic absorption was observed at the level of detection (0.489 ng/mL) in 2 subjects.</p><p><strong>Distribution:</strong> Plasma protein binding of ozenoxacin was moderate (~80 to 85%) and did not appear to be dependent on concentration. Since negligible systemic absorption was observed in clinical studies, tissue distribution has not been investigated in humans.</p><p><strong>Metabolism:</strong> Ozenoxacin was not metabolized in the presence of fresh human skin discs and was minimally metabolized in human hepatocytes.</p><p><strong>Excretion:</strong> Studies have not been investigated in humans due to the negligible systemic absorption observed in clinical studies.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li>Apply a thin layer of Ozenoxacin topically to the affected area twice daily for five days.</li><li>The affected area may be up to 100 cm² in adult and pediatric patients 12 years of age and older or 2% of the total body surface area and not exceeding 100 cm² in pediatric patients less than 12 years of age.</li><li>Wash hands after applying Ozenoxacin cream.</li><li>Ozenoxacin cream is for topical use only.</li><li>Not for oral, ophthalmic, intranasal, or intravaginal use.</li><li>The treated area may be covered with a sterile bandage or gauze dressing.</li><li> <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> None</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Because clinical trials are conducted under widely varying conditions, adverse reaction rates observed in the clinical trials of a drug cannot be directly compared to rates in the clinical trials of another drug and may not reflect the rates observed in practice. The safety profile of Qupet was assessed in two clinical trials (Trial 1 and Trial 2) in 362 adult and pediatric patients two months of age and older with impetigo. The patients used at least one dose from a 5-day, twice-a-day regimen of Qupet. Control groups included 361 patients who used a placebo and 152 patients who used retapamulin ointment. The median age of the patients enrolled in the clinical trials was 10 years; 3% of patients were 2 months to less than 2 years of age, 55% of patients were 2 to less than 12 years of age, 11% of patients were 12 to less than 18 years of age, and 31% of patients were 18 years of age or older. Adverse reactions (rosacea and seborrheic dermatitis) were reported in 1 adult patient treated with Qupet.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> There are no available data on the use of ozenoxacin in pregnant women to inform a drug-associated risk. Systemic absorption of ozenoxacin in humans is negligible following topical administration of ozenoxacin (up to twice the concentration of the marketed formulation). Due to the negligible systemic exposure, it is not expected that maternal use of ozenoxacin will result in fetal exposure to the drug. Animal reproduction studies were not conducted with ozenoxacin. However, toxicity studies conducted in pregnant rats and rabbits administered the oral form of ozenoxacin showed no significant adverse developmental effects (at &gt;10,000 times the maximum human plasma concentration seen with dermal application of ozenoxacin). The estimated background risk of major birth defects and miscarriage for the indicated population is unknown. In the U.S. general population, the estimated background risk of major birth defects and miscarriage in clinically recognized pregnancies is 2% to 4% and 15% to 20%, respectively.</p><p><strong>Lactation:</strong> No data are available regarding the presence of ozenoxacin in human milk, and the effects of ozenoxacin on the breastfed infant or on milk production. However, breastfeeding is not expected to result in exposure of the child to ozenoxacin due to the negligible systemic absorption of ozenoxacin in humans following topical administration of ozenoxacin. The developmental and health benefits of breastfeeding should be considered along with the mother’s clinical need for ozenoxacin and any potential adverse effects on the breast-fed child from ozenoxacin or from the underlying maternal condition.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> <strong>Potential for Microbial Overgrowth:</strong> The prolonged use of Qupet may result in overgrowth of nonsusceptible bacteria and fungi. If such infections occur during therapy, discontinue use and institute appropriate supportive measures.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> The safety and effectiveness of Qupet in the treatment of impetigo have been established in pediatric patients 2 months to 17 years of age. Use of Qupet in pediatric patients (2 months to 17 years of age) is supported by evidence from adequate and well-controlled studies of Qupet in which 251 pediatric patients received at least one dose of Qupet. The median age of the patients enrolled in the clinical trials was 10 years; 3% of patients were 2 months to less than 2 years of age, 55% of patients were 2 to less than 12 years of age, 11% of patients were 12 to less than 18 years of age, and 31% of patients were 18 years of age or older. In these studies, the maximum dose applied was approximately 0.5 g of Qupet applied twice daily for up to 5 days (i.e., up to 10 applications total). The safety profile of Qupet in pediatric patients 2 months and older was similar to that of adults. The safety and effectiveness of Qupet in pediatric patients younger than 2 months of age have not been established.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Clinical studies of Qupet did not include sufficient numbers of subjects aged 65 and older to determine whether they respond differently from younger subjects. Other reported clinical experience has not identified differences in responses between the elderly and younger patients.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p> Any sign or symptom of overdose, either topically or by accidental ingestion, should be treated symptomatically. No specific antidote is known.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Topical Antibiotic Preparations</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at 20ºC - 25ºC; excursions permitted to 15ºC to 30ºC. Keep away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1928_1742381665.jpg', 2, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 15:54:25', '2025-03-19 15:54:25'),
(3522, 'Revolade', 'Eltrombopag Olamine', 916.00, 7, 'tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Revolade tablet is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Treatment of thrombocytopenia in patients with persistent or chronic immune thrombocytopenia.</li><li>Treatment of thrombocytopenia in patients with hepatitis C infection.</li><li>Treatment of severe aplastic anemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Limitations of use:</strong> Revolade tablet is not indicated for the treatment of patients with myelodysplastic syndromes (MDS). Safety and efficacy have not been established in combination with direct-acting antiviral agents used without interferon for the treatment of chronic hepatitis C infection.</p><p> <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Eltrombopag Olamine is a small molecule thrombopoietin (TPO) receptor agonist. Eltrombopag interacts with the transmembrane domain of the human TPO receptor and initiates signaling cascades that induce proliferation and differentiation from bone marrow progenitor cells, finally leading to increased platelet production.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p> <strong>Route of administration:</strong> Eltrombopag tablet should be taken orally, either without a meal or with a meal low in calcium (&lt;50 mg).</p><ul><li><strong>Persistent or chronic immune thrombocytopenia:</strong> Use the lowest dose necessary to maintain a platelet count ≥50×10⁹/L to reduce the risk of bleeding. Do not exceed 75 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Adult and pediatric patients (≥6 years) with ITP:</strong> Start with 50 mg once daily (25 mg for Asian ancestry or hepatic impairment).</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients (1–5 years) with ITP:</strong> Start with 25 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Chronic hepatitis C-associated thrombocytopenia:</strong> Start with 25 mg once daily, adjusting every 2 weeks as necessary (max: 100 mg daily).</li><li><strong>First-line severe aplastic anemia:</strong> Dosage varies by age:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2.5 mg/kg (ages 2–5 years)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">75 mg (ages 6–11 years)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">150 mg (ages 12+ years)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Reduce dose by 50% for Asian ancestry or hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Refractory severe aplastic anemia:</strong> Start with 50 mg daily, titrate up to 150 mg if needed (25 mg for Asian ancestry or hepatic impairment).</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With medication:</strong> Take eltrombopag at least 2 hours before or 4 hours after medications containing polyvalent cations (e.g., antacids, iron, calcium, magnesium, zinc).</li><li><strong>With food:</strong> Take eltrombopag at least 2 hours before or 4 hours after calcium-rich foods.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Eltrombopag is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to eltrombopag or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Anemia</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Fever (pyrexia)</li><li>Increased ALT levels</li><li>Cough</li><li>Fatigue</li><li>Headache</li><li>Diarrhea</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There is insufficient data on the risks of eltrombopag during pregnancy. Its presence in human milk and effects on infants are unknown. Due to potential risks, breastfeeding is not recommended during treatment.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In chronic hepatitis C patients, Revolade with interferon and ribavirin may increase hepatic decompensation risk. Monitor liver function regularly.</li><li>There is an increased risk of death and progression of MDS to acute myeloid leukemia.</li><li>Portal vein thrombosis has been reported in patients with chronic liver disease.</li><li>Regular monitoring of platelet counts is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children &amp; adolescents:</strong> Safety and efficacy not established in patients below 1 year with ITP or in pediatric patients with thrombocytopenia due to chronic hepatitis C or refractory severe aplastic anemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p> Overdose may cause excessive platelet increase, leading to thrombotic complications. In case of overdose, consider using metal cation preparations (e.g., calcium, aluminum, magnesium) to chelate eltrombopag and reduce absorption.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Haemostatic drugs</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool (&lt;25°C) and dry place, protected from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1299_1742381977.webp', 40, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 15:59:37', '2025-03-19 15:59:37');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3523, 'Sefril', 'Cephradine', 15.00, 4, 'Capsule', '4 Capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '500mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Sefril is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. These include:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper respiratory tract infections</strong>: Sinusitis, pharyngitis, tonsillitis, laryngo-tracheo bronchitis, and otitis media.</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong>: Bronchitis (acute and chronic), lobar pneumonia, and bronchopneumonia.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: Cystitis, urethritis, and pyelonephritis.</li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: Abscess, cellulitis, furunculosis, and impetigo.</li></ul><p>The following microorganisms are susceptible to Sefril:</p><ul><li><strong>Gram-positive</strong>: Staphylococci (both penicillin-sensitive and resistant strains and penicillinase-producing species), Streptococci, Streptococcus pyogenes (beta-haemolytic), Streptococcus pneumoniae.</li><li><strong>Gram-negative</strong>: Escherichia coli, Klebsiella spp, Proteus mirabilis, Haemophilus influenzae, Shigella spp, Salmonella spp (including Salmonella typhi), Neisseria spp.</li></ul><p>Many strains of <em>E. coli</em> and <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> that produce penicillinase and are resistant to ampicillin are susceptible to Sefril, as it is unaffected by this enzyme.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cephradine is a semisynthetic broad-spectrum bactericidal antibiotic, active against infections caused by both Gram-positive and Gram-negative microorganisms. It inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by preventing the cross-linking process, leading to cell lysis due to osmotic pressure.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>For oral administration</strong></p><p><strong>Adults</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: 500 mg four times daily or 1 g twice daily. Higher doses may be needed for severe infections.</li><li><strong>Respiratory tract infections</strong>: 250-500 mg four times daily or 500 mg-1 g twice daily, depending on infection severity.</li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: 250-500 mg four times daily or 500 mg-1 g twice daily, based on infection severity.</li></ul><p><strong>Children</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>General infections</strong>: 25-50 mg/kg daily, divided into two or four doses.</li><li><strong>Otitis media</strong>: 75-100 mg/kg daily, divided into doses every 6-12 hours.</li><li><strong>Maximum daily dose</strong>: 4 g.</li></ul><p><strong>Elderly</strong>: Normal adult dose applies. Patients with renal or hepatic impairment should be monitored.</p><p><strong>For injectable administration</strong></p><p><strong>Adults</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Usual dose</strong>: 2-4 g daily in four divided doses, up to 8 g daily.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis</strong>: 1-2 g intramuscularly or intravenously preoperatively.</li></ul><p><strong>Children</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>General infections</strong>: 50-100 mg/kg daily in four divided doses, up to 300 mg/kg daily for severe infections.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With medications</strong>: Aminoglycosides may increase kidney toxicity. Diuretics (e.g., frusemide, ethacrynic acid) and probenecid may enhance renal toxicity.</li><li><strong>With food</strong>: No significant interactions reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Cephradine should not be used in patients with known hypersensitivity to cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain.</li><li><strong>Allergic reactions</strong>: Urticaria, skin rashes, joint pain, edema.</li><li><strong>Blood disorders</strong>: Thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, agranulocytosis, aplastic anemia.</li><li><strong>Nervous system</strong>: Dizziness, hyperactivity, confusion, sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Hepatobiliary</strong>: Liver enzyme disturbances, hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice.</li><li><strong>Renal</strong>: Reversible interstitial nephritis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>No confirmed teratogenic effects, but safety in pregnancy is not established.</li><li>Excreted in breast milk—use with caution in lactating mothers.</li><li>May cause dizziness—patients should avoid operating heavy machinery.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged use may lead to superinfection due to resistant organisms.</li><li>Cross-sensitivity may occur in penicillin-allergic patients.</li><li>May cause false-positive urine glucose test results.</li><li>Dosage adjustments needed in renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> <strong>Renal Impairment</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CrCl &gt;20 ml/min</strong>: 500 mg every 6 hours.</li><li><strong>CrCl 5-20 ml/min</strong>: 250 mg every 6 hours.</li><li><strong>CrCl &lt;5 ml/min</strong>: 250 mg every 50-70 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>For patients on haemodialysis</strong></p><ul><li><strong>250 mg at the start of haemodialysis</strong>.</li><li><strong>250 mg 6 to 12 hours later</strong>.</li><li><strong>250 mg 36 to 48 hours after start</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p> Symptoms include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and gastric upset. Supportive treatment is required. Gastric lavage may be necessary if a large amount has been ingested.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> First-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Sefril Suspension</strong>: Use within 7 days at room temperature or within 14 days if refrigerated.</li><li><strong>Sefril Injection</strong>: Use within 2 hours at room temperature or within 12 hours if refrigerated.</li><li><strong>Store below 25°C, protected from light</strong>.</li><li><strong>Keep out of reach of children</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1260_1742382284.webp', 5, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 16:04:44', '2025-03-19 16:04:44'),
(3524, 'Neofloxin-D', 'Ciprofloxacin + Dexamethasone', 75.00, 1, 'x 5 ml drop', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '0.3%+0.1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p><strong>Eye:</strong> This is indicated for the treatment of steroid-responsive inflammatory ocular conditions where bacterial infections or risk of bacterial infections co-exist. The combination can also be used for post-operative inflammation and any other ocular inflammation associated with infection.</p><p><strong>Ear:</strong> This is also indicated in the treatment of ear infections accompanied by inflammation such as otitis externa, acute and chronic otitis media, etc. The combination may also be used post-operatively in the ear.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Dexamethasone is a glucocorticoid. It has an anti-inflammatory and anti-allergic action. It is used topically in the treatment of inflammatory conditions of the anterior segment of the eye. It reduces prostaglandin synthesis by inhibiting the enzyme phospholipase A2. Also, Dexamethasone inhibits the chemotactic infiltration of neutrophils into the site of inflammation.</p><p>Ciprofloxacin has in vitro activity against a wide range of gram-negative and gram-positive organisms, possessing the greatest antibacterial activity of all quinolones. The bactericidal action of Ciprofloxacin results from interference with the enzyme DNA gyrase, which is needed for the synthesis of bacterial DNA.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>For Eye:</strong> 1 drop to be instilled into the conjunctival sac(s) every four to six hours. During the initial 24 to 48 hours, the dosage may be increased to 1 drop every two hours.</p><p><strong>For Ear:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Acute otitis media in pediatric patients with tympanostomy tube:</strong> 4 drops instilled into the affected ear 2 times daily for 7 days.</li><li><strong>Acute otitis externa:</strong> 4 drops instilled into the affected ear 2 times daily for 7 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients below 1 year have not been established.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>Specific drug interaction studies have not been conducted with ophthalmic Ciprofloxacin and Dexamethasone. However, the systemic administration of some quinolones has been shown to:</p><ul><li>Elevate plasma concentrations of theophylline</li><li>Interfere with the metabolism of caffeine</li><li>Enhance the effects of the oral anticoagulant warfarin and its derivatives</li><li>Be associated with transient elevations in serum creatinine in patients receiving cyclosporin concomitantly</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient of the product. Herpes simplex and other viral conditions, mycosis, glaucoma, newborn babies, fungal diseases of ocular or auricular structures.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p><strong>Eye:</strong> The most frequently reported drug-related adverse reactions seen with Ciprofloxacin are transient ocular burning or discomfort. Other reported reactions include stinging, redness, itching, conjunctivitis/keratitis, periocular/facial edema, foreign body sensation, photophobia, blurred vision, tearing, dryness, and eye pain. Rare reports of dizziness have been received. The reactions due to the steroid component include:</p><ul><li>Elevation of intraocular pressure (IOP) with possible development of glaucoma</li><li>Infrequent optic nerve damage</li><li>Posterior subcapsular cataract formation</li><li>Delayed wound healing</li></ul><p><strong>Ear:</strong> The most common adverse effects are discomfort and ear pain. Other reported reactions include irritability, dizziness, and erythema.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. This Sterile Eye &amp; Ear Drops should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Nursing Mothers:</strong> It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when using this Sterile Eye &amp; Ear Drops in a nursing mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>The possibility of fungal infections of the cornea should be considered after long-term steroid dosing and in patients with persistent corneal ulceration.</li><li>Prolonged use of antibiotics may result in the overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms.</li><li>Prolonged use of topical ophthalmic steroids may result in glaucoma, with damage to the optic nerve, defects in visual acuity and fields of vision, and posterior subcapsular cataract formation.</li><li>Intraocular pressure should be routinely monitored.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Aural steroid &amp; antibiotic combined preparations</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place, away from light. Keep out of reach of children. Shake well before each use. The drug is to be used within 30 days after first opening. The bottle is to be closed tightly immediately after use.</p>', 'active', '823_1742382754.png', 3, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 16:12:34', '2025-04-23 09:38:35'),
(3525, 'Nalid', 'Nalidixic Acid', 41.75, 1, 'Oral Suspension', '50 ml bottle', 'Ophthalmic Suspension', '300mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Nalid is indicated in the treatment of urinary tract infections caused by susceptible Gram-negative organisms, including <em>Proteus</em> species, <em>Klebsiella</em> species, <em>Enterobacter</em> species, and <em>Escherichia coli.</em></p><p> <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Nalidixic acid is a synthetic narrow-spectrum antibacterial. It is bacteriostatic or bactericidal depending on the concentration. Nalidixic acid appears to act by inhibiting bacterial DNA synthesis, possibly by interfering with DNA polymerization. It is rapidly and completely absorbed from the G.I. tract. The parent drug and active metabolites are distributed to most tissues, especially to the kidney and urine.</p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Half-life:</strong>Normal renal function: 1.1 to 2.5 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Impaired renal function: Up to 21 hours</li><li><strong>Excretion:</strong> Rapidly and almost completely excreted within 24 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults:</strong> Initially 1 g every 6 hours for 7 days, reducing to 500 mg every 6 hours.</p><p><strong>Children (Infants and children 3 months and older):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Initial dose:</strong> Oral 13.75 mg per kg body weight every six hours for one or two weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance dose:</strong> Oral 8.25 mg per kg body weight every six hours or as prescribed by the physician.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Melphalan:</strong> There have been reports of death from severe blood-containing diarrhea caused by hemorrhagic ulcerative colitis when used with Nalid.</li><li><strong>Probenecid:</strong> Inhibits tubular secretion of Nalid, which may elevate serum concentration and enhance toxicity.</li><li><strong>Chlorpromazine &amp; Perphenazine:</strong> Have been shown to potentiate the effect of Nalid in vitro.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Nalidixic acid is contraindicated in the following cases:</p><ul><li>Infants under 3 months</li><li>Epilepsy</li><li>CNS lesions</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea</li><li><strong>Hematologic:</strong> Hemolysis in G6PD deficiency</li><li><strong>Allergic Reactions:</strong> Urticaria, rashes, fever, arthralgia, eosinophilia</li><li><strong>Musculoskeletal:</strong> Myalgia, muscle weakness</li><li><strong>Other:</strong> Phototoxicity, jaundice, visual disturbances, and convulsions</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>There is a possibility that Nalid may cause cartilage damage, and as it is a DNA-gyrase inhibitor, it may also pose a risk of DNA damage.</li><li>Nalidixic acid is excreted in breast milk, with a reported case of hemolytic anemia in a breastfed child of an azotemic mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Risk-benefit must be considered during the first trimester of pregnancy and during breastfeeding.</li><li>Caution is advised in patients with impaired renal or hepatic function.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-diarrheal antimicrobial drugs, Other anti-protozoals, Systemic urinary anti-infectives</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1455_1742383023.jpg', 4, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 16:17:03', '2025-03-19 16:17:03'),
(3526, 'Nalbun', 'Nalbuphine', 100.00, 1, 'IM/IV Injection', '1 IM/IV Injection', 'Injection', '20 mgper 2ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Nalbun DS Hydrochloride is indicated for the relief of moderate to severe pain. It is used for pain relief associated with myocardial infarction (MI). Nalbun DS Hydrochloride can also be used as:</p><ul><li>A supplement to balanced anesthesia</li><li>Preoperative and postoperative analgesia</li><li>Obstetrical analgesia during labor and delivery</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Nalbuphine Hydrochloride is a synthetic potent analgesic, with an analgesic potency essentially equivalent to that of morphine on a milligram basis. It primarily acts as a <strong>kappa agonist/partial mu antagonist</strong> analgesic.</p><ul><li><strong>Receptor binding:</strong> Binds to mu, kappa, and delta receptors but not sigma receptors.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Onset of action:Intravenous (IV):</strong> Within 2-3 minutes</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Subcutaneous (SC) or Intramuscular (IM):</strong> Within 15 minutes</li><li><strong>Plasma half-life:</strong> 5 hours</li><li><strong>Duration of analgesic activity:</strong> 3 to 6 hours</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>Usual recommended dose: <strong>10 mg</strong> for a 70 kg individual, administered <strong>IM or IV</strong>, repeated every <strong>3 to 6 hours</strong> as necessary.</li><li><strong>Moderate to severe pain:</strong> <strong>IV or IM</strong> 10-20 mg for a 70 kg patient, adjusted as required.</li><li><strong>Preoperative anesthesia:</strong> <strong>IV or IM</strong> 0.1-0.2 mg/kg.</li><li><strong>Obstetrical analgesia during labor &amp; delivery:</strong> <strong>IV</strong> 0.3-1 mg/kg over 10-15 minutes, with maintenance doses of 0.25-0.5 mg/kg as needed.</li><li><strong>Intraoperative analgesia:</strong> <strong>IV</strong> 0.25 - 0.5 mg/kg at 30-minute intervals.</li><li><strong>Myocardial infarction:</strong> <strong>Slow IV injection</strong> 10-20 mg, repeated after 30 minutes if necessary.</li><li><strong>Higher doses are required when used as an anesthesia supplement.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Children (18 months to 15 years old):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Usual dose:</strong> 0.2 mg/kg body weight, given preferably by <strong>IV or IM injection</strong>.</li><li><strong>Maintenance doses:</strong> Every <strong>4 to 6 hours</strong> or as determined by a physician.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> No hazardous interactions have been identified with Nalbun DS. However, <strong>interactions similar to other opioids</strong> may occur. Additive effects may be observed when used with:</p><ul><li>Narcotic analgesics</li><li>General anesthesia</li><li>Phenothiazines or other tranquilizers</li><li>Sedatives, hypnotics, or other CNS depressants (including alcohol)</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Nalbuphine Hydrochloride.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Nalbun DS is generally well tolerated. However, some side effects may include:</p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Sedation, sweating, nausea, vomiting, dizziness, vertigo, dry mouth, headache.</li><li><strong>Respiratory:</strong> Respiratory depression, dyspnea, asthma.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pregnancy Category B:Placental transfer:</strong> High and rapid.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Should be used <strong>only if clearly needed</strong> due to a lack of well-controlled studies in pregnant women.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Lactation:</strong>Limited data suggest Nalbuphine is excreted in breast milk (&lt;1% of dose).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clinically insignificant effects on nursing infants.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Caution is advised when administering to a nursing mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Caution should be taken in patients with:</p><ul><li>Impaired respiration</li><li>Impaired renal or hepatic function</li><li>Biliary tract surgery</li><li>Myocardial infarction</li><li>Hypotension</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p> Symptoms of overdose may include <strong>sleepiness and mild dyspnea</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>Antidote:</strong> Immediate <strong>IV administration of an opioid antagonist</strong> such as <strong>Naloxone or Nalmefene</strong>.</li><li><strong>Supportive measures:</strong> Oxygen, IV fluids, vasopressors as needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, protected from light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '417_1742383521.jpg', 2, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 16:25:21', '2025-03-19 16:25:21'),
(3527, 'Ziton', 'Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate', 30.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '10 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Ziton is indicated for the treatment of <strong>zinc deficiency</strong> and/or <strong>zinc-losing conditions</strong>. Zinc deficiency can result from:</p><ul><li><strong>Inadequate diet</strong> or <strong>malabsorption</strong></li><li><strong>Excessive loss</strong> due to trauma, burns, diarrhea, or protein-losing conditions</li></ul><p>A <strong>zinc supplement</strong> is given until clinical improvement occurs. In severe cases of malabsorption, metabolic disease, or zinc-losing states, continued supplementation may be necessary.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p><strong>Zinc sulfate monohydrate</strong> is an <strong>essential trace element</strong> involved in multiple enzymatic processes in the body. Zinc is necessary for:</p><ul><li><strong>Normal growth &amp; health</strong></li><li><strong>Sexual maturation &amp; reproduction</strong></li><li><strong>Dark vision adaptation</strong></li><li><strong>Olfactory &amp; gustatory activity</strong></li><li><strong>Insulin storage &amp; release</strong></li><li><strong>Immune system function</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Zinc deficiency</strong> can lead to:</p><ul><li><strong>Impaired immunity &amp; delayed wound healing</strong></li><li><strong>Loss of taste &amp; smell</strong></li><li><strong>Increased infection risk</strong></li><li><strong>Poor night vision</strong></li><li><strong>Increased risk of miscarriage</strong></li><li><strong>Alopecia &amp; skin changes</strong></li><li><strong>Poor development of reproductive organs</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Dosage</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children under 10 kg:</strong> 5 ml (<strong>1 teaspoonful</strong>) <strong>twice daily</strong> after food</li><li><strong>Children between 10-30 kg:</strong> 10 ml (<strong>2 teaspoonfuls</strong>) <strong>1-3 times daily</strong> after food</li><li><strong>Adults &amp; children over 30 kg:</strong> 20 ml (<strong>4 teaspoonfuls</strong>) <strong>1-3 times daily</strong> after food</li></ul><p>For best effectiveness, take at least <strong>1 hour before or 2 hours after meals</strong>. However, if stomach upset occurs, take with food.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>For <strong>dispersible tablet:</strong></p><ol><li>Place the tablet in a teaspoon.</li><li>Add an adequate amount of water.</li><li>Let the tablet dissolve completely.</li><li>Give the entire spoonful solution.</li></ol><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tetracyclines &amp; zinc:</strong> May decrease the absorption of both.</li><li><strong>Quinolone drugs &amp; zinc:</strong> May decrease the absorption of both.</li><li><strong>Penicillamine &amp; zinc:</strong> May reduce zinc absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of this preparation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Ziton may cause:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal disturbances:</strong> Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, stomach upset, heartburn, and gastritis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The <strong>safety of Ziton in pregnancy</strong> has not been fully established.</li><li><strong>Zinc crosses the placenta</strong> and is present in <strong>breast milk</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In <strong>acute renal failure</strong>, zinc may accumulate in the body; <strong>dose adjustment is required</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Specific mineral preparations</strong></li><li><strong>Vaccines, Anti-sera &amp; Immunoglobulin</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a <strong>dry place</strong>, away from <strong>light and heat</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of <strong>children</strong>.</li></ul>', 'active', '274_1742383828.webp', 9, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 16:30:28', '2025-05-07 20:42:39'),
(3528, 'Zedex DS', 'Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate', 30.00, 1, 'bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '10 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p><strong>Zedex DS</strong> is indicated for the treatment of <strong>zinc deficiency</strong> and/or <strong>zinc-losing conditions</strong>. Zinc deficiency can occur due to:</p><ul><li><strong>Inadequate diet</strong> or <strong>malabsorption</strong></li><li><strong>Excessive loss</strong> from trauma, burns, diarrhea, or protein-losing conditions</li></ul><p>A <strong>zinc supplement</strong> is given until clinical improvement occurs. In severe cases of <strong>malabsorption, metabolic disease, or zinc-losing states</strong>, long-term supplementation may be necessary.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p><strong>Zinc sulfate monohydrate</strong> is an <strong>essential trace element</strong> involved in multiple enzymatic processes. Zinc is required for:</p><ul><li><strong>Normal growth &amp; health</strong></li><li><strong>Sexual maturation &amp; reproduction</strong></li><li><strong>Dark vision adaptation</strong></li><li><strong>Olfactory &amp; gustatory activity</strong></li><li><strong>Insulin storage &amp; release</strong></li><li><strong>Immune system function</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Zinc deficiency</strong> may lead to:</p><ul><li><strong>Impaired immunity &amp; delayed wound healing</strong></li><li><strong>Loss of taste &amp; smell</strong></li><li><strong>Increased infection risk</strong></li><li><strong>Poor night vision</strong></li><li><strong>Increased risk of miscarriage</strong></li><li><strong>Alopecia &amp; skin changes</strong></li><li><strong>Poor development of reproductive organs</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Children under 10 kg:</strong> 5 ml (<strong>1 teaspoonful</strong>) <strong>twice daily</strong> after food</li><li><strong>Children between 10-30 kg:</strong> 10 ml (<strong>2 teaspoonfuls</strong>) <strong>1-3 times daily</strong> after food</li><li><strong>Adults &amp; children over 30 kg:</strong> 20 ml (<strong>4 teaspoonfuls</strong>) <strong>1-3 times daily</strong> after food</li></ul><p>For best results, take at least <strong>1 hour before or 2 hours after meals</strong>. If stomach upset occurs, take with food.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><h3><strong>Administration</strong></h3><p>For <strong>dispersible tablet:</strong></p><ol><li>Place the tablet in a teaspoon.</li><li>Add an adequate amount of water.</li><li>Let the tablet dissolve completely.</li><li>Give the entire spoonful solution.</li></ol><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><h3><strong>Interaction</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Tetracyclines &amp; zinc:</strong> May reduce the absorption of both.</li><li><strong>Quinolone drugs &amp; zinc:</strong> May reduce the absorption of both.</li><li><strong>Penicillamine &amp; zinc:</strong> May decrease zinc absorption.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of this preparation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p>Zedex DS may cause:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal disturbances</strong>: Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, stomach upset, heartburn, and gastritis.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>The <strong>safety of Zedex DS in pregnancy</strong> has not been fully established.</li><li><strong>Zinc crosses the placenta</strong> and is present in <strong>breast milk</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>In <strong>acute renal failure</strong>, zinc may accumulate in the body; <strong>dose adjustment is required</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Specific mineral preparations</strong></li><li><strong>Vaccines, Anti-sera &amp; Immunoglobulin</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>dry place</strong>, away from <strong>light and heat</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of <strong>children</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1834_1742384085.webp', 3, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 16:34:45', '2025-03-19 16:34:45'),
(3529, 'ZIS DS', 'Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate', 38.11, 1, 'bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '10 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p><strong>Zis-DS</strong> is indicated for the treatment of <strong>zinc deficiency</strong> and <strong>zinc-losing conditions</strong>. Zinc deficiency may occur due to:</p><ul><li><strong>Inadequate diet</strong> or <strong>malabsorption</strong></li><li><strong>Excessive loss</strong> from trauma, burns, diarrhea, or protein-losing conditions</li></ul><p>Zinc supplementation is required <strong>until clinical improvement occurs</strong>. In cases of <strong>severe malabsorption, metabolic disease, or chronic zinc-losing states</strong>, continued supplementation may be necessary.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p><strong>Zinc sulfate monohydrate</strong> is an <strong>essential trace element</strong> that plays a crucial role in multiple enzymatic and physiological functions. It is necessary for:</p><ul><li><strong>Normal growth &amp; development</strong></li><li><strong>Sexual maturation &amp; reproduction</strong></li><li><strong>Dark vision adaptation</strong></li><li><strong>Olfactory &amp; gustatory function</strong></li><li><strong>Insulin storage &amp; release</strong></li><li><strong>Immune system support</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Zinc deficiency</strong> may lead to:</p><ul><li><strong>Weakened immunity &amp; slow wound healing</strong></li><li><strong>Loss of taste &amp; smell</strong></li><li><strong>Increased infection risk</strong></li><li><strong>Poor night vision</strong></li><li><strong>Increased risk of miscarriage</strong></li><li><strong>Alopecia &amp; skin changes</strong></li><li><strong>Delayed reproductive development</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Children under 10 kg:</strong> 5 ml (<strong>1 teaspoonful</strong>) <strong>twice daily</strong> after food</li><li><strong>Children between 10-30 kg:</strong> 10 ml (<strong>2 teaspoonfuls</strong>) <strong>1-3 times daily</strong> after food</li><li><strong>Adults &amp; children over 30 kg:</strong> 20 ml (<strong>4 teaspoonfuls</strong>) <strong>1-3 times daily</strong> after food</li></ul><p><strong>For best results:</strong> Take <strong>1 hour before or 2 hours after meals</strong>. If stomach upset occurs, take with food.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><h3><strong>Administration</strong></h3><p>For <strong>dispersible tablet:</strong></p><ol><li>Place the tablet in a teaspoon.</li><li>Add an adequate amount of water.</li><li>Let the tablet dissolve completely.</li><li>Give the entire spoonful solution.</li></ol><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><h3><strong>Interaction</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Tetracyclines &amp; zinc:</strong> May reduce the absorption of both.</li><li><strong>Quinolone drugs &amp; zinc:</strong> May reduce the absorption of both.</li><li><strong>Penicillamine &amp; zinc:</strong> May decrease zinc absorption.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of this preparation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p><strong>Zis-DS</strong> may cause:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal disturbances</strong>: Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, stomach upset, heartburn, and gastritis.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>The <strong>safety of Zis-DS in pregnancy</strong> has not been fully established.</li><li><strong>Zinc crosses the placenta</strong> and is present in <strong>breast milk</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>In <strong>acute renal failure</strong>, zinc may accumulate in the body; <strong>dose adjustment is required</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Specific mineral preparations</strong></li><li><strong>Vaccines, Anti-sera &amp; Immunoglobulin</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>dry place</strong>, away from <strong>light and heat</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of <strong>children</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '317_1742384259.webp', 5, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 16:37:39', '2025-03-19 16:37:39'),
(3530, 'Biozinc', 'Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate', 35.00, 1, 'bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '10 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p><strong>Biozinc</strong> is indicated for the treatment of <strong>zinc deficiency</strong> and <strong>zinc-losing conditions</strong>. Zinc deficiency may occur due to:</p><ul><li><strong>Inadequate diet</strong> or <strong>malabsorption</strong></li><li><strong>Excessive loss</strong> from trauma, burns, diarrhea, or protein-losing conditions</li></ul><p>Zinc supplementation is given until <strong>clinical improvement occurs</strong>. In cases of <strong>severe malabsorption, metabolic disease, or chronic zinc-losing states</strong>, supplementation may need to be continued.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p><strong>Zinc sulfate monohydrate</strong> is an <strong>essential trace element</strong> that plays a critical role in various bodily functions, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Normal growth &amp; development</strong></li><li><strong>Sexual maturation &amp; reproduction</strong></li><li><strong>Dark vision adaptation</strong></li><li><strong>Olfactory &amp; gustatory activity</strong></li><li><strong>Insulin storage &amp; release</strong></li><li><strong>Immune defense systems</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Zinc deficiency</strong> can lead to:</p><ul><li><strong>Weakened immune system &amp; delayed wound healing</strong></li><li><strong>Loss of taste &amp; smell</strong></li><li><strong>Increased infection risk</strong></li><li><strong>Poor night vision</strong></li><li><strong>Increased risk of miscarriage</strong></li><li><strong>Alopecia &amp; skin changes</strong></li><li><strong>Delayed reproductive development</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Children under 10 kg:</strong> 5 ml (<strong>1 teaspoonful</strong>) <strong>twice daily</strong> after food</li><li><strong>Children between 10-30 kg:</strong> 10 ml (<strong>2 teaspoonfuls</strong>) <strong>1-3 times daily</strong> after food</li><li><strong>Adults &amp; children over 30 kg:</strong> 20 ml (<strong>4 teaspoonfuls</strong>) <strong>1-3 times daily</strong> after food</li></ul><p><strong>For best results:</strong> Take <strong>1 hour before or 2 hours after meals</strong>. If stomach upset occurs, take with food.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><h3><strong>Administration</strong></h3><p>For <strong>dispersible tablet:</strong></p><ol><li>Place the tablet in a teaspoon.</li><li>Add an adequate amount of water.</li><li>Let the tablet dissolve completely.</li><li>Give the entire spoonful solution.</li></ol><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><h3><strong>Interaction</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Tetracyclines &amp; zinc:</strong> May reduce absorption of both.</li><li><strong>Quinolone drugs &amp; zinc:</strong> May reduce absorption of both.</li><li><strong>Penicillamine &amp; zinc:</strong> May decrease zinc absorption.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of this preparation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p><strong>Biozinc</strong> may cause:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal symptoms</strong>: Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, stomach upset, heartburn, and gastritis.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>The <strong>safety of Biozinc during pregnancy</strong> has not been established.</li><li><strong>Zinc crosses the placenta</strong> and is present in <strong>breast milk</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>In <strong>acute renal failure</strong>, zinc may accumulate in the body; <strong>dose adjustment</strong> is necessary.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Specific mineral preparations</strong></li><li><strong>Vaccines, Anti-sera &amp; Immunoglobulin</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>dry place</strong>, away from <strong>light and heat</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of <strong>children</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1171_1742384436.jpg', 30, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 16:40:36', '2025-03-19 16:40:36'),
(3531, 'Terbilab', 'Terbinafine', 60.00, 10, 'Tablet', '1 pack', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p>', 'active', '5694_1747098243.png', 68, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 17:17:10', '2025-05-13 05:04:03'),
(3532, 'Bihale', 'Vilanterol Trifenatate + Fluticasone Furoate', 30.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 Capsule  in a strip', 'Dry Powder Inhalation Capsule', '25 mcg+100 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Bihale 25 mcg+100 mcg:</strong></p><ul><li>Long-term, once-daily, maintenance treatment of airflow obstruction and reducing exacerbations in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD).</li><li>Once-daily treatment of asthma in patients aged 18 years and older.</li><li><strong>Important limitation of use:</strong> Not indicated for relief of acute bronchospasm.</li></ul><p><strong>Composition:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>25/100 inhalation capsule:</strong> Each dry powder inhaler capsule contains Vilanterol 25 mcg (as Vilanterol Trifenatate INN) &amp; Fluticasone Furoate INN 100 mcg.</li><li><strong>25/200 inhalation capsule:</strong> Each dry powder inhaler capsule contains Vilanterol 25 mcg (as Vilanterol Trifenatate INN) &amp; Fluticasone Furoate INN 200 mcg.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Bihale 25 mcg+100 mcg:</strong></p><ul><li>This inhalation capsule must not be swallowed; only to be used with the device.</li><li>Remove the capsule from the blister pack immediately before use.</li><li><strong>After inhalation:</strong> Rinse your mouth with water without swallowing to reduce the risk of oropharyngeal candidiasis.</li><li><strong>Route of administration:</strong> Oral inhalation.</li></ul><p><strong>For Maintenance Treatment of COPD:</strong></p><ul><li>1 inhalation of 25/100 mcg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For Asthma:</strong></p><ul><li>1 inhalation of 25/100 mcg or 25/200 mcg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Bihale 25 mcg+100 mcg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Strong cytochrome P450 3A4 inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole):</strong> Use with caution, may cause systemic corticosteroid and cardiovascular effects.</li><li><strong>Monoamine oxidase inhibitors and tricyclic antidepressants:</strong> Use with extreme caution, may potentiate the effect of vilanterol on the vascular system.</li><li><strong>Beta-blockers:</strong> Use with caution as they may block bronchodilatory effects and cause severe bronchospasm.</li><li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> Use with caution as electrocardiographic changes and/or hypokalemia may worsen with beta-agonists.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Primary treatment of status asthmaticus or acute episodes of COPD or asthma requiring intensive measures.</li><li>Severe hypersensitivity to milk proteins or any ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Bihale 25 mcg+100 mcg:</strong></p><p><strong>COPD:</strong></p><ul><li>Most common adverse reactions (≥3%): nasopharyngitis, upper respiratory tract infection, headache, oral candidiasis, back pain, pneumonia, bronchitis, sinusitis, cough, oropharyngeal pain, arthralgia, hypertension, influenza, pharyngitis, and pyrexia.</li></ul><p><strong>Asthma:</strong></p><ul><li>Most common adverse reactions (≥2%): nasopharyngitis, oral candidiasis, headache, influenza, upper respiratory tract infection, bronchitis, sinusitis, oropharyngeal pain, dysphonia, and cough.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>LABA monotherapy increases the risk of serious asthma-related events.</li><li>Do not initiate in acutely deteriorating COPD or asthma. Not for use in acute symptoms.</li><li>Do not use with an additional LABA-containing medicine due to risk of overdose.</li><li>Monitor for candidiasis (oral thrush), especially in patients with respiratory conditions.</li><li>Increased risk of pneumonia in COPD patients. Monitor for signs of pneumonia.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with pre-existing infections, cardiovascular disorders, and a history of chickenpox or measles.</li><li>Monitor for changes in bone mineral density, glaucoma, cataracts, and adrenal function.</li><li>May cause paradoxical bronchospasm—discontinue and switch to alternative therapy if this occurs.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with cardiovascular issues, convulsive disorders, thyrotoxicosis, diabetes, and ketoacidosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Bihale 25 mcg+100 mcg:</strong></p><ul><li>No data available from clinical trials on overdose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place away from direct sunlight or heat.</li><li>Keep away from eyes and out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Respiratory corticosteroids.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vilanterol (LABA):</strong> Stimulates intracellular adenyl cyclase, converting ATP to cyclic AMP, causing bronchodilation and inhibiting the release of hypersensitivity mediators from mast cells.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Furoate (ICS):</strong> Reduces inflammation by targeting a range of immune cells and mediators involved in the inflammatory response.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><ul><li>Insufficient data on the use in pregnant or lactating women.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses:</strong></p><ul><li>Not indicated for children and adolescents (under 18 years). Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong></p><ul><li>May increase systemic exposure in patients with moderate or severe impairment. Monitor for systemic corticosteroid effects.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '1047_1742386761.webp', 5, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 17:19:21', '2025-05-08 09:37:21'),
(3533, 'Biotab', 'Biotin', 25.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5000 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Biotab 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>Biotab 5000 mcg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Hair loss</li><li>Weak, brittle, splitting, or soft nails</li><li>Eczema and dermatitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Herbal and Nutraceuticals</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Biotab 5000 mcg is an essential vitamin, also known as vitamin H. It plays a crucial role in physiological functions like fatty acid synthesis, amino acid catabolism, and gluconeogenesis. It acts as a cofactor in the enzymatic carboxylation of pyruvate and acetyl-CoA. Biotab 5000 mcg influences the growth and differentiation of epidermal cells, which makes it important for the formation and renewal of skin, hair, and nails. As a coenzyme for mitochondrial carboxylases in hair roots, it improves keratin structure. A deficiency in Biotab 5000 mcg may lead to hair loss and various systemic symptoms such as dermatitis and aciduria. It is one of the most prescribed nutritional supplements for any type of hair loss.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Biotab 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>The dosage of Biotab 5000 mcg varies depending on the disease condition and age group. Generally:</p><ul><li>1-3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician.</li><li>In children with Biotab 5000 mcg deficiency (particularly between 1 week and 2 years of age), symptoms may include seizures, hypotonia, developmental delay, ataxia, hyperventilation, and coma. In such cases, 5-10 tablets daily, crushed and mixed with water or fruit juice, or as advised by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Biotab 5000 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>Anticonvulsant drugs may lower plasma levels of Biotab 5000 mcg.</li><li>Antibiotic use may reduce Biotab 5000 mcg contributions from the microflora of the large intestine.</li><li>Excessive consumption of raw egg whites may interfere with Biotab 5000 mcg absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Sufficient clinical data is not available to use in pregnant women and lactating mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '1578_1742387262.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 17:27:42', '2025-05-08 09:38:28');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3534, 'Valporin CR', 'Sodium Valproate', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Valporin CR 200 mg</h3><p>Valporin CR 200 mg is primarily used in the treatment of various types of epilepsy, including:</p><ul><li>Partial seizures</li><li>Absence seizures (petit mal)</li><li>Generalized tonic-clonic seizures (grand mal)</li><li>Myoclonic seizures</li><li>Atonic seizures</li><li>Mixed seizures that include absence attacks</li><li>Prophylaxis of febrile convulsion</li><li>Prophylaxis of post-traumatic epilepsy</li></ul><p>It is also indicated for the treatment of bipolar disorder and the prophylaxis of migraine.</p><p><br></p><h3>Composition</h3><p><strong>Valporin CR 200 mg tablet:</strong></p><ul><li>Each enteric-coated tablet contains Valporin CR 200 mg BP.</li></ul><p><strong>Valporin CR 200 mg syrup:</strong></p><ul><li>Each 5 ml contains Valporin CR 200 mg BP.</li></ul><p><strong>Valporin CR 200 mg controlled release tablets:</strong></p><ul><li>200 mg tablet: Contains 133.2 mg Valporin CR and 58 mg Valproic acid.</li><li>300 mg tablet: Contains 199.8 mg Valporin CR and 87 mg Valproic acid.</li><li>500 mg tablet: Contains 333 mg Valporin CR and 145 mg Valproic acid.</li></ul><p><strong>Valporin CR 200 mg injection:</strong></p><ul><li>Each 5 ml injectable solution contains Valporin CR 200 mg, equivalent to Valproic Acid 500 mg.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Primary anti-epileptic drugs</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Valporin CR 200 mg exhibits antiepileptic properties across various types of seizures. Though the exact mechanism isn\'t fully understood, it is suggested that its effectiveness is linked to an increase in brain levels of Gamma Amino Butyric Acid (GABA).</p><p><br></p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><p><strong>Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>Initial: 600 mg/day in 2 divided doses, preferably after food.</li><li>The dose may be increased by 200 mg/day at 3-day intervals, up to a maximum of 2.5 g daily, until seizure control is achieved.</li><li>Maintenance dose: 1-2 g daily (20-30 mg/kg daily).</li></ul><p><strong>Children (up to 20 kg):</strong></p><ul><li>Initial: 20 mg/kg/day in divided doses.</li><li>Severe cases may require dose adjustments with plasma concentration monitoring.</li></ul><p><strong>Children (over 20 kg):</strong></p><ul><li>Initial: 400 mg/day (regardless of weight).</li><li>May be increased to 20-30 mg/kg to control seizures.</li></ul><p><strong>Febrile Convulsion:</strong></p><ul><li>20-30 mg/kg/day in 3 divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Bipolar Disorder:</strong></p><ul><li>Initial: 600 mg/day (20-30 mg/kg/day) in 2-3 divided doses.</li><li>Maintenance dosage: 1000-2000 mg daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Prophylaxis of Migraine:</strong></p><ul><li>400-600 mg daily, with some patients requiring up to 1000-1500 mg daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Injection (Adults):</strong></p><ul><li>Slow intravenous injection over 3-5 minutes, usually 400-800 mg (up to 10 mg/kg), followed by infusion up to a maximum of 2500 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Injection (Children):</strong></p><ul><li>Initial: 20-30 mg/kg/day.</li><li>Maximum: 40 mg/kg/day.</li></ul><h3>Interaction</h3><p>Valporin CR 200 mg may act as a non-specific inhibitor of drug metabolism. Drugs it interacts significantly with include:</p><ul><li>Phenobarbital</li><li>Phenytoin</li><li>Warfarin</li><li>Aspirin</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Valporin CR 200 mg or its components</li><li>Liver dysfunction</li><li>Pregnancy (especially in women of childbearing potential)</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><p>Common side effects:</p><ul><li>Anorexia, nausea, and vomiting (lessened with enteric-coated tablets)</li><li>CNS effects: Sedation, ataxia, tremor (usually responding to dose reduction)</li><li>Rash, alopecia, and increased appetite</li></ul><p>Serious side effects:</p><ul><li>Hepatic dysfunction (elevation of liver enzymes in up to 40% of patients)</li><li>Fulminant hepatitis (rare but potentially fatal)</li><li>Children under 2 years of age, especially those with other conditions, are at higher risk for liver injury, acute pancreatitis, and hyperammonemia.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Valporin CR 200 mg crosses the placenta and has been associated with neural tube defects (e.g., anencephaly, spina bifida) if taken in the first trimester.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Valporin CR 200 mg is excreted in breast milk, but it is generally considered to present no significant risk to the newborn.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Monitor liver function, particularly during the first 6 months of therapy.</li><li>Ensure no undue bleeding risks before starting treatment or major surgery.</li><li>Use cautiously in renal impairment, pregnancy, breastfeeding, and systemic lupus erythematosus.</li><li>Sudden withdrawal of therapy should be avoided.</li><li>Valporin CR 200 mg can affect urine ketone tests.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store at or below 30°C.</li><li>Keep away from light and out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Valporin CR 200 mg\'s exact mechanism remains unclear, but it is believed to increase brain levels of GABA, which helps in managing seizures.</p>', 'active', '15_1742387528.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 17:32:08', '2025-03-19 17:32:08'),
(3535, 'Simvatin', 'Simvastatin', 18.11, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Primary hypercholesterolemia (type IIa and IIb) in patients who have not responded adequately to diet and other appropriate measures. Coronary heart disease and elevated plasma cholesterol level.</p><p> <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Simvastatin is a preparation of Simvastatin which acts as a cholesterol-lowering agent. The main mechanism of reduction of low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol is that following inhibition of HMG-CoA reductase activity, the LDL receptor density on the liver cells is increased, leading to an increased removal of LDL cholesterol from the plasma and increased catabolism of LDL cholesterol. In addition, there is a reduction in the very low-density lipoprotein (VLDL) cholesterol and reduced formation of LDL from VLDL. Simvastatin is extensively metabolized in the liver, which is also the main site of action of the drug.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p> The patient should be placed on a standard cholesterol-lowering diet before receiving Simvastatin and should continue on this during treatment with Simvastatin. The usual starting dose is 10 mg/day given as a single dose in the evening. Adjustment of dosage, if required, should be made at intervals of not less than four weeks, to a maximum of 40 mg daily given as a single dose in the evening. If LDL-cholesterol levels fall below 2 mmol/L or total plasma cholesterol levels fall below 3.5 mmol/L, consideration should be given to reducing the dose of Simvastatin. In hypercholesterolemia, the recommended starting dose is 5-10 mg once a day in the evening, and the recommended dosing range is 5-40 mg per day as a single dose in the evening. In patients with coronary heart disease and hypercholesterolemia, the starting dose should be 20 mg once a day in the evening. Because Simvastatin does not undergo significant renal excretion, modification of dosage should not be necessary in patients with renal insufficiency. Safety and effectiveness in children and adolescents have not been established.</p><p> <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Digoxin</strong>: Concomitant administration of Simvastatin and Digoxin in normal volunteers resulted in a slight elevation (less than 0.3 µgm/ml) in drug concentrations in plasma compared to concomitant administration of placebo and Digoxin.</li><li><strong>Coumarin derivatives</strong>: Slightly enhance the anticoagulant effect of Warfarin (mean changes in prothrombin time less than two seconds) in normal volunteers maintained in a state of low therapeutic anticoagulation.</li><li><strong>Others</strong>: In clinical studies, Simvastatin was used concomitantly with ACE inhibitors, beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, diuretics, and NSAIDs without evidence of clinically significant adverse interactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Simvastatin should not be used in:</p><ul><li>Active liver disease</li><li>Pregnant and breastfeeding mothers</li><li>Women of childbearing age unless they have been adequately protected by contraception</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the preparation</li><li>Patients with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia who have a complete absence of LDL receptors</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Simvastatin is generally well-tolerated.</p><ul><li>Common side effects: headache, fatigue, insomnia, gastrointestinal effects like nausea, constipation or diarrhea, flatulence, dyspepsia, abdominal cramps, and muscular effects like myalgia, myositis, and myopathy.</li><li>Rare cases of rhabdomyolysis with acute renal failure secondary to myoglobinuria have been associated with Simvastatin therapy.</li><li>Other side effects: Hepatitis, pancreatitis, rash, angioedema.</li><li> No potentially life-threatening effects have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Category X: Studies in animals or humans have demonstrated fetal abnormalities or there is evidence of fetal risk based on human experience or both, and the risk of the use of the drug in pregnant women clearly outweighs any possible benefit. The drug is contraindicated in women who are or may become pregnant.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>If there is a history of liver disease.</li><li>Those who take high alcohol.</li><li>Liver function tests should be done before and during treatment.</li><li>If serum transaminase rises three times the upper limit of normal, treatment should be discontinued.</li><li>Avoid pregnancy during and for one month after treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p> There are no data available on overdose. No antidote is available. General measures should be adopted and liver function should be monitored.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs, Statins</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place, away from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1109_1742388546.jpg', 5, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 17:49:06', '2025-03-19 17:49:06'),
(3536, 'Travolar', 'Travoprost', 470.00, 1, 'Drop', '3 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '0.004 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Travolar 0.004%</strong></p><p> Travolar 0.004% Eye Drops is indicated for the reduction of elevated intraocular pressure in patients with open-angle glaucoma or ocular hypertension who are intolerant of or insufficiently responsive to another intraocular pressure-lowering medication, as monotherapy or as adjunctive therapy.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for miotics and glaucoma</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Travolar 0.004%, an isopropyl ester prodrug, is a synthetic prostaglandin F2 alpha analogue that is rapidly hydrolyzed by esterases in the cornea to its biologically active free acid. The travoprost free acid is potent and highly selective for the FP prostanoid receptor. Travolar 0.004% free acid is a selective FP prostanoid receptor agonist and is believed to reduce intraocular pressure by increasing the drainage of aqueous humor, primarily through increased uveoscleral outflow and, to a lesser extent, trabecular outflow facility.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Travolar 0.004%</strong></p><p> <strong>Use in adults:</strong> The recommended dose is one drop of Travolar 0.004% in the conjunctival sac of the affected eye(s) once daily in the evening. If more than one topical ophthalmic product is being used, the medicines must be administered at least 5 minutes apart. When substituting another ophthalmic antiglaucoma agent with Travolar 0.004%, discontinue the other agent and start the following day with Travolar 0.004%.</p><p><strong>Pediatric patients:</strong> The efficacy and safety of Travolar 0.004% eye drops in patients below the age of 18 years have not been established.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Travolar 0.004%</strong></p><p> Reduced therapeutic effect with NSAIDs.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Travolar 0.004% Eye Drops is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to Travolar 0.004% or any excipients of this preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Travolar 0.004%</strong></p><p> The most frequently reported treatment-related side effect is ocular hyperemia.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There are no adequate and well-controlled clinical studies in pregnant women. Travolar 0.004% should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether the drug or its metabolites are excreted in human milk. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk, caution should be exercised when Travolar 0.004% is administered to a lactating woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Travolar 0.004% should be used with caution in patients with active intraocular inflammation (iritis/uveitis). Travolar 0.004% should not be administered while wearing contact lenses. Contact lenses should be removed prior to the administration of the solution. Lenses may be reinserted 15 minutes following administration of Travolar 0.004%.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place and protect from light. Keep out of reach of children. Discard the container 4 weeks after opening.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Drugs for miotics and glaucoma</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Travolar 0.004%, an isopropyl ester prodrug, is a synthetic prostaglandin F2 alpha analogue that is rapidly hydrolyzed by esterases in the cornea to its biologically active free acid. The travoprost free acid is potent and highly selective for the FP prostanoid receptor. Travolar 0.004% free acid is a selective FP prostanoid receptor agonist and is believed to reduce intraocular pressure by increasing the drainage of aqueous humor, primarily through increased uveoscleral outflow and, to a lesser extent, trabecular outflow facility.</p>', 'active', '330_1742395824.jpg', 4, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 19:50:24', '2025-03-19 19:50:24'),
(3537, 'Carbolin', 'Carbocisteine', 2.50, 10, 'Capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '375 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Carbolin 375 mg</strong></p><p> Carbolin 375 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Acute bronchitis</li><li>Chronic bronchitis</li><li>Bronchial asthma</li><li>Upper respiratory tract inflammation (pharyngitis, laryngitis)</li><li>Cystic fibrosis</li><li>Bronchiectasis</li><li>Pulmonary tuberculosis</li><li>Drainage in chronic sinusitis and pneumonia</li><li>Drainage in otitis media in children</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Cough expectorants &amp; mucolytics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Carbolin 375 mg reduces goblet cell hyperplasia and therefore plays a role in the management of disorders characterized by abnormal mucus. Carbolin 375 mg loosens thick sputum effectively by reducing its viscosity, helping to expel mucus more easily from the respiratory tract. The mucolytic action occurs through several mechanisms: it breaks the disulfide bonds that cross-link certain glycoprotein molecules in mucus. The mucus produced under the influence of Carbolin 375 mg has high sialomucin and low fucomucin content. This combined effect ultimately reduces sputum viscosity and increases its volume.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Carbolin 375 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults:</strong> Initially, 2.25 g daily in divided doses, then 1.5 g daily in divided doses as the condition improves.</p><p><strong>Children:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>2-5 years:</strong> 62.5-125 mg four times daily</li><li><strong>6-12 years:</strong> 250 mg three times daily</li></ul><p>Should be taken with food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Carbolin 375 mg</strong></p><p> Neither hazardous nor therapeutically useful interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Contraindicated in active peptic ulceration and in patients with hypersensitivity to the drug.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Carbolin 375 mg</strong></p><p> Gastrointestinal discomfort, nausea, diarrhea, gastrointestinal bleeding, palpitations, dizziness, headache, heartburn, and skin rash may occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There is no information on the use of Carbolin 375 mg during lactation. While there are no reports of teratogenic effects, the manufacturers do not recommend the use of Carbolin 375 mg in the first trimester.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> No specific precautions are recommended, but Carbolin 375 mg should be used with caution in patients with a recent history of peptic ulcer and recurrent gastrointestinal bleeding.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Carbolin 375 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Symptoms:</strong> Gastrointestinal disturbances.</p><p> <strong>Management:</strong> Gastric lavage may be performed, followed by observation.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Cough expectorants &amp; mucolytics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Carbolin 375 mg reduces goblet cell hyperplasia and therefore plays a role in the management of disorders characterized by abnormal mucus. Carbolin 375 mg loosens thick sputum effectively by reducing its viscosity, helping to expel mucus more easily from the respiratory tract. The mucolytic action occurs through several mechanisms: it breaks the disulfide bonds that cross-link certain glycoprotein molecules in mucus. The mucus produced under the influence of Carbolin 375 mg has high sialomucin and low fucomucin content. This combined effect ultimately reduces sputum viscosity and increases its volume.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> There is no information on the use of Carbolin 375 mg during lactation. While there are no reports of teratogenic effects, the manufacturers do not recommend the use of Carbolin 375 mg in the first trimester.</p>', 'active', '1442_1742396211.jpeg', 53, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 19:56:51', '2025-03-19 19:56:51'),
(3538, 'Roxicef', 'Cephradine', 12.55, 4, 'Capsule', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Roxicef 500 mg</strong></p><p> Roxicef 500 mg is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. These include:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper respiratory tract infections</strong>: sinusitis, pharyngitis, tonsillitis, laryngo-tracheo bronchitis, and otitis media</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong>: bronchitis (acute and chronic), lobar pneumonia, and bronchopneumonia</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: cystitis, urethritis, and pyelonephritis</li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: abscess, cellulitis, furunculosis, and impetigo</li></ul><p><strong>Susceptible microorganisms:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gram-positive</strong>: Staphylococci (both penicillin-sensitive and resistant strains, and penicillinase-producing species), Streptococci, Streptococcus pyogenes (beta-haemolytic), and Streptococcus pneumoniae</li><li><strong>Gram-negative</strong>: Escherichia coli, Klebsiella spp., Proteus mirabilis, Haemophilus influenzae, Shigella spp., Salmonella spp. (including Salmonella typhi), and Neisseria spp.</li></ul><p>Many strains of E. coli and Staphylococcus aureus that produce penicillinase and are ampicillin-resistant are susceptible to Roxicef 500 mg, which is unaffected by this enzyme.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> First-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Roxicef 500 mg is a cephalosporin antibiotic with broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against both Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. It interferes with bacterial cell wall synthesis by inhibiting the transpeptidase enzyme. This weakens the bacterial cell wall, causing the cell to swell and rupture.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Roxicef 500 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Respiratory tract infections (except lobar pneumonia)</strong> and <strong>skin infections</strong>: 250 mg every 6 hours or 500 mg every 12 hours</li><li><strong>Lobar pneumonia</strong>: 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 g every 12 hours</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong>: 500 mg every 12 hours</li><li><strong>Severe urinary tract infections, including prostatitis</strong>: 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 g every 12 hours</li></ul><p><strong>Children (over 9 months old):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General infections</strong>: 25-50 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 6 or 12 hours</li><li><strong>Otitis media (due to H. influenzae)</strong>: 75-100 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 6 or 12 hours (max: 4 g/day)</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Roxicef 500 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Oral administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: 500 mg four times daily or 1 g twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Respiratory tract infections</strong>: 250-500 mg four times daily or 500 mg-1 g twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: 250-500 mg four times daily or 500 mg-1 g twice daily</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 25-50 mg/kg/day in divided doses (two or four times daily)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Otitis media</strong>: 75-100 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 6-12 hours (max: 4 g/day)</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Normal adult dose is appropriate</li><li><strong>Renal or hepatic impairment</strong>: Patients should be monitored</li></ul><p><strong>Injectable administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 2-4 g daily in four divided doses (max: 8 g daily)</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis</strong>: 1-2 g IM/IV as a single preoperative dose</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 50-100 mg/kg/day in four divided doses (max: 300 mg/kg/day in severe infections)</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Roxicef 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant use of nephrotoxic drugs (e.g., aminoglycosides) may increase the risk of kidney damage.</li><li>Diuretics (e.g., furosemide, ethacrynic acid) and probenecid may enhance renal toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Roxicef 500 mg should not be used in patients with known or suspected hypersensitivity to cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Roxicef 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Gastrointestinal disturbances, hypersensitivity reactions (rash, urticaria, edema), and skin reactions</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Glossitis, heartburn, dizziness, tightness in the chest, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, vaginitis, and candida overgrowth</li><li><strong>Blood disorders</strong>: Thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, agranulocytosis, aplastic anemia, hemolytic anemia</li><li><strong>Immune reactions</strong>: Fever, serum sickness-like reactions, anaphylaxis</li><li><strong>Psychiatric effects</strong>: Confusion, sleep disturbances</li><li><strong>Nervous system</strong>: Hyperactivity, hypertonia, nervousness, headaches</li><li><strong>Hepatic effects</strong>: Liver enzyme disturbances, transient hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice</li><li><strong>Renal effects</strong>: Reversible interstitial nephritis</li><li><strong>Investigations</strong>: Elevated blood urea nitrogen, serum creatinine, liver enzymes</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: FDA Pregnancy Category B. Should be used only if clearly needed, as animal studies do not always predict human response.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Excreted in human milk; caution is advised when administered to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged use may lead to superinfection due to resistant organisms.</li><li>Cross-sensitivity may occur in penicillin-hypersensitive patients.</li><li>Cephalosporins may cause a false-positive Coombs\' test and urine glucose test (with Benedict’s or Fehling’s solutions).</li><li>Dose adjustment is necessary in renal impairment.</li><li>Contains lactose—patients with lactose intolerance should avoid this medication.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Roxicef 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, gastric upset</li><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: Supportive care; gastric lavage if a large amount is ingested</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Suspension</strong>: Use within 7 days at room temperature or 14 days if refrigerated</li><li><strong>Injection solutions</strong>: Use within 2 hours at room temperature or within 12 hours if stored at 5°C</li><li>Store in a cool, dry place away from light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> First-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Roxicef 500 mg is a semisynthetic, broad-spectrum bactericidal antibiotic effective against Gram-positive and Gram-negative microorganisms. It targets the bacterial cell wall, inhibiting cross-linking of peptidoglycan, which leads to cell lysis due to osmotic pressure.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Animal studies have not shown teratogenic effects, but safety in pregnancy has not been established. Roxicef 500 mg is excreted in breast milk and should be used with caution in lactating mothers. Since dizziness may occur, patients should be cautioned about operating hazardous machinery.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> <strong>Renal Impairment:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CrCl &gt;20 ml/min</strong>: 500 mg every 6 hours</li><li><strong>CrCl 5-20 ml/min</strong>: 250 mg every 6 hours</li><li><strong>CrCl &lt;5 ml/min</strong>: 250 mg every 50-70 hours</li></ul><p><strong>Patients on hemodialysis:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>250 mg at the start of dialysis</strong></li><li><strong>250 mg 6-12 hours after the start</strong></li><li><strong>250 mg 36-48 hours after the start</strong></li><li><strong>250 mg at the next session if &gt;30 hours since the last dose</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Additional Information</strong></p><ul><li>Higher doses (up to 1 g four times daily) may be required for severe infections.</li><li>Treatment should continue for at least 2-3 days after symptoms resolve.</li><li>Chronic urinary tract infections require long-term monitoring.</li><li>Pediatric dosages should not exceed adult recommendations.</li><li>Persistent infections may require weeks of therapy.</li></ul>', 'active', '1372_1742396783.jpg', 17, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 20:06:23', '2025-03-19 20:06:23'),
(3539, 'Savatril', 'Sacubitril + Valsartan', 28.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '24 mg plus 26 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Savatril 24 mg + 26 mg</strong></p><p> This medication is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Reducing the risk of cardiovascular death and hospitalization due to heart failure in patients with chronic heart failure (NYHA Class II-IV) and reduced ejection fraction.</li><li>Treating symptomatic heart failure with systemic left ventricular systolic dysfunction in pediatric patients aged one year and older.</li><li>It is usually administered in conjunction with other heart failure therapies, replacing an ACE inhibitor (ACEi) or another angiotensin receptor blocker (ARB).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Combined antihypertensive preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Sacubitril</strong>: The active metabolite, LBQ657, inhibits neprilysin, which is a neutral endopeptidase that breaks down natriuretic peptides such as ANP, BNP, and CNP. These peptides promote vasodilation and natriuresis, contributing to improved cardiovascular function.</li><li><strong>Valsartan</strong>: An angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB) that blocks the AT1 receptor, preventing vasoconstriction and leading to vasodilation and lowered blood pressure.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult Heart Failure</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Starting dose: 49/51 mg orally twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increase to target dose of 97/103 mg twice daily after 2 to 4 weeks, as tolerated.</li><li><strong>Special Populations</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For patients not currently taking ACE inhibitors or ARBs, or previously on low doses, the starting dose is 24/26 mg twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric Heart Failure</strong>: Refer to the dose guidelines for pediatric patients aged 1 year and older.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component.</li><li>History of angioedema related to ACE inhibitors or ARBs.</li><li>Concomitant use with ACE inhibitors or aliskiren (in patients with diabetes).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Angioedema, hypotension, impaired renal function, hyperkalemia, cough, dizziness.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Not recommended during pregnancy. Discuss alternative treatment options with women planning pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Not recommended during breastfeeding, as sacubitril/valsartan is present in rat milk and could pose risks to breastfed infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Angioedema</strong>: Avoid in patients with a history of angioedema related to ACEi or ARBs.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Monitor kidney function closely.</li><li><strong>Potassium</strong>: Regularly check serum potassium levels.</li><li><strong>Blood Pressure</strong>: Monitor for hypotension, especially during the initial dose titration.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Effects</strong>: Likely to result in hypotension due to the blood-pressure-lowering effects. Symptomatic treatment is advised.</li><li><strong>Hemodialysis</strong>: Unlikely to remove this medication due to high protein binding.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place below 30°C, away from moisture and out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness not established in patients under 1 year of age.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No significant pharmacokinetic differences noted in elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Starting dose of 24/26 mg twice daily recommended for severe renal impairment (eGFR &lt; 30 mL/min/1.73 m²).</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Use a starting dose of 24/26 mg twice daily in moderate hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh B).</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Contains a neprilysin inhibitor (sacubitril) and an angiotensin receptor blocker (valsartan). Sacubitril increases levels of natriuretic peptides, improving cardiovascular and renal function, while valsartan blocks the action of angiotensin II to reduce vasoconstriction and blood pressure.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you\'d like any further clarification on any specific part of this!</p>', 'active', '1010_1742402373.jpg', 9, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 21:39:33', '2025-03-19 21:39:33'),
(3540, 'Zis-DS', 'Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate', 38.11, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '10 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zis-DS 100 ml</strong></p><p>Zis-DS 100 ml is indicated in zinc deficiency and/or zinc losing conditions. Zinc deficiency can occur as a result of inadequate diet or malabsorption. Excessive loss of zinc can occur in trauma, burns, diarrhea, and protein losing conditions. A zinc supplement is given until clinical improvement occurs, but it may need to be continued in severe malabsorption, metabolic disease, or in zinc losing states.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zinc sulphate monohydrate is an essential trace element and is involved in a number of body enzyme systems. The body needs zinc for normal growth and health. Zinc is also vital for sexual maturation and reproduction, dark vision adaptation, olfactory and gustatory activity, insulin storage &amp; release, and for a variety of host immune defenses. Zinc deficiency may lead to impaired immune function, delayed wound healing, a decrease in sense of taste and smell, a reduced ability to fight infections, poor night vision, increased risk of abortion, alopecia, mental lethargy, skin changes, and poor development of reproductive organs.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Zis-DS 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Child under 10 kg:</strong> 5 ml (1 teaspoonful) 2 times daily after food.</li><li><strong>Child between 10-30 kg:</strong> 10 ml (2 teaspoonfuls) 1-3 times daily after food.</li><li><strong>Adults and child over 30 kg:</strong> 20 ml (4 teaspoonfuls) 1-3 times daily after food.</li></ul><p>This drug is most effective if taken at least 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals. However, if it causes stomach upset, it may be taken with a meal.</p><p><strong>Administration of Zis-DS 100 ml</strong></p><p>For dispersible tablet:</p><ol><li>Place the tablet in a teaspoon.</li><li>Add an adequate amount of water.</li><li>Let the tablet dissolve completely.</li><li>Give the entire spoonful solution.</li></ol><p><strong>Interaction of Zis-DS 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant intake of a tetracycline and zinc may decrease the absorption of both the tetracycline and zinc.</li><li>Similarly, concomitant administration of zinc and quinolone drugs may also decrease the absorption of both.</li><li>Concomitant intake of penicillamine and zinc may decrease absorption of zinc.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in those who are hypersensitive to any component of the ingredient of this preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zis-DS 100 ml</strong></p><p>Zinc may cause nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, stomach upset, heartburn, and gastritis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of this product in human pregnancy has not been established. Zinc crosses the placenta and is present in breast milk.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In acute renal failure, zinc accumulation may occur in the body; so dose adjustment is needed.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '616_1742402511.webp', 5, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 21:41:51', '2025-05-05 22:34:22'),
(3541, 'Terbilab', 'Terbinafine Hydrochloride', 50.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'in-active', '659_1742402629.jpg', 50, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 21:43:49', '2025-05-13 05:03:29'),
(3542, 'Algisum DX', 'Sodium Alginate + Sodium Bicarbonate + Calcium Carbonate', 290.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '(500 mg+213 mg+325 mg)/10 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Algisum DX 200 ml</strong></h3><p>Algisum DX 200 ml is indicated for individuals experiencing:</p><ul><li>Gastric reflux</li><li>Heartburn</li><li>Flatulence associated with gastric reflux</li><li>Heartburn during pregnancy</li><li>Epigastric and retrosternal discomfort where the underlying cause is gastric reflux</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Antacids</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Algisum DX 200 ml works through a physical mechanism that does not rely on absorption into the systemic circulation. Upon ingestion, it reacts rapidly with gastric acid to form a protective alginic acid gel raft with a nearly neutral pH. This raft floats on stomach contents, effectively preventing gastro-esophageal reflux for up to 4 hours. In severe cases, the raft itself may be refluxed into the esophagus, providing a soothing effect rather than the stomach contents.</p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: 10-20 mL after meals and at bedtime, up to four times a day.</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 12 years</strong>: 5-10 mL after meals and at bedtime, up to four times a day.</li><li><strong>Children under 6 years</strong>: Not recommended.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dose modifications required.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dose modifications required.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: Use caution if a highly restricted salt diet is required.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li>A 2-hour time interval should be observed between taking this medication and other medicinal products, especially:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tetracyclines, digoxin, fluoroquinolones, iron salts, ketoconazole, neuroleptics, thyroid hormones, penicillamine, beta-blockers, glucocorticoids, chloroquine, bisphosphonates, and estramustine.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to the active ingredients or excipients.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Constipation</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Belching</li><li>Swelling in case of excessive dose.</li></ul><p>If side effects occur, consult a physician.</p><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Safe for use during pregnancy when clinically necessary, based on clinical studies and post-marketing data.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: Safe for use during breastfeeding.</li><li><strong>Fertility</strong>: No detrimental effects on fertility observed.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>If symptoms do not improve within 7 days, reassess the clinical condition.</li><li>Each 10 mL dose contains:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>141 mg of sodium</strong> (equivalent to 6.2 mmol)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>160 mg of calcium carbonate</strong> (equivalent to 1.6 mmol)</li><li>These should be considered when prescribing for patients on a restricted salt diet, especially those with congestive heart failure or renal impairment.</li><li>Caution in patients with hypercalcemia, nephrocalcinosis, or recurrent calcium-containing renal calculi.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>In case of overdose, symptomatic treatment should be given. Abdominal distension may occur.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a cool (below 30°C) and dry place, away from light. Keep out of the reach of children. Do not refrigerate or freeze.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Not recommended for children under 6 years.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No dose modifications required.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Antacids</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Algisum DX 200 ml operates through a physical mechanism and does not rely on systemic absorption. The medication combines <strong>Calcium Carbonate</strong> and <strong>Sodium Bicarbonate</strong> with <strong>Alginate</strong>. Upon ingestion, it reacts with gastric acid to form a gel raft. This raft reduces exposure to esophageal acid and protects the esophagus from stomach acid, pepsin, and bile, offering relief from gastro-esophageal reflux for up to 4 hours.</p>', 'active', '471_1742402857.webp', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 21:47:37', '2025-05-02 22:59:37'),
(3543, 'Merotrax', 'Meropenem Trihydrate', 1300.00, 1, 'x 1 gm vial', '1 gm vial', 'IV Injection', '1 gm/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you provided formatted in a clearer and more structured manner:</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Indications of Merotrax 500 mg/vial</strong></h3><p>Meropenem is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by bacteria sensitive to it, in both adults and children. The following infections are treated with Meropenem:</p><ul><li>Pneumonia and Nosocomial Pneumonia</li><li>Urinary Tract Infections</li><li>Intra-abdominal Infections</li><li>Gynaecological Infections (e.g., endometritis, pelvic inflammatory disease)</li><li>Skin and Skin Structure Infections</li><li>Meningitis</li><li>Septicaemia</li><li>Pulmonary infections in cystic fibrosis</li><li>Empiric treatment for presumed infections in febrile neutropenia patients</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Other beta-lactam Antibiotics</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Meropenem is a carbapenem antibiotic for parenteral use. It acts by interfering with bacterial cell wall synthesis. It penetrates bacterial cell walls and has a high stability to all serine beta-lactamases and strong affinity for Penicillin Binding Proteins (PBPs). It exhibits potent bactericidal activity against a broad spectrum of both Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria, including aerobic and anaerobic species.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Dosage of Merotrax 500 mg/vial</strong></h3><h4><strong>Adults</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Usual Dose</strong>: 500 mg to 1 g intravenously every 8 hours, depending on the infection type and severity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pneumonia, Urinary Tract Infections, Gynaecological Infections (e.g., endometritis, pelvic inflammatory disease), Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>: 500 mg IV every 8 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Nosocomial Pneumonias, Peritonitis, Presumed Infections in Neutropenic Patients, Septicaemia</strong>: 1 g IV every 8 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intra-abdominal Infections</strong>: 500 mg to 1 g every 8 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cystic Fibrosis</strong>: Up to 2 g every 8 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Meningitis</strong>: 2 g IV every 8 hours</li></ul><h4><strong>Children (3 months to 12 years)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Usual Dose</strong>: 10 to 40 mg/kg intravenously every 8 hours, depending on the infection type and severity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intra-abdominal Infections</strong>: 20 mg/kg every 8 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cystic Fibrosis (4-18 years)</strong>: 25-40 mg/kg every 8 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Meningitis</strong>: 40 mg/kg IV every 8 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Febrile Neutropenia</strong>: 20 mg/kg every 8 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children over 50 kg</strong>: Use adult dosage</li></ul><p>There is no experience in children with hepatic or renal impairment.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Administration of Merotrax 500 mg/vial</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Intravenous Infusion</strong>: Administer over 15-30 minutes.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Bolus</strong>: Administer over 3-5 minutes (5 to 20 ml).</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Probenecid</strong>: Increases the elimination half-life and plasma concentration of Meropenem by inhibiting renal excretion.</li><li><strong>Valproic Acid</strong>: Meropenem may reduce serum levels of valproic acid, leading to subtherapeutic levels.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Meropenem or any of its components.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Inflammation or pain at the injection site</li><li>Thrombophlebitis</li><li>Rash, pruritus, urticaria</li><li>Abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea</li><li>Headache</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: No adequate, well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Use during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Caution is advised as Meropenem is excreted in human milk.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Discontinue the drug and take appropriate action if an allergic reaction occurs.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with hepatic disease, monitoring transaminase and bilirubin levels.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><p>In case of overdose, treatment should be symptomatic. In patients with renal impairment, hemodialysis can remove Meropenem and its metabolite.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Reduce dosage for patients with creatinine clearance &lt;51 ml/min.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustments needed for liver impairment.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustments unless creatinine clearance &lt;51 ml/min.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Efficacy and tolerability have not been established in infants under 3 months.</li></ul><h3><strong>Reconstitution</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>For Intravenous Bolus</strong>: Reconstitute with sterile water for injection to obtain a clear or pale yellow solution.</li><li><strong>For Intravenous Infusion</strong>: Meropenem may be directly reconstituted with compatible infusion fluids and diluted with compatible fluids as necessary.</li></ul><p>Compatible infusion fluids include:</p><ul><li>0.9% sodium chloride, 5% glucose, mannitol, normosol-M in 5% glucose, etc.</li></ul><p>The solution should be used freshly prepared but maintains potency for up to 3 hours at up to 25°C or up to 13 hours at 5°C.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Drug Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Other beta-lactam antibiotics</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Meropenem is a carbapenem antibiotic that acts by disrupting bacterial cell wall synthesis, ensuring bactericidal activity against a broad spectrum of bacteria.</p><p><br></p><p>This detailed guide covers the various aspects of Merotrax (Meropenem) and its use in medical practice. Let me know if you need any more information or clarification!</p>', 'active', '3846_1746376364.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 21:53:16', '2025-05-04 20:32:44');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3544, 'Depian', 'Dehydroepiandrosterone', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Depian 25 mg</strong></p><p>Depian 25 mg (DHEA) and Depian 25 mg sulfate (DHEAS), collectively referred to as DHEA/S, have not demonstrated consistent efficacy in long-term, adequately powered clinical trials. Reviews of studies have found insufficient evidence to support DHEA\'s role in improving cognitive function, physical strength in elderly patients, treating postmenopausal symptoms in women, or addressing conditions like hyperlipidemia, insulin resistance, schizophrenia, or cancer. However, some evidence supports the use of DHEA/S supplementation in specific contexts, such as:</p><ul><li>Women with diminished ovarian reserves</li><li>Subpopulations of elderly women with osteoporosis</li><li>Mild systemic lupus erythematosus</li></ul><p>DHEA is considered a third-line option for treating major depressive disorder (MDD) by the Canadian Network for Mood and Anxiety Treatments (CANMAT) clinical guidelines, and there is limited evidence suggesting its potential role as an anxiolytic (anti-anxiety) agent.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Depian 25 mg</strong></p><p>The typical dosage of Depian 25 mg varies depending on the condition being treated:</p><ul><li><strong>Adrenal Insufficiency:</strong> 50 mg/day for 3 months (replacement dose); 200 mg/day (pharmacological dose).</li><li><strong>Anorexia Nervosa:</strong> 100 mg/day for 6 months in pilot studies.</li><li><strong>Diminished Ovarian Reserve:</strong> 50-75 mg/day (in divided doses) for assisted reproduction studies.</li><li><strong>Exercise-Induced Muscle Damage:</strong> 100 mg/day for 5 days in young men undergoing exercise training.</li><li><strong>Major Depressive Disorder:</strong> Doses between 30 to 450 mg/day for 6-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Metabolic Syndrome:</strong> 100 mg/day for 3 months in pre- and postmenopausal women.</li><li><strong>Postmenopausal Women:</strong> 25 mg/day is suggested to minimize androgenic side effects, though studies with 50 mg/day or more showed positive hormonal replacement outcomes.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Depian 25 mg</strong></p><p>Supraphysiological levels of DHEAS from DHEA supplementation can interfere with commercial progesterone assays, leading to false-positive increases in serum progesterone levels.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Depian 25 mg is not recommended for individuals with:</p><ul><li>Breast cancer</li><li>Prostate cancer</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Depian 25 mg</strong></p><p>DHEA is generally well-tolerated, especially in adrenal insufficiency. However, long-term safety data is lacking. Notable side effects include:</p><ul><li>Mania and hypomania</li><li>Acne, hirsutism (excessive hair growth), gynecomastia (enlarged male breasts), and testicular changes</li><li>Increased blood pressure and decreased HDL (good cholesterol)</li><li>Psychiatric effects such as agitation, confusion, anxiety, paranoia, and suicidal thoughts</li><li>Potential erythrocytosis (increase in red blood cells)</li><li>Increased insulin resistance or sensitivity (caution in diabetes)</li><li>Liver dysfunction exacerbation</li><li>Worsening of polycystic ovarian syndrome (PCOS)</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C (86°F)</li><li>Keep away from light and moisture</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>DHEA sulfate (DHEAS) acts as a noncompetitive antagonist of GABA receptors and a positive allosteric modulator of NMDA receptors. It exhibits neuroprotective, antioxidant, antihypertensive, and anti-inflammatory properties. Serum DHEA levels peak 60 to 480 minutes after administration.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Safety and efficacy during pregnancy and lactation are not well-established. DHEA supplementation has been studied for improving oocyte production in infertility treatments but its use in pregnancy is not recommended without proper medical guidance.</p>', 'active', '1255_1742403497.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 21:58:17', '2025-03-19 21:58:17'),
(3545, 'Terbex', 'Terbinafine Hydrochloride', 35.00, 1, 'x 5 gm tube', '5 gm tube', 'Cream', '1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Terbex 5 gm</strong></p><p><strong>Terbinafine tablet</strong>:</p><ul><li>For the treatment of onychomycosis (fungal infection of the nails) caused by dermatophytes (e.g., <em>Trichophyton</em> species) in toenails and fingernails.</li></ul><p><strong>Terbinafine granules</strong>:</p><ul><li>For the treatment of Tinea Capitis (scalp ringworm).</li></ul><p><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>:</p><ul><li>For fungal infections of the skin caused by dermatophytes such as <em>Trichophyton</em> species (e.g., <em>T. rubrum</em>, <em>T. mentagrophytes</em>, <em>T. verrucosum</em>), <em>Microsporum canis</em>, and <em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em>.</li><li>For yeast infections of the skin, especially those caused by <em>Candida</em> species (e.g., <em>C. albicans</em>).</li><li>For Pityriasis (tinea) versicolor caused by <em>Malassezia furfur</em> (formerly known as <em>Pityrosporum orbiculare</em>).</li></ul><p><strong>Terbinafine 1% Spray</strong>:</p><ul><li>For the treatment of tinea infections of the skin and Pityriasis versicolor.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</p><ul><li>Antifungal medications for skin and subcutaneous infections. Includes topical antifungal preparations.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><ul><li>Terbinafine is an allylamine that works by inhibiting squalene epoxidase, a key enzyme in fungal cell membrane synthesis. This leads to an accumulation of squalene and a lack of ergosterol, causing fungal cell death. It is effective against dermatophytes, molds, certain dimorphic fungi, and yeasts, depending on the species.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Apply to affected areas once or twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea corporis &amp; Tinea cruris</strong>: 1-2 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea pedis</strong>: 2-4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cutaneous candidiasis</strong>: 1-2 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong>: 2 weeks.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine tablet</strong> (for nail infections):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Finger nail onychomycosis</strong>: 250 mg (one tablet) once daily for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Toe nail onychomycosis</strong>: 250 mg once daily for 12 weeks.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine granules</strong> (for Tinea Capitis):</li><li>Dosage varies by weight:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">&lt;25 kg: 125 mg/day for up to 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">25-35 kg: 187.5 mg/day for up to 6 weeks.</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">35 kg: 250 mg/day for up to 6 weeks.</blockquote><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine 1% Spray</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Apply once or twice daily depending on the condition.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea pedis</strong>: once daily for 1 week.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea corporis/cruris</strong>: once daily for 1 week.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong>: twice daily for 1 week.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong>:</p><ul><li>Terbinafine inhibits CYP450 2D6 isozyme, affecting the metabolism of drugs metabolized by this enzyme, such as tricyclic antidepressants, beta-blockers, antiarrhythmics, and monoamine oxidase inhibitors. Careful monitoring and possible dose adjustments are recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to terbinafine.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li>Common: Diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain, rashes, pruritus, and taste disturbances.</li><li>Rare but severe: Liver failure, serious skin reactions (e.g., Stevens-Johnson syndrome), neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, and anaphylaxis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine tablet</strong>: Not recommended during pregnancy due to lack of adequate studies and because treatment can be postponed. It is present in breast milk, so not recommended during breastfeeding.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>: No clinical experience in pregnant women. Should be used only if the benefits outweigh the risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Liver</strong>: Terbinafine may cause liver failure, especially in individuals with preexisting liver conditions. Liver function should be monitored, and the drug should be discontinued if signs of liver injury appear.</li><li><strong>Skin</strong>: Serious skin reactions like Stevens-Johnson syndrome or toxic epidermal necrolysis may occur. Discontinue if rash develops.</li><li><strong>Avoid eye contact</strong>: Terbinafine cream is for external use only.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose include nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dizziness, rash, frequent urination, and headache. Doses up to 5 gm (20 times the therapeutic dose) have been taken without serious adverse effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place below 30°C, and protect from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Terbinafine cream is effective and well-tolerated for treating tinea corporis and tinea cruris in children.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No special dosage adjustments are needed for elderly patients, but reduce the dose for those with significant liver or kidney impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong>:</p><ul><li>Other antifungal preparations, topical antifungal preparations.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</p><ul><li>Terbinafine inhibits ergosterol biosynthesis in fungal cell membranes by blocking the enzyme squalene epoxidase. This leads to increased membrane permeability and fungal cell death.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine tablet</strong>: Not recommended during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine cream</strong>: Should be used only if the benefits outweigh the risks during pregnancy.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>:</p><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of terbinafine have not been established in pediatric patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in the Elderly</strong>:</p><ul><li>Generally safe, though dose reduction may be necessary for those with significant hepatic or renal impairment.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '160_1742403818.png', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 22:03:38', '2025-05-13 04:55:59'),
(3546, 'Cataflam', 'Diclofenac Potassium', 6.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Cataflam 50 mg (Diclofenac) - Summary</h3><p><strong>Indications:</strong> Cataflam 50 mg is used for the short-term treatment of acute conditions such as:</p><ul><li>Post-traumatic pain, inflammation, and swelling (e.g., sprains)</li><li>Post-operative pain, inflammation, and swelling (e.g., following dental or orthopedic surgery)</li><li>Painful/inflammatory conditions in gynecology (e.g., primary dysmenorrhea or adnexitis)</li><li>Migraine attacks</li><li>Painful syndromes of the vertebral column</li><li>Non-articular rheumatism</li><li>As an adjuvant in severe painful inflammatory infections of the ear, nose, or throat (e.g., pharyngotonsillitis, otitis)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Diclofenac works by inhibiting cyclooxygenase (COX-1 and COX-2), reducing prostaglandin synthesis.</li><li>Prostaglandins mediate pain and inflammation, and by inhibiting them, diclofenac reduces pain and inflammation.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> Start with a loading dose of 50 mg, followed by 25-50 mg every 8 hours as needed.</li><li><strong>Migraine:</strong> Start with 50 mg, then another 25-50 mg after 2 hours if necessary. Maximum daily dose is 150 mg.</li><li><strong>Children (over 14 years):</strong> Up to 75 mg daily in divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Lithium &amp; Digoxin:</strong> May increase plasma concentrations.</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants:</strong> Risk of increased bleeding.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporine:</strong> Risk of nephrotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate:</strong> Increased risk of serious toxicity when used together within 24 hours.</li><li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> Diclofenac may inhibit the effects of diuretics.</li><li><strong>Other NSAIDs &amp; Steroids:</strong> Increased risk of unwanted effects when co-administered.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Diclofenac.</li><li>Should not be used in patients with a history of asthma, urticaria, or allergic reactions after taking NSAIDs.</li><li>Contraindicated for perioperative pain following coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common (Occasional):</strong> Epigastric pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal cramps, dyspepsia, flatulence, anorexia.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Gastrointestinal bleeding, peptic ulcer (with or without bleeding or perforation), bloody diarrhea.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Not recommended, especially in the third trimester. Should only be used if absolutely necessary, with the lowest effective dose.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Small amounts may pass into breast milk, but no harmful effects on infants are expected.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Diclofenac reduces pain and inflammation by inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis in peripheral tissues.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '813_1742404349.png', 40, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 22:12:29', '2025-03-19 22:12:29'),
(3547, 'Amotid CL', 'Amoxicillin + Clavulanic Acid', 30.00, 7, 'tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg plus 125 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Amotid CL 500 mg + 125 mg (Co-amoxiclav)</strong></p><p> Co-amoxiclav is indicated for short-term treatment of bacterial infections in the following areas:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper respiratory tract infections</strong> (including ENT), such as tonsillitis, sinusitis, and otitis media</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong>, including acute and chronic bronchitis, lobar and bronchopneumonia</li><li><strong>Genito-urinary tract infections</strong>, such as cystitis, urethritis, and pyelonephritis</li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong></li><li><strong>Bone and joint infections</strong>, e.g., osteomyelitis</li><li><strong>Other infections</strong>, such as septic abortion, puerperal sepsis, and intra-abdominal sepsis</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><ul><li><strong>375 mg tablet</strong>: Amoxicillin 250 mg (as Amoxicillin Trihydrate BP) and Clavulanic Acid 125 mg (as Diluted Potassium Clavulanate BP)</li><li><strong>625 mg tablet</strong>: Amoxicillin 500 mg (as Amoxicillin Trihydrate BP) and Clavulanic Acid 125 mg (as Diluted Potassium Clavulanate BP)</li><li><strong>1 gm tablet</strong>: Amoxicillin 875 mg (as Amoxicillin Trihydrate BP) and Clavulanic Acid 125 mg (as Diluted Potassium Clavulanate BP)</li><li><strong>Powder for Suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5 ml of reconstituted suspension contains Amoxicillin 125 mg and Clavulanic Acid 31.25 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Forte suspension: Each 5 ml of reconstituted suspension contains Amoxicillin 400 mg and Clavulanic Acid 57.5 mg</li><li><strong>1.2 gm Injection</strong>: Amoxicillin Sodium 1 gm and Clavulanate Potassium 200 mg</li><li><strong>0.6 gm Injection</strong>: Amoxicillin Sodium 500 mg and Clavulanate Potassium 100 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Broad spectrum penicillins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Co-amoxiclav is a combination of <strong>Amoxicillin</strong>, a broad-spectrum bactericidal antibiotic, and <strong>Clavulanic Acid</strong>, a beta-lactamase inhibitor. Amoxicillin is effective against many Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria but is vulnerable to degradation by beta-lactamases, which are produced by resistant bacteria. Clavulanic acid inhibits beta-lactamase enzymes, protecting amoxicillin from degradation and extending its antimicrobial spectrum.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral Dosage</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: 375 mg tablet, 3 times a day. For severe infections, 625 mg tablet, 3 times a day or 1 gm tablet twice a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6-12 years)</strong>: 2 teaspoonful of suspension every 8 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (1-6 years)</strong>: 1 teaspoonful of suspension every 8 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children under 1 year</strong>: 25 mg/kg/day, divided doses every 8 hours</li><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: 1.2 gm every 6-8 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (up to 3 months)</strong>: 30 mg/kg every 8 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (3 months to 12 years)</strong>: 30 mg/kg every 6-8 hours</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral dosage</strong>: Take with meals to reduce gastrointestinal intolerance, though absorption of clavulanate potassium is enhanced if taken at the start of a meal.</li><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>: Administer intravenously either as an injection (over 2 minutes) or slow infusion (30 minutes). Must be used within 20 minutes of reconstitution.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged <strong>bleeding time</strong> and <strong>prothrombin time</strong> may occur.</li><li>Co-amoxiclav may <strong>reduce the efficacy of oral contraceptives</strong>.</li><li>Concomitant use of <strong>allopurinol</strong> may increase the risk of allergic skin reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Penicillin hypersensitivity</strong></li><li><strong>Cross-sensitivity</strong> with other beta-lactam antibiotics (e.g., cephalosporins)</li><li><strong>History of Co-amoxiclav or Penicillin-associated cholestatic jaundice</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Mild and transient side effects, including:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Diarrhoea</strong>, <strong>indigestion</strong>, <strong>nausea</strong>, <strong>vomiting</strong>, and <strong>candidiasis</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rare cases of <strong>hepatitis</strong>, <strong>cholestatic jaundice</strong>, <strong>urticarial rashes</strong>, and more severe skin reactions (e.g., <strong>Stevens-Johnson syndrome</strong>, <strong>exfoliative dermatitis</strong>)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Angioedema</strong> and <strong>anaphylaxis</strong> (rare)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Use only if considered essential. Limited human experience shows no adverse effects.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Trace amounts of amoxicillin are found in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients on <strong>anticoagulation therapy</strong> or with severe <strong>hepatic dysfunction</strong>.</li><li>In cases of <strong>renal impairment</strong>, adjust dosage.</li><li>Maintain adequate <strong>fluid intake</strong> and <strong>urinary output</strong> during high-dose therapy to minimize crystalluria.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose is unlikely but could cause <strong>gastrointestinal symptoms</strong> and disturbances in <strong>fluid/electrolyte balance</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hemodialysis</strong> can remove the drug from the circulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below <strong>25°C</strong>, away from light and moisture.</li><li><strong>Suspension</strong> should be refrigerated and used within <strong>7 days</strong> after reconstitution.</li><li><strong>IV vials</strong> should be used within <strong>20 minutes</strong> of reconstitution.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: Adjust doses for mild to severe impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: Use with caution and monitor liver function.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Adjust dosage for children with renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Broad spectrum penicillins</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Amoxicillin is bactericidal against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative microorganisms. Clavulanic acid inactivates beta-lactamases, extending the activity of Amoxicillin.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1623_1742404747.png', 69, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 22:19:07', '2025-03-19 22:19:07'),
(3548, 'Milium', 'Domperidone Maleate', 2.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Milium 10 mg</strong></p><p>Dyspeptic symptom complex, often linked with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, may include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric sensation of fullness</li><li>Abdominal distension</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Belching</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Early satiety</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms can occur in various conditions, such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine. Additionally, in Parkinson\'s disease, it can manifest as dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting. For radiological studies, it can be used to speed up barium transit in follow-through radiological examinations.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Motility Stimulants</li><li>Motility Stimulants/Dopamine Antagonist</li><li>Prokinetic Drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Milium 10 mg functions as a dopamine receptor antagonist. It exerts gastroprokinetic action by inhibiting dopamine receptors found in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier means it has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Milium 10 mg</strong></p><p>For adults, the recommended dosage is 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily. Children should take 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily. When used for acute nausea and vomiting, the maximum treatment duration should not exceed 12 weeks. It is advisable to take this medication 15-30 minutes before a meal.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage of Milium 10 mg</strong></p><p>Milium 10 mg should be taken 15-30 minutes before meals, and if needed, before bedtime. The typical recommended oral dosage of Domperidone is as follows:</p><ul><li><strong>For Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The maximum daily dose of Domperidone is 80 mg.</li><li><strong>For Children:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg of body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>In cases of dyspeptic symptoms:</p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Adults:</strong>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Children:</strong>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>For acute and sub-acute conditions, especially in cases of acute nausea and vomiting:</p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Adults:</strong>20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Children:</strong>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>When administered rectally in the form of suppositories:</p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Adults (including elderly):</strong>30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Children:</strong>The maximum daily rectal dose for children weighing between 10 to 25 kg is 30 mg. The dose may be divided throughout the day if necessary.</li></ul><p>Please note that the maximum treatment period for acute nausea and vomiting is 12 weeks.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction of Milium 10 mg</strong></p><p>The simultaneous use of anticholinergic drugs may counteract the antidyspeptic effects of domperidone. It\'s important to note that antacids and antisecretory drugs should not be administered concurrently with domperidone as they can reduce its oral bioavailability. Domperidone primarily undergoes metabolism through the CYP3A4 enzyme. In vitro data suggest that coadministration with drugs significantly inhibiting this enzyme may lead to elevated plasma levels of domperidone. Examples of CYP3A4 inhibitors include azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, nefazodone, and others.</p><p>Theoretically, as domperidone has gastro-kinetic effects, it could potentially impact the absorption of concurrently orally administered drugs, especially those with sustained release or enteric-coated formulations. However, in patients already stabilized on medications like digoxin or paracetamol, the concurrent use of domperidone did not affect the blood levels of these drugs. Domperidone does not potentiate the action of neuroleptics, and it suppresses the unwanted peripheral effects such as digestive disorders, nausea, and vomiting caused by dopaminergic agonists like bromocriptine and L-dopa without counteracting their central properties.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Milium 10 mg is not to be used by patients with a known hypersensitivity to this medication or in the case of neonates. It should also be avoided in situations where gastrointestinal stimulation could be unsafe, such as in cases of gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation. Additionally, it is contraindicated in patients with a prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma).</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Side Effects of Milium 10 mg</strong></p><p>Side effects are uncommon, with occasional reports of transient intestinal cramps. Extrapyramidal symptoms are rare among young children and exceptionally rare in adults. These symptoms typically resolve spontaneously and completely upon discontinuing the treatment. Since the pituitary gland is situated outside the blood-brain barrier, domperidone may lead to an increase in plasma prolactin levels. In rare cases, this hyperprolactinemia can result in neuroendocrinological effects like galactorrhea and gynecomastia. In situations where the blood-brain barrier is immature (as in infants) or compromised, the possibility of neurological side effects cannot be entirely ruled out. Additionally, there have been occasional reports of rare allergic reactions, including rash and urticaria.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of using Milium 10 mg during pregnancy has not been established; hence, its use is not recommended during pregnancy. Animal studies have not shown any teratogenic effects on the fetus. Domperidone may induce galactorrhea and enhance post-natal lactation. It is excreted in breast milk in minimal quantities, which are insufficient to be considered harmful.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Milium 10 mg should be employed with utmost care in children due to the potential for an increased risk of extrapyramidal reactions, which can be more pronounced in young children with an incompletely developed blood-brain barrier. Additionally, considering that domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should also be used cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Milium 10 mg</strong></p><p>Signs of an overdose can manifest as drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions, particularly in children. If an overdose occurs, it is advisable to administer activated charcoal and closely monitor the patient. The use of anticholinergic drugs, antiparkinson drugs, or antihistamines with anticholinergic properties may be beneficial in managing extrapyramidal reactions.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Motility Stimulants</li><li>Motility Stimulants/Dopamine Antagonist</li><li>Prokinetic Drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Milium 10 mg acts as a dopamine antagonist, primarily by blocking dopamine receptors located in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its gastroprokinetic effect is achieved by inhibiting dopamine receptors that influence the motility of the gastrointestinal tract. Because it has limited ability to penetrate the blood-brain barrier, Domperidone has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects. Domperidone restores normal motility and tone in the upper gastrointestinal tract, promotes gastric emptying, enhances antral and duodenal peristalsis, and regulates pylorus contraction. Additionally, it increases esophageal peristalsis and lowers esophageal sphincter pressure, preventing the regurgitation of gastric contents.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in infants:</strong> Given that the metabolic and blood-brain barrier functions are not fully developed during the first months of life, any medication should be administered to infants with great caution and under close medical supervision. Although the typical absence of neurological side effects with domperidone is primarily due to its limited penetration through the blood-brain barrier, the potential occurrence of such effects cannot be entirely ruled out in infants under 1 year of age.</li><li><strong>Use in liver disorders:</strong> Because domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should be employed cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Use in kidney disorders:</strong> In individuals with severe renal insufficiency (serum creatinine &gt;6 mg/100ml, i.e., &gt;0.6 mmol/l), the elimination half-life of domperidone increases from 7.4 to 20.8 hours, although plasma drug levels remain lower than in healthy volunteers. Since very little unchanged drug is excreted through the kidneys, it is unlikely that the dose of a single acute administration needs to be adjusted in patients with renal insufficiency. However, for repeated administration, the dosing frequency should be reduced to once or twice daily, depending on the severity of the impairment, and the dose may need to be lowered. In general, patients on prolonged therapy should undergo regular reviews.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1481_1742404905.jpg', 67, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 22:21:45', '2025-03-19 22:21:45'),
(3549, 'Prucalo', 'Prucalopride Succinate', 14.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '1 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information on Prucalo 1 mg in a more organized and formatted structure:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Prucalo 1 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptomatic treatment of chronic constipation</strong> in adults where laxatives fail to provide adequate relief.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Osmotic purgatives</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Prucalopride selectively stimulates the <strong>5-HT4 receptors</strong>, with no interaction with the <strong>hERG channel</strong> or <strong>5-HT1 receptors</strong>, reducing cardiovascular risk compared to other similar drugs.</li><li>5-HT4 receptors are primarily located in smooth muscle cells, enterochromaffin cells, and the myenteric plexus of the gastrointestinal tract.</li><li>Activation of these receptors induces <strong>acetylcholine release</strong>, stimulating motility by causing contraction of the colon’s muscle layer and relaxation of the circular muscle layer, leading to <strong>propulsion of luminal content</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Prucalo 1 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 2 mg once daily (with or without food, at any time of the day). Exceeding 2 mg daily is not expected to increase efficacy.</li><li><strong>Older Adults</strong>: Start with 1 mg once daily; the dose can be increased to 2 mg if needed.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Not recommended for use in children or adolescents under 18 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Prucalo 1 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Low interaction potential</strong> with other drugs.</li><li>Prucalopride does not significantly affect the <strong>CYP-mediated metabolism</strong> of co-administered drugs.</li><li><strong>Ketoconazole</strong> (200 mg b.i.d.) and other potent <strong>P-glycoprotein inhibitors</strong> like verapamil, cyclosporine A, and quinidine may increase prucalopride\'s systemic exposure slightly (approximately 40%), but this effect is not clinically relevant.</li><li>No clinically relevant effects on the pharmacokinetics of warfarin, digoxin, alcohol, paroxetine, or oral contraceptives.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to prucalopride or any excipients.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong> requiring dialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Prucalo 1 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common adverse reactions</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Headache</strong> (17.8%)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastrointestinal symptoms</strong> (abdominal pain, nausea, diarrhea).</li><li>These effects are most common at the start of therapy and typically resolve with continued treatment.</li><li>Other adverse reactions are generally mild to moderate in intensity.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Not recommended during pregnancy. Women of childbearing potential should use effective contraception during treatment.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Not recommended due to a lack of human data.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Excretion</strong>: Prucalopride is primarily eliminated through renal excretion. For patients with severe renal impairment, a dose of 1 mg once daily is recommended.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Use with caution in patients with severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh class C). Starting with 1 mg once daily may be increased to 2 mg if required.</li><li><strong>Diarrhea</strong>: May reduce the efficacy of oral contraceptives, so an additional contraceptive method is recommended.</li><li><strong>Lactose Content</strong>: Not recommended for patients with rare hereditary problems of galactose intolerance or glucose-galactose malabsorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Prucalo 1 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Headache, nausea, and diarrhea (exaggeration of known pharmacodynamic effects).</li><li>Treatment: Symptomatic and supportive measures as required. Fluid loss due to diarrhea or vomiting may need correction of electrolyte disturbances.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store at room temperature below <strong>30°C</strong>. Keep in the original bottle and do not remove desiccant.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: For severe renal impairment (GFR &lt;30 ml/min/1.73 m²), the dose is <strong>1 mg once daily</strong>. No dose adjustment needed for mild to moderate renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: For severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh class C), start with 1 mg once daily, which may be increased to 2 mg if needed. No adjustment is required for mild to moderate hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Osmotic purgatives</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Selectively stimulates the <strong>5-HT4 receptors</strong> in the gastrointestinal tract, enhancing motility through acetylcholine release, promoting muscle contraction in the colon, and relaxation of circular muscles, thus aiding the propulsion of intestinal contents.</li></ul><p>If you need further details or clarification on any aspect, feel free to ask!</p>', 'active', '23_1742405104.webp', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 22:25:04', '2025-03-19 22:25:04'),
(3550, 'Biocal Kit', 'Ibandronic Acid + Algae Calcium + Vitamin D3', 56.66, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '150 mg plus 600 mg plus 400 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1776_1742405251.jpg', 44, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 22:27:31', '2025-05-03 00:52:34'),
(3551, 'Renofix', 'Roxadustat', 500.00, 12, 'tablet', '12 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '70 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Renofix 70 mg</h3><p>Renofix 70 mg is indicated for the treatment of adult patients with symptomatic anemia associated with chronic kidney disease (CKD).</p><h3>Dosage of Renofix 70 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Recommended Dose</strong>: The appropriate dose of Renofix 70 mg must be taken orally three times per week and not on consecutive days. The dose should be individualized to achieve and maintain target hemoglobin (Hb) levels of 10 to 12 g/dL.</li><li><strong>Patients not on erythropoiesis-stimulating agent treatment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For adults, the usual starting dose is 50 mg three times weekly.</li><li>The recommended starting dose of Renofix 70 mg is:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">70 mg three times per week for patients weighing less than 100 kg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">100 mg three times per week for patients weighing 100 kg and over.</li><li><strong>Patients switching from erythropoiesis-stimulating agents</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For adults, the usual starting dose is 70 or 100 mg three times weekly. The dosage thereafter should be adjusted according to the patient\'s condition.</li><li><strong>Dose Adjustment</strong>: When dose adjustments are required, increase or decrease the dose according to the \"Dose increase/decrease table\" and \"Stepwise Dose adjustment sequence\" below. Once adjusted, maintain the dose level for ≥4 weeks. If the hemoglobin concentration increases rapidly (&gt;2.0 g/dL) within 4 weeks of a dose increase, decrease the dose or suspend the treatment immediately.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The stepwise dose adjustments should follow this sequence: 20 mg → 40 mg → 50 mg → 70 mg → 100 mg → 150 mg → 200 mg → 250 mg → 300 mg → 400 mg (only for CKD patients on dialysis).</li><li><strong>Missed Dose</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">When there is ≥ 24-hour interval until the next scheduled dosing time, take the missed dose immediately and follow the prescribed schedule for subsequent doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If there is &lt;24 hours until the next scheduled dosing time, skip the missed dose and take the next dose as scheduled. Do not take 2 doses on the same day.</li><li><strong>Method of Administration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Renofix 70 mg tablets are to be taken orally with or without food.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Renofix 70 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Renofix 70 mg with Phosphate Binders and Other Products Containing Multivalent Cations (except lanthanum carbonate)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Risk</strong>: Decreased Renofix 70 mg AUC by 67% and 46%, and Cmax by 66% and 52%.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Renofix 70 mg should be taken at least 1 hour after administration of phosphate binders or other medicinal products or supplements containing multivalent cations.</li><li><strong>Renofix 70 mg with gemfibrozil (CYP2C8 and OATP1B1 inhibitor) or probenecid (UGT and OAT1/OAT3 inhibitor)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Risk</strong>: Increased Renofix 70 mg AUC by 2.3-fold and Cmax by 1.4-fold.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Adjust the dose of Renofix 70 mg following dose adjustment rules based on Hb monitoring.</li><li><strong>Renofix 70 mg with OATP1B1 or BCRP Substrates (e.g., simvastatin, rosuvastatin, atorvastatin)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Risk</strong>: Increased AUC and Cmax.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Adjust the dose of Renofix 70 mg following dose adjustment rules based on Hb monitoring.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Renofix 70 mg</h3><p>Common adverse reactions include:</p><ul><li>Hypertension</li><li>Vascular access thrombosis</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Peripheral edema</li><li>Hyperkalemia</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Thrombotic Vascular Events (TVEs)</strong>: Renofix 70 mg may initiate thrombotic vascular events, particularly in patients with pre-existing risk factors for TVEs, including obesity and a history of TVEs.</li><li><strong>Seizures</strong>: Use with caution in patients with a history of seizures.</li><li><strong>Infections</strong>: Should not be administered if the patient has serious signs and symptoms of an infection.</li><li><strong>Liver Disorder</strong>: Should not be administered to patients with liver disorders.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Renofix 70 mg should not be initiated in pregnant women.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Renofix 70 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Increased heart rate and elevated hemoglobin concentration may occur with overdosage.</li><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: Appropriate measures such as dose reduction or interruption should be taken. This drug is not removed by dialysis.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a cool (below 30°C), dry place, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li>Drugs for Hemolytic Hypoplastic &amp; Renal Anemia</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Renofix 70 mg is an orally bioavailable, hypoxia-inducible factor prolyl hydroxylase inhibitor (HIF-PHI) that enhances erythropoiesis. By inhibiting HIF-PH, Renofix 70 mg prevents the breakdown of hypoxia-inducible factors (HIFs), leading to increased erythropoietin (EPO) production, improved iron availability, and higher hemoglobin levels. It also reduces the expression of hepcidin, promotes iron absorption, and aids in hemoglobin production. Renofix 70 mg has been shown to improve red blood cell count while maintaining physiological EPO levels in CKD patients.</p><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li>Renofix 70 mg is contraindicated during pregnancy and breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3>Pediatric Uses</h3><ul><li>Renofix 70 mg is not indicated for use in children.</li></ul><h3>Liver Dysfunction Patients</h3><ul><li>Renofix 70 mg is not recommended for use in patients with severe hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '461_1742405430.jpg', 67, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 22:30:30', '2025-03-19 22:30:30'),
(3552, 'Valporin CR', 'Sodium Valproate', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Valporin CR 200 mg</h3><p>Valporin CR 200 mg (Sodium Valproate) is indicated for the treatment of various types of epilepsy, including:</p><ul><li>Partial seizures</li><li>Absence seizures (petit mal)</li><li>Generalized tonic-clonic seizures (grand mal)</li><li>Myoclonic seizures</li><li>Atonic seizures</li><li>Mixed seizures that include absence attacks</li></ul><p>Additionally, it is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis of febrile convulsion</li><li>Prophylaxis of post-traumatic epilepsy</li><li>Treatment of bipolar disorder</li><li>Prophylaxis of migraine</li></ul><h3>Composition</h3><ul><li><strong>Valporin CR 200 mg Tablet</strong>: Each enteric-coated tablet contains Valporin CR 200 mg BP.</li><li><strong>Valporin CR 200 mg Syrup</strong>: Each 5 ml contains Valporin CR 200 mg BP.</li><li><strong>Valporin CR 200 mg Controlled Release Tablet (200 mg, 300 mg, 500 mg)</strong>: Contains Valporin CR 200 mg and Valproic acid BP in varying amounts.</li><li><strong>Valporin CR 200 mg Injection</strong>: Each 5 ml solution contains Valporin CR 200 mg BP equivalent to Valproic Acid 500 mg.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Primary anti-epileptic drugs</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>The exact mechanism of action is not fully established, but it is suggested that Valporin CR 200 mg increases brain levels of gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA), which helps in controlling seizures.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><h4>Adults:</h4><ul><li><strong>Initial Dose</strong>: 600 mg/day in 2 divided doses, preferably after food. The dose may be increased by 200 mg/day every 3 days until a maximum of 2.5 g/day is reached.</li><li><strong>Maintenance Dose</strong>: 1-2 g/day (20-30 mg/kg/day).</li></ul><h4>Children:</h4><ul><li><strong>Up to 20 kg</strong>: Initial dose of 20 mg/kg/day in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Over 20 kg</strong>: Initial dose of 400 mg/day, increased by 20-30 mg/kg/day to achieve control.</li></ul><h4>Febrile Convulsion:</h4><ul><li><strong>20-30 mg/kg/day</strong> in 3 divided doses.</li></ul><h4>Bipolar Disorder:</h4><ul><li><strong>Initial Dose</strong>: 600 mg/day (20-30 mg/kg/day) in 2-3 divided doses.</li><li><strong>Maintenance Dose</strong>: 1000-2000 mg/day.</li></ul><h4>Prophylaxis of Migraine:</h4><ul><li>400-600 mg/day, may require 1000-1500 mg/day.</li></ul><h4>Injection (Adults):</h4><ul><li>May be given intravenously, starting with 400-800 mg, followed by continuous or repeated infusion up to a maximum of 2500 mg/day.</li></ul><h3>Drug Interactions</h3><p>Valporin CR 200 mg may interact with:</p><ul><li>Phenobarbital</li><li>Phenytoin</li><li>Warfarin</li><li>Aspirin</li></ul><p>It may act as a non-specific inhibitor of drug metabolism.</p><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Valporin CR 200 mg is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to the drug</li><li>Liver dysfunction</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Anorexia</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Sedation, ataxia, and tremor (infrequent)</li><li>Rash, alopecia, and stimulation of appetite</li><li>Elevation of liver enzymes (up to 40% of patients)</li><li>Rarely, fulminant hepatitis, acute pancreatitis, and hyperammonemia</li><li>Children under 2 years of age, especially those treated with multiple antiepileptic agents, are more prone to liver damage.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Valporin CR 200 mg crosses the placenta and has been associated with neural tube defects such as anencephaly and spina bifida in the first trimester. It should be avoided during pregnancy and in women of childbearing potential.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Valporin CR 200 mg is excreted in breast milk but breastfeeding is generally considered to pose no significant risk to the child.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Monitor liver function before therapy and during the first 6 months.</li><li>Caution in patients with renal impairment, pregnancy, breastfeeding, and systemic lupus erythematosus.</li><li>Sudden withdrawal should be avoided.</li><li>It may cause a false result in urine ketone tests.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C.</li><li>Keep away from light and out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Valporin CR 200 mg exerts its anti-epileptic effects, likely by increasing GABA levels in the brain, though the exact mechanism is not yet fully understood.</p><h3>Summary:</h3><p>Valporin CR 200 mg is a key anti-epileptic medication used for various types of seizures, bipolar disorder, and migraine prophylaxis. It requires careful monitoring, especially for liver function and during pregnancy.</p>', 'in-active', '211_1742405602.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 22:33:22', '2025-05-13 05:43:21');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3553, 'Omari', 'Omarigliptin', 80.00, 4, 'tablet', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '12.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1406_1742405744.webp', 5, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 22:35:44', '2025-03-19 22:35:44'),
(3554, 'Glipxen-M', 'Linagliptin + Metformin Hydrochloride', 14.00, 7, 'tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.5 mg plus 850 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Glipxen-M 2.5 mg + 850 mg</strong></h3><p>This combination is indicated as an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycemic control in adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus when treatment with both Linagliptin and Metformin Hydrochloride is appropriate.</p><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p>Combination Oral Hypoglycemic Preparations</p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Linagliptin</strong>: Linagliptin is an inhibitor of DPP-4 (dipeptidyl peptidase-4), an enzyme that degrades the incretin hormones GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1) and GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide). By inhibiting DPP-4, Linagliptin increases the concentration of active incretin hormones, stimulating insulin release from pancreatic beta (β) cells in a glucose-dependent manner and decreasing glucagon secretion from pancreatic alpha (α) cells.</li><li><strong>Metformin Hydrochloride</strong>: Metformin is a biguanide that works by lowering both basal and postprandial plasma glucose levels. It does so by decreasing hepatic glucose production, reducing intestinal absorption of glucose, and improving insulin sensitivity, which increases peripheral glucose uptake and utilization. It does not cause hypoglycemia, unlike sulfonylureas.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Linagliptin &amp; Metformin Immediate Release Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Starting Dose</strong>: In patients not treated with Metformin, initiate with 2.5 mg Linagliptin and 500 mg Metformin twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In patients already on Metformin, start with 2.5 mg Linagliptin and the current dose of Metformin.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The maximum dose is 2.5 mg Linagliptin and 1000 mg Metformin twice daily with meals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gradual dose escalation is recommended to reduce gastrointestinal side effects.</li><li><strong>Linagliptin &amp; Metformin Extended Release Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Starting Dose</strong>: For those not treated with Metformin, start with 5 mg Linagliptin and 1000 mg Metformin once daily with a meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum daily dose: 5 mg Linagliptin and 2000 mg Metformin.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Switch to extended-release tablets if the patient is already on the immediate-release form.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Cationic Drugs</strong> (e.g., amiloride, digoxin, morphine, ranitidine, trimethoprim) may reduce Metformin elimination.</li><li><strong>P-glycoprotein/CYP3A4 Inducers</strong> (e.g., rifampin) can reduce the efficacy of this combination.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with <strong>renal impairment</strong>, acute or chronic metabolic acidosis (e.g., diabetic ketoacidosis), and hypersensitivity to either Linagliptin or Metformin Hydrochloride.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Common side effects include <strong>nasopharyngitis</strong> and <strong>diarrhea</strong>.</li><li>Hypoglycemia may occur, especially in patients also treated with sulfonylureas.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>There are no well-controlled studies in pregnant women with this combination, so it should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed.</li><li>Caution should be exercised when administered to lactating mothers.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Lactic Acidosis</strong>: In the event of lactic acidosis, discontinue Metformin immediately and start supportive therapy.</li><li>There have been postmarketing reports of <strong>acute pancreatitis</strong>. If pancreatitis is suspected, discontinue treatment.</li><li>Temporarily discontinue this medication before radiologic studies or surgery that restrict food and fluid intake.</li><li>Metformin may reduce <strong>Vitamin B12</strong> levels; monitor hematologic parameters annually.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>In case of overdose, employ supportive measures and monitor the patient clinically.</li><li><strong>Metformin</strong> overdose can lead to <strong>lactic acidosis</strong>, and <strong>Linagliptin</strong> is unlikely to be removed by dialysis.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><p>Combination Oral Hypoglycemic Preparations</p><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Linagliptin</strong> increases incretin hormones, which helps stimulate insulin release and suppress glucagon secretion in a glucose-dependent manner.</li><li><strong>Metformin Hydrochloride</strong> reduces glucose production by the liver, increases glucose uptake by cells, and decreases glucose absorption from the intestines.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h3><ul><li>There is insufficient data on the use of this combination in pregnant women. Use only if clearly needed. Exercise caution if used by lactating mothers.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1471_1742405875.jpg', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 22:37:55', '2025-03-19 22:37:55'),
(3555, 'Unigerd', 'Vonoprazan', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '611_1742406001.webp', 11, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 22:40:01', '2025-03-19 22:40:01'),
(3556, 'Zolaren Plus', 'Brinzolamide + Brimonidine Tartrate', 550.00, 1, 'x 5 ml drop', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Suspension', '5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Zolaren Plus 5 ml</h3><p>Zolaren Plus 5 ml is indicated for the reduction of elevated intraocular pressure in patients with open-angle glaucoma or ocular hypertension.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Drugs for miotics and glaucoma.</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li><strong>Brinzolamide</strong> is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor that reduces intraocular pressure (IOP) by decreasing aqueous humor secretion in the ciliary processes of the eye.</li><li><strong>Brimonidine Tartrate</strong> is an alpha-2 adrenergic receptor agonist that reduces IOP by decreasing aqueous humor production and increasing uveoscleral outflow.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zolaren Plus 5 ml</h3><p>Instill one drop of Zolaren Plus 5 ml in the affected eye(s) three times daily. If more than one topical ophthalmic drug is being used, the drugs should be administered at least five minutes apart. Shake well before use.</p><h3>Interaction of Zolaren Plus 5 ml</h3><p>Rare instances of drug interactions have occurred with:</p><ul><li>High-dose salicylate therapy</li><li>CNS depressants</li><li>Antihypertensives/Cardiac Glycosides</li><li>Tricyclic antidepressants</li><li>Monoamine oxidase inhibitors</li></ul><p>Therefore, the potential for such drug interactions should be considered in patients receiving Zolaren Plus 5 ml.</p><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Zolaren Plus 5 ml is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients who are hypersensitive to Brinzolamide, Brimonidine Tartrate, or any ingredient in the formulation.</li><li>Neonates and infants (under the age of 2 years).</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Zolaren Plus 5 ml</h3><p>The most commonly reported side effects include:</p><ul><li>Blurred vision</li><li>Eye irritation</li><li>Bad taste</li><li>Dry mouth</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy category C</strong>: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Zolaren Plus 5 ml should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li>It is not known whether Brinzolamide or Brimonidine Tartrate are excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised while giving this ophthalmic suspension to a nursing mother.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Shake well before use.</li><li>For ophthalmic use only.</li><li>Contact lenses should be removed during instillation, but may be reinserted 15 minutes after instillation.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Zolaren Plus 5 ml</h3><ul><li><strong>Brinzolamide</strong>: Electrolyte imbalance, acidotic state, and nervous system effects may occur following oral overdose. Serum electrolyte levels (especially potassium) and blood pH levels should be monitored.</li><li><strong>Brimonidine Tartrate</strong>: In neonates, infants, and children, overdose can lead to hypotension. Supportive and symptomatic therapy is the treatment, and maintaining a patent airway is essential.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store at room temperature and protect from light.</li><li>Do not touch the dropper tip to any surface.</li><li>The contents should be used no more than one month after opening the bottle.</li><li>Shake well before use and do not freeze.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Use in children</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in children below the age of 2 years have not been established.</li><li><strong>Use in elderly patients</strong>: No overall differences in safety and effectiveness have been observed between elderly and other adult patients.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><p>Drugs for miotics and glaucoma.</p><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Brinzolamide</strong>: Reduces IOP by decreasing aqueous humor secretion in the ciliary processes of the eye.</li><li><strong>Brimonidine Tartrate</strong>: Reduces IOP by decreasing aqueous humor production and increasing uveoscleral outflow.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy</h3><p>Pregnancy category C. The ophthalmic suspension should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether Brinzolamide or Brimonidine Tartrate are excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised while giving this ophthalmic suspension to a nursing mother.</p><h3>Pediatric Uses</h3><p>Safety and effectiveness in children below the age of 2 years have not been established.</p>', 'active', '292_1742406285.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 22:44:45', '2025-05-05 22:37:33'),
(3557, 'Loterex-G', 'Loteprednol Etabonate + Gatifloxacin', 250.00, 1, 'x 5 ml drop', '5 ml drop', 'Drop', '0.5 percent plus 0.3 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the text formatted with the headings in bold for better clarity:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Loterex-G 0.5%+0.3%</strong></p><p>This ophthalmic suspension is indicated for steroid-responsive inflammatory ocular conditions for which a corticosteroid is indicated and where superficial bacterial ocular infection or a risk of bacterial ocular infection exists. Ocular steroids are indicated in inflammatory conditions of the palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva, cornea, and anterior segment of the globe such as allergic conjunctivitis, acne rosacea, superficial punctate keratitis, herpes zoster keratitis, iritis, and cyclitis. It is also indicated for the treatment of post-operative inflammation following ocular surgery.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each ml sterile ophthalmic suspension contains:</p><ul><li>Active substances: Loteprednol Etabonate 5 mg and Gatifloxacin 3 mg.</li><li>Preservative: Benzalkonium Chloride 0.05 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Ophthalmic steroid - antibiotic combined preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This is a sterile ophthalmic suspension containing Loteprednol Etabonate (corticosteroid) and Gatifloxacin (antibiotic) for topical administration to the eyes.</p><ul><li><strong>Loteprednol</strong>: These are thought to act by the induction of phospholipase A2 inhibitory proteins, collectively called lipocortins. It is postulated that these proteins control the biosynthesis of potent mediators of inflammation such as prostaglandins and leukotrienes by inhibiting the release of their common precursor arachidonic acid.</li><li><strong>Gatifloxacin</strong>: The antibacterial action of gatifloxacin results from inhibition of DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV. Gatifloxacin is active against most isolates of both aerobic Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria, including <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, and others.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p>Apply one or two drops of sterile ophthalmic suspension into the conjunctival sac of the affected eye(s) every four to six hours. During the initial 24 to 48 hours, the dosing may be increased to every one to two hours. Frequency should be decreased gradually as warranted by improvement in clinical signs. Care should be taken not to discontinue therapy prematurely.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Loterex-G 0.5%+0.3%</strong></p><p>No drug interaction has been reported yet. If several medicines are to be administered to the eye, there should be an interval of at least 5 minutes between each application.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>This sterile ophthalmic suspension is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Most viral diseases of the cornea and conjunctiva including epithelial herpes simplex.</li><li>Mycobacterial infections of the eye and fungal diseases of ocular structures.</li><li>Known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of this preparation.</li><li>Patients with a history of hypersensitivity to other quinolones, acetylsalicylic acid, and other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory medicines.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Loterex-G 0.5%+0.3%</strong></p><p>Adverse reactions may occur from the steroid/anti-infective combination, which can be attributed to the steroid component, the anti-infective component, or both. Some common side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Steroid-related</strong>: Elevated IOP, optic nerve damage, visual acuity defects, posterior subcapsular cataract formation, delayed wound healing, secondary ocular infections, and perforation of the globe.</li><li><strong>Gatifloxacin-related</strong>: Conjunctival irritation, increased lacrimation, keratitis, papillary conjunctivitis, chemosis, conjunctival hemorrhage, dry eye, eye irritation, and other mild symptoms.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy Category C: No adequate and well-controlled studies have been conducted in pregnant women. It should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li>It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>The preparation should not be injected sub-conjunctively, nor should it be introduced directly into the anterior chamber of the eye.</li><li>In case of prolonged use (10 days or longer), intraocular pressure should be monitored.</li><li>Fungus invasion must be considered in any persistent corneal ulceration where a steroid has been used or is in use.</li><li>If redness or itching becomes aggravated, the patient should be advised to consult a physician.</li><li>Patients who wear soft contact lenses and whose eyes are not red should wait at least ten minutes after instilling the drops before inserting their contact lenses.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at room temperature &amp; protect from light. Do not touch the dropper tip to any surface. The contents should not be used one month after first opening the bottle. Protect from freezing.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Ophthalmic Steroid preparations.</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Loteprednol</strong> (corticosteroid): Acts by the induction of phospholipase A2 inhibitory proteins (lipocortins), inhibiting the release of arachidonic acid, which controls the production of inflammation mediators like prostaglandins and leukotrienes.</li><li><strong>Gatifloxacin</strong> (antibiotic): Inhibits DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV, enzymes involved in bacterial DNA replication and division.</li></ul><p>This format should make it easier to read and understand the information about Loterex-G. Let me know if you\'d like further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '1408_1742406609.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 22:50:09', '2025-05-03 23:34:06'),
(3558, 'Diasulin N', 'Insulin Human [rDNA]', 415.00, 1, 'x 10 ml vial', '10 ml vial', 'SC Injection', '100 IU/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Diasulin N 100 IU/ml</strong></p><p>This is indicated in <strong>Type 1</strong> and <strong>Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus</strong>.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Insulin Human R Injection 40 IU/ml</strong>: Each ml solution contains Insulin Human (rDNA) USP 40 IU (equivalent to 1.388 mg) as Soluble Insulin Human (Regular).</li><li><strong>Insulin Human R Injection 100 IU/ml</strong>: Each ml solution contains Insulin Human (rDNA) USP 100 IU (equivalent to 3.47 mg) as Soluble Insulin Human (Regular).</li><li><strong>Insulin Human N Injection 40 IU/ml</strong>: Each ml suspension contains Insulin Human (rDNA) USP 40 IU (equivalent to 1.388 mg) as Isophane Insulin Human.</li><li><strong>Insulin Human N Injection 100 IU/ml</strong>: Each ml suspension contains Insulin Human (rDNA) USP 100 IU (equivalent to 3.47 mg) as Isophane Insulin Human.</li><li><strong>Insulin Human 30/70 Injection 40 IU/ml</strong>: Each ml suspension contains Insulin Human (rDNA) USP 40 IU (equivalent to 1.388 mg) as 30% Soluble Insulin Human (Regular) and 70% Isophane Insulin Human.</li><li><strong>Insulin Human 30/70 Injection 100 IU/ml</strong>: Each ml suspension contains Insulin Human (rDNA) USP 100 IU (equivalent to 3.47 mg) as 30% Soluble Insulin Human (Regular) and 70% Isophane Insulin Human.</li><li><strong>Insulin Human 50/50 Injection 100 IU/ml</strong>: Each ml suspension contains Insulin Human (rDNA) USP 100 IU (equivalent to 3.47 mg) as 50% Soluble Insulin Human (Regular) and 50% Isophane Insulin Human.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Diasulin N 100 IU/ml</strong></p><p>The dosage form, dosage, and administration time of the insulin vary depending on individual patient needs. It is influenced by factors such as food intake, exercise, and working patterns. The insulin should be used under the guidance of a healthcare provider.</p><ul><li><strong>Type 1 Diabetes</strong>: The average daily insulin requirement for maintenance therapy ranges from 0.5 to 1.0 IU/kg. In pre-pubertal children, it usually varies from 0.7 to 1.0 IU/kg. Insulin-resistant cases (e.g., puberty or obesity) may require higher doses.</li><li><strong>Type 2 Diabetes</strong>: The initial insulin dosage is usually lower, ranging from 0.3 to 0.6 IU/kg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Diasulin N 100 IU/ml</strong></p><p>Insulin should be injected <strong>subcutaneously 15 minutes to one hour before meals</strong>. The exact timing is determined by a doctor based on the individual’s needs.</p><p><strong>Steps for Preparing and Administering Insulin</strong>:</p><ol><li><strong>Prepare</strong>: Wash your hands. Shake or rotate the vial gently to mix the solution. Ensure the insulin has a normal appearance.</li><li><strong>Drawing the Insulin</strong>: If using a new vial, remove the plastic cap and wipe the rubber stopper with an alcohol swab. Draw air into the syringe equal to the amount of insulin needed, puncture the vial, and inject air into it. Invert the vial and syringe, then withdraw the correct dose of insulin.</li><li><strong>Check for Bubbles</strong>: Ensure there are no air bubbles in the syringe. Tap the syringe to release any air bubbles.</li><li><strong>Injection Sites</strong>: Choose areas with less tight skin such as the upper arm, thigh, buttock, and abdomen. Avoid injecting in the same spot repeatedly to prevent tissue damage.</li><li><strong>Injection Method</strong>: Clean the skin with alcohol. Inject the insulin at a <strong>45° angle</strong> and apply gentle pressure to the site after withdrawal. Do not rub the injection site.</li></ol><p><strong>Interaction of Diasulin N 100 IU/ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Drugs that may increase blood glucose</strong> (e.g., oral contraceptives, adrenal corticosteroids, thyroid hormones) may require an increase in insulin dosage.</li><li><strong>Drugs that may decrease blood glucose</strong> (e.g., hypoglycemic agents, salicylates, sulfonamides, antidepressants) may require a reduction in insulin dosage.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong></li><li><strong>Allergy</strong> to insulin or any of the excipients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Diasulin N 100 IU/ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong> is the most common side effect, which may occur suddenly.</li><li><strong>Allergic reactions</strong> (e.g., redness, swelling, itching) may occur but typically subside after a few days. These reactions may also be due to other factors such as disinfectants or improper injection technique.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Inadequate dosing or discontinuation, especially in <strong>Type 1 Diabetes</strong>, can lead to <strong>hyperglycemia</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong> may result if the insulin dose is too high or if meals are skipped or strenuous exercise is undertaken without adjustment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Diasulin N 100 IU/ml</strong></p><p>Excessive insulin use can cause <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>. Mild to moderate symptoms may occur suddenly, requiring immediate treatment. Consult a doctor if frequent hypoglycemia occurs to adjust therapy, diet, or exercise.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store <strong>refrigerated at 2°C–8°C</strong>.</li><li>Do <strong>not freeze</strong>.</li><li>Insulin in use can be kept at room temperature for up to <strong>one month</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Medium Acting Insulin</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Diasulin N is human insulin made using recombinant DNA technology. It mimics the action of natural insulin, regulating glucose metabolism and stimulating glucose uptake by tissues such as liver, muscles, and fat.</p><ul><li><strong>Soluble Insulin (Regular)</strong>: Begins working within <strong>30 minutes</strong>, peaks in <strong>1-3 hours</strong>, and lasts about <strong>4-8 hours</strong>.</li><li><strong>Isophane Insulin (NPH)</strong>: Begins working slowly, peaks in <strong>6-9 hours</strong>, and lasts about <strong>24 hours</strong>.</li><li><strong>30/70 &amp; 50/50 Insulin</strong>: Begins working within <strong>30 minutes</strong>, peaks in <strong>2-8 hours</strong>, and lasts about <strong>24 hours</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Insulin therapy during pregnancy is safe as insulin does not cross the placental barrier. It is also safe for use by nursing mothers.</p>', 'in-active', '1056_1742406804.webp', 44, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 22:53:24', '2025-05-08 14:34:43'),
(3559, 'Ranitid', 'Ranitidine Hydrochloride', 3.00, 10, 't', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '150 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ranitid 150 mg</strong></p><p> Ranitid 150 mg is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Treatment of active duodenal ulcer</li><li>Benign gastric ulcer</li><li>Treatment &amp; prevention of ulcer associated with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory agent</li><li>Post-operative stress ulcer</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</li><li>Gastro-intestinal haemorrhage from stress ulcer in seriously ill patients</li><li>Recurrent haemorrhage in patients with bleeding peptic ulcer</li><li>Before general anesthesia in patients considered to be at risk of acid aspiration, particularly obstetric patients</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> H2 receptor antagonist</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Ranitid 150 mg competitively blocks histamine at H2-receptors of the gastric parietal cells, inhibiting gastric acid secretion. It does not affect pepsin secretion, pentagastrin-stimulated intrinsic factor secretion, or serum gastrin.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ranitid 150 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Ranitid 150 mg Tablet &amp; Syrup:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal and gastric ulcer:</strong> 150 mg twice daily (morning and evening) or 300 mg as a single daily dose at night for 4 to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Reflux oesophagitis:</strong> 150 mg twice daily or 300 mg at bedtime for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</strong> 150 mg three times daily, increased if necessary up to 6 g daily in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Episodic dyspepsia:</strong> 150 mg twice daily or 300 mg at bedtime for up to 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> 150 mg at night to prevent recurrences.</li><li><strong>Children (peptic ulcer):</strong> 2-4 mg/kg twice daily, maximum 300 mg daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Ranitid 150 mg IV injection &amp; IV Infusion:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Slow IV injection:</strong> 50 mg over at least two minutes, after dilution to a volume of 20 ml per 50 mg dose, repeated every 6 to 8 hours.</li><li><strong>Intermittent IV infusion:</strong> 25 mg per hour for two hours, repeated at 6 to 8-hour intervals.</li><li><strong>Intramuscular injection:</strong> 50 mg (2 ml) every 6 to 8 hours.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of haemorrhage from stress ulceration in seriously ill patients:</strong> Initial dose of 50 mg as a slow IV injection, followed by continuous IV infusion of 0.125-0.250 mg/kg/hour.</li><li><strong>Aspiration syndrome prevention:</strong> 50 mg IV/IM injection 45 to 60 minutes before induction of general anesthesia.</li></ul><p><strong>Children:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Peptic ulcer treatment:</strong> 2-4 mg/kg twice daily, maximum 300 mg/day.</li><li>Safety and effectiveness of IV administration in children have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ranitid 150 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Delayed absorption and increased peak serum concentration with propantheline bromide.</li><li>Minimally inhibits hepatic metabolism of coumarin anticoagulants, theophylline, diazepam, and propranolol.</li><li>May alter absorption of pH-dependent drugs (e.g., ketoconazole, midazolam, glipizide).</li><li>May reduce bioavailability with antacids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Ranitid 150 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ranitid 150 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Generally well tolerated; side effects are uncommon.</li><li>Rare cases of altered bowel habits, dizziness, rash, tiredness, reversible confusion, headache, decreased blood counts, muscle or joint pain.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Ranitid 150 mg crosses the placenta. No evidence of impaired fertility or fetal harm, but should only be used if essential.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Excreted in breast milk; caution advised when administered to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Reduced dosage required for patients with impaired renal and hepatic function.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ranitid 150 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No major issues expected due to its specific action.</li><li>Supportive therapy should be provided if needed.</li><li>Hemodialysis can remove the drug from plasma if required.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly patients:</strong> No significant difference in ulcer healing rates or adverse effects compared to younger patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Slow IV injection:</strong> 50 mg diluted to ≤2.5 mg/mL (total of 20 mL) with NaCl 0.9%, dextrose 5% or 10%, lactated Ringer’s, or Na bicarbonate 5%.</li><li><strong>Intermittent slow IV infusion:</strong> 50 mg diluted to ≤0.5 mg/mL (total of 100 mL) with the same solutions.</li><li><strong>Continuous IV infusion:</strong> 150 mg diluted in 250 mL of the same solutions.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome or hypersecretory conditions:</strong> Dilute to ≤2.5 mg/mL with dextrose 5% or NaCl 0.9%, lactated Ringer’s, or Na bicarbonate 5%.</li></ul>', 'active', '1435_1742407029.jpg', 7, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 22:57:09', '2025-03-19 22:57:09'),
(3560, 'Relupros', 'Relugolix', 250.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '120 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Relupros 120 mg</h3><p>Relupros 120 mg tablet is a gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) receptor antagonist indicated for the treatment of adult patients with advanced prostate cancer.</p><h3>Dosage of Relupros 120 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Loading Dose</strong>: 360 mg on the first day of treatment.</li><li><strong>Maintenance Dose</strong>: 120 mg taken once daily, at approximately the same time each day.</li><li>Relupros 120 mg can be taken with or without food.</li><li>Advise patients to take a missed dose of Relupros 120 mg as soon as they remember. If the dose was missed by more than 12 hours, patients should not take the missed dose and resume with the next scheduled dose.</li><li>If treatment is interrupted for greater than 7 days, restart Relupros 120 mg with a loading dose of 360 mg on the first day, and continue with a dose of 120 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: The safety and efficacy of Relupros 120 mg in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No overall differences in safety or effectiveness were observed between older and younger subjects.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Relupros 120 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>P-gp Inhibitors</strong>: Avoid co-administration. If unavoidable, take Relupros 120 mg first, separate dosing by at least 6 hours, and monitor patients more frequently for adverse reactions.</li><li><strong>Combined P-gp and Strong CYP3A Inducers</strong>: Avoid co-administration. If unavoidable, increase the Relupros 120 mg dose to 240 mg once daily.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Relupros 120 mg</h3><h4>Adverse Reactions:</h4><ul><li><strong>Most Common (≥10%)</strong>: Hot flushes, glucose increased, triglycerides increased, musculoskeletal pain, hemoglobin decreased, alanine aminotransferase (ALT) increased, fatigue, aspartate aminotransferase (AST) increased, constipation, diarrhea.</li></ul><h4>Common Side Effects:</h4><ul><li>Hot flushes</li><li>Increased blood sugar levels</li><li>Increased blood fat (triglyceride) levels</li><li>Muscle and joint pain</li><li>Decreased hemoglobin levels</li><li>Increased liver enzymes</li><li>Tiredness</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Diarrhea</li></ul><h4>Rare Side Effects:</h4><ul><li>Changes in the electrical activity of the heart (QT prolongation)</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Fainting</li><li>Palpitations</li><li>Chest pain</li><li>Weight gain</li><li>Decreased sex drive</li><li>Erectile function problems</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>QT/QTc Interval Prolongation</strong>: Androgen deprivation therapy may prolong the QT interval.</li><li><strong>Embryo-Fetal Toxicity</strong>: Relupros 120 mg can cause fetal harm. Advise males with female partners of reproductive potential to use effective contraception.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Relupros 120 mg</h3><p>There is no specific experience in the management of Relupros 120 mg overdose in patients.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><p>Store in a cool and dry place, protect from light and moisture. Keep all medicines out of the reach of children.</p><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li>Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) antagonist.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Relupros 120 mg is a non-peptide small molecule, GnRH receptor antagonist. It competitively binds to pituitary GnRH receptors, reducing the release of luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), thereby decreasing testosterone levels.</p><h3>Pharmacokinetics</h3><ul><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: Relupros 120 mg is a substrate for intestinal P-gp. The mean (CV%) absolute bioavailability is approximately 12%. The median (range) Tmax is 2.25 hours.</li><li><strong>Effect of Food</strong>: No clinically meaningful differences in pharmacokinetics were observed after consuming a high-calorie, high-fat meal.</li><li><strong>Distribution</strong>: Plasma protein binding is 68-71%, primarily to albumin and α1-acid glycoprotein. The mean blood-to-plasma ratio is 0.78.</li><li><strong>Elimination</strong>: The mean effective half-life is 25 hours. The terminal elimination half-life is 60.8 hours (11% CV).</li><li><strong>Metabolism</strong>: Primarily metabolized by CYP3A and, to a lesser extent, by CYP2C8.</li><li><strong>Excretion</strong>: Approximately 81% of the radioactivity is recovered in feces, and 4.1% in urine (with 2.2% unchanged).</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy</h3><p>The safety and efficacy of Relupros 120 mg at the recommended dose have not been established in females. Relupros 120 mg may impair fertility in males of reproductive potential based on animal studies and its mechanism of action. Males with female partners of reproductive potential should use effective contraception.</p>', 'active', '273_1742407092.png', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 22:58:12', '2025-03-19 22:58:12'),
(3561, 'Sutinib', 'Sunitinib', 950.00, 7, 'Capsule', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sutinib 50 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Gastrointestinal Stromal Tumor (GIST):</strong> Sutinib 50 mg is indicated for the treatment of gastrointestinal stromal tumor after disease progression on or intolerance to imatinib mesylate.</p><p> <strong>Advanced Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC):</strong> Sutinib 50 mg is indicated for the treatment of advanced renal cell carcinoma.</p><p> <strong>Advanced Pancreatic Neuroendocrine Tumors (pNET):</strong> Sutinib 50 mg is indicated for the treatment of progressive, well-differentiated pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors in patients with unresectable locally advanced or metastatic disease.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Targeted Cancer Therapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sutinib 50 mg inhibits cellular signaling by targeting multiple receptor tyrosine kinases (RTKs). These include all receptors for platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF-Rs) and vascular endothelial growth factor receptors (VEGFRs), which play a role in both tumor angiogenesis and tumor cell proliferation. The simultaneous inhibition of these targets reduces tumor vascularization and triggers cancer cell apoptosis, resulting in tumor shrinkage.</p><p> Sutinib 50 mg also inhibits CD117 (c-KIT), the receptor tyrosine kinase that, when improperly activated by mutation, drives most gastrointestinal stromal cell tumors. It is recommended as a second-line therapy for patients whose tumors develop mutations in c-KIT that make them resistant to imatinib or who cannot tolerate the drug.</p><p> Additionally, Sutinib 50 mg binds other receptors such as RET, CD114, and CD135. The broad targeting of different receptors contributes to its side effects, including hand-foot syndrome, stomatitis, and other dermatologic toxicities.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sutinib 50 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Recommended Dose for GIST and RCC:</strong></p><ul><li>The recommended dose is 50 mg orally once daily, on a schedule of 4 weeks on treatment followed by 2 weeks off (Schedule 4/2).</li><li>May be taken with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Recommended Dose for pNET:</strong></p><ul><li>The recommended dose is 37.5 mg orally once daily continuously without a scheduled off-treatment period.</li><li>May be taken with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Dose Modification:</strong></p><ul><li>Dose interruption and/or modification in 12.5 mg increments or decrements is recommended based on individual safety and tolerability.</li><li>The maximum dose in the Phase 3 pNET study was 50 mg daily.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 Inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole):</strong> May increase Sutinib 50 mg plasma concentrations. Consider alternative medications or reduce Sutinib 50 mg dose to 37.5 mg (GIST and RCC) or 25 mg (pNET) daily.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 Inducers (e.g., rifampin):</strong> May decrease plasma concentrations. Consider alternative medications or increase Sutinib 50 mg dose to a maximum of 87.5 mg (GIST and RCC) or 62.5 mg (pNET) daily, monitoring for toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sutinib 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Increased plasma concentration with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole, ritonavir, itraconazole, erythromycin, clarithromycin, grapefruit juice).</li><li>Decreased plasma concentration with strong CYP3A4 inducers (e.g., rifampin, dexamethasone, phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, St. John\'s wort).</li><li>Anticoagulants (e.g., warfarin, acenocoumarol) may require periodic monitoring of platelets, prothrombin time/INR, and physical examination.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Sutinib 50 mg.</li><li>Renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Sutinib 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Fatigue, gastrointestinal disorders, skin discoloration, rash, palmar-plantar erythrodysesthesia, dry skin, hair color changes, mucosal inflammation, asthenia, dysgeusia, anorexia, hypertension, neutropenia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category D:</strong> There is positive evidence of human fetal risk based on adverse reaction data from investigational or marketing experience or studies in humans. However, potential benefits may warrant use despite potential risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sutinib 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment consists of general supportive measures.</li><li>No specific antidote exists.</li><li>Overdose cases were associated with known adverse reactions or no adverse reactions.</li><li>A case of intentional overdose (1,500 mg) was reported without adverse reaction.</li><li>In non-clinical studies, mortality was observed following 5 daily doses of 500 mg/kg (3,000 mg/m²) in rats, with signs of toxicity such as impaired muscle coordination, head shakes, hypoactivity, ocular discharge, piloerection, and gastrointestinal distress.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Targeted Cancer Therapy</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Sutinib 50 mg inhibits cellular signaling by targeting multiple RTKs, including PDGF-Rs and VEGFRs, which are involved in tumor angiogenesis and proliferation. It also inhibits CD117 (c-KIT) and other receptors such as RET, CD114, and CD135, contributing to its therapeutic effects and side effects.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category D:</strong> There is positive evidence of fetal risk, but the drug may be used if benefits outweigh risks.</li></ul>', 'active', '1463_1742407309.jpg', 9, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 23:01:49', '2025-03-19 23:01:49'),
(3562, 'Palopag', 'Selexipag', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Palopag 200 mg</h3><p>Palopag 200 mg is indicated for the treatment of <strong>pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH, WHO Group I)</strong> to <strong>delay disease progression</strong> and reduce the risk of hospitalization for PAH.</p><h3>Dosage of Palopag 200 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Starting Dose</strong>: 200 mcg twice daily.</li><li>Tolerability may be improved when taken with food.</li><li><strong>Dose Adjustment</strong>: Increase the dose by 200 mcg twice daily at weekly intervals to the highest tolerated dose, up to <strong>1600 mcg twice daily</strong>.</li><li>The maintenance dose is determined by tolerability.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p> <strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No overall differences have been observed.</p><p> <strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>:</p><ul><li>For moderate hepatic impairment patients, start with 200 mcg once daily, increasing the dose by 200 mcg once daily at weekly intervals up to 1600 mcg.</li><li><strong>Avoid</strong> use of Palopag 200 mg in patients with severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh class C).</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Palopag 200 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Moderate CYP2C8 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., deferasirox and teriflunomide) may increase exposure to the active metabolite of Palopag 200 mg.</li><li><strong>CYP2C8 inducers</strong> (e.g., rifampicin) decrease exposure to the active metabolite and may increase concentration up to twice the dose of Palopag 200 mg.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Palopag 200 mg</h3><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Nausea</strong></li><li><strong>Diarrhea</strong></li><li><strong>Vomiting</strong></li><li><strong>Headache</strong></li><li><strong>Jaw pain</strong></li><li><strong>Muscle pain</strong></li><li><strong>Low appetite</strong></li><li><strong>Low RBC count</strong></li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Discontinue Palopag 200 mg if the patient develops signs and symptoms of <strong>Pulmonary Veno-Occlusive Disease (PVOD)</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Palopag 200 mg</h3><ul><li>Isolated cases of overdose up to <strong>3200 mcg</strong> have been reported. <strong>Mild transient nausea</strong> was the only reported consequence. In the event of overdose, <strong>supportive measures</strong> must be taken as required.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Keep in a <strong>dry place</strong>, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of <strong>reach of children</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li><strong>Non-prostanoid IP prostacyclin receptor agonist</strong></li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>Palopag 200 mg is a selective non-prostanoid IP prostacyclin receptor agonist.</li><li>It is a <strong>pale yellow crystalline powder</strong> that is practically insoluble in water.</li><li>Palopag 200 mg is <strong>not hygroscopic</strong> and <strong>not light sensitive</strong> in its solid-state.</li><li>The drug is hydrolyzed by <strong>carboxylesterase 1</strong> to yield its active metabolite, which is approximately <strong>37-fold more potent</strong> than Palopag 200 mg.</li><li>Palopag 200 mg and its active metabolite are selective for the <strong>IP receptor</strong> compared to other prostanoid receptors (EP1-4, DP, FP, and TP).</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li>There are no <strong>adequate and well-controlled studies</strong> with Palopag 200 mg in pregnant women.</li><li>It is <strong>not known</strong> if Palopag 200 mg is present in human milk.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong> should be discontinued during Palopag 200 mg therapy.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1563_1742407363.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 23:02:43', '2025-03-19 23:02:43'),
(3563, 'Timothy', 'Tiemonium Methylsulfate', 25.00, 1, 'IV injection', '5 IV injection', 'IV Injection', '5 mg per 2 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Timothy 5 mg/2 ml</strong> Timothy 5 mg/2 ml is an antispasmodic drug that reduces muscle spasms of the intestine, biliary system, bladder, and uterus. It is used in the symptomatic treatment of pain related to functional disorders of the digestive tract and biliary system. It is also indicated for the treatment of spasm and pain in urological and gynecological diseases.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong> Anticholinergics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong> Timothy 5 mg/2 ml is a competitive antagonist of Acetylcholine and Histamine and strengthens the calcium bond with membrane phospholipids and proteins. Thus, it inhibits intracellular contractile protein of visceral cells, which causes inhibition of visceral spasm and pain.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Timothy 5 mg/2 ml</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet/SyrupAdults:</strong> Usual dose is 2-6 tablets or 3-9 teaspoonfuls of syrup daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong> 3 ml/kg or 6 mg/kg body weight daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection</strong>1 Timothy 5 mg/2 ml injection 3 times daily, through intravenous route slowly or intramuscular route.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppository</strong>20 mg Timothy 5 mg/2 ml suppository two or three times daily, through rectal route.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Timothy 5 mg/2 ml</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet/SyrupAdults:</strong> Usual dose is 2-6 tablets or 3-9 teaspoonfuls of syrup daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong> 3 ml/kg or 6 mg/kg body weight daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection</strong>1 Timothy 5 mg/2 ml injection 3 times daily, through intravenous route slowly or intramuscular route.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppository</strong>20 mg Timothy 5 mg/2 ml suppository two or three times daily, through rectal route.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Timothy 5 mg/2 ml</strong> Tiemonium methylsulphate tablets should not be used with other drugs without prior consultation with a registered physician to avoid possible drug interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong> It should not be used in urethroprostatic disorders involving a risk of urine retention. It is contraindicated in patients with a risk of angle-closure glaucoma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Timothy 5 mg/2 ml</strong> Timothy 5 mg/2 ml may have the risk of hypotension and tachycardia in certain individuals.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong> The results of animal studies of Timothy 5 mg/2 ml did not reveal any teratogenic effects; no deformities have been reported up to now with normal use. In the absence of sufficient data, prudence should be the rule for nursing mothers, although no problems have been reported with normal use.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong> Caution should be taken during the treatment of patients with disorders of the prostate. Caution should also be taken in cases of chronic bronchitis, coronary insufficiency, ambient hyperthermia, and renal &amp; hepatic insufficiency. The risks of visual disturbances can make it dangerous to drive or use machines.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Timothy 5 mg/2 ml</strong> There is no available data regarding the overdose of Tiemonium methylsulphate tablets.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong> Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness of Tiemonium methylsulphate in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Efficacy and safety were maintained with increasing age.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong> Anticholinergics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong> Timothy 5 mg/2 ml is a competitive antagonist of Acetylcholine and Histamine and strengthens the calcium bond with membrane phospholipids and proteins. Thus, it inhibits intracellular contractile protein of visceral cells, which causes inhibition of visceral spasm and pain.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong> The results of animal studies of Timothy 5 mg/2 ml did not reveal any teratogenic effects; no deformities have been reported up to now with normal use. In the absence of sufficient data, prudence should be the rule for nursing mothers, although no problems have been reported with normal use.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness of Tiemonium methylsulphate in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Efficacy and safety were maintained with increasing age.</li></ul>', 'active', '357_1742408023.jpg', 53, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 23:13:43', '2025-03-19 23:13:43'),
(3564, 'Tynex Abdominal Super', 'Caesarean Belt', 180.00, 1, 'x 1 binder', '1 binder', 'Surgical', '0 mg/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1643_1742408349.png', 74, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 23:19:09', '2025-05-04 22:39:19');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3565, 'XeroSoft Junior', 'White Soft Paraffin + Liquid Paraffin', 180.00, 1, 'x 50 gm tube', '50 gm tube', 'Cream', '15%+6%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of XeroSoft Junior 15%+6% 50 gm</strong></p><p>For general use as an emollient in the symptomatic relief of dry skin conditions.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Emollients &amp; combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>White Soft Paraffin and Liquid Paraffin are emollients with occlusive properties for topical application. There is no significant systemic absorption of the active ingredients but it has substantial penetration into the stratum corneum.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of XeroSoft Junior 15%+6% 50 gm</strong></p><p>Apply a thin film of the ointment to the affected area of the skin, in the direction of hair growth, as required. Repeat as necessary. Ideally, the product should be applied three or four times a day or at least twice a day. In adults, where a large area of the body is affected, up to 500 g a week may be used. For topical administration only.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of XeroSoft Junior 15%+6% 50 gm</strong></p><p>Prolonged use may cause folliculitis; should this occur, the use of the product should be discontinued.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of White Soft Paraffin and Liquid Paraffin during pregnancy and lactation has not been established, but use during these periods is not considered to constitute a hazard.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The ointment should be applied in the direction of hair growth to reduce the incidence of folliculitis.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of XeroSoft Junior 15%+6% 50 gm</strong></p><p>Emosoft ointment has low toxicity, and if ingested, treatment should be supportive and symptomatic. Gastric lavage should not be attempted following ingestion of paraffin-based products, due to the risk of inhalation or aspiration into the lungs.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Do not store above 30°C. Keep away from light and out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '472_1742408508.webp', 8, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 23:21:48', '2025-05-07 20:03:13'),
(3566, 'Mycofin 1%', 'Terbinafine Hydrochloride', 50.00, 1, 'Cream', '5 gm tube', 'Cream', '5 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Mycofin 1% 5 gm</strong></p><p> Terbinafine tablet: This tablet is indicated for the treatment of onychomycosis of the toenail or fingernail due to dermatophytes (tinea unguium).</p><p> Terbinafine granules: This is indicated in Tinea Capitis.</p><p> Terbinafine cream: Fungal infection of the skin caused by Trichophyton (e.g. <em>T. rubrum, T. mentagrophytes, T. verrucosum, T. violaceum</em>), <em>Microsporum canis</em> and <em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em>. Yeast infections of the skin, principally those caused by the genus <em>Candida</em> (e.g. <em>C. albicans</em>). Pityriasis (tinea) versicolor due to <em>Pityrosporum orbicular</em> (also known as <em>Malassezia furfur</em>).</p><p> Terbinafine 1% Spray: This spray is indicated in the treatment of tinea infections of the skin. This spray is also indicated in the treatment of pityriasis (tinea) versicolor due to <em>Malassezia furfur</em>.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses, Topical Antifungal preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Terbinafine is an allylamine with a range of antifungal activity. It is fungicidal against dermatophytes, moulds, and certain dimorphic fungi. Terbinafine is either fungicidal or fungistatic against yeasts, depending on the species. Terbinafine interferes with fungal ergosterol biosynthesis by inhibiting squalene epoxidase in the fungal cell membrane at an early stage. This leads to a deficiency in ergosterol and intracellular accumulation of squalene, resulting in fungal cell death. Terbinafine is highly effective in fungal infections of the skin, hair, and nails caused by <em>Trichophyton</em> spp., <em>Microsporum</em> spp., and <em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em>. It is also effective against yeast infections of the skin, principally those caused by the genus <em>Candida</em>. Topical terbinafine appears to be effective in pityriasis versicolor due to <em>Pityrosporum arbiculare</em>.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Mycofin 1% 5 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Topical application:</strong> Terbinafine cream should be applied to affected areas once or twice daily for 1-2 weeks. Some infections may require oral terbinafine tablet therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea corporis and Tinea cruris:</strong> 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea pedis:</strong> 2-4 weeks (one week of treatment will normally suffice if applied twice daily)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cutaneous candidiasis:</strong> 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pityriasis (tinea) versicolor:</strong> 2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">To prevent relapses, treatment should continue for an adequate duration.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clean and dry affected areas thoroughly before applying the cream in a thin layer. In intertriginous infections, covering with gauze may help.</li><li><strong>Oral administration:</strong> Terbinafine tablet is necessary for hair or nail infections.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Fingernail onychomycosis:</strong> Terbinafine 250 mg once daily for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Toenail onychomycosis:</strong> Terbinafine 250 mg once daily for 12 weeks</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Mycofin 1% 5 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine tablet:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Fingernail onychomycosis:</strong> 250 mg once daily for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Toenail onychomycosis:</strong> 250 mg once daily for 12 weeks</li><li><strong>Terbinafine granules:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Body weight &lt;25 kg: 125 mg/day up to 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Body weight 25-35 kg: 187.5 mg/day up to 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Body weight &gt;35 kg: 250 mg/day up to 6 weeks</li><li><strong>Terbinafine cream:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Applied once or twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea corporis, cruris:</strong> 1-2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea pedis:</strong> 1 week</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cutaneous candidiasis:</strong> 2 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pityriasis versicolor:</strong> 2 weeks</li><li><strong>Terbinafine 1% Spray:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea pedis:</strong> Once daily, 1 week</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea corporis/cruris:</strong> Once daily, 1 week</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pityriasis versicolor:</strong> Twice daily, 1 week</li></ul><p>If no improvement is seen after two weeks, the diagnosis should be reassessed.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Mycofin 1% 5 gm</strong></p><p> Terbinafine inhibits the CYP450 2D6 isozyme, affecting drugs metabolized by it, including tricyclic antidepressants, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, beta-blockers, antiarrhythmics (e.g., flecainide, propafenone), and MAO inhibitors Type B. Co-administration should be monitored carefully, and dose adjustment may be needed.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Terbinafine tablet and cream are contraindicated in individuals hypersensitive to terbinafine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Mycofin 1% 5 gm</strong></p><p> Reported side effects include gastrointestinal symptoms (diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain), liver test abnormalities, rashes, urticaria, pruritus, and taste disturbances. Rare but severe effects include liver failure, skin reactions (e.g., Stevens-Johnson Syndrome), severe neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, angioedema, and allergic reactions (including anaphylaxis).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine tablet:</strong> Should not be used during pregnancy as studies are inadequate. It is present in breast milk; therefore, not recommended for nursing mothers.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine cream:</strong> Animal studies suggest no adverse effects, but human data are lacking. Should only be used if benefits outweigh risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Terbinafine tablets:</strong> Rare cases of liver failure (some fatal) have been reported, primarily in patients with underlying conditions. Treatment should be discontinued if liver injury occurs. Serious skin reactions (e.g., Stevens-Johnson Syndrome) may also occur.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine cream:</strong> For external use only; avoid eye contact.</li><li><strong>Precautions:</strong> Not recommended for patients with chronic or active liver disease. Liver function tests (ALT, AST) should be performed before starting oral terbinafine.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Mycofin 1% 5 gm</strong></p><p> Limited data on terbinafine overdose. Doses up to 5 gm (20 times the therapeutic dose) have been reported without serious effects. Symptoms include nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dizziness, rash, frequent urination, and headache.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, below 30°C, and protect from light.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children:</strong> Terbinafine cream appears effective and well-tolerated in tinea corporis and tinea cruris in children.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Generally safe, but dose reduction is needed in hepatic or renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Other Antifungal preparations, Topical Antifungal preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Terbinafine, an allylamine antifungal, inhibits ergosterol biosynthesis via inhibition of the squalene epoxidase enzyme. This results in fungal cell death due to increased membrane permeability mediated by squalene accumulation. Terbinafine is active against <em>Trichophyton mentagrophyte</em> and <em>Trichophyton rubrum</em>.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use:</strong> Safety and efficacy not well established in children.</li><li><strong>Elderly use:</strong> No evidence of altered effects in elderly patients, though dose reduction is advised in hepatic or renal impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '759_1742408516.jpg', 53, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 23:21:56', '2025-03-19 23:21:56'),
(3567, 'Hepatolin', 'Rohitakarista', 240.00, 1, 'x 450 ml bottle', '450 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '450 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1681_1742408662.webp', 5, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 23:24:22', '2025-05-03 18:55:12'),
(3568, 'Pulmosis', 'Pirfenidone', 45.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '267 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pulmosis 267 mg</strong></p><p> Pulmosis 267 mg is indicated in adults for the treatment of mild to moderate Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (IPF).</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Immunosuppressant</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Pulmosis 267 mg is an orally active, small molecule that shows a wide range of biologic activity. In vitro evidence has shown that Pulmosis 267 mg inhibits collagen synthesis, down-regulates profibrotic cytokines, and decreases fibroblast proliferation. Pulmosis 267 mg leads to a reduction of TGF-beta2 mRNA levels and of the mature TGF-beta2 protein due to decreased expression and direct inhibition of the TGF-beta pro-protein convertase furin. In addition, Pulmosis 267 mg reduces the protein levels of the matrix metalloproteinase (MMP)-11, a TGF-beta target gene and furin substrate involved in carcinogenesis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Pulmosis 267 mg</strong></p><p> Treatment with Pulmosis 267 mg should be initiated and supervised by specialist physicians experienced in the diagnosis and treatment of IPF. Upon initiating treatment, the dose should be titrated to the recommended daily dose of nine 267 mg tablets or one 801 mg tablet per day over a 14-day period for adults as follows:</p><ul><li><strong>Days 1 to 7:</strong> One 267 mg tablet administered three times a day (801 mg/day)</li><li><strong>Days 8 to 14:</strong> One 534 mg tablet administered three times a day (1602 mg/day)</li><li><strong>Day 15 onward:</strong> Three 267 mg tablets or one 801 mg tablet administered three times a day (2403 mg/day)</li></ul><p>The recommended daily dose of Pulmosis 267 mg for patients with IPF is three 267 mg tablets or one 801 mg three times a day with food for a total of 2403 mg/day. Doses above 2403 mg/day are not recommended for any patient. Patients who miss 14 consecutive days or more of Pulmosis 267 mg treatment should re-initiate therapy by undergoing the initial 2-week titration regimen up to the recommended daily dose. For treatment interruption of less than 14 consecutive days, the dose can be resumed at the previous recommended daily dose without titration.</p><p><strong>Dose adjustments and other considerations for safe use:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal events:</strong> Patients should be reminded to take the medication with food. If symptoms persist, Pulmosis 267 mg may be reduced to 1-2 capsules (267 mg-534 mg) 2-3 times/day with food, with re-escalation to the recommended daily dose as tolerated. If symptoms continue, patients may be instructed to interrupt treatment for 1 to 2 weeks to allow symptoms to resolve.</li><li><strong>Photosensitivity reaction or rash:</strong> Patients should use a sunblock daily and avoid sun exposure. The dose may be reduced to 3 capsules/day (1 capsule three times a day). If the rash persists after 7 days, Pulmosis 267 mg should be discontinued for 15 days, with re-escalation in the same manner as the dose escalation period. Patients with severe photosensitivity reaction or rash should interrupt the dose and seek medical advice.</li><li><strong>Hepatic function:</strong> In case of significant elevation of ALT/AST with or without bilirubin elevation, the dose should be adjusted or treatment discontinued.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Pulmosis 267 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With medicine:</strong> Moderate (e.g., ciprofloxacin) and strong inhibitors of CYP1A2 (e.g., fluvoxamine) increase the systemic exposure of Pulmosis 267 mg and may alter the adverse reaction profile. Discontinue fluvoxamine prior to administration of Pulmosis 267 mg or reduce to 267 mg three times a day. Consider dosage reduction with ciprofloxacin.</li><li><strong>With food and others:</strong> None. A reduced incidence of adverse reactions was observed in the fed group compared to the fasted group.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to the active substance or excipients, concomitant use of fluvoxamine, severe hepatic impairment or end-stage liver disease, severe renal impairment (CrCl &lt;30 ml/min), or end-stage renal disease requiring dialysis.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Pulmosis 267 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Nausea, rash, abdominal pain, upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fatigue, headache, dyspepsia, dizziness, vomiting, anorexia, gastroesophageal reflux disease, sinusitis, insomnia, weight decrease, and arthralgia.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Photosensitivity reaction, decreased appetite, pruritus, asthenia, dysgeusia, and non-cardiac chest pain.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> No data available for pregnant women. As a precaution, Pulmosis 267 mg should be avoided during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is unknown if Pulmosis 267 mg is excreted in human milk. A decision should be made whether to discontinue breastfeeding or to discontinue Pulmosis 267 mg therapy.</li><li><strong>Fertility:</strong> No adverse effects on fertility were observed in preclinical studies.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elevated liver enzymes:</strong> Monitor ALT, AST, and bilirubin before and during treatment. Temporary dosage reductions or discontinuations may be required.</li><li><strong>Photosensitivity and rash:</strong> Avoid exposure to sunlight and sunlamps. Wear sunscreen and protective clothing daily. Temporary dosage reductions or discontinuations may be required.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal disorders:</strong> Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, dyspepsia, gastroesophageal reflux disease, and abdominal pain have been reported. Temporary dosage reductions or discontinuations may be required.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Pulmosis 267 mg</strong></p><p> Limited clinical experience with overdose. Multiple doses up to 4806 mg/day were administered, and adverse reactions were mild, transient, and consistent with known side effects.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool &amp; dry place. Protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep all medicines out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Immunosuppressant</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Pulmosis 267 mg is a novel agent with anti-inflammatory, antioxidant, and anti-fibrotic properties. It may improve lung function and reduce acute exacerbations in IPF patients. Its precise molecular targets remain unclear, but it suppresses TGF-β1, a key cytokine in fibrosis. It also downregulates TNF-α, interleukin-1, and interferon-gamma. In animal models, Pulmosis 267 mg inhibits inflammatory cell influx and pulmonary vascular permeability induced by bleomycin.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children &amp; Adolescents:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in children &amp; adolescents are unknown.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Monitor for adverse reactions and consider dosage modification or discontinuation. Not recommended for severe hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Monitor for adverse reactions and consider dosage modification or discontinuation. Not recommended for end-stage renal disease on dialysis.</li><li><strong>Smokers:</strong> Decreased exposure in smokers may alter efficacy.</li></ul>', 'active', '152_1742408718.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 23:25:18', '2025-03-19 23:25:18'),
(3569, 'Synamet Plus', 'Levodopa + Carbidopa + Entacapone', 25.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg+25 mg+200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Synamet Plus 100 mg+25 mg+200 mg</strong></p><p>This medicine is indicated for the treatment of adult patients with Parkinson\'s disease and end-of-dose motor fluctuations not stabilized on levodopa/dopa decarboxylase (DDC) inhibitor treatment.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each tablet contains a 1:4 ratio of carbidopa to levodopa and 200 mg of entacapone (mg of carbidopa per mg of levodopa per mg of entacapone).</p><ul><li><strong>Example dosages</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Stalevo 50: 12.5 mg carbidopa, 50 mg levodopa, 200 mg entacapone</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Stalevo 75: 18.75 mg carbidopa, 75 mg levodopa, 200 mg entacapone</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Stalevo 100: 25 mg carbidopa, 100 mg levodopa, 200 mg entacapone</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Stalevo 125: 31.25 mg carbidopa, 125 mg levodopa, 200 mg entacapone</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Stalevo 150: 37.5 mg carbidopa, 150 mg levodopa, 200 mg entacapone</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Stalevo 200: 50 mg carbidopa, 200 mg levodopa, 200 mg entacapone</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antiparkinson drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Levodopa</strong>: The metabolic precursor of dopamine. It crosses the blood-brain barrier and is converted into dopamine in the brain.</li><li><strong>Carbidopa</strong>: Increases the amount of levodopa that is transported into the CNS by inhibiting the decarboxylation of peripheral levodopa.</li><li><strong>Entacapone</strong>: A selective inhibitor of COMT, which alters the pharmacokinetics of levodopa, resulting in increased and more sustained levodopa serum levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Synamet Plus 100 mg+25 mg+200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: The optimum daily dose must be determined by careful titration of levodopa in each patient. Patients should take only 1 tablet per dose administration. The maximum recommended daily dose of entacapone is 2,000 mg. This combination is usually used in patients currently treated with corresponding doses of standard release levodopa or DDC inhibitor and entacapone.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Synamet Plus 100 mg+25 mg+200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>May be taken with or without food.</li><li>Keep a consistent diet. A change in diet to foods high in protein may delay levodopa absorption and reduce the amount taken up in circulation. Excessive acidity also delays stomach emptying and thus delays levodopa absorption.</li><li>Iron salts may also reduce the amount of levodopa available to the body.</li><li>Swallow whole.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Synamet Plus 100 mg+25 mg+200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptomatic postural hypotension may occur when levodopa is added to the treatment of patients already receiving antihypertensive drugs. Dose adjustment of the antihypertensive agent may be required.</li><li>Dopamine receptor antagonists (e.g., some antipsychotics and antiemetics), phenytoin, and papaverine may reduce the therapeutic effect of levodopa.</li><li>Patients taking these medicinal products with levodopa/carbidopa/entacapone should be carefully observed for loss of therapeutic response.</li><li>Levodopa competes with certain amino acids, which may impair the absorption of Levodopa, Carbidopa, and Entacapone in some patients on a high-protein diet.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Narrow-angle glaucoma, pheochromocytoma, history of neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS) and/or non-traumatic rhabdomyolysis.</li><li>Severe hepatic impairment.</li><li>Concurrent use of or within 14 days of discontinuing non-selective MAOIs.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Synamet Plus 100 mg+25 mg+200 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Dyskinesia</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Hyperkinesia</li><li>Change in urine color</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Stomach pain</li></ul><p>Other side effects may include:</p><ul><li>Severe diarrhea</li><li>Colitis</li><li>Hallucinations</li><li>Mental disturbances</li><li>Orthostatic hypotension</li><li>Rhabdomyolysis</li><li>Symptoms resembling neuroleptic malignant syndrome (high fever, muscle stiffness, and confusion)</li><li>Fibrosis</li><li>Skin cancer, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy category C</strong>. The combination of levodopa/carbidopa/entacapone should not be used during pregnancy unless the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li>The safety of this combination in the infant is not known.</li><li>Women should not breast-feed during treatment with this combination.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>May cause dizziness and symptomatic orthostatism. Therefore, caution should be exercised when driving or using machines.</li><li>Periodic evaluations of hepatic, hematopoietic, cardiovascular, and renal function are recommended during extended therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Synamet Plus 100 mg+25 mg+200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Acute symptoms include agitation, confusional state, coma, bradycardia, ventricular tachycardia, Cheyne Stokes respiration, discolorations of skin, tongue, and conjunctiva, and chromaturia.</li><li>Management is similar to that of acute overdose with levodopa. Pyridoxine is not effective in reversing the actions of levodopa/carbidopa/entacapone.</li><li>Hospitalization is advised with immediate gastric lavage and repeated doses of charcoal over time.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place. Protect from light.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dose adjustment is required for elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impaired patients</strong>: Should be administered cautiously to patients with mild to moderate hepatic impairment. Dose reduction may be needed.</li><li><strong>Renally impaired patients</strong>: Should be administered cautiously to patients with severe renal impairment, including those receiving dialysis therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Antiparkinson drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Levodopa: Precursor of dopamine, converted to dopamine in the brain.</li><li>Carbidopa: Increases levodopa transport into the CNS by inhibiting peripheral decarboxylation.</li><li>Entacapone: COMT inhibitor that alters levodopa pharmacokinetics, increasing and sustaining serum levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category C</strong>: Use during pregnancy should be considered only if the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li>Not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and effectiveness have not been established.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dose adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Hepatic and Renal impaired patients</strong>: Caution is required for patients with hepatic or renal impairments, with possible dose adjustments.</li></ul>', 'active', '1095_1742408899.webp', 1, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 23:28:19', '2025-03-21 21:57:08'),
(3570, 'Rezor Plus', 'Olmesartan Medoxomil + Hydrochlorothiazide', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg+12.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Rezor Plus 20 mg + 12.5 mg</strong></h3><p>Olmesartan Medoxomil &amp; Hydrochlorothiazide combination is indicated for the treatment of <strong>hypertension</strong>.</p><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p>Combined antihypertensive preparations.</p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong> inhibits the reabsorption of sodium (Na) in the distal tubules, causing increased excretion of Na and water, including potassium (K) and hydrogen ions.</li><li><strong>Olmesartan</strong> blocks the vasoconstrictor effects of angiotensin II by selectively blocking its binding to the AT1 receptor in vascular smooth muscle, independently of the pathways for angiotensin II synthesis. It has a 12,500-fold greater affinity for the AT1 receptor than the AT2 receptor, which is not associated with cardiovascular homeostasis. Olmesartan does not inhibit ACE, thus it doesn\'t affect the response to bradykinin.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rezor Plus 20 mg + 12.5 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Starting dose</strong>: 20 mg once daily for Olmesartan Medoxomil (monotherapy) for patients who are not volume-contracted.</li><li>For further blood pressure reduction after 2 weeks, increase to <strong>40 mg</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong> is effective in doses between 12.5 mg and 50 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Replacement Therapy</strong>: Olmesartan &amp; Hydrochlorothiazide may be substituted for their titrated components.</li><li><strong>Titration by Clinical Effect</strong>: The usual dose is 20/12.5 mg once daily, but it may be titrated based on blood pressure response at intervals of 2–4 weeks.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage of Rezor Plus 20 mg + 12.5 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: The usual starting dose is 20/12.5 mg once daily. Dose may be titrated to two tablets (40/25 mg) once daily depending on blood pressure response.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction of Rezor Plus 20 mg + 12.5 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Olmesartan</strong>: No significant drug interactions were found with hydrochlorothiazide, digoxin, or warfarin.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: Possible interactions with alcohol, barbiturates, narcotics (orthostatic hypotension), antidiabetic drugs (may require adjustment), and other antihypertensive drugs (additive effect).</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Olmesartan &amp; Hydrochlorothiazide</strong> are contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to any of its components or have <strong>anuria</strong> or hypersensitivity to sulfonamide-derived drugs.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Rezor Plus 20 mg + 12.5 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Common side effects</strong>: Nausea, headache, dizziness, hyperuricemia, upper respiratory tract infection, urinary tract infection.</li><li><strong>Other adverse effects</strong>: Chest pain, back pain, peripheral edema, abdominal pain, dyspepsia, gastroenteritis, diarrhea.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Categories C (first trimester) and D (second and third trimesters). This combination should not be used during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Olmesartan is not known to be excreted in human milk. Hydrochlorothiazide does appear in human milk, and due to potential adverse effects on the nursing infant, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or the drug, considering its importance to the mother.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Periodic monitoring of serum electrolytes is important to detect imbalances like <strong>hypokalemia</strong>, <strong>hyponatremia</strong>, and <strong>hypochloremic alkalosis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hyperuricemia</strong> may occur in patients on thiazide therapy.</li><li>Caution in patients with impaired <strong>renal function</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Rezor Plus 20 mg + 12.5 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Olmesartan</strong>: Likely to cause <strong>hypotension</strong> and <strong>tachycardia</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: Symptoms from electrolyte depletion such as <strong>hypokalemia</strong>, <strong>hypochloremia</strong>, and dehydration, especially in the presence of <strong>digitalis</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><p>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: Can be used if creatinine clearance &gt;30 ml/min.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment required.</li><li><strong>Pediatric use</strong>: Safety and efficacy in children below 18 years not established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric use</strong>: No specific dose adjustments, but care should be taken in elderly patients.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><p>Combined antihypertensive preparations.</p><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Olmesartan</strong>: Reversibly inhibits the <strong>AT1 receptor</strong> for angiotensin II, leading to the reduction of its vasoconstrictor and aldosterone-secreting effects.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: A thiazide diuretic that increases sodium and chloride excretion and indirectly reduces plasma volume, increasing renin, aldosterone secretion, and potassium loss.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h3><p>Safety and effectiveness during pregnancy have not been established. Discontinue use during pregnancy.</p><h3><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Not recommended for severe renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment required.</li><li><strong>Pediatric use</strong>: Safety and efficacy not established for children.</li><li><strong>Geriatric use</strong>: Clinical studies did not include enough subjects aged 65 or older to determine differences in response compared to younger patients. Caution is advised when dosing elderly patients.</li></ul>', 'active', '543_1742409162.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 23:32:42', '2025-03-21 21:33:49'),
(3571, 'Raditrend', 'Carvedilol', 22.07, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a stripp', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Raditrend 25 mg</strong></p><p> Raditrend 25 mg is indicated for the treatment of mild, moderate, or severe heart failure of ischemic or cardiomyopathic origin, in conjunction with digitalis, diuretics, and ACE inhibitors. It helps to reduce the progression of disease as evidenced by cardiovascular death, cardiovascular hospitalization, or the need to adjust other heart failure medications. Raditrend 25 mg may also be used in patients unable to tolerate an ACE inhibitor, as well as in patients who are not receiving digitalis, hydralazine, or nitrate therapy.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Alpha adrenoceptor blocking drugs</li><li>Beta-adrenoceptor blocking drugs</li><li>Beta-blockers</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Raditrend 25 mg is a non-selective β-adrenergic blocking agent, which causes vasodilation by blocking the activity of α-1 receptors. It exerts an antihypertensive effect partly by reducing total peripheral resistance and vasodilation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: Initially 12.5 mg once daily, increased after 2 days to the usual dose of 25 mg once daily. If necessary, the dose may be further increased at intervals of at least 2 weeks to a maximum of 50 mg daily in single or divided doses.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: An initial dose of 12.5 mg daily may provide satisfactory control.</li><li><strong>Angina</strong>: Initially 12.5 mg twice daily for the first 2 days, then 25 mg twice daily. For elderly patients, the maximum daily dose is 50 mg in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Heart failure (under special supervision)</strong>: Initially, 3.125 mg twice daily (with food), with the dose increased at intervals of at least 2 weeks to 6.25 mg twice daily, then to 12.5 mg twice daily, then to 25 mg twice daily. The maximum dose is 25 mg twice daily in patients with severe heart failure or body weight less than 85 kg, and 50 mg twice daily in patients over 85 kg.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Digoxin</strong>: Raditrend 25 mg increases digoxin levels. Monitor digoxin levels carefully when initiating, adjusting, or discontinuing Raditrend 25 mg.</li><li><strong>Rifampin</strong>: Reduces Cmax and AUC of Raditrend 25 mg by 60%.</li><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>: No effect on the in vitro plasma protein binding of warfarin.</li><li><strong>Clonidine</strong>: β-receptor antagonists can potentiate the pressor reaction after sudden withdrawal of clonidine, but Raditrend’s α-blocking action may modify this effect.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Raditrend 25 mg is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Decompensated heart failure requiring intravenous inotropic therapy</li><li>Bronchial asthma or related bronchospastic conditions</li><li>Second or third-degree AV block</li><li>Sick sinus syndrome (unless a permanent pacemaker is in place)</li><li>Cardiogenic shock or severe bradycardia</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Postural hypotension, dizziness, headache, fatigue, gastrointestinal disturbances, bradycardia</li><li>Less common: Dry mouth, dry eyes, impotence, disturbed vision, peripheral edema, nasal stuffiness, wheezing, sleep disturbances</li><li>Rare: Angina, AV block, exacerbation of intermittent claudication, Raynaud\'s phenomenon, allergic skin reactions, exacerbation of psoriasis, depression, heart failure, thrombocytopenia, leukopenia</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Raditrend 25 mg should not be used during pregnancy as no studies have been performed in this group. Animal studies suggest it crosses the placental barrier.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Raditrend 25 mg is excreted in breast milk. Therefore, breastfeeding is not recommended during its administration.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in hepatic impairment and in heart failure. Monitor clinical status for 2-3 hours after initiation and dose adjustments.</li><li>Ensure renal function and heart failure status are stable before increasing doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Severe hypotension, bradycardia, heart failure, cardiogenic shock, cardiac arrest, bronchospasm, vomiting, seizures.</li><li>Management: Symptomatic and supportive treatment, atropine for bradycardia, IV glucagon, or sympathomimetics for ventricular support, noradrenaline for vasodilation, IV diazepam or clonazepam for seizures.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Raditrend 25 mg in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Raditrend 25 mg is a cardiovascular drug that non-selectively antagonizes β-adrenergic receptors and also blocks α-adrenergic receptors. It has antiproliferative properties and scavenges free radicals. It is used for hypertension, angina pectoris, and congestive heart failure.</p>', 'active', '1053_1742409510.jpg', 10, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 23:38:30', '2025-03-19 23:38:30'),
(3572, 'Sasolin MR', 'Tamsulosin Hydrochloride', 10.07, 8, 'Capsule', '8 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '0.4 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sasolin MR 0.4 mg</strong></p><p> Sasolin MR 0.4 mg is indicated for the treatment of the signs and symptoms of Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH).</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> BPH/ Urinary retention/ Urinary incontinence</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Tamsulosin is an antagonist of α1A-adrenoreceptors in the prostate. It binds selectively and competitively to postsynaptic α1A-adrenoreceptors, which convey smooth muscle contraction, thereby relaxing prostatic and urethral smooth muscle.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sasolin MR 0.4 mg</strong></p><p> The recommended dose of Tamsulosin is 0.4 mg once daily. It should be administered approximately half/hour following the same meal each day. For those patients who fail to respond to the 0.4 mg dose after two to four weeks of dosing, the dose of Tamsulosin can be increased to 0.8 mg once daily. If Tamsulosin administration is discontinued or interrupted for several days at either the 0.4 mg or 0.8 mg dose, therapy should be started again with the 0.4 mg once daily dose.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Sasolin MR 0.4 mg</strong></p><p> Increased plasma concentration with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g. ketoconazole). Moderate CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g. erythromycin), strong (e.g. paroxetine) or moderate (e.g. terbinafine) CYP2D6 inhibitors may increase exposure of tamsulosin. Increased plasma concentration with cimetidine. Additive effect with other α-adrenergic blocking agents. Concomitant use with PDE5 inhibitors may lead to symptomatic hypotension. Decreased plasma concentration with furosemide.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> History of orthostatic hypotension. Severe hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sasolin MR 0.4 mg</strong></p><p> The following adverse reactions have been reported during the use of Tamsulosin: dizziness, abnormal ejaculation, and less frequently (1-2%) headache, asthenia, postural hypotension, palpitations, and rhinitis. Gastrointestinal reactions such as nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, and constipation can occasionally occur. Hypersensitivity reactions such as rash, pruritus, and urticaria can occur occasionally. As with other alpha-blockers, drowsiness, blurred vision, dry mouth, or edema can occur. Syncope has been reported rarely, and there have been very rare reports of angioedema and priapism.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Sasolin MR 0.4 mg capsules are not indicated for use in women.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Not indicated for use in women. Severe renal impairment (CrCl &lt;10 mL/min). Pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sasolin MR 0.4 mg</strong></p><p> As overdose of Sasolin MR 0.4 mg capsules lead to hypotension, support of the cardiovascular system is of first importance. Restoration of blood pressure and normalization of heart rate may be accomplished by keeping the patient in a supine position. If this measure is inadequate, then administration of intravenous fluid should be considered. Measures, such as emesis, can be taken to impede absorption. When large quantities are involved, gastric lavage can be applied and activated charcoal and an osmotic laxative, such as sodium sulphate, can be administered.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store between 20-25° C.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> Sasolin MR 0.4 mg capsules are not indicated for use in pediatric populations.</p>', 'active', '1311_1742409653.jpg', 44, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 23:40:53', '2025-03-19 23:40:53'),
(3573, 'WHISPER MAXI FIT SANITARY NAPKIN', 'Sanitary Napkin', 150.00, 8, 'x 8 Pads in a pack', '8 Pads in a pack', 'Pad', 'pad', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Description</strong></p><p>Stay confident and worry-free on your heavy flow days with <strong>WHISPER MAXI FIT SANITARY NAPKIN</strong>. Its advanced features keep you dry, fresh, and comfortable. The pad’s interior instantly absorbs and evenly distributes fluid. Also, it has an extra layer of protection to prevent back leakage.</p><p><strong>Usage Instructions Of WHISPER MAXI FIT SANITARY NAPKIN</strong></p><ol><li>Choose the right size and absorbency for your flow.</li><li>Apply the <strong>WHISPER MAXI FIT SANITARY NAPKIN</strong> to your underwear, with the adhesive side down.</li><li>Secure the <strong>WHISPER MAXI FIT SANITARY NAPKIN</strong> in place by folding the wings down.</li><li>Change the <strong>WHISPER MAXI FIT SANITARY NAPKIN</strong> every 4 to 6 hours, or more often if it becomes wet or soiled.</li></ol><p><strong>Storage Guidelines Of WHISPER MAXI FIT SANITARY NAPKIN</strong></p><ol><li>Store <strong>WHISPER MAXI FIT SANITARY NAPKIN</strong>s in a cool and dry place.</li><li>Do not store <strong>WHISPER MAXI FIT SANITARY NAPKIN</strong>s in a humid environment.</li><li>Keep <strong>WHISPER MAXI FIT SANITARY NAPKIN</strong>s out of reach of children.</li><li>Do not store used <strong>WHISPER MAXI FIT SANITARY NAPKIN</strong>s.</li></ol><p><strong>Safety Information</strong></p><ol><li>Choose the right size and absorbency for your flow.</li><li>Secure the <strong>WHISPER MAXI FIT SANITARY NAPKIN</strong> in place by folding the wings down.</li><li>Change the <strong>WHISPER MAXI FIT SANITARY NAPKIN</strong> every 4 to 6 hours, or more often if it becomes wet or soiled.</li><li>Dispose of the used <strong>WHISPER MAXI FIT SANITARY NAPKIN</strong> in a sealed bag or container.</li></ol>', 'active', '1382_1742409675.png', 50, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 23:41:15', '2025-05-05 18:00:28'),
(3574, 'Raltrox', 'Nalbuphine', 60.23, 1, 'IV injection', '3 IV injection', 'IV Injection', '10 mg per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Raltrox 10 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Raltrox 10 mg/ml Hydrochloride is indicated for the relief of moderate to severe pain. It is used for relief of moderate to severe pain associated with myocardial infarction (MI). Raltrox 10 mg/ml Hydrochloride can also be used as a supplement to balanced anesthesia, for preoperative and postoperative analgesia, and for obstetrical analgesia during labor and delivery.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Raltrox 10 mg/ml Hydrochloride is a synthetic potent analgesic. Its analgesic potency is essentially equivalent to that of morphine on a milligram basis. Receptor studies show that Raltrox 10 mg/ml binds to mu, kappa, and delta receptors, but not to sigma receptors. Raltrox 10 mg/ml is primarily a kappa agonist/partial mu antagonist analgesic.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Raltrox 10 mg/ml</strong></p><p> The usual recommended adult dose is 10 mg for a 70 kg individual, administered subcutaneously, intramuscularly, or intravenously; this dose may be repeated every 3 to 6 hours as necessary. Dosage should be adjusted according to the severity of the pain, physical status of the patient, and other medications the patient may be receiving.</p><ul><li>Moderate to severe pain: by intravenous or intramuscular injection 10-20 mg for a 70 kg patient, adjusted as required; child up to 0.3 mg/kg repeated once or twice as necessary.</li><li>Preoperative anesthesia: by intravenous or intramuscular injection 0.1-0.2 mg/kg.</li><li>Obstetrical analgesia during labor &amp; delivery: by intravenous injection 0.3-1 mg/kg over 10-15 minutes with maintenance doses of 0.25-0.5 mg/kg in single intravenous administration as required.</li><li>Intraoperative analgesia: by intravenous injection 0.25-0.5 mg/kg at 30-minute intervals.</li><li>Myocardial infarction: By slow intravenous injection 10-20 mg, repeated after 30 minutes if necessary. Larger dose is required when used as a supplement of anesthesia than that required for analgesia.</li><li>Children from 18 months to 15 years old: usually 0.2 mg/kg body-weight, given preferably by intravenous or intramuscular injection. Maintenance doses may be given at intervals of 4 to 6 hours or the dose must be determined by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Raltrox 10 mg/ml</strong></p><p> No hazardous interactions have been identified with Raltrox 10 mg/ml; however, interactions described with other opioids may be anticipated. Patients receiving a narcotic analgesic, general anesthesia, phenothiazines or other tranquilizers, sedatives, hypnotics, or other CNS depressants (including alcohol) concomitantly with Raltrox 10 mg/ml may exhibit an additive effect.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Known hypersensitivity to Raltrox 10 mg/ml Hydrochloride.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Raltrox 10 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Generally, Raltrox 10 mg/ml is well tolerated. However, few side effects like sedation, sweating, nausea, vomiting, dizziness, vertigo, dry mouth, headache, respiratory depression, dyspnea, and asthma may be seen.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy category B. The placental transfer of Raltrox 10 mg/ml is high and rapid. There are no well-controlled studies in pregnant women. This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Limited data suggest that Raltrox 10 mg/ml is excreted in maternal milk but only in a small amount (less than 1% of the administered dose) and with a clinically insignificant effect. Caution should be exercised when Raltrox 10 mg/ml is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Caution should be taken in the following conditions: impaired respiration, impaired renal or hepatic function, biliary tract surgery, myocardial infarction, and hypotension.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Raltrox 10 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Sleepiness and mild dyspnea may occur due to overdose. The immediate intravenous administration of an opiate antagonist such as Naloxone or Nalmefene is a specific antidote. Oxygen, intravenous fluids, vasopressors, and other supportive measures should be used as indicated.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep it in a cool and dry place, protected from light and moisture.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Raltrox 10 mg/ml Hydrochloride is a synthetic potent analgesic. Its analgesic potency is essentially equivalent to that of morphine on a milligram basis. Receptor studies show that Raltrox 10 mg/ml binds to mu, kappa, and delta receptors, but not to sigma receptors. Raltrox 10 mg/ml is primarily a kappa agonist/partial mu antagonist analgesic.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy category B. The placental transfer of Raltrox 10 mg/ml is high and rapid. There are no well-controlled studies in pregnant women. This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Limited data suggest that Raltrox 10 mg/ml is excreted in maternal milk but only in a small amount (less than 1% of the administered dose) and with a clinically insignificant effect. Caution should be exercised when Raltrox 10 mg/ml is administered to a nursing woman.</p>', 'active', '1787_1742409852.jpg', 7, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 23:44:12', '2025-03-19 23:44:12');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3575, 'Zoledron', 'Zoledronic Acid [For osteoporosis]', 5950.00, 1, 'IV Infusion', '100 ml bottle', 'IV Infusion', '5 mg per 100 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zoledron 5 mg/100 ml</strong></p><p> Zoledronic Acid is indicated for the treatment of osteoporosis in postmenopausal women to reduce the incidence of hip, vertebral, and non-vertebral fractures; prevention of clinical fractures after a hip fracture; treatment and prevention of glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis; treatment of osteoporosis in men and for the treatment of Paget\'s disease of bone. Treatment should be restricted to three annual doses.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Bisphosphonate preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Zoledronic acid belongs to the class of nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates and acts primarily on bone. It is an inhibitor of osteoclast-mediated bone resorption. The action of bisphosphonates on bone is based on their high affinity for mineralized bone. Intravenously administered Zoledronic acid is rapidly distributed to bone. The main molecular target of Zoledronic acid in the osteoclast is the enzyme farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase, but this does not exclude other mechanisms. There was no accumulation of the active substance in plasma after multiple doses given every 28 days. Zoledronic acid is not metabolized and is excreted unchanged via the kidney.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Zoledron 5 mg/100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Treatment of postmenopausal osteoporosis</strong>: Recommended dose is a single intravenous infusion of 5 mg Zoledronic Acid administered once a year. Adequate supplemental Calcium and Vitamin-D intake is important in women with osteoporosis if dietary intake is inadequate.</li><li><strong>Prevention of clinical fractures after a hip fracture</strong>: Recommended dose is a single intravenous infusion of 5 mg Zoledronic Acid administered once a year. In patients with a recent low-trauma hip fracture, it is recommended to give the first Zoledronic Acid solution for infusion two or more weeks after hip fracture repairs. It is also recommended to have a loading dose of 50,000 to 125,000 IU of Vitamin-D given orally or via intramuscular route prior to the first administration of Zoledronic Acid solution for infusion. Supplemental Calcium and Vitamin-D intake is recommended for patients treated to prevent clinical fractures after a hip fracture.</li><li><strong>Treatment of osteoporosis in men</strong>: Recommended dose is a single intravenous infusion of 5 mg Zoledronic Acid administered once a year. Adequate supplemental Calcium and Vitamin-D intake is important in men with osteoporosis if dietary intake is inadequate.</li><li><strong>Treatment and prevention of glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis</strong>: Recommended dose is a single intravenous infusion of 5 mg Zoledronic Acid administered once a year. Adequate supplemental Calcium and Vitamin-D intake is important in patients with osteoporosis if dietary intake is inadequate.</li><li><strong>Treatment of Paget\'s disease of bone</strong>: Recommended dose is a single intravenous infusion of 5 mg Zoledronic Acid. Re-treatment with Zoledronic Acid may be considered in patients who have relapsed, based on increases in serum alkaline phosphatase, in patients who failed to achieve normalization of serum alkaline phosphatase, or in patients with symptoms, as dictated by medical practice 12 months after the initial dose. In patients with Paget\'s disease, adequate Vitamin-D intake is recommended in association with Zoledronic Acid administration. It is also strongly advised that adequate supplemental Calcium corresponding to at least 500 mg elemental Calcium twice daily is ensured in patients with Paget\'s disease for at least 10 days following Zoledronic Acid administration.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zoledron 5 mg/100 ml</strong></p><p> The dose of 5 mg Zoledronic acid must be administered over at least 15 minutes. Zoledronic Acid should be administered intravenously via an infusion line, given at a constant infusion rate. The infusion time must not be less than 15 minutes.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Zoledron 5 mg/100 ml</strong></p><p> Specific drug-drug interaction studies have not been conducted with Zoledronic acid. Zoledronic acid is eliminated by renal excretion. Caution is indicated when Zoledronic Acid is administered in conjunction with drugs that can significantly impact renal function (e.g. aminoglycosides or diuretics that may cause dehydration).</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> The drug is contraindicated if patients have hypersensitivity to the active substance or to any of the excipients or to any bisphosphonates, hypocalcaemia, renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;35 mL/min), current or recent uveitis, or a history of bisphosphonate-associated uveitis, pregnancy, and lactation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zoledron 5 mg/100 ml</strong></p><p> The post-dose side effects are fever, myalgia, flu-like symptoms, arthralgia, and headache, the majority of which occur within the first 3 days following Zoledronic Acid administration. The majority of these symptoms were mild to moderate in nature and resolved within 3 days of the event onset. The incidence of these symptoms occurring within the first 3 days after administration of Zoledronic Acid can be reduced with the administration of Paracetamol or Ibuprofen shortly following Zoledronic Acid administration. Severe and occasionally incapacitating bone, joint, and/or muscle pain have been infrequently reported in patients taking Zoledronic Acid.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Zoledronic Acid is contraindicated during pregnancy and in breastfeeding women. It is also not recommended for use in children and adolescents below 18 years of age.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Patients must be appropriately hydrated prior to administration of Zoledronic Acid. This is especially important in the elderly and for patients receiving diuretic therapy. Adequate hydration can be achieved by the patient drinking two glasses of fluid (such as water) before and after the infusion. Pre-existing hypocalcaemia must be treated by adequate intake of Calcium and Vitamin-D before initiating therapy with Zoledronic Acid. Other disturbances of mineral metabolism must also be effectively treated (e.g. diminished parathyroid reserve, thyroid surgery, parathyroid surgery, intestinal Calcium malabsorption). Physicians should consider clinical monitoring for these patients.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zoledron 5 mg/100 ml</strong></p><p> Clinical experience with acute overdose is limited. Patients who have received doses higher than those recommended should be carefully monitored. In the event of overdose leading to clinically significant hypocalcaemia, reversal may be achieved with supplemental oral Calcium and/or an infusion of Calcium.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30° C prior to opening. Protect from moisture and light. Zoledronic Acid must be kept out of the reach and sight of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Patients with renal impairment</strong>: The use of Zoledronic Acid in patients with creatinine clearance &lt;35 mL/min is not recommended due to limited clinical safety data in such patients. No dose adjustment is necessary in patients with creatinine clearance &gt;35 mL/min.</li><li><strong>Patients with hepatic impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment is required for patients with hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Elderly patients</strong>: No dose adjustment is required. However, because decreased renal function occurs more commonly in the elderly, special care should be taken to monitor renal function.</li></ul><p>Zoledronic Acid must not be mixed or given intravenously with any other medication and must be given through a separate infusion line at a constant infusion rate. If refrigerated, allow the refrigerated solution to reach room temperature before administration. Aseptic techniques must be followed during the preparation of the infusion. Any unused solution should be discarded. Only clear solutions free from particles and discoloration should be used.</p><p> After opening, the solution is chemically and physically stable for at least 24 hours at 2°C to 8°C. From a microbiological point of view, the product should be used immediately. Zoledronic Acid solution for infusion must not be allowed to come into contact with any Calcium or other divalent cation-containing solutions.</p>', 'active', '784_1742410105.jpg', 7, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 23:48:25', '2025-03-19 23:48:25'),
(3576, 'Zithrox', 'Azithromycin Dihydrate', 100.00, 1, 'x 20 ml bottle', '20 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '200 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml is prescribed for infections (produced by susceptible organisms) in the upper respiratory tract, including sinusitis and pharyngitis/tonsillitis, the lower respiratory tract, including bronchitis and pneumonia, the otitis media, and the skin and soft tissue. Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml is recommended for the treatment of non-gonococcal urethritis and cervicitis caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> in sexually transmitted illnesses in both men and women.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Macrolides</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml belongs to the macrolide subclass of antibiotics known as azalide. In order to prevent microbial protein synthesis, Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml binds to the 50s ribosomal subunit of susceptible species. Moraxella catarrhalis, anaerobes such as <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>, <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Bordetella pertussis</em>, <em>Bordetella parapertussis</em>, <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em>, <em>Haemophilus ducreyi</em>, and <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>, as well as gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria such as <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>. Moreover, in vitro testing of Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml has shown action against <em>Campylobacter</em> sp., <em>Toxoplasma gondii</em>, <em>Treponema pallidum</em>, <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>, <em>Legionella pneumophila</em>, and <em>Chlamydia hominis</em>.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: 500 mg once daily orally for 3 days or 500 mg once on day 1, then 250 mg once on days 2-5 for 4 days. For sexually transmitted diseases caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> in adults, the dose is 1 gm given as a single dose or 500 mg once on day 1, followed by 250 mg once daily for the next 2 days may also be given.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 10 mg/kg body weight once daily for 3 days for children over 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">200 mg (1 teaspoonful) for 3 days if body weight is 15-25 kg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">300 mg (1½ teaspoonfuls) for 3 days if body weight is 26-35 kg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">400 mg (2 teaspoonfuls) for 3 days if body weight is 36-45 kg.</li><li>In typhoid fever, 500 mg (2½ teaspoonfuls) once daily for 7-10 days is given.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Procedure for reinstatement after suspension:</p><ul><li>Step 1: Thoroughly shake the bottle to break up the powder.</li><li>Step 2: Fill the bottle with boiled and cooled water to the watermark on the label.</li><li>Step 3: Mix the powder and water thoroughly to combine.</li><li>Take Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml at least one hour before or two hours after eating.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>When food and antacids are present, Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml absorption is lessened. Due to the potential for ergotism brought on by an interaction between Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml and the cytochrome P-450 system, Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml should be avoided in patients who are receiving ergot alkaloids. With co-administration, care should be taken since macrolides raise the plasma levels of cyclosporin and digoxin. Warfarin, Theophylline, Carbamazepine, Methylprednisolone, or Cimetidine have not been found to interact with Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those who are hypersensitive to Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml or any other macrolide antibiotic should not take Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml. Ergot derivatives and Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml should not be administered together. Those with liver disorders should not use Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Low-frequency side effects are experienced after taking Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml. A therapeutic withdrawal will reverse the adverse effects, which include nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain or cramps, flatulence, diarrhea, headaches, and skin rashes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml pregnancy category is B. Studies on animal reproduction have shown that Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml has no evidence of harming the fetus. Pregnant women have not been the subject of any good, controlled studies. Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml should only be used during pregnancy if no other suitable options are available, as research on animal reproduction are not always indicative of human response. The presence of Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml in breast milk is unknown. Hence, when giving Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml to nursing mothers, care should be taken.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>It is advised to keep an eye out for any indications of superinfection with non-susceptible organisms, such as fungi, when using any antibiotic. Those with renal impairment don\'t require a dose adjustment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>There is no information on Zithrox 200 mg/5 ml overdosage. Macrolide antibiotic overdose symptoms can include hearing loss, extreme nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. The use of gastric lavage and other supportive treatments is advised.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep dry and away from heat and light. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p>', 'active', '9_1742410426.jpg', 53, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-19 23:53:46', '2025-05-07 20:42:24'),
(3577, 'Roxetil', 'Cefpodoxime Proxetil', 100.00, 1, 'Bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '40 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Roxetil 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Cefpodoxime is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by susceptible microorganisms, listed below:</p><ul><li>Acute otitis media caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains).</li><li>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li>Acute maxillary sinusitis caused by <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>.</li><li>Community-acquired pneumonia caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em> or <em>H. influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains).</li><li>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em>, <em>H. influenzae</em> (non-beta-lactamase-producing strains only), or <em>M. catarrhalis</em>.</li><li>Skin and skin structure infections caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections caused by <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, or <em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em>.</li><li>Uncomplicated gonorrhea caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li><li>Rectal gonococcal infections in women due to <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cefpodoxime is an oral 3rd generation cephalosporin, which has good stability to beta-lactamases and activity against Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. It is indicated for the treatment of infections either before the infecting organism has been identified. It is a prodrug; its active metabolite is Cefpodoxime. Approximately 29 to 33% of Cefpodoxime is excreted unchanged in the urine within 12 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Roxetil 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (13 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/tonsillitis: 100 mg 12 hourly, 5 to 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute maxillary sinusitis: 200 mg 12 hourly, 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Community-acquired pneumonia: 200 mg 12 hourly, 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bacterial exacerbations of chronic bronchitis: 200 mg 12 hourly, 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin and skin structure infections: 400 mg 12 hourly, 7 to 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: 100 mg 12 hourly, 7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated gonorrhea: single dose of 200 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rectal gonococcal infections in women: single dose of 200 mg</li><li><strong>Infants and Pediatric Patients (2 months to 12 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute otitis media: 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly, 5 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/tonsillitis: 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly, 5 to 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute maxillary sinusitis: 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly, 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Roxetil 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (13 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/tonsillitis: 100 mg 12 hourly, 5 to 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute maxillary sinusitis: 200 mg 12 hourly, 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Community-acquired pneumonia: 200 mg 12 hourly, 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bacterial exacerbations of chronic bronchitis: 200 mg 12 hourly, 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin and skin structure infections: 400 mg 12 hourly, 7 to 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: 100 mg 12 hourly, 7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated gonorrhea: single dose of 200 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rectal gonococcal infections in women: single dose of 200 mg</li><li><strong>Infants and Pediatric Patients (2 months to 12 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute otitis media: 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly, 5 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/tonsillitis: 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly, 5 to 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute maxillary sinusitis: 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly, 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Roxetil 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Cefpodoxime concomitant administration of high doses of antacids (sodium bicarbonate and aluminum hydroxide) or H2 blockers reduces peak plasma levels and the extent of absorption, respectively. Renal excretion of Cefpodoxime is inhibited by probenecid.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Cefpodoxime is contraindicated in patients with a known allergy to cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Roxetil 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Cefpodoxime has very few side effects. Possible side effects include gastrointestinal disorders (such as diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain), rash, urticaria, and itching.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> US FDA pregnancy category of Cefpodoxime is B. There is, however, no adequate and well-controlled study in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Cefpodoxime has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Cefpodoxime is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> In patients with transient or persistent reduction in urinary output due to renal insufficiency, the total daily dose of Cefpodoxime should be reduced. Cefpodoxime should be administered with caution to patients receiving concurrent treatment with potent diuretics. As with other antibiotics, prolonged use of Cefpodoxime may result in overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> Patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min) should have the dosing intervals increased to 24 hours. The dosage adjustment is not required in cases of hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li>Step 1: Shake the bottle well to loosen the powder.</li><li>Step 2: Add boiled and cooled water in the bottle.</li><li>Step 3: Shake until powder is completely mixed with water.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Cefpodoxime is an oral 3rd generation cephalosporin, which has good stability to beta-lactamases and activity against Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. It is indicated for the treatment of infections either before the infecting organism has been identified. It is a prodrug; its active metabolite is Cefpodoxime. Approximately 29 to 33% of Cefpodoxime is excreted unchanged in the urine within 12 hours.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> US FDA pregnancy category of Cefpodoxime is B. There is, however, no adequate and well-controlled study in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Cefpodoxime has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Cefpodoxime is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min) should have the dosing intervals increased to 24 hours. The dosage adjustment is not required in cases of hepatic impairment.</p>', 'active', '498_1742410904.jpg', 1, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 00:01:44', '2025-03-20 00:01:44'),
(3578, 'Zolopt', 'Brinzolamide', 500.00, 1, 'Drop', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Suspension', '1 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zolopt 1%</strong></p><p> Zolopt 1% Ophthalmic Suspension is indicated as monotherapy, or as adjunctive therapy to beta-blockers in the treatment of elevated intraocular pressure in ocular hypertension, or open-angle glaucoma.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for miotics and glaucoma</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Carbonic anhydrase (CA) is an enzyme found in many tissues of the body, including the eye. It catalyzes the reversible reaction involving the hydration of carbon dioxide and the dehydration of carbonic acid. It exists as a number of isoenzymes, with the most active being carbonic anhydrase II (CA-II), found primarily in red blood cells but also in other tissues. Inhibition of carbonic anhydrase in the ciliary processes of the eye decreases aqueous humor secretion, presumably by slowing the formation of bicarbonate ions with subsequent reduction in sodium and fluid transport. Following topical ocular administration, Zolopt 1% inhibits aqueous humor formation and reduces elevated intraocular pressure. Elevated intraocular pressure is a major risk factor in the pathogenesis of optic nerve damage and glaucomatous visual field loss.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zolopt 1%</strong></p><p> The recommended dose is one drop of this eye drop in the conjunctival sac of the affected eye(s) twice daily. Some patients may have a better response with one drop three times a day. Shake well before use.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Zolopt 1%</strong></p><p> The recommended dose is one drop of this eye drop in the conjunctival sac of the affected eye(s) twice daily. Some patients may have a better response with one drop three times a day. Shake well before use.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Zolopt 1%</strong></p><p> In patients treated with oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, rare instances of drug interactions have occurred with high-dose salicylate therapy. Therefore, the potential for such drug interactions should be considered in patients receiving Zolopt 1%.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> It is contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to any component of this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zolopt 1%</strong></p><p> Reported side effects include blurred vision and a bitter, sour, or unusual taste. Other side effects include blepharitis, dermatitis, dry eye, foreign body sensation, headache, hyperemia, ocular discharge, ocular discomfort, ocular keratitis, ocular pain, ocular pruritus, and rhinitis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Zolopt 1% ophthalmic suspension should be used during pregnancy only if the benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p> Use in lactation: It is not known whether Zolopt 1% ophthalmic suspension is excreted in human milk. Lactating mothers should either discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, depending upon the importance of the drug to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Zolopt 1% ophthalmic suspension and its metabolite are excreted predominantly by the kidney, so it is not recommended in patients with severe renal impairment. Zolopt 1% ophthalmic suspension has not been studied in patients with hepatic impairment and should be used with caution in such patients. The concomitant administration of Zolopt 1% ophthalmic suspension and oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitors is not recommended due to no additional benefits. If hypersensitivity reactions occur after instillation, patients should be advised to discontinue the use of Zolopt 1% and consult a physician.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zolopt 1%</strong></p><p> Although no human data are available, electrolyte imbalance, development of an acidosis state, and possible nervous system effects may occur following oral administration of an overdose. Serum electrolyte levels (particularly potassium) and blood pH levels should be monitored.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at room temperature and protect from light. Do not touch the dropper tip to any surface. It is desirable that the contents should not be used more than four weeks after first opening the bottle. Protect from freezing.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Drugs for miotics and glaucoma</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Carbonic anhydrase (CA) is an enzyme found in many tissues of the body, including the eye. It catalyzes the reversible reaction involving the hydration of carbon dioxide and the dehydration of carbonic acid. It exists as a number of isoenzymes, with the most active being carbonic anhydrase II (CA-II), found primarily in red blood cells but also in other tissues. Inhibition of carbonic anhydrase in the ciliary processes of the eye decreases aqueous humor secretion, presumably by slowing the formation of bicarbonate ions with subsequent reduction in sodium and fluid transport. Following topical ocular administration, Zolopt 1% inhibits aqueous humor formation and reduces elevated intraocular pressure. Elevated intraocular pressure is a major risk factor in the pathogenesis of optic nerve damage and glaucomatous visual field loss.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Zolopt 1% ophthalmic suspension should be used during pregnancy only if the benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p> Use in lactation: It is not known whether Zolopt 1% ophthalmic suspension is excreted in human milk. Lactating mothers should either discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, depending upon the importance of the drug to the mother.</p>', 'active', '1350_1742411080.jpg', 53, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 00:04:40', '2025-03-20 00:04:40'),
(3579, 'Tenomin', 'Atenolol', 0.77, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Tenomin 50 mg</strong></p><p> Tenomin 50 mg is indicated- In the management of hypertension. It may be used alone or concomitantly with other antihypertensive agents, particularly with a thiazide-type diuretic. For the long-term management of patients with angina pectoris. In the management of hemodynamically stable patients with definite or suspected acute myocardial infarction to reduce cardiovascular mortality.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Beta-adrenoceptor blocking drugs, Beta-blockers</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> The synthesis of Tenomin 50 mg resulted from attempts to produce a β-adrenoceptor antagonist that would competitively block β1 (cardiac) receptors but have no effect on β2-receptors. It is classified as a β1 selective (cardioselective) β-adrenergic receptor antagonist with no membrane-stability activity and no partial agonist activity. It is markedly the most hydrophilic of the currently available β-blockers and thus penetrates the lipid of cell membranes poorly.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Tenomin 50 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Hypertension</strong>: The initial dose of Tenomin 50 mg is 50 mg given as one tablet a day either alone or added to diuretic therapy. The full effect of this dose will usually be seen within one to two weeks. If an optimal response is not achieved, the dosage should be increased to Tenomin 50 mg 100 mg given as one tablet a day. Increasing the dosage beyond 100 mg a day is unlikely to produce any further benefit.</p><p> <strong>Angina Pectoris</strong>: The initial dose of Tenomin 50 mg is 50 mg given as one tablet a day. If an optimal response is not achieved within one week, the dosage should be increased to Tenomin 50 mg 100 mg given as one tablet a day. Some patients may require a dosage of 200 mg once a day for optimal effect.</p><p> Twenty-four-hour control with once daily dosing is achieved by giving doses larger than necessary to achieve an immediate maximum effect. The maximum early effect on exercise tolerance occurs with doses of 50 to 100 mg, but at these doses the effect at 24 hours is attenuated, averaging about 50% to 75% of that observed with once a day oral doses of 200 mg.</p><p> <strong>Acute Myocardial Infarction</strong>: In patients with definite or suspected acute myocardial infarction, treatment with Tenomin 50 mg I.V. Injection should be initiated as soon as possible after the patient\'s arrival in the hospital and after eligibility is established. Treatment should begin with the intravenous administration of 5 mg Tenomin 50 mg over 5 minutes followed by another 5 mg intravenous injection 10 minutes later. In patients who tolerate the full intravenous dose (10 mg), Tenomin 50 mg Tablets 50 mg should be initiated 10 minutes after the last intravenous dose followed by another 50 mg oral dose 12 hours later. Thereafter, Tenomin 50 mg can be given orally either 100 mg once daily or 50 mg twice a day for a further 6-9 days or until discharge from the hospital. If bradycardia or hypotension requiring treatment or any other untoward effects occur, Tenomin 50 mg should be discontinued.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Tenomin 50 mg</strong></p><p> Catecholamine-depleting drugs (e.g., reserpine) may have an additive effect when given with beta-blocking agents. Patients treated with Tenomin 50 mg plus a catecholamine depletor should therefore be closely observed for evidence of hypotension and/or marked bradycardia which may produce vertigo, syncope, or postural hypotension.</p><p> Calcium channel blockers may also have an additive effect when given with Tenomin 50 mg. Disopyramide is a Type I antiarrhythmic drug with potent negative inotropic and chronotropic effects. Disopyramide has been associated with severe bradycardia, asystole, and heart failure when administered with beta-blockers.</p><p> Amiodarone is an antiarrhythmic agent with negative chronotropic properties that may be additive to those seen with beta-blockers.</p><p> Beta blockers may exacerbate the rebound hypertension which can follow the withdrawal of clonidine. If the two drugs are coadministered, the beta blocker should be withdrawn several days before the gradual withdrawal of clonidine. If replacing clonidine by beta-blocker therapy, the introduction of beta blockers should be delayed for several days after clonidine administration has stopped.</p><p> Concomitant use of prostaglandin synthase inhibiting drugs, e.g., indomethacin, may decrease the hypotensive effects of beta blockers.</p><p> While taking beta blockers, patients with a history of anaphylactic reaction to a variety of allergens may have a more severe reaction on repeated challenge, either accidental, diagnostic, or therapeutic. Such patients may be unresponsive to the usual doses of epinephrine used to treat the allergic reaction.</p><p> Both digitalis glycosides and beta-blockers slow atrioventricular conduction and decrease heart rate. Concomitant use can increase the risk of bradycardia.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Tenomin 50 mg is contraindicated in- Sinus bradycardia, heart block greater than first degree, cardiogenic shock, and overt cardiac failure. Those patients with a history of hypersensitivity to the Tenomin 50 mg or any of the drug product’s components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Tenomin 50 mg</strong></p><p> In a series of investigations in the treatment of acute myocardial infarction, bradycardia and hypotension occurred more commonly, as expected for any beta blocker. In addition, a variety of adverse effects has been reported with other beta-adrenergic blocking agents, and may be considered potential adverse effects of Tenomin 50 mg.</p><p> <strong>Hematologic</strong>: Agranulocytosis.</p><p> <strong>Allergic</strong>: Fever, combined with aching and sore throat, laryngospasm, and respiratory distress.</p><p> <strong>Central Nervous System</strong>: Reversible mental depression progressing to catatonia; an acute reversible syndrome characterized by disorientation of time and place; short term memory loss; emotional lability with slightly clouded sensorium; and, decreased performance on neuropsychometrics.</p><p> <strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Mesenteric arterial thrombosis, ischemic colitis.</p><p> <strong>Miscellaneous</strong>: There have been reports of skin rashes and/or dry eyes associated with the use of beta-adrenergic blocking drugs. Discontinuance of the drug should be considered if any such reaction is not otherwise explicable. Patients should be closely monitored following cessation of therapy.</p><p> <strong>Other</strong>: Erythematous rash</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category D. Caution should be exercised when Tenomin 50 mg is administered to a nursing woman. Clinically significant bradycardia has been reported in breast-fed infants. Premature infants, or infants with impaired renal function, may be more likely to develop adverse effects.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> <strong>General</strong>: Patients already on a beta blocker must be evaluated carefully before Tenomin 50 mg is administered. Initial and subsequent Tenomin 50 mg dosages can be adjusted downward depending on clinical observations including pulse and blood pressure. Tenomin 50 mg may aggravate peripheral arterial circulatory disorders.</p><p> <strong>Impaired Renal Function</strong>: The drug should be used with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</p><p> <strong>Geriatric Use</strong>:</p><p> <strong>Hypertension and Angina Pectoris</strong>: Due to Coronary Atherosclerosis: Dose selection for an elderly patient should be cautious, usually starting at the low end of the dosing range, reflecting the greater frequency of decreased hepatic, renal, or cardiac function, and of concomitant disease or other drug therapy.</p><p> <strong>Acute Myocardial Infarction</strong>: Dose selection for an elderly patient should be cautious, usually starting at the low end of the dosing range, reflecting greater frequency of decreased hepatic, renal, or cardiac function, and of concomitant disease or other drug therapy. Evaluation of patients with hypertension or myocardial infarction should always include assessment of renal function.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Tenomin 50 mg</strong></p><p> Overdosage with Tenomin 50 mg has been reported with patients surviving acute doses as high as 5 g. One death was reported in a man who may have taken as much as 10 g acutely. The predominant symptoms reported following Tenomin 50 mg overdose are lethargy, disorder of respiratory drive, wheezing, sinus pause, and bradycardia. Additionally, common effects associated with overdosage of any beta-adrenergic blocking agent and which might also be expected in Tenomin 50 mg overdose are congestive heart failure, hypotension, bronchospasm and/or hypoglycemia.</p><p> Treatment of overdose should be directed to the removal of any unabsorbed drug by induced emesis, gastric lavage, or administration of activated charcoal. Tenomin 50 mg can be removed from the general circulation by hemodialysis. Based on the severity of symptoms, management may require intensive support care and facilities for applying cardiac and respiratory support.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Do not use later than the date of expiry. Keep all medicines out of the reach of children. To be dispensed only on the prescription of a registered physician.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> <strong>Pediatric use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p> <strong>Renal</strong>: Some renal impaired or elderly patients being treated for hypertension may require a lower starting dose of Tenomin 50 mg: 25 mg given as one tablet a day.</p><p> <strong>Hemodialysis</strong>: Patients on hemodialysis should be given 25 mg or 50 mg after each dialysis; this should be done under hospital supervision as marked falls in blood pressure can occur.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Beta-adrenoceptor blocking drugs, Beta-blockers</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> The synthesis of Tenomin 50 mg resulted from attempts to produce a β-adrenoceptor antagonist that would competitively block β1 (cardiac) receptors but have no effect on β2-receptors. It is classified as a β1 selective (cardioselective) β-adrenergic receptor antagonist with no membrane-stability activity and no partial agonist activity. It is markedly the most hydrophilic of the currently available β-blockers and thus penetrates the lipid of cell membranes poorly.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category D. Caution should be exercised when Tenomin 50 mg is administered to a nursing woman. Clinically significant bradycardia has been reported in breast-fed infants. Premature infants, or infants with impaired renal function, may be more likely to develop adverse effects.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> <strong>Elderly Patients or Patients with Renal Impairment</strong>: Tenomin 50 mg is excreted by the kidneys; consequently dosage should be adjusted in cases of severe impairment of renal function. In general, dose selection for an elderly patient should be cautious, usually starting at the low end of the dosing range, reflecting greater frequency of decreased hepatic, renal, or cardiac function, and of concomitant disease or other drug therapy. The following maximum oral dosages are recommended for elderly, renal impaired patients and for patients with renal impairment due to other causes:</p><p> Creatinine clearance 15-35 ml/min/1.73 m2: Maximum dosage 50 mg daily</p><p> Creatinine clearance &lt;15 mL/min/1.73 m2: Maximum dosage 25 mg daily</p><p> Some renal impaired or elderly patients being treated for hypertension may require a lower starting dose of Tenomin 50 mg: 25 mg given as one tablet a day. Patients on hemodialysis should be given 25 mg or 50 mg after each dialysis; this should be done under hospital supervision as marked falls in blood pressure can occur.</p>', 'active', '357_1742411361.jpg', 56, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 00:09:21', '2025-03-20 00:09:21'),
(3580, 'Ranidin', 'Ranitidine Hydrochloride', 3.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '150 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ranidin 150 mg</strong></p><p> Ranidin 150 mg is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Treatment of active duodenal ulcer</li><li>Benign gastric ulcer</li><li>Treatment &amp; prevention of ulcer associated with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory agents</li><li>Post-operative stress ulcer</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</li><li>Gastro-intestinal haemorrhage from stress ulcer in seriously ill patients</li><li>Recurrent haemorrhage in patients with bleeding peptic ulcer</li><li>Before general anesthesia in patients considered to be at risk of acid aspiration, particularly obstetric patients</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> H2 receptor antagonist</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Ranidin 150 mg competitively blocks histamine at H2-receptors of the gastric parietal cells, which inhibits gastric acid secretion. It does not affect pepsin secretion, pentagastrin-stimulated intrinsic factor secretion, or serum gastrin.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ranidin 150 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet &amp; Syrup:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal and gastric ulcer:</strong> 150 mg twice daily (morning and evening) or 300 mg as a single dose at night for 4 to 8 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Reflux oesophagitis:</strong> 150 mg twice daily or 300 mg at bedtime for up to 8 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</strong> 150 mg three times daily, increased if necessary up to 6 g daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Episodic dyspepsia:</strong> 150 mg twice daily or 300 mg at bedtime for up to 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance:</strong> 150 mg at night for preventing recurrences.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (peptic ulcer):</strong> 2-4 mg/kg twice daily, maximum 300 mg daily.</li><li><strong>IV Injection &amp; IV Infusion:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Slow IV injection: 50 mg over at least two minutes, diluted to 20 mL per 50 mg dose, repeated every 6 to 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Intermittent IV infusion: 25 mg per hour for two hours, repeated at 6 to 8-hour intervals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Intramuscular injection: 50 mg (2 mL) every 6 to 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of haemorrhage from stress ulceration:</strong>50 mg slow IV injection followed by continuous IV infusion of 0.125-0.250 mg/kg/hour.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Aspiration syndrome risk:</strong>50 mg IM or slow IV injection 45-60 minutes before general anesthesia.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Children:</strong>2-4 mg/kg twice daily (max 300 mg per day).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Safety of IV injection in children not established.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ranidin 150 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Delayed absorption and increased peak serum concentration with propantheline bromide.</li><li>Minimally inhibits hepatic metabolism of coumarin anticoagulants, theophylline, diazepam, and propranolol.</li><li>May alter absorption of pH-dependent drugs (e.g., ketoconazole, midazolam, glipizide).</li><li>May reduce bioavailability with antacids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Patients hypersensitive to Ranidin 150 mg.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ranidin 150 mg</strong></p><p> Ranidin 150 mg is well tolerated, and side effects are uncommon. Rarely reported effects include:</p><ul><li>Altered bowel habit</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Rash</li><li>Tiredness</li><li>Reversible confusional states</li><li>Headache</li><li>Decreased blood counts</li><li>Muscle or joint pain</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Crosses the placenta but no evidence of harm to the fetus. Should be used only if essential.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Excreted in human breast milk, so caution is advised for nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Should be given in reduced dosage to patients with impaired renal and hepatic function.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ranidin 150 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Highly specific in action; no major issues expected from overdose.</li><li>Symptomatic and supportive therapy should be given as needed.</li><li>Hemodialysis may be used if necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place. Protect from light.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly patients:</strong> Similar ulcer healing rates as younger patients, with no significant difference in adverse effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Slow IV injection:</strong> 50 mg diluted to ≤2.5 mg/mL (e.g., total 20 mL) with NaCl 0.9%, dextrose 5% or 10%, lactated Ringer\'s, Na bicarbonate 5% solution.</li><li><strong>Intermittent IV infusion:</strong> 50 mg diluted to ≤0.5 mg/mL (e.g., total 100 mL) with dextrose 5%, NaCl 0.9%, lactated Ringer\'s, Na bicarbonate 5% solution.</li><li><strong>Continuous IV infusion:</strong> 150 mg diluted in 250 mL of dextrose 5%, NaCl 0.9%, lactated Ringer\'s, Na bicarbonate 5% solution.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome or hypersecretory conditions:</strong> Dilute to ≤2.5 mg/mL with dextrose 5% or NaCl 0.9%, lactated Ringer\'s, Na bicarbonate 5% solution.</li></ul>', 'active', '422_1742438780.jpg', 5, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 07:46:20', '2025-03-20 07:46:20');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3581, 'Ranison', 'Ranitidine Hydrochloride', 10.03, 1, 'IV injection', '5 IV injection', 'IV Injection', '50 mg per 2 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ranison 50 mg/2 ml</strong></p><p> Ranison 50 mg/2 ml is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Treatment of active duodenal ulcer</li><li>Benign gastric ulcer</li><li>Treatment &amp; prevention of ulcer associated with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory agents</li><li>Post-operative stress ulcer</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</li><li>Gastro-intestinal haemorrhage from stress ulcer in seriously ill patients</li><li>Recurrent haemorrhage in patients with bleeding peptic ulcer</li><li>Before general anesthesia in patients considered to be at risk of acid aspiration, particularly obstetric patients</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> H2 receptor antagonist</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Ranison 50 mg/2 ml competitively blocks histamine at H2-receptors of the gastric parietal cells, inhibiting gastric acid secretion. It does not affect pepsin secretion, pentagastrin-stimulated intrinsic factor secretion, or serum gastrin.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ranison 50 mg/2 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet &amp; Syrup:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal and gastric ulcer:</strong> 150 mg twice daily (morning and evening) or 300 mg as a single dose at night for 4 to 8 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Reflux oesophagitis:</strong> 150 mg twice daily or 300 mg at bedtime for up to 8 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</strong> 150 mg three times daily, increased if necessary up to 6 g daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Episodic dyspepsia:</strong> 150 mg twice daily or 300 mg at bedtime for up to 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance:</strong> 150 mg at night for preventing recurrences.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (peptic ulcer):</strong> 2-4 mg/kg twice daily, maximum 300 mg daily.</li><li><strong>IV Injection &amp; IV Infusion:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Slow IV injection: 50 mg over at least two minutes, diluted to 20 mL per 50 mg dose, repeated every 6 to 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Intermittent IV infusion: 25 mg per hour for two hours, repeated at 6 to 8-hour intervals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Intramuscular injection: 50 mg (2 mL) every 6 to 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of haemorrhage from stress ulceration:</strong>50 mg slow IV injection followed by continuous IV infusion of 0.125-0.250 mg/kg/hour.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Aspiration syndrome risk:</strong>50 mg IM or slow IV injection 45-60 minutes before general anesthesia.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Children:</strong>2-4 mg/kg twice daily (max 300 mg per day).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Safety of IV injection in children not established.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ranison 50 mg/2 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Delayed absorption and increased peak serum concentration with propantheline bromide.</li><li>Minimally inhibits hepatic metabolism of coumarin anticoagulants, theophylline, diazepam, and propranolol.</li><li>May alter absorption of pH-dependent drugs (e.g., ketoconazole, midazolam, glipizide).</li><li>May reduce bioavailability with antacids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Patients hypersensitive to Ranison 50 mg/2 ml.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ranison 50 mg/2 ml</strong></p><p> Ranison 50 mg/2 ml is well tolerated, and side effects are uncommon. Rarely reported effects include:</p><ul><li>Altered bowel habit</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Rash</li><li>Tiredness</li><li>Reversible confusional states</li><li>Headache</li><li>Decreased blood counts</li><li>Muscle or joint pain</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Crosses the placenta but no evidence of harm to the fetus. Should be used only if essential.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Excreted in human breast milk, so caution is advised for nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Should be given in reduced dosage to patients with impaired renal and hepatic function.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ranison 50 mg/2 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Highly specific in action; no major issues expected from overdose.</li><li>Symptomatic and supportive therapy should be given as needed.</li><li>Hemodialysis may be used if necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place. Protect from light.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly patients:</strong> Similar ulcer healing rates as younger patients, with no significant difference in adverse effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Slow IV injection:</strong> 50 mg diluted to ≤2.5 mg/mL (e.g., total 20 mL) with NaCl 0.9%, dextrose 5% or 10%, lactated Ringer\'s, Na bicarbonate 5% solution.</li><li><strong>Intermittent IV infusion:</strong> 50 mg diluted to ≤0.5 mg/mL (e.g., total 100 mL) with dextrose 5%, NaCl 0.9%, lactated Ringer\'s, Na bicarbonate 5% solution.</li><li><strong>Continuous IV infusion:</strong> 150 mg diluted in 250 mL of dextrose 5%, NaCl 0.9%, lactated Ringer\'s, Na bicarbonate 5% solution.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome or hypersecretory conditions:</strong> Dilute to ≤2.5 mg/mL with dextrose 5% or NaCl 0.9%, lactated Ringer\'s, Na bicarbonate 5% solution.</li></ul>', 'active', '160_1742439103.jpg', 28, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 07:51:43', '2025-03-20 07:51:43'),
(3582, 'Spar', 'Sparfloxacin', 20.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Spar 200 mg</strong></p><p> Spar 200 mg is indicated for the treatment of adults (≥18 years of age) with the following infections caused by susceptible strains of the designated microorganisms:</p><ul><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia (CAP)</strong> caused by <em>Chlamydia pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Haemophilus parainfluenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis, Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbations of chronic bronchitis (ABECB)</strong> caused by <em>Chlamydia pneumoniae, Enterobacter cloacae, Haemophilus influenzae, Haemophilus parainfluenzae, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Moraxella catarrhalis, Staphylococcus aureus</em>, or <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>.</li><li><strong>Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)</strong></li><li><strong>Acute Maxillary Sinusitis (AMS)</strong></li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong> including gonococcal and nongonococcal urethritis, chancroid, and other sexually transmitted diseases.</li><li><strong>Bacterial prostatitis</strong></li><li><strong>Bacterial diarrhea</strong></li><li><strong>Osteomyelitis</strong></li><li><strong>Other uses</strong> include treatment of tuberculosis in combination with rifampicin and isoniazid, and treatment of leprosy in combination with standard drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> 4-Quinolone preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Spar 200 mg is a synthetic, broad-spectrum antibacterial agent from the difluoroquinolone family. It has been reported to be more active in vitro than ciprofloxacin against some organisms, including <em>staphylococci</em> and <em>Mycobacteria</em>, and has a much longer plasma half-life (16 hours). Spar 200 mg inhibits the supercoiling activity of DNA gyrase, an enzyme essential for DNA replication, thus promoting the breakage of DNA structures. It has activity against <em>S. pneumoniae, S. aureus, H. influenzae, K. pneumoniae, M. catarrhalis</em>, and <em>Mycobacterium spp.</em></p><p><strong>Dosage of Spar 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>The recommended daily dose in patients with normal renal function is two 200 mg tablets taken on the first day as a loading dose. Thereafter, one 200 mg tablet should be taken every 24 hours for a total of 10 days of therapy (11 tablets).</li><li>In patients with renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;50 mL/min), two 200 mg tablets should be taken on the first day as a loading dose, followed by one 200-mg tablet every 48 hours for a total of 9 days of therapy (6 tablets).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Spar 200 mg</strong></p><p> Spar 200 mg can be taken with or without food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Spar 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Aluminium and Magnesium cations in antacids and sucralfate form chelation complexes with Spar 200 mg.</li><li>Concomitant use with medications known to increase the QTc interval and/or Torsade de Pointes (e.g., terfenadine).</li><li>Spar 200 mg does not interact with theophylline, caffeine, warfarin, or cimetidine.</li><li>Probenecid does not alter the pharmacokinetics of Spar 200 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient of Spar 200 mg.</li><li>Pregnancy and lactation.</li><li>Glucose-6-Phosphate Dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency.</li><li>History of Achilles tendinitis following fluoroquinolone use.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Spar 200 mg</strong></p><p> Most side effects are mild to moderate and transient. Commonly reported effects include:</p><ul><li>Photosensitivity reaction</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Headache</li><li>Dyspepsia</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Insomnia</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Pruritus</li><li>Taste perversion</li><li>QTc interval prolongation</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Vasodilation</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Spar 200 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Special warnings:</strong> Moderate to severe phototoxic reactions may occur in patients exposed to direct or indirect sunlight or artificial UV light (e.g., sunlamps). Patients should discontinue Spar 200 mg at the first signs of phototoxicity, such as:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sensation of skin burning</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Swelling</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rash</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Itching</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dermatitis</li><li>Increases in QTc interval may occur.</li><li>The safety and effectiveness of Spar 200 mg in children, adolescents (&lt;18 years), pregnant women, and lactating women have not been established.</li><li>Adequate hydration should be maintained to prevent highly concentrated urine.</li><li>Caution should be exercised in patients with renal insufficiency.</li><li>Avoid co-administration with medications that prolong the QTc interval (e.g., erythromycin, terfenadine).</li><li>Excessive sunlight exposure should be avoided.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep out of reach of children. Store below 30°C, away from heat and direct light.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children:</strong> Safety and effectiveness have not been established in patients below 18 years.</li><li><strong>Geriatric use:</strong> Pharmacokinetics are not altered in elderly patients with normal renal function.</li><li><strong>Paediatric use:</strong> Not studied.</li><li><strong>Gender:</strong> No significant pharmacokinetic differences between men and women.</li><li><strong>Renal insufficiency:</strong> In patients with renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;50 mL/min), plasma concentrations are approximately double those observed in normal renal function patients.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment:</strong> Pharmacokinetics are not altered in patients with mild or moderate hepatic impairment without cholestasis.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> 4-Quinolone preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Spar 200 mg is a broad-spectrum antibacterial agent that inhibits DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV, effectively killing bacteria that infect the respiratory tract and lungs. It has shown superior efficacy against <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Staphylococcus aureus, Mycobacteria</em>, and <em>Chlamydia spp.</em></p><p><strong>Absorption</strong></p><ul><li>Well absorbed orally with a bioavailability of 92%.</li><li>Maximum plasma concentration of a 400 mg dose is ~1.3 (±0.2) µg/mL.</li><li>Peak plasma levels occur ~4 hours post-administration.</li><li>Antacids containing magnesium or aluminum reduce bioavailability by up to 50%.</li></ul><p><strong>Distribution</strong></p><ul><li>Large distribution volume (Vdss ~3.9 L/kg).</li><li>Low plasma protein binding (~45%).</li><li>High penetration into respiratory tissues and alveolar macrophages.</li></ul><p><strong>Metabolism</strong></p><ul><li>Primarily metabolized in the liver via glucuronidation.</li><li>Does not interact with cytochrome P450 enzymes.</li></ul><p><strong>Excretion</strong></p><ul><li>Excreted equally in feces (50%) and urine (50%).</li><li>Approximately 10% excreted unchanged in urine.</li></ul>', 'active', '1699_1742439288.jpg', 64, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 07:54:48', '2025-03-20 07:54:48'),
(3583, 'Zoventa DS', 'Ceftibuten Dihydrate', 800.00, 1, 'Bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '180 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zoventa DS 180 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Ceftibuten is indicated for the treatment of individuals with mild-to-moderate infections caused by susceptible strains of the designated microorganisms in the specific conditions listed below.</p><ul><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Exacerbations of Chronic Bronchitis:</strong> Due to <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including β-lactamase producing strains), <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (including β-lactamase producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> (penicillin-susceptible strains only).</li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media:</strong> Due to <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including β-lactamase producing strains), <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (including β-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Pharyngitis and Tonsillitis:</strong> Due to <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> As with most beta-lactam antibiotics, the bactericidal activity of ceftibuten results from the inhibition of bacterial cell wall synthesis. Due to its chemical structure, ceftibuten is highly stable to beta-lactamases. Many beta-lactamase-producing microorganisms, which are resistant to penicillins or other cephalosporins, may be inhibited by ceftibuten.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zoventa DS 180 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> As with other oral antibiotics, duration of treatment generally ranges from 5-10 days. For treatment of infections due to <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, a therapeutic dose of ceftibuten should be administered for at least 10 days.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> Recommended dose: 400 mg daily. Ceftibuten capsules may be taken without regard to mealtime. For treatment in the following indications, this may be administered as 400 mg once daily:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bacterial sinusitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bronchitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute exacerbations of chronic bronchitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Complicated or uncomplicated urinary tract infections</li><li><strong>Community-Acquired Pneumonia:</strong> 200 mg every 12 hrs.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> Recommended dose: 9 mg/kg/day (maximum of 400 mg daily) of the oral suspension. This may be administered as a single daily dose for:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis with or without tonsillitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute otitis media with effusion</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Complicated or uncomplicated urinary tract infections</li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Enteritis in Children:</strong> The total daily dosage may be administered in two divided doses of 4.5 mg/kg every 12 hrs.</li><li><strong>Children &gt;45 kg or &gt;10 years:</strong> May receive the recommended adult dose.</li><li><strong>Ceftibuten suspension:</strong> Should be taken approximately 1 or 2 hrs before or after mealtime. Shake bottle well before measuring each dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Zoventa DS 180 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Otitis MediaAdult:</strong> 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric (&gt;6 months):</strong> 9 mg/kg/day (max 400 mg) every 24 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tonsillitis/PharyngitisAdult:</strong> 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric (&gt;6 months):</strong> 9 mg/kg/day (max 400 mg) every 24 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>BronchitisAdult:</strong> 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric (&gt;12 years):</strong> 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>CystitisAdult:</strong> 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric (&gt;12 years):</strong> 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>PneumoniaAdult:</strong> 200 mg orally every 12 hours for 7 to 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric (&gt;12 years):</strong> 200 mg orally every 12 hours for 7 to 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>SinusitisAdult:</strong> 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 10 to 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric (&gt;12 years):</strong> 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 10 to 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Urinary Tract InfectionAdult:</strong> 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric (&gt;6 months):</strong> 9 mg/kg/day (max 400 mg) every 24 hours for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zoventa DS 180 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Theophylline &amp; antacid do not alter the pharmacokinetic profile of ceftibuten. Ranitidine increases the Cmax &amp; AUC of ceftibuten.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Ceftibuten is contraindicated in patients with known allergy to the cephalosporin group of antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zoventa DS 180 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Nausea, headache, diarrhea, dyspepsia, dizziness, abdominal pain, vomiting.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Ceftibuten has been assigned to pregnancy category B by the FDA. There are no controlled data in human pregnancy. Ceftibuten should be used during pregnancy only when clearly needed. It is acceptable to use during breastfeeding as single maternal doses up to 200 mg produce low levels in milk.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Prolonged treatment may result in the emergence of resistant organisms. The dose may require adjustment in patients with renal insufficiency. Use with caution in individuals with a history of gastrointestinal disease, particularly colitis.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zoventa DS 180 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Overdose symptoms may include seizures.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 25°C, protected from light and moisture. After reconstitution, suspension may be used for 14 days while stored at 2° to 8°C. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children:</strong> Safety and efficacy in infants &lt;6 months not established.</li><li><strong>Adult Patients with Renal Impairment:</strong> Dosage modifications required for creatinine clearance &lt;50 mL/min.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>CrCl 5 to 29 ml/min:</em> 2.25 mg/kg or 100 mg orally once a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>CrCl 30 to 49 ml/min:</em> 4.5 mg/kg or 200 mg orally once a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>CrCl 5-29 ml/min:</em> 400 mg every 4 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Hemodialysis patients:</em> 400 mg may be administered at the end of each session.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Ceftibuten is a semi-synthetic cephalosporin antibiotic that exerts its bactericidal action by binding to essential target proteins of the bacterial cell wall, leading to inhibition of cell-wall synthesis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category B. Use only if the benefit outweighs the risk. Caution should be exercised when administered to nursing women.</p>', 'active', '1703_1742439483.jpg', 1, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 07:58:03', '2025-03-20 07:58:03'),
(3584, 'Zymarin', 'Gatifloxacin', 140.00, 1, 'Drop', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '0.5%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zymarin 0.5%</strong></p><p>Zymarin 0.5% is indicated in the following infections: Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis due to Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Haemophilus parainfluenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis, or Staphylococcus aureus. Acute sinusitis due to Streptococcus pneumoniae or Haemophilus influenzae. Community-acquired pneumonia due to Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Haemophilus parainfluenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis, Staphylococcus aureus, Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Chlamydia pneumoniae, or Legionella pneumophila. Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infections (i.e., simple abscesses, furuncles, folliculitis, wound infections, and cellulitis) due to Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pyogenes. Uncomplicated urinary tract infections due to Escherichia coli, Klebsiella pneumoniae, or Proteus mirabilis. Complicated urinary tract infections due to Escherichia coli, Klebsiella pneumoniae, or Proteus mirabilis. Pyelonephritis due to Escherichia coli. Uncomplicated urethral and cervical gonorrhea due to Neisseria gonorrhoeae. Acute, uncomplicated rectal infections in women due to Neisseria gonorrhoeae.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>4-Quinolone preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zymarin 0.5% is a fluoroquinolone antibacterial which acts by inhibiting DNA synthesis in susceptible organisms via inhibition of both DNA gyrase (essential for bacterial reproduction) and topoisomerase IV (essential during bacterial cell division).</p><p><strong>Dosage of Zymarin 0.5%</strong></p><ul><li>Acute Bacterial Exacerbation of Chronic Bronchitis: 400 mg for 5 days</li><li>Acute Sinusitis: 400 mg for 10 days</li><li>Community-acquired Pneumonia: 400 mg for 7-14 days</li><li>Uncomplicated Skin and Skin Structure Infections: 400 mg for 7-10 days</li><li>Uncomplicated Urinary Tract Infections: 400 mg Single Dose or 200 mg for 3 days</li><li>Complicated Urinary Tract Infections: 400 mg for 7-10 days</li><li>Acute Pyelonephritis: 400 mg for 7-10 days</li><li>Uncomplicated Urethral Gonorrhea in Men: 400 mg Single dose</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zymarin 0.5%</strong></p><p>Zymarin 0.5% can be administered without regard to food.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Zymarin 0.5% is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Zymarin 0.5%, quinolone antimicrobial agents, or any other components of this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zymarin 0.5%</strong></p><p>Zymarin 0.5% is generally well tolerated. The most common side effects that can occur while taking this drug are usually mild and include nausea, vomiting, stomach pain, diarrhea, dizziness, and headache.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Zymarin 0.5% should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is not known whether Zymarin 0.5% is excreted in human milk. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk, caution should be exercised when Zymarin 0.5% is administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Zymarin 0.5% should be administered with caution in the presence of renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zymarin 0.5%</strong></p><p>Zymarin 0.5% exhibits a low potential for acute toxicity in animal studies. In the event of acute oral overdose, the stomach should be emptied by inducing vomiting or by gastric lavage. The patient should be carefully observed (including ECG monitoring) and given symptomatic and supportive treatment. Adequate hydration should be maintained.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Impaired Patients:</strong>Creatinine Clearance 40 mL/min: 400 mg every day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Creatinine Clearance &lt; 40 mL/min: 400 mg 200 mg every day.</li><li>Dialysis Patients:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Hemodialysis: 400 mg 200 mg every day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Continuous peritoneal dialysis: 400 mg 200 mg every day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong>The safety and effectiveness of Zymarin 0.5% in pediatric populations (&lt;18 years of age) have not been established.</li></ul>', 'active', '96_1742439750.jpg', 53, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 08:02:30', '2025-03-21 21:12:11'),
(3585, 'Xenim', 'Cefepime Hydrochloride', 550.00, 1, 'x 1 gm vial', '1 gm vial', 'IV Injection', '1 gm/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xenim 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Cefepime is indicated for the treatment of the following infections caused by susceptible strains of microorganisms:</p><ul><li><strong>Pneumonia (moderate to severe):</strong> Caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> (including cases associated with concurrent bacteremia), <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Enterobacter</em> species.</li><li><strong>Febrile Neutropenia:</strong> Cefepime as monotherapy is indicated for empiric treatment of febrile neutropenic patients. In high-risk patients (recent bone marrow transplantation, hypotension, hematologic malignancy, or severe/prolonged neutropenia), monotherapy may not be appropriate.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated and Complicated Urinary Tract Infections (including pyelonephritis):</strong> Caused by <em>Escherichia coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em> (severe cases) or <em>E. coli, K. pneumoniae, Proteus mirabilis</em> (mild to moderate cases), including cases with concurrent bacteremia.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Skin and Skin Structure Infections:</strong> Caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (methicillin-susceptible) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections (used with metronidazole):</strong> Caused by <em>E. coli, viridans group streptococci, P. aeruginosa, K. pneumoniae, Enterobacter</em> species, or <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Fourth-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cephalosporins are bactericidal and work like other beta-lactam antibiotics (e.g., penicillins). They disrupt bacterial cell wall synthesis by inhibiting the final transpeptidation step, facilitated by transpeptidases known as penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs).</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xenim 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Cefepime should be administered intravenously over approximately 30 minutes.</p><ul><li><strong>Moderate to Severe Pneumonia:</strong> 1-2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Empiric therapy for febrile neutropenic patients:</strong> 2 gm IV every 8 hours for 7 days (or until neutropenia resolves).</li><li><strong>Mild to Moderate Urinary Tract Infections:</strong> 0.5-1 gm IV/IM every 12 hours for 7-10 days.</li><li><strong>Severe Urinary Tract Infections:</strong> 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Skin and Skin Structure Infections:</strong> 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections (with metronidazole):</strong> 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 7-10 days.</li></ul><p><em>Note:</em></p><ul><li><em>IM administration is only for mild to moderate uncomplicated or complicated UTIs due to E. coli when IM is preferred.</em></li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xenim 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Caution is advised when using high-dose aminoglycosides with cefepime due to increased nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity risks. Concomitant use with potent diuretics (e.g., furosemide) may enhance nephrotoxicity.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Cefepime is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to cefepime, cephalosporins, penicillin, or other beta-lactam antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xenim 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Common side effects include hypersensitivity reactions, rash, diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and, in rare cases, encephalopathy, seizures, or myoclonus.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> No adequate studies in pregnant women. Use only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Cefepime is excreted in breast milk in low concentrations. Caution is advised when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use only when a bacterial infection is confirmed or strongly suspected to prevent antibiotic resistance.</li><li>Prolonged use may lead to overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms.</li><li>Monitor prothrombin time in at-risk patients (renal/hepatic impairment, poor nutrition, prolonged therapy).</li><li>Use with caution in patients with a history of gastrointestinal disease, particularly colitis.</li><li>Arginine in cefepime formulations may transiently elevate serum potassium and alter glucose metabolism.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xenim 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdose include encephalopathy, hallucinations, stupor, coma, myoclonus, seizures, and neuromuscular excitability. In renal impairment, hemodialysis is recommended to aid drug removal.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Do not use beyond the expiry date.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Dispense only with a registered physician\'s prescription.</li></ul><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use (2 months - 16 years):</strong> Maximum dose should not exceed the adult dose. Usual dosage: 50 mg/kg every 12 hours (every 8 hours for febrile neutropenia).</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Increased risk of severe adverse effects in patients with renal impairment. Monitor renal function closely.</li><li><strong>Impaired Hepatic Function:</strong> No dose adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Impaired Renal Function:</strong> Dose should be adjusted if creatinine clearance is &lt;60 ml/min.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Intravenous:</strong> Compatible with sterile water, 0.9% sodium chloride, 5% dextrose, lactated Ringer’s, and more.</li><li><strong>Intramuscular:</strong> Compatible with sterile water, 0.9% sodium chloride, 5% dextrose, or lidocaine hydrochloride.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Fourth-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Cephalosporins disrupt bacterial cell wall synthesis by inhibiting the final transpeptidation step, which is facilitated by PBPs.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category B:</strong> Use only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Low excretion in breast milk. Use with caution.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children (2 months - 16 years):</strong> Dosage should not exceed adult recommendations.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Higher risk of encephalopathy, seizures, and myoclonus in patients with renal insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Impaired Hepatic Function:</strong> No adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Impaired Renal Function:</strong> Dose adjustment required if creatinine clearance &lt;60 ml/min.</li></ul><p>This version keeps the content well-structured and easier to read while using normal bold text for headings as per your preference.</p>', 'active', '1823_1742439993.jpg', 7, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 08:06:33', '2025-05-07 19:56:21'),
(3586, 'Tynium', 'Tiemonium Methylsulfate', 88.00, 1, 'Bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '10 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Tynium 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Tynium 10 mg/5 ml is an antispasmodic drug that reduces muscle spasms of the intestine, biliary system, bladder, and uterus. It is used in the symptomatic treatment of pain related to functional disorders of the digestive tract and biliary system. It is also indicated for the treatment of spasm and pain in urological and gynecological diseases.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anticholinergics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Tynium 10 mg/5 ml is a competitive antagonist of Acetylcholine and Histamine and strengthens the calcium bond with membrane phospholipids and proteins. This inhibits intracellular contractile proteins of visceral cells, which causes inhibition of visceral spasm and pain.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Tynium 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet/Syrup:Adults:</strong> Usual dose is 2-6 tablets or 3-9 teaspoonfuls of syrup daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong> 3 ml/kg or 6 mg/kg body weight daily in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> 1 Tynium 10 mg/5 ml Injection 3 times daily, through intravenous route slowly or intramuscular route.</li><li><strong>Suppository:</strong> 20 mg Tynium 10 mg/5 ml suppository two or three times daily, through the rectal route.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Tynium 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet/Syrup:Adults:</strong> Usual dose is 2-6 tablets or 3-9 teaspoonfuls of syrup daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong> 3 ml/kg or 6 mg/kg body weight daily in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> 1 Tynium 10 mg/5 ml Injection 3 times daily, through intravenous route slowly or intramuscular route.</li><li><strong>Suppository:</strong> 20 mg Tynium 10 mg/5 ml suppository two or three times daily, through the rectal route.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Tynium 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Tiemonium methylsulphate tablets should not be used with other drugs without prior consultation with a registered physician to avoid possible drug interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> It should not be used in urethroprostatic disorders involving a risk of urine retention. It is contraindicated in patients at risk of angle-closure glaucoma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Tynium 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Tynium 10 mg/5 ml may have the risk of hypotension and tachycardia in certain individuals.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The results of animal studies of Tynium 10 mg/5 ml did not reveal any teratogenic effects. No deformities have been reported with normal use. In the absence of sufficient data, caution should be exercised for nursing mothers, although no problems have been reported with normal use.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Caution should be taken during the treatment of patients with disorders of the prostate. Caution should also be taken in cases of chronic bronchitis, coronary insufficiency, ambient hyperthermia, renal and hepatic insufficiency. The risks of visual disturbances can make it dangerous to drive or operate machinery.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Tynium 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> There is no available data regarding the overdose of Tiemonium methylsulphate tablets.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness of Tiemonium methylsulphate in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric use:</strong> Efficacy and safety were maintained with increasing age.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Anticholinergics</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Tynium 10 mg/5 ml is a competitive antagonist of Acetylcholine and Histamine and strengthens the calcium bond with membrane phospholipids and proteins. This inhibits intracellular contractile proteins of visceral cells, which causes inhibition of visceral spasm and pain.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The results of animal studies of Tynium 10 mg/5 ml did not reveal any teratogenic effects. No deformities have been reported with normal use. In the absence of sufficient data, caution should be exercised for nursing mothers, although no problems have been reported with normal use.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness of Tiemonium methylsulphate in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric use:</strong> Efficacy and safety were maintained with increasing age.</li></ul>', 'active', '866_1742440352.jpg', 44, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 08:12:32', '2025-03-20 08:12:32'),
(3587, 'Tada', 'Tadalafil', 35.00, 5, 'Tablet', '5 Tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Tada 10 mg</strong></p><p> Tada 10 mg is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Erectile Dysfunction (ED)</li><li>Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH)</li><li>Both Erectile Dysfunction and signs and symptoms of Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for Erectile Dysfunction</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Tada 10 mg is a selective phosphodiesterase type 5 (PDE5) inhibitor. Inhibition of PDE5 increases cGMP in smooth muscle cells. cGMP causes smooth muscle relaxation and increased blood flow into the corpus cavernosum, causing penile erection. PDE5 is also present in smooth muscles of the prostate and bladder wall. Inhibiting PDE5 increases cGMP concentrations, leading to relaxation of smooth muscle in the prostate and bladder. Smooth muscle relaxation may improve blood flow to the urinary tract and widen the opening of the bladder neck, resulting in improved voiding.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Tada 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Erectile Dysfunction:</strong> For most patients, the recommended starting dose is 10 mg. The dose may be increased to 20 mg or decreased to 5 mg based on requirement. The maximum dosing frequency is once daily. Tada 10 mg is effective for up to 36 hours.</li><li><strong>Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia:</strong> The recommended dose is 5 mg taken at the same time every day.</li><li><strong>Combined Erectile Dysfunction and Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia:</strong> The recommended dose is 5 mg at the same time every day.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Tada 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Erectile Dysfunction:</strong> For most patients, the recommended starting dose is 10 mg. The dose may be increased to 20 mg or decreased to 5 mg based on requirement. The maximum dosing frequency is once daily. Tada 10 mg is effective for up to 36 hours.</li><li><strong>Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia:</strong> The recommended dose is 5 mg taken at the same time every day.</li><li><strong>Combined Erectile Dysfunction and Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia:</strong> The recommended dose is 5 mg at the same time every day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Tada 10 mg</strong></p><p> May interact with:</p><ul><li>Nitrates (e.g., Isosorbide, Nitroglycerin)</li><li>Alpha-adrenergic blockers</li><li>Antihypertensives</li><li>Alcohol</li><li>Antacids (Magnesium hydroxide/Aluminum hydroxide)</li><li>Ketoconazole</li><li>Ritonavir</li><li>Erythromycin</li><li>Itraconazole</li><li>Grapefruit juice</li><li>Other HIV protease inhibitors</li><li>Rifampin</li><li>Carbamazepine</li><li>Phenytoin</li><li>Phenobarbital</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Use of Nitrates (e.g., Nitroglycerine, Isosorbide) as it may increase hypotensive effects of Nitrates</li><li>Hypersensitivity reactions to Tada 10 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Tada 10 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Dyspepsia</li><li>Back pain</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Nasopharyngitis</li><li>Nasal congestion</li></ul><p>Rare side effects:</p><ul><li>Change in color vision</li><li>Sudden vision loss</li><li>Hearing loss</li><li>Stevens-Johnson Syndrome</li><li>Exfoliative dermatitis</li><li>Angina</li><li>Stroke</li><li>Myocardial infarction</li><li>Severe hypotension</li><li>Tachycardia</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Tada 10 mg has been assigned to pregnancy category B by the USFDA. Tada 10 mg is only recommended for use during pregnancy when the benefit outweighs the risk. There are no data on the excretion of Tada 10 mg in human milk. Caution should be used when administering Tada 10 mg to nursing women.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Precaution should be taken in patients with:</p><ul><li>Angina</li><li>Renal impairment</li><li>Hepatic impairment</li><li>Bleeding disorders</li><li>Concomitant use of Nitrates, Alpha Blockers, Alcohol, CYP3A4 Inhibitors (e.g., Ritonavir, Ketoconazole, Itraconazole), or other PDE5 inhibitors</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Drugs for Erectile Dysfunction</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Tada 10 mg is a selective phosphodiesterase type 5 (PDE5) inhibitor. Inhibition of PDE5 increases cGMP in smooth muscle cells. cGMP causes smooth muscle relaxation and increased blood flow into the corpus cavernosum, causing penile erection. PDE5 is also present in smooth muscles of the prostate and bladder wall. Inhibiting PDE5 increases cGMP concentrations, leading to relaxation of smooth muscle in the prostate and bladder. Smooth muscle relaxation may improve blood flow to the urinary tract and widen the opening of the bladder neck, resulting in improved voiding.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Tada 10 mg has been assigned to pregnancy category B by the USFDA. Tada 10 mg is only recommended for use during pregnancy when the benefit outweighs the risk. There are no data on the excretion of Tada 10 mg in human milk. Caution should be used when administering Tada 10 mg to nursing women.</p>', 'active', '1269_1742440701.jpg', 53, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 08:18:21', '2025-03-20 08:18:21');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3588, 'Staxim', 'Cefuroxime Axetil', 170.00, 1, 'Bottle', '70 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '125 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Staxim 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> It is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive bacteria.</p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Maxillary Sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non-beta-lactamase producing strains).</li><li><strong>Lower Respiratory Tract Infections</strong> including pneumonia caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp., Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes, E. coli</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Exacerbation of Chronic Bronchitis and Secondary Bacterial Infections of Acute Bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains) or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains).</li><li><strong>Skin and Skin-Structure Infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>.</li><li><strong>Bone and Joint Infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Gonorrhoea</strong> caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase-producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>.</li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease (erythema migrans)</strong> caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime is a well-characterized and effective antibacterial agent with broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against a wide range of common pathogens, including β-lactamase-producing strains. It has good stability to bacterial β-lactamase and is active against many ampicillin-resistant and amoxicillin-resistant strains.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Staxim 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet or Suspension (Adolescents and Adults - 13 years and older):</strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis: 250 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute Bacterial Maxillary Sinusitis: 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute Bacterial Exacerbation of Chronic Bronchitis: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Secondary Bacterial Infections of Acute Bronchitis: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated Skin and Skin Structure Infections: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated Urinary Tract Infections: 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated Gonorrhoea: 1000 mg Single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Community Acquired Pneumonia: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">MDR Typhoid Fever: 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Early Lyme Disease: 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Paediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years):</strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis: 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute Otitis Media: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute Bacterial Maxillary Sinusitis: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Impetigo: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Parenteral (Adults and Children Above 3 Months):</strong>750 mg three times daily by IM or IV injection. In severe infections, up to 1.5 g three times daily by IV injection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Surgical prophylaxis: 1.5 g IV at induction of anesthesia; up to 3 further doses of 750 mg IV/IM every 8 hours for high-risk procedures.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pneumonia: 1.5 g IV twice daily for 2-3 days, followed by 500 mg twice daily (oral) for 7-10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute Exacerbations of Chronic Bronchitis: 750 mg twice daily (IM or IV) for 2-3 days, followed by 500 mg twice daily (oral) for 5-10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gonorrhoea: 1.5 g as a single dose (2 x 750 mg injections IM at different sites).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Meningitis: Adults: 3 g IV three times daily. Children: 200-240 mg/kg/day IV in 3-4 divided doses, reduced to 100 mg/kg/day after 3 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Bone and Joint Infections: Adults: 1.5 g IV four times daily. Children: 150 mg/kg/day (not exceeding adult dose) in equally divided doses every 8 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Staxim 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Use freshly reconstituted solution. Maintains potency for at least 24 hours at room temperature or 48 hours at 5°C.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Staxim 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> No potentially hazardous interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime is contraindicated in patients with known allergy to cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Staxim 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Adverse effects are infrequent and mild, including rashes and gastrointestinal disturbances. Prolonged use may result in overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms, such as <em>Candida</em>.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> US FDA pregnancy category B. Use in pregnancy only if clearly needed. Excreted in human milk; caution advised when administering to nursing women.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime should be used with caution in patients receiving concurrent potent diuretics or those with a history of colitis. Cephalosporins may be safely given to penicillin-hypersensitive patients, but cross-reactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C in a cool, dry place away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>750 mg IM Injection:</strong> Add 3 ml water for injection and shake gently.</li><li><strong>750 mg IV Injection:</strong> Add 8 ml water for injection and shake gently. Inject slowly over 3-5 minutes.</li><li><strong>1.5 g IV Injection:</strong> Add 16 ml water for injection and shake gently. Inject slowly over 3-5 minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime is a broad-spectrum bactericidal agent with activity against β-lactamase-producing strains, stable against bacterial β-lactamase, and active against ampicillin- and amoxicillin-resistant strains.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> US FDA pregnancy category B. Use in pregnancy only if necessary. Excreted in human milk; use with caution in nursing women.</p>', 'active', '323_1742440909.jpg', 33, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 08:21:49', '2025-03-20 08:21:49'),
(3589, 'Seropin', 'Quetiapine Fumarate', 3.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Seropin 25 mg</strong></p><p> Quetiapine is indicated for the treatment of acute and chronic psychoses, including schizophrenia, Bipolar Disorder including: treatment of manic episodes satisfying DSM-IV criteria for mania associated with bipolar disorder, treatment of depressive episodes associated with bipolar disorder, maintenance treatment of bipolar I disorder, in combination with a mood stabilizer, for the prevention of recurrence of manic, depressive, or mixed episodes.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Atypical neuroleptic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Quetiapine is a dibenzothiazepine atypical antipsychotic agent. Its clinical antipsychotic properties and low extrapyramidal side effects are mediated through a combination of D2 and 5-HT2 receptor antagonism. It has high affinity at histaminergic H1 and adrenergic α1 receptors, moderate affinity at adrenergic α2 receptors, and moderate to high affinity at several antimuscarinic receptors.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Seropin 25 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Acute and chronic psychoses, including schizophrenia</strong>: Quetiapine should be administered twice daily, with or without food. The total daily dose for the first four days of therapy is 50 mg (Day 1), 100 mg (Day 2), 200 mg (Day 3), and 300 mg (Day 4). From Day 4 onwards, the dose should be titrated to the usual effective dose range of 300-450 mg/day. However, this may be adjusted depending on the clinical response and tolerability of the individual patient, within the range of 150 to 750 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Manic episodes associated with bipolar disorder</strong>: Quetiapine should be administered twice daily, with or without food. The total daily dose for the first four days of therapy is 100 mg (Day 1), 200 mg (Day 2), 300 mg (Day 3), and 400 mg (Day 4). Further dosage adjustments up to 800 mg/day by Day 6 should be in increments of no greater than 200 mg/day. The dose may be adjusted depending on clinical response &amp; tolerability of the individual patient, within the range of 200 to 800 mg/day. The usual effective dose is in the range of 400 to 800 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Depressive episodes associated with bipolar disorder</strong>: Quetiapine should be administered once daily at bedtime, with or without food. The usual dose is 300 mg/day. The daily dose for the first four days of therapy is 50 mg (Day 1), 100 mg (Day 2), 200 mg (Day 3), and 300 mg (Day 4). Quetiapine can be titrated to 400 mg on Day 5 and up to 600 mg by Day 8. Antidepressant efficacy was demonstrated with Quetiapine at 300 mg and 600 mg, however, no additional benefit was seen in the 600 mg group during short-term treatment.</li><li><strong>Maintenance treatment of bipolar I disorder in combination with mood stabilizers</strong>: Patients who have responded to Quetiapine in combination therapy with a mood stabilizer for acute treatment of bipolar disorder should continue on Quetiapine therapy at the same dose. Quetiapine dose can be re-adjusted depending on clinical response and tolerability of the individual patient. Efficacy was demonstrated with Quetiapine (administered twice daily totaling 400 mg to 800 mg a day) as combination therapy with a mood stabilizer.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Seropin 25 mg</strong></p><p> Caution should be exercised when Quetiapine is used concomitantly with medicines known to cause electrolyte imbalance or to increase QT interval. Co-administration of Quetiapine and thioridazine or carbamazepine caused increases in the clearance of Quetiapine. Co-administration of Quetiapine with another microsomal enzyme inducer, phenytoin, also caused increases in the clearance of Quetiapine.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Quetiapine is contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to it.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Seropin 25 mg</strong></p><p> The most commonly reported Adverse Drug Reactions (ADRs) with Quetiapine are somnolence, dizziness, dry mouth, withdrawal (discontinuation) symptoms, elevations in serum triglyceride levels, elevations in total cholesterol (predominantly LDL cholesterol), decreases in HDL cholesterol, weight gain, decreased haemoglobin, and extrapyramidal symptoms.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The safety and efficacy of Quetiapine during human pregnancy have not been established. Therefore, Quetiapine should only be used during pregnancy if the benefits justify the potential risks, and the administered dose and duration of treatment should be as low and as short as possible. The degree to which Quetiapine is excreted into human milk is unknown. Women who are breastfeeding should therefore be advised to avoid breastfeeding while taking Quetiapine.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, tardive dyskinesia, hypotension and syncope, especially during the initial dose titration period. Conduct eye examinations prior to or shortly after starting Quetiapine and at 6-month intervals thereafter; discontinue the drug if clinically significant lens changes are observed. History of seizures, hypothyroidism, hyperprolactinemia, antiemetic effect, suicide. Use with great caution in moderate or severe hepatic impairments, renal impairment, cardiovascular disease, disruption of body temperature regulation, hyperglycemia, and lactation (avoid breastfeeding).</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Seropin 25 mg</strong></p><p> In clinical trials, survival has been reported in acute overdoses of up to 30 grams of Quetiapine. There is no specific antidote to Quetiapine. In cases of severe intoxication, the possibility of multiple drug involvement should be considered, and intensive care procedures are recommended, including establishing and maintaining a patent airway, ensuring adequate oxygenation and ventilation, and monitoring and support of the cardiovascular system. In cases of Quetiapine overdose, refractory hypotension should be treated with appropriate measures such as intravenous fluids and/or sympathomimetic agents (adrenaline and dopamine should be avoided, since beta stimulation may worsen hypotension in the setting of Quetiapine-induced alpha blockade). Close medical supervision and monitoring should be continued until the patient recovers.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use</strong>: Quetiapine is not indicated for use in children and adolescents below 18 years of age.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: As with other antipsychotics, Quetiapine should be used with caution in the elderly, especially during the initial dosing period. The rate of dose titration may need to be slower, and the daily therapeutic dose lower, than that used in younger patients, depending on the clinical response and tolerability of the individual patient. The mean plasma clearance of Quetiapine was reduced by 30% to 50% in elderly subjects when compared with younger patients.</li></ul>', 'active', '1382_1742441112.jpg', 53, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 08:25:12', '2025-03-20 08:25:12'),
(3590, 'Roxarel', 'Rivaroxaban', 35.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '15 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Roxarel 15 mg</strong></p><p> Roxarel 15 mg 2.5 mg: For the prevention of atherothrombotic events in adult patients after an Acute Coronary Syndrome (ACS) with elevated cardiac biomarkers (Troponin or CK-MB). It is co-administered with Aspirin alone or with Aspirin plus Clopidogrel or Tidopidine.</p><p> Roxarel 15 mg 10-20 mg: To reduce the risk of stroke and systemic embolism in patients with nonvalvular atrial fibrillation, deep vein thrombosis (DVT) &amp; pulmonary embolism (PE) and reduction in the risk of recurrence of DVT and of PE. For the prophylaxis of DVT, which may lead to PE in patients undergoing knee or hip replacement surgery.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Oral Anti-coagulants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Roxarel 15 mg is a highly selective direct factor Xa inhibitor. Inhibition of factor Xa interrupts the intrinsic and extrinsic pathway of the blood coagulation cascade, inhibiting thrombin formation. Roxarel 15 mg does not inhibit thrombin (activated factor II) and no effects on platelets have been demonstrated.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Roxarel 15 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Roxarel 15 mg 2.5 mg</strong>: The recommended dose is 2.5 mg twice daily. Patients should also take a daily dose of 75-100 mg Aspirin or a daily dose of 75-100 mg Aspirin in addition to either a daily dose of 75 mg clopidogrel or a standard daily dose of ticlopidine.</li><li><strong>Roxarel 15 mg 10-20 mg</strong>:</li><li><strong>Nonvalvular Atrial Fibrillation</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">For patients with Creatinine Clearance &gt;50 mL/min: 20 mg orally, once daily with the evening meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For patients with Creatinine Clearance 15-50 mL/min: 15 mg orally, once daily with the evening meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of DVT &amp; PE</strong>: 15 mg orally twice daily with food for the first 21 days for the initial treatment of acute DVT or PE. After the initial treatment period, 20 mg orally once daily with food for the remaining treatment.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention in the risk of recurrence of DVT and of PE</strong>: 20 mg once daily with food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of DVT following Hip replacement surgery</strong>: 10 mg once daily for 35 days.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of DVT following knee replacement surgery</strong>: 10 mg once daily for 12 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"> May be taken with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Roxarel 15 mg</strong></p><p> May be taken with or without food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Roxarel 15 mg</strong></p><p> Concomitant use with drugs that are combined P-gp and CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, ritonavir, clarithromycin, erythromycin, fluconazole, diltiazem, verapamil, dronedarone) increases in Roxarel 15 mg exposure and pharmacodynamic effects (i.e., factor Xa inhibition and PT prolongation), so these should be avoided. Co-administration of Roxarel 15 mg with a combined P-gp and strong CYP3A4 inducer (e.g., rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine) decreases the efficacy of Roxarel 15 mg and also should be avoided. The concomitant use of other drugs like anti-platelet agents, heparin, fibrinolytic therapy, NSAIDs may cause an increased risk of bleeding.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Roxarel 15 mg or any of the excipients of the product. It is also contraindicated in patients with active pathological bleeding.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Roxarel 15 mg</strong></p><p> The most common side effects of Roxarel 15 mg include an increased chance of bleeding, spinal or epidural hematoma, and increased risk of stroke after discontinuation in nonvalvular atrial fibrillation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There are no adequate or well-controlled studies of Roxarel 15 mg in pregnant women, and dosing for pregnant women has not been established. Safety and efficacy of Roxarel 15 mg have not been established in breastfeeding women.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Early discontinuation of Roxarel 15 mg, in the absence of adequate alternative anticoagulation, increases the risk of thrombotic events. Roxarel 15 mg increases the risk of bleeding that can be fatal in the presence of the following risk factors: bleeding disorders, uncontrolled severe arterial hypertension, gastrointestinal disease (e.g., inflammatory bowel disease, oesophagitis, gastritis, and gastroesophageal reflux disease), vascular retinopathy, bronchiectasis, history of pulmonary bleeding. Signs or symptoms of neurological impairment should be monitored in case of neuraxial anesthesia (spinal/epidural anesthesia) or spinal puncture as epidural or spinal hematoma can occur. Roxarel 15 mg is not recommended in patients with pulmonary embolism who present with hemodynamic instability or who may receive thrombolysis or pulmonary embolectomy.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Roxarel 15 mg</strong></p><p> Overdose of Roxarel 15 mg may lead to hemorrhage. Roxarel 15 mg systemic exposure is not further increased at single doses &gt;50 mg due to limited absorption. A specific antidote for Roxarel 15 mg is not available. The use of activated charcoal to reduce absorption in case of Roxarel 15 mg overdose may be considered. Partial reversal of laboratory anticoagulation parameters may be achieved with the use of plasma products.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool (below 30°C) &amp; dry place protected from light. Keep away from the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Oral Anti-coagulants</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Roxarel 15 mg is a highly selective direct factor Xa inhibitor. Inhibition of factor Xa interrupts the intrinsic and extrinsic pathway of the blood coagulation cascade, inhibiting thrombin formation. Roxarel 15 mg does not inhibit thrombin (activated factor II) and no effects on platelets have been demonstrated.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Roxarel 15 mg is a pregnancy category C drug. There are no adequate or well-controlled studies of Roxarel 15 mg in pregnant women, and dosing for pregnant women has not been established. It is not known if Roxarel 15 mg is excreted in human milk. The safety and efficacy of Roxarel 15 mg have not been established in breastfeeding women.</p>', 'active', '959_1742441336.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 08:28:56', '2025-03-20 08:28:56'),
(3591, 'Zemicef', 'Cefixime Trihydrate', 145.00, 1, 'x 30 ml bottle', '30 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '100 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zemicef 30 ml</strong></p><p>Cefixime is an orally active cephalosporin antibiotic with marked in-vitro bactericidal activity against a wide variety of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms. Zemicef 30 ml is indicated for the treatment of the following acute infections when caused by susceptible microorganisms:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper Respiratory Tract Infections (URTI)</strong>: e.g. otitis media and other URTIs where the causative organism is known or suspected to be resistant to other commonly used antibiotics, or where treatment failure may carry significant risk.</li><li><strong>Lower Respiratory Tract Infections</strong>: e.g. bronchitis.</li><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections</strong>: e.g. cystitis, cystourethritis, pyelonephritis.</li><li>Clinical efficacy has been demonstrated in infections caused by commonly occurring pathogens, including <em>Streptococcus pneumonia</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, <em>Klebsiella</em> species, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase positive and negative), <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase positive and negative), and <em>Enterobacter</em> species. Cefixime is highly stable in the presence of beta-lactamase enzymes.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefixime is a third-generation semisynthetic cephalosporin antibiotic for oral administration. It is bactericidal against a broad spectrum of gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria at easily achievable plasma concentrations. It kills bacteria by interfering in the synthesis of bacterial cell walls. It is highly stable in the presence of beta-lactamase enzymes. As a result, many organisms resistant to penicillins and some cephalosporins due to the presence of beta-lactamases may be susceptible to Cefixime. Absorption is about 40% to 50%, whether administered with or without food.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zemicef 30 ml</strong></p><p>The usual course of treatment is 7 days, which may be continued for up to 14 days depending on the severity of the infection.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: The recommended dose is 200-400 mg (1 to 2 capsules) daily, given either as a single dose or in two divided doses. For the treatment of uncomplicated cervical/urethral gonococcal infections, a single oral dose of 400 mg is recommended.</li><li><strong>Children (6 months or older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">½-1 year: 75 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-4 years: 100 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-10 years: 200 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">11-12 years: 300 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>In typhoid fever</strong>: 10 mg/kg/day for 14 days</li><li><strong>Children (under 6 months)</strong>: The safety and efficacy of Cefixime have not been established in children aged less than 6 months.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Zemicef 30 ml</strong></p><p>Absorption of Cefixime is not significantly modified by the presence of food. The usual course of treatment is 7 days, which may be continued for up to 14 days if required.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 10 years</strong>: The recommended dosage is 200-400 mg daily, depending on the severity of the infection, given either as a single dose or in two divided doses.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Elderly patients may be given the same dose as recommended for adults. Renal function should be assessed, and dosage adjusted in severe renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: The recommended dosage for children is 8 mg/kg/day, administered as a single dose or in two divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6 months up to 1 year: 3.75 ml daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-4 years: 5 ml daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-10 years: 10 ml daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>In typhoid</strong>: the recommended children\'s dose is 5 mg/kg body weight twice daily for 10-14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For children weighing more than 50 kg or older than 10 years</strong>: Should be treated with the recommended adult dose (200-400 mg daily) depending on the severity of infection.</li><li><strong>Children aged less than 6 months</strong>: The safety and efficacy of Cefixime have not been established in children aged less than 6 months.</li><li><strong>Dosage in Renal Impairment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Creatinine clearance ≥20 ml/min</strong>: Normal dose and schedule may be given.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Creatinine clearance &lt;20 ml/min</strong>: It is recommended that the dose should not exceed 200 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For patients on chronic ambulatory peritoneal dialysis or hemodialysis</strong>: Follow the same recommendations for creatinine clearance &lt;20 ml/min.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zemicef 30 ml</strong></p><p>In common with other cephalosporins, increases in prothrombin times have been noted in a few patients. Care should therefore be taken in patients receiving anticoagulant therapy.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Patients with known hypersensitivity to cephalosporin antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zemicef 30 ml</strong></p><p>Cefixime is generally well tolerated, with most adverse reactions being mild and self-limiting in nature.</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal disturbances</strong>: Diarrhea and stool changes; diarrhea is more commonly associated with higher doses. Other gastrointestinal side effects include nausea, abdominal pain, dyspepsia, vomiting, and flatulence. Pseudomembranous colitis has been reported.</li><li><strong>Central nervous system</strong>: Headache and dizziness.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity reactions</strong>: Allergies such as rash, pruritus, urticaria, drug fever, and arthralgia have been observed. These usually subsided upon discontinuation of therapy.</li><li><strong>Hematological and clinical chemistry</strong>: Thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, and eosinophilia have been reported. These reactions were infrequent and reversible. Mild transient changes in liver and renal function tests have been observed.</li><li><strong>Miscellaneous</strong>: Other possible reactions include genital pruritis and vaginitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, so this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. It is not known whether Cefixime is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administering Cefixime to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Cefixime should be given with caution to patients who have shown hypersensitivity to other drugs. Cephalosporins should be given with caution to penicillin-sensitive patients, as there is some evidence of partial cross-allergenicity between penicillins and cephalosporins. Severe reactions, including anaphylaxis, have occurred with both drug classes. If an allergic effect occurs with Cefixime, the drug should be discontinued, and the patient treated with appropriate agents.</p><p>Cefixime should be administered with caution in patients with markedly impaired renal function. Broad-spectrum antibiotics alter the normal flora of the colon and may permit overgrowth of clostridia, which is a primary cause of antibiotic-associated diarrhea.</p><p><strong>Use in pregnancy and lactation</strong>: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women, so Cefixime should only be used if essential.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zemicef 30 ml</strong></p><p>Gastric lavage may be indicated; otherwise, no specific antidote exists. Cefixime is not removed in significant quantities from circulation by hemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis. Adverse reactions in healthy adult volunteers receiving single doses up to 2 g did not differ from the profile seen in patients treated with recommended doses.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30ºC, protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Cefixime is a third-generation semisynthetic cephalosporin antibiotic for oral administration. It is bactericidal against a broad spectrum of gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria at easily achievable plasma concentrations. It kills bacteria by interfering in the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall. It is highly stable in the presence of beta-lactamase enzymes. Many organisms resistant to penicillins and some cephalosporins due to the presence of beta-lactamases may be susceptible to Cefixime. Absorption is about 40% to 50%, whether administered with or without food.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, so this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. It is not known whether Cefixime is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Cefixime is administered to a nursing woman.</p>', 'active', '107_1742441528.jpg', 17, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 08:32:08', '2025-05-07 20:24:16'),
(3592, 'Rapine', 'Mirtazapine', 6.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '7.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rapine 7.5 mg</strong></p><p> Rapine 7.5 mg Tablets are indicated for the treatment of major depressive disorder (MDD).</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Atypical anti-depressant drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Rapine 7.5 mg, a piperazinoazepine tetracyclic antidepressant, enhances noradrenergic and serotonergic activity through blockade of central presynaptic adrenergic α2-receptors.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rapine 7.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: Initially, 15 mg daily; may be increased gradually depending on clinical response. Change dose at intervals of at least 1-2 weeks. Usual effective dose: 15-45 mg daily given as a single dose, preferably at bedtime, or in 2 divided doses.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and efficacy not established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric</strong>: Use with caution.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Rapine 7.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult dose</strong>: The recommended starting dose for Rapine 7.5 mg tablets is 15 mg/day, administered in a single dose, preferably in the evening or prior to sleep. The effective dose range is generally 15 to 45 mg/day, and patients not responding to the initial 15 mg dose may benefit from dose increases up to 30 mg to a maximum of 45 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Use in children</strong>: Not recommended.</li><li><strong>Missed Dose</strong>: If a dose is missed, take it as soon as remembered unless it\'s close to the next dose. If close to the next dose, skip the missed dose and continue with the regular schedule. Do not take a double dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Rapine 7.5 mg</strong></p><p> Rapine 7.5 mg has clinically significant interactions with Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAOI) &amp; other serotonergic drugs such as tryptophan, triptans, linezolid, serotonin reuptake inhibitors, venlafaxine, lithium, tramadol, or St. John\'s Wort. It may interfere with the metabolism or activity of Carbamazepine, Phenytoin, or Cimetidine. Avoid alcohol and Diazepam while taking Rapine 7.5 mg.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Rapine 7.5 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to the drug or any excipients.</li><li><strong>Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors</strong>: Concomitant use with MAO inhibitors is contraindicated. Rapine 7.5 mg should not be used within 14 days of initiating or discontinuing therapy with an MAOI.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Rapine 7.5 mg</strong></p><p> The most common side effects are dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, increased appetite, and weight gain.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Rapine 7.5 mg should not be used during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Patients, families, and caregivers should be alert to signs of anxiety, agitation, panic attacks, insomnia, irritability, hostility, aggression, impulsivity, akathisia, mania, changes in behavior, worsening depression, and suicidal thoughts, especially early in treatment or when adjusting the dose. Agranulocytosis is a potential risk. The drug may impair judgment, thinking, and motor skills due to its sedative effects. Clinically significant ALT (SGPT) elevations may occur.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rapine 7.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Disorientation, drowsiness, impaired memory, tachycardia.</li><li><strong>Management</strong>: Ensure an adequate airway, oxygenation, and ventilation. Monitor cardiac function. General supportive measures are recommended. Do not induce emesis. Gastric lavage may be used if performed soon after ingestion or in symptomatic patients. Administer activated charcoal. No specific antidotes are known.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store away from light and moisture. Keep below 30º C. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Atypical anti-depressant drugs</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pharmacodynamics</strong>: Rapine 7.5 mg enhances central noradrenergic and serotonergic activity by acting as an antagonist at central presynaptic α2-adrenergic inhibitory autoreceptors and heteroreceptors, resulting in increased central noradrenergic and serotonergic activity. It also antagonizes 5-HT2 and 5-HT3 receptors, has a potent histamine (H1) receptor antagonism (explaining its sedative effects), and moderate peripheral α1-adrenergic antagonism.</li><li><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong>: After oral administration, Rapine 7.5 mg is rapidly absorbed, reaching peak plasma levels in about 2 hours. It has a half-life of 20-40 hours, which justifies once-a-day dosing. The drug is extensively metabolized and eliminated via urine and faeces.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category-C. Patients should notify their physician if pregnant or planning to become pregnant during treatment. The safety of Rapine 7.5 mg during breastfeeding is unknown.</p>', 'active', '362_1742441771.jpg', 14, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 08:36:11', '2025-03-20 08:36:11'),
(3593, 'Zafir', 'Zafirlukast', 361.26, 20, 'Tablet', '20s pack', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zafir 20 mg</strong></p><p> Zafir 20 mg is indicated for the prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma in adults and children 5 years of age and older.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Leukotriene receptor antagonists</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Zafir 20 mg is a selective and competitive leukotriene-receptor antagonist (LTRA) of leukotriene D4 and E4 (LTD4 and LTE4), components of slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis (SRSA). Cysteinyl leukotriene production and receptor occupation have been correlated with the pathophysiology of asthma, including airway oedema, smooth muscle constriction, and altered cellular activity associated with the inflammatory process, which contribute to the signs and symptoms of asthma.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zafir 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children under 7 years of age</strong>: No clinical experience of the use of Zafir 20 mg in this age group until safety information is available.</li><li><strong>Children over 7 years through 11 years of age</strong>: The recommended dose is 10 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Adults and children aged 12 years and over</strong>: The dosage is one 20 mg tablet twice daily. This dosage should not be exceeded. Higher doses may be associated with elevations of liver enzymes consistent with hepatotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Food considerations</strong>: Zafir 20 mg should not be taken with meals, as food may reduce bioavailability.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: The clearance of Zafir 20 mg is reduced in elderly patients (over 65 years old), and Cmax and AUC are approximately double those of younger adults. Caution is recommended as clinical experience in this group is limited.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary in patients with mild renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zafir 20 mg</strong></p><p> Zafir 20 mg can be administered with other therapies used for asthma and allergy management without adverse interaction.</p><ul><li>Co-administration with <strong>Warfarin</strong> results in an increase in maximum prothrombin time by approximately 35%. Monitoring of prothrombin time is recommended.</li><li>Co-administration with <strong>Theophylline</strong> results in decreased plasma levels of Zafir 20 mg by approximately 30%, with no effect on plasma Theophylline levels.</li><li>Co-administration with <strong>Terfenadine</strong> resulted in a 54% decrease in AUC for Zafir 20 mg.</li><li>Co-administration with <strong>Acetylsalicylic acid</strong> (650 mg four times a day) increases plasma levels of Zafir 20 mg by approximately 45%.</li><li>Co-administration with <strong>Erythromycin</strong> results in decreased plasma levels of Zafir 20 mg by approximately 40%.</li><li>The clearance of Zafir 20 mg may be increased by approximately 20% in smokers.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Zafir 20 mg should not be given to:</p><ul><li>Patients with hypersensitivity to the product or any of its ingredients.</li><li>Patients with moderate or severe renal impairment.</li><li>Patients with hepatic impairment or cirrhosis (has not been studied in hepatitis or long-term studies in cirrhosis).</li><li>Children under 7 years of age until safety information is available.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Zafir 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Effect on ability to drive or operate machinery</strong>: No evidence suggests Zafir 20 mg affects the ability to drive or use machinery.</li><li>Common side effects include headache, nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, and abdominal pain, which are usually mild.</li><li>Rare side effects include bruising, bleeding disorders (e.g., menorrhagia), thrombocytopaenia, and agranulocytosis.</li><li>Hypersensitivity reactions such as urticaria and angio-oedema, as well as rashes (including blistering), have been reported.</li><li>Rare occurrences of elevated serum transaminase levels and drug-induced hepatitis.</li><li>Increased incidence of infections, especially respiratory tract infections, in elderly patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: The safety of Zafir 20 mg during human pregnancy has not been established. It should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Zafir 20 mg is excreted in human breast milk and should not be administered to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Zafir 20 mg should be taken regularly to achieve benefit, even during symptom-free periods.</li><li>Zafir 20 mg therapy should continue during acute asthma exacerbations.</li><li>Zafir 20 mg is not a substitute for the reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks.</li><li>Not recommended for labile or unstable asthma.</li><li>Cases of <strong>Churg Strauss syndrome</strong> have been reported. If symptoms develop, Zafir 20 mg should be discontinued.</li><li><strong>Liver dysfunction</strong>: Elevations in serum transaminases may occur, potentially indicating hepatotoxicity. If symptoms of liver dysfunction occur (e.g., fatigue, jaundice), Zafir 20 mg should be discontinued.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store between 20-25° C. Protect from light and moisture.</p>', 'active', '906_1742442214.jpg', 5, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 08:43:34', '2025-03-20 08:43:34'),
(3594, 'Vancomin', 'Vancomycin Hydrochloride', 250.94, 1, 'IV Infusion', '500 mg vial infusion', 'IV Infusion', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vancomin 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Vancomycin is indicated in potentially life-threatening infections which cannot be treated with other effective, less toxic antimicrobial drugs including the penicillins and cephalosporins. Vancomycin is useful in the therapy of severe staphylococcal infections in patients who cannot receive or who have failed to respond to the penicillins and cephalosporins or who have infections with staphylococci, resistant to other antibiotics. Vancomycin is used in the treatment of endocarditis and as prophylaxis against endocarditis in patients undergoing dental or surgical procedures. Its effectiveness has been documented in other infections due to staphylococci including osteomyelitis, pneumonia, septicemia, and soft tissue infections.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Glycopeptide</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Vancomycin binds tightly to D-alanyl-D-alanine portion cell wall precursor causing blockage of glycopeptide polymerisation which produces immediate inhibition of cell wall synthesis and secondary damage to the cytoplasmic membrane.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Vancomin 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Concentrations of no more than 5 mg/ml and rates of no more than 10 mg/min are recommended in adults. In selected patients in need of fluid restriction, a concentration up to 10 mg/ml may be used.</p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Patients with Normal Renal Function:Adults:</strong> Usual daily dose is 2 gm (in 4 or 2 divided doses).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong> Total daily dose is 40 mg/Kg (in 4 divided doses).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Infants and Neonates:</strong> An initial dose of 15 mg/Kg is suggested followed by 10 mg/Kg every 12 hours in the first week, then every 8 hours up to 1 month.</li><li><strong>Patients with Impaired Renal Function and Elderly Patients:</strong></li><li> Dosage adjustment must be made in patients with impaired renal function. In premature infants and the elderly, dosage reduction may be necessary to a greater extent than expected because of decreasing renal function. If creatinine clearance can be measured or estimated accurately, the dosage for most patients with renal impairment can be calculated using the following chart:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 100 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 1545 mg/24 h</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 90 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 1390 mg/24 h</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 80 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 1235 mg/24 h</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 70 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 1080 mg/24 h</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 60 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 925 mg/24 h</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 50 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 770 mg/24 h</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 40 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 620 mg/24 h</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 30 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 465 mg/24 h</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 20 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 310 mg/24 h</li><li>CrCl 10 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 155 mg/24 h</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"> The initial dose should be not less than 15 mg/kg even in patients with mild to moderate renal insufficiency. Above chart is not valid for functionally anephric patients. For such patients, an initial dose of 15 mg/kg of body weight should be given in order to achieve prompt therapeutic serum concentrations. The dose required to maintain stable concentrations is 1.9 mg/kg/24 h. Since individual maintenance doses of 250-1,000 mg are convenient, 1 dose may be given every several days rather than on a daily basis in patients with marked renal impairment. In anuria, a dose of 1000 mg every 7-10 days has been recommended. Intermittent infusion is the recommended method of administration. Intraperitoneal administration is not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Vancomycin is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vancomycin.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Vancomin 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Vancomycin is well tolerated. However, during or soon after rapid infusion of Vancomycin, patients may develop anaphylactic reactions including hypotension, wheezing, dyspnoea, urticaria or pruritus. Rapid infusion may also cause flushing of the upper body (\"red neck\") or pain and muscle spasm of the chest and back. These reactions usually resolve within 20 minutes but may persist for several hours. Such events are infrequent if Vancomycin is given by a slow infusion over 60 minutes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> It is not known whether it causes foetal harm or not. Vancomycin should be given in pregnancy only if clearly needed, and blood levels should be monitored carefully to minimise the risk of foetal toxicity. Vancomin 500 mg/vial is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Vancomycin is administered to a nursing woman. It is unlikely that a nursing infant can absorb a significant amount of Vancomycin from its gastro-intestinal tract.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Patients with borderline renal function and individuals over the age of 60 should be given serial tests of auditory function and of Vancomycin blood levels. All patients receiving the drug should have periodic haematological studies, urine analysis, and renal function tests. Vancomycin is very irritating to tissue and causes injection site necrosis when injected intramuscularly. It must be infused intravenously. Injection site pain and thrombophlebitis occur in many patients receiving Vancomycin and are occasionally severe.</p><p> Prolonged use of Vancomycin may result in the overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms. Careful observation of the patient is essential. If superinfection occurs during therapy, appropriate measures should be taken. In rare instances, there have been reports of pseudomembranous colitis due to <em>C. difficile</em>, developing in patients who received intravenous Vancomycin.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Vancomin 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Supportive care is advised with maintenance of glomerular filtration. Vancomycin is poorly removed from the blood by haemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis. Haemoperfusion with Amberlite resin XAD-4 has been reported to be of limited benefit.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Other antibiotic</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Vancomycin binds tightly to D-alanyl-D-alanine portion cell wall precursor causing blockage of glycopeptide polymerisation which produces immediate inhibition of cell wall synthesis and secondary damage to the cytoplasmic membrane.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> It is not known whether it causes foetal harm or not. Vancomycin should be given in pregnancy only if clearly needed and blood levels should be monitored carefully to minimise the risk of foetal toxicity. Vancomin 500 mg/vial is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Vancomycin is administered to a nursing woman. It is unlikely that a nursing infant can absorb a significant amount of Vancomycin from its gastro-intestinal tract.</p>', 'active', '171_1742442648.jpg', 7, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 08:50:48', '2025-03-20 08:50:48');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3595, 'Lacrison', 'Loteprednol Etabonate', 175.00, 1, 'drop', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Suspension', '0.5parcent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p><strong>0.5% Ophthalmic Suspension:</strong></p><p>Lacrison suspension is indicated in steroid-responsive inflammatory conditions of the palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva, cornea, and anterior segment of the eyeball, such as allergic conjunctivitis, acne rosacea, superficial punctate keratitis, herpes zoster keratitis, iritis, and cyclitis. It is also effective in post-operative inflammation.</p><p><strong>1% Ophthalmic Suspension:</strong></p><p>Lacrison suspension is indicated in steroid-responsive inflammatory conditions of the palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva, cornea, and anterior segment of the eyeball, such as allergic conjunctivitis, acne rosacea, superficial punctate keratitis, herpes zoster keratitis, iritis, and cyclitis. It is also effective in post-operative inflammation.</p><p><strong>0.5% Ophthalmic Ointment:</strong></p><p>Lacrison ointment is indicated for the treatment of steroid-responsive inflammatory conditions of the palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva, cornea, and anterior segment of the globe, such as allergic conjunctivitis, acne rosacea, superficial punctate keratitis, herpes zoster keratitis, iritis, cyclitis, and uveitis. Lacrison is also indicated for the treatment of post-operative inflammation and pain following ocular surgery.</p><p><strong>0.5% Ophthalmic Gel:</strong></p><p>Lacrison gel is indicated for the treatment of post-operative inflammation and pain following ocular surgery. It is also effective in steroid-responsive inflammatory conditions of the palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva, cornea, and anterior segment of the eyeball, such as allergic conjunctivitis, acne rosacea, superficial punctate keratitis, herpes zoster keratitis, iritis, and cyclitis.</p><p><strong>* রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</strong></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Corticosteroids inhibit the inflammatory response to a variety of inciting agents and probably delay or slow healing. They inhibit edema, fibrin deposition, capillary dilation, leukocyte migration, capillary proliferation, fibroblast proliferation, deposition of collagen, and scar formation associated with inflammation.</p><p>There is no generally accepted explanation for the mechanism of action of ocular corticosteroids. However, corticosteroids are thought to act by inducing phospholipase A2 inhibitory proteins, collectively called lipocortins. It is postulated that these proteins control the biosynthesis of potent mediators of inflammation, such as prostaglandins and leukotrienes, by inhibiting the release of their common precursor, arachidonic acid, which is released from membrane phospholipids by phospholipase A2.</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>0.5% Ophthalmic Suspension:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For steroid-responsive ocular inflammation:</strong> Instill 1 drop of Loteprednol Ophthalmic Suspension into the conjunctival sac of the eye(s) four times a day.</li><li><strong>For inflammation after surgery:</strong> Instill 1 drop of Loteprednol Ophthalmic Suspension into the conjunctival sac of the operated eye(s) four times a day, beginning 24 hours after surgery and continuing throughout the first two weeks after surgery.</li></ul><p><strong>1% Ophthalmic Suspension:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For steroid-responsive ocular inflammation:</strong> Instill 1 drop of Loteprednol DS Ophthalmic Suspension into the conjunctival sac of the eye(s) two times a day.</li><li><strong>For inflammation after surgery:</strong> Instill 1 drop of Loteprednol DS Ophthalmic Suspension into the conjunctival sac of the operated eye(s) two times a day, beginning 24 hours after surgery and continuing throughout the first two weeks after surgery.</li></ul><p><strong>0.5% Ophthalmic Ointment:</strong></p><ul><li>Apply a small amount (approximately 1/2 inch ribbon) into the conjunctival sac(s) four times daily, beginning 24 hours after surgery and continuing throughout the first two weeks of the postoperative period.</li></ul><p><strong>Ophthalmic Gel:</strong></p><ul><li>Instill one to two drops of Loteprednol Sterile Ophthalmic Gel into the conjunctival sac of the affected eye four times daily, beginning the day after surgery and continuing throughout the first two weeks of the postoperative period.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Children:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> No overall differences in safety and effectiveness have been observed between elderly and younger patients.</p><p><strong>* রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</strong></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>Since Lacrison is not detected in plasma following topical administration, it is not expected to affect the pharmacokinetics of systemically administered medicinal products.</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Loteprednol, as with other ophthalmic corticosteroids, is contraindicated in most viral diseases of the cornea and conjunctiva, including epithelial herpes simplex keratitis (dendritic keratitis), vaccinia, and varicella. It is also contraindicated in mycobacterial infections of the eye and fungal diseases of ocular structures.</p><p>Additionally, it should not be used in individuals with known or suspected hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of this preparation and to other corticosteroids.</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Reactions associated with ophthalmic steroids include:</p><ul><li><strong>Common (5%-15% of patients):</strong> Elevated intraocular pressure, optic nerve damage, visual acuity and field defects, posterior subcapsular cataract formation, secondary ocular infections (including herpes simplex), and perforation of the globe in cases of corneal or scleral thinning.</li><li><strong>Less Common (&lt;5% of patients):</strong> Conjunctivitis, corneal abnormalities, eyelid erythema, keratoconjunctivitis, ocular irritation, pain, discomfort, and papillae.</li><li><strong>Non-Ocular Adverse Reactions (&lt;15% of patients):</strong> Headache, rhinitis, and pharyngitis.</li></ul><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C:</strong> There are no clinical data on exposed pregnancies. Animal studies have shown reproductive toxicity. The potential risk for humans is unknown. Use during pregnancy only if clearly necessary.</li><li>It is unknown whether topical ophthalmic corticosteroids are absorbed systemically in amounts sufficient to produce detectable quantities in human milk. Caution is advised when administering to nursing women.</li></ul><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>If signs and symptoms fail to improve after two days, the patient should be re-evaluated.</li><li>If Lacrison is used for 10 days or longer, intraocular pressure should be monitored.</li><li>Fungal invasion must be considered in any persistent corneal ulceration where a steroid has been used or is in use.</li><li>If redness or itching worsens, the patient should consult a physician.</li><li>Patients should avoid wearing contact lenses during treatment.</li></ul><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Ophthalmic Steroid Preparations</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a dry place protected from light.</li><li>Do not touch the tube tip to any surface.</li><li>Contents should not be used one month after first opening.</li><li>Protect from freezing.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1955_1742443011.png', 3, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 08:56:51', '2025-03-20 08:56:51'),
(3596, 'Optabac', 'Chloramphenicol', 34.27, 1, 'drop', '10 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '0.5 parcent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Optabac is indicated for the treatment of ocular infections involving the conjunctiva and/or cornea caused by Optabac-susceptible organisms, such as <em>Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pneumoniae, E. coli, H. influenzae, Klebsiella/Enterobacter spp, Moraxella lacunata,</em> and <em>Neisseria</em> species.</p><p><strong>* রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</strong></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Chloramphenicol is a broad-spectrum bacteriostatic antibiotic that acts through the inhibition of bacterial protein synthesis by interfering with the transfer of activated amino acids from soluble RNA to ribosomes.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Adults and Children:</strong> Instill 1 or 2 drops in the conjunctival sac 4-6 times per day for the first 72 hours and then every 4 hours thereafter. Treatment should be continued for approximately 7 days but should not be continued for more than 3 weeks without re-evaluation by the physician.</p><p><strong>* রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</strong></p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>Chymotrypsin may be inhibited if given simultaneously with Optabac.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in individuals with a history of hypersensitivity to Chloramphenicol or any ingredients of the preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>The systemic adverse reaction has not been observed within short-term topical use of Optabac. The most frequently reported adverse reactions have been burning, stinging, conjunctival hyperemia, blood dyscrasia, allergic or inflammatory reactions, vesicular and maculopapular dermatitis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Safety for use in pregnancy and lactation has not been established. Therefore, use only when considered essential by physicians.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Optabac ophthalmic solution should never be given for minor infections or for prophylaxis. Repeated courses and prolonged treatment should be avoided. Blood dyscrasias (<em>granulocytopenia, thrombocytopenia,</em> and <em>moderate anaemia</em>) may occur after prolonged ophthalmic use.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>Accidental ingestion of the medicine is unlikely to cause any toxicity due to the low content of antibiotic.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Ear Anti-Infectives &amp; Antiseptics, Eye Anti-Infectives &amp; Antiseptics, Macrolides</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool (between 2°C-8°C) and dry place, protect from light, and keep out of reach of children. Do not touch the dropper tip to any surface since this may contaminate the solution. Do not use after 30 days of the first opening.</p>', 'active', '1777_1742443414.webp', 17, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 09:03:34', '2025-03-20 09:03:34'),
(3597, 'Nepagen', 'Nepafenac', 150.00, 1, 'drop', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Suspension', '0.1 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> <strong>Nepagen 0.1% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension:</strong> Used for the treatment of post-operative ocular pain and inflammation, including cataract surgery, inhibition of surgery-induced miosis, and prevention of post-operative cystoid macular edema (CME).</p><p><strong>Nepagen 0.3% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension:</strong> Used for post-operative pain and inflammation associated with cataract surgery.</p><ul><li>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Nepafenac is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory prodrug (NSAID). After instillation in the eye, it penetrates the cornea and is converted by ocular tissue hydrolase to Amfenac, a potent nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug. Amfenac is thought to inhibit the action of the cyclooxygenase enzyme, which is required for prostaglandin synthesis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p> <strong>Nepafenac 0.1% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For post-operative pain &amp; inflammation:</strong> Instill 1 drop 3 times daily 1 day prior to cataract surgery and continue on the day of surgery and through the first 2 weeks of the post-operative period.</li><li><strong>For surgery-induced miosis:</strong> Instill 1 drop 3 times daily 1 day before surgery and on the day of surgery.</li><li><strong>For prevention of post-operative CME:</strong> Instill 1 drop 3 times daily 1 day before surgery and continue on the day of surgery and through the first 6 weeks of the post-operative period.</li></ul><p><strong>Nepafenac 0.3% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For post-operative pain &amp; inflammation:</strong> Instill 1 drop once daily 1 day prior to cataract surgery and continue on the day of surgery and through the first 2 weeks of the post-operative period. An additional drop should be administered 30 to 120 minutes before surgery.</li><li>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Contraindicated in patients with previously demonstrated hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients in the formulation or to other NSAIDs.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Reported side effects include foreign body sensation, lid margin crusting, ocular discomfort, and ocular hyperemia.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in pregnancy:</strong> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Nepafenac should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Use in lactation:</strong> It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Nepafenac ophthalmic suspension is administered to a nursing mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Nepagen should be used with caution in patients with known bleeding tendencies or those receiving medications that may prolong bleeding time.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients below 18 years of age have not been established.</li><li><strong>Use in elderly patients:</strong> No overall differences in safety or effectiveness have been observed between elderly and younger patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic Non-Steroidal Drugs</p>', 'active', '17_1742443749.jpg', 37, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 09:09:09', '2025-03-20 09:09:09'),
(3598, 'Nepafen', 'Nepafenac', 150.45, 1, 'drop', '5 ml drop', 'Oral Suspension', '0.1percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> <strong>Nepafen 0.1% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension:</strong> Used for the treatment of post-operative ocular pain and inflammation, including cataract surgery, inhibition of surgery-induced miosis, and prevention of post-operative cystoid macular edema (CME).</p><p><strong>Nepafen 0.3% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension:</strong> Used for post-operative pain and inflammation associated with cataract surgery.</p><ul><li>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Nepafenac is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory prodrug (NSAID). After instillation in the eye, it penetrates the cornea and is converted by ocular tissue hydrolase to Amfenac, a potent nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug. Amfenac is thought to inhibit the action of the cyclooxygenase enzyme, which is required for prostaglandin synthesis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p> <strong>Nepafenac 0.1% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For post-operative pain &amp; inflammation:</strong> Instill 1 drop 3 times daily 1 day prior to cataract surgery and continue on the day of surgery and through the first 2 weeks of the post-operative period.</li><li><strong>For surgery-induced miosis:</strong> Instill 1 drop 3 times daily 1 day before surgery and on the day of surgery.</li><li><strong>For prevention of post-operative CME:</strong> Instill 1 drop 3 times daily 1 day before surgery and continue on the day of surgery and through the first 6 weeks of the post-operative period.</li></ul><p><strong>Nepafenac 0.3% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For post-operative pain &amp; inflammation:</strong> Instill 1 drop once daily 1 day prior to cataract surgery and continue on the day of surgery and through the first 2 weeks of the post-operative period. An additional drop should be administered 30 to 120 minutes before surgery.</li><li>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Contraindicated in patients with previously demonstrated hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients in the formulation or to other NSAIDs.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Reported side effects include foreign body sensation, lid margin crusting, ocular discomfort, and ocular hyperemia.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in pregnancy:</strong> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Nepafenac should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Use in lactation:</strong> It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Nepafenac ophthalmic suspension is administered to a nursing mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Nepafen should be used with caution in patients with known bleeding tendencies or those receiving medications that may prolong bleeding time.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients below 18 years of age have not been established.</li><li><strong>Use in elderly patients:</strong> No overall differences in safety or effectiveness have been observed between elderly and younger patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic Non-Steroidal Drugs</p>', 'active', '1029_1742444003.jpg', 7, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 09:13:23', '2025-03-20 09:13:23'),
(3599, 'Monas', 'Montelukast Sodium', 9.00, 15, 'Tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Chewable Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Nepafenac Ophthalmic Suspension</strong></p><p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p><strong>Nepafen 0.1% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension:</strong> Used for the treatment of post-operative ocular pain and inflammation, including cataract surgery, inhibition of surgery-induced miosis, and prevention of post-operative cystoid macular edema (CME).</p><p><strong>Nepafen 0.3% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension:</strong> Used for post-operative pain and inflammation associated with cataract surgery.</p><p><strong>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</strong></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Nepafenac is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory prodrug (NSAID). After instillation in the eye, it penetrates the cornea and is converted by ocular tissue hydrolase to Amfenac, a potent nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug. Amfenac is thought to inhibit the action of the cyclooxygenase enzyme, which is required for prostaglandin synthesis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Nepafenac 0.1% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For post-operative pain &amp; inflammation:</strong> Instill 1 drop 3 times daily 1 day prior to cataract surgery and continue on the day of surgery and through the first 2 weeks of the post-operative period.</li><li><strong>For surgery-induced miosis:</strong> Instill 1 drop 3 times daily 1 day before surgery and on the day of surgery.</li><li><strong>For prevention of post-operative CME:</strong> Instill 1 drop 3 times daily 1 day before surgery and continue on the day of surgery and through the first 6 weeks of the post-operative period.</li></ul><p><strong>Nepafenac 0.3% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For post-operative pain &amp; inflammation:</strong> Instill 1 drop once daily 1 day prior to cataract surgery and continue on the day of surgery and through the first 2 weeks of the post-operative period. An additional drop should be administered 30 to 120 minutes before surgery.</li></ul><p><strong>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</strong></p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in patients with previously demonstrated hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients in the formulation or to other NSAIDs.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Reported side effects include foreign body sensation, lid margin crusting, ocular discomfort, and ocular hyperemia.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in pregnancy:</strong> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Nepafenac should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Use in lactation:</strong> It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Nepafenac ophthalmic suspension is administered to a nursing mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Nepafen should be used with caution in patients with known bleeding tendencies or those receiving medications that may prolong bleeding time.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients below 18 years of age have not been established.</li><li><strong>Use in elderly patients:</strong> No overall differences in safety or effectiveness have been observed between elderly and younger patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Ophthalmic Non-Steroidal Drugs</p><p><strong>Monas (Montelukast)</strong></p><p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Monas is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</strong></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Montelukast is a selective and orally active leukotriene receptor antagonist that inhibits the cysteinyl leukotriene receptor (CysLT1). The cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, LTE4) are products of arachidonic acid metabolism and are released from various cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. Cysteinyl leukotrienes and leukotriene receptor occupation have been correlated with the pathophysiology of asthma &amp; allergic rhinitis, including airway edema, smooth muscle contraction, and altered cellular activity associated with the inflammatory process, which contribute to the signs and symptoms of asthma.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (≥15 years):</strong> Montelukast 10 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients (6-14 years):</strong> Montelukast 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients (2-5 years):</strong> Montelukast 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients (6 months-5 years):</strong> Montelukast 4 mg oral granules once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic &amp; Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage adjustment is required for mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency or renal insufficiency.</li><li>No dosage adjustment is required for elderly patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>Monas has no significant interactions with commonly prescribed drugs. However, caution should be taken when co-administering with phenobarbital or rifampin.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to any component of the product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common: Diarrhea, fever, headache, nausea, vomiting, skin reactions, upper respiratory tract infection. Uncommon: Anxiety, depression, dizziness, sleep disorders. Rare: Angioedema, hallucination, hepatic disorders, suicidal tendencies.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Use during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Caution is advised when administered to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Not for acute asthma attacks.</li><li>Not a substitute for corticosteroids.</li><li>Patients should continue avoiding NSAIDs if they have aspirin sensitivity.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>Overdose effects include abdominal pain, somnolence, thirst, headache, vomiting, and psychomotor hyperactivity.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture, keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>General Instructions</strong></p><ul><li>Take Monas exactly as prescribed by your doctor.</li><li>Do not stop using it without your doctor\'s advice.</li><li>It is not a fast-acting rescue medicine for asthma attacks.</li><li>Avoid asthma triggers.</li><li>Consult your doctor before use during pregnancy or lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</strong></p>', 'active', '3161_1744994479.jpg', 5, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 09:18:39', '2025-04-18 20:41:50'),
(3600, 'Ridon Fast', 'Domperidone Maleate', 3.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Ridon Fast is indicated in:</p><ul><li><strong>Dyspeptic symptom complex</strong>, often associated with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Epigastric sense of fullness, feeling of abdominal distension, upper abdominal pain</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Eructation, flatulence, early satiety</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea and vomiting</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Heartburn with or without regurgitations of gastric contents in the mouth</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Non-ulcer dyspepsia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or in migraine</li><li><strong>Parkinson\'s disease</strong>: In dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Radiological studies</strong>: Speeding barium transit in follow-through radiological studies.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Domperidone is a dopamine antagonist that blocks dopamine receptors in the <strong>Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ)</strong> and stomach. Its gastroprokinetic action is due to blocking dopamine receptors influencing the motility of the gastrointestinal tract. Due to its weak penetration across the blood-brain barrier, Domperidone has minimal effect on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, minimizing psychotropic and neurologic side effects. Domperidone restores normal motility and tone of the upper gastrointestinal tract, facilitates gastric emptying, enhances antral and duodenal peristalsis, and regulates pyloric contraction. It also increases esophageal peristalsis and lower esophageal sphincter pressure, preventing regurgitation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension), every 6-8 hours daily. The maximum dose is 80 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 2-4 ml suspension/10 kg body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml pediatric drops/10 kg body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>In dyspeptic symptoms</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension), every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml suspension/10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml pediatric drops/10 kg) body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>In acute and sub-acute conditions</strong> (mainly in acute nausea and vomiting):</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension), every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml suspension/10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml pediatric drops/10 kg) body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><em>Maximum period of treatment: 12 weeks.</em></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Anticholinergic drugs</strong> may antagonize the antidyspeptic effect of domperidone.</li><li><strong>Antacids and antisecretory drugs</strong> lower domperidone\'s oral bioavailability.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors) may increase plasma levels of domperidone.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Domperidone.</li><li>Neonates.</li><li>Gastrointestinal stimulation is dangerous (e.g., gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, perforation).</li><li><strong>Prolactinoma</strong> (pituitary tumor).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Rare intestinal cramps.</li><li>Extrapyramidal effects (rare in children).</li><li>Hyperprolactinemia leading to galactorrhea and gynaecomastia.</li><li>Rare allergic reactions (rash, urticaria).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Domperidone is not teratogenic in animals, but should be used in pregnancy only if absolutely necessary.</li><li>Domperidone is excreted in breast milk; it is not recommended for nursing mothers unless the benefits outweigh risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in <strong>children</strong> due to the risk of extrapyramidal reactions.</li><li>Use with caution in <strong>hepatic impairment</strong>.</li><li>Use with caution in <strong>renal insufficiency</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms may include drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions (especially in children).</li><li>Activated charcoal and close observation recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Motility Stimulants, Dopamine Antagonists, Prokinetic Drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Common Questions about Ridon Fast 10 mg Tablet</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>What is Ridon Fast 10 mg DT used for?</strong>Ridon Fast 10 mg DT is used to treat nausea, vomiting, and indigestion.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Quick Tips</strong>Take Ridon Fast 10 mg DT before meals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">It may cause dizziness or sleepiness; avoid driving until you know how it affects you.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Avoid alcohol while using Ridon Fast.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not take for longer than 7 days without consulting a doctor.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><p>4o mini</p><p><br></p><p><br></p>', 'active', '1751_1742444962.webp', 26, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 09:29:22', '2025-03-20 09:29:22'),
(3601, 'Degut', 'Domperidone Maleate', 2.00, 15, 'tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Degut is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Dyspeptic symptom complex</strong>, often associated with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, including:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Epigastric sense of fullness, feeling of abdominal distension, upper abdominal pain</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Eructation, flatulence, early satiety</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea and vomiting</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Heartburn with or without regurgitation of gastric contents in the mouth</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Non-ulcer dyspepsia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or in migraine.</li><li><strong>Parkinson\'s disease</strong>: In dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Radiological studies</strong>: Speeding barium transit in follow-through radiological studies.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Domperidone is a dopamine antagonist that blocks dopamine receptors located in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and stomach. Its gastroprokinetic action is due to the blockade of dopamine receptors affecting gastrointestinal motility. Due to weak penetration across the blood-brain barrier, Domperidone has minimal effect on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, excluding psychotropic and neurological side effects. It restores normal motility and tone of the upper gastrointestinal tract, facilitates gastric emptying, enhances antral and duodenal peristalsis, and regulates pyloric contraction. Additionally, Domperidone increases esophageal peristalsis and lower esophageal sphincter pressure, thus preventing regurgitation of gastric contents.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension), every 6-8 hours daily. The maximum dose is 80 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 2-4 ml suspension/10 kg body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml pediatric drops/10 kg body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>In Dyspeptic Symptoms</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension), every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>In Acute and Sub-Acute Conditions (Mainly in Acute Nausea and Vomiting)</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension), every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg body weight, every 6-8 hours daily. (Max 12 weeks of treatment in acute nausea and vomiting).</li></ul><p><strong>By Rectum (Suppositories)</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults (Including Elderly)</strong>: 30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Max daily dose rectally is 30 mg for those weighing 10 to 25 kg.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Anticholinergic drugs</strong> may reduce the antidyspeptic effect of domperidone.</li><li><strong>Antacids and antisecretory drugs</strong> reduce its oral bioavailability.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, etc.) may increase plasma levels of domperidone.</li><li>Concomitant use with <strong>dopaminergic agonists</strong> (e.g., bromocriptine, L-dopa) suppresses peripheral side effects without affecting central properties.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to domperidone.</li><li>Neonates.</li><li>When gastrointestinal stimulation may be dangerous (e.g., gastrointestinal hemorrhage, obstruction, perforation).</li><li>Prolactin-releasing pituitary tumors (prolactinoma).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Rare: Transient intestinal cramps, extrapyramidal phenomena (mainly in young children), and rare allergic reactions (rash, urticaria).</li><li>Possible increase in plasma prolactin leading to <strong>galactorrhea</strong> and <strong>gynaecomastia</strong>.</li><li>Neurological side effects in infants under 1 year due to immature blood-brain barrier.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Should only be used during the first trimester if deemed necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Domperidone is excreted in breast milk but at much lower concentrations than in plasma. It is not recommended for breastfeeding mothers unless benefits outweigh the risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in <strong>children</strong> due to the risk of extrapyramidal reactions.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with <strong>hepatic</strong> or <strong>renal</strong> impairment.</li><li><strong>Infants</strong>: Should only be used under close medical supervision due to the developing blood-brain barrier.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Drowsiness, disorientation, extrapyramidal reactions (especially in children).</li><li>Management: Activated charcoal, close observation, and use of anticholinergic, antiparkinson drugs, or antihistamines for extrapyramidal reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Common Questions about Degut 10 mg Tablet</strong></p><ul><li><strong>What is Degut 10 mg Tablet used for?</strong></li><li> Degut 10 mg Tablet is a dopamine antagonist used to treat nausea and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Quick Tips</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Helps relieve nausea, vomiting, and indigestion.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Should be taken before meals as prescribed by your doctor.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">May cause dizziness and sleepiness—avoid activities requiring focus.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Avoid alcohol as it can cause excessive drowsiness.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If you experience symptoms like watery diarrhea, fever, or persistent stomach pain, consult your doctor.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not use for longer than 7 days without consulting a doctor.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><p>4o mini</p>', 'active', '1395_1742445205.png', 33, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 09:33:25', '2025-03-20 09:33:25'),
(3602, 'Paridon', 'Domperidone Maleate', 3.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications:</h3><ul><li><strong>Dyspeptic symptoms</strong>: Includes fullness, upper abdominal pain, early satiety, nausea, vomiting, and heartburn.</li><li><strong>Parkinson\'s Disease</strong>: Specifically for dopamine-agonist-induced nausea and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Radiological studies</strong>: To speed up barium transit in follow-through radiological studies.</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology:</h3><ul><li><strong>Domperidone</strong> is a dopamine antagonist, mainly acting on the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and the gastrointestinal tract.</li><li>It helps with gastric motility, speeds up gastric emptying, and increases esophageal peristalsis.</li><li><strong>Limited brain penetration</strong>: Domperidone does not affect the brain’s dopaminergic receptors significantly, which helps avoid neurological side effects.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration:</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10-20 mg every 6-8 hours (maximum dose: 80 mg/day).</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Based on body weight (0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 6-8 hours).</li><li>It should be taken 15-30 minutes before meals.</li><li><strong>Maximum treatment period for acute conditions</strong>: 12 weeks.</li></ul><h3>Interactions:</h3><ul><li>Avoid <strong>anticholinergic drugs</strong> and certain <strong>CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics) due to increased plasma levels.</li><li>May interact with <strong>dopaminergic agonists</strong> like bromocriptine and L-dopa, but does not potentiate their action.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects:</h3><ul><li>Rare side effects include transient intestinal cramps, extrapyramidal symptoms (mostly in children), and possible increased prolactin levels leading to conditions like galactorrhea or gynaecomastia.</li><li>Allergic reactions like rash or urticaria are also rare.</li></ul><h3>Warnings &amp; Precautions:</h3><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Risk of extrapyramidal symptoms due to an immature blood-brain barrier.</li><li><strong>Liver impairment</strong>: Caution is needed, as domperidone is metabolized in the liver.</li><li><strong>Kidney impairment</strong>: Dosing frequency may need adjustment.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</h3><ul><li>Should be used cautiously during pregnancy, particularly in the first trimester.</li><li>Not recommended during breastfeeding unless the benefits outweigh the risks.</li></ul><h3>Overdose:</h3><ul><li>Symptoms include drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions, especially in children. Activated charcoal and close monitoring are recommended.</li></ul><h3>Storage:</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture, and out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '688_1742445436.jpg', 9, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 09:37:16', '2025-03-20 09:37:16'),
(3603, 'Deflux Meltab', 'Domperidone Maleate', 3.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications:</h3><ul><li><strong>Dyspeptic Symptoms</strong>: Often linked to delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis. Symptoms may include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Epigastric fullness, abdominal distension, and upper abdominal pain</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Eructation, flatulence, and early satiety</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea and vomiting</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Heartburn with or without regurgitation of gastric contents</li><li><strong>Non-Ulcer Dyspepsia</strong>: Indigestion without ulceration.</li><li><strong>Acute Nausea and Vomiting</strong>: Of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or in migraine.</li><li><strong>Parkinson\'s Disease</strong>: For dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Radiological Studies</strong>: To accelerate barium transit in follow-through radiological studies.</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology:</h3><ul><li><strong>Domperidone</strong> (active ingredient) is a dopamine antagonist that blocks dopamine receptors located in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and the stomach. It enhances gastrointestinal motility and facilitates gastric emptying. It also increases esophageal peristalsis and lowers esophageal sphincter pressure, which prevents gastric regurgitation.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration:</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours. Maximum daily dose: 80 mg.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg body weight every 6-8 hours.</li><li><strong>Acute conditions</strong>: 20 mg every 6-8 hours (Adults). In acute nausea and vomiting, treatment duration should not exceed 12 weeks.</li><li><strong>Rectal Administration (Suppositories)</strong>: 30-60 mg every 4-8 hours (Adults).</li></ul><h3>Contraindications:</h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Domperidone.</li><li>Neonates and patients with gastrointestinal issues like hemorrhage, obstruction, or perforation.</li><li>Patients with prolactin-releasing pituitary tumors (prolactinoma).</li></ul><h3>Side Effects:</h3><ul><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Transient intestinal cramps, extrapyramidal phenomena (mainly in young children), and hyperprolactinemia leading to conditions like galactorrhea and gynecomastia.</li><li><strong>Allergic reactions</strong>: Rash and urticaria.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</h3><ul><li>Use during pregnancy only when necessary and under medical supervision.</li><li>Not recommended during lactation as the drug is excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</h3><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Increased risk of extrapyramidal reactions due to the immature blood-brain barrier.</li><li><strong>Liver Impairment</strong>: Use with caution in liver dysfunction.</li><li><strong>Kidney Impairment</strong>: In severe renal insufficiency, reduce dosing frequency.</li></ul><h3>Overdose:</h3><ul><li>Symptoms include drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions (especially in children). Activated charcoal and observation are recommended.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class:</h3><ul><li>Motility Stimulants, Dopamine Antagonist, Prokinetic Drugs.</li></ul><h3>Storage:</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protect from light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><h3>Quick Tips:</h3><ul><li>Take <strong>Deflux Meltab 10 mg</strong> before meals as prescribed.</li><li>Avoid alcohol, as it may cause excessive drowsiness.</li><li>If side effects like dry mouth occur, frequent mouth rinses and good oral hygiene can help.</li><li>Consult a doctor if symptoms persist for more than 7 days.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1062_1742445673.jpg', 3, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 09:41:13', '2025-03-20 09:41:13'),
(3604, 'Sagdon', 'Domperidone Maleate', 2.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong>:</p><ul><li>Relieves symptoms of dyspepsia (e.g., epigastric fullness, nausea, vomiting, heartburn).</li><li>Used for nausea and vomiting related to functional, organic, infectious, and drug-induced conditions.</li><li>Also indicated for speeding barium transit in radiological studies.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Domperidone</strong> blocks dopamine receptors, improving gastrointestinal motility and reducing symptoms like nausea and vomiting.</li><li>It doesn\'t cross the blood-brain barrier significantly, reducing the risk of central nervous system side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage</strong>:</p><ul><li>For adults, 10-20 mg every 6-8 hours, up to a maximum of 80 mg daily.</li><li>For children, dosing is based on body weight, with a typical dose every 6-8 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li>Rare side effects include intestinal cramps and extrapyramidal symptoms, especially in children.</li><li>Possible increase in prolactin levels, leading to conditions like galactorrhoea (milk production) and gynaecomastia (breast development in men).</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Use cautiously in children due to the risk of extrapyramidal symptoms.</li><li>Should be used with caution in patients with liver or kidney disorders.</li><li>Not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li>Symptoms may include drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions, particularly in children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1695_1742445891.jpg', 58, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 09:44:51', '2025-03-20 09:44:51');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3605, 'Vomitop', 'Domperidone Maleate', 3.20, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Dyspeptic symptoms</strong> like epigastric fullness, abdominal distension, upper abdominal pain, belching, flatulence, early satiety, nausea, vomiting, and heartburn (with or without regurgitation).</li><li><strong>Non-ulcer dyspepsia</strong>.</li><li><strong>Acute nausea and vomiting</strong> caused by functional, organic, infectious, dietetic factors, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine.</li><li><strong>Parkinson\'s disease</strong>: Dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting.</li><li>Used in <strong>radiological studies</strong> to speed up barium transit.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Domperidone</strong> is a dopamine antagonist that works by blocking dopamine receptors in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and stomach.</li><li>Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier minimizes central nervous system side effects, offering a safer profile with minimal psychotropic effects.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily (maximum 80 mg daily).</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily.</li><li>Take <strong>15-30 minutes before meals</strong>, and if needed, before bedtime.</li><li>In <strong>acute conditions</strong>, such as acute nausea/vomiting, the maximum treatment duration is <strong>12 weeks</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Anticholinergic drugs</strong>, <strong>antacids</strong>, and <strong>antisecretory drugs</strong> may reduce the effectiveness of <strong>Domperidone</strong>.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics) can increase <strong>Domperidone</strong> levels in the blood.</li><li><strong>Neuroleptics</strong>: <strong>Domperidone</strong> doesn\'t potentiate their action but suppresses side effects like nausea and vomiting caused by dopaminergic agonists.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to the drug.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal hemorrhage</strong>, <strong>mechanical obstruction</strong>, or <strong>perforation</strong>.</li><li><strong>Prolactinoma</strong> (pituitary tumor).</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Transient intestinal cramps, extrapyramidal symptoms (rare in adults, more common in children), increased <strong>prolactin</strong> levels causing <strong>galactorrhea</strong> (milk production) and <strong>gynecomastia</strong> (breast enlargement in men).</li><li>Rare <strong>allergic reactions</strong>, such as rash and urticaria.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Not recommended during <strong>pregnancy</strong> unless necessary, as its safety has not been fully established.</li><li>Excreted in <strong>breast milk</strong> but in minimal quantities; nursing is not recommended during treatment unless the benefits outweigh the risks.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Use cautiously in <strong>children</strong>, especially those under 1 year, due to the risk of extrapyramidal symptoms.</li><li>Should be used carefully in patients with <strong>liver or kidney disorders</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Overdose symptoms may include <strong>drowsiness</strong>, <strong>disorientation</strong>, and <strong>extrapyramidal reactions</strong>.</li><li>Treatment includes <strong>activated charcoal</strong>, and in case of extrapyramidal symptoms, <strong>anticholinergic drugs</strong> or <strong>antiparkinson drugs</strong> may help.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, protected from light and moisture, and keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Motility Stimulants</strong>, <strong>Dopamine Antagonists</strong>, <strong>Prokinetic Drugs</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '648_1742446180.jpg', 62, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 09:49:40', '2025-03-20 09:49:40'),
(3606, 'Domidon', 'Domperidone Maleate', 3.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Dyspeptic symptoms</strong> (e.g., epigastric fullness, abdominal distension, pain, belching, flatulence, early satiety, nausea, vomiting, heartburn).</li><li><strong>Conditions</strong> like non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea/vomiting from various causes (functional, organic, infectious, dietetic, radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine).</li><li><strong>Parkinson’s disease</strong>: Dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting.</li><li>Used in <strong>radiological studies</strong> to speed up barium transit.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Domidon</strong> is a <strong>dopamine receptor antagonist</strong> that promotes gastroprokinetic action by blocking dopamine receptors in the stomach and the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ).</li><li>Its limited ability to cross the <strong>blood-brain barrier</strong> minimizes central nervous system side effects.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily (max. 80 mg/day).</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily.</li><li>It is advised to take the medication <strong>15-30 minutes before meals</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Anticholinergic drugs</strong> may reduce its antidyspeptic effects.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., azole antifungals, macrolides) may increase its plasma levels.</li><li><strong>Antacids and antisecretory drugs</strong> should not be taken together as they decrease its oral bioavailability.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to the drug or neonates.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal hemorrhage</strong>, <strong>obstruction</strong>, or <strong>perforation</strong>.</li><li><strong>Prolactinoma</strong> (pituitary tumor).</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Transient intestinal cramps, extrapyramidal symptoms in children, increased prolactin leading to <strong>galactorrhea</strong> (milk production) and <strong>gynecomastia</strong> (enlarged breasts in men).</li><li><strong>Allergic reactions</strong> like rash and urticaria.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Safety during <strong>pregnancy</strong> is not established; avoid unless necessary.</li><li><strong>Excreted in breast milk</strong> but in minimal amounts; generally not harmful.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Use cautiously in <strong>children</strong>, especially those under 1 year, due to the risk of neurological side effects.</li><li>Caution in patients with <strong>hepatic or renal disorders</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Symptoms may include <strong>drowsiness</strong>, <strong>disorientation</strong>, and <strong>extrapyramidal symptoms</strong>.</li><li>Treatment: <strong>Activated charcoal</strong>, and if necessary, <strong>anticholinergic drugs</strong> or <strong>antiparkinson drugs</strong> to manage side effects.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from light and moisture, and keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Motility Stimulants</strong>, <strong>Dopamine Antagonists</strong>, <strong>Prokinetic Drugs</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Blocks dopamine receptors in the stomach and CTZ, promoting gastric emptying, enhancing peristalsis, regulating pyloric contractions, and increasing esophageal motility, preventing regurgitation of gastric contents.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Infants</strong>: Use with caution due to the immature blood-brain barrier and potential for side effects.</li><li><strong>Liver or kidney disorders</strong>: Adjustments may be needed due to extensive metabolism in the liver or impaired renal elimination.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '394_1742446440.jpg', 27, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 09:54:00', '2025-03-20 09:54:00'),
