i></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Transient intestinal cramps, extrapyramidal symptoms (more common in children), increased prolactin leading to <strong>galactorrhea</strong> and <strong>gynecomastia</strong>.</li><li><strong>Allergic reactions</strong> (e.g., rash, urticaria) are infrequent.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Safety not established; avoid unless necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It may enhance <strong>lactation</strong>, and is excreted in minimal amounts in breast milk, which is generally considered not harmful.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Increased risk of <strong>extrapyramidal reactions</strong>, especially with an immature blood-brain barrier.</li><li>Caution in patients with <strong>hepatic</strong> or <strong>renal disorders</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Overdose may cause <strong>drowsiness</strong>, <strong>disorientation</strong>, and <strong>extrapyramidal symptoms</strong> (especially in children).</li><li>Treatment: <strong>Activated charcoal</strong>, and use <strong>anticholinergic drugs</strong> or <strong>antiparkinson drugs</strong> to manage extrapyramidal symptoms.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Motility Stimulants</strong>, <strong>Dopamine Antagonists</strong>, <strong>Prokinetic Drugs</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Acts by blocking dopamine receptors, enhancing gastrointestinal motility, regulating pylorus contractions, and increasing gastric emptying. It also increases esophageal peristalsis and lowers esophageal sphincter pressure to prevent gastric regurgitation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Infants</strong>: Use cautiously due to potential neurological side effects from immature blood-brain barrier.</li><li><strong>Liver</strong> or <strong>Kidney disorders</strong>: Adjustments needed for patients with liver or renal impairments due to altered drug metabolism and elimination.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '785_1742446680.jpg', 43, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 09:58:00', '2025-03-20 09:58:00'),
(3609, 'Perion', 'Domperidone Maleate', 2.30, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Perion 10 mg Tablet:</h3><p>Perion is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ol><li><strong>Dyspeptic Symptom Complex</strong>, often associated with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, which may include:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Epigastric fullness</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Abdominal distension</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Upper abdominal pain</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Eructation (belching)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Flatulence</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Early satiety (feeling full quickly)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea and vomiting</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Non-Ulcer Dyspepsia</strong> (indigestion without an ulcer)</li><li><strong>Acute Nausea and Vomiting</strong>, which may be functional, organic, infectious, dietetic, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine.</li><li><strong>Parkinson’s Disease</strong>: For nausea and vomiting induced by dopamine-agonist therapy.</li><li><strong>Radiological Studies</strong>: To speed up barium transit during follow-through radiological exams.</li></ol><h3>Pharmacology:</h3><p>Perion (Domperidone) is a dopamine antagonist that primarily blocks dopamine receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and stomach. This results in its gastroprokinetic effect, restoring normal motility and tone in the upper gastrointestinal tract, promoting gastric emptying, and preventing gastric reflux. Due to its weak penetration across the blood-brain barrier, it avoids central nervous system side effects.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration:</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours. The maximum daily dose is 80 mg.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 6-8 hours (2-4 ml suspension/10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml pediatric drops/10 kg).</li><li>For acute and sub-acute conditions: 20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours.</li><li><strong>Rectal administration</strong> (suppositories):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults (including elderly): 30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children: Max 30 mg/day for children weighing 10-25 kg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The maximum treatment duration is 12 weeks.</li></ul><h3>Interaction:</h3><ul><li><strong>Anticholinergic drugs</strong> may counteract the antidyspeptic effect of domperidone.</li><li><strong>Antacids</strong> and <strong>antisecretory drugs</strong> should not be used together with domperidone as they reduce its bioavailability.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics) may increase plasma levels of domperidone.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications:</h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to domperidone.</li><li>Neonates.</li><li>Gastrointestinal issues such as hemorrhage, obstruction, or perforation.</li><li>Prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma).</li></ul><h3>Side Effects:</h3><ul><li>Rare side effects include transient intestinal cramps and rare extrapyramidal reactions (mainly in children).</li><li>Hyperprolactinemia (increased prolactin levels) can cause galactorrhea and gynecomastia.</li><li>Rare allergic reactions, such as rash and urticaria.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong>: Use during pregnancy is not recommended unless absolutely necessary. It is excreted in breast milk, but at lower concentrations, and should be avoided in breastfeeding unless the benefits outweigh risks.</li></ul><h3>Precautions:</h3><ul><li>Use with caution in children due to the potential for extrapyramidal effects.</li><li>Caution in patients with liver or kidney impairments.</li><li>Domperidone should be used with caution in patients with a history of gastrointestinal issues or severe renal insufficiency.</li></ul><h3>Overdose:</h3><p>Symptoms may include drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal symptoms, especially in children. Treatment includes activated charcoal and close observation.</p><h3>Storage Conditions:</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Quick Tips:</h3><ul><li>Take Perion 10 mg before meals.</li><li>May cause dizziness and sleepiness—avoid driving or operating machinery.</li><li>Avoid alcohol as it may increase drowsiness.</li><li>If you experience watery diarrhea, fever, or persistent stomach pain, contact your doctor.</li><li>Do not use for more than 7 days without medical advice.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '994_1742446895.jpg', 64, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 10:01:35', '2025-03-20 10:01:35'),
(3610, 'Perion', 'Domperidone Maleate', 2.30, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Perion 10 mg Tablet:</h3><p>Perion is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ol><li><strong>Dyspeptic Symptom Complex</strong>, often associated with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, which may include:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Epigastric fullness</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Abdominal distension</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Upper abdominal pain</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Eructation (belching)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Flatulence</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Early satiety (feeling full quickly)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea and vomiting</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Non-Ulcer Dyspepsia</strong> (indigestion without an ulcer)</li><li><strong>Acute Nausea and Vomiting</strong>, which may be functional, organic, infectious, dietetic, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine.</li><li><strong>Parkinson’s Disease</strong>: For nausea and vomiting induced by dopamine-agonist therapy.</li><li><strong>Radiological Studies</strong>: To speed up barium transit during follow-through radiological exams.</li></ol><h3>Pharmacology:</h3><p>Perion (Domperidone) is a dopamine antagonist that primarily blocks dopamine receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and stomach. This results in its gastroprokinetic effect, restoring normal motility and tone in the upper gastrointestinal tract, promoting gastric emptying, and preventing gastric reflux. Due to its weak penetration across the blood-brain barrier, it avoids central nervous system side effects.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration:</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours. The maximum daily dose is 80 mg.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 6-8 hours (2-4 ml suspension/10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml pediatric drops/10 kg).</li><li>For acute and sub-acute conditions: 20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours.</li><li><strong>Rectal administration</strong> (suppositories):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults (including elderly): 30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children: Max 30 mg/day for children weighing 10-25 kg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The maximum treatment duration is 12 weeks.</li></ul><h3>Interaction:</h3><ul><li><strong>Anticholinergic drugs</strong> may counteract the antidyspeptic effect of domperidone.</li><li><strong>Antacids</strong> and <strong>antisecretory drugs</strong> should not be used together with domperidone as they reduce its bioavailability.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics) may increase plasma levels of domperidone.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications:</h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to domperidone.</li><li>Neonates.</li><li>Gastrointestinal issues such as hemorrhage, obstruction, or perforation.</li><li>Prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma).</li></ul><h3>Side Effects:</h3><ul><li>Rare side effects include transient intestinal cramps and rare extrapyramidal reactions (mainly in children).</li><li>Hyperprolactinemia (increased prolactin levels) can cause galactorrhea and gynecomastia.</li><li>Rare allergic reactions, such as rash and urticaria.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong>: Use during pregnancy is not recommended unless absolutely necessary. It is excreted in breast milk, but at lower concentrations, and should be avoided in breastfeeding unless the benefits outweigh risks.</li></ul><h3>Precautions:</h3><ul><li>Use with caution in children due to the potential for extrapyramidal effects.</li><li>Caution in patients with liver or kidney impairments.</li><li>Domperidone should be used with caution in patients with a history of gastrointestinal issues or severe renal insufficiency.</li></ul><h3>Overdose:</h3><p>Symptoms may include drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal symptoms, especially in children. Treatment includes activated charcoal and close observation.</p><h3>Storage Conditions:</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Quick Tips:</h3><ul><li>Take Perion 10 mg before meals.</li><li>May cause dizziness and sleepiness—avoid driving or operating machinery.</li><li>Avoid alcohol as it may increase drowsiness.</li><li>If you experience watery diarrhea, fever, or persistent stomach pain, contact your doctor.</li><li>Do not use for more than 7 days without medical advice.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '131_1742446928.jpg', 64, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 10:02:08', '2025-05-12 17:54:01'),
(3611, 'Prokinet', 'Domperidone Maleate', 2.02, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications:</h3><ul><li>Dyspeptic symptoms (e.g., fullness, bloating, upper abdominal pain)</li><li>Heartburn with or without regurgitation</li><li>Non-ulcer dyspepsia</li><li>Acute nausea and vomiting</li><li>Parkinson’s disease (dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting)</li><li>Speeding up barium transit in radiological studies</li></ul><h3>Dosage:</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10-20 mg every 6-8 hours. Maximum 80 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 6-8 hours.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects:</h3><ul><li>Rare intestinal cramps</li><li>Extrapyramidal symptoms (in young children)</li><li>Possible increase in prolactin levels leading to galactorrhoea or gynaecomastia</li><li>Allergic reactions like rash or urticaria</li></ul><h3>Warnings:</h3><ul><li><strong>Caution in children</strong>, liver or kidney disorders</li><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Use only if the benefit outweighs the risk.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3>Overdose:</h3><p>Symptoms may include drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions. Activated charcoal and monitoring are recommended for overdose.</p>', 'active', '42_1742447124.webp', 11, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 10:05:24', '2025-03-20 10:05:24'),
(3612, 'Domilin', 'Domperidone Maleate', 4.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Dyspeptic symptoms</strong> (epigastric fullness, abdominal pain, bloating, early satiety, nausea, heartburn, etc.)</li><li><strong>Parkinson’s disease</strong>: For dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Radiological studies</strong>: To accelerate barium transit in follow-through studies.</li><li><strong>Acute nausea and vomiting</strong>: Due to functional, organic, infectious, dietetic causes, radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Domperidone blocks dopamine receptors in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and stomach, enhancing gastrointestinal motility.</li><li>It improves gastric emptying, increases esophageal peristalsis, and prevents regurgitation of gastric content.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10-20 mg every 6-8 hours. Maximum 80 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg body weight every 6-8 hours.</li><li><strong>Acute conditions</strong>: 20 mg every 6-8 hours (adults), 0.2-0.4 mg/kg body weight (children).</li><li><strong>Rectal suppositories</strong>: 30-60 mg every 4-8 hours for adults.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Intestinal cramps, extrapyramidal reactions (more common in children), increased prolactin levels (leading to galactorrhoea and gynaecomastia), allergic reactions (rash, urticaria).</li></ul><h3><strong>Warnings</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Caution due to the risk of extrapyramidal reactions.</li><li><strong>Liver or kidney disorders</strong>: Use with caution.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Only during the first trimester if the benefit outweighs the risk.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Not recommended unless benefits justify the risk.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Symptoms include drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions. Activated charcoal and monitoring are advised.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture, and out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><h3><strong>Quick Tips</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Take before meals.</li><li>May cause dizziness and sleepiness; avoid driving or tasks requiring focus.</li><li>Avoid alcohol as it may cause excessive drowsiness.</li><li>Stay hydrated to counteract dry mouth.</li><li>Consult a doctor if diarrhea, fever, or persistent stomach pain occurs.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1982_1742447302.jpg', 37, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 10:08:22', '2025-03-20 10:08:22'),
(3613, 'Loval', 'Domperidone Maleate', 2.01, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Loval is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Dyspeptic symptom complex, often associated with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Epigastric sense of fullness, feeling of abdominal distension, upper abdominal pain</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Eructation, flatulence, early satiety</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea and vomiting</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Heartburn with or without regurgitations of gastric contents in the mouth</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Non-ulcer dyspepsia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin or induced by radiotherapy or drug therapy or induced in migraine.</li><li>Parkinson\'s disease: In dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting.</li><li>Radiological studies: Speeding barium transit in follow-through radiological studies.</li><li> <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Domperidone is a dopamine antagonist that principally blocks dopamine receptors located in the ChemoreceptorTrigger Zone (CTZ) and stomach. Its gastroprokinetic action is based on its blocking effect of dopamine receptors that influence the motility of the gastrointestinal tract. Due to its weak penetration across the blood-brain barrier, Domperidone has almost no effect on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus excluding psychotropic and neurologic side effects. It restores normal motility and tone of the upper gastrointestinal tract, facilitates gastric emptying, enhances antral and duodenal peristalsis, and regulates contraction of the pylorus. Domperidone also increases esophageal peristalsis and lower esophageal sphincter pressure, thus preventing regurgitation of gastric content.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li>Domperidone should be taken 15-30 minutes before meals and, if necessary, before retiring.</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily. The maximum dose of Domperidone is 80 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 2-4 ml suspension/10 kg body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml pediatric drops/10 kg body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>In dyspeptic symptoms</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension), every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml suspension/10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml pediatric drops/10 kg) body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>In acute and sub-acute conditions</strong> (mainly in acute nausea and vomiting):</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension), every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml suspension/10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml pediatric drops/10 kg) body weight, every 6-8 hours daily. (In acute nausea and vomiting, maximum treatment period is 12 weeks).</li></ul><p><strong>By rectum in suppositories</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong> (including elderly): 30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Maximum daily rectal dose in children weighing 10 to 25 kg is 30 mg. The dose may be divided throughout the day if necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> Concomitant administration of anticholinergic drugs may antagonize the antidyspeptic effect of domperidone. Antacids and antisecretory drugs should not be given simultaneously with domperidone as they lower its oral bioavailability. The main metabolic pathway of domperidone is through CYP3A4. In vitro data suggest that the concomitant use of drugs that significantly inhibit this enzyme may result in increased plasma levels of domperidone.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Domperidone is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to this drug and in neonates. It should not be used when gastrointestinal stimulation might be dangerous (e.g., gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation). Domperidone is also contraindicated in patients with prolactin-releasing pituitary tumors (prolactinoma).</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Side effects are rare; however, some transient intestinal cramps have been reported. Extrapyramidal phenomena are rare in young children and exceptional in adults. These reverse spontaneously once the treatment is stopped. Rare allergic reactions, such as rash and urticaria, have also been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Domperidone should be used during the first trimester of pregnancy only if justified by the anticipated therapeutic benefit. The drug is excreted in breast milk, but the concentration is 4 times lower than corresponding plasma concentrations. It is not known if this is harmful to the newborn, so nursing mothers should avoid using this drug unless the benefits outweigh potential risks.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Loval should be used with caution in children due to the potential for extra-pyramidal reactions. It should also be used cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p> Symptoms may include drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions, especially in children. Activated charcoal and close observation are recommended in case of overdose.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '81_1742447488.jpg', 28, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 10:11:28', '2025-03-20 10:11:28'),
(3614, 'Mitosan Plus', 'Telmisartan + Hydrochlorothiazide', 21.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '80 mg+12.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Mitosan Plus tablet is indicated for the treatment of hypertension to lower blood pressure. It can be used alone or in combination with other antihypertensive agents. Lowering blood pressure helps reduce the risk of fatal and nonfatal cardiovascular events, such as strokes and myocardial infarctions.</p><ul><li>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন*</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> <strong>Telmisartan</strong>: Telmisartan works by blocking the angiotensin II receptors (AT1) in various tissues such as vascular smooth muscle and the adrenal gland. This inhibits the effects of angiotensin II, such as vasoconstriction, aldosterone secretion, and sodium reabsorption, thereby reducing blood pressure. Telmisartan has much greater affinity for the AT1 receptor than for the AT2 receptor.</p><p> <strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: This thiazide diuretic affects renal electrolyte reabsorption, causing an increase in sodium and chloride excretion. It indirectly reduces plasma volume, which leads to an increase in plasma renin activity, aldosterone secretion, urinary potassium loss, and a decrease in serum potassium levels. When combined with an angiotensin receptor blocker (ARB) like telmisartan, the potassium loss caused by hydrochlorothiazide is mitigated.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Starting Dose</strong>: 80 mg Telmisartan and 12.5 mg Hydrochlorothiazide once daily.</li><li><strong>Titration</strong>: If necessary, the dose can be increased to 160 mg/25 mg after 2 to 4 weeks.</li><li>Mitosan Plus can be combined with other antihypertensive medications if required.</li><li>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন*</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> Use caution if taking:</p><ul><li>Aliskiren</li><li>Digoxin</li><li>Lithium</li><li>Other high blood pressure medicines</li><li>NSAIDs (aspirin, ibuprofen, naproxen)</li><li>Corticosteroids</li><li>ACE inhibitors or ARBs</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Telmisartan, Hydrochlorothiazide, or other components of Mitosan Plus.</li><li>Do not co-administer with Aliskiren in patients with diabetes.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Common side effects include dizziness, drowsiness, back pain, nausea, diarrhea, and stomach pain. Other possible side effects include leg cramps, muscle spasms, photosensitivity, and weakness.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category D</strong>: The use of drugs affecting the renin-angiotensin system during the second and third trimesters can harm the fetus, leading to renal dysfunction, low amniotic fluid, and fetal death. Discontinue as soon as pregnancy is detected.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is unknown whether Telmisartan is excreted in human milk, but thiazides do appear in human milk. Nursing should be discontinued or the drug should be discontinued, depending on the need for the medication.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Tell your doctor if you have a history of kidney disease, liver disease, dehydration, electrolyte imbalance, or diabetes.</li><li>Check your blood sugar regularly if you have diabetes.</li><li>Avoid potassium supplements or salt substitutes without consulting your doctor.</li><li>Mitosan Plus may cause dizziness. Avoid driving or using heavy machinery until you are aware of how the medication affects you.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Elderly patients should start at a lower dose, considering possible age-related organ function decline.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Use with caution in patients with biliary obstructive disorders or hepatic insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Not recommended for severe renal impairment (Cr.Cl. &lt;30 ml/min). For mild or moderate renal impairment, no dose adjustment is required.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p> Symptoms of overdose may include dizziness, tachycardia, bradycardia, hypokalemia, dehydration, and electrolyte imbalances. Hemodialysis does not effectively remove Telmisartan, and the removal of Hydrochlorothiazide by hemodialysis is not established.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Combined antihypertensive preparations</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p>', 'active', '1349_1742447756.jpg', 57, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 10:15:56', '2025-03-20 10:15:56'),
(3615, 'Palostar', 'Palonosetron', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.5mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><span class=\"ql-cursor\">﻿</span><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Palostar is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Acute and delayed nausea and vomiting</li><li>Uncontrolled nausea and vomiting</li><li>Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute CINV on the day of treatment with certain chemotherapy drugs</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Delayed CINV in the days following treatment</li><li>Radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (RINV)</li><li>Post-operative nausea and vomiting (PONV) and Post-discharge nausea and vomiting (PDNV)</li><li>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন*</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Palonosetron is a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist, which has a strong binding affinity for the 5-HT3 receptors, primarily responsible for initiating the vomiting reflex. This medication blocks serotonin\'s actions at these receptors, reducing nausea and vomiting triggered by chemotherapy, radiation, and surgery. The mechanism involves blocking serotonin release from enterochromaffin cells in the small intestine, which activates the 5-HT3 receptors in the vagus nerve and the central chemoreceptor trigger zone.</p><p> Palonosetron has a longer half-life (40 hours) and greater receptor affinity than other 5-HT3 antagonists.</p><p><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: Palonosetron shows linear pharmacokinetics with a mean maximum plasma concentration (Cmax) ranging from 0.89 to 336 ng/ml.</li><li><strong>Metabolism</strong>: Approximately 50% of Palonosetron is metabolized into inactive metabolites by the liver (mainly via CYP2D6) and kidneys.</li><li><strong>Elimination</strong>: About 40% of the drug is excreted unchanged in the urine within 144 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult Tablet</strong>: 0.5 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Adult IV Dosage</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV): 0.25 mg IV 30 minutes before chemotherapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (RINV): 0.25 mg IV 30 minutes before each radiation fraction.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For post-operative nausea and vomiting (PONV): 0.075 mg IV just before anesthesia induction.</li><li><strong>Children (1 month to 17 years)</strong>: IV dose of 20 mcg/kg body weight (max dose: 1.5 mg).</li><li>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন*</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> Palostar is safe when used with corticosteroids, analgesics, antiemetics, antispasmodics, and anticholinergic agents. It does not inhibit the antitumor activity of common chemotherapy drugs and does not significantly interact with metoclopramide or major CYP enzymes. Therefore, clinically significant drug interactions are unlikely.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Palostar is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to the drug or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> The most common side effects are headaches and constipation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: It is not known whether Palonosetron is excreted in breast milk.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Use with caution while breastfeeding, as the excretion of Palonosetron in milk is not established.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly Patients</strong>: No dosage adjustment is required for patients over 65 years of age.</li><li><strong>Children (1 month to 10 years)</strong>: A single IV dose of 20 mcg/kg body weight, with a maximum dose of 1.5 mg.</li><li><strong>Renal and Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is recommended for patients with renal or hepatic dysfunction.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p> There is no known antidote for Palostar. Overdose should be managed with supportive care.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-emetic drugs</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place, and protect from light.</p>', 'active', '520_1742447980.jpg', 43, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 10:19:40', '2025-03-20 10:19:40');
