(3616, 'Palzen', 'Palonosetron', 25.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.5mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Palzen is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Acute and delayed nausea and vomiting</li><li>Uncontrolled nausea and vomiting</li><li>Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute CINV on the day of treatment with certain chemotherapy drugs</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Delayed CINV in the days following treatment</li><li>Radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (RINV)</li><li>Post-operative nausea and vomiting (PONV) and Post-discharge nausea and vomiting (PDNV)</li><li>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন*</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Palenosetron is a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist with a strong binding affinity for 5-HT3 receptors and little or no affinity for other receptors. It works by blocking serotonin, a chemical associated with nausea and vomiting, at 5-HT3 receptors. Chemotherapy and radiotherapy release serotonin from enterochromaffin cells in the small intestine, which activates 5-HT3 receptors to trigger vomiting. Palenosetron also works in the brain and small intestine to block this effect, reducing nausea and vomiting.</p><p><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></p><p> Palenosetron follows linear dose-proportional pharmacokinetics, with the maximum plasma concentration (Cmax) ranging from 0.89 to 336 ng/ml after intravenous administration. About 50% of Palenosetron is metabolized in the liver and kidneys, and 40% is excreted unchanged in the urine over 144 hours. It has a long half-life of 40 hours and high binding affinity for 5-HT3 receptors.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult Tablet Dosage</strong>: 0.5 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Adult IV Dosage</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV): 0.25 mg IV administered 30 minutes before chemotherapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (RINV): 0.25 mg IV administered 30 minutes before radiation.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Post-operative nausea and vomiting (PONV): 0.075 mg IV administered immediately before anesthesia induction.</li><li><strong>Children (1 month to 17 years)</strong>: IV dose of 20 mcg/kg body weight, maximum dose of 1.5 mg.</li><li>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন*</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> Palzen has been safely used with corticosteroids, analgesics, antiemetics, and anticholinergic agents in clinical trials. There are no significant pharmacokinetic interactions with metoclopramide, and Palzen does not inhibit or induce the activity of major CYP enzymes, suggesting low potential for clinically significant drug interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Palzen is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to the drug or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> The most common side effects are headaches and constipation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: Safety in pregnancy has not been well established, so it should be used with caution.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether Palenosetron is excreted in breast milk, so it should be used cautiously during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustment is required for elderly patients over 65 years of age.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: A single IV dose of 20 mcg/kg body weight, maximum dose of 1.5 mg, is recommended for children aged 1 month to 10 years.</li><li><strong>Renal and Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is recommended for patients with renal or hepatic dysfunction.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p> There is no known antidote for Palzen overdose. Management should focus on supportive care.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-emetic drugs</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</p><p><br></p><p>4o mini</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p>O</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p>Search</p><p>Reason</p><p><br></p><p><br></p>', 'active', '1197_1742448260.webp', 1, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 10:24:20', '2025-03-20 10:24:20'),
(3617, 'Palosis', 'Palonosetron', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.5mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Palosis is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Acute and delayed nausea and vomiting</li><li>Uncontrolled nausea and vomiting</li><li>Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute CINV occurring on the day of chemotherapy treatment</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Delayed CINV occurring days later after chemotherapy</li><li>Radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (RINV)</li><li>Post-operative nausea and vomiting (PONV) and Post-discharge nausea and vomiting (PDNV)</li><li>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন*</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Palonosetron is a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist that binds strongly to the 5-HT3 receptors, preventing serotonin-induced nausea and vomiting. It is believed that chemotherapy agents release serotonin from enterochromaffin cells in the small intestine, activating the 5-HT3 receptors on nerve terminals, which triggers the vomiting reflex. Palonosetron blocks serotonin from activating these receptors, thereby preventing nausea and vomiting. It may work both in the small intestine and the brain to achieve this effect.</p><p><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></p><p> Palonosetron follows linear dose-proportional pharmacokinetics. After intravenous administration, the mean maximum plasma concentration (Cmax) ranges from 0.89 to 336 ng/ml, and the area under the plasma concentration-time curve (AUC) ranges from 13.8 to 957 ng.h/ml. The drug is approximately 62% bound to plasma proteins and undergoes metabolism via liver (CYP2D6, CYP3A4, and CYP1A2) and kidneys. About 40% of the drug is excreted unchanged in the urine after 144 hours. The half-life is around 40 hours, and it exhibits a high affinity for 5-HT3 receptors.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult Tablet Dosage</strong>: 0.5 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Adult IV Dosage</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV): 0.25 mg IV administered over 30 seconds, approximately 30 minutes before chemotherapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (RINV): 0.25 mg IV administered over 30 seconds, approximately 30 minutes before each radiation fraction.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Post-operative nausea and vomiting (PONV): 0.075 mg IV administered over 10 seconds, immediately before anesthesia induction.</li><li><strong>Children (1 month to 17 years)</strong>: 20 mcg/kg body weight, with a maximum dose of 1.5 mg.</li><li>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন*</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> In clinical trials, Palosis has been safely administered with corticosteroids, analgesics, antiemetics, antispasmodics, and anticholinergic agents. It does not inhibit the antitumor activity of chemotherapy drugs like cisplatin, cyclophosphamide, cytarabine, doxorubicin, or mitomycin C. There are no significant pharmacokinetic interactions with metoclopramide, and Palosis does not inhibit or induce major CYP enzymes (CYP1A2, CYP2D6, CYP3A4), indicating a low risk of clinically significant drug interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Palonosetron is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to the drug or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Common side effects include headaches and constipation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: Palonosetron should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed, as its safety has not been fully established.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is unknown if Palonosetron is excreted in breast milk, so it should be used with caution while breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustment is recommended for patients over 65 years of age.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: A single IV dose of 20 mcg/kg body weight, with a maximum dose of 1.5 mg, is recommended for children aged 1 month to 10 years.</li><li><strong>Renal and Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is recommended for patients with renal or hepatic dysfunction.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p> There is no known antidote for Palosis overdose. Supportive care should be provided in case of overdose.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-emetic drugs</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</p>', 'active', '311_1742448493.jpg', 26, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 10:28:13', '2025-03-20 10:28:13'),
