aily.</li><li><strong>Adult IV Dosage</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV): 0.25 mg IV administered over 30 seconds, approximately 30 minutes before chemotherapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (RINV): 0.25 mg IV administered over 30 seconds, approximately 30 minutes before each radiation fraction.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Post-operative nausea and vomiting (PONV): 0.075 mg IV administered over 10 seconds, immediately before anesthesia induction.</li><li><strong>Children (1 month to 17 years)</strong>: 20 mcg/kg body weight, with a maximum dose of 1.5 mg.</li><li>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন*</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>In clinical trials, Palotic has been safely administered with corticosteroids, analgesics, antiemetics, antispasmodics, and anticholinergic agents. It does not inhibit the antitumor activity of chemotherapy drugs like cisplatin, cyclophosphamide, cytarabine, doxorubicin, or mitomycin C. There are no significant pharmacokinetic interactions with metoclopramide, and Palotic does not inhibit or induce major CYP enzymes (CYP1A2, CYP2D6, CYP3A4), suggesting a low risk of clinically significant drug interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Palonosetron is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to the drug or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common side effects include headaches and constipation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: Palonosetron should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed, as its safety has not been fully established.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is unknown if Palonosetron is excreted in breast milk, so it should be used with caution while breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustment is recommended for patients over 65 years of age.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: A single IV dose of 20 mcg/kg body weight, with a maximum dose of 1.5 mg, is recommended for children aged 1 month to 10 years.</li><li><strong>Renal and Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is recommended for patients with renal or hepatic dysfunction.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>There is no known antidote for Palotic overdose. Supportive care should be provided in case of overdose.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Anti-emetic drugs</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</p><p><br></p><p>4o mini</p>', 'in-active', '572_1742448749.webp', 17, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 10:32:29', '2025-05-12 17:50:53'),
(3620, 'Urinide', 'Bumetanide', 18.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Urinide 5 mg</strong></p><p> Urinide 5 mg is indicated for the treatment of edema associated with:</p><ul><li>Congestive heart failure</li><li>Hepatic ascites</li><li>Renal disease, including nephrotic syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Loop diuretics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Urinide 5 mg is a loop diuretic of the sulfamyl category used to treat heart failure. It is often employed in patients where high doses of furosemide are ineffective. There is, however, no reason not to use Urinide 5 mg as a first-choice drug. The key difference between Urinide and furosemide is bioavailability, making Urinide 5 mg a more predictable diuretic with more consistent absorption and effect. Urinide 5 mg is 40 times more potent than furosemide (for patients with normal renal function). It interferes with renal cAMP and/or inhibits the sodium-potassium ATPase pump. This action appears to block active reabsorption of chloride and possibly sodium in the ascending loop of Henle, altering electrolyte transfer in the proximal tubule. As a result, sodium, chloride, and water are excreted, causing diuresis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Urinide 5 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Oral</strong>:</p><ul><li>1 mg in the morning, repeated after 6-8 hours if necessary.</li><li>In severe cases, 5 mg daily, increased by 5 mg every 12-24 hours according to response.</li><li>Elderly: 500 micrograms daily may be sufficient.</li></ul><p><strong>Parenteral</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>By IV Injection</strong>: 1-2 mg, repeated after 20 minutes if necessary.</li><li>Elderly: 500 micrograms (1 ml of Urinide 5 mg) daily may be sufficient.</li><li><strong>By IV Infusion</strong>: 2-5 mg over 30-60 minutes.</li><li>Elderly: 500 micrograms (1 ml of Urinide 5 mg) daily may be sufficient.</li><li><strong>By IM Injection</strong>: 1 mg initially, then adjusted according to response.</li><li>Elderly: 500 micrograms (1 ml of Urinide 5 mg) daily may be sufficient.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Urinide 5 mg</strong></p><p> Concomitant use may potentiate the effects of antihypertensive drugs. It may increase potassium excretion, which can lead to an increase in the sensitivity of the myocardium to the toxic effects of digitalis. As with other diuretics, Urinide 5 mg may cause an increase in blood uric acid levels.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Loop diuretics should be avoided in:</p><ul><li>Severe hypokalemia</li><li>Severe hyponatremia</li><li>Anuria</li><li>Comatose and precomatose states associated with liver cirrhosis</li><li>Renal failure</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Urinide 5 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Fatigue</li><li>Postural hypotension</li><li>Gastrointestinal symptoms</li><li>Skin reactions</li><li>Photosensitivity</li><li>Metabolic disturbances (e.g., reduced glucose tolerance)</li><li>Electrolyte disturbances, especially during long-term treatment</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. It is unknown whether Urinide 5 mg is excreted in human milk.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Serum potassium should be measured periodically.</li><li>Potassium supplements or potassium-sparing diuretics may be necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Urinide 5 mg</strong></p><p> Symptoms of overdose would typically involve excessive diuresis. Empty the stomach by gastric lavage or induce emesis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place. Protect from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> <strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: The safety and effectiveness of Urinide 5 mg in pediatric patients under 18 have not been established.</p><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Loop diuretics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Urinide 5 mg is a loop diuretic belonging to the sulfamyl category. It is used to treat heart failure, especially in patients unresponsive to high doses of furosemide. It has more predictable absorption and potency, being 40 times more potent than furosemide in patients with normal renal function. It interferes with renal cAMP and inhibits the sodium-potassium ATPase pump, blocking active reabsorption of chloride and sodium in the ascending loop of Henle, leading to the excretion of sodium, chloride, and water, resulting in diuresis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. It is not known whether Urinide 5 mg is excreted in human milk.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><p> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients below the age of 18 have not been established.</p>', 'active', '814_1742448829.jpg', 62, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 10:33:49', '2025-03-20 10:33:49'),
(3621, 'Uristat', 'Febuxostat', 12.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the revised text with the headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Uristat 40 mg</strong></p><p> Uristat 40 mg is indicated for the chronic management of hyperuricemia in patients with gout. Uristat 40 mg is not recommended for the treatment of asymptomatic hyperuricemia.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs used in Gout</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Uristat 40 mg is a non-purine, selective xanthine oxidase (XO) inhibitor. It decreases serum uric acid level by inhibiting xanthine oxidase, which is responsible for uric acid production. Xanthine oxidase breaks down hypoxanthine to xanthine and thus to uric acid. Uristat 40 mg is not expected to inhibit other enzymes involved in purine and pyrimidine synthesis and metabolism at therapeutic concentrations.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Uristat 40 mg</strong></p><p> Uristat 40 mg is recommended at 40 mg or 80 mg once daily. The recommended starting dose of Uristat 40 mg is 40 mg once daily. For patients who do not achieve a serum uric acid level less than 6 mg/dL after 2 weeks with 40 mg, Uristat 40 mg 80 mg is recommended. Uristat 40 mg can be administered without regard to food or antacid use. No dose adjustment is necessary when administering Uristat 40 mg to patients with mild to moderate renal or hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Uristat 40 mg</strong></p><p> Concomitant administration of Uristat 40 mg with azathioprine, mercaptopurine, or theophylline could increase plasma concentrations of these drugs, resulting in severe toxicity.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Uristat 40 mg is contraindicated in patients being treated with azathioprine, mercaptopurine, or theophylline.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Uristat 40 mg</strong></p><p> The most common adverse events associated with the use of Uristat 40 mg may include liver function abnormalities, nausea, arthralgia, and rash.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Uristat 40 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Uristat 40 mg is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gout Flare:</strong> An increase in gout flares is frequently observed during initiation of anti-hyperuricemic agents, including Uristat 40 mg. If a gout flare occurs during treatment, Uristat 40 mg need not be discontinued. Prophylactic therapy (i.e., non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) or colchicine upon initiation of treatment) may be beneficial for up to six months.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Events:</strong> A higher rate of cardiovascular thromboembolic events was observed in patients treated with Uristat 40 mg than allopurinol in clinical trials. Monitor for signs and symptoms of MI and stroke.</li><li><strong>Liver Enzyme Elevation:</strong> Transaminase elevations have been observed in Uristat 40 mg-treated patients. Monitor liver function tests periodically.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Uristat 40 mg</strong></p><p> Febustat was studied in healthy subjects in doses up to 300 mg daily for seven days without evidence of dose-limiting toxicities.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients under 18 years of age have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Drugs used in Gout</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Uristat 40 mg is a non-purine, selective xanthine oxidase (XO) inhibitor. It decreases serum uric acid level by inhibiting xanthine oxidase, which is responsible for uric acid production. Xanthine oxidase breaks down hypoxanthine to xanthine and thus to uric acid. Uristat 40 mg is not expected to inhibit other enzymes involved in purine and pyrimidine synthesis and metabolism at therapeutic concentrations.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Uristat 40 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Uristat 40 mg is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Pediatric Use: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients under 18 years of age have not been established.</p>', 'active', '1158_1742448963.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 10:36:03', '2025-03-20 10:36:03'),
(3622, 'Uristat', 'Febuxostat', 22.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '80 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Uristat 80 mg</strong></p><p> Uristat 80 mg is indicated for the chronic management of hyperuricemia in patients with gout. Uristat 80 mg is not recommended for the treatment of asymptomatic hyperuricemia.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs used in Gout</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Uristat 80 mg is a non-purine, selective xanthine oxidase (XO) inhibitor. It decreases serum uric acid levels by inhibiting xanthine oxidase, which is responsible for uric acid production. Xanthine oxidase breaks down hypoxanthine to xanthine and then to uric acid. Uristat 80 mg is not expected to inhibit other enzymes involved in purine and pyrimidine synthesis and metabolism at therapeutic concentrations.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Uristat 80 mg</strong></p><p> Uristat 80 mg is recommended at 40 mg or 80 mg once daily. The recommended starting dose of Uristat 80 mg is 40 mg once daily. For patients who do not achieve a serum uric acid level of less than 6 mg/dL after 2 weeks with 40 mg, 80 mg is recommended. Uristat 80 mg can be administered without regard to food or antacid use. No dose adjustment is necessary for patients with mild to moderate renal or hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Uristat 80 mg</strong></p><p> Concomitant administration of Uristat 80 mg with azathioprine, mercaptopurine, or theophylline could increase plasma concentrations of these drugs, resulting in severe toxicity.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Uristat 80 mg is contraindicated in patients being treated with azathioprine, mercaptopurine, or theophylline.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Uristat 80 mg</strong></p><p> The most common adverse events associated with the use of Uristat 80 mg may include liver function abnormalities, nausea, arthralgia, and rash.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Uristat 80 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Uristat 80 mg is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gout Flare:</strong> An increase in gout flares is frequently observed during initiation of anti-hyperuricemic agents, including Uristat 80 mg. If a gout flare occurs during treatment, Uristat 80 mg need not be discontinued. Prophylactic therapy (i.e., non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) or colchicine upon initiation of treatment) may be beneficial for up to six months.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Events:</strong> A higher rate of cardiovascular thromboembolic events was observed in patients treated with Uristat 80 mg than allopurinol in clinical trials. Monitor for signs and symptoms of MI and stroke.</li><li><strong>Liver Enzyme Elevation:</strong> Transaminase elevations have been observed in Uristat 80 mg-treated patients. Monitor liver function tests periodically.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Uristat 80 mg</strong></p><p> Febuxostat was studied in healthy subjects in doses up to 300 mg daily for seven days without evidence of dose-limiting toxicities.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients under 18 years of age have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Drugs used in Gout</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Uristat 80 mg is a non-purine, selective xanthine oxidase (XO) inhibitor. It decreases serum uric acid levels by inhibiting xanthine oxidase, which is responsible for uric acid production. Xanthine oxidase breaks down hypoxanthine to xanthine and then to uric acid. Uristat 80 mg is not expected to inhibit other enzymes involved in purine and pyrimidine synthesis and metabolism at therapeutic concentrations.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Uristat 80 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Uristat 80 mg is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients under 18 years of age have not been established.</p>', 'active', '1616_1742449151.jpg', 30, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 10:39:11', '2025-03-20 10:39:11'),
(3623, 'Uritone', 'Furosemide + Spironolactone', 8.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg+50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the content you provided, formatted with bold headings for clarity:</p><p><strong>Indications of Uritone 20 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Essential hypertension</li><li>Chronic congestive heart failure</li><li>Hepatic cirrhosis, with collection of fluid in the abdominal cavity (ascites)</li><li>Swelling due to excess fluid retention (edema)</li><li>Hyperaldosteronism</li><li>Resistant edema associated with secondary hyperaldosteronism</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics</li><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics &amp; Aldosterone antagonists</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Spironolactone (potassium-sparing diuretic) and Furosemide (loop diuretic) have different but complementary mechanisms and sites of action. When given together, they produce an additive or synergistic diuretic effect. The Furosemide component inhibits the Na+/K+/2Cl- co-transporter in the ascending Loop of Henle, blocking the reabsorption of sodium, potassium, and chloride ions, thereby increasing sodium and water excretion in the urine. This induces potassium loss. The Spironolactone component inhibits sodium reabsorption in exchange for potassium at the distal tubule by antagonizing aldosterone, so sodium excretion is favored, and the excess potassium loss induced by Furosemide is reduced.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Uritone 20 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Furosemide 20 mg and Spironolactone 50 mg:</strong> 1 to 4 tablets daily (20 to 80 mg of Furosemide and 50 to 200 mg of Spironolactone) according to the patient’s response.</li><li><strong>Furosemide 40 mg and Spironolactone 50 mg:</strong> For previously stabilized patients requiring higher dosages, 1 to 2 tablets daily (Furosemide 40 to 80 mg and Spironolactone 50 to 100 mg).</li><li><strong>Use in children:</strong> Spironolactone and Furosemide are not suitable for children.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Spironolactone and Furosemide may be excreted more slowly in the elderly.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Uritone 20 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Increased risk of hyperkalemia when taken with ACE inhibitors or potassium salts.</li><li>Spironolactone increases the levels of cardiac glycosides (e.g., digoxin), which may lead to digitalis toxicity.</li><li>Corticosteroids may cause hypokalemia when used with Spironolactone.</li><li>Furosemide\'s blood pressure-lowering and diuretic effects may be reduced or abolished when used with indomethacin or other NSAIDs.</li><li>Furosemide may increase the ototoxicity of aminoglycoside antibiotics.</li><li>Simultaneous administration of sucralfate and Furosemide may reduce its natriuretic and antihypertensive effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with anuria, acute renal insufficiency, rapidly deteriorating renal function (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min), hyperkalemia, Addison\'s disease, and in patients hypersensitive to Spironolactone, Furosemide, or sulphonamides.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Uritone 20 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Spironolactone may cause headache, drowsiness, gastrointestinal distress (cramps, diarrhea), ataxia, mental confusion, skin rashes, and gynecomastia.</li><li>Endocrine side effects may include hirsutism, deepening of the voice, menstrual irregularities, and impotence.</li><li>Transient increase in blood urea nitrogen (BUN) concentrations and mild acidosis.</li><li>May cause hyponatremia and hyperkalemia.</li><li>Excessive diuresis can result in dehydration, reduced blood volume, circulatory collapse, and potential vascular thrombosis, especially in elderly patients.</li><li>Serious depletion of potassium and magnesium can lead to cardiac arrhythmias.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Spironolactone and its metabolites may cross the placental barrier. The use of spironolactone in pregnant women requires careful weighing of the anticipated benefit versus potential risks to the mother and fetus. Animal studies show that Furosemide may cause fetal abnormalities, so it should only be used with appropriate contraception or if potential benefits justify risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Spironolactone metabolites are detected in breast milk. If necessary, an alternative feeding method should be considered. Furosemide is also excreted in breast milk, and breastfeeding should be discontinued if treatment is essential.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be exercised in patients prone to electrolyte deficiencies.</li><li>Should be used cautiously in patients with diabetes, enlarged prostate, hypotension, and hypovolemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Uritone 20 mg+50 mg</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdose include drowsiness, mental confusion, dizziness, diarrhea, and vomiting due to excessive diuresis. Treatment should focus on fluid replacement and correcting any electrolyte imbalance.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children:</strong> Not suitable for use in children.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> May be excreted more slowly in elderly patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics</li><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics &amp; Aldosterone antagonists</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Spironolactone (potassium-sparing diuretic) and Furosemide (loop diuretic) work together to produce an additive or synergistic diuretic effect. Furosemide inhibits sodium, potassium, and chloride ion reabsorption in the ascending Loop of Henle, leading to increased sodium and water excretion, which typically causes potassium loss. Spironolactone works by antagonizing aldosterone, inhibiting sodium reabsorption in exchange for potassium, thus reducing potassium loss.</p><p>Let me know if you need further details or clarification!</p>', 'active', '1325_1742449278.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 10:41:18', '2025-05-07 10:26:39'),
(3624, 'Uritone', 'Furosemide + Spironolactone', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg+50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information you provided in a clearer format with normal bold text for the headings:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Uritone 40 mg+50 mg</strong></p><p>Furosemide &amp; Spironolactone combination is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Essential hypertension</li><li>Chronic congestive heart failure</li><li>Hepatic cirrhosis, with collection of fluid in the abdominal cavity (ascites)</li><li>Swelling due to excess fluid retention (edema)</li><li>Hyperaldosteronism</li><li>Resistant edema associated with secondary hyperaldosteronism</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics</li><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics &amp; Aldosterone antagonists</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Spironolactone (potassium-sparing diuretic) and Furosemide (loop diuretic) have different but complementary mechanisms and sites of action. Therefore, when given together, they produce additive or synergistic diuretic effects.</p><ul><li>The Furosemide component inhibits the Na+/K+/2Cl- co-transporter in the ascending Loop of Henle and blocks the reabsorption of sodium, potassium, and chloride ions; thereby increasing the quantity of sodium and the volume of water excreted in the urine. This characteristically induces potassium loss.</li><li>The Spironolactone component inhibits the reabsorption of sodium in exchange for potassium at the distal tubule by antagonizing the action of aldosterone, so that sodium excretion is greatly favored and the excess loss of potassium, induced by Furosemide, is reduced.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Uritone 40 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Furosemide 20 mg and Spironolactone 50 mg:</strong> 1 to 4 tablets daily (20 to 80 mg of Furosemide and 50 to 200 mg of Spironolactone) according to the patient’s response.</li><li><strong>Furosemide 40 mg and Spironolactone 50 mg:</strong> For previously stabilized patients requiring a higher dosage of Spironolactone and Furosemide, this tablet can be used at a dose of one to two tablets daily (Furosemide 40 to 80 mg and Spironolactone 50 to 100 mg).</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Children:</strong></p><p>Spironolactone and Furosemide are not suitable for use in children.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Uritone 40 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>When taken together with ACE inhibitors or potassium salts, there is an increased risk of hyperkalemia.</li><li>Spironolactone increases the levels of cardiac glycosides such as digoxin in the blood, potentially resulting in digitalis toxicity.</li><li>Corticosteroids may cause hypokalemia if used with Spironolactone.</li><li>The blood pressure-lowering and diuretic effects of Furosemide may be reduced or abolished when used together with indomethacin or other NSAIDs.</li><li>Furosemide may increase the ototoxicity of aminoglycoside antibiotics.</li><li>Simultaneous administration of sucralfate and Furosemide may reduce the natriuretic and anti-hypertensive effect of Furosemide.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Anuria</li><li>Acute renal insufficiency</li><li>Rapidly deteriorating or severe impairment of renal function (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min)</li><li>Hyperkalemia</li><li>Addison\'s disease</li><li>Hypersensitivity to Spironolactone, Furosemide, or sulphonamides.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Uritone 40 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Spironolactone may cause headache, drowsiness, gastrointestinal distress (cramps, diarrhea), ataxia, mental confusion, skin rashes, and gynecomastia.</li><li>Other endocrine disorders, including hirsutism, deepening of the voice, menstrual irregularities, and impotence.</li><li>Transient increase in blood urea nitrogen concentrations and mild acidosis have been reported.</li><li>Spironolactone may cause hyponatremia and hyperkalemia.</li><li>Excessive diuresis may result in dehydration, circulatory collapse, vascular thrombosis, and embolism (especially in elderly patients).</li><li>Serious depletion of potassium and magnesium may lead to cardiac arrhythmias.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Spironolactone and its metabolites may cross the placental barrier. The use of Spironolactone in pregnant women requires weighing the anticipated benefits against potential risks to the mother and fetus. Furosemide may cause fetal abnormalities, so it should only be used in women of childbearing age when appropriate contraceptive measures are taken or if the potential benefits justify the risks to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Spironolactone metabolites are detected in breast milk. If Spironolactone is essential, an alternative method of infant feeding should be considered. Furosemide is also excreted in breast milk, and breastfeeding should be discontinued if treatment is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be exercised in patients susceptible to electrolyte deficiency.</li><li>Should be used with caution in patients with diabetes, enlarged prostate, hypotension, or hypovolemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Uritone 40 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdosage include drowsiness, mental confusion, dizziness, diarrhea, and vomiting due to excessive diuresis.</li><li>Treatment should focus on fluid replacement and correction of any electrolyte imbalance.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in Children:</strong> Spironolactone and Furosemide are not suitable for children.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Spironolactone and Furosemide may be excreted more slowly in the elderly.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics</li><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics &amp; Aldosterone antagonists</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Spironolactone and Furosemide work through complementary mechanisms. Furosemide inhibits sodium, potassium, and chloride reabsorption in the Loop of Henle, while Spironolactone inhibits sodium reabsorption at the distal tubule by antagonizing aldosterone.</p><p>This breakdown maintains a clear, easy-to-read format with all the necessary details. Let me know if you need further clarification!</p>', 'active', '1601_1742449410.jpg', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 10:43:30', '2025-05-07 10:26:55');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3625, 'Urobak', 'Nitrofurantoin', 20.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Urobak 100 mg</strong></p><p>Urobak 100 mg is specifically indicated for the treatment and prophylaxis of urinary tract infections caused by susceptible strains of <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Enterococci</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em>, and certain susceptible strains of <em>Klebsiella</em> and <em>Enterobacter</em> species.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Systemic Urinary Anti-infective</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Urobak 100 mg interferes with cell metabolism and cell wall synthesis by inhibiting several enzyme systems, including acetyl coenzyme A. It is bactericidal to most gram-positive and gram-negative urinary tract pathogens.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Urobak 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet (Adults)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated urinary tract infections: 50-100 mg four times a day (the lower dosage is recommended). Therapy should be continued for one week or for at least 3 days after urine sterility is obtained.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Long-term suppressive therapy: 50-100 mg at bedtime may be adequate.</li><li><strong>Capsule (Adults)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute Uncomplicated Urinary Tract Infections (UTIs): 50 mg four times daily for 7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Long-term suppression: 50-100 mg once a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prophylaxis: 50 mg four times daily for the duration of the procedure and for three days thereafter.</li><li><strong>SR Capsule (Adults and Children over 12 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">One 100 mg capsule every 12 hours for seven days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Genito-urinary surgical prophylaxis: One capsule twice daily on the day of the procedure and for the next 3 days.</li><li><strong>Suspension (Children)</strong>:</li><li>5-7 mg/kg/day in four divided doses (contraindicated under one month of age). The average dose of Nintoin suspension for pediatric patients is as follows:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">7 to 11 kg: ½ (2.5 ml) teaspoonfuls 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">12 to 21 kg: 1 (5 ml) teaspoonful 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">22 to 30 kg: 1½ (7.5 ml) teaspoonfuls 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">31 to 41 kg: 2 (10 ml) teaspoonfuls 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Therapy should be continued for one week or at least 3 days after urine sterility is obtained. For long-term suppressive therapy in children, doses as low as 1 mg/kg per 24 hours, given in a single dose or in two divided doses, may be adequate.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Urobak 100 mg</strong></p><p>Urobak 100 mg should be taken with food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Urobak 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Antacids containing Magnesium Trisilicate, when administered with Urobak 100 mg, reduce both the rate and extent of absorption of Uricosuric drugs, such as Probenecid and Sulfinpyrazone, which can inhibit renal tubular secretion of Urobak 100 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Anuria, oliguria, or significant impairment of renal function.</li><li>Pregnant patients at 38-42 weeks, during labor and delivery.</li><li>Known hypersensitivity to Urobak 100 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Urobak 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Nausea, headache, flatulence.</li><li>Less common: Diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain, constipation, emesis, dizziness, drowsiness.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Use during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Urobak 100 mg is detected in human breast milk in trace amounts. Due to the potential for serious adverse reactions in nursing infants under one month of age, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, considering the drug\'s importance to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Discontinue Urobak 100 mg if acute, sub-acute, or chronic pulmonary reactions occur.</li><li>Antacid preparations containing magnesium trisilicate should not be taken while on Urobak 100 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Urobak 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Overdosage may result in vomiting. Induction of emesis is recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at or below 30°C. Keep away from light and out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Systemic Urinary Anti-infective</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Urobak 100 mg is an antibacterial agent specific for urinary tract infections. It is highly soluble in urine, to which it may impart a brown color. It inactivates or alters bacterial ribosomal proteins and other macromolecules, making it effective against various gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria, including <em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em>, <em>Coagulase-negative staphylococci</em>, <em>Enterococcus faecalis</em>, <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella oxytoca</em>, and others.</p>', 'active', '1606_1742449542.jpg', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 10:45:42', '2025-05-04 22:41:55'),
(3626, 'Urodel-K', 'Potassium Citrate + Citric Acid', 200.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Oral Solution', '1500 mg plus 250 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Urodel-K (1500 mg + 250 mg)/5 ml Oral Solution</h3><h4>Indications:</h4><ul><li>To relieve discomfort in urinary tract infections (UTIs)</li><li>To prevent kidney stones</li><li>With a uricosuric agent to prevent gout</li><li>To manage acidosis caused by kidney diseases</li></ul><h4>Therapeutic Class:</h4><ul><li>Prevention of repeated kidney stone formation</li><li>Urinary alkalinizing agent</li></ul><h4>Pharmacology:</h4><ul><li>Potassium Citrate is absorbed and metabolized to Potassium Bicarbonate, acting as a systemic alkalinizer. It works by alkalinizing the urine without causing systemic alkalosis at the recommended doses. Less than 5% of the Potassium Citrate is excreted in the urine unchanged.</li></ul><h4>Dosage and Administration:</h4><p><strong>To relieve discomfort in UTI:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 6 years:</strong> 10 ml, 3 times daily, diluted with 1 glass of water.</li><li><strong>Children 1-6 years:</strong> 5 ml, 3 times daily, diluted with ½ glass of water.</li></ul><p><strong>To prevent kidney stones and acidosis caused by kidney diseases:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 10-15 ml, 4 times daily, diluted with 1 glass of water.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> 5-10 ml, 4 times daily, diluted with ½ glass of water.</li></ul><p><strong>In gout therapy (with uricosuric agent):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 10-15 ml, 4 times daily, diluted with 1 glass of water.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> 5-10 ml, 4 times daily, diluted with ½ glass of water.</li></ul><h4>Interaction:</h4><ul><li>Concurrent use with potassium-containing medications, potassium-sparing diuretics, ACE inhibitors, or cardiac glycosides may lead to toxicity.</li></ul><h4>Contraindications:</h4><ul><li>Severe renal impairment with oliguria or azotemia</li><li>Untreated Addison\'s disease</li><li>Acute dehydration</li><li>Severe myocardial damage</li><li>Hyperkalemia from any cause</li></ul><h4>Side Effects:</h4><ul><li>Generally well tolerated at recommended doses.</li><li>Caution is needed for patients with abnormal renal mechanisms to avoid hyperkalemia or alkalosis.</li><li>Potassium intoxication symptoms include weakness, confusion, tingling, and other signs of high potassium levels.</li></ul><h4>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</h4><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> The combination should not be used unless the potential benefits justify the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is unknown if the drug passes into breast milk. Breastfeeding women should consult a healthcare provider before use.</li></ul><h4>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</h4><ul><li>Use cautiously in patients with low urinary output.</li><li>Ensure proper dilution to prevent gastrointestinal injury from concentrated potassium salts.</li><li>Large doses may cause hyperkalemia and alkalosis, especially in patients with renal disease.</li></ul><h4>Overdose:</h4><ul><li>Hyperkalemia may occur, especially in patients with impaired excretory mechanisms.</li><li>Immediate treatment is needed to manage hyperkalemia, which can become life-threatening.</li><li>Treatment may involve discontinuing potassium sources, administering glucose and insulin, and using exchange resins or dialysis.</li></ul><h4>Storage Conditions:</h4><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h4>Mode of Action:</h4><ul><li>Potassium Citrate and Citric Acid oral solution act as a systemic alkalizer by alkalinizing the urine, without disturbing digestion or neutralizing gastric juice. It is pleasant-tasting and tolerable even with long-term use.</li></ul><p>Please consult with a healthcare provider for more detailed information or if you have specific concerns about the drug\'s use.</p>', 'active', '1789_1742450376.png', 33, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 10:59:36', '2025-05-04 22:42:25'),
(3627, 'Uromitexan', 'Mesna', 215.61, 5, 'x 400 mg vial', '400 mg vial', 'IV Injection', '400 mg per 4 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Uromitexan 400 mg/4 ml</strong></p><p>Uromitexan 400 mg/4 ml is a cytoprotective agent indicated for the prophylactic reduction of the incidence of ifosfamide-induced hemorrhagic cystitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antidote preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Uromitexan 400 mg/4 ml is used to prevent urothelial toxicity associated with oxazaphosphorines like ifosfamide and cyclophosphamide. It works by reacting with thiol groups of urotoxic metabolites, such as acrolein, produced by these cytotoxic drugs. Additionally, it is used as a mucolytic agent in some respiratory tract conditions, like cystic fibrosis, when other mucolytics are ineffective. It reduces the viscosity of pulmonary secretions by reducing disulfide linkages in mucoproteins.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><em>Intravenous (IV) for Prophylaxis Against Urothelial Toxicity:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: Refer to individual and local protocols. The dose is typically calculated based on the cytotoxic dose. It should generally be given in a dose equal to or greater than the cytotoxic dose. Treatment duration should match the cytotoxic treatment duration, plus the time it takes for the concentration of antineoplastic metabolites to fall in the urine. Administration may be done via a short (15-30 minutes) or continuous (24 hours) infusion.</li><li><strong>Child</strong>: Refer to individual and local protocols. Has been used in children older than 4 months.</li></ul><p><em>Oral for Prophylaxis Against Urothelial Toxicity:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: Refer to individual and local protocols. Similar to IV administration, the dose is calculated based on the cytotoxic dose and is typically given in a dose equal to or greater than the cytotoxic dose. Treatment duration is similar to IV administration.</li><li><strong>Child</strong>: Refer to individual and local protocols.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to thiol-containing compounds.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Nausea, vomiting, colic, diarrhea, anorexia, dyspepsia, unpleasant taste, constipation.</li><li>CNS: Headache, malaise, fatigue, depression, irritability, somnolence, dizziness, confusion, hyperesthesia.</li><li>Dermatological: Rash, pruritus, generalized urticaria, alopecia.</li><li>Hematological: Leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, anemia, granulocytopenia.</li><li>Cardiovascular: Chest pain, edema (peripheral, facial, periorbital), hypotension, tachycardia, hypertension, increased heart rate, ST-segment elevation.</li><li>Respiratory: Dyspnea, coughing, pneumonia, tachypnea.</li><li>Miscellaneous: Fever, hypocalcemia, increased sweating, back pain, limb pain, myalgia, increased hepatic enzyme levels, pharyngitis, mucosal ulcers.</li><li>Inhalation: Bronchospasm.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Category B. Animal studies have not shown fetal risk, but there are no controlled studies in pregnant women. The drug has not shown risks in later trimesters, though it may decrease fertility in animal studies.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Protective effect applies only to the urinary tract.</li><li>Caution in pregnancy, lactation, and patients with autoimmune disorders.</li><li>IV formulations may contain benzyl alcohol, which should be avoided in neonates or infants.</li><li>Patients should seek medical attention if their urine becomes discolored.</li><li>Monitor urine for erythrocytes and hematuria during treatment.</li><li>Maintain adequate hydration during treatment.</li><li>If vomiting occurs within 2 hours of taking the oral dose, the dose should be repeated or an IV dose should be administered.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at 15-30°C.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p>Dilute with 50-1000 mL of normal saline, 5% dextrose, or lactated Ringer\'s solution.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Antidote preparations.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Uromitexan 400 mg/4 ml acts to prevent urothelial toxicity from oxazaphosphorines like ifosfamide and cyclophosphamide by reacting with their urotoxic metabolites. It also serves as a mucolytic agent, reducing the viscosity of pulmonary secretions, which helps in conditions like cystic fibrosis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Category B: Animal studies have not shown fetal harm, but no controlled studies are available in pregnant women. There is no evidence of risk in later trimesters.</p><p><strong>Packaging</strong></p><p>10 tablets in a strip.</p><p>Let me know if you\'d like further details on any of these sections!</p>', 'active', '11_1742450631.jpg', 45, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 11:03:51', '2025-05-04 22:42:48'),
(3628, 'Uronase', 'Urokinase', 3523.77, 1, 'x 10 ml vial', '10 ml vial', 'Injection', '500000 IU/10 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Uronase 500000 IU/10 ml</strong></p><p>Uronase 500000 IU/10 ml is indicated for the following conditions in adults:</p><ul><li><strong>Acute massive pulmonary embolism</strong>: Defined as obstruction of blood flow to a lobe or multiple segments of the lung.</li><li><strong>Pulmonary embolism with unstable hemodynamics</strong>: This is characterized by an inability to maintain blood pressure without supportive measures. Diagnosis should be confirmed by objective means, such as pulmonary angiography or noninvasive procedures like lung scanning.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Fibrinolytics (Thrombolytics)</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Uronase 500000 IU/10 ml is used for treating pulmonary embolism and works by acting on the endogenous fibrinolytic system. It converts plasminogen into plasmin, which degrades fibrin clots and fibrinogen. The active compound is a low molecular weight form of human urokinase, which consists of an A chain (2,000 daltons) and a B chain (30,400 daltons). Plasmin then cleaves fibrin and fibrinogen, leading to the breakdown of clots.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Deep vein thrombosis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initial loading dose: 4,400 IU/kg in 15 mL solution over 10 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Followed by 4,400 IU/kg/hr IV infusion for 12-24 hours.</li><li><strong>Pulmonary embolism</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initial loading dose: 4,400 IU/kg in 15 mL solution over 10 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Followed by 4,400 IU/kg/hr IV infusion for 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Peripheral vascular occlusion</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Infuse 2,500 IU/mL (500,000 IU in 200 mL) into the clot at 4,000 IU/min for 2 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Advance the catheter into the remaining occluded area &amp; infuse at 4,000 IU/min for another 2 hours. This may be repeated up to 4 times.</li><li><strong>Hyphaema</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Inject 5,000 IU in 2 mL saline solution and withdraw repeatedly over the iris.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If residual clot remains, leave 0.3 mL in the anterior chamber for 24-48 hours to aid dissolution.</li><li><strong>Clotted arterio-venous shunts</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Instill 5,000-25,000 IU into the affected shunt limb &amp; clamp for 2-4 hours, followed by aspiration of lysate. Repeat if necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant use of <strong>oral anticoagulants</strong>, <strong>heparin</strong>, or drugs affecting platelet function may increase the risk of hemorrhage.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Recent surgery or biopsy.</li><li>Severe <strong>hypertension</strong>.</li><li>Severe <strong>hepatic</strong> or <strong>renal insufficiency</strong>.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong> and the immediate postpartum period.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Overt bleeding.</li><li>Hemorrhagic complications.</li><li>Fever.</li><li>Hematuria.</li><li>Initial severe pain and dull ache in shunt limb.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: Studies on animal reproduction have not shown fetal risks, but there are no controlled studies in pregnant women. Caution is advised.</li><li>There is no evidence of risk in later trimesters based on animal studies.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in patients with <strong>GI lesions</strong> or <strong>multiple intracardiac or intravascular punctures</strong> due to cardiopulmonary resuscitation.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>In case of severe hemorrhage, Uronase 500000 IU/10 ml should be discontinued.</p><ul><li><strong>Treatment options</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Use <strong>Aprotinin</strong> or synthetic inhibitors (e.g., epsilon aminocaproic acid, tranexamic acid, or p-aminomethylbenzoic acid) to inhibit the fibrinolytic action.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In serious cases, administer <strong>human fibrinogen</strong>, <strong>Factor XIII</strong>, <strong>Cohn-Fraction I</strong>, packed red cells, or whole blood as appropriate.</li></ul><p><strong>Packaging</strong></p><ul><li>10 tablets per strip.</li></ul>', 'active', '1600_1742450819.jpg', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 11:06:59', '2025-05-04 22:44:14'),
(3629, 'Urospin', 'Furosemide + Spironolactone', 6.02, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg plus 50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Urospin 20 mg+50 mg (Furosemide &amp; Spironolactone Combination)</strong></p><p><strong>Indications</strong>: Urospin is used for:</p><ul><li>Essential hypertension</li><li>Chronic congestive heart failure</li><li>Hepatic cirrhosis with ascites (fluid accumulation in the abdomen)</li><li>Swelling due to excess fluid retention (edema)</li><li>Hyperaldosteronism</li><li>Resistant edema associated with secondary hyperaldosteronism</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</p><ul><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics</li><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics &amp; Aldosterone antagonists</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Furosemide</strong> is a loop diuretic that inhibits the Na+/K+/2Cl- co-transporter in the ascending Loop of Henle, blocking sodium, potassium, and chloride reabsorption, thus promoting sodium and water excretion and causing potassium loss.</li><li><strong>Spironolactone</strong> is a potassium-sparing diuretic and aldosterone antagonist. It works in the distal tubule to inhibit sodium reabsorption in exchange for potassium, counteracting the potassium loss caused by furosemide.</li></ul><p>Together, the combination provides an additive or synergistic diuretic effect, balancing sodium and potassium levels more effectively.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Furosemide 20 mg and Spironolactone 50 mg</strong>: 1 to 4 tablets daily (20 to 80 mg of Furosemide and 50 to 200 mg of Spironolactone), based on patient response.</li><li><strong>Furosemide 40 mg and Spironolactone 50 mg</strong>: For patients needing higher doses, 1 to 2 tablets daily (40 to 80 mg of Furosemide and 50 to 100 mg of Spironolactone).</li><li><strong>Use in Children</strong>: Not suitable for children.</li><li><strong>Use in the Elderly</strong>: May be excreted more slowly in elderly individuals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong>:</p><ul><li>Increased risk of <strong>hyperkalemia</strong> with ACE inhibitors or potassium salts.</li><li>Spironolactone can increase levels of <strong>digoxin</strong>, potentially leading to toxicity.</li><li><strong>Corticosteroids</strong> may cause <strong>hypokalemia</strong> when used with Spironolactone.</li><li>Diuretic effects of Furosemide may be reduced by <strong>NSAIDs</strong> like indomethacin.</li><li><strong>Aminoglycosides</strong> can increase ototoxicity when combined with Furosemide.</li><li><strong>Sucralfate</strong> may reduce the diuretic and antihypertensive effects of Furosemide.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><ul><li>Anuria</li><li>Acute renal insufficiency</li><li>Severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt; 30 ml/min)</li><li>Hyperkalemia</li><li>Addison’s disease</li><li>Hypersensitivity to Spironolactone, Furosemide, or sulphonamides</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Spironolactone</strong>: Headache, drowsiness, gastrointestinal issues (cramps, diarrhea), ataxia, mental confusion, skin rashes, <strong>gynaecomastia</strong> (breast enlargement), hormonal changes (hirsutism, deepening of voice, menstrual irregularities, impotence), and elevated blood urea nitrogen.</li><li><strong>Furosemide</strong>: Potassium and magnesium depletion, dehydration, hypotension, potential circulatory collapse, and risk of thrombosis or embolism, especially in elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Serious Effects</strong>: Cardiac arrhythmias due to excessive potassium or magnesium depletion.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Spironolactone crosses the placental barrier. The risks to the mother and fetus should be weighed against potential benefits. Furosemide may cause fetal abnormalities.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Both Spironolactone and Furosemide are excreted in breast milk. If essential, alternative infant feeding should be considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Caution in patients with electrolyte imbalances, diabetes, enlarged prostate, hypotension, or hypovolemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li>Symptoms: Drowsiness, confusion, dizziness, diarrhea, vomiting, excessive diuresis.</li><li>Treatment: Fluid replacement and correction of electrolyte imbalances.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong>:</p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Store out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Not suitable.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: May have slower excretion rates.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong>:</p><ul><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics</li><li>Aldosterone antagonists</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</p><ul><li>The combination of <strong>Furosemide</strong> and <strong>Spironolactone</strong> works by enhancing diuresis through complementary mechanisms—Furosemide primarily promotes sodium and water excretion, while Spironolactone prevents excessive potassium loss, promoting a balanced diuretic effect.</li></ul><p><strong>Packaging</strong>:</p><ul><li>Available in strips containing 10 tablets.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need any more details or clarifications!</p>', 'active', '1131_1742450976.webp', 69, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 11:09:36', '2025-03-20 11:09:36'),
(3630, 'Renalaid-A', 'Acidic component [HCO3 Hemodialysis Solution]', 450.87, 1, 'Container', '10 liters container', 'Solution', 'A', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Renalaid-A</strong></p><p> This solution is used in Haemodialysis.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p> Each 1 liter solution contains:</p><ul><li>Sodium Chloride: 161.43 gm</li><li>Potassium Chloride: 5.49 gm</li><li>Calcium Chloride: 9.75 gm</li><li>Magnesium Chloride: 3.74 gm</li><li>Glacial Acetic Acid: 8.85 gm</li><li>Highly purified water: q.s. to 1 liter</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Haemodialysis solutions</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calcium chloride</strong> is used to prevent or treat negative calcium balance. It also regulates action potential excitation threshold to facilitate nerve and muscle performance.</li><li><strong>Potassium chloride</strong> is a major cation of the intracellular fluid. It plays an active role in the conduction of nerve impulses in the heart, brain, and skeletal muscle; contraction of cardiac, skeletal, and smooth muscles; maintenance of normal renal function, acid-base balance, carbohydrate metabolism, and gastric secretion.</li><li><strong>Sodium chloride</strong> is the major extracellular cation. It is important in electrolyte and fluid balance, osmotic pressure control, and water distribution as it restores sodium ions. It is used as a source of electrolytes and water for hydration, treatment of metabolic acidosis, priming solution in haemodialysis, and treatment of hyperosmolar diabetes. It is also used as a diluent for the infusion of compatible drug additives.</li><li><strong>Magnesium Chloride</strong> acts as a cofactor in numerous enzymatic reactions involving protein synthesis and carbohydrate metabolism; depresses the CNS, blocks peripheral neuromuscular transmission, and produces anticonvulsant effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Renalaid-A</strong></p><p> Recommended dose: 5 liters concentrate per dialysis or as required.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Renalaid-A</strong></p><p> There are no known drug interactions and none well documented.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of this preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Renalaid-A</strong></p><p> Fluid and electrolyte imbalance, hypovolemia, hypotension, or muscle cramping may occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category is not Classified. FDA has not yet classified the drug into a specified pregnancy category.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> It is used for hemodialysis purposes only. Do not use solution A or B alone. Discard container in case visible solid particles inside.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Haemodialysis solutions</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calcium chloride</strong> is used to prevent or treat negative calcium balance. It also regulates action potential excitation threshold to facilitate nerve and muscle performance.</li><li><strong>Potassium chloride</strong> is a major cation of the intracellular fluid. It plays an active role in the conduction of nerve impulses in the heart, brain, and skeletal muscle; contraction of cardiac, skeletal, and smooth muscles; maintenance of normal renal function, acid-base balance, carbohydrate metabolism, and gastric secretion.</li><li><strong>Sodium chloride</strong> is the major extracellular cation. It is important in electrolyte and fluid balance, osmotic pressure control, and water distribution as it restores sodium ions. It is used as a source of electrolytes and water for hydration, treatment of metabolic acidosis, priming solution in haemodialysis, and treatment of hyperosmolar diabetes. It is also used as a diluent for the infusion of compatible drug additives.</li><li><strong>Magnesium Chloride</strong> acts as a cofactor in numerous enzymatic reactions involving protein synthesis and carbohydrate metabolism; depresses the CNS, blocks peripheral neuromuscular transmission, and produces anticonvulsant effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category is not Classified. FDA has not yet classified the drug into a specified pregnancy category.</p>', 'active', '797_1742451127.jpg', 68, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 11:12:07', '2025-03-20 11:12:07'),
(3631, 'Vanmycin', 'Vancomycin Hydrochloride', 250.00, 1, 'IV Infusion', '500 mg vial infusion', 'IV Infusion', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vanmycin 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Vancomycin is indicated in potentially life-threatening infections which cannot be treated with other effective, less toxic antimicrobial drugs including the penicillins and cephalosporins. Vancomycin is useful in the therapy of severe staphylococcal infections in patients who cannot receive or who have failed to respond to the penicillins and cephalosporins or who have infections with staphylococci resistant to other antibiotics. Vancomycin is used in the treatment of endocarditis and as prophylaxis against endocarditis in patients undergoing dental or surgical procedures. Its effectiveness has been documented in other infections due to staphylococci including osteomyelitis, pneumonia, septicemia, and soft tissue infections.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Glycopeptide</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Vancomycin binds tightly to the D-alanyl-D-alanine portion of the cell wall precursor causing blockage of glycopeptide polymerization, which produces immediate inhibition of cell wall synthesis and secondary damage to the cytoplasmic membrane.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Vanmycin 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Concentrations of no more than 5 mg/ml and rates of no more than 10 mg/min are recommended in adults. In selected patients in need of fluid restriction, a concentration up to 10 mg/ml may be used.</p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Patients with Normal Renal Function:</strong>Adults: Usual daily dose is 2 gm (in 4 or 2 divided doses).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children: Total daily dose is 40 mg/Kg (in 4 divided doses).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Infants and Neonates: An initial dose of 15 mg/Kg is suggested followed by 10 mg/Kg every 12 hours in the first week, then every 8 hours up to 1 month.</li><li><strong>Patients with Impaired Renal Function and Elderly Patients:</strong></li><li> Dosage adjustment must be made in patients with impaired renal function. In premature infants and the elderly, dosage reduction may be necessary to a greater extent than expected because of decreasing renal function.</li><li> If creatinine clearance can be measured or estimated accurately, the dosage for most patients with renal impairment can be calculated using the following chart:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 100 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 1545 mg/24 h</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 90 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 1390 mg/24 h</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 80 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 1235 mg/24 h</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 70 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 1080 mg/24 h</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 60 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 925 mg/24 h</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 50 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 770 mg/24 h</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 40 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 620 mg/24 h</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 30 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 465 mg/24 h</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 20 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 310 mg/24 h</li><li>CrCl 10 ml/min: Vancomycin Dose 155 mg/24 h</li><li> The initial dose should be not less than 15 mg/kg even in patients with mild to moderate renal insufficiency.</li><li> The above chart is not valid for functionally anephric patients. For such patients, an initial dose of 15 mg/kg of body weight should be given in order to achieve prompt therapeutic serum concentrations. The dose required to maintain stable concentrations is 1.9 mg/kg/24 h. Since individual maintenance doses of 250-1,000 mg are convenient, one dose may be given every several days rather than on a daily basis in patients with marked renal impairment. In anuria, a dose of 1000 mg every 7-10 days has been recommended.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"> Intermittent infusion is the recommended method of administration. Intraperitoneal administration is not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Vancomycin is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vancomycin.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Vanmycin 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Vancomycin is well tolerated. However, during or soon after rapid infusion of Vancomycin, patients may develop anaphylactic reactions including hypotension, wheezing, dyspnoea, urticaria, or pruritus. Rapid infusion may also cause flushing of the upper body (\"red neck\") or pain and muscle spasm of the chest and back. These reactions usually resolve within 20 minutes but may persist for several hours. Such events are infrequent if Vancomycin is given by a slow infusion over 60 minutes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> It is not known whether it causes foetal harm or not. Vancomycin should be given in pregnancy only if clearly needed and blood levels should be monitored carefully to minimize the risk of foetal toxicity. Vanmycin 500 mg/vial is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Vancomycin is administered to a nursing woman. It is unlikely that a nursing infant can absorb a significant amount of Vancomycin from its gastrointestinal tract.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with borderline renal function and individuals over the age of 60 should be given serial tests of auditory function and Vancomycin blood levels.</li><li>All patients receiving the drug should have periodic haematological studies, urine analysis, and renal function tests.</li><li>Vancomycin is very irritating to tissue and causes injection site necrosis when injected intramuscularly. It must be infused intravenously.</li><li>Injection site pain and thrombophlebitis occur in many patients receiving Vancomycin and are occasionally severe.</li><li>Prolonged use of Vancomycin may result in the overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms. Careful observation of the patient is essential. If superinfection occurs during therapy, appropriate measures should be taken.</li><li>In rare instances, there have been reports of pseudomembranous colitis due to C. difficile, developing in patients who received intravenous Vancomycin.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Vanmycin 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Supportive care is advised with maintenance of glomerular filtration. Vancomycin is poorly removed from the blood by haemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis. Haemoperfusion with Amberlite resin XAD-4 has been reported to be of limited benefit.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Other antibiotic</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Vancomycin binds tightly to the D-alanyl-D-alanine portion of the cell wall precursor causing blockage of glycopeptide polymerization, which produces immediate inhibition of cell wall synthesis and secondary damage to the cytoplasmic membrane.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> It is not known whether it causes foetal harm or not. Vancomycin should be given in pregnancy only if clearly needed and blood levels should be monitored carefully to minimize the risk of foetal toxicity. Vanmycin 500 mg/vial is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Vancomycin is administered to a nursing woman. It is unlikely that a nursing infant can absorb a significant amount of Vancomycin from its gastrointestinal tract.</p>', 'active', '561_1742451443.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 11:17:23', '2025-03-20 11:17:23'),
(3632, 'Utrogestan', 'Progesterone Micronized', 36.00, 15, 'capsule', '15 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Utrogestan 100 mg:</strong> Utrogestan 100 mg is used to provide additional progesterone for women undergoing fertility treatment due to low progesterone levels. It can be used in the following cases:</p><ol><li><strong>Infertility treatment</strong>: When progesterone levels are insufficient during the menstrual cycle or in assisted fertility procedures to help support pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy support</strong>: It may be continued after conception to support an early pregnancy.</li></ol><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Infertility</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Utrogestan 100 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Infertility Treatment</strong>: Insert one applicator of Utrogestan 100 mg gel into the vagina each day, starting after ovulation (or on days 18–21 of the menstrual cycle).</li><li><strong>For IVF (In-Vitro Fertilization)</strong>: Start from the day of embryo transfer, using one applicator each day, preferably in the morning.</li><li>Once pregnancy is confirmed, continue for a total of 30 days to support the pregnancy.</li><li>The applicator delivers 1.125 g of gel containing 90 mg of Utrogestan 100 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Utrogestan 100 mg:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Prepare the Applicator</strong>: Shake the applicator to move the gel to the thin end. Twist off the cap.</li><li><strong>Insertion</strong>: Lie back or sit with knees apart. Insert the applicator fully into the vagina, then press firmly on the thick end to deliver the gel.</li><li><strong>Post-Use</strong>: Dispose of the applicator, including any leftover gel. Use each applicator only once.</li></ol><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Do not use Utrogestan 100 mg with other vaginal medicines.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Allergic reaction to any component of Utrogestan 100 mg.</li><li>Unexplained vaginal bleeding.</li><li>Porphyria (a blood disorder).</li><li>History of cancer in the breasts or genital organs.</li><li>History of blood clots, thrombophlebitis, or stroke.</li><li>Pregnancy with a non-viable fetus (missed abortion).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Utrogestan 100 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common (may affect up to 1 in 10 people)</strong>: Headache, fatigue, gastrointestinal cramps, and breast tenderness.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Not typically used during pregnancy unless needed for pregnancy support.</li><li>Not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously if you have liver or kidney problems, heart issues, diabetes, asthma, epilepsy, migraines, or a history of depression.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store at or below 25°C, away from children.</li><li>Dispose of unused medicines properly, not via wastewater or household waste.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>Not for use in children or adolescents.</li></ul><p>If you have any further questions or concerns, it\'s always best to consult your healthcare provider for guidance tailored to your specific situation.</p>', 'active', '1621_1742451515.png', 73, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 11:18:35', '2025-05-04 22:44:45'),
(3633, 'Tycil DS', 'Amoxicillin Trihydrate', 100.00, 1, 'Bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '250 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the reformatted information with headings in normal bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Tycil DS 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Amoxicillin is indicated in the treatment of infections due to susceptible ß-lactamase negative strains of microorganisms. These infections include:</p><ul><li>Ear, nose, and throat infections (e.g., otitis media, sinusitis, tonsillitis, pharyngitis, laryngitis)</li><li>Lower respiratory tract infections (e.g., pneumonia, acute and chronic bronchitis, lung abscess, empyema, bronchiectasis)</li><li>Skin and soft tissue infections (e.g., cellulitis, carbuncles, furunculosis, infected wounds, abscess)</li><li>Genito-urinary tract infections (e.g., pyelonephritis, cystitis, and urethritis)</li><li>Venereal disease (e.g., acute uncomplicated gonorrhoea)</li><li>In dental abscess, it is used as short-term therapy.</li><li>It is also indicated in combination with Clarithromycin and Lansoprazole (as triple therapy) for the treatment of patients with H. pylori infection and duodenal ulcer disease and to reduce the risk of duodenal ulcer recurrence.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Broad spectrum penicillins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Amoxicillin is a broad-spectrum penicillin. It is effective against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. It acts through the inhibition of biosynthesis of cell wall. Amoxicillin is stable in the presence of gastric acid and is rapidly absorbed after oral administration. After an oral dose, peak plasma concentration of Amoxicillin is reached within 1 to 2 hours. Amoxicillin is widely distributed at varying concentrations in body tissues and fluids.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Tycil DS 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 250 mg three times daily, increasing up to 500 mg three times daily for severe infections.</li><li><strong>Children (up to 10 years of age):</strong> 125 mg three times daily, increasing up to 250 mg three times daily for severe infections.</li><li><strong>Severe or recurrent purulent respiratory infection:</strong> 3 gm every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Otitis media:</strong> 1 g three times daily for adults and 40 mg/kg body weight daily in 3 divided doses for children (max. 3 g daily).</li><li><strong>Pneumonia:</strong> 500-1000 mg three times daily.</li><li><strong>Dental abscess:</strong> 3 gm, repeated after 10-12 hours.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections:</strong> 3 gm, repeated after 10-12 hours.</li><li><strong>Gonorrhoea:</strong> Single dose of 2-3 gm with Probenecid 1 gm is recommended (Probenecid is contraindicated in children under 2 years).</li><li><strong>In renal impairment:</strong> it may be necessary to reduce the total daily dosage.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Tycil DS 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Reconstituted suspension can be administered by adding the required amount of suspension to milk, fruit juice, or water. These preparations should then be taken immediately.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Tycil DS 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Concurrent use of Amoxicillin and Probenecid may result in increased and prolonged blood levels of Amoxicillin.</li><li>Amoxicillin may affect the gut flora, leading to lower estrogen reabsorption and reduced efficacy of combined oral estrogen/progesterone contraceptives.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Amoxicillin is contraindicated in penicillin hypersensitive patients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Tycil DS 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Side effects are mild and transient in nature. This may include diarrhoea, indigestion, or occasionally rash. Pseudo-membranous colitis has been reported rarely.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> US FDA pregnancy category of Amoxicillin is B. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Amoxicillin has been shown to be excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Amoxicillin is administered to a lactating mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> The possibility of superinfections with mycotic or bacterial pathogens should be kept in mind during therapy. If superinfections occur, Amoxicillin should be discontinued and appropriate therapy should be instituted.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Amoxycillin 500 mg Injection:</strong>Intramuscular: Add 2.5 ml water for injection to Amoxycillin 500 mg injection vial.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Intravenous: Dissolve Amoxycillin 500 mg injection in 10 ml water for injection.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Broad spectrum penicillins</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Amoxicillin is a broad-spectrum penicillin. It is effective against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. It acts through the inhibition of biosynthesis of the cell wall. Amoxicillin is stable in the presence of gastric acid and is rapidly absorbed after oral administration. After an oral dose, peak plasma concentration of Amoxicillin is reached within 1 to 2 hours. Amoxicillin is widely distributed at varying concentrations in body tissues and fluids.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> US FDA pregnancy category of Amoxicillin is B. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Amoxicillin has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Amoxicillin is administered to a lactating mother.</p>', 'active', '87_1742451996.jpg', 3, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 11:26:36', '2025-03-20 11:26:36');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3634, 'Zoxan', 'Nitazoxanide', 35.00, 1, 'x 30 ml bottle', '30 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '100 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>It seems you\'ve shared a detailed description of Zoxan 100 mg/5 ml, including its indications, dosage, pharmacology, side effects, and other relevant information. Here’s a summary of the key points:</p><h3><strong>Indications:</strong></h3><p>Zoxan 100 mg/5 ml is used for treating diarrhea caused by:</p><ul><li><em>Cryptosporidium parvum</em></li><li><em>Giardia lamblia</em></li><li><em>Entamoeba histolytica</em></li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Anti-diarrhoeal</strong></li><li><strong>Antiprotozoal</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></h3><ul><li>Zoxan works by interfering with the Pyruvate Ferredoxin Oxido Reductase (PFOR) enzyme-dependent electron transfer reaction, which is essential for the anaerobic energy metabolism of protozoa.</li><li>It inhibits the growth of <em>Cryptosporidium parvum</em> sporozoites and oocysts, and <em>Giardia lamblia</em> trophozoites.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>1-3 years:</strong> 1 teaspoon (5 ml) every 12 hours for 3 days</li><li><strong>4-11 years:</strong> 2 teaspoons (10 ml) every 12 hours for 3 days</li><li><strong>12 years and older:</strong> 5 teaspoons (25 ml) every 12 hours or 1 tablet every 12 hours for 3 days</li><li>It is recommended to take it with food.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions:</strong></h3><ul><li>Caution is advised when Zoxan is taken with other drugs that are highly bound to plasma proteins, especially those with a narrow therapeutic index.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications:</strong></h3><ul><li>Not for use in patients with known hypersensitivity to Zoxan or its components.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Abdominal pain, vomiting, headache</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Nausea, anorexia, flatulence, increased appetite, enlarged salivary glands, increased creatinine &amp; SGPT levels, pruritus, rhinitis, sweating, dizziness, discolored urine.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category B (No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Use if necessary).</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Excreted in human milk; caution advised when administered during breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></h3><ul><li>Caution should be exercised in patients with hepatic, biliary, or renal diseases.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage:</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat, and keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></h3><ul><li>Inhibits the anaerobic energy metabolism of protozoa by interfering with the PFOR enzyme.</li></ul><h3><strong>Packaging:</strong></h3><ul><li>10 tablets in a strip.</li></ul><p>This medication is mainly for treating protozoal infections that cause diarrhea, and it’s important to follow the prescribed dosage to avoid complications. If you have any concerns about using Zoxan, especially regarding pregnancy or other conditions, it’s best to consult a healthcare professional.</p><p>Let me know if you need further details or have specific questions!</p>', 'active', '861_1742452177.webp', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 11:29:37', '2025-05-05 22:40:54'),
(3635, 'Zedex Max', 'Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate', 55.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '20 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zedex Max 20 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Zedex Max 20 mg/5 ml is indicated in zinc deficiency and/or zinc-losing conditions. Zinc deficiency can occur due to an inadequate diet or malabsorption. Excessive loss of zinc can happen in conditions such as trauma, burns, diarrhea, and protein-losing states. A zinc supplement is provided until clinical improvement is observed but may need to be continued in cases of severe malabsorption, metabolic disease, or zinc-losing states.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zinc sulphate monohydrate is an essential trace element involved in numerous body enzyme systems. The body requires zinc for normal growth and health. Zinc is crucial for sexual maturation and reproduction, dark vision adaptation, olfactory and gustatory activity, insulin storage and release, and various host immune defenses. Zinc deficiency can lead to impaired immune function, delayed wound healing, reduced sense of taste and smell, lower infection resistance, poor night vision, increased risk of abortion, alopecia, mental lethargy, skin changes, and poor development of reproductive organs.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Zedex Max 20 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Child under 10 kg</strong>: 5 ml (1 teaspoonful) 2 times daily after food.</li><li><strong>Child between 10-30 kg</strong>: 10 ml (2 teaspoonfuls) 1-3 times daily after food.</li><li><strong>Adults and children over 30 kg</strong>: 20 ml (4 teaspoonfuls) 1-3 times daily after food.</li><li>This drug is most effective if taken at least 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals. However, if it causes stomach upset, it may be taken with food.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zedex Max 20 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>For dispersible tablet:</p><ul><li>Place the tablet in a teaspoon.</li><li>Add an adequate amount of water.</li><li>Let the tablet dissolve completely.</li><li>Give the entire spoonful of the solution.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zedex Max 20 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant intake of a tetracycline and zinc may decrease the absorption of both tetracycline and zinc.</li><li>Similarly, the concomitant administration of zinc and quinolone drugs may also decrease the absorption of both.</li><li>Concomitant intake of penicillamine and zinc may decrease the absorption of zinc.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in individuals who are hypersensitive to any component of the ingredient in this preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zedex Max 20 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Zinc may cause nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, stomach upset, heartburn, and gastritis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of this product during pregnancy has not been established. Zinc crosses the placenta and is present in breast milk.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In acute renal failure, zinc accumulation may occur in the body, so dose adjustment is necessary.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '892_1742452338.jpg', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 11:32:18', '2025-05-05 22:16:38'),
(3636, 'Tykerb', 'Lapatinib', 350.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Tykerb 250 mg</h3><p>Tykerb 250 mg is indicated for the treatment of advanced or metastatic breast cancer with HER2 overexpression in combination with:</p><ul><li><strong>Capecitabine</strong>: For patients who have received prior therapy with an Anthracycline, Taxane, and Trastuzumab. Tykerb should be initiated after disease progression on Trastuzumab.</li><li><strong>Letrozole</strong>: For postmenopausal women with hormone receptor-positive metastatic breast cancer overexpressing HER2, when hormonal therapy is indicated. It has not been compared to Trastuzumab-containing chemotherapy regimens for this indication.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Tykerb 250 mg is a 4-anilinoquinazoline tyrosine kinase inhibitor targeting both <strong>EGFR (ErbB1)</strong> and <strong>HER2 (ErbB2)</strong>. It inhibits phosphorylation and activation of downstream signaling molecules (Erk1/2 and Akt) that regulate cell growth and survival in ErbB- and ErbB2-expressing tumors.</p><h3>Dosage</h3><ul><li><strong>HER2-Positive Metastatic Breast Cancer</strong>: 1,250 mg orally once daily, in combination with <strong>Capecitabine</strong> 2,000 mg/m²/day. Take Tykerb at least 1 hour before or after a meal.</li><li><strong>Hormone Receptor-Positive, HER2-Positive Metastatic Breast Cancer</strong>: 1,500 mg orally once daily, in combination with <strong>Letrozole</strong> 2.5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: The safety and effectiveness have not been established.</li></ul><h3>Administration</h3><p>Tykerb 250 mg should be taken on an empty stomach, at least 1 hour before or after meals. Avoid grapefruit products.</p><h3>Drug Interactions</h3><ul><li><strong>CYP3A4 Inhibitors</strong>: May increase Tykerb levels. Example: Ketoconazole increases Tykerb\'s AUC by 3.6 times.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 Inducers</strong>: Decrease Tykerb\'s effectiveness. Example: Carbamazepine reduces Tykerb\'s AUC by 72%.</li><li><strong>Digoxin</strong>: Tykerb increases Digoxin\'s AUC by 2.8 times; monitor Digoxin levels.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Tykerb 250 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to the product or its components.</p><h3>Side Effects</h3><p>Common adverse reactions include:</p><ul><li><strong>Tykerb + Capecitabine</strong>: Diarrhea, palmar-plantar erythrodysesthesia, nausea, rash, vomiting, fatigue.</li><li><strong>Tykerb + Letrozole</strong>: Diarrhea, rash, nausea, fatigue.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category D</strong>: Tykerb may cause fetal harm. It should only be used in pregnant women if absolutely necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Avoid breastfeeding during treatment and for 1 week after the last dose.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Left Ventricular Ejection Fraction (LVEF)</strong>: Tykerb can decrease LVEF. Monitor LVEF prior to and during treatment.</li><li><strong>Hepatotoxicity</strong>: Monitor liver function tests during treatment. Discontinue if severe hepatotoxicity occurs.</li><li><strong>Diarrhea</strong>: Early management with antidiarrheal agents is essential. Severe cases may require interruption or discontinuation of therapy.</li><li><strong>Interstitial Lung Disease/Pneumonitis</strong>: Monitor for pulmonary symptoms.</li><li><strong>QT Prolongation</strong>: Monitor for QTc prolongation, especially in at-risk patients.</li></ul><h3>Overdose</h3><p>No known antidote for Tykerb overdose. Treatment should focus on general supportive measures.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><p>Store below 30°C in a dry place. Keep out of reach of children.</p><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Severe Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Consider dose reduction.</li><li><strong>Potent CYP3A4 Inhibitors</strong>: Avoid concomitant use or consider a dose reduction to 500 mg/day.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Tykerb 250 mg is a kinase inhibitor that targets both EGFR and HER2, inhibiting tumor cell growth. It has shown activity against HER2-positive tumors, even those resistant to Trastuzumab.</p><h3>Absorption &amp; Distribution</h3><p>Tykerb has incomplete and variable absorption. Plasma concentrations peak around 4 hours after oral administration. It is highly bound to plasma proteins (over 99%).</p><h3>Metabolism &amp; Elimination</h3><p>Tykerb undergoes extensive metabolism by <strong>CYP3A4</strong> and has a half-life of 24 hours with minimal renal excretion.</p><h3>Pregnancy</h3><p>Tykerb may cause fetal harm. Advise against use during pregnancy and breastfeeding.</p><h3>Pediatric Use</h3><p>No established safety or efficacy in pediatric patients. Dosing adjustments may be necessary for certain adverse events or co-administration with CYP3A4 inhibitors or inducers.</p>', 'active', '1495_1742452522.jpg', 40, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 11:35:22', '2025-03-20 11:35:22'),
(3637, 'Xelotan Plus', 'Losartan Potassium + Hydrochlorothiazide', 6.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg plus 12.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Xelotan Plus 50 mg+12.5 mg</strong></h3><p>Xelotan Plus is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Treatment of hypertension.</strong></li><li><strong>Reduction of stroke risk</strong> in patients with hypertension and left ventricular hypertrophy.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Combined antihypertensive preparations</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Losartan</strong>: An angiotensin II receptor antagonist. It blocks the vasoconstricting and aldosterone-secreting effects of angiotensin II by antagonizing its binding to AT1 receptors.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: A thiazide diuretic that inhibits sodium reabsorption in the distal tubules, leading to increased excretion of sodium, water, potassium, and hydrogen ions.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xelotan Plus 50 mg+12.5 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual starting dose: <strong>50/12.5 mg</strong> once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Titrate as needed to a maximum dose of <strong>100/25 mg</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hypertensive Patients with Left Ventricular Hypertrophy</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Start with <strong>50/12.5 mg</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Titrate to a maximum of <strong>100/25 mg</strong>.</li><li><strong>Severe Hypertension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Start with <strong>50/12.5 mg</strong> once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If inadequate response after 2–4 weeks, increase to <strong>100/25 mg</strong> once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum dose: <strong>100/25 mg</strong> once daily.</li><li><strong>For elderly patients</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">No initial dosage adjustment is needed for <strong>50/12.5 mg</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The maximum dose of <strong>100/25 mg</strong> should not be used as initial therapy.</li><li><strong>Administration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Can be taken with or without food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">May be combined with other antihypertensive agents.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction of Xelotan Plus 50 mg+12.5 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Losartan Potassium</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">No significant pharmacokinetic interactions with Hydrochlorothiazide, Digoxin, Warfarin, Cimetidine, or Phenobarbital.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Concomitant use with <strong>potassium-sparing diuretics</strong> or <strong>potassium supplements</strong> may increase serum potassium.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>NSAIDs</strong> (e.g., Indomethacin) may reduce the antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">May interact with <strong>alcohol, barbiturates</strong>, or <strong>narcotics</strong>, potentially causing orthostatic hypotension.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">May require dose adjustments in <strong>antidiabetic drugs</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cholestyramine and colestipol resins</strong> may impair hydrochlorothiazide absorption.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to any component of the product.</li><li><strong>Anuria</strong> or hypersensitivity to other sulfonamide-derived drugs.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Xelotan Plus 50 mg+12.5 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Symptomatic hypotension (dizziness), hyperkalemia, vertigo.</li><li><strong>Less common</strong>: Gastrointestinal disturbances, angina, palpitation, edema, dyspnoea, headache, sleep disorders, malaise, urticaria, pruritus, rash.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Hepatitis, atrial fibrillation, cerebrovascular accident, syncope, paraesthesia, pancreatitis, anaphylaxis, erectile dysfunction, anaemia, thrombocytopenia, hyponatremia, arthralgia, myalgia, renal impairment, rhabdomyolysis, tinnitus, photosensitivity, vasculitis (including Henoch-Schonlein purpura).</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Should be discontinued if pregnancy is detected. Angiotensin-II receptor antagonists can adversely affect fetal and neonatal blood pressure control, renal function, and cause skull defects and oligohydramnios.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Losartan is not recommended during breastfeeding. Thiazides appear in human milk, and alternative treatments should be considered.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Monitor <strong>serum electrolytes</strong> periodically to detect any imbalances.</li><li>Risk of <strong>hypokalemia</strong>, particularly with prolonged therapy or in patients with cirrhosis.</li><li>Be cautious with patients with <strong>renal impairment</strong> or <strong>symptomatic hypotension</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Xelotan Plus 50 mg+12.5 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Losartan</strong>: Overdose may cause hypotension and tachycardia; bradycardia from parasympathetic (vagal) stimulation may occur. Supportive treatment is recommended.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: Overdose symptoms typically result from electrolyte depletion (hypokalemia, hypochloremia, dehydration). If digitalis is also used, hypokalemia may accentuate arrhythmias.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric patients</strong>: Safety and effectiveness not established.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: Can be used if <strong>creatinine clearance</strong> is &gt;30 mL/min. Not recommended for severe renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: Not recommended for titration in patients with hepatic impairment due to dosage limitations for Losartan.</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Losartan</strong>: Blocks the vasoconstrictor and aldosterone-secreting effects of angiotensin II by blocking its binding to the AT1 receptor, helping to reduce blood pressure.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: Increases sodium and chloride excretion, reduces plasma volume, and indirectly increases renin and aldosterone levels, which may increase potassium loss. However, co-administration with losartan helps mitigate potassium loss.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Angiotensin-II receptor antagonists</strong> should generally be avoided in pregnancy due to potential risks to fetal development. Alternative treatments with better-established safety profiles during pregnancy and breastfeeding are recommended.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Use in patients with renal impairment</strong>: Suitable for creatinine clearance &gt;30 mL/min, but not for severe impairment.</li><li><strong>Use in patients with hepatic impairment</strong>: Not recommended for titration.</li><li><strong>Use in pediatric patients</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '115_1742452661.jpg', 61, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 11:37:41', '2025-03-20 11:37:41'),
(3638, 'TTO Thermal', 'Tea tree oil + Geothermal water', 950.00, 1, 'Bottle', '45 ml bottle', 'Shampoo', '45 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of TTO Thermal</strong></p><ul><li>চোখের সুরক্ষায়</li><li>চোখ দিয়ে করোনা সংক্রামন প্রতরোধে</li><li>হাত জীবাণুমুক্ত করতে</li><li>চোখের পাতা ও ভ্রূ-এর খুশকী দূরীকরণে</li><li>চোখের শুস্কভাব দূর করতে</li><li>বলিরেখা প্রতিরোধে</li></ul><p>কসমেটিকস পরিস্কার করতে<strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Miscellaneous topical agents</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of TTO Thermal</strong></p><p> সানগ্লাস বা গগলস ব্যবহারের পাশাপাশি রোগজীবানু প্রতিরোধী Tea tree oil ও Geothermal water সমৃদ্ধ Shampoo দিয়ে প্রতিদিন দুইবার করে চোখ পরিস্কার করলে চোখ দিয়ে করোনা ভাইরাসে সংক্রামিত হওয়ার সুযোগ কমে যায় এবং চোখের স্বাস্থ্যও সুরক্ষিত থা<strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> আলো ও আর্দ্রতা থেকে দূরে, ৩০ ডিগ্রী সেঃ তাপমাত্রার নীচে রাখুন। শিশুদের নাগালের বাইরে রাখুন।</p>', 'active', '1382_1742452859.jpg', 7, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 11:40:59', '2025-03-20 11:40:59'),
(3639, 'Veralgin', 'Tiemonium Methylsulfate', 25.00, 1, 'x 2 ml ampoule', '2 ml ampoule', 'IV Injection', '5 mg/2 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information you provided, formatted with bold text headings where necessary:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Veralgin 5 mg/2 ml</strong></p><p>Veralgin 5 mg/2 ml is an antispasmodic drug that reduces muscle spasms of the intestine, biliary system, bladder, and uterus. It is used in the symptomatic treatment of pain related to functional disorders of the digestive tract and biliary system. It is also indicated for the treatment of spasm and pain in urological and gynecological diseases.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Anticholinergics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Veralgin 5 mg/2 ml is a competitive antagonist of Acetylcholine, Histamine, and strengthens the calcium bond with membrane phospholipids and proteins. This inhibits intracellular contractile proteins of visceral cells, which causes the inhibition of visceral spasms and pain.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Veralgin 5 mg/2 ml</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet/SyrupAdult</strong>: Usual dose is 2-6 tablets or 3-9 teaspoonfuls of syrup daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children</strong>: 3 ml/kg or 6 mg/kg body weight daily in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: 1 Veralgin 5 mg/2 ml injection 3 times daily, through intravenous route slowly or intramuscular route.</li><li><strong>Suppository</strong>: 20 mg Veralgin 5 mg/2 ml suppository two or three times daily, through the rectal route.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Veralgin 5 mg/2 ml</strong></p><p>Tiemonium methylsulphate tablet should not be used with other drugs without prior consultation with a registered physician to avoid possible drug interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It should not be used in urethroprostatic disorders involving a risk of urine retention. It is contraindicated in patients with a risk of angle-closure glaucoma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Veralgin 5 mg/2 ml</strong></p><p>Veralgin 5 mg/2 ml may cause hypotension and tachycardia in certain individuals.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The results of animal studies of Veralgin 5 mg/2 ml did not reveal any teratogenic effects; no deformities have been reported with normal use. In the absence of sufficient data, caution should be exercised for nursing mothers, although no problems have been reported with normal use.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be taken during the treatment of patients with disorders of the prostate.</li><li>Caution should also be taken in cases of chronic bronchitis, coronary insufficiency, ambient hyperthermia, renal &amp; hepatic insufficiency.</li><li>The risks of visual disturbances can make it dangerous to drive or use machines.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Veralgin 5 mg/2 ml</strong></p><p>There is no available data regarding the overdose of Tiemonium methylsulphate tablets.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Paediatric use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness of Tiemonium methylsulphate in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric use</strong>: Efficacy and safety were maintained with increasing age.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Anticholinergics</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Veralgin 5 mg/2 ml is a competitive antagonist of Acetylcholine, Histamine, and strengthens the calcium bond with membrane phospholipids and proteins. This inhibits intracellular contractile proteins of visceral cells, which causes inhibition of visceral spasms and pain.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The results of animal studies of Veralgin 5 mg/2 ml did not reveal any teratogenic effects; no deformities have been reported with normal use. In the absence of sufficient data, caution should be exercised for nursing mothers, although no problems have been reported with normal use.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Paediatric use: Safety and effectiveness of Tiemonium methylsulphate in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p>Geriatric use: Efficacy and safety were maintained with increasing age.</p><p>Let me know if you need any further modifications or clarifications!</p>', 'active', '725_1742453217.jpg', 29, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 11:46:57', '2025-05-07 16:30:14'),
(3640, 'Vercef', 'Cefpodoxime Proxetil', 98.00, 1, 'x 50 ml bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '40 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vercef 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by susceptible microorganisms, listed below:</p><ul><li><strong>Acute otitis media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenza</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains).</li><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>.</li><li><strong>Community acquired pneumonia</strong> caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em> or <em>H. influenza</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em>, <em>H. influenzae</em> (non-beta-lactamase-producing strains only), or <em>M. catarrhalis</em>.</li><li><strong>Skin and skin structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, or <em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong> caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Rectal gonococcal infections in women</strong> due to <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is an oral third-generation cephalosporin, which has good stability to beta-lactamases and activity against Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. It is indicated for the treatment of infections either before the infecting organism has been identified. Cefpodoxime is a prodrug, and its active metabolite is Cefpodoxime. Approximately 29 to 33% of Cefpodoxime is excreted unchanged in the urine in 12 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Vercef 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (13 years and older):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis:</strong> 100 mg every 12 hours, for 5 to 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 200 mg every 12 hours, for 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Community acquired pneumonia:</strong> 200 mg every 12 hours, for 14 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbations of chronic bronchitis:</strong> 200 mg every 12 hours, for 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Skin and skin structure infections:</strong> 400 mg every 12 hours, for 7 to 14 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections:</strong> 100 mg every 12 hours, for 7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea:</strong> single dose of 200 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Rectal gonococcal infections in women:</strong> single dose of 200 mg.</li><li><strong>Infants and Pediatric Patients (2 months to 12 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute otitis media:</strong> 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours, for 5 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis:</strong> 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours, for 5 to 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours, for 10 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Vercef 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant administration of high doses of <strong>antacids</strong> (sodium bicarbonate and aluminum hydroxide) or <strong>H2 blockers</strong> reduces peak plasma levels and the extent of absorption, respectively.</li><li>Renal excretion of <strong>Cefpodoxime</strong> is inhibited by <strong>probenecid</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cefpodoxime</strong> is contraindicated in patients with known allergy to cephalosporins.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Vercef 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cefpodoxime</strong> has very few side effects. Possible side effects include gastrointestinal disorders such as diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain.</li><li>Rash, urticaria, and itching may also occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> US FDA pregnancy category of <strong>Cefpodoxime</strong> is B. There is no adequate and well-controlled study in pregnant women. Therefore, it should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> <strong>Cefpodoxime</strong> has been shown to be excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administering <strong>Cefpodoxime</strong> to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In patients with transient or persistent reduction in urinary output due to <strong>renal insufficiency</strong>, the total daily dose of <strong>Cefpodoxime</strong> should be reduced.</li><li><strong>Cefpodoxime</strong> should be administered with caution to patients receiving concurrent treatment with potent diuretics.</li><li>As with other antibiotics, prolonged use of <strong>Cefpodoxime</strong> may result in overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: For patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min), the dosing intervals should be increased to 24 hours.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is required for patients with hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ol><li>Shake the bottle well to loosen the powder.</li><li>Add boiled and cooled water to the bottle.</li><li>Shake until the powder is completely mixed with the water.</li></ol><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Third generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cefpodoxime</strong> is an oral third-generation cephalosporin, with good stability to beta-lactamases and activity against both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. It is a prodrug, and its active metabolite is Cefpodoxime. Approximately 29 to 33% of <strong>Cefpodoxime</strong> is excreted unchanged in the urine within 12 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>For patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min), the dosing intervals should be increased to 24 hours. No dosage adjustment is required in cases of hepatic impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '248_1742453391.jpg', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 11:49:51', '2025-05-07 16:31:04'),
(3641, 'Vercef', 'Cefpodoxime Proxetil', 60.00, 1, 'x 15 ml bottle', '15 ml bottle', 'Drop', '20 mg/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vercef 20 mg/ml</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by susceptible microorganisms, listed below:</p><ul><li><strong>Acute otitis media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenza</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>.</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia</strong> caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em> or <em>H. influenza</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em>, <em>H. influenzae</em> (non-beta-lactamase-producing strains only), or <em>M. catarrhalis</em>.</li><li><strong>Skin and skin structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, or <em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong> caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Rectal gonococcal infections in women</strong> due to <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Third-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is an oral third-generation cephalosporin, which has good stability to beta-lactamases and activity against Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. It is indicated for the treatment of infections, either before the infecting organism has been identified. It is a prodrug, and its active metabolite is Cefpodoxime. Approximately 29 to 33% of Cefpodoxime is excreted unchanged in the urine in 12 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Vercef 20 mg/ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (13 years and older)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/tonsillitis: 100 mg 12 hourly, 5 to 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute maxillary sinusitis: 200 mg 12 hourly, 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Community-acquired pneumonia: 200 mg 12 hourly, 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis: 200 mg 12 hourly, 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin and skin structure infections: 400 mg 12 hourly, 7 to 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: 100 mg 12 hourly, 7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated gonorrhea: single dose of 200 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rectal gonococcal infections in women: single dose of 200 mg</li><li><strong>Infants and Pediatric Patients (2 months to 12 years)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute otitis media: 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly, 5 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/tonsillitis: 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly, 5 to 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute maxillary sinusitis: 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly, 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Vercef 20 mg/ml</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant administration of high doses of antacids (sodium bicarbonate and aluminum hydroxide) or H2 blockers reduces peak plasma levels and the extent of absorption, respectively.</li><li>Renal excretion of Cefpodoxime is inhibited by probenecid.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is contraindicated in patients with a known allergy to cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Vercef 20 mg/ml</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime has very few side effects. Possible side effects include gastrointestinal disorders (such as diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain), rash, urticaria, and itching.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>US FDA pregnancy category of Cefpodoxime is B.</li><li>There is no adequate and well-controlled study in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Cefpodoxime has been shown to be excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Cefpodoxime is administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In patients with transient or persistent reduction in urinary output due to renal insufficiency, the total daily dose of Cefpodoxime should be reduced.</li><li>Cefpodoxime should be administered with caution to patients receiving concurrent treatment with potent diuretics.</li><li>As with other antibiotics, prolonged use of Cefpodoxime may result in overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min): The dosing intervals should be increased to 24 hourly.</li><li>No dosage adjustment is required in cases of hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ol><li>Shake the bottle well to loosen the powder.</li><li>Add boiled and cooled water to the bottle.</li><li>Shake until the powder is completely mixed with water.</li></ol><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Third-generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is an oral third-generation cephalosporin with good stability to beta-lactamases and activity against Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. It is a prodrug, and its active metabolite is Cefpodoxime. Approximately 29 to 33% of Cefpodoxime is excreted unchanged in the urine in 12 hours.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>US FDA pregnancy category of Cefpodoxime is B.</li><li>This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed, as there is no adequate and well-controlled study in pregnant women.</li><li>Cefpodoxime is excreted in human milk; caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>For patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min), the dosing intervals should be increased to 24 hourly.</li><li>No dosage adjustment is required in cases of hepatic impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '537_1742453849.jpg', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 11:57:29', '2025-05-07 16:31:29'),
(3642, 'Vercef DS', 'Cefpodoxime Proxetil', 175.00, 1, 'x 50 ml bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '80 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vercef DS 80 mg/5 ml (Cefpodoxime)</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by susceptible microorganisms, listed below:</p><ul><li><strong>Acute otitis media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenza</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains).</li><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>.</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia</strong> caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em> or <em>H. influenza</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em>, <em>H. influenzae</em> (non-beta-lactamase-producing strains only), or <em>M. catarrhalis</em>.</li><li><strong>Skin and skin structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, or <em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong> caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Rectal gonococcal infections in women</strong> caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Third-generation Cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is an oral 3rd generation cephalosporin with good stability to beta-lactamases and activity against both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. It is a prodrug, and its active metabolite is Cefpodoxime. Approximately 29 to 33% of Cefpodoxime is excreted unchanged in the urine within 12 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Vercef DS 80 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Adults and Adolescents (13 years and older):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis:</strong> 100 mg 12 hourly for 5 to 10 days.</li><li><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 200 mg 12 hourly for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia:</strong> 200 mg 12 hourly for 14 days.</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbations of chronic bronchitis:</strong> 200 mg 12 hourly for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Skin and skin structure infections:</strong> 400 mg 12 hourly for 7 to 14 days.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections:</strong> 100 mg 12 hourly for 7 days.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea:</strong> Single dose of 200 mg.</li><li><strong>Rectal gonococcal infections in women:</strong> Single dose of 200 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Infants and Pediatric Patients (2 months to 12 years):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Acute otitis media:</strong> 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly for 5 days.</li><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis:</strong> 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly for 5 to 10 days.</li><li><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 5 mg/kg body weight 12 hourly for 10 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Vercef DS 80 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Antacids</strong> (sodium bicarbonate and aluminum hydroxide) and <strong>H2 blockers</strong> may reduce peak plasma levels and the extent of absorption of Cefpodoxime.</li><li><strong>Probenecid</strong> inhibits the renal excretion of Cefpodoxime.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is contraindicated in patients with a known allergy to cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Vercef DS 80 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime generally has very few side effects. Possible side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal disorders</strong> such as diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain.</li><li><strong>Rash, urticaria, and itching.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>US FDA pregnancy category</strong>: B.</li><li>There is no adequate and well-controlled study in pregnant women. This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Cefpodoxime has been shown to be excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In patients with transient or persistent reduction in urinary output due to renal insufficiency, the total daily dose of Cefpodoxime should be reduced.</li><li>Cefpodoxime should be administered with caution to patients receiving potent diuretics.</li><li>As with other antibiotics, prolonged use of Cefpodoxime may result in the overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>In patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min), the dosing intervals should be increased to 24 hours.</li><li>No dosage adjustment is required for hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ol><li>Shake the bottle well to loosen the powder.</li><li>Add boiled and cooled water to the bottle.</li><li>Shake until the powder is completely mixed with the water.</li></ol><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is an oral 3rd generation cephalosporin, which has good stability to beta-lactamases and activity against both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. It is a prodrug, and its active metabolite is Cefpodoxime. Approximately 29 to 33% of Cefpodoxime is excreted unchanged in the urine in 12 hours.</p>', 'active', '1621_1742453974.jpg', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 11:59:34', '2025-05-04 22:51:21');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3643, 'Vermicom', 'Levamisole Hydrochloride', 18.00, 1, 'x 30 ml bottle', '30 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '40 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml is a fast-acting drug that acts on nematode nerve ganglia, paralysing the worm’s musculature within seconds of contact. Unable to maintain their position, the worms are then ejected by normal peristaltic movement, usually within 24 hours of Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml administration. Although it is certain that Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml primarily influences the neuromuscular system of nematodes, it is possible that in some helminths, the inhibition of the fumarate reductase system contributes to the anthelmintic efficacy of Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml.</p><p>Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml is indicated for the treatment of infections by the following gastrointestinal worm species:</p><ul><li><strong>Ascaris lumbricoides</strong>: Roundworm</li><li><strong>Necator americanus</strong>: Hookworm</li><li><strong>Ancylostoma duodenal</strong>: Hookworm</li><li><strong>Enterobius vermicularis</strong>: Pinworm</li><li><strong>Trichuris trichuria</strong>: Whipworm</li><li><strong>Strongyloides stercoralis</strong>: Threadworm</li><li><strong>Trichostrongylus colubriformis</strong>: Hookworm</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Anthelmintic</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml is the active laevo-isomer of tetramisole. It works by paralysing susceptible intestinal worms, which are then excreted from the intestines. Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml also enhances cellular immune responses in humans.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>The following doses of Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml are given as a single administration, preferably after a light meal:</p><ul><li><strong>Age 1-4 years</strong>: 1 Tablet or 5 ml Syrup</li><li><strong>Age 5-15 years</strong>: 2 Tablets or 10 ml Syrup</li><li><strong>Age 16 years and over</strong>: 3 Tablets or 15 ml Syrup</li></ul><p>In cases of severe hookworm infection, it is suggested that a second standard dose be given one or seven days after the first, whichever timing is feasible.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction of Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>May increase toxicity of phenytoin</li><li>Increases bioavailability of ivermectin</li><li>Decreases bioavailability of albendazole</li><li>Alcohol causes a disulfiram-like reaction</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>There is no absolute contraindication to the use of Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Side Effects of Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Side effects are infrequent. They are usually mild and transient, including:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Giddiness (dizziness)</li><li>Headache</li></ul><p>An encephalopathy-like syndrome has been reported to occur in a few patients two or three weeks after treatment.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Although studies in animals have shown that Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml produces no teratogenic effects, current medical practice requires that the benefits of any drug used during pregnancy should be weighed against the possible dangers.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Effect on ability to drive or operate machinery</strong>: There is no evidence to suggest that Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml, used for anthelmintic purposes, will produce sedation. Mild and transient giddiness is an infrequent side effect of treatment. No precautions are suggested concerning the ability to drive or operate machinery.</li><li><strong>Concurrent microfilaraemia</strong>: A transient fever may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Counter possible anticholinesterase activity with atropine.</li><li>Control blood pressure and respiration.</li><li>Do not use sedatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>: Store at room temperature and protect from moisture.</li><li><strong>Syrup</strong>: Store at room temperature and protect from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Anthelmintic</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml is the active laevo-isomer of tetramisole. It works by paralysing susceptible intestinal worms, which are then excreted from the intestines. Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml also enhances cellular immune responses in humans.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Although studies in animals have shown that Vermicom 40 mg/5 ml produces no teratogenic effects, current medical practice requires that the benefits of any drug used during pregnancy should be weighed against the possible dangers.</p>', 'active', '828_1742454218.webp', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 12:03:38', '2025-05-04 22:52:56'),
(3644, 'Verorab', 'Rabies Vaccine', 998.00, 1, 'x 2.5 IU vial', '2.5 IU vial', 'IV Injection', '2.5 IU per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Verorab 2.5 IU/ml</strong></p><p>Rabies vaccine is indicated for prophylactic immunization against rabies and treatment of patients following suspected rabies contact.</p><p><strong>Pre-exposure Immunization:</strong></p><p>Professional groups exposed to frequent contamination include:</p><ul><li>Veterinary surgeons (including students at veterinary colleges)</li><li>Technical personnel working with veterinary surgeons</li><li>Laboratory personnel handling material contaminated with rabies virus</li><li>Personnel in abattoirs and knackers yards</li><li>Taxidermists</li><li>Gamekeepers, forestry workers, and naturalists in enzootic areas</li><li>Infants particularly exposed to the risk of rabies</li></ul><p><strong>Post-exposure Immunization:</strong></p><p>Treatment of subjects bitten by rabid animals or those suspected of being so.</p><p>Treatment of contact subjects.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Vaccines, Anti-sera &amp; Immunoglobulin</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rabies vaccine is an inactivated virus vaccine that is used for active immunization against rabies. It can be used for pre- and post-exposure immunization. For post-exposure immunization, it is often used in conjunction with rabies immunoglobulins as it takes about 7-10 days for the specific antibodies to develop.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Verorab 2.5 IU/ml</strong></p><p>To reconstitute the vaccine, transfer the content of the supplied diluent into the vial containing the freeze-dried preparation. Do not shake. After reconstitution, the solution should be homogeneous, clear, and free from any particles. The vaccine must be injected immediately after reconstitution, and the syringe should be destroyed after use.</p><p><strong>Method of administration for intramuscular use:</strong></p><ul><li>The 1 ml dose of rabies vaccine should be given intramuscularly in the deltoid for adults and in the anterolateral aspect of the thigh muscle for children under 1 year. It should not be injected into the gluteal region.</li><li>Do not inject intravenously.</li></ul><p><strong>Method of administration for intradermal use:</strong></p><ul><li>The 0.1 ml dose of rabies vaccine (per site) should be administered intradermally in the upper arm, over the deltoid.</li></ul><p><strong>Pre-exposure Immunization (Primary vaccination):</strong></p><p>1 ml for children and adults.</p><ul><li>According to WHO recommendations: 1 injection by the intramuscular route on days 0, 7, 21 or 28, followed by a booster dose one year later.</li></ul><p><strong>Boosters:</strong></p><ul><li>Thereafter, one injection every 5 years or when the titre is found to be less than 0.5 IU/ml.</li></ul><p><strong>Post-exposure Immunization:</strong></p><p><strong>Local treatment of the wound:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vaccination of non-immunized subjects:</strong></li><li><strong>Intramuscular schedules:</strong></li><li>Standard intramuscular (1-1-1-1-1) regimen:</li><li>Day 0: 1 injection of 1 ml</li><li>Day 3: 1 injection of 1 ml</li><li>Day 7: 1 injection of 1 ml</li><li>Day 14: 1 injection of 1 ml</li><li>Day 28: 1 injection of 1 ml</li><li>Or Abbreviated multisite (2-1-1) regimen:</li><li>Day 0: 2 injections, each of 1 ml, at separate sites</li><li>Day 7: 1 injection of 1 ml</li><li>Day 21: 1 injection of 1 ml</li></ul><p><strong>Intradermal schedules:</strong></p><p>One intradermal (ID) dose is 0.1 ml.</p><ul><li><strong>Thai Red Cross (2-2-2-0-1-1) schedule:</strong></li><li>Day 0: 2 injections, each of 0.1 ml, at separate sites</li><li>Day 3: 2 injections, each of 0.1 ml, at separate sites</li><li>Day 7: 2 injections, each of 0.1 ml, at separate sites</li><li>Day 28: 1 injection of 0.1 ml</li><li>Day 90: 1 injection of 0.1 ml (optional).</li><li><strong>WHO Modified Thai Red Cross (2-2-2-0-2) schedule:</strong></li><li>Day 0: 2 injections, each of 0.1 ml, at separate sites</li><li>Day 3: 2 injections, each of 0.1 ml, at separate sites</li><li>Day 7: 2 injections, each of 0.1 ml, at separate sites</li><li>Day 28: 2 injections, each of 0.1 ml, at separate sites</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Verorab 2.5 IU/ml</strong></p><p><strong>Administrations:</strong></p><p>Prompt and gentle thorough washing with soap or detergent and flushing the wound with running tap water for at least 15 minutes. After washing, disinfectants like either ethanol (700 ml/l) or tincture or aqueous solution of iodine or povidone iodine must be applied. Don’t bandage or suture the wound.</p><p><strong>Co-administration:</strong></p><p>Corticosteroid and immunosuppressive treatment may interfere with antibody production and cause vaccination failure. In these cases, a titration of neutralizing antibodies should be performed.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Verorab 2.5 IU/ml</strong></p><p>Concurrent use with immunosuppressants may reduce the efficacy of vaccines.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Rabies vaccine is contraindicated in the following cases:</p><ul><li><strong>Pre-exposure:</strong></li><li>Severe fever, febrile infection, acute disease, progressive chronic diseases, known hypersensitivity reactions to rabies vaccine or any of its components.</li><li><strong>Post-exposure:</strong></li><li>No contraindication to post-exposure treatment, because rabies is a lethal disease. Any contraindication to exposure, treatment should be considered carefully before disqualifying an individual for anti-rabies treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Verorab 2.5 IU/ml</strong></p><p>Minor local reactions like pain, erythema, oedema, pruritus, and induration at the injection site, lasting 24-48 hours.</p><p>Moderate fever, shivering, fainting, asthenia, dizziness, respiratory manifestations (dyspnoea, wheezing), fever, abdominal pain, vomiting, and allergic skin reactions (urticaria, rash, itching).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></li><li>The potential risk of administration of rabies vaccine during pregnancy is unknown. Due to the severity of the disease, pregnancy is not considered to be a contraindication to post-exposure prophylaxis.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong></li><li>It is not known whether the vaccine is excreted in human breast milk. Due to the severity of the disease, breast-feeding is not considered a contraindication.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p><strong>Precaution:</strong></p><ul><li>Intravenous injection is prohibited.</li><li>The vaccine and anti-rabies immunoglobulin must not be administered with the same syringe and in the same injection site.</li><li>Before use, please carefully check package, label, appearance, and the validity period.</li><li>After reconstitution, the freeze-dried rabies vaccine should be administered as soon as possible.</li><li>Do not shake during and after reconstitution.</li></ul><p><strong>Special precaution for the intradermal route:</strong></p><ul><li>It is essential that intradermal administration of the vaccine be administered only by medical staff trained in the ID technique to ensure the vaccine is delivered intradermally and not subcutaneously.</li><li>For the intradermal route, a sterile syringe with a fixed needle (insulin type) is preferred.</li><li>A sterile needle and syringe must be used to withdraw and administer each dose of the vaccine for each patient to avoid cross-infection.</li><li>Correct intradermal injection should result in a raised papule with an orange peel appearance. If the vaccine has been injected too deeply and a papule is not seen, the needle should be withdrawn and re-inserted nearby.</li><li>This vaccine does not contain a preservative; therefore, great care must be taken to avoid contamination of reconstituted vaccine.</li><li>Any reconstituted vaccine should be used as soon as possible.</li></ul><p><strong>ID route must not be used in the following conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Patients receiving immunosuppressive therapies, including irradiation, anti-metabolites, alkylating agents, cytotoxic drugs, and corticosteroids.</li><li>Immunocompromised individuals.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of the reach and sight of children.</li><li>Store at +2 °C to +8 °C.</li><li>Transportation should also be at +2 °C to +8 °C.</li><li>Protect from light.</li><li>Do not freeze.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p>Reconstitute with the provided diluent.</p>', 'active', '865_1742454408.jpg', 45, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 12:06:48', '2025-05-04 22:53:16'),
(3645, 'Vertex', 'Ceftriaxone Sodium', 190.21, 1, 'x 1 gm vial', '1 gm vial', 'IV Injection', '1 gm/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vertex 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Vertex 1 gm/vial is indicated for the treatment of the following major infections:</p><ul><li>Lower respiratory tract infections</li><li>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media</li><li>Skin and skin structure infections</li><li>Urinary tract infections</li><li>Gonorrhea</li><li>Bacterial Septicemia</li><li>Bone and joint infections</li><li>Meningitis</li><li>Prevention of postoperative infections</li><li>Perioperative prophylaxis of infections associated with surgery</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Third-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Vertex 1 gm/vial is a 3rd generation broad-spectrum parenteral cephalosporin antibiotic. It has potent bactericidal activity against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms. Like other cephalosporins and penicillins, Vertex 1 gm/vial kills bacteria by interfering with the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall. Vertex 1 gm/vial has a high degree of stability in the presence of beta-lactamases. A remarkable feature of Vertex 1 gm/vial is its relatively long plasma elimination half-life of about 6 to 9 hours, which makes single or once-daily dosage of the drug appropriate for most patients. Vertex 1 gm/vial is not metabolized in the body. About 40-65% of a dose of Vertex 1 gm/vial is excreted unchanged in the urine; the remainder is excreted in the bile and ultimately found in the feces as unchanged drug and microbiologically inactive compound. The drug is highly protein-bound (95%).</p><p><strong>Dosage of Vertex 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p><strong>Adult</strong>: The usual dose is 1 to 2 gm by intravenous or intramuscular administration once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day).</p><ul><li>Pneumonia, Bronchitis, Acute bacterial otitis media, Skin and skin structure infection, Urinary tract infections, Bacterial Septicemia, Bone and joint infections, Meningitis: 1 to 2 g IV or IM once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 4 gm/day</li><li>Uncomplicated gonococcal infections: 250 mg IM as a single dose</li><li>Surgical prophylaxis: 1 g IV as a single dose 30 to 120 minutes before surgery</li></ul><p><strong>Infants and Children (01 month or older)</strong>: The usual dose is 50 to 75 mg/kg intravenous or intramuscular administration once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day).</p><ul><li>Pneumonia, Bronchitis, Skin and skin structure infection, Urinary tract infections, Bacterial Septicemia, Bone and joint infections: 50 to 75 mg/kg IV or IM once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 2 gm/day</li><li>Acute bacterial otitis media: 50 mg/kg IM in single dose; Maximum dose: 1 gm/day</li><li>Meningitis: 100 mg/kg IV or IM in single daily dose or (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 4 gm/day</li></ul><p><strong>Duration of therapy</strong>: Continue for more than 2 days after signs and symptoms of infection have disappeared. Usual duration is 4 to 14 days; in complicated infections, longer therapy may be required.</p><p><strong>Administration of Vertex 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p><strong>Preparation of Solutions for Intramuscular / Intravenous Injections</strong>:</p><ul><li>For Intramuscular Injection: 250 mg or 500 mg Vertex 1 gm/vial should be dissolved in 2 ml Lidocaine HCI 1% injection or 1 g Vertex 1 gm/vial in 3.5 ml of Lidocaine HCI 1% injection.</li><li>For Intravenous Injection: 250 mg or 500 mg Vertex 1 gm/vial should be dissolved in 5 ml of Water for injection or 1 g Vertex 1 gm/vial in 10 ml of Water for injection USP or 2 g Vertex 1 gm/vial in 20 ml of Water for injection.</li><li>The injection should be administered over 2-4 minutes, by Intramuscular or Intravenous injection or by tubing infusion over a period of 30 minutes at a concentration between 10 mg/mL and 40 mg/mL. Before starting treatment through Vertex 1 gm/vial injection, patient tolerance test should be checked by administration of a test dose. (The use of freshly reconstituted solution is recommended. However, it maintains potency for at least 6 hours at room temperature or 24 hours at 5°C).</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Vertex 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vertex 1 gm/vial should not be given to patients with a history of hypersensitivity to cephalosporin antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Vertex 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Vertex 1 gm/vial is generally well tolerated. A few side effects such as gastro-intestinal effects including diarrhea, nausea and vomiting, stomatitis and glossitis; cutaneous reactions including rash, pruritus, urticaria, edema, and erythema multiforme; hematologic reactions including eosinophilia, thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, anemia and neutropenia; hepatic reactions including elevations of SGOT or SGPT, bilirubinemia; CNS reactions including nervousness, confusion, sleep disturbances, headache, hyperactivity, convulsion, hypertonia, and dizziness were reported. Local phlebitis occurs rarely following intravenous administration but can be minimized by slow injections over 2-4 minutes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Its safety in human pregnancy has not been established. Therefore, it should not be used in pregnancy unless absolutely indicated. Low concentrations of Vertex 1 gm/vial are excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Vertex 1 gm/vial is administered to a lactating mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>As with other cephalosporins, anaphylactic shock cannot be ruled out even if a thorough patient history is taken. Anaphylactic shock requires immediate countermeasures such as intravenous epinephrine followed by a glucocorticoid. In rare cases, shadows suggesting sludge have been detected by sonograms of the gallbladder. This condition was reversible on discontinuation or completion of Vertex 1 gm/vial therapy. Even if such findings are associated with pain, conservative, nonsurgical management is recommended. During prolonged treatment, the blood picture should be checked at regular intervals.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Vertex 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p>There is no specific antidote. Treatment of overdosage should be symptomatic.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Vial store in a cool, dry place (below 30° C), away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>Vertex 1 gm/vial must not be given to neonates if the neonates is premature and newborn (up to 28 days of age).</p>', 'active', '34_1742454563.jpg', 43, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 12:09:23', '2025-05-07 16:33:00'),
(3646, 'Vertex', 'Ceftriaxone Sodium', 100.30, 1, 'x 250 mg vial', '250 mg vial', 'IV Injection', '250 mg/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vertex 250 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Vertex 250 mg/vial is indicated for the treatment of the following major infections:</p><ul><li>Lower respiratory tract infections</li><li>Acute bacterial otitis media</li><li>Skin and skin structure infections</li><li>Urinary tract infections</li><li>Gonorrhea</li><li>Bacterial septicemia</li><li>Bone and joint infections</li><li>Meningitis</li><li>Prevention of postoperative infections</li><li>Perioperative prophylaxis of infections associated with surgery</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Third generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Vertex 250 mg/vial is a 3rd generation broad-spectrum parenteral cephalosporin antibiotic. It has potent bactericidal activity against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms. Like other cephalosporins and penicillins, Vertex 250 mg/vial kills bacteria by interfering with the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall. Vertex 250 mg/vial has a high degree of stability in the presence of beta-lactamases. A remarkable feature of Vertex 250 mg/vial is its relatively long plasma elimination half-life of about 6 to 9 hours, which makes single or once-daily dosage of the drug appropriate for most patients. Vertex 250 mg/vial is not metabolized in the body. About 40-65% of a dose of Vertex 250 mg/vial is excreted unchanged in the urine; the remainder is excreted in the bile and ultimately found in the feces as unchanged drug and microbiologically inactive compound. The drug is highly protein-bound (95%).</p><p><strong>Dosage of Vertex 250 mg/vial</strong></p><p><strong>Adult:</strong></p><ul><li>The usual dose is 1 to 2 gm by intravenous or intramuscular administration once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day).</li><li>Pneumonia, Bronchitis, Acute bacterial otitis media, Skin and skin structure infections, Urinary tract infections, Bacterial septicemia, Bone and joint infections, Meningitis: 1 to 2 g IV or IM once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 4 gm/day.</li><li>Uncomplicated gonococcal infections: 250 mg IM as a single dose.</li><li>Surgical prophylaxis: 1 g IV as a single dose 30 to 120 minutes before surgery.</li></ul><p><strong>Infants and Children (01 month or older):</strong></p><ul><li>The usual dose is 50 to 75 mg/kg intravenous or intramuscular administration once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day).</li><li>Pneumonia, Bronchitis, Skin and skin structure infections, Urinary tract infections, Bacterial septicemia, Bone and joint infections: 50 to 75 mg/kg IV or IM once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 2 gm/day.</li><li>Acute bacterial otitis media: 50 mg/kg IM in single dose; Maximum dose: 1 gm/day.</li><li>Meningitis: 100 mg/kg IV or IM in single daily dose (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 4 gm/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Duration of therapy:</strong> Continue for more than 2 days after signs and symptoms of infection have disappeared. Usual duration is 4 to 14 days; in complicated infections, longer therapy may be required.</p><p><strong>Administration of Vertex 250 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Preparation of Solutions for Intramuscular / Intravenous Injections:For Intramuscular Injection:</strong> 250 mg or 500 mg Vertex 250 mg/vial should be dissolved in 2 ml Lidocaine HCl 1% injection or 1 g Vertex 250 mg/vial in 3.5 ml of Lidocaine HCl 1% injection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Intravenous Injection:</strong> 250 mg or 500 mg Vertex 250 mg/vial should be dissolved in 5 ml of Water for injection or 1 g Vertex 250 mg/vial in 10 ml of Water for injection USP or 2 g Vertex 250 mg/vial in 20 ml of Water for injection. The injection should be administered over 2-4 minutes, by Intramuscular or Intravenous injection or by tubing infusion over a period of 30 minutes at concentration between 10 mg/mL and 40 mg/mL.</li><li><strong>Patient Tolerance:</strong> Before starting treatment through Vertex 250 mg/vial injection, patient tolerance test should be checked by administration of a test dose. The use of freshly reconstituted solution is recommended. However, it maintains potency for at least 6 hours at room temperature or 24 hours at 5°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Vertex 250 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li>No drug interactions have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Vertex 250 mg/vial should not be given to patients with a history of hypersensitivity to cephalosporin antibiotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Vertex 250 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Vertex 250 mg/vial is generally well tolerated. A few side effects such as:</p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal effects including diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, stomatitis, and glossitis.</li><li>Cutaneous reactions including rash, pruritus, urticaria, edema, and erythema multiforme.</li><li>Hematologic reactions including eosinophilia, thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, anemia, and neutropenia.</li><li>Hepatic reactions including elevations of SGOT or SGPT, bilirubinemia.</li><li>CNS reactions including nervousness, confusion, sleep disturbances, headache, hyperactivity, convulsion, hypertonia, and dizziness.</li></ul><p>Local phlebitis occurs rarely following intravenous administration but can be minimized by slow injections over 2-4 minutes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Its safety in human pregnancy has not been established. Therefore, it should not be used in pregnancy unless absolutely indicated.</li><li>Low concentrations of Vertex 250 mg/vial are excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Vertex 250 mg/vial is administered to a lactating mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>As with other cephalosporins, anaphylactic shock cannot be ruled out even if a thorough patient history is taken. Anaphylactic shock requires immediate countermeasures such as intravenous epinephrine followed by a glucocorticoid.</li><li>In rare cases, shadows suggesting sludge have been detected by sonograms of the gallbladder. This condition was reversible on discontinuation or completion of Vertex 250 mg/vial therapy. Even if such findings are associated with pain, conservative, nonsurgical management is recommended.</li><li>During prolonged treatment, the blood picture should be checked at regular intervals.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Vertex 250 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li>There is no specific antidote. Treatment of overdosage should be symptomatic.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Vial store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Vertex 250 mg/vial must not be given to neonates if the neonates is premature or a newborn (up to 28 days of age).</li></ul>', 'active', '1650_1742454693.jpg', 43, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 12:11:33', '2025-05-07 16:33:07'),
(3647, 'Vertex', 'Ceftriaxone Sodium', 130.39, 1, 'x 500 mg vial', '500 mg vial', 'IV Injection', '500 mg/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vertex 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Vertex 500 mg/vial is indicated for the treatment of the following major infections:</p><ul><li>Lower respiratory tract infections</li><li>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media</li><li>Skin and skin structure infections</li><li>Urinary tract infections</li><li>Gonorrhea</li><li>Bacterial Septicemia</li><li>Bone and joint infections</li><li>Meningitis</li><li>Prevention of postoperative infections</li><li>Perioperative prophylaxis of infections associated with surgery</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Third-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Vertex 500 mg/vial is a third-generation broad-spectrum parenteral cephalosporin antibiotic. It exhibits potent bactericidal activity against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms. Like other cephalosporins and penicillins, Vertex 500 mg/vial kills bacteria by interfering with the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall. Vertex 500 mg/vial is stable in the presence of beta-lactamases. A key feature is its relatively long plasma elimination half-life (6-9 hours), allowing for once-daily administration for most patients. It is not metabolized in the body; 40-65% of a dose is excreted unchanged in the urine, with the remainder excreted in bile and feces. The drug is highly protein-bound (95%).</p><p><strong>Dosage of Vertex 500 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li>The usual dose is 1-2 g by intravenous or intramuscular administration once a day (or divided into two doses per day).</li><li>For conditions such as pneumonia, bronchitis, acute bacterial otitis media, skin infections, urinary tract infections, bacterial septicemia, bone and joint infections, and meningitis: 1-2 g IV or IM once a day (or divided doses). Maximum dose: 4 g/day.</li><li>For uncomplicated gonococcal infections: 250 mg IM as a single dose.</li><li>For surgical prophylaxis: 1 g IV 30-120 minutes before surgery.</li><li><strong>Infants and Children (1 month or older)</strong>:</li><li>The usual dose is 50-75 mg/kg by IV or IM once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day).</li><li>For pneumonia, bronchitis, skin infections, urinary tract infections, bacterial septicemia, bone and joint infections: 50-75 mg/kg IV or IM once a day (or divided doses). Maximum dose: 2 g/day.</li><li>For acute bacterial otitis media: 50 mg/kg IM as a single dose. Maximum dose: 1 g/day.</li><li>For meningitis: 100 mg/kg IV or IM as a single daily dose (or divided doses). Maximum dose: 4 g/day.</li><li>Duration of therapy: Continue for 2+ days after symptoms resolve. Typical duration is 4-14 days; longer therapy may be needed for complicated infections.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Vertex 500 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Intramuscular Injection</strong>:</li><li>Dissolve 250 mg or 500 mg Vertex 500 mg/vial in 2 mL of Lidocaine HCl 1% injection or 1 g Vertex 500 mg/vial in 3.5 mL of Lidocaine HCl 1% injection.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong>:</li><li>Dissolve 250 mg or 500 mg Vertex 500 mg/vial in 5 mL of water for injection or 1 g Vertex 500 mg/vial in 10 mL of water for injection.</li><li>The injection should be administered over 2-4 minutes. For infusion, administer over 30 minutes with concentrations between 10 mg/mL and 40 mg/mL.</li><li>A test dose should be administered before starting treatment.</li><li>Solutions maintain potency for 6 hours at room temperature or 24 hours at 5°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Vertex 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p>No known drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vertex 500 mg/vial should not be administered to patients with a history of hypersensitivity to cephalosporin antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Vertex 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Generally well tolerated, though side effects may include:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, stomatitis, glossitis</li><li><strong>Skin</strong>: Rash, pruritus, urticaria, edema, erythema multiforme</li><li><strong>Hematologic</strong>: Eosinophilia, thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, anemia, neutropenia</li><li><strong>Hepatic</strong>: Elevated SGOT, SGPT, bilirubinemia</li><li><strong>CNS</strong>: Nervousness, confusion, sleep disturbances, headache, hyperactivity, convulsions, hypertonia, dizziness</li><li><strong>Local</strong>: Phlebitis (rare following IV administration, minimized by slow injection)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Safety in pregnancy has not been established. It should only be used if absolutely necessary. Low concentrations are excreted in human milk, so caution is advised when administering to lactating mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Anaphylactic shock is a potential risk with cephalosporins, and immediate treatment is required (e.g., intravenous epinephrine).</li><li>Rarely, sonograms may show shadows suggesting sludge in the gallbladder, which is reversible after discontinuation of therapy. Conservative, non-surgical management is recommended.</li><li>Regular blood tests should be done during prolonged treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Vertex 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p>No specific antidote exists. Treatment is symptomatic.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>Vertex 500 mg/vial should not be used in neonates, particularly premature infants or those less than 28 days of age.</p>', 'active', '858_1742454801.jpg', 43, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 12:13:21', '2025-05-07 16:33:16'),
(3648, 'Viewgut', 'Barium Sulfate', 200.75, 1, 'x 150 gm bag', '150 gm bag', 'Powder For Suspension', '150 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Viewgut 150 gm</strong></p><p>Barium Sulphate is used to help doctors examine the esophagus (tube that connects the mouth and stomach), stomach, and intestines using X-rays or computed tomography (CT scan). Barium sulfate is a class of medications called radiopaque contrast media, which works by coating the esophagus, stomach, or intestine with a material that is not absorbed by the body. This allows diseased or damaged areas to be clearly seen during X-ray examination or CT scan.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Contrast medium for diagnostic procedures.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Barium sulfate increases the absorption of X-rays as they pass through the body, thus highlighting structures where it is localized. Barium sulfate is an insoluble material, and its density provides positive contrast during an X-ray examination. It is an inert, radiopaque substance, not absorbed or metabolized, and is eliminated intact from the body in a manner similar to other non-absorbed inorganic materials. Excretion is based on gastrointestinal transit time.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Viewgut 150 gm</strong></p><p>Barium Sulphate comes in several forms: powder to be mixed with water, suspension (liquid), paste, or tablet. The powder and suspension can be taken by mouth or as an enema. The paste and tablet are taken orally. It is typically taken once or more before an X-ray or CT scan. For enemas, medical staff administers the liquid, and for oral consumption, patients may either take it at home or at the testing center.</p><p>If taking Barium Sulphate at home, follow the instructions carefully, including mixing the powder with water. Shake the liquid well before each use to mix evenly. Patients should follow specific directions regarding food and liquid intake before and after the procedure, as well as the use of laxatives to help clear the substance from the body post-test.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Barium sulfate is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Known or suspected obstruction of the colon</li><li>Gastrointestinal tract perforation</li><li>Suspected tracheoesophageal fistula</li><li>Obstructing lesions of the small intestine</li><li>Pyloric stenosis</li><li>Hypersensitivity to Viewgut 150 gm formulations</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Viewgut 150 gm</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Severe stomach pain</li><li>Cramping</li><li>Diarrhea or constipation</li><li>Sweating</li><li>Ringing in ears</li><li>Confusion</li><li>Fast heart rate</li><li>Pale skin</li><li>Weakness</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Safe use during pregnancy has not been established. Barium sulfate should be used in pregnant women only if the potential benefits outweigh the risks, as radiation is known to harm the unborn fetus exposed in utero.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Procedures involving radiopaque contrast agents should be conducted by trained personnel.</li><li>Special care is needed for those with a history of bronchial asthma, atopy, or allergic reactions to contrast agents.</li><li>Caution should be exercised with severely debilitated patients or those with hypertension or advanced heart disease.</li><li>Ingestion of Viewgut 150 gm suspension is not recommended in patients with a history of food aspiration.</li><li>Rehydration should follow the examination to replace any water loss due to the use of cathartics or liquid diets.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Viewgut 150 gm</strong></p><p>In rare instances, taking too much can result in severe stomach cramps and diarrhea, especially with doses around 30 ounces (900 mL). These cases usually resolve spontaneously and are not life-threatening.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at 25°C.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatrics:</strong> There have been two reported deaths of infants from barium aspiration, but the volume rather than the nature of the material was likely the cause.</li><li><strong>Geriatrics:</strong> Diagnostic studies have not found any geriatrics-specific issues that would limit the use of Viewgut 150 gm. However, colon distention can cause electrocardiographic changes in elderly patients with cardiac disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Contrast medium for diagnostic procedures.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Barium sulfate increases X-ray absorption, creating a positive contrast in X-ray imaging. It is not absorbed or metabolized in the body and is eliminated intact.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Safe use during pregnancy has not been established. Barium sulfate should only be used when the potential benefits outweigh the risks due to the radiation exposure to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Care should be taken in infants, with a history of barium aspiration and caution for elderly patients regarding colon distention and potential heart complications.</p>', 'active', '271_1742455200.webp', 17, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 12:20:00', '2025-05-04 22:56:27'),
(3649, 'Visudyne', 'Verteporfin', 137799.79, 1, 'x 15 mg vial', '15 mg vial', 'Injection', '2 mg/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Visudyne 2 mg/ml</strong></p><p>Visudyne 2 mg/ml therapy is indicated for the treatment of patients with predominantly classic subfoveal choroidal neovascularization due to age-related macular degeneration, pathologic myopia, or presumed ocular histoplasmosis. There is insufficient evidence to indicate Visudyne 2 mg/ml for the treatment of predominantly occult subfoveal choroidal neovascularization.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for Age-Related Macular Degeneration (AMD)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Verteporfm, when activated by low-intensity nonheat-generating laser light at a wavelength corresponding to its absorption peak, generates highly reactive, short-lived singlet O₂ and reactive O₂ radicals that cause local damage to neovascular endothelium, resulting in vessel occlusion. It appears to accumulate preferentially in the neovasculature, e.g., choroidal neovasculature.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Visudyne 2 mg/ml</strong></p><p>A course of Visudyne 2 mg/ml therapy is a two-step process requiring administration of both drug and light. The first step is the intravenous infusion of Visudyne 2 mg/ml. The second step is the activation of Visudyne 2 mg/ml with light from a nonthermal diode laser. The physician should reevaluate the patient 3 months after treatment and if choroidal neovascular leakage is detected on fluorescein angiography, therapy may be repeated.</p><ul><li><strong>Lesion Size Determination</strong>: The greatest linear dimension (GLD) of the lesion should be estimated by fluorescein angiography and color fundus photography. All classic and occult CNV, blood and/or blocked fluorescence, and any serous detachments of the retinal pigment epithelium should be included for this measurement. Fundus cameras with magnification within the range of 2.4-2.6X are recommended. The GLD of the lesion on the fluorescein angiogram must be corrected for the magnification of the fundus camera to obtain the GLD of the lesion on the retina.</li><li><strong>Spot Size Determination</strong>: The treatment spot size should be 1000 microns larger than the GLD of the lesion on the retina to allow a 500-micron border, ensuring full coverage of the lesion. The maximum spot size used in clinical trials was 6400 microns. The nasal edge of the treatment spot must be positioned at least 200 microns from the temporal edge of the optic disc, even if this will result in lack of photoactivation of CNV within 200 microns of the optic nerve.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Visudyne 2 mg/ml</strong></p><ul><li>Increased rate of verteporfm uptake by the vascular endothelium with calcium channel blockers, polymyxin B, or radiation therapy.</li><li>Increased photosensitivity with photosensitizing agents (e.g., tetracyclines, sulfonamides, phenothiazines, sulfonylurea hypoglycaemic agents, thiazide diuretics, and griseofulvin).</li><li>Decreased verteporfm efficacy with compounds that quench active oxygen species or scavenge radicals, e.g., dimethyl sulfoxide, beta-carotene, ethanol, formate, and mannitol.</li><li>Decreased verteporfm efficacy with drugs that decrease clotting, vasoconstriction, or platelet aggregation, e.g., thromboxane A2 inhibitors.</li><li>Anaesthetics may cause hemodynamic effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Visudyne 2 mg/ml is contraindicated for patients with porphyria or a known hypersensitivity to any component of this preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Visudyne 2 mg/ml</strong></p><ul><li>Visual disturbances; severe vision loss with or without subretinal or vitreous bleeding.</li><li>Injection site reactions; nausea; photosensitivity; asthenia; cataracts; blepharitis; conjunctivitis; dry eyes; ocular itching.</li><li>Flu-like syndrome; atrial fibrillation; hypertension; peripheral vascular disorder; varicose veins; eczema; constipation.</li><li>Gl cancers; fever; lachrymation disorder; hypersensitivity reactions; increased LFT.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category</strong>: C. Animal reproduction studies have shown an adverse effect on the fetus, and there are no adequate and well-controlled studies in humans. However, potential benefits may warrant use of the drug in pregnant women despite potential risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Moderate to severe hepatic disorders; biliary disorders.</li><li>Pregnancy.</li><li>Use the largest arm vein possible (e.g., antecubital), especially in the elderly, and avoid small veins in the back of the hand.</li><li>Stop infusion if extravasation occurs and protect the extravasation area from direct light and apply cold compresses.</li><li>The patient must be under close monitoring during verteporfm infusion, and caution should be exercised when general anesthesia is considered.</li><li>Avoid exposure of unprotected skin, eyes, or other body organs to direct sunlight or bright indoor light for 5 days after treatment. Protect all parts of skin and eyes by wearing protective clothing and dark sunglasses (sunscreens are ineffective) if going outdoors in daylight is necessary. Exposure to ambient indoor light is encouraged as it helps in the gradual inactivation of any remaining drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Visudyne 2 mg/ml</strong></p><p>Overdose of the drug and/or light in the treated eye may result in non-perfusion of normal retinal vessels, with the possibility of severe decrease in vision that could be permanent. An overdose of the drug will also result in the prolongation of the period during which the patient remains photosensitive to bright light. In such cases, it is recommended to extend the photosensitivity precautions for a time proportional to the overdose.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Approximately 90% of the patients treated with Visudyne 2 mg/ml in clinical efficacy trials were over the age of 65. A reduced treatment effect was seen with increasing age.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Drugs for Age-Related Macular Degeneration (AMD)</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Verteporfm, when activated by low-intensity nonheat-generating laser light at a wavelength corresponding to its absorption peak, generates highly reactive, short-lived singlet O₂ and reactive O₂ radicals that cause local damage to neovascular endothelium, resulting in vessel occlusion. It appears to accumulate preferentially in the neovasculature, e.g., choroidal neovasculature.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category</strong>: C. Animal reproduction studies have shown an adverse effect on the fetus, and there are no adequate and well-controlled studies in humans. However, potential benefits may warrant use of the drug in pregnant women despite potential risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Approximately 90% of the patients treated with Visudyne 2 mg/ml in the clinical efficacy trials were over the age of 65. A reduced treatment effect was seen with increasing age.</li></ul>', 'active', '464_1742455619.jpg', 40, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 12:26:59', '2025-05-04 23:00:08');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3650, 'Trolen', 'Dantrolene Sodium', 10.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 Capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '25mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p><strong>In Chronic Spasticity:</strong></p><p> Trolen is indicated for controlling clinical spasticity resulting from upper motor neuronal disorders (e.g., spinal cord injury, stroke, cerebral palsy, or multiple sclerosis). It is particularly useful for patients whose functional rehabilitation has been hindered by spasticity. Such patients should have presumably reversible spasticity, where relief would aid in restoring residual function. Trolen is <strong>not</strong> indicated for treating skeletal muscle spasms due to rheumatic disorders. Improvement in spasticity may occasionally occur with Trolen therapy, and information regarding improvement should be solicited from the patient. Withdrawing Trolen for 2 to 4 days may show exacerbation of spasticity and help confirm the clinical impression.</p><p>A decision to continue Trolen on a long-term basis is justified if it:</p><ul><li>Significantly reduces painful or disabling spasticity such as clonus, or</li><li>Allows a significant reduction in the intensity and/or degree of nursing care required, or</li><li>Eliminates any bothersome manifestations of spasticity as deemed important by the patient.</li></ul><p><strong>In Malignant Hyperthermia:</strong></p><p> Trolen is also indicated preoperatively to prevent or reduce signs of malignant hyperthermia in patients who are known or suspected to be susceptible and require anesthesia and/or surgery. Trolen should be administered after a malignant hyperthermic crisis to prevent recurrence.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p> Dantrolene, the active ingredient in Trolen, induces muscle relaxation by affecting the skeletal muscle directly, beyond the myoneural junction. It likely works by disrupting the excitation-contraction coupling, particularly by interfering with the release of calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>For Chronic Spasticity:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li> Start with 25 mg twice per day, gradually increasing to 25-50 mg per day per week. The maximum dosage is 400 mg per day.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients:</strong></li><li> Start with 0.5 mg/kg once daily for 7 days, then increase to 0.5 mg/kg three times daily for 7 days, followed by 1 mg/kg three times daily for 7 days. Eventually, 2 mg/kg three times a day may be needed.</li></ul><p><strong>For Malignant Hyperthermia:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Preoperatively:</strong> Administer 4 to 8 mg/kg/day in 3 to 4 divided doses for 1 or 2 days before surgery.</li><li><strong>Post Crisis Follow-up:</strong> Administer 4 to 8 mg/kg/day in four divided doses for 1 to 3 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Drowsiness may occur, and the use of CNS depressants like sedatives may worsen this effect.</li><li>Hepatotoxicity is more common in women over 35 on estrogen therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Active hepatic disease (e.g., hepatitis, cirrhosis).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p> Common side effects include drowsiness, dizziness, weakness, fatigue, and diarrhea. These effects usually subside early in treatment and can often be mitigated by starting with a lower dose. Severe diarrhea may require temporary withdrawal of Trolen.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C:</strong> Trolen should only be used during pregnancy if the benefit outweighs the risk to the fetus.</li><li>Trolen should <strong>not</strong> be used in nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Liver function studies (SGOT, SGPT, alkaline phosphatase) should be done at the start and during therapy due to potential liver damage.</li><li>Trolen should be withdrawn if symptoms of hepatitis or liver dysfunction appear.</li><li>Caution is needed in patients with pulmonary or cardiac issues and in those with a history of liver disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>The long-term safety of Trolen in children under 5 years has not been established, so careful consideration is required for pediatric use.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Centrally acting Skeletal Muscle Relaxants.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '508_1742457086.jpg', 9, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 12:51:26', '2025-03-20 12:51:26'),
(3651, 'Danlene', 'Dantrolene Sodium', 10.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '25mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p><strong>In Chronic Spasticity:</strong></p><p> Danlene is indicated for controlling clinical spasticity caused by upper motor neuronal disorders such as spinal cord injury, stroke, cerebral palsy, or multiple sclerosis. It is particularly helpful for patients whose rehabilitation has been hindered by spasticity. The drug is useful for patients with presumably reversible spasticity, where relief can aid in restoring function. Danlene is <strong>not</strong> indicated for treating skeletal muscle spasms from rheumatic disorders. Subtle improvements in spasticity may occur with Danlene therapy, and patient feedback should be considered. Withdrawing Danlene for 2 to 4 days may lead to a recurrence of spasticity, which can confirm the clinical impression.</p><p>A decision to continue Danlene long-term is justified if it:</p><ul><li>Significantly reduces painful or disabling spasticity like clonus,</li><li>Decreases the intensity or degree of nursing care required,</li><li>Alleviates any bothersome manifestations of spasticity as reported by the patient.</li></ul><p><strong>In Malignant Hyperthermia:</strong></p><p> Oral Danlene is also indicated for preventing or reducing signs of malignant hyperthermia in patients known or suspected to be susceptible, who require anesthesia or surgery. It should be administered following a malignant hyperthermic crisis to prevent recurrence.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p> Danlene works by relaxing skeletal muscle by affecting its contractile response beyond the myoneural junction. It interferes with excitation-contraction coupling, likely by blocking the release of calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>For Chronic Spasticity:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li> Begin with 25 mg twice daily, increasing to 25-50 mg per day each week as needed. The maximum dose is 400 mg per day.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients:</strong></li><li> Start with 0.5 mg/kg once daily for 7 days, then increase to 0.5 mg/kg three times a day for 7 days, followed by 1 mg/kg three times a day for 7 days. Eventually, 2 mg/kg three times a day may be needed.</li></ul><p><strong>For Malignant Hyperthermia:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Preoperatively:</strong> Administer 4 to 8 mg/kg/day in 3 or 4 divided doses for 1 to 2 days before surgery.</li><li><strong>Post Crisis Follow-up:</strong> Administer 4 to 8 mg/kg/day in four divided doses for 1 to 3 days to prevent recurrence.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Drowsiness is common with Danlene therapy. Concurrent use with CNS depressants like sedatives can enhance this effect.</li><li>Hepatotoxicity is more frequent in women over 35 on estrogen therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Active liver disease (hepatitis, cirrhosis).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p> Common side effects include drowsiness, dizziness, weakness, fatigue, and diarrhea. These effects are typically transient and can be minimized by starting with a lower dose. Severe diarrhea may require stopping therapy.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C:</strong> Danlene should be used in pregnancy only if the benefits justify the risks.</li><li>Danlene should <strong>not</strong> be used in nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Due to the potential for liver damage, Danlene therapy should be stopped if no benefits are seen within 45 days.</li><li>Patients should avoid driving or engaging in hazardous activities while taking Danlene.</li><li>Caution is needed with tranquilizing agents, and patients should be cautious about sun exposure due to possible photosensitivity reactions.</li><li>Liver function tests (SGOT, SGPT, alkaline phosphatase, total bilirubin) should be done before starting and periodically during treatment. If signs of hepatitis or liver dysfunction appear, Danlene should be discontinued.</li><li>Use caution in females and patients over 35, as they are at higher risk for liver damage.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>The long-term safety of Danlene in children under 5 years has not been established. Long-term use in pediatric patients requires careful consideration.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Centrally acting Skeletal Muscle Relaxants.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '640_1742457324.webp', 2, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 12:55:24', '2025-03-20 12:55:24'),
(3652, 'Visral', 'Tiemonium Methylsulfate', 8.00, 1, 'suppository', '1 suppository', 'Suppository', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Visral 20 mg</strong></p><p>Visral 20 mg is an antispasmodic drug that reduces muscle spasms of the intestine, biliary system, bladder, and uterus. It is used in the symptomatic treatment of pain related to functional disorders of the digestive tract and biliary system. It is also indicated for the treatment of spasms and pain in urological and gynaecological diseases.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Anticholinergics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Visral 20 mg is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine, histamine, and strengthens the calcium bond with membrane phospholipids and proteins. This inhibits intracellular contractile proteins of visceral cells, thereby causing the inhibition of visceral spasms and pain.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Visral 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet/SyrupAdults</strong>: Usual dose is 2-6 tablets or 3-9 teaspoonfuls of syrup daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children</strong>: 3 ml/kg or 6 mg/kg body weight daily in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 Visral 20 mg Injection 3 times daily, via intravenous route slowly or intramuscular route.</li><li><strong>Suppository</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg Visral 20 mg suppository two or three times daily, via rectal route.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Visral 20 mg</strong></p><p>Visral 20 mg tablets should not be used with other drugs without prior consultation with a registered physician to avoid possible drug interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It should not be used in patients with urethroprostatic disorders involving a risk of urine retention. It is contraindicated in patients with a risk of angle-closure glaucoma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Visral 20 mg</strong></p><p>Visral 20 mg may have the risk of hypotension and tachycardia in certain individuals.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Animal studies of Visral 20 mg did not reveal any teratogenic effects, and no deformities have been reported with normal use. However, in the absence of sufficient data, caution should be exercised in nursing mothers, although no problems have been reported with normal use.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be taken during the treatment of patients with prostate disorders. Caution should also be taken in cases of chronic bronchitis, coronary insufficiency, ambient hyperthermia, and renal &amp; hepatic insufficiency. Visual disturbances may make it dangerous to drive or use machinery.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Visral 20 mg</strong></p><p>There is no available data regarding the overdose of Visral 20 mg.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness of Visral 20 mg in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric use</strong>: Efficacy and safety are maintained with increasing age.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Anticholinergics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Visral 20 mg is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine, histamine, and strengthens the calcium bond with membrane phospholipids and proteins. This inhibits intracellular contractile proteins of visceral cells, thus inhibiting visceral spasms and pain.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Animal studies of Visral 20 mg did not reveal any teratogenic effects, and no deformities have been reported with normal use. However, in the absence of sufficient data, caution should be exercised in nursing mothers, although no problems have been reported with normal use.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Safety and effectiveness of Visral 20 mg in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><p>Efficacy and safety are maintained with increasing age.</p>', 'active', '1036_1742457519.webp', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 12:58:39', '2025-05-07 19:36:46'),
(3653, 'Telabee', 'Tenofovir Alafenamide', 130.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p><strong>Telabee</strong> is a hepatitis B virus (HBV) nucleoside analog reverse transcriptase inhibitor. It is indicated for the treatment of chronic hepatitis B virus infection in adults with compensated liver disease.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Tenofovir alafenamide, the active ingredient in Telabee, is a prodrug of tenofovir, which is an analog of 2\'-deoxyadenosine monophosphate. It is absorbed by hepatocytes through passive diffusion and specific transporters (OATP1B1 and OATP1B3). Once inside the cells, it is converted to tenofovir and further phosphorylated to form the active metabolite, tenofovir diphosphate. This metabolite inhibits HBV replication by incorporating into viral DNA, causing DNA chain termination. Tenofovir diphosphate is a weak inhibitor of mammalian DNA polymerases, including mitochondrial DNA polymerase γ, but there is no evidence of mitochondrial toxicity in cell culture.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Before Initiation</strong>: Patients should be tested for HIV-1 infection before starting Telabee. It should not be used alone in patients with HIV-1 infection.</li><li><strong>Recommended Dosage for Adults</strong>:</li><li> The recommended dosage of Telabee is 25 mg (one tablet) taken orally once daily with food.</li><li><strong>In Patients with Renal Impairment</strong>:</li><li> No dosage adjustment is needed for patients with an estimated creatinine clearance ≥15 ml/min or those with ESRD undergoing chronic hemodialysis. For these patients, administer Telabee after completing hemodialysis. It is not recommended for patients with ESRD who are not on hemodialysis.</li><li><strong>In Patients with Hepatic Impairment</strong>:</li><li> No dosage adjustment is needed in patients with mild hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh A). It is not recommended for patients with decompensated hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh B or C).</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>:</li><li> The safety and effectiveness of Telabee in pediatric patients under 18 years of age have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>:</li><li> In clinical trials, Telabee was administered to patients aged 65 and older. No significant differences in safety or efficacy were observed between elderly patients and those aged 18-64.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Drugs Affecting Telabee</strong>: Telabee is a substrate of P-glycoprotein (P-gp) and BCRP. Drugs that induce P-gp activity can reduce Telabee absorption, leading to decreased effectiveness. Conversely, drugs that inhibit P-gp or BCRP may increase its absorption and plasma concentration.</li><li><strong>Drugs Affecting Renal Function</strong>: Telabee is primarily excreted by the kidneys. Co-administration with drugs that affect renal function or compete for tubular secretion may increase the concentration of Telabee and other renally eliminated drugs, raising the risk of adverse reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Stomach pain</li><li>Tiredness</li><li>Cough</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Back pain</li></ul><p>Serious side effects:</p><ul><li>Lactic acidosis/severe hepatomegaly with steatosis</li><li>Severe acute exacerbation of hepatitis B</li><li>New onset or worsening of renal impairment</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Studies suggest that the prevalence of birth defects following exposure to TAF-containing regimens during pregnancy is low (4.2% in the first trimester, 3.0% in later trimesters). However, risks should be weighed against benefits.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether Telabee or its metabolites are excreted in human breast milk, but tenofovir has been detected in the milk of lactating animals. The decision to use Telabee during breastfeeding should consider the mother’s clinical need and potential risks to the infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Severe Acute Exacerbation of Hepatitis B</strong>: Discontinuation of Telabee may lead to severe exacerbations of hepatitis B. Patients should be monitored after stopping the drug, and if necessary, anti-hepatitis B therapy should be resumed.</li><li><strong>Risk of HIV-1 Resistance in HBV and HIV-1 Coinfected Patients</strong>: Telabee should not be used alone to treat HIV-1 due to the risk of developing HIV-1 resistance. HIV testing is recommended for all HBV-infected patients before starting treatment with Telabee.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Patients on Telabee should be monitored for renal function (serum creatinine, urine glucose, urine protein). It should be discontinued if renal impairment or Fanconi syndrome develops.</li><li><strong>Lactic Acidosis/Severe Hepatomegaly with Steatosis</strong>: Lactic acidosis and severe hepatomegaly with steatosis, including fatal cases, have been reported. Treatment should be suspended if these conditions occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p> If overdose occurs, supportive measures should be provided. Telabee is removed by hemodialysis with an extraction coefficient of approximately 54%.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Hepatic viral infections (Hepatitis B)</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in the original container, protected from light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '778_1742457576.jpg', 63, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 12:59:36', '2025-03-20 12:59:36'),
(3654, 'Zaltrap', 'Aflibercept', 37201.00, 1, 'x 100 mg vial', '100 mg vial', 'IV Infusion', '100 mg per 4 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml</strong></p><p>Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml, in combination with 5-fluorouracil, leucovorin, irinotecan-(FOLFIRI), is indicated for patients with metastatic colorectal cancer (mCRC) that is resistant to or has progressed following an oxaliplatin-containing regimen.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Ablibercept is a recombinant fusion protein that acts as a decoy receptor for the ligands, vascular endothelial growth factor-A (VEGF-A) and placental growth factor (PIGF). It prevents these ligands from binding to endothelial receptors, VEGFR-1 and VEGFR-2, to suppress neovascularization and decrease vascular permeability. This ultimately will slow vision loss or the progression of metastatic colorectal cancer.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml</strong></p><p>4 mg/kg as an intravenous infusion over 1 hour every 2 weeks. Do not administer as an intravenous (IV) push or bolus.</p><p><strong>Administration of Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml</strong></p><p><em>Preparation for Administration:</em> Inspect vials visually prior to use. Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml is a clear, colorless to pale yellow solution. Do not use vial if the solution is discolored or cloudy or if the solution contains particles. Do not re-enter the vial after the initial puncture. Discard any unused portion left in the vial. Withdraw the prescribed dose of Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml and dilute in 0.9% sodium chloride solution, 5% dextrose solution for injection, to achieve a final concentration of 0.6–8 mg/mL. Use polyvinyl chloride (PVC) infusion bags containing bis (2-ethylhexyl) phthalate (DEHP) or polyolefin infusion bags. Store diluted Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml at 2°–8°C for up to 24 hours, or at controlled room temperature 20°–25°C for up to 8 hours. Discard any unused portion left in the infusion bag.</p><p><em>Administration:</em> Administer the diluted Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml solution as an intravenous infusion over 1 hour through a 0.2-micron polyethersulfone filter. Do not use filters made of polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) or nylon. Do not administer as an intravenous (IV) push or bolus. Do not combine Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml with other drugs in the same infusion bag or intravenous line.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml</strong></p><p>No dedicated drug-drug interaction studies have been conducted for Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml. No clinically important pharmacokinetic drug-drug interactions were found between ziv-Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml and irinotecan/SN-38 or 5-FU, based on cross-study comparisons and population pharmacokinetic analyses.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml</strong></p><p>Most common adverse reactions (all grades, ≥20% incidence and at least 2% greater incidence for the Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml/FOLFIRI regimen) were leukopenia, diarrhea, neutropenia, proteinuria, AST increased, stomatitis, fatigue, thrombocytopenia, ALT increased, hypertension, weight decreased, decreased appetite, epistaxis, abdominal pain, dysphonia, serum creatinine increased, and headache.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies with Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml in pregnant women. Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml was embryotoxic and teratogenic in rabbits at exposure levels lower than human exposures at the recommended dose, with increased incidences of external, visceral, and skeletal fetal malformations. Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml is excreted in human milk. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk and because of the potential for serious adverse reactions in nursing infants from Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Adverse reactions, sometimes severe and life-threatening or fatal, have been seen in clinical trials with Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml, including:</p><ul><li><em>Fistula Formation</em>: Discontinue Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml if fistula occurs.</li><li><em>Hypertension</em>: Monitor blood pressure and treat hypertension. Temporarily suspend Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml if hypertension is not controlled. Discontinue Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml if hypertensive crisis develops.</li><li><em>Arterial Thromboembolic Events (ATE)</em> (e.g., transient ischemic attacks, cerebrovascular accident, angina pectoris): Discontinue Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml if ATE develops.</li><li><em>Proteinuria</em>: Monitor urine protein. Suspend Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml when proteinuria ≥ 2 grams per 24 hours. Discontinue Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml if nephrotic syndrome or thrombotic microangiopathy (TMA) develops.</li><li><em>Neutropenia and Neutropenic Complications</em>: Delay administration of Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml/FOLFIRI until neutrophil count is ≥ 1.5 × 10^9/L.</li><li><em>Diarrhea and Dehydration</em>: Incidence of severe diarrhea and dehydration is increased. Monitor elderly patients more closely.</li><li><em>Reversible Posterior Leukoencephalopathy Syndrome (RPLS)</em>: Discontinue Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml</strong></p><p>There have been no cases of overdose reported with Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml. There is no information on the safety of Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml given at doses exceeding 7 mg per kg every 2 weeks or 9 mg per kg every 3 weeks.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml vials in a refrigerator at 2 to 8° C. Keep the vials in the original outer carton to protect from light.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><em>Pediatric Use</em>: The safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established. In a dose-escalation, safety, and tolerability study, 21 patients ages 2 to 21 years (median age 12.9) with solid tumors received Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml at doses ranging from 2 to 3 mg/kg, IV, every two weeks. The pharmacokinetics of free ziv-Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml were evaluated in 8 of these patients (ages 5 to 17 years). The maximum tolerated dose in the study was 2.5 mg/kg, below the dose known to be safe and effective in adults with mCRC.</li><li><em>Geriatric Use</em>: Of the 611 patients with mCRC, patients treated with Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml/FOLFIRI, 205 (34%) were 65 years or older, and 33 (5%) were 75 years or older. Elderly patients (≥65 years of age) experienced higher incidences (≥5%) of diarrhea, dizziness, asthenia, weight decrease, and dehydration when compared to younger patients. Monitor elderly patients more closely for diarrhea and dehydration. The effect of Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml on overall survival was similar in patients &lt;65 years old and ≥65 years old who received Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml/FOLFIRI. No dose adjustment of Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml is recommended for patients greater than or equal to 65 years of age.</li><li><em>Hepatic Impairment</em>: No dedicated clinical studies have been conducted to evaluate the effect of hepatic impairment on the pharmacokinetics of ziv-Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml. Based on a population PK analysis with data from 1507 patients, ziv-Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml exposure in patients with mild and moderate hepatic impairment were similar to those in patients with normal hepatic function. There are no data available for patients with severe hepatic impairment.</li><li><em>Renal Impairment</em>: No dedicated clinical studies have been conducted to evaluate the effect of renal impairment on the pharmacokinetics of ziv-Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml. Based on a population PK analysis with data from 1507 patients, ziv-Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml exposure in patients with mild, moderate, and severe renal impairment were similar to those in patients with normal renal function.</li><li><em>Females and Males of Reproductive Potential</em>: Male and female reproductive function and fertility may be compromised during treatment with Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml, as suggested by findings in monkeys. These animal findings were reversible within 18 weeks after cessation of treatment. Females and males of reproductive potential should use highly effective contraception during and up to a minimum of 3 months after the last dose of treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Ablibercept is a recombinant fusion protein that acts as a decoy receptor for the ligands, vascular endothelial growth factor-A (VEGF-A) and placental growth factor (PIGF). It prevents these ligands from binding to endothelial receptors, VEGFR-1 and VEGFR-2, to suppress neovascularization and decrease vascular permeability. This ultimately will slow vision loss or the progression of metastatic colorectal cancer.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Pregnancy category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies with Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml in pregnant women. Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml was embryotoxic and teratogenic in rabbits at exposure levels lower than human exposures at the recommended dose, with increased incidences of external, visceral, and skeletal fetal malformations. Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml is excreted in human milk. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk and because of the potential for serious adverse reactions in nursing infants from Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>The safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established. In a dose-escalation, safety, and tolerability study, 21 patients ages 2 to 21 years (median age 12.9) with solid tumors received Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml at doses ranging from 2 to 3 mg/kg, IV, every two weeks. The pharmacokinetics of free ziv-Zaltrap 100 mg/4 ml were evaluated in 8 of these patients (ages 5 to 17 years). The maximum tolerated dose in the study was 2.5 mg/kg, below the dose known to be safe and effective in adults with mCRC.</p>', 'active', '733_1742457770.jpg', 45, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 13:02:50', '2025-05-05 22:15:36'),
(3655, 'Salflu', 'Salmeterol + Fluticasone Propionate', 12.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50 mcg+250 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications:</strong></h3><ul><li>Used for regular treatment of asthma in patients not adequately controlled with inhaled corticosteroids and \"as needed\" inhaled short-acting β2-agonists, or for patients already controlled on both inhaled corticosteroids and long-acting β2-agonists.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol</strong> is a long-acting beta-2 agonist that protects against symptoms of asthma and COPD.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong> is a glucocorticoid that reduces inflammation in the lungs, improving lung function and preventing asthma exacerbations.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage:</strong></h3><ul><li>Available in inhaler and inhalation capsule forms for asthma and COPD. Dosing varies based on the age of the patient and the severity of the condition.</li><li>Inhaler and inhalation capsules are generally used twice daily (morning and evening).</li></ul><h3><strong>Administration:</strong></h3><ul><li>Patients should follow specific instructions for inhaler use to ensure effective delivery of the medication. Regular cleaning of the inhaler is also recommended.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol</strong>: Tremor, headache, palpitations, arrhythmias, and hypersensitivity reactions.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: Hoarseness, oral thrush, skin hypersensitivity, and rare cases of oropharyngeal edema.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions:</strong></h3><ul><li>Not to be used in patients during an asthma exacerbation or for acute symptom relief.</li><li>Paradoxical bronchospasm can occur.</li><li>Use with caution in COPD patients due to a potential increase in pneumonia risk.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation:</strong></h3><ul><li>Use should be considered only if the benefits outweigh the risks, as there is limited data on the safety of these drugs during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage:</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from sunlight and heat, and out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1054_1742457843.webp', 5, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 13:04:03', '2025-03-20 13:04:03'),
(3656, 'Zoana', 'Nitazoxanide', 35.24, 1, 'x 32 ml bottle', '32 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '100 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you provided in a clearer, more standard format with headings in normal bold text:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Zoana 100 mg/5 ml is indicated for the treatment of diarrhea caused by <em>Cryptosporidium parvum</em>, <em>Giardia lamblia</em>, and <em>Entamoeba histolytica</em>.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Anti-diarrhoeal Antiprotozoal</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zoana 100 mg/5 ml is a synthetic antiprotozoal agent for oral administration. The antiprotozoal activity of Zoana 100 mg/5 ml is believed to interfere with the Pyruvate Ferredoxin Oxido Reductase (PFOR) enzyme-dependent electron transfer reaction. This reaction is essential for anaerobic energy metabolism of the protozoa. Zoana 100 mg/5 ml and its metabolites, tizoxanid, are active in vitro in inhibiting the growth of sporozoites and oocysts of <em>Cryptosporidium parvum</em> and trophozoites of <em>Giardia lamblia</em>.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Age 1-3 years</strong>: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) suspension every 12 hours for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Age 4-11 years</strong>: 2 teaspoonfuls (10 ml) suspension every 12 hours for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Age 12 years or above</strong>: 5 teaspoonfuls (25 ml) suspension or 1 tablet every 12 hours for 3 days.</li><li>It is recommended to administer with food.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>Zoana 100 mg/5 ml is highly bound to plasma proteins. Therefore, caution should be exercised when administering Zoana 100 mg/5 ml concurrently with other highly plasma protein-bound drugs with a narrow therapeutic index.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Zoana 100 mg/5 ml is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Zoana 100 mg/5 ml or any components of the preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>The most frequent side effects reported with Zoana 100 mg/5 ml include abdominal pain, vomiting, and headache. These side effects are typically mild and transient. Very rare side effects may include nausea, anorexia, flatulence, increased appetite, enlarged salivary glands, increased creatinine &amp; SGPT levels, pruritus, rhinitis, sweating, dizziness, and discolored urine.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Zoana 100 mg/5 ml is classified under the US FDA pregnancy category B. However, there are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Since animal reproduction studies may not always predict human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Zoana 100 mg/5 ml has been shown to be excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administering Zoana 100 mg/5 ml during lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Zoana 100 mg/5 ml must be administered with caution to patients with hepatic &amp; biliary disease and to those with renal disease.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Anti-diarrhoeal Antiprotozoal</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Zoana 100 mg/5 ml is a synthetic antiprotozoal agent for oral administration. Its antiprotozoal activity is believed to involve interference with the Pyruvate Ferredoxin Oxido Reductase (PFOR) enzyme-dependent electron transfer reaction, which is essential for anaerobic energy metabolism of protozoa. Zoana 100 mg/5 ml and its metabolite, tizoxanid, are active in vitro against the growth of sporozoites and oocysts of <em>Cryptosporidium parvum</em> and trophozoites of <em>Giardia lamblia</em>.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Zoana 100 mg/5 ml is classified as US FDA pregnancy category B. Although animal studies do not always predict human outcomes, it should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. The drug is excreted in human milk, so caution is recommended when used during lactation.</p><p>Let me know if you need any further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '578_1742457925.jpg', 43, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 13:05:25', '2025-05-05 22:36:48'),
(3657, 'Protide', 'Salmeterol + Fluticasone Propionate', 12.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50 mcg+250 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications:</h3><ul><li><strong>Protide</strong> is indicated for asthma treatment, particularly when combining a long-acting β2-agonist and inhaled corticosteroid is suitable.</li><li>It\'s used in patients whose asthma is not adequately controlled with inhaled corticosteroids and short-acting β2-agonists or those already controlled on both.</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology:</h3><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol</strong> works as a long-acting beta-2 agonist (12-hour duration) that helps with bronchodilation.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong> is a potent anti-inflammatory corticosteroid that helps reduce asthma symptoms and prevents flare-ups by acting locally in the lungs.</li></ul><h3>Dosage:</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (12+ years)</strong>: Two puffs twice daily, with varying strengths of Salmeterol (25 µg) and Fluticasone Propionate (50 µg, 125 µg, or 250 µg).</li><li><strong>Children (4-12 years)</strong>: Two puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily.</li><li><strong>Inhalation capsule</strong>: Varying strengths depending on severity, with maximum doses up to <strong>500 µg</strong> of Fluticasone Propionate.</li><li><strong>Maxhaler</strong>: Easy-to-use inhaler for patients 12 years and older, with doses of <strong>100 µg, 250 µg, and 500 µg</strong> twice daily.</li></ul><h3>Administration:</h3><ul><li>The inhaler should be used correctly, as improper usage can reduce the effectiveness of the medication. Regular cleaning of the inhaler is also recommended.</li></ul><h3>Interactions and Contraindications:</h3><ul><li><strong>Avoid non-selective beta-blockers</strong> as they can worsen asthma.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., ketoconazole) may increase the levels of Fluticasone Propionate.</li><li>Not recommended for acute asthma episodes, and it should not be used during an asthma exacerbation.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects:</h3><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol</strong>: Tremors, palpitations, headaches, cardiac arrhythmias, hypersensitivity reactions, muscle cramps.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: Hoarseness, mouth thrush, facial swelling, and rare instances of skin reactions.</li></ul><h3>Precautions:</h3><ul><li>The medication should not be started during an acute asthma exacerbation.</li><li>Monitoring for <strong>pneumonia</strong> in patients with COPD.</li><li>Long-term use may have side effects like <strong>growth reduction</strong> in children and <strong>bone mineral density</strong> loss.</li></ul><h3>Overdose:</h3><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol overdose</strong> may lead to symptoms like arrhythmias, seizures, and muscle cramps.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate overdose</strong> might cause temporary adrenal suppression, requiring no immediate emergency treatment but monitoring may be needed for long-term overdoses.</li></ul><h3>Storage:</h3><ul><li>The inhaler should be stored below <strong>30°C</strong> and away from direct sunlight or heat, and it should not be punctured or broken even when empty.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</h3><ul><li>Use during pregnancy or breastfeeding should only be considered if the benefits outweigh the risks, as there is insufficient data on its effects.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '683_1742458042.webp', 8, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 13:07:22', '2025-03-20 13:07:22'),
(3658, 'Vita-1', 'Thiamine Hydrochloride', 0.73, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Vita-1 100 mg</h3><p>Thiamine is specifically used in the treatment of various manifestations of thiamine deficiency, such as <strong>Beriberi</strong> and <strong>Wernicke\'s encephalopathy</strong>, <strong>neuritis associated with pregnancy</strong>, and <strong>pellagra</strong>. Supplementary thiamine may be indicated prophylactically in conditions where there is low dietary intake, impaired gastrointestinal absorption (e.g., alcohol), or where requirements are increased (e.g., pregnancy, carbohydrate-rich diet).</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Vitamin-B preparations</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Thiamine, in the form of <strong>thiamine pyrophosphate</strong>, acts as the coenzyme for the decarboxylation of α-ketoglutaric acid. A deficiency in thiamine affects the peripheral nervous system, the gastrointestinal tract, and the cardiovascular system. Thiamine is crucial for the optimal growth of infants and children.</p><ul><li><strong>Thiamine</strong> is not stored in the body and is lost during periods of deficiency.</li><li>An adequate amount of thiamine is required every day for normal health.</li><li><strong>Thiamine deficiency</strong> leads to symptoms such as fatigue, anorexia, gastrointestinal disturbances, tachycardia, irritability, and neurological issues.</li><li><strong>Beriberi</strong>, a disease caused by vitamin B1 deficiency, is common in alcoholics, pregnant women with inadequate diets, and people with malabsorption syndrome, prolonged diarrhea, and hepatic disease.</li></ul><p>Thiamine is well absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and widely distributed in the body. It is rapidly absorbed from the upper small intestine and not stored significantly in tissues. Excess thiamine appears in urine either as intact thiamine or as pyrimidine, which is a product of thiamine degradation. The plasma half-life of thiamine is approximately 24 hours.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Vita-1 100 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis</strong>: 3 to 10 mg daily</li><li><strong>Mild chronic deficiency</strong>: 10 to 25 mg daily</li><li><strong>Severe deficiency</strong>: 200 to 300 mg daily</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Vita-1 100 mg</h3><p>No hazardous drug interactions have been reported. Vitamin B1 acts synergistically with other B-complex vitamins, and its potential for causing adverse effects is reduced when used with other vitamins in the B group.</p><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>There are no absolute contraindications for thiamine, but the risk of <strong>anaphylaxis</strong> increases with repeated parenteral administration.</li><li><strong>Mild allergic reactions</strong> such as sneezing or mild asthma should be considered warning signs. Further administration should be done with caution, starting at a much lower dose.</li><li>Thiamine injection should not be given intravenously except in comatose patients.</li><li>Once thiamine deficiency is corrected, parenteral administration or excess doses are unnecessary.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Vita-1 100 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Oral administration</strong>: Vitamin B1 does not cause adverse effects.</li><li><strong>Parenteral administration</strong>: Rare cases of anaphylactic reactions have been reported, especially after intravenous administration of large doses (400 mg), particularly in sensitive patients such as children.</li><li>Mild soreness may occur at the site of intramuscular injection.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><p>The drug is <strong>safe</strong> for use in neonates, children, pregnant women, lactating women, and elderly patients.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Thiamine injection should be protected from <strong>light</strong> and <strong>moisture</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li>Vitamin-B preparations</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Thiamine, as thiamine pyrophosphate, acts as a coenzyme for the decarboxylation of α-ketoglutaric acid. Thiamine deficiency affects the peripheral nervous system, gastrointestinal tract, and cardiovascular system. It is essential for the growth of infants and children, and its deficiency can lead to symptoms such as fatigue, anorexia, gastrointestinal issues, tachycardia, irritability, and neurological symptoms.</p><p>Thiamine is well absorbed and rapidly distributed in the body, with a plasma half-life of 24 hours.</p>', 'active', '889_1742458130.webp', 56, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 13:08:50', '2025-03-20 13:08:50');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3659, 'Vomidyl', 'Ondansetron', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '8 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vomidyl 8 mg</strong></p><p>Vomidyl 8 mg is a serotonin subtype 3 (5-HT3) receptor antagonist indicated for the following uses:</p><ul><li>Prevention of nausea and vomiting associated with initial and repeat courses of emetogenic cancer chemotherapy.</li><li>Prevention and treatment of post-operative nausea and vomiting.</li><li>Prevention of radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Anti-emetic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong> Vomidyl 8 mg is a selective 5-HT3 receptor antagonist. Its mechanism of action is not fully understood, but it is known to block serotonin receptors of the 5-HT3 type, which are present both peripherally on vagal nerve terminals and centrally in the chemoreceptor trigger zone of the area postrema. Vomidyl 8 mg is not a dopamine-receptor antagonist. Chemotherapy-induced nausea is believed to be associated with the release of serotonin from enterochromaffin cells in the small intestine, although the exact mechanisms of action remain unclear.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Vomidyl 8 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prevention of chemotherapy-induced nausea &amp; vomiting (CINV):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Adult (Tablet and Oral Solution):</strong>Highly emetogenic chemotherapy: 24 mg (three 8 mg tablets) given 30 minutes before chemotherapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Moderately emetogenic chemotherapy: 8 mg (one tablet or 10 ml oral solution) twice daily, for 1-2 days after chemotherapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric (4 to 11 years):</strong>4 mg (one tablet or 5 ml solution) three times daily for 1-2 days after chemotherapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection (Adults):</strong>Single 32 mg dose or three 0.15 mg/kg doses infused over 15 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppository (Adults):</strong>16 mg suppository 1-2 hours before chemotherapy; continue for up to 5 days post-treatment.</li><li><strong>Radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adult:</strong> 8 mg orally, 1 to 2 hours before radiotherapy, followed by 8 mg every 8 hours for up to 5 days post-treatment.</li><li><strong>Post-operative nausea and vomiting:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adult (Tablet and Oral Solution):</strong> 16 mg (two 8 mg tablets or 20 ml solution) 1 hour before anesthesia.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection (Adults):</strong> 4 mg intravenously or intramuscularly, given immediately before induction of anesthesia or postoperatively.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration Instructions for Oral Soluble Film:</strong></p><ol><li>Tear the pouch carefully along the edge tear mark.</li><li>Place the Vomidyl 8 mg film on top of your tongue. It will dissolve within 20 seconds.</li><li>Do not chew or swallow the film whole.</li><li>Swallow after the film has dissolved. You may swallow with or without liquid.</li><li>Wash your hands after use.</li></ol><p><strong>Interaction of Vomidyl 8 mg</strong> Vomidyl 8 mg does not appear to induce or inhibit cytochrome P-450 drug-metabolizing enzymes. However, inducers or inhibitors of these enzymes may alter the clearance of Vomidyl 8 mg. No dosage adjustments are necessary based on enzyme interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to the drug or any of its components.</li><li>Concomitant use with apomorphine.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Vomidyl 8 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common side effects:</strong> Headache, constipation, diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Less common:</strong> Rash, sensation of flushing or warmth, hiccups, liver enzyme abnormalities.</li><li><strong>Serious side effects (rare):</strong> Anaphylaxis, bronchospasm, tachycardia, angina, hypokalemia, shortness of breath.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy category B.</strong> No evidence of harm to the fetus in animal studies, but limited human data available. Use during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Vomidyl 8 mg is excreted in animal milk, so caution is advised when administering it to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity reactions have been reported in patients with previous hypersensitivity to 5-HT3 receptor antagonists.</li><li>Not a substitute for nasogastric suction in patients with abdominal surgery.</li><li>Caution is advised in chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting patients, as Vomidyl 8 mg may mask ileus or gastric distension.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Vomidyl 8 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No specific antidote for overdose.</li><li>Symptoms may include hypotension and faintness. All adverse effects resolved completely in reported cases.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C in a dry place. Protect from light and moisture.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric:</strong> Safe for children aged 1 month and older.</li><li><strong>Geriatric:</strong> No dosage adjustment required.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Dose adjustment is recommended in patients with severe hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> No adjustment needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution for IV Infusion:</strong></p><ul><li>Dilute Vomidyl 8 mg in 50 ml dextrose 5% or normal saline before intravenous infusion.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Anti-emetic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong> Vomidyl 8 mg is a selective 5-HT3 receptor antagonist that blocks serotonin receptors, which are involved in the vomiting reflex triggered by chemotherapy, radiotherapy, and surgery.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Carcinogenicity</strong></p><ul><li>No carcinogenic effects seen in long-term studies in rats and mice.</li><li>No evidence of impaired fertility or harm to the fetus in animal studies.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses &amp; Dosage Adjustments</strong></p><ul><li>Pediatric use: Dosage is the same for children over 4 years old, with limited data available for younger children.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Maximum recommended dose is 8 mg for patients with severe hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '481_1742458265.jpg', 12, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 13:11:05', '2025-03-20 13:11:05'),
(3660, 'Zoana', 'Nitazoxanide', 10.07, 4, 'tablet', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zoana 500 mg</strong></p><p> Zoana 500 mg is indicated for the treatment of diarrhea caused by <em>Cryptosporidium parvum</em>, <em>Giardia lamblia</em>, and <em>Entamoeba histolytica</em>.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-diarrhoeal Antiprotozoal</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Zoana 500 mg is a synthetic antiprotozoal agent for oral administration. The antiprotozoal activity of Zoana 500 mg is believed to be interference with the Pyruvate Ferredoxin Oxido Reductase (PFOR) enzyme-dependent electron transfer reaction. This reaction is essential for anaerobic energy metabolism of the protozoa. Zoana 500 mg and its metabolites, tizoxanid, are active in vitro in inhibiting the growth of sporozoites and oocyst of <em>Cryptosporidium parvum</em> and trophozoites of <em>Giardia lamblia</em>.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zoana 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Age 1-3 years</strong>: 1 teaspoonful or 5 ml suspension every 12 hours for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Age 4-11 years</strong>: 2 teaspoonfuls or 10 ml suspension every 12 hours for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Age 12 years or above</strong>: 5 teaspoonfuls (25 ml) suspension or 1 tablet every 12 hours for 3 days.</li><li> It is recommended to be administered with food.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zoana 500 mg</strong></p><p> It is highly bound to plasma protein. Therefore, caution should be exercised when administering Zoana 500 mg concurrently with other highly plasma protein-bound drugs with a narrow therapeutic index.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Zoana 500 mg or any components of the preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zoana 500 mg</strong></p><p> The most frequent side effects reported by Zoana 500 mg are abdominal pain, vomiting, and headache. These side effects are typically mild and transient in nature. Very rare side effects include nausea, anorexia, flatulence, increased appetite, enlarged salivary glands, increased creatinine &amp; SGPT level, pruritus, rhinitis, sweating, dizziness, discolored urine, etc.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> US FDA pregnancy category of Zoana 500 mg is B. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Zoana 500 mg has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Zoana 500 mg is administered during lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Zoana 500 mg must be administered with caution to patients with hepatic &amp; biliary disease and to patients with renal disease.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Anti-diarrhoeal Antiprotozoal</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Zoana 500 mg is a synthetic antiprotozoal agent for oral administration. The antiprotozoal activity of Zoana 500 mg is believed to be interference with the Pyruvate Ferredoxin Oxido Reductase (PFOR) enzyme-dependent electron transfer reaction. This reaction is essential for anaerobic energy metabolism of the protozoa. Zoana 500 mg and its metabolites, tizoxanid, are active in vitro in inhibiting the growth of sporozoites and oocyst of <em>Cryptosporidium parvum</em> and trophozoites of <em>Giardia lamblia</em>.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> US FDA pregnancy category of Zoana 500 mg is B. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Zoana 500 mg has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Zoana 500 mg is administered during lactation.</p>', 'active', '1147_1742458389.jpg', 43, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 13:13:09', '2025-03-20 13:13:09'),
(3661, 'Zedoxim', 'Cefpodoxime Proxetil', 95.00, 1, 'x 50 ml bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '40 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zedoxim 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by susceptible microorganisms, listed below:</p><ul><li><strong>Acute otitis media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>.</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia</strong> caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em> or <em>H. influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em>, <em>H. influenzae</em> (non-beta-lactamase-producing strains only), or <em>M. catarrhalis</em>.</li><li><strong>Skin and skin structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, or <em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong> caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Rectal gonococcal infections in women</strong> due to <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Third-generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is an oral 3rd generation cephalosporin, which has good stability to beta-lactamases and activity against Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. It is indicated for the treatment of infections either before the infecting organism has been identified. It is a prodrug, and its active metabolite is Cefpodoxime. Approximately 29 to 33% of Cefpodoxime is excreted unchanged in the urine within 12 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zedoxim 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Adults and Adolescents (13 years and older)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis:</strong> 100 mg every 12 hours, for 5 to 10 days</li><li><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 200 mg every 12 hours, for 10 days</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia:</strong> 200 mg every 12 hours, for 14 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbations of chronic bronchitis:</strong> 200 mg every 12 hours, for 10 days</li><li><strong>Skin and skin structure infections:</strong> 400 mg every 12 hours, for 7 to 14 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infection:</strong> 100 mg every 12 hours, for 7 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea:</strong> Single dose of 200 mg</li><li><strong>Rectal gonococcal infections in women:</strong> Single dose of 200 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Infants and Pediatric Patients (2 months to 12 years)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Acute otitis media:</strong> 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours, for 5 days</li><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis:</strong> 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours, for 5 to 10 days</li><li><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours, for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Zedoxim 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant administration of high doses of <strong>antacids</strong> (sodium bicarbonate and aluminum hydroxide) or <strong>H2 blockers</strong> reduces peak plasma levels and the extent of absorption, respectively.</li><li><strong>Renal excretion</strong> of Cefpodoxime is inhibited by <strong>probenecid</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is contraindicated in patients with known <strong>allergy</strong> to <strong>cephalosporins</strong>.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zedoxim 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime has very few side effects. Possible side effects include:</p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal disorders (such as diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain)</li><li>Rash</li><li>Urticaria</li><li>Itching</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>US FDA pregnancy category of Cefpodoxime is B.</li><li>There is no adequate and well-controlled study in pregnant women. This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Cefpodoxime has been shown to be excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Cefpodoxime is administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In patients with transient or persistent reduction in urinary output due to renal insufficiency, the total daily dose of Cefpodoxime should be reduced.</li><li>Cefpodoxime should be administered with caution to patients receiving concurrent treatment with potent diuretics.</li><li>As with other antibiotics, prolonged use of Cefpodoxime may result in overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min): The dosing intervals should be increased to 24 hours.</li><li>No dosage adjustment is required in cases of <strong>hepatic impairment</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ol><li>Shake the bottle well to loosen the powder.</li><li>Add boiled and cooled water in the bottle.</li><li>Shake until powder is completely mixed with water.</li></ol><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Third-generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is an oral 3rd generation cephalosporin with stability to beta-lactamases and activity against Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. It is a prodrug whose active metabolite is Cefpodoxime. About 29 to 33% of Cefpodoxime is excreted unchanged in the urine within 12 hours.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>US FDA pregnancy category of Cefpodoxime is B.</li><li>Cefpodoxime should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed and with caution while breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>In patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min), the dosing intervals should be increased to 24 hours.</li><li>No dosage adjustment is required in cases of hepatic impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '548_1742458513.jpg', 64, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 13:15:13', '2025-05-07 20:22:02'),
(3662, 'Xynocard SR', 'Nitroglycerin', 3.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.6 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xynocard SR 2.6 mg</strong></p><p> Xynocard SR 2.6 mg sublingual tablet is indicated for the acute relief of an attack or acute prophylaxis of angina pectoris due to coronary artery disease.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Nitrates: Coronary vasodilators</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Xynocard SR 2.6 mg forms free radical nitric oxide (NO) which activates guanylate cyclase, resulting in an increase of guanosine 3\'5\' monophosphate in smooth muscle and other tissues. These events lead to dephosphorylation of myosin light chains, which regulate the contractile state in smooth muscle and result in vasodilatation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xynocard SR 2.6 mg</strong></p><p> One sublingual tablet should be dissolved under the tongue at the first sign of an acute anginal attack. The dose may be repeated approximately every five minutes until relief is obtained. If the pain persists after a total of 3 tablets in a 15-minute period, prompt medical attention is recommended.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Xynocard SR 2.6 mg</strong></p><p> Patients receiving antihypertensive drugs, beta-adrenergic blockers or phenothiazines, and nitrates should be observed for possible additive hypotensive effects. Marked orthostatic hypotension has been reported when calcium channel blockers and organic nitrates were used concomitantly. Concomitant use of nitrates and alcohol may cause hypotension. The vasodilatory and hemodynamic effects of Xynocard SR 2.6 mg may be enhanced by concomitant administration of aspirin. Patients receiving sublingual Xynocard SR 2.6 mg should avoid ergotamine and related drugs or be monitored for symptoms of ergotism if this is not possible.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Sublingual Xynocard SR 2.6 mg therapy is contraindicated in patients with early myocardial infarction, severe anemia, increased intracranial pressure, and those with a known hypersensitivity to Xynocard SR 2.6 mg. Administration of Xynocard SR 2.6 mg is contraindicated in patients who are using sildenafil citrate since sildenafil citrate has been shown to potentiate the hypotensive effects of organic nitrates.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xynocard SR 2.6 mg</strong></p><p> Headache, which may be severe and persistent, may occur immediately after use. Vertigo, dizziness, weakness, palpitation, and other manifestations of postural hypotension may develop occasionally.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Xynocard SR 2.6 mg should be given to a pregnant woman only if clearly needed. It is not known whether Xynocard SR 2.6 mg is excreted in human milk.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Only the smallest dose required for effective control of the acute anginal attack should be used. Excessive use may lead to the development of tolerance. This drug should be used with caution in patients who may be volume-depleted or are already hypotensive.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place away from light and heat. Keep all medicines out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1730_1742458626.jpg', 59, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 13:17:06', '2025-03-20 13:17:06'),
(3663, 'Xelevo', 'Levofloxacin Hemihydrate', 15.00, 20, 'tablet', '20 tablet in a box', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xelevo 500 mg</strong></p><p>Xelevo 500 mg is indicated for the treatment of respiratory tract infections, including acute bacterial sinusitis, acute exacerbations of chronic bronchitis, community-acquired pneumonia, and nosocomial pneumonia. It is also used in the treatment of uncomplicated and complicated skin and soft tissue infections, chronic bacterial prostatitis, and uncomplicated and complicated urinary tract infections (mild to moderate). It is indicated for the treatment of adults (18 years of age).</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>4-Quinolone preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Xelevo 500 mg is a synthetic, broad-spectrum, third-generation fluoroquinolone antibiotic. Chemically, Xelevo 500 mg is a chiral fluorinated carboxyquinolone. Xelevo 500 mg exerts antibacterial action by inhibiting bacterial topoisomerase IV and DNA gyrase, the enzymes required for DNA replication, transcription repair, and recombination. It has in vitro activity against a wide range of Gram-negative and Gram-positive microorganisms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xelevo 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Acute sinusitis</strong>: 500 mg daily for 10-14 days.</li><li><strong>Acute exacerbations of chronic bronchitis</strong>: 250-500 mg daily for 7-10 days.</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia</strong>: 500 mg once or twice daily for 7-14 days.</li><li><strong>Nosocomial pneumonia</strong>: 750 mg once daily for 7-14 days.</li><li><strong>Skin &amp; soft tissue infections</strong>: 500 mg once or twice daily for 7-14 days.</li><li><strong>Chronic prostatitis</strong>: 500 mg once daily for 28 days.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: 250 mg daily for 7-10 days (for 3 days in uncomplicated infections).</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xelevo 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Antacids, iron, and adsorbents</strong> reduce the absorption of Xelevo 500 mg.</li><li><strong>NSAIDs</strong> may increase the risk of CNS stimulation.</li><li><strong>Warfarin</strong> may increase the risk of bleeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Xelevo 500 mg is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Xelevo 500 mg, other quinolone antimicrobial agents, or any other components of this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xelevo 500 mg</strong></p><p>Xelevo 500 mg is generally well tolerated. However, a few side effects may occur. Common side effects include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, flatulence, and rarely phototoxicity (0.1%). Side effects that may be seen very rarely include tremors, depression, anxiety, and confusion.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Xelevo 500 mg is classified as a <strong>US FDA pregnancy category C</strong>. Therefore, Xelevo 500 mg should be avoided during pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Adequate water intake should be maintained during the treatment period to avoid concentrated urine.</li><li>Dose adjustment is required for patients with impaired renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1198_1742458846.jpg', 61, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 13:20:46', '2025-05-05 18:05:03'),
(3664, 'Zolibac', 'Cefazolin', 125.00, 1, 'x 1 gm vial', '1 gm vial', 'IV Injection', '500 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zolibac 500 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Zolibac 500 mg/5 ml is indicated in the treatment of the following serious infections due to susceptible organisms:</p><ul><li>Respiratory Tract Infections</li><li>Urinary Tract Infections</li><li>Skin and Skin Structure Infections</li><li>Biliary Tract Infections</li><li>Bone and Joint Infections</li><li>Genital Infections</li><li>Septicemia</li><li>Endocarditis</li><li>Perioperative Prophylaxis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>First generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zolibac 500 mg/5 ml is a 1st generation broad spectrum parenteral Cephalosporin antibiotic. It interferes with the synthesis of bacterial cell wall by inhibiting the transpeptidase enzyme. As a result, the bacterial cell wall is weakened, the cell swells, and then ruptures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zolibac 500 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Adult Dose</strong></p><ul><li>Moderate to severe infection: 500 mg to 1 gm every 6 to 8 hours</li><li>Mild infections caused by susceptible Gram-positive cocci: 250 mg to 500 mg every 8 hours</li><li>Acute uncomplicated urinary tract infections: 1 gm every 12 hours</li><li>Pneumococcal pneumonia: 500 mg every 12 hours</li><li>Severe life-threatening infections (e.g., Endocarditis, Septicemia): 1 gm to 1.5 gm every 6 hours. In rare instances, doses up to 12 gm per day can be used</li><li>Perioperative Prophylactic Use:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">To prevent postoperative infection in contaminated or potentially contaminated surgery, recommended doses are: 1 gm IM or IV administered half an hour to 1 hour prior to the start of surgery.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For lengthy operative procedures (e.g., 2 hours or more), 500 mg to 1 gm IM or IV during surgery.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">500 mg to 1 gm IM or IV every 6 to 8 hours for 24 hours postoperatively.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In surgeries where the occurrence of infection may be particularly devastating (e.g., open-heart surgery and prosthetic arthroplasty), prophylactic administration may be continued for 3 to 5 days following surgery.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zolibac 500 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Injection site reactions (pain, swelling, skin rash, or a hard lump)</li><li>Diarrhea, stomach pain, stomach cramps</li><li>Nausea, vomiting, loss of appetite</li><li>Skin rash or itching, hives</li><li>White patches or sores inside the mouth or on the lips</li><li>Vaginal itching or discharge</li><li>Heartburn, gas, rectal itching</li><li>Confusion, weakness, hypotension</li><li>Drowsiness, headache, and allergic reactions</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Zolibac 500 mg/5 ml is contraindicated in patients with known allergy to the Cephalosporin group of Antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zolibac 500 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Injection site reactions (pain, swelling, skin rash, or a hard lump)</li><li>Diarrhea, stomach pain, stomach cramps</li><li>Nausea, vomiting, loss of appetite</li><li>Skin rash or itching, hives</li><li>White patches or sores inside the mouth or on the lips</li><li>Vaginal itching or discharge</li><li>Heartburn, gas, rectal itching</li><li>Confusion, weakness, hypotension</li><li>Drowsiness, headache, and allergic reactions</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Category B. Zolibac 500 mg/5 ml is present in very low concentrations in the milk of nursing mothers. Caution should be exercised when Zolibac 500 mg/5 ml is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Injection site reactions (pain, swelling, skin rash, or a hard lump)</li><li>Diarrhea, stomach pain, stomach cramps</li><li>Nausea, vomiting, loss of appetite</li><li>Skin rash or itching, hives</li><li>White patches or sores inside the mouth or on the lips</li><li>Vaginal itching or discharge</li><li>Heartburn, gas, rectal itching</li><li>Confusion, weakness, hypotension</li><li>Drowsiness, headache, and allergic reactions</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>After reconstitution, Zolibac 500 mg/5 ml is stable for 24 hours at room temperature or for 10 days if stored under refrigeration (5°C or 41°F). Reconstituted solution may range in color from pale yellow to yellow without a change in potency.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Patients with Hepatic Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary in patients with hepatic insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Dose</strong>: In pediatric patients, a total daily dosage of 25 to 50 mg per kg of body weight, divided into 3 or 4 equal doses, is effective for most mild to moderate infections. Total daily dosage may be increased to 100 mg per kg of body weight for severe infections. Safety for use in premature infants and neonates has not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p>The content of one vial is to be dissolved in:</p><ul><li>2 ml (for 500 mg IM/IV Injection)</li><li>2.5 ml (for 1 gm IM/IV Injection) of Water for Injection.</li></ul>', 'active', '1802_1742459060.webp', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 13:24:20', '2025-05-05 22:38:11'),
(3665, 'Vomiset', 'Ondansetron', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '8 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vomiset 8 mg</strong></p><p>Vomiset 8 mg is a serotonin subtype 3 (5-HT3) receptor antagonist indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Prevention of nausea and vomiting</strong> associated with initial and repeat courses of emetogenic cancer chemotherapy.</li><li><strong>Prevention and treatment of post-operative nausea and vomiting (PONV)</strong>.</li><li><strong>Prevention of radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (RINV)</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Anti-emetic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong> Vomiset 8 mg is a selective 5-HT3 receptor antagonist. Its exact mechanism of action is not fully understood, but it acts to block serotonin receptors on vagal nerve terminals and in the chemoreceptor trigger zone of the area postrema. Cytotoxic chemotherapy is believed to trigger serotonin release in the small intestine, which then activates the vomiting reflex.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Vomiset 8 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Chemotherapy-Induced Nausea &amp; Vomiting (CINV)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>AdultsOral tablet/oral solution</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Highly emetogenic chemotherapy: 24 mg (three 8 mg tablets) given once.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Moderately emetogenic chemotherapy: 8 mg (one tablet) twice a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection</strong>: 32 mg as a single dose, infused over 15 minutes before chemotherapy. Followed by 0.15 mg/kg doses 4 and 8 hours after the first dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppository</strong>: 16 mg, 1-2 hours before treatment, then continued for up to 5 days.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patientsTablet/oral solution</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 4-11 years: 4 mg, 3 times a day for 1-2 days after chemotherapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection</strong>: 0.15 mg/kg up to a maximum of 16 mg per dose, infused intravenously.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppository</strong>: Not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Radiotherapy-Induced Nausea and Vomiting (RINV)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 8 mg orally 1-2 hours before radiotherapy. Then, 8 mg every 8 hours for up to 5 days post-treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Post-operative Nausea and Vomiting (PONV)</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>AdultsOral tablet/oral solution</strong>: 16 mg (two 8 mg tablets) 1 hour before induction of anesthesia.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection</strong>: 4 mg intravenously over 2-5 minutes before anesthesia or postoperatively.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppository</strong>: 16 mg 1-2 hours before treatment.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric patientsInjection</strong>: 4 mg for children &gt;40 kg, or 0.1 mg/kg for those ≤40 kg.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Vomiset 8 mg Oral Soluble Film</strong></p><ol><li>Tear the pouch carefully along the tear mark.</li><li>Place the film on your tongue. It will dissolve within 20 seconds.</li><li>Do not chew the film.</li><li>Swallow after it dissolves, with or without liquid.</li><li>Wash your hands after use.</li></ol><p><strong>Interaction of Vomiset 8 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Vomiset 8 mg does not induce or inhibit the cytochrome P-450 drug-metabolizing enzymes but is metabolized by these enzymes. No dosage adjustment is necessary for most drugs, but caution is advised when used with inducers or inhibitors of these enzymes.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Vomiset 8 mg or its components.</li><li>Concomitant use with apomorphine.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Vomiset 8 mg</strong> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Rash (in 1% of patients during chemotherapy)</li><li>Sensation of flushing or warmth</li><li>Hiccups</li><li>Liver enzyme abnormalities</li></ul><p>Serious but rare side effects:</p><ul><li>Anaphylaxis</li><li>Bronchospasm</li><li>Tachycardia</li><li>Angina</li><li>Hypokalemia</li><li>Shortness of breath</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: Animal studies showed no harm to the fetus, but there are no adequate studies in pregnant women. Use only if necessary.</li><li>Vomiset 8 mg is excreted in milk in animals; caution is advised when administering to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Not a gastric or intestinal peristalsis stimulant.</li><li>May mask progressive ileus or gastric distension in chemotherapy or postoperative patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Vomiset 8 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No specific antidote. Overdose may lead to hypotension and faintness, which resolve with appropriate care.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30ºC in a dry place, protected from light and moisture.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use</strong>: Safe for children 1 month and older.</li><li><strong>Geriatric use</strong>: No dosage adjustment required.</li><li><strong>Hepatic function</strong>: Limit the maximum daily dose to 8 mg for patients with severe hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal function</strong>: No dosage adjustment required.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong> Vomiset 8 mg is a potent 5-HT3 receptor antagonist, preventing nausea and vomiting associated with chemotherapy, radiotherapy, and surgery by blocking serotonin receptors in both the peripheral and central nervous systems.</p>', 'active', '12_1742459219.jpg', 50, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 13:26:59', '2025-03-20 13:26:59'),
(3666, 'Xeroder', 'Fluconazole', 200.60, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'IV Infusion', '200 mg/100 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xeroder 200 mg/100 ml</strong></p><p>Xeroder 200 mg/100 ml, a member of the triazole class of antifungal agents, is a potent and selective inhibitor of fungal enzymes necessary for the synthesis of ergosterol. It is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Vaginal candidiasis</strong> (acute or recurrent)</li><li><strong>Candidal balanitis</strong>: Treatment of partners who present with symptomatic genital candidiasis should be considered.</li><li><strong>Mucosal candidiasis</strong>: Includes oropharyngeal, oesophageal, non-invasive bronchopulmonary infections, candiduria, mucocutaneous, and chronic oral atrophic candidiasis (denture sore mouth). Can be treated in both normal hosts and those with compromised immune function.</li><li><strong>Tinea pedis, tinea cruris, tinea versicolor, and dermal candidial infections</strong>: Also indicated for nail fungal infections.</li><li><strong>Systemic candidiasis</strong> including candidaemia, disseminated candidiasis, and other forms of invasive candidal infections (peritoneum, endocardium, pulmonary, urinary tracts).</li><li><strong>Cryptococcosis</strong>: Including cryptococcal meningitis and infections of other sites (pulmonary, cutaneous). It can be used in normal hosts as well as those with AIDS, organ transplants, or other immunosuppressive conditions. Xeroder 200 mg/100 ml may be used for maintenance therapy to prevent relapse of cryptococcal disease in patients with AIDS.</li><li><strong>Prevention of fungal infections in immunocompromised patients</strong>: Particularly those at risk due to neutropenia from cytotoxic chemotherapy or radiotherapy, including bone marrow transplant patients.</li><li><strong>Other uses</strong>: Fungal urinary tract infections, disseminated candidiasis, and acute treatment of other systemic fungal infections such as coccidioidomycosis and histoplasmosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</p><p>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><p>Xeroder 200 mg/100 ml is a triazole antifungal agent. It inhibits fungal cytochrome P-450 dependent enzymes, which are crucial for the synthesis of ergosterol, a component of the fungal cell membrane.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults (oral)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Vaginal candidiasis</strong>: 150 mg as a single dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Oropharyngeal candidiasis</strong>: 200 mg on the first day, then 100 mg once daily. Continue for at least 2 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Esophageal candidiasis</strong>: 200 mg on the first day, then 100 mg daily (up to 400 mg/day may be used). Treatment for at least 3 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Systemic candida infections</strong>: Optimal dosage and duration are not established but up to 400 mg/day may be used.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cryptococcal meningitis</strong>: 400 mg on the first day, then 200 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of fungal infections</strong> in neutropenic patients: 400 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children (oral)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Mucosal candidiasis</strong>: 3-6 mg/kg daily (up to 12 mg/kg is recommended).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Systemic candidiasis and cryptococcal infection</strong>: 6-12 mg/kg daily.</li><li><strong>Intravenous (IV) - Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Invasive candidal infections</strong> (candidaemia, disseminated candidiasis, and cryptococcal infections): 400 mg initially, then 200 mg daily (up to 400 mg daily if necessary). Treatment should be continued according to response (at least 6-8 weeks for cryptococcal meningitis).</li><li><strong>Intravenous (IV) - Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Invasive candidal infections</strong>: 6-12 mg/kg daily, depending on severity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of relapse of cryptococcal meningitis</strong>: 100-200 mg daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Xeroder 200 mg/100 ml increases the prothrombin time when administered with warfarin.</li><li>It prolongs the serum half-life of sulphonylureas (e.g., chlorpropamide, glibenclamide) in diabetic patients.</li><li>Co-administration with <strong>hydrochlorothiazide</strong> may increase plasma concentrations of Xeroder.</li><li>When taken with <strong>phenytoin</strong>, Xeroder can increase phenytoin levels significantly.</li><li>Rifampicin may decrease the effectiveness of Xeroder.</li><li>For <strong>oral contraceptives</strong>, Xeroder does not affect efficacy at 50 mg but increases the plasma levels of ethinyl estradiol and levonorgestrel at 200 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><p>Xeroder 200 mg/100 ml should not be used in patients with known hypersensitivity to this medication or related triazole compounds.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</p><p>Common side effects include gastrointestinal symptoms such as nausea, abdominal discomfort, diarrhea, and flatulence. Rashes are rare (&lt;1%). In very rare cases, anaphylaxis may occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: Xeroder 200 mg/100 ml should be avoided unless the benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li>It is present in breast milk at similar concentrations to plasma, so its use in nursing mothers is not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong>:</p><ul><li>Liver abnormalities may occur, especially in patients with serious underlying diseases like AIDS and cancer.</li><li><strong>Exfoliative skin reactions</strong> like Stevens-Johnson Syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis are rare but should be closely monitored, especially in AIDS patients.</li><li><strong>Hepatic necrosis</strong> is possible, particularly in severely ill patients on multiple medications.</li><li><strong>Use during lactation</strong>: Avoid using in breastfeeding women.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong>:</p><p>In case of overdose, supportive treatment and symptomatic care (such as gastric lavage) are advised. Hemodialysis can reduce plasma levels by approximately 50%.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>:</p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</p><p>Xeroder 200 mg/100 ml is a triazole antifungal agent that inhibits the cytochrome P-450 enzymes in fungi, preventing the synthesis of ergosterol, a critical component of the fungal cell membrane.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>In the elderly</strong>: Normal dosage is used if there is no renal impairment. In cases of renal impairment, dosage intervals may be adjusted.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: Dosage adjustments are necessary for multiple-dose therapy based on creatinine clearance.</li></ul>', 'active', '1596_1742459502.png', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 13:31:42', '2025-05-05 18:15:30'),
(3667, 'Xytrex', 'Olanzapine', 2.52, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Xytrex 5 mg</h3><p>Xytrex 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Acute and maintenance treatment of schizophrenia</strong> and related psychoses, where both positive symptoms (e.g., delusions, hallucinations, disordered thinking, hostility, and suspiciousness) and/or negative symptoms (e.g., flattened affect, emotional and social withdrawal, poverty of speech) are prominent.</li><li><strong>Acute manic or mixed episodes in bipolar disorder</strong>, with or without psychotic features and with or without rapid cycling.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Atypical neuroleptic drugs</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Xytrex 5 mg is an <strong>antipsychotic agent</strong> with affinities for multiple receptors:</p><ul><li><strong>Serotonin receptors</strong>: 5HT2A/2C, 5HT3, 5HT6</li><li><strong>Dopamine receptors</strong>: D1, D2, D3, D4, D5</li><li><strong>Cholinergic muscarinic receptors</strong>: M1-M5</li><li><strong>α1 adrenergic receptors</strong></li><li><strong>Histamine H1 receptors</strong></li></ul><p>Its mechanism of action is not fully understood but is proposed to involve a combination of <strong>dopamine</strong> and <strong>serotonin (5HT2) antagonism</strong>.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Starting dose</strong>: 10 mg/day (administered once daily, without regard to meals).</li><li><strong>Dose adjustments</strong>: Can be adjusted within the range of 5-20 mg/day, based on clinical status.</li><li><strong>Monotherapy for Mania</strong>: Initial dose is 15 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Maximum dose</strong>: 20 mg daily.</li></ul><h3>Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Xytrex 5 mg</strong> can be taken <strong>without regard to meals</strong>.</li><li><strong>Tapering</strong>: Gradual tapering should be considered when discontinuing the drug.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Xytrex 5 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Antagonism</strong>: May antagonize the effects of <strong>levodopa</strong> and <strong>dopamine agonists</strong>.</li><li><strong>Enzyme interactions</strong>: Drugs like <strong>Omeprazole</strong> and <strong>Rifampicin</strong> may increase Xytrex clearance, while <strong>CYP1A2 inhibitors</strong> may reduce Xytrex elimination.</li><li><strong>Carbamazepine</strong>: May increase clearance of Xytrex.</li><li><strong>Activated charcoal</strong>: Reduces bioavailability by 50-60%.</li><li><strong>Caution</strong> with centrally acting drugs and alcohol.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to any ingredient of Xytrex.</li><li><strong>Known risk for narrow-angle glaucoma</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Xytrex 5 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Very common</strong>: Somnolence, weight gain.</li><li><strong>Other possible side effects</strong>: Increased appetite, elevated glucose levels, elevated triglycerides, dizziness, akathisia, Parkinsonism, dyskinesia, orthostatic hypotension, constipation, dry mouth, asthenia, edema, photosensitivity reactions.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Use only if the potential benefits justify the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Not recommended while breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Seizure history</strong>: Use cautiously in patients with a history of seizures or conditions associated with seizures.</li><li><strong>Bone marrow depression</strong>: Use cautiously in patients with low leukocyte and/or neutrophil counts.</li><li><strong>Liver function</strong>: Caution in patients with impaired hepatic function and those using hepatotoxic drugs.</li><li><strong>QT interval</strong>: Caution in patients using medications that increase the QT interval, especially elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Operating machinery</strong>: Caution when operating hazardous machinery, including motor vehicles.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from light and moisture.</li><li><strong>Keep out of reach of children</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Not studied in subjects under 18 years of age.</li><li><strong>Elderly (65 and over)</strong>: Start at 5 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Hepatic or renal impairment</strong>: Start at 5 mg/day.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li>Atypical neuroleptic drugs.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>Xytrex 5 mg\'s efficacy in schizophrenia is thought to involve <strong>dopamine</strong> and <strong>serotonin (5HT2) antagonism</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1403_1742459631.webp', 44, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 13:33:51', '2025-03-20 13:33:51');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3668, 'Xefrim', 'Cefuroxime Axetil', 25.08, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Xefrim 250 mg</h3><p>Xefrim 250 mg is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive bacteria, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Maxillary Sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non beta-lactamase producing strains).</li><li><strong>Lower Respiratory Tract Infections</strong> including pneumonia caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp.</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>E. coli</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Exacerbation of Chronic Bronchitis</strong> and secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains), or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains).</li><li><strong>Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>.</li><li><strong>Bone and Joint Infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Gonorrhoea</strong> caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase-producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>.</li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease</strong> (erythema migrans) caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em>.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Second generation Cephalosporins</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Cefuroxime, the active ingredient in Xefrim, is an antibacterial agent with broad-spectrum bactericidal activity. It is stable against bacterial beta-lactamase, making it effective against many ampicillin- and amoxicillin-resistant strains.</p><h3>Dosage of Xefrim 250 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Tablet or Suspension</strong></li><li><strong>Adolescents and Adults (13 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong>: 250 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Acute Bacterial Maxillary Sinusitis</strong>: 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Acute Bacterial Exacerbation of Chronic Bronchitis</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Secondary Bacterial Infections of Acute Bronchitis</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Uncomplicated Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Uncomplicated Urinary Tract Infections</strong>: 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Uncomplicated Gonorrhoea</strong>: 1000 mg Single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Community Acquired Pneumonia</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>MDR Typhoid Fever</strong>: 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Early Lyme Disease</strong>: 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li><li><strong>Paediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong>: 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Acute Otitis Media</strong>: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Acute Bacterial Maxillary Sinusitis</strong>: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Impetigo</strong>: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Parenteral (IM/IV)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adult</strong>: 750 mg three times daily (IM or IV injection), can be increased to 1.5 gm in severe infections. The frequency can be increased to four times daily, giving total daily doses of 3 to 6 gms.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (above 3 months of age)</strong>: 30-100 mg/kg/day in 3 or 4 equally divided doses. 60 mg/kg/day is appropriate for most infections.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Neonates</strong>: 30-100 mg/kg/day in 2 or 3 equally divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Surgical Prophylaxis</strong>: 1.5 gm by IV injection at induction of anaesthesia; up to 3 further doses of 750 mg may be given by IV/IM injection every 8 hours for high-risk procedures.</li></ul><h3>Administration of Xefrim 250 mg</h3><p>Freshly reconstituted solutions are recommended. Potency is maintained for 24 hours at room temperature or 48 hours at 5°C.</p><h3>Interaction of Xefrim 250 mg</h3><p>No potentially hazardous interactions have been reported.</p><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Xefrim is contraindicated in patients with a known allergy to Cephalosporins.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Xefrim 250 mg</h3><p>Adverse effects to Cefuroxime are infrequent and generally mild and transient. Effects may include:</p><ul><li>Rashes</li><li>Gastrointestinal disturbances</li><li>Prolonged use may result in the overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms (e.g., Candida).</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: US FDA pregnancy category B. Cefuroxime should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Cefuroxime is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Cefuroxime should be given with care to patients receiving potent diuretics or those with a history of colitis.</li><li>Cephalosporins can generally be given safely to patients who are hypersensitive to penicillin, although cross-reactions have been reported.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><p>Store below 30°C, in a cool, dry place, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><h3>Reconstitution</h3><ul><li><strong>750 mg IM injection</strong>: Add 3 mL water for injection to the vial, shake gently.</li><li><strong>750 mg IV injection</strong>: Add 8 mL water for injection, shake gently.</li><li><strong>1.5 gm IV injection</strong>: Add 16 mL water for injection, shake gently. The solution should be injected slowly over 3-5 minutes.</li></ul><h3>Drug Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Second-generation Cephalosporins</strong></li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Cefuroxime is an effective antibacterial agent with broad-spectrum activity, including against beta-lactamase producing strains, making it suitable for resistant bacterial infections.</p>', 'active', '948_1742459861.jpg', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 13:37:41', '2025-03-20 13:37:41'),
(3669, 'Zytiga', 'Abiraterone Acetate', 1920.00, 120, 'tablet', '120 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zytiga 250 mg</strong></p><p>Zytiga 250 mg is a CYP17 inhibitor indicated in combination with prednisone for the treatment of patients with:</p><ul><li>Metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer (CRPC).</li><li>Metastatic high-risk castration-sensitive prostate cancer (CSPC).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Abiraterone is an orally active inhibitor of the steroidal enzyme CYP17A1 (17 alpha-hydroxylase/C17,20 lyase). It inhibits CYP17A1 in a selective and irreversible manner via a covalent binding mechanism. CYP17A1 is an enzyme that catalyzes the biosynthesis of androgen and is highly expressed in testicular, adrenal, and prostatic tumor tissue. More specifically, abiraterone inhibits the conversion of 17-hydroxyprognenolone to dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA) by the enzyme CYP17A1 to decrease serum levels of testosterone and other androgens.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zytiga 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer:</strong> Abiraterone 1,000 mg orally once daily with prednisone 5 mg orally twice daily.</li><li><strong>Metastatic castration-sensitive prostate cancer:</strong> Abiraterone 1,000 mg orally once daily with prednisone 5 mg orally once daily.</li></ul><p>Patients receiving Abiraterone should also receive a gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) analog concurrently or should have had bilateral orchiectomy.</p><p>Abiraterone must be taken on an empty stomach with water at least 1 hour before or 2 hours after a meal. Do not crush or chew tablets.</p><p><strong>Dose Modification:</strong></p><ul><li>For patients with baseline moderate hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh Class B), reduce the Abiraterone starting dose to 250 mg once daily.</li><li>For patients who develop hepatotoxicity during treatment, hold Abiraterone until recovery. Retreatment may be initiated at a reduced dose.</li><li>Abiraterone should be discontinued if patients develop severe hepatotoxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zytiga 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CYP3A4 Inducers:</strong> Avoid concomitant strong CYP3A4 inducers during Abiraterone treatment. If a strong CYP3A4 inducer must be co-administered, increase the Abiraterone dosing frequency.</li><li><strong>CYP2D6 Substrates:</strong> Avoid co-administration of Abiraterone with CYP2D6 substrates that have a narrow therapeutic index. If an alternative treatment cannot be used, exercise caution and consider a dose reduction of the concomitant CYP2D6 substrate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to Abiraterone acetate or to any of the excipients of Abiraterone.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zytiga 250 mg</strong></p><p>The most common adverse reactions are:</p><ul><li>Fatigue</li><li>Arthralgia</li><li>Hypertension</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Edema</li><li>Hypokalemia</li><li>Hot flush</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Upper respiratory infection</li><li>Cough</li><li>Headache</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Abiraterone have not been established in females.</li><li>Based on findings from animal studies and the mechanism of action, Abiraterone can cause fetal harm and potential loss of pregnancy.</li><li>There are no human data on the use of Abiraterone in pregnant women.</li><li>The safety and efficacy of Abiraterone have not been established in females.</li><li>There is no information available on the presence of Zytiga 250 mg in human milk, or on the effects on the breastfed child or milk production.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Mineralocorticoid excess:</strong> Closely monitor patients with cardiovascular disease. Control hypertension and correct hypokalemia before treatment. Monitor blood pressure, serum potassium, and symptoms of fluid retention at least monthly.</li><li><strong>Adrenocortical insufficiency:</strong> Monitor for symptoms and signs of adrenocortical insufficiency. Increased dosage of corticosteroids may be indicated before, during, and after stressful situations.</li><li><strong>Hepatotoxicity:</strong> Can be severe and fatal. Monitor liver function and modify, interrupt, or discontinue Abiraterone dosing as recommended.</li><li><strong>Increased fractures and mortality in combination with radium Ra 223 dichloride:</strong> The use of Abiraterone plus prednisone/prednisolone in combination with radium Ra 223 dichloride is not recommended.</li><li><strong>Embryo-Fetal Toxicity:</strong> Abiraterone can cause fetal harm. Advise males with female partners of reproductive potential to use effective contraception.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zytiga 250 mg</strong></p><p>Human experience of overdose with Abiraterone is limited. There is no specific antidote. In the event of an overdose, stop Abiraterone, undertake general supportive measures, including monitoring for arrhythmias and cardiac failure, and assess liver function.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Abiraterone is an orally active inhibitor of the steroidal enzyme CYP17A1 (17 alpha-hydroxylase/C17,20 lyase). It inhibits CYP17A1 in a selective and irreversible manner via a covalent binding mechanism. CYP17A1 is an enzyme that catalyzes the biosynthesis of androgen and is highly expressed in testicular, adrenal, and prostatic tumor tissue. More specifically, abiraterone inhibits the conversion of 17-hydroxyprognenolone to dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA) by the enzyme CYP17A1 to decrease serum levels of testosterone and other androgens.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Abiraterone have not been established in females. Based on findings from animal studies and the mechanism of action, Abiraterone can cause fetal harm and potential loss of pregnancy. There are no human data on the use of Abiraterone in pregnant women. The safety and efficacy of Abiraterone have not been established in females. There is no information available on the presence of Zytiga 250 mg in human milk, or on the effects on the breastfed child or milk production.</p>', 'active', '166_1742460183.webp', 8, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 13:43:03', '2025-05-05 22:43:00'),
(3670, 'Xclor', 'Cefaclor Monohydrate', 190.57, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '125 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xclor 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Cefaclor is indicated in the treatment of the following infections:</p><ul><li>Otitis media</li><li>Lower respiratory tract infections, including pneumonia, bronchitis, and acute exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</li><li>Upper respiratory tract infections, including pharyngitis and tonsillitis</li><li>Urinary tract infections, including pyelonephritis and cystitis</li><li>Skin and soft tissue infections</li><li>Sinusitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Cefaclor is a second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic that has stability against beta-lactamase inactivation and possesses a broad spectrum of activity. It is active against the following organisms in vitro:</p><ul><li>Alpha and beta-hemolytic Streptococci</li><li>Staphylococci, including coagulase-positive, coagulase-negative, and penicillinase-producing strains</li><li>Streptococcus pneumoniae</li><li>Streptococcus pyogenes (Group A beta-hemolytic Streptococci)</li><li>Branhamella catarrhalis</li><li>Escherichia coli</li><li>Proteus mirabilis</li><li>Klebsiella species</li><li>Haemophilus influenzae, including ampicillin-resistant strains</li></ul><p>Cefaclor is generally effective in the eradication of Streptococci from the nasopharynx.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Xclor 125 mg/5 ml:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual dose: 250 mg every 8 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Bronchitis &amp; pneumonia: 250 mg three times a day (TID)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sinusitis: 500 mg three times a day (TID) for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pneumonia &amp; other more severe infections: Max 4 g/day for 28 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute gonococcal urethritis: 3 g as a single dose combined with probenecid 1 g</li><li><strong>Children:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommended dose: 20 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 8 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Bronchitis &amp; pneumonia: 20 mg/kg/day in divided doses three times a day (TID)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Serious infections, sinusitis, otitis media &amp; infections caused by less susceptible organisms: 40 mg/kg/day in divided doses. Max: 1 g/day</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Xclor 125 mg/5 ml:</strong></p><p>May be taken with or without food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Xclor 125 mg/5 ml:</strong></p><p>The nephrotoxicity of aminoglycoside antibiotics such as gentamicin and tobramicin may be enhanced by any cephalosporin. Therefore, caution is advised when these drugs are used concurrently.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Cefaclor is contraindicated in patients with a known allergy to the Cephalosporin group of antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xclor 125 mg/5 ml:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gastro-intestinal:</strong> Diarrhea, nausea, and vomiting</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity:</strong> Allergic reactions like eruptions, pruritis, and urticaria (usually subside upon discontinuation of therapy). Serum sickness-like reactions have been reported.</li><li><strong>Haematological:</strong> Rare cases of eosinophilia, thrombocytopenia, transient lymphocytosis, and leucopenia</li><li><strong>Hepatic:</strong> Rare cases of transient hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice, and slight elevation in AST, ALT, or alkaline phosphatase values</li><li><strong>Renal:</strong> Rare cases of reversible interstitial nephritis, slight elevations in blood urea, or serum creatinine levels, or abnormal urinalysis</li><li><strong>Central Nervous System:</strong> Rare cases of reversible hyperactivity, nervousness, confusion, hypertonia, dizziness, hallucinations, and somnolence</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><p>There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Cefaclor should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Small amounts of Cefaclor have been detected in mother\'s milk, but the effect on nursing infants is unknown. Caution should be exercised when Cefaclor is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><p>Cefaclor should be administered with caution in the presence of markedly impaired renal function. Dosage adjustments for patients with moderate or severe renal impairment are usually not required.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xclor 125 mg/5 ml:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms:</strong> Nausea, vomiting, epigastric distress, and diarrhea</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> Unless five times the normal total daily dose has been ingested, gastrointestinal decontamination will not be necessary. General management may consist of supportive therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place. Protect from light.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><p>Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Cefaclor is a second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic that has stability against beta-lactamase inactivation and possesses a broad spectrum of activity. It is effective against a wide range of pathogens, including Streptococci, Staphylococci, and Haemophilus influenzae, and works by inhibiting bacterial cell wall synthesis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><p>There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. The drug should be used only if clearly needed. Small amounts of Cefaclor are detected in mother\'s milk, and its effect on nursing infants is not known. Caution is advised when administering Cefaclor to a nursing woman.</p>', 'active', '765_1742461450.jpg', 43, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:04:10', '2025-05-05 18:01:48'),
(3671, 'Zixin', 'Azithromycin Dihydrate', 100.00, 1, 'x 20 ml bottle', '20 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '200 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you provided with headings in normal bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Zixin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Zixin 200 mg/5 ml is prescribed for infections (produced by susceptible organisms) in the upper respiratory tract, including sinusitis and pharyngitis/tonsillitis, the lower respiratory tract, including bronchitis and pneumonia, otitis media, and the skin and soft tissue. Zixin 200 mg/5 ml is recommended for the treatment of non-gonococcal urethritis and cervicitis caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> in sexually transmitted illnesses in both men and women.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Macrolides</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zixin 200 mg/5 ml belongs to the macrolide subclass of antibiotics known as azalide. In order to prevent microbial protein synthesis, Zixin 200 mg/5 ml binds to the 50s ribosomal subunit of susceptible species. It is effective against <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>, anaerobes such as <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>, <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Bordetella pertussis</em>, <em>Bordetella parapertussis</em>, <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em>, <em>Haemophilus ducreyi</em>, and <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>, as well as gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria such as <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus</em>. In vitro testing of Zixin 200 mg/5 ml has shown action against <em>Campylobacter sp.</em>, <em>Toxoplasma gondii</em>, <em>Treponema pallidum</em>, <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>, <em>Legionella pneumophila</em>, and hominins.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Zixin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: 500 mg once daily orally for 3 days or 500 mg once on day 1, then 250 mg once on days 2-5 for 4 days. For sexually transmitted diseases caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> in adults, the dose is 1 gm given as a single dose or 500 mg once on day 1, followed by 250 mg once daily for the next 2 days may also be given.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 10 mg/kg body weight once daily for 3 days for children over 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">200 mg (1 teaspoonful) for 3 days if body weight is 15-25 kg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">300 mg (1½ teaspoonfuls) for 3 days if body weight is 26-35 kg</li><li>400 mg (2 teaspoonfuls) for 3 days if body weight is 36-45 kg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In typhoid fever, 500 mg (2½ teaspoonfuls) once daily for 7-10 days is given.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zixin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Procedure for reinstatement after suspension</strong>:</li><li>Step 1: Thoroughly shake the bottle to break up the powder.</li><li>Step 2: Fill the bottle with boiled and cooled water to the watermark on the label.</li><li>Step 3: Mix the powder and water thoroughly to combine.</li><li>Take Zixin 200 mg/5 ml at least one hour before or two hours after eating.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zixin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>When food and antacids are present, Zixin 200 mg/5 ml absorption is lessened. Due to the potential for ergotism caused by an interaction between Zixin 200 mg/5 ml and the cytochrome P-450 system, Zixin 200 mg/5 ml should be avoided in patients who are receiving ergot alkaloids. With co-administration, care should be taken since macrolides raise the plasma levels of cyclosporin and digoxin. Warfarin, Theophylline, Carbamazepine, Methylprednisolone, or Cimetidine have not been found to interact with Zixin 200 mg/5 ml.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those who are hypersensitive to Zixin 200 mg/5 ml or any other macrolide antibiotic should not take Zixin 200 mg/5 ml. Ergot derivatives and Zixin 200 mg/5 ml should not be administered together. Those with liver disorders should not use Zixin 200 mg/5 ml.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zixin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Low-frequency side effects are experienced after taking Zixin 200 mg/5 ml. A therapeutic withdrawal will reverse the adverse effects, which include nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain or cramps, flatulence, diarrhea, headaches, and skin rashes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The Zixin 200 mg/5 ml pregnancy category is B. Studies on animal reproduction have shown that Zixin 200 mg/5 ml has no evidence of harming the fetus. Pregnant women have not been the subject of any good, controlled studies. Zixin 200 mg/5 ml should only be used during pregnancy if no other suitable options are available, as research on animal reproduction is not always indicative of human response. The presence of Zixin 200 mg/5 ml in breast milk is unknown. Hence, when giving Zixin 200 mg/5 ml to nursing mothers, care should be taken.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>It is advised to keep an eye out for any indications of superinfection with non-susceptible organisms, such as fungi, when using any antibiotic. Those with renal impairment don\'t require a dose adjustment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zixin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>There is no information on Zixin 200 mg/5 ml overdosage. Macrolide antibiotic overdose symptoms can include hearing loss, extreme nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. The use of gastric lavage and other supportive treatments is advised.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep dry and away from heat and light. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p>', 'active', '1514_1742461671.jpg', 50, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:07:51', '2025-05-05 22:35:41'),
(3672, 'Xefer', 'Ferric Carboxymaltose', 700.00, 1, 'x 10 ml vial', '10 ml vial', 'IV Injection', '500 mg per 10 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Xefer 500 mg/10 ml</strong></h3><p>Xefer 500 mg/10 ml is indicated for the treatment of iron deficiency anemia in adult patients:</p><ul><li>Who have intolerance to oral iron or have had unsatisfactory response to oral iron</li><li>Who have non-dialysis dependent chronic kidney disease.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Parenteral Iron Preparations</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Xefer 500 mg/10 ml is a non-dextran, intravenous colloidal iron hydroxide in complex with carboxymaltose, a carbohydrate polymer that releases iron. It works by replacing iron stores in hemoglobin, myoglobin, and enzymes, thereby facilitating oxygen transport via hemoglobin. The macrophage engulfs the ferric carboxymaltose (FCM) from the blood, controlling iron release. Transferrin transports iron to the liver, spleen, and bone marrow.</p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>For patients weighing 50 kg or more</strong>: Administer Xefer 500 mg/10 ml in two doses separated by at least 7 days. Each dose should be 750 mg for a total cumulative dose not exceeding 1500 mg of iron per course.</li><li><strong>For patients weighing less than 50 kg</strong>: Administer Xefer 500 mg/10 ml in two doses separated by at least 7 days. Each dose should be 15 mg/kg body weight for a total cumulative dose not exceeding 1500 mg of iron per course.</li><li>The dosage of Xefer 500 mg/10 ml is expressed in mg of elemental iron. Each mL contains 50 mg of elemental iron.</li><li>Treatment may be repeated if iron deficiency anemia recurs.</li><li><strong>Administration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Administer intravenously, either as an undiluted slow intravenous push or by infusion.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For slow intravenous push: Administer at approximately 100 mg (2 mL) per minute.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For infusion: Dilute up to 750 mg of iron in no more than 250 mL of sterile 0.9% sodium chloride injection, USP, and administer over at least 15 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The solution is physically and chemically stable for 72 hours at room temperature when stored in a 0.9% sodium chloride infusion bag with concentrations between 2 mg to 4 mg of iron per mL.</li><li><strong>Precautions</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Inspect the solution visually for particulate matter or discoloration.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The product contains no preservatives and is for single use only. Any unused drug must be discarded.</li><li><strong>Extravasation</strong>: Avoid extravasation. If it occurs, discontinue the infusion at that site as brown discoloration may be long-lasting.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction</strong></h3><ul><li>No known drug interactions or well-documented interactions.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of its components.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Hypertension</li><li>Flushing</li><li>Decreased blood phosphorus</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Pruritus</li><li>Rash</li><li>Urticaria</li><li>Wheezing</li><li>Injection site discoloration</li><li>Headache</li><li>Increased alanine aminotransferase</li><li>Dysgeusia</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Serious anaphylactic/anaphylactoid reactions</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: Animal reproduction studies have shown an adverse effect on the fetus, and there are no adequate and well-controlled studies in humans. The potential benefits may warrant use in pregnant women despite potential risks.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity Reactions</strong>: Serious hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylactic-type reactions, some of which have been life-threatening or fatal, may occur. Symptoms may include shock, hypotension, loss of consciousness, and collapse. Monitor patients for at least 30 minutes after administration.</li><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: Transient elevations in systolic blood pressure, often with facial flushing, dizziness, or nausea, may occur immediately after dosing and resolve within 30 minutes. Monitor patients for signs of hypertension after administration.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store at or below 30°C. Do not freeze.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><ul><li>Parenteral Iron Preparations</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Xefer 500 mg/10 ml is a colloidal iron hydroxide complexed with carboxymaltose. It releases iron, replacing iron stores in hemoglobin, myoglobin, and enzymes, supporting oxygen transport via hemoglobin. Iron is transported via transferrin into the liver, spleen, and bone marrow.</p><h3><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h3><ul><li>Pregnancy Category C. Animal studies show adverse effects on the fetus, and there are no controlled studies in humans. However, potential benefits may justify its use in pregnant women despite risks.</li></ul>', 'active', '1238_1742461815.jpg', 27, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:10:15', '2025-05-05 18:02:19'),
(3673, 'Xefrim', 'Cefuroxime Axetil', 199.35, 1, 'x 70 ml bottle', '70 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '125 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xefrim 125 mg/5 ml:</strong></p><p>Xefrim is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive bacteria. These include:</p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Maxillary Sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non beta-lactamase producing strains)</li><li><strong>Lower Respiratory Tract Infections</strong>, including pneumonia, caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp.</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>E. coli</em></li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Exacerbation of Chronic Bronchitis</strong> and <strong>Secondary Bacterial Infections of Acute Bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains), or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains)</li><li><strong>Skin and Skin-Structure Infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></li><li><strong>Bone and Joint Infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Gonorrhoea</strong> caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase-producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease</strong> (erythema migrans) caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Second-generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime is a broad-spectrum, bactericidal antibiotic with good stability against bacterial β-lactamase, making it effective against many ampicillin- and amoxicillin-resistant strains.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage of Xefrim 125 mg/5 ml:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adolescents and Adults (13 years and older):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis: 250 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute Bacterial Maxillary Sinusitis: 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute Bacterial Exacerbation of Chronic Bronchitis: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Secondary Bacterial Infections of Acute Bronchitis: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated Skin and Skin Structure Infections: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated Urinary Tract Infections: 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated Gonorrhoea: 1000 mg single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Community Acquired Pneumonia: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">MDR Typhoid Fever: 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Early Lyme Disease: 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li><li><strong>Paediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis: 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute Otitis Media: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute Bacterial Maxillary Sinusitis: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Impetigo: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li><strong>Parenteral:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adult: 750 mg three times daily by IM or IV injection</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (above 3 months of age): 30-100 mg/kg/day given in 3 or 4 divided doses</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral Suspension:</strong> Best when freshly reconstituted, but retains potency for 24 hours at room temperature or 48 hours at 5°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction:</strong> No hazardous interactions have been reported.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Known allergy to Cephalosporins.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Generally mild and transient, including rashes and gastrointestinal disturbances. Prolonged use may result in the overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms such as Candida.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category B (No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women; use only if clearly needed).</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Cefuroxime is excreted in human milk, so caution is advised during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in patients on concurrent diuretics or with a history of colitis.</li><li>Cephalosporins can generally be safely used in penicillin-sensitive individuals, although cross-reactions have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution for Injections:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>750 mg IM:</strong> Add 3 ml of water for injection.</li><li><strong>750 mg IV:</strong> Add 8 ml of water for injection.</li><li><strong>1.5 g IV:</strong> Add 16 ml of water for injection.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime is a broad-spectrum, bactericidal antibiotic with good stability against bacterial β-lactamase, making it effective against many resistant strains.</p><p><br></p><p>This summary provides an overview of Xefrim\'s indications, dosage, and usage details. Let me know if you\'d like to dive deeper into any section!</p>', 'active', '1619_1742461993.jpg', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:13:13', '2025-05-07 19:53:16'),
(3674, 'Xelcom', 'Tiemonium Methylsulfate', 35.00, 1, 'x 2 ml ampoule', '2 ml ampoule', 'IV Injection', '5 mg/2 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Xelcom 5 mg/2 ml</h3><p>Xelcom 5 mg/2 ml is an antispasmodic drug that reduces muscle spasms of the intestine, biliary system, bladder, and uterus. It is used in the symptomatic treatment of pain related to functional disorders of the digestive tract and biliary system. It is also indicated for the treatment of spasm and pain in urological and gynecological diseases.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p><strong>Anticholinergics</strong></p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Xelcom 5 mg/2 ml is a competitive antagonist of Acetylcholine and Histamine and strengthens the calcium bond with membrane phospholipids and proteins. This inhibits the intracellular contractile protein of visceral cells, which causes inhibition of visceral spasm and pain.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xelcom 5 mg/2 ml</h3><ul><li><strong>Tablet/Syrup</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: Usual dose is 2-6 tablets or 3-9 teaspoonfuls of syrup daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children</strong>: 3 ml/kg or 6 mg/kg body weight daily in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 1 Xelcom 5 mg/2 ml Injection 3 times daily, administered slowly through the intravenous route or intramuscular route.</li><li><strong>Suppository</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 20 mg Xelcom 5 mg/2 ml suppository two or three times daily via the rectal route.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Xelcom 5 mg/2 ml</h3><p>Tiemonium methylsulphate tablets should not be used with other drugs without prior consultation with a registered physician to avoid possible drug interactions.</p><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Should not be used in urethroprostatic disorders involving a risk of urine retention.</li><li>Contraindicated in patients at risk for angle closure glaucoma.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Xelcom 5 mg/2 ml</h3><p>Xelcom 5 mg/2 ml may cause hypotension and tachycardia in certain individuals.</p><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>The results of animal studies on Xelcom 5 mg/2 ml did not reveal any teratogenic effects, and no deformities have been reported with normal use.</li><li>In the absence of sufficient data, caution should be exercised during use in nursing mothers, though no problems have been reported with normal use.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Caution should be taken during the treatment of patients with disorders of the prostate.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with chronic bronchitis, coronary insufficiency, ambient hyperthermia, and renal or hepatic insufficiency.</li><li>The risk of visual disturbances may make it dangerous to drive or operate machinery.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Xelcom 5 mg/2 ml</h3><p>There is no available data regarding the overdose of Tiemonium methylsulphate tablets.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness of Tiemonium methylsulphate in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Efficacy and safety were maintained with increasing age.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><p><strong>Anticholinergics</strong></p><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Xelcom 5 mg/2 ml is a competitive antagonist of Acetylcholine and Histamine, strengthening the calcium bond with membrane phospholipids and proteins. This inhibits the intracellular contractile proteins of visceral cells, which prevents visceral spasm and pain.</p><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li>No teratogenic effects were found in animal studies.</li><li>In the absence of sufficient data, prudence should be exercised for nursing mothers, though no problems have been reported with normal use.</li></ul><h3>Pediatric Uses</h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness have not been established in pediatric patients.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Efficacy and safety were maintained with increasing age.</li></ul>', 'active', '719_1742462169.webp', 10, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:16:09', '2025-05-07 19:53:36'),
(3675, 'Vomiren', 'Palonosetron', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.5mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Vomiren is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Acute and delayed nausea and vomiting</li><li>Uncontrolled nausea and vomiting</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Chemotherapy-Induced Nausea and Vomiting (CINV):</strong>Acute CINV occurring on the day of treatment with certain types of chemotherapy</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Delayed CINV occurring days later with certain types of chemotherapy</li><li><strong>Radiotherapy-Induced Nausea and Vomiting (RINV)</strong></li><li><strong>Post-Operative &amp; Post-Discharge Nausea and Vomiting (PONV &amp; PDNV)</strong></li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Palonosetron is a <strong>5-HT3 receptor antagonist</strong> with a strong binding affinity for this receptor and little or no affinity for other receptors. Chemotherapeutic agents produce nausea and vomiting by releasing serotonin from the enterochromaffin cells of the small intestine. The released serotonin activates <strong>5-HT3 receptors</strong> on nerve terminals of the vagus in the periphery and centrally in the <strong>chemoreceptor trigger zone</strong> of the <strong>area postrema</strong>, initiating the vomiting reflex.</p><p>Postoperative nausea and vomiting is influenced by multiple patient, surgical, and anesthesia-related factors. It is triggered by <strong>5-HT3 release</strong>, causing a neuronal cascade involving both the <strong>central nervous system (CNS)</strong> and the <strong>gastrointestinal tract</strong>. The <strong>5-HT3 receptor</strong> selectively participates in the emetic response. <strong>Palonosetron blocks the actions of serotonin</strong> at 5-HT3 receptors, reducing nausea and vomiting.</p><p><strong>Pharmacokinetics:</strong></p><p> Palonosetron exhibits <strong>linear dose-proportional pharmacokinetics</strong> over the <strong>dose range of 1-90 pg/kg</strong> in both healthy subjects and cancer patients. In cancer patients receiving single IV doses:</p><ul><li><strong>Cmax (Maximum Plasma Concentration):</strong> Ranges from <strong>0.89 to 336 ng/mL</strong></li><li><strong>AUC (Area Under the Plasma Concentration-Time Curve):</strong> Ranges from <strong>13.8 to 957 ng.h/mL</strong></li><li><strong>Volume of Distribution:</strong> <strong>6.9-7.9 L/kg</strong></li><li><strong>Protein Binding:</strong> <strong>62%</strong></li><li><strong>Metabolism:</strong> <strong>50% metabolized into two inactive metabolites (&lt;1% of 5-HT3 receptor antagonist activity)</strong></li><li><strong>Excretion:</strong> <strong>40% via kidney, 50% via liver (CYP2D6, CYP3A4, CYP1A2)</strong></li><li><strong>Half-Life:</strong> <strong>40 hours</strong></li><li><strong>Total Body Clearance:</strong> <strong>160±35 mL/h/kg</strong></li><li><strong>Renal Clearance:</strong> <strong>66.5±18.2 mL/h/kg</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p> <strong>Usual Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult Tablet:</strong> <strong>0.5 mg daily</strong></li><li><strong>Adult IV:</strong> <strong>0.075 mg single dose over 10 seconds</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Chemotherapy-Induced Nausea and Vomiting:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong> <strong>0.5 mg</strong> taken <strong>1 hour before chemotherapy</strong></li><li><strong>IV:</strong> <strong>0.25 mg single dose over 30 seconds</strong> taken <strong>30 minutes before chemotherapy</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Radiotherapy-Induced Nausea and Vomiting:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IV:</strong> <strong>0.25 mg single dose over 30 seconds</strong> taken <strong>30 minutes before each weekly radiation fraction</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Post-Operative Nausea and Vomiting:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IV:</strong> <strong>0.075 mg single dose over 10 seconds</strong> immediately <strong>before anesthesia induction</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Children (1 month to 17 years):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IV:</strong> <strong>20 mcg/kg body weight</strong> (Maximum <strong>1.5 mg</strong>)</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Safe with <strong>corticosteroids, analgesics, antiemetics, antispasmodics, and anticholinergics</strong></li><li>Does <strong>not inhibit the antitumor activity</strong> of <strong>cisplatin, cyclophosphamide, cytarabine, doxorubicin, and mitomycin C</strong></li><li>No significant interactions with <strong>metoclopramide</strong></li><li>Not an inhibitor of <strong>CYP1A2, CYP2A6, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2D6, CYP2E1 &amp; CYP3A4/5</strong></li><li>No enzyme induction of <strong>CYP1A2, CYP2D6, or CYP3A4/5</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to <strong>Palonosetron</strong> or any of its components</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> <strong>Headache, Constipation</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong></li><li><strong>Unknown</strong> whether excreted in breast milk</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly Patients (&gt;65 years):</strong> No dosage adjustment required</li><li><strong>Children (1 month to 10 years):</strong> <strong>IV dose of 20 mcg/kg</strong> (Max <strong>1.5 mg</strong>)</li><li><strong>Patients with Renal &amp; Hepatic Dysfunction:</strong> No dosage adjustment required</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>No known antidote</li><li><strong>Managed with supportive care</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Anti-emetic drugs</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Store in a cool, dry place</strong>, protected from light</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '958_1742462275.jpg', 6, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:17:55', '2025-03-20 14:17:55');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3676, 'Xelix SR', 'Indapamide', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '1.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xelix SR 1.5 mg</strong></p><p> Xelix SR 1.5 mg is indicated in the treatment of essential hypertension. It is effective in treating hypertension in patients with renal function impairment, although its diuretic effect is reduced. Xelix SR 1.5 mg is also indicated for the treatment of salt and fluid retention associated with congestive heart failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Thiazide diuretics &amp; related drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Xelix SR 1.5 mg is a diuretic antihypertensive. It appears to cause vasodilation, probably by inhibiting the passage of calcium and other ions (sodium, potassium) across membranes. It has an extra-renal antihypertensive action resulting in a decrease in vascular hyperreactivity and a reduction in total peripheral and arteriolar resistance.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xelix SR 1.5 mg</strong></p><p> One tablet daily, preferably in the morning. In more severe cases, Xelix SR 1.5 mg can be combined with other categories of antihypertensive agents. The safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Xelix SR 1.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Other antihypertensive drugs</strong>: Xelix SR 1.5 mg may add to or potentiate the action of other antihypertensive drugs.</li><li><strong>Norepinephrine</strong>: Xelix SR 1.5 mg, like thiazides, may decrease arterial responsiveness to norepinephrine.</li><li><strong>Lithium</strong>: In general, diuretics should not be given concomitantly with lithium because they reduce its renal clearance and increase the risk of lithium toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> This drug must not be taken in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to sulfonamides</li><li>Severe renal failure</li><li>Hepatic encephalopathy or severe hepatic failure</li><li>Hypokalemia</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xelix SR 1.5 mg</strong></p><p> Side effects include headache, anorexia, gastric irritation, nausea, vomiting, constipation, diarrhea, etc.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women, so Xelix SR 1.5 mg is not recommended. Mothers taking Xelix SR 1.5 mg should not breastfeed.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Monitoring of potassium and uric acid serum levels is recommended, especially in subjects with a predisposition or sensitivity to hypokalemia and in patients with gout. Although no allergic manifestations have been reported during clinical trials, patients with a history of allergy to sulfonamide derivatives should be closely monitored.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xelix SR 1.5 mg</strong></p><p> Symptoms: These could include allergies, skin rashes, epigastric pain, nausea, photosensitivity, dizziness, weakness, and paresthesia.</p><p> Treatment: Treatment is supportive and symptomatic, directed at correcting the electrolyte abnormalities.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place. Protect from light and moisture.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Thiazide diuretics &amp; related drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Xelix SR 1.5 mg is a diuretic antihypertensive. It appears to cause vasodilation, probably by inhibiting the passage of calcium and other ions (sodium, potassium) across membranes. It has an extra-renal antihypertensive action resulting in a decrease in vascular hyperreactivity and a reduction in total peripheral and arteriolar resistance.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women, so Xelix SR 1.5 mg is not recommended. Mothers taking Xelix SR 1.5 mg should not breastfeed.</p>', 'active', '1402_1742462429.jpg', 29, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:20:29', '2025-03-20 14:20:29'),
(3677, 'Xelotan', 'Losartan Potassium', 6.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xelotan 50 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Hypertension</strong>: It is recommended to use Xelotan 50 mg to treat hypertension. It could be used with other antihypertensive medications or taken alone (e.g., thiazide diuretics).</p><p> <strong>Renal Protection in Type-2 Diabetic Patients with Proteinuria</strong>: In hypertensive type-2 diabetics with proteinuria, which is defined as urine albumin to creatinine ratio &gt;300 mg/g, Xelotan 50 mg is advised to postpone the advancement of renal disease.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Angiotensin-II receptor blocker</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> The first angiotensin II receptor blocker that is orally active without a peptide is Xelotan 50 mg. It binds to the AT1 receptor, which is present in numerous tissues (such as the heart, kidneys, adrenal glands, and vascular smooth muscle), and inhibits several critical biological processes, such as vasoconstriction and the production of the hormone aldosterone that causes hypertension.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xelotan 50 mg</strong></p><p> For the majority of patients, the beginning and maintenance dose is 50 mg once daily. Prior to raising the dose, 25 mg twice daily is advised if the antihypertensive impact of 50 mg once daily is insufficient. A starting dose of 25 mg once daily should be taken into consideration for individuals with intravascular volume depletion (such as those on high-dose diuretics). One or two doses of Xelotan 50 mg can be given each day. From 25 mg to 100 mg is the range for the total daily dose.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Xelotan 50 mg</strong></p><p> Levels of the Xelotan 50 mg active metabolite are decreased by rifampicin and fluconazole. The antihypertensive effects of hydrochlorothiazide and Xelotan 50 mg used together may be potentiated. Increases in serum potassium may result from the concurrent use of potassium-sparing diuretics (such as spironolactone, triamterene, and amiloride), potassium supplements, or salt substitutes containing potassium. The non-steroidal anti-inflammatory medicine indomethacin may lessen the antihypertensive effects of losartan. The risk of renal impairment is increased by the concurrent use of an ACE inhibitor, an angiotensin receptor antagonist, an anti-inflammatory medication, and a thiazide diuretic.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Pregnant women and individuals who are hypersensitive to any ingredient in this medication should not use Xelotan 50 mg. Those with diabetes shouldn\'t take Aliskiren and Xelotan 50 mg together.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xelotan 50 mg</strong></p><p> Xelotan 50 mg side effects are minor and short-lived in nature. Dizziness, diarrhea, nasal congestion, coughing, and upper respiratory infections are the most frequent adverse effects. Fatigue, oedema, chest pain, nausea, headaches, and pharyngitis are other adverse effects.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Category D for pregnancies. If Xelotan 50 mg is given during the second or third trimester of pregnancy, the risk to the fetus increases. Given that numerous medicines are excreted in human milk and that it is unknown whether Xelotan 50 mg is one of them, a choice should be taken regarding whether to stop breastfeeding or stop taking the medication, taking into account the significance of the medication to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> In addition to increasing fetal and neonatal morbidity and mortality, the use of Xelotan 50 mg throughout the second and third trimesters of pregnancy decreases fetal renal function. Symptomatic hypotension may happen in people who are intravascularly volume-depleted (such as those taking high-dose diuretics). Patients with cirrhosis have a considerably higher plasma concentration of Xelotan 50 mg. Patients with renal impairment have experienced changes in renal function, including renal failure.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep dry and away from heat and light. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Angiotensin-II receptor blocker</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> The first angiotensin II receptor blocker that is orally active without a peptide is Xelotan 50 mg. It binds to the AT1 receptor, which is present in numerous tissues (such as the heart, kidneys, adrenal glands, and vascular smooth muscle), and inhibits several critical biological processes, such as vasoconstriction and the production of the hormone aldosterone that causes hypertension.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Category D for pregnancies. If Xelotan 50 mg is given during the second or third trimester of pregnancy, the risk to the fetus increases. Given that numerous medicines are excreted in human milk and that it is unknown whether Xelotan 50 mg is one of them, a choice should be taken regarding whether to stop breastfeeding or stop taking the medication, taking into account the significance of the medication to the mother.</p>', 'active', '787_1742462575.jpg', 61, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:22:55', '2025-03-20 14:22:55'),
(3678, 'Paloxiron', 'Palonosetron', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.5mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Paloxiron is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Acute and delayed nausea and vomiting</strong></li><li><strong>Uncontrolled nausea and vomiting</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV):Acute CINV</strong>: Occurs on the day of treatment with certain types of chemotherapy</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Delayed CINV</strong>: Occurs days later with certain types of chemotherapy</li><li><strong>Radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (RINV)</strong></li><li><strong>Post-operative &amp; Post-discharge nausea and vomiting (PONV &amp; PDNV)</strong></li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন।</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Palonosetron is a <strong>5-HT3 receptor antagonist</strong> with a strong binding affinity for this receptor and little or no affinity for other receptors. It is believed that chemotherapeutic agents cause nausea and vomiting by releasing <strong>serotonin</strong> from the enterochromaffin cells of the small intestine. The released serotonin activates <strong>5-HT3 receptors</strong> located on the nerve terminals of the vagus nerve and centrally in the chemoreceptor trigger zone, initiating the vomiting reflex.</p><p>Postoperative nausea and vomiting is influenced by multiple patient, surgical, and anesthesia-related factors and is triggered by the release of <strong>5-HT3 in a cascade of neuronal events</strong> involving both the <strong>central nervous system</strong> and the <strong>gastrointestinal tract</strong>. The <strong>5-HT3 receptor</strong> has been shown to selectively participate in the emetic response. Palonosetron works by <strong>blocking the actions of serotonin</strong> at <strong>5-HT3 receptors</strong>, which are associated with nausea and vomiting. It likely acts in the <strong>small intestine</strong> and possibly in the <strong>brain</strong>.</p><p><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></p><p> Palonosetron exhibits <strong>linear dose-proportional pharmacokinetics</strong> over the dose range of <strong>1-90 µg/kg</strong> in healthy subjects and in cancer patients. In cancer patients receiving single intravenous doses of Palonosetron:</p><ul><li><strong>Cmax (Maximum plasma concentration):</strong> Ranges from <strong>0.89 to 336 ng/ml</strong></li><li><strong>AUC (Area Under Curve):</strong> Ranges from <strong>13.8 to 957 ng·h/ml</strong></li><li><strong>Volume of distribution:</strong> Approximately <strong>6.9-7.9 L/kg</strong></li><li><strong>Plasma protein binding:</strong> Approximately <strong>62%</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Metabolism:50% metabolized</strong> into two inactive metabolites with &lt;1% of the <strong>5-HT3 receptor antagonist activity</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>40% metabolized via kidney</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>50% metabolized by liver CYP enzymes (CYP2D6, CYP3A4, CYP1A2)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Excretion:40% excreted unchanged in urine after 144 hours</strong></li><li><strong>Total body clearance:</strong> <strong>160±35 ml/h/kg</strong></li><li><strong>Renal clearance:</strong> <strong>66.5±18.2 ml/h/kg</strong></li><li><strong>Half-life:</strong> <strong>40 hours</strong></li><li><strong>Greater 5-HT3 receptor binding affinity</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p> <strong>Usual Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult Tablet Dosage:</strong> <strong>0.5 mg daily</strong></li><li><strong>Adult IV Dosage:</strong> <strong>0.075 mg IV</strong> administered over <strong>10 seconds</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult Tablet Dosage:</strong> <strong>0.5 mg</strong>, administered <strong>1 hour prior</strong> to chemotherapy</li><li><strong>Adult IV Dosage:</strong> <strong>0.25 mg IV</strong>, administered <strong>over 30 seconds</strong>, <strong>30 minutes prior</strong> to chemotherapy</li></ul><p><strong>Radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (RINV):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Single IV dose of 0.25 mg</strong>, administered <strong>over 30 seconds, 30 minutes before radiation fraction</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Post-operative nausea and vomiting (PONV):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Single IV dose of 0.075 mg</strong>, administered <strong>over 10 seconds, immediately before induction of anesthesia</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Children Dosage (1 month to 17 years):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Single IV dose of 20 mcg/kg body weight</strong> (Maximum dose: <strong>1.5 mg</strong>)</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন।</em></p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><p> In <strong>controlled clinical trials</strong>, Paloxiron injection has been <strong>safely administered</strong> with:</p><ul><li><strong>Corticosteroids</strong></li><li><strong>Analgesics</strong></li><li><strong>Antiemetics/antinauseants</strong></li><li><strong>Antispasmodics</strong></li><li><strong>Anticholinergic agents</strong></li></ul><p>Paloxiron did not inhibit the <strong>antitumor activity</strong> of <strong>cisplatin, cyclophosphamide, cytarabine, doxorubicin,</strong> and <strong>mitomycin C</strong> in murine tumor models. No significant pharmacokinetic interactions were observed when <strong>Paloxiron and metoclopramide</strong> were administered concomitantly. <strong>In vitro studies</strong> indicate that Paloxiron <strong>does not inhibit</strong> CYP1A2, CYP2A6, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2D6, CYP2E1, &amp; CYP3A4/5 and <strong>does not induce</strong> CYP1A2, CYP2D6, or CYP3A4/5. Therefore, <strong>clinically significant drug interactions</strong> appear to be <strong>low</strong>.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Palonosetron is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in patients with:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to the drug or its components</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Headache</strong></li><li><strong>Constipation</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong></li><li>It is <strong>unknown</strong> whether <strong>Palonosetron is excreted in breast milk</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly Patients (&gt;65 years):</strong> No dosage adjustment required</li><li><strong>Children (1 month to 10 years):</strong> <strong>Single IV dose of 20 mcg/kg</strong> (Maximum dose: <strong>1.5 mg</strong>)</li><li><strong>Patients with Renal &amp; Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment required</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>No known antidote</strong></li><li><strong>Managed with supportive care</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Anti-emetic drugs</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool &amp; dry place</strong></li><li>Protect from <strong>light</strong></li></ul><p><br></p><p><br></p>', 'active', '1770_1742462640.jpg', 2, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:24:00', '2025-03-20 14:24:00'),
(3679, 'Xelpac Premium', 'Paclitaxel', 2000.00, 1, 'x 30 mg vial', '30 mg vial', 'IV Infusion', '6 mg per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xelpac 6 mg/ml</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Ovarian Carcinoma</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Xelpac 6 mg/ml is indicated as first-line and subsequent therapy for the treatment of advanced carcinoma of the ovary.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>First-line therapy</strong>: In combination with cisplatin.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Subsequent therapy</strong>: As a single agent after first-line treatment.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Breast Carcinoma</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adjuvant treatment</strong>: For node-positive breast cancer administered sequentially after standard doxorubicin-containing combination chemotherapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Post-chemotherapy treatment</strong>: For metastatic disease or relapse within 6 months of adjuvant chemotherapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>First-line therapy</strong>: For advanced or metastatic breast cancer, either with doxorubicin or trastuzumab for HER2-positive patients.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Combination with Gemcitabine</strong>: For patients with unresectable or metastatic breast cancer after relapse following adjuvant/neoadjuvant chemotherapy.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Non-Small Cell Lung Carcinoma</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Xelpac 6 mg/ml, in combination with cisplatin, is indicated for first-line treatment in patients who are not candidates for curative surgery or radiation therapy.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Kaposi\'s Sarcoma</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For second-line treatment of AIDS-related Kaposi’s Sarcoma.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Gastric Carcinoma</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Xelpac 6 mg/ml is indicated for the treatment of gastric carcinoma.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Xelpac 6 mg/ml works by promoting microtubule formation and stabilizing existing microtubules, preventing their disassembly. This disrupts normal cell division during the G2 mitotic phase, inhibiting cell replication.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Ovarian Carcinoma (first-line therapy)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">135 mg/m² over 24 hours, followed by cisplatin, repeated at 3-week intervals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Alternatively, 175 mg/m² over 3 hours, followed by cisplatin.</li><li><strong>Breast Cancer (adjuvant therapy)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">175 mg/m² over 3 hours, repeated every 3 weeks for 4 courses.</li><li><strong>AIDS-related Kaposi’s Sarcoma</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">135 mg/m² over 3 hours every 3 weeks, or 100 mg/m² every 2 weeks in patients with poor performance status.</li><li><strong>Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">175 mg/m² over 3 hours followed by cisplatin, repeated every 3 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Premedication</strong></p><ul><li>Dexamethasone, diphenhydramine, and cimetidine or ranitidine to prevent hypersensitivity reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cisplatin</strong>: Increased myelosuppression, potential renal failure risk when combined with Xelpac 6 mg/ml.</li><li><strong>Doxorubicin</strong>: Sequence matters—Xelpac 6 mg/ml should be administered 24 hours after doxorubicin.</li><li><strong>Cytochrome P450 interaction</strong>: Caution with drugs that inhibit CYP2C8 or CYP3A4, such as ketoconazole and erythromycin, as they may increase toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Xelpac 6 mg/ml or its components.</li><li>Baseline neutrophil count &lt;1.5 x 10⁹/L or platelets &lt;100 x 10⁹/L (AIDS-related Kaposi’s Sarcoma: neutrophils &lt;1 x 10⁹/L, platelets &lt;75 x 10⁹/L).</li><li>Severe hepatic impairment.</li><li>Lactation: Contraindicated.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Low blood counts, hair loss, muscle and joint pain, peripheral neuropathy, nausea, vomiting, mouth sores, hypersensitivity reactions, fever, chills.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Edema, liver issues, low blood pressure, skin darkening where previous radiation occurred.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category D</strong>: May cause fetal harm.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Contraindicated.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>No known antidote; management involves monitoring and supportive treatment focused on bone marrow suppression, neurotoxicity, and mucositis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protect from light, and keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li>Dilute Xelpac 6 mg/ml before infusion in saline or dextrose solutions to a concentration of 0.3-1.2 mg/ml.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Must be administered under the supervision of an experienced oncologist.</li><li>Watch for hypersensitivity reactions and extravasation at the infusion site.</li><li>Frequent blood count monitoring is essential.</li><li>Cardiac monitoring may be required for patients developing conduction abnormalities.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Not recommended in severe impairment; use caution in mild to moderate impairment.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Not recommended for children under 18 due to lack of safety and efficacy data.</li></ul><p>This provides a comprehensive overview of <strong>Xelpac 6 mg/ml</strong> for various indications and administration details. If you need further clarification on any aspect, feel free to ask!</p>', 'active', '802_1742462748.jpg', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:25:48', '2025-05-05 18:05:31'),
(3680, 'Paloset', 'Palonosetron', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0.5mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p><strong>Paloset</strong> is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Acute and delayed nausea and vomiting</strong></li><li><strong>Uncontrolled nausea and vomiting</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV)</strong><em>Acute CINV:</em> Occurs on the day of treatment with certain types of chemotherapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Delayed CINV:</em> Occurs on days following chemotherapy.</li><li><strong>Radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (RINV)</strong></li><li><strong>Post-operative &amp; Post-discharge nausea and vomiting (PONV &amp; PDNV)</strong></li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন।</em></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Palonosetron is a <strong>5-HT3 receptor antagonist</strong> with a strong binding affinity for this receptor and minimal or no affinity for other receptors.</p><p>Nausea and vomiting caused by chemotherapy occur due to the release of serotonin from <strong>enterochromaffin cells</strong> in the small intestine. This serotonin activates 5-HT3 receptors located in the vagus nerve and the <strong>chemoreceptor trigger zone</strong> in the brain, initiating the vomiting reflex.</p><p>Postoperative nausea and vomiting involve multiple factors, including patient conditions, surgical effects, and anesthesia. The release of 5-HT3 plays a key role in triggering vomiting through interactions between the <strong>central nervous system</strong> and the <strong>gastrointestinal tract</strong>.</p><p>Palonosetron works by <strong>blocking serotonin</strong> at the <strong>5-HT3 receptors</strong>, preventing nausea and vomiting. It primarily acts in the <strong>small intestine</strong> but may also function in the <strong>brain</strong>.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></h3><ul><li>Palonosetron shows <strong>linear dose-proportional pharmacokinetics</strong> in doses ranging from <strong>1-90 pg/kg</strong>.</li><li>In cancer patients receiving <strong>IV doses</strong>, plasma concentration (Cmax) ranges from <strong>0.89 to 336 ng/ml</strong>, and <strong>AUCo-co</strong> varies from <strong>13.8 to 957 ng.h/ml</strong>.</li><li><strong>Distribution:</strong> Volume of distribution is <strong>6.9-7.9 L/kg</strong>, with <strong>62% plasma protein binding</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Metabolism:50% metabolized</strong> into two inactive metabolites with &lt;1% 5-HT3 receptor antagonist activity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>40% metabolized by the kidney</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>50% metabolized by the liver</strong>, primarily by <strong>CYP2D6</strong>, with minor involvement of <strong>CYP3A4</strong> and <strong>CYP1A2</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Excretion:40% excreted unchanged in urine</strong> within <strong>144 hours</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Total body clearance:</strong> <strong>160±35 ml/h/kg</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal clearance:</strong> <strong>66.5±18.2 ml/h/kg</strong>.</li><li><strong>Half-life:</strong> <strong>40 hours</strong>, with a <strong>strong 5-HT3 receptor binding affinity</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><h4><strong>Usual Dosage</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Adults (Tablet):</strong> <strong>0.5 mg</strong> daily.</li><li><strong>Adults (IV):</strong> <strong>0.075 mg</strong> IV over <strong>10 seconds</strong>.</li></ul><h4><strong>Chemotherapy-induced Nausea and Vomiting (CINV)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong> <strong>0.5 mg</strong>, <strong>1 hour before chemotherapy</strong>.</li><li><strong>IV:</strong> <strong>0.25 mg</strong> IV over <strong>30 seconds</strong>, <strong>30 minutes before chemotherapy</strong>.</li></ul><h4><strong>Radiotherapy-induced Nausea and Vomiting (RINV)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>IV:</strong> <strong>0.25 mg</strong> IV over <strong>30 seconds</strong>, <strong>30 minutes before each radiation session</strong>.</li></ul><h4><strong>Post-operative Nausea and Vomiting (PONV)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>IV:</strong> <strong>0.075 mg</strong> IV over <strong>10 seconds</strong>, <strong>before anesthesia induction</strong>.</li></ul><h4><strong>Pediatric Dosage (1 month to 17 years)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>IV:</strong> <strong>20 mcg/kg</strong> (maximum <strong>1.5 mg</strong>).</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন।</em></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Paloset injection</strong> has been safely used with <strong>corticosteroids, analgesics, antiemetics, antispasmodics, and anticholinergics</strong>.</li><li>Does <strong>not affect</strong> the antitumor action of <strong>cisplatin, cyclophosphamide, cytarabine, doxorubicin, or mitomycin C</strong>.</li><li>No significant pharmacokinetic interactions with <strong>metoclopramide</strong>.</li><li>In vitro studies show <strong>Paloset does not inhibit CYP1A2, CYP2A6, CYP2B6, CYP2C9, CYP2D6, CYP2E1, or CYP3A4/5</strong>.</li><li><strong>Low potential for clinically significant drug interactions</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to <strong>Palonosetron</strong> or any of its components.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Headache, constipation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: No known fetal risks in animal studies.</li><li><strong>Unknown if excreted in breast milk</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Elderly Patients (&gt;65 years):</strong> No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Children (1 month to 10 years):</strong> <strong>20 mcg/kg IV</strong> (max dose <strong>1.5 mg</strong>).</li><li><strong>Patients with Renal or Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment required.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>No known antidote.</li><li><strong>Supportive care</strong> is recommended.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Anti-emetic drugs</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool, dry place</strong>, <strong>protected from light</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '194_1742462840.webp', 4, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:27:20', '2025-03-20 14:27:20'),
(3681, 'Xemi', 'Gemifloxacin', 50.15, 4, 'tablet', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '320 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xemi 320 mg</strong></p><p>Xemi 320 mg is indicated for the treatment of the following bacterial infections in adults caused by sensitive organisms:</p><ul><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong>: Caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em>, or <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>.</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia (mild to moderate severity)</strong>: Caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> (including multi-drug-resistant strains), <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>, <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>, <em>Chlamydia pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>4-Quinolone preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Xemi 320 mg is a fluoroquinolone antibiotic. It is bactericidal with minimum bactericidal concentrations. Xemi 320 mg acts by inhibiting DNA synthesis through inhibition of bacterial type II topoisomerases, DNA gyrase, and/or topoisomerase IV (TOPO IV), which are essential for bacterial growth. Xemi 320 mg is rapidly absorbed after oral administration and is widely distributed throughout the body. It shows good distribution into target tissues such as the lung (epithelial lining fluid, alveolar macrophages, bronchial tissue) and nasal secretions. Approximately 36% and 61% of the dose is excreted in the urine and feces, respectively, as unchanged drug and metabolites.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong>: 320 mg once daily for 5 days.</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia (mild to moderate severity)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Due to known or suspected <em>S. pneumoniae</em>, <em>H. influenzae</em>, <em>M. pneumoniae</em>, or <em>C. pneumoniae</em> infection: One 320 mg tablet daily for 5 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Due to known or suspected multi-drug-resistant <em>S. pneumoniae</em>, <em>K. pneumoniae</em>, or <em>M. catarrhalis</em> infection: One 320 mg tablet daily for 7 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Xemi 320 mg absorption is significantly reduced when taken with aluminum or magnesium-containing antacids and iron salts. It should be taken at least 2 hours before or 3 hours after these agents.</li><li>It should be taken at least 2 hours before sucralfate administration.</li><li>No clinically significant interactions have been observed with omeprazole, theophylline, digoxin, warfarin, and oral contraceptives.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Xemi 320 mg or other quinolones.</li><li>Patients who have previously suffered tendon damage with fluoroquinolones.</li><li>Xemi 320 mg should not be used in children under 18 years of age.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common adverse events include:</p><ul><li>Abdominal pain, diarrhea, headache, nausea, rash, and vomiting.</li></ul><p>Infrequent side effects:</p><ul><li>Fungal overgrowth, dizziness, insomnia, urticaria, pruritis, and maculopapular erythematous skin rash.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Xemi 320 mg should not be used in pregnant or lactating women. The safety and efficacy of Xemi 320 mg in these populations have not been established.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>For patients with severe renal impairment, adjust the dosage regimen to 160 mg once daily.</li><li>Adequate hydration should be maintained to prevent crystalluria.</li><li>Xemi 320 mg may cause dizziness; avoid operating machinery or engaging in activities requiring mental alertness or coordination.</li><li>Tendinitis and tendon ruptures may occur, particularly in elderly patients or those co-administered with corticosteroids. Discontinue if tendinitis is suspected.</li><li>A small increase in the QTc interval has been observed. Use with caution in patients predisposed to QTc interval prolongation.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>No specific antidote is known. Dialysis does not remove Xemi 320 mg sufficiently. In the event of acute oral overdose, the stomach should be emptied by inducing vomiting or gastric lavage. Treatment is symptomatic, and adequate hydration should be maintained.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: For patients with severe renal dysfunction, adjust the dosage to 160 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Creatinine clearance &gt;40 ml/min: See usual dosage.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Creatinine clearance &lt;40 ml/min: 160 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients on hemodialysis or continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis therapy should receive 160 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment required.</li><li><strong>Elderly patients</strong>: Dose adjustment is not required.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>4-Quinolone preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Xemi 320 mg is a fluoroquinolone antibiotic. It inhibits DNA synthesis by targeting bacterial type II topoisomerases, DNA gyrase, and/or topoisomerase IV, essential for bacterial growth.</p>', 'active', '934_1742462862.jpg', 43, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:27:42', '2025-03-20 14:27:42'),
(3682, 'Ribacee', 'Ribavirin', 35.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '200mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h2><strong>Indications</strong></h2><p><strong>Ribacee</strong>, in combination with <strong>Peginterferon alfa-2a</strong> or <strong>Interferon alfa</strong>, is indicated for the treatment of <strong>chronic hepatitis C (CHC) virus infection</strong> in patients <strong>5 years and older</strong> with <strong>compensated liver disease</strong> who have <strong>not been previously treated</strong> with interferon alfa.</p><ul><li><strong>Ribacee as monotherapy is not effective and must not be used alone.</strong></li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন।</em></p><p><br></p><h2><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h2><p>Ribavirin is a <strong>synthetic nucleoside</strong> with <strong>antiviral properties</strong> against:</p><ul><li><strong>Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV)</strong></li><li><strong>Influenza virus</strong></li><li><strong>Herpes simplex virus</strong></li></ul><p>The exact <strong>mechanism of action</strong> is unclear, but it is believed to:</p><ul><li>Interfere with <strong>viral nucleic acid synthesis</strong> by acting on cellular enzymes.</li><li>Have <strong>mono- and triphosphate derivatives</strong>, responsible for its antiviral activity.</li></ul><h2><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h2><h3><strong>Recommended Treatment Duration:</strong></h3><ul><li>Patients <strong>previously untreated</strong> with <strong>Interferon/Peginterferon</strong>: <strong>24 to 48 weeks</strong>.</li><li><strong>Daily dose:</strong> <strong>800 mg to 1200 mg</strong>, taken <strong>orally in two divided doses (morning and evening)</strong>.</li><li><strong>Genotypes 2 and 3:</strong> No increased response beyond <strong>24 weeks</strong> of treatment.</li></ul><h3><strong>Special Dosage Instructions:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Severe adverse reactions or laboratory abnormalities:</strong> Dosage modification is required.</li><li><strong>Hemolysis risk:</strong> Patients with a <strong>history of cardiovascular disease</strong> require special monitoring and dose adjustments.</li></ul><h3><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Creatinine clearance ≤50 ml/min:</strong> Reduce the <strong>total daily dose</strong> of Ribavirin.</li></ul><h3><strong>Elderly Patients (≥65 years):</strong></h3><ul><li>No sufficient clinical studies on safety and efficacy in combination with interferon alfa or pegylated interferon alfa.</li></ul><h3><strong>Children (&lt;5 years):</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Safety and effectiveness not evaluated</strong> in this age group.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন।</em></p><p><br></p><h2><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></h2><p><strong>Ribacee should not be used with:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Didanosine</strong></li><li><strong>Zidovudine</strong></li><li><strong>Azathioprine</strong></li></ul><h2><strong>Contraindications</strong></h2><p>Ribacee is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in patients with:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to Ribavirin or any excipients.</li><li><strong>Pregnant women</strong> or <strong>male patients whose partners are pregnant</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hemoglobinopathies</strong> (e.g., <strong>thalassemia major, sickle cell anemia</strong>).</li><li><strong>Autoimmune hepatitis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hepatic decompensation</strong> before or during treatment.</li></ul><h2><strong>Side Effects</strong></h2><h3><strong>Most Common Adverse Reaction:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hemolytic anemia</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Other Reported Side Effects (Combination Therapy with Interferon/Peginterferon):</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Flu-like symptoms</strong> (fever, chills, sore throat, muscle aches).</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal issues</strong> (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, constipation, abdominal pain).</li><li><strong>Neurological effects</strong> (anxiety, depression, dizziness, headache).</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular effects</strong> (chest pain, tachycardia).</li><li><strong>Respiratory issues</strong> (cough, dyspnea).</li><li><strong>Hematological effects</strong> (neutropenia, thrombocytopenia).</li></ul><h2><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h2><h3><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Ribavirin must not be used during pregnancy</strong> due to its <strong>teratogenic effects</strong>.</li><li><strong>Male patients and their female partners</strong> must use <strong>two effective forms of contraception</strong> during treatment and <strong>for 6 months post-treatment</strong>.</li><li><strong>Negative pregnancy test required</strong> before initiating therapy.</li><li><strong>Monthly pregnancy tests</strong> must be conducted <strong>during therapy and for 6 months post-treatment</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Unknown if Ribavirin is excreted in human milk</strong>.</li><li>Due to potential risks, <strong>breastfeeding should be discontinued or Ribacee therapy should not be initiated</strong>.</li></ul><h2><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Must be prescribed by a physician experienced in hepatitis C treatment.</strong></li><li><strong>Close monitoring required</strong> due to potential moderate to severe adverse reactions.</li><li><strong>Dose reduction or discontinuation may be necessary</strong> in case of severe reactions.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cardiac patients:Anemia may worsen heart disease</strong>, requiring <strong>pre-treatment evaluation</strong> and <strong>regular monitoring</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Electrocardiogram (ECG) recommended</strong> before starting therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Most common risks:Kidney disease</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Blood disorders (e.g., sickle cell anemia, low hemoglobin levels)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Autoimmune hepatitis</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Diabetes</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Allergic reactions</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Laboratory Monitoring:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Before treatment initiation:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Complete blood count (<strong>CBC</strong>)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Differential count</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Platelet count</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Electrolytes</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Serum creatinine</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Liver function tests (LFTs)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uric acid levels</strong></li><li><strong>During treatment:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Hematological tests:</strong> At <strong>2 and 4 weeks</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Biochemical tests:</strong> At <strong>4 weeks</strong>.</li></ul><h2><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Hepatic viral infections (Hepatitis C).</strong></li></ul><h2><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h2><ul><li>Store at a <strong>temperature not exceeding 30°C</strong> in a <strong>dry place</strong>.</li><li><strong>Protect from light</strong>.</li><li><strong>Keep out of reach of children</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1290_1742463142.jpg', 63, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:32:22', '2025-03-20 14:32:22'),
(3683, 'Xemimax', 'Cefepime Hydrochloride', 300.00, 1, 'x 500 mg vial', '500 mg vial', 'IV Injection', '500 mg/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Xemimax 500 mg/vial (Cefepime)</h3><p>Cefepime is indicated for the treatment of the following infections caused by susceptible strains of microorganisms:</p><ul><li><strong>Pneumonia (moderate to severe)</strong>: Caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> (including cases associated with concurrent bacteremia), <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Enterobacter</em> species.</li><li><strong>Febrile Neutropenia</strong>: As monotherapy for empiric treatment in febrile neutropenic patients.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated and Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</strong> (including pyelonephritis): Caused by <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>: Caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (methicillin-susceptible strains only) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections</strong> (used in combination with metronidazole): Caused by <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Viridans group streptococci</em>, <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, <em>Enterobacter</em> species, or <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Fourth Generation Cephalosporins</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Cephalosporins are <strong>bactericidal</strong> and act by disrupting the synthesis of the peptidoglycan layer of bacterial cell walls. This is essential for maintaining the structural integrity of the cell wall, especially in Gram-positive organisms. The final transpeptidation step in peptidoglycan synthesis is facilitated by <strong>penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs)</strong>.</p><p><br></p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xemimax 500 mg/vial</h3><ul><li><strong>Moderate to Severe Pneumonia</strong>: 1-2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Empiric therapy for febrile neutropenic patients</strong>: 2 gm IV every 8 hours for 7 days.</li><li><strong>Mild to Moderate Uncomplicated or Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</strong>: 0.5-1 gm IV/IM every 12 hours for 7-10 days.</li><li><strong>Severe Uncomplicated or Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</strong>: 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Moderate to Severe Uncomplicated Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>: 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections (with metronidazole)</strong>: 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 7-10 days.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Xemimax 500 mg/vial</h3><ul><li><strong>Aminoglycosides</strong>: Careful monitoring of renal function is required when high doses of aminoglycosides are given with cefepime due to the increased potential for nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity.</li><li><strong>Nephrotoxicity</strong>: Caution when given with potent diuretics like furosemide.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with a history of <strong>immediate hypersensitivity reactions</strong> to cefepime, cephalosporins, penicillin, or other beta-lactam antibiotics.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Xemimax 500 mg/vial</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity reactions</strong>: Including anaphylaxis, rash, and fever.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Diarrhea, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Renal</strong>: Elevation in serum creatinine and blood urea nitrogen (BUN).</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: Cefepime should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Cefepime is excreted in breast milk in very low concentrations (0.5 pg/ml). Caution should be exercised when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Prescribing <strong>Cefepime</strong> without a proven bacterial infection or prophylactic indication increases the risk of developing drug-resistant bacteria.</li><li>Prolonged use may lead to overgrowth of non-susceptible microorganisms.</li><li><strong>Prothrombin time</strong> should be monitored in patients at risk, especially those with renal or hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Caution</strong> in individuals with a history of gastrointestinal disease, particularly colitis.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Xemimax 500 mg/vial</h3><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Encephalopathy (confusion, hallucinations, stupor, coma), myoclonus, seizures, and neuromuscular excitability.</li><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: Supportive treatment and, in the presence of renal insufficiency, <strong>hemodialysis</strong> (not peritoneal dialysis) is recommended.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store according to the <strong>expiry date</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li><li>Dispensed <strong>only on a physician’s prescription</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Use In Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use (2 months to 16 years)</strong>: Maximum dose should not exceed the recommended adult dose. The usual recommended dosage for pediatric patients up to 40 kg for uncomplicated urinary tract infections, uncomplicated skin infections, and pneumonia is 50 mg/kg/dose, administered every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Risk of serious adverse events, including encephalopathy, myoclonus, and seizures, is greater in elderly patients with renal insufficiency. Renal function should be monitored.</li><li><strong>Impaired Hepatic Function</strong>: No dose adjustment necessary.</li><li><strong>Impaired Renal Function</strong>: Dose adjustment required for patients with a creatinine clearance &lt;60 ml/min.</li></ul><h3>Reconstitution</h3><ul><li><strong>For IV</strong>: Cefepime is compatible with Sterile Water for Injection. It can be infused with 0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection, Dextrose 5%, Lactated Ringers, etc.</li><li><strong>For IM</strong>: Compatible with Sterile Water for Injection, Sodium Chloride, or Lidocaine Hydrochloride.</li></ul>', 'active', '1745_1742463177.jpg', 61, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:32:57', '2025-05-07 19:54:37');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3684, 'Xemimax', 'Cefepime Hydrochloride', 550.00, 1, 'x 1 gm vial', '1 gm vial', 'IV Injection', '1 gm/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xemimax 1 gm/vial (Cefepime)</strong></p><p>Cefepime is indicated for the treatment of the following infections caused by susceptible strains of microorganisms:</p><ul><li><strong>Pneumonia (moderate to severe)</strong>: caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> (including cases associated with concurrent bacteremia), <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Enterobacter</em> species.</li><li><strong>Febrile Neutropenia</strong>: Cefepime as monotherapy is indicated for empiric treatment of febrile neutropenic patients.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated and Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</strong> (including pyelonephritis): caused by <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, including cases associated with concurrent bacteremia.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>: caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (methicillin-susceptible strains only) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections</strong> (used in combination with metronidazole): caused by <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>viridians group streptococci</em>, <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, <em>Enterobacter</em> species, or <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Fourth-generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Cephalosporins are bactericidal and have the same mode of action as beta-lactam antibiotics (e.g., penicillins). They disrupt bacterial cell wall synthesis by targeting the peptidoglycan layer, important for the structural integrity of the cell wall, especially in Gram-positive organisms.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xemimax 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Cefepime should be administered intravenously over approximately 30 minutes.</p><ul><li><strong>Moderate to Severe Pneumonia</strong> due to <em>S. pneumoniae</em>, <em>P. aeruginosa</em>, <em>K. pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Enterobacter</em> species: 1-2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Empiric therapy for febrile neutropenic patients</strong>: 2 gm IV every 8 hours for 7 days (or until resolution of neutropenia).</li><li><strong>Mild to Moderate Uncomplicated or Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</strong> (including pyelonephritis) due to <em>E. coli</em>, <em>K. pneumoniae</em>, or <em>P. mirabilis</em>: 0.5-1 gm IV/IM every 12 hours for 7-10 days.</li><li><strong>Severe Uncomplicated or Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</strong> due to <em>E. coli</em> or <em>K. pneumoniae</em>: 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Moderate to Severe Uncomplicated Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong> due to <em>S. aureus</em> or <em>S. pyogenes</em>: 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections</strong> (with metronidazole) caused by <em>E. coli</em>, <em>viridians group streptococci</em>, <em>P. aeruginosa</em>, <em>K. pneumoniae</em>, <em>Enterobacter</em> species, or <em>B. fragilis</em>: 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 7-10 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Xemimax 1 gm/vial:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Aminoglycosides</strong>: If high doses of aminoglycosides are given with Cefepime, renal function should be monitored closely due to the increased risk of nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity.</li><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: Concomitant use with potent diuretics (e.g., furosemide) may increase nephrotoxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Immediate hypersensitivity reactions to cefepime, cephalosporins, penicillin, or other beta-lactam antibiotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Can include hypersensitivity reactions, gastrointestinal disturbances, and possible renal issues. Specific side effects depend on the patient\'s condition and dosing.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women, so it should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Cefepime is excreted in human breast milk in very low concentrations. Caution is recommended when administering to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid prescribing Cefepime for bacterial infections that are unproven or unlikely, to prevent the development of drug-resistant bacteria.</li><li>Prolonged use can result in the overgrowth of non-susceptible microorganisms. Monitoring is necessary.</li><li><strong>Prothrombin Time</strong>: Prolonged use, particularly in patients with poor nutritional status or liver/renal impairment, may lower prothrombin activity.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal Disease</strong>: Use with caution in patients with a history of colitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose include <strong>encephalopathy</strong> (confusion, hallucinations, stupor, coma), <strong>myoclonus</strong>, <strong>seizures</strong>, and <strong>neuromuscular excitability</strong>.</li><li>In case of overdose, supportive treatment should be provided, and hemodialysis is recommended for patients with renal insufficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Keep the product out of reach of children and only use before the expiration date.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: The maximum dose should not exceed the recommended adult dose. Dosing for pediatric patients is usually 50 mg/kg/dose every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Elderly patients with renal insufficiency may experience life-threatening reactions (encephalopathy, seizures), so careful dose adjustments are necessary.</li><li><strong>Impaired Renal Function</strong>: Dosing should be adjusted for patients with reduced renal function (creatinine clearance &lt;60 ml/min).</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution and Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>For IV, reconstitute with Sterile Water for Injection. The solution can be infused with several compatible fluids, including Sodium Chloride and Dextrose solutions.</li><li>For IM, use compatible diluents such as Sterile Water for Injection, Sodium Chloride, or Dextrose.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Fourth-generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Disrupts bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), making it effective against a broad range of bacteria.</li></ul>', 'active', '936_1742463302.jpg', 61, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:35:02', '2025-05-05 18:08:37'),
(3685, 'Neosuxa', 'Suxamethonium Chloride', 17.25, 1, 'bottle', '2 ml ampoule', 'Injection', '100 mgper2 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h2><strong>Indications</strong></h2><p><strong>Neosuxa</strong> is a <strong>short-acting depolarizing neuromuscular blocking agent</strong> used in anesthesia as a <strong>muscle relaxant</strong> to:</p><ul><li><strong>Facilitate endotracheal intubation</strong></li><li><strong>Aid in mechanical ventilation</strong></li><li><strong>Assist in a wide range of surgical and obstetric procedures</strong></li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন।</em></p><p><br></p><h2><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h2><p><strong>Suxamethonium</strong> is a <strong>depolarizing neuromuscular blocker</strong> that mimics <strong>acetylcholine</strong> at the neuromuscular junction. It binds to <strong>nicotinic acetylcholine receptors</strong>, causing:</p><ul><li><strong>Opening of sodium channels</strong> → <strong>Sodium influx</strong></li><li><strong>Depolarization of the motor-end plate</strong></li><li><strong>Calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum</strong>, leading to <strong>muscle fasciculation</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></h3><ul><li>Unlike acetylcholine, <strong>Suxamethonium is hydrolyzed by butyrylcholinesterase</strong> (not acetylcholinesterase).</li><li>Since <strong>butyrylcholinesterase hydrolysis is slower</strong>, the muscle <strong>remains depolarized</strong>, preventing further contraction.</li><li>As <strong>calcium is reabsorbed</strong>, <strong>muscle relaxation occurs</strong>, leading to <strong>flaccidity rather than tetany</strong>.</li></ul><h2><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h2><p><strong>Suxamethonium Chloride</strong> is administered via <strong>bolus Intravenous (IV) or Intramuscular (IM) injection</strong>.</p><h3><strong>Adults:</strong></h3><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Endotracheal intubation:</strong> <strong>1 mg/kg IV</strong>Onset: <strong>30-60 seconds</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Duration: <strong>2-6 minutes</strong></li><li><strong>Maintenance dose:</strong> <strong>50%-100% of the initial dose</strong>, every <strong>5-10 minutes</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Continuous IV infusion:Dilution:</strong> <strong>0.1%-0.2% solution</strong> in <strong>5% glucose</strong> or <strong>isotonic saline</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Infusion rate:</strong> <strong>2.5 to 4 mg per minute</strong> (adjust based on response).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Total dose should not exceed 500 mg per hour</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Children:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Infants (&lt;1 year):</strong> <strong>2 mg/kg IV</strong></li><li><strong>Older children:</strong> <strong>1 mg/kg IV</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IM administration:Infants:</strong> <strong>4-5 mg/kg IM</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Older children:</strong> <strong>4 mg/kg IM</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Onset:</strong> <strong>Within 3 minutes</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maximum total dose:</strong> <strong>150 mg</strong></li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন।</em></p><p><br></p><h2><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></h2><p><strong>Neosuxa’s effects may be prolonged when used with drugs that reduce plasma cholinesterase activity, including:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Antihypertensives:</strong> Trimetaphan</li><li><strong>Anticholinesterase agents:</strong> Neostigmine, Pyridostigmine, Physostigmine</li><li><strong>Cytotoxic drugs:</strong> Cyclophosphamide, Mechlorethamine, Triethylene-melamine</li><li><strong>Psychiatric drugs:</strong> Promazine, Chlorpromazine</li><li><strong>Anesthetic agents and opioids:</strong> Ketamine, Morphine, Pethidine, Pancuronium</li></ul><h2><strong>Contraindications</strong></h2><p><strong>Neosuxa is contraindicated in patients with:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity to Suxamethonium</strong></li><li><strong>Atypical plasma cholinesterase activity</strong> (inherited disorder)</li><li><strong>Major trauma recovery</strong></li><li><strong>Severe burns recovery</strong></li><li><strong>Neurological deficits with acute major muscle wasting</strong></li><li><strong>Pre-existing hyperkalemia</strong> (risk of serious <strong>cardiac arrhythmias</strong> and <strong>cardiac arrest</strong>)</li><li><strong>Open eye injuries</strong> (due to increased intraocular pressure)</li><li><strong>Neonates and premature infants</strong></li></ul><h2><strong>Side Effects</strong></h2><h3><strong>Cardiovascular:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Bradycardia</strong>, <strong>tachycardia</strong></li><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>, <strong>hypotension</strong></li><li><strong>Arrhythmias</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Respiratory:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Bronchospasm</strong></li><li><strong>Prolonged respiratory depression and apnea</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Musculoskeletal:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Muscle fasciculation</strong></li><li><strong>Post-operative muscle pain</strong></li><li><strong>Myoglobinemia</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Other Effects:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hyperthermia</strong></li><li><strong>Increased intraocular pressure</strong></li><li><strong>Increased intragastric pressure</strong></li><li><strong>Rash, excessive salivation</strong></li></ul><h2><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Suxamethonium does not easily cross the placental barrier</strong>.</li><li>Should <strong>only be used in pregnancy if the benefits outweigh the risks</strong>.</li></ul><h2><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Administer only under the supervision of an experienced anesthetist.</strong></li><li><strong>Facilities for immediate endotracheal intubation and artificial respiration must be available.</strong></li><li><strong>Elderly patients</strong> are more prone to <strong>cardiac arrhythmias</strong>, especially if taking <strong>digitalis-like drugs</strong>.</li></ul><h2><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Depolarizing muscle relaxants</strong></li></ul><h2><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Store at 2°C-8°C (Refrigerated).</strong></li><li><strong>Do not freeze.</strong></li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '917_1742463433.webp', 17, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:37:13', '2025-03-20 14:37:13'),
(3686, 'Xemocid Gold', 'Amino acids, Glucose & Electrolytes', 1.00, 1, 'x 500 ml bottle', '500 ml bottle', 'IV Infusion', '7%+10%', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you requested with the headings in bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Xemocid Gold 7%+10%</strong></p><p>This is indicated as a source of amino acids, glucose, and electrolytes in adult and pediatric patients needing IV nutrition. This is particularly suitable for patients with basal amino acid requirements.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each 100 ml contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Essential Amino Acids</strong>:</li><li>L-Isoleucine 0.390 gm</li><li>L-Leucine 0.530 gm</li><li>L-Lysine Hydrochloride 0.390 gm</li><li>L-Methionine 0.190 gm</li><li>L-Phenylalanine 0.550 gm</li><li>L-Threonine 0.300 gm</li><li>L-Tryptophan 0.100 gm</li><li>L-Valine 0.430 gm</li><li>L-Histidine 0.240 gm</li><li>L-Tyrosine 0.050 gm</li><li><strong>Non-Essential Amino Acids</strong>:</li><li>L-Arginine 0.330 gm</li><li>L-Aspartic Acid 0.410 gm</li><li>L-Glutamic Acid 0.900 gm</li><li>L-Alanine 0.300 gm</li><li>L-Cystine 0.140 gm</li><li>Glycine (Aminoacetic Acid) 0.210 gm</li><li>L-Proline 0.810 gm</li><li>L-Serine 0.750 gm</li><li><strong>Carbohydrate</strong>:</li><li>Anhydrous Glucose 10.00 gm</li><li><strong>Electrolytes</strong>:</li><li>Sodium (Na+) 50.0 mmol/L</li><li>Potassium (K+) 20.0 mmol/L</li><li>Calcium (Ca++) 2.5 mmol/L</li><li>Magnesium (Mg++) 1.5 mmol/L</li><li>Chloride (Cl-) 32.0 mmol/L</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Parenteral nutritional preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>A crystalline amino acid solution provides crystalline amino acids to promote protein synthesis and wound healing, and to reduce the rate of endogenous protein catabolism. Amino acids given by central venous infusion in combination with concentrated dextrose, electrolytes, vitamins, trace metals, and ancillary fat supplements constitute total parenteral nutrition (TPN). Amino acids can also be administered by peripheral vein with dextrose and maintenance electrolytes.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xemocid Gold 7%+10%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: The nitrogen requirement for maintenance of body protein mass depends on the condition of the patient (nutritional state and degree of metabolic stress). The requirements are:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0.1-0.15 g nitrogen/kg body weight/day (no or minor metabolic stress and normal nutritional state)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0.15-0.2 g nitrogen/kg body weight/day (moderate metabolic stress with or without malnutrition)</li><li>Up to 0.2-0.25 g nitrogen/kg body weight/day (severe catabolism as in burns, sepsis, and trauma)</li><li>The dosage range 0.1-0.25 g nitrogen/kg body weight/day corresponds to 11-27 mL Amino Acid IV Infusion with 10% Glucose and Electrolytes /kg body weight/day, respectively.</li><li>In obese patients, the dose should be based on the estimated ideal weight. Up to 1000-2000 mL of Amino Acid IV Infusion with 10% Glucose and Electrolytes may be infused IV per 24 hrs. The infusion should be administered slowly, at a rate not exceeding 1000 mL in 6 hrs (approximately 2.8 mL/min).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In patients with basal amino acid requirements, the less concentrated Amino Acid IV Infusion with 10% Glucose and Electrolytes may be used.</li><li><strong>Infants and Children</strong>: In children and infants, a maximal rate of infusion of 30 mL Amino Acid IV Infusion with 10% Glucose and Electrolytes/kg body weight/day is recommended, with a stepwise increase in the rate of administration during the 1st week of treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xemocid Gold 7%+10%</strong></p><p>At the recommended dosage, the amino acid solution has no pharmacological effects and is not expected to interact with other medications.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with inborn errors of amino acid metabolism</li><li>Severe liver dysfunction</li><li>Severe uremia when dialysis facilities are not available</li><li>Due to the content of glucose, this drug is contraindicated in patients with hyperosmolar nonketotic diabetic coma.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xemocid Gold 7%+10%</strong></p><ul><li>Nausea occurs rarely.</li><li>Transient increases in liver tests during IV nutrition have been reported.</li><li>Hypersensitivity reactions have been reported with amino acid solutions.</li><li>Thrombophlebitis may occur when peripheral veins are used.</li><li>Hyperphenylalaninemia may occur in severely ill, premature infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>No specific studies have been performed to assess the safety of Amino Acids + Electrolytes + Glucose in pregnancy and lactation. The prescriber should consider the benefit/risk relationship before administering this combination to pregnant or breastfeeding women.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>This IV infusion is accompanied by increased urinary excretion of the trace elements copper and, in particular, zinc, which should be considered when dosing trace elements, particularly during long-term IV nutrition.</li><li>Hyperphenylalaninemia has been noted in severely ill premature infants. In these patients, monitoring of the phenylalanine level is recommended and the infusion rate adjusted as needed.</li><li>This should be used with caution in patients with diabetes mellitus, severe heart failure, or renal function issues in combination with fluid restrictions or oliguria/anuria from other causes.</li><li>In patients with hyperglycemia, administration of exogenous insulin might be necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xemocid Gold 7%+10%</strong></p><p>If Amino Acid, Glucose &amp; Electrolytes combination is administered at a higher rate than recommended, there is an increased risk of:</p><ul><li>Nausea, vomiting, and sweating</li><li>Thrombophlebitis when peripheral veins are used</li><li>Osmotic diuresis with dehydration</li><li>Hyperglycemia-related symptoms.</li><li>In case of symptoms due to overdose, the infusion should be slowed down or discontinued.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Protect from light and store between 15° C to 25° C. Avoid freezing.</p>', 'active', '1172_1742463455.jpg', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:37:35', '2025-05-05 18:09:05'),
(3687, 'Xenapro', 'Naproxen Sodium', 5.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information you provided in a more structured format with headings and bold text for clarity:</p><p><strong>Indications of Xenapro 250 mg</strong></p><p> Naproxen is indicated for the relief of signs and symptoms of:</p><ul><li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li><li>Osteoarthritis</li><li>Ankylosing spondylitis</li><li>Juvenile arthritis</li><li>Tendonitis</li><li>Bursitis</li><li>Acute gout</li></ul><p>It is also indicated for:</p><ul><li>The management of primary dysmenorrhea</li><li>Pain relief</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Naproxen is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) with anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic properties. It is rapidly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and achieves 95% bioavailability.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xenapro 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Naproxen Tablet:Rheumatoid arthritis, osteoarthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis:</strong> The usual dose is 500-1000 mg daily in two divided doses after meals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Management of pain, primary dysmenorrhea, acute tendonitis, and bursitis:</strong> Recommended starting dose is 500 mg, followed by 500 mg every 12 hours or 250 mg every 6-8 hours. The initial total daily dose should not exceed 1250 mg, and thereafter, it should not exceed 1000 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute gout:</strong> Recommended starting dose is 750 mg, followed by 250 mg every 8 hours until the attack subsides.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Naproxen Suspension:For Juvenile rheumatoid arthritis:</strong> The usual dose for children over 2 years is 10 mg/kg/day, given as two divided doses at 12-hour intervals. Therapy is not recommended for children under 2 years of age.</li><li><strong>Naproxen Gel:</strong> Apply 2-6 times a day as required. It is not recommended for use in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xenapro 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ACE inhibitors:</strong> Diminish the antihypertensive effect of ACE inhibitors.</li><li><strong>Antacids &amp; Sucralfate:</strong> Delay the absorption of Naproxen.</li><li><strong>Aspirin:</strong> Increases adverse effects.</li><li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> Reduce the natriuretic effect of Furosemide and Thiazides.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate:</strong> Enhances the toxicity of Methotrexate.</li><li><strong>Warfarin:</strong> Increases the risk of GI bleeding.</li><li><strong>Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors (SSRIs):</strong> Increase the risk of GI bleeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Naproxen is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Naproxen.</li><li>It should not be given to patients who have experienced asthma, urticaria, or allergic-type reactions after taking aspirin or other NSAIDs.</li><li>It is contraindicated for the treatment of perioperative pain in the setting of coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xenapro 250 mg</strong></p><p> Most frequently reported side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Heartburn, abdominal pain, nausea, diarrhea, dyspepsia.</li><li><strong>Central Nervous System:</strong> Headache, vertigo, drowsiness.</li><li><strong>Dermatological:</strong> Pruritus (itching), purpura.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular:</strong> Edema, palpitation.</li><li><strong>Others:</strong> Visual disturbances, hearing disturbances.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Naproxen has a <strong>US FDA pregnancy category of C</strong>. It should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C temperature, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Naproxen is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) with anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic properties. It is rapidly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and achieves 95% bioavailability.</p><p>This provides a well-organized summary of the medication Xenapro 250 mg (Naproxen). If you need more details or have any questions, feel free to ask!</p>', 'active', '244_1742463717.png', 6, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:41:57', '2025-03-20 14:41:57'),
(3688, 'Neurovan', 'Pregabalin', 15.00, 6, 'Capsule', '6 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Neurovan film-coated tablet is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Neuropathic pain associated with diabetic peripheral neuropathy</li><li>Postherpetic neuralgia</li><li>Adjunctive therapy for the treatment of partial-onset seizures in patients 1 month of age and older</li><li>Fibromyalgia</li><li>Neuropathic pain associated with spinal cord injury</li></ul><p>Neurovan controlled release tablet is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Neuropathic pain associated with diabetic peripheral neuropathy</li><li>Postherpetic neuralgia</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Pregabalin is a structural derivative of the inhibitory neurotransmitter gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA). It does not bind directly to GABAA, GABAB, or benzodiazepine receptors. Pregabalin binds with high affinity to the alpha2-delta site (an auxiliary subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels) in central nervous system tissues. Although the mechanism of action of Pregabalin has not been fully elucidated, results in animal studies suggest that binding to the alpha2-delta subunit may be involved in Pregabalin\'s anti-nociceptive and antiseizure effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage</strong></p><p> Neuropathic pain associated with diabetic peripheral neuropathy in adults (DPN): The maximum recommended dose of Pregabalin is 100 mg three times a day (300 mg/day) in patients with creatinine clearance of at least 60 ml/min. Dosing should begin at 50 mg three times a day (150 mg/day). The dose may be increased to 300 mg/day within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability. Begin dosing of Pregabalin CR capsule at 165 mg once daily and increase to 330 mg once daily within 1 week based on individual patient response and tolerability. The maximum recommended dose of Pregabalin CR capsule is 330 mg once daily.</p><p>Postherpetic neuralgia in adults (PHN): The recommended dose of Pregabalin is 75 to 150 mg two times a day or 50 to 100 mg three times a day (150 to 300 mg/day) in patients with creatinine clearance of at least 60 ml/min. Dosing should begin at 75 mg two times a day or 50 mg three times a day (150 mg/day). The dose may be increased to 300 mg/day within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability. Patients who do not experience sufficient pain relief following 2 to 4 weeks of treatment with 300 mg/day and who are able to tolerate Pregabalin, may be treated with up to 300 mg two times a day or 200 mg three times a day (600 mg/day).</p><p>Begin dosing of Pregabalin CR capsule at 165 mg once daily and increase to 330 mg once daily within 1 week based on individual patient response and tolerability. Patients who do not experience sufficient pain relief following 2 to 4 weeks of treatment with 330 mg once daily and who are able to tolerate Pregabalin CR capsule, may be treated with up to 660 mg once daily. In view of the dose-dependent adverse reactions and the higher rate of treatment discontinuation due to adverse reactions, dosing above 330 mg/day should be reserved only for those patients who have ongoing pain and are tolerating 330 mg daily. The maximum recommended dose of Pregabalin CR capsule is 660 mg once daily.</p><p>Management of fibromyalgia in adults: The recommended dose of Pregabalin is 300 to 450 mg/day. Dosing should begin at 75 mg two times a day (150 mg/day). The dose may be increased to 150 mg two times a day (300 mg/day) within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability. Patients who do not experience sufficient benefit with 300 mg/day may be further increased to 225 mg two times a day (450 mg/day).</p><p>Neuropathic pain associated with spinal cord injury in adults: The recommended dose range of Pregabalin is 150 to 600 mg/day. The recommended starting dose is 75 mg two times a day (150 mg/day). The dose may be increased to 150 mg two times a day (300 mg/day) within 1 week based on efficacy and tolerability. Patients who do not experience sufficient pain relief after 2 to 3 weeks of treatment with 150 mg two times a day and who tolerate Pregabalin may be treated with up to 300 mg two times a day.</p><p><strong>Conversion from Pregabalin capsules to Pregabalin CR capsule tablet</strong>:</p><p> When switching from Pregabalin capsules to Pregabalin CR capsule tablet on the day of the switch, instruct patients to take their morning dose of Pregabalin capsule as prescribed and initiate Pregabalin CR capsule therapy after an evening meal.</p><p><strong>Pregabalin tablet total daily dose (dosed 2 or 3 times daily)</strong></p><p> Pregabalin CR capsule dose (dosed once a day):</p><p> 75 mg/daily: 82.5 mg/day</p><p> 150 mg/daily: 165 mg/day</p><p> 225 mg/daily: 247.5 mg/day</p><p> 300 mg/daily: 330 mg/day</p><p> 450 mg/daily: 495 mg/day</p><p> 600 mg/daily: 660 mg/day</p><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p> Route of administration: Pregabalin is taken orally. It can be taken with or without food. Pregabalin CR tablet should be administered after an evening meal. It should be swallowed whole and should not be split, crushed, or chewed. If patients miss taking their dose of Pregabalin CR after an evening meal, then they should take their usual dose of Pregabalin CR prior to bedtime following a snack. If they miss taking the dose of Pregabalin CR prior to bedtime, then they should take their usual dose of Pregabalin CR following a morning meal. If they miss taking the dose of Pregabalin CR following the morning meal, then they should take their usual dose of Pregabalin CR at the usual time that evening following an evening meal. When discontinuing both Pregabalin and Pregabalin CR, it should be gradually tapered over a minimum of 1 week.</p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> Drug interaction with medication: Neurovan is unlikely to be involved in significant pharmacokinetic drug interactions.</p><p> Drug interaction with food and others: Not applicable.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Pregabalin is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Pregabalin or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Most common side effects in adults are dizziness, somnolence, dry mouth, edema, blurred vision, weight gain, and thinking abnormal (primarily difficulty with concentration/attention). Most common side effects in pediatric patients for the treatment of partial onset seizures are increased weight and increased appetite.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies with pregabalin in pregnant women. Pregnant women should be advised of the potential risk to a fetus. Small amounts of pregabalin have been detected in the milk of lactating women. Because of the potential risk of tumorigenicity, breastfeeding is not recommended during treatment with pregabalin.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Angioedema (e.g., swelling of the throat, head, and neck) can occur and may be associated with life-threatening respiratory compromise requiring emergency treatment. Neurovan should be discontinued immediately in these cases. Neurovan should also be discontinued immediately if hypersensitivity reactions (e.g., hives, dyspnea, and wheezing) occur. Antiepileptic drugs, including Neurovan, increase the risk of suicidal thoughts or behavior. Respiratory depression may occur with Neurovan when used with concomitant CNS depressants or in the setting of underlying respiratory impairment. Patients need to be monitored and dosage adjusted as appropriate. Neurovan may cause dizziness and somnolence and impair patients\' ability to drive or operate machinery. Increased seizure frequency or other adverse reactions may occur if Neurovan is rapidly discontinued. Neurovan should be withdrawn gradually over a minimum of 1 week. Neurovan may cause peripheral edema. Caution should be exercised when coadministering Neurovan and thiazolidinedione antidiabetic agents.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> Use in children and adolescents: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established for the management of neuropathic pain associated with diabetic peripheral neuropathy, postherpetic neuralgia, neuropathic pain associated with spinal cord injury, and fibromyalgia. In case of adjunctive therapy for partial onset seizures, safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients below the age of 1 month have not been established. The safety and effectiveness of Neurovan extended-release tablet in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p> In case of overdose with Neurovan, signs and symptoms are reduced consciousness, depression/anxiety, confusional state, agitation, and restlessness. Seizures and heart block have also been reported. There is no specific antidote. If indicated, elimination of unabsorbed drug may be attempted by emesis or gastric lavage; usual precautions should be observed to maintain the airway. General supportive care of the patient is indicated including monitoring of vital signs and observation of the clinical status of the patient.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs, Primary anti-epileptic drugs</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a cool &amp; dry place (below 30°C), protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Chemical Structure</strong></p><p> Molecular Formula: C8H17NO2</p><p> Chemical Structure: Chemical Structure of Pregabalin</p><p><strong>Common Questions about Neurovan 50 mg Capsule</strong></p><p> What is Neurovan 50 mg Capsule?</p><p> Neurovan 50 mg Capsule is an anticonvulsant and is considered to be the first-line of treatment for neurotic pain, shingles, seizures, and fibromyalgia.</p><p>How long do I need to use Neurovan 50 mg Capsule before I see improvement in my condition?</p><p> Neurovan 50 mg Capsule is to be taken until you see an improvement in your health conditions.</p><p>At what frequency do I need to use Neurovan 50 mg Capsule?</p><p> Neurovan 50 mg Capsule should be taken in the dosage as prescribed by the doctor.</p><p>Should I use Neurovan 50 mg Capsule on an empty stomach, before food, or after food?</p><p> Neurovan 50 mg Capsule should be taken after food in a prescribed dosage.</p><p>What are the instructions for the storage and disposal of Neurovan 50 mg Capsule?</p><p> Neurovan 50 mg Capsule should be kept in a cool, dry place and in its original packaging. Make sure this medication remains unreachable to children and pets.</p><p>Does Neurovan 50 mg Capsule work in the same way for each disease?</p><p> No, Neurovan 50 mg Capsule works in different ways for different diseases.</p><p>How long do I need to take Neurovan 50 mg Capsule?</p><p> The duration of Neurovan 50 mg Capsule depends on the condition for which you are consuming this medicine. It is advised to consult the doctor before consuming Neurovan 50 mg Capsule.</p><p>Is it necessary to continue taking Neurovan 50 mg Capsule even if I feel fine?</p><p> Yes, you should not stop taking Neurovan 50 mg Capsule even if you feel fine. Consult your doctor before stopping Neurovan 50 mg Capsule.</p><p>Can I take Diazepam and Neurovan 50 mg Capsule together?</p><p> Yes, Neurovan 50 mg Capsule and Diazepam can be used together.</p><p>Can the use of Neurovan 50 mg Capsule cause weight gain?</p><p> Yes, Neurovan 50 mg Capsule may cause weight gain because it increases your hunger. Consult a dietician if you have any further concerns to keep your weight stable.</p><p><strong>Quick Tips</strong></p><ul><li>Neurovan 50 mg Capsule should be taken as per the dose and duration prescribed by your doctor.</li><li>Neurovan 50 mg Capsule may cause sleepiness. Do not drive or do anything requiring concentration until you know how it affects you.</li><li>Neurovan 50 mg Capsule may cause blurring or loss of vision. Inform your doctor immediately if you notice any vision changes.</li><li>Inform your doctor if you develop any unusual changes in mood or behavior, new or worsening depression, or suicidal thoughts or behavior.</li><li>Do not stop taking Neurovan 50 mg Capsule suddenly without talking to your doctor as it may worsen your underlying condition.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '341_1742463768.jpg', 29, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:42:48', '2025-03-20 14:42:48'),
(3689, 'Xenim', 'Cefepime Hydrochloride', 301.13, 1, 'x 500 mg vial', '500 mg vial', 'IV Injection', '500 mg/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the detailed information regarding <strong>Xenim 500 mg/vial (Cefepime)</strong>:</p><h3>Indications:</h3><p><strong>Cefepime</strong> is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by susceptible microorganisms, including:</p><ol><li><strong>Pneumonia (moderate to severe)</strong>: Caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Enterobacter</em> species.</li><li><strong>Febrile Neutropenia</strong>: Empiric treatment of febrile neutropenic patients.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated and Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</strong>: Caused by <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>: Caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (methicillin-susceptible strains only) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections</strong>: Used with metronidazole to treat infections caused by <em>E. coli</em>, <em>P. aeruginosa</em>, <em>K. pneumoniae</em>, <em>Enterobacter</em>, and <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>.</li></ol><h3>Therapeutic Class:</h3><ul><li><strong>Fourth-generation Cephalosporins</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology:</h3><ul><li><strong>Cephalosporins</strong> are bactericidal, disrupting bacterial cell wall synthesis by interfering with the peptidoglycan layer, especially in Gram-positive organisms. The final transpeptidation step is facilitated by penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs).</li></ul><h3>Dosage and Administration:</h3><ul><li><strong>IV administration</strong>: Cefepime should be administered over approximately 30 minutes.</li><li><strong>Moderate to Severe Pneumonia</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Febrile Neutropenia</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 gm IV every 8 hours for 7 days or until resolution of neutropenia.</li><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mild to moderate: 0.5-1 gm IV/IM every 12 hours for 7-10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe: 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections</strong> (with metronidazole):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 gm IV every 12 hours for 7-10 days.</li></ul><h3>Interaction:</h3><ul><li><strong>Renal function</strong> should be monitored when administered with aminoglycosides due to the increased risk of nephrotoxicity.</li><li>Cefepime can interact with potent diuretics like <strong>furosemide</strong>, increasing nephrotoxicity.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications:</h3><ul><li>Immediate hypersensitivity reactions to <strong>cephalosporins</strong>, <strong>penicillins</strong>, or <strong>beta-lactam antibiotics</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects:</h3><ul><li>Common side effects include <strong>allergic reactions</strong>, <strong>gastrointestinal disturbances</strong>, <strong>skin rashes</strong>, and <strong>elevated liver enzymes</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</h3><ul><li><strong>Category B</strong>: Cefepime should be used during pregnancy only when clearly needed. It is excreted in <strong>breast milk</strong> in very low concentrations, so caution is advised during breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</h3><ul><li>Prolonged use may result in <strong>overgrowth of non-susceptible microorganisms</strong>.</li><li><strong>Prothrombin time</strong> should be monitored in patients at risk (e.g., those with <strong>renal or hepatic impairment</strong> or poor nutritional state).</li><li>Care should be taken with patients who have a history of <strong>gastrointestinal disease</strong>, especially <strong>colitis</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Overdose:</h3><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose include <strong>encephalopathy</strong> (confusion, hallucinations, stupor, coma), <strong>myoclonus</strong>, <strong>seizures</strong>, and <strong>neuromuscular excitability</strong>. Hemodialysis is recommended for patients with <strong>renal insufficiency</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions:</h3><ul><li>Store according to the manufacturer\'s instructions. <strong>Keep out of the reach of children</strong> and use before the expiration date.</li></ul><h3>Special Populations:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Maximum dose should not exceed the adult dose. Typical dose for pediatric patients up to 40 kg is <strong>50 mg/kg/dose</strong> every 12 hours (or 8 hours for febrile neutropenic patients).</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Elderly patients, especially those with <strong>renal insufficiency</strong>, may experience <strong>serious adverse events</strong>. Doses should be adjusted based on renal function.</li><li><strong>Impaired Renal Function</strong>: Adjust dose in patients with creatinine clearance &lt;60 mL/min.</li></ul><h3>Reconstitution:</h3><ul><li><strong>IV</strong>: Compatible with sterile water for injection and certain IV fluids like 0.9% sodium chloride, dextrose, and lactated Ringer’s.</li><li><strong>IM</strong>: Compatible with sterile water, sodium chloride, dextrose, and bacteriostatic water for injection.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action:</h3><ul><li>Disrupts bacterial cell wall synthesis, leading to bacterial lysis.</li></ul><p>This covers the main aspects of Xenim 500 mg/vial (Cefepime) for your reference. Would you like more details on any specific section?</p>', 'active', '23_1742463907.webp', 7, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:45:07', '2025-05-07 19:56:37'),
(3690, 'Xenofer', 'Iron Sucrose Injection [Elemental Iron]', 273.96, 1, 'x 2.5 ml ampoule', '2.5 ml ampoule', 'IV Injection', '50 mg/2.5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xenofer 50 mg/2.5 ml</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment of iron deficiency in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Where there is a clinical need for a rapid iron supply.</li><li>In patients who cannot tolerate oral iron therapy or who are non-compliant.</li><li>In active inflammatory bowel disease where oral iron preparations are ineffective.</li><li>In non-dialysis dependent chronic kidney disease (NDD-CKD) patients receiving erythropoietin.</li><li>In NDD-CKD patients not receiving erythropoietin.</li><li>In hemodialysis dependent chronic kidney disease (HDD-CKD) patients receiving erythropoietin.</li><li>In peritoneal dialysis dependent chronic kidney disease (PDD-CKD) patients receiving erythropoietin.</li><li>It is also indicated in the treatment of iron deficiency anemia in patients undergoing surgical procedures, patients donating blood, and postpartum patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Parenteral Iron Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Iron Sucrose is a haematinic and is used for intravenous administration. It contains approximately 30% sucrose w/v (300 mg/ml) and has a pH of 10.5-11.1. Following intravenous administration, iron sucrose dissociates into iron and sucrose by the reticuloendothelial system. Iron is then transferred to the liver and bone marrow where it binds to ferritin for storage. The iron component binds to plasma transferrin for distribution to tissues. Excess iron is stored in the liver, spleen, and bone marrow. The sucrose component is mainly eliminated via urinary excretion.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Xenofer 50 mg/2.5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Elderly</strong>: 5-10 ml (100-200 mg iron) once to three times a week, depending on hemoglobin levels.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Limited data available. Recommended dosage is not to exceed 0.15 ml (3 mg iron) per kg body weight, once to three times per week, depending on hemoglobin levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Xenofer 50 mg/2.5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong>: Administer undiluted at 1 ml (20 mg iron) per minute. A maximum of 10 ml (200 mg iron) can be injected per dose.</li><li><strong>Infusion</strong>: Dilute 1 ml of iron sucrose (20 mg iron) in 20 ml of 0.9% NaCl for infusion. The duration of the infusion varies based on the dose (e.g., 100 mg iron over 15 minutes, 500 mg iron over 3.5 hours).</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xenofer 50 mg/2.5 ml</strong></p><p>Iron sucrose should not be administered concurrently with oral iron preparations, as this reduces the absorption of oral iron. Oral iron therapy should not be started until 5 days after the last injection of iron sucrose.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to iron sucrose or any of its inactive components.</li><li>Evidence of iron overload.</li><li>Anemia not caused by iron deficiency.</li><li>History of allergic disorders such as asthma, eczema, or anaphylaxis.</li><li>Liver disease and infections.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xenofer 50 mg/2.5 ml</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Hypotension, cramps/leg cramps, nausea, headache, vomiting, diarrhea.</li><li>Other adverse effects:</li><li>Fever, pain, fatigue, chest pain, hypertension, musculoskeletal pain, pruritus, dyspnea, and respiratory issues.</li><li>Hypersensitivity reactions, including wheezing, dyspnea, and anaphylaxis, can occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B. The drug should be used only if necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is unknown if iron sucrose is excreted in human milk, so caution is advised when administering to breastfeeding women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor hematologic parameters (hemoglobin, ferritin, transferrin saturation).</li><li>Avoid iron administration in patients with iron overload.</li><li>Hypersensitivity reactions, although rare, require careful monitoring.</li><li>Hypotension is common in hemodialysis patients; administer according to recommended guidelines.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xenofer 50 mg/2.5 ml</strong></p><p>Excessive doses may lead to iron accumulation in storage sites, causing hemosiderosis. Symptoms of overdose include hypotension, headache, nausea, dizziness, and cardiovascular collapse. Overdose can be managed with IV fluids, hydrocortisone, or antihistamines.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place (15°C-30°C), protected from light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Do not freeze.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety in children has not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No overall safety differences have been observed. However, older individuals may be more sensitive.</li><li><strong>Hemodialysis Dependent-CKD Patients</strong>: Can be administered undiluted or diluted for slow IV infusion.</li><li><strong>Non-Dialysis Dependent-CKD Patients</strong>: Administer 1000 mg over 14 days in divided doses.</li></ul>', 'active', '148_1742464291.jpg', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:51:31', '2025-10-02 18:03:57'),
(3691, 'Xenofer', 'Iron Sucrose Injection [Elemental Iron]', 350.00, 1, 'x 5 ml ampoule', '5 ml ampoule', 'IV Injection', '50 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p>', 'active', '1372_1742464293.jpg', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:51:33', '2025-10-02 18:05:29');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3692, 'Xenthol', 'Methyl Salicylate + Menthol', 40.00, 1, 'x 15 gm tube', '15 gm tube', 'Cream', '30 percent plus 8percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a revised and organized version of the information with bold headings for better clarity:</p><p><strong>Indications of Xenthol 30%+8%</strong></p><p>This cream is indicated for the fast relief of minor aches and pains of muscles &amp; joints associated with:</p><ul><li>Joint Pain</li><li>Back Pain</li><li>Arthritic Pain</li><li>Neck &amp; Shoulder Pain</li><li>Sprains</li><li>Strains</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Local Antipruritic</li><li>Topical Analgesics</li><li>Topical Anti-inflammatory Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Xenthol 30%+8% is a specially formulated Methyl salicylate and Menthol cream. It penetrates the skin to provide fast relief from pain and stiffness due to minor arthritis and muscle aches. This fast-acting cream delivers strong medicine that penetrates deep into the skin for long-lasting and effective relief.</p><ul><li>Methyl salicylate undergoes first-pass metabolism in the skin, releasing active salicylate to alleviate pain and inflammation by inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis in inflamed tissues.</li><li>Menthol increases drug penetration, providing faster action by dilating blood vessels, causing a sensation of coldness followed by an analgesic effect.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult and children 2 years of age and older</strong>: Apply a thin layer to the affected area and gently massage until the cream disappears.</li><li>Apply no more than 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li>For external use only.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li>Methyl salicylate is absorbed through the skin and may increase the action of Warfarin by affecting Vitamin K metabolism or displacing Warfarin from protein-binding sites.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Allergy to salicylates or sensitivity to any component.</li><li>Application to broken skin or raw surfaces is contraindicated.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Redness or irritation may occur, especially in persons with sensitive skin.</li><li>Mild to moderate local irritation, erythema, rash, desquamation, pruritis, and relative local reactions at the application site are possible.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Safety during pregnancy has not been established. It should only be used during pregnancy in compelling circumstances.</li><li>Exercise caution during lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>For external use only. Use only as directed.</li><li>Do not use with a heating pad.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children to avoid accidental ingestion.</li><li>Do not swallow. In case of ingestion, seek medical help immediately.</li><li>Do not bandage tightly.</li><li>Avoid contact with eyes, mucous membranes, broken, or irritated skin.</li><li>If excessive irritation or skin redness develops, or if pain lasts for more than 10 days, or with arthritis-like conditions in children under 12, stop use and consult a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Large amounts applied topically may cause absorption through the skin, leading to salicylism. Symptoms include tinnitus, hearing loss, nausea, vomiting, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, away from light.</li><li>Temperature should not exceed 30°C.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Local Antipruritic</li><li>Topical Analgesics</li><li>Topical Anti-inflammatory Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Xenthol 30%+8% is a specially formulated cream with Methyl salicylate and Menthol. It provides fast, long-lasting relief by penetrating deep into the skin. Methyl salicylate alleviates pain by inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis, while Menthol enhances drug penetration, offering a quicker onset of action.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>This medication should only be used during pregnancy or breastfeeding if clearly needed.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '1794_1742464476.webp', 26, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 14:54:36', '2025-05-05 18:13:12'),
(3693, 'Xentoin', 'Phenytoin Sodium', 4.01, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xentoin 100 mg</strong></p><p>Parenteral Phenytoin is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Treatment of generalized tonic-clonic status epilepticus.</li><li>Prevention and treatment of seizures occurring during neurosurgery.</li><li>Short-term substitution for oral phenytoin when oral administration is not possible.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Phenytoin acts as an anticonvulsant by stabilizing neuronal membranes. It achieves this by increasing efflux or decreasing influx of sodium ions across cell membranes in the motor cortex, reducing seizure activity. It also acts as an antiarrhythmic by extending the effective refractory period and suppressing ventricular pacemaker automaticity, shortening the action potential in the heart.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Xentoin 100 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Oral:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Epilepsy:Adult:</strong> Initially, 3-4 mg/kg daily as a single dose or in divided doses, gradually increasing to 600 mg daily if necessary. Maintenance: 200-500 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Child:</strong> Initially, 5 mg/kg daily in 2-3 divided doses. Maintenance: 4-8 mg/kg daily in divided doses (Max dose: 300 mg daily).</li></ul><p><strong>Intravenous:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tonic-clonic status epilepticus:Adult:</strong> 10-15 mg/kg by slow injection or intermittent infusion at a max rate of 50 mg/min. Maintenance: 100 mg IV (or orally) every 6-8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Child:</strong>Neonates: 20 mg/kg as a loading dose, then 2.5-5 mg/kg bid.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">1 month-12 years: 18 mg/kg as a loading dose, then 2.5-5 mg/kg bid.</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">12 years: 18 mg/kg as a loading dose, then up to 100 mg 3-4 times daily.</blockquote><p><strong>Administration of Xentoin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Should be taken with food.</li><li>For patients on nasogastric or other enteral feeds, do not administer feeds 2 hours before or after a dose. Be consistent with feed times throughout therapy.</li><li>Do not switch dosage forms or brands without prior consultation.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xentoin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Increased effects</strong>: With allopurinol, capecitabine, cimetidine, CYP2C9 inhibitors (e.g., fluconazole), disulfiram, methylphenidate, metronidazole, omeprazole, SSRI, trazodone, trimethoprim.</li><li><strong>Decreased effects</strong>: With acyclovir, antineoplastics, benzodiazepines, ciprofloxacin, carbamazepine, rifampin, folic acid, vigabatrin.</li><li><strong>Enhanced toxic effects</strong>: With paracetamol, lithium.</li><li><strong>Increased risk of osteomalacia</strong>: With acetazolamide.</li><li><strong>Increased metabolism of</strong>: Antiarrhythmics, anticonvulsants, antipsychotics, beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, corticosteroids, theophylline, TCAs.</li><li><strong>Increased levels/effects</strong>: Of dopamine, ticlopidine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Xentoin 100 mg is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>A history of hypersensitivity to phenytoin, other hydantoins, or its inactive ingredients.</li><li>Sinus bradycardia, sino-atrial block, second and third degree A-V block, Adams-Stokes syndrome.</li><li>A history of prior acute hepatotoxicity attributed to phenytoin.</li><li>Coadministration with delavirdine.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xentoin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity, lack of appetite, headache, dizziness, tremor, transient nervousness, insomnia, GI disturbances (e.g., nausea, vomiting, constipation), gum tenderness and hyperplasia, acne, hirsutism, coarsening of facial features, rashes, osteomalacia.</li><li><strong>Phenytoin toxicity</strong>: Nystagmus, diplopia, slurred speech, ataxia, mental confusion, dyskinesias, exacerbation of seizures, hyperglycemia.</li><li>Prolonged use may affect mental function and cognition, particularly in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category D</strong>: Positive evidence of human fetal risk. Use only if the benefits outweigh the risks (e.g., in life-threatening situations or for serious diseases where safer drugs are ineffective or unavailable).</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in cardiovascular disease (e.g., sinus bradycardia, heart block), diabetes, hepatic impairment, hypoalbuminemia, porphyria, debilitated patients, and elderly.</li><li>In IV administration, monitor BP and ECG closely. IV must be given slowly to avoid hypotension, CNS depression, and cardiac arrhythmias.</li><li>Abrupt discontinuation may increase seizure frequency unless rapid withdrawal is necessary for safety.</li><li>May impair the ability to drive or operate machinery.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xentoin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Initial symptoms include nystagmus, ataxia, and dysarthria, progressing to tremor, hyperreflexia, lethargy, slurred speech, blurred vision, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li>Severe overdose may result in coma, hypotension, and respiratory/circulatory depression.</li><li><strong>Lethal dose</strong>: Estimated to be 2-5 grams in adults.</li><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: Supportive care, monitoring respiratory and circulatory systems, hemodialysis, or total exchange transfusion in severe cases.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Intravenous</strong>: Store at 15-30°C.</li><li><strong>Oral</strong>: Store below 30°C, protect from light and moisture.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oral suspension: Store at 20-25°C, protect from light, do not freeze.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: A loading dose of 15-20 mg/kg IV typically achieves target serum concentrations between 10 and 20 mcg/mL. Slow IV injection at a rate of ≤ 1-3 mg/kg/min or 50 mg/min.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Phenytoin clearance may decrease with age, and lower doses or less frequent dosing may be required.</li><li><strong>Renal and Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Increased unbound phenytoin in patients with impaired liver function or hypoalbuminemia requires careful monitoring of phenytoin serum levels.</li></ul><p>This summary should give you a comprehensive understanding of Xentoin 100 mg, including its indications, dosage, and key warnings. Let me know if you need any more information!</p>', 'active', '419_1742465442.jpg', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 15:10:43', '2025-05-05 18:14:25'),
(3694, 'Xeroder', 'Fluconazole', 22.15, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '150 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Xeroder 150 mg</h3><p><strong>Xeroder 150 mg</strong>, a member of the triazole class of antifungal agents, is a potent and selective inhibitor of fungal enzymes necessary for the synthesis of ergosterol. It is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li><strong>Vaginal Candidiasis</strong> (acute or recurrent)</li><li><strong>Candidal Balanitis</strong>: Treatment of partners with symptomatic genital candidiasis should be considered.</li><li><strong>Mucosal Candidiasis</strong>: Includes oropharyngeal, esophageal, non-invasive bronchopulmonary infections, candiduria, mucocutaneous, and chronic oral atrophic candidiasis (denture sore mouth). Can be used in both normal hosts and immunocompromised patients.</li><li><strong>Tinea Infections</strong>: Including tinea pedis, tinea cruris, tinea versicolor, and dermal candidiasis.</li><li><strong>Nail Fungal Infections</strong></li><li><strong>Systemic Candidiasis</strong>: Including candidemia, disseminated candidiasis, and other invasive candidal infections affecting the peritoneum, endocardium, pulmonary, and urinary tracts. May be used in patients with malignancy, in intensive care units, or those receiving cytotoxic or immunosuppressive therapy.</li><li><strong>Cryptococcosis</strong>: Including cryptococcal meningitis and infections at other sites (e.g., pulmonary, cutaneous). Can be used for prevention of relapse in patients with AIDS.</li><li><strong>Prevention of Fungal Infections</strong>: In immunocompromised patients at risk due to neutropenia following chemotherapy or radiotherapy, including bone marrow transplant patients.</li><li><strong>Other Uses</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fungal urinary tract infections</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Disseminated candidiasis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prophylaxis for fungal infections in neutropenic cancer patients</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute treatment of systemic fungal infections such as coccidioidomycosis and histoplasmosis</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Drugs for Subcutaneous and Mycoses</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Xeroder 150 mg is a triazole antifungal agent, functioning as a potent inhibitor of fungal cytochrome P-450 dependent enzymes, which are crucial for the synthesis of ergosterol in fungal cell membranes.</p><p><br></p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Adult (oral)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Vaginal Candidiasis</strong>: 150 mg as a single dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Oropharyngeal Candidiasis</strong>: 200 mg on Day 1, followed by 100 mg once daily for at least 2 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Esophageal Candidiasis</strong>: 200 mg on Day 1, followed by 100 mg once daily, for a minimum of 3 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Systemic Candida Infections</strong>: Doses of 200-400 mg daily, based on response.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cryptococcal Meningitis</strong>: 400 mg on Day 1, followed by 200 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis in Bone Marrow Transplant Patients</strong>: 400 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Mucosal Candidiasis</strong>: 3-6 mg/kg daily, with a possible initial dose of 6 mg/kg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Systemic Candidiasis</strong>: 6-12 mg/kg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention in Neutropenic Patients</strong>: 3-12 mg/kg daily.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Administration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adult</strong>: 400 mg initially, then 200 mg daily for invasive candidiasis, increased if necessary.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Child</strong>: 6-12 mg/kg daily, with adjustments for age and condition.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Xeroder 150 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>: May increase prothrombin time; careful monitoring is recommended.</li><li><strong>Sulphonylureas</strong>: May increase hypoglycemic risk.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: Increases plasma levels of Xeroder 150 mg by 40%.</li><li><strong>Phenytoin</strong>: May increase phenytoin levels significantly.</li><li><strong>Rifampicin</strong>: Decreases levels of Xeroder 150 mg by 25%.</li><li><strong>Oral Contraceptives</strong>: Minor effect on hormone levels at higher doses.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin</strong>: May increase cyclosporin concentrations.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Should not be used in patients with known hypersensitivity to Xeroder or other triazoles.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Gastrointestinal symptoms such as nausea, abdominal discomfort, diarrhea, and flatulence.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Rash (less than 1% of patients).</li><li><strong>Serious</strong>: Anaphylaxis (very rare), liver enzyme abnormalities, and severe cutaneous reactions like Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C (Risk to fetus cannot be ruled out). Avoid unless benefits outweigh risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Xeroder is excreted in breast milk; therefore, its use is not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Liver Issues</strong>: Monitor for liver enzyme changes; rare hepatic necrosis has been reported in severely ill patients.</li><li><strong>Skin Reactions</strong>: Discontinue if severe skin reactions like Stevens-Johnson syndrome or toxic epidermal necrolysis occur.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Adjust dosage intervals in patients with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hematologic Conditions</strong>: Patients with compromised immune systems (e.g., AIDS or cancer) may have an increased risk of severe skin reactions.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: Supportive measures and symptomatic treatment; gastric lavage if necessary. Hemodialysis can reduce plasma levels by about 50%.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Keep in a <strong>dry place</strong>, away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Xeroder 150 mg inhibits fungal cytochrome P-450 enzymes responsible for the synthesis of ergosterol, a vital component of the fungal cell membrane, making it an effective antifungal agent.</p><p><br></p><h3>Pediatric Use</h3><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Normal dose unless renal impairment is present.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Adjust dosages based on creatinine clearance.</li></ul>', 'active', '43_1742465661.jpg', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 15:14:21', '2025-05-05 18:15:46'),
(3695, 'Xeroder', 'Fluconazole', 8.05, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xeroder 50 mg</strong></p><p>Xeroder 50 mg, a member of the triazole class of antifungal agents, is a potent and selective inhibitor of fungal enzymes necessary for the synthesis of ergosterol. Xeroder 50 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ol><li><strong>Vaginal Candidiasis</strong> (acute or recurrent)</li><li><strong>Candidal Balanitis</strong>: The treatment of partners with symptomatic genital candidiasis should be considered.</li><li><strong>Mucosal Candidiasis</strong>: Including oropharyngeal, esophageal, non-invasive bronchopulmonary infections, candiduria, mucocutaneous, and chronic oral atrophic candidiasis (denture sore mouth).</li><li><strong>Tinea Pedis, Tinea Cruris, Tinea Versicolor, and Dermal Candidal Infections</strong>: Also indicated for nail fungal infections.</li><li><strong>Systemic Candidiasis</strong>: Including candidaemia, disseminated candidiasis, and other invasive candidal infections of the peritoneum, endocardium, and pulmonary and urinary tracts.</li><li><strong>Cryptococcosis</strong>: Including cryptococcal meningitis and infections of other sites (e.g., pulmonary cutaneous). Xeroder can also be used for maintaining therapy to prevent relapse of cryptococcal disease in patients with AIDS.</li><li><strong>Prevention of Fungal Infections</strong>: In immunocompromised patients at risk due to neutropenia following cytotoxic chemotherapy or radiotherapy, including bone marrow transplant patients.</li><li><strong>Other Uses</strong>: Fungal urinary tract infections, disseminated candidiasis, and prophylaxis for fungal infection in neutropenic cancer patients. Acute treatment of systemic fungal infections such as coccidioidomycosis and histoplasmosis.</li></ol><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Xeroder 50 mg is a triazole antifungal agent that inhibits fungal cytochrome P-450 dependent enzymes, which are essential for the synthesis of ergosterol, a key component of fungal cell membranes.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult (oral)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Vaginal Candidiasis</strong>: 150 mg as a single dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Oropharyngeal Candidiasis</strong>: 200 mg on the first day, then 100 mg once daily (continue for at least 2 weeks).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Esophageal Candidiasis</strong>: 200 mg on the first day, followed by 100 mg once daily. May increase up to 400 mg/day if necessary. Continue treatment for at least 3 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Systemic Candida Infections</strong>: Optimal therapeutic dosage and duration have not been fully established, but doses up to 400 mg daily have been used.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cryptococcal Meningitis</strong>: 400 mg on the first day, followed by 200 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention in Bone Marrow Transplant Patients</strong>: 400 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children (oral)</strong>: Doses of 3-6 mg/kg daily have been used. For systemic candidiasis and cryptococcal infections, 6-12 mg/kg daily is recommended.</li><li><strong>Intravenous (IV)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adult</strong>: Invasive candidal infections, including candidaemia and disseminated candidiasis: 400 mg initially, followed by 200 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Child</strong>: 6-12 mg/kg daily, with adjustments based on the patient\'s condition.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>: May increase prothrombin time.</li><li><strong>Sulfonylureas</strong>: May cause hypoglycemia in diabetic patients.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: Increases plasma concentrations of Xeroder by 40%.</li><li><strong>Phenytoin</strong>: May increase the level of phenytoin.</li><li><strong>Rifampicin</strong>: May reduce Xeroder levels by 25%.</li><li><strong>Theophylline</strong>: May decrease plasma clearance.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Xeroder 50 mg should not be used in patients with known hypersensitivity to Xeroder 50 mg or other triazole compounds.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Gastrointestinal symptoms (nausea, abdominal discomfort, diarrhea, flatulence).</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Anaphylaxis and skin reactions (e.g., rash).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C. Xeroder 50 mg should be avoided in pregnancy unless the potential benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Xeroder 50 mg is present in breast milk; its use during lactation is not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Liver function should be monitored, particularly in patients with underlying diseases.</li><li><strong>Severe skin reactions</strong>: Discontinue if Stevens-Johnson Syndrome or toxic epidermal necrolysis occurs.</li><li>Use cautiously in <strong>renal impairment</strong>: Dosage adjustments may be required based on renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose</strong></p><ul><li>In the event of an overdose, supportive measures and symptomatic treatment (including gastric lavage) are recommended. Hemodialysis can help reduce the drug\'s plasma levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustment unless renal impairment is present.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Dosage adjustments based on creatinine clearance may be necessary for long-term use.</li></ul>', 'active', '1000_1742465873.jpg', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 15:17:53', '2025-05-05 18:15:57'),
(3696, 'Xerokof', 'Ambroxol Hydrochloride', 40.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '15 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xerokof 15 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>This product is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Productive cough</li><li>Acute and chronic inflammatory disorders of upper and lower respiratory tracts associated with viscid mucus, including acute and chronic bronchitis</li><li>Inflammatory diseases of the rhinopharyngeal tract (laryngitis, pharyngitis, sinusitis, and rhinitis) associated with viscid mucus</li><li>Asthmatic bronchitis and bronchial asthma with thick expectoration</li><li>Bronchiectasis</li><li>Chronic pneumonia</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Cough expectorants &amp; mucolytics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Ambroxol is a metabolite of Bromhexine. It possesses mucokinetic (improvement in mucus transport) and secretolytic (liquefies secretions) properties. Ambroxol stimulates the serous cells of the glands of the mucous membrane of bronchi, increasing the content of mucus secretion. The mucolytic effect is associated with depolymerization and splitting of mucoproteins and mucopolysaccharide fibers, reducing the viscosity of mucus. This facilitates expectoration and eases breathing. Ambroxol also stimulates the production of phospholipids of surfactant by alveolar cells. Additionally, it has anti-inflammatory properties and improves airway patency in patients with COPD. Ambroxol also exhibits antioxidant activity. Long-term use is possible due to its good tolerability.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xerokof 15 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p><em>Preferably after meals</em></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Drops:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0-6 months: 0.5 ml, 2 times a day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 months: 1 ml, 2 times a day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-2 years: 1.25 ml, 2 times a day</li><li><strong>Syrup:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2-5 years: 2.5 ml (1/2 teaspoonful), 2-3 times a day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-10 years: 5 ml (1 teaspoonful), 2-3 times a day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 years and adults: 10 ml (2 teaspoonful), 3 times a day</li><li><strong>Sustained release capsule:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults and children over 12 years old: 1 capsule once daily</li></ul><p><strong>Specific Application Features:</strong></p><p>Ambroxol may be prescribed to patients suffering from diabetes mellitus.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Xerokof 15 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Ambroxol should not be taken simultaneously with antitussives (e.g., Codeine) because phlegm, which has been liquefied by Ambroxol, might not be expectorated.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in known hypersensitivity to Ambroxol or Bromhexine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xerokof 15 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal side effects like epigastric pain and stomach fullness may occur occasionally.</li><li>Rare allergic responses, such as eruptions, urticaria, or angioneurotic edema, have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><p>Teratogenic and fetal toxicity studies have shown no harmful effects of Ambroxol. However, it is advised not to use it during pregnancy, especially in the 1st trimester. Safety during lactation has not been established.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><p>Ambroxol should be given cautiously to patients with gastric and duodenal ulceration or convulsive disorders. It should also be used with caution in patients with hepatic and renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><p>Protect from direct light exposure. Store in a dry place at a temperature not exceeding 30°C. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><p>Cough expectorants &amp; mucolytics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Ambroxol is the active metabolite of Bromhexine and has a more potent bronchosecretolytic effect than Bromhexine. It improves sputum rheology by a hydrating mechanism, which leads to the liquefaction of mucus in the respiratory tract, thus facilitating expectoration and reducing dyspnea. It stimulates the production of phospholipids of surfactant by alveolar cells, which lowers superficial tension in the alveoli. It also reduces bronchial hyperactivity and has anti-inflammatory properties by inhibiting the production of cellular cytokines and arachidonic acid metabolites. In patients with COPD, Ambroxol improves airway patency.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><p>Teratogenic and fetal toxicity studies have shown no harmful effect of Ambroxol. However, it is advised not to use it during pregnancy, especially in the 1st trimester. Safety during lactation has not been established yet.</p>', 'active', '413_1742466046.jpg', 61, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 15:20:46', '2025-05-07 20:02:00'),
(3697, 'Xerokof', 'Ambroxol Hydrochloride', 40.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '15 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xerokof 15 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>This product is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Productive cough</li><li>Acute and chronic inflammatory disorders of upper and lower respiratory tracts associated with viscid mucus, including acute and chronic bronchitis</li><li>Inflammatory diseases of the rhinopharyngeal tract (laryngitis, pharyngitis, sinusitis, and rhinitis) associated with viscid mucus</li><li>Asthmatic bronchitis and bronchial asthma with thick expectoration</li><li>Bronchiectasis</li><li>Chronic pneumonia</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Cough expectorants &amp; mucolytics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Ambroxol is a metabolite of Bromhexine. It possesses mucokinetic (improvement in mucus transport) and secretolytic (liquefies secretions) properties. Ambroxol stimulates the serous cells of the glands of the mucous membrane of bronchi, increasing the content of mucus secretion. The mucolytic effect is associated with depolymerization and splitting of mucoproteins and mucopolysaccharide fibers, reducing the viscosity of mucus. This facilitates expectoration and eases breathing. Ambroxol also stimulates the production of phospholipids of surfactant by alveolar cells. Additionally, it has anti-inflammatory properties and improves airway patency in patients with COPD. Ambroxol also exhibits antioxidant activity. Long-term use is possible due to its good tolerability.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xerokof 15 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p><em>Preferably after meals</em></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Drops:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0-6 months: 0.5 ml, 2 times a day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 months: 1 ml, 2 times a day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-2 years: 1.25 ml, 2 times a day</li><li><strong>Syrup:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2-5 years: 2.5 ml (1/2 teaspoonful), 2-3 times a day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-10 years: 5 ml (1 teaspoonful), 2-3 times a day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 years and adults: 10 ml (2 teaspoonful), 3 times a day</li><li><strong>Sustained release capsule:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults and children over 12 years old: 1 capsule once daily</li></ul><p><strong>Specific Application Features:</strong></p><p>Ambroxol may be prescribed to patients suffering from diabetes mellitus.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Xerokof 15 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Ambroxol should not be taken simultaneously with antitussives (e.g., Codeine) because phlegm, which has been liquefied by Ambroxol, might not be expectorated.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in known hypersensitivity to Ambroxol or Bromhexine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xerokof 15 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal side effects like epigastric pain and stomach fullness may occur occasionally.</li><li>Rare allergic responses, such as eruptions, urticaria, or angioneurotic edema, have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><p>Teratogenic and fetal toxicity studies have shown no harmful effects of Ambroxol. However, it is advised not to use it during pregnancy, especially in the 1st trimester. Safety during lactation has not been established.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><p>Ambroxol should be given cautiously to patients with gastric and duodenal ulceration or convulsive disorders. It should also be used with caution in patients with hepatic and renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><p>Protect from direct light exposure. Store in a dry place at a temperature not exceeding 30°C. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><p>Cough expectorants &amp; mucolytics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Ambroxol is the active metabolite of Bromhexine and has a more potent bronchosecretolytic effect than Bromhexine. It improves sputum rheology by a hydrating mechanism, which leads to the liquefaction of mucus in the respiratory tract, thus facilitating expectoration and reducing dyspnea. It stimulates the production of phospholipids of surfactant by alveolar cells, which lowers superficial tension in the alveoli. It also reduces bronchial hyperactivity and has anti-inflammatory properties by inhibiting the production of cellular cytokines and arachidonic acid metabolites. In patients with COPD, Ambroxol improves airway patency.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><p>Teratogenic and fetal toxicity studies have shown no harmful effect of Ambroxol. However, it is advised not to use it during pregnancy, especially in the 1st trimester. Safety during lactation has not been established yet.</p>', 'in-active', '1713_1742466046.jpg', 61, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 15:20:46', '2025-05-13 05:52:21'),
(3698, 'Xevirol', 'Everolimus', 1500.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xevirol 5 mg</strong></p><p>Xevirol 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Advanced Hormone Receptor-Positive, HER2-Negative Breast Cancer (Advanced HR+ BC)</strong></li><li><strong>Advanced Neuroendocrine Tumors (NET)</strong></li><li><strong>Advanced Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)</strong></li><li><strong>Renal Angiomyolipoma with Tuberous Sclerosis Complex (TSC)</strong></li><li><strong>Subependymal Giant Cell Astrocytoma (SEGA) with Tuberous Sclerosis Complex (TSC)</strong></li><li>In combination with <strong>ciclosporin</strong> for microemulsion &amp; <strong>corticosteroids</strong> for the prophylaxis of organ rejection in adult patients at low to moderate immunological risk receiving an allogeneic <strong>renal or cardiac transplant</strong>.</li><li>In combination with <strong>tacrolimus</strong> &amp; <strong>corticosteroids</strong> for the prophylaxis of organ rejection in patients receiving <strong>hepatic transplant</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Immunosuppressant</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Xevirol 5 mg is a <strong>proliferation signal inhibitor</strong> that prevents allograft rejection by inhibiting T-cell proliferation. It inhibits <strong>T cell-specific interleukins</strong> (e.g., interleukin-2 and interleukin-15), leading to cell cycle arrest. The Xevirol-FKBP-12 complex interferes with <strong>FRAP (m-TOR)</strong> function, which is essential for cell metabolism, growth, and proliferation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General kidney &amp; heart transplant population</strong>: Initially 0.75 mg twice a day (bd), administered as soon as possible after transplantation.</li><li><strong>Hepatic transplant</strong>: 1 mg bd, starting 4 weeks after transplantation.</li><li><strong>Advanced HR+ BC, Advanced NET, Advanced RCC, and Renal Angiomyolipoma with TSC</strong>: Recommended dose is 10 mg, taken once daily at the same time every day. Tablets should be swallowed whole with a glass of water and should not be broken or crushed.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CYP3A4 inhibitors/inducers</strong></li><li><strong>CYP2D6 substrates</strong> with a narrow therapeutic index</li><li><strong>PgP inhibitors</strong></li><li><strong>Rifampicin</strong>, <strong>ACE inhibitors</strong>, <strong>grapefruit juice</strong>, and <strong>live vaccines</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Xevirol 5 mg, other rapamycin derivatives, or excipients.</li><li>History of <strong>hypersensitivity reactions</strong> (e.g., anaphylaxis, dyspnea, chest pain, or angioedema) with Xevirol or other rapamycin derivatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Stomatitis</strong>, <strong>constipation</strong>, <strong>infections</strong>, <strong>fatigue</strong>, <strong>diarrhea</strong>, <strong>rash</strong>, <strong>anemia</strong>, <strong>nausea</strong>, <strong>edema</strong>, <strong>dyspnea</strong>, <strong>headache</strong>, <strong>hypertension</strong>, <strong>tachycardia</strong>, <strong>CHF</strong>, <strong>menstrual irregularities</strong>, <strong>pruritus</strong>, <strong>dry skin</strong>, and <strong>pneumonitis</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category D</strong>: There is positive evidence of fetal risk, but potential benefits may warrant use in pregnant women.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: The distribution into breast milk is unknown. Not recommended for breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Risk of <strong>angioedema</strong> when used with ACE inhibitors.</li><li><strong>Interstitial lung disease</strong> (noninfectious pneumonitis) may occur; requires close monitoring.</li><li>Increased risk for <strong>infections</strong>; some may be severe or fatal.</li><li><strong>Mouth ulcers</strong> (stomatitis), delayed wound healing, and <strong>renal failure</strong> (some fatal) are reported.</li><li>May impair <strong>male fertility</strong> and affect <strong>liver function</strong> in patients with <strong>Child-Pugh Class C hepatic impairment</strong>.</li><li><strong>Avoid live vaccines</strong> during treatment.</li><li>May cause <strong>fetal harm</strong> in pregnant women; advise effective contraception for female patients of reproductive potential.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Immunosuppressant</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Xevirol 5 mg acts as a <strong>proliferation signal inhibitor</strong>, preventing allograft rejection by interfering with T-cell proliferation, which leads to cell cycle arrest at the G1 stage. This occurs through the inhibition of the <strong>p70 S6 kinase</strong> and <strong>FRAP (m-TOR)</strong>, essential proteins for cell growth and metabolism.</p>', 'active', '1423_1742466215.webp', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 15:23:35', '2025-03-20 15:23:35'),
(3699, 'Xevlin', 'Azithromycin Dihydrate', 230.00, 1, 'x 50 ml bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '200 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xevlin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Xevlin 200 mg/5 ml is prescribed for infections caused by susceptible organisms in:</p><ul><li>The upper respiratory tract, including sinusitis and pharyngitis/tonsillitis.</li><li>The lower respiratory tract, including bronchitis and pneumonia.</li><li>Otitis media.</li><li>Skin and soft tissue infections.</li><li>Non-gonococcal urethritis and cervicitis caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> in sexually transmitted illnesses in both men and women.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Macrolides</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Xevlin 200 mg/5 ml is a macrolide antibiotic in the azalide subclass. It works by binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit of susceptible organisms, preventing microbial protein synthesis. It is effective against organisms such as:</p><ul><li><em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>, <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>, <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Bordetella pertussis</em>, <em>Bordetella parapertussis</em>, <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em>, <em>Haemophilus ducreyi</em>, <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus</em>, <em>Campylobacter sp.</em>, <em>Toxoplasma gondii</em>, <em>Treponema pallidum</em>, <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Legionella pneumophila</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Xevlin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Adults</strong>:</p><ul><li>500 mg once daily for 3 days, or 500 mg once on day 1, followed by 250 mg once daily for 4 days.</li><li>For sexually transmitted diseases caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em>: 1 gm as a single dose or 500 mg once on day 1, followed by 250 mg once daily for the next 2 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Children</strong> (over 6 months of age):</p><ul><li>10 mg/kg body weight once daily for 3 days.</li><li>200 mg (1 teaspoonful) for children weighing 15-25 kg.</li><li>300 mg (1½ teaspoonfuls) for children weighing 26-35 kg.</li><li>400 mg (2 teaspoonfuls) for children weighing 36-45 kg.</li></ul><p>For typhoid fever: 500 mg (2½ teaspoonfuls) once daily for 7-10 days.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Administration of Xevlin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Shake the bottle thoroughly to break up the powder.</li><li>Fill the bottle with boiled and cooled water to the watermark on the label.</li><li>Mix the powder and water thoroughly.</li><li>Take Xevlin at least 1 hour before or 2 hours after eating.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xevlin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Food and antacids may reduce absorption.</li><li>Avoid use with ergot alkaloids due to the risk of ergotism.</li><li>Care should be taken when co-administering with cyclosporin and digoxin, as macrolides can raise their plasma levels.</li><li>No significant interactions with Warfarin, Theophylline, Carbamazepine, Methylprednisolone, or Cimetidine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Xevlin or other macrolide antibiotics.</li><li>Co-administration with ergot derivatives.</li><li>Liver disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xevlin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Low-frequency side effects may include:</p><ul><li>Nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain or cramps, flatulence, diarrhea, headaches, and skin rashes.</li><li>Discontinuing the medication typically reverses these side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy Category B: No evidence of harm to the fetus in animal studies, but controlled studies in pregnant women are lacking. It should only be used during pregnancy if no suitable alternatives are available.</li><li>The presence of Xevlin in breast milk is unknown, so caution should be exercised when administering it to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor for signs of superinfection with non-susceptible organisms (e.g., fungi).</li><li>No dose adjustment is required for patients with renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xevlin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>There is no specific information on overdose, but macrolide antibiotic overdose may cause symptoms like hearing loss, severe nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea.</li><li>Supportive treatment and gastric lavage are recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep the medication dry, away from heat and light.</li><li>Store out of reach of children.</li></ul>', 'active', '1523_1742466384.jpg', 67, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 15:26:24', '2025-05-05 18:23:48');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3700, 'Xiclav', 'Cefuroxime Axetil + Clavulanic Acid', 250.00, 1, 'x 70 ml bottle', '70 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '(125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information with headings in bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Xiclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml</strong></p><p>Xiclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml is indicated for treating infections caused by sensitive bacteria, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong> due to <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial otitis media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non beta-lactamase producing strains)</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong>, including pneumonia, caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp.</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, and <em>E. coli</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong> and secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains), or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains)</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin-structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></li><li><strong>Bone and joint infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong> caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase-producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease</strong> (erythema migrans) caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em></li><li><strong>Septicemia</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), and <em>Klebsiella spp.</em></li><li><strong>Meningitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), <em>Neisseria meningitidis</em>, and <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Switch therapy</strong> (injectable to oral)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime is a bactericidal second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic that effectively targets a wide range of susceptible Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including numerous beta-lactamase producing strains. It works by disrupting bacterial cell wall synthesis through interference with the transpeptidation process.</p><p>Clavulanic acid, a naturally derived beta-lactamase inhibitor produced by <em>Streptomyces clavuligerus</em>, shares a similar structure with beta-lactam antibiotics. It irreversibly binds to beta-lactamase enzymes, rendering them inactive. Clavulanic acid provides Cefuroxime with protection against degradation by beta-lactamase enzymes, offering a solution for treating bacterial infections caused by beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Xiclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adolescents and Adults (13 years and older)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/tonsillitis: 250 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis: 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated skin and skin-structure infections: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated urinary tract infections: 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated Gonorrhoea: 1000 mg b.i.d. Single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Community acquired pneumonia: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">MDR Typhoid Fever: 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Early Lyme disease: 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li><li><strong>Paediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis: 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute otitis media: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Impetigo: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Xiclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml</strong></p><p>Xiclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml tablet may be taken without regard to food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Xiclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml</strong></p><p>When probenecid is administered concurrently with Cefuroxime-Clavulanic Acid, it significantly increases the area under the serum concentration-time curve by 50%. The use of medications that reduce gastric acidity may result in decreased bioavailability of Cefuroxime, potentially counteracting its postprandial absorption benefits.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime-Clavulanic Acid is contraindicated in patients with a known allergy to cephalosporins and in patients with Pseudomembranous Colitis.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xiclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml</strong></p><p>Generally, Xiclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml is well tolerated. However, a few side effects like nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal discomfort or pain may occur. As with other broad-spectrum antibiotics, prolonged administration of Cefuroxime and Clavulanic acid combination may result in overgrowth of nonsusceptible microorganisms. Rarely (&lt;0.2%) renal dysfunction, anaphylaxis, angioedema, pruritis, rash, and serum sickness like urticaria may appear.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>While it\'s generally advisable to avoid antibiotics during the first trimester of pregnancy, Xiclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml can be used safely in later stages to address urinary and other infections. It is excreted into breast milk in small amounts, but it\'s essential to consider the potential for sensitizing the infant.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime should be given with care to patients receiving concurrent treatment with potent diuretics &amp; who have a history of colitis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime is a bactericidal second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic with activity against a wide range of susceptible Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including many strains that produce beta-lactamase enzymes. Cefuroxime works by disrupting bacterial cell wall synthesis through interference with the transpeptidation process.</p><p>Clavulanic acid, a naturally derived beta-lactamase inhibitor produced by <em>Streptomyces clavuligerus</em>, shares a structural similarity with beta-lactam antibiotics. It irreversibly binds to beta-lactamase enzymes, rendering them inactive. This protective action of clavulanic acid shields Cefuroxime from degradation by beta-lactamase enzymes, making it an effective solution for treating bacterial infections caused by beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</p>', 'active', '1628_1742466521.webp', 27, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 15:28:41', '2025-05-07 20:05:01'),
(3701, 'Xido', 'Gliclazide', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '80 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xido 80 mg</strong></p><p> Xido 80 mg is an oral antidiabetic medication used to reduce blood sugar levels. It belongs to the sulfonylurea class and is primarily prescribed for the treatment of type 2 diabetes mellitus in adults when diet, exercise, and weight loss alone are not sufficient to control blood sugar levels.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Sulfonylureas</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Xido 80 mg is a second-generation sulfonylurea drug that has hypoglycaemic effects. It works by stimulating the release of insulin from pancreatic β-cells and by facilitating calcium transport across the β-cell membranes. Additionally, it reduces hepatic glucose output.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xido 80 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral route</strong>: The usual initial dose is 40 to 80 mg daily. This dose can be increased up to 320 mg daily in divided doses as needed. Xido 80 mg should be taken before meals.</li><li><strong>Modified release preparation</strong>: The recommended daily dose is one to four tablets (maximum 120 mg) taken at breakfast time. The dose may be adjusted depending on your response to treatment. It is essential to take the tablets with a glass of water and to have a meal after taking the medicine.</li><li><strong>Combination therapy</strong>: When combined with other medications like metformin, thiazolidinediones, or insulin, your doctor will determine the appropriate dosage of each drug.</li><li><strong>Missed Dose</strong>: If a dose is missed, take the next dose at the usual time. Do not take a double dose to make up for the missed one.</li><li><strong>Overdose</strong>: If too many tablets are taken, contact your doctor or the nearest hospital immediately. Overdose may result in low blood sugar (hypoglycaemia), which can be managed by consuming sugar or sugary drinks.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Xido 80 mg</strong></p><p> Xido 80 mg may interact with several other medications, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Medications that enhance the hypoglycaemic effect</strong>: Other antidiabetic drugs, antibiotics (e.g., sulphonamides), and medicines for high blood pressure or heart failure (e.g., ACE inhibitors, beta-blockers).</li><li><strong>Medications that may weaken the hypoglycaemic effect</strong>: Drugs for central nervous system disorders, corticosteroids, and certain asthma medications.</li><li><strong>Increased blood clotting effect</strong>: Xido 80 mg may enhance the effects of anticoagulants like warfarin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Do not take Xido 80 mg if:</p><ul><li>You are allergic to sulfonylureas or other hypoglycaemic sulfonamides.</li><li>You have insulin-dependent diabetes (type 1).</li><li>You have diabetic ketoacidosis, pre-coma, or coma.</li><li>You have severe liver or kidney disease.</li><li>You are breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xido 80 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypoglycaemia</strong>: The most common side effect, which can lead to symptoms such as dizziness, confusion, and loss of consciousness.</li><li><strong>Liver Disorders</strong>: Yellowing of the skin or eyes (jaundice).</li><li><strong>Skin Disorders</strong>: Rash, itching, hives, or severe allergic reactions.</li><li><strong>Blood Disorders</strong>: Reduced blood cell counts, which may cause paleness, bleeding, or fever.</li><li><strong>Digestive Issues</strong>: Nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, and diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Eye Disorders</strong>: Temporary vision changes due to fluctuating blood sugar levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Xido 80 mg is contraindicated during pregnancy and breastfeeding. If pregnant or planning to become pregnant, consult your doctor before taking Xido 80 mg.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Regular blood sugar monitoring is necessary, especially during the initial stages of treatment.</li><li>Be cautious when fasting, skipping meals, or consuming alcohol, as these can trigger hypoglycaemia.</li><li>Close monitoring is advised in patients with kidney or liver issues or those undergoing significant physical activity changes.</li><li>Carry a form of sugar (e.g., glucose tablets) to manage low blood sugar episodes.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C, out of reach of children, and do not use after the expiry date. Dispose of unused medicine through proper channels.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Xido 80 mg stimulates insulin release from the pancreas and reduces glucose production by the liver, leading to a decrease in blood sugar levels.</p><p>For any further information or clarification, please consult your doctor or pharmacist.</p>', 'active', '982_1742466620.png', 33, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 15:30:20', '2025-03-20 15:30:20'),
(3702, 'Xidolac', 'Ketorolac Tromethamine', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you requested with normal bold text for the headings:</p><p><strong>Indications of Xidolac 10 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Xidolac 10 mg/ml is indicated for the short-term management of moderate to severe acute post-operative pain.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-Opioid Analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Xidolac 10 mg/ml is a potent analgesic of the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). It acts by inhibiting the cyclooxygenase enzyme system and hence inhibits prostaglandin synthesis. It demonstrates a minimal anti-inflammatory effect at its analgesic dose.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xidolac 10 mg/ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>: Recommended dose is 10 mg every 4-6 hours. It should be used short-term only (up to 7 days) and is not recommended for chronic use. Doses exceeding 40 mg/day are not recommended.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: Ketorolac injection may be used as a single or multiple doses, on a regular or when necessary schedule for the management of moderately severe, acute pain that requires analgesia at the opioid level, usually in a postoperative setting. When administering Ketorolac injection, the IV bolus must be given over no less than 15 seconds. The IM administration should be given slowly and deeply into the muscle. The analgesic effect begins within 30 minutes, with maximum effect in 1 to 2 hours after dosing IV or IM. Duration of analgesic effect is usually 4 to 6 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Single-Dose Treatment</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IM Dosing (Adult)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients &lt;65 years of age: One dose of 60 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients &gt;65 years of age, renally impaired and/or less than 50 kg of body weight: One dose of 30 mg.</li><li><strong>IV Dosing (Adult)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients &lt;65 years of age: One dose of 30 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients &gt;65 years of age, renally impaired and/or less than 50 kg of body weight: One dose of 15 mg.</li><li><strong>IV or IM Dosing (2 to 16 years of age)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">IM Dosing: One dose of 1 mg/kg up to a maximum of 30 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">IV Dosing: One dose of 0.5 mg/kg up to a maximum of 15 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Multiple-Dose Treatment (IV or IM)</strong></p><ul><li>Patients &lt;65 years of age: The recommended dose is 30 mg Ketorolac injection every 6 hours. The maximum daily dose should not exceed 120 mg.</li><li>Patients &gt;65 years of age, renally impaired patients, and patients less than 50 kg: The recommended dose is 15 mg Ketorolac injection every 6 hours. The maximum daily dose for these populations should not exceed 60 mg.</li></ul><p>For breakthrough pain, do not increase the dose or the frequency of Xidolac 10 mg/ml.</p><p><strong>Conversion from Parenteral to Oral Therapy</strong></p><p> Ketorolac tablets may be used either as monotherapy or as follow-on therapy to parenteral Ketorolac. When Ketorolac tablets are used as follow-on therapy to parenteral Ketorolac, the total combined daily dose of ketorolac (oral + parenteral) should not exceed 120 mg in younger adult patients or 60 mg in elderly patients on the day the change of formulation is made. On subsequent days, oral dosing should not exceed the recommended daily maximum of 40 mg. Ketorolac IM should be replaced by Ketorolac tablet as soon as feasible. The total duration of combined parenteral and oral treatment should not exceed 5 days.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Xidolac 10 mg/ml</strong></p><ul><li>Other NSAIDs or aspirin: Increase the side effects of ketorolac Tromethamine.</li><li>Anti-coagulants: Enhance anti-coagulant effect.</li><li>Beta Blocker: Reduce the anti-hypertensive effect.</li><li>ACE Inhibitors: Increase the risk of renal impairment.</li><li>Methotrexate: Enhance the toxicity of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Ketorolac is contraindicated in patients having hypersensitivity to this drug or other NSAIDs. It should not be used in children under 16 years of age. It is also contraindicated as prophylactic analgesic before surgery.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xidolac 10 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Commonly occurring side effects are nausea, vomiting, gastrointestinal bleeding, melena, peptic ulcer, pancreatitis, anxiety, drowsiness, headache, excessive thirst, fatigue, bradycardia, hypertension, palpitation, chest pain, infertility in female, and pulmonary edema.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> US FDA Pregnancy category of Xidolac 10 mg/ml is C. So, Xidolac 10 mg/ml should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the other outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Caution should be exercised in patients over the age of 65 years. Caution should also be taken in patients with active or suspected peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding, asthma, and liver dysfunction.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1834_1742466831.jpg', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 15:33:51', '2025-03-20 15:33:51'),
(3703, 'Xidolac', 'Ketorolac Tromethamine', 55.00, 1, 'x 1 ml ampoule', '1 ml ampoule', 'IV Injection', '30 mg per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xidolac 30 mg/ml</strong></p><p>Xidolac 30 mg/ml is indicated for the short-term management of moderate to severe acute post-operative pain.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-Opioid Analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Xidolac 30 mg/ml is a potent analgesic of the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). It acts by inhibiting the cyclooxygenase enzyme system and, hence, inhibits prostaglandin synthesis. It demonstrates a minimal anti-inflammatory effect at its analgesic dose.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xidolac 30 mg/ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>:</li><li>The recommended dose is 10 mg every 4-6 hours. It should be used short-term only (up to 7 days) and is not recommended for chronic use. Doses exceeding 40 mg/day are not recommended.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>:</li><li>Ketorolac injection may be used as a single or multiple doses, on a regular or as-needed schedule for the management of moderately severe acute pain that requires analgesia at the opioid level, usually in a postoperative setting. When administering Ketorolac injection, the IV bolus must be given over no less than 15 seconds. The IM administration should be given slowly and deeply into the muscle. The analgesic effect begins within 30 minutes with the maximum effect in 1 to 2 hours after dosing IV or IM. The duration of analgesic effect is usually 4 to 6 hours.</li><li><strong>Single-Dose Treatment</strong></li><li><strong>IM Dosing (Adult)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Patients &lt;65 years of age: One dose of 60 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Patients &gt;65 years of age, renally impaired, and/or less than 50 kg of body weight: One dose of 30 mg.</li><li><strong>IV Dosing (Adult)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Patients &lt;65 years of age: One dose of 30 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Patients &gt;65 years of age, renally impaired, and/or less than 50 kg of body weight: One dose of 15 mg.</li><li><strong>IV or IM Dosing (2 to 16 years of age)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">IM Dosing: One dose of 1 mg/kg up to a maximum of 30 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">IV Dosing: One dose of 0.5 mg/kg up to a maximum of 15 mg.</li><li><strong>Multiple-Dose Treatment (IV or IM)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Patients &lt;65 years of age</strong>: The recommended dose is 30 mg Ketorolac injection every 6 hours. The maximum daily dose should not exceed 120 mg.</li><li><strong>Patients &gt;65 years of age, renally impaired patients, and patients less than 50 kg</strong>: The recommended dose is 15 mg Ketorolac injection every 6 hours. The maximum daily dose for these populations should not exceed 60 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For breakthrough pain, do not increase the dose or the frequency of Xidolac 30 mg/ml.</li><li><strong>Conversion from Parenteral to Oral Therapy</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Ketorolac tablets may be used either as monotherapy or as follow-on therapy to parenteral Ketorolac. When Ketorolac tablets are used as follow-on therapy to parenteral Ketorolac, the total combined daily dose of ketorolac (oral + parenteral) should not exceed 120 mg in younger adult patients or 60 mg in elderly patients on the day the change of formulation is made. On subsequent days, oral dosing should not exceed the recommended daily maximum of 40 mg. Ketorolac IM should be replaced by Ketorolac tablet as soon as feasible. The total duration of combined parenteral and oral treatment should not exceed 5 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xidolac 30 mg/ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Other NSAIDs or aspirin</strong>: Increase the side effects of ketorolac Tromethamine.</li><li><strong>Anti-coagulants</strong>: Enhance anti-coagulant effect.</li><li><strong>Beta Blocker</strong>: Reduce the anti-hypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>ACE Inhibitors</strong>: Increase the risk of renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: Enhance the toxicity of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Ketorolac is contraindicated in patients having hypersensitivity to this drug or other NSAIDs. It should not be used in children under 16 years of age. It is also contraindicated as prophylactic analgesic before surgery.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xidolac 30 mg/ml</strong></p><p>Commonly occurring side effects include nausea, vomiting, gastrointestinal bleeding, melena, peptic ulcer, pancreatitis, anxiety, drowsiness, headache, excessive thirst, fatigue, bradycardia, hypertension, palpitation, chest pain, infertility in females, and pulmonary edema.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>US FDA Pregnancy category of Xidolac 30 mg/ml is C. Therefore, Xidolac 30 mg/ml should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in patients over the age of 65 years. Caution should also be taken in patients with active or suspected peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding, asthma, and liver dysfunction.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1887_1742467123.jpg', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 15:38:43', '2025-05-05 20:44:59'),
(3704, 'Xifamin', 'Rifaximin', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a clearer and more organized presentation of the information you provided about <strong>Xifamin 200 mg</strong>:</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Indications of Xifamin 200 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Traveler\'s Diarrhea</strong> (caused by noninvasive strains of <em>E. coli</em>)</li><li><strong>Reduction in the Risk of Overt Hepatic Encephalopathy</strong></li><li><strong>Bacterial Overgrowth in Irritable Bowel Syndrome</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>4-Quinolone Preparations</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Xifamin 200 mg is a <strong>non-systemic, rifamycin antibiotic</strong> and a <strong>structural analog of rifampin</strong>. It works by binding to the <strong>beta-subunit of bacterial DNA-dependent RNA polymerase</strong>, thereby inhibiting bacterial RNA synthesis.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Traveler\'s Diarrhea</strong> (&gt;12 years of age): 200 mg <strong>orally</strong> three times a day for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Encephalopathy</strong> (&gt;18 years of age): 550 mg <strong>orally</strong> two times a day.</li><li><strong>Bacterial Overgrowth in Irritable Bowel Syndrome</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">400 mg <strong>three times a day</strong> for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Or 550 mg <strong>three times a day</strong> for 14 days.</li></ul><p>Xifamin 200 mg can be taken with or without food.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li>In an in vitro study, Xifamin 200 mg suggested that it <strong>induces CYP3A4</strong>, but at the recommended doses, it is not expected to induce this enzyme in patients with normal liver function.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with a hypersensitivity to <strong>Xifamin 200 mg</strong>, any <strong>rifamycin antimicrobial agents</strong>, or any of the components in this product.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Common side effects include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Flatulence</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Headache</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Abdominal pain</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Rectal tenesmus</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Defecation urgency</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Nausea</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Constipation</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pyrexia</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Vomiting</strong></li><li>Serious side effects include allergic reactions like <strong>anaphylaxis</strong>, <strong>angioneurotic edema</strong>, and <strong>exfoliative dermatitis</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: Based on animal studies, Xifamin 200 mg may cause fetal harm. It should only be used during pregnancy if the potential benefit outweighs the risk.</li><li>It is not known if Xifamin 200 mg is excreted in human milk, so nursing mothers should discontinue breastfeeding or the drug based on the drug\'s importance to the mother.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Xifamin 200 mg is not effective in <strong>diarrhea complicated by fever or blood in the stools</strong>.</li><li>If symptoms worsen or persist for more than 24-48 hours, <strong>discontinue Xifamin 200 mg</strong> and consider alternative antibiotic therapy.</li><li><strong>Pseudomembranous colitis</strong> has been reported with nearly all antibacterial agents and can range from mild to life-threatening.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>No specific information is available for treating an overdose. In case of overdose:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Discontinue Xifamin 200 mg</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treat symptomatically</strong> and provide <strong>supportive measures</strong> as required.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from <strong>light and moisture</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Xifamin 200 mg is a <strong>semisynthetic, rifamycin-based non-systemic antibiotic</strong>. Very little of the drug passes into the bloodstream after oral administration. It inhibits bacterial RNA synthesis through action on the <strong>beta-subunit of DNA-dependent RNA polymerase</strong>, exerting bactericidal effects against a wide range of Gram-positive, Gram-negative, aerobic, and anaerobic bacteria.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: The risk to the fetus is unknown. It should only be used during pregnancy if the potential benefit outweighs the risk.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: The pharmacokinetics of Xifamin 200 mg in patients with impaired renal function has not been studied.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Systemic exposure is significantly increased in patients with hepatic impairment compared to healthy individuals.</li></ul><p>This format should help you better navigate the key points related to <strong>Xifamin 200 mg</strong>. If you need more details or have any other questions, feel free to ask!</p>', 'active', '1532_1742467228.webp', 11, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 15:40:28', '2025-03-20 15:40:28'),
(3705, 'Xifos', 'Ifosfamide', 1800.00, 1, 'x 1 gm vial', '1 gm vial', 'IV Infusion', '1 gm/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you provided, formatted with normal bold text for headings:</p><p><strong>Indications of Xifos 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Xifos 1 gm/vial is indicated for use in combination with certain other approved antineoplastic agents for third-line chemotherapy of germ cell testicular cancer. It should be used in combination with Mesna for prophylaxis of hemorrhagic cystitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Xifos 1 gm/vial is converted to its active metabolites via hepatic microsomal enzymes. These active metabolites act as alkylating agents, disrupting DNA and protein synthesis of the target cells. It is routinely given with mesna to reduce urothelial toxicity.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xifos 1 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Lymphoma, Sarcoma, Solid Tumours</strong>: Different licensed dosage regimens are available.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Regimen 1</strong>: 8-12 gm/m² divided over 3-5 days, repeat course every 2-4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Regimen 2</strong>: 6 gm/m² divided over 5 days, repeat course every 3 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Regimen 3</strong>: 5-6 gm/m² (max: 10 gm), given as a single 24-hr infusion, repeat course every 3-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Germ Cell Testicular Carcinoma</strong>: 1.2 gm/m²/day for 5 days via slow infusion over at least 30 minutes, repeat treatment every 3 weeks or after recovery from haematological toxicity. To be given with mesna and adequate hydration of at least 2 L of oral or IV fluid per day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xifos 1 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li>Causes enhanced toxicity with allopurinol, cisplatin.</li><li>Xifos 1 gm/vial enhances the anticoagulant effect of warfarin.</li><li><strong>CYP2A6 inducers</strong> (e.g. amobarbital, pentobarbital, phenobarbital, rifampin, and secobarbital) may reduce serum levels of Xifos 1 gm/vial, while <strong>CYP2A6 inhibitors</strong> (e.g. isoniazid, methoxsalen, and miconazole) may increase its serum levels.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 inducers</strong> (e.g. aminoglutethimide, carbamazepine, nafcillin, nevirapine, phenobarbital, phenytoin, and rifamycins) may reduce serum levels of Xifos 1 gm/vial, while <strong>CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g. azole antifungals, clarithromycin, diclofenac, doxycycline, erythromycin, imatinib, and isoniazid) may increase its serum levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity; severe bone-marrow depression. Pregnancy, lactation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xifos 1 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Confusion, alopecia, nausea, vomiting, phlebitis, somnolence, depression, hallucinations.</li><li><strong>Potentially Fatal</strong>: Severe myelosuppression, hemorrhagic cystitis, nephrotoxicity, cardiotoxicity, coma.</li><li>Wound healing may be impaired during Xifos 1 gm/vial use.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category D</strong>: There is positive evidence of human fetal risk, but the benefits from use in pregnant women may be acceptable despite the risk (e.g., if the drug is needed in a life-threatening situation or for a serious disease for which safer drugs cannot be used or are ineffective).</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Hepatic or renal dysfunction, compromised bone marrow reserve.</li><li>Use with mesna and ensure high oral/IV fluid intake to reduce urotoxic effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at 20-25°C.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: CrCl &lt;10: Administer 75% of dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p>Add 20 ml of sterile water for injection or sterile bacteriostatic water for injection containing benzyl alcohol or parabens for each 1 g of the drug to produce solutions of 50 mg/ml.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Xifos 1 gm/vial is converted to its active metabolites via hepatic microsomal enzymes. These active metabolites act as alkylating agents, disrupting DNA and protein synthesis of the target cells. It is routinely given with mesna to reduce urothelial toxicity.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category D</strong>: There is positive evidence of human fetal risk, but the benefits from use in pregnant women may be acceptable despite the risk (e.g., if the drug is needed in a life-threatening situation or for a serious disease for which safer drugs cannot be used or are ineffective).</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: CrCl &lt;10: Administer 75% of dose.</li></ul>', 'active', '860_1742467353.webp', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 15:42:33', '2025-05-07 20:05:21'),
(3706, 'Xifos', 'Ifosfamide', 3200.00, 1, 'x 2 gm vial', '2 gm vial', 'IV Infusion', '2 gm/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information you provided formatted with bold headings:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Xifos 2 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Xifos 2 gm/vial is indicated for use in combination with certain other approved antineoplastic agents for third-line chemotherapy of germ cell testicular cancer. It should be used in combination with Mesna for prophylaxis of hemorrhagic cystitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Xifos 2 gm/vial is converted to its active metabolites via hepatic microsomal enzymes. These active metabolites act as alkylating agents, disrupting DNA and protein synthesis of the target cells. It is routinely given with mesna to reduce urothelial toxicity.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xifos 2 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Lymphoma, Sarcoma, Solid Tumours:</strong> Different licensed dosage regimens are available.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Regimen 1:</strong> 8-12 gm/m² divided over 3-5 days, repeat course every 2-4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Regimen 2:</strong> 6 gm/m² divided over 5 days, repeat course every 3 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Regimen 3:</strong> 5-6 gm/m² (max: 10 gm), give as a single 24-hr infusion, repeat course every 3-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Germ Cell Testicular Carcinoma:</strong> 1.2 gm/m²/day for 5 days via slow infusion over at least 30 minutes, repeat treatment every 3 weeks or after recovery from haematological toxicity. To be given with mesna and adequate hydration of at least 2 L of oral or IV fluid per day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xifos 2 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li>Causes enhanced toxicity with allopurinol, cisplatin.</li><li>Xifos 2 gm/vial enhances the anticoagulant effect of warfarin.</li><li>CYP2A6 inducers (e.g., amobarbital, pentobarbital, phenobarbital, rifampin, and secobarbital) may reduce serum levels of Xifos 2 gm/vial, while inhibitors (e.g., isoniazid, methoxsalen, and miconazole) may increase its serum levels.</li><li>CYP3A4 inducers (e.g., aminoglutethimide, carbamazepine, nafcillin, nevirapine, phenobarbital, phenytoin, and rifamycins) may reduce serum levels of Xifos 2 gm/vial, while inhibitors (e.g., azole antifungals, clarithromycin, diclofenac, doxycycline, erythromycin, imatinib, isoniazid) may increase its serum levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity; severe bone-marrow depression.</li><li>Pregnancy, lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xifos 2 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li>Confusion, alopecia, nausea, vomiting, phlebitis, somnolence, depression, hallucinations.</li><li>Wound healing may be impaired during Xifos 2 gm/vial use.</li><li><strong>Potentially Fatal:</strong> Severe myelosuppression, hemorrhagic cystitis, nephrotoxicity, cardiotoxicity, coma.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category D:</strong> There is positive evidence of human fetal risk, but the benefits from use in pregnant women may be acceptable despite the risk (e.g., if the drug is needed in a life-threatening situation or for a serious disease for which safer drugs cannot be used or are ineffective).</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Hepatic or renal dysfunction, compromised bone marrow reserve.</li><li>Use with mesna and ensure high oral/IV fluid intake to reduce urotoxic effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at 20-25°C.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment (CrCl &lt;10):</strong> Administer 75% of dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p>Add 20 ml of sterile water for injection or sterile bacteriostatic water for injection containing benzyl alcohol or parabens for each 1 gm of the drug to produce solutions of 50 mg/ml.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Xifos 2 gm/vial is converted to its active metabolites via hepatic microsomal enzymes. These active metabolites act as alkylating agents, disrupting DNA and protein synthesis of the target cells. It is routinely given with mesna to reduce urothelial toxicity.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Category D: There is positive evidence of human fetal risk, but the benefits from use in pregnant women may be acceptable despite the risk (e.g., if the drug is needed in a life-threatening situation or for a serious disease for which safer drugs cannot be used or are ineffective).</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment (CrCl &lt;10):</strong> Administer 75% of dose.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need any further clarification!</p>', 'active', '1342_1742467478.webp', 55, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 15:44:38', '2025-05-07 20:05:30'),
(3707, 'Ximeclav', 'Cefuroxime Axetil + Clavulanic Acid', 250.00, 1, 'x 70 ml bottle', '70 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '(125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ximeclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml</strong></p><p>Ximeclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml is indicated for treating infections caused by sensitive bacteria, including:</p><ul><li>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis due to <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li>Acute bacterial otitis media caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non beta-lactamase producing strains)</li><li>Lower respiratory tract infections, including pneumonia, caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp.</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, and <em>E. coli</em></li><li>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis and secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains), or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains)</li><li>Uncomplicated skin and skin-structure infections caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></li><li>Bone and joint infections caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li>Uncomplicated gonorrhea caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase-producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></li><li>Early Lyme Disease (erythema migrans) caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em></li><li>Septicemia caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), and <em>Klebsiella spp.</em></li><li>Meningitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), <em>Neisseria meningitidis</em>, and <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li>Switch therapy (injectable to oral)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Second generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime is a bactericidal second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic that effectively targets a wide range of susceptible Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including numerous beta-lactamase producing strains. It works by disrupting bacterial cell wall synthesis through interference with the transpeptidation process.</p><p>Clavulanic acid, a naturally derived beta-lactamase inhibitor produced by <em>Streptomyces clavuligerus</em>, shares a similar structure with beta-lactam antibiotics. It irreversibly binds to beta-lactamase enzymes, rendering them inactive. Clavulanic acid provides Cefuroxime with protection against degradation by beta-lactamase enzymes, offering a solution for treating bacterial infections caused by beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Ximeclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Adolescents and Adults (13 years and older)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong>: 250 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Maxillary Sinusitis</strong>: 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Exacerbation of Chronic Bronchitis</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Secondary Bacterial Infections of Acute Bronchitis</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Skin and Skin-Structure Infections</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Urinary Tract Infections</strong>: 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Gonorrhoea</strong>: 1000 mg b.i.d. single dose</li><li><strong>Community Acquired Pneumonia</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>MDR Typhoid Fever</strong>: 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease</strong>: 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong>: 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 5-10 days</li><li><strong>Acute Otitis Media</strong>: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Maxillary Sinusitis</strong>: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li><strong>Impetigo</strong>: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Ximeclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml</strong></p><p>Ximeclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml tablets may be taken without regard to food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Ximeclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml</strong></p><p>When probenecid is administered concurrently with Cefuroxime-Clavulanic Acid, it significantly increases the area under the serum concentration-time curve by 50%. The use of medications that reduce gastric acidity may result in decreased bioavailability of Cefuroxime, potentially counteracting its postprandial absorption benefits.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime-Clavulanic Acid is contraindicated in patients with a known allergy to cephalosporins and in patients with Pseudomembranous Colitis.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ximeclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml</strong></p><p>Generally, Ximeclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml is well tolerated. However, a few side effects like nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal discomfort, or pain may occur. As with other broad-spectrum antibiotics, prolonged administration of Cefuroxime and Clavulanic acid combination may result in overgrowth of nonsusceptible microorganisms. Rarely (&lt;0.2%), renal dysfunction, anaphylaxis, angioedema, pruritis, rash, and serum sickness-like urticaria may appear.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>While it\'s generally advisable to avoid antibiotics during the first trimester of pregnancy, Ximeclav (125 mg+31.25 mg)/5 ml can be used safely in later stages to address urinary and other infections. It is excreted into breast milk in small amounts, but it\'s essential to consider the potential for sensitizing the infant.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime should be given with care to patients receiving concurrent treatment with potent diuretics and those with a history of colitis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Second generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime is a bactericidal second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic with activity against a wide range of susceptible Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including many strains that produce beta-lactamase enzymes. Cefuroxime works by disrupting the bacterial cell wall synthesis through interference with the transpeptidation process.</p><p>Clavulanic acid, a naturally derived beta-lactamase inhibitor produced by <em>Streptomyces clavuligerus</em>, shares a structural similarity with beta-lactam antibiotics. It irreversibly binds to beta-lactamase enzymes, rendering them inactive. This protective action of clavulanic acid shields Cefuroxime from degradation by beta-lactamase enzymes, making it an effective solution for treating bacterial infections caused by beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</p>', 'active', '827_1742467635.jpg', 64, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 15:47:15', '2025-05-07 20:11:48');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3708, 'Ximeclav', 'Cefuroxime Axetil + Clavulanic Acid', 34.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg+62.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you provided with the headings converted to normal bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Ximeclav 250 mg+62.50 mg</strong></p><p>Ximeclav 250 mg+62.50 mg is indicated for treating infections caused by sensitive bacteria, including:</p><ul><li>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis due to <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li>Acute bacterial otitis media caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non beta-lactamase producing strains)</li><li>Lower respiratory tract infections, including pneumonia, caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp.</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, and <em>E. coli</em></li><li>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis and secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains), or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains)</li><li>Uncomplicated skin and skin-structure infections caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></li><li>Bone and joint infections caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li>Uncomplicated gonorrhea caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase-producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></li><li>Early Lyme Disease (erythema migrans) caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em></li><li>Septicemia caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), and <em>Klebsiella spp.</em></li><li>Meningitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including ampicillin-resistant strains), <em>Neisseria meningitidis</em>, and <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li>Switch therapy (injectable to oral)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Second generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime is a bactericidal second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic that effectively targets a wide range of susceptible Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including numerous beta-lactamase producing strains. It works by disrupting bacterial cell wall synthesis through interference with the transpeptidation process.</p><p>Clavulanic acid, a naturally derived beta-lactamase inhibitor produced by <em>Streptomyces clavuligerus</em>, shares a similar structure with beta-lactam antibiotics. It irreversibly binds to beta-lactamase enzymes, rendering them inactive. Clavulanic acid provides Cefuroxime with protection against degradation by beta-lactamase enzymes, offering a solution for treating bacterial infections caused by beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Ximeclav 250 mg+62.50 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Adolescents and adults (13 years and older)</strong></p><ul><li>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis: 250 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis: 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li>Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li>Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infections: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections: 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li>Uncomplicated Gonorrhoea: 1000 mg b.i.d. Single dose</li><li>Community acquired pneumonia: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li>MDR Typhoid Fever: 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li>Early Lyme disease: 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li></ul><p><strong>Paediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years)</strong></p><ul><li>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis: 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 5-10 days</li><li>Acute otitis media: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li>Impetigo: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Ximeclav 250 mg+62.50 mg</strong></p><p>Ximeclav 250 mg+62.50 mg tablet may be taken without regard of food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Ximeclav 250 mg+62.50 mg</strong></p><p>When probenecid is administered concurrently with Cefuroxime-Clavulanic Acid, it significantly increases the area under the serum concentration-time curve by 50%. The use of medications that reduce gastric acidity may result in decreased bioavailability of Cefuroxime, potentially counteracting its postprandial absorption benefits.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime-Clavulanic Acid is contraindicated in patients with a known allergy to cephalosporins and in patients with Pseudomembranous Colitis.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ximeclav 250 mg+62.50 mg</strong></p><p>Generally, Ximeclav 250 mg+62.50 mg is well tolerated. However, a few side effects like nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal discomfort or pain may occur. As with other broad-spectrum antibiotics, prolonged administration of Cefuroxime and Clavulanic acid combination may result in overgrowth of nonsusceptible microorganisms. Rarely (&lt;0.2%) renal dysfunction, anaphylaxis, angioedema, pruritis, rash, and serum sickness-like urticaria may appear.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>While it\'s generally advisable to avoid antibiotics during the first trimester of pregnancy, Ximeclav 250 mg+62.50 mg can be used safely in later stages to address urinary and other infections. It is excreted into breast milk in small amounts, but it\'s essential to consider the potential for sensitizing the infant.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime should be given with care to patients receiving concurrent treatment with potent diuretics &amp; who have a history of colitis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Second generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime is a bactericidal second-generation cephalosporin antibiotic with activity against a wide range of susceptible Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including many strains that produce beta-lactamase enzymes. Cefuroxime works by disrupting the bacterial cell wall synthesis through interference with the transpeptidation process.</p><p>Clavulanic acid, a naturally derived beta-lactamase inhibitor produced by <em>Streptomyces clavuligerus</em>, shares a structural similarity with beta-lactam antibiotics. It irreversibly binds to beta-lactamase enzymes, rendering them inactive. This protective action of clavulanic acid shields Cefuroxime from degradation by beta-lactamase enzymes, making it an effective solution for treating bacterial infections caused by beta-lactam-resistant bacteria.</p>', 'active', '433_1742467778.jpg', 64, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 15:49:38', '2025-05-07 20:12:09'),
(3709, 'Neozine', 'Silver Sulfadiazine', 30.00, 1, 'cream', '25 gm cream', 'Cream', '25 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Neozine cream is indicated for:</p><ul><li>The topical prophylaxis against bacterial colonization and infection in burn wounds.</li><li>The topical antibacterial management of certain contaminated or infection-prone wounds, other than burns.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> The mechanism of Silver Sulfadiazine\'s antibacterial action has not been fully elucidated. After exposure to the drug, structural changes in the bacterial cell membrane occur, including distortion and enlargement of the cell and a weakening of the cell wall membrane. This is accompanied by reduced viability in sensitive strains.</p><p>The silver sulfadiazine molecule dissociates, and the silver moiety binds to bacterial cells. It is believed that, after penetrating the cell wall, the silver moiety attaches to deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) and prevents bacterial cell proliferation. There is approximately 100 times more DNA in mammalian cells than in bacterial cells. The ratio of silver sulfadiazine to bacterial DNA is high enough to prevent bacterial division but low enough not to block epithelial cell regeneration.</p><p>The sulfadiazine moiety also provides a bacteriostatic action against sensitive organisms. In adults, up to 10% of sulfadiazine may be absorbed, with 60-85% of the absorbed amount excreted in the urine. In children with 13% body surface area burns, the urinary sulfadiazine concentration was 31.8 mg/L.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li>Burn wounds should be cleansed before applying Silver Sulfadiazine.</li><li>The burn areas should remain covered with Silver Sulfadiazine at all times.</li><li>Apply the cream once or twice daily to a thickness of approximately <strong>1/16 inch (1.5 mm)</strong>.</li><li>Reapply as needed to areas where the cream has been removed due to patient activity.</li><li>If dressings are necessary, they may be used.</li><li>Reapply immediately after hydrotherapy.</li><li>Continue treatment until satisfactory healing occurs or until the burn site is ready for grafting.</li><li>Do not discontinue use if there is a risk of infection unless a significant adverse reaction occurs.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Enzymatic debriding agents:</strong> Neozine may inactivate these agents, making their concurrent use inappropriate.</li><li><strong>Oral hypoglycemic agents &amp; Phenytoin:</strong> In patients with large burns where serum sulfadiazine levels may approach therapeutic levels, the action of oral hypoglycemic agents and phenytoin may be potentiated. Blood levels should be monitored.</li><li><strong>Cimetidine:</strong> In patients with large burns, co-administration of cimetidine may increase the incidence of leukopenia.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Neozine is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients hypersensitive to Silver Sulfadiazine or any other ingredients in the preparation.</li><li>Pregnant women approaching or at term.</li><li>Premature infants or newborns during the first <strong>2 months</strong> of life.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Reported side effects include:</p><ul><li>Transient leukopenia</li><li>Skin necrosis</li><li>Erythema multiforme</li><li>Skin discoloration</li><li>Burning sensation</li><li>Rashes</li><li>Interstitial nephritis</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> Use during pregnancy should be justified, especially in women approaching or at term.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is unknown if Silver Sulfadiazine is excreted in human milk. A decision should be made to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, considering its importance to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><p> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>If hepatic and renal function becomes impaired, drug accumulation may occur. Discontinuation should be considered against therapeutic benefits.</li><li>Silver may inactivate topical proteolytic enzymes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Laboratory Tests:</strong>In extensive burn wound treatment, serum sulfa concentrations may approach therapeutic levels (8-12 mg%).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Monitor renal function and check urine for sulfa crystals.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Topical Antibiotic Preparations</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '993_1742470131.webp', 7, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 16:28:51', '2025-03-20 16:28:51'),
(3710, 'Burnsil', 'Silver Sulfadiazine', 45.00, 1, 'x 25 gm tube', '25 gm tube', 'Cream', '1%', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p>Burnsil cream is indicated for:</p><ul><li>The topical prophylaxis against bacterial colonization and infection in burn wounds.</li><li>The topical antibacterial management of certain contaminated or infection-prone wounds, other than burns.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>The mechanism of Silver Sulfadiazine\'s antibacterial action has not been fully elucidated. After exposure to the drug, structural changes in the bacterial cell membrane occur, including distortion and enlargement of the cell and a weakening of the cell wall membrane. This is accompanied by reduced viability in sensitive strains.</p><p>The silver sulfadiazine molecule dissociates, and the silver moiety binds to bacterial cells. It is believed that, after penetrating the cell wall, the silver moiety attaches to deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) and prevents bacterial cell proliferation. There is approximately <strong>100 times more DNA in mammalian cells than in bacterial cells</strong>. The ratio of silver sulfadiazine to bacterial DNA is high enough to prevent bacterial division but low enough not to block epithelial cell regeneration.</p><p>The sulfadiazine moiety also provides a <strong>bacteriostatic action</strong> against sensitive organisms. In adults, up to <strong>10% of sulfadiazine</strong> may be absorbed, with <strong>60-85% of the absorbed amount excreted in the urine</strong>. In children with <strong>13% body surface area burns</strong>, the urinary sulfadiazine concentration was <strong>31.8 mg/L</strong>.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li>Burn wounds should be cleansed before applying <strong>Silver Sulfadiazine</strong>.</li><li>The burn areas should remain covered with Silver Sulfadiazine at all times.</li><li>Apply the cream <strong>once or twice daily</strong> to a thickness of approximately <strong>1/16 inch (1.5 mm)</strong>.</li><li>Reapply as needed to areas where the cream has been removed due to patient activity.</li><li>If dressings are necessary, they may be used.</li><li>Reapply immediately after <strong>hydrotherapy</strong>.</li><li>Continue treatment until satisfactory healing occurs or until the burn site is ready for grafting.</li><li>Do not discontinue use if there is a risk of infection unless a significant adverse reaction occurs.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Interaction</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Enzymatic debriding agents:</strong> Burnsil may inactivate enzymatic debriding agents, making their concurrent use inappropriate.</li><li><strong>Oral hypoglycemic agents &amp; Phenytoin:</strong> In patients with large burns where <strong>serum sulfadiazine levels may approach therapeutic levels</strong>, the action of oral hypoglycemic agents and phenytoin may be potentiated. Blood levels should be monitored.</li><li><strong>Cimetidine:</strong> In patients with large burns, co-administration of cimetidine may <strong>increase the incidence of leukopenia</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><p>Burnsil is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients hypersensitive to Silver Sulfadiazine or any other ingredients in the preparation.</li><li><strong>Pregnant women approaching or at term</strong>.</li><li><strong>Premature infants or newborns during the first 2 months of life</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p>Reported side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Transient leukopenia</strong></li><li><strong>Skin necrosis</strong></li><li><strong>Erythema multiforme</strong></li><li><strong>Skin discoloration</strong></li><li><strong>Burning sensation</strong></li><li><strong>Rashes</strong></li><li><strong>Interstitial nephritis</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> Use during pregnancy should be justified, especially in women approaching or at term.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is unknown if Silver Sulfadiazine is excreted in human milk. A decision should be made to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, considering its importance to the mother.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></h3><p>Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>If hepatic and renal function becomes impaired, <strong>drug accumulation may occur</strong>. Discontinuation should be considered against therapeutic benefits.</li><li><strong>Silver may inactivate topical proteolytic enzymes</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Laboratory Tests:</strong>In extensive burn wound treatment, <strong>serum sulfa concentrations may approach therapeutic levels (8-12 mg%)</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Monitor renal function</strong> and check <strong>urine for sulfa crystals</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p>Topical Antibiotic Preparations</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul>', 'active', '1404_1742470425.jpg', 3, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 16:33:45', '2025-04-15 11:19:46'),
(3711, 'Silcream', 'Silver Sulfadiazine', 14.00, 1, 'cream', '5 gm tube', 'Cream', '1 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p>Silcream cream is indicated for:</p><ul><li>The topical prophylaxis against bacterial colonization and infection in burn wounds.</li><li>The topical antibacterial management of certain contaminated or infection-prone wounds, other than burns.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>The mechanism of <strong>Silver Sulfadiazine\'s</strong> antibacterial action has not been fully elucidated. After exposure to the drug, structural changes occur in the bacterial cell membrane, including <strong>distortion, enlargement, and weakening of the cell wall membrane</strong>, reducing bacterial viability in sensitive strains.</p><p>The <strong>Silver Sulfadiazine molecule dissociates</strong>, and the <strong>silver moiety binds to bacterial cells</strong>. It is believed that after penetrating the bacterial cell wall, the silver moiety <strong>attaches to DNA</strong>, preventing bacterial proliferation. Since mammalian cells contain approximately <strong>100 times more DNA than bacterial cells</strong>, the ratio of Silver Sulfadiazine to bacterial DNA is high enough to prevent bacterial division but low enough not to block epithelial cell regeneration.</p><p>The <strong>sulfadiazine moiety</strong> also provides a <strong>bacteriostatic action</strong> against sensitive organisms.</p><ul><li>In adults, <strong>up to 10% of sulfadiazine</strong> may be absorbed, with <strong>60-85% excreted in urine</strong>.</li><li>In children with <strong>13% body surface area burns</strong>, the <strong>urinary sulfadiazine concentration</strong> was <strong>31.8 mg/L</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Burn wounds should be cleansed</strong> before applying <strong>Silver Sulfadiazine</strong>.</li><li>The burn areas should remain covered with <strong>Silver Sulfadiazine at all times</strong>.</li><li>Apply the cream <strong>once or twice daily</strong> to a thickness of approximately <strong>1/16 inch (1.5 mm)</strong>.</li><li>Reapply as needed to areas where the cream has been removed due to patient activity.</li><li>If dressings are necessary, they may be used.</li><li><strong>Reapply immediately after hydrotherapy</strong>.</li><li>Continue treatment until <strong>satisfactory healing occurs</strong> or until the burn site is ready for grafting.</li><li><strong>Do not discontinue use</strong> if there is a risk of infection unless a significant adverse reaction occurs.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Enzymatic debriding agents:</strong> Silcream may inactivate enzymatic debriding agents, making their concurrent use inappropriate.</li><li><strong>Oral hypoglycemic agents &amp; Phenytoin:</strong> In patients with <strong>large burns</strong>, where <strong>serum sulfadiazine levels may approach therapeutic levels</strong>, the action of oral hypoglycemic agents and phenytoin may be <strong>potentiated</strong>. Blood levels should be monitored.</li><li><strong>Cimetidine:</strong> In patients with <strong>large burns</strong>, co-administration of <strong>cimetidine</strong> may <strong>increase the incidence of leukopenia</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><p>Silcream is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients hypersensitive to <strong>Silver Sulfadiazine</strong> or any other ingredients in the preparation.</li><li><strong>Pregnant women approaching or at term</strong>.</li><li><strong>Premature infants or newborns during the first 2 months of life</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p>Reported side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Transient leukopenia</strong></li><li><strong>Skin necrosis</strong></li><li><strong>Erythema multiforme</strong></li><li><strong>Skin discoloration</strong></li><li><strong>Burning sensation</strong></li><li><strong>Rashes</strong></li><li><strong>Interstitial nephritis</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> Use during pregnancy should be justified, especially in women approaching or at term.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is unknown if Silver Sulfadiazine is excreted in human milk. A decision should be made to <strong>discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug</strong>, considering its importance to the mother.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></h3><p>Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have <strong>not been established</strong>.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>If <strong>hepatic and renal function</strong> becomes impaired, <strong>drug accumulation may occur</strong>. Discontinuation should be considered against therapeutic benefits.</li><li><strong>Silver may inactivate topical proteolytic enzymes</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Laboratory Tests:</strong>In extensive burn wound treatment, <strong>serum sulfa concentrations may approach therapeutic levels (8-12 mg%)</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Monitor renal function</strong> and check <strong>urine for sulfa crystals</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p><strong>Topical Antibiotic Preparations</strong></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from light and moisture.</li><li><strong>Keep out of reach of children.</strong></li></ul><p><br></p><p>4o</p>', 'active', '1202_1742470652.jpg', 28, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 16:37:32', '2025-03-20 16:37:32'),
(3712, 'Ximepime', 'Cefepime Hydrochloride', 1100.00, 1, 'x 2 gm vial', '2 gm vial', 'IV Injection', '2 gm/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ximepime 2 gm/vial (Cefepime)</strong></p><p>Cefepime is indicated for the treatment of the following infections caused by susceptible strains of microorganisms:</p><ul><li><strong>Pneumonia (moderate to severe)</strong>: Caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Enterobacter</em> species.</li><li><strong>Febrile Neutropenia</strong>: Empiric treatment for febrile neutropenic patients, especially for those at high risk for severe infection (e.g., patients with a history of recent bone marrow transplantation, hypotension, hematologic malignancies, or prolonged neutropenia).</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated and Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</strong> (including pyelonephritis): Caused by <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>: Caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (methicillin-susceptible strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections</strong>: Used in combination with metronidazole, caused by <em>E. coli</em>, viridans group streptococci, <em>P. aeruginosa</em>, <em>K. pneumoniae</em>, <em>Enterobacter</em> species, or <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Fourth generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cephalosporins are bactericidal, disrupting the synthesis of the peptidoglycan layer of bacterial cell walls. This process is vital for maintaining the structural integrity of the cell wall, particularly in Gram-positive organisms. The transpeptidation step is facilitated by penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs).</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ximepime 2 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pneumonia</strong>: 1-2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Febrile Neutropenia</strong>: 2 gm IV every 8 hours for 7 days or until resolution of neutropenia.</li><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections (Mild to Moderate)</strong>: 0.5-1 gm IV/IM every 12 hours for 7-10 days.</li><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections (Severe)</strong>: 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Skin Infections</strong>: 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Intra-abdominal Infections</strong>: 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 7-10 days (combined with metronidazole).</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Aminoglycosides</strong>: Renal function should be monitored carefully if administered with high doses of aminoglycosides due to increased nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity risks.</li><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: Nephrotoxicity may occur if used in combination with potent diuretics like furosemide.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Cefepime is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity reactions to cefepime, other cephalosporins, penicillin, or other beta-lactam antibiotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Potential allergic reactions (rash, anaphylaxis)</li><li>Gastrointestinal disturbances (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea)</li><li>Risk of seizures and encephalopathy in patients with renal insufficiency</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B. Cefepime should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed, as animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Excreted in breast milk in very low concentrations. Caution should be exercised when administered to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Risk of <strong>drug-resistant bacteria</strong> due to improper prescribing.</li><li><strong>Prothrombin time</strong> monitoring is recommended in patients with liver or renal impairment.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with <strong>gastrointestinal disease</strong>, particularly colitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose include <strong>encephalopathy</strong>, <strong>myoclonus</strong>, <strong>seizures</strong>, and <strong>neuromuscular excitability</strong>. In the case of overdose, <strong>hemodialysis</strong> is recommended to remove cefepime from the body.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li><li>Do not use after the expiry date.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Maximum dose should not exceed the recommended adult dose. Pediatric doses are typically 50 mg/kg/dose every 12 hours for urinary infections, skin infections, and pneumonia.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Special care is needed, particularly for those with renal insufficiency. Close monitoring is necessary due to the risk of serious adverse events like encephalopathy and seizures.</li><li><strong>Impaired Renal Function</strong>: Dose adjustment is required for patients with creatinine clearance &lt;60 ml/min to compensate for the slower rate of elimination.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For IV administration</strong>: Use Sterile Water for Injection or compatible IV fluids.</li><li><strong>For IM administration</strong>: Compatible with Sterile Water for Injection, 0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection, or 5% Dextrose Injection.</li></ul>', 'active', '804_1742480567.jpg', 64, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 19:22:47', '2025-05-07 20:12:43'),
(3713, 'Ximepime', 'Cefepime Hydrochloride', 300.00, 1, 'x 500 mg vial', '500 mg vial', 'IV Injection', '500 mg/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Ximepime 500 mg/vial (Cefepime) - Indications, Dosage, Side Effects, and More</h3><p><strong>Indications:</strong> Cefepime is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by susceptible microorganisms, including:</p><ol><li><strong>Moderate to Severe Pneumonia</strong>: Caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Enterobacter</em> species.</li><li><strong>Febrile Neutropenia</strong>: Empiric treatment for febrile neutropenic patients.</li><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections (UTIs)</strong>: Both uncomplicated and complicated UTIs, including pyelonephritis, caused by <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>: Caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (methicillin-susceptible strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections</strong>: When used in combination with metronidazole for infections caused by <em>E. coli</em>, <em>P. aeruginosa</em>, <em>K. pneumoniae</em>, <em>Enterobacter</em> species, or <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>.</li></ol><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Fourth-generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Mode of Action</strong>: Cephalosporins are bactericidal and work by disrupting the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall, particularly affecting the peptidoglycan layer, which is critical for structural integrity.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pneumonia (Moderate to Severe)</strong>: 1-2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Febrile Neutropenia</strong>: 2 gm IV every 8 hours for 7 days or until resolution of neutropenia.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated/Complicated UTIs</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mild to moderate: 0.5-1 gm IV/IM every 12 hours for 7-10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe: 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>: 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections</strong>: 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 7-10 days (used with metronidazole).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration Method</strong>:</p><ul><li>IV infusion over 30 minutes.</li><li>IM route recommended only for mild to moderate UTIs.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Use caution when administering Cefepime with high doses of aminoglycosides or potent diuretics like furosemide, as this may increase the risk of nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to cefepime, cephalosporins, penicillin, or beta-lactam antibiotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Rash, diarrhea, and allergic reactions.</li><li>Serious: Encephalopathy, seizures, and neuromuscular excitability (especially in overdose).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: Should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: Excreted in low concentrations in breast milk; caution advised.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged use may lead to overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms.</li><li>Risk of prothrombin time reduction in patients with renal/hepatic impairment or poor nutrition.</li><li>Careful monitoring is essential in patients with gastrointestinal disease, especially colitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose symptoms may include confusion, hallucinations, stupor, seizures, and neuromuscular excitability.</li><li>In cases of overdose, hemodialysis is recommended for patients with renal insufficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store according to product guidelines; keep away from children and use before expiry.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Adjust doses based on age and weight. Maximum dose should not exceed the adult dose.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Elderly patients with renal insufficiency are at higher risk for serious side effects; adjust dose carefully and monitor renal function.</li><li><strong>Renal Function</strong>: In patients with renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;60 ml/min), dose adjustment is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IV administration</strong>: Compatible with sterile water and various infusion fluids.</li><li><strong>IM administration</strong>: Compatible with sterile water, saline, and lidocaine.</li></ul><p>For detailed dosing information and any adjustments based on patient condition, consult a healthcare professional.</p>', 'active', '1860_1742480711.jpg', 64, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 19:25:12', '2025-05-07 20:13:00'),
(3714, 'Ximepime', 'Cefepime Hydrochloride', 550.00, 1, 'x 1 gm vial', '1 gm vial', 'IV Injection', '1 gm/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ximepime 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Cefepime is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by susceptible microorganisms, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Pneumonia (moderate to severe)</strong>: Caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Enterobacter</em> species.</li><li><strong>Febrile Neutropenia</strong>: Cefepime as monotherapy is indicated for empiric treatment in febrile neutropenic patients.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated and Complicated Urinary Tract Infections (including pyelonephritis)</strong>: Caused by <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>: Caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (methicillin-susceptible strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections</strong> (in combination with metronidazole): Caused by <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Viridians group streptococci</em>, <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, <em>Enterobacter</em> species, or <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Fourth-generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefepime is bactericidal and works by disrupting bacterial cell wall synthesis, similar to other beta-lactam antibiotics (like penicillins). It inhibits the final transpeptidation step in peptidoglycan synthesis, facilitated by penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs).</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ximepime 1 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pneumonia</strong>: 1-2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days</li><li><strong>Febrile Neutropenia</strong>: 2 gm IV every 8 hours for 7 days (or until resolution of neutropenia)</li><li><strong>Mild to Moderate Uncomplicated or Complicated UTI</strong>: 0.5-1 gm IV/IM every 12 hours for 7-10 days</li><li><strong>Severe UTI</strong>: 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days</li><li><strong>Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>: 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days</li><li><strong>Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections</strong> (with metronidazole): 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 7-10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Caution when using cefepime with <strong>aminoglycosides</strong>, as it may increase the risk of nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity.</li><li>Use caution with potent <strong>diuretics</strong> (like furosemide) due to the risk of nephrotoxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Cefepime is contraindicated in patients with a <strong>history of hypersensitivity</strong> to cefepime, cephalosporins, penicillins, or other beta-lactam antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Some potential side effects include allergic reactions, gastrointestinal disturbances, or neurological symptoms.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B (no adequate studies in pregnant women, use only if necessary).</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Cefepime is excreted in breast milk in low amounts; caution is advised when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use caution in prescribing cefepime without confirmed bacterial infection, as inappropriate use may lead to <strong>drug-resistant bacteria</strong>.</li><li><strong>Prothrombin time</strong> should be monitored in patients at risk of coagulation abnormalities.</li><li><strong>Colitis</strong>: Exercise caution in patients with a history of gastrointestinal disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms include <strong>encephalopathy</strong>, <strong>seizures</strong>, and <strong>myoclonus</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hemodialysis</strong> can be used to aid cefepime removal in cases of renal insufficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store as per the manufacturer\'s recommendations and keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: The maximum dose for pediatric patients should not exceed the adult dose.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Careful dosing is required due to the increased risk of adverse effects in patients with renal insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Impaired Renal Function</strong>: Dose adjustments are needed in patients with <strong>creatinine clearance &lt;60 ml/min</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li>For IV: Compatible with sterile water or various IV fluids (e.g., sodium chloride, dextrose, lactated Ringer\'s).</li><li>For IM: Compatible with sterile water, sodium chloride, or lidocaine.</li></ul>', 'active', '1344_1742480819.jpg', 64, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 19:26:59', '2025-05-07 20:13:10'),
(3715, 'Ximeprox', 'Cefpodoxime Proxetil', 17.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ximeprox 100 mg (Cefpodoxime)</strong></p><p> Cefpodoxime is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by susceptible microorganisms, including the following:</p><ul><li><strong>Acute otitis media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenza</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>.</li><li><strong>Community acquired pneumonia</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenza</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em>, <em>H. influenzae</em> (non-beta-lactamase-producing strains only), or <em>M. catarrhalis</em>.</li><li><strong>Skin and skin structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, or <em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong> caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Rectal gonococcal infections in women</strong> caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Third-generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cefpodoxime is an oral third-generation cephalosporin with good stability to beta-lactamases, providing activity against both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. As a prodrug, its active metabolite is cefpodoxime. Approximately 29-33% of cefpodoxime is excreted unchanged in the urine within 12 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (13 years and older):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong>: 100 mg every 12 hours for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis</strong>: 200 mg every 12 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Community acquired pneumonia</strong>: 200 mg every 12 hours for 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong>: 200 mg every 12 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Skin and skin structure infections</strong>: 400 mg every 12 hours for 7-14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong>: 100 mg every 12 hours for 7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong>: Single dose of 200 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Rectal gonococcal infections in women</strong>: Single dose of 200 mg</li><li><strong>Infants and Pediatric Patients (2 months to 12 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute otitis media</strong>: 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours for 5 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong>: 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis</strong>: 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant administration of high doses of antacids (sodium bicarbonate and aluminum hydroxide) or H2 blockers may reduce peak plasma levels and the extent of absorption.</li><li>Renal excretion of cefpodoxime may be inhibited by probenecid.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with a known allergy to cephalosporins.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Possible side effects may include:</p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal disorders (diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain)</li><li>Rash, urticaria, and itching</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: FDA pregnancy category B. There are no adequate, well-controlled studies in pregnant women. The drug should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Cefpodoxime is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administering this drug to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In patients with renal insufficiency (reduced urinary output), the total daily dose should be reduced.</li><li>Administer with caution to patients receiving potent diuretics.</li><li>Prolonged use may result in the overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>For patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 mL/min), dosing intervals should be increased to 24 hours.</li><li>No dosage adjustment is required in cases of hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ol><li>Shake the bottle well to loosen the powder.</li><li>Add boiled and cooled water to the bottle.</li><li>Shake until the powder is completely mixed with water.</li></ol><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Third-generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Cefpodoxime is a prodrug that is converted to its active form, cefpodoxime, in the body. It has activity against a broad range of both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria and is stable to beta-lactamases.</p><p>Let me know if you need more details!</p>', 'active', '551_1742480993.webp', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 19:29:53', '2025-03-20 19:29:53');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3716, 'Ximeprox', 'Cefpodoxime Proxetil', 98.00, 1, 'x 50 ml bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '40 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ximeprox 40 mg/5 ml (Cefpodoxime)</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by susceptible microorganisms, listed below:</p><ul><li><strong>Acute otitis media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>.</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non-beta-lactamase-producing strains only), or <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>.</li><li><strong>Skin and skin structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, or <em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong> caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Rectal gonococcal infections in women</strong> caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Third-generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is an oral 3rd generation cephalosporin, which has good stability to beta-lactamases and activity against both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. It is a prodrug, and its active metabolite is Cefpodoxime. Approximately 29 to 33% of Cefpodoxime is excreted unchanged in the urine within 12 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ximeprox 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Adults and Adolescents (13 years and older):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong>: 100 mg 12-hourly for 5 to 10 days</li><li><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis</strong>: 200 mg 12-hourly for 10 days</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia</strong>: 200 mg 12-hourly for 14 days</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbations of chronic bronchitis</strong>: 200 mg 12-hourly for 10 days</li><li><strong>Skin and skin structure infections</strong>: 400 mg 12-hourly for 7 to 14 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infection</strong>: 100 mg 12-hourly for 7 days</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong>: Single dose of 200 mg</li><li><strong>Rectal gonococcal infections in women</strong>: Single dose of 200 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Infants and Pediatric Patients (2 months to 12 years):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Acute otitis media</strong>: 5 mg/kg body weight 12-hourly for 5 days</li><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong>: 5 mg/kg body weight 12-hourly for 5 to 10 days</li><li><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis</strong>: 5 mg/kg body weight 12-hourly for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ximeprox 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Antacids</strong>: High doses of antacids (sodium bicarbonate, aluminum hydroxide) reduce peak plasma levels and absorption of Cefpodoxime.</li><li><strong>H2 blockers</strong>: Reduce the extent of absorption.</li><li><strong>Probenecid</strong>: Inhibits renal excretion of Cefpodoxime.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Allergy</strong> to cephalosporins.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ximeprox 40 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal disorders (diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain)</li><li>Rash, urticaria, and itching</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: US FDA pregnancy category B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women, so it should only be used if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Cefpodoxime is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In patients with renal insufficiency, the total daily dose should be reduced.</li><li>Caution should be exercised in patients receiving concurrent treatment with potent diuretics.</li><li>Prolonged use of Cefpodoxime may result in overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min)</strong>: Increase dosing interval to 24-hourly.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment required.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ol><li>Shake the bottle well to loosen the powder.</li><li>Add boiled and cooled water to the bottle.</li><li>Shake until the powder is completely dissolved.</li></ol><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Third-generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is a third-generation cephalosporin with stability to beta-lactamases. It has activity against both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria, acting as a prodrug with its active metabolite being Cefpodoxime.</p><p>Let me know if you need further details!</p>', 'active', '545_1742481128.webp', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 19:32:08', '2025-05-07 20:15:54'),
(3717, 'Ximeprox DS', 'Cefpodoxime Proxetil', 175.00, 1, 'x 50 ml bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '80 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ximeprox DS 80 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by susceptible microorganisms, listed below:</p><ul><li><strong>Acute otitis media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenza</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains)</li><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em></li><li><strong>Community acquired pneumonia</strong> caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em> or <em>H. influenza</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em>, <em>H. influenzae</em> (non-beta-lactamase-producing strains only), or <em>M. catarrhalis</em></li><li><strong>Skin and skin structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, <em>Proteus mirabilis</em> or <em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em></li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong> caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Rectal gonococcal infections in women</strong> due to <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is an oral 3rd generation cephalosporin with good stability to beta-lactamases and activity against both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. It is a prodrug, and its active metabolite is cefpodoxime. Approximately 29-33% of cefpodoxime is excreted unchanged in the urine within 12 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ximeprox DS 80 mg/5 ml:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (13 years and older):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis:</strong> 100 mg every 12 hours for 5 to 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 200 mg every 12 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Community acquired pneumonia:</strong> 200 mg every 12 hours for 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbations of chronic bronchitis:</strong> 200 mg every 12 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Skin and skin structure infections:</strong> 400 mg every 12 hours for 7 to 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections:</strong> 100 mg every 12 hours for 7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea:</strong> Single dose of 200 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Rectal gonococcal infections in women:</strong> Single dose of 200 mg</li><li><strong>Infants and Pediatric Patients (2 months to 12 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute otitis media:</strong> 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours for 5 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis:</strong> 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours for 5 to 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis:</strong> 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Ximeprox DS 80 mg/5 ml:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Antacids:</strong> High doses of antacids (sodium bicarbonate and aluminum hydroxide) reduce peak plasma levels of cefpodoxime.</li><li><strong>H2 Blockers:</strong> H2 blockers reduce the extent of absorption of cefpodoxime.</li><li><strong>Probenecid:</strong> Inhibits renal excretion of cefpodoxime.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is contraindicated in patients with known allergies to cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ximeprox DS 80 mg/5 ml:</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is generally well-tolerated, with few side effects. Possible side effects include:</p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal disorders (diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain)</li><li>Rash</li><li>Urticaria (hives)</li><li>Itching</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> US FDA pregnancy category B. There are no well-controlled studies in pregnant women. This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Cefpodoxime is excreted in human milk, so caution should be exercised when administering it to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>In patients with renal insufficiency (reduced urinary output), the total daily dose should be reduced.</li><li>Cefpodoxime should be used with caution in patients receiving potent diuretics.</li><li>Prolonged use may result in overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><p>Store in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> In patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min), dosing intervals should be increased to 24 hours.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment is required in cases of hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution:</strong></p><ol><li>Shake the bottle well to loosen the powder.</li><li>Add boiled and cooled water to the bottle.</li><li>Shake the bottle until the powder is completely dissolved.</li></ol><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><p>Third-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is a 3rd generation cephalosporin that is stable against beta-lactamases and is active against both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. It is a prodrug, with its active metabolite being cefpodoxime. It is excreted unchanged in the urine in about 29–33% of cases within 12 hours.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><p>US FDA pregnancy category B. It should be used only when clearly needed, as there is no adequate well-controlled study in pregnant women. It is excreted in human milk, so caution should be exercised during breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses:</strong></p><p>In patients with severe renal impairment, dosing intervals should be extended to 24 hours. No dosage adjustment is required for hepatic impairment.</p>', 'active', '1581_1742481232.webp', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 19:33:52', '2025-05-05 20:48:17'),
(3718, 'Ximetil', 'Cefuroxime Axetil', 25.00, 4, 'tablet', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Ximetil 250 mg</strong></h3><p>Ximetil 250 mg (Cefuroxime) is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive bacteria, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non beta-lactamase producing strains).</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong> including pneumonia, caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp.</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, and <em>E. coli</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong> and <strong>Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains), or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains).</li><li><strong>Skin and skin-structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>.</li><li><strong>Bone and joint infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Gonorrhoea</strong> caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase-producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>.</li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease</strong> (erythema migrans) caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Second generation <strong>Cephalosporins</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Cefuroxime is an antibacterial agent with broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against a wide range of pathogens, including beta-lactamase-producing strains. It is stable to bacterial beta-lactamase, making it effective against ampicillin- and amoxycillin-resistant strains.</p><h3><strong>Dosage</strong></h3><p><strong>Adolescents and Adults (13 years and older):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong>: 250 mg twice daily (b.i.d.) for 5-10 days.</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis</strong>: 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin-structure infections</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong>: 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Gonorrhoea</strong>: 1000 mg as a single dose.</li><li><strong>Community acquired pneumonia</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days.</li><li><strong>MDR Typhoid Fever</strong>: 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days.</li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease</strong>: 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong>: 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 5-10 days.</li><li><strong>Acute otitis media</strong>: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis</strong>: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Impetigo</strong>: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Parenteral Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 750 mg three times daily via IM or IV injection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In severe infections, the dose may be increased to 1.5 gm three times daily by IV injection.</li><li><strong>Children (above 3 months)</strong>: 30-100 mg/kg/day in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Neonates</strong>: 30-100 mg/kg/day in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Surgical Prophylaxis</strong>: 1.5 gm IV at induction of anesthesia, followed by up to 3 additional doses of 750 mg IV/IM every 8 hours for high-risk procedures.</li><li><strong>Pneumonia</strong>: 1.5 gm IV twice daily for 2-3 days, followed by 500 mg twice daily orally for 7-10 days.</li><li><strong>Acute exacerbations of chronic bronchitis</strong>: 750 mg twice daily (IM or IV) for 2-3 days, followed by 500 mg twice daily orally for 5-10 days.</li></ul><h3><strong>Administration</strong></h3><ul><li>The freshly reconstituted solution should be used, maintaining potency for 24 hours at room temperature or 48 hours at 5°C.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions</strong></h3><p>No hazardous interactions have been reported for Ximetil 250 mg.</p><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known allergies to cephalosporins.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Common side effects include rashes and gastrointestinal disturbances. Prolonged use may lead to overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms like Candida.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong> (FDA).</li><li>Use during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Excreted in human milk; caution is advised when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Use with care in patients receiving potent diuretics or with a history of colitis.</li><li>Cephalosporins may be given safely to patients hypersensitive to penicillin, but cross-reactions have been reported.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Reconstitution</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>For 750 mg IM injection</strong>: Add 3 ml water for injection and shake gently.</li><li><strong>For 750 mg IV injection</strong>: Add 8 ml water for injection and shake gently. Administer slowly over 3 to 5 minutes.</li><li><strong>For 1.5 gm IV injection</strong>: Add 16 ml water for injection and shake gently. Administer slowly over 3 to 5 minutes.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Second Generation Cephalosporins</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li>Cefuroxime is a bactericidal agent with broad-spectrum activity against common pathogens, including beta-lactamase-producing strains, making it effective against ampicillin- and amoxycillin-resistant strains.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you\'d like any further details or clarification on any point!</p>', 'active', '1539_1742481372.jpg', 64, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 19:36:12', '2025-03-20 19:36:12'),
(3719, 'Ximetil', 'Cefuroxime Axetil', 45.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ximetil 500 mg</strong></p><p>Ximetil (Cefuroxime) 500 mg is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive bacteria, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase-producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non-beta-lactamase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong>, including pneumonia, caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp.</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>E. coli</em></li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong> and <strong>Secondary bacterial infections of Acute bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase-negative strains), or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase-negative strains)</li><li><strong>Skin and skin-structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></li><li><strong>Bone and Joint Infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li><strong>Gonorrhoea</strong> caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase-producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease</strong> (erythema migrans) caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Second generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime is a broad-spectrum, bactericidal antibiotic with activity against a wide range of pathogens, including beta-lactamase-producing strains. It is stable against bacterial beta-lactamase, which makes it effective against ampicillin- and amoxicillin-resistant strains.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Ximetil 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet or Suspension</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adolescents and Adults (13 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis: 250 mg twice daily for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis: 250 mg twice daily for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis: 250-500 mg twice daily for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis: 250-500 mg twice daily for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infections: 250-500 mg twice daily for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Uncomplicated urinary tract infections: 250 mg twice daily for 7-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Uncomplicated Gonorrhoea: 1000 mg as a single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Community-acquired pneumonia: 250-500 mg twice daily for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">MDR Typhoid Fever: 500 mg twice daily for 10-14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Early Lyme disease: 500 mg twice daily for 20 days</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis: 20 mg/kg/day, twice daily for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Acute Otitis Media: 30 mg/kg/day, twice daily for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis: 30 mg/kg/day, twice daily for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Impetigo: 30 mg/kg/day, twice daily for 10 days</li><li><strong>Parenteral</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adult</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">750 mg three times daily by IM or IV injection. In severe infections, dose can be increased to 1.5 gm three times daily by IV injection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>In Gonorrhoea</strong>: 1.5 gm as a single dose (2 x 750 mg injections)</li><li><strong>Children (above 3 months of age)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">30-100 mg/kg/day given in 3 or 4 equally divided doses. A dose of 60 mg/kg/day is appropriate for most infections.</li><li><strong>Neonate</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">30-100 mg/kg/day given in 2 or 3 equally divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Ximetil 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Freshly reconstituted solution is recommended. It retains potency for at least 24 hours at room temperature or 48 hours at 5°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Ximetil 500 mg</strong></p><p> No potentially hazardous interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime is contraindicated in patients with known allergies to Cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ximetil 500 mg</strong></p><p> Adverse effects are rare but may include:</p><ul><li>Rashes</li><li>Gastrointestinal disturbances</li></ul><p>Prolonged use may lead to overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms, such as Candida.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>US FDA Pregnancy Category: B</li><li>No adequate and well-controlled studies have been conducted in pregnant women. The drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Cefuroxime is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution is advised when giving Cefuroxime to patients on potent diuretics or with a history of colitis.</li><li>Cephalosporins can generally be given safely to penicillin-allergic patients, but cross-reactions have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li>For 750 mg IM injection: Add 3 mL of water for injection.</li><li>For 750 mg IV injection: Add 8 mL of water for injection.</li><li>For 1.5 gm IV injection: Add 16 mL of water for injection.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Second-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime has bactericidal activity against a broad range of pathogens, including beta-lactamase-producing strains. It is resistant to bacterial beta-lactamases, making it effective against resistant strains.</p>', 'active', '962_1742481529.jpg', 64, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 19:38:49', '2025-03-20 19:38:49'),
(3720, 'Ximetil', 'Cefuroxime Axetil', 125.00, 1, 'x 750 mg vial', '750 mg vial', 'IV Injection', '750 mg/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ximetil 750 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Ximetil is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive bacteria, including:</p><ul><li>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase-producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non-beta-lactamase-producing strains)</li><li>Lower respiratory tract infections, including pneumonia, caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp.</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>E. coli</em></li><li>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis and secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase-negative strains), or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase-negative strains)</li><li>Skin and skin-structure infections caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li>Urinary tract infections caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></li><li>Bone and joint infections caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li>Gonorrhoea caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase-producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></li><li>Early Lyme Disease (erythema migrans) caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Second generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime, the active ingredient, is a well-characterized antibacterial agent with broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against a wide range of common pathogens, including β-lactamase-producing strains. It is effective against many ampicillin-resistant and amoxycillin-resistant strains due to its stability to bacterial β-lactamase.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Ximetil 750 mg/vial</strong></p><p><strong>Tablet or Suspension</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adolescents and adults (13 years and older):</strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis: 250 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis: 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infections: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated urinary tract infections: 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated Gonorrhoea: 1000 mg Single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Community acquired pneumonia: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">MDR Typhoid Fever: 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Early Lyme disease: 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li></ul><p><strong>Paediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years):</strong></p><ul><li>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis: 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 5-10 days</li><li>Acute Otitis Media: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li>Impetigo: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Parenteral</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult:</strong> 750 mg three times daily by IM or IV injection. In severe infections, the dose can be increased to 1.5 gm three times daily by IV injection. The frequency may be increased to four times daily, if necessary, giving total daily doses of 3 to 6 grams.</li><li><strong>Children (above 3 months of age):</strong> 30-100 mg/kg/day given in 3 or 4 equally divided doses. A dose of 60 mg/kg/day is appropriate for most infections.</li><li><strong>Neonate:</strong> 30-100 mg/kg/day given in 2 or 3 equally divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Surgical prophylaxis:</strong></p><p>1.5 gm by IV injection at induction of anaesthesia; up to 3 further doses of 750 mg may be given by IV/IM injection every 8 hours for high-risk procedures.</p><p><strong>Pneumonia:</strong></p><p>1.5 gm IV injection twice daily for 2-3 days, followed by 500 mg twice daily (oral) for 7-10 days.</p><p><strong>Acute exacerbations of chronic bronchitis:</strong></p><p>750 mg twice daily (IM or IV injection) for 2-3 days, followed by 500 mg twice daily (oral) for 5-10 days.</p><p><strong>In Gonorrhoea:</strong></p><p>Adult: 1.5 gm as a single dose (as 2 x 750mg injections intramuscularly with different sites).</p><p><strong>In Meningitis:</strong></p><p>Adult: 3 gm IV injection three times daily.</p><p>Children (above 3 months of age): 200-240 mg/kg/day by IV injection in 3 or 4 divided doses reduced to 100 mg/kg/day after 3 days or on clinical improvement.</p><p>Neonate: 100 mg/kg/day by IV injection at initial dose, reduced to 50 mg/kg/day, when clinically indicated.</p><p><strong>In bone and joint infections:</strong></p><p>Adult: 1.5 gm IV injection four times daily.</p><p>Children (above 3 months of age): 150 mg/kg/day (not to exceed the maximum adult dose) in equally divided doses every 8 hours.</p><p><strong>Administration of Ximetil 750 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li>The use of freshly reconstituted solution is recommended. However, it maintains potency for at least 24 hours at room temperature or 48 hours at 5°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ximetil 750 mg/vial</strong></p><p>No potentially hazardous interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime is contraindicated in patients with known allergy to Cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ximetil 750 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Adverse effects to Cefuroxime have occurred infrequently and are generally mild and transient in nature. Reported effects include rashes and gastrointestinal disturbances. Prolonged use may result in overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms (e.g., Candida).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>US FDA pregnancy category B. No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Use during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Cefuroxime is excreted in human milk, so caution should be exercised when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with care in patients receiving potent diuretics and with a history of colitis.</li><li>Cephalosporins may be given safely to patients hypersensitive to penicillin, though cross-reactions have been reported.</li><li>Cefuroxime has shown that cross-reactivity is not likely to be a problem at recommended dose levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li>For 750 mg intramuscular injection: Add 3 ml water for injection to the vial and shake gently.</li><li>For 750 mg intravenous injection: Add 8 ml water for injection to the vial and shake gently. Inject slowly over 3 to 5 minutes.</li><li>For 1.5 gm intravenous injection: Add 16 ml water for injection to the vial and shake gently. Inject slowly over 3 to 5 minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Second generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime is an antibacterial agent with broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against a wide range of common pathogens, including β-lactamase-producing strains. It is effective against many ampicillin-resistant and amoxycillin-resistant strains due to its stability to bacterial β-lactamase.</p>', 'active', '291_1742481860.jpg', 64, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 19:44:20', '2025-05-05 20:48:53'),
(3721, 'Ximetil', 'Cefuroxime Axetil', 200.00, 1, 'x 1.5 gm vial', '1.5 gm vial', 'IV Injection', '1.5 gm per vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ximetil 1.5 gm/vial</strong></p><p>Ximetil 1.5 gm/vial is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive bacteria, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non beta-lactamase producing strains).</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong> including pneumonia caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp.</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>E. coli</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong> and secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains) or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains).</li><li><strong>Skin and skin-structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>.</li><li><strong>Bone and Joint Infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Gonorrhoea</strong> caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>.</li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease (erythema migrans)</strong> caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Second generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefuroxime, the active ingredient, is a well-characterized and effective antibacterial agent with broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against a wide range of pathogens, including β-lactamase-producing strains. It has good stability to bacterial β-lactamase and is active against many ampicillin- and amoxicillin-resistant strains.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Ximetil 1.5 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet or Suspension</strong>:</li><li><em>Adolescents and adults (13 years and older)</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis: 250 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis: 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infections: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Uncomplicated urinary tract infections: 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Uncomplicated Gonorrhoea: 1000 mg Single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Community-acquired pneumonia: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">MDR Typhoid Fever: 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Early Lyme disease: 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li><li><em>Paediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years)</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis: 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Acute otitis media: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Impetigo: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Parenteral (IM/IV)</strong>:</li><li><em>Adult</em>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">750 mg three times daily by IM or IV injection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">In severe infections, dose can be increased up to 1.5 gm three times daily by IV injection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The frequency may be increased to four times daily, giving total daily doses of 3 to 6 gms.</li><li><em>Children (above 3 months of age)</em>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">30 - 100 mg/kg/day in 3 or 4 equally divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">A dose of 60 mg/kg/day is appropriate for most infections.</li><li><em>Neonate</em>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">30 - 100 mg/kg/day in 2 or 3 equally divided doses.</li><li><strong>Surgical prophylaxis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1.5 gm by IV injection at induction of anaesthesia; up to 3 further doses of 750 mg every 8 hours may be given for high-risk procedures.</li><li><strong>Pneumonia</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1.5 gm IV injection twice daily for 2-3 days, followed by 500 mg twice daily (oral) for 7-10 days.</li><li><strong>Acute exacerbations of chronic bronchitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">750 mg twice daily (IM or IV) for 2-3 days, followed by 500 mg twice daily (oral) for 5-10 days.</li><li><strong>Gonorrhoea</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1.5 gm as a single dose (as 2 x 750 mg injections).</li><li><strong>Meningitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">3 gm IV injection three times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children (above 3 months of age): 200-240 mg/kg/day in 3 or 4 divided doses, reduced to 100 mg/kg/day after 3 days or on clinical improvement.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Neonate: 100 mg/kg/day by IV injection initially, reduced to 50 mg/kg/day.</li><li><strong>Bone and joint infections</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">1.5 gm IV injection four times daily for adults.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (above 3 months): 150 mg/kg/day (not exceeding the adult maximum dose) in equally divided doses every 8 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Ximetil 1.5 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li>The freshly reconstituted solution is recommended. It maintains potency for at least 24 hours at room temperature or 48 hours at 5°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Ximetil 1.5 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li>No potentially hazardous interactions have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Cefuroxime is contraindicated in patients with known allergies to Cephalosporins.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ximetil 1.5 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li>Adverse effects are infrequent, mild, and transient, including rashes and gastrointestinal disturbances. Prolonged use may result in the overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms (e.g., Candida).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>US FDA pregnancy category B. While animal studies are not always predictive, this drug should be used in pregnancy only if necessary.</li><li>Cefuroxime is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients receiving potent diuretics or with a history of colitis.</li><li>Cephalosporins may generally be given safely to those hypersensitive to penicillin, though cross-reactions have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li>For 750 mg IM injection: Add 3 ml water for injection and shake gently.</li><li>For 750 mg IV injection: Add 8 ml water for injection and shake gently. Administer over 3-5 minutes.</li><li>For 1.5 gm IV injection: Add 16 ml water for injection and shake gently. Administer over 3-5 minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Second-generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Cefuroxime exhibits broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against a wide range of pathogens, including β-lactamase-producing strains. It is stable against β-lactamase and is effective against many ampicillin- and amoxicillin-resistant strains.</li></ul>', 'active', '486_1742482017.jpg', 64, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 19:46:57', '2025-05-05 20:49:19');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3722, 'Ximetil', 'Cefuroxime Axetil', 198.00, 1, 'x 70 ml bottle', '70 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '125 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ximetil 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Ximetil is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive bacteria. These include:</p><ul><li><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non beta-lactamase producing strains).</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong> including pneumonia, caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella</em> spp., <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, and <em>E. coli</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong> and <strong>Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains), or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains).</li><li><strong>Skin and skin-structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>.</li><li><strong>Bone and Joint Infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Gonorrhoea</strong> caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase-producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>.</li><li><strong>Early Lyme Disease</strong> (erythema migrans) caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Second generation <strong>Cephalosporins</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Cefuroxime is a broad-spectrum, bactericidal agent effective against a wide range of common pathogens, including β-lactamase-producing strains. It has good stability against bacterial β-lactamase, making it active against many ampicillin- and amoxicillin-resistant strains.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage of Ximetil 125 mg/5 ml:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet or Suspension (Adolescents and adults, 13 years and older):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong>: 250 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis</strong>: 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated skin and skin-structure infections</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong>: 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated Gonorrhoea</strong>: 1000 mg single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Community acquired pneumonia</strong>: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>MDR Typhoid Fever</strong>: 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Early Lyme Disease</strong>: 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis</strong>: 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute Otitis Media</strong>: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis</strong>: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Impetigo</strong>: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d for 10 days</li><li><strong>Parenteral (Adult and Pediatric Dosing):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 750 mg three times daily by IM or IV injection. In severe infections, dose may be increased to 1.5 gm three times daily by IV injection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (above 3 months)</strong>: 30-100 mg/kg/day, divided into 3-4 doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Reconstitution</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For 750 mg IM: Add 3 ml water for injection, shake gently.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For 750 mg IV: Add 8 ml water for injection, shake gently, inject slowly over 3-5 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For 1.5 gm IV: Add 16 ml water for injection, shake gently, inject slowly over 3-5 minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Rashes</li><li>Gastrointestinal disturbances</li><li>Prolonged use may lead to overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms (e.g., Candida).</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients receiving diuretics or those with a history of colitis.</li><li>Cross-reactivity may occur in patients with penicillin hypersensitivity.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>US FDA pregnancy category B. Caution is advised as Cefuroxime is excreted in human milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Known allergy to Cephalosporins.</li></ul>', 'active', '1112_1742482377.jpg', 64, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 19:52:57', '2025-05-07 20:16:42'),
(3723, 'Magnason', 'Magnesium Hydroxide', 50.15, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '400 mg/5 m', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p>', 'active', '4398_1746304135.jpg', 4, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 19:57:08', '2025-05-04 00:28:55'),
(3724, 'Rosu', 'Rosuvastatin', 50.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rosu 40 mg:</strong></p><p>Rosu 40 mg is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Heterozygous Hypercholesterolemia (Familial and Non-familial)</li><li>Homozygous Hypercholesterolemia (Familial)</li><li>Mixed Dyslipidemia (Fredrickson Type IIa and IIb)</li><li>Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Other Anti-anginal &amp; Anti-ischaemic drugs</li><li>Statins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Rosu 40 mg is a selective and competitive inhibitor of HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme that converts 3-hydroxy-3-methyl glutaryl coenzyme A to mevalonate, a precursor of cholesterol. It produces its lipid-modifying effects in two ways:</p><ol><li>It increases the number of hepatic LDL receptors on the cell surface to enhance uptake and catabolism of LDL.</li><li>It inhibits hepatic synthesis of VLDL, reducing the total number of VLDL and LDL particles.</li></ol><p><strong>Dosage of Rosu 40 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Dose range: 5-40 mg once daily.</li><li>Use 40 mg dose only for patients not reaching LDL-C goal with 20 mg.</li><li>Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia (HoFH): Starting dose 20 mg/day.</li><li>Pediatric patients with Heterozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia (HeFH): 5-10 mg/day for patients aged 8 to less than 10 years, and 5-20 mg/day for patients aged 10 to 17 years.</li><li>Pediatric patients with Homozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia (HoFH): 20 mg/day for patients aged 7 to 17 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Rosu 40 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Rosu 40 mg can be taken with or without food, at any time of day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Rosu 40 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyclosporine:</strong> Increases exposure; the recommended dose should not exceed 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Gemfibrozil:</strong> Avoid combining these drugs. If necessary, limit the dosage to 10 mg once daily when used together.</li><li><strong>Lopinavir/Ritonavir or Atazanavir/Ritonavir:</strong> Can elevate exposure; the recommended dose should be limited to 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Coumarin anticoagulants:</strong> Can prolong the INR. Achieve a stable INR before initiating treatment and monitor it frequently.</li><li><strong>Concomitant lipid-lowering therapies:</strong> Fibrates and niacin may increase the risk of skeletal muscle effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to product components</li><li>Liver disease, which may include unexplained persistent elevations in hepatic transaminase levels</li><li>Pregnant women and women who may become pregnant</li><li>Nursing mothers</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Rosu 40 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Generally well tolerated, but may cause:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Headache</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Myalgia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Constipation</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Asthenia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Abdominal pain</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety during pregnancy has not been established.</li><li>It is not known whether Rosu 40 mg is excreted in human milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Skeletal muscle effects (e.g., myopathy and rhabdomyolysis):</strong> Risks increase with the use of the 40 mg dose, advanced age (&gt;65 years), hypothyroidism, renal impairment, and the combination with cyclosporine, lopinavir/ritonavir, atazanavir/ritonavir, or certain other lipid-lowering drugs. Patients should report any unexplained muscle pain, tenderness, or weakness. Rosu 40 mg should be discontinued if these signs occur.</li><li><strong>Liver enzyme abnormalities and monitoring:</strong> Persistent elevations in hepatic transaminases can occur. Liver enzymes should be monitored before and during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children:</strong> The safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '1844_1742482942.jpg', 17, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 20:02:22', '2025-05-12 18:22:10'),
(3725, 'Floren', 'Flibanserin', 50.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Floren 100 mg</strong></p><p> Floren 100 mg is indicated for the treatment of premenopausal women with acquired, generalized hypoactive sexual desire disorder (HSDD), as characterized by low sexual desire that causes marked distress or interpersonal difficulty and is not due to:</p><ul><li>A co-existing medical or psychiatric condition,</li><li>Problems within the relationship, or</li><li>The effects of a medication or other drug substance.</li></ul><p>Acquired HSDD refers to HSDD that develops in a patient who previously had no problems with sexual desire. Generalized HSDD refers to HSDD that occurs regardless of the type of stimulation, situation, or partner.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Floren 100 mg</strong></p><p> The recommended dosage of Floren 100 mg is 100 mg administered orally once per day at bedtime. Floren 100 mg is dosed at bedtime because administration during waking hours increases the risks of hypotension, syncope, accidental injury, and central nervous system (CNS) depression (such as somnolence and sedation). If a dose of Floren 100 mg is missed at bedtime, instruct the patient to take the next dose at bedtime on the next day. Instruct the patient not to double the next dose.</p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Floren 100 mg is not indicated for use in pediatric patients.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Floren 100 mg is not indicated for use in geriatric patients. Safety and effectiveness have not been established in geriatric patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Floren 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral Contraceptives and Other Weak CYP3A4 Inhibitors:</strong> Increases Floren 100 mg exposures and incidence of adverse reactions.</li><li><strong>Strong CYP2C19 Inhibitors:</strong> Increases Floren 100 mg exposure, which may increase the risk of hypotension, syncope, and CNS depression.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 Inducers:</strong> Use of Floren 100 mg is not recommended; Floren 100 mg concentrations are substantially reduced.</li><li><strong>Digoxin:</strong> Increases digoxin concentrations, which may lead to digoxin toxicity. Increase monitoring of digoxin concentrations.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Floren 100 mg is contraindicated:</p><ul><li>With use of alcohol</li><li>With concomitant use of moderate or strong CYP3A4 inhibitors</li><li>In patients with hepatic impairment</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Floren 100 mg</strong></p><p> Most common adverse reactions include:</p><ul><li>Dizziness</li><li>Somnolence</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Fatigue</li><li>Insomnia</li><li>Dry mouth</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypotension and Syncope with Floren 100 mg Alone:</strong> Patients with pre-syncope should immediately lie supine and promptly seek medical help if symptoms do not resolve.</li><li><strong>Central Nervous System (CNS) Depression (e.g., Somnolence, Sedation):</strong> Can occur with Floren 100 mg alone. Exacerbated by other CNS depressants and in settings where Floren 100 mg concentrations are increased. Patients should avoid activities requiring full alertness (e.g., operating machinery or driving) until at least 6 hours after each dose and until they know how Floren 100 mg affects them.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SNRI)</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Flibansetrin has a high affinity for serotonin receptors in the brain: it acts as an agonist on 5-HT1A and an antagonist on 5-HT2A. In vivo, Floren 100 mg binds equally to 5-HT1A and 5-HT2A receptors. However, under higher levels of brain 5-HT (i.e., under stress), Floren 100 mg may occupy 5-HT2A receptors in a higher proportion than 5-HT1A receptors. It may also moderately antagonize D4 (dopamine) receptors and 5-HT2B and 5-HT2C. Its action on neurotransmitter receptors may contribute to the reduction in serotonin levels and an increase in dopamine and norepinephrine levels, all of which may play a part in reward processing.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> There are no studies of Floren 100 mg in pregnant women to inform whether there is a drug-associated risk in humans. Floren 100 mg is excreted in rat milk. It is unknown whether Floren 100 mg is present in human milk, whether Floren 100 mg has effects on the breastfed infant, or whether Floren 100 mg affects milk production. Because of the potential for serious adverse reactions, including sedation in a breastfed infant, breastfeeding is not recommended during treatment with Floren 100 mg.</p>', 'active', '1101_1742488254.jpg', 53, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 21:30:54', '2025-03-20 21:30:54'),
(3726, 'Skilin', 'Permethrin', 30.00, 1, 'Cream', '15 gm tube', 'Cream', '5 percent w per w', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Skilin 5% w/w</strong></p><p> Skilin 5% w/w cream is indicated for the treatment of scabies and crab lice infestations (Pediculosis). Scabies can affect everyone in the family. If one is infected, all family members must be treated together.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Parasiticidal preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Skilin 5% w/w induces electrochemical abnormalities across the membranes of excitable cells, leading to sensory hyperexcitability, incoordination, and prostration. Skilin 5% w/w is rapidly metabolized in mammals by ester hydrolysis to inactive metabolites, which are excreted primarily in the urine. The principal metabolites of Skilin 5% w/w are detectable in the urine within hours of a whole-body application of the cream to healthy volunteers or scabies patients. The highest levels of excretion are detectable within the first 48 hours, but very low levels of metabolites are still detectable in the urine of some individuals 28 days after treatment.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Skilin 5% w/w</strong></p><p> <strong>Recommended duration of treatment:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years:</strong> A full tube</li><li><strong>Children aged 6-12 years:</strong> Up to ½ of a tube</li><li><strong>Children aged 1-5 years:</strong> Up to ¼ of a tube</li><li><strong>Children aged 2 months to 1 year:</strong> Up to ⅛ of a tube</li><li><strong>Children less than 2 months:</strong> Dose is not established</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Skilin 5% w/w</strong></p><p> Before application of Skilin 5% w/w cream, the skin should be clean, cool, and dry. Do not have a hot shower or bath before applying.</p><ul><li><strong>For adults and children over 2 years:</strong> Apply the cream to the whole body from the neck down, rubbing lightly into the skin until the cream disappears. It is important to include all skin surfaces, such as between the fingers and toes, under the nails, and on the soles of the feet.</li><li><strong>For babies under 2 years:</strong> Apply to the face, neck, ears, and scalp as well, only avoiding the area immediately around the eyes and mouth.</li></ul><p>Leave the cream on for at least 8 hours before washing off. Reapply to any area that may be washed during the 8-hour treatment time (such as after washing the hands). If necessary, Skilin 5% w/w cream should be used again after 7 days as per the advice of the doctor.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Skilin 5% w/w</strong></p><p> The treatment of eczematous-like reactions with corticosteroids should be withheld before treatment with Skilin 5% w/w, as there is a risk of exacerbating the scabies infestation by reducing the immune response to the mite.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Skilin 5% w/w is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of pyrethroids or Skilin 5% w/w. Nursing staff who routinely apply Skilin 5% w/w may wear gloves to avoid any possible irritation to the hands.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Skilin 5% w/w</strong></p><p> In scabies patients, skin discomfort, usually described as burning, stinging, or tingling, occurs in a few individuals soon after the cream is applied. Other transient signs and symptoms of irritation include:</p><ul><li>Erythema</li><li>Edema</li><li>Eczema</li><li>Rash</li><li>Pruritus</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There are limited data on the use of Skilin 5% w/w in pregnancy, which provide no indication of any risk to the fetus. Furthermore, the amount of Skilin 5% w/w absorbed systemically following a whole-body application is extremely low. The negative mutagenicity tests and the very low mammalian toxicity suggest that any risk to the fetus following treatment with Skilin 5% w/w is minimal.</p><p>Despite this, Skilin 5% w/w is suggested not to be used during pregnancy. It is not known whether Skilin 5% w/w is excreted in human breast milk, so it should not be used in nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Scabies infestation is often accompanied by pruritus, edema, and erythema. Treatment with Skilin 5% w/w may temporarily exacerbate these conditions. Patients should be advised to avoid contact with the eyes during application and to flush with water immediately if the cream gets in the eyes.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Skilin 5% w/w</strong></p><p> There are no reports of overdosage with Skilin 5% w/w. It is possible that excessive application of Skilin 5% w/w might result in localized adverse reactions or more severe skin reactions.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place, away from light, and keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Parasiticidal preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Skilin 5% w/w cream contains Skilin 5% w/w, a pyrethroid, which is a topical scabicidal agent for the treatment of infestation with <em>Sarcoptes scabiei</em> (scabies). It acts on the nerve cell membrane to disrupt the sodium channel current by which the polarization of the membrane is regulated. Delayed repolarization and paralysis of the pests are the consequences of this disturbance.</p><p><strong>Pharmacokinetics:</strong> The only approved route of administration is the topical route, and there are few human data available on systemic exposure or kinetics following Skilin 5% w/w application to the skin. Some presystemic metabolism occurs in the skin. Less than 0.5% of applied Skilin 5% w/w is absorbed during the first 48 hours. Absorbed Skilin 5% w/w is rapidly metabolized by ester hydrolysis, most likely in the liver, and the products are excreted primarily in the urine.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> In the absence of specific studies in pregnant women, its use in pregnancy should only follow medical advice. It is not known whether Skilin 5% w/w is excreted in human milk, so it should not be used in nursing mothers.</p>', 'active', '381_1742488527.jpg', 37, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 21:35:27', '2025-03-20 21:35:27'),
(3727, 'Razithro', 'Azithromycin Dihydrate', 55.50, 6, 'Tablet', '6 Tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Razithro 500 mg</strong></p><p> Razithro 500 mg is prescribed for infections (produced by susceptible organisms) in the upper respiratory tract, including sinusitis and pharyngitis/tonsillitis, the lower respiratory tract, including bronchitis and pneumonia, otitis media, and skin and soft tissue infections. Razithro 500 mg is recommended for the treatment of non-gonococcal urethritis and cervicitis caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> in sexually transmitted illnesses in both men and women.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Macrolides</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Razithro 500 mg belongs to the macrolide subclass of antibiotics known as azalides. To prevent microbial protein synthesis, Razithro 500 mg binds to the 50S ribosomal subunit of susceptible species. It is effective against <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>, anaerobes such as <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>, <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Bordetella pertussis</em>, <em>Bordetella parapertussis</em>, <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em>, <em>Haemophilus ducreyi</em>, and <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>, as well as gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria such as <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> and <em>Streptococcus</em> species.</p><p>Additionally, in vitro testing of Razithro 500 mg has shown action against <em>Campylobacter</em> species, <em>Toxoplasma gondii</em>, <em>Treponema pallidum</em>, <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>, <em>Legionella pneumophila</em>, and <em>Ureaplasma urealyticum</em>.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Razithro 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 500 mg once daily orally for 3 days or 500 mg once on day 1, then 250 mg once daily for the next 4 days.</li><li><strong>Sexually transmitted diseases caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em>:</strong> 1 g as a single dose or 500 mg once on day 1, followed by 250 mg once daily for the next 2 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong>Over 6 months: 10 mg/kg body weight once daily for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">15-25 kg: 200 mg (1 teaspoonful) for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">26-35 kg: 300 mg (1½ teaspoonfuls) for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">36-45 kg: 400 mg (2 teaspoonfuls) for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Typhoid fever:</strong> 500 mg (2½ teaspoonfuls) once daily for 7-10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Razithro 500 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Procedure for suspension preparation:</strong></p><ol><li>Shake the bottle to loosen the powder.</li><li>Fill the bottle with boiled and cooled water up to the watermark on the label.</li><li>Mix thoroughly until fully combined.</li></ol><p>Razithro 500 mg should be taken at least one hour before or two hours after eating.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Razithro 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Food and antacids reduce the absorption of Razithro 500 mg.</li><li>It should be avoided in patients taking ergot alkaloids due to the potential risk of ergotism caused by interactions with the cytochrome P-450 system.</li><li>Co-administration with macrolides can raise plasma levels of cyclosporin and digoxin, requiring caution.</li><li>No significant interaction has been found with warfarin, theophylline, carbamazepine, methylprednisolone, or cimetidine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Patients hypersensitive to Razithro 500 mg or any other macrolide antibiotic should not take it.</li><li>It should not be administered with ergot derivatives.</li><li>Patients with liver disorders should avoid Razithro 500 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Razithro 500 mg</strong></p><p> Side effects are infrequent and usually resolve upon discontinuation of the drug. They include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Abdominal pain or cramps</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Headaches</li><li>Skin rashes</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category B – Animal reproduction studies have shown no evidence of harm to the fetus, but adequate, well-controlled studies in pregnant women are lacking. Razithro 500 mg should only be used if no other suitable options are available.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> The presence of Razithro 500 mg in breast milk is unknown. Caution should be exercised when prescribing it to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor for signs of superinfection with non-susceptible organisms, such as fungi, when using antibiotics.</li><li>No dose adjustment is required for patients with renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Razithro 500 mg</strong></p><p> There is no specific information on Razithro 500 mg overdose. However, symptoms of macrolide antibiotic overdose may include:</p><ul><li>Hearing loss</li><li>Severe nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Diarrhea</li></ul><p>Gastric lavage and supportive treatments are recommended in case of overdose.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a dry place, away from heat and light. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '202_1742488776.jpg', 63, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 21:39:36', '2025-03-20 21:39:36'),
(3728, 'Voluven', 'Hydroxyethyl Starch plus Sodium Chloride', 631.75, 1, 'Bottle', '500 ml bottle', 'IV Infusion', '6 percent plus 0.9 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Voluven 6%+0.9%</strong></p><p> Hydroxyethyl starch (HES 130/0.4) in an isotonic electrolyte solution for intravenous infusion belongs to a group of medicines known as plasma volume substitutes. These medicines increase and maintain circulating blood volume for several hours, helping to stabilize blood pressure. This solution is used for the treatment and prevention of low blood volumes (hypovolemia) in adults and children.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p> Each 1000 ml solution for infusion contains:</p><ul><li>Poly (O-2-hydroxyethyl) starch: 60.00 g</li><li>Sodium acetate trihydrate: 4.63 g</li><li>Sodium chloride: 6.02 g</li><li>Potassium chloride: 0.30 g</li><li>Magnesium chloride hexahydrate: 0.30 g</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Plasma expanders</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Colloidal solution volume expander that contains hydroxyethyl starch.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Voluven 6%+0.9%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> Up to 50 mL per kg of body weight per day (equivalent to 3 g hydroxyethyl starch and 7.7 mEq sodium per kg of body weight). This is equivalent to 3500 mL for a 70 kg patient.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric:Newborns and infants:</strong> Dosage should be tailored to individual patient needs.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>2-12 years:</strong> Mean dose of 36 mL/kg IV.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Initial dose:</strong> Give 10-20 mL by slow IV infusion and monitor for anaphylactoid reaction.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Voluven 6%+0.9%</strong></p><p> This solution will be administered under the direct supervision of a physician, who will closely control the amount given.</p><ul><li><strong>Mode of administration:</strong> Given by intravenous infusion (IV drip).</li><li><strong>Infusion speed &amp; amount:</strong> Determined based on the patient\'s needs, condition, and maximum daily dose.</li><li><strong>Maximum daily dose:</strong> Up to 50 ml/kg of body weight.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Children and Adolescents</strong></p><ul><li>The doctor will carefully evaluate the advantages and disadvantages before use in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Management</strong></p><ul><li>The doctor will ensure that the correct amount is given.</li><li>If overdose occurs, the solution may be stopped immediately, and a diuretic may be administered to remove excess fluid.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Voluven 6%+0.9%</strong></p><ul><li>The safety and compatibility of additives, other than citrate anticoagulants, have not been established.</li><li><strong>Incompatibility:</strong> Must not be mixed with other medicinal products in the absence of compatibility studies.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Do not use this solution if you:</p><ul><li>Have severe generalized infection (sepsis).</li><li>Have severe liver disease.</li><li>Are allergic (hypersensitive) to hydroxyethyl starch.</li><li>Have fluid overload, especially if you have pulmonary edema or congestive heart failure.</li><li>Have pre-existing blood clotting (coagulation) or bleeding disorders.</li><li>Have kidney failure with little or no urine output (unless due to hypovolemia).</li><li>Receive dialysis treatment.</li><li>Have severe hyperkalemia, hypernatremia, or hyperchloremia.</li><li>Have intracranial bleeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Voluven 6%+0.9%</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Common (may affect up to 1 in 10 people):</strong>Itching, particularly with long-term, high-dose use.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Blood dilution effects at high doses, leading to prolonged clotting time.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increased serum amylase levels, which may interfere with pancreatitis diagnosis. However, this solution does not cause pancreatitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> No clinical data is available on the effects of this solution in pregnancy. It should only be used if the benefits outweigh the potential risks to the baby.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is unknown if hydroxyethyl starch is excreted in breast milk. Caution is advised, and the doctor may recommend discontinuing breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Inform your doctor if you have:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Liver impairment</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Heart or circulation problems</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Blood clotting (coagulation) disorders</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Kidney disease (must be avoided in kidney disease and not used in dialysis patients)</li><li>In some severe conditions, salt solutions may be considered as an alternative.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Do not freeze.</li><li>Store below 25°C.</li></ul>', 'active', '725_1742489215.jpg', 10, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 21:46:55', '2025-03-20 21:46:55'),
(3729, 'Tyrokin', 'Imatinib Mesylate', 100.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Tyrokin 100 mg</strong></p><p> Newly diagnosed adult and pediatric patients with Philadelphia chromosome-positive chronic myeloid leukemia (Ph+CML) in chronic phase. Patients with Philadelphia chromosome-positive chronic myeloid leukemia in blast crisis, accelerated phase, or in chronic phase after failure of interferon-alpha therapy.</p><p> Adult patients with relapsed or refractory Philadelphia chromosome-positive acute lymphoblastic leukemia (Ph+ ALL). Pediatric patients with newly diagnosed Philadelphia chromosome-positive acute lymphoblastic leukemia (Ph+ ALL) in combination with chemotherapy.</p><p> Adult patients with myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative diseases associated with platelet-derived growth factor receptor (PDGFR) gene re-arrangements.</p><p> Adult patients with aggressive systemic mastocytosis without the D816V c-Kit mutation or with c-Kit mutational status unknown.</p><p> Adult patients with hypereosinophilic syndrome and/or chronic eosinophilic leukemia who have the FIP1L1-PDGFRα fusion kinase and for patients with HES and/or CEL who are FIP1L1-PDGFRα fusion kinase negative or unknown.</p><p> Adult patients with unresectable, recurrent and/or metastatic dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans.</p><p> Patients with Kit (CD117) positive unresectable and/or metastatic malignant gastrointestinal stromal tumors.</p><p> Adjuvant treatment of adult patients following complete gross resection of Kit (CD117) positive GIST.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Targeted Cancer Therapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Imatinib is a tyrosine kinase inhibitor that inhibits the BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase created by the Philadelphia chromosome abnormality in chronic myeloid leukemia (CML). It blocks proliferation and induces apoptosis in BCR-ABL positive cell lines, as well as fresh leukemic cells from Philadelphia chromosome-positive CML. Imatinib also inhibits receptor kinases for platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF) and stem cell factor (SCF), c-kit, PDGF-, and SCF-mediated cellular events.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Tyrokin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults with Ph+ CML CP:</strong> 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adults with Ph+ CML AP or BC:</strong> 600 mg/day</li><li><strong>Pediatrics with Ph+ CML CP:</strong> 340 mg/m²/day</li><li><strong>Adults with Ph+ ALL:</strong> 600 mg/day</li><li><strong>Pediatrics with Ph+ ALL:</strong> 340 mg/m²/day</li><li><strong>Adults with MDS/MPD:</strong> 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adults with ASM:</strong> 100 mg/day or 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adults with HES/CEL:</strong> 100 mg/day or 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adults with DFSP:</strong> 800 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adults with metastatic and/or unresectable GIST:</strong> 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adjuvant treatment of adults with GIST:</strong> 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Patients with mild to moderate hepatic impairment:</strong> 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Patients with severe hepatic impairment:</strong> 300 mg/day</li></ul><p>All doses should be taken with a meal and a large glass of water. Doses of 400 mg or 600 mg should be administered once daily, whereas a dose of 800 mg should be administered as 400 mg twice a day. Imatinib can be dissolved in water or apple juice for patients having difficulty swallowing.</p><p><strong>Administration of Tyrokin 100 mg</strong></p><p> Should be taken with food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Tyrokin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Agents Inducing CYP3A Metabolism:</strong> Strong CYP3A4 inducers may reduce total exposure of imatinib; consider alternative agents.</li><li><strong>Agents Inhibiting CYP3A Metabolism:</strong> Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors may significantly increase imatinib exposure. Avoid grapefruit juice.</li><li><strong>Interactions with Drugs Metabolized by CYP3A4:</strong> Imatinib increases plasma concentration of CYP3A4 metabolized drugs (e.g., triazolo-benzodiazepines, calcium channel blockers, certain HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors).</li><li><strong>Interactions with Drugs Metabolized by CYP2D6:</strong> Caution when administering Imatinib with CYP2D6 substrates that have a narrow therapeutic window.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity. Lactation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Tyrokin 100 mg</strong></p><p> Serious adverse reactions include:</p><ul><li>Fluid retention and edema</li><li>Hematologic toxicity</li><li>Congestive heart failure and left ventricular dysfunction</li><li>Hepatotoxicity</li><li>Hemorrhage</li><li>Gastrointestinal disorders</li><li>Hypereosinophilic cardiac toxicity</li><li>Dermatologic toxicities</li><li>Hypothyroidism</li><li>Growth retardation in children and adolescents</li><li>Tumor lysis syndrome</li><li>Impairments related to driving and using machinery</li><li>Renal toxicity</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> <strong>Category D:</strong> There is positive evidence of human fetal risk, but benefits may outweigh risks in life-threatening situations.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor weight regularly due to fluid retention.</li><li>Cytopenias (anemia, neutropenia, thrombocytopenia) may occur; manage with dose modifications.</li><li>Monitor cardiac function due to risk of congestive heart failure.</li><li>Monitor liver function monthly due to potential hepatotoxicity.</li><li>Risk of hemorrhage, particularly in newly diagnosed CML and GIST patients.</li><li>Gastrointestinal perforations have been reported.</li><li>Bullous dermatologic reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome have occurred.</li><li>Growth retardation in children requires close monitoring.</li><li>Tumor lysis syndrome requires close monitoring.</li><li>Impairments related to driving and operating machinery have been reported.</li><li>Renal toxicity requires evaluation of renal function during therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Tyrokin 100 mg</strong></p><p> Symptoms of overdose include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, rash, fatigue, muscle spasms, thrombocytopenia, pancytopenia, abdominal pain, and headache. In the event of overdose, symptomatic treatment and monitoring are required.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C in a cool and dry place. Keep away from light and out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Patients on potent CYP3A4 inducers:</strong> Increase dose by 50% and monitor response.</li><li><strong>Severe hepatic impairment:</strong> Reduce dose by 25%.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Targeted Cancer Therapy, Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Imatinib is a small molecule protein-tyrosine kinase inhibitor that inhibits Bcr-Abl tyrosine kinase and receptor TKs (Kit, DDR1, DDR2, CSF-1R, PDGFR-alpha, and PDGFR-beta).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Women of childbearing potential:</strong> Must use effective contraception during treatment and for 15 days after stopping Imatinib.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Imatinib should not be used unless necessary. If used, patients should be informed of fetal risks.</li><li><strong>Breast-feeding:</strong> Women should not breast-feed during treatment and for 15 days after stopping Imatinib.</li><li><strong>Fertility:</strong> No definitive studies on fertility impact in humans, but effects on reproductive parameters have been observed in animal studies.</li></ul>', 'active', '1040_1742489550.jpg', 6, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 21:52:30', '2025-03-20 21:52:30'),
(3730, 'Tensfree', 'Bromazepam', 4.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '3 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Tensfree 3 mg</strong></p><p> Tensfree 3 mg is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Emotional disturbances, such as acute tension and anxiety states</li><li>Difficulties in interpersonal contact</li><li>Agitation, insomnia, anxious and agitated depressive reactions</li><li>Functional disturbances in the cardiovascular and respiratory systems, including pseudoangina pectoris, pericardial anxiety, tachycardia, emotiogenic hypertension, dyspnea, and hyperventilation</li><li>Disturbances in the gastrointestinal tract, such as irritable bowel syndrome, epigastric pain, spasm, bloating, and diarrhea</li><li>Disturbances in the urinary tract, including frequency, irritable bladder, and dysmenorrhea</li><li>Psychosomatic disorders, such as psychogenic headache, asthma, gastric and duodenal ulcers</li><li>Emotional reactions to chronic organic diseases</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Benzodiazepine sedatives</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Tensfree 3 mg is a powerful psychotropic agent. At lower doses, it selectively reduces tension and anxiety, while at higher doses, it exhibits sedative and muscle-relaxant properties. It binds to the GABA-A receptor, producing a conformational change and potentiating its inhibitory effects. Other neurotransmitters are not influenced.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Tensfree 3 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Standard dosage</strong>: The average outpatient dose is 1.5-3 mg up to three times daily. Treatment should start with a low dose and gradually increase to the optimum level.</li><li><strong>Severe cases (hospitalized patients)</strong>: 6-12 mg, 2 or 3 times daily. The total treatment period should generally not exceed 8-12 weeks, although exceptions may require re-evaluation.</li><li><strong>Elderly and debilitated patients</strong>: Lower doses are required, particularly in those with impaired hepatic function.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Not usually indicated, but if necessary, the dose should be adjusted based on body weight (approximately 0.1-0.3 mg/kg).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Tensfree 3 mg</strong></p><p> Tensfree 3 mg tablets are for oral administration.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Tensfree 3 mg</strong></p><p> Tensfree 3 mg enhances the sedative effects of other centrally active drugs, including:</p><ul><li>Antidepressants</li><li>Hypnotics</li><li>Narcotics</li><li>Antipsychotics</li><li>Sedatives</li><li>Antiepileptics</li><li>Sedative antihistamines</li><li>Anesthetics</li></ul><p>Co-administration with <strong>cimetidine</strong> may prolong its elimination half-life. Alcohol should be avoided, as it intensifies the sedative effect.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Tensfree 3 mg is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Tensfree 3 mg</li><li>Severe respiratory insufficiency</li><li>Severe hepatic insufficiency</li><li>Sleep apnea syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Tensfree 3 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Fatigue</li><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Muscle weakness</li><li>Numbed muscle</li><li>Reduced alertness</li><li>Confusion</li><li>Headache</li><li>Ataxia</li></ul><p>These effects are more common at the start of therapy and typically subside with continued use. <strong>Anterograde amnesia</strong> may occur at therapeutic doses.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The safety of Tensfree 3 mg during pregnancy has not been established. Since it is excreted in breast milk, its use should be avoided during lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dependence risk</strong>: Benzodiazepines may cause physical and psychological dependence, particularly in predisposed individuals with a history of alcohol or drug abuse.</li><li><strong>Withdrawal symptoms</strong>: Abrupt discontinuation may cause withdrawal effects such as headache, muscle pain, extreme anxiety, tension, confusion, and irritability. It is recommended to <strong>taper the dose gradually</strong>.</li><li><strong>Not for primary treatment of psychotic insomnia</strong></li><li><strong>Caution with activities requiring alertness</strong>, such as driving or operating machinery</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Benzodiazepine sedatives</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Tensfree 3 mg is a psychotropic agent that reduces tension and anxiety at lower doses and acts as a sedative and muscle relaxant at higher doses. It binds to GABA-A receptors, enhancing their inhibitory effects, with no influence on other neurotransmitters.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The safety of Tensfree 3 mg during pregnancy is not established. Since it is excreted in breast milk, it should be avoided during lactation.</p>', 'active', '211_1742489869.jpg', 64, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 21:57:49', '2025-03-20 21:57:49');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3731, 'Xinolax DR', 'Duloxetine Hydrochloride', 10.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '30 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xinolax DR 30 mg</strong></p><p> Duloxetine is a serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SNRI) indicated for:</p><ul><li>Major Depressive Disorder (MDD)</li><li>Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD)</li><li>Diabetic Peripheral Neuropathic Pain (DPNP)</li><li>Fibromyalgia</li><li>Chronic Musculoskeletal Pain</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SNRI)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Duloxetine is a combined serotonin (5-HT) and noradrenaline (NE) reuptake inhibitor. It weakly inhibits dopamine reuptake with no significant affinity for histaminergic, dopaminergic, cholinergic, and adrenergic receptors. Duloxetine dose-dependently increases extracellular levels of serotonin and noradrenaline in various brain areas.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xinolax DR 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Major Depressive Disorder (MDD)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Starting dose: 20-30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Target dose: 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum dose: 120 mg/day</li><li><strong>Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Starting dose: 60 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Target dose: 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum dose: 120 mg/day</li><li><strong>Diabetic Peripheral Neuropathic Pain (DPNP)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Starting dose: 60 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Target dose: 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum dose: 60 mg/day</li><li><strong>Fibromyalgia</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Starting dose: 30 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Target dose: 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum dose: 60 mg/day</li><li><strong>Chronic Musculoskeletal Pain</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Starting dose: 30 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Target dose: 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum dose: 60 mg/day</li><li><strong>Urinary Stress Incontinence</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Starting dose: 40 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Target dose: 80 mg/day (40 mg twice daily)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum dose: 80 mg/day</li></ul><p>Some patients may benefit from starting at 30 mg once daily. Doses above 60 mg/day have not been shown to provide additional benefit and may increase adverse reactions. A gradual dose reduction is recommended to avoid discontinuation symptoms.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Xinolax DR 30 mg</strong></p><p> Duloxetine is metabolized by <strong>CYP1A2 and CYP2D6</strong> enzymes.</p><ul><li><strong>CYP1A2 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., fluvoxamine, cimetidine, ciprofloxacin) increase Duloxetine levels and should be avoided.</li><li><strong>CYP2D6 inhibitors</strong> may also elevate Duloxetine levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Duloxetine is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Duloxetine or its ingredients</li><li>Bipolar depression</li><li>Substantial alcohol use or chronic liver disease</li><li>Controlled narrow-angle glaucoma (due to risk of mydriasis)</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xinolax DR 30 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Decreased appetite</li><li>Fatigue</li><li>Somnolence</li><li>Increased sweating (hyperhidrosis)</li><li>Asthenia</li></ul><p>Duloxetine may slightly increase blood pressure. No significant differences were observed in QT, PR, and QRS intervals between Duloxetine and placebo.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C. Duloxetine should only be used if the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Labor &amp; Delivery</strong>: Effects on labor and delivery in humans are unknown.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is unknown whether Duloxetine and its metabolites are excreted in human milk; nursing while on Duloxetine is not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Suicidality &amp; Mood Monitoring</strong>: Patients should be closely monitored for worsening depression, suicidality, and unusual behavior changes, especially during the initial months or dose adjustments.</li><li><strong>Blood Pressure</strong>: Blood pressure should be monitored before and during treatment.</li><li><strong>Bleeding Risk</strong>: Increased bleeding risk when combined with NSAIDs, aspirin, or anticoagulants.</li><li><strong>History of Mania</strong>: Use cautiously in patients with a history of mania.</li><li><strong>Seizure Disorder</strong>: Should be prescribed with care in patients with a history of seizures.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xinolax DR 30 mg</strong></p><p> There is limited clinical experience with Duloxetine overdose. No specific antidote exists. Treatment includes:</p><ul><li>Maintaining airway, oxygenation, and ventilation</li><li>Monitoring cardiac rhythm and vital signs</li><li>Avoiding emesis induction</li><li>Considering gastric lavage and activated charcoal if necessary</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C</li><li>Keep away from light</li><li>Keep out of reach of children</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and efficacy in children have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SNRI)</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Xinolax DR 30 mg is a selective serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SNRI) with weak dopamine reuptake inhibition. It has no significant affinity for dopaminergic, adrenergic, cholinergic, histaminergic, opioid, glutamate, or GABA receptors. Duloxetine does not inhibit monoamine oxidase (MAO). It is well absorbed and eliminated primarily via hepatic metabolism.</p>', 'active', '1862_1742490948.jpg', 7, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 22:15:48', '2025-03-20 22:15:48'),
(3732, 'Tocef DS', 'Cefixime Trihydrate', 320.00, 1, 'Bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '200 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Tocef DS 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Cefixime is an orally active cephalosporin antibiotic with marked in-vitro bactericidal activity against a wide variety of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms. Tocef DS 200 mg/5 ml is indicated for the treatment of the following acute infections when caused by susceptible microorganisms:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper Respiratory Tract Infections (URTI):</strong> e.g., otitis media and other URTIs where the causative organism is known or suspected to be resistant to commonly used antibiotics, or where treatment failure may carry significant risk.</li><li><strong>Lower Respiratory Tract Infections:</strong> e.g., bronchitis.</li><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections:</strong> e.g., cystitis, cystourethritis, pyelonephritis.</li></ul><p>Clinical efficacy has been demonstrated in infections caused by commonly occurring pathogens including <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae, Streptococcus pyogenes, Escherichia coli, Proteus mirabilis, Klebsiella species, Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase positive and negative), <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase positive and negative), and <em>Enterobacter</em> species. Cefixime is highly stable in the presence of beta-lactamase enzymes.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Third-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cefixime is a third-generation semisynthetic cephalosporin antibiotic for oral administration. It is bactericidal against a broad spectrum of gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria by interfering with bacterial cell wall synthesis. It is highly stable in the presence of Beta-lactamase enzymes, making it effective against many organisms resistant to penicillins and some cephalosporins. The absorption rate is about 40% to 50%, whether taken with or without food.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Tocef DS 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> The usual course of treatment is 7 days, but it may be extended up to 14 days depending on the severity of the infection.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years:</strong> 200-400 mg daily as a single dose or in two divided doses.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated cervical/urethral gonococcal infections:</strong> Single oral dose of 400 mg.</li><li><strong>Children (6 months or older):</strong> Usually 8 mg/kg/day as a single dose or in two divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">½-1 year: 75 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-4 years: 100 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-10 years: 200 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">11-12 years: 300 mg daily</li><li><strong>Typhoid fever:</strong> 10 mg/kg/day for 14 days.</li><li><strong>Children under 6 months:</strong> Safety and efficacy not established.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage in Renal Impairment</strong></p><p> Cefixime may be administered in patients with impaired renal function.</p><ul><li><strong>Creatinine clearance ≥ 20 ml/min:</strong> Normal dose and schedule.</li><li><strong>Creatinine clearance &lt; 20 ml/min:</strong> Do not exceed 200 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Patients on dialysis:</strong> Follow the same dose recommendation as those with creatinine clearance &lt; 20 ml/min.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Tocef DS 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Cephalosporins may cause an increase in prothrombin times.</li><li>Caution is advised in patients receiving anticoagulant therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to cephalosporin antibiotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Tocef DS 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Cefixime is generally well tolerated. Most adverse effects are mild and self-limiting.</p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Diarrhea, stool changes, nausea, abdominal pain, dyspepsia, vomiting, flatulence, pseudomembranous colitis.</li><li><strong>Central Nervous System:</strong> Headache, dizziness.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity Reactions:</strong> Rash, pruritus, urticaria, drug fever, arthralgia.</li><li><strong>Hematologic &amp; Clinical Chemistry:</strong> Thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, eosinophilia, mild transient liver and renal function changes.</li><li><strong>Miscellaneous:</strong> Genital pruritus, vaginitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> No adequate, well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Use only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Unknown if excreted in human milk. Caution is advised when administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with a history of drug hypersensitivity.</li><li>Cephalosporins should be used cautiously in penicillin-sensitive patients due to possible cross-allergenicity.</li><li>Severe allergic reactions (including anaphylaxis) have been reported.</li><li>Administer with caution in patients with significantly impaired renal function.</li><li>Broad-spectrum antibiotic use may lead to <em>Clostridium difficile</em>-associated diarrhea.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Tocef DS 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Gastric lavage may be necessary.</li><li>No specific antidote exists.</li><li>Cefixime is not significantly removed by hemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis.</li><li>Adverse effects in individuals taking doses up to 2 g did not differ significantly from those observed at recommended doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C.</li><li>Protect from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Third-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Cefixime is a third-generation cephalosporin antibiotic that interferes with bacterial cell wall synthesis, leading to bacterial cell death. It is highly stable against Beta-lactamase enzymes, making it effective against certain resistant bacteria. Absorption is 40-50%, whether taken with or without food.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women.</li><li>Use only if clearly needed.</li><li>Unknown whether excreted in human milk, so caution is advised in nursing mothers.</li></ul>', 'active', '312_1742491168.jpg', 37, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 22:19:28', '2025-03-20 22:19:28'),
(3733, 'Sterisol', 'Glycine', 165.00, 1, 'Bag', '2000 ml bag', 'Solution', '1.5 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sterisol 1.5%</strong></p><p> This is indicated as the irrigating fluid of choice in:</p><ul><li>Transurethral resection of the prostate</li><li>Endoscopic procedures within the urinary tract</li><li>Urinary bladder surgery</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Irrigation Solution used in Urological Surgery</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sterisol 1.5% irrigation solution contains Sterisol 1.5%, an amino acid and a non-electrolyte. It is non-conductive and suitable for irrigation purposes. Sterisol 1.5% Irrigation Solution minimizes the risk of intravascular haemolysis, which can occur from the absorption of plain water. It is rapidly degraded in the liver by Sterisol 1.5% oxidase.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Sterisol 1.5%</strong></p><p> The total volume of solution used for irrigation depends on the judgment of the attending surgeon. A height of approximately 60 cm (2 ft) from the operating table may increase intravascular absorption of Sterisol 1.5%.</p><p><strong>Administration of Sterisol 1.5%</strong></p><p> <strong>Administration Procedure:</strong></p><ol><li>Check the infusion set and solution before use.</li><li>Tear off the protective cover of the Eurohead by pulling moderately.</li><li>Hold the Eurohead lightly, avoiding the bag.</li><li>Open the flow regulator fully and hold the giving set at the top white area, avoiding the membrane venting region.</li><li>Insert the spike of the administration set into the Eurohead and secure the connector firmly to the needle.</li><li>Allow fluid to flow to the needle tip gradually, then close.</li><li>Remove the protective cover of the needle.</li><li>Locate and clean the venipuncture site with an antiseptic solution, then insert the needle.</li><li>Securely tape the puncture site, wings, and tubing.</li><li>Start the infusion while adjusting the drip speed.</li></ol><p><strong>Interaction of Sterisol 1.5%</strong></p><p> Additives may be incompatible. When introducing additives, use aseptic techniques, mix thoroughly, and do not store.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Not for injection via any route.</li><li>Contraindicated in patients with anuria.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Sterisol 1.5%</strong></p><p> Large intravenous doses of Sterisol 1.5% may cause nausea and salivation. Systemic absorption can lead to:</p><ul><li>Electrolyte loss</li><li>Diuresis</li><li>Edema</li><li>Thirst and dehydration</li><li>Cardiovascular and pulmonary disorders</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> No data available.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with cardiovascular disease should be evaluated after transurethral resection of the prostate using Sterisol 1.5%.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired liver or kidney function.</li><li>Aseptic technique is essential during use.</li><li>Discard any unused portion.</li><li>Do not use if the bottle is leaking, the solution is cloudy, contains particles, or is past the expiry date.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at controlled room temperature. Protect from light and heat.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Irrigation Solution used in Urological Surgery</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Sterisol 1.5% irrigation solution contains Sterisol 1.5%, an amino acid and a non-electrolyte. It is non-conductive and suitable for irrigation purposes. It minimizes the risk of intravascular haemolysis due to the absorption of plain water and is rapidly degraded in the liver by Sterisol 1.5% oxidase.</p>', 'active', '333_1742491518.jpg', 17, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 22:25:18', '2025-03-20 22:25:18'),
(3734, 'Zixin', 'Azithromycin Dihydrate', 45.00, 7, 'tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zixin 500 mg</strong></p><p> Zixin 500 mg is prescribed for infections (produced by susceptible organisms) in the upper respiratory tract, including sinusitis and pharyngitis/tonsillitis, the lower respiratory tract, including bronchitis and pneumonia, otitis media, and the skin and soft tissue. Zixin 500 mg is also recommended for the treatment of non-gonococcal urethritis and cervicitis caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> in sexually transmitted illnesses in both men and women.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Macrolides</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Zixin 500 mg belongs to the macrolide subclass of antibiotics known as azalides. To prevent microbial protein synthesis, Zixin 500 mg binds to the 50S ribosomal subunit of susceptible species. <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>, anaerobes such as <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>, <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Bordetella pertussis</em>, <em>Bordetella parapertussis</em>, <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em>, <em>Haemophilus ducreyi</em>, and <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>, as well as gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria like <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus</em>, and others, are affected by Zixin 500 mg. In vitro testing has shown activity against <em>Campylobacter</em> sp., <em>Toxoplasma gondii</em>, <em>Treponema pallidum</em>, <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>, <em>Legionella pneumophila</em>, and <em>Hominins</em>.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Zixin 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 500 mg once daily orally for 3 days or 500 mg once on day 1, then 250 mg once on days 2-5 for 4 days. For sexually transmitted diseases caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> in adults, the dose is 1 gm given as a single dose or 500 mg once on day 1, followed by 250 mg once daily for the next 2 days.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 mg/kg body weight once daily for 3 days for children over 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">200 mg (1 teaspoonful) for 3 days if body weight is 15-25 kg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">300 mg (1½ teaspoonfuls) for 3 days if body weight is 26-35 kg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">400 mg (2 teaspoonfuls) for 3 days if body weight is 36-45 kg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In typhoid fever, 500 mg (2½ teaspoonfuls) once daily for 7-10 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zixin 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Procedure for reinstatement after suspension</strong>:</li></ul><ol><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Thoroughly shake the bottle to break up the powder.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fill the bottle with boiled and cooled water to the watermark on the label.</li><li>Mix the powder and water thoroughly to combine.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"> Take Zixin 500 mg at least one hour before or two hours after eating.</li></ol><p><strong>Interaction of Zixin 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Absorption of Zixin 500 mg is reduced in the presence of food or antacids.</li><li>Zixin 500 mg should be avoided in patients who are taking ergot alkaloids due to the potential for ergotism caused by interaction with the cytochrome P-450 system.</li><li>Caution should be taken when co-administering Zixin 500 mg with cyclosporin or digoxin, as macrolides can increase their plasma levels.</li><li>No significant interactions have been found with warfarin, theophylline, carbamazepine, methylprednisolone, or cimetidine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Zixin 500 mg or any other macrolide antibiotics.</li><li>Co-administration with ergot derivatives.</li><li>Liver disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Zixin 500 mg</strong></p><p> Low-frequency side effects may occur, which are typically reversible upon discontinuation of the medication. These side effects include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Abdominal pain or cramps</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Headaches</li><li>Skin rashes</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Zixin 500 mg is classified as pregnancy category B. Studies on animal reproduction have shown no evidence of harm to the fetus.</li><li>There are no controlled studies in pregnant women. Zixin 500 mg should only be used during pregnancy if no other suitable options are available.</li><li>The presence of Zixin 500 mg in breast milk is unknown, so caution should be exercised when administering Zixin 500 mg to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor for signs of superinfection with non-susceptible organisms, such as fungi, during antibiotic use.</li><li>No dose adjustment is necessary for patients with renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zixin 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>There is no specific information on Zixin 500 mg overdose. Symptoms of macrolide overdose may include hearing loss, extreme nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea.</li><li>Gastric lavage and supportive treatments are advised.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '862_1742491551.jpg', 50, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 22:25:51', '2025-03-20 22:25:51'),
(3735, 'Yogra', 'Sildenafil Citrate', 30.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Yogra 50 mg</strong></p><p>Sildenafil is indicated for the treatment of erectile dysfunction and pulmonary arterial hypertension.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for Erectile Dysfunction</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Sildenafil is a selective inhibitor of cyclic Guanosine Monophosphate (cGMP)-specific phosphodiesterase type 5 (PDE5) used for the treatment of erectile dysfunction. Sildenafil enhances the effect of nitric oxide (NO) by inhibiting phosphodiesterase type 5 (PDE5), which is responsible for the degradation of cGMP in the corpus cavernosum, resulting in smooth muscle relaxation and inflow of blood to the corpus cavernosum.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Yogra 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Erectile Dysfunction</strong>: For most patients, the recommended dose is 50 mg taken, as needed, approximately 1 hour before sexual activity. However, Sildenafil may be taken anywhere from 4 hours to 0.5 hours before sexual activity. Based on effectiveness and toleration, the dose may be increased to a maximum recommended dose of 100 mg or decreased to 25 mg. The maximum recommended dosing frequency is once per day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Factors such as age &gt;65, hepatic impairment, severe renal impairment, and the concomitant use of potent cytochrome P450 3A4 inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole, itraconazole, erythromycin, saquinavir) may increase plasma levels of Sildenafil. In these cases, a starting dose of 25 mg is recommended.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sildenafil potentiates the hypotensive effects of nitrates, and its administration is contraindicated in patients using nitrates or nitric oxide donors in any form.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">When co-administered with an alpha-blocker, patients should be stable on alpha-blocker therapy before initiating Sildenafil, and it should be started at the lowest dose.</li><li><strong>Pulmonary Arterial Hypertension</strong>: The recommended dose of Yogra 50 mg is 20 mg three times a day, taken approximately 4-6 hours apart, with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Yogra 50 mg</strong></p><p>Sildenafil may take longer to work if taken with a heavy meal.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Yogra 50 mg</strong></p><p>Sildenafil metabolism is primarily mediated by the cytochrome P450 (CYP) isoforms 3A4 (major) and 2C9 (minor). Inhibitors of these enzymes may reduce Sildenafil clearance, while inducers may increase its clearance.</p><ul><li>Co-administration with CYP3A4 inhibitors like ketoconazole, erythromycin, or ritonavir can increase Sildenafil plasma levels, sometimes significantly.</li><li>Co-administration with CYP3A4 inducers like rifampin may decrease Sildenafil plasma levels.</li><li>Concomitant use of Sildenafil with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors should be done with caution and may require dose adjustments.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of Sildenafil.</li><li>Concomitant use with organic nitrates or nitric oxide donors, as it can potentiate the hypotensive effects of nitrates.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Yogra 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Body as a Whole</strong>: Face edema, photosensitivity, shock, asthenia, pain, chills, accidental fall, abdominal pain, allergic reactions, chest pain, accidental injury.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular</strong>: Angina pectoris, AV block, migraine, syncope, tachycardia, palpitation, hypotension, postural hypotension, myocardial ischemia, cerebral thrombosis, cardiac arrest, heart failure.</li><li><strong>Digestive</strong>: Vomiting, glossitis, colitis, dysphagia, gastritis, gastroenteritis, esophagitis, stomatitis, dry mouth, liver function test abnormalities, rectal hemorrhage, gingivitis.</li><li><strong>Hemic and Lymphatic</strong>: Anemia, leukopenia.</li><li><strong>Metabolic and Nutritional</strong>: Thirst, edema, gout, unstable diabetes, hyperglycemia, peripheral edema, hyperuricemia, hypoglycemic reactions, hypernatremia.</li><li><strong>Musculoskeletal</strong>: Arthritis, arthrosis, myalgia, tendon rupture, tenosynovitis, bone pain, myasthenia, synovitis.</li><li><strong>Nervous</strong>: Ataxia, hypertonia, neuralgia, neuropathy, paresthesia, tremor, vertigo, depression, insomnia, somnolence, abnormal dreams, decreased reflexes, hyperesthesia.</li><li><strong>Respiratory</strong>: Asthma, dyspnea, laryngitis, pharyngitis, sinusitis, bronchitis, increased sputum, cough.</li><li><strong>Skin and Appendages</strong>: Urticaria, herpes simplex, pruritus, sweating, skin ulcer, contact dermatitis, exfoliative dermatitis.</li><li><strong>Special Senses</strong>: Sudden decrease or loss of hearing, mydriasis, conjunctivitis, photophobia, tinnitus, eye pain, ear pain, eye hemorrhage, cataract, dry eyes.</li><li><strong>Urogenital</strong>: Cystitis, nocturia, urinary frequency, breast enlargement, urinary incontinence, abnormal ejaculation, genital edema, and anorgasmia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy category B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies of Sildenafil in pregnant women.</li><li>Sildenafil is not indicated for use by women.</li><li>Animal studies show no evidence of teratogenicity or embryotoxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution when combining Sildenafil with alpha-blockers, as both are vasodilators and may lower blood pressure.</li><li>Sildenafil should not be used in men for whom sexual activity is inadvisable due to cardiovascular risk.</li><li>Careful consideration is needed in patients with conditions like myocardial infarction, stroke, hypotension, hypertension, cardiac failure, and other cardiovascular diseases.</li><li>Prolonged erections (greater than 4 hours) or priapism (painful erections lasting more than 6 hours) require immediate medical attention.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Yogra 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>In clinical studies, single doses up to 800 mg caused adverse events similar to those seen at lower doses but with increased severity.</li><li>In case of overdose, supportive measures should be applied. Renal dialysis is unlikely to accelerate clearance.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Drugs for Erectile Dysfunction</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Sildenafil enhances the effects of nitric oxide (NO) by inhibiting phosphodiesterase type 5 (PDE5), resulting in increased levels of cGMP in the corpus cavernosum, leading to smooth muscle relaxation and blood flow into the penis. Sildenafil has no effect in the absence of sexual stimulation.</p><p><strong>Pharmacokinetics and Metabolism</strong></p><ul><li>Rapidly absorbed, with a mean bioavailability of 41%.</li><li>Metabolized in the liver, primarily by cytochrome P450 3A4.</li><li>Excreted mainly in the feces and to a lesser extent in the urine.</li><li>Half-life of 4 hours for both Sildenafil and its active metabolite.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacokinetics in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics</strong>: Elderly patients (65 years or older) show reduced clearance of Sildenafil, resulting in higher plasma levels.</li></ul>', 'active', '643_1742491793.jpg', 59, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 22:29:53', '2025-05-05 22:13:06'),
(3736, 'Trexonate', 'Methotrexate', 5.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Trexonate 2.5 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Neoplastic Diseases:</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment of gestational choriocarcinoma, chorioadenoma destruens, and hydatidiform mole</li><li>Acute lymphoblastic leukemia</li><li>Used in combination with other chemotherapeutic agents in the treatment of advanced-stage non-Hodgkin’s lymphomas</li><li>Treatment of breast cancer, epidermoid cancers of the head and neck, advanced mycosis fungoides (cutaneous T-cell lymphoma), and lung cancer (particularly squamous cell and small cell types)</li></ul><p><strong>Rheumatoid Arthritis including Polyarticular-Course Juvenile Rheumatoid Arthritis:</strong></p><ul><li>Indicated for adults with severe, active rheumatoid arthritis and children with active polyarticular-course juvenile rheumatoid arthritis who have not responded adequately to first-line therapy, including full-dose NSAIDs.</li></ul><p><strong>Psoriasis:</strong></p><ul><li>Indicated for the symptomatic control of severe, recalcitrant, disabling psoriasis that does not respond adequately to other forms of therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Antidote preparations, Immunosuppressant</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Trexonate 2.5 mg inhibits dihydrofolic acid reductase, preventing the reduction of dihydrofolates to tetrahydrofolates, which are necessary for purine nucleotide and thymidylate synthesis. This inhibition interferes with DNA synthesis, repair, and cellular replication. Actively proliferating tissues, such as malignant cells, bone marrow, fetal cells, buccal and intestinal mucosa, and urinary bladder cells, are more sensitive to its effects. It may impair malignant growth without causing irreversible damage to normal tissues.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Trexonate 2.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neoplastic Diseases - Choriocarcinoma and similar trophoblastic diseases:</strong> 15 to 30 mg orally or intramuscularly daily for a 5-day course. Courses are repeated 3 to 5 times as required, with rest periods between courses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia:</strong>Induction dose: 3.3 mg/m² with prednisone 60 mg/m² daily for 4 to 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 30 mg/m² orally or intramuscularly twice weekly.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Alternate maintenance: 2.5 mg/kg IV every 14 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Lymphoma - Burkitt\'s Tumor:</strong>Stages I &amp; II: 10 to 25 mg daily for 4 to 8 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Stage III: Given with other antitumor agents.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Requires several courses interposed with 7 to 10-day rest periods.</li><li><strong>Mycosis Fungoides:</strong> 5 to 50 mg once a week or 15 to 37.5 mg twice a week for patients with poor response.</li><li><strong>Breast Cancer:</strong> 40 mg/m² IV on days 1 and 8 every 4 weeks with cyclophosphamide and fluorouracil for 6-12 cycles.</li><li><strong>Head and Neck Cancer:</strong> 40 mg/m² IV weekly until disease progression or toxicity.</li><li><strong>Adult Rheumatoid Arthritis:</strong> 7.5 mg once per week (maximum: 20 mg/week).</li><li><strong>Polyarticular-Course Juvenile Rheumatoid Arthritis:</strong> 10 mg/m² once weekly.</li><li><strong>Psoriasis:</strong> 10 to 25 mg once per week (maximum: 30 mg/week).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Trexonate 2.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Should be taken on an <strong>empty stomach</strong> for best absorption.</li><li>May be taken with meals to <strong>reduce GI discomfort</strong>.</li><li>Avoid taking with <strong>milk-rich products</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Trexonate 2.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>NSAIDs:</strong> Avoid prior to or with high-dose Trexonate 2.5 mg (e.g., in osteosarcoma).</li><li><strong>Salicylates, Phenylbutazone, Phenytoin, Sulfonamides:</strong> May increase toxicity.</li><li><strong>Penicillin, Theophylline, Probenecid, Azathioprine, Retinoids, Sulfasalazine:</strong> Monitor for hepatotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Cisplatin:</strong> Use caution with high-dose Trexonate 2.5 mg.</li><li><strong>Mercaptopurine:</strong> Increases plasma levels of mercaptopurine.</li><li><strong>Tetracycline, Chloramphenicol, Nonabsorbable Broad Spectrum Antibiotics:</strong> May decrease Trexonate 2.5 mg absorption.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid-containing vitamin preparations:</strong> Decrease response to Trexonate 2.5 mg.</li><li><strong>Trimethoprim/Sulfamethoxazole:</strong> Rarely increases bone marrow suppression.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Alcoholism or alcoholic liver disease</li><li>Immunodeficiency syndromes</li><li>Pre-existing blood disorders (bone marrow hypoplasia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, significant anemia)</li><li>Hypersensitivity to Trexonate 2.5 mg</li><li>Pregnant women with psoriasis or rheumatoid arthritis</li><li>Women of childbearing potential</li><li>Nursing mothers</li><li>Pregnancy should be avoided for:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">At least <strong>3 months after therapy</strong> for male patients.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">At least <strong>one ovulatory cycle after therapy</strong> for female patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Trexonate 2.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Ulcerative stomatitis, leukopenia, nausea, and abdominal distress.</li><li><strong>Other:</strong> Malaise, fatigue, chills, fever, dizziness, and increased infection risk.</li><li><strong>Reproductive Effects:</strong> Impaired fertility, oligospermia, menstrual dysfunction (during and shortly after therapy).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category X:</strong> Contraindicated due to fetal abnormalities and risks that outweigh benefits.</li><li><strong>Nursing mothers:</strong> Trexonate 2.5 mg is contraindicated.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Preservative-containing formulations must <strong>not</strong> be used for <strong>intrathecal or high-dose therapy</strong>.</li><li>Patients should be <strong>closely monitored</strong> for toxic effects.</li><li><strong>Reduce dosage or discontinue</strong> if adverse reactions occur.</li><li><strong>Leucovorin calcium</strong> may be used to counteract toxicity if needed.</li><li><strong>Persistent liver function abnormalities</strong> or <strong>low serum albumin</strong> may indicate serious liver toxicity.</li><li><strong>Folate deficiency</strong> may increase Trexonate 2.5 mg toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Trexonate 2.5 mg</strong></p><p> Leucovorin is used to counteract Trexonate 2.5 mg toxicity and should be administered as soon as possible.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at <strong>≤30°C</strong> in a <strong>dry place</strong>.</li><li>Protect from <strong>light and moisture</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Intramuscular:</strong> Dilute with <strong>D5W</strong> or <strong>normal saline</strong> (≤25 mg/ml for 20 mg &amp; 50 mg vials; 50 mg/ml for 1 g vials).</li><li><strong>Intrathecal:</strong> Reconstitute to <strong>2.5-5 mg/ml</strong> with <strong>normal saline, D5W, lactated Ringer\'s, or Elliott\'s B solution</strong> (must be preservative-free).</li><li><strong>Intravenous:</strong> Dilute with <strong>D5W or normal saline</strong> (≤25 mg/ml for 20 mg &amp; 50 mg vials; 50 mg/ml for 1 g vials).</li><li><strong>Parenteral:</strong> Dilute with <strong>D5W or normal saline</strong> (same concentrations as above).</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Antidote preparations, Immunosuppressant</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Trexonate 2.5 mg inhibits dihydrofolic acid reductase, interfering with purine and thymidylate synthesis, leading to impaired DNA synthesis, repair, and replication. It selectively targets rapidly dividing cells, including malignant cells, bone marrow, and epithelial cells.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>US FDA Pregnancy Category X.</strong></li><li>Used in <strong>neoplastic diseases only</strong> when benefits outweigh fetal risk.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Patients:</strong> Weigh risks vs. benefits before using, especially in combination therapy.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Patients:</strong> Monitor closely for <strong>hepatic, bone marrow, and renal toxicity</strong>.</li></ul>', 'active', '444_1742491890.jpg', 55, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 22:31:30', '2025-03-20 22:31:30'),
(3737, 'Ribox', 'Etoricoxib', 6.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '60 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ribox 60 mg</strong></p><p> Ribox 60 mg is indicated for the symptomatic relief of:</p><ul><li>Osteoarthritis (OA)</li><li>Rheumatoid arthritis (RA)</li><li>Ankylosing spondylitis</li><li>The pain and signs of inflammation associated with acute gouty arthritis.</li><li>For the short-term treatment of moderate pain associated with dental surgery.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Ribox 60 mg is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID). It has anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic activities. Ribox 60 mg is a selective cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) inhibitor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ribox 60 mg</strong></p><p> For adult and adolescent patients over 16 years:</p><ul><li>Osteoarthritis, chronic musculoskeletal disorders &amp; dysmenorrhea: The recommended dose is 60 mg once daily.</li><li>Rheumatoid arthritis: The recommended dose is 90 mg once daily.</li><li>Pain following dental surgery &amp; acute gout: The recommended dose is 120 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Ribox 60 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Osteoarthritis</strong>: The recommended dose is 30 mg once daily. In some patients, an increased dose of 60 mg once daily may increase efficacy.</li><li><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis</strong>: The recommended dose is 90 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Ankylosing spondylitis</strong>: The recommended dose is 90 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Acute gouty arthritis</strong>: The recommended dose is 120 mg once daily (for 8 days).</li><li><strong>Postoperative dental surgery pain</strong>: The recommended dose is 90 mg once daily, limited to a maximum of 3 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ribox 60 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With medicine</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oral anticoagulants: In subjects stabilized on chronic warfarin therapy, Ribox 60 mg may increase prothrombin time.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diuretics, ACE inhibitors, and Angiotensin II Antagonists: NSAIDs may reduce the effect of these drugs.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Ciclosporin and tacrolimus: Co-administration may increase nephrotoxic effects.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Lithium: Ribox 60 mg may increase lithium plasma levels.</li><li><strong>With food &amp; others</strong>: Ribox 60 mg can be taken with or without meals.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Ribox 60 mg or any excipients.</li><li>Active peptic ulceration or gastrointestinal bleeding.</li><li>Patients with a history of allergic-type reactions to acetylsalicylic acid or NSAIDs.</li><li>Pregnancy and lactation.</li><li>Severe hepatic dysfunction.</li><li>Renal creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min.</li><li>Children and adolescents under 16 years.</li><li>Inflammatory bowel disease.</li><li>Congestive heart failure (NYHA II-IV).</li><li>Uncontrolled hypertension.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ribox 60 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Palpitations, fatigue, influenza-like symptoms, ecchymosis.</li><li> Less common:</li><li>Dry mouth, taste disturbance, mouth ulcer, appetite and weight changes, atrial fibrillation, transient ischemic attack, chest pain, and others.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Avoid Ribox 60 mg in late pregnancy as it may cause premature closure of the ductus arteriosus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Ribox 60 mg should be avoided during lactation as it is excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution with patients at risk for gastrointestinal complications (elderly, prior history of ulcers).</li><li>Monitor for renal function in at-risk patients.</li><li>Caution in patients with liver dysfunction, cardiac failure, or pre-existing edema.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ribox 60 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Doses up to 500 mg did not result in significant toxicity. Supportive measures should be employed in case of overdose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at temperatures below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture.</li></ul><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Ribox 60 mg is a potent selective COX-2 inhibitor that reduces inflammation, pain, and fever with minimal gastrointestinal toxicity compared to non-selective NSAIDs.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Not recommended in women attempting to conceive and in pregnancy.</li></ul>', 'active', '107_1742492115.jpg', 3, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 22:35:15', '2025-03-20 22:35:15'),
(3738, 'Zeal', 'Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate', 30.15, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '10 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zeal 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Zeal 10 mg/5 ml is indicated in zinc deficiency and/or zinc-losing conditions. Zinc deficiency can occur due to inadequate diet or malabsorption. Excessive loss of zinc can occur in trauma, burns, diarrhea, and protein-losing conditions. A zinc supplement is given until clinical improvement occurs but may need to be continued in cases of severe malabsorption, metabolic disease, or in zinc-losing states.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zinc sulphate monohydrate is an essential trace element and is involved in a number of body enzyme systems. The body needs zinc for normal growth and health. Zinc is also vital for sexual maturation and reproduction, dark vision adaptation, olfactory and gustatory activity, insulin storage &amp; release, and for a variety of host immune defenses. Zinc deficiency may lead to impaired immune function, delayed wound healing, a decrease in sense of taste and smell, reduced ability to fight infections, poor night vision, increased risk of abortion, alopecia, mental lethargy, skin changes, and poor development of reproductive organs.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Zeal 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Child under 10 kg: 5 ml (1 teaspoonful) 2 times daily after food.</li><li>Child between 10-30 kg: 10 ml (2 teaspoonfuls) 1-3 times daily after food.</li><li>Adults and children over 30 kg: 20 ml (4 teaspoonfuls) 1-3 times daily after food.</li><li>This drug is most effective if taken at least 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals. However, if it causes stomach upset, it may be taken with a meal.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zeal 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>For dispersible tablet:</p><ul><li>Place the tablet in a teaspoon.</li><li>Add an adequate amount of water.</li><li>Let the tablet dissolve completely.</li><li>Give the entire spoonful solution.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zeal 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Concomitant intake of a tetracycline and zinc may decrease the absorption of both the tetracycline and zinc. Similarly, concomitant administration of zinc and quinolone drugs may also decrease the absorption of both. Concomitant intake of penicillamine and zinc may decrease the absorption of zinc.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in those who are hypersensitive to any component of the ingredients of this preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zeal 10 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Zinc may cause nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, stomach upset, heartburn, and gastritis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of this product during human pregnancy has not been established. Zinc crosses the placenta and is present in breast milk.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In acute renal failure, zinc accumulation may occur in the body; thus, dose adjustment is needed.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1152_1742493513.jpg', 28, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 22:58:33', '2025-05-05 22:16:07');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3739, 'Zedoxim', 'Cefpodoxime Proxetil', 20.00, 4, 'capsule', '4 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zedoxim 100 mg</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by susceptible microorganisms, including:</p><ul><li>Acute otitis media caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenza</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains).</li><li>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li>Acute maxillary sinusitis caused by <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>.</li><li>Community-acquired pneumonia caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em> or <em>H. influenza</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains).</li><li>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em>, <em>H. influenzae</em> (non-beta-lactamase-producing strains only), or <em>M. catarrhalis</em>.</li><li>Skin and skin structure infections caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections caused by <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, or <em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em>.</li><li>Uncomplicated gonorrhea caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li><li>Rectal gonococcal infections in women due to <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Third-generation Cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is an oral 3rd generation cephalosporin, known for good stability to beta-lactamases and activity against both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. It is a prodrug, with its active metabolite being cefpodoxime. Around 29% to 33% of cefpodoxime is excreted unchanged in the urine over 12 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zedoxim 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (13 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/tonsillitis: 100 mg every 12 hours for 5 to 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute maxillary sinusitis: 200 mg every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Community-acquired pneumonia: 200 mg every 12 hours for 14 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis: 200 mg every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin and skin structure infections: 400 mg every 12 hours for 7 to 14 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: 100 mg every 12 hours for 7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated gonorrhea: single 200 mg dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rectal gonococcal infections in women: single 200 mg dose.</li><li><strong>Infants and Pediatric Patients (2 months to 12 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute otitis media: 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours for 5 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis/tonsillitis: 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours for 5 to 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute maxillary sinusitis: 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours for 10 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant administration of high doses of antacids (sodium bicarbonate and aluminum hydroxide) or H2 blockers reduces peak plasma levels and the extent of absorption.</li><li>Renal excretion of cefpodoxime is inhibited by probenecid.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Cefpodoxime is contraindicated in patients with known allergies to cephalosporins.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Zedoxim 100 mg</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime has very few side effects. Potential side effects include:</p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal disorders (such as diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain).</li><li>Rash, urticaria, and itching.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>US FDA pregnancy category: B. While there are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women, animal reproduction studies do not always predict human responses. Therefore, this drug should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed.</li><li>Cefpodoxime is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In patients with reduced urinary output due to renal insufficiency, the total daily dose should be reduced.</li><li>Caution should be taken when administering to patients receiving potent diuretics.</li><li>Prolonged use of cefpodoxime may lead to the overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>In patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min), dosing intervals should be increased to every 24 hours.</li><li>No dosage adjustment is required in cases of hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ol><li>Shake the bottle well to loosen the powder.</li><li>Add boiled and cooled water to the bottle.</li><li>Shake until the powder is completely mixed with the water.</li></ol><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is a 3rd-generation cephalosporin with excellent stability to beta-lactamases and activity against both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria. It acts as a prodrug, with its active form being cefpodoxime. Around 29% to 33% is excreted unchanged in the urine over 12 hours.</p>', 'active', '743_1742493700.jpg', 64, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 23:01:40', '2025-05-05 22:17:27'),
(3740, 'Zedoxim', 'Cefpodoxime Proxetil', 40.00, 4, 'capsule', '4 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a summary of the information on Zedoxim (Cefpodoxime 200 mg):</p><h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p>Zedoxim (Cefpodoxime) is used for treating infections caused by susceptible microorganisms. These include:</p><ul><li><strong>Acute otitis media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, and <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>.</li><li><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia</strong> caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em> or <em>H. influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em>, <em>H. influenzae</em> (non-beta-lactamase-producing strains), or <em>M. catarrhalis</em>.</li><li><strong>Skin and skin structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, or <em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong> caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Rectal gonococcal infections in women</strong> due to <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Third-generation Cephalosporins</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li>Cefpodoxime is an oral third-generation cephalosporin that is stable against beta-lactamases and effective against both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria.</li><li>It is a prodrug, with its active form being Cefpodoxime.</li><li>Approximately 29-33% of the drug is excreted unchanged in urine within 12 hours.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (13 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong>: 100 mg every 12 hours for 5–10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis</strong>: 200 mg every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia</strong>: 200 mg every 12 hours for 14 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong>: 200 mg every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Skin and skin structure infections</strong>: 400 mg every 12 hours for 7–14 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong>: 100 mg every 12 hours for 7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong>: Single dose of 200 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Rectal gonococcal infections in women</strong>: Single dose of 200 mg.</li><li><strong>Infants and Children (2 months to 12 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute otitis media</strong>: 5 mg/kg every 12 hours for 5 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong>: 5 mg/kg every 12 hours for 5–10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis</strong>: 5 mg/kg every 12 hours for 10 days.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Antacids (e.g., sodium bicarbonate and aluminum hydroxide)</strong>: May reduce peak plasma levels.</li><li><strong>H2 blockers</strong>: May reduce the extent of absorption.</li><li><strong>Probenecid</strong>: Inhibits renal excretion of Cefpodoxime.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Known allergy to cephalosporins.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Common side effects</strong>: Gastrointestinal disturbances (diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain), rash, urticaria, and itching.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B. Use only if clearly needed, as animal studies may not always predict human effects.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Excreted in human milk; caution is advised when administering to nursing women.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Renal insufficiency</strong>: Dosage may need to be adjusted for patients with reduced urinary output.</li><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: Administer with caution for patients on potent diuretics.</li><li><strong>Prolonged use</strong>: May result in overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li>For patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min), increase the dosing interval to 24 hours.</li><li>No dosage adjustment needed for hepatic impairment.</li></ul><h3><strong>Reconstitution</strong></h3><ol><li>Shake the bottle well to loosen the powder.</li><li>Add boiled and cooled water.</li><li>Shake until the powder is fully dissolved.</li></ol><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li>Cefpodoxime is a third-generation cephalosporin that works against both Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria by inhibiting bacterial cell wall synthesis.</li></ul><p>This information should provide you with a comprehensive overview of Zedoxim (Cefpodoxime 200 mg). Let me know if you need further details!</p>', 'active', '211_1742493913.jpg', 64, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 23:05:13', '2025-05-05 22:17:40'),
(3741, 'Zedoxim', 'Cefpodoxime Proxetil', 60.00, 1, 'x 15 ml bottle', '15 ml bottle', 'Pediatric Drops', '20 mg/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zedoxim 20 mg/ml</strong></p><p>Cefpodoxime is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by susceptible microorganisms, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Acute otitis media</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenza</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong> caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis</strong> caused by <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>.</li><li><strong>Community acquired pneumonia</strong> caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em> or <em>H. influenza</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis</strong> caused by <em>S. pneumoniae</em>, <em>H. influenzae</em> (non-beta-lactamase-producing strains only), or <em>M. catarrhalis</em>.</li><li><strong>Skin and skin structure infections</strong> caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong> caused by <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, or <em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em>.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong> caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li><li><strong>Rectal gonococcal infections in women</strong> caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> (including penicillinase-producing strains).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Third generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Cefpodoxime is an oral 3rd generation cephalosporin, with good stability to beta-lactamases and activity against both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria.</li><li>It is a prodrug, and its active metabolite is Cefpodoxime.</li><li>Approximately 29% to 33% of Cefpodoxime is excreted unchanged in the urine within 12 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zedoxim 20 mg/ml:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (13 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong>: 100 mg every 12 hours for 5 to 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis</strong>: 200 mg every 12 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Community-acquired pneumonia</strong>: 200 mg every 12 hours for 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute bacterial exacerbations of chronic bronchitis</strong>: 200 mg every 12 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Skin and skin structure infections</strong>: 400 mg every 12 hours for 7 to 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong>: 100 mg every 12 hours for 7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated gonorrhea</strong>: Single dose of 200 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Rectal gonococcal infections in women</strong>: Single dose of 200 mg</li><li><strong>Infants and Pediatric Patients (2 months to 12 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute otitis media</strong>: 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours for 5 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pharyngitis/tonsillitis</strong>: 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours for 5 to 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute maxillary sinusitis</strong>: 5 mg/kg body weight every 12 hours for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant administration of high doses of antacids (sodium bicarbonate and aluminum hydroxide) or H2 blockers may reduce peak plasma levels and the extent of absorption.</li><li>Renal excretion of Cefpodoxime is inhibited by probenecid.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Cefpodoxime is contraindicated in patients with known allergies to cephalosporins.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Rare side effects include gastrointestinal disorders (diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain), rash, urticaria, and itching.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong> (US FDA): Cefpodoxime should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Cefpodoxime is excreted in human milk, so caution is advised when administering it to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>In patients with reduced urinary output due to renal insufficiency, the total daily dose of Cefpodoxime should be reduced.</li><li>Caution should be taken when administering Cefpodoxime to patients receiving potent diuretics.</li><li>Prolonged use may lead to overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Severe renal impairment</strong> (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min): Increase dosing interval to every 24 hours.</li><li>No dosage adjustment required for <strong>hepatic impairment</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution:</strong></p><ol><li>Shake the bottle well to loosen the powder.</li><li>Add boiled and cooled water to the bottle.</li><li>Shake until the powder is completely mixed with the water.</li></ol><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Third-generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Cefpodoxime is a prodrug with good beta-lactamase stability and activity against Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. Its active metabolite is Cefpodoxime. Approximately 29-33% of Cefpodoxime is excreted unchanged in the urine within 12 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li>For patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min), dosing intervals should be adjusted to 24 hours. No dosage adjustments are required for hepatic impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '858_1742494114.jpg', 64, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 23:08:34', '2025-05-07 20:23:35'),
(3742, 'Zeropain', 'Ketorolac Tromethamine', 100.00, 1, 'x 2 ml ampoule', '2 ml ampoule', 'IV Injection', '60 mg/2 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zeropain 60 mg/2 ml</strong></p><p>Zeropain 60 mg/2 ml is indicated for the short-term management of moderate to severe acute post-operative pain.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-Opioid Analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zeropain 60 mg/2 ml is a potent analgesic of the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). It acts by inhibiting the cyclooxygenase enzyme system and hence inhibits prostaglandin synthesis. It demonstrates a minimal anti-inflammatory effect at its analgesic dose.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zeropain 60 mg/2 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>:</li><li>Recommended dose is 10 mg every 4-6 hours. It should be used short-term only (up to 7 days) and is not recommended for chronic use. Doses exceeding 40 mg/day are not recommended.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>:</li><li>Ketorolac injection may be used as a single or multiple doses, on a regular or as-needed schedule for the management of moderately severe, acute pain that requires analgesia at the opioid level, usually in a postoperative setting.</li><li>When administering Ketorolac injection:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The IV bolus must be given over no less than 15 seconds.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The IM administration should be given slowly and deeply into the muscle.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The analgesic effect begins within 30 minutes, with maximum effect in 1 to 2 hours after dosing (IV or IM).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Duration of analgesic effect is usually 4 to 6 hours.</li><li><strong>Single-Dose Treatment</strong>:</li><li><strong>IM Dosing (Adult)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients &lt;65 years: One dose of 60 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients &gt;65 years, renally impaired, and/or less than 50 kg of body weight: One dose of 30 mg.</li><li><strong>IV Dosing (Adult)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients &lt;65 years: One dose of 30 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients &gt;65 years, renally impaired, and/or less than 50 kg of body weight: One dose of 15 mg.</li><li><strong>IV or IM Dosing (2 to 16 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IM Dosing</strong>: One dose of 1 mg/kg up to a maximum of 30 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV Dosing</strong>: One dose of 0.5 mg/kg up to a maximum of 15 mg.</li><li><strong>Multiple-Dose Treatment (IV or IM)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Patients &lt;65 years</strong>: The recommended dose is 30 mg Ketorolac injection every 6 hours. The maximum daily dose should not exceed 120 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Patients &gt;65 years, renally impaired, and patients less than 50 kg</strong>: The recommended dose is 15 mg Ketorolac injection every 6 hours. The maximum daily dose for these populations should not exceed 60 mg.</li><li>For breakthrough pain, do not increase the dose or the frequency of Zeropain 60 mg/2 ml.</li><li><strong>Conversion from Parenteral to Oral Therapy</strong>:</li><li>Ketorolac tablets may be used either as monotherapy or as follow-on therapy to parenteral Ketorolac. When Ketorolac tablets are used as follow-on therapy, the total combined daily dose of ketorolac (oral + parenteral) should not exceed 120 mg in younger adult patients or 60 mg in elderly patients on the day the change of formulation is made. On subsequent days, oral dosing should not exceed the recommended daily maximum of 40 mg.</li><li>Ketorolac IM should be replaced by Ketorolac tablet as soon as feasible. The total duration of combined parenteral and oral treatment should not exceed 5 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zeropain 60 mg/2 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Other NSAIDs or aspirin</strong>: Increase the side effects of ketorolac Tromethamine.</li><li><strong>Anti-coagulants</strong>: Enhance anti-coagulant effect.</li><li><strong>Beta Blocker</strong>: Reduce the anti-hypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>ACE Inhibitors</strong>: Increase the risk of renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: Enhance the toxicity of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Ketorolac is contraindicated in patients having hypersensitivity to this drug or other NSAIDs. It should not be used in children under 16 years of age. It is also contraindicated as a prophylactic analgesic before surgery.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zeropain 60 mg/2 ml</strong></p><p>Commonly occurring side effects are nausea, vomiting, gastrointestinal bleeding, melena, peptic ulcer, pancreatitis, anxiety, drowsiness, headache, excessive thirst, fatigue, bradycardia, hypertension, palpitation, chest pain, infertility in females, and pulmonary edema.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>US FDA Pregnancy category of Zeropain 60 mg/2 ml is C. Zeropain 60 mg/2 ml should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in patients over the age of 65 years. Caution should also be taken in patients with active or suspected peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding, asthma, and liver dysfunction.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '333_1742494265.webp', 1, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 23:11:05', '2025-05-07 20:33:36'),
(3743, 'Zeropain', 'Ketorolac Tromethamine', 60.00, 1, 'x 1 ml ampoule', '1 ml ampoule', 'IV Injection', '30 mg/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the provided information with headings in normal bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Zeropain 30 mg/ml</strong></p><p>Zeropain 30 mg/ml is indicated for the short-term management of moderate to severe acute post-operative pain.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-Opioid Analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zeropain 30 mg/ml is a potent analgesic of the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). It acts by inhibiting the cyclooxygenase enzyme system and hence inhibits the prostaglandin synthesis. It demonstrates a minimal anti-inflammatory effect at its analgesic dose.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zeropain 30 mg/ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>: Recommended dose is 10 mg every 4-6 hours. It should be used short-term only (up to 7 days) and are not recommended for chronic use. Doses exceeding 40 mg/day is not recommended.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: Ketorolac injection may be used as a single or multiple doses, on a regular or when necessary schedule for the management of moderately severe, acute pain that requires analgesia at the opioid level, usually in a postoperative setting. When administering Ketorolac injection, the IV bolus must be given over no less than 15 seconds. The IM administration should be given slowly and deeply into the muscle. The analgesic effect begins within 30 minutes with maximum effect in 1 to 2 hours after dosing IV or IM. Duration of analgesic effect is usually 4 to 6 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Single-Dose Treatment</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IM Dosing (Adult)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients &lt;65 years of age: One dose of 60 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients &gt;65 years of age, renally impaired and/or less than 50 kg of body weight: One dose of 30 mg.</li><li><strong>IV Dosing (Adult)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients &lt;65 years of age: One dose of 30 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients &gt;65 years of age, renally impaired and/or less than 50 kg of body weight: One dose of 15 mg.</li><li><strong>IV or IM Dosing (2 to 16 years of age)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">IM Dosing: One dose of 1 mg/kg up to a maximum of 30 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">IV Dosing: One dose of 0.5 mg/kg up to a maximum of 15 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Multiple-Dose Treatment (IV or IM)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Patients &lt;65 years of age</strong>: The recommended dose is 30 mg Ketorolac injection every 6 hours. The maximum daily dose should not exceed 120 mg.</li><li><strong>Patients &gt;65 years of age, renally impaired patients and patients less than 50 kg</strong>: The recommended dose is 15 mg Ketorolac injection every 6 hours. The maximum daily dose for these populations should not exceed 60 mg.</li><li>For breakthrough pain, do not increase the dose or the frequency of Zeropain 30 mg/ml.</li></ul><p><strong>Conversion from Parenteral to Oral Therapy</strong></p><p>Ketorolac tablets may be used either as monotherapy or as follow-on therapy to parenteral Ketorolac. When Ketorolac tablets are used as a follow-on therapy to parenteral Ketorolac, the total combined daily dose of ketorolac (oral + parenteral) should not exceed 120 mg in younger adult patients or 60 mg in elderly patients on the day the change of formulation is made. On subsequent days, oral dosing should not exceed the recommended daily maximum of 40 mg. Ketorolac IM should be replaced by Ketorolac tablet as soon as feasible. The total duration of combined parenteral and oral treatment should not exceed 5 days.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Zeropain 30 mg/ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Other NSAIDs or aspirin</strong>: Increase the side effects of ketorolac Tromethamine.</li><li><strong>Anti-coagulants</strong>: Enhance anti-coagulant effect.</li><li><strong>Beta Blocker</strong>: Reduce the anti-hypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>ACE Inhibitors</strong>: Increase the risk of renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: Enhance the toxicity of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Ketorolac is contraindicated in patients having hypersensitivity to this drug or other NSAIDs. It should not be used in children under 16 years of age. It is also contraindicated as prophylactic analgesic before surgery.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zeropain 30 mg/ml</strong></p><p>Commonly occurring side effects are nausea, vomiting, gastrointestinal bleeding, melana, peptic ulcer, pancreatitis, anxiety, drowsiness, headache, excessive thirst, fatigue, bradycardia, hypertension, palpitation, chest pain, infertility in females, and pulmonary edema.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>US FDA Pregnancy category of Zeropain 30 mg/ml is C. So, Zeropain 30 mg/ml should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the other outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in patients over the age of 65 years. Caution should also be taken in patients with active or suspected peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding or asthma and liver dysfunction.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1332_1742494835.webp', 1, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 23:20:35', '2025-05-07 20:33:49'),
(3744, 'Zitum', 'Ceftazidime Pentahydrate', 115.78, 1, 'x 500 mg vial', '500 mg vial', 'IV Injection', '500 mg/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zitum 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Ceftazidime Injection is indicated for the treatment of patients with infections caused by susceptible strains of the designated organisms in the following diseases:</p><ol><li><strong>Lower Respiratory Tract Infections</strong> (including pneumonia), caused by:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pseudomonas aeruginosa</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Haemophilus influenzae (including ampicillin-resistant strains)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Klebsiella spp.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Enterobacter spp.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Proteus mirabilis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Escherichia coli</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Serratia spp.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Citrobacter spp.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Streptococcus pneumoniae</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Staphylococcus aureus (methicillin-susceptible strains)</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>, caused by:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pseudomonas aeruginosa</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Klebsiella spp.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Escherichia coli</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Proteus spp., including Proteus mirabilis and indole-positive Proteus</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Enterobacter spp.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Serratia spp.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Staphylococcus aureus (methicillin-susceptible strains)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Streptococcus pyogenes (group A beta-hemolytic streptococci)</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections</strong> (both complicated and uncomplicated), caused by:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pseudomonas aeruginosa</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Enterobacter spp.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Proteus spp., including Proteus mirabilis and indole-positive Proteus</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Klebsiella spp.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Escherichia coli</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Bacterial Septicemia</strong>, caused by:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pseudomonas aeruginosa</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Klebsiella spp.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Haemophilus influenzae</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Escherichia coli</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Serratia spp.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Streptococcus pneumoniae</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Staphylococcus aureus (methicillin-susceptible strains)</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Bone and Joint Infections</strong>, caused by:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pseudomonas aeruginosa</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Klebsiella spp.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Enterobacter spp.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Staphylococcus aureus (methicillin-susceptible strains)</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Gynecologic Infections</strong>, including endometritis, pelvic cellulitis, and other infections of the female genital tract, caused by Escherichia coli.</li><li><strong>Intraabdominal Infections</strong>, including peritonitis caused by:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Escherichia coli</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Klebsiella spp.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Staphylococcus aureus (methicillin-susceptible strains)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Polymicrobial infections caused by aerobic and anaerobic organisms, including <em>Bacteroides</em> spp.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Central Nervous System Infections</strong>, including meningitis, caused by:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Haemophilus influenzae</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Neisseria meningitidis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pseudomonas aeruginosa</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Streptococcus pneumoniae</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Third-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Ceftazidime binds to 1 or more of the penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), inhibiting the final transpeptidation step of peptidoglycan synthesis in the bacterial cell wall. This action inhibits biosynthesis and arrests cell wall assembly, resulting in bacterial cell death.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zitum 500 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis of surgical infection (prostate surgery)</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 1 g at the induction of anesthesia, repeated if necessary upon removal of the catheter. Administered as a deep IM injection, slow IV injection (3-5 min), or IV infusion (up to 30 min).</li><li><strong>Elderly (&gt;80 years)</strong>: Max dose of 3 g daily.</li><li><strong>Pseudomonal lung infections in cystic fibrosis</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 100-150 mg/kg every 8 hours (deep IM, slow IV, or IV infusion). Max dose: 9 g daily.</li><li><strong>Children (&lt;40 kg)</strong>: 150 mg/kg daily in 3 divided doses. Max dose: 6 g daily.</li><li><strong>Elderly (&gt;80 years)</strong>: Max dose of 3 g daily.</li><li><strong>Bone and joint infections, complicated intra-abdominal infections, skin infections (complicated)</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 1-2 g every 8 hours. Administered via deep IM, slow IV, or IV infusion (up to 30 min).</li><li><strong>Children (&lt;40 kg)</strong>: 100-150 mg/kg daily in 3 divided doses. Max dose: 6 g daily.</li><li><strong>Elderly (&gt;80 years)</strong>: Max dose of 3 g daily.</li><li><strong>Bacterial meningitis, febrile neutropenic patients, nosocomial pneumonia</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 2 g every 8 hours (IV or IM injection).</li><li><strong>Children (&lt;40 kg)</strong>: 150 mg/kg daily in 3 divided doses. Max dose: 6 g daily.</li><li><strong>Elderly (&gt;80 years)</strong>: Max dose of 3 g daily.</li><li><strong>Complicated urinary tract infections</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 1-2 g every 8-12 hours (IV/IM).</li><li><strong>Children (&lt;40 kg)</strong>: 100-150 mg/kg daily in 3 divided doses. Max dose: 6 g daily.</li><li><strong>Elderly (&gt;80 years)</strong>: Max dose of 3 g daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>Ceftazidime can be administered intravenously (IV) or by deep intramuscular (IM) injection. The IM injection should be into a large muscle mass such as the gluteus maximus or the lateral thigh. <strong>Ceftazidime should be reconstituted with the supplied Sterile Water for Injection</strong>.</p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>Concomitant administration with <strong>aminoglycoside antibiotics</strong> can increase nephrotoxicity.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Ceftazidime is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Ceftazidime or the cephalosporin group of antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Local reactions</strong> (following IV injection)</li><li><strong>Allergic reactions</strong> (pruritus, rash, fever)</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal reactions</strong> (diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain)</li><li><strong>CNS reactions</strong> (headache, dizziness, paresthesia)</li><li>Rare reactions: Angioedema, anaphylaxis</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Ceftazidime should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed, as animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Ceftazidime is excreted in low concentrations in human milk. The safety of the drug for nursing infants has not been established, so a decision should be made whether to discontinue breastfeeding or the drug based on the importance of the drug to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal insufficiency</strong>: The total daily dosage should be reduced for patients with renal insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal disease</strong>: Caution should be used in individuals with a history of gastrointestinal diseases, particularly colitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose, particularly in patients with renal failure, may result in <strong>seizure activity</strong>, <strong>encephalopathy</strong>, <strong>neuromuscular excitability</strong>, and <strong>coma</strong>.</li><li>Supportive treatment is recommended for patients with overdose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below <strong>25°C</strong>, protected from light and moisture. Reconstituted solutions are stable for up to <strong>24 hours</strong> when stored between <strong>2°C - 8°C</strong>.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IM/IV Administration</strong>: Add the recommended volume of sterile water for injection (WFI) as specified for the respective vial sizes. Shake gently until a clear solution is obtained.</li></ul>', 'active', '525_1742495335.webp', 44, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 23:28:55', '2025-05-05 22:34:52'),
(3745, 'Zitum', 'Ceftazidime Pentahydrate', 226.53, 1, 'x 1 gm vial', '1 gm vial', 'IV Injection', '1 gm/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zitum 1 gm/vial (Ceftazidime Injection)</strong></p><p>Ceftazidime Injection is indicated for the treatment of patients with infections caused by susceptible strains of the designated organisms in the following conditions:</p><ol><li><strong>Lower Respiratory Tract Infections</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Including pneumonia, caused by Pseudomonas aeruginosa and other Pseudomonas spp., Haemophilus influenzae (including ampicillin-resistant strains), Klebsiella spp., Enterobacter spp., Proteus mirabilis, Escherichia coli, Serratia spp., Citrobacter spp., Streptococcus pneumoniae, and Staphylococcus aureus (methicillin-susceptible strains).</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Caused by Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Klebsiella spp., Escherichia coli, Proteus spp., Enterobacter spp., Serratia spp., Staphylococcus aureus (methicillin-susceptible strains), and Streptococcus pyogenes.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Both complicated and uncomplicated, caused by Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Enterobacter spp., Proteus spp., Klebsiella spp., and Escherichia coli.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Bacterial Septicemia</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Caused by Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Klebsiella spp., Haemophilus influenzae, Escherichia coli, Serratia spp., Streptococcus pneumoniae, and Staphylococcus aureus (methicillin-susceptible strains).</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Bone and Joint Infections</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Caused by Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Klebsiella spp., Enterobacter spp., and Staphylococcus aureus (methicillin-susceptible strains).</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Gynecologic Infections</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Including endometritis, pelvic cellulitis, and other infections of the female genital tract caused by Escherichia coli.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Intra-abdominal Infections</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Including peritonitis caused by Escherichia coli, Klebsiella spp., Staphylococcus aureus (methicillin-susceptible strains), and polymicrobial infections involving both aerobic and anaerobic organisms.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Central Nervous System Infections</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Including meningitis caused by Haemophilus influenzae, Neisseria meningitidis, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and Streptococcus pneumoniae.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Third generation Cephalosporins</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Ceftazidime binds to penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), inhibiting the final step of peptidoglycan synthesis in bacterial cell walls, leading to bacterial cell death.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Prophylaxis of Surgical Infection</strong> (e.g., prostate surgery):</p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: 1 g at the induction of anesthesia, repeated if necessary upon removal of the catheter. Administer as deep IM injection, slow IV injection (3-5 min), or IV infusion (up to 30 min).</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong> (&gt;80 years): Max 3 g daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Pseudomonal Lung Infections in Cystic Fibrosis</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: 100-150 mg/kg every 8 hours, max 9 g daily.</li><li><strong>Child</strong> (&lt;40 kg): 150 mg/kg daily in 3 divided doses, max 6 g daily.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong> (&gt;80 years): Max 3 g daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Bone and Joint Infections, Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections, Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: 1-2 g every 8 hours, as IM or IV.</li><li><strong>Child</strong> (&lt;40 kg): 100-150 mg/kg daily in 3 divided doses, max 6 g daily.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong> (&gt;80 years): Max 3 g daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Bacterial Meningitis, Nosocomial Pneumonia, Febrile Neutropenia</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: 2 g every 8 hours, as IM or IV.</li><li><strong>Child</strong> (&lt;40 kg): 150 mg/kg daily in 3 divided doses, max 6 g daily.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong> (&gt;80 years): Max 3 g daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: 1-2 g every 8-12 hours, as IM or IV.</li><li><strong>Child</strong> (&lt;40 kg): 100-150 mg/kg daily in 3 divided doses, max 6 g daily.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong> (&gt;80 years): Max 3 g daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage for Adults</strong>:</p><ul><li>1 to 6 g per day, divided into 8 or 12-hour intervals, depending on infection severity and renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><ul><li>Ceftazidime can be given via intravenous or deep intramuscular injection.</li><li>Reconstitute with sterile water for injection before use.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li>Increased nephrotoxicity may occur with concomitant aminoglycoside antibiotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to Ceftazidime or cephalosporins.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Local reactions, allergic, and gastrointestinal reactions (e.g., rash, fever, diarrhea, nausea).</li><li>Rare: Angioedema, anaphylaxis, CNS effects (headache, dizziness, paresthesia).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Use only if necessary during pregnancy.</li><li>Excreted in breast milk; caution is advised when breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Adjust dosage for patients with renal insufficiency.</li><li>Caution in individuals with a history of gastrointestinal diseases, especially colitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms may include seizures, encephalopathy, and coma. Provide supportive treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 25°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Reconstituted solutions are stable for 24 hours at 2°C–8°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li>Add the recommended volume of solvent to the vial (listed for different doses), shake gently to dissolve, and withdraw the solution.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Ceftazidime is bactericidal and inhibits cell-wall synthesis by binding to penicillin-binding proteins.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>No well-controlled studies have been conducted, use only if necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Adjust dosage based on the child’s age and renal function, with specific guidelines for infants and children up to 2 months of age.</li></ul>', 'active', '1753_1742495470.jpg', 44, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 23:31:10', '2025-05-05 22:35:13');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3746, 'Zocil', 'Cilostazol', 7.00, 7, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information you provided with the headings formatted in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Zocil 50 mg</strong></p><p> Zocil 50 mg is indicated for the reduction of symptoms of intermittent claudication, as indicated by an increased walking distance.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Peripheral Vasodilator drugs: Intermittent Claudication</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Zocil 50 mg is a preparation of Zocil 50 mg, which is a quinolinone derivative. The mechanism of action is to specifically inhibit cellular phosphodiesterase III (PDE III) and suppress cAMP degradation with a resultant increase in cAMP in platelets and blood vessels, leading to inhibition of platelet aggregation and vasodilation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zocil 50 mg</strong></p><p> The recommended dosage of Zocil 50 mg is 100 mg bid, taken at least half an hour before or two hours after breakfast and dinner. A dose of 50 mg bid should be considered during coadministration of Fluconazole, Ketoconazole, Itraconazole, Erythromycin, Clarithromycin, Fluvoxamine, Fluoxetine, Nefazodone, Sertraline, and Diltiazem.</p><p><strong>Pediatric use:</strong></p><p> The safety and effectiveness of Zocil 50 mg in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Zocil 50 mg</strong></p><p> Pharmacokinetic studies have demonstrated that Omeprazole and Erythromycin significantly increased the systemic exposure of Zocil 50 mg and/or its major metabolites. Population pharmacokinetic studies showed higher concentrations of Zocil 50 mg among patients concurrently treated with Diltiazem.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Zocil 50 mg is contraindicated in patients with congestive heart failure of any severity. Zocil 50 mg is also contraindicated in patients with known or suspected hypersensitivity to any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zocil 50 mg</strong></p><p> The most common side effects are headache, diarrhoea, vomiting, leg cramps, rash, etc. The less frequent side effects are anorexia and edema.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Transfer of Zocil 50 mg into milk has been reported in experimental animals. Because of the potential risk to nursing infants, a decision should be made to discontinue nursing or to discontinue Zocil 50 mg.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Zocil 50 mg should be used with caution in patients with any degree of heart failure. There is no information with respect to the efficacy or safety of the concurrent use of Zocil 50 mg and Clopidogrel.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zocil 50 mg</strong></p><p> Severe headache, diarrhoea, hypotension, tachycardia, and possibly cardiac arrhythmias. Careful observation and supportive treatment are recommended.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool &amp; dry place, protected from light and moisture.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> Paediatric use: The safety and effectiveness of Zocil 50 mg in paediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Peripheral Vasodilator drugs: Intermittent Claudication</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Zocil 50 mg is a preparation of Zocil 50 mg, which is a quinolinone derivative. The mechanism of action is to specifically inhibit cellular phosphodiesterase III (PDE III) and suppress cAMP degradation with a resultant increase in cAMP in platelets and blood vessels, leading to inhibition of platelet aggregation and vasodilation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Transfer of Zocil 50 mg into milk has been reported in experimental animals. Because of the potential risk to nursing infants, a decision should be made to discontinue nursing or to discontinue Zocil 50 mg.</p><p>Let me know if you\'d like to make any further adjustments or if you need more information!</p>', 'active', '614_1742495699.jpg', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 23:34:59', '2025-03-20 23:34:59'),
(3747, 'Viusid', 'N/A', 145.00, 1, 'sachet', '1 sachet', 'Sachet', '0.14 oz/4g', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '899_1742496155.jpg', 50, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 23:42:35', '2025-05-05 17:45:21'),
(3748, 'Uroben XR', 'Mirabegron', 55.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Uroben XR 50 mg</strong></p><p>Uroben XR 50 mg is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of urgency, increased micturition frequency, and urgency incontinence as may occur in adult patients with overactive bladder (OAB) syndrome.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> BPH/ Urinary retention/ Urinary incontinence</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Uroben XR 50 mg is a potent and selective beta 3-adrenoceptor agonist. Uroben XR 50 mg showed relaxation of bladder smooth muscle in rat and human isolated tissue, increased cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) concentrations in rat bladder tissue, and showed a bladder relaxant effect in rat urinary bladder function models. Uroben XR 50 mg increased mean voided volume per micturition, and decreased the frequency of non-voiding contractions, without affecting voiding pressure or residual urine in rat models of bladder overactivity. In a monkey model, Uroben XR 50 mg showed decreased voiding frequency. These results indicate that Uroben XR 50 mg enhances urine storage function by stimulating beta 3-adrenoceptors in the bladder.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Uroben XR 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Recommended starting dose</strong>: 25 mg once daily, alone or in combination with solifenacin succinate 5 mg, once daily.</li><li>Based on individual efficacy and tolerability, may increase dose to 50 mg once daily, alone or in combination with solifenacin succinate 5 mg, once daily.</li><li>Swallow whole with water, with or without food; do not chew, divide, or crush.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Dosage Considerations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Severe Renal Impairment or Moderate Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Maximum dose is 25 mg Uroben XR 50 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>End-Stage Renal Disease (ESRD) or Severe Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p> Uroben XR 50 mg tablet is to be taken once daily, with liquids, swallowed whole and is not to be chewed, divided, or crushed.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Uroben XR 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Effect of enzyme inhibitors</strong>: Uroben XR 50 mg exposure was increased 1.8-fold in the presence of the strong inhibitor of CYP3A/P-gp ketoconazole. No dose adjustment needed for moderate inhibitors but recommended dose is 25 mg for mild to moderate renal or hepatic impairment with strong CYP3A inhibitors.</li><li><strong>Effect of enzyme inducers</strong>: Substances that are inducers of CYP3A or P-gp decrease the plasma concentrations of Uroben XR 50 mg.</li><li><strong>Effect on CYP2D6 substrates</strong>: Caution is advised when co-administered with drugs metabolized by CYP2D6, such as thioridazine, antiarrhythmics, and tricyclic antidepressants.</li><li><strong>Other interactions</strong>: No clinically relevant interactions with solifenacin, tamsulosin, warfarin, metformin, or combined oral contraceptives.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Uroben XR 50 mg is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to the active substance or to any of the excipients.</li><li>Severe uncontrolled hypertension (systolic blood pressure 180 mm Hg and/or diastolic blood pressure 110 mm Hg).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Uroben XR 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Most common: Tachycardia (1.2%) and urinary tract infections (2.9%).</li><li>Serious: Atrial fibrillation (0.2%).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in Pregnancy</strong>: Limited data in pregnant women. Animal studies show reproductive toxicity. Not recommended during pregnancy and for women planning to conceive.</li><li><strong>Use in Lactation</strong>: Excreted in rodent milk. Should not be administered during breast-feeding as its effects on the breastfed child are unknown.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Not recommended for use in patients with end-stage renal disease. Caution and dose reduction are required in patients with severe renal impairment (GFR 15 to 29 mL/min/1.73 m2).</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Not recommended for use in severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh Class C). Avoid use in moderate hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh Class B) with strong CYP3A inhibitors.</li><li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: Can increase blood pressure. Monitor blood pressure, especially in hypertensive patients.</li><li><strong>QT Prolongation</strong>: Caution in patients with congenital or acquired QT prolongation.</li><li><strong>Bladder Outlet Obstruction</strong>: Caution in patients with clinically significant bladder outlet obstruction.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Uroben XR 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Tachycardia, increased pulse rate.</li><li>Treatment: Symptomatic and supportive treatment. Monitor pulse rate, blood pressure, and ECG.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal and Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Not studied in end-stage renal disease and severe hepatic impairment. Dose adjustments for severe renal impairment (GFR 15 to 29 mL/min/1.73 m2) are recommended.</li><li><strong>Gender</strong>: No dose adjustment necessary.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established for children under 18 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> BPH/ Urinary retention/ Urinary incontinence</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Uroben XR 50 mg is the first beta-3 adrenoceptor agonist, increasing cyclic AMP levels and leading to relaxation of the detrusor smooth muscle during the storage phase of the bladder fill-void cycle.</p>', 'active', '602_1742496300.jpg', 2, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 23:45:00', '2025-03-20 23:45:00'),
(3749, 'Zycin', 'Azithromycin Dihydrate', 185.00, 1, 'x 50 ml bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '200 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zycin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Zycin 200 mg/5 ml is prescribed for infections caused by susceptible organisms, including:</p><ul><li>Upper respiratory tract infections (e.g., sinusitis, pharyngitis/tonsillitis)</li><li>Lower respiratory tract infections (e.g., bronchitis, pneumonia)</li><li>Otitis media</li><li>Skin and soft tissue infections</li><li>Non-gonococcal urethritis and cervicitis caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> in sexually transmitted diseases in both men and women</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Macrolides</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zycin 200 mg/5 ml is part of the macrolide subclass of antibiotics known as azalides. It works by binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit of susceptible species to inhibit microbial protein synthesis. It is effective against a range of bacteria, including:</p><ul><li><em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>, anaerobes such as <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>, <em>Escherichia coli</em></li><li><em>Bordetella pertussis</em>, <em>Bordetella parapertussis</em>, <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em></li><li><em>Haemophilus ducreyi</em>, <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></li><li><em>Streptococcus</em> species, <em>Campylobacter</em> sp., <em>Toxoplasma gondii</em>, <em>Treponema pallidum</em></li><li><em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>, <em>Legionella pneumophila</em>, and hominins</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Zycin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">500 mg once daily for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Alternatively, 500 mg on Day 1, then 250 mg once daily for 4 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For sexually transmitted diseases caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em>: 1 gm as a single dose, or 500 mg on Day 1 followed by 250 mg daily for 2 more days.</li><li><strong>Children</strong> (over 6 months):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 mg/kg body weight once daily for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">200 mg (1 teaspoonful) for 15-25 kg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">300 mg (1½ teaspoonfuls) for 26-35 kg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">400 mg (2 teaspoonfuls) for 36-45 kg</li><li>In typhoid fever, 500 mg (2½ teaspoonfuls) once daily for 7-10 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zycin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Reconstitution</strong>:</li></ul><ol><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Shake the bottle thoroughly to break up the powder.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fill the bottle with boiled and cooled water up to the watermark on the label.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mix the powder and water thoroughly.</li></ol><ul><li>Take Zycin 200 mg/5 ml at least one hour before or two hours after eating.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zycin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Absorption may be decreased</strong> in the presence of food and antacids.</li><li><strong>Avoid combining</strong> Zycin with ergot alkaloids due to potential for ergotism.</li><li>Care should be taken with co-administration of cyclosporin and digoxin as macrolides can increase their plasma levels.</li><li>No known significant interactions with warfarin, theophylline, carbamazepine, methylprednisolone, or cimetidine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to Zycin 200 mg/5 ml or other macrolide antibiotics.</li><li><strong>Ergot derivatives</strong> should not be administered with Zycin.</li><li>Contraindicated in <strong>patients with liver disorders</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Zycin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Low-frequency side effects include:</p><ul><li>Nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain or cramps</li><li>Flatulence, diarrhea, headaches, skin rashes</li></ul><p>Therapeutic withdrawal typically reverses these effects.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: No evidence of harm to the fetus in animal studies, but controlled studies in pregnant women are lacking. Use only if no other suitable options are available.</li><li>The presence of Zycin in breast milk is unknown, so caution should be exercised when administering it to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor for <strong>superinfection</strong> with non-susceptible organisms, such as fungi, during antibiotic therapy.</li><li>No dose adjustment needed for patients with <strong>renal impairment</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zycin 200 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>No specific information on overdose, but symptoms of macrolide overdose may include <strong>hearing loss, severe nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea</strong>.</li><li><strong>Gastric lavage and supportive treatment</strong> are recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a <strong>dry place, away from heat and light</strong>.</li><li><strong>Keep out of reach of children</strong>.</li></ul>', 'active', '497_1742496461.jpg', 64, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-20 23:47:41', '2025-05-05 22:41:55'),
(3750, 'Solas', 'Mebendazole', 1.15, 18, 'tablet', '18 tablet in a strip', 'Chewable Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a summary of the key points from the information you provided about <strong>Solas 100 mg</strong>:</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p>Solas 100 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Threadworms</li><li>Whipworms</li><li>Roundworms</li><li>Hookworms</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Anthelmintic</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li>Solas 100 mg is a synthetic derivative of benzimidazole with potent anthelmintic activity.</li><li>Effective against <strong>ascariasis</strong>, <strong>enterobiasis</strong>, <strong>trichuriasis</strong>, and <strong>hookworm infections</strong>.</li><li>It works by inhibiting glucose uptake in the parasites but does not affect blood glucose in the host.</li><li>Can be used for single or mixed infections.</li><li>The medication does not require special procedures (such as purging or dietary changes) during treatment.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Adult &amp; Children over 2 years</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Threadworms</strong>: 100 mg (1 teaspoon) - Single dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Whipworms, Roundworms, Hookworms</strong>: 100 mg (1 teaspoon) - Twice daily for 3 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Reinfection</strong>: If reinfection occurs, a second dose may be needed after 2 weeks.</li></ul><h3><strong>Administration</strong></h3><ul><li>Can be taken with or without food.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Cimetidine</strong>: May inhibit Solas 100 mg metabolism, increasing plasma concentration.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to Solas 100 mg or any of its components.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Abdominal pain and diarrhea (in case of massive infection and expulsion of worms).</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Rash, urticaria, and angioedema (rare).</li><li><strong>Central Nervous System</strong>: Very rare convulsions.</li><li><strong>Hematologic</strong>: Neutropenia and agranulocytosis.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Not recommended during pregnancy, particularly in the first trimester.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: The safety during lactation is unknown. It should be used only if the benefits outweigh the risks.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>General</strong>: Periodic organ system assessments (including hematopoietic and hepatic) are advised during prolonged therapy.</li><li><strong>Patient Information</strong>: Inform patients of potential fetal risks during pregnancy and emphasize cleanliness to prevent reinfection.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>In case of overdose, gastrointestinal symptoms like vomiting and diarrhea may occur. Induce vomiting and purging.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below 25°C in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Anthelmintic</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li>Solas 100 mg inhibits microtubule formation in worms, leading to glucose depletion in the parasite.</li><li>Only 2-10% of the dose is absorbed through the gastrointestinal tract.</li><li>Peak plasma concentration is reached within 30 minutes to 7 hours.</li><li>Highly bound to plasma proteins, with an elimination half-life of 2.8 to 9 hours.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></h3><ul><li>The safety and efficacy in children under 2 years have not been extensively studied. The benefit-risk ratio should be considered in these cases.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further clarification or additional details!</p>', 'active', '1443_1742498753.jpg', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 00:25:53', '2025-05-04 21:15:20'),
(3751, 'Cosarin', 'Glucosamine Sulfate + Diacerein', 14.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '750 mg plus 50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cosarin 750 mg + 50 mg</strong></p><p>This is primarily indicated in conditions like Osteoarthritis, Rheumatoid arthritis, and Bone &amp; Joint injuries.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Stimulation of Cartilage formation</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Glucosamine is a natural substance produced in the human body and found in certain foods. It stimulates the production of glycosaminoglycan &amp; proteoglycan, two essential building blocks of cartilage. As a supplement, Glucosamine acts as a joint lubricant and shock absorber necessary to maintain healthy cartilage and joint function.</p><p>Diacerein is an anti-inflammatory analgesic and antipyretic drug, indicated especially for the treatment of arthritic conditions. It directly inhibits IL-1 synthesis and release in vitro, down-modulating IL-1 induced activities and relieving Osteoarthritis symptoms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cosarin 750 mg + 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children above 12 years</strong>: One tablet (Glucosamine Sulphate 750 mg and Diacerein 50 mg) twice daily for 4-12 weeks, taken with food.</li><li><strong>Creatinine Clearance (CrCl) &lt; 30 ml/min</strong>: The dose of Diacerein should be reduced.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Cosarin 750 mg + 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>There have been no reports of significant drug interactions between Glucosamine and antibiotics, antidepressants, antihypertensives, nitrates, antiarrhythmics, anxiolytics, hypoglycemic agents, or antisecretives.</li><li>Decreased absorption of Diacerein when co-administered with aluminum and/or magnesium hydroxide antacids.</li><li>Increased risk of diarrhea with laxatives and antibiotics.</li><li>Avoid co-administration with fibers and phytic acids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>There are no known contraindications for Glucosamine, but proven hypersensitivity to Glucosamine is a contraindication.</li><li>Diabetics are advised to monitor blood glucose levels regularly when taking Glucosamine.</li><li>No special studies were performed in patients with renal and/or hepatic insufficiency. However, administration to patients with severe hepatic or renal insufficiency should be under appropriate medical supervision.</li><li>Children should not be supplemented with Glucosamine.</li><li>Diacerein is contraindicated in pregnancy, lactation, and hypersensitivity to anthraquinone derivatives.</li><li>Diacerein is also contraindicated for individuals with allergies to shellfish (e.g., shrimp, crab), those who have diabetes, asthma, alcohol dependence, or liver disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Cosarin 750 mg + 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Diarrhea, constipation, epigastric pain, heartburn, nausea, vomiting, headache, skin rashes, drowsiness, intense yellow coloring of urine.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Diacerein is contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation.</li><li>Glucosamine should not be taken by pregnant women and nursing mothers due to the lack of scientific data regarding its effect on the fetus and infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be practiced when administering Glucosamine + Diacerein to those patients who are allergic to Glucosamine and/or Diacerein or to shellfish (e.g., shrimp, crab).</li><li>Patients with diabetes, asthma, alcohol dependence, or liver disease should use this medication with caution.</li><li>Before taking Glucosamine + Diacerein, complete blood count, liver function, and urinalysis should be monitored regularly.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Cosarin 750 mg + 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Diarrhea may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, protect from light and moisture.</li></ul>', 'active', '1090_1742498916.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 00:28:36', '2025-05-03 01:41:11'),
(3752, 'Seroxyn', 'Salmeterol + Fluticasone Propionate', 820.00, 1, 'x 120 metered doses', '120 metered doses', 'Inhaler', '25 mcg plus 250 mcg per puff', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Seroxyn (25 mcg+250 mcg)/puff</strong></p><p>Seroxyn is indicated for the regular treatment of asthma where the use of a combination product (long-acting β2-agonist and inhaled corticosteroid) is appropriate. It is especially useful for:</p><ul><li>Patients not adequately controlled with inhaled corticosteroids and \'as needed\' inhaled short-acting β2-agonists.</li><li>Patients already adequately controlled on both inhaled corticosteroid and long-acting β2-agonists.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants</li><li>Respiratory corticosteroids</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate</strong>: A selective, long-acting β2-agonist used in the treatment of asthma and other forms of diffuse airways obstruction.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: A corticosteroid with mainly glucocorticoid activity. It exerts a topical effect on the lungs without systemic effects at usual doses. It improves lung function and prevents exacerbations.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Inhalation Aerosol</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol + 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily or 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol + 125 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily or 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol + 250 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4-12 years)</strong>: 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol + 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Powder in Capsule (For Asthma)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg or 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 250 µg twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric Patients (4-11 years)</strong>: Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Powder in Maxhaler (For Asthma)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: One Inhalation of 50/100, 50/250, or 50/500 Maxhaler twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4 years and older)</strong>: One Inhalation of 50/100 Maxhaler twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration Instructions</strong></p><p>To use the inhaler effectively:</p><ol><li>Take off the cap and shake the inhaler vigorously.</li><li>Breathe out fully and hold the inhaler upright.</li><li>Place the actuator in your mouth and close your lips around it.</li><li>While breathing in slowly, press the canister to release the medicine.</li><li>Hold your breath for at least 10 seconds.</li><li>Wait for 1 minute between puffs if more than one inhalation is prescribed.</li><li>Rinse your mouth with water after use.</li></ol><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>Avoid using non-selective and selective β-blockers in patients with asthma unless necessary. Be cautious when co-administering strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole, ritonavir) as they can increase the systemic exposure to Fluticasone Propionate.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol</strong>: Tremor, palpitations, headache, cardiac arrhythmias, arthralgia, hypersensitivity reactions (rash, edema, angioedema), oropharyngeal irritation.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: Hoarseness, candidiasis (thrush), cutaneous hypersensitivity reactions, oropharyngeal edema.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Use during pregnancy and lactation should only be considered if the benefits to the mother outweigh any potential risks to the fetus or child. There is insufficient data regarding the use of Salmeterol and Fluticasone Propionate during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in patients with active or quiescent pulmonary tuberculosis.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with thyrotoxicosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C. Do not puncture or incinerate the pressurized canister. Keep it away from eyes and out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Special Patient Groups</strong></p><p>No dose adjustment is needed for elderly patients or those with renal impairment. There are no data for use in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants</li><li>Respiratory corticosteroids</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate</strong>: Long-acting β2-agonist that works by binding to the exo-site of the β2-receptor.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: Inhaled glucocorticoid that reduces symptoms and exacerbations of asthma.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><p>In pediatric patients, orally inhaled corticosteroids may cause a reduction in growth velocity. Long-term effects, including the impact on adult height, are unknown.</p>', 'in-active', '1625_1742499118.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 00:31:58', '2025-05-12 19:21:19'),
(3753, 'Fenadin', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '180 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Fenadin 180 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Allergic Rhinitis</strong>: In adults and children 12 years of age and older, Fenadin 180 mg is used to alleviate seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis symptoms, including sneezing, rhinorrhea, lacrimation, itchy eyes, and itchy nose, palate, and throat. It helps improve work/activity productivity and quality of life.</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>: It is also recommended for the treatment of chronic idiopathic urticaria in adults and children 12 years and older. Fenadin helps reduce the number of wheals and pruritus associated with chronic idiopathic urticaria, enhancing overall productivity and quality of life.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Fenadin 180 mg is a selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist. It is quickly absorbed after oral administration, with peak plasma concentration reached in 2 to 3 hours. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Seasonal Allergic RhinitisAdults and Children 12 years and older</strong>: Tablet: 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children from 6 to 11 years</strong>: Tablet: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children from 2 to 11 years</strong>: Suspension: 30 mg (5 ml) twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Impaired renal function</strong>: Reduce dose as recommended for each age group.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Chronic Idiopathic UrticariaAdults and Children 12 years and older</strong>: Tablet: 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children from 6 months to 11 years</strong>: Suspension: 15 mg (2.5 ml) twice daily for younger children or 30 mg (5 ml) twice daily for older children</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Impaired renal function</strong>: Adjust the dosage as necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Co-administration with erythromycin or ketoconazole increases fexofenadine plasma levels 2-3 times, but does not affect the QT interval or lead to significant adverse reactions.</li><li>No interaction with omeprazole.</li><li><strong>Antacids containing aluminum and magnesium hydroxide</strong> decrease bioavailability of Fenadin 180 mg. It is recommended to wait two hours before taking antacids after Fenadin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Fenadin 180 mg or any of its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Headaches, fatigue, vertigo, nausea, and general site issues.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Exhaustion.</li><li><strong>Serious</strong>: Hypersensitivity reactions such as angioedema, tightness in the chest, dyspnea, flushing, and systemic anaphylaxis.</li><li><strong>Others</strong>: Anxiety, nightmares, palpitations, tachycardia, diarrhea, rash, and pruritus.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category C</strong> by the US FDA for pregnancy. Use only if the benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li>Not recommended for use in breastfeeding mothers as fexofenadine passes into breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in elderly patients and those with renal or hepatic impairments.</li><li>Patients with cardiovascular disease should be informed of potential adverse effects like tachycardia and palpitations.</li><li>Fenadin 180 mg has minimal impact on central nervous system functions; however, individual reactions should be monitored before driving or operating machinery.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose include dry mouth, sleepiness, fatigue, and dizziness.</li><li>No significant adverse reactions even with high doses (up to 800 mg).</li><li>Hemodialysis does not efficiently remove fexofenadine from the blood.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep dry, away from heat and light. Store out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Fenadin stabilizes the inactive version of the H1 receptor, preventing the receptor’s activation and subsequent allergic response (e.g., itching, watery eyes, sneezing).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Insufficient data for use in pregnancy. It should only be used if absolutely necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Adjust dosage as necessary for children with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Similar adverse effects in patients under 65 and those over 65, but pharmacokinetics are altered in older patients, with enhanced bioavailability.</li></ul><p><strong>Important Note</strong>: Always follow the prescribed dosages and consult with healthcare providers for any concerns regarding the use of Fenadin 180 mg.</p>', 'active', '962_1742499291.webp', 6, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 00:34:51', '2025-05-02 17:45:39'),
(3754, 'Cavic-C', 'Calcium Lactate Gluconate + Calcium Carbonate + Vitamin C', 16.25, 12, 'tablet', '12 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '1000 mg plus 327 mg plus 500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here\'s the provided information with proper formatting, and the headings in bold text:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Cavic-C</strong></p><p>This is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Increased demand for Calcium and Vitamin-C, e.g. pregnancy, lactation, periods of rapid growth (childhood, adolescence), in old age</li><li>During infectious disease and convalescence</li><li>Treatment of calcium and vitamin-C deficiency</li><li>Osteoporosis</li><li>Premenstrual syndrome</li><li>Postmenopausal problems</li><li>Adjuvant in colds and influenza</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calcium</strong> is used as a pharmacological agent in humans almost entirely to remedy deficiency. Adequate calcium in the blood is so vital to a wide variety of bodily functions that our internal biochemistry will not tolerate a deficiency even for short periods.</li><li><strong>Vitamin-C</strong> is an essential component of the diet as man cannot synthesize vitamin-C. It is a very powerful reducing agent. Vitamin-C plays an important part in the response of the body to stress. It is important in the defense against infection.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cavic-C</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children above 7 years:</strong> 1 effervescent tablet daily</li><li><strong>Children 3 to 7 years:</strong> ½ effervescent tablet daily</li><li><strong>Infants:</strong> As prescribed by the physician</li><li>Dissolve one tablet in half a glass (100 ml) of water.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Cavic-C</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calcium Gluconate:</strong> Co-admin of high calcium doses with thiazide diuretics may result in milk-alkali syndrome and hypercalcaemia. May potentiate digoxin toxicity. Decreases effects of calcium channel blockers. Enhanced absorption with calcitriol (a vitamin D metabolite).</li><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate:</strong> Co-administration with thiazide diuretics or vitamin D may lead to milk-alkali syndrome and hypercalcaemia. Decreased absorption with corticosteroids. Decreases absorption of tetracyclines, atenolol, iron, quinolones, alendronate, Na fluoride, Zn, and calcium-channel blockers. Enhances cardiac effects of digitalis glycosides and may precipitate digitalis intoxication.</li><li><strong>Vitamin C:</strong> Deferroxamine, hormonal contraceptives, flufenazine, warfarin, elemental iron, salicylates, warfarin, fluphenazine, disulfiram, mexiletine, vitamin B12.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypercalcemia (e.g., in hyperparathyroidism, vitamin-D overdosage, decalcifying tumors such as plasmocytoma, bone metastases); severe hypercalciuria; severe renal failure.</li><li>Patients with hyperoxalauria, glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, or iron overload. Larger doses may lead to gastrointestinal tract upset.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Cavic-C</strong></p><p>In rare cases, mild gastrointestinal disturbances (bloating, diarrhoea) can occur. In predisposed patients, prolonged treatment with high doses may promote the formation of calculi in the urinary tract.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Calcium-containing drugs have been widely used in pregnancy by way of oral calcium supplementation or antacid therapy. Calcium Carbonate can be safely used in lactating women.</li><li>Vitamin-C may be taken safely during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>For patients with mild hypercalciuria (exceeding 300 mg = 7.5 mmol/24 hours), with mild or moderate impairment of renal function, or with a history of urinary concrements, monitoring of calcium excretion in the urine is required. If necessary, the dosage should be reduced or therapy should be discontinued.</li><li>High doses of vitamin D and derivatives should be avoided during treatment with this preparation unless especially indicated.</li><li>Since citrate salts have been reported to increase aluminium absorption, this medicine should be used with caution in patients with severely impaired renal function, especially those receiving aluminium-containing preparations.</li><li>The sugar content should be taken into account by diabetic patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Cavic-C</strong></p><p>Acute overdosage has not been reported. It would be expected to cause gastrointestinal disturbances but not to result in hypercalcemia, except in patients treated with a very high dosage of vitamin-D and derivatives.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a cool and dry place and protected from light and moisture.</p><p>Let me know if you need further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '1229_1742499545.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 00:39:05', '2025-05-03 01:14:59'),
(3755, 'Ferix-V', 'Carbonyl Iron + Folic Acid + Zinc Sulfate', 4.50, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50 mg+0.50 mg+61.80 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ferix-V</strong></p><p>It is indicated for the treatment and prophylaxis of Iron, Folic Acid, and Zinc deficiency, especially during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each capsule contains:</p><ul><li>Elemental iron 50 mg (As carbonyl iron INN)</li><li>Folic acid BP 500 microgram</li><li>Zinc sulphate monohydrate USP 61.80 mg (equivalent to 22.50 mg zinc)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined preparation</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Carbonyl iron is a form of the mineral iron. Iron is important for many functions in the body, especially for the transport of oxygen in the blood. Carbonyl iron is used as a dietary supplement and to prevent and treat iron deficiencies and iron deficiency anemia.</p><p>Folic acid enhances chemical reactions that contribute to the production of red blood cells, the manufacture of DNA needed for cell replication, and the metabolism of amino acids (compounds necessary for the manufacture of proteins).</p><p>Zinc is essential to numerous physiological processes, including the function of many enzymes in the body. Deficiency may lead to poor night vision, slow healing of wounds, poor sexual development and function in males, poor appetite (perhaps owing to a decrease in the sense of taste and smell), a reduced ability to ward off infections, diarrhea, dermatitis, and in children, retarded growth.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ferix-V</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Elderly:</strong> 1 capsule daily. In more severe cases, 2 capsules daily may be required.</li><li><strong>Children</strong> (Aged over 1 year): 1 capsule daily. The capsule may be opened, and the pellets mixed with soft, cool food, but they must not be chewed.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Ferix-V</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult:</strong> One capsule daily before food or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ferix-V</strong></p><ul><li>Carbonyl Iron decreases the absorption of tetracycline antibiotics, quinolone antibiotics, levodopa, levothyroxine, methyldopa, and penicillamine.</li><li>Folic Acid interacts with antiepileptics, so plasma concentrations of phenobarbital, phenytoin, and primidone may be reduced.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of its components or those with Iron overload.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ferix-V</strong></p><p>Gastrointestinal irritations such as nausea, anorexia, vomiting, discomfort, constipation, and diarrhea may occur. Patients may complain of dark stool. Carbonyl Iron pellets incorporated into the capsules to reduce the possibility of gastrointestinal irritations. Rarely, there may be allergic reactions.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Use of any drug during the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided if possible. Thus, administration of iron during the first trimester requires definite evidence of iron deficiency. Prophylaxis of iron deficiency where inadequate diet calls for supplementary zinc and folic acid is justified during the remainder of pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Special care should be taken in patients with Iron overload states, such as haemochromatosis, haemolytic anemia, or red blood cell aplasia. Failure to respond to the treatment requires further investigations to exclude other causes of anemia. In patients with renal failure, there may be the risk of Zinc accumulation.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ferix-V</strong></p><p>Symptoms of Carbonyl Iron overdose include decreased energy, nausea, abdominal pain, tarry stool, weak rapid pulse, fever, coma, and seizures.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C and keep away from light and moisture. Keep all medicines out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined preparation</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This timed-release capsule is the combined preparation of Carbonyl Iron, Folic Acid, and Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate. It contains Carbonyl Iron with not less than 98% Iron content. Carbonyl Iron, having high bioavailability and low toxicity, is a safer and more effective choice for iron supplementation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Use of any drug during the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided if possible. Thus, administration of Iron during the first trimester requires definite evidence of Iron deficiency. Prophylaxis of Iron deficiency where inadequate diet calls for supplementary Zinc and Folic acid is justified during the remainder of pregnancy.</p>', 'active', '1979_1742499757.webp', 6, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 00:42:37', '2025-04-19 10:04:43');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3756, 'Calbo Jr', 'Elemental Calcium', 4.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Chewable Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Calbo Jr 250 mg</strong></p><p><strong>250 mg or 500 mg tablet:</strong> This is used for the treatment or prevention of calcium depletion in patients in whom dietary measures are inadequate. Conditions that may be associated with calcium deficiency include hypoparathyroidism, achlorhydria, chronic diarrhea, vitamin D deficiency, steatorrhea, sprue, pregnancy and lactation, menopause, pancreatitis, renal failure, alkalosis, and hyperphosphataemia. Calbo Jr 250 mg is being used increasingly often to treat hyperphosphataemia in chronic renal failure as well as those on continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis (CAPD) and haemodialysis. Many patients are unable to tolerate sufficient doses for complete phosphate control and require additional measures such as stringent dietary phosphate restriction or relatively small doses of aluminium hydroxide. Calbo Jr 250 mg containing preparations can provide short-term relief of dyspeptic symptoms but are no longer recommended for long-term treatment of peptic ulceration.</p><p><strong>1000 mg tablet:</strong> This is indicated for the management of conditions associated with hyperidity and for fast relief of acid indigestion, heartburn, sour stomach, and upset stomach.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Minerals in bone formation, Specific mineral preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Calcium carbonate reacts with gastric acid to produce a salt and water. For Calbo Jr 250 mg, the postulated chemical reaction is:</p><p>CaCO₃ + 2HCl = CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂</p><p>Two grams of Calbo Jr 250 mg will readily bring 100 ml of hydrochloric acid to a pH above 6. The increase in gastric pH diminishes the activity of pepsin in the gastric secretion. Up to 30% of the oral calcium load may be absorbed.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Calbo Jr 250 mg</strong></p><p><strong>250 mg or 500 mg tablet:</strong></p><p>Calbo Jr 250 mg is always used orally and when used as an antacid, the recommended doses for adults are equivalent to 540-2000 mg Calbo Jr 250 mg per day, with doses for children being half of those for adults. As a dietary supplement, such as for the prevention of osteoporosis, 1250-3750 mg Calbo Jr 250 mg (500-1500 mg calcium) daily is recommended in general, but this will need to be tailored to the individual patient depending on any specific disease such as calcium deficiency, malabsorption, or parathyroid function. In pregnancy and lactation, the recommended daily dose of calcium is 1200-1500 mg. In chronic renal failure, the doses used vary from 2.5-9.0 gm Calbo Jr 250 mg per day and need to be adjusted according to the individual patient. To maximize effective phosphate binding in this context, the Calbo Jr 250 mg should be given with meals.</p><p><strong>1000 mg tablet:</strong> 2000-3000 mg tablet when symptoms occur; may be repeated hourly if needed or as directed by the physician.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction of Calbo Jr 250 mg</strong></p><p>Calbo Jr 250 mg may enhance the cardiac effects of digoxin and other cardiac glycosides, if systemic hypercalcaemia occurs. Calbo Jr 250 mg may interfere with the absorption of concomitantly administered tetracycline preparations and in chronic renal failure, modification of vitamin D therapy may be required to avoid hypercalcaemia when Calbo Jr 250 mg is used as the primary phosphate binder.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypercalcaemia and hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Hypercalciuria and nephrolithiasis</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Concomitant digoxin therapy (requires careful monitoring of serum calcium level)</li><li>When hypercalcaemia occurs, discontinuation of the drug is usually sufficient to return serum calcium concentrations to normal.</li><li>Calcium salts should be used cautiously in patients with sarcoidosis, renal or cardiac disease, and in patients receiving cardiac glycosides.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Calbo Jr 250 mg</strong></p><p>Orally administered Calbo Jr 250 mg may be irritating to the GI tract. It may also cause constipation. Hypercalcaemia is rarely produced by administration of calcium alone, but may occur when large doses are given to patients with chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Calcium-containing drugs have been widely used in pregnancy by way of oral calcium supplementation or antacid therapy. Calbo Jr 250 mg can be used in lactating women too.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place at controlled room temperature.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p><strong>Use in children:</strong> Calcium carbonate has been extensively studied in children and infants with chronic renal failure and is both safe and effective.</p><p><strong>Use in elderly:</strong> In case of elderly patients with renal failure, when Calbo Jr 250 mg is taken, constipation may be troublesome for this group. For this reason, monitoring of serum calcium and phosphate is indicated for elderly patients.</p>', 'active', '1249_1742499882.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 00:44:42', '2025-05-03 01:04:11'),
(3757, 'Esonix', 'Esomeprazole', 10.00, 14, 'Tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Esonix 40 mg</strong></p><p>Esonix 40 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>To alleviate GERD-related symptoms, such as persistent heartburn.</li><li>To treat erosive esophagitis.</li><li>To maintain the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>For the eradication of Helicobacter pylori infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>For Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>For duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Esonix 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. Most patients heal within this period. If healing is incomplete, an additional 4-8 weeks may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg once daily (clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve, an additional 4 weeks may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori Eradication (Triple Therapy for Duodenal Ulcer Recurrence):</strong> 40 mg once daily for 10 days, with amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily and clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> 20-80 mg once daily. The dosage should be individualized, and treatment continued as long as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks, based on response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> 40 mg once daily via intravenous injection (minimum 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10-30 minutes).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Esonix 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take one pill or capsule of Esonix 40 mg whole, at least one hour before a meal.</li><li>For <strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>, mix the contents of one packet with 15 ml of water in a small glass. Stir well, let it thicken for 2-3 minutes, stir again, and consume within 30 minutes.</li><li>If using a <strong>nasogastric or gastric tube</strong>, shake the syringe with 15 ml of water, wait 2-3 minutes, and administer into the stomach.</li><li>For <strong>intravenous administration</strong>, 5 ml of the specified solvent (WFI) is added to the vial. The reconstituted solution should be given over a minimum of 3 minutes and used within 12 hours at room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Esonix 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Esonix is metabolized by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 enzymes.</li><li>It does not significantly interact with drugs metabolized by other CYP enzymes (e.g., CYP1A2, 2A6, 2C9, 2D6, 2E1, and 3A4).</li><li>Esonix may decrease the absorption of drugs like ketoconazole, iron salts, and digoxin, which are affected by gastrointestinal pH.</li><li>Co-administration with diazepam may increase diazepam plasma levels due to CYP2C19 inhibition.</li><li>Esonix does not interact clinically with phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Esonix is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of its formulations.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Esonix 40 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>These side effects are typically more common during short-term use, though similar side effects may occur during long-term treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>A positive response to treatment does not rule out the possibility of stomach cancer.</li><li>Esonix should be taken at least one hour before meals.</li><li>For patients with swallowing difficulties, the capsule may be opened and the pellets mixed with applesauce (cold and soft enough to swallow without chewing).</li><li>Antacids can be used while on Esonix.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Esonix 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose symptoms in animal studies include decreased motor activity, changes in breathing, tremors, ataxia, and occasional convulsions.</li><li>No specific antidote for esomeprazole overdose.</li><li>Symptomatic and supportive care should be provided. Dialysis is unlikely to remove esomeprazole due to its protein binding.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, at a temperature not exceeding 30°C, and shield from moisture and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Esonix works by inhibiting the H+/K+-ATPase proton pump in the gastric parietal cells, thereby reducing stomach acid secretion.</p><ul><li><strong>Absorption:</strong> Peak plasma levels occur approximately 1.5 hours after oral dosing.</li><li><strong>Metabolism:</strong> Esonix is extensively metabolized in the liver by the CYP enzyme system, primarily CYP2C19 and CYP3A4.</li><li><strong>Excretion:</strong> 80% is excreted in the urine as inactive metabolites, while 20% is excreted in the feces.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>No sufficient data on pregnant women. Animal studies have not shown teratogenic effects, but the use of esomeprazole during pregnancy should only be considered if necessary, and breastfeeding should be avoided.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>No significant differences in safety and efficacy between elderly and younger adults. However, older patients may be more sensitive.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage change for mild to moderate hepatic insufficiency. For severe hepatic impairment, limit the dose to 20 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>As less than 1% of esomeprazole is excreted unchanged in the urine, renal impairment is not expected to significantly affect its pharmacokinetics.</li></ul>', 'active', '1570_1742499992.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 00:46:32', '2025-05-02 12:35:08'),
(3758, 'Telukast', 'Montelukast Sodium', 17.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Telukast 10 mg</strong></p><p>Telukast 10 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. Cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) are located in the human airway, and these eicosanoids bind to them. The orally active drug Telukast 10 mg has a strong affinity and specificity for the CysLT1 receptor. Without any agonist activity, it inhibits LTD4\'s physiological effects at the CysLT1 receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Telukast 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</strong></li><li>The dosage for adults and adolescents 15 years of age and older: <strong>Monas 10 mg</strong> tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</strong>The dosage for pediatric patients 6 to 14 years of age: <strong>Monas 5 mg</strong> tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 2 years to 5 years of age: <strong>Monas 4 mg</strong> tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 6 months to 5 years of age: <strong>Monas 4 mg oral granules</strong> once daily. This can be administered either directly in the mouth or mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Pediatric Patients</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Monas have been established in adequate and well-controlled studies in pediatric patients with asthma 6 months to 14 years of age. Safety and efficacy profiles in this age group are similar to those seen in adults.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency:</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is required in patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is recommended in patients with renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Elderly Use:</strong></p><p>The pharmacokinetic profile and the oral bioavailability of a single 10-mg oral dose of Monas are similar in elderly and younger adults. The plasma half-life of Monas is slightly longer in the elderly. No dosage adjustment in the elderly is required.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Telukast 10 mg</strong></p><p>In the prevention and long-term treatment of asthma, Telukast 10 mg has been provided alongside other commonly used treatments without seeming to increase the risk of negative side effects. According to studies on drug interactions, theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives (norethindrone 1mg/ethinyl estradiol 35mcg), terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin did not exhibit any clinically significant effects on the pharmacokinetics at the recommended clinical dose of it. In clinical investigations, the medication was used concurrently with a variety of routinely prescribed medications without showing any signs of clinically harmful interactions, despite the fact that more detailed interaction studies were not carried out. Thyroid hormones, hypnotic sedatives, benzodiazepines, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and decongestants were among these drugs. Following a single dose of this solution, the hepatic metabolism-inducing drug phenobarbital reduced the drug\'s AUC by about 40%. For this medication, no dosage modification is advised. When powerful cytochrome P450 enzyme inducers, such as phenobarbital or rifampin, are provided alongside this solution, it is fair to use proper clinical monitoring.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those who have an extreme sensitivity to any ingredient in this medication should not take it.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Telukast 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon:</strong> Akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, asthenia, aberrant behavior, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, hemorrhage, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, oedema, seizure, odd feeling, and sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, decreased attention, disorientation, erythema nodosum, hallucinations, hepatic problems, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, and tremor.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Telukast 10 mg is classified as pregnancy category B. Although it has been demonstrated that the medication crosses the placenta of pregnant rats and rabbits, there are no records of its use in pregnant humans. Although there is some evidence that it is also secreted in breast milk, little is known about the implications of this discovery. Before starting the therapy in nursing moms, caution should be exercised.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>The reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks, including status asthmaticus, is not recommended as usage for Telukast 10 mg. Patients should be told to have access to the proper rescue medication.</li><li>Asthma exacerbations that are acute can still be treated with the solution.</li><li>While the dosage of an inhaled corticosteroid may be gradually decreased under medical supervision, the use of this solution instead of an inhaled or oral corticosteroid should not be started suddenly.</li><li>Exercise-induced bronchospasm should not be treated and managed with a single dose of it.</li><li>While taking it, patients with documented aspirin sensitivity should continue to refrain from taking aspirin or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Although aspirin sensitivity in asthmatics is a known side effect of the drug, it has not been demonstrated to shorten the bronchoconstrictor response to aspirin and other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs).</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Telukast 10 mg</strong></p><p>The majority of overdose reports did not include any negative experiences. The most frequent side effects, which were in line with Telukast 10 mg\'s safety profile, were nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain. In the case of an overdose, it is permissible to take the customary supportive measures, such as removing any unabsorbed material from the digestive tract, using clinical monitoring, and if necessary, instituting supportive therapy.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry environment while keeping out light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>The cysteinyl leukotriene receptor is inhibited by the selective and orally active leukotriene receptor antagonist known as Telukast 10 mg (CysLT1). The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. The pathophysiology of asthma and allergic rhinitis, including airway edema, smooth muscle contraction, and altered cellular activity associated with the inflammatory process, have been linked to cysteinyl leukotrienes and leukotriene receptor occupation. These factors all contribute to the signs and symptoms of asthma.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>After oral administration, Telukast 10 mg passes the placenta in rats and rabbits. Nevertheless, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. Because the human reaction to medications is not usually predicted by research on animal reproduction, pregnant women should only use it if absolutely necessary. When giving the pill to a nursing mother, care should be taken because many medications are secreted in human milk.</p>', 'active', '140_1742500121.jpg', 37, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 00:48:41', '2025-05-04 22:19:23'),
(3759, 'D-Vine', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 35.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '40000 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of D-Vine 40000 IU:</strong> Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated in the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also indicated as an adjunct to specific therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcaemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Colecalciferol, a synthetic form of Vitamin D, is essential for normal bone growth and development and to maintain bone density. It is also necessary for the utilization of both calcium and phosphorus. Babies need Vitamin D3 for healthy growth and development. It acts as a hormone.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation</li><li>Vitamin-D preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. Vitamin D3 is essential for normal bone growth and to maintain bone density. It also reduces the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, prevents the risk of certain cancers (breast, colorectal), and helps to maintain adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of D-Vine 40000 IU:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>For capsule:Adults:</strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 40000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks. Maintenance therapy: 1400-2000 IU/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 20000 IU every 4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Addition to specific therapy for osteoporosis: 20000 IU once a month.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Children (12-18 years):</strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 20000 IU once every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 20000 IU every 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For film-coated tablet:</strong>1000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily, or as directed by physician. Take the medicine with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For oroflash or chewable tablets:</strong>1000 IU to 2000 IU daily, or as directed by physician. Take the medicine with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li><li><strong>For Syrup:</strong>For patients with risk of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">0-1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml)</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-2\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml)</blockquote><ul><li>For Vitamin D3 deficient patients:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">0-1 year: 2000 IU/day (+50000 IU/week) for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 years: 2000 IU/day for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Injection:</strong>Prevention for infants receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e., 100000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Nursed infants or those not receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk, or young children up to 5 years of age: 1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e., 200000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adolescents: 1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e., 200000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Pregnancy: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e., 100000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Elderly: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e., 100000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">For digestive disorders, concomitant treatment with antiepileptics, or other particular conditions: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e., 100000 IU or 1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e., 200000 IU every 3 or 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vitamin D deficiency: 1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e., 200000 IU, which can be repeated every 1 to 6 months, or as directed by the registered physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of D-Vine 40000 IU:</strong> It interferes with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (such as liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong> It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of D-Vine 40000 IU:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypercalcaemia</li><li>Hypercalciuria</li><li>Skin rash</li><li>Pruritus</li><li>Urticaria</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Abdominal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong> Studies have shown safe use of doses up to 4000 IU during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU; however, women who are considered Vitamin D3 deficient may require a higher dose. Women should follow the advice of their medical practitioner, as their requirements may vary based on the severity of their condition and response to treatment. Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. Overdose in infants induced by nursing mothers has not been observed; however, when prescribing additional Vitamin D3 to a breastfed child, the practitioner should consider the dose of any additional Vitamin D3 given to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Should be used with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of D-Vine 40000 IU:</strong> It can lead to hypervitaminosis D.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong> Keep below 30ºC, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong> The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation</li><li>Vitamin-D preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) helps with the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is essential for normal bone growth and the maintenance of bone density. It also reduces the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, reduces the risk of certain cancers (breast, colorectal), and helps maintain adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Studies have shown safe use of doses up to 4000 IU during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU, but women who are considered Vitamin D3 deficient may need a higher dose. Women should follow the advice of their healthcare provider, as their requirements may vary depending on the severity of their disease and response to treatment. Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. Overdose in infants induced by nursing mothers has not been observed; however, when prescribing additional Vitamin D3 to a breastfed child, the practitioner should consider the dose of any additional Vitamin D3 given to the mother.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses:</strong> The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</p>', 'active', '1991_1742500302.jpg', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 00:51:42', '2025-05-01 19:53:35'),
(3760, 'Napa', 'Paracetamol', 8.00, 5, 'suppository', '1 suppository', 'Suppository', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Napa 500 mg</strong></p><p>Napa 500 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Fever</li><li>Common cold and influenza</li><li>Headache, toothache, earache, bodyache</li><li>Myalgia, neuralgia, dysmenorrhoea</li><li>Sprains, colic pain, back pain</li><li>Post-operative pain, postpartum pain</li><li>Inflammatory pain, post-vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic &amp; osteoarthritic pain and stiffness of joints</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Napa 500 mg has analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity. Napa 500 mg (Acetaminophen) is thought to act primarily in the CNS, increasing the pain threshold by inhibiting both isoforms of cyclooxygenase (COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3) enzymes involved in prostaglandin (PG) synthesis. Napa 500 mg is a para-aminophenol derivative, one of the most widely used, safest, and fast-acting analgesics. It is well tolerated and free from various side effects of aspirin.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Napa 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Adults</em>: 1-2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours, up to a maximum of 4 gm (8 tablets) daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Children (6-12 years)</em>: ½ to 1 tablet 3 to 4 times daily. For long-term treatment, it is wise not to exceed 2.6 gm/day.</li><li><strong>Extended Release Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years</em>: 2 tablets every 6 to 8 hours (maximum 6 tablets in 24 hours). The tablet must not be crushed.</li><li><strong>Syrup/Suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Children under 3 months</em>: 10 mg/kg body weight (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced), 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>3 months to below 1 year</em>: ½ to 1 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>1-5 years</em>: 1-2 teaspoonfuls 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>6-12 years</em>: 2-A teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Adults</em>: 4-8 teaspoonfuls 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Suppository</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Children 3-12 months</em>: 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Children 1-5 years</em>: 125-250 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Children 6-12 years</em>: 250-500 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Adults &amp; children over 12 years</em>: 0.5-1 gm, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Paediatric Drop</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Children up to 3 months</em>: 0.5 ml (40 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>4 to 11 months</em>: 1.0 ml (80 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>1 to 2 years</em>: 1.5 ml (120 mg). Do not exceed more than 5 doses daily for a maximum of 5 days.</li><li><strong>Napa 500 mg Tablet with Actizorb Technology</strong>:</li><li>This tablet dissolves up to five times faster than standard Napa 500 mg tablets. It is a fast-acting and safe analgesic with marked antipyretic properties. Specially suitable for patients who cannot tolerate aspirin or other analgesics.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Adults and children (aged 12 years and over)</em>: Take 1 to 2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not take more than 8 caplets in 24 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Children (7 to 11 years)</em>: Take ½ to 1 tablet every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not take more than 4 caplets in 24 hours. Not recommended for children under 7 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Napa 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Patients who have taken barbiturates, tricyclic antidepressants, or alcohol may show diminished ability to metabolize large doses of Napa 500 mg.</li><li>Alcohol can increase the hepatotoxicity of Napa 500 mg overdose.</li><li>Chronic ingestion of anticonvulsants or oral steroid contraceptives may induce liver enzymes and prevent attainment of therapeutic Napa 500 mg levels by increasing first-pass metabolism or clearance.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Napa 500 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Napa 500 mg</strong></p><p>Side effects are usually mild, but may include:</p><ul><li>Haematological reactions like thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, pancytopenia, neutropenia, and agranulocytosis.</li><li>Pancreatitis, skin rashes, and other allergic reactions may occur occasionally.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy category B according to the USFDA. Should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed.</li><li>Napa 500 mg is excreted in breast milk. Maternal ingestion in normal therapeutic doses does not appear to pose a risk to the nursing infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Napa 500 mg should be used with caution in patients with impaired kidney or liver function.</li><li>It should also be used carefully in patients taking other drugs that affect the liver.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Napa 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms in the first 24 hours include pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and abdominal pain.</li><li>Liver damage may appear 12-48 hours after ingestion.</li><li>Abnormalities of glucose metabolism and metabolic acidosis may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Napa 500 mg has analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity. It works primarily in the CNS by inhibiting COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3 enzymes, which play a role in prostaglandin synthesis. Napa 500 mg is widely used, safe, and fast-acting, free from many of the side effects associated with aspirin.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy category B (USFDA). Should only be used if clearly needed during pregnancy.</li></ul>', 'active', '1381_1742500430.png', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 00:53:50', '2025-05-06 17:54:39'),
(3761, 'Bexitrol F', 'Salmeterol + Fluticasone Propionate', 595.00, 1, 'x 120 metered doses', '120 metered doses', 'Inhaler', '(25 mcg+125 mcg)/puff', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Bexitrol F 25 mcg + 125 mcg</h3><p>Bexitrol F is indicated for the regular treatment of asthma in patients where a combination of a long-acting β2-agonist and an inhaled corticosteroid is appropriate. This includes:</p><ul><li><strong>Patients not adequately controlled with inhaled corticosteroids and \'as needed\' short-acting β2-agonists.</strong></li><li><strong>Patients already adequately controlled on both inhaled corticosteroids and long-acting β2-agonists.</strong></li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants.</strong></li><li><strong>Respiratory corticosteroids.</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate</strong>: A selective, long-acting β2-agonist, used for the treatment of asthma and other forms of diffuse airway obstruction. It has a 12-hour action.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: A corticosteroid with potent glucocorticoid activity, which exerts a topical effect on the lungs, reducing asthma symptoms and preventing exacerbations. At recommended doses, it has minimal systemic effects.</li></ul><p>The combination of Salmeterol (which protects against symptoms) and Fluticasone (which improves lung function and reduces exacerbations) provides a more convenient treatment option for patients already on both therapies.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><h4>Inhalation Aerosol</h4><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol + 50 µg Fluticasone</strong> twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol + 125 µg Fluticasone</strong> twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol + 250 µg Fluticasone</strong> twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children (4–12 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol + 50 µg Fluticasone</strong> twice daily.</li></ul><h4>Inhalation Powder in Capsule (For Asthma)</h4><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Salmeterol 50 µg + Fluticasone 100 µg</strong> or <strong>250 µg</strong> twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The maximum recommended dosage is <strong>Salmeterol 50 µg + Fluticasone 500 µg</strong> twice daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric (4–11 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Salmeterol 50 µg + Fluticasone 100 µg</strong> twice daily.</li></ul><h4>Maxhaler (For Asthma)</h4><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>50/100 Maxhaler</strong>: 1 inhalation twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>50/250 Maxhaler</strong>: 1 inhalation twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>50/500 Maxhaler</strong>: 1 inhalation twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children (4 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>50/100 Maxhaler</strong>: 1 inhalation twice daily.</li></ul><h4>Inhalation Powder in Capsule (For COPD)</h4><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Salmeterol 50 µg + Fluticasone 250 µg</strong> twice daily.</li></ul><h3>Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Using the Inhaler</strong>:</li></ul><ol><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Remove the cap and shake the inhaler.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Exhale fully, then place the mouthpiece between your teeth and close lips around it.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Inhale deeply while pressing down the canister.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hold your breath for at least 10 seconds.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Wait 1 minute between puffs, if prescribed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Replace the cap after use and rinse your mouth with water.</li></ol><h4>Cleaning:</h4><ul><li>Clean the inhaler weekly by removing the canister and rinsing the actuator and cap with warm water. Do not immerse the canister in water. Dry thoroughly.</li></ul><h3>Interaction</h3><ul><li>Avoid non-selective and selective β-blockers in asthma patients, unless necessary.</li><li>Use caution with <strong>strong CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., ketoconazole, ritonavir), as they may increase systemic exposure to Fluticasone Propionate.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><h4>Salmeterol:</h4><ul><li>Tremors, palpitations, headache (typically transient).</li><li>Cardiac arrhythmias (in susceptible patients), muscle cramps, arthralgia, hypersensitivity reactions (rash, oedema).</li><li>Oropharyngeal irritation.</li></ul><h4>Fluticasone Propionate:</h4><ul><li>Hoarseness, oral candidiasis (thrush), hypersensitivity reactions.</li><li>Rare facial and oropharyngeal oedema.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>Use only if the expected benefit to the mother outweighs the potential risk to the fetus or infant, as there is limited data on the use of Salmeterol Xinafoate and Fluticasone Propionate during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Consider additional corticosteroid therapy if an infection is present.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with active or quiescent pulmonary tuberculosis or thyrotoxicosis.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C. Avoid direct sunlight or heat. Keep away from eyes and out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric</strong>: Inhaled corticosteroids may reduce growth velocity in children.</li><li><strong>Elderly &amp; Renal Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No data available for this population.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li><strong>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants.</strong></li><li><strong>Respiratory corticosteroids.</strong></li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol</strong>: A long-acting beta-2 agonist (12-hour action) that binds to the β2 receptors, providing symptom relief.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: A corticosteroid that reduces inflammation in the lungs, minimizing asthma symptoms and preventing exacerbations.</li></ul>', 'active', '174_1742500594.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 00:56:34', '2025-05-06 17:42:11'),
(3762, 'Montair', 'Montelukast Sodium', 6.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Chewable Tablet', '4 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the revised version with the headings in normal bold text:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Montair 4 mg</strong></p><p>Montair 4 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. Cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) are located in the human airway, and these eicosanoids bind to them. The orally active drug Montair 4 mg has a strong affinity and specificity for the CysLT1 receptor. Without any agonist activity, it inhibits LTD4\'s physiological effects at the CysLT1 receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Montair 4 mg</strong></p><p>Adults and adolescents with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</p><ul><li>The dosage for adults and adolescents 15 years of age and older: Monas 10 mg tablet once daily.</li></ul><p>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</p><ul><li>The dosage for pediatric patients 6 to 14 years of age: Monas 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li>The dosage for pediatric patients 2 years to 5 years of age: Monas 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li>The dosage for pediatric patients 6 months to 5 years of age: Monas 4 mg oral granules once daily. This can be administered either directly in the mouth, or mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in the pediatric patient</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Monas have been established in adequate and well-controlled studies in pediatric patients with asthma 6 months to 14 years of age. Safety and efficacy profiles in this age group are similar to those seen in adults.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is required in patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is recommended in patients with renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Elderly use</strong></p><p>The pharmacokinetic profile and the oral bioavailability of a single 10-mg oral dose of Monas are similar in elderly and younger adults. The plasma half-life of Monas is slightly longer in the elderly. No dosage adjustment in the elderly is required.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Montair 4 mg</strong></p><p>In the prevention and long-term treatment of asthma, Montair 4 mg has been provided alongside other commonly used treatments without seeming to increase the risk of negative side effects. According to studies on drug interactions, theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives (norethindrone 1mg/ethinyl estradiol 35mcg), terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin did not exhibit any clinically significant effects on the pharmacokinetics at the recommended clinical dose of it. In clinical investigations, the medication was used concurrently with a variety of routinely prescribed medications without showing any signs of clinically harmful interactions, despite the fact that more detailed interaction studies were not carried out. Thyroid hormones, hypnotic sedatives, benzodiazepines, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and decongestants were among these drugs. Following a single dose of this solution, the hepatic metabolism-inducing drug phenobarbital reduced the drug\'s AUC by about 40%. For this medication, no dosage modification is advised. When powerful cytochrome P450 enzyme inducers, such as phenobarbital or rifampin, are provided alongside this solution, it is fair to use proper clinical monitoring.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those who have an extreme sensitivity to any ingredient in this medication should not take it.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Montair 4 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, asthenia, aberrant behavior, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, hemorrhage, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, oedema, seizure, odd feeling, and sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, decreased attention, disorientation, erythema nodosum, hallucinations, hepatic problems, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, and tremor.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Montair 4 mg is classified as pregnancy category B. Although it has been demonstrated that the medication crosses the placenta of pregnant rats and rabbits, there are no records of its use in pregnant humans. Although there is some evidence that it is also secreted in breast milk, little is known about the implications of this discovery. Before starting the therapy in nursing moms, caution should be exercised.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks, including status asthmaticus, is not recommended as usage for Montair 4 mg. Patients should be told to have access to the proper rescue medication. Asthma exacerbations that are acute can still be treated with the solution. While the dosage of an inhaled corticosteroid may be gradually decreased under medical supervision, the use of this solution instead of an inhaled or oral corticosteroid should not be started suddenly. Exercise-induced bronchospasm should not be treated and managed with a single dose of it. While taking it, patients with documented aspirin sensitivity should continue to refrain from taking aspirin or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Although aspirin sensitivity in asthmatics is a known side effect of the drug, it has not been demonstrated to shorten the bronchoconstrictor response to aspirin and other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs).</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Montair 4 mg</strong></p><p>The majority of overdose reports did not include any negative experiences. The most frequent side effects, which were in line with Montair 4 mg\'s safety profile, were nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain. In the case of an overdose, it is permissible to take the customary supportive measures, such as removing any unabsorbed material from the digestive tract, using clinical monitoring, and if necessary, instituting supportive therapy.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry environment while keeping out light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>The cysteinyl leukotriene receptor is inhibited by the selective and orally active leukotriene receptor antagonist known as Montair 4 mg (CysLT1). The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. The pathophysiology of asthma and allergic rhinitis, including airway edema, smooth muscle contraction, and altered cellular activity associated with the inflammatory process, have been linked to cysteinyl leukotrienes and leukotriene receptor occupation. These factors all contribute to the signs and symptoms of asthma.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>After oral administration, Montair 4 mg passes the placenta in rats and rabbits. Nevertheless, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. Because the human reaction to medications is not usually predicted by research on animal reproduction, pregnant women should only use it if absolutely necessary. When giving the pill to a nursing mother, care should be taken because many medications are secreted in human milk.</p><p>Let me know if you\'d like any more adjustments or if there\'s something else you\'d like to add!</p>', 'active', '539_1742500707.jpg', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 00:58:27', '2025-05-04 00:25:48');
