(3763, 'Lanso', 'Lansoprazole', 6.04, 6, 'capsule', '6 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '30 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Lanso 30 mg</strong></p><p>Lanso 30 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Short term treatment of active duodenal ulcer</li><li>Maintenance of healed duodenal ulcers</li><li>Short term treatment of active benign gastric ulcers</li><li>Short term treatment of active erosive esophagitis</li><li>Maintenance of healing of erosive esophagitis</li><li>Pathological hypersecretory conditions including Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</li><li>H. pylori eradication to reduce the risk of duodenal ulcer recurrence</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Lanso 30 mg is a substituted benzimidazole, and is also known as a PPI due to its property to block the final step of acid secretion by inhibiting the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system in gastric parietal cells. Both basal and stimulated acid secretion are inhibited.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Lanso 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Benign gastric ulcer:</strong> 30 mg daily in the morning for 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer:</strong> 30 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks; maintenance 15 mg.</li><li><strong>NSAID-associated duodenal or gastric ulcer:</strong> 15-30 mg daily for 4 weeks, followed by an additional 4 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (and other hypersecretory conditions):</strong> Initially 60 mg once daily, adjusted according to response; daily doses of 120 mg or more are given in two divided doses.</li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD):</strong> 30 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, followed by an additional 4 weeks if not fully healed; maintenance 15-30 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Acid-related dyspepsia:</strong> 15-30 mg daily in the morning for 2-4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Lanso 30 mg</strong></p><p>Lanso 30 mg appears to be a selective inhibitor of the cytochrome P-450 monooxygenase system; there may be an effect on hepatic clearance, but there have been no reports of clinically relevant interactions. There is some uncertainty over the effect of Lanso 30 mg on the oral combined contraceptive pill. Further assessment is ongoing. Physiological changes similar to those found with Omeprazole may take place due to reduced gastric acid, which could influence bacterial colonization of the stomach and duodenum and also vitamin B12 absorption.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Lanso 30 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Lanso 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Severe or irreversible adverse effects:</strong> Possible induction of carcinoid tumors due to profound acid suppression, and a rise in serum gastrin. Long-term treatment with a proton pump inhibitor in patients with Helicobacter pylori infection may accelerate the development of atrophic gastritis.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Symptomatic adverse effects:</strong>Dose-dependent diarrhea occurs with an incidence of about 4% at 30 mg per day, rising to 8% at 60 mg per day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Headache occurs in 2-3% of treated patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Lanso 30 mg should be avoided in pregnancy unless there are compelling reasons.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Gastric malignancy should be ruled out.</li><li>Hepatic impairment.</li><li>Pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at 25°C.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neonates:</strong> There is no relevant human data. The drug is not recommended for use in neonates.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> The youngest person to have received Lanso 30 mg in clinical trials was 13 years old.</li><li><strong>The Elderly:</strong> No problems have been encountered in clinical use, and there has been no increase in adverse drug reactions in the elderly.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Lanso 30 mg is a substituted benzimidazole, and is also known as a PPI due to its property to block the final step of acid secretion by inhibiting the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system in gastric parietal cells. Both basal and stimulated acid secretion are inhibited.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Lanso 30 mg should be avoided in pregnancy unless there are compelling reasons.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neonates:</strong> There is no relevant human data. The drug is not recommended for use in neonates.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> The youngest person to have received Lanso 30 mg in clinical trials was 13 years old.</li><li><strong>The Elderly:</strong> No problems have been encountered in clinical use, and there has been no increase in adverse drug reactions in the elderly.</li></ul>', 'active', '1391_1742500837.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:00:37', '2025-05-03 18:53:42'),
(3764, 'Flindof', 'Doxophylline', 6.00, 6, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Flindof 200 mg</strong></p><p>Flindof 200 mg is used to treat the following indications:</p><ul><li>Bronchial asthma</li><li>Bronchospasm</li><li>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</li><li>Pulmonary disease with a spastic bronchial component</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Flindof 200 mg is a novel bronchodilator that structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. It selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. However, unlike Theophylline, Flindof 200 mg appears to have decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, potentially accounting for its improved safety profile. Additionally, it is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Flindof 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 400 mg tablet two or three times daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li></ul><blockquote><strong>Children:</strong>12 years of age: 10 ml syrup or 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</blockquote><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><br></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years of age: 6-9 mg/kg body weight two times daily, i.e. if body weight is 10 kg, 3 ml (60 mg) two times daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Flindof 200 mg</strong></p><p>Flindof 200 mg should not be administered together with other xanthine derivatives. Toxic synergism with ephedrine has been documented for xanthines. Like other xanthines, concomitant therapy with troleandomycin, lincomycin, clindamycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, ranitidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccine may decrease the hepatic clearance of xanthines, leading to an increase in blood levels.</p><p>No evidence of a relationship between Flindof 200 mg serum concentrations and toxic events has been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Flindof 200 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Acute myocardial infarction</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Lactating women</li><li>Patients who have shown hypersensitivity to its components</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Flindof 200 mg</strong></p><p>Flindof 200 mg rarely causes serious side effects; however, possible side effects are similar to taking an excess amount of caffeine. These include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Headache</li><li>Upset stomach</li><li>Heartburn</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Animal reproduction studies indicate that Flindof 200 mg does not cause fetal harm when administered to pregnant animals or affect reproduction capacity. However, due to limited experience in pregnant humans, xanthines should only be given to pregnant women when clearly necessary. It is important to note that Flindof 200 mg is contraindicated in nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The half-life of xanthine derivatives is influenced by a number of known variables. It may be prolonged in patients with liver disease, congestive heart failure, and those taking certain other drugs like erythromycin, troleandomycin, lincomycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccine. In these cases, a lower dose of Flindof 200 mg may be needed. Phenytoin, other anticonvulsants, and smoking may cause an increase in clearance with a shorter mean half-life. In these cases, higher doses of Flindof 200 mg may be needed.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Flindof 200 mg</strong></p><p>In case of overdose, severe cardiac arrhythmias and tonic-clonic seizures may occur. These effects may represent the first signs of intoxication. The appearance of side effects may require discontinuation of treatment, which, if necessary, at the physician’s discretion, may be resumed at lower doses after all signs and symptoms of toxicity have subsided. As there is no specific antidote, in case of overdose, symptomatic treatment of cardiovascular collapse should be instituted.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children. Flindof 200 mg should be used only on the prescription of a specialist physician.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Bronchodilator</li><li>Methyl xanthine derivatives</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Flindof 200 mg is a novel bronchodilator. It structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. Flindof 200 mg selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, thereby relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. However, differently from Theophylline, Flindof 200 mg appears to have decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, which may account for its better safety profile. Flindof 200 mg is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p>', 'active', '1028_1742500950.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:02:30', '2025-05-03 08:07:17'),
(3765, 'Trilock', 'Montelukast Sodium', 17.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Trilock 10 mg</strong></p><p>Trilock 10 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. Cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) are located in the human airway, and these eicosanoids bind to them. The orally active drug Trilock 10 mg has a strong affinity and specificity for the CysLT1 receptor. Without any agonist activity, it inhibits LTD4\'s physiological effects at the CysLT1 receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Trilock 10 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Adults and adolescents with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</strong></p><ul><li>The dosage for adults and adolescents 15 years of age and older: Monas 10 mg tablet once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</strong></p><ul><li>The dosage for pediatric patients 6 to 14 years of age: Monas 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li>The dosage for pediatric patients 2 years to 5 years of age: Monas 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li>The dosage for pediatric patients 6 months to 5 years of age: Monas 4 mg oral granules once daily. This can be administered either directly in the mouth or mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in pediatric patients:</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Monas have been established in adequate and well-controlled studies in pediatric patients with asthma 6 months to 14 years of age. Safety and efficacy profiles in this age group are similar to those seen in adults.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency:</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is required in patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is recommended in patients with renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Elderly use:</strong></p><p>The pharmacokinetic profile and the oral bioavailability of a single 10-mg oral dose of Monas are similar in elderly and younger adults. The plasma half-life of Monas is slightly longer in the elderly. No dosage adjustment in the elderly is required.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Trilock 10 mg</strong></p><p>In the prevention and long-term treatment of asthma, Trilock 10 mg has been provided alongside other commonly used treatments without seeming to increase the risk of negative side effects. According to studies on drug interactions, theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives (norethindrone 1mg/ethinyl estradiol 35mcg), terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin did not exhibit any clinically significant effects on the pharmacokinetics at the recommended clinical dose of it. In clinical investigations, the medication was used concurrently with a variety of routinely prescribed medications without showing any signs of clinically harmful interactions, despite the fact that more detailed interaction studies were not carried out. Thyroid hormones, hypnotic sedatives, benzodiazepines, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and decongestants were among these drugs. Following a single dose of this solution, the hepatic metabolism-inducing drug phenobarbital reduced the drug\'s AUC by about 40%. For this medication, no dosage modification is advised. When powerful cytochrome P450 enzyme inducers, such as phenobarbital or rifampin, are provided alongside this solution, it is fair to use proper clinical monitoring.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those who have an extreme sensitivity to any ingredient in this medication should not take it.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Trilock 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon:</strong> akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, asthenia, aberrant behavior, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, hemorrhage, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, oedema, seizure, odd feeling, and sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, decreased attention, disorientation, erythema nodosum, hallucinations, hepatic problems, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, and tremor.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Trilock 10 mg is classified as pregnancy category B. Although it has been demonstrated that the medication crosses the placenta of pregnant rats and rabbits, there are no records of its use in pregnant humans. Although there is some evidence that it is also secreted in breast milk, little is known about the implications of this discovery. Before starting the therapy in nursing moms, caution should be exercised.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>The reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks, including status asthmaticus, is not recommended as usage for Trilock 10 mg. Patients should be told to have access to the proper rescue medication.</li><li>Asthma exacerbations that are acute can still be treated with the solution.</li><li>While the dosage of an inhaled corticosteroid may be gradually decreased under medical supervision, the use of this solution instead of an inhaled or oral corticosteroid should not be started suddenly.</li><li>Exercise-induced bronchospasm should not be treated and managed with a single dose of it.</li><li>While taking it, patients with documented aspirin sensitivity should continue to refrain from taking aspirin or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Although aspirin sensitivity in asthmatics is a known side effect of the drug, it has not been demonstrated to shorten the bronchoconstrictor response to aspirin and other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs).</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Trilock 10 mg</strong></p><p>The majority of overdose reports did not include any negative experiences. The most frequent side effects, which were in line with Trilock 10 mg\'s safety profile, were nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain. In the case of an overdose, it is permissible to take the customary supportive measures, such as removing any unabsorbed material from the digestive tract, using clinical monitoring, and if necessary, instituting supportive therapy.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry environment while keeping out light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>The cysteinyl leukotriene receptor is inhibited by the selective and orally active leukotriene receptor antagonist known as Trilock 10 mg (CysLT1). The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. The pathophysiology of asthma and allergic rhinitis, including airway edema, smooth muscle contraction, and altered cellular activity associated with the inflammatory process, have been linked to cysteinyl leukotrienes and leukotriene receptor occupation. These factors all contribute to the signs and symptoms of asthma.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>After oral administration, Trilock 10 mg passes the placenta in rats and rabbits. Nevertheless, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. Because the human reaction to medications is not usually predicted by research on animal reproduction, pregnant women should only use it if absolutely necessary. When giving the pill to a nursing mother, care should be taken because many medications are secreted in human milk.</p>', 'active', '540_1742501053.jpg', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:04:13', '2025-05-04 22:34:07'),
(3766, 'D-Balance', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20000 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of D-Balance 200000 IU/ml</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated in the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also used as an adjunct to specific therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Colecalciferol, the synthetic form of Vitamin D, is essential for normal bone growth and development and to maintain bone density. It is also necessary for the proper utilization of calcium and phosphorus. Babies need Vitamin D3 for healthy growth and development. It acts as a hormone.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong> Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. Vitamin D3 is essential for normal bone growth and to maintain bone density. It also reduces the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, helps prevent certain cancers (breast, colorectal), and aids in maintaining adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of D-Balance 200000 IU/ml</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>For Capsules:Adults:Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks. Maintenance therapy is 1,400–2,000 IU/day. To confirm the target level of 25-hydroxyvitamin D, measurement should be done 3-4 months after initiating maintenance therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 20,000 IU every 4 weeks. Higher doses may be required in certain situations.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Addition to specific therapy for osteoporosis:</strong> 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Children (12-18 years):Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 20,000 IU once every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Film-coated tablets:</strong>1,000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily, or as directed by the physician. Take the medicine with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Oroflash or Chewable Tablets:</strong>1,000–2,000 IU daily, or as directed by the physician. Take the medicine with food or within 1 hour after a meal. Place the tablet in the mouth, chew, then swallow.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\"><strong>For Syrup:For patients at risk of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong>0–1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml)</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-2\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml)</blockquote><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-3\"><strong>For Vitamin D3 deficient patients:</strong>0–1 year: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1–18 years: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\"><strong>For Injection:Prevention:</strong>Infants receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk: ½ ampoule (0.5ml) i.e. 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Nursed infants or infants not receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk or young children up to 5 years of age: 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e. 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Adolescents: 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e. 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Pregnancy: ½ ampoule (0.5ml) i.e. 100,000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Elderly: ½ ampoule (0.5ml) i.e. 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Digestive disorders, concomitant treatment with antiepileptics &amp; other special conditions: ½ ampoule (0.5ml) i.e. 100,000 IU or 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e. 200,000 IU every 3 or 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Vitamin D deficiency:</strong> 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e. 200,000 IU, which can be repeated 1 to 6 months later or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of D-Balance 200000 IU/ml:</strong> It may interfere with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (such as liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong> It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Side Effects of D-Balance 200000 IU/ml:</strong> The general side effects include hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, and abdominal pain.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Studies have shown that doses up to 4,000 IU during pregnancy are safe.</li><li>The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU. However, in women who are Vitamin D3 deficient, a higher dose may be required. During pregnancy, women should follow the advice of their medical practitioner.</li><li>Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. Overdose in infants from nursing mothers has not been observed; however, the dose of any additional Vitamin D3 given to a breastfed child should be carefully considered by the practitioner.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong> It should be used with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of D-Balance 200000 IU/ml:</strong> Overdose may lead to hypervitaminosis D.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong> Keep below 30º C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong> The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong> Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. Vitamin D3 is essential for normal bone growth and to maintain bone density. It also reduces the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, helps prevent certain cancers (breast, colorectal), and helps maintain adequate insulin levels for type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses:</strong> The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</p>', 'active', '715_1742501145.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:05:45', '2025-05-03 01:44:22'),
(3767, 'Rocal-M Vita', 'Calcium + Vitamin D3 + Multimineral', 20.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', 'N/A', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rocal-M Vita:</strong></p><ul><li>Prevention and treatment of osteoporosis</li><li>To maintain strong bone growth</li><li>For proper functioning of the heart, muscles, and nerves</li><li>As a nutritional supplement</li><li>For bone development and regeneration</li><li>During pregnancy &amp; lactation</li><li>Deficiency of Calcium, Vitamin D3, Magnesium, Zinc, Copper, Manganese, and Boron</li></ul><p><strong>Composition:</strong> Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate BP equivalent to 600 mg elemental Calcium</li><li>Vitamin D3 BP (as Cholecalciferol) 200 IU</li><li>Magnesium Oxide BP equivalent to 40 mg Magnesium</li><li>Manganese Sulfate Monohydrate BP equivalent to 1.8 mg Manganese</li><li>Cupric Oxide equivalent to 1 mg Copper</li><li>Boron Citrate equivalent to 250 mcg Boron</li><li>Zinc Oxide BP equivalent to 7.5 mg Zinc</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong> Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Essential for preventing osteoporosis and other bone-related diseases.</li><li>Calcium, Magnesium, and Vitamin D3 are key nutrients for bone health, with Vitamin D3 helping in calcium absorption.</li><li>Magnesium helps increase bone strength and rigidity.</li><li>Copper, Manganese, Zinc, and Boron play an important role in bone health, and their deficiencies are linked to osteoporosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>2 tablets per day: 1 tablet in the morning and 1 in the evening.</li><li>Take with or just after meals with a full glass of water.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction:</strong></p><ul><li>Risk of hypercalcemia with thiazide diuretics (can reduce calcium excretion).</li><li>Avoid hypocalcemia in digitalized patients.</li><li>Certain foods (e.g., oxalic acid, phosphate, phytinic acid) can reduce calcium absorption.</li><li>Interaction with phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids (may reduce Vitamin D3 efficacy).</li><li>Avoid concurrent use of calcium with thyroxin, bisphosphonates, fluoride, quinolones, tetracycline antibiotics, or iron (space out by at least 4 hours).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Absolute: Hypercalcemia due to myeloma, bone metastasis, sarcoidosis, primary hyperparathyroidism, Vitamin D3 overdose, severe renal failure, hypersensitivity to any ingredient.</li><li>Relative: Osteoporosis from prolonged immobilization, renal stones, severe hypercalciuria.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Mild gastrointestinal disturbances (constipation, flatulence, nausea, gastric pain, diarrhea).</li><li>Occasional skin rash from Vitamin D3.</li><li>Long-term high-dose use may lead to hypercalciuria or, rarely, hypercalcemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Should be used under medical supervision during pregnancy and breastfeeding, as calcium and Vitamin D3 requirements increase.</li><li>Overdosing of Vitamin D3 in pregnancy has teratogenic effects and can lead to mental/physical retardation or other complications in newborns.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Close supervision in patients with mild to moderate renal failure or hypercalciuria.</li><li>Regular checks for plasma calcium levels and urinary calcium excretion are required in such cases.</li><li>Periodic monitoring for kidney function is necessary with long-term use.</li><li>Separate calcium and iron supplementation by at least 4 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms include nausea, vomiting, dry mouth, drowsiness, loss of appetite, metallic taste, stomach cramps, diarrhea, headache, and constipation.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Nutrition plays a vital role in preventing osteoporosis and bone-related diseases.</li><li>Calcium, Magnesium, and Vitamin D3 are essential for bone health, with Vitamin D3 facilitating calcium absorption.</li><li>Micronutrients like Copper, Manganese, Zinc, and Boron contribute to bone strength, and their deficiency is often linked to osteoporosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><ul><li>Requires physician guidance during pregnancy and lactation, as calcium and Vitamin D3 needs increase. Ensure allowance for supplements from other sources.</li><li>Long-term hypercalcemia in pregnancy can cause significant health issues for the newborn.</li></ul><p>This preparation helps address nutritional gaps and support bone health, particularly in patients with osteoporosis, during pregnancy, or those deficient in essential minerals and vitamins.</p>', 'active', '1409_1742501334.jpg', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:08:54', '2025-05-06 08:30:23'),
(3768, 'Rocal-M Vita', 'Calcium + Vitamin D3 + Multimineral', 20.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', 'mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rocal-M Vita:</strong></p><ul><li>Prevention and treatment of osteoporosis</li><li>To maintain strong bone growth</li><li>For proper functioning of the heart, muscles, and nerves</li><li>As a nutritional supplement</li><li>For bone development and regeneration</li><li>During pregnancy &amp; lactation</li><li>Deficiency of Calcium, Vitamin D3, Magnesium, Zinc, Copper, Manganese, and Boron</li></ul><p><strong>Composition:</strong> Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate BP equivalent to 600 mg elemental Calcium</li><li>Vitamin D3 BP (as Cholecalciferol) 200 IU</li><li>Magnesium Oxide BP equivalent to 40 mg Magnesium</li><li>Manganese Sulfate Monohydrate BP equivalent to 1.8 mg Manganese</li><li>Cupric Oxide equivalent to 1 mg Copper</li><li>Boron Citrate equivalent to 250 mcg Boron</li><li>Zinc Oxide BP equivalent to 7.5 mg Zinc</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong> Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Essential for preventing osteoporosis and other bone-related diseases.</li><li>Calcium, Magnesium, and Vitamin D3 are key nutrients for bone health, with Vitamin D3 helping in calcium absorption.</li><li>Magnesium helps increase bone strength and rigidity.</li><li>Copper, Manganese, Zinc, and Boron play an important role in bone health, and their deficiencies are linked to osteoporosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>2 tablets per day: 1 tablet in the morning and 1 in the evening.</li><li>Take with or just after meals with a full glass of water.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction:</strong></p><ul><li>Risk of hypercalcemia with thiazide diuretics (can reduce calcium excretion).</li><li>Avoid hypocalcemia in digitalized patients.</li><li>Certain foods (e.g., oxalic acid, phosphate, phytinic acid) can reduce calcium absorption.</li><li>Interaction with phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids (may reduce Vitamin D3 efficacy).</li><li>Avoid concurrent use of calcium with thyroxin, bisphosphonates, fluoride, quinolones, tetracycline antibiotics, or iron (space out by at least 4 hours).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Absolute: Hypercalcemia due to myeloma, bone metastasis, sarcoidosis, primary hyperparathyroidism, Vitamin D3 overdose, severe renal failure, hypersensitivity to any ingredient.</li><li>Relative: Osteoporosis from prolonged immobilization, renal stones, severe hypercalciuria.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Mild gastrointestinal disturbances (constipation, flatulence, nausea, gastric pain, diarrhea).</li><li>Occasional skin rash from Vitamin D3.</li><li>Long-term high-dose use may lead to hypercalciuria or, rarely, hypercalcemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Should be used under medical supervision during pregnancy and breastfeeding, as calcium and Vitamin D3 requirements increase.</li><li>Overdosing of Vitamin D3 in pregnancy has teratogenic effects and can lead to mental/physical retardation or other complications in newborns.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Close supervision in patients with mild to moderate renal failure or hypercalciuria.</li><li>Regular checks for plasma calcium levels and urinary calcium excretion are required in such cases.</li><li>Periodic monitoring for kidney function is necessary with long-term use.</li><li>Separate calcium and iron supplementation by at least 4 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms include nausea, vomiting, dry mouth, drowsiness, loss of appetite, metallic taste, stomach cramps, diarrhea, headache, and constipation.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Nutrition plays a vital role in preventing osteoporosis and bone-related diseases.</li><li>Calcium, Magnesium, and Vitamin D3 are essential for bone health, with Vitamin D3 facilitating calcium absorption.</li><li>Micronutrients like Copper, Manganese, Zinc, and Boron contribute to bone strength, and their deficiency is often linked to osteoporosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><ul><li>Requires physician guidance during pregnancy and lactation, as calcium and Vitamin D3 needs increase. Ensure allowance for supplements from other sources.</li><li>Long-term hypercalcemia in pregnancy can cause significant health issues for the newborn.</li></ul><p>This preparation helps address nutritional gaps and support bone health, particularly in patients with osteoporosis, during pregnancy, or those deficient in essential minerals and vitamins.</p>', 'in-active', '1181_1742501338.jpg', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:08:58', '2025-05-12 18:12:51'),
(3769, 'Feofol-Cl', 'Carbonyl Iron + Folic Acid', 2.50, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50 mg plus 0.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Feofol-Cl 50 mg + 0.5 mg</strong></p><p>This capsule is used for the treatment, control, prevention, and improvement of the following diseases, conditions, and symptoms:</p><ul><li>Iron deficiency</li><li>Treatment of megaloblastic anemias due to a deficiency of folic acid</li><li>Treatment of anemias of nutritional origin, pregnancy, infancy, or childhood</li><li>This may also be used for purposes not listed here.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Iron &amp; Vitamin Combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This capsule is used for iron deficiency, treatment of megaloblastic anemias due to a deficiency of folic acid, and treatment of anemias of nutritional origin, pregnancy, infancy, or childhood, and other conditions. This capsule may also be used for purposes not listed in this medication guide. It contains Carbonyl Iron and Folic Acid as active ingredients. It works by producing red blood cells and transporting oxygen in the body, acting on megaloblastic bone marrow to produce normoblastic marrow.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Feofol-Cl 50 mg + 0.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>One capsule daily. In more severe cases, 2 capsules a day may be required, or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Feofol-Cl 50 mg + 0.5 mg</strong></p><p>If you use other drugs or over-the-counter products at the same time, the effects of this capsule may change. This may increase your risk for side effects or cause the drug not to work properly. Inform your doctor about all the drugs, vitamins, and herbal supplements you are using, so they can help you prevent or manage drug interactions.</p><p>It may interact with the following drugs and products:</p><ul><li>Alcohol</li><li>Antibiotics</li><li>Barbiturates</li><li>Bisphosphonates</li><li>Diphenylhydantoin</li><li>Levodopa</li><li>Levothyroxine</li><li>Methotrexate</li><li>Methyldopa</li><li>Nitrofurantoin</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to this capsule</li><li>This capsule should not be used if you have the following conditions:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypersensitivity</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Intolerance to the drug</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Feofol-Cl 50 mg + 0.5 mg</strong></p><p>The following is a list of possible side effects that may occur from all constituting ingredients of this capsule. This is not a comprehensive list, and some side effects may be rare but serious. Consult your doctor if you observe any of the following, especially if they do not go away:</p><ul><li>Constipation</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Upset stomach</li><li>Allergic reactions</li><li>Anorexia</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Abdominal distention</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Bitter or bad taste</li><li>Altered sleep patterns</li><li>Difficulty concentrating</li><li>Irritability</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Before using this capsule, inform your doctor about your current list of medications, over-the-counter products (e.g., vitamins, herbal supplements, etc.), allergies, pre-existing diseases, and current health conditions (e.g., pregnancy, upcoming surgery, etc.). Some health conditions may make you more susceptible to the side effects of the drug.</p><ul><li>Take as directed by your doctor or follow the directions printed on the product insert. Dosage is based on your condition.</li><li>Tell your doctor if your condition persists or worsens.</li><li>Important counseling points:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Administration of folic acid alone is improper therapy for pernicious anemia and other megaloblastic anemias in which vitamin B12 is deficient.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Avoid alcohol consumption, especially if having alcoholic cirrhosis.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Avoid taking antacids within 2 hours while taking this medicine.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not take this medicine if you have excessive iron buildup.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Folic acid in doses above 0.1 mg daily may obscure the diagnosis of pernicious anemia.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pregnant, planning to become pregnant, or breastfeeding: Risk of obscuring the diagnosis of pernicious anemia.</li><li>Take this medicine without a meal.</li><li>Vitamin B12 is used to treat pernicious anemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store medicines at room temperature, away from heat and direct light. Do not freeze medicines unless required by the package insert. Keep medicines away from children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Iron &amp; Vitamin Combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This capsule works by producing red blood cells, transporting oxygen in the body, and acting on megaloblastic bone marrow to produce normoblastic marrow. It is used for iron deficiency, treatment of megaloblastic anemias due to a deficiency of folic acid, treatment of anemias of nutritional origin, pregnancy, infancy, or childhood, and other conditions.</p>', 'active', '206_1742501477.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:11:17', '2025-05-03 10:35:05'),
(3770, 'Ebasten', 'Ebastine', 9.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a formatted version of the information for <strong>Ebasten 10 mg</strong> in a clear, bold-text style for easy reading:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Ebasten 10 mg</strong></p><p>Ebasten 10 mg is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of:</p><ul><li><strong>Seasonal and Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</strong></li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Non-sedating antihistamines</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Ebasten 10 mg, a piperidine derivative, is a long-acting, nonsedating, second-generation histamine receptor antagonist that binds preferentially to peripheral H1 receptors. It is metabolised to the active metabolite, carEbasten 10 mg. It has antihistaminic and antiallergic activity and prevents histamine-induced bronchoconstriction. It does not have significant sedative or antimuscarinic actions.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Ebasten 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults (more than 12 years of age): <strong>10 mg</strong> (one tablet) once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (6-12 years of age): <strong>5 mg</strong> (half tablet) once daily</li><li><strong>Syrup</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (2-5 years of age): <strong>2.5 ml</strong> once daily (up to <strong>5 ml</strong> in severe cases such as Perennial Allergic Rhinitis)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (6-12 years of age): <strong>5 ml</strong> once daily (up to <strong>10 ml</strong> in severe cases such as Perennial Allergic Rhinitis)</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Ebasten 10 mg</strong></p><p>Ebasten 10 mg may be taken <strong>with or without food</strong>.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Ebasten 10 mg</strong></p><p>Ebasten 10 mg in combination with either <strong>ketoconazole</strong> or <strong>erythromycin</strong> increases the plasma level of Ebasten 10 mg and prolongs the QTc interval.</p><p>Ebasten 10 mg does not interact with the pharmacokinetics of <strong>theophylline</strong>, <strong>warfarin</strong>, <strong>cimetidine</strong>, <strong>diazepam</strong>, or <strong>alcohol</strong>. However, the sedation effect of alcohol and diazepam may be enhanced.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with a known hypersensitivity to Ebasten 10 mg or any of its ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ebasten 10 mg</strong></p><p>The most common side effects are:</p><ul><li><strong>Headache</strong></li><li><strong>Dry mouth</strong></li><li><strong>Drowsiness</strong></li></ul><p>Less commonly reported side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Abdominal pain</strong></li><li><strong>Dyspepsia</strong></li><li><strong>Nausea</strong></li><li><strong>Insomnia</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The safety of Ebasten 10 mg during pregnancy and lactation has not been established.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Should only be used if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether Ebasten 10 mg is excreted in breast milk; therefore, it should not be used during lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Exercise caution when using Ebasten 10 mg in patients known to have:</p><ul><li><strong>Long QT syndrome</strong></li><li><strong>Hypokalaemia</strong></li><li><strong>Treatment with any medicine known to produce an increase in QT interval</strong> or inhibit <strong>CYP3A4 enzyme systems</strong>, such as azole antifungals and macrolide antibiotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ebasten 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No clinically meaningful signs or symptoms were observed up to <strong>100 mg</strong> given once daily.</li><li>There is no specific antidote for Ebasten 10 mg.</li><li>In case of accidental overdose, <strong>gastric lavage</strong>, monitoring of vital functions (including ECG), and <strong>symptomatic treatment</strong> should be carried out.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong> in a cool and dry place, away from light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Non-sedating antihistamines</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Ebasten 10 mg is a long-acting and selective <strong>H1-histamine receptor antagonist</strong>. After repeated administration, inhibition of peripheral receptors remains at a constant level. Ebasten 10 mg is rapidly absorbed and undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism following oral administration. It is almost totally converted to the pharmacologically active acid metabolite, <strong>carEbasten 10 mg</strong>.</p><p>This should give you a clearer understanding of the product! Let me know if you need any more details or explanations!</p>', 'active', '851_1742501584.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:13:04', '2025-05-02 09:05:30');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3771, 'Fexofast', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 9.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '120 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Fexofast 120 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Allergic Rhinitis:</strong>In adults and children 12 years of age and older, Fexofast 120 mg is used to alleviate symptoms of seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis such as sneezing, rhinorrhea, lacrimation, itchy/red eyes, itchy nose, palate, and throat. It enhances work/activity productivity and improves health-related quality of life.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria:</strong>For the treatment of chronic idiopathic urticaria in adults and children 12 years and older, Fexofast 120 mg reduces the number of wheals and pruritus. It also improves work/activity productivity and health-related quality of life.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Fexofast 120 mg is a selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist. After oral administration, it is quickly absorbed, reaching peak plasma concentration in 2-3 hours. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Seasonal Allergic Rhinitis:Adults and children 12 years and older:</strong> 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6-11 years:</strong> 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 2-11 years (suspension):</strong> 30 mg (5 ml) twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria:Adults and children 12 years and older:</strong> 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6-11 years:</strong> 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6 months to less than 2 years (suspension):</strong> 15 mg (2.5 ml) twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 2-11 years (suspension):</strong> 30 mg (5 ml) twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Fexofast 120 mg has minimal hepatic metabolism, reducing the likelihood of interactions with other drugs processed by the liver.</li><li>Co-administration with erythromycin or ketoconazole increases fexofenadine plasma levels by 2-3 times. However, no significant QT interval changes or adverse reactions were observed.</li><li>The absorption of Fexofast 120 mg is reduced when taken with antacids containing aluminum and magnesium hydroxide. It is recommended to wait 2 hours between taking Fexofast 120 mg and these antacids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Fexofast 120 mg should not be used in individuals with known hypersensitivity to Fexofast or its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Headaches, fatigue, vertigo, nausea, and issues at the administration site.</li><li><strong>Uncommon:</strong> Exhaustion, hypersensitivity reactions (e.g., angioedema, tight chest, dyspnea), anxiety, palpitations, tachycardia, rash, urticaria, and pruritus.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category C by the FDA. It should only be used if the benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Fexofenadine is excreted in breast milk and is not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly patients</strong> and those with <strong>renal or hepatic impairment</strong> should use Fexofast 120 mg with caution.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular risk:</strong> Antihistamines can cause tachycardia and palpitations, so caution is advised for patients with cardiovascular conditions.</li><li>Fexofenadine has minimal effects on driving or operating machinery, but individual responses should be monitored.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Overdoses may cause dry mouth, sleepiness, fatigue, and dizziness. In trials, high doses (up to 800 mg) were tolerated without significant adverse effects. Standard procedures should be followed for treating overdose, but hemodialysis is not effective in removing fexofenadine.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Fexofenadine is an inverse agonist of the H1 histamine receptor, preventing allergic responses by stabilizing the receptor in its inactive state, reducing downstream effects like pruritus, rhinorrhea, and watery eyes.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Dose adjustments may be needed for patients with impaired renal function.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Pharmacokinetics change with age, but side effects remain similar to younger adults.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need any further information or clarification!</p>', 'active', '1980_1742501702.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:15:02', '2025-05-02 17:54:49'),
(3772, 'Santogen', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral [A-Z gold preparation]', 11.00, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', 'pod', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Santogen</strong></p><p>Santogen is indicated for preventing and treating deficiencies in vitamins and minerals. This comprehensive daily nutritional supplement is also recommended to meet the heightened requirements for vitamins and minerals in various situations, including physical and emotional stress, chronic diseases, infectious illnesses, osteoporosis, injuries or wounds, surgeries, poor digestion, old age, pregnancy and lactation, decreased appetite, excessive dieting, exposure to environmental pollution, and strenuous exercise, among others.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each tablet of Santogen contains the following essential nutrients:</p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin A</strong>: 5000 IU</li><li><strong>Vitamin C</strong>: 60 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin D</strong>: 400 IU</li><li><strong>Vitamin E</strong>: 30 IU</li><li><strong>Vitamin K</strong>: 25 mcg</li><li><strong>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine)</strong>: 1.5 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin)</strong>: 1.7 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin B3 (Niacin)</strong>: 20 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine)</strong>: 2 mg</li><li><strong>Folic acid</strong>: 400 mcg</li><li><strong>Biotin</strong>: 30 mcg</li><li><strong>Pantothenate</strong>: 10 mg</li><li><strong>Calcium</strong>: 162 mg</li><li><strong>Iron</strong>: 18 mg</li><li><strong>Phosphorus</strong>: 109 mg</li><li><strong>Iodine</strong>: 150 mcg</li><li><strong>Magnesium</strong>: 100 mg</li><li><strong>Zinc</strong>: 15 mg</li><li><strong>Selenium</strong>: 20 mcg</li><li><strong>Copper</strong>: 2 mg</li><li><strong>Manganese</strong>: 2 mg</li><li><strong>Chromium</strong>: 120 mcg</li><li><strong>Molybdenum</strong>: 75 mcg</li><li><strong>Chloride</strong>: 72 mg</li><li><strong>Potassium</strong>: 80 mg</li><li><strong>Boron</strong>: 150 mcg</li><li><strong>Nickel</strong>: 5 mcg</li><li><strong>Silicon</strong>: 2 mg</li><li><strong>Tin</strong>: 10 mcg</li><li><strong>Vanadium</strong>: 10 mcg</li><li><strong>Lutein</strong>: 250 mcg</li><li><strong>Cyanocobalamin</strong>: 6 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Multivitamin + Multimineral A-Z Gold Preparation is a supplement that combines most vitamins and minerals in a single tablet. This preparation typically contains 32 high-potency vitamins and minerals. It maintains a healthy body and an active lifestyle by providing essential nutrients that may be lacking in one\'s diet.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Santogen</strong></p><p>The general dosage for adults is one tablet daily. The tablet can be taken with or without food. If you are pregnant or lactating, you may need to take a higher dosage of Santogen.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Santogen</strong></p><p>Santogen is generally considered safe and has no known drug interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Santogen is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Santogen</strong></p><p>In most cases, this preparation is well tolerated. However, there may be occasional occurrences of diarrhea while undergoing beta-carotene treatment, and it\'s possible for the skin to take on a mild yellowish tint. Vitamin C and vitamin E may lead to diarrhea and other gastrointestinal disturbances.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Santogen is generally safe for pregnant and lactating women, but it is important to speak to your doctor before taking any supplement during pregnancy or lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Long-term intake of high levels of vitamin A (excluding that sourced from beta carotene) may increase the risk of osteoporosis in postmenopausal women.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>This is a film-coated tablet, which combines 32 high potency vitamins and minerals. This preparation maintains a healthy body and active lifestyle.</p>', 'active', '571_1742501874.webp', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:17:54', '2025-05-04 20:49:40'),
(3773, 'Neubin', 'Vitamin B1, B6 & B12', 9.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg plus 200 mg plus 200 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Neubin</strong></p><p>Neubin is indicated for the treatment of vitamin B1, B6 &amp; B12 deficiency syndrome. It is also indicated for the supportive treatment of neuritis &amp; non-inflammatory diseases of the nerves, such as:</p><ul><li>Diabetic neuropathy</li><li>Peripheral neuralgia</li><li>Lumbago</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Optic neuritis</li><li>Sciatica</li><li>Facial neuralgia</li><li>Intercostal neuralgia</li><li>Spinal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate) 100 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride) 200 mg</li><li>Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) 200 mcg</li></ul><p>Each 3 ml ampoule contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) 100 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) 100 mg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) 1000 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Neubin is a combined preparation of Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate), Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride), and Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin). These vitamins play an essential role as co-enzymes in the metabolism of the nervous system. They support the regeneration of nerve fibers and myelin sheaths by activating nerve cell metabolism and the natural repair mechanisms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Neubin</strong></p><p><strong>Tablets:</strong> 1 to 3 tablets per day or as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Injection:</strong> Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly (deep intragluteal).</p><ul><li>In severe cases: 1 ampoule daily until the acute symptoms subside.</li><li>For milder cases and follow-up therapy: 2 to 3 ampoules per week.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Neubin</strong></p><p><strong>Tablet:</strong> 1-3 Tablets per day or as advised by the physician.</p><p><strong>Injection:</strong></p><ul><li>In severe (acute) cases: 1 injection daily until the acute symptoms subside or as advised by the physician.</li><li>In mild cases: 1 injection 2-3 times per week.</li><li>Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly.</li><li><strong>Use in children:</strong> There is no information on the use of this drug in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Neubin</strong></p><p>No drug interaction has been reported yet.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vitamin B1, Vitamin B6, and Vitamin B12 are contraindicated in patients on levodopa therapy and in patients with hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of the preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Neubin</strong></p><p>Generally well tolerated, but allergic reactions may be observed in a few cases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>No risks have become known associated with the use of this combination during pregnancy at the recommended dosage. Vitamin B1, B6 &amp; B12 are secreted into breast milk, but risks of overdose for the infant are not known.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Cyanocobalamin should not be given to patients with subacute degeneration of the spinal cord. Cyanocobalamin is not a suitable form of vitamin B12 for the treatment of optic neuropathies associated with raised plasma concentrations of cyanocobalamin.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Neubin</strong></p><p>No overdosage symptoms are expected at the recommended dosage. If overdose is known, treatment should be symptomatic &amp; supportive.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep out of reach of children. Store in a cool (below 25°C temperature) and dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin B1</strong> converts carbohydrates, fatty acids, and amino acids into energy, promotes healthy nerves, improves mood, and strengthens the heart.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6</strong> forms RBCs, helps cells to make proteins, manufactures neurotransmitters (e.g., serotonin), releases stored forms of energy, helps prevent cardiovascular diseases and stroke, helps lift depression, and eases insomnia.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12</strong> is essential for cell replication and RBC production, prevents anemia, helps prevent depression, reduces nerve pain, numbness, tingling, and lowers the risk of heart diseases.</li></ul><p>The vitamin ingredients are absorbed well through oral administration and are widely distributed to most tissues, appearing in breast milk. Thiamine is not stored in the body and excess amounts are excreted in the urine as unchanged thiamine or metabolites. Pyridoxine, pyridoxal, and pyridoxamine are readily absorbed from the GIT following oral administration and are converted to active forms in the liver, where they are oxidized and excreted in the urine.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Oral tablet form is recommended. However, due to the presence of benzyl alcohol, the injection form is not recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</p>', 'active', '229_1742502100.webp', 27, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:21:40', '2025-05-04 00:36:43'),
(3774, 'Atoz Premium', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral [A-Z gold preparation]', 13.00, 9, 'tablet', '9 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0 mg/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Atoz Premium</strong></p><p>Atoz Premium is indicated for preventing and treating deficiencies in vitamins and minerals. This comprehensive daily nutritional supplement is also recommended to meet the heightened requirements for vitamins and minerals in various situations, including physical and emotional stress, chronic diseases, infectious illnesses, osteoporosis, injuries or wounds, surgeries, poor digestion, old age, pregnancy and lactation, decreased appetite, excessive dieting, exposure to environmental pollution, and strenuous exercise, among others.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each tablet of Atoz Premium contains the following essential nutrients:</p><ul><li>Vitamin A: 5000 IU</li><li>Vitamin C: 60 mg</li><li>Vitamin D: 400 IU</li><li>Vitamin E: 30 IU</li><li>Vitamin K: 25 mcg</li><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine): 1.5 mg</li><li>Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin): 1.7 mg</li><li>Vitamin B3 (Niacin): 20 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine): 2 mg</li><li>Folic acid: 400 mcg</li><li>Biotin: 30 mcg</li><li>Pantothenate: 10 mg</li><li>Calcium: 162 mg</li><li>Iron: 18 mg</li><li>Phosphorus: 109 mg</li><li>Iodine: 150 mcg</li><li>Magnesium: 100 mg</li><li>Zinc: 15 mg</li><li>Selenium: 20 mcg</li><li>Copper: 2 mg</li><li>Manganese: 2 mg</li><li>Chromium: 120 mcg</li><li>Molybdenum: 75 mcg</li><li>Chloride: 72 mg</li><li>Potassium: 80 mg</li><li>Boron: 150 mcg</li><li>Nickel: 5 mcg</li><li>Silicon: 2 mg</li><li>Tin: 10 mcg</li><li>Vanadium: 10 mcg</li><li>Lutein: 250 mcg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin: 6 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Multivitamin + Multimineral A-Z Gold Preparation is a supplement that combines most vitamins and minerals in a single tablet. This preparation typically contains 32 high-potency vitamins and minerals. It maintains a healthy body and an active lifestyle by providing essential nutrients that may be lacking in one\'s diet.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Atoz Premium</strong></p><p>The general dosage for adults is one tablet daily. The tablet can be taken with or without food. If you are pregnant or lactating, you may need to take a higher dosage of Atoz Premium.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Atoz Premium</strong></p><p>Atoz Premium is generally considered safe and has no known drug interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Atoz Premium is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Atoz Premium</strong></p><p>In most cases, this preparation is well tolerated. However, there may be occasional occurrences of diarrhea while undergoing beta-carotene treatment, and it\'s possible for the skin to take on a mild yellowish tint. Vitamin C and vitamin E may lead to diarrhea and other gastrointestinal disturbances.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Atoz Premium is generally safe for pregnant and lactating women, but it is important to speak to your doctor before taking any supplement during pregnancy or lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Long-term intake of high levels of vitamin A (excluding that sourced from beta carotene) may increase the risk of osteoporosis in postmenopausal women.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This is a film-coated tablet, which combines 32 high-potency vitamins and minerals. This preparation maintains a healthy body and an active lifestyle.</p>', 'active', '1630_1742502619.webp', 10, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:30:19', '2025-05-03 00:05:16'),
(3775, 'EC Plus', 'Vitamin C + Vitamin E', 6.02, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', 'pot', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of EC Plus</strong></p><ul><li>Keratosis</li><li>Rough skin</li><li>Wrinkles associated with aging</li><li>Alzheimer\'s disease</li><li>Cancer prevention</li><li>Dementia</li><li>Degenerative diseases</li><li>Coronary heart diseases</li><li>End stage renal disease</li><li>Growth and repair of body tissue, bone, skin, teeth, and hair</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Anti-oxidant Multivitamin preparations</li><li>Specific combined vitamin preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin C:</strong> Necessary for collagen formation and tissue repair; plays a role in oxidation/reduction reactions as well as other metabolic pathways including synthesis of catecholamines, carnitine, and steroids; also plays a role in conversion of folic acid to folinic acid.</li><li><strong>Vitamin E:</strong> Plays a role in protecting red blood cells against hemolysis; has protective effects against free radicals on polyunsaturated fatty acids found in cell membranes; plays a role in preventing oxidation of vitamin A and C.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of EC Plus</strong></p><ul><li>One tablet or capsule daily or as directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of EC Plus</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin C:</strong> Deferroxamine, hormonal contraceptives, flufenazine, warfarin, elemental iron, salicylates, fluphenazine, disulfiram, mexiletine, vitamin B12.</li><li><strong>Vitamin E:</strong> Colestyramine, colestipol, and orlistat may interfere with vitamin E absorption. High doses of vitamin E potentiate the anticoagulant action of warfarin. Large doses of vitamin E may impair response to iron supplementation.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with a known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of EC Plus</strong></p><ul><li>Generally, this preparation is well-tolerated.</li><li>Diarrhea may occasionally occur during treatment with beta carotene, and the skin may assume a slightly yellow discoloration. The side effects of vitamin A are reversible.</li><li>Vitamin C and vitamin E may cause diarrhea and other gastrointestinal disturbances.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category A.</strong> Adequate and well-controlled human studies have failed to demonstrate a risk to the fetus in the first trimester of pregnancy (and there is no evidence of risk in later trimesters).</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Hypervitaminosis.</li><li>Avoid use in early pregnancy.</li><li>Patients on anticoagulant therapy should not use ascorbic acid for a prolonged period of time.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Anti-oxidant Multivitamin preparations</li><li>Specific combined vitamin preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin C:</strong> Necessary for collagen formation and tissue repair; plays a role in oxidation/reduction reactions as well as other metabolic pathways including synthesis of catecholamines, carnitine, and steroids; also plays a role in conversion of folic acid to folinic acid.</li><li><strong>Vitamin E:</strong> Plays a role in protecting red blood cells against hemolysis; has protective effects against free radicals on polyunsaturated fatty acids found in cell membranes; plays a role in preventing oxidation of vitamin A and C.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category A.</strong> Adequate and well-controlled human studies have failed to demonstrate a risk to the fetus in the first trimester of pregnancy (and there is no evidence of risk in later trimesters).</li></ul>', 'active', '993_1742502871.jpg', 43, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:34:31', '2025-05-03 10:02:07'),
(3776, 'Irobest', 'Iron Polymaltose Complex + Folic Acid + Zinc Sulfate', 3.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '47 mg plus 0.5 mg plus 22.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Irobest</strong></p><p>This tablet is indicated for the treatment and prevention of Iron, Folic Acid, and Zinc deficiencies.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex INN 188 mg equivalent to 47 mg elemental Iron</li><li>Folic Acid BP 0.50 mg</li><li>Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate USP 61.80 mg equivalent to 22.50 mg elemental Zinc.</li></ul><p>Or each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex INN 192 mg equivalent to 48 mg elemental Iron</li><li>Folic Acid BP 0.5 mg</li><li>Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate USP 61.8 mg equivalent to 22.5 mg elemental Zinc.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined preparation</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This tablet is a preparation of Iron, Folic Acid, and Zinc. In this preparation, Iron is present as Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex, a noble Iron preparation. It contains non-ionic Ferric Iron and Polymaltose in a stable complex. This complex facilitates controlled absorption of Iron when it comes in contact with the mucosal cell surface. Due to its non-ionic nature, Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex is more stable than conventional Iron forms. Folic Acid aids in the proper development of the fetus, while Zinc helps enzymes function and metabolize proteins.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Irobest</strong></p><p>One tablet daily. Two tablets may be required a day in severe cases or as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Irobest</strong></p><p>Since Iron is complex bound, ionic interaction with foodstuff components (phytates, oxalates, tannin, etc.) and concomitant administration of medications (tetracyclines, antacids) are unlikely to occur.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>This tablet is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Irobest</strong></p><p>This tablet is generally well tolerated. However, a few side effects of oral Iron preparations, including nausea, vomiting, constipation, or diarrhea, may occur rarely.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Use of any drug during the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided if possible. Thus, the administration of Iron in the first trimester requires definite evidence of Iron deficiency.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>As with all Iron preparations, dark coloration of the stool may occur, which is without clinical significance.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Irobest</strong></p><p>No intoxication or Iron overload has been reported in cases of overdose.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a cool &amp; dry place away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined preparation</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>This tablet is a preparation of Iron, Folic Acid, and Zinc. In this preparation, Iron is present as Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex, a noble Iron preparation. It contains non-ionic Ferric Iron and Polymaltose in a stable complex. This complex facilitates controlled absorption of Iron when it comes in contact with the mucosal cell surface. Due to its non-ionic nature, Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex is more stable than conventional Iron forms. Folic Acid aids in the proper development of the fetus, while Zinc helps enzymes function and metabolize proteins.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Use of any drug during the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided if possible. Thus, the administration of Iron in the first trimester requires definite evidence of Iron deficiency.</p>', 'active', '922_1742502976.webp', 11, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:36:16', '2025-05-03 21:06:42'),
(3777, 'Zeltas', 'Azelastine Hydrochloride + Fluticasone Propionate', 320.00, 1, 'x 120 metered sprays', '120 metered sprays', 'Nasal Spray', '(137 mcg+50 mcg)/spray', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here\'s a cleaned-up and bolded version of the provided information:</p><p><strong>Indications of Zeltas</strong></p><p>Azelastine and Fluticasone Nasal Spray is indicated for the relief of symptoms of seasonal allergic rhinitis in patients 6 years of age and older who require treatment with both Azelastine Hydrochloride and Fluticasone Propionate for symptomatic relief.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Nasal Steroid Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Azelastine Hydrochloride exhibits histamine H1-receptor antagonist activity in isolated tissues. The major metabolite, desmethylazelastine, also possesses H1-receptor antagonist activity.</li><li>Fluticasone Propionate is a synthetic trifluorinated corticosteroid with anti-inflammatory activity. The precise mechanism through which Fluticasone Propionate affects allergic rhinitis symptoms is not known. Corticosteroids have been shown to have a wide range of effects on multiple cell types (e.g., mast cells, eosinophils, neutrophils, macrophages, and lymphocytes) and mediators (e.g., histamine, eicosanoids, leukotrienes, and cytokines) involved in inflammation.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zeltas</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: The recommended dosage is one spray in each nostril twice daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric</strong>: The safety and effectiveness of Azelastine Hydrochloride and Fluticasone Propionate Nasal Spray have not been established for patients less than 6 years of age.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zeltas</strong></p><ol><li>Shake the bottle gently and remove the dust cover.</li><li>Hold the spray with your forefinger and middle finger on either side of the nozzle and your thumb underneath the bottle.</li><li>Press down until a fine spray appears.</li><li>If using for the first time or after not using for a week or more, press the nasal applicator several times until a fine mist comes out.</li><li>Gently blow your nose to clear the nostrils.</li><li>Close one nostril and carefully insert the nasal applicator into the open nostril. Tilt your head forward slightly and keep the spray upright.</li><li>Breathe in through your nose and while breathing in, press the white collar of the nasal applicator firmly down once to release a spray.</li><li>Breathe out through your mouth.</li><li>Repeat the above steps in the same or other nostril for consecutive doses.</li></ol><p><strong>Cleaning</strong>: The nasal spray should be cleaned at least once a week. Remove the dust cover, gently pull off the nasal applicator, and wash both the applicator and dust cover in warm water. Shake off excess water and leave to dry naturally. Avoid applying additional heat. Reassemble the applicator and dust cover once dry.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>There is no known contraindication.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zeltas</strong></p><p>The most common adverse reactions (&gt;2% incidence) are:</p><ul><li>Dysgeusia</li><li>Epistaxis</li><li>Headache</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy category C</strong>: There are no adequate and well-controlled clinical trials of Azelastine Hydrochloride and Fluticasone Propionate Nasal Spray, Azelastine Hydrochloride only, or Fluticasone Propionate only in pregnant women. It should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit to the mother justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Nursing Mothers</strong>: It is not known whether Azelastine Hydrochloride and Fluticasone Propionate Nasal Spray is excreted in human breast milk. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk, caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Engagement in hazardous occupations requiring complete mental alertness, such as driving or operating machinery, should be avoided when taking Azelastine and Fluticasone Nasal Spray.</li><li>Concurrent use of alcohol or other central nervous system (CNS) depressants with this Nasal Spray should also be avoided due to further decreased alertness and CNS impairment.</li><li>Hypercorticism and adrenal suppression with very high dosages or at the regular dosage in susceptible individuals may occur. If such changes occur, the spray should be discontinued slowly.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zeltas</strong></p><ul><li>There have been no reported overdoses with Azelastine Hydrochloride. Acute Azelastine Hydrochloride overdosage in adults with this dosage form is unlikely to result in clinically significant adverse events, other than increased somnolence.</li><li>Chronic Fluticasone Propionate overdosage may result in symptoms of hypercorticism.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 25°C. Protect from light. Do not store in the refrigerator. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Nasal Steroid Preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Azelastine Hydrochloride exhibits histamine H1-receptor antagonist activity in isolated tissues. The major metabolite, desmethylazelastine, also possesses H1-receptor antagonist activity.</li><li>Fluticasone Propionate is a synthetic trifluorinated corticosteroid with anti-inflammatory activity. The precise mechanism through which Fluticasone Propionate affects allergic rhinitis symptoms is not known. Corticosteroids have been shown to have a wide range of effects on multiple cell types (e.g., mast cells, eosinophils, neutrophils, macrophages, and lymphocytes) and mediators (e.g., histamine, eicosanoids, leukotrienes, and cytokines) involved in inflammation.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy category C</strong>: There are no adequate and well-controlled clinical trials of Azelastine Hydrochloride and Fluticasone Propionate Nasal Spray, Azelastine Hydrochloride only, or Fluticasone Propionate only in pregnant women. It should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit to the mother justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Nursing Mothers</strong>: It is not known whether Azelastine Hydrochloride and Fluticasone Propionate Nasal Spray is excreted in human breast milk. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk, caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you\'d like to modify or add any more details!</p>', 'active', '1401_1742503108.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:38:28', '2025-04-23 08:04:35'),
(3778, 'Rabeca', 'Rabeprazole Sodium', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Rabeca 20 mg</h3><p>Gastro-resistant tablets of Rabeca (Rabeprazole) are recommended for the treatment of:</p><ul><li><strong>Active duodenal ulcer</strong></li><li><strong>Active benign gastric ulcer</strong></li><li><strong>Symptomatic gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</strong>, erosive or ulcerative type</li><li><strong>GERD maintenance</strong> (Long-term management of GERD)</li><li><strong>Moderate to severe GERD symptoms</strong> (Symptomatic GERD)</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong></li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication</strong> in patients with peptic ulcer disease, in combination with suitable antibacterial therapy regimens</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Proton Pump Inhibitor</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Rabeprazole inhibits the <strong>gastric H+/K+-ATPase</strong> at the secretory surface of stomach parietal cells, reducing gastric acid secretion. It is considered a <strong>gastric proton pump inhibitor</strong> since it targets the acid (proton) pump within the parietal cell.</p><p><br></p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rabeca 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer</strong>: 20 mg in the morning for 4 weeks. Healing is typically observed within 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Erosive or ulcerative GERD</strong>: 20 mg daily for 4 to 8 weeks. If necessary, an additional 8-week course may be considered.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily in the morning for 4 weeks. If symptoms persist after 4 weeks, additional treatment may be required.</li><li><strong>GERD maintenance</strong>: 20 mg once daily. Controlled studies do not extend beyond 12 months.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: Starting dose is 60 mg once daily. The dose may be adjusted according to individual needs, with possible doses up to 100 mg daily or 60 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication</strong>: Rabeprazole 20 mg, Amoxicillin 1000 mg, Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily with meals for 7 days.</li></ul><h3>Administration</h3><ul><li>Rabeca 20 mg should be taken <strong>whole</strong>, not chewed or mashed.</li><li>While the time of day or food intake does not affect the medication’s efficacy, it is easier for patients to follow a treatment plan if taken in the morning before meals.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Rabeca 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Itraconazole</strong> and <strong>Ketoconazole</strong>: Co-administration with Rabeca 20 mg may cause a significant decrease in antifungal plasma levels.</li><li><strong>Atazanavir</strong>: Proton pump inhibitors, such as Rabeca, should not be taken alongside Atazanavir due to pH-dependent absorption.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Rabeca\'s active ingredients or excipients.</li><li>Use during pregnancy and breastfeeding is <strong>not advised</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Rabeca 20 mg</h3><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Muscle pain</li><li>Sleepiness</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Increased or decreased appetite</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>FDA pregnancy category \"C\"</strong>: No sufficient human studies on pregnant women.</li><li>Rabeprazole is likely excreted in human milk, so the decision to <strong>continue breastfeeding</strong> or <strong>discontinue medication</strong> should be made based on the mother\'s need for the drug.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Malignancy</strong>: Before starting treatment with Rabeca, rule out stomach or esophageal cancer.</li><li><strong>Long-term use</strong>: Regular monitoring is advised, especially for patients who have been on the medication for over a year.</li><li><strong>Fractures</strong>: Long-term use (especially at high doses) may slightly increase the risk of hip, wrist, and spine fractures.</li><li><strong>Osteoporosis risk</strong>: Ensure adequate calcium and vitamin D intake.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Rabeca 20 mg</h3><ul><li>Overdose symptoms are typically mild, similar to known side effects, and reversible without medical intervention.</li><li><strong>No specific antidote</strong> for overdose. <strong>Symptomatic treatment</strong> should be used.</li><li>Rabeprazole is <strong>highly protein-bound</strong>, so it cannot be dialyzed.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Limited data on safety and efficacy, so Rabeca is <strong>not recommended</strong> in pediatric populations.</li><li><strong>Hepatic and renal impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary for patients with these conditions.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Rabeprazole inhibits the <strong>gastric H+/K+-ATPase</strong>, which reduces gastric acid secretion, acting as a proton pump inhibitor at the stomach parietal cells.</p><p><br></p><h3>Pediatric Uses</h3><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in children are not established, so Rabeca is <strong>not recommended</strong> for pediatric use.</li></ul>', 'active', '818_1742503235.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:40:35', '2025-05-04 15:00:35'),
(3779, 'Evit Soft', 'Vitamin E [Alpha Tocopherol Acetate]', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the text you provided, formatted with the headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Evit Soft 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>As a dietary supplement</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vitamin E deficiency resulting from impaired absorption</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increased requirements due to a diet rich in polyunsaturated fats</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For healthy hair &amp; skin</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">As an antioxidant</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hemolytic anemia due to Vitamin E deficiency</li><li><strong>Therapeutic use</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Cardiovascular disease</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Heavy metal poisoning</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hepatotoxin poisoning</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hemolytic anemia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oxygen therapy</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In nutritional deficiency states</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Vitamin-E Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Vitamin E acts as an antioxidant in the body. Vitamin E protects polyunsaturated fatty acids (which are components of cellular membranes) and other oxygen-sensitive substances such as vitamin A &amp; vitamin C from oxidation. In premature neonates, irritability, edema, thrombosis, and hemolytic anemia may be caused due to vitamin E deficiency. Creatinuria, ceroid deposition, muscle weakness, decreased erythrocyte survival, or increased in vitro hemolysis by oxidizing agents have been identified in adults and children with low serum tocopherol concentrations.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Evit Soft 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Betterment of cardiovascular health</strong>: 400 IU-800 IU per day</li><li><strong>Deficiency syndrome in adults</strong>: 200 IU-400 IU per day</li><li><strong>Deficiency syndrome in children</strong>: 200 IU per day</li><li><strong>Thalassemia</strong>: 800 IU per day</li><li><strong>Sickle-cell anemia</strong>: 400 IU per day</li><li><strong>Betterment of skin &amp; hair</strong>: 200 IU-400 IU per day (Topical use is also established for beautification)</li><li><strong>Chronic cold in adults</strong>: 200 IU per day</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Evit Soft 400 mg</strong></p><p>Vitamin E may impair the absorption of Vitamin A &amp; function of Vitamin K and potentiates the effect of Warfarin.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>No known contraindications found.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Evit Soft 400 mg</strong></p><p>Overdosage (&gt;1 gm) has been associated with minor side effects, including hypertension, fatigue, diarrhea, and myopathy.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Vitamin E is safe in pregnancy and lactation, when used at recommended doses. Higher doses are not established.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>It may increase the risk of thrombosis in some patients, such as those taking estrogens.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>Vitamin E is safe for children.</p>', 'active', '60_1742503339.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:42:19', '2025-05-03 10:16:40'),
(3780, 'Flexi', 'Aceclofenac', 7.00, 7, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information in a more organized and bold format:</p><p><strong>Indications of Flexi 100 mg</strong></p><p>Flexi 100 mg is indicated for the relief of pain and inflammation in:</p><ul><li>Osteoarthritis</li><li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li><li>Ankylosing spondylitis</li><li>Toothache</li><li>Trauma</li><li>Lumbago</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Flexi 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Flexi 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Extended release tablet</strong>: The recommended dose in adults is one 200 mg Flexi 100 mg tablet daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Film-coated tablet</strong>: The recommended dose in adults is 100 mg, twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Flexi 100 mg</strong></p><p>No significant drug interactions have been observed, but close monitoring of patients is required when it is used with:</p><ul><li><strong>Lithium and Digoxin</strong>: May increase plasma concentration of lithium and digoxin.</li><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: May interact with the activity of diuretics.</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants</strong>: May enhance the activity of anticoagulants.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: May increase the plasma level of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Flexi 100 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to it or in whom aspirin or NSAIDs precipitate attacks of asthma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Flexi 100 mg</strong></p><p>Flexi 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, involved in the production of prostaglandins. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of Flexi 100 mg should be avoided during pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised for patients with:</p><ul><li>Active or suspected peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding</li><li>Moderate to severe hepatic impairment</li><li>Cardiac or renal impairment</li><li>Dizziness or urticaria</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Flexi 100 mg in children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Flexi 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The use of Flexi 100 mg should be avoided during pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Flexi 100 mg in children.</p>', 'active', '1683_1742503434.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:43:54', '2025-05-03 10:46:59');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3781, 'Defrol', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 6.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '1000 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Defrol 1000 IU</h3><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated for the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also used as an adjunct in the therapy of:</p><ul><li>Osteoporosis</li><li>Osteomalacia</li><li>Hypocalcemia</li><li>Tetany</li><li>Rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency.</li></ul><p>Vitamin D3, a synthetic form of Vitamin D, is essential for normal bone growth, development, and maintaining bone density. It also plays a critical role in the utilization of calcium and phosphorus. Babies require Vitamin D3 for healthy growth and development. Additionally, Vitamin D3 acts as a hormone that can aid in the prevention of bacterial infections, improve lung function, reduce the risk of certain cancers (e.g., breast and colorectal), and help maintain adequate insulin levels for individuals with type 2 diabetes.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation</li><li>Vitamin D preparations</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) enhances the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is vital for normal bone growth and maintaining bone density. Beyond bone health, Vitamin D3 is also involved in immune function and reducing the risk of certain diseases.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Defrol 1000 IU</h3><p><strong>Capsules:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance therapy</strong>: 1,400–2,000 IU/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adjunct therapy for osteoporosis</strong>: 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li><strong>Children (12–18 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU once every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Film-coated Tablets:</strong></p><ul><li>1,000 IU (1–2 tablets) daily, or as directed by a physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li></ul><p><strong>Chewable Tablets:</strong></p><ul><li>1,000–2,000 IU daily, or as directed by a physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li></ul><p><strong>Syrup:</strong></p><ul><li>For patients at risk of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0–1 year: 400 IU/day (2 mL)</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 mL)</blockquote><ul><li>For Vitamin D3 deficient patients:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0–1 year: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1–18 years: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks</li></ul><p><strong>Injection:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prevention</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Infants receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 mL) = 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Non-breastfed infants or children up to 5 years: 1 ampoule (1 mL) = 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adolescents: 1 ampoule (1 mL) = 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pregnancy (from the 6th or 7th month): 1/2 ampoule (0.5 mL) = 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Elderly: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 mL) = 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In cases of digestive disorders, anticonvulsant treatment, or other conditions: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 mL) = 100,000 IU or 1 ampoule (1 mL) = 200,000 IU every 3–6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vitamin D deficiency: 1 ampoule (1 mL) = 200,000 IU, repeat after 1 to 6 months, or as directed by a physician.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Defrol 1000 IU</h3><p>It interferes with:</p><ul><li>Phenytoin</li><li>Barbiturates</li><li>Glucocorticoids</li><li>Certain laxatives (e.g., liquid paraffin)</li><li>Actinomycin</li><li>Imidazole antifungal agents</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</p><h3>Side Effects of Defrol 1000 IU</h3><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Hypercalcemia</li><li>Hypercalciuria</li><li>Skin rash</li><li>Pruritus</li><li>Urticaria</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Abdominal pain</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>Studies show that doses up to 4,000 IU are safe during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU, but higher doses may be required in cases of Vitamin D3 deficiency.</li><li>Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk, but overdose in infants from nursing mothers has not been observed. If additional Vitamin D3 is prescribed to a breastfed child, the dose of any additional Vitamin D3 given to the mother should be considered.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Defrol 1000 IU</h3><p>Overdose can lead to <strong>hypervitaminosis D</strong>.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation</li><li>Vitamin D preparations</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus, essential for bone health, immune function, and reducing disease risk.</p>', 'active', '309_1742503528.jpg', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:45:28', '2025-03-21 01:45:28'),
(3782, 'Voltalin', 'Diclofenac Sodium', 60.00, 1, 'suppository', '1 suppository', 'Suppository', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Voltalin 50 mg:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Rheumatology</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Inflammatory and degenerative forms of rheumatism</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Chronic involutive polyarthritis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Ankylosing spondylarthritis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Osteoarthritis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Spondylarthroses</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute gout</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Peri-articular rheumatic disorders</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Surgery and Traumatology</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sprains</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Bruises</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dislocations</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fractures</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Soft tissue injuries</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Surgical interventions</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Obstetrics and Gynecology</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Primary dysmenorrhea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Episiotomy</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adnexitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Endometritis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Parametritis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Salpingitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mastitis</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Otorhinolaryngology</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pre-operative medication for the prevention of pain, inflammation, and swelling.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Dentistry</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Post-operative and post-traumatic pain, inflammation, and swelling.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Other indications</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For the prevention of pain and treatment of inflammation and swelling in patients operated in the urogenital tract</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Renal and biliary colic</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><p>Voltalin 50 mg is a potent NSAID with significant anti-rheumatic, anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic properties. It also has some uricosuric effects. Diclofenac works by inhibiting prostaglandin biosynthesis, which plays a major role in causing inflammation, pain, and fever. Diclofenac is rapidly absorbed when taken with or after a meal, reaching peak plasma concentrations within 2 hours. It is highly bound to plasma proteins (99.7%) and is metabolized in the liver.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Enteric-coated tablet</strong>: 75-150 mg daily, in 2-3 divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Sustained-release tablets</strong>: 1 tablet daily, taken with liquid during a meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppositories</strong>: 75-150 mg daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection</strong>: 1 ampoule once (or in severe cases, twice) daily by intramuscular injection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gel</strong>: 2-4 g applied 3-4 times daily to the affected site.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Enteric-coated tablet</strong>: 1-3 mg/kg per day in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppositories</strong>: 1-3 mg/kg body weight in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection</strong>: In juvenile chronic arthritis, 1-3 mg/kg body weight daily in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Elderly patients</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The lowest effective dosage is recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Voltalin 50 mg</strong>:</p><ul><li>Diuretics, digoxin, methotrexate, cyclosporine, and lithium may interact with Voltalin, requiring close monitoring for toxicities.</li><li>Combining Voltalin with other NSAIDs or glucocorticoids increases the risk of side effects like gastrointestinal bleeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient in the product.</li><li>Peptic ulcer, asthma, and other conditions triggered by NSAIDs (e.g., urticaria, acute rhinitis).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li>Epigastric pain, nausea, dizziness, and headache (rare and mild)</li><li>Peripheral edema and skin reactions (rash, eczema)</li><li>Voltalin gel may cause local irritation and skin rash.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li>Diclofenac should be avoided during pregnancy unless necessary. It’s not recommended in the first trimester.</li><li>Very little Diclofenac is found in breast milk, with no known adverse effects on infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong>:</p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with a history of peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding.</li><li>Elderly patients should be closely monitored.</li><li>Avoid contact of Voltalin Gel with eyes or mucous membranes.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li><li>Store below 30°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</p><ul><li>Diclofenac sodium inhibits prostaglandin biosynthesis, providing anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic effects.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further clarification on any of these points!</p>', 'active', '608_1742503667.webp', 40, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:47:47', '2025-05-07 19:46:26'),
(3783, 'Amodis', 'Metronidazole', 35.00, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '200 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Amodis 60 ml</strong></p><p>Amodis 60 ml is indicated in the treatment of the following diseases:</p><ul><li>The prevention of post-operative infections due to anaerobic bacteria (particularly species of <em>Bacteroides</em> and anaerobic streptococci).</li><li>The treatment of septicemia, bacteremia, peritonitis, brain abscess, pelvic abscess, pelvic cellulitis, and post-operative wound infections caused by anaerobes.</li><li>In the treatment of urogenital trichomoniasis.</li><li>Bacterial vaginosis (also known as non-specific vaginitis).</li><li>All forms of amoebiasis (intestinal, extra-intestinal disease, and that of symptomless cyst passers).</li><li>Giardiasis.</li><li>Acute ulcerative gingivitis.</li><li>Anaerobically infected leg ulcers and pressure sores.</li><li>Acute dental infections due to anaerobic organisms.</li><li>Antibiotic-associated pseudomembranous colitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Amoebicides</li><li>Anti-diarrhoeal</li><li>Antiprotozoal</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Amodis 60 ml is a member of the imidazole class of antibacterial drugs and is classified therapeutically as an antiprotozoal agent. The 5-nitro group of Amodis 60 ml is reduced by anaerobes metabolically. Studies have demonstrated that the reduced form of this drug interacts with DNA and gives bactericidal action.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Amodis 60 ml</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet and Suspension:Trichomoniasis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong> 200 mg tid or 400 mg bid for 7 days; 800 mg in the morning and 1-2 gm at night for 2 days; 2 gm as a single dose for 1 day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Trichomoniasis (Children):</strong>Children 7-10 yrs: 100 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 3-7 yrs: 100 mg bid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 yrs: 50 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intestinal Amoebiasis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong> 800 mg tid for 5 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Intestinal Amoebiasis (Children):</strong>Children 7-10 yrs: 400 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 3-7 yrs: 200 mg qid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 yrs: 200 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Extra-intestinal &amp; Asymptomatic Amoebiasis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong> 400-800 mg tid for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Extra-intestinal &amp; Asymptomatic Amoebiasis (Children):</strong>Children 7-10 yrs: 200-400 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 3-7 yrs: 100-200 mg qid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 yrs: 100-200 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Giardiasis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong> 2 gm once daily for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Giardiasis (Children):</strong>Children 7-10 yrs: 1 gm once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 3-7 yrs: 600-800 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 yrs: 500 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute Ulcerative Gingivitis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong> 200 mg tid for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Acute Ulcerative Gingivitis (Children):</strong>Children 7-10 yrs: 100 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 3-7 yrs: 100 mg bid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 yrs: 50 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute Dental Infections (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong> 200 mg tid for 3-7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Bacterial Vaginosis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong> 400 mg bid for 7 days; 2 gm as a single dose for 1 day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Leg Ulcers and Pressure Sores (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong> 400 mg tid for 7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Anaerobic Infections (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong> 800 mg initially and then 400 mg tid for 7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Anaerobic Infections (Children):</strong> Children 1-10 yrs: 7.5 mg/kg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Surgical Prophylaxis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong> 400 mg tid started 24 hours before surgery for 1 day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Surgical Prophylaxis (Children):</strong> Children 1-10 yrs: 7.5 mg/kg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Vaginal Gel:</strong> One applicator full (approximately 37.5 mg of Amodis 60 ml) intravaginally once or twice a day for 5 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Suppository:Anaerobic Infections (Adults):</strong> 1 g every 8 hours for 3 days, then 1 g every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\"><strong>Anaerobic Infections (Children):</strong>5-10 years: 500 mg every 8 hours for 3 days, then every 12 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Over 10 years: Adult dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Surgical Prophylaxis (Adults):</strong> 1 g 2 hours before surgery; up to 3 further doses of 1 g every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Surgical Prophylaxis (Children):</strong> 500 mg 2 hours before surgery; up to 3 further doses of 500 mg every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV Infusion (Adults and Children over 12 years):</strong> Infuse 500 mg 8 hourly at a rate of 5 ml/minute, not exceeding 4 g in 24 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV Infusion (Children under 12 years):</strong> 7.5 mg/kg body weight/day every 8 hours at a rate of 5 ml/minute.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Amodis 60 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Disulfiram:</strong> Psychotic reactions when used concurrently.</li><li><strong>Alcohol:</strong> Avoid alcohol during therapy and for at least 1 day after, to prevent a disulfiram-like reaction.</li><li><strong>Oral Anticoagulants (e.g., Warfarin):</strong> Increased risk of bleeding. Monitor prothrombin time and adjust dosage.</li><li><strong>Lithium:</strong> Plasma levels may be increased.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin:</strong> Monitor serum levels closely.</li><li><strong>Phenytoin/Phenobarbital:</strong> Increased elimination of Amodis 60 ml, reducing its plasma concentration.</li><li><strong>5-Fluorouracil:</strong> May increase toxicity of 5-fluorouracil.</li><li><strong>Busulfan:</strong> Plasma levels may increase, leading to toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Amodis 60 ml or other Nitroimidazole derivatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Amodis 60 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Metallic taste, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, drowsiness, and rashes may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category:</strong> B (US FDA).</li><li>Should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Amodis 60 ml is excreted in human milk. Caution is advised for nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Long-term use may require regular hematological tests, especially leucocyte counts.</li><li>Caution in patients with hepatic encephalopathy.</li><li>May darken urine.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Amodis 60 ml</strong></p><ul><li>In case of overdose (up to 12 g), symptoms include vomiting, ataxia, and slight disorientation.</li><li>No specific antidote. Symptomatic and supportive treatment should be given.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light. Keep out of the reach of children. Do not use after the expiry date.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Amodis 60 ml is an imidazole antibacterial drug that exerts its bactericidal effect by interacting with DNA after being reduced metabolically by anaerobes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Use only if clearly needed during pregnancy, as animal studies do not predict human response reliably. Amodis 60 ml is excreted in milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hepatic impairment:</strong> Dose may need to be reduced.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> No dosage reduction required for renal failure but must be monitored, especially during hemodialysis.</li></ul>', 'active', '1232_1742503857.png', 16, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:50:57', '2025-04-30 14:38:50'),
(3784, 'Dermasol-S', 'Clobetasol Propionate', 200.61, 1, 'x 25 ml bottle', '25 ml bottle', 'Scalp Solution', '0.05%', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information you provided formatted with headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Dermasol-S 0.05%</strong></p><p>Clobetasol Propionate scalp solution is indicated in the topical therapy of recalcitrant corticosteroid-responsive dermatoses of the scalp, including recalcitrant cases of psoriasis and seborrheic dermatitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Other Topical corticosteroids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Clobetasol Propionate is a very potent topical corticosteroid. It has anti-inflammatory, antipruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties. It shows anti-inflammatory activity via multiple mechanisms to inhibit late-phase allergic reactions. It decreases the density of mast cells, chemotaxis, and activation of eosinophils. It also reduces cytokine production and inhibits the metabolism of arachidonic acid.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Dermasol-S 0.05%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate spray</strong>: Apply the required quantity of spray once or twice daily to the affected areas of the scalp and gently rub in. The total dose applied should not exceed 50 ml weekly. If necessary, Clobetasol Propionate scalp solution may be massaged into the scalp using the tips of the fingers. Therapy should be discontinued if no response is noted after one week or as soon as the lesion heals. It is advisable to use Clobetasol Propionate scalp solution for brief periods only.</li><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate shampoo</strong>: It should be applied to the dry (not wet) scalp once a day to the affected areas only. It should be massaged gently into the lesions and left in place for 15 minutes before lathering and rinsing. Treatment should be limited to 4 consecutive weeks. Total dosage of shampoo should not exceed 50 g per week. Under 18 years, this preparation is not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Infections of the scalp</li><li>Hypersensitivity to the preparation</li><li>Use is not indicated in dermatoses in children under one year of age</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Dermasol-S 0.05%</strong></p><p>As with other corticosteroids, prolonged use of large amounts or treatment of extensive areas can result in sufficient systemic absorption to produce the features of hypercortisolism. This effect is more likely to occur in infants and children, and if occlusive dressings are used. Local atrophy may occur after prolonged treatment. In rare instances, treatment of psoriasis with corticosteroids (or its withdrawal) is thought to have provoked the pustular form of the disease. If signs of hypersensitivity appear with the use of Clobetasol Propionate Scalp Solution, then application should be stopped immediately.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Topical administration of corticosteroids to pregnant animals can cause abnormalities of fetal development. The relevance of this finding to humans has not been established; however, topical steroids should not be used extensively in pregnancy, i.e., in large amounts for prolonged periods. The safe use of Clobetasol Propionate during lactation has not been established.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Care must be taken to keep the preparation away from the eyes. Long-term continuous therapy with Clobetasol Propionate scalp solution should be avoided where possible, particularly in infants and children, as adrenal suppression can occur even without occlusion. Topical corticosteroids may be hazardous in psoriasis for a number of reasons, including rebound relapses, development of tolerance, risk of generalized pustular psoriasis, and development of local or systemic toxicity due to impaired barrier function of the skin. If used on psoriasis, careful patient supervision is important. Appropriate antimicrobial therapy should be used whenever treating inflammatory lesions that have become infected. Any spread of infection requires withdrawal of topical corticosteroid therapy and systemic administration of antimicrobial agents. Bacterial infection is encouraged by the warm, moist conditions induced by occlusive dressings, and so the skin should be cleansed before a fresh dressing is applied.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, protected from light and moisture. Do not freeze. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p>Let me know if you need further formatting or additional details!</p>', 'active', '1172_1742504059.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:54:19', '2025-04-23 07:54:55'),
(3785, 'Opton', 'Esomeprazole', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the provided information with headings formatted in bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Opton 20 mg</strong></p><p>Opton 20 mg is indicated:</p><ul><li>To alleviate GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn and others.</li><li>In order to treat erosive esophagitis.</li><li>To maintain the erosive esophagitis\'s ability to recover.</li><li>For the eradication of Helicobacter pylori infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Syndrome of Zollinger-Ellison.</li><li>Duodenal &amp; gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Opton 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. The majority of patients are healed within 4 to 8 weeks. For patients who don\'t heal after 4-8 weeks, an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg once daily (Clinical studies did not extend 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely after 4 weeks, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication:</strong> Triple Therapy to reduce the risk of duodenal ulcer recurrence—Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days, Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> The dose is 20-80 mg once daily. The dosage should be adjusted individually and treatment continued as long as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks according to the response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Injection:</strong></p><p>The recommended adult dose is 40 mg Opton 20 mg given once daily by intravenous injection (not less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes). Esomeprazole IV injection should not be administered concomitantly with any other medications through the same intravenous site. Treatment with Esomeprazole IV injection should be discontinued as soon as the patient is able to resume treatment with Esomeprazole delayed-release capsules. Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><strong>Administration of Opton 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>One hour before a meal, take one Opton 20 mg pill or capsule and consume it whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension Use Guidelines:</strong> The entire packet\'s contents should be placed in a little glass with 15 ml of water. After thoroughly stirring the mixture, give it two to three minutes to thicken. Within 30 minutes, stir once more and consume. Add extra water, swirl, and drink right away if any medicine is still present after consumption.</li><li>If the suspension is to be taken via a nasogastric or gastric tube, 15 ml of water should be placed in the syringe. After shaking the syringe, wait two to three minutes for the suspension to thicken. Within 30 minutes, shake the syringe and administer a gastric or nasogastric injection into the stomach. Use a syringe that is the right size. Shake and ingest any leftover substances from the gastric or nasogastric tube.</li><li>A gradual intravenous injection of esomeprazole IV is the recommended method of administration. 5 ml of the specified solvent (WFI) is added to the vial to create the IV injection solution. Upon reconstitution, the injection should be administered gradually over a minimum of three minutes. When kept at room temperature up to 30°C, the solution must be used within 12 hours of reconstitution. Nothing needs to be refrigerated. If there are any visible particles in the reconstituted solution, do not use them.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Opton 20 mg</strong></p><p>CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 substantially metabolize esomeprazole in the liver. Studies conducted both in vivo and in vitro have demonstrated that esomeprazole is unlikely to inhibit the CYPs 1A2, 2A6, 2C9, 2D6, 2E1, and 3A4. There shouldn\'t be any clinically significant interactions with medications that these CYP enzymes process. According to studies on drug interactions, esomeprazole does not interact clinically with phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin. The main enzyme that metabolizes esomeprazole, CYP2C19, may be affected by esomeprazole. Diazepam\'s clearance was reduced by 45% when Esomeprazole 30 mg and diazepam, a CYP2C19 substrate, were taken together. Diazepam plasma levels have been seen to rise 12 hours after dosage and beyond. Esomeprazole reduces the production of stomach acid. As a result, esomeprazole may hinder the absorption of medications whose bioavailability is significantly influenced by gastrointestinal pH (e.g., ketoconazole, iron salts, and digoxin). The pharmacokinetic profile of esomeprazole does not appear to be altered by the co-administration of oral contraceptives, diazepam, phenytoin, or quinidine. Esomeprazole and 14-hydroxy clarithromycin plasma levels have increased as a result of co-administration with clarithromycin, amoxicillin, and other medications.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those with known hypersensitivity to any of the formulations should not take esomeprazole.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Opton 20 mg</strong></p><p>The side effects of Opton 20 mg that are most frequently mentioned are headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, and dry mouth. When compared to short-term treatment, relevant side events occurring after maintenance therapy up to 12 months do not differ in type.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General:</strong> A positive symptom response to Opton 20 mg does not rule out the possibility of stomach cancer. Patients should take esomeprazole capsules at least an hour before meals. One spoonful of applesauce can be put into an empty bowl, and the Opton 20 mg capsules can be opened, with the pellets inside the capsule carefully dumped onto the applesauce for patients who have trouble swallowing capsules. After combining the pellets and applesauce, immediately ingest the mixture. Use only cold applesauce that is soft enough to be taken without chewing. You shouldn\'t chew or shatter the pellets. It is not advised to keep the pellet/applesauce mixture for later usage. While taking esomeprazole, antacids are acceptable.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Opton 20 mg</strong></p><p>Opton 20 mg has been shown to be deadly in rats at a single oral dose of 510 mg/kg, or nearly 103 times the human dose on the basis of body surface area. The main symptoms of acute poisoning are decreased motor activity, changes in breathing frequency, tremors, ataxia, and sporadic clonic convulsions. Esomeprazole overdoses are not known to have occurred. Esomeprazole has no known particular countermeasure. Dialysis isn\'t expected to remove esomeprazole because of how widely it is protein bound. If an overdose occurs, symptomatic and supportive care should be provided. The possibility of ingesting numerous drugs needs to be taken into account, just like with any overdose.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry area at a temperature that does not go above 30°C. Shield from moisture and light. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>By specifically inhibiting the H+/K+-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell, the proton pump inhibitor esomeprazole reduces stomach acid output. The first single optical isomer of a proton pump inhibitor, esomeprazole (S-isomer of omeprazole), offers superior acid control over racemic proton pump inhibitors.</p><ul><li><strong>Absorption:</strong> The enteric-coated pellet version of esomeprazole magnesium is found in esomeprazole capsules. Peak plasma levels (Cmax) after oral dosing happen about 1.5 hours later (Tmax). The area under the plasma concentration-time curve (AUC) increases by a factor of three from 20 to 40 mg, and the Cmax increases correspondingly when the dose is raised. The systemic bioavailability is about 90% at repeated once-daily dosage as opposed to 64% following a single dose. When compared to fasting settings, the AUC following a single dosage of esomeprazole is reduced by 33-53% after eating. It is recommended to take esomeprazole at least an hour before meals.</li><li><strong>Distribution:</strong> Plasma proteins are 97% bound to esomeprazole. Across the concentration range of 2–20 mmol/L, plasma protein binding remains stable. In healthy volunteers, the apparent volume of distribution at steady state is roughly 16 L. Esomeprazole is extensively processed by the cytochrome P450 (CYP) enzyme system in the liver. Esomeprazole\'s metabolites don\'t have any anti-secretory properties. The CYP2C19 isoenzyme, which produces the hydroxy and desmethyl metabolites, is essential for the majority of the metabolism of esomeprazole. The CYP3A4 enzyme, which produces the sulphone metabolite, determines the residual amount.</li><li><strong>Excretion:</strong> Esomeprazole has a plasma elimination half-life of about 1-1.5 hours. The amount of parent medication discharged in urine is less than 1%. Esomeprazole is an oral medication that is excreted in two ways: 80% of an oral dose is found in the urine as inactive metabolites, and the remaining 20% is found in the feces as inactive metabolites.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There is no sufficient and reliable research on expectant mothers. No teratogenic effects have been identified in animal investigations. Esomeprazole\'s excretion in milk has not been investigated. So, if the usage of esomeprazole is deemed necessary, breastfeeding should be stopped.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Pediatric patients\' safety and efficacy have not been shown.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><p>There haven\'t been any overall variations in safety and effectiveness between elderly and younger people, and other recorded clinical experience hasn\'t found differences in reactions between elderly and younger patients, although it\'s possible that certain older people may be more sensitive.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><p>For individuals with mild to moderate hepatic insufficiency, no dosage change is advised. On the other hand, a dose of 20 mg once daily should not be exceeded in individuals with severe hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><p>As less than 1% of esomeprazole is eliminated unaltered in the urine, it is not anticipated that the pharmacokinetics of esomeprazole in patients with renal impairment will differ from those in healthy volunteers.</p>', 'active', '779_1742504206.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:56:46', '2025-05-04 01:21:35'),
(3786, 'Esomep', 'Esomeprazole', 8.02, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p>', 'active', '8147_1746167294.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 01:58:55', '2025-05-02 10:28:34'),
(3787, 'Tuspel', 'Guaifenesin + Dextromethorphan + Menthol', 85.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '200 mg+15 mg+15 mg)/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information you provided in normal bold text format for easy reference:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Tuspel 100 ml</strong></p><p>Fast &amp; effective relief of Chest congestion, Cough, Sore throat</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Combined cough expectorants, Combined cough suppressants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Guaifenesin</strong> is an expectorant. It helps loosen congestion in the chest and throat, making it easier to cough out through the mouth.</li><li><strong>Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide</strong> is a cough suppressant. It affects the signals in the brain that trigger the cough reflex.</li><li><strong>Menthol</strong> is an organic compound made synthetically or obtained from corn mint, peppermint, or other mint oils. It has local anesthetic and counterirritant qualities, and it is widely used to relieve minor throat irritation.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Tuspel 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult Use Only (12 years and over):</strong> 2 teaspoonful syrup (10 ml) every 6 hours. Maximum 8 teaspoonful syrup (40 ml)/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Tuspel 100 ml</strong></p><p>Possible reactions systemically may occur with acetaminophen/hydrocodone, diphenhydramine, escitalopram, acetaminophen/codeine, alprazolam, and ondansetron.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>If you are now taking a prescription monoamine oxidase inhibitor (MAOI) (certain drugs for depression, psychiatric or emotional conditions, or Parkinson’s disease), or for two weeks after stopping the MAOI drug. If you do not know if your prescription drug contains an MAOI, ask a doctor or pharmacist before taking this product.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Tuspel 100 ml</strong></p><p>Common side effects may include:</p><ul><li>Dizziness, drowsiness</li><li>Gastrointestinal disturbance</li><li>Feeling nervous, restless, anxious, or irritable</li><li>Do not use for children less than 12 years of age.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide-guaifenesin</strong> have been assigned to pregnancy category C by the FDA. There are no controlled data on this combination product in human pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide-guaifenesin</strong> is only recommended for use during pregnancy when the benefit outweighs the risk.</li><li>There are no data on the excretion of dextromethorphan-guaifenesin into human milk. In nursing infants, a decision should be made to discontinue.</li><li>There are no human studies on the use of menthol during pregnancy; thus, its risk is undetermined.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Talk to a doctor before using this product if you have:</p><ul><li>Asthma</li><li>Chronic lung disease or shortness of breath</li><li>Persistent/chronic cough</li><li>Taking a drug for depression, including monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitor drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Tuspel 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Overdosage with <strong>guaifenesin</strong> is unlikely to produce toxic effects since its toxicity is low. In severe cases of overdosage, treatment should be aimed at reducing further absorption of the drug. Gastric emptying (emesis and/or gastric lavage) is recommended as soon as possible after ingestion.</li><li>Overdosage with <strong>Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide</strong> may produce excitement and mental confusion. Very high doses may produce respiratory depression. One case of toxic psychosis (hyper-activity, marked visual and auditory hallucinations) after ingestion of a single 300 mg dose of Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide has been reported.</li><li><strong>Menthol</strong> can be harmful in large amounts. Symptoms may include abdominal pain, diarrhea, nausea and vomiting, dizziness, rapid breathing, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li><li>Keep in a cool &amp; dry place.</li><li>Protect from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Combined cough expectorants, Combined cough suppressants</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Guaifenesin</strong> is an expectorant. It helps loosen congestion in the chest and throat, making it easier to cough out through the mouth.</li><li><strong>Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide</strong> is a cough suppressant. It affects the signals in the brain that trigger the cough reflex.</li><li><strong>Menthol</strong> is an organic compound made synthetically or obtained from corn mint, peppermint, or other mint oils. It has local anesthetic and counterirritant qualities, and it is widely used to relieve minor throat irritation.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide-guaifenesin</strong> have been assigned to pregnancy category C by the FDA. There are no controlled data on this combination product in human pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide-guaifenesin</strong> is only recommended for use during pregnancy when the benefit outweighs the risk.</li><li>There are no data on the excretion of dextromethorphan-guaifenesin into human milk. In nursing infants, a decision should be made to discontinue.</li><li>There are no human studies on the use of menthol during pregnancy; thus, its risk is undetermined.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you\'d like any further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '276_1742504491.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:01:31', '2025-04-16 12:27:23'),
(3788, 'B126', 'Vitamin B1, B6 & B12', 12.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a stirp', 'Tablet', '100 mg+200 mg+200 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of B126</strong></p><p>B126 is indicated for the treatment of vitamin B1, B6, and B12 deficiency syndrome. It is also used for the supportive treatment of neuritis and non-inflammatory diseases of the nerves, such as:</p><ul><li>Diabetic neuropathy</li><li>Peripheral neuralgia</li><li>Lumbago</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Optic neuritis</li><li>Sciatica</li><li>Facial neuralgia</li><li>Intercostal neuralgia</li><li>Spinal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate) 100 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride) 200 mg</li><li>Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) 200 mcg</li></ul><p>Each 3 ml ampoule contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) 100 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) 100 mg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) 1000 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>B126 is a combined preparation of Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate), Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride), and Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin). These vitamins act as coenzymes in the metabolism of the nervous system, supporting the regeneration of nerve fibers and the myelin sheath. This is done through the activation of nerve cell metabolism and the natural repair mechanisms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Tablets</strong>: 1 to 3 tablets per day, or as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Injection</strong>:</p><ul><li>Ampoules should be injected intramuscularly (preferably deep intragluteal).</li><li>In severe cases: 1 ampoule daily until acute symptoms subside.</li><li>For milder cases and follow-up therapy: 2 to 3 ampoules per week.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Children</strong></p><p>There is no information available on the use of this drug in children.</p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported for B126.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vitamin B1, Vitamin B6, and Vitamin B12 are contraindicated in patients on levodopa therapy and in patients with hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of the preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>B126 is generally well tolerated, though allergic reactions may occur in some cases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>There are no known risks associated with the use of this combination during pregnancy at the recommended dosage.</li><li>Vitamin B1, B6, and B12 are secreted into breast milk, but the risks of overdose for the infant are not known.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Cyanocobalamin should not be given to patients with subacute degeneration of the spinal cord.</li><li>Cyanocobalamin is not suitable for the treatment of optic neuropathies associated with raised plasma concentrations of cyanocobalamin.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>No overdosage symptoms are expected with the recommended dosage. If an overdose occurs, treatment is symptomatic and supportive.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep out of reach of children. Store in a cool (below 25°C) and dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin B1</strong>: Converts carbohydrates, fatty acids, and amino acids into energy. Promotes healthy nerves, improves mood, and strengthens the heart.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6</strong>: Forms RBCs, helps cells make proteins, manufactures neurotransmitters (e.g., serotonin), releases stored energy, helps prevent cardiovascular diseases and stroke, alleviates depression, and eases insomnia.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12</strong>: Essential for cell replication and RBC production. Prevents anemia, helps prevent depression, reduces nerve pain, numbness, tingling, and lowers the risk of heart diseases.</li></ul><p>These vitamin ingredients are well absorbed orally and are widely distributed to most tissues. Thiamine is not stored in the body to any significant extent, and excess amounts are excreted in urine. Pyridoxine is absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and converted to its active forms in the liver, where it is stored and then excreted as inactive metabolites in urine.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Oral tablet form is recommended. Due to the presence of benzyl alcohol, the injection form is not recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</p>', 'active', '1453_1742504643.webp', 17, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:04:03', '2025-05-03 00:27:03');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3789, 'Aristo D3', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 120.00, 1, 'x 1 ml ampoule', '1 ml ampoule', 'Injection', '200000 IU/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Aristo D3 200000 IU/ml</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated for the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also used as an adjunct to specific therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcaemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Cholecalciferol, a synthetic form of Vitamin D, is essential for normal bone growth, development, and maintaining bone density. It is also necessary for the utilization of calcium and phosphorus. Babies require Vitamin D3 for healthy growth and development. Additionally, it acts as a hormone.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is crucial for normal bone growth and maintaining bone density. Vitamin D3 also helps reduce the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, reduces the risk of cancers (e.g., breast, colorectal), and helps maintain adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Aristo D3 200000 IU/ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Capsules:Adults</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Maintenance therapy: 1,400–2,000 IU/day. Confirm target levels of 25-hydroxyvitamin D 3–4 months after starting maintenance therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 20,000 IU every 4 weeks. Higher doses may be required in specific situations.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Addition to specific therapy for osteoporosis: 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li><strong>Children (12-18 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 20,000 IU once every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>For Film-Coated Tablets:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1,000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily or as directed by the physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li><li><strong>For Syrup:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\"><strong>For patients at risk of Vitamin D deficiency:</strong>0-1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml)</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-2\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml)</blockquote><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-3\"><strong>For Vitamin D3 deficient patients:</strong>0-1 year: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 years: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong></li><li>Prevention:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Infants on Vitamin D-enriched milk: ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Nursed infants or infants not on Vitamin D-enriched milk, or children up to 5 years: 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adolescents: 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Elderly:</strong> ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Other conditions (e.g., digestive disorders, antiepileptic treatment):</strong> ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU or 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 3–6 months.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><p>It interferes with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (e.g., liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypercalcaemia</li><li>Hypercalciuria</li><li>Skin rash</li><li>Pruritus</li><li>Urticaria</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Abdominal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Studies show that doses up to 4,000 IU are safe during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU. However, higher doses may be necessary for women with Vitamin D3 deficiency.</li><li>Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. Overdose in infants due to nursing mothers has not been observed. However, additional Vitamin D3 given to the mother should be considered when prescribing it to a breastfed child.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><p>Can lead to hypervitaminosis D.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is essential for normal bone growth and maintaining bone density. It also helps to reduce bacterial infections, improve lung function, reduce the risk of cancers (e.g., breast, colorectal), and maintain insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p>', 'active', '1965_1742504810.webp', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:06:50', '2025-04-30 14:48:15'),
(3790, 'Brodil', 'Salbutamol', 23.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '2 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Brodil 100 ml</h3><p><strong>Brodil 100 ml</strong> is indicated as a bronchodilator for use in:</p><ul><li>Asthma</li><li>Chronic Bronchitis</li><li>Emphysema</li><li>Other conditions associated with airway obstruction</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p><strong>Brodil 100 ml</strong> is a selective beta2-adrenoceptor agonist. At therapeutic doses, it acts on the beta 2-adrenoceptors of bronchial smooth muscle, with little or no action on the ß1-adrenoceptors of cardiac muscle. Brodil 100 ml provides short-acting (4-6 hours) bronchodilatation with a fast onset (within 5 minutes) in reversible airways obstruction. It also has an anti-inflammatory effect on mast cells, causing inhibition of the release of bronchoconstrictor mediators including histamine, neutrophil chemotactic factor (NCF), and prostaglandin D2.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Brodil 100 ml</h3><p><strong>Oral:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong>2-6 years: 2.5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years: 5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Over 12 years: 5-10 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily (or 2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong>2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily. Maximum single dose is 8 mg tablet.</li></ul><p><strong>Injection:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Subcutaneous route:</strong> 500 mcg (8 mcg/kg) every four hours as required.</li><li><strong>Intramuscular route:</strong> 500 mcg (8 mcg/kg) every four hours as required.</li><li><strong>Slow intravenous injection:</strong> 250 mcg (4 mcg/kg), injected slowly. May be repeated if necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Population:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy of Brodil 100 ml injection in children under 12 have not been established. No recommendation on dosage can be made for this age group.</li></ul><p><strong>Nebulizer Solution:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Elderly:</strong> 0.5 ml-1.0 ml up to four times a day. Dilute the solution with sterile normal saline.</li><li><strong>Children under 12 years:</strong> 0.5 ml of the solution diluted to 2.0-4.0 ml with normal saline, repeated four times a day. Higher doses up to 1.0 ml may be required.</li></ul><p><strong>Inhalation Capsules:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> For bronchospasm relief, one or two 200 microgram capsules every 4-6 hours.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> One inhalation capsule for acute bronchospasm or maintenance, 3-4 times a day.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Brodil 100 ml is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Brodil 100 ml or any of its components.</p><h3>Side Effects of Brodil 100 ml</h3><ul><li>Fine tremor of skeletal muscles (especially hands)</li><li>Palpitations</li><li>Muscle cramps</li><li>Tachycardia</li><li>Tenseness</li><li>Headaches</li><li>Peripheral vasodilatation</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Should be used only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is not known whether Brodil 100 ml is excreted in human milk. Due to potential tumorigenicity shown in animal studies, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or the drug, considering its importance to the mother.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with hyperthyroidism, cardiovascular disease, hypertension, aneurysms, and occlusive vascular disorders.</li><li>Hypokalemia associated with high doses may increase susceptibility to digitalis-induced cardiac arrhythmias.</li><li>Tachyphylaxis may occur with prolonged use of high doses.</li><li>Care should be taken when treating patients with diabetes, closed-angle glaucoma, or those on antihypertensive therapy.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><p>Overdose symptoms include:</p><ul><li>Angina</li><li>Headache</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Tremor</li></ul><p><strong>Preferred Antidote:</strong> Cardio-selective beta-blocking agents (use with caution in patients with a history of bronchospasm).</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p><strong>Brodil 100 ml</strong> is a synthetic sympathomimetic agent with predominant beta-2 adrenergic activity. It produces bronchodilatation through the stimulation of beta-2 adrenergic receptors in bronchial smooth muscles, leading to relaxation of the bronchial muscle fibers. This action improves pulmonary function, as demonstrated by spirometric measurements.</p><p><br></p><p>Let me know if you need further details or clarification!</p>', 'active', '1883_1742504918.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:08:38', '2025-04-22 12:41:09'),
(3791, 'Esolok', 'Esomeprazole', 7.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Esolok 20 mg:</strong></p><p>Esolok 20 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li><strong>GERD-related symptoms</strong>: To alleviate persistent heartburn and other symptoms associated with GERD.</li><li><strong>Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: To treat and help heal erosive esophagitis.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: To maintain healing after the erosive esophagitis has been treated.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication</strong>: To eradicate Helicobacter pylori infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in combination with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: For managing Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</li><li><strong>Duodenal &amp; Gastric Ulcers</strong>: To treat ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Esolok 20 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. If the patient does not heal after 4-8 weeks, an additional 4-8 weeks may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily. Clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. An additional 4 weeks may be considered if symptoms do not resolve.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication (Triple Therapy)</strong>: 40 mg once daily for 10 days, combined with amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily and clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg once daily. Dosage should be adjusted individually, and treatment continued as long as clinically necessary.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: 40 mg once daily, administered by intravenous injection (minimum 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10-30 minutes).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Esolok 20 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Take one Esolok 20 mg pill or capsule <strong>one hour before a meal</strong>. Swallow the capsule whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>: Mix the contents of the packet with 15 ml of water in a glass, stir for 2-3 minutes, then wait 30 minutes before drinking. If the suspension is given via a nasogastric or gastric tube, prepare it in a syringe and administer directly.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong>: Reconstitute with 5 ml of solvent (WFI) and administer the injection gradually over 3 minutes. Use within 12 hours of reconstitution.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Esolok 20 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CYP2C19 and CYP3A4</strong> metabolize esomeprazole. It has no clinically significant interactions with drugs metabolized by CYP1A2, 2A6, 2C9, 2D6, 2E1, and 3A4.</li><li><strong>Diazepam</strong>: Esomeprazole reduces diazepam clearance by 45%, increasing plasma levels.</li><li><strong>Medications affected by stomach acid</strong>: Esomeprazole may hinder absorption of medications such as ketoconazole, iron salts, and digoxin.</li><li><strong>Clarithromycin</strong>: Co-administration increases 14-hydroxy clarithromycin plasma levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to esomeprazole or any of its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Esolok 20 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects: Headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, dry mouth.</li><li>Long-term maintenance therapy (up to 12 months) does not result in significantly different side effects compared to short-term use.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Positive symptom response does not rule out the possibility of stomach cancer.</li><li>Take capsules at least an hour before meals.</li><li>For patients who have difficulty swallowing capsules, the pellets can be mixed with applesauce.</li><li>Antacids can be used while taking esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Esolok 20 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose in rats include reduced motor activity, breathing changes, tremors, and seizures.</li><li>Overdose is rare in humans. Symptomatic and supportive care should be provided, as esomeprazole is highly protein-bound and unlikely to be removed by dialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place at temperatures below 30°C, protected from moisture and light.</li><li>Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor that works by inhibiting the H+/K+-ATPase in gastric parietal cells, reducing stomach acid output.</li><li>The systemic bioavailability of esomeprazole is about 90% with repeated daily dosing.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><ul><li>Insufficient data on the use of esomeprazole in pregnancy. Animal studies show no teratogenic effects. Breastfeeding should be discontinued if esomeprazole is deemed necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li>No significant difference in safety or efficacy between elderly and younger patients, although elderly individuals may be more sensitive to certain medications.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency:</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage adjustment is needed for mild to moderate hepatic insufficiency, but the maximum dose should not exceed 20 mg in patients with severe hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is excreted in inactive metabolites, with less than 1% of the drug excreted unchanged in urine, so renal insufficiency is unlikely to affect its pharmacokinetics.</li></ul>', 'active', '588_1742505025.jpg', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:10:25', '2025-05-02 10:23:47'),
(3792, 'Afrin', 'Oxymetazoline Hydrochloride', 65.00, 1, 'x 10 ml drop', '10 ml drop', 'Nasal Drop', '0.025%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Afrin 0.025%</strong></p><p>Oxymetazoline Hydrochloride is indicated for the treatment of nasal congestion associated with acute or chronic rhinitis, common cold, sinusitis, upper respiratory allergies, epistaxis, and hay fever. It is also used as a nasal decongestant in otitis media.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Nasal Anti-histamine Preparations, Nasal Decongestants &amp; Other Nasal Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Afrin 0.025% is an imidazoline derivative sympathomimetic amine. It is a direct agonist at α-adrenoceptors but has no action on β-adrenoceptors. It is used as a topical agent on the nasal mucosa, producing rapid and long-acting vasoconstriction of the arterioles. This reduces blood flow and diminishes swelling of the mucosa, improving airway potency and facilitating better drainage of the nasal sinuses.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Afrin 0.025%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oxymetazoline nasal spray</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children (6 years of age and older)</strong>: 2 or 3 sprays in each nostril twice daily for 3-5 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage reduction is required for nasal administration.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children below 6 years of age</strong>: Not suitable for children below 6 years.</li><li><strong>Oxymetazoline nasal drops</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; children (6 years and older)</strong>: 2-3 drops of 0.05% Nasal Drops in each nostril twice daily (morning and evening).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (2-5 years)</strong>: 2-3 drops of 0.025% Nasal Drops in each nostril twice daily (morning and evening).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Afrin 0.025%</strong></p><p>Oxymetazoline Hydrochloride Nasal preparation is recommended for intranasal use only. It should be used for no longer than 5 days.</p><p><strong>How to use Oxymetazoline nasal spray</strong>:</p><ol><li>Remove the dust cover cap.</li><li>Tilt your head slightly forward and keep the container upright while applying the nasal spray.</li><li>After each use, gently wipe the spray tip with a clean, soft cloth or tissue paper. Finally, replace the dust cover cap.</li></ol><p><strong>Interaction of Afrin 0.025%</strong></p><p>Afrin 0.025% Nasal preparation should not be given to patients being treated with MAO inhibitors or within 14 days of ceasing such treatment.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with cardiovascular disease, hyperthyroidism, prostatic enlargement, angle closure glaucoma, and hypersensitivity to any of its ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Afrin 0.025%</strong></p><p>Occasionally, it may cause local irritation and dryness of the mouth and throat. Temporary discomfort such as burning, stinging, sneezing, or increased nasal discharge may also occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of Afrin 0.025% Nasal Spray during pregnancy and lactation has not been established, but it is not thought to constitute a hazard during these periods.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Keep away from the eyes.</li><li>The spray should not be used for longer than seven days without medical advice.</li><li>Frequent or prolonged use may cause nasal congestion to recur or worsen.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Afrin 0.025%</strong></p><p>Overdose may result in local irritation and rebound congestion. Treatment should be symptomatic.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a dry place, protect from light. Do not refrigerate or freeze. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '80_1742505148.webp', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:12:28', '2025-05-02 22:51:58'),
(3793, 'Jasocaine Jelly', 'Jasocaine', 18.00, 1, 'x 5 gm tube', '5 gm tube', 'Cream', '2 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Jasocaine Jelly 2% 30 gm</strong></p><p>Lidocaine Injection is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Local anaesthesia for use in infiltration blockade and intravenous regional analgesia.</li><li>Prevention and treatment of ventricular tachyarrhythmias.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Local &amp; Surface Anaesthesia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Lidocaine primarily works by inhibiting sodium influx through sodium-specific ion channels in the neuronal cell membrane, especially the voltage-gated sodium channels. When sodium influx is blocked, an action potential cannot arise, and signal conduction is inhibited. The receptor site is believed to be located at the inner (cytoplasmic) portion of the sodium channel. Lidocaine binds more readily to sodium channels in their activated state, leading to faster onset of blockade in neurons that are rapidly firing. This is known as state-dependent blockade.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Jasocaine Jelly 2% 30 gm</strong></p><p>The dosage should be adjusted according to patient response and the site of administration.</p><p><strong>Adult Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li>The maximum individual dose should not exceed 4.5 mg/kg of body weight. The total dose should generally not exceed 300 mg. The following doses are for guidance in most routine procedures:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Infiltration-Percutaneous:</strong> Total dose 5-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intravenous Regional:</strong> Total dose 50-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Peripheral Nerve Blocks:</strong>Brachial: Total dose 225-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Dental: Total dose 20-100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Intercostal: Total dose 30 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Paravertebral: Total dose 30-50 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Pudendal (each side): Total dose 100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Paracervical Obstetrical analgesia: Total dose 100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Sympathetic Nerve Blocks:</strong>Cervical: Total dose 50 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Lumbar: Total dose 50-100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Central Neural Blocks:</strong>Epidural: Total dose 200-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Thoracic: Total dose 200-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Lumbar Analgesia: Total dose 250-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Anesthesia: Total dose 200-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Caudal Obstetrical analgesia: Total dose 200-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Surgical anesthesia: Total dose 225-300 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Jasocaine Jelly 2% 30 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Antiarrhythmic treatment:</strong> An intravenous dose of 1 mg/kg may be given over 2 minutes and repeated after 5 minutes. Alternatively, an infusion of 2-4 mg/min may be administered to achieve a plasma level of 2-4 mg/L. A loading dose of approximately 1 mg/kg is usually given intravenously over 2 minutes at the outset to avoid a delay in achieving a steady state.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Jasocaine Jelly 2% 30 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cimetidine</strong> can impair the metabolism of lidocaine, leading to delayed elimination and increased risk of adverse reactions.</li><li><strong>Beta-blockers</strong> (e.g., propranolol, metoprolol, nadolol) can significantly increase plasma lidocaine concentrations.</li><li>Lidocaine may prolong the action of <strong>suxamethonium</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to anaesthetics of the amide type.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Jasocaine Jelly 2% 30 gm</strong></p><ul><li>As with other local anaesthetics, adverse reactions are rare and are usually caused by excessively high blood concentrations due to intravascular injection, excessive dosage, rapid absorption, or hypersensitivity. Systemic effects may involve the central nervous system and/or cardiovascular system.</li><li><strong>CNS Reactions:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Excitatory or depressant effects, such as nervousness, dizziness, blurred vision, tremors, followed by drowsiness, convulsions, unconsciousness, and possibly respiratory arrest.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Reactions:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypotension, myocardial depression, bradycardia, and possibly cardiac arrest.</li><li><strong>Allergic Reactions:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Extremely rare but may include cutaneous lesions, urticaria, oedema, or anaphylactoid reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Although no animal studies show harm to the fetus, like all drugs, it should not be used during early pregnancy unless the benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li>Caution should be exercised when administering to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Lidocaine should be used only by clinicians well-versed in diagnosing and managing dose-related toxicity.</li><li>Proper dosage, technique, and precautions are critical for safety and effectiveness. Resuscitative equipment should be available for emergencies.</li><li>Special care is needed for patients with epilepsy, impaired cardiac conduction, bradycardia, impaired respiratory function, or liver disease.</li><li>The effect of local anaesthetics may be reduced when injected into inflamed or infected areas.</li><li>Repeated doses can significantly increase blood levels due to slow drug or metabolite accumulation.</li><li>Regular monitoring of cardiovascular and respiratory vital signs, along with consciousness levels, is necessary after each injection.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 25°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Pediatric dosages should be reduced based on age, body weight, and physical condition.</li><li>Elderly patients may require dose adjustments due to heightened sensitivity to systemic effects.</li></ul>', 'active', '953_1742505320.webp', 28, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:15:20', '2025-05-03 23:01:01'),
(3794, 'Sandocal', 'Elemental Calcium', 6.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sandocal 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>250 mg or 500 mg tablet</strong>: This is used for the treatment or prevention of calcium depletion in patients in whom dietary measures are inadequate. Conditions that may be associated with calcium deficiency include hypoparathyroidism, achlorhydria, chronic diarrhea, vitamin D deficiency, steatorrhea, sprue, pregnancy and lactation, menopause, pancreatitis, renal failure, alkalosis, and hyperphosphataemia. Sandocal 500 mg is being increasingly used to treat hyperphosphataemia in chronic renal failure, as well as in patients on continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis (CAPD) and haemodialysis. Some patients may require additional measures, such as dietary phosphate restriction or small doses of aluminium hydroxide, as they may be unable to tolerate sufficient doses for complete phosphate control. Sandocal 500 mg can provide short-term relief of dyspeptic symptoms but is no longer recommended for long-term treatment of peptic ulceration.</li><li><strong>1000 mg tablet</strong>: This is indicated for the management of conditions associated with hyperidity and for fast relief of acid indigestion, heartburn, sour stomach, and upset stomach.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Minerals in bone formation, Specific mineral preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Calcium carbonate reacts with gastric acid to produce a salt and water. The postulated chemical reaction for Sandocal 500 mg is:</p><p>CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂</p><p>Two grams of Sandocal 500 mg will readily bring 100 mL of hydrochloric acid to a pH above 6. This increase in gastric pH diminishes the activity of pepsin in the gastric secretion. Up to 30% of the oral calcium load may be absorbed.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sandocal 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>250 mg or 500 mg tablet</strong>:</li><li>Sandocal 500 mg is always used orally. When used as an antacid, the recommended doses for adults are equivalent to 540-2000 mg Sandocal 500 mg per day, with doses for children being half of those for adults. As a dietary supplement for the prevention of osteoporosis, the recommended dose is generally 1250-3750 mg Sandocal 500 mg (500-1500 mg calcium) daily, but it should be tailored to the individual patient depending on specific conditions such as calcium deficiency, malabsorption, or parathyroid function.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In pregnancy and lactation, the recommended daily dose of calcium is 1200-1500 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In chronic renal failure, doses may vary from 2.5-9.0 gm Sandocal 500 mg per day and should be adjusted according to the individual patient\'s needs. To maximize effective phosphate binding in this context, Sandocal 500 mg should be taken with meals.</li><li><strong>1000 mg tablet</strong>:</li><li>The recommended dose is 2000-3000 mg per tablet when symptoms occur, which may be repeated hourly if needed or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sandocal 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Sandocal 500 mg may enhance the cardiac effects of digoxin and other cardiac glycosides if systemic hypercalcaemia occurs.</li><li>Sandocal 500 mg may interfere with the absorption of concurrently administered tetracycline preparations.</li><li>In chronic renal failure, modification of vitamin D therapy may be required to avoid hypercalcaemia when Sandocal 500 mg is used as the primary phosphate binder.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypercalcaemia and hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Hypercalciuria and nephrolithiasis</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Concomitant digoxin therapy (requires careful monitoring of serum calcium level)</li><li>Calcium salts should be used cautiously in patients with sarcoidosis, renal or cardiac disease, and in patients receiving cardiac glycosides.</li><li>Discontinuation of Sandocal 500 mg is usually sufficient to return serum calcium concentrations to normal when hypercalcaemia occurs.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Sandocal 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Orally administered Sandocal 500 mg may be irritating to the GI tract and may cause constipation.</li><li>Hypercalcaemia is rare with calcium alone but may occur when large doses are given to patients with chronic renal failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Calcium-containing drugs have been widely used in pregnancy via oral calcium supplementation or antacid therapy. Sandocal 500 mg can also be used in lactating women.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place at controlled room temperature.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children</strong>: Calcium carbonate has been extensively studied in children and infants with chronic renal failure and is both safe and effective.</li><li><strong>Use in elderly</strong>: In elderly patients with renal failure, constipation may be a troublesome side effect. Monitoring of serum calcium and phosphate levels is indicated for elderly patients taking Sandocal 500 mg.</li></ul>', 'active', '1537_1742505679.webp', 57, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:21:19', '2025-05-04 20:48:15'),
(3795, 'Renova', 'Paracetamol', 3.51, 5, 'suppository', '1 suppository', 'Suppository', '60 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Renova 60 ml: Indications, Dosage, Side Effects, and More</h3><p><strong>Indications:</strong> Renova 60 ml is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Fever</li><li>Common cold and influenza</li><li>Headache, toothache, earache, bodyache, myalgia, and neuralgia</li><li>Dysmenorrhoea (menstrual pain)</li><li>Sprains and colic pain</li><li>Back pain, post-operative pain, postpartum pain, and inflammatory pain</li><li>Post-vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic &amp; osteoarthritic pain and stiffness of joints</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Renova 60 ml contains <strong>acetaminophen</strong>, a para-aminophenol derivative, known for its analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory effects. It primarily acts in the central nervous system (CNS) by inhibiting the cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes, COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3, involved in prostaglandin synthesis, which helps increase the pain threshold. It is one of the safest, fast-acting, and widely used analgesics, offering an alternative to aspirin with fewer side effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet (Adult)</strong>: 1-2 tablets every 4-6 hours, not exceeding 4 gm (8 tablets) per day.</li><li><strong>Children (6-12 years)</strong>: ½ to 1 tablet 3-4 times daily, with a maximum of 2.6 gm/day for long-term use.</li><li><strong>Extended Release Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults &amp; children over 12 years: 2 tablets every 6-8 hours (maximum of 6 tablets in 24 hours). Do not crush.</li><li><strong>Syrup/Suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Under 3 months</strong>: 10 mg/kg body weight (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced), 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>3 months to below 1 year</strong>: ½ to 1 teaspoonful, 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1-5 years</strong>: 1-2 teaspoonfuls, 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>6-12 years</strong>: 2-4 teaspoonfuls, 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 4-8 teaspoonfuls, 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Suppository</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>3-12 months</strong>: 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1-5 years</strong>: 125-250 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>6-12 years</strong>: 250-500 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; children over 12 years</strong>: 0.5-1 gm, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Paediatric Drop</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Up to 3 months</strong>: 0.5 ml (40 mg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>4 to 11 months</strong>: 1.0 ml (80 mg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1-2 years</strong>: 1.5 ml (120 mg). Do not exceed more than 5 doses daily for a maximum of 5 days.</li><li><strong>Renova 60 ml tablet with Actizorb technology</strong>: Dissolves 5 times faster, providing a fast-acting and safe analgesic with marked antipyretic properties.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children over 12</strong>: 1-2 tablets every 4-6 hours, up to 8 tablets per day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (7-11 years)</strong>: ½-1 tablet every 4-6 hours, up to 4 tablets per day. Not recommended for children under 7.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Barbiturates, tricyclic antidepressants, and alcohol may impair Renova 60 ml metabolism.</li><li>Chronic alcohol use can enhance hepatotoxicity when overdosing.</li><li>Liver enzyme-inducing drugs (e.g., anticonvulsants or oral steroids) may reduce Renova 60 ml\'s effectiveness.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Renova 60 ml.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Usually mild, but may include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hematological reactions: Thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, neutropenia, agranulocytosis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pancreatitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin rashes and allergic reactions</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong> (USFDA): Use only if necessary.</li><li>Excreted in breast milk, but normal therapeutic doses do not seem to pose a risk to the nursing infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired kidney or liver function.</li><li>Caution in patients on other liver-affecting drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, abdominal pain within the first 24 hours.</li><li>Liver damage may appear 12-48 hours after ingestion.</li><li>Possible abnormalities in glucose metabolism and metabolic acidosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Acts primarily in the CNS to increase the pain threshold by inhibiting COX enzymes involved in prostaglandin synthesis.</li></ul>', 'active', '1888_1742505795.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:23:15', '2025-05-04 15:12:29'),
(3796, 'Napa', 'Paracetamol', 20.00, 1, 'x 15 ml bottle', '15 ml bottle', 'Pediatric Drops', '80 mg/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Napa 15 ml</strong></p><p>Napa 15 ml is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Fever</li><li>Common cold and influenza</li><li>Headache</li><li>Toothache</li><li>Earache</li><li>Bodyache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Neuralgia</li><li>Dysmenorrhoea</li><li>Sprains</li><li>Colic pain</li><li>Back pain</li><li>Post-operative pain</li><li>Postpartum pain</li><li>Inflammatory pain</li><li>Post-vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic &amp; osteoarthritic pain</li><li>Stiffness of joints</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Napa 15 ml has analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity. It is primarily thought to act in the CNS by increasing the pain threshold through the inhibition of both isoforms of cyclooxygenase (COX-1, COX-2) and COX-3 enzymes involved in prostaglandin (PG) synthesis. As a para-aminophenol derivative, Napa 15 ml is widely used due to its safety, fast action, and mild side effects compared to aspirin.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Napa 15 ml</strong></p><p><em>Tablet:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 1-2 tablets every 4-6 hours, up to a maximum of 4 gm (8 tablets) daily.</li><li><strong>Children (6-12 years):</strong> ½ to 1 tablet 3-4 times daily. For long-term treatment, do not exceed 2.6 gm/day.</li></ul><p><em>Extended Release Tablet:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children (12 years+):</strong> 2 tablets every 6-8 hours (maximum of 6 tablets in 24 hours). Do not crush.</li></ul><p><em>Syrup/Suspension:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Children under 3 months:</strong> 10 mg/kg body weight, 3-4 times daily (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced).</li><li><strong>3 months to below 1 year:</strong> ½ to 1 teaspoonful, 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>1-5 years:</strong> 1-2 teaspoonfuls, 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>6-12 years:</strong> 2-4 teaspoonfuls, 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 4-8 teaspoonfuls, 3-4 times daily.</li></ul><p><em>Suppository:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Children 3-12 months:</strong> 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children 1-5 years:</strong> 125-250 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6-12 years:</strong> 250-500 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults &amp; children 12 years+:</strong> 0.5-1 gm, 4 times daily.</li></ul><p><em>Paediatric Drop:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Children under 3 months:</strong> 0.5 ml (40 mg).</li><li><strong>4 to 11 months:</strong> 1.0 ml (80 mg).</li><li><strong>1 to 2 years:</strong> 1.5 ml (120 mg). Do not exceed 5 doses daily for a maximum of 5 days.</li></ul><p><em>Napa 15 ml Tablet with Actizorb Technology:</em></p><ul><li>This version dissolves up to five times faster than standard tablets, providing fast relief. It is especially useful for patients who cannot tolerate aspirin or other analgesics.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Napa 15 ml:</strong></p><ul><li>Alcohol and certain drugs (e.g., barbiturates, tricyclic antidepressants, anticonvulsants, oral contraceptives) may affect the metabolism and effectiveness of Napa 15 ml. Alcohol increases the risk of liver toxicity in overdose.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Napa 15 ml.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Napa 15 ml:</strong></p><ul><li>Mild side effects such as skin rashes, pancreatitis, and allergic reactions. Hematological reactions like thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, pancytopenia, neutropenia, and agranulocytosis are rare but possible.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B (USFDA)</strong>: Should be used only if necessary.</li><li>Napa 15 ml is excreted in breast milk but does not pose a significant risk to the nursing infant at therapeutic doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired liver or kidney function.</li><li>Care should be taken with patients on medications affecting the liver.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Napa 15 ml:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms within the first 24 hours include pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and abdominal pain. Liver damage may appear 12-48 hours after ingestion. Metabolic issues may also occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Napa 15 ml acts mainly in the CNS to increase pain thresholds by inhibiting COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3 enzymes, leading to reduced prostaglandin synthesis and providing analgesic and antipyretic effects.</li></ul>', 'active', '1273_1742505954.png', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:25:54', '2025-05-04 00:21:56');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3797, 'Pantonix', 'Pantoprazole Sodium', 10.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pantonix 40 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Pantonix 40 mg/vial is recommended when inhibiting acid secretion is therapeutically beneficial, i.e. in:</p><ul><li>Stomach ulcer disorders</li><li>Illnesses caused by gastroesophageal reflux</li><li>Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug-induced ulcer (NSAIDs)</li><li>Helicobacter pylori eradication (in combination with antibiotics)</li><li>Syndrome of Zollinger-Ellison</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Blocker</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Pantonix 40 mg/vial is a proton pump inhibitor that works by covalently attaching to the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system on the surface of the gastric parietal cell to decrease the final stage of gastric acid generation. Regardless of the stimulus, this effect lasts for more than 24 hours and results in a suppression of both basal and induced stomach acid secretion.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Pantonix 40 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Benign gastric ulcer</strong>: 40 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, continued for a further 4 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastro-esophageal reflux disease</strong>: 20-40 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, continued for a further 4 weeks if not fully healed; maintenance dose is 20 mg daily, which may be increased to 40 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal ulcer</strong>: 40 mg daily in the morning for 2 weeks, continued for a further 2 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer associated with Helicobacter pylori</strong>: Pantonix 40 mg/vial is recommended at a dose of 40 mg twice daily in association with antimicrobial agents as detailed below:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Amoxycillin 1 g and Clarithromycin 500 mg both twice daily for one week, or</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clarithromycin 250 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg both twice daily for one week.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of NSAID-associated gastric or duodenal ulcer</strong>: 20 mg daily for those requiring long-term NSAID treatment.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: Initially 80 mg once daily, adjusted according to response (elderly max. 40 mg daily); daily doses above 80 mg given in 2 divided doses.</li><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal ulcer and gastric ulcer</strong>: 40 mg once daily for 7-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease associated with a history of erosive esophagitis</strong>: 40 mg once daily for 7-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of rebleeding in peptic ulcer</strong>: IV 80 mg, followed by 8 mg/hour infusion for 72 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration</strong>: 80 mg IV every 12 h for 24 h, followed by 40 mg every 12 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Long-term management of Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome and other pathological hypersecretory conditions</strong>: 80 mg IV every 12 hours, may increase to 80 mg every 8 hours if needed, may titrate to higher doses depending on acid output.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Pantonix 40 mg/vial</strong></p><p>In clinical investigations, no significant medication interactions were found.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those with known hypersensitivity to any of the formulation\'s ingredients should not take Pantonix 40 mg/vial.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Pantonix 40 mg/vial</strong></p><p>Both in the short-term and long-term, Pantonix 40 mg/vial is well tolerated. The most frequent adverse effects are headache and diarrhea, while abdominal pain, flatulence, rash, sleeplessness, and hyperglycemia are only rarely reported side effects.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pantonix 40 mg/vial is classified as a <strong>B</strong> pregnancy drug by the US FDA. However, there is no well-controlled research on pregnant women. This medication should only be used during pregnancy if clearly necessary, as human response to treatments is not usually predicted by studies on animal reproduction. It has been shown that Pantonix 40 mg/vial is excreted in human milk. Therefore, a decision should be made whether to discontinue breastfeeding or stop the medication, considering the benefits to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Patients should be advised not to split, chew, or crush the Pantonix 40 mg/vial pill. The use of Pantonix 40 mg/vial for an extended period of time may cause malabsorption of cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) or raise the risk of problems related to osteoporosis.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Pantonix 40 mg/vial</strong></p><p>There are no recognized signs of human overdose. Due to its strong protein binding, Pantonix 40 mg/vial is not easily dialyzable. There is no specific therapy that is advised, but symptomatic and supportive management may be necessary.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep dry and away from heat and light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p><strong>Direction for use of IV injection</strong>:</p><p>Pantonix 40 mg/vial lyophilized powder with 0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection must only be administered intravenously and under no other circumstances. A gradual intravenous dose of Pantonix 40 mg/vial should be administered. The powder-containing vial is mixed with 10 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride injection to create the IV injection solution. Upon reconstitution, the injection should be administered gradually over a minimum of 2 to 5 minutes. Use freshly made solutions. The reconstituted solution can be kept for up to 4 hours at room temperature (up to 30°C).</p><p><strong>How to administer an IV infusion</strong>:</p><p>An intravenous infusion of Pantonix 40 mg/vial should be administered over roughly 15 minutes. An IV infusion of Pantonix 40 mg/vial needs to be reconstituted with 10 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride injection before being further diluted (admixed) to a final volume of 100 ml using 0.9% sodium chloride injection, 5% dextrose, or lactated ringer\'s injection. Prior to further dilution, the reconstituted solution may be kept at room temperature (up to 30°C) for a maximum of 4 hours. The admixed solution must be used within 24 hours of the initial reconstitution and may be kept at room temperature (up to 30°C).</p>', 'active', '463_1742506093.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:28:13', '2025-05-04 10:15:59'),
(3798, 'Bontiv', 'Glucosamine Sulfate + Diacerein', 12.00, 4, 'tablet', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '750 mg+50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Bontiv 750 mg+50 mg</strong></p><p>This is primarily indicated in conditions like Osteoarthritis, Rheumatoid arthritis, Bone &amp; Joint injuries.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Stimulation of Cartilage formation</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Glucosamine is a natural substance produced in the human body and found in certain foods. Glucosamine stimulates the production of glycosaminoglycan &amp; proteoglycan, two essential building blocks of cartilage. Glucosamine acts, as a supplement, as a joint lubricant and shock absorber necessary to maintain healthy cartilage and joint function. Diacerein is an anti-inflammatory analgesic and antipyretic drug, indicated specially for the treatment of arthritic conditions. It directly inhibits IL-1 synthesis and release in vitro and down-modulates IL-1 induced activities and relieves Osteoarthritis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Bontiv 750 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult &amp; Children above 12 years</strong>: One tablet (Glucosamine Sulphate 750 mg and Diacerein 50 mg) twice daily for 4-12 weeks, taken with food.</li><li><strong>Creatinine Clearance (CrCl) &lt; 30 ml/min</strong>: The dose of Diacerein to be reduced.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Bontiv 750 mg+50 mg</strong></p><p>There have been no reports of significant drug interactions of Glucosamine with antibiotics/antidepressants/antihypertensives/nitrates/antiarrhythmics/anxiolytic/hypoglycemic agents/antisecretives. However, there is decreased absorption of Diacerein with aluminum and/or magnesium hydroxide antacids.</p><p>Increased risk of diarrhea with laxatives and antibiotics. Avoid co-administration with fibers and phytic acids.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>There are no known contraindications for Glucosamine, but proven hypersensitivity to Glucosamine is a contraindication.</li><li>Diabetics are advised to monitor blood glucose levels regularly when taking Glucosamine.</li><li>No special studies were performed in patients with renal and/or hepatic insufficiency.</li><li>Administration to patients with severe hepatic or renal insufficiency should be under appropriate medical supervision.</li><li>Children should not be supplemented with Glucosamine.</li><li>Diacerein is contraindicated in pregnancy, lactation, and hypersensitivity to anthraquinone derivatives.</li><li>Avoid in patients with allergies to shellfish (e.g., shrimp, crab), diabetes, asthma, alcohol dependence, or liver disease.</li><li>Complete blood count, liver function, and urinalysis should be monitored regularly while taking this drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Bontiv 750 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Epigastric pain</li><li>Heartburn</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Headache</li><li>Skin rashes</li><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Intense yellow coloring of urine</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Diacerein is contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation.</li><li>Glucosamine should not be taken by pregnant women and nursing mothers due to lack of scientific data regarding its effect on the fetus and infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be practiced when administering Glucosamine + Diacerein to those patients who are allergic to Glucosamine and/or Diacerein, or to shellfish (e.g., shrimp, crab); who have diabetes, asthma, alcohol dependence, or liver disease.</p><p>Before taking Glucosamine + Diacerein, complete blood count, liver function, and urinalysis should be monitored regularly.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Bontiv 750 mg+50 mg</strong></p><p>Diarrhea may occur.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool &amp; dry place, protect from light &amp; moisture.</p>', 'active', '342_1742506207.webp', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:30:07', '2025-05-03 01:00:28'),
(3799, 'Zepiron-Plus', 'Iron Polymaltose Complex + Folic Acid + Zinc + Vitamin B-Complex', 5.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', 'capsule', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Zepiron Plus: Indications, Composition, Dosage, and More</h3><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><p>Zepiron Plus is indicated for the treatment and prophylaxis of deficiencies in Zinc, Iron, Folic Acid, and B-vitamins, especially during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Composition (per capsule):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Iron BP</strong>: 47 mg (as Iron Polymaltose Complex 188 mg)</li><li><strong>Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate USP</strong>: 61.8 mg (equivalent to 22.5 mg of elemental zinc)</li><li><strong>Folic Acid BP</strong>: 0.50 mg</li><li><strong>Thiamine Hydrochloride USP</strong>: 5 mg</li><li><strong>Riboflavin BP</strong>: 2 mg</li><li><strong>Nicotinamide USP</strong>: 20 mg</li><li><strong>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride BP</strong>: 2 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Iron &amp; Vitamin Combined Preparations</p><h3>Pharmacology:</h3><ul><li><strong>Iron:</strong> Present as Iron Polymaltose Complex, a stable, non-ionic form that facilitates controlled absorption when it interacts with the mucosal cell surface.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid:</strong> Supports proper development of the baby.</li><li><strong>Zinc:</strong> Essential for enzyme activity and protein metabolism.</li><li><strong>B-Vitamins (Thiamine, Riboflavin, Pyridoxine, Nicotinamide):</strong> Vital for tissue development, energy release, and the proper functioning of various body systems.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration:</h3><ul><li><strong>Standard Dosage:</strong> One capsule daily.</li><li><strong>Severe Cases:</strong> Two capsules a day may be required or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><h3>Interaction:</h3><ul><li>No significant drug interactions have been reported.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications:</h3><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects:</h3><ul><li>Generally well tolerated, but a few allergic reactions may occur.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> It is generally advised to avoid the use of any drug during the first trimester if possible. Iron supplementation should only be administered if there is definite evidence of Iron deficiency. Supplementation of Iron, Zinc, and Folic Acid is justified during the remainder of pregnancy, particularly if dietary intake is insufficient.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Safe for use during lactation.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</h3><ul><li><strong>Iron Overload:</strong> Care should be taken in patients with conditions that predispose to iron overload, such as haemochromatosis or haemolytic anaemia.</li><li><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong> Iron chelates with tetracyclines, potentially impairing absorption.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects:</h3><ul><li><strong>Iron Overdose:</strong> Can lead to serious poisoning, especially in children under 6 years. Symptoms may include vomiting, diarrhea, acidosis, dark stools, and abdominal pain.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B Overdose:</strong> Riboflavin is safe, and Pyridoxine may cause sensory neuropathy at doses exceeding 200 mg for prolonged periods.</li><li><strong>Zinc Overdose:</strong> Chronic intake of 150–450 mg per day can lead to copper deficiency, altered iron function, immune system impairment, and reduced HDL levels.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions:</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light, and out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes:</h3><ul><li>Iron &amp; Vitamin Combined Preparations</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action:</h3><p>This preparation contains Iron Polymaltose Complex, Folic Acid, Zinc, and B-vitamins, which work synergistically to address nutritional deficiencies. The iron is more efficiently absorbed and utilized in hemoglobin synthesis. Folic Acid helps prevent neural tube defects, while Zinc supports immune function, blood formation, and wound healing. B-vitamins are critical for energy production, brain development, and maintaining healthy nervous and digestive systems.</p><h3>Recommended Use in Pregnancy:</h3><ul><li>Zepiron Plus is recommended for use during pregnancy and lactation to support the nutritional needs of both the mother and the developing baby.</li></ul>', 'active', '1121_1742506361.jpg', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:32:41', '2025-05-05 22:20:12'),
(3800, 'Mervan', 'Aceclofenac', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s your content formatted with headings in normal bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Mervan 100 mg</strong></p><p>Mervan 100 mg is indicated for the relief of pain and inflammation in osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, toothache, trauma, and lumbago.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for Osteoarthritis, Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Mervan 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Mervan 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Extended release tablet</strong>: The recommended dose in adults is one 200 mg Mervan 100 mg tablet daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Film-coated tablet</strong>: The recommended dose in adults is 100 mg, twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Mervan 100 mg</strong></p><p>No significant drug interactions have been observed, but close monitoring of patients is required when it is used with:</p><ul><li><strong>Lithium and Digoxin</strong>: May increase plasma concentration of lithium and digoxin.</li><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: May interact with the activity of diuretics.</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants</strong>: May enhance the activity of anticoagulants.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: May increase the plasma level of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Mervan 100 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to it or in whom aspirin or NSAIDs precipitate attacks of asthma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Mervan 100 mg</strong></p><p>Mervan 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of Mervan 100 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in patients with active or suspected peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding, moderate to severe hepatic impairment, and cardiac or renal impairment. Caution should also be exercised in patients suffering from dizziness or urticaria.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Mervan 100 mg in children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Drugs for Osteoarthritis, Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Mervan 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The use of Mervan 100 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Mervan 100 mg in children.</p><p>Let me know if you\'d like any further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '1402_1742506511.webp', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:35:11', '2025-05-04 00:00:37'),
(3801, 'Magmil', 'Magnesium Hydroxide', 100.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '400 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Magmil 100 ml</strong></p><p>Magmil 100 ml suspension is used in constipation, heartburn, gas, and nausea. It is also indicated in acute and long-acting constipation due to hyperacidity, peptic ulcer, or stomatitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antacid with laxative action</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Magmil 100 ml is popularly known as Milk of Magnesia. It is a mildly acting antacid and laxative. It is poorly and slowly absorbed and acts by its osmotic properties in the luminal fluid, which causes retention of fluid in the bowel. It is useful for emptying the bowel prior to surgical, radiological, and colonoscopic procedures and can help eliminate parasites following appropriate therapy and toxic material in some cases of poisoning. Magmil 100 ml is converted into Magnesium Chloride in the stomach without forming carbon dioxide.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Magmil 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>As a Laxative</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 2-4 tablespoonfuls with a full glass of water.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">6-11 years: 1-2 tablespoonfuls with a full glass of water.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2-5 years: 1-3 teaspoonfuls with a full glass of water.</li><li><strong>As an Antacid</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 1-3 teaspoonfuls (5 to 15 ml) up to 4 times daily with water.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">1-3 years: ¼ teaspoonful.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">3-6 years: ¼-½ teaspoonful.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years: ½-1 teaspoonful.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Magmil 100 ml</strong></p><p>Magnesium hydroxide can decrease the absorption of other drugs such as digoxin, mycophenolate, phosphate supplements (e.g., potassium phosphate), tetracycline antibiotics, certain azole antifungals (ketoconazole, itraconazole), and quinolone antibiotics (e.g., ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin).</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Magmil 100 ml should not be administered where the use of a laxative is contraindicated. Long-term treatment of Magmil 100 ml is contraindicated in patients with renal failure.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Magmil 100 ml</strong></p><p>Magmil 100 ml, in common with other magnesium salts, may cause diarrhea.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The drug should be avoided if possible in patients with renal and hepatic failure and in those with heart block and myocardial disease. The drug may be used cautiously in pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool &amp; dry place, protected from light. Keep all medicines out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Antacid with laxative action</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Magmil 100 ml is popularly known as Milk of Magnesia. It is a mildly acting antacid and laxative. It is poorly and slowly absorbed and acts by its osmotic properties in the luminal fluid, which causes retention of fluid in the bowel. It is useful for emptying the bowel prior to surgical, radiological, and colonoscopic procedures and can help eliminate parasites following appropriate therapy and toxic material in some cases of poisoning. Magmil 100 ml is converted into Magnesium Chloride in the stomach without forming carbon dioxide.</p>', 'active', '3697_1746375615.webp', 56, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:37:14', '2025-05-04 20:20:15'),
(3802, 'Savlon Antiseptic', 'Chlorhexidine Gluconate + Cetrimide', 1200.00, 1, 'x 5 Liter pack', '5 Liter pack', 'Anti acidity Saline', '0 mg/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Savlon Antiseptic 5 Litre</h3><ul><li>For cleansing and irrigating the skin and dirty wounds where an added surfactant effect is required.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Chlorhexidine &amp; Chloroxylenol preparations</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li><strong>Cetrimide</strong>: A quaternary ammonium antiseptic with bactericidal activity against both gram-positive and gram-negative organisms.</li><li><strong>Chlorhexidine</strong>: An antimicrobial agent active against a wide range of gram-negative and gram-positive bacteria, yeasts, dermatophyte fungi, and lipophilic viruses.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Savlon Antiseptic 5 Litre</h3><ul><li><strong>Adult Dosage</strong>: Topical application; each ml contains 30 mg of cetrimide and 3 mg of chlorhexidine. Use as directed.</li></ul><ol><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rinse the area to be cleaned with water.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Apply the minimum amount of irrigation necessary to cover the wound area and wash gently.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rinse again thoroughly.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Apply to wound as necessary and discard remaining solution after use.</li></ol><ul><li><strong>Paediatric Use</strong>: Use with care in neonates, particularly in premature infants, as chlorhexidine may cause irritation or chemical burns.</li></ul><h3>Interaction</h3><ul><li>No known drug interactions are well documented.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to either chlorhexidine or cetrimide.</li><li>Do not use to irrigate the brain, meninges, eyes, or perforated eardrum.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li>Irritative skin reactions and hypersensitivity reactions to chlorhexidine.</li><li>Cetrimide may have a prolonging effect on the wound healing process.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category not classified; the FDA has not yet specified the drug’s pregnancy category.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: It is unlikely that the product is excreted in breast milk, but rinse the nipples thoroughly before breastfeeding as a precaution.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>For external use only; not for injection, for irrigation only.</li><li>Not isotonic and is haemolytic.</li><li>Cetrimide may prolong the wound healing process.</li><li>Use only for infection control and cleansing of wounds, not for general wound management.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li>Accidental ingestion is unlikely to cause systemic effects due to poor absorption.</li><li>High concentrations could cause irritation of the gastrointestinal mucosa (gastritis).</li><li>If swallowed, wash out the mouth, drink plenty of milk or water, and seek medical advice.</li><li>Symptomatic treatment may be necessary. Seek medical attention or contact a poison control center.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 25°C and protect from light.</li><li>Single-use only. Discard unused portion.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li>Chlorhexidine &amp; Chloroxylenol preparations</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Chlorhexidine</strong>: Antiseptic and disinfectant effective against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, some viruses, and fungi. It is more active against Gram-positive bacteria and is less effective against bacterial spores.</li><li><strong>Cetrimide</strong>: A quaternary ammonium disinfectant with emulsifying and detergent properties, effective against both Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, but less effective against bacterial spores, acid-fast bacteria, viruses, and fungi.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Breastfeeding</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Chlorhexidine and cetrimide are poorly absorbed following topical application, so the potential risk is very low.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: The products are unlikely to be excreted in breast milk due to poor absorption. However, rinse nipples thoroughly before breastfeeding as a precaution.</li></ul>', 'active', '1482_1742506944.png', 14, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:42:24', '2025-05-04 20:54:17'),
(3803, 'VOLINAC', 'Diclofenac Sodium + Methyl Salicylate + Linseed Oil + Menthol', 100.00, 1, 'x 50 gm tube', '1 pack', 'Gel', '1% w/w', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information formatted with the headings in bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Volinac 50 gm</strong></p><p>Indicated for the quick relief from pain, swelling, and inflammation due to musculo-skeletal disorders such as sprains, strains, tendinitis, bursitis, hands, neck &amp; shoulder pain, sciatica, muscle stiffness, joint pain, back ache, and lumbago.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Diclofenac Diethylamine is systemically absorbed through the skin; it inhibits the enzyme cyclooxygenase, thus reducing the formation of PGE2. Moreover, it also increases the uptake of Arachidonic acid into the cellular pool. Menthol is a vasodilator. It dilates the blood vessels, produces a feeling of coolness, and produces analgesia. Methyl salicylate is a known anti-inflammatory agent.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Volinac 50 gm</strong></p><p>Approximately one-inch band of gel should be applied to the affected site three to four times daily with rubbing until the film disappears.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Volinac 50 gm</strong></p><p>Approximately one-inch band of gel should be applied to the affected site three to four times daily with rubbing until the film disappears. Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Known hypersensitivity to any part of the preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Volinac 50 gm</strong></p><p>Usually well tolerated. Extremely low frequency of hypersensitivity reactions.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of this gel has not been established during pregnancy. There are no well-controlled studies of diclofenac in pregnant women.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>For external use only. Avoid contact with the eyes. Stop use and ask a doctor if the condition worsens or does not improve within 7 days. Keep out of the reach of children. If swallowed, get medical help or contact a poison control center right away.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 25°C. Protect from light. Do not freeze. Keep out of reach of children. Close the cap tightly after each application.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p>Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Diclofenac Diethylamine is systemically absorbed through the skin; it inhibits the enzyme cyclooxygenase, thus reducing the formation of PGE2. Moreover, it also increases the uptake of Arachidonic acid into the cellular pool. Menthol is a vasodilator. It dilates the blood vessels, produces a feeling of coolness, and produces analgesia. Methyl salicylate is a known anti-inflammatory agent.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The safety of this gel has not been established during pregnancy. There are no well-controlled studies of diclofenac in pregnant women.</p>', 'active', '1762_1742507096.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:44:56', '2025-04-22 12:43:26'),
(3804, 'Flumetol', 'Salmeterol + Fluticasone Propionate', 11.00, 11, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Dry Powder Inhalation Capsule', '50 mcg+250 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Flumetol 50 mcg + 250 mcg</strong></p><p>This combination is indicated for the regular treatment of asthma in cases where the use of a combination product (long-acting β2-agonist and inhaled corticosteroid) is appropriate. It is recommended for:</p><ul><li>Patients not adequately controlled with inhaled corticosteroids and \"as needed\" inhaled short-acting β2-agonists.</li><li>Patients already adequately controlled on both inhaled corticosteroids and long-acting β2-agonists.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants</li><li>Respiratory corticosteroids</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate</strong> is a selective, long-acting beta-2 agonist used to treat asthma and other forms of diffuse airways obstruction.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong> is a corticosteroid with mainly glucocorticoid activity and has a potent anti-inflammatory effect on the lungs. It is designed to work locally in the lungs, minimizing systemic effects when used at the recommended doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Inhalation Aerosol</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily, or 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 125 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily, or 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 250 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4-12 years)</strong>: 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Capsule (For Asthma)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg or 250 µg twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric patients (4-11 years)</strong>: Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Powder in Maxhaler (For Asthma)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: One inhalation twice daily (50/100, 50/250, or 50/500).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4 years and older)</strong>: One inhalation of 50/100 twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration Instructions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Inhaler Usage</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Shake the inhaler well before each use.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Breathe out fully, hold the inhaler upright, and place it in the mouth between the teeth with lips closed around the mouthpiece.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Inhale deeply and press down on the canister to release the medicine.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hold your breath for at least 10 seconds or as long as comfortable.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Wait 1 minute between puffs if more than one inhalation is prescribed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rinse the mouth with water after use.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clean the inhaler weekly.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid non-selective and selective β-blockers in asthma patients unless necessary.</li><li>Caution when co-administering strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole, ritonavir), as it may increase systemic exposure to Fluticasone Propionate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol</strong>: Tremors, palpitations, headache, cardiac arrhythmias, muscle cramps, arthralgia, hypersensitivity reactions, and oropharyngeal irritation.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: Hoarseness, candidiasis (thrush), cutaneous hypersensitivity reactions, and rare facial or oropharyngeal edema.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The use of Flumetol during pregnancy should be considered only if the benefit outweighs the risk to the fetus.</li><li>There is insufficient experience regarding the use of Salmeterol and Fluticasone Propionate during lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution is required in patients with active or quiescent pulmonary tuberculosis or thyrotoxicosis.</li><li>Regular inhalation use is recommended even if asymptomatic.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C.</li><li>Keep away from direct sunlight, heat, and out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Long-term use in children may reduce growth velocity.</li><li>No dose adjustments are necessary for elderly patients or those with renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants</li><li>Respiratory corticosteroids</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol</strong>: Long-acting (12 hours) beta-2 agonist.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: Inhaled corticosteroid with glucocorticoid anti-inflammatory action within the lungs.</li></ul>', 'active', '1737_1742507514.jpg', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:51:54', '2025-04-18 21:57:30'),
(3805, 'Defrol', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 35.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '40000 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Osteo-D 40000 IU (Colecalciferol - Vitamin D3)</h3><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency.</li><li>Adjunct therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency.</li><li>Essential for normal bone growth and development, maintaining bone density, and facilitating calcium and phosphorus utilization.</li><li>Beneficial for babies to promote healthy growth and development.</li><li>Acts as a hormone essential for various bodily functions, including reducing bacterial infection severity, improving lung function, reducing cancer risks (breast, colorectal), and aiding in insulin regulation for type 2 diabetes.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Aids the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus.</li><li>Essential for bone growth and density maintenance.</li><li>Also helps reduce bacterial infection severity, improves lung function, reduces cancer risks, and regulates insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration:</h3><h4>For Capsules:</h4><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:Vitamin D3 Deficiency Treatment</strong>: 40,000 IU weekly for 7 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance Therapy</strong>: 1,400-2,000 IU/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adjunct to Osteoporosis Therapy</strong>: 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (12-18 years):Deficiency Treatment</strong>: 20,000 IU every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Deficiency Prevention</strong>: 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li></ul><h4>For Film-Coated Tablets:</h4><ul><li><strong>Adults and Children</strong>: 1,000 IU to 2,000 IU daily with food.</li></ul><h4>For Syrup:</h4><ul><li><strong>Risk of Deficiency (0-1 yr)</strong>: 400 IU/day (2 ml).</li><li><strong>Risk of Deficiency (&gt;1 yr)</strong>: 600 IU/day (3 ml).</li><li><strong>Deficient Patients</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>0-1 yr</strong>: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1-18 yrs</strong>: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li></ul><h4>For Injection:</h4><ul><li><strong>Prevention for Infants (Vitamin D-enriched milk)</strong>: 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) = 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li><strong>For Non-Vitamin D-enriched milk infants/children up to 5 years</strong>: 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li><strong>Adolescents &amp; Elderly</strong>: 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter or every 3 months (elderly).</li></ul><h3>Interactions:</h3><ul><li>Can interfere with medications such as phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, some laxatives (e.g., liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications:</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects:</h3><ul><li>Hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, abdominal pain.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Safe up to 4,000 IU/day. Recommended daily intake is 400 IU. Higher doses may be required in cases of deficiency.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. Overdose in infants due to nursing mothers has not been observed.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</h3><ul><li>Use cautiously in patients with impaired renal function.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects:</h3><ul><li>Hypervitaminosis D, leading to excessive calcium in the blood.</li></ul><h3>Storage:</h3><ul><li>Keep below 30ºC, protected from light and moisture, and out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Special Populations:</h3><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and efficacy for children under 12 years have not been established.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action:</h3><ul><li>Promotes calcium and phosphorus absorption and reabsorption.</li><li>Necessary for bone growth and density, with additional benefits such as improving lung function, reducing infection severity, and regulating insulin in type 2 diabetes.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you\'d like any further details on this!</p>', 'in-active', '457_1742507688.jpg', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:54:48', '2025-05-08 14:28:10'),
(3806, 'Dopadon', 'Domperidone Maleate', 3.00, 10, 'strip', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Dopadon 10 mg</strong></p><p>Dyspeptic symptom complex, often linked with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, may include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric sensation of fullness</li><li>Abdominal distension</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Belching</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Early satiety</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms can occur in various conditions, such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine. Additionally, in Parkinson\'s disease, it can manifest as dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting. For radiological studies, it can be used to speed up barium transit in follow-through radiological examinations.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Motility Stimulants</li><li>Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist</li><li>Prokinetic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Dopadon 10 mg functions as a dopamine receptor antagonist. It exerts gastroprokinetic action by inhibiting dopamine receptors found in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier means it has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Dopadon 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For adults</strong>, the recommended dosage is 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily.</li><li><strong>For children</strong>, it should be 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily.</li><li>When used for acute nausea and vomiting, the maximum treatment duration should not exceed 12 weeks. It is advisable to take this medication 15-30 minutes before a meal.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage for Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li>The maximum daily dose of Domperidone is 80 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg of body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>In cases of dyspeptic symptoms:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Adults:</strong> 10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li><strong>For Children:</strong> 0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For acute and sub-acute conditions (especially in cases of acute nausea and vomiting):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Adults:</strong> 20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li><strong>For Children:</strong> 0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>When administered rectally in the form of suppositories:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Adults (including elderly):</strong> 30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li><li><strong>For Children:</strong> The maximum daily rectal dose for children weighing between 10 to 25 kg is 30 mg. The dose may be divided throughout the day if necessary.</li></ul><p>Please note that the maximum treatment period for acute nausea and vomiting is 12 weeks.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction of Dopadon 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>The simultaneous use of anticholinergic drugs may counteract the antidyspeptic effects of domperidone.</li><li>Antacids and antisecretory drugs should not be administered concurrently with domperidone as they can reduce its oral bioavailability.</li><li>Domperidone primarily undergoes metabolism through the CYP3A4 enzyme. In vitro data suggest that coadministration with drugs significantly inhibiting this enzyme may lead to elevated plasma levels of domperidone (e.g., azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, nefazodone).</li><li>As domperidone has gastro-kinetic effects, it could potentially impact the absorption of concurrently orally administered drugs, especially sustained-release or enteric-coated formulations.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Dopadon 10 mg is contraindicated in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to this medication.</li><li>Neonates.</li><li>Gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation.</li><li>Patients with a prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Dopadon 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common side effects</strong>: Rare reports of transient intestinal cramps.</li><li><strong>Extrapyramidal symptoms</strong>: Rare among young children and exceptionally rare in adults.</li><li><strong>Neuroendocrine effects</strong>: Potential increase in plasma prolactin levels, leading to galactorrhea and gynecomastia.</li><li><strong>Allergic reactions</strong>: Occasional rash and urticaria.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The safety of using Dopadon 10 mg during pregnancy has not been established, so its use is not recommended during pregnancy.</li><li>Domperidone may induce galactorrhea and enhance post-natal lactation. It is excreted in breast milk in minimal quantities, which are insufficient to be considered harmful.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with care in children, as there is an increased risk of extrapyramidal reactions.</li><li>Caution should be exercised in patients with hepatic impairment since domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Dopadon 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions (especially in children).</li><li><strong>Management</strong>: Administer activated charcoal and closely monitor the patient. Anticholinergic drugs, antiparkinson drugs, or antihistamines with anticholinergic properties may help manage extrapyramidal reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Motility Stimulants</li><li>Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist</li><li>Prokinetic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Dopadon 10 mg acts as a dopamine antagonist, primarily by blocking dopamine receptors located in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its gastroprokinetic effect is achieved by inhibiting dopamine receptors that influence the motility of the gastrointestinal tract. Because it has limited ability to penetrate the blood-brain barrier, Domperidone has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Infants</strong>: Use with caution as the blood-brain barrier is not fully developed.</li><li><strong>Liver disorders</strong>: Caution in patients with hepatic impairment due to extensive metabolism in the liver.</li><li><strong>Kidney disorders</strong>: In patients with severe renal insufficiency, dosing frequency should be reduced, and the dose may need adjustment.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1237_1742507806.jpg', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:56:46', '2025-03-21 02:56:46');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3807, 'Lanso D', 'Dexlansoprazole', 18.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '60 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is a more concise and organized version of the information provided, with the headings in bold:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Lanso D 60 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis (EE):</strong> Lanso D 60 mg is indicated for the healing of all grades of erosive esophagitis for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> Lanso D 60 mg is indicated to maintain healing of EE and relief of heartburn for up to 6 months.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive GERD:</strong> Lanso D 60 mg is indicated for the treatment of heartburn associated with symptomatic non-erosive gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) for 4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Lanso D 60 mg is a delayed-release capsule and the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole. It inhibits gastric acid secretion by blocking the (H+/K+)-ATPase in gastric parietal cells, which is the final step of acid production. It is formulated as a Dual Delayed Release (DDR) capsule containing two types of enteric-coated granules with different pH-dependent dissolution profiles.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Lanso D 60 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 60 mg once daily for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis &amp; Relief of Heartburn:</strong> 30 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive GERD:</strong> 30 mg once daily for 4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Lanso D 60 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Lanso D 60 mg can be taken without regard to food. The capsule should be swallowed whole. Alternatively, the capsule can be opened, and the granules sprinkled on one tablespoon of food and swallowed immediately. Do not chew the granules. If a dose is missed, take it as soon as possible, unless it\'s close to the next dose, in which case only the prescribed dose should be taken.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With Medicines:</strong> Atazanavir, Warfarin, Tacrolimus, Clopidogrel, Methotrexate.</li><li><strong>With Food/Others:</strong> No specific data available.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and flatulence.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>There are no adequate or well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Lanso D 60 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the expected benefits outweigh the potential risks.</li><li>It is unknown whether Lanso D 60 mg is excreted in human milk, but it is excreted in rat milk. Caution is advised when used in nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Gastric malignancy, Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea, bone fractures, hypomagnesemia, and caution with concomitant use of Methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>No significant overdose reports. Multiple doses of 120 mg and a single dose of 300 mg did not result in death or severe adverse events.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a dry place, protected from light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics:</strong> No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics:</strong> Safety and effectiveness have not been established in patients under 12 years.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No adjustment needed for mild impairment. A maximum dose of 30 mg daily is recommended for moderate impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Lanso D 60 mg suppresses gastric acid secretion by inhibiting the (H+/K+)-ATPase in gastric parietal cells, which blocks the final step of acid production. It is metabolized in the liver and excreted via urine.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><ul><li>Category B. No adequate studies in pregnant women. Use only if benefits outweigh the risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and effectiveness not established for patients under 12 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage adjustment necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage adjustment needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong></p><ul><li>No adjustment needed for mild impairment. For moderate impairment, limit to 30 mg daily.</li></ul><p>This summary ensures clarity and organization for better comprehension. Let me know if you need further details!</p>', 'active', '317_1742507938.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 02:58:58', '2025-04-18 21:35:35'),
(3808, 'Corabon-D', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 10.03, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg plus 200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the text with headings in bold as requested:</p><p><strong>Indications of Corabon-D</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment &amp; prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, disorders of osteogenesis. Also used as a supplement in case of inadequate intake of Calcium in childhood diet, rickets, pregnancy &amp; lactation, elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis, phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p>Or</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 200 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate Calcium in the blood is vital to a wide variety of bodily functions that our internal biochemistry will not tolerate a deficiency even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that Calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of Osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is also essential for healthy bones as it aids in Calcium absorption from the GI tract. In addition to this, it stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies also show that Calcium and Vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth as well as in Osteoporosis and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Corabon-D</strong></p><p>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night or as directed by the Physician.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Corabon-D</strong></p><p>One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or as directed by the physician. Taking it with a full stomach ensures better absorption.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Corabon-D</strong></p><ul><li>Oral Calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline &amp; fluoride preparations, and a minimum of 3 hours should be allowed between ingestion of these medications.</li><li>Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of Calcium.</li><li>Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce the metabolism of Vitamin D3.</li><li>Concomitant ingestion of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of Calcium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to any of the components, hypocalcemia resulting from overdose of Vitamin D3, hyperparathyroidism, bone metastases, severe renal insufficiency, severe hypercalciuria, renal calculi, etc.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Corabon-D</strong></p><p>Flatulence, diarrhoea, constipation, upper GI discomfort, etc., are rare manifestations. Hypercalcaemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It should be used as directed by the physician during Pregnancy and Lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with reduction of dose may be needed. Plasma and serum Calcium levels should be monitored in mild to moderate renal impairment and also in case of long-term use. Patients with renal stones or with such a history should also take precautions.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Corabon-D</strong></p><p>At high doses, it may result in nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc. Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>This is a Calcium and Vitamin D3 preparation where Calcium Carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The Calcium Carbonate from Coral has a chemical structure that is very similar to the composition of human bone. Coral Calcium is similar to other sources but ensures better absorption. Vitamin D3 aids in the absorption of Calcium from the GI tract and helps to maintain Calcium balance in the body.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of the physician.</p>', 'active', '810_1742508192.webp', 58, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 03:03:12', '2025-05-03 01:36:51'),
(3809, 'Halotaz', 'Halobetasol Propionate + Tazarotene', 600.00, 1, 'x 45 gm tube', '45 gm tube', 'Lotion', '0.01 percent plus 0.045 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Halotaz 0.01% + 0.045%</strong></h3><p>This lotion is a combination of halobetasol propionate and tazarotene, indicated for the topical treatment of plaque psoriasis in adults.</p><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Other Topical Corticosteroids</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Corticosteroids:</strong> Play a role in cellular signaling, immune function, inflammation, and protein regulation. However, the precise mechanism of action in plaque psoriasis is unknown.</li><li><strong>Tazarotene:</strong> A retinoid prodrug that is converted to its active form, tazarotenic acid, by deesterification. Tazarotenic acid binds to all three members of the retinoic acid receptor (RAR) family: RARα, RARβ, and RARγ, with relative selectivity for RARβ and RARγ. The clinical significance of this for treating plaque psoriasis is unknown.</li><li><strong>Vasoconstrictor Assay:</strong> In healthy subjects, this lotion showed high to super-high potency in comparison to other topical corticosteroids.</li><li><strong>HPA Axis Suppression Study:</strong> 3 out of 20 subjects (15%) showed HPA axis suppression at Week 4, but none (0%) showed suppression at Week 8 in a study where the lotion was applied daily for 8 weeks.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li>Apply a thin layer once daily to the affected areas and rub in gently.</li><li>If a bath or shower is taken before application, ensure the skin is dry before applying the lotion.</li><li>Do not exceed approximately 50 g per week to avoid potential suppression of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis.</li><li>Avoid occlusive dressings unless directed by a physician.</li><li>Discontinue treatment once control is achieved.</li><li>Avoid use on the face, groin, or axillae.</li><li>Not for oral, ophthalmic, or intravaginal use.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> This lotion is contraindicated in pregnancy due to potential fetal harm.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Common side effects include redness, itching, swelling, burning, stinging, application site pain, folliculitis, skin thinning (atrophy), peeling, and rash.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Based on animal studies, retinoid pharmacology, and the potential for systemic absorption, this lotion may cause fetal harm and is contraindicated during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> There are no data on the presence of tazarotene, halobetasol propionate, or their metabolites in human milk, nor the effects on breastfed infants or milk production.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Teratogenic Risk:</strong> Tazarotene is a teratogenic substance. Females of reproductive potential should have a negative pregnancy test within 2 weeks prior to treatment and use effective contraception during treatment.</li><li><strong>HPA Axis Suppression:</strong> Reversible HPA axis suppression may occur, with potential glucocorticosteroid insufficiency during or after treatment.</li><li><strong>Systemic Effects of Corticosteroids:</strong> These can include Cushing\'s syndrome, hyperglycemia, and glucosuria.</li><li><strong>Photosensitivity &amp; Risk for Sunburn:</strong> Avoid exposure to sunlight, sunlamps, and extreme weather conditions. Caution should be used if the patient is taking drugs known to be photosensitizers.</li><li><strong>Ophthalmic Adverse Reactions:</strong> Topical corticosteroids may increase the risk of cataracts and glaucoma.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li>The safety and effectiveness of this lotion in pediatric patients under the age of 18 have not been evaluated.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><ul><li>Other Topical Corticosteroids</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Corticosteroids:</strong> Help with cellular signaling, immune function, inflammation, and protein regulation, though their exact mechanism in plaque psoriasis is unclear.</li><li><strong>Tazarotene:</strong> A retinoid that, when converted to tazarotenic acid, binds to RAR receptors to potentially modify gene expression.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></h3><ul><li>The safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients under the age of 18 years have not been evaluated.</li></ul>', 'active', '1512_1742508401.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 03:06:41', '2025-05-03 18:47:41'),
(3810, 'ZnF', 'Folic Acid + Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate', 2.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of ZnF 5 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p>This tablet is indicated for the treatment and prophylaxis of Folic Acid and Zinc deficiencies.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zinc is an essential element in human health with a wide range of roles. It works as a coenzyme for more than 200 enzymes. These enzymes are involved in nucleic acid and protein metabolism and the production of energy. Zinc is vital for growth and development. Zinc deficiency can result in growth retardation, immune dysfunction, increased incidence of infections, anorexia, weight loss, neural tube defects of the fetus, increased risk for abortion, and mental lethargy. Folic Acid is a member of the B-vitamin group. Folic Acid participates in several key biological processes, including the synthesis of DNA, RNA, and proteins. It is necessary for DNA replication and is involved in the regulation of gene expression. Folic Acid deficiency in pregnant women results in an increased incidence of neural tube defects, including meningomyelocele (e.g., spina bifida), anencephaly, meningocele, and craniorachischisis in their neonates. Folic Acid deficiency leads to megaloblastic anemia. Other symptoms and signs of Folic Acid deficiency include weakness, fatigue, irritability, headache, difficulty concentrating, palpitations, etc.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of ZnF 5 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p>One tablet daily or as recommended by the physician.</p><p><strong>Dosage of ZnF 5 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p>Orally 1 tablet daily or as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of ZnF 5 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p>A large amount of Calcium decreases the absorption of Zinc. In the case of Folic Acid, no drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Zinc is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to Zinc. Folic Acid is contraindicated in untreated cobalamin deficiency.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of ZnF 5 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p>Folic Acid &amp; Zinc are well tolerated in the recommended dose. Occasionally, gastrointestinal disturbances like abdominal pain, dyspepsia, nausea, vomiting, fever, and respiratory distress may occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Recommended during pregnancy &amp; lactation.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of ZnF 5 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p>Zinc Sulfate is corrosive in overdose. Symptoms are corrosion and inflammation of the mucous membrane of the mouth and stomach.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This tablet is a special preparation of Folic Acid &amp; Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate. Zinc is an essential trace element required for human nutrition and involved in a number of body enzyme systems. Severe Zinc deficiency causes skin lesions, alopecia, diarrhea, increased susceptibility to infections, cognitive impairment, etc. Folic Acid is a member of the B-Vitamins group. It is reduced in the body to tetrahydrofolate, which is the coenzyme for various metabolic processes, including the synthesis of purine and pyrimidine nucleotides, and hence the synthesis of DNA. It is also involved in some amino acid conversions. Deficiency of Folic Acid may cause megaloblastic anemia, which develops when the dietary intake is inadequate, as in malnutrition, from malabsorption, from increased utilization as in pregnancy or conditions such as hemolytic anemia, and as a result of the administration of folate antagonists.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Recommended during pregnancy &amp; lactation.</p>', 'active', '1843_1742508512.webp', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 03:08:32', '2025-04-19 07:08:38'),
(3811, 'Tamen Turbo', 'Paracetamol + Caffeine', 3.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg plus 65 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information with the heading in bold text as requested:</p><p><strong>Indications of Tamen Turbo 500 mg+65 mg</strong></p><p>In the situation described below, Tamen Turbo 500 mg+65 mg is indicated:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Migraine</li><li>Toothache</li><li>Neuralgia</li><li>Feverishness</li><li>Period pain</li><li>Sore throat</li><li>Backache</li><li>Help to reduce the temperature</li><li>Aches and pain of colds and flu</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Tamen Turbo 500 mg+65 mg is the result of mixing caffeine with paracetamol (acetaminophen). It has antipyretic and analgesic qualities. The analgesic action of paracetamol is enhanced by caffeine. One of the most popular and safest analgesics and antipyretics is paracetamol. It acts as an analgesic by lowering the pain threshold and as an antipyretic by affecting the hypothalamus heat regulatory area. An alkaloid similar to theophylline and Xanthine, caffeine is a stimulant. Caffeine improves the solubility and transmembrane permeability of paracetamol through intermolecular interaction with it. Caffeine also raises one\'s pain tolerance and pain threshold. Another advantage of caffeine for the treatment of migraine and headaches is its inherent ability to increase cerebral blood vessel tone.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Tamen Turbo 500 mg+65 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult dose</strong>: 1-2 tablets every 4-6 hours. Maximum dose: 8 tablets daily.</li><li><strong>Child dose</strong>: Not recommended for children below 12 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Tamen Turbo 500 mg+65 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Anticonvulsants may lower serum levels (e.g. phenytoin, barbiturates, carbamazepine).</li><li>Warfarin and other coumarins\' anticoagulant effects may be strengthened by extended use.</li><li>Increased absorption of domperidone with metoclopramide.</li><li>Probenecid may lead to an increase in serum levels.</li><li>Chloramphenicol serum concentrations might rise.</li><li>Colestyramine within 1 hour of administration may decrease absorption.</li><li>Phenothiazine may significantly increase hypothermia.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those with severe renal function impairment and hepatic illness should not use paracetamol (Viral Hepatitis). Known sensitivity to caffeine or aspirin.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Tamen Turbo 500 mg+65 mg</strong></p><p>Although hematological responses such as thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, pancytopenia, neutropenia, and agranulocytosis have been described, paracetamol side effects are typically moderate. There are sporadic cases of pancreatitis, skin rashes, and other adverse responses.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Doctors should be consulted before pregnant women take caffeine and paracetamol. It is safe to take caffeine and paracetamol during breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The following situations necessitate cautious administration of caffeine and paracetamol:</p><ul><li>While taking additional hepatotoxic medications by patients with hepatic or renal impairment.</li><li>Avoid using the medicine for an extended period of time without first consulting a doctor.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Tamen Turbo 500 mg+65 mg</strong></p><p>Pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and stomach discomfort are the initial 24-hour paracetamol overdose symptoms. 12 to 40 hours after intake, liver damage might be noticed. There may be metabolic acidosis and abnormalities in glucose metabolism.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep away from light and moisture in a cool, dry location. Keep all medications out of the children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Tamen Turbo 500 mg+65 mg contains both caffeine and paracetamol. The antipyretic and analgesic effects of paracetamol are combined with a negligible anti-inflammatory effect. A xanthine derivative that resembles theophylline, caffeine is an alkaloid. Caffeine improves the solubility and transmembrane permeability of paracetamol through intermolecular interaction with it. Coffee also raises one\'s pain tolerance and pain threshold. Another advantage of caffeine for treating migraines and headaches is its inherent ability to increase brain vascular tone.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Doctors should be consulted before pregnant women take caffeine and paracetamol. It is safe to take caffeine and paracetamol during breastfeeding.</p>', 'in-active', '873_1742508657.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 03:10:57', '2025-05-12 20:55:14'),
(3812, 'Naprosyn Plus', 'Naproxen Sodium + Esomeprazole Magnesium', 17.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '375 mg+20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Naprosyn Plus 375 mg + 20 mg</strong></p><p>It is indicated for the relief of signs and symptoms of osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis, and to decrease the risk of developing gastric ulcers in patients at risk of developing NSAID-associated gastric ulcers.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Naprosyn Plus is a preparation of Naproxen and Esomeprazole.</p><ul><li><strong>Naproxen</strong> is a NSAID with analgesic and antipyretic activities. It inhibits prostaglandin synthesis.</li><li><strong>Esomeprazole</strong> is a proton pump inhibitor that suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the H+/K+ ATPase in the gastric parietal cell. By acting specifically on the proton pump, Esomeprazole blocks the final step in acid production, thus reducing gastric acidity.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Naprosyn Plus 375 mg + 20 mg</strong></p><p>The recommended dose is one tablet twice daily.</p><ul><li>Do not split, chew, crush, or dissolve the tablet.</li><li>This tablet is to be taken at least 30 minutes before meals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Naprosyn Plus 375 mg + 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ACE Inhibitors, Diuretics, and Beta-blockers</strong>: May reduce the antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin</strong>: Increases the risk of nephrotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Lithium</strong>: Increases plasma level of lithium.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: May increase the serum levels of methotrexate.</li><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>: May increase the risk of bleeding complications.</li><li><strong>Digoxin</strong>: May increase digoxin toxicity.</li><li><strong>Clopidogrel</strong>: May decrease the exposure to the active metabolite of clopidogrel.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any components of this product. It is also contraindicated in patients who have experienced asthma, urticaria, or allergic-type reactions after taking aspirin or other NSAIDs and for the treatment of per-operative pain in the setting of coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Naprosyn Plus 375 mg + 20 mg</strong></p><p>The most common side effects are:</p><ul><li>Gastritis</li><li>Erosive gastritis</li><li>Dyspepsia</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Gastric ulcer</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of this tablet should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Fatal cardiovascular thrombotic events like myocardial infarction, stroke, hypertension, congestive heart failure, and edema.</li><li>Serious GI adverse events.</li><li>Hypomagnesaemia.</li><li>Renal or hepatic insufficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '842_1742508770.webp', 10, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 03:12:50', '2025-05-04 21:21:56'),
(3813, 'Ratinol Forte', 'Vitamin A', 1.93, 15, 'capsule', '15 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50000 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you provided in a clearer format with proper headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Ratinol Forte 50000 IU</strong></p><ul><li>Illness due to vitamin A deficiency in ophthalmology, such as night blindness, xerophthalmia.</li><li>Dermatological issues such as changes in skin, hair, and nails.</li><li>Concomitant therapy for mucosal illnesses, such as sinusitis and bronchitis.</li><li>In conditions like acne vulgaris, ichthyosis, Darier\'s disease, and psoriasis.</li><li>To meet vitamin A demand in growth, resistance to infections, and night blindness.</li><li>To address vitamin A deficiency after diarrhea.</li><li>Prophylaxis for measles.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin A preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Ratinol Forte 50000 IU plays an essential role in the function of the retina and is essential for growth and differentiation of epithelial tissue.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For severe deficiency with corneal changes:</strong> 500,000 IU/day for 3 days, followed by 50,000 IU/day for 2 weeks, then 10,000-20,000 IU/day for 2 months as follow-up therapy.</li><li><strong>For cases without corneal changes:</strong> 10,000-25,000 IU/day until clinical improvement occurs (usually within 1-2 weeks).</li><li><strong>For Adults:</strong> 50,000 IU - 100,000 IU daily, up to 200,000 IU if necessary.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Children (Above 1 year):</strong>Night blindness, Bitot\'s spots, Xerophthalmia: 200,000 IU on the 1st day, 2nd day, and 14th day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Measles: 200,000 IU on the 1st day, 2nd day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diarrhea, Respiratory tract infection: 200,000 IU every time after disease.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe malnutrition: 200,000 IU as a single dose or as directed by the registered physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li>Decreased absorption with neomycin.</li><li>Increased risk of hypervitaminosis A with synthetic retinoids like acitretin, isotretinoin, and tretinoin.</li><li>Increased risk of toxicity when used with alcohol.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypervitaminosis of vitamin A.</li><li>Sensitivity to any of the ingredients in this preparation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Ratinol Forte 50000 IU intoxication includes irritability, vomiting, loss of appetite, headache, dry and pruritic skin, skin desquamation, fatigue, pain in ankles and feet, myalgia, loss of body hair, papilledema, nystagmus, liver sclerosis, and cirrhosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category A:</strong> Adequate and well-controlled human studies have failed to demonstrate a risk to the fetus in the first trimester of pregnancy (and there is no evidence of risk in later trimesters).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Precautions:</strong>Ensure Ratinol Forte 50000 IU has a free interval after long-term therapy with vitamin A.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">No daily dose over 5,000 IU during pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Doses over 50,000 IU should only be taken under medical supervision.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 25°C temperature, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin-A preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Beta-carotene, retinol, and retinal have effective and reliable vitamin A activity.</li><li>Retinal combines with the rod pigment, opsin, in the retina to form rhodopsin, necessary for visual dark adaptation.</li><li>Ratinol Forte 50000 IU prevents growth retardation and preserves the integrity of epithelial cells.</li><li>It is absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and transported in the blood to the liver.</li><li>Ratinol Forte 50000 IU is stored in the liver, mainly as retinol palmitate.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy (continued)</strong></p><ul><li>The safety of amounts exceeding 6,000 IU of vitamin A daily during pregnancy has not been established. Excessive vitamin A may cause fetal harm, including malformations of the central nervous system, the eye, the palate, and the urogenital tract.</li><li>Ratinol Forte 50000 IU in excess of the recommended dietary allowance is contraindicated in pregnant women or those who may become pregnant.</li><li>The U.S. Recommended Daily Allowance (RDA) of vitamin A for nursing mothers is 5,000 IU.</li></ul><p>This version is more organized and highlights key information under bold headings. Let me know if you need further clarification!</p>', 'active', '1656_1742508887.webp', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 03:14:47', '2025-05-04 15:06:44'),
(3814, 'Rynex', 'Oxymetazoline Hydrochloride', 40.00, 1, 'x 10 ml drop', '10 ml drop', 'Nasal Drop', '0.025%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rynex 200 Metered</strong></p><p>Oxymetazoline Hydrochloride is indicated for the treatment of nasal congestion associated with acute or chronic rhinitis, common cold, sinusitis, upper respiratory allergies, epistaxis, and hay fever. It is also used as a nasal decongestant in otitis media.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Nasal Anti-histamine preparations</li><li>Nasal Decongestants &amp; Other Nasal Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rynex 200 Metered is an imidazoline derivative sympathomimetic amine. It is a direct agonist at α-adrenoceptors but has no action on β-adrenoceptors. It is used as a topical agent on the nasal mucosa, producing rapid and long-acting vasoconstriction of the arterioles, thus reducing blood flow and diminishing swelling of the mucosa. This results in improved potency of the airway and better drainage of the nasal sinus.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Rynex 200 Metered</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oxymetazoline Nasal Spray</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults and children (6 years of age and older): 2 or 3 sprays in each nostril twice daily for 3-5 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Elderly: No dosage reduction necessary for nasal administration.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children below 6 years: Not suitable for children below 6 years of age.</li><li><strong>Oxymetazoline Nasal Drop</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults &amp; children 6 years of age: 2-3 drops of 0.05% Nasal Drops in each nostril twice daily (morning and evening).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 2-5 years of age: 2-3 drops of 0.025% Nasal Drops in each nostril twice daily (morning and evening).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Rynex 200 Metered</strong></p><p>Oxymetazoline Hydrochloride Nasal preparation is recommended for intranasal use only. It should not be used for longer than 5 days.</p><p><strong>How to Use Oxymetazoline Nasal Spray</strong></p><ol><li>Remove the dust cover cap.</li><li>Tilt your head slightly forward and keep the container upright during application, then use the nasal spray.</li><li>After each use, gently wipe the spray tip with a clean &amp; soft cloth or tissue paper. Finally, replace the dust cover cap.</li></ol><p><strong>Interaction of Rynex 200 Metered</strong></p><p>Rynex 200 Metered Nasal preparation should not be given to patients being treated with MAO inhibitors or within 14 days of ceasing such treatment.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Cardiovascular disease</li><li>Hyperthyroidism</li><li>Prostatic enlargement</li><li>Angle closure glaucoma</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any of its ingredients</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Rynex 200 Metered</strong></p><p>Occasionally, it may cause local irritation and dryness of the mouth and throat. Temporary discomfort such as burning, stinging, sneezing, or increased nasal discharge may occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of Rynex 200 Metered Nasal Spray during pregnancy and lactation has not been established, but it is not thought to constitute a hazard during these periods.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Keep away from the eyes.</li><li>The spray should not be used for longer than seven days without medical advice.</li><li>Frequent or prolonged use may cause nasal congestion to recur or worsen.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rynex 200 Metered</strong></p><p>Overdose may result in local irritation and rebound congestion. Treatment should be symptomatic.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C in a dry place, protect from light. Do not refrigerate or freeze. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1991_1746376712.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 03:18:18', '2025-05-06 08:53:57'),
(3815, 'Seclo', 'Omeprazole', 9.00, 9, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Seclo 40 mg</strong></p><p>Seclo 40 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcers.</li><li>Gastric and duodenal ulcers caused by NSAIDs.</li><li>Prevention of duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by NSAIDs in individuals with a history of such ulcers.</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD).</li><li>Long-term care of acid reflux disease.</li><li>Dyspepsia caused by acid.</li><li>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers.</li><li>Prevention of aspiration of acid during general anesthesia.</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>Peptic ulcers caused by Helicobacter pylori.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Seclo 40 mg is a benzimidazole derivative that acts as an inhibitor of stomach acid secretion. It works by inhibiting the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in the gastric parietal cells, thus preventing the release of gastric acid.</p><ul><li>After oral administration, the antisecretory effect begins within 1 hour, peaks at 2 hours, and lasts for up to 72 hours.</li><li>Secretory activity gradually resumes after stopping the medication, over 3 to 5 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Seclo 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral</strong>:</li><li><strong>Benign gastric and duodenal ulcer</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">20 mg once daily for 4 weeks in duodenal ulcer, 8 weeks in gastric ulcer.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">For severe or recurrent cases, increase to 40 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Maintenance dose for recurrent duodenal ulcers: 20 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prevention of relapse in duodenal ulcer: 10-20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>NSAID-associated ulcers</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, extend for 4 more weeks if not fully healed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg once daily for prophylaxis in patients with a history of NSAID-associated ulcers.</li><li><strong>GERD</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, extend for 4-8 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">40 mg once daily for 8 weeks in GERD refractory to other treatments.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Acid-related dyspepsia</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10-20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Prevention of acid aspiration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">40 mg the evening before surgery, then 40 mg 2-6 hours before surgery.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initially, 60 mg once daily; usual range: 20-120 mg daily (if more than 80 mg, divide the dose).</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication regimen</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg twice daily with antimicrobial agents (Amoxicillin 500 mg, Metronidazole 400 mg, Clarithromycin 250 mg).</li><li><strong>Pediatric use</strong>:</li><li>For severe ulcerating reflux esophagitis (children &gt;1 year):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Body weight 10-20 kg: 10-20 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Body weight &gt;20 kg: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>:</li><li><strong>Prevention of acid aspiration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">40 mg intravenously, one hour before surgery.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer, gastric ulcer, or reflux esophagitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">40 mg once daily if oral medication is not appropriate.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initially, 60 mg intravenously daily; dose may be increased and divided if necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Seclo 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Seclo 40 mg lyophilized powder should be reconstituted with 10 ml of water for injection and administered slowly over 2-5 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Use the reconstituted solution within 4 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For IV infusion, dissolve the contents in 100 ml of saline or 5% dextrose and administer over 20-30 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Use saline solution within 12 hours, and dextrose within 6 hours. Do not mix with other medications.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Seclo 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>May increase the levels of warfarin, phenytoin, and diazepam, requiring dosage adjustments.</li><li>No significant interaction with theophylline, propranolol, or antacids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>History of hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Seclo 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Most individuals tolerate Seclo 40 mg well.</li><li>Mild side effects like nausea, stomach cramping, paresthesia, dizziness, and headache are rare and usually do not require dose adjustments.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Category C (FDA).</li><li>No adverse effects have been reported in pregnancy, though its entry into breast milk is unknown. If necessary, discontinue breastfeeding during Seclo 40 mg treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Rule out gastric cancer before starting therapy for suspected stomach ulcers, as the medication may mask symptoms and delay diagnosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Seclo 40 mg inhibits stomach acid production by blocking the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system in the gastric parietal cells.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Category C, with no known negative impact based on studies, though breastfeeding should be discontinued if the drug is used.</p>', 'active', '1370_1742509195.jpg', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 03:19:55', '2025-05-04 20:59:19'),
(3816, 'Proceptin', 'Omeprazole', 5.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Proceptin 20 mg:</h3><p>Proceptin 20 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal and gastric ulcers</strong></li><li><strong>Gastric and duodenal ulcers caused by NSAIDs</strong></li><li><strong>Prevention of duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by NSAIDs in individuals with a history of such ulcers</strong></li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</strong></li><li><strong>Long-term management of acid reflux disease</strong></li><li><strong>Acid-induced dyspepsia</strong></li><li><strong>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers</strong></li><li><strong>Prevention of acid aspiration during general anesthesia</strong></li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong></li><li><strong>Peptic ulcers caused by Helicobacter pylori</strong></li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class:</h3><ul><li><strong>Proton Pump Blocker</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology:</h3><p>Proceptin 20 mg is a benzimidazole derivative that inhibits stomach acid secretion. It works by blocking the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in the gastric parietal cells, preventing the release of gastric acid.</p><ul><li><strong>Onset of action</strong>: The antisecretory effect starts within an hour.</li><li><strong>Peak effect</strong>: Peaks in about two hours.</li><li><strong>Duration</strong>: The inhibition lasts up to 72 hours.</li><li><strong>Secretory activity</strong>: Resumes gradually after stopping the medication over 3 to 5 days.</li></ul><h3>Dosage of Proceptin 20 mg:</h3><h4>Oral:</h4><ul><li><strong>Benign gastric and duodenal ulcers</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg once daily for 4 weeks (duodenal ulcer), 8 weeks (gastric ulcer).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For severe or recurrent cases: 40 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance: 20 mg once daily for recurrent duodenal ulcers.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prevention: 10-20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>NSAID-associated duodenal or gastric ulcer</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continued for a further 4 weeks if not healed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prophylaxis: 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Gastro-esophageal reflux disease (GERD)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continued for 4-8 weeks if not healed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Refractory GERD: 40 mg once daily for 8 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance: 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Long-term acid reflux disease management</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10-20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Acid-related dyspepsia</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10-20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Prevention of acid aspiration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">40 mg the evening before surgery, then 40 mg 2-6 hours before surgery.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Start with 60 mg once daily; usual range 20-120 mg daily (doses &gt; 80 mg should be divided into 2 doses).</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication in peptic ulcer</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg twice daily with antibiotics for 1 week.</li></ul><h4>Pediatric Use (Severe ulcerating reflux esophagitis):</h4><ul><li><strong>Child weight 10-20 kg</strong>: 10-20 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li><li><strong>Child weight &gt; 20 kg</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li></ul><h4>IV Injection:</h4><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">40 mg IV slowly, 1 hour before surgery.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer, gastric ulcer, or reflux oesophagitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">40 mg IV once daily (if oral medication is inappropriate).</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">60 mg IV daily initially. Higher doses (up to 120 mg) may be required and divided if &gt; 60 mg/day.</li></ul><h3>Administration of Proceptin 20 mg:</h3><h4>IV Injection:</h4><ul><li>Dissolve 10 ml of water for injection with the powder to prepare the solution.</li><li>Administer slowly (over 2-5 minutes) with a maximum flow rate of 4 ml/minute.</li><li>Use the freshly prepared solution within 4 hours.</li></ul><h4>IV Infusion:</h4><ul><li>Reconstitute with 100 ml of 5% dextrose or saline.</li><li>Infuse over 20-30 minutes.</li><li>If dissolved in saline, use within 12 hours; in dextrose, use within 6 hours.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Proceptin 20 mg:</h3><ul><li><strong>Warfarin, phenytoin, and diazepam</strong>: These drugs may stay longer in the body with Proceptin 20 mg. Dose adjustments might be necessary.</li><li><strong>Theophylline, propranolol, antacids</strong>: No significant interaction with Proceptin 20 mg.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications:</h3><ul><li>History of hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Proceptin 20 mg:</h3><ul><li>Most people tolerate it well.</li><li>Rare and mild side effects include nausea, stomach cramping, paresthesia, dizziness, and headache.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C (FDA). No significant adverse effects on pregnancy have been observed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Unknown effects on breast milk. Discontinue breastfeeding if Proceptin 20 mg is necessary.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</h3><ul><li><strong>Rule out gastric cancer</strong> before starting therapy for a suspected stomach ulcer, as it may worsen symptoms and delay diagnosis.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions:</h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Drug Class:</h3><ul><li><strong>Proton Pump Blocker</strong></li></ul><h3>Mode of Action:</h3><p>Proceptin 20 mg inhibits the production of stomach acid by blocking the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme in gastric parietal cells, preventing the release of gastric acid. The effect starts within an hour, peaks in 2 hours, and lasts up to 72 hours. Secretory activity resumes gradually within 3 to 5 days after stopping.</p>', 'active', '147_1742509365.png', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 03:22:45', '2025-05-04 10:31:00');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3817, 'Calboral-DX', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 16.00, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '600 mg plus 400 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Calboral-DX</strong></h3><p>Calboral-DX tablet is indicated for the treatment and prevention of:</p><ul><li>Osteoporosis</li><li>Osteomalacia</li><li>Tetany</li><li>Hypoparathyroidism</li><li>Disorders of osteogenesis</li></ul><p>Calboral-DX tablet is also used as a supplement in case of inadequate intake of calcium in childhood diet, rickets, pregnancy &amp; lactation, and elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis, phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Composition</strong></h3><p>Each tablet contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate USP</strong> 1250 mg (Coral source) equivalent to 500 mg of elemental Calcium &amp; <strong>Vitamin D USP</strong> 200 IU.</li></ul><p>Each DX tablet contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate USP</strong> 1500 mg (Coral source) equivalent to 600 mg of elemental Calcium &amp; <strong>Vitamin D USP</strong> 400 IU.</li></ul><p>Each effervescent tablet contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate (Coral source) USP</strong> 1500 mg equivalent to 600 mg of elemental Calcium and <strong>Vitamin D3 400 IU</strong> as Cholecalciferol USP.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Adequate calcium in the blood is vital to a wide variety of bodily functions, and the body will not tolerate a deficiency even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is also essential for healthy bones as it aids in calcium absorption from the GI tract and stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies show that calcium and vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth, as well as osteoporosis and fracture prevention.</p><p>In this preparation, <strong>Calcium Carbonate</strong> is sourced from coral, which has a chemical structure very similar to that of human bone, ensuring better absorption. <strong>Vitamin D3</strong> aids in the absorption of calcium from the GI tract and helps maintain calcium balance in the body.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Calboral-DX</strong></h3><ul><li>One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or as directed by the physician. Taking with a full stomach ensures better absorption.</li><li>Dissolve one effervescent tablet in a glass of water to drink orally. Stir the solution before drinking. Taking with a full stomach ensures better absorption.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction of Calboral-DX</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Thiazide diuretics</strong> reduce urinary calcium excretion, increasing the risk of hypercalcemia. Serum calcium should be regularly monitored during concurrent use.</li><li><strong>Systemic corticosteroids</strong> reduce calcium absorption. The calcium dose may need to be increased with concurrent use.</li><li><strong>Ion exchange resins</strong> (e.g., cholestyramine) or <strong>laxatives</strong> (e.g., paraffin oil) may reduce the gastrointestinal absorption of vitamin D.</li><li><strong>Tetracyclines</strong>: Calcium carbonate may interfere with tetracycline absorption. Tetracycline should be administered at least two hours before or four to six hours after calcium intake.</li><li><strong>Cardiac glycosides</strong>: Hypercalcemia may increase the toxicity of cardiac glycosides, requiring ECG and serum calcium monitoring.</li><li><strong>Bisphosphonates or sodium fluoride</strong>: Calboral-DX should be administered at least three hours before these medications due to reduced gastrointestinal absorption.</li><li><strong>Oxalic acid (e.g., spinach, rhubarb)</strong> and <strong>phytic acid (e.g., whole cereals)</strong> may inhibit calcium absorption. Avoid taking calcium products within two hours of consuming these foods.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypercalcemia and hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Hypercalciuria and nephrolithiasis</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of this preparation</li><li>Severe renal insufficiency</li><li>Concomitant digoxin therapy (requires careful monitoring of serum calcium levels)</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Calboral-DX</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>GI Tract Irritation</strong>: Constipation is common with oral calcium carbonate administration.</li><li><strong>Hypercalcemia</strong>: Rarely occurs with calcium alone but may happen with large doses in patients with chronic renal failure.</li><li><strong>Other side effects</strong>: Allergic reactions, irregular heartbeats, nausea, vomiting, decreased appetite, dry mouth, and drowsiness.</li><li><strong>Vitamin D side effects</strong>: Skin rash may occur.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Daily intake should not exceed 1500 mg calcium and 600 IU cholecalciferol (15 mcg vitamin D). Overdose of calcium and vitamin D can lead to adverse effects on the developing fetus. There are no indications that therapeutic doses of vitamin D are teratogenic.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Calcium and vitamin D3 pass into breast milk. This should be considered when giving additional vitamin D to the child.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Long-term treatment</strong>: Monitor serum calcium and renal function regularly, especially in elderly patients and those on cardiac glycosides or diuretics.</li><li><strong>Hypercalcemia</strong>: If symptoms of hypercalcemia or impaired renal function occur, reduce or discontinue the dose.</li><li><strong>Renal insufficiency</strong>: Vitamin D should be used with caution in these patients.</li><li><strong>Sarcoidosis</strong>: Monitor serum calcium and urinary calcium excretion closely.</li><li><strong>Immobilized patients with osteoporosis</strong>: Increased risk of hypercalcemia.</li><li><strong>Other vitamin D-containing drugs</strong>: Additional doses should be taken under medical supervision.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Calboral-DX</strong></h3><p>Overdose can lead to:</p><ul><li>Hypervitaminosis D and hypercalcemia.</li><li>Symptoms of hypercalcemia include anorexia, thirst, nausea, vomiting, constipation, abdominal pain, muscle weakness, fatigue, mental disturbances, polydipsia, polyuria, bone pain, nephrocalcinosis, nephrolithiasis, and in severe cases, cardiac arrhythmias.</li></ul><p>Treatment for hypercalcemia includes discontinuing calcium treatment and other contributing medications (e.g., thiazide diuretics, lithium). Rehydration, and depending on the severity, isolated or combined treatment with loop diuretics, bisphosphonates, calcitonin, and corticosteroids may be required.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protect from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul>', 'active', '1818_1742509584.jpg', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 03:26:24', '2025-05-03 01:07:46'),
(3818, 'Labcal-D', 'Elemental Calcium + Vitamin D3', 8.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg plus 200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Labcal-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>This combination is used for the treatment of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, rickets, tetany, and parathyroid disease. Calcium supplements are often used to ensure adequate dietary intake in conditions such as pregnancy &amp; lactation, osteogenesis, and tooth formation (adjunct with definite treatment), as well as therapy with anti-seizure medications. It is also used as a routine supplement and phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Labcal-D 500 mg+200 IU is a preparation of Calcium Carbonate and Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol). Calcium is necessary for many normal functions of the body, especially bone formation and maintenance. Vitamin D3 helps with the absorption &amp; reabsorption of calcium. Vitamin D3 also stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies have shown that Calcium and Vitamin D3 together help in bone growth and in the prevention of osteoporosis &amp; bone fractures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Labcal-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 200 IU Tablet</strong>: 2 tablets daily or 1 tablet twice daily. Labcal-D 500 mg+200 IU is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li><li><strong>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 400 IU Tablet</strong>: 1 tablet twice daily. It is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Labcal-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>It may interact with calcium, aluminium, or magnesium-containing antacids &amp; other calcium supplements, calcitriol, &amp; other vitamin D3 supplements; digoxin, tetracycline, doxycycline, minocycline, or oxytetracycline.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Labcal-D 500 mg+200 IU is contraindicated in cases of hypercalcaemia, hyperthyroidism, renal calculi &amp; nephrolithiasis, and Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Labcal-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>Labcal-D 500 mg+200 IU is generally well tolerated. If symptoms such as nausea, vomiting, stomach cramps, dry mouth, increased thirst, or increased urination are experienced, consult a physician. Constipation may also occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This combination should be used as directed by a physician during pregnancy or while breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>If there is any pre-existing heart disease or kidney disease, precautions should be taken.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Labcal-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdosage may include nausea and vomiting, severe drowsiness, dry mouth, loss of appetite, metallic taste, stomach cramps, diarrhea, headache, and constipation.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1217_1742511317.webp', 68, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 03:55:17', '2025-05-03 23:16:49'),
(3819, 'Neuvital', 'Vitamin B1, B6 & B12', 9.00, 9, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg plus 200 mg plus 200 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Neuvital</strong></p><p>Neuvital is indicated for the treatment of vitamin B1, B6 &amp; B12 deficiency syndrome. It is also indicated for the supportive treatment of neuritis &amp; non-inflammatory diseases of the nerves, e.g., diabetic neuropathy, peripheral neuralgia, lumbago, myalgia, optic neuritis, sciatica, facial neuralgia, intercostal neuralgia, and spinal pain.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate) 100 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride) 200 mg</li><li>Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) 200 microgram</li></ul><p>Each 3 ml ampoule contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) 100 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) 100 mg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) 1000 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Neuvital is a combined preparation of Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate), Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride), and Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin). These vitamins play an essential role as co-enzymes in the metabolism of the nervous system and support the regeneration of nerve fibers and myelin sheath by activating nerve cell metabolism and the natural repair mechanism.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Neuvital</strong></p><p><strong>Tablets:</strong> 1 to 3 tablets per day or as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Injection:</strong> Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly (deep intragluteal). In severe cases, 1 ampoule daily until the acute symptoms subside. For milder cases and follow-up therapy, 2 to 3 ampoules per week.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Neuvital</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong> 1-3 Tablets per day or as advised by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong></li><li>In severe (acute) cases: 1 injection daily until the acute symptoms subside or as advised by the physician.</li><li>In mild cases: 1 injection 2-3 times per week. Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in children:</strong> There is no information on the use of this drug in children.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Neuvital</strong></p><p>No drug interaction has been reported yet.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vitamin B1, Vitamin B6, and Vitamin B12 are contraindicated in patients on levodopa therapy and in patients with hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of the preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Neuvital</strong></p><p>Generally well-tolerated but allergic reactions may be observed in a few cases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>No risks have been known associated with the use of this combination during pregnancy at the recommended dosage. Vitamin B1, B6, and B12 are secreted into breast milk, but risks of overdose for the infant are not known.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Cyanocobalamin should not be given to patients with subacute degeneration of the spinal cord. Cyanocobalamin is not a suitable form of vitamin B12 for the treatment of optic neuropathies associated with raised plasma concentrations of cyanocobalamin.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Neuvital</strong></p><p>No overdosage symptoms are expected at the recommended dosage. If an overdose occurs, treatment should be symptomatic and supportive.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep out of reach of children. Store in a cool (below 25°C) and dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin B1</strong>: Converts carbohydrates, fatty acids, and amino acids into energy, promotes healthy nerves, improves mood, strengthens the heart.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6</strong>: Forms RBCs, helps cells make proteins, manufactures neurotransmitters (e.g., serotonin), releases stored energy, helps prevent CVS diseases, stroke, and eases insomnia.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12</strong>: Essential for cell replication, RBC production, prevents anemia, helps reduce nerve pain, numbness, tingling, and lowers the risk of heart diseases.</li></ul><p>The vitamin ingredients are absorbed well in per oral reception. They are widely distributed to most tissues and appear in breast milk. Thiamine is mostly present as diphosphate in the cell. It is not stored appreciably in the body and excess amounts are excreted in the urine. Pyridoxine and its metabolites are readily absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and stored mainly in the liver, where they are converted to inactive metabolites excreted in the urine.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Oral tablet form is recommended, but due to the presence of benzyl alcohol, injection is not recommended during pregnancy &amp; lactation.</p>', 'active', '1729_1742511443.webp', 8, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 03:57:23', '2025-05-04 00:39:14'),
(3820, 'Lograin', 'Tolfenamic acid', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Lograin 200 mg</h3><p>Lograin 200 mg is used specifically for relieving the pain of <strong>migraine headaches</strong> and is also recommended for use as an <strong>analgesic</strong> in <strong>post-operative pain</strong> and <strong>fever</strong>.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</strong></li><li><strong>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</strong></li><li><strong>Other drugs for migraine</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Lograin 200 mg inhibits the biosynthesis of <strong>prostaglandins</strong> and also presents inhibitory actions on prostaglandin receptors. Its mechanism of action is based on the inhibition of <strong>COX-1</strong> and <strong>COX-2</strong> pathways, which suppresses prostaglandin secretion, thereby exerting anti-inflammatory and pain-blocking effects. Additionally, some reports indicate that Lograin 200 mg inhibits <strong>leukotriene B4 chemotaxis</strong> of human polymorphonuclear leukocytes, which leads to a <strong>25% inhibition</strong> of the chemotactic response. This activity serves as an additional anti-inflammatory mechanism.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Lograin 200 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Acute Migraine Attacks</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 200 mg when the first symptoms appear; may be repeated after 1-2 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Dose adjustments may be needed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Severe Renal Impairment</strong>: Avoid use.</li><li><strong>Mild to Moderate Pain</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children</strong>: No pediatric dosage regimen has been established.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 100-200 mg three times a day (tid).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Dose adjustments may be needed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Severe Renal Impairment</strong>: Avoid use.</li></ul><p>Lograin 200 mg should be taken with food, ideally with water during or immediately after meals.</p><h4>Preclinical Safety Data:</h4><p>Lograin 200 mg has a high therapeutic index, and gastrointestinal ulceration and kidney changes have only been observed with oral doses approximately 6-10 times the maximum recommended therapeutic dose.</p><h3>Special Precautions</h3><p>Use with caution in patients with:</p><ul><li><strong>Asthma</strong>, <strong>bronchospasm</strong></li><li><strong>Bleeding disorders</strong>, <strong>cardiovascular diseases</strong>, <strong>hypertension</strong></li><li><strong>History of peptic ulceration</strong></li><li><strong>Liver</strong>, <strong>cardiac</strong>, or <strong>renal function impairment</strong></li><li><strong>Infections</strong></li><li><strong>Elderly patients</strong></li><li>Increase water intake or reduce the dose to prevent <strong>dysuria</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Lograin 200 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: Increases with <strong>Metoclopramide</strong> and <strong>Magnesium hydroxide</strong>, decreases with <strong>Aluminium hydroxide</strong>.</li><li><strong>Risk of bleeding</strong>: Increases with <strong>anticoagulants</strong> and other <strong>NSAIDs</strong>.</li><li><strong>Decreased antihypertensive effect</strong>: With <strong>loop diuretics</strong>, <strong>β-blockers</strong>, and <strong>ACE inhibitors</strong>.</li><li><strong>Increased plasma concentration</strong>: Of <strong>Lithium</strong>, <strong>Methotrexate</strong>, and <strong>cardiac glycosides</strong>.</li><li><strong>Risk of nephrotoxicity</strong>: With <strong>ACE inhibitors</strong>, <strong>Ciclosporin</strong>, <strong>Tacrolimus</strong>, or <strong>diuretics</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Active <strong>peptic ulcer</strong> or <strong>bleeding in the gut</strong>.</li><li>Severe <strong>heart</strong>, <strong>kidney</strong>, or <strong>liver failure</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Lograin 200 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Dysuria (especially in males), diarrhea, nausea, epigastric pain, vomiting, dyspepsia, erythema, headache, tremor, euphoria, fatigue, pulmonary infiltration, and hematuria.</li><li><strong>Potentially Fatal</strong>: Blood dyscrasias, hepatitis.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Not recommended unless considered essential by the doctor, particularly during the first and third trimesters. <strong>Avoid during the third trimester</strong>.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: NSAIDs can appear in breast milk in very low concentrations, so <strong>avoid use</strong> when breastfeeding if possible.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with <strong>asthma</strong>, <strong>bronchospasm</strong>, <strong>bleeding disorders</strong>, <strong>cardiovascular diseases</strong>, <strong>peptic ulceration</strong>, <strong>hypertension</strong>, <strong>liver infection</strong>, <strong>cardiac or renal function impairment</strong>, and the <strong>elderly</strong>.</li><li>Increase water intake or reduce dose to prevent <strong>dysuria</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Lograin 200 mg</h3><p>Symptoms of overdose include:</p><ul><li>Headache, nausea, vomiting, epigastric pain, gastrointestinal bleeding, diarrhea, excitation, coma, drowsiness, dizziness, tinnitus, fainting, and convulsions.</li><li>In cases of severe poisoning, <strong>acute renal failure</strong> and <strong>liver damage</strong> may occur.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool, dry place</strong>, away from light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of <strong>children</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li><strong>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</strong></li><li><strong>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</strong></li><li><strong>Other drugs for migraine</strong></li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Lograin 200 mg (N-(2-methyl-3-chlorophenyl) anthranilic acid) belongs to the <strong>fenamate group</strong> and is a potent inhibitor of the <strong>cyclooxygenase enzyme</strong>. It inhibits the synthesis of inflammatory mediators such as <strong>thromboxane B2</strong> and <strong>prostaglandin E2</strong>. Prostaglandins are responsible for causing swelling, pain, and inflammation.</p><h3>Pharmacokinetic Properties</h3><ul><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: Readily absorbed from the GI tract. Peak plasma concentration occurs within <strong>60-90 minutes</strong>.</li><li><strong>Bioavailability</strong>: 85%.</li><li><strong>Distribution</strong>: 99% protein-bound.</li><li><strong>Plasma half-life</strong>: 2 hours.</li><li><strong>Metabolism</strong>: Metabolized in the liver with enterohepatic circulation.</li><li><strong>Excretion</strong>: 90% excreted in urine, the remainder in feces.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy</h3><p>This medicine is <strong>not recommended</strong> during pregnancy unless deemed essential by your doctor, particularly in the first and third trimesters. <strong>Avoid during the third trimester</strong>.</p><p><br></p><p>Let me know if you need more details or clarification!</p>', 'active', '99_1742511554.jpg', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 03:59:14', '2025-05-03 23:31:03'),
(3821, 'Feglo-FZ', 'Ferrous Ascorbate + Folic Acid + Zinc Sulfate', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '48 mg plus 0.5 mg plus 22.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Feglo-FZ</strong></p><p>Feglo-FZ is indicated for the prophylaxis of iron deficiency, especially when an inadequate diet calls for supplementary zinc and iron during pregnancy and anemia.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined preparation</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Feglo-FZ, when administered, is converted to ferric form and is immediately reduced to the ferrous form in the stomach. This reduced ferrous form is then transferred to the duodenum, where it is highly absorbed. Ferrous Ascorbate has the advantage of providing both ferrous ion and ascorbate in the same compound. There is no dissociation upon entering the GI tract due to the stable chelate of iron with ascorbate. Folic Acid itself is biochemically inactive and is converted to tetrahydrofolic acid and methyltetrahydrofolate by dihydrofolate reductase in the liver. These folic acid congeners are transported across cells by receptor-mediated endocytosis, where they are needed to maintain various body functions. Folic acid enhances chemical reactions that contribute to the production of red blood cells, the manufacture of DNA needed for cell replication, and the metabolism of amino acids, which are necessary for protein synthesis. Zinc is essential to numerous physiological processes, including the function of many enzymes in the body. Deficiency may lead to poor night vision, slow healing of wounds, poor sexual development and function in males, poor appetite (possibly due to a decrease in the sense of taste and smell), a reduced ability to ward off infections, diarrhea, dermatitis, and, in children, retarded growth.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Feglo-FZ</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult &amp; Elderly</strong>: One tablet a day before or after a meal (food-independent absorption) or as directed by the physician. In more severe cases, two tablets a day may be required as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Feglo-FZ</strong></p><p>Iron chelates with tetracycline. Since oral iron products interfere with the absorption of oral tetracycline antibiotics, Feglo-FZ should not be taken within two hours of each other. Occasional gastrointestinal discomfort may be minimized by taking with meals. Absorption of iron may be impaired by concurrent administration of penicillamine and antacids. In patients with renal failure, there may be a risk of zinc accumulation.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Feglo-FZ is contraindicated in patients with hemolytic anemia, those with increased hypersensitivity to any of its components, and in conditions with increased body iron content.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Feglo-FZ</strong></p><p>Side effects of Feglo-FZ supplementation are mild and transient. These include epigastric pain, nausea, constipation, vomiting, diarrhea, heartburn, etc. Allergic sensitization has been reported following both oral and parenteral administration of folic acid.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>If pregnant, planning to become pregnant, or currently breastfeeding, please contact your physician before taking or continuing the drug. Administration in the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided unless definite evidence of iron deficiency is observed. Prophylaxis of iron deficiency is justified during the remainder of pregnancy, specifically when zinc supplementation is required.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Care should be taken in patients who may develop iron overload, such as those with hemochromatosis, hemolytic anemia, or red cell aplasia. Failure to respond to treatment may indicate other causes of anemia and should be further investigated.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Feglo-FZ</strong></p><p>The clinical course of acute iron overdosage can be variable. Initial symptoms may include abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, tarry stools, melena, hematemesis, hypotension, tachycardia, metabolic acidosis, hyperglycemia, dehydration, drowsiness, pallor, cyanosis, lassitude, seizures, shock, and coma.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined preparation</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Ferrous Ascorbate, Folic Acid, and Zinc form a combination of three nutritional supplements. Feglo-FZ is a combination of iron and vitamin C. Iron replenishes iron stores in the body and corrects iron deficiency anemia. Vitamin C (ascorbate) is added to enhance iron absorption in the body. Folic Acid is a form of vitamin B and plays a vital role in the formation of red blood cells, which carry oxygen throughout the body. It is also essential in pregnancy due to its role in the development of the unborn baby\'s brain and spinal cord. Zinc is a micromineral that provides essential nutrition.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>If pregnant, planning to become pregnant, or currently breastfeeding, please contact your physician before taking or continuing the drug. Administration in the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided unless definite evidence of iron deficiency is observed. Prophylaxis of iron deficiency is justified during the remainder of pregnancy, specifically when zinc supplementation is required.</p>', 'in-active', '1042_1742511669.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 04:01:09', '2025-05-08 14:54:40'),
(3822, 'Betameson', 'Betamethasone Dipropionate', 45.00, 1, 'x 20 gm tube', '20 gm tube', 'Cream', '0.05%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Betameson 20 gm:</strong></p><p>Betameson 20 gm cream and ointment are indicated for the relief of inflammatory and pruritic manifestations of resistant or severe corticosteroid-responsive dermatoses, including:</p><ul><li>Atopic eczema</li><li>Nummular eczema</li><li>Contact dermatitis</li><li>Neurodermatitis</li><li>Anogenital and senile pruritus</li><li>Lichen planus</li><li>Psoriasis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Corticosteroid</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Betameson 20 gm is a potent topical corticosteroid. Topical corticosteroids have anti-inflammatory, antipruritic, and vasoconstrictive actions when administered. Betamethasone works by inhibiting phospholipase A2 activity, reducing the formation of prostaglandins and leukotrienes in local tissues.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>Apply a thin film once or twice daily to cover the affected area.</li><li>For patients with chronic psoriasis who show significant improvement (around 80%) in psoriatic lesions, a pulse dosing regimen can be used. This involves applying up to 3.5g each of Betameson 20 gm cream and ointment, twelve hours apart (e.g., morning, evening, following morning) to the previously affected areas once a week.</li><li>If relapse occurs, revert to conventional dosing.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction:</strong></p><ul><li>No known evidence of drug interactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Betameson 20 gm, other corticosteroids, or any components in the preparation.</li><li>Contraindicated in viral infections (e.g., vaccinia, varicella, Herpes simplex), tuberculosis, acne rosacea, fungal skin infections (e.g., moniliasis), perioral dermatitis, and ulcerative conditions.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Mild to moderate transient burning/stinging, dry skin, pruritus, irritation, and folliculitis.</li><li>Rare: Tingling, prickly skin, skin tightening or cracking, warm feeling, laminar scaling, perilesional scaling, follicular rash, skin atrophy, erythema, urticaria, vesiculation, telangiectasia, acneiform papules, hyperaesthesia.</li><li>Other local reactions: Itching, hypertrichosis, hypopigmentation, perioral dermatitis, allergic contact dermatitis, maceration of skin, secondary infection, striae, miliaria, and exacerbation of untreated infections.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Do not use near the eyes due to the potential risk of glaucoma and cataracts.</li><li>Discontinue treatment if irritation or sensitization occurs, and initiate appropriate therapy.</li><li>In the presence of infection, use an appropriate antifungal or antibacterial agent, and discontinue Betameson 20 gm if no prompt response is observed.</li><li>In prolonged or extensive treatment, consider the possibility of systemic effects due to percutaneous absorption.</li><li>Avoid use under occlusive dressings, which may increase systemic absorption.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with stasis dermatitis and impaired circulation.</li><li>Prolonged use may cause striae or skin atrophy. Discontinue treatment if this occurs.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Acute overdose is unlikely and not expected to be life-threatening. Avoid using for longer than the prescribed period.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Corticosteroid</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Betameson 20 gm is a topical corticosteroid with anti-inflammatory, antipruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties. It induces lipocortins, which antagonize phospholipase A2, reducing the formation and release of inflammatory mediators like prostaglandins, kinins, and histamine.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacokinetics:</strong></p><ul><li>Betameson 20 gm can be absorbed through normal intact skin. Corticosteroids are bound to plasma proteins, metabolized primarily in the liver, and excreted by the kidneys.</li></ul>', 'active', '1879_1742511895.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 04:04:55', '2025-04-30 20:34:00'),
(3823, 'D-Balance', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 20.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20000 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of D-Balance 20000 IU</strong></h3><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated for the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also used as an adjunct therapy for conditions such as osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Vitamin D3 is essential for normal bone growth, development, and bone density maintenance. It is also necessary for the utilization of calcium and phosphorus. Babies need Vitamin D3 for healthy growth and development. Vitamin D3 acts as a hormone and is important for overall health.</p><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation</li><li>Vitamin D preparations</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. Vitamin D3 is essential for bone growth and density. It also helps in reducing the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, lowers cancer risk (e.g., breast and colorectal cancer), and assists in maintaining adequate insulin levels for type 2 diabetes patients.</p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of D-Balance 20000 IU</strong></h3><p><strong>For Capsules:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks. Maintenance therapy is 1,400–2,000 IU per day. Measurement of 25-hydroxyvitamin D should be done 3–4 months after initiating maintenance therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 4 weeks. Higher doses may be required in certain cases.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adjunct therapy for osteoporosis</strong>: 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (12-18 years):Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>For Film-Coated Tablets:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children (as directed by a physician)</strong>: 1,000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily, or as prescribed. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li></ul><p><strong>For Oroflash or Chewable Tablets:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children (as directed by a physician)</strong>: 1,000–2,000 IU daily, or as prescribed. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal. Place the tablet in the mouth, chew, and swallow.</li></ul><p><strong>For Syrup:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For patients at risk of Cholecalciferol deficiency</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0–1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml).</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml).</blockquote><ul><li><strong>For Cholecalciferol-deficient patients</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0–1 year: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1–18 years: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>For Injection:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prevention</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Infants receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk: ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Non-nursed infants or young children (up to 5 years): 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adolescents: 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pregnancy: ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Elderly: ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For patients with digestive disorders or receiving antiepileptic treatments: ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU or 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 3 or 6 months.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions of D-Balance 20000 IU</strong></h3><p>It may interfere with medications like phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (e.g., liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</p><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</p><h3><strong>Side Effects of D-Balance 20000 IU</strong></h3><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Hypercalcemia</li><li>Hypercalciuria</li><li>Skin rash</li><li>Pruritus</li><li>Urticaria</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Abdominal pain</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>Studies have shown safe use of doses up to 4,000 IU during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU, but higher doses may be required for those with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Pregnant women should follow their physician\'s advice based on the severity of their condition.</li><li>Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. Overdose in infants from nursing mothers is not common, but additional doses of Vitamin D3 for breastfed infants should be considered in consultation with a doctor.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of D-Balance 20000 IU</strong></h3><p>Overdose can lead to hypervitaminosis D.</p><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation</li><li>Vitamin D preparations</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) enhances the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus, essential for normal bone growth and density maintenance. It also helps improve lung function, reduces bacterial infection severity, lowers cancer risks, and maintains adequate insulin levels for type 2 diabetes patients.</p><h3><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></h3><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '1468_1742529484.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 08:58:04', '2025-05-08 14:23:53'),
(3824, 'Ocof', 'Dextromethorphan + Phenylephrine + Triprolidine', 110.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '(20 mg+10 mg+2.5 mg)/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ocof 100 ml</strong></p><p>Temporarily relieves these symptoms due to common cold, hay fever (allergic rhinitis), or other upper respiratory allergies:</p><ul><li>Cough due to minor throat or bronchial irritation</li><li>Runny nose</li><li>Sneezing</li><li>Itching of the nose or throat</li><li>Itchy, watery eyes</li><li>Nasal congestion</li><li>Reduces swelling of nasal passages</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Combined cough suppressants</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This preparation is a mixture of antitussive, decongestant, and antihistamine agents.</p><ul><li><strong>Dextromethorphan</strong>: A safe, effective, non-narcotic antitussive agent with a central action on the cough center in the medulla. It has no analgesic or habit-forming properties and generally has little sedative activity.</li><li><strong>Phenylephrine</strong>: A decongestant that shrinks blood vessels in the nasal passage, treating nasal and sinus congestion.</li><li><strong>Triprolidine</strong>: A potent competitive histamine H1-receptor antagonist of the pyrrolidine class with mild CNS depressant properties, which may cause drowsiness.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Ocof 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children 12 years of age and older</strong>: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) every 4 hours, or as directed by a doctor.</li><li><strong>Children 6 to under 12 years of age</strong>: ½ teaspoonful (2.5 ml) every 4 hours, or as directed by a doctor.</li><li><strong>Children below 2 years old</strong>: Not to be used in children below 2 years old. Use with caution in children aged 2 to 6 years, and as advised by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Ocof 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Take with or without food. If it causes an upset stomach, take with food.</li><li>Measure liquid doses carefully. Use the measuring device that comes with the medicine.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ocof 100 ml</strong></p><p>Taking certain MAO inhibitors with this medication may cause a serious (possibly fatal) drug interaction. Avoid taking the following during treatment with this medication:</p><ul><li>Isocarboxazid, methylene blue, moclobemide, phenelzine, procarbazine, rasagiline, selegiline, or tranylcypromine</li><li>Caution should be exercised while taking this drug with other medicines like antihistamines, antispasmodics, beta blockers, Parkinson\'s drugs, anti-seizure drugs, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Should be avoided in patients with liver disease or asthma.</li><li>Contraindicated in patients taking MAO inhibitors or within 2 weeks of stopping such treatment.</li><li>Contraindicated in patients with severe hypertension or severe coronary artery disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ocof 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li>May cause drowsiness, dizziness, and constipation.</li><li>Other side effects may include GIT discomfort, transient hypertension, dry mouth, restlessness, palpitations, allergic reactions, thickening of bronchial secretions, and toxic psychosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>There are no specific data on the use of this combination during pregnancy &amp; lactation. As with any other drugs, use in pregnancy and lactation is best avoided.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>May cause drowsiness. Avoid driving or operating machinery if affected.</li><li>Avoid alcoholic beverages while on this medication.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with epilepsy, prostatic hypertrophy, glaucoma, hepatic disease, hypertension, heart disease, diabetes, hyperthyroidism, etc.</li><li>Consult a physician if symptoms do not improve within one week or if accompanied by high fever.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ocof 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li>In case of overdose, hospital admission is advised. Symptoms may include respiratory depression, paranoid psychosis, delusions, hallucinations, and convulsions.</li><li>Treatment includes gastric lavage and sedative administration like diazepam for convulsions.</li><li>Severe overdose of phenylephrine may cause hypertension, which can be treated with alpha-receptor blockers (e.g., phentolamine) and bradycardia treated with atropine.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li><li>Store in a cool and dry place. Protect from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Combined cough suppressants</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dextromethorphan</strong>: Antitussive agent acting on the cough center.</li><li><strong>Phenylephrine</strong>: Decongestant that reduces nasal congestion.</li><li><strong>Triprolidine</strong>: Antihistamine that provides symptomatic relief in allergic conditions.</li></ul>', 'active', '876_1742529666.png', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 09:01:06', '2025-05-04 01:13:24'),
(3825, 'Cal-D Zen', 'Elemental Calcium + Vitamin D3', 5.75, 15, 'Tablet', '30 tablet in a pack', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cal-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>This combination is used for the treatment of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, rickets, tetany, and in parathyroid disease. Calcium supplements are often used to ensure adequate dietary intake in conditions such as pregnancy &amp; lactation, osteogenesis, and tooth formation (adjunct with definite treatment) and therapy with anti-seizure medications. It is also used as a routine supplement and phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cal-D 500 mg+200 IU is a preparation of Calcium Carbonate and Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol). Calcium is necessary for many normal functions of our body, especially bone formation and maintenance. Vitamin D3 helps with the absorption &amp; reabsorption of Calcium. Vitamin D3 also stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies showed that Calcium and Vitamin D3 together help in bone growth, and in the prevention of osteoporosis &amp; bone fractures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cal-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 200 IU Tablet</strong>: 2 tablets daily or 1 tablet twice daily. Cal-D 500 mg+200 IU is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li><li><strong>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 400 IU Tablet</strong>: 1 tablet twice daily. It is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Cal-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>It has possible interactions with:</p><ul><li>Calcium, aluminium, or magnesium-containing antacids &amp; other calcium supplements</li><li>Calcitriol &amp; other vitamin D3 supplements</li><li>Digoxin, tetracycline, doxycycline, minocycline, or oxytetracycline.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Cal-D 500 mg+200 IU is contraindicated in the case of:</p><ul><li>Hypercalcaemia</li><li>Hyperthyroidism</li><li>Renal calculi &amp; nephrolithiasis</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Cal-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>Cal-D 500 mg+200 IU is generally well tolerated. However, some side effects may include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Increased thirst</li><li>Increased urination</li><li>Constipation (may occur)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This combination should be used as directed by a physician during pregnancy or while breast-feeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>If there is any pre-existing heart disease or kidney disease, precautions should be taken.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Cal-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdosage may include:</p><ul><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Severe drowsiness</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Loss of appetite</li><li>Metallic taste</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Headache</li><li>Constipation</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1784_1742529953.png', 79, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 09:05:53', '2025-04-18 21:03:46');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3826, 'Omegut', 'Omeprazole', 5.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Omegut 20 mg</strong></p><p>Omegut 20 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcer.</li><li>Gastric and duodenal ulcers brought on by NSAIDs.</li><li>In individuals who have a history of duodenal and stomach ulcers brought on by NSAIDs, as a preventative treatment.</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD).</li><li>Care of acid reflux disease throughout the long term.</li><li>Dyspepsia brought on by acid.</li><li>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers.</li><li>Prevention of aspiration of acid while under general anesthesia.</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>Peptic ulcer brought on by Helicobacter pylori.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Blocker</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Omegut 20 mg is an inhibitor of stomach acid secretion, a benzimidazole replacement. By inhibiting the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in the gastric parietal cell, it prevents the release of gastric acid. Following oral administration, the antisecretory effect starts to take effect in about an hour, peaks in two hours, and the inhibition of secretion lasts up to 72 hours. Secretory activity gradually resumes when the medicine is stopped, over a period of 3 to 5 days.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Omegut 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Benign gastric and duodenal ulcer: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks in duodenal ulceration, 8 weeks in gastric ulceration. For severe or recurrent cases, the dose may be increased to 40 mg daily. Maintenance dose for recurrent duodenal ulcer: 20 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">NSAID-associated duodenal or gastric ulcer: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continued for another 4 weeks if not fully healed. 20 mg once daily for prophylaxis in patients with a history of NSAID-associated ulcers.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">GERD: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continued for 4-8 weeks if not fully healed. 40 mg once daily for 8 weeks in GERD refractory to other treatment. Maintenance dose: 20 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Long-term management of acid reflux disease: 10-20 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acid-related dyspepsia: 10-20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prophylaxis of acid aspiration: 40 mg on the preceding evening, then 40 mg 2-6 hours before surgery.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Zollinger-Ellison syndrome: Initially 60 mg once daily; usual range: 20-120 mg daily (if daily dose exceeds 80 mg, divide into two doses).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Helicobacter pylori eradication regimen: 20 mg twice daily in combination with antimicrobial agents (Amoxicillin 500 mg, Metronidazole 400 mg, or Clarithromycin 250 mg).</li><li>Pediatric use (severe ulcerating reflux esophagitis, child &gt;1 year):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">If body weight is 10-20 kg: 10-20 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If body weight is over 20 kg: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li><li><strong>IV Injection:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prophylaxis of acid aspiration: Omegut 20 mg 40 mg slowly (over 5 minutes) as an intravenous injection, one hour before surgery.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Duodenal ulcer, gastric ulcer, or reflux esophagitis: IV 40 mg once daily if oral medication is inappropriate.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (ZES): Initial dose 60 mg daily. Higher doses may be required and adjusted individually. If doses exceed 60 mg daily, divide the dose into two daily administrations.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Omegut 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Direction for IV Injection:</strong></li><li>Omegut 20 mg should only be administered intravenously; do not administer any other way. Reconstitute by adding 10 ml of water for injection to the vial. Administer slowly over a period of at least 2 to 5 minutes. Use the reconstituted solution within 4 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For IV infusion: Omegut 20 mg should be dissolved in 100 ml of 5% dextrose or saline for infusion. The solution should be used within 12 hours when dissolved in saline and within 6 hours when dissolved in dextrose.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Omegut 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Warfarin, phenytoin, and diazepam may take longer to leave the body when taken with Omegut 20 mg. A dose reduction may be required for these medications.</li><li>Theophylline, propranolol, or antacids have not shown any interaction with Omegut 20 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Those with a history of hypersensitivity to any ingredient of Omegut 20 mg should not use it.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Omegut 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Most people tolerate Omegut 20 mg well, with mild and temporary side effects like nausea, stomach cramping, paresthesia, dizziness, and headache. These rarely require dosage adjustments.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Omegut 20 mg falls under FDA pregnancy category C. Three prospective epidemiological studies have shown no negative impact on pregnancy or fetal health. However, the entry of Omegut 20 mg into breast milk and its effects on the developing baby are unknown. If Omegut 20 mg is deemed necessary, breastfeeding should be discontinued.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Before starting Omegut 20 mg therapy, rule out the possibility of gastric cancer, as it could mask symptoms and delay diagnosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep dry and away from heat and light. Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Blocker</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Omegut 20 mg inhibits the production of stomach acid by blocking the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system in gastric parietal cells. The effect begins within one hour, peaks in two hours, and lasts up to 72 hours. After stopping the medication, secretory activity resumes gradually over 3 to 5 days.</li></ul>', 'active', '1568_1742530065.webp', 17, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 09:07:45', '2025-05-05 09:37:09'),
(3827, 'Ace', 'Paracetamol', 7.00, 1, 'suppository', '1 suppository', 'Suppository', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ace 250 mg</strong></p><p>Ace 250 mg is indicated for the treatment of the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Fever</li><li>Common cold and influenza</li><li>Headache, toothache, earache</li><li>Bodyache, myalgia, neuralgia</li><li>Dysmenorrhoea (painful menstruation)</li><li>Sprains, colic pain, back pain</li><li>Post-operative and postpartum pain</li><li>Inflammatory pain and post-vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic and osteoarthritic pain and stiffness of joints</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Ace 250 mg (Acetaminophen) has analgesic (pain-relieving) and antipyretic (fever-reducing) properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity. It works primarily in the central nervous system (CNS) by increasing the pain threshold. This occurs through inhibition of the COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3 enzymes, which are involved in prostaglandin (PG) synthesis. As a para-aminophenol derivative, Ace 250 mg is one of the safest, most widely used analgesics, with minimal side effects compared to aspirin.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ace 250 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Tablet</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 1-2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours, up to a maximum of 4 gm (8 tablets) daily.</li><li><strong>Children (6-12 years)</strong>: ½ to 1 tablet 3 to 4 times daily (do not exceed 2.6 gm/day for long-term use).</li></ul><p><strong>Extended Release Tablet</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years</strong>: 2 tablets every 6 to 8 hours (maximum of 6 tablets in 24 hours). Do not crush the tablet.</li></ul><p><strong>Syrup/Suspension</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Children under 3 months</strong>: 10 mg/kg body weight (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced), 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>3 months to below 1 year</strong>: ½ to 1 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>1-5 years</strong>: 1-2 teaspoonfuls 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>6-12 years</strong>: 2-4 teaspoonfuls 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 4-8 teaspoonfuls 3 to 4 times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Suppository</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Children 3-12 months</strong>: 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children 1-5 years</strong>: 125-250 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6-12 years</strong>: 250-500 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults &amp; children over 12 years</strong>: 0.5-1 gm, 4 times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Paediatric Drop</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Children up to 3 months</strong>: 0.5 ml (40 mg).</li><li><strong>4 to 11 months</strong>: 1.0 ml (80 mg).</li><li><strong>1 to 2 years</strong>: 1.5 ml (120 mg). Do not exceed more than 5 doses daily for a maximum of 5 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Ace 250 mg Tablet with Actizorb Technology</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children (12 years and over)</strong>: Take 1-2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not exceed 8 tablets in 24 hours.</li><li><strong>Children (7 to 11 years)</strong>: Take ½-1 tablet every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not exceed 4 tablets in 24 hours. Not recommended for children under 7 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ace 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Alcohol, barbiturates, and tricyclic antidepressants may reduce the ability to metabolize large doses of Ace 250 mg.</li><li>Chronic use of anticonvulsants or oral contraceptives may induce liver enzymes, increasing the metabolism and clearance of Ace 250 mg, reducing its therapeutic effect.</li><li>Alcohol increases the risk of liver toxicity in cases of overdose.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Ace 250 mg (Acetaminophen).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ace 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Generally mild but may include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hematological reactions like thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, pancytopenia, neutropenia, and agranulocytosis.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pancreatitis, skin rashes, and other allergic reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B (USFDA)</strong>: Should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Ace 250 mg is excreted in breast milk, but maternal use at therapeutic doses does not appear to present a risk to the infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired liver or kidney function.</li><li>Caution is advised when using this drug in patients taking other liver-affecting drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ace 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Early symptoms (within 24 hours) include pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and abdominal pain.</li><li>Liver damage can occur 12-48 hours post-ingestion, along with potential metabolic acidosis and glucose metabolism abnormalities.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Ace 250 mg exerts its effects by inhibiting COX enzymes, thus reducing prostaglandin synthesis and increasing the pain threshold. It has analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity.</li></ul>', 'active', '1529_1742530184.jpg', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 09:09:44', '2025-05-06 20:39:53'),
(3828, 'Fenadin', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 55.00, 1, 'x 50 ml bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '30 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Fenadin 50 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Allergic Rhinitis:</strong></p><p>In adults and children aged 12 years and older, Fenadin 50 ml is recommended for the alleviation of seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis symptoms such as sneezing, rhinorrhea, lacrimation, itchy and red eyes, itchy nose, palate, and throat. Fexofenadine improves both work/activity productivity and health-related quality of life.</p><p><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria:</strong></p><p>Fexofenadine is recommended for the treatment of symptoms associated with chronic idiopathic urticaria in adults and children aged 12 years and older. It effectively reduces the number of wheals and pruritus, leading to improved work/activity productivity and quality of life.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Fexofenadine is a selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist. After oral administration, it is rapidly absorbed and reaches peak plasma concentrations within 2 to 3 hours. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Fenadin 50 ml:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Seasonal Allergic Rhinitis</strong></li><li>Adults and children 12 years and older:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tablet: 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 60 mg once daily</li><li>Children 6 to 11 years:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tablet: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 30 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 2 to 11 years (Suspension): 30 mg or 5 ml twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 30 mg or 5 ml once daily</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong></li><li>Adults and children 12 years and older:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tablet: 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 60 mg once daily</li><li>Children 6 to 11 years:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tablet: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 30 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 6 months to less than 2 years (Suspension): 15 mg or 2.5 ml (1/2 tsp) twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 15 mg or 2.5 ml (1/2 tsp) once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 2 to 11 years (Suspension): 30 mg or 5 ml (1 tsp) twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 30 mg or 5 ml (1 tsp) once daily</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Fenadin 50 ml:</strong></p><p>Fexofenadine does not undergo significant hepatic biotransformation, so it does not interact with medications metabolized by the liver. However, co-administration with erythromycin or ketoconazole may cause a 2-3 fold increase in fexofenadine levels. It’s advised to wait two hours between taking Fenadin 50 ml and antacids containing aluminum and magnesium hydroxide.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Fenadin 50 ml should not be used by individuals with known hypersensitivity to fexofenadine or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Fenadin 50 ml:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Headache, fatigue, vertigo, nausea</li><li><strong>Uncommon:</strong> Dizziness</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Post-marketing (Not frequent):</strong>Hypersensitivity reactions (angioedema, chest tightness, dyspnea, flushing, anaphylaxis)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mental disturbances (anxiety, nightmares, insomnia, vivid dreams)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Cardiac issues (palpitations, tachycardia)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gastrointestinal issues (diarrhea)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin disorders (rash, urticaria, pruritus)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><p>Fenadin 50 ml is categorized as <strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong> by the US FDA, indicating potential risks that should be weighed against the benefits. It is not recommended during pregnancy and lactation unless necessary. Fexofenadine passes into breast milk and is therefore not advised for nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly &amp; Renal/Hepatic Impairment:</strong></li><li>Care should be taken in elderly patients and those with renal or hepatic impairments.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Risk:</strong></li><li>Patients with a history of cardiovascular disease should be cautious as antihistamines may be linked to tachycardia and palpitations.</li><li><strong>Driving &amp; Operating Machinery:</strong></li><li>Based on its pharmacodynamic profile, Fenadin 50 ml is unlikely to impair the ability to drive or operate machinery, but individual responses may vary.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Fenadin 50 ml:</strong></p><p>Overdose symptoms include dry mouth, drowsiness, fatigue, and dizziness. Healthy individuals have tolerated high doses (up to 800 mg) without significant adverse effects. Hemodialysis is not effective in removing the drug from the blood. Symptomatic treatment is recommended.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><p>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Fexofenadine is an inverse agonist of the H1 histamine receptor. It prevents histamine from activating the receptor, which inhibits allergic reactions such as pruritus, rhinorrhea, and watery eyes. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it does not cause central nervous system side effects.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses &amp; Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Dosing should be adjusted for patients with renal impairment due to increased bioavailability and half-life.</li><li><strong>Elderly (65+):</strong> The pharmacokinetics of fexofenadine may be altered in elderly patients, with increased bioavailability.</li></ul>', 'active', '9062_1745390779.jpg', 6, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 09:12:11', '2025-04-23 10:46:19'),
(3829, 'D3', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 35.00, 4, 'capsule', '4 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '40000 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of D3 40000 IU</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Treatment &amp; prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>.</li><li><strong>Adjunct therapy</strong> for conditions such as:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Osteoporosis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Osteomalacia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypocalcaemia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tetany</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rickets, particularly in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency.</li></ul><p>Colecalciferol is the synthetic form of Vitamin D3, which is crucial for:</p><ul><li>Normal bone growth and development.</li><li>Maintaining bone density.</li><li>Proper utilization of calcium and phosphorus.</li></ul><p>It is also essential for the healthy growth and development of babies. Vitamin D3 acts as a hormone in the body.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</p><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin-D preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) helps with:</p><ul><li><strong>Absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus</strong>.</li><li>Essential for bone growth and maintenance of bone density.</li><li>Reduces bacterial infection severity, improves lung function, prevents certain cancers (e.g., breast, colorectal), and supports insulin levels for type 2 diabetes.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of D3 40000 IU</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Capsules</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Maintenance therapy: 1,400–2,000 IU/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">To confirm target 25 hydroxyvitamin D levels: Measure after 3-4 months of maintenance therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Prevention: 20,000 IU every 4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For osteoporosis: 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li><strong>Children (12–18 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 20,000 IU once every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prevention: 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>For Film-Coated Tablets</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1,000 IU to 2,000 IU daily or as directed by a physician. Take with food or within 1 hour of a meal.</li><li><strong>For Syrup</strong>:</li><li>At risk of deficiency:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">0-1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml).</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml).</blockquote><ul><li>For deficient patients:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">0-1 year: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week for 6 weeks).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 years: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>:</li><li><strong>Prevention</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Infants receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk: ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Nursed infants or those not receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk (up to 5 years): 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adolescents: 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Pregnancy: ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Elderly: ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For patients with digestive disorders, antiepileptic treatment, or other specific conditions: ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU or 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 3 or 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Vitamin D deficiency</strong>: 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU, repeat 1–6 months later or as directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Interferes with drugs like phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (e.g., liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Common side effects</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypercalcaemia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypercalciuria</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin rash</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pruritus</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Urticaria</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Abdominal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li>Vitamin D3 is considered safe at doses up to 4,000 IU during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake is 400 IU. Higher doses may be needed for pregnant women who are deficient in Vitamin D3.</li><li>Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. However, overdose in infants induced by nursing mothers has not been observed.</li><li>If additional Vitamin D3 is prescribed for a breastfeeding child, consider the dose given to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong>:</p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with <strong>impaired renal function</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong>:</p><ul><li>Can lead to <strong>hypervitaminosis D</strong> (excessive Vitamin D).</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Keep below <strong>30ºC</strong>, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong>:</p><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children <strong>under 12 years</strong> have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3)</strong> helps with the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It plays a critical role in normal bone growth, density maintenance, and overall health, including reducing infection severity, improving lung function, and preventing cancer. It is also important for the regulation of insulin in type 2 diabetes.</li></ul>', 'active', '1776_1742530503.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 09:15:03', '2025-05-01 19:58:23'),
(3830, 'Famotid', 'Famotidine', 3.00, 14, 'Tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Famotid 20 mg</strong></p><p>Famotid 20 mg is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Gastric ulcer</li><li>Duodenal ulcer</li><li>Anastomotic ulcer</li><li>Acute stress ulcer</li><li>Reflux esophagitis</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Famotid 20 mg is also indicated for the treatment of acute gastritis and chronic gastritis in the acute exacerbation stage.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>H2 receptor antagonist</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Famotid 20 mg is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist. It completely inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors of the parietal cell and inhibits basal, overnight, and pentagastrin-stimulated gastric acid secretion. The H2-receptor antagonist activity of Famotid 20 mg is slowly reversible, as the drug dissociates slowly from the H2-receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Famotid 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer</strong>: 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks</li><li><strong>Benign gastric ulcer</strong>: 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks</li><li><strong>Maintenance therapy</strong>: 20 mg at night to prevent recurrence of duodenal ulceration</li><li><strong>Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</strong>: 20 mg twice daily for 6 to 12 weeks</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: The recommended starting dose is 20 mg every six hours. Dosage should then be adjusted to individual response, with doses up to 160 mg every six hours in some patients without significant adverse effects.</li><li><strong>Acute gastritis and chronic gastritis in the acute exacerbation stage</strong>: 20 mg twice daily (after breakfast and supper or before bedtime). Alternatively, 40 mg once daily at bedtime.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Famotid 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>: For gastric ulcer, duodenal ulcer, anastomotic ulcer, upper gastrointestinal hemorrhage, reflux esophagitis, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</li><li>Usual dose for adults is 20 mg twice daily (after breakfast and supper or before bedtime) or 40 mg once daily at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Acute gastritis and chronic gastritis in acute exacerbation stage</strong>: Usual dosage is 20 mg twice a day or 40 mg once daily at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Pediatric dosage</strong>:</li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">&lt;1 year of age: 0.5 mg/kg/dose up to 8 weeks, once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Age 3 to 11 months: 0.5 mg/kg/dose twice daily up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Age 1 to 2 months: 0.5 mg/kg/dose once daily up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Neonates: 0.5 mg/kg/dose maximum once daily up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients 1-16 years of age: 1 mg/kg/day, divided b.i.d., up to 40 mg b.i.d.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: 40 mg 3 times daily</li></ul><p><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong></p><p>For patients unable to take oral medication or in hospitalized patients with pathological hypersecretory conditions, Famotid 20 mg can be administered intravenously. The recommended dosage for Famotid 20 mg Injection Premixed in adults is 20 mg intravenously every 12 hours. For pediatric patients 1-16 years, the starting dose is 0.25 mg/kg intravenously q 12 hours, up to 40 mg/day.</p><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><p>No clinically significant drug interactions have been identified. Famotid 20 mg does not interact with the cytochrome P450 drug-metabolizing enzyme system.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to any component of the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Famotid 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Eruption, constipation, diarrhea, dry mouth, nausea, vomiting, tachycardia, high blood pressure, headache, drowsiness, or insomnia may rarely occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Famotid 20 mg is classified as Pregnancy Category B. There are no adequate, well-controlled studies in pregnant women, but it is known to cross the placenta and should be prescribed only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether Famotid 20 mg is secreted into human milk, so nursing mothers should either stop nursing or stop taking the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The drug should be used in the minimum required amount depending on the conditions of the diseases. Caution should be exercised when administering Famotid 20 mg to elderly patients or those with renal failure or hepatic disorders.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Famotid 20 mg is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist that inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors of parietal cells, reducing gastric acid secretion.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy Category B. Should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed. Caution should be exercised when administering Famotid 20 mg to nursing women.</li></ul>', 'active', '142_1742530661.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 09:17:41', '2025-05-02 17:29:00'),
(3831, 'Lumenta', 'Montelukast Sodium', 17.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Lumenta 10 mg</h3><p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</strong></li><li><strong>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR):</strong> Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Lumenta 10 mg works by inhibiting the cysteinyl leukotriene receptor (CysLT1). Leukotrienes are produced during the metabolism of arachidonic acid, leading to inflammation and bronchoconstriction in conditions such as asthma and allergic rhinitis. Lumenta has a strong affinity for CysLT1, blocking the physiological effects of leukotrienes, such as LTD4, which contribute to the symptoms of asthma and allergic rhinitis.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (15 years and older):</strong> 10 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6 to 14 years: 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 to 5 years: 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6 months to 5 years: 4 mg oral granules once daily. This can be administered directly or mixed with cold water or soft food at room temperature.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency:</strong> No dosage adjustment needed for mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong> No dosage adjustment recommended.</li><li><strong>Elderly Use:</strong> No dosage adjustment required; plasma half-life may be slightly longer in the elderly.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction:</strong> Lumenta 10 mg has been found to be safe with commonly used treatments like theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives, terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin. The use of phenobarbital or rifampin may reduce the drug\'s effectiveness, so clinical monitoring is advised.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to any of the ingredients in this medication.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Upper respiratory tract infections, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon:</strong> Akathisia, anxiety, depression, dizziness, dry mouth, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, oedema, sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, hallucinations, hepatic issues, memory loss, suicidal thoughts, tremor.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> Use during pregnancy only if necessary. Animal studies show it crosses the placenta.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Secreted in breast milk, caution should be exercised when administering to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Acute asthma attacks:</strong> Lumenta 10 mg is not recommended for the reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks. Patients should have access to a rescue medication.</li><li><strong>Aspirin Sensitivity:</strong> Patients with asthma and aspirin sensitivity should avoid aspirin and NSAIDs.</li><li><strong>Corticosteroid Use:</strong> The use of Lumenta should not replace or abruptly discontinue inhaled or oral corticosteroids.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong> Most overdose reports have been mild, with side effects such as nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain. Supportive care is recommended, and any unabsorbed material should be removed from the digestive tract if necessary.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry place, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Lumenta 10 mg selectively inhibits the CysLT1 receptor, preventing the binding of cysteinyl leukotrienes. This helps in reducing the inflammatory processes that contribute to asthma and allergic rhinitis symptoms, including airway edema and smooth muscle contraction.</p>', 'active', '1575_1742530848.webp', 57, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 09:20:48', '2025-05-03 23:35:21'),
(3832, 'Sina Gold', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral [A-Z gold preparation]', 9.00, 15, 'Tablet', '15 tablet in a pack', 'Tablet', 'pot', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sina Gold</strong></p><p>Sina Gold is indicated for preventing and treating deficiencies in vitamins and minerals. This comprehensive daily nutritional supplement is also recommended to meet the heightened requirements for vitamins and minerals in various situations, including:</p><ul><li>Physical and emotional stress</li><li>Chronic diseases</li><li>Infectious illnesses</li><li>Osteoporosis</li><li>Injuries or wounds</li><li>Surgeries</li><li>Poor digestion</li><li>Old age</li><li>Pregnancy and lactation</li><li>Decreased appetite</li><li>Excessive dieting</li><li>Exposure to environmental pollution</li><li>Strenuous exercise, among others.</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each tablet of Sina Gold contains the following essential nutrients:</p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin A</strong>: 5000 IU</li><li><strong>Vitamin C</strong>: 60 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin D</strong>: 400 IU</li><li><strong>Vitamin E</strong>: 30 IU</li><li><strong>Vitamin K</strong>: 25 mcg</li><li><strong>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine)</strong>: 1.5 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin)</strong>: 1.7 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin B3 (Niacin)</strong>: 20 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine)</strong>: 2 mg</li><li><strong>Folic acid</strong>: 400 mcg</li><li><strong>Biotin</strong>: 30 mcg</li><li><strong>Pantothenate</strong>: 10 mg</li><li><strong>Calcium</strong>: 162 mg</li><li><strong>Iron</strong>: 18 mg</li><li><strong>Phosphorus</strong>: 109 mg</li><li><strong>Iodine</strong>: 150 mcg</li><li><strong>Magnesium</strong>: 100 mg</li><li><strong>Zinc</strong>: 15 mg</li><li><strong>Selenium</strong>: 20 mcg</li><li><strong>Copper</strong>: 2 mg</li><li><strong>Manganese</strong>: 2 mg</li><li><strong>Chromium</strong>: 120 mcg</li><li><strong>Molybdenum</strong>: 75 mcg</li><li><strong>Chloride</strong>: 72 mg</li><li><strong>Potassium</strong>: 80 mg</li><li><strong>Boron</strong>: 150 mcg</li><li><strong>Nickel</strong>: 5 mcg</li><li><strong>Silicon</strong>: 2 mg</li><li><strong>Tin</strong>: 10 mcg</li><li><strong>Vanadium</strong>: 10 mcg</li><li><strong>Lutein</strong>: 250 mcg</li><li><strong>Cyanocobalamin</strong>: 6 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Multivitamin + Multimineral A-Z Gold Preparation is a supplement that combines most vitamins and minerals in a single tablet. This preparation typically contains 32 high-potency vitamins and minerals. It maintains a healthy body and an active lifestyle by providing essential nutrients that may be lacking in one\'s diet.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sina Gold</strong></p><p>The general dosage for adults is one tablet daily. The tablet can be taken with or without food. If you are pregnant or lactating, you may need to take a higher dosage of Sina Gold.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction of Sina Gold</strong></p><p>Sina Gold is generally considered safe and has no known drug interactions.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Sina Gold is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sina Gold</strong></p><p>In most cases, this preparation is well tolerated. However, there may be occasional occurrences of diarrhea while undergoing beta-carotene treatment, and it\'s possible for the skin to take on a mild yellowish tint. Vitamin C and vitamin E may lead to diarrhea and other gastrointestinal disturbances.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Sina Gold is generally safe for pregnant and lactating women, but it is important to speak to your doctor before taking any supplement during pregnancy or lactation.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Long-term intake of high levels of vitamin A (excluding that sourced from beta carotene) may increase the risk of osteoporosis in postmenopausal women.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This is a film-coated tablet, which combines 32 high-potency vitamins and minerals. This preparation maintains a healthy body and active lifestyle.</p>', 'active', '479_1742530991.jpg', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 09:23:11', '2025-04-19 08:50:00'),
(3833, 'Ostocal GX', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 16.00, 16, 'tablet', '8 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg plus 400 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ostocal GX</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment &amp; prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, and disorders of osteogenesis. It is also used as a supplement in case of inadequate intake of Calcium in childhood diet, rickets, pregnancy &amp; lactation, and elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis, phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg (from Coral source), equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li><li>Or</li><li>Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg (from Coral source), equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 200 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate Calcium in the blood is vital to a wide variety of bodily functions. Clinical evidence suggests that Calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of Osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is essential for healthy bones, aiding in Calcium absorption from the GI tract and stimulating bone formation. Studies show that Calcium and Vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth and in Osteoporosis and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ostocal GX</strong></p><ul><li>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night or as directed by the Physician</li><li>Dosage of Ostocal GX: One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or as directed by the physician. Taking it on a full stomach ensures better absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ostocal GX</strong></p><ul><li>Oral Calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline &amp; fluoride preparations; a minimum 3-hour interval should be allowed between the ingestion of these medications.</li><li>Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of Calcium.</li><li>Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce the metabolism of Vitamin D3.</li><li>Concomitant ingestion of foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of Calcium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the components</li><li>Hypocalcaemia resulting from overdose of Vitamin D3</li><li>Hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Bone metastases</li><li>Severe renal insufficiency</li><li>Severe hypercalciuria</li><li>Renal calculi, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ostocal GX</strong></p><p>Rare manifestations include:</p><ul><li>Flatulence</li><li>Diarrhoea</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Upper GI discomfort</li><li>Hypercalcaemia due to prolonged use (rarely reported).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It should be used as directed by the physician during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with dose reduction may be needed.</li><li>Plasma and serum Calcium levels should be monitored in cases of mild to moderate renal impairment and during long-term use.</li><li>Patients with renal stones or a history of renal stones should take precautions.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ostocal GX</strong></p><p>At high doses, symptoms may include nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, and irritability. Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature below 30°C, protect from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This is a Calcium and Vitamin D3 preparation where Calcium Carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The Calcium Carbonate from Coral has a chemical structure that is very similar to human bone composition, ensuring better absorption. Vitamin D3 aids in the absorption of Calcium from the GI tract and helps maintain Calcium balance in the body.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of the physician.</p>', 'active', '282_1742532255.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 09:44:15', '2025-05-04 01:34:20'),
(3834, 'Proviten Gold', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral [A-Z gold preparation]', 12.00, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', 'pot', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the text with the headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Proviten Gold</strong></p><p>Proviten Gold is indicated for preventing and treating deficiencies in vitamins and minerals. This comprehensive daily nutritional supplement is also recommended to meet the heightened requirements for vitamins and minerals in various situations, including physical and emotional stress, chronic diseases, infectious illnesses, osteoporosis, injuries or wounds, surgeries, poor digestion, old age, pregnancy and lactation, decreased appetite, excessive dieting, exposure to environmental pollution, and strenuous exercise, among others.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each tablet of Proviten Gold contains the following essential nutrients:</p><ul><li>Vitamin A: 5000 IU</li><li>Vitamin C: 60 mg</li><li>Vitamin D: 400 IU</li><li>Vitamin E: 30 IU</li><li>Vitamin K: 25 mcg</li><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine): 1.5 mg</li><li>Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin): 1.7 mg</li><li>Vitamin B3 (Niacin): 20 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine): 2 mg</li><li>Folic acid: 400 mcg</li><li>Biotin: 30 mcg</li><li>Pantothenate: 10 mg</li><li>Calcium: 162 mg</li><li>Iron: 18 mg</li><li>Phosphorus: 109 mg</li><li>Iodine: 150 mcg</li><li>Magnesium: 100 mg</li><li>Zinc: 15 mg</li><li>Selenium: 20 mcg</li><li>Copper: 2 mg</li><li>Manganese: 2 mg</li><li>Chromium: 120 mcg</li><li>Molybdenum: 75 mcg</li><li>Chloride: 72 mg</li><li>Potassium: 80 mg</li><li>Boron: 150 mcg</li><li>Nickel: 5 mcg</li><li>Silicon: 2 mg</li><li>Tin: 10 mcg</li><li>Vanadium: 10 mcg</li><li>Lutein: 250 mcg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin: 6 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Multivitamin + Multimineral A-Z Gold Preparation is a supplement that combines most vitamins and minerals in a single tablet. This preparation typically contains 32 high-potency vitamins and minerals. It maintains a healthy body and an active lifestyle by providing essential nutrients that may be lacking in one\'s diet.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Proviten Gold</strong></p><p>The general dosage for adults is one tablet daily. The tablet can be taken with or without food. If you are pregnant or lactating, you may need to take a higher dosage of Proviten Gold.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Proviten Gold</strong></p><p>Proviten Gold is generally considered safe and has no known drug interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Proviten Gold is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Proviten Gold</strong></p><p>In most cases, this preparation is well tolerated. However, there may be occasional occurrences of diarrhea while undergoing beta-carotene treatment, and it\'s possible for the skin to take on a mild yellowish tint. Vitamin C and vitamin E may lead to diarrhea and other gastrointestinal disturbances.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Proviten Gold is generally safe for pregnant and lactating women, but it is important to speak to your doctor before taking any supplement during pregnancy or lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Long-term intake of high levels of vitamin A (excluding that sourced from beta-carotene) may increase the risk of osteoporosis in postmenopausal women.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>This is a film-coated tablet, which combines 32 high-potency vitamins and minerals. This preparation maintains a healthy body and active lifestyle.</p>', 'active', '1484_1742532373.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 09:46:13', '2025-05-04 14:39:23');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3835, 'Sompraz', 'Esomeprazole', 7.00, 7, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Sompraz 20 mg</h3><p>Sompraz 20 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Alleviating GERD-related symptoms, such as persistent heartburn.</li><li>Treating erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Maintaining the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Eradicating Helicobacter pylori infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Treating Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>Managing duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><h3>Dosage of Sompraz 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. If necessary, an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (Clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms persist, an additional 4 weeks may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori Eradication</strong>: Triple Therapy to reduce the risk of duodenal ulcer recurrence: Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days, amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: The dose ranges from 20 mg to 80 mg once daily. The dosage should be adjusted individually.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks based on response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: The recommended adult dose is 40 mg of Sompraz 20 mg once daily by intravenous injection (over at least 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes). Esomeprazole IV should not be administered with any other medications through the same intravenous site.</li></ul><h3>Administration of Sompraz 20 mg</h3><ul><li>Take one Sompraz 20 mg pill or capsule at least one hour before a meal, swallowing it whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mix the contents of a packet with 15 ml of water in a glass, stir thoroughly, and wait 2-3 minutes for the mixture to thicken. Stir again and consume within 30 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For nasogastric or gastric tube use, prepare the mixture in a syringe as described above, wait 2-3 minutes for thickening, and administer into the stomach.</li><li><strong>IV Administration</strong>: Reconstitute with 5 ml of water for injection (WFI) and administer over a minimum of 3 minutes. The solution should be used within 12 hours at room temperature (up to 30°C).</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Sompraz 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>CYP2C19 and CYP3A4</strong> significantly metabolize esomeprazole. Esomeprazole does not clinically interact with medications metabolized by CYP1A2, 2A6, 2C9, 2D6, 2E1, and 3A4.</li><li>Esomeprazole may affect the metabolism of drugs like <strong>diazepam</strong> (which can have increased plasma levels).</li><li>It may reduce the absorption of drugs that require a specific gastrointestinal pH (e.g., ketoconazole, iron salts, digoxin).</li><li><strong>No significant interaction</strong> with oral contraceptives, phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Contraindicated in individuals with known hypersensitivity to any of the components.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Sompraz 20 mg</h3><ul><li>Common side effects include headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, and dry mouth.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>A positive symptom response does not rule out the possibility of stomach cancer.</li><li>Take Sompraz 20 mg capsules at least one hour before meals.</li><li>For patients who have trouble swallowing capsules, the pellets inside the capsule may be mixed with applesauce for easier ingestion.</li><li>Antacids are acceptable while taking esomeprazole.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Sompraz 20 mg</h3><ul><li>Overdose in rats showed symptoms such as decreased motor activity, tremors, ataxia, and convulsions. No specific countermeasure for overdose.</li><li>Symptomatic and supportive care should be provided, as esomeprazole is highly protein-bound and unlikely to be removed by dialysis.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place, below 30°C, away from moisture and light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>Esomeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor that reduces stomach acid production by inhibiting the H+/K+-ATPase in gastric parietal cells.</li><li>It is metabolized by <strong>CYP2C19</strong> and <strong>CYP3A4</strong> enzymes, with a bioavailability of approximately 90% after repeated dosing.</li><li>Esomeprazole is primarily excreted in the urine as inactive metabolites.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy and Pediatric Use</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: No sufficient studies on pregnant women. Animal studies show no teratogenic effects. It is recommended to stop breastfeeding if using esomeprazole.</li><li><strong>Pediatric</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No significant differences in safety and efficacy between elderly and younger patients. However, elderly individuals may be more sensitive.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage change is needed for mild to moderate hepatic impairment. Do not exceed 20 mg daily in severe hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: No significant difference in pharmacokinetics in patients with renal impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '1463_1742532480.webp', 16, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 09:48:00', '2025-05-04 21:17:33'),
(3836, 'Lumona FT', 'Montelukast Sodium', 3.00, 3, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Lumona 5 mg</strong></p><p>Lumona 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. Cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) are located in the human airway, and these eicosanoids bind to them. The orally active drug Lumona 5 mg has a strong affinity and specificity for the CysLT1 receptor. Without any agonist activity, it inhibits LTD4\'s physiological effects at the CysLT1 receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Lumona 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</strong></li><li>The dosage for adults and adolescents 15 years of age and older: Monas 10 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</strong></li><li>The dosage for pediatric patients 6 to 14 years of age: Monas 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li>The dosage for pediatric patients 2 years to 5 years of age: Monas 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li>The dosage for pediatric patients 6 months to 5 years of age: Monas 4 mg oral granules once daily. This can be administered either directly in the mouth or mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in the Pediatric Patient</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Monas have been established in adequate and well-controlled studies in pediatric patients with asthma 6 months to 14 years of age. Safety and efficacy profiles in this age group are similar to those seen in adults.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency:</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is required in patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is recommended in patients with renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Elderly Use:</strong></p><p>The pharmacokinetic profile and the oral bioavailability of a single 10-mg oral dose of Monas are similar in elderly and younger adults. The plasma half-life of Monas is slightly longer in the elderly. No dosage adjustment in the elderly is required.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Lumona 5 mg</strong></p><p>In the prevention and long-term treatment of asthma, Lumona 5 mg has been provided alongside other commonly used treatments without seeming to increase the risk of negative side effects. According to studies on drug interactions, theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives (norethindrone 1mg/ethinyl estradiol 35mcg), terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin did not exhibit any clinically significant effects on the pharmacokinetics at the recommended clinical dose of it. In clinical investigations, the medication was used concurrently with a variety of routinely prescribed medications without showing any signs of clinically harmful interactions, despite the fact that more detailed interaction studies were not carried out. Thyroid hormones, hypnotic sedatives, benzodiazepines, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and decongestants were among these drugs. Following a single dose of this solution, the hepatic metabolism-inducing drug phenobarbital reduced the drug\'s AUC by about 40%. For this medication, no dosage modification is advised. When powerful cytochrome P450 enzyme inducers, such as phenobarbital or rifampin, are provided alongside this solution, it is fair to use proper clinical monitoring.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those who have an extreme sensitivity to any ingredient in this medication should not take it.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Lumona 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon:</strong> Akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, asthenia, aberrant behavior, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, hemorrhage, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, oedema, seizure, odd feeling, and sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, decreased attention, disorientation, erythema nodosum, hallucinations, hepatic problems, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, and tremor.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Lumona 5 mg is classified as pregnancy category B. Although it has been demonstrated that the medication crosses the placenta of pregnant rats and rabbits, there are no records of its use in pregnant humans. Although there is some evidence that it is also secreted in breast milk, little is known about the implications of this discovery. Before starting therapy in nursing mothers, caution should be exercised.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>The reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks, including status asthmaticus, is not recommended as usage for Lumona 5 mg. Patients should be told to have access to the proper rescue medication.</li><li>Asthma exacerbations that are acute can still be treated with the solution.</li><li>While the dosage of an inhaled corticosteroid may be gradually decreased under medical supervision, the use of this solution instead of an inhaled or oral corticosteroid should not be started suddenly.</li><li>Exercise-induced bronchospasm should not be treated and managed with a single dose of it.</li><li>While taking it, patients with documented aspirin sensitivity should continue to refrain from taking aspirin or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Although aspirin sensitivity in asthmatics is a known side effect of the drug, it has not been demonstrated to shorten the bronchoconstrictor response to aspirin and other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs).</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Lumona 5 mg</strong></p><p>The majority of overdose reports did not include any negative experiences. The most frequent side effects, which were in line with Lumona 5 mg\'s safety profile, were nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain. In the case of an overdose, it is permissible to take the customary supportive measures, such as removing any unabsorbed material from the digestive tract, using clinical monitoring, and if necessary, instituting supportive therapy.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry environment while keeping out light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>The cysteinyl leukotriene receptor is inhibited by the selective and orally active leukotriene receptor antagonist known as Lumona 5 mg (CysLT1). The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. The pathophysiology of asthma and allergic rhinitis, including airway edema, smooth muscle contraction, and altered cellular activity associated with the inflammatory process, have been linked to cysteinyl leukotrienes and leukotriene receptor occupation. These factors all contribute to the signs and symptoms of asthma.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>After oral administration, Lumona 5 mg passes the placenta in rats and rabbits. Nevertheless, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. Because the human reaction to medications is not usually predicted by research on animal reproduction, pregnant women should only use it if absolutely necessary. When giving the pill to a nursing mother, care should be taken because many medications are secreted in human milk.</p>', 'active', '1084_1742532632.png', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 09:50:32', '2025-05-03 23:36:55'),
(3837, 'Tilex Max', 'Glucosamine Sulfate + Diacerein', 12.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '750 mg plus 50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Tilex Max 750 mg+50 mg</strong></p><p>This is primarily indicated in conditions like Osteoarthritis, Rheumatoid arthritis, and Bone &amp; Joint injuries.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Stimulation of Cartilage formation</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Glucosamine</strong> is a natural substance produced in the human body and found in certain foods. It stimulates the production of glycosaminoglycan &amp; proteoglycan, two essential building blocks of cartilage. Glucosamine acts as a joint lubricant and shock absorber necessary to maintain healthy cartilage and joint function.</li><li><strong>Diacerein</strong> is an anti-inflammatory analgesic and antipyretic drug, indicated specifically for the treatment of arthritic conditions. It directly inhibits IL-1 synthesis and release in vitro and down-modulates IL-1 induced activities, relieving Osteoarthritis.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Tilex Max 750 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children above 12 years</strong>: One tablet (Glucosamine Sulphate 750 mg and Diacerein 50 mg) twice daily for 4-12 weeks, taken with food.</li><li><strong>Creatinine Clearance (CrCl) &lt; 30 ml/min</strong>: The dose of Diacerein should be reduced.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Tilex Max 750 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>There have been no reports of significant drug interactions of Glucosamine with antibiotics, antidepressants, antihypertensives, nitrates, antiarrhythmics, anxiolytics, hypoglycemic agents, or antisecretives.</li><li><strong>Diacerein</strong>: Decreased absorption with aluminum and/or magnesium hydroxide antacids. Increased risk of diarrhea with laxatives or antibiotics. Avoid co-administration with fibers and phytic acids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>No known contraindications for Glucosamine, but proven hypersensitivity to Glucosamine is a contraindication.</li><li>Diabetics should monitor blood glucose levels regularly when taking Glucosamine.</li><li>No special studies were conducted in patients with renal and/or hepatic insufficiency. However, administration to patients with severe hepatic or renal insufficiency should be under appropriate medical supervision.</li><li>Children should not be supplemented with Glucosamine.</li><li><strong>Diacerein</strong>: Contraindicated in pregnancy, lactation, and hypersensitivity to anthraquinone derivatives.</li><li>Caution in patients with diabetes, asthma, alcohol dependence, or liver disease.</li><li>Regular monitoring of complete blood count, liver function, and urinalysis is recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Tilex Max 750 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Diarrhea, constipation, epigastric pain, heartburn, nausea, vomiting, headache, skin rashes, drowsiness, and intense yellow coloring of urine.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Diacerein</strong>: Contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation.</li><li><strong>Glucosamine</strong>: Should not be taken by pregnant women and nursing mothers due to lack of scientific data regarding its effect on the fetus and infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be practiced when administering Glucosamine + Diacerein in patients who are allergic to Glucosamine, Diacerein, or shellfish (e.g., shrimp, crab); those with diabetes, asthma, alcohol dependence, or liver disease.</li><li>Before taking this medication, complete blood count, liver function, and urinalysis should be monitored regularly.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Tilex Max 750 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Diarrhea may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool &amp; dry place, protect from light &amp; moisture.</li></ul>', 'active', '1795_1742532842.png', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 09:54:02', '2025-05-04 22:26:49'),
(3838, 'Aristovit B', 'Vitamin B complex', 0.62, 60, 'tablet', '60 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '5 mg plus 2 mg plus 2 mg plus 20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information you provided, with the heading styled in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Aristovit B</strong></p><p>Vitamin-B complex is indicated for prophylactic or therapeutic nutritional supplementation in physiologically stressful conditions. These include: Conditions causing depletion, or reduced absorption or bioavailability of essential B-vitamins manifested by glossitis, stomatitis, cheilosis, beriberi.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each Tablet/Capsule contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride 5 mg</li><li>Riboflavin 2 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine hydrochloride 2 mg</li><li>Nicotinamide 20 mg</li></ul><p>Each 5 ml Syrup contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride 5 mg</li><li>Riboflavin 2 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine hydrochloride 2 mg</li><li>Nicotinamide 20 mg</li></ul><p>Each 2 ml Injection contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride 50 mg</li><li>Riboflavin 4 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine hydrochloride 10 mg</li><li>Nicotinamide 100 mg</li><li>D-Panthenol 5 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Vitamin-B complex contains the most important members of the vitamin B group in pure form and in therapeutically balanced proportions. The members of the vitamin B group contained in Vitamin-B complex are components of enzyme systems that regulate various stages of carbohydrate, fat, and protein metabolism, each of the components playing a specific biological role. Deficiency of B vitamins causes glossitis, stomatitis, cheilosis, polyneuritis, beriberi, pellagra, and vascularization of the cornea.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Aristovit B</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet/ capsule</strong>: Usual recommended dose is 1-2 tablet/capsule 3 times daily or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Syrup</strong>: 2-3 teaspoonfuls daily or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: For intramuscular and intravenous administration. Usual recommended dose is 2 ml daily or as directed by the physician. The injectable dosage form contains D-Panthenol 5 mg in addition to Thiamine, Riboflavin, Nicotinamide, and Pyridoxine.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Aristovit B</strong></p><p>As little as 5 mg pyridoxine daily can decrease the efficacy of levodopa in the treatment of parkinsonism. Therefore, Vitamin-B complex is not recommended for patients undergoing such therapy.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vitamin-B complex is contraindicated in patients hypersensitive to any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Aristovit B</strong></p><p>Adverse reactions have been reported with specific vitamins and minerals, but generally at levels substantially higher than those in Vitamin-B complex. However, allergic and idiosyncratic reactions are possible at lower levels. Iron, even at the usual recommended level, has been associated with gastrointestinal intolerance in some patients.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It is safe to use Vitamin-B complex in pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Vitamin-B complex contains the most important members of the vitamin B group in pure form and in therapeutically balanced proportions. The members of the vitamin B group contained in Vitamin-B complex are components of enzyme systems that regulate various stages of carbohydrate, fat, and protein metabolism, each of the components playing a specific biological role. Deficiency of B vitamins causes glossitis, stomatitis, cheilosis, polyneuritis, beriberi, pellagra, and vascularization of the cornea.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>It is safe to use Vitamin-B complex in pregnancy and lactation.</p>', 'active', '618_1742533265.webp', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 10:01:05', '2025-05-02 23:53:23'),
(3839, 'Reversair', 'Montelukast Sodium', 9.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Reversair 5 mg</strong></p><p>Reversair 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. Cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) are located in the human airway, and these eicosanoids bind to them. The orally active drug Reversair 5 mg has a strong affinity and specificity for the CysLT1 receptor. Without any agonist activity, it inhibits LTD4\'s physiological effects at the CysLT1 receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Reversair 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for adults and adolescents 15 years of age and older: 10 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 6 to 14 years of age: 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 2 years to 5 years of age: 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 6 months to 5 years of age: 4 mg oral granules once daily. This can be administered either directly in the mouth or mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Pediatric Patients</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Monas have been established in adequate and well-controlled studies in pediatric patients with asthma 6 months to 14 years of age. Safety and efficacy profiles in this age group are similar to those seen in adults.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is required in patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is recommended in patients with renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Elderly Use</strong></p><p>The pharmacokinetic profile and the oral bioavailability of a single 10-mg oral dose of Monas are similar in elderly and younger adults. The plasma half-life of Monas is slightly longer in the elderly. No dosage adjustment in the elderly is required.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Reversair 5 mg</strong></p><p>In the prevention and long-term treatment of asthma, Reversair 5 mg has been provided alongside other commonly used treatments without seeming to increase the risk of negative side effects. According to studies on drug interactions, theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives (norethindrone 1mg/ethinyl estradiol 35mcg), terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin did not exhibit any clinically significant effects on the pharmacokinetics at the recommended clinical dose of it. In clinical investigations, the medication was used concurrently with a variety of routinely prescribed medications without showing any signs of clinically harmful interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those who have an extreme sensitivity to any ingredient in this medication should not take it.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Reversair 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, asthenia, aberrant behavior, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, hemorrhage, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, oedema, seizure, odd feeling, and sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, decreased attention, disorientation, erythema nodosum, hallucinations, hepatic problems, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, and tremor.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Reversair 5 mg is classified as pregnancy category B. Although it has been demonstrated that the medication crosses the placenta of pregnant rats and rabbits, there are no records of its use in pregnant humans. Although there is some evidence that it is also secreted in breast milk, little is known about the implications of this discovery. Before starting the therapy in nursing moms, caution should be exercised.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks, including status asthmaticus, is not recommended as usage for Reversair 5 mg. Patients should be told to have access to the proper rescue medication. Asthma exacerbations that are acute can still be treated with the solution. While the dosage of an inhaled corticosteroid may be gradually decreased under medical supervision, the use of this solution instead of an inhaled or oral corticosteroid should not be started suddenly. Exercise-induced bronchospasm should not be treated and managed with a single dose of it. While taking it, patients with documented aspirin sensitivity should continue to refrain from taking aspirin or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Reversair 5 mg</strong></p><p>The majority of overdose reports did not include any negative experiences. The most frequent side effects, which were in line with Reversair 5 mg\'s safety profile, were nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain. In the case of an overdose, it is permissible to take the customary supportive measures, such as removing any unabsorbed material from the digestive tract, using clinical monitoring, and if necessary, instituting supportive therapy.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry environment while keeping out light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>The cysteinyl leukotriene receptor is inhibited by the selective and orally active leukotriene receptor antagonist known as Reversair 5 mg (CysLT1). The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. The pathophysiology of asthma and allergic rhinitis, including airway edema, smooth muscle contraction, and altered cellular activity associated with the inflammatory process, have been linked to cysteinyl leukotrienes and leukotriene receptor occupation. These factors all contribute to the signs and symptoms of asthma.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>After oral administration, Reversair 5 mg passes the placenta in rats and rabbits. Nevertheless, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. Because the human reaction to medications is not usually predicted by research on animal reproduction, pregnant women should only use it if absolutely necessary. When giving the pill to a nursing mother, care should be taken because many medications are secreted in human milk.</p>', 'active', '581_1742533468.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 10:04:28', '2025-05-04 16:41:08'),
(3840, 'Multivit Plus', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral', 2.50, 30, 'Tablet', '30 tablet in a pack', 'Tablet', 'pot', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '161_1742533988.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 10:13:08', '2025-04-19 08:52:09'),
(3841, 'Ipec Super.', 'Iron Polymaltose Complex + Folic Acid + Zinc + Vitamin B-Complex', 5.00, 6, 'capsule', '6 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', 'vitamin', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Ipec Super</strong></h3><p>Ipec Super is indicated for the treatment and prophylaxis of deficiencies in Zinc, Iron, Folic Acid, and B-vitamins, particularly during pregnancy and lactation.</p><h3><strong>Composition</strong></h3><p>Each capsule contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Iron BP</strong>: 47 mg (as Iron Polymaltose Complex 188 mg)</li><li><strong>Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate USP</strong>: 61.8 mg (equivalent to 22.5 mg of elemental zinc)</li><li><strong>Folic Acid BP</strong>: 0.50 mg</li><li><strong>Thiamine Hydrochloride USP</strong>: 5 mg</li><li><strong>Riboflavin BP</strong>: 2 mg</li><li><strong>Nicotinamide USP</strong>: 20 mg</li><li><strong>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride BP</strong>: 2 mg</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p>Iron &amp; Vitamin Combined preparations</p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Iron</strong> is provided as Iron Polymaltose Complex, a stable complex of nonionic Ferric Iron and Polymaltose. This facilitates controlled absorption and ensures stability compared to conventional iron preparations.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong> plays a critical role in the development of the baby during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Zinc</strong> supports enzyme activity and protein metabolism.</li><li><strong>B-Vitamins</strong> (Thiamine, Riboflavin, Pyridoxine, and Nicotinamide) help in energy release, tissue building, and overall metabolic processes.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Standard Dose</strong>: One capsule daily.</li><li>In more severe cases, 2 capsules per day may be required, or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions</strong></h3><p>No known drug interactions have been reported.</p><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><p>This product is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of its ingredients.</p><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p>Ipec Super is generally well tolerated, though a few allergic reactions may occur.</p><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Use in Pregnancy</strong>: The use of any drug during the first trimester should be avoided if possible. Iron supplementation during the first trimester should only be considered in the presence of confirmed iron deficiency. Supplementation of iron, zinc, and folic acid is generally justified during the remainder of pregnancy.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients at risk of iron overload (e.g., those with haemochromatosis, haemolytic anaemia, or red cell aplasia).</li><li>Iron absorption may be impaired when taken with tetracycline antibiotics.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Iron</strong>: Overdose is a leading cause of fatal poisoning in children under 6 years. Symptoms may include bloody diarrhea, vomiting, acidosis, darkened stools, and abdominal pain.</li><li><strong>Riboflavin</strong>: No toxic symptoms have been reported.</li><li><strong>Nicotinamide</strong>: High doses may cause vomiting or diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Pyridoxine</strong>: Chronic high doses may cause sensory neuropathy (observed in doses greater than 200 mg).</li><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong>: No overdose cases have been reported.</li><li><strong>Zinc</strong>: Toxicity can cause reduced immune function, low copper status, and altered iron function at doses over 150–450 mg per day.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C.</li><li>Keep away from light and out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Class</strong></h3><p>Iron &amp; Vitamin Combined preparations</p><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Ipec Super is formulated with Iron Polymaltose Complex, Folic Acid, Zinc, and B-vitamins:</p><ul><li><strong>Iron Polymaltose Complex</strong> is a water-soluble, macromolecular complex of iron that does not interact with food components or medications, ensuring faster utilization in haemoglobin synthesis.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong> helps prevent neural tube defects.</li><li><strong>Zinc</strong> is essential for metabolic processes, blood formation, wound healing, and immune function.</li><li><strong>B-Vitamins</strong> support energy production, digestion, immune function, and development of the nervous system.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h3><p>Ipec Super is recommended during pregnancy and lactation to ensure adequate nutrient intake for both mother and child.</p>', 'active', '718_1742534142.webp', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 10:15:42', '2025-05-03 21:04:52'),
(3842, 'Zepiron', 'Iron Polymaltose Complex + Folic Acid + Zinc Sulfate', 3.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '47 mg  plus 0.5 mg plus 22.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zepiron</strong></p><p>This tablet is indicated for the treatment and prevention of Iron, Folic Acid, and Zinc deficiencies.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex INN 188 mg equivalent to 47 mg elemental Iron</li><li>Folic Acid BP 0.50 mg</li><li>Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate USP 61.80 mg equivalent to 22.50 mg elemental Zinc</li></ul><p>Or, each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex INN 192 mg equivalent to 48 mg elemental Iron</li><li>Folic Acid BP 0.5 mg</li><li>Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate USP 61.8 mg equivalent to 22.5 mg elemental Zinc</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined preparation</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This tablet is a preparation of Iron, Folic Acid, and Zinc. In this preparation, Iron is present as Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex, a noble Iron preparation. It contains non-ionic Ferric Iron and Polymaltose in a stable complex. This complex facilitates a controlled absorption of the Iron when it comes in contact with the mucosal cell surface. Due to the non-ionic nature, Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex is more stable than conventional Iron forms. Folic Acid helps in the proper development of the fetus. Zinc keeps enzymes working and helps to metabolize proteins.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zepiron</strong></p><p>One tablet daily. Two tablets may be required a day in severe cases or as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Zepiron</strong></p><p>Since Iron is complex-bound, ionic interaction with foodstuff components (phytates, oxalates, tannin, etc.) and concomitant administration of medicaments (tetracyclines, antacids) are unlikely to occur.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>This tablet is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zepiron</strong></p><p>This tablet is generally well tolerated. However, a few side effects of oral Iron preparations, including nausea, vomiting, constipation, or diarrhoea, may occur rarely.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Use of any drug during the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided if possible. Thus, administration of Iron in the first trimester requires definite evidence of Iron deficiency.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>As with all Iron preparations, dark coloration of the stool may occur, which is without clinical significance.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zepiron</strong></p><p>No intoxication or Iron overload has been reported in the case of overdose.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a cool &amp; dry place away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined preparation</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This tablet is the preparation of Iron, Folic Acid, and Zinc. In this preparation, Iron is present as Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex, a noble Iron preparation. It contains non-ionic Ferric Iron and Polymaltose in a stable complex. This complex facilitates a controlled absorption of the Iron when it comes in contact with the mucosal cell surface. Due to the non-ionic nature, Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex is more stable than conventional Iron forms. Folic Acid helps in the proper development of the fetus. Zinc keeps enzymes working and helps to metabolize proteins.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Use of any drug during the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided if possible. Thus, administration of Iron in the first trimester requires definite evidence of Iron deficiency.</p>', 'active', '1187_1742534295.jpg', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 10:18:15', '2025-05-05 22:19:54'),
(3843, 'Dexpoten Plus', 'Dextromethorphan + Phenylephrine + Triprolidine', 110.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '(20 mg+10 mg+2.5 mg)/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Dexpoten Plus 100 ml</h3><p><strong>Indications:</strong> Dexpoten Plus is used to temporarily relieve symptoms associated with the common cold, hay fever (allergic rhinitis), or other upper respiratory allergies. These symptoms include:</p><ul><li>Cough due to minor throat or bronchial irritation</li><li>Runny nose</li><li>Sneezing</li><li>Itching of the nose or throat</li><li>Itchy, watery eyes</li><li>Nasal congestion</li><li>Reduces swelling of nasal passages</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong> Combined cough suppressants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> This preparation combines antitussive, decongestant, and antihistamine agents:</p><ul><li><strong>Dextromethorphan</strong>: A non-narcotic antitussive that acts on the cough center in the medulla, with minimal sedative activity and no analgesic or habit-forming properties.</li><li><strong>Phenylephrine</strong>: A decongestant that works by shrinking blood vessels in the nasal passages, helping with nasal and sinus congestion.</li><li><strong>Triprolidine</strong>: A potent histamine H1-receptor antagonist with mild CNS depressant properties, providing symptomatic relief from allergies, but may cause drowsiness.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children 12 years and older</strong>: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) every 4 hours, or as directed by a doctor.</li><li><strong>Children 6 to under 12 years</strong>: ½ teaspoonful (2.5 ml) every 4 hours, or as directed by a doctor.</li><li><strong>Children below 2 years</strong>: Not recommended. Use with caution for children aged 2 to 6 years, under physician supervision.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong></p><ul><li>Can be taken with or without food. If it causes stomach upset, take with food.</li><li>Measure liquid doses carefully using the provided measuring device.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Serious Drug Interactions</strong>: Avoid taking with MAO inhibitors (e.g., isocarboxazid, methylene blue, phenelzine).</li><li><strong>Other interactions</strong>: Caution with antihistamines, antispasmodics, beta-blockers, Parkinson\'s medications, tricyclic antidepressants, anti-seizure drugs, and certain psychiatric medications.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid in patients with liver disease, asthma, or those on monoamine oxidase inhibitors (within 2 weeks of stopping treatment).</li><li>Contraindicated in patients with severe hypertension, coronary artery disease, or hypersensitivity to the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Drowsiness, dizziness, constipation, GIT discomfort.</li><li>Less common: Transient hypertension, dry mouth, restlessness, palpitations, allergic reactions (e.g., rashes, tightness in chest), thickening of bronchial secretions, toxic psychosis, blood dyscrasias.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Use during pregnancy and lactation is not specifically studied. Best avoided unless under medical supervision.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>May cause drowsiness. Avoid driving or operating machinery if affected.</li><li>Avoid alcohol.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with conditions like epilepsy, prostatic hypertrophy, glaucoma, liver disease, hypertension, heart disease, diabetes, hyperthyroidism, or bladder neck obstruction.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms may include respiratory depression, paranoid psychosis, delusions, hallucinations, and convulsions.</li><li>Treatment: Gastric lavage, sedatives (e.g., diazepam), alpha-receptor blocking agents (e.g., phentolamine mesilate), and atropine for bradycardia.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li><li>Store in a cool, dry place, and protect from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Combined cough suppressants</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dextromethorphan</strong>: Central action on the cough center in the medulla, without sedative or analgesic effects.</li><li><strong>Phenylephrine</strong>: Shrinks nasal blood vessels to relieve congestion.</li><li><strong>Triprolidine</strong>: Antihistamine with mild CNS depressant effects to relieve allergy symptoms.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><ul><li>As with other drugs, use during pregnancy and lactation should be avoided unless absolutely necessary and prescribed by a doctor.</li></ul>', 'active', '641_1742534575.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 10:22:55', '2025-04-16 09:13:35'),
(3844, 'Ascobex', 'Vitamin C [Ascorbic acid]', 1.89, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Chewable Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ascobex 250 mg</strong></p><p>Ascorbic Acid (Vitamin C) is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Preventing and treating scurvy</li><li>Helping in the healing of wounds and broken bones</li><li>Assisting in the formation of collagen in connective tissues</li><li>Aiding in iron absorption and helping treat anemia</li><li>Contributing to the production of hemoglobin and red blood cells in bone marrow</li><li>Supporting the immune system by preventing many types of viral and bacterial infections</li><li>Helping in the treatment and prevention of the common cold</li><li>Promoting healthy capillaries, gums, and teeth</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin-C Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Vitamin C, a water-soluble vitamin, is readily absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and distributed throughout body tissues. It is involved in biological oxidation-reduction reactions essential for cellular respiration and collagen synthesis. Deficiency leads to scurvy, marked by capillary fragility, bleeding (especially from small blood vessels and gums), anemia, cartilage and bone lesions, and slow wound healing.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ascobex 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral Administration:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Scurvy:</strong> 1 tablet daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Scurvy:</strong> 1-2 tablets daily (dose can be increased based on severity)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Reduction of Stroke Risk in the Elderly:</strong> 1-2 tablets daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Other Cases:</strong> 1 tablet daily or as directed by a physician</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maximum Safe Dose:</strong> 2000 mg daily in divided doses</li><li><strong>Parenteral Administration:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vitamin C is typically administered orally. If oral intake is not feasible or malabsorption is suspected, it can be given IM, IV, or subcutaneously.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For <strong>IV administration</strong>, it should be diluted with Normal Saline, Water for Injection, or Glucose to minimize adverse reactions.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Standard Dose for Adults:</strong> 70 to 150 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Scurvy Treatment:</strong> 300 mg to 1 g daily, with up to 6 g administered without toxicity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Wound Healing:</strong> 300 to 500 mg daily for 7-10 days pre- and postoperatively.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Burn Treatment:</strong> 1-2 g daily for severe burns.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ascobex 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Potentially Hazardous Interactions:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Ascorbic acid is incompatible in solution with aminophylline, bleomycin, erythromycin, lactobionate, nafcillin, nitrofurantoin sodium, conjugated estrogens, sodium bicarbonate, sulfafurazole diethanolamine, chloramphenicol sodium succinate, chlorthiazide sodium, and hydrocortisone sodium succinate.</li><li><strong>Useful Interactions:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Enhances the half-life of paracetamol.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increases iron absorption from the gastrointestinal tract.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ascobex 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin C has little toxicity; however, megadoses may cause:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diarrhea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Abdominal bloating</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Iron over-absorption (harmful in conditions like thalassemia, sideroblastic anemia, and hemochromatosis)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hyperoxaluria, hyperuricosuria, and hemolysis in individuals with glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency</li><li>Pregnant women taking more than 5 g/day may experience fetal abortion.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin C is safe in normal doses during pregnancy, but doses above 5 g/day may cause abortion.</li><li>The drug is safe during lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Megadoses</strong> (more than 1000 mg daily) during pregnancy can lead to scurvy in neonates.</li><li>Contraindicated for patients with hyperoxaluria.</li><li>Ascorbic acid can cause false positive reactions in certain analytical tests (for glucose, uric acid, creatine, and occult blood).</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place below 30˚C.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin-C Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Ascorbic Acid (Vitamin C) is essential for tissue growth and repair, adrenal gland function, immune system support, and healthy gums. It acts as an antioxidant, protecting against pollution and infection, reducing cellular damage, and as a coenzyme in wound healing, drug metabolism, and metabolism of cholesterol and folic acid.</li></ul>', 'active', '1840_1742534693.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 10:24:53', '2025-05-01 22:16:42');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3845, 'Solbion', 'Vitamin B1 + B6 + B12', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', 'vitamin', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information you’ve provided, formatted with proper headings and bold text where necessary:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Solbion</strong></p><p>Solbion is indicated for the treatment of vitamin B1, B6 &amp; B12 deficiency syndrome. It is also indicated for the supportive treatment of neuritis &amp; non-inflammatory diseases of the nerves, including:</p><ul><li>Diabetic neuropathy</li><li>Peripheral neuralgia</li><li>Lumbago</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Optic neuritis</li><li>Sciatica</li><li>Facial neuralgia</li><li>Intercostal neuralgia</li><li>Spinal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Each film-coated tablet contains:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate) 100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride) 200 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) 200 mcg</li><li><strong>Each 3 ml ampoule contains:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) 100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) 100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) 1000 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Solbion is a combined preparation of Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate), Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride), and Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin). These vitamins play an essential role as coenzymes in the metabolism of the nervous system and support the regeneration of nerve fibers and the myelin sheath by activating nerve cell metabolism and the natural repair mechanism.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Solbion</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablets:</strong> 1 to 3 tablets per day or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly (deep intragluteal).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In severe cases: 1 ampoule daily until the acute symptoms subside.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For milder cases and follow-up therapy: 2 to 3 ampoules per week.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Solbion</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong> 1-3 Tablets per day or as advised by the physician.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection:</strong>In severe (acute) cases: 1 injection daily until the acute symptoms subside or as advised by the physician.</li><li>In mild cases: 1 injection 2-3 times per week.</li><li>Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Use in children:</strong> There is no information on the use of this drug in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Solbion</strong></p><p>No drug interaction has been reported yet.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vitamin B1, Vitamin B6, and Vitamin B12 are contraindicated in patients on levodopa therapy and in patients with hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of the preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Solbion</strong></p><p>Generally well tolerated, but allergic reactions may be observed in a few cases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>No risks have been associated with the use of this combination during pregnancy at the recommended dosage. Vitamin B1, B6, &amp; B12 are secreted into breast milk, but the risks of overdose for the infant are not known.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Cyanocobalamin should not be given in patients with subacute degeneration of the spinal cord.</li><li>Cyanocobalamin is not suitable for the treatment of optic neuropathies associated with raised plasma concentrations of cyanocobalamin.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Solbion</strong></p><p>No overdosage symptoms are expected at the recommended dosage. In the case of an overdose, treatment is symptomatic and supportive.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Store in a cool (below 25°C), dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin B1</strong>: Converts carbohydrates, fatty acids, and amino acids into energy, promotes healthy nerves, improves mood, and strengthens the heart.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6</strong>: Forms RBCs, helps cells make proteins, manufactures neurotransmitters (e.g., serotonin), releases stored energy, prevents cardiovascular diseases and stroke, eases depression, and insomnia.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12</strong>: Essential for cell replication and RBC production, prevents anemia, helps prevent depression, reduces nerve pain, numbness, tingling, and lowers the risk of heart diseases.</li></ul><p>The vitamin ingredients are absorbed well upon oral administration. They are widely distributed to most tissues and appear in breast milk.</p><ul><li>Thiamine is not stored significantly in the body, and excess amounts are excreted in urine as unchanged thiamine or metabolites.</li><li>Pyridoxine is absorbed and converted to active forms in the liver, where it is stored, and excess amounts are excreted in urine.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Oral tablet form is recommended, but due to the presence of benzyl alcohol, the injection is not recommended during pregnancy &amp; lactation.</p><p>Let me know if you\'d like any additional details or adjustments!</p>', 'active', '939_1742534962.png', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 10:29:22', '2025-05-04 21:15:51'),
(3846, 'Calvimax Plus', 'Calcium + Vitamin D3 + Multimineral', 6.50, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', 'vitamin', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Calvimax Plus</strong></p><p>This preparation is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Prevention and treatment of osteoporosis</li><li>To maintain strong bone growth</li><li>For proper functioning of heart, muscles, and nerves</li><li>As a nutritional supplement</li><li>For bone development and regeneration</li><li>Pregnancy &amp; lactation</li><li>Deficiency state of Calcium, Vitamin D3, Magnesium, Zinc, Copper, Manganese, &amp; Boron</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate BP equivalent to 600 mg elemental Calcium</li><li>Vitamin D3 BP (as Cholecalciferol) 200 IU</li><li>Magnesium Oxide BP equivalent to 40 mg Magnesium</li><li>Manganese Sulfate Monohydrate BP equivalent to 1.8 mg Manganese</li><li>Cupric Oxide equivalent to 1 mg Copper</li><li>Boron Citrate equivalent to 250 mcg Boron</li><li>Zinc Oxide BP equivalent to 7.5 mg Zinc</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Nutrition is crucial in preventing osteoporosis and other bone-related diseases. Calcium, Magnesium, and Vitamin D3 are essential for bone health. Without Vitamin D3, calcium absorption is minimal. Magnesium, like calcium, contributes to bone strength. Studies have shown that certain micronutrients, such as Copper, Manganese, Zinc, and Boron, play significant roles in bone health. Deficiency of these micronutrients is common in patients with osteoporosis.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Calvimax Plus</strong></p><ul><li>2 tablets per day, preferably 1 tablet in the morning and 1 tablet in the evening.</li><li>It is best taken with or just after main meals with a full glass of water.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Calvimax Plus</strong></p><ul><li>Hypercalcemia risk should be considered in patients taking thiazide diuretics, as these drugs can reduce urinary calcium excretion.</li><li>Hypocalcemia must be avoided in digitalized patients.</li><li>Certain foods (e.g., those containing oxalic acid, phosphate, or phytinic acid) may reduce calcium absorption.</li><li>Concomitant treatment with phenytoin or barbiturates can decrease the effect of Vitamin D3.</li><li>Use of glucocorticoids can decrease the effect of Vitamin D3.</li><li>The effects of digitalis and other cardiac glycosides may be reduced when calcium combined with Vitamin D3 is taken orally.</li><li>Calcium salts may reduce the absorption of thyroxin, bisphosphonates, sodium fluoride, quinolone or tetracycline antibiotics, or iron. A minimum period of four hours should be allowed between calcium administration and these drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Absolute contraindications include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypercalcemia due to myeloma, bone metastasis, or other malignant bone disease</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sarcoidosis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Primary hyperparathyroidism</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vitamin D3 overdose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe renal failure</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypersensitivity to any tablet ingredient</li><li>Relative contraindications include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Osteoporosis due to prolonged immobilization</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Renal stones</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe hypercalciuria</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Calvimax Plus</strong></p><ul><li>Rare mild gastrointestinal disturbances such as constipation, flatulence, nausea, gastric pain, or diarrhea.</li><li>Occasional skin rash due to Vitamin D3 supplementation.</li><li>Hypercalciuria and, in rare cases, hypercalcemia with long-term high-dose treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>This combination should be used as directed by a physician during pregnancy or while breastfeeding. Calcium and Vitamin D3 requirements increase during pregnancy and lactation, but allowances should be made for other sources of these nutrients.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Careful supervision is needed for patients with mild to moderate renal failure or mild hypercalciuria.</li><li>Periodic checks of plasma calcium levels and urinary calcium excretion are recommended.</li><li>In patients with a history of renal stones, urinary calcium excretion should be measured to exclude hypercalciuria.</li><li>Long-term treatment may require monitoring of serum and urinary calcium levels and kidney function. Treatment should be reduced or temporarily stopped if urinary calcium exceeds 7.5 mmol/24 hours.</li><li>Be mindful of calcium and Vitamin D3 supplements from other sources.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Calvimax Plus</strong></p><p>Symptoms may include:</p><ul><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Severe drowsiness</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Loss of appetite</li><li>Metallic taste</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Headache</li><li>Constipation</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Calcium, Magnesium, and Vitamin D3 are essential nutrients for preventing osteoporosis and improving bone health. Vitamin D3 enhances calcium absorption, and magnesium increases bone strength. Additionally, Copper, Manganese, Zinc, and Boron contribute to bone health, and deficiencies in these micronutrients are observed in osteoporosis patients.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment during pregnancy and lactation should always be under the direction of a physician. If calcium and iron supplements are both required, they should be taken at different times.</li><li>Overdose of Vitamin D3 has shown teratogenic effects in animals, and long-term hypercalcemia in humans can cause serious birth defects. Vitamin D3 and its metabolites pass into breast milk.</li></ul>', 'active', '609_1742535095.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 10:31:35', '2025-05-03 01:11:44'),
(3847, 'Monas', 'Montelukast Sodium', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Chewable Tablet', '4 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Monas 4 4 mg - Medication Information</h3><p><strong>Indications:</strong> Monas 4 4 mg is used for:</p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</strong></li><li><strong>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR)</strong>: Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Leukotriene receptor antagonists (CysLT1 receptor antagonists)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Monas 4 4 mg inhibits cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, LTE4) by blocking the CysLT1 receptor. These leukotrienes are involved in inflammatory processes such as airway edema, smooth muscle contraction, and cellular activity that contribute to asthma and allergic rhinitis symptoms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and adolescents (15 years and older):</strong>Monas 10 mg tablet, once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric patients:6 to 14 years:</strong> Monas 5 mg tablet, once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>2 to 5 years:</strong> Monas 4 mg tablet, once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>6 months to 5 years:</strong> Monas 4 mg oral granules, once daily (can be taken directly or mixed with cold water or soft food).</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Pediatric Patients:</strong></p><p>Safety and efficacy have been established for asthma treatment in patients aged 6 months to 14 years. Profiles in pediatric patients are similar to adults.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency:</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment required for mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is recommended for renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Elderly Use:</strong></p><p>Pharmacokinetic profile is similar between elderly and younger adults. No dosage adjustment required.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><p>Monas 4 4 mg can be taken alongside medications such as:</p><ul><li><strong>Theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives, terfenadine, digoxin, warfarin, and others</strong> without clinically significant interactions.</li></ul><p>Caution when used with <strong>strong cytochrome P450 inducers</strong> like phenobarbital and rifampin, which may reduce Monas 4 4 mg\'s efficacy.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Monas 4 4 mg is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to any ingredient in the medication.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon:</strong> Akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, depression, dizziness, sleep disturbances, and others.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, hallucinations, memory loss, suicidal thoughts, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category B (animal studies show passage through placenta in rats and rabbits, but no human studies). Use only if absolutely necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> May pass into breast milk, use with caution.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Not for acute asthma attacks</strong> or status asthmaticus.</li><li><strong>Not to be used as the sole treatment for exercise-induced bronchospasm.</strong></li><li>Patients with <strong>aspirin sensitivity</strong> should continue to avoid aspirin or NSAIDs.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdose may include nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, headache, and stomach pain. Supportive measures (e.g., removing unabsorbed material, monitoring) are recommended.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry place, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need more information!</p>', 'active', '1011_1742535459.jpg', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 10:37:39', '2025-05-04 00:07:17'),
(3848, 'Acitrin', 'Cetirizine Hydrochloride', 3.01, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Acitrin 10 mg</h3><p>Acitrin 10 mg is indicated for the relief of symptoms associated with seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis. It is also used to treat the uncomplicated skin manifestations of chronic idiopathic urticaria and allergen-induced asthma.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Sedating Anti-histamine</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Acitrin 10 mg is a potent H1 receptor antagonist with minimal anticholinergic and antiserotonic effects. At pharmacologically active doses, it causes little to no drowsiness and does not result in behavioral changes. It works by inhibiting the histamine-mediated early phase of allergic reactions and reducing the migration of inflammatory cells and the release of mediators involved in the late phase of the allergic response.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Acitrin 10 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults and Children (6 years and older)</strong>: 1 tablet or 2 teaspoonfuls daily, or 1 teaspoonful twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children (2-6 years)</strong>: 1 teaspoonful once daily or 1/2 teaspoonful twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children (6 months to 2 years)</strong>: 1/2 teaspoonful once daily.</li><li><strong>Children (12-23 months)</strong>: The dose can be increased to a maximum of 1/2 teaspoonful every 12 hours.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Acitrin 10 mg</h3><p>No clinically significant drug interactions have been found with:</p><ul><li>Theophylline</li><li>Azithromycin</li><li>Pseudoephedrine</li><li>Ketoconazole</li><li>Erythromycin</li><li>Other drugs</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Acitrin 10 mg is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Cetirizine or Hydroxyzine.</p><h3>Side Effects of Acitrin 10 mg</h3><p>The most common side effect of Acitrin 10 mg is somnolence (drowsiness).</p><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: US FDA Pregnancy Category B. Although animal reproduction studies do not always predict human response, Acitrin 10 mg should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Cetirizine (the active ingredient) is excreted in human milk. Caution is recommended when administering Acitrin 10 mg to nursing mothers.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><p>Caution should be exercised when driving a car or operating heavy machinery, as the drug may cause drowsiness.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul>', 'active', '1901_1742535579.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 10:39:39', '2025-05-02 22:47:06'),
(3849, 'Tri-B Tablet', 'Vitamin B1, B6 & B12', 9.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg+200 mg+200 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Tri-B</strong></p><p>Tri-B is indicated for the treatment of vitamin B1, B6 &amp; B12 deficiency syndrome. It is also indicated for the supportive treatment of neuritis and non-inflammatory diseases of the nerves, including:</p><ul><li>Diabetic neuropathy</li><li>Peripheral neuralgia</li><li>Lumbago</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Optic neuritis</li><li>Sciatica</li><li>Facial neuralgia</li><li>Intercostal neuralgia</li><li>Spinal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate) 100 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride) 200 mg</li><li>Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) 200 mcg</li></ul><p>Each 3 ml ampoule contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) 100 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) 100 mg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) 1000 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Tri-B is a combined preparation of Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate), Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride), and Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin). These vitamins play an essential role as coenzymes in the metabolism of the nervous system. They support the regeneration of nerve fibers and myelin sheath by activating nerve cell metabolism and the natural repair mechanism.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Tri-B</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablets</strong>: 1 to 3 tablets per day or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly (deep intragluteal).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In severe cases: 1 ampoule daily until acute symptoms subside.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For milder cases and follow-up therapy: 2 to 3 ampoules per week.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Tri-B</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>: 1-3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In severe (acute) cases: 1 injection daily until acute symptoms subside or as advised by the physician.</li><li>In mild cases: 1 injection 2-3 times per week.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly.</li><li><strong>Use in children</strong>: There is no information on the use of this drug in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Tri-B</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported yet.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vitamin B1, Vitamin B6, and Vitamin B12 are contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients on levodopa therapy</li><li>Patients with hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of the preparation</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Tri-B</strong></p><p>Tri-B is generally well tolerated, but allergic reactions may be observed in some cases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>There are no known risks associated with the use of this combination during pregnancy at the recommended dosage.</li><li>Vitamin B1, B6 &amp; B12 are secreted into breast milk, but the risks of overdose for the infant are not known.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Cyanocobalamin should not be given to patients with subacute degeneration of the spinal cord.</li><li>Cyanocobalamin is not suitable for the treatment of optic neuropathies associated with raised plasma concentrations of cyanocobalamin.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Tri-B</strong></p><p>No overdosage symptoms are expected at the recommended dosage. If an overdose occurs, treatment is symptomatic and supportive.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Store in a cool (below 25°C) and dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin B1</strong>: Converts carbohydrates, fatty acids, and amino acids into energy, promotes healthy nerves, improves mood, and strengthens the heart.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6</strong>: Forms RBCs, helps cells make proteins, manufactures neurotransmitters (e.g., serotonin), releases stored forms of energy, helps prevent cardiovascular diseases and strokes, alleviates depression, and eases insomnia.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12</strong>: Essential for cell replication, important for RBC production, prevents anemia, helps prevent depression, reduces nerve pain, numbness, tingling, and lowers the risk of heart disease.</li></ul><p>The vitamin ingredients are well absorbed via oral administration and widely distributed to most tissues, appearing in breast milk.</p><ul><li><strong>Thiamine</strong> is not stored significantly in the body; excess amounts are excreted in the urine.</li><li><strong>Pyridoxine</strong> and its active forms are stored mainly in the liver and excreted in urine. At higher doses, more is excreted unchanged.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Oral tablet form is recommended. Due to the presence of benzyl alcohol, the injection is not recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</p>', 'active', '1369_1742535768.png', 59, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 10:42:48', '2025-05-04 22:33:07'),
(3850, 'Osticare', 'Glucosamine Sulfate + Diacerein', 12.00, 6, 'Tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '750 mg+50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Osticare 750 mg + 50 mg</strong></p><p>This is primarily indicated in conditions like Osteoarthritis, Rheumatoid arthritis, Bone &amp; Joint injuries.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Stimulation of Cartilage formation</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Glucosamine</strong>: A natural substance produced in the human body and found in certain foods. It stimulates the production of glycosaminoglycan &amp; proteoglycan, two essential building blocks of cartilage. Glucosamine acts as a joint lubricant and shock absorber necessary to maintain healthy cartilage and joint function.</li><li><strong>Diacerein</strong>: An anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic drug, specially indicated for the treatment of arthritic conditions. It directly inhibits IL-1 synthesis and release in vitro and down-modulates IL-1 induced activities to relieve Osteoarthritis.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Osticare 750 mg + 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children above 12 years</strong>: One tablet (Glucosamine Sulphate 750 mg and Diacerein 50 mg) twice daily for 4-12 weeks, taken with food.</li><li><strong>Creatinine Clearance (CrCl) &lt; 30 ml/min</strong>: The dose of Diacerein should be reduced.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Osticare 750 mg + 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>There have been no reports of significant drug interactions of Glucosamine with antibiotics, antidepressants, antihypertensives, nitrates, antiarrhythmics, anxiolytics, hypoglycemic agents, or antisecretives.</li><li>Decreased absorption of Diacerein with aluminium and/or magnesium hydroxide antacids.</li><li>Increased risk of diarrhea with laxatives and antibiotics.</li><li>Avoid co-administration with fibers and phytic acids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>There are no known contraindications for Glucosamine, but proven hypersensitivity to Glucosamine is a contraindication.</li><li>Diabetics are advised to monitor blood glucose levels regularly when taking Glucosamine.</li><li>No special studies were conducted in patients with renal and/or hepatic insufficiency. However, administration to patients with severe hepatic or renal insufficiency should be under appropriate medical supervision.</li><li>Children should not be supplemented with Glucosamine, Diacerein, or shellfish (e.g., shrimp, crab).</li><li>Contraindicated in patients with diabetes, asthma, alcohol dependence, or liver disease.</li><li>Diacerein is contraindicated in pregnancy, lactation, and hypersensitivity to anthraquinone derivatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Osticare 750 mg + 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Diarrhea, constipation, epigastric pain, heartburn, nausea, vomiting, headache, skin rashes, drowsiness, intense yellow coloring of urine.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Diacerein is contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation.</li><li>Glucosamine should not be taken by pregnant women or nursing mothers due to lack of scientific data regarding its effect on the fetus and infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be practiced when administering Glucosamine + Diacerein to patients who are allergic to Glucosamine, Diacerein, or shellfish (e.g., shrimp, crab); who have diabetes, asthma, alcohol dependence, or liver disease.</li><li>Before taking Glucosamine + Diacerein, a complete blood count, liver function, and urinalysis should be monitored regularly.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Osticare 750 mg + 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Diarrhea may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool &amp; dry place, protect from light &amp; moisture.</li></ul>', 'active', '597_1742536252.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 10:50:52', '2025-04-19 07:57:51'),
(3851, 'Acical-MX', 'Calcium + Vitamin D3 + Multimineral', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', 'vitamin', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Acical-MX</h3><p>This preparation is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Prevention and treatment of osteoporosis</li><li>To maintain strong bone growth</li><li>For proper functioning of heart, muscle, and nerves</li><li>As a nutritional supplement</li><li>For bone development and regeneration of bone</li><li>Pregnancy &amp; lactation</li><li>Deficiency state of Calcium, Vitamin D3, Magnesium, Zinc, Copper, Manganese &amp; Boron</li></ul><h3>Composition</h3><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate BP</strong> equivalent to 600 mg elemental Calcium</li><li><strong>Vitamin D3 BP</strong> (as Cholecalciferol) 200 IU</li><li><strong>Magnesium Oxide BP</strong> equivalent to 40 mg Magnesium</li><li><strong>Manganese Sulfate Monohydrate BP</strong> equivalent to 1.8 mg Manganese</li><li><strong>Cupric Oxide</strong> equivalent to 1 mg Copper</li><li><strong>Boron Citrate</strong> equivalent to 250 mcg Boron</li><li><strong>Zinc Oxide BP</strong> equivalent to 7.5 mg Zinc</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Nutrition is crucial for preventing osteoporosis and other bone-related diseases. Calcium, Magnesium &amp; Vitamin D3 are essential for bone health. Without Vitamin D3, calcium absorption is limited. Magnesium, like calcium, helps in increasing bone strength and rigidity. Some micronutrients like Copper, Manganese, Zinc, and Boron play vital roles in bone health, with deficiencies observed in patients suffering from osteoporosis.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Dosage</strong>: 2 tablets per day, preferably 1 tablet in the morning and 1 tablet in the evening.</li><li><strong>Best taken</strong>: With or just after main meals, with a full glass of water.</li></ul><h3>Interaction</h3><ul><li>Risk of hypercalcemia in patients taking <strong>thiazide diuretics</strong> due to reduced urinary calcium excretion.</li><li>Hypocalcemia should be avoided in <strong>digitalized</strong> patients.</li><li>Certain foods (e.g., oxalic acid, phosphate, or phytinic acid) may reduce calcium absorption.</li><li><strong>Phenytoin</strong> or <strong>barbiturates</strong> can decrease Vitamin D3 effect.</li><li><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong> can also reduce the effectiveness of Vitamin D3.</li><li>Calcium may reduce the absorption of <strong>thyroxin</strong>, <strong>bisphosphonates</strong>, <strong>quinolone</strong>, <strong>tetracycline antibiotics</strong>, or <strong>iron</strong>. It\'s advisable to leave a minimum of 4 hours between calcium and these medications.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Absolute</strong>: Hypercalcemia due to myeloma, bone metastasis, or malignant bone disease; sarcoidosis; primary hyperparathyroidism; Vitamin D3 overdose; severe renal failure; hypersensitivity to any of the tablet ingredients.</li><li><strong>Relative</strong>: Osteoporosis due to prolonged immobilization, renal stones, severe hypercalciuria.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li>Mild gastrointestinal disturbances (constipation, flatulence, nausea, gastric pain, diarrhea).</li><li>Occasional skin rash due to Vitamin D3.</li><li><strong>Hypercalciuria</strong> and <strong>hypercalcemia</strong> may occur with long-term high-dose treatment.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>Use during pregnancy or lactation only under the direction of a physician. Calcium and Vitamin D3 requirements increase during pregnancy.</li><li>When both calcium and iron supplements are required, they should be taken at different times.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Patients with mild to moderate renal failure or hypercalciuria should be carefully monitored, with checks of plasma calcium and urinary calcium excretion.</li><li>Long-term use should be monitored for serum and urinary calcium levels and kidney function.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><p>Symptoms of overdosage may include:</p><ul><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Severe drowsiness</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Loss of appetite</li><li>Metallic taste</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Headache</li><li>Constipation</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>Calcium, Magnesium, and Vitamin D3 are essential macronutrients for bone health. Without Vitamin D3, calcium absorption is limited. These micronutrients contribute to bone strength and rigidity, and deficiencies are often seen in osteoporosis patients.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li>Treatment during pregnancy or lactation should always be under the guidance of a physician. Vitamin D3 and its metabolites pass into breast milk, and long-term hypercalcemia can lead to serious health issues for both mother and baby, including developmental and physical impairments.</li></ul>', 'active', '1278_1742536479.png', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 10:54:39', '2025-05-02 22:46:31'),
(3852, 'Nizoder', 'Ketoconazole', 20.00, 30, 'x 6 ml sachet', '6 ml sachet', 'Shampoo', '2%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nizoder 2%</strong></p><p>Nizoder 2% therapeutic shampoo is used for the prevention and treatment of infections where the yeast <em>Malassezia furfur</em> (previously called <em>Pityrosporum orbiculare</em>) is likely to be involved, such as dandruff, seborrhoeic dermatitis, and tinea (pityriasis) versicolor.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Nizoder 2% shampoo is a broad-spectrum imidazole-dioxolane, synthetic antifungal agent that inhibits the in vitro growth of the following common dermatophytes and yeasts by altering the permeability of the cell membrane:</p><ul><li>Dermatophytes: <em>Trichophyton rubrum, T. mentagrophytes, T. tonsurans, Microsporum canis, M. audouini, M. gypseum</em> and <em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em>;</li><li>Yeasts: <em>Candida albicans, Malassezia ovale (Pityrosporum ovale)</em> and <em>C. tropicalis</em>;</li><li>Organism responsible for tinea versicolor: <em>Malassezia furfur</em> (Pityrosporum orbiculare).</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Nizoder 2%</strong></p><p>For topical administration in adults, elderly, and children:</p><ul><li><strong>Treatment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dandruff and seborrhoeic dermatitis</strong>: Wash hair twice weekly for 2-4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea versicolor</strong>: Once daily for a maximum of 5 days.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dandruff and seborrhoeic dermatitis</strong>: Use once every 1-2 weeks.</li><li><strong>Tinea versicolor</strong>: Once daily for a maximum of 3 days before exposure to sunshine.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If clinical improvement is not observed after the treatment period, the diagnosis should be reconsidered.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Nizoder 2%</strong></p><p>No information is available for the shampoo form of Nizoder 2%.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Nizoder 2% therapeutic shampoo is contraindicated in individuals who have shown hypersensitivity to Nizoder 2% or any of the excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Nizoder 2%</strong></p><p>In 11 double-blind trials involving 264 patients using Nizoder 2% shampoo for the treatment of dandruff or seborrheic dermatitis, an increase in normal hair loss and irritation occurred in less than 1% of patients.</p><p>In three open-label safety trials in which 41 patients shampooed 4-10 times weekly for six months, the following adverse experiences occurred once: abnormal hair texture, scalp pustules, mild dryness of the skin, and itching.</p><p>Other common side effects reported include oiliness and dryness of the hair and scalp.</p><p>In a double-blind, placebo-controlled trial with patients suffering from tinea versicolor, drug-related adverse events occurred in 5 (5%), 7 (7%), and 4 (4%) patients in the respective treatment groups. The events reported were pruritus, application site reaction, and dry skin, none of which occurred in more than 3% of patients in any treatment group. Rare reports of hair discoloration have been noted globally.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: There are no well-controlled studies in pregnant or lactating women. Limited data on exposed pregnancies show no adverse effects of topical Nizoder 2% on pregnancy or the health of the fetus/newborn.</li><li>Animal studies indicate reproductive toxicity at doses not relevant to topical administration.</li><li>No effects on the breastfed newborn or infant are anticipated.</li><li>Plasma concentrations of Nizoder 2% were undetectable after topical application on the scalp but were detectable on the whole body.</li><li>Nizoder 2% shampoo should only be used in pregnancy if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In patients on prolonged treatment with topical corticosteroids, it is recommended to gradually withdraw the steroid therapy over 2-3 weeks while using Nizoder 2% shampoo to avoid a potential rebound effect.</li><li>Avoid contact with the eyes. If the shampoo comes into contact with the eyes, rinse immediately with plenty of water.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Nizoder 2%</strong></p><p>No specific information is available on overdose treatment for Nizoder 2% shampoo. In the event of accidental ingestion, supportive and symptomatic measures should be carried out.</p><p>To avoid aspiration, neither emesis nor gastric lavage should be initiated.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at temperatures below 25°C, away from heat and light. Do not store in the bathroom. Keep away from children and pets.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in children have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Normal adult dosage is appropriate.</li></ul>', 'active', '724_1742536666.png', 8, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 10:57:46', '2025-05-04 00:44:32'),
(3853, 'Lansodin', 'Lansoprazole', 3.51, 4, 'capsule', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '15mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Lansodin 15 mg</h3><h4><strong>Indications</strong></h4><p>Lansodin 15 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ol><li>Short-term treatment of active duodenal ulcers.</li><li>Maintenance of healed duodenal ulcers.</li><li>Short-term treatment of active benign gastric ulcers.</li><li>Short-term treatment of active erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Maintenance of healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Pathological hypersecretory conditions including Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</li><li>H. pylori eradication to reduce the risk of duodenal ulcer recurrence.</li></ol><h4><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h4><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</li></ul><h4><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h4><p>Lansodin 15 mg is a substituted benzimidazole, which works as a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI). It inhibits the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system in gastric parietal cells, blocking the final step in acid secretion. Both basal and stimulated acid secretion are inhibited.</p><h4><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h4><ol><li><strong>Benign Gastric Ulcer:</strong> 30 mg daily in the morning for 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer:</strong> 30 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks; maintenance 15 mg.</li><li><strong>NSAID-associated Duodenal or Gastric Ulcer:</strong> 15-30 mg daily for 4 weeks, followed by a further 4 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome (and other hypersecretory conditions):</strong> Initially 60 mg once daily, adjusted according to response. Daily doses of 120 mg or more are given in two divided doses.</li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD):</strong> 30 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, followed by a further 4 weeks if not fully healed. Maintenance 15-30 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia:</strong> 15-30 mg daily in the morning for 2-4 weeks.</li></ol><h4><strong>Interactions</strong></h4><p>Lansodin 15 mg is a selective inhibitor of the cytochrome P-450 monooxygenase system. While there may be effects on hepatic clearance, there have been no clinically relevant interactions reported. The potential effect on the oral combined contraceptive pill is still under assessment. Long-term use can influence bacterial colonization in the stomach and duodenum, as well as affect vitamin B12 absorption.</p><h4><strong>Contraindications</strong></h4><p>Lansodin 15 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</p><h4><strong>Side Effects</strong></h4><ol><li><strong>Severe/Irreversible:</strong> Potential induction of carcinoid tumors due to profound acid suppression and an increase in serum gastrin levels, which may persist in long-term treatment. It may also accelerate the development of atrophic gastritis in patients with H. pylori infection.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic:</strong> Dose-dependent diarrhea (4% at 30 mg/day, 8% at 60 mg/day) and headache (2-3% of treated patients).</li></ol><h4><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h4><p>Lansodin 15 mg should be avoided during pregnancy unless there are compelling reasons.</p><h4><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h4><ol><li>Gastric malignancy should be ruled out before treatment.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with hepatic impairment.</li><li>Monitor closely during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ol><h4><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h4><p>Store at 25°C.</p><h4><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Neonates:</strong> No relevant data is available. Not recommended for neonates.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> The youngest person in clinical trials was 13 years old.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> No issues have been reported in the elderly, and there is no increase in adverse reactions in this population.</li></ul><h4><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h4><p>Lansodin 15 mg is a substituted benzimidazole that blocks the final step of acid secretion in the stomach by inhibiting the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system in gastric parietal cells, suppressing both basal and stimulated acid production.</p><h4><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h4><p>Lansodin 15 mg should be avoided in pregnancy unless there are compelling reasons for its use.</p><h4><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Neonates:</strong> Not recommended due to lack of relevant data.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> Clinical trials have included individuals as young as 13 years old.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Safe to use with no observed increase in adverse reactions.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '574_1742537265.webp', 5, 12, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 11:07:45', '2025-03-21 11:07:45'),
(3854, 'Xorel', 'Rabeprazole Sodium', 4.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xorel 20 mg (Rabeprazole Gastro-resistant Tablets)</strong></p><p>Xorel 20 mg is recommended for the treatment of:</p><ol><li><strong>Active Duodenal Ulcer</strong></li><li><strong>Active Benign Gastric Ulcer</strong></li><li><strong>Symptomatic Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</strong> – Erosive or Ulcerative</li><li><strong>Long-Term Management of GERD</strong></li><li><strong>Moderate to Severe GERD Symptoms</strong></li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong></li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori Eradication</strong> – In combination with suitable antibacterial regimens for peptic ulcer disease.</li></ol><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rabeprazole works by inhibiting the gastric H+/K+-ATPase at the secretory surface of the stomach parietal cell, which reduces gastric acid secretion. It is considered an inhibitor of the gastric proton pump.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xorel 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily in the morning for 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Erosive or Ulcerative GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 to 8 weeks. An additional 8-week course may be considered if symptoms persist.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve, further treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>GERD Maintenance (Long-Term):</strong> 20 mg once daily. Studies do not exceed 12 months.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> Starting dose of 60 mg daily, adjust as needed up to 100 mg once daily or 60 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori Eradication:</strong> Rabeprazole 20 mg, Amoxicillin 1000 mg, Clarithromycin 500 mg, taken twice daily for 7 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><ul><li>Take in the morning before meals.</li><li>Swallow tablets whole; do not chew or crush.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs like <strong>Itraconazole</strong> and <strong>Ketoconazole</strong> may interact, potentially lowering their plasma levels.</li><li><strong>Atazanavir</strong> should not be taken with proton pump inhibitors like rabeprazole due to pH-dependent absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient.</li><li>Not recommended during pregnancy and breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Headache, nausea, vomiting, constipation, dry mouth, muscle pain, sleepiness, dizziness.</li><li>These side effects are typically mild and reversible.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>FDA Pregnancy Category C:</strong> No controlled studies on pregnant women, and rabeprazole may be excreted in breast milk. Consider the medication\'s importance to the mother when deciding to continue breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Rule out malignancy before initiating treatment.</li><li>Long-term therapy may increase the risk of fractures in elderly patients, especially with risk factors for osteoporosis.</li><li>Regular monitoring is recommended for prolonged use (more than 1 year).</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>No specific antidote for overdose.</li><li>Effects are typically mild and reversible without additional medical assistance.</li><li>Rabeprazole is highly protein-bound and cannot be dialyzed.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and effectiveness for children are not established.</li><li>No dosage adjustments needed for patients with hepatic or renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Rabeprazole reduces gastric acid secretion by inhibiting the H+/K+-ATPase enzyme at the parietal cells of the stomach.</p>', 'active', '919_1742537485.webp', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 11:11:25', '2025-05-05 20:50:24');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3855, 'Nizoder', 'Ketoconazole', 90.00, 1, 'x 30 gm tube', '30 gm tube', 'Cream', '2%', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the provided information with headings in bold text:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Nizoder 2%</strong></p><p>Nizoder 2% therapeutic shampoo is used for prevention and treatment of infections in which the yeast <em>Malassezia furfur</em> (previously called <em>Pityrosporum orbiculare</em>) is likely to be involved, such as dandruff, seborrhoeic dermatitis, and tinea (pityriasis) versicolor.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Nizoder 2% shampoo is a broad-spectrum imidazole-dioxolane, synthetic antifungal agent that inhibits the in vitro growth of the following common dermatophytes and yeasts by altering the permeability of the cell membrane:</p><ul><li>Dermatophytes: <em>Trichophyton rubrum</em>, <em>T. mentagrophytes</em>, <em>T. tonsurans</em>, <em>Microsporum canis</em>, <em>M. audouini</em>, <em>M. gypseum</em>, and <em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em></li><li>Yeasts: <em>Candida albicans</em>, <em>Malassezia ovale</em> (Pityrosporum ovale), <em>C. tropicalis</em>, and the organism responsible for tinea versicolor, <em>Malassezia furfur</em> (Pityrosporum orbiculare).</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Nizoder 2%</strong></p><p>For topical administration in adults, elderly, and children:</p><ul><li>Wash affected areas and leave for 3-5 minutes before rinsing.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment:</strong>Dandruff and seborrhoeic dermatitis: Wash hair twice weekly for 2-4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tinea versicolor: Once daily for a maximum of 5 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis:</strong>Dandruff and seborrhoeic dermatitis: Use once every 1-2 weeks.</li><li>Tinea versicolor: Once daily for a maximum of 3 days before exposure to sunshine.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If a patient shows no clinical improvement after the treatment period, the diagnosis should be redetermined.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Nizoder 2%</strong></p><p>No information was found for the shampoo form of Nizoder 2%.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Nizoder 2% therapeutic shampoo is contraindicated in persons who have shown hypersensitivity to Nizoder 2% or any of the excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Nizoder 2%</strong></p><p>In 11 double-blind trials with 264 patients using Nizoder 2% shampoo for the treatment of dandruff or seborrheic dermatitis, an increase in normal hair loss and irritation occurred in less than 1% of patients. In three open-label safety trials with 41 patients shampooing 4-10 times weekly for six months, the following adverse experiences each occurred once: abnormal hair texture, scalp pustules, mild dryness of the skin, and itching. As with other shampoos, oiliness and dryness of hair and scalp have been reported.</p><p>In a double-blind, placebo-controlled trial in which patients with tinea versicolor were treated with either a single application of Nizoder 2% shampoo (n=106), a daily application for three consecutive days (n=107), or placebo (n=105), drug-related adverse events occurred in 5 (5%), 7 (7%), and 4 (4%) of patients, respectively. The only events that occurred in more than one patient in any one of the three treatment groups were pruritus, application site reaction, and dry skin; none of these events occurred in more than 3% of the patients in any one of the three groups. In worldwide experience with Nizoder 2% shampoo, there have been rare reports of hair discoloration.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant or lactating women. Data on a limited number of exposed pregnancies indicate no adverse effects of topical Nizoder 2% on pregnancy or on the health of the fetus/newborn child. Animal studies have shown reproductive toxicity at doses that are not relevant to the topical administration of Nizoder 2%. No effects on the breastfed newborn/infant are anticipated. Plasma concentrations of Nizoder 2% were not detectable after topical administration of Nizoder 2% shampoo to the scalp of non-pregnant humans. Plasma levels were detected after topical administration of Nizoder 2% shampoo on the whole body. There are no known risks associated with the use of Nizoder 2% shampoo in pregnancy or lactation. Nizoder 2% shampoo should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In patients who have been on prolonged treatment with topical corticosteroids, it is recommended that the steroid therapy be gradually withdrawn over a period of 2 to 3 weeks while using Nizoder 2% therapeutic shampoo, to prevent any potential rebound effect. Avoid contact with eyes. If the shampoo enters the eyes, rinse immediately with plenty of water.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Nizoder 2%</strong></p><p>No specific information is available on the treatment of overdosage with Nizoder 2% shampoo unless taken at doses higher than the recommended dose. In the event of accidental ingestion, supportive and symptomatic measures should be carried out. In order to avoid aspiration, neither emesis nor gastric lavage should be instigated.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature below 25°C away from heat and light. Do not store in the bathroom. Keep away from children and pets.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in children have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Normal adult dosage is appropriate.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further formatting or clarification!</p>', 'active', '1111_1742537596.webp', 8, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 11:13:16', '2025-05-04 00:44:59'),
(3856, 'Lansec', 'Lansoprazole', 3.55, 10, 'capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '15mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the text with headings changed to normal bold text:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Lansec 15 mg</strong></p><p> Lansec 15 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Short-term treatment of active duodenal ulcer</li><li>Maintenance of healed duodenal ulcers</li><li>Short-term treatment of active benign gastric ulcers</li><li>Short-term treatment of active erosive esophagitis</li><li>Maintenance of healing of erosive esophagitis</li><li>Pathological hypersecretory conditions including Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</li><li>H. pylori eradication to reduce the risk of duodenal ulcer recurrence</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Lansec 15 mg is a substituted benzimidazole and is also known as a PPI due to its property of blocking the final step of acid secretion by inhibiting the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system in gastric parietal cells. Both basal and stimulated acid secretion are inhibited.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Lansec 15 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Benign gastric ulcer:</strong> 30 mg daily in the morning for 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer:</strong> 30 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks; maintenance 15 mg.</li><li><strong>NSAID-associated duodenal or gastric ulcer:</strong> 15-30 mg daily for 4 weeks, followed by a further 4 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (and other hypersecretory conditions):</strong> Initially 60 mg once daily, adjusted according to response; daily doses of 120 mg or more are given in two divided doses.</li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease:</strong> 30 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, followed by a further 4 weeks if not fully healed; maintenance 15-30 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Acid-related dyspepsia:</strong> 15-30 mg daily in the morning for 2-4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Lansec 15 mg</strong></p><p> Lansec 15 mg appears to be a selective inhibitor of the cytochrome P-450 monooxygenase system. There may be an effect on hepatic clearance, but no clinically relevant interactions have been reported to date. The effect of Lansec 15 mg on oral combined contraceptive pills is under further assessment. Physiological changes similar to those found with Omeprazole are likely, affecting bacterial colonization of the stomach and duodenum and vitamin B12 absorption.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Lansec 15 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Lansec 15 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Severe or irreversible adverse effects:</strong> Profound acid suppression may induce carcinoid tumors and elevate serum gastrin levels. Long-term PPI use in Helicobacter pylori infection may accelerate atrophic gastritis development.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic adverse effects:</strong> Dose-dependent diarrhea (4% at 30 mg/day, 8% at 60 mg/day) and headache (2-3% of patients).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Lansec 15 mg should be avoided during pregnancy unless there are compelling reasons.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Rule out gastric malignancy before use.</li><li>Caution in hepatic impairment.</li><li>Avoid in pregnancy and lactation unless necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at 25°C.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neonates:</strong> No relevant human data; not recommended for neonates.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> The youngest participant in clinical trials was 13 years old.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> No significant adverse drug reactions have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Lansec 15 mg is a substituted benzimidazole and works by inhibiting the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system in gastric parietal cells, blocking both basal and stimulated acid secretion.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Lansec 15 mg should be avoided in pregnancy unless there are compelling reasons.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neonates:</strong> No relevant human data; not recommended.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> The youngest participant in clinical trials was 13 years old.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> No increased adverse reactions reported.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '236_1742538304.webp', 9, 12, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 11:25:04', '2025-03-21 11:25:04'),
(3857, 'Alben', 'Albendazole', 23.00, 1, 'x 10 ml bottle', '10 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '10 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Alben 10 ml</strong></p><p>Alben 10 ml is indicated for single and mixed infestations of:</p><ul><li>Hookworm (Ancylostoma, Necator)</li><li>Roundworm (Ascaris)</li><li>Threadworm (Enterobius)</li><li>Whipworm (Trichuris)</li><li>Strongyloides</li><li>Tapeworm</li><li>Opisthorchi</li><li>Hydatid</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Anthelmintic</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Alben 10 ml is a broad-spectrum benzimidazole anthelminthic drug. In addition to its vermicidal properties, it has both ovicidal and larvicidal activities.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Alben 10 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; children over 2 years</strong>: 400 mg (1 tablet or 10 ml suspension) as a single dose for Enterobius vermicularis, Trichuris trichiura, Ascaris lumbricoides, Ancylostoma duodenale, and Necator americanus. For strongyloidiasis or taeniasis, 400 mg (1 tablet or 10 ml suspension) daily for 3 consecutive days. If not cured after 3 weeks, a second course is indicated.</li><li><strong>Children of 1-2 years</strong>: 200 mg (5 ml suspension) as a single dose.</li><li><strong>Children under 1 year</strong>: Not recommended.</li><li><strong>In Hydatid Disease (Echinococcosis)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For patients &gt;60 kg: 400 mg twice daily for 28 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For patients &lt;60 kg: 15 mg/kg body weight daily in two divided doses (max 800 mg/day).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Cystic echinococcosis: The 28-day course may be repeated after 14 days without treatment, for up to 3 treatment cycles.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Alveolar echinococcosis: Cycles of 28 days of treatment followed by 14 days without treatment, which may continue for months or years.</li><li><strong>Giardiasis</strong>: 400 mg (1 tablet or 10 ml suspension) once daily for 5 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Alben 10 ml</strong></p><p>No interaction involving Alben 10 ml, either pharmacodynamic or pharmacokinetic, has been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neonates</strong>: Alben 10 ml is not recommended for neonates.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Dose reduction to 200 mg may be indicated for children weighing &lt;10 kg, but no general reduction in dosage is recommended.</li><li><strong>Pregnant Women</strong>: Should not be given during pregnancy or in women suspected to be pregnant. No information is available on placental transfer.</li><li><strong>Concurrent Disease</strong>: No evidence suggests dose alterations for renal, hepatic, or cardiac failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Alben 10 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal disturbances</li><li>Headache</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Changes in liver enzymes</li><li>Rarely, reversible alopecia, rash, fever</li><li>Blood disorders, including leucopenia and pancytopenia</li><li>Allergic shock in case of cyst leakage</li><li>Convulsions and meningism in cerebral disease</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Alben 10 ml is in US FDA Pregnancy category C. It should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits outweigh the risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Perform blood counts and liver function tests before treatment and twice during each cycle.</li><li>Breastfeeding: Exclude pregnancy before starting treatment.</li><li>Alben 10 ml should only be used in treating Echinococcosis under constant medical supervision with regular monitoring of serum transaminase concentrations, leucocyte, and platelet counts.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Anthelmintic</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Alben 10 ml is a broad-spectrum anthelmintic that exhibits vermicidal, ovicidal, and larvicidal activities. It works by blocking glucose uptake in susceptible helminths, depleting their energy reserves, and causing immobilization. The drug also inhibits tubulin polymerization in parasites. It is metabolized primarily in the liver, and its principal metabolite, Alben 10 ml sulphoxide, has anthelmintic activity. It is poorly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>US FDA Pregnancy category C. Alben 10 ml should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the benefits outweigh the risks to the fetus.</p>', 'active', '780_1742538434.png', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 11:27:14', '2025-05-02 22:53:37'),
(3858, 'Arokast', 'Montelukast Sodium', 16.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Arokast 10 mg</strong></h3><p>Arokast 10 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</strong></li><li><strong>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR)</strong>: Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Arokast 10 mg works by inhibiting cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4), which are produced from arachidonic acid metabolism in cells like mast cells and eosinophils. These leukotrienes bind to cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) in the human airway. Arokast has a high affinity for the CysLT1 receptor and inhibits the physiological effects of LTD4 without any agonist activity at this receptor.</p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (15 years and older)</strong>: 10 mg tablet once daily</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients (6 to 14 years)</strong>: 5 mg tablet once daily</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients (2 to 5 years)</strong>: 4 mg tablet once daily</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients (6 months to 5 years)</strong>: 4 mg oral granules once daily (can be mixed with cold water or soft food)</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage adjustment required for mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage adjustment recommended.</p><p><strong>Elderly Use</strong>: The pharmacokinetic profile is similar between elderly and younger adults. No dosage adjustment is required, but the plasma half-life may be slightly longer in elderly patients.</p><h3><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives, terfenadine, digoxin, warfarin</strong>: No clinically significant interactions observed.</li><li><strong>Phenobarbital and rifampin</strong>: These powerful cytochrome P450 enzyme inducers reduce Arokast\'s AUC by approximately 40%. Clinical monitoring is advised.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to any ingredient in Arokast.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, vomiting</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Anxiety, arthralgia, dizziness, dry mouth, irritability, muscle complaints, oedema, sleep disturbances</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, hepatic problems, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: No controlled studies in humans; use only if necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Arokast is secreted in breast milk, but its effects on nursing infants are not well studied. Caution is advised.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Not recommended for reversal of acute bronchospasm in asthma attacks.</li><li>Should not be used as a sole treatment for exercise-induced bronchospasm.</li><li>Patients with <strong>aspirin sensitivity</strong> should continue to avoid aspirin and NSAIDs.</li><li>Asthma exacerbations can still be treated with rescue medication as needed.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Overdose side effects are typically nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, and headache. Supportive measures like removing unabsorbed material and clinical monitoring should be taken.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry environment, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li>Arokast 10 mg is a selective leukotriene receptor antagonist that blocks the cysteinyl leukotriene receptor (CysLT1), which plays a role in airway inflammation and constriction in asthma and allergic rhinitis.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h3><ul><li>Arokast crosses the placenta in animal studies. Use during pregnancy should only occur if necessary, as there are no well-controlled human studies. Caution is advised for breastfeeding women as the drug is secreted in breast milk.</li></ul>', 'active', '1880_1742538665.jpg', 62, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 11:31:05', '2025-05-02 23:55:49'),
(3859, 'Zerocal Stevia', 'Steviol Glycoside', 4.00, 1, 'packet', '30\'s pack', 'Oral Powder', 'N/A', 'yes', 'no', '<p>,</p>', 'active', '1717_1742538724.webp', 4, 12, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 11:32:04', '2025-05-13 07:25:49'),
(3860, 'Ovocal-D', 'Eggshell Calcium + Vitamin D3', 13.00, 13, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg plus 200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ovocal-D</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment and prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, and osteogenesis. It can be used as a supplement in cases of inadequate calcium intake in childhood diets, pregnancy and lactation, rickets, and poor absorption during old age. Other indications include pancreatitis and as a phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral and vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Calcium is absorbed in the intestine, which then passes through the intestine into the blood and is deposited into bone. Active transport through cells is dependent on Vitamin D3. Vitamin D3 helps keep bones healthy by increasing the absorption of calcium in the intestines. Without Vitamin D3, the body can only absorb 10% to 15% of the calcium from the diet.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ovocal-D</strong></p><p>One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or food as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Ovocal-D</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Major Interaction:</strong> Administering intravenous Ceftriaxone and Calcium can result in life-threatening damage to the lungs and kidneys. Calcium should not be administered intravenously within 48 hours of intravenous Ceftriaxone.</li><li><strong>Minor Interaction:</strong> Calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline and fluoride preparations. A minimum of 3 hours should be allowed between ingestion of these medications. Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of calcium. Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce the metabolism of Vitamin D3. Intake of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of calcium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Increased activity of the parathyroid gland, hypercalcemia, constipation, kidney stones, and extreme loss of body water are conditions in which calcium intake is contraindicated.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ovocal-D</strong></p><p>Flatulence, diarrhea, constipation, and allergic reactions, etc., are rare. Hypercalcemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This should be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of a physician. Toxicity was tested on pregnant and lactating females with excellent results.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In mild hypercalciuria, reducing the dose is sufficient to return to normal serum calcium concentration. Patients with kidney stones or a history of them should be advised to increase their fluid intake.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</p>', 'active', '1960_1742538887.jpg', 55, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 11:34:47', '2025-04-18 22:08:59'),
(3861, 'Ceevit', 'Vitamin C [Ascorbic acid]', 40.00, 1, 'syrup', '1 bottle', 'Syrup', '100mg/5ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a well-organized and concise summary of the information you provided about <strong>Ceevit 100 ml (Ascorbic Acid - Vitamin C):</strong></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p>Ceevit 100 ml is used for:</p><ul><li>Preventing &amp; treating <strong>scurvy</strong></li><li><strong>Wound healing</strong> &amp; <strong>bone repair</strong></li><li>Collagen formation in <strong>connective tissues</strong></li><li>Enhancing <strong>iron absorption</strong> &amp; treating <strong>anemia</strong></li><li>Supporting <strong>hemoglobin</strong> &amp; <strong>red blood cell</strong> production</li><li>Strengthening the <strong>immune system</strong></li><li>Reducing risk of <strong>common cold</strong> &amp; <strong>infections</strong></li><li>Maintaining <strong>healthy capillaries, gums, and teeth</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Vitamin-C Preparations</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Vitamin C is a <strong>water-soluble vitamin</strong> essential for:</p><ul><li><strong>Biological oxidation-reduction reactions</strong></li><li><strong>Collagen synthesis</strong></li><li><strong>Cellular respiration</strong></li><li>Deficiency leads to <strong>scurvy</strong>, characterized by <strong>bleeding gums, anemia, bone lesions, and poor wound healing</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><h4><strong>Oral Administration</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Prevention of scurvy:</strong> 1 tablet daily</li><li><strong>Treatment of scurvy:</strong> 1-2 tablets daily (may increase based on severity)</li><li><strong>Stroke risk reduction (elderly):</strong> 1-2 tablets daily</li><li><strong>General use:</strong> 1 tablet daily or as per physician’s advice</li><li><strong>Maximum safe dose:</strong> 2000 mg/day (divided doses)</li></ul><p><strong>Sachet:</strong> 1 sachet daily (max 4 sachets/day)</p><h4><strong>Parenteral Administration (Injection: IM, IV, SC)</strong></h4><p>Used when oral administration is not possible.</p><ul><li><strong>General protective dose:</strong> 70-150 mg/day</li><li><strong>Scurvy treatment:</strong> 300 mg - 1 g/day</li><li><strong>Wound healing:</strong> 300-500 mg/day for 7-10 days</li><li><strong>Severe burns:</strong> 1-2 g/day</li><li><strong>Higher doses (e.g., 6 g) have been used safely</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Note:</strong> IV injections should be diluted in Normal Saline, Water for Injection, or Glucose to minimize adverse reactions.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Interactions</strong></h3><p><strong>Incompatible with:</strong></p><ul><li>Aminophylline, Bleomycin, Erythromycin Lactobionate, Nafcillin, Nitrofurantoin Sodium, Sodium Bicarbonate, and some other medications.</li></ul><p><strong>Useful interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Increases Paracetamol half-life</strong></li><li><strong>Enhances iron absorption</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Generally safe, but high doses may cause:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Diarrhea, bloating</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Iron overload (harmful in thalassemia, anemia, hemochromatosis)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Hyperoxaluria, hyperuricosuria (kidney-related issues)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Hemolysis (in G6PD deficiency)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Risk of fetal abortion (if taken &gt;5 g/day during pregnancy)</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Safe in normal doses</strong></li><li><strong>High doses (&gt;5 g/day) may cause abortion</strong></li><li><strong>Safe during lactation</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Mega doses (&gt;1000 mg/day) in pregnancy may cause neonatal scurvy</strong></li><li><strong>Avoid in hyperoxaluria patients</strong></li><li>May cause <strong>false-positive lab test results</strong> for glucose, uric acid, creatinine, and occult blood.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage</strong></h3><p>Store in a <strong>dry place below 30°C</strong></p>', 'active', '126_1742541029.webp', 4, 12, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 11:38:32', '2025-03-21 12:10:29'),
(3862, 'Ridon EG', 'Domperidone Maleate', 6.00, 1, 'x 10 mg sachet', '10 mg sachet', 'Sachet', '10 mg/sachet', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Ridon EG 10 mg/3 gm</strong></h3><p>Ridon EG 10 mg/3 gm is used to treat dyspeptic symptom complexes, which are often associated with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis. Symptoms include:</p><ul><li><strong>Epigastric sensation of fullness</strong></li><li><strong>Abdominal distension</strong></li><li><strong>Upper abdominal pain</strong></li><li><strong>Belching</strong></li><li><strong>Flatulence</strong></li><li><strong>Early satiety</strong></li><li><strong>Nausea and vomiting</strong></li><li><strong>Heartburn</strong>, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms may occur in conditions such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of various origins (functional, organic, infectious, dietetic, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine). In Parkinson\'s disease, this may include dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting. Ridon EG is also used in radiological studies to speed up barium transit in follow-through examinations.</p><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Motility Stimulants</strong></li><li><strong>Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist</strong></li><li><strong>Prokinetic drugs</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Ridon EG 10 mg/3 gm acts as a <strong>dopamine receptor antagonist</strong>, exerting gastroprokinetic effects by inhibiting dopamine receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. It has a limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier, minimizing the impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain and thus reducing psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours.</li></ul><p>For acute nausea and vomiting, the maximum treatment duration is <strong>12 weeks</strong>. The medication should be taken <strong>15-30 minutes before meals</strong>.</p><h4><strong>Adult Dosage</strong></h4><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every <strong>6-8 hours daily</strong>.</li><li>Maximum daily dose: <strong>80 mg</strong>.</li></ul><h4><strong>Children\'s Dosage</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Suspension</strong>: 2-4 ml per 10 kg body weight every 6-8 hours.</li><li><strong>Pediatric drops</strong>: 0.4-0.8 ml per 10 kg body weight every 6-8 hours.</li></ul><h4><strong>Acute and Sub-acute Conditions</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours.</li></ul><h4><strong>Rectal Administration</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li><li><strong>Children (10-25 kg)</strong>: Maximum daily dose is 30 mg.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>CYP3A4 Inhibitors</strong> (e.g., azole antifungals, macrolides) may increase plasma levels of Ridon EG.</li><li><strong>Antacids</strong> and <strong>Antisecretory drugs</strong> should not be taken with Ridon EG as they reduce its bioavailability.</li><li><strong>Anticholinergic drugs</strong> may counteract the antidyspeptic effects.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to Ridon EG</li><li><strong>Neonates</strong></li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation</strong></li><li><strong>Prolactin-releasing pituitary tumors (prolactinoma)</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Rare: <strong>Intestinal cramps</strong>, <strong>extrapyramidal symptoms</strong> (mainly in children), <strong>hyperprolactinemia</strong> (leading to galactorrhea, gynecomastia).</li><li><strong>Allergic reactions</strong>: Rash, urticaria.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Safety not established; <strong>not recommended</strong> during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Excreted in breast milk in small amounts; minimal harmful effects.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Use cautiously in <strong>children</strong> and <strong>hepatic impairment</strong>.</li><li><strong>Severe renal insufficiency</strong> may require dose adjustments, especially for prolonged therapy.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><p>Signs of overdose include <strong>drowsiness</strong>, <strong>disorientation</strong>, and <strong>extrapyramidal reactions</strong> (especially in children). Activated charcoal may be used for overdose management.</p><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, <strong>protected from light &amp; moisture</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of <strong>children\'s reach</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Ridon EG 10 mg/3 gm works as a <strong>dopamine antagonist</strong> that promotes gastric emptying, enhances gastrointestinal motility, and helps regulate pyloric contraction. It reduces <strong>esophageal sphincter pressure</strong>, preventing gastric contents from regurgitating into the mouth.</p><h3><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></h3><p>Use in <strong>infants</strong> with great caution, as their blood-brain barrier is not fully developed. There may be a risk of neurological side effects, although rare.</p><h3><strong>Use in Liver &amp; Kidney Disorders</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Liver</strong>: Caution in hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Kidney</strong>: Adjust dose frequency in <strong>severe renal insufficiency</strong>.</li></ul>', 'active', '250_1742539133.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 11:38:53', '2025-04-16 20:01:01'),
(3863, 'ConviHaler', 'Inhaler device', 300.00, 1, 'Inhaler', '1', 'Inhaler', '0', 'yes', 'no', '<h2>Medicine overview</h2>', 'active', '1889_1742540994.webp', 2, 12, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 11:42:14', '2025-03-21 12:09:54'),
(3864, 'D3', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 120.00, 1, 'x 1 ml ampoule', '1 ml ampoule', 'Injection', '200000 IU/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of D3 200000 IU/ml</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency.</li><li>Adjunct therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcaemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency.</li><li>Essential for normal bone growth, maintenance of bone density, and utilization of calcium and phosphorus.</li><li>Necessary for healthy growth and development in babies and acts as a hormone.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin D3 helps with the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus.</li><li>Reduces bacterial infection severity, improves lung function, reduces cancer risk (breast, colorectal), and helps maintain insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of D3 200000 IU/ml:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Capsule:</strong></li></ol><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Maintenance therapy: 1,400-2,000 IU/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 20,000 IU every 4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Addition to osteoporosis therapy: 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li><strong>Children (12-18 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 20,000 IU every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Film-coated Tablets:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1,000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily, or as directed by a physician.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Syrup:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>At risk of Cholecalciferol deficiency:</strong>0-1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml).</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml).</blockquote><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Deficient patients:</strong>0-1 year: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 years: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Injection:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention (Infants receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk):</strong>1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e., 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Infants or young children (not receiving enriched milk):</strong>1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e., 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adolescents:</strong>1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e., 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pregnancy:</strong>1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e., 100,000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly:</strong>1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e., 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>In special conditions (e.g., digestive disorders):</strong>1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e., 100,000 IU or 1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e., 200,000 IU every 3 or 6 months.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction:</strong></p><ul><li>Interferes with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (like liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypercalcaemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, abdominal pain.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Studies show safe use of doses up to 4,000 IU during pregnancy.</li><li>The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU, but a higher dose may be necessary for Vitamin D3-deficient women.</li><li>Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. Overdose in infants induced by nursing mothers has not been observed. When prescribing additional vitamin D3 to a breastfed child, the additional dose given to the mother should be considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Can lead to hypervitaminosis D.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30º C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in children under 12 years have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Helps in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus.</li><li>Essential for normal bone growth, maintaining bone density, reducing bacterial infection severity, improving lung function, reducing cancer risk, and maintaining insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses:</strong></p><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</li></ul>', 'active', '808_1742539491.jpg', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 11:44:51', '2025-05-01 19:55:30'),
(3865, 'Xylovin', 'Xylometazoline Hydrochloride', 18.00, 1, 'x 15 ml drop', '15 ml drop', 'Nasal Drop', '0.05%', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the formatted text with the heading changed to normal bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Xylovin 0.05%</strong></p><p>For the symptomatic relief of nasal congestion, perennial allergic rhinitis (including hay fever), sinusitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Nasal Anti-histamine preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Xylovin 0.05% is a sympathomimetic agent with marked alpha-adrenergic activity, and is intended for use in the nose. It constricts the nasal blood vessels, thereby decongesting the mucosa of the nose and neighboring regions of the pharynx. This enables patients suffering from colds to breathe more easily through the nose. The effect of Xylovin 0.05% begins within a few minutes and persists for several hours. Xylovin 0.05% is generally well tolerated and does not impair the function of ciliated epithelium. Systemic absorption may occur following nasal application of Xylovin 0.05%.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xylovin 0.05%</strong></p><p><strong>Adults:</strong> 2 or 3 drops of Xylovin 0.05% Adult formula (0.1%) two to three times daily. This adult formula should not be used for children under the age of 12 years.</p><p><strong>Children under 12 years:</strong> 1 or 2 drops of the Xylovin 0.05% children\'s formula (0.05%) in each nostril once or twice daily. Not to be used in infants less than 3 months.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Xylovin 0.05%</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Xylovin 0.05% nasal drops is contraindicated in patients with trans-sphenoidal hypophysectomy or surgery exposing the dura mater. It is also contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to Xylovin 0.05%.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xylovin 0.05%</strong></p><p>The following side effects have occasionally been encountered: a burning sensation in the nose and throat, local irritation, nausea, headache, and dryness of the nasal mucosa. Systemic cardiovascular effects have occurred, and this should be kept in mind when giving Xylovin 0.05% to people with cardiovascular disease.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>No fetal toxicity or fertility studies have been carried out in animals. In view of its potential systemic vasoconstrictor effect, it is advisable to take the precaution of not using Xylovin 0.05% during pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Each Xylovin 0.05% pack should be used by one person only to prevent any cross-infection. Patients are advised not to take decongestants for more than seven consecutive days.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Protect from heat. For reasons of hygiene, do not use the bottle more than 28 days after opening it.</p>', 'active', '6551_1746634770.webp', 43, 12, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 11:47:44', '2025-05-07 20:19:30'),
(3866, 'Zilon', 'Omeprazole', 5.02, 6, 'capsule', '6', 'Capsule', '20mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p>Zilon 20 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcers</li><li>Gastric and duodenal ulcers caused by NSAIDs</li><li>Prevention of NSAID-induced ulcers in patients with a history of ulcers</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</li><li>Long-term management of acid reflux disease</li><li>Acid-related dyspepsia</li><li>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers</li><li>Prevention of acid aspiration during general anesthesia</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Peptic ulcers caused by <em>Helicobacter pylori</em></li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Zilon 20 mg is a benzimidazole-derived proton pump inhibitor that suppresses gastric acid secretion. It works by inhibiting the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in gastric parietal cells. The onset of action occurs within 1 hour, peaks at 2 hours, and lasts up to 72 hours. Acid secretion resumes within 3 to 5 days after discontinuation.</p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><h4><strong>Oral Administration</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Benign Gastric &amp; Duodenal Ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks (duodenal ulcer) or 8 weeks (gastric ulcer); severe cases may require 40 mg daily.</li><li><strong>NSAID-Associated Ulcers:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks; prophylaxis: 20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks; maintenance dose: 20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Long-term Acid Reflux Management:</strong> 10-20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Acid-Related Dyspepsia:</strong> 10-20 mg daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Acid Aspiration Prophylaxis:</strong> 40 mg the evening before surgery, followed by 40 mg 2-6 hours before surgery.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> Initially 60 mg daily; dose range: 20-120 mg/day (doses &gt;80 mg should be divided into two).</li><li><strong><em>H. pylori</em> Eradication in Peptic Ulcer Disease:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Zilon 20 mg twice daily + Amoxicillin 500 mg + Metronidazole 400 mg (both three times daily for 1 week)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">OR Zilon 20 mg twice daily + Clarithromycin 250 mg + Metronidazole 400 mg (both twice daily for 1 week)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">OR Zilon 20 mg twice daily + Amoxicillin 1 g + Clarithromycin 500 mg (both twice daily for 1 week)</li></ul><h4><strong>Pediatric Use (Severe Ulcerating Reflux Esophagitis)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Children &gt;1 year:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>10-20 kg:</strong> 10-20 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>&gt;20 kg:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks</li></ul><h4><strong>Intravenous (IV) Administration</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Acid Aspiration Prophylaxis:</strong> 40 mg IV slowly over 5 minutes, 1 hour before surgery.</li><li><strong>Ulcers or Reflux Esophagitis (if oral therapy is inappropriate):</strong> 40 mg IV once daily.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> Initial IV dose: 60 mg daily (higher doses may be needed; doses &gt;60 mg should be divided into two doses).</li></ul><h3><strong>Administration Guidelines</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>IV Injection:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Reconstitute with 10 ml of water for injection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Administer slowly over 2-5 minutes (max flow rate: 4 ml/min).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Use solution within 4 hours of reconstitution.</li><li><strong>IV Infusion:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dissolve in 100 ml of either 5% dextrose or saline.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If dissolved in saline, use within 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If dissolved in 5% dextrose, use within 6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not mix with other medications in the same infusion set.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Increased elimination time for:</strong> Warfarin, Phenytoin, Diazepam (may require dose adjustments)</li><li><strong>No interaction with:</strong> Theophylline, Propranolol, Antacids</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p>Generally well-tolerated; mild and transient side effects include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Paresthesia</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Headache</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C (US FDA):</strong> No observed harmful effects in epidemiological studies.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong> Unknown if excreted in breast milk; discontinue breastfeeding if necessary.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Rule out gastric cancer before initiating therapy, as Zilon 20 mg may mask symptoms and delay diagnosis.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Keep dry and away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Class</strong></h3><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</p><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Zilon 20 mg works by inhibiting gastric acid secretion through blocking the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system in gastric parietal cells. The effect starts within an hour, peaks at two hours, and lasts up to 72 hours. After discontinuation, acid secretion gradually returns over 3-5 days.</p>', 'active', '1264_1742540943.webp', 10, 12, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 11:53:57', '2025-03-21 12:09:03');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3867, 'Zilon', 'Omeprazole', 7.52, 6, 'capsule', '6 stip', 'Capsule', '40', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zilon 40 mg</strong></p><p>Zilon 40 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcers</li><li>Gastric and duodenal ulcers brought on by NSAIDs</li><li>Preventative treatment in individuals with a history of NSAID-induced ulcers</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</li><li>Long-term management of acid reflux disease</li><li>Dyspepsia caused by acid</li><li>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers</li><li>Prevention of acid aspiration during general anesthesia</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Peptic ulcer caused by Helicobacter pylori</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Blocker</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zilon 40 mg is a benzimidazole-derived inhibitor of stomach acid secretion. It works by blocking the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in gastric parietal cells, reducing gastric acid release. The antisecretory effect starts within an hour, peaks in two hours, and lasts up to 72 hours. Secretory activity resumes 3 to 5 days after discontinuation.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Zilon 40 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Oral:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Benign gastric and duodenal ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks (duodenal ulcer) or 8 weeks (gastric ulcer); in severe or recurrent cases, 40 mg daily; maintenance dose: 20 mg daily</li><li><strong>NSAID-associated ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks; if not fully healed, continue for another 4 weeks; for prophylaxis, 20 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks; 40 mg once daily for 8 weeks in refractory GERD; maintenance: 20 mg daily</li><li><strong>Long-term acid reflux management:</strong> 10-20 mg daily</li><li><strong>Acid-related dyspepsia:</strong> 10-20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration:</strong> 40 mg the evening before and 40 mg 2-6 hours before surgery</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</strong> Initially 60 mg daily; range: 20-120 mg daily (if &gt;80 mg, divide into two doses)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication (with antibiotics):</strong>20 mg twice daily + Amoxicillin 500 mg + Metronidazole 400 mg (three times daily for one week)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg twice daily + Clarithromycin 250 mg + Metronidazole 400 mg (twice daily for one week)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg twice daily + Amoxicillin 1 g + Clarithromycin 500 mg (twice daily for one week)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric (severe reflux esophagitis, &gt;1 year):</strong>10-20 kg: 10-20 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 kg: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks</blockquote><p><strong>IV Injection:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration:</strong> 40 mg IV (slowly over 5 minutes) one hour before surgery</li><li><strong>Ulcers or reflux esophagitis (when oral use is inappropriate):</strong> 40 mg IV once daily</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</strong> Initial IV dose 60 mg daily; higher doses may be required, divided if &gt;60 mg/day</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zilon 40 mg</strong></p><p><strong>IV Injection:</strong></p><ul><li>Administer only via IV route</li><li>Reconstitute with 10 ml of sterile water and inject slowly over 2-5 minutes (max 4 ml/min)</li><li>Use freshly prepared solution within 4 hours</li><li>IV infusion: Dissolve in 100 ml of 5% dextrose or saline; use within 12 hours (saline) or 6 hours (dextrose)</li><li>Do not mix with other medications in the same infusion set</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zilon 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>May prolong the elimination of warfarin, phenytoin, and diazepam, requiring dose adjustment</li><li>No interactions with theophylline, propranolol, or antacids</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any components of Zilon 40 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Zilon 40 mg</strong></p><p>Generally well-tolerated. Rare mild and temporary side effects include nausea, stomach cramps, paresthesia, dizziness, and headache.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category C (US FDA). Epidemiological studies suggest no harmful effects on pregnancy or fetal health</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Unknown if it enters breast milk; discontinue breastfeeding if necessary</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Rule out gastric cancer before treating suspected ulcers, as Zilon 40 mg may mask symptoms and delay diagnosis</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from heat and light</li><li>Keep out of reach of children</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Blocker</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Zilon 40 mg inhibits gastric acid production by blocking the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system in gastric parietal cells. Effects start within an hour, peak in two hours, and last up to 72 hours. Secretory activity resumes over 3-5 days after stopping the medication.</p>', 'active', '1730_1742540912.webp', 10, 12, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 11:58:39', '2025-03-21 12:08:32'),
(3868, 'Zeefol TR', 'Ferrous Sulfate + Folic Acid + Zinc Sulfate', 3.00, 15, 'capsule', '15 strip', 'Capsule', '150 mg+0.5 mg+61.8 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a cleaned-up version with proper formatting and clarity:</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Indications of Zeefol TR 150 mg+0.5 mg+61.8 mg</strong></h3><p>Zeefol TR is indicated for the treatment and prevention of iron, folic acid, and zinc deficiency, particularly during pregnancy and lactation.</p><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined Preparation</p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Iron:</strong> Essential for hemoglobin formation and effective erythropoiesis; acts as a cofactor for several essential enzymes, including cytochromes involved in electron transport. It helps transport oxygen via hemoglobin.</li><li><strong>Zinc Sulphate:</strong> Plays a crucial role in various physiological processes, including enzyme functions. Zinc deficiency may lead to poor night vision, slow wound healing, reduced immune function, diarrhea, dermatitis, and growth retardation in children.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid:</strong> Required for nucleoprotein synthesis and erythropoiesis. It converts to tetrahydrofolic acid, its active form, in the liver and plasma. It helps prevent neural tube defects in women of childbearing age, with higher doses required during pregnancy.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Elderly:</strong> 1 capsule/tablet daily; in severe cases, 2 capsules/tablets daily may be required.</li><li><strong>Children (Above 1 year):</strong> 1 capsule/tablet daily. Capsules may be opened and mixed with soft, cool food but must not be chewed.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li>Iron and zinc chelate with tetracycline, potentially impairing the absorption of all three.</li><li>Zinc absorption may be reduced in the presence of iron.</li><li>Iron absorption may be impaired by penicillamine and antacids. To minimize interactions, these agents should be taken several hours apart.</li><li>Patients with renal failure may be at risk of zinc accumulation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Patients with <strong>haemolytic anaemia</strong></li><li>Hypersensitivity to any component</li><li>Conditions with increased body iron content</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Dark stools (common during iron therapy)</li><li>Gastrointestinal irritation (nausea, anorexia, vomiting, discomfort, constipation, diarrhea)</li><li>Zinc-related gastrointestinal upset</li><li>Rare cases of allergic reactions</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Should be avoided in the first trimester unless iron deficiency is confirmed. Iron supplementation is recommended for the rest of pregnancy, particularly when zinc is needed.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Patients at risk of iron overload (e.g., haemochromatosis, haemolytic anaemia, red cell aplasia) should take precautions.</li><li>Lack of response to treatment may indicate other underlying causes of anaemia.</li><li>Patients with renal failure may have an increased risk of zinc accumulation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Iron overdose:</strong> Can be dangerous, especially in children. Immediate medical attention is required. Treatment includes gastric lavage, inducing vomiting (if safe), and administering oral or IV desferrioxamine if necessary.</li><li><strong>Zinc overdose:</strong> Can cause gastrointestinal irritation, hemorrhage, mucosal corrosion, and stricture formation. Avoid gastric lavage or emesis. Administer milk or demulcents and consider chelating agents such as dimercaprol, penicillamine, or edetic acid.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><p>Store in a dry place below <strong>25°C</strong>, protected from light. Keep out of reach of children to prevent accidental iron poisoning.</p><p><br></p>', 'active', '1621_1742540550.webp', 26, 12, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 12:02:30', '2025-03-21 12:02:30'),
(3869, 'Zico Plus', 'Vitamin B Complex + Zinc', 57.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '100ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zico Plus</strong></p><p>Zico Plus is indicated for the treatment and prevention of vitamin B and zinc deficiencies.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each Zico Plus 5 ml Syrup contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride BP 5 mg</li><li>Riboflavin-5-Phosphate Sodium BP 2.74 mg (equivalent to Riboflavin 2 mg)</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride BP 2 mg</li><li>Nicotinamide BP 20 mg</li><li>Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate USP 27.45 mg (equivalent to elemental Zinc 10 mg)</li></ul><p>Each Zico Plus Tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride BP 5 mg</li><li>Riboflavin-5-Phosphate Sodium BP 2.74 mg (equivalent to Riboflavin 2 mg)</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride BP 2 mg</li><li>Nicotinamide BP 20 mg</li><li>Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate USP 27.45 mg (equivalent to elemental Zinc 10 mg)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zico Plus is a preparation of Vitamin B complex and Zinc. Vitamin B complex regulates various stages of carbohydrate, fat, and protein metabolism. Zinc is an essential trace element required for human nutrition and is involved in the function of numerous enzyme systems.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><em>For Syrup:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 10 ml (2 teaspoonfuls) 2 to 3 times daily or as recommended by the physician.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> 10 ml (2 teaspoonfuls) 1 to 3 times daily or as recommended by the physician.</li><li><strong>Infants:</strong> 5 ml (1 teaspoonful) 1 to 2 times daily or as recommended by the physician.</li></ul><p><em>For Tablets:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 30 kg:</strong> 1 to 2 tablets 2 to 3 times daily or as recommended by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant intake of tetracyclines and zinc may decrease the gastrointestinal (GI) absorption and serum levels of tetracyclines.</li><li>Similarly, co-administration of zinc and fluoroquinolones may decrease the GI absorption and serum levels of some fluoroquinolones.</li><li>Coadministration of Niacin and HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors (e.g., lovastatin) may result in myopathy and rhabdomyolysis.</li><li>Pyridoxine reduces levodopa\'s effectiveness by increasing its peripheral metabolism.</li><li>Co-administration of pyridoxine with phenytoin may decrease serum levels of phenytoin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Zico Plus is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to any of its ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Zico Plus is generally well tolerated. However, some side effects like nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and stomach upset may occur. Side effects have been reported with specific vitamins, but generally at levels substantially higher than recommended doses.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Zico Plus is recommended for use during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In acute renal failure, zinc accumulation may occur, requiring dosage adjustment.</li><li>Zico Plus is not intended for the treatment of severe specific deficiencies.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>In case of overdose, symptoms such as epigastric pain, diarrhea, and vomiting may occur. Emergency medical attention should be sought. Initially, an emetic should be administered, followed by gastric lavage and general supportive measures.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light. Keep all medicines out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Zinc:</strong> Essential for biological functions such as immunity enhancement, wound healing, digestion, reproduction, physical growth, and mental development. Zinc also supports normal growth during pregnancy, childhood, and adolescence. It has antioxidant properties and is used to treat ADHD in children. In adults, zinc deficiency may lead to loss of appetite, poor sense of taste and smell, depression, white marks on fingernails, frequent infections, low fertility, prostate problems, mental issues, poor wound healing, diarrhea, mental lethargy, rough skin, and weight loss.</li><li><strong>B-Vitamins:</strong> Essential for energy release from food and maintaining healthy brain and nerve function. They play a crucial role in the formation of healthy red blood cells in children and adults. B-vitamin deficiencies in adults can cause fatigue, neurological issues (weakness, poor balance, confusion, irritability, memory loss, nervousness, tingling limbs, loss of coordination), sleep disturbances, nausea, poor appetite, frequent infections, and skin lesions.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy Recommendation</strong></p><p>Zico Plus is recommended for use during pregnancy and lactation.</p>', 'active', '1991_1742540847.jpg', 37, 12, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 12:06:12', '2025-05-07 20:34:31'),
(3870, 'Urobak', 'Nitrofurantoin', 60.00, 1, 'Bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '25 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Urobak 100 ml</strong></p><p>Urobak 100 ml is specifically indicated for the treatment &amp; prophylaxis of urinary tract infections caused by susceptible strains of <em>Escherichia coli, Enterococci, Staphylococcus aureus, Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em> and certain susceptible strains of <em>Klebsiella</em> and <em>Enterobacter</em> species.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Systemic Urinary Anti-infective</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Urobak 100 ml interferes with cell metabolism and cell wall synthesis by inhibiting several enzyme systems, including acetyl coenzyme A. It is bactericidal to most gram-positive and gram-negative urinary tract pathogens.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Urobak 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Urobak 100 ml tablet (In adults)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated urinary tract infections: 50-100 mg four times a day (the lower dosage level is recommended). Therapy should be continued for one week or for at least 3 days after sterility of the urine is obtained.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Long-term suppressive therapy: 50-100 mg at bedtime may be adequate.</li><li><strong>Urobak 100 ml capsule (In adults)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute Uncomplicated Urinary Tract Infections (UTIs): 50 mg four times daily for 7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Long-term suppression: 50-100 mg once a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prophylaxis: 50 mg four times daily for the duration of the procedure and for three days thereafter.</li><li><strong>Urobak 100 ml SR capsule</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults and Children over 12 years: One 100 mg capsule every 12 hours for seven days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Genito-urinary surgical prophylaxis: One capsule twice daily on the day of the procedure and for the next 3 days.</li><li><strong>Urobak 100 ml suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children</strong>: 5-7 mg/kg/day in four divided doses (contraindicated under one month of age).</li><li>The average dose of Urobak suspension for pediatric patients:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">7 to 11 kg: ½ (2.5 ml) teaspoonful 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">12 to 21 kg: 1 (5 ml) teaspoonful 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">22 to 30 kg: 1½ (7.5 ml) teaspoonfuls 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">31 to 41 kg: 2 (10 ml) teaspoonfuls 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Therapy should be continued for one week or for at least 3 days after sterility of the urine is obtained.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For long-term suppressive therapy in children, doses as low as 1 mg/kg per 24 hours, given in a single dose or in two divided doses, may be adequate.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Urobak 100 ml</strong></p><p>Urobak 100 ml should be taken with food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Urobak 100 ml</strong></p><p>Antacids containing magnesium trisilicate, when administered concomitantly with Urobak 100 ml, reduce both the rate and extent of absorption. Uricosuric drugs, such as Probenecid and Sulfinpyrazone, can inhibit renal tubular secretion of Urobak 100 ml.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Anuria, oliguria, or significant impairment of renal function are contraindications. This drug is contraindicated in pregnant patients at 38-42 weeks, during labor and delivery. Urobak 100 ml is also contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Urobak 100 ml.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Urobak 100 ml</strong></p><p>The most frequent clinical adverse events are nausea, headache, and flatulence. Other less common adverse events include diarrhea, dyspepsia, abdominal pain, constipation, emesis, dizziness, and drowsiness.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Urobak 100 ml has been detected in human breast milk in trace amounts. Because of the potential for serious adverse reactions in nursing infants under one month of age, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or to discontinue the drug, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>If acute, sub-acute, or chronic pulmonary reactions occur, Urobak 100 ml should be discontinued. Antacid preparations containing magnesium trisilicate should not be taken while using Urobak 100 ml.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Urobak 100 ml</strong></p><p>Occasional incidents of acute overdosage have not resulted in any specific symptoms other than vomiting. Induction of emesis is recommended.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Do not store above 30°C. Keep away from light and out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Systemic Urinary Anti-infective</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Urobak 100 ml is an antibacterial agent specific for urinary tract infections. It is highly soluble in urine, to which it may impart a brown color. Urobak 100 ml inactivates or alters bacterial ribosomal proteins and other macromolecules.</p><p><strong>Susceptible Bacteria</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gram-Positive Aerobes</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Coagulase-negative staphylococci (<em>including Staphylococcus epidermidis</em>)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Enterococcus faecalis</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Streptococcus agalactiae</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Group D streptococci</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Viridans group streptococci</li><li><strong>Gram-Negative Aerobes</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Escherichia coli</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Citrobacter amalonaticus</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Citrobacter diversus</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Citrobacter freundii</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Klebsiella oxytoca</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Klebsiella ozaenae</em></li></ul>', 'in-active', '1873_1742543069.jpg', 7, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 12:44:29', '2025-05-13 05:39:21'),
(3871, 'SK-cef', 'Cephradine', 95.00, 1, 'Bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '125 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of SK-cef 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> SK-cef 125 mg/5 ml is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. These include:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper respiratory tract infections</strong>: sinusitis, pharyngitis, tonsillitis, laryngo-tracheobronchitis, and otitis media.</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong>: bronchitis (acute and chronic), lobar pneumonia, and bronchopneumonia.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: cystitis, urethritis, and pyelonephritis.</li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: abscess, cellulitis, furunculosis, and impetigo.</li></ul><p>The following microorganisms are susceptible to SK-cef 125 mg/5 ml:</p><ul><li><strong>Gram-positive</strong>: Staphylococci (both penicillin-sensitive and resistant strains, including penicillinase-producing species), Streptococci, Streptococcus pyogenes (beta-haemolytic), Streptococcus pneumoniae.</li><li><strong>Gram-negative</strong>: Escherichia coli, Klebsiella spp., Proteus mirabilis, Haemophilus influenzae, Shigella spp., Salmonella spp. (including Salmonella typhi), Neisseria spp.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> First-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> SK-cef 125 mg/5 ml is a cephalosporin antibiotic with broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against both gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria. It interferes with the synthesis of bacterial cell walls by inhibiting transpeptidase enzyme, weakening the bacterial cell wall, leading to cell rupture.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of SK-cef 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p><strong>For oral administration:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:Urinary tract infections</strong>: 500 mg four times daily or 1 g twice daily. Higher doses may be needed for severe or chronic infections.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Respiratory tract infections</strong>: 250 to 500 mg four times daily or 500 mg to 1 g twice daily, depending on severity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: 250 to 500 mg four times daily or 500 mg to 1 g twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong>Total daily dose: 25 to 50 mg/kg in two or four divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Otitis media</strong>: 75 to 100 mg/kg in divided doses every 6 to 12 hours (max: 4 g/day).</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Normal adult dose applies, but renal or hepatic function should be monitored.</li></ul><p><strong>For injectable administration:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong>Usual dose: 2-4 g daily in four equally divided doses (up to 8 g daily in severe cases).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For prophylaxis:</strong> A single preoperative dose of 1-2 g intramuscularly or intravenously.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong>Dose: 50-100 mg/kg daily in four equally divided doses (up to 300 mg/kg in severe infections).</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of SK-cef 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Nephrotoxic drugs (e.g., aminoglycosides) may increase the risk of kidney damage.</li><li>Diuretics (e.g., furosemide, ethacrynic acid) and probenecid enhance the risk of renal toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> SK-cef 125 mg/5 ml should not be used in patients with known or suspected hypersensitivity to cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of SK-cef 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Gastrointestinal disturbances, hypersensitivity reactions (more likely in individuals with a history of allergies, asthma, or hay fever).</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Glossitis, heartburn, dizziness, chest tightness, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, vaginitis, candida overgrowth.</li><li><strong>Severe (unknown frequency)</strong>: Blood disorders, serum sickness-like reactions, anaphylaxis, sleep disturbances, hyperactivity, liver enzyme disturbances, reversible interstitial nephritis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: US FDA Pregnancy Category B. Should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Excreted in breast milk; use with caution in nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged use may lead to the development of superinfections due to resistant organisms.</li><li>Patients allergic to penicillins should use SK-cef 125 mg/5 ml with caution due to possible cross-sensitivity.</li><li>Cephalosporins may cause a false-positive Coombs’ test and urine glucose test.</li><li>Dosage adjustments are needed in renal impairment.</li><li>Contains lactose; not suitable for patients with galactose intolerance.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of SK-cef 125 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Symptoms include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and gastric upset. Treatment is supportive, and gastric lavage may be required for large doses.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Reconstituted suspension: Use within 7 days if kept at room temperature or 14 days if refrigerated.</li><li>Injection solutions: Use within 2 hours at room temperature or 12 hours if stored at 5°C.</li><li>Store out of reach of children and do not use beyond the expiry date.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> First-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> SK-cef 125 mg/5 ml is a semisynthetic broad-spectrum bactericidal antibiotic, active against both gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria, including penicillinase-producing Staphylococci. It inhibits bacterial cell wall cross-linking, leading to bacterial lysis due to osmotic pressure.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Animal studies have not shown teratogenicity, but safety in pregnancy is not established. SK-cef 125 mg/5 ml is excreted in breast milk and should be used cautiously in lactating mothers. It may cause dizziness; caution is advised when operating hazardous machinery.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Impairment Dosage Adjustments:CrCl &gt;20 ml/min:</strong> 500 mg every 6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>CrCl 5-20 ml/min:</strong> 250 mg every 6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>CrCl &lt;5 ml/min:</strong> 250 mg every 50-70 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Hemodialysis Patients:</strong>250 mg at the start of dialysis, then 250 mg 6 to 12 hours later, 250 mg 36 to 48 hours later, and 250 mg at the next dialysis session if &gt;30 hours have passed.</li></ul><p><strong>Additional Information</strong></p><ul><li>Higher doses (up to 1 g four times daily) may be required for severe infections.</li><li>Treatment should continue for 2-3 days after symptoms resolve, and for at least 10 days in streptococcal infections to prevent complications like rheumatic fever.</li><li>Chronic urinary tract infections require extended monitoring.</li><li>Pediatric dosages should not exceed adult recommendations, regardless of severity.</li></ul>', 'active', '1305_1742543356.jpg', 53, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 12:49:16', '2025-03-21 12:49:16'),
(3872, 'Rinase', 'Ipratropium Bromide', 130.39, 1, 'Bottle', '20 ml bottle', 'Nebuliser Solution', '250 mg per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rinase 250 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Nebuliser Solution:</strong> It is indicated for the treatment of reversible bronchospasm associated with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). It is also indicated, when used concomitantly with inhaled beta 2-agonists, for treatment of reversible airway obstruction as in acute and chronic asthma.</p><p> <strong>Inhaler:</strong> As a bronchodilator in the treatment of chronic reversible airway obstruction as in asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, including chronic bronchitis and emphysema. Treatment of acute reversible airway obstruction.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anticholinergic bronchodilators</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Rinase 250 mcg/ml is a quaternary ammonium compound with anticholinergic properties. It appears to inhibit vagal reflexes by antagonizing the action of acetylcholine (the transmitter agent released from the vagus nerve). Anticholinergics prevent the increase in intracellular concentration of cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cyclic GMP) caused by interaction of acetylcholine with the muscarinic receptor on bronchial smooth muscle.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rinase 250 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Children over 3 years:</strong> The usual dose is 0.4-2.0 ml Rinase 250 mcg/ml Solution (100-500 micrograms) up to three times daily.</p><p> <strong>Adults (including elderly and adolescents) over 12 years of age:</strong> The usual dose is 0.4-2.0 ml Rinase 250 mcg/ml Solution (100-500 micrograms) up to four times daily.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Rinase 250 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Nebuliser Solution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults (including the elderly) and children over 12 years of age:</strong> 250-500 micrograms (i.e., 1 ml or 2 ml) 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>For treatment of acute bronchospasm:</strong> 500 micrograms/2 ml. It is advisable not to exceed the recommended daily dose during either acute or maintenance treatment. Daily doses exceeding 2 mg in adults and children over 12 years of age should only be given under medical supervision.</li><li><strong>Children 6-12 years of age:</strong> 250 micrograms (i.e., 1 ml) up to a total daily dose of 1 mg (4 ml). The time interval between doses may be determined by the physician.</li><li><strong>Children 0-5 years of age (for treatment of acute asthma only):</strong> 125-250 micrograms (i.e., 0.5 ml-1 ml) up to a total daily dose of 1 mg (4 ml). Ipratropium bromide should be administered no more frequently than 6 hourly in children under 5 years of age.</li></ul><p>Ipratropium Nebuliser Solution may be combined with a short-acting beta 2-agonist in the same nebulizer chamber. The solution should be used as soon as possible after mixing, and any unused solution should be discarded.</p><p><strong>Inhaler</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> The usual dose is 1-2 puffs (20 gm/spray) three or four times daily. A single dose of up to 80 gm (4 puffs) may be required to obtain maximum benefit during early treatment. Patients may take additional inhalations as required; however, the total number of inhalations should not exceed 12 in 24 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:6-12 years:</strong> Usually 1-2 puffs two to three times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Below 6 years:</strong> The usual dose is 1 puff (20 gm) three times daily.</li></ul><p>In order to ensure that the inhaler is used correctly, administration should be supervised by an adult. No specific information on the use of the product in the elderly is available. Clinical trials have included patients over 65 years, and no adverse reactions specific to this age group have been reported.</p><p><strong>Administration of Rinase 250 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> Using an inhaler seems simple, but most patients do not know how to use it properly. If the inhaler is used incorrectly, less medicine reaches the lungs. Correct and regular use of the inhaler will prevent or lessen the severity of asthma attacks.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Rinase 250 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Inhaler:</strong> Ipratropium has been used concomitantly with other drugs, including sympathomimetic bronchodilators, methylxanthines, steroids, and cromolyn sodium, commonly used in the treatment of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, without adverse drug reactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> <strong>Nebuliser Solution:</strong> Known hypersensitivity to atropine or its derivatives, or to any other component of the product.</p><p> <strong>Inhaler:</strong> Known hypersensitivity to Ipratropium, Atropine, or its derivatives. Also contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to soya lecithin or related food products such as soybean, lecithin, and peanut.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Rinase 250 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Nebuliser Solution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Headache, dizziness, cough, inhalation-induced bronchospasm, dryness of mouth, vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon:</strong> Urticaria, tachycardia, skin rash, pruritus.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Anaphylactic reaction, eye pain, mydriasis, increased intraocular pressure, palpitations, atrial fibrillation, nausea.</li></ul><p><strong>Inhaler</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Potentially life-threatening effects:</strong> Idiosyncratic reactions to Ipratropium bromide are rare.</li><li><strong>Severe/Irreversible adverse effects:</strong> No such effects have been reported.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic adverse effects:</strong> Regular use of Ipratropium can lead to a dry mouth. Other common adverse reactions include cough, headache, dizziness, blurred vision, and nausea. Less frequent effects include tachycardia, nervousness, drowsiness, itching, mucosal ulceration, and allergic reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The safety of Rinase 250 mcg/ml during human pregnancy has not been established. The benefits of using Rinase 250 mcg/ml during a confirmed or suspected pregnancy must be weighed against possible hazards to the unborn child. It is not known whether Rinase 250 mcg/ml is excreted into breast milk.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> <strong>Nebuliser Solution:</strong></p><ul><li>Caution is advised in patients with narrow-angle glaucoma, prostatic hyperplasia, bladder-outflow obstruction, or cystic fibrosis.</li><li>Patients must be instructed in the correct administration of the Ipratropium Nebuliser Solution. Care must be taken not to allow the solution or mist to enter the eyes.</li></ul><p><strong>Inhaler:</strong></p><ul><li>Patients should be advised that temporary blurring of vision, precipitation, or worsening of narrow-angle glaucoma or eye pain may result if the aerosol is sprayed into the eyes.</li><li>If recommended dosage does not provide relief or symptoms worsen, patients should seek immediate medical attention.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rinase 250 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> Acute overdosage by inhalation is unlikely since Ipratropium bromide is not well absorbed systemically after aerosol administration. Inhaled dosage of 5 mg produces an increase in heart rate and palpitation.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Do not store above 25°C.</li><li>As the product contains no preservative, a fresh ampoule should be used for each dose, and the ampoule should be opened immediately before administration.</li><li>Any solution left in the ampoule should be discarded.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Anticholinergic bronchodilators</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Rinase 250 mcg/ml inhibits vagal reflexes by antagonizing acetylcholine. Anticholinergics prevent the increase in cyclic GMP levels, leading to bronchodilation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The benefits of using Ipratropium Nebuliser Solution during pregnancy must be weighed against the possible hazards to the fetus. It is not known whether Rinase 250 mcg/ml is excreted into breast milk. Caution should be exercised when administering it to nursing mothers.</p>', 'active', '1392_1742543612.jpg', 14, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 12:53:32', '2025-03-21 12:53:32'),
(3873, 'Mensil N', 'Norethisterone Acetate', 7.50, 28, 'Tablet', '28 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Mensil N 5 mg</strong></p><p> Norethisterone is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Metropathia hemorrhagica (dysfunctional uterine bleeding)</li><li>Premenstrual syndrome</li><li>Postponement of menstruation</li><li>Endometriosis &amp; Menorrhagia</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Mensil N 5 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Oral administration:</strong> Not intended for use in children.</p><ul><li><strong>Metropathia haemorrhagica (dysfunctional uterine bleeding):</strong> 1 tablet 3 times daily for 10 days. Bleeding is usually arrested within 1-3 days. A withdrawal bleeding resembling normal menstruation occurs within 2-4 days after discontinuing treatment.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis against recurrence of dysfunctional bleeding:</strong> If there are no signs of resumption of normal ovarian function (no rise in the second half of the cycle of the morning temperature), recurrence must be anticipated. Cyclical bleeding can be established with 1 tablet twice daily from the 19th to the 26th day of the cycle.</li><li><strong>Premenstrual syndrome (including premenstrual mastalgia):</strong> Premenstrual symptoms such as headache, migraine, breast discomfort, water retention, tachycardia, and psychological disturbances may be relieved by taking 2-3 tablets daily from the 19th to the 26th day of the cycle. Treatment should be repeated for several cycles. When treatment is stopped, the patient may remain symptom-free for a number of months.</li><li><strong>Postponement of menstruation:</strong> In cases of too frequent menstrual bleeding and in special circumstances (e.g., operations, travel, sports), the postponement of menstruation is possible. 1 tablet of Norethisterone three times daily, starting 3 days before the expected onset of menstruation. A normal period should occur 2-3 days after stopping the tablets.</li><li><strong>Endometriosis (pseudo-pregnancy therapy):</strong> Long-term treatment starts on the 5th day of the cycle with 2 tablets of Norethisterone daily for the first few weeks. In the event of spotting, the dosage is increased to 4, and, if necessary, 5 tablets daily. After bleeding has ceased, the initial dose is usually sufficient. Duration of treatment: 4-6 months continuously, or longer if necessary.</li><li><strong>Menorrhagia (hypermenorrhoea):</strong> 1 tablet 2-3 times a day from the 19th to the 26th day of the cycle (counting the first day of menstruation as day 1).</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Mensil N 5 mg</strong></p><p> Not known.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy</li><li>Severe disturbances of liver function</li><li>Dubin-Johnson syndrome</li><li>Rotor syndrome</li><li>Previous or existing liver tumors</li><li>History during pregnancy of idiopathic jaundice, severe pruritus, or herpes gestationis</li><li>Current thromboembolic processes</li><li>Hypersensitivity to the active substances or any of the excipients</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Mensil N 5 mg</strong></p><p> Side effects are more common during the first months of use and subside with continued treatment.</p><ul><li><strong>Eye disorders:</strong> Visual disturbances</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal disorders:</strong> Nausea</li><li><strong>General disorders:</strong> Headache, edema</li><li><strong>Nervous system disorders:</strong> Migraine</li><li><strong>Respiratory, thoracic, and mediastinal disorders:</strong> Dyspnoea</li><li><strong>Skin and subcutaneous tissue disorders:</strong> Hypersensitivity reactions (e.g., rash, urticaria)</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>There is statistical evidence that users of combined oral contraceptives experience a higher risk of venous thromboembolism, arterial thrombosis (including cerebral and myocardial infarction), and subarachnoid hemorrhage. These conditions can be fatal.</li><li>Although Norethisterone does not contain estrogen, an increased thromboembolic risk should be considered, especially in patients with a history of thromboembolic disease, severe diabetes with vascular changes, or sickle-cell anemia.</li><li>In rare cases, benign and malignant liver tumors have been observed with hormonal treatments, sometimes leading to life-threatening intra-abdominal hemorrhage. If severe upper abdominal pain, liver enlargement, or signs of intra-abdominal hemorrhage occur, a liver tumor should be considered.</li><li>Norethisterone can influence carbohydrate metabolism, and diabetic patients should be monitored regularly during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Reasons for stopping Norethisterone immediately:</strong></p><ul><li>First occurrence of migrainous headaches or unusually severe headaches</li><li>Sudden perceptual disorders (e.g., disturbances in vision or hearing)</li><li>First signs of thrombophlebitis or thromboembolic symptoms, chest pain, or tightness</li><li>Pending surgery (six weeks beforehand) or immobilization (e.g., after accidents)</li><li>Onset of jaundice, hepatitis, or generalized pruritus</li><li>Significant rise in blood pressure</li><li>Pregnancy</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Mensil N 5 mg</strong></p><p> No reports of overdosage. Treatment is generally unnecessary. No specific antidotes exist; treatment should be symptomatic.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C in a cool and dry place. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for menopausal symptoms: Hormone replacement therapy</li><li>Female sex hormones</li><li>Oral contraceptive preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Norethisterone is a progestogen with actions similar to progesterone but is a more potent inhibitor of ovulation and has weak estrogenic and androgenic properties. It is used for menstrual cycle disorders. It is absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract, with effects lasting at least 24 hours, and is excreted in urine.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The use of Norethisterone during pregnancy is contraindicated. It should not be used during lactation.</p>', 'active', '1032_1742543841.jpg', 1, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 12:57:21', '2025-03-21 12:57:21'),
(3874, 'Risperdex', 'Risperidone', 3.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Risperdex 2 mg</strong></p><p> Risperdex 2 mg tablet is indicated for the treatment of acute and chronic schizophrenic psychoses, and other psychotic conditions, in which positive symptoms (such as hallucinations, delusions, thought disturbances, hostility, suspiciousness), and/or negative symptoms (such as blunted affect, emotional and social withdrawal, poverty of speech) are prominent. Risperdex 2 mg also alleviates affective symptoms (such as depression, guilt feelings, anxiety) associated with schizophrenia. Risperdex 2 mg is also effective in maintaining clinical improvement during continuation therapy in patients who have shown an initial treatment response. Risperdex 2 mg is indicated for the treatment of mania in bipolar disorder.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Atypical neuroleptic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Risperdex 2 mg is a selective monoaminergic antagonist having a high affinity for serotoninergic 5-HT2 and dopaminergic D2 receptors. Risperdex 2 mg also binds to alpha1 adrenergic receptors and, with lower affinity, to H1 histamine and alpha2 adrenergic receptors. Risperdex 2 mg has no affinity for cholinergic receptors. As a potent D2 antagonist, it improves the positive symptoms of schizophrenia but causes less depression of motor activity. Balanced central serotonin and dopamine antagonism may reduce extrapyramidal side effect liability and extend the therapeutic activity to the negative and affective symptoms of schizophrenia.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Risperdex 2 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Psychoses</strong>: 2 mg in 1-2 divided doses on the 1st day, then 4 mg in 1-2 divided doses on the second day (slower titration is appropriate in some patients). Usual dose range: 4-6 mg daily. Doses above 10 mg daily should be used only if the benefit outweighs the risk (max. 16 mg daily). For elderly patients or those with hepatic or renal impairment, the initial dose is 1 mg daily in two divided doses, increased in steps of 1-2 mg twice daily. Not recommended for children under 15 years.</li><li><strong>Mania</strong>: Initially 2 mg once daily, increased if necessary in steps of 1 mg daily. Usual dose range: 1-6 mg daily. Elderly patients or those with hepatic or renal impairment should start with 1 mg daily in two divided doses, increased in steps of 1-2 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Schizophrenia</strong>: Initially 1 mg BID, with increases in increments of 1 mg BID on the second and third day, as tolerated, to a target dose of 3 mg BID by the third day. Further dosage adjustments, if needed, should generally occur at intervals of no less than 1 week. When adjusting dosage, small increments or decrements of 1-2 mg are recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Risperdex 2 mg</strong></p><ul><li>May antagonize the effects of levodopa and dopamine agonists.</li><li>Chronic administration with Carbamazepine reduces plasma clearance of Risperdex 2 mg.</li><li>Chronic administration with Clozapine may decrease the clearance of Risperdex 2 mg.</li><li>May enhance the effects of certain antihypertensives.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Risperdex 2 mg is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to the product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Risperdex 2 mg</strong></p><p> Insomnia, agitation, anxiety, headache, drowsiness, impaired concentration, fatigue, blurred vision, constipation, nausea, vomiting, dyspepsia, abdominal pain, hyperprolactinemia, urine incontinence, tachycardia, hypertension, edema, rash, rhinitis, cerebrovascular accident, neutropenia, and thrombocytopenia have been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Although animal studies did not show direct reproductive toxicity, some indirect, prolactin- and CNS-mediated effects were observed. No teratogenic effects were noted in any study. The safety of Risperdex 2 mg for use during human pregnancy has not been established.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Risperdex 2 mg and its active metabolite are excreted in human breast milk. Women receiving Risperdex 2 mg should not breastfeed.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Special precautions should be taken in patients with preexisting cardiovascular diseases. Discontinue use if signs and symptoms of tardive dyskinesia occur. Use caution in patients with renal and hepatic impairment, elderly patients, epilepsy, Parkinson\'s disease, and in pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Risperdex 2 mg</strong></p><p> Drowsiness, sedation, tachycardia, hypotension, and extrapyramidal symptoms may occur. In case of severe extrapyramidal symptoms, anticholinergic medication should be administered. Close medical supervision and monitoring should continue until the patient recovers. There is no specific antidote to Risperdex 2 mg.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</p>', 'active', '879_1742544250.jpg', 7, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 13:04:10', '2025-03-21 13:04:10');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3875, 'Varivax', 'Varicella Virus Vaccine', 10.00, 1, 'Injection', '0.5 ml vial sc injection', 'Injection', '0.5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Varivax 0.5 ml</strong></p><p> This is a vaccine indicated for active immunization for the prevention of varicella in individuals 12 months of age and older.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Vaccines, Anti-sera &amp; Immunoglobulin</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Live varicella vaccine is used to stimulate active immunity to varicella (chickenpox).</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Varivax 0.5 ml</strong></p><p> Varivax 0.5 ml is administered as an approximately 0.5-mL dose by subcutaneous injection into the outer aspect of the upper arm (deltoid region) or the anterolateral thigh. Do not administer this product intravascularly or intramuscularly.</p><ul><li><strong>Children (12 months to 12 years of age):</strong> If a second dose is administered, there should be a minimum interval of 3 months between doses.</li><li><strong>Adolescents (≥ 13 years of age) and Adults:</strong> Two doses of vaccine, to be administered with a minimum interval of 4 weeks between doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Varivax 0.5 ml</strong></p><p> Each 0.5 mL dose of Varivax 0.5 ml is administered subcutaneously. Inject the vaccine subcutaneously into the outer aspect of the deltoid region of the upper arm or into the higher anterolateral area of the thigh.</p><ul><li><strong>Children (12 months to 12 years of age):</strong> The first dose is administered at 12 to 15 months of age but may be given anytime through 12 years of age. The second dose is administered at 4 to 6 years of age. At least 3 months should elapse between a dose of varicella-containing vaccine. At least 1 month should elapse between a dose of measles-containing vaccine and a dose of Varivax 0.5 ml if the vaccines are not given concurrently.</li><li><strong>Adolescents (≥13 years of age) and Adults:</strong> Two doses of Varivax 0.5 ml are administered at a minimum interval of 4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Varivax 0.5 ml</strong></p><p> Reye syndrome has been reported in children and adolescents following the use of salicylates during wild-type varicella infection. Administration of immune globulins and other blood products concurrently with Varivax 0.5 ml vaccine may interfere with the expected immune response. Varivax 0.5 ml vaccination may result in a temporary depression of purified protein derivative (PPD) tuberculin skin sensitivity.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Severe Allergic Reaction:</strong> Do not administer Varivax 0.5 ml to individuals with a history of anaphylactic or severe allergic reaction to any component of the vaccine (including neomycin and gelatin) or to a previous dose of a varicella-containing vaccine.</li><li><strong>Immunosuppression:</strong> Do not administer Varivax 0.5 ml to individuals who are immunodeficient or immunosuppressed due to disease or medical therapy. Disseminated varicella disease and extensive vaccine-associated rash have been reported in such individuals who were inadvertently vaccinated.</li><li><strong>Moderate or Severe Febrile Illness:</strong> Do not administer Varivax 0.5 ml to individuals with an active febrile illness with fever &gt;38.5°C.</li><li><strong>Active Untreated Tuberculosis:</strong> Do not administer Varivax 0.5 ml to individuals with active, untreated tuberculosis (TB).</li><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Do not administer Varivax 0.5 ml to individuals who are pregnant or planning on becoming pregnant in the next 3 months. Wild-type varicella is known to cause fetal harm.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Varivax 0.5 ml</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Frequently reported (≥10%) adverse reactions in children (1 to 12 years):</strong>Fever ≥102.0°F (38.9°C) oral: 14.7%</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Injection-site complaints: 19.3%</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Frequently reported (≥10%) adverse reactions in adolescents and adults (≥13 years):</strong>Fever ≥100.0°F (37.8°C) oral: 10.2%</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Injection-site complaints: 24.4%</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Other reported adverse reactions:</strong>Varicella-like rash (injection site)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Varicella-like rash (generalized)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C:</strong> Either studies in animals have revealed adverse effects on the fetus (teratogenic, embryocidal, or other), or there are no controlled studies in women. Drugs should only be given if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Evaluate individuals for immune competence before administration if there is a family history of congenital or hereditary immunodeficiency.</li><li>Avoid close contact with high-risk individuals susceptible to varicella because of possible transmission of the vaccine virus.</li><li>Immune globulins (IG) and other blood products should not be given concomitantly with Varivax 0.5 ml.</li><li>Avoid use of salicylates for 6 weeks following administration in children and adolescents.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store between 2-8°C. Do not freeze. Protect from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> No clinical data are available on safety or efficacy in children less than 12 months of age.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Clinical studies did not include sufficient numbers of seronegative subjects aged 65 and over to determine whether they respond differently from younger subjects.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li>Use only the sterile diluent supplied with Varivax 0.5 ml. The sterile diluent does not contain preservatives or antiviral substances.</li><li>Use a sterile syringe free of preservatives, antiseptics, and detergents, as these substances may inactivate the vaccine virus.</li><li>Withdraw the total volume of the provided sterile diluent into a syringe. Inject all of the withdrawn diluent into the vial of lyophilized vaccine and gently agitate to mix thoroughly.</li><li>Withdraw the entire contents into the syringe and inject the total volume (approximately 0.5 mL) subcutaneously.</li><li>When reconstituted, Varivax 0.5 ml appears as a clear, colorless to pale yellow liquid.</li><li>Inspect visually for particulate matter and discoloration before administration. Do not use if particulates are present or if the solution appears discolored.</li><li><strong>To minimize loss of potency, administer Varivax 0.5 ml immediately after reconstitution.</strong></li><li><strong>Discard if not used within 30 minutes.</strong></li><li><strong>Do not freeze reconstituted vaccine.</strong></li><li><strong>Do not combine with any other vaccine through reconstitution or mixing.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Vaccines, Anti-sera &amp; Immunoglobulin</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Varivax 0.5 ml induces both cell-mediated and humoral immune responses to varicella-zoster virus. The relative contributions of humoral and cell-mediated immunity to protection from varicella are unknown.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Varivax 0.5 ml is contraindicated for use in pregnant women because it contains live, attenuated varicella virus. Wild-type varicella virus, if acquired during pregnancy, can cause congenital varicella syndrome. No increased risk for miscarriage, major birth defect, or congenital varicella syndrome was observed in a pregnancy exposure registry that monitored outcomes after inadvertent use.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><p> No clinical data are available on safety or efficacy in children less than 12 months of age.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><p> Clinical studies of Varivax 0.5 ml did not include sufficient numbers of seronegative subjects aged 65 and over to determine whether they respond differently from younger subjects.</p>', 'active', '181_1742544463.jpg', 1, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 13:07:43', '2025-03-21 13:07:43'),
(3876, 'Rituxim', 'Rituximab', 11000.00, 1, 'IV Infusion', '100 mg vial infusion', 'IV Infusion', '10 mg/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rituxim 10 mg/ml</strong></p><p><strong>Non-Hodgkin\'s Lymphoma (NHL):</strong> Rituxim 10 mg/ml is indicated for the treatment of patients with:</p><ul><li>Relapsed or refractory, low-grade or follicular, CD20-positive, B-cell NHL as a single agent.</li><li>Previously untreated follicular, CD20-positive, B-cell NHL in combination with first-line chemotherapy and, in patients achieving a complete or partial response to Rituxim in combination with chemotherapy, as single-agent maintenance therapy.</li><li>Non-progressing (including stable disease), low-grade, CD20-positive, B-cell NHL as a single agent after first-line CVP chemotherapy.</li><li>Previously untreated diffuse large B-cell, CD20-positive NHL in combination with CHOP or other anthracycline-based chemotherapy regimens.</li></ul><p><strong>Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL):</strong> Rituxim 10 mg/ml is indicated, in combination with fludarabine and cyclophosphamide (FC), for the treatment of patients with previously untreated and previously treated CD20-positive CLL.</p><p><strong>Rheumatoid Arthritis (RA):</strong> Rituxim 10 mg/ml in combination with methotrexate is indicated for the treatment of adult patients with moderately to severely active rheumatoid arthritis who have had an inadequate response to one or more TNF antagonist therapies.</p><p><strong>Granulomatosis With Polyangiitis (GPA) (Wegener\'s Granulomatosis) and Microscopic Polyangiitis (MPA):</strong> Rituxim 10 mg/ml, in combination with glucocorticoids, is indicated for the treatment of adult patients with GPA and MPA.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Cytotoxic immunosuppressants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rituxim 10 mg/ml is a chimeric monoclonal antibody to CD20 antigen, which regulates cell cycle initiation. It binds to the antigen on the cell surface, activating complement-dependent B-cell cytotoxicity and binding to human Fc receptors, mediating cell killing through antibody-dependent cellular toxicity.</p><p><strong>Duration:</strong> Detectable in serum 3-6 months after completion of therapy; B-cell recovery 6-12 months following completion of therapy.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rituxim 10 mg/ml</strong></p><p><strong>Intravenous (Adult)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Non-Hodgkin\'s Lymphoma, Refractory or Relapsed Follicular Lymphoma:</strong> As a single agent, 375 mg/m² infusion once weekly for 4 doses at an initial rate of 50 mg/hr. May increase by 50 mg/hr every 30 min. If tolerated, subsequent infusions can start at 100 mg/hr and increase by 100 mg/hr every 30 min (Max rate: 400 mg/hr).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">When used with CVP (cyclophosphamide, vincristine, and prednisolone) for follicular lymphoma or CHOP (cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, and prednisone) for diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, 375 mg/m² is given on day 1 of the chemotherapy cycle after the corticosteroid regimen, for up to 8 cycles.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For patients treated with 6-8 cycles of CVP chemotherapy and who have not progressed, 375 mg/m² may be given once weekly for 4 doses, repeated every 6 months, for up to 16 doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance:</strong> 375 mg/m² once every 3 months (for those who responded to induction chemotherapy), or once every 2 months (initiated 2 months after the last dose of induction chemotherapy for previously untreated patients) for a maximum period of 2 years.</li><li><strong>Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia:</strong> 375 mg/m² on the day prior to chemotherapy in cycle 1, followed by 500 mg/m² on day 1 (every 28 days) of cycles 2-6.</li><li><strong>Rheumatoid Arthritis:</strong> Given as two 1 g IV infusion doses, separated by 2 weeks, in combination with methotrexate. Corticosteroids may be given before each infusion to minimize the risk and severity of infusion reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Rituxim 10 mg/ml</strong></p><p>Increased risk of renal toxicity with cisplatin.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to murine proteins. Patients with active, severe infections. Severely immunocompromised state. Concomitant use with live viral vaccines. Lactation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Rituxim 10 mg/ml</strong></p><ul><li>Reactivation of hepatitis B virus</li><li>Fever and rigors</li><li>Pruritus, skin rashes, dyspnea, bronchospasm, angioedema, transient hypotension, and flushing</li><li>Asthenia, headache, rhinitis</li><li>Thrombocytopenia, neutropenia, anemia</li><li>Abdominal pain, bowel obstruction, and perforation</li><li>Exacerbation of heart failure and angina pectoris</li><li>Reversible interstitial pneumonia and interstitial fibrosis</li><li>Depletion of immunoglobulin concentrations</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p><strong>Pregnancy Category C:</strong> Either studies in animals have revealed adverse effects on the fetus (teratogenic, embryocidal, or other) and there are no controlled studies in women, or studies in women and animals are not available. Drugs should be given only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Extensive tumor burden, pulmonary insufficiency, or pulmonary tumor infiltration</li><li>History of cardiac disease</li><li>Pregnancy</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store between 2-8°C.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p>Dilute the appropriate dose with sodium chloride 0.9% or glucose 5% to a final concentration of between 1 and 4 mg/mL.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Cytotoxic immunosuppressants</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Rituxim 10 mg/ml is a chimeric monoclonal antibody to CD20 antigen, which regulates cell cycle initiation. It binds to the antigen on the cell surface, activating complement-dependent B-cell cytotoxicity and binding to human Fc receptors, mediating cell killing through antibody-dependent cellular toxicity.</p><p><strong>Duration:</strong> Detectable in serum 3-6 months after completion of therapy; B-cell recovery 6-12 months following completion of therapy.</p>', 'active', '382_1742544798.jpg', 55, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 13:13:18', '2025-03-21 13:33:36'),
(3877, 'Rito', 'Etoricoxib', 12.05, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '90 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rito 90 mg</strong></p><p> Rito 90 mg is indicated for the symptomatic relief of:</p><ul><li>Osteoarthritis (OA)</li><li>Rheumatoid arthritis (RA)</li><li>Ankylosing spondylitis</li><li>The pain and signs of inflammation associated with acute gouty arthritis</li><li>Short-term treatment of moderate pain associated with dental surgery</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Rito 90 mg is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID). It has anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic activities. Rito 90 mg is a selective cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) inhibitor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rito 90 mg</strong></p><p> For adults and adolescents over 16 years:</p><ul><li><strong>Osteoarthritis, chronic musculoskeletal disorders &amp; dysmenorrhea:</strong> 60 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis:</strong> 90 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Pain following dental surgery &amp; acute gout:</strong> 120 mg once daily</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Rito 90 mg</strong></p><p> For adults and adolescents over 16 years:</p><ul><li><strong>Osteoarthritis:</strong> 30 mg once daily (may increase to 60 mg once daily if needed)</li><li><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis:</strong> 90 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Ankylosing spondylitis:</strong> 90 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Acute gouty arthritis:</strong> 120 mg once daily (for up to 8 days)</li><li><strong>Postoperative dental surgery pain:</strong> 90 mg once daily (for up to 3 days)</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Rito 90 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>With medicine:</strong>May increase prothrombin time in patients on warfarin therapy</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">May reduce the effect of diuretics, ACE inhibitors, and Angiotensin II Antagonists</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Can be used with low-dose acetylsalicylic acid for cardiovascular prophylaxis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">May increase nephrotoxicity when co-administered with ciclosporin or tacrolimus</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">May increase lithium plasma levels due to decreased renal excretion</li><li><strong>With food &amp; others:</strong> Take without regard to meals</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to the active substance or excipients</li><li>Active peptic ulceration or gastrointestinal (GI) bleeding</li><li>History of allergic reactions to NSAIDs</li><li>Severe hepatic dysfunction (serum albumin &lt;25 g/l or Child-Pugh score 10)</li><li>Estimated renal creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min</li><li>Children and adolescents under 16 years of age</li><li>Inflammatory bowel disease</li><li>Congestive heart failure (NYHA II-IV)</li><li>Uncontrolled hypertension (&gt;140/90 mmHg)</li><li>Established ischemic heart disease, peripheral arterial disease, and/or cerebrovascular disease</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Rito 90 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Palpitations, fatigue, flu-like symptoms, bruising</li><li>Dry mouth, taste disturbance, mouth ulcers</li><li>Appetite and weight changes, atrial fibrillation</li><li>Chest pain, flushing, cough, dyspnea, epistaxis</li><li>Anxiety, impaired mental acuity, paresthesia</li><li>Electrolyte disturbances, myalgia, arthralgia</li><li>Rare cases of confusion and hallucinations</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Should be avoided in late pregnancy as it may cause premature closure of the ductus arteriosus</li><li>Excreted in breast milk; should be avoided during lactation</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use caution in patients at risk for gastrointestinal complications, including the elderly and those with a history of GI disease</li><li>Cardiovascular risk factors (hypertension, hyperlipidemia, diabetes, smoking) should be assessed before use</li><li>May reduce renal blood flow and impair renal function</li><li>Caution in patients with heart failure, left ventricular dysfunction, or edema</li><li>Monitor liver function tests; discontinue use if hepatic insufficiency occurs</li><li>Discontinue use if hypersensitivity reactions occur</li><li>May mask fever and signs of infection</li><li>Use caution with warfarin and other oral anticoagulants</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rito 90 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Doses up to 500 mg (single) and 150 mg/day (for 21 days) did not show significant toxicity</li><li>Supportive measures should be taken in case of overdose</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protect from light and moisture</li><li>Keep out of reach of children</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Selectively inhibits COX-2, reducing pain, inflammation, and fever</li><li>Does not inhibit COX-1, reducing gastrointestinal toxicity compared to non-selective NSAIDs</li><li>Inhibits COX-2 without affecting gastric prostaglandin synthesis</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Not recommended for women attempting to conceive</li><li>Unknown if excreted in human milk; excreted in rat milk</li><li>Women using Rito 90 mg should not breastfeed</li></ul>', 'active', '456_1742544917.jpg', 7, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 13:15:17', '2025-03-21 13:15:17'),
(3878, 'Cino Plus', 'Cinchocaine plus Hydrocortisone plus Neomycin plus Esculin', 8.13, 10, 'Pieces', '10 pieces', 'Suppository', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cino Plus 5mg + 10mg + 5mg + 10mg</strong></p><p> This is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Internal and external hemorrhoids</li><li>Post-partum hemorrhoidal condition</li><li>Anal pruritus, peri-anal eczema, anal fissures, and proctitis</li><li>Post-hemorrhoidectomy application to relieve pain and discomfort</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p> Each gram of ointment contains:</p><ul><li>Cinchocaine Hydrochloride BP 5 mg</li><li>Hydrocortisone USP 5 mg</li><li>Neomycin Sulphate BP equivalent to 10 mg Neomycin</li><li>Esculin Sesquihydrate equivalent to 10 mg Esculin</li></ul><p>Each suppository contains:</p><ul><li>Cinchocaine Hydrochloride BP 5 mg</li><li>Hydrocortisone USP 5 mg</li><li>Neomycin Sulphate BP equivalent to 10 mg Neomycin</li><li>Esculin Sesquihydrate equivalent to 10 mg Esculin</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Compound steroidal preparations, Drugs used in the ano-rectal region</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cinchocaine</strong> is a local anesthetic that relieves severe pain and relaxes sphincteric spasm.</li><li><strong>Hydrocortisone</strong> acts as an anti-inflammatory and anti-pruritic agent, eliminating itching and inflammation.</li><li><strong>Neomycin</strong> is a broad-spectrum antibiotic that eradicates most infections that may be present or arise in lesions of the anorectal area.</li><li><strong>Esculin</strong> improves skin vasculature and is effective in the management of cellulitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cino Plus 5mg + 10mg + 5mg + 10mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Ointment:</strong> A small quantity should be applied with a finger to the painful pruritic area in the morning, evening, and after each stool. For deep application, the nozzle should be attached to the tube, inserted fully, and squeezed gently while withdrawing.</li><li><strong>Suppository:</strong> One suppository in the morning, one in the evening, and one after each stool.</li><li><strong>Use in children:</strong> Not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Cino Plus 5mg + 10mg + 5mg + 10mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Ointment:</strong> Apply a small quantity with a finger to the affected area in the morning, evening, and after each stool. For deep application, use the nozzle attached to the tube, insert fully, and squeeze gently while withdrawing.</li><li><strong>Suppository:</strong> One suppository in the morning, one in the evening, and one after each stool.</li><li><strong>Use in children:</strong> Not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Contraindicated in patients hypersensitive to any component of the product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Cino Plus 5mg + 10mg + 5mg + 10mg</strong></p><p> Side effects of individual constituents may occur, and appropriate precautions should be taken when using this preparation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in pregnancy:</strong> The safe use of topical corticosteroids during pregnancy has not been fully established.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Long-term use should be avoided as <strong>hydrocortisone</strong> may be absorbed in sufficient amounts to produce systemic effects.</li><li>Adrenal suppression may occur even without occlusion.</li><li>Prolonged use may lead to striae (skin stretching marks).</li><li><strong>Neomycin</strong> may cause skin sensitization.</li><li>Absorption of the antibiotic from wounds or inflamed skin may occur, potentially causing irreversible hearing damage.</li><li>Should not be applied to extensively damaged skin.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a cool, dry place protected from light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Compound steroidal preparations, Drugs used in the ano-rectal region</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cinchocaine</strong> provides local anesthesia, relieving severe pain and relaxing sphincteric spasm.</li><li><strong>Hydrocortisone</strong> reduces inflammation and itching.</li><li><strong>Neomycin</strong> eliminates bacterial infections in the anorectal area.</li><li><strong>Esculin</strong> enhances skin vasculature and aids in managing cellulitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The safe use of topical corticosteroids during pregnancy has not been fully established.</p>', 'active', '1797_1742545413.jpg', 5, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 13:23:33', '2025-03-21 13:23:33'),
(3879, 'Zostiva', 'Valacyclovir', 40.00, 4, 'Tablet', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zostiva 500 mg</strong></p><p> Zostiva 500 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Herpes zoster (shingles)</li><li>Genital herpes in immunocompetent individuals (treatment or suppression)</li><li>Suppression of recurrent genital herpes in HIV-infected individuals</li><li>Cold sores (Herpes labialis)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Herpes simplex &amp; Varicella-zoster virus infections</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Zostiva 500 mg is the hydrochloride salt of L-valyl ester of the antiviral drug <strong>acyclovir</strong>. It slows the growth and spread of the herpes virus, allowing the body to fight off the infection. Zostiva 500 mg reduces symptoms and shortens the duration of illness. It is used for the treatment and suppression of <strong>genital herpes, shingles, and cold sores</strong>.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zostiva 500 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Adult Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cold Sores:</strong> 2 grams every 12 hours for 1 day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Genital Herpes:Initial episode:</strong> 1 gram twice daily for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Recurrent episodes:</strong> 500 mg twice daily for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppressive Therapy:Immunocompetent patients:</strong> 1 gram once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Alternate dose (for &lt; 9 recurrences/year):</strong> 500 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>HIV-infected patients:</strong> 500 mg twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Reduction of transmission:</strong> 500 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Herpes Zoster:</strong> 1 gram 3 times daily for 7 days</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cold Sores (&gt; 12 years of age):</strong> 2 grams every 12 hours for 1 day</li><li><strong>Chickenpox (2 to &lt; 18 years of age):</strong> 20 mg/kg 3 times daily for 5 days (not exceeding 1 gram 3 times daily)</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Zostiva 500 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Adult Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cold Sores:</strong> 2 grams every 12 hours for 1 day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Genital Herpes:Initial episode:</strong> 1 gram twice daily for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Recurrent episodes:</strong> 500 mg twice daily for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppressive Therapy:Immunocompetent patients:</strong> 1 gram once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Alternate dose (for &lt; 9 recurrences/year):</strong> 500 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>HIV-infected patients:</strong> 500 mg twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Reduction of transmission:</strong> 500 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Herpes Zoster:</strong> 1 gram 3 times daily for 7 days</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cold Sores (&gt; 12 years of age):</strong> 2 grams every 12 hours for 1 day</li><li><strong>Chickenpox (2 to &lt; 18 years of age):</strong> 20 mg/kg 3 times daily for 5 days (not exceeding 1 gram 3 times daily)</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zostiva 500 mg</strong></p><p> No dosage adjustment is recommended when coadministered with <strong>digoxin, antacids, thiazide diuretics, cimetidine, or probenecid</strong> in patients with normal renal function.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Zostiva 500 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity or intolerance to <strong>Zostiva 500 mg, acyclovir, or any component of the formulation</strong>.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zostiva 500 mg</strong></p><p> The most frequently reported adverse reactions include:</p><ul><li><strong>Nausea (15%)</strong></li><li><strong>Headache (14%)</strong></li><li><strong>Vomiting (6%)</strong></li><li><strong>Dizziness (3%)</strong></li><li><strong>Abdominal pain (3%)</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: Zostiva 500 mg was <strong>not teratogenic</strong> in rats or rabbits at 10 and 7 times human plasma levels, respectively, during major organogenesis. However, there are no <strong>adequate and well-controlled</strong> studies in pregnant women.</li><li><strong>Nursing Mothers</strong>: Unchanged Zostiva 500 mg was <strong>not detected</strong> in maternal serum, breast milk, or infant urine. It should be used with <strong>caution</strong> in nursing mothers and only when indicated.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dosage reduction</strong> is recommended in patients with <strong>renal impairment</strong>.</li><li>Caution should be exercised when administering to <strong>geriatric patients</strong> and those receiving <strong>nephrotoxic agents</strong>.</li><li>Safety and efficacy have not been established in <strong>immunocompromised patients</strong>, except for the suppression of <strong>genital herpes in HIV-infected patients</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool and dry place</strong>, away from children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and effectiveness in <strong>pre-pubertal pediatric patients</strong> have <strong>not been established</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Elderly Use (Over 65 years):</strong></p><ul><li>Elderly patients may require <strong>dose reduction</strong> due to <strong>low body weight</strong> or associated disorders (renal, CNS, etc.).</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Herpes simplex &amp; Varicella-zoster virus infections</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Zostiva 500 mg is the <strong>hydrochloride salt of L-valyl ester of acyclovir</strong>. It slows the <strong>growth and spread</strong> of the herpes virus, helping the body fight off infection. It <strong>reduces symptoms and shortens the illness duration</strong>, making it effective for treating and suppressing <strong>genital herpes, shingles, and cold sores</strong>.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: Not teratogenic in <strong>rats or rabbits</strong>, but lacks adequate studies in <strong>pregnant women</strong>.</li><li><strong>Nursing Mothers</strong>: Zostiva 500 mg was <strong>not detected</strong> in maternal serum, breast milk, or infant urine. Use <strong>with caution</strong> and only when necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and effectiveness in <strong>pre-pubertal pediatric patients</strong> have <strong>not been established</strong>.</li><li><strong>Elderly Patients (Over 65 years)</strong>: May require <strong>dose reduction</strong> due to <strong>low body weight or associated disorders</strong> (renal, CNS, etc.).</li></ul>', 'active', '1744_1742546570.jpg', 8, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 13:42:50', '2025-03-21 13:42:50'),
(3880, 'Rocipro', 'Ciprofloxacin', 18.00, 8, 'Tablet', '8 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '750 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rocipro 750 mg</strong></p><p> Rocipro 750 mg is indicated for the treatment of single infection or mixed infections caused by two or more susceptible organisms. It can also be used for infections caused by organisms resistant to other antibiotics including Aminoglycosides, Penicillins, and Cephalosporins. As antibacterial concentrations of Rocipro 750 mg are obtained in serum and body tissues as well as in the urine following administration by mouth, Rocipro 750 mg has been suggested for use in the treatment of a wide range of infections caused by susceptible organisms including infections of the urinary, respiratory, and gastrointestinal tracts, gonorrhoea, and septicaemia. The extensive tissue penetration of Rocipro 750 mg combined with its enhanced antibacterial activity (including antipseudomonal activity), enables Rocipro 750 mg to be used alone (pending sensitivity results) or in combination with an Aminoglycoside or with beta-lactam antibiotics for instance when severe neutropenia is present or with an antibiotic active against anaerobes where the presence of <em>bacteroides fragilis</em> is suspected. Rocipro 750 mg is indicated for the treatment of the following infections caused by sensitive bacteria:</p><ul><li><strong>Severe systemic infections</strong>: e.g. septicaemia, bacteraemia, peritonitis, infections in immunosuppressed patients with haematological or solid tumors and in patients in intensive care unit with specific problems such as infected burns.</li><li><strong>Respiratory tract infections</strong>: Lobar and bronchopneumonia, acute and chronic bronchitis, acute exacerbation of cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis, empyema.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: Uncomplicated and complicated urethritis, cystitis, pyelonephritis, prostatitis, epididymitis.</li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: e.g. infected ulcers, wound infections, abscesses, cellulitis, otitis externa, erysipelas, infected burns.</li><li><strong>Gastro-intestinal infections</strong>: e.g. enteric fever, infective diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Infection of the biliary tract</strong>: e.g. cholangitis, cholecystitis, empyema of the gall bladder.</li><li><strong>Intra-abdominal infections</strong>: e.g. peritonitis, intra-abdominal abscesses.</li><li><strong>Bone and joint infection</strong>: Osteomyelitis, septic arthritis.</li><li><strong>Pelvic infections</strong>: e.g. salpingitis, endometritis, pelvic inflammatory diseases.</li><li><strong>Eye, ear, nose, and throat infections</strong>: e.g. otitis media, sinusitis, mastoiditis, tonsillitis.</li><li><strong>Gonorrhoea</strong>: Including urethral, rectal, and pharyngeal gonorrhoea caused by beta-lactamase producing organisms or organisms moderately sensitive to penicillin.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> 4-Quinolone preparations, Anti-diarrhoeal Antimicrobial drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Rocipro 750 mg is a synthetic fluoroquinolone. It has bactericidal activity against a wide range of gram-positive and gram-negative organisms. It inhibits bacterial DNA synthesis by binding with the bacterial enzyme-DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV, which are responsible for DNA supercoiling.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Rocipro 750 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>General dosage recommendations</strong>: The dosage of Rocipro 750 mg is determined by the severity and type of infection, the sensitivity of the causative organism(s), and the age, weight, and renal function of the patient.</p><p> <strong>Adults</strong>: The dosage range for adults is 100-750 mg twice daily.</p><ul><li>In infections of the lower and upper urinary tract (depending on severity): 250-500 mg twice daily.</li><li>In respiratory tract infections: 250-500 mg twice daily for both upper and lower respiratory tract infections, depending on the severity.</li><li>For the treatment of known <em>Streptococcus pneumonia</em> infection, the recommended dosage is 750 mg twice daily.</li><li>In gonorrhoea: A single dose of 250 or 500 mg.</li><li>In the majority of other infections: 500-750 mg twice daily should be administered.</li><li><strong>Cystic fibrosis</strong>: In adults with pseudomonal infections of the lower respiratory tract, the normal dose is 750 mg twice daily. As the pharmacokinetics of Rocipro 750 mg remain unchanged in patients with cystic fibrosis, the low body weight of these patients would be considered when determining dosage.</li><li> <strong>Impaired renal function</strong>: Dosage adjustment is not usually required except in patients with severe renal impairment. (serum creatinine &gt;265 micromol/l or creatinine clearance &lt;20 ml/min). If adjustment is necessary, this may be achieved by reducing the total daily dose by half, although monitoring of drug serum levels provides the most reliable basis for dose adjustment.</li><li> <strong>Elderly</strong>: Although higher Rocipro 750 mg serum levels are found in the elderly, no adjustment of dosage is necessary.</li><li> <strong>Adolescents and children</strong>: As with other drugs in its class, Rocipro 750 mg has been shown to cause arthropathy in weight-bearing joints of immature animals. Although the relevance of this to humans is unknown, its use in children, growing children, and growing adolescents is not recommended. However, where the benefit of using Rocipro 750 mg is considered to outweigh the potential risk, the dosage should be 7.5-15 mg/kg/day depending upon the severity of infection, administered in two divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Duration of treatment</strong>: The duration of treatment depends upon the severity of infection, clinical response, and bacteriological findings.</p><ul><li>For acute infections: The usual treatment period is 5 to 10 days with Rocipro 750 mg tablets. Generally, treatment should be continued for three days after the signs and symptoms of the infection have disappeared.</li></ul><p><strong>Extended-release tablet</strong>: In uncomplicated urinary tract infection (acute cystitis), the recommended dose of extended-release tablet is 1000 mg tablet once daily for three days.</p><p><strong>For IV infusion</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mild to Moderate: 200 mg 12 hourly for 7-14 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe or Complicated: 400 mg 12 hourly for 7-14 days.</li><li><strong>Lower Respiratory Tract Infection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mild to Moderate: 400 mg 12 hourly for 7-14 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe or Complicated: 400 mg 8 hourly for 7-14 days.</li><li><strong>Nosocomial Pneumonia</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mild/Moderate/Severe: 400 mg 8 hourly for 10-14 days.</li><li><strong>Skin and Skin Structure</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mild to Moderate: 400 mg 12 hourly for 7-14 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe or Complicated: 400 mg 8 hourly for 7-14 days.</li><li><strong>Bone and Joint Infection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mild to Moderate: 400 mg 12 hourly for more than 4-6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe/Complicated: 400 mg 8 hourly for more than 4-6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Intra-abdominal (Acute abdomen)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Complicated: 400 mg 12 hourly for 7-14 days.</li><li><strong>Acute Sinusitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mild/Moderate: 400 mg 12 hourly for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Chronic Bacterial Prostatitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mild/Moderate: 400 mg 12 hourly for 28 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Rocipro 750 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Instruction for the use of Rocipro 750 mg IV infusion</strong>:</p><p> Check the bag for minute leaks by squeezing the inner bag firmly. If leaks are found, or if the seal is not intact, discard the solution. Do not use if the solution is cloudy or a precipitate is present. Do not use flexible bags in series connections. Close the flow control clamp of the administration set. Remove the cover from the port at the bottom of the bag. Insert the piercing pin of the administration set into the port with a twisting motion until the pin is firmly seated. Suspend the bag from the hanger. Squeeze and release the drip chamber to establish the proper fluid level in the chamber during the infusion of Rocipro 750 mg IV infusion. Open the flow control clamp to expel air from the set. Close the clamp. Regulate the rate of administration with the flow control clamp.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Rocipro 750 mg</strong></p><p> Concurrent administration of Rocipro 750 mg should be avoided with magnesium or aluminum-containing antacids or sucralfate or with other products containing calcium, iron, or zinc. These products may be taken two hours after or six hours before Rocipro 750 mg. Rocipro 750 mg should not be taken concurrently with milk or other dairy products, since absorption of Rocipro 750 mg may be significantly reduced. Dietary calcium as part of a meal, however, does not significantly affect the absorption of Rocipro 750 mg.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Rocipro 750 mg or to other quinolones.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Rocipro 750 mg</strong></p><p> Gastrointestinal disturbances e.g. nausea, diarrhoea, vomiting, dyspepsia, abdominal pain. Disturbances of the central nervous system e.g. headache, dizziness, tiredness, confusion, convulsion &amp; risk of retinal detachment. Hypersensitivity reactions e.g. skin rash (very rarely Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis), pruritus, and possible systemic reactions. Other less reported reactions include joint pain, mild photosensitivity, and transient increases in liver enzymes (particularly in patients with previous liver damage), serum bilirubin, urea, or creatinine levels. Risk of hypoglycemia &amp; mental health adverse effects.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Rocipro 750 mg should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus and mother. Rocipro 750 mg is excreted in human milk. Due to the potential risk of articular damage, Rocipro 750 mg should not be used during lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> It should be used with caution in patients with suspected or known CNS disorders such as arteriosclerosis or epilepsy or other factors which predispose to seizures and convulsions. Rocipro 750 mg may be taken with or without meals and fluids should be consumed liberally. Concurrent administration of Rocipro 750 mg should be avoided with magnesium/aluminium antacids, sucralfate, or with other products containing calcium, iron, and zinc. These products may be taken two hours after or six hours before Rocipro 750 mg. Rocipro 750 mg should not be taken concurrently with milk or yogurt alone, since absorption of Rocipro 750 mg may be significantly reduced. Dietary calcium as part of a meal, however, does not significantly affect the absorption of Rocipro 750 mg.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rocipro 750 mg</strong></p><p> Overdose following Rocipro 750 mg administration may lead to seizures, hallucinations, confusion, abdominal discomfort, renal and hepatic impairment, as well as crystalluria, haematuria, and reversible renal toxicity.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C temperature, protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> Although effective in clinical trials, Rocipro 750 mg is not a drug of first choice in the pediatric population.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> 4-Quinolone preparations, Anti-diarrhoeal Antimicrobial drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Rocipro 750 mg is a synthetic quinolone anti-infective agent. Ciprofloxacin has a broad spectrum of activity. It is active against most gram-negative aerobic bacteria including <em>Enterobacteriaceae</em> and <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>. Rocipro 750 mg is also active against gram-positive aerobic bacteria including penicillinase-producing, non-penicillinase-producing, and methicillin-resistant staphylococci, although many strains of streptococci are relatively resistant to the drug. The bactericidal action of Rocipro 750 mg results from interference with the enzyme DNA gyrase needed for the synthesis of bacterial DNA. Following oral administration, it is rapidly and well absorbed from the G.I. tract. It is widely distributed into the body tissues and fluids. The half-life is about 3.5 hours. About 30% to 50% of an oral dose of Rocipro 750 mg is excreted in the urine within 24 hours as unchanged drug and biologically active metabolites.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Reproduction studies performed in mice, rats, and rabbits using parenteral and oral administration did not reveal any evidence of teratogenicity, impairment of fertility, or impairment of peri/postnatal development. However, as with other quinolones, Rocipro 750 mg has been shown to cause arthropathy in immature animals, and therefore its use during pregnancy is not recommended. Studies in rats have indicated that Rocipro 750 mg is secreted in milk, and administration to nursing mothers is thus not recommended.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Although effective in clinical trials, Rocipro 750 mg is not a drug of first choice in the pediatric population.</p>', 'active', '1476_1742546732.jpg', 1, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 13:45:32', '2025-03-21 13:45:32');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3881, 'Traxef', 'Ceftriaxone Sodium', 320.00, 1, 'Injection', '1 gm vial injection', 'Injection', '1 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Traxef 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> Traxef 1 gm/vial is indicated for the treatment of the following major infections:</p><ul><li>Lower respiratory tract infections</li><li>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media</li><li>Skin and skin structure infections</li><li>Urinary tract infections</li><li>Gonorrhea</li><li>Bacterial Septicemia</li><li>Bone and joint infections</li><li>Meningitis</li><li>Prevention of postoperative infections</li><li>Perioperative prophylaxis of infections associated with surgery</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Traxef 1 gm/vial is a 3rd generation broad-spectrum parenteral cephalosporin antibiotic. It has potent bactericidal activity against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms. Like other cephalosporins and penicillins, Traxef 1 gm/vial kills bacteria by interfering with the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall. Traxef 1 gm/vial has a high degree of stability in the presence of beta lactamases. A remarkable feature of Traxef 1 gm/vial is its relatively long plasma elimination half-life of about 6 to 9 hours, which makes single or once-daily dosage of the drug appropriate for most patients. Traxef 1 gm/vial is not metabolized in the body. About 40-65% of a dose of Traxef 1 gm/vial is excreted unchanged in the urine; the remainder is excreted in the bile and ultimately found in the feces as unchanged drug and microbiologically inactive compound. The drug is highly protein bound (95%).</p><p><strong>Dosage of Traxef 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> <strong>Adult</strong>: The usual dose is 1 to 2 gm by intravenous or intramuscular administration once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day).</p><ul><li>Pneumonia, Bronchitis, Acute bacterial otitis media, Skin and skin structure infection, Urinary tract infections, Bacterial Septicemia, Bone and joint infections, Meningitis: 1 to 2 g IV or IM once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 4 gm/day</li><li>Uncomplicated gonococcal infections: 250 mg IM as a single dose</li><li>Surgical prophylaxis: 1 g IV as a single dose 30 to 120 minutes before surgery</li></ul><p><strong>Infants and Children (01 month or older)</strong>: The usual dose is 50 to 75 mg/kg intravenous or intramuscular administration once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day).</p><ul><li>Pneumonia, Bronchitis, Skin and skin structure infection, Urinary tract infections, Bacterial Septicemia, Bone and joint infections: 50 to 75 mg/kg IV or IM once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 2 gm/day</li><li>Acute bacterial otitis media: 50 mg/kg IM in single dose; Maximum dose: 1 gm/day</li><li>Meningitis: 100 mg/kg IV or IM in single daily dose or (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 4 gm/day</li></ul><p><strong>Duration of therapy</strong>: Continue for more than 2 days after signs and symptoms of infection have disappeared. Usual duration is 4 to 14 days; in complicated infections, longer therapy may be required.</p><p><strong>Administration of Traxef 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> <strong>Preparation of Solutions for Intramuscular / Intravenous Injections</strong>:</p><ul><li>For Intramuscular Injection: 250 mg or 500 mg Traxef 1 gm/vial should be dissolved in 2 ml Lidocaine HCI 1% injection or 1 g Traxef 1 gm/vial in 3.5 ml of Lidocaine HCI 1% injection.</li><li>For Intravenous Injection: 250 mg or 500 mg Traxef 1 gm/vial should be dissolved in 5 ml of Water for injection or 1 g Traxef 1 gm/vial in 10 ml of Water for injection USP or 2 g Traxef 1 gm/vial in 20 ml of Water for injection. The injection should be administered over 2-4 minutes, by Intramuscular or Intravenous injection or by tubing infusion over a period of 30 minutes at concentration between 10 mg/mL and 40 mg/mL. Before starting treatment through Traxef 1 gm/vial injection, patient tolerance test should be checked by administration of a test dose. (The use of freshly reconstituted solution is recommended. However, it maintains potency for at least 6 hours at room temperature or 24 hours at 5°C).</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Traxef 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> No drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Traxef 1 gm/vial should not be given to patients with a history of hypersensitivity to cephalosporin antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Traxef 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> Traxef 1 gm/vial is generally well tolerated. A few side effects such as gastro-intestinal effects including diarrhea, nausea and vomiting, stomatitis and glossitis; cutaneous reactions including rash, pruritus, urticaria, edema and erythema multiforme; hematologic reactions including eosinophilia, thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, anemia and neutropenia; hepatic reactions including elevations of SGOT or SGPT, bilirubinemia; CNS reactions including nervousness, confusion, sleep disturbances, headache, hyperactivity, convulsion, hypertonia and dizziness were reported. Local phlebitis occurs rarely following intravenous administration but can be minimized by slow injections over 2-4 minutes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Its safety in human pregnancy has not been established. Therefore, it should not be used in pregnancy unless absolutely indicated. Low concentrations of Traxef 1 gm/vial are excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Traxef 1 gm/vial is administered to a lactating mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> As with other cephalosporins, anaphylactic shock cannot be ruled out even if a thorough patient history is taken. Anaphylactic shock requires immediate countermeasures such as intravenous epinephrine followed by a glucocorticoid. In rare cases, shadows suggesting sludge have been detected by sonograms of the gallbladder. This condition was reversible on discontinuation or completion of Traxef 1 gm/vial therapy. Even if such findings are associated with pain, conservative, nonsurgical management is recommended. During prolonged treatment the blood picture should be checked at regular intervals.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Traxef 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> There is no specific antidote. Treatment of overdosage should be symptomatic.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Vial store in a cool, dry place (below 30° C), away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> Traxef 1 gm/vial must not be given to neonates if the neonates is premature and newborn (up to 28 days of age).</p>', 'active', '496_1742547001.jpg', 55, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 13:50:01', '2025-03-21 13:50:01'),
(3882, 'Sucotab', 'Gliclazide', 7.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '80 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sucotab 80 mg</strong></p><p> Sucotab 80 mg is a medicine that reduces blood sugar levels (oral antidiabetic medicine belonging to the sulphonylurea group). Sucotab 80 mg is used in a certain form of diabetes (type 2 diabetes Mellitus) in adults, when diet, exercise, and weight loss alone do not have an adequate effect on keeping blood sugar at the correct level.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Sulfonylureas</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sucotab 80 mg is a second-generation sulfonylurea drug that has hypoglycaemic and potentially useful haemobiological properties. It stimulates the release of insulin from pancreatic ß-cells by facilitating Ca2+ transport across the ß-cell membranes and decreases hepatic glucose output.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sucotab 80 mg</strong></p><p> Oral route: The usual initial dose is 40 to 80 mg daily. The dose can be increased up to 320 mg daily in divided doses when needed. The drug should be taken before meals. For children, Sucotab 80 mg is not used because it is contraindicated in juvenile-onset diabetes.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Sucotab 80 mg</strong></p><p> Film-coated tablet: The usual initial dose is 40 to 80 mg daily. The dose can be increased up to 320 mg daily in divided doses when needed. The drug should be taken before meals. For children, Sucotab 80 mg is not used because it is contraindicated in juvenile-onset diabetes.</p><p> Modified release preparation: Always take this medicine exactly as your doctor or pharmacist has told you. Check with your doctor or pharmacist if you are not sure. The dose is determined by the doctor, depending on your blood and possibly urine sugar levels. Changes in external factors (weight reduction, lifestyle, stress) or improvements in blood sugar control may require changed Sucotab 80 mg doses.</p><p> The recommended daily dose is one to four tablets (maximum 120 mg) in a single intake at breakfast time. This depends on the response to treatment. Sucotab 80 mg MR tablet is for oral use. Take your tablet(s) with a glass of water at breakfast time (and preferably at the same time each day). Swallow your whole tablet(s) in one piece. Do not chew or crush. You must always eat a meal after taking your tablet(s).</p><p>If a combination therapy of Sucotab 80 mg with metformin, an alpha-glucosidase inhibitor, a thiazolidinedione, a dipeptidyl peptidase-4 inhibitor, a GLP-1 receptor agonist, or insulin is initiated, your doctor will determine the proper dose of each medicine individually for you.</p><p> If you take more Sucotab 80 mg tablets than you should: If you take too many tablets, contact your doctor or the nearest hospital Accident &amp; Emergency department immediately. The signs of overdose are those of low blood sugar (hypoglycaemia). The symptoms can be helped by taking sugar (4 to 6 lumps) or sugary drinks straight away, followed by a substantial snack or meal. If the patient is unconscious, immediately inform a doctor and call the emergency services. The same should be done if somebody (for instance, a child) has taken the product unintentionally. Unconscious patients must not be given food or drink. It should be ensured that there is always a pre-informed person that can call a doctor in case of emergency.</p><p> If you forget to take Sucotab 80 mg tablet: It is important to take your medicine every day as regular treatment works better. However, if you forget to take a dose of Sucotab 80 mg MR tablet, take the next dose at the usual time. Do not take a double dose to make up for a forgotten dose.</p><p> If you stop taking Sucotab 80 mg MR tablet: As the treatment for diabetes is usually lifelong, you should discuss with your doctor before stopping this medicinal product. Stopping could cause high blood sugar (hyperglycaemia), which increases the risk of developing complications of diabetes. If you have any further questions on the use of this product, ask your doctor or pharmacist.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Sucotab 80 mg</strong></p><p> Other medicines and Sucotab 80 mg: Tell your doctor or pharmacist if you are taking or have recently taken any other medicines. The blood sugar-lowering effect of Sucotab 80 mg may be strengthened and signs of low blood sugar levels may occur when one of the following medicines is taken:</p><ul><li>Other medicines used to treat high blood sugar (oral antidiabetics, GLP-1 receptor agonists or insulin),</li><li>Antibiotics (sulphonamides, clarithromycin),</li><li>Medicines to treat high blood pressure or heart failure (beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors such as captopril, or enalapril),</li><li>Medicines to treat fungal infections (miconazole, fluconazole),</li><li>Medicines to treat ulcers in the stomach or duodenum (H2 receptor antagonists),</li><li>Medicines to treat depression (monoamine oxidase inhibitors),</li><li>Painkillers or antirheumatics (phenylbutazone, ibuprofen),</li><li>Medicines containing alcohol.</li></ul><p>The blood-glucose-lowering effect of Sucotab 80 mg may be weakened and raised blood sugar levels may occur when one of the following medicines is taken:</p><ul><li>Medicines to treat disorders of the central nervous system (chlorpromazine),</li><li>Medicines reducing inflammation (corticosteroids),</li><li>Medicines to treat asthma or used during labour (intravenous salbutamol, ritodrine, and terbutaline),</li><li>Medicines to treat breast disorders, heavy menstrual bleeding, and endometriosis (danazol),</li><li>St John\'s Wort- Hypericum perforatum preparations.</li><li> Blood glucose disturbance (low blood sugar and high blood sugar) can occur when a medicine belonging to a class of antibiotics called fluoroquinolones is taken at the same time as Sucotab 80 mg, especially in elderly patients.</li><li> Sucotab 80 mg may increase the effects of medicines that reduce blood clotting (warfarin). Consult your doctor before you start taking another medicinal product. If you go into hospital, tell the medical staff you are taking Sucotab 80 mg.</li><li> Sucotab 80 mg with food and drink: Sucotab 80 mg can be taken with food and non-alcoholic drinks. Drinking alcohol is not recommended as it can alter the control of your diabetes in an unpredictable manner.</li><li> Driving and using machines: Your ability to concentrate or react may be impaired if your blood sugar is too low (hypoglycaemia), or too high (hyperglycaemia) or if you develop visual problems as a result of such conditions. Bear in mind that you could endanger yourself or others (for instance, when driving a car or using machines). Please ask your doctor whether you can drive a car if you:</li><li>Have frequent episodes of low blood sugar (hypoglycaemia),</li><li>Have few or no warning signals of low blood sugar (hypoglycaemia).</li><li> Sucotab 80 mg contains lactose. If you have been told by your doctor that you have an intolerance to some sugars, contact your doctor before taking this medicine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Do not take Sucotab 80 mg:</p><ul><li>If you are allergic to Sucotab 80 mg or to other medicines of the same group (sulfonylurea), or to other related medicines (hypoglycaemic sulfonamides),</li><li>If you have insulin-dependent diabetes (type 1),</li><li>If you have ketone bodies and sugar in your urine (this may mean you have diabetic ketoacidosis), a diabetic pre-coma or coma,</li><li>If you have severe kidney or liver disease,</li><li>If you are taking medicines to treat fungal infections,</li><li>If you are breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Sucotab 80 mg</strong></p><p> Like all medicines, Sucotab 80 mg can cause side effects, although not everybody gets them. The most commonly observed side effect is low blood sugar (hypoglycaemia). If left untreated, these symptoms could progress to drowsiness, loss of consciousness, or possibly coma. If an episode of low blood sugar is severe or prolonged, even if temporarily controlled by eating sugar, you should seek immediate medical attention.</p><p> Liver disorders: There have been isolated reports of abnormal liver function, which can cause yellow skin and eyes. If you get this, see your doctor immediately. The symptoms generally disappear if the medicine is stopped. Your doctor will decide whether to stop your treatment.</p><p> Skin disorders: Skin reactions such as rash, redness, itching, hives, blisters, angioedema (rapid swelling of tissues such as eyelids, face, lips, mouth, tongue, or throat that may result in breathing difficulty) have been reported. Rash may progress to widespread blistering or peeling of the skin. If you develop this, stop taking, seek urgent advice from a doctor, and tell him that you are taking this medicine. Exceptionally, signs of severe hypersensitivity reactions have been reported: initially as flu-like symptoms and a rash on the face, then an extended rash with a high temperature.</p><p> Blood disorders: Decrease in the number of cells in the blood (e.g., platelets, red and white blood cells) which may cause paleness, prolonged bleeding, bruising, sore throat, and fever have been reported. These symptoms usually vanish when the treatment is discontinued.</p><p> Digestive disorders: Abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, indigestion, diarrhoea, and constipation. These effects are reduced when Sucotab 80 mg is taken with a meal as recommended.</p><p> Eye disorders: Your vision may be affected for a short time, especially at the start of treatment. This effect is due to changes in blood sugar levels. As for another sulfonylurea, the following adverse events have been observed: cases of severe changes in the number of blood cells and allergic inflammation of the wall of blood vessels, reduction in blood sodium (hyponatraemia), symptoms of liver impairment (for instance, jaundice), which in most cases disappeared after withdrawal of the sulfonylurea, but may lead to life-threatening liver failure in isolated cases.</p><p><strong>Reporting of side effects</strong></p><p> If you get any side effects, talk to your doctor or pharmacist. This includes any possible side effects not listed in this leaflet. By reporting side effects, you can help provide more information on the safety of this medicine.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Sucotab 80 mg is contraindicated in pregnant women. It should not be used in breastfeeding mothers.</p>', 'active', '1350_1742547202.jpg', 64, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 13:53:22', '2025-03-21 13:53:22'),
(3883, 'Tussibic', 'Butamirate Citrate', 80.00, 1, 'Syrup', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '7.5 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml is used to relieve dry (non-productive) cough. Dry cough may be caused by a recent viral infection. Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml is also used for pre &amp; post-operative cough sedation in patients who will undergo surgical procedures and bronchoscopy. It can be used in the acute cough of any etiology, whooping cough, and cough due to acute lower respiratory tract infections (tracheitis, laryngitis, bronchitis), etc.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Cough suppressant</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml belongs to the anti-cough medicines of central action. It exerts expectorant, moderate bronchodilating, and anti-inflammatory action. Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml also increases the spirometry indexes and blood oxygenation. It is safe and non-sedating, which is neither chemically nor pharmacologically related to opium alkaloids. The active metabolites of Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml have also antitussive action.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Tablet:</p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: 2-3 tablets daily at 8 to 12 hours intervals</li><li><strong>Adolescents over 12 years old</strong>: 1-2 tablets daily</li></ul><p>Syrup:</p><ul><li><strong>Children (3-6 years)</strong>: 5 ml 3 times daily</li><li><strong>Children (6-12 years)</strong>: 10 ml 3 times daily</li><li><strong>Adolescent</strong>: 15 ml 3 times daily</li><li><strong>Adult</strong>: 15 ml 4 times daily</li></ul><p>Pediatric Drops:</p><ul><li><strong>Children (1-3 years)</strong>: 0.75 ml 4 times daily</li><li><strong>Children (2 months-1 year)</strong>: 0.50 ml 4 times daily</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in adults</strong>: Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml 50 mg tablet: 2-3 tablets daily. Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml syrup: 15 ml 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Use in children &amp; adolescents</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml 50 mg tablet: Adolescent over 12 years old: 1-2 tablets daily.</li><li>Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml syrup:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children (3-6 years): 5 ml 3 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children (6-12 years): 10 ml 3 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adolescent: 15 ml 3 times daily</li><li>Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml pediatric drops:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children (2 months-1 year): 0.50 ml 4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (1-3 years): 0.75 ml 4 times daily</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Concomitant use with expectorants should be avoided.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to the active ingredient.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Tolerance of Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml is good. Adverse reactions such as rash, nausea, diarrhea, and vertigo have been observed in a few rare cases, resolving after dose reduction or treatment withdrawal.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml should not be used during the first trimester of pregnancy. During the remainder of pregnancy, it can be used if indicated by a physician but with caution. As a general rule, for safety reasons, in the absence of data on elimination of the active substance in breast milk, the benefits of Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml administration during breastfeeding should be carefully weighed against the risks.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml suppresses the cough reflex and therefore the concomitant use with expectorants should be avoided, since it may lead to mucus retention in the airways, which increases the risk of bronchospasm and respiratory infections. If the cough persists for more than 7 days (more than 3 days in children younger than 12 years of age), doctor must be consulted.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Accidental overdose with Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml can cause the following symptoms: drowsiness, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, loss of balance, and hypotension. Standard emergency procedures should be followed: activated charcoal, saline laxatives, and standard cardio-respiratory resuscitation.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep away from light and moisture, store below 30°C. Keep all the medicines out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Cough suppressant</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml acts directly on the brain\'s cough center to suppress cough. Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml is safe and non-sedating, which is neither chemically nor pharmacologically related to opium alkaloids. Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml is rapidly and completely absorbed after oral administration. Maximum concentration is reached within 9 hours with the sustained-release tablet. This is extremely protein-bound and plasma elimination half-life is about 13 hours. The active metabolites of Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml have also antitussive action.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml should not be used during the first trimester of pregnancy. During the remainder of pregnancy, it can be used if indicated by a physician but with caution. As a general rule, for safety reasons, in the absence of data on elimination of the active substance in breast milk, the benefits of Tussibic 7.5 mg/5 ml administration during breastfeeding should be carefully weighed against the risks.</p>', 'active', '1854_1742547366.jpg', 8, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 13:56:06', '2025-03-21 13:56:06'),
(3884, 'Vinblastin', 'Vinblastine Sulfate', 684.00, 1, 'Injection', '10 mg vial injection', 'Injection', '10 mg per 10 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vinblastine 10 mg/10 ml</strong></p><p> Vinblastine is effective as a single agent, but its therapeutic effect is enhanced when used in combination with other antineoplastic drugs. Vinblastine has been used in the treatment of Hodgkin’s disease (Stages III and IV) in combination therapy (with adriamycin (doxorubicin), bleomycin, and dacarbazine as ABVD) and in the treatment of advanced testicular carcinoma (with cisplatin and bleomycin). Vinblastine has been used in the palliative treatment of lymphocytic lymphoma, histiocytic lymphoma, advanced stages of mycosis fungoides, Kaposi\'s sarcoma, and Histiocytosis X. Vinblastine may be used in the treatment of choriocarcinoma resistant to other chemotherapeutic agents; carcinoma of the breast, unresponsive to appropriate endocrine surgery and hormonal therapy. One of the most effective single agents for treatment of Hodgkin’s disease is vinblastine. A protocol substituting cyclophosphamide for nitrogen mustard and vinblastine for vincristine in MOPP is an alternative therapy for previously untreated patients with advanced Hodgkin’s disease. Patients suffering relapse have also responded to combination therapy that included vinblastine. Advanced testicular germ-cell cancers are sensitive to vinblastine alone, but the administration of vinblastine concomitantly with other antineoplastic agents, produces better clinical results. Bleomycin effectiveness is enhanced when vinblastine is administered 6 to 8 hours prior to bleomycin administration; this schedule permits more cells to be arrested during metaphase, in which bleomycin is active.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Vinblastine is M phase specific. It binds to microtubular proteins and arrests mitosis at the metaphase by disrupting mitotic spindle formation. It blocks glutamic acid utilization, thus inhibiting purine synthesis, the citric acid cycle, and the formation of urea. It may also interfere with nucleic acid and protein synthesis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Vinblastine 10 mg/10 ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Adult (Intravenous)</strong>: Initially, 3.7 mg/m², increase dose weekly based on WBC counts in increments of about 1.8 mg/m² until leukocyte count decreases to about 3000/mm³, or max weekly dose of 18.5 mg/m² reached. Do not increase dose if leukocyte count is reduced to approximately 3000 cells/mm³; administer the max dose that does not cause leucopenia for maintenance. Usual dose: 5.5-7.4 mg/m² per week. Do not administer the next dose, even though 7 days have lapsed, unless the leukocyte count has returned to at least 4000/mm³.</p><p><strong>Child (Intravenous)</strong>: Initial 2.5 mg/m² of BSA, increased dose at weekly intervals in increments of about 1.25 mg/m² until leukocyte count decreases to about 3000/mm³, or max weekly dose of 12.5 mg/m² reached. Do not increase dose once leukocyte count reaches approximately 3000 cells/mm³, instead, a dose of 1 increment smaller to be administered at weekly intervals for maintenance, i.e., patient receives the max dose that does not cause leucopenia. Do not administer the next dose, even though 7 days have lapsed, unless the leukocyte count has returned to at least 4000/mm³. Duration of maintenance therapy depends on disease state and the antineoplastic agent combination.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Vinblastine 10 mg/10 ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Adult (Intravenous)</strong>: Initially, 3.7 mg/m², increase dose weekly based on WBC counts in increments of about 1.8 mg/m² until leukocyte count decreases to about 3000/mm³, or max weekly dose of 18.5 mg/m² reached. Do not increase dose if leukocyte count is reduced to approximately 3000 cells/mm³; administer the max dose that does not cause leucopenia for maintenance. Usual dose: 5.5-7.4 mg/m² per week. Do not administer the next dose, even though 7 days have lapsed, unless the leukocyte count has returned to at least 4000/mm³.</p><p><strong>Child (Intravenous)</strong>: Initial 2.5 mg/m² of BSA, increased dose at weekly intervals in increments of about 1.25 mg/m² until leukocyte count decreases to about 3000/mm³, or max weekly dose of 12.5 mg/m² reached. Do not increase dose once leukocyte count reaches approximately 3000 cells/mm³, instead, a dose of 1 increment smaller to be administered at weekly intervals for maintenance, i.e., patient receives the max dose that does not cause leucopenia. Do not administer the next dose, even though 7 days have lapsed, unless the leukocyte count has returned to at least 4000/mm³. Duration of maintenance therapy depends on disease state and the antineoplastic agent combination.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Vinblastine 10 mg/10 ml</strong></p><p> Possible increase in vinblastine levels with aprepitant. Reduced vinblastine metabolism with miconazole. Variable interactions with phenytoin, monitor serum phenytoin levels. Reduced immune response with vaccines. Additive myelotoxicity with zidovudine. Concurrent administration of vinblastine with CYP3A inhibitors may cause an earlier onset and/or an increased severity of side effects.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Severe bone marrow suppression; presence of bacterial infection; malignant cell infiltration of bone marrow; injection into extremity with poor circulation; porphyria; granulocytopenia. Elderly with cachexia or extreme skin ulcerations. Pregnancy; lactation. Intrathecal use may result in death.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Vinblastine 10 mg/10 ml</strong></p><p> Alopecia, constipation, malaise, stomatitis, dose-limiting bone marrow suppression (e.g. granulocytopenia, thrombocytopenia, anemia), hypertension, central and peripheral neurotoxicity, 8th cranial nerve damage resulting in vestibular and auditory toxicity, ischemic cardiac toxicity, breathlessness, bone, tumor or jaw pain. Nausea, vomiting, GI bleed, syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone. Necrosis, cellulitis if extravasation occurs.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Category D: There is positive evidence of human fetal risk, but the benefits from use in pregnant women may be acceptable despite the risk (e.g., if the drug is needed in a life-threatening situation or for a serious disease for which safer drugs cannot be used or are ineffective).</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Hepatic impairment; neurotoxicity; ischemic heart disease; preexisting pulmonary dysfunction; extravasation may cause tissue damage and pain. Discontinue immediately if extravasation occurs, with local injection of hyaluronidase and local heat application to decrease discomfort and risk of cellulitis; remaining injections to be injected into another vein. Routine prophylaxis against constipation recommended, especially in high doses. Nadir in leukocyte count occurs 4-10 days after vinblastine administration; recovery observed 7-14 days after treatment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Vinblastine 10 mg/10 ml</strong></p><p> Symptoms: Severe bone marrow suppression and extensions of its usual side effects.</p><p> Management: Treatment is supportive. Restrict fluid and use of loop diuretics to counteract the effects of syndrome of inappropriate secretion of antidiuretic hormone. Monitor the patient\'s cardiovascular system and daily blood counts for transfusion requirement.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at 2-8°C.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> <strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Serum bilirubin &gt;3 mg/100ml: Reduce dose by 50%.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Vinblastine is M phase specific. It binds to microtubular proteins and arrests mitosis at the metaphase by disrupting mitotic spindle formation. It blocks glutamic acid utilization, thus inhibiting purine synthesis, the citric acid cycle, and the formation of urea. It may also interfere with nucleic acid and protein synthesis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Category D: There is positive evidence of human fetal risk, but the benefits from use in pregnant women may be acceptable despite the risk (e.g., if the drug is needed in a life-threatening situation or for a serious disease for which safer drugs cannot be used or are ineffective).</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> <strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Serum bilirubin &gt;3 mg/100ml: Reduce dose by 50%.</p>', 'active', '596_1742547601.jpg', 60, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:00:01', '2025-03-21 14:00:01'),
(3885, 'Xylovin', 'Xylometazoline Hydrochloride', 20.00, 1, 'x 15 ml drop', '15 ml drop', 'Nasal Drop', '0.1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s your formatted text with the heading in normal bold text:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Xylovin 0.1%</strong></p><p>For the symptomatic relief of nasal congestion, perennial allergic rhinitis (including hay fever), sinusitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Nasal Anti-histamine preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Xylovin 0.1% is a sympathomimetic agent with marked alpha-adrenergic activity, and is intended for use in the nose. It constricts the nasal blood vessels, thereby decongesting the mucosa of the nose and neighboring regions of the pharynx. This enables patients suffering from colds to breathe more easily through the nose. The effect of Xylovin 0.1% begins within a few minutes and persists for several hours. Xylovin 0.1% is generally well tolerated and does not impair the function of ciliated epithelium. Systemic absorption may occur following nasal application of Xylovin 0.1%.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xylovin 0.1%</strong></p><p><strong>Adults:</strong> 2 or 3 drops of Xylovin 0.1% Adult formula (0.1%) two to three times daily. This adult formula should not be used for children under the age of 12 years.</p><p><strong>Children under 12 years:</strong> 1 or 2 drops of the Xylovin 0.1% children\'s formula (0.05%) in each nostril once or twice daily. Not to be used in infants less than 3 months.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Xylovin 0.1%</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Xylovin 0.1% nasal drops are contraindicated in patients with trans-sphenoidal hypophysectomy or surgery exposing the dura mater. It is also contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to Xylovin 0.1%.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xylovin 0.1%</strong></p><p>The following side effects have occasionally been encountered: a burning sensation in the nose and throat, local irritation, nausea, headache, and dryness of the nasal mucosa. Systemic cardiovascular effects have occurred, and this should be kept in mind when giving Xylovin 0.1% to people with cardiovascular disease.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>No fetal toxicity or fertility studies have been carried out in animals. In view of its potential systemic vasoconstrictor effect, it is advisable to take the precaution of not using Xylovin 0.1% during pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Each Xylovin 0.1% pack should be used by one person only to prevent any cross-infection. Patients are advised not to take decongestants for more than seven consecutive days.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Protect from heat. For reasons of hygiene, do not use the bottle more than 28 days after opening it.</p>', 'active', '731_1742547620.webp', 43, 12, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:00:20', '2025-05-07 20:19:57'),
(3886, 'Ultrapime', 'Cefepime Hydrochloride', 300.00, 1, 'Injection', '500 mg vial injection', 'Injection', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ultrapime 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Cefepime is indicated for the treatment of the following infections caused by susceptible strains of microorganisms:</p><ul><li><strong>Pneumonia (moderate to severe)</strong>: Caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, including cases associated with concurrent bacteremia, <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Enterobacter</em> species.</li><li><strong>Febrile Neutropenia</strong>: Cefepime as monotherapy is indicated for empiric treatment of febrile neutropenic patients. In patients at high risk for severe infection, antimicrobial monotherapy may not be appropriate.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated and Complicated Urinary Tract Infections (including pyelonephritis)</strong>: Caused by <em>Escherichia coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, when the infection is severe, or caused by <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, when the infection is mild to moderate, including cases associated with concurrent bacteremia with these microorganisms.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong>: Caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (methicillin-susceptible strains only) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections (used in combination with metronidazole)</strong>: Caused by <em>Escherichia coli</em>, viridians group streptococci, <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, <em>Enterobacter</em> species, or <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Fourth generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cephalosporins are bactericidal and have the same mode of action as other beta-lactam antibiotics (such as penicillins). Cephalosporins disrupt the synthesis of the peptidoglycan layer of bacterial cell walls. The peptidoglycan layer is important for cell wall structural integrity, especially in Gram-positive organisms. The final transpeptidation step in the synthesis of the peptidoglycan is facilitated by transpeptidases known as penicillin binding proteins (PBPs).</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ultrapime 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Cefepime should be administered intravenously over approximately 30 minutes.</p><ul><li><strong>Moderate to Severe Pneumonia</strong> due to <em>S. pneumoniae</em>, <em>P. aeruginosa</em>, <em>K. pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Enterobacter</em> species: 1-2 gm IV 12 hourly for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Empiric therapy for febrile neutropenic patients</strong>: 2 gm IV 8 hourly for 7 days or until resolution of neutropenia.</li><li><strong>Mild to Moderate Uncomplicated or Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</strong>, including pyelonephritis, due to <em>E. coli</em>, <em>K. pneumoniae</em>, or <em>P. mirabilis</em>: 0.5-1 gm IV/IM 12 hourly for 7-10 days.</li><li><strong>Severe Uncomplicated or Complicated Urinary Tract Infections</strong>, including pyelonephritis, due to <em>E. coli</em> or <em>K. pneumoniae</em>: 2 gm IV 12 hourly for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Moderate to Severe Uncomplicated Skin and Skin Structure Infections</strong> due to <em>S. aureus</em> or <em>S. pyogenes</em>: 2 gm IV 12 hourly for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Complicated Intra-abdominal Infections</strong> (used in combination with metronidazole) caused by <em>E. coli</em>, viridans group streptococci, <em>P. aeruginosa</em>, <em>K. pneumoniae</em>, <em>Enterobacter</em> species, or <em>B. fragilis</em>: 2 gm IV 12 hourly for 7-10 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ultrapime 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Renal function should be monitored carefully if high doses of aminoglycosides are to be administered with Cefepime because of the increased potential of nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity. Nephrotoxicity has been reported following concomitant administration of other cephalosporins with potent diuretics such as furosemide.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Cefepime is contraindicated in patients who have shown immediate hypersensitivity reactions to cefepime or the cephalosporin class of antibiotics, penicillin, or other beta-lactam antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ultrapime 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Cefepime is contraindicated in patients who have shown immediate hypersensitivity reactions to cefepime or the cephalosporin class of antibiotics, penicillin, or other beta-lactam antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category B. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies of cefepime use in pregnant women. Cefepime is excreted in human breast milk in very low concentrations (0.5 pg/ml). Caution should be exercised when cefepime is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Prescribing Cefepime in the absence of a proven or strongly suspected bacterial infection or a prophylactic indication is unlikely to provide benefit to the patient and increases the risk of the development of drug-resistant bacteria. Prolonged use of Cefepime may result in overgrowth of non-susceptible microorganisms. Repeated evaluation of the patient\'s condition is essential. Many cephalosporins, including cefepime, have been associated with a fall in prothrombin activity. Prothrombin time should be monitored in patients at risk.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ultrapime 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Patients who receive an overdose should be carefully observed and given supportive treatment. In the presence of renal insufficiency, hemodialysis (not peritoneal dialysis) is recommended to aid the removal of cefepime from the body. Symptoms of overdose include encephalopathy, myoclonus, seizures, and neuromuscular excitability.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Do not use later than the date of expiry. Keep all medicines out of the reach of children. To be dispensed only on the prescription of a registered physician.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use (2 months up to 16 years)</strong>: The maximum dose for pediatric patients should not exceed the recommended adult dose.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Serious adverse events have occurred in geriatric patients with renal insufficiency given unadjusted doses of cefepime. Care should be taken in dose selection, and renal function should be monitored.</li><li><strong>Impaired Hepatic Function</strong>: No adjustment is necessary for patients with impaired hepatic function.</li><li><strong>Impaired Renal Function</strong>: In patients with impaired renal function (creatinine clearance &lt;60 ml/min), the dose of Cefepime should be adjusted.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p> For IV: The resulting solution should be injected directly into the vein over 3-5 minutes or injected into the tubing of an administration set.</p><p> Cefepime is compatible with Sterile Water for Injection and with several IV infusion fluids (e.g., 0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection, 5% Dextrose Injection, Lactated Ringers).</p><p> For IM: Cefepime is compatible with 0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection, 5% Dextrose Injection, and other diluents.</p>', 'active', '428_1742547774.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:02:54', '2025-03-21 14:02:54'),
(3887, 'Cod Liver Oil', 'Cod Liver Oil', 3.00, 15, 'capsule', '15 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '600 IU+85 IU+0.3 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is your text with the headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Cod Liver Oil</strong></p><p>For the betterment of healthy body, bone, teeth, and muscles. It is widely taken to ease the pain and joint stiffness associated with arthritis. Promotes resistance towards cough, cold, chest, and bronchial troubles. It has anti-thrombotic, anti-arrhythmic, anti-inflammatory, anti-hypertensive effects. Decreases the triglycerides and increases HDL levels. Facilitates the normal growth, development, and function of the central nervous system of fetus and infant. Helps to prevent vitamin A &amp; Vitamin D deficiency. Pregnant women using Cod Liver Oil have infants with a lower risk for juvenile type 1 diabetes.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each Liquid-filled capsule contains 0.3 ml Cod Liver Oil which has:</p><ul><li>Vitamin A: 600 IU</li><li>Vitamin D3: 85 IU</li><li>Vitamin E: 0.3 IU</li><li>Eicosapentaenoic Acid (EPA): 25 mg</li><li>Docosahexaenoic Acid (DHA): 30 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cod Liver Oil is one of the most effective providers of Omega-3 Fatty Acids, and an excellent source of Vitamin A and Vitamin D. It contains Eicosapentaenoic Acid (EPA: 18%) &amp; Docosahexaenoic Acid (DHA: 12%) which are clinically proven to have positive effects on heart, bone, brain health. They also help to nourish skin, hair, and nails.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cod Liver Oil</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Capsule</strong>Adults and children over 6 years: Take 1-2 capsule(s) 3 times a day with meals, or as professionally prescribed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children under 6 years: Only as professionally prescribed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pregnant and lactating women: Take 1 capsule 3 times a day with meals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Emulsion</strong>Infant: ½ tsf daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child (1-4 years): 1 tsf daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child over 4 years: 1.5 tsf daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Cod Liver Oil</strong></p><p>The absorption of Cod Liver Oil may be reduced by taking barbiturates or anti-convulsants.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypervitaminosis of Vitamin A, D, or E and hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients in this preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Cod Liver Oil</strong></p><p>Prolonged usage of large amounts can lead to hypervitaminosis symptoms, which include dry mouth, rough skin, painful joints, swelling, fatigue, anorexia, loss of weight, vomiting, and other gastrointestinal disturbances. These may disappear on discontinuation of supplementation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Safety of amounts exceeding 6,000 Units of Vitamin A daily during pregnancy has not been established yet. The recommended daily allowance (RDA) of vitamin A is 5,000 Units for nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The product must be protected from light. Liver storage should be adequate before discontinuation of therapy. Patients with chronic renal failure should be suggested Cod Liver Oil with caution.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Cod Liver Oil</strong></p><p>When taken in large amounts, Cod Liver Oil can lead to complications including increased risk for hemorrhagic stroke and internal bleeding.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light and moisture.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Cod Liver Oil is one of the most effective providers of Omega-3 Fatty Acids and an excellent source of Vitamin A and Vitamin D. It contains Eicosapentaenoic Acid (EPA: 18%) and Docosahexaenoic Acid (DHA: 12%) which are clinically proven to have positive effects on heart, bone, brain health. They also help to nourish skin, hair, and nails.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Safety of amounts exceeding 6,000 Units of Vitamin A daily during pregnancy has not been established yet. The recommended daily allowance (RDA) of vitamin A is 5,000 Units for nursing mothers.</p><p>Let me know if you need any additional modifications!</p>', 'active', '934_1742548101.webp', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:08:21', '2025-05-03 01:28:57');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3888, 'SK-mox', 'Amoxicillin Trihydrate', 70.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '125 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of SK-mox 125 mg/1.25 ml</strong></p><p>Amoxicillin is indicated in the treatment of infections due to susceptible ß-lactamase negative strains of microorganisms. These infections include:</p><ul><li>Ear, nose and throat infections (i.e. otitis media, sinusitis, tonsillitis, pharyngitis, laryngitis)</li><li>Lower respiratory tract infections (i.e. pneumonia, acute and chronic bronchitis, lung abscess, empyema, bronchiectasis)</li><li>Skin and soft tissue infections (i.e. cellulitis, carbuncles, furunculosis, infected wounds, abscess)</li><li>Genito-urinary tract infections (i.e. pyelonephritis, cystitis, and urethritis)</li><li>Venereal disease (i.e. acute uncomplicated gonorrhoea)</li><li>In dental abscess, it is used as short-term therapy.</li><li>It is also indicated in combination with Clarithromycin and Lansoprazole (as triple therapy), for the treatment of patients with H. pylori infection and duodenal ulcer disease and to reduce the risk of duodenal ulcer recurrence.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Broad spectrum penicillins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Amoxicillin is a broad spectrum penicillin. It is effective against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. It acts through the inhibition of biosynthesis of cell wall. Amoxicillin is stable in the presence of gastric acid and is rapidly absorbed after oral administration. After an oral dose, peak plasma concentration of Amoxicillin is reached within 1 to 2 hours. Amoxicillin is widely distributed at varying concentration in body tissues and fluids.</p><p><strong>Dosage of SK-mox 125 mg/1.25 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: 250 mg three times daily, increasing up to 500 mg three times daily for severe infections.</li><li><strong>Children (up to 10 years of age)</strong>: 125 mg three times daily, increasing up to 250 mg three times daily for severe infections.</li><li>Severe or recurrent purulent respiratory infection: 3 gm every 12 hours.</li><li>Otitis media: Recommended dose is 1 g three times daily for adults and 40 mg/kg body weight daily in 3 divided doses for children (max. 3 g daily).</li><li>Pneumonia: Recommended dose is 500-1000 mg three times daily.</li><li>Dental abscess: Recommended dose is 3 gm, repeated after 10-12 hours.</li><li>Urinary tract infections: Recommended dose is 3 gm, repeated after 10-12 hours.</li><li>Gonorrhoea: Single dose of 2-3 gm with Probenecid 1 gm is recommended (Probenecid is contraindicated in children under 2 years).</li><li>In renal impairment: it may be necessary to reduce the total daily dosage.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of SK-mox 125 mg/1.25 ml</strong></p><p>Reconstituted suspension can be administered by adding the required amount of suspension to milk, fruit juice, or water. These preparations should then be taken immediately.</p><p><strong>Interaction of SK-mox 125 mg/1.25 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Concurrent use of Amoxicillin and Probenecid may result in increased and prolonged blood levels of Amoxicillin.</li><li>Amoxicillin may affect the gut flora, leading to lower estrogen reabsorption and reduced efficacy of combined oral estrogen/progesterone contraceptives.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Amoxicillin is contraindicated in penicillin hypersensitive patients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of SK-mox 125 mg/1.25 ml</strong></p><p>Side effects are mild and transient in nature. This may include diarrhoea, indigestion, or occasionally rash. Pseudo-membranous colitis has been reported rarely.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>US FDA pregnancy category of Amoxicillin is B. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Amoxicillin has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Amoxicillin is administered to a lactating mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The possibility of superinfections with mycotic or bacterial pathogens should be kept in mind during therapy. If superinfections occur, Amoxicillin should be discontinued and appropriate therapy should be instituted.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li>Amoxycillin 500 mg Injection:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intramuscular</strong>: Add 2.5 ml water for injection to Amoxycillin 500 mg injection vial.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intravenous</strong>: Dissolve Amoxycillin 500 mg injection in 10 ml water for injection.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Broad spectrum penicillins</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Amoxicillin is a broad spectrum penicillin. It is effective against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. It acts through the inhibition of biosynthesis of cell wall. Amoxicillin is stable in the presence of gastric acid and is rapidly absorbed after oral administration. After an oral dose, peak plasma concentration of Amoxicillin is reached within 1 to 2 hours. Amoxicillin is widely distributed at varying concentration in body tissues and fluids.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>US FDA pregnancy category of Amoxicillin is B. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Amoxicillin has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Amoxicillin is administered to a lactating mother.</p>', 'active', '1836_1742548143.jpg', 53, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:09:03', '2025-05-06 10:07:42'),
(3889, 'PulmoCare (with device', 'Salbutamol', 11.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '200 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg</strong></p><p>PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg is indicated as a bronchodilator for use in asthma, chronic bronchitis, emphysema, and other conditions associated with airways obstruction.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg is a selective beta2-adrenoceptor agonist. At therapeutic doses, it acts on the beta 2-adrenoceptors of bronchial smooth muscle, with little or no action on the ß1-adrenoceptors of cardiac muscle. PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg provides short-acting (4-6 hours) bronchodilatation with a fast onset (within 5 minutes) in reversible airways obstruction. It also has an anti-inflammatory effect on mast cells causing inhibition of the release of bronchoconstrictor mediators including histamine, neutrophil chemotactic factor (NCF), and prostaglandin D2.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg</strong></p><p><em>Oral</em></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong>2-6 years: 2.5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years: 5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Over 12 years: 5-10 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily (2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily)</li><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily. Maximum single dose is 8 mg tablet.</li></ul><p><em>Injection</em></p><p>PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg Injection may be administered by the subcutaneous, intramuscular, or intravenous route, under the direction of a physician.</p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong>Subcutaneous route: 500 mcg (8 mcg/kg) repeated every four hours as required.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Intramuscular route: 500 mcg (8 mcg/kg) repeated every four hours as required.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Slow intravenous injection: 250 mcg (4 mcg/kg) injected slowly. If necessary, the dose may be repeated.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The use of PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg Injection 500 mcg in 1 ml (500 mcg/ml, for intravenous administration) may be facilitated by dilution to 10 ml with Water for Injection BP (final concentration of 50 mcg/ml).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric Population:</strong> The safety and efficacy of PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg Injection in children under the age of 12 has not been established.</li></ul><p><em>PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg Respirator Solution:</em></p><p>To be used with a suitable nebulizer device under the direction of a physician.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Elderly:</strong> 0.5 ml-1.0 ml PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg up to four times a day.</li><li><strong>Children under 12 years:</strong> 0.5 ml solution diluted to 2.0-4.0 ml with normal saline. Some children may, however, require higher doses up to 1.0 ml of the solution.</li></ul><p><em>Inhalation Capsule</em></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 1 or 2 inhalation capsules as a single dose for relief of bronchospasm.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> 1 inhalation capsule for relief of acute bronchospasm.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg</strong></p><p>PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg may cause fine tremor of skeletal muscles, palpitations, muscle cramps, tachycardia, tenseness, headaches, and peripheral vasodilatation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> The drug should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk of the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Due to potential tumorigenicity shown in some animal studies, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or to discontinue the drug, considering the importance of the drug to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg should be used with caution in patients with hyperthyroidism, cardiovascular disease, occlusive vascular disorders, hypertension, and aneurysms.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdose include angina, headache, nausea, vomiting, and tremor. The preferred antidote for overdose with PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg is a cardio-selective beta-blocking agent, but beta-blocking drugs should be used with caution in patients with a history of bronchospasm.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>PulmoCare (with device) 200 mcg is a synthetic sympathomimetic agent with predominant beta-2 adrenergic activity. It produces bronchodilatation through stimulation of beta-2-adrenergic receptors in bronchial smooth muscles, thereby causing relaxation of bronchial muscle fibers and improvement in pulmonary function.</p>', 'active', '152_1742548273.jpg', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:11:13', '2025-05-04 14:55:27'),
(3890, 'Ufol', 'Propofol', 300.00, 5, 'Injection', '5 infusion', 'Injection', '200 mg per 20 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml</strong></p><p> Induction and maintenance of general anaesthesia. Sedation during intensive care. Sedation for surgical and diagnostic procedures.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> General (Intravenous) anesthetics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Ufol 200 mg/20 ml is a sedative-hypnotic agent for use in the induction and maintenance of anesthesia or sedation. Intravenous injection of a therapeutic dose of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml produces hypnosis rapidly with minimal excitation, usually within 40 seconds from the start of an injection (the time for one arm-brain circulation). The action of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml involves a positive modulation of the inhibitory function of the neurotransmitter gama-aminobutyric acid (GABA) through GABA-A receptors.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults:</strong></p><p> Induction of general anaesthesia: The dosage of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml should be titrated individually against the response of the patient. The ordinary initial dosage in adults is 40 mg (4 ml) by slow intravenous bolus injection at intervals of 10 seconds until the clinical signs show the onset of anaesthesia. The ordinary induction dose in healthy patients below 55 years of age is 2.2-2.5 mg/kg. A dose of 1.0-1.5 mg/kg is often sufficient for older patients. Lower doses, most often 20 mg (2 ml) at intervals of 10 seconds, are recommended for patients of ASA grades 3 and 4.</p><p> Maintenance of general anaesthesia: Anaesthesia can be maintained by administering Ufol 200 mg/20 ml either by continuous infusion or by repeat bolus injections to maintain sufficient anaesthesia.</p><ul><li>Continuous infusion: The required rate of infusion varies considerably between patients. At the onset of anaesthesia (during roughly the first 10-20 minutes), some patients may require a slightly higher infusion rate (8-10 mg/kg/h). However, sufficient anaesthesia is normally achieved by infusing 4-6 (up to 12) mg/kg/h of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml.</li><li>Repeat bolus injections: 25-50 mg (2.5-5.0 ml) bolus injections, depending on response.</li><li> Sedation during intensive care: A bolus injection of 1.0-2.0 mg/kg should be given first, followed by continuous infusion adjusted according to required degree of sedation. An infusion rate of 0.3-4 mg/kg/h is usually sufficient.</li><li> Sedation for surgical and diagnostic procedures: Dosages shall be adjusted individually. Sufficient sedation for surgical and diagnostic procedures can usually be achieved by administering initially 0.5-1 mg/kg during 1-5 minutes, and maintained by continuous at a rate of 1-4.5 mg/kg/h. Bolus dose of 10-20 mg can be given in addition, should deeper sedation be suddenly required. Lower doses of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml are often sufficient for patients of ASA grades 3 and 4, and for older patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Children:</strong></p><p> Ufol 200 mg/20 ml is not recommended for use in children less than 3 years of age as its safety has not been demonstrated.</p><p> Induction of general anaesthesia: Dosage of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml in children shall be adjusted for weight and age. The mean induction dosage in children over 8 years is 2.5 mg/kg, given by slow intravenous injection until the clinical signs show the onset of anaesthesia. Younger children may need slightly higher doses of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml per kilogram of weight. Lower dosages are recommended for children of ASA grades 3 and 4.</p><p> Maintenance of general anaesthesia: Anaesthesia can be maintained by administering Ufol 200 mg/20 ml either by continuous infusion or by repeat bolus injections. Dosage shall be adjusted individually, but an infusion rate of 9-15 mg/kg/h is usually sufficient to achieve satisfactory anaesthesia.</p><p> Sedation during intensive care, surgical diagnostic procedures: Ufol 200 mg/20 ml is not recommended for sedation in children as its efficacy and safety have not been demonstrated. Although no causal relationship has been established, serious adverse events (including fatalities) have been reported in cases, where Ufol 200 mg/20 ml has been used against recommendations. Adverse events have most commonly been seen in children with respiratory tract infections given doses in excess of those recommended for adults.</p><p><strong>Administration of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml</strong></p><p> Method of administration: In order to reduce pain on injection, the induction dose of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml may be mixed immediately before injection in the plastic syringe with lidocaine 10 mg/ml injection, in a ratio of 1 part of lidocaine injection for 20 parts of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml.</p><p> Ufol 200 mg/20 ml can be administered either undiluted or diluted for infusion. Before injection or dilution, each ampoule or vial shall be inspected for any irregularity. Should any changes be observed, the product shall not be used. Suitable equipment shall be used to ensure correct rate of infusion. Volumetric infusion pumps and syringe pumps, for example, are suitable for this purpose. The ordinary infusion set used alone is not sufficient to prevent accidental overdosage reliably enough.</p><p> Compatibility: Ufol 200 mg/20 ml may be diluted only with 5% dextrose infusion. Dilution shall not exceed 1 in 5 (containing 2 mg/ml Ufol 200 mg/20 ml) and shall be prepared in a PVC infusion bag or glass infusion bottle. If a PVC infusion bag is used, the bag should be full and the dilution be prepared by withdrawing a volume of infusion fluid and replacing it with an equal volume of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml. Special attention shall be paid to preparing the dilution aseptically, immediately before administration. Any diluted solution shall be used within 6 hours of preparation. Any remaining solution shall be discarded.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml</strong></p><p> Ufol 200 mg/20 ml has been used in association with spinal and epidural anaesthesia as well as with various types of premedicants, muscle relaxants, inhalation anaesthetics, and analgesics. No pharmacological incompatibility has been observed. Lower doses of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml may be sufficient in case Ufol 200 mg/20 ml is used as an adjunct to local anaesthetic techniques. In doses applied clinically, Ufol 200 mg/20 ml will not inhibit the synthesis of adrenocortical hormones. Simultaneous administration of opiates may potentiate respiratory depression caused by Ufol 200 mg/20 ml.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Allergy to Ufol 200 mg/20 ml or any other ingredient in the preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml</strong></p><p> Local: Ufol 200 mg/20 ml is normally well tolerated. Its most common undesirable effect is pain at the site of injection that can be reduced by mixing the preparation with lidocaine or by injecting it into one of the larger veins of the forearm or the intercubital fossa. Thrombosis and phlebitis are rare.</p><p> General: Hypotension and transient apnea may occur at the induction of anaesthesia, and may be severe especially in patients who are in a poor general condition. Epileptic movement, convulsions, and dystonic reactions have been seen in rare cases. Pulmonary oedema has also been reported. Headache, nausea, and, more rarely, vomiting may occur in some patients during recovery. Recovery may also be associated with another short period of impaired consciousness. Hypersensitivity has been reported in some cases, connected with anaphylactic symptoms such as marked hypotension, bronchospasm, oedema, and facial erythema. Some cases of cardiac arrest have occurred in connection with the administration of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml. In connection with long-term administration of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml, green or reddish-brown discolouration of urine may occur. This is caused by the quinol metabolites of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml, and is not dangerous. As with other anaesthetics, altered sexual behaviour may occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Due to insufficient experience, Ufol 200 mg/20 ml shall not be used during pregnancy. Ufol 200 mg/20 ml is rapidly distributed to the foetus and shall therefore not be used for obstetric anaesthesia. Safety to the neonate has not been established in cases, where Ufol 200 mg/20 ml has been administered to lactating women.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Ufol 200 mg/20 ml should only be given by specialists in anaesthesiology or under their supervision. The physician performing a surgical or diagnostic procedure should not administer Ufol 200 mg/20 ml. Facilities for resuscitation in case of any complication should be available at the treatment unit. During the administration of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml, patients shall be monitored continuously to observe possible hypotension, obstruction in the respiratory tract, hypoventilation or insufficient oxygen intake at a sufficiently early stage. Special attention shall be paid to patients sedated by Ufol 200 mg/20 ml for a surgical or diagnostic procedure, who are not artificially ventilated. Caution shall be taken in administering Ufol 200 mg/20 ml to patients with cardiac, respiratory, renal, or hepatic insufficiency. Hypovolemic patients and those with poor general condition form another risk group.</p><p> Since Ufol 200 mg/20 ml lacks vagolytic activity, bradycardia, even asystole, may occur. Intravenous administration of an anticholinergic agent before induction and during maintenance of anaesthesia should be considered, especially if Ufol 200 mg/20 ml is used in conjunction with other agents likely to cause bradycardia and in situations where vagal tone is likely to predominate. Since Ufol 200 mg/20 ml is a lipid emulsion, appropriate care should be applied in patients with severe disorders of fat metabolism such as pathological hyperlipidemia. If Ufol 200 mg/20 ml is administered to a patient for whom excessive fat intake may be risky, blood lipid values shall be monitored and Ufol 200 mg/20 ml dosage decreased if necessary. If the patient is receiving other parenteral lipid emulsions in addition to Ufol 200 mg/20 ml, the amount of lipid in Ufol 200 mg/20 ml (0.1 g/ml) shall be taken into account, when calculating the total intake of fat. In epileptic patients, Ufol 200 mg/20 ml may lead to convulsions. The analgesic effect of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml as such is insufficient. Analgesics shall be used to ensure sufficient analgesia. Full recovery from general anaesthesia shall be confirmed prior to discharge. It shall be noted that the aftermath of general anaesthesia may impair the patient\'s ability to understand instructions given postoperatively.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml</strong></p><p> Overdosage may cause cardiorespiratory depression. Respiratory depression should be treated by artificial ventilation with oxygen, and cardiovascular depression by lowering of the patient\'s head and elevating of his/her legs. Pressor agents and plasma expanders or Ringer-type electrolyte solutions may be used, if necessary.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Ufol 200 mg/20 ml should be stored at a temperature not exceeding 25º C. It must not be frozen. Any unused solution shall be discarded.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> Use in children: Ufol 200 mg/20 ml is not recommended for use in children less than 3 years of age as its safety has not been demonstrated.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p> Ufol 200 mg/20 ml should not be mixed prior to intravenous injection with solutions or infusion fluids other than 5% dextrose or lidocaine 10 mg/ml injection.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> General (Intravenous) anesthetics</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Ufol 200 mg/20 ml is a sedative-hypnotic agent for use in the induction and maintenance of anesthesia or sedation. Intravenous injection of a therapeutic dose of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml produces hypnosis rapidly with minimal excitation, usually within 40 seconds from the start of an injection (the time for one arm-brain circulation). The action of Ufol 200 mg/20 ml involves a positive modulation of the inhibitory function of the neurotransmitter gama-aminobutyric acid (GABA) through GABA-A receptors.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Due to insufficient experience, Ufol 200 mg/20 ml shall not be used during pregnancy. Ufol 200 mg/20 ml is rapidly distributed to the foetus and shall therefore not be used for obstetric anaesthesia. Safety to the neonate has not been established in cases, where Ufol 200 mg/20 ml has been administered to lactating women.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Use in children: Ufol 200 mg/20 ml is not recommended for use in children less than 3 years of age as its safety has not been demonstrated.</p>', 'active', '1508_1742548456.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:14:16', '2025-03-21 14:14:16'),
(3891, 'Finix', 'Rabeprazole Sodium', 3.50, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Finix 10 mg - Gastro-resistant Tablets of Rabeprazole</strong></h3><h4><strong>Indications</strong></h4><p>Finix 10 mg (Rabeprazole) is recommended for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Active duodenal ulcer.</li><li>Active benign gastric ulcer.</li><li>Symptomatic gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) that is erosive or ulcerative.</li><li>Long-term management of GERD (GERD Maintenance).</li><li>Moderate to severe symptomatic GERD.</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</li><li>Helicobacter pylori eradication in patients with peptic ulcer disease when combined with appropriate antibacterial therapy.</li></ul><h4><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h4><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</li></ul><h4><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h4><p>Rabeprazole inhibits the gastric H+/K+-ATPase enzyme, which is located at the secretory surface of parietal cells in the stomach. This enzyme is responsible for acid (proton) secretion, so by blocking it, rabeprazole effectively reduces gastric acid production.</p><h4><strong>Dosage and Administration</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Active Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily in the morning for 4 weeks. Some patients may need an additional 4 weeks for healing.</li><li><strong>Active Benign Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily in the morning. Healing typically occurs within 6 weeks, with a few patients requiring additional therapy.</li><li><strong>Erosive/Ulcerative GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. An additional 8 weeks may be considered if healing does not occur within the first 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms are unresolved, additional treatment may be needed.</li><li><strong>GERD Maintenance</strong>: 10 mg once daily for long-term management, adjusted based on patient response.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: Start with 60 mg once daily. Doses may be increased as needed up to 120 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori Eradication</strong>: 20 mg twice daily along with amoxicillin (1g) and clarithromycin (500 mg) for 7 days.</li></ul><h4><strong>Administration</strong></h4><ul><li>Take the tablet whole, not crushed or chewed.</li><li>It can be taken with or without food, although taking it in the morning before meals may help with adherence.</li></ul><h4><strong>Interactions</strong></h4><ul><li>Co-administration with drugs like itraconazole and ketoconazole may reduce their effectiveness due to changes in stomach pH.</li><li>Atazanavir, a drug absorbed in a pH-dependent manner, should not be used with rabeprazole.</li><li>No significant interaction with liquid antacids.</li></ul><h4><strong>Contraindications</strong></h4><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to rabeprazole or any excipients.</li><li>Not recommended for use during pregnancy or breastfeeding.</li></ul><h4><strong>Side Effects</strong></h4><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Nausea, vomiting, constipation</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Appetite changes (increased or decreased)</li><li>Muscle pain</li><li>Dizziness, sleepiness</li></ul><h4><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>FDA Pregnancy Category C</strong>: No adequate studies in pregnant women; use only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk.</li><li>Rabeprazole is likely excreted in human milk; breastfeeding should be avoided or discontinued based on the importance of the drug to the mother.</li></ul><h4><strong>Precautions and Warnings</strong></h4><ul><li>Rule out malignancy in patients with symptoms suggestive of gastric or esophageal cancer.</li><li>Long-term use may increase the risk of fractures (hip, wrist, spine), particularly in older patients.</li><li>Monitor for signs of osteoporosis and ensure adequate calcium and vitamin D intake.</li><li>Regular monitoring is recommended for patients on prolonged therapy.</li></ul><h4><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h4><ul><li>Overdose is generally mild and reversible without additional medical intervention.</li><li>No specific antidote; treatment should be symptomatic.</li><li>Rabeprazole is highly protein-bound, so it cannot be dialyzed.</li></ul><h4><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h4><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h4><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Hepatic and Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustments are necessary for these conditions.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Insufficient data on the safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients. Not recommended for children.</li></ul><p>This provides a comprehensive overview of Finix 10 mg (rabeprazole), including its indications, dosage, potential side effects, interactions, and precautions. If you have any further questions or need clarification, feel free to ask!</p>', 'active', '1441_1742548497.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:14:57', '2025-05-03 10:38:49'),
(3892, 'Synflex', 'Glucosamine Sulfate plus Chondroitin', 8.50, 6, 'Tablet', '6 Tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Synflex 250 mg+200 mg</strong></p><p> Indicated for the treatment of osteoarthritis of the knee, hip, spine, hand, and other locations as a dietary supplement. It is also beneficial in rheumatoid arthritis, sports injuries, migraines, different skin problems (e.g., psoriasis), vascular complications (e.g., atherosclerosis), kidney stones, and inflammatory bowel disease (e.g., ulcerative colitis, leaky gut syndrome).</p><p><strong>Dosage of Synflex 250 mg+200 mg</strong></p><p> 250/200 mg tablet: 1 to 2 tablets, three times daily. Dose may be adjusted according to the response of the drug and body weight. Doses can be tapered after 60 days as per the requirement of the individual and for cost convenience. Typical dosage recommendation, based on body weight is as follows:</p><ul><li>Under 54 Kg: 1000 mg Glucosamine Sulfate &amp; 800 mg Chondroitin Sulfate per day</li><li>54 Kg to 91 Kg: 1500 mg Glucosamine Sulfate &amp; 1200 mg Chondroitin Sulfate per day</li><li>Over 91 Kg: 2000 mg Glucosamine Sulfate &amp; 1600 mg Chondroitin Sulfate per day.</li><li> 750/600 mg tablet: 1 tablet two times daily or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Synflex 250 mg+200 mg</strong></p><p> There have been no reports of significant drug interactions of Glucosamine and Chondroitin with Antibiotics/ Antidepressants/ Antihypertensives/ Nitrates/ Antiarrythmics/ Anxiolytics/ Hypoglycemic agents/ Antisecretives/ Antiasthmatics. Chondroitin may enhance the blood thinning effects of anticoagulants like Warfarin, Heparin.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> There are no known contraindications for Glucosamine and Chondroitin. But proven hypersensitivity (e.g., allergic to shellfish or sulfur) to Glucosamine and Chondroitin is a contraindication.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Synflex 250 mg+200 mg</strong></p><p> Safety studies with Glucosamine Sulfate &amp; Chondroitin Sulfate show no demonstrable side effects. Rarely occurring side effects (such as, mild &amp; reversible intestinal flatulence) are almost like placebo.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Patients with Diabetes Mellitus are advised to monitor blood glucose levels regularly when taking Glucosamine. No special studies were formed in patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency. The toxicological and pharmacokinetic profile of Glucosamine and Chondroitin does not indicate limitations for these patients. However, administration to patients with severe hepatic or renal insufficiency should be under appropriate medical supervision. Children should not be supplemented with Glucosamine and Chondroitin.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Stimulation of Cartilage formation</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Glucosamine is a natural amino-sugar, produced by the body and found in certain foods. It is the most fundamental building block required for biosynthesis of glycosaminoglycans (GAGs) like Hyaluronic Acid, Keratan Sulfate, and Chondroitin Sulfate. GAGs bind with protein and form proteoglycans, the essential building block of articular cartilage. When cartilage in a joint deteriorates, osteoarthritis develops. It also helps to form ligaments, tendons, nails, and various other connective tissues. When we take artificially synthesized Glucosamine Sulfate supplement, it increases Glucosamine levels in the body, thus facilitating the production and repair of cartilage. Glucosamine also activates chondrocytes in the cartilage which help produce GAGs and proteoglycans.</p><p> Chondroitin Sulfate is a glycosaminoglycan (acid muco polysaccharide) found in connective tissue, especially in the articular cartilage of all mammals. Chondroitin Sulfate supplement acts similarly to Glucosamine Sulfate, since it also provides substrate for proteoglycans. Chondroitin also protects existing healthy cartilage from premature decline by preventing the MMP (Matrix metalloproteinase) enzyme that breaks down the proteoglycans. Combining Glucosamine with Chondroitin Sulfate shows a synergistic effect. Data supports that this combination has been shown to be very effective in severe cases of Osteoarthritis that treat both signs and symptoms of Osteoarthritis &amp; modifies disease progression. It prevents Osteoarthritis in normal adults. In Osteoarthritic pain, it is as effective as NSAIDs with significantly better tolerability and clinical compliance. It is also helpful during the repair phase of musculoskeletal soft tissue injuries such as tendon or ligament strains.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Women who are pregnant or who could become pregnant should not supplement with Glucosamine Sulfate or Chondroitin Sulfate. Glucosamine and Chondroitin have not been studied enough to determine their effects on a developing fetus. No studies have evaluated the use of Glucosamine and Chondroitin during pregnancy or lactation. It should be taken with caution and medical advice during pregnancy and lactation.</p>', 'active', '1669_1742548628.jpg', 1, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:17:08', '2025-03-21 14:17:08'),
(3893, 'Roxarel', 'Rivaroxaban', 25.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p><strong>Roxarel 2.5 mg:</strong></p><p> For the prevention of atherothrombotic events in adult patients after an Acute Coronary Syndrome (ACS) with elevated cardiac biomarkers (Troponin or CK-MB). It is co-administered with Aspirin alone or with Aspirin plus Clopidogrel or Tidopidine.</p><p><strong>Roxarel 10-20 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>To reduce the risk of stroke and systemic embolism in patients with nonvalvular atrial fibrillation.</li><li>Treatment of deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and pulmonary embolism (PE) and reduction in the risk of recurrence of DVT and PE.</li><li>For the prophylaxis of DVT, which may lead to PE in patients undergoing knee or hip replacement surgery.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Rivaroxaban is a highly selective direct factor Xa inhibitor. Inhibition of factor Xa interrupts the intrinsic and extrinsic pathway of the blood coagulation cascade and inhibits thrombin formation. Rivaroxaban does not inhibit thrombin (activated factor II), and no effects on platelets have been demonstrated.</p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><p><strong>Rivaroxaban 2.5 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>The recommended dose is 2.5 mg twice daily. Patients should also take a daily dose of 75-100 mg Aspirin or a daily dose of 75-100 mg Aspirin in addition to either a daily dose of 75 mg Clopidogrel or a standard daily dose of Ticlopidine.</li></ul><p><strong>Rivaroxaban 10-20 mg:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Nonvalvular Atrial Fibrillation:</strong>Creatinine Clearance &gt;50 mL/min: 20 mg orally, once daily with the evening meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Creatinine Clearance 15-50 mL/min: 15 mg orally, once daily with the evening meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of DVT &amp; PE:</strong>15 mg orally twice daily with food for the first 21 days. After the initial treatment period, 20 mg orally once daily with food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of recurrent DVT &amp; PE:</strong>20 mg once daily with food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of DVT:Following Hip Replacement Surgery:</strong> 10 mg once daily for 35 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Following Knee Replacement Surgery:</strong> 10 mg once daily for 12 days.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><h3><strong>Interaction</strong></h3><ul><li>Concomitant use with <strong>P-gp and CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (ketoconazole, ritonavir, clarithromycin, erythromycin, fluconazole, diltiazem, verapamil, dronedarone) increases Roxarel exposure and should be avoided.</li><li>Co-administration with <strong>P-gp and strong CYP3A4 inducers</strong> (rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine) decreases the efficacy of Roxarel and should also be avoided.</li><li>The use of <strong>anti-platelet agents, heparin, fibrinolytic therapy, NSAIDs</strong> may increase the risk of bleeding.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Patients with known hypersensitivity to Rivaroxaban or any excipients of the product.</li><li>Patients with active pathological bleeding.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p>The most common side effects of Roxarel include:</p><ul><li>Increased chance of bleeding.</li><li>Spinal or epidural hematoma.</li><li>Increased risk of stroke after discontinuation in nonvalvular atrial fibrillation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>Rivaroxaban is a <strong>pregnancy category C drug</strong>. There are no adequate or well-controlled studies in pregnant women.</li><li>It is not known if Rivaroxaban is excreted in human milk, and its safety in breastfeeding women has not been established.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Early discontinuation of Roxarel without adequate alternative anticoagulation increases the risk of <strong>thrombotic events</strong>.</li><li>Roxarel increases the risk of <strong>fatal bleeding</strong>, especially in patients with:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Bleeding disorders</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncontrolled severe arterial hypertension</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gastrointestinal diseases (e.g., inflammatory bowel disease, oesophagitis, gastritis, GERD)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vascular retinopathy, bronchiectasis, or history of pulmonary bleeding</li><li><strong>Neuraxial anesthesia (spinal/epidural anesthesia) or spinal puncture</strong> can lead to epidural or spinal hematoma, requiring monitoring for neurological impairment.</li><li>Roxarel is <strong>not recommended</strong> for patients with pulmonary embolism who present with <strong>hemodynamic instability</strong> or require <strong>thrombolysis or pulmonary embolectomy</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Overdose may cause <strong>hemorrhage</strong>.</li><li>Roxarel systemic exposure is not further increased at single doses &gt;50 mg due to <strong>limited absorption</strong>.</li><li>No specific <strong>antidote</strong> is available.</li><li><strong>Activated charcoal</strong> may be used to reduce absorption in case of overdose.</li><li>Partial reversal of laboratory anticoagulation parameters may be achieved with <strong>plasma products</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p><strong>Oral Anticoagulants</strong></p><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool (below 30°C) &amp; dry place</strong> protected from light.</li><li>Keep away from the <strong>reach of children</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1346_1742548637.webp', 2, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:17:17', '2025-03-21 14:17:17');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3894, 'Don-A', 'Domperidone Maleate', 40.00, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '5 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information presented in a clearer, bolded format as per your request:</p><p><strong>Indications of Don-A 5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Dyspeptic symptom complex, often linked with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, may include:</p><ul><li><strong>Epigastric sensation of fullness</strong></li><li><strong>Abdominal distension</strong></li><li><strong>Upper abdominal pain</strong></li><li><strong>Belching</strong></li><li><strong>Flatulence</strong></li><li><strong>Early satiety</strong></li><li><strong>Nausea and vomiting</strong></li><li><strong>Heartburn</strong>, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms can occur in various conditions, such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine. Additionally, in Parkinson\'s disease, it can manifest as dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting. For radiological studies, it can be used to speed up barium transit in follow-through radiological examinations.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Motility Stimulants</strong></li><li><strong>Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist</strong></li><li><strong>Prokinetic drugs</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Don-A 5 mg/5 ml functions as a dopamine receptor antagonist. It exerts gastroprokinetic action by inhibiting dopamine receptors found in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier means it has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Don-A 5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>For adults, the recommended dosage is <strong>10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily</strong>. Children should take <strong>0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily</strong>. When used for acute nausea and vomiting, the maximum treatment duration should not exceed <strong>12 weeks</strong>. It is advisable to take this medication <strong>15-30 minutes before a meal</strong>.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Don-A 5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>10-20 mg</strong> (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The maximum daily dose of Domperidone is <strong>80 mg</strong>.</li><li><strong>For Children:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg</strong> of body weight or <strong>0.4-0.8 ml</strong> of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>In cases of dyspeptic symptoms:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Adults:</strong> 10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li><strong>For Children:</strong> 0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For acute and sub-acute conditions, especially in cases of acute nausea and vomiting:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Adults:</strong> 20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li><strong>For Children:</strong> 0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>When administered rectally in the form of suppositories:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Adults (including elderly):</strong> 30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li><li><strong>For Children:</strong> The maximum daily rectal dose for children weighing between 10 to 25 kg is 30 mg. The dose may be divided throughout the day if necessary.</li></ul><p>Please note that the maximum treatment period for acute nausea and vomiting is <strong>12 weeks</strong>.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction of Don-A 5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>The simultaneous use of <strong>anticholinergic drugs</strong> may counteract the antidyspeptic effects of domperidone.</li><li>Antacids and antisecretory drugs should not be administered concurrently with domperidone as they can reduce its <strong>oral bioavailability</strong>.</li><li>Domperidone primarily undergoes metabolism through the <strong>CYP3A4 enzyme</strong>. Co-administration with drugs significantly inhibiting this enzyme may lead to elevated plasma levels of domperidone (e.g., azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, nefazodone).</li><li><strong>Caution</strong> when used with drugs that have sustained release or enteric-coated formulations as it may affect their absorption.</li><li>Domperidone does not potentiate the action of <strong>neuroleptics</strong> and suppresses the unwanted peripheral effects caused by dopaminergic agonists like bromocriptine and L-dopa.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Don-A 5 mg/5 ml is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li><strong>Known hypersensitivity</strong> to this medication.</li><li><strong>Neonates</strong>.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal hemorrhage</strong>, <strong>mechanical obstruction</strong>, or <strong>perforation</strong>.</li><li><strong>Prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor</strong> (prolactinoma).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Don-A 5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Rare side effects include <strong>transient intestinal cramps</strong>.</li><li><strong>Extrapyramidal symptoms</strong> are rare in young children and exceptionally rare in adults, typically resolving upon discontinuing the treatment.</li><li>Can lead to <strong>hyperprolactinemia</strong>, causing effects such as <strong>galactorrhea</strong> and <strong>gynecomastia</strong>.</li><li><strong>Allergic reactions</strong> including <strong>rash</strong> and <strong>urticaria</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The safety of Don-A 5 mg/5 ml during pregnancy has not been established. It is <strong>not recommended during pregnancy</strong>.</li><li>Domperidone may induce <strong>galactorrhea</strong> and enhance <strong>post-natal lactation</strong>. It is excreted in breast milk in minimal quantities.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in <strong>children</strong> due to an increased risk of extrapyramidal reactions, especially in those with an immature blood-brain barrier.</li><li>Use cautiously in patients with <strong>hepatic impairment</strong> as domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Don-A 5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose include <strong>drowsiness</strong>, <strong>disorientation</strong>, and <strong>extrapyramidal reactions</strong>, especially in children.</li><li>Treatment for overdose may involve <strong>activated charcoal</strong> and the use of <strong>anticholinergic drugs</strong>, antiparkinson drugs, or antihistamines with anticholinergic properties.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, protected from light and moisture.</li><li><strong>Keep out of children\'s reach</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Motility Stimulants</strong></li><li><strong>Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist</strong></li><li><strong>Prokinetic drugs</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Don-A 5 mg/5 ml acts as a <strong>dopamine antagonist</strong>, primarily blocking dopamine receptors in the <strong>Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ)</strong> and the stomach. It restores normal motility and tone in the upper gastrointestinal tract, promotes gastric emptying, enhances antral and duodenal peristalsis, and regulates pylorus contraction. It also increases <strong>esophageal peristalsis</strong> and lowers <strong>esophageal sphincter pressure</strong>, preventing regurgitation of gastric contents.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in infants:</strong> Caution is advised due to the potential for neurological side effects, especially for infants under <strong>1 year of age</strong>.</li><li><strong>Use in liver disorders:</strong> Caution in patients with hepatic impairment as domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver.</li><li><strong>Use in kidney disorders:</strong> In individuals with <strong>severe renal insufficiency</strong>, dosing frequency should be reduced, and regular reviews are recommended for prolonged therapy.</li></ul>', 'active', '1470_1742548682.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:18:02', '2025-05-02 08:41:47'),
(3895, 'Cosec', 'Omeprazole', 5.05, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Cosec 20 mg</h3><p>Cosec 20 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcers</li><li>Gastric and duodenal ulcers caused by NSAIDs</li><li>Prevention of duodenal and stomach ulcers in individuals with a history of NSAID-related ulcers</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</li><li>Long-term management of acid reflux disease</li><li>Dyspepsia caused by acid</li><li>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers</li><li>Prevention of acid aspiration during general anesthesia</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Peptic ulcers caused by Helicobacter pylori</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Proton Pump Inhibitor</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Cosec 20 mg is a benzimidazole derivative that inhibits stomach acid secretion. It works by blocking the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in gastric parietal cells, preventing gastric acid release. After oral administration:</p><ul><li>The antisecretory effect begins within 1 hour</li><li>Peaks in 2 hours</li><li>Lasts up to 72 hours Secretory activity gradually resumes after discontinuing the medication, typically over 3-5 days.</li></ul><h3>Dosage of Cosec 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Oral</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Benign gastric and duodenal ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks (duodenal ulcer), 8 weeks (gastric ulcer); for severe or recurrent cases, increase to 40 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>NSAID-associated duodenal or gastric ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, may continue for an additional 4 weeks if not fully healed</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, may extend to 4-8 weeks if needed; for refractory cases, 40 mg once daily for 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Long-term management of acid reflux</strong>: 10-20 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acid-related dyspepsia</strong>: 10-20 mg daily for 2-4 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration</strong>: 40 mg the evening before surgery, then 40 mg 2-6 hours before surgery</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: Initial dose 60 mg once daily; usual range is 20-120 mg daily (if &gt;80 mg daily, divide dose)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication</strong>: 20 mg twice daily with amoxicillin and metronidazole or clarithromycin for one week</li><li><strong>Pediatric use (severe ulcerating reflux esophagitis)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">10-20 kg body weight: 10-20 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 kg body weight: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks</blockquote><ul><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration</strong>: 40 mg, slowly injected over 5 minutes, one hour before surgery</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal ulcer, gastric ulcer, or reflux esophagitis</strong>: 40 mg once daily IV if oral administration is inappropriate</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: Initial dose 60 mg IV daily; higher doses may be needed, adjusted individually</li></ul><h3>Administration of Cosec 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Reconstitute with 10 mL of water for injection, then administer slowly over 2-5 minutes (maximum flow rate 4 mL/min)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Use within 4 hours of reconstitution</li><li><strong>IV Infusion</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Reconstitute with 100 mL of 5% dextrose or saline for infusion</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Administer over 20-30 minutes</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Use within 12 hours if using saline, or 6 hours if using dextrose</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Cosec 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Warfarin, phenytoin, and diazepam</strong>: May increase their levels in the body; dosage adjustments may be needed.</li><li><strong>Theophylline, propranolol, antacids</strong>: No significant interaction.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to any ingredient in the formulation.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Cosec 20 mg</h3><p>Common side effects are mild and may include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Stomach cramping</li><li>Paresthesia</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Headache These typically do not require dosage adjustments.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Cosec 20 mg is classified as Category C by the US FDA. Epidemiological studies show no adverse effects on pregnancy or fetal health.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: The drug\'s effects on breast milk and infants are unknown. If needed, breastfeeding should be discontinued.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Gastric cancer</strong>: Rule out gastric cancer before starting treatment for suspected stomach ulcers, as this medication may delay diagnosis.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Drug Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Proton Pump Inhibitor</strong></li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>Cosec 20 mg inhibits stomach acid secretion by blocking the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system in parietal cells.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li>As noted, Cosec 20 mg is Category C and has been shown to have no significant negative impact during pregnancy. However, breastfeeding should be stopped if the drug is deemed necessary.</li></ul>', 'active', '789_1742548781.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:19:41', '2025-05-03 01:41:51'),
(3896, 'Esotac', 'Esomeprazole', 5.75, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Esotac 20 mg</h3><p>Esotac 20 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Alleviating GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn.</li><li>Treating erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Maintaining healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Eradicating <strong>Helicobacter pylori</strong> infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in combination with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Treating <strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>.</li><li>Managing duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><h3>Dosage of Esotac 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. If symptoms persist, an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (Clinical studies did not extend 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms persist, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication</strong> (Triple Therapy): Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days, amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg once daily. Adjust dosage individually and continue as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks according to response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: 40 mg Esotac 20 mg given once daily via intravenous injection (not less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes).</li></ul><h3>Administration of Esotac 20 mg</h3><ul><li>Take <strong>one pill or capsule</strong> of Esotac 20 mg <strong>one hour before a meal</strong> and swallow it whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>: Mix the contents of the packet with 15 ml of water, stir thoroughly, and wait for 2-3 minutes for thickening. Consume the mixture within 30 minutes. If still present, add more water, stir, and drink immediately.</li><li><strong>Nasogastric or Gastric Tube</strong>: Place 15 ml of water in a syringe, add the suspension, wait for 2-3 minutes to thicken, shake, and administer via the tube.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong>: Administer the IV injection slowly over a minimum of 3 minutes after dissolving with 5 ml of the specified solvent.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Esotac 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>CYP2C19 and CYP3A4</strong> metabolize esomeprazole in the liver.</li><li>No clinically significant interactions with <strong>phenytoin</strong>, <strong>warfarin</strong>, <strong>quinidine</strong>, <strong>clarithromycin</strong>, or <strong>amoxicillin</strong>.</li><li><strong>Diazepam</strong> clearance reduced by 45% when taken with Esomeprazole.</li><li>Esomeprazole may hinder the absorption of medications significantly influenced by gastrointestinal pH (e.g., <strong>ketoconazole</strong>, <strong>iron salts</strong>, and <strong>digoxin</strong>).</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Contraindicated for patients with known hypersensitivity to any formulation of esomeprazole.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Esotac 20 mg</h3><ul><li>Most common side effects include <strong>headache</strong>, <strong>diarrhea</strong>, <strong>nausea</strong>, <strong>flatulence</strong>, <strong>abdominal pain</strong>, <strong>constipation</strong>, and <strong>dry mouth</strong>.</li><li>Long-term treatment (up to 12 months) generally does not result in additional side effects.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>A positive symptom response to Esotac 20 mg does not rule out the possibility of stomach cancer.</li><li>Take Esomeprazole at least an hour before meals.</li><li><strong>Capsules</strong> can be opened and mixed with applesauce for those with difficulty swallowing, but do not chew the pellets.</li><li><strong>Antacids</strong> are acceptable while using Esomeprazole.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Esotac 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Acute poisoning</strong> symptoms may include decreased motor activity, changes in breathing, tremors, ataxia, and convulsions.</li><li>Esomeprazole overdoses are rare and have no specific countermeasures.</li><li><strong>Dialysis</strong> is not expected to remove Esomeprazole due to its protein binding.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>dry area</strong> at temperatures not exceeding <strong>30°C</strong>. Keep away from <strong>moisture</strong> and <strong>light</strong>. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Esomeprazole</strong> inhibits the <strong>H+/K+-ATPase</strong> in gastric parietal cells, reducing stomach acid production.</li><li>It is the <strong>S-isomer</strong> of omeprazole, offering superior acid control compared to racemic proton pump inhibitors.</li><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: Peak plasma levels occur about 1.5 hours post-dose, with a bioavailability of about 90% at repeated dosing.</li><li><strong>Metabolism</strong>: Esomeprazole is metabolized by <strong>CYP2C19</strong> and <strong>CYP3A4</strong> enzymes in the liver.</li><li><strong>Excretion</strong>: 80% of the dose is excreted in urine as inactive metabolites, with 20% excreted in feces.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Breastfeeding</h3><ul><li>No sufficient data on <strong>pregnancy</strong>. No teratogenic effects in animals.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: The excretion in milk is not studied, so breastfeeding should be discontinued if Esomeprazole use is deemed necessary.</li></ul><h3>Pediatric Use</h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No significant differences in safety and efficacy between elderly and younger patients, although elderly patients may be more sensitive.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong>: No dose change required for mild to moderate hepatic impairment; do not exceed 20 mg daily for severe hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: No significant change in pharmacokinetics in patients with renal impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '1098_1742548875.webp', 62, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:21:15', '2025-05-03 10:14:49'),
(3897, 'Xerosec', 'Omeprazole', 8.02, 1, 'capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '40mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xerosec 40 mg</strong></p><p> Xerosec 40 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcer.</li><li>Gastric and duodenal ulcers brought on by NSAIDs.</li><li>In individuals who have a history of duodenal and stomach ulcers brought on by NSAIDs, as a preventative treatment.</li><li>The disease of gastroesophageal reflux.</li><li>Care of acid reflux illness throughout the long term.</li><li>Dyspepsia brought on by acid.</li><li>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers.</li><li>Prevention of aspiration of acid while under general anesthesia.</li><li>Syndrome of Zollinger-Ellison.</li><li>Peptic ulcer brought on by Helicobacter pylori.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Proton Pump Blocker</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> An inhibitor of stomach acid secretion, Xerosec 40 mg is a benzimidazole replacement. By inhibiting the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in the gastric parietal cell, it prevents the release of gastric acid. Following oral administration, the antisecretory effect starts to take effect in about an hour, peaks in two hours, and the inhibition of secretion lasts up to 72 hours. Secretory activity gradually resumes when the medicine is stopped, over a period of 3 to 5 days.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Xerosec 40 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Oral:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Benign gastric and duodenal ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks in duodenal ulceration, 8 weeks in gastric ulceration; in severe or recurrent cases, dose to be increased to 40 mg daily; maintenance dose for recurrent duodenal ulcer, 20 mg once daily; in prevention of relapse in duodenal ulcer, 10-20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>NSAID-associated duodenal or gastric ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continued for another 4 weeks if not fully healed. 20 mg once daily is used as prophylaxis in patients with a history of NSAID-associated duodenal or gastric ulcers.</li><li><strong>Gastro-esophageal reflux disease:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continued for another 4-8 weeks if not fully healed; 40 mg once daily has been given for 8 weeks in gastro-esophageal reflux disease refractory to other treatment; maintenance dose is 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Long-term management of acid reflux disease:</strong> 10-20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Acid-related dyspepsia:</strong> 10-20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration:</strong> 40 mg on the preceding evening, then 40 mg 2-6 hours before surgery.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</strong> Initially 60 mg once daily; usual range 20-120 mg daily (If daily dose is more than 80 mg, it should be divided into two doses).</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication regimen in peptic ulcer disease:</strong> Xerosec 40 mg is recommended at a dose of 20 mg twice daily in association with antimicrobial agents such as:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Amoxicillin 500 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg both three times a day for one week, or</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clarithromycin 250 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg both twice a day for one week, or</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Amoxicillin 1 g and Clarithromycin 500 mg both twice a day for one week.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric use in severe ulcerating reflux esophagitis (Child &gt;1 year):</strong>If body weight 10-20 kg: 10-20 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If body weight over 20 kg: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>IV Injection:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration:</strong> Xerosec 40 mg should be given slowly (over a period of 5 minutes) as an intravenous injection, one hour before surgery.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer, gastric ulcer, or reflux oesophagitis:</strong> In cases where oral medication is inappropriate, 40 mg IV once daily is recommended.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (ZES):</strong> The recommended initial dose is 60 mg daily via IV. Higher daily doses may be required and should be adjusted individually. If doses exceed 60 mg daily, they should be divided and given twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Xerosec 40 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Direction for IV Injection:</strong></p><ul><li>Xerosec 40 mg lyophilized powder and water for injection should only be administered intravenously.</li><li>Slow IV injections should be given over a period of at least 2 to 5 minutes at a maximum flow rate of 4 ml/minute.</li><li>To prepare the solution, add 10 ml of water for injection to the vial containing the powder.</li><li>The injection must be administered immediately after reconstitution and used within four hours.</li><li>For IV infusion, dissolve one vial in 100 ml of either 5% dextrose or saline and administer over 20-30 minutes.</li><li>When dissolved in saline, use within 12 hours; when dissolved in 5% dextrose, use within 6 hours.</li><li>No other medication should be mixed or administered in the same infusion set as the reconstituted solution.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xerosec 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Warfarin, phenytoin, and diazepam may take longer to leave the body when taken with Xerosec 40 mg, requiring a dose reduction.</li><li>Theophylline, propranolol, or antacids do not interact with Xerosec 40 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Individuals with a known hypersensitivity to any ingredients in the formulation should not take Xerosec 40 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xerosec 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Generally well-tolerated.</li><li>Mild and temporary side effects such as nausea, stomach cramping, paresthesia, dizziness, and headache may occur but rarely require dosage adjustments.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Xerosec 40 mg falls under <strong>Category C</strong> of the US FDA pregnancy classification.</li><li>Studies suggest no adverse effects on pregnancy, fetus, or newborn health.</li><li>The presence of Xerosec 40 mg in breast milk and its effects on infants are unknown. If deemed necessary, breastfeeding should be stopped.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Before starting therapy for suspected stomach ulcers, gastric cancer should be ruled out, as the medication may mask symptoms and delay diagnosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep dry and away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Blocker</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Xerosec 40 mg is a benzimidazole derivative that inhibits stomach acid production by blocking the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme in gastric parietal cells.</li><li>Its antisecretory effect begins within an hour, peaks within two hours, and lasts up to 72 hours.</li><li>Secretory activity gradually resumes 3 to 5 days after discontinuation.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Falls under <strong>Category C</strong> according to the US FDA.</li><li>Studies suggest no harmful effects on pregnancy or newborn health.</li><li>The presence of the drug in breast milk is unknown; breastfeeding should be stopped if necessary.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1621_1742548992.jpg', 45, 12, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:23:12', '2025-03-21 14:23:12'),
(3898, 'Triderm', 'Clotrimazole', 35.00, 1, 'Cream', '10 gm tube', 'Cream', '1 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Triderm 1%</strong></p><p> The confirmed indication for Triderm 1% include:</p><ul><li>All dermatomycoses due to dermatophytes (e.g. Trichophyton species).</li><li>All dermatomycoses due to yeasts (Candida species).</li><li>Dermatomycoses due to moulds and other fungi.</li><li>Skin diseases showing superinfections with these fungi.</li></ul><p>To the dermatomycoses listed under 1-4 belong, for example:</p><ul><li>Interdigital mycoses (e.g. athlete’s foot)</li><li>Paronychias (associated with nail mycoses)</li><li>Mycoses in skin folds</li><li>Candida vulvitis</li><li>Candida balanitis</li><li>Pityriasis versicolor</li><li>Erythrasma</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses, Topical Antifungal preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Triderm 1% is a broad-spectrum antifungal. It also exhibits activity against Trichomonas, Staphylococci, Streptococci, and Bacteroides. Triderm 1% kills fungi and yeasts by interfering with their cell membranes. It works by stopping the production of ergosterol, which is an essential component of fungal cell membranes, resulting in increased membrane permeability and leakage of essential constituents of the fungal cells.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Triderm 1%</strong></p><p> Wash skin with soap and water and dry thoroughly. Apply a thin layer of the cream or solution 2-3 times daily and gently massage over the affected area as directed by the doctor. For Athlete’s foot, pay special attention to the spaces between the toes. Best results in Athlete\'s foot and ringworm are usually obtained with 4 weeks of use of this product. The cream should be applied two or three times daily for one month or for at least two weeks after the disappearance of all signs of infection. If satisfactory results have not occurred within these times, consultation with a doctor is essential. For best results, follow directions and continue treatment for the length of time indicated.</p><p>The duration of treatment varies; it depends on other factors, including the extent and localization of the disease.</p><p> Recommended duration of treatment:</p><ul><li>Dermatomycoses: 3-4 weeks</li><li>Candida vulvitis and Candida balanitis: 1-2 weeks</li><li>Erythrasma and pityriasis versicolor (approx.): 3 weeks</li><li> In fungal infection of the feet, to prevent relapses: Treatment should be continued for about 2 weeks beyond the disappearance of all signs of disease. Triderm 1% cream is odourless, can be washed off, and does not stain.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to Triderm 1%.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Triderm 1%</strong></p><p> Triderm 1% is generally well tolerated after local application, but a few side effects have been reported infrequently, such as erythema, stinging, blistering, peeling, oedema, pruritus, urticaria, and general irritation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category B. Triderm 1% is recommended during pregnancy only after first consulting a doctor.</p><p> Lactation: Because systemic absorption of Triderm 1% following topical application is marginal, there should be no risk for the infant during lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> If local intolerance develops, consider withdrawal of the medicine and institution of appropriate therapy. Triderm 1% solution is not intended for ophthalmic use.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Triderm 1%</strong></p><p> Supportive measures should be taken in case of accidental oral ingestion.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at a cool and dry place. Protect from light. Do not freeze.</p><p><strong>Duration Of Treatment</strong></p><p> For the treatment to become a complete success, reliable and sufficiently long application of Triderm 1% cream is important. The duration of treatment varies; it depends on other factors, including the extent and localization of the disease.</p>', 'active', '242_1742549009.jpg', 1, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:23:29', '2025-03-21 14:23:29'),
(3899, 'Rex', 'Betacarotene + Vitamin C + Vitamin E', 3.50, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '6 mg+200 mg+50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Rex</h3><p>Antioxidant vitamins are used in a wide range of conditions where free radical damage plays a role. An antioxidant vitamin combination is used in the prevention of coronary heart diseases, certain types of cancer, aging, as well as free radical damage caused by excessive exercise, illness, certain medications, air pollution, smoke, radiation, and pesticides. The main roles of the antioxidant vitamins are as follows:</p><ul><li><strong>β-Carotene</strong>: Prevents free radical formation by quenching singlet oxygen, a highly reactive form of oxygen.</li><li><strong>Vitamin C</strong>: A powerful free radical scavenger that deactivates free radicals, especially in plasma, lung fluid, aqueous humor, and interstitial fluid. It enhances white blood cell activity and plays an important role in the biochemistry of antibodies, prostaglandin E1, B and T lymphocytes, and interferon.</li><li><strong>Vitamin E</strong>: Scavenges free radicals in the blood along with β-carotene and vitamin C. It also protects against some ill effects of smog and smoke and protects vitamin A from being destroyed in the body.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Anti-oxidant Multivitamin preparations</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li><strong>Beta-carotene</strong>: Converted to vitamin A (Retinol) when required, supporting functions in the retina, growth, tissue differentiation, and immunological response. It also has some anti-cancer activity.</li><li><strong>Vitamin C</strong>: Known as the most powerful reducing agent in living tissues. It plays crucial roles in various biological processes, including the conversion of proline to hydroxyproline and dopamine to noradrenaline. It’s essential for the synthesis of adrenal steroid hormones and important in the defense against infections. Ascorbic acid has anti-inflammatory activity and protects cells against oxidation of essential molecules. In high doses (1-2g daily), it can increase iron absorption.</li><li><strong>Vitamin E</strong>: Serves as a defense against oxidative stress and lipid peroxidation. Tocopherol mops up peroxide radicals and needs a supply of reduced hydrogen to restore the steady-state situation, which is usually supplied by ascorbic acid or reduced glutathione.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rex</h3><p>This tablet is administered orally. The adult dose of this combination of antioxidant vitamin tablets is <strong>1 tablet daily</strong> or as prescribed by the physician.</p><h3>Interactions of Rex</h3><ul><li><strong>Cholestyramine, Colestipol, Neomycin</strong>: Decrease absorption of β-carotene.</li><li><strong>Oral contraceptives containing estrogen, Tetracycline, and Aspirin</strong>: Can reduce circulating vitamin C levels due to impaired absorption or increased utilization for drug metabolism.</li><li><strong>Vitamin E</strong>: May enhance the anticoagulant activity of anticoagulant drugs. High doses of vitamin E can impair intestinal absorption of vitamins A and K.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Rex is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to any of its components.</p><h3>Side Effects of Rex</h3><ul><li><strong>β-Carotene</strong>: Safe at high and prolonged exposure. Excessive intake may cause <strong>hypercarotenosis</strong>, a yellowish discoloration of the skin and high carotenoid levels in plasma, which is benign and resolves with reduced intake.</li><li><strong>Vitamin C</strong>: Generally safe in normal doses. Larger doses may lead to gastrointestinal upset and renal stone formation.</li><li><strong>Vitamin E</strong>: Generally safe even in large doses. Doses over 800 mg may cause <strong>diarrhea</strong>, abdominal pain, cramps, fatigue, reduced bacterial resistance, and transiently raised blood pressure.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><p>β-carotene, vitamin C, and vitamin E have no teratogenic effects in humans. However, like any other drugs, caution should be exercised when prescribing to pregnant women.</p><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>β-Carotene</strong>: Caution in heavy smokers and alcoholics, as it may cause harm.</li><li><strong>Vitamin C</strong>: Should be given cautiously to patients with hyperoxaluria.</li><li><strong>Vitamin E</strong>: Should be used with caution in patients on anticoagulant drugs, as it may enhance their activity.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><p>Store in a dry place below <strong>30˚C</strong>.</p><h3>Drug Classes</h3><p>Anti-oxidant Multivitamin preparations</p><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Beta-carotene</strong> is converted to vitamin A (retinol) when required, supporting retina, growth, tissue differentiation, and immune function.</li><li><strong>Vitamin C</strong> is a potent reducing agent and cofactor for various biological processes, essential for infection defense, and helps increase iron absorption in high doses.</li><li><strong>Vitamin E</strong> serves as a defense against oxidative stress, lipid peroxidation, and protects cell membranes.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy</h3><p>β-carotene, vitamin C, and vitamin E have no teratogenic effects. Caution is recommended when prescribing these during pregnancy.</p>', 'active', '11_1742549039.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:23:59', '2025-05-04 16:42:21'),
(3900, 'Slipaid', 'Doxepin', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '6 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Slipaid 6 mg</strong></p><p> Slipaid 6 mg tablet is indicated for the treatment of insomnia characterized by difficulties with sleep maintenance. Slipaid 6 mg capsule is recommended for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Psychoneurotic patients with depression and/or anxiety.</li><li>Depression and/or anxiety associated with alcoholism (not to be taken concomitantly with alcohol).</li><li>Depression and/or anxiety associated with organic disease (the possibility of drug interaction should be considered if the patient is receiving other drugs concomitantly).</li><li>Psychotic depressive disorders with associated anxiety including involutional depression and manic-depressive disorders.</li></ul><p>The target symptoms of psychoneurosis that respond particularly well to Slipaid 6 mg include anxiety, tension, depression, somatic symptoms and concerns, sleep disturbances, guilt, lack of energy, fear, apprehension, and worry.</p><p>Clinical experience has shown that Slipaid 6 mg is safe and well tolerated even in the elderly patient. Owing to the lack of clinical experience in the pediatric population, Slipaid 6 mg is not recommended for use in children under 12 years of age.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Tricyclic &amp; related anti-depressant drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> The mechanism of action of Slipaid 6 mg is not definitely known. It is not a central nervous system stimulant nor a monoamine oxidase inhibitor. The current hypothesis is that the clinical effects are due, at least in part, to influences on the adrenergic activity at the synapses so that deactivation of norepinephrine by reuptake into the nerve terminals is prevented.</p><p>Animal studies suggest that Slipaid 6 mg does not appreciably antagonize the antihypertensive action of guanethidine. In animal studies, anticholinergic, anti-serotonin, and anti-histamine effects on smooth muscle have been demonstrated. At higher than usual clinical doses, norepinephrine response was potentiated in animals. This effect was not demonstrated in humans.</p><p>At clinical dosages up to 150 mg per day, Slipaid 6 mg can be given to humans concomitantly with guanethidine and related compounds without blocking the antihypertensive effect. At dosages above 150 mg per day, blocking of the antihypertensive effect of these compounds has been reported. Slipaid 6 mg is virtually devoid of euphoria as a side effect. Characteristic of this type of compound, Slipaid 6 mg has not been demonstrated to produce the physical tolerance or psychological dependence associated with addictive compounds.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Slipaid 6 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Capsule</strong>:</p><p> For most patients with mild to moderate severity illness, a starting daily dose of 75 mg is recommended. Dose may subsequently be increased or decreased at appropriate intervals and according to individual response. The usual optimum dose range is 75 mg/day to 150 mg/day. In more severely ill patients, higher doses may be required with subsequent gradual increase to 300 mg/day if necessary. Additional therapeutic effect is rarely obtained by exceeding a dose of 300 mg/day. In patients with very mild symptomatology or emotional symptoms accompanying organic disease, lower doses may suffice. Some of these patients have been controlled on doses as low as 25 to 50 mg/day.</p><p>The total daily dosage of Slipaid 6 mg (as the hydrochloride) may be given on a divided or once-a-day dosage schedule. If the once-a-day schedule is employed, the maximum recommended dose is 150 mg/day. This dose may be given at bedtime. The 150 mg capsule strength is intended for maintenance therapy only and is not recommended for initiation of treatment. Antianxiety effect is apparent before the antidepressant effect. Optimal antidepressant effect may not be evident for 2 to 3 weeks.</p><p><strong>Tablet</strong>:</p><p> Initial dose: 6 mg, once daily for adults and 3 mg, once daily for the elderly. Take within 30 minutes of bedtime. Total daily dose should not exceed 6 mg. Should not be taken within 3 hours of a meal.</p><p><strong>Administration of Slipaid 6 mg</strong></p><p> May be taken with or without food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Slipaid 6 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Methylphenidate may increase plasma Slipaid 6 mg levels.</li><li>Potentially fatal: Potentiates hypertensive action of sympathomimetics.</li><li>Increased anticholinergic effects with MAOIs.</li><li>Additive CNS effects with anticholinergics, CNS depressants, and alcohol.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Slipaid 6 mg is contraindicated in individuals who have shown hypersensitivity to the drug. Possibility of cross-sensitivity with other dibenzoxepines should be kept in mind. Slipaid 6 mg is contraindicated in patients with glaucoma or a tendency to urinary retention. These disorders should be ruled out, particularly in older patients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Slipaid 6 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Sedation, fatigue, weakness, lethargy</li><li>Dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision, headache</li><li>Agitation, insomnia, anxiety, nausea, vomiting</li><li>Sweating, confusion, extrapyramidal symptoms (EPS), dizziness, paresthesia</li><li>Orthostatic hypotension, ECG changes, tachycardia, increased LFTs</li><li>Tinnitus, sexual dysfunction, rash, seizure</li><li>Agranulocytosis, thrombocytopenia, eosinophilia</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Reproduction studies have been performed in rats, rabbits, monkeys, and dogs, and there was no evidence of harm to the animal fetus. The relevance to humans is not known. Since there is no experience in pregnant women who have received this drug, safety in pregnancy has not been established. There has been a report of apnea and drowsiness occurring in a nursing infant whose mother was taking Slipaid 6 mg.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Epilepsy, CV disease, pregnancy, history of urinary retention, glaucoma</li><li>Gradual withdrawal</li><li>May impair the ability to drive or operate machinery.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at 15-30°C.</p>', 'active', '652_1742549137.jpg', 55, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:25:37', '2025-03-21 14:25:37');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3901, 'Yamadin', 'Famotidine', 19.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Yamadin 20 mg</strong></p><p>Yamadin 20 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Gastric ulcer</li><li>Duodenal ulcer</li><li>Anastomotic ulcer</li><li>Acute stress ulcer</li><li>Reflux esophagitis</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li></ul><p>Yamadin 20 mg is also indicated for the treatment of acute gastritis and chronic gastritis during the acute exacerbation stage.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>H2 receptor antagonist</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Yamadin 20 mg is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist. It completely inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors of parietal cells, thereby reducing gastric acid secretion. It inhibits basal, overnight, and pentagastrin-stimulated gastric acid secretion. The H2-receptor antagonist activity of Yamadin 20 mg is slowly reversible, as the drug dissociates slowly from the H2-receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer</strong>: 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks</li><li><strong>Benign gastric ulcer</strong>: 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks; Maintenance therapy: 20 mg at night to prevent recurrence</li><li><strong>Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</strong>: 20 mg twice daily for 6 to 12 weeks</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: Starting dose is 20 mg every six hours, with doses adjusted based on individual response. Doses up to 160 mg every six hours have been administered to some patients without significant adverse effects</li><li><strong>Dosage can be administered irrespective of meals</strong>. Antacids may be given concurrently if needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage Forms</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Tablet</strong></li></ol><ul><li>Gastric ulcer, duodenal ulcer, anastomotic ulcer, upper gastrointestinal hemorrhage, reflux esophagitis, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual dose: 20 mg twice daily (after breakfast and after supper or before bedtime) or 40 mg once daily at bedtime</li><li>For acute gastritis or chronic gastritis during the acute exacerbation stage:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">20 mg twice daily (after breakfast and after supper or before bedtime) or 40 mg once daily at bedtime</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance therapy: 20 mg once daily</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Powder for Suspension</strong></li></ol><ul><li>GERD (for pediatric patients):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">&lt;1 year: 0.5 mg/kg/dose once daily for up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Age 3 to 11 months: 0.5 mg/kg/dose twice daily for up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Age 1 to 2 months: 0.5 mg/kg/dose once daily for up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Neonates: 0.5 mg/kg/dose once daily for up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Age 1-16 years: 1 mg/kg/day divided b.i.d. up to 40 mg b.i.d.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Duodenal ulcer and peptic ulcer: 0.5 mg/kg/day divided b.i.d. up to 40 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance therapy: 40 mg once daily at bedtime</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong></li></ol><ul><li>For hospitalized patients with pathological hypersecretory conditions or intractable ulcers, or for patients unable to take oral medication:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommended dose: 20 mg intravenously every 12 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For pediatric patients (1-16 years): Starting dose is 0.25 mg/kg intravenously every 12 hours, up to 40 mg/day</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>No clinically significant drug interactions have been identified. Yamadin 20 mg does not interact with the cytochrome P450 enzyme system.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to any component of the drug</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Common side effects may include:</p><ul><li>Eruption</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Tachycardia</li><li>High blood pressure</li><li>Headache</li><li>Drowsiness or insomnia (rarely)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B. There are no well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Yamadin 20 mg should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is unknown whether Yamadin 20 mg is secreted in human milk. Nursing mothers should either stop nursing or stop taking the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use the minimum required dose for the conditions being treated.</li><li>Exercise caution in elderly patients, and those with renal or hepatic disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Yamadin 20 mg is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist. It inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors in parietal cells, reducing gastric acid secretion. Its activity is slowly reversible, as it dissociates slowly from the H2-receptor.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy Category</strong></p><p>Category B. Yamadin 20 mg should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Caution should be exercised when administering the drug to nursing mothers.</p>', 'active', '1482_1742549238.png', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:27:18', '2025-05-05 22:10:59'),
(3902, 'Xone-3', 'Ceftriaxone Sodium', 100.00, 1, 'Injection', '500 mg vial', 'Injection', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xone-3 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Xone-3 500 mg/vial is indicated for the treatment of the following major infections:</p><ul><li>Lower respiratory tract infections</li><li>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media</li><li>Skin and skin structure infections</li><li>Urinary tract infections</li><li>Gonorrhea</li><li>Bacterial Septicemia</li><li>Bone and joint infections</li><li>Meningitis</li><li>Prevention of postoperative infections</li><li>Perioperative prophylaxis of infections associated with surgery</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Xone-3 500 mg/vial is a 3rd generation broad-spectrum parenteral cephalosporin antibiotic. It has potent bactericidal activity against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms. Like other cephalosporins and penicillins, Xone-3 500 mg/vial kills bacteria by interfering with the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall.</p><p>Xone-3 500 mg/vial has a high degree of stability in the presence of beta-lactamases. A remarkable feature of Xone-3 500 mg/vial is its relatively long plasma elimination half-life of about 6 to 9 hours, which makes single or once-daily dosage of the drug appropriate for most patients. Xone-3 500 mg/vial is not metabolized in the body. About 40-65% of a dose of Xone-3 500 mg/vial is excreted unchanged in the urine; the remainder is excreted in the bile and ultimately found in the feces as unchanged drug and microbiologically inactive compound. The drug is highly protein-bound (95%).</p><p><strong>Dosage of Xone-3 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> <strong>Adult</strong>:</p><p> The usual dose is 1 to 2 gm by intravenous or intramuscular administration once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day).</p><ul><li>Pneumonia, Bronchitis, Acute bacterial otitis media, Skin and skin structure infection, Urinary tract infections, Bacterial Septicemia, Bone and joint infections, Meningitis: 1 to 2 g IV or IM once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 4 gm/day</li><li>Uncomplicated gonococcal infections: 250 mg IM as a single dose</li><li>Surgical prophylaxis: 1 g IV as a single dose 30 to 120 minutes before surgery</li></ul><p><strong>Infants and Children (1 month or older)</strong>:</p><p> The usual dose is 50 to 75 mg/kg intravenous or intramuscular administration once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day).</p><ul><li>Pneumonia, Bronchitis, Skin and skin structure infection, Urinary tract infections, Bacterial Septicemia, Bone and joint infections: 50 to 75 mg/kg IV or IM once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 2 gm/day</li><li>Acute bacterial otitis media: 50 mg/kg IM in single dose; Maximum dose: 1 gm/day</li><li>Meningitis: 100 mg/kg IV or IM in single daily dose or (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 4 gm/day</li></ul><p><strong>Duration of therapy</strong>:</p><p> Continue for more than 2 days after signs and symptoms of infection have disappeared. Usual duration is 4 to 14 days; in complicated infections, longer therapy may be required.</p><p><strong>Administration of Xone-3 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> <strong>Preparation of Solutions for Intramuscular / Intravenous Injections</strong>:</p><p> For Intramuscular Injection:</p><ul><li>250 mg or 500 mg Xone-3 500 mg/vial should be dissolved in 2 ml Lidocaine HCI 1% injection or 1 g Xone-3 500 mg/vial in 3.5 ml of Lidocaine HCI 1% injection.</li></ul><p>For Intravenous Injection:</p><ul><li>250 mg or 500 mg Xone-3 500 mg/vial should be dissolved in 5 ml of Water for injection or 1 g Xone-3 500 mg/vial in 10 ml of Water for injection USP or 2 g Xone-3 500 mg/vial in 20 ml of Water for injection.</li></ul><p>The injection should be administered over 2-4 minutes, by Intramuscular or Intravenous injection or by tubing infusion over a period of 30 minutes at concentration between 10 mg/mL and 40 mg/mL. Before starting treatment through Xone-3 500 mg/vial injection, patient tolerance test should be checked by administration of a test dose. (The use of freshly reconstituted solution is recommended. However, it maintains potency for at least 6 hours at room temperature or 24 hours at 5°C).</p><p><strong>Interaction of Xone-3 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> No drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Xone-3 500 mg/vial should not be given to patients with a history of hypersensitivity to cephalosporin antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xone-3 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Xone-3 500 mg/vial is generally well tolerated. A few side effects such as:</p><ul><li>Gastro-intestinal effects including diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, stomatitis, and glossitis</li><li>Cutaneous reactions including rash, pruritus, urticaria, edema, and erythema multiforme</li><li>Hematologic reactions including eosinophilia, thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, anemia, and neutropenia</li><li>Hepatic reactions including elevations of SGOT or SGPT, bilirubinemia</li><li>CNS reactions including nervousness, confusion, sleep disturbances, headache, hyperactivity, convulsion, hypertonia, and dizziness</li></ul><p>Local phlebitis occurs rarely following intravenous administration but can be minimized by slow injections over 2-4 minutes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Its safety in human pregnancy has not been established. Therefore, it should not be used in pregnancy unless absolutely indicated. Low concentrations of Xone-3 500 mg/vial are excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Xone-3 500 mg/vial is administered to a lactating mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>As with other cephalosporins, anaphylactic shock cannot be ruled out even if a thorough patient history is taken. Anaphylactic shock requires immediate countermeasures such as intravenous epinephrine followed by a glucocorticoid.</li><li>In rare cases, shadows suggesting sludge have been detected by sonograms of the gallbladder. This condition was reversible on discontinuation or completion of Xone-3 500 mg/vial therapy. Even if such findings are associated with pain, conservative, nonsurgical management is recommended.</li><li>During prolonged treatment, the blood picture should be checked at regular intervals.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xone-3 500 mg/vial</strong></p><p> There is no specific antidote. Treatment of overdosage should be symptomatic.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Vial store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> Xone-3 500 mg/vial must not be given to neonates if the neonate is premature and newborn (up to 28 days of age).</p>', 'active', '1960_1742549294.jpg', 33, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:28:14', '2025-03-21 14:28:14'),
(3903, 'Alarup', 'Rupatadine Fumarate', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information with the headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Alarup 10 mg</strong></p><p>Alarup 10 mg is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis and Urticaria.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Alarup 10 mg is a second generation, non-sedating, long-acting histamine antagonist with selective peripheral H1 receptor antagonist activity. It further blocks the receptors of the platelet-activating factor (PAF) according to in vitro and in vivo studies. Rupatadine possesses anti-allergic properties such as the inhibition of the degranulation of mast cells induced by immunological and non-immunological stimuli, and inhibition of the release of cytokines, particularly of the tumor necrosis factors (TNF) in human mast cells and monocytes.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Alarup 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Rupatadine Tablet</strong>: The recommended dosage is 10 mg once daily, with or without food.</li><li><strong>Rupatadine Oral Solution (Children aged 2 to 11 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing 25 kg or more: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) of oral solution once daily with or without food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing equal or more than 10 kg to less than 25 kg: 1/2 teaspoonful (2.5 ml) oral solution once daily with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Alarup 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (over 12 years)</strong>: The recommended dose is 10 mg once a day. Rupatadine may be taken with or without food.</li><li><strong>Children aged 2 to 11 years</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing 25 kg or more: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) of the oral solution once daily with or without food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing equal or more than 10 kg to less than 25 kg: 1/2 teaspoonful (2.5 ml) oral solution once daily with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Alarup 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With medicine</strong>: The concomitant administration of Rupatadine 20 mg and ketoconazole or erythromycin increases the systemic exposure. Rupatadine should be used with caution when it is administered concomitantly with these drug substances and other inhibitors of the isozyme CYP3A4. Rupatadine should be used with caution when it is co-administered with statins, CNS depressants, or alcohol.</li><li><strong>With food</strong>: Grapefruit and Grapefruit juice should not be taken simultaneously with Rupatadine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to Rupatadine or to any of the excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Alarup 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Asthenia, dizziness, drowsiness.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Appetite increased, arthralgia, back pain, concentration impaired, constipation, cough, diarrhea, dry throat, epistaxis, fever, gastrointestinal discomfort, increased risk of infection, irritability, malaise, myalgia, nasal dryness, nausea, oropharyngeal pain, rash, thirst, vomiting, weight increased.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Palpitations, tachycardia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy category B2 (by TGA, Australia). There is no clinical data available on the exposure of Rupatadine during pregnancy. Pregnant women should therefore not use Rupatadine, unless the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk for the fetus. No information is available, whether Rupatadine is excreted in the mother\'s milk. Therefore, it should not be used during lactation, unless the potential benefits for the mother justify the potential risk to the infant.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Alarup 10 mg should be used with caution in patients with known prolongation of the QT interval, patients with uncorrected hypokalemia, and patients with ongoing proarrhythmic conditions, such as clinically significant bradycardia or acute myocardial ischemia. Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly patients (65 years and older). As there is no clinical experience in patients with impaired kidney or liver function, the use of Rupatadine 10 mg tablets is at present not recommended in these patients.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Alarup 10 mg</strong></p><p>The most common adverse reaction was somnolence. If accidental ingestion of very high doses occurs, symptomatic treatment together with the required supportive measures should be given.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool &amp; dry place below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly. No information is available that indicates the requirement of any dose adjustment in this population.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Neither the safety nor the efficacy of Rupatadine has been established in patients less than 12 years of age.</li><li><strong>Patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency</strong>: Use of Rupatadine is not recommended in patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency. As no relevant clinical data is available.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Alarup 10 mg is a long-acting, non-sedative antagonist of histamine H1-receptors. It also antagonizes the platelet activating factor (PAF). Both histamine and PAF cause bronchoconstriction which leads to an increase in the vascular permeability and act as a mediator in the inflammatory process. With the dual mode of action, Rupatadine shows better therapeutic effect than an isolated antihistamine. Rupatadine possesses other anti-allergic properties such as the inhibition of the degranulation of mast cells induced by immunological and non-immunological stimuli and inhibition of the release of cytokines, particularly of the tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF α) in human mastocytes and monocytes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There is no clinical data available on the exposure of Rupatadine during pregnancy. Pregnant women should therefore not use Rupatadine unless the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk for the infant. No information is available, whether Rupatadine is excreted in the mother\'s milk. Therefore, it should not be used during lactation, unless the potential benefits for the mother justify the potential risk to the infant.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly. No information is available that indicates the requirement of any dose adjustment in this population.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Neither the safety nor the efficacy of Rupatadine has been established in patients less than 12 years of age.</li><li><strong>Patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency</strong>: Use of Rupatadine is not recommended in patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency. As no relevant clinical data is available.</li></ul>', 'active', '1905_1742549332.webp', 17, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:28:52', '2025-05-02 22:52:26'),
(3904, 'Xenocort', 'Clobetasol Propionate', 40.07, 1, 'x 10 gm tube', '10 gm tube', 'Cream', '0.05%', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications:</strong></h3><p>Used for adults, elderly, and children over 1 year for:</p><ul><li><strong>Psoriasis</strong> (excluding widespread plaque psoriasis)</li><li><strong>Recalcitrant dermatoses</strong></li><li><strong>Lichen planus</strong></li><li><strong>Discoid lupus erythematosus</strong></li><li><strong>Other skin conditions resistant to weaker steroids</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></h3><p>A <strong>very potent corticosteroid</strong> with <strong>anti-inflammatory, antipruritic, and vasoconstrictive</strong> effects. It works by reducing immune responses, mast cells, eosinophil activation, and cytokine production.</p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Cream &amp; Ointment:</strong> Apply a thin layer <strong>twice daily</strong> (max <strong>50g/week</strong>). Use for a <strong>maximum of 2 weeks</strong>. In children, limit use to <strong>5 days</strong>.</li><li><strong>Scalp Solution:</strong> Apply <strong>once or twice daily</strong>, max <strong>50ml/week</strong>. Use for <strong>short periods</strong> only.</li><li><strong>Topical Spray:</strong> Apply <strong>twice daily</strong> (<strong>max 52 sprays/day, 50g per week</strong>). Use for <strong>up to 4 weeks</strong> if necessary.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications:</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to components</li><li>Rosacea, acne vulgaris, perioral dermatitis</li><li>Genital &amp; perianal pruritus, pruritus without inflammation</li><li>Untreated skin infections</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects:</strong></h3><ul><li>Common: Burning, stinging, itching</li><li>Less common: Skin atrophy, cracking, <strong>Cushing’s syndrome</strong> (with prolonged use)</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions:</strong></h3><ul><li>Use <strong>cautiously in psoriasis</strong> to avoid rebound flare-ups.</li><li><strong>Avoid eyes</strong> (risk of cataracts, glaucoma).</li><li><strong>Pregnancy &amp; lactation:</strong> Use <strong>only if benefits outweigh risks</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Risks:</strong></h3><ul><li>Chronic overuse can lead to <strong>hypercortisolism (Cushing’s syndrome)</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage:</strong></h3><ul><li>Store <strong>below 30°C</strong>, protected from <strong>light &amp; moisture</strong>.</li><li><strong>Do not freeze</strong>. Keep away from children.</li></ul>', 'active', '450_1742549349.jpg', 43, 12, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:29:09', '2025-05-07 19:57:24'),
(3905, 'Ezygo', 'Sodium Picosulfate', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ezygo 10 mg</strong></p><p> Ezygo 10 mg is indicated in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Constipation of any etiology</li><li>Relief from prolonged &amp; recurrent constipation</li><li>Bowel clearance before surgery, childbirth, or radiological investigations</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Osmotic purgatives</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sodium picosulfate is a stimulant laxative that, in conjunction with magnesium citrate, produces a purgative effect on stools. In a multicenter, observational study comprising patients undergoing colonoscopy, more than 93% of the patients receiving Ezygo 10 mg-containing preparations reported the colon cleansing effect to be effective. Sodium picosulfate is a contact laxative. It inhibits the absorption of water and electrolytes and increases their secretion into the intestinal lumen. It is hydrolyzed by colonic bacterial enzyme, sulfatase, to form an active metabolite bis-(p-hydroxy-phenyl)-pyridyl-2-methane (BHPM), which acts directly on the colonic mucosa to stimulate colonic peristalsis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ezygo 10 mg</strong></p><p> For oral administration. The following dosages are recommended to be taken at night to produce evacuation the following morning. It is recommended to start with the lowest dose. The dose may be adjusted up to the maximum recommended dose to produce regular stools. The maximum recommended daily dose should not be exceeded:</p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children over 10 years of age</strong>Tablet: 5-10 mg per day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oral Solution: 5-10 ml or one to two teaspoonfuls per day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children aged 4-10 years</strong>Tablet: 2.5-5 mg per day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oral Solution: 2.5-5 ml or Half to one teaspoonful per day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children under 4 years of age</strong>Oral Solution: 0.25 ml/kg body weight per day</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ezygo 10 mg</strong></p><p> The concomitant use of diuretics or adreno-corticosteroids may increase the risk of electrolyte imbalance if excessive doses are taken. Concurrent administration of antibiotics may reduce the laxative action of this product.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Ileus or intestinal obstruction</li><li>Severe painful and/or feverish acute abdominal conditions (e.g., appendicitis) potentially associated with nausea and vomiting</li><li>Acute inflammatory bowel diseases</li><li>Severe dehydration</li><li>Known hypersensitivity to Ezygo 10 mg or any other component of the product</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ezygo 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Syncope</li><li>Vasovagal response</li><li>Gastrointestinal disorders</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Abdominal pain and abdominal cramps</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Long experience has shown no evidence of undesirable or damaging effects during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Clinical data show that neither the active moiety of sodium Picosulfate (BHPM or bis-(p-hydroxyphenyl)-pyridyl-2-methane) nor its glucuronides are excreted into the milk of healthy lactating females.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged excessive use may lead to fluid and electrolyte imbalance and hypokalemia.</li><li>Harmful for those suffering from alcoholism.</li><li>To be taken into account in pregnant or breastfeeding women, children, and high-risk groups such as patients with liver disease or epilepsy.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ezygo 10 mg</strong></p><p> Laxatives when taken in chronic overdosage may cause chronic diarrhea, abdominal pain, hypokalemia, secondary hyperaldosteronism, and renal calculi. Renal tubular damage, metabolic alkalosis, and muscle weakness secondary to hypokalemia have also been described in association with chronic laxative abuse.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Osmotic purgatives</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Ezygo 10 mg is a triarylmethane group derivative stimulant laxative. After oral administration, it is activated by the colonic bacteria and acts locally in the colon. The active form then stimulates the nerve endings of the intestinal wall and results in colonic peristalsis with promotion of accumulation of water and electrolytes in the colonic lumen. This results in stimulation of defecation, reduction of transit time, and softening of the stool. Stimulation of the rectum causes increased motility and a feeling of rectal fullness. The rectal effect may help to restore the \"call to stool\".</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> There are no reports of undesirable or damaging effects during pregnancy or to the fetus attributable to the use of this product. Use of the drug should be avoided during the first trimester. Clinical data show that neither the active moiety of sodium Picosulfate (BHPM or bis-(p-hydroxyphenyl)-pyridyl-2-methane) nor its glucuronides are excreted into the milk of healthy lactating females.</p>', 'active', '313_1742549426.jpg', 30, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:30:26', '2025-03-21 14:30:26'),
(3906, 'Atoz Senior', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral [A-Z silver preparation]', 13.00, 9, 'tablet', '9 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0 mg/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a version of your text with the headings in bold, as requested:</p><p><strong>Indications of Atoz Senior</strong></p><p>This is specially formulated for the prevention and treatment of vitamin and mineral deficiencies for adults over 45 years of age. This Silver is also indicated to meet the increased demands of vitamins and minerals for adults over 45 years of age.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Vitamin A 3500 IU</li><li>Vitamin C 60 mg</li><li>Vitamin D 400 IU</li><li>Vitamin E 45 IU</li><li>Vitamin K 10 mcg</li><li>Thiamin 1.5 mg</li><li>Riboflavin 1.7 mg</li><li>Niacin 20 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 3 mg</li><li>Folic acid 400 mcg</li><li>Vitamin B12 25 mcg</li><li>Biotin 30 mcg</li><li>Pantothenic acid 10 mg</li><li>Calcium 200 mg</li><li>Phosphorous 48 mg</li><li>Iodine 150 mcg</li><li>Magnesium 100 mg</li><li>Zinc 15 mg</li><li>Selenium 20 mcg</li><li>Copper 2 mg</li><li>Manganese 2 mg</li><li>Chromium 150 mcg</li><li>Molybdenum 75 mcg</li><li>Chloride 72 mg</li><li>Potassium 80 mg</li><li>Boron 150 mcg</li><li>Nickel 5 mcg</li><li>Silicon 2 mg</li><li>Vanadium 10 mcg</li><li>Lutein 250 mcg</li><li>Lycopene 300 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This preparation is a comprehensive, well-balanced multivitamin and multimineral preparation scientifically adjusted and designed to serve as the complete nutritional program for elderly people. This preparation maintains a healthy body and active lifestyle and ensures proper nutrition for elderly people.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Atoz Senior</strong></p><p>One tablet once daily with food or as indicated by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Atoz Senior</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>This product is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients. Do not take this product if taking other vitamin A supplements.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Atoz Senior</strong></p><p>Generally, this preparation is well tolerated. Allergic sensitization has been reported following oral administration of folic acid. Vitamin C and vitamin E may cause diarrhea and other gastrointestinal disturbances.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Recommended by the consultation with a physician.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Long-term intake of high levels of vitamin A (excluding that sourced from beta-carotene) may increase the risk of osteoporosis in postmenopausal women.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This preparation is a comprehensive, well-balanced multivitamin and multimineral preparation scientifically adjusted and designed to serve as the complete nutritional program for elderly people. This preparation maintains a healthy body and active lifestyle and ensures proper nutrition for elderly people.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Recommended by the consultation with a physician.</p>', 'active', '373_1742549482.webp', 10, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:31:22', '2025-05-03 00:05:44'),
(3907, 'Triject', 'Ceftriaxone Sodium', 250.00, 1, 'Injection', '1 gm vial injection', 'Injection', '1 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Triject 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> Triject 1 gm/vial is indicated for the treatment of the following major infections:</p><ul><li>Lower respiratory tract infections</li><li>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media</li><li>Skin and skin structure infections</li><li>Urinary tract infections</li><li>Gonorrhea</li><li>Bacterial Septicemia</li><li>Bone and joint infections</li><li>Meningitis</li><li>Prevention of postoperative infections</li><li>Perioperative prophylaxis of infections associated with surgery</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Triject 1 gm/vial is a 3rd generation broad-spectrum parenteral cephalosporin antibiotic. It has potent bactericidal activity against a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms. Like other cephalosporins and penicillins, Triject 1 gm/vial kills bacteria by interfering with the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall. Triject 1 gm/vial has a high degree of stability in the presence of beta-lactamases. A remarkable feature of Triject 1 gm/vial is its relatively long plasma elimination half-life of about 6 to 9 hours, which makes single or once-daily dosage of the drug appropriate for most patients. Triject 1 gm/vial is not metabolized in the body. About 40-65% of a dose of Triject 1 gm/vial is excreted unchanged in the urine; the remainder is excreted in the bile and ultimately found in the feces as unchanged drug and microbiologically inactive compound. The drug is highly protein-bound (95%).</p><p><strong>Dosage of Triject 1 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: The usual dose is 1 to 2 gm by intravenous or intramuscular administration once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pneumonia, Bronchitis, Acute bacterial otitis media, Skin and skin structure infection, Urinary tract infections, Bacterial Septicemia, Bone and joint infections, Meningitis: 1 to 2 g IV or IM once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 4 gm/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Uncomplicated gonococcal infections: 250 mg IM as a single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Surgical prophylaxis: 1 g IV as a single dose 30 to 120 minutes before surgery</li><li><strong>Infants and Children (01 month or older)</strong>: The usual dose is 50 to 75 mg/kg intravenous or intramuscular administration once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pneumonia, Bronchitis, Skin and skin structure infection, Urinary tract infections, Bacterial Septicemia, Bone and joint infections: 50 to 75 mg/kg IV or IM once a day (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 2 gm/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute bacterial otitis media: 50 mg/kg IM in a single dose; Maximum dose: 1 gm/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Meningitis: 100 mg/kg IV or IM in a single daily dose or (or in equally divided doses twice a day); Maximum dose: 4 gm/day</li><li><strong>Duration of therapy</strong>: Continue for more than 2 days after signs and symptoms of infection have disappeared. Usual duration is 4 to 14 days; in complicated infections, longer therapy may be required.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Triject 1 gm/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Preparation of Solutions for Intramuscular / Intravenous Injections</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Intramuscular Injection</strong>: 250 mg or 500 mg Triject 1 gm/vial should be dissolved in 2 ml Lidocaine HCI 1% injection or 1 g Triject 1 gm/vial in 3.5 ml of Lidocaine HCI 1% injection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Intravenous Injection</strong>: 250 mg or 500 mg Triject 1 gm/vial should be dissolved in 5 ml of Water for injection or 1 g Triject 1 gm/vial in 10 ml of Water for injection USP or 2 g Triject 1 gm/vial in 20 ml of Water for injection. The injection should be administered over 2-4 minutes, by Intramuscular or Intravenous injection or by tubing infusion over a period of 30 minutes at concentration between 10 mg/mL and 40 mg/mL. Before starting treatment through Triject 1 gm/vial injection, patient tolerance test should be checked by administration of a test dose. (The use of freshly reconstituted solution is recommended. However, it maintains potency for at least 6 hours at room temperature or 24 hours at 5°C).</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Triject 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> No drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Triject 1 gm/vial should not be given to patients with a history of hypersensitivity to cephalosporin antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Triject 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> Triject 1 gm/vial is generally well tolerated. A few side effects such as:</p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal effects including diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, stomatitis, and glossitis</li><li>Cutaneous reactions including rash, pruritus, urticaria, edema, and erythema multiforme</li><li>Hematologic reactions including eosinophilia, thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, anemia, and neutropenia</li><li>Hepatic reactions including elevations of SGOT or SGPT, bilirubinemia</li><li>CNS reactions including nervousness, confusion, sleep disturbances, headache, hyperactivity, convulsion, hypertonia, and dizziness</li><li>Local phlebitis occurs rarely following intravenous administration but can be minimized by slow injections over 2-4 minutes</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Its safety in human pregnancy has not been established. Therefore, it should not be used in pregnancy unless absolutely indicated.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Low concentrations of Triject 1 gm/vial are excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Triject 1 gm/vial is administered to a lactating mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>As with other cephalosporins, anaphylactic shock cannot be ruled out even if a thorough patient history is taken. Anaphylactic shock requires immediate countermeasures such as intravenous epinephrine followed by a glucocorticoid.</li><li>In rare cases, shadows suggesting sludge have been detected by sonograms of the gallbladder. This condition was reversible on discontinuation or completion of Triject 1 gm/vial therapy. Even if such findings are associated with pain, conservative, nonsurgical management is recommended.</li><li>During prolonged treatment, the blood picture should be checked at regular intervals.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Triject 1 gm/vial</strong></p><p> There is no specific antidote. Treatment of overdosage should be symptomatic.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Vial store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> Triject 1 gm/vial must not be given to neonates if the neonates are premature and newborn (up to 28 days of age).</p>', 'active', '1155_1742549577.jpg', 53, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:32:57', '2025-03-21 14:32:57'),
(3908, 'Pansos', 'Pantoprazole Sodium', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a more streamlined version of the information you provided about <strong>Pansos 40 mg</strong>:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications</strong>:</p><p>Pansos 40 mg is used to inhibit acid secretion in conditions like:</p><ul><li>Stomach ulcers</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</li><li>NSAID-induced ulcers</li><li>Helicobacter pylori eradication (with antibiotics)</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><p>Pansos 40 mg is a PPI that inhibits gastric acid secretion by binding to the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme in parietal cells, suppressing both basal and stimulated acid secretion for over 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Oral Dosage</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Benign Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 40 mg daily for 4 weeks, extended if not healed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastro-esophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</strong>: 20-40 mg daily for 4 weeks, continued if necessary; maintenance dose 20 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 40 mg daily for 2 weeks, extended if not healed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal Ulcer with H. pylori</strong>: 40 mg twice daily for one week with antibiotics (Amoxycillin + Clarithromycin or Metronidazole).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>NSAID-associated Ulcers</strong>: 20 mg daily for those on long-term NSAIDs.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: Start at 80 mg daily, adjust based on response.</li><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastric/duodenal ulcers</strong>: 40 mg daily for 7-10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>GERD with erosive esophagitis</strong>: 40 mg daily for 7-10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of rebleeding in peptic ulcer</strong>: IV 80 mg, followed by 8 mg/hour for 72 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: IV 80 mg every 12 hours, adjust as needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong>:</p><p>No significant drug interactions have been reported in clinical studies.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</p><p>Avoid use if allergic to any ingredient in the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</p><p>Generally well tolerated, but possible side effects include:</p><ul><li>Common: Headache, diarrhea</li><li>Rare: Abdominal pain, flatulence, rash, insomnia, hyperglycemia</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B. Use only if clearly necessary, as human studies are lacking.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Excreted in breast milk; decision to continue or stop should be based on the benefit to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong>:</p><ul><li>Do not chew, crush, or split the tablet.</li><li>Prolonged use may cause Vitamin B12 deficiency or increase the risk of osteoporosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong>:</p><p>No known overdose symptoms. Symptomatic and supportive treatment recommended as the drug is not easily dialyzable.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Keep in a dry, cool place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>IV Reconstitution Instructions</strong>:</p><p>For intravenous use only:</p><ol><li>Mix 40 mg vial with 10 mL of 0.9% sodium chloride injection.</li><li>Administer over 2-5 minutes.</li><li>Store reconstituted solution for up to 4 hours at room temperature.</li><li>For infusion, further dilute to 100 mL with 0.9% sodium chloride, 5% dextrose, or Lactated Ringer’s solution, and use within 24 hours.</li></ol><p>This breakdown should make it easier to understand the essential information about Pansos 40 mg. Let me know if you need further details!</p>', 'active', '422_1742549594.webp', 11, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:33:14', '2025-05-04 10:13:18');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3909, 'Xenocort', 'Clobetasol Propionate', 40.27, 1, 'cream', '1 Tube', 'Cream', '10gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xeridon 5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Dyspeptic symptom complex, often linked with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, may include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric sensation of fullness</li><li>Abdominal distension</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Belching</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Early satiety</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms can occur in various conditions, such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine. Additionally, in Parkinson\'s disease, it can manifest as dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting. For radiological studies, it can be used to speed up barium transit in follow-through radiological examinations.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Xeridon 5 mg/5 ml functions as a dopamine receptor antagonist. It exerts gastroprokinetic action by inhibiting dopamine receptors found in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier means it has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xeridon 5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>For adults, the recommended dosage is 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily. Children should take 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily. When used for acute nausea and vomiting, the maximum treatment duration should not exceed 12 weeks. It is advisable to take this medication 15-30 minutes before a meal.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Xeridon 5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Xeridon 5 mg/5 ml should be taken 15-30 minutes before meals, and if needed, before bedtime. The typical recommended oral dosage of Domperidone is as follows:</p><p><strong>For Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li>The maximum daily dose of Domperidone is 80 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg of body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>In cases of dyspeptic symptoms:</strong></p><p><strong>For Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For acute and sub-acute conditions, especially in cases of acute nausea and vomiting:</strong></p><p><strong>For Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>When administered rectally in the form of suppositories:</strong></p><p><strong>For Adults (including elderly):</strong></p><ul><li>30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>The maximum daily rectal dose for children weighing between 10 to 25 kg is 30 mg. The dose may be divided throughout the day if necessary.</li></ul><p>Please note that the maximum treatment period for acute nausea and vomiting is 12 weeks.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Xeridon 5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>The simultaneous use of anticholinergic drugs may counteract the antidyspeptic effects of domperidone. It\'s important to note that antacids and antisecretory drugs should not be administered concurrently with domperidone as they can reduce its oral bioavailability. Domperidone primarily undergoes metabolism through the CYP3A4 enzyme. In vitro data suggest that coadministration with drugs significantly inhibiting this enzyme may lead to elevated plasma levels of domperidone. Examples of CYP3A4 inhibitors include azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, nefazodone, and others.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Xeridon 5 mg/5 ml is not to be used by patients with a known hypersensitivity to this medication or in the case of neonates. It should also be avoided in situations where gastrointestinal stimulation could be unsafe, such as in cases of gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation. Additionally, it is contraindicated in patients with a prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma).</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xeridon 5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Side effects are uncommon, with occasional reports of transient intestinal cramps. Extrapyramidal symptoms are rare among young children and exceptionally rare in adults. These symptoms typically resolve spontaneously and completely upon discontinuing the treatment.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of using Xeridon 5 mg/5 ml during pregnancy has not been established; hence, its use is not recommended during pregnancy. Animal studies have not shown any teratogenic effects on the fetus. Domperidone may induce galactorrhea and enhance post-natal lactation. It is excreted in breast milk in minimal quantities, which are insufficient to be considered harmful.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Xeridon 5 mg/5 ml should be employed with utmost care in children due to the potential for an increased risk of extrapyramidal reactions, which can be more pronounced in young children with an incompletely developed blood-brain barrier.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xeridon 5 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p>Signs of an overdose can manifest as drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions, particularly in children. If an overdose occurs, it is advisable to administer activated charcoal and closely monitor the patient. The use of anticholinergic drugs, antiparkinson drugs, or antihistamines with anticholinergic properties may be beneficial in managing extrapyramidal reactions.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Xeridon 5 mg/5 ml acts as a dopamine antagonist, primarily by blocking dopamine receptors located in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its gastroprokinetic effect is achieved by inhibiting dopamine receptors that influence the motility of the gastrointestinal tract.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Use in infants: Given that the metabolic and blood-brain barrier functions are not fully developed during the first months of life, any medication should be administered to infants with great caution and under close medical supervision.</p><p><strong>Use in liver disorders:</strong> Domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, so it should be employed cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Use in kidney disorders:</strong> In individuals with severe renal insufficiency, the elimination half-life of domperidone increases, although plasma drug levels remain lower than in healthy volunteers. Since very little unchanged drug is excreted through the kidneys, dosage adjustments may be necessary for repeated administration.</p><p><br></p><p>This version has bold headings for clarity while keeping the formatting intact. Let me know if you need any further adjustments!</p>', 'in-active', '973_1742549679.jpg', 43, 12, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:34:39', '2025-05-13 05:51:40'),
(3910, 'MarinCal-D', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 12.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of MarinCal-D</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment &amp; prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, and disorders of osteogenesis. It is also used as a supplement in cases of inadequate intake of calcium in the childhood diet, rickets, pregnancy &amp; lactation, and elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis, phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p>OR</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 200 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate calcium in the blood is vital for a variety of bodily functions. Our internal biochemistry cannot tolerate even short periods of calcium deficiency. Clinical evidence suggests that calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is essential for healthy bones as it aids calcium absorption from the GI tract and stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies show that calcium and vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth as well as in osteoporosis and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of MarinCal-D</strong></p><ul><li>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night, or as directed by the physician.</li><li>One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water, or as directed by the physician.</li><li>Taking it with a full stomach ensures better absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of MarinCal-D</strong></p><ul><li>Oral calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline &amp; fluoride preparations. At least 3 hours should be allowed between ingestion of these medications.</li><li>Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of calcium.</li><li>Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce the metabolism of Vitamin D3.</li><li>Concomitant ingestion of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of calcium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the components</li><li>Hypocalcaemia resulting from overdose of Vitamin D3</li><li>Hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Bone metastases</li><li>Severe renal insufficiency</li><li>Severe hypercalciuria</li><li>Renal calculi, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of MarinCal-D</strong></p><p>Flatulence, diarrhoea, constipation, upper GI discomfort, etc., are rare. Hypercalcaemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It should be used as directed by the physician during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with reduction of dose may be needed.</li><li>Plasma and serum calcium levels should be monitored in mild to moderate renal impairment and also in case of long-term use.</li><li>Patients with renal stones or with a previous history should take precautions.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of MarinCal-D</strong></p><p>At high doses, it may result in nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc. Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature below 30°C, protect from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This is a calcium and vitamin D3 preparation where calcium carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The calcium carbonate from coral has a chemical structure very similar to the composition of human bone. Coral calcium is similar to other sources but ensures better absorption. Vitamin D3 aids in the absorption of calcium from the GI tract and helps maintain calcium balance in the body.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of a physician.</p>', 'active', '1862_1742549711.jpg', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:35:11', '2025-05-03 23:52:10'),
(3911, 'Solone', 'Prednisolone', 1.72, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Solone 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Rheumatic Disorders</strong>: Psoriatic arthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, juvenile rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, acute and subacute bursitis, acute nonspecific tenosynovitis, acute gouty arthritis, post-traumatic osteoarthritis.</li><li><strong>Endocrine Disorders</strong>: Primary or secondary adrenocortical insufficiency, congenital adrenal hyperplasia, nonsuppurative thyroiditis, hypercalcemia associated with cancer.</li><li><strong>Dermatologic Diseases</strong>: Pemphigus, bullous dermatitis herpetiformis, severe erythema multiforme, exfoliative dermatitis, mycosis fungoides, severe psoriasis.</li><li><strong>Allergic States</strong>: Seasonal or perennial allergic rhinitis, bronchial asthma, contact dermatitis, atopic dermatitis, serum sickness, drug hypersensitivity reactions.</li><li><strong>Respiratory Diseases</strong>: Symptomatic sarcoidosis, berylliosis, fulminating, aspiration pneumonitis.</li><li><strong>Hematologic Disorders</strong>: Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura, secondary thrombocytopenia, acquired (autoimmune) hemolytic anemia, erythroblastopenia (RBC anemia).</li><li><strong>Edematous States</strong>: To induce a diuresis or remission of proteinuria in the nephrotic syndrome, without uremia, of the idiopathic type or that due to lupus erythematosus.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal Diseases</strong>: Ulcerative colitis, regional enteritis.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Glucocorticoids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Solone 5 mg decreases inflammation by inhibition of migration of polymorphonuclear leukocytes and reversal of increased capillary permeability. It suppresses the immune system by reducing the activity and production of the lymphocytes and eosinophils.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Solone 5 mg</strong></p><p> The initial dose may vary from 5 mg to 60 mg per day depending on the specific disease. After a favorable response is noted, the proper maintenance dosage should be determined by decreasing the initial drug dosage in small decrements at appropriate time intervals until the lowest dosage which will maintain an adequate clinical response is reached. Constant monitoring is needed in regard to drug dosage. If after long-term therapy the drug is to be stopped, it is recommended that it should be withdrawn gradually rather than abruptly.</p><ul><li><strong>Multiple Sclerosis</strong>: In the treatment of acute exacerbations of multiple sclerosis, daily doses of 200 mg of Solone 5 mg for a week followed by 80 mg every other day for 1 month have been shown to be effective.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Solone 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>AdultNephrotic Syndrome</strong>: Initial: 2 mg/kg/day (maximum 80 mg/day) in divided doses 3 to 4 times/day until urine is protein-free for 3 consecutive days (maximum: 28 days); followed by 1 to 1.5 mg/kg/dose given every other day for 4 weeks. Maintenance dose: 0.5 to 1 mg/kg/dose given every other day for 3 to 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Anti-inflammatory</strong>: 5 to 60 mg per day in divided doses 1 to 4 times/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute Asthma</strong>: 40-60 mg/day PO in a single daily dose or divided q12 hr for 3-10 days.</li><li><strong>Allergic Conditions</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Day 1: 10 mg PO before breakfast, 5 mg after lunch and after dinner, and 10 mg at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Day 2: 5 mg PO before breakfast, after lunch, and after dinner, and 10 mg at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Day 3: 5 mg PO before breakfast, after lunch, after dinner, and at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Day 4: 5 mg PO before breakfast, after lunch, and at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Day 5: 5 mg PO before breakfast and at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Day 6: 5 mg PO before breakfast.</li><li><strong>PediatricAsthma</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">1 year: Acute: 10 mg orally every 12 hours. Maintenance: 10 mg orally every other day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">1 to 4 years: Acute: 20 mg orally every 12 hours. Maintenance: 20 mg orally every other day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">5 to 12 years: Acute: 30 mg orally every 12 hours. Maintenance: 30 mg orally every other day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">12 years: Acute: 40 mg orally every 12 hours. Maintenance: 40 mg orally every other day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Anti-inflammatory</strong>: 0.05 to 2 mg/kg/day divided 1 to 4 times/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Immunosuppression</strong>: 0.05 to 2 mg/kg/day divided 1 to 4 times/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Solone 5 mg</strong></p><p> The efficacy of Solone 5 mg is reduced by Aminoglutethimide, Antacids, Barbiturates, Carbamazepine, Griseofulvin, Mitotane, Phenylbutazone, Phenytoin, Primidone, and Rifampin. Solone 5 mg reduces the amount of potassium in the blood. Digitalis can cause cardiac arrhythmias if hypokalemia occurs. Immunization should be done very carefully.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Systemic infections unless specific anti-infective therapy is employed.</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient.</li><li>Ocular herpes simplex because of possible perforation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Solone 5 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include increased appetite, indigestion, nervousness or restlessness. Less frequent or rare side effects are darkening or lightening of skin color, dizziness or lightheadedness, flushing of face or cheeks, hiccups, increased sweating, and the sensation of spinning.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Since adequate human reproduction studies have not been done with corticosteroids, the use of these drugs in pregnancy, nursing mothers, or women of childbearing potential requires that the possible benefits of the drug be weighed against the potential hazards to the mother and embryo or fetus. Infants born of mothers who have received substantial doses of corticosteroids during pregnancy should be carefully observed for signs of hypoadrenalism. Dietary salt restriction and potassium supplementation may be necessary. All corticosteroids increase calcium excretion.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Precaution has to be taken in diabetes, hypertension, psychological disturbances, osteoporosis, post-menopausal women, pregnancy, and chronic nephritis. Long-term use of Solone 5 mg can cause Cushing\'s habitus, hyperglycemia, muscular weakness, increased susceptibility to infection, delayed wound healing, and psychiatric disturbances.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Solone 5 mg</strong></p><p> Adverse effects related to prednisone normally develop only after prolonged use of doses in excess of the normal physiological requirement. Treatment is symptomatic and, where possible, the prednisone dose should be reduced gradually.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Glucocorticoids</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Solone 5 mg is a synthetic adrenocortical drug with predominantly glucocorticoid properties. It directly inhibits the action of the Phospholipase A2 enzyme, which is responsible for the production of different inflammatory mediators like Leukotrienes, SRS-A, Prostaglandins, etc. Solone 5 mg is rapidly and well absorbed from the GI tract following oral administration. The medicine is 70-90% protein-bound in the plasma, and it is eliminated from the plasma with a half-life of 2 to 4 hours. It is metabolized mainly in the liver and excreted in the urine.</p>', 'active', '777_1742549733.jpg', 44, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:35:33', '2025-03-21 14:35:33'),
(3912, 'Multi Seas', 'Multivitamin + Cod Liver Oil', 100.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '100 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1669_1742549844.webp', 37, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:37:24', '2025-05-04 00:10:53'),
(3913, 'Neugaba', 'Pregabalin', 30.10, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '150mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Neugaba 150 mg</strong></h3><p>Neugaba 150 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Neuropathic pain linked to diabetic peripheral neuropathy (DPN)</strong></li><li><strong>Neuralgia following shingles (PHN)</strong></li><li><strong>Treatment of partial-onset seizures in patients older than one month with adjunctive therapy</strong></li><li><strong>Fibromyalgia</strong></li><li><strong>Neuropathic pain brought on by spinal cord damage</strong></li></ul><p>The following conditions are treated with Neugaba 150 mg CR tablets:</p><ul><li><strong>Neuropathic pain brought on by diabetic peripheral neuropathy (DPN)</strong></li><li><strong>Neuralgia following shingles (PHN)</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p><strong>Supplemental antiepileptic medications</strong></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Gamma-amino butyric acid (GABA), an inhibitory neurotransmitter, is the structural basis of Neugaba 150 mg. It does not directly bind to <strong>GABAA, GABAB,</strong> or <strong>benzodiazepine receptors</strong>. Neugaba 150 mg binds to the <strong>alpha-2-delta site</strong> (an auxiliary subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels) in central nervous system tissues with high affinity.</p><p>Neugaba 150 mg has a <strong>90% oral bioavailability</strong> that is dose-independent. In patients with normal renal function, it is <strong>largely excreted through the kidneys</strong> as an unaltered drug, with a <strong>mean elimination half-life of 6.3 hours</strong>.</p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Neugaba 150 mg</strong></h3><h4><strong>Neuropathic pain associated with diabetic peripheral neuropathy (DPN):</strong></h4><ul><li>Maximum recommended dose: <strong>100 mg three times a day (300 mg/day)</strong> in patients with <strong>creatinine clearance of at least 60 mL/min</strong>.</li><li><strong>Starting dose:</strong> 50 mg <strong>three times a day (150 mg/day)</strong>.</li><li>Dose may be <strong>increased to 300 mg/day</strong> within <strong>1 week</strong>, based on efficacy and tolerability.</li></ul><h4><strong>Post-herpetic neuralgia (PHN):</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Recommended dose:</strong> 75 to 150 mg <strong>twice a day</strong>, or <strong>50 to 100 mg three times a day (150 to 300 mg/day)</strong>.</li><li><strong>Starting dose:</strong> 75 mg <strong>twice a day</strong>, or <strong>50 mg three times a day (150 mg/day)</strong>.</li><li>Dose may be <strong>increased to 300 mg/day</strong> within <strong>1 week</strong> based on efficacy and tolerability.</li></ul><h4><strong>Adjunctive therapy for adult patients with partial-onset seizures:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Starting dose:</strong> 75 mg <strong>twice a day</strong>, or <strong>50 mg three times a day (150 mg/day)</strong>.</li><li>Dose may be <strong>increased to a maximum of 600 mg/day</strong> based on individual patient response and tolerability.</li></ul><h4><strong>Management of Fibromyalgia:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Recommended dose:</strong> <strong>300 to 450 mg/day</strong>.</li><li><strong>Starting dose:</strong> 75 mg <strong>twice a day (150 mg/day)</strong>.</li><li>Dose may be <strong>increased to 150 mg twice a day (300 mg/day) within 1 week</strong> based on efficacy and tolerability.</li></ul><h4><strong>Neuropathic pain associated with spinal cord injury:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Recommended dose:</strong> <strong>150 to 600 mg/day</strong>.</li><li><strong>Starting dose:</strong> 75 mg <strong>twice a day (150 mg/day)</strong>.</li><li>Dose may be <strong>increased to 150 mg twice a day (300 mg/day) within 1 week</strong> based on efficacy and tolerability.</li></ul><h3><strong>Administration of Neugaba 150 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Route:</strong> Oral</li><li>Can be taken <strong>with or without food</strong>.</li><li><strong>Neugaba 150 mg CR tablets should be taken after dinner</strong> and must be swallowed <strong>whole (not crushed, chewed, or divided).</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction of Neugaba 150 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong> There are <strong>no significant pharmacokinetic drug interactions</strong> expected with Neugaba 150 mg.</li><li><strong>Food Interactions:</strong> No known drug-food interactions.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity:</strong> Should not be taken by individuals with a <strong>known hypersensitivity</strong> to Neugaba 150 mg or its ingredients.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Neugaba 150 mg</strong></h3><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Common side effects in adults:Dizziness</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Somnolence (drowsiness)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dry mouth</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Edema (swelling)</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Impaired vision</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Weight gain</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Difficulty with concentration/attention</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Common side effects in children:Increased weight</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Increased hunger</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: Should <strong>only be used if the benefits outweigh the potential risks</strong> to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong> Not recommended as <strong>Neugaba 150 mg may be excreted in breast milk</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Angioedema (swelling of throat, head, or neck)</strong> can occur, potentially leading to <strong>breathing difficulties requiring immediate medical attention</strong>.</li><li><strong>Suicidal thoughts or behaviors</strong> may increase with Neugaba 150 mg and other antiepileptic drugs.</li><li><strong>Respiratory depression risk</strong> increases when taken with <strong>CNS depressants</strong> or in patients with <strong>underlying respiratory impairment</strong>.</li><li><strong>May cause drowsiness and dizziness</strong>, which can <strong>impair driving or machinery operation</strong>.</li><li><strong>Should not be stopped abruptly</strong>; taper off over <strong>at least two weeks</strong> to prevent withdrawal effects.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Neugaba 150 mg</strong></h3><p><strong>Symptoms of overdose:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Reduced consciousness</strong></li><li><strong>Depression or anxiety</strong></li><li><strong>Confusion and agitation</strong></li><li><strong>Heart block</strong></li><li><strong>Seizures</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Management:</strong></p><ul><li>There is <strong>no specific antidote</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment includes:Inducing vomiting or stomach lavage</strong> (if needed)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Supportive care</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Monitoring vital signs</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool, dry place (below 30°C)</strong>, away from <strong>light and moisture</strong>.</li><li><strong>Keep out of reach of children</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric Use:Safety and efficacy not established</strong> for treating fibromyalgia, PHN, DPN, or spinal cord injury pain in children.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Not proven safe</strong> for pediatric patients under <strong>one month</strong> for partial-onset seizures.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Neugaba 150 mg CR tablet safety in children is not established</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</strong></li><li><strong>Primary anti-epileptic drugs</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li>Structurally similar to <strong>Gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA)</strong> but <strong>does not bind directly to GABAA, GABAB, or benzodiazepine receptors</strong>.</li><li>Binds to <strong>alpha2-delta subunit</strong> of voltage-gated calcium channels in the CNS, which may contribute to its <strong>anti-seizure and pain-relief properties</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy Considerations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>No adequate and well-controlled studies</strong> in pregnant women.</li><li><strong>Detected in breast milk</strong>, so <strong>breastfeeding is not recommended</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Not established for treatment</strong> of <strong>fibromyalgia, PHN, DPN, spinal cord injury pain, or partial seizures in infants under 1 month</strong>.</li><li><strong>Neugaba 150 mg CR tablet safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been shown</strong>.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '531_1742549920.webp', 16, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:38:40', '2025-03-21 14:38:40'),
(3914, 'Neparact TS', 'Nepafenac', 145.00, 1, 'drop', '3 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Suspension', '0.3 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> <strong>Neparact TS 0.1% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension:</strong> The treatment of post-operative ocular pain and inflammation including cataract surgery, inhibition of surgery-induced miosis, and prevention of post-operative cystoid macular edema (CME).</p><p><strong>Neparact TS 0.3% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension:</strong> Post-operative pain and inflammation associated with cataract surgery.</p><p> <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Nepafenac is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory prodrug (NSAID). After instillation in the eye, it penetrates the cornea and is converted by ocular tissue hydrolase to Amfenac, a potent nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug. Amfenac is thought to inhibit the action of the cyclooxygenase enzyme. This enzyme is required for prostaglandin synthesis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p> <strong>Nepafenac 0.1% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For post-operative pain &amp; inflammation:</strong> Instill 1 drop 3 times daily 1 day prior to cataract surgery and continue on the day of surgery and through the first 2 weeks of the post-operative period.</li><li><strong>For surgery-induced miosis:</strong> Instill 1 drop 3 times daily 1 day before surgery &amp; on the day of surgery.</li><li><strong>For prevention of post-operative CME:</strong> Instill 1 drop 3 times daily 1 day before surgery and continue on the day of surgery and through the first 6 weeks of the post-operative period.</li></ul><p><strong>Nepafenac 0.3% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For post-operative pain &amp; inflammation:</strong> Instill 1 drop once daily 1 day prior to cataract surgery and continue on the day of surgery and through the first 2 weeks of the post-operative period. An additional drop should be administered 30 to 120 minutes prior to surgery.</li><li> <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Contraindicated in patients with previously demonstrated hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients in the formulation or to other NSAIDs.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Reported side effects are foreign body sensation, lid margin crusting, ocular discomfort, ocular hyperemia, etc.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in pregnancy:</strong> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Nepafenac should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Use in lactation:</strong> It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Nepafenac ophthalmic suspension is administered to a nursing mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Neparact TS should be used with caution in patients with known bleeding tendencies or who are receiving medications that may prolong bleeding time.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients below 18 years of age have not been established.</li><li><strong>Use in elderly patients:</strong> No overall differences in safety or effectiveness have been observed between elderly and younger patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic Non-Steroid drugs</p>', 'active', '26_1742550201.jpg', 62, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:43:21', '2025-03-21 14:43:21'),
(3915, 'Thiosina', 'Thiamine Hydrochloride', 0.84, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Thiosina is specifically used in the treatment of various manifestations of thiamine deficiency such as Beriberi and Wernicke\'s encephalopathy, neuritis associated with pregnancy, and pellagra. Supplementary Thiosina may be indicated prophylactically in conditions where there is low dietary intake or impaired gastrointestinal absorption of thiamine (e.g., alcohol consumption) or where requirements are increased (pregnancy, carbohydrate-rich diet).</p><p> <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) is an essential component for the metabolism of carbohydrates and several amino acids. In thiamine deficiency, the utilization of pyruvic acid and amino acids in tissues decreases, leading to the accumulation of pyruvic acid and lactic acid, which causes Beriberi. In adults, approximately 1 mg of thiamine per day is completely degraded by the tissues, which represents the minimal daily requirement. Thiamine is well absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and is widely distributed in body tissues. Excess amounts beyond the body\'s requirements are excreted in the urine, either unchanged or as metabolites.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li>In the treatment of mild chronic thiamine deficiency, doses up to <strong>30 mg daily</strong> in single or divided doses are recommended.</li><li>In severe thiamine deficiency, doses up to <strong>300 mg daily</strong> may be given.</li><li> <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> Thiosina has no known severe interactions with other drugs.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to thiamine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> No adverse effects occur when Thiosina is administered orally, even in amounts significantly higher than the therapeutic dose.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The drug may be safely administered to neonates, children, pregnant and lactating women, and elderly patients.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> A very serious allergic reaction to this vitamin is rare. However, seek immediate medical attention if you experience symptoms of a serious allergic reaction, including rash, itching/swelling (especially of the face, tongue, or throat), severe dizziness, or difficulty breathing.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p> Thiosina overdose does not cause toxicity.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Vitamin-B preparations</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1519_1742550411.jpg', 30, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 14:46:51', '2025-03-21 14:46:51'),
(3916, 'Tone', 'Thiamine Hydrochloride', 0.73, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p>Tone is specifically used in the treatment of various manifestations of thiamine deficiency, such as <strong>Beriberi</strong> and <strong>Wernicke\'s encephalopathy</strong>, neuritis associated with pregnancy, and pellagra. Supplementary Tone may be indicated prophylactically in conditions where there is <strong>low dietary intake</strong> or <strong>impaired gastrointestinal absorption</strong> of thiamine (e.g., alcohol consumption) or where requirements are increased (pregnancy, carbohydrate-rich diet).</p><p> <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Thiamine Hydrochloride (<strong>Vitamin B1</strong>) is an essential component for the metabolism of <strong>carbohydrates</strong> and several <strong>amino acids</strong>. In thiamine deficiency, the utilization of <strong>pyruvic acid</strong> and <strong>amino acids</strong> in tissues decreases, leading to the accumulation of <strong>pyruvic acid and lactic acid</strong>, which causes <strong>Beriberi</strong>. In adults, approximately <strong>1 mg of thiamine per day</strong> is completely degraded by the tissues, which represents the minimal daily requirement. Thiamine is well absorbed from the <strong>gastrointestinal tract</strong> and is widely distributed in body tissues. Excess amounts beyond the body\'s requirements are <strong>excreted in the urine</strong>, either unchanged or as metabolites.</p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li>For mild chronic thiamine deficiency: <strong>Up to 30 mg daily</strong>, in single or divided doses.</li><li>For severe thiamine deficiency: <strong>Up to 300 mg daily</strong> may be given.</li><li> <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction</strong></h3><p>Tone has <strong>no known severe interactions</strong> with other drugs.</p><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity to thiamine.</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>No adverse effects occur when Tone is administered orally, even in amounts significantly higher than the therapeutic dose.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>The drug may be safely administered to <strong>neonates, children, pregnant and lactating women,</strong> and <strong>elderly patients</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><p>A very serious allergic reaction to this vitamin is <strong>rare</strong>. However, seek <strong>immediate medical attention</strong> if you experience any of the following symptoms:</p><ul><li>Rash</li><li>Itching/swelling (especially of the <strong>face, tongue, or throat</strong>)</li><li>Severe dizziness</li><li>Difficulty breathing</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Tone overdose <strong>does not cause toxicity</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Vitamin-B preparations</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Keep below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from <strong>light &amp; moisture</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1365_1742551303.jpg', 43, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 15:01:43', '2025-03-21 15:01:43'),
(3917, 'Lizid', 'Linezolid', 85.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Lizid is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Nosocomial pneumonia</li><li>Community-acquired pneumonia</li><li>Complicated skin and skin structure infection including diabetic foot infections, without concomitant osteomyelitis</li><li>Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infections</li><li>Vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus faecium infections</li></ul><p><strong>Limitations of use</strong>: Lizid is not indicated for the treatment of Gram-negative infections. The safety and efficacy of Lizid given for longer than 28 days have not been evaluated in controlled clinical trials.</p><p> <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Linezolid is a synthetic, antibacterial agent belonging to a new class of antibiotics, the oxazolidinones, with in vitro activity against Gram-positive aerobic bacteria, some Gram-positive anaerobic bacteria, and certain Gram-negative bacteria. It selectively inhibits bacterial protein synthesis via a mechanism of action different from that of other antibacterial agents. Linezolid binds to the 23S ribosomal RNA of the 50S subunit of the bacterial ribosome and prevents the formation of a functional 70S initiation complex, which is an essential component of the bacterial translation process. The results of time-kill studies have shown Linezolid to be bacteriostatic against enterococci and staphylococci. For streptococci, Linezolid was found to be bactericidal for the majority of strains.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p> Linezolid tablets should be taken orally with or without food. Linezolid IV infusion should be administered by intravenous infusion over a period of 30 to 120 minutes. The recommended daily dosage for Linezolid tablets and IV infusion is described in the following table:</p><p><strong>InfectionPediatric patients</strong> (birth through 11 years of age)<strong>Adults and adolescents</strong> (12 years and older)<strong>Recommended duration of treatment</strong> (consecutive days)Nosocomial pneumonia10 mg/kg orally or intravenously every 8 hours600 mg orally or intravenously every 12 hours10 to 14Community-acquired pneumonia, including concurrent bacteremia10 mg/kg orally or intravenously every 8 hours600 mg orally or intravenously every 12 hours14 to 28Complicated skin and skin structure infections10 mg/kg orally or intravenously every 8 hours600 mg orally or intravenously every 12 hours14 to 28Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infectionsless than 5 years: 10 mg/kg orally every 8 hoursAdults: 400 mg orally every 12 hours10 to 14</p><p><em>Neonates less than 7 days</em>: Most pre-term neonates less than 7 days of age (gestational age less than 34 weeks) have lower systemic linezolid clearance values and larger AUC values than many full-term neonates and older infants. These neonates should be initiated with a dosing regimen of 10 mg/kg every 12 hours. Consideration may be given to the use of 10 mg/kg every 8 hours regimen in neonates with a sub-optimal clinical response. All neonatal patients should receive 10 mg/kg every 8 hours by 7 days of life.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Drug interaction with medication</strong>: Lizid is a reversible, nonselective inhibitor of monoamine oxidase. Therefore, Lizid has the potential for interaction with adrenergic and serotonergic agents. Some individuals receiving Lizid may experience a reversible enhancement of the pressor response to indirect acting sympathomimetic agents, vasopressor or dopaminergic agents. Unless patients are carefully observed for signs and/or symptoms of serotonin syndrome, Lizid should not be administered to patients with carcinoid syndrome and/or patients taking serotonin re-uptake inhibitors, tricyclic antidepressants, serotonin 5-HT1 receptor agonists (triptans), meperidine or buspirone.</li><li><strong>Drug interaction with food and others</strong>: Advise patients to avoid large quantities of foods or beverages with high tyramine content while taking Lizid.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Linezolid formulations are contraindicated for use in patients who have known hypersensitivity to Linezolid or any of the other product components. Linezolid should not be used in patients taking any medicinal product which inhibits monoamine oxidases A or B (e.g. Phenelzine, Isocarboxazid) or within two weeks of taking any such medicinal product. Linezolid should not be administered to patients with uncontrolled hypertension, pheochromocytoma, thyrotoxicosis, carcinoid syndrome, and/or patients taking directly and indirectly acting sympathomimetic agents (e.g. Pseudoephedrine), vasopressive agents (e.g. Epinephrine, Norepinephrine), dopaminergic agents (e.g. Dopamine, Dobutamine), serotonin re-uptake inhibitors, tricyclic antidepressants, serotonin 5-HT1 receptor agonists (triptans), meperidine or buspirone.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> Most of the adverse events reported with Lizid were mild to moderate in intensity. The most common adverse events in patients treated with Lizid were diarrhea, headache, and nausea. Other adverse events include oral moniliasis, vaginal moniliasis, hypertension, dyspepsia, localized abdominal pain, pruritus, and tongue discoloration.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Available data with linezolid use in pregnant women have not identified a drug-associated risk of major birth defects, miscarriage, or adverse maternal or fetal outcomes. Linezolid should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Linezolid is present in breast milk. The developmental and health benefits of breastfeeding should be considered along with the mother’s clinical need for linezolid and any potential adverse effects on the breastfed child from linezolid or from the underlying maternal condition. Advise lactating women to monitor a breastfed infant for diarrhea and vomiting.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Complete blood counts should be monitored weekly in patients who receive Lizid, particularly in those who receive Lizid for longer than two weeks, those with pre-existing myelosuppression, those receiving concomitant drugs that produce bone marrow suppression, or those with a chronic infection who have received previous or concomitant antibiotic therapy. If patients experience symptoms of visual impairment, such as changes in visual acuity, changes in color vision, blurred vision, or visual field defect, prompt ophthalmic evaluation is recommended. Lizid is not approved and should not be used for the treatment of patients with catheter-related bloodstream infections or catheter-site infections.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p> No cases of overdose have been reported. Symptomatic and supportive care is advised together with maintenance of glomerular filtration. Approximately 30% of a Lizid dose is removed during 3 hours of haemodialysis. No data are available for the removal of Lizid by peritoneal dialysis or haemoperfusion.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Macrolides</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p> <strong>Reconstitution of Oral Suspension</strong>: Shake the bottle to loosen powder. Add 75 ml (with the help of the given cup) of boiled and cooled water to the dry mixture in the bottle. For the ease of preparation, add water to the bottle in two portions. Shake well after each addition until all the powder is in suspension.</p><p><strong>Note</strong>: Shake the suspension well before each use. Keep the bottle tightly closed. The reconstituted suspension should be stored in a cool and dry place. Use within 21 days after constitution.</p><p><strong>Intravenous Administration</strong>: Lizid IV Injection is supplied in single-use, ready-to-use infusion bottles. Lizid IV Injection should be administered by intravenous infusion over a period of 30 to 120 minutes. The intravenous infusion bottles should not be used in series connections. Additives should not be introduced into this solution. The infusion bottles should be stored at room temperature and protected from freezing. Lizid IV Injection may exhibit a yellow color that can intensify over time without adversely affecting potency.</p>', 'active', '1095_1742551489.webp', 10, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 15:04:49', '2025-03-21 15:04:49');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3918, 'Covirin', 'Ribavirin', 33.00, 30, 'Capsule', '30 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '200mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><p> Covirin, when combined with Peginterferon alfa-2a or Interferon alfa, is indicated for the treatment of patients aged 5 years and older with chronic hepatitis C (CHC) virus infection who have compensated liver disease and have not previously been treated with interferon alpha. Covirin alone is not effective and should not be used as monotherapy.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন.</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p> Ribavirin is a synthetic nucleoside with inhibitory effects against respiratory syncytial virus, influenza virus, and herpes simplex virus. Although the exact mechanism of action is unclear, it may interfere with viral nucleic acid synthesis through several pathways, including cellular enzymes. The mono- and triphosphate derivatives are responsible for its antiviral activity.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><p> For patients previously untreated with Interferon/Peginterferon, the recommended treatment duration is 24 to 48 weeks. The daily dose of Ribavirin is 800 mg to 1200 mg, administered orally in two divided doses (morning and evening). For Genotypes 2 and 3, the response to treatment does not increase beyond 24 weeks.</p><ul><li><strong>Special dosage instructions:</strong> If severe adverse reactions or laboratory abnormalities develop during therapy, dose adjustments are necessary. Ribavirin is associated with hemolysis, and additional guidelines exist for patients with cardiovascular disease.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> For patients with creatinine clearance ≤ 50 ml/min, reduce the total daily dose of Ribavirin.</li><li><strong>Elderly patients (65+ years):</strong> No sufficient clinical data are available on the safety and effectiveness of Ribavirin in combination with interferon alfa or pegylated interferon alfa in this age group.</li><li><strong>Children under 5 years:</strong> Safety and efficacy have not been established.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন.</em></p><p><strong>Interaction:</strong></p><p> Covirin should not be used with:</p><ul><li>Didanosine</li><li>Zidovudine</li><li>Azathioprine</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p> Covirin is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to Ribavirin or any excipients, women who are pregnant, men whose female partners are pregnant, patients with hemoglobinopathies (e.g., thalassemia major, sickle cell anemia), autoimmune hepatitis, or hepatic decompensation during treatment.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common adverse reactions:</strong> Haemolytic anaemia, cough, anxiety, depression, dizziness, nausea, vomiting, flu-like symptoms, headache, abdominal pain, diarrhea, constipation, chest pain, tachycardia, dyspnoea, neutropenia, thrombocytopenia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Ribavirin is teratogenic and should not be used by pregnant women or those planning to conceive. Male patients and their female partners must use two forms of contraception during treatment and for 6 months post-treatment. A negative pregnancy test is required before starting therapy, and monthly pregnancy tests should be performed during and for 6 months after treatment.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong> It is unknown whether Ribavirin is excreted in human milk. Due to potential adverse effects on infants, a decision should be made whether to discontinue breastfeeding or not start therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Covirin should only be used under the supervision of a qualified physician experienced in hepatitis C treatment. It may cause moderate to severe adverse reactions, requiring dose adjustments, cessation, or discontinuation.</li><li><strong>Cardiac concerns:</strong> Due to potential worsening of cardiac disease from Covirin-induced anemia, patients should undergo cardiovascular assessments before therapy. ECG should be considered for patients with pre-existing heart conditions.</li><li><strong>Regular testing:</strong> Complete blood counts, liver function tests, and other relevant tests should be conducted before starting therapy and monitored during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p> Hepatic viral infections (Hepatitis C)</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><p> Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C, in a dry place, and protect from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1960_1742551762.jpg', 67, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 15:09:22', '2025-03-21 15:09:22'),
(3919, 'Viracin', 'Ribavirin', 35.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '200mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong></p><p> Viracin, in combination with Peginterferon alfa-2a or Interferon alfa, is indicated for the treatment of patients aged 5 years and older with chronic hepatitis C (CHC) virus infection who have compensated liver disease and have not previously been treated with interferon alpha. Viracin as monotherapy is not effective and should not be used alone.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন.</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p> Ribavirin is a synthetic nucleoside with inhibitory actions against respiratory syncytial virus, influenza virus, and herpes simplex virus. Its exact mechanism of action is not fully understood but is believed to act at several sites, including cellular enzymes, to interfere with viral nucleic acid synthesis. The mono- and triphosphate derivatives are responsible for the antiviral activity.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><p> For patients previously untreated with Interferon/Peginterferon, the recommended treatment duration is 24 to 48 weeks. The daily dose of Ribavirin is 800 mg to 1200 mg, administered orally in two divided doses (morning and evening). Genotypes 2 and 3 show no increased response to treatment beyond 24 weeks.</p><ul><li><strong>Special dosage instructions:</strong> If severe adverse reactions or laboratory abnormalities occur, dosage modifications are required until adverse reactions subside. Due to the hemolysis associated with Ribavirin therapy, special guidelines are available for patients with cardiovascular disease.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> For patients with creatinine clearance ≤ 50 ml/min, the total daily dose of Ribavirin should be reduced.</li><li><strong>Elderly patients (65+ years):</strong> No sufficient clinical data are available regarding the safety and efficacy of Ribavirin in combination with interferon alfa or pegylated interferon alfa for elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Children under 5 years:</strong> The safety and efficacy of Ribavirin in combination with interferon alfa or pegylated interferon alfa have not been evaluated.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন.</em></p><p><strong>Interaction:</strong></p><p> Viracin should not be used with the following medications:</p><ul><li>Didanosine</li><li>Zidovudine</li><li>Azathioprine</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p> Viracin is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to Ribavirin or any excipients, women who are pregnant, men whose female partners are pregnant, patients with hemoglobinopathies (e.g., thalassemia major, sickle cell anemia), autoimmune hepatitis, or hepatic decompensation before or during treatment.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p> The primary adverse reaction of Viracin is hemolytic anemia. Other adverse reactions that may occur with combination therapy with interferon alfa or pegylated interferon alfa include:</p><ul><li>Cough, anxiety, depression, dizziness, nausea, vomiting, flu-like symptoms (fever, chills, sore throat, muscle aches), headache, abdominal pain, diarrhea, constipation, chest pain, tachycardia, dyspnea, neutropenia, thrombocytopenia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Ribavirin is teratogenic and must not be used by pregnant women or those planning to conceive. Male patients and their female partners of childbearing age must use two forms of contraception during treatment and for 6 months after treatment ends. Ribavirin should not be initiated until a negative pregnancy test is obtained before starting therapy. Monthly pregnancy tests are required during therapy and for 6 months after treatment.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong> It is unknown whether Ribavirin is excreted in human milk. Due to the potential risk for adverse reactions in nursing infants, a decision should be made whether to discontinue breastfeeding or avoid starting therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Viracin used in combination therapy should be administered under the supervision of a qualified physician experienced in hepatitis C treatment. It can cause moderate to severe adverse reactions requiring dose reductions, temporary cessation, or discontinuation.</li><li><strong>Cardiac concerns:</strong> Due to the risk of worsening cardiac disease from Viracin-induced anemia, patients should be evaluated for cardiovascular health before initiating therapy. ECG is recommended for patients with pre-existing heart conditions.</li><li><strong>Regular monitoring:</strong> Prior to starting therapy, patients should have a complete blood count (CBC), liver function tests, and other relevant tests. After starting therapy, hematological tests should be done at 2 and 4 weeks, and biochemical tests at 4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p> Hepatic viral infections (Hepatitis C)</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><p> Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C in a dry place, away from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><br></p><p>4o mini</p>', 'active', '1970_1742551989.webp', 3, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 15:13:09', '2025-03-21 15:13:09'),
(3920, 'Candinil', 'Fluconazole', 8.00, 6, 'Capsule', '6capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications:</strong> Candinil is indicated for the treatment of the following conditions:</p><ul><li><strong>Vaginal Candidiasis</strong></li><li><strong>Oropharyngeal Candidiasis</strong></li><li><strong>Esophageal Candidiasis</strong></li><li><strong>Tinea infections</strong> (Tinea corporis, Tinea cruris, Tinea pedis, Other Tinea)</li><li><strong>Kerion</strong></li><li><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong></li><li><strong>Onychomycosis</strong></li><li><strong>Invasive candidal infections and cryptococcal infections</strong> (including meningitis)</li><li><strong>Prevention of cryptococcal meningitis</strong></li><li><strong>Prevention of fungal infections in immunocompromised patients</strong></li><li><strong>Systemic Candidiasis and Cryptococcal infections</strong></li><li><strong>Superficial Candidiasis</strong></li><li><strong>Other indications</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fungal urinary tract infections</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Disseminated candidiasis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prophylaxis for fungal infection in neutropenic cancer patients</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acute treatment of systemic fungal infections (e.g., coccidioidomycosis, histoplasmosis)</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন.</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p> Fluconazole is a triazole antifungal agent that inhibits fungal cytochrome P-450-dependent enzymes, which are involved in the synthesis of ergosterol, a critical component of the fungal cell membrane.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Vaginal Candidiasis</strong>: 150 mg as a single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Oropharyngeal Candidiasis</strong>: 200 mg on Day 1, followed by 100 mg daily for 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Esophageal Candidiasis</strong>: 200 mg on Day 1, followed by 100 mg daily for 14-30 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea infections</strong>: 150 mg weekly for 4-6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Kerion</strong>: 50 mg daily for 20 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong>: 400 mg as a single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Onychomycosis</strong>: 150 mg weekly for 12 months</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Invasive candidal infections &amp; cryptococcal infections</strong>: 400 mg on Day 1, followed by 200-400 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of cryptococcal meningitis</strong>: 200 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of fungal infections in immunocompromised patients</strong>: 50-400 mg daily</li><li><strong>Children (over 1 year):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Superficial Candidiasis</strong>: 1-2 mg/kg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Systemic Candidiasis &amp; Cryptococcal infection</strong>: 3-6 mg/kg daily (up to 12 mg/kg daily in serious life-threatening infections)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Specific doses for children based on weight are provided in the instructions (e.g., for a child weighing 9 kg, ½ measuring spoonful, and so on).</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Specific Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> The normal dose should be used if there is no renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Adjustments in dosage intervals are required for patients with renal impairment. For example, for those with creatinine clearance &lt;40 ml/min, the dosing schedule needs to be modified (see detailed dosage instructions).</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন.</em></p><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>: Fluconazole can increase prothrombin time when taken with warfarin.</li><li><strong>Sulphonylureas</strong>: Fluconazole may increase the serum half-life of sulphonylureas like chlorpropamide and glibenclamide, potentially leading to hypoglycemia.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: This diuretic may increase plasma concentrations of fluconazole by 40%, but no dosage change is usually necessary.</li><li><strong>Phenytoin &amp; Rifampicin</strong>: Fluconazole can increase phenytoin levels. Rifampicin reduces fluconazole’s efficacy, and a dosage increase might be required for fluconazole.</li><li><strong>Oral Contraceptives</strong>: Fluconazole at 50 mg/day has no effect on the efficacy of oral contraceptives, but higher doses may slightly increase levels of ethinyl estradiol and levonorgestrel.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin</strong>: Fluconazole may increase cyclosporine concentrations in renal transplant patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Fluconazole is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to fluconazole or related triazole compounds.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Nausea, abdominal discomfort, diarrhea, and flatulence.</li><li>Rare: Rash, anaphylaxis, and exfoliative skin reactions (e.g., Stevens-Johnson Syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Fluconazole should not be used during pregnancy or by women of childbearing potential unless adequate contraception is employed. Fetal effects have been observed in animals at doses much higher than therapeutic doses.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Fluconazole is excreted in human milk, and its use in breastfeeding mothers is not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with severe underlying diseases (e.g., AIDS, cancer) may experience liver, renal, or hematologic abnormalities during treatment.</li><li><strong>Liver toxicity:</strong> Rare cases of hepatic necrosis have been reported, particularly in patients with severe underlying diseases. Monitoring of liver enzymes is recommended.</li><li><strong>Skin reactions:</strong> Discontinue therapy if a rash develops, especially if it progresses to severe reactions.</li><li><strong>Use during lactation:</strong> Due to the presence of fluconazole in breast milk, its use in nursing mothers is not advised.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Use the normal dose unless renal impairment is present.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Dosing adjustments are necessary for patients with renal impairment as described above.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>In case of overdose, supportive treatment and symptomatic measures such as gastric lavage may be appropriate. Hemodialysis can reduce plasma levels by approximately 50%.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Antifungal agents for systemic and subcutaneous mycoses.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p><p>4o mini</p>', 'active', '275_1742552537.webp', 1, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 15:22:17', '2025-03-21 15:22:17'),
(3921, 'Flucostan', 'Fluconazole', 5.00, 4, 'Capsule', '4 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Vaginal, oropharyngeal, and esophageal candidiasis</li><li>Tinea (body, groin, foot) and other fungal infections like Kerion, pityriasis versicolor, and onychomycosis</li><li>Invasive candidal infections and cryptococcal infections (including meningitis)</li><li>Prevention of cryptococcal meningitis, systemic fungal infections, and fungal infections in immunocompromised patients</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Fluconazole is a triazole antifungal agent that inhibits fungal cytochrome P-450 dependent enzymes, which are essential for the synthesis of ergosterol, a key component of the fungal cell membrane.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vaginal Candidiasis: 150 mg (single dose)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oropharyngeal Candidiasis: 200 mg on day 1, followed by 100 mg for 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Esophageal Candidiasis: Similar to oropharyngeal but may continue for 14-30 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tinea and other infections: Varies from 50 mg to 400 mg based on infection type</li><li><strong>Children (over 1 year)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adjusted based on weight and infection severity (1-2 mg/kg for superficial infections, up to 12 mg/kg for life-threatening infections)</li></ul><h3><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Creatinine Clearance</strong>: Adjust dosage intervals based on kidney function (normal, moderate, or severe impairment).</li><li><strong>Dialysis</strong>: Dosage after each session.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Increased prothrombin time with warfarin, prolonged effects with sulfonylureas (e.g., hypoglycemia risk in diabetics), and changes in phenytoin and rifampicin concentrations.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to fluconazole or other azoles.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Common: Gastrointestinal issues (nausea, diarrhea)</li><li>Rare: Anaphylaxis, liver damage, rash (especially in immunocompromised patients)</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Monitor liver enzymes, especially for patients with underlying diseases like cancer or AIDS.</li><li>Rare cases of severe cutaneous reactions like Stevens-Johnson syndrome.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Not recommended unless benefits outweigh risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Excreted in breast milk; not recommended for nursing mothers.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '963_1742552733.jpg', 27, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 15:25:33', '2025-03-21 15:25:33'),
(3922, 'Roin', 'Ceftibuten Dihydrate', 120.00, 5, 'Tablet', '5 Tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Roin 400 mg</strong></p><p> Ceftibuten is indicated for the treatment of individuals with mild-to-moderate infections caused by susceptible strains of the designated microorganisms in the specific conditions listed below:</p><ul><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Exacerbations of Chronic Bronchitis</strong>: Due to <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including β-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (including β-lactamase-producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> (penicillin-susceptible strains only).</li><li><strong>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media</strong>: Due to <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including β-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (including β-lactamase-producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Pharyngitis and Tonsillitis</strong>: Due to <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Third-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> As with most beta-lactam antibiotics, the bactericidal activity of ceftibuten results from the inhibition of bacterial cell wall synthesis. Due to its chemical structure, ceftibuten is highly stable to beta-lactamases. Many beta-lactamase-producing microorganisms, which are resistant to penicillins or other cephalosporins, may be inhibited by ceftibuten.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Roin 400 mg</strong></p><p> As with other oral antibiotics, the duration of treatment generally ranges from 5-10 days. For treatment of infections due to <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, a therapeutic dose of Ceftibuten should be administered for at least 10 days.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommended Dose: 400 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For treatment of the following infections, this may be administered as 400 mg once daily: Acute bacterial sinusitis, acute bronchitis, acute exacerbations of chronic bronchitis, and complicated or uncomplicated urinary tract infections.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For the treatment of community-acquired pneumonia in patients in whom oral therapy is appropriate, the recommended dose is 200 mg every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommended Dose: 9 mg/kg/day (maximum of 400 mg daily) of the oral suspension. This may be administered as a single daily dose for treatment of the following infections: Pharyngitis with or without tonsillitis, acute otitis media with effusion, and complicated or uncomplicated urinary tract infections.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For the treatment of acute bacterial enteritis in children, the total daily dosage may be administered in 2 divided doses of 4.5 mg/kg every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing &gt;45 kg or &gt;10 years may receive the recommended adult dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Roin 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Otitis Media</strong>Adult Dosage: 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pediatric Dosage: &gt;6 months: 9 mg/kg/day (maximum 400 mg) every 24 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tonsillitis/Pharyngitis</strong>Adult Dosage: 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pediatric Dosage: &gt;6 months: 9 mg/kg/day (maximum 400 mg) every 24 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Bronchitis</strong>Adult Dosage: 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pediatric Dosage: &gt;12 years: 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Cystitis</strong>Adult Dosage: 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pediatric Dosage: &gt;12 years: 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pneumonia</strong>Adult Dosage: 200 mg orally every 12 hours for 7 to 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pediatric Dosage: &gt;12 years: 200 mg orally every 12 hours for 7 to 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Sinusitis</strong>Adult Dosage: 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 10 to 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pediatric Dosage: &gt;12 years: 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 10 to 14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Urinary Tract Infection</strong>Adult Dosage: 400 mg orally every 24 hours for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pediatric Dosage: &gt;6 months: 9 mg/kg/day (maximum 400 mg) every 24 hours for 10 days</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Roin 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Theophylline &amp; Antacids do not alter the pharmacokinetic profile of Ceftibuten.</li><li>Ranitidine increases the Cmax &amp; AUC of Ceftibuten.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Ceftibuten is contraindicated in patients with known allergy to the cephalosporin group of antibiotics.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Roin 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Nausea, headache, diarrhea, dyspepsia, dizziness, abdominal pain, vomiting.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Ceftibuten has been assigned to pregnancy category B by the FDA. Animal studies have failed to reveal evidence of fetal harm. There are no controlled data in human pregnancy. Ceftibuten should only be given during pregnancy when the need has been clearly established.</li><li>Ceftibuten is acceptable to use during breastfeeding. Limited information indicates that single maternal doses of ceftibuten up to 200 mg produce low levels in milk that are not expected to cause adverse effects in breastfed infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>As with other broad-spectrum antibiotics, prolonged treatment may result in the possible emergence and overgrowth of resistant organisms. Careful observation of the patient is essential.</li><li>The dose of Ceftibuten may require adjustment in patients with varying degrees of renal insufficiency.</li><li>Ceftibuten should be prescribed with caution to individuals with a history of gastrointestinal disease, particularly colitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Roin 400 mg</strong></p><p> Overdose symptoms may include seizures (convulsions).</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 25°C, protected from light and moisture.</p><ul><li>For Suspension: After reconstitution, the suspension may be used for 14 days while stored at 2° to 8°C.</li><li> Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children</strong>: Safety and efficacy of Ceftibuten in infants &lt;6 months have not been established.</li><li><strong>Adult Patients with Renal Impairment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Ceftibuten pharmacokinetics are not affected sufficiently to require dosage modifications unless creatinine clearance values are &lt;50 mL/min.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If creatinine clearance is from 49 to 30 mL/min, the daily dose should be decreased to 200 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">With creatinine clearance values of 29 to 5 mL/min, the recommended daily dose is 100 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If alteration of dosing frequency is preferred, a 400-mg dose of Ceftibuten may be administered every 48 hrs (every 2 days) to a patient with a creatinine clearance of 30-49 mL/min, and every 96 hrs (every 4 days) if creatinine clearance is 5-29 mL/min.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In patients undergoing hemodialysis 2 or 3 times weekly, a single dose of Ceftibuten 400 mg may be administered at the end of each hemodialysis session.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Third-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Ceftibuten is the dihydrate salt of Ceftibuten, a semi-synthetic Cephalosporin antibiotic for oral administration. Ceftibuten exerts its bactericidal action by binding to essential target proteins of the bacterial cell wall. This binding leads to the inhibition of cell-wall synthesis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category B. There are no controlled data on the use of Ceftibuten in pregnant women. Ceftibuten should be used in pregnancy only when the benefit clearly outweighs the risk. It is not known whether Ceftibuten (recommended dosage) is excreted in human milk. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk, caution should be exercised when Ceftibuten is administered to nursing women.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>CrCl 5 to 29 ml/min: 2.25 mg/kg or 100 mg orally once a day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 30 to 49 ml/min: 4.5 mg/kg or 200 mg orally once a day</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Dose adjustment is not necessary.</li></ul>', 'active', '409_1742558305.jpg', 27, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 16:58:25', '2025-03-21 16:58:25'),
(3923, 'Targocid', 'Teicoplanin', 1590.00, 1, 'Injection', 'Injection', 'Injection', '400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Targocid 400 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Targocid 400 mg/vial is indicated in adults and in children for the following infections:</p><ul><li>Complicated skin and soft tissue infections</li><li>Bone and joint infections</li><li>Hospital-acquired pneumonia</li><li>Community-acquired pneumonia</li><li>Complicated urinary tract infections</li><li>Infective endocarditis</li></ul><p>Targocid 400 mg/vial is also indicated as an alternative oral treatment for Clostridium difficile infection-associated diarrhea and colitis. Where appropriate, Targocid 400 mg/vial should be administered in combination with other antibacterial agents.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Glycopeptide</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Targocid 400 mg/vial is a glycopeptide antibiotic that has shown in vitro bactericidal activity against both anaerobic and aerobic gram-positive organisms. Targocid 400 mg/vial inhibits the growth of susceptible organisms by interfering with cell-wall biosynthesis at a site different from that affected by beta-lactams. It is active against staphylococci (including those resistant to methicillin and other beta-lactam antibiotics), streptococci, enterococci, Listeria monocytogenes, micrococci, group JK corynebacteria, and gram-positive anaerobes including Clostridium difficile and peptococci.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Targocid 400 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Targocid 400 mg/vial can be given intravenously (as a bolus injection or infusion) or intramuscularly. The intravenous injection may be administered either as a bolus over 3 to 5 minutes or as a 30-minute infusion. Only the infusion method should be used in neonates. It should be given once daily after one or more loading doses. The dosage of Targocid 400 mg/vial should be adapted based on the severity of the infection and patient’s response to treatment.</p><p>In adults (Normal kidney function):</p><ul><li>On the first day, a dose of 6 mg/kg (generally 400 mg) intravenously is recommended.</li><li>On the following days, the dose may be 6 mg/kg/day (generally 400 mg) intravenously or 3 mg/kg/day (generally 200 mg) intravenously or intramuscularly as a single daily dose. The highest dose and the intravenous route are recommended for serious infections.</li><li>In potentially fatal infections, treatment should be started with 6 mg/kg (usually 400 mg) twice daily for 1 to 4 days (loading dose) and continued with 6 mg/kg/day intravenously on the following days (maintenance dose).</li></ul><p>In adults and elderly (Kidney failure):</p><p> In patients with kidney failure, the dosage should be adjusted from the 4th day of treatment as follows:</p><ul><li>In moderate kidney failure (creatinine clearance between 40 and 60 ml/min), the daily dose of Targocid 400 mg/vial should be halved or given on alternate days.</li><li>In severe kidney failure (creatinine clearance less than 40 ml/min) and in hemodialysis patients, the daily dose of Targocid 400 mg/vial should be reduced to a third, or given every 3 days.</li><li>If creatinine clearance is equal to or less than 20 ml/min, treatment with Targocid 400 mg/vial may be given only if monitoring of drug levels in the blood can be guaranteed.</li></ul><p>In children (Normal kidney function):</p><ul><li>In Children (2 months to 12 years): Treatment should be started at a dose of 10 mg/kg every 12 hours for a total of 3 doses and then continued at a dose of 6-10 mg/kg/day, the highest dose being used for the most serious infections or for children with neutropenia.</li><li>In neonates (up to 2 months): Treatment should be started at a dose of 16 mg/kg on the first day, followed by maintenance doses of 8 mg/kg/day by slow intravenous infusion (lasting approximately 30 minutes).</li></ul><p>In children (Kidney failure):</p><p> As like adults, dosage adjustment is recommended. Measuring Targocid 400 mg/vial levels in the blood may be useful to make treatment as effective as possible.</p><p>Clostridium difficile infection-associated diarrhea: The recommended dose is 100-200 mg administered twice a day for 7 to 14 days. Giving Targocid 400 mg/vial along with another suitable bactericidal antibiotic is recommended for infections requiring maximum bactericidal activity or in situations where the presence of Gram-negative bacteria cannot be ruled out (empirical treatment of fever in patients with neutropenia). In most patients, this medicine can be seen to have an effect 48 to 72 hours after the start of treatment. However, the total duration of treatment is determined based on the type and severity of the infection and the patient’s response to treatment. In endocarditis and osteomyelitis, a period of at least 3 weeks is recommended.</p><p><strong>Administration of Targocid 400 mg/vial</strong></p><p> 3 ml water for injection should be added slowly down the side wall of the vial of Targocid 400 mg/vial 200 mg or 400 mg. The vial should be rolled gently between the palms until the powder is completely dissolved. During the rolling, we have to be cautious about the solution that it does not become foamy. The solution must not be shaken. If foam forms, it should be allowed to stand for 15 minutes for the foam to subside. The entire contents from the vial should be withdrawn slowly into a syringe.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Targocid 400 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Due to the potential for increased adverse effects, Targocid 400 mg/vial should be administered with caution in patients receiving concurrent nephrotoxic or ototoxic drugs, such as Aminoglycosides, Amphotericin B, Cyclosporin, and Furosemide.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Targocid 400 mg/vial is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to the drug.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Targocid 400 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Local reaction:</strong> Erythema, injection site abscess when given intramuscularly.</li><li><strong>Allergic reaction:</strong> Rash, pruritus, fever, shivering, anaphylactic reactions.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal reactions:</strong> Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea.</li><li><strong>CNS reactions:</strong> Physical weakness, dizziness, headache.</li><li><strong>Hearing reactions:</strong> Ringing in the ears, balance disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women about the administration of Targocid 400 mg/vial; this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Information about the excretion of Targocid 400 mg/vial in milk is not known.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Targocid 400 mg/vial should be administered with caution in patients known to be hypersensitive to Vancomycin since cross-hypersensitivity may occur.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at a temperature not exceeding 30°C in a dry place. Protect from light. The reconstituted solution should be used immediately or stored under refrigeration (2 to 8° C) for 24 hours maximum.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> <strong>Patients with renal impairment:</strong> For patients with impaired renal function, reduction of dosage is not required until the fourth day of Targocid 400 mg/vial treatment. From the fourth day of treatment:</p><ul><li>In mild renal insufficiency: Targocid 400 mg/vial dose should be halved either by administering the initial unit dose every two days, or by administering half of this dose once a day when creatinine clearance is 40-60 ml/min.</li><li>In severe renal insufficiency: Targocid 400 mg/vial dose should be 1/3 of the normal either by administering the initial unit dose every third day or by administering 1/3 of this dose once a day when creatinine clearance is less than 40 ml/min and in hemodialyzed patients.</li><li> Targocid 400 mg/vial is not removed by dialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p> 3 ml water for injection should be added slowly down the side wall of the vial of Targocid 400 mg/vial 200 mg or 400 mg. The vial should be rolled gently between the palms until the powder is completely dissolved. During the rolling, we have to be cautious about the solution that it does not become foamy. The solution must not be shaken. If foam forms, it should be allowed to stand for 15 minutes for the foam to subside. The entire contents from the vial should be withdrawn slowly into a syringe.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Glycopeptide</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Targocid 400 mg/vial is a glycopeptide-antibiotic produced by Actinoplanes teichomyceticus. It is presented as a sterile, pyrogen-free ivory white powder for reconstitution with water for injection. It is freely soluble in water and on reconstitution gives a clear solution. Targocid 400 mg/vial inhibits the growth of susceptible organisms by interfering with cell-wall biosynthesis at a site different from that affected by beta-lactams. Peptidoglycan synthesis is blocked by specific binding to D-alanyl-D-alanine residues.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category B3. Targocid 400 mg/vial should not be used during confirmed or presumed pregnancy or during lactation unless the potential benefits outweigh possible risks.</p>', 'active', '947_1742558469.jpg', 45, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 17:01:09', '2025-03-21 17:01:09'),
(3924, 'Rubavax-M', 'Measles and Rubella', 400.00, 1, 'Injection', 'Injection', 'Injection', '0.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rubavax-M 0.5 ml</strong></p><p> It is indicated for combined immunization against measles, mumps, and rubella. As a general guide, the first dose of the MMR vaccine is administered to children aged 12 months. A second (booster) dose of MMR or monovalent measles vaccine is recommended before starting school at 3-6 years of age. Children presenting for their pre-school booster who have not received their first dose of MMR vaccine should be given a dose of MMR vaccine, followed by a second dose 3 months later. MMR vaccine can be used in place of measles vaccine for children and should be given irrespective of previous measles, mumps, or rubella infection. It is also used to control outbreaks of measles and should be offered to susceptible children within 3 days of exposure to the infection.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Vaccines, Anti-sera &amp; Immunoglobulin</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> This vaccine contains live attenuated measles, mumps, and rubella viruses. It is used in the active immunization against these diseases.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rubavax-M 0.5 ml</strong></p><p> Adults and Children: A single dose of 0.5 ml of the reconstituted vaccine is given by deep subcutaneous injection. Do not administer intravenously.</p><p> Expanded Program on Immunization schedule:</p><ul><li>After completion of 9 months, 1 dose of measles-rubella vaccine should be given.</li><li>After completion of 15 months, 1 dose of only measles-containing vaccine should be given.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Rubavax-M 0.5 ml</strong></p><p> Immune globulins may reduce the efficacy of vaccines. It may also reduce the diagnostic effect of tuberculin tests.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Current febrile respiratory illness or other febrile infection</li><li>Patients receiving immunosuppressive therapy; primary and acquired immunodeficiency states</li><li>Blood dyscrasias, cancers affecting the bone marrow or lymphatic systems</li><li>Pregnancy</li><li>Active untreated tuberculosis</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Rubavax-M 0.5 ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Common side-effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Pain and tenderness at the injection site may occur within 24 hours after vaccination, usually resolving within 2-3 days</li><li>Transient fever reaction may occur within 1-2 weeks after vaccination</li><li>Mild rash within 12 days after vaccination, generally lasting no more than 2 days</li></ul><p><strong>Rare side-effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Severe fever reaction: physical methods and drugs may be used for symptomatic treatment to prevent febrile seizures</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Very rare side-effects:</strong>Allergic rash (urticaria) may occur within 72 hours after vaccination; anti-allergy treatment should be given promptly</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Anaphylactic shock: occurs generally within 1 hour after vaccination; epinephrine and other emergency measures should be administered immediately</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Anaphylactoid purpura: medical care should be promptly provided, and corticosteroids can be used for anti-allergy treatment</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Thrombocytopenia purpura</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Arthritis, pain, and swelling of large joints may develop in adults</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> <strong>Category C:</strong> Either studies in animals have revealed adverse effects on the fetus (teratogenic or embryocidal) and there are no controlled studies in women, or studies in women and animals are not available. Drugs should be given only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with a history of cerebral injury or seizures</li><li>Patients with thrombocytopenia or those who develop thrombocytopenia after the first dose</li><li>For patients with active tuberculosis, tuberculosis treatment should be initiated before using the vaccine</li><li>Vaccination may be deferred in patients with moderate or severe acute illness</li><li>Pregnancy should be avoided for 28 days after vaccination</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Powder:</strong> Store at 2-8°C. Freezing does not affect potency.</li><li><strong>Diluent:</strong> May store in refrigerator or at room temperature; do not freeze.</li><li><strong>Reconstituted solution:</strong> Store at 2-8°C; protect from light; discard if not used within 8 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p> Reconstitute the vaccine with the provided diluent. Mix thoroughly. Discard if powder does not dissolve. Use immediately after reconstitution.</p>', 'active', '1527_1742558587.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 17:03:07', '2025-03-21 17:03:07'),
(3925, 'Roket', 'Ketorolac Tromethamine', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Roket 10 mg</strong></p><p> Roket 10 mg is indicated for the short-term management of moderate to severe acute post-operative pain.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-Opioid Analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Roket 10 mg is a potent analgesic of the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). It acts by inhibiting the cyclooxygenase enzyme system, thereby inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis. It demonstrates a minimal anti-inflammatory effect at its analgesic dose.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Roket 10 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Tablet</strong></p><ul><li>Recommended dose is 10 mg every 4-6 hours. It should be used short-term only (up to 7 days) and is not recommended for chronic use. Doses exceeding 40 mg/day are not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Injection</strong></p><ul><li>Ketorolac injection may be used as a single or multiple dose, on a regular or when necessary schedule for the management of moderately severe acute pain that requires analgesia at the opioid level, usually in a postoperative setting.</li><li>When administering Ketorolac injection, the IV bolus must be given over no less than 15 seconds. The IM administration should be given slowly and deeply into the muscle.</li><li>The analgesic effect begins within 30 minutes with maximum effect in 1 to 2 hours after IV or IM dosing. Duration of analgesic effect is usually 4 to 6 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Single-Dose Treatment</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IM Dosing (Adult):</strong>Patients &lt;65 years of age: One dose of 60 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients &gt;65 years of age, renally impaired, and/or less than 50 kg body weight: One dose of 30 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV Dosing (Adult):</strong>Patients &lt;65 years of age: One dose of 30 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients &gt;65 years of age, renally impaired, and/or less than 50 kg body weight: One dose of 15 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV or IM Dosing (2 to 16 years of age):IM Dosing:</strong> One dose of 1 mg/kg up to a maximum of 30 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV Dosing:</strong> One dose of 0.5 mg/kg up to a maximum of 15 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Multiple-Dose Treatment (IV or IM)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Patients &lt;65 years of age:</strong> The recommended dose is 30 mg Ketorolac injection every 6 hours. The maximum daily dose should not exceed 120 mg.</li><li><strong>Patients &gt;65 years of age, renally impaired patients, and patients less than 50 kg:</strong> The recommended dose is 15 mg Ketorolac injection every 6 hours. The maximum daily dose for these populations should not exceed 60 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>For breakthrough pain:</strong> Do not increase the dose or frequency of Roket 10 mg.</p><p><strong>Conversion from Parenteral to Oral Therapy</strong></p><p> Ketorolac tablets may be used either as monotherapy or as follow-on therapy to parenteral Ketorolac. When Ketorolac tablets are used as follow-on therapy to parenteral Ketorolac, the total combined daily dose of ketorolac (oral + parenteral) should not exceed 120 mg in younger adult patients or 60 mg in elderly patients on the day the change of formulation is made. On subsequent days, oral dosing should not exceed the recommended daily maximum of 40 mg. Ketorolac IM should be replaced by Ketorolac tablet as soon as feasible. The total duration of combined parenteral and oral treatment should not exceed 5 days.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Roket 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Other NSAIDs or aspirin:</strong> Increase the side effects of ketorolac Tromethamine.</li><li><strong>Anti-coagulants:</strong> Enhance anti-coagulant effect.</li><li><strong>Beta Blocker:</strong> Reduce the anti-hypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>ACE Inhibitors:</strong> Increase the risk of renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate:</strong> Enhance the toxicity of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Ketorolac is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to this drug or other NSAIDs. It should not be used in children under 16 years of age. It is also contraindicated as a prophylactic analgesic before surgery.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Roket 10 mg</strong></p><p> Commonly occurring side effects are nausea, vomiting, gastrointestinal bleeding, melena, peptic ulcer, pancreatitis, anxiety, drowsiness, headache, excessive thirst, fatigue, bradycardia, hypertension, palpitation, chest pain, infertility in females, and pulmonary edema.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> US FDA Pregnancy category of Roket 10 mg is C. Roket 10 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Caution should be exercised in patients over the age of 65 years. Caution should also be taken in patients with active or suspected peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding, asthma, and liver dysfunction.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '187_1742558704.jpg', 64, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 17:05:04', '2025-03-21 17:05:04');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3926, 'Peranel', 'Perampanel', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Peranel 2 mg</strong></p><p> Peranel 2 mg, an anti-epileptic drug, is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Treatment of partial-onset seizures with or without secondarily generalized seizures in patients with epilepsy from 4 years of age and older.</li><li>Adjunctive therapy in the treatment of primary generalized tonic-clonic seizures in patients with epilepsy from 12 years of age and older.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Peranel 2 mg</strong></p><p> Peranel 2 mg can be taken without regard to food. The dosage of Peranel 2 mg is as follows:</p><ul><li><strong>Starting dose:</strong> 2 mg once daily at bedtime.</li><li>May increase dose based on clinical response and tolerability by increments of 2 mg once daily, no more frequently than at weekly intervals.</li><li><strong>Recommended maintenance dose in monotherapy or adjunctive therapy for partial-onset seizures:</strong> 8 mg to 12 mg once daily at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Recommended maintenance dose in adjunctive therapy for primary generalized tonic-clonic seizures:</strong> 8 mg once daily at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Mild and Moderate Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Maximum recommended daily dose is 6 mg (mild) and 4 mg (moderate) once daily at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Severe Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Not recommended.</li><li><strong>Severe Renal Impairment:</strong> Not recommended.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Increase dose no more frequently than every 2 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Peranel 2 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Contraceptives:</strong> 12 mg once daily may decrease the effectiveness of hormonal contraceptives containing levonorgestrel.</li><li><strong>Moderate and Strong CYP3A4 Inducers</strong> (including carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, and phenytoin): Increase the clearance of Peranel 2 mg and decrease Peranel 2 mg plasma concentrations.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> None.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Peranel 2 mg</strong></p><p> Peranel 2 mg may cause:</p><ul><li>Serious psychiatric and behavioral reactions, suicidal behavior and ideation, dizziness, somnolence, fatigue, irritability, falls, nausea, ataxia, and balance disorder.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Suicidal Behavior and Ideation:</strong> Monitor for suicidal thoughts or behavior.</li><li><strong>Neurologic Effects:</strong> Monitor for dizziness, gait disturbance, somnolence, and fatigue.</li><li><strong>Falls:</strong> Monitor for falls and injuries.</li><li><strong>Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms/Multi-organ Hypersensitivity:</strong> Discontinue if no alternate etiology.</li><li><strong>Withdrawal of Antiepileptic Drugs:</strong> In patients with epilepsy, there may be an increase in seizure frequency.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Protect from light and moisture, store below 30°C. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Adjunct anti-epileptic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Peranel 2 mg is a non-competitive antagonist of the ionotropic α-amino-3-hydroxy-5-methyl-4-isoxazolepropionic acid (AMPA) glutamate receptor on post-synaptic neurons. Glutamate is the primary excitatory neurotransmitter in the central nervous system and is implicated in a number of neurological disorders caused by neuronal over-excitation. The precise mechanism by which Peranel 2 mg exerts its antiepileptic effects in humans is unknown.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Based on animal data, may cause fetal harm. There are no data on the presence of Peranel 2 mg in human milk, the effects on the breastfed child, or the effects of the drug on milk production.</p>', 'active', '1202_1742558894.jpg', 4, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 17:08:14', '2025-03-21 17:08:14'),
(3927, 'Solodex', 'Sodium Chloride + Dextrose', 75.89, 1, 'x 500 ml bag', '500 ml bag', 'IV Infusion', '0.9%+5%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Solodex 0.9%+5%</strong></p><p>This solution is indicated when there is combined water and sodium depletion. It provides Dextrose as a nutrient in a suitable medium of Sodium Chloride, which is isotonic to body fluid, or it may also be employed as a source of isotonic Sodium Chloride or both. It is usually used in the maintenance and replacement of fluid, electrolyte, and carbohydrate in patients who are unable to take fluid and nutrients by mouth, e.g., in cases of persistent vomiting, during and after surgery, shock, or accidents.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Intravenous fluid preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Dextrose is a monosaccharide that is used as a source of calories and water for hydration. It helps to reduce loss of body protein and nitrogen. It also promotes glycogen deposition in the liver. When used with insulin, it stimulates the uptake of potassium by cells, especially in muscle tissue, thus lowering serum potassium levels.</p><p>Sodium chloride is the major extracellular cation. It is important in electrolyte and fluid balance, osmotic pressure control, and water distribution as it restores sodium ions. It is used as a source of electrolytes and water for hydration, treatment of metabolic acidosis, priming solution in haemodialysis, and treatment of hyperosmolar diabetes. It is also used as diluents for infusion of compatible drug additives.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Solodex 0.9%+5%</strong></p><p>Dose is variable. Generally, 60-120 drops/minute are given intravenously or according to physician’s recommendation. Dose depends on the clinical condition, age, and body surface area of the patients.</p><p><strong>Administration of Solodex 0.9%+5%</strong></p><p>Check the infusion set and infusion solution prior to use. Pull moderately to tear off the protective cover of the Eurocap. Hold lightly the Eurocap but not the bottle body. Open the flow regulator fully and hold the giving set on the top white area, but not the membrane venting region. Insert the spike of the administration set to the Eurocap and fit the connector of the administration set firmly to the needle. Gradually allow the fluid to flow down to the needle tip and close. Remove the protective cover of the needle. Locate the venpuncture site, clean the site with an antiseptic solution, and then insert the needle. Securely tape the puncture site. Securely tape the wings and tubing. Start infusion while adjusting drip speed.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Solodex 0.9%+5%</strong></p><p>There are no known drug interactions and none well documented.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Do not take this medicine and tell your doctor if:</p><p>Solutions containing dextrose may be contraindicated in patients with known allergy to corn or corn products.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Solodex 0.9%+5%</strong></p><p>Reactions that may occur because of the solution or the technique of administration include febrile response, infection at the site of injection, venous thrombosis or phlebitis extending from the site of injection, extravasation, and hypervolemia. If an adverse reaction does occur, discontinue the infusion, evaluate the patient, institute appropriate therapeutic countermeasures, and save the remainder of the fluid for examination if deemed necessary.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Category - C</p><p>Animal reproduction studies have shown an adverse effect on the fetus, and there are no adequate and well-controlled studies in humans, but potential benefits may warrant use of the drug in pregnant women despite potential risks.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>As the preparation contains Sodium Chloride, it should be administered with caution to patients with congestive heart failure, peripheral or pulmonary edema, impaired renal function, or pre-eclampsia. Serum glucose concentration should also be carefully monitored, and concurrent use of insulin may be needed in case of diabetic patients. Infusion of fluid should be immediately discontinued if rigor arises for any reason during the process. Do not use if the solution is cloudy, contains particles, or after the expiry date.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Intravenous fluid preparations, Parenteral nutritional preparations</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>FDA pregnancy category C. It is not known whether dextrose 5% in water will harm an unborn baby. Tell your doctor if you are pregnant or plan to become pregnant while using this medication. It is not known whether dextrose 5% in water passes into breast milk or if it could harm a nursing baby. Do not use this medication without telling your doctor if you are breast-feeding a baby.</p>', 'active', '4554_1746511829.webp', 4, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 17:10:35', '2025-05-06 10:10:29'),
(3928, 'Solodex', 'Sodium Chloride + Dextrose', 101.20, 1, 'x 1000 ml bag', '1000 ml bag', 'IV Infusion', '0.9%+5%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Solodex 0.9%+5%</strong></p><p>This solution is indicated when there is combined water and sodium depletion. It provides Dextrose as a nutrient in a suitable medium of Sodium Chloride, which is isotonic to body fluid, or it may also be employed as a source of isotonic Sodium Chloride or both. It is usually used in the maintenance and replacement of fluid, electrolyte, and carbohydrate in patients who are unable to take fluid and nutrients by mouth, e.g., in cases of persistent vomiting, during and after surgery, shock, or accidents.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Intravenous fluid preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Dextrose is a monosaccharide that is used as a source of calories and water for hydration. It helps to reduce loss of body protein and nitrogen. It also promotes glycogen deposition in the liver. When used with insulin, it stimulates the uptake of potassium by cells, especially in muscle tissue, thus lowering serum potassium levels.</p><p>Sodium chloride is the major extracellular cation. It is important in electrolyte and fluid balance, osmotic pressure control, and water distribution as it restores sodium ions. It is used as a source of electrolytes and water for hydration, treatment of metabolic acidosis, priming solution in haemodialysis, and treatment of hyperosmolar diabetes. It is also used as diluents for infusion of compatible drug additives.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Solodex 0.9%+5%</strong></p><p>Dose is variable. Generally, 60-120 drops/minute are given intravenously or according to physician’s recommendation. Dose depends on the clinical condition, age, and body surface area of the patients.</p><p><strong>Administration of Solodex 0.9%+5%</strong></p><p>Check the infusion set and infusion solution prior to use. Pull moderately to tear off the protective cover of the Eurocap. Hold lightly the Eurocap but not the bottle body. Open the flow regulator fully and hold the giving set on the top white area, but not the membrane venting region. Insert the spike of the administration set to the Eurocap and fit the connector of the administration set firmly to the needle. Gradually allow the fluid to flow down to the needle tip and close. Remove the protective cover of the needle. Locate the venpuncture site, clean the site with an antiseptic solution, and then insert the needle. Securely tape the puncture site. Securely tape the wings and tubing. Start infusion while adjusting drip speed.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Solodex 0.9%+5%</strong></p><p>There are no known drug interactions and none well documented.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Do not take this medicine and tell your doctor if:</p><p>Solutions containing dextrose may be contraindicated in patients with known allergy to corn or corn products.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Solodex 0.9%+5%</strong></p><p>Reactions that may occur because of the solution or the technique of administration include febrile response, infection at the site of injection, venous thrombosis or phlebitis extending from the site of injection, extravasation, and hypervolemia. If an adverse reaction does occur, discontinue the infusion, evaluate the patient, institute appropriate therapeutic countermeasures, and save the remainder of the fluid for examination if deemed necessary.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Category - C</p><p>Animal reproduction studies have shown an adverse effect on the fetus, and there are no adequate and well-controlled studies in humans, but potential benefits may warrant use of the drug in pregnant women despite potential risks.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>As the preparation contains Sodium Chloride, it should be administered with caution to patients with congestive heart failure, peripheral or pulmonary edema, impaired renal function, or pre-eclampsia. Serum glucose concentration should also be carefully monitored, and concurrent use of insulin may be needed in case of diabetic patients. Infusion of fluid should be immediately discontinued if rigor arises for any reason during the process. Do not use if the solution is cloudy, contains particles, or after the expiry date.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Intravenous fluid preparations, Parenteral nutritional preparations</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>FDA pregnancy category C. It is not known whether dextrose 5% in water will harm an unborn baby. Tell your doctor if you are pregnant or plan to become pregnant while using this medication. It is not known whether dextrose 5% in water passes into breast milk or if it could harm a nursing baby. Do not use this medication without telling your doctor if you are breast-feeding a baby.</p>', 'active', '8703_1746511906.webp', 4, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 17:10:35', '2025-05-06 10:11:46'),
(3929, 'Safoxol', 'Ambroxol Hydrochloride', 50.00, 1, 'Syrup', '500 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '15 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Safoxol 15 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> This is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Productive cough</li><li>Acute and chronic inflammatory disorders of upper and lower respiratory tracts associated with viscid mucus, including acute and chronic bronchitis</li><li>Inflammatory disease of the rhinopharyngeal tract (laryngitis, pharyngitis, sinusitis, and rhinitis) associated with viscid mucus</li><li>Asthmatic bronchitis, bronchial asthma with thick expectoration</li><li>Bronchiectasis, chronic pneumonia, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Cough expectorants &amp; mucolytics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Ambroxol is a metabolite of Bromhexine. It possesses mucokinetic (improvement in mucus transport) and secretolytic (liquefies secretions) properties. Ambroxol stimulates the serous cells of the glands of the mucous membrane of bronchi, increasing the content of mucus secretion. The mucolytic effect is associated with depolymerization and splitting of mucoproteins and mucopolysaccharide fibers, which leads to reduction in the viscosity of mucus. Expectoration of mucus is facilitated, and breathing is eased considerably. Ambroxol stimulates production of phospholipids of surfactant by alveolar cells. Ambroxol has anti-inflammatory properties. In patients with COPD, it improves airway patency. Besides these, Ambroxol also exhibits antioxidant activity. Long-term use is possible because of the good tolerability of the preparation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Safoxol 15 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Average daily dose (preferably after meal):</p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Drops</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0-6 months: 0.5 ml 2 times a day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 months: 1 ml 2 times a day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-2 years: 1.25 ml 2 times a day</li><li><strong>Syrup</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2-5 years: 2.5 ml (1/2 teaspoonful) 2-3 times a day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">5-10 years: 5 ml (1 teaspoonful) 2-3 times a day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 years and adults: 10 ml (2 teaspoonful) 3 times a day</li><li><strong>Sustained release capsule</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adult and children over 12 years old: 1 capsule once daily</li></ul><p>Specific application features: Ambroxol may be prescribed to patients suffering from diabetes mellitus.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Safoxol 15 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Ambroxol should not be taken simultaneously with antitussives (e.g., Codeine) because phlegm, which has been liquefied by Ambroxol, might not be expectorated.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Contraindicated in known hypersensitivity to Ambroxol or Bromhexine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Safoxol 15 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Gastrointestinal side effects like epigastric pain, stomach overfill feeling may occur occasionally. Rarely, allergic responses such as eruption, urticaria, or angioneurotic edema have been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Teratogenic and fetal toxicity studies have shown no harmful effect of Ambroxol. However, it is advised not to use during pregnancy, especially in the 1st trimester. Safety during lactation has not been established.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Ambroxol should be given cautiously to patients with gastric and duodenal ulceration or convulsive disorders. Patients with hepatic and renal insufficiency should take it with caution.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Protect from direct light exposure. Store in a dry place at a temperature not exceeding 30°C. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Cough expectorants &amp; mucolytics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Ambroxol is the active metabolite of bromhexine, and it has been proven that this metabolite possesses a greater bronchosecretolytic effect than bromhexine. It improves sputum rheology by a hydrating mechanism leading to liquefaction of mucus in the lumen of the respiratory tract, thus facilitating expectoration of mucus and reducing dyspnea. It stimulates the production of phospholipids of surfactant by alveolar cells, contributing to the lowering of superficial tension in the alveoli. It also reduces bronchial hyperactivity. Ambroxol has anti-inflammatory properties due to its inhibitory effect on the production of cellular cytokines and arachidonic acid metabolites. In patients with COPD, it traditionally improves airway patency.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Teratogenic and fetal toxicity studies have shown no harmful effect of Ambroxol. However, it is advised not to use it during pregnancy, especially during the 1st trimester. Safety during lactation has not been established yet.	</p>', 'active', '1548_1742559154.jpg', 55, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 17:12:34', '2025-03-21 17:12:34'),
(3930, 'Roxibac', 'Cefuroxime Axetil', 30.00, 7, 'Tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Roxibac 250 mg</strong></p><p> It is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive bacteria, including:</p><ul><li>Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li>Acute Bacterial Otitis Media caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em> (beta-lactamase producing strains), or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li>Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> or <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (non beta-lactamase producing strains)</li><li>Lower respiratory tract infections including pneumonia caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (including beta-lactamase-producing strains), <em>Klebsiella spp.</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains), <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>, <em>E. coli</em></li><li>Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis and secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains), or <em>Haemophilus parainfluenzae</em> (beta-lactamase negative strains)</li><li>Skin and skin-structure infections caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including beta-lactamase producing strains) or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em></li><li>Urinary tract infections caused by <em>E. coli</em> or <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></li><li>Bone and joint infections caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (penicillinase- and non-penicillinase-producing strains)</li><li>Gonorrhoea caused by penicillinase-producing and non-penicillinase producing strains of <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em></li><li>Early Lyme Disease (erythema migrans) caused by <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Second generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime is a well-characterized and effective antibacterial agent, which has broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against a wide range of common pathogens, including β-lactamase producing strains. Cefuroxime has good stability to bacterial β-lactamase and consequently, is active against many ampicillin-resistant and amoxycillin-resistant strains.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Roxibac 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet or Suspension</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adolescents and adults (13 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis: 250 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis: 250 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Acute bacterial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Secondary bacterial infections of acute bronchitis: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Uncomplicated skin and skin structure infections: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Uncomplicated urinary tract infections: 250 mg b.i.d. for 7-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Uncomplicated Gonorrhoea: 1000 mg single dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Community acquired pneumonia: 250-500 mg b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">MDR Typhoid Fever: 500 mg b.i.d. for 10-14 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Early Lyme disease: 500 mg b.i.d. for 20 days</li><li><strong>Paediatric Patients (3 months to 12 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Pharyngitis/Tonsillitis: 20 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Acute otitis media: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Impetigo: 30 mg/kg/day b.i.d. for 10 days</li><li><strong>Parenteral</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adult</strong>: 750 mg three times daily by IM or IV injection. In severe infections, the dose can be increased up to 1.5 gm three times daily by IV injection. The frequency may be increased to four times daily, if necessary, giving total daily doses of 3 to 6 gms.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (above 3 months of age)</strong>: 30-100 mg/kg/day given in 3 or 4 equally divided doses. A dose of 60 mg/kg/day is appropriate for most infections.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Neonate</strong>: 30-100 mg/kg/day given in 2 or 3 equally divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Surgical prophylaxis</strong>: 1.5 gm by IV injection at induction of anaesthesia; up to 3 further doses of 750 mg may be given by IV/IM injection every 8 hours for high-risk procedures.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pneumonia</strong>: 1.5 gm IV injection twice daily for 2-3 days, followed by 500 mg twice daily (oral) for 7-10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute exacerbations of chronic bronchitis</strong>: 750 mg twice daily (IM or IV injection) for 2-3 days, followed by 500 mg twice daily (oral) for 5-10 days.</li><li><strong>In Gonorrhoea</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adult</strong>: 1.5 gm as a single dose (as 2 x 750mg injections intramuscularly with different sites, e.g., each buttock).</li><li><strong>In Meningitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Adult</strong>: 3 gm IV injection three times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Children (above 3 months of age)</strong>: 200-240 mg/kg/day by IV injection in 3 or 4 divided doses reduced to 100 mg/kg/day after 3 days or on clinical improvement.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Neonate</strong>: 100 mg/kg/day by IV injection at initial dose, reduced to 50 mg/kg/day, when clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>In bone and joint infections</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Adult</strong>: 1.5 gm IV injection four times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (above 3 months of age)</strong>: 150 mg/kg/day (not to exceed the maximum adult dose) in equally divided doses every 8 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Roxibac 250 mg</strong></p><p> The use of freshly reconstituted solution is recommended. However, it maintains potency for at least 24 hours at room temperature or 48 hours at 5°C.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Roxibac 250 mg</strong></p><p> No potentially hazardous interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime is contraindicated in patients with known allergy to Cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Roxibac 250 mg</strong></p><p> Adverse effects to Cefuroxime have occurred infrequently and have been generally mild and transient in nature. Effects reported include rashes and gastrointestinal disturbances. As with other antibiotics, prolonged use may result in the overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms (e.g., Candida).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> US FDA pregnancy category of Cefuroxime is B. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Cefuroxime has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Cefuroxime is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime should be given with care to patients receiving concurrent treatment with potent diuretics and those with a history of colitis. Cephalosporin antibiotics may, in general, be given safely to patients who are hypersensitive to penicillin, although cross-reactions have been reported. Cefuroxime has shown that this is not likely to be a problem at the recommended dose levels.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place (below 30°C), away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li>For 750 mg intramuscular injection: Add 3 ml water for injection to vial and then shake gently for dispersion.</li><li>For 750 mg intravenous injection: Add 8 ml water for injection to vial and then shake gently for dispersion. The solution should be slowly injected directly into a vein over a 3 to 5-minute period.</li><li>For 1.5 g intravenous injection: Add 16 ml Water for injection to vial and then shake gently for dispersion. The solution should be slowly injected directly into a vein over a 3 to 5-minute period.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Second generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Cefuroxime is a well-characterized and effective antibacterial agent, which has broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against a wide range of common pathogens, including β-lactamase producing strains. Cefuroxime has good stability to bacterial β-lactamase and consequently, is active against many ampicillin-resistant and amoxycillin-resistant strains.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> US FDA pregnancy category of Cefuroxime is B. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Cefuroxime has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Cefuroxime is administered to a nursing woman.</p>', 'active', '1146_1742559284.jpg', 63, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 17:14:44', '2025-03-21 17:14:44'),
(3931, 'Silofast', 'Silodosin', 9.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '4 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Silofast 4 mg</strong></p><p> Silofast 4 mg, a selective alpha-1 adrenergic receptor antagonist, is indicated for the treatment of the signs and symptoms of benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH). Silofast 4 mg is not indicated for the treatment of hypertension. Highly effective for the treatment of Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia. Highest uroselectivity to alpha-1 adrenergic receptor. Effective treatment option for BPH who are not responding to Tamsulosin. Does not cause Orthostatic hypotension. Convenient once-daily dosing.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> BPH/ Urinary retention/ Urinary incontinence</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Silofast 4 mg is a selective antagonist of post-synaptic alpha-1 adrenoreceptors, which are located in the human prostate, bladder base, bladder neck, prostatic capsule, and prostatic urethra. Blockade of these alpha-1 adrenoreceptors can cause smooth muscle in these tissues to relax, resulting in an improvement in urine flow and a reduction in BPH symptoms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Silofast 4 mg</strong></p><p> The recommended dose is Silofast 4 mg 8 mg orally once daily with a meal. 4 mg capsules taken orally once daily with a meal for those with moderate renal impairment (CrCl 30-50 mL/min).</p><p><strong>Interaction of Silofast 4 mg</strong></p><p> Strong P-glycoprotein inhibitors (e.g., cyclosporine): Co-administration may increase plasma Silofast 4 mg concentration. Concomitant use of PDE5 inhibitors with alpha-blockers including Silofast 4 mg can potentially cause symptomatic hypotension.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Patients with severe renal &amp; hepatic impairment, concomitant administration with strong Cytochrome P450 3A4 (CYP3A4) inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole, clarithromycin, itraconazole, ritonavir) and patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Silofast 4 mg.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Silofast 4 mg</strong></p><p> Most common adverse reactions are retrograde ejaculation, dizziness, diarrhea, orthostatic hypotension, headache, nasopharyngitis, and nasal congestion.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category B. Silofast 4 mg is not indicated for use in women. An embryo/fetal study in rabbits showed decreased maternal body weight at 200 mg/kg/day (approximately 13-25 times the maximum recommended human exposure or MRHE of Silofast 4 mg via AUC). No statistically significant teratogenicity was observed at this dose. Silofast 4 mg was not teratogenic when administered to pregnant rats during organogenesis at 1000 mg/kg/day (estimated to be approximately 20 times the MRHE). No maternal or fetal effects were observed at this dose. Rats and rabbits do not produce glucuronidated Silofast 4 mg, which is present in human serum at approximately 4 times the level of circulating Silofast 4 mg and which has similar pharmacological activity to Silofast 4 mg. No effects on physical or behavioral development of offspring were observed when rats were treated during pregnancy and lactation at up to 300 mg/kg/day.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Postural hypotension with or without symptoms (e.g. dizziness) may develop when beginning Silofast 4 mg treatment. Silofast 4 mg should not be used in combination with other alpha-blockers. Inform patients planning cataract surgery to notify their ophthalmologist that they are taking Silofast 4 mg because of the possibility of Intraoperative Floppy Iris Syndrome (IFIS).</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Silofast 4 mg</strong></p><p> Silofast 4 mg was evaluated at doses of up to 48 mg/day in healthy male subjects. The dose-limiting adverse event was postural hypotension. Should overdose of Silofast 4 mg lead to hypotension, support of the cardiovascular system is of first importance. Restoration of blood pressure and normalization of heart rate may be accomplished by maintaining the patient in the supine position. If this measure is inadequate, administration of intravenous fluid should be considered. If necessary, vasopressors could be used, and renal function should be monitored and supported as needed. Dialysis is unlikely to be of significant benefit since Silofast 4 mg is highly (97%) protein bound.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at below 30°C in a dry place protected from light. Keep out of reach of Children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p> <strong>Pediatric patients:</strong> Silofast 4 mg is not indicated for use in pediatric patients.</p><p> <strong>Geriatric use:</strong> In double-blind, placebo-controlled, 12-week clinical studies of Silofast 4 mg, 259 (55.6%) were under 65 years of age, 207 (44.4%) patients were 65 years of age and over, while 60 (12.9%) patients were 75 years of age and over. Orthostatic hypotension was reported in 2.3% of Silofast 4 mg patients &lt; 65 years of age (1.2% for placebo), 2.9% of Silofast 4 mg patients &gt; 65 years of age (1.9% for placebo), and 5.0% of patients &gt; 75 years of age (0% for placebo). There were otherwise no significant differences in safety or effectiveness between older and younger patients.</p><p> <strong>Renal impairment:</strong> Silofast 4 mg is contra-indicated in patients with severe renal impairment (CCr &lt;30 mL/min). In patients with moderate renal impairment (CCr 30-50 mL/min), the dose should be reduced to Silofast 4 mg 4 mg once daily taken with a meal. No dosage adjustment is needed in patients with mild renal impairment (CCr 50-80 mL/min).</p><p> <strong>Hepatic impairment:</strong> Silofast 4 mg has not been studied in patients with severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh score &gt;10) and is therefore contra-indicated in these patients. No dosage adjustment is needed in patients with mild or moderate hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> BPH/ Urinary retention/ Urinary incontinence</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Silofast 4 mg is a selective antagonist of post-synaptic alpha-1 adrenoreceptors, which are located in the human prostate, bladder base, bladder neck, prostatic capsule, and prostatic urethra. Blockade of these alpha-1 adrenoreceptors can cause smooth muscle in these tissues to relax, resulting in an improvement in urine flow and a reduction in BPH symptoms.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category B. Silofast 4 mg is not indicated for use in women. An embryo/fetal study in rabbits showed decreased maternal body weight at 200 mg/kg/day (approximately 13-25 times the maximum recommended human exposure or MRHE of Silofast 4 mg via AUC). No statistically significant teratogenicity was observed at this dose. Silofast 4 mg was not teratogenic when administered to pregnant rats during organogenesis at 1000 mg/kg/day (estimated to be approximately 20 times the MRHE). No maternal or fetal effects were observed at this dose. Rats and rabbits do not produce glucuronidated Silofast 4 mg, which is present in human serum at approximately 4 times the level of circulating Silofast 4 mg and which has similar pharmacological activity to Silofast 4 mg. No effects on physical or behavioral development of offspring were observed when rats were treated during pregnancy and lactation at up to 300 mg/kg/day.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Pediatric patients: Silofast 4 mg is not indicated for use in pediatric patients.</p><p> Geriatric use: In double-blind, placebo-controlled, 12-week clinical studies of Silofast 4 mg, 259 (55.6%) were under 65 years of age, 207 (44.4%) patients were 65 years of age and over, while 60 (12.9%) patients were 75 years of age and over. Orthostatic hypotension was reported in 2.3% of Silofast 4 mg patients &lt; 65 years of age (1.2% for placebo), 2.9% of Silofast 4 mg patients &gt; 65 years of age (1.9% for placebo), and 5.0% of patients &gt; 75 years of age (0% for placebo). There were otherwise no significant differences in safety or effectiveness between older and younger patients.</p><p> Renal impairment: Silofast 4 mg is contra-indicated in patients with severe renal impairment (CCr &lt;30 mL/min). In patients with moderate renal impairment (CCr 30-50 mL/min), the dose should be reduced to Silofast 4 mg 4 mg once daily taken with a meal. No dosage adjustment is needed in patients with mild renal impairment (CCr 50-80 mL/min).</p><p> Hepatic impairment: Silofast 4 mg has not been studied in patients with severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh score &gt;10) and is therefore contra-indicated in these patients. No dosage adjustment is needed in patients with mild or moderate hepatic impairment.</p>', 'active', '662_1742559415.jpg', 9, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 17:16:55', '2025-03-21 17:16:55'),
(3932, 'Clobesol SA', 'Clobetasol Propionate + Salicylic Acid', 70.00, 1, 'x 10 gm tube', '10 gm tube', 'Ointment', '0.05%+3%', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Clobesol SA 10 gm</strong></h3><p>This ointment is indicated for the relief of the inflammatory manifestations of hyperkeratotic and dry corticosteroid-responsive dermatoses such as:</p><ul><li>Psoriasis</li><li>Chronic atopic dermatitis</li><li>Neurodermatitis (Lichen Simplex, Chronicus)</li><li>Lichen planus</li><li>Eczema (including nummular eczema, hand eczema, eczematous dermatitis)</li><li>Seborrheic dermatitis of the scalp</li><li>Ichthyosis vulgaris and other ichthyotic conditions</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p><strong>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</strong></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p><strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong> is a high-potency corticosteroid. Corticosteroids decrease inflammation by:</p><ul><li>Stabilizing leukocyte lysosomal membranes</li><li>Preventing the release of destructive acid hydrolases from leukocytes</li><li>Inhibiting macrophage accumulation in inflamed areas</li><li>Reducing leukocyte adhesion to capillary endothelium</li><li>Reducing capillary wall permeability and edema formation</li><li>Decreasing complement components</li><li>Antagonizing histamine activity and release of kinin from substrates</li><li>Reducing fibroblast proliferation, collagen deposition, and subsequent scar tissue formation</li></ul><p><strong>Salicylic Acid</strong> has a potent keratolytic action and a slight antiseptic action when applied topically. It:</p><ul><li>Softens and destroys the stratum corneum by increasing endogenous hydration</li><li>Causes the horny layer of the skin to swell, soften, and then desquamate</li><li>Has a caustic effect at high concentrations</li><li>Possesses weak antifungal and antibacterial activity</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage of Clobesol SA 10 gm</strong></h3><h4><strong>Adults:</strong></h4><p>Apply a thin layer of this ointment to the affected skin areas <strong>twice daily</strong> and rub in gently &amp; completely.</p><ul><li>Adequate maintenance therapy may be achieved with less frequent application.</li><li>Therapy should be discontinued when control has been achieved.</li><li>If no improvement is seen within <strong>2 weeks</strong>, reassessment of the diagnosis may be necessary.</li><li><strong>Do not exceed 50 gm/week</strong> due to the potential for suppression of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis.</li><li><strong>Not to be used with occlusive dressing.</strong></li></ul><h4><strong>Children:</strong></h4><p>Use in pediatric patients under <strong>12 years of age is not recommended.</strong></p><h3><strong>Administration of Clobesol SA 10 gm</strong></h3><p><strong>For external use only.</strong></p><h3><strong>Interaction of Clobesol SA 10 gm</strong></h3><ul><li>No reported interactions with <strong>Clobetasol Propionate ointment and cream.</strong></li><li>No known interactions of <strong>Salicylic Acid</strong> when used as indicated.</li><li><strong>Caution:</strong> Salicylic Acid may increase the absorption of other topically applied medicines. Avoid concomitant use with other topical medicines on the same area of skin.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><p>Clobetasol Propionate is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to Clobetasol Propionate</li><li><strong>Primary infected bacterial or fungal skin lesions</strong> if no anti-infective agent is used simultaneously</li><li><strong>Primary cutaneous viral infections</strong> (e.g., herpes simplex, vaccinia, varicella)</li><li><strong>Tuberculous skin lesions</strong></li><li><strong>Children under 1 year of age</strong>, including for dermatitis and diaper eruptions</li></ul><p>Salicylic Acid is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li><strong>Salicylate hypersensitivity</strong></li><li>Sensitivity to any other ingredient in the preparation</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Clobesol SA 10 gm</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Prolonged use</strong> of large amounts may result in <strong>systemic absorption</strong>, leading to hypercortisolism.</li><li>More likely to occur in <strong>infants and children</strong>, especially with <strong>occlusive dressings.</strong></li><li><strong>Local atrophy</strong> may occur with prolonged treatment.</li><li>In rare cases, <strong>psoriasis treatment with corticosteroids</strong> (or withdrawal) may trigger <strong>pustular psoriasis.</strong></li><li>Possible <strong>hypersensitivity reactions, dryness, and irritation</strong> with Salicylic Acid.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Topical corticosteroids</strong> may cause <strong>fetal abnormalities</strong> in animal studies.</li><li>The safe use of <strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong> during lactation has not been established.</li><li>Should only be used in <strong>pregnancy/lactation</strong> if the <strong>benefit outweighs the risk.</strong></li><li><strong>Avoid prolonged or extensive use during pregnancy.</strong></li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid ointment</strong> should be used <strong>with caution</strong> during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Avoid <strong>prolonged use in high concentrations</strong> or on <strong>large areas of the body.</strong></li><li>Use <strong>with caution</strong> in patients with <strong>impaired peripheral circulation or diabetes.</strong></li><li><strong>Avoid contact with broken skin, mouth, eyes, mucous membranes, and the anogenital region.</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Clobesol SA 10 gm</strong></h3><p><strong>Acute overdose</strong> is unlikely. However, chronic overuse or misuse may result in:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypercortisolism</strong> due to excessive absorption of Clobetasol Propionate</li><li><strong>Systemic salicylate poisoning</strong> (symptoms: tinnitus, dizziness, deafness) when applied over large areas for prolonged periods</li><li><strong>Salicylism</strong> in case of ingestion</li><li><strong>Plasma salicylate concentrations &gt;350 mg/L</strong> are associated with toxicity.</li><li><strong>Concentrations exceeding 700 mg/L</strong> can be fatal in adults.</li><li><strong>Single doses &lt;100 mg/kg</strong> are unlikely to cause serious poisoning.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool and dry place, protected from light.</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Class</strong></h3><p><strong>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</strong></p><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate:</strong> High-potency corticosteroid that reduces inflammation by multiple mechanisms.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid:</strong> Acts as a keratolytic and weak antiseptic, softening the skin and promoting exfoliation.</li></ul>', 'active', '76_1742560738.webp', 29, 1, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 17:38:58', '2025-05-01 18:04:24'),
(3933, 'Progut MUPS', 'Esomeprazole', 15.00, 1, 'MUPS Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'MUPS Tablet', '40mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Progut MUPS 40 mg</strong></p><p> Progut MUPS 40 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Treatment of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</li><li>Healing of erosive esophagitis</li><li>Maintenance of healing of erosive esophagitis</li><li>Symptomatic Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</li><li>Reduction of NSAID-associated gastric ulcer</li><li>H. pylori eradication (Triple therapy)</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need any further formatting changes!</p>', 'active', '1646_1742561176.webp', 17, 1, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 17:46:16', '2025-03-21 17:46:16');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3934, 'Zibac', 'Azithromycin Dihydrate', 30.00, 4, 'Tablet', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zibac 250 mg</strong></p><p> Zibac 250 mg is prescribed for infections (produced by susceptible organisms) in the upper respiratory tract, including sinusitis and pharyngitis/tonsillitis, the lower respiratory tract, including bronchitis and pneumonia, the otitis media, and the skin and soft tissue. Zibac 250 mg is recommended for the treatment of non-gonococcal urethritis and cervicitis caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> in sexually transmitted illnesses in both men and women.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Macrolides</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Zibac 250 mg belongs to the macrolide subclass of antibiotics known as azalide. In order to prevent microbial protein synthesis, Zibac 250 mg binds to the 50s ribosomal subunit of susceptible species. <em>Moraxella catarrhalis</em>, anaerobes such as <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>, <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Bordetella pertussis</em>, <em>Bordetella parapertussis</em>, <em>Borrelia burgdorferi</em>, <em>Haemophilus ducreyi</em>, and <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>, as well as gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria such as <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus spp.</em>, and others. Moreover, in vitro testing of Zibac 250 mg has shown action against <em>Campylobacter sp.</em>, <em>Toxoplasma gondii</em>, <em>Treponema pallidum</em>, <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>, <em>Legionella pneumophila</em>, and <em>Ureaplasma urealyticum</em>.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Zibac 250 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults:</strong> 500 mg once daily orally for 3 days or 500 mg once on day 1, then 250 mg once on days 2-5 for 4 days. For sexually transmitted diseases caused by <em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> in adults, the dose is 1 gm given as a single dose or 500 mg once on day 1, followed by 250 mg once daily for the next 2 days.</p><p><strong>Children:</strong></p><ul><li>10 mg/kg body weight once daily for 3 days for children over 6 months</li><li>200 mg (1 teaspoonful) for 3 days if body weight is 15-25 kg</li><li>300 mg (1½ teaspoonfuls) for 3 days if body weight is 26-35 kg</li><li>400 mg (2 teaspoonfuls) for 3 days if body weight is 36-45 kg</li><li>In typhoid fever, 500 mg (2½ teaspoonfuls) once daily for 7-10 days is given.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zibac 250 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Procedure for reinstatement after suspension:</strong></p><ol><li>Thoroughly shake the bottle to break up the powder.</li><li>Fill the bottle with boiled and cooled water to the watermark on the label.</li><li>Mix the powder and water thoroughly to combine.</li></ol><p>Take Zibac 250 mg at least one hour before or two hours after eating.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Zibac 250 mg</strong></p><p> When food and antacids are present, Zibac 250 mg absorption is lessened. Due to the potential for ergotism brought on by an interaction between Zibac 250 mg and the cytochrome P-450 system, Zibac 250 mg should be avoided in patients who are receiving ergot alkaloids. With co-administration, care should be taken since macrolides raise the plasma levels of cyclosporin and digoxin. Warfarin, Theophylline, Carbamazepine, Methylprednisolone, or Cimetidine have not been found to interact with Zibac 250 mg.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Those who are hypersensitive to Zibac 250 mg or any other macrolide antibiotic should not take Zibac 250 mg. Ergot derivatives and Zibac 250 mg should not be administered together. Those with liver disorders should not use Zibac 250 mg.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zibac 250 mg</strong></p><p> Low-frequency side effects are experienced after taking Zibac 250 mg. A therapeutic withdrawal will reverse the adverse effects, which include nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain or cramps, flatulence, diarrhea, headaches, and skin rashes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The Zibac 250 mg pregnancy category is <strong>B</strong>. Studies on animal reproduction have shown that Zibac 250 mg has no evidence of harming the fetus. Pregnant women have not been the subject of any good, controlled studies. Zibac 250 mg should only be used during pregnancy if no other suitable options are available, as research on animal reproduction is not always indicative of human response. The presence of Zibac 250 mg in breast milk is unknown. Hence, when giving Zibac 250 mg to nursing mothers, care should be taken.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> It is advised to keep an eye out for any indications of superinfection with non-susceptible organisms, such as fungi when using any antibiotic. Those with renal impairment don\'t require a dose adjustment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zibac 250 mg</strong></p><p> There is no information on Zibac 250 mg overdosage. Macrolide antibiotic overdose symptoms can include hearing loss, extreme nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. The use of gastric lavage and other supportive treatments is advised.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep dry and away from heat and light. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p>', 'active', '71_1742561252.jpg', 17, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 17:47:32', '2025-03-21 17:47:32'),
(3935, 'S-Kinase', 'Streptokinase', 4500.00, 1, 'Injection', 'Injection', 'Injection', '1.5 million', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of S-Kinase 1.5 Million Unit</strong></p><p> S-Kinase 1.5 million unit is indicated for use in the management of acute myocardial infarction, for the lysis of intracoronary thrombi, for the improvement of ventricular function, and reduction of mortality when administered by either the intravenous or intracoronary route. Earlier administration of S-Kinase 1.5 million unit is correlated with greater clinical benefit, with the greatest benefit (in terms of mortality reduction) being seen when it is administered within the first 4 hours after the onset of symptoms. The treatment should always commence within 6 hours of the onset of pain.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Fibrinolytics (Thrombolytics)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> S-Kinase 1.5 million unit forms a complex with plasminogen, which then converts plasminogen to plasmin. Plasmin breaks down clots as well as fibrinogen and other plasma proteins.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of S-Kinase 1.5 Million Unit</strong></p><p> S-Kinase 1.5 million unit can be reconstituted using 5 ml sodium chloride injection or 5% dextrose injection, directing the diluent at the side of the vacuum-packed vial rather than into the drug powder.</p><ul><li><strong>Acute Myocardial Infarction:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>IV Infusion:</strong>Vial size (IU): 1,500,000</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Total solution volume: 45 ml</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Infusion rate: Infuse 45 ml within 60 mins</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Intracoronary Infusion:</strong>Vial size (IU): 250,000</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Total solution volume: 125 ml</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">20,000 IU bolus</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\"><strong>Infusion rate:</strong>Loading dose of 10 ml at 2,000 IU/min for 60 minutes</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">60 ml/hour</li><li><strong>Pulmonary Embolism, Deep Vein Thrombosis, Arterial Thrombosis or Embolism:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">250,000 IU loading dose over 30 min</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vial size (IU): 1,500,000</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Total solution volume: 90 ml</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Infusion rate:</strong>Infuse 30 ml/hour for 30 min</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 100,000 IU/hour (Infuse 6 ml/hour)</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of S-Kinase 1.5 Million Unit</strong></p><p> There is an increased risk of hemorrhage in patients simultaneously or previously receiving anticoagulants (such as Heparin or Coumarin derivatives) or drugs that inhibit platelet formation or function (e.g., platelet aggregation inhibitors, dextrans, phenylbutazone, dipyridamole, NSAIDs).</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> S-Kinase 1.5 million unit is contraindicated in cases of:</p><ul><li>Existing or recent hemorrhage and hemorrhagic diathesis</li><li>Peptic ulcer, ulcerative colitis, diverticulitis, or visceral tumors</li><li>Recent cerebrovascular accident (within 2 months)</li><li>Severe uncontrolled hypertension</li><li>Endocarditis or pericarditis</li><li>More than 5 days and less than 12 months since previous S-Kinase 1.5 million unit therapy</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of S-Kinase 1.5 Million Unit</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General disorders:</strong> Headache, back pain, muscle pain, fever, asthenia/malaise</li><li><strong>Hemorrhage and bleeding:</strong> Bleeding at disturbed sites, gastrointestinal or genitourinary bleeding, epistaxis</li><li><strong>Cardiac complications:</strong> Hypotension, heart rhythm disorders, angina, recurrent ischemia</li><li><strong>Immune system disorders:</strong> Rash, itching, urticaria, anaphylactic reactions</li><li><strong>Respiratory disorders:</strong> Rare cases of non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy category C:</strong> It is unknown if S-Kinase 1.5 million unit is excreted in breast milk. Breastfeeding should be discontinued if treatment is required.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Re-treatment within 5 days to 12 months may be ineffective due to anti-S-Kinase antibodies.</li><li>Thrombolytic treatment should not commence before the 10th postoperative day.</li><li>Active heparinization should be neutralized with protamine sulfate before therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of S-Kinase 1.5 Million Unit</strong></p><p> If uncontrollable bleeding occurs due to overdose, infusion should be stopped immediately. Treatment includes blood loss management and, if necessary, administration of aminocaproic acid or aprotinin.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at <strong>2 to 25°C</strong>.</li><li>Reconstituted solutions should be used immediately or stored for up to <strong>24 hours at 2 to 8°C</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in children have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li>Slowly add 5 mL Sodium Chloride Injection or 5% Dextrose Injection to the vial, directing the diluent at the side of the vial.</li><li>Roll and tilt the vial gently to reconstitute (avoid shaking).</li><li>If needed, dilute further up to 50 mL in plastic containers.</li><li>Use immediately; discard any unused reconstituted drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Fibrinolytics (Thrombolytics)</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> S-Kinase 1.5 million unit forms a complex with plasminogen, converting it to plasmin, which then breaks down clots.</p>', 'active', '1791_1742561453.jpg', 17, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 17:50:53', '2025-03-21 17:50:53'),
(3936, 'Thenglate SR', 'Theophylline', 31.04, 1, 'Syrup', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '120 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Thenglate SR 400 mg</strong></p><p> Thenglate SR 400 mg is indicated for the control of acute asthma and the management of chronic asthma (both symptomatic and prophylactic treatment). It is also used for controlling nocturnal asthma and early morning wheezing, managing chronic obstructive lung disease (chronic bronchitis and emphysema), acute exacerbation of chronic obstructive lung disease, and controlling apnea of prematurity.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Bronchodilator, Thenglate SR 400 mg &amp; related drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Thenglate SR 400 mg is a bronchodilator, structurally classified as a Methylxanthine. It has two distinct actions in the airways of patients with reversible obstruction: smooth muscle relaxation and suppression of the response of the airways to stimuli. Thenglate SR 400 mg also increases the force of contraction of diaphragmatic muscles. The half-life of Thenglate SR 400 mg is influenced by various factors. In adult nonsmokers with uncomplicated asthma, the half-life ranges from 3 to 9 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Thenglate SR 400 mg</strong></p><p> The dose of Thenglate SR 400 mg must be individualized based on peak serum concentration measurements to achieve the maximum benefit with minimal risk of adverse effects. Most sustained-release preparations may be administered every 12 hours in adults, while every 8-hour administration may be necessary in some children with rapid hepatic metabolism of Thenglate SR 400 mg. The recommended maintenance dose within the accepted therapeutic range is as follows:</p><ul><li><strong>16 years or older:</strong> 10 mg/Kg/day (Do not exceed 900 mg/day)</li><li><strong>12 years-15 years:</strong> 13 mg/Kg/day</li><li><strong>9-11 years:</strong> 16 mg/Kg/day</li><li><strong>1 year-8 years:</strong> 24 mg/Kg/day</li><li><strong>6 months-1 year:</strong> 12-18 mg/Kg/day</li><li><strong>1-6 months:</strong> 10 mg/Kg/day</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Thenglate SR 400 mg</strong></p><p> Thenglate SR 400 mg should not be used concurrently with other xanthine derivatives. Its clearance is increased by barbiturates, carbamazepine, lithium, phenytoin, rifampicin, and sulphinpyrazone, necessitating a higher dose. Conversely, its clearance is reduced by allopurinol, cimetidine, ciprofloxacin, corticosteroids, erythromycin, furosemide, isoprenaline, oral contraceptives, and thiabendazole, requiring a lower dose to avoid side effects. Thenglate SR 400 mg can potentiate hypokalemia caused by beta-2-agonist therapy, steroids, diuretics, and hypoxia; thus, serum potassium levels should be monitored.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Thenglate SR 400 mg is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to Thenglate SR 400 mg or any component of the product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Thenglate SR 400 mg</strong></p><p> Side effects are rare at normal dosages but may include gastrointestinal discomfort, headache, nausea, insomnia, and hypotension. CNS stimulation and diuresis may also occur, especially in children.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> It is not known whether Thenglate SR 400 mg causes fetal harm when administered to pregnant women. Xanthines should be given to pregnant women only if clearly needed. Thenglate SR 400 mg is excreted into breast milk and may cause irritability or mild toxicity in nursing infants. Serious adverse effects in infants are unlikely unless the mother has toxic serum Thenglate SR 400 mg concentrations.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Careful consideration is required for interacting drugs and physiological conditions that can alter Thenglate SR 400 mg clearance. Dosage adjustment is required before initiating therapy, increasing the dose, and during follow-ups. The starting dose should be low and increased gradually if tolerated.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep all medicines out of reach of children. Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> The clearance of Thenglate SR 400 mg is very low in neonates. Careful dosage selection and monitoring of serum Thenglate SR 400 mg concentrations are required in pediatric patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Bronchodilator, Thenglate SR 400 mg &amp; related drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Thenglate SR 400 mg is a bronchodilator, classified as a Methylxanthine. It relaxes smooth muscles and suppresses airway responses to stimuli. Additionally, it increases the force of diaphragmatic muscle contraction. The half-life of Thenglate SR 400 mg varies, ranging from 3 to 9 hours in adult nonsmokers with uncomplicated asthma.</p>', 'active', '808_1742561596.jpg', 5, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 17:53:16', '2025-03-21 17:53:16'),
(3937, 'Xelopes', 'Omeprazole', 5.02, 1, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xelopes 20 mg</strong></p><p> Xelopes 20 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcer.</li><li>Gastric and duodenal ulcers brought on by NSAIDs.</li><li>Preventative treatment for individuals with a history of NSAID-associated ulcers.</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD).</li><li>Long-term management of acid reflux disease.</li><li>Acid-related dyspepsia.</li><li>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers.</li><li>Prevention of aspiration of acid while under general anesthesia.</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>Peptic ulcer caused by Helicobacter pylori.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Proton Pump Blocker</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Xelopes 20 mg is a benzimidazole derivative that inhibits stomach acid secretion. It works by blocking the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in the gastric parietal cells, preventing gastric acid release. After oral administration, its antisecretory effect begins within an hour, peaks in two hours, and lasts up to 72 hours. After discontinuation, secretory activity resumes over 3 to 5 days.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Xelopes 20 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Oral Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Benign gastric and duodenal ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks (duodenal ulcer) or 8 weeks (gastric ulcer). Severe cases may require 40 mg daily. Maintenance dose: 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>NSAID-associated ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, extendable for another 4 weeks if needed.</li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD):</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, extendable for another 4-8 weeks if necessary. Refractory GERD: 40 mg once daily for 8 weeks. Maintenance: 20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Long-term acid reflux management:</strong> 10-20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Acid-related dyspepsia:</strong> 10-20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration:</strong> 40 mg the evening before surgery, then 40 mg 2-6 hours before surgery.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</strong> Initially 60 mg once daily, adjusted as needed (usual range: 20-120 mg daily). If &gt;80 mg, divide into two doses.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication:</strong> 20 mg twice daily with:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Amoxicillin 500 mg + Metronidazole 400 mg (three times daily for one week), or</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clarithromycin 250 mg + Metronidazole 400 mg (twice daily for one week), or</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Amoxicillin 1 g + Clarithromycin 500 mg (twice daily for one week).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric use (Severe ulcerating reflux esophagitis, Child &gt;1 year):10-20 kg:</strong> 10-20 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>&gt;20 kg:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>IV Injection:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration:</strong> 40 mg intravenously over 5 minutes, one hour before surgery.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer, gastric ulcer, or reflux esophagitis (when oral is inappropriate):</strong> 40 mg IV once daily.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</strong> Initial IV dose of 60 mg daily, adjusted individually. If &gt;60 mg, divide into two doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Xelopes 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IV Injection:</strong> Should only be administered intravenously.</li><li><strong>Preparation:</strong> Dissolve the lyophilized powder in 10 ml of water for injection. Administer over 2-5 minutes at a max rate of 4 ml/min. Use within 4 hours.</li><li><strong>IV Infusion:</strong> Dissolve in 100 ml of 5% dextrose or saline. Use within 12 hours (saline) or 6 hours (dextrose). Do not mix with other drugs in the same infusion set.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xelopes 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>May prolong elimination of <strong>Warfarin, Phenytoin, and Diazepam</strong>, requiring dose adjustments.</li><li>No significant interaction with <strong>Theophylline, Propranolol, or Antacids</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Not for individuals with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xelopes 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Generally well tolerated.</li><li>Mild and temporary side effects: nausea, stomach cramping, paresthesia, dizziness, headache.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C (US FDA).</strong> Epidemiological studies suggest no harmful effects on pregnancy or fetal health.</li><li>Unknown if it passes into breast milk; discontinue breastfeeding if deemed necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Rule out <strong>gastric cancer</strong> before starting treatment, as symptoms may be masked.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Proton Pump Blocker</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Xelopes 20 mg inhibits stomach acid production by blocking the <strong>H+/K+ ATPase</strong> enzyme in gastric parietal cells. It begins working within an hour, peaks in two hours, and lasts up to 72 hours. Secretion resumes within 3-5 days after discontinuation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category C (US FDA).</strong> Epidemiological studies indicate no significant risks.</li><li>Unknown if it enters breast milk; discontinue breastfeeding if necessary.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '653_1742562339.jpg', 55, 1, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 18:05:39', '2025-03-21 18:05:39'),
(3938, 'Vitared', 'Vitamin C + Vitamin E + Lutein + Copper + Zinc', 10.00, 1, 'capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '60 mg+30 mg+6 mg+2 mg+15 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h2>Medicine overview</h2>', 'active', '754_1742562574.jpg', 62, 1, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 18:09:34', '2025-03-21 18:09:34'),
(3939, 'Locaine', 'Lidocaine Hydrochloride', 28.74, 1, 'Injection', '1 Injection', 'Injection', '50ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Locaine 2%</strong></p><p>Lidocaine Injection is indicated:</p><ul><li>As a local anesthetic for use in infiltration blockade and intravenous regional analgesia.</li><li>For the prevention and treatment of ventricular tachyarrhythmias.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Local &amp; Surface Anesthesia</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Lidocaine acts mainly by inhibiting sodium influx through sodium-specific ion channels in the neuronal cell membrane, particularly voltage-gated sodium channels. When sodium influx is interrupted, an action potential cannot arise, inhibiting signal conduction. The receptor site is thought to be located at the cytoplasmic (inner) portion of the sodium channel. Lidocaine binds more readily to sodium channels in an activated state, leading to a faster onset of neuronal blockade in rapidly firing neurons. This is known as state-dependent blockade.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Locaine 2%</strong></p><p>The dosage is adjusted according to the patient\'s response and site of administration.</p><p><strong>Adult Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Maximum Individual Dose:</strong> 4.5 mg/kg of body weight</li><li><strong>Maximum Total Dose:</strong> 300 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Recommended Doses for Routine Procedures:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Infiltration - Percutaneous:</strong> 5-300 mg</li><li><strong>Intravenous Regional:</strong> 50-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Peripheral Nerve Blocks:</strong>Brachial: 225-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dental: 20-100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Intercostal: 30 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Paravertebral: 30-50 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pudendal (each side): 100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Sympathetic Nerve Blocks:</strong>Cervical: 50 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Lumbar: 50-100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Central Neural Blocks:</strong>Epidural: 200-300 mg (2-3 mL/dermatome)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Thoracic: 200-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Lumbar Analgesia: 250-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Anesthesia: 200-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Caudal Obstetrical Analgesia: 200-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Surgical Anesthesia: 225-300 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Locaine 2%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Antiarrhythmic Treatment:</strong> An intravenous dose of 1 mg/kg may be given over 2 minutes and repeated after 5 minutes. A continuous infusion of 2-4 mg/min is preferred to maintain plasma levels of 2-4 mg/L.</li><li><strong>Loading Dose:</strong> To achieve a steady-state level quickly, a loading dose of approximately 1 mg/kg is usually given intravenously over 2 minutes at the outset.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Locaine 2%</strong></p><ul><li>Cimetidine can impair the metabolism of lidocaine, delaying elimination and increasing the risk of adverse reactions.</li><li>Beta-blockers (e.g., propranolol, metoprolol, nadolol) can significantly increase plasma lidocaine concentrations.</li><li>Prolongs the duration of action of suxamethonium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to amide-type anesthetics.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Locaine 2%</strong></p><p>Adverse reactions to lidocaine are rare but may result from high blood concentrations due to intravascular injection, excessive dosage, rapid absorption, or hypersensitivity.</p><ul><li><strong>Central Nervous System (CNS) Effects:</strong> Nervousness, dizziness, blurred vision, tremors, drowsiness, convulsions, unconsciousness, respiratory arrest.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Effects:</strong> Hypotension, myocardial depression, bradycardia, cardiac arrest.</li><li><strong>Allergic Reactions:</strong> Rare but may include skin lesions, urticaria, edema, or anaphylactoid reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>No evidence of harm from animal studies, but should not be used in early pregnancy unless benefits outweigh risks.</li><li>Caution is advised when administering to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Should only be used by clinicians experienced in dose-related toxicity management.</li><li>Proper dosage, technique, and emergency preparedness are essential.</li><li>Avoid use in inflamed or infected areas, as effectiveness may be reduced.</li><li>Use cautiously in patients with epilepsy, cardiac conduction impairment, bradycardia, respiratory issues, or hepatic dysfunction.</li><li>Repeated doses may cause drug accumulation, increasing blood levels and risk of toxicity.</li><li>Continuous monitoring of cardiovascular, respiratory, and neurological status is necessary after administration.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 25°C.</li><li>Keep away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Dosage in children should be reduced based on age, body weight, and physical condition.</li><li>Elderly patients may be more sensitive to systemic effects and may require dose adjustments.</li></ul>', 'active', '619_1742562922.jpg', 29, 1, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 18:15:22', '2025-03-21 18:41:18'),
(3940, 'Eprazol', 'Esomeprazole', 7.02, 1, 'capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '20mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Eprazol 20 mg</strong></p><p> Eprazol 20 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Alleviating GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn.</li><li>Treating erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Maintaining the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Eradicating Helicobacter pylori infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</li><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Eprazol 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. The majority of patients heal within 4-8 weeks. If not, an additional 4-8 weeks may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms persist, an additional 4 weeks may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication</strong> (Triple Therapy):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg once daily, adjusted individually. Treatment continues as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The recommended adult dose is 40 mg once daily via intravenous injection (not less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10-30 minutes).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Should not be administered with other medications through the same IV site.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Discontinue IV treatment once oral esomeprazole is possible.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Eprazol 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take one tablet or capsule whole, one hour before a meal.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mix contents in 15 ml of water, stir, wait 2-3 minutes, stir again, and drink within 30 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If medicine remains, add more water, stir, and drink immediately.</li><li><strong>Nasogastric/Gastric Tube Administration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mix in 15 ml of water, shake, wait 2-3 minutes, shake again, and administer within 30 minutes.</li><li><strong>IV Administration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Reconstitute with 5 ml of WFI and inject over a minimum of 3 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Use within 12 hours at room temperature. Do not refrigerate.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not use if visible particles are present.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Eprazol 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Metabolized by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 in the liver.</li><li>Does not significantly inhibit CYP1A2, 2A6, 2C9, 2D6, 2E1, or 3A4.</li><li>No clinically significant interactions with phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin.</li><li>May reduce diazepam clearance by 45%, increasing its plasma levels.</li><li>May reduce absorption of drugs dependent on stomach acidity (e.g., ketoconazole, iron salts, digoxin).</li><li>No alteration of esomeprazole pharmacokinetics with oral contraceptives, diazepam, phenytoin, or quinidine.</li><li>Co-administration with clarithromycin or amoxicillin may increase plasma levels of esomeprazole and 14-hydroxy clarithromycin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any formulation of esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Eprazol 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects: headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, dry mouth.</li><li>Long-term therapy (up to 12 months) does not show different side effects compared to short-term use.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Symptom relief does not rule out stomach cancer.</li><li>Capsules can be opened and mixed with applesauce for patients who cannot swallow them whole. Do not chew or store for later use.</li><li>Antacids can be taken alongside esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Eprazol 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Lethal doses in rats equate to 103 times the human dose based on body surface area.</li><li>Symptoms: decreased motor activity, breathing changes, tremors, ataxia, sporadic clonic convulsions.</li><li>No known antidote; treatment should be supportive and symptomatic.</li><li>Dialysis is unlikely to be effective due to protein binding.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place below 30°C.</li><li>Protect from moisture and light.</li><li>Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor that blocks H+/K+-ATPase in gastric parietal cells, reducing stomach acid production.</li><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: Peak plasma levels occur ~1.5 hours after dosing. Systemic bioavailability is ~90% with repeated dosing. Food reduces absorption by 33-53%.</li><li><strong>Distribution</strong>: 97% plasma protein bound. Volume of distribution: ~16 L.</li><li><strong>Metabolism</strong>: Extensively metabolized in the liver by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4.</li><li><strong>Excretion</strong>: Plasma half-life ~1-1.5 hours. ~80% excreted in urine, ~20% in feces as inactive metabolites.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>No sufficient studies in pregnant women.</li><li>Animal studies show no teratogenic effects.</li><li>Unknown if excreted in breast milk; breastfeeding should be discontinued if treatment is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No significant differences in response compared to younger patients, though sensitivity may vary.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mild to moderate: No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe: Maximum dose of 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Since &lt;1% is excreted unchanged in urine, no significant pharmacokinetic differences are expected.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '132_1742563199.jpg', 68, 1, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 18:19:59', '2025-03-21 18:19:59'),
(3941, 'Fondy', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 8.00, 1, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '120g', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Fondy 120 mg</strong></h3><p><strong>Allergic Rhinitis:</strong> In adults and children 12 years of age and older, <strong>Fondy 120 mg</strong> is recommended for the alleviation of <strong>seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis symptoms.</strong> Symptoms such as sneezing, rhinorrhea, lacrimation, itchy/red eyes, and itchy nose, palate, and throat can be properly addressed. Both <strong>work/activity productivity</strong> and <strong>health-related quality of life</strong> are enhanced with <strong>fexofenadine.</strong></p><p>For the treatment of <strong>chronic idiopathic urticaria</strong> symptoms in adults and children 12 years of age and older, <strong>fexofenadine</strong> is recommended. It significantly reduces <strong>wheals and pruritus.</strong></p><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p><strong>Non-sedating antihistamines</strong></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p><strong>Fondy 120 mg</strong> is a <strong>selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist</strong>. After oral administration, it is quickly absorbed, reaching peak <strong>plasma concentration in 2 to 3 hours.</strong> It does not cross the <strong>blood-brain barrier.</strong></p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Fondy 120 mg</strong></h3><h4><strong>Seasonal Allergic Rhinitis</strong></h4><p><strong>Adults and children (12 years and older):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong> 60 mg twice daily or <strong>120 mg once daily</strong> or <strong>180 mg once daily</strong></li><li><strong>Impaired renal function:</strong> 60 mg once daily</li></ul><p><strong>Children (6 to 11 years):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong> 30 mg twice daily or <strong>60 mg once daily</strong></li><li><strong>Impaired renal function:</strong> 30 mg once daily</li></ul><p><strong>Children (2 to 11 years):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Suspension:</strong> 30 mg or 5 ml twice daily</li><li><strong>Impaired renal function:</strong> 30 mg or 5 ml once daily</li></ul><h4><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong></h4><p><strong>Adults and children (12 years and older):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong> 60 mg twice daily or <strong>120 mg once daily</strong> or <strong>180 mg once daily</strong></li><li><strong>Impaired renal function:</strong> 60 mg once daily</li></ul><p><strong>Children (6 to 11 years):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong> 30 mg twice daily or <strong>60 mg once daily</strong></li><li><strong>Impaired renal function:</strong> 30 mg once daily</li></ul><p><strong>Children (6 months to 2 years):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Suspension:</strong> 15 mg or 2.5 ml (½ tsp) twice daily</li><li><strong>Impaired renal function:</strong> 15 mg or 2.5 ml (½ tsp) once daily</li></ul><p><strong>Children (2 to 11 years):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Suspension:</strong> 30 mg or 5 ml (1 tsp) twice daily</li><li><strong>Impaired renal function:</strong> 30 mg or 5 ml (1 tsp) once daily</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction of Fondy 120 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Fondy 120 mg does not undergo hepatic biotransformation</strong>, so it does not interact with liver-processed drugs.</li><li>Co-administration with <strong>erythromycin</strong> or <strong>ketoconazole</strong> increases <strong>plasma fexofenadine levels</strong> by <strong>2 to 3 times</strong>, but without QT interval effects or adverse reactions.</li><li>No interaction with <strong>omeprazole</strong>.</li><li><strong>Aluminum and magnesium hydroxide antacids</strong> reduce its <strong>bioavailability</strong> if taken <strong>15 minutes before Fondy 120 mg</strong>. <strong>(Recommended: wait 2 hours before taking an antacid.)</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to Fondy 120 mg or any of its components.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Fondy 120 mg</strong></h3><p><strong>Common:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Nervous system disorders:</strong> Headache, fatigue, dizziness</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal issues:</strong> Nausea</li></ul><p><strong>Uncommon:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Fatigue</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Rare (Post-marketing surveillance):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Immune system reactions:</strong> Angioedema, chest tightness, dyspnea, systemic anaphylaxis</li><li><strong>Mental disorders:</strong> Anxiety, nightmares, insomnia</li><li><strong>Cardiac issues:</strong> Palpitations, tachycardia</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal conditions:</strong> Diarrhea</li><li><strong>Skin conditions:</strong> Rash, urticaria, pruritus</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Category C (US FDA):</strong> Avoid use unless benefits outweigh risks.</li><li><strong>Fexofenadine is found in breast milk</strong> (from studies with Terfenadine). Not recommended for <strong>nursing mothers.</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Elderly patients &amp; those with renal or hepatic impairment:</strong> Use with caution.</li><li><strong>Patients with cardiovascular disease:</strong> Be cautious, as antihistamines can cause tachycardia and palpitations.</li><li><strong>Effects on driving &amp; machinery:</strong> No significant impact, but individuals should assess their own response.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Fondy 120 mg</strong></h3><p><strong>Symptoms:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dry mouth, drowsiness, fatigue, dizziness</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Studies have shown:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Single doses up to 800 mg</strong>, <strong>690 mg twice daily for 1 month</strong>, or <strong>240 mg daily for 1 year</strong> showed no clinically significant adverse effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Management:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>No effective hemodialysis removal.</strong></li><li><strong>Standard overdose management &amp; symptomatic treatment.</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light.</strong></li><li><strong>Keep out of children’s reach.</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Class</strong></h3><p><strong>Non-sedating antihistamines</strong></p><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>H1 histamine receptor mediates allergic reactions.</strong></li><li><strong>Fexofenadine prevents receptor activation, reducing allergic responses.</strong></li><li><strong>Does not cross the blood-brain barrier → Minimal sedation.</strong></li><li><strong>No antidopaminergic, antiserotonergic, or anticholinergic effects.</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Uses &amp; Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> Adjust dose (bioavailability &amp; half-life increased).</li><li><strong>Hepatic disease:</strong> No significant impact.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Comparable adverse effects, but increased bioavailability.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '19_1742563619.webp', 58, 1, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 18:26:59', '2025-03-21 18:26:59'),
(3942, 'Zovia Teen B', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral [A-Z teen boys preparation]', 6.00, 1, 'tablet', '30', 'Tablet', '0', 'yes', 'no', '<h2>Medicine overview</h2><p><br></p>', 'active', '1289_1742563830.jpg', 7, 1, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 18:30:30', '2025-03-21 18:30:30'),
(3943, 'Xylo Plus', 'Sodium Cromoglicate + Xylometazoline Hydrochloride', 110.00, 1, 'Nasal Drop', 'nasal drop', 'Nasal Drop', '120spray', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Xylo Plus (2.6 mg+0.0325 mg)/spray</h3><p>This Nasal Spray is indicated for the prophylaxis and treatment of allergic rhinitis (such as hay fever and perennial rhinitis) where this is accompanied by nasal congestion.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Mast Cell Stabiliser</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Xylometazoline Hydrochloride is a sympathomimetic agent with alpha-adrenergic activity. It produces vasoconstriction thus reducing nasal congestion. This enables patients suffering from colds to breathe more easily through the nose. Sodium Cromoglicate usually acts through a local effect on the nasal mucosa. It prevents release of the mediators of type-I allergic reactions, including histamine and slow reacting substance of anaphylaxis (SRS-A) from sensitized mast cells after the antigen-antibody union has taken place. The drug does not inhibit the binding of IgE and the specific antigen; instead, it suppresses the release of substances (e.g., histamine, SRS-A) in response to this reaction. The drug also inhibits Type-III (late allergic, Arthus) reactions to a lesser extent.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xylo Plus (2.6 mg+0.0325 mg)/spray</h3><p><strong>Adults (including the elderly) and children:</strong> One spray each nostril four times daily.</p><h3>Administration of Xylo Plus (2.6 mg+0.0325 mg)/spray</h3><p><strong>How to use the Nasal Spray:</strong></p><ul><li>Shake the bottle gently and remove the dust cover.</li><li>Hold the spray with your forefinger and middle finger on either side of the nozzle and your thumb underneath the bottle.</li><li>Press down until a fine spray appears. If using for the first time or if you have not used it for a week or more, press the nasal applicator several times until a fine mist comes out from the container.</li><li>Gently blow the nose to clear the nostrils.</li><li>Close one nostril and carefully insert the nasal applicator into the open nostril.</li><li>Tilt your head forward slightly and keep the spray upright.</li><li>Breathe in through your nose and while breathing in, press the white-collar of the nasal applicator firmly down once to release a spray.</li><li>Breathe out through your mouth.</li><li>Repeat the above steps in the same/other nostril for consecutive doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Cleaning:</strong> The nasal spray should be cleaned at least once a week. The procedures are as follows:</p><ul><li>Remove the dust cover.</li><li>Gently pull off the nasal applicator.</li><li>Wash the applicator and dust cover in warm water.</li><li>Shake off the excess water and leave it to dry in a normal place. Avoid applying additional heat.</li><li>Gently push the applicator back on the top of the bottle and re-fix the dust cover.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to any ingredients of the preparation.</p><h3>Side Effects of Xylo Plus (2.6 mg+0.0325 mg)/spray</h3><p>No serious side effects have been reported. Occasional irritation of the nasal mucosa may occur during the first day of use. In rare cases, with Sodium Cromoglicate solutions alone, wheezing and tightness of the chest have been reported. Because a lower dose of Xylometazoline is employed in this preparation, it causes mild side effects such as nasal irritation, dryness of the nose, sneezing, headache, insomnia, drowsiness, and palpitations.</p><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><p>As with all medicines, caution should be exercised during pregnancy, especially during the first trimester.</p><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><p>The prolonged use or abuse of decongestants in general may lead to rebound congestion or drug-induced rhinitis.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><h3>Drug Classes</h3><p>Mast Cell Stabiliser</p><h3>Mode Of Action</h3><p>Xylometazoline Hydrochloride is a sympathomimetic agent with alpha-adrenergic activity. It produces vasoconstriction, thus reducing nasal congestion. This enables patients suffering from colds to breathe more easily through the nose. Sodium Cromoglicate usually acts through a local effect on the nasal mucosa. It prevents the release of the mediators of type-I allergic reactions, including histamine and slow-reacting substances of anaphylaxis (SRS-A) from sensitized mast cells after the antigen-antibody union has taken place. The drug does not inhibit the binding of IgE and the specific antigen; instead, it suppresses the release of substances (e.g., histamine, SRS-A) in response to this reaction. The drug also inhibits Type-III (late allergic, Arthus) reactions to a lesser extent.</p>', 'active', '723_1742564104.webp', 2, 1, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 18:35:04', '2025-03-21 18:35:04');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3944, 'Protocid', 'Pantoprazole Sodium', 5.00, 1, 'tablet', '14', 'Tablet', '20mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Protocid 20 mg</strong></p><p> Protocid 20 mg is recommended when inhibiting acid secretion is therapeutically beneficial, such as:</p><ul><li>Stomach ulcer disorders</li><li>Illnesses caused by gastroesophageal reflux</li><li>Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug-induced ulcer (NSAIDs)</li><li>Helicobacter pylori eradication (in combination with antibiotics)</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Proton Pump Blocker</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Protocid 20 mg is a proton pump inhibitor that works by covalently attaching to the H+/K+ATPase enzyme system on the surface of the gastric parietal cell to decrease the final stage of gastric acid generation. Regardless of the stimulus, this effect remains for more than 24 hours, leading to the suppression of both basal and induced stomach acid secretion.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Protocid 20 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Oral:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Benign gastric ulcer:</strong> 40 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, continued for another 4 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li><strong>Gastro-esophageal reflux disease:</strong> 20-40 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, continued for another 4 weeks if not fully healed; maintenance dose is 20 mg daily, which may be increased to 40 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer:</strong> 40 mg daily in the morning for 2 weeks, continued for another 2 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer associated with Helicobacter pylori:</strong> 40 mg twice daily in combination with antimicrobial agents such as:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Amoxicillin 1 g and Clarithromycin 500 mg, both twice daily for one week, or</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clarithromycin 250 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg, both twice daily for one week.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of NSAID-associated gastric or duodenal ulcer:</strong> 20 mg daily for those requiring long-term NSAID treatment.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> Initially 80 mg once daily, adjusted according to response (elderly max. 40 mg daily); daily doses above 80 mg should be given in 2 divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>IV Injection:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer and gastric ulcer:</strong> 40 mg once daily for 7-10 days.</li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease associated with erosive esophagitis:</strong> 40 mg once daily for 7-10 days.</li><li><strong>Prevention of rebleeding in peptic ulcer:</strong> IV 80 mg, followed by 8 mg/hour infusion for 72 hours.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration:</strong> 80 mg IV every 12 hours for 24 hours, followed by 40 mg every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Long-term management of Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome and other pathological hypersecretory conditions:</strong> 80 mg IV every 12 hours, may increase to 80 mg every 8 hours if needed, with titration based on acid output.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Protocid 20 mg</strong></p><p> In clinical investigations, no significant drug interactions were found.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the formulation’s ingredients should not take Protocid 20 mg.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Protocid 20 mg</strong></p><p> Protocid 20 mg is well tolerated in both short-term and long-term use. The most common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li> Rare side effects:</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Rash</li><li>Insomnia</li><li>Hyperglycemia</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Protocid 20 mg is classified as a Category B drug by the US FDA. There are no well-controlled studies in pregnant women, so it should only be used if clearly necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Protocid 20 mg is excreted in human milk. A decision should be made to discontinue breastfeeding or stop the medication, considering the benefits to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Patients should not split, chew, or crush the Protocid 20 mg tablet.</li><li>Long-term use may cause malabsorption of Vitamin B12 or increase the risk of osteoporosis-related fractures.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Protocid 20 mg</strong></p><p> There are no recognized overdose symptoms. Due to its strong protein binding, Protocid 20 mg is not easily dialyzable. Supportive and symptomatic management is recommended.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p> <strong>Direction for IV injection:</strong></p><ul><li>Protocid 20 mg lyophilized powder should be mixed with 10 ml of 0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection and administered intravenously over 2 to 5 minutes.</li><li>Use freshly prepared solutions. The reconstituted solution can be kept for up to 4 hours at room temperature (up to 30°C).</li></ul><p><strong>IV Infusion:</strong></p><ul><li>Reconstitute Protocid 20 mg with 10 ml of 0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection, then dilute further to 100 ml using 0.9% sodium chloride, 5% dextrose, or lactated Ringer\'s injection.</li><li>The reconstituted solution can be stored for up to 4 hours at room temperature before further dilution.</li><li>The final admixed solution must be used within 24 hours and kept at room temperature (up to 30°C).</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1877_1742564279.webp', 5, 1, 'mohsinfm78@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 18:37:59', '2025-03-21 18:37:59'),
(3945, 'Provair', 'Montelukast', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Chewable Tablet', '4 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Provair 4 mg</h3><p>Provair 4 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</strong></li><li><strong>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR)</strong>: Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. Cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) are located in the human airway, and these eicosanoids bind to them. The orally active drug Provair 4 mg has a strong affinity and specificity for the CysLT1 receptor. Without any agonist activity, it inhibits LTD4\'s physiological effects at the CysLT1 receptor.</p><p><br></p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Provair 4 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for adults and adolescents 15 years of age and older: Monas 10 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 6 to 14 years of age: Monas 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 2 years to 5 years of age: Monas 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 6 months to 5 years of age: Monas 4 mg oral granules once daily. This can be administered either directly in the mouth, or mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature.</li><li><strong>Use in the pediatric patient</strong>:</li><li>The safety and efficacy of Monas have been established in adequate and well-controlled studies in pediatric patients with asthma 6 months to 14 years of age. Safety and efficacy profiles in this age group are similar to those seen in adults.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong>:</li><li>No dosage adjustment is required in patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>:</li><li>No dosage adjustment is recommended in patients with renal insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Elderly use</strong>:</li><li>The pharmacokinetic profile and the oral bioavailability of a single 10-mg oral dose of Monas are similar in elderly and younger adults. The plasma half-life of Monas is slightly longer in the elderly. No dosage adjustment in the elderly is required.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Provair 4 mg</h3><p>Provair 4 mg has been provided alongside other commonly used treatments without seeming to increase the risk of negative side effects. Studies on drug interactions show that:</p><ul><li><strong>Drugs with no significant effects on pharmacokinetics</strong>:</li><li>Theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives (norethindrone 1mg/ethinyl estradiol 35mcg), terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin did not exhibit any clinically significant effects at the recommended clinical dose of Provair 4 mg.</li><li><strong>Potential clinical monitoring</strong>:</li><li>When powerful cytochrome P450 enzyme inducers, such as phenobarbital or rifampin, are used, clinical monitoring is advised.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Extreme sensitivity</strong> to any ingredient in this medication.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Provair 4 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>:</li><li>Upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>:</li><li>Akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, asthenia, aberrant behavior, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, hemorrhage, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, oedema, seizure, odd feeling, and sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>:</li><li>Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, decreased attention, disorientation, erythema nodosum, hallucinations, hepatic problems, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, and tremor.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>:</li><li>Although the medication crosses the placenta in rats and rabbits, there are no records of its use in pregnant humans. Use during pregnancy should be considered only if absolutely necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>:</li><li>The drug is secreted in breast milk, and caution should be exercised when administering it to nursing mothers.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Acute asthma attacks</strong>:</li><li>Provair 4 mg should not be used for the reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks, including status asthmaticus. Patients should have access to the proper rescue medication.</li><li><strong>Not for use in acute exercise-induced bronchospasm</strong>:</li><li>Exercise-induced bronchospasm should not be treated with a single dose of Provair 4 mg.</li><li><strong>Aspirin sensitivity</strong>:</li><li>Patients with documented aspirin sensitivity should avoid taking aspirin or NSAIDs while using Provair 4 mg.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Provair 4 mg</h3><ul><li>The most frequent side effects are nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of overdose, supportive measures such as removing unabsorbed material from the digestive tract and clinical monitoring are recommended.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry environment, keeping it away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li><strong>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</strong></li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Provair 4 mg is a selective and orally active leukotriene receptor antagonist. It inhibits the cysteinyl leukotriene receptor (CysLT1), preventing the binding of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4). This action helps reduce inflammation and smooth muscle contraction, contributing to the relief of asthma and allergic rhinitis symptoms.</p><p><br></p><p>Let me know if you\'d like more details or have further questions!</p>', 'active', '298_1742564632.png', 8, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 18:43:52', '2025-05-04 14:17:44'),
(3946, 'Oceancal-D', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 11.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Oceancal-D</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment &amp; prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, disorders of osteogenesis. Also used as a supplement in cases of inadequate intake of Calcium in childhood diet, rickets, pregnancy &amp; lactation, and elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis, phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg (from Coral source), equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg, Vitamin D3 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li><li>Or Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg (from Coral source), equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg, Vitamin D3 200 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate Calcium in the blood is vital to a wide variety of bodily functions, and our internal biochemistry will not tolerate a deficiency even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that Calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of Osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is essential for healthy bones as it aids in Calcium absorption from the GI tract. Additionally, it stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies show that Calcium and Vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth and in Osteoporosis and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Oceancal-D</strong></p><ul><li>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night or as directed by the Physician</li><li>Dosage: One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or as directed by the physician. Taking with a full stomach ensures better absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Oceancal-D</strong></p><ul><li>Oral Calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline &amp; fluoride preparations, and a minimum 3-hour gap should be allowed between ingestion of these medications.</li><li>Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of Calcium.</li><li>Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce the metabolism of Vitamin D3.</li><li>Concomitant ingestion of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of Calcium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the components</li><li>Hypocalcaemia resulting from overdose of Vitamin D3</li><li>Hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Bone metastases</li><li>Severe renal insufficiency</li><li>Severe hypercalciuria</li><li>Renal calculi, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Oceancal-D</strong></p><ul><li>Flatulence, diarrhea, constipation, upper GI discomfort, etc., are rare manifestations.</li><li>Hypercalcaemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Should be used as directed by the physician during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with a reduction of dose may be needed.</li><li>Plasma and serum Calcium levels should be monitored in mild to moderate renal impairment and also in case of long-term use.</li><li>Patients with renal stones or a history of renal stones should also take precautions.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Oceancal-D</strong></p><ul><li>At high doses, it may result in nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc.</li><li>Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at a temperature below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>This is a Calcium and Vitamin D3 preparation where Calcium Carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The Calcium Carbonate from Coral has a chemical structure similar to the composition of human bone. Coral Calcium is similar to other sources but ensures better absorption. Vitamin D3 aids in the absorption of Calcium from the GI tract and helps maintain Calcium balance in the body.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of a physician.</li></ul>', 'active', '7_1742564760.jpg', 37, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 18:46:00', '2025-05-04 01:12:31'),
(3947, 'Domar', 'Domperidone Maleate', 3.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a revised version of the provided text with the heading in bold text for clarity:</p><p><strong>Indications of Domar 10 mg</strong></p><p>Dyspeptic symptom complex, often linked with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, may include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric sensation of fullness</li><li>Abdominal distension</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Belching</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Early satiety</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms can occur in various conditions, such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine. Additionally, in Parkinson\'s disease, it can manifest as dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting. For radiological studies, it can be used to speed up barium transit in follow-through radiological examinations.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Domar 10 mg functions as a dopamine receptor antagonist. It exerts gastroprokinetic action by inhibiting dopamine receptors found in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier means it has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Domar 10 mg</strong> For adults, the recommended dosage is 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily. Children should take 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily. When used for acute nausea and vomiting, the maximum treatment duration should not exceed 12 weeks. It is advisable to take this medication 15-30 minutes before a meal.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Domar 10 mg</strong> Domar 10 mg should be taken 15-30 minutes before meals, and if needed, before bedtime. The typical recommended oral dosage of Domperidone is as follows:</p><p>For Adults:</p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li>The maximum daily dose of Domperidone is 80 mg.</li></ul><p>For Children:</p><ul><li>2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg of body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>In cases of dyspeptic symptoms: For Adults:</p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>For Children:</p><ul><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>For acute and sub-acute conditions, especially in cases of acute nausea and vomiting: For Adults:</p><ul><li>20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>For Children:</p><ul><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>When administered rectally in the form of suppositories: For Adults (including elderly):</p><ul><li>30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li></ul><p>For Children:</p><ul><li>The maximum daily rectal dose for children weighing between 10 to 25 kg is 30 mg. The dose may be divided throughout the day if necessary.</li></ul><p>Please note that the maximum treatment period for acute nausea and vomiting is 12 weeks.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Domar 10 mg</strong> The simultaneous use of anticholinergic drugs may counteract the antidyspeptic effects of domperidone. It\'s important to note that antacids and antisecretory drugs should not be administered concurrently with domperidone as they can reduce its oral bioavailability. Domperidone primarily undergoes metabolism through the CYP3A4 enzyme. In vitro data suggest that coadministration with drugs significantly inhibiting this enzyme may lead to elevated plasma levels of domperidone. Examples of CYP3A4 inhibitors include azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, nefazodone, and others.</p><p>Theoretically, as domperidone has gastro-kinetic effects, it could potentially impact the absorption of concurrently orally administered drugs, especially those with sustained release or enteric-coated formulations. However, in patients already stabilized on medications like digoxin or paracetamol, the concurrent use of domperidone did not affect the blood levels of these drugs. Domperidone does not potentiate the action of neuroleptics, and it suppresses the unwanted peripheral effects such as digestive disorders, nausea, and vomiting caused by dopaminergic agonists like bromocriptine and L-dopa without counteracting their central properties.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong> Domar 10 mg is not to be used by patients with a known hypersensitivity to this medication or in the case of neonates. It should also be avoided in situations where gastrointestinal stimulation could be unsafe, such as in cases of gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation. Additionally, it is contraindicated in patients with a prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma).</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Domar 10 mg</strong> Side effects are uncommon, with occasional reports of transient intestinal cramps. Extrapyramidal symptoms are rare among young children and exceptionally rare in adults. These symptoms typically resolve spontaneously and completely upon discontinuing the treatment. Since the pituitary gland is situated outside the blood-brain barrier, domperidone may lead to an increase in plasma prolactin levels. In rare cases, this hyperprolactinemia can result in neuroendocrinological effects like galactorrhea and gynecomastia. In situations where the blood-brain barrier is immature (as in infants) or compromised, the possibility of neurological side effects cannot be entirely ruled out. Additionally, there have been occasional reports of rare allergic reactions, including rash and urticaria.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong> The safety of using Domar 10 mg during pregnancy has not been established; hence, its use is not recommended during pregnancy. Animal studies have not shown any teratogenic effects on the fetus. Domperidone may induce galactorrhea and enhance post-natal lactation. It is excreted in breast milk in minimal quantities, which are insufficient to be considered harmful.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong> Domar 10 mg should be employed with utmost care in children due to the potential for an increased risk of extrapyramidal reactions, which can be more pronounced in young children with an incompletely developed blood-brain barrier. Additionally, considering that domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should also be used cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Domar 10 mg</strong> Signs of an overdose can manifest as drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions, particularly in children. If an overdose occurs, it is advisable to administer activated charcoal and closely monitor the patient. The use of anticholinergic drugs, antiparkinson drugs, or antihistamines with anticholinergic properties may be beneficial in managing extrapyramidal reactions.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong> Store below 30°C, protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong> Domar 10 mg acts as a dopamine antagonist, primarily by blocking dopamine receptors located in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its gastroprokinetic effect is achieved by inhibiting dopamine receptors that influence the motility of the gastrointestinal tract. Because it has limited ability to penetrate the blood-brain barrier, Domperidone has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects. Domperidone restores normal motility and tone in the upper gastrointestinal tract, promotes gastric emptying, enhances antral and duodenal peristalsis, and regulates pylorus contraction. Additionally, it increases esophageal peristalsis and lowers esophageal sphincter pressure, preventing the regurgitation of gastric contents.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong> Use in infants: Given that the metabolic and blood-brain barrier functions are not fully developed during the first months of life, any medication should be administered to infants with great caution and under close medical supervision. Although the typical absence of neurological side effects with domperidone is primarily due to its limited penetration through the blood-brain barrier, the potential occurrence of such effects cannot be entirely ruled out in infants under 1 year of age.</p><p><strong>Use in liver disorders:</strong> Because domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should be employed cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Use in kidney disorders:</strong> In individuals with severe renal insufficiency (serum creatinine &gt;6 mg/100ml, i.e., &gt;0.6 mmol/l), the elimination half-life of domperidone increases from 7.4 to 20.8 hours, although plasma drug levels remain lower than in healthy volunteers. Since very little unchanged drug is excreted through the kidneys, it is unlikely that the dose of a single acute administration needs to be adjusted in patients with renal insufficiency. However, for repeated administration, the dosing frequency should be reduced to once or twice daily, depending on the severity of the impairment, and the dose may need to be lowered. In general, patients on prolonged therapy should undergo regular reviews.</p>', 'active', '35_1742564919.webp', 56, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 18:48:39', '2025-05-03 08:25:31'),
(3948, 'Ace', 'Paracetamol', 35.00, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '120 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ace 60 ml</strong></p><p>Ace 60 ml is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Fever</li><li>Common cold and influenza</li><li>Headache, toothache, earache, bodyache, myalgia, neuralgia</li><li>Dysmenorrhoea</li><li>Sprains, colic pain, back pain</li><li>Post-operative pain, postpartum pain</li><li>Inflammatory pain and post-vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic &amp; osteoarthritic pain and stiffness of joints.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Ace 60 ml has analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity. It is a para-aminophenol derivative that primarily acts in the CNS, increasing the pain threshold by inhibiting the COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3 enzymes involved in prostaglandin synthesis. Ace 60 ml is widely used, safe, and fast-acting, with few side effects compared to aspirin.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ace 60 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 1-2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours, up to a maximum of 4 gm (8 tablets) daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6-12 years)</strong>: ½ to 1 tablet 3 to 4 times daily. For long-term treatment, do not exceed 2.6 gm/day.</li><li><strong>Extended Release Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years</strong>: Two tablets every 6 to 8 hours (maximum of 6 tablets in 24 hours). Do not crush.</li><li><strong>Syrup/Suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children under 3 months</strong>: 10 mg/kg body weight (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced) 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>3 months to below 1 year</strong>: ½ to 1 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1-5 years</strong>: 1-2 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>6-12 years</strong>: 2-4 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 4-8 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Suppository</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 3-12 months</strong>: 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 1-5 years</strong>: 125-250 mg 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6-12 years</strong>: 250-500 mg 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; children over 12 years</strong>: 0.5-1 gm 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Paediatric Drop</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children up to 3 months</strong>: 0.5 ml (40 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>4 to 11 months</strong>: 1.0 ml (80 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1 to 2 years</strong>: 1.5 ml (120 mg). Do not exceed more than 5 doses daily for a maximum of 5 days.</li><li><strong>Ace 60 ml Tablet with Actizorb Technology</strong>: This tablet dissolves up to five times faster than standard tablets, providing a fast-acting and safe analgesic with marked antipyretic properties. It\'s suitable for patients who cannot tolerate aspirin or other analgesics.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: Take 1 to 2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not take more than 8 tablets in 24 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 7 to 11 years</strong>: Take ½-1 tablet every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not take more than 4 tablets in 24 hours. Not recommended for children under 7 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ace 60 ml</strong></p><p>Patients who have taken barbiturates, tricyclic antidepressants, and alcohol may have a diminished ability to metabolize large doses of Ace 60 ml. Alcohol can increase the hepatotoxicity of Ace 60 ml in case of overdose. Chronic use of anticonvulsants or oral steroid contraceptives may induce liver enzymes and reduce Ace 60 ml’s therapeutic effectiveness.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in known hypersensitivity to Ace 60 ml.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ace 60 ml</strong></p><p>Side effects are usually mild but can include:</p><ul><li>Haematological reactions (thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, pancytopenia, neutropenia, agranulocytosis)</li><li>Pancreatitis</li><li>Skin rashes</li><li>Other allergic reactions</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B according to USFDA. Use only if clearly needed during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Excreted in breast milk. Maternal use in normal therapeutic doses does not appear to present a risk to the nursing infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution is advised in patients with impaired kidney or liver function.</li><li>Use with care in patients taking other drugs affecting the liver.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ace 60 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong> (within the first 24 hours): Pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and abdominal pain.</li><li><strong>Liver damage</strong> may become evident 12-48 hours after ingestion.</li><li><strong>Other symptoms</strong>: Abnormalities in glucose metabolism and metabolic acidosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Ace 60 ml provides analgesic and antipyretic effects, with weak anti-inflammatory activity, by inhibiting COX enzymes involved in prostaglandin synthesis in the CNS.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>As mentioned, Ace 60 ml should only be used during pregnancy when absolutely necessary (USFDA Category B). It is excreted in breast milk but does not appear to present a risk when used at therapeutic doses.</p>', 'active', '664_1742565073.png', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 18:51:13', '2025-04-30 09:02:05'),
(3949, 'Milk Of Magnesia', 'Magnesium Hydroxide', 66.00, 1, 'x 114 ml bottle', '114 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '400 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Milk Of Magnesia 114 ml</h3><p>Milk Of Magnesia 114 ml suspension is used in:</p><ul><li><strong>Constipation</strong></li><li><strong>Heartburn</strong></li><li><strong>Gas</strong></li><li><strong>Nausea</strong></li></ul><p>It is also indicated in:</p><ul><li>Acute and long-acting constipation due to hyperacidity and peptic ulcers or stomatitis.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class:</h3><ul><li><strong>Antacid with laxative action</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology:</h3><p>Milk Of Magnesia 114 ml is popularly known as Milk of Magnesia. It is a mildly acting antacid and laxative. It is poorly and slowly absorbed and acts by its osmotic properties in the luminal fluid, causing retention of fluid in the bowel. It is useful for:</p><ul><li>Emptying the bowel prior to surgical, radiological, and colonoscopic procedures.</li><li>Helping to eliminate parasites following appropriate therapy.</li><li>Eliminating toxic material in some cases of poisoning.</li></ul><p>Milk Of Magnesia 114 ml is converted into Magnesium Chloride in the stomach without forming carbon dioxide.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration:</h3><h4>As Laxative:</h4><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 2-4 Tablespoonfuls with a full glass of water.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong>6-11 years: 1-2 Tablespoonfuls with a full glass of water.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2-5 years: 1-3 Teaspoonfuls with a full glass of water.</li></ul><h4>As Antacid:</h4><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 1-3 teaspoonfuls (5 to 15 ml) up to 4 times daily with water.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong>1-3 years: ¼ teaspoonful.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">3-6 years: ¼-½ teaspoonful.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years: ½-1 teaspoonful.</li></ul><h3>Interaction:</h3><p>Magnesium hydroxide can decrease the absorption of other drugs such as:</p><ul><li>Digoxin</li><li>Mycophenolate</li><li>Phosphate supplements (e.g., potassium phosphate)</li><li>Tetracycline antibiotics</li><li>Certain azole antifungals (ketoconazole, itraconazole)</li><li>Quinolone antibiotics (e.g., ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin)</li></ul><h3>Contraindications:</h3><p>Milk Of Magnesia 114 ml should not be administered where the use of a laxative is contraindicated. Long-term treatment is contraindicated in patients with renal failure.</p><h3>Side Effects:</h3><p>Milk Of Magnesia 114 ml, like other magnesium salts, may cause <strong>diarrhea</strong>.</p><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</h3><p>The drug should be avoided if possible in:</p><ul><li>Patients with renal and hepatic failure</li><li>Those with heart block and myocardial disease</li></ul><p>The drug may be used cautiously during pregnancy.</p><h3>Storage Conditions:</h3><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</li><li>Keep all medicines out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes:</h3><ul><li>Antacid with laxative action</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action:</h3><p>Milk Of Magnesia 114 ml is a mildly acting antacid and laxative. It is poorly and slowly absorbed, acting by its osmotic properties in the luminal fluid, which causes fluid retention in the bowel. It is useful for emptying the bowel prior to surgical, radiological, and colonoscopic procedures, and for eliminating parasites and toxic materials in some cases of poisoning. It is converted into Magnesium Chloride in the stomach without forming carbon dioxide.</p>', 'active', '1163_1742565216.png', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 18:53:36', '2025-05-04 00:05:08'),
(3950, 'Dilast', 'Torasemide', 8.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Dilast 20 mg</strong></p><p> Dilast 20 mg is indicated for the management of edema of cardiac, renal, and hepatic origin. It is also used for the management of hypertension, either as a sole therapeutic agent or in combination with other classes of antihypertensive agents.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Loop diuretics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Dilast 20 mg inhibits the Na+/K+/2Cl- carrier system (via interference of the chloride binding site) in the lumen of the thick ascending portion of the loop of Henle, resulting in a decrease in the reabsorption of sodium and chloride. This increases the rate of delivery of tubular fluid and electrolytes to the distal sites of hydrogen and potassium ion secretion, while plasma volume contraction increases aldosterone production. The increased delivery and high aldosterone levels promote sodium reabsorption at the distal tubules. By increasing the delivery of sodium to the distal renal tubule, Dilast 20 mg indirectly increases potassium excretion via the sodium-potassium exchange mechanism.</p><p>Dilast 20 mg increases the urinary excretion of sodium, chloride, and water but does not significantly alter glomerular filtration rate, renal plasma flow, or acid-base balance. Its antihypertensive effects are due to its diuretic action, reducing extracellular and plasma fluid volume, temporarily lowering blood pressure, and decreasing cardiac output.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Dilast 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Congestive heart failure:</strong> The usual initial oral dose is 10 mg or 20 mg once daily. If the diuretic response is inadequate, the dose should be titrated upward by approximately doubling until the desired diuretic response is obtained. Single doses higher than 200 mg have not been adequately studied.</li><li><strong>Chronic renal failure:</strong> The usual initial oral dose is 20 mg once daily. If the diuretic response is inadequate, the dose should be titrated upward by approximately doubling until the desired diuretic response is obtained. Single doses higher than 200 mg have not been adequately studied.</li><li><strong>Hepatic cirrhosis:</strong> The usual initial oral dose is 5 mg or 10 mg once daily, administered together with an aldosterone antagonist or a potassium-sparing diuretic. If the diuretic response is inadequate, the dose should be titrated upward by approximately doubling until the desired diuretic response is obtained. Single doses higher than 40 mg have not been adequately studied. Chronic use of any diuretic in hepatic disease has not been studied in adequate and well-controlled trials.</li><li><strong>Hypertension:</strong> The usual initial oral dose is 2.5-5 mg once daily. If the 5 mg dose does not provide adequate reduction of blood pressure within 4 to 6 weeks, the dose may be increased to 10 mg once daily. If the response to 10 mg is insufficient, an additional antihypertensive should be added to the treatment regimen.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Dilast 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Increased risk of severe hypokalemia with amphotericin B, corticosteroids, carbenoxolone, and other hypokalemia-causing medications.</li><li>Increased risk of lithium toxicity.</li><li>Increased potential for ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity with nephrotoxic or ototoxic medications (e.g., aminoglycosides).</li><li>High-dose salicylates may increase the risk of salicylate toxicity.</li><li>Increased risk of toxicity with digoxin.</li><li>Reduced diuretic effect with NSAIDs.</li><li>Increased risk of hypotension with antihypertensives.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Dilast 20 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Dilast 20 mg and other sulfonylureas. It is also contraindicated in patients who are anuric.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Dilast 20 mg</strong></p><p> Dilast 20 mg is usually well tolerated. However, some side effects may occur, including dry mouth, dizziness, tiredness, skin rash, diarrhea, constipation, nausea, vomiting, orthostatic hypotension, and muscle cramps. These side effects are usually mild and transient.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Adequate and well-controlled studies of Dilast 20 mg have not been conducted in pregnant women. Since animal reproduction studies do not always predict human response, Dilast 20 mg should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Nursing mothers:</strong> It is unknown whether Dilast 20 mg is excreted in human milk. Since many drugs are excreted in human milk, caution should be exercised when administering Dilast 20 mg to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Precautions should be taken when administering Dilast 20 mg in conditions such as diabetes, gout, hypotension, and liver failure.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Dilast 20 mg</strong></p><p> There is no human experience with overdoses of Dilast 20 mg. However, symptoms of overdose may include dehydration, hypovolemia, hypotension, and hypokalemia. Treatment should consist of fluid and electrolyte supplementation.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store Dilast 20 mg at room temperature below 30°C in a cool, dry place away from moisture and sunlight. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children:</strong> Safety and efficacy of Dilast 20 mg in children have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Loop diuretics</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Dilast 20 mg inhibits the Na+/K+/2Cl- carrier system (via interference of the chloride binding site) in the lumen of the thick ascending portion of the loop of Henle, resulting in decreased reabsorption of sodium and chloride. This leads to increased delivery of tubular fluid and electrolytes to the distal sites of hydrogen and potassium ion secretion while plasma volume contraction increases aldosterone production. The increased delivery and high aldosterone levels promote sodium reabsorption at the distal tubules. By increasing the delivery of sodium to the distal renal tubule, Dilast 20 mg indirectly increases potassium excretion via the sodium-potassium exchange mechanism.</p><p>Dilast 20 mg increases the urinary excretion of sodium, chloride, and water but does not significantly alter glomerular filtration rate, renal plasma flow, or acid-base balance. Its antihypertensive effects are due to its diuretic action, reducing extracellular and plasma fluid volume, temporarily lowering blood pressure, and decreasing cardiac output.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Safety and efficacy of Dilast 20 mg in children have not been established.</p>', 'active', '1005_1742565552.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 18:59:12', '2025-03-21 18:59:12'),
(3951, 'Caftalast', 'Alcaftadine', 430.00, 1, 'Ophthalmic Solution', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Caftalast 5 ml</strong></p><p> Caftalast 5 ml eye drops is indicated for the prevention of itching associated with allergic conjunctivitis. Caftalast 5 ml ophthalmic solution is an H1 receptor antagonist, in a sterile ophthalmic solution for topical ophthalmic use.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic Anti-allergic preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> This eye drop is a sterile topical ophthalmic solution containing Caftalast 5 ml. Caftalast 5 ml is a histamine receptor antagonist and inhibitor of the release of histamine from mast cells. It is also a potent histamine H2 and H4 receptor antagonist that has demonstrated anti-inflammatory properties. In doing so, its antihistaminic effect provides relief from itching associated with the early phase of the ocular allergic response, whereas mast cell stabilization inhibits the release of mediators such as cytokines and lipid mediators that play a role in the late-phase response of allergic conjunctivitis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Caftalast 5 ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults and children 2 years and over:</strong> Instill one drop in each eye once daily.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Caftalast 5 ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults and children 2 years and over:</strong> Instill one drop in each eye once daily.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Caftalast 5 ml is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to any component in the product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Caftalast 5 ml</strong></p><p> The most frequent ocular reactions are eye irritation, burning and/or stinging upon instillation, eye redness, and eye pruritus, as well as nasopharyngitis and headache.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> <strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women; thus, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> To minimize eye injury and contamination of the dropper tip and solution, care should be taken not to touch the eyelids or surrounding areas with the dropper tip of the bottle. Patients should be advised not to wear contact lenses if their eye is red. This preparation should not be used to treat contact lens-related irritation and should not be instilled while wearing contact lenses. Remove contact lenses prior to instilling this preparation. Lenses may be reinserted 10 minutes after administration. If more than one topical ophthalmic medicinal product is being used, each one should be administered at least 5 minutes apart.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool (15°C - 25°C) and dry place protected from light. Do not use after one month once the cap is opened.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> <strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients below the age of 2 years have not been established.</p><p> <strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> No overall differences in safety or effectiveness were observed between elderly and younger subjects.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic Anti-allergic preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> This eye drop is a sterile topical ophthalmic solution containing Caftalast 5 ml. Caftalast 5 ml is a histamine receptor antagonist and inhibitor of the release of histamine from mast cells. It is also a potent histamine H2 and H4 receptor antagonist that has demonstrated anti-inflammatory properties. In doing so, its antihistaminic effect provides relief from itching associated with the early phase of the ocular allergic response, whereas mast cell stabilization inhibits the release of mediators such as cytokines and lipid mediators that play a role in the late-phase response of allergic conjunctivitis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> <strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women; thus, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> <strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients below the age of 2 years have not been established.</p><p> <strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> No overall differences in safety or effectiveness were observed between elderly and younger subjects.</p>', 'active', '1141_1742565769.jpg', 7, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:02:49', '2025-03-21 19:02:49');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3952, 'Sartra', 'Sertraline Hydrochloride', 6.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sartra 50 mg</strong></p><p> Sartra 50 mg is indicated for Depressive illness, Obsessive-compulsive disorder, Post-traumatic stress disorder, and Panic disorder.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> SSRIs &amp; related anti-depressant drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sartra 50 mg has a potent and selective inhibitory action on CNS neuronal reuptake of 5-HT, resulting in increased 5-HT concentrations at the synaptic clefts, leading to the facilitation of its sustained activity at the postsynaptic receptor sites. This ultimately results in an improvement in depression. Reduction of serotonin turnover in the brain by Sartra 50 mg is also another contributing factor implicated in its action. Its prolonged elimination half-life offers the benefit of once-daily administration.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sartra 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Depressive illness:Adult:</strong> Initially 50 mg daily, increased if necessary by increments of 50 mg over several weeks to a maximum of 200 mg daily. The usual maintenance dose is 50 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Child and adolescent (less than 18 years):</strong> Not recommended.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Obsessive-compulsive disorder:Adult and adolescent (over 13 years):</strong> Initially 50 mg daily, increased if necessary in steps of 50 mg over several weeks. The usual dose range is 50-200 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Child (6-12 years):</strong> Initially 25 mg daily, increased to 50 mg daily after 1 week, further increased if necessary in steps of 50 mg at intervals of at least 1 week (maximum 200 mg daily).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Post-traumatic stress disorder:Adult:</strong> Initially 25 mg daily, increased after 1 week to 50 mg daily; if response is partial and if the drug is tolerated, the dose can be increased in steps of 50 mg over several weeks to a maximum of 200 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Child and adolescent (less than 18 years):</strong> Not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sartra 50 mg</strong></p><p> Potential effects of co-administration of drugs that are highly bound to plasma proteins – Since Sartra 50 mg is tightly bound to plasma protein, its administration to a patient taking another drug that is also tightly bound to protein (e.g., warfarin, digitoxin) may cause a shift in plasma concentrations, potentially resulting in an adverse effect. Conversely, adverse effects may result from the displacement of protein-bound Sartra 50 mg by other tightly bound drugs. Sartra 50 mg may also interact with drugs such as Cimetidine, CNS active drugs like Diazepam, Hypoglycemic drugs, Atenolol, etc.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Sartra 50 mg is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to Sartra 50 mg or any of the excipients in the drug.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sartra 50 mg</strong></p><p> Sartra 50 mg may cause side effects such as upset stomach, diarrhea, constipation, vomiting, dry mouth, loss of appetite, weight changes, drowsiness, dizziness, headache, pain, burning or tingling in the hands or feet, excitement, and sore throat.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Although animal studies did not provide any evidence of teratogenicity, the safety of Sartra 50 mg during human pregnancy has not been established.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Sartra 50 mg is known to be excreted in breast milk. Its effects on the nursing infant have not yet been established. If treatment with Sartra 50 mg is considered necessary, discontinuation of breastfeeding should be considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Precaution should be taken in cases of liver problems, kidney diseases, seizures, heart problems, and any allergies. This drug may cause dizziness or drowsiness, so caution should be taken with activities requiring alertness, such as driving or using machinery. Caution is advised when using this product in the elderly, as they may be more sensitive to its effects. Do not take this drug if you have taken a monoamine oxidase inhibitor in the last five weeks.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at 25°C.</p>', 'active', '875_1742565897.jpg', 56, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:04:57', '2025-03-21 19:04:57'),
(3953, 'Scabfre', 'Permethrin', 55.00, 1, 'Cream', '30 gm tube', 'Cream', '5 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Scabfre 5% w/w</strong></p><p> Scabfre 5% w/w cream is indicated for the treatment of scabies and crab lice infestations (Pediculosis). Scabies can affect everyone in the family. If one person is infected, all family members must be treated together.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Parasiticidal preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Scabfre 5% w/w induces electrochemical abnormalities across the membranes of excitable cells, leading to sensory hyperexcitability, incoordination, and prostration. Scabfre 5% w/w is rapidly metabolized in mammals by ester hydrolysis to inactive metabolites, which are excreted primarily in the urine. The principal metabolites of Scabfre 5% w/w are detectable in the urine within hours of whole-body application in healthy volunteers or scabies patients. The highest levels of excretion occur within the first 48 hours, but very low levels of metabolites may still be detectable in the urine of some individuals 28 days after treatment.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Scabfre 5% w/w</strong></p><p> <strong>Recommended duration of treatment:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years:</strong> A full tube</li><li><strong>Children aged 6-12 years:</strong> Up to ½ of a tube</li><li><strong>Children aged 1-5 years:</strong> Up to ¼ of a tube</li><li><strong>Children aged 2 months to 1 year:</strong> Up to ⅛ of a tube</li><li><strong>Children less than 2 months:</strong> Dose is not established</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Scabfre 5% w/w</strong></p><p> Before applying Scabfre 5% w/w cream, the skin should be clean, cool, and dry. Do not take a hot shower or bath before application.</p><ul><li><strong>For adults and children over 2 years:</strong> Apply the cream to the whole body from the neck down, rubbing lightly into the skin until it disappears. It is important to include all skin surfaces, such as between the fingers and toes, under the nails, and on the soles of the feet.</li><li><strong>For babies under 2 years:</strong> Apply to the face, neck, ears, and scalp as well, avoiding the area immediately around the eyes and mouth.</li></ul><p>Leave the cream on for at least 8 hours before washing it off. Reapply to any area that may be washed during the 8-hour treatment time (such as after washing the hands). If necessary, Scabfre 5% w/w cream should be used again after 7 days as per the doctor’s advice.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Scabfre 5% w/w</strong></p><p> The treatment of eczematous-like reactions with corticosteroids should be withheld before treatment with Scabfre 5% w/w, as there is a risk of exacerbating the scabies infestation by reducing the immune response to the mite.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Scabfre 5% w/w is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of pyrethroids or Scabfre 5% w/w. Nursing staff who routinely apply Scabfre 5% w/w may wear gloves to avoid any possible irritation to the hands.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Scabfre 5% w/w</strong></p><p> In scabies patients, skin discomfort, usually described as burning, stinging, or tingling, occurs in a few individuals soon after application. Other transient signs and symptoms of irritation include erythema, edema, eczema, rash, and pruritus.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> There are limited data on the use of Scabfre 5% w/w in pregnancy, which provide no indication of any risk to the fetus. Furthermore, the amount of Scabfre 5% w/w absorbed systemically following whole-body application is extremely low. The negative mutagenicity tests and very low mammalian toxicity suggest that any risk to the fetus following treatment is minimal. Despite this, Scabfre 5% w/w is not recommended for use during pregnancy.</p><p>It is not known whether Scabfre 5% w/w is excreted in human breast milk. Therefore, it should not be used in nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Scabies infestation is often accompanied by pruritus, edema, and erythema. Treatment with Scabfre 5% w/w may temporarily exacerbate these conditions. Patients should be advised to avoid contact with eyes during application and to rinse immediately with water if the cream gets into the eyes.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Scabfre 5% w/w</strong></p><p> There are no reports of overdose with Scabfre 5% w/w. However, excessive application may result in localized adverse reactions or more severe skin reactions.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool, dry place, away from light. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Parasiticidal preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Scabfre 5% w/w cream contains a pyrethroid, which is a topical scabicidal agent for the treatment of infestation with <em>Sarcoptes scabiei</em> (scabies). It acts on the nerve cell membrane to disrupt the sodium channel current, which regulates membrane polarization. Delayed repolarization and paralysis of the pests are the consequences of this disturbance.</p><p><strong>Pharmacokinetics:</strong> The only approved route of administration is topical application, and there are few human data available on systemic exposure or kinetics following skin application. Some presystemic metabolism occurs in the skin. Less than 0.5% of applied Scabfre 5% w/w is absorbed during the first 48 hours. Absorbed Scabfre 5% w/w is rapidly metabolized by ester hydrolysis, most likely in the liver, and its metabolites are excreted primarily in the urine.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> In the absence of specific studies in pregnant women, its use in pregnancy should only follow medical advice. It is not known whether Scabfre 5% w/w is excreted in human milk, so it should not be used in nursing mothers.</p>', 'active', '844_1742566045.jpg', 56, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:07:25', '2025-03-21 19:07:25'),
(3954, 'Cora-DX', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 16.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a stirp', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Cora-DX</h3><p>Cora-DX is indicated for the treatment and prevention of:</p><ul><li>Osteoporosis</li><li>Osteomalacia</li><li>Tetany</li><li>Hypoparathyroidism</li><li>Disorders of osteogenesis</li><li>Inadequate calcium intake in childhood diets</li><li>Rickets</li><li>Pregnancy and lactation</li><li>Elderly patients</li></ul><p>Other indications include:</p><ul><li>Pancreatitis</li><li>Phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</li></ul><h3>Composition</h3><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate</strong> 1500 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to <strong>elemental Calcium 600 mg</strong> and <strong>Vitamin D3 400 IU</strong> as Cholecalciferol Or</li><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate</strong> 1250 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to <strong>elemental Calcium 500 mg</strong> and <strong>Vitamin D3 200 IU</strong> as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations.</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Calcium is vital for various bodily functions, and deficiency in blood calcium is not tolerated even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that calcium is effective in preventing and treating osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is also essential for healthy bones as it helps absorb calcium from the GI tract and stimulates bone formation. The combination of calcium and vitamin D has a synergistic effect on bone growth and fracture prevention.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night, or as directed by the physician.</li><li>Dosage: One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water, or as directed by the physician. Taking it with a full stomach ensures better absorption.</li></ul><h3>Interaction</h3><ul><li>Oral calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline and fluoride preparations, so at least 3 hours should be allowed between taking these medications.</li><li>Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of calcium.</li><li>Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce metabolism of Vitamin D3.</li><li>Certain foods like spinach, cereals, and milk may reduce calcium absorption.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component</li><li>Hypocalcemia due to overdose of Vitamin D3</li><li>Hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Bone metastases</li><li>Severe renal insufficiency</li><li>Severe hypercalciuria</li><li>Renal calculi</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li>Rare manifestations include flatulence, diarrhea, constipation, and upper GI discomfort.</li><li>Hypercalcemia from prolonged use has rarely been reported.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>It should be used as directed by the physician during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring and possible dose reduction may be needed.</li><li>Plasma and serum calcium levels should be monitored in cases of mild to moderate renal impairment or with long-term use.</li><li>Patients with a history of renal stones should take precautions.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose include nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, and irritability.</li><li>Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protect from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations.</p><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Cora-DX contains calcium carbonate sourced from coral, which has a chemical structure similar to human bone and ensures better absorption. Vitamin D3 helps in the absorption of calcium from the GI tract and maintains calcium balance in the body.</p><h3>Pregnancy</h3><p>Can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the physician\'s recommendation.</p>', 'active', '639_1742566138.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:08:58', '2025-05-03 01:36:42'),
(3955, 'Tazocilin', 'Piperacillin plus Tazobactam', 1003.00, 1, 'IV Infusion', '4.5 gm vial infusion', 'IV Infusion', '4 gm+0.5 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Piperacillin and tazobactam is a combination product consisting of a penicillin-class antibacterial, piperacillin, and a β-lactamase inhibitor, tazobactam, indicated for the treatment of patients with moderate to severe infections caused by susceptible isolates of the designated bacteria in the conditions listed below:</p><ul><li><strong>Intra-abdominal Infections:</strong> Appendicitis (complicated by rupture or abscess) and peritonitis caused by β-lactamase-producing isolates of <em>Escherichia coli</em> or the following members of the <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em> group: <em>B. fragilis, B. ovatus, B. thetaiotaomicron,</em> or <em>B. vulgatus</em>.</li><li><strong>Skin and Skin Structure Infections:</strong> Uncomplicated and complicated skin and skin structure infections, including cellulitis, cutaneous abscesses, and ischemic/diabetic foot infections caused by β-lactamase-producing isolates of <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>.</li><li><strong>Female Pelvic Infections:</strong> Postpartum endometritis or pelvic inflammatory disease caused by β-lactamase-producing isolates of <em>Escherichia coli</em>.</li><li><strong>Community-acquired Pneumonia:</strong> Moderate severity pneumonia caused by β-lactamase-producing isolates of <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>.</li><li><strong>Nosocomial Pneumonia:</strong> Moderate to severe nosocomial pneumonia caused by β-lactamase-producing isolates of <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> and by piperacillin/tazobactam-susceptible <em>Acinetobacter baumanii, Haemophilus influenzae, Klebsiella pneumoniae,</em> and <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>. Nosocomial pneumonia caused by <em>P. aeruginosa</em> should be treated in combination with an aminoglycoside.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Broad-spectrum penicillins, Other beta-lactam antibiotics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Piperacillin, a broad-spectrum, semi-synthetic penicillin active against many gram-positive and gram-negative aerobic and anaerobic bacteria, exerts bactericidal activity by inhibition of both septum and cell wall synthesis. Tazobactam is a penicillanic acid sulfone derivative with β-lactamase inhibitory properties. In combination, tazobactam enhances the activity of piperacillin against β-lactamase-producing bacteria. Piperacillin and tazobactam have a wide range of activity and are active against gram-positive and gram-negative aerobic and anaerobic bacteria.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years:</strong> Piperacillin &amp; Tazobactam may be given by slow intravenous infusion (over 20-30 minutes). The usual dosage is Piperacillin 4 gm and Tazobactam 0.5 gm IV infusion given every eight hours. The total daily dose depends on the severity and localization of the infection and can vary from 2.25 gm to 4.50 gm administered every six or eight hours.</li><li><strong>In neutropenia:</strong> Recommended dose is Piperacillin 4 gm and Tazobactam 0.5 gm given every six hours in combination with an aminoglycoside.</li><li><strong>Children weighing less than 40 kg:</strong> Adjusted dose to 90 mg/kg administered every six hours, in combination with an aminoglycoside, not exceeding 4.5 gm every six hours.</li><li><strong>Children under 2 years:</strong> Not recommended due to insufficient safety data.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage</strong></p><p>Piperacillin and tazobactam should be administered by intravenous infusion over 30 minutes.</p><ul><li><strong>Adult Patients:</strong> The usual total daily dose is 3.375 g every six hours totaling 13.5 g (12.0 g piperacillin/1.5 g tazobactam). Usual treatment duration is 7-10 days.</li><li><strong>Nosocomial Pneumonia:</strong> Initial presumptive treatment should start with 4.5 g every six hours plus an aminoglycoside, totaling 18.0 g (16.0 g piperacillin/2.0 g tazobactam). Recommended duration is 7-14 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric Patients:9 months and older (weighing up to 40 kg):</strong> 100 mg piperacillin/12.5 mg tazobactam per kg every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>2 to 9 months:</strong> 80 mg piperacillin/10 mg tazobactam per kg every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Weighing over 40 kg:</strong> Should receive the adult dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> No established dosage adjustments for pediatric patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Aminoglycosides:</strong> Piperacillin may inactivate aminoglycosides; monitoring is required, especially for tobramycin.</li><li><strong>Probenecid:</strong> Prolongs the half-life of piperacillin and tazobactam; co-administration should be justified.</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants:</strong> Coagulation parameters should be frequently tested when used with heparin, oral anticoagulants, or thrombocyte function-affecting drugs.</li><li><strong>Vecuronium:</strong> May prolong neuromuscular blockade.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate:</strong> May reduce methotrexate clearance; monitoring required.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with a history of allergic reactions to penicillins, cephalosporins, or β-lactamase inhibitors.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Skin reactions:</strong> Rash, pruritus, purpura</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Diarrhea, constipation, nausea, vomiting, dyspepsia, abdominal pain</li><li><strong>General:</strong> Fever, injection site reactions, rigors</li><li><strong>Immune reactions:</strong> Anaphylaxis, hypersensitivity reactions</li><li><strong>Infections:</strong> Candidiasis, pseudomembranous colitis</li><li><strong>Metabolism disorders:</strong> Hypoglycemia</li><li><strong>Musculoskeletal:</strong> Myalgia, arthralgia</li><li><strong>Psychiatric:</strong> Insomnia</li><li><strong>Vascular disorders:</strong> Phlebitis, thrombophlebitis, hypotension, flushing</li><li><strong>Respiratory:</strong> Epistaxis</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> Piperacillin and tazobactam cross the placenta; limited human data available.</li><li>Piperacillin is excreted in low concentrations in breast milk; tazobactam\'s presence in milk is unknown. Caution is advised.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity reactions:</strong> Discontinue immediately if anaphylactic reactions occur.</li><li><strong>Severe skin reactions:</strong> Stevens-Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis may occur.</li><li><strong>Hematologic effects:</strong> Monitor blood counts during prolonged therapy.</li><li><strong>Nephrotoxicity:</strong> Increased risk in critically ill patients; renal function should be monitored.</li><li><strong>Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea:</strong> Evaluate if diarrhea occurs.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>Symptoms may include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, neuromuscular excitability, and convulsions (especially with renal failure). Treatment is supportive and symptomatic.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Established for appendicitis/peritonitis in children 2 months and older. No dosage guidance for renal impairment in children.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> No increased risk solely due to age, but dosage adjustments needed for renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Dose reduction necessary for creatinine clearance ≤40 mL/min and dialysis patients.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment needed for hepatic cirrhosis.</li><li><strong>Cystic Fibrosis:</strong> Higher incidence of fever and rash observed.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p>Reconstitute initially (2.25 g in 10 mL, 4.5 g in 20 mL) with water for injection, glucose 5%, or NaCl 0.9%, then further dilute to 50-150 mL with compatible infusion solution.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Broad-spectrum penicillins, Other beta-lactam antibiotics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Piperacillin inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis, while tazobactam enhances its activity by inhibiting beta-lactamases.</p>', 'active', '1628_1742566278.jpg', 4, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:11:18', '2025-03-21 21:24:16'),
(3956, 'Flamyd', 'Metronidazole', 35.00, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '200 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the provided information with the headings in bold text as requested:</p><p><strong>Indications of Flamyd 60 ml</strong></p><p>Flamyd 60 ml is indicated in the treatment of the following diseases:</p><ul><li>The prevention of post-operative infections due to anaerobic bacteria (particularly species of Bacteroides and anaerobic streptococci).</li><li>The treatment of septicaemia, bacteraemia, peritonitis, brain abscess, pelvic abscess, pelvic cellulitis, and post-operative wound infections caused by anaerobes.</li><li>In the treatment of urogenital trichomoniasis.</li><li>Bacterial vaginosis (also known as non-specific vaginitis).</li><li>All forms of amoebiasis (intestinal, extra-intestinal disease, and that of symptomless cyst passers).</li><li>Giardiasis.</li><li>Acute ulcerative gingivitis.</li><li>Anaerobically infected leg ulcers and pressure sores.</li><li>Acute dental infections due to anaerobic organisms.</li><li>Antibiotic-associated pseudomembranous colitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Amoebicides, Anti-diarrhoeal, Antiprotozoal</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Flamyd 60 ml is a member of the imidazole class of antibacterial drugs and is classified therapeutically as an antiprotozoal agent. The 5-nitro group of Flamyd 60 ml is reduced by anaerobes metabolically. Studies have demonstrated that the reduced form of this drug interacts with DNA and gives bactericidal action of Flamyd 60 ml.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Flamyd 60 ml</strong></p><p><em>Tablet and Suspension:</em></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Trichomoniasis</strong>Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs: 200 mg tid or 400 mg bid for 7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 7-10 yrs: 100 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 3-7 yrs: 100 mg bid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 yrs: 50 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intestinal Amoebiasis</strong>Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs: 800 mg tid for 5 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 7-10 yrs: 400 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 3-7 yrs: 200 mg qid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 yrs: 200 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Extra-intestinal &amp; Asymptomatic Amoebiasis</strong>Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs: 400-800 mg tid for 5-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 7-10 yrs: 200-400 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 3-7 yrs: 100-200 mg qid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 yrs: 100-200 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Giardiasis</strong>Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs: 2 gm once daily for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 7-10 yrs: 1 gm once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 3-7 yrs: 600-800 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 yrs: 500 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute Ulcerative Gingivitis</strong>Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs: 200 mg tid for 3 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 7-10 yrs: 100 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 3-7 yrs: 100 mg bid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 yrs: 50 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute Dental Infections</strong>Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs: 200 mg tid for 3-7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Bacterial Vaginosis</strong>Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs: 400 mg bid for 7 days or 2 gm as a single dose for 1 day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Leg Ulcers and Pressure Sores</strong>Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs: 400 mg tid for 7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Anaerobic Infections</strong>Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs: 800 mg initially, then 400 mg tid for 7 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-10 yrs: 7.5 mg/kg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Surgical Prophylaxis</strong>Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs: 400 mg tid started 24 hours before surgery for 1 day</li><li>Children 1-10 yrs: 7.5 mg/kg tid</li><li><em>Vaginal Gel:</em></li><li>The recommended dose is one applicator full of Flamyd 60 ml gel (approximately 5 grams containing approximately 37.5 mg of Flamyd 60 ml) intravaginally once or twice a day for 5 days. For once a day dosing, Flamyd 60 ml gel should be administered at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Suppository:</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Anaerobic Infections</strong>Adults: 1 g every 8 hours for 3 days, then 1 g every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 5-10 years: 500 mg every 8 hours for 3 days, then every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Surgical Prophylaxis</strong>Adults: 1 g 2 hours before surgery; up to 3 further doses of 1 g may be given every 8 hours for high-risk procedures.</li><li>Children 5-10 years: 500 mg 2 hours before surgery; up to 3 further doses of 500 mg may be given every 8 hours for high-risk procedures.</li><li><em>IV Infusion:</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Flamyd 60 ml intravenous infusion requires no dilution and should not be mixed with any other drugs prior to administration.</li><li>Adults and children over 12 years: Infuse 500 mg 8 hourly at a rate of 5 ml/minute, and a maximum of 4 g should not be exceeded during a 24-hour period.</li><li>Children under 12 years: 7.5 mg/kg body weight/day every 8 hours at a rate of 5 ml/minute.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Flamyd 60 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Disulfiram</strong>: Psychotic reactions have been reported when using Flamyd 60 ml with disulfiram.</li><li><strong>Alcohol</strong>: Alcoholic beverages and drugs containing alcohol should not be consumed during therapy and for at least one day afterwards.</li><li><strong>Oral Anticoagulant Therapy (Warfarin Type)</strong>: Potentiation of anticoagulant effects.</li><li><strong>Lithium</strong>: Plasma levels of lithium may be increased by Flamyd 60 ml.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin</strong>: Serum cyclosporin and creatinine should be closely monitored.</li><li><strong>Phenytoin or Phenobarbital</strong>: Increased elimination of Flamyd 60 ml.</li><li><strong>5-Fluorouracil</strong>: Reduced clearance of 5-fluorouracil, increasing toxicity.</li><li><strong>Busulfan</strong>: Increased plasma levels of busulfan, leading to potential toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Flamyd 60 ml is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Flamyd 60 ml or other Nitroimidazole derivatives.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Flamyd 60 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Metallic taste, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, drowsiness, and rashes may be observed during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>US FDA Pregnancy Category B.</li><li>Flamyd 60 ml is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor for adverse reactions such as peripheral or central neuropathy (paresthesia, ataxia, dizziness, convulsive seizures).</li><li>Caution in patients with hepatic encephalopathy.</li><li>Flamyd 60 ml may darken urine.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Flamyd 60 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Single oral doses up to 12 g have been reported. Symptoms include vomiting, ataxia, and slight disorientation.</li><li>There is no specific antidote for overdoses. Symptomatic and supportive treatment should be initiated in case of overdose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C. Keep protected from light. Keep out of reach of children. Do not use beyond the expiry date.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Amoebicides, Anti-diarrhoeal, Antiprotozoal</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Flamyd 60 ml is a member of the imidazole class of antibacterial drugs and exerts bactericidal action by interacting with DNA in anaerobes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>US FDA Pregnancy Category B. Use only if clearly needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: Caution should be exercised in patients with hepatic encephalopathy.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: No dosage reduction needed unless undergoing hemodialysis, in which case Flamyd 60 ml should be re-administered immediately after dialysis.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further details or clarification!</p>', 'active', '348_1742566341.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:12:21', '2025-05-03 10:44:47'),
(3957, 'Sebivo', 'Telbivudine', 312.50, 14, 'Tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sebivo 600 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Chronic Hepatitis B:</strong> Sebivo 600 mg is indicated for the treatment of chronic hepatitis B in adult patients with evidence of viral replication and either persistent elevations in serum aminotransferases (ALT or AST) or histologically active disease.</p><p>The following points should be considered when initiating therapy with Sebivo 600 mg:</p><ul><li>This indication is based on virologic, serologic, biochemical, and histologic responses in nucleoside treatment-naïve adult patients with HBeAg-positive and HBeAg-negative chronic hepatitis B with compensated liver disease.</li><li>For HBeAg-positive patients, Sebivo 600 mg should only be initiated in patients with HBV DNA less than 9 log10 copies per mL and ALT greater than or equal to 2× Upper Limit of Normal (ULN) prior to treatment.</li><li>For HBeAg-negative patients, Sebivo 600 mg should only be initiated in patients with HBV DNA less than 7 log10 copies per mL prior to treatment.</li><li>On-treatment response should guide continued therapy.</li></ul><p>Sebivo 600 mg has not been evaluated in patients co-infected with HIV, HCV, or HDV. It has also not been studied in liver transplant recipients, patients with decompensated liver disease, or those with established nucleoside analog reverse transcriptase inhibitor-resistant hepatitis B virus infection. The safety and efficacy of Sebivo 600 mg have not been evaluated in Black/African American or Hispanic patients.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Hepatic viral infections (Hepatitis B)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sebivo 600 mg is a synthetic thymidine nucleoside analogue with activity against hepatitis B virus (HBV). It is the unmodified β–L enantiomer of the naturally occurring nucleoside thymidine. It undergoes phosphorylation via interaction with cellular kinases to form the active metabolite, Sebivo 600 mg 5\'-triphosphate.</p><p>Sebivo 600 mg 5\'-triphosphate inhibits HBV DNA polymerase (reverse transcriptase) by competing with the natural substrate, thymidine 5\'-triphosphate. This leads to chain termination of DNA synthesis, thereby inhibiting viral replication. Incorporation of Sebivo 600 mg 5\'-triphosphate into viral DNA also causes DNA chain termination, resulting in inhibition of HBV replication.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sebivo 600 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults and Adolescents (16 years of age and older):</strong> Due to higher rates of resistance that may develop with longer-term treatment among patients with incomplete viral suppression, treatment should only be initiated if pre-treatment HBV DNA and ALT measurements are known, in the following patient populations:</p><ul><li><strong>For HBeAg-positive patients:</strong> HBV DNA should be less than 9 log10 copies per mL, and ALT should be greater than or equal to 2× ULN prior to treatment.</li><li><strong>For HBeAg-negative patients:</strong> HBV DNA should be less than 7 log10 copies per mL prior to treatment.</li></ul><p>HBV DNA levels should be monitored at 24 weeks of treatment to ensure complete viral suppression (HBV DNA less than 300 copies per mL). Alternate therapy should be initiated for patients who have detectable HBV DNA after 24 weeks of treatment. Optimal therapy should be guided by further resistance testing.</p><p><strong>Recommended dosage:</strong> 600 mg once daily, taken orally, with or without food. Sebivo 600 mg Oral Solution (30 mL) may be considered for patients who have difficulty swallowing tablets.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Sebivo 600 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Altered plasma concentration with drugs that affect renal function (e.g., aminoglycosides, loop diuretics, platinum compounds, vancomycin, amphotericin B).</li><li>May increase the risk of myopathy with other drugs associated with myopathy (e.g., azole antifungals, ciclosporin, corticosteroids, erythromycin, fibrates, HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors, penicillamine, zidovudine).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity</li><li>Combination with pegylated interferon alfa-2a is contraindicated due to increased risk of peripheral neuropathy.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Sebivo 600 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Cough, dizziness, fatigue, GI effects (e.g., abdominal pain, diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, dyspepsia), rash, arthralgia, myalgia, myopathy, malaise, back pain, nasopharyngitis, headache, flu or flu-like symptoms, insomnia.</li><li>Increased serum amylase, lipase, creatine phosphokinase, alanine aminotransferase levels.</li><li>Peripheral neuropathy, rhabdomyolysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> <strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> Sebivo 600 mg is not teratogenic and has shown no adverse effects in developing embryos and fetuses in preclinical studies. Studies in pregnant rats and rabbits showed that Sebivo 600 mg crosses the placenta.</p><p>Developmental toxicity studies revealed no evidence of harm to the fetus in rats and rabbits at doses up to 1000 mg/kg/day, providing exposure levels 6- and 37-times higher, respectively, than those observed with the 600 mg per day dose in humans.</p><p>There are no adequate and well-controlled trials of Tyzeka in pregnant women. Because animal reproductive toxicity studies are not always predictive of human response, Tyzeka should be used during pregnancy only if potential benefits outweigh the risks.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Severe acute exacerbations of hepatitis B: Monitor hepatic function if therapy is discontinued.</li><li>Risk of lactic acidosis, severe hepatomegaly with steatosis, myopathy, and rhabdomyolysis. Discontinue if myopathy is diagnosed.</li><li>Increased risk of peripheral neuropathy when combined with pegylated interferon α-2a.</li><li>Use with caution in decompensated cirrhosis, renal impairment, patients on hemodialysis, liver transplant recipients, or those under immunosuppressant therapy.</li><li>May impair the ability to drive or operate machinery.</li><li>Not recommended for children under 16 years of age.</li><li>Use with caution in elderly patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store Sebivo 600 mg Tablets and Oral Solution in the original bottle at room temperature (15° to 30°C).</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Hepatic viral infections (Hepatitis B)</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Sebivo 600 mg is a synthetic thymidine nucleoside analogue with activity against HBV. It undergoes phosphorylation to form Sebivo 600 mg 5\'-triphosphate, which inhibits HBV DNA polymerase, leading to viral DNA chain termination and suppression of HBV replication.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> <strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> Not teratogenic, with no adverse effects on developing embryos or fetuses in preclinical studies. However, due to a lack of well-controlled trials in pregnant women, it should only be used if the potential benefits outweigh the risks.</p>', 'active', '85_1742566443.jpg', 40, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:14:03', '2025-03-21 19:14:03'),
(3958, 'Vergon', 'Prochlorperazine Maleate', 0.65, 40, 'tablet', '40 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Vergon 5 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Control of severe nausea and vomiting</strong> caused by radiation therapy, cancer chemotherapy, surgery, and other conditions.</li><li><strong>Relieving nausea, vomiting, and attacks of dizziness or spinning sensations (vertigo)</strong> associated with Meniere\'s disease and other inner ear disorders.</li><li><strong>Treatment of psychotic illness</strong> such as schizophrenia (hallucinations and hostility).</li><li><strong>Acute mania.</strong></li><li><strong>Short-term treatment of generalized non-psychotic anxiety.</strong></li></ul><h3>Composition</h3><ul><li><strong>Vergon 5 mg Maleate Tablet</strong>: Each tablet contains Vergon 5 mg maleate.</li><li><strong>Vergon 5 mg Mesilate Syrup</strong>: Each 5 ml syrup contains Vergon 5 mg maleate.</li><li><strong>Vergon 5 mg Mesilate Injection</strong>: Each 1 ml ampoule contains Vergon 5 mg mesilate, 12.5 mg.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Anti-vertigo drugs</li><li>Anti-emetic drugs</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Vergon 5 mg is a potent phenothiazine antipsychotic, a derivative of the piperazine group, primarily used as an antiemetic and to treat vertigo. It exerts effects at all levels of the nervous system. Vergon 5 mg:</p><ul><li>Antagonizes postsynaptic dopamine D1 receptors, which activate adenylate cyclase and the synthesis of cAMP.</li><li>Antagonizes D2 receptors that do not activate adenylate cyclase.</li><li>Inhibits pre- and post-synaptic adrenoreceptors, acetylcholine, serotonin, and histamine receptors. These various pharmacodynamic actions combine to produce antipsychotic, central antiemetic, and sedative effects.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Antiemetic:</strong></li><li><strong>Children (not recommended for children &lt;10 kg or &lt;2 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">10-14 kg: 2.5 mg every 12-24 hours as needed (max: 7.5 mg/day).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">15-18 kg: 2.5 mg every 8-12 hours as needed (max: 10 mg/day).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">19-39 kg: 2.5 mg every 8 hours or 5 mg every 12 hours as needed (max: 15 mg/day).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 5-10 mg 3-4 times/day (max: 40 mg/day).</li><li><strong>Antipsychotic:</strong></li><li><strong>Children (2-12 years, not recommended for children &lt;10 kg or &lt;2 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2.5 mg 2-3 times/day, increasing dosage as needed to a maximum daily dose of 20 mg for 2-5 years and 25 mg for 6-12 years.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 5-10 mg 3-4 times/day; doses up to 150 mg/day may be required for severe disturbances.</li><li><strong>Nonpsychotic anxiety:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: Usual dose: 15-20 mg/day in divided doses (max: 20 mg/day for no longer than 12 weeks).</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Initial dose of 2.5-5 mg 1-2 times/day; titrate dose every 4-7 days by 2.5-5 mg/day (max: 75 mg/day).</li></ul><p>Vergon 5 mg may be administered without regard to meals.</p><h3>Interaction of Vergon 5 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Increased CNS depressant effects</strong> with alcohol, barbiturates, and other sedatives.</li><li><strong>Antacids, antiparkinson\'s drugs, and lithium</strong> may interfere with Vergon 5 mg absorption.</li><li>Vergon 5 mg may alter the plasma concentrations of <strong>Propranolol</strong> and <strong>Phenobarbital</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Vergon 5 mg or any of its components.</li><li>Severe CNS depression; coma.</li><li>Not recommended for children &lt;2 years of age or &lt;10 kg.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Vergon 5 mg</h3><ul><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Jaw, neck, and back muscle spasms</li><li>Fine, worm-like tongue movements</li><li>Rhythmic face, mouth, or jaw movements</li><li>Slow or difficult speech</li><li>Difficulty swallowing</li><li>Restlessness and pacing</li><li>Tremors, shuffling walk</li><li>Skin rash</li><li>Yellowing of the skin or eyes</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>No reported evidence of adverse effects during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Caution should be taken when performing tasks that require alertness, such as driving or using machinery.</li><li>Alcohol may cause extreme drowsiness.</li><li>This medication may increase sensitivity to sunlight; prolonged sun exposure should be avoided. Use sunscreen and protective clothing when exposed to the sun.</li><li>Vergon 5 mg can reduce sweating, making one more susceptible to heat stroke.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose include deep sleep, coma, extrapyramidal symptoms, abnormal involuntary muscle movements, and hypotension.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light and moisture.</li></ul>', 'active', '511_1742566488.png', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:14:48', '2025-05-04 22:52:33'),
(3959, 'Kalcoral-DX', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 16.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Kalcoral-DX</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment &amp; prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, disorders of osteogenesis. Also used as a supplement in case of inadequate intake of Calcium in childhood diet, rickets, pregnancy &amp; lactation, elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis, phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate</strong> 1500 mg (from Coral source), equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin D3</strong> 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li><li>Or</li><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate</strong> 1250 mg (from Coral source), equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin D3</strong> 200 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate Calcium in the blood is vital to a wide variety of bodily functions, and our internal biochemistry will not tolerate a deficiency even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that Calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of Osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is also essential for healthy bones as it aids in Calcium absorption from the GI tract. Additionally, it stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies show that Calcium and Vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth as well as in Osteoporosis and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Kalcoral-DX</strong></p><p>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night or as directed by the physician.</p><p>Dosage: One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or as directed by the physician. Taking it on a full stomach ensures better absorption.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Kalcoral-DX</strong></p><p>Oral Calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline &amp; fluoride preparations, and a minimum 3-hour gap should be allowed between ingestion of these medications.</p><p>Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of Calcium.</p><p>Phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids may induce metabolism of Vitamin D3.</p><p>Concomitant ingestion of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of Calcium.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to any of the components, hypocalcemia resulting from overdose of Vitamin D3, hyperparathyroidism, bone metastases, severe renal insufficiency, severe hypercalciuria, renal calculi, etc.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Kalcoral-DX</strong></p><p>Flatulence, diarrhea, constipation, upper GI discomfort, etc. are rare manifestations. Hypercalcemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It should be used as directed by the physician during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with a reduction of dose may be needed.</p><p>Plasma and serum Calcium levels should be monitored in mild to moderate renal impairment and also in case of long-term use.</p><p>Patients with renal stones or with such a history should also take precautions.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Kalcoral-DX</strong></p><p>At high doses, it may result in nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc.</p><p>Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This is a Calcium and Vitamin D3 preparation where Calcium Carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The Calcium Carbonate from Coral has a chemical structure similar to the composition of human bone. Coral Calcium ensures better absorption compared to other sources. Vitamin D3 aids in the absorption of Calcium from the GI tract and helps to maintain Calcium balance in the body.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of the physician.</p>', 'active', '174_1742566588.webp', 17, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:16:28', '2025-05-03 23:04:29');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3960, 'Velatas', 'Sofosbuvir + Velpatasvir', 1000.00, 7, 'Tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '400 mg plus 100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Velatas 400 mg+100 mg</strong></p><p> Sofosbuvir and Velpatasvir combination is indicated for the treatment of adult patients with chronic hepatitis C virus (HCV) genotype 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, or 6 infection:</p><ul><li>Without cirrhosis or with compensated cirrhosis</li><li>With decompensated cirrhosis for use in combination with ribavirin</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Hepatic viral infections (Hepatitis C)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> It is a fixed-dose combination tablet containing sofosbuvir and velpatasvir for oral administration. Sofosbuvir is a nucleotide analog HCV NS5B polymerase inhibitor, and velpatasvir is an NS5A inhibitor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Velatas 400 mg+100 mg</strong></p><p> The recommended dosage is one tablet (400 mg of Sofosbuvir and 100 mg of Velpatasvir) taken orally once daily.</p><p><strong>Recommended treatment regimen:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Patients without cirrhosis and patients with compensated cirrhosis (Child-Pugh A):</strong> One tablet once daily for 12 weeks.</li><li><strong>Patients with decompensated cirrhosis (Child-Pugh B or C):</strong> One tablet once daily and Ribavirin for 12 weeks. The recommended dosage of Ribavirin is based on body weight (1000 mg/day for patients &lt; 75 kg and 1200 mg/day for ≥ 75 kg, in two divided doses per day).</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Velatas 400 mg+100 mg</strong></p><p> Same as above.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Velatas 400 mg+100 mg</strong></p><p> Drugs that may decrease the concentrations of sofosbuvir and/or velpatasvir:</p><ul><li>Antacids, H2-receptor antagonists, Proton-pump inhibitors, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Coadministration is not recommended with:</strong></p><ul><li>Topotecan, Carbamazepine, Phenytoin, Phenobarbital, Oxcarbazepine, Rifabutin, Rifampin, Rifapentine, Efavirenz, Tipranavir, Ritonavir, Hypericum perforatum.</li><li>Coadministration of Sofosbuvir and Velpatasvir with Rosuvastatin and Atorvastatin may significantly increase their concentrations.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Sofosbuvir, Velpatasvir, and Ribavirin combination regimen is contraindicated in patients for whom Ribavirin is contraindicated.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Velatas 400 mg+100 mg</strong></p><p> The most common side effects include headache and tiredness. Treatment may result in slowing of the heart rate along with other symptoms when taken with amiodarone (a medicine used to treat certain heart problems).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> No adequate human data are available to establish whether or not Sofosbuvir and Velpatasvir combination poses a risk to pregnancy outcomes. If administered with Ribavirin, the combination regimen is contraindicated in pregnant women and in men whose female partner is pregnant or planning to conceive in the next six months.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Serious symptomatic bradycardia may occur in patients taking amiodarone, particularly in those also receiving beta blockers or with underlying cardiac comorbidities and/or advanced liver disease.</li><li>Coadministration of amiodarone with Sofosbuvir and Velpatasvir is not recommended.</li><li>In patients without alternative viable treatment options, cardiac monitoring is recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Velatas 400 mg+100 mg</strong></p><p> If overdose occurs, the patient must be monitored for evidence of toxicity. Treatment includes monitoring of vital signs as well as observation of the clinical status of the patient.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place (preferably below 30°C). Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> <strong>Renal impairment patients:</strong> No dosage recommendation can be given for patients with severe renal impairment (eGFR ≤30 mL/min/1.73 m²) or with ESRD due to higher exposures of the predominant sofosbuvir metabolite.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Hepatic viral infections (Hepatitis C)</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> It is a fixed-dose combination tablet containing sofosbuvir and velpatasvir for oral administration. Sofosbuvir is a nucleotide analog HCV NS5B polymerase inhibitor, and velpatasvir is an NS5A inhibitor.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> No adequate human data are available to establish whether or not Sofosbuvir and Velpatasvir combination poses a risk to pregnancy outcomes. If administered with Ribavirin, the combination regimen is contraindicated in pregnant women and in men whose female partner is pregnant or planning to conceive in the next six months.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Same as renal impairment patients.</p>', 'active', '170_1742566629.jpg', 1, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:17:09', '2025-03-21 19:17:09'),
(3961, 'Xorubin', 'Doxorubicin Hydrochloride', 300.00, 1, 'IV Infusion', '10 mg vial infusion', 'IV Infusion', '2 mg per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xorubin 2 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Doxorubicin is an anthracycline topoisomerase II inhibitor indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Ovarian cancer</strong>: After failure of platinum-based chemotherapy.</li><li><strong>AIDS-related Kaposi’s Sarcoma</strong>: After failure of prior systemic chemotherapy or intolerance to such therapy.</li><li><strong>Multiple Myeloma</strong>: In combination with bortezomib in patients who have not previously received bortezomib and have received at least one prior therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Doxorubicin is a cytotoxic anthracycline antibiotic. The cytotoxic action results from its binding to DNA and inhibition of nucleic acid synthesis. Doxorubicin has been shown to produce regression in a variety of disseminated malignancies.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xorubin 2 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Administer Doxorubicin at an initial rate of 1 mg/min to minimize the risk of infusion reactions. If no infusion-related reactions occur, increase the rate of infusion to complete administration over 1 hour. Do not administer as bolus injection or undiluted solution.</p><ul><li><strong>Ovarian cancer</strong>: 50 mg/m² IV every 4 weeks</li><li><strong>AIDS-related Kaposi’s Sarcoma</strong>: 20 mg/m² IV every 3 weeks</li><li><strong>Multiple Myeloma</strong>: 30 mg/m² IV on day 4 following bortezomib</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xorubin 2 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Doxorubicin interacts with a number of other drugs, e.g., antibiotics (aminoglycosides), steroids, aminophylline, and propranolol.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Cardiac disease, neonates, pregnancy and lactation, prior irradiation to mediastinum, IM/SC administration, severe myelosuppression due to previous treatment with antitumor agents or radiotherapy.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xorubin 2 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Leucopenia, thrombocytopenia, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea. Rarely, facial flushing, rash, alopecia, blurred vision, headache, seizures, paresthesia, confusion, malaise, lethargy, skin pigmentation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Category D: There is positive evidence of human fetal risk, but the benefits from use in pregnant women may be acceptable despite the risk (e.g., if the drug is needed in a life-threatening situation or for a serious disease for which safer drugs cannot be used or are ineffective).</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Elderly, children, hepatic impairment. Monitor blood counts and ECG.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xorubin 2 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Acute overdosage may increase the toxic effects of mucositis, leukopenia, and thrombocytopenia. Treatment includes hospitalization of the severely myelosuppressed patient, antimicrobials, platelet transfusions, and symptomatic treatment of mucositis. Use of hematopoietic growth factor (G-CSF, GM-CSF) may be considered. Cumulative dosage increases the risk of cardiomyopathy and resultant congestive heart failure, which may be managed with digitalis preparations, diuretics, and afterload reducers such as ACE inhibitors.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Powder for injection: Store at 15-30°C.</p><p> Solution for injection &amp; liposomal formulations: Refrigerate at 2-8°C. Do not freeze.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Serum-bilirubin: 12-30 mcg/ml: Half the normal dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Serum-bilirubin: &gt;30 mcg/ml: Quarter of the usual dose</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Doxorubicin is a cytotoxic anthracycline antibiotic. The cytotoxic action results from its binding to DNA and inhibition of nucleic acid synthesis. Doxorubicin has been shown to produce regression in a variety of disseminated malignancies.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Category D: There is positive evidence of human fetal risk, but the benefits from use in pregnant women may be acceptable despite the risk (e.g., if the drug is needed in a life-threatening situation or for a serious disease for which safer drugs cannot be used or are ineffective).</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Hepatic Impairment:</p><ul><li>Serum-bilirubin: 12-30 mcg/ml: Half the normal dose</li><li>Serum-bilirubin: &gt;30 mcg/ml: Quarter of the usual dose</li></ul>', 'active', '966_1742566838.jpg', 55, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:20:38', '2025-03-21 19:20:38'),
(3962, 'Sedron', 'Risedronate Sodium', 35.25, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '35 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sedron 35 mg</strong></p><p> This is in a group of medicines called bisphosphonates. It alters the cycle of bone formation and breakdown in the body. It slows bone loss while increasing bone mass, which may prevent bone fractures. Risedronate is a prescription medicine used:</p><ul><li>To prevent and treat osteoporosis in postmenopausal women.</li><li>To increase bone mass in men with osteoporosis.</li><li>To prevent and treat osteoporosis in men and women that is caused by treatment with steroid medicines such as prednisone.</li><li>To treat Paget’s disease of bone in men and women.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Bisphosphonate preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Risedronate has an affinity for hydroxyapatite crystals in bone and acts as an antiresorptive agent. At the cellular level, Risedronate inhibits osteoclasts. The osteoclasts adhere normally to the bone surface, but show evidence of reduced active resorption.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Sedron 35 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Treatment of postmenopausal osteoporosis</strong> to reduce the risk of vertebral fractures.</li><li><strong>Treatment of established postmenopausal osteoporosis</strong> to reduce the risk of hip fractures: The recommended dose is Sedron 35 mg 5 mg once daily or Sedron 35 mg 35 mg once weekly on the same day each week or Sedron 35 mg 150 mg once monthly.</li><li><strong>Prevention of osteoporosis</strong> in postmenopausal women with increased risk of osteoporosis: The recommended dose is Sedron 35 mg 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>To maintain or increase bone mass in postmenopausal women</strong> undergoing long-term (more than 3 months) systemic corticosteroid treatment at doses 7.5 mg/day prednisone or equivalent. The recommended dose is Sedron 35 mg 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Treatment of osteoporosis in men at high risk of fractures</strong>: The recommended dose is Sedron 35 mg 35 mg once weekly. The tablet should be taken on the same day each week.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Sedron 35 mg</strong></p><p> The absorption of Sedron 35 mg is affected by food, thus to ensure adequate absorption patients should take Risedronate tablets at least 30 minutes before the first food, other medicinal product, or drink (other than plain water) of the day. In the particular instances that before breakfast dosing is not practical, Risedronate 5 mg tablet can be taken between meals or in the evening at the same time every day, with strict adherence to the following instructions, to ensure Sedron 35 mg tablet is taken on an empty stomach:</p><ul><li><strong>Between meals</strong>: Risedronate Sodium tablet should be taken at least 2 hours before and at least 2 hours after any food, medicinal product, or drink (other than plain water).</li><li><strong>In the evening</strong>: Sedron 35 mg tablet should be taken at least 2 hours after the last food, medicinal product, or drink (other than plain water) of the day.</li><li><strong>If a dose is missed</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Risedronate 5 mg tablet: If an occasional dose is missed, Risedronate 5 mg tablet can be taken before breakfast, between meals, or in the evening according to the instructions above.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Risedronate 35 mg tablet: Patients should be instructed that if a dose is missed, just take one Risedronate 35 mg tablet on the morning after remembering. Patients should then return to taking one tablet once a week on the day the tablet is normally taken. Two tablets should not be taken on the same day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Risedronate 150 mg tablet: Patient should be instructed that if a dose is missed and the next month’s scheduled dose is more than 7 days away, they should take the missed tablet in the morning after the day it is remembered. Patients should then return to taking one tablet once a month as normally taken.</li></ul><p>The tablet must be swallowed whole and not sucked or chewed. To aid delivery of the tablet to the stomach, Risedronate tablet is to be taken while in an upright position with a glass of plain water (120 ml or more). Patients should not lie down for 30 minutes after taking the tablet. Supplemental calcium and vitamin D should be considered if the dietary intake is inadequate.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Sedron 35 mg</strong></p><p> Please tell your doctor if you are taking or have recently taken any other medicines, including medicines obtained without a prescription, especially aspirin or other NSAIDs. Antacids, supplements, or medicines that contain aluminum, calcium, magnesium, or other minerals can interfere with how your body absorbs Sedron 35 mg. If you use these other medicines, do not take them for at least 30 minutes after taking a Sedron 35 mg tablet.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Sedron 35 mg tablet is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Sedron 35 mg and in patients with hypocalcaemia, severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance lower than 30 ml/min), during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sedron 35 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Serious side effects</strong>: Chest pain; difficulty or pain when swallowing; pain or burning under the ribs or in the back; new or worsening heartburn; severe joint, bone, or muscle pain; or jaw pain, numbness, or swelling.</li><li><strong>Less serious side effects</strong>: Mild heartburn or stomach upset; diarrhea, gas, or constipation; mild joint or back pain; headache.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Sedron 35 mg is meant for use only in postmenopausal women. Therefore, Sedron 35 mg should not be taken during pregnancy. If you take it by accident during pregnancy, stop taking it straight away and talk to your doctor.</li><li><strong>Breast-feeding</strong>: Sedron 35 mg is meant for use only in postmenopausal women. Therefore, breast-feeding women should not take this medicine. If you take it by accident during breast-feeding, stop taking it straight away and talk to your doctor.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Take special care with Risedronate if you:</p><ul><li>Have low blood calcium (hypocalcemia)</li><li>Cannot sit or stand up for 30 minutes</li><li>Have kidneys that work poorly</li><li>Have an allergy to Sedron 35 mg</li></ul><p>Before taking Risedronate, talk to your doctor if you:</p><ul><li>Are pregnant or may become pregnant. We do not know if Sedron 35 mg can harm your unborn child.</li><li>Are breast-feeding or plan to breast-feed. We do not know if Sedron 35 mg can pass through your milk and if it can harm your baby.</li><li>Have kidney problems. Sedron 35 mg may not be right for you.</li><li>Risedronate sodium is not intended for use in children.</li></ul><p>During treatment, if you experience an allergic reaction (such as swelling of the face, tongue, or throat, difficulty in breathing or swallowing, skin rash), you must stop taking Risedronate sodium and seek medical advice immediately. If you have stopped treatment due to hypersensitivity reactions, you should not re-start therapy with Risedronate sodium.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sedron 35 mg</strong></p><p> No specific information is available on the treatment of overdose with Sedron 35 mg. Decreases in serum calcium following substantial overdose may be expected. Signs and symptoms of hypocalcaemia may also occur in some of these patients. Milk or antacids containing magnesium, calcium, or aluminum should be given to bind Risedronate and reduce absorption of Sedron 35 mg. In cases of substantial overdose, gastric lavage may be considered to remove unabsorbed Sedron 35 mg.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place. Protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary since bioavailability, distribution, and elimination were similar in elderly (60 years of age or older) compared to younger subjects. This has also been shown in the very elderly, 75 years old and above postmenopausal population.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is required for those patients with mild to moderate renal impairment. The use of Sedron 35 mg is contraindicated in patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance lower than 30 ml/min).</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Bisphosphonate preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Your body is constantly breaking down old bone and making new bone tissue. If you have osteoporosis, your body breaks down more bone than it forms so gradually bone loss occurs and your bones become thinner and fragile. This is especially common in women after menopause (change of life). Many people with osteoporosis have no symptoms and you may not even know that you have it. However, osteoporosis makes you more likely to have fractures (break bones), especially in your spine, hips, and wrists.</p><p> Risedronate has an affinity for hydroxyapatite crystals in bone and acts as an antiresorptive agent. At the cellular level, Risedronate inhibits osteoclasts. The osteoclasts adhere normally to the bone surface but show evidence of reduced active resorption.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Sedron 35 mg is meant for use only in postmenopausal women. Therefore, Sedron 35 mg should not be taken during pregnancy. If you take it by accident during pregnancy, stop taking it straight away and talk to your doctor. Sedron 35 mg is meant for use only in postmenopausal women. Therefore, breast-feeding women should not take this medicine. If you take it by accident during breast-feeding, stop taking it straight away and talk to your doctor.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Pediatric use: The safety and efficacy of Sedron 35 mg tablets have not been established in children and adolescents.</p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary since bioavailability, distribution, and elimination were similar in elderly (60 years of age or older) compared to younger subjects. This has also been shown in the very elderly, 75 years old and above postmenopausal population.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is required for those patients with mild to moderate renal impairment. The use of Sedron 35 mg is contraindicated in patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance lower than 30 ml/min).</li></ul>', 'active', '1249_1742567009.jpg', 37, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:23:29', '2025-03-21 19:23:29'),
(3963, 'Sefanid', 'Cefadroxil Monohydrate', 12.05, 10, 'Capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sefanid 500 mg</strong></p><p> It is indicated for the treatment of upper respiratory tract infections (pharyngitis and tonsillitis) caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em> (group-A beta-hemolytic Streptococci) and <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>; urinary tract infections caused by <em>E. coli</em>, <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, and <em>Klebsiella</em> species, and skin &amp; soft tissue infections caused by Staphylococci (including penicillinase-producing bacteria) and Streptococci.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> First generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cefadroxil inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to 1 or more of the penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), which in turn inhibits the final transpeptidation step of peptidoglycan synthesis in bacterial cell walls. This results in the inhibition of cell wall biosynthesis and arresting cell wall assembly, leading to bacterial cell death.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sefanid 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis and Tonsillitis: 1 g per day in one or two divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Urinary Tract Infections: 1 or 2 g per day in one or two divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin and Skin Structure Infections: 1 g per day in one or two divided doses.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 30 mg/kg daily in divided doses every 12 hours.</li><li> It may be taken with meals or on an empty stomach. Administration with food may help reduce potential gastrointestinal complaints.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Sefanid 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis and Tonsillitis: 1 g per day in one or two divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Urinary Tract Infections: 1 or 2 g per day in one or two divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin and Skin Structure Infections: 1 g per day in one or two divided doses.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 30 mg/kg daily in divided doses every 12 hours.</li><li> It may be taken with meals or on an empty stomach. Administration with food may help reduce potential gastrointestinal complaints.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sefanid 500 mg</strong></p><p> There is no significant drug interaction with other drugs.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Cefadroxil is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Cefadroxil or any of the ingredients in it.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sefanid 500 mg</strong></p><p> Generally, Cefadroxil is well tolerated. However, the most commonly reported side effects are gastrointestinal disturbances and hypersensitivity phenomena.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> US FDA pregnancy category of Cefadroxil is B. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Cefadroxil has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Cefadroxil is administered during lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Use of this antibiotic may cause pseudomembranous colitis; so caution should be taken during diagnosis in patients who develop diarrhea in association with Cefadroxil therapy.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> First generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Cefadroxil inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to 1 or more of the penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), which in turn inhibits the final transpeptidation step of peptidoglycan synthesis in bacterial cell walls. This results in the inhibition of cell wall biosynthesis and arresting cell wall assembly, leading to bacterial cell death.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> US FDA pregnancy category of Cefadroxil is B. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Cefadroxil has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Cefadroxil is administered during lactation.</p>', 'active', '1240_1742567158.jpg', 9, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:25:58', '2025-03-21 19:25:58'),
(3964, 'Nebanol', 'Neomycin Sulfate + Bacitracin Zinc', 25.00, 12, 'x 10 gm pack', '10 gm pack', 'Topical Solution', '(5 mg+250 IU)/gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nebanol 10 gm</strong></p><p>This is indicated in the treatment of topical bacterial infections. This is particularly effective in atopic, contact stasis and infections, eczematoid dermatitis, neurodermatitis, eczema, anogenital pruritus. It may also be useful as an adjunct in certain pyodermas, such as impetigo, during specific systemic antibiotic therapy for these infections.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Ophthalmic and Topical antibacterial products</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Bacitracin</strong> inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis and is active against many gram-positive bacteria (e.g., staphylococci, streptococci, corynebacteria, and Clostridia) and some gram-negative species (e.g., Neisseria and Haemophilus influenzae). These are often found in combinations in topical preparations as broad-spectrum antibacterial agents.</li><li><strong>Neomycin</strong>, an aminoglycoside with antimicrobial spectrum similar to gentamicin, binds to the 30S subunits of the bacterial ribosome, inhibiting protein synthesis and thereby disrupting DNA synthesis. It is active against many gram-negative aerobes and some strains of staphylococci.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Nebanol 10 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Ointment</strong>: Before use, clean the area for application gently. Remove debris such as pus or crusts from the affected area. Apply 2 to 3 times daily to the affected skin area.</li><li><strong>Dusting Powder</strong>: Antibiotic powder is a non-sterile dusting powder used for superficial skin infections. A light dusting of the powder should be applied to the affected skin area up to 4 times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Nebanol 10 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Bacitracin Zinc</strong>: Increased risk of nephrotoxicity when used with other nephrotoxic drugs. May enhance the action of neuromuscular-blocking agents.</li><li><strong>Neomycin Sulphate</strong>: Additive nephrotoxic and neurotoxic effect with other aminoglycosides, bacitracin, cisplatin, vancomycin, amphotericin B, polymyxin B, colistin, and viomycin. Enhanced toxicity with potent diuretics. May impair the absorption of other drugs. May enhance the effect of acarbose. May enhance the effect of non-depolarizing muscle relaxants. May antagonize the parasympathomimetic effect of neostigmine and pyridostigmine. May increase the risk of hypocalcaemia in patients receiving bisphosphonates. May alter INR when given with anticoagulants. May inactivate oral typhoid vaccine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Nebanol 10 gm</strong></p><p>This preparation is usually well-tolerated. In rare cases, allergic reactions may occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safe use of this preparation during pregnancy &amp; lactation has not been established. Therefore, it should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Bacitracin &amp; Neomycin antibiotic powder should not be used on large open wounds or severely injured skin or on areas that exude large volumes of fluid, as hard crusts may form.</li><li>Since this skin ointment contains Neomycin, it should not be used for the treatment of otitis externa when the eardrum is perforated because of the risk of ototoxicity.</li><li>Patients considering self-medication with a topical anti-infective for deep or puncture wounds, animal bites, or serious burns should be advised to consult a physician.</li><li>Patients using the preparations for the prevention of infection in minor skin injuries (e.g., cuts, scrapes, burns) should be advised to discontinue the topical anti-infective preparation and consult a physician if the condition persists or worsens. It should not be used for longer than 1 week unless directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep all medicines out of reach of children. Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children and Infants</strong>: This ointment is suitable for use in children at the same dose as adults, but the dose should be reduced in infants. This ointment is not recommended for use in neonates.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Ophthalmic and Topical antibacterial products</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Bacitracin</strong> inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis and is active against many gram-positive bacteria (e.g., staphylococci, streptococci, corynebacteria, and Clostridia) and some gram-negative species (e.g., Neisseria and Haemophilus influenzae). These are often found in combinations in topical preparations as broad-spectrum antibacterial agents.</li><li><strong>Neomycin</strong>, an aminoglycoside with antimicrobial spectrum similar to gentamicin, binds to the 30S subunits of the bacterial ribosome, inhibiting protein synthesis and disrupting DNA synthesis. It is active against many gram-negative aerobes and some strains of staphylococci.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The safe use of this preparation during pregnancy &amp; lactation has not been established. Therefore, it should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children and Infants</strong>: This ointment is suitable for use in children at the same dose as adults, but the dose should be reduced in infants. This ointment is not recommended for use in neonates.</li></ul>', 'active', '1242_1742567180.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:26:20', '2025-05-04 00:32:24'),
(3965, 'Sefin', 'Cephradine', 7.00, 4, 'Capsule', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sefin 250 mg</strong></p><p> Sefin 250 mg is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. These include:</p><ul><li>Undesirable Upper respiratory tract infections: sinusitis, pharyngitis, tonsillitis, laryngo-tracheo bronchitis, and otitis media.</li><li>Lower respiratory tract infections: bronchitis (acute and chronic), lobar pneumonia, and bronchopneumonia.</li><li>Urinary tract infections: cystitis, urethritis, and pyelonephritis.</li><li>Skin and soft tissue infections: abscess, cellulitis, furunculosis, and impetigo.</li></ul><p>The following microorganisms are susceptible, in vitro, to Sefin 250 mg:</p><ul><li><strong>Gram-positive</strong>: Staphylococci (both penicillin-sensitive and resistant strains, and penicillinase-producing species), Streptococci, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em> (beta-hemolytic), <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>.</li><li><strong>Gram-negative</strong>: <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella</em> spp, <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Shigella</em> spp, <em>Salmonella</em> spp (including <em>Salmonella typhi</em>), <em>Neisseria</em> spp.</li></ul><p>Many strains of <em>E. coli</em> and <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> that produce the enzyme penicillinase and thus are ampicillin-resistant are susceptible to Sefin 250 mg, which is unaffected by this enzyme.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> First generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sefin 250 mg is a cephalosporin antibiotic with broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against both gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria. Sefin 250 mg interferes with the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall by inhibiting the transpeptidase enzyme. As a result, the bacterial cell wall is weakened, the cell swells, and then ruptures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sefin 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For respiratory tract infections (other than lobar pneumonia) and skin and skin structure infections, the usual dose is 250 mg every 6 hours or 500 mg every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For lobar pneumonia, the usual dose is 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 g every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For uncomplicated urinary tract infections, the usual dose is 500 mg every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In more serious urinary tract infections, including prostatitis, 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 g every 12 hours may be administered.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: The usual dose in children over nine months of age is 25 to 50 mg/kg/day administered in equally divided doses every 6 or 12 hours. For otitis media due to <em>H. influenzae</em>, doses are from 75 to 100 mg/kg/day administered in equally divided doses every 6 or 12 hours, but should not exceed 4 g per day.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Sefin 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral Administration</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Urinary tract infections: 500 mg four times daily or 1 g twice daily. Severe or chronic infections may require higher doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Respiratory tract infections: 250 to 500 mg four times daily or 500 mg to 1 g twice daily, depending on the site and severity of the infection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin and soft tissue infections: 250 to 500 mg four times daily or 500 mg to 1 g twice daily, again depending on the site and severity of the infection.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Total daily dose of 25 to 50 mg/kg given in two or four equally divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Otitis media: Total daily dose of 75 to 100 mg/kg given in divided doses every 6 or 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum daily dosage: 4 gm.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly</strong>: The normal adult dose is appropriate.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal or Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Patients should be monitored during treatment.</li><li><strong>Injectable Administration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: The usual dose is 2-4 gm daily in four equally divided doses, up to 8 gm daily. For prophylaxis, a single preoperative dose of 1-2 gm intramuscularly or intravenously is given.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children</strong>: The dose is 50-100 mg/kg daily in four equally divided doses, up to 300 mg/kg daily in severe infections.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sefin 250 mg</strong></p><p> The concomitant use of nephrotoxic drugs such as aminoglycosides with Cefradine may increase the risk of kidney damage. Diuretics (e.g., frusemide, ethacrynic acid) and probenecid enhance the possibility of renal toxicity.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Sefin 250 mg should not be used in patients with known or suspected hypersensitivity to cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sefin 250 mg</strong></p><p> Limited essentially to gastrointestinal disturbances and, on occasion, hypersensitivity phenomena. The latter are more likely to occur in individuals who have previously demonstrated hypersensitivity or have a history of allergy, asthma, hay fever, or urticaria. Skin reactions have occasionally been reported.</p><ul><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Glossitis, heartburn, dizziness, tightness in the chest, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, vaginitis, candida overgrowth.</li><li><strong>Skin and Hypersensitivity Reactions</strong>: Urticaria, skin rashes, joint pains, edema.</li><li><strong>Blood and Lymphatic System Disorders</strong>: Unknown: blood disorders (including thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, agranulocytosis, aplastic anemia, and hemolytic anemia).</li><li><strong>Immune System Disorders</strong>: Unknown: Fever, serum sickness-like reactions, anaphylaxis.</li><li><strong>Psychiatric Disorders</strong>: Unknown: Confusion, sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Nervous System Disorders</strong>: Unknown: Hyperactivity, hypertonia, dizziness, nervousness; rarely, headache.</li><li><strong>Hepatobiliary Disorders</strong>: Frequency unknown: Liver enzyme disturbances, transient hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice.</li><li><strong>Renal and Urinary Disorders</strong>: Unknown: Reversible interstitial nephritis.</li><li><strong>Investigations</strong>: Unknown: Elevation of blood urea nitrogen, serum creatinine, alanine aminotransferase, aspartate aminotransferase, total bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> US FDA Pregnancy Category of Cefradine is B. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Cefradine has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Cefradine is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Prolonged use of an anti-infective may result in the development of superinfection due to the emergence of resistant organisms. Sefin 250 mg should be administered with care to patients hypersensitive to penicillins because of the risk of cross-sensitivity between beta-lactam antibiotics. Cephalosporin antibiotics may cause a positive result in Coombs’ testing. When Coombs testing is performed on neonates whose mothers received cephalosporins prior to labor, it should be noted that a positive result may be due to the drug. Sefin 250 mg may cause a false positive urine glucose result when Benedict’s or Fehling’s solutions or tablets such as Clinitest are used in the testing. This does not occur with enzyme-based tests (e.g., Clinistix, Diastix). Dosage adjustment is necessary in renal impairment. This product contains lactose. Patients with rare hereditary problems of galactose intolerance, the Lapp lactase deficiency, or glucose-galactose malabsorption should not take this medicine.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sefin 250 mg</strong></p><p> The symptoms of Sefrad overdose are non-specific and generally include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and gastric upsets. Treatment is mainly supportive, although gastric lavage will be necessary if a large amount has been ingested.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Sefin 250 mg Suspension should be freshly prepared. Reconstituted suspension should be used within 7 days if kept at room temperature or within 14 days if kept in a refrigerator. Sefin 250 mg Injection solutions should be used within 2 hours when kept at room temperature. When stored at 5°C, solutions retain potency for 12 hours. Reconstituted solutions may vary in color from light to straw yellow; however, this does not affect potency. Do not use after the expiry date. Keep all medicines out of the reach of children. To be dispensed only on the prescription of a registered physician.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> First generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Sefin 250 mg is a semisynthetic broad-spectrum bactericidal antibiotic, active against infections caused by both gram-positive and gram-negative microorganisms. Both penicillinase-producing and non-producing staphylococci are sensitive to Sefin 250 mg. The main site of action of Sefin 250 mg is the cell wall of bacteria. The cell wall of sensitive organisms contains peptidoglycan. Sefin 250 mg inhibits the cross-linking process, resulting in the formation of a cell wall with many pores, thus causing bacterial lysis due to external osmotic pressure.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Although animal studies have not demonstrated any teratogenicity, safety in pregnancy has not been established. Sefin 250 mg is excreted in breast milk and should be used with caution in lactating mothers. Since the medicine may cause dizziness, patients should be cautioned about operating hazardous machinery, including automobiles.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> Renal Impairment: The following doses are recommended (based on 500 mg every 6 hours) for patients not on hemodialysis:</p><ul><li>CrCl &gt;20 ml/min: 500 mg every 6 hours</li><li>CrCl 5-20 ml/min: 250 mg every 6 hours</li><li>CrCl &lt;5 ml/min: 250 mg every 50-70 hours</li></ul><p>Recommendations for patients on chronic, intermittent hemodialysis:</p><ul><li>250 mg at the start of hemodialysis</li><li>250 mg 6 to 12 hours after the start</li><li>250 mg 36 to 48 hours after the start</li><li>250 mg at the start of the next hemodialysis session if more than 30 hours have elapsed since the last dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Additional Information for All Patients</strong></p><p> Regardless of patient age or weight, higher doses of up to 1 gm four times daily may be required for infections that are chronic or severe. Treatment should continue for at least 2 to 3 days after symptoms have resolved or bacteria have been eradicated. To reduce the possibility of rheumatic fever or glomerulonephritis resulting from infections with hemolytic streptococci, treatment should be continued for at least 10 days. Throughout treatment of chronic urinary tract infections and for several months thereafter, regular bacteriological and clinical monitoring is required. Doses below those recommended above should not be prescribed. Pediatric dosages should not exceed those specified for adults, regardless of the severity of infection. It may be necessary to continue Sefin 250 mg therapy for several weeks in persistent infections. Patients may be transferred from intramuscular/intravenous Sefin 250 mg therapy to oral treatment at the same dosage level.</p>', 'active', '1218_1742567297.jpg', 43, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:28:17', '2025-03-21 19:28:17'),
(3966, 'Dextrim', 'Dextromethorphan + Phenylephrine + Triprolidine', 100.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '(20 mg+10 mg+2.5 mg)/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Dextrim 100 ml</strong></p><p>Temporarily relieves these symptoms due to common cold, hay fever (allergic rhinitis), or other upper respiratory allergies:</p><ul><li>Cough due to minor throat or bronchial irritation</li><li>Runny nose</li><li>Sneezing</li><li>Itching of the nose or throat</li><li>Itchy, watery eyes</li><li>Nasal congestion (reduces swelling of nasal passages)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Combined cough suppressants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This preparation is a mixture of antitussive, decongestant, and antihistamine agents:</p><ul><li><strong>Dextromethorphan</strong> is a non-narcotic antitussive that acts centrally on the cough center in the medulla. It has no analgesic, habit-forming properties, and little sedative activity.</li><li><strong>Phenylephrine</strong> is a decongestant that shrinks blood vessels in the nasal passage, helping with nasal and sinus congestion.</li><li><strong>Triprolidine</strong> is an antihistamine providing symptomatic relief for conditions triggered by histamine release. It is a potent competitive H1-receptor antagonist with mild CNS depressant properties, which may cause drowsiness.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Dextrim 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children 12 years and older</strong>: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) every 4 hours, or as directed by a doctor.</li><li><strong>Children 6 to under 12 years</strong>: ½ teaspoonful (2.5 ml) every 4 hours, or as directed by a doctor.</li><li><strong>Children below 2 years old</strong>: Not recommended.</li><li><strong>Children aged 2 to 6 years</strong>: To be used with caution, and as advised by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Dextrim 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Can be taken with or without food.</li><li>Take with food if it causes an upset stomach.</li><li>Measure liquid doses carefully using the measuring device that comes with this medicine.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Dextrim 100 ml</strong></p><p>Avoid taking with:</p><ul><li>MAO inhibitors (e.g., isocarboxazid, phenelzine, selegiline, etc.)</li><li>Antihistamines, antispasmodics, beta blockers, Parkinson\'s drugs, certain antidepressants, anti-seizure drugs, muscle relaxants, narcotic pain relievers, psychiatric medicines, and others.</li><li>Caution should be exercised when using these medications together.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Avoid in:</p><ul><li>Patients with liver disease or asthma.</li><li>Patients taking MAO inhibitors or within 2 weeks of stopping such treatment.</li><li>Known hypersensitivity to phenylephrine, dextromethorphan, or other components.</li><li>Acute asthma attacks, severe hypertension, or coronary artery disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Dextrim 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects: Drowsiness, dizziness, constipation, GIT discomfort.</li><li>Less common side effects: Transient hypertension, dry mouth, restlessness, palpitations, allergic reactions (e.g., rashes, chest tightness, bronchial secretions), toxic psychosis, blood dyscrasias.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>There are no specific data on the use of this combination during pregnancy and lactation, so it is best avoided unless necessary and prescribed by a doctor.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>May cause drowsiness; avoid operating machinery or driving if affected.</li><li>Avoid alcohol while taking this medication.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with epilepsy, prostatic hypertrophy, glaucoma, hepatic disease, hypertension, heart disease, diabetes, hyperthyroidism, etc.</li><li>If symptoms do not improve within a week or are accompanied by a high fever, consult a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Dextrim 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Signs of overdose</strong>: Respiratory depression, paranoid psychosis, delusions, hallucinations, convulsions.</li><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: Empty the stomach (aspiration or gastric lavage), treat with sedatives (e.g., diazepam), and use supportive care.</li><li><strong>Phenylephrine overdose</strong>: May cause hypertension and bradycardia, treated with alpha-blockers (e.g., phentolamine) and atropine if necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li><li>Store in a cool, dry place.</li><li>Protect from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Combined cough suppressants</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Dextromethorphan acts centrally to suppress cough.</li><li>Phenylephrine acts as a decongestant.</li><li>Triprolidine provides antihistamine effects for symptomatic relief of allergies.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>As with any medication, use during pregnancy and lactation is best avoided unless prescribed by a doctor.</p>', 'active', '1446_1742567302.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:28:22', '2025-05-03 08:23:59');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3967, 'Cecon', 'Vitamin C [Ascorbic acid]', 1.90, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Chewable Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Cecon 250 mg (Ascorbic Acid - Vitamin C)</h3><ul><li><strong>Prevention &amp; Treatment of Scurvy</strong>: Essential in preventing and treating scurvy, a condition caused by vitamin C deficiency.</li><li><strong>Wound Healing &amp; Bone Repair</strong>: Aids in the healing of wounds and broken bones.</li><li><strong>Collagen Formation</strong>: Crucial for forming collagen in connective tissues.</li><li><strong>Iron Absorption &amp; Anemia</strong>: Enhances iron absorption, contributing to the treatment of anemia.</li><li><strong>Hemoglobin &amp; RBC Production</strong>: Supports the production of hemoglobin and red blood cells in the bone marrow.</li><li><strong>Immune System Support</strong>: Enhances immunity and helps in preventing many viral and bacterial infections.</li><li><strong>Common Cold Prevention</strong>: Aids in the treatment and prevention of the common cold.</li><li><strong>Capillary &amp; Gum Health</strong>: Promotes the health of capillaries, gums, and teeth.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Vitamin-C Preparations</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li>Ascorbic acid (vitamin C) is a water-soluble vitamin, absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and widely distributed throughout body tissues. It is essential for collagen synthesis and intracellular material formation. A deficiency leads to scurvy, characterized by capillary fragility, bleeding (especially from gums), anemia, cartilage and bone lesions, and slow wound healing.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cecon 250 mg</h3><h4>Oral Administration</h4><ul><li><strong>Prevention of Scurvy</strong>: 1 tablet daily</li><li><strong>Treatment of Scurvy</strong>: 1-2 tablets daily (dose may be adjusted depending on severity)</li><li><strong>Reduction of Stroke Risk in the Elderly</strong>: 1-2 tablets daily</li><li><strong>General Use</strong>: 1 tablet daily or as prescribed by the physician</li><li><strong>Maximum Safe Dose</strong>: 2000 mg daily in divided doses</li></ul><h4>Parenteral Administration</h4><ul><li>Administered IM, IV, or subcutaneously when oral administration is not feasible or when malabsorption is suspected.</li><li>Preferred parenteral route: <strong>IM administration</strong></li><li><strong>Average Protective Dose for Adults</strong>: 70 to 150 mg daily</li><li><strong>For Scurvy</strong>: 300 mg to 1 g daily</li><li><strong>Wound Healing</strong>: 300 to 500 mg daily for 7–10 days, both preoperatively and postoperatively</li><li><strong>Severe Burns</strong>: Daily doses of 1 to 2 g</li><li><strong>Maximum Dose Administered</strong>: 6 g parenterally without toxicity</li></ul><h4>Oral Sachet Administration</h4><ul><li>1 sachet daily or as prescribed by a physician</li><li><strong>Maximum Dose</strong>: 2000 mg/day (4 sachets/day)</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Cecon 250 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Potentially Hazardous Interactions</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Incompatible with aminophylline, bleomycin, erythromycin, lactobionate, nafcillin, nitrofurantoin sodium, conjugated estrogens, sodium bicarbonate, chloramphenicol sodium succinate, chlorthiazide sodium, hydrocortisone sodium succinate.</li><li><strong>Useful Interactions</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increases the half-life of paracetamol.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Enhances iron absorption from the gastrointestinal tract.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Cecon 250 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Common Side Effects</strong>: Diarrhea, abdominal bloating, iron over-absorption (harmful in patients with thalassemia, sideroblastic anemia, and hemochromatosis), hyperoxaluria, hyperuricosuria, hemolysis in individuals with glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency.</li><li><strong>Overdose</strong>: More than 5 gm/day in pregnancy may cause fetal abortion.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Safe in normal doses, but more than 5 gm/day may cause fetal abortion.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Safe for use during lactation.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Mega-doses (&gt;1000 mg daily)</strong> during pregnancy can cause scurvy in neonates.</li><li>Contraindicated for patients with <strong>hyperoxaluria</strong>.</li><li><strong>False positive reactions</strong> may occur in some tests for glucose, uric acid, creatine, and occult blood.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place, below 30°C.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li><strong>Vitamin-C Preparations</strong></li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Vitamin C</strong> is essential for tissue growth and repair, adrenal gland function, immune system enhancement, and healthy gums. As an antioxidant, it provides protection against pollution, infection, and reduces cellular damage. As a coenzyme, it is necessary for wound healing, drug metabolism, and cholesterol/folic acid metabolism. It also supports healthy skin, hair, and overall health.</li></ul>', 'active', '900_1742567396.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:29:56', '2025-05-03 01:15:23'),
(3968, 'Sinatrim', 'Sulphamethoxazole plus Trimethoprim', 2.10, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '800 mg plus 160 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sinatrim 400 mg+80 mg</strong></p><p> Sinatrim 400 mg+80 mg is bactericidal in vitro to a wide range of Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms, including Streptococcus, Staphylococcus, Pneumococcus, Neisseria, B. catarrhalis, Escherichia coli, Klebsiella, Proteus spp., Haemophilus, Salmonella, Shigella, Vibrio cholerae, Brucella, Pneumocystis carinii, Nocardia, and Bordetella. A particularly high degree of activity is exhibited against Haemophilus influenzae, E. coli, and Proteus spp., making Sinatrim 400 mg+80 mg particularly suitable for the treatment of chronic bronchitis and urinary tract infections. Sinatrim 400 mg+80 mg exerts its bactericidal action by the sequential blockade of two bacterial enzyme systems in the biosynthesis of Folinic acid in the microorganisms. The synergy thus produced accounts for the high degree of bactericidal activity.</p><p> Indications include:</p><ul><li>Respiratory tract infections: acute and chronic bronchitis (treatment and prophylaxis), bronchiectasis, lung abscess, lobar and broncho-pneumonia, Pneumocystis carinii pneumonitis, sinusitis, and otitis media.</li><li>Genito-urinary tract infections: urethritis, acute and chronic cystitis, pyelonephritis, prostatitis, and gonorrhoea.</li><li>Gastro-intestinal tract infections: caused by Salmonella typhi and Salmonella paratyphi, including the chronic carrier state.</li><li>Other infections: acute and chronic osteomyelitis, acute brucellosis, skin infections (including pyoderma, abscesses, and wound infections), septicaemia, bacillary dysentery, and cholera (as an adjuvant to fluid and electrolyte replacement), nocardiosis, and mycetoma.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-diarrhoeal Antimicrobial drugs, Sulphonamides &amp; Trimethoprim</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sinatrim 400 mg+80 mg has broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against a wide range of gram-positive &amp; gram-negative bacteria and some protozoa. Co-trimoxazole containing Trimethoprim and Sulphamethoxazole in a 1:5 combination exerts its bactericidal action by the sequential blockade of two bacterial enzyme systems in the biosynthesis of folinic acid in the microorganism.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sinatrim 400 mg+80 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Sinatrim 400 mg+80 mg double strength tablet</strong></li><li>Over 12 years:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">For mild to moderate infections: 1 tablet twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">For severe infections: 1.5 tablets twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Long-term therapy (&gt;14 days): 0.5 tablet twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gonorrhoea: 2 tablets every 12 hours for two days or 2.5 tablets followed by a further dose of 2.5 tablets after 8 hours.</li><li><strong>Sinatrim 400 mg+80 mg tablet</strong></li><li>Over 12 years:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">For mild to moderate infections: 2 tablets twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">For severe infections: 2 tablets thrice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Long-term therapy (&gt;14 days): 1 tablet twice daily.</li><li><strong>Sinatrim 400 mg+80 mg suspension</strong></li><li>Under 12 years:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">6-12 years: 2 teaspoonfuls twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">6 months-5 years: 1 teaspoonful twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6 weeks-6 months: 0.5 teaspoonful twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to trimethoprim or sulphonamides.</li><li>Patients with documented megaloblastic anaemia due to folate deficiency.</li><li>Patients showing marked liver parenchymal damage, blood dyscrasia, severe renal insufficiency, glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Sinatrim 400 mg+80 mg</strong></p><p> Side effects such as crystalluria, allergic reactions, haemolysis, thrombocytopenia, neutropenia, and agranulocytosis have been reported rarely with the Sulphamethoxazole-Trimethoprim combination. Other side effects are less serious in nature, such as malaise, headache, nausea, and vomiting. These are normally transient and do not require withdrawal of treatment.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy: Sulphonamides pass the placenta and may cause kernicterus.</li><li>Lactation: Sulphonamides are excreted in breast milk and should be used with caution during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged full-dose treatment with sulfamethoxazole-trimethoprim combination is associated with the risk of macrocytic anaemia due to the drug’s interference in the conversion of Folic acid into Folinic acid. If this occurs, it can be reversed by giving Folinic acid.</li><li>Care should be taken when giving this combination to diabetic patients receiving sulphonylurea drugs for the possible potentiation of the action of sulphonylurea.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul>', 'active', '1437_1742567471.jpg', 30, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:31:11', '2025-03-21 19:31:11'),
(3969, 'Calcefer', 'Calcium Lactate Gluconate + Calcium Carbonate + Vitamin C', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '1000 mg+327 mg+500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Calcefer 1000 mg + 327 mg + 500 mg</strong></p><p>This is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Increased demand for Calcium and Vitamin-C, e.g., pregnancy, lactation, periods of rapid growth (childhood, adolescence), in old age</li><li>During infectious disease and convalescence</li><li>Treatment of calcium and vitamin-C deficiency</li><li>Osteoporosis</li><li>Premenstrual syndrome</li><li>Postmenopausal problems</li><li>Adjuvant in colds and influenza</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Calcium is used as a pharmacological agent in humans almost entirely to remedy deficiency. Adequate calcium in the blood is vital to a wide variety of bodily functions, and our internal biochemistry will not tolerate a deficiency even for short periods.</li><li>Vitamin-C is an essential component of the diet, as humans cannot synthesize vitamin-C. It is a very powerful reducing agent and plays an important role in the body’s response to stress and in the defense against infection.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Calcefer 1000 mg + 327 mg + 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children above 7 years</strong>: 1 effervescent tablet daily</li><li><strong>Children 3 to 7 years</strong>: ½ effervescent tablet daily</li><li><strong>Infants</strong>: As prescribed by the physician</li><li>Dissolve one tablet in half a glass (100 ml) of water.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Calcefer 1000 mg + 327 mg + 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calcium Gluconate</strong>: Co-administration of high calcium doses with thiazide diuretics may result in milk-alkali syndrome and hypercalcemia. May potentiate digoxin toxicity. Decreases the effects of calcium channel blockers. Enhanced absorption with calcitriol (a vitamin D metabolite).</li><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate</strong>: Co-administration with thiazide diuretics or vitamin D may lead to milk-alkali syndrome and hypercalcemia. Decreased absorption with corticosteroids. Decreases absorption of tetracyclines, atenolol, iron, quinolones, alendronate, Na fluoride, Zn, and calcium-channel blockers. Enhances cardiac effects of digitalis glycosides and may precipitate digitalis intoxication.</li><li><strong>Vitamin C</strong>: Interacts with deferroxamine, hormonal contraceptives, flufenazine, warfarin, elemental iron, salicylates, disulfiram, mexiletine, and vitamin B12.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypercalcemia (e.g., in hyperparathyroidism, vitamin-D overdosage, decalcifying tumors such as plasmocytoma, bone metastases)</li><li>Severe hypercalciuria</li><li>Severe renal failure</li><li>Patients with hyperoxalauria, glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, or iron overload</li><li>Larger doses may lead to gastrointestinal upset.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Calcefer 1000 mg + 327 mg + 500 mg</strong></p><p>In rare cases, mild gastrointestinal disturbances (bloating, diarrhea) can occur. In predisposed patients, prolonged treatment with high doses may promote the formation of calculi in the urinary tract.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Calcium-containing drugs have been widely used during pregnancy for oral calcium supplementation or antacid therapy. Calcium Carbonate can be safely used in lactating women.</li><li>Vitamin-C may be taken safely during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>For patients with mild hypercalciuria (exceeding 300 mg = 7.5 mmol/24 hours), with mild or moderate impairment of renal function, or with a history of urinary concrements, monitoring of calcium excretion in the urine is required. If necessary, the dosage should be reduced or therapy discontinued.</li><li>High doses of vitamin-D and its derivatives should be avoided during treatment with this preparation unless especially indicated.</li><li>Since citrate salts have been reported to increase aluminum absorption, this medicine should be used with caution in patients with severely impaired renal function, especially those receiving aluminum-containing preparations.</li><li>The sugar content should be taken into account by diabetic patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Calcefer 1000 mg + 327 mg + 500 mg</strong></p><p>Acute overdosage has not been reported. It would be expected to cause gastrointestinal disturbances but not to result in hypercalcemia, except in patients treated with a very high dosage of vitamin-D and derivatives.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a cool and dry place, protected from light and moisture.</p>', 'active', '1321_1742567477.jpg', 6, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:31:17', '2025-05-03 01:08:13'),
(3970, 'Naprosyn Plus', 'Naproxen Sodium + Esomeprazole Magnesium', 21.00, 8, 'tablet', '8 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Naprosyn Plus 500 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p>It is indicated for the relief of signs and symptoms of osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis, and to decrease the risk of developing gastric ulcers in patients at risk of developing NSAID-associated gastric ulcers.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>It is a preparation of Naproxen and Esomeprazole.</p><ul><li><strong>Naproxen</strong> is a NSAID with analgesic and antipyretic activities. It inhibits prostaglandin synthesis.</li><li><strong>Esomeprazole</strong> is a proton pump inhibitor that suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the H+/K+ ATPase in the gastric parietal cell. By acting specifically on the proton pump, Esomeprazole blocks the final step in acid production, thus reducing gastric acidity.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Naprosyn Plus 500 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p>The recommended dose is one tablet twice daily.</p><p>Do not split, chew, crush, or dissolve the tablet. This tablet is to be taken at least 30 minutes before meals.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Naprosyn Plus 500 mg+20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ACE Inhibitors, diuretics, and beta-blockers</strong>: may reduce the antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin</strong>: increases the risk of nephrotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Lithium</strong>: increases plasma level of lithium.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: may increase the serum levels of methotrexate.</li><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>: may increase the risk of bleeding complications.</li><li><strong>Digoxin</strong>: may increase the digoxin toxicity.</li><li><strong>Clopidogrel</strong>: may decrease the exposure to the active metabolite of clopidogrel.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any components of this product. It is also contraindicated in patients who have experienced asthma, urticaria, or allergic-type reactions after taking aspirin or other NSAIDs and for the treatment of per-operative pain in the setting of coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Naprosyn Plus 500 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p>The most common side effects are gastritis, erosive gastritis, dyspepsia, diarrhea, gastric ulcer, upper abdominal pain, and nausea.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of this tablet should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Fatal cardiovascular thrombotic events like myocardial infarction, stroke, hypertension, congestive heart failure, and edema.</li><li>Serious GI adverse events.</li><li>Hypomagnesaemia.</li><li>Renal or hepatic insufficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat.</p><p>Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '962_1742567573.webp', 10, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:32:53', '2025-05-04 00:22:41'),
(3971, 'Provair', 'Montelukast', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Chewable Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Provair 5 mg</h3><p>Provair 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</strong></li><li><strong>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR):</strong> Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Provair 5 mg is an orally active leukotriene receptor antagonist that inhibits the effects of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, LTE4), which are involved in inflammation and constriction of the airways. By binding to the CysLT1 receptor, it prevents these leukotrienes from causing bronchoconstriction and inflammation, helping to manage asthma and allergic rhinitis symptoms.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Provair 5 mg</h3><h4>For Adults and Adolescents (15 years and older):</h4><ul><li><strong>Monas 10 mg tablet</strong> once daily for asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis.</li></ul><h4>For Pediatric Patients:</h4><ul><li><strong>6 to 14 years:</strong> Monas 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>2 to 5 years:</strong> Monas 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>6 months to 5 years:</strong> Monas 4 mg oral granules once daily, either directly in the mouth or mixed with cold water or soft food.</li></ul><h4>Special Populations:</h4><ul><li><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency:</strong> No dosage adjustment needed for mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong> No dosage adjustment recommended.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> No dosage adjustment necessary; pharmacokinetics are similar to younger adults.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Provair 5 mg</h3><ul><li>No clinically significant interactions with theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives, terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin.</li><li>Caution when co-administered with drugs that induce hepatic metabolism (e.g., phenobarbital or rifampin), as they may reduce Provair\'s AUC by 40%.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to any ingredient in the medication.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Provair 5 mg</h3><h4>Common Side Effects:</h4><ul><li>Upper respiratory tract infection</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Fever</li><li>Gastrointestinal pain</li><li>Headache</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li></ul><h4>Uncommon Side Effects:</h4><ul><li>Akathisia</li><li>Anxiety</li><li>Arthralgia</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Aberrant behavior</li><li>Depression</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Irritability</li><li>Sleep disturbances</li></ul><h4>Rare Side Effects:</h4><ul><li>Pulmonary eosinophilia</li><li>Angioedema</li><li>Memory loss</li><li>Palpitations</li><li>Suicidal thoughts</li><li>Tremor</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> Provair 5 mg crosses the placenta in rats and rabbits. There is no adequate data for human pregnancies, so it should be used only when absolutely necessary.</li><li>It is secreted in breast milk, so caution should be exercised when administered to breastfeeding mothers.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Not recommended for the reversal of acute bronchospasm or asthma attacks (e.g., status asthmaticus). Rescue medication should be available.</li><li>Gradual reduction of inhaled corticosteroids should be done under medical supervision.</li><li>Avoid using as a single dose to treat exercise-induced bronchospasm.</li><li>Patients with aspirin sensitivity should avoid taking aspirin or NSAIDs while on Provair 5 mg.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Provair 5 mg</h3><p>Overdose reports generally show mild symptoms consistent with its side effect profile (e.g., nausea, vomiting, headache). Supportive measures such as removing unabsorbed material from the digestive tract are advised, along with clinical monitoring if necessary.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry place, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Provair 5 mg selectively inhibits the cysteinyl leukotriene receptor (CysLT1), blocking the action of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, LTE4). These leukotrienes contribute to inflammation, airway edema, and bronchoconstriction, which are key components of asthma and allergic rhinitis.</p><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li>As Provair 5 mg passes the placenta in animals, it should only be used during pregnancy if absolutely necessary. The effects on human pregnancy are not well understood. Caution is advised for nursing mothers as it is excreted in breast milk.</li></ul>', 'active', '1101_1742567811.png', 8, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:36:51', '2025-05-04 14:36:24'),
(3972, 'Progut', 'Esomeprazole', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Progut 20 mg</strong></p><p>Progut 20 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>To alleviate GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn and others.</li><li>To treat erosive esophagitis.</li><li>To maintain the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>For the eradication of <em>Helicobacter pylori</em> infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</li><li>Duodenal &amp; gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Progut 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. Most patients heal within 4 to 8 weeks. An additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered if healing does not occur after 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely after 4 weeks, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication</strong>: Triple therapy to reduce the risk of duodenal ulcer recurrence: Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days, Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: The dose is 20-80 mg once daily. The dosage should be adjusted individually and continued as long as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks based on the response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: 40 mg Progut 20 mg once daily via intravenous injection (not less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes). Esomeprazole IV injection should not be administered with any other medications through the same intravenous site. Discontinue IV treatment as soon as the patient can resume treatment with esomeprazole delayed-release capsules.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Progut 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take one Progut 20 mg pill or capsule <strong>one hour before a meal</strong> and consume it whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>: Mix the contents of one packet with 15 ml of water in a small glass. Stir thoroughly, and wait 2-3 minutes for thickening. Stir again and consume within 30 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If taken through a nasogastric or gastric tube, add 15 ml of water to a syringe, shake, wait 2-3 minutes, then administer via the tube.</li><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>: Add 5 ml of solvent (WFI) to the vial to create the IV solution. Administer the injection over a minimum of three minutes. The solution should be used within 12 hours of reconstitution at room temperature. Do not refrigerate.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Progut 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is metabolized by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 in the liver.</li><li>It does not significantly interact with drugs metabolized by CYP1A2, 2A6, 2C9, 2D6, 2E1, or 3A4.</li><li><strong>Drug interactions</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Co-administration with <strong>diazepam</strong> may increase diazepam plasma levels by 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Esomeprazole may reduce the absorption of drugs like <strong>ketoconazole</strong>, <strong>iron salts</strong>, and <strong>digoxin</strong> (whose bioavailability is pH-sensitive).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">No significant interaction with <strong>phenytoin</strong>, <strong>warfarin</strong>, <strong>quinidine</strong>, <strong>clarithromycin</strong>, or <strong>amoxicillin</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the formulations of esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Progut 20 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Dry mouth</li></ul><p>Long-term treatment (up to 12 months) does not lead to different side effects compared to short-term use.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>A positive symptom response does not rule out the possibility of stomach cancer.</li><li>Take capsules at least one hour before meals.</li><li>For patients with difficulty swallowing capsules, open the capsule and mix the pellets with applesauce (cold and soft) and consume immediately. Do not chew the pellets.</li><li>Antacids can be used during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Progut 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Fatal overdose is not known in humans.</li><li>In animal studies, symptoms of overdose include decreased motor activity, tremors, ataxia, and convulsions.</li><li>There is no specific countermeasure for overdose. Dialysis is unlikely to remove esomeprazole due to protein binding. Provide symptomatic and supportive care.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place at a temperature not exceeding 30°C.</li><li>Protect from moisture and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole inhibits the H+/K+-ATPase in gastric parietal cells, reducing stomach acid secretion.</li><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: The enteric-coated pellets in the capsules provide a peak plasma level about 1.5 hours after oral dosing.</li><li><strong>Distribution</strong>: 97% plasma protein binding.</li><li><strong>Metabolism</strong>: Processed by CYP450 enzymes in the liver, mainly via CYP2C19.</li><li><strong>Excretion</strong>: 80% excreted via urine as inactive metabolites, and 20% through feces.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>There is insufficient research on esomeprazole in pregnant women.</li><li>No teratogenic effects have been found in animals, but esomeprazole is excreted in milk. Breastfeeding should be discontinued if esomeprazole is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>No significant differences in safety or efficacy between elderly and younger patients, though older patients may be more sensitive.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage change is required for mild to moderate hepatic insufficiency. For severe hepatic impairment, do not exceed 20 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole\'s pharmacokinetics are unlikely to differ in patients with renal impairment, as less than 1% is excreted unchanged in urine.</li></ul>', 'active', '1472_1742567933.webp', 17, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:38:53', '2025-05-04 10:32:11'),
(3973, 'VC-250', 'Vitamin C [Ascorbic acid]', 1.90, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Chewable Tablet', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of VC-250 250 mg (Ascorbic Acid - Vitamin C):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prevents and treats scurvy</strong></li><li><strong>Helps in healing wounds and broken bones</strong></li><li><strong>Aids in collagen formation in connective tissues</strong></li><li><strong>Promotes iron absorption and treats anemia</strong></li><li><strong>Supports the production of hemoglobin and red blood cells in the bone marrow</strong></li><li><strong>Strengthens the immune system, aiding in the prevention of viral and bacterial infections</strong></li><li><strong>Assists in the treatment and prevention of the common cold</strong></li><li><strong>Promotes healthy capillaries, gums, and teeth</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin-C Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Vitamin C, a water-soluble vitamin, is absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and distributed throughout body tissues. It plays a role in cellular respiration, collagen synthesis, and intracellular material formation. Vitamin C deficiency leads to scurvy, a condition marked by bleeding gums, anemia, and slow wound healing.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of VC-250 250 mg:</strong></p><p><em>Oral administration:</em></p><ul><li><strong>For the prevention of scurvy:</strong> 1 tablet daily</li><li><strong>For the treatment of scurvy:</strong> 1-2 tablets daily (dose may be increased based on severity)</li><li><strong>To reduce the risk of stroke in the elderly:</strong> 1-2 tablets daily</li><li><strong>Other cases:</strong> 1 tablet daily or as directed by the physician</li><li><strong>Maximum safe dose:</strong> 2000 mg daily in divided doses</li></ul><p><em>Parenteral administration:</em></p><ul><li>When oral intake is not possible, vitamin C may be administered IM, IV, or subcutaneously.</li><li><strong>Intravenous injection:</strong> Dilute into a large volume of parenteral solutions such as Normal Saline or Water for Injection to minimize adverse reactions.</li><li><strong>For scurvy:</strong> 300 mg to 1 g daily is recommended, with doses as high as 6 g given parenterally without toxicity.</li><li><strong>To promote wound healing:</strong> 300 to 500 mg daily for a week or ten days before and after surgery is recommended.</li><li><strong>For burns:</strong> Doses range from 1 to 2 g depending on the extent of tissue injury.</li></ul><p><strong>Maximum oral dose:</strong> 2000 mg/day (4 sachets/day).</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interactions of VC-250 250 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hazardous interactions:</strong> Incompatible in solution with certain drugs like aminophylline, bleomycin, erythromycin, lactobionate, nafcillin, nitrofurantoin sodium, and others.</li><li><strong>Useful interactions:</strong> Vitamin C enhances iron absorption and increases the half-life of paracetamol.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of VC-250 250 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common side effects:</strong> Minimal toxicity.</li><li><strong>Possible adverse effects of mega-doses:</strong> Diarrhea, abdominal bloating, iron over-absorption, hyperoxaluria, hyperuricosuria, hemolysis (especially in G6PD deficiency).</li><li><strong>High doses during pregnancy (above 5 gm/day) may lead to abortion.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Safe in normal doses during pregnancy. Doses over 5 gm/day may cause fetal abortion.</li><li>Safe for use during lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Mega-doses (over 1000 mg daily)</strong> can lead to scurvy in neonates.</li><li><strong>Contraindicated in patients with hyperoxaluria.</strong></li><li><strong>May cause false positive results in some diagnostic tests</strong> (for glucose, uric acid, creatinine, and occult blood).</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place below 30°C.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin-C Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>As an essential nutrient, Vitamin C supports tissue repair, enhances immunity, promotes wound healing, and protects against cellular damage from pollution and infection. It is necessary for various metabolic processes, including cholesterol and folic acid metabolism.</li></ul>', 'active', '1654_1742568022.jpg', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:40:22', '2025-05-04 22:48:53'),
(3974, 'Ostocal G', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 11.00, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ostocal G</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment &amp; prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, and disorders of osteogenesis. It is also used as a supplement in cases of inadequate intake of Calcium in childhood diet, rickets, pregnancy &amp; lactation, and elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis, phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg</strong> (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin D3 400 IU</strong> as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p>Or</p><ul><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg</strong> (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin D3 200 IU</strong> as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate Calcium in the blood is vital for a wide variety of bodily functions. Our internal biochemistry will not tolerate a deficiency of Calcium even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that Calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of Osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is essential for healthy bones as it aids in Calcium absorption from the GI tract. Additionally, it stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies show that Calcium and Vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth, Osteoporosis, and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ostocal G</strong></p><p>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night or as directed by the physician.</p><p>Take one tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or as directed by the physician. Taking it with a full stomach ensures better absorption.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Ostocal G</strong></p><p>Oral Calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline &amp; fluoride preparations. A minimum of 3 hours should be allowed between ingestion of these medications. Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of Calcium. Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce the metabolism of Vitamin D3. Concomitant ingestion of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of Calcium.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to any of the components, hypocalcaemia resulting from an overdose of Vitamin D3, hyperparathyroidism, bone metastases, severe renal insufficiency, severe hypercalciuria, renal calculi, etc.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ostocal G</strong></p><p>Flatulence, diarrhoea, constipation, upper GI discomfort, etc. are rare manifestations. Hypercalcaemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It should be used as directed by the physician during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with dose reduction may be needed. Plasma and serum Calcium levels should be monitored in mild to moderate renal impairment and in cases of long-term use. Patients with renal stones or a previous history of renal stones should also take precautions.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ostocal G</strong></p><p>At high doses, it may result in nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc. Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>This is a Calcium and Vitamin D3 preparation where Calcium Carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The Calcium Carbonate from coral has a chemical structure that is very similar to the composition of human bone. Coral Calcium is similar to other sources but ensures better absorption. Vitamin D3 aids in the absorption of Calcium from the GI tract and helps maintain Calcium balance in the body.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of a physician.</p>', 'active', '1545_1742568209.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:43:29', '2025-05-04 01:34:07'),
(3975, 'MarinCal-DX', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 16.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1570_1742568362.jpg', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:46:02', '2025-05-03 23:52:21'),
(3976, 'Calcitrol', 'Calcitriol', 10.03, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '0.25 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Calcitrol 0.25 mcg</strong></p><p>Calcitrol 0.25 mcg is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Post-menopausal osteoporosis</li><li>Renal osteodystrophy</li><li>Postsurgical hypoparathyroidism</li><li>Idiopathic hypoparathyroidism</li><li>Pseudohypoparathyroidism</li><li>Secondary hyperparathyroidism in patients with moderate to severe chronic renal failure (pre-dialysis)</li><li>Vitamin D-dependent rickets</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation</li><li>Vitamin-D preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Calcitrol 0.25 mcg is one of the most important active metabolites of vitamin D3. It is normally formed in the kidneys from its precursor, 25-hydroxycholecalciferol. Calcitrol promotes intestinal absorption of calcium and regulates bone mineralization. The key role of Calcitrol is the regulation of calcium homeostasis, which includes stimulation effects on osteoblastic activity in the skeleton.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Calcitrol 0.25 mcg</strong></p><p>The optimal daily dose of Calcitrol 0.25 mcg capsule must be carefully determined for each patient based on their serum calcium level.</p><ul><li><strong>Post-menopausal Osteoporosis</strong>: The recommended dose is 0.25 mcg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Renal Osteodystrophy (dialysis patients)</strong>: The initial daily dose is 0.25 mcg. In patients with normal or only slightly reduced calcium levels, doses of 0.25 mcg every other day may be sufficient.</li><li><strong>Hypoparathyroidism and Rickets</strong>: The recommended initial dosage is 0.25 mcg/day given in the morning. If a satisfactory response is not observed within 2-4 weeks, the dose may be increased at 2-4 week intervals.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Initial Dose</strong> (for severe hypocalcemia or secondary hyperparathyroidism): The recommended initial dose is 1 mcg (0.02 mcg/kg) to 2 mcg administered three times weekly, approximately every other day. Doses from 0.5 mcg to 4 mcg three times weekly have been used. If no satisfactory response is observed, the dose may be increased by 0.5 to 1 mcg at two to four-week intervals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Calcitrol 0.25 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Thiazide diuretics</strong>: Increases the risk of hypercalcemia.</li><li><strong>Digitalis</strong>: Hypercalcemia may precipitate cardiac arrhythmias in these patients.</li><li><strong>Magnesium-containing drugs (e.g., antacids)</strong>: May cause hypermagnesemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Calcitrol 0.25 mcg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of its ingredients and in diseases associated with hypercalcemia.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Side Effects of Calcitrol 0.25 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>Occasional: Anorexia, headache, vomiting, constipation.</li><li>Chronic: Dystrophy, fever, polyuria, dehydration, apathy, urinary tract infection.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: There is no evidence that vitamin D is teratogenic in humans. Calcitrol 0.25 mcg may be used during pregnancy only if the benefits outweigh the potential risks to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Mothers may breastfeed while taking Calcitrol 0.25 mcg, but serum calcium levels of the mother and infant should be monitored.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>If serum calcium levels rise to 1 mg/100 ml above normal or serum creatinine rises to &gt;120 µmol/l, the dosage should be reduced or treatment stopped.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Secondary Hyperparathyroidism (pre-dialysis patients)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For adults and pediatric patients 3 years of age or older, the recommended initial dosage is 0.25 mcg/day. This dosage may be increased to 0.5 mcg/day if necessary.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For pediatric patients under 3 years of age, the recommended initial dosage is 10 to 15 ng/kg/day.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustments are required.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation</li><li>Vitamin-D preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Calcitrol 0.25 mcg, as an active metabolite of vitamin D3, promotes the intestinal absorption of calcium and regulates bone mineralization. It plays a crucial role in the regulation of calcium homeostasis and stimulates osteoblastic activity in the skeleton.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Secondary Hyperparathyroidism in pre-dialysis patients</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initial dosage for adults and pediatric patients 3 years and older: 0.25 mcg/day, which may be increased to 0.5 mcg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For children less than 3 years, initial dosage is 10 to 15 ng/kg/day.</li></ul>', 'active', '1826_1742568592.jpg', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:49:52', '2025-05-01 23:03:14');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3977, 'Dermasol', 'Clobetasol Propionate', 70.48, 1, 'x 20 gm tube', '20 gm tube', 'Cream', '0.05%', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Dermasol 20 gm</h3><p>Clobetasol Propionate is indicated for adults, elderly, and children over 1 year in the following dermatoses:</p><ul><li>Psoriasis (excluding widespread plaque psoriasis)</li><li>Recalcitrant dermatoses</li><li>Lichen planus</li><li>Discoid lupus erythematosus</li><li>Other skin conditions that do not respond satisfactorily to less potent steroids</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Other Topical Corticosteroids</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Clobetasol Propionate is a very potent topical corticosteroid with the following properties:</p><ul><li>Anti-inflammatory</li><li>Antipruritic</li><li>Vasoconstrictive</li></ul><p>It works by inhibiting late-phase allergic reactions, reducing mast cell density, decreasing chemotaxis and activation of eosinophils, reducing cytokine production, and inhibiting the metabolism of arachidonic acid.</p><h3>Dosage of Dermasol 20 gm</h3><p><strong>Cream &amp; Ointment:</strong></p><ul><li>Adults, elderly, and children over 1 year: Apply a thin layer twice daily to affected skin and rub in gently.</li><li>Use should be limited to 2 consecutive weeks. The maximum dose should not exceed 50 gm per week.</li><li>For children, courses should be limited to five days and reviewed weekly.</li></ul><p><strong>Scalp Solution:</strong></p><ul><li>Apply once or twice daily to the scalp areas and rub in gently.</li><li>The total weekly dose should not exceed 50 ml.</li><li>Discontinue therapy if no response after 1 week or once the lesion heals.</li></ul><p><strong>Topical Spray:</strong></p><ul><li>Apply twice daily to affected areas and rub in gently.</li><li>Total dosage should not exceed 50 g per week.</li><li>Treatment should be limited to 4 weeks, with no more than 2 weeks for moderate to severe plaque psoriasis.</li></ul><h3>Administration of Dermasol 20 gm</h3><p><strong>Route of administration</strong>: Cutaneous</p><ul><li>Creams are best for moist or weeping surfaces.</li><li>Ointments are best for dry, lichenified, or scaly lesions.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Dermasol 20 gm</h3><ul><li>Co-administered drugs that inhibit CYP3A4 (e.g., ritonavir, itraconazole) can increase systemic exposure to corticosteroids.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the preparation</li><li>Not for use in rosacea, acne vulgaris, perioral dermatitis, pruritus without inflammation, untreated cutaneous infections, or perianal/genital pruritus.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Dermasol 20 gm</h3><ul><li><strong>Common side effects</strong>: Burning, stinging sensation</li><li><strong>Less frequent reactions</strong>: Itching, skin atrophy, cracking, and fissuring</li><li><strong>Serious side effect</strong>: Cushing syndrome due to prolonged use, especially in infants and adults</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>Use only if the benefit outweighs potential risks.</li><li>It is unknown if the drug is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when used by nursing women.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Avoid occlusive dressings unless necessary. Clean skin before applying fresh dressings.</li><li>Caution in psoriasis, as rebound relapses and local/systemic toxicity may occur.</li><li>Limit treatment on the face to 5 days and avoid eyes due to the risk of cataract and glaucoma.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Dermasol 20 gm</h3><ul><li>Acute overdosage is unlikely, but in chronic overdosage, hypercortisolism symptoms may appear. Discontinue topical steroid treatment in such cases.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Infants and children under 12</strong>: Long-term use should be avoided to prevent adrenal suppression.</li><li>Children are more susceptible to atrophic changes from topical corticosteroids.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further details or a summary on specific sections!</p>', 'active', '1918_1742568767.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:52:47', '2025-05-01 20:18:29'),
(3978, 'TPC', 'Vitamin B1, B6 & B12', 11.00, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '100 mg+200 mg+200 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you\'ve provided, formatted with appropriate headings in bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of TPC</strong></p><p>TPC is indicated for the treatment of vitamin B1, B6 &amp; B12 deficiency syndrome. It is also indicated for the supportive treatment of neuritis &amp; non-inflammatory diseases of the nerves, e.g., Diabetic neuropathy, Peripheral neuralgia, Lumbago, Myalgia, Optic neuritis, Sciatica, Facial neuralgia, Intercostal neuralgia, Spinal pain.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate) 100 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride) 200 mg</li><li>Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) 200 mcg</li></ul><p>Each 3 ml ampoule contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) 100 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) 100 mg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) 1000 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>TPC is a combined preparation of Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate), Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride), and Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin). These vitamins play an essential role as co-enzymes in the metabolism of the nervous system. They support the regeneration of nerve fibers and the myelin sheath by activating the nerve cell metabolism and the natural repair mechanism.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of TPC</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablets</strong>: 1 to 3 tablets per day or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly (deep intragluteal). In severe cases, 1 ampoule daily until the acute symptoms subside. For milder cases and follow-up therapy, 2 to 3 ampoules per week.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of TPC</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>: 1-3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: In severe (acute) cases, 1 injection daily until the acute symptoms subside or as advised by the physician. In mild cases: 1 injection 2-3 times per week. Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly.</li><li><strong>Use in Children</strong>: There is no information on the use of this drug in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of TPC</strong></p><p>No drug interaction has been reported yet.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vitamin B1, Vitamin B6, and Vitamin B12 are contraindicated in patients on levodopa therapy and in patients with hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of the preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of TPC</strong></p><p>Generally well tolerated, but allergic reactions may be observed in a few cases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>No risks have been reported with the use of this combination during pregnancy at the recommended dosage. Vitamin B1, B6, and B12 are secreted into breast milk, but risks of overdose for the infant are not known.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Cyanocobalamin should not be given to patients with subacute degeneration of the spinal cord.</li><li>Cyanocobalamin is not a suitable form of Vitamin B12 for the treatment of optic neuropathies associated with raised plasma concentrations of cyanocobalamin.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of TPC</strong></p><p>No overdosage symptoms are expected at the recommended dosage. If an overdose occurs, treatment is symptomatic and supportive.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep out of reach of children. Store in a cool (below 25°C temperature) and dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin B1 converts carbohydrates, fatty acids, and amino acids into energy, promotes healthy nerves, improves mood, and strengthens the heart.</li><li>Vitamin B6 forms RBCs, helps cells make proteins, manufactures neurotransmitters (e.g., serotonin), releases stored energy, helps prevent cardiovascular diseases and stroke, eases depression, and alleviates insomnia.</li><li>Vitamin B12 is essential for cell replication and RBC production, prevents anemia, reduces nerve pain, numbness, tingling, and lowers the risk of heart diseases.</li></ul><p>The vitamin ingredients are well absorbed via oral intake. They are widely distributed to most tissues and appear in breast milk. Thiamine is mostly present as diphosphate within cells, and excess amounts are excreted in urine. Pyridoxine, pyridoxal, and pyridoxamine are absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract following oral administration and are converted to their active forms in the liver.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The oral tablet form is recommended, but due to the presence of benzyl alcohol, the injection is not recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</p>', 'active', '127_1742568906.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:55:06', '2025-05-04 22:31:17'),
(3979, 'Dominol', 'Domperidone Maleate', 2.50, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Dominol 10 mg</strong></p><p>Dyspeptic symptom complex, often linked with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, may include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric sensation of fullness</li><li>Abdominal distension</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Belching</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Early satiety</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms can occur in various conditions, such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine. Additionally, in Parkinson\'s disease, it can manifest as dopamine-agonist-induced nausea and vomiting. For radiological studies, it can be used to speed up barium transit in follow-through radiological examinations.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Motility Stimulants</li><li>Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist</li><li>Prokinetic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Dominol 10 mg functions as a dopamine receptor antagonist. It exerts gastroprokinetic action by inhibiting dopamine receptors found in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier means it has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Dominol 10 mg</strong></p><p>For adults, the recommended dosage is 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily. Children should take 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily. When used for acute nausea and vomiting, the maximum treatment duration should not exceed 12 weeks. It is advisable to take this medication 15-30 minutes before a meal.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Dominol 10 mg</strong></p><p>Dominol 10 mg should be taken 15-30 minutes before meals, and if needed, before bedtime. The typical recommended oral dosage of Domperidone is as follows:</p><p><strong>For Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li>The maximum daily dose of Domperidone is 80 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg of body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>In cases of dyspeptic symptoms:</strong></p><p><strong>For Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For acute and sub-acute conditions, especially in cases of acute nausea and vomiting:</strong></p><p><strong>For Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>When administered rectally in the form of suppositories:</strong></p><p><strong>For Adults (including elderly):</strong></p><ul><li>30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>The maximum daily rectal dose for children weighing between 10 to 25 kg is 30 mg. The dose may be divided throughout the day if necessary.</li></ul><p>Please note that the maximum treatment period for acute nausea and vomiting is 12 weeks.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction of Dominol 10 mg</strong></p><p>The simultaneous use of anticholinergic drugs may counteract the antidyspeptic effects of domperidone. It\'s important to note that antacids and antisecretory drugs should not be administered concurrently with domperidone as they can reduce its oral bioavailability. Domperidone primarily undergoes metabolism through the CYP3A4 enzyme. In vitro data suggest that coadministration with drugs significantly inhibiting this enzyme may lead to elevated plasma levels of domperidone. Examples of CYP3A4 inhibitors include azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, nefazodone, and others.</p><p>Theoretically, as domperidone has gastro-kinetic effects, it could potentially impact the absorption of concurrently orally administered drugs, especially those with sustained release or enteric-coated formulations. However, in patients already stabilized on medications like digoxin or paracetamol, the concurrent use of domperidone did not affect the blood levels of these drugs. Domperidone does not potentiate the action of neuroleptics, and it suppresses the unwanted peripheral effects such as digestive disorders, nausea, and vomiting caused by dopaminergic agonists like bromocriptine and L-dopa without counteracting their central properties.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Dominol 10 mg is not to be used by patients with a known hypersensitivity to this medication or in the case of neonates. It should also be avoided in situations where gastrointestinal stimulation could be unsafe, such as in cases of gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation. Additionally, it is contraindicated in patients with a prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma).</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Dominol 10 mg</strong></p><p>Side effects are uncommon, with occasional reports of transient intestinal cramps. Extrapyramidal symptoms are rare among young children and exceptionally rare in adults. These symptoms typically resolve spontaneously and completely upon discontinuing the treatment. Since the pituitary gland is situated outside the blood-brain barrier, domperidone may lead to an increase in plasma prolactin levels. In rare cases, this hyperprolactinemia can result in neuroendocrinological effects like galactorrhea and gynecomastia. In situations where the blood-brain barrier is immature (as in infants) or compromised, the possibility of neurological side effects cannot be entirely ruled out. Additionally, there have been occasional reports of rare allergic reactions, including rash and urticaria.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of using Dominol 10 mg during pregnancy has not been established; hence, its use is not recommended during pregnancy. Animal studies have not shown any teratogenic effects on the fetus. Domperidone may induce galactorrhea and enhance post-natal lactation. It is excreted in breast milk in minimal quantities, which are insufficient to be considered harmful.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Dominol 10 mg should be employed with utmost care in children due to the potential for an increased risk of extrapyramidal reactions, which can be more pronounced in young children with an incompletely developed blood-brain barrier. Additionally, considering that domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should also be used cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Dominol 10 mg</strong></p><p>Signs of an overdose can manifest as drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions, particularly in children. If an overdose occurs, it is advisable to administer activated charcoal and closely monitor the patient. The use of anticholinergic drugs, antiparkinson drugs, or antihistamines with anticholinergic properties may be beneficial in managing extrapyramidal reactions.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Motility Stimulants</li><li>Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist</li><li>Prokinetic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Dominol 10 mg acts as a dopamine antagonist, primarily by blocking dopamine receptors located in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its gastroprokinetic effect is achieved by inhibiting dopamine receptors that influence the motility of the gastrointestinal tract. Because it has limited ability to penetrate the blood-brain barrier, Domperidone has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects. Domperidone restores normal motility and tone in the upper gastrointestinal tract, promotes gastric emptying, enhances antral and duodenal peristalsis, and regulates pylorus contraction. Additionally, it increases esophageal peristalsis and lowers esophageal sphincter pressure, preventing the regurgitation of gastric contents.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Use in infants: Given that the metabolic and blood-brain barrier functions are not fully developed during the first months of life, any medication should be administered to infants with great caution and under close medical supervision. Although the typical absence of neurological side effects with domperidone is primarily due to its limited penetration through the blood-brain barrier, the potential occurrence of such effects cannot be entirely ruled out in infants under 1 year of age.</p><p><strong>Use in Liver Disorders</strong></p><p>Because domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should be employed cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Use in Kidney Disorders</strong></p><p>In individuals with severe renal insufficiency (serum creatinine &gt;6 mg/100ml, i.e., &gt;0.6 mmol/l), the elimination half-life of domperidone increases from 7.4 to 20.8 hours, although plasma drug levels remain lower than in healthy volunteers. Since very little unchanged drug is excreted through the kidneys, it is unlikely that the dose of a single acute administration needs to be adjusted in patients with renal insufficiency. However, for repeated administration, the dosing frequency should be reduced to once or twice daily, depending on the severity of the impairment, and the dose may need to be lowered. In general, patients on prolonged therapy should undergo regular reviews.</p>', 'active', '1012_1742569125.jpg', 59, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 19:58:45', '2025-05-03 08:34:14'),
(3980, 'Opton', 'Esomeprazole', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><span style=\"color: var(--bs-card-color); background-color: var(--bs-card-bg);\"> </span>Indications of Opton 20 mg</h3><p>Opton 20 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>GERD-related symptoms</strong>: To alleviate persistent heartburn and other symptoms related to GERD.</li><li><strong>Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: To treat and maintain healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication</strong>: In combination with amoxicillin and clarithromycin to eradicate Helicobacter pylori infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: For treatment of this condition.</li><li><strong>Duodenal &amp; Gastric Ulcers</strong>: Caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><h3>Dosage of Opton 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. If healing is not achieved in 4-8 weeks, an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (up to 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve, an additional 4 weeks may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication</strong> (Triple Therapy): Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days, Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, and Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg once daily. Adjust dosage based on clinical response.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: 40 mg once daily by intravenous injection over a minimum of 3 minutes or intravenous infusion over 10-30 minutes.</li></ul><h3>Administration of Opton 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Oral</strong>: Take one capsule or pill whole, at least one hour before a meal.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>: Mix the contents of the packet with 15 ml of water, stir, and wait for 2-3 minutes to thicken. Drink within 30 minutes.</li><li><strong>Intravenous</strong>: Reconstitute with 5 ml of solvent (WFI), administer over at least 3 minutes. Do not use if particles are visible.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Opton 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>CYP2C19 and CYP3A4</strong>: These enzymes metabolize esomeprazole in the liver. Esomeprazole does not significantly interact with drugs metabolized by other CYP enzymes, including phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin.</li><li><strong>Diazepam</strong>: Co-administration may increase diazepam plasma levels by 45%.</li><li><strong>Gastric pH</strong>: Esomeprazole may reduce the absorption of certain medications, such as ketoconazole, iron salts, and digoxin, that depend on acidic gastric pH.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Contraindicated in individuals with known hypersensitivity to esomeprazole or any of its components.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Opton 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, dry mouth.</li><li><strong>Long-term therapy</strong>: No significant differences in side effects compared to short-term therapy.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Stomach Cancer</strong>: A positive symptom response does not rule out stomach cancer.</li><li><strong>Administration</strong>: Take capsules one hour before meals. For difficulty swallowing, the capsules can be opened and the pellets mixed with applesauce (cold and soft enough to swallow without chewing).</li><li><strong>Antacids</strong>: Acceptable during treatment.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Opton 20 mg</h3><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose include decreased motor activity, changes in breathing frequency, tremors, ataxia, and convulsions.</li><li><strong>Management</strong>: There is no specific countermeasure. Symptomatic and supportive care should be provided.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a dry area at temperatures not exceeding 30°C, away from moisture and light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Proton Pump Inhibition</strong>: Esomeprazole reduces stomach acid output by inhibiting the H+/K+-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell.</li><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: 90% bioavailability at repeated dosing. Take on an empty stomach.</li><li><strong>Metabolism</strong>: Metabolized by the CYP enzyme system in the liver, primarily CYP2C19 and CYP3A4.</li><li><strong>Excretion</strong>: Excreted mainly in urine (inactive metabolites).</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li>Limited research on pregnant women. No teratogenic effects identified in animal studies.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: Not studied; discontinue breastfeeding if esomeprazole is necessary.</li></ul><h3>Pediatric Use</h3><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><h3>Geriatric Use</h3><ul><li>No significant differences in safety or effectiveness in elderly versus younger patients.</li></ul><h3>Hepatic Insufficiency</h3><ul><li>For mild to moderate hepatic impairment, no dose change is needed. Limit to 20 mg/day in severe hepatic impairment.</li></ul><h3>Renal Insufficiency</h3><ul><li>No expected pharmacokinetic changes in patients with renal impairment as less than 1% is excreted unchanged in the urine.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '1898_1742569263.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 20:01:03', '2025-05-12 17:45:03'),
(3981, 'Fossical-D', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 13.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Sure, here is the text formatted with the headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Fossical-D</strong></p><p> This is indicated for the treatment &amp; prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, disorders of osteogenesis. Also used as a supplement in case of inadequate intake of Calcium in childhood diet, rickets, pregnancy &amp; lactation, elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis, phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p> Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li><li> Or</li><li>Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 200 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Adequate Calcium in the blood is so vital to a wide variety of bodily functions that our internal biochemistry will not tolerate a deficiency even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that Calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of Osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is also essential for healthy bones as it aids in Calcium absorption from the GI tract. In addition to this, it stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies also show that Calcium and Vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth as well as in Osteoporosis and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Fossical-D</strong></p><p> One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night or as directed by the Physician</p><p><strong>Dosage of Fossical-D</strong></p><p> One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or as directed by the physician. Taking it with a full stomach ensures better absorption.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Fossical-D</strong></p><p> Oral Calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline &amp; fluoride preparations, and a minimum of 3 hours should be allowed between ingestion of these medications. Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of Calcium. Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce metabolism of Vitamin D3. Concomitant ingestion of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of Calcium.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to any of the components, hypocalcemia resulting from overdose of Vitamin D3, hyperparathyroidism, bone metastases, severe renal insufficiency, severe hypercalciuria, renal calculi, etc.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Fossical-D</strong></p><p> Flatulence, diarrhea, constipation, upper GI discomfort, etc., are rare manifestations. Hypercalcemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> It should be used as directed by the physician during Pregnancy and Lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with a reduction of dose may be needed. Plasma and serum Calcium levels should be monitored in mild to moderate renal impairment and also in case of long-term use. Patients with renal stones or with a previous history of such stones should also take precautions.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Fossical-D</strong></p><p> At high doses, it may result in nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc. Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store at a temperature of below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> This is a Calcium and Vitamin D3 preparation where Calcium Carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The Calcium Carbonate from Coral has a chemical structure that is very similar to the composition of human bone. Coral Calcium is similar to other sources but ensures better absorption. Vitamin D3 aids in the absorption of Calcium from the GI tract and helps to maintain Calcium balance in the body.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of a physician.</p>', 'active', '1916_1742569388.jpg', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 20:03:08', '2025-03-21 20:03:08'),
(3982, 'Esonix M', 'Esomeprazole', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Esonix M 20 mg</strong></p><p>Esonix M 20 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Treatment of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</li><li>Healing of erosive esophagitis</li><li>Maintenance of healing of erosive esophagitis</li><li>Symptomatic Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</li><li>Reduction of NSAID-associated gastric ulcer</li><li>H. pylori eradication (Triple therapy)</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Esonix M 20 mg exerts its stomach acid-suppressing effects by preventing the final step in gastric acid production. It covalently binds to sulfhydryl groups of cysteines found on the H+/K+-ATPase enzyme at the secretory surface of gastric parietal cells. This effect inhibits both basal and stimulated gastric acid secretion, irrespective of the stimulus. As the binding of Esonix M 20 mg to the H+/K+-ATPase enzyme is irreversible, new enzyme expression is required to resume acid secretion, resulting in a duration of antisecretory effect lasting longer than 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Esonix M 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet or Capsule:</strong><em>Healing of erosive esophagitis:</em> 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4 to 8 weeks. The majority of patients heal within 4 to 8 weeks. For patients who do not heal, an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Maintenance of healing of erosive esophagitis:</em> 20 mg once daily (Controlled studies did not extend beyond six months).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Symptomatic GERD:</em> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>H. pylori eradication (Triple therapy):</em> 20 mg twice daily for 10 days along with Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days and Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection:</strong>The recommended adult dose in GERD with erosive esophagitis is either 20 or 40 mg once daily by intravenous injection (no less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Esonix M 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>A solution for intravenous infusion is prepared by first reconstituting the contents of one vial with 5 ml of 0.9% Sodium Chloride and further diluting the resulting solution to a final volume of 50 ml. The resultant concentration after dilution is 0.8 mg/ml.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>20 mg dose:</em> Withdraw 25 ml of the final solution and administer as an intravenous infusion over 10 to 30 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>10 mg dose:</em> Withdraw 12.5 ml of the final solution and administer as an intravenous infusion over 10 to 30 minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Esonix M 20 mg</strong></p><p>Esonix M 20 mg is a selective inhibitor of the cytochrome P450 mono-oxygenase system. There may be an effect on hepatic clearance, but no clinically relevant interactions have been reported. The effect of Esonix M 20 mg on the oral combined contraceptive pill is uncertain, and physiological changes due to reduced gastric acid can influence bacterial colonization in the stomach and duodenum and may affect vitamin B12 absorption.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Esonix M 20 mg is contraindicated in patients who have known hypersensitivity to any of the components of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Esonix M 20 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects reported include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Abdominal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The US FDA pregnancy category for Esonix M 20 mg is C. It should be avoided during pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Exclude the possibility of malignancy when gastric ulcer is suspected and before treatment for dyspepsia.</li><li>When using in combination with antibiotics, refer to the prescribing information of the respective antibiotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li>Directions for reconstitution: Prepare the solution for injection by adding 5 ml of 0.9% Sodium Chloride for intravenous injection into the vial containing the dry powder. The reconstituted solution is clear and colorless to very slightly yellow.</li></ul>', 'active', '150_1742569477.png', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 20:04:37', '2025-05-02 12:36:36'),
(3983, 'Entacyd Plus', 'Aluminium Hydroxide + Magnesium Hydroxide + Simethicone', 90.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '(200 mg+400 mg+30 mg)/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Entacyd 250 mg + 400 mg</strong></p><p>Aluminium Hydroxide and Magnesium Hydroxide is indicated for hyperacidity, peptic ulcer, gastritis, heartburn, sour stomach, and dyspepsia.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Dried Aluminium Hydroxide Gel BP (Al₂O₃ 47%, minimum): 250 mg</li><li>Magnesium Hydroxide BP: 400 mg</li></ul><p>Each 5 ml contains:</p><ul><li>Aluminium Oxide (Equivalent amount of Aluminium Hydroxide Gel USP): 175 mg</li><li>Magnesium Hydroxide (Equivalent amount of Magnesium Hydroxide Paste USP): 225 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This drug is a well-balanced combination of essential non-systemic antacids that excel in efficacy and palatability. These are dependable antacid preparations without acid rebound, constipating, or cathartic effects. Both preparations provide symptomatic relief of hyperacidity associated with heartburn, acid ingestion, or sour stomach.</p><ul><li>Aluminium hydroxide gel is a slow-acting antacid and adsorbent with a prolonged effect, having high neutralizing power.</li><li>Magnesium Hydroxide possesses a slow but sustained acid-neutralizing property.</li></ul><p>Antacids of both tablet and suspension forms possess adsorbent properties, forming a protective coating over the ulcer surface, facilitating its healing, and thus protecting the sensitive mucosa of the stomach and duodenum from further irritation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Entacyd 250 mg + 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>: Two tablets 1–3 hours after meals and at bedtime, or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Suspension</strong>: 2 teaspoonfuls 1–3 hours after meals and at bedtime, or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Entacyd 250 mg + 400 mg</strong></p><p>This drug inhibits the absorption of the following drugs:</p><ul><li>Azithromycin, cefpodoxime, ciprofloxacin, isoniazid, rifampicin, norfloxacin, ofloxacin, pivampicillin, tetracyclines, gabapentin, phenytoin, itraconazole, ketoconazole, chloroquine, hydroxychloroquine, and phenothiazines.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>This drug is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Hypophosphatemia</li><li>Alkalosis</li><li>Hypermagnesemia, particularly where abdominal distention may be due to partial or complete intestinal obstruction.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Entacyd 250 mg + 400 mg</strong></p><p>Long-term use of any antacid may result in alkaluria, which can predispose to nephrolithiasis by precipitating calcium phosphate.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>It is advised to avoid antacid preparations in the first trimester of pregnancy.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Antacids may reduce the absorption of tetracycline when given concomitantly. These should not be used together.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This drug is a well-balanced combination of essential non-systemic antacids, providing effective and palatable relief without acid rebound, constipation, or cathartic effects. Both the tablet and suspension forms help neutralize excess stomach acid and protect the ulcer surface, promoting healing.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>It is advised to avoid antacid preparations in the first trimester of pregnancy.</li></ul>', 'active', '1323_1742569690.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 20:08:10', '2025-05-03 10:06:55'),
(3984, 'Ovocal-DX', 'Eggshell Calcium + Vitamin D3', 17.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ovocal-DX</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment and prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, and osteogenesis. It can be used as a supplement in cases of inadequate calcium intake in childhood diet, pregnancy, and lactation, rickets, and poor absorption during old age. Other indications include pancreatitis and phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Calcium is absorbed in the intestine, then passed through the intestine into the blood and deposited into bone. Active transport through cells is dependent on Vitamin D3. Vitamin D3 keeps bones healthy by increasing the absorption of calcium in the intestines. Without Vitamin D3, the body will be able to absorb only 10% to 15% of the calcium from the diet.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ovocal-DX</strong></p><p>One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or food, as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interactions of Ovocal-DX</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Major interaction</strong>: Administering intravenous Ceftriaxone &amp; Calcium can result in life-threatening damage to the lungs &amp; kidneys. Calcium should not be administered intravenously within 48 hours of intravenous Ceftriaxone.</li><li><strong>Minor interaction</strong>: Calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline &amp; fluoride preparations. A minimum of 3 hours should be allowed between ingestion of these medications. Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of calcium. Phenytoin, barbiturates &amp; glucocorticoids may induce the metabolism of Vitamin D3. Intake of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of calcium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Calcium intake is contraindicated in conditions like increased activity of the parathyroid gland, hypercalcemia, constipation, kidney stones, and extreme loss of body water.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ovocal-DX</strong></p><p>Flatulence, diarrhea, constipation, and allergic reactions are rare. Hypercalcemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This should be given to pregnant &amp; lactating mothers as per the recommendation of a physician. Toxicity was tested on pregnant &amp; lactating females with excellent results.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In mild hypercalciuria, a reduction in the dose is sufficient to return to normal serum calcium concentration. Patients with kidney stones or a history of such should be recommended to increase their fluid intake.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</p>', 'active', '682_1742569784.jpg', 55, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 20:09:44', '2025-04-18 22:09:14'),
(3985, 'Lumona', 'Montelukast Sodium', 8.00, 30, 'x 3.5 gm sachet', '3.5 gm sachet', 'Oral Powder', '4 mg/3.5 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Lumona 4 Sachet</strong></p><p>Lumona 4 Sachet is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The metabolism of arachidonic acid results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from various cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. Cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) are located in the human airway, and these eicosanoids bind to them. The orally active drug Lumona 4 Sachet has a strong affinity and specificity for the CysLT1 receptor. Without any agonist activity, it inhibits LTD4\'s physiological effects at the CysLT1 receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Lumona 4 Sachet</strong></p><p><strong>Adults and adolescents with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</strong></p><ul><li>Dosage for adults and adolescents 15 years of age and older: 10 mg tablet once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</strong></p><ul><li>Dosage for pediatric patients 6 to 14 years of age: 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li>Dosage for pediatric patients 2 years to 5 years of age: 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li>Dosage for pediatric patients 6 months to 5 years of age: 4 mg oral granules once daily. This can be administered directly in the mouth or mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in pediatric patients:</strong></p><p>Safety and efficacy of Lumona have been established in controlled studies with pediatric patients (6 months to 14 years of age). The safety and efficacy profiles in this age group are similar to those seen in adults.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency:</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is required for patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is recommended for patients with renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Elderly Use:</strong></p><p>The pharmacokinetic profile and oral bioavailability of a single 10-mg oral dose are similar in elderly and younger adults. The plasma half-life is slightly longer in the elderly. No dosage adjustment is required for elderly patients.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Lumona 4 Sachet</strong></p><p>In the prevention and long-term treatment of asthma, Lumona 4 Sachet has been used alongside other common treatments without significant negative side effects. Studies on drug interactions show no clinically significant effects on pharmacokinetics when used with theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives (norethindrone 1mg/ethinyl estradiol 35mcg), terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin at recommended doses. However, when used with hepatic metabolism-inducing drugs like phenobarbital, AUC was reduced by about 40%. Proper clinical monitoring is advised when combined with potent cytochrome P450 enzyme inducers like phenobarbital or rifampin.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Lumona 4 Sachet should not be taken by individuals with a hypersensitivity to any ingredient in the medication.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Lumona 4 Sachet</strong></p><p>Common:</p><ul><li>Upper respiratory tract infection</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Fever</li><li>Gastrointestinal pain</li><li>Headache</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li></ul><p>Uncommon:</p><ul><li>Akathisia</li><li>Anxiety</li><li>Arthralgia</li><li>Asthenia</li><li>Aberrant behavior</li><li>Depression</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Hemorrhage</li><li>Irritability</li><li>Malaise</li><li>Muscle complaints</li><li>Oedema</li><li>Seizure</li><li>Odd feeling</li><li>Sleep disturbances</li></ul><p>Rare:</p><ul><li>Pulmonary eosinophilia</li><li>Angioedema</li><li>Decreased attention</li><li>Disorientation</li><li>Erythema nodosum</li><li>Hallucinations</li><li>Hepatic problems</li><li>Memory loss</li><li>Palpitations</li><li>Suicidal thoughts</li><li>Tremor</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Lumona 4 Sachet is classified as pregnancy category B. Although it has been demonstrated to cross the placenta in pregnant rats and rabbits, there is insufficient data on its use in pregnant humans. While some evidence suggests it is secreted in breast milk, the implications are not fully understood. Caution should be exercised when administering to nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Lumona 4 Sachet should not be used to reverse bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks, including status asthmaticus. Patients should have access to appropriate rescue medication for acute asthma exacerbations. This medication should not replace corticosteroids for acute asthma management. Patients with documented aspirin sensitivity should avoid aspirin or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) during treatment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Lumona 4 Sachet</strong></p><p>Overdose reports mostly show mild side effects such as nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain. In case of overdose, supportive measures like removing unabsorbed material from the digestive tract and clinical monitoring are recommended.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry place, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Lumona 4 Sachet is a selective and orally active leukotriene receptor antagonist. It inhibits the CysLT1 receptor, blocking the effects of cysteinyl leukotrienes in the pathophysiology of asthma and allergic rhinitis. This helps reduce airway edema, smooth muscle contraction, and inflammatory responses associated with these conditions.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>After oral administration, Lumona 4 Sachet crosses the placenta in rats and rabbits, but there is limited information on its effects in pregnant humans. It should only be used during pregnancy if absolutely necessary. Care should be taken when administering to breastfeeding mothers, as it may be secreted in breast milk.</p>', 'active', '152_1742569956.png', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 20:12:36', '2025-05-03 23:36:40');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3986, 'Zocil', 'Cilostazol', 12.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information on <strong>Zocil 100 mg</strong> in a cleaner format with proper bold headings:</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Indications of Zocil 100 mg</strong></h3><p>Zocil 100 mg is indicated for the reduction of symptoms of intermittent claudication, as evidenced by an increased walking distance.</p><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p>Peripheral Vasodilator drugs: Intermittent Claudication</p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Zocil 100 mg is a preparation of Zocil 100 mg, which is a quinolinone derivative. Its mechanism of action is the specific inhibition of cellular phosphodiesterase III (PDE III), suppressing cAMP degradation. This results in increased cAMP in platelets and blood vessels, leading to inhibition of platelet aggregation and vasodilation.</p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><p>The recommended dosage of Zocil 100 mg is 100 mg twice daily (bid), taken at least half an hour before or two hours after breakfast and dinner.</p><ul><li>A dose of <strong>50 mg bid</strong> should be considered when coadministering with:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fluconazole</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Ketoconazole</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Itraconazole</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Erythromycin</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clarithromycin</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fluvoxamine</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fluoxetine</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nefazodone</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sertraline</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diltiazem</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric use:</strong> The safety and effectiveness of Zocil 100 mg in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><h3><strong>Interaction of Zocil 100 mg</strong></h3><ul><li>Pharmacokinetic studies have demonstrated that <strong>Omeprazole</strong> and <strong>Erythromycin</strong> significantly increase the systemic exposure of Zocil 100 mg and/or its major metabolites.</li><li>Population pharmacokinetic studies showed higher concentrations of Zocil 100 mg in patients concurrently treated with <strong>Diltiazem</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><p>Zocil 100 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients with congestive heart failure of any severity.</li><li>Patients with known or suspected hypersensitivity to any of its components.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Most common side effects</strong>: headache, diarrhea, vomiting, leg cramps, rash.</li><li><strong>Less frequent side effects</strong>: anorexia, edema.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women.</li><li>Transfer of Zocil 100 mg into milk has been reported in experimental animals. Due to the potential risk to nursing infants, a decision should be made to discontinue nursing or to discontinue Zocil 100 mg.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><p>Zocil 100 mg should be used with caution in patients with any degree of heart failure. There is no information regarding the efficacy or safety of concurrent use with <strong>Clopidogrel</strong>.</p><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><p>In case of overdose, symptoms may include:</p><ul><li>Severe headache</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Tachycardia</li><li>Possibly cardiac arrhythmias Supportive treatment and careful observation are recommended.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><p>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light and moisture.</p><h3><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use</strong>: The safety and effectiveness of Zocil 100 mg in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><p>Peripheral Vasodilator drugs: Intermittent Claudication</p><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Zocil 100 mg is a quinolinone derivative. It works by inhibiting cellular phosphodiesterase III (PDE III), which suppresses cAMP degradation. This leads to increased cAMP in platelets and blood vessels, resulting in inhibition of platelet aggregation and vasodilation.</p><h3><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h3><p>There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Transfer of Zocil 100 mg into milk has been reported in experimental animals. Due to the potential risk to nursing infants, a decision should be made to discontinue nursing or to discontinue Zocil 100 mg.</p><p><br></p><p>Let me know if you need further details!</p>', 'active', '298_1742575691.webp', 3, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 21:48:11', '2025-05-05 22:37:07'),
(3987, 'Zometa', 'Zoledronic Acid [For hypercalcemia]', 10182.92, 1, 'x 4 mg vial', '4 mg vial', 'IV Infusion', '4 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Zometa 4 mg/5 ml</h3><p>Zoledronic Acid (Zometa) is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypercalcemia of malignancy</strong></li><li><strong>Bone metastases associated with solid tumors</strong></li><li><strong>Osteolytic lesions associated with multiple myeloma</strong></li><li><strong>Corticosteroid-induced osteoporosis</strong></li><li><strong>Increase in bone mass in men with osteoporosis</strong></li><li><strong>Osteoporosis in postmenopausal women</strong></li><li><strong>Paget\'s disease of bone</strong></li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of postmenopausal osteoporosis</strong></li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Bisphosphonate preparations</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Zoledronic acid is a nitrogen-containing bisphosphonate that acts primarily on bone by inhibiting osteoclast-mediated bone resorption. It has a high affinity for mineralized bone and targets farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in osteoclasts, although other mechanisms may also be involved. Clinical studies have shown that Zoledronic acid can decrease serum calcium and urinary calcium excretion in hypercalcemia of malignancy. It also has anti-tumor, anti-angiogenic, anti-pain, cytostatic, and pro-apoptotic activities. Zoledronic acid is not metabolized and is excreted unchanged via the kidneys.</p><h3>Dosage of Zometa 4 mg/5 ml</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypercalcemia of malignancy</strong>: The maximum recommended dose is <strong>4 mg</strong> administered as a single intravenous infusion. Dose adjustment is not required for mild-to-moderate renal impairment. Re-treatment can be considered after 7 days if serum calcium does not normalize.</li><li><strong>Multiple myeloma and bone metastases of solid tumors</strong>: The recommended dose is <strong>4 mg</strong> every 3-4 weeks. Patients should also receive an oral calcium supplement of <strong>500 mg</strong> and <strong>400 IU</strong> of Vitamin D daily.</li></ul><h3>Administration of Zometa 4 mg/5 ml</h3><ul><li>Dilute the required amount of concentrate from one vial with <strong>100 ml of calcium-free infusion solution</strong> (0.9% sodium chloride or 5% glucose solution).</li><li>The infusion duration should not be less than <strong>15 minutes</strong>.</li><li>If stored, the infusion solution should be kept at <strong>2-8º C</strong> for up to <strong>24 hours</strong>. Allow it to return to room temperature before administration.</li><li>Zoledronic acid should not be mixed with calcium or other divalent cation-containing infusion solutions (e.g., Lactated Ringer\'s solution).</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Zometa 4 mg/5 ml</h3><ul><li>Zoledronic acid has been used with common anticancer agents, diuretics, antibiotics, and analgesics without significant interactions.</li><li><strong>Aminoglycosides</strong> may have an additive effect to lower serum calcium levels.</li><li><strong>Thalidomide</strong> with Zoledronic acid may increase the risk of renal dysfunction.</li><li><strong>Loop diuretics</strong> may increase the risk of hypocalcemia.</li><li>Caution is advised when using Zoledronic acid with other potentially nephrotoxic drugs.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to Zoledronic acid or any of its excipients or bisphosphonates</li><li><strong>Severe renal impairment</strong> (Creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min)</li><li><strong>Pregnancy and lactation</strong></li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Zometa 4 mg/5 ml</h3><p>Possible side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache, nausea, anorexia, fatigue, osteonecrosis of jaw, anemia, bone pain, constipation, fever, vomiting, flu-like syndrome, hypocalcemia, myalgia, arthralgia, and hypophosphatemia.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Contraindicated during pregnancy</strong> and <strong>lactation</strong>.</li><li>Not recommended for children and adolescents below 18 years.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Ensure <strong>adequate hydration</strong> prior to administration, especially for elderly patients or those on diuretic therapy.</li><li>Monitor <strong>serum calcium, phosphate, magnesium, potassium</strong>, and <strong>creatinine</strong> after therapy.</li><li>If <strong>hypocalcemia, hypophosphatemia</strong>, or <strong>hypomagnesemia</strong> occurs, short-term supplemental therapy may be needed.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Zometa 4 mg/5 ml</h3><p>Overdose may cause <strong>hypocalcemia</strong>, <strong>hypophosphatemia</strong>, and <strong>hypomagnesemia</strong>. Treatment includes intravenous administration of:</p><ul><li><strong>Calcium gluconate</strong> (for hypocalcemia)</li><li><strong>Potassium or sodium phosphate</strong> (for hypophosphatemia)</li><li><strong>Magnesium sulfate</strong> (for hypomagnesemia)</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong> prior to opening.</li><li>Protect from moisture and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach and sight of children.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>: Zoledronic acid is not recommended in patients with severe renal impairment (Creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min). No dose adjustment is necessary for patients with creatinine clearance &gt;60 ml/min. For impaired renal function, doses should be adjusted based on the following:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl &gt; 60 ml/min: 4 mg (5 ml)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 50-60 ml/min: 3.5 mg (4.4 ml)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 40-49 ml/min: 3.3 mg (4.1 ml)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">CrCl 30-39 ml/min: 3 mg (3.8 ml)</li></ul><h3>Reconstitution</h3><ul><li>Dilute the required amount in <strong>100 mL of NaCl 0.9%</strong> or <strong>dextrose 5%</strong>.</li></ul>', 'active', '1635_1742575829.jpg', 40, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 21:50:29', '2025-05-05 22:38:43'),
(3988, 'Zopime', 'Cefepime Hydrochloride', 300.00, 1, 'x 500 mg vial', '500 mg vial', 'IV Injection', '500 mg/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<p>It seems like you\'ve shared a comprehensive set of details about <strong>Zopime (Cefepime)</strong>, including its indications, dosage instructions, interactions, contraindications, side effects, and more. Here\'s a more concise summary of the key points:</p><h3><strong>Indications of Zopime (Cefepime 500 mg/vial)</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pneumonia (moderate to severe)</strong>: Caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Enterobacter</em> species.</li><li><strong>Febrile Neutropenia</strong>: Empiric treatment of febrile neutropenic patients, especially in high-risk cases.</li><li><strong>Urinary Tract Infections (UTIs)</strong>: Both uncomplicated and complicated, including pyelonephritis caused by <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, or <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>.</li><li><strong>Skin Infections</strong>: Uncomplicated skin infections caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> or <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>.</li><li><strong>Intra-abdominal Infections</strong>: In combination with metronidazole, for infections caused by <em>E. coli</em>, <em>P. aeruginosa</em>, <em>K. pneumoniae</em>, <em>Enterobacter</em> species, or <em>Bacteroides fragilis</em>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Intravenous (IV)</strong>: Administered over 30 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pneumonia</strong>: 1-2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Febrile Neutropenia</strong>: 2 gm IV every 8 hours for 7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>UTIs</strong>: 0.5-1 gm IV every 12 hours for 7-10 days (for mild to moderate); 2 gm every 12 hours for severe cases.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Skin Infections</strong>: 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intra-abdominal Infections</strong>: 2 gm IV every 12 hours for 7-10 days.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Can cause hypersensitivity reactions (rash, anaphylaxis).</li><li>Possible gastrointestinal disturbances (diarrhea, nausea).</li><li><strong>Neurotoxicity</strong>: High doses may cause encephalopathy, seizures, or myoclonus, especially in patients with renal insufficiency.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Dosage adjustments are needed for patients with kidney dysfunction.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Elderly patients with renal issues are at higher risk for serious adverse effects.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B; use only if clearly needed during pregnancy, and with caution in breastfeeding women.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</h3><ul><li>History of hypersensitivity reactions to cephalosporins or beta-lactam antibiotics.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place and use before the expiry date. Keep away from children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Special Populations</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric</strong>: Dosage should not exceed adult recommendations. Typically, 50 mg/kg every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Function</strong>: No adjustment needed for impaired liver function.</li><li><strong>Renal Function</strong>: Adjust dosage for patients with creatinine clearance &lt;60 ml/min.</li></ul><p>This summary includes the essential information, but always follow the specific guidelines provided by your healthcare provider. Let me know if you need additional details!</p>', 'active', '1841_1742576004.jpg', 1, 12, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 21:53:24', '2025-05-05 22:39:06'),
(3989, 'Neotison', 'Fluticasone Furoate', 275.00, 1, 'x 120 metered sprays', '120 metered sprays', 'Nasal Spray', '27.5 mcg/spray', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Neotison 27.5 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neotison 27.5 mcg Nasal Spray</strong>: For the treatment of the symptoms of seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis in patients aged 2 years and older.</li><li><strong>Neotison 27.5 mcg Inhalation Powder</strong>: For once-daily maintenance treatment of asthma as prophylactic therapy in patients aged 5 years and older. This is <strong>not</strong> for the relief of acute bronchospasm.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Nasal Steroid Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Neotison 27.5 mcg is a synthetic trifluorinated corticosteroid with potent anti-inflammatory activity. It acts on multiple cell types (e.g., mast cells, eosinophils, neutrophils, macrophages, lymphocytes) and mediators (e.g., histamine, eicosanoids, leukotrienes, cytokines) involved in inflammation.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Neotison 27.5 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neotison 27.5 mcg Nasal Spray</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults &amp; Children over 12 years: 2 sprays in each nostril once a day. In some cases, 2 sprays into each nostril twice daily, not exceeding 4 sprays.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children under 12 years (2-11 Years): 1 spray in each nostril once a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children under 2 years: No data to recommend use for the treatment of allergic rhinitis.</li><li><strong>Neotison 27.5 mcg Inhalation Capsule</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 inhalation once daily by the orally inhaled route.</li><li>Adults and Adolescents Aged 12 Years and Older:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Starting dosage should be based on asthma severity, with a typical starting dose of 100 mcg for those not on an inhaled corticosteroid (ICS).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">If no response after 2 weeks, consider increasing the dose to 200 mcg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The highest recommended dose is 200 mcg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric Patients Aged 5 to 11 Years</strong>: 50 mcg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Neotison 27.5 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>How to Use the Nasal Spray</strong>:</li></ul><ol><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Shake the bottle gently and remove the dust cover.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hold the spray with your forefinger and middle finger on either side of the nozzle and your thumb underneath the bottle.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Press down until a fine spray appears.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If using for the first time or after 1 week of non-use, prime the device by pressing the nozzle 6 times until a fine mist is visible.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gently blow your nose to clear the nostrils.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Close one nostril and insert the applicator into the open nostril. Tilt your head slightly forward and spray while inhaling through your nose. Repeat in the other nostril.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clean the spray weekly: Remove the dust cover, gently pull off the applicator, wash it with warm water, and leave to dry.</li></ol><p><strong>Interactions of Neotison 27.5 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>Potent inhibitors of cytochrome P450 3A4 (CYP3A4) may increase exposure to Neotison 27.5 mcg.</li><li><strong>Avoid coadministration with ritonavir</strong> and use caution with other potent CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ketoconazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Neotison 27.5 mcg nasal spray is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to any of its ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Neotison 27.5 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Nasal Spray</strong>: Dryness of the nose and increased incidence of nosebleeds.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Capsule</strong> (≥5% incidence in adults and adolescents): Nasopharyngitis, bronchitis, upper respiratory tract infection, headache.</li><li><strong>In Pediatric Subjects (5-11 years)</strong>: Pharyngitis, bronchitis, viral infection.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C. Neotison 27.5 mcg should only be used during pregnancy if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known if Neotison 27.5 mcg is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administering to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Rare hypersensitivity reactions, contact dermatitis, wheezing, nasal septum perforation, cataracts, glaucoma, and increased intraocular pressure may occur.</li><li>Systemic effects are minimal with the recommended doses, but the risk increases with higher doses. Avoid larger-than-recommended doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at a temperature not exceeding 30°C. Do not refrigerate. Protect from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in children under 2 years of age have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Use caution due to a higher frequency of decreased hepatic, renal, or cardiac function in elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Use Neotison 27.5 mcg with caution in patients with severe hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is required in patients with renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Nasal Steroid Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Neotison 27.5 mcg is a synthetic trifluorinated corticosteroid that exerts potent anti-inflammatory effects by interacting with various cell types and mediators involved in the inflammatory response.</p>', 'active', '1969_1742576176.jpg', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 21:56:16', '2025-05-04 00:35:49'),
(3990, 'D-Vine', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 75.00, 1, 'x 15 ml bottle', '15 ml bottle', 'Pediatric Drops', '200 IU/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the revised text with headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of D-Vine 15 ml</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated in the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also indicated as an adjunct to specific therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcaemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with vitamin D3 deficiency. Cholecalciferol, a synthetic form of Vitamin D, is essential for normal bone growth and development and maintaining bone density. It is also necessary for the utilization of both calcium and phosphorus. Babies need Vitamin D3 for healthy growth and development. It acts as a hormone.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin-D preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) helps with the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. Vitamin D3 is essential for normal bone growth and to maintain bone density. It also reduces the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, prevents the risk of cancer (breast, colorectal), and helps to maintain adequate insulin levels for type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of D-Vine 15 ml</strong></p><p>For <strong>capsules</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance therapy: 1,400-2,000 IU/day. Measurement of 25-hydroxyvitamin D levels should be done 3-4 months after initiating maintenance therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 20,000 IU every 4 weeks. Higher doses may be required in certain situations.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For osteoporosis therapy: 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li><strong>Children (12-18 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 20,000 IU once every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>For film-coated tablets</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1,000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily or as directed by the physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li><li><strong>For chewable or oroflash tablets</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1,000 IU to 2,000 IU daily, or as directed by the physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li><li><strong>For Syrup</strong>:</li><li>For patients with risk of Cholecalciferol deficiency:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">0-1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml)</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml)</blockquote><ul><li>For Cholecalciferol deficient patients:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">0-1 year: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 years: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>:</li><li>Prevention:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Infants receiving Vitamin D enriched milk: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Nursed infants or young children up to 5 years: 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adolescents: 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Pregnancy: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Elderly: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of D-Vine 15 ml</strong></p><p>It interferes with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (such as liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of D-Vine 15 ml</strong></p><p>General side effects include hypercalcaemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, and abdominal pain.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Studies have shown the safe use of doses up to 4,000 IU during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU. However, in women considered to be Vitamin D3 deficient, a higher dose may be required. During pregnancy, women should follow the advice of their medical practitioner as their requirements may vary depending on the severity of their condition and their response to treatment. Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. Overdose in infants induced by nursing mothers has not been observed; however, when prescribing additional Vitamin D3 to a breast-fed child, the practitioner should consider the dose of any additional Vitamin D3 given to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>It should be used with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of D-Vine 15 ml</strong></p><p>It can lead to hypervitaminosis D.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) helps with the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. Vitamin D3 is essential for normal bone growth and to maintain bone density. It also reduces the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, prevents the risk of cancer (breast, colorectal), and helps to maintain adequate insulin levels for type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Studies have shown safe use of doses up to 4,000 IU during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU, but a higher dose may be required for those who are Vitamin D3 deficient. During pregnancy, women should follow their medical practitioner’s advice based on the severity of their condition.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</p>', 'active', '1570_1742576582.webp', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:03:02', '2025-05-03 07:49:50'),
(3991, 'Seclo MUPS', 'Omeprazole', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Seclo MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><p>Omeprazole (Multiple-Unit Pellet System) is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and Gastric ulcers</li><li>NSAID-induced gastric and duodenal ulcers</li><li>Reflux Esophagitis</li><li>GERD (Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease)</li><li>Eradication of H. pylori with appropriate antibiotics</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated MUPS tablet contains 20 mg Omeprazole enteric-coated micro pellets.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Omeprazole belongs to a class of antisecretory compounds, the substituted benzimidazoles, which suppress gastric acid secretion by specifically inhibiting the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system at the secretory surface of the gastric parietal cell. This system is considered the \"proton pump,\" and omeprazole blocks the final step of acid production. The effect is dose-dependent and inhibits both basal and stimulated acid secretion, regardless of the stimulus.</p><p><strong>Clinical Advantages of Omeprazole MUPS Tablet Over Conventional Modified-Release Tablets and Pellet-Filled Capsules</strong></p><ul><li>Greater bioavailability</li><li>Uniform emptying of micro pellets from the stomach into the small intestine</li><li>Rapid dissolution of the enteric coating and drug release, resulting in early Tmax and Cmax (peak time and peak plasma concentration)</li><li>Lesser possibility of dose dumping</li><li>Combination of fast-acting and sustained action</li><li>Once-daily dosing</li><li>Lesser chance of localized irritation</li><li>Better and more uniform drug absorption</li><li>Superior in reducing esophageal residence time</li><li>Minimizes fluctuation in plasma concentration of the drug</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Seclo MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>GERD (Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease):</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastric ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks; in severe cases, twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks; in severe cases, twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>NSAID-induced ulceration:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Reflux esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks; in severe cases, twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>H. pylori eradication (with Amoxicillin and Clarithromycin or Metronidazole):</strong> 20 mg twice daily for 1 week</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> 60 mg once daily; usual maintenance dose is 20-120 mg daily</li><li><strong>Children:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acid regurgitation in GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Reflux esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Seclo MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Swallow the tablet whole with a glass of water. Do not chew or crush the tablet.</li><li>If swallowing is difficult, place the tablet in a glass of water (use only water). Stir until the tablet disintegrates and drink the mixture within 30 minutes. Stir just before drinking.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Seclo MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><p>Omeprazole is metabolized via CYP2C19. When starting or stopping treatment with Omeprazole, potential interactions with other drugs metabolized by CYP2C19 should be considered.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Omeprazole is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Not known to be harmful. Omeprazole can be used during pregnancy.</li><li>Omeprazole is excreted in breast milk, but it is not likely to influence the child when used in therapeutic doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Omeprazole should be used with caution in patients with severe liver dysfunction or severe renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool place (below 30°C), dry, and protected from light and moisture.</li></ul>', 'active', '1454_1742576815.jpg', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:06:55', '2025-05-04 20:59:33'),
(3992, 'Solas', 'Mebendazole', 18.25, 1, 'x 30 ml bottle', '30 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '100 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Solas 30 ml</strong></p><p>Solas 30 ml is indicated for the treatment of threadworms, whipworms, roundworms, and hookworms.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Anthelmintic</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Solas 30 ml is a synthetic derivative of benzimidazole with potent anthelmintic activity against the most common nematodes and some cestodes. It is highly effective against ascariasis, enterobiasis, trichuriasis, and hookworm infections. It is equally effective in single or mixed infections. The drug\'s effect is due to its ability to irreversibly inhibit glucose uptake, but it does not affect blood glucose concentration in the host, even in high doses. Solas 30 ml does not necessitate any dosage calculation since the same dosage is used for both children and adults. It can be taken at any time, and no special procedures, such as purging, use of laxatives, or dietary changes, are required. Parasite immobilization and death occur slowly, and clearance from the gastrointestinal tract may not be completed until 3 days after administration.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Solas 30 ml</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children over 2 years:Threadworms:</strong> 100 mg or 1 teaspoonful – Single dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Whipworms, Roundworms, Hookworms:</strong> 100 mg or 1 teaspoonful – Twice daily for 3 days. If reinfection occurs, a second dose may be needed after 2 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Solas 30 ml</strong></p><p>May be taken with or without food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Solas 30 ml</strong></p><p>Preliminary evidence suggests that cimetidine inhibits Solas 30 ml metabolism and may result in an increase in plasma concentration.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Solas 30 ml is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Solas 30 ml or to any component of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Solas 30 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Transient symptoms of abdominal pain and diarrhea in case of massive infection and expulsion of worms.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity:</strong> Rash, urticaria, and angioedema have been observed on rare occasions.</li><li><strong>Central Nervous System:</strong> Very rare cases of convulsions have been reported.</li><li><strong>Hematologic:</strong> Neutropenia and agranulocytosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Solas 30 ml should not be administered during pregnancy or to women thought to be pregnant. It is not known whether Solas 30 ml or its metabolites are secreted in human breast milk. Therefore, it should not be used during lactation unless the potential benefits outweigh the potential risks.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General:</strong> Periodic assessment of organ system functions, including hematopoietic and hepatic, is advisable during prolonged therapy.</li><li><strong>Information for Patients:</strong> Patients should be informed of the potential risk to the fetus in women taking Solas 30 ml during pregnancy, especially during the first trimester. Patients should also be informed that cleanliness is important to prevent reinfection and transmission of the infection.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Solas 30 ml</strong></p><p>In the event of accidental overdosage, gastrointestinal complaints lasting up to a few hours may occur. Vomiting and purging should be induced.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool (below 25°C) and dry place, protected from light. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Anthelmintic</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Solas 30 ml is a synthetic broad-spectrum anthelmintic that is active against most nematodes and some other worms. Solas 30 ml is principally used in the treatment of intestinal nematode infection. Solas 30 ml inhibits the formation of the worms\' microtubules, causing the worms\' glucose depletion. After oral administration, about 2-10% of the oral dose is absorbed from the GI tract, with peak plasma concentration occurring within 30 minutes to 7 hours. Solas 30 ml is highly bound to plasma proteins. Its elimination half-life is 2.8 to 9 hours.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Solas 30 ml is not recommended in pregnant women. It is not known whether Solas 30 ml is excreted in human milk. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk, caution should be exercised when it is administered to a nursing mother.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>The drug has not been extensively studied in children under two years; therefore, in the treatment of children under two years, the relative benefit/risk should be considered.</p>', 'active', '735_1742576967.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:09:27', '2025-05-04 21:15:05'),
(3993, 'Renovit', 'Vitamin B1, B6 & B12', 9.00, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '100 mg+200 mg+200 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Renovit</strong></p><p>Renovit is indicated for the treatment of vitamin B1, B6, and B12 deficiency syndrome. It is also used for the supportive treatment of neuritis and non-inflammatory diseases of the nerves, such as:</p><ul><li>Diabetic neuropathy</li><li>Peripheral neuralgia</li><li>Lumbago</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Optic neuritis</li><li>Sciatica</li><li>Facial neuralgia</li><li>Intercostal neuralgia</li><li>Spinal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate) 100 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride) 200 mg</li><li>Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) 200 mcg</li></ul><p>Each 3 ml ampoule contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) 100 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) 100 mg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) 1000 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Renovit is a combined preparation of Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate), Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride), and Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin). These vitamins play essential roles as coenzymes in the metabolism of the nervous system. They support the regeneration of nerve fibers and the myelin sheath by activating nerve cell metabolism and natural repair mechanisms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablets</strong>: 1 to 3 tablets per day or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly (deep intragluteal).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In severe cases, 1 ampoule daily until the acute symptoms subside.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For milder cases and follow-up therapy, 2 to 3 ampoules per week.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Children</strong></p><p>There is no information available on the use of this drug in children.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Renovit</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported yet.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Renovit is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients on levodopa therapy</li><li>Patients with hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Renovit</strong></p><p>Generally well tolerated, but allergic reactions may be observed in some cases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>No risks have been identified with the use of this combination during pregnancy at the recommended dosage.</li><li>Vitamin B1, B6, and B12 are secreted into breast milk, but the risks of overdose for the infant are not known.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Cyanocobalamin should not be given to patients with subacute degeneration of the spinal cord.</li><li>Cyanocobalamin is not suitable for the treatment of optic neuropathies associated with raised plasma concentrations of cyanocobalamin.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Renovit</strong></p><p>No overdosage symptoms are expected when following the recommended dosage. In case of overdose, treatment should be symptomatic and supportive.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Store in a cool (below 25°C) and dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin B1</strong>: Converts carbohydrates, fatty acids, and amino acids into energy, promotes healthy nerves, improves mood, and strengthens the heart.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6</strong>: Forms RBCs, helps cells make proteins, manufactures neurotransmitters like serotonin, releases stored energy, helps prevent cardiovascular diseases, stroke, depression, and eases insomnia.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12</strong>: Essential for cell replication, RBC production, preventing anemia, preventing depression, reducing nerve pain, numbness, tingling, and lowering the risk of heart diseases.</li></ul><p>The vitamin ingredients are well absorbed when taken orally and widely distributed to most tissues. They appear in breast milk. Thiamine is mostly present as diphosphate within the cell and is not stored in the body in significant amounts. Pyridoxine is absorbed from the GIT and converted to active forms, stored mainly in the liver, and excreted in the urine. As the dose increases, greater amounts are excreted unchanged in the urine.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Oral tablet form is recommended, but due to the presence of benzyl alcohol, the injection is not recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</p>', 'active', '843_1742577116.jpg', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:11:56', '2025-05-04 15:13:24'),
(3994, 'Filwel Silver', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral [A-Z silver preparation]', 12.00, 30, 'Tablet', '30 tablet in a pack', 'Tablet', 'N/A', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Sure! Here\'s a revised version of the information you provided with the headings in bold:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Filwel Silver</strong></p><p>This is specially formulated for the prevention and treatment of vitamin and mineral deficiencies for adults over 45 years of age. This Silver is also indicated to meet the increased demands of vitamins and minerals for adults over 45 years of age.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Vitamin A 3500 IU</li><li>Vitamin C 60 mg</li><li>Vitamin D 400 IU</li><li>Vitamin E 45 IU</li><li>Vitamin K 10 mcg</li><li>Thiamin 1.5 mg</li><li>Riboflavin 1.7 mg</li><li>Niacin 20 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 3 mg</li><li>Folic acid 400 mcg</li><li>Vitamin B12 25 mcg</li><li>Biotin 30 mcg</li><li>Pantothenic acid 10 mg</li><li>Calcium 200 mg</li><li>Phosphorous 48 mg</li><li>Iodine 150 mcg</li><li>Magnesium 100 mg</li><li>Zinc 15 mg</li><li>Selenium 20 mcg</li><li>Copper 2 mg</li><li>Manganese 2 mg</li><li>Chromium 150 mcg</li><li>Molybdenum 75 mcg</li><li>Chloride 72 mg</li><li>Potassium 80 mg</li><li>Boron 150 mcg</li><li>Nickel 5 mcg</li><li>Silicon 2 mg</li><li>Vanadium 10 mcg</li><li>Lutein 250 mcg</li><li>Lycopene 300 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This preparation is a comprehensive, well-balanced multivitamin and multimineral preparation scientifically adjusted and designed to serve as the complete nutritional program for elderly people. This preparation maintains a healthy body and active lifestyle and ensures proper nutrition for elderly people.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Filwel Silver</strong></p><p>One tablet once daily with food or as indicated by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Filwel Silver</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>This product is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients. Do not take this product if taking other vitamin A supplements.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Filwel Silver</strong></p><p>Generally, this preparation is well tolerated. Allergic sensitization has been reported following oral administration of folic acid. Vitamin C and vitamin E may cause diarrhea and other gastrointestinal disturbances.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Recommended by consultation with a physician.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Long-term intake of high levels of vitamin A (excluding that sourced from beta carotene) may increase the risk of osteoporosis in postmenopausal women.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This preparation is a comprehensive, well-balanced multivitamin and multimineral preparation scientifically adjusted and designed to serve as the complete nutritional program for elderly people. It maintains a healthy body and active lifestyle and ensures proper nutrition for elderly people.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Recommended by consultation with a physician.</p><p>Let me know if you need any further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '1691_1742577336.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:15:36', '2025-05-02 18:05:36');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(3995, 'Renova XR', 'Paracetamol', 2.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '665 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Renova XR 665 mg: Comprehensive Information</strong></p><p><strong>Indications:</strong> Renova XR 665 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Fever</li><li>Common cold and influenza</li><li>Headache</li><li>Toothache</li><li>Earache</li><li>Bodyache</li><li>Myalgia (muscle pain)</li><li>Neuralgia (nerve pain)</li><li>Dysmenorrhoea (menstrual pain)</li><li>Sprains</li><li>Colic pain</li><li>Back pain</li><li>Post-operative pain</li><li>Postpartum pain</li><li>Inflammatory pain</li><li>Post-vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic and osteoarthritic pain</li><li>Stiffness of joints</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Renova XR 665 mg, containing <strong>acetaminophen</strong>, is primarily an analgesic (pain reliever) and antipyretic (fever reducer). It has weak anti-inflammatory properties. Acetaminophen is believed to work primarily in the central nervous system (CNS) by inhibiting the cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes, including COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3, which are involved in prostaglandin (PG) synthesis. These enzymes are responsible for producing substances that promote pain and inflammation, so inhibiting them raises the pain threshold. This makes Renova XR 665 mg one of the most commonly used, safest, and fast-acting analgesics.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Tablet:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong> 1-2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours, up to a maximum of 4 gm (8 tablets) daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6-12 years):</strong> ½ to 1 tablet 3 to 4 times daily, with a maximum of 2.6 gm/day for long-term treatment.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Extended Release Tablet:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years:</strong> Two tablets every 6 to 8 hours (maximum of 6 tablets in 24 hours). Tablets should be swallowed whole, not crushed.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Syrup/Suspension:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children under 3 months:</strong> 10 mg/kg body weight (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced), 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>3 months to below 1 year:</strong> ½ to 1 teaspoonful, 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1-5 years:</strong> 1-2 teaspoonfuls, 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>6-12 years:</strong> 2-4 teaspoonfuls, 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong> 4-8 teaspoonfuls, 3 to 4 times daily.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Suppository:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 3-12 months:</strong> 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 1-5 years:</strong> 125-250 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6-12 years:</strong> 250-500 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years:</strong> 0.5-1 gm, 4 times daily.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Paediatric Drop:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children up to 3 months:</strong> 0.5 ml (40 mg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>4 to 11 months:</strong> 1.0 ml (80 mg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1 to 2 years:</strong> 1.5 ml (120 mg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maximum dose:</strong> Do not exceed more than 5 doses daily for a maximum of 5 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Renova XR 665 mg with Actizorb Technology:</strong></p><p>This variant dissolves up to five times faster than standard Renova XR 665 mg tablets, making it a faster-acting analgesic. It is particularly suitable for patients who cannot tolerate aspirin or other analgesics.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children (12 years and over):</strong> 1-2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours as needed, not exceeding 8 tablets in 24 hours.</li><li><strong>Children (7-11 years):</strong> ½-1 tablet every 4 to 6 hours as needed, not exceeding 4 tablets in 24 hours.</li><li><strong>Not recommended for children under 7 years.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Alcohol and other liver-affecting drugs (e.g., barbiturates, tricyclic antidepressants, anticonvulsants) can interfere with the metabolism of Renova XR 665 mg, leading to potential hepatotoxicity.</li><li>Chronic ingestion of anticonvulsants or oral steroids can reduce the therapeutic effects of Renova XR 665 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Renova XR 665 mg (acetaminophen).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Typically mild, but may include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hematological reactions (thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, pancytopenia, neutropenia, agranulocytosis).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pancreatitis, skin rashes, and allergic reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category B (USFDA). Should be used only if clearly needed during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Excreted in breast milk. Maternal use in normal therapeutic doses does not seem to present a risk to the nursing infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with liver or kidney dysfunction.</li><li>Caution should be exercised in patients on other liver-affecting medications.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Early symptoms (first 24 hours): Pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, abdominal pain.</li><li>Later stages (12-48 hours): Liver damage, glucose metabolism abnormalities, metabolic acidosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Renova XR 665 mg has analgesic and antipyretic properties and weak anti-inflammatory action. It inhibits COX enzymes (COX-1, COX-2, COX-3) to reduce pain and fever.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><ul><li>Category B by the US FDA, recommended use only if clearly needed during pregnancy.</li></ul>', 'active', '731_1742577494.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:18:14', '2025-05-04 15:12:49'),
(3996, 'Alatrol', 'Cetirizine Hydrochloride', 28.00, 1, 'x 15 ml bottle', '15 ml bottle', 'Pediatric Drops', '2.5 mg/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Alatrol 15 ml</strong></p><p>Alatrol 15 ml is indicated for the relief of symptoms associated with seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis. It is also indicated for the treatment of the uncomplicated skin manifestations of chronic idiopathic urticaria and allergen-induced asthma.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Sedating Anti-histamine</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Alatrol 15 ml is a potent H1 receptor antagonist without any significant anticholinergic and antiserotonic effects. At pharmacologically active dose levels, it has almost no drowsiness effect and does not cause behavioral changes. It inhibits the histamine-mediated early phase of the allergic reaction and also reduces the migration of inflammatory cells and the release of mediators associated with the late phase of the allergic reaction.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Alatrol 15 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Children 6 years and older:</strong> 1 tablet or 2 teaspoonfuls daily (or 1 teaspoonful twice daily).</li><li><strong>Children 2-6 years:</strong> 1 teaspoonful once daily or 1/2 teaspoonful twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6 months to 2 years:</strong> 1/2 teaspoonful once daily.</li><li><strong>Children 12-23 months:</strong> The dose can be increased to a maximum of 1/2 teaspoonful every 12 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Alatrol 15 ml</strong></p><p>No clinically significant drug interactions have been found with Theophylline, Azithromycin, Pseudoephedrine, Ketoconazole, Erythromycin, or with other drugs.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Cetirizine or hydroxyzine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Alatrol 15 ml</strong></p><p>The most common side effect that occurred more frequently with Cetirizine is somnolence.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> The US FDA Pregnancy Category of Cetirizine Hydrochloride is B. However, there are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Since animal reproduction studies may not always predict human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Cetirizine Hydrochloride has been shown to be excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when Cetirizine Hydrochloride is administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised when driving a car or operating heavy machinery.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1545_1742577866.jpg', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:24:26', '2025-05-02 22:53:06'),
(3997, 'Dermomix', 'Clobetasol Propionate + Ofloxacin + Ornidazole + Terbinafine', 200.00, 1, 'x 15 gm tube', '15 gm tube', 'Cream', '0.05%+0.75%+2%+1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Dermomix 15 gm</strong></p><p>This cream is indicated in the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Mixed and complicated fungal, protozoal, and bacterial infections</li><li>Mild to moderate vaginitis</li><li>Candidiasis and trichomoniasis</li><li>Pruritus vulvae</li><li>Dermatitis</li><li>Eczema</li><li>Genito-urinary infections</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each gram of cream contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate USP</strong>: 0.50 mg</li><li><strong>Ofloxacin USP</strong>: 7.50 mg</li><li><strong>Ornidazole INN</strong>: 20 mg</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Hydrochloride BP</strong>: 10 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This cream is a combination cream which exhibits anti-bacterial, anti-fungal, anti-protozoal, and anti-inflammatory actions:</p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol</strong> is a potent corticosteroid with anti-inflammatory, anti-pruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties.</li><li><strong>Ofloxacin</strong> is a broad-spectrum antibiotic effective against many gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria.</li><li><strong>Ornidazole</strong> belongs to the nitroimidazole group of antibiotics and is used to treat amoeba and trichomonas infections.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine</strong> is an allylamine antifungal that inhibits the biosynthesis of ergosterol (an essential component of the fungal cell membrane), causing fungal cell death.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li>Apply gently onto the affected area twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li>No hazardous interactions have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients hypersensitive to any of the components of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Burning</li><li>Itching</li><li>Irritation</li><li>Dry skin</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The safe use of this preparation during pregnancy and lactation has not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>For external use only.</li><li>Do not swallow.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C.</li><li>Keep away from light and out of reach of children.</li><li>Do not freeze.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This cream exhibits anti-bacterial, anti-fungal, anti-protozoal, and anti-inflammatory actions:</p><ul><li><strong>Ofloxacin</strong>: Broad-spectrum antibiotic.</li><li><strong>Ornidazole</strong>: Treats amoeba and trichomonas infections.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine</strong>: Antifungal that inhibits ergosterol biosynthesis.</li><li><strong>Clobetasol</strong>: Potent corticosteroid with anti-inflammatory, anti-pruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>The safe use of this preparation during pregnancy and lactation has not been established.</li></ul>', 'active', '1086_1742578052.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:27:32', '2025-05-01 20:23:14'),
(3998, 'Dermomix', 'Clobetasol Propionate + Ofloxacin + Ornidazole + Terbinafine', 200.00, 1, 'x 15 gm tube', '15 gm tube', 'Cream', '0.05%+0.75%+2%+1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Dermomix 15 gm</strong></p><p>This cream is indicated in the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Mixed and complicated fungal, protozoal, and bacterial infections</li><li>Mild to moderate vaginitis</li><li>Candidiasis and trichomoniasis</li><li>Pruritus vulvae</li><li>Dermatitis</li><li>Eczema</li><li>Genito-urinary infections</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each gram of cream contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate USP</strong>: 0.50 mg</li><li><strong>Ofloxacin USP</strong>: 7.50 mg</li><li><strong>Ornidazole INN</strong>: 20 mg</li><li><strong>Terbinafine Hydrochloride BP</strong>: 10 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This cream is a combination cream which exhibits anti-bacterial, anti-fungal, anti-protozoal, and anti-inflammatory actions:</p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol</strong> is a potent corticosteroid with anti-inflammatory, anti-pruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties.</li><li><strong>Ofloxacin</strong> is a broad-spectrum antibiotic effective against many gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria.</li><li><strong>Ornidazole</strong> belongs to the nitroimidazole group of antibiotics and is used to treat amoeba and trichomonas infections.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine</strong> is an allylamine antifungal that inhibits the biosynthesis of ergosterol (an essential component of the fungal cell membrane), causing fungal cell death.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li>Apply gently onto the affected area twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li>No hazardous interactions have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients hypersensitive to any of the components of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Burning</li><li>Itching</li><li>Irritation</li><li>Dry skin</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The safe use of this preparation during pregnancy and lactation has not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>For external use only.</li><li>Do not swallow.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C.</li><li>Keep away from light and out of reach of children.</li><li>Do not freeze.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This cream exhibits anti-bacterial, anti-fungal, anti-protozoal, and anti-inflammatory actions:</p><ul><li><strong>Ofloxacin</strong>: Broad-spectrum antibiotic.</li><li><strong>Ornidazole</strong>: Treats amoeba and trichomonas infections.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine</strong>: Antifungal that inhibits ergosterol biosynthesis.</li><li><strong>Clobetasol</strong>: Potent corticosteroid with anti-inflammatory, anti-pruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>The safe use of this preparation during pregnancy and lactation has not been established.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '1660_1742578053.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:27:33', '2025-05-08 14:31:23'),
(3999, 'Delanzo', 'Dexlansoprazole', 10.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '30 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the revised text with headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Delanzo 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: Delanzo 30 mg is indicated for the healing of all grades of erosive esophagitis (EE) for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: Delanzo 30 mg is indicated to maintain healing of EE and relief of heartburn for up to 6 months.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease</strong>: Delanzo 30 mg is indicated for the treatment of heartburn associated with symptomatic non-erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD) for 4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Delanzo 30 mg delayed-release capsule is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI), which inhibits gastric acid secretion. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole (a racemic mixture of the R- and S-enantiomers). This is supplied as a Dual Delayed Release (DDR) formulation in a capsule for oral administration. Delanzo 30 mg capsule contains a mixture of two types of enteric-coated granules with different pH-dependent dissolution profiles. Delanzo 30 mg suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell. By acting specifically on the proton pump, Delanzo 30 mg blocks the final step of acid production.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Delanzo 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis and Relief of Heartburn</strong>: 30 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive GERD</strong>: 30 mg once daily for 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 60 mg once daily for up to 8 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Delanzo 30 mg</strong></p><p>Delanzo 30 mg can be taken without regard to food. It should be swallowed whole. Alternatively, Delanzo 30 mg capsules can be administered as follows: Open the capsule, sprinkle intact granules on one tablespoon of food, and swallow immediately. Granules should not be chewed. If a dose is missed, it should be taken as soon as possible, but if it is close to the time for the next dose, only the prescribed dose should be taken at the appointed time. A double dose should not be taken.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Delanzo 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With Medicine</strong>: Atazanavir, Warfarin, Tacrolimus, Clopidogrel, and Methotrexate.</li><li><strong>With Food &amp; Others</strong>: No data available.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Delanzo 30 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Delanzo 30 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and flatulence.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>There are no adequate or well-controlled studies in pregnant women with Delanzo 30 mg. Exposure in clinical trials was very limited. Delanzo 30 mg should not be administered to pregnant women unless the expected benefits outweigh the potential risks. It is not known whether Delanzo 30 mg is excreted in human milk. However, Delanzo 30 mg and its metabolites are excreted in the milk of rats. As many drugs are excreted in human milk, Delanzo 30 mg should not be given to nursing mothers unless its use is considered necessary.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Gastric malignancy</li><li>Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea</li><li>Bone fracture</li><li>Hypomagnesemia</li><li>Concomitant use with Methotrexate</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Delanzo 30 mg</strong></p><p>There have been no reports of significant overdose with Delanzo 30 mg. Multiple doses of Delanzo 30 mg (120 mg) and a single dose of Delanzo 30 mg (300 mg) did not result in death or other severe adverse events.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C and in a dry place, protected from light. Keep all medicines out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary for elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: Safety and effectiveness of Delanzo 30 mg in patients below 12 years of age have not been established.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary for patients with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No adjustment of Delanzo 30 mg is necessary for patients with mild hepatic impairment. A maximum daily dose of 30 mg for patients with moderate hepatic impairment may be considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Delanzo 30 mg delayed-release capsule is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI) that suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell. By acting specifically on the proton pump, Delanzo 30 mg blocks the final step of acid production. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole (a racemic mixture of the R- and S-enantiomers). Delanzo 30 mg is supplied as a Dual Delayed Release (DDR) formulation in a capsule for oral administration. This capsule contains a mixture of two types of enteric-coated granules with different pH-dependent dissolution profiles. The dual delayed-release formulation of Delanzo 30 mg results in a plasma concentration-time profile with two distinct peaks: the first peak occurs 1 to 2 hours after administration, followed by a second peak within 4 to 5 hours. After oral administration, the mean Cmax and AUC value of Delanzo 30 mg increased approximately dose-proportionally. Delanzo 30 mg is extensively metabolized in the liver and excreted by urine.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Category B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies with Delanzo 30 mg in pregnant women.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in Children &amp; Adolescents</strong>: Safety and effectiveness of Delanzo 30 mg in patients below 12 years of age have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No dose adjustment is necessary for elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment is necessary for patients with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment for Delanzo 30 mg is necessary for patients with mild hepatic impairment. A maximum daily dose of 30 mg should be considered for patients with moderate hepatic impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '410_1742578199.jpg', 58, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:29:59', '2025-05-03 07:52:18'),
(4000, 'Reservix', 'Aceclofenac', 5.70, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the revised text with the headings in normal bold format:</p><p><strong>Indications of Reservix 100 mg</strong></p><p>Reservix 100 mg is indicated for the relief of pain and inflammation in osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, toothache, trauma, and lumbago.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for Osteoarthritis, Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Reservix 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Reservix 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Extended release tablet:</strong> The recommended dose in adults is one 200 mg Reservix 100 mg tablet daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Film-coated tablet:</strong> The recommended dose in adults is 100 mg, twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Reservix 100 mg</strong></p><p>No significant drug interactions have been observed, but close monitoring of patients is required when it is used with:</p><ul><li><strong>Lithium and Digoxin:</strong> May increase plasma concentration of lithium and digoxin.</li><li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> May interact with the activity of diuretics.</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants:</strong> May enhance the activity of anticoagulants.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate:</strong> May increase the plasma level of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Reservix 100 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to it or in whom aspirin or NSAIDs precipitate attacks of asthma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Reservix 100 mg</strong></p><p>Reservix 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of Reservix 100 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in patients with active or suspected peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding, moderate to severe hepatic impairment, and cardiac or renal impairment. Caution should also be exercised in patients suffering from dizziness or urticaria.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Reservix 100 mg in children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Drugs for Osteoarthritis, Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Reservix 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The use of Reservix 100 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Reservix 100 mg in children.</p>', 'active', '1103_1742578346.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:32:26', '2025-05-04 15:13:45'),
(4001, 'Biocal-DX', 'Algae Calcium + Vitamin D3', 16.00, 1, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '305_1742578643.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:37:23', '2025-05-01 22:56:57'),
(4002, 'Monas OFT', 'Montelukast Sodium', 7.50, 15, 'tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '4 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Monas OFT 4 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p>Monas OFT 4 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Monas OFT 4 mg is an orally active drug that targets cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT1), inhibiting the effects of LTD4 without any agonist activity. This action prevents physiological effects at the CysLT1 receptor, which are involved in conditions like asthma and allergic rhinitis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (15 years and older)</strong>: Monas 10 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients (6 to 14 years)</strong>: Monas 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients (2 to 5 years)</strong>: Monas 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients (6 months to 5 years)</strong>: Monas 4 mg oral granules once daily. The granules can be administered directly in the mouth or mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Pediatric Patients</strong></p><p>Monas has been studied in pediatric patients 6 months to 14 years of age, showing similar safety and efficacy profiles as seen in adults.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is required for patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is recommended for patients with renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Elderly Use</strong></p><p>Pharmacokinetic data show that elderly patients have a slightly longer plasma half-life of Monas but no dosage adjustment is necessary.</p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><p>Monas OFT 4 mg can be used alongside various other treatments without significant interactions. Drugs like theophylline, prednisone, oral contraceptives, and others did not show clinically significant effects on pharmacokinetics. However, drugs that induce hepatic metabolism, such as phenobarbital or rifampin, may reduce the drug\'s effectiveness, and monitoring is recommended.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Monas OFT 4 mg is contraindicated in individuals with extreme sensitivity to any ingredient in the medication.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, asthenia, aberrant behavior, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, oedema, seizure, odd feeling, sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, decreased attention, disorientation, erythema nodosum, hallucinations, hepatic issues, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, tremor.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Monas OFT 4 mg is classified as pregnancy category B. Although it crosses the placenta in animal studies, there is no controlled research in pregnant humans. It is secreted in breast milk, but the implications of this are unclear. Caution is advised when used in pregnant or breastfeeding women.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Monas OFT 4 mg should not be used for the reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks (e.g., status asthmaticus). Patients should have access to appropriate rescue medication. Asthma exacerbations should still be treated accordingly. Discontinuation of inhaled or oral corticosteroids should be done gradually under medical supervision. Patients with documented aspirin sensitivity should avoid aspirin or NSAIDs while on this medication.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>Most overdose cases have not resulted in severe outcomes. Common symptoms include nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain. In case of overdose, standard supportive measures (such as removing unabsorbed material and monitoring) should be followed.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry place, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Monas OFT 4 mg selectively inhibits the cysteinyl leukotriene receptor (CysLT1), reducing the effects of leukotrienes on airway inflammation and smooth muscle contraction in asthma and allergic rhinitis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Monas OFT 4 mg passes the placenta in animal studies but has not been well studied in humans. It should only be used during pregnancy if absolutely necessary. Caution is also advised when administered to nursing mothers due to its secretion in breast milk.</p>', 'active', '164_1742578774.jpg', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:39:34', '2025-05-04 00:07:28'),
(4003, 'Metryl', 'Metronidazole', 1.70, 30, 'Tablet', '30 tablet in a pack', 'Tablet', '400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Metryl 400 mg</h3><p>Metryl 400 mg is indicated in the treatment of the following diseases:</p><ul><li><strong>Prevention of post-operative infections</strong> due to anaerobic bacteria (particularly species of <em>Bacteroides</em> and anaerobic streptococci).</li><li><strong>Treatment of septicaemia, bacteraemia, peritonitis, brain abscess, pelvic abscess, pelvic cellulitis</strong>, and post-operative wound infections caused by anaerobes.</li><li><strong>Urogenital trichomoniasis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Bacterial vaginosis</strong> (also known as non-specific vaginitis).</li><li><strong>All forms of amoebiasis</strong> (intestinal, extra-intestinal disease, and that of symptomless cyst passers).</li><li><strong>Giardiasis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Acute ulcerative gingivitis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Anaerobically infected leg ulcers and pressure sores</strong>.</li><li><strong>Acute dental infections</strong> due to anaerobic organisms.</li><li><strong>Antibiotic-associated pseudomembranous colitis</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Amoebicides</strong>, <strong>Anti-diarrhoeal</strong>, <strong>Antiprotozoal</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Metryl 400 mg is a member of the imidazole class of antibacterial drugs and is classified therapeutically as an antiprotozoal agent. The 5-nitro group of Metryl 400 mg is reduced by anaerobes metabolically. Studies have demonstrated that the reduced form of this drug interacts with DNA, giving it a bactericidal action.</p><p><br></p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><h4>Tablet and Suspension</h4><ul><li><strong>Trichomoniasis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">200 mg tid or 400 mg bid for 7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">800 mg in the morning and 1-2 gm at night for 2 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 gm as a single dose for 1 day.</li><li><strong>Trichomoniasis (Children):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 7-10 yrs: 100 mg tid.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 3-7 yrs: 100 mg bid.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 yrs: 50 mg tid.</li><li><strong>Intestinal Amoebiasis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">800 mg tid for 5 days.</li><li><strong>Intestinal Amoebiasis (Children):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 7-10 yrs: 400 mg tid.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 3-7 yrs: 200 mg qid.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 yrs: 200 mg tid.</li><li><strong>Extra-intestinal &amp; Asymptomatic Amoebiasis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">400-800 mg tid for 5-10 days.</li><li><strong>Giardiasis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 gm once daily for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Acute Ulcerative Gingivitis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">200 mg tid for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Acute Dental Infections (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">200 mg tid for 3-7 days.</li><li><strong>Bacterial Vaginosis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">400 mg bid for 7 days or 2 gm as a single dose for 1 day.</li><li><strong>Anaerobic Infections (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">800 mg initially, then 400 mg tid for 7 days.</li><li><strong>Surgical Prophylaxis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">400 mg tid, started 24 hours before surgery for 1 day.</li></ul><h4>Vaginal Gel</h4><ul><li><strong>One applicator full</strong> (approximately 5 grams containing 37.5 mg) intravaginally once or twice a day for 5 days.</li></ul><h4>Suppository</h4><ul><li><strong>Anaerobic Infections (Adults):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 gm every 8 hours for 3 days, then 1 gm every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Surgical Prophylaxis (Adults):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 gm 2 hours before surgery; up to 3 further doses of 1 gm may be given every 8 hours.</li></ul><h4>IV Infusion</h4><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">500 mg every 8 hours at a rate of 5 ml/minute. Maximum dose: 4 g in 24 hours.</li><li><strong>Children under 12 years:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">7.5 mg/kg body weight/day every 8 hours.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Metryl 400 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Disulfiram:</strong> Psychotic reactions when used concurrently.</li><li><strong>Alcohol:</strong> Avoid alcoholic beverages during therapy due to possible disulfiram-like reactions.</li><li><strong>Oral Anticoagulants (Warfarin):</strong> Increased hemorrhagic risk; monitor prothrombin time.</li><li><strong>Lithium:</strong> Plasma levels may be increased.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin:</strong> Close monitoring of serum cyclosporin and creatinine required.</li><li><strong>Phenytoin or Phenobarbital:</strong> Increased elimination of Metryl, reducing its plasma levels.</li><li><strong>5-Fluorouracil:</strong> Reduced clearance, increasing 5-fluorouracil toxicity.</li><li><strong>Busulfan:</strong> Increased plasma levels, leading to potential toxicity.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Metryl 400 mg is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Metryl 400 mg or other Nitroimidazole derivatives.</p><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li>Metallic taste</li><li>Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea</li><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Rashes</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>US FDA Pregnancy Category B:</strong> Caution should be exercised during pregnancy. It is excreted in human milk, so caution is needed when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Monitor for adverse reactions (e.g., neuropathy) if therapy exceeds the recommended duration.</li><li>Caution with hepatic encephalopathy.</li><li>May darken urine.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li>Symptoms: Vomiting, ataxia, disorientation.</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> Symptomatic and supportive care, as there is no specific antidote.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C.</li><li>Protect from light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Do not use past the expiry date.</li></ul>', 'active', '1719_1742578929.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:42:09', '2025-03-22 21:05:56'),
(4004, 'Jointec Max', 'Glucosamine Sulfate + Diacerein', 12.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '750 mg+50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Jointec Max 750 mg + 50 mg</strong></p><p>This is primarily indicated in conditions like osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, and bone &amp; joint injuries.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Stimulation of cartilage formation.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Glucosamine is a natural substance produced in the human body and found in certain foods. It stimulates the production of glycosaminoglycan &amp; proteoglycan, two essential building blocks of cartilage. Glucosamine acts, as a supplement, as a joint lubricant and shock absorber necessary to maintain healthy cartilage and joint function.</p><p>Diacerein is an anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic drug, specially indicated for the treatment of arthritic conditions. It directly inhibits IL-1 synthesis and release in vitro and down-modulates IL-1 induced activities, relieving osteoarthritis symptoms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Jointec Max 750 mg + 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children above 12 years:</strong> One tablet (Glucosamine Sulphate 750 mg and Diacerein 50 mg) twice daily for 4-12 weeks, taken with food.</li><li><strong>Creatinine Clearance (CrCl) &lt; 30 ml/min:</strong> The dose of Diacerein should be reduced.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Jointec Max 750 mg + 50 mg</strong></p><p>There have been no reports of significant drug interactions of Glucosamine with antibiotics, antidepressants, antihypertensives, nitrates, antiarrhythmics, anxiolytics, hypoglycemic agents, or antisecretives.</p><ul><li><strong>Diacerein</strong>: Decreased absorption with aluminum and/or magnesium hydroxide antacids. Increased risk of diarrhea with laxatives and antibiotics. Avoid co-administration with fibers and phytic acids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Glucosamine:</strong> There are no known contraindications except proven hypersensitivity to Glucosamine. Diabetics are advised to monitor blood glucose levels regularly while taking Glucosamine.</li><li><strong>Diacerein:</strong> Contraindicated in pregnancy, lactation, and in patients with hypersensitivity to anthraquinone derivatives. Not recommended for children, individuals with severe hepatic or renal insufficiency, or those allergic to shellfish (e.g., shrimp, crab).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Jointec Max 750 mg + 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Diarrhea, constipation, epigastric pain, heartburn, nausea, vomiting, headache, skin rashes, drowsiness, intense yellow coloring of urine.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Diacerein is contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation.</li><li>Glucosamine should not be taken by pregnant women and nursing mothers due to lack of scientific data regarding its effect on fetus and infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be practiced when administering Glucosamine + Diacerein to patients who are allergic to Glucosamine, Diacerein, or shellfish (e.g., shrimp, crab), or have diabetes, asthma, alcohol dependence, or liver disease.</li><li>Before taking Glucosamine + Diacerein, complete blood count, liver function, and urinalysis should be monitored regularly.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Jointec Max 750 mg + 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Diarrhea may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, protect from light and moisture.</li></ul>', 'active', '452_1742579044.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:44:04', '2025-05-03 23:01:24'),
(4005, 'Seasonix', 'Levocetirizine Dihydrochloride', 4.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Seasonix 5 mg:</strong> Levocetirizine is indicated for the treatment of symptoms associated with allergic conditions such as:</p><ul><li>Perennial allergic rhinitis</li><li>Seasonal allergic rhinitis</li><li>Chronic idiopathic urticaria</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong> Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Seasonix 5 mg contains Levocetirizine, the active (levo) isomer of Cetirizine. Levocetirizine is a highly effective and well-tolerated non-sedating antihistamine with potent antiallergic properties. It has a two-fold higher affinity for H1 receptors than Cetirizine. Levocetirizine provides rapid and long-lasting relief, allowing once-a-day administration.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children 12 years and older:</strong> 5 mg once daily in the evening.</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 11 years of age:</strong> 2.5 mg once daily in the evening.</li><li><strong>Children 6 months to 5 years of age:</strong> 1.25 mg (1/2 teaspoon oral solution) once daily in the evening.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Adjust dose for patients aged 12 years and older with decreased renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Impairment Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Mild renal impairment (creatinine clearance: 50-80 ml/min):</strong> 2.5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Moderate renal impairment (creatinine clearance: 30-50 ml/min):</strong> 2.5 mg once every other day.</li><li><strong>Severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance: 10-30 ml/min):</strong> 2.5 mg twice weekly (administered once every 3-4 days).</li><li><strong>End-stage renal disease or patients undergoing hemodialysis:</strong> Should not receive Levocetirizine.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction:</strong> Levocetirizine is not known to interact with other drugs.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Levocetirizine, Cetirizine, or its parent compound Hydroxyzine.</li><li>Patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance: &lt;10 ml/min).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong> Levocetirizine is generally well-tolerated. Rare side effects may include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Fatigue</li><li>Skin rash</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> The safety of Levocetirizine in pregnancy has not been established. Use with caution during pregnancy if the potential benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Levocetirizine passes into breast milk. Use with caution in nursing mothers if the expected benefit to the mother outweighs the risk to the infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with epilepsy or those at risk of convulsions.</li><li>Renal impairment should be considered when dosing.</li><li>Exercise caution during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong> No clinically relevant adverse effects have been reported in cases of overdose. Symptomatic and supportive treatment is recommended.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong> Store at a temperature not exceeding 30°C in a dry place. Protect from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Paediatric use:</strong> Not recommended for use in children less than 6 years of age.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong> Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Levocetirizine, as the active (Levo) isomer of Cetirizine, is a non-sedating antihistamine with potent antiallergic properties. It has a higher affinity for H1 receptors than Cetirizine, providing rapid and long-lasting relief.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy Category:</strong> Category B. Levocetirizine should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. It is excreted in breast milk, so it is not recommended for use by nursing mothers.</p>', 'active', '379_1742579121.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:45:21', '2025-05-04 20:58:34');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4006, 'Doxiva', 'Doxophylline', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Doxiva 400 mg</strong></p><p>Doxiva 400 mg is used to treat the following indications:</p><ul><li>Bronchial asthma</li><li>Bronchospasm</li><li>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</li><li>Pulmonary disease with a spastic bronchial component</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Doxiva 400 mg is a novel bronchodilator that structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. It selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. However, unlike Theophylline, Doxiva 400 mg appears to have decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, potentially accounting for its improved safety profile. Additionally, it is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Doxiva 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 400 mg tablet two or three times daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:&gt;12 years of age:</strong> 10 ml syrup or 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>6-12 years of age:</strong> 6-9 mg/kg body weight two times daily. For example, if the body weight is 10 kg, 3 ml (60 mg) two times daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Doxiva 400 mg</strong></p><p>Doxiva 400 mg should not be administered together with other xanthine derivatives. Toxic synergism with ephedrine has been documented for xanthines. Like other xanthines, concomitant therapy with troleandomycin, lincomycin, clindamycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, ranitidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccine may decrease the hepatic clearance of xanthines, leading to an increase in blood levels.</p><p>No evidence of a relationship between Doxiva 400 mg serum concentrations and toxic events has been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Doxiva 400 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Acute myocardial infarction</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Lactating women</li><li>Patients who have shown hypersensitivity to its components</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Doxiva 400 mg</strong></p><p>Doxiva 400 mg rarely causes serious side effects; however, possible side effects are similar to taking an excess amount of caffeine. These include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Headache</li><li>Upset stomach</li><li>Heartburn</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Animal reproduction studies indicate that Doxiva 400 mg does not cause fetal harm when administered to pregnant animals or affect reproduction capacity. However, due to limited experience in pregnant humans, xanthines should only be given to pregnant women when clearly necessary.</p><p>It\'s important to note that Doxiva 400 mg is contraindicated in nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The half-life of xanthine derivatives is influenced by a number of known variables. It may be prolonged in:</p><ul><li>Patients with liver disease</li><li>Patients with congestive heart failure</li><li>Patients taking certain other drugs like erythromycin, troleandomycin, lincomycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccine.</li><li>In these cases, a lower dose of Doxiva 400 mg may be needed.</li><li>Phenytoin, other anticonvulsants, and smoking may cause an increase in clearance with a shorter mean half-life. In these cases, higher doses of Doxiva 400 mg may be needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Doxiva 400 mg</strong></p><p>In case of overdose, severe cardiac arrhythmias and tonic-clonic seizure may occur. These effects may represent the first signs of intoxication. The appearance of side effects may require discontinuation of the treatment, which, if necessary, at the physician’s discretion, may be resumed at lower doses after all signs and symptoms of toxicity have subsided. As there is no specific antidote, in case of overdose, symptomatic treatment of cardiovascular collapse should be instituted.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children. Doxiva 400 mg should be used only on the prescription of a specialist physician.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</li></ul><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Doxiva 400 mg is a novel bronchodilator. It structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. Doxiva 400 mg selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, thereby relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. However, differently from Theophylline, Doxiva 400 mg appears to have decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, which may account for the better safety profile of the drug. Doxiva 400 mg is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p>', 'active', '902_1742579219.webp', 11, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:46:59', '2025-05-02 08:46:34'),
(4007, 'Servipep', 'Famotidine', 4.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the text with the headings formatted in <strong>bold text</strong>:</p><p><strong>Indications of Servipep 20 mg</strong></p><p>Servipep 20 mg is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Gastric ulcer</li><li>Duodenal ulcer</li><li>Anastomotic ulcer</li><li>Acute stress ulcer</li><li>Reflux esophagitis</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Servipep 20 mg is also indicated for the treatment of acute gastritis, chronic gastritis in the acute exacerbation stage.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>H2 receptor antagonist</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Servipep 20 mg is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist. Servipep 20 mg completely inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors of parietal cells. It inhibits basal, overnight, and pentagastrin-stimulated gastric acid secretion. The H2-receptor antagonist activity of Servipep 20 mg is slowly reversible, since the drug dissociates slowly from the H2-receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Servipep 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer</strong>: 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks</li><li><strong>Benign gastric ulcer</strong>: 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks</li><li><strong>Maintenance therapy</strong>: 20 mg at night for preventing the recurrence of duodenal ulceration</li><li><strong>Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease</strong>: 20 mg twice daily for 6 to 12 weeks</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: The recommended starting dose is 20 mg every six hours. Dosage should then be adjusted to individual response. Doses up to 160 mg every six hours have been administered to some patients without the development of significant adverse effects.</li><li>Dosage can be administered irrespective of meals. Antacids may be given concomitantly if needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Servipep 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>:</li><li>For gastric ulcer, duodenal ulcer, anastomotic ulcer, upper gastro-intestinal hemorrhage, reflux esophagitis, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</li><li>Usual dose for adults: Servipep 20 mg 20 mg twice daily (after breakfast and after supper or before bed time) or Servipep 20 mg 40 mg can be administered orally once daily at bed time.</li><li>For the treatment of acute gastritis, chronic gastritis in acute exacerbation stage: Usual dosage for adults is Servipep 20 mg 20 mg orally twice a day (after breakfast and after supper or before bed time). Also, Servipep 20 mg 40 mg can be orally administered once a day (before bed time).</li><li>Most ulcer patients heal within 4-8 weeks. For maintenance therapy, the recommended oral dose is 20 mg once daily. Or, as directed by the registered physician.</li><li><strong>Powder for Suspension</strong>:</li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">&lt;1 year of age: 0.5 mg/kg/dose of Servipep 20 mg oral suspension up to 8 weeks once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Age 3 to 11 months: 0.5 mg/kg/dose twice daily up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Age 1 to 2 months: 0.5 mg/kg/dose once daily up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Neonates: 0.5 mg/kg/dose maximum once daily up to 8 weeks</li><li>Patients 1-16 years of age:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">GERD: 1 mg/kg/day p.o. divided b.i.d. up to 40 mg b.i.d.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Duodenal ulcer: 0.5 mg/kg/day p.o. at bedtime or divided b.i.d. up to 40 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Peptic ulcer: 0.5 mg/kg/day p.o. at bedtime or divided b.i.d. up to 40 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Maintenance therapy: 40 mg at daily night</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Reflux esophagitis: 2 mg/kg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome: 40 mg 3 times daily</li><li><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong>:</li><li>In some hospitalized patients with pathological hypersecretory conditions or intractable ulcers, or in patients who are unable to take oral medication, Servipep 20 mg Injection Premixed or Servipep 20 mg Injection may be administered until oral therapy can be instituted.</li><li>The recommended dosage for Servipep 20 mg Injection Premixed and Servipep 20 mg Injection in adult patients is 20 mg intravenously q 12 h.</li><li>The doses and regimen for parenteral administration in patients with GERD have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage for Pediatric Patients</strong></p><p>Pediatric Patients suggest that the starting dose in pediatric patients 1-16 years of age is 0.25 mg/kg intravenously (injected over a period of not less than two minutes or as a 15-minute infusion) q 12 h up to 40 mg/day.</p><p>While published uncontrolled clinical studies suggest the effectiveness of Servipep 20 mg in the treatment of peptic ulcer, data in pediatric patients are insufficient to establish percent response with dose and duration of therapy. Therefore, treatment duration (initially based on adult duration recommendations) and dose should be individualized based on clinical response and/or gastric pH determination and endoscopy.</p><p>Published uncontrolled studies in pediatric patients have demonstrated gastric acid suppression with doses up to 0.5 mg/kg intravenously q 12 h. No pharmacokinetic or pharmacodynamic data are available on pediatric patients under 1 year of age.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Servipep 20 mg</strong></p><p>No clinically important drug interactions have been identified. Servipep 20 mg does not interact with the cytochrome P450-linked drug-metabolizing enzyme system.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Known hypersensitivity to any component of the drug.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Servipep 20 mg</strong></p><p>Eruption, constipation, diarrhoea, dry mouth, nausea, vomiting, tachycardia, high blood pressure, headache, drowsiness, or insomnia may rarely occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: There are no adequate, well-controlled studies on Servipep 20 mg in pregnancy, but it is known to cross the placenta and should be prescribed only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether Servipep 20 mg is secreted into human milk, nursing mothers should either stop nursing or stop taking the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The drug should be used in the minimum required amount depending upon the conditions of the diseases. The drug should be administered carefully with elderly patients, patients with renal failure, and hepatic disorders.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Servipep 20 mg is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist. Servipep 20 mg completely inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors of parietal cells. It inhibits basal, overnight, and pentagastrin-stimulated gastric acid secretion. The H2-receptor antagonist activity of Servipep 20 mg is slowly reversible, since the drug dissociates slowly from the H2-receptor.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Pregnancy category B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Caution should be exercised when Famotidine is administered to a nursing woman.</p>', 'active', '1593_1742579303.jpg', 57, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:48:23', '2025-05-04 21:07:16'),
(4008, 'Atrizin', 'Cetirizine Hydrochloride', 3.00, 15, 'tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Atrizin 10 mg</strong></p><p>Atrizin 10 mg is indicated for the relief of symptoms associated with seasonal &amp; perennial allergic rhinitis. It is also indicated for the treatment of the uncomplicated skin manifestations of chronic idiopathic urticaria and allergen-induced asthma.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Sedating Anti-histamine</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Atrizin 10 mg is a potent H1 receptor antagonist without any significant anticholinergic and antiserotonic effects. At pharmacologically active dose levels, it has almost no drowsiness effect and does not cause behavioral changes. It inhibits the histamine-mediated early phase of the allergic reaction and also reduces the migration of inflammatory cells and the release of mediators associated with the late phase of the allergic reaction.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Atrizin 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Children 6 years and older:</strong> 1 tablet or 2 teaspoonfuls daily (or 1 teaspoonful twice daily).</li><li><strong>Children 2-6 years:</strong> 1 teaspoonful once daily or 1/2 teaspoonful twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6 months to 2 years:</strong> 1/2 teaspoonful once daily.</li><li>The dose in children 12-23 months of age can be increased to a maximum dose of 1/2 teaspoonful every 12 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Atrizin 10 mg</strong></p><p>No clinically significant drug interactions have been found with Theophylline, Azithromycin, Pseudoephedrine, Ketoconazole, or Erythromycin and with other drugs.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Cetirizine or Hydroxyzine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Atrizin 10 mg</strong></p><p>The most common side effect that occurred more frequently with Cetirizine is somnolence.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>US FDA Pregnancy Category of Cetirizine Hydrochloride is B. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Cetirizine Hydrochloride has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Cetirizine Hydrochloride is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised when driving a car or operating heavy machinery.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1813_1742579397.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:49:57', '2025-05-03 00:07:17'),
(4009, 'Lucent', 'Calcitriol', 10.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '0.25 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Lucent 0.25 mcg</strong></p><p>Lucent 0.25 mcg is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Post-menopausal osteoporosis</li><li>Renal osteodystrophy</li><li>Postsurgical hypoparathyroidism</li><li>Idiopathic hypoparathyroidism</li><li>Pseudohypoparathyroidism</li><li>Secondary hyperparathyroidism in patients with moderate to severe chronic renal failure (pre-dialysis)</li><li>Vitamin D-dependent rickets</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin-D preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Lucent 0.25 mcg is one of the most important active metabolites of vitamin D3. It is normally formed in the kidneys from its precursor, 25-hydroxycholecalciferol. Lucent 0.25 mcg promotes intestinal absorption of calcium and regulates bone mineralization. The key role of Lucent 0.25 mcg is the regulation of calcium homeostasis, which includes stimulation effects on osteoblastic activity in the skeleton.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Lucent 0.25 mcg</strong></p><p>The optimal daily dose of Lucent 0.25 mcg capsule must be carefully determined for each patient based on serum calcium level.</p><ul><li><strong>Post-menopausal Osteoporosis</strong>: The recommended dose of Lucent 0.25 mcg capsule is 0.25 mcg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Renal Osteodystrophy (dialysis patients)</strong>: The initial daily dose is 0.25 mcg of Lucent 0.25 mcg capsule. In patients with normal or only slightly reduced calcium levels, doses of 0.25 mcg every other day are sufficient.</li><li><strong>Hypoparathyroidism and Rickets</strong>: The recommended initial dosage of Lucent 0.25 mcg capsule is 0.25 mcg/day, given in the morning. If satisfactory response is not observed within 2-4 weeks, the dose may be increased at 2-4 week intervals. The recommended intravenous initial dose of Lucent 0.25 mcg injection, depending on the severity of hypocalcemia and/or secondary hyperparathyroidism, is 1 mcg (0.02 mcg/kg) to 2 mcg administered three times weekly, approximately every other day. Doses as small as 0.5 mcg and as large as 4 mcg three times weekly have been used as an initial dose. If a satisfactory response is not observed, the dose may be increased by 0.5 to 1 mcg at two to four-week intervals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Lucent 0.25 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant treatment with thiazide diuretics increases the risk of hypercalcemia.</li><li>Lucent 0.25 mcg dosage must be determined with care in patients undergoing treatment with digitalis, as hypercalcemia in such patients may precipitate cardiac arrhythmias.</li><li>Magnesium-containing drugs (e.g., antacids) may cause hypermagnesemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Lucent 0.25 mcg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of its ingredients. It is also contraindicated in all diseases associated with hypercalcemia.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Lucent 0.25 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>Occasional symptoms: anorexia, headache, vomiting, and constipation.</li><li>Chronic effects may include dystrophy, fever, polyuria, dehydration, apathy, and urinary tract infection.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>There is no evidence that vitamin D is teratogenic in humans. Lucent 0.25 mcg may be used during pregnancy only if the benefits outweigh the potential risk to the fetus. Mothers may breastfeed while taking Lucent 0.25 mcg, but serum calcium levels of the mother and infant should be monitored.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>During Lucent 0.25 mcg therapy, if the serum calcium levels rise to 1 mg/100 ml above normal or serum creatinine rises to &gt;120 µmol/l, the dosage of Lucent 0.25 mcg should be substantially reduced or treatment stopped.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Secondary hyperparathyroidism in patients with moderate to severe chronic renal failure (pre-dialysis)</strong>: The recommended initial dosage of Lucent 0.25 mcg is 0.25 mcg/day in adults and pediatric patients 3 years of age or older. This dosage may be increased to 0.5 mcg/day if necessary. For pediatric patients less than 3 years of age, the recommended initial dosage of Lucent 0.25 mcg is 10 to 15 ng/kg/day.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustments are required.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin-D preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Lucent 0.25 mcg is one of the most important active metabolites of vitamin D3. It is normally formed in the kidneys from its precursor, 25-hydroxycholecalciferol. Lucent 0.25 mcg promotes intestinal absorption of calcium and regulates bone mineralization. The key role of Lucent 0.25 mcg is the regulation of calcium homeostasis, which includes stimulating osteoblastic activity in the skeleton.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There is no evidence that vitamin D is teratogenic in humans. Lucent 0.25 mcg may be used during pregnancy only if the benefits outweigh the potential risk to the fetus. Mothers may breastfeed while taking Lucent 0.25 mcg, but serum calcium levels of the mother and infant should be monitored.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Secondary hyperparathyroidism in patients with moderate to severe chronic renal failure (pre-dialysis)</strong>: The recommended initial dosage of Lucent 0.25 mcg is 0.25 mcg/day in adults and pediatric patients 3 years of age or older. This dosage may be increased if necessary to 0.5 mcg/day. For pediatric patients less than 3 years of age, the recommended initial dosage of Lucent 0.25 mcg is 10 to 15 ng/kg/day.</li></ul>', 'active', '1880_1742579491.jpg', 6, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:51:31', '2025-05-03 23:34:46'),
(4010, 'PPI', 'Omeprazole', 5.01, 14, 'capsule', '14 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of PPI 20 mg</strong></p><p>PPI 20 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcers.</li><li>Gastric and duodenal ulcers caused by NSAIDs.</li><li>Prevention of duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by NSAIDs in individuals with a history of these conditions.</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD).</li><li>Long-term management of acid reflux disease.</li><li>Acid-related dyspepsia.</li><li>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers.</li><li>Prevention of aspiration of acid during general anesthesia.</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>Peptic ulcer caused by Helicobacter pylori.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>PPI 20 mg is a benzimidazole derivative that inhibits stomach acid secretion. It works by inhibiting the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in the gastric parietal cells, preventing the release of gastric acid. After oral administration, the antisecretory effect begins in about one hour, peaks in two hours, and lasts up to 72 hours. Secretory activity resumes gradually after stopping the medication over a period of 3 to 5 days.</p><p><strong>Dosage of PPI 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Benign gastric and duodenal ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks (duodenal ulcer), 8 weeks (gastric ulcer). In severe/recurrent cases, increase to 40 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>NSAID-associated ulcers:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, with an additional 4 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastro-esophageal reflux disease:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. For refractory cases, increase to 40 mg daily for 8 weeks. Maintenance dose: 20 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acid-related dyspepsia:</strong> 10-20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration:</strong> 40 mg one evening before surgery, then 40 mg 2-6 hours before surgery.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</strong> Start with 60 mg daily. Adjust dosage to 20-120 mg daily (divided dose for doses over 80 mg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication in peptic ulcer disease:</strong> 20 mg twice daily in combination with antibiotics for one week.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Severe ulcerating reflux esophagitis (Child &gt;1 year):</strong>If weight 10-20 kg, 10-20 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If weight &gt;20 kg, 20-40 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li><li><strong>IV Injection:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration:</strong> 40 mg intravenously, given slowly over 5 minutes, 1 hour before surgery.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal/gastric ulcer or reflux esophagitis:</strong> 40 mg IV once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</strong> Start with 60 mg daily intravenously. Adjust as needed, and for doses over 60 mg daily, divide into two doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of PPI 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV Injection:</strong>Reconstitute with 10 ml water for injection. Administer slowly over 2-5 minutes at a maximum rate of 4 ml/min.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Use freshly prepared solutions within 4 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>IV Infusion:</strong>Reconstitute in 100 ml saline or 5% dextrose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Administer as an infusion over 20-30 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Use saline solution within 12 hours, and dextrose within 6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not mix with other medications.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of PPI 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Warfarin, phenytoin, and diazepam may take longer to be cleared from the body when taken with PPI 20 mg, possibly requiring dosage adjustments.</li><li>No significant interaction with theophylline, propranolol, or antacids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient in the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of PPI 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Mild side effects may include nausea, stomach cramping, paresthesia, dizziness, and headache. These are usually temporary and don\'t typically require dosage adjustments.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>PPI 20 mg is classified under FDA pregnancy category C.</li><li>It has not been shown to negatively affect pregnancy or fetal health based on studies.</li><li>PPI 20 mg\'s effects on breast milk are unknown, so breastfeeding should be stopped if the drug is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Rule out gastric cancer before starting therapy, as PPI 20 mg may mask symptoms and delay diagnosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Blocker</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>PPI 20 mg inhibits stomach acid production by blocking the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system in parietal cells, reducing gastric acid secretion. Effects start within an hour and peak in two hours, lasting up to 72 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Category C, with no proven adverse effects on pregnancy or fetus based on clinical studies.</li></ul>', 'active', '843_1742579671.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:54:31', '2025-05-04 10:25:27'),
(4011, 'Sultolin', 'Salbutamol', 250.00, 1, 'x 200 metered doses', '1 inhaler', 'Metered-Dose Inhaler (MDI)', '100 mcg/puff', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sultolin 100 mcg</strong></p><p>Sultolin 100 mcg is indicated as a bronchodilator for use in:</p><ul><li>Asthma</li><li>Chronic Bronchitis</li><li>Emphysema</li><li>Other conditions associated with airway obstruction</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Sultolin 100 mcg is a selective beta2-adrenoceptor agonist. At therapeutic doses, it acts on the beta 2-adrenoceptors of bronchial smooth muscle, with little or no action on the ß1-adrenoceptors of cardiac muscle. Sultolin 100 mcg provides short-acting (4-6 hours) bronchodilatation with a fast onset (within 5 minutes) in reversible airway obstruction. It also has an anti-inflammatory effect on mast cells, inhibiting the release of bronchoconstrictor mediators such as histamine, neutrophil chemotactic factor (NCF), and prostaglandin D2.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sultolin 100 mcg</strong></p><p><strong>Oral</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong>2-6 years: 2.5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years: 5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Over 12 years: 5-10 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily (2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong>2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily (Maximum single dose is 8 mg tablet)</li></ul><p><strong>Injection</strong></p><p>Sultolin 100 mcg Injection may be administered subcutaneously, intramuscularly, or intravenously, under the direction of a physician.</p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong>Subcutaneous or Intramuscular: 500 mcg (8 mcg/kg) every 4 hours as required</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Slow Intravenous Injection: 250 mcg (4 mcg/kg) slowly, repeat as necessary</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric Population:</strong>Safety and efficacy in children under 12 years have not been established</li></ul><p><strong>Sultolin 100 mcg Respiratory Solution</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; Elderly:</strong>0.5 ml - 1.0 ml, up to four times a day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children under 12 years:</strong>0.5 ml, diluted to 2.0-4.0 ml with normal saline, may require up to 1.0 ml</li></ul><p><strong>Nebulizer Administration</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; Elderly:</strong>2.5 mg to 5 mg up to 4 times a day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children under 12 years:</strong>2.5 mg, up to 4 times a day (may increase to 5 mg as needed)</li></ul><p><strong>Sultolin 100 mcg Inhalation Capsule</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong>For acute bronchospasm: 1-2 capsules per dose</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For maintenance: 1 capsule every 4 to 6 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4 years and older):</strong>1 capsule for acute bronchospasm or before exercise, 3-4 times a day for maintenance</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>History of hypersensitivity to Sultolin 100 mcg or any of its components</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Sultolin 100 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>Fine tremor (particularly of hands)</li><li>Palpitations</li><li>Muscle cramps</li><li>Tachycardia</li><li>Tenseness</li><li>Headaches</li><li>Peripheral vasodilatation</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Should be used only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is not known whether Sultolin 100 mcg is excreted in human milk. Due to potential tumorigenicity shown in animal studies, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in patients with hyperthyroidism, cardiovascular disease, hypertension, or aneurysms.</li><li>Risk of hypokalemia, particularly with high doses, which may increase susceptibility to digitalis-induced cardiac arrhythmia.</li><li>Tachyphylaxis with prolonged use of high doses may occur.</li><li>Caution is necessary when treating patients with diabetes mellitus, closed-angle glaucoma, or those on antihypertensive therapy.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sultolin 100 mcg</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdose may include:</p><ul><li>Angina</li><li>Headache</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Tremor</li><li>The preferred antidote is a cardio-selective beta-blocking agent, but beta-blockers should be used with caution in patients with bronchospasm.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Sultolin 100 mcg is a synthetic sympathomimetic agent with predominant beta-2 adrenergic activity. It causes bronchodilation by stimulating beta-2 adrenergic receptors in bronchial smooth muscles, leading to the relaxation of bronchial muscle fibers and improved pulmonary function.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>The drug should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li>It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk, and due to potential tumorigenicity in animal studies, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, taking into account its importance to the mother.</li></ul>', 'active', '312_1742579820.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:57:00', '2025-04-23 07:37:16'),
(4012, 'Fossical-DX', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 17.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the text with the headings in normal bold formatting:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Fossical-DX</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment &amp; prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, disorders of osteogenesis. Also used as a supplement in case of inadequate intake of Calcium in childhood diet, rickets, pregnancy &amp; lactation, elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis, phosphate binder in chronic renal failure etc.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p>Or</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 200 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate Calcium in the blood is so vital to a wide variety of bodily functions that our internal biochemistry will not tolerate a deficiency even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that Calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of Osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is also essential for healthy bones as it aids in Calcium absorption from the GI tract. In addition to this, it stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies also show that Calcium and Vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth as well as in Osteoporosis and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Fossical-DX</strong></p><p>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night or as directed by the Physician</p><p><strong>Dosage of Fossical-DX</strong></p><p>One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or as directed by the physician. Taking in full stomach ensures better absorption.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Fossical-DX</strong></p><p>Oral Calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline &amp; fluoride preparations, and a minimum of 3 hours should be allowed between ingestion of these medications. Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of Calcium. Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce the metabolism of Vitamin D3. Concomitant ingestion of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of Calcium.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to any of the components, hypocalcaemia resulting from overdose of Vitamin D3, hyperparathyroidism, bone metastases, severe renal insufficiency, severe hypercalciuria, renal calculi, etc.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Fossical-DX</strong></p><p>Flatulence, diarrhoea, constipation, upper GI discomfort, etc. are rare manifestations. Hypercalcaemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It should be used as directed by the physician during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with reduction of dose may be needed. Plasma and serum Calcium levels should be monitored in mild to moderate renal impairment and also in case of long-term use. Patients with renal stones or with such previous history should also take precautions.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Fossical-DX</strong></p><p>At high doses, it may result in nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc. Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature of below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This is a Calcium and Vitamin D3 preparation where Calcium Carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The Calcium Carbonate from Coral has a chemical structure that is very similar to the composition of human bone. Coral Calcium is similar to other sources but ensures better absorption. Vitamin D3 aids in the absorption of Calcium from the GI tract and helps to maintain Calcium balance in the body.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of the physician.</p>', 'active', '1652_1742579928.jpg', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 22:58:48', '2025-05-03 10:51:46'),
(4013, 'Reef-DX', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 16.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Reef-DX</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment &amp; prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, and disorders of osteogenesis. It is also used as a supplement in cases of inadequate calcium intake in childhood diets, rickets, pregnancy &amp; lactation, and in elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis and as a phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg (from Coral source), equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg, Vitamin D3 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li><li>OR</li><li>Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg (from Coral source), equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg, Vitamin D3 200 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate Calcium in the blood is vital for various bodily functions, and the body will not tolerate a deficiency even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that Calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is essential for healthy bones, as it aids in calcium absorption from the GI tract and stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies also show that calcium and vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Reef-DX</strong></p><ul><li>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night or as directed by the physician.</li><li>One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or as directed by the physician. Taking with food ensures better absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Reef-DX</strong></p><ul><li>Oral calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline &amp; fluoride preparations; a minimum of 3 hours should be allowed between ingestion of these medications.</li><li>Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of calcium.</li><li>Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce the metabolism of Vitamin D3.</li><li>Concomitant ingestion of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of calcium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the components</li><li>Hypocalcemia resulting from overdose of Vitamin D3</li><li>Hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Bone metastases</li><li>Severe renal insufficiency</li><li>Severe hypercalciuria</li><li>Renal calculi</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Reef-DX</strong></p><p>Flatulence, diarrhea, constipation, upper GI discomfort, etc., are rare manifestations. Hypercalcemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It should be used as directed by the physician during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with a reduction of dose may be needed.</li><li>Plasma and serum calcium levels should be monitored in mild to moderate renal impairment and also during long-term use.</li><li>Patients with renal stones or a previous history of renal stones should also take precautions.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Reef-DX</strong></p><p>At high doses, it may result in nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc. Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This is a Calcium and Vitamin D3 preparation where Calcium Carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The calcium carbonate from coral has a chemical structure similar to human bone, ensuring better absorption compared to other sources. Vitamin D3 aids in the absorption of calcium from the GI tract and helps maintain calcium balance in the body.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of a physician.</p>', 'active', '1792_1742580071.webp', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:01:11', '2025-05-04 15:07:59'),
(4014, 'Supra-B', 'Vitamin B1, B6 & B12', 9.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg+200 mg+200 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Supra-B</h3><p>Supra-B is indicated for the treatment of vitamin B1, B6 &amp; B12 deficiency syndrome. It is also indicated for the supportive treatment of neuritis &amp; non-inflammatory diseases of the nerves, e.g.:</p><ul><li>Diabetic neuropathy</li><li>Peripheral neuralgia</li><li>Lumbago</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Optic neuritis</li><li>Sciatica</li><li>Facial neuralgia</li><li>Intercostal neuralgia</li><li>Spinal pain</li></ul><h3>Composition</h3><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate) 100 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride) 200 mg</li><li>Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) 200 mcg</li></ul><p>Each 3 ml ampoule contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) 100 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) 100 mg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) 1000 mcg</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Supra-B is a combined preparation of Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate), Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride), and Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin). These vitamins play an essential role as coenzymes in the metabolism of the nervous system and support the regeneration of nerve fibers and myelin sheath by activating nerve cell metabolism and natural repair mechanisms.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Supra-B</h3><ul><li><strong>Tablets</strong>: 1 to 3 tablets per day or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly (deep intragluteal).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In severe cases: 1 ampoule daily until the acute symptoms subside.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For milder cases and follow-up therapy: 2 to 3 ampoules per week.</li></ul><h3>Dosage of Supra-B</h3><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>: 1-3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In severe (acute) cases: 1 injection daily until the acute symptoms subside or as advised by the physician.</li><li>In mild cases: 1 injection 2-3 times per week.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Children</strong>: There is no information on the use of this drug in children.</p><h3>Interaction of Supra-B</h3><p>No drug interactions have been reported yet.</p><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Vitamin B1, Vitamin B6, and Vitamin B12 are contraindicated in patients on levodopa therapy and in patients with hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of the preparation.</p><h3>Side Effects of Supra-B</h3><p>Generally well tolerated, but allergic reactions may be observed in some cases.</p><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><p>No risks have become known associated with the use of this combination during pregnancy at the recommended dosage. Vitamin B1, B6, &amp; B12 are secreted into breast milk, but the risks of overdose for the infant are not known.</p><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><p>Cyanocobalamin should not be given to patients with subacute degeneration of the spinal cord. Cyanocobalamin is not suitable for the treatment of optic neuropathies associated with raised plasma concentrations of cyanocobalamin.</p><h3>Overdose Effects of Supra-B</h3><p>No overdosage symptoms are expected at the recommended dosage. If an overdose is known, treatment is symptomatic and supportive.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><p>Keep out of reach of children. Store in a cool (below 25°C) and dry place, protected from light.</p><h3>Drug Classes</h3><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Vitamin B1</strong>: Converts carbohydrates, fatty acids, and amino acids into energy, promotes healthy nerves, improves mood, and strengthens the heart.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6</strong>: Forms RBCs, helps cells make proteins, manufactures neurotransmitters (e.g., serotonin), releases stored forms of energy, helps prevent cardiovascular diseases and stroke, lifts depression, and eases insomnia.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12</strong>: Essential for cell replication, important for RBC production, prevents anemia, reduces nerve pain, numbness, and tingling, and lowers the risk of heart diseases.</li></ul><p>The vitamin ingredients are absorbed well with peroral reception and are widely distributed to most tissues, appearing in breast milk. Thiamine is mostly present as diphosphate within the cell and is excreted in the urine if in excess. Pyridoxine, pyridoxal, and pyridoxamine are readily absorbed and converted to active forms that are stored mainly in the liver and excreted in the urine.</p><h3>Pregnancy</h3><p>Oral tablet form is recommended, but due to the presence of benzyl alcohol, the injection is not recommended during pregnancy &amp; lactation.</p>', 'active', '232_1742580186.webp', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:03:06', '2025-05-04 21:27:22');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4015, 'Calcin-D', 'Elemental Calcium + Vitamin D3', 8.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Calcin-D</strong></p><p>This combination is used for the treatment of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, rickets, tetany, and parathyroid disease. Calcium supplements are often used to ensure adequate dietary intake in conditions such as pregnancy &amp; lactation, osteogenesis, and tooth formation (adjunct with definite treatment) and therapy with anti-seizure medications. It is also used as a routine supplement and phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Calcin-D is a preparation of Calcium Carbonate and Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol). Calcium is necessary for many normal functions of the body, especially bone formation and maintenance. Vitamin D3 helps with the absorption and reabsorption of Calcium. Vitamin D3 also stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies have shown that Calcium and Vitamin D3 together help in bone growth and in the prevention of osteoporosis &amp; bone fractures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Calcin-D</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 200 IU Tablet</strong>: 2 tablets daily or 1 tablet twice daily.</li><li><strong>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 400 IU Tablet</strong>: 1 tablet twice daily.</li></ul><p>It is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Calcin-D</strong></p><p>It has possible interactions with calcium, aluminium, or magnesium-containing antacids &amp; other calcium supplements, calcitriol &amp; other vitamin D3 supplements; digoxin, tetracycline, doxycycline, minocycline, or oxytetracycline.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Calcin-D is contraindicated in cases of hypercalcaemia, hyperthyroidism, renal calculi &amp; nephrolithiasis, and Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Calcin-D</strong></p><p>Calcin-D is generally well tolerated. If symptoms like nausea, vomiting, stomach cramps, dry mouth, increased thirst, or increased urination occur, consult a physician. Constipation may also occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This combination should be used as directed by a physician during pregnancy or while breast-feeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Precautions should be taken if there is any pre-existing heart disease or kidney disease.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Calcin-D</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdosage may include nausea and vomiting, severe drowsiness, dry mouth, loss of appetite, metallic taste, stomach cramps, diarrhea, headache, and constipation.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1596_1742580346.jpg', 6, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:05:46', '2025-05-03 01:08:45'),
(4016, 'Combicid', 'Clobetasol Propionate + Ofloxacin + Ornidazole + Terbinafine', 180.00, 1, 'x 15 gm tube', '15 gm tube', 'Cream', '0.05%+0.75%+2%+1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Combicid 15 gm</strong></p><p>This cream is indicated in the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Mixed and complicated Fungal, Protozoal &amp; Bacterial infections</li><li>Mild to moderate vaginitis</li><li>Candidiasis and trichomoniasis</li><li>Pruritus vulvae</li><li>Dermatitis</li><li>Eczema</li><li>Genito-urinary infections</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each gram of cream contains:</p><ul><li>Clobetasol Propionate USP 0.50 mg</li><li>Ofloxacin USP 7.50 mg</li><li>Ornidazole INN 20 mg</li><li>Terbinafine Hydrochloride BP 10 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This cream is a combination cream which exhibits anti-bacterial, anti-fungal, anti-protozoal &amp; anti-inflammatory actions.</p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol</strong> is a potent corticosteroid which exhibits anti-inflammatory, anti-pruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties.</li><li><strong>Ofloxacin</strong> is a broad-spectrum antibiotic that acts against many gram-positive &amp; gram-negative bacteria.</li><li><strong>Ornidazole</strong> belongs to the nitroimidazole group of antibiotics and is used to treat amoeba and trichomonas infections.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine</strong> is an Allylamine antifungal that inhibits biosynthesis of Ergosterol (an essential component of the fungal cell membrane) via inhibition of Squalene Epoxidase enzyme, causing fungal cell death.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Combicid 15 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Apply by gently rubbing onto the affected area twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Combicid 15 gm</strong></p><ul><li>No hazardous interactions have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients hypersensitive to any of the components of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Combicid 15 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Burning, itching, irritation, dry skin</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The safe use of this preparation during pregnancy &amp; lactation has not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Do not swallow. For external use only.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C. Keep away from light and out of the reach of children. Do not freeze.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This cream is a combination cream which exhibits anti-bacterial, anti-fungal, anti-protozoal &amp; anti-inflammatory actions.</p><ul><li><strong>Ofloxacin</strong> is a broad-spectrum antibiotic that acts against many gram-positive &amp; gram-negative bacteria.</li><li><strong>Ornidazole</strong> belongs to the nitroimidazole group of antibiotics and is used to treat amoeba and trichomonas infections.</li><li><strong>Terbinafine</strong> is an Allylamine antifungal that inhibits biosynthesis of Ergosterol (an essential component of the fungal cell membrane) via inhibition of Squalene Epoxidase enzyme, causing fungal cell death.</li><li><strong>Clobetasol</strong> is a potent corticosteroid which exhibits anti-inflammatory, anti-pruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>The safe use of this preparation during pregnancy &amp; lactation has not been established.</li></ul>', 'active', '617_1742580476.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:07:56', '2025-05-03 01:33:30'),
(4017, 'E-Gel DS', 'Vitamin E [Alpha Tocopherol Acetate]', 6.02, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '400 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the revised version with headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of E-Gel DS 400 IU</strong></p><p>As a dietary supplement:</p><ul><li>Vitamin E deficiency resulting from impaired absorption</li><li>Increased requirements due to a diet rich in polyunsaturated fats</li><li>For healthy hair &amp; skin</li><li>As an antioxidant</li><li>Hemolytic anemia due to Vitamin E deficiency</li></ul><p>Therapeutic use:</p><ul><li>Cardiovascular disease</li><li>Heavy metal poisoning</li><li>Hepatotoxin poisoning</li><li>Hemolytic anemia</li><li>Oxygen therapy</li><li>In nutritional deficiency states</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin-E Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Vitamin E acts as an antioxidant in the body. It protects polyunsaturated fatty acids (which are components of cellular membranes) and other oxygen-sensitive substances such as vitamin A and vitamin C from oxidation. In premature neonates, irritability, edema, thrombosis, and hemolytic anemia may be caused by vitamin E deficiency. Creatinuria, ceroid deposition, muscle weakness, decreased erythrocyte survival, or increased in vitro hemolysis by oxidizing agents have been identified in adults and children with low serum tocopherol concentrations.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of E-Gel DS 400 IU</strong></p><ul><li>Betterment of cardiovascular health: 400 IU-800 IU per day</li><li>Deficiency syndrome in adults: 200 IU-400 IU per day</li><li>Deficiency syndrome in children: 200 IU per day</li><li>Thalassemia: 800 IU per day</li><li>Sickle-cell anemia: 400 IU per day</li><li>Betterment of skin &amp; hair: 200 IU-400 IU per day (Topical use is also established for beautification)</li><li>Chronic cold in adults: 200 IU per day</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of E-Gel DS 400 IU</strong></p><p>Vitamin E may impair the absorption of Vitamin A and the function of Vitamin K and potentiates the effect of Warfarin.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>No known contraindications found.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of E-Gel DS 400 IU</strong></p><p>Overdosage (&gt;1 gm) has been associated with minor side effects, including hypertension, fatigue, diarrhea, and myopathy.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Vitamin E is safe in pregnancy and lactation when used at recommended doses. Higher doses are not established.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>It may increase the risk of thrombosis in some patients, such as those taking estrogens.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>Vitamin E is safe for children.</p>', 'active', '1893_1742580571.jpg', 6, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:09:31', '2025-05-03 09:59:58'),
(4018, 'Zerocal Box', 'Zero Calories Sugar Substitute', 250.00, 1, 'x 75 sachet in a box', '75 sachet in a box', 'Sachet', 'N/A', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '49_1742580993.png', 13, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:16:33', '2025-05-07 20:33:13'),
(4019, 'Purisal', 'Levosalbutamol', 45.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '1 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Purisal 100 ml</strong></p><p>Purisal 100 ml is indicated for the treatment and prevention of bronchospasm in adults, adolescents, and children of 6 years of age and older with reversible obstructive airway diseases.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Purisal 100 ml is a single isomer beta-2 agonist that differs from racemic salbutamol by the elimination of (S)-salbutamol. Purisal 100 ml is an effective bronchodilator whose primary mechanism of action is not inhibited by (S)-salbutamol. Thus, when compared with racemic salbutamol, clinically comparable bronchodilation can be achieved. Purisal 100 ml produces bronchodilatation through stimulation of beta-2-adrenergic receptors in bronchial smooth muscles, thereby causing relaxation of bronchial muscle fibers.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Purisal 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children (6-11 years):</strong> 5 ml (1 mg), three times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and above):</strong> 5-10 ml (1-2 mg), three times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Purisal 100 ml</strong></p><p>Other short-acting sympathomimetic bronchodilators or epinephrine should be used with caution with Purisal 100 ml. If additional adrenergic drugs are to be administered by any route, they should be used with caution to avoid deleterious cardiovascular effects.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Purisal 100 ml is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Purisal 100 ml or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Purisal 100 ml</strong></p><p>Hypocalcaemia, palpitation, fine tremors of the skeletal muscle, and muscle cramps may occur. The other likely side effects are nausea, vomiting, epigastric pain, and diarrhea.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The drug should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether Purisal 100 ml is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when oral Purisal 100 ml is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Particular caution is advised in acute severe asthma as this effect may be potentiated by hypoxia and by concomitant treatment with xanthine derivatives, steroids, and diuretics. Serum potassium levels should be monitored in such situations.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place and away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1798_1742581162.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:19:22', '2025-05-04 14:57:13'),
(4020, 'Neuroxen', 'Vitamin B1, B6 & B12', 12.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg+200 mg+200 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Neuroxen</strong></p><p>Neuroxen is indicated for the treatment of vitamin B1, B6 &amp; B12 deficiency syndrome. It is also indicated for the supportive treatment of neuritis and non-inflammatory diseases of the nerves, such as:</p><ul><li>Diabetic neuropathy</li><li>Peripheral neuralgia</li><li>Lumbago</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Optic neuritis</li><li>Sciatica</li><li>Facial neuralgia</li><li>Intercostal neuralgia</li><li>Spinal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate) 100 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride) 200 mg</li><li>Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) 200 mcg</li></ul><p>Each 3 ml ampoule contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) 100 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) 100 mg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) 1000 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Neuroxen is a combined preparation of Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate), Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride), and Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin). These vitamins play an essential role as co-enzymes in the metabolism of the nervous system. It supports the regeneration of nerve fibers and myelin sheath by activating the nerve cell metabolism and the natural repair mechanism.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Neuroxen</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablets</strong>: 1 to 3 tablets per day or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly (deep intragluteal). In severe cases, 1 ampoule daily until the acute symptoms subside. For milder cases and follow-up therapy, 2 to 3 ampoules per week.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Neuroxen</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>: 1-3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In severe (acute) cases: 1 injection daily until the acute symptoms subside or as advised by the physician.</li><li>In mild cases: 1 injection 2-3 times per week.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Children</strong></p><p>There is no information on the use of this drug in children.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Neuroxen</strong></p><p>No drug interaction has been reported yet.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vitamin B1, Vitamin B6, and Vitamin B12 are contraindicated in patients on levodopa therapy and in patients with hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of the preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Neuroxen</strong></p><p>Generally well tolerated, but allergic reactions may be observed in a few cases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>No risks have been identified with the use of this combination during pregnancy at the recommended dosage. Vitamin B1, B6 &amp; B12 are secreted into breast milk, but the risks of overdose for the infant are not known.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Cyanocobalamin should not be given to patients with subacute degeneration of the spinal cord.</li><li>Cyanocobalamin is not a suitable form of Vitamin B12 for the treatment of optic neuropathies associated with raised plasma concentrations of cyanocobalamin.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Neuroxen</strong></p><p>No overdosage symptoms are expected at the recommended dosage. In case of overdose, treatment is symptomatic and supportive.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep out of reach of children. Store in a cool (below 25°C) and dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin B1</strong>: Converts carbohydrates, fatty acids, and amino acids into energy, promotes healthy nerves, improves mood, and strengthens the heart.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6</strong>: Forms RBCs, helps cells to make proteins, manufactures neurotransmitters like serotonin, and releases stored forms of energy. It helps to prevent cardiovascular diseases, stroke, lift depression, and ease insomnia.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12</strong>: Essential for cell replication, RBC production, and prevents anemia. It helps prevent depression, reduces nerve pain, numbness, tingling, and lowers the risk of heart disease.</li></ul><p>The vitamin ingredients are absorbed well in oral form. They are widely distributed to most tissues and appear in breast milk. Within the cell, thiamine is mostly present as diphosphate. Thiamine is not stored in the body and any excess is excreted in the urine. Pyridoxine, pyridoxal, and pyridoxamine are absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract, converted to active forms, and stored mainly in the liver, where they are oxidized to inactive metabolites excreted in the urine.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Oral tablet form is recommended, but due to the presence of benzyl alcohol, injections are not recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</p>', 'in-active', '119_1742581303.jpg', 67, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:21:44', '2025-05-12 17:35:09'),
(4021, 'Odmon', 'Montelukast Sodium', 16.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Odmon 10 mg</h3><p>Odmon 10 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</strong></li><li><strong>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</strong></li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from various cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. Cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) are located in the human airway, and these eicosanoids bind to them. Odmon 10 mg, an orally active drug, has a strong affinity and specificity for the CysLT1 receptor. Without any agonist activity, it inhibits the physiological effects of LTD4 at the CysLT1 receptor.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Odmon 10 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (15 years and older)</strong>: 10 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6 to 14 years: 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 to 5 years: 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6 months to 5 years: 4 mg oral granules once daily (may be administered directly or mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature).</li></ul><h3>Use in Pediatric Patients</h3><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Monas have been established in well-controlled studies for pediatric patients with asthma aged 6 months to 14 years, showing similar safety and efficacy profiles as in adults.</li></ul><h3>Hepatic and Renal Insufficiency</h3><ul><li><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage adjustment is required for mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage adjustment is recommended.</li></ul><h3>Elderly Use</h3><ul><li>The pharmacokinetic profile of Odmon 10 mg is similar in elderly and younger adults. No dosage adjustment is required in elderly patients.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Odmon 10 mg</h3><ul><li>Odmon 10 mg has shown no clinically significant interactions with theophylline, prednisone, oral contraceptives, terfenadine, digoxin, or warfarin.</li><li>When combined with cytochrome P450 enzyme inducers like phenobarbital or rifampin, clinical monitoring is advised due to potential changes in pharmacokinetics.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Odmon 10 mg is contraindicated in patients with extreme sensitivity to any ingredient in the medication.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Odmon 10 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, asthenia, aberrant behavior, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, hemorrhage, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, oedema, seizure, odd feeling, and sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, decreased attention, disorientation, erythema nodosum, hallucinations, hepatic issues, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, and tremor.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: The drug crosses the placenta in rats and rabbits, but there are no well-controlled studies in humans. It should only be used during pregnancy if absolutely necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is secreted in breast milk, but its implications are unclear. Caution should be exercised when used by nursing mothers.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Odmon 10 mg should not be used for the reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks (e.g., status asthmaticus).</li><li><strong>Aspirin Sensitivity</strong>: Patients with documented aspirin sensitivity should avoid taking aspirin or NSAIDs while on this medication.</li><li>Asthma exacerbations and exercise-induced bronchospasm should not be treated with a single dose of Odmon 10 mg.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Odmon 10 mg</h3><ul><li>Overdose may cause nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain. In case of overdose, customary supportive measures, such as removing unabsorbed material and clinical monitoring, should be followed.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry environment, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li><strong>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</strong></li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Odmon 10 mg inhibits the cysteinyl leukotriene receptor (CysLT1), which plays a key role in the pathophysiology of asthma and allergic rhinitis, including airway edema, smooth muscle contraction, and inflammatory processes.</p><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li>The drug passes the placenta in animal studies, but there is limited data in humans. It should be used during pregnancy only if necessary. Caution is also advised for nursing mothers due to the secretion in breast milk.</li></ul>', 'active', '389_1742581399.jpg', 6, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:23:19', '2025-05-04 01:14:40'),
(4022, 'Alcet', 'Levocetirizine Dihydrochloride', 50.00, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Oral Solution', '2.5 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Alcet 60 ml</h3><p>Levocetirizine is indicated in the treatment of symptoms associated with allergic conditions such as:</p><ul><li>Perennial allergic rhinitis</li><li>Seasonal allergic rhinitis</li><li>Chronic idiopathic urticaria</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Alcet 60 ml is a preparation containing Levocetirizine, the active (levo) isomer of Cetirizine. It is a new, highly effective, and well-tolerated non-sedating antihistamine with potent antiallergic properties. Levocetirizine has a two-fold higher affinity for H1 receptors than Cetirizine. It has rapid and long-lasting action, allowing once-a-day administration.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults and children 12 years of age and older</strong>: 5 mg once daily in the evening</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 11 years of age</strong>: 2.5 mg once daily in the evening</li><li><strong>Children 6 months to 5 years of age</strong>: 1.25 mg (1/2 teaspoon oral solution) once daily in the evening</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Adjust the dose in patients 12 years of age and older with decreased renal function</li></ul><h4>Renal Impairment Dosing:</h4><ul><li><strong>Mild renal impairment (creatinine clearance: 50-80 ml/min)</strong>: 2.5 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Moderate renal impairment (creatinine clearance: 30-50 ml/min)</strong>: 2.5 mg once every other day</li><li><strong>Severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance: 10-30 ml/min)</strong>: 2.5 mg twice weekly (administered once every 3-4 days)</li><li><strong>End-stage renal disease (creatinine clearance: &lt;10 ml/min)</strong>: Should not receive Levocetirizine</li></ul><h3>Interaction</h3><p>Levocetirizine is not known to have any interactions with other drugs.</p><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Levocetirizine, Cetirizine, or its parent compound Hydroxyzine</li><li>Patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance: &lt;10 ml/min)</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><p>Generally, Levocetirizine is well-tolerated, but a few side effects have been reported rarely:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Fatigue</li><li>Skin rash</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: The safety of Levocetirizine during pregnancy has not been established. Use with caution during pregnancy only if the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the risks to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Levocetirizine passes into breast milk, so it should be used with caution in nursing mothers if the benefit to the mother outweighs the risk to the infant.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Epileptic patients and those at risk of convulsions</li><li>Renal impairment</li><li>Pregnancy and lactation</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><p>No clinically relevant adverse events have been reported in the event of an overdose. Symptomatic and supportive treatment is recommended.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><p>Store at a temperature not exceeding 30°C in a dry place. Protect from light.</p><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Paediatric use</strong>: Not recommended for children under 6 years of age.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Levocetirizine is a highly effective non-sedating antihistamine with potent antiallergic properties. It has a two-fold higher affinity for H1 receptors than Cetirizine, allowing rapid and long-acting effects, which enables once-a-day administration.</p><h3>Pregnancy Category</h3><p>Category B. No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Levocetirizine should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Since Levocetirizine is excreted in breast milk, it is not recommended for use by nursing mothers.</p>', 'active', '1765_1742581586.png', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:26:26', '2025-05-02 22:54:04'),
(4023, 'Gaviflux', 'Sodium Alginate + Sodium Bicarbonate + Calcium Carbonate', 300.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '(500 mg+267 mg+160 mg)/10 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Gaviflux 200 ml</strong></p><p>Gaviflux 200 ml is indicated for individuals experiencing:</p><ul><li>Gastric reflux</li><li>Heartburn</li><li>Flatulence associated with gastric reflux</li><li>Heartburn during pregnancy</li><li>Epigastric and retrosternal discomfort due to gastric reflux</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Antacids</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Gaviflux 200 ml acts physically, without being absorbed into the systemic circulation. Upon ingestion, it reacts with gastric acid, forming a protective alginic acid gel raft with a nearly neutral pH. This raft floats on the stomach contents, preventing gastro-esophageal reflux for up to 4 hours. In severe cases, the raft may reflux into the esophagus, providing a soothing effect.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years:</strong> 10-20 ml after meals and at bedtime, up to four times a day.</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 12 years:</strong> 5-10 ml after meals and at bedtime, up to four times a day.</li><li><strong>Children under 6 years:</strong> Not recommended.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> No dosage adjustment required.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No dosage modification necessary.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong> Use caution if a highly restricted salt diet is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong> A 2-hour interval should be observed between taking Gaviflux and other medications, especially:</p><ul><li>Tetracyclines, digoxin, fluoroquinolones, iron salts, ketoconazole</li><li>Neuroleptics, thyroid hormones, penicillamine, beta-blockers</li><li>Glucocorticoids, chloroquine, bisphosphonates, estramustine</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong> Do not use in patients with hypersensitivity to any of the active ingredients or excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong> Potential side effects include:</p><ul><li>Constipation</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Belching</li></ul><p>Excessive dose may lead to a sensation of swelling, in which case medical advice should be sought.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Safe when clinically necessary, based on clinical studies and post-marketing data.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong> No adverse effects observed in breastfed infants.</li><li><strong>Fertility:</strong> No harmful effects on fertility or reproduction.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>If symptoms persist beyond 7 days, reassess the clinical condition.</li><li>Each 10 mL dose contains 141 mg of sodium (6.2 mmol), which should be considered in cases of a restricted salt diet, particularly for patients with cardiac failure or renal impairment.</li><li>Each 10 mL dose contains 160 mg of calcium carbonate (1.6 mmol), so caution is needed for patients with hypercalcemia, nephrocalcinosis, or recurrent calcium-containing renal calculi.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong> In the case of overdose, symptomatic treatment should be given. Abdominal distension may occur.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool (below 30°C), dry place, away from light.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li><li>Do not refrigerate or freeze.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Not recommended for children under 6 years.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> No dose modification needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Antacids</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Gaviflux 200 ml combines Calcium Carbonate and Sodium Bicarbonate with Alginate. It forms a gel raft that floats on the stomach contents, neutralizing acid and protecting the esophagus from acid reflux. The raft also entraps bile and pepsin, further protecting the esophagus. Calcium Carbonate and Sodium Bicarbonate work together to neutralize stomach acid, providing rapid relief from indigestion and heartburn.</p>', 'active', '1103_1742581921.webp', 55, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:32:01', '2025-05-03 18:11:07'),
(4024, 'Esonix', 'Esomeprazole', 7.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Esonix 20 mg</strong></p><p>Esonix 20 mg is indicated for the following:</p><ul><li>To alleviate GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn.</li><li>To treat erosive esophagitis.</li><li>To maintain the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>For the eradication of <em>Helicobacter pylori</em> infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease, when used in combination with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>For the treatment of Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</li><li>For duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Esonix 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. Most patients heal within 4 to 8 weeks. For those who do not heal, an additional 4-8 weeks may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely after 4 weeks, another 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication</strong>: For duodenal ulcer recurrence prevention, use 40 mg esomeprazole once daily for 10 days, along with 1000 mg amoxicillin twice daily and 500 mg clarithromycin twice daily.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg once daily, adjusted individually, with treatment continuing as long as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks based on response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: 40 mg once daily by intravenous injection (not less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes). The solution should not be administered with any other medications through the same intravenous site.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Esonix 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take one Esonix 20 mg capsule or pill whole, one hour before meals.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>: Mix the contents of one packet with 15 ml of water in a small glass, stir thoroughly, and allow it to thicken for 2-3 minutes. Stir again and consume within 30 minutes. If any medicine remains, add extra water, swirl, and drink right away.</li><li>For nasogastric or gastric tube administration, mix the suspension in a syringe and administer as described above.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong>: Add 5 ml of solvent to the vial and administer the reconstituted solution over a minimum of 3 minutes. Use within 12 hours and keep at room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Esonix 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CYP2C19 and CYP3A4</strong> metabolize esomeprazole.</li><li><strong>No significant interactions</strong> with medications metabolized by CYPs 1A2, 2A6, 2C9, 2D6, 2E1, and 3A4.</li><li><strong>Diazepam</strong>: Esomeprazole reduces clearance of diazepam by 45%, increasing plasma levels.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal pH</strong>: Esomeprazole may reduce the absorption of medications dependent on gastric pH (e.g., ketoconazole, iron salts, digoxin).</li><li><strong>Clarithromycin</strong>: Co-administration may increase plasma levels of esomeprazole and 14-hydroxy clarithromycin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any formulation of esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Esonix 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, and dry mouth.</li><li>Side effects during maintenance therapy (up to 12 months) are similar to those during short-term treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>A positive response to symptoms does not rule out the possibility of stomach cancer.</li><li>Esonix 20 mg capsules should be taken at least one hour before meals.</li><li>For patients who have difficulty swallowing capsules, the pellets can be mixed with cold applesauce and consumed immediately. Do not chew or crush the pellets.</li><li>Antacids can be used while taking esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Esonix 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Acute poisoning in animals has caused decreased motor activity, changes in breathing, tremors, ataxia, and convulsions.</li><li>No specific countermeasures for overdose.</li><li>Dialysis is not expected to remove esomeprazole due to its protein binding.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place at temperatures not exceeding 30°C.</li><li>Keep away from moisture and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor that inhibits H+/K+-ATPase in gastric parietal cells, reducing acid secretion.</li><li>It is the S-isomer of omeprazole, offering superior acid control compared to racemic proton pump inhibitors.</li><li>Esomeprazole is extensively metabolized by the liver and excreted in urine (80%) and feces (20%) as inactive metabolites.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>No sufficient and reliable research on its use in pregnant women.</li><li>Animal studies show no teratogenic effects.</li><li>Esomeprazole is excreted in milk, so breastfeeding should be discontinued if esomeprazole is deemed necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>No significant differences in safety and efficacy between elderly and younger patients, though older individuals may be more sensitive.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage change is needed for mild to moderate hepatic insufficiency.</li><li>For severe hepatic impairment, limit the dose to 20 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>As esomeprazole is minimally excreted unchanged in urine, renal impairment is not expected to affect its pharmacokinetics.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '555_1742582229.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:37:09', '2025-05-08 14:51:28'),
(4025, 'Rhinozol', 'Xylometazoline Hydrochloride', 20.00, 1, 'x 15 ml drop', '15 ml drop', 'Nasal Drop', '0.1%', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Rhinozol 0.1% 15 ml</strong></h3><ul><li>For the symptomatic relief of nasal congestion, perennial allergic rhinitis (including hay fever), and sinusitis.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Nasal Anti-histamine preparations</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li>Rhinozol 0.1% 15 ml is a sympathomimetic agent with marked alpha-adrenergic activity, intended for use in the nose.</li><li>It constricts the nasal blood vessels, decongesting the mucosa of the nose and neighboring regions of the pharynx, helping patients suffering from colds to breathe more easily through the nose.</li><li>The effect begins within a few minutes and lasts for several hours.</li><li>It is generally well tolerated and does not impair the function of ciliated epithelium.</li><li>Systemic absorption may occur following nasal application.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 2 or 3 drops of Rhinozol 0.1% 15 ml (Adult formula) two to three times daily. This formula should not be used for children under 12 years.</li><li><strong>Children under 12 years</strong>: 1 or 2 drops of Rhinozol 0.1% 15 ml (children\'s formula 0.05%) in each nostril once or twice daily.</li><li><strong>Not to be used in infants less than 3 months.</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction</strong></h3><ul><li>No drug interactions have been reported.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Rhinozol 0.1% 15 ml is contraindicated in patients with trans-sphenoidal hypophysectomy or surgery exposing the dura mater.</li><li>It is also contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to Rhinozol 0.1% 15 ml.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Occasionally encountered side effects include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Burning sensation in the nose and throat</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Local irritation</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Headache</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dryness of the nasal mucosa</li><li>Systemic cardiovascular effects may occur. This should be kept in mind when giving Rhinozol 0.1% 15 ml to people with cardiovascular disease.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>No fetal toxicity or fertility studies have been conducted in animals.</li><li>Due to its potential systemic vasoconstrictor effect, it is advisable to avoid using Rhinozol 0.1% 15 ml during pregnancy.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Each Rhinozol 0.1% 15 ml pack should be used by one person only to prevent cross-infection.</li><li>Patients are advised not to use decongestants for more than seven consecutive days.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Protect from heat.</li><li>For hygiene reasons, do not use the bottle more than 28 days after opening.</li></ul>', 'active', '921_1742582345.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:39:05', '2025-05-04 16:58:12'),
(4026, 'Acical-D', 'Elemental Calcium + Vitamin D3', 8.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet i a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Acical-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>This combination is used for the treatment of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, rickets, tetany, and in parathyroid disease. Calcium supplements are often used to ensure adequate dietary intake in conditions such as pregnancy &amp; lactation, osteogenesis, and tooth formation (adjunct with definite treatment) and therapy with anti-seizure medications. It is also used as a routine supplement and phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Acical-D 500 mg+200 IU is the preparation of Calcium Carbonate and Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol). Calcium is necessary for many normal functions of our body, especially bone formation and maintenance. Vitamin D3 helps with the absorption &amp; reabsorption of Calcium. Vitamin D3 also stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies showed that Calcium and Vitamin D3 together help in bone growth and in the prevention of osteoporosis &amp; bone fractures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Acical-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 200 IU Tablet</strong>: 2 tablets daily or 1 tablet twice daily. Acical-D 500 mg+200 IU is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li><li><strong>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 400 IU Tablet</strong>: 1 tablet twice daily. It is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Acical-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>It has possible interaction with calcium, aluminium or magnesium-containing antacids &amp; other calcium supplements, calcitriol &amp; other vitamin D3 supplements, digoxin, tetracycline, doxycycline, minocycline, or oxytetracycline.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Acical-D 500 mg+200 IU is contraindicated in cases of hypercalcaemia, hyperthyroidism, renal calculi &amp; nephrolithiasis, and Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Acical-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>Acical-D 500 mg+200 IU is generally well tolerated. However, if there is experience of nausea, vomiting, stomach cramps, dry mouth, increased thirst, increased urination while taking, it should be reported to a physician. Constipation may also occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This combination should be used as directed by a physician during pregnancy or while breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>If there is any pre-existing heart disease or kidney disease, precautions should be taken.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Acical-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdosage may include nausea and vomiting, severe drowsiness, dry mouth, loss of appetite, metallic taste, stomach cramps, diarrhea, headache, and constipation.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '902_1742582428.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:40:28', '2025-05-02 22:46:21'),
(4027, 'Algecal Dx', 'Algae Calcium + Vitamin D3', 16.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you provided with the headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Algecal Dx 600 mg+400 IU</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment and prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism &amp; osteogenesis. It can be used as a supplement in case of inadequate intake of Calcium in childhood diet, pregnancy &amp; lactation, rickets &amp; poor absorption during old age. Other indications include pancreatitis &amp; phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Calcium is absorbed in the intestine which then passes through the intestine to the blood and is deposited into the bone. Active transport through the cell is dependent on Vitamin D3. Vitamin D3 keeps the bone healthy by increasing the absorption of calcium in the intestines. Without Vitamin D3, the body will be able to absorb only 10% to 15% of the Calcium from the diet.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Algecal Dx 600 mg+400 IU</strong></p><p>One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or food, as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Algecal Dx 600 mg+400 IU</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Major interaction</strong>: Administering intravenous Ceftriaxone &amp; Calcium can result in life-threatening damage to the lungs &amp; kidneys. Calcium should not be administered intravenously within 48 hours of intravenous Ceftriaxone.</li><li><strong>Minor interaction</strong>: Calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline &amp; fluoride preparations, and a minimum of 3 hours should be allowed between the ingestion of these medications. Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of calcium. Phenytoin, barbiturates &amp; glucocorticoids may induce metabolism of Vitamin D3. Intake of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of calcium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Increased activity of the parathyroid gland, hypercalcemia, constipation, kidney stones, and extreme loss of body water are conditions in which calcium intake is contraindicated.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Algecal Dx 600 mg+400 IU</strong></p><p>Flatulence, diarrhea, constipation, and allergic reactions, etc., are rare manifestations. Hypercalcemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This should be given to pregnant &amp; lactating mothers as per the recommendation of a physician. Toxicity was tested on pregnant &amp; lactating females, which resulted in excellent outcomes.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In mild hypercalciuria, reduction of the dose is sufficient to return to normal serum calcium concentration. Patients with kidney stones or with a previous history of kidney stones should be recommended to increase their fluid intake.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</p>', 'active', '1837_1742582517.jpg', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:41:57', '2025-05-02 22:54:58');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4028, 'Famomax', 'Famotidine', 3.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Famomax 20 mg</strong></p><p>Famomax 20 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Gastric ulcer</li><li>Duodenal ulcer</li><li>Anastomotic ulcer</li><li>Acute stress ulcer</li><li>Reflux esophagitis</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Famomax 20 mg is also indicated for the treatment of acute gastritis and chronic gastritis in the acute exacerbation stage.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>H2 receptor antagonist</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Famomax 20 mg is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist. It completely inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors of parietal cells, inhibiting basal, overnight, and pentagastrin-stimulated gastric acid secretion. The H2-receptor antagonist activity of Famomax 20 mg is slowly reversible, as the drug dissociates slowly from the H2-receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Famomax 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer:</strong> 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks</li><li><strong>Benign gastric ulcer:</strong> 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks; Maintenance therapy: 20 mg at night to prevent the recurrence of duodenal ulceration</li><li><strong>Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease:</strong> 20 mg twice daily for 6 to 12 weeks</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</strong> Starting dose of 20 mg every six hours, adjusted to individual response. Doses up to 160 mg every six hours have been administered to some patients without significant adverse effects</li><li><strong>Acute gastritis, chronic gastritis (acute exacerbation stage):</strong> 20 mg twice a day (after breakfast and supper or before bedtime); alternatively, 40 mg at night</li><li>Famomax 20 mg can be administered irrespective of meals. Antacids may be given concomitantly if needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Famomax 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet (Adults):</strong></li><li>Gastric ulcer, duodenal ulcer, anastomotic ulcer, upper gastrointestinal hemorrhage, reflux esophagitis, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg twice daily (after breakfast and supper or before bedtime) or 40 mg orally once daily at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute gastritis, chronic gastritis in acute exacerbation:</strong>20 mg twice daily (after breakfast and supper or before bedtime) or 40 mg once daily at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance therapy:</strong> 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Powder for Suspension (Pediatric Patients):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD):</strong>&lt;1 year of age: 0.5 mg/kg/dose once daily up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Age 3 to 11 months: 0.5 mg/kg/dose twice daily up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Age 1 to 2 months: 0.5 mg/kg/dose once daily up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Neonates: 0.5 mg/kg/dose, maximum once daily up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\"><strong>Pediatric Patients (1-16 years):</strong>GERD: 1 mg/kg/day divided twice daily up to 40 mg b.i.d.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Duodenal ulcer, Peptic ulcer: 0.5 mg/kg/day, divided up to 40 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Maintenance therapy: 40 mg at night</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Reflux esophagitis: 2 mg/kg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Zollinger-Ellison syndrome: 40 mg three times daily</li><li><strong>Intravenous Injection (Adults):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For patients with pathological hypersecretory conditions or intractable ulcers, Famomax 20 mg Injection is administered 20 mg intravenously every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For pediatric patients (1-16 years), the starting dose is 0.25 mg/kg intravenously every 12 hours, up to 40 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Famomax 20 mg</strong></p><p>No clinically important drug interactions have been identified. Famomax 20 mg does not interact with the cytochrome P450 drug-metabolizing enzyme system.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Known hypersensitivity to any component of the drug.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Famomax 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Eruption</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Tachycardia</li><li>High blood pressure</li><li>Headache</li><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Insomnia (rare)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Famomax 20 mg should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed. It is known to cross the placenta.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is not known whether Famomax 20 mg is secreted into human milk. Nursing mothers should either stop nursing or discontinue the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The drug should be used in the minimum required amount depending on the disease conditions. Careful administration is needed for elderly patients, as well as those with renal failure and hepatic disorders.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Famomax 20 mg is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist. It completely inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors of parietal cells, inhibiting gastric acid secretion. The antagonist activity is slowly reversible as the drug dissociates slowly from the H2-receptor.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy Category</strong></p><p>B. There are no adequate, well-controlled studies in pregnant women, so this drug should be used only if clearly needed. Caution should be exercised when administering Famomax 20 mg to nursing women.</p>', 'active', '4_1742582792.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:46:32', '2025-05-02 17:26:37'),
(4029, 'Deslor', 'Desloratadine', 30.09, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '2.5 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Deslor 60 ml</strong></p><p>Deslor 60 ml is indicated for the relief of symptoms associated with seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis, such as sneezing, nasal discharge and itching, congestion or stuffiness, ocular itching, tearing and redness, itching of the palate, and coughing. Deslor 60 ml is also indicated for the relief of symptoms associated with chronic idiopathic urticaria, such as relief from itching and reduction in the size and number of hives.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Deslor 60 ml is a long-acting, tricyclic, non-sedating, selective peripheral histamine H1-receptor antagonist that inhibits the release of pro-inflammatory mediators from human mast cells and basophils.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Deslor 60 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Drops:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child 6-11 months of age: 2 ml drops once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child 1-2 years of age: 2.5 ml drops once daily</li><li><strong>Syrup:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child 6-11 months of age: 2 ml once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child 1-5 years of age: 2.5 ml once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child 6-11 years of age: 5 ml once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults &amp; &gt; 12 years of age: 10 ml once daily</li><li><strong>Tablet:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults and children 12 years of age and over: 5 mg daily</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Deslor 60 ml</strong></p><p>No clinically relevant drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Deslor 60 ml is contraindicated in patients who have hypersensitivity to this medication or any of its ingredients or Loratadine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Deslor 60 ml</strong></p><p>Less common side effects may include headache, nausea, fatigue, dizziness, pharyngitis, dyspepsia, and myalgia.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, Deslor 60 ml should be used in pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Deslor 60 ml passes into breast milk. Therefore, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or to discontinue Deslor 60 ml, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In adult patients with liver or renal impairment, a starting dose of one 5 mg tablet every other day is recommended based on pharmacokinetic data. If the patient has a medical or familial history of seizures, caution is advised.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Deslor 60 ml</strong></p><p>No clinically relevant adverse effects have been reported from overdose.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light. Store below 30°C. Keep all medicines out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> The safety and effectiveness of Deslor 60 ml in pediatric patients under 2 years of age have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Dose selection for elderly patients should be cautious, reflecting the greater frequency of decreased hepatic, renal, or cardiac function, and of concomitant disease or other drug therapy.</li><li><strong>Liver or Renal Impairment:</strong> A starting dose of one 5 mg tablet every other day is recommended based on pharmacokinetic data.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Deslor 60 ml, the major active metabolite of Loratadine, is a non-sedating, long-acting tricyclic histamine antagonist with selective H1-receptor histamine antagonist activity. It also inhibits histamine release from human mast cells.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Category C. The safe use of Deslor 60 ml during pregnancy has not been established. Therefore, Deslor 60 ml should not be used during pregnancy unless clearly indicated. Deslor 60 ml passes into breast milk, so a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue Deslor 60 ml, considering the drug\'s importance to the mother.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Deslor 60 ml is recommended for use in children and adolescents, but the safety in children under 2 years of age has not been established.</p>', 'active', '1300_1742582923.png', 43, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:48:43', '2025-05-01 20:24:51'),
(4030, 'Othera', 'Esomeprazole', 17.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Othera 40 mg</strong></p><p>Othera 40 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Treatment of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</li><li>Healing of erosive esophagitis</li><li>Maintenance of healing of erosive esophagitis</li><li>Symptomatic Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</li><li>Reduction of NSAID-associated gastric ulcer</li><li>H. pylori eradication (Triple therapy)</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Othera 40 mg exerts its stomach acid-suppressing effects by preventing the final step in gastric acid production. It covalently binds to sulfhydryl groups of cysteines found on the H+/K+-ATPase enzyme at the secretory surface of gastric parietal cells. This binding leads to the inhibition of both basal and stimulated gastric acid secretion, regardless of the stimulus. Since the binding of Othera 40 mg to the enzyme is irreversible, new enzyme needs to be expressed to resume acid secretion, which leads to a duration of antisecretory effects lasting longer than 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Othera 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet or CapsuleHealing of erosive esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4 to 8 weeks. Most patients heal within 4 to 8 weeks. If healing does not occur after 4-8 weeks, an additional 4-8 weeks treatment may be considered.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance of healing of erosive esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily. Controlled studies did not extend beyond six months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely after 4 weeks, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>H. pylori eradication (Triple therapy)</strong>: Othera 40 mg 20 mg twice daily for 10 days, Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, and Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>InjectionGERD with erosive esophagitis</strong>: 20 or 40 mg given once daily via intravenous injection (no less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Othera 40 mg</strong></p><p>For intravenous infusion, prepare the solution by reconstituting one vial with 5 ml of 0.9% Sodium Chloride, then dilute to a final volume of 50 ml. The resultant concentration after dilution is 0.8 mg/ml.</p><ul><li><strong>20 mg dose</strong>: Withdraw 25 ml of the final solution and administer as an intravenous infusion over 10 to 30 minutes.</li><li><strong>10 mg dose</strong>: Withdraw 12.5 ml of the final solution and administer as an intravenous infusion over 10 to 30 minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Othera 40 mg</strong></p><p>Othera 40 mg is a selective inhibitor of the cytochrome P450 mono-oxygenase system, which may affect hepatic clearance. However, no clinically relevant interactions have been reported. The reduction in gastric acid could influence bacterial colonization of the stomach and duodenum, as well as vitamin B12 absorption. There is some uncertainty about the effect of Othera 40 mg on the oral combined contraceptive pill.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Othera 40 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Othera 40 mg</strong></p><p>Side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Abdominal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Othera 40 mg has a US FDA pregnancy category of C. It should be avoided during pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Exclude the possibility of malignancy when gastric ulcers are suspected and before treating dyspepsia.</li><li>When used in combination with antibiotics, refer to the prescribing information of the respective antibiotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p>To reconstitute the solution for injection, add 5 ml of 0.9% Sodium Chloride for intravenous injection into the vial containing the dry powder. The reconstituted solution should be clear and colorless to very slightly yellow.</p>', 'active', '1563_1742583016.jpg', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:50:16', '2025-05-04 01:39:15'),
(4031, 'Zerocal', 'Sucralose', 1.40, 2, 'x inactive', '100 tablet in a box', 'Tablet', '6.5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1236_1742583185.png', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:53:05', '2025-05-07 20:32:54'),
(4032, 'Becoral Dx', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Becoral Dx</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment and prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, and disorders of osteogenesis. It is also used as a supplement in cases of inadequate calcium intake in childhood diets, rickets, pregnancy, lactation, and in elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis and as a phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg (from Coral source), equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p>Alternatively:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg (from Coral source), equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 200 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate calcium in the blood is essential for a wide variety of bodily functions. Clinical evidence suggests that calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is also essential for healthy bones, as it aids in calcium absorption from the gastrointestinal tract and stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies show that calcium and vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth as well as in osteoporosis and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Becoral Dx</strong></p><p>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night, or as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Becoral Dx</strong></p><p>One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water, or as directed by the physician. Taking with a full stomach ensures better absorption.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Becoral Dx</strong></p><ul><li>Oral calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline and fluoride preparations, and at least 3 hours should be allowed between ingestion of these medications.</li><li>Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of calcium.</li><li>Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce metabolism of vitamin D3.</li><li>Certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of calcium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the components</li><li>Hypocalcemia resulting from overdose of vitamin D3</li><li>Hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Bone metastases</li><li>Severe renal insufficiency</li><li>Severe hypercalciuria</li><li>Renal calculi</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Becoral Dx</strong></p><p>Flatulence, diarrhea, constipation, and upper GI discomfort are rare. Hypercalcemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It should be used as directed by the physician during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with dose reduction may be needed.</li><li>Plasma and serum calcium levels should be monitored in mild to moderate renal impairment, especially with long-term use.</li><li>Patients with renal stones or a history of them should take precautions.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Becoral Dx</strong></p><p>At high doses, it may result in nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc. Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature below 30°C, protect from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This is a calcium and vitamin D3 preparation where calcium carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The calcium carbonate from coral has a chemical structure that is very similar to human bone. Coral calcium ensures better absorption than other sources. Vitamin D3 aids in calcium absorption from the GI tract and helps maintain calcium balance in the body.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of a physician.</p>', 'active', '1247_1742583285.webp', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:54:45', '2025-05-03 00:29:03'),
(4033, 'Montene', 'Montelukast Sodium', 9.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information in a more structured format with headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Montene 5 mg</strong></p><p>Montene 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. Cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) are located in the human airway, and these eicosanoids bind to them. The orally active drug Montene 5 mg has a strong affinity and specificity for the CysLT1 receptor. Without any agonist activity, it inhibits LTD4\'s physiological effects at the CysLT1 receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Montene 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis</strong>:</li><li>The dosage for adults and adolescents 15 years of age and older: 10 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6 to 14 years of age: 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 to 5 years of age: 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6 months to 5 years of age: 4 mg oral granules once daily. This can be administered either directly in the mouth or mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Pediatric Patients</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Montene have been established in adequate and well-controlled studies in pediatric patients with asthma 6 months to 14 years of age. Safety and efficacy profiles in this age group are similar to those seen in adults.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is required in patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is recommended in patients with renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Elderly Use</strong></p><p>The pharmacokinetic profile and the oral bioavailability of a single 10-mg oral dose of Montene are similar in elderly and younger adults. The plasma half-life of Montene is slightly longer in the elderly. No dosage adjustment in the elderly is required.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Montene 5 mg</strong></p><p>Montene 5 mg has been provided alongside other commonly used treatments for asthma without significant side effects. Some common drugs like theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives, terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin did not show any clinically significant effects on the pharmacokinetics at the recommended clinical dose. However, co-administration with hepatic metabolism-inducing drugs such as phenobarbital may reduce Montene’s AUC by about 40%. It’s important to monitor clinically when used with potent cytochrome P450 enzyme inducers like phenobarbital or rifampin.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those with extreme sensitivity to any ingredient in this medication should not take it.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Montene 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, asthenia, aberrant behavior, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, hemorrhage, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, edema, seizure, odd feelings, and sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, decreased attention, disorientation, erythema nodosum, hallucinations, hepatic problems, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, and tremor.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Classified as pregnancy category B. The drug has been shown to cross the placenta in rats and rabbits, but there are no adequate studies in pregnant humans. It should be used only if necessary during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is known to be secreted in breast milk, and caution should be exercised when administered to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Montene 5 mg is not recommended for the reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks, including status asthmaticus.</li><li>Asthma exacerbations that are acute should be treated with proper rescue medication.</li><li>Do not suddenly replace inhaled or oral corticosteroids with Montene without medical supervision.</li><li>Patients with aspirin sensitivity should avoid aspirin or NSAIDs while using Montene.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Montene 5 mg</strong></p><p>In the case of overdose, common side effects include nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain. Supportive measures such as removing unabsorbed material from the digestive tract may be used.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry environment, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Montene 5 mg is a selective, orally active leukotriene receptor antagonist that inhibits the cysteinyl leukotriene receptor (CysLT1), blocking the physiological effects of LTD4 on this receptor.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy Considerations</strong></p><ul><li>Montene 5 mg crosses the placenta in animal studies (rats and rabbits). There is no controlled research in pregnant humans. It should be used only if absolutely necessary during pregnancy.</li></ul><p>This revised format includes the requested headings and highlights key information.</p>', 'active', '1804_1742583368.png', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:56:08', '2025-05-04 00:07:47'),
(4034, 'Loratin', 'Loratadine', 3.01, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Loratin 10 mg</strong></p><p>Loratin 10 mg tablet provides fast, effective relief from the symptoms of seasonal allergic rhinitis, perennial allergic rhinitis, and skin allergies, including chronic urticaria. It is also effective in alleviating symptoms of allergic rhinitis such as sneezing, nasal discharge, itching, ocular itching, and burning. Nasal and ocular symptoms are relieved rapidly after oral administration. Loratin 10 mg tablet is also indicated for idiopathic urticaria.</p><p>In children over 2 years, Loratin 10 mg tablet is indicated for the symptomatic relief of seasonal allergic rhinitis and allergic skin conditions such as urticaria (nettlerash).</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Loratin 10 mg is a tricyclic antihistamine with selective peripheral histamine H1-receptor antagonistic activity. It is devoid of anticholinergic activity. It is rapidly effective and long-lasting, allowing for once-a-day administration.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Loratin 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children (6 years and older):</strong> 10 mg daily (1 tablet or 2 teaspoonfuls of syrup) once daily.</li><li><strong>Children (2 to 5 years):</strong> 5 mg (1 teaspoonful of syrup) once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6 years and older):Body weight over 30 kg:</strong> One Loratin 10 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Body weight below 30 kg:</strong> Half Loratin 10 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Below 2 years:</strong> Not recommended as safety and efficacy have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Loratin 10 mg</strong></p><p>There are no reports of potentially hazardous interactions with other drugs. Loratin 10 mg has no potentiating effects when administered with alcohol, as measured by psychomotor performance studies. Concomitant therapy with drugs that inhibit or are metabolized by hepatic cytochromes P450 3A4 and 2D6 may elevate plasma concentrations of either drug, possibly leading to adverse effects. Cimetidine, erythromycin, and ketoconazole can increase Loratin 10 mg serum concentrations, but no adverse effects have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Loratin 10 mg is contraindicated in patients who have shown hypersensitivity or idiosyncrasy to any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Loratin 10 mg</strong></p><p>The incidence of adverse events, including sedation and anticholinergic effects, observed with 10 mg Loratin was comparable to placebo. Studies on driving performance, cognitive, and psychomotor functioning show it to be comparable to placebo.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>US FDA Pregnancy Category: B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Loratin should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Loratin 10 mg is excreted in human milk in small amounts. Nursing mothers should exercise caution when using Loratin.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be taken in patients with liver impairment or renal insufficiency (eGFR &lt;30 ml/min).</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Loratin 10 mg</strong></p><p>In adults, symptoms of overdose (greater than 10 mg) may include somnolence, tachycardia, and headache. In children, symptoms may include extrapyramidal signs and palpitations with overdoses greater than 10 mg. In case of overdose, general symptomatic and supportive measures should be initiated immediately. Early treatment with activated charcoal may be considered, or gastric lavage may be performed.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Loratin 10 mg is a long-acting, tricyclic antihistamine with selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonistic activity and no central sedative or anticholinergic effects. It provides rapid relief and has a long-lasting effect, allowing once-daily administration.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There is no experience with Loratin 10 mg during human pregnancy, so its use is not advised during pregnancy. Loratin 10 mg is excreted in breast milk in small amounts. Therefore, nursing mothers are advised not to take the drug.</p>', 'active', '927_1742583459.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:57:39', '2025-05-03 23:31:46'),
(4035, 'Kalcoral-D', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 11.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Kalcoral-D</strong></p><p>Kalcoral-D is indicated for the treatment and prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, and disorders of osteogenesis. It is also used as a supplement in cases of inadequate calcium intake in childhood diets, rickets, pregnancy, lactation, and in elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis and as a phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg</strong> (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin D3 400 IU</strong> as Cholecalciferol</li><li>or</li><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg</strong> (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin D3 200 IU</strong> as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate calcium in the blood is essential for a wide variety of bodily functions. The body will not tolerate a deficiency, even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is crucial for healthy bones as it aids calcium absorption from the gastrointestinal tract and stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies also show that calcium and vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth, as well as in osteoporosis and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Kalcoral-D</strong></p><p>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night, or as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Kalcoral-D</strong></p><p>One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water, or as directed by the physician. Taking the tablet with a full stomach ensures better absorption.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Kalcoral-D</strong></p><ul><li>Oral calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline and fluoride preparations. A minimum of 3 hours should be allowed between the ingestion of these medications.</li><li>Thiazide diuretics reduce renal excretion of calcium.</li><li>Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce the metabolism of Vitamin D3.</li><li>Concomitant ingestion of certain foods, like spinach, cereals, and dairy products, may reduce calcium absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the components</li><li>Hypocalcaemia resulting from an overdose of Vitamin D3</li><li>Hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Bone metastases</li><li>Severe renal insufficiency</li><li>Severe hypercalciuria</li><li>Renal calculi</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Kalcoral-D</strong></p><p>Rare manifestations include flatulence, diarrhea, constipation, and upper GI discomfort. Hypercalcaemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Kalcoral-D should be used as directed by a physician during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring and dose reduction may be necessary.</li><li>Plasma and serum calcium levels should be monitored in cases of mild to moderate renal impairment or with long-term use.</li><li>Patients with renal stones or a history of them should also take precautions.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Kalcoral-D</strong></p><p>At high doses, Kalcoral-D may cause nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, and irritability. Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature below 30°C. Protect from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Kalcoral-D is a calcium and vitamin D3 preparation, where calcium carbonate is sourced from coral. The calcium carbonate from coral has a chemical structure similar to human bone, ensuring better absorption compared to other sources. Vitamin D3 aids in calcium absorption from the gastrointestinal tract and helps maintain calcium balance in the body.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Kalcoral-D can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of a physician.</p>', 'active', '241_1742583557.png', 17, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-21 23:59:17', '2025-05-03 23:04:00'),
(4036, 'Dinovo', 'Naproxen Sodium + Esomeprazole Magnesium', 15.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Dinovo 500 mg + 20 mg</h3><p>It is indicated for the relief of signs and symptoms of osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis, and to decrease the risk of developing gastric ulcers in patients at risk of developing NSAID-associated gastric ulcers.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Dinovo 500 mg + 20 mg is a preparation of <strong>Naproxen</strong> and <strong>Esomeprazole</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>Naproxen</strong> is a NSAID with analgesic and antipyretic activities. It inhibits prostaglandin synthesis.</li><li><strong>Esomeprazole</strong> is a proton pump inhibitor that suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the H+/K+ ATPase in the gastric parietal cell. By acting specifically on the proton pump, Esomeprazole blocks the final step in acid production, thus reducing gastric acidity.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Dinovo 500 mg + 20 mg</h3><p>The recommended dose is <strong>one tablet twice daily</strong>.</p><ul><li>Do not split, chew, crush, or dissolve the tablet.</li><li>The tablet is to be taken at least 30 minutes before meals.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Dinovo 500 mg + 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>ACE Inhibitors, diuretics, and beta-blockers:</strong> May reduce the antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin:</strong> Increases the risk of nephrotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Lithium:</strong> Increases plasma levels of lithium.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate:</strong> May increase the serum levels of methotrexate.</li><li><strong>Warfarin:</strong> May increase the risk of bleeding complications.</li><li><strong>Digoxin:</strong> May increase digoxin toxicity.</li><li><strong>Clopidogrel:</strong> May decrease the exposure to the active metabolite of clopidogrel.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any components of this product.</li><li>Also contraindicated in patients who have experienced asthma, urticaria, or allergic-type reactions after taking aspirin or other NSAIDs.</li><li>Contraindicated for the treatment of per-operative pain in the setting of coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Dinovo 500 mg + 20 mg</h3><ul><li>The most common side effects include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gastritis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Erosive gastritis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dyspepsia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diarrhea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gastric ulcer</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Upper abdominal pain</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>The use of this tablet should be avoided during pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><p>Caution should be exercised in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Fatal cardiovascular thrombotic events (e.g., myocardial infarction, stroke, hypertension, congestive heart failure, and edema)</li><li>Serious gastrointestinal (GI) adverse events</li><li>Hypomagnesaemia</li><li>Renal or hepatic insufficiency</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul>', 'active', '1415_1742583657.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:00:57', '2025-05-01 21:00:19'),
(4037, 'Flamyd', 'Metronidazole', 2.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Flamyd 500 mg:</strong></p><p>Flamyd 500 mg is indicated for the treatment and prevention of various conditions, including:</p><ul><li>Prevention of post-operative infections due to anaerobic bacteria, particularly species of Bacteroides and anaerobic streptococci.</li><li>Treatment of:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Septicaemia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Bacteraemia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Peritonitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Brain abscess</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pelvic abscess</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pelvic cellulitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Post-operative wound infections caused by anaerobes</li><li>Treatment of urogenital trichomoniasis.</li><li>Treatment of bacterial vaginosis (non-specific vaginitis).</li><li>Treatment of all forms of amoebiasis (intestinal, extra-intestinal, and symptomless cyst carriers).</li><li>Treatment of giardiasis.</li><li>Treatment of acute ulcerative gingivitis.</li><li>Treatment of anaerobically infected leg ulcers and pressure sores.</li><li>Treatment of acute dental infections caused by anaerobic organisms.</li><li>Treatment of antibiotic-associated pseudomembranous colitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Amoebicides, Anti-diarrhoeal Antiprotozoal</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Flamyd 500 mg belongs to the imidazole class of antibacterial drugs and is classified as an antiprotozoal agent. The 5-nitro group of Flamyd 500 mg is reduced by anaerobes, and the reduced form interacts with DNA, exhibiting bactericidal action.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet and Suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Trichomoniasis</em> (Adults &amp; Children over 10 years): 200 mg tid or 400 mg bid for 7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Intestinal Amoebiasis</em> (Adults &amp; Children over 10 years): 800 mg tid for 5 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Giardiasis</em> (Adults &amp; Children over 10 years): 2 gm once daily for 3 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Acute Ulcerative Gingivitis</em> (Adults &amp; Children over 10 years): 200 mg tid for 3 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Bacterial Vaginosis</em> (Adults &amp; Children over 10 years): 400 mg bid for 7 days or 2 gm as a single dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Anaerobic Infections</em> (Adults &amp; Children over 10 years): 800 mg initially, followed by 400 mg tid for 7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Surgical Prophylaxis</em> (Adults &amp; Children over 10 years): 400 mg tid started 24 hours before surgery for 1 day.</li><li><strong>Vaginal Gel</strong>:</li><li>One applicator full of Flamyd 500 mg gel (approximately 5 grams containing 37.5 mg of Flamyd) intravaginally once or twice a day for 5 days.</li><li><strong>Suppository</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Anaerobic Infections</em>: 1 gm every 8 hours for 3 days, then every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Surgical Prophylaxis</em>: 1 gm 2 hours before surgery, with up to 3 further doses every 8 hours for high-risk procedures.</li><li><strong>IV Infusion</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults and children over 12 years: Infuse 500 mg every 8 hours at a rate of 5 ml/minute (max 4 gm per 24 hours).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children under 12 years: 7.5 mg/kg every 8 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><em>Disulfiram</em>: Psychotic reactions may occur with concurrent use.</li><li><em>Alcohol</em>: Alcoholic beverages should be avoided during therapy and for 1 day afterward due to the risk of a disulfiram-like reaction.</li><li><em>Warfarin (oral anticoagulants)</em>: May increase bleeding risk, monitor prothrombin time closely.</li><li><em>Lithium</em>: Plasma levels may be increased.</li><li><em>Cyclosporin</em>: Serum cyclosporin and creatinine should be monitored.</li><li><em>Phenytoin/Phenobarbital</em>: May reduce Flamyd levels.</li><li><em>5-Fluorouracil</em>: Increased toxicity of 5-fluorouracil.</li><li><em>Busulfan</em>: May increase busulfan toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Flamyd 500 mg or other Nitroimidazole derivatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Metallic taste, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, drowsiness, and rashes may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><em>Pregnancy</em>: Category B. Should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><em>Lactation</em>: Excreted in human milk. Caution is advised when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Regular hematological tests are recommended if treatment duration exceeds usual duration.</li><li>Monitor for symptoms of neuropathy in patients with prolonged use.</li><li>Exercise caution in patients with hepatic encephalopathy.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose include vomiting, ataxia, and slight disorientation. There is no specific antidote, and treatment is supportive.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light, and keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Flamyd 500 mg is metabolized by anaerobes, and the reduced form interacts with DNA, leading to bactericidal effects.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses:</strong></p><ul><li>In cases of hepatic impairment, dosage should be reduced, and treatment monitored.</li><li>In renal failure, no dosage reduction is needed, but dialysis patients should receive a dose after hemodialysis.</li></ul>', 'active', '1093_1742583785.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:03:05', '2025-05-03 08:02:50'),
(4038, 'Aristovit M', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral', 2.00, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '0 mg/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1292_1742583908.webp', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:05:08', '2025-05-02 23:54:21');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4039, 'MaxD', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 2.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '1000 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of MaxD 1000 IU</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated in the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also indicated as an adjunct to specific therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Colecalciferol, a synthetic form of Vitamin D, is essential for normal bone growth and development and for maintaining bone density. It also aids in the utilization of both calcium and phosphorus. Babies need Vitamin D3 for healthy growth and development. It functions as a hormone in the body.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin-D preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) assists with the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is essential for normal bone growth and to maintain bone density. Vitamin D3 also helps reduce the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, prevents the risk of certain cancers (breast, colorectal), and helps maintain adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of MaxD 1000 IU</strong></p><p><strong>For Capsules:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 Deficiency:</strong> 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks. Maintenance therapy is 1,400-2,000 IU/day. To confirm the target level of 25-hydroxyvitamin D, it should be measured 3-4 months after initiating maintenance therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 Deficiency:</strong> 20,000 IU every 4 weeks. Higher doses may be required in certain situations.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adjunct to Therapy for Osteoporosis:</strong> 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li><strong>Children (12-18 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 Deficiency:</strong> 20,000 IU once every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 Deficiency:</strong> 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>For Film-Coated Tablets:</strong></p><ul><li>1,000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily, or as directed by the physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li></ul><p><strong>For Oroflash or Chewable Tablets:</strong></p><ul><li>1,000 IU to 2,000 IU daily, or as directed by the physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal. Chew the tablet before swallowing.</li></ul><p><strong>For Syrup:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For patients at risk of Vitamin D deficiency:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0-1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml)</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml)</blockquote><ul><li><strong>For Vitamin D deficient patients:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0-1 year: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 years: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks</li></ul><p><strong>For Injection:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prevention:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Infants receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e. 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nursed infants or infants not receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk, or young children up to 5 years of age: 1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e. 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adolescents: 1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e. 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e. 100,000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly:</strong> 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e. 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li><strong>For Vitamin D Deficiency:</strong></li><li>1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e. 200,000 IU, which can be repeated every 1 to 6 months or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of MaxD 1000 IU</strong></p><p>It interferes with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (e.g., liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of MaxD 1000 IU</strong></p><p>The general side effects include hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, and abdominal pain.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Studies have shown that doses up to 4,000 IU during pregnancy are safe. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU; however, women who are considered Vitamin D3 deficient may need a higher dose. During pregnancy, women should follow the advice of their medical practitioner as their needs may vary depending on the severity of their condition and their response to treatment. Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. There has been no observed overdose in infants induced by nursing mothers, but when prescribing additional Vitamin D3 to a breastfed child, the physician should consider the dose of any Vitamin D3 given to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>MaxD should be used with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of MaxD 1000 IU</strong></p><p>Overdose can lead to hypervitaminosis D.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years of age have not been established.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) assists with the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is essential for normal bone growth and maintaining bone density. It also reduces the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, prevents certain cancers (breast, colorectal), and helps maintain adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Studies have shown that doses up to 4,000 IU during pregnancy are safe. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU, but higher doses may be required in those with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Women should consult their physician for the appropriate dosage.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years of age have not been established.</p>', 'active', '1124_1742583996.jpg', 27, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:06:36', '2025-05-03 23:53:19'),
(4040, 'Pregcare', 'L-arginie + Proanthocyanidin + Folic Acid + Zinc', 60.00, 25, 'x 5 gm sachet', '5 gm sachet', 'Oral Powder', '(3 gm+75 mg+22.5 mg+5 mg)/5 gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '98_1742584350.webp', 50, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:12:30', '2025-05-04 10:28:09'),
(4041, 'Tebast', 'Ebastine', 6.02, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information with the heading in normal bold text as requested:</p><p><strong>Indications of Tebast 10 mg</strong></p><p>Tebast 10 mg is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of:</p><ul><li>Seasonal and Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li><li>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Tebast 10 mg, a piperidine derivative, is a long-acting, nonsedating, second-generation histamine receptor antagonist that binds preferentially to peripheral H1 receptors. It is metabolised to active metabolite, carTebast 10 mg. It has antihistaminic, antiallergic activity and prevents histamine-induced bronchoconstriction. It does not have significant sedative or antimuscarinic actions.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Tebast 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet:</strong>Adults (more than 12 years of age): 10 mg (one tablet) once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (6-12 years of age): 5 mg (half tablet) once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Syrup:</strong>Children (2-5 years of age): 2.5 ml once daily (up to 5 ml in severe cases such as Perennial Allergic Rhinitis)</li><li>Children (6-12 years of age): 5 ml once daily (up to 10 ml in severe cases such as Perennial Allergic Rhinitis)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tebast 10 mg may be taken with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Tebast 10 mg</strong></p><p>Tebast 10 mg may be taken with or without food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Tebast 10 mg</strong></p><p>Tebast 10 mg in combination with either ketoconazole or erythromycin increases plasma levels of Tebast 10 mg and prolongs the QTc interval. Tebast 10 mg does not interact with the pharmacokinetics of theophylline, warfarin, cimetidine, diazepam, or alcohol. The sedation effect of alcohol and diazepam may be enhanced.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Patients with a known hypersensitivity to Tebast 10 mg or any of its ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Tebast 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>The most common side effects are headache, dry mouth, and drowsiness.</li><li>Less commonly reported side effects include abdominal pain, dyspepsia, nausea, and insomnia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of Tebast 10 mg during pregnancy and lactation has not been established.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>It is advisable to exercise caution when using Tebast 10 mg in patients known to have the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Long QT syndrome</li><li>Hypokalemia</li><li>Treatment with any medicine known to produce an increase in QT interval or inhibit CYP3A4 enzyme systems, such as azole antifungals and macrolide antibiotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Pregnancy and Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of Tebast 10 mg during pregnancy and lactation has not been established. Tebast 10 mg should not be used during pregnancy unless clearly needed. It is not known whether Tebast 10 mg is excreted in milk, therefore, Tebast 10 mg should not be used during lactation.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Tebast 10 mg</strong></p><p>No clinically meaningful signs or symptoms were observed up to 100 mg given once daily. There is no specific antidote for Tebast 10 mg. In case of accidental overdoses, gastric lavage, monitoring of vital functions including ECG, and symptomatic treatment should be carried out.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C at a cool and dry place, away from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Tebast 10 mg is a long-acting and selective H1-histamine receptor antagonist. After repeated administration, inhibition of peripheral receptors remains at a constant level. Tebast 10 mg is rapidly absorbed and undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism following oral administration. Tebast 10 mg is almost totally converted to the pharmacologically active acid metabolite, carTebast 10 mg.</p>', 'active', '1311_1742584524.jpg', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:15:24', '2025-05-04 22:18:18'),
(4042, 'Filmet', 'Metronidazole', 35.00, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '200 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Filmet 60 ml</strong></p><p>Filmet 60 ml is indicated for the treatment of the following conditions:</p><ol><li><strong>Prevention of post-operative infections</strong> due to anaerobic bacteria, particularly species of <em>Bacteroides</em> and anaerobic streptococci.</li><li><strong>Treatment of infections</strong> such as:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Septicaemia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Bacteraemia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Peritonitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Brain abscess</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pelvic abscess</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pelvic cellulitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Post-operative wound infections caused by anaerobes</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Treatment of urogenital trichomoniasis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Bacterial vaginosis</strong> (non-specific vaginitis).</li><li><strong>All forms of amoebiasis</strong>, including intestinal, extra-intestinal disease, and symptomless cyst carriers.</li><li><strong>Giardiasis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Acute ulcerative gingivitis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Anaerobically infected leg ulcers and pressure sores</strong>.</li><li><strong>Acute dental infections</strong> due to anaerobic organisms.</li><li><strong>Antibiotic-associated pseudomembranous colitis</strong>.</li></ol><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Amoebicides</li><li>Anti-diarrhoeal</li><li>Antiprotozoal</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Filmet 60 ml belongs to the imidazole class of antibacterial drugs and is classified as an antiprotozoal agent. The 5-nitro group of Filmet 60 ml is reduced by anaerobes metabolically. Studies have shown that the reduced form interacts with DNA, resulting in the bactericidal action of Filmet 60 ml.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Filmet 60 ml</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Tablet and Suspension</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li><strong>Trichomoniasis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adults &amp; children &gt;10 years: 200 mg TID or 400 mg BID for 7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 7-10 years: 100 mg TID.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 3-7 years: 100 mg BID.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 years: 50 mg TID.</li><li><strong>Intestinal Amoebiasis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adults &amp; children &gt;10 years: 800 mg TID for 5 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 7-10 years: 400 mg TID.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 3-7 years: 200 mg QID.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 years: 200 mg TID.</li><li><strong>Extra-intestinal &amp; Asymptomatic Amoebiasis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adults &amp; children &gt;10 years: 400-800 mg TID for 5-10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 7-10 years: 200-400 mg TID.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 3-7 years: 100-200 mg QID.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 years: 100-200 mg TID.</li><li><strong>Giardiasis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adults &amp; children &gt;10 years: 2 gm once daily for 3 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 7-10 years: 1 gm once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 3-7 years: 600-800 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 years: 500 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Acute Ulcerative Gingivitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adults &amp; children &gt;10 years: 200 mg TID for 3 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 7-10 years: 100 mg TID.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 3-7 years: 100 mg BID.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 years: 50 mg TID.</li><li><strong>Acute Dental Infections</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults &amp; children &gt;10 years: 200 mg TID for 3-7 days.</li><li><strong>Bacterial Vaginosis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults &amp; children &gt;10 years: 400 mg BID for 7 days or 2 gm as a single dose.</li><li><strong>Leg Ulcers and Pressure Sores</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults &amp; children &gt;10 years: 400 mg TID for 7 days.</li><li><strong>Anaerobic Infections</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adults &amp; children &gt;10 years: 800 mg initially, then 400 mg TID for 7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-10 years: 7.5 mg/kg TID.</li><li><strong>Surgical Prophylaxis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adults &amp; children &gt;10 years: 400 mg TID starting 24 hours before surgery for 1 day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-10 years: 7.5 mg/kg TID.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Vaginal Gel</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommended dose: one applicator full of Filmet 60 ml gel (approximately 5 grams containing approximately 37.5 mg of Filmet 60 ml) intravaginally once or twice a day for 5 days.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Suppository</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li><strong>Anaerobic Infections</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adults: 1 gm every 8 hours for 3 days, then 1 gm every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 5-10 years: 500 mg every 8 hours for 3 days, then every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children &gt;10 years: Adult dose.</li><li><strong>Surgical Prophylaxis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adults: 1 gm 2 hours before surgery, up to 3 further doses of 1 gm every 8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 5-10 years: 500 mg 2 hours before surgery, up to 3 further doses of 500 mg every 8 hours.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>IV Infusion</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; children &gt;12 years</strong>: Infuse 500 mg 8 hourly at a rate of 5 ml/min. Maximum 4 gm in 24 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children &lt;12 years</strong>: 7.5 mg/kg body weight/day every 8 hours at a rate of 5 ml/min.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Filmet 60 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Disulfiram</strong>: Psychotic reactions may occur when used concurrently with Filmet 60 ml.</li><li><strong>Alcohol</strong>: Alcoholic beverages and alcohol-containing drugs should be avoided during therapy and for at least one day after, due to the possibility of a disulfiram-like reaction.</li><li><strong>Oral Anticoagulants (Warfarin)</strong>: Filmet 60 ml may potentiate anticoagulant effects, increasing hemorrhagic risk.</li><li><strong>Lithium</strong>: Filmet 60 ml may increase plasma lithium levels.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin</strong>: Serum cyclosporine and creatinine levels should be closely monitored when co-administered.</li><li><strong>Phenytoin/Phenobarbital</strong>: Increased elimination of Filmet 60 ml, resulting in reduced plasma levels.</li><li><strong>5-Fluorouracil</strong>: Reduced clearance, increasing toxicity of 5-fluorouracil.</li><li><strong>Busulfan</strong>: Plasma levels of busulfan may be increased, leading to toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Filmet 60 ml is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Filmet 60 ml or other Nitroimidazole derivatives.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Metallic taste</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Diarrhoea</li><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Rashes</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Filmet 60 ml is classified under <strong>US FDA Pregnancy Category B</strong>. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women, so it should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Filmet 60 ml is excreted in human milk, so caution should be exercised when administering it to nursing women.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Regular monitoring of hematological parameters is advised if Filmet 60 ml is administered for an extended period.</li><li>Caution is required for patients with hepatic encephalopathy.</li><li>Patients should be informed that Filmet 60 ml may darken urine.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Vomiting, ataxia, and slight disorientation.</li><li>Treatment: No specific antidote. Symptomatic and supportive treatment should be administered.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C.</li><li>Protect from light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Do not use after the expiry date.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Amoebicides</li><li>Anti-diarrhoeal</li><li>Antiprotozoal</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Filmet 60 ml belongs to the imidazole class and acts by interacting with DNA in anaerobic organisms, exerting bactericidal effects.</p>', 'active', '637_1742584687.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:18:07', '2025-05-03 10:38:04'),
(4043, 'Axodin', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '120 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information about <strong>Axodin 120 mg</strong> (Fexofenadine) in a concise format with the key headings in bold text:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Axodin 120 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Allergic Rhinitis:</strong></li><li>For adults and children aged 12 years and older, Axodin 120 mg is used to alleviate symptoms of seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis such as sneezing, rhinorrhea, lacrimation, itchy, red eyes, and itchy nose, palate, and throat. It improves work/activity productivity and quality of life.</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria:</strong></li><li>Fexofenadine is recommended for adults and children aged 12 years and older for treating chronic idiopathic urticaria symptoms. It reduces wheals and pruritus.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></li><li>Axodin 120 mg is a selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist. It prevents the activation of H1 receptors, reducing allergic symptoms. It doesn\'t cross the blood-brain barrier, so it doesn’t cause sedation.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Axodin 120 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Seasonal Allergic Rhinitis:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults and children 12 years and older: 60 mg twice daily or 120 mg once daily or 180 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 6-11 years: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 2-11 years (suspension): 30 mg or 5 ml twice daily.</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults and children 12 years and older: 60 mg twice daily or 120 mg once daily or 180 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 6-11 years: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 6 months-2 years (suspension): 15 mg or 2.5 ml (1/2 tsp) twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Axodin 120 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Co-administration with <strong>erythromycin</strong> or <strong>ketoconazole</strong> can increase plasma fexofenadine levels by 2-3 times, but without affecting the QT interval.</li><li><strong>Antacids</strong> with aluminum and magnesium hydroxide may decrease the bioavailability of fexofenadine. It’s recommended to wait 2 hours before taking such antacids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Axodin 120 mg or its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Axodin 120 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Headaches, fatigue, vertigo, nausea, and site-related issues.</li><li><strong>Uncommon:</strong> Exhaustion, hypersensitivity reactions like angioedema, tight chest, dyspnea, flushing, and anaphylaxis.</li><li><strong>Very rare:</strong> Mental health issues (e.g., anxiety, nightmares), tachycardia, and gastrointestinal disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category C (US FDA) - avoid unless necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Not recommended as it may pass into breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Caution is advised in elderly, and those with renal or hepatic impairment.</li><li>May cause tachycardia or palpitations in patients with active cardiovascular disease.</li><li>Fexofenadine is unlikely to impair driving or machinery operation, but individual responses should be checked.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose include dry mouth, sleepiness, fatigue, and dizziness. Treatment involves symptom management, and hemodialysis is not effective for removing the drug from the bloodstream.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light. Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> For patients with renal impairment, 60 mg once daily is recommended.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Pharmacokinetics are altered in patients over 65, with enhanced bioavailability.</li></ul><p>This summary organizes key points of <strong>Axodin 120 mg</strong> for easier reference. Let me know if you need more details!</p>', 'active', '302_1742584764.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:19:24', '2025-05-03 00:11:33'),
(4044, 'Ribosina', 'Riboflavin', 0.30, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ribosina 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>To prevent and treat deficiencies of vitamin B2, such as:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Cheilosis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Angular stomatitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Glossitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Keratitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Seborrhoeic dermatitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin B preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Ribosina 5 mg is a B vitamin. It can be found in certain foods such as milk, meat, eggs, nuts, enriched flour, and green vegetables. Ribosina 5 mg is frequently used in combination with other B vitamins in vitamin B complex products. Vitamin B complex generally includes vitamin B1 (thiamine), vitamin B2 (Ribosina 5 mg), vitamin B3 (niacin/niacinamide), vitamin B5 (pantothenic acid), vitamin B6 (pyridoxine), vitamin B12 (cyanocobalamin), and folic acid. However, some products do not contain all of these ingredients and some may include others, such as biotin, para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA), choline bitartrate, and inositol.</p><p>Ribosina 5 mg is used for preventing low levels of Ribosina 5 mg (Ribosina 5 mg deficiency), cervical cancer, and migraine headaches. It is also used for treating Ribosina 5 mg deficiency, acne, muscle cramps, burning feet syndrome, carpal tunnel syndrome, and blood disorders such as congenital methemoglobinemia and red blood cell aplasia. Some people use Ribosina 5 mg for eye conditions including eye fatigue, cataracts, and glaucoma.</p><p>Other uses include:</p><ul><li>Increasing energy levels</li><li>Boosting immune system function</li><li>Maintaining healthy hair, skin, mucous membranes, and nails</li><li>Slowing aging</li><li>Boosting athletic performance</li><li>Promoting healthy reproductive function</li><li>Canker sores</li><li>Memory loss, including Alzheimer\'s disease</li><li>Ulcers</li><li>Burns</li><li>Alcoholism</li><li>Liver disease</li><li>Sickle cell anemia</li><li>Treating lactic acidosis brought on by NRTI drugs used in AIDS treatment</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ribosina 5 mg</strong></p><p>For treating low levels of Ribosina 5 mg (Ribosina 5 mg deficiency) in adults:</p><ul><li>5-30 mg of Ribosina 5 mg daily in divided doses.</li></ul><p>For preventing migraine headaches:</p><ul><li>400 mg of Ribosina 5 mg (Vitamin B2) per day. It may take up to three months to get best results.</li></ul><p>For preventing cataracts:</p><ul><li>A daily dietary intake of approximately 2.6 mg of Ribosina 5 mg (Vitamin B2) has been used.</li><li>A combination of 3 mg of Ribosina 5 mg (Vitamin B2) plus 40 mg of niacin daily has also been used.</li></ul><p>The daily recommended dietary allowances (RDAs) of Ribosina 5 mg (Vitamin B2) are:</p><ul><li>Infants 0-6 months: 0.3 mg</li><li>Infants 7-12 months: 0.4 mg</li><li>Children 1-3 years: 0.5 mg</li><li>Children 4-8 years: 0.6 mg</li><li>Children 9-13 years: 0.9 mg</li><li>Men 14 years or older: 1.3 mg</li><li>Women 14-18 years: 1 mg</li><li>Women over 18 years: 1.1 mg</li><li>Pregnant women: 1.4 mg</li><li>Breastfeeding women: 1.6 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ribosina 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Drying medications (Anticholinergic drugs)</li><li>Medications for depression (Tricyclic antidepressants)</li><li>Phenobarbital</li><li>Probenecid</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Ribosina 5 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ribosina 5 mg</strong></p><p>Ribosina 5 mg is likely safe for most people. In some, it may cause the urine to turn a yellow-orange color. When taken in high doses, Ribosina 5 mg might cause:</p><ul><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Increased urine output</li><li>Other side effects</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Ribosina 5 mg is <strong>LIKELY SAFE</strong> for pregnant or breastfeeding women when taken in the amounts recommended:</p><ul><li>Pregnant women: 1.4 mg per day</li><li>Breastfeeding women: 1.6 mg per day</li></ul><p>Ribosina 5 mg is <strong>POSSIBLY SAFE</strong> when taken by mouth in larger doses, short-term. Some research shows that Ribosina 5 mg is safe when taken at a dose of 15 mg once every 2 weeks for 10 weeks.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Ribosina 5 mg is non-toxic. No warnings or precautions have been established.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a cool, dry place. Protect from light.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin B preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Ribosina 5 mg (Vitamin B2) is an easily absorbed, water-soluble micronutrient with a key role in maintaining human health. Like other B vitamins, it supports energy production by aiding in the metabolism of fats, carbohydrates, and proteins. Ribosina 5 mg is also required for red blood cell formation, respiration, antibody production, and regulating growth and reproduction. It is essential for healthy skin, nails, and hair growth. It also helps in the prevention or treatment of various eye disorders, including some cases of cataracts.</p><p>Ribosina 5 mg binds to Ribosina 5 mg hydrogenase, Ribosina 5 mg kinase, and Ribosina 5 mg synthase. It is the precursor of flavin mononucleotide (FMN) and flavin adenine dinucleotide (FAD). The antioxidant activity of Ribosina 5 mg is derived from its role as a precursor of FAD, which plays a role in the production of the antioxidant reduced glutathione. Reduced glutathione is a major cofactor for antioxidants like glutathione peroxidases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Ribosina 5 mg is <strong>likely safe</strong> for pregnant or breastfeeding women when taken in the recommended amounts.</p>', 'active', '1885_1742584852.jpg', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:20:52', '2025-05-04 16:59:34'),
(4045, 'Montril', 'Montelukast Sodium', 17.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Montril 10 mg</strong></p><p>Montril 10 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. Cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) are located in the human airway, and these eicosanoids bind to them. The orally active drug Montril 10 mg has a strong affinity and specificity for the CysLT1 receptor. Without any agonist activity, it inhibits LTD4\'s physiological effects at the CysLT1 receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Montril 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for adults and adolescents 15 years of age and older: 10 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 6 to 14 years of age: 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 2 years to 5 years of age: 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 6 months to 5 years of age: 4 mg oral granules once daily. This can be administered either directly in the mouth, or mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in the Pediatric Patient</strong>:</p><p>The safety and efficacy of Monas have been established in adequate and well-controlled studies in pediatric patients with asthma 6 months to 14 years of age. Safety and efficacy profiles in this age group are similar to those seen in adults.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong>:</p><p>No dosage adjustment is required in patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>:</p><p>No dosage adjustment is recommended in patients with renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Elderly use</strong>:</p><p>The pharmacokinetic profile and the oral bioavailability of a single 10-mg oral dose of Monas are similar in elderly and younger adults. The plasma half-life of Monas is slightly longer in the elderly. No dosage adjustment in the elderly is required.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Montril 10 mg</strong></p><p>In the prevention and long-term treatment of asthma, Montril 10 mg has been provided alongside other commonly used treatments without seeming to increase the risk of negative side effects. According to studies on drug interactions, theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives (norethindrone 1mg/ethinyl estradiol 35mcg), terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin did not exhibit any clinically significant effects on the pharmacokinetics at the recommended clinical dose of it. In clinical investigations, the medication was used concurrently with a variety of routinely prescribed medications without showing any signs of clinically harmful interactions, despite the fact that more detailed interaction studies were not carried out. Thyroid hormones, hypnotic sedatives, benzodiazepines, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and decongestants were among these drugs. Following a single dose of this solution, the hepatic metabolism-inducing drug phenobarbital reduced the drug\'s AUC by about 40%. For this medication, no dosage modification is advised. When powerful cytochrome P450 enzyme inducers, such as phenobarbital or rifampin, are provided alongside this solution, it is fair to use proper clinical monitoring.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those who have an extreme sensitivity to any ingredient in this medication should not take it.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Montril 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, asthenia, aberrant behavior, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, hemorrhage, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, oedema, seizure, odd feeling, and sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, decreased attention, disorientation, erythema nodosum, hallucinations, hepatic problems, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, and tremor.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Montril 10 mg is classified as pregnancy category B. Although it has been demonstrated that the medication crosses the placenta of pregnant rats and rabbits, there are no records of its use in pregnant humans. Although there is some evidence that it is also secreted in breast milk, little is known about the implications of this discovery. Before starting the therapy in nursing moms, caution should be exercised.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks, including status asthmaticus, is not recommended as usage for Montril 10 mg. Patients should be told to have access to the proper rescue medication. Asthma exacerbations that are acute can still be treated with the solution. While the dosage of an inhaled corticosteroid may be gradually decreased under medical supervision, the use of this solution instead of an inhaled or oral corticosteroid should not be started suddenly. Exercise-induced bronchospasm should not be treated and managed with a single dose of it. While taking it, patients with documented aspirin sensitivity should continue to refrain from taking aspirin or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Although aspirin sensitivity in asthmatics is a known side effect of the drug, it has not been demonstrated to shorten the bronchoconstrictor response to aspirin and other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs).</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Montril 10 mg</strong></p><p>The majority of overdose reports did not include any negative experiences. The most frequent side effects, which were in line with Montril 10 mg\'s safety profile, were nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain. In the case of an overdose, it is permissible to take the customary supportive measures, such as removing any unabsorbed material from the digestive tract, using clinical monitoring, and if necessary, instituting supportive therapy.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry environment while keeping out light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>The cysteinyl leukotriene receptor is inhibited by the selective and orally active leukotriene receptor antagonist known as Montril 10 mg (CysLT1). The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. The pathophysiology of asthma and allergic rhinitis, including airway edema, smooth muscle contraction, and altered cellular activity associated with the inflammatory process, have been linked to cysteinyl leukotrienes and leukotriene receptor occupation. These factors all contribute to the signs and symptoms of asthma.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>After oral administration, Montril 10 mg passes the placenta in rats and rabbits. Nevertheless, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. Because the human reaction to medications is not usually predicted by research on animal reproduction, pregnant women should only use it if absolutely necessary. When giving the pill to a nursing mother, care should be taken because many medications are secreted in human milk.</p>', 'active', '1132_1742584966.jpg', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:22:46', '2025-05-04 00:08:54'),
(4046, 'Zeefol-CI', 'Carbonyl Iron + Folic Acid + Zinc Sulfate', 4.50, 15, 'capsule', '15 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50 mg+0.50 mg+61.80 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information formatted with bold headings for better readability:</p><p><strong>Indications of Zeefol-CI</strong></p><p>It is indicated for the treatment and prophylaxis of Iron, Folic Acid, and Zinc deficiency, especially during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each capsule contains:</p><ul><li>Elemental iron 50 mg (As carbonyl iron INN)</li><li>Folic acid BP 500 microgram</li><li>Zinc sulphate monohydrate USP 61.80 mg (equivalent to 22.50 mg zinc)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined preparation</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Carbonyl iron</strong>: A form of the mineral iron, essential for oxygen transport in the blood. It is used to prevent and treat iron deficiencies and iron deficiency anemia.</li><li><strong>Folic acid</strong>: Enhances chemical reactions for red blood cell production, DNA synthesis for cell replication, and amino acid metabolism.</li><li><strong>Zinc</strong>: Essential for numerous physiological processes, including enzyme function. Zinc deficiency may cause poor night vision, slow wound healing, poor sexual function, decreased appetite, increased infection susceptibility, and retarded growth in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zeefol-CI</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Elderly</strong>: 1 capsule daily. In more severe cases, 2 capsules daily may be required.</li><li><strong>Children</strong> (aged over 1 year): 1 capsule daily. The capsule may be opened, and the pellets can be mixed with soft, cool food, but they must not be chewed.</li><li><strong>Adult</strong>: One capsule daily before food or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zeefol-CI</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Carbonyl Iron</strong>: Decreases the absorption of tetracycline antibiotics, quinolone antibiotics, levodopa, levothyroxine, methyldopa, and penicillamine.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong>: Interacts with antiepileptics, potentially reducing plasma concentrations of phenobarbital, phenytoin, and primidone.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of its components or those with iron overload.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zeefol-CI</strong></p><p>Gastrointestinal irritations such as nausea, anorexia, vomiting, discomfort, constipation, and diarrhea may occur. Dark stools may also be observed. Carbonyl Iron pellets incorporated into the capsules help reduce gastrointestinal irritation. Rarely, allergic reactions may occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The use of any drug during the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided if possible. Iron supplementation during the first trimester requires clear evidence of iron deficiency.</li><li>Prophylaxis of iron deficiency, where inadequate diet necessitates supplementary zinc and folic acid, is justified during the remainder of pregnancy.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Special care should be taken in patients with iron overload states, such as hemochromatosis, hemolytic anemia, or red blood cell aplasia.</li><li>Failure to respond to treatment requires further investigation to exclude other causes of anemia.</li><li>In patients with renal failure, there may be a risk of zinc accumulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zeefol-CI</strong></p><p>Symptoms of carbonyl iron overdose include decreased energy, nausea, abdominal pain, tarry stool, weak, rapid pulse, fever, coma, and seizures.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C and keep away from light and moisture. Keep all medicines out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined preparation</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This timed-release capsule combines carbonyl iron, folic acid, and zinc sulfate monohydrate. Carbonyl iron, with high bioavailability and low toxicity, is a safer and more effective choice for iron supplementation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Use of any drug during the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided if possible. Iron supplementation during the first trimester requires clear evidence of iron deficiency. Prophylaxis for iron deficiency, where an inadequate diet calls for supplementary zinc and folic acid, is justified during the remainder of pregnancy.</p>', 'active', '333_1742585084.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:24:44', '2025-05-05 22:19:08'),
(4047, 'Xpa XR', 'Paracetamol', 2.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '665 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a breakdown of the information for <strong>Xpa XR 665 mg</strong>:</p><h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p>Xpa XR 665 mg is used for:</p><ul><li>Fever</li><li>Common cold and influenza</li><li>Headache, toothache, earache, bodyache, myalgia, neuralgia, dysmenorrhoea</li><li>Sprains, colic pain, back pain, post-operative pain, postpartum pain, inflammatory pain, post-vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic and osteoarthritic pain and joint stiffness</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Xpa XR 665 mg (Acetaminophen) has:</p><ul><li><strong>Analgesic and antipyretic</strong> properties</li><li><strong>Weak anti-inflammatory activity</strong></li><li>Works mainly in the CNS by inhibiting COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3 enzymes, which are involved in prostaglandin synthesis</li><li>It is considered safe, fast-acting, and generally free from aspirin-related side effects.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 1-2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours (max 4 gm/day).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (6-12 years): ½ to 1 tablet 3-4 times daily (max 2.6 gm/day).</li><li><strong>Extended Release Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults &amp; children &gt;12 years: 2 tablets every 6-8 hours (max 6 tablets/24 hrs). Do not crush.</li><li><strong>Syrup/Suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Varies by age group (from 10 mg/kg for infants to 8 tsp/day for adults).</li><li><strong>Suppository</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Varies by age group (from 60 mg for infants to 1 gm for adults/children &gt;12 years).</li><li><strong>Paediatric Drop</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Age-specific doses for infants and children up to 2 years.</li><li><strong>Tablet with Actizorb technology</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Faster dissolving, used for patients intolerant to aspirin or other analgesics.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li>Reduced ability to metabolize high doses with alcohol, barbiturates, or tricyclic antidepressants.</li><li>Chronic use of anticonvulsants or steroids may affect Xpa XR 665 mg’s effectiveness by increasing clearance.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Xpa XR 665 mg.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Usually mild, but could include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hematological reactions (thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, etc.)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pancreatitis, skin rashes, allergic reactions.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>Pregnancy Category B (USFDA): Use only if clearly needed.</li><li>Excreted in breast milk but not expected to harm a nursing infant at therapeutic doses.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired kidney or liver function.</li><li>Careful with patients on other liver-affecting drugs.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Initial symptoms (24 hrs): Pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, abdominal pain.</li><li>Liver damage may appear 12-48 hours post-ingestion, with potential for glucose metabolism abnormalities and metabolic acidosis.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage</strong></h3><ul><li>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Analgesic</strong> and <strong>antipyretic</strong> effects with <strong>weak anti-inflammatory</strong> activity.</li><li>Primarily works in the CNS by inhibiting cyclooxygenase enzymes (COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3).</li></ul><p>This should cover all the key points about Xpa XR 665 mg! Let me know if you\'d like further details on any specific part.</p>', 'active', '951_1742585165.jpg', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:26:05', '2025-05-05 20:50:42');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4048, 'Salflu', 'Salmeterol + Fluticasone Propionate', 12.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 Capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50 mcg+250 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Salflu 50 mcg + 250 mcg</h3><p>This combination medication is indicated for the regular treatment of asthma where the use of a combination product (long-acting β2-agonist and inhaled corticosteroid) is appropriate. This includes:</p><ul><li>Patients not adequately controlled with inhaled corticosteroids and \"as-needed\" inhaled short-acting β2-agonists.</li><li>Patients already adequately controlled on both inhaled corticosteroids and long-acting β2-agonists.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants</strong></li><li><strong>Respiratory corticosteroids</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate</strong>: A selective, long-acting beta-2 agonist used in the treatment of asthma and other forms of diffuse airways obstruction. It offers symptom protection by binding to the exo-site of the receptor.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: A corticosteroid with primarily glucocorticoid activity. It provides a potent anti-inflammatory effect on the lungs, improving lung function and preventing exacerbations without the systemic effects typically seen with oral corticosteroids.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Inhalation Aerosol</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 125 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 250 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily</li><li><strong>Children (4-12 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily</li><li><strong>Inhalation Capsule (for asthma)</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg or Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 250 µg twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The maximum recommended dose is Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 500 µg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients (4 to 11 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Powder (Maxhaler)</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">One inhalation twice daily (50/100, 50/250, or 50/500 Maxhaler).</li><li><strong>Children (4 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">One inhalation twice daily (50/100 Maxhaler). The maximum dose for children is 100 µg twice daily.</li></ul><h3>Administration of Salflu 50 mcg + 250 mcg</h3><p>To ensure the proper dosage reaches the lungs, follow these steps when using the inhaler:</p><ol><li><strong>Take off the cap</strong> and <strong>shake the inhaler</strong> vigorously at least 6 times.</li><li><strong>Breathe out</strong> as fully as comfortable, keeping the inhaler upright.</li><li><strong>Place the actuator</strong> in your mouth between your teeth and close your lips around the mouthpiece.</li><li><strong>Press down firmly on the canister</strong> while breathing deeply and slowly through your mouth.</li><li><strong>Hold your breath</strong> for at least 10 seconds, or as long as comfortable.</li><li>If more than one inhalation is prescribed, <strong>wait 1 minute</strong> between puffs and repeat the steps.</li><li>After use, <strong>rinse your mouth</strong> with water and replace the cap.</li></ol><h3>Interaction of Salflu 50 mcg + 250 mcg</h3><ul><li><strong>β-blockers</strong>: Both selective and non-selective β-blockers should be avoided in patients with asthma unless necessary.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., ketoconazole, ritonavir) may increase systemic exposure to Fluticasone Propionate, requiring caution.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: This product is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol</strong>: May cause tremors, palpitations, headaches, arrhythmias (including atrial fibrillation and tachycardia), hypersensitivity reactions, oropharyngeal irritation, and rare muscle cramps.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: Possible side effects include hoarseness, candidiasis (thrush), skin hypersensitivity, and rare cases of facial and oropharyngeal edema.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Should only be used if the expected benefit outweighs the potential risk to the fetus. There is insufficient data on its safety during pregnancy and lactation.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: There is no available data on whether the components are excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Pulmonary Tuberculosis</strong>: Use with caution in patients with active or latent pulmonary tuberculosis.</li><li><strong>Thyrotoxicosis</strong>: Use with caution in patients with thyrotoxicosis.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li><strong>Storage</strong>: Store the pressurized canister below 30°C, away from direct sunlight, and do not puncture, break, or incinerate even if empty. Keep away from eyes and out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Inhaled corticosteroids may affect growth velocity in pediatric patients. The long-term effects on adult height are unknown.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate</strong>: A long-acting beta-2 agonist that provides symptom relief by binding to the exo-site of the receptor.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: Provides a potent anti-inflammatory effect in the lungs, reducing symptoms and preventing exacerbations.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you\'d like more detailed information or have any questions!</p>', 'active', '733_1742585312.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:28:32', '2025-05-04 20:45:19'),
(4049, 'Pantobex', 'Pantoprazole Sodium', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pantobex 40 mg</strong></p><p>Pantobex 40 mg is recommended when inhibiting acid secretion is therapeutically beneficial, i.e. Stomach ulcer disorders, illnesses caused by gastroesophageal reflux, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug-induced ulcer (NSAIDs), Helicobacter pylori eradication (in combination with antibiotics), and Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Blocker.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Pantobex 40 mg is a proton pump inhibitor that works by covalently attaching to the H+/K+ATPase enzyme system on the surface of the gastric parietal cell to decrease the last stage of gastric acid generation. Regardless of the stimulus that remains for more than 24 hours, this effect results in a suppression of both basal and induced stomach acid secretion.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Pantobex 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Benign gastric ulcer:</strong> 40 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, continued for another 4 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastro-esophageal reflux disease:</strong> 20-40 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, continued for further 4 weeks if not fully healed; maintenance dose is 20 mg daily, which may be increased to 40 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal ulcer:</strong> 40 mg daily in the morning for 2 weeks, continued for further 2 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer associated with Helicobacter pylori:</strong> Pantobex 40 mg is recommended at a dose of 40 mg twice daily in association with antimicrobial agents:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Amoxycillin 1 g and Clarithromycin 500 mg both twice daily for one week, or</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clarithromycin 250 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg both twice daily for one week.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of NSAID-associated gastric or duodenal ulcer:</strong> 20 mg daily for those requiring long-term NSAID treatment.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> Initially 80 mg once daily adjusted according to response (elderly max. 40 mg daily); daily doses above 80 mg given in 2 divided doses.</li><li><strong>IV Injection:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal ulcer and gastric ulcer:</strong> 40 mg once daily for 7-10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease associated with a history of erosive esophagitis:</strong> 40 mg once daily for 7-10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of rebleeding in peptic ulcer:</strong> IV 80 mg, followed by 8 mg/hour infusion for 72 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration:</strong> 80 mg IV every 12 h for 24 h, followed by 40 mg every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Long-term management of Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome and other pathological hypersecretory conditions:</strong> 80 mg IV every 12 hours, may increase to 80 mg every 8 hours if needed, and may titrate to higher doses depending on acid output.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Pantobex 40 mg</strong></p><p>In clinical investigations, no significant medication interactions were found.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those with known hypersensitivity to any of the formulation\'s ingredients should not take Pantobex 40 mg.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Pantobex 40 mg</strong></p><p>Both in the short-term and long-term, Pantobex 40 mg is well tolerated. The most frequent adverse effects are headache and diarrhea, while abdominal pain, flatulence, rash, sleeplessness, and hyperglycemia are only rarely reported side effects.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pantobex 40 mg is classified as a B pregnancy drug by the US FDA. Nevertheless, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. This medication should only be used during pregnancy if clearly necessary. It has been demonstrated that Pantobex 40 mg is excreted in human milk. So, a decision should be made on whether to stop nursing or stop taking the medication, taking into account the benefit of the medication to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Patients should be advised not to split, chew, or crush the Pantobex 40 mg pill. The use of Pantobex 40 mg for an extended period of time may cause malabsorption of cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) or raise the risk of problems linked to osteoporosis.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Pantobex 40 mg</strong></p><p>There are no recognized signs of human overdose. Due to its strong protein binding, Pantobex 40 mg is not easily dialyzable. There is no specific therapy that is advised, only symptomatic and supportive management.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep dry and away from heat and light. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Direction for use of IV injection:</strong></li><li>Pantobex 40 mg lyophilized powder with 0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection must only be administered intravenously and under no other circumstances. A gradual intravenous dose of Pantobex 40 mg should be administered. The powder-containing vial is mixed with 10 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride injection to create the IV injection solution. Upon reconstitution, the injection should be administered gradually over a minimum of 2 to 5 minutes. Use only freshly made solutions. The reconstituted solution can be kept for up to 4 hours at room temperature (up to 30° C).</li><li><strong>How to administer an IV infusion:</strong></li><li>An intravenous infusion of Pantobex 40 mg should be administered over approximately 15 minutes. An IV infusion of Pantobex 40 mg needs to be reconstituted with 10 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride injection before being further diluted (admixed) to a final volume of 100 ml using 0.9% sodium chloride injection, 5% dextrose, or lactated ringer\'s injection. Prior to further dilution, the reconstituted solution may be kept at room temperature (up to 30° C) for a maximum of 4 hours. The admixed solution must be used within 24 hours of the initial reconstitution and may be kept at room temperature (up to 30° C).</li></ul>', 'active', '1336_1742585405.png', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:30:05', '2025-05-04 10:14:56'),
(4050, 'Maxima', 'Esomeprazole', 7.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Maxima 20 mg</h3><p>Maxima 20 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Alleviating GERD-related symptoms</strong> such as persistent heartburn and others.</li><li><strong>Treatment of erosive esophagitis.</strong></li><li><strong>Maintenance of healing in erosive esophagitis.</strong></li><li><strong>Eradication of Helicobacter pylori</strong> infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</strong></li><li><strong>Duodenal and gastric ulcers</strong> caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><h3>Dosage of Maxima 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. An additional 4-8 weeks may be considered for patients who do not heal within this time.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (Clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication</strong> (Triple Therapy for duodenal ulcer recurrence): 40 mg esomeprazole once daily for 10 days, amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg once daily. The dosage should be adjusted individually.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks based on the response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: 40 mg once daily via intravenous injection (not less than 3 minutes) or infusion (10 to 30 minutes).</li></ul><h3>Administration of Maxima 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Oral Administration</strong>: Take one capsule or pill at least one hour before a meal. The capsule should be swallowed whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>: Mix the contents of one packet with 15 ml of water. Stir thoroughly, wait 2-3 minutes for it to thicken, stir again, and consume within 30 minutes. If any medicine remains after consumption, add extra water, swirl, and drink immediately.</li><li><strong>Nasogastric or Gastric Tube</strong>: Mix with 15 ml of water in a syringe, shake for 2-3 minutes, then administer through the tube.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Administration</strong>: Add 5 ml of solvent (WFI) to the vial, reconstitute, and administer over at least 3 minutes.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Maxima 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>CYP Enzyme Metabolism</strong>: Esomeprazole is metabolized mainly by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4.</li><li><strong>Drug Interactions</strong>: Esomeprazole has no clinically significant interactions with phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin.</li><li><strong>Diazepam</strong>: Co-administration may reduce diazepam clearance by 45%, increasing its plasma levels.</li><li><strong>Absorption Effect</strong>: Esomeprazole reduces stomach acid, potentially affecting the absorption of medications like ketoconazole, iron salts, and digoxin.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Contraindicated in individuals with known hypersensitivity to esomeprazole or its formulations.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Maxima 20 mg</h3><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Headache</strong></li><li><strong>Diarrhea</strong></li><li><strong>Nausea</strong></li><li><strong>Flatulence</strong></li><li><strong>Abdominal pain</strong></li><li><strong>Constipation</strong></li><li><strong>Dry mouth</strong></li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>A positive symptom response does not rule out stomach cancer.</li><li>Maxima 20 mg should be taken an hour before meals.</li><li>For patients with difficulty swallowing capsules, the pellets inside the capsule can be mixed with cold applesauce and ingested immediately.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Maxima 20 mg</h3><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose may include decreased motor activity, changes in breathing, tremors, ataxia, and clonic convulsions.</li><li><strong>No known antidote</strong> for overdose.</li><li><strong>Supportive care</strong> should be provided.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place at temperatures not exceeding <strong>30°C</strong>. Keep away from moisture and light.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>Esomeprazole inhibits H+/K+-ATPase in the gastric parietal cells, reducing stomach acid production.</li><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: Peak plasma levels occur about 1.5 hours after dosing.</li><li><strong>Metabolism</strong>: Primarily metabolized by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 in the liver.</li><li><strong>Excretion</strong>: 80% is excreted in urine as inactive metabolites, with the remaining 20% in feces.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li><strong>No sufficient studies</strong> available for pregnant women.</li><li>No teratogenic effects in animal studies.</li><li>Esomeprazole is excreted in milk; breastfeeding should be discontinued if esomeprazole is used.</li></ul><h3>Pediatric and Geriatric Use</h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric</strong>: No significant differences in safety and effectiveness between older and younger patients, though older adults may be more sensitive.</li></ul><h3>Hepatic and Renal Insufficiency</h3><ul><li><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage change for mild to moderate liver impairment; maximum dose should not exceed 20 mg in severe hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: No adjustment needed as less than 1% of esomeprazole is excreted unchanged in the urine.</li></ul>', 'active', '967_1742585492.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:31:32', '2025-05-03 23:57:33'),
(4051, 'Nexum MUPS MUPS', 'Esomeprazole', 14.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nexum MUPS 40 mg</strong></p><p>Nexum MUPS 40 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>To alleviate GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn and others.</li><li>To treat erosive esophagitis.</li><li>To maintain the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>For the eradication of <em>Helicobacter pylori</em> infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>For the treatment of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>For duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Nexum MUPS 40 mg</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. Most patients are healed within 4 to 8 weeks. For patients who do not heal after 4-8 weeks, an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely after 4 weeks, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori Eradication (Triple Therapy for Duodenal Ulcer Recurrence)</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg once daily. Dosage should be adjusted individually, and treatment continued as long as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks according to the response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: The recommended adult dose is 40 mg Nexum MUPS 40 mg given once daily by intravenous injection (not less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes).</li></ol><p><strong>Administration of Nexum MUPS 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral Administration</strong>: Take one Nexum MUPS 40 mg pill or capsule one hour before a meal. Swallow the capsule whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>: Place the contents of the entire packet in 15 ml of water. Stir thoroughly and wait 2-3 minutes to thicken. Stir again and consume within 30 minutes. If using a nasogastric or gastric tube, prepare as described and administer accordingly.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Administration</strong>: Reconstitute the solution with 5 ml of water for injection (WFI). Administer the solution gradually over a minimum of 3 minutes. Use within 12 hours of reconstitution and avoid refrigeration.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Nexum MUPS 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is metabolized by <strong>CYP2C19</strong> and <strong>CYP3A4</strong> enzymes. It is unlikely to inhibit other CYP enzymes significantly, so there are no clinically significant interactions with medications processed by these enzymes.</li><li>Co-administration with <strong>phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin</strong> does not show clinical interaction.</li><li><strong>Diazepam</strong> clearance may be reduced by 45%, leading to higher plasma levels of diazepam.</li><li>Esomeprazole may reduce the absorption of certain drugs affected by gastrointestinal pH (e.g., ketoconazole, iron salts, digoxin).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to esomeprazole or any of its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Nexum MUPS 40 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Dry mouth</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>A positive symptom response to Nexum MUPS 40 mg does not rule out stomach cancer.</li><li>Take esomeprazole at least an hour before meals.</li><li>For patients with difficulty swallowing capsules, the pellets inside the capsule can be mixed with applesauce and ingested immediately.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Nexum MUPS 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose in rats included decreased motor activity, changes in breathing, tremors, ataxia, and clonic convulsions.</li><li>No known specific countermeasure for esomeprazole overdose. Symptomatic and supportive care is recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry area at a temperature not exceeding 30°C. Protect from moisture and light. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Esomeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor that reduces stomach acid production by specifically inhibiting the <strong>H+/K+-ATPase</strong> in the gastric parietal cells. It is the S-isomer of omeprazole, providing superior acid control.</p><ul><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: The bioavailability of esomeprazole is about 90% with repeated daily doses.</li><li><strong>Metabolism</strong>: Metabolized by the <strong>CYP2C19</strong> and <strong>CYP3A4</strong> enzymes in the liver.</li><li><strong>Excretion</strong>: Around 80% is excreted in the urine as inactive metabolites.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Insufficient reliable data in humans, though no teratogenic effects were found in animal studies. Breastfeeding should be discontinued if esomeprazole is deemed necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>No significant differences between elderly and younger patients in terms of safety or efficacy, though older individuals may be more sensitive to the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage change is needed for mild to moderate hepatic insufficiency. In severe hepatic impairment, the dose should not exceed 20 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole pharmacokinetics are not expected to differ significantly in patients with renal impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '461_1742585641.jpg', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:34:01', '2025-05-04 00:39:59'),
(4052, 'Asynta Max', 'Sodium Alginate + Sodium Bicarbonate + Calcium Carbonate', 300.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '(500 mg+213 mg+325 mg)/10 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Asynta Max 200 ml</strong></p><p>Asynta Max 200 ml is indicated for individuals experiencing:</p><ul><li>Gastric reflux</li><li>Heartburn</li><li>Flatulence associated with gastric reflux</li><li>Heartburn during pregnancy</li><li>Epigastric and retrosternal discomfort where the underlying cause is gastric reflux.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The mode of action of the product is physical and does not depend on absorption into the systemic circulation. Upon ingestion, Asynta Max 200 ml rapidly reacts with gastric acid, forming a protective Alginic acid gel raft with a nearly neutral pH. This raft floats on the stomach contents, effectively preventing gastro-esophageal reflux for up to 4 hours. In severe cases, the raft itself may be refluxed into the esophagus, providing a soothing effect rather than the stomach contents.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Asynta Max 200 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: Take 10-20ml after meals and at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 12 years</strong>: Take 5-10ml after meals and at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Children under 6 years</strong>: Not recommended.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustment is needed for this age group.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dose modification necessary.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: Use caution if a highly restricted salt diet is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Asynta Max 200 ml</strong></p><p>A time interval of 2 hours should be observed between taking this medication and the administration of other medicinal products, particularly:</p><ul><li>Tetracyclines</li><li>Digoxin</li><li>Fluoroquinolones</li><li>Iron salts</li><li>Ketoconazole</li><li>Neuroleptics</li><li>Thyroid hormones</li><li>Penicillamine</li><li>Beta-blockers (atenolol, metoprolol, propranolol)</li><li>Glucocorticoids</li><li>Chloroquine</li><li>Bisphosphonates (diphosphonates)</li><li>Estramustine</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Asynta Max 200 ml should not be used in patients with a known or suspected hypersensitivity to the active ingredients or any of the excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Asynta Max 200 ml</strong></p><p>In addition to the intended effects of the medication, certain side effects may occur, including:</p><ul><li>Constipation</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Belching</li></ul><p>If you experience any of these symptoms, please consult a physician. If an excessive dose is taken, you may experience a sensation of swelling. In such cases, it is advisable to seek medical advice.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Clinical studies involving over 500 pregnant women have shown no evidence of malformation or fetal/neonatal toxicity from the active substances. This medication can be safely used during pregnancy when clinically necessary.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: No adverse effects of the active substances have been observed in breastfed newborns/infants of mothers undergoing treatment. This medication is considered safe for use during breastfeeding.</li><li><strong>Fertility</strong>: Pre-clinical investigations have demonstrated that alginate has no detrimental effects on parental or offspring fertility or reproduction. Clinical data do not suggest any impact of this medication on human fertility.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>If symptoms do not show improvement within 7 days, it is advisable to reassess the clinical condition.</li><li>Each 10 mL dose contains 141 mg of sodium (equivalent to 6.2 mmol), which should be considered when prescribing a highly restricted salt diet, especially in cases of congestive cardiac failure and renal impairment.</li><li>Each 10 mL dose contains 160 mg of calcium carbonate (equivalent to 1.6 mmol). Caution is warranted when treating patients with hypercalcemia, nephrocalcinosis, and recurrent calcium-containing renal calculi.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Asynta Max 200 ml</strong></p><p>In the event of overdose, symptomatic treatment should be given. The patient may notice abdominal distension.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool (below 30°C) and dry place, away from light. Keep out of the reach of children. Do not refrigerate or freeze.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Paediatric Use</strong>: This medication is not recommended for children under 6 years of age.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No dose modification is required for this age group.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Asynta Max 200 ml operates through a physical mechanism and does not rely on absorption into the systemic circulation. This product combines two antacids—Calcium Carbonate and Sodium Bicarbonate—with Alginate. Upon ingestion, the medication reacts with gastric acid to form a protective Alginic Acid gel raft with a nearly neutral pH. This raft floats on the stomach contents and helps reduce gastro-esophageal reflux for up to 4 hours. Additionally, the raft entraps bile and pepsin, further protecting the esophagus from these gastric components. Calcium Carbonate and Sodium Bicarbonate help neutralize gastric acid, providing rapid relief from indigestion and heartburn.</p>', 'active', '871_1742585748.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:35:48', '2025-05-03 00:04:05'),
(4053, 'Supravit-M', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral', 3.55, 15, 'capsule', '15 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', 'N/A', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1865_1742585858.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:37:38', '2025-05-04 21:28:19'),
(4054, 'Esolok', 'Esomeprazole', 9.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p>', 'active', '3644_1746167099.jpg', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:38:59', '2025-05-02 10:24:59'),
(4055, 'Prenat-CI', 'Carbonyl Iron + Folic Acid + Zinc Sulfate', 5.00, 12, 'capsule', '12 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50 mg+0.50 mg+61.80 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '166_1742586017.jpg', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:40:17', '2025-05-04 10:28:48'),
(4056, 'DDR', 'Dexlansoprazole', 11.50, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '30 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of DDR 30 mg</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis (EE)</strong>: DDR 30 mg is indicated for the healing of all grades of erosive esophagitis (EE) for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: DDR 30 mg is indicated to maintain the healing of EE and relief of heartburn for up to 6 months.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</strong>: DDR 30 mg is indicated for the treatment of heartburn associated with symptomatic non-erosive GERD for 4 weeks.</li></ol><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>DDR 30 mg is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI) that inhibits gastric acid secretion. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole, a racemic mixture of the R- and S-enantiomers. DDR 30 mg is supplied as a Dual Delayed Release (DDR) formulation in a capsule for oral administration. This formulation contains two types of enteric-coated granules with different pH-dependent dissolution profiles. DDR 30 mg specifically inhibits the (H+/K+)-ATPase in gastric parietal cells, blocking the final step of acid production.</p><p><strong>Dosage of DDR 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 60 mg once daily for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis and Relief of Heartburn</strong>: 30 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive GERD</strong>: 30 mg once daily for 4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of DDR 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Can be taken with or without food.</li><li>Should be swallowed whole or the capsule can be opened, and granules can be sprinkled on one tablespoon of food and swallowed immediately without chewing.</li><li>If a dose is missed, take it as soon as possible unless it\'s close to the next dose. Do not double the dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of DDR 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With Medicine</strong>: Atazanavir, Warfarin, Tacrolimus, Clopidogrel, Methotrexate.</li><li><strong>With Food &amp; Others</strong>: No data available.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of DDR 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and flatulence.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>There are no well-controlled studies in pregnant women. DDR 30 mg should only be used if the benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li>Not known if DDR 30 mg is excreted in human milk. It is excreted in rat milk, so use in nursing mothers should be considered with caution.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gastric Malignancy</strong></li><li><strong>Clostridium difficile-associated Diarrhea</strong></li><li><strong>Bone Fracture</strong></li><li><strong>Hypomagnesemia</strong></li><li><strong>Concomitant use with Methotrexate</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of DDR 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No significant overdose reports. Multiple doses of DDR 30 mg 120 mg and a single dose of 300 mg did not result in severe adverse events.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, in a dry place, protected from light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics</strong>: No dosage adjustment necessary.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: Safety and effectiveness not established for children under 12 years of age.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment necessary.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No adjustment necessary for mild hepatic impairment. For moderate hepatic impairment, a maximum dose of 30 mg daily may be considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>DDR 30 mg suppresses gastric acid secretion by specifically inhibiting the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cells. The dual delayed release formulation produces a plasma concentration-time profile with two distinct peaks, leading to a more prolonged effect.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Category B. There are no well-controlled studies in pregnant women, and it should only be used when the benefits outweigh the risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Not established for patients under 12 years old.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>No dose adjustment necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Impairment</strong></p><ul><li>No dose adjustment necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong></p><ul><li>No dose adjustment necessary for mild impairment. Consider a maximum of 30 mg for moderate impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '660_1742586110.jpg', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:41:50', '2025-05-03 07:50:36'),
(4057, 'Povital', 'Vitamin B1, B6 & B12', 12.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg+200 mg+200 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s a more structured, bolded format for the information you provided:</p><p><strong>Indications of Povital 100 mg+200 mg+200 mg</strong></p><p>Povital 100 mg+200 mg+200 mg is indicated for the treatment of <strong>Vitamin B1, B6, &amp; B12 deficiency syndrome</strong>. It is also indicated for the <strong>supportive treatment of neuritis</strong> and non-inflammatory diseases of the nerves, such as:</p><ul><li><strong>Diabetic neuropathy</strong></li><li><strong>Peripheral neuralgia</strong></li><li><strong>Lumbago</strong></li><li><strong>Myalgia</strong></li><li><strong>Optic neuritis</strong></li><li><strong>Sciatica</strong></li><li><strong>Facial neuralgia</strong></li><li><strong>Intercostal neuralgia</strong></li><li><strong>Spinal pain</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate)</strong> 100 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride)</strong> 200 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin)</strong> 200 micrograms</li></ul><p>Each 3 ml ampoule contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1)</strong> 100 mg</li><li><strong>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6)</strong> 100 mg</li><li><strong>Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12)</strong> 1000 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p><strong>Specific combined vitamin preparations</strong></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Povital 100 mg+200 mg+200 mg is a combined preparation of <strong>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate)</strong>, <strong>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride)</strong>, and <strong>Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin)</strong>. These vitamins play an essential role as co-enzymes in the metabolism of the nervous system. They support the regeneration of nerve fibers and the myelin sheath by activating nerve cell metabolism and the natural repair mechanism.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablets</strong>: 1 to 3 tablets per day or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly (deep intragluteal).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In severe cases: 1 ampoule daily until the acute symptoms subside.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For milder cases and follow-up therapy: 2 to 3 ampoules per week.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Children</strong>: There is no information on the use of this drug in children.</p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported yet.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Levodopa therapy</strong>: Vitamin B1, Vitamin B6, and Vitamin B12 are contraindicated in patients on levodopa therapy.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients in the preparation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Generally well tolerated, but <strong>allergic reactions</strong> may occur in some cases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>No risks have been known associated with the use of this combination during pregnancy at the recommended dosage.</li><li>Vitamin B1, B6, and B12 are secreted into breast milk, but risks of overdose for the infant are not known.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cyanocobalamin</strong> should not be given to patients with <strong>subacute degeneration of the spinal cord</strong>.</li><li>Cyanocobalamin is not suitable for the treatment of <strong>optic neuropathies</strong> associated with raised plasma concentrations of cyanocobalamin.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>No overdosage symptoms are expected at the recommended dosage. In case of overdose, treatment is <strong>symptomatic and supportive</strong>.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Store in a cool place (below 25°C), dry, and protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p><strong>Specific combined vitamin preparations</strong></p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin B1</strong>: Converts carbohydrates, fatty acids, and amino acids into energy, promotes healthy nerves, improves mood, and strengthens the heart.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6</strong>: Forms red blood cells (RBCs), helps cells make proteins, manufactures neurotransmitters (e.g., serotonin), releases stored forms of energy, prevents cardiovascular diseases, eases depression, and promotes better sleep.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12</strong>: Essential for cell replication, RBC production, prevents anemia, helps prevent depression, reduces nerve pain, and lowers the risk of heart diseases.</li></ul><p>The vitamin ingredients are absorbed well when taken orally, widely distributed to most tissues, and appear in breast milk. Thiamine is not stored in the body to any significant extent and is excreted in urine, while Pyridoxine and its metabolites are mainly stored in the liver.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Oral tablet form is recommended; however, due to the presence of <strong>benzyl alcohol</strong>, the injection is not recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need any additional information!</p>', 'active', '1643_1742586189.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:43:09', '2025-05-04 10:24:54'),
(4058, 'Ostocal DX', 'Elemental Calcium + Vitamin D3', 10.00, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ostocal DX</strong></p><p>This combination is used for the treatment of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, rickets, tetany, and parathyroid disease. Calcium supplements are often used to ensure adequate dietary intake in conditions such as pregnancy &amp; lactation, osteogenesis, and tooth formation (adjunct with definite treatment) and therapy with anti-seizure medications. It is also used as a routine supplement and phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Ostocal DX is a preparation of Calcium Carbonate and Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol). Calcium is necessary for many normal functions of our body, especially bone formation and maintenance. Vitamin D3 helps with the absorption &amp; reabsorption of Calcium. Vitamin D3 also stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies showed that Calcium and Vitamin D3 together help in bone growth and in the prevention of osteoporosis &amp; bone fractures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ostocal DX</strong></p><ul><li>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 200 IU Tablet: 2 tablets daily or 1 tablet twice daily. Ostocal DX is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li><li>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 400 IU Tablet: 1 tablet twice daily. It is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ostocal DX</strong></p><p>It may interact with calcium, aluminium or magnesium-containing antacids &amp; other calcium supplements, calcitriol &amp; other vitamin D3 supplements; digoxin, tetracycline, doxycycline, minocycline, or oxytetracycline.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Ostocal DX is contraindicated in cases of hypercalcaemia, hyperthyroidism, renal calculi &amp; nephrolithiasis, and Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ostocal DX</strong></p><p>Ostocal DX is generally well tolerated. However, if you experience nausea, vomiting, stomach cramps, dry mouth, increased thirst, or increased urination while taking it, consult a physician. Constipation may also occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This combination should be used as directed by a physician during pregnancy or while breast-feeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>If there is any pre-existing heart disease or kidney disease, precautions should be taken.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ostocal DX</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdosage may include nausea and vomiting, severe drowsiness, dry mouth, loss of appetite, metallic taste, stomach cramps, diarrhea, headache, and constipation.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '914_1742586328.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:45:28', '2025-05-04 01:33:50');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4059, 'Betameson', 'Betamethasone Dipropionate', 48.00, 1, 'x 20 gm tube', '20 gm tube', 'Ointment', '0.05%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Betameson 20 gm</strong></p><p>Betameson 20 gm cream and ointment are indicated for the relief of the inflammatory and pruritic manifestations of resistant or severe corticosteroid-responsive dermatoses. These include:</p><ul><li>Atopic eczema</li><li>Nummular eczema</li><li>Contact dermatitis</li><li>Neurodermatitis</li><li>Anogenital and senile pruritus</li><li>Lichen planus</li><li>Psoriasis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Corticosteroid</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Betameson 20 gm is a potent topical corticosteroid. Topical corticosteroids have anti-inflammatory, antipruritic, and vasoconstrictive actions when administered. Betamethasone acts by inhibition of phospholipase A2 activity and reduces the formation of prostaglandins and leukotrienes in local tissues.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Betameson 20 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Apply a thin film once or twice daily to cover completely the affected area.</li><li>Patients with chronic psoriasis who have achieved at least a marked improvement in their psoriatic lesion (i.e., approximately 80% improvement) with Betameson 20 gm may be maintained in remission with a pulse dosing regimen. This consists of three consecutive applications of up to 3.5 g each of Betameson 20 gm cream and ointment, twelve hours apart (e.g., morning, evening, following morning), once each week.</li><li>For this purpose, Betameson 20 gm cream and ointment should be applied to the lesion sites previously affected and treated.</li><li>Patients on this pulse dose regimen who relapse should be reverted back to the conventional dosing regimen.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Betameson 20 gm</strong></p><p>There is no evidence of any kind of interaction.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Betameson 20 gm is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Betameson 20 gm, other corticosteroids, or any components in this preparation.</li><li>Viral infections of the skin, such as vaccinia, varicella, and Herpes simplex.</li><li>Tuberculosis, acne rosacea, fungal skin infections (moniliasis), perioral dermatitis, and ulcerative conditions.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Betameson 20 gm</strong></p><p>The most frequent side effects reported with Betameson 20 gm include:</p><ul><li>Mild to moderate transient burning/stinging, dry skin, pruritus, irritation, and folliculitis.</li><li>Rarely reported adverse effects include tingling, prickly skin/tightening or cracking of skin, warm feeling, laminar scaling, perilesional scaling, follicular rash, skin atrophy, erythema, urticaria, vesiculation, telangiectasia, acneiform papules, and hyperaesthesia.</li><li>Adverse reactions reported with the use of the Betameson 20 gm ointment pulse dose regimen were mild intermittent hypertension and paraesthesia.</li></ul><p>Other local adverse reactions may include:</p><ul><li>Itching, hypertrichosis, hypopigmentation, perioral dermatitis, allergic contact dermatitis, maceration of the skin, secondary infection, striae, miliaria, and exacerbation of untreated infections.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Betameson 20 gm should not be used in or near the eyes, as there is a potential risk of developing glaucoma and cataract.</li><li>If irritation or sensitisation develops with the use of Betameson 20 gm, treatment should be discontinued and appropriate therapy instituted.</li><li>In the presence of an infection, an appropriate antifungal or antibacterial agent should be administered.</li><li>If a favourable response does not occur promptly, Betameson 20 gm should be discontinued until the infection has been controlled adequately.</li><li>Prolonged use of topical corticosteroid preparations may cause striae or atrophy of the skin or subcutaneous tissue.</li><li>Betameson 20 gm is not intended for use under occlusive dressings since this may increase systemic absorption.</li><li>In infants, the napkin may act as an occlusive dressing, increasing absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Betameson 20 gm</strong></p><p>Acute overdose with the ointment is unlikely and would not be expected to lead to a life-threatening situation. The ointment should not be used for longer than the prescribed time period.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Corticosteroid</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Betameson 20 gm is a topical corticosteroid with anti-inflammatory, antipruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties. Betameson 20 gm induces peptides called lipocortins. Lipocortins antagonize phospholipase A2, an enzyme that causes the breakdown of leukocyte lysosomal membranes to release arachidonic acid. This action decreases the subsequent formation and release of endogenous inflammatory mediators, including prostaglandins, kinins, and histamine.</p><p><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></p><p>Betameson 20 gm can be absorbed from normal intact skin. Corticosteroids are bound to plasma proteins in varying degrees. They are metabolized primarily in the liver and excreted by the kidneys.</p>', 'active', '373_1742586432.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:47:12', '2025-04-30 20:33:06'),
(4060, 'Zifolet', 'Folic Acid + Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate', 2.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zifolet 5 mg + 20 mg</strong></p><p>This tablet is indicated for the treatment and prophylaxis of folic acid and zinc deficiencies.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Zinc</strong> is an essential element in human health, playing a role as a coenzyme for over 200 enzymes involved in nucleic acid and protein metabolism, as well as energy production. It is vital for growth and development. Zinc deficiency can lead to growth retardation, immune dysfunction, increased incidence of infections, anorexia, weight loss, neural tube defects in fetuses, increased risk for abortion, and mental lethargy.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong> is a member of the B-vitamin group and is essential for processes like DNA, RNA, and protein synthesis. It plays a crucial role in DNA replication and gene expression regulation. Folic acid deficiency during pregnancy increases the risk of neural tube defects such as spina bifida, anencephaly, and meningocele. Folic acid deficiency can also cause megaloblastic anemia, fatigue, irritability, weakness, headache, difficulty concentrating, and palpitations.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li>One tablet daily or as recommended by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li>A large amount of calcium decreases the absorption of zinc. No drug interactions have been reported for folic acid.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Zinc is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to zinc.</li><li>Folic acid is contraindicated in cases of untreated cobalamin deficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Folic acid and zinc are generally well tolerated at recommended doses. Occasionally, gastrointestinal disturbances such as abdominal pain, dyspepsia, nausea, vomiting, fever, and respiratory distress may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Zinc sulfate is corrosive in overdose. Symptoms may include corrosion and inflammation of the mucous membranes of the mouth and stomach.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at a temperature below 30°C, protect from light and moisture, and keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>This tablet is a special preparation of folic acid and zinc sulfate monohydrate. Zinc is an essential trace element involved in body enzyme systems. Severe zinc deficiency can cause skin lesions, alopecia, diarrhea, increased susceptibility to infections, and cognitive impairment.</li><li>Folic acid is reduced in the body to tetrahydrofolate, which is essential for purine and pyrimidine nucleotide synthesis and DNA synthesis. It is also involved in some amino acid conversions. Folic acid deficiency can lead to megaloblastic anemia, commonly due to inadequate dietary intake, malabsorption, increased utilization (as in pregnancy), or the use of folate antagonists.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul>', 'active', '114_1742586511.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:48:31', '2025-05-05 22:33:38'),
(4061, 'Maxima', 'Esomeprazole', 7.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Maxima 20 mg</strong></p><p>Maxima 20 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Alleviating GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn and others.</li><li>Treating erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Maintaining the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Eradicating Helicobacter pylori infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Treating Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>Treating duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Maxima 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. Most patients are healed within 4 to 8 weeks. If healing does not occur within this time, an additional 4-8 weeks may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (clinical studies did not extend 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms persist, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication</strong>: Triple Therapy to reduce duodenal ulcer recurrence: Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days, Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg once daily. Adjust the dosage individually and continue as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks based on the response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: 40 mg Maxima 20 mg once daily by intravenous injection (not less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes). Do not administer with other medications through the same intravenous site. Discontinue the IV treatment as soon as oral therapy can be resumed.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Maxima 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take one Maxima 20 mg pill or capsule whole, one hour before a meal.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>: Place the contents of one packet in 15 ml of water. Stir, let thicken for 2-3 minutes, stir again, and consume within 30 minutes. If using a nasogastric or gastric tube, follow similar steps and ensure the syringe is the right size.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong>: Add 5 ml of the specified solvent (WFI) to the vial. Administer the reconstituted solution slowly over at least 3 minutes. The solution should be used within 12 hours of reconstitution and should not be refrigerated.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Maxima 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is metabolized by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 enzymes in the liver. It is unlikely to inhibit other CYP enzymes like 1A2, 2A6, 2C9, 2D6, 2E1, and 3A4.</li><li>No significant interactions with phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin.</li><li><strong>Diazepam</strong>: The clearance of diazepam is reduced by 45%, leading to increased plasma levels when taken with esomeprazole.</li><li><strong>Drugs affected by stomach pH</strong>: Esomeprazole can hinder the absorption of drugs like ketoconazole, iron salts, and digoxin.</li><li><strong>Clarithromycin</strong>: Esomeprazole increases the plasma levels of 14-hydroxy clarithromycin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the formulations of esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Maxima 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, and dry mouth.</li><li>No significant difference in side effects between short-term and maintenance therapy up to 12 months.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>A positive response to treatment does not rule out stomach cancer.</li><li>Take esomeprazole capsules at least an hour before meals.</li><li>For patients who have trouble swallowing capsules, the pellets can be mixed with a spoonful of applesauce.</li><li>Do not chew or crush the pellets.</li><li>Antacids can be used while taking esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Maxima 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose may cause symptoms like decreased motor activity, changes in breathing frequency, tremors, ataxia, and clonic convulsions.</li><li>No known specific antidote. Dialysis is unlikely to remove esomeprazole due to its protein binding.</li><li>Symptomatic and supportive care is advised in case of overdose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. Keep out of reach of children and shield from moisture and light.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole inhibits H+/K+-ATPase in the gastric parietal cells, reducing stomach acid production. It is the S-isomer of omeprazole and offers superior acid control over racemic proton pump inhibitors.</li><li>Absorption: Peak plasma levels occur about 1.5 hours after oral dosing.</li><li>Distribution: 97% of esomeprazole binds to plasma proteins.</li><li>Metabolism: It is extensively metabolized in the liver by CYP enzymes, with the majority of metabolites being inactive.</li><li>Excretion: Less than 1% is excreted unchanged in the urine, and about 80% of an oral dose is excreted in the urine as inactive metabolites.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>No sufficient and reliable research on its use during pregnancy. No teratogenic effects have been found in animal studies.</li><li>Esomeprazole is excreted in milk, so breastfeeding should be stopped if its use is deemed necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>No significant differences in safety and efficacy between elderly and younger patients have been noted, although older individuals may be more sensitive to the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage change for mild to moderate hepatic insufficiency. Limit the dose to 20 mg once daily in severe hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>No anticipated changes in pharmacokinetics in patients with renal impairment, as less than 1% is excreted unchanged in the urine.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '490_1742586596.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:49:56', '2025-05-08 20:24:17'),
(4062, 'Famotid', 'Famotidine', 5.00, 14, 'Tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Famotid 40 mg</strong></p><p>Famotid 40 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Gastric ulcer</li><li>Duodenal ulcer</li><li>Anastomotic ulcer</li><li>Acute stress ulcer</li><li>Reflux esophagitis</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Famotid 40 mg is also indicated for the treatment of acute gastritis and chronic gastritis in the acute exacerbation stage.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>H2 receptor antagonist</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Famotid 40 mg is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist. It completely inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors of parietal cells. Famotid 40 mg inhibits basal, overnight, and pentagastrin-stimulated gastric acid secretion. The H2-receptor antagonist activity of Famotid 40 mg is slowly reversible, as the drug dissociates slowly from the H2-receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Famotid 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer</strong>: 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks</li><li><strong>Benign gastric ulcer</strong>: 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks; Maintenance therapy: 20 mg at night to prevent recurrence of duodenal ulceration</li><li><strong>Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</strong>: 20 mg twice daily for 6 to 12 weeks</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: Starting dose is 20 mg every six hours, adjusted based on individual response. Doses up to 160 mg every six hours have been used without significant adverse effects</li><li><strong>Dosage can be administered irrespective of meals</strong>. Antacids may be taken concomitantly if needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage for Famotid 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>:</li><li><strong>Gastric ulcer, duodenal ulcer, anastomotic ulcer, upper gastro-intestinal hemorrhage, reflux esophagitis, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Usual dose for adults: 20 mg twice daily (after breakfast and supper or before bedtime)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Alternatively, 40 mg once daily at bedtime</li><li><strong>Acute gastritis, chronic gastritis in the acute exacerbation stage</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">20 mg twice daily (after breakfast and supper or before bedtime)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Alternatively, 40 mg once daily at bedtime</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance therapy</strong>: 20 mg once daily, or as directed by the physician.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Most ulcer patients heal within 4-8 weeks</strong>.</li><li><strong>Powder for Suspension</strong>:</li><li><strong>GERD (in pediatric patients)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">&lt;1 year: 0.5 mg/kg/dose once daily up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">1-2 months: 0.5 mg/kg/dose once daily up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">3 to 11 months: 0.5 mg/kg/dose twice daily up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">1-16 years: 1 mg/kg/day divided twice daily, up to 40 mg twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 40 mg three times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong></p><p>For hospitalized patients unable to take oral medication, Famotid 40 mg Injection Premixed or Famotid 40 mg Injection may be administered until oral therapy can be started.</p><ul><li><strong>Adult dosage</strong>: 20 mg intravenously every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Pediatric dosage (1-16 years)</strong>: Starting dose of 0.25 mg/kg intravenously every 12 hours, up to 40 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Famotid 40 mg</strong></p><p>No clinically significant drug interactions have been identified. Famotid 40 mg does not interact with the cytochrome P450-linked drug-metabolizing enzyme system.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to any component of the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Famotid 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Eruption, constipation, diarrhea, dry mouth, nausea, vomiting, tachycardia, high blood pressure, headache, drowsiness, or insomnia may rarely occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. It should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known if Famotid 40 mg is secreted in human milk, so nursing mothers should either stop nursing or discontinue the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use the minimum required amount depending on the condition of the disease.</li><li>Exercise caution in elderly patients, patients with renal failure, and those with hepatic disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Famotid 40 mg is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist that inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors of parietal cells, reducing gastric acid secretion. Its antagonist activity is slowly reversible, as it dissociates slowly from the H2-receptor.</p>', 'active', '343_1742586668.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:51:08', '2025-05-02 17:28:51'),
(4063, 'Algecal D', 'Algae Calcium + Vitamin D3', 11.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Algecal D 500 mg + 200 IU</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment and prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, and osteogenesis. It can be used as a supplement in case of inadequate intake of Calcium in childhood diet, pregnancy &amp; lactation, rickets, and poor absorption during old age. Other indications include pancreatitis and phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Calcium is absorbed in the intestine, which is then passed through the intestine to the blood and deposited into the bone. Active transport through cells is dependent on Vitamin D3. Vitamin D3 keeps bones healthy by increasing the absorption of calcium in the intestines. Without Vitamin D3, the body will only be able to absorb 10% to 15% of the Calcium from the diet.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Algecal D 500 mg + 200 IU</strong></p><p>One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or food as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Algecal D 500 mg + 200 IU</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Major interaction:</strong> Administering intravenous Ceftriaxone &amp; Calcium can result in life-threatening damage to the lung &amp; kidneys. Calcium should not be administered intravenously within 48 hours of intravenous Ceftriaxone.</li><li><strong>Minor interaction:</strong> Calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline &amp; fluoride preparations, and a minimum of 3 hours should be allowed between ingestion of these medications. Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of calcium. Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce the metabolism of Vitamin D3. Intake of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of calcium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Increased activity of the parathyroid gland, hypercalcemia, constipation, kidney stones, and extreme loss of body water are conditions in which calcium intake is contraindicated.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Algecal D 500 mg + 200 IU</strong></p><p>Flatulence, diarrhea, constipation, and allergic reactions are rare manifestations. Hypercalcemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This should be given to pregnant &amp; lactating mothers as per the recommendation of a physician. Toxicity was tested on pregnant &amp; lactating females, with excellent results.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In mild hypercalciuria, reduction of the dose is sufficient to return to normal serum calcium concentration. Patients with kidney stones or a history of kidney stones should be recommended to increase their fluid intake.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</p>', 'active', '1182_1742586751.jpg', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:52:31', '2025-05-02 22:54:48'),
(4064, 'Vave', 'Domperidone Maleate', 4.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information with the headings in bold as requested:</p><p><strong>Indications of Vave 10 mg</strong></p><p>Dyspeptic symptom complex, often linked with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, may include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric sensation of fullness</li><li>Abdominal distension</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Belching</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Early satiety</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms can occur in various conditions, such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine. Additionally, in Parkinson\'s disease, it can manifest as dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting. For radiological studies, it can be used to speed up barium transit in follow-through radiological examinations.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Vave 10 mg functions as a dopamine receptor antagonist. It exerts gastroprokinetic action by inhibiting dopamine receptors found in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier means it has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Vave 10 mg</strong></p><p>For adults, the recommended dosage is 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily. Children should take 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily. When used for acute nausea and vomiting, the maximum treatment duration should not exceed 12 weeks. It is advisable to take this medication 15-30 minutes before a meal.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Vave 10 mg</strong></p><p>Vave 10 mg should be taken 15-30 minutes before meals, and if needed, before bedtime. The typical recommended oral dosage of Domperidone is as follows:</p><p><strong>For Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li>The maximum daily dose of Domperidone is 80 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg of body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>In cases of dyspeptic symptoms:</strong></p><p><strong>For Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For acute and sub-acute conditions, especially in cases of acute nausea and vomiting:</strong></p><p><strong>For Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>When administered rectally in the form of suppositories:</strong></p><p><strong>For Adults (including elderly):</strong></p><ul><li>30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>The maximum daily rectal dose for children weighing between 10 to 25 kg is 30 mg. The dose may be divided throughout the day if necessary.</li></ul><p>Please note that the maximum treatment period for acute nausea and vomiting is 12 weeks.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Vave 10 mg</strong></p><p>The simultaneous use of anticholinergic drugs may counteract the antidyspeptic effects of domperidone. It\'s important to note that antacids and antisecretory drugs should not be administered concurrently with domperidone as they can reduce its oral bioavailability. Domperidone primarily undergoes metabolism through the CYP3A4 enzyme. In vitro data suggest that coadministration with drugs significantly inhibiting this enzyme may lead to elevated plasma levels of domperidone. Examples of CYP3A4 inhibitors include azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, nefazodone, and others.</p><p>Theoretically, as domperidone has gastro-kinetic effects, it could potentially impact the absorption of concurrently orally administered drugs, especially those with sustained release or enteric-coated formulations. However, in patients already stabilized on medications like digoxin or paracetamol, the concurrent use of domperidone did not affect the blood levels of these drugs. Domperidone does not potentiate the action of neuroleptics, and it suppresses the unwanted peripheral effects such as digestive disorders, nausea, and vomiting caused by dopaminergic agonists like bromocriptine and L-dopa without counteracting their central properties.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vave 10 mg is not to be used by patients with a known hypersensitivity to this medication or in the case of neonates. It should also be avoided in situations where gastrointestinal stimulation could be unsafe, such as in cases of gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation. Additionally, it is contraindicated in patients with a prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma).</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Vave 10 mg</strong></p><p>Side effects are uncommon, with occasional reports of transient intestinal cramps. Extrapyramidal symptoms are rare among young children and exceptionally rare in adults. These symptoms typically resolve spontaneously and completely upon discontinuing the treatment. Since the pituitary gland is situated outside the blood-brain barrier, domperidone may lead to an increase in plasma prolactin levels. In rare cases, this hyperprolactinemia can result in neuroendocrinological effects like galactorrhea and gynecomastia. In situations where the blood-brain barrier is immature (as in infants) or compromised, the possibility of neurological side effects cannot be entirely ruled out. Additionally, there have been occasional reports of rare allergic reactions, including rash and urticaria.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of using Vave 10 mg during pregnancy has not been established; hence, its use is not recommended during pregnancy. Animal studies have not shown any teratogenic effects on the fetus. Domperidone may induce galactorrhea and enhance post-natal lactation. It is excreted in breast milk in minimal quantities, which are insufficient to be considered harmful.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Vave 10 mg should be employed with utmost care in children due to the potential for an increased risk of extrapyramidal reactions, which can be more pronounced in young children with an incompletely developed blood-brain barrier. Additionally, considering that domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should also be used cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Vave 10 mg</strong></p><p>Signs of an overdose can manifest as drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions, particularly in children. If an overdose occurs, it is advisable to administer activated charcoal and closely monitor the patient. The use of anticholinergic drugs, antiparkinson drugs, or antihistamines with anticholinergic properties may be beneficial in managing extrapyramidal reactions.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, Protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Vave 10 mg acts as a dopamine antagonist, primarily by blocking dopamine receptors located in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its gastroprokinetic effect is achieved by inhibiting dopamine receptors that influence the motility of the gastrointestinal tract. Because it has limited ability to penetrate the blood-brain barrier, Domperidone has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects. Domperidone restores normal motility and tone in the upper gastrointestinal tract, promotes gastric emptying, enhances antral and duodenal peristalsis, and regulates pylorus contraction. Additionally, it increases esophageal peristalsis and lowers esophageal sphincter pressure, preventing the regurgitation of gastric contents.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Use in infants: Given that the metabolic and blood-brain barrier functions are not fully developed during the first months of life, any medication should be administered to infants with great caution and under close medical supervision. Although the typical absence of neurological side effects with domperidone is primarily due to its limited penetration through the blood-brain barrier, the potential occurrence of such effects cannot be entirely ruled out in infants under 1 year of age.</p><p><strong>Use in liver disorders:</strong></p><p>Because domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should be employed cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Use in kidney disorders:</strong></p><p>In individuals with severe renal insufficiency (serum creatinine &gt;6 mg/100ml, i.e., &gt;0.6 mmol/l), the elimination half-life of domperidone increases from 7.4 to 20.8 hours, although plasma drug levels remain lower than in healthy volunteers. Since very little unchanged drug is excreted through the kidneys, it is unlikely that the dose of a single acute administration needs to be adjusted in patients with renal insufficiency. However, for repeated administration, the dosing frequency should be reduced to once or twice daily, depending on the severity of the impairment, and the dose may need to be lowered. In general, patients on prolonged therapy should undergo regular reviews.</p>', 'active', '753_1742586835.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:53:55', '2025-05-04 22:48:36'),
(4065, 'Lysivin', 'Multivitamin + L-Lysine', 4.00, 30, 'Tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', 'N/A', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Lysivin</strong></p><p>Lysivin is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Improves appetite</li><li>Promotes growth in height &amp; weight</li><li>Helps in building of muscle protein</li><li>Helps to absorb Calcium</li><li>Ensures energy production</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each Tablet contains:</p><ul><li>L-Lysine 50 mg</li><li>Vitamin A 1500 IU</li><li>Vitamin D3 100 IU</li><li>Vitamin E 10 IU</li><li>Vitamin B1 0.25 mg</li><li>Vitamin B2 0.25 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 0.25 mg</li><li>Vitamin B12 2 mcg</li><li>Vitamin C 50 mg</li><li>Nicotinamide 2.5 mg</li><li>Folic Acid 0.25 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Lysivin</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: 03 Tablets daily</li><li><strong>Children (from 01 year of age)</strong>: 1-3 Tablets daily depending on the age &amp; physical condition</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>The product is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to any of the active ingredients of the product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Lysivin</strong></p><p>The product is usually well tolerated and exerts no untoward effects if taken in the dosage recommended.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The specific information is not available in this respect.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep container well closed after taking medicine.</li><li>Store below 25°C.</li><li>Protect from light &amp; moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The specific information is not available in this respect.</p>', 'active', '1751_1742586940.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:55:40', '2025-04-19 08:20:17'),
(4066, 'Ternilla', 'Aceclofenac', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ternilla 100 mg</strong></p><p>Ternilla 100 mg is indicated for the relief of pain and inflammation in osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, toothache, trauma, and lumbago.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Ternilla 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ternilla 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Extended release tablet:</strong> The recommended dose in adults is one 200 mg Ternilla 100 mg tablet daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Film coated tablet:</strong> The recommended dose in adults is 100 mg, twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ternilla 100 mg</strong></p><p>No significant drug interactions have been observed, but close monitoring of patients is required when it is used with:</p><ul><li><strong>Lithium and Digoxin:</strong> May increase plasma concentration of lithium and digoxin.</li><li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> May interact with the activity of diuretics.</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants:</strong> May enhance the activity of anticoagulants.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate:</strong> May increase the plasma level of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Ternilla 100 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to it or in whom aspirin or NSAIDs precipitate attacks of asthma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ternilla 100 mg</strong></p><p>Ternilla 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of Ternilla 100 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised with patients who have:</p><ul><li>Active or suspected peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding</li><li>Moderate to severe hepatic impairment</li><li>Cardiac or renal impairment</li><li>Dizziness or urticaria</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Ternilla 100 mg in children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Ternilla 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The use of Ternilla 100 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Ternilla 100 mg in children.</p>', 'active', '90_1742587047.webp', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:57:27', '2025-05-04 22:22:11'),
(4067, 'Fylox', 'Doxophylline', 8.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information on Fylox 200 mg with headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Fylox 200 mg</strong></p><p>Fylox 200 mg is used to treat the following indications:</p><ul><li>Bronchial asthma</li><li>Bronchospasm</li><li>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</li><li>Pulmonary disease with a spastic bronchial component</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Fylox 200 mg is a novel bronchodilator that structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. It selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. However, unlike Theophylline, Fylox 200 mg appears to have decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, potentially accounting for its improved safety profile. Additionally, it is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Fylox 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 400 mg tablet two or three times daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li></ul><blockquote><strong>Children:</strong>12 years of age: 10 ml syrup or 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</blockquote><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><br></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years of age: 6-9 mg/kg body weight two times daily, e.g., if body weight is 10 kg, 3 ml (60 mg) two times daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Fylox 200 mg</strong></p><p>Fylox 200 mg should not be administered together with other xanthine derivatives. Toxic synergism with ephedrine has been documented for xanthines. Like other xanthines, concomitant therapy with troleandomycin, lincomycin, clindamycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, ranitidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccine may decrease the hepatic clearance of xanthines, leading to an increase in blood levels.</p><p>No evidence of a relationship between Fylox 200 mg serum concentrations and toxic events has been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Fylox 200 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Acute myocardial infarction</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Lactating women</li><li>Patients who have shown hypersensitivity to its components</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Fylox 200 mg</strong></p><p>Fylox 200 mg rarely causes serious side effects; however, possible side effects are similar to taking excess amounts of caffeine. These include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Headache</li><li>Upset stomach</li><li>Heartburn</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Animal reproduction studies indicate that Fylox 200 mg does not cause fetal harm when administered to pregnant animals or affect reproduction capacity. However, due to limited experience in pregnant humans, xanthines should only be given to pregnant women when clearly necessary. Fylox 200 mg is contraindicated in nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The half-life of xanthine derivatives is influenced by several known variables. It may be prolonged in patients with liver disease, congestive heart failure, or those taking certain other drugs like erythromycin, troleandomycin, lincomycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccine. In these cases, a lower dose of Fylox 200 mg may be needed.</p><p>Phenytoin, other anticonvulsants, and smoking may cause an increase in clearance with a shorter mean half-life. In these cases, higher doses of Fylox 200 mg may be needed.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Fylox 200 mg</strong></p><p>In case of overdose, severe cardiac arrhythmias and tonic-clonic seizures may occur. These effects may represent the first signs of intoxication. The appearance of side effects may require discontinuation of treatment which, if necessary, may be resumed at lower doses after all signs and symptoms of toxicity have subsided. As there is no specific antidote, symptomatic treatment for cardiovascular collapse should be instituted.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children. Fylox 200 mg should be used only on the prescription of a specialist physician.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Fylox 200 mg is a novel bronchodilator. It structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. Fylox 200 mg selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, thereby relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. However, differently from Theophylline, Fylox 200 mg appears to have decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, which may account for the better safety profile of the drug. Fylox 200 mg is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p>', 'active', '1727_1742587122.webp', 10, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 00:58:42', '2025-05-03 18:06:53');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4068, 'Montex', 'Montelukast Sodium', 16.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Montex 10 mg</strong></p><p>Montex 10 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</strong></li><li><strong>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR)</strong>: Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. Cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) are located in the human airway, and these eicosanoids bind to them. The orally active drug Montex 10 mg has a strong affinity and specificity for the CysLT1 receptor. Without any agonist activity, it inhibits LTD4\'s physiological effects at the CysLT1 receptor.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Montex 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis</strong>:</li><li>The dosage for adults and adolescents 15 years of age and older: Montex 10 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For pediatric patients 6 to 14 years of age: Montex 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For pediatric patients 2 years to 5 years of age: Montex 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For pediatric patients 6 months to 5 years of age: Montex 4 mg oral granules once daily. This can be administered either directly in the mouth, or mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature.</li><li><strong>Use in the pediatric patient</strong>:</li><li>The safety and efficacy of Montex have been established in adequate and well-controlled studies in pediatric patients with asthma 6 months to 14 years of age. Safety and efficacy profiles in this age group are similar to those seen in adults.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage adjustment is required in patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage adjustment is recommended in patients with renal insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Elderly use</strong>:</li><li>The pharmacokinetic profile and the oral bioavailability of a single 10-mg oral dose of Montex are similar in elderly and younger adults. The plasma half-life of Montex is slightly longer in the elderly. No dosage adjustment in the elderly is required.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Montex 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>In the prevention and long-term treatment of asthma, Montex 10 mg has been provided alongside other commonly used treatments without seeming to increase the risk of negative side effects. According to studies on drug interactions, theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives (norethindrone 1mg/ethinyl estradiol 35mcg), terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin did not exhibit any clinically significant effects on the pharmacokinetics at the recommended clinical dose of it.</li><li>In clinical investigations, the medication was used concurrently with a variety of routinely prescribed medications without showing any signs of clinically harmful interactions, despite the fact that more detailed interaction studies were not carried out. Thyroid hormones, hypnotic sedatives, benzodiazepines, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and decongestants were among these drugs.</li><li>Following a single dose of this solution, the hepatic metabolism-inducing drug phenobarbital reduced the drug\'s AUC by about 40%. For this medication, no dosage modification is advised. When powerful cytochrome P450 enzyme inducers, such as phenobarbital or rifampin, are provided alongside this solution, it is fair to use proper clinical monitoring.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Those who have an extreme sensitivity to any ingredient in this medication should not take it.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Montex 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, asthenia, aberrant behavior, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, hemorrhage, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, edema, seizure, odd feeling, and sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, decreased attention, disorientation, erythema nodosum, hallucinations, hepatic problems, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, and tremor.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: Montex 10 mg passes the placenta in rats and rabbits, but no controlled human studies are available. Use during pregnancy should only be considered if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Although it is secreted in breast milk, the implications of this are not well understood. Caution is advised when administering this medication to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Acute Asthma Attacks</strong>: Montex 10 mg is not recommended for the reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks, including status asthmaticus. Patients should have access to the appropriate rescue medication.</li><li><strong>Acute Asthma Exacerbations</strong>: These can still be treated with other solutions.</li><li><strong>Corticosteroid Use</strong>: While the dosage of an inhaled corticosteroid may be gradually decreased under medical supervision, Montex 10 mg should not replace inhaled or oral corticosteroids suddenly.</li><li><strong>Aspirin Sensitivity</strong>: Patients with documented aspirin sensitivity should continue to avoid taking aspirin or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs while using Montex 10 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Montex 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose reports indicate no significant negative experiences. Common side effects reported are nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain. In case of overdose, standard supportive measures, including removal of unabsorbed material and clinical monitoring, should be used.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry environment, protecting from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Montex 10 mg inhibits the cysteinyl leukotriene receptor (CysLT1) in the airway, preventing the effects of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4), which contribute to the pathophysiology of asthma and allergic rhinitis. These leukotrienes are involved in airway edema, smooth muscle contraction, and inflammatory cellular activity, which lead to symptoms of asthma.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy Considerations</strong></p><ul><li>Montex 10 mg crosses the placenta in rats and rabbits. Use during pregnancy should be only when necessary, as controlled studies in humans are lacking. The drug is also secreted in human milk, so caution should be taken when administered to nursing mothers.</li></ul>', 'active', '980_1742587267.jpg', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:01:07', '2025-05-04 00:08:14'),
(4069, 'Myelin', 'Vitamin B1, B6 & B12', 9.00, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a box', 'Tablet', '100 mg+200 mg+200 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Myelin</strong></p><p>Myelin is indicated for the treatment of vitamin B1, B6, and B12 deficiency syndrome. It is also used for the supportive treatment of neuritis and non-inflammatory diseases of the nerves, such as diabetic neuropathy, peripheral neuralgia, lumbago, myalgia, optic neuritis, sciatica, facial neuralgia, intercostal neuralgia, and spinal pain.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate) 100 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride) 200 mg</li><li>Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) 200 micrograms</li></ul><p>Each 3 ml ampoule contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) 100 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) 100 mg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) 1000 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Myelin is a combined preparation of Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate), Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride), and Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin). These vitamins are essential coenzymes in the metabolism of the nervous system, supporting nerve fiber regeneration and the repair of the myelin sheath by activating nerve cell metabolism and natural repair mechanisms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Myelin</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablets</strong>: 1 to 3 tablets per day or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly (deep intragluteal).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In severe cases: 1 ampoule daily until the acute symptoms subside.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For milder cases and follow-up therapy: 2 to 3 ampoules per week.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Children</strong></p><p>There is no information available on the use of this drug in children.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Myelin</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported yet.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin B1, Vitamin B6, and Vitamin B12 are contraindicated in patients on levodopa therapy.</li><li>Contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Myelin</strong></p><p>Generally well tolerated, but allergic reactions may be observed in some cases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>No risks have been reported associated with the use of this combination during pregnancy at the recommended dosage.</li><li>Vitamin B1, B6, and B12 are secreted into breast milk, but the risks of overdose for the infant are not known.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Cyanocobalamin should not be given to patients with subacute degeneration of the spinal cord.</li><li>Cyanocobalamin is not suitable for the treatment of optic neuropathies associated with raised plasma concentrations of cyanocobalamin.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Myelin</strong></p><p>No overdose symptoms are expected at the recommended dosage. In case of known overdose, treatment should be symptomatic and supportive.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Store in a cool place (below 25°C) and dry, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin B1</strong>: Converts carbohydrates, fatty acids, and amino acids into energy, promotes healthy nerves, improves mood, and strengthens the heart.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6</strong>: Forms RBCs, helps cells make proteins, manufactures neurotransmitters like serotonin, releases stored forms of energy, prevents cardiovascular diseases and stroke, helps alleviate depression and insomnia.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12</strong>: Essential for cell replication and RBC production, prevents anemia, reduces nerve pain, numbness, and tingling, and lowers the risk of heart diseases.</li></ul><p>The vitamin ingredients are absorbed well when taken orally and are widely distributed to tissues, including breast milk. Thiamine is primarily present in the body as diphosphate and is excreted in urine when in excess. Pyridoxine, pyridoxal, and pyridoxamine are absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and converted to active forms, stored mainly in the liver, and excreted as inactive metabolites in the urine.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>The oral tablet form is recommended, but due to the presence of benzyl alcohol, the injection form is not recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul>', 'active', '1285_1742587438.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:03:58', '2025-05-04 00:12:11'),
(4070, 'Dexogut', 'Dexlansoprazole', 11.50, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '30 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Dexogut 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis (EE):</strong> Dexogut 30 mg is indicated for the healing of all grades of erosive esophagitis for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> Dexogut 30 mg is indicated to maintain healing of EE and relief of heartburn for up to 6 months.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD):</strong> Dexogut 30 mg is indicated for the treatment of heartburn associated with symptomatic non-erosive GERD for 4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Dexogut 30 mg is a Proton Pump Inhibitor that suppresses gastric acid secretion by specifically inhibiting the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole, a racemic mixture of R- and S-enantiomers. The dual delayed-release formulation in the capsule provides a plasma concentration-time profile with two distinct peaks. Dexogut 30 mg is extensively metabolized in the liver and excreted in urine.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Dexogut 30 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis and Relief of Heartburn:</strong> 30 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive GERD:</strong> 30 mg once daily for 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 60 mg once daily for up to 8 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Dexogut 30 mg:</strong></p><p>Dexogut 30 mg can be taken without regard to food. Swallow the capsule whole, or open the capsule and sprinkle intact granules on one tablespoon of food, and swallow immediately (granules should not be chewed). If a dose is missed, take it as soon as possible unless it\'s close to the time of the next dose.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Dexogut 30 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Medicines:</strong> Atazanavir, Warfarin, Tacrolimus, Clopidogrel, Methotrexate.</li><li><strong>Food &amp; Others:</strong> No data available.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Dexogut 30 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Dexogut 30 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects: Diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, flatulence.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>There are no adequate or well-controlled studies in pregnant women. It should be used during pregnancy only if the benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li>Dexogut 30 mg is excreted in rat milk, but it is unknown whether it is excreted in human milk. It should be avoided during breastfeeding unless necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Gastric Malignancy</li><li>Clostridium difficile Associated Diarrhea</li><li>Bone Fracture</li><li>Hypomagnesemia</li><li>Concomitant use with Methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Dexogut 30 mg:</strong></p><p>There have been no reports of significant overdose. Even multiple doses of 120 mg or a single dose of 300 mg did not result in severe adverse events.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, in a dry place, and protected from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics:</strong> No dosage adjustment is necessary for elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in patients under 12 years of age have not been established.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment is necessary.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No adjustment is needed for mild hepatic impairment. For moderate hepatic impairment, a maximum daily dose of 30 mg may be considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Dexogut 30 mg inhibits gastric acid secretion by blocking the proton pump (H+/K+-ATPase) in gastric parietal cells, preventing acid production.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy Category:</strong></p><p>Category B - No adequate or well-controlled studies in pregnant women.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong></p><p>Safety and effectiveness have not been established in patients under 12 years old.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment necessary.</p><p><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment necessary.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong></p><p>For moderate impairment, a maximum daily dose of 30 mg may be considered.</p>', 'active', '481_1742587521.webp', 17, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:05:21', '2025-05-03 08:22:19'),
(4071, 'iMAX', 'Esomeprazole', 5.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of iMAX 20 mg</strong></p><p>iMAX 20 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>To alleviate GERD-related symptoms, such as persistent heartburn and others.</li><li>To treat erosive esophagitis.</li><li>To maintain the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>For the eradication of <em>Helicobacter pylori</em> infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>For the treatment of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>For duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of iMAX 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. Most patients heal within this timeframe. If not, consider an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (Clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely, consider an additional 4 weeks of treatment.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori Eradication</strong>: Triple Therapy with Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily, Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily, and Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg once daily. Adjust dosage individually and continue treatment as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: 40 mg iMAX 20 mg once daily, given via intravenous injection (minimum 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10-30 minutes).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of iMAX 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take one iMAX 20 mg pill or capsule whole, at least 1 hour before meals.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mix the entire packet’s contents in 15 ml of water.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Stir thoroughly and wait 2-3 minutes for thickening.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Stir again and consume within 30 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If the mixture remains, add water, swirl, and drink immediately.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For nasogastric or gastric tube administration, use a syringe to administer the suspension after it has thickened.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong>: Reconstitute with 5 ml of solvent (WFI) and administer over at least 3 minutes. Discard the reconstituted solution if not used within 12 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of iMAX 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>iMAX 20 mg is metabolized primarily by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 enzymes.</li><li>Esomeprazole does not clinically interact with drugs metabolized by other enzymes like CYP1A2, 2A6, 2C9, 2D6, and 2E1.</li><li>Co-administration with diazepam may increase its plasma levels due to reduced clearance.</li><li>Esomeprazole can reduce the absorption of certain medications, like ketoconazole, iron salts, and digoxin, whose bioavailability is pH-dependent.</li><li>Co-administration with clarithromycin and amoxicillin may increase plasma levels of 14-hydroxy clarithromycin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of iMAX 20 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of iMAX 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, and dry mouth.</li><li>Long-term treatment (up to 12 months) generally does not result in a different type of side effects compared to short-term use.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>A positive response to iMAX 20 mg does not rule out the possibility of stomach cancer.</li><li>Patients with difficulty swallowing capsules may open the capsule and mix the pellets with a spoonful of applesauce for easier consumption.</li><li>Do not chew or crush the pellets, and use only cold applesauce that is soft enough to swallow.</li><li>Antacids are acceptable during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of iMAX 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose include reduced motor activity, tremors, ataxia, and convulsions.</li><li>There are no specific countermeasures, and dialysis is not expected to remove esomeprazole due to its high protein binding.</li><li>Symptomatic and supportive care is recommended in the case of an overdose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry area, at temperatures not exceeding 30°C, away from moisture and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>iMAX 20 mg inhibits H+/K+-ATPase in gastric parietal cells, reducing stomach acid output.</li><li>It is the S-isomer of omeprazole, providing superior acid control over racemic proton pump inhibitors.</li><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: Peak plasma levels occur about 1.5 hours after oral dosing. The bioavailability increases with the dose.</li><li><strong>Excretion</strong>: 80% is excreted in the urine as inactive metabolites, with the remainder in the feces.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Insufficient and unreliable research on the use of esomeprazole during pregnancy.</li><li>No teratogenic effects have been observed in animals, but breastfeeding should be discontinued if esomeprazole is used.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric and Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Pediatric safety and efficacy have not been established.</li><li>In elderly patients, no significant differences in safety and efficacy have been noted, although they may be more sensitive to the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic and Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage adjustment is needed for mild to moderate hepatic insufficiency.</li><li>In severe hepatic impairment, do not exceed 20 mg once daily.</li><li>Renal impairment does not significantly affect the pharmacokinetics of esomeprazole.</li></ul>', 'active', '915_1742587601.webp', 33, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:06:41', '2025-05-03 21:00:53'),
(4072, 'Ticamet', 'Salmeterol + Fluticasone Propionate', 797.39, 1, 'x 120 metered doses', '120 metered doses', 'Inhaler', '(25 mcg+250 mcg)/puff', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Ticamet 25 mcg + 250 mcg</h3><p>Ticamet is indicated for the regular treatment of asthma where the use of a combination product (long-acting β2-agonist and inhaled corticosteroid) is appropriate. It is prescribed for:</p><ul><li>Patients not adequately controlled with inhaled corticosteroids and \"as needed\" inhaled short-acting β2-agonist.</li><li>Patients already adequately controlled on both inhaled corticosteroid and long-acting β2-agonist.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants</li><li>Respiratory corticosteroids</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate</strong>: A selective, long-acting β2-agonist used in the treatment of asthma and other forms of diffuse airways obstruction. Salmeterol protects against symptoms.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: A corticosteroid with primarily glucocorticoid activity. It exerts a topical effect on the lungs, reducing inflammation, improving lung function, and preventing exacerbations without systemic effects when used at the recommended dose.</li></ul><p>Both drugs combined offer a convenient regimen for patients who need both β-agonist and inhaled corticosteroid therapy.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Inhalation Aerosol</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily, or 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 125 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily, or 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 250 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4-12 years)</strong>: 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Capsule (For Asthma)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg or Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 250 µg twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric patients (4-11 years)</strong>: For asthma not controlled with an inhaled corticosteroid, the dosage is Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Maxhaler (For Asthma)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: One inhalation of 50/100, 50/250, or 50/500 Maxhaler twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4 years and older)</strong>: One inhalation of 50/100 Maxhaler twice daily.</li></ul><h3>Administration Instructions</h3><ol><li><strong>Shake the inhaler</strong> at least six times before use.</li><li><strong>Breathe out fully</strong> and hold the inhaler upright.</li><li><strong>Place the inhaler</strong> between the teeth and close lips around the mouthpiece.</li><li><strong>Press down firmly</strong> on the canister while breathing in deeply and slowly.</li><li><strong>Hold your breath</strong> for at least 10 seconds.</li><li>If more than one puff is prescribed, <strong>wait 1 minute</strong> between puffs.</li></ol><h3>Interaction</h3><ul><li>Non-selective and selective β-blockers should be avoided in patients with asthma unless there are compelling reasons.</li><li>Use caution when co-administering with strong <strong>CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., ketoconazole, ritonavir), as it may increase systemic exposure to Fluticasone Propionate.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol</strong>: Tremor, palpitations, headache, cardiac arrhythmias, muscle cramps, arthralgia, and hypersensitivity reactions.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: Hoarseness, thrush (oral candidiasis), and cutaneous hypersensitivity reactions.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: The use of Ticamet during pregnancy should only be considered if the expected benefit to the mother outweighs any potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Limited data available; the use of Ticamet during breastfeeding should be evaluated with caution.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with active or quiescent pulmonary tuberculosis.</li><li>Monitor patients with thyrotoxicosis.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C and keep away from direct sunlight and heat.</li><li>Do not puncture or incinerate the canister.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use</strong>: Inhaled corticosteroids may reduce growth velocity in children. The long-term effects on adult height are unknown.</li></ul>', 'active', '628_1742587708.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:08:28', '2025-05-04 22:25:09'),
(4073, 'V3N', 'Vitamin B1, B6 & B12', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg+200 mg+200 mcg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of V3N 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg</strong></p><p>V3N 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg is indicated for the treatment of Vitamin B1, B6, and B12 deficiency syndrome. It is also used for the supportive treatment of neuritis and non-inflammatory diseases of the nerves, such as:</p><ul><li>Diabetic neuropathy</li><li>Peripheral neuralgia</li><li>Lumbago</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Optic neuritis</li><li>Sciatica</li><li>Facial neuralgia</li><li>Intercostal neuralgia</li><li>Spinal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate) 100 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride) 200 mg</li><li>Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) 200 mcg</li></ul><p>Each 3 ml ampoule contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) 100 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) 100 mg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) 1000 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>V3N 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg is a combined preparation of Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate), Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride), and Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin). These vitamins play an essential role as coenzymes in the metabolism of the nervous system, supporting the regeneration of nerve fibers and myelin sheaths by activating nerve cell metabolism and natural repair mechanisms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Tablets:</strong></p><p>1 to 3 tablets per day or as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Injection:</strong></p><ul><li>Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly (deep intragluteal).</li><li>In severe cases, 1 ampoule daily until the acute symptoms subside.</li><li>For milder cases and follow-up therapy, 2 to 3 ampoules per week.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage for V3N 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg</strong></p><p><strong>Tablet:</strong></p><p>1 to 3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician.</p><p><strong>Injection:</strong></p><ul><li>In severe (acute) cases: 1 injection daily until the acute symptoms subside, or as advised by the physician.</li><li>In mild cases: 1 injection 2-3 times per week.</li><li>Ampoules should be injected intramuscularly.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in children:</strong></p><p>No information is available on the use of this drug in children.</p><p><strong>Interactions of V3N 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported yet.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>This medication is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients on levodopa therapy.</li><li>Patients with hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of the preparation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of V3N 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg</strong></p><p>Generally well tolerated, but allergic reactions may be observed in some cases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>No risks have been identified with the use of this combination during pregnancy at the recommended dosage.</li><li>Vitamin B1, B6, and B12 are secreted into breast milk, but the risks of overdose for the infant are not known.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Cyanocobalamin should not be given to patients with subacute degeneration of the spinal cord.</li><li>Cyanocobalamin is not suitable for the treatment of optic neuropathies associated with raised plasma concentrations of cyanocobalamin.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of V3N 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg</strong></p><p>No overdose symptoms are expected with the recommended dosage. If an overdose occurs, treatment is symptomatic and supportive.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Store in a cool (below 25°C) and dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin B1</strong>: Converts carbohydrates, fatty acids, and amino acids into energy, promotes healthy nerves, improves mood, and strengthens the heart.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6</strong>: Forms RBCs, helps cells make proteins, manufactures neurotransmitters like serotonin, releases stored energy, helps prevent cardiovascular diseases and strokes, eases insomnia and depression.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12</strong>: Essential for cell replication, RBC production, prevents anemia, reduces nerve pain, numbness, tingling, and lowers the risk of heart diseases.</li></ul><p>The vitamins are absorbed well when taken orally and are widely distributed to most tissues, appearing in breast milk. Thiamine is not stored significantly in the body and excess amounts are excreted in urine. Pyridoxine is stored mainly in the liver and excess amounts are excreted in urine.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The oral tablet form is recommended, but due to the presence of benzyl alcohol, injections are not recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</p>', 'active', '597_1742587799.webp', 11, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:09:59', '2025-05-04 22:47:18'),
(4074, 'Pantid', 'Pantoprazole Sodium', 6.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pantid 20 mg</strong></p><p>Pantid 20 mg is recommended when inhibiting acid secretion is therapeutically beneficial, including for:</p><ul><li>Stomach ulcer disorders</li><li>Illnesses caused by gastroesophageal reflux</li><li>Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug-induced ulcers (NSAIDs)</li><li>Helicobacter pylori eradication (in combination with antibiotics)</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Blocker</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Pantid 20 mg is a proton pump inhibitor that works by covalently attaching to the H+/K+ATPase enzyme system on the surface of the gastric parietal cell to decrease the final stage of gastric acid generation. This effect suppresses both basal and induced stomach acid secretion, lasting more than 24 hours, regardless of the stimulus.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Pantid 20 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Oral:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Benign gastric ulcer:</strong> 40 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, continued for another 4 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease:</strong> 20-40 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, continued for another 4 weeks if not fully healed. Maintenance dose is 20 mg daily, which may be increased to 40 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer:</strong> 40 mg daily in the morning for 2 weeks, continued for another 2 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer associated with Helicobacter pylori:</strong> 40 mg twice daily in combination with antimicrobial agents:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Amoxycillin 1 g and Clarithromycin 500 mg both twice daily for one week, or</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clarithromycin 250 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg both twice daily for one week.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of NSAID-associated gastric or duodenal ulcer:</strong> 20 mg daily for those requiring long-term NSAID treatment.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> Initially 80 mg once daily, adjusted according to response (elderly max. 40 mg daily); daily doses above 80 mg should be given in two divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>IV Injection:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer and gastric ulcer:</strong> 40 mg once daily for 7-10 days</li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease with erosive esophagitis:</strong> 40 mg once daily for 7-10 days</li><li><strong>Prevention of rebleeding in peptic ulcer:</strong> IV 80 mg, followed by 8 mg/hour infusion for 72 hours</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration:</strong> 80 mg IV every 12 hours for 24 hours, followed by 40 mg every 12 hours</li><li><strong>Long-term management of Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome and other pathological hypersecretory conditions:</strong> 80 mg IV every 12 hours, may increase to 80 mg every 8 hours, may titrate to higher doses depending on acid output.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Pantid 20 mg</strong></p><p>In clinical investigations, no significant medication interactions were found.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Pantid 20 mg should not be taken by those with known hypersensitivity to any of the formulation\'s ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Pantid 20 mg</strong></p><p>Pantid 20 mg is generally well tolerated both in the short-term and long-term. The most frequent side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Other rare side effects include:</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Rash</li><li>Sleeplessness</li><li>Hyperglycemia</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pantid 20 mg is classified as a <strong>B</strong> pregnancy drug by the US FDA. However, there is limited controlled research on pregnant women. It should only be used during pregnancy if clearly necessary. Pantid 20 mg is excreted in human milk, so a decision should be made whether to stop breastfeeding or discontinue the medication, based on the benefit to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Do not split, chew, or crush the Pantid 20 mg pill.</li><li>Long-term use of Pantid 20 mg may cause malabsorption of cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) or increase the risk of osteoporosis-related issues.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Pantid 20 mg</strong></p><p>No recognized signs of human overdose. Due to its strong protein binding, Pantid 20 mg is not easily dialyzable. No specific therapy is advised, but symptomatic and supportive management should be provided.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p><strong>Direction for use of IV injection:</strong></p><p>Pantid 20 mg lyophilized powder with 0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection must only be administered intravenously. Reconstitute the powder-containing vial with 10 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride injection to create the IV solution. Upon reconstitution, administer gradually over a minimum of 2 to 5 minutes. Use only freshly made solutions, and the reconstituted solution can be kept at room temperature (up to 30°C) for up to 4 hours.</p><p><strong>How to administer an IV infusion:</strong></p><p>An intravenous infusion should be administered over 15 minutes. The solution should be reconstituted with 10 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride injection, then further diluted (admixed) to a final volume of 100 ml using 0.9% sodium chloride injection, 5% dextrose, or lactated ringer’s injection. Prior to further dilution, the reconstituted solution may be kept at room temperature for a maximum of 4 hours. The admixed solution must be used within 24 hours and can be stored at room temperature (up to 30°C).</p>', 'active', '1834_1742587892.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:11:32', '2025-05-04 10:14:23');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4075, 'Apuldon', 'Domperidone Maleate', 3.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Here is the information with the headings in bold as you requested:</p><p><strong>Indications of Apuldon 10 mg</strong></p><p>Dyspeptic symptom complex, often linked with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, may include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric sensation of fullness</li><li>Abdominal distension</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Belching</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Early satiety</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms can occur in various conditions, such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine. Additionally, in Parkinson\'s disease, it can manifest as dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting. For radiological studies, it can be used to speed up barium transit in follow-through radiological examinations.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Apuldon 10 mg functions as a dopamine receptor antagonist. It exerts gastroprokinetic action by inhibiting dopamine receptors found in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier means it has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Apuldon 10 mg</strong></p><p>For adults, the recommended dosage is 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily. Children should take 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily. When used for acute nausea and vomiting, the maximum treatment duration should not exceed 12 weeks. It is advisable to take this medication 15-30 minutes before a meal.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Apuldon 10 mg</strong></p><p>Apuldon 10 mg should be taken 15-30 minutes before meals, and if needed, before bedtime. The typical recommended oral dosage of Domperidone is as follows:</p><p><strong>For Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li>The maximum daily dose of Domperidone is 80 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg of body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>In cases of dyspeptic symptoms:</p><p><strong>For Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>For acute and sub-acute conditions, especially in cases of acute nausea and vomiting:</p><p><strong>For Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>When administered rectally in the form of suppositories:</p><p><strong>For Adults (including elderly):</strong></p><ul><li>30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>The maximum daily rectal dose for children weighing between 10 to 25 kg is 30 mg. The dose may be divided throughout the day if necessary.</li><li>Please note that the maximum treatment period for acute nausea and vomiting is 12 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Apuldon 10 mg</strong></p><p>The simultaneous use of anticholinergic drugs may counteract the antidyspeptic effects of domperidone. It\'s important to note that antacids and antisecretory drugs should not be administered concurrently with domperidone as they can reduce its oral bioavailability. Domperidone primarily undergoes metabolism through the CYP3A4 enzyme. In vitro data suggest that coadministration with drugs significantly inhibiting this enzyme may lead to elevated plasma levels of domperidone. Examples of CYP3A4 inhibitors include azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, nefazodone, and others.</p><p>Theoretically, as domperidone has gastro-kinetic effects, it could potentially impact the absorption of concurrently orally administered drugs, especially those with sustained release or enteric-coated formulations. However, in patients already stabilized on medications like digoxin or paracetamol, the concurrent use of domperidone did not affect the blood levels of these drugs. Domperidone does not potentiate the action of neuroleptics, and it suppresses the unwanted peripheral effects such as digestive disorders, nausea, and vomiting caused by dopaminergic agonists like bromocriptine and L-dopa without counteracting their central properties.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Apuldon 10 mg is not to be used by patients with a known hypersensitivity to this medication or in the case of neonates. It should also be avoided in situations where gastrointestinal stimulation could be unsafe, such as in cases of gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation. Additionally, it is contraindicated in patients with a prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma).</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Apuldon 10 mg</strong></p><p>Side effects are uncommon, with occasional reports of transient intestinal cramps. Extrapyramidal symptoms are rare among young children and exceptionally rare in adults. These symptoms typically resolve spontaneously and completely upon discontinuing the treatment. Since the pituitary gland is situated outside the blood-brain barrier, domperidone may lead to an increase in plasma prolactin levels. In rare cases, this hyperprolactinemia can result in neuroendocrinological effects like galactorrhea and gynecomastia. In situations where the blood-brain barrier is immature (as in infants) or compromised, the possibility of neurological side effects cannot be entirely ruled out. Additionally, there have been occasional reports of rare allergic reactions, including rash and urticaria.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of using Apuldon 10 mg during pregnancy has not been established; hence, its use is not recommended during pregnancy. Animal studies have not shown any teratogenic effects on the fetus. Domperidone may induce galactorrhea and enhance post-natal lactation. It is excreted in breast milk in minimal quantities, which are insufficient to be considered harmful.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Apuldon 10 mg should be employed with utmost care in children due to the potential for an increased risk of extrapyramidal reactions, which can be more pronounced in young children with an incompletely developed blood-brain barrier. Additionally, considering that domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should also be used cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Apuldon 10 mg</strong></p><p>Signs of an overdose can manifest as drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions, particularly in children. If an overdose occurs, it is advisable to administer activated charcoal and closely monitor the patient. The use of anticholinergic drugs, antiparkinson drugs, or antihistamines with anticholinergic properties may be beneficial in managing extrapyramidal reactions.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, Protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Apuldon 10 mg acts as a dopamine antagonist, primarily by blocking dopamine receptors located in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its gastroprokinetic effect is achieved by inhibiting dopamine receptors that influence the motility of the gastrointestinal tract. Because it has limited ability to penetrate the blood-brain barrier, Domperidone has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects. Domperidone restores normal motility and tone in the upper gastrointestinal tract, promotes gastric emptying, enhances antral and duodenal peristalsis, and regulates pylorus contraction. Additionally, it increases esophageal peristalsis and lowers esophageal sphincter pressure, preventing the regurgitation of gastric contents.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p><strong>Use in infants:</strong> Given that the metabolic and blood-brain barrier functions are not fully developed during the first months of life, any medication should be administered to infants with great caution and under close medical supervision. Although the typical absence of neurological side effects with domperidone is primarily due to its limited penetration through the blood-brain barrier, the potential occurrence of such effects cannot be entirely ruled out in infants under 1 year of age.</p><p><strong>Use in liver disorders:</strong> Because domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should be employed cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Use in kidney disorders:</strong> In individuals with severe renal insufficiency (serum creatinine &gt;6 mg/100ml, i.e., &gt;0.6 mmol/l), the elimination half-life of domperidone increases from 7.4 to 20.8 hours, although plasma drug levels remain lower than in healthy volunteers. Since very little unchanged drug is excreted through the kidneys, it is unlikely that the dose of a single acute administration needs to be adjusted in patients with renal insufficiency. However, for repeated administration, the dosing frequency should be reduced to once or twice daily, depending on the severity of the impairment, and the dose may need to be lowered. In general, patients on prolonged therapy should undergo regular reviews.</p>', 'active', '1395_1742587982.webp', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:13:02', '2025-05-02 23:45:13'),
(4076, 'Sun D', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 15.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '40000 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sun D 40000 IU</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated in the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also indicated as an adjunct to specific therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcaemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with vitamin D3 deficiency. Colecalciferol is the synthetic form of Vitamin D, essential for normal bone growth, development, and maintenance of bone density. It also helps with the utilization of calcium and phosphorus. Vitamin D3 is crucial for babies\' healthy growth and development and acts as a hormone.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation</li><li>Vitamin D preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) helps with the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. Vitamin D3 is essential for normal bone growth and density maintenance. It also reduces the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, prevents the risk of cancer (breast, colorectal), and helps maintain adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sun D 40000 IU</strong></p><p><strong>Capsule:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance therapy:</strong> 1,400-2,000 IU/day. Measure 25-hydroxyvitamin D levels 3-4 months after initiating maintenance therapy to confirm target levels.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 20,000 IU every 4 weeks. Higher doses may be required in some situations.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adjunct to osteoporosis therapy:</strong> 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li><strong>Children (12-18 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 20,000 IU every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Film-coated Tablet:</strong></p><ul><li>1,000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily, or as directed by a physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li></ul><p><strong>Chewable Tablets:</strong></p><ul><li>1,000 IU to 2,000 IU daily, or as directed by a physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal. Swallow after chewing.</li></ul><p><strong>Syrup:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For patients at risk of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong>0-1 yr: 400 IU/day (2 ml).</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 yr: 600 IU/day (3 ml).</blockquote><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Vitamin D3 deficient patients:</strong>0-1 yr: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 yrs: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Injection:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prevention (for infants receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk):</strong> 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li><strong>Nursed infants, infants not receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk, or young children up to 5 years:</strong> 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li><strong>Adolescents:</strong> 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li><strong>For digestive disorders, antiepileptic treatment, or other specific conditions not listed:</strong> 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU or 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 3 or 6 months.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sun D 40000 IU</strong></p><p>It interferes with:</p><ul><li>Phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids</li><li>Certain laxatives (e.g., liquid paraffin)</li><li>Actinomycin</li><li>Imidazole antifungal agents</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sun D 40000 IU</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Hypercalcaemia</li><li>Hypercalciuria</li><li>Skin rash</li><li>Pruritus</li><li>Urticaria</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Abdominal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Studies show safe use of doses up to 4,000 IU during pregnancy.</li><li>The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU, but those with Vitamin D3 deficiency may require higher doses.</li><li>Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. Overdose in infants induced by nursing mothers has not been observed.</li><li>When prescribing additional Vitamin D3 to a breastfed child, consider the dose of Vitamin D3 given to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sun D 40000 IU</strong></p><ul><li>Can lead to hypervitaminosis D.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30ºC, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation</li><li>Vitamin D preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus, which are essential for bone health. It also helps reduce bacterial infection severity, improve lung function, prevent certain cancers, and maintain adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Safe up to 4,000 IU during pregnancy.</li><li>The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU, but those with deficiency may need higher doses. Always follow the advice of a medical practitioner.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</li></ul>', 'active', '137_1742588078.jpg', 33, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:14:38', '2025-05-04 21:27:11'),
(4077, 'E-Cap', 'Vitamin E [Alpha Tocopherol Acetate]', 8.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '600 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of E-Cap 600 IU</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>As a dietary supplement</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vitamin E deficiency resulting from impaired absorption</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increased requirements due to a diet rich in polyunsaturated fats</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For healthy hair and skin</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">As an antioxidant</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hemolytic anemia due to Vitamin E deficiency</li><li><strong>Therapeutic use</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Cardiovascular disease</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Heavy metal poisoning</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hepatotoxin poisoning</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hemolytic anemia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oxygen therapy</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In nutritional deficiency states</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Vitamin-E Preparations</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li>Vitamin E acts as an antioxidant in the body.</li><li>It protects polyunsaturated fatty acids (which are components of cellular membranes) and other oxygen-sensitive substances such as vitamin A &amp; vitamin C from oxidation.</li><li>In premature neonates, irritability, edema, thrombosis, and hemolytic anemia may occur due to Vitamin E deficiency.</li><li>Creatinuria, ceroid deposition, muscle weakness, decreased erythrocyte survival, or increased in vitro hemolysis by oxidizing agents have been identified in adults and children with low serum tocopherol concentrations.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of E-Cap 600 IU</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Betterment of cardiovascular health</strong>: 400 IU - 800 IU per day</li><li><strong>Deficiency syndrome in adults</strong>: 200 IU - 400 IU per day</li><li><strong>Deficiency syndrome in children</strong>: 200 IU per day</li><li><strong>Thalassemia</strong>: 800 IU per day</li><li><strong>Sickle-cell anemia</strong>: 400 IU per day</li><li><strong>Betterment of skin &amp; hair</strong>: 200 IU - 400 IU per day (Topical use is also established for beautification)</li><li><strong>Chronic cold in adults</strong>: 200 IU per day</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction of E-Cap 600 IU</strong></h3><ul><li>Vitamin E may impair the absorption of Vitamin A &amp; function of Vitamin K and potentiates the effect of Warfarin.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>No known contraindications found.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of E-Cap 600 IU</strong></h3><ul><li>Overdosage (&gt;1 gm) has been associated with minor side effects, including:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypertension</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fatigue</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diarrhea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Myopathy</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>Vitamin E is safe in pregnancy and lactation when used at recommended doses. Higher doses have not been established.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>It may increase the risk of thrombosis in some patients, such as those taking estrogens.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li>Vitamin E is safe for children.</li></ul>', 'active', '88_1742588174.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:16:14', '2025-05-01 22:22:11'),
(4078, 'Coralex DX', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 16.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Coralex DX 600 mg + 400 IU</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment &amp; prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, and disorders of osteogenesis. It is also used as a supplement in case of inadequate intake of calcium in childhood diets, rickets, pregnancy &amp; lactation, and for elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis, phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li><li>OR</li><li>Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 200 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate calcium in the blood is crucial to a variety of bodily functions, and the body will not tolerate a deficiency, even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is also essential for healthy bones, as it aids in calcium absorption from the GI tract and stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies show that calcium and vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth, osteoporosis, and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Coralex DX 600 mg + 400 IU</strong></p><ul><li>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night, or as directed by the physician</li><li>Dosage: One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or as directed by the physician. Taking on a full stomach ensures better absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Coralex DX 600 mg + 400 IU</strong></p><ul><li>Oral calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline and fluoride preparations. A minimum of 3 hours should be allowed between ingestion of these medications.</li><li>Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of calcium.</li><li>Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce the metabolism of vitamin D3.</li><li>Certain foods like spinach, cereals, and milk can reduce the intestinal uptake of calcium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the components</li><li>Hypocalcemia resulting from an overdose of Vitamin D3</li><li>Hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Bone metastases</li><li>Severe renal insufficiency</li><li>Severe hypercalciuria</li><li>Renal calculi</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Coralex DX 600 mg + 400 IU</strong></p><ul><li>Flatulence, diarrhea, constipation, upper GI discomfort, etc. are rare.</li><li>Hypercalcemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It should be used as directed by the physician during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with dose reduction may be needed.</li><li>Plasma and serum calcium levels should be monitored in cases of mild to moderate renal impairment and during long-term use.</li><li>Patients with renal stones or a previous history of such should take precautions.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Coralex DX 600 mg + 400 IU</strong></p><ul><li>At high doses, it may result in nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc.</li><li>Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at a temperature below 30°C. Protect from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This is a calcium and vitamin D3 preparation where calcium carbonate is sourced from coral. The calcium carbonate from coral has a chemical structure very similar to human bone composition. Coral calcium ensures better absorption compared to other sources. Vitamin D3 aids in calcium absorption from the GI tract and helps maintain calcium balance in the body.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of a physician.</p>', 'active', '1039_1742588293.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:18:13', '2025-05-03 01:40:24'),
(4079, 'Delfex', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '120 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Delfex 120 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Allergic Rhinitis</strong>: For adults and children aged 12 years and older, Delfex 120 mg is recommended to alleviate symptoms of seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis such as sneezing, rhinorrhea, lacrimation, itchy or red eyes, and itchy nose, palate, and throat. It improves work/activity productivity and health-related quality of life.</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>: For adults and children aged 12 years and older, Delfex 120 mg is also recommended for treating the symptoms of chronic idiopathic urticaria, significantly reducing wheals and pruritus, and improving work/activity productivity and health-related quality of life.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Delfex 120 mg is a selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist. After oral administration, it is rapidly absorbed, reaching peak plasma concentration in 2 to 3 hours. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Seasonal Allergic Rhinitis</strong>:</li><li>Adults and children 12 years and older:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">60 mg twice daily, or 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 60 mg once daily</li><li>Children 6 to 11 years:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">30 mg twice daily, or 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 30 mg once daily</li><li>Children 2 to 11 years:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Suspension: 30 mg (5 mL) twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 30 mg (5 mL) once daily</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>:</li><li>Adults and children 12 years and older:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">60 mg twice daily, or 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 60 mg once daily</li><li>Children 6 to 11 years:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">30 mg twice daily, or 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 30 mg once daily</li><li>Children 6 months to less than 2 years:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Suspension: 15 mg (2.5 mL) twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 15 mg (2.5 mL) once daily</li><li>Children 2 to 11 years:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Suspension: 30 mg (5 mL) twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 30 mg (5 mL) once daily</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Delfex 120 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Erythromycin or Ketoconazole</strong>: Co-administration with Delfex 120 mg may increase fexofenadine levels by 2 to 3 times without affecting the QT interval or increasing adverse reactions.</li><li><strong>Omeprazole</strong>: No interaction observed.</li><li><strong>Antacids (containing aluminum and magnesium hydroxide)</strong>: Fexofenadine’s bioavailability decreases when taken 15 minutes before antacids. It is advised to wait two hours after taking Delfex before using antacids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Delfex 120 mg or any of its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Delfex 120 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Headaches, fatigue, and vertigo. Gastrointestinal issues such as nausea.</li><li><strong>Unusual</strong>: Exhaustion.</li><li><strong>Post-marketing Surveillance</strong>: Hypersensitivity reactions like angioedema, chest tightness, dyspnea, flushing, and systemic anaphylaxis; mental health issues like anxiety, nightmares, insomnia, or excessive dreaming; cardiac problems like palpitations and tachycardia; gastrointestinal issues like diarrhea; skin disorders like rash, urticaria, and pruritus.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C by the US FDA. Avoid use during pregnancy unless necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Fexofenadine passes into breast milk, so it is not recommended for nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in elderly individuals and patients with renal or hepatic impairment.</li><li>Patients with a history of cardiovascular disease should be warned of potential tachycardia and palpitations.</li><li><strong>Driving and Operating Machinery</strong>: Unlikely to affect the ability to drive or operate machinery, but individual responses should be monitored.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Delfex 120 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms may include dry mouth, sleepiness, fatigue, and dizziness. Treatment should focus on symptomatic relief and psychological support. Fexofenadine is not significantly removed by hemodialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep dry and away from heat and light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Fexofenadine works as an inverse agonist of the H1 receptor, preventing histamine-induced allergic reactions without crossing the blood-brain barrier.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>In patients with renal impairment, a dose of 60 mg once daily is recommended. Fexofenadine’s pharmacokinetics are not significantly impacted by moderate to severe hepatic disease.</li><li>For patients over 65, the pharmacokinetics of fexofenadine are enhanced, and adverse effects are comparable to those seen in younger patients.</li></ul>', 'active', '790_1742588386.jpg', 33, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:19:46', '2025-05-03 07:52:30'),
(4080, 'D-Cap', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 35.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '40000 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of D-Cap 40000 IU</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated in the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also indicated as an adjunct to specific therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcaemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Colecalciferol, a synthetic form of Vitamin D, is essential for normal bone growth and development and helps maintain bone density. It is also necessary for the utilization of both calcium and phosphorus. Babies need Vitamin D3 for healthy growth and development, and it acts as a hormone.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is essential for normal bone growth and maintaining bone density. Vitamin D3 also reduces the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, prevents the risk of certain cancers (such as breast and colorectal cancer), and helps maintain adequate insulin levels in patients with type 2 diabetes.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of D-Cap 40000 IU</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>For capsule:AdultsTreatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 40000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks. Maintenance therapy is 1400-2000 IU/day. Measure 25-hydroxyvitamin D levels 3-4 months after starting maintenance therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 20000 IU every 4 weeks. Higher doses may be required in specific situations.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adjunct to osteoporosis therapy:</strong> 20000 IU once a month.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Children (12-18 years):Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 20000 IU every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 20000 IU every 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For film-coated tablet:</strong>1000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily, or as directed by the physician.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For oroflash or chewable tablets:</strong>1000 IU to 2000 IU daily, or as directed by the physician. Take the medicine with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>For syrup:Risk of cholecalciferol deficiency:</strong>0-1 yr: 400 IU/day (2 ml)</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 yr: 600 IU/day (3 ml)</blockquote><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>For cholecalciferol deficient patients:</strong>0-1 yr: 2000 IU/day (+50000 IU/week) for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 yrs: 2000 IU/day for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection (for prevention):Infants receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk:</strong> 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) i.e., 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Nursed infants or infants not receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk, or young children up to 5 years:</strong> 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e., 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adolescents:</strong> 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e., 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) i.e., 100,000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly:</strong> 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) i.e., 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For digestive disorders, antiepileptic treatment, or other special conditions:</strong> 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) i.e., 100,000 IU or 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e., 200,000 IU every 3 or 6 months.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of D-Cap 40000 IU</strong></p><p>It interferes with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (such as liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of D-Cap 40000 IU</strong></p><p>Common side effects include hypercalcaemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, and abdominal pain.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Studies have shown safe use of doses up to 4000 IU during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU; however, higher doses may be required for those considered Vitamin D3 deficient. Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. While overdose in infants induced by nursing mothers has not been observed, the practitioner should consider any additional Vitamin D3 given to the mother when prescribing to a breastfed child.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Use with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of D-Cap 40000 IU</strong></p><p>Can lead to hypervitaminosis D.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30ºC, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>Safety and efficacy in children under 12 years have not been established.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) helps with the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus, which are essential for normal bone growth and maintaining bone density. Additionally, it reduces bacterial infection severity, improves lung function, prevents certain cancers, and helps maintain insulin levels for type 2 diabetes patients.</p>', 'active', '546_1742588510.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:21:50', '2025-05-03 01:44:51'),
(4081, 'Motigut', 'Domperidone Maleate', 40.00, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '5 mg/5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p>Sure, here\'s the information you provided with the headings in <strong>bold</strong>:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Motigut 60 ml</strong></p><p>Dyspeptic symptom complex, often linked with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, may include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric sensation of fullness</li><li>Abdominal distension</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Belching</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Early satiety</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms can occur in various conditions, such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine. Additionally, in Parkinson\'s disease, it can manifest as dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting. For radiological studies, it can be used to speed up barium transit in follow-through radiological examinations.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Motigut 60 ml functions as a dopamine receptor antagonist. It exerts gastroprokinetic action by inhibiting dopamine receptors found in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier means it has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Motigut 60 ml</strong></p><p>For adults, the recommended dosage is 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily. Children should take 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily. When used for acute nausea and vomiting, the maximum treatment duration should not exceed 12 weeks. It is advisable to take this medication 15-30 minutes before a meal.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Motigut 60 ml</strong></p><p>Motigut 60 ml should be taken 15-30 minutes before meals, and if needed, before bedtime. The typical recommended oral dosage of Domperidone is as follows:</p><p><strong>For Adults:</strong></p><p>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</p><p>The maximum daily dose of Domperidone is 80 mg.</p><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><p>2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg of body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</p><p><strong>In cases of dyspeptic symptoms:</strong></p><p><strong>For Adults:</strong></p><p>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</p><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><p>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</p><p><strong>For acute and sub-acute conditions, especially in cases of acute nausea and vomiting:</strong></p><p><strong>For Adults:</strong></p><p>20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</p><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><p>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</p><p><strong>When administered rectally in the form of suppositories:</strong></p><p><strong>For Adults (including elderly):</strong></p><p>30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</p><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><p>The maximum daily rectal dose for children weighing between 10 to 25 kg is 30 mg. The dose may be divided throughout the day if necessary.</p><p>Please note that the maximum treatment period for acute nausea and vomiting is 12 weeks.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction of Motigut 60 ml</strong></p><p>The simultaneous use of anticholinergic drugs may counteract the antidyspeptic effects of domperidone. It\'s important to note that antacids and antisecretory drugs should not be administered concurrently with domperidone as they can reduce its oral bioavailability. Domperidone primarily undergoes metabolism through the CYP3A4 enzyme. In vitro data suggest that coadministration with drugs significantly inhibiting this enzyme may lead to elevated plasma levels of domperidone. Examples of CYP3A4 inhibitors include azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, nefazodone, and others.</p><p>Theoretically, as domperidone has gastro-kinetic effects, it could potentially impact the absorption of concurrently orally administered drugs, especially those with sustained release or enteric-coated formulations. However, in patients already stabilized on medications like digoxin or paracetamol, the concurrent use of domperidone did not affect the blood levels of these drugs. Domperidone does not potentiate the action of neuroleptics, and it suppresses the unwanted peripheral effects such as digestive disorders, nausea, and vomiting caused by dopaminergic agonists like bromocriptine and L-dopa without counteracting their central properties.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Motigut 60 ml is not to be used by patients with a known hypersensitivity to this medication or in the case of neonates. It should also be avoided in situations where gastrointestinal stimulation could be unsafe, such as in cases of gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation. Additionally, it is contraindicated in patients with a prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma).</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Motigut 60 ml</strong></p><p>Side effects are uncommon, with occasional reports of transient intestinal cramps. Extrapyramidal symptoms are rare among young children and exceptionally rare in adults. These symptoms typically resolve spontaneously and completely upon discontinuing the treatment. Since the pituitary gland is situated outside the blood-brain barrier, domperidone may lead to an increase in plasma prolactin levels. In rare cases, this hyperprolactinemia can result in neuroendocrinological effects like galactorrhea and gynecomastia. In situations where the blood-brain barrier is immature (as in infants) or compromised, the possibility of neurological side effects cannot be entirely ruled out. Additionally, there have been occasional reports of rare allergic reactions, including rash and urticaria.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of using Motigut 60 ml during pregnancy has not been established; hence, its use is not recommended during pregnancy. Animal studies have not shown any teratogenic effects on the fetus. Domperidone may induce galactorrhea and enhance post-natal lactation. It is excreted in breast milk in minimal quantities, which are insufficient to be considered harmful.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Motigut 60 ml should be employed with utmost care in children due to the potential for an increased risk of extrapyramidal reactions, which can be more pronounced in young children with an incompletely developed blood-brain barrier. Additionally, considering that domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should also be used cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Motigut 60 ml</strong></p><p>Signs of an overdose can manifest as drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions, particularly in children. If an overdose occurs, it is advisable to administer activated charcoal and closely monitor the patient. The use of anticholinergic drugs, antiparkinson drugs, or antihistamines with anticholinergic properties may be beneficial in managing extrapyramidal reactions.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, Protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Motigut 60 ml acts as a dopamine antagonist, primarily by blocking dopamine receptors located in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its gastroprokinetic effect is achieved by inhibiting dopamine receptors that influence the motility of the gastrointestinal tract. Because it has limited ability to penetrate the blood-brain barrier, Domperidone has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects. Domperidone restores normal motility and tone in the upper gastrointestinal tract, promotes gastric emptying, enhances antral and duodenal peristalsis, and regulates pylorus contraction. Additionally, it increases esophageal peristalsis and lowers esophageal sphincter pressure, preventing the regurgitation of gastric contents.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in infants:</strong> Given that the metabolic and blood-brain barrier functions are not fully developed during the first months of life, any medication should be administered to infants with great caution and under close medical supervision. Although the typical absence of neurological side effects with domperidone is primarily due to its limited penetration through the blood-brain barrier, the potential occurrence of such effects cannot be entirely ruled out in infants under 1 year of age.</li><li><strong>Use in liver disorders:</strong> Because domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should be employed cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Use in kidney disorders:</strong> In individuals with severe renal insufficiency (serum creatinine &gt;6 mg/100ml, i.e., &gt;0.6 mmol/l), the elimination half-life of domperidone increases from 7.4 to 20.8 hours, although plasma drug levels remain lower than in healthy volunteers. Since very little unchanged drug is excreted through the kidneys, it is unlikely that the dose of a single acute administration needs to be adjusted in patients with renal insufficiency. However, for repeated administration, the dosing frequency should be reduced to once or twice daily, depending on the severity of the impairment, and the dose may need to be lowered. In general, patients on prolonged therapy should undergo regular reviews.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you\'d like any further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '972_1742588703.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:25:03', '2025-05-04 00:09:23');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4082, 'Ferozi-CI', 'Carbonyl Iron + Folic Acid + Zinc Sulfate', 4.50, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50 mg+0.50 mg+61.80 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ferozi-CI</strong></p><p>It is indicated for the treatment and prophylaxis of Iron, Folic Acid, and Zinc deficiency, especially during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each capsule contains:</p><ul><li>Elemental iron 50 mg (As carbonyl iron INN)</li><li>Folic acid BP 500 microgram</li><li>Zinc sulphate monohydrate USP 61.80 mg (equivalent to 22.50 mg zinc)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined preparation</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Carbonyl Iron</strong>: A form of the mineral iron, essential for oxygen transport in the blood. It is used to prevent and treat iron deficiencies and iron deficiency anemia.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong>: Enhances chemical reactions for the production of red blood cells, DNA synthesis for cell replication, and amino acid metabolism (important for protein production).</li><li><strong>Zinc</strong>: Necessary for many physiological processes, including enzyme functions. Deficiency may cause poor night vision, slow wound healing, poor sexual development and function in males, poor appetite, decreased immune function, diarrhea, dermatitis, and retarded growth in children.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ferozi-CI</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Elderly</strong>: 1 capsule daily. In more severe cases, 2 capsules daily may be required.</li><li><strong>Children (Aged over 1 year)</strong>: 1 capsule daily. The capsule may be opened, and the pellets mixed with soft, cool food, but should not be chewed.</li><li><strong>Adult Dosage</strong>: One capsule daily before food or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ferozi-CI</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Carbonyl Iron</strong>: Decreases absorption of tetracycline antibiotics, quinolone antibiotics, levodopa, levothyroxine, methyldopa, and penicillamine.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong>: Interacts with antiepileptics, potentially reducing plasma concentrations of phenobarbital, phenytoin, and primidone.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of its components or those with iron overload.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ferozi-CI</strong></p><p>Gastrointestinal irritations such as nausea, anorexia, vomiting, discomfort, constipation, and diarrhea may occur. Patients may experience dark stool. Carbonyl Iron pellets incorporated into the capsules help reduce gastrointestinal irritation. Rare allergic reactions may also occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Use of any drug during the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided if possible. Therefore, the administration of iron during the first trimester requires evidence of iron deficiency. Prophylaxis of iron deficiency, where an inadequate diet calls for supplementary zinc and folic acid, is justified during the remainder of pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Special care should be taken in patients with iron overload states such as hemochromatosis, hemolytic anemia, or red blood cell aplasia. Failure to respond to treatment requires further investigations to exclude other causes of anemia. In patients with renal failure, there may be a risk of zinc accumulation.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ferozi-CI</strong></p><p>Symptoms of carbonyl iron overdose include decreased energy, nausea, abdominal pain, tarry stool, weak, rapid pulse, fever, coma, and seizures.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C. Keep away from light and moisture. Keep all medicines out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined preparation</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This timed-release capsule is a combined preparation of Carbonyl Iron, Folic Acid, and Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate. It contains Carbonyl Iron with not less than 98% iron content. Carbonyl Iron, with its high bioavailability and low toxicity, is a safer and more effective choice for iron supplementation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Use of any drug during the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided if possible. Administration of iron during the first trimester requires evidence of iron deficiency. Prophylaxis of iron deficiency, where inadequate diet calls for supplementary zinc and folic acid, is justified during the remainder of pregnancy.</p>', 'active', '1130_1742588810.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:26:50', '2025-05-03 10:36:21'),
(4083, 'Ace Power', 'Paracetamol', 2.25, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '1000 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ace Power 1000 mg</strong></p><p>Ace Power 1000 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Fever, common cold and influenza</li><li>Headache, toothache, earache, bodyache, myalgia, neuralgia, dysmenorrhoea</li><li>Sprains, colic pain, back pain, post-operative pain, postpartum pain, inflammatory pain</li><li>Post-vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic and osteoarthritic pain and stiffness of joints</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Ace Power 1000 mg has analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity. It works primarily in the CNS, increasing the pain threshold by inhibiting COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3 enzymes involved in prostaglandin synthesis. As a para-aminophenol derivative, it is one of the safest and fast-acting analgesics with minimal side effects compared to aspirin.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Tablet</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 1-2 tablets every 4-6 hours (maximum 4 gm or 8 tablets daily).</li><li><strong>Children (6-12 years)</strong>: ½ to 1 tablet 3-4 times daily. Do not exceed 2.6 gm/day for long-term use.</li></ul><p><strong>Extended Release Tablet</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years</strong>: Two tablets every 6-8 hours (maximum of 6 tablets daily). Do not crush the tablet.</li></ul><p><strong>Syrup/Suspension</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Children under 3 months</strong>: 10 mg/kg body weight (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced) 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>3 months to 1 year</strong>: ½ to 1 teaspoonful 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>1-5 years</strong>: 1-2 teaspoonfuls 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>6-12 years</strong>: 2 teaspoonfuls 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 4-8 teaspoonfuls 3-4 times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Suppository</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Children (3-12 months)</strong>: 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children (1-5 years)</strong>: 125-250 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children (6-12 years)</strong>: 250-500 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults &amp; children over 12 years</strong>: 0.5-1 gm, 4 times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Paediatric Drop</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Children under 3 months</strong>: 0.5 ml (40 mg)</li><li><strong>4-11 months</strong>: 1.0 ml (80 mg)</li><li><strong>1-2 years</strong>: 1.5 ml (120 mg).</li><li>Do not exceed more than 5 doses per day for a maximum of 5 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Ace Power 1000 mg with Actizorb Technology</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children (12 years and over)</strong>: 1-2 tablets every 4-6 hours as needed (maximum 8 caplets in 24 hours).</li><li><strong>Children (7-11 years)</strong>: ½-1 tablet every 4-6 hours (maximum 4 caplets in 24 hours).</li><li>Not recommended for children under 7 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Barbiturates, tricyclic antidepressants, and alcohol may reduce the ability to metabolize large doses.</li><li>Alcohol can increase hepatotoxicity with overdose.</li><li>Chronic use of anticonvulsants or oral contraceptives may increase the clearance, reducing the effectiveness of Ace Power 1000 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Ace Power 1000 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Generally mild, including:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Haematological reactions: Thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, pancytopenia, neutropenia, and agranulocytosis.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pancreatitis, skin rashes, and occasional allergic reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy Category B (USFDA). Use only if clearly needed.</li><li>Excreted in breast milk. Maternal use in normal therapeutic doses does not seem to present a risk to the infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution with patients who have impaired liver or kidney function.</li><li>Careful use in patients on other liver-affecting medications.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms within 24 hours: Pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, abdominal pain.</li><li>Liver damage may appear 12-48 hours after ingestion.</li><li>Possible abnormalities in glucose metabolism and metabolic acidosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Ace Power 1000 mg acts by inhibiting COX enzymes in the CNS, increasing the pain threshold. It has analgesic, antipyretic properties, and weak anti-inflammatory effects, making it a fast-acting and well-tolerated analgesic.</li></ul>', 'active', '1623_1742588902.jpg', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:28:22', '2025-05-02 22:45:48'),
(4084, 'Doxoven', 'Doxophylline', 8.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Doxoven 200 mg</strong></p><p>Doxoven 200 mg is used to treat the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Bronchial asthma</li><li>Bronchospasm</li><li>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</li><li>Pulmonary disease with a spastic bronchial component</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Doxoven 200 mg is a novel bronchodilator that structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. It selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. However, unlike Theophylline, Doxoven 200 mg appears to have decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, potentially accounting for its improved safety profile. Additionally, it is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Doxoven 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 400 mg tablet two or three times daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Children ( &gt;12 years of age)</strong>: 10 ml syrup or 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</li><li><strong>Children (6-12 years of age)</strong>: 6-9 mg/kg body weight two times daily (e.g., if body weight is 10 kg, 3 ml (60 mg) two times daily or as prescribed by the physician).</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Doxoven 200 mg</strong></p><p>Doxoven 200 mg should not be administered together with other xanthine derivatives. Toxic synergism with ephedrine has been documented for xanthines. Concomitant therapy with troleandomycin, lincomycin, clindamycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, ranitidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccine may decrease the hepatic clearance of xanthines, leading to an increase in blood levels.</p><p>There is no evidence of a relationship between Doxoven 200 mg serum concentrations and toxic events.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Doxoven 200 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Acute myocardial infarction</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Lactating women</li><li>Patients who have shown hypersensitivity to its components</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Doxoven 200 mg</strong></p><p>Doxoven 200 mg rarely causes serious side effects; however, possible side effects are similar to those from excessive caffeine intake, including:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Headache</li><li>Upset stomach</li><li>Heartburn</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Animal reproduction studies indicate that Doxoven 200 mg does not cause fetal harm when administered to pregnant animals or affect reproduction capacity. However, due to limited experience in pregnant humans, xanthines should only be given to pregnant women when clearly necessary. Doxoven 200 mg is contraindicated in nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The half-life of xanthine derivatives can be influenced by several variables. It may be prolonged in patients with liver disease, congestive heart failure, or those taking drugs such as erythromycin, troleandomycin, lincomycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, propanolol, and anti-flu vaccines. In these cases, a lower dose of Doxoven 200 mg may be needed. Conversely, phenytoin, other anticonvulsants, and smoking can increase clearance, potentially requiring higher doses.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Doxoven 200 mg</strong></p><p>In the case of overdose, severe cardiac arrhythmias and tonic-clonic seizures may occur. These may be the first signs of intoxication. Discontinuation of treatment may be required, and after all signs and symptoms of toxicity subside, treatment may be resumed at lower doses if deemed necessary by the physician. As there is no specific antidote, symptomatic treatment of cardiovascular collapse should be provided.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children. Doxoven 200 mg should only be used on the prescription of a specialist physician.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Doxoven 200 mg is a novel bronchodilator that structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. Doxoven 200 mg selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. It has decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, which may account for its improved safety profile. Additionally, it inhibits platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the production of leukotrienes.</p>', 'active', '533_1742589040.jpg', 55, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:30:40', '2025-05-02 08:51:34'),
(4085, 'Almex', 'Albendazole', 5.02, 4, 'tablet', '4 tablet in a strip', 'Chewable Tablet', '400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Almex 400 mg</strong></p><p>Almex 400 mg is indicated in single and mixed infestations of:</p><ul><li>Hookworm (Ancylostoma, Necator)</li><li>Roundworm (Ascaris)</li><li>Threadworm (Enterobius)</li><li>Whipworm (Trichuris)</li><li>Strongyloides</li><li>Tapeworm</li><li>Opisthorchi</li><li>Hydatid</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Anthelmintic</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong> Almex 400 mg is a broad-spectrum benzimidazole anthelminthic drug. In addition to its vermicidal properties, it has both ovicidal and larvicidal activities.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Almex 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; children over 2 years:</strong> 400 mg (1 tablet or 10 ml suspension) as a single dose in cases of Enterobius vermicularis, Trichuris trichiura, Ascaris lumbricoides, Ancylostoma duodenale, and Necator americanus. For strongyloidiasis or taeniasis, 400 mg (1 tablet or 10 ml suspension) daily for 3 consecutive days. If the patient is not cured after three weeks, a second course of treatment is indicated.</li><li><strong>Children 1-2 years:</strong> 200 mg (5 ml suspension) as a single dose.</li><li><strong>Children under 1 year:</strong> Not recommended.</li><li><strong>In Hydatid disease (Echinococcosis):</strong> Almex 400 mg is taken with meals. A dose of 400 mg twice daily for 28 days for patients weighing over 60 kg. For patients under 60 kg, the dose is 15 mg/kg body weight daily in two divided doses (max 800 mg/day). In cystic echinococcosis, the 28-day course may be repeated after 14 days without treatment. For alveolar echinococcosis, treatment may continue for months or years.</li><li><strong>In giardiasis:</strong> 400 mg (1 tablet or 10 ml suspension) once daily for five days.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Almex 400 mg</strong> No interaction involving Almex 400 mg, either pharmacodynamic or pharmacokinetic, has been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neonates:</strong> Almex 400 mg is not normally used in neonates.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> A reduction of the dose from 400 mg to 200 mg may be indicated in children weighing less than 10 kg, but no general reduction in dosage is recommended.</li><li><strong>Pregnant women:</strong> Almex 400 mg should not be given during pregnancy or to women thought to be pregnant. No information is available on placental transfer.</li><li><strong>Concurrent disease:</strong> There is no evidence to suggest that the dose should be altered in renal, hepatic, or cardiac failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Almex 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal disturbances</li><li>Headache</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Changes in liver enzymes</li><li>Rarely reversible alopecia</li><li>Rash</li><li>Fever</li><li>Blood disorders including leucopenia and pancytopenia</li><li>Allergic shock (if cyst leakage occurs)</li><li>Convulsions and meningism in cerebral disease</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong> Almex 400 mg is classified as US FDA Pregnancy category C. It should be avoided during pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Blood counts and liver function tests should be done before treatment and twice during each cycle.</li><li>Breastfeeding should be avoided unless necessary.</li><li>Pregnancy should be excluded before starting treatment.</li><li>Almex 400 mg should only be used in the treatment of Echinococcosis under constant medical supervision with regular monitoring of serum-transaminase concentrations, leucocyte, and platelet counts.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Anthelmintic</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong> Almex 400 mg is a broad-spectrum anthelmintic. It exerts its anthelmintic effect by blocking glucose uptake in the susceptible helminths, depleting their energy levels, and eventually leading to their immobilization. This may be due to the binding and inhibition of parasite tubulin polymerization by Almex 400 mg and its metabolites. The drug also binds to human tubulin. Almex 400 mg is extensively metabolized in the liver and is poorly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract. The principal metabolite, Almex 400 mg sulphoxide, has anthelmintic activity and a plasma half-life of about 8.5 hours. It is excreted in the urine along with other metabolites.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong> Almex 400 mg is a US FDA Pregnancy Category C drug. It should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the risks to the fetus.</p>', 'active', '718_1742589179.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:32:59', '2025-05-02 23:01:10'),
(4086, 'Xyloken', 'Lidocaine Hydrochloride', 100.30, 1, 'x 10 ml bottle', '10 ml bottle', 'Spray', '10 mg/spray', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xyloken 10 ml (Lidocaine Injection)</strong></p><p>Lidocaine Injection is indicated:</p><ul><li>As a local anaesthetic for use in infiltration blockade and intravenous regional analgesia.</li><li>For the prevention and treatment of ventricular tachyarrhythmias.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Local &amp; Surface Anesthesia</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Lidocaine primarily works by inhibiting sodium influx through sodium-specific ion channels in the neuronal cell membrane, particularly the voltage-gated sodium channels. By interrupting the influx of sodium, action potentials cannot arise, and signal conduction is inhibited. The receptor site for lidocaine is thought to be located at the cytoplasmic (inner) portion of the sodium channel. Lidocaine binds more readily to sodium channels in the activated state, which leads to a faster onset of blockade in neurons that are rapidly firing. This is referred to as state-dependent blockade.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Xyloken 10 ml:</strong></p><p>The dosage is adjusted according to the response of the patient and site of administration.</p><ul><li><strong>Adult:</strong> For healthy adults, the maximum individual dose should not exceed 4.5 mg/kg of body weight. The maximum total dose should generally not exceed 300 mg.</li></ul><p>Recommended doses for routine procedures (as a guide):</p><ul><li><strong>Infiltration (Percutaneous):</strong> 5-300 mg</li><li><strong>Intravenous Regional:</strong> 50-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Peripheral Nerve Blocks:</strong>Brachial: 225-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dental: 20-100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Intercostal: 30 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Paravertebral: 30-50 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pudenal (each side): Variable</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Paracervical Obstetrical Analgesia: 100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Sympathetic Nerve Blocks:</strong>Cervical: 50 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Lumbar: 50-100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Central Neural Blocks:</strong>Epidural: 200-300 mg (based on dermatomes anesthetized, typically 2-3 mL/dermatome)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Thoracic: 200-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Lumbar Analgesia: 250-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Anesthesia: 200-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Caudal Obstetrical Analgesia: 200-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Surgical Anesthesia: 225-300 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Xyloken 10 ml:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Antiarrhythmic Treatment:</strong> An intravenous dose of 1 mg/kg can be given over 2 minutes and repeated after 5 minutes. Alternatively, an infusion of 2-4 mg/min can be given to produce a plasma level of 2-4 mg/L. A loading dose of approximately 1 mg/kg is typically administered intravenously over 2 minutes initially to avoid a delay in achieving a steady state.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xyloken 10 ml:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cimetidine</strong> can impair the metabolism of lidocaine, delaying elimination and increasing the risk of adverse reactions.</li><li><strong>Beta Blockers</strong> such as propranolol, metoprolol, or nadolol may significantly increase plasma lidocaine concentrations.</li><li>It <strong>prolongs</strong> the duration of action of <strong>suxamethonium</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to anaesthetics of the amide type.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xyloken 10 ml:</strong></p><p>Adverse reactions to lidocaine are rare but can occur, especially when blood concentrations are excessively high due to inadvertent intravascular injection, excessive dosage, or rapid absorption. Systemic effects may involve:</p><ul><li><strong>CNS reactions:</strong> Excitatory (nervousness, dizziness, blurred vision, tremors) followed by depressive (drowsiness, convulsions, unconsciousness, respiratory arrest).</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular reactions:</strong> Hypotension, myocardial depression, bradycardia, and possibly cardiac arrest.</li><li><strong>Allergic reactions:</strong> Rare, but may include cutaneous lesions, urticaria, edema, or anaphylactoid reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Although no evidence from animal studies suggests harm to the fetus, it should not be given during early pregnancy unless the benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li>Caution is advised when administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Lidocaine should be used by clinicians who are well-trained in the management of dose-related toxicity.</li><li>Proper dosage, technique, and readiness for emergencies are critical to the safety and effectiveness of lidocaine.</li><li>Resuscitative equipment, oxygen, and other drugs should be available.</li><li>Use cautiously in patients with epilepsy, impaired cardiac conduction, bradycardia, impaired respiratory function, and liver function.</li><li>Injections into inflamed or infected areas may reduce the effect of local anaesthetics.</li><li>Repeated doses can lead to significant increases in blood levels, so monitoring of vital signs and patient status is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 25°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>Pediatric dosages should be reduced based on age, body weight, and condition.</li><li>Elderly patients may be more sensitive to systemic effects and may require dose reductions.</li></ul>', 'active', '882_1742589331.jpg', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:35:31', '2025-05-05 22:09:23'),
(4087, 'Maxima MUPS', 'Esomeprazole', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Maxima MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><p>Maxima MUPS 20 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Alleviating GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn and others.</li><li>Treating erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Maintaining the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Eradicating Helicobacter pylori infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Maxima MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. Most patients heal within 4 to 8 weeks. If healing does not occur, an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely after 4 weeks, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication (Triple Therapy)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg once daily, adjusted individually and continued as long as clinically necessary.</li><li><strong>Acid-Related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks, depending on the response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommended adult dose: 40 mg once daily via intravenous injection (not less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Esomeprazole IV injection should not be administered concomitantly with other medications through the same intravenous site.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">When treatment with IV is completed, it should be switched to esomeprazole delayed-release capsules when the patient can resume oral intake.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Maxima MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take one Maxima MUPS 20 mg pill or capsule at least one hour before a meal. Swallow it whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mix the entire packet’s contents with 15 ml of water in a small glass. Stir thoroughly, then allow the mixture to thicken for 2-3 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">After stirring again, consume within 30 minutes. If medicine remains after consumption, add more water, swirl, and drink immediately.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If using a nasogastric or gastric tube, place 15 ml of water in a syringe, shake, wait 2-3 minutes, then administer the suspension via the tube.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Administration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Reconstitute the injection with 5 ml of solvent (WFI).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Administer intravenously over a minimum of 3 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Discard reconstituted solution if there are visible particles.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Maxima MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is metabolized primarily by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4.</li><li>No clinically significant interactions with drugs metabolized by CYP1A2, 2A6, 2C9, 2D6, 2E1, or 3A4.</li><li>Esomeprazole does not interact clinically with phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin.</li><li><strong>Diazepam</strong>: Co-administration may reduce its clearance by 45%, increasing plasma levels.</li><li><strong>Drugs affected by pH</strong>: Esomeprazole may affect absorption of drugs that are sensitive to gastrointestinal pH (e.g., ketoconazole, iron salts, digoxin).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Maxima MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common: headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, dry mouth.</li><li>No significant difference in side effects between short-term and long-term treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>A positive symptom response does not exclude the possibility of stomach cancer.</li><li>Esomeprazole capsules should be taken at least one hour before meals.</li><li>For patients with difficulty swallowing capsules, the contents can be mixed with applesauce and immediately ingested.</li><li>Avoid chewing or crushing pellets.</li><li>Antacids may be used while taking esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Maxima MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Toxicity has been observed in rats at a dose of 510 mg/kg.</li><li>Symptoms of overdose may include decreased motor activity, changes in breathing frequency, tremors, ataxia, and clonic convulsions.</li><li>No specific countermeasure for overdose; symptomatic and supportive care is advised.</li><li>Dialysis is unlikely to remove esomeprazole due to protein binding.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place at a temperature not exceeding 30°C, away from moisture and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole inhibits the H+/K+-ATPase proton pump in gastric parietal cells, reducing stomach acid production.</li><li>Esomeprazole is the S-isomer of omeprazole and offers superior acid control.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: Peak plasma levels occur about 1.5 hours after oral dosing.</li><li><strong>Bioavailability</strong>: Approximately 90% with repeated dosing.</li><li><strong>Distribution</strong>: 97% plasma protein-bound.</li><li><strong>Metabolism</strong>: Metabolized mainly by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4.</li><li><strong>Excretion</strong>: Excreted in urine (80%) and feces (20%).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Insufficient reliable data on the use of esomeprazole during pregnancy.</li><li>No teratogenic effects in animal studies.</li><li>Esomeprazole is excreted in milk; breastfeeding should be discontinued if esomeprazole is used.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy have not been established in pediatric patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>No significant difference in safety or effectiveness between elderly and younger patients. However, older patients may be more sensitive.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>No dose adjustment is required for mild to moderate hepatic insufficiency.</li><li>For severe hepatic impairment, do not exceed 20 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole pharmacokinetics are not significantly affected by renal impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '45_1742589562.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:39:22', '2025-05-03 23:58:16'),
(4088, 'Cinkara', 'Herbal Multivitamin', 250.00, 1, 'x 450 ml bottle', '450 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '0 mg/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cinkara 450 ml</strong></p><p>This Cinkara syrup is indicated in:</p><ul><li>General debility</li><li>Mental weakness</li><li>Anaemia of pregnancy</li><li>Hypolactation</li><li>Malnutrition</li><li>Lack of appetite</li><li>Vitamin deficiencies</li><li>Strenuous exercise</li><li>Rundown and debilitated condition</li><li>During convalescence</li><li>Loss of weight</li><li>Elemental deficiency</li><li>Excessive metabolism</li><li>Vitamin deficiency due to antibiotics</li><li>Stress and acute illness</li><li>Nervousness &amp; undue tendency to fatigue</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each 5 ml syrup contains:</p><ul><li>Carrot (Daucus carota): 200 mg</li><li>Emblic Myrobalan (Phyllanthus emblica): 100 mg</li><li>Agarwood (Aquilaria agallocha): 50 mg</li><li>White Sandalwood (Santalum ablum): 50 mg</li><li>Cinnamon (Cinnamomum zeylanicum): 50 mg</li><li>Jatamansi (Nardostachys jatamansi): 50 mg</li><li>Rose (Rosa damascena): 50 mg</li><li>Black cardamom (Amomum subulatum): 50 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Cinkara 450 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 6 teaspoonfuls</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 2 teaspoonfuls</li><li>Twice daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>There is no known contraindication.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Cinkara 450 ml</strong></p><p>No significant side effect has been observed with proper usage.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at a cool and dry place, protect from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Herbal and Nutraceuticals</li></ul><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>This Cinkara 450 ml syrup is a non-alcoholic vitaminized herbal tonic with proven bioavailability in mental performance, anemia of pregnancy, lactating mothers, and liver protection. This syrup can be used as a tonic in all seasons by the whole family. It provides optimum quantities of essential minerals, trace elements, and vitamins required by the body. It also provides sufficient zinc, which plays a significant role in the intellectual functioning of children. This syrup contains a balanced proportion of extracts of various vitalizing herbs, successfully used for centuries to provide energy and stimulation of muscles and nerves.</p>', 'active', '51_1742589676.png', 66, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:41:16', '2025-05-03 01:21:25'),
(4089, 'Sergel', 'Esomeprazole', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sergel 20 mg tablet</strong></p><p>Sergel 20 mg tablet is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Alleviating GERD-related symptoms, such as persistent heartburn.</li><li>Treating erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Maintaining the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Eradicating Helicobacter pylori infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Treating Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</li><li>Managing duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Sergel 20 mg tablet</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. Most patients heal within this period, but additional treatment may be considered if necessary.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (Clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms persist, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication</strong> (Triple Therapy to reduce duodenal ulcer recurrence): Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily, amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily, clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg once daily, adjusted individually as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks, based on response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Injection</strong>: 40 mg Sergel 20 mg tablet (via intravenous injection) once daily, infused over a minimum of 3 minutes. Do not administer other medications through the same intravenous site.</p><p><strong>Administration of Sergel 20 mg tablet</strong></p><ul><li>Take one tablet or capsule whole, at least one hour before meals.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>: Mix the contents of the packet with 15 ml of water, stir well, and wait 2-3 minutes for thickening. Stir again and consume within 30 minutes. For nasogastric or gastric tube administration, follow specific guidelines for suspension preparation and delivery.</li><li><strong>Intravenous</strong>: Administer as a gradual injection over at least 3 minutes. The solution must be used within 12 hours of reconstitution.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sergel 20 mg tablet</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is metabolized by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4. No clinically significant interactions are expected with medications processed by other CYP enzymes, such as phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin.</li><li><strong>Diazepam</strong>: Esomeprazole may reduce diazepam clearance by 45%, leading to higher plasma levels.</li><li><strong>Acid-sensitive medications</strong>: Medications whose absorption depends on stomach pH (e.g., ketoconazole, iron salts, digoxin) may be affected by esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to any of the components of Sergel 20 mg tablet.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Sergel 20 mg tablet</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Dry mouth</li></ul><p>Long-term maintenance therapy side effects are similar to short-term treatment.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>A positive symptom response does not exclude the possibility of stomach cancer.</li><li>Take esomeprazole capsules at least an hour before meals.</li><li>For patients with difficulty swallowing capsules, the pellets can be mixed with applesauce.</li><li><strong>Antacids</strong>: Use of antacids is acceptable while taking esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sergel 20 mg tablet</strong></p><ul><li>In animal studies, a lethal dose was observed in rats at a dose 103 times the human dose.</li><li>Symptoms of overdose may include reduced motor activity, tremors, ataxia, and convulsions.</li><li>There is no specific antidote for esomeprazole overdose. Treatment should be symptomatic and supportive.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry area at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. Protect from moisture and light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor that reduces gastric acid output by inhibiting the H+/K+-ATPase in parietal cells.</li><li>It is the S-isomer of omeprazole, offering superior acid control compared to racemic proton pump inhibitors.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Insufficient data is available on the safety of esomeprazole during pregnancy. No teratogenic effects were found in animal studies.</li><li>Esomeprazole is excreted in breast milk, so breastfeeding should be discontinued if treatment is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>No significant differences in safety or efficacy between elderly and younger patients have been observed, though older individuals may be more sensitive to the medication.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>For patients with mild to moderate hepatic insufficiency, no dosage change is necessary. However, a 20 mg dose should not be exceeded in patients with severe hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>As less than 1% of esomeprazole is excreted unchanged in the urine, no pharmacokinetic differences are expected in patients with renal impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '8094_1746509284.jpg', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:42:49', '2025-05-06 09:28:04'),
(4090, 'Clovate', 'Clobetasol Propionate', 70.00, 1, 'x 10 gm tube', '10 gm tube', 'Ointment', '0.05%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Clovate 10 gm</strong></p><p>Clobetasol Propionate is indicated for adults, elderly, and children over 1 year in the following dermatoses:</p><ul><li>Psoriasis (excluding widespread plaque psoriasis)</li><li>Recalcitrant dermatoses</li><li>Lichen planus</li><li>Discoid lupus erythematosus</li><li>Other skin conditions which do not respond satisfactorily to less potent steroids</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Other Topical Corticosteroids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Clobetasol Propionate is a very potent topical corticosteroid. It has anti-inflammatory, antipruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties. It exerts anti-inflammatory activity via multiple mechanisms to inhibit late-phase allergic reactions. It decreases the density of mast cells, chemotaxis, and activation of eosinophils. It also reduces cytokine production and inhibits the metabolism of arachidonic acid.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Clovate 10 gm</strong></p><p><em>Cream &amp; Ointment:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Adults, elderly, and children over 1 year</strong>: Apply a thin layer of Clobetasol Propionate Cream or Ointment to the affected skin areas twice daily and rub in gently and completely.</li><li>Repeated short courses of Clobetasol Propionate may be used to control exacerbations.</li><li>In more resistant lesions, especially where there is hyperkeratosis, the effect can be enhanced by occluding the treatment area with polythene film. Overnight occlusion is usually adequate.</li><li>Treatment should be limited to 2 consecutive weeks.</li><li>The maximum weekly dose should not exceed 50 gm/week.</li><li>For children, courses should be limited to five days and reviewed weekly.</li></ul><p><em>Scalp Solution:</em></p><ul><li>Apply the required quantity of Clobetasol Scalp Solution once or twice daily to the affected areas of the scalp and rub in gently.</li><li>The total dose should not exceed 50 ml weekly.</li><li>If no response is noted after one week, therapy should be discontinued.</li></ul><p><em>Topical Spray:</em></p><ul><li>Spray directly onto the affected skin areas twice daily and rub in gently.</li><li>The total dosage should not exceed 50 g (59 ml or 2 fluid ounces) per week.</li><li>Treatment should be limited to 4 weeks and should be discontinued when control is achieved.</li><li>Do not exceed 26 sprays per application or 52 sprays per day.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Clovate 10 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Route of administration:</strong> Cutaneous</li><li>Creams are especially appropriate for moist or weeping surfaces, while ointments are suitable for dry, lichenified, or scaly lesions.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Clovate 10 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Co-administered drugs that inhibit CYP3A4 (e.g., ritonavir, itraconazole) may increase systemic exposure to corticosteroids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the preparation</li><li>Should not be used in rosacea, acne vulgaris, perioral dermatitis, perianal and genital pruritus, pruritus without inflammation, and untreated cutaneous infections.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Clovate 10 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Most common: Burning and stinging sensation</li><li>Less frequent: Itching, skin atrophy, cracking, and fissuring of the skin.</li><li>Prolonged use may cause Cushing syndrome, especially in infants and adults.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Limited data on use during pregnancy. Use only if the benefit to the mother outweighs the possible risk.</li><li>Unknown whether the drug is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In case of using occlusive dressings, ensure the skin is cleansed before applying a fresh dressing.</li><li>Caution in psoriasis as rebound relapses or local/systemic toxicity may occur due to impaired skin barrier function.</li><li>Treatment on the face should be limited to 5 days.</li><li>Avoid use around the eyes, as cataracts or glaucoma may result from repeated exposure.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Clovate 10 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Acute overdosage is unlikely.</li><li>Chronic overdosage or misuse may lead to hypercortisolism. Discontinue use in such cases.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Do not freeze.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>In infants and children under 12 years, long-term continuous use should be avoided, as adrenal suppression may occur.</li><li>Children are more susceptible to the atrophic effects of topical corticosteroids.</li></ul>', 'active', '520_1742589861.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:44:21', '2025-05-01 18:10:42');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4091, 'Nebanol', 'Neomycin Sulfate + Bacitracin Zinc', 44.20, 1, 'x 20 gm tube', '20 gm tube', 'Ointment', '(5 mg+500 IU)/gm', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nebanol 20 gm</strong></p><p>This is indicated in the treatment of topical bacterial infections. It is particularly effective for conditions like atopic dermatitis, contact dermatitis, stasis dermatitis, eczematoid dermatitis, neurodermatitis, eczema, and anogenital pruritus. It may also be useful as an adjunct in certain pyodermas, such as impetigo, when used alongside specific systemic antibiotic therapy for these infections.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Ophthalmic and Topical antibacterial products</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Bacitracin</strong>: Inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis and is active against many gram-positive bacteria (e.g., staphylococci, streptococci, corynebacteria, and Clostridia) and some gram-negative species (e.g., Neisseria and Haemophilus influenzae). It is often used in combination in topical preparations for broad-spectrum antibacterial activity.</li><li><strong>Neomycin</strong>: An aminoglycoside that binds to the 30S subunit of bacterial ribosomes, inhibiting protein synthesis and disrupting DNA synthesis. It is effective against many gram-negative aerobes and some strains of staphylococci.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Nebanol 20 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Ointment</strong>: Clean the affected area gently before use. Remove debris, such as pus or crusts, from the area. Apply 2 to 3 times daily to the affected skin.</li><li><strong>Dusting Powder</strong>: Apply a light dusting of the antibiotic powder to the affected area up to 4 times daily. It is used for superficial skin infections.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Nebanol 20 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Bacitracin Zinc</strong>: Increased risk of nephrotoxicity when used with other nephrotoxic drugs. May enhance the action of neuromuscular-blocking agents.</li><li><strong>Neomycin Sulphate</strong>: Additive nephrotoxic and neurotoxic effects with other aminoglycosides, bacitracin, cisplatin, vancomycin, amphotericin B, polymyxin B, colistin, and viomycin. Enhanced toxicity with potent diuretics. It may impair the absorption of other drugs, enhance the effect of acarbose, and alter INR when used with anticoagulants. It may also inactivate oral typhoid vaccine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Nebanol 20 gm</strong></p><p>This preparation is generally well tolerated, but in rare cases, allergic reactions may occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safe use of this preparation during pregnancy and lactation has not been established. It should only be used during pregnancy if the potential benefits justify the potential risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Bacitracin and Neomycin antibiotic powder should not be used on large open wounds or severely injured skin areas with significant fluid exudation, as hard crusts may form.</li><li>This skin ointment should not be used for treating otitis externa if the eardrum is perforated, due to the risk of ototoxicity.</li><li>Patients considering self-medication for deep or puncture wounds, animal bites, or serious burns should consult a physician first.</li><li>If the condition worsens or persists in minor skin injuries (e.g., cuts, scrapes, burns), discontinue the use and consult a physician. It should not be used for more than one week unless directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children and Infants</strong>: Suitable for use in children at the same dose as adults, but the dose should be reduced in infants. Not recommended for neonates.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Ophthalmic and Topical antibacterial products</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Bacitracin</strong>: Inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis and acts on many gram-positive bacteria (e.g., staphylococci, streptococci, corynebacteria, and Clostridia) and some gram-negative species (e.g., Neisseria and Haemophilus influenzae).</li><li><strong>Neomycin</strong>: Binds to the 30S subunit of bacterial ribosomes, inhibiting protein synthesis and disrupting DNA synthesis. It is effective against many gram-negative aerobes and some strains of staphylococci.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The safe use of this preparation during pregnancy and lactation has not been established. Use only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>This ointment is suitable for children at the same dose as adults, but the dose should be reduced in infants. Not recommended for neonates.</p>', 'active', '1327_1742589973.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:46:13', '2025-05-04 00:32:54'),
(4092, 'Rynex', 'Oxymetazoline Hydrochloride', 45.00, 1, 'x 10 ml drop', '10 ml drop', 'Nasal Drop', '0.05%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rynex 0.05%</strong></p><p>Oxymetazoline Hydrochloride is indicated for the treatment of nasal congestion associated with acute or chronic rhinitis, common cold, sinusitis, upper respiratory allergies, epistaxis, and hay fever. It is also used as a nasal decongestant in otitis media.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Nasal Anti-histamine preparations, Nasal Decongestants &amp; Other Nasal Preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rynex 0.05% is an imidazoline derivative sympathomimetic amine. It is a direct agonist at α-adrenoceptors but has no action on β-adrenoceptors. It is used as a topical agent on the nasal mucosa, producing a rapid and long-acting vasoconstriction of the arterioles, thus reducing blood flow and diminishing swelling of the mucosa. This results in improved potency of the airway and better drainage of the nasal sinus.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Rynex 0.05%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oxymetazoline nasal spray</strong></li><li><em>Adults and children (6 years of age and older)</em>: 2 or 3 sprays in each nostril twice daily for 3-5 days.</li><li><em>Elderly</em>: No dosage reduction is necessary for nasal administration.</li><li><em>Children below 6 years of age</em>: Not suitable for children below 6 years of age.</li><li><strong>Oxymetazoline nasal drop</strong></li><li><em>Adults &amp; children 6 years of age</em>: 2-3 drops of 0.05% nasal drops in each nostril twice daily in the morning and evening.</li><li><em>Children 2-5 years of age</em>: 2-3 drops of 0.025% nasal drops in each nostril twice daily in the morning and evening.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Rynex 0.05%</strong></p><p>Oxymetazoline Hydrochloride Nasal preparation is recommended for intranasal use only. It should be used for no longer than 5 days.</p><p><strong>How to use Oxymetazoline nasal spray</strong></p><ol><li>Remove the dust cover cap.</li><li>During application, tilt your head slightly forward and keep the container upright, then use the nasal spray.</li><li>After each use, gently wipe the spray tip with a clean &amp; soft cloth or tissue paper. Finally, set the dust cover cap back in place.</li></ol><p><strong>Interaction of Rynex 0.05%</strong></p><p>Rynex 0.05% Nasal preparation should not be given to patients being treated with MAO inhibitors or within 14 days of ceasing such treatment.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with cardiovascular disease, hyperthyroidism, prostatic enlargement, angle closure glaucoma, and hypersensitivity to any of its ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Rynex 0.05%</strong></p><p>Occasionally, it may cause local irritation and dryness of the mouth and throat. Temporary discomfort such as burning, stinging, sneezing, or an increase in nasal discharge may occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of Rynex 0.05% Nasal Spray during pregnancy and lactation has not been established but is not thought to constitute a hazard during these periods.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Keep away from the eyes. The spray should not be used for longer than seven days without medical advice. Frequent or prolonged use may cause nasal congestion to recur or worsen.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rynex 0.05%</strong></p><p>Overdose may give rise to local irritation and rebound congestion. Treatment need only be symptomatic.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C in a dry place, protect from light. Do not refrigerate or freeze. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1367_1742590092.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:48:12', '2025-05-04 20:39:15'),
(4093, 'Reef-D', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 11.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Reef-D</h3><p>This is indicated for the treatment &amp; prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, and disorders of osteogenesis. It is also used as a supplement in cases of inadequate intake of Calcium in childhood diet, rickets, pregnancy &amp; lactation, and in elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis, phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</p><h3>Composition</h3><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate</strong> 1500 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg</li><li><strong>OR</strong></li><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate</strong> 1250 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin D3</strong> 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Adequate Calcium in the blood is essential for various bodily functions, and our internal biochemistry will not tolerate even a short period of deficiency. Clinical evidence suggests that Calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is essential for healthy bones as it aids in Calcium absorption from the GI tract and stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies also show that Calcium and Vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth, as well as in osteoporosis and fracture prevention.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Reef-D</h3><p>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night, or as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Dosage:</strong> One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water, or as directed by the physician. Taking with a full stomach ensures better absorption.</p><h3>Interaction of Reef-D</h3><ul><li>Oral Calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline &amp; fluoride preparations; a minimum of 3 hours should be allowed between ingestion of these medications.</li><li>Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of Calcium.</li><li>Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce metabolism of Vitamin D3.</li><li>Concomitant ingestion of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of Calcium.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the components</li><li>Hypocalcaemia resulting from overdose of Vitamin D3</li><li>Hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Bone metastases</li><li>Severe renal insufficiency</li><li>Severe hypercalciuria</li><li>Renal calculi</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Reef-D</h3><ul><li>Flatulence, diarrhoea, constipation, upper GI discomfort, etc., are rare manifestations.</li><li>Hypercalcaemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><p>It should be used as directed by the physician during pregnancy and lactation.</p><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with dose reduction may be needed.</li><li>Plasma and serum Calcium levels should be monitored in cases of mild to moderate renal impairment, as well as with long-term use.</li><li>Patients with renal stones or a history of renal stones should also take precautions.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Reef-D</h3><p>At high doses, it may result in nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc.</p><p><strong>Treatment:</strong> Cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><p>Store at a temperature below 30°C. Protect from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><h3>Drug Classes</h3><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><h3>Mode Of Action</h3><p>This is a Calcium and Vitamin D3 preparation where Calcium Carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The Calcium Carbonate from coral has a chemical structure similar to human bone composition, ensuring better absorption. Vitamin D3 aids in the absorption of Calcium from the GI tract and helps maintain Calcium balance in the body.</p><h3>Pregnancy</h3><p>This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the physician\'s recommendation.</p>', 'active', '870_1742590218.webp', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:50:18', '2025-05-04 15:07:45'),
(4094, 'Contilex TS', 'Glucosamine Sulfate + Chondroitin', 20.00, 20, 'tablet', '20 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '750 mg+600 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Contilex TS</strong></p><p>This is primarily indicated in conditions like Osteoarthritis, Rheumatoid arthritis, Bone &amp; Joint injuries.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Stimulation of Cartilage formation</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Glucosamine is a natural substance produced in the human body and found in certain foods. Glucosamine stimulates the production of glycosaminoglycan &amp; proteoglycan, two essential building blocks of cartilage. Glucosamine acts, as a supplement, as a joint lubricant and shock absorber necessary to maintain healthy cartilage and joint function. Diacerein is an anti-inflammatory analgesic and antipyretic drug, indicated specially for the treatment of arthritic conditions. It directly inhibits IL-1 synthesis and release in vitro and down-modulates IL-1 induced activities, relieving Osteoarthritis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Contilex TS</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult &amp; Children above 12 years:</strong> One tablet (Glucosamine Sulphate 750 mg and Diacerein 50 mg) twice daily for 4-12 weeks, taken with food.</li><li><strong>Creatinine Clearance (CrCl) &lt; 30 ml/min:</strong> The dose of Diacerein should be reduced.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Contilex TS</strong></p><ul><li>There have been no reports of significant drug interactions of Glucosamine with antibiotics, antidepressants, antihypertensives, nitrates, antiarrhythmics, anxiolytics, hypoglycemic agents, or antisecretives.</li><li>Decreased absorption of Diacerein with aluminum and/or magnesium hydroxide antacids.</li><li>Increased risk of diarrhea with laxatives and antibiotics.</li><li>Avoid co-administration with fibers and phytic acids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>There are no known contraindications for Glucosamine. However, proven hypersensitivity to Glucosamine is a contraindication.</li><li>Diabetics are advised to monitor blood glucose levels regularly when taking Glucosamine.</li><li>No special studies have been conducted in patients with renal and/or hepatic insufficiency. The toxicological and pharmacokinetic profile of Glucosamine does not indicate limitations for these patients. However, administration to patients with severe hepatic or renal insufficiency should be under appropriate medical supervision.</li><li>Children should not be supplemented with Glucosamine.</li><li>Diacerein is contraindicated in pregnancy, lactation, and in individuals with hypersensitivity to anthraquinone derivatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Contilex TS</strong></p><ul><li>Diarrhea, constipation, epigastric pain, heartburn, nausea, vomiting, headache, skin rashes, drowsiness, intense yellow coloring of urine.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Diacerein is contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation.</li><li>Glucosamine should not be taken by pregnant women and nursing mothers due to lack of scientific data regarding its effect on the fetus and infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be practiced when administering Glucosamine + Diacerein in patients who are allergic to Glucosamine and/or Diacerein or to shellfish (e.g., shrimp, crab); have diabetes, asthma, alcohol dependence, or liver disease.</li><li>Before taking Glucosamine + Diacerein, complete blood count, liver function, and urinalysis should be monitored regularly.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Contilex TS</strong></p><ul><li>Diarrhea may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, protect from light and moisture.</li></ul>', 'active', '665_1742590330.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:52:10', '2025-05-03 01:34:15'),
(4095, 'Solivo', 'Naproxen Sodium + Esomeprazole Magnesium', 15.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+20 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Solivo 500 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p>It is indicated for the relief of signs and symptoms of osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis, as well as to decrease the risk of developing gastric ulcers in patients at risk of developing NSAID-associated gastric ulcers.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Solivo 500 mg+20 mg is a combination of Naproxen and Esomeprazole.</p><ul><li><strong>Naproxen</strong> is an NSAID with analgesic and antipyretic properties, inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis.</li><li><strong>Esomeprazole</strong> is a proton pump inhibitor that suppresses gastric acid secretion by inhibiting the H+/K+ ATPase in the gastric parietal cell. By targeting the proton pump, Esomeprazole blocks the final step in acid production, thereby reducing gastric acidity.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Solivo 500 mg+20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>The recommended dose is one tablet twice daily.</li><li>Do not split, chew, crush, or dissolve the tablet.</li><li>Take the tablet at least 30 minutes before meals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Solivo 500 mg+20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ACE Inhibitors, diuretics, and beta-blockers</strong>: may reduce the antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin</strong>: increases the risk of nephrotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Lithium</strong>: increases plasma levels of lithium.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: may increase the serum levels of methotrexate.</li><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>: may increase the risk of bleeding complications.</li><li><strong>Digoxin</strong>: may increase digoxin toxicity.</li><li><strong>Clopidogrel</strong>: may decrease exposure to the active metabolite of clopidogrel.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of this product.</li><li>Also contraindicated in patients who have experienced asthma, urticaria, or allergic-type reactions after taking aspirin or other NSAIDs.</li><li>Contraindicated for the treatment of per-operative pain in the setting of coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Solivo 500 mg+20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>The most common side effects include gastritis, erosive gastritis, dyspepsia, diarrhea, gastric ulcer, upper abdominal pain, and nausea.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The use of this tablet should be avoided during pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be exercised in the presence of the following conditions:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fatal cardiovascular thrombotic events (e.g., myocardial infarction, stroke, hypertension, congestive heart failure, and edema).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Serious gastrointestinal (GI) adverse events.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypomagnesaemia.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Renal or hepatic insufficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul>', 'active', '1702_1742590431.webp', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:53:51', '2025-05-04 21:17:14'),
(4096, 'Sandocal-D', 'Elemental Calcium + Vitamin D3', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sandocal-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>This combination is used for the treatment of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, rickets, tetany, and parathyroid disease. Calcium supplements are often used to ensure adequate dietary intake in conditions such as pregnancy and lactation, osteogenesis, and tooth formation (as an adjunct to definite treatment) and therapy with anti-seizure medications. It is also used as a routine supplement and phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Sandocal-D 500 mg+200 IU is a preparation of Calcium Carbonate and Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol). Calcium is necessary for many normal functions in the body, especially bone formation and maintenance. Vitamin D3 helps with the absorption and reabsorption of calcium. Vitamin D3 also stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies show that Calcium and Vitamin D3 together help in bone growth and the prevention of osteoporosis and bone fractures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sandocal-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><ul><li>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 200 IU Tablet: 2 tablets daily or 1 tablet twice daily. Sandocal-D 500 mg+200 IU is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li><li>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 400 IU Tablet: 1 tablet twice daily. It is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sandocal-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>It has possible interaction with calcium, aluminium or magnesium-containing antacids and other calcium supplements, calcitriol and other vitamin D3 supplements; digoxin, tetracycline, doxycycline, minocycline, or oxytetracycline.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Sandocal-D 500 mg+200 IU is contraindicated in cases of hypercalcaemia, hyperthyroidism, renal calculi and nephrolithiasis, and Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sandocal-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>Sandocal-D 500 mg+200 IU is generally well tolerated. However, if any of the following occur, notify a physician: nausea, vomiting, stomach cramps, dry mouth, increased thirst, or increased urination. Constipation may also occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This combination should be used as directed by a physician during pregnancy or while breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>If there is any pre-existing heart disease or kidney disease, precautions should be taken.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sandocal-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdosage may include nausea and vomiting, severe drowsiness, dry mouth, loss of appetite, metallic taste, stomach cramps, diarrhea, headache, and constipation.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1786_1742590586.webp', 57, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 01:56:26', '2025-05-04 20:48:23'),
(4097, 'Sinapol', 'Paracetamol', 35.00, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '120 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><br></p>', 'active', '1862_1746510552.jpg', 30, 1, 'fazlearabbi97@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 18:01:19', '2025-05-06 09:50:56'),
(4098, 'Renova', 'Paracetamol', 35.00, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '120 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p>Description updated ..</p>', 'active', '427_1742648687.jpg', 7, 1, 'fazlearabbi97@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 18:04:47', '2025-05-06 07:55:09'),
(4099, 'Iprasol', 'Salbutamol + Ipratropium', 250.00, 1, 'x 200 metered doses', '200 metered doses', 'Inhaler', '(100 mcg+20 mcg)/puff', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Iprasol 100 mcg + 20 mcg</strong></p><p>This inhaler is indicated for use in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) who are on a regular aerosol bronchodilator but continue to show evidence of bronchospasm, and who require a second bronchodilator.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Combined Bronchodilators</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Ipratropium and Salbutamol Inhaler contains Ipratropium bromide and Salbutamol.</p><ul><li><strong>Ipratropium bromide</strong>: An anticholinergic (parasympatholytic) agent that inhibits vagally-mediated reflexes by antagonizing acetylcholine released from the vagus nerve. It prevents the increase in intracellular cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGMP), which is caused by acetylcholine interacting with muscarinic receptors on bronchial smooth muscle, leading to bronchodilation.</li><li><strong>Salbutamol</strong>: A selective beta2-adrenoceptor agonist that acts on beta2-adrenoceptors of bronchial smooth muscle, providing short-acting (4-6 hours) bronchodilation with a fast onset (within 5 minutes) in reversible airway obstruction.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Iprasol 100 mcg + 20 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Inhaler puffs</strong>: 2 puffs, 4 times a day. Additional puffs may be taken if needed, but the total number of puffs should not exceed 12 in 24 hours.</li><li><strong>Inhaler solution</strong>: Administer the solution from a nebulizer or an intermittent positive pressure ventilator.</li><li><strong>Adults (including elderly)</strong>: One ampoule as required for symptom relief or as directed by a physician. Up to 3-4 ampoules daily.</li><li>Patients should consult a doctor immediately in case of acute or rapidly worsening dyspnoea, if additional inhalations do not provide adequate improvement.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Iprasol 100 mcg + 20 mcg</strong></p><p>Iprasol inhalation aerosol has been used with other medications like sympathomimetic bronchodilators, methylxanthines, and steroids commonly used in COPD treatment without adverse drug reactions. No formal drug interaction studies have been performed.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Iprasol inhaler is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>A history of hypersensitivity to soya lecithin or related food products like soybeans and peanuts.</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the drug, or to atropine and its derivatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Iprasol 100 mcg + 20 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salbutamol</strong>: Mild tremors and headache (usually transient). Very rare reports of muscle cramps, hypersensitivity reactions including angioedema, urticaria, bronchospasm, hypotension, and collapse.</li><li><strong>Ipratropium</strong>: Headache, pain, influenza, chest pain, nausea, bronchitis, dyspnea, coughing, pneumonia, bronchospasm in the lower respiratory tract, and pharyngitis, sinusitis, rhinitis in the upper respiratory tract.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C. No adequate and well-controlled studies have been conducted. Use only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is unknown whether the components are excreted in human milk. Should not be used by breastfeeding mothers unless the benefit outweighs the risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with narrow-angle glaucoma, prostatic hyperplasia, or bladder-neck obstruction.</li><li>Salbutamol should be used with caution in patients with convulsive disorders, hyperthyroidism, or diabetes mellitus.</li><li>Beta-adrenergic agents may cause hypokalemia, potentially leading to adverse cardiovascular effects.</li><li>The inhaler combination has not been studied in patients with hepatic or renal insufficiency, and should be used cautiously in these populations.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Iprasol 100 mcg + 20 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Overdose primarily relates to Salbutamol, as Ipratropium Bromide is not well absorbed systemically. Symptoms may include tachycardia, anginal pain, hypertension, hypotension, palpitations, tremor, arrhythmia, and flushing.</li><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: Administration of sedatives, tranquillisers, or intensive therapy in severe cases. Beta-receptor blockers (preferably beta1-selective) may be used as specific antidotes, but caution is needed to avoid worsening bronchial obstruction.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul>', 'active', '1113_1742648710.webp', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 18:05:10', '2025-05-03 21:06:24'),
(4100, 'Fast', 'Paracetamol', 35.00, 1, 'Bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '120 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p>Description Updated ..</p>', 'active', '566_1742648837.jpg', 5, 1, 'fazlearabbi97@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 18:07:17', '2025-03-22 18:07:17'),
