(4128, 'Lulicure', 'Luliconazole', 280.00, 1, 'Tube', '30 gm tube', 'Cream', '1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p>updated</p>', 'active', '1737_1742660412.jpg', 8, 12, 'fazlearabbi97@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 21:20:12', '2025-03-22 21:20:12'),
(4129, 'Pansec', 'Pantoprazole Sodium', 6.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pansec 20 mg</strong></p><p>Pansec 20 mg is recommended when inhibiting acid secretion is therapeutically beneficial, such as:</p><ul><li>Stomach ulcer disorders</li><li>Illnesses caused by gastroesophageal reflux</li><li>Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug-induced ulcer (NSAIDs)</li><li>Helicobacter pylori eradication (in combination with antibiotics)</li><li>Syndrome of Zollinger-Ellison</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Pansec 20 mg is a proton pump inhibitor that works by covalently attaching to the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system on the surface of the gastric parietal cell to decrease the final stage of gastric acid generation. Regardless of the stimulus that remains for more than 24 hours, this effect results in suppression of both basal and induced stomach acid secretion.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Pansec 20 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Oral:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Benign gastric ulcer:</strong> 40 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, continued for further 4 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li><strong>Gastro-esophageal reflux disease:</strong> 20-40 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, continued for further 4 weeks if not fully healed; maintenance dose is 20 mg daily, which may be increased to 40 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer:</strong> 40 mg daily in the morning for 2 weeks, continued for further 2 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer associated with Helicobacter pylori:</strong> Pansec 20 mg is recommended at a dose of 40 mg twice daily in association with antimicrobial agents as detailed below:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Amoxycillin 1 g and Clarithromycin 500 mg both twice daily for one week, or</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clarithromycin 250 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg both twice daily for one week.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of NSAID-associated gastric or duodenal ulcer:</strong> 20 mg daily for those requiring long-term NSAID treatment.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> Initially 80 mg once daily adjusted according to response (elderly max. 40 mg daily); daily doses above 80 mg given in 2 divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>IV Injection:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer and gastric ulcer:</strong> 40 mg once daily for 7-10 days</li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease associated with a history of erosive esophagitis:</strong> 40 mg once daily for 7-10 days</li><li><strong>Prevention of rebleeding in peptic ulcer:</strong> IV 80 mg, followed by 8 mg/hour infusion for 72 hours</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration:</strong> 80 mg IV every 12 h for 24 h, followed by 40 mg every 12 hours</li><li><strong>Long-term management of Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome and other pathological hypersecretory conditions:</strong> 80 mg IV every 12 hours, may increase to 80 mg every 8 hours if needed, may titrate to higher doses depending on acid output.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Pansec 20 mg</strong></p><p>In clinical investigations, no significant medication interactions were found.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those with known hypersensitivity to any of the formulation\'s ingredients should not take Pansec 20 mg.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Pansec 20 mg</strong></p><p>Both in the short-term and the long-term, Pansec 20 mg is well tolerated. The most frequent adverse effects are headache and diarrhea, while abdominal pain, flatulence, rash, sleeplessness, and hyperglycemia are only rarely reported side effects.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pansec 20 mg is classified as a B pregnancy drug by the US FDA. Nevertheless, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. This medication should only be used during pregnancy if clearly necessary, as a human response to treatments is not usually predicted by studies on animal reproduction. It has been demonstrated that Pansec 20 mg is excreted in human milk. So, a choice should be made on whether to stop nursing or stop taking the medication, taking into account the benefit of the medication to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Patients should be advised not to split, chew, or crush the Pansec 20 mg pill. The use of Pansec 20 mg for an extended period of time may cause malabsorption of cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) or raise the risk of problems linked to osteoporosis.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Pansec 20 mg</strong></p><p>There are no recognized signs of human overdose. Due to its strong protein binding, Pansec 20 mg is not easily dialyzable. There is no specific therapy that is advised, other symptomatic and supportive management.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep dry and away from heat and light. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p><strong>Direction for use of IV injection:</strong></p><p>Pansec 20 mg lyophilized powder with 0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection must only be administered intravenously and under no other circumstances. A gradual intravenous dose of Pansec 20 mg should be administered. The powder-containing vial is mixed with 10 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride injection to create the IV injection solution. Upon reconstitution, the injection should be administered gradually over a minimum of 2 to 5 minutes. Use just recently made solutions. The reconstituted solution can be kept for up to 4 hours at room temperature (up to 30° C).</p><p><strong>How to administer an IV infusion:</strong></p><p>An intravenous infusion of Pansec 20 mg should be administered over roughly 15 minutes. An IV infusion of Pansec 20 mg needs to be reconstituted with 10 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride injection before being further diluted (admixed) to a final volume of 100 ml using 0.9% sodium chloride injection, 5% dextrose, or lactated ringer\'s injection. Prior to further dilution, the reconstituted solution may be kept at room temperature (up to 30° C) for a maximum of 4 hours. The admixed solution must be used within 24 hours of the initial reconstitution and may be kept at room temperature (up to 30° C).</p>', 'active', '1539_1742666471.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:01:11', '2025-05-04 10:12:59'),
(4130, 'Savlon', 'Chlorhexidine Gluconate + Cetrimide', 60.00, 1, 'x 112 ml bottle', '112 ml bottle', 'Solution', '0.3%+3%', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1279_1746377568.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:04:55', '2025-05-06 09:16:54'),
(4131, 'Dextac', 'Dexlansoprazole', 10.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '30 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is the content with headings formatted in bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Dextac 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: Dextac 30 mg is indicated for the healing of all grades of erosive esophagitis (EE) for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: Dextac 30 mg is indicated to maintain healing of EE and relief of heartburn for up to 6 months.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease</strong>: Dextac 30 mg is indicated for the treatment of heartburn associated with symptomatic non-erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD) for 4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Dextac 30 mg delayed-release capsule is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI), that inhibits gastric acid secretion. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole (A racemic mixture of the R- and S-enantiomers). This is supplied as a Dual Delayed Release (DDR) formulation in a capsule for oral administration. Dextac 30 mg capsule contains a mixture of two types of enteric-coated granules with different pH-dependent dissolution profiles.</li><li>Dextac 30 mg suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell. By acting specifically on the proton pump, Dextac 30 mg blocks the final step of acid production.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Dextac 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis and Relief of Heartburn</strong>: 30 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive GERD</strong>: 30 mg once daily for 4 weeks</li><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 60 mg once daily for up to 8 weeks</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Dextac 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Dextac 30 mg can be taken without regard to food. It should be swallowed whole.</li><li>Alternatively, Dextac 30 mg capsules can be administered as follows: Open the capsule, sprinkle intact granules on one tablespoon of food, and swallow immediately. Granules should not be chewed.</li><li>If a capsule is missed at its usual time, it should be taken as soon as possible. But if it is too close to the time of the next dose, only the prescribed dose should be taken at the appointed time. A double dose should not be taken.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Dextac 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With Medicine</strong>: Atazanavir, Warfarin, Tacrolimus, Clopidogrel &amp; Methotrexate.</li><li><strong>With Food &amp; Others</strong>: No data available.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Dextac 30 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Dextac 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects: Diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and flatulence.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>There are no adequate or well-controlled studies in pregnant women with Dextac 30 mg. Exposure in clinical trials was very limited. Dextac 30 mg should not be administered to pregnant women unless the expected benefits outweigh the potential risks.</li><li>It is not known whether Dextac 30 mg is excreted in human milk. However, Dextac 30 mg and its metabolites are excreted in the milk of rats. As many drugs are excreted in human milk, Dextac 30 mg should not be given to nursing mothers unless its use is considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Gastric Malignancy, Clostridium difficile-associated Diarrhea, Bone fracture, Hypomagnesemia, and concomitant use of Dextac 30 mg with Methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Dextac 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li>There have been no reports of a significant overdose of Dextac 30 mg. Multiple doses of Dextac 30 mg (120 mg) and a single dose of Dextac 30 mg (300 mg) did not result in death or other severe adverse events.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C temperature &amp; in a dry place, protected from light. Keep all medicines out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary for elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: Safety and effectiveness of Dextac 30 mg in patients below 12 years of age have not been established yet.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary for patients with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No adjustment of Dextac 30 mg is necessary for patients with mild hepatic impairment. A maximum daily dose of 30 mg for patients with moderate hepatic impairment may be considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Dextac 30 mg delayed-release capsule is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI) which suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell. By acting specifically on the proton pump, Dextac 30 mg blocks the final step of acid production. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole (A racemic mixture of the R- and S-enantiomers).</li><li>The dual delayed-release formulation in Dextac 30 mg leads to a plasma concentration-time profile with two distinct peaks: the first peak occurs 1 to 2 hours after administration, followed by a second peak within 4 to 5 hours. After oral administration, mean Cmax and AUC value of Dextac 30 mg increased approximately dose proportionally. Dextac 30 mg is extensively metabolized in the liver and excreted by urine.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy Category B. There is no adequate and well-controlled study with Dextac 30 mg in pregnant women.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in Children &amp; Adolescents</strong>: Safety and effectiveness of Dextac 30 mg in patients below 12 years of age have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No dose adjustment is necessary for elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment of Dextac 30 mg is necessary for patients with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment for Dextac 30 mg is necessary for patients with mild hepatic impairment. A maximum daily dose of 30 mg should be considered for patients with moderate hepatic impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '707_1742666781.webp', 62, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:06:21', '2025-05-03 08:23:35'),
(4132, 'Brodil', 'Salbutamol', 0.46, 20, 'Tablet', '20 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '4 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Brodil 4 mg</h3><p><strong>Indications:</strong> Brodil 4 mg is indicated as a bronchodilator for use in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Asthma</li><li>Chronic Bronchitis</li><li>Emphysema</li><li>Other conditions associated with airway obstruction.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Brodil 4 mg is a selective beta2-adrenoceptor agonist. At therapeutic doses, it primarily acts on the beta-2-adrenoceptors of bronchial smooth muscle, with little or no action on the β1-adrenoceptors of cardiac muscle. It provides short-acting (4-6 hours) bronchodilatation with a fast onset (within 5 minutes) in reversible airway obstruction. It also has an anti-inflammatory effect on mast cells, inhibiting the release of bronchoconstrictor mediators, including histamine, neutrophil chemotactic factor (NCF), and prostaglandin D2.</p><p><br></p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><p><strong>Oral Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong>2-6 years: 2.5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years: 5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Over 12 years: 5-10 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily (2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong>2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily. Maximum single dose is 8 mg tablet.</li></ul><p><strong>Injection Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Subcutaneous Route:</strong> 500 mcg (8 mcg/kg), repeated every 4 hours as needed.</li><li><strong>Intramuscular Route:</strong> 500 mcg (8 mcg/kg), repeated every 4 hours as needed.</li><li><strong>Slow Intravenous Injection:</strong> 250 mcg (4 mcg/kg) injected slowly. The dose may be repeated if necessary.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Population:</strong> Safety and efficacy of Brodil 4 mg Injection in children under 12 have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Brodil 4 mg Respirator Solution:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults (and the elderly):</strong> 0.5 ml - 1.0 ml up to four times a day. Maximum of 40 mg/day under strict medical supervision.</li><li><strong>Children under 12 years:</strong> 0.5 ml of the solution diluted to 2.0-4.0 ml with normal saline. May be repeated up to four times a day.</li></ul><p><strong>Brodil 4 mg Inhalation Capsule:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 1-2 inhalation capsules every 4-6 hours as needed. Max dose based on individual needs.</li><li><strong>Children (4 years and older):</strong> 1 inhalation capsule, 3-4 times daily. May increase to 2 capsules as needed.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications:</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Brodil 4 mg or any of its components.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects:</h3><p>Brodil 4 mg may cause:</p><ul><li>Fine tremor of skeletal muscles (especially hands)</li><li>Palpitations</li><li>Muscle cramps</li><li>Tachycardia</li><li>Tenseness</li><li>Headaches</li><li>Peripheral vasodilatation (particularly after large doses).</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Use only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> The drug’s excretion in human milk is not known. Due to potential tumorigenicity observed in animal studies, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or the drug, considering the importance of the drug to the mother.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hyperthyroidism</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Cardiovascular disease</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypertension</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Occlusive vascular disorders</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Aneurysms</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diabetes mellitus</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Closed-angle glaucoma</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Antihypertensive therapy.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects:</h3><p>Symptoms of overdose include:</p><ul><li>Angina</li><li>Headache</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Tremor</li></ul><p><strong>Antidote for Overdose:</strong> A cardio-selective beta-blocking agent, but caution is needed in patients with a history of bronchospasm.</p><p><br></p><h3>Storage Conditions:</h3><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Drug Class:</h3><ul><li>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Brodil 4 mg is a synthetic sympathomimetic agent with predominant beta-2 adrenergic activity. It produces bronchodilatation by stimulating beta-2 adrenergic receptors in bronchial smooth muscles, causing relaxation of the bronchial muscle fibers and improving pulmonary function.</p><p><br></p><p>Please ensure that Brodil 4 mg is used under the guidance of a physician and according to the prescribed dosage.</p>', 'active', '831_1742666898.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:08:18', '2025-05-01 09:08:27');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4133, 'Napa', 'Paracetamol', 5.00, 5, 'suppository', '1 suppository', 'Suppository', '250 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Napa 250 mg</strong></p><p>Napa 250 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Fever</li><li>Common cold and influenza</li><li>Headache, toothache, earache</li><li>Bodyache, myalgia, neuralgia</li><li>Dysmenorrhoea</li><li>Sprains</li><li>Colic pain, back pain</li><li>Post-operative and postpartum pain</li><li>Inflammatory pain and post-vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic and osteoarthritic pain and stiffness of joints</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Napa 250 mg (Acetaminophen) possesses analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity. It primarily works in the CNS, raising the pain threshold by inhibiting the COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3 enzymes involved in prostaglandin synthesis. Napa 250 mg is a para-aminophenol derivative, widely used for its safety and effectiveness as a fast-acting analgesic with minimal side effects compared to aspirin.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Napa 250 mg</strong></p><p><em>Tablet:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 1-2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours, up to a maximum of 4 gm (8 tablets) daily.</li><li><strong>Children (6-12 years):</strong> ½ to 1 tablet 3 to 4 times daily (max dose 2.6 gm/day for long-term use).</li></ul><p><em>Extended Release Tablet:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years:</strong> 2 tablets every 6 to 8 hours (max 6 tablets in 24 hours). Do not crush.</li></ul><p><em>Syrup/Suspension:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Children under 3 months:</strong> 10 mg/kg body weight (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced), 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>3 months to below 1 year:</strong> ½ to 1 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>1-5 years:</strong> 1-2 teaspoonfuls 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>6-12 years:</strong> 2-4 teaspoonfuls 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 4-8 teaspoonfuls 3 to 4 times daily.</li></ul><p><em>Suppository:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Children 3-12 months:</strong> 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children 1-5 years:</strong> 125-250 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6-12 years:</strong> 250-500 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults &amp; children over 12 years:</strong> 0.5-1 gm, 4 times daily.</li></ul><p><em>Paediatric Drop:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Children up to 3 months:</strong> 0.5 ml (40 mg)</li><li><strong>4 to 11 months:</strong> 1.0 ml (80 mg)</li><li><strong>1 to 2 years:</strong> 1.5 ml (120 mg).</li><li>Do not exceed more than 5 doses daily for up to 5 days.</li></ul><p><em>Napa 250 mg Tablet with Actizorb Technology:</em></p><p>This formulation dissolves up to five times faster than standard Napa 250 mg tablets and is especially suitable for patients who cannot tolerate aspirin or other analgesics.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Napa 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Barbiturates, tricyclic antidepressants, and alcohol may reduce the metabolism of large doses of Napa 250 mg.</li><li>Alcohol increases the hepatotoxicity risk of an overdose.</li><li>Chronic ingestion of anticonvulsants or oral steroid contraceptives may increase first-pass metabolism or clearance, reducing therapeutic levels of Napa 250 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Napa 250 mg.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Napa 250 mg</strong></p><p>Side effects are typically mild, but may include:</p><ul><li>Hematological reactions (e.g., thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, pancytopenia)</li><li>Neutropenia, agranulocytosis</li><li>Pancreatitis, skin rashes, and allergic reactions</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy Category B according to USFDA. Use during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Napa 250 mg is excreted in breast milk, but maternal ingestion of normal therapeutic doses does not appear to pose a significant risk to the nursing infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired kidney or liver function.</li><li>Exercise care in patients taking other drugs that affect the liver.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Napa 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Early symptoms (within 24 hours): Pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, abdominal pain.</li><li>Liver damage may appear 12-48 hours after ingestion.</li><li>Abnormalities of glucose metabolism and metabolic acidosis can occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Napa 250 mg has analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity. It works by inhibiting cyclooxygenase enzymes (COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3), which are involved in prostaglandin synthesis. It is a safe and fast-acting analgesic commonly used for various types of pain.</p>', 'active', '81_1742666995.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:09:55', '2025-05-06 17:53:57'),
(4134, 'Coralcin-D', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 11.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Coralcin-D</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment &amp; prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, and disorders of osteogenesis. It is also used as a supplement in cases of inadequate intake of Calcium in childhood diets, rickets, pregnancy &amp; lactation, and elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis and as a phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate</strong> 1500 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin D3</strong> 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p>Or</p><ul><li><strong>Calcium Carbonate</strong> 1250 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin D3</strong> 200 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate Calcium in the blood is vital to a wide variety of bodily functions. Clinical evidence suggests that Calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is essential for healthy bones, as it aids in Calcium absorption from the GI tract. Additionally, it stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies show that Calcium and Vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth, as well as in osteoporosis and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Coralcin-D</strong></p><p>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night, or as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Coralcin-D</strong></p><p>One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water, or as directed by the physician. Taking it with a full stomach ensures better absorption.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Coralcin-D</strong></p><p>Oral Calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline and fluoride preparations, and a minimum of 3 hours should be allowed between ingestion of these medications. Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of Calcium. Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce metabolism of Vitamin D3. Concomitant ingestion of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of Calcium.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to any of the components, hypocalcemia resulting from an overdose of Vitamin D3, hyperparathyroidism, bone metastases, severe renal insufficiency, severe hypercalciuria, renal calculi, etc.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Coralcin-D</strong></p><p>Flatulence, diarrhea, constipation, upper GI discomfort, etc., are rare manifestations. Hypercalcemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It should be used as directed by the physician during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with reduction of dose may be needed. Plasma and serum Calcium levels should be monitored in mild to moderate renal impairment and also in case of long-term use. Patients with renal stones or a history of such should also take precautions.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Coralcin-D</strong></p><p>At high doses, it may result in nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc. Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This is a Calcium and Vitamin D3 preparation where Calcium Carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The Calcium Carbonate from coral has a chemical structure that is very similar to the composition of human bone. Coral Calcium ensures better absorption. Vitamin D3 aids in the absorption of Calcium from the GI tract and helps maintain Calcium balance in the body.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of a physician.</p>', 'active', '111_1742667097.webp', 6, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:11:37', '2025-05-03 01:40:00'),
(4135, 'Dexalax', 'Dexlansoprazole', 6.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '30 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Dexalax 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis (EE)</strong>: Dexalax 30 mg is indicated for the healing of all grades of erosive esophagitis (EE) for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: Dexalax 30 mg is indicated to maintain healing of EE and relief of heartburn for up to 6 months.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</strong>: Dexalax 30 mg is indicated for the treatment of heartburn associated with symptomatic non-erosive GERD for 4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Dexalax 30 mg is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI) that inhibits gastric acid secretion. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole, a racemic mixture of the R- and S-enantiomers. The drug is supplied as a Dual Delayed Release (DDR) formulation in a capsule for oral administration, containing two types of enteric-coated granules with different pH-dependent dissolution profiles. Dexalax 30 mg suppresses gastric acid secretion by inhibiting the (H+/K+)-ATPase enzyme in the gastric parietal cells, blocking the final step of acid production.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Dexalax 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis and Relief of Heartburn</strong>: 30 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive GERD</strong>: 30 mg once daily for 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 60 mg once daily for up to 8 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Dexalax 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Dexalax 30 mg can be taken without regard to food.</li><li>Swallow the capsule whole, or alternatively, open the capsule and sprinkle the intact granules on a tablespoon of food and swallow immediately (granules should not be chewed).</li><li>If a dose is missed, take it as soon as possible unless it\'s close to the time for the next dose. Do not take a double dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Dexalax 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With medicine</strong>: Atazanavir, Warfarin, Tacrolimus, Clopidogrel, Methotrexate.</li><li><strong>With food &amp; others</strong>: No data available.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Dexalax 30 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Dexalax 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and flatulence.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>There are no adequate or well-controlled studies in pregnant women. It should not be administered unless the expected benefits outweigh potential risks.</li><li>It is not known if Dexalax 30 mg is excreted in human milk. The drug and its metabolites are excreted in rat milk, so it should not be given to nursing mothers unless necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with gastric malignancy, Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea, bone fractures, hypomagnesemia, and when used concomitantly with methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Dexalax 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No significant overdose reports. Multiple doses of 120 mg or a single dose of 300 mg did not result in death or severe adverse events.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a dry place, protected from light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: Safety and effectiveness have not been established in children under 12 years.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment required.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No adjustment necessary for mild hepatic impairment. Consider a maximum daily dose of 30 mg for moderate hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Dexalax 30 mg inhibits gastric acid secretion by blocking the (H+/K+)-ATPase enzyme in the gastric parietal cells, halting the final step of acid production. It uses a Dual Delayed Release (DDR) formulation, resulting in two peaks in plasma concentration after administration. It is metabolized in the liver and excreted in the urine.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy Category</strong></p><ul><li>Category B: No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and effectiveness in patients below 12 years of age have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage adjustment necessary for elderly patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Impairment</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage adjustment necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong></p><ul><li>For mild hepatic impairment, no adjustment is needed. For moderate hepatic impairment, a maximum daily dose of 30 mg is recommended.</li></ul>', 'active', '1983_1742667259.jpg', 33, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:14:19', '2025-05-03 08:21:14'),
(4136, 'Aeron FT', 'Montelukast Sodium', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '4 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Aeron FT 4 mg</strong></p><p>Aeron FT 4 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</strong></li><li><strong>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR)</strong>: Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. Cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) are located in the human airway, and these eicosanoids bind to them. The orally active drug Aeron FT 4 mg has a strong affinity and specificity for the CysLT1 receptor. Without any agonist activity, it inhibits LTD4\'s physiological effects at the CysLT1 receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Aeron FT 4 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for adults and adolescents 15 years of age and older: <strong>Monas 10 mg tablet once daily</strong>.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 6 to 14 years of age: <strong>Monas 5 mg tablet once daily</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 2 years to 5 years of age: <strong>Monas 4 mg tablet once daily</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 6 months to 5 years of age: <strong>Monas 4 mg oral granules once daily</strong>. This can be administered either directly in the mouth or mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature.</li><li><strong>Use in pediatric patients</strong>:</li><li>The safety and efficacy of Monas have been established in adequate and well-controlled studies in pediatric patients with asthma 6 months to 14 years of age. Safety and efficacy profiles in this age group are similar to those seen in adults.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage adjustment is required in patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage adjustment is recommended in patients with renal insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Elderly use</strong>: The pharmacokinetic profile and the oral bioavailability of a single 10-mg oral dose of Monas are similar in elderly and younger adults. The plasma half-life of Monas is slightly longer in the elderly. No dosage adjustment in the elderly is required.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Aeron FT 4 mg</strong></p><p>In the prevention and long-term treatment of asthma, Aeron FT 4 mg has been provided alongside other commonly used treatments without seeming to increase the risk of negative side effects. According to studies on drug interactions, theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives (norethindrone 1mg/ethinyl estradiol 35mcg), terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin did not exhibit any clinically significant effects on the pharmacokinetics at the recommended clinical dose of it. In clinical investigations, the medication was used concurrently with a variety of routinely prescribed medications without showing any signs of clinically harmful interactions, despite the fact that more detailed interaction studies were not carried out.</p><p>Thyroid hormones, hypnotic sedatives, benzodiazepines, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and decongestants were among these drugs. Following a single dose of this solution, the hepatic metabolism-inducing drug phenobarbital reduced the drug\'s AUC by about 40%. For this medication, no dosage modification is advised. When powerful cytochrome P450 enzyme inducers, such as phenobarbital or rifampin, are provided alongside this solution, it is fair to use proper clinical monitoring.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those who have an extreme sensitivity to any ingredient in this medication should not take it.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Aeron FT 4 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, asthenia, aberrant behavior, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, hemorrhage, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, oedema, seizure, odd feeling, and sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, decreased attention, disorientation, erythema nodosum, hallucinations, hepatic problems, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, and tremor.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Aeron FT 4 mg is classified as <strong>pregnancy category B</strong>. Although it has been demonstrated that the medication crosses the placenta of pregnant rats and rabbits, there are no records of its use in pregnant humans. Although there is some evidence that it is also secreted in breast milk, little is known about the implications of this discovery. Before starting the therapy in nursing moms, caution should be exercised.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks, including status asthmaticus, is not recommended as usage for Aeron FT 4 mg. Patients should be told to have access to the proper rescue medication. Asthma exacerbations that are acute can still be treated with the solution. While the dosage of an inhaled corticosteroid may be gradually decreased under medical supervision, the use of this solution instead of an inhaled or oral corticosteroid should not be started suddenly. Exercise-induced bronchospasm should not be treated and managed with a single dose of it. While taking it, patients with documented aspirin sensitivity should continue to refrain from taking aspirin or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Although aspirin sensitivity in asthmatics is a known side effect of the drug, it has not been demonstrated to shorten the bronchoconstrictor response to aspirin and other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs).</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Aeron FT 4 mg</strong></p><p>The majority of overdose reports did not include any negative experiences. The most frequent side effects, which were in line with Aeron FT 4 mg\'s safety profile, were nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain. In the case of an overdose, it is permissible to take the customary supportive measures, such as removing any unabsorbed material from the digestive tract, using clinical monitoring, and if necessary, instituting supportive therapy.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry environment while keeping out light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>The cysteinyl leukotriene receptor is inhibited by the selective and orally active leukotriene receptor antagonist known as Aeron FT 4 mg (CysLT1). The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. The pathophysiology of asthma and allergic rhinitis, including airway edema, smooth muscle contraction, and altered cellular activity associated with the inflammatory process, have been linked to cysteinyl leukotrienes and leukotriene receptor occupation. These factors all contribute to the signs and symptoms of asthma.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>After oral administration, Aeron FT 4 mg passes the placenta in rats and rabbits. Nevertheless, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. Because the human reaction to medications is not usually predicted by research on animal reproduction, pregnant women should only use it if absolutely necessary. When giving the pill to a nursing mother, care should be taken because many medications are secreted in human milk.</p>', 'active', '1438_1742667349.jpg', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:15:49', '2025-05-02 22:50:53'),
(4137, 'D-Rise', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 120.00, 1, 'x 1 ml ampoule', '1 ml ampoule', 'Injection', '200000 IU/ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of D-Rise 200000 IU/ml:</strong> Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated in the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also used as an adjunct in the therapy of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcaemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Colecalciferol, a synthetic form of Vitamin D, is essential for normal bone growth and development, maintaining bone density, and for the proper utilization of calcium and phosphorus. It is crucial for the healthy growth and development of babies and acts as a hormone.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong> Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. Vitamin D3 is vital for normal bone growth and maintaining bone density. It also helps reduce the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, lowers the risk of cancer (breast, colorectal), and helps to maintain adequate insulin levels for patients with type 2 diabetes.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><p><strong>For Capsules:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance therapy</strong>: 1,400 - 2,000 IU/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Measurement of 25-hydroxyvitamin D should be done 3-4 months after starting maintenance therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Higher doses may be required in certain situations.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adjunct for osteoporosis</strong>: 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li><strong>Children (12-18 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU once every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>For Film-Coated Tablets:</strong></p><ul><li>1,000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily, or as directed by the physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li></ul><p><strong>For Oroflash or Chewable Tablets:</strong></p><ul><li>1,000 IU to 2,000 IU daily, or as directed by the physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal. Place the tablet in the mouth, chew, and swallow.</li></ul><p><strong>For Syrup:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For patients at risk of Cholecalciferol deficiency:</strong>0-1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml).</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml).</blockquote><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Cholecalciferol-deficient patients:</strong>0-1 year: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 years: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Injection:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prevention:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Infants receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml), 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nursed infants or infants not receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk or young children up to 5 years of age: 1 ampoule (1 ml), 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adolescents: 1 ampoule (1 ml), 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pregnancy: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml), 100,000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Elderly: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml), 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Digestive disorders or concomitant treatment with antiepileptics: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) or 1 ampoule (1 ml) every 3 or 6 months.</li><li><strong>Vitamin D Deficiency:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 ampoule (1 ml), 200,000 IU, which can be repeated 1 to 6 months later or as directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong> D-Rise 200,000 IU/ml may interfere with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (e.g., liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong> It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>General side effects include hypercalcaemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, and abdominal pain.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Studies have shown safe use of doses up to 4,000 IU during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU. Higher doses may be required for those considered Vitamin D3 deficient. Pregnant women should follow the advice of their healthcare provider, as their requirements may vary.</li><li>Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. Overdose in infants induced by nursing mothers has not been observed. When prescribing additional Vitamin D3 to a breast-fed child, the practitioner should consider the dose of Vitamin D3 given to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong> Use with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong> Overdose can lead to hypervitaminosis D.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong> Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong> The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years of age have not been established.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) helps in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. Vitamin D3 is vital for normal bone growth and maintenance of bone density. It also reduces the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, prevents the risk of certain cancers (e.g., breast, colorectal), and helps maintain adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Studies have shown safe use of doses up to 4,000 IU during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake is 400 IU, but higher doses may be needed for deficient individuals. Pregnant women should follow the advice of their medical practitioner. Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses:</strong> The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</p>', 'active', '793_1742667493.webp', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:18:13', '2025-04-18 12:48:32'),
(4138, 'Losectil', 'Omeprazole', 9.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '40 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Losectil 40 mg</strong></p><p>Losectil 40 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcer</li><li>Gastric and duodenal ulcers caused by NSAIDs</li><li>Prevention of ulcers in individuals with a history of duodenal and stomach ulcers caused by NSAIDs</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</li><li>Long-term management of acid reflux disease</li><li>Acid-related dyspepsia</li><li>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers</li><li>Prevention of aspiration of acid during general anesthesia</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Peptic ulcers caused by <em>Helicobacter pylori</em></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Losectil 40 mg is a benzimidazole derivative that inhibits stomach acid secretion. It works by inhibiting the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in the gastric parietal cells, preventing the release of gastric acid.</p><ul><li><strong>Onset of action</strong>: About 1 hour</li><li><strong>Peak effect</strong>: 2 hours</li><li><strong>Duration of effect</strong>: Up to 72 hours</li><li><strong>Return of secretion</strong>: Gradually after 3 to 5 days once the medication is stopped.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Losectil 40 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Oral Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Benign gastric and duodenal ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks (duodenal) or 8 weeks (gastric); increase to 40 mg for severe or recurrent cases.</li><li><strong>NSAID-associated ulcers</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continue for another 4 weeks if not fully healed; for prevention, 20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Gastro-esophageal reflux disease (GERD)</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continue for 4-8 weeks if not fully healed; for refractory cases, 40 mg once daily for 8 weeks; maintenance dose of 20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Long-term management of acid reflux disease</strong>: 10-20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Acid-related dyspepsia</strong>: 10-20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration</strong>: 40 mg the evening before surgery, then 40 mg 2-6 hours before surgery.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: Initially 60 mg once daily, adjust as needed, typically between 20-120 mg daily (divided doses if &gt;80 mg).</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication (peptic ulcer)</strong>: 20 mg twice daily with amoxicillin and metronidazole or clarithromycin for 1 week.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong> (severe ulcerating reflux esophagitis)</p><ul><li>For children &gt;1 year:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Body weight 10-20 kg: 10-20 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Body weight &gt;20 kg: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks</li></ul><p><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration</strong>: 40 mg IV one hour before surgery.</li><li><strong>Duodenal/gastric ulcers or reflux esophagitis</strong>: 40 mg once daily for patients who cannot take oral medication.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: 60 mg IV daily, adjust as necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Losectil 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>: Reconstitute with 10 ml water for injection, administer slowly (over 2-5 minutes).</li><li><strong>IV Infusion</strong>: Dissolve in 100 ml of saline or 5% dextrose, administer over 20-30 minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Warfarin, phenytoin, and diazepam may have delayed clearance when taken with Losectil, requiring dose adjustments.</li><li>No significant interactions with theophylline, propranolol, or antacids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to any of the components of Losectil 40 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Rare side effects may include nausea, stomach cramping, paresthesia, dizziness, and headaches. Most side effects are mild and temporary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Category C (US FDA): No harmful effects on pregnancy or fetus have been found in epidemiological studies.</li><li>The effect on breastfeeding is unknown; discontinue breastfeeding if Losectil is used.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Before starting therapy for suspected stomach ulcers, rule out gastric cancer, as Losectil may mask symptoms and delay diagnosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light. Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Losectil 40 mg inhibits gastric acid production by blocking the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme in parietal cells, leading to a reduction in gastric acid secretion.</p>', 'active', '554_1742667683.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:21:23', '2025-05-03 23:32:37'),
(4139, 'Flutica', 'Fluticasone Furoate', 275.00, 1, 'x 120 metered sprays', '120 metered sprays', 'Nasal Spray', '27.5 mcg/spray', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Flutica 27.5 Spray</strong></p><p>Flutica 27.5 Spray Nasal Spray is indicated for the treatment of the symptoms of seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis in patients aged 2 years and older.</p><p>Flutica 27.5 Spray Inhalation Powder is indicated for the once-daily maintenance treatment of asthma as prophylactic therapy in patients aged 5 years and older. This is not indicated for the relief of acute bronchospasm.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Nasal Steroid Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Flutica 27.5 Spray is a synthetic trifluorinated corticosteroid with potent anti-inflammatory activity. Like other corticosteroids, Flutica 27.5 Spray acts on multiple cell types (e.g., mast cells, eosinophils, neutrophils, macrophages, lymphocytes) and mediators (e.g., histamine, eicosanoids, leukotrienes, cytokines) involved in inflammation.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Flutica 27.5 Spray:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Nasal Spray:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults &amp; Children over 12 years: 2 sprays in each nostril once a day. In some cases, 2 sprays into each nostril twice daily, not exceeding 4 sprays.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children under 12 years (2-11 Years): 1 spray in each nostril once a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children under 2 years: There are no data to recommend use for the treatment of allergic rhinitis in children under 2 years of age.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Capsule:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Should be administered as 1 inhalation once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults and Adolescents Aged 12 Years and Older: Starting dosage based on asthma severity. The usual starting dose for adults and adolescents aged 12 years and older is 100 mcg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pediatric Patients Aged 5 to 11 Years: The recommended dosage is 50 mcg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Flutica 27.5 Spray:</strong></p><p><strong>How to use the Nasal Spray:</strong></p><ol><li>Shake the bottle gently and remove the dust cover.</li><li>Hold the spray with your forefinger and middle finger on either side of the nozzle and your thumb underneath.</li><li>Press down until a fine spray appears.</li><li>If using for the first time or if not used for a week or more, prime the device by pressing the nasal applicator several times.</li><li>Gently blow your nose to clear the nostrils.</li><li>Close one nostril and insert the nasal applicator into the open nostril. Tilt your head slightly forward, keeping the spray upright.</li><li>Breathe in through your nose and press the white collar of the nasal applicator firmly down once to release a spray. Breathe out through your mouth.</li><li>Repeat in the other nostril if necessary.</li></ol><p><strong>Cleaning:</strong></p><ul><li>Remove the dust cover.</li><li>Gently pull off the nasal applicator and wash the applicator and dust cover in warm water.</li><li>Shake off excess water and let it dry naturally.</li><li>Avoid using additional heat.</li><li>Push the applicator back onto the bottle and reattach the dust cover.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Flutica 27.5 Spray:</strong></p><ul><li>Potent inhibitors of cytochrome P450 3A4 (CYP3A4) may increase exposure to Flutica 27.5 Spray.</li><li>Coadministration of ritonavir is not recommended. Use caution with other potent CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ketoconazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Instructions:</strong></p><ul><li>Priming is necessary before first use and if the cap is left off or after 30 days without use.</li><li>Shake the nasal spray vigorously for 10 seconds and press the button about 6 times until a fine mist appears. Shake the nasal spray before each use.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Flutica 27.5 Spray is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to any of its ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Flutica 27.5 Spray:</strong></p><ul><li>Nasal spray: Dryness in the nose and an increase in nosebleeds may occur.</li><li>Inhalation capsule (≥5% in adults and adolescents): Nasopharyngitis, bronchitis, upper respiratory tract infection, and headache.</li><li>In pediatric patients (≥3% in children aged 5-11 years): Pharyngitis, bronchitis, and viral infections.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category C. Use only if potential benefit justifies potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Excretion into breast milk not studied. Caution advised when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Rare immediate hypersensitivity reactions or contact dermatitis may occur.</li><li>Rare cases of wheezing, nasal septum perforation, cataracts, glaucoma, and increased intraocular pressure have been reported.</li><li>Larger doses of Flutica 27.5 Spray should be avoided as the potential risk increases with larger doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store at temperatures not exceeding 30°C.</li><li>Do not refrigerate. Protect from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and efficacy not established for children under 2 years.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Use with caution, considering the frequency of decreased hepatic, renal, or cardiac function.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Use with caution in patients with severe hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment required.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><p>Nasal Steroid Preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Flutica 27.5 Spray is a synthetic corticosteroid with anti-inflammatory activity. It acts on a variety of cell types and mediators involved in inflammation, such as mast cells, eosinophils, neutrophils, macrophages, and lymphocytes, and reduces the release of inflammatory mediators like histamine, leukotrienes, and cytokines.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><p>Category C. Use only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric and Geriatric Use:</strong></p><p>Use cautiously in elderly patients and those with liver or kidney issues.</p>', 'active', '471_1742667817.jpg', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:23:37', '2025-05-03 08:36:21');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4140, 'Rupin', 'Rupatadine Fumarate', 12.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is the information about <strong>Rupin 10 mg</strong> with the headings in normal bold text:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Rupin 10 mg</strong></p><p>Rupin 10 mg is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis and Urticaria.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rupin 10 mg is a second generation, non-sedating, long-acting histamine antagonist with selective peripheral H1 receptor antagonist activity. It further blocks the receptors of the platelet-activating factor (PAF) according to in vitro and in vivo studies. Rupatadine possesses anti-allergic properties such as the inhibition of the degranulation of mast cells induced by immunological and non-immunological stimuli, and inhibition of the release of cytokines, particularly of the tumor necrosis factors (TNF) in human mast cells and monocytes.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rupin 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Rupatadine Tablet</strong>: The recommended dosage is 10 mg once daily, with or without food.</li><li><strong>Rupatadine Oral Solution (Children aged 2 to 11 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing 25 kg or more: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) of oral solution once daily with or without food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing equal or more than 10 kg to less than 25 kg: 1/2 teaspoonful (2.5 ml) oral solution once daily with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Rupin 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (over 12 years)</strong>: The recommended dose is 10 mg once a day. Rupatadine may be taken with or without food.</li><li><strong>Children aged 2 to 11 years</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing 25 kg or more: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) of the oral solution once daily with or without food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing equal or more than 10 kg to less than 25 kg: 1/2 teaspoonful (2.5 ml) oral solution once daily with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Rupin 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With medicine</strong>: The concomitant administration of Rupatadine 20 mg and ketoconazole or erythromycin increases the systemic exposure. Rupatadine should be used with caution when it is administered concomitantly with these drug substances and other inhibitors of the isozyme CYP3A4. Rupatadine should be used with caution when it is co-administered with statins, CNS depressants, or alcohol.</li><li><strong>With food</strong>: Grapefruit and Grapefruit juice should not be taken simultaneously with Rupatadine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to Rupatadine or to any of the excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Rupin 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Asthenia, dizziness, drowsiness.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Appetite increased, arthralgia, back pain, concentration impaired, constipation, cough, diarrhea, dry throat, epistaxis, fever, gastrointestinal discomfort, increased risk of infection, irritability, malaise, myalgia, nasal dryness, nausea, oropharyngeal pain, rash, thirst, vomiting, weight increased.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Palpitations, tachycardia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B2 (by TGA, Australia). There is no clinical data available on the exposure of Rupatadine during pregnancy. Pregnant women should therefore not use Rupatadine, unless the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk for the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: No information is available, whether Rupatadine is excreted in the mother\'s milk. Therefore, it should not be used during lactation, unless the potential benefits for the mother justify the potential risk to the infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Rupin 10 mg should be used with caution in patients with known prolongation of the QT interval, patients with uncorrected hypokalemia, and patients with ongoing proarrhythmic conditions, such as clinically significant bradycardia or acute myocardial ischemia. Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly patients (65 years and older). As there is no clinical experience in patients with impaired kidney or liver function, the use of Rupatadine 10 mg tablets is at present not recommended in these patients.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rupin 10 mg</strong></p><p>The most common adverse reaction was somnolence. If accidental ingestion of very high doses occurs, symptomatic treatment together with the required supportive measures should be given.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in cool &amp; dry place below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly. No information is available that indicates the requirement of any dose adjustment in this population.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Neither the safety nor the efficacy of Rupatadine has been established in patients less than 12 years of age.</li><li><strong>Patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency</strong>: Use of Rupatadine is not recommended in patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency. As no relevant clinical data is available.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Rupin 10 mg is a long-acting, non-sedative antagonist of histamine H1-receptors. It also antagonizes the platelet-activating factor (PAF). Both histamine and PAF cause bronchoconstriction which leads to an increase in the vascular permeability and act as a mediator in the inflammatory process. With the dual mode of action, Rupatadine shows better therapeutic effect than an isolated antihistamine. Rupatadine possesses other anti-allergic properties such as the inhibition of the degranulation of mast cells induced by immunological and non-immunological stimuli and inhibition of the release of cytokines, particularly of the tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF α) in human mastocytes and monocytes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There is no clinical data available on the exposure of Rupatadine during pregnancy. Pregnant women should therefore not use Rupatadine unless the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk for the infant. No information is available, whether Rupatadine is excreted in the mother\'s milk. Therefore, it should not be used during lactation, unless the potential benefits for the mother justify the potential risk to the infant.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly. No information is available that indicates the requirement of any dose adjustment in this population.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Neither the safety nor the efficacy of Rupatadine has been established in patients less than 12 years of age.</li><li><strong>Patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency</strong>: Use of Rupatadine is not recommended in patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency. As no relevant clinical data is available.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need any further modifications!</p>', 'active', '540_1742667918.jpg', 27, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:25:18', '2025-05-04 20:36:26'),
(4141, 'Losectil V', 'Omeprazole', 5.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Losectil V 20 mg</strong></p><p>Losectil V 20 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcer</li><li>Gastric and duodenal ulcers caused by NSAIDs</li><li>Prevention of duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by NSAIDs in individuals with a history of these ulcers</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</li><li>Long-term care of acid reflux disease</li><li>Dyspepsia caused by acid</li><li>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers</li><li>Prevention of acid aspiration during general anesthesia</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Peptic ulcer caused by Helicobacter pylori</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Blocker</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Losectil V 20 mg is a benzimidazole derivative that inhibits stomach acid secretion. It works by inhibiting the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in the gastric parietal cell, preventing gastric acid release.</p><ul><li><strong>Onset of effect</strong>: About 1 hour after oral administration</li><li><strong>Peak effect</strong>: 2 hours</li><li><strong>Duration of action</strong>: Up to 72 hours</li><li><strong>Recovery of secretory activity</strong>: Gradual over 3 to 5 days after stopping the medication</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Losectil V 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Benign gastric and duodenal ulcers</strong>:</li><li>20 mg once daily for 4 weeks (duodenal ulcer), 8 weeks (gastric ulcer). In severe or recurrent cases, increase to 40 mg daily.</li><li>Maintenance dose for recurrent duodenal ulcer: 20 mg once daily</li><li>Prevention of relapse in duodenal ulcer: 10-20 mg daily</li><li><strong>NSAID-associated ulcers</strong>:</li><li>20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, with a continued dose for another 4 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li>20 mg once daily as prophylaxis in patients with a history of NSAID-induced ulcers</li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</strong>:</li><li>20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continue for 4-8 weeks if not healed.</li><li>40 mg once daily for 8 weeks if GERD is refractory to other treatments.</li><li>Maintenance dose: 20 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Long-term acid reflux management</strong>:</li><li>10-20 mg daily</li><li><strong>Acid-related dyspepsia</strong>:</li><li>10-20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration</strong>:</li><li>40 mg the evening before surgery, then 40 mg 2-6 hours before surgery</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>:</li><li>Initial dose of 60 mg once daily. Usual range: 20-120 mg daily (if the dose exceeds 80 mg, split into two doses)</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication in peptic ulcer disease</strong>:</li><li>20 mg twice daily in combination with antimicrobial agents:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Amoxicillin 500 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg, three times daily for 1 week</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clarithromycin 250 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg, twice daily for 1 week</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Amoxicillin 1 g and Clarithromycin 500 mg, twice daily for 1 week</li><li><strong>Pediatric use for severe ulcerating reflux esophagitis (children &gt;1 year)</strong>:</li><li>If body weight is 10-20 kg: 10-20 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks</li><li>If body weight &gt;20 kg: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks</li></ul><p><strong>IV Injection</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration</strong>:</li><li>40 mg IV, administered slowly over 5 minutes, one hour before surgery</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer, gastric ulcer, reflux esophagitis</strong>:</li><li>40 mg IV once daily</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>:</li><li>Initial dose: 60 mg IV daily. Doses may need to be adjusted. For doses exceeding 60 mg daily, divide into two doses</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Losectil V 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>:</li><li>Dissolve the powder in 10 ml of water for injection. Administer slowly over 2-5 minutes at a maximum rate of 4 ml/min. Use freshly made solutions within 4 hours.</li><li>For IV infusion:</li><li>Dissolve the powder in 100 ml of 5% dextrose or saline. Infuse over 20-30 minutes. Solutions in saline must be used within 12 hours; in dextrose, within 6 hours.</li><li>Do not mix with other medications in the same infusion set.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Losectil V 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>May prolong the elimination of warfarin, phenytoin, and diazepam, requiring dose adjustments.</li><li>No interaction with theophylline, propranolol, or antacids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Do not use in individuals with known hypersensitivity to any components of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Most people tolerate Losectil V 20 mg well, but mild and temporary side effects such as nausea, stomach cramping, paresthesia, dizziness, and headache can occur. These side effects rarely require dosage adjustments.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Losectil V 20 mg is classified as Category C by the FDA.</li><li>Three studies suggest no adverse effects on pregnancy or fetal health.</li><li>It is unknown whether it passes into breast milk. If necessary, discontinue breastfeeding during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Rule out gastric cancer before starting treatment for suspected stomach ulcers, as Losectil V 20 mg may mask symptoms and delay diagnosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Blocker</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Losectil V 20 mg inhibits gastric acid production by blocking the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system in parietal cells. Effects start within 1 hour, peak at 2 hours, and last up to 72 hours. Secretory activity resumes gradually within 3-5 days after stopping the medication.</p>', 'active', '1698_1742668018.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:26:58', '2025-05-03 23:33:45'),
(4142, 'Okical-DX', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is the content you provided with the headings in normal bold text:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Okical-DX</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment &amp; prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, disorders of osteogenesis. Also used as a supplement in case of inadequate intake of Calcium in childhood diet, rickets, pregnancy &amp; lactation, elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis, phosphate binder in chronic renal failure etc.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li><li>Or</li><li>Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 200 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate Calcium in the blood is so vital to a wide variety of bodily functions that our internal biochemistry will not tolerate a deficiency even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that Calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of Osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is also essential for healthy bones as it aids in Calcium absorption from the GI tract. In addition to this, it stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies also show that Calcium and Vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth as well as in Osteoporosis and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Okical-DX</strong></p><p>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night or as directed by the Physician.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Okical-DX</strong></p><p>One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or as directed by the physician. Taking in a full stomach ensures better absorption.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Okical-DX</strong></p><p>Oral Calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline &amp; fluoride preparations, and a minimum of 3 hours should be allowed between ingestion of these medications. Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of Calcium. Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce the metabolism of Vitamin D3. Concomitant ingestion of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of Calcium.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to any of the components, hypocalcaemia resulting from overdose of Vitamin D3, hyperparathyroidism, bone metastases, severe renal insufficiency, severe hypercalciuria, renal calculi, etc.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Okical-DX</strong></p><p>Flatulence, diarrhoea, constipation, upper GI discomfort, etc. are rare manifestations. Hypercalcaemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It should be used as directed by the physician during Pregnancy and Lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with reduction of dose may be needed. Plasma and serum Calcium levels should be monitored in mild to moderate renal impairment and also in case of long-term use. Patients with renal stones or with such previous history should also take precautions.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Okical-DX</strong></p><p>At high doses, it may result in nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc. Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This is a Calcium and Vitamin D3 preparation where Calcium Carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The Calcium Carbonate from Coral has a chemical structure that is very similar to the composition of human bone. Coral Calcium is similar to other sources but ensures better absorption. Vitamin D3 aids in the absorption of Calcium from the GI tract and helps to maintain Calcium balance in the body.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of the physician.</p><p>I hope this formatting is what you were looking for! Let me know if you need any further changes.</p>', 'active', '1076_1742668155.webp', 8, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:29:15', '2025-05-04 01:15:27'),
(4143, 'Evagren', 'Super antioxidant [vitamins & minerals]', 4.00, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '0 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1984_1742668333.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:32:13', '2025-05-03 10:16:27'),
(4144, 'Pulmino', 'Doxophylline', 6.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pulmino 200 mg</strong></p><p>Pulmino 200 mg is used to treat the following indications:</p><ul><li>Bronchial asthma</li><li>Bronchospasm</li><li>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</li><li>Pulmonary disease with a spastic bronchial component</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Pulmino 200 mg is a novel bronchodilator that structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. It selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. However, unlike Theophylline, Pulmino 200 mg appears to have decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, potentially accounting for its improved safety profile. Additionally, it is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Pulmino 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 400 mg tablet two or three times daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li></ul><blockquote>12 years of age: 10 ml syrup or 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</blockquote><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><br></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years of age: 6-9 mg/kg body weight two times daily. For example, if body weight is 10 kg, 3 ml (60 mg) two times daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Pulmino 200 mg</strong></p><p>Pulmino 200 mg should not be administered together with other xanthine derivatives. Toxic synergism with ephedrine has been documented for xanthines. Like other xanthines, concomitant therapy with troleandomycin, lincomycin, clindamycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, ranitidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccine may decrease the hepatic clearance of xanthines, leading to an increase in blood levels.</p><p>No evidence of a relationship between Pulmino 200 mg serum concentrations and toxic events has been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Pulmino 200 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Acute myocardial infarction</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Lactating women</li><li>Patients who have shown hypersensitivity to its components</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Pulmino 200 mg</strong></p><p>Pulmino 200 mg rarely causes serious side effects; however, possible side effects are similar to those associated with excess caffeine intake, including:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Headache</li><li>Upset stomach</li><li>Heartburn</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Animal reproduction studies indicate that Pulmino 200 mg does not cause fetal harm when administered to pregnant animals or affect reproduction capacity. However, due to limited experience in pregnant humans, xanthines should only be given to pregnant women when clearly necessary. Pulmino 200 mg is contraindicated in nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The half-life of xanthine derivatives is influenced by a number of known variables. It may be prolonged in:</p><ul><li>Patients with liver disease</li><li>Patients with congestive heart failure</li><li>Those taking certain drugs such as erythromycin, troleandomycin, lincomycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccine</li></ul><p>In these cases, a lower dose of Pulmino 200 mg may be needed. Phenytoin, other anticonvulsants, and smoking may cause an increase in clearance with a shorter mean half-life. In these cases, higher doses of Pulmino 200 mg may be needed.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Pulmino 200 mg</strong></p><p>In case of overdose, severe cardiac arrhythmias and tonic-clonic seizures may occur. These effects may represent the first signs of intoxication. The appearance of side effects may require discontinuation of the treatment, which, if necessary, can be resumed at lower doses after all signs and symptoms of toxicity have subsided. As there is no specific antidote, in case of overdose, symptomatic treatment of cardiovascular collapse should be instituted.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children. Pulmino 200 mg should be used only on the prescription of a specialist physician.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Bronchodilator</li><li>Methyl xanthine derivatives</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Pulmino 200 mg is a novel bronchodilator. It structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. Pulmino 200 mg selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, thereby relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. However, unlike Theophylline, Pulmino 200 mg appears to have decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, which may account for the better safety profile of the drug. Pulmino 200 mg is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p>', 'active', '153_1742668432.jpg', 6, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:33:52', '2025-05-04 14:42:16'),
(4145, 'Ipical-D', 'Elemental Calcium + Vitamin D3', 5.00, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ipical-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>This combination is used for the treatment of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, rickets, tetany, and in parathyroid disease. Calcium supplements are often used to ensure adequate dietary intake in conditions such as pregnancy &amp; lactation, osteogenesis, and tooth formation (adjunct with definite treatment) and therapy with anti-seizure medications. It is also used as a routine supplement and phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Ipical-D 500 mg+200 IU is a preparation of Calcium Carbonate and Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol). Calcium is necessary for many normal functions of our body, especially bone formation and maintenance. Vitamin D3 helps with the absorption &amp; reabsorption of Calcium. Vitamin D3 also stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies have shown that Calcium and Vitamin D3 together help in bone growth and in the prevention of osteoporosis &amp; bone fractures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ipical-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><ul><li>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 200 IU Tablet: 2 tablets daily or 1 tablet twice daily. Ipical-D 500 mg+200 IU is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li><li>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 400 IU Tablet: 1 tablet twice daily. It is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ipical-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>It has possible interactions with calcium, aluminium, or magnesium-containing antacids &amp; other calcium supplements, calcitriol &amp; other vitamin D3 supplements, digoxin, tetracycline, doxycycline, minocycline, or oxytetracycline.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Ipical-D 500 mg+200 IU is contraindicated in the case of hypercalcaemia, hyperthyroidism, renal calculi &amp; nephrolithiasis, and Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ipical-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>Ipical-D 500 mg+200 IU is generally well tolerated. However, if symptoms like nausea, vomiting, stomach cramps, dry mouth, increased thirst, increased urination occur while taking it, consult with a physician. Constipation may also occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This combination should be used as directed by a physician during pregnancy or while breast-feeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>If there is any pre-existing heart disease or kidney disease, precautions should be taken.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ipical-D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdosage may include nausea and vomiting, severe drowsiness, dry mouth, loss of appetite, metallic taste, stomach cramps, diarrhea, headache, and constipation.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '263_1742668545.jpg', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:35:45', '2025-05-03 21:05:49'),
(4146, 'M-lucas', 'Montelukast Sodium', 17.00, 15, 'tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of M-lucas 10 mg</strong></p><p>M-lucas 10 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. Cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) are located in the human airway, and these eicosanoids bind to them. The orally active drug M-lucas 10 mg has a strong affinity and specificity for the CysLT1 receptor. Without any agonist activity, it inhibits LTD4\'s physiological effects at the CysLT1 receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of M-lucas 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for adults and adolescents 15 years of age and older: M-lucas 10 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 6 to 14 years of age: M-lucas 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 2 years to 5 years of age: M-lucas 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 6 months to 5 years of age: M-lucas 4 mg oral granules once daily. This can be administered either directly in the mouth, or mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Pediatric Patients</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of M-lucas have been established in adequate and well-controlled studies in pediatric patients with asthma 6 months to 14 years of age. Safety and efficacy profiles in this age group are similar to those seen in adults.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is required in patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is recommended in patients with renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Elderly Use</strong></p><p>The pharmacokinetic profile and the oral bioavailability of a single 10-mg oral dose of M-lucas are similar in elderly and younger adults. The plasma half-life of M-lucas is slightly longer in the elderly. No dosage adjustment in the elderly is required.</p><p><strong>Interaction of M-lucas 10 mg</strong></p><p>In the prevention and long-term treatment of asthma, M-lucas 10 mg has been provided alongside other commonly used treatments without seeming to increase the risk of negative side effects. According to studies on drug interactions, theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives (norethindrone 1mg/ethinyl estradiol 35mcg), terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin did not exhibit any clinically significant effects on the pharmacokinetics at the recommended clinical dose. In clinical investigations, the medication was used concurrently with a variety of routinely prescribed medications without showing any signs of clinically harmful interactions, despite the fact that more detailed interaction studies were not carried out. Thyroid hormones, hypnotic sedatives, benzodiazepines, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and decongestants were among these drugs. Following a single dose of this solution, the hepatic metabolism-inducing drug phenobarbital reduced the drug\'s AUC by about 40%. For this medication, no dosage modification is advised. When powerful cytochrome P450 enzyme inducers, such as phenobarbital or rifampin, are provided alongside this solution, it is fair to use proper clinical monitoring.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those who have an extreme sensitivity to any ingredient in this medication should not take it.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of M-lucas 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, asthenia, aberrant behavior, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, hemorrhage, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, oedema, seizure, odd feeling, and sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, decreased attention, disorientation, erythema nodosum, hallucinations, hepatic problems, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, and tremor.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>M-lucas 10 mg is classified as pregnancy category B. Although it has been demonstrated that the medication crosses the placenta of pregnant rats and rabbits, there are no records of its use in pregnant humans. Although there is some evidence that it is also secreted in breast milk, little is known about the implications of this discovery. Before starting the therapy in nursing moms, caution should be exercised.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>The reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks, including status asthmaticus, is not recommended as usage for M-lucas 10 mg. Patients should be told to have access to the proper rescue medication. Asthma exacerbations that are acute can still be treated with the solution.</li><li>While the dosage of an inhaled corticosteroid may be gradually decreased under medical supervision, the use of this solution instead of an inhaled or oral corticosteroid should not be started suddenly.</li><li>Exercise-induced bronchospasm should not be treated and managed with a single dose of M-lucas.</li><li>While taking it, patients with documented aspirin sensitivity should continue to refrain from taking aspirin or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Although aspirin sensitivity in asthmatics is a known side effect of the drug, it has not been demonstrated to shorten the bronchoconstrictor response to aspirin and other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs).</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of M-lucas 10 mg</strong></p><p>The majority of overdose reports did not include any negative experiences. The most frequent side effects, which were in line with M-lucas 10 mg\'s safety profile, were nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain. In the case of an overdose, it is permissible to take the customary supportive measures, such as removing any unabsorbed material from the digestive tract, using clinical monitoring, and if necessary, instituting supportive therapy.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry environment while keeping out light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>The cysteinyl leukotriene receptor is inhibited by the selective and orally active leukotriene receptor antagonist known as M-lucas 10 mg (CysLT1). The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. The pathophysiology of asthma and allergic rhinitis, including airway edema, smooth muscle contraction, and altered cellular activity associated with the inflammatory process, have been linked to cysteinyl leukotrienes and leukotriene receptor occupation. These factors all contribute to the signs and symptoms of asthma.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>After oral administration, M-lucas 10 mg passes the placenta in rats and rabbits. Nevertheless, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. Because the human reaction to medications is not usually predicted by research on animal reproduction, pregnant women should only use it if absolutely necessary. When giving the pill to a nursing mother, care should be taken because many medications are secreted in human milk.</p>', 'in-active', '1314_1742668635.jpg', 17, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:37:15', '2025-05-03 23:40:38'),
(4147, 'Dermasol-N', 'Clobetasol Propionate + Neomycin Sulphate + Nystatin', 100.00, 1, 'x 25 gm tube', '25 gm tube', 'Cream', '(0.5 mg+5 mg+1 Lac IU)/gm', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Dermasol-N 25 gm:</strong> This preparation is indicated for the short-course treatment of:</p><ul><li>Recalcitrant eczemas</li><li>Neurodermatoses</li><li>Psoriasis (excluding widespread plaque psoriasis) with or without secondary bacterial or fungal infection</li><li>Other inflammatory skin conditions that do not respond satisfactorily to less active steroids.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate:</strong> A highly active corticosteroid with topical anti-inflammatory activity. Its major effect on the skin is a non-specific anti-inflammatory response, partially due to vasoconstriction and decreased collagen synthesis.</li><li><strong>Neomycin Sulphate:</strong> A broad-spectrum antibiotic of the aminoglycoside class. It binds to bacterial ribosomal subunits (30S and 50S) and inhibits protein synthesis. It also causes a misreading of the genetic codes of mRNA, leading to incorrect amino acids in the polypeptide chain.</li><li><strong>Nystatin:</strong> An antifungal agent that kills fungi and yeasts by disrupting their cell membranes.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate:</strong> Reduces inflammation by vasoconstriction and decreased collagen synthesis.</li><li><strong>Neomycin Sulphate:</strong> Inhibits bacterial protein synthesis, leading to bacterial cell death.</li><li><strong>Nystatin:</strong> Alters the permeability of fungal cell membranes, causing leakage of intracellular contents and ultimately killing the fungi.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 2 years:</strong> Apply sparingly once or twice daily to the affected area until improvement is noted. Do not use for more than 7 days without medical supervision. If necessary, treatment can be continued for up to 4 weeks with medical review.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Suitable for use, but caution is advised in those with decreased renal function.</li><li><strong>Children under 2 years:</strong> Not recommended for use.</li><li>In cases of very resistant lesions, occlusive dressing (polythene) may enhance the anti-inflammatory effect.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neomycin Sulphate:</strong> May intensify the respiratory depressant effects of neuromuscular blocking agents with significant systemic absorption. However, when used as directed, the risk of significant drug interactions is minimal. No reported hazardous interactions with Clobetasol Propionate or Nystatin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Rosacea</li><li>Acne vulgaris</li><li>Perioral dermatitis</li><li>Primary cutaneous viral infections (e.g., Herpes simplex, chickenpox)</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the components</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged use or excessive amounts can lead to systemic absorption, resulting in hypercortisolism symptoms.</li><li>Local side effects include skin atrophy, striae, and dilatation of blood vessels, especially when occlusive dressings are used.</li><li>Pigmentation changes and hypertrichosis (excessive hair growth) may also occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C:</strong> Safety during pregnancy and lactation has not been established.</li><li>The use of this preparation during pregnancy and lactation is not recommended due to potential risks, including fetal toxicity from Neomycin and the unknown effects of Clobetasol Propionate.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Long-term continuous use, especially in infants and children, should be avoided due to the risk of adrenal suppression.</li><li>Care should be taken to prevent the cream from entering the eyes to avoid potential glaucoma.</li><li>If the preparation enters the eyes, wash thoroughly with water.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Acute overdose is unlikely, but chronic misuse or overdosage may lead to hypercortisolism. If this occurs, discontinue the topical steroid gradually.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 25°C. Do not freeze. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action Summary:</strong></p><ul><li>Clobetasol Propionate provides anti-inflammatory effects.</li><li>Neomycin Sulphate inhibits bacterial protein synthesis.</li><li>Nystatin disrupts fungal cell membranes.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you\'d like any additional details or have further questions!</p>', 'active', '1676_1742668751.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:39:11', '2025-05-01 20:22:15'),
(4148, 'Zerocal', 'Sucralose', 1.00, 1, 'x inactive', '100 tablet in a box', 'Tablet', '6.5 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1336_1742668945.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:42:25', '2025-05-07 20:32:40'),
(4149, 'Remmo', 'Esomeprazole', 14.00, 15, 'tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Remmo 40 mg</h3><p>Remmo 40 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Alleviation of GERD-related symptoms</strong> such as persistent heartburn and others.</li><li><strong>Treatment of erosive esophagitis.</strong></li><li><strong>Maintenance of erosive esophagitis healing.</strong></li><li><strong>Eradication of Helicobacter pylori infection</strong> in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</strong></li><li><strong>Duodenal &amp; gastric ulcers</strong> caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><h3>Dosage of Remmo 40 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. An additional 4-8 weeks may be considered if healing does not occur within 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (clinical studies did not extend 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms persist, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication</strong> (Triple Therapy to reduce duodenal ulcer recurrence): Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily, amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily, and clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg once daily, dosage adjusted individually as long as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks depending on response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: 40 mg once daily by intravenous injection (not less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes).</li></ul><h3>Administration of Remmo 40 mg</h3><ul><li>Take one Remmo 40 mg pill or capsule <strong>one hour before a meal</strong> and consume it whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>: Mix the contents of the packet with 15 ml of water, stir thoroughly, and wait for 2-3 minutes to thicken. Stir again and consume within 30 minutes. If needed, additional water can be added.</li><li><strong>Intravenous (IV)</strong>: Add 5 ml of solvent to the vial to prepare the IV solution. Administer it gradually over at least 3 minutes.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Remmo 40 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>CYP2C19 and CYP3A4</strong> metabolize esomeprazole. Esomeprazole is unlikely to inhibit most CYP enzymes, so interactions with drugs metabolized by these enzymes are unlikely.</li><li><strong>Diazepam</strong>: Esomeprazole may increase diazepam plasma levels.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal pH</strong>: Esomeprazole may reduce the absorption of drugs whose bioavailability is pH-dependent (e.g., ketoconazole, iron salts, digoxin).</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to any of the components of the formulation.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Remmo 40 mg</h3><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, dry mouth.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>A positive symptom response does not rule out <strong>stomach cancer</strong>.</li><li>Remmo 40 mg should be taken <strong>at least an hour before meals</strong>.</li><li>For patients who have difficulty swallowing capsules, the pellets can be mixed with <strong>cold applesauce</strong> (do not chew the pellets).</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Remmo 40 mg</h3><ul><li>Symptoms of acute poisoning include <strong>motor activity reduction</strong>, changes in breathing, <strong>tremors</strong>, <strong>ataxia</strong>, and <strong>clonic convulsions</strong>.</li><li>No specific countermeasure for overdose; supportive care is recommended.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>dry place</strong>, at a temperature not exceeding <strong>30°C</strong>.</li><li><strong>Protect from moisture and light</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Esomeprazole reduces stomach acid output by inhibiting the <strong>H+/K+-ATPase</strong> in gastric parietal cells. It is the <strong>S-isomer</strong> of omeprazole and offers superior acid control compared to racemic proton pump inhibitors.</p><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li>There is no sufficient data on <strong>pregnancy</strong>. Esomeprazole has shown <strong>no teratogenic effects</strong> in animal studies. It is advised to <strong>stop breastfeeding</strong> if esomeprazole use is necessary.</li></ul><h3>Pediatric Uses</h3><ul><li><strong>Safety and efficacy</strong> have not been established in pediatric patients.</li></ul><h3>Geriatric Use</h3><ul><li><strong>No significant difference</strong> in safety or effectiveness between elderly and younger patients.</li></ul><h3>Hepatic and Renal Insufficiency</h3><ul><li>For those with <strong>mild to moderate hepatic insufficiency</strong>, no dosage adjustment is needed.</li><li>For those with <strong>severe hepatic impairment</strong>, the dose should not exceed <strong>20 mg once daily</strong>.</li></ul>', 'active', '1960_1742669035.webp', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:43:55', '2025-05-04 15:09:45');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4150, 'E-Gel', 'Vitamin E [Alpha Tocopherol Acetate]', 4.01, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '200 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is the information formatted with headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of E-Gel 200 IU</strong></p><ul><li>As a dietary supplement:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vitamin E deficiency resulting from impaired absorption</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increased requirements due to diet rich in polyunsaturated fats</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For healthy hair &amp; skin</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">As an antioxidant</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hemolytic anemia due to Vitamin E deficiency</li><li>Therapeutic use:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Cardiovascular disease</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Heavy metal poisoning</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hepatotoxin poisoning</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hemolytic anemia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oxygen therapy</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In nutritional deficiency states</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin-E Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin E acts as an antioxidant in the body. Vitamin E protects polyunsaturated fatty acids (which are components of cellular membranes) and other oxygen-sensitive substances such as vitamin A &amp; vitamin C from oxidation.</li><li>In premature neonates, irritability, edema, thrombosis, and hemolytic anemia may be caused due to vitamin E deficiency.</li><li>Creatinuria, ceroid deposition, muscle weakness, decreased erythrocyte survival, or increased in vitro hemolysis by oxidizing agents have been identified in adults and children with low serum tocopherol concentrations.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of E-Gel 200 IU</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Betterment of cardiovascular health:</strong> 400 IU-800 IU per day</li><li><strong>Deficiency syndrome in adults:</strong> 200 IU-400 IU per day</li><li><strong>Deficiency syndrome in children:</strong> 200 IU per day</li><li><strong>Thalassemia:</strong> 800 IU per day</li><li><strong>Sickle-cell anemia:</strong> 400 IU per day</li><li><strong>Betterment of skin &amp; hair:</strong> 200 IU-400 IU per day (Topical use is also established for beautification)</li><li><strong>Chronic cold in adults:</strong> 200 IU per day</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of E-Gel 200 IU</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin E may impair the absorption of Vitamin A &amp; function of Vitamin K and potentiates the effect of Warfarin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>No known contraindications found.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of E-Gel 200 IU</strong></p><ul><li>Overdosage (&gt;1 gm) has been associated with minor side effects, including hypertension, fatigue, diarrhea, and myopathy.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin E is safe in pregnancy and lactation when used at recommended doses. Higher doses are not established.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>It may increase the risk of thrombosis in some patients, such as those taking estrogens.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin E is safe for children.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need any further information or adjustments!</p>', 'active', '207_1742669196.png', 6, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:46:36', '2025-05-01 22:22:33'),
(4151, 'Rocal-D Vita', 'Elemental Calcium + Vitamin D3', 17.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rocal-D Vita</strong></p><p>This combination is used for the treatment of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, rickets, tetany, and parathyroid disease. Calcium supplements are often used to ensure adequate dietary intake in conditions such as pregnancy &amp; lactation, osteogenesis, and tooth formation (adjunct with definite treatment), as well as therapy with anti-seizure medications. It is also used as a routine supplement and phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rocal-D Vita is a preparation of Calcium Carbonate and Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol). Calcium is necessary for many normal functions of our body, especially bone formation and maintenance. Vitamin D3 helps for the absorption &amp; reabsorption of Calcium. Vitamin D3 also stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies have shown that Calcium and Vitamin D3 together help in bone growth, as well as the prevention of osteoporosis &amp; bone fractures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rocal-D Vita</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 200 IU Tablet:</strong> 2 tablets daily or 1 tablet twice daily. It is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li><li><strong>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 400 IU Tablet:</strong> 1 tablet twice daily. It is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Rocal-D Vita</strong></p><p>It may have possible interactions with:</p><ul><li>Calcium, aluminium, or magnesium-containing antacids &amp; other calcium supplements</li><li>Calcitriol &amp; other Vitamin D3 supplements</li><li>Digoxin, tetracycline, doxycycline, minocycline, or oxytetracycline.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Rocal-D Vita is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Hypercalcaemia</li><li>Hyperthyroidism</li><li>Renal calculi &amp; nephrolithiasis</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Rocal-D Vita</strong></p><p>Rocal-D Vita is generally well tolerated, but possible side effects include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Increased thirst</li><li>Increased urination</li><li>Constipation (may occur).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This combination should be used as directed by a physician during pregnancy or while breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Precautions should be taken if there is any pre-existing heart disease or kidney disease.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rocal-D Vita</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdosage may include:</p><ul><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Severe drowsiness</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Loss of appetite</li><li>Metallic taste</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Headache</li><li>Constipation.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1442_1742669382.jpg', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:49:42', '2025-05-04 17:03:49'),
(4152, 'Maxpro Mups', 'Esomeprazole', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Maxpro Mups 40 mg</strong></p><p> Maxpro Mups 40 mg is indicated for the following:</p><ul><li>Alleviating GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn and others.</li><li>Treating erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Maintaining the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Eradicating Helicobacter pylori infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease, in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Managing Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>Treating duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Maxpro Mups 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. Most patients heal within 4 to 8 weeks. If healing doesn\'t occur, an additional 4-8 weeks may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg once daily (clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms persist, another 4 weeks may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication (Triple Therapy):</strong> Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days, amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> 20-80 mg once daily. Dosage should be individualized, and treatment should continue as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks, depending on response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection (IV):</strong> 40 mg once daily by intravenous injection (minimum 3 minutes) or infusion (10-30 minutes). Treatment with IV esomeprazole should be discontinued as soon as oral therapy can resume.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Maxpro Mups 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take one pill or capsule of Maxpro Mups 40 mg at least one hour before a meal, swallowing it whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension:</strong> Mix the contents of the packet with 15 ml of water in a small glass. Stir well and let it thicken for 2-3 minutes. Stir again and consume within 30 minutes. If using a nasogastric or gastric tube, prepare as above and administer through the tube.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Injection:</strong> Reconstitute the IV solution with 5 ml of water for injection (WFI). Administer the injection slowly over at least 3 minutes. Use within 12 hours of reconstitution, and do not refrigerate.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Maxpro Mups 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is metabolized by the CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 enzymes.</li><li>No significant interactions with phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin.</li><li>Co-administration with diazepam may increase diazepam levels by 45%.</li><li>Esomeprazole can reduce the absorption of drugs dependent on stomach acidity (e.g., ketoconazole, iron salts, digoxin).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to esomeprazole or any component of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Maxpro Mups 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects: headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, and dry mouth.</li><li>No significant differences in side effects between short-term and long-term treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>A positive symptom response does not rule out stomach cancer.</li><li>Take Maxpro Mups 40 mg at least one hour before meals.</li><li>For patients who have difficulty swallowing capsules, the pellets inside can be mixed with cold applesauce and consumed immediately.</li><li>Antacids can be used while on esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Maxpro Mups 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose may include decreased motor activity, breathing changes, tremors, ataxia, and convulsions.</li><li>There is no specific antidote for esomeprazole overdose. Symptomatic and supportive care should be provided.</li><li>Dialysis is not expected to remove esomeprazole from the system.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place at temperatures not exceeding 30°C.</li><li>Protect from moisture and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole inhibits the H+/K+-ATPase proton pump in gastric parietal cells, reducing stomach acid production.</li><li>It is the S-isomer of omeprazole, providing superior acid control.</li><li>Esomeprazole is extensively metabolized in the liver via CYP enzymes.</li><li>It is excreted in the urine and feces as inactive metabolites.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Insufficient data on use during pregnancy. No teratogenic effects were found in animal studies.</li><li>Esomeprazole is excreted in milk, so breastfeeding should be discontinued if use is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy have not been established for pediatric patients.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> No significant differences between elderly and younger adults, though older patients may be more sensitive.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency:</strong> No dosage change for mild to moderate impairment. For severe hepatic impairment, the dose should not exceed 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong> No anticipated change in pharmacokinetics in patients with renal impairment.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '476_1742669517.jpg', 6, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:51:57', '2025-03-22 23:51:57'),
(4153, 'Gynomix', 'Metronidazole + Neomycin Sulphate + Polymyxin B + Nystatin', 26.00, 6, 'Suppository', '1 Suppository', 'Suppository', '0 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Gynomix</strong></p><p>This combination is indicated in vaginal trichomoniasis, vaginal leucorrhoeas, and mixed vaginal infections (fungal or bacterial).</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each vaginal suppository contains:</p><ul><li>Metronidazole BP 200 mg</li><li>Neomycin Sulphate BP 35,000 IU</li><li>Polymyxin B Sulphate BP 35,000 IU</li><li>Nystatin BP 100,000 IU</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs used in Vaginal and Vulval conditions</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Metronidazole</strong>: A nitroimidazole, active against various anaerobic bacteria and protozoa.</li><li><strong>Nystatin</strong>: A polyene antibiotic with antifungal activity.</li><li><strong>Neomycin</strong>: An aminoglycoside, bactericidal, and active against some gram-positive and gram-negative organisms.</li><li><strong>Polymyxin</strong>: Active against gram-negative organisms, including <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Gynomix</strong></p><p>One suppository to be inserted into the vagina at bedtime for 12 days, or as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to Metronidazole, Neomycin Sulphate, Polymyxin B Sulphate, or Nystatin.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Gynomix</strong></p><p>Skin rash and urticaria may occur rarely.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Caution should be taken in case of pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be taken in patients with renal impairment.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 25ºC, protected from light and moisture.</p>', 'active', '297_1742669633.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:53:53', '2025-05-06 17:51:57'),
(4154, 'Zif-A', 'Ferrous Ascorbate + Folic Acid + Zinc Sulfate', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '48 mg+0.5 mg+22.5 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Zif-A 48 mg+0.5 mg+22.5 mg</h3><p>Zif-A 48 mg+0.5 mg+22.5 mg is indicated for the prophylaxis of iron deficiency, especially when inadequate diet calls for supplementary zinc and iron during pregnancy and anemia.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined preparation</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Zif-A 48 mg+0.5 mg+22.5 mg, when administered, is converted to the ferric form and immediately reduced to the ferrous form in the stomach. This reduced ferrous form is then transferred to the duodenum where it is highly absorbed. Ferrous Ascorbate has the advantage of providing both ferrous ion and ascorbate in the same compound. There is no dissociation upon entering the GI tract due to the stable chelate of iron with ascorbate. Folic Acid itself is biochemically inactive but is converted to tetrahydrofolic acid and methyltetrahydrofolate by dihydrofolate reductase in the liver. These folic acid congeners are transported across cells by receptor-mediated endocytosis, where they are needed to maintain numerous body functions. Folic acid enhances chemical reactions that contribute to the production of red blood cells, the manufacture of DNA needed for cell replication, and the metabolism of amino acids (compounds necessary for the manufacture of proteins). Zinc is essential to numerous physiological processes, including the function of many enzymes in the body. Deficiency may lead to poor night vision, slow healing of wounds, poor sexual development and function in males, poor appetite (perhaps due to a decrease in the sense of taste and smell), a reduced ability to ward off infections, diarrhea, dermatitis, and in children, retarded growth.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zif-A 48 mg+0.5 mg+22.5 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Adult &amp; Elderly:</strong> One tablet a day before or after meals (food-independent absorption) or as directed by the physician. In more severe cases, two tablets a day may be required as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Zif-A 48 mg+0.5 mg+22.5 mg</h3><ul><li>Iron chelates with tetracycline. Since oral iron products interfere with the absorption of oral tetracycline antibiotics, Zif-A 48 mg+0.5 mg+22.5 mg should not be taken within two hours of each other.</li><li>Occasional gastrointestinal discomfort may be minimized by taking with meals.</li><li>Absorption of iron may be impaired by concurrent administration of penicillamine and antacids.</li><li>In patients with renal failure, there may be a risk of zinc accumulation.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Zif-A 48 mg+0.5 mg+22.5 mg is contraindicated in patients with <strong>hemolytic anemia</strong> and in conditions with increased hypersensitivity to any of its components and increased body iron content.</p><h3>Side Effects of Zif-A 48 mg+0.5 mg+22.5 mg</h3><p>Side effects of Zif-A 48 mg+0.5 mg+22.5 mg supplementation are mild and transient. These include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric pain</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Heartburn, etc. Allergic sensitization has been reported following both oral and parenteral administration of folic acid.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><p>If pregnant, planning to become pregnant, or currently breastfeeding, please contact your physician before taking or continuing the drug. Administration in the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided unless definite evidence of iron deficiency is observed. Prophylaxis of iron deficiency is justified during the remainder of pregnancy, specifically when zinc supplementation is required.</p><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><p>Care should be taken in patients who may develop iron overload, such as those with <strong>hemachromatosis</strong>, <strong>hemolytic anemia</strong>, or <strong>red cell aplasia</strong>. Failure to respond to treatment may indicate other causes of anemia and should be further investigated.</p><h3>Overdose Effects of Zif-A 48 mg+0.5 mg+22.5 mg</h3><p>The clinical course of acute iron overdosage can be variable. Initial symptoms may include:</p><ul><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Tarry stools</li><li>Melena</li><li>Hematemesis</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Tachycardia</li><li>Metabolic acidosis</li><li>Hyperglycemia</li><li>Dehydration</li><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Pallor</li><li>Cyanosis</li><li>Lassitude</li><li>Seizures</li><li>Shock</li><li>Coma</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined preparation</p><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Ferrous Ascorbate, Folic Acid, &amp; Zinc is a combination of three nutritional supplements. Zif-A 48 mg+0.5 mg+22.5 mg is a combination of iron and vitamin C. Iron replenishes the iron stores in your body and corrects iron deficiency anemia. Vitamin C (ascorbate) is added to enhance the absorption of iron in the body. Folic Acid is a form of vitamin B. It plays a vital role in the formation of red blood cells, which carry oxygen throughout the body. It is also essential in pregnancy due to its role in the development of the unborn baby\'s brain and spinal cord. Zinc is a micromineral that provides nutrition.</p><h3>Pregnancy</h3><p>If pregnant, or planning to become pregnant, or currently breastfeeding, please contact your physician before taking or continuing the drug. Administration in the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided unless definite evidence of iron deficiency is observed. Prophylaxis of iron deficiency is justified during the remainder of pregnancy, specifically when zinc supplementation is required.</p>', 'active', '249_1742669731.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:55:31', '2025-05-05 22:33:12'),
(4155, 'Voltalin D', 'Diclofenac Sodium', 8.02, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '46.5 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Voltalin D 46.5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Rheumatology</strong>: Inflammatory and degenerative forms of rheumatism, chronic involutive polyarthritis, ankylosing spondylarthritis, osteoarthritis, spondylarthroses, acute gout, peri-articular rheumatic disorders.</li><li><strong>Surgery and Traumatology</strong>: Sprain, bruises, dislocations, fractures, soft tissue injuries, surgical interventions.</li><li><strong>Obstetrics and Gynecology</strong>: Primary dysmenorrhoea, episiotomy, adnexitis, endometritis, parametritis, salpingitis, and mastitis.</li><li><strong>Otorhinolaryngology</strong>: As pre-operative medication for the prevention of pain, inflammation, and swelling.</li><li><strong>Dentistry</strong>: Post-operative and post-traumatic pain, inflammation, and swelling.</li><li><strong>Other indications</strong>: For the prevention of pain and treatment of inflammation and swelling in patients operated in the urogenital tract, renal and biliary colic.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Voltalin D 46.5 mg is a potent non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) with pronounced anti-rheumatic, anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic properties. It also has some uricosuric effects.</p><p>Diclofenac exerts its effect by inhibiting prostaglandin biosynthesis, which plays a major role in causing inflammation, pain, and fever. Diclofenac is rapidly and completely absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract when taken with or after meals. Peak plasma concentrations are reached within an average of 2 hours after ingestion. At therapeutic concentrations, it is 99.7% bound to plasma proteins. Diclofenac is metabolized in the liver and undergoes first-pass metabolism.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet/Suppository/GelAdults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Enteric-coated tablet: 75-150 mg daily in two or three divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Sustained-release tablets: 1 tablet daily, taken whole with liquid, preferably during meals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Suppositories: 75-150 mg daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gel: Depending on the painful site, 2-4 g of gel may be applied 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Enteric-coated tablet: 1-3 mg/kg per day in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Sustained-release tablets: Not recommended.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Suppositories: 1-3 mg/kg body weight in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adults: 1 ampoule daily, or in severe cases, twice daily by intramuscular injection.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Renal colic: 1 ampoule daily intramuscularly. A further ampoule may be administered after 30 minutes if necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant use with diuretics can inhibit their activity.</li><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics may cause increased serum potassium levels.</li><li>Diclofenac may interact with digoxin, methotrexate, cyclosporine, or lithium, requiring monitoring for specific toxicities.</li><li>Concurrent use with other NSAIDs or glucocorticoids may increase side effects like gastrointestinal bleeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient of Voltalin D.</li><li>Peptic ulcers, hypersensitivity to Diclofenac, or other NSAIDs.</li><li>Contraindicated in asthmatic patients who may have attacks triggered by acetylsalicylic acid or other prostaglandin inhibitors.</li><li>Do not use under occlusive airtight dressings for Voltalin D Gel.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Epigastric pain, eructation, nausea, diarrhea, dizziness, and headache.</li><li><strong>Less common</strong>: Peripheral edema, skin reactions (rash, eczema), and local irritation with Voltalin D Gel.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Diclofenac should be avoided during pregnancy unless the potential benefit outweighs the risk to the fetus.</li><li>Diclofenac is not recommended during the first trimester of pregnancy.</li><li>A small amount of Diclofenac may pass into breast milk but is unlikely to affect the infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor for peptic ulceration or gastrointestinal bleeding.</li><li>Use caution in elderly patients or those with advanced age.</li><li>Voltalin D Gel should not contact the eyes or mucous membranes. Wash hands thoroughly after application and avoid ingestion.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</li><li>Keep below 30°C and out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Voltalin D 46.5 mg works by inhibiting the biosynthesis of prostaglandins, which are responsible for causing inflammation, pain, and fever.</p>', 'active', '1028_1742669814.webp', 40, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-22 23:56:54', '2025-05-05 17:52:36'),
(4156, 'Dexilend', 'Dexlansoprazole', 16.00, 8, 'capsule', '8 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '60 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is the information in a more organized format with headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Dexilend 60 mg</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis (EE):</strong> Dexilend 60 mg is indicated for the healing of all grades of erosive esophagitis for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> Dexilend 60 mg is indicated to maintain healing of EE and relief of heartburn for up to 6 months.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD):</strong> Dexilend 60 mg is indicated for the treatment of heartburn associated with symptomatic non-erosive GERD for 4 weeks.</li></ol><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Dexilend 60 mg is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI) that inhibits gastric acid secretion. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole, a racemic mixture of R- and S-enantiomers. Dexilend 60 mg is a delayed-release formulation in a capsule for oral administration, containing two types of enteric-coated granules with different pH-dependent dissolution profiles. It suppresses gastric acid secretion by inhibiting the (H+/K+)-ATPase in gastric parietal cells.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Dexilend 60 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis &amp; Relief of Heartburn:</strong> 30 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive GERD:</strong> 30 mg once daily for 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 60 mg once daily for up to 8 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Dexilend 60 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Can be taken without regard to food. Swallow the capsule whole.</li><li>Alternatively, open the capsule, sprinkle the intact granules on one tablespoon of applesauce, and swallow immediately. Granules should not be chewed.</li><li>If a dose is missed, take it as soon as possible, but if it’s near the next dose, take only the prescribed amount at the appointed time. Do not double the dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With medicines:</strong> Atazanavir, Warfarin, Tacrolimus, Clopidogrel, Methotrexate.</li><li><strong>With food &amp; others:</strong> No data available.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Dexilend 60 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and flatulence.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>There are no adequate or well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Dexilend 60 mg should not be administered to pregnant women unless the expected benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li>It is not known if Dexilend 60 mg is excreted in human milk, but it is excreted in the milk of rats. It should not be given to nursing mothers unless considered essential.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Gastric malignancy, Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea, bone fractures, hypomagnesemia, and concomitant use with Methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Dexilend 60 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>No reports of significant overdose. Multiple doses of 120 mg or a single dose of 300 mg did not result in death or severe adverse events.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, in a dry place, protected from light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics:</strong> No dosage adjustment is necessary for elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in patients below 12 years old have not been established.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment is necessary.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment is necessary for mild hepatic impairment. For moderate hepatic impairment, a maximum daily dose of 30 mg may be considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Dexilend 60 mg suppresses gastric acid secretion by inhibiting the (H+/K+)-ATPase in gastric parietal cells. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole and is delivered in a Dual Delayed Release (DDR) formulation, with two distinct peaks in plasma concentration.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><ul><li>Category B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies with Dexilend 60 mg in pregnant women.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and effectiveness of Dexilend 60 mg in patients below 12 years old have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li>No dose adjustment is necessary for elderly patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong></p><ul><li>No dose adjustment is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong></p><ul><li>No dose adjustment is necessary for mild hepatic impairment. Consider a maximum daily dose of 30 mg for patients with moderate hepatic impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '1670_1742670187.jpg', 27, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:03:07', '2025-05-03 08:22:05'),
(4157, 'Supravit-G', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral [A-Z gold preparation]', 10.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', 'N/A', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Supravit-G</strong></p><p>Supravit-G is indicated for preventing and treating deficiencies in vitamins and minerals. This comprehensive daily nutritional supplement is also recommended to meet the heightened requirements for vitamins and minerals in various situations, including physical and emotional stress, chronic diseases, infectious illnesses, osteoporosis, injuries or wounds, surgeries, poor digestion, old age, pregnancy and lactation, decreased appetite, excessive dieting, exposure to environmental pollution, and strenuous exercise, among others.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each tablet of Supravit-G contains the following essential nutrients:</p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin A:</strong> 5000 IU</li><li><strong>Vitamin C:</strong> 60 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin D:</strong> 400 IU</li><li><strong>Vitamin E:</strong> 30 IU</li><li><strong>Vitamin K:</strong> 25 mcg</li><li><strong>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine):</strong> 1.5 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin):</strong> 1.7 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin B3 (Niacin):</strong> 20 mg</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine):</strong> 2 mg</li><li><strong>Folic acid:</strong> 400 mcg</li><li><strong>Biotin:</strong> 30 mcg</li><li><strong>Pantothenate:</strong> 10 mg</li><li><strong>Calcium:</strong> 162 mg</li><li><strong>Iron:</strong> 18 mg</li><li><strong>Phosphorus:</strong> 109 mg</li><li><strong>Iodine:</strong> 150 mcg</li><li><strong>Magnesium:</strong> 100 mg</li><li><strong>Zinc:</strong> 15 mg</li><li><strong>Selenium:</strong> 20 mcg</li><li><strong>Copper:</strong> 2 mg</li><li><strong>Manganese:</strong> 2 mg</li><li><strong>Chromium:</strong> 120 mcg</li><li><strong>Molybdenum:</strong> 75 mcg</li><li><strong>Chloride:</strong> 72 mg</li><li><strong>Potassium:</strong> 80 mg</li><li><strong>Boron:</strong> 150 mcg</li><li><strong>Nickel:</strong> 5 mcg</li><li><strong>Silicon:</strong> 2 mg</li><li><strong>Tin:</strong> 10 mcg</li><li><strong>Vanadium:</strong> 10 mcg</li><li><strong>Lutein:</strong> 250 mcg</li><li><strong>Cyanocobalamin:</strong> 6 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Multivitamin + Multimineral A-Z Gold Preparation is a supplement that combines most vitamins and minerals in a single tablet. This preparation typically contains 32 high-potency vitamins and minerals. It maintains a healthy body and an active lifestyle by providing essential nutrients that may be lacking in one\'s diet.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Supravit-G</strong></p><p>The general dosage for adults is one tablet daily. The tablet can be taken with or without food. If you are pregnant or lactating, you may need to take a higher dosage of Supravit-G.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Supravit-G</strong></p><p>Supravit-G is generally considered safe and has no known drug interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Supravit-G is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Supravit-G</strong></p><p>In most cases, this preparation is well tolerated. However, there may be occasional occurrences of diarrhea while undergoing beta-carotene treatment, and it\'s possible for the skin to take on a mild yellowish tint. Vitamin C and vitamin E may lead to diarrhea and other gastrointestinal disturbances.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Supravit-G is generally safe for pregnant and lactating women, but it is important to speak to your doctor before taking any supplement during pregnancy or lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Long term intake of high levels of vitamin A (excluding that sourced from beta carotene) may increase the risk of osteoporosis in postmenopausal women.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>This is a film-coated tablet, which combines 32 high-potency vitamins and minerals. This preparation maintains a healthy body and an active lifestyle.</p>', 'active', '867_1742670292.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:04:52', '2025-05-04 21:28:08'),
(4158, 'Fixolin', 'Doxophylline', 8.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Fixolin 200 mg</strong></p><p>Fixolin 200 mg is used to treat the following indications:</p><ul><li>Bronchial asthma</li><li>Bronchospasm</li><li>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</li><li>Pulmonary disease with a spastic bronchial component</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Fixolin 200 mg is a novel bronchodilator that structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. It selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. However, unlike Theophylline, Fixolin 200 mg appears to have decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, potentially accounting for its improved safety profile. Additionally, it is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Fixolin 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> 200 mg tablet two or three times daily</li><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 400 mg tablet two or three times daily or as prescribed by the physician</li></ul><blockquote><strong>Children:</strong>12 years of age: 10 ml syrup or 200 mg tablet two or three times daily</blockquote><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><br></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years of age: 6-9 mg/kg body weight two times daily, i.e., if body weight is 10 kg, 3 ml (60 mg) two times daily, or as prescribed by the physician</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Fixolin 200 mg</strong></p><p>Fixolin 200 mg should not be administered together with other xanthine derivatives. Toxic synergism with ephedrine has been documented for xanthines. Like other xanthines, concomitant therapy with troleandomycin, lincomycin, clindamycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, ranitidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccine may decrease the hepatic clearance of xanthines, leading to an increase in blood levels.</p><p>No evidence of a relationship between Fixolin 200 mg serum concentrations and toxic events has been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Fixolin 200 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Acute myocardial infarction</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Lactating women</li><li>Patients who have shown hypersensitivity to its components</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Fixolin 200 mg</strong></p><p>Fixolin 200 mg rarely causes serious side effects. However, possible side effects are similar to those of taking an excess amount of caffeine. These include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Headache</li><li>Upset stomach</li><li>Heartburn</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Animal reproduction studies indicate that Fixolin 200 mg does not cause fetal harm when administered to pregnant animals or affect reproduction capacity. However, due to limited experience in pregnant humans, xanthines should only be given to pregnant women when clearly necessary. Fixolin 200 mg is contraindicated in nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The half-life of xanthine derivatives is influenced by a number of known variables. It may be prolonged in:</p><ul><li>Patients with liver disease</li><li>Patients with congestive heart failure</li><li>Patients taking certain other drugs like erythromycin, troleandomycin, lincomycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccine</li></ul><p>In these cases, a lower dose of Fixolin 200 mg may be needed. Phenytoin, other anticonvulsants, and smoking may cause an increase in clearance with a shorter mean half-life. In these cases, higher doses of Fixolin 200 mg may be needed.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Fixolin 200 mg</strong></p><p>In case of overdose, severe cardiac arrhythmias and tonic-clonic seizures may occur. These effects may represent the first signs of intoxication. The appearance of side effects may require discontinuation of the treatment, which, if necessary, at the physician’s discretion, may be resumed at lower doses after all signs and symptoms of toxicity have subsided. As there is no specific antidote, in case of overdose, symptomatic treatment of cardiovascular collapse should be instituted.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children. Fixolin 200 mg should be used only on the prescription of a specialist physician.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Fixolin 200 mg is a novel bronchodilator. It structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. Fixolin 200 mg selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, thereby relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. However, differently from Theophylline, Fixolin 200 mg appears to have decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, which may account for the better safety profile of the drug. Fixolin 200 mg is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p>', 'active', '1439_1742670420.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:07:00', '2025-05-03 07:58:33'),
(4159, 'Famomax', 'Famotidine', 90.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '40 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Famomax 50 ml</strong></p><p>Famomax 50 ml is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Gastric ulcer</li><li>Duodenal ulcer</li><li>Anastomotic ulcer</li><li>Acute stress ulcer</li><li>Reflux esophagitis</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Acute gastritis</li><li>Chronic gastritis in the acute exacerbation stage</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>H2 receptor antagonist</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Famomax 50 ml is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist. It completely inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors of parietal cells, reducing gastric acid secretion. Famomax inhibits both basal and pentagastrin-stimulated gastric acid secretion. Its H2-receptor antagonist activity is slowly reversible since the drug dissociates slowly from the H2-receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer</strong>: 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks</li><li><strong>Benign gastric ulcer</strong>: 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks; Maintenance therapy: 20 mg at night to prevent recurrence of duodenal ulceration</li><li><strong>Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</strong>: 20 mg twice daily for 6 to 12 weeks</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: Start with 20 mg every 6 hours, adjust based on individual response. Some patients have received up to 160 mg every 6 hours without significant adverse effects.</li><li>Famomax can be administered irrespective of meals, and antacids may be taken concomitantly if needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Tablet Dosage for Adults</strong>:</p><ul><li>For gastric ulcer, duodenal ulcer, anastomotic ulcer, upper gastrointestinal hemorrhage, reflux esophagitis, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</li><li>Usual dose: 20 mg twice daily or 40 mg once at bedtime.</li><li>For acute and chronic gastritis (acute exacerbation stage):</li><li>20 mg twice a day or 40 mg once a day at bedtime.</li><li>For maintenance therapy: 20 mg once daily, or as directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Powder for Suspension</strong> (Pediatric Dosage):</p><ul><li><strong>GERD</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">&lt;1 year: 0.5 mg/kg/dose once daily for up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">3-11 months: 0.5 mg/kg/dose twice daily for up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-2 months: 0.5 mg/kg/dose once daily for up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-16 years: 1 mg/kg/day divided twice daily, up to 40 mg b.i.d.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer</strong>: 0.5 mg/kg/day, once at bedtime or divided b.i.d., up to 40 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Peptic ulcer</strong>: 0.5 mg/kg/day, once at bedtime or divided b.i.d., up to 40 mg/day.</li><li><strong>Reflux esophagitis</strong>: 2 mg/kg/day.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 40 mg three times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Intravenous Injection (Adults)</strong>:</p><p>For hospitalized patients with pathological hypersecretory conditions or intractable ulcers, Famomax Injection may be given intravenously:</p><ul><li>20 mg every 12 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Intravenous Dosage</strong>:</p><p>Starting dose: 0.25 mg/kg intravenously (injected over at least two minutes or as a 15-minute infusion) every 12 hours, up to 40 mg/day.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Famomax 50 ml</strong></p><p>No clinically important drug interactions have been identified. Famomax 50 ml does not interact with the cytochrome P450-linked drug-metabolizing enzyme system.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Known hypersensitivity to any component of the drug.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Famomax 50 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Eruption</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Tachycardia</li><li>High blood pressure</li><li>Headache</li><li>Drowsiness or insomnia (rarely)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Famomax 50 ml should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. It is known to cross the placenta.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether Famomax 50 ml is excreted in human milk, so nursing mothers should either stop breastfeeding or stop taking the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use the minimum required dose depending on the disease condition.</li><li>Exercise caution when administering Famomax to elderly patients, patients with renal failure, or hepatic disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Famomax 50 ml is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist that inhibits histamine action on H2-receptors of parietal cells, reducing gastric acid secretion. Its activity is slowly reversible as the drug dissociates slowly from the H2-receptor.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy Category</strong></p><p>Category B. There are no adequate, well-controlled studies on Famomax 50 ml in pregnancy, but it should be prescribed only if clearly needed. Caution is advised when administering Famomax to nursing women.</p>', 'active', '149_1742670569.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:09:29', '2025-05-03 10:27:04');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4160, 'Emep', 'Esomeprazole', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Emep 20 mg</strong></p><p>Emep 20 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>To alleviate GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn and others.</li><li>To treat erosive esophagitis.</li><li>To maintain the ability to recover from erosive esophagitis.</li><li>For the eradication of Helicobacter pylori infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease, when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</li><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Emep 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. Most patients heal within 4 to 8 weeks. An additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered if healing does not occur.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication</strong> (Triple Therapy to reduce duodenal ulcer recurrence): Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days, Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: The dose is 20-80 mg once daily. Dosage should be adjusted individually, and treatment continued as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks, depending on the response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: 40 mg Emep 20 mg once daily by intravenous injection (not less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes). Esomeprazole IV injection should not be administered concomitantly with any other medications through the same intravenous site.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Emep 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take one Emep 20 mg pill or capsule whole, one hour before a meal.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>: Mix the contents of the packet in 15 ml of water. Stir, wait 2-3 minutes for thickening, stir again, and consume within 30 minutes.</li><li>If administering through a nasogastric or gastric tube, shake the syringe with 15 ml of water to thicken the suspension, wait 2-3 minutes, then administer into the stomach.</li><li>For intravenous injection, esomeprazole should be given gradually over a minimum of three minutes. Do not use if there are visible particles in the solution.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Emep 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is metabolized by the liver through CYP2C19 and CYP3A4.</li><li>It does not clinically interact with medications processed by CYP1A2, 2A6, 2C9, 2D6, 2E1, and 3A4.</li><li>May reduce the clearance of drugs like diazepam (45% reduction) when co-administered.</li><li>Esomeprazole may interfere with the absorption of certain drugs (e.g., ketoconazole, iron salts, digoxin).</li><li>It does not interact with oral contraceptives, phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, or amoxicillin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole should not be used by those with known hypersensitivity to any of its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Emep 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, and dry mouth.</li><li>Side effects are similar in both short-term and long-term treatments.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>A positive symptom response does not exclude stomach cancer.</li><li>Take esomeprazole capsules at least one hour before meals.</li><li>For patients who have difficulty swallowing capsules, the contents can be mixed with a spoonful of cold applesauce. Do not chew or crush the pellets.</li><li>Antacids can be taken with esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Emep 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole overdose has been lethal in rats at a dose of 510 mg/kg. Symptoms include decreased motor activity, changes in breathing, tremors, ataxia, and occasional convulsions.</li><li>There are no known specific countermeasures for overdose, and dialysis is not expected to remove esomeprazole. Symptomatic and supportive care is recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry area at temperatures not exceeding 30°C.</li><li>Protect from moisture and light.</li><li>Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole inhibits the H+/K+-ATPase in gastric parietal cells, reducing stomach acid production.</li><li>It is the S-isomer of omeprazole and provides superior acid control.</li><li>Esomeprazole is extensively metabolized by the liver, primarily by CYP2C19, and excreted in urine (80%) and feces (20%) as inactive metabolites.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>There is insufficient data on the use of esomeprazole in pregnant women. No teratogenic effects have been observed in animal studies.</li><li>Esomeprazole is excreted in milk, so breastfeeding should be discontinued if esomeprazole is deemed necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No significant differences in safety or efficacy between elderly and younger patients, though older patients may be more sensitive.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage adjustment is needed for mild to moderate hepatic insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: As esomeprazole is minimally excreted unchanged in the urine, renal impairment is unlikely to affect its pharmacokinetics.</li></ul>', 'active', '254_1742670686.webp', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:11:26', '2025-05-03 10:04:57'),
(4161, 'Carticare Max', 'Glucosamine Sulfate + Diacerein', 22.00, 9, 'tablet', '9 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '750 mg+50 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Carticare Max</strong></p><p>This is primarily indicated in conditions like Osteoarthritis, Rheumatoid arthritis, and Bone &amp; Joint injuries.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Stimulation of Cartilage formation.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Glucosamine is a natural substance produced in the human body and found in certain foods. It stimulates the production of glycosaminoglycan and proteoglycan, two essential building blocks of cartilage. Glucosamine acts as a joint lubricant and shock absorber, necessary to maintain healthy cartilage and joint function.</p><p>Diacerein is an anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic drug, specially indicated for the treatment of arthritic conditions. It directly inhibits IL-1 synthesis and release in vitro and down-modulates IL-1 induced activities, relieving Osteoarthritis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Carticare Max</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult &amp; Children above 12 years</strong>: One tablet (Glucosamine Sulphate 750 mg and Diacerein 50 mg) twice daily for 4-12 weeks, taken with food.</li><li><strong>Creatinine Clearance (CrCl) &lt; 30 ml/min</strong>: The dose of Diacerein should be reduced.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Carticare Max</strong></p><p>There have been no reports of significant drug interactions of Glucosamine with antibiotics, antidepressants, antihypertensives, nitrates, antiarrhythmics, anxiolytics, hypoglycemic agents, or antisecretives. However, decreased absorption of Diacerein may occur with aluminium and/or magnesium hydroxide antacids.</p><p>There is an increased risk of diarrhea with laxatives and antibiotics. Avoid co-administration with fibers and phytic acids.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Proven hypersensitivity to Glucosamine.</li><li>Diabetics should monitor blood glucose levels regularly when taking Glucosamine.</li><li>No special studies have been performed in patients with renal and/or hepatic insufficiency, but it is recommended that patients with severe hepatic or renal insufficiency should use this medication under appropriate medical supervision.</li><li>Children should not be supplemented with Glucosamine.</li><li>Contraindicated for those allergic to Diacerein, shellfish (e.g., shrimp, crab), or those with diabetes, asthma, alcohol dependence, or liver disease.</li><li>Regular monitoring of complete blood count, liver function, and urinalysis is advised while taking the drug.</li><li>Diacerein is contraindicated in pregnancy, lactation, and in those with hypersensitivity to anthraquinone derivatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Carticare Max</strong></p><ul><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Epigastric pain</li><li>Heartburn</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Headache</li><li>Skin rashes</li><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Intense yellow coloring of urine</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Diacerein is contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation.</li><li>Glucosamine should not be taken by pregnant women and nursing mothers due to a lack of scientific data regarding its effect on the fetus and infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be practiced when administering Glucosamine + Diacerein in patients allergic to Glucosamine and/or Diacerein, shellfish (e.g., shrimp, crab), or those with diabetes, asthma, alcohol dependence, or liver disease.</li><li>Complete blood count, liver function, and urinalysis should be monitored regularly before taking Glucosamine + Diacerein.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Carticare Max</strong></p><ul><li>Diarrhea may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool &amp; dry place, protect from light &amp; moisture.</li></ul>', 'active', '1409_1742670787.jpg', 10, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:13:07', '2025-05-03 01:14:08'),
(4162, 'Servipep', 'Famotidine', 6.10, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Servipep 40 mg</h3><p>Servipep 40 mg is indicated in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Gastric ulcer</li><li>Duodenal ulcer</li><li>Anastomotic ulcer</li><li>Acute stress ulcer</li><li>Reflux esophagitis</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Acute gastritis</li><li>Chronic gastritis in the acute exacerbation stage</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>H2 receptor antagonist</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Servipep 40 mg is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist. It completely inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors of parietal cells, reducing gastric acid secretion. The drug inhibits basal, overnight, and pentagastrin-stimulated gastric acid secretion. The H2-receptor antagonist activity of Servipep 40 mg is slowly reversible, as the drug dissociates slowly from H2-receptors.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer</strong>: 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks</li><li><strong>Benign gastric ulcer</strong>: 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks; Maintenance therapy: 20 mg at night to prevent recurrences of duodenal ulceration</li><li><strong>Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</strong>: 20 mg twice daily for 6 to 12 weeks</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: Starting dose 20 mg every six hours; may increase up to 160 mg every six hours</li><li><strong>Acute gastritis and chronic gastritis in acute exacerbation</strong>: 20 mg orally twice a day or 40 mg once a day at bedtime</li><li><strong>Maintenance therapy</strong>: 20 mg once daily</li></ul><h3>Dosage for Specific Forms</h3><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 20 mg twice daily (after breakfast and after supper or bedtime) or 40 mg once daily at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For <strong>maintenance therapy</strong>, 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Powder for Suspension (Pediatric)</strong>:</li><li>For <strong>GERD</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">&lt;1 year of age: 0.5 mg/kg/dose once daily up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Age 3-11 months: 0.5 mg/kg/dose twice daily up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Age 1-2 months: 0.5 mg/kg/dose once daily up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-16 years: 1 mg/kg/day, divided twice daily (up to 40 mg twice daily)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal ulcer</strong>: 0.5 mg/kg/day, at bedtime or divided twice daily, up to 40 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Peptic ulcer</strong>: 0.5 mg/kg/day at bedtime or divided twice daily, up to 40 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance therapy</strong>: 40 mg at night</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Reflux esophagitis</strong>: 2 mg/kg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 40 mg 3 times daily</li><li><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 20 mg intravenously every 12 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric patients</strong> (1-16 years): 0.25 mg/kg intravenously, up to 40 mg/day</li></ul><h3>Interaction</h3><p>No clinically important drug interactions have been identified. Servipep 40 mg does not interact with the cytochrome P450-linked drug-metabolizing enzyme system.</p><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to any component of the drug.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li>Eruption, constipation, diarrhea, dry mouth, nausea, vomiting, tachycardia, high blood pressure, headache, drowsiness, insomnia may rarely occur.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: There are no adequate, well-controlled studies on Servipep 40 mg in pregnancy, but it is known to cross the placenta and should be prescribed only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether Servipep 40 mg is secreted into human milk, so nursing mothers should either stop nursing or discontinue the drug.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>The drug should be used at the minimum required dosage depending on the condition being treated.</li><li>Use with caution in elderly patients, patients with renal failure, and those with hepatic disorders.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Servipep 40 mg is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist. It inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors in parietal cells, thereby reducing gastric acid secretion. The drug dissociates slowly from the H2-receptor, making its activity slowly reversible.</p><h3>Pregnancy Category</h3><p>Category B: No adequate, well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Caution is advised when administering to nursing women.</p>', 'active', '44_1742670870.jpg', 57, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:14:30', '2025-03-23 00:14:30'),
(4163, 'Rupa', 'Rupatadine Fumarate 5 mg/5 ml', 75.00, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '5 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Rupa 60 ml: Comprehensive Information</h3><h4><strong>Indications</strong>:</h4><ul><li><strong>Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</strong></li><li><strong>Urticaria</strong></li></ul><h4><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</h4><ul><li><strong>Non-sedating antihistamines</strong></li></ul><h4><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</h4><ul><li>Rupa 60 ml contains <strong>Rupatadine</strong>, a second-generation, non-sedating, long-acting histamine antagonist. It selectively targets peripheral <strong>H1 receptors</strong> and inhibits platelet-activating factor (PAF) receptors. This dual action provides anti-allergic effects by:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Inhibiting the degranulation of mast cells</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Blocking the release of cytokines (including <strong>TNF-α</strong>)</li></ul><h4><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong>:</h4><ol><li><strong>Rupatadine Tablets</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: 10 mg once daily (with or without food).</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Rupatadine Oral Solution (for children aged 2 to 11 years)</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children ≥ 25 kg</strong>: 1 teaspoon (5 ml) once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 10 kg to &lt; 25 kg</strong>: 1/2 teaspoon (2.5 ml) once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">(With or without food).</li></ul><h4><strong>Interactions</strong>:</h4><ul><li><strong>With medicines</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Ketoconazole</strong> and <strong>Erythromycin</strong> increase systemic exposure to Rupatadine, requiring caution.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Caution should be observed when co-administered with <strong>CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong>, <strong>statins</strong>, <strong>CNS depressants</strong>, or <strong>alcohol</strong>.</li><li><strong>With food</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Grapefruit</strong> and <strong>grapefruit juice</strong> should not be taken with Rupatadine.</li></ul><h4><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</h4><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to Rupatadine or any of its excipients.</li></ul><h4><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</h4><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Asthenia, dizziness, drowsiness.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Increased appetite, back pain, gastrointestinal discomfort, rash, dry throat, and more.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Palpitations, tachycardia.</li></ul><h4><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</h4><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B2 (TGA, Australia). No clinical data available; use only if the benefit outweighs risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: No data on excretion in breast milk; should be avoided unless the benefit for the mother justifies potential risks to the infant.</li></ul><h4><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong>:</h4><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with prolonged <strong>QT interval</strong>, <strong>hypokalemia</strong>, and <strong>proarrhythmic conditions</strong>.</li><li>Caution in <strong>elderly patients (65+)</strong>.</li><li>No clinical experience with patients having <strong>renal</strong> or <strong>hepatic insufficiency</strong>; use is not recommended.</li></ul><h4><strong>Overdose Effects</strong>:</h4><ul><li>The most common adverse effect is <strong>somnolence</strong>.</li><li><strong>Management</strong>: Symptomatic treatment with supportive measures.</li></ul><h4><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>:</h4><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool, dry place</strong> below <strong>30°C</strong>.</li><li>Protect from <strong>light</strong> and <strong>moisture</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of <strong>reach of children</strong>.</li></ul><h4><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong>:</h4><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Use with caution; no specific dose adjustments suggested.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established for those under 12 years.</li><li><strong>Renal or Hepatic Insufficiency</strong>: Use is not recommended.</li></ul><h4><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</h4><ul><li><strong>Rupatadine</strong> works by blocking <strong>H1 receptors</strong> and <strong>PAF receptors</strong>, both of which are involved in allergic reactions like <strong>bronchoconstriction</strong> and increased <strong>vascular permeability</strong>. This dual mechanism leads to better therapeutic effects compared to isolated antihistamines.</li></ul><p>This summary encapsulates the most important points about <strong>Rupa 60 ml</strong>, focusing on its uses, dosage, interactions, side effects, and other critical considerations.</p>', 'active', '584_1742670966.jpg', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:16:06', '2025-05-04 20:34:29'),
(4164, 'Fexomin', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 8.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '120 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here’s a summarized overview of the key points regarding Fexomin 120 mg:</p><h3><strong>Indications</strong>:</h3><ol><li><strong>Allergic Rhinitis</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For adults and children 12+ years, Fexomin helps alleviate symptoms of seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis, such as sneezing, runny nose, itchy eyes, and throat.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">It enhances productivity and health-related quality of life.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommended for adults and children 12+ years to treat symptoms such as wheals and pruritus.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Improves productivity and quality of life.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Non-sedating antihistamines</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</h3><ul><li>A selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist.</li><li>Quickly absorbed after oral administration with peak plasma concentration in 2-3 hours.</li><li>Does not cross the blood-brain barrier.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>For Adults and children 12+ years</strong>: 60 mg twice daily or 120 mg once daily or 180 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>For Children 6-11 years</strong>: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>For Children 2-11 years</strong> (suspension): 30 mg or 5 ml twice daily.</li><li>Adjust dosage in cases of impaired renal function.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Fexomin does not undergo hepatic biotransformation, so interactions with other medications are limited.</li><li>Co-administration with erythromycin or ketoconazole can increase plasma levels of Fexomin.</li><li>Avoid combining with aluminum and magnesium hydroxide-containing antacids (wait 2 hours between doses).</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Fexomin or any of its components.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Headaches, fatigue, dizziness, nausea.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Hypersensitivity reactions (e.g., angioedema, anaphylaxis), palpitations, tachycardia.</li><li><strong>Post-marketing</strong>: Anxiety, insomnia, rash, and pruritus.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C (avoid unless benefits outweigh risks).</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Avoid use as Fexomin can pass into breast milk.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Use cautiously in elderly or patients with renal or hepatic impairment.</li><li>Can cause tachycardia and palpitations; monitor cardiovascular health.</li><li>Unlikely to impair the ability to drive or operate machinery but monitor individual reactions.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Symptoms may include dry mouth, sleepiness, fatigue, and dizziness.</li><li>No known upper limit of tolerable dosage.</li><li>Standard procedures for overdose treatment apply (e.g., removal of unabsorbed medication).</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from heat and light. Keep out of children’s reach.</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Fexomin is an <strong>inverse agonist</strong> of the H1 receptor, preventing allergic responses like itching, sneezing, and runny nose without affecting other neurotransmitter systems.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>:</h3><ul><li>In renal impairment, children may need adjusted doses. Fexomin is safe for children aged 2 and up but should be used with caution in young children.</li></ul><p>This summary covers the essential details of Fexomin 120 mg, including its use, dosage guidelines, side effects, and more. Let me know if you need more information!</p>', 'active', '1535_1742671084.jpg', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:18:04', '2025-05-02 17:55:47'),
(4165, 'Xenovate', 'Clobetasol Propionate', 150.00, 1, 'x 30 gm tube', '30 gm tube', 'Ointment', '0.05%', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xenovate 0.05%</strong></p><p>Clobetasol Propionate is indicated for adults, elderly, and children over 1 year for the following dermatoses:</p><ul><li>Psoriasis (excluding widespread plaque psoriasis)</li><li>Recalcitrant dermatoses</li><li>Lichen planus</li><li>Discoid lupus erythematosus</li><li>Other skin conditions that do not respond satisfactorily to less potent steroids</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Other Topical Corticosteroids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Clobetasol Propionate is a very potent topical corticosteroid. It has anti-inflammatory, antipruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties. It shows anti-inflammatory activity via multiple mechanisms to inhibit late-phase allergic reactions. It decreases the density of mast cells, chemotaxis, and activation of eosinophils. It also reduces cytokine production and inhibits the metabolism of arachidonic acid.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Xenovate 0.05%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cream &amp; Ointment</strong>:</li><li>Adults, elderly, and children over 1 year: Apply a thin layer of Clobetasol Propionate Cream or Ointment to the affected skin areas twice daily and rub in gently and completely. Repeated short courses of Clobetasol Propionate may be used to control exacerbations.</li><li>In more resistant lesions, especially where there is hyperkeratosis, the effect of Clobetasol can be enhanced, if necessary, by occluding the treatment area with polythene film. Overnight occlusion is usually adequate to bring about a satisfactory response.</li><li><strong>Important</strong>: Treatment should be limited to 2 consecutive weeks. The maximum weekly dose should not exceed 50 gm/week. For children, courses should be limited to five days and reviewed weekly.</li><li><strong>Scalp Solution</strong>:</li><li>Apply the required quantity of spray to the affected areas of the scalp once or twice daily and gently rub in. The total dose applied should not exceed 50 ml weekly. Therapy should be discontinued if no response is noted after one week or when the lesion heals. Clobetasol Scalp Solution should be used for brief periods only.</li><li><strong>Topical Spray</strong>:</li><li>Spray directly onto the affected skin areas twice daily and rub in gently and completely. The total dosage should not exceed 50 g (59 ml or 2 fluid ounces) per week. Do not use more than 26 sprays per application or 52 sprays per day.</li><li>Treatment should be limited to 4 weeks. If no improvement is seen within 2 weeks, reassessment of the diagnosis may be necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Xenovate 0.05%</strong></p><ul><li>Route of administration: Cutaneous.</li><li><strong>Creams</strong> are suitable for moist or weeping surfaces.</li><li><strong>Ointments</strong> are appropriate for dry, lichenified, or scaly lesions.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Xenovate 0.05%</strong></p><p>Co-administered drugs that inhibit CYP3A4 (e.g., ritonavir, itraconazole) can inhibit the metabolism of corticosteroids, leading to increased systemic exposure.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to any component of the preparation. It should not be used in:</p><ul><li>Rosacea</li><li>Acne vulgaris</li><li>Perioral dermatitis</li><li>Perianal and genital pruritus</li><li>Pruritus without inflammation</li><li>Untreated cutaneous infections</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xenovate 0.05%</strong></p><ul><li>Most common side effects: Burning and stinging sensation.</li><li>Less frequent side effects: Itching, skin atrophy, cracking and fissuring of the skin.</li><li>Cushing syndrome has been reported in infants and adults as a result of prolonged use.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>There are limited data on the use of Clobetasol Propionate cream in pregnant women. Topical administration to pregnant animals can cause abnormalities in fetal development. Its relevance to humans is unclear, but it should only be used if the benefit to the mother outweighs the possible risks.</p><p>It is unknown whether Clobetasol Propionate is excreted in human milk, so caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>If using occlusive dressings, cleanse the skin before applying a fresh dressing.</li><li>Topical corticosteroids should be used with caution in psoriasis, as rebound relapses and local or systemic toxicity may occur.</li><li>Treatment on the face should be limited to 5 days.</li><li>Avoid applying Clobetasol Propionate near the eyes, as repeated exposure may lead to cataracts or glaucoma.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xenovate 0.05%</strong></p><p>Acute overdosage is very unlikely. In the case of chronic overdosage or misuse, signs of hypercortisolism may occur. In such cases, topical steroids should be discontinued.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Do not freeze. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>In infants and children under 12 years, long-term continuous topical corticosteroid therapy should be avoided, as it can cause adrenal suppression. Children are more susceptible to the atrophic effects of topical corticosteroids.</p>', 'active', '282_1742671167.webp', 8, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:19:27', '2025-05-05 18:12:52'),
(4166, 'Joinix D', 'Glucosamine Sulfate + Diacerein', 12.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '750 mg+50 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Joinix D</strong></p><p>Joinix D is primarily indicated for conditions such as:</p><ul><li>Osteoarthritis</li><li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li><li>Bone &amp; Joint injuries</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Stimulation of Cartilage Formation</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Glucosamine</strong> is a natural substance produced in the human body and found in certain foods. It stimulates the production of glycosaminoglycan and proteoglycan, which are essential building blocks of cartilage. Glucosamine acts as a joint lubricant and shock absorber, necessary to maintain healthy cartilage and joint function.</li><li><strong>Diacerein</strong> is an anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic drug, specifically indicated for the treatment of arthritic conditions. It directly inhibits IL-1 synthesis and release in vitro, downmodulates IL-1 induced activities, and relieves osteoarthritis symptoms.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Joinix D</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children above 12 years</strong>: One tablet (Glucosamine Sulphate 750 mg and Diacerein 50 mg) twice daily for 4-12 weeks, taken with food.</li><li><strong>Creatinine Clearance (CrCl) &lt; 30 ml/min</strong>: The dose of Diacerein should be reduced.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Joinix D</strong></p><ul><li>No significant drug interactions reported for Glucosamine with antibiotics, antidepressants, antihypertensives, nitrates, antiarrhythmics, anxiolytics, hypoglycemic agents, or antisecretives.</li><li>Decreased absorption of Diacerein with aluminum and/or magnesium hydroxide antacids.</li><li>Increased risk of diarrhea with laxatives and antibiotics.</li><li>Avoid co-administration with fibers and phytic acids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>No known contraindications for Glucosamine, but proven hypersensitivity to Glucosamine is a contraindication.</li><li>Diabetics should monitor blood glucose levels regularly while taking Glucosamine.</li><li>No special studies were conducted in patients with renal and/or hepatic insufficiency. However, administration should be under appropriate medical supervision for patients with severe renal or hepatic insufficiency.</li><li>Not recommended for children.</li><li>Diacerein is contraindicated in pregnancy, lactation, and hypersensitivity to anthraquinone derivatives.</li><li>Glucosamine should be avoided in individuals with shellfish allergies (e.g., shrimp, crab), diabetes, asthma, alcohol dependence, or liver disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Joinix D</strong></p><ul><li>Diarrhea, constipation, epigastric pain, heartburn, nausea, vomiting, headache, skin rashes, drowsiness, intense yellow coloring of urine.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Diacerein is contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation.</li><li>Glucosamine should not be taken by pregnant women and nursing mothers due to a lack of scientific data regarding its effect on the fetus and infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution is required when administering Glucosamine + Diacerein to individuals who are allergic to Glucosamine and/or Diacerein or to shellfish (e.g., shrimp, crab); have diabetes, asthma, alcohol dependence, or liver disease.</li><li>Before taking Glucosamine + Diacerein, complete blood count, liver function, and urinalysis should be monitored regularly.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Joinix D</strong></p><ul><li>Diarrhea may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light and moisture.</li></ul>', 'active', '846_1742671297.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:21:37', '2025-05-03 23:01:14'),
(4167, 'Aristo D3', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 2.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2000 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Aristo D3 2000 IU</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated in the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also used as an adjunct to specific therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Vitamin D3 is essential for normal bone growth and development, maintaining bone density, and for the utilization of both calcium and phosphorus. Babies need Vitamin D3 for healthy growth and development, as it acts as a hormone.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. Vitamin D3 is vital for normal bone growth and the maintenance of bone density. It also helps reduce the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, lowers the risk of cancers (such as breast and colorectal), and helps maintain adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Aristo D3 2000 IU</strong></p><p>For <strong>capsules</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks. Maintenance therapy doses are 1,400-2,000 IU/day. Target levels of 25-hydroxyvitamin D should be checked 3-4 months after starting maintenance therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 4 weeks. Higher doses may be needed in certain situations.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Osteoporosis adjunct therapy</strong>: 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li><strong>Children (12-18 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Film-coated tablets</strong>: 1,000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily, or as directed by a physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li><li><strong>Oroflash or chewable tablets</strong>: 1,000-2,000 IU daily, or as directed by a physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li><li><strong>Syrup</strong>:</li><li><strong>For patients at risk of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">0-1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml)</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml)</blockquote><ul><li><strong>For Vitamin D3 deficient patients</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">0-1 year: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 years: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>:</li><li><strong>Prevention</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Infants on Vitamin D-enriched milk: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Infants or children up to 5 years not on Vitamin D-enriched milk: 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adolescents: 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly</strong>: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Special conditions</strong>: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU or 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 3 or 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Vitamin D deficiency</strong>: 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU, can be repeated every 1-6 months.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Aristo D3 2000 IU</strong></p><p>It may interfere with medications such as phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (e.g., liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Aristo D3 2000 IU</strong></p><p>Common side effects include hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, and abdominal pain.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Studies have shown the safe use of doses up to 4,000 IU during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU. However, in women with Vitamin D3 deficiency, a higher dose may be required. Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. Overdose in infants from nursing mothers has not been observed. When prescribing Vitamin D3 to a breastfeeding child, the dose of any additional Vitamin D3 given to the mother should be considered.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution is advised when using Vitamin D3 in patients with impaired renal function.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Aristo D3 2000 IU</strong></p><p>Overdose can lead to hypervitaminosis D.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is essential for normal bone growth and maintaining bone density. It also helps reduce the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, reduces the risk of certain cancers, and supports insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</p>', 'active', '1715_1742671407.webp', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:23:27', '2025-05-01 22:33:05'),
(4168, 'Reset', 'Paracetamol', 1.20, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Reset 500 mg</strong></p><p>Reset 500 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Fever</li><li>Common cold and influenza</li><li>Headache, toothache, earache, bodyache, myalgia, neuralgia</li><li>Dysmenorrhoea</li><li>Sprains, colic pain, back pain</li><li>Post-operative and postpartum pain</li><li>Inflammatory pain and post-vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic and osteoarthritic pain</li><li>Stiffness of joints</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Reset 500 mg has analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity. It acts primarily in the CNS, increasing the pain threshold by inhibiting both isoforms of cyclooxygenase (COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3), which are involved in prostaglandin synthesis. Reset 500 mg (Acetaminophen) is a para-aminophenol derivative, known for being one of the most widely used, safest, and fast-acting analgesics. It is well tolerated and free from many side effects associated with aspirin.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Reset 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Adults</em>: 1-2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours, up to a maximum of 4 gm (8 tablets) daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Children (6-12 years)</em>: ½ to 1 tablet 3 to 4 times daily. For long-term treatment, it’s advisable not to exceed 2.6 gm/day.</li><li><strong>Extended Release Tablet:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years</em>: Two tablets, swallowed whole, every 6 to 8 hours (maximum of 6 tablets in any 24 hours). The tablet must not be crushed.</li><li><strong>Syrup/Suspension:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Children under 3 months</em>: 10 mg/kg body weight (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced) 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>3 months to below 1 year</em>: ½ to 1 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>1-5 years</em>: 1-2 teaspoonfuls 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>6-12 years</em>: 2-4 teaspoonfuls 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Adults</em>: 4-8 teaspoonfuls 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Suppository:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Children (3-12 months)</em>: 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Children (1-5 years)</em>: 125-250 mg 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Children (6-12 years)</em>: 250-500 mg 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Adults &amp; children over 12 years</em>: 0.5-1 gm 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Paediatric Drop:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Children Up to 3 months</em>: 0.5 ml (40 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>4 to 11 months</em>: 1.0 ml (80 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>1 to 2 years</em>: 1.5 ml (120 mg). Do not exceed more than 5 doses daily for a maximum of 5 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Reset 500 mg Tablet with Actizorb Technology</strong></p><p>This technology enables the tablet to dissolve up to five times faster than standard Reset 500 mg tablets. It is a fast-acting and safe analgesic with marked antipyretic properties. It is especially suitable for patients who cannot tolerate aspirin or other analgesics.</p><ul><li><em>Adults and children (12 years and older)</em>: Take 1-2 tablets every four to six hours as needed. Do not exceed 8 tablets in 24 hours.</li><li><em>Children (7 to 11 years)</em>: Take ½-1 tablet every four to six hours as needed. Do not exceed 4 tablets in 24 hours. Not recommended for children under 7 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Reset 500 mg</strong></p><p>Patients who have taken barbiturates, tricyclic antidepressants, and alcohol may show diminished ability to metabolize large doses of Reset 500 mg. Alcohol can increase the hepatotoxicity of Reset 500 mg in cases of overdosage. Chronic ingestion of anticonvulsants or oral steroid contraceptives can induce liver enzymes, preventing therapeutic Reset 500 mg levels by increasing first-pass metabolism or clearance.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in individuals with known hypersensitivity to Reset 500 mg.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Reset 500 mg</strong></p><p>The side effects of Reset 500 mg are generally mild, but hematological reactions, such as thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, pancytopenia, neutropenia, and agranulocytosis, have been reported. Pancreatitis, skin rashes, and other allergic reactions occur occasionally.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy category B according to the USFDA. This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Reset 500 mg is excreted in breast milk. Maternal ingestion of Reset 500 mg at normal therapeutic doses does not appear to pose a risk to the nursing infant.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Reset 500 mg should be administered with caution to patients with impaired kidney or liver function. It should also be used with care in patients taking other drugs that affect the liver.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Reset 500 mg</strong></p><p>Symptoms of an overdose within the first 24 hours may include pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and abdominal pain. Liver damage may become evident 12-48 hours after ingestion. Abnormalities of glucose metabolism and metabolic acidosis may occur.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Reset 500 mg has analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity. It primarily acts in the CNS, raising the pain threshold by inhibiting cyclooxygenase enzymes (COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3) involved in prostaglandin synthesis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Pregnancy category B according to USFDA. This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</p>', 'active', '186_1742671563.png', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:26:03', '2025-05-04 15:14:14');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4169, 'Esotid MUPS', 'Esomeprazole', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Esotid Mups 20 mg</strong></p><p>Esotid Mups 20 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Treatment of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</li><li>Healing of erosive esophagitis</li><li>Maintenance of healing of erosive esophagitis</li><li>Symptomatic GERD</li><li>Reduction of NSAID-associated gastric ulcer</li><li>H. pylori eradication (Triple therapy)</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Esotid Mups 20 mg exerts its stomach acid-suppressing effects by preventing the final step in gastric acid production. It achieves this by covalently binding to sulfhydryl groups of cysteines found on the H+/K+-ATPase enzyme at the secretory surface of gastric parietal cells. This leads to inhibition of both basal and stimulated gastric acid secretion, irrespective of the stimulus. As the binding is irreversible, new enzyme needs to be expressed for acid secretion to resume, resulting in a prolonged antisecretory effect lasting more than 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Esotid Mups 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet or Capsule</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Healing of erosive esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4 to 8 weeks. If healing does not occur after 4-8 weeks, an additional 4-8 weeks treatment may be considered.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance of healing of erosive esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily. Controlled studies did not extend beyond six months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely after 4 weeks, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>H. pylori eradication (Triple therapy)</strong>: Esotid Mups 20 mg 20 mg twice daily for 10 days, Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, and Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The recommended adult dose for GERD with Erosive Esophagitis is either 20 mg or 40 mg Esotid Mups 20 mg given once daily by intravenous injection (no less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Esotid Mups 20 mg</strong></p><p>A solution for intravenous infusion is prepared by first reconstituting the contents of one vial with 5 ml of 0.9% Sodium Chloride and further diluting the resulting solution to a final volume of 50 ml. The resultant concentration after diluting to a final volume of 50 ml is 0.8 mg/ml.</p><ul><li><strong>20 mg dose</strong>: Withdraw 25 ml of the final solution and administer as an intravenous infusion over 10 minutes to 30 minutes.</li><li><strong>10 mg dose</strong>: Withdraw 12.5 ml of the final solution and administer as an intravenous infusion over 10 minutes to 30 minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Esotid Mups 20 mg</strong></p><p>Esotid Mups 20 mg is a selective inhibitor of the cytochrome P450 mono-oxygenase system, which may affect hepatic clearance. However, no clinically relevant interactions have been reported to date. There is some uncertainty about its effect on the oral combined contraceptive pill. Physiological changes, similar to those found with omeprazole, may occur due to the reduction in gastric acid, potentially affecting bacterial colonization in the stomach and duodenum, as well as vitamin B12 absorption.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Esotid Mups 20 mg is contraindicated in patients who have known hypersensitivity to any other components of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Esotid Mups 20 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Abdominal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Esotid Mups 20 mg has a US FDA pregnancy category of C. It should be avoided during pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits outweigh the risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Exclude the possibility of malignancy when gastric ulcer is suspected before treating for dyspepsia.</li><li>When using in combination with antibiotics, refer to the prescribing information of the respective antibiotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p>Solution for injection is prepared by adding 5 ml of 0.9% Sodium Chloride for intravenous injection into the vial containing the dry powder. The reconstituted solution for injection is clear and colorless to very slightly yellow.</p>', 'active', '1611_1742671682.png', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:28:02', '2025-05-02 12:40:19'),
(4170, 'Zolfin', 'Aceclofenac', 4.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information you provided formatted with bold headings for better readability:</p><p><strong>Indications of Zolfin 100 mg</strong></p><p>Zolfin 100 mg is indicated for the relief of pain and inflammation in osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, toothache, trauma, and lumbago.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Zolfin 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zolfin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Extended release tablet</strong>: The recommended dose in adults is one 200 mg Zolfin 100 mg tablet daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Film coated tablet</strong>: The recommended dose in adults is 100 mg, twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zolfin 100 mg</strong></p><p>No significant drug interactions have been observed, but close monitoring of patients is required when it is used with:</p><ul><li><strong>Lithium and Digoxin</strong>: May increase plasma concentration of lithium and digoxin.</li><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: May interact with the activity of diuretics.</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants</strong>: May enhance the activity of anticoagulants.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: May increase the plasma level of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Zolfin 100 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to it or in whom aspirin or NSAIDs precipitate attacks of asthma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zolfin 100 mg</strong></p><p>Zolfin 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of Zolfin 100 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in patients with:</p><ul><li>Active or suspected peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding</li><li>Moderate to severe hepatic impairment</li><li>Cardiac or renal impairment</li><li>Dizziness or urticaria</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Zolfin 100 mg in children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Zolfin 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The use of Zolfin 100 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Zolfin 100 mg in children.</p>', 'active', '830_1742671779.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:29:39', '2025-05-05 22:37:49'),
(4171, 'Flexi SR', 'Aceclofenac', 8.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Flexi SR 200 mg</strong></p><p>Flexi SR 200 mg is indicated for the relief of pain and inflammation in osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, toothache, trauma, and lumbago.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for Osteoarthritis, Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Flexi SR 200 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Flexi SR 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Extended release tablet</strong>: The recommended dose in adults is one 200 mg Flexi SR 200 mg tablet daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Film-coated tablet</strong>: The recommended dose in adults is 100 mg, twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Flexi SR 200 mg</strong></p><p>No significant drug interactions have been observed, but close monitoring of patients is required when it is used with:</p><ul><li><strong>Lithium and Digoxin</strong>: May increase plasma concentration of lithium and digoxin.</li><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: May interact with the activity of diuretics.</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants</strong>: May enhance the activity of anticoagulants.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: May increase the plasma level of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Flexi SR 200 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to it or in whom aspirin or NSAIDs precipitate attacks of asthma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Flexi SR 200 mg</strong></p><p>Flexi SR 200 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of Flexi SR 200 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in patients with active or suspected peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding, moderate to severe hepatic impairment, and cardiac or renal impairment. Caution should also be exercised in patients suffering from dizziness or urticaria.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Flexi SR 200 mg in children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Drugs for Osteoarthritis, Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Flexi SR 200 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The use of Flexi SR 200 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Flexi SR 200 mg in children.</p>', 'active', '1671_1742671884.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:31:24', '2025-05-03 10:47:10'),
(4172, 'Feofol', 'Ferrous Sulfate + Folic Acid', 2.41, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '150 mg+0.5 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Feofol 150 mg+0.5 mg</strong></p><p>This capsule is a haematinic preparation for the treatment and prophylaxis of iron and folic acid deficiency, especially during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Iron &amp; Vitamin Combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p><strong>Ferrous Sulphate (Iron):</strong> Essential component in the formation of hemoglobin; adequate amounts of iron are necessary for effective erythropoiesis; also serves as a cofactor of several essential enzymes, including cytochromes that are involved in electron transport. Replacement of iron stores found in hemoglobin, myoglobin, and enzymes; works to transport oxygen via hemoglobin.</p><p><strong>Folic acid:</strong> Required for nucleoprotein synthesis and the maintenance of normal erythropoiesis; folic acid is converted in the liver and plasma to its metabolically active form, tetrahydrofolic acid, by dihydrofolate reductase; prevents neural tube defects in women of childbearing potential and higher doses required during pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Feofol 150 mg+0.5 mg</strong></p><p>Take Ferrous sulfate and Folic acid on an empty stomach, at least 1 hour before or 2 hours after a meal. Avoid taking antacids or antibiotics within 2 hours before or after taking ferrous sulfate and folic acid. Ferrous sulfate and folic acid are only part of a complete program of treatment that may also include a special diet. It is very important to follow the diet plan created for you by your doctor or nutrition counselor. You should become very familiar with the list of foods you should eat to make sure you get enough iron and folic acid from both your diet and your medication. Do not crush, chew, break, or open the extended-release tablet. Swallow the pill whole. Breaking or opening the pill may cause too much of the drug to be released at one time.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Feofol 150 mg+0.5 mg</strong></p><p>1 (one) capsule a day, throughout pregnancy and lactation. Some patients may need a higher dose because of dietary or other factors.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Feofol 150 mg+0.5 mg</strong></p><p>The absorption of both iron salts and tetracyclines is diminished when they are taken concomitantly by mouth. The absorption of iron salts may also be decreased by magnesium trisilicate-containing antacid.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in patients receiving repeated blood transfusions or in patients with anaemias not produced by iron deficiency unless iron deficiency is also present.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Feofol 150 mg+0.5 mg</strong></p><p>Because iron salts are astringent, gastrointestinal irritation may occur. Nausea and epigastric pain are dose-related.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Category - Not Classified. FDA has not yet classified the drug into a specified pregnancy category.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Iron chelates with antacid and tetracycline and absorption of all these may be impaired if taken concurrently. However, the administration of tetracycline during pregnancy is contraindicated.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Feofol 150 mg+0.5 mg</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdosage with iron salts include epigastric pain, nausea and vomiting, haematemesis, and circulatory collapse. In severe cases, encephalopathy, acute hepatic necrosis, and acute renal failure may develop after a latent period. The timed release capsule presentation of ferrous sulfate may delay excessive absorption of iron and allow more time for the initiation of appropriate countermeasures. Treatment consists of gastric lavage followed by the introduction of 5 gm desferrioxamine into the stomach. Serum iron levels should be monitored, in severe cases intravenous desferrioxamine should be given together with supportive and symptomatic measures as required.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Do not store above 25°C temperature, keep away from light and wet places. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Iron &amp; Vitamin Combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Iron is an essential constituent of the body, necessary for haemoglobin formation and the oxidative processes of living tissues. Iron is primarily absorbed from the duodenum and upper jejunum. The ferrous salt form is absorbed three times more readily than the ferric salt form. The adult male has a requirement of only 13 µg/kg per day (about 1 mg), whereas the menstruating female requires about 21 µg/kg per day (about 1.4 mg). In the last two trimesters of pregnancy, requirements increase to about 80 µg/kg per day (5 to 6 mg).</p><p>Folic acid is an important factor in cell division and without it, division stops. Adequate folic acid is required for normal erythropoiesis. Deficiency of folic acid causes megaloblastic anaemia.</p>', 'active', '897_1742671996.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:33:16', '2025-05-03 10:34:23'),
(4173, 'Brodil Levo', 'Levosalbutamol', 50.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '1 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is your text with the headings in bold as requested:</p><p><strong>Indications of Brodil Levo 100 ml</strong></p><p>Brodil Levo 100 ml is indicated for the treatment and prevention of bronchospasm in adults, adolescents, and children of 6 years of age and older with reversible obstructive airway diseases.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Brodil Levo 100 ml is a single isomer beta-2 agonist that differs from racemic salbutamol by elimination of (S)-salbutamol. Brodil Levo 100 ml is an effective bronchodilator whose primary mechanism of action is not inhibited by (S)-salbutamol. Thus, when compared with racemic salbutamol, clinically comparable bronchodilation can be achieved. Brodil Levo 100 ml produces bronchodilatation through stimulation of beta-2-adrenergic receptors in bronchial smooth muscles, thereby causing relaxation of bronchial muscle fibers.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Brodil Levo 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Children (6 -11 years): 5 ml (1 mg), three times daily.</li><li>Adults and adolescents above 12 years: 5-10 ml (1-2 mg), three times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Brodil Levo 100 ml</strong></p><p>Other short-acting sympathomimetic bronchodilators or epinephrine should be used with caution with Brodil Levo 100 ml. If additional adrenergic drugs are to be administered by any route, they should be used with caution to avoid deleterious cardiovascular effects.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Brodil Levo 100 ml is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Brodil Levo 100 ml or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Brodil Levo 100 ml</strong></p><p>Hypocalcemia, palpitation, fine tremors of the skeletal muscle, and muscle cramps may occur. The other likely side effects are nausea, vomiting, epigastric pain, and diarrhea.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The drug should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether Brodil Levo 100 ml is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when oral Brodil Levo 100 ml is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Particular caution is advised in acute severe asthma as this effect may be potentiated by hypoxia and by concomitant treatment with xanthine derivatives, steroids, and diuretics. Serum potassium levels should be monitored in such situations.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place and away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1947_1742672177.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:36:17', '2025-05-03 01:03:21'),
(4174, 'Folneed', 'Folic Acid + Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate', 2.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here\'s the product gallery and information for <em>Folneed 5 mg + 20 mg</em>:</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Product Gallery</strong></h3><p><strong>Folneed 5 mg + 20 mg Tablet</strong></p><p><em>Folic Acid + Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate</em></p><p>Manufactured by <strong>Incepta Pharmaceuticals Ltd.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>MRP:</strong> ৳ 2.00</li><li><strong>Discount:</strong> 10% Off</li><li><strong>Best Price:</strong> ৳ 1.80 per piece</li></ul><p><strong>Add to Cart Options:</strong></p><ul><li>[Button: -] Quantity: 1 [Button: +]</li><li><strong>Add to Cart</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Alternatives:</strong></h3><ol><li><strong>Folic-Z 5 mg + 20 mg</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Opsonin Pharma Ltd.</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Price:</strong> ৳ 22.50 (MRP: ৳ 25.00)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Add to Cart</strong></li></ul><ol><li><strong>ZnF 5 mg + 20 mg</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Aristopharma Ltd.</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Price:</strong> ৳ 18.20 (MRP: ৳ 20.00)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Add to Cart</strong></li></ul><ol><li><strong>Zifolet 5 mg + 20 mg</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Square Pharmaceuticals PLC.</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Price:</strong> ৳ 18.00 (MRP: ৳ 20.00)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Add to Cart</strong></li></ul><ol><li><strong>Folzin 5 mg + 20 mg</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Pacific Pharmaceuticals Ltd.</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Price:</strong> ৳ 1.75 (MRP: ৳ 1.90)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Add to Cart</strong></li></ul><ol><li><strong>PreMum 5 mg + 20 mg</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Radiant Pharmaceuticals Ltd.</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Price:</strong> ৳ 3.68 (MRP: ৳ 4.00)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Add to Cart</strong></li></ul><ol><li><strong>Feozin 5 mg + 20 mg</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Eskayef Bangladesh Ltd.</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Price:</strong> ৳ 1.82 (MRP: ৳ 2.00)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Add to Cart</strong></li></ul><ol><li><strong>Defaz 5 mg + 20 mg</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>ACME Laboratories Ltd.</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Price:</strong> ৳ 13.59 (MRP: ৳ 15.10)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Add to Cart</strong></li></ul><ol><li><strong>Lifozin 5 mg + 20 mg</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Ibn-Sina Pharmaceuticals Ltd.</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Price:</strong> ৳ 1.80 (MRP: ৳ 2.00)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Add to Cart</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Medicine Overview:</strong></h3><p><strong>Indications of Folneed 5 mg + 20 mg</strong></p><p>This tablet is indicated for the treatment and prophylaxis of folic acid and zinc deficiencies.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Zinc</strong> plays a vital role in enzyme systems, growth, and development. Deficiency may lead to growth retardation, immune dysfunction, anorexia, weight loss, neural tube defects, and mental lethargy.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong> is involved in DNA, RNA, and protein synthesis. It is essential for DNA replication and gene expression regulation. Deficiency, particularly during pregnancy, can cause neural tube defects in infants and megaloblastic anemia.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></h3><ul><li>Take <strong>1 tablet daily</strong>, or as directed by your physician.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Zinc</strong>: Large amounts of calcium can reduce zinc absorption.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong>: No known drug interactions.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Zinc</strong>: Contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to zinc.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong>: Contraindicated in untreated cobalamin deficiency.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects:</strong></h3><ul><li>Folic Acid &amp; Zinc are generally well tolerated. However, some may experience mild gastrointestinal disturbances such as:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Abdominal pain</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dyspepsia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vomiting</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fever</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Respiratory distress</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></h3><ul><li>Safe and recommended for use during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Zinc Sulfate</strong>: In overdose, zinc is corrosive, leading to inflammation of the mucous membranes in the mouth and stomach.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, protect from light &amp; moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></h3><ul><li>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></h3><p>This tablet combines <strong>Folic Acid</strong> and <strong>Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate</strong>, essential for growth, immune function, and DNA synthesis.</p><ul><li><strong>Zinc</strong> is crucial for enzyme functions and immune system support.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong> aids in the synthesis of DNA and is vital during pregnancy to prevent neural tube defects and anemia.</li></ul><p>This provides a comprehensive overview of the product, its alternatives, and essential details for consumers!</p>', 'active', '1400_1742672280.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:38:00', '2025-05-03 10:51:27'),
(4175, 'Savlon', 'Chlorhexidine Hydrochloride + Cetrimide', 30.00, 1, 'x 60 gm tube', '60 gm tube', 'Cream', '0.1%+0.5%', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Savlon Cream 60gm</h3><p>Cetrimide &amp; Chlorhexidine Hydrochloride is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Insect bites</li><li>Sunburn</li><li>Disinfection</li><li>Cuts</li><li>Scratches</li><li>Blisters</li><li>Grazes</li><li>Nappy rash</li><li>Cracked and itchy skin</li><li>Cleansing and irrigating the skin or dirty wounds</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Chlorhexidine &amp; Chloroxylenol preparations</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li><strong>Cetrimide</strong> is a quaternary ammonium antiseptic with bactericidal activity against both gram-positive and gram-negative organisms.</li><li><strong>Chlorhexidine</strong> is an antimicrobial agent active against a wide range of gram-negative and gram-positive vegetative bacteria, yeasts, dermatophyte fungi, and lipophilic viruses.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Savlon Cream 60gm</h3><ul><li><strong>Adult and children</strong>: Apply 2-3 times daily or as directed.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Savlon Cream 60gm</h3><ul><li>There are no known drug interactions, and none are well documented.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Not to be used in the eye, ear, mucous membranes, or body cavities.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Savlon Cream 60gm</h3><ul><li>Repeated application may lead to skin irritation.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: Animal reproduction studies have shown an adverse effect on the fetus, and there are no adequate and well-controlled studies in humans. However, potential benefits may warrant the use of the drug in pregnant women despite potential risks.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Prolonged and repeated use may increase the risk of hypersensitivity reactions.</li><li>Blood, soap, cotton, or other organic matter may decrease antibacterial efficacy.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li>Chlorhexidine &amp; Chloroxylenol preparations</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Cetrimide</strong>: A quaternary ammonium antiseptic with bactericidal activity against gram-positive and gram-negative organisms.</li><li><strong>Chlorhexidine</strong>: An antimicrobial agent effective against a broad range of bacteria, yeasts, dermatophyte fungi, and lipophilic viruses.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: Animal studies have shown an adverse effect on the fetus. There are no adequate studies in humans, but the potential benefits may justify its use in pregnant women despite the risks.</li></ul>', 'active', '1873_1742672388.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:39:48', '2025-05-04 20:50:52'),
(4176, 'Aeron FT', 'Montelukast Sodium', 9.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Aeron FT 5 mg - Indications, Dosage, Side Effects, and Other Information</h3><p><strong>Indications:</strong> Aeron FT 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li>Aeron FT 5 mg works by inhibiting cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, LTE4), which are produced during arachidonic acid metabolism. These leukotrienes bind to receptors (CysLT1) located in the human airway. Aeron FT 5 mg has a strong affinity for CysLT1 receptors and inhibits the physiological effects of LTD4 at these receptors.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></h3><p><strong>For Asthma or Allergic Rhinitis:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (15 years and older):</strong></li><li>10 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>6-14 years:</strong> 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>2-5 years:</strong> 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>6 months - 5 years:</strong> 4 mg oral granules once daily (can be mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature).</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency:</strong> No dosage adjustment required for mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong> No dosage adjustment recommended.</p><p><strong>Elderly Use:</strong> No dosage adjustment required. The pharmacokinetic profile is similar in elderly and younger adults, though the plasma half-life may be slightly longer in the elderly.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Interactions:</strong></h3><p>Aeron FT 5 mg has not shown any clinically significant drug interactions with:</p><ul><li>Theophylline</li><li>Prednisone, Prednisolone</li><li>Oral contraceptives</li><li>Terfenadine</li><li>Digoxin</li><li>Warfarin</li><li>Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p>However, drugs like phenobarbital and rifampin (cytochrome P450 enzyme inducers) may reduce the drug’s AUC by approximately 40%. Clinical monitoring is recommended when co-administering such drugs.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Contraindications:</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the medication.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects:</strong></h3><p><strong>Common:</strong></p><ul><li>Upper respiratory tract infection</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Fever</li><li>Gastrointestinal pain</li><li>Headache</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li></ul><p><strong>Uncommon:</strong></p><ul><li>Anxiety, depression, irritability, sleep disturbances</li><li>Muscle complaints, oedema</li><li>Seizure, dry mouth, dizziness</li><li>Palpitations</li></ul><p><strong>Rare:</strong></p><ul><li>Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema</li><li>Hallucinations, memory loss, hepatic problems</li><li>Suicidal thoughts, tremor</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: Though studies in pregnant rats and rabbits show the medication crosses the placenta, there is limited data for human pregnancy. It should only be used if absolutely necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: The drug is secreted in breast milk, so caution should be exercised when prescribing to nursing mothers.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Acute Asthma Attacks</strong>: Aeron FT 5 mg is not for the reversal of acute bronchospasm in asthma attacks (e.g., status asthmaticus). It is not a substitute for rescue medication.</li><li><strong>Exercise-Induced Bronchospasm</strong>: It is not recommended as the sole treatment for exercise-induced bronchospasm.</li><li><strong>Aspirin Sensitivity</strong>: Patients with documented aspirin sensitivity should avoid aspirin or NSAIDs while taking this medication.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></h3><p>Overdose is generally not associated with serious adverse effects. Common symptoms of overdose include:</p><ul><li>Nausea, vomiting</li><li>Psychomotor hyperactivity</li><li>Thirst, headache, stomach pain</li></ul><p>Supportive measures, including removing unabsorbed material from the digestive tract and clinical monitoring, should be considered.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry place, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Class:</strong></h3><ul><li>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></h3><p>Aeron FT 5 mg works as a selective leukotriene receptor antagonist by inhibiting the CysLT1 receptor. It prevents the physiological effects of cysteinyl leukotrienes, which are involved in the inflammatory processes of asthma and allergic rhinitis, helping to reduce airway edema, smooth muscle contraction, and altered cellular activity.</p>', 'active', '1951_1742672494.jpg', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:41:34', '2025-05-02 22:51:19'),
(4177, 'Longpara', 'Paracetamol', 2.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '665 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3><strong>Longpara 665 mg</strong></h3><h4><strong>Indications</strong></h4><p>Longpara 665 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Fever</li><li>Common cold and influenza</li><li>Headache</li><li>Toothache</li><li>Earache</li><li>Bodyache</li><li>Myalgia (muscle pain)</li><li>Neuralgia (nerve pain)</li><li>Dysmenorrhoea (painful menstruation)</li><li>Sprains</li><li>Colic pain</li><li>Back pain</li><li>Post-operative pain</li><li>Postpartum pain</li><li>Inflammatory pain</li><li>Post-vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic and osteoarthritic pain</li><li>Joint stiffness</li></ul><h4><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Non-opioid analgesics</strong></li></ul><h4><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Mechanism of Action</strong>: Longpara 665 mg (Acetaminophen) has analgesic (pain-relieving) and antipyretic (fever-reducing) properties. It acts primarily in the central nervous system (CNS), increasing the pain threshold by inhibiting cyclooxygenase enzymes (COX-1, COX-2, COX-3) involved in prostaglandin synthesis.</li><li><strong>Properties</strong>: It is a para-aminophenol derivative with weak anti-inflammatory activity, known for being one of the safest and most widely used analgesics.</li></ul><h4><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 1-2 tablets every 4-6 hours (max 4 gm/day).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6-12 years)</strong>: ½ to 1 tablet 3-4 times daily (max 2.6 gm/day for long-term use).</li><li><strong>Extended Release Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; children (12 years and over)</strong>: 2 tablets every 6-8 hours (max 6 tablets/day). Do not crush the tablet.</li><li><strong>Syrup/Suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children under 3 months</strong>: 10 mg/kg body weight (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced), 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 3 months to below 1 year</strong>: ½ to 1 teaspoonful, 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 1-5 years</strong>: 1-2 teaspoonfuls, 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6-12 years</strong>: 2-4 teaspoonfuls, 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 4-8 teaspoonfuls, 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Suppository</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 3-12 months</strong>: 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 1-5 years</strong>: 125-250 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6-12 years</strong>: 250-500 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; children over 12 years</strong>: 0.5-1 gm, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Paediatric Drops</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children up to 3 months</strong>: 0.5 ml (40 mg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 4-11 months</strong>: 1.0 ml (80 mg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 1-2 years</strong>: 1.5 ml (120 mg). Do not exceed 5 doses/day for a maximum of 5 days.</li><li><strong>Actizorb Technology Tablets</strong>: Faster dissolving than standard tablets.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; children (12 years and over)</strong>: 1-2 tablets every 4-6 hours (max 8 tablets/day).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (7-11 years)</strong>: ½-1 tablet every 4-6 hours (max 4 tablets/day). Not recommended for children under 7 years.</li></ul><h4><strong>Interactions</strong></h4><ul><li>Patients taking <strong>barbiturates, tricyclic antidepressants, and alcohol</strong> may experience reduced ability to metabolize high doses of Longpara 665 mg.</li><li><strong>Alcohol</strong> can increase the risk of liver damage (hepatotoxicity) in case of overdose.</li><li><strong>Anticonvulsants and oral contraceptives</strong> may reduce the effectiveness of Longpara 665 mg by increasing its metabolism.</li></ul><h4><strong>Contraindications</strong></h4><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity (allergy) to Longpara 665 mg.</li></ul><h4><strong>Side Effects</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Mild side effects</strong>: Common side effects are rare but may include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Haematological reactions (e.g., thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, pancytopenia, neutropenia, agranulocytosis)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pancreatitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin rashes</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Allergic reactions (e.g., rash, itching)</li></ul><h4><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B according to the USFDA. Use during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Longpara 665 mg is excreted in breast milk, but maternal use in normal therapeutic doses does not appear to pose a risk to the infant.</li></ul><h4><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h4><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired <strong>kidney</strong> or <strong>liver</strong> function.</li><li>Should be used with care in individuals taking other liver-affecting drugs.</li></ul><h4><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Initial symptoms</strong> (first 24 hours): Pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and abdominal pain.</li><li><strong>Later symptoms (12-48 hours post-ingestion)</strong>: Liver damage, abnormalities in glucose metabolism, and metabolic acidosis.</li></ul><h4><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h4><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h4><strong>Drug Class</strong></h4><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics</li></ul><h4><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h4><ul><li>Increases pain threshold by inhibiting COX enzymes involved in prostaglandin synthesis, resulting in analgesic and antipyretic effects with weak anti-inflammatory activity.</li></ul>', 'active', '1102_1742672601.jpg', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:43:21', '2025-05-03 23:31:14'),
(4178, 'Preneed-CI', 'Carbonyl Iron + Folic Acid + Zinc Sulfate', 4.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg+0.50 mg+61.80 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is the text with the heading in bold, as requested:</p><p><strong>Indications of Preneed-CI</strong></p><p>It is indicated for the treatment and prophylaxis of Iron, Folic Acid, and Zinc deficiency, especially during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each capsule contains:</p><ul><li>Elemental iron 50 mg (As carbonyl iron INN)</li><li>Folic acid BP 500 micrograms</li><li>Zinc sulphate monohydrate USP 61.80 mg (equivalent to 22.50 mg zinc)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined preparation</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Carbonyl iron is a form of the mineral iron. Iron is important for many functions in the body, especially for the transport of oxygen in the blood. Carbonyl iron is used as a dietary supplement, and to prevent and treat iron deficiencies and iron deficiency anemia. Folic acid enhances chemical reactions that contribute to the production of red blood cells, the manufacture of DNA needed for cell replication, and the metabolism of amino acids (compounds necessary for the manufacture of proteins). Zinc is essential to numerous physiological processes, including the function of many enzymes in the body. Deficiency may lead to poor night vision, slow healing of wounds, poor sexual development and function in males, poor appetite (perhaps owing to a decrease in the sense of taste and smell), a reduced ability to ward off infections, diarrhea, dermatitis, and in children, retarded growth.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Preneed-CI</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Elderly:</strong> 1 capsule daily. In more severe cases, 2 capsules daily may be required.</li><li><strong>Children (Aged over 1 year):</strong> 1 capsule daily. The capsule may be opened and the pellets mixed with soft cool food, but they must not be chewed.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Preneed-CI</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult:</strong> One capsule daily before food or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Preneed-CI</strong></p><ul><li>Carbonyl Iron decreases the absorption of tetracycline antibiotics, quinolone antibiotics, levodopa, levothyroxine, methyldopa, and penicillamine.</li><li>Folic acid interacts with antiepileptics, so plasma concentrations of phenobarbital, phenytoin, and primidone are possibly reduced.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of its components or those with iron overload.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Preneed-CI</strong></p><p>Gastrointestinal irritations such as nausea, anorexia, vomiting, discomfort, constipation, and diarrhea may occur. Patients may complain of dark stool. Carbonyl Iron pellets incorporated into the capsules reduce the possibility of gastrointestinal irritations. Rarely, there may be allergic reactions.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Use of any drug during the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided if possible. Thus, administration of iron during the first trimester requires definite evidence of iron deficiency. Prophylaxis of iron deficiency where inadequate diet calls for supplementary zinc and folic acid is justified during the remainder of pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Special care should be taken in patients with iron overload states, such as haemochromatosis, haemolytic anemia, or red blood cell aplasia. Failure to respond to the treatment requires further investigations to exclude other causes of anemia. In patients with renal failure, there may be a risk of zinc accumulation.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Preneed-CI</strong></p><p>Symptoms of carbonyl iron overdose include decreased energy, nausea, abdominal pain, tarry stool, weak, rapid pulse, fever, coma, and seizures.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C and keep away from light and moisture. Keep all medicines out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined preparation</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This timed-release capsule is the combined preparation of Carbonyl Iron, Folic Acid, and Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate. It contains Carbonyl Iron with not less than 98% iron content. Carbonyl Iron, having high bioavailability and low toxicity, is a safer and more effective choice for iron supplementation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Use of any drug during the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided if possible. Thus, administration of iron during the first trimester requires definite evidence of iron deficiency. Prophylaxis of iron deficiency where inadequate diet calls for supplementary zinc and folic acid is justified during the remainder of pregnancy.</p>', 'active', '1785_1742672718.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:45:18', '2025-05-04 10:29:17');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4179, 'Exovate N', 'Clobetasol Propionate + Neomycin Sulphate + Nystatin', 90.00, 1, 'x 25 gm tube', '25 gm tube', 'Cream', '(0.5 mg+5 mg+1 Lac IU)/gm', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Exovate N 25 gm</strong></p><p>This preparation is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Short courses treatment of recalcitrant eczemas.</li><li>Neurodermatoses.</li><li>Psoriasis (excluding widespread plaque psoriasis) where secondary bacterial or fungal infection is present, suspected, or likely to occur.</li><li>Other inflammatory conditions that do not respond satisfactorily to less active steroids.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Clobetasol Propionate is a highly active corticosteroid with topical anti-inflammatory activity. The major effect of Clobetasol Propionate on the skin is a non-specific anti-inflammatory response, partially due to vasoconstriction and decreased collagen synthesis.</p><p>Neomycin Sulphate is a broad-spectrum antibiotic of the aminoglycoside type, used to treat bacterial infections. It binds to the ribosomal 30s and 50s sub-units of susceptible bacteria, inhibiting protein synthesis and causing a misreading of genetic codes on the mRNA template, which results in incorrect amino acids being incorporated into the growing polypeptide chain.</p><p>Nystatin is an antifungal medicine that kills fungi and yeasts by interfering with their cell membranes.</p><p>The combination of Neomycin and Nystatin provides broad-spectrum antibacterial and anti-candidal activity, while Clobetasol Propionate offers anti-inflammatory effects for conditions that may be infected or likely to become infected.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Exovate N 25 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 2 years:</strong> Apply sparingly to the affected area once or twice daily until improvement occurs. Therapy should be discontinued once control is achieved.</li><li><strong>Resistant lesions:</strong> If lesions are resistant, especially in cases of hyperkeratosis, the anti-inflammatory effect can be enhanced by occluding the area with polythene.</li><li><strong>Maximum duration:</strong> Treatment should not exceed 7 days without medical supervision. If longer use is required, review the patient\'s condition after 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Suitable for elderly patients. Caution should be exercised in those with decreased renal function due to potential systemic absorption of Neomycin Sulphate.</li><li><strong>Children under 2 years:</strong> This preparation is not recommended for children under 2 years of age.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Exovate N 25 gm</strong></p><p>Neomycin Sulphate may intensify and prolong the respiratory depressant effects of neuromuscular blocking agents after significant systemic absorption. However, if used as recommended, systemic exposure to Neomycin Sulphate is minimal, and drug interactions are unlikely. No hazardous interactions have been reported with Clobetasol Propionate or Nystatin.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>This medication is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Rosacea</li><li>Acne vulgaris</li><li>Perioral dermatitis</li><li>Primary cutaneous viral infections (e.g., Herpes simplex, chickenpox)</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the preparation</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Exovate N 25 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged use of large amounts or treatment of extensive areas may result in systemic absorption and features of hypercortisolism, especially in infants and children or when occlusive dressings are used.</li><li>Long-term use may cause local atrophic changes in the skin, including thinning, striae, and dilatation of superficial blood vessels, particularly in skin folds or when occlusive dressings are used.</li><li>Possible pigmentation changes and hypertrichosis with topical steroids.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy category C. The safe use of this preparation during pregnancy and lactation has not been established.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Long-term continuous therapy should be avoided, especially in infants and children, as adrenal suppression can occur easily, even without occlusion.</li><li>Care should be taken to avoid the medication entering the eye, as it can result in glaucoma. If contact with the eye occurs, rinse thoroughly with water.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Exovate N 25 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Acute overdose is unlikely.</li><li>In the case of chronic overdosage or misuse, features of hypercortisolism may appear. Topical steroids should be discontinued gradually.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 25°C. Do not freeze. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong>: A potent corticosteroid, it is prescribed to treat severe inflammatory skin disorders such as eczema and psoriasis that have not responded to weaker corticosteroids.</li><li><strong>Neomycin Sulphate</strong>: An antibiotic that inhibits protein synthesis in bacteria by binding to ribosomal sub-units.</li><li><strong>Nystatin</strong>: An antifungal that disrupts the fungal cell membrane, causing leakage of intracellular contents.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>The effects of topically applied Neomycin during pregnancy and lactation are not well established. Neomycin can cross the placenta and may pose a theoretical risk to the fetus. Its use is not recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul>', 'active', '199_1742672808.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:46:48', '2025-05-03 10:21:52'),
(4180, 'Esoprol', 'Esomeprazole', 5.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Esoprol 20 mg</strong></p><p>Esoprol 20 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Alleviating GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn and others.</li><li>Treating erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Maintaining the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Eradicating Helicobacter pylori infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in combination with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Treating Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>Managing duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Esoprol 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. Most patients heal within 4 to 8 weeks. If healing does not occur, an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (Clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms are not resolved, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori Eradication</strong>: Triple therapy with 40 mg Esomeprazole once daily for 10 days, Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, and Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg once daily. Dosage should be adjusted individually, and treatment continued as long as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-Related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks based on response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: 40 mg Esoprol 20 mg once daily by intravenous injection (administered over 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (administered over 10-30 minutes). Esomeprazole IV should not be administered through the same IV site as other medications.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Esoprol 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take one Esoprol 20 mg pill or capsule <strong>one hour before a meal</strong> and swallow it whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>: Mix the entire contents of one packet with 15 ml of water in a small glass. Stir well and wait 2-3 minutes to thicken. Stir again and consume within 30 minutes.</li><li><strong>Nasogastric or Gastric Tube Use</strong>: Mix 15 ml of water with the suspension in a syringe, shake, and wait for 2-3 minutes. Administer into the stomach and stir again if any medicine remains in the syringe.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Administration</strong>: Reconstitute with 5 ml of solvent (WFI), and administer over a minimum of 3 minutes. Use within 12 hours, and do not refrigerate.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Esoprol 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Metabolism</strong>: Esomeprazole is primarily metabolized by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 enzymes in the liver.</li><li><strong>Drugs with Potential Interactions</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Diazepam</strong>: Esomeprazole can reduce diazepam clearance by 45%, increasing plasma levels.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Ketoconazole, Iron salts, and Digoxin</strong>: Esomeprazole can reduce the absorption of drugs significantly influenced by gastric pH.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Esomeprazole does not significantly interact with phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to any of the formulations.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Esoprol 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, and dry mouth.</li><li>No significant difference in side effects between short-term and maintenance therapy (up to 12 months).</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>A positive response to Esoprol 20 mg does not exclude the possibility of stomach cancer.</li><li>It should be taken <strong>one hour before meals</strong>.</li><li>If capsules are hard to swallow, the pellets inside can be mixed with cold applesauce. Do not chew or crush the pellets.</li><li>Antacids may be used concurrently.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Esoprol 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms of Overdose</strong>: Decreased motor activity, changes in breathing, tremors, ataxia, and convulsions (seen in animal studies).</li><li><strong>Management</strong>: Symptomatic and supportive care. Dialysis is unlikely to remove esomeprazole due to its high protein binding.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a <strong>dry place</strong> below 30°C.</li><li>Protect from moisture and light.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole reduces stomach acid output by inhibiting H+/K+-ATPase in gastric parietal cells.</li><li>It is the <strong>S-isomer</strong> of omeprazole, offering superior acid control compared to racemic proton pump inhibitors.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>No sufficient research on use during pregnancy. Animal studies show no teratogenic effects.</li><li>Esomeprazole is excreted in milk, so breastfeeding should be stopped if esomeprazole is deemed necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric and Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li>No overall safety differences have been observed between elderly and younger patients. However, elderly patients may be more sensitive to the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic and Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>For patients with severe hepatic insufficiency, limit dosage to <strong>20 mg once daily</strong>.</li><li>No significant pharmacokinetic differences in renal impairment, as less than 1% of the drug is excreted unchanged in urine.</li></ul>', 'active', '1574_1742672906.jpg', 27, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:48:26', '2025-05-02 12:37:28'),
(4181, 'Acifix', 'Rabeprazole Sodium', 8.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Acifix 20 mg (Rabeprazole)</strong></p><p>Gastro-resistant tablets of rabeprazole are recommended for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Active duodenal ulcer</li><li>Active benign stomach ulcer</li><li>Symptomatic gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) that is erosive or ulcerative</li><li>GERD long-term maintenance</li><li>Moderate to very severe symptomatic GERD</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Helicobacter pylori eradication in patients with peptic ulcer disease in combination with appropriate antibacterial therapy regimens</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rabeprazole inhibits the gastric H+/K+-ATPase at the secretory surface of the stomach parietal cell, thus reducing gastric acid secretion. This enzyme is known as the acid (proton) pump within the parietal cell.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Acifix 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: Rabeprazole 20 mg once daily in the morning for 4 weeks. Most patients heal within 4 weeks; some may require an additional 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Erosive/Ulcerative GERD</strong>: Rabeprazole 20 mg once daily in the morning for 4 to 8 weeks. If not healed after 8 weeks, an additional 8-week course may be considered.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: Rabeprazole 20 mg once daily in the morning for 4 weeks. An additional course may be considered if symptoms do not resolve.</li><li><strong>GERD Long-term Maintenance</strong>: Rabeprazole 20 mg once daily (up to 12 months).</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: Start with 60 mg once daily. Doses may be adjusted based on individual needs. Some may need doses up to 100 mg daily or 60 mg twice daily.</li><li><strong>H. pylori Eradication</strong>: Rabeprazole 20 mg, Amoxicillin 1000 mg, Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 7 days</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Acifix 20 mg (Additional Details)</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Active Duodenal Ulcer &amp; Active Benign Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily in the morning. Healing typically occurs in 4 to 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Erosive/Ulcerative GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Long-term Management of GERD</strong>: Maintenance dose of Acifix 20 mg or 10 mg daily, depending on patient response.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD (moderate to severe)</strong>: 10 mg once daily. If symptoms do not resolve in 4 weeks, further investigation is required. After symptom resolution, the 10 mg regimen can be used as needed.</li><li><strong>Pediatric GERD Treatment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 to 11 years, &lt;15 kg: 5 mg once daily for 12 weeks (may increase to 10 mg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 to 11 years, ≥15 kg: 10 mg once daily for 12 weeks.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: Start with 60 mg daily, increasing as needed. Doses can go up to 120 mg/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Acifix 20 mg</strong></p><p>Acifix 20 mg should be taken in the morning before eating. The tablet should be swallowed whole and not chewed or crushed. Food intake does not significantly affect the medication\'s activity.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Acifix 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Antifungals (Itraconazole, Ketoconazole)</strong>: Co-administration may reduce plasma levels of antifungals due to altered pH. Dosage adjustments may be required.</li><li><strong>Atazanavir</strong>: Should not be taken with Acifix 20 mg due to altered pH-dependent absorption.</li><li><strong>Liquid Antacids</strong>: No significant interaction.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the product</li><li>Not recommended during pregnancy or breastfeeding</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Acifix 20 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects may include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Changes in appetite (increased or decreased)</li><li>Muscle pain</li><li>Sleepiness</li><li>Dizziness</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: FDA category C. No controlled studies in pregnant women. Animal studies show no harm, but human data is insufficient.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: Rabeprazole is likely excreted in human milk, so a decision should be made to either discontinue the drug or stop breastfeeding based on the drug\'s importance to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Rule out malignancy before starting treatment as symptoms may be similar to those of stomach or esophageal cancer.</li><li>Long-term use (especially beyond 1 year) may increase the risk of fractures, particularly in elderly patients or those with other risk factors.</li><li>Patients at risk for osteoporosis should ensure adequate calcium and vitamin D intake.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Acifix 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No specific antidote for overdose. Symptoms are typically mild and reversible. Treatment should be symptomatic and supportive.</li><li>Rabeprazole is highly protein-bound and cannot be dialyzed.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and efficacy are not established.</li><li><strong>Hepatic &amp; Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment required.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Rabeprazole reduces gastric acid secretion by inhibiting the gastric H+/K+-ATPase, the proton pump, within the parietal cells of the stomach.</p>', 'active', '201_1742673039.png', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:50:39', '2025-05-02 22:46:55'),
(4182, 'Antazol', 'Xylometazoline Hydrochloride', 18.00, 1, 'x 15 ml drop', '15 ml drop', 'Nasal Drop', '0.05%', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Antazol 0.05%</strong></p><p>For the symptomatic relief of nasal congestion, perennial allergic rhinitis (including hay fever), and sinusitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Nasal Anti-histamine preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Antazol 0.05% is a sympathomimetic agent with marked alpha-adrenergic activity, intended for use in the nose. It constricts the nasal blood vessels, thereby decongesting the mucosa of the nose and neighboring regions of the pharynx. This enables patients suffering from colds to breathe more easily through the nose. The effect of Antazol 0.05% begins within a few minutes and persists for several hours. Antazol 0.05% is generally well tolerated and does not impair the function of ciliated epithelium. Systemic absorption may occur following nasal application of Antazol 0.05%.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Antazol 0.05%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 2 or 3 drops of Antazol 0.05% Adult formula (0.1%) two to three times daily. This adult formula should not be used for children under the age of 12 years.</li><li><strong>Children under 12 years</strong>: 1 or 2 drops of the Antazol 0.05% children\'s formula (0.05%) in each nostril once or twice daily. Not to be used in infants less than 3 months.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Antazol 0.05%</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Antazol 0.05% nasal drops are contraindicated in patients with trans-sphenoidal hypophysectomy or surgery exposing the dura mater. It is also contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to Antazol 0.05%.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Antazol 0.05%</strong></p><p>The following side effects have occasionally been encountered:</p><ul><li>A burning sensation in the nose and throat</li><li>Local irritation</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Headache</li><li>Dryness of the nasal mucosa</li></ul><p>Systemic cardiovascular effects have occurred, and this should be kept in mind when giving Antazol 0.05% to people with cardiovascular disease.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>No fetal toxicity or fertility studies have been carried out in animals. In view of its potential systemic vasoconstrictor effect, it is advisable to take the precaution of not using Antazol 0.05% during pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Each Antazol 0.05% pack should be used by one person only to prevent any cross-infection. Patients are advised not to take decongestants for more than seven consecutive days.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Protect from heat. For reasons of hygiene, do not use the bottle more than 28 days after opening it.</p>', 'active', '1906_1742673176.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:52:56', '2025-05-02 23:42:36'),
(4183, 'Renocal Plus', 'Calcium acetate + Magnesium carbonate', 12.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '435 mg+ 235 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '573_1742673278.png', 67, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 00:54:38', '2025-05-04 15:09:56'),
(4184, 'Azmasol', 'Salbutamol', 2.50, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '200 mcg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Azmasol Bexicap 200 mcg</strong></p><p>Azmasol Bexicap 200 mcg is indicated as a bronchodilator for use in:</p><ul><li>Asthma</li><li>Chronic Bronchitis</li><li>Emphysema</li><li>Other conditions associated with airways obstruction</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Azmasol Bexicap 200 mcg is a selective beta2-adrenoceptor agonist. At therapeutic doses, it acts on the beta-2 adrenoceptors of bronchial smooth muscle, with little or no action on the β1-adrenoceptors of cardiac muscle. Azmasol Bexicap 200 mcg provides short-acting (4-6 hours) bronchodilatation with a fast onset (within 5 minutes) in reversible airways obstruction. It also has an anti-inflammatory effect on mast cells, causing inhibition of the release of bronchoconstrictor mediators, including histamine, neutrophil chemotactic factor (NCF), and prostaglandin D2.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Azmasol Bexicap 200 mcg</strong></p><p><strong>Oral:</strong></p><ul><li>Children (2-6 years): 2.5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li>Children (6-12 years): 5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li>Children (over 12 years): 5-10 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li>Adults: 2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily (Maximum single dose is 8 mg tablet)</li></ul><p><strong>Injection:</strong></p><p>Azmasol Bexicap 200 mcg Injection may be administered by subcutaneous, intramuscular, or intravenous route under the direction of a physician.</p><ul><li>Adults: Subcutaneous route: 500 mcg (8 mcg/kg) and repeated every 4 hours as required.</li><li>Intramuscular route: 500 mcg (8 mcg/kg) and repeated every 4 hours as required.</li><li>Slow intravenous injection: 250 mcg (4 mcg/kg), injected slowly. If necessary, the dose may be repeated.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Population:</strong></p><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Azmasol Bexicap 200 mcg Injection in children under 12 years have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Azmasol Bexicap 200 mcg Syrup/Tablet:</strong></p><ul><li>Children (2-6 years): 2.5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li>Children (6-12 years): 5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li>Over 12 years: 5-10 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily (2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily)</li><li>Adults: 2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily. Maximum single dose is 8 mg tablet.</li></ul><p><strong>Azmasol Bexicap 200 mcg Respirator Solution:</strong></p><p>To be used with a suitable nebulizer device under the direction of a physician.</p><p><strong>Method 1 (Intermittent Administration):</strong></p><ul><li>Adults: 0.5 ml-1.0 ml Azmasol Bexicap 200 mcg up to four times a day.</li><li>Children (under 12 years): 0.5 ml of the solution diluted to 2.0-4.0 ml with normal saline. Some children may require up to 1.0 ml of the solution.</li></ul><p><strong>Method 2 (Continuous administration):</strong></p><p>1-2 ml solution is diluted to make up to 100 ml with normal saline solution. This is then administered by a suitable nebulizer device.</p><p><strong>Azmasol Bexicap 200 mcg Inhalation Capsule:</strong></p><ul><li>Adults: For relief of bronchospasm and managing intermittent episodes of asthma, inhale one or two inhalation capsules every 4 to 6 hours using a device.</li><li>Children (4 years and older): Inhale one capsule for maintenance or prophylactic therapy 3-4 times a day.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Azmasol Bexicap 200 mcg is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Azmasol Bexicap 200 mcg or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Azmasol Bexicap 200 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>Fine tremor of skeletal muscles (particularly hands), palpitations, muscle cramps, tachycardia, tenseness, headaches, and peripheral vasodilatation may occur after large doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: The drug should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. A decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, considering the importance of the drug to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Azmasol Bexicap 200 mcg should be used with caution in patients with:</p><ul><li>Hyperthyroidism</li><li>Cardiovascular disease</li><li>Occlusive vascular disorders</li><li>Hypertension</li><li>Aneurysms</li></ul><p>High doses may cause hypokalaemia, leading to increased susceptibility to digitalis-induced cardiac arrhythmia. Care is necessary when treating patients with diabetes mellitus or closed-angle glaucoma.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Azmasol Bexicap 200 mcg</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdosage include angina, headache, nausea, vomiting, tremor, etc. The preferred antidote is a cardio-selective beta-blocking agent, but these should be used with caution in patients with a history of bronchospasm.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Azmasol Bexicap 200 mcg is a synthetic sympathomimetic agent with predominant beta-2 adrenergic activity. It produces bronchodilatation through stimulation of beta-2-adrenergic receptors in bronchial smooth muscles, leading to the relaxation of bronchial muscle fibers and improvement in pulmonary function.</p>', 'active', '1003_1742673742.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:02:22', '2025-05-03 00:13:32'),
(4185, 'Metro', 'Metronidazole', 1.70, 7, 'tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '400 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Metro 400 mg</h3><p>Metro 400 mg is indicated in the treatment of the following diseases:</p><ul><li><strong>Prevention of post-operative infections</strong> due to anaerobic bacteria (particularly species of <em>Bacteroides</em> and anaerobic streptococci).</li><li><strong>Treatment of septicaemia, bacteraemia, peritonitis, brain abscess, pelvic abscess, pelvic cellulitis</strong>, and post-operative wound infections caused by anaerobes.</li><li><strong>Urogenital trichomoniasis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Bacterial vaginosis</strong> (non-specific vaginitis).</li><li><strong>All forms of amoebiasis</strong> (intestinal, extra-intestinal disease, and asymptomatic cyst passers).</li><li><strong>Giardiasis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Acute ulcerative gingivitis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Anaerobically infected leg ulcers and pressure sores</strong>.</li><li><strong>Acute dental infections</strong> due to anaerobic organisms.</li><li><strong>Antibiotic-associated pseudomembranous colitis</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Amoebicides</strong>, <strong>Anti-diarrhoeal</strong>, <strong>Antiprotozoal</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Metro 400 mg belongs to the imidazole class of antibacterial drugs and is classified as an antiprotozoal agent. The 5-nitro group of Metro 400 mg is reduced metabolically by anaerobes. The reduced form interacts with DNA, leading to the bactericidal action of the drug.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Metro 400 mg</h3><p><strong>Tablet and Suspension:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Trichomoniasis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong> 200 mg tid or 400 mg bid for 7 days, 800 mg in the morning and 1-2 gm at night for 2 days, or 2 gm as a single dose for 1 day.</li><li><strong>Intestinal amoebiasis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong> 800 mg tid for 5 days.</li><li><strong>Giardiasis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong> 2 gm once daily for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Acute ulcerative gingivitis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong> 200 mg tid for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Acute dental infections (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong> 200 mg tid for 3-7 days.</li><li><strong>Bacterial Vaginosis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong> 400 mg bid for 7 days or 2 gm as a single dose.</li><li><strong>Surgical Prophylaxis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs):</strong> 400 mg tid started 24 hours before surgery for 1 day.</li></ul><p><strong>Vaginal Gel:</strong></p><ul><li>One applicator full of Metro 400 mg gel (approximately 37.5 mg) intravaginally once or twice a day for 5 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Suppository:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Anaerobic Infections (Adults):</strong> 1 g every 8 hours for 3 days, then 1 g every 12 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>IV Infusion:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years:</strong> Infuse 500 mg 8 hourly at a rate of 5 ml/minute, with a maximum of 4 g per 24 hours.</li></ul><h3>Interactions of Metro 400 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Disulfiram:</strong> Psychotic reactions can occur with concurrent use of disulfiram.</li><li><strong>Alcohol:</strong> Alcoholic beverages and alcohol-containing drugs should be avoided during therapy and for at least one day after.</li><li><strong>Oral Anticoagulants (warfarin):</strong> Increased risk of bleeding; prothrombin time should be closely monitored.</li><li><strong>Lithium:</strong> Plasma levels may increase.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin:</strong> Serum levels should be closely monitored.</li><li><strong>Phenytoin/Phenobarbital:</strong> Increased elimination of Metro 400 mg, reducing its efficacy.</li><li><strong>5-Fluorouracil:</strong> Metro 400 mg reduces clearance, increasing 5-fluorouracil toxicity.</li><li><strong>Busulfan:</strong> Increased plasma levels may lead to toxicity.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Metro 400 mg is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Metro 400 mg or other Nitroimidazole derivatives.</p><h3>Side Effects of Metro 400 mg</h3><ul><li>Metallic taste, nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, drowsiness, rashes.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category:</strong> B. Metro 400 mg should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Metro 400 mg is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised during breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Regular hematological tests should be conducted if prolonged treatment is necessary.</li><li>Caution in patients with hepatic encephalopathy.</li><li>Metro 400 mg may darken urine.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li>Overdose may result in symptoms such as vomiting, ataxia, and slight disorientation.</li><li>There is no specific antidote; symptomatic and supportive treatment should be given.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light, and out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>The 5-nitro group of Metro 400 mg is reduced by anaerobes, leading to interaction with DNA and bactericidal effects.</li></ul><h3>Pediatric Use</h3><ul><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Caution is needed in patients with advanced hepatic insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment is necessary for patients with renal failure, though caution is required for those undergoing dialysis.</li></ul><p>This comprehensive overview provides information on the clinical uses, dosing, interactions, side effects, and storage guidelines for Metro 400 mg.</p>', 'active', '1101_1742673868.jpg', 50, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:04:28', '2025-05-04 00:02:32'),
(4186, 'B-9', 'Folic Acid', 50.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '2.5 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of B-9 100 ml</strong></p><p>B-9 100 ml is indicated for the treatment of Folic-acid deficiency, Megaloblastic anemia, and Anemias of nutritional origins, pregnancy, infancy, or childhood.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for Megaloblastic Anemia, Vitamin-B preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>B-9 100 ml is essential for the production of certain coenzymes in many metabolic systems such as purine and pyrimidine synthesis. It is also essential in the synthesis and maintenance of nucleoprotein in erythropoiesis. It also promotes WBC and platelet production in folate-deficiency anemia.</p><p><strong>Dosage of B-9 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initially: 5 mg daily for 4 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance: 5 mg every 1-7 days, depending on the underlying disease.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Up to 1 year: 500 mcg/kg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Over 1 year: Same as the adult dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of B-9 100 ml</strong></p><p>May be taken with or without food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of B-9 100 ml</strong></p><p>None has been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>B-9 100 ml is contraindicated in patients who have shown previous intolerance to the drug.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of B-9 100 ml</strong></p><p>B-9 100 ml is generally well tolerated. Gastrointestinal disturbances may occur. Hypersensitivity reactions have been reported rarely.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Category A. Adequate and well-controlled human studies have not shown any risk to the fetus in the first trimester of pregnancy (and there is no evidence of risk in later trimesters).</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>It should be used with caution in patients who may have folate-dependent tumors.</li><li>It should never be given alone or in conjunction with inadequate amounts of vitamin B12 for the treatment of undiagnosed megaloblastic anemia.</li><li>Although B-9 100 ml may produce a hematopoietic response in patients with megaloblastic anemia due to vitamin B12 deficiency, it should not be administered alone in vitamin B12 deficiency states, as it may precipitate the onset of subacute combined degeneration of the cord.</li><li>In elderly individuals, a cobalamin absorption test should be conducted before long-term folate use. No harmful results have been observed from short courses of folate.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a dry place, away from light. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Drugs for Megaloblastic Anemia, Vitamin-B preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>B-9 100 ml, although biochemically inactive, undergoes conversion to tetrahydrofolic-acid and methyltetrahydrofolate through the action of dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR). These folic-acid derivatives are transported into cells via receptor-mediated endocytosis, where they play a crucial role in maintaining normal erythropoiesis, synthesizing purine and thymidylate nucleic acids, facilitating amino acid interconversion, methylating tRNA, and producing and utilizing formate. In the presence of vitamin B12 as a cofactor, B-9 100 ml can effectively reduce elevated homocysteine levels by remethylating homocysteine into methionine through methionine synthetase.</p>', 'active', '932_1742674051.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:07:31', '2025-05-03 00:26:47'),
(4187, 'Magfin', 'Magnesium Hydroxide + Liquid Paraffin', 95.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Emulsion', '(300 mg+1.25 ml)/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Magfin 100 ml</strong></p><p>This preparation is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Constipation</li><li>Hyperacidity with constipation</li><li>Anorectal disorder</li><li>Post-operative constipation</li><li>Constipation associated with chronic cholecystitis</li><li>Hernia</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Antacid with laxative action</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Magfin 100 ml is a dual-relief formulation containing Magnesium Hydroxide as an osmotic laxative and Liquid Paraffin as a lubricant. The osmotic laxative works by drawing water into the bowel, which softens the stools, and the lubricant makes them easier to pass out of the body. It is recommended for the temporary relief of constipation.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Magfin 100 ml</strong></p><p>The recommended oral doses are as follows:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years:</strong> 15-30 ml before breakfast or at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Children over 7 years:</strong> 7.5 ml-15 ml at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Children over 3-7 years:</strong> 5-10 ml at bedtime.</li></ul><p>The dose may be mixed with milk or half a glass of water if desired. Do not take for more than 1 week unless under a doctor\'s supervision.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Magfin 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cimetidine</strong>, <strong>Diuretics</strong>, <strong>Famotidine</strong>, and <strong>Ranitidine</strong> may cause irritation of the stomach or bowel.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Acute gastrointestinal conditions like abdominal pain.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Magfin 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Rectal irritation</li><li>Potassium loss (thirst, weakness, nausea, and diarrhea)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Magfin 100 ml can be used in pregnant women and lactating mothers only upon physician’s recommendation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Renal and hepatic impairment</li><li>Maintain adequate fluid intake</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Magfin 100 ml</strong></p><p>There are no reported cases of overdosage.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light &amp; moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Antacid with laxative action</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This preparation has a dual-relief formulation containing a gentle laxative ingredient and a lubricant that eases the discomfort associated with straining and bowel movements. It is recommended for the temporary relief of constipation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers under the supervision of a physician.</p>', 'active', '1392_1742674175.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:09:35', '2025-04-22 18:03:44'),
(4188, 'Vaxtin', 'Aceclofenac', 6.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vaxtin 100 mg</strong></p><p>Vaxtin 100 mg is indicated for the relief of pain and inflammation in osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, toothache, trauma, and lumbago.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Vaxtin 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Vaxtin 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Extended release tablet:</strong> The recommended dose in adults is one 200 mg Vaxtin 100 mg tablet daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Film-coated tablet:</strong> The recommended dose in adults is 100 mg, twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Vaxtin 100 mg</strong></p><p>No significant drug interactions have been observed, but close monitoring of patients is required when it is used with:</p><ul><li><strong>Lithium and Digoxin:</strong> May increase plasma concentration of lithium and digoxin.</li><li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> May interact with the activity of diuretics.</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants:</strong> May enhance the activity of anticoagulants.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate:</strong> May increase the plasma level of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vaxtin 100 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to it or in whom aspirin or NSAIDs precipitate attacks of asthma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Vaxtin 100 mg</strong></p><p>Vaxtin 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of Vaxtin 100 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in patients with active or suspected peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding, moderate to severe hepatic impairment, and cardiac or renal impairment. Caution should also be exercised in patients suffering from dizziness or urticaria.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Vaxtin 100 mg in children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Vaxtin 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The use of Vaxtin 100 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Vaxtin 100 mg in children.</p>', 'active', '40_1742674288.jpg', 57, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:11:28', '2025-05-04 22:48:45'),
(4189, 'Fast', 'Paracetamol', 35.00, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '120 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Fast 60 ml</strong></p><p>Fast 60 ml is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Fever</li><li>Common cold and influenza</li><li>Headache</li><li>Toothache</li><li>Earache</li><li>Body ache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Neuralgia</li><li>Dysmenorrhoea</li><li>Sprains</li><li>Colic pain</li><li>Back pain</li><li>Post-operative pain</li><li>Postpartum pain</li><li>Inflammatory pain</li><li>Post-vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic &amp; osteoarthritic pain and stiffness of joints</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Fast 60 ml has analgesic and antipyretic properties, with weak anti-inflammatory activity. Fast 60 ml (Acetaminophen) is thought to primarily act in the CNS by increasing the pain threshold through the inhibition of both COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3 enzymes, which are involved in prostaglandin (PG) synthesis. It is a para-aminophenol derivative that is widely recognized as one of the safest, fastest-acting analgesics, with minimal side effects compared to aspirin.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 1-2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours, up to a maximum of 4 gm (8 tablets) daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (6-12 years): ½ to 1 tablet 3 to 4 times daily (for long-term treatment, not exceeding 2.6 gm/day).</li><li><strong>Extended Release Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults &amp; children over 12 years: Two tablets every 6 to 8 hours (maximum of 6 tablets in 24 hours). Tablets should not be crushed.</li><li><strong>Syrup/Suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children under 3 months: 10 mg/kg body weight (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced) 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">3 months to below 1 year: ½ to 1 teaspoonful 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-5 years: 1-2 teaspoonful 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years: 2-4 teaspoonful 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults: 4-8 teaspoonful 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Suppository</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 3-12 months: 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-5 years: 125-250 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 6-12 years: 250-500 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults &amp; children over 12 years: 0.5-1 gm, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Paediatric Drop</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children up to 3 months: 0.5 ml (40 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">4 to 11 months: 1.0 ml (80 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 to 2 years: 1.5 ml (120 mg). Maximum of 5 doses per day for up to 5 days.</li><li><strong>Fast 60 ml tablet with Actizorb technology</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults and children (12+): 1 to 2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours as needed (maximum 8 caplets in 24 hours).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (7-11 years): ½-1 tablet every 4 to 6 hours as needed (maximum 4 caplets in 24 hours).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Not recommended for children under 7 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Alcohol</strong>: Increases the hepatotoxicity of Fast 60 ml overdosage.</li><li><strong>Barbiturates, tricyclic antidepressants</strong>: Can reduce the ability to metabolize large doses.</li><li><strong>Chronic ingestion of anticonvulsants or oral contraceptives</strong>: May increase metabolism, preventing therapeutic Fast 60 ml levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Fast 60 ml</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Usually mild, but haematological reactions such as thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, pancytopenia, neutropenia, and agranulocytosis have been reported.</li><li>Pancreatitis, skin rashes, and other allergic reactions may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong> (US FDA). Use during pregnancy should only occur if clearly needed.</li><li>Excreted in breast milk. Maternal use at therapeutic doses does not pose a risk to the infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired liver or kidney function.</li><li>Caution should be exercised with patients on medications affecting liver function.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms within 24 hours include pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and abdominal pain.</li><li>Liver damage may occur 12-48 hours after ingestion.</li><li>Abnormal glucose metabolism and metabolic acidosis can occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Fast 60 ml provides analgesic and antipyretic effects by inhibiting COX enzymes involved in prostaglandin synthesis, resulting in pain relief and reduced fever.</li></ul>', 'active', '341_1742674413.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:13:33', '2025-05-03 10:27:56');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4190, 'V-Plex', 'Vitamin B complex', 0.60, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+2 mg+2 mg+20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of V-Plex</strong></p><p>Vitamin B complex is indicated for prophylactic or therapeutic nutritional supplementation in physiologically stressful conditions. These include conditions causing depletion, or reduced absorption or bioavailability of essential B-vitamins, manifested by glossitis, stomatitis, cheilosis, beriberi, etc.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each Tablet/Capsule contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride 5 mg</li><li>Riboflavin 2 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride 2 mg</li><li>Nicotinamide 20 mg</li></ul><p>Each 5 ml Syrup contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride 5 mg</li><li>Riboflavin 2 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride 2 mg</li><li>Nicotinamide 20 mg</li></ul><p>Each 2 ml Injection contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride 50 mg</li><li>Riboflavin 4 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride 10 mg</li><li>Nicotinamide 100 mg</li><li>D-Panthenol 5 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Vitamin B complex contains the most important members of the vitamin B group in pure form and in therapeutically balanced proportions. The members of the vitamin B group contained in Vitamin B complex are components of enzyme systems that regulate various stages of carbohydrate, fat, and protein metabolism. Each component plays a specific biological role. Deficiency of B vitamins causes glossitis, stomatitis, cheilosis, polyneuritis, beriberi, pellagra, and vascularization of the cornea.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of V-Plex</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet/Capsule</strong>: Usual recommended dose is 1-2 tablets/capsules 3 times daily or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Syrup</strong>: 2-3 teaspoonfuls daily or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: It is for intramuscular and intravenous administration. Usual recommended dose is 2 ml daily or as directed by the physician. The injectable dosage form also contains D-Panthenol (5 mg).</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of V-Plex</strong></p><p>As little as 5 mg of pyridoxine daily can decrease the efficacy of levodopa in the treatment of parkinsonism. Therefore, Vitamin B complex is not recommended for patients undergoing such therapy.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vitamin B complex is contraindicated in patients hypersensitive to any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of V-Plex</strong></p><p>Adverse reactions have been reported with specific vitamins and minerals, generally at levels substantially higher than those in Vitamin B complex. However, allergic and idiosyncratic reactions are possible at lower levels. Iron, even at the usual recommended level, has been associated with gastrointestinal intolerance in some patients.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It is safe to use Vitamin B complex during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Vitamin B complex contains the most important members of the vitamin B group in pure form and in therapeutically balanced proportions. These vitamins are components of enzyme systems that regulate various stages of carbohydrate, fat, and protein metabolism. Each component plays a specific biological role. Deficiency of B vitamins causes glossitis, stomatitis, cheilosis, polyneuritis, beriberi, pellagra, and vascularization of the cornea.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>It is safe to use Vitamin B complex during pregnancy and lactation.</p>', 'active', '568_1742674530.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:15:30', '2025-05-04 22:46:25'),
(4191, 'Algicid', 'Sodium Alginate + Potassium Bicarbonate', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Chewable Tablet', '500 mg+100 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Algicid 500 mg + 100 mg</strong></p><p>This preparation is indicated for the treatment of symptoms resulting from the reflux of acid, bile, and pepsin into the esophagus, such as acid regurgitation, heartburn, and indigestion. It is useful in conditions such as after gastric surgery, as a result of hiatus hernia, during pregnancy, and accompanying reflux esophagitis. It also addresses symptoms of laryngopharyngeal reflux, such as hoarseness, voice disorders, sore throats, and cough. Additionally, it is beneficial in treating gastroesophageal reflux during concomitant treatment or following the withdrawal of acid-suppressing therapy.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Sodium Alginate is a naturally occurring substance that reacts with stomach acid to form a gel. Potassium Bicarbonate reacts with stomach acid to release carbon dioxide bubbles, which are trapped by the gel, creating a raft that floats on top of the stomach contents. This raft prevents acid reflux into the esophagus, thus relieving symptoms such as heartburn. This preparation works physically, without relying on systemic absorption. The raft can last up to 4 hours before being broken down and excreted.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Algicid 500 mg + 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet:</strong>Adults and children over 12 years: 1-2 tablets 3-4 times daily, after meals and at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 6-12 years: ½-1 tablet 3-4 times daily, after meals and at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suspension:</strong>Adults and children 12 years and above: 5-10 ml (1-2 teaspoonfuls) 3-4 times daily, after meals and at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 2-12 years: 2.5-5 ml (½-1 teaspoonful) 3-4 times daily, after meals and at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Not recommended for children under 2 years of age.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><p>A 2-hour interval should be observed between this medication and other drugs, especially tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, iron salts, thyroid hormones, chloroquine, bisphosphonates, and estramustine.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Sodium Alginate or Potassium Bicarbonate.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p>Possible side effects include nausea, constipation, diarrhea, and headache. In cases of overdose, a sensation of swelling may occur. If any side effects are observed, consult a physician.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Safe to use during pregnancy if clinically needed.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong> No known effect on breastfed infants. Safe to use during breastfeeding.</li><li><strong>Fertility:</strong> No known effect on human fertility.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><p>Use with caution in patients with renal impairment and congestive cardiac failure. Care is needed for patients with hypercalcemia, nephrocalcinosis, or recurrent calcium-containing renal calculi. Reduced efficacy may occur in patients with very low levels of gastric acid. If symptoms do not improve after seven days, the clinical situation should be reassessed.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><p>Overdose is rare. In case of overdose, consult a physician.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> No dose modifications are necessary.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No dose modifications necessary.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong> Caution if a highly restricted salt diet is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>This preparation combines Sodium Alginate and Potassium Bicarbonate. Sodium Alginate reacts with stomach acid to form a gel, while Potassium Bicarbonate releases carbon dioxide bubbles, which are trapped by the gel, creating a raft that prevents acid reflux. This provides relief from reflux symptoms like heartburn, and the effect is physical, not reliant on systemic absorption. The raft lasts up to 4 hours before being broken down and excreted in the feces.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><p>This combination can be used during pregnancy if clinically necessary, with no known effect on breastfeeding infants. It is also safe during breastfeeding.</p>', 'active', '1051_1742674652.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:17:32', '2025-05-02 22:55:37'),
(4192, 'Clofenac', 'Diclofenac Sodium', 40.00, 1, 'x 20 gm tube', '20 gm tube', 'Gel', '1% w/w', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Clofenac 20 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Rheumatology</strong>: Inflammatory and degenerative forms of rheumatism, chronic involutive polyarthritis, ankylosing spondylarthritis, osteoarthritis, spondylarthroses, acute gout, peri-articular rheumatic disorders.</li><li><strong>Surgery and Traumatology</strong>: Sprains, bruises, dislocations, fractures, soft tissue injuries, surgical interventions.</li><li><strong>Obstetrics and Gynecology</strong>: Primary dysmenorrhoea, episiotomy, adnexitis, endometritis, parametritis, salpingitis, and mastitis.</li><li><strong>Otorhinolaryngology</strong>: As pre-operative medication for the prevention of pain, inflammation, and swelling.</li><li><strong>Dentistry</strong>: Post-operative and post-traumatic pain, inflammation, and swelling.</li><li><strong>Other indications</strong>: Prevention of pain and treatment of inflammation and swelling in patients who have undergone surgery in the urogenital tract, renal, and biliary colic.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Clofenac 20 gm is a potent non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) with significant anti-rheumatic, anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic properties. It also has some uricosuric effects. Diclofenac works by inhibiting prostaglandin biosynthesis, which plays a key role in causing inflammation, pain, and fever. It is rapidly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract when taken with or after meals. Peak plasma concentrations are reached within 2 hours after ingestion. Diclofenac is highly protein-bound in plasma (99.7%) and is metabolized in the liver with first-pass metabolism.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet/Suppository/Gel</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Enteric-coated tablet</strong>: 75-150 mg daily in two or three divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Sustained-release tablets</strong>: One tablet daily, taken whole with liquid, preferably during a meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Suppositories</strong>: 75-150 mg daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gel</strong>: Depending on the painful site, apply 2-4 g of gel 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Enteric-coated tablet</strong>: 1-3 mg/kg per day in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Sustained-release tablets</strong>: Not recommended.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppositories</strong>: 1-3 mg/kg body weight in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 1 ampoule once (or in severe cases, twice) daily intramuscularly.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal colic</strong>: 1 ampoule intramuscularly, a second dose may be administered after 30 minutes if needed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly</strong>: Use the lowest effective dosage based on age and physical status.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Clofenac 20 gm</strong></p><ul><li>NSAIDs may inhibit the activity of diuretics.</li><li>Concurrent use with potassium-sparing diuretics may raise serum potassium levels.</li><li>Monitor for potential toxicities if used with digoxin, methotrexate, cyclosporine, or lithium.</li><li>Concomitant use of other NSAIDs or glucocorticoids may increase the risk of side effects such as gastrointestinal bleeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient.</li><li>Peptic ulcer.</li><li>Contraindicated in asthmatic patients who experience asthma attacks, urticaria, or acute rhinitis due to acetylsalicylic acid or other NSAIDs.</li><li><strong>Gel</strong>: Should not be used under occlusive airtight dressings.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Generally well-tolerated with mild, rare, and transient side effects.</li><li>Potential side effects include epigastric pain, nausea, diarrhea, dizziness, headache, peripheral edema, skin rash, and eczema.</li><li><strong>Gel</strong>: May cause local irritation, skin redness, and rash.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Should be used only if absolutely necessary and the lowest effective dose should be applied. NSAIDs are not recommended during the first trimester.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Very small quantities may be present in breast milk, but no adverse effects on the infant are expected.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in elderly or debilitated patients.</li><li>Monitor for gastrointestinal bleeding or peptic ulceration.</li><li>Avoid contact of <strong>Clofenac 20 gm Gel</strong> with eyes or mucous membranes. Wash hands thoroughly after applying.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, away from light.</li><li>Keep below 30°C and out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Osteoarthritis treatments, Rheumatoid Arthritis treatments, NSAIDs.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Diclofenac sodium works by inhibiting prostaglandin biosynthesis, effectively reducing inflammation, pain, and fever.</li></ul>', 'active', '1582_1742674768.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:19:28', '2025-05-03 01:22:55'),
(4193, 'Nebanol Plus', 'Neomycin Sulfate + Bacitracin Zinc + Polymyxin B Sulfate', 30.20, 1, 'x 10 gm tube', '10 gm tube', 'Ointment', '(3.5 mg+400 IU+5000 IU)/gm', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nebanol Plus 10 gm:</strong></p><p>Nebanol Plus Ointment is used to treat and prevent infections in various skin conditions, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Infected wounds, burns, or skin grafts</strong></li><li><strong>Preparation of donor sites for skin grafting</strong></li><li><strong>Prevention of infection in extensive burns and contaminated wounds</strong></li><li><strong>Chronic varicose or other indolent ulcers</strong></li><li><strong>Furuncles, carbuncles, pyoderma, sycosis barbae, impetigo, and acne</strong></li><li><strong>Secondary infected skin lesions of scabies, pediculosis, tinea pedis, and contact or allergic dermatitis</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Composition:</strong></p><ul><li>Neomycin Sulphate BP: 3.5 mg per gram</li><li>Bacitracin Zinc BP: 400 IU per gram</li><li>Polymyxin B Sulphate BP: 5000 IU per gram</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Aural Anti-bacterial preparations</li><li>Ophthalmic antibacterial drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neomycin</strong> is an aminoglycoside that binds to the bacterial ribosome, disrupting protein and DNA synthesis, effective against gram-negative aerobes and some staphylococci.</li><li><strong>Bacitracin</strong> inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis, effective against gram-positive bacteria and some gram-negative species.</li><li><strong>Polymyxin B</strong> disrupts the bacterial cytoplasmic membrane of gram-negative organisms by binding to membrane phospholipids.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult use:</strong> Apply a thin layer to the affected area 1-3 times daily until the infection is controlled and healing is complete.</li><li><strong>Eye Drops:</strong> 1-2 drops 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Eye Ointment:</strong> Apply a 1/2 inch ribbon to the conjunctival sac every 3-4 hours for 7-10 days for acute infections.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Not for use in individuals with sensitivity to any of the components.</li><li>Avoid use in neonates due to the risk of increased absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include nausea, vomiting, skin irritation, rashes, kidney issues, hearing loss, dizziness, muscle twitching, and seizures.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C:</strong> Animal studies show potential harm to the fetus, and there\'s insufficient human data. Use only if benefits outweigh risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> The drug may be excreted in breast milk; use with caution.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Not suitable for use in surgical procedures involving the abdominal or thoracic cavities, as neomycin may potentiate neuromuscular block and cause respiratory paralysis.</li><li>Prolonged use may lead to the overgrowth of non-susceptible organisms.</li><li>Ototoxicity may occur with neomycin.</li><li>In infants and neonates, absorption through immature skin may be enhanced.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, and keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness have not been established for children under 2 years.</li><li><strong>Geriatric use:</strong> No significant differences in safety or efficacy for elderly patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong> Topical antibiotic preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>The combination of <strong>Polymyxin B</strong>, <strong>Neomycin</strong>, and <strong>Bacitracin</strong> creates a broad-spectrum antibacterial effect, targeting both gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria. This combination enhances the overall bactericidal activity and provides a more effective treatment for topical bacterial infections.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy Considerations:</strong></p><ul><li>The effects of topically applied neomycin during pregnancy are not well-established. However, due to potential fetal risks, it should only be used if absolutely necessary and the benefits outweigh the potential risks.</li></ul>', 'active', '818_1742674883.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:21:23', '2025-05-04 00:33:19'),
(4194, 'Naprox Plus', 'Naproxen Sodium + Esomeprazole Magnesium', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Naprox Plus 500 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p> It is indicated for the relief of signs and symptoms of osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis, and to decrease the risk of developing gastric ulcers in patients at risk of developing NSAID-associated gastric ulcers.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Naprox Plus is a combination of Naproxen and Esomeprazole. Naproxen is a NSAID with analgesic and antipyretic activities. It inhibits prostaglandin synthesis. Esomeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor that suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the H+/K+ ATPase in the gastric parietal cell. By acting specifically on the proton pump, Esomeprazole blocks the final step in acid production, thus reducing gastric acidity.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Naprox Plus 500 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p> The recommended dose is one tablet twice daily. Do not split, chew, crush, or dissolve the tablet. This tablet should be taken at least 30 minutes before meals.</p><p><strong>Interactions of Naprox Plus 500 mg+20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ACE Inhibitors, diuretics, and beta-blockers</strong>: May reduce the antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin</strong>: Increases the risk of nephrotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Lithium</strong>: Increases plasma levels of lithium.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: May increase the serum levels of methotrexate.</li><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>: May increase the risk of bleeding complications.</li><li><strong>Digoxin</strong>: May increase digoxin toxicity.</li><li><strong>Clopidogrel</strong>: May decrease the exposure to the active metabolite of clopidogrel.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any components of this product. It is also contraindicated in patients who have experienced asthma, urticaria, or allergic-type reactions after taking aspirin or other NSAIDs and for the treatment of perioperative pain in the setting of coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Naprox Plus 500 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p> The most common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Gastritis</li><li>Erosive gastritis</li><li>Dyspepsia</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Gastric ulcer</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The use of this tablet should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Caution should be exercised in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Fatal cardiovascular thrombotic events like myocardial infarction, stroke, hypertension, congestive heart failure, and edema.</li><li>Serious GI adverse events.</li><li>Hypomagnesaemia.</li><li>Renal or hepatic insufficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '101_1742675008.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:23:28', '2025-03-23 01:23:28'),
(4195, 'Antazol Plus', 'Sodium Cromoglicate + Xylometazoline Hydrochloride', 130.00, 1, 'x 120 metered sprays', '120 metered sprays', 'Nasal Spray', '(2.6 mg+0.0325 mg)/spray', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is the text with the headings in normal bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Antazol Plus</strong></p><p>This Nasal Spray is indicated for the prophylaxis and treatment of allergic rhinitis (such as hay fever and perennial rhinitis) where this is accompanied by nasal congestion.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Mast Cell Stabiliser</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Xylometazoline Hydrochloride is a sympathomimetic agent with alpha-adrenergic activity. It produces vasoconstriction thus reducing nasal congestion. This enables patients suffering from colds to breathe more easily through the nose. Sodium Cromoglicate usually acts through a local effect on the nasal mucosa. It prevents the release of the mediators of type-I allergic reactions, including histamine and slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis (SRS-A) from sensitized mast cells after the antigen-antibody union has taken place. The drug does not inhibit the binding of IgE and the specific antigen; instead, it suppresses the release of substances (e.g., histamine, SRS-A) in response to this reaction. The drug also inhibits Type-III (late allergic, Arthers) reactions to a lesser extent.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Antazol Plus</strong></p><p>Adults (including the elderly) and children: One spray each nostril four times daily.</p><p><strong>Administration of Antazol Plus</strong></p><p>How to use the Nasal Spray- Shake the bottle gently and remove the dust cover. Hold the spray with your forefinger and middle finger on either side of the nozzle and your thumb underneath the bottle. Press down until a fine spray appears. If using for the first time or if you have not used it for a week or more, press the nasal applicator several times until a fine mist comes out from the container. Gently blow the nose to clear the nostrils. Close one nostril and carefully insert the nasal applicator into the open nostril. Tilt your head forward slightly and keep the spray upright. Breathe in through your nose, and while breathing in, press the white collar of the nasal applicator firmly down once to release a spray. Breathe out through your mouth. Repeat the above steps in the same/other nostril for consecutive doses.</p><p><strong>Cleaning</strong></p><p>The nasal spray should be cleaned at least once a week. The procedures are as follows: Remove the dust cover. Gently pull off the nasal applicator. Wash the applicator and dust cover in warm water. Shake off the excess water and leave to dry in a normal place. Avoid applying additional heat. Gently push the applicator back on the top of the bottle and re-fix the dust cover.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to any ingredients of the preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Antazol Plus</strong></p><p>No serious side effects have been reported. Occasional irritation of the nasal mucosa may occur during the first day of use. In rare cases with Sodium Cromoglicate solutions alone, wheezing and tightness of the chest have been reported. Because a lower dose of Xylometazoline is employed in this preparation, it causes mild side effects such as nasal irritation, dryness of the nose, sneezing, headache, insomnia, drowsiness, and palpitations.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>As with all medicines, caution should be exercised during pregnancy, especially during the first trimester.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The prolonged use or abuse of decongestants in general may lead to rebound congestion or drug-induced rhinitis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Mast Cell Stabiliser</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Xylometazoline Hydrochloride is a sympathomimetic agent with alpha-adrenergic activity. It produces vasoconstriction thus reducing nasal congestion. This enables patients suffering from colds to breathe more easily through the nose. Sodium Cromoglicate usually acts through a local effect on the nasal mucosa. It prevents the release of the mediators of type-I allergic reactions, including histamine and slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis (SRS-A) from sensitized mast cells after the antigen-antibody union has taken place. The drug does not inhibit the binding of IgE and the specific antigen; instead, it suppresses the release of substances (e.g., histamine, SRS-A) in response to this reaction. The drug also inhibits Type-III (late allergic, Arthers) reactions to a lesser extent.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>As with all medicines, caution should be exercised during pregnancy, especially during the first trimester.</p>', 'active', '314_1742675688.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:34:48', '2025-05-02 23:43:40'),
(4196, 'Osteo-D', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 75.00, 1, 'x 15 ml bottle', '15 ml bottle', 'Pediatric Drops', '200 IU/ml', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Osteo-D 15 ml Indications</h3><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Treatment and prevention</strong> of Vitamin D3 deficiency.</li><li><strong>Adjunct therapy</strong> for conditions like osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcaemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency.</li></ul><p>Colecalciferol is essential for normal bone growth, development, and maintaining bone density. It also aids in the absorption of calcium and phosphorus, and is crucial for healthy development in babies. It functions as a hormone and plays a role in various other health benefits, such as reducing the severity of bacterial infections, improving lung function, and supporting type 2 diabetes management.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Vitamin in bone formation</strong></li><li><strong>Vitamin D preparations</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Colecalciferol aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is vital for bone health, helps prevent bone loss, and supports other functions like immune response, lung health, and maintaining insulin levels in diabetes.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Vitamin D3 deficiency treatment</strong>: 40,000 IU weekly for 7 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance therapy</strong>: 1,400-2,000 IU/day. After initiating maintenance therapy, check 25-hydroxyvitamin D levels 3-4 months later.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Osteoporosis adjunct therapy</strong>: 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li><strong>Children (12-18 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Syrup:</strong></li><li><strong>Risk of deficiency</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">0-1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml).</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml).</blockquote><ul><li><strong>Deficiency treatment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">0-1 year: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 years: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection (for prevention and deficiency):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Infants (receiving Vitamin D enriched milk)</strong>: 0.5 ml (100,000 IU) every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Nursed infants or children up to 5 years</strong>: 1 ml (200,000 IU) every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adolescents</strong>: 1 ml (200,000 IU) every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: 0.5 ml (100,000 IU) from the 6th/7th month.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly</strong>: 0.5 ml (100,000 IU) every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Patients with digestive disorders or taking antiepileptics</strong>: 0.5-1 ml (100,000–200,000 IU) every 3-6 months.</li></ul><h3>Interaction</h3><ul><li><strong>Drug interactions</strong>: Phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, liquid paraffin (laxatives), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to Vitamin D3.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>Common side effects</strong>: Hypercalcaemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, abdominal pain.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>Safe use up to <strong>4,000 IU</strong> during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Recommended intake for pregnant women</strong> is 400 IU, but higher doses may be needed for those deficient.</li><li><strong>Vitamin D3 and its metabolites</strong> are excreted in breast milk, so consider the total dose if additional supplements are given to a breastfed child.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Caution</strong>: In patients with impaired renal function.</li><li><strong>Overdose</strong>: Can lead to hypervitaminosis D (excessive vitamin D).</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30ºC</strong>, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li>The safety and efficacy in <strong>children under 12 years</strong> have not been established.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Colecalciferol</strong> enhances calcium and phosphorus absorption/reabsorption, supports bone health, immune system function, and helps reduce cancer risk and manage type 2 diabetes.</li></ul>', 'active', '860_1742675814.jpg', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:36:54', '2025-05-04 01:32:26'),
(4197, 'Fenofex', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '120 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Fenofex 120 mg</strong></h3><p><strong>Allergic Rhinitis:</strong></p><ul><li>In adults and children 12 years of age and older, Fenofex 120 mg is recommended for the alleviation of seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis symptoms, such as sneezing, rhinorrhea, lacrimation, itchy red eyes, and itchy nose, palate, and throat.</li><li>Fenofex enhances both work/activity productivity and health-related quality of life.</li></ul><p><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria:</strong></p><ul><li>For the treatment of chronic idiopathic urticaria symptoms in adults and children 12 years and older, Fenofex 120 mg is recommended.</li><li>It significantly reduces the number of wheals and pruritus associated with chronic idiopathic urticaria, improving work/activity productivity and quality of life.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li>Fenofex 120 mg is a selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist and works as an antihistamine.</li><li>After oral administration, it is quickly absorbed and reaches peak plasma concentration in 2 to 3 hours.</li><li>It does not appear to cross the blood-brain barrier.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><p><strong>Seasonal Allergic Rhinitis:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children 12 years and older:</strong>Tablet: 60 mg twice daily or 120 mg once daily or 180 mg once daily</li><li><strong>In case of impaired renal function:</strong> 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children from 6 to 11 years:</strong>Tablet: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily</li><li><strong>In case of impaired renal function:</strong> 30 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children from 2 to 11 years:</strong>Suspension: 30 mg or 5 ml twice daily</li><li><strong>In case of impaired renal function:</strong> 30 mg or 5 ml once daily</li></ul><p><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children 12 years and older:</strong>Tablet: 60 mg twice daily or 120 mg once daily or 180 mg once daily</li><li><strong>In case of impaired renal function:</strong> 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children from 6 to 11 years:</strong>Tablet: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily</li><li><strong>In case of impaired renal function:</strong> 30 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children from 6 months to less than 2 years:</strong>Suspension: 15 mg or 2.5 ml (1/2 tsp) twice daily</li><li><strong>In case of impaired renal function:</strong> 15 mg or 2.5 ml (1/2 tsp) once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children from 2 to 11 years:</strong>Suspension: 30 mg or 5 ml (1 tsp) twice daily</li><li><strong>In case of impaired renal function:</strong> 30 mg or 5 ml (1 tsp) once daily</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li>Co-administration with <strong>erythromycin</strong> or <strong>ketoconazole</strong> increases fexofenadine plasma levels by 2 to 3 times.</li><li>There is <strong>no effect</strong> on the QT interval and <strong>no increase in adverse reactions</strong>.</li><li><strong>Omeprazole</strong> does not interact with Fenofex.</li><li>Bioavailability decreases if Fenofex 120 mg is taken <strong>15 minutes before antacids</strong> containing aluminum and magnesium hydroxide. It is advised to wait <strong>two hours</strong> before taking antacids.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Fenofex 120 mg or any of its components.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><p><strong>Common:</strong></p><ul><li>Nervous system: <strong>headaches</strong>, <strong>fatigue</strong>, <strong>vertigo</strong>.</li><li>Gastrointestinal: <strong>nausea</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Uncommon:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Exhaustion</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Post-marketing (Frequency not known):</strong></p><ul><li>Immune system: <strong>hypersensitivity</strong>, <strong>angioedema</strong>, <strong>tightness in the chest</strong>, <strong>dyspnea</strong>, <strong>flushing</strong>, and <strong>systemic anaphylaxis</strong>.</li><li>Mental: <strong>anxiety</strong>, <strong>nightmares</strong>, <strong>insomnia</strong>, <strong>excessive dreaming</strong>.</li><li>Cardiovascular: <strong>palpitations</strong>, <strong>tachycardia</strong>.</li><li>Gastrointestinal: <strong>diarrhea</strong>.</li><li>Skin: <strong>rash</strong>, <strong>urticaria</strong>, <strong>pruritus</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>Fenofex 120 mg is <strong>Category C</strong> by the US FDA for pregnancy, meaning it should only be used if the potential benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li>It <strong>passes into human breast milk</strong> and should not be used by breastfeeding mothers unless absolutely necessary.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Renal or hepatic impairment:</strong> Fenofex should be used with caution in patients with renal or hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular concerns:</strong> Patients with a history of active cardiovascular disease should be aware of potential issues like <strong>tachycardia</strong> and <strong>palpitations</strong>.</li><li><strong>Driving &amp; machinery use:</strong> Fenofex is <strong>unlikely</strong> to affect the ability to drive or operate machinery, but it is advisable to assess individual responses.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Overdose may result in <strong>dry mouth</strong>, <strong>sleepiness</strong>, <strong>fatigue</strong>, and <strong>dizziness</strong>.</li><li>Fenofex has been tolerated in doses up to <strong>800 mg</strong> without significant adverse effects.</li><li>In case of overdose, <strong>symptomatic treatment</strong> is recommended, as Fenofex is not effectively removed by hemodialysis.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Store dry</strong> and <strong>away from heat and light</strong>.</li><li>Keep <strong>out of reach of children</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li>Fenofex is an <strong>inverse agonist</strong> of the H1 histamine receptor. It prevents the activation of the receptor and reduces the downstream allergic reactions such as pruritus, rhinorrhea, and watery eyes.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> In patients with renal impairment, the dose should be reduced to <strong>60 mg once daily</strong> due to increased bioavailability and half-life.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment:</strong> Fexofenadine pharmacokinetics are not significantly impacted by moderate to severe hepatic disease.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Fexofenadine\'s pharmacokinetics are enhanced in those over 65, but adverse effects remain comparable to younger individuals.</li></ul>', 'active', '1948_1742675922.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:38:42', '2025-05-02 17:48:33'),
(4198, 'Pulmodox', 'Doxophylline', 8.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pulmodox 200 mg</strong></p><p>Pulmodox 200 mg is used to treat the following indications:</p><ul><li>Bronchial asthma</li><li>Bronchospasm</li><li>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</li><li>Pulmonary disease with a spastic bronchial component</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Pulmodox 200 mg is a novel bronchodilator that structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. It selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. However, unlike Theophylline, Pulmodox 200 mg appears to have decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, potentially accounting for its improved safety profile. Additionally, it is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Pulmodox 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 400 mg tablet two or three times daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>&gt;12 years of age</strong>: 10 ml syrup or 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>6-12 years of age</strong>: 6-9 mg/kg body weight two times daily (e.g., if body weight is 10 kg, 3 ml (60 mg) two times daily), or as prescribed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Pulmodox 200 mg</strong></p><p>Pulmodox 200 mg should not be administered together with other xanthine derivatives. Toxic synergism with ephedrine has been documented for xanthines. Like other xanthines, concomitant therapy with troleandomycin, lincomycin, clindamycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, ranitidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccine may decrease the hepatic clearance of xanthines, leading to an increase in blood levels.</p><p>No evidence of a relationship between Pulmodox 200 mg serum concentrations and toxic events has been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Pulmodox 200 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Acute myocardial infarction</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Lactating women</li><li>Patients who have shown hypersensitivity to its components</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Pulmodox 200 mg</strong></p><p>Pulmodox 200 mg rarely causes serious side effects; however, possible side effects are similar to those of taking excess amounts of caffeine, including:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Headache</li><li>Upset stomach</li><li>Heartburn</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Animal reproduction studies indicate that Pulmodox 200 mg does not cause fetal harm when administered to pregnant animals or affect reproductive capacity. However, due to limited experience in pregnant humans, xanthines should only be given to pregnant women when clearly necessary. It is important to note that Pulmodox 200 mg is contraindicated in nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The half-life of xanthine derivatives is influenced by several known variables. It may be prolonged in patients with liver disease, congestive heart failure, and those taking certain other drugs like erythromycin, troleandomycin, lincomycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccine. In these cases, a lower dose of Pulmodox 200 mg may be needed.</p><p>Phenytoin, other anticonvulsants, and smoking may cause an increase in clearance with a shorter mean half-life. In these cases, higher doses of Pulmodox 200 mg may be needed.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Pulmodox 200 mg</strong></p><p>In case of overdose, severe cardiac arrhythmias and tonic-clonic seizures may occur. These effects may represent the first signs of intoxication. The appearance of side effects may require discontinuation of treatment, which, if necessary, at the physician’s discretion, may be resumed at lower doses after all signs and symptoms of toxicity have subsided. As there is no specific antidote, symptomatic treatment of cardiovascular collapse should be instituted in case of overdose.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children. Pulmodox 200 mg should be used only on the prescription of a specialist physician.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Pulmodox 200 mg is a novel bronchodilator. It structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. Pulmodox 200 mg selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, thereby relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. However, differently from Theophylline, Pulmodox 200 mg appears to have decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, which may account for its better safety profile. Pulmodox 200 mg is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p>', 'active', '1865_1742676122.webp', 8, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:42:02', '2025-05-04 14:55:43');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4199, 'Asynta', 'Sodium Alginate + Potassium Bicarbonate', 150.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '(500 mg+100 mg)/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Asynta 200 ml</strong></h3><p>This preparation is indicated for the treatment of symptoms resulting from the reflux of acid, bile, and pepsin into the esophagus. These symptoms include acid regurgitation, heartburn, and indigestion. It may be used in cases such as:</p><ul><li>After gastric surgery</li><li>As a result of hiatus hernia</li><li>During pregnancy</li><li>Accompanying reflux esophagitis</li><li>Symptoms of laryngopharyngeal reflux (e.g., hoarseness, voice disorders, sore throat, and cough)</li></ul><p>Asynta is also used to treat the symptoms of gastroesophageal reflux during concomitant treatment with or following the withdrawal of acid-suppressing therapy.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Antacids</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Sodium Alginate is a naturally occurring substance. It reacts with the acid in the stomach to form a gel. Potassium Bicarbonate also reacts with stomach acid to form carbon dioxide bubbles. These bubbles are trapped by the gel, allowing it to float like a raft on top of the stomach contents. This raft prevents acid from flowing back into the esophagus, alleviating reflux symptoms like heartburn.</p><p>The mode of action is physical and does not depend on absorption into the bloodstream. The raft lasts for up to 4 hours before being broken down and excreted in the feces.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Asynta 200 ml</strong></h3><p><strong>Tablet</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: 1-2 tablets, 4 times daily (after meals and at bedtime)</li><li><strong>Children 6-12 years</strong>: 1 tablet, 4 times daily (after meals and at bedtime)</li></ul><p><strong>Suspension</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: 5-10 ml, 4 times daily (after meals and at bedtime)</li><li><strong>Children 2-12 years</strong>: 2.5-5 ml, 4 times daily (after meals and at bedtime)</li></ul><p><strong>Not recommended</strong> for children under 2 years of age.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Interaction of Asynta 200 ml</strong></h3><p>A time interval of 2 hours should be observed between the intake of Asynta and other medicinal products, especially:</p><ul><li>Tetracyclines</li><li>Fluoroquinolones</li><li>Iron salts</li><li>Thyroid hormones</li><li>Chloroquine</li><li>Bisphosphonates</li><li>Estramustine</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Sodium Alginate or Potassium Bicarbonate</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Asynta 200 ml</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Common side effects</strong>: Nausea, constipation, diarrhea, or headache</li><li><strong>Overdose effects</strong>: Sensation of swelling, consult a physician in such cases.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Can be used during pregnancy if clinically needed.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: No known effect on breastfed infants. Can be used during breastfeeding.</li><li><strong>Fertility</strong>: No known effect on human fertility.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Caution in patients with renal impairment or congestive heart failure.</li><li>Take care with patients having hypercalcemia, nephrocalcinosis, or recurrent calcium-containing renal calculi.</li><li>Reduced efficacy may occur in patients with very low levels of gastric acid.</li><li>If symptoms do not improve after 7 days, review the clinical situation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Asynta 200 ml</strong></h3><p>Overdosage is rare. In case of overdose, consult a registered physician.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dose modifications are necessary.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dose modifications are necessary.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: Caution if a highly restricted salt diet is necessary.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Antacids</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Asynta combines <strong>Sodium Alginate</strong> and <strong>Potassium Bicarbonate</strong>:</p><ul><li>Sodium Alginate reacts with stomach acid to form a gel.</li><li>Potassium Bicarbonate reacts with acid to produce carbon dioxide bubbles.</li><li>These bubbles are trapped by the gel, creating a raft that floats on top of the stomach contents, preventing acid reflux.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Can be used during pregnancy if clinically needed. No known effect on breast-fed infants. Can be used during breastfeeding.</p>', 'active', '115_1742676210.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:43:30', '2025-05-03 00:03:09'),
(4200, 'Zo-MUPS', 'Esomeprazole', 11.00, 14, 'Tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'MUPS Tablet', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Zo-MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><p>Zo-MUPS 20 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Alleviating GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn.</li><li>Treating erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Maintaining the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Eradicating Helicobacter pylori infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Treating Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</li><li>Managing duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Zo-MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. If healing does not occur after 4-8 weeks, further treatment may be considered for an additional 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms persist, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication</strong>: Triple therapy with esomeprazole 40 mg once daily, amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily, and clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg once daily. Dose should be adjusted individually and continued as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks, depending on response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: 40 mg once daily by intravenous injection (administered over a minimum of 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Zo-MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take the capsule one hour before a meal and swallow it whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>: Dissolve the contents of the entire packet in 15 ml of water. Stir thoroughly, wait 2-3 minutes for it to thicken, then consume within 30 minutes.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Administration</strong>: Reconstitute the solution with 5 ml of water for injection (WFI), and administer over at least 3 minutes. Do not use if there are visible particles in the solution.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Zo-MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CYP2C19 and CYP3A4</strong> metabolize esomeprazole.</li><li><strong>Inhibits CYP2C19</strong>: Co-administration of esomeprazole with diazepam may increase diazepam plasma levels by 45%.</li><li>Esomeprazole may reduce the absorption of drugs that depend on gastric pH (e.g., ketoconazole, iron salts, digoxin).</li><li>No significant interactions with warfarin, phenytoin, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Zo-MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, dry mouth.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>A positive response to treatment does not rule out the possibility of stomach cancer.</li><li>Take esomeprazole capsules at least one hour before meals.</li><li>If swallowing capsules is difficult, mix the pellets with cold applesauce and ingest immediately.</li><li>Antacids may be used while taking esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Zo-MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>In rats, a lethal dose is 510 mg/kg (about 103 times the human dose based on body surface area).</li><li>Symptoms of overdose include tremors, ataxia, decreased motor activity, and convulsions.</li><li>Symptomatic and supportive care should be provided in case of overdose, as no specific countermeasure is known.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place at a temperature not exceeding 30°C. Protect from moisture and light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor that works by inhibiting the H+/K+-ATPase enzyme in gastric parietal cells, reducing stomach acid production.</li><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: Peak plasma levels occur about 1.5 hours after oral dosing.</li><li><strong>Distribution</strong>: Plasma protein binding is 97%.</li><li><strong>Metabolism</strong>: Esomeprazole is metabolized mainly by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 in the liver.</li><li><strong>Excretion</strong>: 80% of an oral dose is excreted in urine as inactive metabolites, and 20% in feces.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Insufficient and unreliable data on use during pregnancy. No teratogenic effects observed in animal studies.</li><li>Excretion in breast milk not investigated; breastfeeding should be discontinued if esomeprazole is used.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No significant differences in safety or effectiveness in elderly patients, though they may be more sensitive.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage change for mild to moderate hepatic insufficiency. For severe hepatic impairment, do not exceed 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage adjustments are necessary as less than 1% of the drug is excreted unchanged in the urine.</li></ul>', 'active', '1153_1742676346.png', 67, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:45:46', '2025-05-13 07:29:27'),
(4201, 'Sulprex', 'Salbutamol + Ipratropium', 20.00, 5, 'x 3 ml ampoule', '3 ml ampoule', 'Nebuliser Solution', '(2.5 mg+500 mcg)/3 ml', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Sulprex 3 ml</h3><p>Sulprex 3 ml Inhaler is indicated for use in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) who are on a regular aerosol bronchodilator and continue to experience evidence of bronchospasm. It is used when a second bronchodilator is required.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Combined bronchodilators</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Sulprex 3 ml contains Ipratropium Bromide and Salbutamol.</p><ul><li><strong>Ipratropium Bromide</strong> is an anticholinergic agent that works by inhibiting vagally-mediated reflexes. It antagonizes acetylcholine, which is released by the vagus nerve, and prevents the increase of intracellular cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGMP). This action opens the bronchi and leads to bronchodilation.</li><li><strong>Salbutamol</strong> is a selective beta2-adrenoceptor agonist. At therapeutic doses, it primarily targets beta2-adrenoceptors in bronchial smooth muscles, causing bronchodilation. Its effects are short-acting (lasting 4-6 hours) with a rapid onset (within 5 minutes) for reversible airway obstruction.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Inhaler Puffs</strong>: 2 puffs, 4 times a day. Additional puffs may be taken as required, but the total number of puffs should not exceed 12 in 24 hours.</li><li><strong>Inhaler Solution</strong>: The inhalation solution in ampoules may be administered from a suitable nebulizer or an intermittent positive pressure ventilator.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults (including elderly)</strong>: One ampoule as required for symptom relief, or as directed. Up to 3-4 ampoules may be used daily.</li><li><strong>Emergency</strong>: If acute or worsening dyspnea occurs and additional inhalations do not help, patients should consult a doctor or go to the nearest hospital immediately.</li></ul><h3>Interaction</h3><p>Sulprex 3 ml can be used with other medications commonly used for COPD, including sympathomimetic bronchodilators, methylxanthines, and steroids, without significant adverse drug reactions. However, no formal drug interaction studies have been conducted.</p><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to soya lecithin, soybean, peanuts, or any other component of the drug product.</li><li><strong>Allergy to Atropine or its derivatives</strong>: Contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to atropine or its derivatives.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>Salbutamol</strong>: Rare side effects include mild tremors, headaches, and muscle cramps. Severe reactions like angioedema, urticaria, bronchospasm, hypotension, and collapse may occur very rarely.</li><li><strong>Ipratropium</strong>: Side effects may include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Lower respiratory</strong>: Bronchitis, dyspnea, coughing, pneumonia, bronchospasm</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Upper respiratory</strong>: Pharyngitis, sinusitis, rhinitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Others</strong>: Headache, chest pain, nausea, influenza</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: There are no well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Use during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether the components of Sulprex 3 ml are excreted in human milk. It should not be used by breastfeeding mothers unless the benefit outweighs the risk.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Caution is needed</strong> in patients with:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Narrow-angle glaucoma</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prostatic hyperplasia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Bladder-neck obstruction</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Convulsive disorders</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hyperthyroidism</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diabetes mellitus</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sensitivity to sympathomimetic amines</li><li><strong>Beta-adrenergic agents</strong> may cause hypokalemia, which can lead to cardiovascular issues. The reduction in serum potassium is typically transient and doesn\'t require supplementation.</li><li><strong>Hepatic or renal insufficiency</strong>: The combined Ipratropium and Salbutamol Inhaler has not been studied in patients with hepatic or renal insufficiency, so caution is advised.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>Salbutamol Overdose</strong>: Overdose symptoms may include tachycardia, angina, hypertension, hypotension, palpitations, tremors, arrhythmia, and flushing.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment</strong>: Sedatives and tranquilizers may be used. In severe cases, intensive therapy may be required. Beta-receptor blockers (preferably beta1-selective) can act as specific antidotes but should be used carefully in patients with asthma or bronchial obstruction.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and efficacy in pediatric populations have not been established.</li></ul>', 'active', '638_1742676531.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:48:51', '2025-05-04 21:26:24'),
(4202, 'Rhinil', 'Cetirizine Hydrochloride', 3.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rhinil 10 mg</strong></p><p>Rhinil 10 mg is indicated for the relief of symptoms associated with seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis. It is also indicated for the treatment of the uncomplicated skin manifestations of chronic idiopathic urticaria and allergen-induced asthma.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Sedating Anti-histamine</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rhinil 10 mg is a potent H1 receptor antagonist without any significant anticholinergic and antiserotonic effects. At pharmacologically active dose levels, it has almost no drowsiness effect and does not cause behavioral changes. It inhibits the histamine-mediated early phase of the allergic reaction and also reduces the migration of inflammatory cells and the release of mediators associated with the late phase of the allergic reaction.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rhinil 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Children 6 years and older</strong>: 1 tablet or 2 teaspoonfuls daily (or 1 teaspoonful twice daily).</li><li><strong>Children 2-6 years</strong>: 1 teaspoonful once daily or 1/2 teaspoonful twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6 months to 2 years</strong>: 1/2 teaspoonful once daily. The dose in children 12-23 months of age can be increased to a maximum dose as 1/2 teaspoonful every 12 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Rhinil 10 mg</strong></p><p>No clinically significant drug interactions have been found with Theophylline, Azithromycin, Pseudoephedrine, Ketoconazole, or Erythromycin and with other drugs.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Cetirizine or hydroxyzine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Rhinil 10 mg</strong></p><p>The most common side effect that occurred more frequently with Cetirizine is somnolence.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>US FDA Pregnancy Category of Cetirizine Hydrochloride is B. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Cetirizine Hydrochloride has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Cetirizine Hydrochloride is administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised when driving a car or operating heavy machinery.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1668_1742676612.jpg', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:50:12', '2025-05-04 16:43:02'),
(4203, 'Algita DX', 'Algae Calcium + Vitamin D3', 16.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Algita DX</strong></p><ul><li>Treatment of osteoporosis, rickets, osteomalacia, tetany, and hypoparathyroidism</li><li>In pregnancy and lactation due to increased demand</li><li>In kidney disease and pancreatitis</li><li>During therapy with antiseizure medications</li><li>The prevention and treatment of calcium and vitamin D3 deficiency</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate (Algae Source) BP 1250 mg, equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 200 IU as Cholecalciferol USP</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Algita DX</strong></p><ul><li>Usual adult dose: 1 tablet in the morning and 1 tablet at night.</li><li>Tablet should be taken orally.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Algita DX</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With medicine:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Calcium salts reduce the absorption of bisphosphonates, ciprofloxacin, fluorides, iron, levothyroxine, tetracycline, and zinc.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Absorption of calcium salts is reduced by corticosteroids.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increased risk of hypercalcemia when calcium and vitamin D3 are given with thiazides and related diuretics.</li><li><strong>With food and others:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">No clinically significant food-drug interactions have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypocalcemia and hypercalciuria</li><li>Metastatic calcification</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the components of calcium and vitamin D3 preparation</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Algita DX</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common side effects</strong> may include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Irregular heartbeat</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dry mouth</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Weakness</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Headache</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">A metallic taste in the mouth</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Muscle or bone pain</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Drowsiness</li><li><strong>Rare side effects</strong> include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">High calcium levels in the blood</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Constipation</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diarrhea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Loss of appetite</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be taken in patients with:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Renal impairment</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sarcoidosis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypercalcemia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypercalciuria</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Algita DX</strong></p><ul><li>At high doses, it may result in:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, etc.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C and away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>This is a combination of Calcium (Algae Source) and Vitamin D3.</li><li>Calcium (Algae Source) is essential for building strong bones and is clinically proven to be useful in the prevention and treatment of osteoporosis and associated fractures.</li><li>Vitamin D3 aids in calcium absorption from the gastrointestinal tract and stimulates bone formation.</li><li>Together, Calcium and Vitamin D3 have synergistic effects on bone growth and the prevention of osteoporosis and fractures.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>During pregnancy and lactation, calcium and vitamin D3 should be used considering the risk-benefit ratio.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Not recommended for children under 12 years.</li></ul>', 'active', '1542_1742676764.jpg', 68, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:52:44', '2025-05-02 23:00:31'),
(4204, 'Topiclo S', 'Clobetasol Propionate + Salicylic Acid', 70.00, 1, 'x 10 gm tube', '10 gm tube', 'Ointment', '0.05%+3%', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Topiclo S 3% 10 gm</h3><p>This ointment is indicated for the relief of the inflammatory manifestations of hyperkeratotic and dry corticosteroid-responsive dermatoses such as:</p><ul><li>Psoriasis</li><li>Chronic atopic dermatitis</li><li>Neurodermatitis (lichen simplex chronicus)</li><li>Lichen planus</li><li>Eczema (including nummular eczema, hand eczema, eczematous dermatitis)</li><li>Seborrheic dermatitis of the scalp</li><li>Ichthyosis vulgaris and other ichthyotic conditions.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong>: A high-potency corticosteroid that decreases inflammation by stabilizing leukocyte lysosomal membranes, preventing the release of destructive acid hydrolases from leukocytes, inhibiting macrophage accumulation in inflamed areas, reducing leukocyte adhesion to capillary endothelium, and more.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid</strong>: Has a potent keratolytic action and slight antiseptic activity. It softens and destroys the stratum corneum by increasing endogenous hydration, causing the horny layer of the skin to swell, soften, and desquamate. At high concentrations, it has a caustic effect and possesses weak antifungal and antibacterial activity.</li></ul><h3>Dosage of Topiclo S 3% 10 gm</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: Apply a thin layer to affected areas twice daily, rubbing in gently and completely. For some patients, maintenance therapy may be achieved with less frequent application. Treatment should be discontinued when control is achieved. If no improvement is seen within 2 weeks, reassessment may be necessary.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Not recommended for pediatric patients under 12 years of age.</li><li>Should not be used with occlusive dressing. Treatment beyond 2 consecutive weeks should not exceed 50 gm/week due to potential suppression of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis.</li></ul><h3>Administration</h3><ul><li>For external use only.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Topiclo S 3% 10 gm</h3><ul><li>No known interactions with Clobetasol Propionate ointment and cream.</li><li>Salicylic acid may increase absorption of other topically applied medicines. Avoid using it with other topical medicines on the same area of skin.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong>: Contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to Clobetasol Propionate. Should not be used for primary infected bacterial or fungal skin lesions without an anti-infective agent, in primary cutaneous viral infections (e.g., herpes simplex, vaccinia, varicella), or in tuberculous skin lesions.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid</strong>: Contraindicated in patients with salicylate hypersensitivity or sensitivity to any other ingredient in the preparation.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Topiclo S 3% 10 gm</h3><ul><li>Prolonged use or large amounts can lead to hypercortisolism, especially in infants and children, and if occlusive dressings are used.</li><li>Local atrophy after prolonged treatment.</li><li>In rare instances, corticosteroid treatment may provoke the pustular form of psoriasis.</li><li>Sensitivity reactions, drying, and irritation due to Salicylic Acid may occur.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>Topical administration of corticosteroids to pregnant animals has caused abnormalities in fetal development. The relevance to humans has not been established.</li><li>Safe use of Clobetasol Propionate during lactation has not been established. It should be used only if the benefit to the mother outweighs the risk to the fetus. Should not be used extensively in pregnant patients for prolonged periods.</li><li>Salicylic Acid ointment safety during pregnancy and lactation is not fully established. Caution is recommended.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Not for prolonged use on large areas of the body.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired peripheral circulation or diabetes.</li><li>Avoid broken skin, mouth, eyes, mucous membranes, and the anogenital region.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Topiclo S 3% 10 gm</h3><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong>: Chronic overdosage may lead to hypercortisolism and adrenal suppression. Discontinue under medical supervision.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid</strong>: Systemic salicylate poisoning symptoms (e.g., tinnitus, dizziness) may occur with prolonged use or misuse of large quantities. Salicylism is unlikely to occur with proper use. Salicylate poisoning is typically associated with plasma concentrations &gt;350 mg/L.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong>: Reduces inflammation by stabilizing leukocyte lysosomal membranes and reducing various inflammatory responses.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid</strong>: Softens and desquamates the stratum corneum and has weak antifungal and antibacterial activity.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li>The safety of Clobetasol Propionate during pregnancy has not been fully established. It should be used only if the benefit to the mother outweighs any potential risks to the fetus. It should not be used extensively or for prolonged periods in pregnant patients. The safety of Salicylic Acid ointment during pregnancy and lactation has not been fully established, and should therefore be used with caution.</li></ul>', 'active', '1773_1742676956.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:55:56', '2025-05-04 22:30:09'),
(4205, 'Osteo-D', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 6.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Chewable Tablet', '1000 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Osteo-D 1000 IU</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated in the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also indicated as an adjunct to specific therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Colecalciferol, a synthetic form of Vitamin D, is essential for normal bone growth and development and for maintaining bone density. It is also necessary for the utilization of both calcium and phosphorus. Babies require Vitamin D3 for healthy growth and development. It acts as a hormone.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin-D preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) helps in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. Vitamin D3 is essential for normal bone growth and to maintain bone density. It also reduces the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, prevents the risk of cancers (e.g., breast, colorectal), and helps maintain adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Osteo-D 1000 IU</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>For Capsules:Adults:Treatment of Vitamin D3 Deficiency:</strong> 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Maintenance Therapy:</strong> 1,400-2,000 IU/day. The target level of 25-hydroxyvitamin D should be confirmed 3-4 months after initiating maintenance therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 Deficiency:</strong> 20,000 IU every 4 weeks. Higher doses may be required in certain situations.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Osteoporosis Therapy:</strong> 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Children (12-18 years):Treatment of Vitamin D3 Deficiency:</strong> 20,000 IU once every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 Deficiency:</strong> 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Film-Coated Tablets:Adults:</strong> 1,000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily, or as directed by a physician. Take the medicine with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Oroflash or Chewable Tablets:Adults:</strong> 1,000 IU to 2,000 IU daily, or as directed by a physician. Take the medicine with food or within 1 hour after a meal. Place the tablet in mouth and swallow after chewing.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>For Syrup:At risk of Cholecalciferol Deficiency:</strong>0-1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml).</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml).</blockquote><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Cholecalciferol Deficient Patients:</strong>0-1 year: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 years: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>For Injection:Prevention:</strong>Infants receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk: ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e., 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Nursed infants or infants not receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk or young children up to 5 years: 1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e., 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adolescents: 1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e., 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Pregnancy: ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e., 100,000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Elderly: ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e., 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Digestive disorders or concomitant treatment with antiepileptics: ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e., 100,000 IU or 1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e., 200,000 IU every 3 or 6 months.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Osteo-D 1000 IU</strong></p><p>It interferes with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (such as liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Osteo-D 1000 IU</strong></p><p>The general side effects include hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, and abdominal pain.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Studies have shown the safe use of doses up to 4,000 IU during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU. However, in women who are Vitamin D3 deficient, a higher dose may be required. Pregnant women should follow their medical practitioner\'s advice, as their needs may vary depending on the severity of the deficiency and their response to treatment.</p><p>Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. Overdose in infants induced by nursing mothers has not been observed. However, additional Vitamin D3 prescribed to a breastfed child should consider the dose of Vitamin D3 given to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be used in patients with impaired renal function.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Osteo-D 1000 IU</strong></p><p>Overdose can lead to hypervitaminosis D.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30ºC, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin-D preparations.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. Vitamin D3 is vital for normal bone growth and maintaining bone density. It also reduces bacterial infection severity, improves lung function, helps prevent certain cancers (breast, colorectal), and supports adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p>', 'active', '1348_1742677076.jpg', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:57:56', '2025-05-04 01:33:11'),
(4206, 'Flonaspray', 'Fluticasone Propionate', 251.70, 1, 'x 120 metered sprays', '120 metered sprays', 'Nasal Spray', '50 mcg/spray', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here’s a summary of the information you provided, with the headings in bold as requested:</p><p><strong>Indications of Flonaspray</strong></p><p>Flonaspray is indicated for the relief of inflammatory and pruritic manifestations of corticosteroid-responsive eczema or dermatitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Fluticasone &amp; combined preparations topical</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Fluticasone propionate is a glucocorticoid with high topical anti-inflammatory potency but low HPA-axis suppressive activity after dermal administration. It has a therapeutic index greater than most commonly available steroids. Fluticasone propionate has high selectivity for the glucocorticoid receptor and shows strong affinity for and agonist activity at human glucocorticoid receptors. The half-life of the fluticasone propionate glucocorticoid-receptor complex is approximately 10 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Flonaspray</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cream</strong>: Apply a thin layer of Fluticasone propionate cream to the affected skin areas once daily.</li><li><strong>Ointment</strong>: Apply a thin layer of Fluticasone propionate ointment to the affected skin areas twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Fluticasone propionate is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Rosacea</li><li>Acne vulgaris</li><li>Peri-oral dermatitis</li><li>Primary cutaneous viral infections (e.g., herpes simplex, chicken pox)</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients</li><li>Perianal and genital pruritus</li><li>It is not indicated in the treatment of primarily infected skin lesions caused by fungi or bacteria, and is not recommended for dermatoses in children under one year of age.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Flonaspray</strong></p><p>Flonaspray is generally well tolerated, but some side effects include:</p><ul><li>Local burning and pruritus</li><li>Hypersensitivity reactions (application should be stopped immediately if signs appear)</li><li>Prolonged use may cause local atrophic changes such as thinning, striae, dilatation of blood vessels, hypertrichosis, and hypopigmentation</li><li>Secondary infections, especially with occlusive dressings</li><li>Exacerbation of dermatoses symptoms</li><li>Systemic absorption leading to hypercorticism in cases of prolonged use, especially in infants and children</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Flonaspray should only be used during pregnancy if the benefit to the mother outweighs the potential risk to the fetus. The excretion of Flonaspray into human breast milk has not been investigated, but its plasma levels are likely to be low following dermal application. The benefits and risks should be weighed for breastfeeding mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged use of high doses on large body areas, especially in infants, may lead to adrenal suppression.</li><li>The face may show atrophic changes after prolonged use of potent corticosteroids.</li><li>Appropriate antimicrobial therapy should be used for infected inflammatory lesions.</li><li>Any spread of infection requires discontinuation of topical corticosteroid therapy and systemic antimicrobial treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Flonaspray</strong></p><p>Acute overdose is very unlikely, but chronic misuse can result in features of hypercorticism. If overdosage occurs, discontinue use. Overdose by ingestion is unlikely due to low oral bioavailability.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30˚C. Do not freeze.</p>', 'active', '702_1742677161.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 01:59:21', '2025-05-03 10:48:41'),
(4207, 'Brofex', 'Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide', 40.13, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '10 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p>Sure! Here’s the text with the headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Brofex 100 ml</strong></p><p>Dextromethorphan is indicated in Chronic dry cough or unproductive cough; Acute dry cough which is interfering with normal function or sleep.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Cough suppressant</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Dextromethorphan suppresses the cough reflex by a direct action on the cough center in the medulla of the brain. Dextromethorphan shows high affinity binding to several regions of the brain, including the medullary cough center. This compound is an NMDA receptor antagonist and acts as a non-competitive channel blocker. It is one of the widely used antitussives, and is also used to study the involvement of glutamate receptors in neurotoxicity.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Brofex 100 ml</strong></p><p>Adults and Children over 12 years: 15 to 30 mg three to four times per day. However, 60 mg doses up to four times per day have been used without increased side effects.</p><p>Children between 6 and 12 years: 5-15 mg up to four times per day.</p><p>Children between 2 and 6 years: 2.5-5 mg up to four times per day.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Brofex 100 ml</strong></p><p>The following medicines should be taken carefully while concomitantly use with Dextromethorphan: Amiodarone, Fluoxetine, Quinidine, CNS depressants and Monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitors.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to Dextromethorphan or any other component.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Brofex 100 ml</strong></p><p>Adverse effects with Dextromethorphan are rare, but nausea and dizziness sometimes occur. The drug produces no analgesia or addiction and little or no CNS depression. Excitation, confusion, and respiratory depression may occur after overdosage.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy: Adequate and well-controlled studies in humans have not been done. However, Dextromethorphan has not been reported to cause birth defects.</p><p>Lactation: It is not known whether dextromethorphan passes into breast milk. However, Dextromethorphan has not been reported to cause problems in nursing babies.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Do not use Dextromethorphan to control a cough that is associated with smoking, asthma, or emphysema, or a cough that is productive (produces sputum or phlegm).</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Brofex 100 ml</strong></p><p>Symptoms: In mild overdose, tachycardia, hypertension, vomiting, mydriasis, diaphoresis, nystagmus, euphoria, loss of motor coordination, and giggling; in moderate intoxication, in addition to those listed above, hallucinations and a plodding ataxic gait; in severe intoxication, agitation or somnolence.</p><p>Management: treatment is symptomatic and supportive. Naloxone may be useful in reversing toxicity.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at 15-30° C</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Cough suppressant</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Dextromethorphan suppresses the cough reflex by a direct action on the cough center in the medulla of the brain. Dextromethorphan shows high affinity binding to several regions of the brain, including the medullary cough center. This compound is an NMDA receptor antagonist and acts as a non-competitive channel blocker. It is one of the widely used antitussives, and is also used to study the involvement of glutamate receptors in neurotoxicity.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Pregnancy: Adequate and well-controlled studies in humans have not been done. However, Dextromethorphan has not been reported to cause birth defects.</p><p>Lactation: It is not known whether dextromethorphan passes into breast milk. However, Dextromethorphan has not been reported to cause problems in nursing babies.</p><p>Let me know if you need any further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '1037_1742677278.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:01:18', '2025-05-03 01:03:45');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4208, 'Omidon', 'Domperidone Maleate', 25.00, 1, 'x 15 ml drop', '15 ml drop', 'Pediatric Drops', '5 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<p>Sure! Here’s the text with the headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Omidon 15 ml</strong></p><p>Dyspeptic symptom complex, often linked with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, may include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric sensation of fullness</li><li>Abdominal distension</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Belching</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Early satiety</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms can occur in various conditions, such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine. Additionally, in Parkinson\'s disease, it can manifest as dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting. For radiological studies, it can be used to speed up barium transit in follow-through radiological examinations.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Omidon 15 ml functions as a dopamine receptor antagonist. It exerts gastroprokinetic action by inhibiting dopamine receptors found in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier means it has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Omidon 15 ml</strong></p><p>For adults, the recommended dosage is 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily. Children should take 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily. When used for acute nausea and vomiting, the maximum treatment duration should not exceed 12 weeks. It is advisable to take this medication 15-30 minutes before a meal.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Omidon 15 ml</strong></p><p>Omidon 15 ml should be taken 15-30 minutes before meals, and if needed, before bedtime. The typical recommended oral dosage of Domperidone is as follows:</p><p>For Adults:</p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li>The maximum daily dose of Domperidone is 80 mg.</li></ul><p>For Children:</p><ul><li>2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg of body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>In cases of dyspeptic symptoms: For Adults:</p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>For Children:</p><ul><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>For acute and sub-acute conditions, especially in cases of acute nausea and vomiting: For Adults:</p><ul><li>20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>For Children:</p><ul><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>When administered rectally in the form of suppositories:</p><p>For Adults (including elderly):</p><ul><li>30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li></ul><p>For Children:</p><ul><li>The maximum daily rectal dose for children weighing between 10 to 25 kg is 30 mg. The dose may be divided throughout the day if necessary.</li></ul><p>Please note that the maximum treatment period for acute nausea and vomiting is 12 weeks.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Omidon 15 ml</strong></p><p>The simultaneous use of anticholinergic drugs may counteract the antidyspeptic effects of domperidone. It\'s important to note that antacids and antisecretory drugs should not be administered concurrently with domperidone as they can reduce its oral bioavailability. Domperidone primarily undergoes metabolism through the CYP3A4 enzyme. In vitro data suggest that coadministration with drugs significantly inhibiting this enzyme may lead to elevated plasma levels of domperidone. Examples of CYP3A4 inhibitors include azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, nefazodone, and others.</p><p>Theoretically, as domperidone has gastro-kinetic effects, it could potentially impact the absorption of concurrently orally administered drugs, especially those with sustained release or enteric-coated formulations. However, in patients already stabilized on medications like digoxin or paracetamol, the concurrent use of domperidone did not affect the blood levels of these drugs. Domperidone does not potentiate the action of neuroleptics, and it suppresses the unwanted peripheral effects such as digestive disorders, nausea, and vomiting caused by dopaminergic agonists like bromocriptine and L-dopa without counteracting their central properties.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Omidon 15 ml is not to be used by patients with a known hypersensitivity to this medication or in the case of neonates. It should also be avoided in situations where gastrointestinal stimulation could be unsafe, such as in cases of gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation. Additionally, it is contraindicated in patients with a prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma).</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Omidon 15 ml</strong></p><p>Side effects are uncommon, with occasional reports of transient intestinal cramps. Extrapyramidal symptoms are rare among young children and exceptionally rare in adults. These symptoms typically resolve spontaneously and completely upon discontinuing the treatment. Since the pituitary gland is situated outside the blood-brain barrier, domperidone may lead to an increase in plasma prolactin levels. In rare cases, this hyperprolactinemia can result in neuroendocrinological effects like galactorrhea and gynecomastia. In situations where the blood-brain barrier is immature (as in infants) or compromised, the possibility of neurological side effects cannot be entirely ruled out. Additionally, there have been occasional reports of rare allergic reactions, including rash and urticaria.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of using Omidon 15 ml during pregnancy has not been established; hence, its use is not recommended during pregnancy. Animal studies have not shown any teratogenic effects on the fetus. Domperidone may induce galactorrhea and enhance post-natal lactation. It is excreted in breast milk in minimal quantities, which are insufficient to be considered harmful.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Omidon 15 ml should be employed with utmost care in children due to the potential for an increased risk of extrapyramidal reactions, which can be more pronounced in young children with an incompletely developed blood-brain barrier. Additionally, considering that domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should also be used cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Omidon 15 ml</strong></p><p>Signs of an overdose can manifest as drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions, particularly in children. If an overdose occurs, it is advisable to administer activated charcoal and closely monitor the patient. The use of anticholinergic drugs, antiparkinson drugs, or antihistamines with anticholinergic properties may be beneficial in managing extrapyramidal reactions.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, Protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Omidon 15 ml acts as a dopamine antagonist, primarily by blocking dopamine receptors located in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its gastroprokinetic effect is achieved by inhibiting dopamine receptors that influence the motility of the gastrointestinal tract. Because it has limited ability to penetrate the blood-brain barrier, Domperidone has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects. Domperidone restores normal motility and tone in the upper gastrointestinal tract, promotes gastric emptying, enhances antral and duodenal peristalsis, and regulates pylorus contraction. Additionally, it increases esophageal peristalsis and lowers esophageal sphincter pressure, preventing the regurgitation of gastric contents.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Use in infants: Given that the metabolic and blood-brain barrier functions are not fully developed during the first months of life, any medication should be administered to infants with great caution and under close medical supervision. Although the typical absence of neurological side effects with domperidone is primarily due to its limited penetration through the blood-brain barrier, the potential occurrence of such effects cannot be entirely ruled out in infants under 1 year of age.</p><p>Use in liver disorders: Because domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should be employed cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p>Use in kidney disorders: In individuals with severe renal insufficiency (serum creatinine &gt;6 mg/100ml, i.e., &gt;0.6 mmol/l), the elimination half-life of domperidone increases from 7.4 to 20.8 hours, although plasma drug levels remain lower than in healthy volunteers. Since very little unchanged drug is excreted through the kidneys, it is unlikely that the dose of a single acute administration needs to be adjusted in patients with renal insufficiency. However, for repeated administration, the dosing frequency should be reduced to once or twice daily, depending on the severity of the impairment, and the dose may need to be lowered. In general, patients on prolonged therapy should undergo regular reviews.</p>', 'active', '341_1742677409.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:03:29', '2025-05-04 01:19:23'),
(4209, 'Algicid Plus', 'Sodium Alginate + Sodium Bicarbonate + Calcium Carbonate', 250.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '(500 mg+267 mg+160 mg)/10 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Algicid Plus 200 ml</strong></p><p>Algicid Plus 200 ml is indicated for individuals experiencing gastric reflux, heartburn, flatulence associated with gastric reflux, heartburn during pregnancy, and any instances of epigastric and retrosternal discomfort where the underlying cause is gastric reflux.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The mode of action of the product is physical and does not depend on absorption into the systemic circulation. Upon ingestion, Algicid Plus 200 ml rapidly reacts with gastric acid, forming a protective Alginic acid gel raft with a nearly neutral pH. This raft floats on the stomach contents, effectively preventing gastro-esophageal reflux for up to 4 hours. In severe cases, the raft itself may be refluxed into the esophagus, providing a soothing effect rather than the stomach contents.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Algicid Plus 200 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: Take 10-20ml after meals and at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 12 years</strong>: Take 5-10ml after meals and at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Children under 6 years</strong>: Not recommended.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustment is needed for this age group.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dose modification necessary.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: Use caution if a highly restricted salt diet is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Algicid Plus 200 ml</strong></p><p>A time interval of 2 hours should be observed between taking this medication and the administration of other medicinal products, particularly:</p><ul><li>Tetracyclines, Digoxin, Fluoroquinolones, Iron salts, Ketoconazole, Neuroleptics, Thyroid hormones, Penicillamine, Beta-blockers (Atenolol, Metoprolol, Propranolol), Glucocorticoids, Chloroquine, Bisphosphonates (Diphosphonates), Estramustine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>The Algicid Plus 200 ml should not be used in patients with a known or suspected hypersensitivity to the active ingredients or any of the excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Algicid Plus 200 ml</strong></p><p>In addition to the intended effects of the medication, certain side effects may occur, including:</p><ul><li>Constipation</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Belching</li><li>If you experience any of these symptoms, please consult a physician. If an excessive dose is taken, you may experience a sensation of swelling. In such cases, it is advisable to seek medical advice.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Clinical studies involving over 500 pregnant women, along with extensive post-marketing data, have shown no evidence of malformation or fetal/neonatal toxicity from the active substances. This medication can be safely used during pregnancy when clinically necessary.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: No adverse effects of the active substances have been observed in breastfed newborns/infants of mothers undergoing treatment. This medication is considered safe for use during breastfeeding.</li><li><strong>Fertility</strong>: Pre-clinical investigations have demonstrated that alginate has no detrimental effects on parental or offspring fertility or reproduction. Clinical data also do not suggest any impact of this medication on human fertility.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>If symptoms do not show improvement within 7 days, it is advisable to reassess the clinical condition.</li><li>Each 10 mL dose contains 141 mg of sodium (equivalent to 6.2 mmol). This information should be considered when prescribing a highly restricted salt diet, especially in cases of congestive cardiac failure and renal impairment.</li><li>Each 10 mL dose contains 160 mg of calcium carbonate (equivalent to 1.6 mmol). Caution is warranted when treating patients with hypercalcemia, nephrocalcinosis, and recurrent calcium-containing renal calculi.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Algicid Plus 200 ml</strong></p><p>In the event of overdose, symptomatic treatment should be given. The patient may notice abdominal distension.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool (below 30°C) and dry place, away from light. Keep out of the reach of children. Do not refrigerate or freeze.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Paediatric Use</strong>: This medication is not recommended for children under 6 years of age.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No dose modification is required for this age group.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Algicid Plus 200 ml operates through a physical mechanism and does not rely on absorption into the systemic circulation. This product combines two antacids, Calcium Carbonate and Sodium Bicarbonate, with Alginate. When ingested, the medication quickly reacts with gastric acid, forming a protective Alginic Acid gel raft with a nearly neutral pH. Studies have demonstrated that this raft interacts with and covers the acid pocket in the stomach, reducing exposure to esophageal acid. The raft effectively floats on the stomach contents, providing up to 4 hours of relief from gastro-esophageal reflux and safeguarding the esophagus from Acid, Pepsin, and Bile.</p><p>In more severe cases, the raft itself may be refluxed into the esophagus, providing a soothing effect instead of the stomach contents. Additionally, in vitro evidence suggests that the raft has a secondary function by entrapping bile and pepsin within its structure, offering further protection to the esophagus from these gastric components. Calcium Carbonate neutralizes gastric acid, delivering rapid relief from indigestion and heartburn. This effect is enhanced by the inclusion of Sodium Bicarbonate, which also has a neutralizing effect.</p>', 'active', '745_1742677550.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:05:50', '2025-05-02 22:57:20'),
(4210, 'Ritch', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 9.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '120 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Ritch 120 mg</h3><p><strong>Allergic Rhinitis</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children (12 years and older)</strong>: For the relief of seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis symptoms, including sneezing, rhinorrhea, lacrimation, itchy/red eyes, itchy nose, palate, and throat.</li><li>Fexofenadine helps enhance both work/activity productivity and health-related quality of life.</li></ul><p><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children (12 years and older)</strong>: For the treatment of symptoms, significantly reducing the number of wheals and pruritus associated with chronic idiopathic urticaria.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines.</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li><strong>Mode of Action</strong>: Fexofenadine is a selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist, blocking the histamine receptor\'s activation and reducing allergic reactions. It prevents the release of pro-inflammatory cytokines, which alleviates symptoms like pruritus and rhinorrhea. Fexofenadine does not cross the blood-brain barrier, which minimizes central nervous system effects.</li><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: Quickly absorbed after oral administration, reaching peak plasma concentration in 2 to 3 hours.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><p><strong>For Allergic Rhinitis</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children (12 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet</strong>: 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: 60 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children (6 to 11 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet</strong>: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: 30 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children (2 to 11 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suspension</strong>: 30 mg or 5 ml twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: 30 mg or 5 ml once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children (12 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet</strong>: 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: 60 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children (6 to 11 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet</strong>: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: 30 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children (6 months to 2 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suspension</strong>: 15 mg or 2.5 ml (1/2 tsp) twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: 15 mg or 2.5 ml (1/2 tsp) once daily.</li><li><strong>Children (2 to 11 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suspension</strong>: 30 mg or 5 ml (1 tsp) twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: 30 mg or 5 ml (1 tsp) once daily.</li></ul><h3>Interaction</h3><ul><li><strong>Erythromycin/Ketoconazole</strong>: Co-administration can increase fexofenadine plasma levels by 2-3 times, but no significant QT interval changes or adverse reactions were observed.</li><li><strong>Antacids containing aluminum and magnesium hydroxide</strong>: Bioavailability of fexofenadine may decrease if taken together. It\'s advised to wait 2 hours between taking fexofenadine and such antacids.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Do not use if allergic to fexofenadine or any of its components.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><p><strong>Common</strong>:</p><ul><li>Nervous system: Headaches, fatigue, dizziness.</li><li>Gastrointestinal: Nausea, general discomfort.</li></ul><p><strong>Uncommon</strong>:</p><ul><li>Fatigue.</li></ul><p><strong>Rare</strong>:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity reactions: Angioedema, chest tightness, dyspnea, flushing, anaphylaxis.</li><li>Mental health issues: Anxiety, nightmares, insomnia, excessive dreaming.</li><li>Cardiac: Palpitations, tachycardia.</li><li>Skin: Rash, urticaria, pruritus.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C (US FDA). It’s advised to avoid during pregnancy unless necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Fexofenadine passes into breast milk, so it’s not recommended for nursing mothers.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Renal/Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Use with caution in patients with impaired kidney or liver function.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Disease</strong>: Antihistamines can lead to tachycardia and palpitations, so patients with cardiovascular conditions should be monitored.</li><li><strong>Driving/Operating Machinery</strong>: Fexofenadine is unlikely to impair the ability to drive or operate machinery, but individual responses should be assessed.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li>Symptoms: Dry mouth, sleepiness, fatigue, dizziness.</li><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: No specific antidote. Standard procedures for handling overdoses should be followed.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Pediatric Use &amp; Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Adjust dosage to 60 mg once daily for patients with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Elderly (over 65 years)</strong>: Pharmacokinetics are altered, but adverse effects are comparable to those under 65.</li></ul>', 'active', '195_1742677740.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:09:00', '2025-05-04 17:02:57'),
(4211, 'Trupan', 'Pantoprazole Sodium', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Trupan 40 mg</strong></p><p>Trupan 40 mg is recommended when inhibiting acid secretion is therapeutically beneficial, i.e. stomach ulcer disorders, illnesses caused by gastroesophageal reflux, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug-induced ulcers (NSAIDs), Helicobacter pylori eradication (in combination with antibiotics), and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Blocker.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Trupan 40 mg is a proton pump inhibitor that works by covalently attaching to the H+/K+ATPase enzyme system on the surface of the gastric parietal cell to decrease the final stage of gastric acid production. Regardless of the stimulus, this impact results in a suppression of both basal and induced stomach acid secretion that lasts for more than 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Trupan 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Oral:Benign gastric ulcer:</strong> 40 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, continued for a further 4 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastro-esophageal reflux disease:</strong> 20-40 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, continued for a further 4 weeks if not fully healed; maintenance dose is 20 mg daily, which may be increased to 40 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal ulcer:</strong> 40 mg daily in the morning for 2 weeks, continued for a further 2 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer associated with Helicobacter pylori:</strong> 40 mg twice daily in association with antimicrobial agents:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Amoxycillin 1 g and Clarithromycin 500 mg both twice daily for one week, or</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clarithromycin 250 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg both twice daily for one week.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of NSAID-associated gastric or duodenal ulcer:</strong> 20 mg daily for those requiring long-term NSAID treatment.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> Initially 80 mg once daily, adjusted according to response (elderly max. 40 mg daily); daily doses above 80 mg should be given in 2 divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV Injection:Duodenal ulcer and gastric ulcer:</strong> 40 mg once daily for 7-10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease associated with a history of erosive esophagitis:</strong> 40 mg once daily for 7-10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of rebleeding in peptic ulcer:</strong> IV 80 mg, followed by 8 mg/hour infusion for 72 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration:</strong> 80 mg IV every 12 hours for 24 hours, followed by 40 mg every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Long-term management of Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome and other pathological hypersecretory conditions:</strong> 80 mg IV every 12 hours, may increase to 80 mg every 8 hours if needed, may titrate to higher doses depending on acid output.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Trupan 40 mg</strong></p><p>In clinical investigations, no significant medication interactions were found.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those with known hypersensitivity to any of the formulation\'s ingredients should not take Trupan 40 mg.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Trupan 40 mg</strong></p><p>Both in the short-term and the long-term, Trupan 40 mg is well tolerated. The most frequent adverse effects are headache and diarrhea, while abdominal pain, flatulence, rash, sleeplessness, and hyperglycemia are only rarely reported side effects.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Trupan 40 mg is classified as a B pregnancy drug by the US FDA. Nevertheless, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. This medication should only be used during pregnancy if clearly necessary, as a human response to treatments is not usually predicted by studies on animal reproduction. It has been demonstrated that Trupan 40 mg is excreted in human milk. So, a choice should be made on whether to stop nursing or stop taking the medication, considering the benefit of the medication to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Patients should be advised not to split, chew, or crush the Trupan 40 mg pill. The use of Trupan 40 mg for an extended period of time may cause malabsorption of cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) or raise the risk of problems linked to osteoporosis.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Trupan 40 mg</strong></p><p>There are no recognized signs of human overdose. Due to its strong protein binding, Trupan 40 mg is not easily dialyzable. There is no specific therapy that is advised, only symptomatic and supportive management.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep dry and away from heat and light. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p><strong>Direction for use of IV injection:</strong> Trupan 40 mg lyophilized powder with 0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection must only be administered intravenously and under no other circumstances. A gradual intravenous dose of Trupan 40 mg should be administered. The powder-containing vial is mixed with 10 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride injection to create the IV injection solution. Upon reconstitution, the injection should be administered gradually over a minimum of 2 to 5 minutes. Use just freshly made solutions. The reconstituted solution can be kept for up to 4 hours at room temperature (up to 30°C).</p><p><strong>How to administer an IV infusion:</strong></p><p>An intravenous infusion of Trupan 40 mg should be administered over roughly 15 minutes. An IV infusion of Trupan 40 mg needs to be reconstituted with 10 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride injection before being further diluted (admixed) to a final volume of 100 ml using 0.9% sodium chloride injection, 5% dextrose, or lactated Ringer\'s injection. Prior to further dilution, the reconstituted solution may be kept at room temperature (up to 30°C) for a maximum of 4 hours. The admixed solution must be used within 24 hours of the initial reconstitution and may be kept at room temperature (up to 30°C).</p>', 'active', '69_1742677825.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:10:25', '2025-05-04 22:36:20'),
(4212, 'Caldil-Plus', 'Elemental Calcium + Vitamin D3', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Caldil-Plus</strong></p><p>This combination is used for the treatment of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, rickets, tetany, and parathyroid disease. Calcium supplements are often used to ensure adequate dietary intake in conditions such as pregnancy &amp; lactation, osteogenesis, and tooth formation (adjunct with definite treatment), and therapy with anti-seizure medications. It is also used as a routine supplement and phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Caldil-Plus is the preparation of Calcium Carbonate and Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol). Calcium is necessary for many normal functions of our body, especially bone formation and maintenance. Vitamin D3 helps with the absorption &amp; reabsorption of Calcium. Vitamin D3 also stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies showed that Calcium and Vitamin D3 together help in bone growth, and in the prevention of osteoporosis &amp; bone fractures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Caldil-Plus</strong></p><ul><li>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 200 IU Tablet: 2 tablets daily or 1 tablet twice daily. Caldil-Plus is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li><li>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 400 IU Tablet: 1 tablet twice daily. It is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Caldil-Plus</strong></p><p>It has possible interactions with calcium, aluminium or magnesium-containing antacids &amp; other calcium supplements, calcitriol &amp; other vitamin D3 supplements; digoxin, tetracycline, doxycycline, minocycline, or oxytetracycline.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Caldil-Plus is contraindicated in the case of hypercalcaemia, hyperthyroidism, renal calculi &amp; nephrolithiasis, and Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Caldil-Plus</strong></p><p>Caldil-Plus is generally well tolerated. If symptoms like nausea, vomiting, stomach cramps, dry mouth, increased thirst, or increased urination occur while taking it, they should be reported to a physician. Constipation may also occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This combination should be used as directed by a physician during pregnancy or while breast-feeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>If there is any pre-existing heart disease or kidney disease, precautions should be taken.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Caldil-Plus</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdosage may include nausea and vomiting, severe drowsiness, dry mouth, loss of appetite, metallic taste, stomach cramps, diarrhea, headache, and constipation.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '715_1742678276.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:17:56', '2025-05-03 01:10:41'),
(4213, 'Telfast', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 13.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '180 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Telfast 180 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Allergic Rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In adults and children aged 12 years and older, Telfast 180 mg is recommended for alleviating symptoms of seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis. These symptoms include sneezing, rhinorrhea, lacrimation, itchy and red eyes, and itchy nose, palate, and throat.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Telfast enhances both work/activity productivity and health-related quality of life.</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In adults and children 12 years of age and older, Telfast 180 mg is also recommended for treating symptoms of chronic idiopathic urticaria. It significantly decreases the number of wheals and the associated pruritus, improving quality of life and productivity.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class:</h3><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology:</h3><ul><li><strong>Mode of Action</strong>: Telfast (fexofenadine) is a selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist, preventing the activation of the H1 receptor and thus reducing allergy symptoms like pruritus, rhinorrhea, and watery eyes. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier, meaning it does not typically affect the central nervous system.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration:</h3><ul><li><strong>For Seasonal Allergic Rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children 12 years and older</strong>: 60 mg twice daily or 120 mg once daily or 180 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>In case of impaired renal function</strong>: 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6 to 11 years)</strong>: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>In case of impaired renal function</strong>: 30 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (2 to 11 years)</strong>: Suspension (30 mg or 5 ml) twice daily or 30 mg once daily in case of renal impairment</li><li><strong>For Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children 12 years and older</strong>: 60 mg twice daily or 120 mg once daily or 180 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>In case of impaired renal function</strong>: 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6 to 11 years)</strong>: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6 months to less than 2 years)</strong>: 15 mg or 2.5 ml (1/2 tsp) twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (2 to 11 years)</strong>: Suspension (30 mg or 5 ml) twice daily</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Telfast 180 mg:</h3><ul><li><strong>Erythromycin or Ketoconazole</strong>: Co-administration with these drugs may increase fexofenadine levels in plasma (2-3 fold).</li><li><strong>Antacids (containing aluminum or magnesium hydroxide)</strong>: Reduces bioavailability. It\'s advised to wait two hours before or after taking an antacid.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications:</h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Telfast 180 mg or any of its components.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects:</h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Headache, fatigue, dizziness, nausea, and general discomfort.</li><li><strong>Unusual</strong>: Fatigue</li><li><strong>Post-marketing side effects</strong>: Hypersensitivity reactions like angioedema, tightness in chest, dyspnea, systemic anaphylaxis, and mental health issues like anxiety and nightmares.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C by the US FDA. Use during pregnancy should be avoided unless benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Fexofenadine passes into breast milk; therefore, it is not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</h3><ul><li>Use cautiously in elderly individuals or those with renal or hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular caution</strong>: Patients with a history of cardiovascular issues should be informed of possible tachycardia and palpitations.</li><li>Fexofenadine does not appear to impair the ability to drive or operate machinery, but individual responses should be monitored.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects:</h3><ul><li>Symptoms include dry mouth, sleepiness, fatigue, and dizziness.</li><li>Telfast has been well tolerated even at higher doses. Standard procedures should be followed to remove unabsorbed medication.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions:</h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from heat and light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Pediatric Use:</h3><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Dose adjustment may be necessary.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No significant effect on pharmacokinetics.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Dosing may need adjustment due to enhanced bioavailability in those over 65.</li></ul>', 'active', '1578_1742678359.webp', 45, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:19:19', '2025-05-04 22:18:59'),
(4214, 'SMC PLUS ( Lemon Flavored)', 'Lemon Flavored Electrolyte Drink', 40.00, 1, 'x 250 ml bottle', '250 ml bottle', 'Saline', '0 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '780_1742678513.png', 12, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:21:54', '2025-05-04 21:12:52'),
(4215, 'Fenadin', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 4.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '60 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Fenadin 60 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Indications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Allergic Rhinitis</strong>:</li><li>In adults and children aged 12 years and older, Fenadin 60 mg is recommended for the relief of symptoms of seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis, including sneezing, rhinorrhea, lacrimation, itchy/red eyes, and itchy nose, palate, and throat. Fenadin can improve both work/activity productivity and quality of life.</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>:</li><li>For the treatment of chronic idiopathic urticaria in adults and children 12 years and older, Fenadin is effective in reducing wheals and pruritus. It also enhances productivity and quality of life.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Fenadin (fexofenadine) is a selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist. After oral administration, it is quickly absorbed and reaches peak plasma concentration within 2 to 3 hours. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Seasonal Allergic Rhinitis</strong></li><li><strong>Adults and children 12 years and older</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">60 mg twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">120 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">180 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 11 years</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">30 mg twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">60 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Children 2 to 11 years</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Suspension: 30 mg (5 ml) twice daily</li><li><strong>Impaired Renal Function</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">60 mg once daily (adults)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">30 mg once daily (children)</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong></li><li><strong>Adults and children 12 years and older</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">60 mg twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">120 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">180 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 11 years</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">30 mg twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">60 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Children 6 months to less than 2 years</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Suspension: 15 mg (2.5 ml) twice daily</li><li><strong>Impaired Renal Function</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">60 mg once daily (adults)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">30 mg once daily (children)</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Co-administration with <strong>erythromycin</strong> or <strong>ketoconazole</strong> may increase plasma levels of fexofenadine by 2 to 3 times but does not affect the QT interval.</li><li><strong>Antacids</strong> (containing aluminum or magnesium hydroxide) may reduce the bioavailability of Fenadin if taken within 15 minutes of dosing. It is advised to wait 2 hours between doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Fenadin 60 mg or any of its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Headache, fatigue, dizziness, nausea.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Exhaustion.</li><li><strong>Serious side effects (rare)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypersensitivity reactions (e.g., angioedema, chest tightness, dyspnea, systemic anaphylaxis), anxiety, nightmares, insomnia, tachycardia, palpitations, rash, pruritus.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Fenadin is classified as <strong>Category C</strong> by the FDA. It should only be used during pregnancy if the benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Fenadin may pass into breast milk. It is not recommended for nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Care should be taken when administering Fenadin to elderly patients or those with renal or hepatic impairment.</li><li>Patients with a history of cardiovascular disease should be aware of the potential for tachycardia and palpitations.</li><li>Fenadin is unlikely to affect the ability to drive or operate machinery, but individual responses should be monitored.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose may include dry mouth, sleepiness, fatigue, and dizziness.</li><li>No significant adverse responses have been reported with high doses (up to 800 mg). Treatment should involve symptomatic care and psychological support.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from heat and light. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Fenadin binds to the H1 histamine receptor, stabilizing it in an inactive state and preventing activation by histamine. This action helps alleviate allergic symptoms like pruritus, rhinorrhea, and watery eyes. It does not have significant effects on other receptors (dopamine, serotonin, cholinergic, adrenergic).</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal and Hepatic Impairment</strong>: In patients with renal impairment, the dose should be reduced (60 mg once daily) due to altered pharmacokinetics.</li><li><strong>Patients over 65</strong>: Pharmacokinetics may be altered in this group, with increased bioavailability.</li></ul>', 'active', '425_1742678613.webp', 6, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:23:33', '2025-05-02 17:45:31'),
(4216, 'Closalic 30 gm', 'Clobetasol Propionate + Salicylic Acid', 150.00, 1, 'x 30 gm tube', '30 gm tube', 'Ointment', '0.05%+3%', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Closalic 30 gm 0.05%+3%</strong></p><p>This ointment is indicated for the relief of the inflammatory manifestations of hyperkeratotic and dry corticosteroid-responsive dermatoses such as:</p><ul><li>Psoriasis</li><li>Chronic atopic dermatitis</li><li>Neurodermatitis (Lichen Simplex Chronicus)</li><li>Lichen planus</li><li>Eczema (including nummular eczema, hand eczema, eczematous dermatitis)</li><li>Seborrheic dermatitis of the scalp</li><li>Ichthyosis vulgaris and other ichthyotic conditions</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong>: A high potency corticosteroid that decreases inflammation by stabilizing leukocyte lysosomal membranes, preventing the release of destructive acid hydrolases, inhibiting macrophage accumulation, reducing leukocyte adhesion, decreasing capillary permeability and edema formation, antagonizing histamine activity, and reducing fibroblast proliferation and collagen deposition, ultimately preventing scar tissue formation.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid</strong>: Has potent keratolytic action and slight antiseptic properties. It softens and destroys the stratum corneum by increasing endogenous hydration, causing the horny layer of the skin to swell, soften, and desquamate. At high concentrations, it has a caustic effect. It also possesses weak antifungal and antibacterial activity.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Closalic 30 gm 0.05%+3%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: Apply a thin layer of ointment to the affected skin areas twice daily and rub in gently and completely. For some patients, adequate maintenance therapy may be achieved with less frequent application. Discontinue therapy when control has been achieved. If no improvement is seen within 2 weeks, reassessment of the diagnosis may be necessary. Treatment beyond 2 consecutive weeks should not exceed 50 gm/week due to the potential suppression of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Not recommended for pediatric patients under 12 years of age.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Closalic 30 gm 0.05%+3%</strong></p><ul><li>For external use only.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Closalic 30 gm 0.05%+3%</strong></p><ul><li>No known interactions with Clobetasol Propionate ointment and cream.</li><li>Salicylic Acid may increase the absorption of other topically applied medicines. Therefore, avoid concomitant use with other topical medicines on the same skin area.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong>: Contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to Clobetasol Propionate. Not recommended for use in primary infected bacterial or fungal skin lesions (unless anti-infective agents are also used), primary cutaneous viral infections (e.g., herpes simplex, varicella), and tuberculous skin lesions. Also contraindicated in dermatoses in children under one year of age, including dermatitis and diaper eruptions.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid</strong>: Contraindicated in patients displaying hypersensitivity to salicylates or any other ingredients in the preparation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Closalic 30 gm 0.05%+3%</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged use or large amounts can lead to systemic absorption and hypercortisolism, especially in infants and children or with occlusive dressings.</li><li>Possible local atrophy with prolonged treatment.</li><li>In rare instances, corticosteroid treatment of psoriasis can provoke the pustular form of the disease.</li><li>Hypersensitivity reactions, drying, and irritation may occur due to Salicylic Acid.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Corticosteroids</strong>: Topical administration in pregnant animals has caused fetal development abnormalities. The relevance to humans is unknown. The use of Clobetasol Propionate during pregnancy and lactation should only be considered if the benefit to the mother outweighs any potential risk to the fetus. It should not be used extensively or in large amounts during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid</strong>: Its safety during pregnancy and lactation is not established. Use with caution.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Not for prolonged use or application on large areas.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired peripheral circulation or diabetes.</li><li>Avoid contact with broken skin, eyes, mucous membranes, and the anogenital region.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Closalic 30 gm 0.05%+3%</strong></p><ul><li>Acute overdose is unlikely, but chronic misuse can result in hypercortisolism, which should be managed under medical supervision.</li><li>Salicylate poisoning symptoms (e.g., tinnitus, dizziness, deafness) may occur after prolonged or large-area use. Salicylate poisoning is more likely if large quantities are ingested or applied.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong>: A corticosteroid that reduces inflammation through several pathways, including inhibiting leukocyte activity and reducing capillary permeability.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid</strong>: A keratolytic agent that helps to soften and exfoliate the skin, promoting the shedding of the stratum corneum.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Use during pregnancy and lactation should only be considered if the benefits outweigh any risks to the fetus or infant.</li></ul>', 'active', '94_1742678879.jpg', 27, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:27:59', '2025-05-03 01:25:14');
