(4217, 'Bextram Kids', 'Multivitamin + Cod Liver Oil', 90.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', 'N/A', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1074_1742678979.webp', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:29:39', '2025-04-22 19:28:38'),
(4218, 'Reset ER', 'Paracetamol', 2.00, 15, 'tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '665 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Reset ER 665 mg</strong></p><p>Reset ER 665 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Fever, common cold, and influenza</li><li>Headache, toothache, earache, body ache, myalgia, neuralgia, dysmenorrhoea, sprains</li><li>Colic pain, back pain, post-operative pain, postpartum pain</li><li>Inflammatory pain and post-vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic &amp; osteoarthritic pain and stiffness of joints</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Reset ER 665 mg (Acetaminophen) has analgesic and antipyretic properties, with weak anti-inflammatory activity. It is thought to act primarily in the CNS, increasing the pain threshold by inhibiting the COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3 enzymes involved in prostaglandin synthesis. Reset ER 665 mg is a para-aminophenol derivative, and it is one of the most widely used, safest, and fast-acting analgesics. It is well tolerated and free from many of the side effects of aspirin.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 1-2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours, up to a maximum of 4 gm (8 tablets) daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6-12 years)</strong>: ½ to 1 tablet 3 to 4 times daily. For long-term treatment, the dose should not exceed 2.6 gm/day.</li><li><strong>Extended Release Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years</strong>: Two tablets every 6 to 8 hours (maximum of 6 tablets in any 24 hours). Do not crush the tablet.</li><li><strong>Syrup/Suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children under 3 months</strong>: 10 mg/kg body weight (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced) 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 3 months to below 1 year</strong>: ½ to 1 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 1-5 years</strong>: 1-2 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6-12 years</strong>: 2-4 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults</strong>: 4-8 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Suppository</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 3-12 months</strong>: 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 1-5 years</strong>: 125-250 mg 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6-12 years</strong>: 250-500 mg 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; children over 12 years</strong>: 0.5-1 gm 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Paediatric Drop</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children Up to 3 months</strong>: 0.5 ml (40 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>4 to 11 months</strong>: 1.0 ml (80 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1 to 2 years</strong>: 1.5 ml (120 mg). Do not exceed more than 5 doses daily for a maximum of 5 days.</li><li><strong>Reset ER 665 mg tablet with Actizorb Technology</strong>:</li><li>Dissolves up to five times faster than standard Reset ER 665 mg tablets. This is a fast-acting, safe analgesic with marked antipyretic properties. It is especially suitable for patients who cannot tolerate aspirin or other analgesics.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children (12 years and older)</strong>: 1 to 2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not exceed 8 tablets in 24 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (7 to 11 years)</strong>: ½-1 tablet every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not exceed 4 tablets in 24 hours. Not recommended for children under 7 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Alcohol</strong>: Increases the hepatotoxicity of Reset ER 665 mg overdose.</li><li><strong>Barbiturates, tricyclic antidepressants</strong>: May diminish the ability to metabolize large doses of Reset ER 665 mg.</li><li><strong>Anticonvulsants &amp; Oral steroid contraceptives</strong>: May induce liver enzymes, affecting the therapeutic levels of Reset ER 665 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Reset ER 665 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Usually mild but may include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Hematological reactions</strong>: Thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, pancytopenia, neutropenia, and agranulocytosis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Occasional reactions</strong>: Pancreatitis, skin rashes, and other allergic reactions</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy category B (USFDA): Should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed.</li><li>Excreted in breast milk; maternal use in therapeutic doses does not appear to present a risk to the nursing infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired kidney or liver function.</li><li>Caution is advised for patients taking other drugs affecting the liver.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>First 24 hours</strong>: Pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and abdominal pain.</li><li><strong>12-48 hours post-ingestion</strong>: Liver damage becomes apparent, and abnormalities of glucose metabolism and metabolic acidosis may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Reset ER 665 mg has analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity. It acts in the CNS, increasing the pain threshold by inhibiting COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3 enzymes involved in prostaglandin synthesis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy category B (USFDA). Should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li></ul>', 'active', '215_1742679105.png', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:31:45', '2025-05-04 15:14:53'),
(4219, 'Riz', 'Cetirizine Hydrochloride', 3.01, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Riz 10 mg</strong></p><p>Riz 10 mg is indicated for the relief of symptoms associated with seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis. It is also indicated for the treatment of the uncomplicated skin manifestations of chronic idiopathic urticaria and allergen-induced asthma.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Sedating Anti-histamine</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Riz 10 mg is a potent H1 receptor antagonist without any significant anticholinergic and antiserotonic effects. At pharmacologically active dose levels, it has almost no drowsiness effect and does not cause behavioral changes. It inhibits the histamine-mediated early phase of the allergic reaction and also reduces the migration of inflammatory cells and the release of mediators associated with the late phase of the allergic reaction.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Riz 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Children 6 years and older</strong>: 1 tablet or 2 teaspoonfuls daily (or 1 teaspoonful twice daily).</li><li><strong>Children 2-6 years</strong>: 1 teaspoonful once daily or 1/2 teaspoonful twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6 months to 2 years</strong>: 1/2 teaspoonful once daily.</li><li>The dose in children 12-23 months of age can be increased to a maximum dose of 1/2 teaspoonful every 12 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Riz 10 mg</strong></p><p>No clinically significant drug interactions have been found with Theophylline, Azithromycin, Pseudoephedrine, Ketoconazole, or Erythromycin and with other drugs.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Cetirizine or hydroxyzine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Riz 10 mg</strong></p><p>The most common side effect that occurred more frequently with Cetirizine is somnolence.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The US FDA Pregnancy Category of Cetirizine Hydrochloride is B. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Cetirizine Hydrochloride has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Cetirizine Hydrochloride is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised when driving a car or operating heavy machinery.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1665_1742679240.jpg', 43, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:34:00', '2025-05-04 17:03:07'),
(4220, 'Calboplex', 'Calcium + Vitamin D3 + Multimineral', 6.00, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '0 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Calboplex</strong></p><p>This preparation is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Prevention and treatment of osteoporosis</li><li>To maintain strong bone growth</li><li>For proper functioning of heart, muscle, and nerves</li><li>As a nutritional supplement</li><li>For bone development and regeneration</li><li>Pregnancy &amp; lactation</li><li>Deficiency state of Calcium, Vitamin D3, Magnesium, Zinc, Copper, Manganese, and Boron</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate BP equivalent to 600 mg elemental Calcium</li><li>Vitamin D3 BP (as Cholecalciferol) 200 IU</li><li>Magnesium Oxide BP equivalent to 40 mg Magnesium</li><li>Manganese Sulfate Monohydrate BP equivalent to 1.8 mg Manganese</li><li>Cupric Oxide equivalent to 1 mg Copper</li><li>Boron Citrate equivalent to 250 mcg Boron</li><li>Zinc Oxide BP equivalent to 7.5 mg Zinc</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Nutrition is key to preventing osteoporosis and other bone-related diseases. Calcium, Magnesium, and Vitamin D3 are vital macronutrients for bones. Without Vitamin D3, little Calcium is absorbed. Magnesium also increases bone strength and rigidity. Recent studies show that micronutrients like Copper, Manganese, Zinc, and Boron are important for bone health. Deficiencies of these micronutrients are observed in patients with osteoporosis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li>2 tablets per day, preferably 1 tablet in the morning and 1 tablet in the evening.</li><li>It is best taken with or just after main meals with a full glass of water.</li><li>As directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Calboplex</strong></p><ul><li>Risk of hypercalcemia in patients taking thiazide diuretics (reduces urinary calcium excretion).</li><li>Hypocalcemia should be avoided in digitalized patients.</li><li>Foods containing oxalic acid, phosphate, or phytinic acid may reduce calcium absorption.</li><li>Concomitant treatment with phenytoin or barbiturates can decrease Vitamin D3 effectiveness.</li><li>Glucocorticoids may also decrease the effect of Vitamin D3.</li><li>Calcium may reduce the absorption of thyroxin, bisphosphonates, sodium fluoride, quinolone, or tetracycline antibiotics, and iron. It is advisable to allow a minimum period of four hours before taking calcium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Absolute contraindications:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypercalcemia resulting from myeloma, bone metastasis, or malignant bone disease</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sarcoidosis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Primary hyperparathyroidism and Vitamin D3 overdosage</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe renal failure</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypersensitivity to any tablet ingredients</li><li>Relative contraindications:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Osteoporosis due to prolonged immobilization</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Renal stones</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe hypercalciuria</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Calboplex</strong></p><ul><li>Rare mild gastrointestinal disturbances: constipation, flatulence, nausea, gastric pain, diarrhea.</li><li>Skin rash may occur with Vitamin D3 supplements.</li><li>Hypercalciuria and in rare cases, hypercalcemia, with long-term high-dose treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>This combination should be used as directed by a physician during pregnancy or lactation.</li><li>Increased calcium and Vitamin D3 requirements during pregnancy and lactation should be addressed with physician guidance.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Careful supervision is required for patients with mild to moderate renal failure or mild hypercalciuria.</li><li>Regular checks of plasma calcium levels and urinary calcium excretion are recommended.</li><li>In patients with a history of renal stones, monitor urinary calcium to exclude hypercalciuria.</li><li>Long-term treatment requires monitoring of serum and urinary calcium levels, and kidney function.</li><li>If urinary calcium exceeds 7.5 mmol/24 hours, reduce or stop treatment temporarily.</li><li>Take into account calcium and Vitamin D3 supplements from other sources.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Calboplex</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdosage may include:</p><ul><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Severe drowsiness</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Loss of appetite</li><li>Metallic taste</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Headache</li><li>Constipation</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Nutrition is crucial for preventing osteoporosis and bone diseases. Calcium, Magnesium, and Vitamin D3 are essential for bones. Without Vitamin D3, little Calcium is absorbed, while Magnesium supports bone strength. Micronutrients such as Copper, Manganese, Zinc, and Boron also play key roles in bone health.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>During pregnancy and lactation, treatment should always be under physician guidance. Calcium and Vitamin D3 requirements increase during these times. If both calcium and iron supplements are necessary, take them at different times. Vitamin D3 overdose in animals has shown teratogenic effects, and long-term hypercalcemia in humans can lead to physical and mental retardation in newborns. Vitamin D3 and its metabolites pass into breast milk.</p>', 'active', '709_1742679385.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:36:25', '2025-05-03 01:06:39'),
(4221, 'Ceevit Forte', 'Vitamin C [Ascorbic acid]', 18.40, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '1000 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Ceevit Forte 1000 mg (Ascorbic Acid / Vitamin C)</h3><ul><li><strong>Prevents &amp; treats scurvy</strong></li><li><strong>Helps in healing wounds &amp; broken bones</strong></li><li><strong>Supports the formation of collagen in connective tissues</strong></li><li><strong>Aids in iron absorption &amp; helps treat anemia</strong></li><li><strong>Contributes to the production of hemoglobin &amp; red blood cells in the bone marrow</strong></li><li><strong>Helps prevent many types of viral and bacterial infections</strong></li><li><strong>Potentiates the immune system</strong></li><li><strong>Aids in the treatment &amp; prevention of the common cold</strong></li><li><strong>Promotes healthy capillaries, gums &amp; teeth</strong></li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Vitamin-C Preparations</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li>Vitamin C is a water-soluble vitamin, absorbed readily from the gastrointestinal tract and widely distributed in body tissues.</li><li>It plays a role in biological oxidation-reduction reactions used in cellular respiration.</li><li>It is essential for collagen synthesis and intracellular material.</li><li>Vitamin C deficiency leads to scurvy, which is characterized by bleeding, anemia, slow wound healing, cartilage, and bone lesions.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><h4>Oral Administration</h4><ul><li><strong>Prevention of Scurvy</strong>: 1 tablet daily</li><li><strong>Treatment of Scurvy</strong>: 1-2 tablets daily (dose may be increased depending on the severity)</li><li><strong>Reduction of stroke risk in the elderly</strong>: 1-2 tablets daily</li><li><strong>Other cases</strong>: 1 tablet daily or as directed by a physician</li><li><strong>Maximum safe dose</strong>: 2000 mg daily in divided doses</li></ul><h4>Parenteral Administration</h4><ul><li><strong>Preferred route</strong>: Intramuscular (IM)</li><li><strong>Intravenous (IV)</strong>: Dilution into a large volume parenteral (Normal Saline, Water for Injection, or Glucose) is recommended to minimize adverse reactions.</li><li><strong>Typical dose for adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Protective dose: 70-150 mg daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In scurvy: 300 mg to 1 g daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">To enhance wound healing: 300-500 mg daily for a week or ten days pre/post-surgery</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For burns: 1-2 g daily, depending on the severity</li></ul><h3>Interaction</h3><ul><li><strong>Potentially Hazardous Interactions</strong>:</li><li>Incompatible in solution with certain medications such as aminophylline, bleomycin, erythromycin, etc.</li><li><strong>Useful Interactions</strong>:</li><li>Enhances iron absorption and increases the apparent half-life of paracetamol.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>General</strong>: Rare, but megadoses may cause:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diarrhea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Abdominal bloating</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Iron over-absorption (harmful in certain anemia conditions)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hyperoxaluria, hyperuricosuria, hemolysis in glucose-6 phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency</li><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Excessive intake (5 gm/day) may cause fetal abortion.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Safe during lactation in normal doses.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>High doses (over 1000 mg/day) during pregnancy can result in scurvy in neonates.</li><li>Contraindicated in patients with hyperoxaluria.</li><li>Vitamin C can cause false positives in glucose, uric acid, creatine, and occult blood tests.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place below 30˚C.</li></ul><h3>Drug Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Vitamin-C Preparations</strong></li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>As an essential nutrient, Vitamin C supports tissue growth, adrenal gland function, immunity, healthy gums, and skin.</li><li>It has antioxidant properties, protecting against pollution, infection, and cellular damage.</li><li>It is essential for wound healing, drug metabolism, and the metabolism of cholesterol &amp; folic acid.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further details on any of the sections!</p>', 'active', '1544_1742679644.jpg', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:40:44', '2025-05-03 01:16:15'),
(4222, 'Ticamet', 'Salmeterol + Fluticasone Propionate', 695.00, 1, 'x 120 metered doses (refill)', '120 metered doses (refill)', 'Inhaler', '(25 mcg+250 mcg)/puff', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ticamet 25 mcg + 250 mcg</strong></p><p>This combination medication is indicated for the regular treatment of asthma where the use of a combination product (long-acting β2-agonist and inhaled corticosteroid) is appropriate. It is suitable for:</p><ul><li>Patients who are not adequately controlled with inhaled corticosteroids and \'as needed\' inhaled short-acting β2-agonists.</li><li>Patients already adequately controlled on both inhaled corticosteroids and long-acting β2-agonists.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants</li><li>Respiratory corticosteroids</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate</strong>: A selective, long-acting beta-2 agonist used to treat asthma and other forms of diffuse airways obstruction.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: A corticosteroid with potent glucocorticoid activity, which exerts a topical effect on the lungs and reduces asthma symptoms and exacerbations without systemic effects when used at recommended doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Inhalation Aerosol</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily, or 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 125 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily, or 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 250 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4-12 years)</strong>: 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Powder in Capsule (For Asthma)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg or Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 250 µg twice daily, approximately 12 hours apart. The maximum recommended dose is Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 500 µg twice daily.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Powder in Maxhaler (For Asthma)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>: One inhalation of 50/100, 50/250, or 50/500 Maxhaler twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Ticamet 25 mcg + 250 mcg</strong></p><p>To ensure proper inhaler use and effectiveness:</p><ol><li><strong>Shake</strong> the inhaler (at least six times) before each use.</li><li><strong>Breathe out</strong> as fully as possible.</li><li><strong>Place</strong> the inhaler in your mouth and close lips around the mouthpiece.</li><li><strong>Press</strong> down on the inhaler while breathing in deeply.</li><li><strong>Hold breath</strong> for at least 10 seconds, then exhale slowly.</li><li><strong>Wait 1 minute</strong> between puffs if multiple inhalations are prescribed.</li><li><strong>Rinse mouth</strong> after each use to prevent oral irritation.</li></ol><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Non-selective and selective β-blockers should be avoided in asthma patients, unless absolutely necessary.</li><li>Care should be taken when co-administering strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole, ritonavir), as these can increase systemic exposure to Fluticasone Propionate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to any ingredients of the medication.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol</strong>: Common side effects may include tremor, palpitations, headache, arrhythmias, and muscle cramps. Rarely, hypersensitivity reactions like rash or angioedema may occur.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: Hoarseness, oral thrush, cutaneous hypersensitivity, and rarely facial and oropharyngeal edema.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Administration should only be considered if the benefit to the mother outweighs the potential risk to the fetus or child. There is insufficient data on the use of Salmeterol and Fluticasone Propionate during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with active pulmonary tuberculosis and thyrotoxicosis.</li><li>Rinsing the mouth after inhalation is advised to prevent oral side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C. Do not puncture, break, or incinerate the canister. Keep away from sunlight, heat, and children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Inhaled corticosteroids may reduce growth velocity in pediatric patients. The long-term effects, including the impact on final adult height, are unknown.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate</strong>: A long-acting beta-2 agonist that protects against asthma symptoms.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: A potent anti-inflammatory corticosteroid that reduces asthma symptoms and prevents exacerbations.</li></ul>', 'active', '434_1742679819.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:43:39', '2025-05-04 22:24:09'),
(4223, 'Folzin', 'Folic Acid + Zinc Sulfate Monohydrate', 1.90, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Folzin 5 mg + 20 mg</strong></p><p>This tablet is indicated for the treatment and prophylaxis of folic acid and zinc deficiencies.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Zinc</strong> is an essential element in human health with a wide range of roles. It works as a coenzyme for more than 200 enzymes. These enzymes are involved in nucleic acid and protein metabolism and the production of energy. Zinc is vital for growth and development. Zinc deficiency can result in growth retardation, immune dysfunction, increased incidence of infections, anorexia, weight loss, neural tube defects of the fetus, increased risk for abortion, and mental lethargy.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong> is a member of the B-vitamin group. It participates in several key biological processes, including the synthesis of DNA, RNA, and proteins. It is necessary for DNA replication and is involved in the regulation of gene expression. Folic acid deficiency in pregnant women results in an increased incidence of neural tube defects, including meningomyelocele (e.g., spina bifida), anencephaly, meningocele, and craniorachischisis in their neonates. Folic acid deficiency leads to megaloblastic anemia. Other symptoms and signs of folic acid deficiency include weakness, fatigue, irritability, headache, difficulty concentrating, and palpitations.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Folzin 5 mg + 20 mg</strong></p><p>One tablet daily or as recommended by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Folzin 5 mg + 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>A large amount of Calcium decreases the absorption of Zinc.</li><li>In case of Folic Acid, no drug interactions have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Zinc is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to Zinc.</li><li>Folic Acid is contraindicated in untreated cobalamine deficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Folzin 5 mg + 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Folic acid and zinc are well tolerated in the recommended dose. Occasionally, gastrointestinal disturbances like abdominal pain, dyspepsia, nausea, vomiting, fever, and respiratory distress may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Recommended during pregnancy &amp; lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Folzin 5 mg + 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Zinc sulfate is corrosive in overdose. Symptoms include corrosion and inflammation of the mucous membrane of the mouth and stomach.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at a temperature below 30°C, protect from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>This tablet is a special preparation of folic acid and zinc sulfate monohydrate. Zinc is an essential trace element required for human nutrition and involved in a number of body enzyme systems. Severe zinc deficiency causes skin lesions, alopecia, diarrhea, increased susceptibility to infections, cognitive impairment, etc.</li><li>Folic acid is a member of the B-Vitamins group. It is reduced in the body to tetrahydrofolate, which is the coenzyme for various metabolic processes, including the synthesis of purine and pyrimidine nucleotides, and hence the synthesis of DNA. It is also involved in some amino acid conversions. Deficiency of folic acid may cause megaloblastic anemia, which develops when the dietary intake is inadequate, as in malnutrition, from malabsorption, from increased utilization (e.g., in pregnancy), or conditions such as hemolytic anemia, and as a result of the administration of folate antagonists.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Recommended during pregnancy &amp; lactation.</li></ul>', 'active', '1190_1742680281.jpg', 56, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:51:21', '2025-05-03 10:51:36'),
(4224, 'Famotack', 'Famotidine', 5.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here’s the text with the headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Famotack 40 mg</strong></p><p>Famotack 40 mg is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Gastric ulcer</li><li>Duodenal ulcer</li><li>Anastomotic ulcer</li><li>Acute stress ulcer</li><li>Reflux esophagitis</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li></ul><p>Famotack 40 mg is also indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Acute gastritis</li><li>Chronic gastritis in the acute exacerbation stage</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>H2 receptor antagonist</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Famotack 40 mg is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist. Famotack 40 mg completely inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors of parietal cells. It inhibits basal, overnight, and pentagastrin-stimulated gastric acid secretion. The H2-receptor antagonist activity of Famotack 40 mg is slowly reversible, since the drug dissociates slowly from the H2-receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Famotack 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer</strong>: 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks</li><li><strong>Benign gastric ulcer</strong>: 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks; Maintenance therapy: 20 mg at night for preventing the recurrence of duodenal ulceration</li><li><strong>Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease</strong>: 20 mg twice daily for 6 to 12 weeks</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: The recommended starting dose is 20 mg every six hours. Dosage should then be adjusted to individual response. Doses up to 160 mg every six hours have been administered to some patients without the development of significant adverse effects.</li><li>Dosage can be administered irrespective of meals. Antacids may be given concomitantly if needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Famotack 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>:</li><li>For gastric ulcer, duodenal ulcer, anastomotic ulcer, upper gastro-intestinal hemorrhage, reflux esophagitis, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</li><li>Usual dose for adults: Famotack 40 mg 20 mg twice daily (after breakfast and after supper or before bedtime) or Famotack 40 mg 40 mg can be administered orally once daily at bedtime.</li><li>For the treatment of acute gastritis, chronic gastritis in acute exacerbation stage:</li><li>Usual dosage for adults is Famotack 40 mg 20 mg orally twice a day (after breakfast and after supper or before bedtime).</li><li>Also, Famotack 40 mg 40 mg can be orally administered once a day (before bedtime), dosage should be adjusted according to age and symptoms.</li><li>Most ulcer patients heal within 4-8 weeks. For maintenance therapy, the recommended oral dose is 20 mg once daily. Or, as directed by the registered physician.</li><li><strong>Powder for Suspension</strong>:</li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</strong>:</li><li>&lt;1 year of age: 0.5 mg/kg/dose of Famotack 40 mg oral suspension up to 8 weeks once daily.</li><li>Age 3 to 11 months: 0.5 mg/kg/dose twice daily up to 8 weeks.</li><li>Age 1 to 2 months: 0.5 mg/kg/dose once daily up to 8 weeks.</li><li>Neonates: 0.5 mg/kg/dose maximum once daily up to 8 weeks.</li><li>Patients 1-16 years of age:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</strong>: 1 mg/kg/day p.o. divided b.i.d. up to 40 mg b.i.d.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Duodenal ulcer</strong>: 0.5 mg/kg/day p.o. at bedtime or divided b.i.d. up to 40 mg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Peptic ulcer</strong>: 0.5 mg/kg/day p.o. at bedtime or divided b.i.d. up to 40 mg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Maintenance therapy</strong>: 40 mg at daily night.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Reflux esophagitis</strong>: 2 mg/kg/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 40 mg 3 times daily.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong>:</li><li>In some hospitalized patients with pathological hypersecretory conditions or intractable ulcers, or in patients who are unable to take oral medication, Famotack 40 mg Injection Premixed or Famotack 40 mg Injection may be administered until oral therapy can be instituted.</li><li>The recommended dosage for Famotack 40 mg Injection Premixed and Famotack 40 mg Injection in adult patients is 20 mg intravenously every 12 hours.</li><li>The doses and regimen for parenteral administration in patients with GERD have not been established.</li><li><strong>Dosage for Pediatric Patients</strong>:</li><li>Pediatric patients suggest that the starting dose in pediatric patients 1-16 years of age is 0.25 mg/kg intravenously (injected over a period of not less than two minutes or as a 15-minute infusion) every 12 hours up to 40 mg/day.</li><li>While published uncontrolled clinical studies suggest the effectiveness of Famotack 40 mg in the treatment of peptic ulcer, data in pediatric patients are insufficient to establish percent response with dose and duration of therapy. Therefore, treatment duration (initially based on adult duration recommendations) and dose should be individualized based on clinical response and/or gastric pH determination and endoscopy.</li><li>Published uncontrolled studies in pediatric patients have demonstrated gastric acid suppression with doses up to 0.5 mg/kg intravenously every 12 hours.</li><li>No pharmacokinetic or pharmacodynamic data are available on pediatric patients under 1 year of age.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Famotack 40 mg</strong></p><p>No clinically important drug interactions have been identified. Famotack 40 mg does not interact with the cytochrome P450-linked drug-metabolizing enzyme system.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Known hypersensitivity to any component of the drug.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Famotack 40 mg</strong></p><p>Eruption, constipation, diarrhea, dry mouth, nausea, vomiting, tachycardia, high blood pressure, headache, drowsiness, or insomnia may rarely occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: There are no adequate, well-controlled studies on Famotack 40 mg in pregnancy, but it is known to cross the placenta and should be prescribed only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether Famotack 40 mg is secreted into human milk. Nursing mothers should either stop nursing or stop taking the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The drug should be used in the minimum required amount depending upon the conditions of the diseases. The drug should be administered carefully with elderly patients, patients with renal failure, and hepatic disorders.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Famotack 40 mg is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist. Famotack 40 mg completely inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors of parietal cells. It inhibits basal, overnight, and pentagastrin-stimulated gastric acid secretion. The H2-receptor antagonist activity of Famotack 40 mg is slowly reversible, since the drug dissociates slowly from the H2-receptor.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Pregnancy category B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. This drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Caution should be exercised when Famotack is administered to a nursing woman.</p>', 'active', '451_1742680421.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:53:41', '2025-05-02 17:26:47'),
(4225, 'Starcal D', 'Elemental Calcium + Vitamin D3', 8.00, 15, 'tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Starcal D</strong></p><p>This combination is used for the treatment of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, rickets, tetany, and in parathyroid disease. Calcium supplements are often used to ensure adequate dietary intake in conditions such as pregnancy &amp; lactation, osteogenesis, and tooth formation (as an adjunct to definitive treatment) and therapy with anti-seizure medications. It is also used as a routine supplement and phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Starcal D is a preparation of Calcium Carbonate and Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol). Calcium is necessary for many normal functions of our body, especially bone formation and maintenance. Vitamin D3 helps with the absorption &amp; reabsorption of Calcium. Vitamin D3 also stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies show that Calcium and Vitamin D3 together help in bone growth and in the prevention of osteoporosis &amp; bone fractures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Starcal D</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 200 IU Tablet</strong>: 2 tablets daily or 1 tablet twice daily. Starcal D is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li><li><strong>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 400 IU Tablet</strong>: 1 tablet twice daily. It is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Starcal D</strong></p><p>Starcal D may have possible interactions with:</p><ul><li>Calcium, aluminium, or magnesium-containing antacids &amp; other calcium supplements</li><li>Calcitriol &amp; other vitamin D3 supplements</li><li>Digoxin, tetracycline, doxycycline, minocycline, or oxytetracycline</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Starcal D is contraindicated in the case of:</p><ul><li>Hypercalcaemia</li><li>Hyperthyroidism</li><li>Renal calculi &amp; nephrolithiasis</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Starcal D</strong></p><p>Starcal D is generally well tolerated. However, if symptoms such as nausea, vomiting, stomach cramps, dry mouth, increased thirst, increased urination are experienced while taking it, these should be reported to a physician. Constipation may also occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This combination should be used as directed by a physician during pregnancy or while breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>If there is any pre-existing heart disease or kidney disease, precautions should be taken.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Starcal D</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdosage may include:</p><ul><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Severe drowsiness</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Loss of appetite</li><li>Metallic taste</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Headache</li><li>Constipation</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '984_1742680601.webp', 11, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:56:41', '2025-05-04 21:23:43'),
(4226, 'Doxiva', 'Doxophylline', 110.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '100 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Doxiva 100 ml</strong></p><p>Doxiva 100 ml is used to treat the following indications:</p><ul><li>Bronchial asthma</li><li>Bronchospasm</li><li>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</li><li>Pulmonary disease with a spastic bronchial component</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Doxiva 100 ml is a novel bronchodilator that structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. It selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. However, unlike Theophylline, Doxiva 100 ml appears to have decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, potentially accounting for its improved safety profile. Additionally, it is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Doxiva 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 400 mg tablet two or three times daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li></ul><blockquote><strong>Children:</strong>12 years of age: 10 ml syrup or 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</blockquote><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><br></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years of age: 6-9 mg/kg body weight two times daily. For instance, if the body weight is 10 kg, 3 ml (60 mg) two times daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Doxiva 100 ml</strong></p><p>Doxiva 100 ml should not be administered together with other xanthine derivatives. Toxic synergism with ephedrine has been documented for xanthines. Like other xanthines, concomitant therapy with troleandomycin, lincomycin, clindamycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, ranitidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccine may decrease the hepatic clearance of xanthines, leading to an increase in blood levels.</p><p>No evidence of a relationship between Doxiva 100 ml serum concentrations and toxic events has been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Doxiva 100 ml is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Acute myocardial infarction</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Lactating women</li><li>Patients who have shown hypersensitivity to its components</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Doxiva 100 ml</strong></p><p>Doxiva 100 ml rarely causes serious side effects; however, possible side effects are similar to taking an excess amount of caffeine. These include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Headache</li><li>Upset stomach</li><li>Heartburn</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Animal reproduction studies indicate that Doxiva 100 ml does not cause fetal harm when administered to pregnant animals or affect reproduction capacity. However, due to limited experience in pregnant humans, xanthines should only be given to pregnant women when clearly necessary. It\'s important to note that Doxiva 100 ml is contraindicated in nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The half-life of xanthine derivatives is influenced by several known variables. It may be prolonged in patients with liver disease, congestive heart failure, and those taking certain other drugs like erythromycin, troleandomycin, lincomycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccines. In these cases, a lower dose of Doxiva 100 ml may be needed. Phenytoin, other anticonvulsants, and smoking may cause an increase in clearance with a shorter mean half-life. In these cases, higher doses of Doxiva 100 ml may be needed.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Doxiva 100 ml</strong></p><p>In case of overdose, severe cardiac arrhythmias and tonic-clonic seizures may occur. These effects may represent the first signs of intoxication. The appearance of side effects may require discontinuation of the treatment, which, if necessary, may be resumed at lower doses after all signs and symptoms of toxicity have subsided. As there is no specific antidote, in case of overdose, symptomatic treatment of cardiovascular collapse should be instituted.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children. Doxiva 100 ml should be used only on the prescription of a specialist physician.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Doxiva 100 ml is a novel bronchodilator. It structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. Doxiva 100 ml selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, thereby relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. However, unlike Theophylline, Doxiva 100 ml appears to have decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, which may account for the better safety profile of the drug. Doxiva 100 ml is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p>', 'active', '1733_1742680725.jpg', 11, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 02:58:45', '2025-05-02 08:45:39');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4227, 'Ometac', 'Omeprazole', 5.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ometac 20 mg</strong></p><p>Ometac 20 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcers</li><li>Gastric and duodenal ulcers caused by NSAIDs</li><li>Prevention in individuals with a history of duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by NSAIDs</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</li><li>Long-term management of acid reflux disease</li><li>Acid-related dyspepsia</li><li>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers</li><li>Prevention of acid aspiration during general anesthesia</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Peptic ulcers caused by <em>Helicobacter pylori</em></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Ometac 20 mg is a proton pump inhibitor (PPI) and a benzimidazole derivative. It works by inhibiting the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in gastric parietal cells, reducing gastric acid secretion. After oral administration, the antisecretory effect starts within about an hour, peaks in 2 hours, and lasts up to 72 hours. Secretory activity gradually resumes over a period of 3 to 5 days after discontinuation.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Ometac 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Benign gastric and duodenal ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks for duodenal ulcer, 8 weeks for gastric ulcer; for severe or recurrent cases, increase to 40 mg daily; maintenance dose for recurrent duodenal ulcer is 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>NSAID-associated ulcers</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continue for an additional 4 weeks if not fully healed. Prophylaxis: 20 mg once daily for patients with a history of NSAID-induced ulcers.</li><li><strong>Gastro-esophageal reflux disease (GERD)</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continue for an additional 4-8 weeks if necessary; for refractory cases, increase to 40 mg once daily for 8 weeks. Maintenance dose: 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Acid-related dyspepsia</strong>: 10-20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: Initial dose: 60 mg once daily; typical range: 20-120 mg daily (divided doses for &gt;80 mg/day).</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication</strong>: 20 mg twice daily with antimicrobial agents such as Amoxicillin (500 mg) and Metronidazole (400 mg), or Clarithromycin (250 mg) and Metronidazole (400 mg), all for one week.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Use (for severe ulcerating reflux esophagitis)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child weight 10-20 kg: 10-20 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child weight &gt;20 kg: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>IV Injection Dosage</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration</strong>: 40 mg IV, slow injection (5 minutes) one hour before surgery.</li><li><strong>Duodenal or gastric ulcer or reflux esophagitis</strong>: 40 mg IV once daily.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: Initial dose: 60 mg IV daily, may need dose adjustments and divided doses if &gt;60 mg daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Ometac 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>: Reconstitute with 10 mL of water for injection. Administer slowly over 2-5 minutes (max flow rate of 4 mL/min). Use freshly prepared solutions within 4 hours.</li><li><strong>IV Infusion</strong>: Reconstitute with 100 mL of 5% dextrose or saline. Use within 6 hours if in dextrose, and within 12 hours if in saline. Do not mix with other medications.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>May increase the half-life of Warfarin, Phenytoin, and Diazepam, requiring dose adjustments.</li><li>No significant interactions with Theophylline, Propranolol, or Antacids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to any of the formulation\'s ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ometac 20 mg</strong></p><p>Generally well tolerated. Mild, temporary side effects such as nausea, stomach cramping, paresthesia, dizziness, and headache may occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Ometac 20 mg is classified as a Category C drug by the FDA. While studies show no harmful effects on pregnancy or the fetus, it is unknown if it passes into breast milk. If necessary, breastfeeding should be discontinued during treatment.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Before using Ometac 20 mg for suspected stomach ulcers, rule out gastric cancer, as the medication may mask symptoms and delay diagnosis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Inhibits gastric acid secretion by blocking the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase enzyme in gastric parietal cells, reducing acid production.</p>', 'active', '1632_1742680913.png', 62, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 03:01:53', '2025-05-04 01:18:29'),
(4228, 'Neptor', 'Esomeprazole', 9.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Neptor 20 mg</strong></p><p>Neptor 20 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Alleviating GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn.</li><li>Treating erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Maintaining the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Eradicating Helicobacter pylori infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease, when used in combination with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Neptor 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. An additional 4-8 weeks may be considered if healing is not achieved.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg once daily (clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms persist, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication (Triple Therapy):</strong> 40 mg once daily for 10 days (with amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily and clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily).</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> 20-80 mg once daily, adjusted individually as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> 40 mg once daily by intravenous injection (minimum 3 minutes) or infusion (10-30 minutes). IV therapy should be discontinued when the patient can resume oral treatment with esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Neptor 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take one capsule or pill one hour before a meal, and swallow it whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension:</strong> Mix the contents of the packet with 15 ml of water. Stir and let thicken for 2-3 minutes, then consume within 30 minutes. If administering via a nasogastric or gastric tube, follow specific instructions for administration.</li><li><strong>IV Administration:</strong> Reconstitute with 5 ml of solvent (WFI) and administer over at least 3 minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Neptor 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Metabolized by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 in the liver.</li><li>Esomeprazole does not significantly interact with medications processed by CYP enzymes such as phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin.</li><li>Co-administration with diazepam may reduce diazepam clearance, leading to higher plasma levels.</li><li>Esomeprazole may hinder absorption of drugs requiring an acidic environment (e.g., ketoconazole, iron salts, digoxin).</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to any of the formulations of esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Neptor 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, and dry mouth.</li><li>Long-term maintenance therapy does not show significant differences in side effects compared to short-term use.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>A positive response to Neptor 20 mg does not rule out the possibility of stomach cancer.</li><li>For patients who have difficulty swallowing capsules, the contents can be mixed with applesauce and taken immediately.</li><li>Antacids can be taken while on Neptor 20 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Neptor 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of acute overdose may include decreased motor activity, changes in breathing frequency, tremors, ataxia, and clonic convulsions.</li><li>No specific countermeasure for overdose; supportive care is recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry area at temperatures not exceeding 30°C. Keep away from moisture and light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor that reduces stomach acid by inhibiting the H+/K+-ATPase enzyme in gastric parietal cells.</li><li>It has superior acid control compared to racemic proton pump inhibitors, as it is the S-isomer of omeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Insufficient data for use during pregnancy. No teratogenic effects in animal studies. It is recommended to stop breastfeeding if esomeprazole is deemed necessary during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>No significant differences in safety and effectiveness between elderly and younger patients have been noted, but older adults may be more sensitive to treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage adjustment is required for mild to moderate hepatic insufficiency. For severe hepatic impairment, the dose should not exceed 20 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>As less than 1% of esomeprazole is excreted unchanged in the urine, no significant differences in pharmacokinetics are expected in patients with renal impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '1706_1742681058.jpg', 57, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 03:04:18', '2025-05-04 00:36:18'),
(4229, 'Saloprim', 'Sulphadoxine plus Pyrimethamine', 3.60, 50, 'Tablet', '50\'s pack', 'Tablet', '500 mg plus 25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Saloprim 500 mg+25 mg</strong></p><p> Treatment of all forms of malaria due to <em>Plasmodium falciparum, Plasmodium vivax, Plasmodium malariae</em>. The drug is also indicated for suppressive or prophylactic management, particularly in areas where resistance to chloroquine is known to exist.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anti-malarial drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sulphadoxine &amp; Pyrimethamine is an antimalarial agent embodying the concept of synergism. Individually, the components of this preparation exert inferior results with higher doses compared to the combination. Moreover, this preparation is effective against strains resistant to other antimalarial agents, and the risk of resistance development is minimal. It is an extremely long-acting drug that attacks different development stages of the parasite and attains effective concentration with a single dose. The protective effect of a single dose lasts for approximately four weeks. This preparation is compatible with other antimalarial drugs and antibiotics. It does not influence the action of antidiabetic agents.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Saloprim 500 mg+25 mg</strong></p><p><em>Curative treatment of malaria with a single dose</em></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 2-3 tablets</li><li><strong>Children under 4 years:</strong> ½ tablet</li><li><strong>4-8 years:</strong> 1 tablet</li><li><strong>9-14 years:</strong> 2 tablets</li></ul><p>In severe cases, Sulphadoxine &amp; Pyrimethamine can be beneficially combined with quinine.</p><p><em>Suppressive or prophylactic management</em></p><p> The dose given below should be taken at one time:</p><p>For <strong>semi-immune subjects</strong> (Dose: once every four weeks):</p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 2-3 tablets</li><li><strong>Children under 4 years:</strong> ½ tablet</li><li><strong>4-8 years:</strong> 1 tablet</li><li><strong>9-14 years:</strong> 2 tablets</li></ul><p>For <strong>non-immune subjects</strong> (Dose: once every two weeks):</p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 2 tablets</li><li><strong>Children under 4 years:</strong> 1½ tablets</li><li><strong>4-8 years:</strong> 1 tablet</li><li><strong>9-14 years:</strong> 1½ tablets</li></ul><p>For <strong>malaria prophylaxis:</strong></p><p> The first dose of Sulphadoxine &amp; Pyrimethamine should be taken one or two days before departure for an endemic area. To ensure protection, administration should be continued in the above dosage during the stay and for four weeks after return or as prescribed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Saloprim 500 mg+25 mg</strong></p><p> Concurrent administration of other preparations containing folate antagonists (e.g., cotrimoxazole, methotrexate, anticonvulsants) can result in increased impairment of folic acid metabolism, leading to hematological side effects.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Prophylactic (repeated) use of Pyrimethamine + Sulphadoxine is contraindicated in patients with severe renal insufficiency, marked liver parenchymal damage, or blood dyscrasias. Treatment must be immediately discontinued upon the appearance of any mucocutaneous signs or symptoms such as pruritus, erythema, rash, orogenital lesions, or pharyngitis.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Saloprim 500 mg+25 mg</strong></p><p> Sulphadoxine &amp; Pyrimethamine at the recommended dose is well tolerated. Main side effects include:</p><ul><li><strong>Skin reactions:</strong> Drug rash, pruritus, and slight hair loss have been observed.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal reactions:</strong> Feeling of fullness, nausea, rarely vomiting, stomatitis.</li><li><strong>Hematological reactions:</strong> In rare cases, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, and megaloblastic anemia have been observed.</li><li><strong>Other reactions:</strong> Fatigue, headache, fever, polyneuritis may occasionally occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Sulphadoxine &amp; Pyrimethamine is contraindicated during pregnancy, in premature and newborn infants during the first weeks of life, and in cases of intolerance to sulfonamides. If pregnancy cannot be excluded, the possible risks should be balanced against the therapeutic effect. Both Pyrimethamine and Sulphadoxine are excreted in maternal breast milk. Nursing mothers should not take this preparation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired renal or hepatic function, folate deficiency, severe allergy, bronchial asthma, or G6PD deficiency.</li><li>Take with plenty of water to prevent crystalluria.</li><li>Avoid excessive sun exposure.</li><li>Discontinue at the first sign of rash.</li><li>Discontinue if signs of folic acid deficiency develop.</li><li>Regular CBC monitoring, LFT, and urine analysis for crystalluria are recommended when administered for more than 3 months.</li><li>Take with food to minimize gastrointestinal effects (e.g., anorexia and vomiting).</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Anti-malarial drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Sulphadoxine &amp; Pyrimethamine is an antimalarial agent that works synergistically. Individually, its components yield inferior results with higher doses compared to their combination. This preparation is effective against strains resistant to other antimalarial agents, with minimal risk of resistance development. It is a long-acting drug that attacks various parasite development stages, achieving effective concentrations with a single dose. A single dose provides protection for approximately four weeks. This preparation is compatible with other antimalarial drugs and antibiotics and does not interfere with antidiabetic agents.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Sulphadoxine &amp; Pyrimethamine is contraindicated during pregnancy, in premature and newborn infants during the first weeks of life, and in cases of intolerance to sulfonamides. If pregnancy cannot be excluded, the potential risks should be weighed against the therapeutic benefits. Both Pyrimethamine and Sulphadoxine are excreted in maternal breast milk. Nursing mothers should avoid this preparation.</p>', 'active', '67_1742685541.jpg', 7, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 04:19:01', '2025-03-23 04:19:01'),
(4230, 'Sefrad DS', 'Cephradine', 130.39, 1, 'Bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '250 mg per 5 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sefrad DS 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Sefrad DS 250 mg/5 ml is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. These include:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper respiratory tract infections</strong>: Sinusitis, pharyngitis, tonsillitis, laryngo-tracheo bronchitis, and otitis media.</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong>: Bronchitis (acute and chronic), lobar pneumonia, and bronchopneumonia.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: Cystitis, urethritis, and pyelonephritis.</li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: Abscess, cellulitis, furunculosis, and impetigo.</li></ul><p>The following microorganisms are susceptible to Sefrad DS 250 mg/5 ml:</p><ul><li><strong>Gram-positive</strong>: Staphylococci (both penicillin-sensitive and resistant strains, including penicillinase-producing species), Streptococci, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em> (beta-haemolytic), <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae.</em></li><li><strong>Gram-negative</strong>: <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Klebsiella spp.</em>, <em>Proteus mirabilis</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Shigella spp.</em>, <em>Salmonella spp.</em> (including <em>Salmonella typhi</em>), <em>Neisseria spp.</em> Many strains of <em>E. coli</em> and <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> that produce the enzyme penicillinase and are ampicillin-resistant are susceptible to Sefrad DS 250 mg/5 ml, which is unaffected by this enzyme.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> First-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sefrad DS 250 mg/5 ml is a cephalosporin antibiotic with broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against both Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. It interferes with bacterial cell wall synthesis by inhibiting the transpeptidase enzyme. As a result, the bacterial cell wall weakens, swells, and ruptures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sefrad DS 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p><em>For oral administration:</em></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:Respiratory tract infections (other than lobar pneumonia) and skin/skin structure infections</strong>: 250 mg every 6 hours or 500 mg every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Lobar pneumonia</strong>: 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 g every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong>: 500 mg every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Severe urinary tract infections, including prostatitis</strong>: 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 g every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (over 9 months of age):</strong>25-50 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 6 or 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Otitis media due to <em>H. influenzae</em></strong>: 75-100 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 6 or 12 hours (not exceeding 4 g/day).</li></ul><p><em>For injectable administration:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 2-4 g daily in four divided doses, up to 8 g daily. For prophylaxis, a single preoperative dose of 1-2 g intramuscularly or intravenously.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> 50-100 mg/kg daily in four divided doses, up to 300 mg/kg daily for severe infections.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sefrad DS 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>The use of nephrotoxic drugs (e.g., aminoglycosides) with Cefradine may increase the risk of kidney damage.</li><li>Diuretics (e.g., furosemide, ethacrynic acid) and probenecid may enhance renal toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Sefrad DS 250 mg/5 ml should not be used in patients with known or suspected hypersensitivity to cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sefrad DS 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Gastrointestinal disturbances, hypersensitivity reactions.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Glossitis, heartburn, dizziness, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, vaginitis, candida overgrowth.</li><li><strong>Skin reactions:</strong> Urticaria, skin rashes, joint pain, edema.</li><li><strong>Blood disorders:</strong> Thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, agranulocytosis, aplastic anemia, hemolytic anemia.</li><li><strong>Hepatobiliary disorders:</strong> Liver enzyme disturbances, transient hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice.</li><li><strong>Renal disorders:</strong> Reversible interstitial nephritis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>US FDA Pregnancy Category B.</strong> No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Use only if clearly needed.</li><li>Excreted in human milk; caution advised in nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged use may cause superinfection due to resistant organisms.</li><li>Use with caution in patients hypersensitive to penicillins due to cross-reactivity.</li><li>May cause false-positive Coombs\' test and urine glucose tests.</li><li>Dosage adjustment necessary in renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sefrad DS 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Symptoms of overdose include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and gastric upset. Treatment is supportive; gastric lavage may be required in cases of large ingestion.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Suspension:</strong> Use within 7 days if kept at room temperature or within 14 days if refrigerated.</li><li><strong>Injection solutions:</strong> Use within 2 hours at room temperature or 12 hours if stored at 5°C.</li><li>Reconstituted solutions may range from light to straw yellow, which does not affect potency.</li><li>Store all medicines out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> First-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Sefrad DS 250 mg/5 ml is a semisynthetic broad-spectrum bactericidal antibiotic, active against Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. It inhibits bacterial cell wall cross-linking, causing bacterial lysis due to external osmotic pressure.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Animal studies have not shown teratogenicity, but safety in pregnancy has not been established. It is excreted in breast milk and should be used with caution in lactating mothers. Patients should be cautious when operating hazardous machinery, including automobiles.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> <strong>Renal Impairment:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CrCl &gt;20 ml/min:</strong> 500 mg every 6 hours.</li><li><strong>CrCl 5-20 ml/min:</strong> 250 mg every 6 hours.</li><li><strong>CrCl &lt;5 ml/min:</strong> 250 mg every 50-70 hours.</li><li><strong>Haemodialysis patients:</strong> 250 mg at the start, 250 mg 6-12 hours after, 250 mg 36-48 hours after, and 250 mg at the next dialysis session if &gt;30 hours have passed.</li></ul><p>Higher doses (up to 1 g four times daily) may be required for severe infections. Treatment should continue for at least 2-3 days after symptoms resolve. Chronic urinary tract infections require prolonged monitoring. Pediatric dosages should not exceed adult dosages. Intramuscular/intravenous therapy may transition to oral treatment at the same dosage level.</p>', 'active', '1229_1742685745.jpg', 45, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 04:22:25', '2025-03-23 04:22:25'),
(4231, 'Metarin XR', 'Metformin Hydrochloride', 7.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Metarin XR 500 mg</strong></p><p> Treatment of type 2 diabetes mellitus, particularly in overweight patients when dietary management and exercise alone does not result in adequate glycaemic control.</p><ul><li><strong>In adults:</strong> Metformin may be used as monotherapy or in combination with other oral antidiabetic agents or with insulin.</li><li><strong>In children from 10 years of age and adolescents:</strong> Metformin may be used as monotherapy or in combination with insulin.</li><li> A reduction of diabetic complications has been shown in overweight type 2 diabetic adult patients treated with metformin as first-line therapy after diet failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Biguanides</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Metformin is a biguanide-type oral antihyperglycemic drug used in the management of type 2 diabetes. It lowers both basal and postprandial plasma glucose. Its mechanism of action is different from those of sulfonylureas, and it does not produce hypoglycemia. Metformin decreases hepatic glucose production, decreases intestinal absorption of glucose, and improves insulin sensitivity by an increase in peripheral glucose uptake and utilization.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Metarin XR 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Metformin immediate release tablet:</strong>Dosage must be individualized based on effectiveness and tolerance while not exceeding the maximum recommended daily doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong> Usual starting dose is 500 mg twice a day or 850 mg once a day, given with meals. Dosage increases should be made in increments of 500 mg weekly or 850 mg every 2 weeks, up to a total of 2000 mg per day, given in divided doses. For patients requiring additional glycemic control, the dose may be increased to a maximum of 2550 mg per day. Doses above 2000 mg may be better tolerated when given three times a day with meals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong> Usual starting dose is 500 mg twice a day, given with meals. Dosage increases should be made in increments of 500 mg weekly up to a maximum of 2000 mg per day, given in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Metformin extended release tablet:</strong>Swallow whole; do not crush, cut, or chew.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong> Usual starting dose is 500 mg once daily with the evening meal. Dose should be increased in increments of 500 mg weekly, up to a maximum of 2000 mg once daily with the evening meal, or alternatively increased to 1000 mg twice daily taken with meals. Patients switching from immediate-release tablets may be given extended-release tablets up to the maximum recommended daily dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong> Not studied in children.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal impaired patients:</strong>Do not use in patients with eGFR below 30 mL/min/1.73 m2. Assess risk/benefit if eGFR falls below 45 mL/min/1.73 m2.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Metarin XR 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Co-administration with carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (Topiramate, Zonisamide) may increase the risk of lactic acidosis.</li><li>Drugs (Ranolazine, Dolutegravir, Cimetidine) that reduce Metformin clearance may increase its accumulation.</li><li>Alcohol can potentiate the effect of Metformin on lactate metabolism.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to the active substance or excipients.</li><li>Acute metabolic acidosis (e.g., lactic acidosis, diabetic ketoacidosis).</li><li>Severe renal failure (GFR &lt;30 mL/min).</li><li>Acute conditions affecting renal function (e.g., dehydration, severe infection, shock).</li><li>Acute or chronic disease causing tissue hypoxia (e.g., cardiac or respiratory failure, recent myocardial infarction).</li><li>Hepatic insufficiency, acute alcohol intoxication, alcoholism.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Metarin XR 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Blood and lymphatic system disorders:</strong> Hemolytic anemia (not known).</li><li><strong>Metabolism and nutrition disorders:</strong> Very rare: Lactic acidosis. Decreased vitamin B12 absorption over long-term use, potentially causing megaloblastic anemia.</li><li><strong>Nervous system disorders:</strong> Common: Taste disturbance. Not known: Encephalopathy.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal disorders:</strong> Very common: Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, and loss of appetite, mostly at therapy initiation.</li><li><strong>Hepatobiliary disorders:</strong> Very rare: Liver function abnormalities or hepatitis resolving upon discontinuation.</li><li><strong>Skin disorders:</strong> Very rare: Reactions such as erythema, pruritus, urticaria.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> No clear association with major birth defects, miscarriage, or adverse maternal or fetal outcomes when used during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Metformin is excreted in breast milk, but no adverse effects have been observed. The decision to discontinue breastfeeding should be based on benefit-risk assessment.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Metformin is substantially excreted by the kidney; accumulation increases the risk of lactic acidosis.</li><li>May lower vitamin B12 levels.</li><li>Increased risk of hypoglycemia when used with insulin or insulin secretagogues.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Metarin XR 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Hypoglycemia has not been observed with doses up to 85 g, but lactic acidosis may occur.</li><li>Lactic acidosis is a medical emergency and requires hospital treatment. Hemodialysis is the most effective method to remove lactate and Metformin.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Biguanides</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Metformin is a biguanide-type oral antihyperglycemic drug that lowers both basal and postprandial plasma glucose. It decreases hepatic glucose production, reduces intestinal glucose absorption, and improves insulin sensitivity by increasing peripheral glucose uptake and utilization.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Uncontrolled diabetes during pregnancy is linked to congenital abnormalities and perinatal mortality. It is recommended to use insulin instead of Metformin to maintain normal blood glucose levels.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong> Metformin is excreted in breast milk; limited data available. Breastfeeding is not recommended during Metformin treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Due to potential renal function decline, dosage should be adjusted based on renal function. Regular renal function assessments are necessary.</li><li><strong>Children (10-12 years):</strong> Particular caution is recommended when prescribing to children in this age group.</li><li><strong>Renal function:</strong> Metformin should not be used in patients with GFR &lt;30 mL/min and should be temporarily discontinued in conditions that impair renal function.</li></ul>', 'active', '628_1742685896.jpg', 17, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 04:24:56', '2025-03-23 04:24:56'),
(4232, 'Aristen', 'Clotrimazole', 80.00, 1, 'Tube', '4 gm tube', 'Ophthalmic Ointment', '1 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Aristen 1%</strong></p><p> The confirmed indications for Aristen 1% include:</p><ul><li>All dermatomycoses due to dermatophytes (e.g. Trichophyton species).</li><li>All dermatomycoses due to yeasts (Candida species).</li><li>Dermatomycoses due to moulds and other fungi.</li><li>Skin diseases showing superinfections with these fungi.</li></ul><p>Examples of these dermatomycoses include interdigital mycoses (e.g. athlete’s foot), paronychias (associated with nail mycoses), mycoses in skin folds, Candida vulvitis, Candida balanitis, Pityriasis versicolor, and erythrasma.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs for subcutaneous and mycoses, Topical Antifungal preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Aristen 1% is a broad-spectrum antifungal. It also exhibits activity against Trichomonas, Staphylococci, Streptococci, and Bacteroides. Aristen 1% kills fungi and yeasts by interfering with their cell membranes. It works by stopping the production of ergosterol, an essential component of fungal cell membranes, resulting in increased membrane permeability and leakage of essential constituents of the fungal cells.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Aristen 1%</strong></p><p> Wash the skin with soap and water and dry thoroughly. Apply a thin layer of the cream or solution 2-3 times daily and gently massage over the affected area as directed by the doctor.</p><p>For athlete’s foot, pay special attention to the spaces between the toes. Best results in athlete\'s foot and ringworm are usually obtained with 4 weeks of use of this product. The cream should be applied two or three times daily for one month or for at least two weeks after the disappearance of all signs of infection. If satisfactory results have not occurred within this time, consultation with a doctor is essential.</p><p><strong>Recommended duration of treatment:</strong></p><ul><li>Dermatomycoses: 3-4 weeks</li><li>Candida vulvitis and Candida balanitis: 1-2 weeks</li><li>Erythrasma and Pityriasis versicolor: approx. 3 weeks</li><li>In fungal infection of the feet, to prevent relapses: treatment should be continued for about 2 weeks beyond the disappearance of all signs of disease.</li></ul><p>Aristen 1% cream is odorless, can be washed off, and does not stain.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to Aristen 1%.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Aristen 1%</strong></p><p> Aristen 1% is generally well tolerated after local application, but a few side effects have been reported infrequently, including erythema, stinging, blistering, peeling, edema, pruritus, urticaria, and general irritation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category B. Aristen 1% is recommended during pregnancy only after first consulting a doctor.</p><p><strong>Lactation:</strong> Since systemic absorption of Aristen 1% following topical application is marginal, there should be no risk for the infant during lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> If local intolerance develops, consider withdrawal of the medicine and institution of appropriate therapy. Aristen 1% solution is not intended for ophthalmic use.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Aristen 1%</strong></p><p> Supportive measures should be taken in case of accidental oral ingestion.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place. Protect from light. Do not freeze.</p><p><strong>Duration of Treatment</strong></p><p> For the treatment to be completely successful, reliable and sufficiently long application of Aristen 1% cream is important. The duration of treatment varies depending on the extent and localization of the disease.</p>', 'active', '501_1742686181.jpg', 29, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 04:29:41', '2025-03-23 04:29:41'),
(4233, 'Docefrez', 'Docetaxel Trihydrate', 12688.00, 1, 'IV Infusion', '80 mg vial iv infusion', 'IV Infusion', '80 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Docefrez 80 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Docetaxel is a microtubule inhibitor indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Breast Cancer (BC):</strong> Single agent for locally advanced or metastatic BC after chemotherapy failure; and with doxorubicin and cyclophosphamide as adjuvant treatment of operable node-positive BC.</li><li><strong>Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC):</strong> Single agent for locally advanced or metastatic NSCLC after platinum therapy failure; and with cisplatin for unresectable, locally advanced, or metastatic untreated NSCLC.</li><li><strong>Castration-Resistant Prostate Cancer (CRPC):</strong> With prednisone in metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer.</li><li><strong>Gastric Adenocarcinoma (GC):</strong> With cisplatin and fluorouracil for untreated, advanced GC, including the gastroesophageal junction.</li><li><strong>Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the Head and Neck (SCCHN):</strong> With cisplatin and fluorouracil for induction treatment of locally advanced SCCHN.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Docetaxel is an antineoplastic agent that disrupts the microtubular network in cells, which is essential for mitotic and interphase cellular functions. It promotes the assembly of tubulin into stable microtubules while inhibiting their disassembly. By binding to free tubulin, docetaxel decreases intracellular tubulin concentration, leading to microtubule stabilization and inhibition of mitosis.</p><p>Docetaxel has demonstrated cytotoxicity in vitro against various murine and human tumor cell lines, including those overexpressing p-glycoprotein, which is associated with multidrug resistance.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Docefrez 80 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Administer in a facility equipped to manage possible complications (e.g., anaphylaxis). Administer intravenously (IV) over 1 hour every 3 weeks. PVC equipment is not recommended. Use only a 21-gauge needle to withdraw docetaxel from the vial.</p><ul><li><strong>BC (locally advanced or metastatic):</strong> 60 mg/m² to 100 mg/m² as a single agent.</li><li><strong>BC (adjuvant):</strong> 75 mg/m², administered 1 hour after doxorubicin (50 mg/m²) and cyclophosphamide (500 mg/m²) every 3 weeks for 6 cycles.</li><li><strong>NSCLC (after platinum therapy failure):</strong> 75 mg/m² as a single agent.</li><li><strong>NSCLC (chemotherapy-naïve):</strong> 75 mg/m² followed by cisplatin 75 mg/m².</li><li><strong>CRPC:</strong> 75 mg/m² with prednisone (5 mg twice daily continuously).</li><li><strong>GC:</strong> 75 mg/m² followed by cisplatin 75 mg/m² (both on day 1) followed by fluorouracil 750 mg/m² per day as a 24-hour IV infusion (days 1-5), starting at the end of cisplatin infusion.</li><li><strong>SCCHN:</strong> 75 mg/m² followed by cisplatin 75 mg/m² IV (day 1), followed by fluorouracil 750 mg/m² per day as a 24-hour IV infusion (days 1–5), starting at the end of the cisplatin infusion (for 4 cycles).</li><li><strong>SCCHN (alternative regimen):</strong> 75 mg/m² followed by cisplatin 100 mg/m² IV (day 1), followed by fluorouracil 1000 mg/m² per day as a 24-hour IV infusion (days 1–4) for 3 cycles.</li></ul><p><strong>For all patients:</strong> Premedicate with oral corticosteroids. Adjust dose as needed.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Docefrez 80 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Docetaxel is a CYP3A4 substrate. In vitro studies indicate that its metabolism may be altered by compounds that induce, inhibit, or are metabolized by cytochrome P450 3A4.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Docetaxel is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Neutrophil counts of &lt;1500 cells/mm³.</li><li>A history of severe hypersensitivity reactions to docetaxel or other drugs formulated with polysorbate 80.</li><li>Severe reactions, including anaphylaxis.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Docefrez 80 mg/vial</strong></p><p> Common adverse reactions across all docetaxel indications include:</p><ul><li>Infections, neutropenia, anemia, febrile neutropenia, hypersensitivity, thrombocytopenia.</li><li>Neuropathy, dysgeusia, dyspnea, constipation, anorexia, nail disorders, fluid retention.</li><li>Asthenia, pain, nausea, diarrhea, vomiting, mucositis, alopecia, skin reactions, myalgia.</li></ul><p>The incidence of adverse reactions varies depending on the indication.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Based on animal reproduction studies and its mechanism of action, docetaxel can cause fetal harm if administered to a pregnant woman.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> There is no information on the presence of docetaxel in human milk, its effects on milk production, or its impact on a breastfed child. No lactation studies have been conducted in animals.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Docetaxel should be administered under the supervision of a physician experienced in the use of antineoplastic agents.</li><li>There is a higher risk of severe adverse reactions, including toxic death and fatal gastrointestinal hemorrhage, in patients with hepatic impairment.</li><li>Should not be given to patients with neutrophil counts &lt;1500 cells/mm³.</li><li>Fatal cases of enterocolitis, including ischemic colitis, colitis, and neutropenic enterocolitis, have been reported.</li><li>Severe hypersensitivity reactions, with potential fatal outcomes, may require immediate discontinuation. Patients should be closely monitored.</li><li>Treatment-related acute myeloid leukemia may occur.</li><li>No studies have been conducted to assess the carcinogenic potential of docetaxel.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Docefrez 80 mg/vial</strong></p><p> There are few reports of overdosage. No known antidote exists for docetaxel overdose. In case of overdose:</p><ul><li>Keep the patient in a specialized unit and closely monitor vital functions.</li><li>Overdosage may cause bone marrow suppression, peripheral neurotoxicity, and mucositis.</li><li>Patients should receive therapeutic G-CSF as soon as possible after overdose discovery.</li><li>Other appropriate symptomatic measures should be taken as needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store between 2°C and 25°C, protected from light. Freezing does not adversely affect the product.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Docetaxel is an antineoplastic agent that disrupts the microtubular network essential for mitosis and interphase cellular functions. It promotes tubulin polymerization into stable microtubules while inhibiting disassembly, leading to microtubule stabilization and inhibition of mitosis.</p><p>Unlike other spindle poisons, docetaxel does not alter the number of protofilaments in bound microtubules. It has shown cytotoxicity against various tumor cell lines, including those overexpressing p-glycoprotein, which is encoded by the multidrug-resistant gene.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Based on animal studies and its mechanism of action, docetaxel can cause fetal harm if administered to a pregnant woman. No information is available regarding its presence in human milk, effects on milk production, or effects on the breastfed child. No lactation studies have been conducted in animals.</p>', 'active', '148_1742686447.jpg', 1, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 04:34:07', '2025-03-23 04:34:07');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4234, 'Extracef', 'Cephradine', 8.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Extracef 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Extracef 250 mg/5 ml is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. These include:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper respiratory tract infections</strong>: Sinusitis, pharyngitis, tonsillitis, laryngo-tracheo bronchitis, and otitis media.</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections</strong>: Bronchitis (acute and chronic), lobar pneumonia, and bronchopneumonia.</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: Cystitis, urethritis, and pyelonephritis.</li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: Abscess, cellulitis, furunculosis, and impetigo.</li></ul><p><strong>Susceptible Microorganisms</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Gram-positive</strong>: Staphylococci (penicillin-sensitive and resistant strains), Streptococci, Streptococcus pyogenes (beta-haemolytic), and Streptococcus pneumoniae.</li><li><strong>Gram-negative</strong>: Escherichia coli, Klebsiella spp., Proteus mirabilis, Haemophilus influenzae, Shigella spp., Salmonella spp. (including Salmonella typhi), and Neisseria spp.</li></ul><p>Many strains of <strong>E. coli</strong> and <strong>Staphylococcus aureus</strong> that produce penicillinase and are ampicillin-resistant are susceptible to Extracef 250 mg/5 ml.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> First-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Extracef 250 mg/5 ml is a cephalosporin antibiotic with broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria. It interferes with bacterial cell wall synthesis by inhibiting the transpeptidase enzyme, leading to bacterial cell weakening, swelling, and rupture.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Adults:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Respiratory tract infections (except lobar pneumonia), skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: 250 mg every 6 hours or 500 mg every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Lobar pneumonia</strong>: 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 g every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections</strong>: 500 mg every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Severe urinary tract infections (including prostatitis)</strong>: 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 g every 12 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Children (over nine months old):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>General infections</strong>: 25–50 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 6 or 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Otitis media due to H. influenzae</strong>: 75–100 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 6 or 12 hours (not exceeding 4 g/day).</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Extracef 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p><strong>For oral administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Urinary tract infections</strong>: 500 mg four times daily or 1 g twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Respiratory tract infections</strong>: 250–500 mg four times daily or 500 mg–1 g twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections</strong>: 250–500 mg four times daily or 500 mg–1 g twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>General infections</strong>: 25–50 mg/kg/day in 2 or 4 divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Otitis media</strong>: 75–100 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 6 to 12 hours (max 4 g/day).</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Normal adult dose applies, but renal and hepatic function should be monitored.</li></ul><p><strong>For injectable administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 2–4 g daily in four equally divided doses, up to 8 g daily.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis</strong>: A single preoperative dose of 1–2 g intramuscularly or intravenously.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 50–100 mg/kg daily in four divided doses, up to 300 mg/kg daily in severe infection.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Extracef 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant use of <strong>nephrotoxic drugs</strong> (e.g., aminoglycosides) increases the risk of kidney damage.</li><li><strong>Diuretics (e.g., frusemide, ethacrynic acid)</strong> and <strong>probenecid</strong> enhance renal toxicity risk.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Extracef 250 mg/5 ml should not be used in patients with known or suspected <strong>hypersensitivity to cephalosporins</strong>.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Extracef 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Common side effects include <strong>gastrointestinal disturbances</strong> and <strong>hypersensitivity reactions</strong>.</p><p><strong>Rare Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Glossitis, heartburn, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain.</li><li><strong>Skin &amp; Hypersensitivity</strong>: Urticaria, rashes, joint pain, edema.</li><li><strong>Blood Disorders</strong>: Thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, agranulocytosis, aplastic anemia, hemolytic anemia.</li><li><strong>Liver Issues</strong>: Liver enzyme disturbances, transient hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice.</li><li><strong>Renal Issues</strong>: Reversible interstitial nephritis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: FDA Pregnancy Category B. Should be used in pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Excreted in human milk; caution is advised when administering to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged use may lead to <strong>superinfection</strong> due to resistant organisms.</li><li>Cross-sensitivity may occur in <strong>penicillin-allergic</strong> patients.</li><li>May cause <strong>false-positive Coombs’ test</strong> and <strong>urine glucose test</strong> results.</li><li><strong>Dose adjustment required in renal impairment</strong>.</li><li>Contains lactose; <strong>not suitable for patients with lactose intolerance</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Extracef 250 mg/5 ml</strong></p><p> Symptoms include <strong>nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and gastric upset</strong>. Treatment is mainly <strong>supportive</strong>, and gastric lavage may be needed in case of significant overdose.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Suspension</strong>: Use within <strong>7 days at room temperature</strong> or <strong>14 days if refrigerated</strong>.</li><li><strong>Injection solutions</strong>: Use within <strong>2 hours at room temperature</strong> or <strong>12 hours if stored at 5°C</strong>.</li><li>Store away from children. <strong>Prescription required</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> First-generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Extracef 250 mg/5 ml inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by disrupting peptidoglycan cross-linking, leading to <strong>cell lysis and bacterial death</strong>.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment Adjustments</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>CrCl &gt; 20 ml/min</strong>: 500 mg every 6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>CrCl 5–20 ml/min</strong>: 250 mg every 6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>CrCl &lt; 5 ml/min</strong>: 250 mg every 50–70 hours.</li><li><strong>Hemodialysis Patients</strong>: Specific dosing schedules apply.</li></ul><p>For <strong>chronic or severe infections</strong>, doses up to <strong>1 g four times daily</strong> may be required. <strong>Treatment should continue for at least 10 days</strong> in cases of hemolytic streptococcal infections to prevent complications like <strong>rheumatic fever or glomerulonephritis</strong>.</p>', 'active', '1037_1742686658.jpg', 29, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 04:37:38', '2025-03-23 04:37:38'),
(4235, 'Galet M', 'Vildagliptin plus Metformin Hydrochloride', 21.50, 30, 'Tablet', '30\'s pack', 'Tablet', '50 mg plus 500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Galet M 50 mg+500 mg tablet is indicated as an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycaemic control in patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus whose diabetes is not adequately controlled on Metformin Hydrochloride or Vildagliptin alone or who are already treated with the combination of Vildagliptin and Metformin Hydrochloride as separate tablets.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Combination Oral Hypoglycemic Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Vildagliptin acts primarily by inhibiting DPP-4 (Dipeptidyl peptidase-4), the enzyme responsible for the degradation of the incretin hormones GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1) and GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide). The administration of Vildagliptin results in a rapid and complete inhibition of DPP-4 activity, leading to increased fasting and postprandial endogenous levels of the incretin hormones GLP-1 and GIP. By increasing these incretin hormone levels, Vildagliptin enhances insulin secretion from pancreatic beta cells and decreases glucagon secretion from alpha cells. This results in a decreased fasting and postprandial hepatic glucose production, leading to reduced glycaemia.</p><p>Metformin Hydrochloride is a biguanide-type oral antihyperglycemic drug used in the management of type 2 diabetes. It lowers both basal and postprandial plasma glucose without producing hypoglycemia. Metformin works by decreasing hepatic glucose production, reducing intestinal glucose absorption, and improving insulin sensitivity through increased peripheral glucose uptake and utilization.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: Based on the patient\'s current dose of Metformin, Galet M 50 mg+500 mg may be initiated at twice daily, 1 tablet in the morning and 1 tablet in the evening.</li><li>Patients receiving Vildagliptin and Metformin from separate tablets may switch to this combination containing the same doses of each component.</li><li>Doses higher than 100 mg of Vildagliptin are not recommended.</li><li>There is no clinical experience of Vildagliptin and Metformin in triple combination with other antidiabetic agents.</li><li>Taking this combination with or just after food may reduce gastrointestinal symptoms associated with Metformin.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> No clinically relevant pharmacokinetic interaction was observed when Vildagliptin (100 mg once daily) was co-administered with Metformin Hydrochloride (1,000 mg once daily). Galet M 50 mg+500 mg has a low potential for drug interactions. Vildagliptin is not a cytochrome P450 (CYP) 450 enzyme substrate nor does it inhibit or induce CYP 450 enzymes, making it unlikely to interact with other medications metabolized by these enzymes.</p><p>No clinically relevant interactions were observed when co-administered with:</p><ul><li>Oral antidiabetics (glibenclamide, pioglitazone, metformin hydrochloride)</li><li>Amlodipine, digoxin, ramipril, simvastatin, valsartan, or warfarin</li></ul><p>However, furosemide, nifedipine, and glyburide increase Cmax and blood AUC of Metformin without changing its renal clearance.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Galet M 50 mg+500 mg is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Vildagliptin, Metformin Hydrochloride, or any excipients</li><li>Renal disease or renal dysfunction</li><li>Acute myocardial infarction</li><li>Septicemia</li><li>Congestive heart failure</li><li>Acute or chronic metabolic acidosis, including diabetic ketoacidosis</li><li>Patients undergoing radiologic studies involving intravascular iodinated contrast materials due to the risk of acute renal function alteration</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> The most common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Tremor</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Hypoglycemia</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Therefore, this combination should only be used during pregnancy if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li>It is unknown whether Vildagliptin and/or Metformin Hydrochloride is excreted in human milk. Hence, this combination should not be administered to breastfeeding women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Lactic Acidosis</strong>: Metformin accumulation can lead to lactic acidosis. If metabolic acidosis is suspected, treatment should be discontinued, and the patient should be hospitalized immediately.</li><li><strong>Renal Function</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Monitor serum creatinine at least once a year in patients with normal renal function.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Monitor 2–4 times a year in patients with serum creatinine at the upper limit of normal and in elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Liver Function</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Liver Function Tests (LFTs) should be monitored before starting treatment, then at three-month intervals during the first year, and periodically thereafter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If transaminase levels increase, a second liver function test should confirm the findings. Frequent monitoring should continue until levels normalize.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If AST or ALT persist at 3× the upper limit of normal, treatment should be discontinued.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If signs of liver dysfunction (e.g., jaundice) develop, Vildagliptin &amp; Metformin should be permanently discontinued.</li><li><strong>Surgery</strong>: Treatment should be discontinued 48 hours before elective surgery with general anesthesia and should not be resumed earlier than 48 hours afterward.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The safety and effectiveness of this combination in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Not recommended for children below 18 years of age.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Since Metformin is excreted via the kidney, elderly patients should have their renal function monitored regularly.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">This combination should only be used in elderly patients with normal renal function.</li><li><strong>Patients with Renal Impairment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">This combination should not be used in patients with renal failure or renal dysfunction (serum creatinine &gt;1.5 mg/dl in males or &gt;1.4 mg/dl in females).</li><li><strong>Patients with Hepatic Impairment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Not recommended in patients with hepatic impairment, including those with pre-treatment ALT or AST &gt;3× the upper limit of normal.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Combination Oral Hypoglycemic Preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Vildagliptin acts by inhibiting DPP-4, increasing endogenous levels of incretin hormones (GLP-1 and GIP). This enhances insulin secretion from pancreatic beta cells and reduces glucagon secretion from alpha cells, resulting in lower glucose production and improved glycaemic control.</p><p>Metformin Hydrochloride is a biguanide antihyperglycemic drug that decreases hepatic glucose production, reduces intestinal glucose absorption, and enhances insulin sensitivity by increasing peripheral glucose uptake and utilization.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> No adequate and well-controlled studies have been conducted in pregnant women. Therefore, this combination should only be used if the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Not recommended for children below 18 years of age due to a lack of established safety and effectiveness.</li><li>Use in elderly patients should be accompanied by regular renal function monitoring.</li></ul>', 'active', '1337_1742687014.jpg', 59, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 04:43:34', '2025-03-23 04:43:34'),
(4236, 'Imacent', 'Imatinib Mesylate', 100.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Imacent 100 mg</strong></p><p> Newly diagnosed adult and pediatric patients with Philadelphia chromosome positive chronic myeloid leukemia (Ph+CML) in chronic phase. Patients with Philadelphia chromosome positive chronic myeloid leukemia in blast crisis, accelerated phase, or in chronic phase after failure of interferon-alpha therapy.</p><p> Adult patients with relapsed or refractory Philadelphia chromosome positive acute lymphoblastic leukemia (Ph+ ALL). Pediatric patients with newly diagnosed Philadelphia chromosome positive acute lymphoblastic leukemia (Ph+ ALL) in combination with chemotherapy.</p><p> Adult patients with myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative diseases associated with platelet-derived growth factor receptor (PDGFR) gene re-arrangements.</p><p> Adult patients with aggressive systemic mastocytosis without the D816V c-Kit mutation or with c-Kit mutational status unknown.</p><p> Adult patients with hypereosinophilic syndrome and/or chronic eosinophilic leukemia who have the FIP1L1-PDGFR a fusion kinase (mutational analysis or fluorescence in situ hybridization [FISH] demonstration of CHIC2 allele deletion) and for patients with HES and/or CEL who are FIP1L1-PDGFRa fusion kinase negative or unknown.</p><p> Adult patients with unresectable, recurrent and/or metastatic dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans.</p><p> Patients with Kit (CD117) positive unresectable and/or metastatic malignant gastrointestinal stromal tumors.</p><p> Adjuvant treatment of adult patients following complete gross resection of Kit (CD117) positive GIST.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Targeted Cancer Therapy</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Imatinib is a tyrosine kinase inhibitor that inhibits the BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase created by the Philadelphia chromosome abnormality in chronic myeloid leukemia (CML). It blocks proliferation and induces apoptosis in BCR-ABL positive cell lines, as well as fresh leukemic cells from Philadelphia chromosome positive CML. Imatinib also inhibits receptor kinases for platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF) and stem cell factor (SCF), c-kit, PDGF- and SCF-mediated cellular events.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Imacent 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults with Ph+ CML CP:</strong> 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adults with Ph+ CML AP or BC:</strong> 600 mg/day</li><li><strong>Pediatrics with Ph+ CML CP:</strong> 340 mg/m²/day</li><li><strong>Adults with Ph+ ALL:</strong> 600 mg/day</li><li><strong>Pediatrics with Ph+ ALL:</strong> 340 mg/m²/day</li><li><strong>Adults with MDS/MPD:</strong> 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adults with ASM:</strong> 100 mg/day or 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adults with HES/CEL:</strong> 100 mg/day or 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adults with DFSP:</strong> 800 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adults with metastatic and/or unresectable GIST:</strong> 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Adjuvant treatment of adults with GIST:</strong> 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Patients with mild to moderate hepatic impairment:</strong> 400 mg/day</li><li><strong>Patients with severe hepatic impairment:</strong> 300 mg/day</li></ul><p>All doses of Imatinib should be taken with a meal and a large glass of water. Doses of 400 mg or 600 mg should be administered once daily, whereas a dose of 800 mg should be administered as 400 mg twice a day. Imatinib can be dissolved in water or apple juice for patients having difficulty swallowing. Daily dosing of 800 mg and above should be accomplished using the 400-mg tablet to reduce exposure to iron.</p><p><strong>Administration of Imacent 100 mg</strong></p><p> Should be taken with food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Imacent 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Agents Inducing CYP3A Metabolism:</strong> Concomitant administration of Imatinib and strong CYP3A4 inducers may reduce total exposure of imatinib; consider alternative agents.</li><li><strong>Agents Inhibiting CYP3A Metabolism:</strong> Concomitant administration of Imatinib and strong CYP3A4 inhibitors may result in a significant imatinib exposure increase. Grapefruit juice may also increase plasma concentrations of imatinib; avoid grapefruit juice.</li><li><strong>Interactions with Drugs Metabolized by CYP3A4:</strong> Imatinib will increase plasma concentration of CYP3A4 metabolized drugs (e.g., triazolo-benzodiazepines, dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, certain HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors, etc.).</li><li><strong>Interactions with Drugs Metabolized by CYP2D6:</strong> Use caution when administering Imatinib with CYP2D6 substrates that have a narrow therapeutic window.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity. Lactation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Imacent 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Fluid Retention and Edema</li><li>Hematologic Toxicity</li><li>Congestive Heart Failure and Left Ventricular Dysfunction</li><li>Hepatotoxicity</li><li>Hemorrhage</li><li>Gastrointestinal Disorders</li><li>Hypereosinophilic Cardiac Toxicity</li><li>Dermatologic Toxicities</li><li>Hypothyroidism</li><li>Growth Retardation in Children and Adolescents</li><li>Tumor Lysis Syndrome</li><li>Impairments Related to Driving and Using Machinery</li><li>Renal Toxicity</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> <strong>Category D:</strong> There is positive evidence of human fetal risk, but the benefits from use in pregnant women may be acceptable despite the risk (e.g., in a life-threatening situation or for a serious disease where safer drugs are ineffective).</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor weight regularly and manage unexpected rapid weight gain by drug interruption and diuretics.</li><li>Cytopenias (anemia, neutropenia, and thrombocytopenia) may occur; manage with dose adjustments.</li><li>Severe congestive heart failure and left ventricular dysfunction have been reported. Monitor cardiac function.</li><li>Severe hepatotoxicity, including fatalities, may occur. Monitor liver function regularly.</li><li>Grade 3/4 hemorrhage has been reported in clinical studies in patients with newly diagnosed CML and GIST.</li><li>Gastrointestinal perforations, some fatal, have been reported.</li><li>Cardiogenic shock/left ventricular dysfunction has been associated with the initiation of Imatinib in conditions with high eosinophil levels.</li><li>Stevens-Johnson syndrome and other severe dermatologic reactions have been reported.</li><li>Growth retardation in children has been noted; monitor growth closely.</li><li>Tumor Lysis Syndrome requires close monitoring.</li><li>Renal toxicity has been reported; evaluate renal function regularly.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Imacent 100 mg</strong></p><p> Experience with doses higher than the recommended therapeutic dose is limited. Patients should be observed and given symptomatic treatment as needed.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 30°C, in a cool and dry place. Keep away from light. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Patients on potent CYP3A4 inducers:</strong> Increase dose by 50% and monitor response.</li><li><strong>Severe Hepatic Impairment:</strong> Reduce dose by 25%.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Targeted Cancer Therapy, Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Imatinib is a small molecule protein-tyrosine kinase inhibitor that potently inhibits the activity of the Bcr-Abl tyrosine kinase (TK), as well as several receptor TKs: Kit, discoidin domain receptors (DDR1 and DDR2), colony stimulating factor receptor (CSF-1R), and platelet-derived growth factor receptors alpha and beta (PDGFR-alpha and PDGFR-beta).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Women of childbearing potential:</strong> Use effective contraception during treatment and for at least 15 days after stopping treatment.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong> Women should not breastfeed during treatment and for at least 15 days after stopping treatment with Imatinib.</li><li><strong>Fertility:</strong> Effects on human fertility are unknown; consult a physician for concerns.</li></ul>', 'active', '1972_1742687162.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 04:46:02', '2025-03-23 04:46:02'),
(4237, 'Ipratop', 'Ipratropium Bromide', 18.00, 1, 'Nebuliser Solution', '5 Nebuliser Solution', 'Nebuliser Solution', '250 mcg per ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ipratop 250 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Nebuliser Solution:</strong> It is indicated for the treatment of reversible bronchospasm associated with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). It is also indicated, when used concomitantly with inhaled beta 2-agonists, for treatment of reversible airway obstruction as in acute and chronic asthma.</p><p> <strong>Inhaler:</strong> As a bronchodilator in the treatment of chronic reversible airway obstruction as in asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, including chronic bronchitis and emphysema. Treatment of acute reversible airway obstruction.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Anticholinergic bronchodilators</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Ipratop 250 mcg/ml is a quaternary ammonium compound with anticholinergic properties. It appears to inhibit vagal reflexes by antagonizing the action of acetylcholine (the transmitter agent released from the vagus nerve). Anticholinergics prevent the increase in intracellular concentration of cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cyclic GMP) caused by the interaction of acetylcholine with the muscarinic receptor on bronchial smooth muscle.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ipratop 250 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Children over 3 years:</strong> The usual dose is 0.4-2.0 ml Ipratop 250 mcg/ml Solution (100-500 micrograms) up to three times daily.</p><p> <strong>Adults (including elderly and adolescents) over 12 years of age:</strong> The usual dose is 0.4-2.0 ml Ipratop 250 mcg/ml Solution (100-500 micrograms) up to four times daily.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Ipratop 250 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Nebuliser Solution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults (including the elderly) and children over 12 years of age:</strong> 250-500 micrograms (i.e., 1 ml or 2 ml) 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>For treatment of acute bronchospasm:</strong> 500 micrograms/2 ml. It is advisable not to exceed the recommended daily dose during either acute or maintenance treatment. Daily doses exceeding 2 mg in adults and children over 12 years of age should only be given under medical supervision.</li><li><strong>Children 6-12 years of age:</strong> 250 micrograms (i.e., 1 ml) up to a total daily dose of 1 mg (4 ml). The time interval between doses may be determined by the physician.</li><li><strong>Children 0-5 years of age (for treatment of acute asthma only):</strong> 125-250 micrograms (i.e., 0.5 ml-1 ml) up to a total daily dose of 1 mg (4 ml). Ipratropium bromide should be administered no more frequently than 6 hourly in children under 5 years of age.</li></ul><p>Ipratropium Nebuliser Solution may be combined with a short-acting beta 2-agonist in the same nebulizer chamber. The solution should be used as soon as possible after mixing, and any unused solution should be discarded. It can be administered using a range of commercially available nebulizing devices. If dilution is necessary, use only sterile sodium chloride 0.9% solution.</p><p><strong>Inhaler</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> The usual dose is 1-2 puffs (20 gm/spray) three or four times daily. A single dose up to 80 gm (4 puffs) may be required to obtain maximum benefit during early treatment. Patients may take additional inhalations as required; however, the total number of inhalations should not exceed 12 in 24 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:6-12 years:</strong> Usually 1-2 puffs two to three times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Below 6 years:</strong> The usual dose is 1 puff (20 gm) three times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Ipratop 250 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> Using an inhaler seems simple, but many patients do not use it correctly. Proper inhaler technique ensures that more medicine reaches the lungs. Follow these steps to use an inhaler effectively:</p><ol><li>Take off the cap.</li><li>Shake the inhaler vigorously (at least six times) before each use.</li><li>If the inhaler is new or hasn’t been used for a week or more, shake it well and release one puff into the air.</li><li>Breathe out fully and hold the inhaler upright.</li><li>Place the mouthpiece between the teeth and close lips around it.</li><li>While breathing deeply and slowly through the mouth, press down firmly on the canister to release medicine.</li><li>Remove the inhaler from the mouth and hold breath for at least 10 seconds.</li><li>If more than one inhalation is needed, wait 1 minute between puffs.</li><li>Replace the cap on the mouthpiece after use.</li><li>Rinse the mouth with water after each treatment.</li></ol><p><strong>Instructions for Cleaning Inhaler:</strong></p><ul><li>Clean the inhaler at least once a week.</li><li>Remove the canister and rinse the plastic actuator and cap in warm water. Do not put the metal canister in water.</li><li>Dry the actuator and cap thoroughly before replacing the canister.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ipratop 250 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> Ipratropium has been used concomitantly with other drugs, including sympathomimetic bronchodilators, methylxanthines, steroids, and cromolyn sodium, without adverse drug reactions. However, there are no studies fully evaluating the interaction effects of Ipratropium with these drugs in terms of effectiveness.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> <strong>Nebuliser Solution:</strong> Known hypersensitivity to atropine or its derivatives or any component of the product.</p><p> <strong>Inhaler:</strong> Contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to Ipratropium, Atropine, or its derivatives, and in those allergic to soya lecithin, soybean, or peanuts.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Ipratop 250 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> <strong>Nebuliser Solution:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Headache, dizziness, cough, inhalation-induced bronchospasm, dry mouth, vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon:</strong> Urticaria, tachycardia, skin rash, pruritus.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Anaphylactic reaction, eye pain, mydriasis, increased intraocular pressure, palpitations, atrial fibrillation, nausea.</li></ul><p><strong>Inhaler:</strong></p><ul><li>Most common side effects include dryness of the mouth, cough, irritation, headache, nausea, dizziness, and blurred vision.</li><li>Less frequent side effects include tachycardia, nervousness, paresthesia, drowsiness, itching, flushing, constipation, tremor, and mucosal ulceration.</li><li>Allergic-type reactions such as skin rash, angioedema, and anaphylaxis have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> The safety of Ipratop 250 mcg/ml during human pregnancy has not been established. The benefits of use must be weighed against potential risks to the fetus. It is unknown if the drug is excreted into breast milk.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with narrow-angle glaucoma, prostatic hyperplasia, or bladder-outflow obstruction.</li><li>Avoid contact with the eyes, as it may cause ocular complications such as mydriasis or increased intraocular pressure.</li><li>If symptoms worsen or do not improve, seek medical attention immediately.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ipratop 250 mcg/ml</strong></p><p> Acute overdose by inhalation is unlikely. However, doses exceeding 5 mg may cause increased heart rate and palpitations. High oral doses (30 mg) may cause anticholinergic side effects but are not typically severe.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 25°C.</li><li>Use a fresh ampoule for each dose and discard any leftover solution.</li><li>Keep the ampoule in the outer carton.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Anticholinergic bronchodilators</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Ipratop 250 mcg/ml is a quaternary ammonium compound that inhibits vagal reflexes by antagonizing acetylcholine. This prevents the increase in cyclic GMP, leading to bronchodilation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Use during pregnancy should be carefully considered, weighing potential risks. The drug\'s excretion into breast milk is unknown.</p><p>This version follows your preferred formatting style with <strong>bold headings</strong> while keeping the content clear and structured.</p>', 'active', '894_1742687442.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 04:50:42', '2025-03-23 04:50:42'),
(4238, 'Keranil', 'Salicylic Acid', 50.00, 1, 'Cream', '30 gm tube', 'Cream', '6 percent', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Keranil 6%</strong></p><p> This topical preparation treats the following common scaly conditions:</p><ul><li>Chronic atopic dermatitis</li><li>Lichen simplex</li><li>Psoriasis</li><li>Seborrhoeic dermatitis</li><li>Ichthyosis</li></ul><p><strong>12% Keranil 6%</strong></p><p> This topical preparation treats the following common scaly conditions:</p><ul><li>Warts (small excessive growths of skin caused by a type of virus, often occurring on fingers or back of the hands)</li><li>Verruca (occurs only on the sole of the feet, often painful, and looks like a small white ring of skin with a black dot in the center)</li><li>Corns and Calluses (hard, thick pads of skin caused by pressure and friction, typically on the feet due to poorly fitting shoes and sometimes on the hands)</li></ul><p><strong>2% Keranil 6%</strong></p><p> This topical preparation is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Mild to moderate acne</li><li>Blackheads and whiteheads</li><li>Redness &amp; swelling of the face</li><li>Pimples</li><li>To prevent future acne breakouts</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Topical salicylic preparation</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Keranil 6% has a potent keratolytic &amp; a slight antiseptic action when applied topically to the skin. In low concentrations, the drug has keratoplastic activity (correction of abnormal keratinization), and in higher concentrations, it has keratolytic activity (causing peeling of skin). Keranil 6% softens &amp; destroys the stratum corneum by increasing endogenous hydration (water concentration), likely due to decreased pH, which causes the cornified epithelium (horny layer of the skin) to swell, soften, and then desquamate. Necrosis of normal skin has been associated with overuse of the drug. At high concentrations, Keranil 6% has a caustic effect. Moisture is essential for Keranil 6% to exert its action on the skin and for maceration and desquamation of epidermal tissue to occur. The drug has weak antifungal &amp; antibacterial activity.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Keranil 6%</strong></p><p> Apply to the affected area once daily. Hydrate the area for 5 minutes prior to application if possible. Occlude the area at night and wash it off in the morning. Keranil 6% is used in children over 2 years.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Keranil 6%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>6% &amp; 12% Keranil 6%</strong>: Apply to the affected area once daily. Hydrate the area for 5 minutes prior to application if possible. Occlude the area at night and wash it off in the morning. Keranil 6% is used in children over 2 years.</li><li><strong>2% Keranil 6%</strong>: Apply enough medicine to cover the affected area once daily in the 1st week, then 2 to 3 times daily from the 2nd week and rub gently. After 3-5 minutes, rinse the face with cool water. Keranil 6% is used in children over 2 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Keranil 6%</strong></p><p> Do not use other topical preparations on the treated area unless otherwise directed by your healthcare provider. They may interfere with treatment or increase skin irritation.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> It should not be used in any patient known to be sensitive to Keranil 6% or any other listed ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Keranil 6%</strong></p><p> An allergic reaction (shortness of breath, closing of the throat, swelling of the lips, face or tongue, or hives) or severe skin irritation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. If used by nursing mothers, it should not be used on the chest area to avoid accidental contamination of the child.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> For external use only. Avoid contact with eyes and other mucous membranes.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Keranil 6%</strong></p><p> An overdose of Keranil 6% topical is unlikely to occur. If the medication has been ingested or an overdose is suspected, the patient needs to be hospitalized immediately.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Do not store above 30°C. Keep away from light and out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> Keranil 6% is used in children over 2 years.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Topical salicylic preparation</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Keranil 6% has a potent keratolytic &amp; a slight antiseptic action when applied topically to the skin. In low concentrations, the drug has keratoplastic activity (correction of abnormal keratinization) &amp; in higher concentrations, the drug has keratolytic activity (causing peeling of skin). Keranil 6% softens &amp; destroys the stratum corneum by increasing endogenous hydration (water concentration), likely due to decreased pH, which causes the cornified epithelium (horny layer of the skin) to swell, soften, and then desquamate. Necrosis of normal skin has been associated with overuse of the drug. At high concentrations, Keranil 6% has a caustic effect. Moisture is essential for Keranil 6% to exert its action on the skin and for maceration and desquamation of epidermal tissue to occur. The drug has weak antifungal &amp; antibacterial activity.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. If used by nursing mothers, it should not be used on the chest area to avoid accidental contamination of the child.</p>', 'active', '198_1742687615.jpg', 30, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 04:53:35', '2025-03-23 04:53:35'),
(4239, 'Lacitone', 'Furosemide + Spironolactone', 7.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg+50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Lacitone 20 mg+50 mg</strong></p><p> Frusemide &amp; Spironolactone combination is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Essential hypertension</li><li>Chronic congestive heart failure</li><li>Hepatic cirrhosis, with collection of fluid in the abdominal cavity (ascites)</li><li>Swelling due to excess fluid retention (edema)</li><li>Hyperaldosteronism</li><li>Resistant edema associated with secondary hyperaldosteronism</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics</li><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics &amp; Aldosterone antagonists</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Spironolactone (potassium-sparing diuretic) and Furosemide (loop diuretic) have different but complementary mechanisms and sites of action. Therefore, when given together, they produce an additive or synergistic diuretic effect. The Furosemide component inhibits the Na+/K+/2Cl- co-transporter in the ascending Loop of Henle and blocks the reabsorption of sodium, potassium, and chloride ions, thereby increasing the quantity of sodium and the volume of water excreted in the urine. This characteristically induces potassium loss. The spironolactone component inhibits the reabsorption of sodium in exchange for potassium at the distal tubule by antagonizing the action of aldosterone, so that sodium excretion is greatly favored and the excess loss of potassium induced by Furosemide is reduced.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Lacitone 20 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Furosemide 20 and Spironolactone 50 mg</strong>: 1 to 4 tablets daily (20 to 80 mg of Furosemide and 50 to 200 mg of Spironolactone) according to the patient’s response.</li><li><strong>Furosemide 40 and Spironolactone 50 mg</strong>: For previously stabilized patients requiring higher doses of Spironolactone and Furosemide, this tablet can be used at a dose of one to two tablets daily (Furosemide 40 to 80 mg and Spironolactone 50 to 100 mg).</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Lacitone 20 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Furosemide 20 and Spironolactone 50 mg</strong>: 1 to 4 tablets daily (20 to 80 mg of Furosemide and 50 to 200 mg of Spironolactone) according to the patient’s response.</li><li><strong>Furosemide 40 and Spironolactone 50 mg</strong>: For previously stabilized patients requiring higher doses of Spironolactone and Furosemide, this tablet can be used at a dose of one to two tablets daily (Furosemide 40 to 80 mg and Spironolactone 50 to 100 mg).</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Children</strong></p><p> Spironolactone and Furosemide are not suitable for use in children. Both Spironolactone and Furosemide may be excreted more slowly in the elderly.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Lacitone 20 mg+50 mg</strong></p><p> When taken together with ACE inhibitors or potassium salts, there is an increased risk of hyperkalemia. Spironolactone increases the levels of cardiac glycosides such as digoxin in the blood, which may result in digitalis toxicity. Corticosteroids may cause hypokalemia if they are used with Spironolactone. The blood pressure-lowering and diuretic effects of Furosemide may be reduced or abolished when used together with indomethacin and possibly other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). Furosemide may increase the ototoxicity of aminoglycoside antibiotics. Simultaneous administration of sucralfate and Furosemide may reduce the natriuretic and anti-hypertensive effect of Furosemide.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Anuria</li><li>Acute renal insufficiency</li><li>Rapidly deteriorating or severe impairment of renal function (creatinine clearance &lt;30 ml/min)</li><li>Hyperkalemia</li><li>Addison\'s disease</li><li>Hypersensitivity to Spironolactone, Furosemide, or sulphonamides</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Lacitone 20 mg+50 mg</strong></p><p> Spironolactone may give rise to:</p><ul><li>Headache and drowsiness</li><li>Gastrointestinal distress, including cramping and diarrhea</li><li>Ataxia, mental confusion, and skin rashes</li><li>Gynaecomastia (breast enlargement), which may persist in rare cases</li><li>Other endocrine disorders, including hirsutism, deepening of the voice, menstrual irregularities, and impotence</li><li>Transient increase in blood-urea-nitrogen concentrations and mild acidosis</li><li>Hyponatremia and hyperkalemia</li><li>Excessive diuresis, which may result in dehydration, reduced blood volume, circulatory collapse, vascular thrombosis, and embolism, particularly in elderly patients</li><li>Serious depletion of potassium and magnesium leading to cardiac arrhythmias</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Spironolactone and its metabolites may cross the placental barrier. The use of Spironolactone in pregnant women requires weighing the anticipated benefit against possible hazards to the mother and fetus. Animal teratology studies indicate that Furosemide may cause fetal abnormalities. Therefore, Furosemide should only be used in women of childbearing age when appropriate contraceptive measures are taken or if the potential benefits justify the risks to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Metabolites of Spironolactone have been detected in breast milk. If the use of Spironolactone is considered essential, an alternative method of infant feeding should be instituted. Furosemide is excreted in breast milk, and breastfeeding should be discontinued if treatment is essential.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Caution should be taken in patients liable to electrolyte deficiency. This preparation should also be used with caution in:</p><ul><li>Diabetes</li><li>Enlarged prostate</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Hypovolemia</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Lacitone 20 mg+50 mg</strong></p><p> Symptoms of overdosage include:</p><ul><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Mental confusion</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Diarrhea and vomiting due to excessive diuresis.</li></ul><p>Treatment should focus on the replacement of fluid and correction of any electrolyte imbalance.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p> Spironolactone and Furosemide are not suitable for use in children. Both may be excreted more slowly in the elderly.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics</li><li>Potassium-sparing diuretics &amp; Aldosterone antagonists</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Spironolactone (potassium-sparing diuretic) and Furosemide (loop diuretic) have different but complementary mechanisms and sites of action. Therefore, when given together, they produce an additive or synergistic diuretic effect. The Furosemide component inhibits the Na+/K+/2Cl- co-transporter in the ascending Loop of Henle and blocks the reabsorption of sodium, potassium, and chloride ions, thereby increasing the quantity of sodium and the volume of water excreted in the urine. This characteristically induces potassium loss. The spironolactone component inhibits the reabsorption of sodium in exchange for potassium at the distal tubule by antagonizing the action of aldosterone, so that sodium excretion is greatly favored and the excess loss of potassium induced by Furosemide is reduced.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Spironolactone and its metabolites may cross the placental barrier. The use of Spironolactone in pregnant women requires weighing the anticipated benefit against possible hazards to the mother and fetus. Animal teratology studies indicate that Furosemide may cause fetal abnormalities. Therefore, Furosemide should only be used in women of childbearing age when appropriate contraceptive measures are taken or if the potential benefits justify the risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Lactation</strong></p><p> Metabolites of Spironolactone have been detected in breast milk. If the use of Spironolactone is considered essential, an alternative method of infant feeding should be instituted. Furosemide is excreted in breast milk, and breastfeeding should be discontinued if treatment is essential.</p>', 'active', '86_1742687743.jpg', 37, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 04:55:43', '2025-03-23 04:55:43');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4240, 'Maxitrol', 'Dexamethasone + Neomycin Sulphate + Polymyxin B Sulphate', 90.00, 1, 'Drop', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Solution', '(1 mg+3500 IU+6000 IU)/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Maxitrol (1 mg+3500 IU+6000 IU)/ml</strong></p><p> This is indicated in ocular inflammation when concurrent use of an antimicrobial is judged necessary.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic steroid - antibiotic combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Dexamethasone (Corticosteroids) suppress inflammatory response to a variety of agents and they probably delay or slow healing. Since corticosteroid may inhibit the body\'s defense mechanism against infection, a concomitant antimicrobial drug may be used when this inhibition is considered to be clinically significant in a particular case. The anti-infective component in the combination is included to provide action against specific organisms susceptible to it. Neomycin Sulphate is considered active against the following microorganisms: Staphylococcus aureus, Corynebacterium diphtheriae, Streptococcus Viridans, Escherichia coli, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Proteus vulgaris, Aerobacter aerogenes, and Haemophilus influenzae. Polymyxin B Sulphate is considered active against the following microorganism: Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Aerobacter aerogenes, Escherichia coli, Klebsiella pneumoniae, and Koch-Weeks bacillus. When a decision to administer both a corticosteroid and an antimicrobial is made, the administration of such drugs in combination has the advantage of greater patient compliance and convenience, with the added assurance that the appropriate dosage of both drugs is administered, plus assured compatibility of ingredients when both types of drugs are in the same formulation and, particularly, that the correct volume of drug is delivered and retained.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Maxitrol (1 mg+3500 IU+6000 IU)/ml</strong></p><p> Eye drops: One drop topically into the conjunctival sac(s). In severe disease, drops may be used hourly, being tapered to discontinuation as the inflammation subsides. In mild disease, drops may be used up to four to six times daily.</p><p> Eye ointment: It should be applied thinly and evenly to the conjunctival sac(s) at night (if eye drops used during the day) or 3-4 times daily (if eye ointment used alone).</p><p><strong>Interaction of Maxitrol (1 mg+3500 IU+6000 IU)/ml</strong></p><p> There are no known drug interactions and none well documented.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Epithelial herpes simplex keratitis (dentritic keratitis), vaccinia, varicella, and many other viral diseases of the cornea and conjunctiva. Hypersensitivity to a component of the medication (Hypersensitivity to the antibiotic component occurs at a higher rate than other components). The use of these combinations is always contraindicated after uncomplicated removal of a corneal foreign body.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Maxitrol (1 mg+3500 IU+6000 IU)/ml</strong></p><p> Adverse reactions have occurred with steroid/anti-infective combination drugs which can be attributed to the steroid component, the anti-infective component, or the combination. Exact incidence figures are not available since no denominator of treated patients is available. Reactions occurring most often from the presence of the anti-infective ingredient are allergic sensitizations. The reactions due to the steroid component in decreasing order of frequency are: elevation of intraocular pressure (IOP) with possible development of glaucoma, and infrequent optic nerve damage, posterior sub-capsular cataract formation, and delayed wound healing.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. Animal reproduction studies have shown an adverse effect on the fetus and there are no adequate and well-controlled studies in humans, but potential benefits may warrant use of the drug in pregnant women despite potential risks.</p><p> Lactation: Unknown if excreted in breast milk; minimal systemic absorption with ophthalmic administration.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Prolonged use may cause glaucoma, damage to the optic nerve, defects in visual acuity and fields of vision, posterior subcapsular cataract formation, and increased risk of secondary ocular infections. Increased risk of perforations in diseases which can cause thinning of the cornea or sclera. Corticosteroids may mask infection or worsen existing infections in acute purulent conditions of the eye. Regular monitoring of the intraocular pressure is recommended if product is used for 10 days. Long term application of topical corticosteroids may also increase the risk of ocular fungal infections. Neomycin sulfate may cause cutaneous sensitisation. Pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic steroid - antibiotic combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p> Dexamethasone (Corticosteroids) suppress inflammatory response to a variety of agents and they probably delay or slow healing. Since corticosteroid may inhibit the body\'s defense mechanism against infection, a concomitant antimicrobial drug may be used when this inhibition is considered to be clinically significant in a particular case. The anti-infective component in the combination is included to provide action against specific organisms susceptible to it. Neomycin Sulphate is considered active against the following microorganisms: Staphylococcus aureus, Corynebacterium diphtheriae, Streptococcus Viridans, Escherichia coli, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Proteus vulgaris, Aerobacter aerogenes, and Haemophilus influenzae. Polymyxin B Sulphate is considered active against the following microorganism: Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Aerobacter aerogenes, Escherichia coli, Klebsiella pneumoniae, and Koch-Weeks bacillus. When a decision to administer both a corticosteroid and an antimicrobial is made, the administration of such drugs in combination has the advantage of greater patient compliance and convenience, with the added assurance that the appropriate dosage of both drugs is administered, plus assured compatibility of ingredients when both types of drugs are in the same formulation and, particularly, that the correct volume of drug is delivered and retained.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Pregnancy Category C. Animal reproduction studies have shown an adverse effect on the fetus and there are no adequate and well-controlled studies in humans, but potential benefits may warrant use of the drug in pregnant women despite potential risks.</p><p> Lactation: Unknown if excreted in breast milk; minimal systemic absorption with ophthalmic administration.</p>', 'active', '525_1742687961.jpg', 60, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 04:59:21', '2025-03-23 04:59:21'),
(4241, 'Movifast', 'Macrogol + Electrolytes', 160.00, 1, 'x Each sachet', 'Each sachet', 'Syrup', '(13.125 gm+350.7 mg+178.5 mg+46.6 mg)/sachet', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Movifast</strong></p><p>For use in adults and children over 12 years of age for effective relief from constipation and treatment of chronic constipation. Also effective in resolving faecal impaction, defined as refractory constipation with faecal loading of the rectum and colon.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each 25 ml concentrated oral solution contains:</p><ul><li>Macrogol (3350) 13.125 gm</li><li>Sodium Bicarbonate 178.5 mg</li><li>Sodium Chloride 350.7 mg</li><li>Potassium Chloride 46.6 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Osmotic purgatives</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Macrogol (3350) exerts an osmotic action in the gut, which induces a laxative effect. Macrogol (3350) increases the stool volume, which triggers colon motility via neuromuscular pathways. The physiological consequence is an improved propulsive colonic transportation of the softened stools and a facilitation of defecation. Electrolytes combined with Macrogol (3350) are exchanged across the intestinal barrier (mucosa) with serum electrolytes and excreted without net gain or loss of sodium, potassium, and water. Macrogol (3350) is unchanged along the gut. It is virtually unabsorbed from the gastrointestinal tract.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Movifast</strong></p><p>Measure 25 mL of this oral solution with the measuring cup provided, then add this to 100 mL of water. Any unused diluted solution should be discarded within 24 hours.</p><ul><li><strong>Constipation</strong>: 25 mL of this oral solution added to 100 mL of water once daily (to make a total volume of 125 mL). This may be increased to 2 - 3 doses of 25 mL daily (each 25 mL dose added to 100 mL of water), if required according to individual response.</li><li><strong>Fecal Impaction</strong>: 8 doses of 25 mL daily (each 25 mL dose added to 100 mL of water). A course of treatment for faecal impaction does not normally exceed 3 days.</li><li><strong>Children (12 - 18 years)</strong>: 25 mL of this oral solution added to 100 mL of water once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Movifast</strong></p><p>There is a possibility that the absorption of other medicinal products could be transiently reduced during use with this oral solution. There have been isolated reports of decreased efficacy with some concomitantly administered medicinal products, e.g., anti-epileptics.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Intestinal perforation or obstruction due to structural or functional disorder of the gut wall, ileus, severe inflammatory conditions of the intestinal tract, such as Crohn\'s disease and ulcerative colitis, and toxic megacolon. Hypersensitivity to the active substances.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Movifast</strong></p><p>In the treatment of chronic constipation, diarrhoea or loose stools normally respond to a reduction in dose. Diarrhoea, abdominal distension, anorectal discomfort, and mild vomiting are more often observed during the treatment for faecal impaction. Vomiting may be resolved if the dose is reduced or delayed.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Clinically, no effects during pregnancy are anticipated, since systemic exposure to Macrogol (3350) is negligible. Movifast oral solution can be used during pregnancy.</p><p>No effects on the breastfed newborn/infant are anticipated since the systemic exposure of the breastfeeding woman to Macrogol (3350) is negligible. Movifast oral solution can be used during breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>This medicinal product contains 8.125 mmol of sodium in each dose of 125 mL. The sodium content of this oral solution should be taken into consideration when administering the product to patients on a controlled sodium diet.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Movifast</strong></p><p>Extensive fluid loss by diarrhea or vomiting may require correction of electrolyte disturbances.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30° C and in a place protected from light. Do not refrigerate.</p>', 'active', '1865_1746722595.jpg', 27, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 05:02:44', '2025-05-08 20:43:15'),
(4242, 'Nab-Xelpac', 'Nab-Paclitaxel [Nanoparticle Albumin-Bound Paclitaxel]', 25000.00, 1, 'IV Infusion', '100 mg vial infusion', 'IV Infusion', '100 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nab-Xelpac 100 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Metastatic Breast Cancer</strong>: Nab-Paclitaxel is indicated for the treatment of breast cancer after failure of combination chemotherapy for metastatic disease or relapse within 6 months of adjuvant chemotherapy. Prior therapy should have included an anthracycline unless clinically contraindicated.</li><li><strong>Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer</strong>: Nab-Paclitaxel is indicated for the first-line treatment of locally advanced or metastatic non-small cell lung cancer, in combination with carboplatin, in patients who are not candidates for curative surgery or radiation therapy.</li><li><strong>Adenocarcinoma of the Pancreas</strong>: Nab-Paclitaxel is indicated for the first-line treatment of patients with metastatic adenocarcinoma of the pancreas, in combination with gemcitabine.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Nab-Paclitaxel is a microtubule inhibitor that promotes the assembly of microtubules from tubulin dimers and stabilizes microtubules by preventing depolymerization. This stability results in the inhibition of the normal dynamic reorganization of the microtubule network that is essential for vital interphase and mitotic cellular functions. Paclitaxel induces abnormal arrays or \"bundles\" of microtubules throughout the cell cycle and multiple asters of microtubules during mitosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Nab-Xelpac 100 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Metastatic Breast Cancer</strong>: After failure of combination chemotherapy for metastatic breast cancer or relapse within 6 months of adjuvant chemotherapy, the recommended regimen for Nab-Paclitaxel is 260 mg/m² administered intravenously over 30 minutes every 3 weeks.</li><li><strong>Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer</strong>: The recommended dose of Nab-Paclitaxel is 100 mg/m² administered as an intravenous infusion over 30 minutes on Days 1, 8, and 15 of each 21-day cycle. Administer carboplatin on Day 1 of each 21-day cycle immediately after Nab-Paclitaxel.</li><li><strong>Adenocarcinoma of the Pancreas</strong>: The recommended dose of Nab-Paclitaxel is 125 mg/m² administered as an intravenous infusion over 30-40 minutes on Days 1, 8, and 15 of each 28-day cycle. Administer gemcitabine immediately after Nab-Paclitaxel on Days 1, 8, and 15 of each 28-day cycle.</li><li><strong>Dosage in Patients with Hepatic Impairment</strong>: For patients with mild hepatic impairment (total bilirubin greater than ULN and less than or equal to 1.5 x ULN and AST less than or equal to 10 x ULN), no dose adjustments are required, regardless of indication. Do not administer Nab-Paclitaxel to patients with total bilirubin greater than 5 x ULN or AST greater than 10 x ULN, as these patients have not been studied.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Nab-Xelpac 100 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li>The metabolism of paclitaxel is catalyzed by CYP2C8 and CYP3A4. Caution should be exercised when administering Nab-Paclitaxel concomitantly with medicines known to inhibit (e.g., ketoconazole and other imidazole antifungals, erythromycin, fluoxetine, gemfibrozil, cimetidine, ritonavir, saquinavir, indinavir, and nelfinavir) or induce (e.g., rifampicin, carbamazepine, phenytoin, efavirenz, and nevirapine) either CYP2C8 or CYP3A4.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Nab-Paclitaxel should not be used in patients who have baseline neutrophil counts of &lt;1,500 cells/mm³. Patients who experience a severe hypersensitivity reaction to Nab-Paclitaxel should not be rechallenged with the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Nab-Xelpac 100 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li>The most common adverse reactions (≥20%) with single-agent use of Nab-Paclitaxel in metastatic breast cancer are alopecia, neutropenia, sensory neuropathy, abnormal ECG, fatigue/asthenia, myalgia/arthralgia, AST elevation, alkaline phosphatase elevation, anemia, nausea, infections, and diarrhea.</li><li>The most common adverse reactions (≥20%) of Nab-Paclitaxel in combination with carboplatin for non-small cell lung cancer are anemia, neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, alopecia, peripheral neuropathy, nausea, and fatigue.</li><li>The most common serious adverse reactions of Nab-Paclitaxel in combination with carboplatin for non-small cell lung cancer are anemia (4%) and pneumonia (3%).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category D</strong>: Nab-Paclitaxel can cause fetal harm when administered to a pregnant woman. Administration of paclitaxel formulated as albumin-bound particles to rats during pregnancy at doses lower than the maximum recommended human dose caused embryo-fetal toxicities, including intrauterine mortality, increased resorptions, reduced numbers of live fetuses, and malformations. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women receiving Nab-Paclitaxel. Women of childbearing potential should be advised to avoid becoming pregnant while receiving Nab-Paclitaxel.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether paclitaxel is excreted in human milk. Since many drugs are excreted in human milk and because of the potential for serious adverse reactions in nursing infants, a decision should be made to discontinue nursing or discontinue the drug, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Nab-Xelpac 100 mg/vial</strong></p><ul><li>There is no known antidote for Nab-Paclitaxel overdosage. The primary anticipated complications of overdosage would consist of bone marrow suppression, sensory neurotoxicity, and mucositis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store the vials in original cartons at 20°C to 25°C. Retain in the original package to protect from bright light.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: The safety and effectiveness of Nab-Paclitaxel in pediatric patients have not been evaluated.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No toxicities occurred notably more frequently among patients aged 65 years or older. However, a higher incidence of certain adverse effects, such as epistaxis, diarrhea, dehydration, fatigue, and peripheral edema, was noted in older patients.</li><li><strong>Patients with Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Reduce the starting dose in patients with moderate to severe hepatic impairment. Do not administer Nab-Paclitaxel to patients with total bilirubin &gt; 5 x ULN or AST &gt; 10 x ULN.</li><li><strong>Patients with Renal Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment is required for patients with mild to moderate renal impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '24_1742688318.jpg', 55, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 05:05:18', '2025-03-23 05:05:18'),
(4243, 'Ramoril Plus', 'Ramipril + Hydrochlorothiazide', 8.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg+25 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Ramoril Plus tablet is indicated for the treatment of mild to moderate hypertension in patients (in whom combination therapy is appropriate) who have been stabilized on the individual components given in the same proportion.</p><p> <strong><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></strong></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> <strong>Ramipril</strong> is an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor, which after hydrolysis to ramiprilat, blocks the conversion of angiotensin I to the vasoconstrictor substance, angiotensin II. Inhibition of ACE by ramipril results in decreased plasma angiotensin II, leading to decreased vasopressor activity and decreased aldosterone secretion. Thus, ramipril exerts its antihypertensive activity. It is also effective in the management of heart failure and reduction of the risk of stroke, myocardial infarction, and death from cardiovascular events. It is long-acting and well tolerated, so it can be used in long-term therapy.</p><p><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong> is a thiazide diuretic. Thiazides affect the renal tubular mechanisms of electrolyte reabsorption, directly increasing excretion of sodium and chloride in approximately equivalent amounts. Indirectly, the diuretic action of hydrochlorothiazide reduces plasma volume, with consequent increases in plasma renin activity, increases in aldosterone secretion, increases in urinary potassium loss, and decreases in serum potassium. The renin-aldosterone link is mediated by angiotensin II, so co-administration of an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor tends to reverse the potassium loss associated with these diuretics.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p> A patient whose blood pressure is not adequately controlled with ramipril (or another ACE inhibitor) alone or with hydrochlorothiazide (or another thiazide diuretic) alone may be switched to this combination therapy. The usual starting dose is one tablet once daily. If necessary, the dose may be increased to two tablets once daily.</p><p><strong>Dosage in renal impairment:</strong> In patients with a creatinine clearance between 60 and 30 ml/min, treatment should be initiated with ramipril 1.25 mg monotherapy. If blood pressure is not adequately controlled, the dose of ramipril may be increased to 2.5 mg. If blood pressure is still not controlled, the patient may be switched to one tablet of ramipril BP 2.5 mg and hydrochlorothiazide BP 12.5 mg once daily. Dosage may be titrated upward to ramipril BP 5 mg and hydrochlorothiazide BP 25 mg until blood pressure is controlled.</p><p> <strong><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></strong></p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> <strong>Ramipril:</strong> Concomitant administration with diuretics may lead to serious hypotension and, in addition, dangerous hyperkalemia with potassium-sparing diuretics. Concomitant therapy with lithium may increase the serum lithium concentration. NSAIDs may reduce the antihypertensive effect of ramipril and cause deterioration of renal function.</p><p><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide:</strong> When administered concurrently, the following drugs may interact with thiazide diuretics: alcohol, barbiturates, or narcotics. Antidiabetic drugs (oral agents and insulin) - dosage adjustment of the antidiabetic drug may be required. Other antihypertensive drugs may have an additive effect or potentiation.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> This preparation must not be used in patients with hypersensitivity to ramipril, hydrochlorothiazide, or other thiazide diuretics. It is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>History of hereditary angioneurotic edema</li><li>Severe impairment of renal function</li><li>Haemodynamically relevant unilateral or bilateral renal artery stenosis, mitral stenosis, or aortic stenosis</li><li>Low blood pressure (hypotensive patients) or haemodynamically unstable patients where there might be a risk of a life-threatening fall in blood pressure and renal failure</li><li>Clinically relevant electrolyte disturbances such as hypokalemia, hyponatremia, or hypercalcemia, which may worsen following treatment</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p> <strong>Ramipril</strong> can cause profound hypotension, renal impairment, and a persistent dry cough. It may also cause angioedema (onset may be delayed), rash (which may be associated with pruritus and urticaria), pancreatitis, and upper respiratory-tract symptoms such as sinusitis and rhinitis. Gastrointestinal effects reported with ACE inhibitors include nausea, vomiting, dyspepsia, diarrhea, and constipation. Altered liver function tests, cholestatic jaundice, and hepatitis have been reported. Blood disorders, including thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, neutropenia, and hemolytic anemia, have also been reported. Other reported side effects include headache, dizziness, fatigue, taste disturbance, bronchospasm, fever, and photosensitivity.</p><p><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong> can cause tachycardia, serum sickness, weight loss, maculopapular rash, photosensitivity, flushing, and acidosis.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Pregnancy should be excluded before the start of treatment with ramipril and avoided during treatment. Exposure of the mother to ACE inhibitors in mid or late pregnancy has been associated with oligohydramnios and neonatal hypotension with anuria or renal failure. From animal experiments, it is known that the use of ramipril may cause decreased utero-placental perfusion. There is also a potential risk of fetal or postnatal effects as ACE inhibitors influence the local renin-angiotensin system. In peri-postnatal studies, increased renal pelvic dilatation was observed in the first-generation offspring. However, ramipril was not fetotoxic in studies, although ACE inhibitors have shown fetotoxicity in some species. Ramipril should not be used during lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> This medication should be used with caution in patients with impaired renal function, hyperkalemia, hypotension, surgery/anesthesia, and impaired hepatic function.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p> <strong>Ramipril:</strong> Limited data on human overdosage are available. The most likely clinical manifestations would be symptoms attributable to hypotension. Because the hypotensive effect of ramipril is achieved through vasodilation and effective hypovolemia, it is reasonable to treat ramipril overdosage by infusion of normal saline solution.</p><p><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide:</strong> The most common signs and symptoms observed are those caused by electrolyte depletion (hypokalemia, hypochloremia, and dehydration resulting from excessive diuresis). If digitalis has also been administered, hypokalemia may accentuate cardiac arrhythmias. The degree to which hydrochlorothiazide is removed by hemodialysis has not been established.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Combined antihypertensive preparations</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</p>', 'active', '1056_1742703158.webp', 2, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 09:12:38', '2025-03-23 09:12:38'),
(4244, 'Ramicard Plus', 'Ramipril + Hydrochlorothiazide', 8.04, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5mg+25mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> <strong>Ramicard Plus</strong> tablet is indicated for the treatment of mild to moderate hypertension in patients (in whom combination therapy is appropriate) who have been stabilized on the individual components given in the same proportion.</p><p> <em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন.</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> <strong>Ramipril</strong> is an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor, which after hydrolysis to ramiprilat, blocks the conversion of angiotensin I to the vasoconstrictor substance, angiotensin II. Inhibition of ACE by ramipril results in decreased plasma angiotensin II, leading to decreased vasopressor activity and decreased aldosterone secretion. Thus, ramipril exerts its antihypertensive activity. It is also effective in the management of heart failure and reduction of the risk of stroke, myocardial infarction, and death from cardiovascular events. It is long-acting and well tolerated, making it suitable for long-term therapy.</p><p><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong> is a thiazide diuretic. Thiazides affect the renal tubular mechanisms of electrolyte reabsorption, directly increasing excretion of sodium and chloride in approximately equivalent amounts. Indirectly, the diuretic action of hydrochlorothiazide reduces plasma volume, leading to increased plasma renin activity, increased aldosterone secretion, increased urinary potassium loss, and decreased serum potassium. The renin-aldosterone link is mediated by angiotensin II, so co-administration of an ACE inhibitor tends to reverse the potassium loss associated with these diuretics.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p> A patient whose blood pressure is not adequately controlled with Ramipril (or another ACE inhibitor) alone or with Hydrochlorothiazide (or another thiazide diuretic) alone may be switched to this combination therapy. The usual starting dose is <strong>one tablet once daily</strong>. If necessary, the dose may be increased to <strong>two tablets once daily</strong>.</p><p><strong>Dosage in Renal Impairment:</strong></p><ul><li>In patients with a creatinine clearance between <strong>60 and 30 ml/min</strong>, treatment should be initiated with <strong>Ramipril 1.25 mg monotherapy</strong>.</li><li>If blood pressure is not adequately controlled, the dose of <strong>Ramipril may be increased to 2.5 mg</strong>.</li><li>If blood pressure is still not controlled, the patient may be switched to <strong>one tablet of Ramipril BP 2.5 mg and Hydrochlorothiazide BP 12.5 mg once daily</strong>.</li><li>Dosage may be titrated upward to <strong>Ramipril BP 5 mg and Hydrochlorothiazide BP 25 mg</strong> until blood pressure is controlled.</li></ul><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন.</em></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Drug Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Ramipril</strong>: Concomitant administration with diuretics may lead to serious <strong>hypotension</strong> and dangerous <strong>hyperkalemia</strong> with potassium-sparing diuretics. Concomitant therapy with <strong>lithium</strong> may increase serum lithium concentration. <strong>NSAIDs</strong> may reduce the antihypertensive effect of Ramipril and cause deterioration of renal function.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: When administered concurrently, the following drugs may interact with thiazide diuretics: <strong>alcohol, barbiturates, or narcotics</strong>. <strong>Antidiabetic drugs</strong> (oral agents and insulin) may require dosage adjustment. Other <strong>antihypertensive drugs</strong> may have an additive effect or potentiation.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> This preparation must not be used in patients with:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to <strong>ramipril, hydrochlorothiazide, or other thiazide diuretics</strong>.</li><li>History of <strong>hereditary angioneurotic edema</strong>.</li><li><strong>Severe impairment of renal function</strong>.</li><li><strong>Haemodynamically relevant unilateral or bilateral renal artery stenosis, mitral stenosis, aortic stenosis</strong>.</li><li>Patients with <strong>low blood pressure (hypotensive patients)</strong> or an <strong>unstable circulatory situation</strong>.</li><li>Clinically relevant <strong>electrolyte disturbances</strong> such as <strong>hypokalemia, hyponatremia, or hypercalcemia</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Ramipril</strong> may cause <strong>profound hypotension, renal impairment, and a persistent dry cough</strong>. Other side effects include <strong>angioedema, rash, pancreatitis, sinusitis, rhinitis, nausea, vomiting, dyspepsia, diarrhea, constipation, altered liver function tests, headache, dizziness, fatigue, taste disturbance, bronchospasm, fever, and photosensitivity</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong> may cause <strong>tachycardia, serum sickness, weight loss, maculopapular rash, photosensitivity, flushing, and acidosis</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Pregnancy should be excluded before starting treatment with Ramipril and avoided during treatment. Exposure to <strong>ACE inhibitors</strong> during mid or late pregnancy has been associated with <strong>oligohydramnios and neonatal hypotension</strong>.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Ramipril should <strong>not be used during lactation</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> This should be used with caution in patients with:</p><ul><li><strong>Impaired renal function</strong></li><li><strong>Hyperkalemia</strong></li><li><strong>Hypotension</strong></li><li><strong>Surgery/anesthesia</strong></li><li><strong>Impaired hepatic function</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Ramipril</strong>: Overdosage may cause <strong>severe hypotension</strong>, which should be treated by <strong>infusion of normal saline solution</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hydrochlorothiazide</strong>: Overdosage may lead to <strong>electrolyte depletion (hypokalemia, hypochloremia, dehydration)</strong>. If digitalis has also been administered, <strong>hypokalemia may accentuate cardiac arrhythmias</strong>. The extent of <strong>hemodialysis removal of hydrochlorothiazide</strong> is not well established.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Combined antihypertensive preparations</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a <strong>cool and dry place</strong>, protected from light.</p>', 'active', '567_1742703397.webp', 9, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 09:16:37', '2025-03-23 09:16:37'),
(4245, 'Neoclomide', 'Cyclophosphamide', 170.00, 1, 'x 200 mg vial', '200 mg vial', 'IV Infusion', '200 mg/vial', 'yes', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications</strong></h3><p>Neoclomide, although effective alone in susceptible malignancies, is more frequently used concurrently or sequentially with other antineoplastic drugs. The following malignancies are often susceptible to Neoclomide treatment:</p><ul><li><strong>Malignant lymphomas</strong> (Stages III and IV of the Ann Arbor staging system), Hodgkin’s disease, lymphocytic lymphoma (nodular or diffuse), mixed-cell type lymphoma, histiocytic lymphoma, Burkitt’s lymphoma.</li><li><strong>Multiple myeloma</strong></li><li><strong>Leukemias:</strong> Chronic lymphocytic leukemia, chronic granulocytic leukemia (it is usually ineffective in acute blastic crisis), acute myelogenous and monocytic leukemia, acute lymphoblastic (stem-cell) leukemia in children (Neoclomide given during remission is effective in prolonging its duration).</li><li><strong>Mycosis fungoides</strong> (advanced disease).</li><li><strong>Neuroblastoma</strong> (disseminated disease).</li><li><strong>Adenocarcinoma of the ovary</strong></li><li><strong>Retinoblastoma</strong></li><li><strong>Carcinoma of the breast</strong></li></ul><h4><strong>Nonmalignant Disease:</strong></h4><p><strong>Biopsy Proven \"Minimal Change\" Nephrotic Syndrome in Children</strong></p><p>Neoclomide is useful in carefully selected cases of biopsy-proven \"minimal change\" nephrotic syndrome in children but should not be used as primary therapy. In children whose disease fails to respond adequately to appropriate adrenocorticosteroid therapy or in whom the adrenocorticosteroid therapy produces or threatens to produce intolerable side effects, Neoclomide may induce a remission. Neoclomide is <strong>not indicated</strong> for nephrotic syndrome in adults or for any other renal disease.</p><p><strong><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></strong></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Cyclophosphamide is biotransformed principally in the liver to active alkylating metabolites by a mixed-function microsomal oxidase system. These metabolites interfere with the growth of susceptible rapidly proliferating malignant cells. The mechanism of action is thought to involve cross-linking of tumor cell DNA.</p><ul><li><strong>Absorption:</strong> Well absorbed after oral administration with a bioavailability greater than 75%.</li><li><strong>Elimination half-life:</strong> 3 to 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Excretion:</strong> Primarily in the form of metabolites; 5-25% of the dose is excreted in urine as unchanged drug.</li><li><strong>Metabolite concentrations:</strong> Peak in plasma 2 to 3 hours after an intravenous dose.</li><li><strong>Plasma protein binding:</strong> Low for the unchanged drug but some metabolites are bound &gt;60%.</li><li><strong>Renal failure impact:</strong> Elevated metabolite levels observed, but no increased clinical toxicity demonstrated.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><h4><strong>Treatment of Malignant Diseases (Adults and Children)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Intravenous (IV) Regimens:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">40 to 50 mg/kg in divided doses over <strong>2 to 5 days</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 to 15 mg/kg <strong>every 7 to 10 days</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">3 to 5 mg/kg <strong>twice weekly</strong>.</li><li><strong>Oral Regimens:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Initial and maintenance dosing:</strong> 1 to 5 mg/kg/day.</li></ul><p>Dosages must be adjusted based on <strong>antitumor activity</strong> and/or <strong>leukopenia</strong>. <strong>Leukocyte count</strong> is a key dosage guide:</p><ul><li><strong>Short courses:</strong> Total white blood cell count may drop to <strong>2000 cells/mm³</strong> without serious risk of infection.</li><li><strong>Continuing therapy:</strong> Reduction to <strong>3000 cells/mm³</strong> is tolerable unless granulocytopenia develops.</li></ul><p>Patients with <strong>compromised renal function</strong> may require <strong>pharmacokinetic adjustments</strong>, but no consistent evidence supports dosage modification.</p><h4><strong>Treatment of Nonmalignant Diseases (Biopsy Proven \"Minimal Change\" Nephrotic Syndrome in Children)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Oral dose:</strong> 2.5 to 3 mg/kg daily for <strong>60 to 90 days</strong>.</li><li><strong>Duration &amp; Risks:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Beyond 60 days:</strong> Increased risk of oligospermia and azoospermia.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Beyond 90 days:</strong> Higher probability of sterility.</li></ul><p>Adrenocorticosteroid therapy may be <strong>tapered and discontinued</strong> during cyclophosphamide treatment.</p><p><strong><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></strong></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Interaction</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Phenobarbital:</strong> Chronic high doses increase Neoclomide metabolism and leukopenic activity.</li><li><strong>Cholinesterase inhibition:</strong> Neoclomide potentiates the effect of <strong>succinylcholine chloride</strong>.</li><li><strong>Anesthesia caution:</strong> If Neoclomide was used within <strong>10 days</strong> before general anesthesia, the anesthesiologist should be informed.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><p>Neoclomide <strong>should not be used</strong> in:</p><ul><li><strong>Severely depressed bone marrow function</strong>.</li><li><strong>Patients with previous hypersensitivity</strong> to cyclophosphamide.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><h4><strong>Digestive System:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Nausea, vomiting.</li><li><strong>Less Frequent:</strong> Anorexia, abdominal pain, diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Hemorrhagic colitis, oral mucosal ulceration, jaundice.</li></ul><h4><strong>Skin &amp; Its Structures:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Alopecia (hair regrowth may differ in texture or color).</li><li><strong>Occasional:</strong> Skin rash, pigmentation, nail changes.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Stevens-Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis.</li></ul><h4><strong>Hematopoietic System:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Leukopenia (dose-related).</li><li><strong>Occasional:</strong> Thrombocytopenia, anemia.</li><li><strong>Recovery:</strong> Begins <strong>7 to 10 days</strong> after therapy cessation.</li></ul><h4><strong>Urinary System:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Possible:</strong> Hemorrhagic ureteritis, renal tubular necrosis (resolves after stopping treatment).</li></ul><h4><strong>Respiratory System:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Reported:</strong> Interstitial pneumonitis, interstitial pulmonary fibrosis (with prolonged high-dose use).</li></ul><h4><strong>Other:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Severe cases:</strong> Anaphylactic reactions, SIADH (syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion).</li><li><strong>Postmarketing reports:</strong> Malaise, asthenia, fever without documented infection in neutropenic patients.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category:</strong> <strong>D</strong> (Risk to fetus).</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong> Cyclophosphamide is excreted in breast milk. <strong>Discontinue drug or nursing</strong>, depending on importance to the mother.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><p><strong>Monitor closely in patients with:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Leukopenia</strong></li><li><strong>Thrombocytopenia</strong></li><li><strong>Bone marrow infiltration by tumor cells</strong></li><li><strong>Previous X-ray or cytotoxic therapy</strong></li><li><strong>Impaired hepatic/renal function</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><h4><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong></h4><ul><li>Safety profile <strong>similar</strong> to adults.</li></ul><h4><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong></h4><ul><li>Elderly patients <strong>may be more susceptible</strong> to Neoclomide toxicities.</li><li><strong>Dose selection should be cautious</strong>, starting at the lower end of the range.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>No specific antidote</strong> available.</li><li>Supportive management required for <strong>infections, myelosuppression, or cardiac toxicity</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p><strong>Cytotoxic Chemotherapy</strong></p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store <strong>below 25°C</strong> in a <strong>dry place</strong>.</li><li><strong>Protect from light &amp; moisture</strong>.</li><li><strong>Do not freeze</strong>.</li></ul>', 'active', '984_1742703794.jpg', 1, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 09:23:14', '2025-05-05 08:21:10'),
(4246, 'Cyclotox', 'Cyclophosphamide', 180.00, 1, 'unit', '1 unit', 'IV Infusion', '200mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> Cyclotox, although effective alone in susceptible malignancies, is more frequently used concurrently or sequentially with other antineoplastic drugs. The following malignancies are often susceptible to Cyclotox treatment:</p><ul><li><strong>Malignant lymphomas</strong> (Stages III and IV of the Ann Arbor staging system), Hodgkin’s disease, lymphocytic lymphoma (nodular or diffuse), mixed-cell type lymphoma, histiocytic lymphoma, Burkitt’s lymphoma.</li><li><strong>Multiple myeloma</strong></li><li><strong>Leukemias:</strong> Chronic lymphocytic leukemia, chronic granulocytic leukemia (it is usually ineffective in acute blastic crisis), acute myelogenous and monocytic leukemia, acute lymphoblastic (stem-cell) leukemia in children (Cyclotox given during remission is effective in prolonging its duration).</li><li><strong>Mycosis fungoides</strong> (advanced disease).</li><li><strong>Neuroblastoma</strong> (disseminated disease).</li><li><strong>Adenocarcinoma of the ovary</strong></li><li><strong>Retinoblastoma</strong></li><li><strong>Carcinoma of the breast</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Nonmalignant Disease:</strong> <strong>Biopsy Proven \"Minimal Change\" Nephrotic Syndrome in Children</strong>: Cyclotox is useful in carefully selected cases of biopsy-proven \"minimal change\" nephrotic syndrome in children but should not be used as primary therapy. In children whose disease fails to respond adequately to appropriate adrenocorticosteroid therapy or in whom the adrenocorticosteroid therapy produces or threatens to produce intolerable side effects, Cyclotox may induce a remission. Cyclotox is not indicated for the nephrotic syndrome in adults or for any other renal disease.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Cyclophosphamide is biotransformed principally in the liver to active alkylating metabolites by a mixed function microsomal oxidase system. These metabolites interfere with the growth of susceptible rapidly proliferating malignant cells. The mechanism of action is thought to involve cross-linking of tumor cell DNA. Cyclophosphamide is well absorbed after oral administration with a bioavailability greater than 75%. The unchanged drug has an elimination half-life of 3 to 12 hours. It is eliminated primarily in the form of metabolites, but from 5 to 25% of the dose is excreted in urine as unchanged drug. Several cytotoxic and noncytotoxic metabolites have been identified in urine and in plasma. Concentrations of metabolites reach a maximum in plasma 2 to 3 hours after an intravenous dose. Plasma protein binding of unchanged drug is low but some metabolites are bound to an extent greater than 60%. It has not been demonstrated that any single metabolite is responsible for either the therapeutic or toxic effects of cyclophosphamide. Although elevated levels of metabolites of cyclophosphamide have been observed in patients with renal failure, increased clinical toxicity in such patients has not been demonstrated.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p> <strong>Treatment of Malignant Diseases:</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults and Children:</strong> When used as the only oncolytic drug therapy, the initial course of cyclophosphamide for patients with no hematologic deficiency usually consists of 40 to 50 mg/kg given intravenously in divided doses over a period of 2 to 5 days. Other intravenous regimens include 10 to 15 mg/kg given every 7 to 10 days or 3 to 5 mg/kg twice weekly. Oral cyclophosphamide dosing is usually in the range of 1 to 5 mg/kg/day for both initial and maintenance dosing.</p><p>Many other regimens of intravenous and oral cyclophosphamide have been reported. Dosages must be adjusted in accord with evidence of antitumor activity and/or leukopenia. The total leukocyte count is a good, objective guide for regulating dosage. Transient decreases in the total white blood cell count to 2000 cells/mm³ (following short courses) or more persistent reduction to 3000 cells/mm³ (with continuing therapy) are tolerated without serious risk of infection if there is no marked granulocytopenia.</p><p>When cyclophosphamide is included in combined cytotoxic regimens, it may be necessary to reduce the dose of cyclophosphamide as well as that of the other drugs. Cyclophosphamide and its metabolites are dialyzable although there are probably quantitative differences depending upon the dialysis system being used. Patients with compromised renal function may show some measurable changes in pharmacokinetic parameters of cyclophosphamide metabolism, but there is no consistent evidence indicating a need for cyclophosphamide dosage modification in patients with renal function impairment.</p><p><strong>Treatment of Nonmalignant Diseases:</strong> <strong>Biopsy Proven \"Minimal Change\" Nephrotic Syndrome in Children:</strong> An oral dose of 2.5 to 3 mg/kg daily for a period of 60 to 90 days is recommended. In males, the incidence of oligospermia and azoospermia increases if the duration of cyclophosphamide treatment exceeds 60 days. Treatment beyond 90 days increases the probability of sterility. Adrenocorticosteroid therapy may be tapered and discontinued during the course of cyclophosphamide therapy.</p><p><em>রেজিস্টার্ড চিকিৎসকের পরামর্শ মোতাবেক ঔষধ সেবন করুন</em></p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p> The rate of metabolism and the leukopenic activity of Cyclotox reportedly are increased by chronic administration of high doses of phenobarbital. The physician should be alert for possible combined drug actions, desirable or undesirable, involving Cyclotox even though Cyclotox has been used successfully concurrently with other drugs, including other cytotoxic drugs. Cyclotox treatment, which causes a marked and persistent inhibition of cholinesterase activity, potentiates the effect of succinylcholine chloride. If a patient has been treated with Cyclotox within 10 days of general anesthesia, the anesthesiologist should be alerted.</p><p>This format keeps the headings bold while keeping everything else in normal text. Let me know if you need any further modifications!</p><p><br></p><p>4o</p>', 'active', '1295_1742704118.webp', 55, 12, 'mdlikhon202324@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 09:28:38', '2025-03-23 09:28:38');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4247, 'CTZ', 'Cetirizine Hydrochloride', 2.00, 15, 'tablet', '15 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>CTZ 10 mg</strong> is primarily used for the treatment of symptoms related to allergies and certain skin conditions. Here’s a detailed overview based on the information provided:</p><h3><strong>Indications of CTZ 10 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</strong>: Relieves symptoms like sneezing, runny nose, and itching associated with allergies.</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>: Treats skin conditions like hives.</li><li><strong>Allergen-Induced Asthma</strong>: Helps manage asthma symptoms triggered by allergens.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Sedating Anti-histamine</strong>: CTZ is an antihistamine, meaning it works by blocking histamine receptors, which play a key role in allergic reactions.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>H1 Receptor Antagonist</strong>: CTZ blocks H1 receptors involved in allergic reactions, reducing symptoms.</li><li><strong>Low Sedation</strong>: Though it\'s a sedating antihistamine, it has minimal sedative effects at normal doses and does not induce significant drowsiness or behavioral changes.</li><li><strong>Reduces Allergic Reaction Phases</strong>: It helps prevent both the early (histamine-driven) and late (inflammatory cell-driven) phases of allergic reactions.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Adults and Children (6 years and older)</strong>: 1 tablet or 2 teaspoonfuls daily, or 1 teaspoonful twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children (2–6 years)</strong>: 1 teaspoonful once daily or 1/2 teaspoonful twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children (6 months to 2 years)</strong>: 1/2 teaspoonful once daily.</li><li><strong>Children (12–23 months)</strong>: 1/2 teaspoonful every 12 hours, up to a maximum dose.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>No Significant Interactions</strong>: No clinically significant interactions with drugs like Theophylline, Azithromycin, Pseudoephedrine, Ketoconazole, or Erythromycin.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Not suitable for patients with a known allergy to Cetirizine or Hydroxyzine.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Common Side Effect</strong>: The most frequent side effect is somnolence (drowsiness), though it is less pronounced than in many other antihistamines.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: US FDA Category B. It should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed, as there are no sufficient studies on human pregnancy outcomes.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Cetirizine is excreted in breast milk, so it should be used cautiously when breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Driving and Machinery</strong>: Use caution when operating heavy machinery or driving, as even minimal sedation may impair performance.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Storage</strong>: Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children to prevent accidental ingestion.</li></ul><p>This medication is commonly used to relieve symptoms of allergic reactions but should be used cautiously in certain populations, particularly pregnant or breastfeeding women, and when engaging in activities requiring full alertness.</p>', 'active', '1104_1742708700.webp', 33, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 10:45:00', '2025-05-03 01:43:26'),
(4248, 'Reefcal-DX', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Reefcal-DX 600 mg+400 IU</strong></p><p>This combination is used for the treatment of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, rickets, tetany, and parathyroid disease. Calcium supplements are often used to ensure adequate dietary intake in conditions such as pregnancy &amp; lactation, osteogenesis, and tooth formation (adjunct with definite treatment), and therapy with anti-seizure medications. It is also used as a routine supplement and phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Reefcal-DX 600 mg+400 IU is a preparation of Calcium Carbonate and Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol). Calcium is necessary for many normal functions of the body, especially bone formation and maintenance. Vitamin D3 helps the absorption &amp; reabsorption of Calcium and also stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies have shown that Calcium and Vitamin D3 together help in bone growth and in the prevention of osteoporosis &amp; bone fractures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Reefcal-DX 600 mg+400 IU</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 200 IU Tablet</strong>: 2 tablets daily or 1 tablet twice daily. Reefcal-DX 600 mg+400 IU is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li><li><strong>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 400 IU Tablet</strong>: 1 tablet twice daily. It is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Reefcal-DX 600 mg+400 IU</strong></p><p>It has possible interactions with calcium, aluminium, or magnesium-containing antacids &amp; other calcium supplements, calcitriol &amp; other vitamin D3 supplements; digoxin, tetracycline, doxycycline, minocycline, or oxytetracycline.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Reefcal-DX 600 mg+400 IU is contraindicated in the case of hypercalcaemia, hyperthyroidism, renal calculi &amp; nephrolithiasis, and Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Reefcal-DX 600 mg+400 IU</strong></p><p>Reefcal-DX 600 mg+400 IU is generally well tolerated. If symptoms such as nausea, vomiting, stomach cramps, dry mouth, increased thirst, and increased urination occur, inform a physician. Constipation may also occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This combination should be used as directed by a physician during pregnancy or while breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>If there is any pre-existing heart disease or kidney disease, precautions should be taken.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Reefcal-DX 600 mg+400 IU</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdosage may include nausea and vomiting, severe drowsiness, dry mouth, loss of appetite, metallic taste, stomach cramps, diarrhea, headache, and constipation.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1310_1742708849.jpg', 27, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 10:47:29', '2025-05-04 15:08:16'),
(4249, 'Avamist', 'Fluticasone Furoate', 275.00, 1, 'x 120 metered sprays', '120 metered sprays', 'Nasal Spray', '27.5 mcg/spray', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Avamist 27.5 mcg/spray</h3><p>Avamist 27.5 mcg/spray Nasal Spray is indicated for the treatment of symptoms of <strong>seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis</strong> in patients aged <strong>2 years and older</strong>.</p><p>Avamist 27.5 mcg/spray inhalation powder is indicated for the <strong>once-daily maintenance treatment of asthma</strong> as prophylactic therapy in patients aged <strong>5 years and older</strong>. This is <strong>not</strong> indicated for the relief of acute bronchospasm.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Nasal Steroid Preparations</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Avamist 27.5 mcg/spray is a <strong>synthetic trifluorinated corticosteroid</strong> with potent anti-inflammatory activity. Like other corticosteroids, Avamist 27.5 mcg/spray has a wide range of actions on multiple cell types (e.g., mast cells, eosinophils, neutrophils, macrophages, lymphocytes) and mediators (e.g., histamine, eicosanoids, leukotrienes, cytokines) involved in inflammation.</p><h3>Dosage of Avamist 27.5 mcg/spray</h3><h4><strong>Avamist 27.5 mcg/spray Nasal Spray:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years:</strong> 2 sprays in each nostril once a day. In some cases, 2 sprays into each nostril twice daily, not exceeding 4 sprays.</li><li><strong>Children under 12 years (2-11 Years):</strong> 1 spray in each nostril once a day.</li><li><strong>Children (under 2 years of age):</strong> No data to recommend use.</li></ul><h4><strong>Avamist 27.5 mcg/spray Inhalation Capsule:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>1 inhalation once daily by the orally inhaled route.</strong></li><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents Aged 12 Years and Older:</strong> The starting dosage for asthma treatment should be based on asthma severity. The usual starting dose is 100 mcg for those not on an inhaled corticosteroid (ICS). Dosage may be increased to 200 mcg after 2 weeks if the response is insufficient.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients Aged 5 to 11 Years:</strong> 50 mcg once daily.</li></ul><h3>Administration of Avamist 27.5 mcg/spray</h3><h4><strong>How to use the Nasal Spray:</strong></h4><ol><li>Shake the bottle gently and remove the dust cover.</li><li>Hold the spray with your forefinger and middle finger on either side of the nozzle and your thumb underneath the bottle.</li><li>Press down until a fine spray appears.</li><li>If using for the first time, or after 30 days without use, press the nasal applicator several times until a fine mist is seen.</li><li>Gently blow the nose to clear the nostrils.</li><li>Close one nostril, insert the applicator into the other nostril, and tilt your head forward slightly.</li><li>While breathing in through your nose, press the applicator firmly to release a spray.</li><li>Repeat in the other nostril.</li><li><strong>Cleaning:</strong> Wash the nasal applicator and dust cover in warm water at least once a week.</li></ol><h3>Interaction of Avamist 27.5 mcg/spray</h3><ul><li><strong>Potent inhibitors of cytochrome P450 3A4 (CYP3A4)</strong> may increase exposure to Avamist 27.5 mcg/spray. Coadministration with <strong>ritonavir</strong> is not recommended.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to any of Avamist 27.5 mcg/spray\'s ingredients.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Avamist 27.5 mcg/spray</h3><ul><li><strong>Common side effects</strong> for the nasal spray include drying of the nose and increased incidence of nosebleeds.</li><li><strong>Common adverse reactions</strong> for the inhalation powder (≥5% of patients) include nasopharyngitis, bronchitis, upper respiratory tract infection, and headache.</li><li><strong>Pediatric reactions (≥3% of patients aged 5-11 years)</strong> include pharyngitis, bronchitis, and viral infection.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Category C. Avamist 27.5 mcg/spray should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> It is not known whether Avamist 27.5 mcg/spray is excreted in human breast milk, but caution should be exercised when administering to nursing women.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Rarely, <strong>immediate hypersensitivity reactions</strong> or <strong>contact dermatitis</strong> may occur.</li><li>There have been reports of <strong>wheezing</strong>, <strong>nasal septum perforation</strong>, <strong>cataracts</strong>, <strong>glaucoma</strong>, and increased <strong>intraocular pressure</strong> following intranasal corticosteroid use.</li><li><strong>Systemic effects</strong> are minimal with recommended doses, but larger doses should be avoided.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store at <strong>room temperature</strong>, not exceeding <strong>30°C</strong>. Do not refrigerate. Protect from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness for children under 2 years have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric use:</strong> Caution is needed due to the greater likelihood of decreased hepatic, renal, or cardiac function, and other diseases or drug therapies.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment:</strong> Use with caution in severe hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment required for renal impairment.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Avamist 27.5 mcg/spray is a <strong>synthetic corticosteroid</strong> that acts on a wide range of cells and mediators involved in inflammation, helping to reduce symptoms of allergic rhinitis and asthma.</p>', 'active', '212_1742708978.jpg', 11, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 10:49:38', '2025-05-03 00:09:17'),
(4250, 'Domiren', 'Domperidone Maleate', 3.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Domiren 10 mg</strong></p><p>Dyspeptic symptom complex, often linked with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, may include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric sensation of fullness</li><li>Abdominal distension</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Belching</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Early satiety</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms can occur in various conditions, such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine. Additionally, in Parkinson\'s disease, it can manifest as dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting. For radiological studies, it can be used to speed up barium transit in follow-through radiological examinations.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Motility Stimulants</li><li>Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist</li><li>Prokinetic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Domiren 10 mg functions as a dopamine receptor antagonist. It exerts gastroprokinetic action by inhibiting dopamine receptors found in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier means it has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Domiren 10 mg</strong></p><p>For adults, the recommended dosage is 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily. Children should take 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily. When used for acute nausea and vomiting, the maximum treatment duration should not exceed 12 weeks. It is advisable to take this medication 15-30 minutes before a meal.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Domiren 10 mg</strong></p><p>Domiren 10 mg should be taken 15-30 minutes before meals, and if needed, before bedtime. The typical recommended oral dosage of Domperidone is as follows:</p><p><strong>For Adults</strong>:</p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li>The maximum daily dose of Domperidone is 80 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children</strong>:</p><ul><li>2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg of body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>In cases of dyspeptic symptoms: <strong>For Adults</strong>:</p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children</strong>:</p><ul><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>For acute and sub-acute conditions, especially in cases of acute nausea and vomiting: <strong>For Adults</strong>:</p><ul><li>20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children</strong>:</p><ul><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>When administered rectally in the form of suppositories:</p><p><strong>For Adults (including elderly)</strong>:</p><ul><li>30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children</strong>:</p><ul><li>The maximum daily rectal dose for children weighing between 10 to 25 kg is 30 mg. The dose may be divided throughout the day if necessary.</li></ul><p>Please note that the maximum treatment period for acute nausea and vomiting is 12 weeks.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Domiren 10 mg</strong></p><p>The simultaneous use of anticholinergic drugs may counteract the antidyspeptic effects of domperidone. It\'s important to note that antacids and antisecretory drugs should not be administered concurrently with domperidone as they can reduce its oral bioavailability. Domperidone primarily undergoes metabolism through the CYP3A4 enzyme. In vitro data suggest that coadministration with drugs significantly inhibiting this enzyme may lead to elevated plasma levels of domperidone. Examples of CYP3A4 inhibitors include azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, nefazodone, and others.</p><p>Theoretically, as domperidone has gastro-kinetic effects, it could potentially impact the absorption of concurrently orally administered drugs, especially those with sustained release or enteric-coated formulations. However, in patients already stabilized on medications like digoxin or paracetamol, the concurrent use of domperidone did not affect the blood levels of these drugs. Domperidone does not potentiate the action of neuroleptics, and it suppresses the unwanted peripheral effects such as digestive disorders, nausea, and vomiting caused by dopaminergic agonists like bromocriptine and L-dopa without counteracting their central properties.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Domiren 10 mg is not to be used by patients with a known hypersensitivity to this medication or in the case of neonates. It should also be avoided in situations where gastrointestinal stimulation could be unsafe, such as in cases of gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation. Additionally, it is contraindicated in patients with a prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma).</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Domiren 10 mg</strong></p><p>Side effects are uncommon, with occasional reports of transient intestinal cramps. Extrapyramidal symptoms are rare among young children and exceptionally rare in adults. These symptoms typically resolve spontaneously and completely upon discontinuing the treatment. Since the pituitary gland is situated outside the blood-brain barrier, domperidone may lead to an increase in plasma prolactin levels. In rare cases, this hyperprolactinemia can result in neuroendocrinological effects like galactorrhea and gynecomastia. In situations where the blood-brain barrier is immature (as in infants) or compromised, the possibility of neurological side effects cannot be entirely ruled out. Additionally, there have been occasional reports of rare allergic reactions, including rash and urticaria.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of using Domiren 10 mg during pregnancy has not been established; hence, its use is not recommended during pregnancy. Animal studies have not shown any teratogenic effects on the fetus. Domperidone may induce galactorrhea and enhance post-natal lactation. It is excreted in breast milk in minimal quantities, which are insufficient to be considered harmful.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Domiren 10 mg should be employed with utmost care in children due to the potential for an increased risk of extrapyramidal reactions, which can be more pronounced in young children with an incompletely developed blood-brain barrier. Additionally, considering that domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should also be used cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Domiren 10 mg</strong></p><p>Signs of an overdose can manifest as drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions, particularly in children. If an overdose occurs, it is advisable to administer activated charcoal and closely monitor the patient. The use of anticholinergic drugs, antiparkinson drugs, or antihistamines with anticholinergic properties may be beneficial in managing extrapyramidal reactions.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, Protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Motility Stimulants</li><li>Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist</li><li>Prokinetic drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Domiren 10 mg acts as a dopamine antagonist, primarily by blocking dopamine receptors located in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its gastroprokinetic effect is achieved by inhibiting dopamine receptors that influence the motility of the gastrointestinal tract. Because it has limited ability to penetrate the blood-brain barrier, Domperidone has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects. Domperidone restores normal motility and tone in the upper gastrointestinal tract, promotes gastric emptying, enhances antral and duodenal peristalsis, and regulates pylorus contraction. Additionally, it increases esophageal peristalsis and lowers esophageal sphincter pressure, preventing the regurgitation of gastric contents.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p><strong>Use in infants:</strong> Given that the metabolic and blood-brain barrier functions are not fully developed during the first months of life, any medication should be administered to infants with great caution and under close medical supervision. Although the typical absence of neurological side effects with domperidone is primarily due to its limited penetration through the blood-brain barrier, the potential occurrence of such effects cannot be entirely ruled out in infants under 1 year of age.</p><p><strong>Use in liver disorders:</strong> Because domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should be employed cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Use in kidney disorders:</strong> In individuals with severe renal insufficiency (serum creatinine &gt;6 mg/100ml, i.e., &gt;0.6 mmol/l), the elimination half-life of domperidone increases from 7.4 to 20.8 hours, although plasma drug levels remain lower than in healthy volunteers. Since very little unchanged drug is excreted through the kidneys, it is unlikely that the dose of a single acute administration needs to be adjusted in patients with renal insufficiency. However, for repeated administration, the dosing frequency should be reduced to once or twice daily, depending on the severity of the impairment, and the dose may need to be lowered. In general, patients on prolonged therapy should undergo regular reviews.</p>', 'active', '1465_1742709077.png', 6, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 10:51:17', '2025-05-03 09:09:55'),
(4251, 'Xenole', 'Naproxen Sodium + Esomeprazole Magnesium', 15.00, 15, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xenole 500 mg</strong></p><p>It is indicated for the relief of signs and symptoms of osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis, and to decrease the risk of developing gastric ulcers in patients at risk of developing NSAID-associated gastric ulcers.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>It is a preparation of Naproxen and Esomeprazole.</p><ul><li><strong>Naproxen</strong>: An NSAID with analgesic and antipyretic activities. It inhibits prostaglandin synthesis.</li><li><strong>Esomeprazole</strong>: A proton pump inhibitor that suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the H+/K+ ATPase in the gastric parietal cell. By acting specifically on the proton pump, Esomeprazole blocks the final step in acid production, thus reducing gastric acidity.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xenole 500 mg</strong></p><p>The recommended dose is one tablet twice daily. Do not split, chew, crush, or dissolve the tablet. This tablet is to be taken at least 30 minutes before meals.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Xenole 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ACE Inhibitors, diuretics, and beta-blockers</strong>: May reduce the antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin</strong>: Increases the risk of nephrotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Lithium</strong>: Increases plasma levels of lithium.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: May increase the serum levels of methotrexate.</li><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>: May increase the risk of bleeding complications.</li><li><strong>Digoxin</strong>: May increase digoxin toxicity.</li><li><strong>Clopidogrel</strong>: May decrease the exposure to the active metabolite of clopidogrel.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any components of this product. It is also contraindicated in patients who have experienced asthma, urticaria, or allergic-type reactions after taking aspirin or other NSAIDs, and for the treatment of perioperative pain in the setting of coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xenole 500 mg</strong></p><p>The most common side effects are gastritis, erosive gastritis, dyspepsia, diarrhea, gastric ulcer, upper abdominal pain, and nausea, etc.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of this tablet should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Fatal cardiovascular thrombotic events like myocardial infarction, stroke, hypertension, congestive heart failure, and edema.</li><li>Serious GI adverse events.</li><li>Hypomagnesaemia.</li><li>Renal or hepatic insufficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1522_1742709204.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 10:53:24', '2025-05-05 18:12:01'),
(4252, 'Aristo D3', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 35.00, 7, 'capsule', '7 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '40000 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Aristo D3 40000 IU</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated in the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also indicated as an adjunct to specific therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcaemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with vitamin D3 deficiency. Colecalciferol, a synthetic form of Vitamin D, is essential for normal bone growth and development and maintaining bone density. It is also necessary for the proper utilization of both calcium and phosphorus. Babies need Vitamin D3 for healthy growth and development. It acts as a hormone.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin-D preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is crucial for normal bone growth and maintaining bone density. It also helps reduce the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, reduces the risk of cancers (such as breast and colorectal cancer), and helps maintain adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Aristo D3 40000 IU</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>For CapsuleAdults:</strong><em>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</em> 40000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks. Maintenance therapy dose: 1400-2000 IU/day. To confirm the target level of 25-hydroxyvitamin D, measurement should be done 3-4 months after initiating maintenance therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><em>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</em> 20000 IU every 4 weeks. Higher doses may be required in specific situations.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Adjunct therapy for osteoporosis:</em> 20000 IU once a month.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Children (12-18 years):</strong><em>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</em> 20000 IU once every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</em> 20000 IU every 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Film-coated Tablet (1000 IU/tablet):</strong>Take 1-2 tablets daily or as directed by the physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Chewable Tablets (1000-2000 IU):</strong>Take 1000 IU to 2000 IU daily, or as directed by the physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\"><strong>For Syrup:</strong><em>For patients at risk of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</em>0-1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml)</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-2\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml)</blockquote><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-3\"><em>For Vitamin D3 deficient patients:</em>0-1 year: 2000 IU/day (+50000 IU/week) for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 years: 2000 IU/day for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Injection (Prevention):</strong><em>Infants receiving Vitamin D enriched milk:</em> 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) i.e. 100000 IU every 6 months</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><em>Nursed infants or infants not receiving Vitamin D enriched milk or young children up to 5 years:</em> 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e. 200000 IU every 6 months</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><em>Adolescents:</em> 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e. 200000 IU every 6 months during winter</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><em>Pregnancy:</em> 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) i.e. 100000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><em>Elderly:</em> 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) i.e. 100000 IU every 3 months</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>For digestive disorders, concomitant treatment with antiepileptics, and other specific conditions:</em> 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) i.e. 100000 IU or 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e. 200000 IU every 3 or 6 months</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Aristo D3 40000 IU</strong></p><p>It interferes with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (such as liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Aristo D3 40000 IU</strong></p><p>Common side effects include hypercalcaemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, and abdominal pain.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Studies have shown that doses up to 4000 IU are safe during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU, though higher doses may be required for those deficient in Vitamin D3. During pregnancy, women should follow their physician’s advice, as requirements may vary depending on the severity of the disease and treatment response. Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. No overdose in infants from nursing mothers has been observed, but when prescribing additional Vitamin D3 to a breastfed child, the practitioner should consider the dose of any additional Vitamin D3 given to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>It should be used with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Aristo D3 40000 IU</strong></p><p>It can lead to hypervitaminosis D.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30ºC, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin-D preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus, essential for normal bone growth and maintaining bone density. It also reduces bacterial infection severity, improves lung function, reduces cancer risk (breast, colorectal), and helps maintain insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</p>', 'active', '1924_1742709309.webp', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 10:55:09', '2025-05-01 22:28:25'),
(4253, 'Glympa', 'Empagliflozin + Linagliptin', 40.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '25 mg+5 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Glympa 25 mg + 5 mg</h3><p>Glympa 25 mg + 5 mg is a combination medication containing <strong>empagliflozin</strong> (a sodium-glucose co-transporter 2 [SGLT2] inhibitor) and <strong>linagliptin</strong> (a dipeptidyl peptidase-4 [DPP-4] inhibitor). It is indicated as an adjunct to diet and exercise for improving glycemic control in adults with <strong>type 2 diabetes mellitus</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>Empagliflozin</strong> is also indicated to reduce the risk of cardiovascular death in adults with type 2 diabetes and established cardiovascular disease.</li></ul><h3>Dosage of Glympa 25 mg + 5 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Renal Function Assessment</strong>: Perform an assessment of renal function before starting the medication and as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Recommended Dose</strong>: Start with <strong>10 mg empagliflozin</strong> and <strong>5 mg linagliptin</strong> once daily, taken in the morning, with or without food. This dose may be increased to <strong>25 mg empagliflozin</strong> and <strong>5 mg linagliptin</strong> once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients</strong>: The safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Patients</strong>: There is a higher incidence of adverse reactions related to volume depletion and reduced renal function in geriatric patients.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: There is a higher incidence of adverse reactions in patients with reduced renal function.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Glympa 25 mg + 5 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Common Side Effects</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Urinary tract infections</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Stuffy or runny nose and sore throat</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Upper respiratory tract infections</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Pancreatitis</strong>: There have been reports of acute pancreatitis, including fatal pancreatitis. Discontinue the medication if pancreatitis is suspected.</li><li><strong>Ketoacidosis</strong>: Monitor patients for signs of metabolic acidosis and ketoacidosis, even if blood glucose levels are normal. Discontinue if ketoacidosis is suspected.</li><li><strong>Volume Depletion</strong>: Assess volume status and renal function, especially in patients with impaired renal function, elderly patients, or those on loop diuretics. Monitor during therapy.</li><li><strong>Urosepsis and Pyelonephritis</strong>: Treat urinary tract infections promptly if diagnosed.</li><li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong>: Consider lowering the dose of insulin or insulin secretagogues when initiating this medication.</li><li><strong>Necrotizing Fasciitis (Fournier’s Gangrene)</strong>: Serious, life-threatening cases have occurred. Monitor for pain, swelling, erythema, fever, or malaise in the genital or perineal area.</li><li><strong>Genital Mycotic Infections</strong>: Monitor and treat as necessary.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity Reactions</strong>: Severe allergic reactions (e.g., anaphylaxis, angioedema, exfoliative skin conditions) may occur. Discontinue if any hypersensitivity reaction happens.</li><li><strong>Arthralgia</strong>: Severe joint pain may occur with DPP-4 inhibitors. Discontinue if appropriate.</li><li><strong>Bullous Pemphigoid</strong>: Blisters or erosions may develop. If suspected, discontinue the medication.</li><li><strong>Heart Failure</strong>: There is a risk of heart failure with other DPP-4 inhibitors. Consider the risks and benefits before starting therapy, and monitor for symptoms of heart failure.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong> away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Empagliflozin</strong>: Inhibits <strong>SGLT2</strong>, reducing glucose reabsorption in the kidneys, which increases urinary glucose excretion.</li><li><strong>Linagliptin</strong>: Inhibits <strong>DPP-4</strong>, increasing the levels of <strong>GLP-1</strong> and <strong>GIP</strong> hormones, which stimulate insulin release and reduce glucagon secretion in a glucose-dependent manner.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy and Breastfeeding</h3><ul><li>This medication should <strong>not</strong> be used during <strong>pregnancy</strong>, particularly in the second and third trimesters, due to potential fetal risks.</li><li>It is <strong>not recommended during breastfeeding</strong>.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further details or clarification!</p>', 'active', '1487_1742710969.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 11:22:49', '2025-05-03 18:28:17'),
(4254, 'Rexiet', 'Rabeprazole Sodium0', 8.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Glympa 25 mg + 5 mg</h3><p>Glympa 25 mg + 5 mg is a combination medication containing <strong>empagliflozin</strong> (a sodium-glucose co-transporter 2 [SGLT2] inhibitor) and <strong>linagliptin</strong> (a dipeptidyl peptidase-4 [DPP-4] inhibitor). It is indicated as an adjunct to diet and exercise for improving glycemic control in adults with <strong>type 2 diabetes mellitus</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>Empagliflozin</strong> is also indicated to reduce the risk of cardiovascular death in adults with type 2 diabetes and established cardiovascular disease.</li></ul><h3>Dosage of Glympa 25 mg + 5 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Renal Function Assessment</strong>: Perform an assessment of renal function before starting the medication and as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Recommended Dose</strong>: Start with <strong>10 mg empagliflozin</strong> and <strong>5 mg linagliptin</strong> once daily, taken in the morning, with or without food. This dose may be increased to <strong>25 mg empagliflozin</strong> and <strong>5 mg linagliptin</strong> once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients</strong>: The safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Patients</strong>: There is a higher incidence of adverse reactions related to volume depletion and reduced renal function in geriatric patients.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: There is a higher incidence of adverse reactions in patients with reduced renal function.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Glympa 25 mg + 5 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Common Side Effects</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Urinary tract infections</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Stuffy or runny nose and sore throat</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Upper respiratory tract infections</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Pancreatitis</strong>: There have been reports of acute pancreatitis, including fatal pancreatitis. Discontinue the medication if pancreatitis is suspected.</li><li><strong>Ketoacidosis</strong>: Monitor patients for signs of metabolic acidosis and ketoacidosis, even if blood glucose levels are normal. Discontinue if ketoacidosis is suspected.</li><li><strong>Volume Depletion</strong>: Assess volume status and renal function, especially in patients with impaired renal function, elderly patients, or those on loop diuretics. Monitor during therapy.</li><li><strong>Urosepsis and Pyelonephritis</strong>: Treat urinary tract infections promptly if diagnosed.</li><li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong>: Consider lowering the dose of insulin or insulin secretagogues when initiating this medication.</li><li><strong>Necrotizing Fasciitis (Fournier’s Gangrene)</strong>: Serious, life-threatening cases have occurred. Monitor for pain, swelling, erythema, fever, or malaise in the genital or perineal area.</li><li><strong>Genital Mycotic Infections</strong>: Monitor and treat as necessary.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity Reactions</strong>: Severe allergic reactions (e.g., anaphylaxis, angioedema, exfoliative skin conditions) may occur. Discontinue if any hypersensitivity reaction happens.</li><li><strong>Arthralgia</strong>: Severe joint pain may occur with DPP-4 inhibitors. Discontinue if appropriate.</li><li><strong>Bullous Pemphigoid</strong>: Blisters or erosions may develop. If suspected, discontinue the medication.</li><li><strong>Heart Failure</strong>: There is a risk of heart failure with other DPP-4 inhibitors. Consider the risks and benefits before starting therapy, and monitor for symptoms of heart failure.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong> away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Empagliflozin</strong>: Inhibits <strong>SGLT2</strong>, reducing glucose reabsorption in the kidneys, which increases urinary glucose excretion.</li><li><strong>Linagliptin</strong>: Inhibits <strong>DPP-4</strong>, increasing the levels of <strong>GLP-1</strong> and <strong>GIP</strong> hormones, which stimulate insulin release and reduce glucagon secretion in a glucose-dependent manner.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy and Breastfeeding</h3><ul><li>This medication should <strong>not</strong> be used during <strong>pregnancy</strong>, particularly in the second and third trimesters, due to potential fetal risks.</li><li>It is <strong>not recommended during breastfeeding</strong>.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further details or clarification!</p>', 'active', '898_1742711115.jpg', 68, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 11:25:15', '2025-05-04 16:42:44'),
(4255, 'Napa', 'Paracetamol', 4.00, 5, 'suppository', '1 suppository', 'Suppository', '125 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Napa 125 mg</strong></p><p>Napa 125 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Fever</li><li>Common cold and influenza</li><li>Headache, toothache, earache, bodyache, myalgia, neuralgia</li><li>Dysmenorrhoea, sprains, colic pain, back pain</li><li>Post-operative pain, postpartum pain, inflammatory pain, and post-vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic and osteoarthritic pain and stiffness of joints</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Napa 125 mg (Acetaminophen) has analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity. It acts primarily in the CNS, increasing the pain threshold by inhibiting cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes (COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3) involved in prostaglandin synthesis. It is a para-aminophenol derivative and one of the most widely used, safest, and fast-acting analgesics. Napa 125 mg is well tolerated and free from many side effects seen with aspirin.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong> 1-2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours, up to a maximum of 4 gm (8 tablets) daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6-12 years):</strong> ½ to 1 tablet, 3 to 4 times daily. For long-term treatment, do not exceed 2.6 gm/day.</li><li><strong>Extended Release Tablet:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; Children (over 12 years):</strong> Two tablets every 6 to 8 hours (maximum of 6 tablets in 24 hours). Do not crush.</li><li><strong>Syrup/Suspension:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children under 3 months:</strong> 10 mg/kg body weight (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced), 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>3 months to below 1 year:</strong> ½ to 1 teaspoonful, 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1-5 years:</strong> 1-2 teaspoonful, 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>6-12 years:</strong> 2-4 teaspoonful, 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong> 4-8 teaspoonful, 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Suppository:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 3-12 months:</strong> 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 1-5 years:</strong> 125-250 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6-12 years:</strong> 250-500 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years:</strong> 0.5-1 gm, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Paediatric Drop:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children up to 3 months:</strong> 0.5 ml (40 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>4 to 11 months:</strong> 1.0 ml (80 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1 to 2 years:</strong> 1.5 ml (120 mg). Do not exceed more than 5 doses daily for a maximum of 5 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Napa 125 mg Tablet with Actizorb Technology</strong> It dissolves up to five times faster than standard Napa 125 mg tablets, offering a fast-acting and safe analgesic with marked antipyretic properties. Especially suitable for patients who cannot tolerate aspirin or other analgesics.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Children (12 years and over):</strong> 1 to 2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not exceed 8 tablets in 24 hours.</li><li><strong>Children (7 to 11 years):</strong> ½-1 tablet every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not exceed 4 tablets in 24 hours.</li><li><strong>Not recommended for children under 7 years.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Napa 125 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Alcohol, barbiturates, tricyclic antidepressants may reduce the ability to metabolize large doses of Napa 125 mg.</li><li>Chronic ingestion of anticonvulsants or oral steroid contraceptives may increase first-pass metabolism or clearance, preventing therapeutic levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Napa 125 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Napa 125 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Mild side effects, such as thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, neutropenia, and agranulocytosis.</li><li>Pancreatitis, skin rashes, and other allergic reactions may occur occasionally.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong> (according to the USFDA). Napa 125 mg should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>It is excreted in breast milk, but maternal ingestion of therapeutic doses does not appear to present a risk to the nursing infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be taken in patients with impaired kidney or liver function.</li><li>Care should be exercised when used alongside other drugs affecting the liver.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Napa 125 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms in the first 24 hours: pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, abdominal pain.</li><li>Liver damage may become apparent 12-48 hours after ingestion.</li><li>Abnormalities in glucose metabolism and metabolic acidosis may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Napa 125 mg increases the pain threshold by inhibiting COX enzymes, leading to reduced prostaglandin synthesis, offering analgesic and antipyretic effects with weak anti-inflammatory action.</li></ul>', 'active', '994_1742711437.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 11:30:37', '2025-05-06 17:54:13');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4256, 'Clovate', 'Clobetasol Propionate', 58.17, 1, 'x 10 gm tube', '10 gm tube', 'Cream', '0.05%', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Clovate 10 gm</strong></p><p>Clobetasol Propionate is indicated for adults, elderly, and children over 1 year in the following dermatoses:</p><ul><li>Psoriasis (excluding widespread plaque psoriasis)</li><li>Recalcitrant dermatoses</li><li>Lichen planus</li><li>Discoid lupus erythematosus</li><li>Other skin conditions which do not respond satisfactorily to less potent steroids</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Other Topical Corticosteroids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Clobetasol Propionate is a very potent topical corticosteroid. It has anti-inflammatory, antipruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties. It shows anti-inflammatory activity via multiple mechanisms to inhibit late-phase allergic reactions. It decreases the density of mast cells, chemotaxis, and activation of eosinophils. It also reduces cytokine production and inhibits the metabolism of arachidonic acid.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Clovate 10 gm</strong></p><p><em>Cream &amp; Ointment</em>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults, elderly, and children over 1 year</strong>: Apply a thin layer of Clobetasol Propionate Cream or Ointment to the affected skin areas twice daily and rub in gently and completely. Repeated short courses of Clobetasol Propionate may be used to control exacerbations.</li><li>In more resistant lesions, especially where there is hyperkeratosis, the effect of Clobetasol can be enhanced by occluding the treatment area with polythene film. Overnight occlusion is usually adequate to bring about a satisfactory response.</li><li>Clobetasol Propionate is a super-high potency topical corticosteroid; therefore, treatment should be limited to 2 consecutive weeks. The maximum weekly dose should not exceed 50 gm/week. In children, courses should be limited to five days and reviewed weekly.</li></ul><p><em>Scalp Solution</em>:</p><ul><li>Apply the required quantity of spray to the affected areas of the scalp once or twice daily and gently rub in. The total dose applied should not exceed 50 ml weekly.</li><li>Therapy should be discontinued if no response is noted after one week or as soon as the lesion heals.</li></ul><p><em>Topical Spray</em>:</p><ul><li>For topical use only. Spray should be directly applied to the affected skin areas twice daily and rubbed in gently and completely. The total dosage should not exceed 50 gm (59 ml or 2 fluid ounces) per week.</li><li>Treatment should be limited to 4 weeks. Therapy should be discontinued when control is achieved.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Clovate 10 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Route of administration</strong>: Cutaneous. Creams are especially appropriate for moist or weeping surfaces. Ointments are suitable for dry, lichenified, or scaly lesions.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Clovate 10 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Co-administered drugs that can inhibit CYP3A4 (e.g., ritonavir, itraconazole) have been shown to inhibit the metabolism of corticosteroids leading to increased systemic exposure.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the preparation.</li><li>Should not be used in:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rosacea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acne vulgaris</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Perioral dermatitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Perianal and genital pruritus</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pruritus without inflammation</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Untreated cutaneous infections</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Clovate 10 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Most reported side effects include burning and stinging sensation.</li><li>Less frequent adverse reactions are:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Itching</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin atrophy</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Cracking and fissuring of the skin</li><li>Cushing syndrome has been reported in infants and adults from prolonged use of topical Clobetasol Propionate formulations.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>There is limited data on the use of Clobetasol Propionate cream in pregnant women.</li><li>Topical corticosteroids applied to pregnant animals can cause abnormalities in fetal development. The relevance of this finding to humans has not been established.</li><li>Clobetasol Propionate Cream should only be used during pregnancy and lactation if the expected benefit to the mother outweighs the possible risks.</li><li>It is unknown whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Caution is advised when administering Clobetasol Propionate Cream to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be exercised when using occlusive dressings; the skin should be cleansed before applying a fresh dressing.</li><li>Topical corticosteroids should be used with caution in psoriasis due to the potential for rebound relapses and local or systemic toxicity due to impaired skin barrier function.</li><li>When used on the face, treatment should be limited to 5 days.</li><li>Care should be taken to avoid eyes, especially when applying on eyelids, as repeated exposure may result in cataracts or glaucoma.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Clovate 10 gm</strong></p><ul><li>Acute overdosage is unlikely. In the case of chronic over-dosage or misuse, signs of hypercortisolism may occur. In such cases, topical steroid use should be discontinued.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Do not freeze.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>In infants and children under 12 years, long-term continuous topical corticosteroid therapy should be avoided if possible, as adrenal suppression can occur.</li><li>Children are more susceptible to the use of topical corticosteroids, leading to atrophic changes.</li></ul>', 'active', '1019_1742711662.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 11:34:22', '2025-05-01 18:10:06'),
(4257, 'Azecol', 'Vitamin C + Vitamin E + Lutein + Copper + Zinc', 12.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '60 mg+30 mg+6 mg+2 mg+15 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '552_1742711959.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 11:39:19', '2025-05-03 00:12:22'),
(4258, 'Famotid', 'Famotidine', 55.00, 1, 'x 50 ml bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '40 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Famotid 50 ml</h3><p>Famotid 50 ml is indicated in:</p><ul><li><strong>Gastric ulcer</strong></li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer</strong></li><li><strong>Anastomotic ulcer</strong></li><li><strong>Acute stress ulcer</strong></li><li><strong>Reflux esophagitis</strong></li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong></li><li><strong>Acute gastritis</strong></li><li><strong>Chronic gastritis in the acute exacerbation stage</strong></li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>H2 receptor antagonist</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Famotid 50 ml is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist. It completely inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors of parietal cells and reduces gastric acid secretion, including basal, overnight, and pentagastrin-stimulated gastric acid secretion. The drug’s H2-receptor antagonist activity is slowly reversible, as it dissociates slowly from the H2-receptor.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Famotid 50 ml</h3><h4>Duodenal Ulcer:</h4><ul><li><strong>40 mg at night</strong> for 4 to 8 weeks</li></ul><h4>Benign Gastric Ulcer:</h4><ul><li><strong>40 mg at night</strong> for 4 to 8 weeks</li><li><strong>Maintenance therapy</strong>: 20 mg at night to prevent recurrence</li></ul><h4>Gastro-oesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD):</h4><ul><li><strong>20 mg twice daily</strong> for 6 to 12 weeks</li></ul><h4>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</h4><ul><li><strong>Starting dose</strong>: 20 mg every six hours, adjusted based on individual response (doses up to 160 mg every six hours have been used in some cases without significant adverse effects)</li></ul><h4>Acute Gastritis / Chronic Gastritis (acute exacerbation):</h4><ul><li><strong>20 mg twice daily</strong> or <strong>40 mg once at bedtime</strong></li></ul><h4>Dosage can be taken irrespective of meals. Antacids may be used concomitantly if needed.</h4><h3>Dosage for Specific Forms of Famotid 50 ml</h3><h4>Tablets:</h4><ul><li>For <strong>gastric ulcer</strong>, <strong>duodenal ulcer</strong>, <strong>anastomotic ulcer</strong>, <strong>upper gastro-intestinal hemorrhage</strong>, <strong>reflux esophagitis</strong>, and <strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>20 mg twice daily</strong> (after breakfast and before bed) or <strong>40 mg once daily</strong> at bedtime.</li></ul><h4>Powder for Suspension (for children):</h4><ul><li><strong>GERD</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>&lt;1 year of age</strong>: 0.5 mg/kg/dose once daily for up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>3 to 11 months</strong>: 0.5 mg/kg/dose twice daily for up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1 to 2 months</strong>: 0.5 mg/kg/dose once daily for up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Neonates</strong>: 0.5 mg/kg/dose, maximum once daily for up to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1-16 years</strong>: 1 mg/kg/day p.o., divided b.i.d. up to 40 mg b.i.d.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 40 mg three times daily</li></ul><h4>Intravenous Injection:</h4><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 20 mg intravenously every 12 hours</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients (1-16 years)</strong>: 0.25 mg/kg intravenously q 12 hours up to 40 mg/day</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Famotid 50 ml</h3><p>No clinically significant drug interactions have been identified. Famotid 50 ml does not interact with the cytochrome P450 drug-metabolizing enzyme system.</p><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to any component of the drug.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Famotid 50 ml</h3><ul><li>Eruption</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Diarrhoea</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Tachycardia</li><li>High blood pressure</li><li>Headache</li><li>Drowsiness or insomnia</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B. Famotid 50 ml should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. It crosses the placenta.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is unknown whether Famotid 50 ml is secreted into human milk. Nursing mothers should either stop nursing or discontinue the drug.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Use the minimum required dose based on disease conditions.</li><li>Caution is advised in elderly patients and those with renal failure or hepatic disorders.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Famotid 50 ml is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist that inhibits gastric acid secretion by blocking histamine action on H2-receptors of parietal cells. The effect is slowly reversible as the drug dissociates slowly from the receptor.</p><h3>Pregnancy Category</h3><ul><li><strong>Category B</strong>: No adequate well-controlled studies on Famotid 50 ml in pregnancy, but should only be used if clearly needed.</li></ul>', 'active', '739_1742712229.webp', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 11:43:49', '2025-05-02 17:28:36'),
(4259, 'Napro-A Plus', 'Naproxen Sodium + Esomeprazole Magnesium', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Napro-A Plus 500 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p>It is indicated for the relief of signs and symptoms of osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis and to decrease the risk of developing gastric ulcers in patients at risk of developing NSAID-associated gastric ulcers.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Napro-A Plus is a preparation of Naproxen and Esomeprazole. Naproxen is an NSAID with analgesic and antipyretic activities. It inhibits prostaglandin synthesis. Esomeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor that suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the H+/K+ ATPase in the gastric parietal cell. By acting specifically on the proton pump, Esomeprazole blocks the final step in acid production, thus reducing gastric acidity.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Napro-A Plus 500 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p>The recommended dose is one tablet twice daily. Do not split, chew, crush, or dissolve the tablet. This tablet should be taken at least 30 minutes before meals.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Napro-A Plus 500 mg+20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ACE Inhibitors, diuretics, and beta-blockers</strong>: May reduce the antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin</strong>: Increases the risk of nephrotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Lithium</strong>: Increases plasma levels of lithium.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: May increase the serum levels of methotrexate.</li><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>: May increase the risk of bleeding complications.</li><li><strong>Digoxin</strong>: May increase the toxicity of digoxin.</li><li><strong>Clopidogrel</strong>: May decrease the exposure to the active metabolite of clopidogrel.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any components of this product. It is also contraindicated in patients who have experienced asthma, urticaria, or allergic-type reactions after taking aspirin or other NSAIDs and for the treatment of per-operative pain in the setting of coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Napro-A Plus 500 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p>The most common side effects are gastritis, erosive gastritis, dyspepsia, diarrhea, gastric ulcer, upper abdominal pain, and nausea.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of this tablet should be avoided during pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Fatal cardiovascular thrombotic events like myocardial infarction, stroke, hypertension, congestive heart failure, and edema.</li><li>Serious GI adverse events.</li><li>Hypomagnesaemia.</li><li>Renal or hepatic insufficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '415_1742712451.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 11:47:31', '2025-05-04 00:22:27'),
(4260, 'Vifas', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 8.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '120 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Vifas 120 mg (Fexofenadine) Information</strong></p><p><strong>Indications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Allergic Rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For adults and children aged 12 years and older.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Alleviates symptoms of seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis, such as sneezing, rhinorrhea, itchy, red eyes, and itchy nose, palate, and throat.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Improves work/activity productivity and health-related quality of life.</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For adults and children aged 12 years and older.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Reduces wheals and pruritus associated with chronic idiopathic urticaria.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Enhances work/activity productivity and health-related quality of life.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Fexofenadine is a selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist.</li><li>After oral administration, it is rapidly absorbed, reaching peak plasma concentration in 2-3 hours.</li><li>Does not cross the blood-brain barrier.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Seasonal Allergic Rhinitis</strong>:</li><li>Adults and children 12 years and older:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tablet: 60 mg twice daily or 120 mg once daily or 180 mg once daily.</li><li>Children 6 to 11 years:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Tablet: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of renal impairment: 30 mg once daily.</li><li>Children 2 to 11 years (Suspension):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">30 mg (5 mL) twice daily or 30 mg (5 mL) once daily in case of renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>:</li><li>Adults and children 12 years and older:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tablet: 60 mg twice daily or 120 mg once daily or 180 mg once daily.</li><li>Children 6 to 11 years:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Tablet: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of renal impairment: 30 mg once daily.</li><li>Children 6 months to less than 2 years (Suspension):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">15 mg (2.5 mL) twice daily or 15 mg (2.5 mL) once daily in case of renal impairment.</li><li>Children 2 to 11 years (Suspension):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">30 mg (5 mL) twice daily or 30 mg (5 mL) once daily in case of renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Erythromycin or Ketoconazole</strong>: May increase fexofenadine plasma levels by 2-3 times.</li><li><strong>Antacids</strong> containing aluminum and magnesium hydroxide: Reduce bioavailability. Wait two hours after taking fexofenadine before taking these antacids.</li><li><strong>Omeprazole</strong>: No interaction with fexofenadine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to fexofenadine or any of its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Headaches, fatigue, and vertigo.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong>: Nausea, general discomfort.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Exhaustion.</li><li><strong>Serious reactions</strong> (Post-marketing surveillance):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypersensitivity reactions like angioedema, chest tightness, dyspnea, flushing, and anaphylaxis.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Anxiety, nightmares, insomnia.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Cardiac issues like palpitations and tachycardia.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diarrhea, rash, urticaria, pruritus.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Category C (US FDA).</li><li>Use only if the benefits outweigh risks.</li><li>Fexofenadine passes into breast milk. Not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in the elderly and in patients with renal or hepatic impairment.</li><li>Antihistamines may cause tachycardia or palpitations. Inform patients with active cardiovascular disease.</li><li>No significant impact on the ability to drive or operate machinery, but individual responses should be monitored.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms: Dry mouth, sleepiness, fatigue, dizziness.</li><li>No significant adverse reactions at doses as high as 800 mg.</li><li>Standard procedures should be used for removing unabsorbed medication. Fexofenadine cannot be removed via hemodialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Fexofenadine binds to the inactive form of the H1 receptor, stabilizing it and preventing allergic responses.</li><li>Does not affect dopamine, serotonin, acetylcholine, or adrenergic receptors.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Start with 60 mg once daily due to increased bioavailability and half-life.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Pharmacokinetics may be altered with enhanced bioavailability, though side effects are similar to those under 65.</li></ul>', 'active', '198_1742713028.webp', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 11:57:08', '2025-05-04 22:57:24'),
(4261, 'Tamen XR', 'Paracetamol', 2.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '665 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Tamen XR 665 mg</strong></p><p>Tamen XR 665 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Fever, common cold and influenza</li><li>Headache, toothache, earache, bodyache, myalgia, neuralgia, dysmenorrhoea</li><li>Sprains, colic pain, back pain, post-operative pain, postpartum pain, inflammatory pain, and post-vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic &amp; osteoarthritic pain and stiffness of joints</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Tamen XR 665 mg has analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity. It acts primarily in the CNS, increasing the pain threshold by inhibiting COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3 enzymes involved in prostaglandin synthesis. It is a para-aminophenol derivative, widely used for its safety and fast-acting analgesic effects, and is well tolerated without the side effects associated with aspirin.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Tamen XR 665 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet:</strong><em>Adult:</em> 1-2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours, up to a maximum of 4 gm (8 tablets) daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Children (6-12 years):</em> ½ to 1 tablet 3 to 4 times daily. For long-term treatment, it is advised not to exceed 2.6 gm/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Extended Release Tablet:</strong><em>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years:</em> Two tablets every 6 to 8 hours (maximum of 6 tablets in any 24 hours). The tablet must not be crushed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Syrup/Suspension:</strong><em>Children under 3 months:</em> 10 mg/kg body weight (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced) 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>3 months to below 1 year:</em> ½ to 1 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>1-5 years:</em> 1-2 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>6-12 years:</em> 2-4 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Adults:</em> 4-8 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppository:</strong><em>Children 3-12 months:</em> 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Children 1-5 years:</em> 125-250 mg 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Children 6-12 years:</em> 250-500 mg 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Adults &amp; children over 12 years:</em> 0.5-1 gm 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Paediatric Drop:</strong><em>Children Up to 3 months:</em> 0.5 ml (40 mg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>4 to 11 months:</em> 1.0 ml (80 mg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>7 to 2 years:</em> 1.5 ml (120 mg). Do not exceed 5 doses daily for a maximum of 5 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Tamen XR 665 mg Tablet with Actizorb Technology:</strong></p><p>This formulation dissolves up to five times faster than standard Tamen XR 665 mg tablets, providing rapid action.</p><ul><li><em>Adults and children (12 years and over):</em> Take 1-2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not take more than 8 caplets in 24 hours.</li><li><em>Children (7-11 years):</em> Take ½-1 tablet every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not exceed 4 caplets in 24 hours. Not recommended in children under 7 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Tamen XR 665 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Patients on barbiturates, tricyclic antidepressants, and alcohol may have a diminished ability to metabolize large doses of Tamen XR 665 mg.</li><li>Alcohol can increase the hepatotoxicity of Tamen XR 665 mg overdose.</li><li>Chronic use of anticonvulsants or oral steroid contraceptives may induce liver enzymes and affect therapeutic levels of Tamen XR 665 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Tamen XR 665 mg is contraindicated in known hypersensitivity to the drug.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Tamen XR 665 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Mild side effects are common.</li><li>Haematological reactions (thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, pancytopenia, neutropenia, and agranulocytosis) have been reported.</li><li>Pancreatitis, skin rashes, and other allergic reactions can occur occasionally.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><em>Pregnancy category B</em> (according to USFDA).</li><li>Tamen XR 665 mg should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>It is excreted in breast milk, but maternal use in normal therapeutic doses does not appear to present a risk to the nursing infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be used in patients with impaired kidney or liver function.</li><li>Tamen XR 665 mg should be carefully administered to patients taking other liver-affecting drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Tamen XR 665 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms in the first 24 hours include pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and abdominal pain.</li><li>Liver damage may become evident 12-48 hours after ingestion.</li><li>Abnormal glucose metabolism and metabolic acidosis may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Tamen XR 665 mg has analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity. It works primarily in the CNS by inhibiting COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3 enzymes involved in prostaglandin synthesis. Tamen XR 665 mg is known for being one of the safest and most widely used analgesics.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Tamen XR 665 mg is a category B drug according to USFDA guidelines. It should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed.</li></ul>', 'active', '705_1742713105.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 11:58:25', '2025-05-04 21:32:12'),
(4262, 'Dexend', 'Dexlansoprazole', 11.50, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '30 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Dexend 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis (EE)</strong>: Dexend 30 mg is indicated for the healing of all grades of erosive esophagitis (EE) for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: Dexend 30 mg is indicated to maintain healing of EE and relief of heartburn for up to 6 months.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</strong>: Dexend 30 mg is indicated for the treatment of heartburn associated with symptomatic non-erosive GERD for 4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>: Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><p>Dexend 30 mg is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI) that inhibits gastric acid secretion. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole (a racemic mixture of the R- and S-enantiomers). This is supplied as a Dual Delayed Release (DDR) formulation in a capsule for oral administration. Dexend 30 mg suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell, blocking the final step of acid production.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Dexend 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis and Relief of Heartburn</strong>: 30 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive GERD</strong>: 30 mg once daily for 4 weeks</li><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 60 mg once daily for up to 8 weeks</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Dexend 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Dexend 30 mg can be taken without regard to food. It should be swallowed whole. Alternatively, it can be administered by opening the capsule and sprinkling the intact granules on one tablespoon of applesauce or soft food, swallowing immediately. Granules should not be chewed.</li><li>If a dose is missed, take it as soon as possible, but do not take a double dose if it\'s close to the next dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Dexend 30 mg with Medicine</strong></p><p>Dexend 30 mg may interact with the following medications:</p><ul><li>Atazanavir</li><li>Warfarin</li><li>Tacrolimus</li><li>Clopidogrel</li><li>Methotrexate</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Dexend 30 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Dexend 30 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Flatulence</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: There are no adequate or well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Dexend 30 mg should not be administered to pregnant women unless the expected benefits outweigh the potential risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether Dexend 30 mg is excreted in human milk, though it is excreted in rat milk. Dexend 30 mg should not be given to nursing mothers unless its use is considered essential.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Gastric Malignancy</li><li>Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea</li><li>Bone fractures</li><li>Hypomagnesemia</li><li>Concomitant use with Methotrexate</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Dexend 30 mg</strong></p><p>No significant overdose reports. Doses up to 120 mg daily or a single dose of 300 mg have not resulted in death or severe adverse events.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a dry place, protected from light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics</strong>: No dosage adjustment necessary for elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: Safety and effectiveness have not been established for children under 12 years.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed for renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No adjustment necessary for mild hepatic impairment. For moderate hepatic impairment, a maximum daily dose of 30 mg may be considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong>: Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>: Dexend 30 mg inhibits gastric acid secretion by blocking the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell, the final step of acid production. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy Category</strong>: B</p><p>There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in patients under 12 years have not been established.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No dose adjustment necessary for elderly patients.</p><p><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment needed.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: For moderate impairment, a daily dose of 30 mg is recommended.</p>', 'active', '1321_1742713214.jpg', 68, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 12:00:14', '2025-05-01 20:44:59'),
(4263, 'Dexend', 'Dexlansoprazole', 11.50, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '30 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Dexend 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis (EE)</strong>: Dexend 30 mg is indicated for the healing of all grades of erosive esophagitis (EE) for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: Dexend 30 mg is indicated to maintain healing of EE and relief of heartburn for up to 6 months.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</strong>: Dexend 30 mg is indicated for the treatment of heartburn associated with symptomatic non-erosive GERD for 4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>: Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</p><p>Dexend 30 mg is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI) that inhibits gastric acid secretion. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole (a racemic mixture of the R- and S-enantiomers). This is supplied as a Dual Delayed Release (DDR) formulation in a capsule for oral administration. Dexend 30 mg suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell, blocking the final step of acid production.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Dexend 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis and Relief of Heartburn</strong>: 30 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive GERD</strong>: 30 mg once daily for 4 weeks</li><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 60 mg once daily for up to 8 weeks</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Dexend 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Dexend 30 mg can be taken without regard to food. It should be swallowed whole. Alternatively, it can be administered by opening the capsule and sprinkling the intact granules on one tablespoon of applesauce or soft food, swallowing immediately. Granules should not be chewed.</li><li>If a dose is missed, take it as soon as possible, but do not take a double dose if it\'s close to the next dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Dexend 30 mg with Medicine</strong></p><p>Dexend 30 mg may interact with the following medications:</p><ul><li>Atazanavir</li><li>Warfarin</li><li>Tacrolimus</li><li>Clopidogrel</li><li>Methotrexate</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Dexend 30 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Dexend 30 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Flatulence</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: There are no adequate or well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Dexend 30 mg should not be administered to pregnant women unless the expected benefits outweigh the potential risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether Dexend 30 mg is excreted in human milk, though it is excreted in rat milk. Dexend 30 mg should not be given to nursing mothers unless its use is considered essential.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Gastric Malignancy</li><li>Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea</li><li>Bone fractures</li><li>Hypomagnesemia</li><li>Concomitant use with Methotrexate</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Dexend 30 mg</strong></p><p>No significant overdose reports. Doses up to 120 mg daily or a single dose of 300 mg have not resulted in death or severe adverse events.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a dry place, protected from light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics</strong>: No dosage adjustment necessary for elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: Safety and effectiveness have not been established for children under 12 years.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed for renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No adjustment necessary for mild hepatic impairment. For moderate hepatic impairment, a maximum daily dose of 30 mg may be considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong>: Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong>: Dexend 30 mg inhibits gastric acid secretion by blocking the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell, the final step of acid production. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy Category</strong>: B</p><p>There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in patients under 12 years have not been established.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No dose adjustment necessary for elderly patients.</p><p><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment needed.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: For moderate impairment, a daily dose of 30 mg is recommended.</p>', 'in-active', '1299_1742713215.jpg', 68, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 12:00:15', '2025-05-08 14:33:17'),
(4264, 'Omenix', 'Omeprazole', 5.00, 8, 'capsule', '8 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Omenix 20 mg</strong> is a proton pump inhibitor used to treat various conditions related to excess stomach acid. Below is a summary of its key aspects:</p><h3><strong>Indications:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Duodenal and gastric ulcers</strong></li><li><strong>NSAID-associated ulcers</strong> (gastric and duodenal)</li><li><strong>Prevention of recurrence in NSAID-related ulcers</strong></li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</strong> and long-term acid reflux management</li><li><strong>Dyspepsia</strong> (acid-related)</li><li><strong>Severe reflux esophagitis</strong> with ulcers</li><li><strong>Prevention of acid aspiration</strong> during anesthesia</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong> (excessive acid production)</li><li><strong>Peptic ulcers caused by Helicobacter pylori</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Proton Pump Inhibitor</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></h3><ul><li>Omenix 20 mg works by inhibiting the <strong>H+/K+ ATPase enzyme</strong> in gastric parietal cells, which blocks the secretion of gastric acid.</li><li>The effects begin within <strong>1 hour</strong> of oral administration, peak at <strong>2 hours</strong>, and can last up to <strong>72 hours</strong>.</li><li>Secretory activity resumes gradually within <strong>3 to 5 days</strong> after discontinuation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage:</strong></h3><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Oral Dosage:Benign gastric/duodenal ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily (4 weeks for duodenal ulcers, 8 weeks for gastric ulcers).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>NSAID-induced ulcers</strong>: 20 mg daily for 4 weeks (may extend for another 4 weeks if not healed).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>GERD</strong>: 20 mg daily for 4-8 weeks; can be increased to 40 mg for refractory cases.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Long-term acid reflux management</strong>: 10-20 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: Start with 60 mg daily, up to 120 mg (divided doses for doses above 80 mg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication</strong>: 20 mg twice daily, in combination with antimicrobial agents.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV Dosage:Prophylaxis of acid aspiration</strong>: 40 mg one hour before surgery.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV for gastric ulcers or reflux esophagitis</strong>: 40 mg daily if oral administration is not feasible.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 60 mg daily (adjust based on individual needs).</li></ul><h3><strong>Administration:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Oral</strong>: Taken once daily, typically before a meal.</li><li><strong>IV</strong>: Only administered intravenously. After reconstitution, it should be administered slowly over 2-5 minutes or infused over 20-30 minutes.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions:</strong></h3><ul><li>May increase the levels of <strong>Warfarin</strong>, <strong>phenytoin</strong>, and <strong>diazepam</strong>, requiring dose adjustments.</li><li><strong>Theophylline</strong>, <strong>propranolol</strong>, and <strong>antacids</strong> do not interact significantly with Omenix 20 mg.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to Omenix 20 mg or its ingredients.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Common side effects</strong>: Nausea, stomach cramping, paresthesia, dizziness, and headache (typically mild and temporary).</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy category C</strong>: It has not shown harmful effects on pregnancy, but its effects on the fetus are not fully known.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: Should be stopped if Omenix 20 mg is used, as its entry into breast milk is not well understood.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Rule out gastric cancer</strong> before starting therapy, as the medication may mask symptoms.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage:</strong></h3><ul><li>Keep <strong>dry</strong>, away from <strong>heat</strong> and <strong>light</strong>, and <strong>out of children\'s reach</strong>.</li></ul><p>Omenix 20 mg is a powerful acid secretion inhibitor that is used for various acid-related gastrointestinal conditions, with a strong safety profile when used as directed. However, as with all medications, care should be taken to follow the prescribed dosage and recommendations, especially in special populations like pregnant women or those on other medications.</p>', 'active', '752_1742713292.png', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 12:01:32', '2025-05-04 01:16:45'),
(4265, 'Feviz Plus', 'Iron Polymaltose Complex + Folic Acid + Zinc + Vitamin B-Complexx', 6.02, 10, 'Capsule', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', 'N/A', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Feviz Plus</strong></p><p>Feviz Plus is indicated for the treatment and prevention of deficiencies in Zinc, Iron, Folic Acid, and B-vitamins, especially during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each capsule contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Iron BP</strong>: 47 mg (as Iron Polymaltose Complex 188 mg)</li><li><strong>Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate USP</strong>: 61.8 mg (equivalent to 22.5 mg of elemental zinc)</li><li><strong>Folic Acid BP</strong>: 0.50 mg</li><li><strong>Thiamine Hydrochloride USP</strong>: 5 mg</li><li><strong>Riboflavin BP</strong>: 2 mg</li><li><strong>Nicotinamide USP</strong>: 20 mg</li><li><strong>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride BP</strong>: 2 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Iron &amp; Vitamin Combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This preparation contains Iron as Iron Polymaltose Complex, a novel form of iron that contains non-ionic Ferric Iron and Polymaltose in a stable complex. This facilitates controlled absorption of Iron when it comes in contact with the mucosal cell surface. The non-ionic nature of Iron Polymaltose Complex provides greater stability compared to conventional iron forms.</p><ul><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong> aids in the proper development of the baby.</li><li><strong>Zinc</strong> supports enzyme function and helps metabolize proteins.</li><li><strong>Vitamins</strong> help build tissue and release energy from the breakdown of foods.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Standard Dose</strong>: One capsule daily.</li><li><strong>Severe Cases</strong>: Two capsules a day may be required or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>This product is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Generally well tolerated, but a few allergic reactions may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in Pregnancy</strong>: The use of any drug during the first trimester should be avoided if possible. Iron supplementation during the first trimester should be based on confirmed Iron deficiency. Prophylactic iron supplementation with Zinc and Folic Acid is justified during the remainder of pregnancy where the diet is inadequate.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Care should be taken with patients who may develop iron overload, such as those with haemochromatosis, haemolytic anaemia, or red cell aplasia.</li><li>Iron may chelate with tetracycline, impairing absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Iron Overdose</strong>: Accidental overdose of iron-containing products is a leading cause of fatal poisoning in children under 6.</li><li><strong>Liver Disease &amp; Hemochromatosis</strong>: Avoid higher doses.</li><li><strong>Symptoms of Overdose</strong>: Bloody diarrhea, vomiting, acidosis, darkened stools, and abdominal pain.</li><li><strong>Riboflavin</strong>: No toxicity reported.</li><li><strong>Nicotinamide</strong>: Higher doses may cause vomiting or diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Pyridoxine</strong>: Sensory neuropathy observed with long-term use of more than 200 mg.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong>: No known overdose issues.</li><li><strong>Zinc</strong>: Toxicity can occur with chronic high doses (150-450 mg/day), leading to reduced copper status, impaired immune function, and altered iron function.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C.</li><li>Keep away from light and out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Iron &amp; Vitamin Combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Feviz Plus is a special preparation of Iron Polymaltose Complex, Folic Acid, Zinc, and B-vitamins. The Iron Polymaltose Complex is a water-soluble, macromolecular complex of poly-nuclear iron (III) hydroxide and partially hydrolyzed dextrin (Polymaltose). It does not interact with food components or other medications, ensuring the bioavailability of iron is not decreased. This facilitates quicker utilization of iron in hemoglobin synthesis.</p><ul><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong> helps prevent neural tube defects.</li><li><strong>Zinc</strong> is essential for metabolic processes, blood formation, wound healing, and immune function.</li><li><strong>Thiamine</strong> supports the nervous and digestive systems.</li><li><strong>Riboflavin</strong> aids digestion, immune support, and energy production.</li><li><strong>Pyridoxine</strong> is crucial for brain development, red blood cell production, protein metabolism, and reducing morning sickness.</li><li><strong>Nicotinamide</strong> supports energy, blood pressure, and circulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul>', 'active', '1200_1742713400.webp', 17, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 12:03:20', '2025-04-17 07:57:29');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4266, 'Snofas', 'Fluticasone Furoate', 275.00, 1, 'x 120 metered sprays', '120 metered sprays', 'Nasal Spray', '27.5 mcg/spray', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray</strong></p><p>Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray Nasal Spray is indicated for the treatment of the symptoms of seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis in patients aged 2 years and older. Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray inhalation powder is indicated for the once-daily maintenance treatment of asthma as prophylactic therapy in patients aged 5 years and older. This is not indicated for the relief of acute bronchospasm.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Nasal Steroid Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong> Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray is a synthetic trifluorinated corticosteroid with potent anti-inflammatory activity. Like other corticosteroids, Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray has a wide range of actions on multiple cell types (e.g., mast cells, eosinophils, neutrophils, macrophages, lymphocytes) and mediators (e.g., histamine, eicosanoids, leukotrienes, cytokines) involved in inflammation.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray Nasal Spray</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years</strong>: 2 sprays in each nostril once a day. In some cases, 2 sprays into each nostril twice daily, not exceeding 4 sprays.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children under 12 years (2-11 Years)</strong>: 1 spray in each nostril once a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (under 2 years of age)</strong>: There are no data to recommend use of Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray Nasal Spray for the treatment of seasonal or perennial allergic rhinitis in children under 2 years of age.</li><li><strong>Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray Inhalation Capsule</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Administer 1 inhalation once daily. The dosage should be based on the patient’s asthma severity.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and Adolescents Aged 12 Years and Older</strong>: The starting dosage for patients not on an inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) is 100 mcg. If a 100 mcg dose is ineffective after 2 weeks, the dose may be increased to 200 mcg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric Patients Aged 5 to 11 Years</strong>: The recommended dosage for children aged 5 to 11 years is 50 mcg administered once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray</strong></p><ul><li><strong>How to use the Nasal Spray</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Shake the bottle gently and remove the dust cover.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hold the spray with your forefinger and middle finger on either side of the nozzle and your thumb underneath the bottle. Press down until a fine spray appears.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If using for the first time, or if you have not used it for a week or more, press the nasal applicator several times until a fine mist comes out.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gently blow the nose to clear the nostrils.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Close one nostril and carefully insert the nasal applicator into the open nostril. Tilt your head forward slightly and keep the spray upright. Breathe in through your nose and while breathing in, press the white-collar of nasal applicator firmly down once to release a spray. Breathe out through your mouth.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Repeat for the other nostril if necessary.</li><li><strong>Cleaning</strong>: Clean the nasal spray at least once a week. Remove the dust cover and gently pull off the nasal applicator. Wash the applicator and dust cover in warm water, shake off excess water, and leave to dry. Avoid using additional heat.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray</strong></p><ul><li>Potent inhibitors of cytochrome P450 3A4 (CYP3A4) may increase exposure to Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray. Coadministration of ritonavir is not recommended. Use caution with coadministration of other potent CYP3A4 inhibitors, such as ketoconazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray nasal spray is contraindicated in patients with a hypersensitivity to any of its ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Nasal Spray</strong>: Dryness of the nose and an increase in nosebleeds may occur.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Capsule (≥5% of adult and adolescent subjects)</strong>: Nasopharyngitis, bronchitis, upper respiratory tract infection, and headache.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Capsule (≥3% of pediatric subjects aged 5 to 11 years)</strong>: Pharyngitis, bronchitis, and viral infection.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray should be used only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray is excreted in human breast milk. Caution should be exercised when administering to a nursing woman.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Rarely, immediate hypersensitivity reactions or contact dermatitis may occur after the administration of Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray.</li><li>Instances of wheezing, nasal septum perforation, cataracts, glaucoma, and increased intraocular pressure have been reported with intranasal corticosteroids, including Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray.</li><li>Systemic effects are minimal with the recommended doses, but larger doses should be avoided.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at a temperature not exceeding 30°C. Do not refrigerate. Protect from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established in children under 2 years of age.</li><li><strong>Geriatric use</strong>: In elderly patients, dose selection should be cautious, considering the greater frequency of decreased hepatic, renal, or cardiac function, and concomitant disease or drug therapy.</li><li><strong>Use in hepatic impairment</strong>: Use with caution in patients with severe hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Use in renal impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment required.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Nasal Steroid Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Snofas 27.5 mcg/spray is a synthetic trifluorinated corticosteroid with potent anti-inflammatory activity. It acts on multiple cell types and mediators involved in inflammation.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category C</strong>: Use only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and effectiveness in children younger than 2 years have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Caution is advised in elderly patients with diminished hepatic, renal, or cardiac function.</li></ul>', 'active', '122_1742713570.png', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 12:06:10', '2025-05-04 21:13:36'),
(4267, 'Mervan SR', 'Aceclofenac', 9.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is the text with the headings converted to bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Mervan SR 200 mg</strong></p><p>Mervan SR 200 mg is indicated for the relief of pain and inflammation in osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, toothache, trauma, and lumbago.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Drugs for Osteoarthritis, Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Mervan SR 200 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Mervan SR 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Extended release tablet:</strong> The recommended dose in adults is one 200 mg Mervan SR 200 mg tablet daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Film-coated tablet:</strong> The recommended dose in adults is 100 mg, twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Mervan SR 200 mg</strong></p><p>No significant drug interactions have been observed, but close monitoring of patients is required when it is used with:</p><ul><li><strong>Lithium and Digoxin:</strong> May increase plasma concentration of lithium and digoxin.</li><li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> May interact with the activity of diuretics.</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants:</strong> May enhance the activity of anticoagulants.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate:</strong> May increase the plasma level of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Mervan SR 200 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to it or in whom aspirin or NSAIDs precipitate attacks of asthma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Mervan SR 200 mg</strong></p><p>Mervan SR 200 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of Mervan SR 200 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in patients with active or suspected peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding, moderate to severe hepatic impairment, and cardiac or renal impairment. Caution should also be exercised in patients suffering from dizziness or urticaria.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Mervan SR 200 mg in children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Drugs for Osteoarthritis, Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Mervan SR 200 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The use of Mervan SR 200 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Mervan SR 200 mg in children.</p>', 'active', '491_1742713715.webp', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 12:08:35', '2025-05-04 00:00:46'),
(4268, 'Savlon Liquid Antiseptic', 'Cetrimide + Chlorhexidine Gluconate', 60.00, 1, 'x 112 ml Bottle', '112 ml Bottle', 'Anti acidity Saline', '0 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '624_1742713870.png', 14, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 12:11:10', '2025-05-04 20:57:20'),
(4269, 'Docopa', 'Doxophylline', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '400 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Docopa 400 mg</strong></p><p>Docopa 400 mg is used to treat the following indications:</p><ul><li>Bronchial asthma</li><li>Bronchospasm</li><li>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</li><li>Pulmonary disease with a spastic bronchial component</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Docopa 400 mg is a novel bronchodilator that structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. It selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. However, unlike Theophylline, Docopa 400 mg appears to have decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, potentially accounting for its improved safety profile. Additionally, it is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Docopa 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 400 mg tablet two or three times daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>&gt;12 years of age</strong>: 10 ml syrup or 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>6-12 years of age</strong>: 6-9 mg/kg body weight two times daily (e.g., if body weight is 10 kg, 3 ml [60 mg] two times daily) or as prescribed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Docopa 400 mg</strong></p><p>Docopa 400 mg should not be administered together with other xanthine derivatives. Toxic synergism with ephedrine has been documented for xanthines. Like other xanthines, concomitant therapy with troleandomycin, lincomycin, clindamycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, ranitidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccine may decrease the hepatic clearance of xanthines, leading to an increase in blood levels.</p><p>No evidence of a relationship between Docopa 400 mg serum concentrations and toxic events has been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Docopa 400 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Acute myocardial infarction</li><li>Hypotension</li><li>Lactating women</li><li>Patients who have shown hypersensitivity to its components</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Docopa 400 mg</strong></p><p>Docopa 400 mg rarely causes serious side effects. However, possible side effects are similar to taking an excess amount of caffeine, including:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Headache</li><li>Upset stomach</li><li>Heartburn</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Animal reproduction studies indicate that Docopa 400 mg does not cause fetal harm when administered to pregnant animals or affect reproduction capacity. However, due to limited experience in pregnant humans, xanthines should only be given to pregnant women when clearly necessary.</p><p>Docopa 400 mg is contraindicated in nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The half-life of xanthine derivatives is influenced by several known variables. It may be prolonged in patients with liver disease, congestive heart failure, and those taking certain other drugs like erythromycin, troleandomycin, lincomycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccine. In these cases, a lower dose of Docopa 400 mg may be needed.</p><p>Phenytoin, other anticonvulsants, and smoking may cause an increase in clearance with a shorter mean half-life. In these cases, higher doses of Docopa 400 mg may be needed.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Docopa 400 mg</strong></p><p>In case of overdose, severe cardiac arrhythmias and tonic-clonic seizures may occur. These effects may represent the first signs of intoxication. The appearance of side effects may require discontinuation of the treatment, which, if necessary, at the physician’s discretion, may be resumed at lower doses after all signs and symptoms of toxicity have subsided.</p><p>As there is no specific antidote, in case of overdose, symptomatic treatment of cardiovascular collapse should be instituted.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children. Docopa 400 mg should be used only on the prescription of a specialist physician.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Docopa 400 mg is a novel bronchodilator. It structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. Docopa 400 mg selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, thereby relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. However, differently from Theophylline, Docopa 400 mg appears to have decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, which may account for the better safety profile of the drug. Docopa 400 mg is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p>', 'active', '314_1742713971.webp', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 12:12:51', '2025-04-16 13:02:49'),
(4270, 'Ace', 'Paracetamol', 35.00, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Oral Solution', '120 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Ace 60 ml</h3><p>Ace 60 ml is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Fever</li><li>Common cold and influenza</li><li>Headache</li><li>Toothache</li><li>Earache</li><li>Bodyache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Neuralgia</li><li>Dysmenorrhoea</li><li>Sprains</li><li>Colic pain</li><li>Back pain</li><li>Post-operative pain</li><li>Postpartum pain</li><li>Inflammatory pain</li><li>Post-vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic &amp; osteoarthritic pain</li><li>Stiffness of joints</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Ace 60 ml (Acetaminophen) has analgesic and antipyretic properties, with weak anti-inflammatory activity. It acts primarily in the CNS by increasing the pain threshold and inhibiting both isoforms of cyclooxygenase (COX-1, COX-2, COX-3), enzymes involved in prostaglandin (PG) synthesis. It is one of the most widely used, safest, and fast-acting analgesics and is well tolerated, free from many side effects of aspirin.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ace 60 ml</h3><p><strong>Tablet:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 1-2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours, up to a maximum of 4 gm (8 tablets) daily.</li><li><strong>Children (6-12 years):</strong> ½ to 1 tablet 3 to 4 times daily. For long-term treatment, do not exceed 2.6 gm/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Extended Release Tablet:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years:</strong> Two tablets, swallowed whole, every 6 to 8 hours (maximum of 6 tablets in any 24 hours). The tablet must not be crushed.</li></ul><p><strong>Syrup/Suspension:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children under 3 months:</strong> 10 mg/kg body weight (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced) 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>3 months to below 1 year:</strong> ½ to 1 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>1-5 years:</strong> 1-2 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>6-12 years:</strong> 2-4 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 4-8 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Suppository:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children 3-12 months:</strong> 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children 1-5 years:</strong> 125-250 mg 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6-12 years:</strong> 250-500 mg 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults &amp; children over 12 years:</strong> 0.5-1 gm 4 times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Paediatric Drop:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children Up to 3 months:</strong> 0.5 ml (40 mg)</li><li><strong>4 to 11 months:</strong> 1.0 ml (80 mg)</li><li><strong>1 to 2 years:</strong> 1.5 ml (120 mg). Do not exceed more than 5 doses daily for a maximum of 5 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Ace 60 ml Tablet with Actizorb Technology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children (12 years and over):</strong> 1 to 2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not take more than 8 caplets in 24 hours.</li><li><strong>Children (7 to 11 years):</strong> ½-1 tablet every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not take more than 4 caplets in 24 hours. Not recommended in children under 7 years.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Ace 60 ml</h3><ul><li><strong>Barbiturates, tricyclic antidepressants, and alcohol:</strong> May decrease the ability to metabolize large doses of Ace 60 ml.</li><li><strong>Alcohol:</strong> Can increase the hepatotoxicity of Ace 60 ml overdose.</li><li><strong>Anticonvulsants or oral steroid contraceptives:</strong> May induce liver enzymes and reduce therapeutic Ace 60 ml levels by increasing first-pass metabolism or clearance.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Ace 60 ml.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Ace 60 ml</h3><p>Side effects are generally mild but can include:</p><ul><li><strong>Haematological reactions:</strong> Thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, pancytopenia, neutropenia, and agranulocytosis.</li><li><strong>Pancreatitis, skin rashes</strong>, and other allergic reactions.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy category B</strong> (according to USFDA). Should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Excreted in breast milk</strong>. Maternal ingestion of Ace 60 ml in normal therapeutic doses does not appear to present a risk to the nursing infant.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired kidney or liver function.</li><li>Caution in patients taking other drugs that affect the liver.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Ace 60 ml</h3><ul><li><strong>First 24 hours:</strong> Pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and abdominal pain.</li><li><strong>12-48 hours:</strong> Liver damage may become apparent. Abnormalities of glucose metabolism and metabolic acidosis may occur.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Ace 60 ml acts primarily in the CNS, increasing the pain threshold by inhibiting cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes involved in prostaglandin synthesis. It has analgesic, antipyretic, and weak anti-inflammatory properties.</p>', 'active', '344_1742714106.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 12:15:06', '2025-05-02 22:39:55'),
(4271, 'Esoral', 'Esomeprazole', 8.00, 14, 'Tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Esoral 40 mg</strong></p><p>Esoral 40 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Alleviation of GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn and others.</li><li>Treatment of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Maintenance of healing for erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Eradication of <em>Helicobacter pylori</em> infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Esoral 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. If healing is not achieved after 4-8 weeks, an additional 4-8 weeks may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. An additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered if symptoms persist.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication</strong>: Triple therapy to reduce duodenal ulcer recurrence: Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days, amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg once daily, adjusted individually. Treatment should continue as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: The recommended adult dose is 40 mg Esoral 40 mg once daily via intravenous injection (over at least 3 minutes) or infusion (10-30 minutes).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Esoral 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral Administration</strong>: Take one Esoral 40 mg pill or capsule whole, at least one hour before a meal.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>: Mix the entire contents of the packet with 15 ml of water. After stirring thoroughly, allow it to thicken for 2-3 minutes. Stir again within 30 minutes before consuming. If using a nasogastric or gastric tube, prepare the suspension similarly in a syringe and administer accordingly.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong>: Add 5 ml of solvent (WFI) to the vial to create the injection solution. Administer the injection slowly over at least 3 minutes. Use the solution within 12 hours of reconstitution.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Esoral 40 mg</strong></p><p>Esoral 40 mg is metabolized by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4. It does not significantly interact with medications processed by other CYP enzymes. Notable interactions include:</p><ul><li><strong>Diazepam</strong>: Esomeprazole can reduce diazepam clearance, potentially increasing its plasma levels.</li><li><strong>Drugs affected by stomach pH</strong>: Esomeprazole reduces stomach acid, which may affect the absorption of medications such as ketoconazole, iron salts, and digoxin.</li><li><strong>Clarithromycin and Amoxicillin</strong>: Co-administration increases the plasma levels of esomeprazole and its metabolites.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Esoral 40 mg is contraindicated in individuals with a known hypersensitivity to any of its formulations.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Esoral 40 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Dry mouth</li></ul><p>Long-term maintenance therapy does not differ in side effects compared to short-term treatment.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>A positive response to treatment does not rule out the possibility of stomach cancer.</li><li>Esoral should be taken at least one hour before meals.</li><li>For those who have difficulty swallowing capsules, the pellets can be mixed with applesauce. The mixture should be ingested immediately.</li><li>Antacids may be used alongside Esoral.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Esoral 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of acute overdose may include decreased motor activity, tremors, ataxia, and clonic convulsions.</li><li>No specific countermeasure is known for esomeprazole overdose.</li><li>Dialysis is not expected to be effective.</li><li>Symptomatic and supportive care should be provided in the event of an overdose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a dry place at temperatures not exceeding 30°C, away from moisture and light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Esoral 40 mg inhibits the H+/K+-ATPase (proton pump) in the gastric parietal cell, reducing stomach acid production. It is the S-isomer of omeprazole, offering superior acid control. Esomeprazole is metabolized in the liver primarily by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4, with its metabolites having no anti-secretory properties.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There is no reliable data on the use of esomeprazole during pregnancy. Although no teratogenic effects have been found in animal studies, it is advised to stop breastfeeding if esomeprazole is deemed necessary for use during lactation.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><p>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><p>No significant differences in safety and effectiveness between elderly and younger patients have been observed. However, older patients may be more sensitive to the drug.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><p>No dosage change is required for mild to moderate hepatic insufficiency. In patients with severe hepatic impairment, a dose of 20 mg once daily should not be exceeded.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><p>As less than 1% of esomeprazole is excreted unchanged in urine, renal impairment is unlikely to affect the pharmacokinetics of the drug.</p>', 'active', '1019_1742714205.jpg', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 12:16:45', '2025-05-02 12:38:07'),
(4272, 'Zovia Gold', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral [A-Z syrup preparation]', 90.27, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', 'N/A', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1608_1742714387.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 12:19:47', '2025-04-22 19:31:09'),
(4273, 'Duvent', 'Rupatadine Fumarate', 10.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Sure! Here\'s the text with the headings in normal bold text, as requested:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Duvent 10 mg</strong></p><p>Duvent 10 mg is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis and Urticaria.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Duvent 10 mg is a second generation, non-sedating, long-acting histamine antagonist with selective peripheral H1 receptor antagonist activity. It further blocks the receptors of the platelet-activating factor (PAF) according to in vitro and in vivo studies. Rupatadine possesses anti-allergic properties such as the inhibition of the degranulation of mast cells induced by immunological and non-immunological stimuli, and inhibition of the release of cytokines, particularly of the tumor necrosis factors (TNF) in human mast cells and monocytes.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Duvent 10 mg</strong></p><p>Rupatadine Tablet: The recommended dosage is 10 mg once daily, with or without food.</p><p>Rupatadine Oral Solution (Children aged 2 to 11 year):</p><ul><li>Children weighing 25 kg or more: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) of oral solution once daily with or without food.</li><li>Children weighing equal or more than 10 kg to less than 25 kg: 1/2 teaspoonful (2.5 ml) oral solution once daily with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Duvent 10 mg</strong></p><p>Adults and adolescents (over 12 years): The recommended dose is 10 mg once a day. Rupatadine may be taken with or without food.</p><p>Children aged 2 to 11 years:</p><ul><li>Children weighing 25 kg or more: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) of the oral solution once daily with or without food.</li><li>Children weighing equal or more than 10 kg to less than 25 kg: 1/2 teaspoonful (2.5 ml) oral solution once daily with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Duvent 10 mg</strong></p><p>With medicine: The concomitant administration of Rupatadine 20 mg and ketoconazole or erythromycin increases the systemic exposure. Rupatadine should be used with caution when it is administered concomitantly with these drug substances and other inhibitors of the isozyme CYP3A4. Rupatadine should be used with caution when it is co-administered with statins, CNS depressants or alcohol.</p><p>With food: Grapefruit and Grapefruit juice should not be taken simultaneously with Rupatadine.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to Rupatadine or to any of the excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Duvent 10 mg</strong></p><p>Common: Asthenia, dizziness, drowsiness.</p><p>Uncommon: Appetite increased, arthralgia, back pain, concentration impaired, constipation, cough, diarrhea, dry throat, epistaxis, fever, gastrointestinal discomfort, increased risk of infection, irritability, malaise, myalgia, nasal dryness, nausea, oropharyngeal pain, rash, thirst, vomiting, weight increased.</p><p>Rare: Palpitations, tachycardia.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy category B2 (by TGA, Australia). There is no clinical data available on the exposure of Rupatadine during pregnancy. Pregnant women should therefore not use Rupatadine, unless the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk for the fetus. No information is available, whether Rupatadine is excreted in the mother\'s milk. Therefore, it should not be used during lactation, unless the potential benefits for the mother justify the potential risk to the infant.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Duvent 10 mg should be used with caution in patients with known prolongation of the QT interval, patients with uncorrected hypokalemia, and patients with ongoing proarrhythmic conditions, such as clinically significant bradycardia or acute myocardial ischemia. Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly patients (65 years and older). As there is no clinical experience in patients with impaired kidney or liver function, the use of Rupatadine 10 mg tablets is at present not recommended in these patients.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Duvent 10 mg</strong></p><p>The most common adverse reaction was somnolence. If accidental ingestion of very high doses occurs, symptomatic treatment together with the required supportive measures should be given.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in cool &amp; dry place below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p>Elderly: Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly. No information is available that indicates the requirement of any dose adjustment in this population.</p><p>Children: Neither the safety nor the efficacy of Rupatadine has been established in patients less than 12 years of age.</p><p>Patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency: Use of Rupatadine is not recommended in patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency. As no relevant clinical data is available.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Duvent 10 mg is a long-acting, non-sedative antagonist of histamine H1-receptors. It also antagonizes the platelet activating factor (PAF). Both histamine and PAF cause bronchoconstriction which leads to an increase in the vascular permeability and act as a mediator in the inflammatory process. With the dual mode of action, Rupatadine shows better therapeutic effect than an isolated antihistamine. Rupatadine possesses other anti-allergic properties such as the inhibition of the degranulation of mast cells induced by immunological and non-immunological stimuli and inhibition of the release of cytokines, particularly of the tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF α) in human mastocytes and monocytes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There is no clinical data available on the exposure of Rupatadine during pregnancy. Pregnant women should therefore not use Rupatadine unless the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk for the infant. No information is available, whether Rupatadine is excreted in the mother\'s milk. Therefore, it should not be used during lactation, unless the potential benefits for the mother justify the potential risk to the infant.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Elderly: Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly. No information is available that indicates the requirement of any dose adjustment in this population.</p><p>Children: Neither the safety nor the efficacy of Rupatadine has been established in patients less than 12 years of age.</p><p>Patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency: Use of Rupatadine is not recommended in patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency. As no relevant clinical data is available.</p><p>Let me know if you need any further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '306_1742715837.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 12:43:57', '2025-05-02 08:54:12'),
(4274, 'Nature-E', 'Vitamin E [Alpha Tocopherol Acetate]', 7.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '400 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nature-E 400 IU</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>As a dietary supplement:</strong>Vitamin E deficiency resulting from impaired absorption</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increased requirements due to diet rich in polyunsaturated fats</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For healthy hair &amp; skin</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">As an antioxidant</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hemolytic anemia due to Vitamin E deficiency</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Therapeutic use:</strong>Cardiovascular disease</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Heavy metal poisoning</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hepatotoxin poisoning</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hemolytic anemia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oxygen therapy</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In nutritional deficiency states</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin-E Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Vitamin E acts as an antioxidant in the body. It protects polyunsaturated fatty acids (which are components of cellular membranes) and other oxygen-sensitive substances such as Vitamin A &amp; Vitamin C from oxidation. In premature neonates, irritability, edema, thrombosis, and hemolytic anemia may result from Vitamin E deficiency. In adults and children with low serum tocopherol concentrations, issues like creatinuria, ceroid deposition, muscle weakness, decreased erythrocyte survival, or increased in vitro hemolysis by oxidizing agents have been identified.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Nature-E 400 IU</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Betterment of cardiovascular health:</strong></li><li>400 IU-800 IU per day</li><li><strong>Deficiency syndrome in adults:</strong></li><li>200 IU-400 IU per day</li><li><strong>Deficiency syndrome in children:</strong></li><li>200 IU per day</li><li><strong>Thalassemia:</strong></li><li>800 IU per day</li><li><strong>Sickle-cell anemia:</strong></li><li>400 IU per day</li><li><strong>Betterment of skin &amp; hair:</strong></li><li>200 IU-400 IU per day (Topical use is also established for beautification)</li><li><strong>Chronic cold in adults:</strong></li><li>200 IU per day</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Nature-E 400 IU</strong></p><p>Vitamin E may impair the absorption of Vitamin A, affect the function of Vitamin K, and potentiate the effect of Warfarin.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>No known contraindications found.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Nature-E 400 IU</strong></p><p>Overdosage (&gt;1 gm) has been associated with minor side effects, including:</p><ul><li>Hypertension</li><li>Fatigue</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Myopathy</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Vitamin E is safe during pregnancy and lactation when used as recommended doses. Higher doses are not well established.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>It may increase the risk of thrombosis in some patients, such as those taking estrogens.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>Vitamin E is safe for children.</p>', 'active', '1882_1742715928.webp', 8, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 12:45:28', '2025-05-04 00:29:25'),
(4275, 'Esotid', 'Esomeprazole', 6.50, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Esotid 20 mg</strong></p><p>Esotid 20 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Alleviating GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn.</li><li>Treating erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Maintaining the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Eradicating <em>Helicobacter pylori</em> infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in combination with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Treating Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>Treating duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Esotid 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. Most patients heal within 4-8 weeks. If healing doesn\'t occur after this time, an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms persist, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication (Triple Therapy)</strong>: Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days, amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg once daily. The dosage should be adjusted individually and treatment continued as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks, depending on the response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: The recommended adult dose is 40 mg Esotid 20 mg given once daily by intravenous injection (not less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes). Esomeprazole IV should not be administered concomitantly with other medications through the same intravenous site.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Esotid 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take one Esotid 20 mg pill or capsule at least one hour before meals and swallow it whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>: Mix the entire packet with 15 ml of water in a small glass. Stir the mixture, wait 2-3 minutes for thickening, and stir again. Consume within 30 minutes. If taken through a nasogastric or gastric tube, prepare and administer similarly.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong>: Reconstitute the solution with 5 ml of water for injection (WFI), then administer the injection over at least 3 minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Esotid 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Metabolism</strong>: Esomeprazole is metabolized by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 enzymes in the liver. It does not significantly interact with medications metabolized by CYP enzymes 1A2, 2A6, 2C9, 2D6, 2E1, and 3A4.</li><li><strong>Drug Interactions</strong>: No significant interactions with phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin. However, it may reduce the absorption of medications dependent on stomach acidity (e.g., ketoconazole, iron salts, and digoxin).</li><li><strong>CYP2C19 and Diazepam</strong>: When taken together, esomeprazole may increase diazepam plasma levels, potentially leading to increased effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to esomeprazole or any of its ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Esotid 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, and dry mouth. Long-term maintenance therapy (up to 12 months) does not increase the risk of significant side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Stomach Cancer</strong>: A positive response to treatment with Esotid 20 mg does not rule out the possibility of stomach cancer.</li><li><strong>Administration in Capsules</strong>: For patients who have trouble swallowing capsules, esomeprazole pellets can be mixed with applesauce (cold and soft). The mixture should be consumed immediately.</li><li><strong>Antacids</strong>: Can be taken alongside Esotid 20 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Esotid 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Acute poisoning may cause symptoms like decreased motor activity, changes in breathing, tremors, ataxia, and sporadic convulsions. In case of overdose, provide symptomatic and supportive care. Esomeprazole is highly protein-bound, so dialysis is unlikely to remove it.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place at temperatures not exceeding 30°C, shielded from moisture and light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Mechanism</strong>: Esomeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor (PPI) that inhibits the H+/K+-ATPase enzyme in gastric parietal cells, reducing acid production.</li><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: Esomeprazole is absorbed rapidly after oral administration, with peak plasma concentrations occurring about 1.5 hours after dosing. It has 90% bioavailability with repeated dosing.</li><li><strong>Metabolism</strong>: Esomeprazole is primarily metabolized in the liver by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 enzymes.</li><li><strong>Excretion</strong>: Excreted mainly as inactive metabolites in the urine (80%) and feces (20%).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Insufficient reliable data on the use of esomeprazole during pregnancy. No teratogenic effects were observed in animal studies. It is recommended to discontinue breastfeeding if esomeprazole use is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric and Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No significant differences in safety and effectiveness between elderly and younger patients. However, older adults may be more sensitive to the drug\'s effects.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage adjustment is required for mild to moderate hepatic impairment. For severe hepatic impairment, the dose should not exceed 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: No significant pharmacokinetic changes are expected in patients with renal insufficiency.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '112_1742716015.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 12:46:55', '2025-05-08 14:52:28');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4276, 'Tamen', 'Paracetamol', 1.20, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Tamen 500 mg</h3><p><strong>Indications:</strong> Tamen 500 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Fever</li><li>Common cold and influenza</li><li>Headache</li><li>Toothache</li><li>Earache</li><li>Bodyache</li><li>Myalgia (muscle pain)</li><li>Neuralgia (nerve pain)</li><li>Dysmenorrhoea (menstrual pain)</li><li>Sprains</li><li>Colic pain</li><li>Back pain</li><li>Post-operative pain</li><li>Postpartum pain</li><li>Inflammatory pain</li><li>Post-vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic &amp; osteoarthritic pain</li><li>Stiffness of joints</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Tamen 500 mg contains <strong>Acetaminophen</strong> and is primarily used for its <strong>analgesic</strong> and <strong>antipyretic</strong> properties, with weak anti-inflammatory activity. It is believed to act mainly in the <strong>central nervous system (CNS)</strong> by increasing the pain threshold, inhibiting both <strong>COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3</strong> enzymes involved in <strong>prostaglandin synthesis</strong>. Acetaminophen is one of the safest, widely used, and fast-acting analgesics, well tolerated, and free from side effects associated with <strong>aspirin</strong>.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong> 1-2 tablets every 4-6 hours, up to a maximum of <strong>4 gm (8 tablets)</strong> daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6-12 years):</strong> ½ to 1 tablet 3-4 times daily. Do not exceed <strong>2.6 gm/day</strong> for long-term use.</li><li><strong>Extended Release Tablet:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; Children (over 12 years):</strong> 2 tablets every <strong>6-8 hours</strong> (maximum of 6 tablets in 24 hours). The tablet must not be crushed.</li><li><strong>Syrup/Suspension:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children under 3 months:</strong> 10 mg/kg body weight (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced) 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>3 months to below 1 year:</strong> ½ to 1 teaspoonful 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1-5 years:</strong> 1-2 teaspoonfuls 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>6-12 years:</strong> 2-4 teaspoonfuls 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong> 4-8 teaspoonfuls 3-4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Suppository:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 3-12 months:</strong> 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 1-5 years:</strong> 125-250 mg 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6-12 years:</strong> 250-500 mg 4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; children over 12 years:</strong> 0.5-1 gm 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Paediatric Drop:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children up to 3 months:</strong> 0.5 ml (40 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>4 to 11 months:</strong> 1.0 ml (80 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1 to 2 years:</strong> 1.5 ml (120 mg). Do not exceed more than <strong>5 doses daily</strong> for up to <strong>5 days</strong>.</li><li><strong>Tamen 500 mg Tablet with Actizorb Technology:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; children (12 years and over):</strong> 1-2 tablets every 4-6 hours as needed. Do not exceed <strong>8 caplets</strong> in 24 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (7 to 11 years):</strong> ½-1 tablet every 4-6 hours as needed. Do not exceed <strong>4 caplets</strong> in 24 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Alcohol, <strong>barbiturates</strong>, <strong>tricyclic antidepressants</strong>, and anticonvulsants may impair the metabolism of large doses of Tamen 500 mg.</li><li>Chronic use of oral steroid contraceptives or anticonvulsants may increase the metabolism, reducing Tamen 500 mg\'s effectiveness.</li><li>Alcohol may increase the risk of <strong>hepatotoxicity</strong> with overdose.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Tamen 500 mg (Acetaminophen).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Most side effects are mild, but include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Haematological reactions (e.g., thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, neutropenia, agranulocytosis)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pancreatitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Skin rashes</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Allergic reactions</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B (US FDA):</strong> Tamen 500 mg should only be used during pregnancy if absolutely necessary.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Tamen 500 mg is excreted in breast milk, but maternal ingestion in normal therapeutic doses is not considered harmful to the nursing infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Caution is advised in patients with impaired liver or kidney function.</li><li>Patients taking other drugs that affect the liver should use Tamen 500 mg with care.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms in the first 24 hours: Pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and abdominal pain.</li><li><strong>Liver damage</strong> may develop 12-48 hours after ingestion.</li><li>Metabolic disturbances like <strong>glucose metabolism abnormalities</strong> and <strong>metabolic acidosis</strong> may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a <strong>dry place</strong> away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Tamen 500 mg (Acetaminophen) has analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity, primarily acting in the CNS to increase the pain threshold by inhibiting cyclooxygenase enzymes involved in prostaglandin synthesis.</li></ul>', 'active', '797_1742716316.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 12:51:56', '2025-05-04 21:31:13'),
(4277, 'Esoral MUPS', 'Esomeprazole', 16.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Esoral MUPS 40 mg</strong></p><p>Esoral MUPS 40 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>To alleviate GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn and others.</li><li>To treat erosive esophagitis.</li><li>To maintain the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>For the eradication of <em>Helicobacter pylori</em> infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</li><li>Duodenal &amp; gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Esoral MUPS 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. The majority of patients are healed within 4 to 8 weeks. For patients who don\'t heal after 4-8 weeks, an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg once daily (Clinical studies did not extend 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely after 4 weeks, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori eradication (Triple Therapy):</strong> Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days, amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> The dose is 20-80 mg once daily. The dosage should be adjusted individually, and treatment continued as long as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks according to the response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric ulcer:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> The recommended adult dose is 40 mg Esoral MUPS 40 mg given once daily by intravenous injection (not less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes). Esomeprazole IV injection should not be administered concomitantly with any other medications through the same intravenous site.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Esoral MUPS 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take one Esoral MUPS 40 mg pill or capsule one hour before a meal and consume it whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension:</strong> The entire packet\'s contents should be mixed with 15 ml of water. Stir thoroughly, wait 2-3 minutes for it to thicken, then stir again and consume within 30 minutes. If the suspension is taken via a tube, follow the same procedure and administer the mixture through a nasogastric or gastric tube.</li><li><strong>IV Injection:</strong> Administer 40 mg of Esoral MUPS 40 mg as an intravenous injection over at least three minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Esoral MUPS 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is metabolized by the enzymes CYP2C19 and CYP3A4. It does not significantly interact with medications processed by other CYP enzymes like 1A2, 2A6, 2C9, 2D6, 2E1, or 3A4.</li><li>Drugs that may interact with esomeprazole include diazepam, which can increase plasma levels when co-administered.</li><li>Medications with altered absorption due to pH changes, like ketoconazole and iron salts, may have reduced bioavailability when taken with esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Esoral MUPS 40 mg should not be used by individuals with known hypersensitivity to any of its formulations.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Esoral MUPS 40 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, and dry mouth.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Positive symptom response does not rule out the possibility of stomach cancer.</li><li>Esoral MUPS 40 mg should be taken at least one hour before meals.</li><li>For patients who have difficulty swallowing capsules, the pellets inside can be mixed with applesauce, but they should not be chewed or broken.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Esoral MUPS 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of acute overdose may include decreased motor activity, changes in breathing frequency, tremors, ataxia, and clonic convulsions.</li><li>Dialysis is not expected to remove esomeprazole effectively.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry area, below 30°C, protected from moisture and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Esomeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor that inhibits H+/K+-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell, reducing stomach acid production. It is a single optical isomer of omeprazole (S-isomer) with enhanced acid control.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>There is insufficient data regarding the safety of esomeprazole in pregnant women. Teratogenic effects have not been observed in animal studies.</li><li>It is recommended to stop breastfeeding if esomeprazole is deemed necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy have not been established in pediatric patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>No significant differences in safety or effectiveness between elderly and younger patients have been noted.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>For individuals with severe hepatic impairment, a dose of 20 mg once daily should not be exceeded.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>Since less than 1% of esomeprazole is excreted unchanged in urine, no dosage adjustment is needed in patients with renal impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '463_1742716852.jpg', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 13:00:52', '2025-05-03 10:14:39'),
(4278, 'Prompton', 'Rabeprazole Sodium', 9.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Prompton 20 mg (Rabeprazole) - Comprehensive Information</h3><h4><strong>Indications:</strong></h4><p>Prompton 20 mg gastro-resistant tablets of Rabeprazole are recommended for the treatment of:</p><ul><li><strong>Active Duodenal Ulcer</strong></li><li><strong>Active Benign Stomach Ulcer</strong></li><li><strong>Symptomatic Erosive or Ulcerative Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</strong></li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD) Long-Term Maintenance</strong></li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD (Moderate to Severe)</strong></li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong></li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication</strong> (in combination with suitable antibiotics)</li></ul><h4><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></h4><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</p><h4><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></h4><p>Rabeprazole inhibits gastric H+/K+-ATPase (the proton pump) at the parietal cell surface, leading to a decrease in gastric acid secretion. By blocking this proton pump, Rabeprazole reduces stomach acid production, aiding in the healing of ulcers and the treatment of GERD.</p><p><br></p><h4><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></h4><p><strong>For Active Duodenal Ulcer and Active Benign Gastric Ulcer:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>20 mg</strong> once daily in the morning for <strong>4 to 6 weeks</strong> (duodenal ulcer healing is typically achieved in 4 weeks; gastric ulcer may take longer).</li></ul><p><strong>For Erosive or Ulcerative GERD:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>20 mg</strong> once daily in the morning for <strong>4 to 8 weeks</strong>. If healing is not achieved after 8 weeks, an additional 8-week course may be considered.</li></ul><p><strong>For Symptomatic GERD (Moderate to Severe):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>20 mg</strong> once daily for <strong>4 weeks</strong>. If symptoms persist, another treatment course may be necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>For GERD Maintenance:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>20 mg</strong> once daily (controlled studies do not extend beyond <strong>12 months</strong>).</li></ul><p><strong>For Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong></p><ul><li>Start with <strong>60 mg</strong> once daily; dosage can be adjusted based on individual patient needs. Some may require doses up to <strong>100 mg</strong> daily or <strong>60 mg twice daily</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>For Helicobacter pylori Eradication (with Antibiotics):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Rabeprazole 20 mg</strong>, <strong>Amoxicillin 1000 mg</strong>, and <strong>Clarithromycin 500 mg</strong> twice daily for <strong>7 days</strong>.</li></ul><h4><strong>Administration:</strong></h4><ul><li>Take <strong>Prompton 20 mg</strong> in the morning before eating for optimal absorption and adherence.</li><li>Do not chew or crush the tablets; swallow them whole.</li></ul><h4><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Itraconazole/Ketoconazole:</strong> Rabeprazole may reduce absorption of these antifungals, requiring dose adjustments.</li><li><strong>Atazanavir:</strong> PPI use can reduce atazanavir absorption, and co-administration should be avoided.</li></ul><h4><strong>Contraindications:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to any component of Prompton 20 mg.</li><li>Not recommended for use during <strong>pregnancy</strong> or <strong>breastfeeding</strong>.</li></ul><h4><strong>Side Effects:</strong></h4><ul><li>Generally well tolerated but may cause:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Headache</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vomiting</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Constipation</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dry mouth</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Muscle pain</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dizziness</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sleepiness</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Changes in appetite</li></ul><h4><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>FDA Pregnancy Category C:</strong> No evidence from animal studies of harm to the fetus, but no controlled human studies are available. Rabeprazole should only be used if the benefit outweighs the risk.</li><li>Likely excreted in <strong>human milk</strong>; breastfeeding should be evaluated for benefit versus risk.</li></ul><h4><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Malignancy risk:</strong> Exclude malignancy (stomach/esophageal cancer) before treatment.</li><li><strong>Long-term use:</strong> May increase the risk of <strong>fractures</strong> (hip, wrist, spine), especially in elderly patients.</li><li>Patients at risk for <strong>osteoporosis</strong> should ensure adequate intake of <strong>calcium</strong> and <strong>vitamin D</strong>.</li></ul><h4><strong>Overdose:</strong></h4><ul><li>No specific antidote. Overdose effects are generally mild and reversible. Treatment should be symptomatic, with supportive measures as needed.</li></ul><h4><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></h4><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, away from light, at temperatures below <strong>30°C</strong>.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h4><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></h4><ul><li>Not recommended for use in children due to insufficient safety and efficacy data.</li><li><strong>Hepatic and renal impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment required for these conditions.</li></ul><h4><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></h4><p>Rabeprazole reduces gastric acid secretion by inhibiting the <strong>H+/K+-ATPase</strong> enzyme in the parietal cells of the stomach.</p>', 'active', '1821_1742716945.png', 10, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 13:02:25', '2025-05-04 10:32:44'),
(4279, 'Sina Silver', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral [A-Z silver preparation]', 9.00, 30, 'Tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', 'N/A', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sina Silver</strong></p><p>This is specially formulated for the prevention and treatment of vitamin and mineral deficiencies for adults over 45 years of age. This Silver is also indicated to meet the increased demands of vitamins and minerals for adults over 45 years of age.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Vitamin A 3500 IU</li><li>Vitamin C 60 mg</li><li>Vitamin D 400 IU</li><li>Vitamin E 45 IU</li><li>Vitamin K 10 mcg</li><li>Thiamine 1.5 mg</li><li>Riboflavin 1.7 mg</li><li>Niacin 20 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 3 mg</li><li>Folic acid 400 mcg</li><li>Vitamin B12 25 mcg</li><li>Biotin 30 mcg</li><li>Pantothenic acid 10 mg</li><li>Calcium 200 mg</li><li>Phosphorus 48 mg</li><li>Iodine 150 mcg</li><li>Magnesium 100 mg</li><li>Zinc 15 mg</li><li>Selenium 20 mcg</li><li>Copper 2 mg</li><li>Manganese 2 mg</li><li>Chromium 150 mcg</li><li>Molybdenum 75 mcg</li><li>Chloride 72 mg</li><li>Potassium 80 mg</li><li>Boron 150 mcg</li><li>Nickel 5 mcg</li><li>Silicon 2 mg</li><li>Vanadium 10 mcg</li><li>Lutein 250 mcg</li><li>Lycopene 300 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This preparation is a comprehensive, well-balanced multivitamin and multimineral preparation scientifically adjusted and designed to serve as the complete nutritional program for elderly people. This preparation helps maintain a healthy body, active lifestyle, and proper nutrition for elderly people.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sina Silver</strong></p><p>One tablet once daily with food or as indicated by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Sina Silver</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>This product is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients. Do not take this product if taking other vitamin A supplements.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sina Silver</strong></p><p>Generally, this preparation is well tolerated. Allergic sensitization has been reported following oral administration of folic acid. Vitamin C and vitamin E may cause diarrhea and other gastrointestinal disturbances.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Recommended by consultation with a physician.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Long-term intake of high levels of vitamin A (excluding that sourced from beta-carotene) may increase the risk of osteoporosis in postmenopausal women.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>This preparation is a comprehensive, well-balanced multivitamin and multimineral preparation scientifically adjusted and designed to serve as the complete nutritional program for elderly people. This preparation helps maintain a healthy body, active lifestyle, and proper nutrition for elderly people.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Recommended by consultation with a physician.</p>', 'active', '1416_1742717121.jpg', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 13:05:21', '2025-04-19 08:47:02'),
(4280, 'Nyclobate', 'Clobetasol Propionate', 200.00, 1, 'x 30 ml bottle', '30 ml bottle', 'Solution', '0.05%', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Nyclobate 30 ml</strong></p><p>Clobetasol Propionate scalp solution is indicated in the topical therapy of recalcitrant corticosteroid-responsive dermatoses of the scalp, including recalcitrant cases of psoriasis and seborrheic dermatitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Other Topical corticosteroids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Clobetasol Propionate is a very potent topical corticosteroid. It has anti-inflammatory, antipruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties. It shows anti-inflammatory activity via multiple mechanisms to inhibit late phase allergic reactions. It decreases the density of mast cells, chemotaxis, and activation of eosinophils. It also reduces cytokine production and inhibits the metabolism of arachidonic acid.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Nyclobate 30 ml</strong></p><p>Clobetasol Propionate spray: Apply the required quantity of spray once or twice daily to the affected areas of the scalp and gently rub in. The total dose applied should not exceed 50 ml weekly. If necessary, Clobetasol Propionate scalp solution may be massaged into the scalp using the tips of the fingers. Therapy should be discontinued if no response is noted after one week or as soon as the lesion heals. It is advisable to use Clobetasol Propionate scalp solution for brief periods only.</p><p>Clobetasol Propionate shampoo: Apply to the dry (not wet) scalp once a day to the affected areas only. Massage gently into the lesions and leave in place for 15 minutes before lathering and rinsing. Treatment should be limited to 4 consecutive weeks. The total dosage of shampoo should not exceed 50 g per week. This preparation is not recommended for those under 18 years of age.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Infections of the scalp</li><li>Hypersensitivity to the preparation</li><li>Not recommended for dermatoses in children under one year of age</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Nyclobate 30 ml</strong></p><p>As with other corticosteroids, prolonged use of large amounts or treatment of extensive areas can result in sufficient systemic absorption to produce the features of hypercortisolism. This effect is more likely to occur in infants and children, and if occlusive dressings are used.</p><p>Local atrophy may occur after prolonged treatment.</p><p>In rare instances, treatment of psoriasis with corticosteroids (or its withdrawal) is thought to have provoked the pustular form of the disease.</p><p>If signs of hypersensitivity appear with the use of Clobetasol Propionate Scalp Solution, the application should be stopped immediately.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Topical administration of corticosteroids to pregnant animals can cause abnormalities of fetal development. The relevance of this finding to humans has not been established; however, topical steroids should not be used extensively in pregnancy, i.e. in large amounts for prolonged periods.</p><p>The safe use of Clobetasol Propionate during lactation has not been established.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Care must be taken to keep the preparation away from the eyes.</li><li>Long-term continuous therapy with Clobetasol Propionate scalp solution should be avoided where possible, particularly in infants and children, as adrenal suppression can occur even without occlusion.</li><li>Topical corticosteroids may be hazardous in psoriasis for a number of reasons, including rebound relapses, development of tolerance, risk of generalized pustular psoriasis, and development of local or systemic toxicity due to impaired barrier function of the skin.</li><li>If used on psoriasis, careful patient supervision is important.</li><li>Appropriate antimicrobial therapy should be used whenever treating inflammatory lesions which have become infected. Any spread of infection requires withdrawal of topical corticosteroid therapy and systemic administration of antimicrobial agents.</li><li>Bacterial infection is encouraged by the warm, moist conditions induced by occlusive dressings, so the skin should be cleansed before applying a fresh dressing.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Do not freeze. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1053_1742717413.png', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 13:10:13', '2025-05-04 01:07:46'),
(4281, 'Avenac', 'Aceclofenac', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Avenac 100 mg</strong></p><p>Avenac 100 mg is indicated for the relief of pain and inflammation in osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, toothache, trauma, and lumbago.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Avenac 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Avenac 100 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Extended release tablet</strong>: The recommended dose in adults is one 200 mg Avenac 100 mg tablet daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Film-coated tablet</strong>: The recommended dose in adults is 100 mg, twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Avenac 100 mg</strong></p><p>No significant drug interactions have been observed, but close monitoring of patients is required when it is used with:</p><ul><li><strong>Lithium and Digoxin</strong>: May increase plasma concentration of lithium and digoxin.</li><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: May interact with the activity of diuretics.</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants</strong>: May enhance the activity of anticoagulants.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: May increase the plasma level of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Avenac 100 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to it or in whom aspirin or NSAIDs precipitate attacks of asthma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Avenac 100 mg</strong></p><p>Avenac 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of Avenac 100 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in patients with active or suspected peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding, moderate to severe hepatic impairment, and cardiac or renal impairment. Caution should also be exercised in patients suffering from dizziness or urticaria.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Avenac 100 mg in children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Avenac 100 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The use of Avenac 100 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Avenac 100 mg in children.</p>', 'active', '1922_1742717557.webp', 10, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 13:12:37', '2025-05-03 00:10:41'),
(4282, 'Becobion', 'Vitamin B1, B6 & B12', 13.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg+200 mg+200 mcg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Becobion 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg</strong></p><p>Becobion 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg is indicated for the treatment of vitamin B1, B6 &amp; B12 deficiency syndrome. It is also indicated for the supportive treatment of neuritis and non-inflammatory diseases of the nerves, such as:</p><ul><li>Diabetic neuropathy</li><li>Peripheral neuralgia</li><li>Lumbago</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Optic neuritis</li><li>Sciatica</li><li>Facial neuralgia</li><li>Intercostal neuralgia</li><li>Spinal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate) 100 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride) 200 mg</li><li>Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) 200 mcg</li></ul><p>Each 3 ml ampoule contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) 100 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) 100 mg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) 1000 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Becobion 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg is a combined preparation of Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate), Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride), and Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin). These vitamins play an essential role as coenzymes in the metabolism of the nervous system, supporting the regeneration of nerve fibers and myelin sheath by activating nerve cell metabolism and the natural repair mechanism.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablets</strong>: 1 to 3 tablets per day or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly (deep intragluteal). In severe cases, administer 1 ampoule daily until the acute symptoms subside. For milder cases and follow-up therapy, 2 to 3 ampoules per week.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Children</strong></p><p>There is no information available on the use of this drug in children.</p><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vitamin B1, Vitamin B6, and Vitamin B12 are contraindicated in patients on levodopa therapy and in patients with hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of the preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Generally well tolerated, but allergic reactions may be observed in a few cases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>No known risks associated with the use of this combination during pregnancy at the recommended dosage. Vitamin B1, B6, and B12 are secreted into breast milk, but the risks of overdose for the infant are not known.</p><p><strong>Note</strong>: Due to the presence of benzyl alcohol, the injection form is not recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Cyanocobalamin should not be given to patients with subacute degeneration of the spinal cord. Cyanocobalamin is also not suitable for the treatment of optic neuropathies associated with raised plasma concentrations of cyanocobalamin.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><p>No overdosage symptoms are expected at the recommended dosage. In the case of overdose, treatment is symptomatic and supportive.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep out of reach of children. Store in a cool (below 25°C), dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin B1</strong>: Converts carbohydrates, fatty acids, and amino acids into energy, promotes healthy nerves, improves mood, and strengthens the heart.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6</strong>: Forms RBCs, helps cells make proteins, manufactures neurotransmitters (e.g., serotonin), releases stored forms of energy, helps to prevent CVS diseases, and eases insomnia.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12</strong>: Essential for cell replication, important for RBC production, prevents anemia, reduces nerve pain, numbness, tingling, and lowers the risk of heart diseases.</li></ul><p>These vitamins are well absorbed when taken orally and are widely distributed to tissues. Thiamine is excreted in the urine, while pyridoxine and its metabolites are stored in the liver and excreted in urine.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Oral tablet form is recommended, but due to the presence of benzyl alcohol, injection is not recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</p>', 'active', '1826_1742717657.webp', 55, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 13:14:17', '2025-05-03 00:28:26'),
(4283, 'Cevion', 'Vitamin C [Ascorbic acid]', 8.08, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '1000 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Cevion 1000 mg (Ascorbic Acid/Vitamin C)</h3><p><strong>Cevion 1000 mg</strong> is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Prevention and treatment of scurvy</strong>: Scurvy is caused by a deficiency of vitamin C, leading to symptoms such as bleeding gums, anemia, and slow wound healing.</li><li><strong>Healing wounds and broken bones</strong>: Vitamin C aids in the repair of tissues and bone healing.</li><li><strong>Collagen formation</strong>: Essential for the formation of collagen in connective tissues.</li><li><strong>Iron absorption and anemia treatment</strong>: Helps in the absorption of iron, which is critical in preventing and treating anemia.</li><li><strong>Hemoglobin production</strong>: Contributes to the production of hemoglobin and red blood cells in the bone marrow.</li><li><strong>Immune system support</strong>: Boosts the immune system, helping prevent many viral and bacterial infections.</li><li><strong>Common cold prevention</strong>: Aids in the treatment and prevention of the common cold.</li><li><strong>Promotes healthy capillaries, gums, and teeth</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class:</h3><ul><li><strong>Vitamin-C Preparations</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology:</h3><p>Vitamin C, a water-soluble vitamin, is absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and is distributed throughout the body tissues. It plays a key role in biological oxidation, cellular respiration, and collagen synthesis. A deficiency leads to scurvy, with symptoms such as bleeding gums, anemia, cartilage and bone lesions, and slow wound healing.</p><p><br></p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cevion 1000 mg:</h3><h4>Oral Administration:</h4><ul><li><strong>Prevention of scurvy</strong>: 1 tablet daily.</li><li><strong>Treatment of scurvy</strong>: 1-2 tablets daily, increasing the dose based on severity.</li><li><strong>Reduction of stroke risk in the elderly</strong>: 1-2 tablets daily.</li><li><strong>Other conditions</strong>: 1 tablet daily or as directed by a physician.</li><li><strong>Maximum safe dose</strong>: 2000 mg daily in divided doses.</li></ul><h4>Parenteral Administration:</h4><ul><li><strong>Preferred Route</strong>: Intramuscular (IM) administration.</li><li><strong>Intravenous (IV) Injection</strong>: Dilute into Normal Saline, Water for Injection, or Glucose to minimize adverse reactions.</li><li><strong>Typical Dose</strong>: 70-150 mg daily for general protection, or 300 mg to 1 g daily for scurvy.</li><li><strong>Maximum Dose</strong>: Up to 6 g parenterally in adults without toxicity.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Cevion 1000 mg:</h3><p><strong>Potentially hazardous interactions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Incompatible with aminophylline, bleomycin, erythromycin, and other drugs.</li></ul><p><strong>Useful interactions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Enhances iron absorption and increases the half-life of paracetamol.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Cevion 1000 mg:</h3><ul><li><strong>Mild toxicity</strong>: Mega-doses may cause diarrhea, abdominal bloating, and iron over-absorption.</li><li><strong>Serious side effects</strong>: Hyperoxaluria, hyperuricosuria, and hemolysis in certain conditions.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: High doses (over 5 g/day) may cause fetal abortion.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Safe in normal doses, but excessive intake (5 g/day or more) can lead to abortion.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Safe to use during breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</h3><ul><li><strong>Mega-doses</strong>: Can cause scurvy in neonates and should be avoided, especially during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Contraindications</strong>: Avoid in patients with hyperoxaluria or who are at risk of kidney stones.</li><li><strong>False positives</strong>: May interfere with certain laboratory tests (e.g., glucose, uric acid).</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions:</h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place, below 30°C.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes:</h3><ul><li><strong>Vitamin-C Preparations</strong></li></ul><h3>Mode of Action:</h3><p>Ascorbic Acid is essential for tissue growth and repair, immune function, and healthy gums. It is an antioxidant that protects against cellular damage caused by pollution and infection. As a coenzyme, it aids in wound healing, drug metabolism, and cholesterol metabolism.</p>', 'active', '630_1742717835.webp', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 13:17:15', '2025-05-03 01:20:56'),
(4284, 'Progut MUPS', 'Esomeprazole', 11.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Progut MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><p>Progut MUPS 20 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Treatment of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</li><li>Healing of erosive esophagitis</li><li>Maintenance of healing of erosive esophagitis</li><li>Symptomatic GERD</li><li>Reduction of NSAID-associated gastric ulcer</li><li>H. pylori eradication (Triple therapy)</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Progut MUPS 20 mg exerts its stomach acid-suppressing effects by preventing the final step in gastric acid production by covalently binding to sulfhydryl groups of cysteines found on the H+/K+-ATPase enzyme at the secretory surface of gastric parietal cells. This leads to inhibition of both basal and stimulated gastric acid secretion, irrespective of the stimulus. Since the binding of Progut MUPS 20 mg to the H+/K+-ATPase enzyme is irreversible, new enzyme needs to be expressed to resume acid secretion, and this effect persists longer than 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Progut MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tablet or Capsule:</strong>Healing of erosive esophagitis: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4 to 8 weeks. The majority of patients heal within 4 to 8 weeks. For patients who do not heal after 4-8 weeks, an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance of healing of erosive esophagitis: 20 mg once daily. Controlled studies did not extend beyond six months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Symptomatic GERD: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely after 4 weeks, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">H. pylori eradication (Triple therapy): 20 mg twice daily for 10 days, in combination with Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days and Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection:</strong>GERD with Erosive Esophagitis: 20 or 40 mg given once daily by intravenous injection (no less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Progut MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>A solution for intravenous infusion is prepared by first reconstituting the contents of one vial with 5 ml of 0.9% Sodium Chloride and further diluting the resulting solution to a final volume of 50 ml. The resultant concentration after diluting to a final volume of 50 ml is 0.8 mg/ml.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg dose: Withdraw 25 ml of the final solution and administer as an intravenous infusion over 10 to 30 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10 mg dose: Withdraw 12.5 ml of the final solution and administer as an intravenous infusion over 10 to 30 minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Progut MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><p>Progut MUPS 20 mg appears to be a selective inhibitor of the cytochrome P450 mono-oxygenase system. There may be an effect on hepatic clearance, but no reports of clinically relevant interactions. There is some uncertainty about its effect on the oral combined contraceptive pill. Physiological changes similar to those found with omeprazole are likely to occur due to the reduction in gastric acid, which may influence bacterial colonization of the stomach and duodenum, as well as vitamin B12 absorption.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Progut MUPS 20 mg is contraindicated in patients who have known hypersensitivity to any other components of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Progut MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><p>Reported side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Abdominal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>US FDA pregnancy category of Progut MUPS 20 mg is C. Therefore, it should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Exclude the possibility of malignancy when gastric ulcer is suspected and before treatment for dyspepsia.</li><li>When using in combination with antibiotics, refer to the prescribing information of the respective antibiotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place and away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p>Directions for reconstitution of solution:</p><ul><li>Solution for injection is prepared by adding 5 ml of 0.9% Sodium Chloride for intravenous injection into the vial containing the dry powder.</li><li>The reconstituted solution for injection is clear and colorless to very slightly yellow.</li></ul>', 'active', '769_1742717964.webp', 17, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 13:19:24', '2025-05-04 10:32:19');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4285, 'Algicid', 'Sodium Alginate + Potassium Bicarbonate', 150.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '(500 mg+100 mg)/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is the provided information with bold text where appropriate:</p><p><strong>Indications of Algicid 200 ml</strong></p><p>This preparation is indicated for the treatment of symptoms resulting from the reflux of acid, bile, and pepsin into the esophagus such as acid regurgitation, heartburn, indigestion for instance, after gastric surgery, as a result of hiatus hernia, during pregnancy, accompanying reflux esophagitis, including symptoms of laryngopharyngeal reflux such as hoarseness and other voice disorders, sore throats, and cough. This preparation can also be used to treat the symptoms of gastroesophageal reflux during concomitant treatment with or following the withdrawal of acid-suppressing therapy.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Sodium Alginate is a naturally occurring substance. It reacts with the acid in the stomach to form a gel. Potassium Bicarbonate also reacts with the acid in the stomach to form bubbles of carbon dioxide. These bubbles are trapped by the gel formed by Sodium Alginate and they allow the gel to float like a raft on top of the stomach contents. The raft prevents acid in the stomach from flowing back into the food pipe. This relieves the symptoms of reflux such as heartburn. The mode of action of this preparation is physical and does not depend on absorption into the systemic circulation. The raft lasts for up to 4 hours on top of the stomach contents and is then broken down in the digestive system and excreted in the feces.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Algicid 200 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Tablet</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: 1-2 tablets daily 4 times, after meals and at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Children 6-12 years</strong>: 1 tablet daily 4 times, after meals and at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Suspension</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: 5-10 ml daily 4 times, after meals and at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Children 2-12 years</strong>: 2.5-5 ml daily 4 times, after meals and at bedtime.</li><li>This is not recommended for children under 2 years of age.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Algicid 200 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Tablet</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: 1-2 tablets 3-4 times daily, after meals and before bedtime.</li><li><strong>Children 6-12 years</strong>: ½-1 tablet 3-4 times daily, after meals and before bedtime.</li><li><strong>Suspension</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults and children 12 years and above</strong>: 5-10 ml (1-2 teaspoonfuls) 3-4 times daily, after meals and before bedtime.</li><li><strong>Children 2-12 years</strong>: 2.5-5 ml (½-1 teaspoonful) 3-4 times daily, after meals and before bedtime.</li><li>Use in children: This is not recommended under 2 years of age.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dose modifications are necessary for this age group.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dose modifications are necessary.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: Caution if a highly restricted salt diet is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Algicid 200 ml</strong></p><p>A time interval of 2 hours should be considered between this intake and the administration of other medicinal products, especially <strong>tetracyclines</strong>, <strong>fluoroquinolones</strong>, <strong>iron salts</strong>, <strong>thyroid hormones</strong>, <strong>chloroquine</strong>, <strong>bisphosphonates</strong>, and <strong>estramustine</strong>.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Sodium alginate and potassium bicarbonate are contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to these.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Algicid 200 ml</strong></p><p>In addition to the desired effect of the drug, some side effects may appear such as: <strong>nausea</strong>, <strong>constipation</strong>, <strong>diarrhea</strong>, or <strong>headache</strong>. In these cases, consult a physician. In case too large a dosage has been taken, there might appear a sensation of swelling. In this case, it is advisable to consult a physician.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: This combination may be used during pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: No known effect on breastfed infants. Can be used during breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Fertility</strong>: No known effect on human fertility.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Sodium Alginate and Potassium Bicarbonate should be prescribed with caution in patients with <strong>renal impairment</strong> and <strong>congestive cardiac failure</strong>. Care needs to be taken in treating patients with <strong>hypercalcemia</strong>, <strong>nephrocalcinosis</strong>, and <strong>recurrent calcium-containing renal calculi</strong>. There is a possibility of reduced efficacy in patients with very low levels of gastric acid. If symptoms do not improve after seven days, the clinical situation should be reviewed.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Algicid 200 ml</strong></p><p>Overdosage with this formulation is rare. In case of overdose, please consult with a registered physician.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dose modifications necessary for this age group.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dose modifications necessary.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: Caution if a highly restricted salt diet is necessary.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>This is the combination of <strong>Sodium Alginate</strong> and <strong>Potassium Bicarbonate</strong>. Sodium Alginate is a naturally occurring substance. It reacts with the acid in the stomach to form a gel. Potassium Bicarbonate also reacts with the acid in the stomach to form bubbles of carbon dioxide. These bubbles are trapped by the gel formed by Sodium Alginate and they allow the gel to float like a raft on top of the stomach contents. The raft prevents acid in the stomach from flowing back into the esophagus. This relieves the symptoms of reflux such as heartburn. The mode of action of this is physical and does not depend on absorption into the systemic circulation. The raft lasts for up to 4 hours on top of the stomach contents and is then broken down in the digestive system and excreted in the feces.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This combination (Sodium Alginate and Potassium Bicarbonate) can be used during pregnancy, if clinically needed. No known effect on breast-fed infants. This combination can be used during breastfeeding.</p>', 'active', '1693_1742718054.jpg', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 13:20:54', '2025-05-02 22:56:06'),
(4286, 'Ometid', 'Omeprazole', 6.00, 6, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Ometid 20 mg</h3><p>Ometid 20 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcers.</li><li>Gastric and duodenal ulcers caused by NSAIDs.</li><li>Prevention of duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by NSAIDs in individuals with a history of such ulcers.</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD).</li><li>Long-term management of acid reflux disease.</li><li>Acid-induced dyspepsia.</li><li>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers.</li><li>Prevention of acid aspiration during general anesthesia.</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>Peptic ulcer caused by Helicobacter pylori.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Ometid 20 mg is a benzimidazole derivative that inhibits stomach acid secretion. It works by blocking the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in the gastric parietal cells, preventing gastric acid release. After oral administration, the antisecretory effect begins within about 1 hour, peaks at 2 hours, and lasts for up to 72 hours. Secretory activity gradually resumes over a period of 3 to 5 days once the medication is stopped.</p><h3>Dosage of Ometid 20 mg</h3><p><strong>Oral Administration:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Benign gastric and duodenal ulcer:</strong>20 mg once daily for 4 weeks (duodenal ulcer) or 8 weeks (gastric ulcer).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In severe or recurrent cases, increase the dose to 40 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose for recurrent duodenal ulcer: 20 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>NSAID-associated ulcers:</strong>20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. Continue for an additional 4 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prophylaxis in patients with a history of NSAID-associated ulcers: 20 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD):</strong>20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If refractory, 40 mg once daily for 8 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 20 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Long-term management of acid reflux disease:</strong>10-20 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acid-related dyspepsia:</strong>10-20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of acid aspiration:</strong>40 mg the evening before surgery, then 40 mg 2-6 hours before surgery.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</strong>Initially, 60 mg once daily, with a usual range of 20-120 mg daily (divide the dose if more than 80 mg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication regimen in peptic ulcer disease:</strong>20 mg twice daily in combination with antimicrobial agents (Amoxicillin 500 mg, Metronidazole 400 mg, Clarithromycin 250 mg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric use for severe ulcerating reflux esophagitis (children &gt; 1 year):</strong>Weight 10-20 kg: 10-20 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Weight &gt; 20 kg: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Intravenous (IV) Administration:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration:</strong>40 mg IV slowly (over 5 minutes) one hour before surgery.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal ulcer, gastric ulcer, or reflux esophagitis (when oral medication is inappropriate):</strong>40 mg IV once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (ZES):</strong>Initial dose: 60 mg IV daily, may require higher doses adjusted individually.</li></ul><h3>Administration of Ometid 20 mg</h3><p><strong>For IV Injection:</strong></p><ul><li>Reconstitute the lyophilized powder with 10 ml of water for injection. Administer slowly (over 2-5 minutes) at a maximum rate of 4 ml/min.</li><li>Use freshly prepared solutions within 4 hours.</li><li>IV infusion: Dissolve the powder in 100 ml of 5% dextrose or saline. Infuse over 20-30 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Use saline solutions within 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Use dextrose solutions within 6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not mix with other medications in the same infusion set.</li></ul><h3>Interactions of Ometid 20 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Warfarin, phenytoin, and diazepam</strong> may have prolonged effects when taken with Ometid 20 mg, requiring possible dose adjustments.</li><li>No significant interaction with <strong>theophylline</strong>, <strong>propranolol</strong>, or <strong>antacids</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of Ometid 20 mg.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Ometid 20 mg</h3><p>Common and mild side effects include:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Stomach cramping</li><li>Paresthesia</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Headache</li></ul><p>These are usually temporary and may not require dosage adjustment.</p><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: Ometid 20 mg has not shown adverse effects in three prospective epidemiological studies, but its safety during pregnancy is not fully established.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Unknown if Ometid 20 mg passes into breast milk. It is advised to stop breastfeeding if the drug is necessary.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Gastric cancer</strong> should be ruled out before starting therapy with Ometid 20 mg for suspected stomach ulcers, as it may mask symptoms and delay diagnosis.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Ometid 20 mg inhibits stomach acid production by blocking the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase enzyme system in gastric parietal cells. The antisecretory effect begins within an hour and lasts for up to 72 hours, gradually resuming over 3-5 days after discontinuation.</p>', 'active', '1740_1742726494.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 15:41:34', '2025-05-04 01:18:54'),
(4287, 'Proviten Silver', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral [A-Z silver preparation]', 12.00, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', 'N/A', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here’s the text with the headings in bold as requested:</p><p><strong>Indications of Proviten Silver</strong></p><p>This is specially formulated for the prevention and treatment of vitamin and mineral deficiencies for adults over 45 years of age. This Silver is also indicated to meet the increased demands of vitamins and minerals for adults over 45 years of age.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each tablet contains: Vitamin A 3500 IU, Vitamin C 60 mg, Vitamin D 400 IU, Vitamin E 45 IU, Vitamin K 10 mcg, Thiamin 1.5 mg, Riboflavin 1.7 mg, Niacin 20 mg, Vitamin B6 3 mg, Folic acid 400 mcg, Vitamin B12 25 mcg, Biotin 30 mcg, Pantothenic acid 10 mg, Calcium 200 mg, Phosphorous 48 mg, Iodine 150 mcg, Magnesium 100 mg, Zinc 15 mg, Selenium 20 mcg, Copper 2 mg, Manganese 2 mg, Chromium 150 mcg, Molybdenum 75 mcg, Chloride 72 mg, Potassium 80 mg, Boron 150 mcg, Nickel 5 mcg, Silicon 2 mg, Vanadium 10 mcg, Lutein 250 mcg, Lycopene 300 mcg.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This preparation is a comprehensive well-balanced multivitamin and multimineral preparation scientifically adjusted and designed to serve as the complete nutritional program for elderly people. This preparation maintains a healthy body and active lifestyle and keeps proper nutrition covered for elderly people.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Proviten Silver</strong></p><p>One tablet once daily with food or as indicated by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Proviten Silver</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>This product is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients. Do not take this product if taking other vitamin A supplements.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Proviten Silver</strong></p><p>Generally, this preparation is well tolerated. Allergic sensitization has been reported following oral administration of folic acid. Vitamin C and vitamin E may cause diarrhea and other gastrointestinal disturbances.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Recommended by the consultation with physician.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Long-term intake of high levels of vitamin A (excluding that sourced from beta carotene) may increase the risk of osteoporosis in postmenopausal women.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>This preparation is a comprehensive well-balanced multivitamin and multimineral preparation scientifically adjusted and designed to serve as the complete nutritional program for elderly people. This preparation maintains a healthy body and active lifestyle and keeps proper nutrition covered for elderly people.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Recommended by the consultation with physician.</p>', 'active', '1624_1742726633.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 15:43:53', '2025-05-05 19:43:34'),
(4288, 'Viscocid', 'Sodium Alginate + Potassium Bicarbonate', 150.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '(500 mg+100 mg)/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Viscocid 200 ml</strong></p><p>This preparation is indicated for the treatment of symptoms resulting from the reflux of acid, bile, and pepsin into the esophagus, such as acid regurgitation, heartburn, indigestion (for instance, after gastric surgery), as a result of hiatus hernia, during pregnancy, accompanying reflux esophagitis, including symptoms of laryngopharyngeal reflux such as hoarseness and other voice disorders, sore throats, and cough. It can also be used to treat the symptoms of gastroesophageal reflux during concomitant treatment with or following the withdrawal of acid-suppressing therapy.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Sodium Alginate is a naturally occurring substance. It reacts with the acid in the stomach to form a gel. Potassium Bicarbonate also reacts with the acid in the stomach to form bubbles of carbon dioxide. These bubbles are trapped by the gel formed by Sodium Alginate, allowing the gel to float like a raft on top of the stomach contents. The raft prevents acid in the stomach from flowing back into the food pipe, relieving symptoms of reflux such as heartburn. This preparation\'s mode of action is physical and does not depend on absorption into the systemic circulation. The raft lasts for up to 4 hours on top of the stomach contents and is then broken down in the digestive system and excreted in the feces.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Viscocid 200 ml</strong></p><p><em>Tablet</em>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adult and child over 12 years</strong>: 1-2 tablets, 4 times daily, after meals and at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Child 6-12 years</strong>: 1 tablet, 4 times daily, after meals and at bedtime.</li><li><em>Suspension</em>:</li><li><strong>Adult and child over 12 years</strong>: 5-10 ml, 4 times daily, after meals and at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Child 2-12 years</strong>: 2.5-5 ml, 4 times daily, after meals and at bedtime.</li><li><em>Not recommended for children under 2 years of age.</em></li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Viscocid 200 ml</strong></p><p><em>Tablet</em>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: 1-2 tablets, 3-4 times daily, after meals and before bedtime.</li><li><strong>Children 6-12 years</strong>: ½-1 tablet, 3-4 times daily, after meals and before bedtime.</li><li><em>Suspension</em>:</li><li><strong>Adults and children 12 years and above</strong>: 5-10 ml (1-2 teaspoonfuls), 3-4 times daily, after meals and before bedtime.</li><li><strong>Children 2-12 years</strong>: 2.5-5 ml (½-1 teaspoonful), 3-4 times daily, after meals and before bedtime.</li><li><em>Use in children: Not recommended under 2 years of age.</em></li></ul><p><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dose modifications necessary.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dose modifications necessary.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: Caution if a highly restricted salt diet is necessary.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Viscocid 200 ml</strong></p><p>A time interval of 2 hours should be considered between this intake and the administration of other medicinal products, especially tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, iron salts, thyroid hormones, chloroquine, bisphosphonates, and estramustine.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Sodium Alginate and Potassium Bicarbonate are contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to these components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Viscocid 200 ml</strong></p><p>In addition to the desired effects, some side effects may appear such as nausea, constipation, diarrhea, or headache. In case of overdose, a sensation of swelling might appear. If this happens, it is advisable to consult a physician.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: This combination may be used during pregnancy if clinically needed.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: No known effect on breastfed infants. It can be used during breastfeeding.</li><li><strong>Fertility</strong>: No known effect on human fertility.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Sodium Alginate and Potassium Bicarbonate should be prescribed with caution in patients with renal impairment and congestive cardiac failure. Caution is also required in treating patients with hypercalcemia, nephrocalcinosis, and recurrent calcium-containing renal calculi. There is a possibility of reduced efficacy in patients with very low levels of gastric acid. If symptoms do not improve after seven days, the clinical situation should be reviewed.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Viscocid 200 ml</strong></p><p>Overdosage with this formulation is rare. In case of overdose, consult a registered physician.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dose modifications necessary.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dose modifications necessary.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: Caution if a highly restricted salt diet is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This preparation contains Sodium Alginate and Potassium Bicarbonate. Sodium Alginate reacts with stomach acid to form a gel, while Potassium Bicarbonate produces bubbles of carbon dioxide that are trapped in the gel. This allows the gel to float like a raft on top of the stomach contents, preventing acid reflux into the esophagus. This action is physical and not dependent on systemic absorption. The raft lasts for up to 4 hours and is then broken down and excreted in the feces.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This combination can be used during pregnancy if clinically necessary. No known effect on breast-fed infants. It can be used during breastfeeding.</p>', 'active', '1058_1742726759.webp', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 15:45:59', '2025-05-04 22:58:09'),
(4289, 'Ridon', 'Domperidone Maleate', 2.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ridon 10 mg</strong></p><p>Ridon 10 mg is indicated for the treatment of dyspeptic symptom complexes, often linked with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis. These symptoms may include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric sensation of fullness</li><li>Abdominal distension</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Belching</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Early satiety</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms can occur in various conditions, such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine. In Parkinson\'s disease, it can also manifest as dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting. Additionally, in radiological studies, it can be used to speed up barium transit in follow-through radiological examinations.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Motility Stimulants</li><li>Motility Stimulants/Dopamine Antagonist</li><li>Prokinetic Drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Ridon 10 mg functions as a dopamine receptor antagonist, exerting gastroprokinetic action by inhibiting dopamine receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier means minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ridon 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Adults</strong>: The recommended dosage is 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily.</li><li><strong>For Children</strong>: 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>When used for acute nausea and vomiting, the maximum treatment duration should not exceed 12 weeks. It is advisable to take this medication 15-30 minutes before meals.</p><p><strong>Dosage Guidelines</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Adults</strong>:</li><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily. The maximum daily dose is 80 mg.</li><li><strong>For Children</strong>:</li><li>2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg of body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ridon 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Anticholinergic Drugs</strong>: May counteract the antidyspeptic effects of domperidone.</li><li><strong>Antacids and Antisecretory Drugs</strong>: Should not be used concurrently as they can reduce its oral bioavailability.</li><li><strong>CYP3A4 Inhibitors</strong>: Co-administration with drugs that significantly inhibit this enzyme (e.g., azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics) may lead to elevated plasma levels of domperidone.</li><li><strong>Gastro-kinetic Effects</strong>: Domperidone may affect the absorption of concurrently administered oral drugs, especially sustained-release or enteric-coated formulations.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Ridon 10 mg is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients with known hypersensitivity to this medication or neonates.</li><li>Patients with gastrointestinal conditions where stimulation could be unsafe, such as in cases of gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation.</li><li>Patients with a prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ridon 10 mg</strong></p><p>Side effects are uncommon but may include:</p><ul><li>Transient intestinal cramps.</li><li>Rare extrapyramidal symptoms in young children, which resolve upon discontinuing treatment.</li><li>Increased plasma prolactin levels, potentially leading to galactorrhea or gynecomastia.</li><li>Rare allergic reactions, including rash and urticaria.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>The safety of Ridon 10 mg during pregnancy has not been established; hence, its use is not recommended.</li><li>Domperidone may induce galactorrhea and enhance post-natal lactation. It is excreted in breast milk in minimal quantities.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in children, as the risk of extrapyramidal reactions is higher due to an incompletely developed blood-brain barrier.</li><li>Caution is also advised in patients with hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ridon 10 mg</strong></p><p>Signs of overdose include:</p><ul><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Disorientation</li><li>Extrapyramidal reactions (particularly in children)</li></ul><p>If overdose occurs, administer activated charcoal and monitor the patient closely. Anticholinergic drugs, antiparkinson drugs, or antihistamines with anticholinergic properties may help manage extrapyramidal reactions.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Ridon 10 mg acts as a dopamine antagonist, blocking dopamine receptors in the CTZ and the stomach. It enhances gastric emptying, promotes antral and duodenal peristalsis, and regulates pyloric contraction. It also increases esophageal peristalsis and lowers esophageal sphincter pressure, preventing gastric contents from regurgitating.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Use with caution in infants and children under 1 year of age due to the immature blood-brain barrier, which may increase the risk of neurological side effects.</p><p><strong>Use in Liver and Kidney Disorders</strong></p><ul><li>In patients with hepatic impairment, Ridon 10 mg should be used with caution due to extensive metabolism in the liver.</li><li>For patients with severe renal insufficiency, the dosing frequency should be reduced, and regular reviews are advised.</li></ul><p>This medication is typically used to manage gastrointestinal motility disorders, and its careful administration is essential to avoid side effects.</p>', 'active', '945_1742726867.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 15:47:47', '2025-05-04 16:59:48'),
(4290, 'Opal', 'Omeprazole', 5.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Opal 20 mg</strong></p><p>Opal 20 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcer</li><li>Gastric and duodenal ulcers brought on by NSAIDs</li><li>In individuals who have a history of duodenal and stomach ulcers brought on by NSAIDs, as a preventative treatment</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</li><li>Care of acid reflux disease throughout the long term</li><li>Dyspepsia brought on by acid</li><li>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers</li><li>Prevention of aspiration of acid while under general anesthesia</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Peptic ulcer brought on by Helicobacter pylori</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Blocker</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Opal 20 mg is a proton pump inhibitor that works by inhibiting the secretion of stomach acid. It acts as a benzimidazole replacement, blocking the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in the gastric parietal cells. This prevents the release of gastric acid. The antisecretory effect starts within an hour of oral administration, peaks in two hours, and lasts up to 72 hours. After discontinuation, secretory activity gradually resumes within 3 to 5 days.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Opal 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Benign gastric and duodenal ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks in duodenal ulcers, 8 weeks in gastric ulcers; increase to 40 mg for severe or recurrent cases.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>NSAID-associated duodenal or gastric ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continued for 4 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastro-esophageal reflux disease</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continue for 4-8 weeks if not fully healed; 40 mg once daily for 8 weeks if GERD is refractory to other treatments.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acid-related dyspepsia</strong>: 10-20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: Initial dose 60 mg once daily, with usual range 20-120 mg daily (divide doses if &gt;80 mg).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication regimen</strong>: 20 mg twice daily with antimicrobial agents like Amoxicillin 500 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg, or Clarithromycin 250 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg for 1 week.</li><li><strong>Pediatric use in severe ulcerating reflux esophagitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Child weight 10-20 kg: 10-20 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child weight &gt;20 kg: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li><li><strong>IV Injection</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration</strong>: 40 mg IV, slow injection 1 hour before surgery.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal, gastric ulcer or reflux esophagitis</strong>: 40 mg IV once daily when oral administration is inappropriate.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: Initial dose 60 mg IV, adjust dose as needed (divide doses if &gt;60 mg).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Opal 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>: Reconstitute by adding 10 ml of water to the vial, then administer slowly (over 2-5 minutes) at a maximum flow rate of 4 ml/min. Use within 4 hours after reconstitution.</li><li><strong>IV Infusion</strong>: Dissolve the vial contents in 100 ml of 5% dextrose or saline for infusion. Use saline solution within 12 hours and dextrose solution within 6 hours. Administer infusion over 20-30 minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Opal 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>, <strong>phenytoin</strong>, and <strong>diazepam</strong>: May increase blood levels, requiring dose adjustments.</li><li>No significant interactions with <strong>theophylline</strong>, <strong>propranolol</strong>, or <strong>antacids</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient in Opal 20 mg formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Opal 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Generally well tolerated, but may cause mild and temporary side effects such as nausea, stomach cramping, paresthesia, dizziness, and headache. These rarely require dosage adjustments.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: No significant risk found in epidemiological studies, but use during pregnancy should be evaluated carefully.</li><li>Opal 20 mg\'s effects on breast milk and infants are not well-known; breastfeeding should be discontinued if the drug is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Rule out gastric cancer before starting therapy for a suspected stomach ulcer as Opal 20 mg could mask symptoms and delay diagnosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Blocker</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Opal 20 mg inhibits gastric acid production by blocking the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in parietal cells.</li></ul>', 'active', '1851_1742727016.webp', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 15:50:16', '2025-05-04 01:19:42'),
(4291, 'Clofenac', 'Diclofenac Sodium', 2.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Clofenac 50 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Rheumatology:</strong> Inflammatory and degenerative forms of rheumatism, chronic involutive polyarthritis, ankylosing spondylarthritis, osteoarthritis, spondylarthroses, acute gout, peri-articular rheumatic disorders.</li><li><strong>Surgery and Traumatology:</strong> Sprains, bruises, dislocations, fractures, soft tissue injuries, surgical interventions.</li><li><strong>Obstetrics and Gynecology:</strong> Primary dysmenorrhoea, episiotomy, adnexitis, endometritis, parametritis, salpingitis, mastitis.</li><li><strong>Otorhinolaryngology:</strong> Pre-operative medication for the prevention of pain, inflammation, and swelling.</li><li><strong>Dentistry:</strong> Post-operative and post-traumatic pain, inflammation, and swelling.</li><li><strong>Other indications:</strong> Prevention of pain and treatment of inflammation and swelling in patients after urogenital tract surgery, renal and biliary colic.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Clofenac 50 mg is a potent NSAID with significant anti-rheumatic, anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic properties. It also has some uricosuric effects. Diclofenac works by inhibiting prostaglandin biosynthesis, which plays a major role in causing inflammation, pain, and fever. It is rapidly absorbed after ingestion, with peak plasma concentrations typically reached within 2 hours. Diclofenac is 99.7% bound to plasma proteins and is metabolized in the liver.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Enteric Coated Tablet:</strong> 75-150 mg daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Sustained Release Tablets:</strong> One tablet daily, preferably during meals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppositories:</strong> 75-150 mg daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection:</strong> 1 ampoule daily, or 2 in severe cases.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gel:</strong> 2-4 g applied 3-4 times daily depending on the affected area.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Enteric Coated Tablet:</strong> 1-3 mg/kg per day in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Sustained Release Tablets:</strong> Not recommended.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppositories:</strong> 1-3 mg/kg body weight daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection (Juvenile Chronic Arthritis):</strong> 1-3 mg/kg daily in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Elderly Patients:</strong> Use the lowest effective dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>NSAIDs may inhibit diuretic activity.</li><li>Concomitant use with potassium-sparing diuretics may increase serum potassium levels.</li><li>Use with digoxin, methotrexate, cyclosporine, or lithium may require close monitoring for toxicity.</li><li>Combining with other NSAIDs or glucocorticoids may increase the risk of side effects like gastrointestinal bleeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Diclofenac or any of its ingredients.</li><li>Peptic ulcer, asthma, or allergic reactions to NSAIDs.</li><li>Diclofenac gel should not be applied under occlusive dressings.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Epigastric pain, nausea, diarrhea, dizziness, headache.</li><li>Less common: Peripheral edema, skin reactions (rash, eczema), irritation from the gel.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid use during pregnancy unless necessary. Use the lowest effective dose if absolutely required.</li><li>The gel is not recommended during pregnancy.</li><li>Diclofenac is excreted in breast milk in very small amounts and is unlikely to affect infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in patients with a history of gastrointestinal issues, such as peptic ulcers or bleeding.</li><li>Monitor elderly patients closely for side effects.</li><li>Avoid contact with eyes or mucous membranes when using the gel.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, away from light, below 30°C.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Diclofenac inhibits prostaglandin biosynthesis, reducing inflammation, pain, and fever. It is rapidly absorbed and has a high plasma protein binding rate (99.7%).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy Considerations:</strong></p><ul><li>Diclofenac should only be used in pregnancy when absolutely necessary. Avoid in the first trimester, and use the lowest effective dose.</li></ul>', 'active', '1350_1742727193.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 15:53:13', '2025-05-03 01:23:09'),
(4292, 'Minista', 'Rupatadine Fumarate', 12.04, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you provided with the headings in normal bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Minista 10 mg</strong></p><p>Minista 10 mg is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis and Urticaria.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Minista 10 mg is a second generation, non-sedating, long-acting histamine antagonist with selective peripheral H1 receptor antagonist activity. It further blocks the receptors of the platelet-activating factor (PAF) according to in vitro and in vivo studies. Rupatadine possesses anti-allergic properties such as the inhibition of the degranulation of mast cells induced by immunological and non-immunological stimuli, and inhibition of the release of cytokines, particularly of the tumor necrosis factors (TNF) in human mast cells and monocytes.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Minista 10 mg</strong></p><p>Rupatadine Tablet: The recommended dosage is 10 mg once daily, with or without food.</p><p>Rupatadine Oral Solution (Children aged 2 to 11 years):</p><ul><li>Children weighing 25 kg or more: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) of oral solution once daily with or without food.</li><li>Children weighing equal or more than 10 kg to less than 25 kg: 1/2 teaspoonful (2.5 ml) oral solution once daily with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Minista 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Adults and adolescents (over 12 years): The recommended dose is 10 mg once a day. Rupatadine may be taken with or without food.</li><li>Children aged 2 to 11 years:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing 25 kg or more: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) of the oral solution once daily with or without food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing equal or more than 10 kg to less than 25 kg: 1/2 teaspoonful (2.5 ml) oral solution once daily with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Minista 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>With medicine: The concomitant administration of Rupatadine 20 mg and ketoconazole or erythromycin increases the systemic exposure. Rupatadine should be used with caution when it is administered concomitantly with these drug substances and other inhibitors of the isozyme CYP3A4. Rupatadine should be used with caution when it is co-administered with statins, CNS depressants, or alcohol.</li><li>With food: Grapefruit and Grapefruit juice should not be taken simultaneously with Rupatadine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to Rupatadine or to any of the excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Minista 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common: Asthenia, dizziness, drowsiness.</li><li>Uncommon: Appetite increased, arthralgia, back pain, concentration impaired, constipation, cough, diarrhea, dry throat, epistaxis, fever, gastrointestinal discomfort, increased risk of infection, irritability, malaise, myalgia, nasal dryness, nausea, oropharyngeal pain, rash, thirst, vomiting, weight increased.</li><li>Rare: Palpitations, tachycardia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pregnancy category B2 (by TGA, Australia). There is no clinical data available on the exposure of Rupatadine during pregnancy. Pregnant women should therefore not use Rupatadine, unless the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk for the fetus. No information is available whether Rupatadine is excreted in the mother\'s milk. Therefore, it should not be used during lactation, unless the potential benefits for the mother justify the potential risk to the infant.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Minista 10 mg should be used with caution in patients with known prolongation of the QT interval, patients with uncorrected hypokalemia, and patients with ongoing proarrhythmic conditions, such as clinically significant bradycardia or acute myocardial ischemia. Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly patients (65 years and older). As there is no clinical experience in patients with impaired kidney or liver function, the use of Rupatadine 10 mg tablets is at present not recommended in these patients.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Minista 10 mg</strong></p><p>The most common adverse reaction was somnolence. If accidental ingestion of very high doses occurs, symptomatic treatment together with the required supportive measures should be given.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in cool &amp; dry place below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly. No information is available that indicates the requirement of any dose adjustment in this population.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Neither the safety nor the efficacy of Rupatadine has been established in patients less than 12 years of age.</li><li><strong>Patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency</strong>: Use of Rupatadine is not recommended in patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency. As no relevant clinical data is available.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Minista 10 mg is a long-acting, non-sedative antagonist of histamine H1-receptors. It also antagonizes the platelet activating factor (PAF). Both histamine and PAF cause bronchoconstriction which leads to an increase in vascular permeability and act as a mediator in the inflammatory process. With the dual mode of action, Rupatadine shows better therapeutic effect than an isolated antihistamine. Rupatadine possesses other anti-allergic properties such as the inhibition of the degranulation of mast cells induced by immunological and non-immunological stimuli and inhibition of the release of cytokines, particularly of the tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF α) in human mastocytes and monocytes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There is no clinical data available on the exposure of Rupatadine during pregnancy. Pregnant women should therefore not use Rupatadine unless the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk for the infant. No information is available whether Rupatadine is excreted in the mother\'s milk. Therefore, it should not be used during lactation, unless the potential benefits for the mother justify the potential risk to the infant.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly. No information is available that indicates the requirement of any dose adjustment in this population.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Neither the safety nor the efficacy of Rupatadine has been established in patients less than 12 years of age.</li><li><strong>Patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency</strong>: Use of Rupatadine is not recommended in patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency. As no relevant clinical data is available.</li></ul>', 'active', '1483_1742727476.webp', 10, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 15:57:56', '2025-05-04 00:05:21');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4293, 'Montilab', 'Montelukast Sodium', 17.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Montilab 10 mg</h3><p>Montilab 10 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</strong></li><li><strong>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</strong></li><li><strong>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</strong></li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li>The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from various cells, including mast cells and eosinophils.</li><li>Cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) are located in the human airway, and these eicosanoids bind to them.</li><li>Montilab 10 mg is an orally active drug with a strong affinity and specificity for the CysLT1 receptor. It inhibits LTD4\'s physiological effects at the CysLT1 receptor without any agonist activity.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Montilab 10 mg</h3><h4>Adults and Adolescents with Asthma or Seasonal Allergic Rhinitis:</h4><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (15 years and older):</strong> 10 mg tablet once daily.</li></ul><h4>Pediatric Patients with Asthma or Seasonal Allergic Rhinitis:</h4><ul><li><strong>Pediatric patients (6 to 14 years):</strong> 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients (2 to 5 years):</strong> 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients (6 months to 5 years):</strong> 4 mg oral granules once daily (can be administered directly in the mouth or mixed with cold water or soft food at room temperature).</li></ul><h4>Use in Pediatric Patients:</h4><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Montilab have been established in controlled studies for pediatric patients aged 6 months to 14 years. The safety and efficacy profiles in this group are similar to those in adults.</li></ul><h4>Hepatic Insufficiency:</h4><ul><li>No dosage adjustment is required for patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</li></ul><h4>Renal Insufficiency:</h4><ul><li>No dosage adjustment is recommended for patients with renal insufficiency.</li></ul><h4>Elderly Use:</h4><ul><li>The pharmacokinetic profile of Montilab is similar in elderly and younger adults. The plasma half-life is slightly longer in the elderly, but no dosage adjustment is needed.</li></ul><h3>Interactions of Montilab 10 mg</h3><ul><li>In asthma treatment, Montilab can be used alongside other treatments without significant side effects. Drug interactions with theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives, terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin showed no clinically significant effects at the recommended dose.</li><li>However, hepatic enzyme-inducing drugs (e.g., phenobarbital) may reduce Montilab\'s AUC by about 40%. Clinical monitoring is recommended when used with such drugs.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Extreme sensitivity</strong> to any ingredient in Montilab 10 mg.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Montilab 10 mg</h3><h4>Common:</h4><ul><li>Upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, vomiting.</li></ul><h4>Uncommon:</h4><ul><li>Akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, asthenia, aberrant behavior, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, hemorrhage, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, edema, seizure, odd feelings, and sleep disturbances.</li></ul><h4>Rare:</h4><ul><li>Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, decreased attention, disorientation, erythema nodosum, hallucinations, hepatic problems, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, and tremors.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>Montilab 10 mg is classified as <strong>pregnancy category B</strong>. Although the medication crosses the placenta in rats and rabbits, there is no data for humans.</li><li>Montilab is secreted in breast milk, but little is known about its implications. Caution should be exercised when administering it to nursing mothers.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Not for use in acute asthma attacks</strong> (e.g., status asthmaticus). Patients should have access to appropriate rescue medications.</li><li>Should not be used to treat <strong>Exercise-Induced Bronchospasm (EIB)</strong> with a single dose.</li><li>Patients with <strong>aspirin sensitivity</strong> should avoid aspirin or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) while taking Montilab, as aspirin sensitivity may not be alleviated by the drug.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Montilab 10 mg</h3><ul><li>Overdose reports typically show common side effects such as nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain.</li><li>In case of overdose, <strong>supportive measures</strong> such as removing unabsorbed material and clinical monitoring are recommended.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong> in a cool, dry environment, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><h3>Drug Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</strong></li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>Montilab 10 mg inhibits the cysteinyl leukotriene receptor (CysLT1), which plays a key role in the inflammatory processes of asthma and allergic rhinitis, including airway edema and smooth muscle contraction.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li>Montilab 10 mg passes the placenta in rats and rabbits after oral administration. However, controlled studies in humans are lacking. It should only be used during pregnancy if absolutely necessary. Caution is advised when administering to nursing mothers due to secretion in breast milk.</li></ul>', 'active', '1484_1742727626.jpg', 68, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 16:00:26', '2025-05-04 00:08:28'),
(4294, 'Ebatrol', 'Ebastine', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ebatrol 10 mg</strong></p><p>Ebatrol 10 mg is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of:</p><ul><li>Seasonal and Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li><li>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Ebatrol 10 mg, a piperidine derivative, is a long-acting, nonsedating, second-generation histamine receptor antagonist that binds preferentially to peripheral H1 receptors. It is metabolized to an active metabolite, carEbatrol 10 mg. It has antihistaminic and antiallergic activity, preventing histamine-induced bronchoconstriction. It does not have significant sedative or antimuscarinic actions.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Ebatrol 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults (more than 12 years of age): 10 mg (one tablet) once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (6-12 years of age): 5 mg (half tablet) once daily.</li><li><strong>Syrup</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (2-5 years of age): 2.5 ml once daily (up to 5 ml in severe cases such as Perennial Allergic Rhinitis).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children (6-12 years of age): 5 ml once daily (up to 10 ml in severe cases such as Perennial Allergic Rhinitis).</li></ul><p>Ebatrol 10 mg may be taken with or without food.</p><p><strong>Administration of Ebatrol 10 mg</strong></p><p>Ebatrol 10 mg may be taken with or without food.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Ebatrol 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Ebatrol 10 mg in combination with either ketoconazole or erythromycin increases the plasma level of Ebatrol 10 mg and prolongs the QTc interval.</li><li>Ebatrol 10 mg does not interact with the pharmacokinetics of theophylline, warfarin, cimetidine, diazepam, or alcohol. The sedative effect of alcohol and diazepam may be enhanced.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with a known hypersensitivity to Ebatrol 10 mg or any of its ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ebatrol 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>The most common side effects are headache, dry mouth, and drowsiness.</li><li>Less commonly reported side effects include abdominal pain, dyspepsia, nausea, and insomnia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of Ebatrol 10 mg during pregnancy and lactation has not been established.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Exercise caution when using Ebatrol 10 mg in patients with the following conditions: long QT syndrome, hypokalemia, or treatment with any medicine known to produce an increase in the QT interval or inhibit CYP3A4 enzyme systems, such as azole antifungals and macrolide antibiotics.</li><li><strong>Use in Pregnancy and Lactation</strong>: The safety of Ebatrol 10 mg during pregnancy and lactation has not been established. Ebatrol 10 mg should not be used during pregnancy unless clearly needed. It is not known whether Ebatrol 10 mg is excreted in milk, therefore it should not be used during lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ebatrol 10 mg</strong></p><p>No clinically meaningful signs or symptoms were observed up to 100 mg given once daily. There is no specific antidote for Ebatrol 10 mg. In case of accidental overdose, gastric lavage, monitoring of vital functions including ECG, and symptomatic treatment should be carried out.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry place, away from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Ebatrol 10 mg is a long-acting and selective H1-histamine receptor antagonist. After repeated administration, inhibition of peripheral receptors remains at a constant level. Ebatrol 10 mg is rapidly absorbed and undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism following oral administration. Ebatrol 10 mg is almost totally converted to the pharmacologically active acid metabolite, carEbatrol 10 mg.</p>', 'active', '980_1742727808.jpg', 33, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 16:03:28', '2025-05-03 10:00:48'),
(4295, 'Improcal', 'Calcitriol', 9.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '0.25 mcg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Improcal 0.25 mcg</strong></p><p>Improcal 0.25 mcg is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Post-menopausal osteoporosis</li><li>Renal osteodystrophy</li><li>Postsurgical hypoparathyroidism</li><li>Idiopathic hypoparathyroidism</li><li>Pseudohypoparathyroidism</li><li>Secondary hyperparathyroidism in patients with moderate to severe chronic renal failure (pre-dialysis)</li><li>Vitamin D dependent rickets</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin-D preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Improcal 0.25 mcg is one of the most important active metabolites of vitamin D3. It is normally formed in the kidneys from its precursor, 25-hydroxycholecalciferol. Improcal 0.25 mcg promotes intestinal absorption of calcium and regulates bone mineralization. The key role of Improcal 0.25 mcg is the regulation of calcium homeostasis, which includes stimulation effects on osteoblastic activity in the skeleton.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Improcal 0.25 mcg</strong></p><p>The optimal daily dose of Improcal 0.25 mcg capsule must be carefully determined for each patient on the basis of serum calcium level.</p><ul><li><strong>In Post-menopausal Osteoporosis:</strong> The recommended dose of Improcal 0.25 mcg capsule is 0.25 mcg twice daily.</li><li><strong>In Renal Osteodystrophy (dialysis patients):</strong> The initial daily dose is 0.25 mcg of Improcal 0.25 mcg capsule. In patients with normal or only slightly reduced calcium levels, doses of 0.25 mcg every other day are sufficient.</li><li><strong>In Hypoparathyroidism and Rickets:</strong> The recommended initial dosage of Improcal 0.25 mcg capsule is 0.25 mcg/day, which is given in the morning. If within 2-4 weeks satisfactory response is not observed by the usual dose, then the dose may be increased at 2-4 week intervals.</li><li><strong>For Intravenous administration (Hypoparathyroidism):</strong> The recommended initial intravenous dose of Improcal 0.25 mcg, depending on the severity of hypocalcemia and/or secondary hyperparathyroidism, is 1 mcg (0.02 mcg/kg) to 2 mcg administered three times weekly, approximately every other day. Doses as small as 0.5 mcg and as large as 4 mcg three times weekly have been used as an initial dose. If a satisfactory response is not observed, the dose may be increased by 0.5 to 1 mcg at two to four week intervals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Improcal 0.25 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant treatment with thiazide diuretics increases the risk of hypercalcemia.</li><li>Improcal 0.25 mcg dosage must be determined with care in patients undergoing treatment with digitalis, as hypercalcemia in such patients may precipitate cardiac arrhythmias.</li><li>Magnesium-containing drugs (e.g., antacids) may cause hypermagnesemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Improcal 0.25 mcg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of its ingredients. Improcal 0.25 mcg is also contraindicated in all diseases associated with hypercalcemia.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Improcal 0.25 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>Occasional symptoms include anorexia, headache, vomiting, and constipation.</li><li>Chronic effects may include dystrophy, fever, polyuria, dehydration, apathy, and urinary tract infection.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>There is no evidence that vitamin D is teratogenic in humans. Improcal 0.25 mcg may be used during pregnancy only if the benefits outweigh the potential risk to the fetus. Mothers may breastfeed while taking Improcal 0.25 mcg, but serum calcium levels of the mother and infant should be monitored.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>During Improcal 0.25 mcg therapy, if the serum calcium levels rise to 1 mg/100 ml above normal or serum creatinine rises to &gt;120 µmol/l, the dosage of Improcal 0.25 mcg should be substantially reduced or treatment stopped.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Secondary hyperparathyroidism in patients with moderate to severe chronic renal failure (pre-dialysis):</strong> The recommended initial dosage of Improcal 0.25 mcg is 0.25 mcg/day in adults and pediatric patients 3 years of age or older. This dosage may be increased if necessary to 0.5 mcg/day. For pediatric patients less than 3 years of age, the recommended initial dosage of Improcal 0.25 mcg is 10 to 15 ng/kg/day.</li><li><strong>In Elderly:</strong> No dosage adjustments are required.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin-D preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Improcal 0.25 mcg is one of the most important active metabolites of vitamin D3. It is normally formed in the kidneys from its precursor, 25-hydroxycholecalciferol. Improcal 0.25 mcg promotes intestinal absorption of calcium and regulates bone mineralization. The key role of Improcal 0.25 mcg is the regulation of calcium homeostasis, which includes stimulation effects on osteoblastic activity in the skeleton.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There is no evidence that vitamin D is teratogenic in humans. Improcal 0.25 mcg may be used during pregnancy only if the benefits outweigh the potential risk to the fetus. Mothers may breastfeed while taking Improcal 0.25 mcg, but serum calcium levels of the mother and infant should be monitored.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Secondary hyperparathyroidism in patients with moderate to severe chronic renal failure (pre-dialysis):</strong> The recommended initial dosage of Improcal 0.25 mcg is 0.25 mcg/day in adults and pediatric patients 3 years of age or older. This dosage may be increased if necessary to 0.5 mcg/day. For pediatric patients less than 3 years of age, the recommended initial dosage of Improcal 0.25 mcg is 10 to 15 ng/kg/day.</li><li><strong>In Elderly:</strong> No dosage adjustments are required.</li></ul>', 'active', '992_1742727951.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 16:05:51', '2025-05-03 21:01:06'),
(4296, 'Tasteme Orange Flavour', 'Instant Soft Drink Powder', 12.00, 1, 'x 25 gm bottle', '25 gm bottle', 'Powder', '0 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1132_1742746980.png', 12, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 21:23:00', '2025-05-04 22:18:05'),
(4297, 'Rynex', 'Oxymetazoline Hydrochloride', 40.00, 1, 'x 200 metered spray', '200 metered spray', 'Nasal Spray', '25 mcg/actuation', 'no', 'no', '<p><br></p>', 'active', '5714_1746507148.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 21:27:39', '2025-05-06 08:53:43'),
(4298, 'Dysnov', 'Domperidone Maleate', 3.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Dysnov 10 mg</strong></p><p>Dyspeptic symptom complex, often linked with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, may include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric sensation of fullness</li><li>Abdominal distension</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Belching</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Early satiety</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms can occur in various conditions, such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine. Additionally, in Parkinson\'s disease, it can manifest as dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting. For radiological studies, it can be used to speed up barium transit in follow-through radiological examinations.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Dysnov 10 mg functions as a dopamine receptor antagonist. It exerts gastroprokinetic action by inhibiting dopamine receptors found in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier means it has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Dysnov 10 mg</strong></p><p>For adults, the recommended dosage is 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily. Children should take 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily. When used for acute nausea and vomiting, the maximum treatment duration should not exceed 12 weeks. It is advisable to take this medication 15-30 minutes before a meal.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Dysnov 10 mg</strong></p><p>Dysnov 10 mg should be taken 15-30 minutes before meals, and if needed, before bedtime. The typical recommended oral dosage of Domperidone is as follows:</p><p><strong>For Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li>The maximum daily dose of Domperidone is 80 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg of body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>In cases of dyspeptic symptoms: <strong>For Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>For acute and sub-acute conditions, especially in cases of acute nausea and vomiting: <strong>For Adults:</strong></p><ul><li>20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>When administered rectally in the form of suppositories: <strong>For Adults (including elderly):</strong></p><ul><li>30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>For Children:</strong></p><ul><li>The maximum daily rectal dose for children weighing between 10 to 25 kg is 30 mg. The dose may be divided throughout the day if necessary.</li></ul><p>Please note that the maximum treatment period for acute nausea and vomiting is 12 weeks.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction of Dysnov 10 mg</strong></p><p>The simultaneous use of anticholinergic drugs may counteract the antidyspeptic effects of domperidone. It\'s important to note that antacids and antisecretory drugs should not be administered concurrently with domperidone as they can reduce its oral bioavailability. Domperidone primarily undergoes metabolism through the CYP3A4 enzyme. In vitro data suggest that coadministration with drugs significantly inhibiting this enzyme may lead to elevated plasma levels of domperidone. Examples of CYP3A4 inhibitors include azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, nefazodone, and others.</p><p>Theoretically, as domperidone has gastro-kinetic effects, it could potentially impact the absorption of concurrently orally administered drugs, especially those with sustained release or enteric-coated formulations. However, in patients already stabilized on medications like digoxin or paracetamol, the concurrent use of domperidone did not affect the blood levels of these drugs. Domperidone does not potentiate the action of neuroleptics, and it suppresses the unwanted peripheral effects such as digestive disorders, nausea, and vomiting caused by dopaminergic agonists like bromocriptine and L-dopa without counteracting their central properties.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Dysnov 10 mg is not to be used by patients with a known hypersensitivity to this medication or in the case of neonates. It should also be avoided in situations where gastrointestinal stimulation could be unsafe, such as in cases of gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation. Additionally, it is contraindicated in patients with a prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma).</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Dysnov 10 mg</strong></p><p>Side effects are uncommon, with occasional reports of transient intestinal cramps. Extrapyramidal symptoms are rare among young children and exceptionally rare in adults. These symptoms typically resolve spontaneously and completely upon discontinuing the treatment. Since the pituitary gland is situated outside the blood-brain barrier, domperidone may lead to an increase in plasma prolactin levels. In rare cases, this hyperprolactinemia can result in neuroendocrinological effects like galactorrhea and gynecomastia. In situations where the blood-brain barrier is immature (as in infants) or compromised, the possibility of neurological side effects cannot be entirely ruled out. Additionally, there have been occasional reports of rare allergic reactions, including rash and urticaria.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of using Dysnov 10 mg during pregnancy has not been established; hence, its use is not recommended during pregnancy. Animal studies have not shown any teratogenic effects on the fetus. Domperidone may induce galactorrhea and enhance post-natal lactation. It is excreted in breast milk in minimal quantities, which are insufficient to be considered harmful.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Dysnov 10 mg should be employed with utmost care in children due to the potential for an increased risk of extrapyramidal reactions, which can be more pronounced in young children with an incompletely developed blood-brain barrier. Additionally, considering that domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should also be used cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Dysnov 10 mg</strong></p><p>Signs of an overdose can manifest as drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions, particularly in children. If an overdose occurs, it is advisable to administer activated charcoal and closely monitor the patient. The use of anticholinergic drugs, antiparkinson drugs, or antihistamines with anticholinergic properties may be beneficial in managing extrapyramidal reactions.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, Protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Dysnov 10 mg acts as a dopamine antagonist, primarily by blocking dopamine receptors located in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its gastroprokinetic effect is achieved by inhibiting dopamine receptors that influence the motility of the gastrointestinal tract. Because it has limited ability to penetrate the blood-brain barrier, Domperidone has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects. Domperidone restores normal motility and tone in the upper gastrointestinal tract, promotes gastric emptying, enhances antral and duodenal peristalsis, and regulates pylorus contraction. Additionally, it increases esophageal peristalsis and lowers esophageal sphincter pressure, preventing the regurgitation of gastric contents.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p><strong>Use in infants:</strong> Given that the metabolic and blood-brain barrier functions are not fully developed during the first months of life, any medication should be administered to infants with great caution and under close medical supervision. Although the typical absence of neurological side effects with domperidone is primarily due to its limited penetration through the blood-brain barrier, the potential occurrence of such effects cannot be entirely ruled out in infants under 1 year of age.</p><p><strong>Use in liver disorders:</strong> Because domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should be employed cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Use in kidney disorders:</strong> In individuals with severe renal insufficiency (serum creatinine &gt;6 mg/100ml, i.e., &gt;0.6 mmol/l), the elimination half-life of domperidone increases from 7.4 to 20.8 hours, although plasma drug levels remain lower than in healthy volunteers. Since very little unchanged drug is excreted through the kidneys, it is unlikely that the dose of a single acute administration needs to be adjusted in patients with renal insufficiency. However, for repeated administration, the dosing frequency should be reduced to once or twice daily, depending on the severity of the impairment, and the dose may need to be lowered. In general, patients on prolonged therapy should undergo regular reviews.</p>', 'active', '1780_1742747352.webp', 8, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 21:29:12', '2025-05-03 09:59:43'),
(4299, 'Montiva', 'Montelukast Sodium', 16.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Montiva 10 mg</strong></p><p>Montiva 10 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. Cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) are located in the human airway, and these eicosanoids bind to them. The orally active drug Montiva 10 mg has a strong affinity and specificity for the CysLT1 receptor. Without any agonist activity, it inhibits LTD4\'s physiological effects at the CysLT1 receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Montiva 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</strong></li><li>The dosage for adults and adolescents 15 years of age and older: Montiva 10 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</strong>6 to 14 years of age: Montiva 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 to 5 years of age: Montiva 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6 months to 5 years of age: Montiva 4 mg oral granules once daily. This can be administered either directly in the mouth, or mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in the pediatric patient:</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Montiva have been established in adequate and well-controlled studies in pediatric patients with asthma 6 months to 14 years of age. Safety and efficacy profiles in this age group are similar to those seen in adults.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency:</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is required in patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is recommended in patients with renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Elderly use:</strong></p><p>The pharmacokinetic profile and the oral bioavailability of a single 10-mg oral dose of Montiva are similar in elderly and younger adults. The plasma half-life of Montiva is slightly longer in the elderly. No dosage adjustment in the elderly is required.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Montiva 10 mg</strong></p><p>In the prevention and long-term treatment of asthma, Montiva 10 mg has been provided alongside other commonly used treatments without seeming to increase the risk of negative side effects. According to studies on drug interactions, theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives (norethindrone 1mg/ethinyl estradiol 35mcg), terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin did not exhibit any clinically significant effects on the pharmacokinetics at the recommended clinical dose of it. In clinical investigations, the medication was used concurrently with a variety of routinely prescribed medications without showing any signs of clinically harmful interactions, despite the fact that more detailed interaction studies were not carried out. Thyroid hormones, hypnotic sedatives, benzodiazepines, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and decongestants were among these drugs. Following a single dose of this solution, the hepatic metabolism-inducing drug phenobarbital reduced the drug\'s AUC by about 40%. For this medication, no dosage modification is advised. When powerful cytochrome P450 enzyme inducers, such as phenobarbital or rifampin, are provided alongside this solution, it is fair to use proper clinical monitoring.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those who have an extreme sensitivity to any ingredient in this medication should not take it.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Montiva 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon:</strong> Akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, asthenia, aberrant behavior, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, hemorrhage, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, oedema, seizure, odd feeling, and sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, decreased attention, disorientation, erythema nodosum, hallucinations, hepatic problems, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, and tremor.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Montiva 10 mg is classified as pregnancy category B. Although it has been demonstrated that the medication crosses the placenta of pregnant rats and rabbits, there are no records of its use in pregnant humans. Although there is some evidence that it is also secreted in breast milk, little is known about the implications of this discovery. Before starting the therapy in nursing moms, caution should be exercised.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>The reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks, including status asthmaticus, is not recommended as usage for Montiva 10 mg. Patients should be told to have access to the proper rescue medication.</li><li>Asthma exacerbations that are acute can still be treated with the solution.</li><li>While the dosage of an inhaled corticosteroid may be gradually decreased under medical supervision, the use of this solution instead of an inhaled or oral corticosteroid should not be started suddenly.</li><li>Exercise-induced bronchospasm should not be treated and managed with a single dose of Montiva.</li><li>While taking Montiva, patients with documented aspirin sensitivity should continue to refrain from taking aspirin or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). Although aspirin sensitivity in asthmatics is a known side effect of the drug, it has not been demonstrated to shorten the bronchoconstrictor response to aspirin and other NSAIDs.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Montiva 10 mg</strong></p><p>The majority of overdose reports did not include any negative experiences. The most frequent side effects, which were in line with Montiva 10 mg\'s safety profile, were nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain. In the case of an overdose, it is permissible to take the customary supportive measures, such as removing any unabsorbed material from the digestive tract, using clinical monitoring, and if necessary, instituting supportive therapy.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry environment while keeping out light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>The cysteinyl leukotriene receptor is inhibited by the selective and orally active leukotriene receptor antagonist known as Montiva 10 mg (CysLT1). The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. The pathophysiology of asthma and allergic rhinitis, including airway edema, smooth muscle contraction, and altered cellular activity associated with the inflammatory process, have been linked to cysteinyl leukotrienes and leukotriene receptor occupation. These factors all contribute to the signs and symptoms of asthma.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>After oral administration, Montiva 10 mg passes the placenta in rats and rabbits. Nevertheless, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. Because the human reaction to medications is not usually predicted by research on animal reproduction, pregnant women should only use it if absolutely necessary. When giving the pill to a nursing mother, care should be taken because many medications are secreted in human milk.</p>', 'active', '89_1742748076.webp', 11, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 21:41:16', '2025-05-04 00:08:36'),
(4300, 'ZIF Forte', 'Carbonyl Iron + Folic Acid + Zinc Sulfate + Vitamin B Complex + Vitamin C', 4.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '0 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of ZIF Forte</strong></p><p>It is indicated for the treatment and prophylaxis of Iron, Folic Acid, Vitamin B Complex, Vitamin C, and Zinc deficiency, especially during pregnancy and lactation. It is also indicated for geriatric patients with generalized weakness due to vitamins and minerals deficiency.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each capsule contains:</p><ul><li>Elemental Iron 50 mg (as Carbonyl Iron)</li><li>Folic Acid 0.50 mg</li><li>Thiamine Mononitrate 2 mg</li><li>Riboflavin 2 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride 1 mg</li><li>Nicotinamide 10 mg</li><li>Vitamin C (as Ascorbic Acid) 50 mg</li><li>Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate 61.80 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Iron, Vitamin &amp; Mineral Combined preparation</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This is a haematinic preparation with vitamins and minerals. Carbonyl Iron is a more effective and safer choice for Iron supplementation as it has higher bioavailability, low toxicity, and better GI tolerance. Folic acid is required to maintain normal healthy development of the neural tube and is vital for cell division from a single cell to a fully developed baby. Vitamin B Complex is required for the growth and development of unborn babies. Vitamin C plays a role in the structure of collagen in the fetal membrane. Zinc is a critical nutrient for fetal growth &amp; development, central nervous system development &amp; function, and ensures better maternal &amp; infant health.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of ZIF Forte</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: One Capsule daily before food or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of ZIF Forte</strong></p><ul><li>Carbonyl Iron decreases the absorption of tetracycline antibiotics, quinolone antibiotics, levodopa, levothyroxine, methyldopa, and penicillamine.</li><li>Folic Acid interacts with antiepileptics, so plasma concentrations of phenobarbital, phenytoin, and primidone are possibly reduced.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of its components or those with Iron overload.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of ZIF Forte</strong></p><p>Gastrointestinal irritations such as nausea, anorexia, vomiting, discomfort, constipation, and diarrhea may occur. Patients may complain of dark stools. Carbonyl Iron pellets incorporated into the capsules reduce the possibility of gastrointestinal irritations. Rarely, allergic reactions may occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Use of any drug during the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided if possible. Thus, administration of Iron during the first trimester requires definite evidence of Iron deficiency. Prophylaxis of Iron deficiency where inadequate diet calls for supplementary Zinc and Folic acid is justified during the remainder of pregnancy.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Special care should be taken in patients with Iron overload states, such as haemochromatosis, haemolytic anaemia, or red blood cell aplasia. Failure to respond to the treatment requires further investigations to exclude other causes of anaemia. In patients with renal failure, there may be a risk of Zinc accumulation.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of ZIF Forte</strong></p><p>Symptoms of Carbonyl Iron overdose include decreased energy, nausea, abdominal pain, tarry stools, weak, rapid pulse, fever, coma, and seizures.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place, keep away from light and moisture. Keep all medicines out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '88_1742748213.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 21:43:33', '2025-05-05 22:32:54'),
(4301, 'Voltagel DS', 'Diclofenac Sodium', 70.00, 1, 'x 20 gm tube', '20 gm tube', 'Gel', '2% w/w', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Halobet 0.05%</strong></p><p>Halobet 0.05% is a super-high potent corticosteroid indicated for the relief of inflammatory and pruritic manifestations of corticosteroid-responsive dermatoses. Treatment beyond two consecutive weeks is not recommended, and the total dosage should not exceed 50 gm/week due to the potential for the drug to suppress the Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Adrenal (HPA) axis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Other Topical corticosteroids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Like other topical corticosteroids, Halobet 0.05% has anti-inflammatory, antipruritic, and vasoconstrictive actions. The mechanism of the anti-inflammatory activity of topical corticosteroids, in general, is unclear. However, corticosteroids are thought to act by the induction of phospholipase A2 inhibitory proteins, collectively called lipocortins. These proteins control the biosynthesis of potent mediators of inflammation, such as prostaglandins and leukotrienes, by inhibiting the release of their common precursor, arachidonic acid. Arachidonic acid is released from membrane phospholipids by phospholipase A2.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Halobet 0.05%</strong></p><p>Apply a thin layer of Halobetasol Cream or Ointment to the affected skin once or twice daily, as directed by the physician. Rub in gently and completely. Halobetasol 0.05% is a super-high potency corticosteroid; therefore, treatment should be limited to two weeks, and amounts greater than 50 gm/week should not be used. As with other highly active corticosteroids, therapy should be discontinued when control has been achieved. If no improvement is seen within 2 weeks, reassessment of the diagnosis may be necessary. Halobetasol should not be used with occlusive dressings. Use in children under 12 years of age is not recommended.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Halobet 0.05% Cream/Ointment is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to any of the components of the preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Halobet 0.05%</strong></p><p>The following adverse effects have been reported infrequently with topical corticosteroids:</p><ul><li>Burning</li><li>Itching</li><li>Dryness</li><li>Folliculitis</li><li>Acne</li><li>Hypopigmentation</li><li>Perioral dermatitis</li><li>Allergic contact dermatitis</li><li>Skin atrophy</li><li>Secondary infections</li><li>Striae</li><li>Miliaria</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Topical corticosteroids should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether topical administration of corticosteroids could result in sufficient systemic absorption to produce detectable quantities in breast milk. Therefore, caution should be exercised when topical corticosteroids are administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Systemic absorption of topical corticosteroids may cause reversible Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Adrenal (HPA) axis suppression, manifestations of Cushing\'s syndrome, hyperglycemia, and glucosuria. Patients receiving large doses of a potent topical steroid applied to a large surface area or under an occlusive dressing should be evaluated periodically for evidence of HPA axis suppression using urinary free cortisol and ACTH stimulation tests. If HPA axis suppression is noted, the drug should be withdrawn, the frequency of application should be reduced, or a less potent steroid should be substituted.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Halobet 0.05%</strong></p><p>Topically applied Halobetasol propionate Cream can be absorbed in sufficient amounts to produce systemic effects.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C. Keep all medicines out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children under 12 years of age:</strong> Not recommended.</li><li><strong>Use in pediatric patients:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established. Pediatric patients are at greater risk than adults of HPA axis suppression when treated with topical corticosteroids.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Other Topical corticosteroids</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Like other topical corticosteroids, Halobet 0.05% has anti-inflammatory, antipruritic, and vasoconstrictive actions. The mechanism of action involves the induction of lipocortins, which inhibit the release of arachidonic acid and subsequent production of inflammatory mediators such as prostaglandins and leukotrienes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Topical corticosteroids should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Use in children under 12 years of age is not recommended due to a greater risk of HPA axis suppression in pediatric patients when treated with topical corticosteroids.</p>', 'active', '7630_1744698371.jpg', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 21:58:57', '2025-04-15 10:26:11');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4302, 'Fexofast', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 50.00, 1, 'x 50 ml bottle', '50 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '30 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Fexofast 50 ml</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Allergic Rhinitis</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In adults and children 12 years and older, Fexofast 50 ml is used for the alleviation of seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis symptoms. These include sneezing, rhinorrhea, lacrimation, itchy red eyes, and itchy nose, palate, and throat.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fexofenadine improves work/activity productivity and health-related quality of life.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fexofenadine is recommended for the treatment of symptoms of chronic idiopathic urticaria (hives) in adults and children 12 years and older.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">It significantly reduces the number of wheals (hives) and pruritus (itching).</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines.</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Fexofast 50 ml is a selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist, an antihistamine.</li><li>After oral administration, it is quickly absorbed, reaching peak plasma concentration in 2 to 3 hours.</li><li>Fexofenadine does not cross the blood-brain barrier.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Fexofast 50 ml</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Seasonal Allergic Rhinitis</strong></li></ol><ul><li><strong>Adults and children 12 years and older</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Tablet: 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 60 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 11 years</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Tablet: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 30 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children 2 to 11 years (Suspension)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">30 mg or 5 ml twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 30 mg or 5 ml once daily.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong></li></ol><ul><li><strong>Adults and children 12 years and older</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Tablet: 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 60 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 11 years</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Tablet: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 30 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6 months to less than 2 years (Suspension)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">15 mg or 2.5 ml (1/2 tsp) twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 15 mg or 2.5 ml once daily.</li><li><strong>Children 2 to 11 years (Suspension)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">30 mg or 5 ml (1 tsp) twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In case of impaired renal function: 30 mg or 5 ml once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Co-administration with <strong>erythromycin</strong> or <strong>ketoconazole</strong> can cause a 2- to 3-fold increase in fexofenadine plasma levels.</li><li>Fexofenadine has no interaction with <strong>omeprazole</strong>.</li><li><strong>Antacids</strong> (aluminum and magnesium hydroxide) can reduce fexofenadine\'s bioavailability. Wait at least 2 hours before taking an antacid.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Fexofast 50 ml or any of its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Fexofast 50 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Headache, fatigue, and vertigo.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Nausea, general discomfort, and issues at the administration site.</li><li><strong>Rare/Very Rare</strong>: Allergic reactions like angioedema, chest tightness, dyspnea, flushing, anaphylaxis, anxiety, nightmares, insomnia, palpitations, tachycardia, diarrhea, rash, urticaria, and pruritus.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Fexofast 50 ml is Category C by the FDA. Use only if the benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Fexofenadine passes into breast milk, so it\'s not recommended for nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in <strong>elderly</strong> and patients with <strong>renal or hepatic impairment</strong>.</li><li>Patients with a history of <strong>cardiovascular disease</strong> should be informed about potential tachycardia or palpitations.</li><li><strong>Driving or operating machinery</strong>: Fexofast 50 ml is unlikely to impair these activities but individual responses should be monitored.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose may include <strong>dry mouth</strong>, <strong>sleepiness</strong>, <strong>fatigue</strong>, and <strong>dizziness</strong>.</li><li>No clinically significant adverse effects were observed in doses up to 800 mg.</li><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: Standard procedures for removing unabsorbed medication and psychological support. Fexofenadine is not efficiently removed by hemodialysis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Fexofenadine is an <strong>inverse agonist</strong> of the H1 receptor, blocking allergic reactions mediated by histamine.</li><li>Fexofenadine has no significant central nervous system effects and does not cross the blood-brain barrier.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal and Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Dose adjustments are required for patients with renal impairment (60 mg once daily).</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No significant differences in adverse effects between those over 65 and younger patients. Bioavailability may be higher in elderly patients.</li></ul>', 'active', '1364_1742750310.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:18:30', '2025-04-16 19:58:40'),
(4303, 'Metryl', 'Metronidazole', 35.00, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '200 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Metryl 60 ml</strong></p><p>Metryl 60 ml is indicated in the treatment of the following diseases:</p><ul><li>The prevention of post-operative infections due to anaerobic bacteria (particularly species of <em>Bacteroides</em> and anaerobic streptococci).</li><li>The treatment of septicaemia, bacteraemia, peritonitis, brain abscess, pelvic abscess, pelvic cellulitis, and post-operative wound infections caused by anaerobes.</li><li>In the treatment of urogenital trichomoniasis.</li><li>Bacterial vaginosis (also known as non-specific vaginitis).</li><li>All forms of amoebiasis (intestinal, extra-intestinal disease, and symptomless cyst passers).</li><li>Giardiasis.</li><li>Acute ulcerative gingivitis.</li><li>Anaerobically infected leg ulcers and pressure sores.</li><li>Acute dental infections due to anaerobic organisms.</li><li>Antibiotic-associated pseudomembranous colitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Amoebicides</li><li>Anti-diarrhoeal</li><li>Antiprotozoal</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Metryl 60 ml is a member of the imidazole class of antibacterial drugs and is classified therapeutically as an antiprotozoal agent. The 5-nitro group of Metryl 60 ml is reduced by anaerobes metabolically. Studies have demonstrated that the reduced form of this drug interacts with DNA, providing bactericidal action.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Metryl 60 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet and Suspension:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Trichomoniasis</em> (Adults &amp; Children over 10 years): 200 mg three times a day (tid) or 400 mg twice a day (bid) for 7 days; 800 mg in the morning and 1-2 gm at night for 2 days; 2 gm as a single dose for 1 day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Intestinal Amoebiasis</em> (Adults &amp; Children over 10 years): 800 mg three times a day (tid) for 5 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Giardiasis</em> (Adults &amp; Children over 10 years): 2 gm once daily for 3 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Acute Ulcerative Gingivitis</em> (Adults &amp; Children over 10 years): 200 mg tid for 3 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Bacterial Vaginosis</em> (Adults &amp; Children over 10 years): 400 mg bid for 7 days or 2 gm as a single dose for 1 day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Leg Ulcers and Pressure Sores</em> (Adults &amp; Children over 10 years): 400 mg tid for 7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Anaerobic Infections</em> (Adults &amp; Children over 10 years): 800 mg initially, then 400 mg tid for 7 days.</li><li><strong>Vaginal Gel</strong>: One applicator full (approximately 5 grams containing approximately 37.5 mg of Metryl 60 ml) intravaginally once or twice a day for 5 days.</li><li><strong>Suppository</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Anaerobic Infections</em> (Adults): 1 g every 8 hours for 3 days, then 1 g every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Surgical Prophylaxis</em> (Adults): 1 g 2 hours before surgery; additional doses may be given.</li><li><strong>IV Infusion</strong>: 500 mg every 8 hours at a rate of 5 ml/minute. A maximum of 4 g should not be exceeded during a 24-hour period. Treatment for 7 days is typically sufficient.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Metryl 60 ml</strong></p><ul><li><em>Disulfiram</em>: Psychotic reactions may occur when used concurrently.</li><li><em>Alcohol</em>: Avoid alcohol during therapy and for at least one day afterward due to the possibility of a disulfiram-like reaction.</li><li><em>Oral Anticoagulants (warfarin type)</em>: Increased anticoagulant effect and hemorrhagic risk.</li><li><em>Lithium</em>: May increase plasma levels of lithium.</li><li><em>Cyclosporin</em>: Monitor serum cyclosporin and creatinine levels.</li><li><em>Phenytoin/Phenobarbital</em>: Increased elimination of Metryl 60 ml, reducing plasma levels.</li><li><em>5-Fluorouracil</em>: Reduced clearance, leading to increased toxicity.</li><li><em>Busulfan</em>: Plasma levels may increase, leading to severe toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Metryl 60 ml is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Metryl 60 ml or other Nitroimidazole derivatives.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Metryl 60 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Metallic taste</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Diarrhoea</li><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Rashes</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: There are no adequate studies in pregnant women. Use only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Excreted in human milk. Caution is advised when administering to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Regular hematological tests, especially leucocyte count, are recommended if administered for longer durations.</li><li>Metryl 60 ml may darken urine.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with hepatic encephalopathy.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Metryl 60 ml</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdose may include vomiting, ataxia, and slight disorientation. There is no specific antidote, and symptomatic treatment should be used.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, protected from light, and keep out of reach of children. Do not use after the expiry date.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Amoebicides</li><li>Anti-diarrhoeal</li><li>Antiprotozoal</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Metryl 60 ml interacts with the DNA of anaerobic organisms, providing bactericidal effects.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Metryl 60 ml should be administered with caution in children, particularly for those with hepatic or renal impairment. Adjustments in dosage may be needed for patients with hepatic insufficiency.</p>', 'active', '855_1742750547.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:22:27', '2025-05-04 00:03:06'),
(4304, 'Nyclobate', 'Clobetasol Propionate', 80.00, 1, 'x 20 gm tube', '20 gm tube', 'Ointment', '0.05%', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is the information about <strong>Nyclobate 20 gm</strong> (Clobetasol Propionate) in bold text for easier readability:</p><h3><strong>Indications of Nyclobate 20 gm</strong></h3><p>Clobetasol Propionate is indicated for adults, elderly, and children over 1 year in the following dermatoses:</p><ul><li><strong>Psoriasis</strong> (excluding widespread plaque psoriasis)</li><li><strong>Recalcitrant dermatoses</strong></li><li><strong>Lichen planus</strong></li><li><strong>Discoid lupus erythematosus</strong></li><li>Other skin conditions which do not respond satisfactorily to less potent steroids</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p>Other <strong>Topical corticosteroids</strong></p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Clobetasol Propionate is a <strong>very potent topical corticosteroid</strong>. It has <strong>anti-inflammatory</strong>, <strong>antipruritic</strong>, and <strong>vasoconstrictive properties</strong>. It shows anti-inflammatory activity via multiple mechanisms to inhibit late-phase allergic reactions. It decreases the density of mast cells, chemotaxis, and activation of eosinophils. It also reduces cytokine production and inhibits the metabolism of arachidonic acid.</p><h3><strong>Dosage of Nyclobate 20 gm</strong></h3><h4><strong>Cream &amp; Ointment:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Adults, elderly, and children over 1 year</strong>: Apply a thin layer of Clobetasol Propionate Cream or Ointment to the affected skin areas <strong>twice daily</strong> and rub in gently and completely.</li><li>Repeated short courses of Clobetasol Propionate may be used to control exacerbations.</li><li>In more resistant lesions, especially where there is hyperkeratosis, the effect can be enhanced by occluding the treatment area with polythene film. Overnight occlusion is usually adequate.</li><li>Treatment should be limited to <strong>2 consecutive weeks</strong>. The maximum weekly dose should not exceed <strong>50 gm/week</strong>.</li><li>In children, courses should be limited to five days and reviewed weekly.</li></ul><h4><strong>Scalp Solution:</strong></h4><ul><li>Apply the required quantity of spray of Clobetasol Scalp Solution <strong>once or twice daily</strong> to the affected areas of the scalp and gently rub in.</li><li>The total dose applied should not exceed <strong>50 ml weekly</strong>.</li><li>Discontinue therapy if no response is noted after <strong>one week</strong> or as soon as the lesion heals.</li></ul><h4><strong>Topical Spray:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol 0.05% topical spray</strong> is for topical use only. <strong>Do not use for ophthalmic, oral, or intravaginal use</strong>.</li><li>Apply <strong>twice daily</strong> and rub in gently and completely.</li><li>The total dosage should not exceed <strong>50 g (59 ml or 2 fluid ounces) per week</strong>. Do not use more than <strong>26 sprays per application</strong> or <strong>52 sprays per day</strong>.</li><li>Treatment should be limited to <strong>4 weeks</strong>. If no improvement is seen within <strong>2 weeks</strong>, reassessment of diagnosis may be necessary.</li></ul><h3><strong>Administration of Nyclobate 20 gm</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Route of administration</strong>: Cutaneous.</li><li><strong>Creams</strong> are especially appropriate for <strong>moist or weeping surfaces</strong>.</li><li><strong>Ointments</strong> are especially appropriate for <strong>dry, lichenified, or scaly lesions</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction of Nyclobate 20 gm</strong></h3><ul><li>Co-administered drugs that can inhibit <strong>CYP3A4</strong> (e.g., <strong>ritonavir</strong>, <strong>itraconazole</strong>) have been shown to inhibit the metabolism of corticosteroids, leading to <strong>increased systemic exposure</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>It is contraindicated in patients with <strong>hypersensitivity</strong> to any component of the preparation.</li><li>Should not be used in:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Rosacea</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acne vulgaris</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Perioral dermatitis</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Perianal and genital pruritus</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pruritus without inflammation</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Untreated cutaneous infections</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Nyclobate 20 gm</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Most common side effects</strong>: Burning and stinging sensation.</li><li><strong>Less frequent side effects</strong>: Itching, skin atrophy, cracking, and fissuring of the skin.</li><li><strong>Cushing syndrome</strong> has been reported in infants and adults as a result of prolonged use.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>There are limited data on the use of Clobetasol Propionate cream in pregnant women.</li><li>The administration of Clobetasol Propionate Cream during pregnancy and lactation should only be considered if the <strong>expected benefit</strong> to the mother outweighs the <strong>possible risks</strong> of treatment.</li><li>It is unknown whether this drug is excreted in human milk. <strong>Caution</strong> should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>In case of using <strong>occlusive dressings</strong>, the skin should be cleansed before applying a fresh dressing.</li><li>Topical corticosteroids should be used with caution in <strong>psoriasis</strong>, as rebound relapses and development of local or systemic toxicity due to impaired barrier function may occur.</li><li>If used on the <strong>face</strong>, treatment should be limited to <strong>5 days</strong>.</li><li>When Clobetasol Propionate is used on <strong>eyelids</strong>, care should be taken to avoid the eyes as <strong>cataracts</strong> and <strong>glaucoma</strong> might result from repeated exposure.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Nyclobate 20 gm</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Acute overdosage</strong> is very unlikely.</li><li>In the case of <strong>chronic over-dosage</strong> or misuse, features of <strong>hypercortisolism</strong> may occur, and in this situation, topical steroid treatment should be <strong>discontinued</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Keep below <strong>30°C</strong>, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Do not freeze.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li>In <strong>infants and children</strong> under 12 years of age, long-term continuous topical corticosteroid therapy should be avoided where possible, as <strong>adrenal suppression</strong> can occur.</li><li>Children are more susceptible to the use of topical corticosteroids, which may lead to <strong>atrophic changes</strong>.</li></ul>', 'active', '661_1742750654.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:24:14', '2025-05-04 01:08:13'),
(4305, 'Reservix SR', 'Aceclofenac', 8.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Reservix SR 200 mg</strong></p><p>Reservix SR 200 mg is indicated for the relief of pain and inflammation in:</p><ul><li>Osteoarthritis</li><li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li><li>Ankylosing spondylitis</li><li>Toothache</li><li>Trauma</li><li>Lumbago</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Reservix SR 200 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Reservix SR 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Extended-release tablet</strong>: The recommended dose in adults is one 200 mg Reservix SR 200 mg tablet daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Film-coated tablet</strong>: The recommended dose in adults is 100 mg, twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Reservix SR 200 mg</strong></p><p>No significant drug interactions have been observed, but close monitoring of patients is required when it is used with:</p><ul><li><strong>Lithium and Digoxin</strong>: May increase plasma concentration of lithium and digoxin.</li><li><strong>Diuretics</strong>: May interact with the activity of diuretics.</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants</strong>: May enhance the activity of anticoagulants.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: May increase the plasma level of methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Reservix SR 200 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to it or in those for whom aspirin or NSAIDs precipitate attacks of asthma.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Reservix SR 200 mg</strong></p><p>Reservix SR 200 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of Reservix SR 200 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in patients with:</p><ul><li>Active or suspected peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding</li><li>Moderate to severe hepatic impairment</li><li>Cardiac or renal impairment</li><li>Dizziness or urticaria</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Reservix SR 200 mg in children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Reservix SR 200 mg is a non-steroidal drug with anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. It is a potent inhibitor of the enzyme cyclooxygenase, which is involved in the production of prostaglandin. After oral administration, it is rapidly and completely absorbed as an unchanged drug.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The use of Reservix SR 200 mg should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>There are no clinical data on the use of Reservix SR 200 mg in children.</p>', 'active', '424_1742750784.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:26:24', '2025-05-04 15:14:01'),
(4306, 'Clovate-N', 'Clobetasol Propionate + Neomycin Sulphate + Nystatin', 80.00, 1, 'x 10 gm tube', '10 gm tube', 'Ointment', '0.05%', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Clovate-N 15 gm:</strong> This preparation is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Short courses treatment of recalcitrant eczemas.</li><li>Neurodermatoses.</li><li>Psoriasis (excluding widespread plaque psoriasis) where secondary bacterial infection or fungal infection is present, suspected, or likely to occur.</li><li>Other inflammatory conditions which do not respond satisfactorily to less active steroids.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong> Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Clobetasol Propionate is a highly active corticosteroid with topical anti-inflammatory activity. The major effect of Clobetasol Propionate on skin is a non-specific anti-inflammatory response, partially due to vasoconstriction and a decrease in collagen synthesis.</p><p>Neomycin Sulphate is a broad-spectrum antibiotic of the aminoglycoside type used to treat infections with bacteria. It binds to the ribosomal 30s and 50s sub-units of susceptible bacteria and inhibits protein synthesis. Neomycin also causes a misreading of the genetic codes of the mRNA template, leading to the incorporation of incorrect amino acids into the growing polypeptide chain.</p><p>Nystatin is an antifungal that kills fungi and yeasts by interfering with their cell membranes.</p><p>The combined activity of Clobetasol, Neomycin, and Nystatin allows this preparation to be effective for conditions that are or are likely to become infected.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 2 years</strong>: Apply sparingly to the affected area once or twice daily until improvement occurs. Treatment should not exceed 7 days without medical supervision. If necessary, treatment can last up to 4 weeks under medical review.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Suitable for use, but caution should be exercised if renal function is reduced, as significant systemic absorption of Neomycin may occur.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Suitable for children 2 years and older, at the same dose as adults. It is not recommended for use in children under 2 years due to the possibility of increased absorption in very young children.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong> Neomycin Sulphate can intensify and prolong the respiratory depressant effects of neuromuscular blocking agents following significant systemic absorption. However, systemic exposure is expected to be minimal, so drug interactions are unlikely to be significant. No hazardous interactions have been reported with Clobetasol Propionate or Nystatin.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Rosacea</li><li>Acne vulgaris</li><li>Perioral dermatitis</li><li>Primary cutaneous viral infections (e.g., Herpes simplex, chickenpox)</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the preparation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged use or treatment of extensive areas may result in systemic absorption, causing hypercortisolism.</li><li>Local atrophic changes such as thinning, striae, and dilatation of superficial blood vessels may occur, particularly when occlusive dressings are used or when skin folds are involved.</li><li>Pigmentation changes and hypertrichosis may also be reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy category C</strong>: The safe use during pregnancy and lactation has not been established.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not recommended during lactation due to insufficient safety data.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Long-term continuous topical therapy should be avoided, especially in infants and children, as adrenal suppression can occur.</li><li>When applied to the eyelids, care should be taken to avoid contact with the eyes, as glaucoma could result.</li><li>In case of accidental eye contact, rinse the eye thoroughly with water.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Acute overdosage is unlikely. However, chronic overdosage or misuse could lead to the features of hypercortisolism. In this case, the topical steroids should be discontinued gradually.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 25°C.</li><li>Do not freeze.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong> Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Clobetasol Propionate is a potent corticosteroid with anti-inflammatory activity. Neomycin Sulphate is an antibiotic that inhibits bacterial protein synthesis, while Nystatin is an antifungal that disrupts fungal cell membranes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><ul><li>Limited information is available on the effects of topically applied Neomycin during pregnancy and lactation. Since Neomycin can cross the placenta, it poses a theoretical risk of fetal toxicity, and its use is not recommended during pregnancy and lactation. The safety of Clobetasol Propionate has not been established in lactating mothers.</li></ul>', 'active', '124_1742751009.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:30:09', '2025-05-03 01:26:55'),
(4307, 'Montene', 'Montelukast Sodium', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '4 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Montene 4 mg</strong></p><p>Montene 4 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. Cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) are located in the human airway, and these eicosanoids bind to them. The orally active drug Montene 4 mg has a strong affinity and specificity for the CysLT1 receptor. Without any agonist activity, it inhibits LTD4\'s physiological effects at the CysLT1 receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Montene 4 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for adults and adolescents 15 years of age and older: Montene 10 mg tablet once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 6 to 14 years of age: Montene 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 2 years to 5 years of age: Montene 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 6 months to 5 years of age: Montene 4 mg oral granules once daily. This can be administered either directly in the mouth, or mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature.</li><li><strong>Use in the pediatric patient</strong>: The safety and efficacy of Montene have been established in adequate and well-controlled studies in pediatric patients with asthma 6 months to 14 years of age. Safety and efficacy profiles in this age group are similar to those seen in adults.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage adjustment is required in patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage adjustment is recommended in patients with renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Elderly use</strong>: The pharmacokinetic profile and the oral bioavailability of a single 10-mg oral dose of Montene are similar in elderly and younger adults. The plasma half-life of Montene is slightly longer in the elderly. No dosage adjustment in the elderly is required.</p><p><strong>Interactions of Montene 4 mg</strong></p><p>In the prevention and long-term treatment of asthma, Montene 4 mg has been provided alongside other commonly used treatments without seeming to increase the risk of negative side effects. According to studies on drug interactions, theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives (norethindrone 1mg/ethinyl estradiol 35mcg), terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin did not exhibit any clinically significant effects on the pharmacokinetics at the recommended clinical dose of it. In clinical investigations, the medication was used concurrently with a variety of routinely prescribed medications without showing any signs of clinically harmful interactions, despite the fact that more detailed interaction studies were not carried out. Thyroid hormones, hypnotic sedatives, benzodiazepines, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and decongestants were among these drugs. Following a single dose of this solution, the hepatic metabolism-inducing drug phenobarbital reduced the drug\'s AUC by about 40%. For this medication, no dosage modification is advised. When powerful cytochrome P450 enzyme inducers, such as phenobarbital or rifampin, are provided alongside this solution, it is fair to use proper clinical monitoring.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those who have an extreme sensitivity to any ingredient in this medication should not take it.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Montene 4 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, asthenia, aberrant behavior, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, hemorrhage, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, oedema, seizure, odd feeling, and sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, decreased attention, disorientation, erythema nodosum, hallucinations, hepatic problems, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, and tremor.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Montene 4 mg is classified as pregnancy category B. Although it has been demonstrated that the medication crosses the placenta of pregnant rats and rabbits, there are no records of its use in pregnant humans. Although there is some evidence that it is also secreted in breast milk, little is known about the implications of this discovery. Before starting the therapy in nursing moms, caution should be exercised.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks, including status asthmaticus, is not recommended as usage for Montene 4 mg. Patients should be told to have access to the proper rescue medication. Asthma exacerbations that are acute can still be treated with the solution. While the dosage of an inhaled corticosteroid may be gradually decreased under medical supervision, the use of this solution instead of an inhaled or oral corticosteroid should not be started suddenly. Exercise-induced bronchospasm should not be treated and managed with a single dose of it. While taking it, patients with documented aspirin sensitivity should continue to refrain from taking aspirin or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Although aspirin sensitivity in asthmatics is a known side effect of the drug, it has not been demonstrated to shorten the bronchoconstrictor response to aspirin and other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs).</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Montene 4 mg</strong></p><p>The majority of overdose reports did not include any negative experiences. The most frequent side effects, which were in line with Montene 4 mg\'s safety profile, were nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain. In the case of an overdose, it is permissible to take the customary supportive measures, such as removing any unabsorbed material from the digestive tract, using clinical monitoring, and if necessary, instituting supportive therapy.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry environment while keeping out light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>The cysteinyl leukotriene receptor is inhibited by the selective and orally active leukotriene receptor antagonist known as Montene 4 mg (CysLT1). The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. The pathophysiology of asthma and allergic rhinitis, including airway edema, smooth muscle contraction, and altered cellular activity associated with the inflammatory process, have been linked to cysteinyl leukotrienes and leukotriene receptor occupation. These factors all contribute to the signs and symptoms of asthma.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>After oral administration, Montene 4 mg passes the placenta in rats and rabbits. Nevertheless, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. Because the human reaction to medications is not usually predicted by research on animal reproduction, pregnant women should only use it if absolutely necessary. When giving the pill to a nursing mother, care should be taken because many medications are secreted in human milk.</p>', 'active', '1527_1742751088.png', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:31:28', '2025-05-04 00:07:58'),
(4308, 'Opton', 'Esomeprazole', 8.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is the information in a clearer format with bold headings:</p><p><strong>Indications of Opton 40 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>To alleviate GERD-related symptoms, such as persistent heartburn.</li><li>To treat erosive esophagitis.</li><li>To maintain the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>For the eradication of Helicobacter pylori infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in combination with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>For Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>For duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Opton 40 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. If not healed after 4-8 weeks, an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg once daily (clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. An additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered if symptoms are not completely resolved.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori Eradication:</strong> Triple Therapy (Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily, Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily, Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily) for 10 days to reduce the risk of duodenal ulcer recurrence.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> 20-80 mg once daily, adjusted individually as required.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks based on response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> 40 mg once daily by intravenous injection or infusion. The injection should be given gradually (minimum 3 minutes) and should not be administered with other medications through the same intravenous site.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Opton 40 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Take <strong>one capsule</strong> or pill <strong>one hour before a meal</strong>, swallowing it whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>: Mix the contents of the packet with 15 ml of water in a glass, stir thoroughly, wait for 2-3 minutes for it to thicken, stir again, and consume within 30 minutes. If administered via a nasogastric or gastric tube, follow the same procedure with a syringe.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong>: Add 5 ml of solvent to the vial to prepare the IV solution. Administer slowly over a minimum of three minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Opton 40 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Metabolism</strong>: Esomeprazole is metabolized by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4. It does not inhibit most CYP enzymes, except for CYP2C19.</li><li><strong>Drug Interactions</strong>: It does not interact clinically with phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin. However, diazepam’s clearance is reduced, potentially raising plasma levels.</li><li><strong>Impact on Absorption</strong>: Esomeprazole may interfere with the absorption of drugs that depend on stomach acidity, such as ketoconazole, iron salts, and digoxin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Avoid use in individuals with known hypersensitivity to any of the components of esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Opton 40 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common Side Effects</strong>: Headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, and dry mouth. Long-term therapy does not differ in side effects compared to short-term treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cancer Risk</strong>: A positive response to Opton 40 mg does not rule out stomach cancer.</li><li><strong>Capsule Administration</strong>: Take capsules at least one hour before meals. For patients who have difficulty swallowing capsules, the pellets inside the capsule can be mixed with applesauce.</li><li><strong>Antacids</strong>: Antacids can be used while taking Opton 40 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Opton 40 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Decreased motor activity, changes in breathing, tremors, ataxia, and convulsions. No known specific antidote.</li><li><strong>Management</strong>: Provide symptomatic and supportive care. Dialysis is not effective.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a <strong>dry place</strong> at a temperature below 30°C, away from moisture and light.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Mechanism</strong>: Esomeprazole inhibits the H+/K+-ATPase proton pump in the stomach, reducing acid secretion.</li><li><strong>Absorption</strong>: Absorbed quickly, with peak plasma levels occurring about 1.5 hours after oral dosing.</li><li><strong>Excretion</strong>: Excreted primarily in the urine (80%) and feces (20%) as inactive metabolites.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><ul><li>Insufficient data on safety in pregnant women. No teratogenic effects in animal studies. Discontinue breastfeeding if esomeprazole is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Safety</strong>: Not established for pediatric use.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li>No significant differences in safety and efficacy between elderly and younger patients, but older individuals may be more sensitive.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency:</strong></p><ul><li>For mild to moderate hepatic impairment, no dosage change is needed. In severe hepatic impairment, limit the dose to 20 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong></p><ul><li>Minimal effect on pharmacokinetics in renal impairment, as less than 1% of esomeprazole is excreted unchanged in the urine.</li></ul>', 'active', '1176_1742751265.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:34:25', '2025-05-04 01:21:46');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4309, 'Dsec', 'Dexlansoprazole', 10.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '30 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Dsec 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: Dsec 30 mg is indicated for the healing of all grades of erosive esophagitis (EE) for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: Dsec 30 mg is indicated to maintain healing of EE and relief of heartburn for up to 6 months.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</strong>: Dsec 30 mg is indicated for the treatment of heartburn associated with symptomatic non-erosive GERD for 4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Dsec 30 mg delayed-release capsule is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI) that inhibits gastric acid secretion. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole, a racemic mixture of R- and S-enantiomers. This is supplied as a Dual Delayed Release (DDR) formulation in a capsule for oral administration. The capsule contains a mixture of two types of enteric-coated granules with different pH-dependent dissolution profiles.</li><li>Dsec 30 mg suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell, blocking the final step of acid production.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Dsec 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis and Relief of Heartburn</strong>: 30 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive GERD</strong>: 30 mg once daily for 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 60 mg once daily for up to 8 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Dsec 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Dsec 30 mg can be taken without regard to food. It should be swallowed whole.</li><li>Alternatively, the capsule can be opened, and the intact granules can be sprinkled on one tablespoon of food and swallowed immediately. Granules should not be chewed.</li><li>If a dose is missed, it should be taken as soon as possible. If it is too close to the next dose, only the prescribed dose should be taken at the appointed time. A double dose should not be taken.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Dsec 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With medicines</strong>: Atazanavir, Warfarin, Tacrolimus, Clopidogrel, Methotrexate.</li><li><strong>With food &amp; others</strong>: No data available.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Dsec 30 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Dsec 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects: Diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, flatulence.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>There are no adequate or well-controlled studies in pregnant women with Dsec 30 mg. Exposure in clinical trials was very limited. Dsec 30 mg should not be administered to pregnant women unless the expected benefits outweigh the potential risks.</li><li>It is not known whether Dsec 30 mg is excreted in human milk. However, it and its metabolites are excreted in the milk of rats. As many drugs are excreted in human milk, Dsec 30 mg should not be given to nursing mothers unless its use is considered necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Gastric malignancy, Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea, bone fractures, hypomagnesemia, and concomitant use of Dsec 30 mg with Methotrexate.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Dsec 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li>There have been no reports of a significant overdose of Dsec 30 mg. Multiple doses of 120 mg and a single dose of 300 mg did not result in death or severe adverse events.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a dry place, protected from light. Keep all medicines out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary for elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in patients below 12 years of age have not been established.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary for patients with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary for patients with mild hepatic impairment. A maximum daily dose of 30 mg may be considered for patients with moderate hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Dsec 30 mg is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI) that suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell, blocking the final step of acid production. The dual delayed-release formulation in Dsec 30 mg provides two distinct plasma concentration peaks: the first peak occurs 1 to 2 hours after administration, followed by a second peak within 4 to 5 hours. Dsec 30 mg is extensively metabolized in the liver and excreted in urine.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy Category B. There are no adequate and well-controlled studies with Dsec 30 mg in pregnant women. There are no adequate studies in lactating mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and effectiveness in children and adolescents below 12 years of age have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>No dose adjustment is necessary for elderly patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Impairment</strong></p><ul><li>No dose adjustment is necessary for patients with renal impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong></p><ul><li>No dose adjustment is necessary for patients with mild hepatic impairment. A maximum daily dose of 30 mg should be considered for patients with moderate hepatic impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '161_1742751450.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:37:30', '2025-05-03 09:58:47'),
(4310, 'NexCap', 'Esomeprazole', 7.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of NexCap 20 mg</strong></p><p>NexCap 20 mg is indicated:</p><ul><li>To alleviate GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn and others.</li><li>To treat erosive esophagitis.</li><li>To maintain the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>For the eradication of <em>Helicobacter pylori</em> infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>In the treatment of Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</li><li>For duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of NexCap 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. Most patients heal within 4 to 8 weeks. If not healed after 4-8 weeks, an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg once daily (clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication (Triple Therapy for Duodenal Ulcer Recurrence):</strong>Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> The dose is 20-80 mg once daily. The dosage should be adjusted individually, and treatment continued as long as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks according to response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> The recommended adult dose is 40 mg NexCap 20 mg given once daily by intravenous injection (not less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of NexCap 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take one NexCap 20 mg pill or capsule whole, one hour before a meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension Use:</strong>Mix the entire packet\'s contents with 15 ml of water in a glass.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Stir for 2-3 minutes, let it thicken, then stir again before consumption within 30 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If taking via nasogastric or gastric tube, mix in a syringe, wait for it to thicken, and administer within 30 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Use the appropriate syringe size and shake to administer any remaining suspension.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intravenous Administration:</strong>Add 5 ml of solvent to the vial to create the IV injection solution.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Administer the injection over a minimum of 3 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The solution must be used within 12 hours of reconstitution at room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of NexCap 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is metabolized by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 in the liver.</li><li>It does not significantly interact with medications metabolized by CYPs 1A2, 2A6, 2C9, 2D6, 2E1, or 3A4.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Drugs with potential interactions:</strong>Diazepam clearance reduced by 45% when taken with esomeprazole.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Esomeprazole can affect absorption of medications affected by gastrointestinal pH, such as ketoconazole, iron salts, and digoxin.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Co-administration with clarithromycin and amoxicillin increases plasma levels of 14-hydroxy clarithromycin.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to any formulation of esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of NexCap 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Most common side effects: headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, and dry mouth.</li><li>Side effects with long-term use (up to 12 months) are similar in nature to those with short-term treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Positive symptom response does not rule out stomach cancer.</li><li>Take NexCap 20 mg at least an hour before meals.</li><li>For patients with difficulty swallowing capsules, open the capsule and mix the pellets with a spoonful of cold applesauce. Do not chew or crush pellets.</li><li>Antacids can be used while taking NexCap 20 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of NexCap 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No specific countermeasure for overdose.</li><li>Symptoms of overdose may include decreased motor activity, changes in breathing, tremors, ataxia, and convulsions.</li><li>Dialysis is not expected to remove esomeprazole due to its protein binding.</li><li>Supportive care is recommended in case of overdose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry area at temperatures not exceeding 30°C.</li><li>Protect from moisture and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole works by inhibiting the H+/K+-ATPase proton pump in the stomach parietal cells, reducing stomach acid production.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pharmacokinetics:</strong>Esomeprazole reaches peak plasma levels about 1.5 hours after oral dosing.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Systemic bioavailability is around 90%.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">It is metabolized in the liver by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Excretion occurs via urine (80%) and feces (20%).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Insufficient reliable research on its safety during pregnancy.</li><li>No teratogenic effects in animal studies, but excretion in breast milk has not been investigated.</li><li>If necessary, discontinue breastfeeding during treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>No significant differences in safety and efficacy between elderly and younger patients, though some elderly may be more sensitive.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage change needed for mild to moderate hepatic impairment.</li><li>For severe hepatic impairment, limit the dose to 20 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>Minimal impact on pharmacokinetics in patients with renal impairment, as less than 1% of esomeprazole is eliminated unchanged in the urine.</li></ul>', 'active', '1266_1742751600.webp', 8, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:40:00', '2025-05-04 00:39:32'),
(4311, 'SMC PLUS ( Orange Flavored)', 'Lemon Flavored Electrolyte Drink', 40.00, 1, 'x 250 ml bottle', '250 ml bottle', 'Food', '0 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '1792_1742751794.png', 12, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:43:14', '2025-05-04 21:12:35'),
(4312, 'Voltalin Forte', 'Diclofenac Sodium', 8.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Voltalin Forte 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Rheumatology:</strong> Inflammatory and degenerative forms of rheumatism, chronic involutive polyarthritis, ankylosing spondylarthritis, osteoarthritis, spondylarthroses, acute gout, peri-articular rheumatic disorders.</li><li><strong>Surgery and Traumatology:</strong> Sprain, bruises, dislocations, fractures, soft tissue injuries, surgical interventions.</li><li><strong>Obstetrics and Gynecology:</strong> Primary dysmenorrhoea, episiotomy, adnexitis, endometritis, parametritis, salpingitis, mastitis.</li><li><strong>Otorhinolaryngology:</strong> Pre-operative medication for the prevention of pain, inflammation, and swelling.</li><li><strong>Dentistry:</strong> Post-operative and post-traumatic pain, inflammation, and swelling.</li><li><strong>Other indications:</strong> For the prevention of pain and treatment of inflammation and swelling in patients operated in the urogenital tract, renal and biliary colic.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Voltalin Forte 50 mg is a potent non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) with anti-rheumatic, anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic properties. It also has some uricosuric effect. Diclofenac, the active ingredient, inhibits prostaglandin biosynthesis, which is central in causing inflammation, pain, and fever. It is rapidly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and reaches peak plasma concentrations within an average of 2 hours after ingestion. It is highly protein-bound (99.7%) and metabolized in the liver.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:</strong>Enteric-coated tablet: 75-150 mg daily in two or three divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sustained release tablet: One tablet daily, preferably with a meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Suppositories: 75-150 mg daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gel: 2-4 g applied to the painful site 3-4 times daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Injection: 1 ampoule daily, up to 2 ampoules in severe cases.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong>Enteric-coated tablet: 1-3 mg/kg daily in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Suppositories: 1-3 mg/kg body weight in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly patients:</strong> Start with the lowest effective dose, considering age and physical condition.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction with Other Medications:</strong></p><ul><li>Diclofenac may inhibit the activity of diuretics.</li><li>Caution is needed when taken with digoxin, methotrexate, cyclosporine, or lithium.</li><li>Combining with other NSAIDs or glucocorticoids may increase side effects, such as gastrointestinal bleeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient in the product.</li><li>Peptic ulcer or gastrointestinal bleeding.</li><li>Asthmatic patients sensitive to acetylsalicylic acid or other prostaglandin inhibitors.</li><li>Should not be applied under occlusive airtight dressings.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Generally mild, rare, and transient.</li><li>Common effects: Epigastric pain, nausea, diarrhea, dizziness, and headache.</li><li>Peripheral edema, rash, and eczema.</li><li><strong>Gel-related effects:</strong> Skin irritation, reddening, or rash.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Use only if the benefits outweigh the risks. Not recommended during the first trimester.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Small amounts of diclofenac may be detected in breast milk, but no significant effects on the infant are expected.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor for peptic ulceration or gastrointestinal bleeding, especially in elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Gel use:</strong> Avoid contact with eyes or mucous membranes. Wash hands thoroughly after application.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place below 30°C, and keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Diclofenac works by inhibiting prostaglandin biosynthesis, reducing inflammation, pain, and fever. It is well absorbed and has high plasma protein binding, with a significant first-pass metabolism in the liver.</p>', 'active', '350_1742751904.webp', 40, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:45:04', '2025-05-05 17:52:49'),
(4313, 'Imruza', 'Azathioprine', 20.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Imruza 50 mg</strong></p><p>Imruza 50 mg is used as an immunosuppressant antimetabolite either alone or, more commonly, in combination with other agents (usually corticosteroids) and procedures that influence the immune response. Therapeutic effects may be evident only after weeks or months and can include a steroid-sparing effect, reducing the toxicity associated with high-dose and prolonged use of corticosteroids.</p><p>Imruza 50 mg, when combined with corticosteroids and/or other immunosuppressive agents and procedures, is indicated to enhance the survival of organ transplants, such as:</p><ul><li>Renal transplants</li><li>Cardiac transplants</li><li>Hepatic transplants</li></ul><p>It also reduces the corticosteroid requirements for renal transplant recipients.</p><p>Imruza 50 mg is indicated for the treatment of moderate to severe inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), including Crohn\'s disease or ulcerative colitis, in patients requiring corticosteroid therapy, who cannot tolerate corticosteroid therapy, or whose disease is refractory to other first-line treatments.</p><p>It has been used with clinical benefit (which may include a reduction of dosage or discontinuation of corticosteroids) in patients with:</p><ul><li>Severe active rheumatoid arthritis</li><li>Systemic lupus erythematosus</li><li>Dermatomyositis and polymyositis</li><li>Autoimmune chronic active hepatitis</li><li>Pemphigus vulgaris</li><li>Polyarteritis nodosa</li><li>Autoimmune hemolytic anemia</li><li>Chronic refractory idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Cytotoxic immunosuppressants</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong> Imruza 50 mg antagonizes purine metabolism and may inhibit the synthesis of DNA, RNA, and proteins. It may also interfere with cellular metabolism and inhibit mitosis. Its mechanism of action is likely due to the incorporation of thiopurine analogues into the DNA structure, causing chain termination and cytotoxicity.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Imruza 50 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Renal Homotransplantation:</strong> The dose required to prevent rejection and minimize toxicity will vary with individual patients. Initial doses are typically 3 to 5 mg/kg daily, beginning at the time of transplant. Maintenance levels of 1 to 3 mg/kg daily are usually sufficient.</p><p><strong>Rheumatoid Arthritis:</strong></p><ul><li>Initial dose: Approximately 1.0 mg/kg (50 to 100 mg) given as a single dose or on a twice-daily schedule.</li><li>Dose may be increased after 6 to 8 weeks, up to 2.5 mg/kg per day, depending on the patient’s response and absence of severe toxicities.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Imruza 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Allopurinol/Oxipurinol/Thiopurinol</strong>: If taken with these drugs, the dose of Imruza 50 mg should be reduced to 25% of the original dose.</li><li><strong>Neuromuscular Blocking Agents</strong>: Interaction with drugs like succinylcholine may vary.</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants</strong>: Imruza may inhibit the effect of anticoagulants like warfarin.</li><li><strong>Cytostatic/myelosuppressive agents</strong>: Caution is advised when combining Imruza with these agents, such as penicillamine.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: Dose adjustments may be needed when used together to maintain an adequate white blood cell count.</li><li><strong>Vaccines</strong>: The immunosuppressive activity of Imruza could result in an atypical response to live vaccines.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Imruza 50 mg or any of its components.</li><li>Hypersensitivity to 6-mercaptopurine should alert prescribers to a probable sensitivity to Imruza 50 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Imruza 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Infections and infestations</strong>: Viral, fungal, and bacterial infections in transplant patients.</li><li><strong>Neoplasms</strong>: Rare reports of lymphoproliferative disorders, skin cancers, and leukemia.</li><li><strong>Blood and lymphatic system disorders</strong>: Depression of bone marrow function, leucopenia, thrombocytopenia.</li><li><strong>Gastrointestinal disorders</strong>: Common side effects include nausea.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category D</strong>: Positive evidence of fetal risk. The use of Imruza during pregnancy should be carefully assessed for risk versus benefit.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is recommended that mothers receiving Imruza 50 mg should not breastfeed, as the drug has been found in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Monitoring</strong>: Regular blood count monitoring is essential due to the potential risk of bone marrow suppression, especially in the first 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Hepatotoxicity</strong>: Routine liver function tests should be performed due to potential liver damage.</li><li><strong>Renal and Hepatic Insufficiency</strong>: Dose adjustments are recommended for patients with renal or hepatic impairments.</li><li><strong>Carcinogenicity</strong>: Patients on long-term immunosuppressive therapy are at an increased risk of cancers, including lymphomas and skin cancers.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Imruza 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms include infections, ulceration of the throat, bruising, and bleeding.</li><li>Treatment involves close monitoring of blood counts and general supportive care.</li></ul>', 'active', '1658_1742752021.webp', 55, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:47:01', '2025-05-03 21:01:17'),
(4314, 'Gastrocon-A', 'Sodium Alginate + Potassium Bicarbonate', 180.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '(500 mg+100 mg)/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Gastrocon-A 200 ml</strong></p><p>This preparation is indicated for the treatment of symptoms resulting from the reflux of acid, bile, and pepsin into the esophagus, such as acid regurgitation, heartburn, and indigestion. It may be used after gastric surgery, as a result of hiatus hernia, during pregnancy, or accompanying reflux esophagitis. It also helps in treating symptoms of laryngopharyngeal reflux, such as hoarseness, other voice disorders, sore throats, and cough. This preparation can also be used to treat gastroesophageal reflux symptoms during or after the withdrawal of acid-suppressing therapy.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Sodium Alginate is a naturally occurring substance. It reacts with the acid in the stomach to form a gel. Potassium Bicarbonate also reacts with stomach acid, producing bubbles of carbon dioxide. These bubbles are trapped by the gel, forming a raft on top of the stomach contents. The raft prevents stomach acid from flowing back into the esophagus, relieving symptoms like heartburn. The action is physical and does not rely on absorption into the bloodstream. The raft lasts up to 4 hours and is eventually broken down and excreted in the feces.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; children over 12 years</strong>: 1-2 tablets 3-4 times daily after meals and at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6-12 years</strong>: ½-1 tablet 3-4 times daily after meals and at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; children over 12 years</strong>: 5-10 ml (1-2 teaspoonfuls) 3-4 times daily after meals and at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 2-12 years</strong>: 2.5-5 ml (½-1 teaspoonful) 3-4 times daily after meals and at bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Not recommended for children under 2 years</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction:</strong></p><p>A 2-hour time gap should be considered between the intake of this product and other medications, especially tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, iron salts, thyroid hormones, chloroquine, bisphosphonates, and estramustine.</p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Sodium Alginate and Potassium Bicarbonate should not be used by patients who are hypersensitive to these ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p>Possible side effects include nausea, constipation, diarrhea, or headache. Consult a doctor if these symptoms appear. Overdose may cause a sensation of swelling, and medical consultation is recommended in such cases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Can be used during pregnancy if clinically needed.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: No known effect on breastfed infants. Safe for use during breastfeeding.</li><li><strong>Fertility</strong>: No known effect on human fertility.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with renal impairment or congestive cardiac failure.</li><li>Take care when treating patients with hypercalcemia, nephrocalcinosis, or recurrent calcium-containing renal stones.</li><li>May be less effective in patients with very low gastric acid levels.</li><li>If symptoms persist for more than 7 days, the clinical situation should be re-evaluated.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><p>Overdosage is rare. If it occurs, consult a physician immediately.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dose adjustments necessary.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustments necessary.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: Use with caution if a highly restricted salt diet is required.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Sodium Alginate and Potassium Bicarbonate combine to form a raft on top of the stomach contents, preventing acid reflux into the esophagus and providing relief from heartburn and other symptoms. The action is physical and does not involve absorption into the systemic circulation. The raft remains effective for up to 4 hours.</p>', 'active', '283_1742752136.jpg', 8, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:48:56', '2025-05-03 18:08:21'),
(4315, 'Purisal', 'Levosalbutamol', 1.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '1 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Purisal 1 mg</strong></p><p>Purisal 1 mg is indicated for the treatment and prevention of bronchospasm in adults, adolescents, and children aged 6 years and older with reversible obstructive airway diseases.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Purisal 1 mg is a single isomer beta-2 agonist that differs from racemic salbutamol by elimination of (S)-salbutamol. Purisal 1 mg is an effective bronchodilator whose primary mechanism of action is not inhibited by (S)-salbutamol. Thus, when compared with racemic salbutamol, clinically comparable bronchodilation can be achieved. Purisal 1 mg produces bronchodilatation through stimulation of beta-2-adrenergic receptors in bronchial smooth muscles, thereby causing relaxation of bronchial muscle fibers.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Purisal 1 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children (6 - 11 years):</strong> 5 ml (1 mg), three times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults and adolescents above 12 years:</strong> 5-10 ml (1-2 mg), three times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Purisal 1 mg</strong></p><p>Other short-acting sympathomimetic bronchodilators or epinephrine should be used with caution with Purisal 1 mg. If additional adrenergic drugs are to be administered by any route, they should be used with caution to avoid deleterious cardiovascular effects.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Purisal 1 mg is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Purisal 1 mg or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Purisal 1 mg</strong></p><p>Hypocalcemia, palpitation, fine tremors of the skeletal muscle, and muscle cramps may occur. The other likely side effects are nausea, vomiting, epigastric pain, and diarrhea.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The drug should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether Purisal 1 mg is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when oral Purisal 1 mg is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Particular caution is advised in acute severe asthma as this effect may be potentiated by hypoxia and by concomitant treatment with xanthine derivatives, steroids, and diuretics. Serum potassium levels should be monitored in such situations.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '452_1742752251.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:50:51', '2025-05-04 14:57:32'),
(4316, 'Axodin', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 9.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '180 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Axodin 180 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Allergic Rhinitis</strong>: Recommended for adults and children 12 years and older to alleviate seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis symptoms such as sneezing, rhinorrhea, lacrimation, itchy red eyes, and itchy nose, palate, and throat. It enhances work/activity productivity and improves health-related quality of life.</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>: Recommended for adults and children 12 years and older to treat symptoms such as wheals and pruritus. It reduces these symptoms and improves work/activity productivity and quality of life.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Non-sedating Antihistamines</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li><strong>Mode of Action</strong>: Axodin (fexofenadine) is a selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist. It prevents the activation of the H1 receptor, which mediates hypersensitivity and allergic reactions.</li><li><strong>Absorption and Peak Plasma</strong>: After oral administration, fexofenadine is quickly absorbed, reaching peak plasma concentration in 2 to 3 hours. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Seasonal Allergic Rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; Children 12+ years</strong>: 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Impaired renal function</strong>: 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6-11 years</strong>: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Impaired renal function</strong>: 30 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 2-11 years (Suspension)</strong>: 30 mg (5 ml) twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Impaired renal function</strong>: 30 mg (5 ml) once daily.</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; Children 12+ years</strong>: 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Impaired renal function</strong>: 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6-11 years</strong>: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Impaired renal function</strong>: 30 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6 months to less than 2 years (Suspension)</strong>: 15 mg (2.5 ml) twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Impaired renal function</strong>: 15 mg (2.5 ml) once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 2-11 years (Suspension)</strong>: 30 mg (5 ml) twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Impaired renal function</strong>: 30 mg (5 ml) once daily.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Axodin 180 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Erythromycin or Ketoconazole</strong>: Can cause a 2-3 fold increase in fexofenadine levels, but it does not affect the QT interval or increase side effects.</li><li><strong>Antacids (Aluminum and Magnesium Hydroxide)</strong>: Should wait 2 hours between taking Axodin and an antacid to avoid reduced bioavailability.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Should not be used in patients known to be hypersensitive to Axodin or any of its components.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Headaches, fatigue, and dizziness.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Nausea and issues at the administration site.</li><li><strong>Post-marketing surveillance</strong>: Hypersensitivity reactions (angioedema, tightness in the chest), mental disturbances (anxiety, nightmares), cardiac issues (palpitations), gastrointestinal issues (diarrhea), and skin conditions (rash, urticaria).</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Category C (FDA)</strong>: Avoid using unless benefits outweigh risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Fexofenadine passes into human breast milk, so it is not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Elderly &amp; Renal/Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Use with caution in these populations.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Concerns</strong>: May cause tachycardia or palpitations.</li><li><strong>Driving/Operating Machinery</strong>: Fexofenadine is unlikely to impair driving or machine operation. However, individual responses should be monitored.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Dry mouth, sleepiness, fatigue, and dizziness.</li><li><strong>Management</strong>: Overdoses have not resulted in severe adverse reactions. Standard procedures should be followed to remove unabsorbed medication. Fexofenadine is not effectively removed by hemodialysis.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from heat and light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>Fexofenadine stabilizes the inactive form of the H1 receptor, preventing activation and the release of inflammatory mediators like histamine. This reduces allergy symptoms like itching, runny nose, and watery eyes.</li></ul><h3>Pediatric Use</h3><ul><li><strong>Renal &amp; Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Fexofenadine\'s pharmacokinetics are altered in patients with renal impairment, and a lower dose of 60 mg once daily is recommended for these patients. There is no significant impact from moderate to severe hepatic disease.</li><li><strong>Patients Over 65</strong>: The pharmacokinetics are altered in older adults, with enhanced bioavailability, but adverse effects are similar to those in younger patients.</li></ul><p>This is a summary of the key information regarding Axodin 180 mg. Always consult with a healthcare professional before using any medication.</p>', 'active', '525_1742752408.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:53:28', '2025-05-03 00:11:58'),
(4317, 'Inhibita', 'Omeprazole', 4.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Inhibita 20 mg</strong></p><p>Inhibita 20 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcer</li><li>Gastric and duodenal ulcers brought on by NSAIDs</li><li>In individuals with a history of duodenal and stomach ulcers caused by NSAIDs, as a preventative treatment</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</li><li>Long-term care of acid reflux disease</li><li>Dyspepsia caused by acid</li><li>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers</li><li>Prevention of acid aspiration during general anesthesia</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Peptic ulcer caused by <em>Helicobacter pylori</em></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Blocker</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Inhibita 20 mg is a benzimidazole derivative that inhibits stomach acid secretion. It works by inhibiting the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in the gastric parietal cells, thereby preventing gastric acid release. After oral administration, the antisecretory effect begins within an hour, peaks in two hours, and lasts up to 72 hours. Secretion resumes gradually after discontinuation, over a period of 3 to 5 days.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Inhibita 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Benign gastric and duodenal ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks for duodenal ulcers and 8 weeks for gastric ulcers; for severe or recurrent cases, increase to 40 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>NSAID-associated duodenal or gastric ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks; continue for a further 4 weeks if not fully healed. 20 mg once daily for prophylaxis in patients with a history of NSAID-associated ulcers.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks; continue for an additional 4-8 weeks if not fully healed. 40 mg once daily for 8 weeks in GERD resistant to other treatments; maintenance dose of 20 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Long-term management of acid reflux disease</strong>: 10-20 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acid-related dyspepsia</strong>: 10-20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration</strong>: 40 mg the evening before, then 40 mg 2-6 hours before surgery.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: Initially 60 mg daily; usual range 20-120 mg daily (If daily dose exceeds 80 mg, divide into two doses).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication regimen in peptic ulcer disease</strong>: 20 mg twice daily, in combination with Amoxicillin and Metronidazole or Clarithromycin.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric use (severe ulcerating reflux esophagitis)</strong>: For children &gt;1 year with a body weight of 10-20 kg, 10-20 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks; for children &gt;20 kg, 20-40 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li><li><strong>IV Injection</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration</strong>: Inhibita 20 mg 40 mg, administered slowly (over 5 minutes) 1 hour before surgery.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal ulcer, gastric ulcer, or reflux esophagitis</strong>: Inhibita 20 mg IV 40 mg once daily when oral medication is inappropriate.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: Initial dose of 60 mg IV daily; higher doses may be needed and adjusted individually. For doses exceeding 60 mg daily, split the dose into two divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Inhibita 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>: Reconstitute with 10 ml of water for injection, then administer slowly over 2-5 minutes at a rate of 4 ml/min. Use within 4 hours of reconstitution.</li><li><strong>IV Infusion</strong>: Dissolve in 100 ml of 5% dextrose or saline and infuse over 20-30 minutes. Use the solution within 12 hours if dissolved in saline, or 6 hours if dissolved in dextrose. No other medications should be mixed with the reconstituted solution.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Inhibita 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Inhibita 20 mg may slow the elimination of warfarin, phenytoin, and diazepam. Dosage adjustments of these drugs may be required when used concomitantly.</li><li>No interactions have been observed with theophylline, propranolol, or antacids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient in the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Inhibita 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Most individuals tolerate Inhibita 20 mg well. Mild side effects such as nausea, stomach cramping, paresthesia, dizziness, and headaches may occur, but they are typically temporary and rarely require dose adjustments.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Inhibita 20 mg is categorized as C by the US FDA in pregnancy. Three studies have shown no negative impact on pregnancy or fetal health. However, its effects on breast milk are unknown. If necessary, breastfeeding should be discontinued.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Before starting therapy, rule out gastric cancer as the medication may worsen symptoms and delay diagnosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Blocker</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Inhibita 20 mg is a benzimidazole that inhibits stomach acid production by blocking the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system in the gastric parietal cells. This prevents gastric acid release, with effects lasting up to 72 hours after oral administration.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>As previously mentioned, it is classified as a C category drug during pregnancy, but studies show no significant harm to pregnancy or the fetus. Breastfeeding should be stopped if Inhibita 20 mg is used.</li></ul>', 'active', '1299_1742752493.webp', 33, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:54:53', '2025-05-03 21:02:35'),
(4318, 'Bicozin-I', 'Iron Polymaltose Complex + Vitamin B Complex + Zinc', 60.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', 'N/A', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Bicozin-I 100 ml</strong></p><p>This syrup is indicated for the treatment and prevention of Iron, Vitamin B complex, and Zinc deficiencies, especially during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each 5 ml syrup contains:</p><ul><li>Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex equivalent to elemental Iron 50 mg</li><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride 5 mg</li><li>Riboflavin-5-Phosphate Sodium equivalent to Riboflavin 2 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride 2 mg</li><li>Nicotinamide 20 mg</li><li>Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate equivalent to elemental Zinc 10 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Iron &amp; Vitamin Combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This syrup is a preparation of Iron, Vitamin B complex, and Zinc. In this preparation, Iron is present as Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex. Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex facilitates controlled absorption of the iron when it comes in contact with the mucosal cell surface. Due to its non-ionic nature, this Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex is more stable than conventional Iron forms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Bicozin-I 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 5 ml-10 ml (1-2 teaspoonful) 3 times daily or as recommended by the physician.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 5 ml (1 teaspoonful) 3 times daily or as recommended by the physician.</li><li><strong>Infants</strong>: 0.33 ml/kg body weight daily or as recommended by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Bicozin-I 100 ml</strong></p><p>Since Iron is complex bound, ionic interaction with foodstuff components (phytates, oxalates, tannins, etc.) and concomitant administration of medicaments (tetracyclines, antacids) are unlikely to occur.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of this product.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Bicozin-I 100 ml</strong></p><p>This syrup is generally well tolerated. However, some side effects of oral Iron preparations may occur, including nausea, vomiting, constipation, or diarrhea.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be taken in conditions where there is a risk of Iron overload, such as hemochromatosis, thalassemia, hemosiderosis, or hemolytic anemia.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Bicozin-I 100 ml</strong></p><p>In case of overdose, epigastric pain, diarrhea, vomiting, metabolic acidosis, and convulsion may occur. Emergency medical attention should be sought in the case of overdose. Initially, an emetic should be given, followed by gastric lavage and general supportive measures.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>Recommended for use in children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Iron &amp; Vitamin Combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This syrup is a preparation of Iron, Vitamin B complex, and Zinc. Iron is present as Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex, which facilitates controlled absorption of the iron when it comes in contact with the mucosal cell surface. Due to its non-ionic nature, this Iron (III) Hydroxide Polymaltose Complex is more stable than conventional Iron forms.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Recommended for children.</p>', 'active', '258_1742752590.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:56:30', '2025-04-22 19:35:23');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4319, 'Esonix', 'Esomeprazole', 10.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Esonix 40 mg</strong></p><p>Esonix 40 mg is indicated:</p><ul><li>To alleviate GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn and others.</li><li>To treat erosive esophagitis.</li><li>To maintain the erosive esophagitis\'s ability to recover.</li><li>For the eradication of Helicobacter pylori infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</li><li>Duodenal &amp; gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Esonix 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. The majority of patients heal within 4 to 8 weeks. For patients who don\'t heal after 4-8 weeks, an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 20 mg once daily (Clinical studies did not extend 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely after 4 weeks, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori eradication (Triple Therapy):</strong> Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days, Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> 20-80 mg once daily. The dosage should be adjusted individually, and treatment should continue as long as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks according to the response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer:</strong> 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> The recommended adult dose is 40 mg Esonix 40 mg given once daily by intravenous injection (not less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes).</li><li><em>Esomeprazole IV injection should not be administered concomitantly with any other medications through the same intravenous site. Treatment with Esomeprazole IV injection should be discontinued as soon as the patient is able to resume treatment with Esomeprazole delayed-release capsules.</em></li><li><em>Safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</em></li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Esonix 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take one Esonix 40 mg pill or capsule one hour before a meal and consume it whole.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension Use Guidelines:</strong>Place the contents of the entire packet in a small glass with 15 ml of water.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Stir thoroughly and let it thicken for 2-3 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Stir again within 30 minutes and consume.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If any medicine remains, add extra water, swirl, and drink immediately.</li><li>For nasogastric or gastric tube use:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Place 15 ml of water in a syringe and shake.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Wait 2-3 minutes for thickening, then administer into the stomach.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Shake and ingest any remaining substances.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intravenous Injection:</strong>Add 5 ml of solvent (WFI) to the vial to prepare the IV injection solution.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Administer over a minimum of 3 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Use within 12 hours of reconstitution.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not refrigerate.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not use if visible particles are in the reconstituted solution.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Esonix 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is metabolized by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 in the liver.</li><li>Esomeprazole does not interact clinically with phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin.</li><li>Diazepam clearance was reduced by 45% when taken with Esomeprazole.</li><li>Esomeprazole may affect the absorption of drugs like ketoconazole, iron salts, and digoxin due to reduced stomach acid production.</li><li>Esomeprazole\'s pharmacokinetics are unaffected by oral contraceptives, diazepam, phenytoin, or quinidine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the formulations.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Esonix 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, and dry mouth.</li><li>No significant difference in side effects between short-term treatment and maintenance therapy (up to 12 months).</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>A positive symptom response to Esonix 40 mg does not exclude the possibility of stomach cancer.</li><li>Take Esonix capsules at least an hour before meals.</li><li>For patients who have difficulty swallowing capsules, the pellets inside can be mixed with a spoonful of cold applesauce and taken immediately. Do not chew or crush the pellets.</li><li>Antacids can be used while taking Esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Esonix 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose in rats has shown toxic effects at doses 103 times the human dose.</li><li>Symptoms may include decreased motor activity, breathing changes, tremors, ataxia, and convulsions.</li><li>There is no known antidote for Esomeprazole overdose. Dialysis is not effective. Symptomatic and supportive care should be provided.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place at temperatures not exceeding 30°C.</li><li>Keep out of moisture and light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole inhibits the H+/K+-ATPase proton pump in gastric parietal cells, reducing stomach acid production.</li><li>Esomeprazole is the S-isomer of omeprazole, offering superior acid control compared to racemic proton pump inhibitors.</li><li>It is extensively metabolized by the CYP450 enzyme system, with the majority metabolized by CYP2C19.</li><li>Excretion occurs via urine (80%) and feces (20%).</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>No sufficient and reliable research on expectant mothers.</li><li>No teratogenic effects found in animal studies.</li><li>Excretion in breast milk not studied. If esomeprazole is deemed necessary, breastfeeding should be stopped.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric patients have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>No overall differences in safety and efficacy between elderly and younger individuals. However, older individuals may be more sensitive to the drug.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage change required for mild to moderate hepatic insufficiency.</li><li>For severe hepatic impairment, the dose should not exceed 20 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>No expected changes in pharmacokinetics for patients with renal impairment, as less than 1% of esomeprazole is excreted unchanged in the urine.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '144_1742752672.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:57:52', '2025-05-08 14:51:45'),
(4320, 'Domilux', 'Domperidone Maleate', 4.00, 20, 'tablet', '20 tablet in a box', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Domilux 10 mg</strong></p><p>Dyspeptic symptom complex, often linked with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, may include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric sensation of fullness</li><li>Abdominal distension</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Belching</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Early satiety</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms can occur in various conditions, such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine. Additionally, in Parkinson\'s disease, it can manifest as dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting. For radiological studies, it can be used to speed up barium transit in follow-through radiological examinations.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Motility Stimulants</li><li>Motility Stimulants/Dopamine Antagonist</li><li>Prokinetic Drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Domilux 10 mg functions as a dopamine receptor antagonist. It exerts gastroprokinetic action by inhibiting dopamine receptors found in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier means it has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Domilux 10 mg</strong></p><p>For adults, the recommended dosage is 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily. Children should take 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily. When used for acute nausea and vomiting, the maximum treatment duration should not exceed 12 weeks. It is advisable to take this medication 15-30 minutes before a meal.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Domilux 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li>The maximum daily dose of Domperidone is 80 mg.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong></li><li>2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg of body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>For acute and sub-acute conditions, especially in cases of acute nausea and vomiting:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li>20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong></li><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>When administered rectally in the form of suppositories:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults (including elderly):</strong></li><li>30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong></li><li>The maximum daily rectal dose for children weighing between 10 to 25 kg is 30 mg. The dose may be divided throughout the day if necessary.</li></ul><p>Please note that the maximum treatment period for acute nausea and vomiting is 12 weeks.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction of Domilux 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>The simultaneous use of anticholinergic drugs may counteract the antidyspeptic effects of domperidone.</li><li>Antacids and antisecretory drugs should not be administered concurrently with domperidone as they can reduce its oral bioavailability.</li><li>Domperidone primarily undergoes metabolism through the CYP3A4 enzyme. In vitro data suggest that coadministration with drugs significantly inhibiting this enzyme may lead to elevated plasma levels of domperidone. Examples of CYP3A4 inhibitors include azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, nefazodone, and others.</li><li>Domperidone could potentially impact the absorption of concurrently orally administered drugs, especially those with sustained release or enteric-coated formulations. However, in patients already stabilized on medications like digoxin or paracetamol, the concurrent use of domperidone did not affect the blood levels of these drugs.</li><li>Domperidone does not potentiate the action of neuroleptics, and it suppresses the unwanted peripheral effects such as digestive disorders, nausea, and vomiting caused by dopaminergic agonists like bromocriptine and L-dopa without counteracting their central properties.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Domilux 10 mg</li><li>Neonates</li><li>Gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation</li><li>Prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma)</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Domilux 10 mg</strong></p><p>Side effects are uncommon, with occasional reports of transient intestinal cramps. Extrapyramidal symptoms are rare among young children and exceptionally rare in adults. These symptoms typically resolve spontaneously and completely upon discontinuing the treatment. Since the pituitary gland is situated outside the blood-brain barrier, domperidone may lead to an increase in plasma prolactin levels. In rare cases, this hyperprolactinemia can result in neuroendocrinological effects like galactorrhea and gynecomastia. In situations where the blood-brain barrier is immature (as in infants) or compromised, the possibility of neurological side effects cannot be entirely ruled out. Additionally, there have been occasional reports of rare allergic reactions, including rash and urticaria.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of using Domilux 10 mg during pregnancy has not been established; hence, its use is not recommended during pregnancy. Animal studies have not shown any teratogenic effects on the fetus. Domperidone may induce galactorrhea and enhance post-natal lactation. It is excreted in breast milk in minimal quantities, which are insufficient to be considered harmful.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Domilux 10 mg should be employed with utmost care in children due to the potential for an increased risk of extrapyramidal reactions, which can be more pronounced in young children with an incompletely developed blood-brain barrier. Additionally, considering that domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should also be used cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Domilux 10 mg</strong></p><p>Signs of an overdose can manifest as drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions, particularly in children. If an overdose occurs, it is advisable to administer activated charcoal and closely monitor the patient. The use of anticholinergic drugs, antiparkinson drugs, or antihistamines with anticholinergic properties may be beneficial in managing extrapyramidal reactions.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C</li><li>Protected from light &amp; moisture</li><li>Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Motility Stimulants</li><li>Motility Stimulants/Dopamine Antagonist</li><li>Prokinetic Drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Domilux 10 mg acts as a dopamine antagonist, primarily by blocking dopamine receptors located in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its gastroprokinetic effect is achieved by inhibiting dopamine receptors that influence the motility of the gastrointestinal tract. Because it has limited ability to penetrate the blood-brain barrier, Domperidone has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects. Domperidone restores normal motility and tone in the upper gastrointestinal tract, promotes gastric emptying, enhances antral and duodenal peristalsis, and regulates pylorus contraction. Additionally, it increases esophageal peristalsis and lowers esophageal sphincter pressure, preventing the regurgitation of gastric contents.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in infants:</strong> Given that the metabolic and blood-brain barrier functions are not fully developed during the first months of life, any medication should be administered to infants with great caution and under close medical supervision. Although the typical absence of neurological side effects with domperidone is primarily due to its limited penetration through the blood-brain barrier, the potential occurrence of such effects cannot be entirely ruled out in infants under 1 year of age.</li><li><strong>Use in liver disorders:</strong> Domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver and should be employed cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Use in kidney disorders:</strong> In individuals with severe renal insufficiency (serum creatinine &gt;6 mg/100ml, i.e., &gt;0.6 mmol/l), the elimination half-life of domperidone increases from 7.4 to 20.8 hours, although plasma drug levels remain lower than in healthy volunteers. Since very little unchanged drug is excreted through the kidneys, it is unlikely that the dose of a single acute administration needs to be adjusted in patients with renal insufficiency. However, for repeated administration, the dosing frequency should be reduced to once or twice daily, depending on the severity of the impairment, and the dose may need to be lowered. In general, patients on prolonged therapy should undergo regular reviews.</li></ul>', 'active', '1785_1742752790.webp', 17, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 22:59:50', '2025-05-03 08:26:14'),
(4321, 'Seacal-DX', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 15.00, 7, 'tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Seacal-DX</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment &amp; prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, and disorders of osteogenesis. It is also used as a supplement in cases of inadequate calcium intake in childhood diet, rickets, pregnancy &amp; lactation, and in elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis and phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li><li>Or</li><li>Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 200 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate calcium in the blood is essential for a wide variety of bodily functions. Our internal biochemistry will not tolerate a deficiency, even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is also essential for healthy bones as it aids in calcium absorption from the GI tract and stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies show that calcium and vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth, osteoporosis, and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Seacal-DX</strong></p><p>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night, or as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Seacal-DX</strong></p><p>One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or as directed by the physician. Taking it with a full stomach ensures better absorption.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Seacal-DX</strong></p><ul><li>Oral calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline and fluoride preparations; a minimum of 3 hours should be allowed between the ingestion of these medications.</li><li>Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of calcium.</li><li>Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce metabolism of Vitamin D3.</li><li>Concomitant ingestion of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of calcium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the components</li><li>Hypocalcemia resulting from an overdose of Vitamin D3</li><li>Hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Bone metastases</li><li>Severe renal insufficiency</li><li>Severe hypercalciuria</li><li>Renal calculi, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Seacal-DX</strong></p><ul><li>Flatulence, diarrhea, constipation, upper GI discomfort, etc., are rare manifestations.</li><li>Hypercalcemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It should be used as directed by the physician during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with a reduction of the dose may be needed.</li><li>Plasma and serum calcium levels should be monitored in mild to moderate renal impairment and also during long-term use.</li><li>Patients with renal stones or a history of renal stones should take precautions.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Seacal-DX</strong></p><p>At high doses, it may result in nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc. Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature below 30°C, protect from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This is a calcium and Vitamin D3 preparation where calcium carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The calcium carbonate from coral has a chemical structure similar to the composition of human bone. Coral calcium ensures better absorption than other sources. Vitamin D3 aids in the absorption of calcium from the GI tract and helps maintain calcium balance in the body.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of a physician.</p>', 'active', '841_1742752878.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:01:18', '2025-05-04 20:57:59'),
(4322, 'Xenosal', 'Clobetasol Propionate + Salicylic Acid', 150.00, 1, 'x 30 gm tube', '30 gm tube', 'Ointment', '0.05%+3%', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Xenosal 0.05% + 3%</strong></p><p>This ointment is indicated for the relief of the inflammatory manifestations of hyperkeratotic and dry corticosteroid-responsive dermatoses such as:</p><ul><li>Psoriasis</li><li>Chronic atopic dermatitis</li><li>Neurodermatitis (Lichen Simplex Chronicus)</li><li>Lichen planus</li><li>Eczema (including nummular eczema, hand eczema, eczematous dermatitis)</li><li>Seborrheic dermatitis of the scalp</li><li>Ichthyosis vulgaris and other ichthyotic conditions.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong>: A high-potency corticosteroid that decreases inflammation by stabilizing leukocyte lysosomal membranes, preventing the release of destructive acid hydrolases from leukocytes, inhibiting macrophage accumulation in inflamed areas, reducing leukocyte adhesion to capillary endothelium, reducing capillary wall permeability and edema formation, decreasing complement components, antagonizing histamine activity, and reducing fibroblast proliferation, collagen deposition, and subsequent scar tissue formation.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid</strong>: A potent keratolytic agent that softens and destroys the stratum corneum by increasing endogenous hydration, causing the horny layer of the skin to swell, soften, and desquamate. At high concentrations, salicylic acid has a caustic effect and also possesses weak antifungal and antibacterial activity.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Xenosal 0.05% + 3%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: Apply a thin layer to affected skin areas twice daily and rub in gently &amp; completely. Some patients may achieve adequate maintenance therapy with less frequent applications. Therapy should be discontinued once control has been achieved. If no improvement is seen within 2 weeks, reassessment of diagnosis may be necessary. It should not be used with occlusive dressing. Treatment beyond 2 consecutive weeks should not exceed 50 gm/week to avoid suppression of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Use in pediatric patients under 12 years of age is not recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Xenosal 0.05% + 3%</strong></p><p>For external use only.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Xenosal 0.05% + 3%</strong></p><ul><li>No significant interaction with Clobetasol Propionate ointment and cream or with Salicylic Acid when used as indicated. However, topical salicylic acid may increase the absorption of other topically applied medicines, so concurrent use with other topical medicines in the same area should be avoided.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong>: Contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to Clobetasol Propionate and in the treatment of primary infected bacterial or fungal skin lesions (if no anti-infective agent is used simultaneously), primary cutaneous viral infections (e.g., herpes simplex, vaccinia, varicella), and tuberculous skin lesions. Contraindicated in dermatoses in children under one year of age (including dermatitis and diaper eruptions).</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid</strong>: Contraindicated in patients with salicylate hypersensitivity or sensitivity to any other ingredient in the preparation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Xenosal 0.05% + 3%</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged use of large amounts of Clobetasol Propionate can result in systemic absorption leading to hypercortisolism, especially in infants and children, and if occlusive dressings are used. Local atrophy may occur with prolonged treatment.</li><li>Rarely, corticosteroid treatment (or withdrawal) of psoriasis can provoke the pustular form of the disease.</li><li>Salicylic Acid may cause sensitivity reactions, dryness, and irritation.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong>: Corticosteroid administration during pregnancy has been linked to fetal developmental abnormalities in animals, although relevance to humans is not established. Its use during lactation is not well established, and it should only be used if the benefits outweigh the risks. It should not be used extensively or in large amounts for prolonged periods during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid</strong>: No known contraindications for use during pregnancy and lactation, but safety has not been firmly established. Caution is advised.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Not for prolonged use in high concentrations or on large areas of the body.</li><li>Avoid use on broken skin, mucous membranes, mouth, eyes, or the anogenital region.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired peripheral circulation or diabetes.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Xenosal 0.05% + 3%</strong></p><ul><li>Acute overdose is unlikely, but chronic overdosage or misuse could lead to symptoms of hypercortisolism. If this occurs, corticosteroid use should be gradually tapered under medical supervision.</li><li>Symptoms of systemic salicylate poisoning (e.g., tinnitus, dizziness, deafness) may occur with excessive application of Salicylic Acid to large areas of skin over prolonged periods.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong>: A high-potency corticosteroid that decreases inflammation as outlined above.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid</strong>: A keratolytic agent that softens and destroys the stratum corneum by increasing endogenous hydration.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy Considerations</strong></p><ul><li>Corticosteroid use during pregnancy should be avoided unless absolutely necessary, and Salicylic Acid should be used with caution during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further clarifications or additional details on any part!</p>', 'active', '1395_1742752976.webp', 8, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:02:56', '2025-05-05 18:12:28'),
(4323, 'Algicid DX', 'Sodium Alginate + Sodium Bicarbonate + Calcium Carbonate', 300.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '(500 mg+213 mg+325 mg)/10 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Algicid DX 200 ml</strong></p><p>Algicid DX 200 ml is indicated for individuals experiencing:</p><ul><li>Gastric reflux</li><li>Heartburn</li><li>Flatulence associated with gastric reflux</li><li>Heartburn during pregnancy</li><li>Epigastric and retrosternal discomfort where the underlying cause is gastric reflux.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The mode of action of Algicid DX 200 ml is physical and does not depend on absorption into the systemic circulation. Upon ingestion, it rapidly reacts with gastric acid, forming a protective alginic acid gel raft with a nearly neutral pH. This raft floats on the stomach contents, effectively preventing gastroesophageal reflux for up to 4 hours. In severe cases, the raft itself may be refluxed into the esophagus, providing a soothing effect instead of the stomach contents.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Algicid DX 200 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years</strong>: Take 10-20 ml after meals and at bedtime, up to four times a day.</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 12 years</strong>: Take 5-10 ml after meals and at bedtime, up to four times a day.</li><li><strong>Children under 6 years</strong>: Not recommended.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: No dosage adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dosage modification necessary.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: Use caution if a highly restricted salt diet is required.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Algicid DX 200 ml</strong></p><p>A time interval of 2 hours should be observed between taking Algicid DX and the administration of other medicinal products, particularly:</p><ul><li>Tetracyclines</li><li>Digoxin</li><li>Fluoroquinolones</li><li>Iron salts</li><li>Ketoconazole</li><li>Neuroleptics</li><li>Thyroid hormones</li><li>Penicillamine</li><li>Beta-blockers (e.g., atenolol, metoprolol, propranolol)</li><li>Glucocorticoids</li><li>Chloroquine</li><li>Bisphosphonates (e.g., diphosphonates)</li><li>Estramustine</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Algicid DX should not be used in patients with a known or suspected hypersensitivity to any of its active ingredients or excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Algicid DX 200 ml</strong></p><p>Possible side effects include:</p><ul><li>Constipation</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Belching</li></ul><p>If an excessive dose is taken, patients may experience a sensation of swelling. If any of these symptoms occur, consult a physician.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Studies involving over 500 pregnant women have shown no malformation or fetal/neonatal toxicity from the active substances. It can be safely used during pregnancy when clinically necessary.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding</strong>: No adverse effects have been observed in breastfed infants. This medication is considered safe for use during breastfeeding.</li><li><strong>Fertility</strong>: Pre-clinical studies have shown no detrimental effects on fertility or reproduction.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>If symptoms do not improve within 7 days, reassess the clinical condition.</li><li>Each 10 mL dose contains 141 mg of sodium (6.2 mmol). This should be considered for patients on a restricted salt diet.</li><li>Each 10 mL dose contains 160 mg of calcium carbonate (1.6 mmol). Caution is needed for patients with hypercalcemia, nephrocalcinosis, and recurrent calcium-containing renal stones.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Algicid DX 200 ml</strong></p><p>In the event of overdose, symptomatic treatment should be administered. Abdominal distension may occur.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool (below 30°C) and dry place, away from light.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li><li>Do not refrigerate or freeze.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Not recommended for children under 6 years.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No dose modification is required for this age group.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Algicid DX operates through a physical mechanism and does not rely on absorption into the systemic circulation. It combines two antacids, Calcium Carbonate and Sodium Bicarbonate, with Alginate. Upon ingestion, it rapidly reacts with gastric acid to form a protective alginic acid gel raft, which floats on the stomach contents, effectively reducing gastroesophageal reflux for up to 4 hours. The raft also entraps bile and pepsin, providing additional protection for the esophagus.</p>', 'active', '1789_1742753070.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:04:30', '2025-05-02 22:56:38'),
(4324, 'Trilock', 'Montelukast Sodium', 8.06, 10, 'x 3.5 gm sachet', '3.5 gm sachet', 'Oral Powder', '4 mg/3.5 gm', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Trilock 4 mg/3.5 gm</h3><p>Trilock 4 mg/3.5 gm is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Trilock 4 mg/3.5 gm works by inhibiting the effects of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) at the CysLT1 receptor, which plays a role in asthma and allergic rhinitis. It has a strong affinity for the CysLT1 receptor, without any agonist activity, thereby inhibiting LTD4’s physiological effects.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (15 years and older):</strong> 10 mg tablet once daily</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6 to 14 years of age: 5 mg tablet once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 to 5 years of age: 4 mg tablet once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6 months to 5 years of age: 4 mg oral granules once daily (administered directly or mixed with cold water/soft food)</li><li><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency:</strong> No dosage adjustment required in mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong> No dosage adjustment recommended.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> No dosage adjustment required, although the plasma half-life may be slightly longer.</li></ul><h3>Drug Interactions</h3><ul><li>Trilock 4 mg/3.5 gm does not show clinically significant interactions with theophylline, prednisone, oral contraceptives, terfenadine, digoxin, warfarin, or other commonly used treatments.</li><li><strong>Cytochrome P450 inducers</strong> like phenobarbital and rifampin may decrease the drug\'s effectiveness, so clinical monitoring is advised.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Individuals with hypersensitivity to any ingredient in Trilock 4 mg/3.5 gm should avoid using it.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><p><strong>Common:</strong></p><ul><li>Upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, vomiting.</li></ul><p><strong>Uncommon:</strong></p><ul><li>Akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, irritability, muscle complaints, oedema, sleep disturbances.</li></ul><p><strong>Rare:</strong></p><ul><li>Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, hallucinations, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, and tremor.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>Classified as <strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>. While shown to cross the placenta in animal studies, no controlled human studies exist. Caution is advised in pregnant women.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> The drug is secreted in breast milk; caution is advised when used by nursing mothers.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Not recommended for reversal of acute asthma attacks or status asthmaticus.</li><li>Patients should have access to appropriate rescue medications.</li><li>Exercise-induced bronchospasm should not be managed with a single dose of Trilock.</li><li>Patients with aspirin sensitivity should avoid aspirin or NSAIDs during treatment.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>Common symptoms:</strong> Nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, headache, stomach pain.</li><li><strong>Management:</strong> Supportive measures, including removing unabsorbed material and clinical monitoring.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, in a cool, dry place, away from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Drug Class</h3><ul><li>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>Trilock selectively inhibits the CysLT1 receptor, preventing the effects of cysteinyl leukotrienes, which are involved in the inflammatory process contributing to asthma and allergic rhinitis symptoms.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li>Passes the placenta in animal studies; human data is limited. Should be used during pregnancy only if necessary, and caution should be exercised when used by nursing mothers.</li></ul>', 'active', '1512_1742753226.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:07:06', '2025-05-04 22:35:02'),
(4325, 'Zovia Kids', 'Multivitamin + Cod Liver Oil', 100.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', 'N/A', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '423_1742753716.jpg', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:15:16', '2025-04-22 19:34:02'),
(4326, 'Pantolok', 'Pantoprazole Sodium', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Pantolok 20 mg</strong></p><p>Pantolok 20 mg is recommended when inhibiting acid secretion is therapeutically beneficial, including:</p><ul><li>Stomach ulcer disorders</li><li>Illnesses caused by gastroesophageal reflux</li><li>Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug-induced ulcer (NSAIDs)</li><li>Helicobacter pylori eradication (in combination with antibiotics)</li><li>Syndrome of Zollinger-Ellison</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Blocker</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Pantolok 20 mg is a proton pump inhibitor that works by covalently attaching to the H+/K+ATPase enzyme system on the surface of the gastric parietal cell to decrease the last stage of gastric acid generation. Regardless of the stimulus, this impact results in the suppression of both basal and induced stomach acid secretion, lasting for more than 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Pantolok 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Benign gastric ulcer</strong>: 40 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, continued for a further 4 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastro-esophageal reflux disease</strong>: 20-40 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, continued for a further 4 weeks if not fully healed; maintenance dose is 20 mg daily, which may be increased to 40 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal ulcer</strong>: 40 mg daily in the morning for 2 weeks, continued for a further 2 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal ulcer associated with Helicobacter pylori</strong>: Pantolok 20 mg is recommended at a dose of 40 mg twice daily in combination with antimicrobial agents (Amoxycillin 1 g and Clarithromycin 500 mg both twice daily for one week, or Clarithromycin 250 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg both twice daily for one week).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of NSAID-associated gastric or duodenal ulcer</strong>: 20 mg daily for those requiring long-term NSAID treatment.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: Initially 80 mg once daily, adjusted according to response (elderly max. 40 mg daily); daily doses above 80 mg given in 2 divided doses.</li><li><strong>IV Injection</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Duodenal ulcer and gastric ulcer</strong>: 40 mg once daily for 7-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease associated with a history of erosive esophagitis</strong>: 40 mg once daily for 7-10 days</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of rebleeding in peptic ulcer</strong>: IV 80 mg, followed by 8 mg/hour infusion for 72 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration</strong>: 80 mg IV every 12 hours for 24 hours, followed by 40 mg every 12 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Long-term management of Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome and other pathological hypersecretory conditions</strong>: 80 mg IV every 12 hours, may increase to 80 mg every 8 hours if needed, may titrate to higher doses depending on acid output.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Pantolok 20 mg</strong></p><p>In clinical investigations, no significant medication interactions were found.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those with known hypersensitivity to any of the formulation\'s ingredients should not take Pantolok 20 mg.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Pantolok 20 mg</strong></p><p>Both in the short-term and the long-term, Pantolok 20 mg is well tolerated. The most frequent adverse effects are headache and diarrhea, while abdominal pain, flatulence, rash, sleeplessness, and hyperglycemia are only rarely reported side effects.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pantolok 20 mg is classified as a B pregnancy drug by the US FDA. However, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. This medication should only be used during pregnancy if clearly necessary, as a human response to treatments is not usually predicted by studies on animal reproduction. It has been demonstrated that Pantolok 20 mg is excreted in human milk. So, a choice should be made whether to stop nursing or stop taking the medication, taking into account the benefit of the medication to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Patients should be advised not to split, chew, or crush the Pantolok 20 mg pill. The use of Pantolok 20 mg for an extended period of time may cause malabsorption of cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) or raise the risk of problems linked to osteoporosis.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Pantolok 20 mg</strong></p><p>There are no recognized signs of human overdose. Due to its strong protein binding, Pantolok 20 mg is not easily dialyzable. There is no specific therapy that is advised, other than symptomatic and supportive management.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep dry and away from heat and light. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Direction for use of IV injection</strong>: Pantolok 20 mg lyophilized powder with 0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection must only be administered intravenously and under no other circumstances. A gradual intravenous dose of Pantolok 20 mg should be administered. The powder-containing vial is mixed with 10 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride injection to create the IV injection solution. Upon reconstitution, the injection should be administered gradually over a minimum of 2 to 5 minutes. Use just recently made solutions. The reconstituted solution can be kept for up to 4 hours at room temperature (up to 30°C).</li><li><strong>How to administer an IV infusion</strong>: An intravenous infusion of Pantolok 20 mg should be administered over roughly 15 minutes. An IV infusion of Pantolok 20 mg needs to be reconstituted with 10 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride injection before being further diluted (admixed) to a final volume of 100 ml using 0.9% sodium chloride injection, 5% dextrose, or lactated Ringer\'s injection. Prior to further dilution, the reconstituted solution may be kept at room temperature (up to 30°C) for a maximum of 4 hours. The admixed solution must be used within 24 hours of the initial reconstitution and may be kept at room temperature (up to 30°C).</li></ul>', 'active', '18_1742753806.jpg', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:16:46', '2025-05-04 10:15:47'),
(4327, 'Surgical Gauze', 'Sterile Gauze', 18.00, 1, 'x 1 Bandage', '1 Bandage', 'Bandage', '0 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '299_1742753958.jpg', 74, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:19:18', '2025-05-04 21:29:45');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4328, 'Clarigen', 'Levocetirizine Dihydrochloride', 2.25, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Clarigen 5 mg</strong></p><p>Levocetirizine is indicated in the treatment of symptoms associated with allergic conditions such as:</p><ul><li>Perennial allergic rhinitis</li><li>Seasonal allergic rhinitis</li><li>Chronic idiopathic urticaria</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Clarigen 5 mg is a preparation of Levocetirizine, which is the active (levo) isomer of Cetirizine. It is a new, highly effective, and well-tolerated non-sedating antihistamine with potent antiallergic properties. It has a two-fold higher affinity for H1-receptors than Cetirizine. Levocetirizine has a rapid and long-lasting action, allowing once-a-day administration.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Clarigen 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children 12 years of age and older</strong>: 5 mg once daily in the evening</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 11 years of age</strong>: 2.5 mg once daily in the evening</li><li><strong>Children 6 months to 5 years of age</strong>: 1.25 mg (1/2 teaspoon oral solution) once daily in the evening</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Adjust the dose in patients 12 years of age and older with decreased renal function</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Clarigen 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children 12 years of age and older</strong>: The recommended dose is 5 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 11 years of age</strong>: The recommended dose is 2.5 mg (½ tablet or 1 teaspoon oral solution) once daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6 months to 5 years of age</strong>: The recommended dose is 1.25 mg (½ teaspoon oral solution) once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric use</strong>: The recommended dose for the treatment of the uncomplicated skin manifestations of chronic idiopathic urticaria in patients 6 months to 17 years of age is based on extrapolation of efficacy from adults 18 years of age and older.</li><li><strong>Geriatric use</strong>: In clinical studies, each approved indication did not include sufficient numbers of patients aged 65 years and older to determine whether they respond differently than younger patients.</li><li><strong>Hepatic impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is required in patients with solely hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Renal impairment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mild renal impairment (creatinine clearance: 50-80 ml/min): a dose of 2.5 mg once daily is recommended.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Moderate renal impairment (creatinine clearance: 30-50 ml/min): a dose of 2.5 mg once every other day is recommended.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance: 10-30 ml/min): a dose of 2.5 mg twice weekly (administered once every 3-4 days) is recommended.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">End-stage renal disease patients (creatinine clearance: &lt;10 ml/min) and patients undergoing hemodialysis: should not receive Levocetirizine.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Clarigen 5 mg</strong></p><p>Levocetirizine is not known to have any interactions with other drugs.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to levocetirizine, cetirizine, or its parent compound hydroxyzine.</li><li>Patients with severe renal impairment (creatinine clearance: &lt;10 ml/min) should not be administered levocetirizine.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Clarigen 5 mg</strong></p><p>Generally, levocetirizine is well-tolerated. However, a few side effects like headache, dry mouth, fatigue, and skin rash have been reported rarely.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: The safety of Levocetirizine in pregnancy has not been established. Therefore, it should be used with caution during pregnancy and only if the potential benefits to the mother outweigh any risks to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Levocetirizine passes into breast milk. It should be used with caution in nursing mothers and only if the expected benefit to the mother is greater than the risk to the nursing infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Epileptic patients and patients at risk of convulsions.</li><li>Renal impairment.</li><li>Pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Clarigen 5 mg</strong></p><p>No clinically relevant adverse events have been reported in case of overdose. However, in the event of overdosage, symptomatic and supportive treatment is recommended.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature not exceeding 30°C in a dry place. Protect from light.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Paediatric use</strong>: Not recommended for use in children less than 6 years of age.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This preparation contains Levocetirizine, the active (Levo) isomer of cetirizine. It is a highly effective and well-tolerated nonsedating antihistamine with potent antiallergic properties. It has a two-fold higher affinity for H1 receptors than cetirizine. Levocetirizine has a rapid and long-acting action, allowing once-a-day administration.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong>: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Since animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, levocetirizine should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li>Levocetirizine is excreted in breast milk and is not recommended for use by nursing mothers.</li></ul>', 'active', '979_1742754138.webp', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:22:18', '2025-05-03 01:22:18'),
(4329, 'Defrol', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 35.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '40000 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Defrol 40000 IU:</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated in the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also used as an adjunct to specific therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Vitamin D3, in its synthetic form (Cholecalciferol), is essential for normal bone growth, development, and maintaining bone density. It is also crucial for the utilization of both calcium and phosphorus. Babies need Vitamin D3 for healthy growth and development. It acts as a hormone.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation</li><li>Vitamin D preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) assists in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. Vitamin D3 is essential for normal bone growth and maintaining bone density. It also reduces the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, prevents the risk of certain cancers (such as breast and colorectal cancer), and helps maintain adequate insulin levels for type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Defrol 40000 IU:</strong></p><p><strong>For Capsule:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 40000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance therapy:</strong> 1400-2000 IU/day. To confirm the target level of 25-hydroxyvitamin D, measurement should be done 3-4 months after initiating maintenance therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 20000 IU every 4 weeks. Higher doses may be required in certain situations.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Addition to specific therapy for osteoporosis:</strong> 20000 IU once a month.</li><li><strong>Children (12-18 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 20000 IU once every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 20000 IU every 6 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>For Film-Coated Tablet:</strong></p><ul><li>1000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily, or as directed by a physician. Take the medicine with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li></ul><p><strong>For Oroflash or Chewable Tablets:</strong></p><ul><li>1000 IU to 2000 IU daily, or as directed by a physician. Take the medicine with food or within 1 hour after a meal. Place the tablet in the mouth and swallow after chewing.</li></ul><p><strong>For Syrup:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For patients at risk of Cholecalciferol deficiency:0-1 year:</strong> 400 IU/day (2 ml)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>&gt;1 year:</strong> 600 IU/day (3 ml)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Cholecalciferol-deficient patients:0-1 year:</strong> 2000 IU/day (+50000 IU/week) for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1-18 years:</strong> 2000 IU/day for 6 weeks</li></ul><p><strong>Injection:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention:</strong>Infants receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nursed infants or infants not receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk, or young children up to 5 years of age: 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adolescents: 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pregnancy: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Elderly: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For digestive disorders, concomitant treatment with antiepileptics, and other particular conditions not described above: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU or 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 3 or 6 months.</li></ul><p><strong>Vitamin D Deficiency:</strong></p><ul><li>1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU, which can be repeated 1 to 6 months later or as directed by the registered physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Defrol 40000 IU:</strong></p><p>It interferes with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (such as liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Defrol 40000 IU:</strong></p><ul><li>General side effects include hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, and abdominal pain.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Studies show safe use of doses up to 4000 IU during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU, but higher doses may be required for those with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Pregnant women should follow the advice of their medical practitioner, as their requirements may vary depending on disease severity and treatment response. Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk, but overdose in infants from nursing mothers has not been observed. However, additional Vitamin D3 should be considered when prescribing it to a breast-fed child.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Defrol 40000 IU:</strong></p><ul><li>May lead to hypervitaminosis D.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30ºC, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation</li><li>Vitamin D preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus, essential for normal bone growth and maintenance of bone density. It also reduces the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, reduces the risk of certain cancers, and helps maintain adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses:</strong></p><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</li></ul>', 'in-active', '1664_1742754232.jpg', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:23:52', '2025-05-08 14:28:41'),
(4330, 'Clofenac SR', 'Diclofenac Sodium', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Clofenac SR 100 mg is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) used to treat a range of conditions involving inflammation, pain, and swelling. Here\'s a summary of its indications, pharmacology, dosage, and other key information:</p><h3><strong>Indications:</strong></h3><ol><li><strong>Rheumatology:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Inflammatory and degenerative forms of rheumatism</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Chronic involutive polyarthritis, ankylosing spondylarthritis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Osteoarthritis, spondylarthroses, acute gout</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Peri-articular rheumatic disorders</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Surgery and Traumatology:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sprains, bruises, dislocations, fractures</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Soft tissue injuries and post-surgical inflammation and pain</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Obstetrics and Gynecology:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Primary dysmenorrhea (painful menstruation)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Episiotomy, adnexitis, endometritis, salpingitis, mastitis</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Otorhinolaryngology:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pre-operative medication for pain, inflammation, and swelling prevention</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Dentistry:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Post-operative and post-traumatic pain, inflammation, and swelling</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Other indications:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For pain and inflammation prevention in urogenital tract surgeries</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Renal and biliary colic</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></h3><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></h3><ul><li>Diclofenac inhibits prostaglandin biosynthesis, which is responsible for inflammation, pain, and fever.</li><li>It has anti-rheumatic, anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic properties.</li><li>Diclofenac is rapidly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and reaches peak plasma concentration in about 2 hours.</li><li>It is 99.7% bound to plasma proteins and undergoes liver metabolism.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sustained-release tablets: 100 mg once daily with liquid, preferably during a meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Conventional tablets: 75-150 mg daily, divided into two or three doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Suppositories: 75-150 mg daily, divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gel: 2-4 g applied 3-4 times daily to the affected area.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-3 mg/kg body weight per day in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The lowest effective dosage is recommended due to age and physical status.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects:</strong></h3><ul><li>Common: Epigastric pain, nausea, dizziness, headache, rash, and peripheral edema.</li><li>Rare: Local skin irritation, redness, and skin rash (with gel application).</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions:</strong></h3><ul><li>Caution when using with digoxin, methotrexate, cyclosporine, or lithium.</li><li>Use with other NSAIDs or corticosteroids may increase side effects like gastrointestinal bleeding.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications:</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to diclofenac or any other ingredients.</li><li>Peptic ulcers or gastrointestinal bleeding.</li><li>Asthma attacks triggered by NSAIDs.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></h3><ul><li>Avoid during pregnancy, especially in the first trimester, unless benefits outweigh risks.</li><li>Small amounts of diclofenac may be found in breast milk, but it\'s unlikely to affect the infant.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage:</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light, below 30°C.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p>This drug is part of the NSAID class, widely used for managing pain and inflammation in various conditions like arthritis, trauma, and post-surgical recovery. Always use it under medical supervision, especially with prolonged use or in patients with pre-existing conditions like peptic ulcers.</p>', 'active', '1800_1742754354.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:25:54', '2025-05-03 01:23:19'),
(4331, 'Fexofast', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 9.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '120 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Fexofast 180 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Allergic Rhinitis</strong>: Fexofast 180 mg is indicated for the alleviation of symptoms associated with both seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis in adults and children 12 years of age and older. Symptoms treated include sneezing, rhinorrhea, lacrimation, itchy, red eyes, and itchy nose, palate, and throat. Fexofast improves work/activity productivity and quality of life in individuals with allergic rhinitis.</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>: Fexofast 180 mg is recommended for the treatment of chronic idiopathic urticaria symptoms in adults and children aged 12 years and older. It reduces the number of wheals and pruritus associated with the condition, leading to improvements in work/activity productivity and overall quality of life.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Non-sedating Antihistamines</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li><strong>Mechanism of Action</strong>: Fexofast 180 mg is a selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist (antihistamine). After oral administration, it is rapidly absorbed and reaches peak plasma concentration in 2 to 3 hours. Fexofast does not cross the blood-brain barrier, which minimizes central nervous system effects.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Seasonal Allergic Rhinitis</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children ≥ 12 years</strong>: 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6 to 11 years</strong>: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 2 to 11 years (suspension)</strong>: 30 mg or 5 ml twice daily.</li><li>In cases of impaired renal function:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children ≥ 12 years</strong>: 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6 to 11 years</strong>: 30 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 2 to 11 years (suspension)</strong>: 30 mg or 5 ml once daily.</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children ≥ 12 years</strong>: 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6 to 11 years</strong>: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6 months to 2 years (suspension)</strong>: 15 mg or 2.5 ml twice daily.</li><li>In cases of impaired renal function:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children ≥ 12 years</strong>: 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children 6 months to 11 years (suspension)</strong>: 15 mg or 2.5 ml once daily.</li></ul><h3>Interactions</h3><ul><li><strong>Erythromycin or Ketoconazole</strong>: Co-administration may cause a 2- to 3-fold increase in plasma levels of fexofenadine, but it does not significantly impact the QT interval or result in increased adverse reactions.</li><li><strong>Aluminum and Magnesium Hydroxide Antacids</strong>: Fexofast 180 mg should not be taken within 15 minutes of antacids containing aluminum and magnesium hydroxide, as this may reduce bioavailability. It is recommended to wait at least two hours before taking them together.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong>: Fexofast 180 mg should not be used by individuals with known hypersensitivity to fexofenadine or any of its components.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Headache, fatigue, dizziness, and nausea.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Exhaustion, hypersensitivity reactions (e.g., angioedema, chest tightness, dyspnea, flushing, systemic anaphylaxis), anxiety, nightmares, insomnia, palpitations, tachycardia, rash, urticaria, and pruritus.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Fexofast 180 mg is categorized as <strong>Category C</strong> by the FDA, meaning its use should be avoided unless the benefits outweigh the risks to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Fexofast 180 mg is not recommended for breastfeeding mothers, as it may pass into breast milk.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Elderly Patients</strong>: Special care should be taken when administering Fexofast 180 mg to elderly individuals due to potential pharmacokinetic changes.</li><li><strong>Renal and Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Fexofast should be used cautiously in patients with renal or hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Concerns</strong>: Antihistamines like Fexofast can be associated with tachycardia and palpitations, so individuals with cardiovascular conditions should use it carefully.</li><li><strong>Driving and Operating Machinery</strong>: Fexofast does not have a significant effect on the central nervous system, making it unlikely to impair the ability to drive or operate machinery. However, patients should assess their individual response before engaging in these activities.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose include dry mouth, drowsiness, fatigue, and dizziness. Higher doses have been studied without significant adverse effects compared to placebo. In case of overdose, symptomatic treatment is recommended.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li><strong>Storage</strong>: Store in a dry place, away from heat and light. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>Fexofast 180 mg acts as an <strong>inverse agonist</strong> at the H1 histamine receptor, stabilizing the receptor in its inactive form and preventing downstream allergic reactions. This action helps alleviate symptoms like itching, watery eyes, and runny nose caused by histamine release during allergic reactions.</li></ul><h3>Pediatric Use</h3><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Dosage adjustments may be necessary for children with renal impairment, and 60 mg once daily is recommended for children with significant renal issues.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Moderate to severe liver disease does not significantly alter the pharmacokinetics of Fexofast.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further details on any specific aspect!</p>', 'in-active', '1249_1742754446.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:27:26', '2025-05-08 14:56:19'),
(4332, 'Rablet', 'Rabeprazole Sodium', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here’s a summarized version of the information on <strong>Rablet 20 mg</strong> (Rabeprazole) in normal bold text:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Rablet 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Active duodenal ulcer</strong></li><li><strong>Active benign stomach ulcer</strong></li><li><strong>Symptomatic gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</strong> (erosive or ulcerative)</li><li><strong>GERD maintenance</strong></li><li><strong>Moderate to severe GERD symptoms</strong></li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong></li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication</strong> (in combination with antibiotics for peptic ulcer disease)</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Proton Pump Inhibitor</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Rabeprazole</strong> inhibits the gastric H+/K+-ATPase, which reduces gastric acid secretion.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer:</strong> 20 mg in the morning for 4 weeks</li><li><strong>Erosive/ulcerative GERD:</strong> 20 mg daily for 4-8 weeks</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks (if symptoms persist, consider further treatment)</li><li><strong>GERD maintenance:</strong> 20 mg daily</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</strong> 60 mg once daily (up to 120 mg/day based on individual needs)</li><li><strong>H. pylori eradication:</strong> 20 mg with Amoxicillin (1000 mg) and Clarithromycin (500 mg) twice daily for 7 days</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><ul><li>Take <strong>Rablet 20 mg</strong> in the morning, ideally before eating.</li><li><strong>Do not chew or crush the tablet.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Itraconazole</strong> and <strong>ketoconazole</strong> absorption can be affected due to reduced stomach acidity.</li><li><strong>Atazanavir</strong> should not be co-administered with rabeprazole due to pH-dependent absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation</li><li>Not recommended during pregnancy or breastfeeding</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Headache, nausea, vomiting, constipation, dry mouth, muscle pain, dizziness, sleepiness, appetite changes.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>FDA pregnancy category C</strong> (use if clearly needed).</li><li>Rabeprazole may be excreted in breast milk; the decision to continue breastfeeding or medication should be carefully considered.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Rule out malignancy before starting treatment, as rabeprazole may mask symptoms of stomach/oesophageal cancer.</li><li><strong>Long-term use</strong> (especially &gt;1 year) may increase the risk of fractures in elderly patients.</li><li>Ensure adequate calcium and vitamin D intake if at risk for osteoporosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms are generally mild and reversible. There is no specific antidote. Treatment is symptomatic.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of children\'s reach.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in children have not been established.</li><li>No dose adjustments are required for <strong>hepatic or renal impairment</strong>.</li></ul><p>This covers the essential information about <strong>Rablet 20 mg</strong>. If you need further details or clarification, feel free to ask!</p>', 'active', '68_1742754544.jpg', 11, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:29:04', '2025-05-04 15:03:10'),
(4333, 'Ostel-D', 'Alendronic Acid + Vitamin D3', 30.20, 8, 'tablet', '8 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '70 mg+2800 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is a revised version of your provided content with headings in bold text as requested:</p><p><strong>Indications of Ostel-D 70 mg+2800 IU</strong></p><p>Alendronic Acid &amp; Vitamin D3 is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Treatment of osteoporosis in postmenopausal women.</li><li>Treatment of osteoporosis in men.</li><li>Treatment of osteoporosis as it increases the bone mass and reduces the incidence of fractures, including those of the hip and spine.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Combined preparations: Inhibiting bone resorption.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Alendronate Sodium is a bisphosphonate that acts as a specific inhibitor of osteoclast-mediated bone resorption. Alendronate is effective when administered at least 30 minutes before breakfast. It transiently distributes to soft tissues following administration but is then rapidly distributed to bone or excreted in the urine. Protein binding in human plasma is approximately 78%. There is no evidence that Alendronate is metabolized in animals or humans. At the cellular level, Alendronate shows preferential localization to sites of bone resorption, especially under osteoclasts. It inhibits osteoclast activity. In addition, bone formation exceeds bone resorption, leading to progressive gains in bone mass. Vitamin D is required for normal bone formation. Vitamin D insufficiency develops when both sunlight exposure and dietary intake are inadequate. Insufficiency is associated with negative calcium balance, increased parathyroid hormone levels, bone loss, and increased risk of skeletal fracture.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Ostel-D 70 mg+2800 IU</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Treatment of Osteoporosis in Postmenopausal Women</strong>: The recommended dosage is 70 mg alendronate and 2800 IU vitamin D3 or 70 mg alendronate and 5600 IU vitamin D3 tablet once weekly. For most osteoporotic women, the appropriate dose is 70 mg alendronate and 5600 IU vitamin D3 once weekly.</li><li><strong>Treatment to Increase Bone Mass in Men with Osteoporosis</strong>: The recommended dosage is 70 mg alendronate and 2800 IU vitamin D3 or 70 mg alendronate and 5600 IU vitamin D3 tablet once weekly. For most osteoporotic men, the appropriate dose is 70 mg alendronate and 5600 IU vitamin D3 once weekly.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Ostel-D 70 mg+2800 IU</strong></p><p>To permit adequate absorption, Alendronate &amp; Colecalciferol must be taken at least 30 minutes before the first food, beverage, or medication of the day with plain water only. Other beverages (including mineral water), food, and some medications are likely to reduce the absorption of Alendronate. To facilitate delivery to the stomach and reduce the potential for esophageal irritation, Alendronate &amp; Colecalciferol tablet should only be swallowed upon rising for the day with a full glass of water. Patients should not lie down for at least 30 minutes after taking Alendronate until after their first food of the day. Alendronate &amp; Colecalciferol should not be taken at bedtime.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Ostel-D 70 mg+2800 IU</strong></p><ul><li>Calcium supplements, antacids, and some oral medications will interfere with the absorption of Alendronate if taken at the same time.</li><li>Intravenous ranitidine increases the bioavailability of oral Alendronate.</li><li>The incidence of upper gastrointestinal adverse events associated with NSAID and aspirin appears to be greater with the concomitant administration of Alendronate.</li><li>Mineral oils, orlistat, and bile acid sequestrants (e.g., cholestyramine, colestipol) may impair the absorption of vitamin D.</li><li>Anticonvulsants, cimetidine, and thiazides may increase the catabolism of vitamin D.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Abnormalities of the esophagus which delay esophageal emptying, such as stricture or achalasia.</li><li>Inability to stand or sit upright for at least 30 minutes.</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of this product.</li><li>Hypocalcaemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ostel-D 70 mg+2800 IU</strong></p><p>Usually mild and generally do not require discontinuation of therapy. Side effects include:</p><ul><li>Esophageal reactions, abdominal pain and distension, diarrhea or constipation, flatulence.</li><li>Musculoskeletal pain, headache, rash, erythema, and transient decreases in serum calcium and phosphate.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy category C. Overdoses of vitamin D have shown teratogenic effects in pregnant animals. Alendronic Acid &amp; Vitamin D3 (Colecalciferol) should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the mother and fetus.</li><li>Cholecalciferol and some of its active metabolites pass into breast milk. It is not known whether alendronate is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to lactating women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Hypocalcaemia and other disturbances of mineral metabolism should be corrected before initiation of therapy.</li><li>Alendronate can cause local irritation of the upper gastrointestinal mucosa.</li><li>Caution should be used when Alendronate is given to patients with active upper gastrointestinal problems such as dysphagia, esophageal disease, gastritis, duodenitis, or ulcers.</li><li>Patients should stop taking the medicine and consult their physician if they develop esophageal diseases.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ostel-D 70 mg+2800 IU</strong></p><ul><li>Hypocalcemia, hypophosphatemia, and upper gastrointestinal adverse events, such as upset stomach, heartburn, esophagitis, gastritis, or ulcer, may result from oral overdosage.</li><li>Signs and symptoms of vitamin D toxicity include hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, anorexia, nausea, vomiting, polyuria, polydipsia, weakness, and lethargy.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place. Protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dosing in Elderly and Renal Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary for the elderly or for patients with mild-to-moderate renal insufficiency (creatinine clearance 35 to 60 mL/min). Ostel-D 70 mg+2800 IU (Colecalciferol) is not recommended for patients with more severe renal insufficiency (creatinine clearance &lt;35 mL/min) due to lack of experience.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Combined preparations: Inhibiting bone resorption.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Alendronate Sodium is a bisphosphonate that acts as a specific inhibitor of osteoclast-mediated bone resorption. Alendronate is effective when administered at least 30 minutes before breakfast. It transiently distributes to soft tissues following administration but is then rapidly distributed to bone or excreted in the urine. Protein binding in human plasma is approximately 78%. There is no evidence that Alendronate is metabolized in animals or humans. At the cellular level, Alendronate shows preferential localization to sites of bone resorption, especially under osteoclasts. It inhibits osteoclast activity. In addition, bone formation exceeds bone resorption, leading to progressive gains in bone mass. Vitamin D is required for normal bone formation. Vitamin D insufficiency develops when both sunlight exposure and dietary intake are inadequate. Insufficiency is associated with negative calcium balance, increased parathyroid hormone levels, bone loss, and increased risk of skeletal fracture.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy category C. Overdoses of vitamin D have shown teratogenic effects in pregnant animals. Alendronic Acid &amp; Vitamin D3 (Colecalciferol) should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the mother and fetus.</li><li>Cholecalciferol and some of its active metabolites pass into breast milk. It is not known whether alendronate is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to lactating women.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dosing in Elderly and Renal Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary for the elderly or for patients with mild-to-moderate renal insufficiency (creatinine clearance 35 to 60 mL/min). Ostel-D 70 mg+2800 IU (Colecalciferol) is not recommended for patients with more severe renal insufficiency (creatinine clearance &lt;35 mL/min) due to lack of experience.</li></ul>', 'active', '1523_1742754638.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:30:38', '2025-05-04 01:31:34'),
(4334, 'Voltalin SR', 'Diclofenac Sodium', 18.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Voltalin SR 100 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Rheumatology:</strong></p><ul><li>Inflammatory and degenerative forms of rheumatism</li><li>Chronic involutive polyarthritis</li><li>Ankylosing spondylarthritis</li><li>Osteoarthritis</li><li>Spondylarthroses</li><li>Acute gout</li><li>Peri-articular rheumatic disorders</li></ul><p><strong>Surgery and Traumatology:</strong></p><ul><li>Sprain, bruises, dislocations</li><li>Fractures</li><li>Soft tissue injuries</li><li>Surgical interventions</li></ul><p><strong>Obstetrics and Gynecology:</strong></p><ul><li>Primary dysmenorrhoea</li><li>Episiotomy</li><li>Adnexitis</li><li>Endometritis</li><li>Parametritis</li><li>Salpingitis</li><li>Mastitis</li></ul><p><strong>Otorhinolaryngology:</strong></p><ul><li>As pre-operative medication for pain, inflammation, and swelling prevention</li></ul><p><strong>Dentistry:</strong></p><ul><li>Post-operative and post-traumatic pain, inflammation, and swelling</li></ul><p><strong>Other indications:</strong></p><ul><li>Prevention of pain and treatment of inflammation and swelling in urogenital tract surgeries</li><li>Renal and biliary colic</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Voltalin SR 100 mg contains Diclofenac, a potent NSAID with anti-rheumatic, anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic properties. It inhibits prostaglandin biosynthesis, which plays a major role in causing inflammation, pain, and fever. Diclofenac has some uricosuric effects and is rapidly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract when taken with or after meals. Peak plasma concentrations are reached within 2 hours of ingestion, and it is 99.7% bound to plasma proteins. Diclofenac is metabolized in the liver.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><p><strong>Adults:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Enteric-coated tablet:</strong> 75-150 mg daily in divided doses</li><li><strong>Sustained-release tablet:</strong> 1 tablet daily, preferably with meals</li><li><strong>Suppositories:</strong> 75-150 mg daily in divided doses</li><li><strong>Gel:</strong> 2-4 g applied 3-4 times daily, depending on the painful site</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> 1 ampoule daily (may increase to 2 for severe cases)</li></ul><p><strong>Children:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Enteric-coated tablet:</strong> 1-3 mg/kg per day in divided doses</li><li><strong>Sustained-release tablets:</strong> Not recommended</li><li><strong>Suppositories:</strong> 1-3 mg/kg body weight in divided doses</li><li><strong>Injection (juvenile chronic arthritis):</strong> 1-3 mg/kg body weight daily</li></ul><p><strong>Elderly:</strong></p><ul><li>Lowest effective dose is recommended, considering age and physical status.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Voltalin SR 100 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>May inhibit diuretics’ activity</li><li>Concomitant use with potassium-sparing diuretics may increase serum potassium</li><li>Caution when taken with digoxin, methotrexate, cyclosporine, or lithium</li><li>Increased risk of side effects when used with other NSAIDs or glucocorticoids (e.g., gastrointestinal bleeding)</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient</li><li>Peptic ulcer</li><li>Asthma, urticaria, or rhinitis caused by acetylsalicylic acid or other NSAIDs</li><li>Avoid under occlusive airtight dressings for the gel</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><p>Voltalin SR 100 mg is generally well tolerated, but mild and transient side effects may include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric pain, nausea, diarrhea</li><li>Dizziness, headache</li><li>Peripheral edema</li><li>Skin reactions (rash, eczema)</li><li>Local irritation from the gel, skin reddening</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid in pregnancy unless benefits outweigh risks</li><li>Not recommended during the first trimester of pregnancy</li><li>A small amount of Diclofenac may appear in breast milk, but no significant effect on the infant is expected</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor for peptic ulceration or gastrointestinal bleeding</li><li>Use caution in elderly patients</li><li>Avoid contact with eyes or mucous membranes after using the gel</li><li>Wash hands after application, and do not ingest the gel</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light</li><li>Keep below 30°C</li><li>Keep out of reach of children</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Diclofenac Sodium is an NSAID that inhibits prostaglandin biosynthesis, reducing inflammation, pain, and fever. It is well absorbed in the gastrointestinal tract and is extensively bound to plasma proteins. It is metabolized in the liver with first-pass metabolism.</p>', 'active', '1844_1742754852.webp', 40, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:34:12', '2025-05-05 17:53:02'),
(4335, 'Rasonix', 'Rabeprazole Sodium', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rasonix 20 mg</strong></p><p>Gastro-resistant tablets of rabeprazole are recommended for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Active ulcer in the duodenum</li><li>Benign stomach ulcer that is active</li><li>Symptomatic gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) that is erosive or ulcerative</li><li>GERD long-term maintenance</li><li>Moderate to extremely severe GERD symptoms (symptomatic GERD)</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Helicobacter pylori eradication in patients with peptic ulcer disease, in conjunction with suitable antibacterial therapy regimens</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rabeprazole inhibits the gastric H+/K+-ATPase at the secretory surface of the stomach parietal cell, reducing gastric acid secretion. It is considered an inhibitor of the gastric proton pump, which is believed to be the acid (proton) pump in the parietal cell.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rasonix 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer:</strong> 20 mg in the morning for 4 weeks. Most patients heal within 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Erosive or ulcerative GERD:</strong> 20 mg daily in the morning for 4 to 8 weeks. If healing does not occur after 8 weeks, an additional 8-week course may be considered.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily in the morning for 4 weeks. If symptoms persist, an additional course may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of erosive or ulcerative GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily. Studies do not extend beyond 12 months.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> Starting dose of 60 mg once a day, adjusted based on patient needs. Doses up to 100 mg once daily and 60 mg twice daily have been used.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication:</strong> 20 mg of Rabeprazole, 1000 mg of Amoxicillin, and 500 mg of Clarithromycin twice daily for 7 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Rasonix 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Active Duodenal Ulcer and Active Benign Gastric Ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily in the morning. Healing is typically achieved within 4 weeks for duodenal ulcers and 6 weeks for gastric ulcers.</li><li><strong>Erosive or Ulcerative GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>GERD Long-Term Management:</strong> Maintenance dose of 20 mg or 10 mg once daily, depending on the patient response.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 10 mg once daily. If symptoms do not resolve in 4 weeks, further investigation is needed.</li><li><strong>Pediatric GERD (1-11 years, &lt;15 kg):</strong> 5 mg once daily for 12 weeks, with the option to increase to 10 mg if response is inadequate.</li><li><strong>Pediatric GERD (1-11 years, ≥15 kg):</strong> 10 mg once daily for 12 weeks.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> Starting dose of 60 mg once daily, adjustable up to 120 mg/day based on patient needs.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Rasonix 20 mg</strong></p><p>Take Rabeprazole in the morning before eating for ease of adherence. Tablets should be swallowed whole and not chewed or crushed.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Rasonix 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Itraconazole and Ketoconazole:</strong> Co-administration may decrease antifungal plasma levels. Monitoring and dose adjustments may be required.</li><li><strong>Atazanavir:</strong> Proton pump inhibitors like Rabeprazole should not be taken alongside atazanavir, as it is pH-dependent.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any excipient or active ingredient</li><li>Not recommended during pregnancy and breastfeeding</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Rasonix 20 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include headache, nausea, vomiting, constipation, dry mouth, appetite changes, muscle pain, sleepiness, and dizziness.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>FDA Pregnancy Category C:</strong> There is no proven harm to the fetus in animal studies, but there is limited controlled data in pregnant women.</li><li>Rabeprazole may be excreted in human milk, so breastfeeding should be carefully considered in relation to the benefits of the medication for the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Rule out malignancy before starting treatment, as symptomatic improvement may occur in cases of gastric or esophageal cancer.</li><li>Long-term use (especially &gt;1 year) may slightly increase the risk of fractures, particularly in elderly patients. Regular monitoring is recommended, and calcium and vitamin D intake should be adequate.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rasonix 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No specific antidote for overdose. Symptoms are typically mild and reversible.</li><li>Treatment is symptomatic, and supportive measures should be taken.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in pediatric populations are not established.</li><li>No dosage adjustment is needed for hepatic or renal impairment.</li></ul>', 'active', '367_1742754954.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:35:54', '2025-05-04 15:05:31');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4336, 'Exor', 'Esomeprazole', 8.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Exor 20 mg</strong></p><p>Exor 20 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Alleviating GERD-related symptoms such as persistent heartburn.</li><li>Treating erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Maintaining the healing of erosive esophagitis.</li><li>Eradicating Helicobacter pylori infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li>Managing Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</li><li>Treating duodenal and gastric ulcers caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Exor 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks. If healing does not occur after 4-8 weeks, an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 20 mg once daily (clinical studies did not extend beyond 6 months).</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve after 4 weeks, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori Eradication</strong>: Triple Therapy to reduce the risk of duodenal ulcer recurrence: Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days, amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>: 20-80 mg once daily. The dose should be adjusted individually and continued as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks based on response.</li><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: 40 mg Exor 20 mg once daily via intravenous injection (not less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10-30 minutes).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Exor 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Take one Exor 20 mg pill or capsule one hour before a meal. Swallow it whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>: Dissolve the contents of a packet in 15 ml of water, stir, and allow the mixture to thicken for 2-3 minutes. Stir again and consume within 30 minutes.</li><li>For administration through a nasogastric or gastric tube, mix the suspension as described and administer through the tube.</li><li><strong>Intravenous Injection</strong>: Esomeprazole should be reconstituted with 5 ml of water for injection (WFI) and administered over at least 3 minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Exor 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Esomeprazole is metabolized by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4.</li><li>It does not significantly interact with phenytoin, warfarin, quinidine, clarithromycin, or amoxicillin.</li><li>Esomeprazole may reduce the clearance of diazepam by 45%, increasing its plasma levels.</li><li>Co-administration with medications like ketoconazole, iron salts, or digoxin may be affected due to changes in stomach pH.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of esomeprazole.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Exor 20 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Abdominal pain</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Dry mouth</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Positive response to Exor 20 mg does not rule out stomach cancer.</li><li>Take capsules at least one hour before meals.</li><li>For patients who have difficulty swallowing capsules, the pellets can be mixed with cold applesauce (without chewing) and consumed immediately.</li><li>Antacids can be used alongside Exor 20 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Exor 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Overdose in rats has shown significant toxicity, but no known human overdose incidents.</li><li>Symptoms may include decreased motor activity, breathing changes, tremors, ataxia, and convulsions.</li><li>Symptomatic and supportive care should be provided in case of overdose.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place at a temperature not exceeding 30°C. Protect from moisture and light. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Esomeprazole inhibits the H+/K+-ATPase proton pump in gastric parietal cells, reducing stomach acid secretion. It provides superior acid control compared to racemic proton pump inhibitors.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>No sufficient research on its effects during pregnancy. No teratogenic effects identified in animal studies. It is not known if esomeprazole is excreted in breast milk. Breastfeeding should be discontinued if esomeprazole is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Safety and efficacy have not been established in pediatric patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>No significant differences in safety and efficacy between elderly and younger patients, although older patients may be more sensitive.</li></ul><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage change needed for mild to moderate hepatic impairment. For severe hepatic impairment, limit the dose to 20 mg once daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong></p><ul><li>No pharmacokinetic differences expected in patients with renal impairment, as less than 1% of the drug is eliminated unaltered in the urine.</li></ul>', 'active', '1773_1742755045.jpg', 43, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:37:25', '2025-05-02 17:12:17'),
(4337, 'Ace', 'Paracetamol', 30.00, 1, 'x 30 ml bottle', '30 ml bottle', 'Pediatric Drops', '80 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Ace 30 ml</h3><p>Ace 30 ml is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li><strong>Fever</strong>, common cold, and influenza</li><li><strong>Headache</strong>, toothache, earache, bodyache, myalgia, neuralgia, dysmenorrhoea, and colic pain</li><li><strong>Sprains</strong>, back pain, post-operative pain, postpartum pain, and inflammatory pain</li><li><strong>Post-vaccination pain</strong> in children</li><li><strong>Rheumatic &amp; osteoarthritic pain</strong> and stiffness of joints</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Non-opioid analgesics</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Ace 30 ml (Acetaminophen) has <strong>analgesic</strong> and <strong>antipyretic</strong> properties with weak <strong>anti-inflammatory</strong> activity. It is thought to act primarily in the CNS, increasing the pain threshold by inhibiting both isoforms of <strong>cyclooxygenase (COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3)</strong> enzymes involved in <strong>prostaglandin (PG)</strong> synthesis.</p><p>Ace 30 ml is a <strong>para-aminophenol derivative</strong> that is one of the most widely used, safest, and fast-acting analgesics. It is well tolerated and free from various side effects associated with aspirin.</p><p><br></p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ace 30 ml</h3><p><strong>Tablet:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 1-2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours, up to a maximum of 4 gm (8 tablets) daily.</li><li><strong>Children (6-12 years):</strong> ½ to 1 tablet 3 to 4 times daily. For long-term treatment, it is wise not to exceed 2.6 gm/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Extended Release Tablet:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years:</strong> Two tablets, swallowed whole, every 6 to 8 hours (maximum of 6 tablets in any 24 hours). The tablet must not be crushed.</li></ul><p><strong>Syrup/Suspension:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children under 3 months:</strong> 10 mg/kg body weight (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced) 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>3 months to below 1 year:</strong> ½ to 1 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>1-5 years:</strong> 1-2 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>6-12 years:</strong> 2-A teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 4-8 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Suppository:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children 3-12 months:</strong> 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children 1-5 years:</strong> 125-250 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6-12 years:</strong> 250-500 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults &amp; children over 12 years:</strong> 0.5-1 gm, 4 times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Paediatric Drop:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children Up to 3 months:</strong> 0.5 ml (40 mg)</li><li><strong>4 to 11 months:</strong> 1.0 ml (80 mg)</li><li><strong>1 to 2 years:</strong> 1.5 ml (120 mg). Do not exceed more than 5 doses daily for a maximum of 5 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Ace 30 ml Tablet with Actizorb Technology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children (12 years and over):</strong> 1 to 2 Tablets every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not take more than 8 caplets in 24 hours.</li><li><strong>Children (7 to 11 years):</strong> ½-1 Tablet every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not take more than 4 caplets in 24 hours. Not recommended for children under 7 years.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Ace 30 ml</h3><ul><li><strong>Barbiturates</strong>, <strong>tricyclic antidepressants</strong>, and <strong>alcohol</strong> may impair the ability to metabolize large doses of Ace 30 ml.</li><li><strong>Alcohol</strong> increases the hepatotoxicity of Ace 30 ml overdose.</li><li><strong>Chronic ingestion</strong> of anticonvulsants or oral steroid contraceptives can induce liver enzymes, which may prevent Ace 30 ml from reaching therapeutic levels.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Ace 30 ml is contraindicated in individuals who have a known hypersensitivity to Ace 30 ml.</p><p><br></p><h3>Side Effects of Ace 30 ml</h3><p>Side effects are generally mild, but can include:</p><ul><li><strong>Hematological reactions</strong> such as thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, pancytopenia, neutropenia, and agranulocytosis</li><li><strong>Pancreatitis</strong>, skin rashes, and other allergic reactions</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong> (according to the USFDA): Ace 30 ml should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Ace 30 ml is excreted in breast milk. Maternal ingestion in normal therapeutic doses does not appear to pose a risk to the nursing infant.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Ace 30 ml should be used with caution in patients with impaired <strong>kidney</strong> or <strong>liver</strong> function.</li><li>It should be used carefully in patients taking other drugs that affect the liver.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Ace 30 ml</h3><p>Symptoms of an overdose in the first 24 hours include:</p><ul><li><strong>Pallor</strong>, <strong>nausea</strong>, <strong>vomiting</strong>, <strong>anorexia</strong>, and <strong>abdominal pain</strong>.</li><li>Liver damage may become apparent 12-48 hours after ingestion.</li><li><strong>Abnormalities of glucose metabolism</strong> and <strong>metabolic acidosis</strong> may occur.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>dry place</strong> away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li><strong>Non-opioid analgesics</strong></li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Ace 30 ml (Acetaminophen) has analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity. It acts by inhibiting <strong>COX-1, COX-2, and COX-3 enzymes</strong>, which are involved in the synthesis of prostaglandins, and increases the pain threshold in the <strong>central nervous system (CNS)</strong>. It is a safe, fast-acting analgesic with minimal side effects compared to aspirin.</p>', 'active', '337_1742755140.jpg', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:39:00', '2025-05-02 22:42:56'),
(4338, 'Momento', 'Desloratadine', 2.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Momento 5 mg</strong></p><p>Momento 5 mg is indicated for the relief of symptoms associated with seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis, such as sneezing, nasal discharge &amp; itching, congestion or stuffiness, as well as ocular itching, tearing, and redness, itching of the palate, and coughing. It is also indicated for the relief of symptoms associated with chronic idiopathic urticaria, such as the relief of itching and the size &amp; number of hives.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Momento 5 mg is a long-acting, tricyclic, non-sedating, selective peripheral histamine H1-receptor antagonist. It inhibits the release of pro-inflammatory mediators from human mast cells and basophils.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Momento 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Drops</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child 6 -11 months: 2 ml drops once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child 1 -2 years: 2.5 ml drops once daily</li><li><strong>Syrup</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child 6-11 months: 2 ml once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child 1-5 years: 2.5 ml once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child 6-11 years: 5 ml once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults &amp; &gt; 12 years: 10 ml once daily</li><li><strong>Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults and children 12 years and over: 1 tablet daily</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Momento 5 mg</strong></p><p>No clinically relevant drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Momento 5 mg is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to this medication or any of its ingredients, or to Loratadine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Momento 5 mg</strong></p><p>Less common side effects may include headache, nausea, fatigue, dizziness, pharyngitis, dyspepsia, and myalgia.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, Momento 5 mg should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Momento 5 mg passes into breast milk. Therefore, a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or to discontinue Momento 5 mg, considering the importance of the drug to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In adult patients with liver or renal impairment: A starting dose of one 5 mg tablet every other day is recommended based on pharmacokinetic data. If the patient has a medical or familial history of seizures, use with caution.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Momento 5 mg</strong></p><p>No clinically relevant adverse effects have been reported.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light. Store below 30°C. Keep all medicines out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: The safety and effectiveness of Momento 5 mg in pediatric patients under 2 years of age have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: In general, dose selection for elderly patients should be cautious, reflecting the greater frequency of decreased hepatic, renal, or cardiac function, and of concomitant disease or other drug therapy.</li><li><strong>Liver or Renal Impairment</strong>: In adult patients with liver or renal impairment, a starting dose of one 5 mg tablet every other day is recommended based on pharmacokinetic data.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Momento 5 mg, the major active metabolite of Loratadine, is a non-sedating, long-acting tricyclic histamine antagonist with selective H1-receptor histamine antagonist activity. It also inhibits histamine release from human mast cells.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Pregnancy Category C. The safe use of Momento 5 mg during pregnancy has not been established. Therefore, Momento 5 mg is not to be used during pregnancy unless clearly indicated. Momento 5 mg passes into breast milk, so a decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or to discontinue Momento 5 mg, considering the importance of the drug to the mother.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Momento 5 mg is recommended for use in children and adolescents.</p>', 'active', '1827_1742755251.png', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:40:51', '2025-05-04 00:06:45'),
(4339, 'Neuromate', 'Vitamin B1, B6 & B12', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg+200 mg+200 mcg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Neuromate</strong></p><p>Neuromate is indicated for the treatment of vitamin B1, B6 &amp; B12 deficiency syndrome. It is also indicated for the supportive treatment of neuritis and non-inflammatory diseases of the nerves, including:</p><ul><li>Diabetic neuropathy</li><li>Peripheral neuralgia</li><li>Lumbago</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Optic neuritis</li><li>Sciatica</li><li>Facial neuralgia</li><li>Intercostal neuralgia</li><li>Spinal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate) 100 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride) 200 mg</li><li>Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) 200 micrograms</li></ul><p>Each 3 ml ampoule contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) 100 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) 100 mg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) 1000 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Neuromate is a combined preparation of Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate), Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride), and Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin). These vitamins play an essential role as co-enzymes in the metabolism of the nervous system and support the regeneration of nerve fibers and the myelin sheath by activating the nerve cell metabolism and natural repair mechanisms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Neuromate</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablets</strong>: 1 to 3 tablets per day or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly (deep intragluteal).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In severe cases: 1 ampoule daily until the acute symptoms subside.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For milder cases and follow-up therapy: 2 to 3 ampoules per week.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in children</strong>: There is no information on the use of this drug in children.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Neuromate</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported yet.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin B1, Vitamin B6, and Vitamin B12 are contraindicated in patients on levodopa therapy.</li><li>Also contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients of the preparation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Neuromate</strong></p><p>Generally well-tolerated, but allergic reactions may be observed in a few cases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>No risks have been identified associated with the use of this combination during pregnancy at the recommended dosage.</li><li>Vitamin B1, B6, and B12 are secreted into breast milk, but the risks of overdose for the infant are not known.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Cyanocobalamin should not be given to patients with subacute degeneration of the spinal cord.</li><li>Cyanocobalamin is not suitable for the treatment of optic neuropathies associated with raised plasma concentrations of cyanocobalamin.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Neuromate</strong></p><p>No overdosage symptoms are expected at the recommended dosage. In case of overdose, treatment is symptomatic and supportive.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Store in a cool place (below 25°C) and dry, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin B1</strong>: Converts carbohydrates, fatty acids, and amino acids into energy, promotes healthy nerves, improves mood, and strengthens the heart.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6</strong>: Forms RBCs, helps cells make proteins, manufactures neurotransmitters (e.g., serotonin), and releases stored forms of energy. It helps to prevent cardiovascular diseases and stroke, eases insomnia, and lifts depression.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12</strong>: Essential for cell replication and RBC production, prevents anemia, helps prevent depression, reduces nerve pain, numbness, and tingling, and lowers the risk of heart diseases.</li></ul><p>The vitamin ingredients are well absorbed after oral administration, widely distributed in most tissues, and appear in breast milk. Thiamine is primarily present as diphosphate within the cell and is excreted in the urine in excess amounts. Pyridoxine, pyridoxal, and pyridoxamine are readily absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract and converted to their active forms. They are stored mainly in the liver, where they are oxidized to inactive metabolites and excreted in the urine.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Oral tablet form is recommended. However, due to the presence of benzyl alcohol, the injection is not recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</p>', 'active', '917_1742755338.webp', 68, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:42:18', '2025-05-04 00:38:43'),
(4340, 'Losectil', 'Omeprazole', 3.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you provided, formatted with normal bold text where appropriate:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Losectil 10 mg</strong></p><p>Losectil 10 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcer</li><li>Gastric and duodenal ulcers brought on by NSAIDs</li><li>In individuals who have a history of duodenal and stomach ulcers brought on by NSAIDs, as a preventative treatment</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</li><li>Long-term care of acid reflux disease</li><li>Dyspepsia caused by acid</li><li>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers</li><li>Prevention of aspiration of acid while under general anesthesia</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Peptic ulcer caused by <em>Helicobacter pylori</em></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Blocker</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Losectil 10 mg is an inhibitor of stomach acid secretion and a benzimidazole derivative. By inhibiting the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in the gastric parietal cell, it prevents the release of gastric acid. After oral administration, the antisecretory effect begins in about an hour, peaks at two hours, and lasts up to 72 hours. Secretory activity gradually resumes over 3 to 5 days once the medicine is stopped.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Losectil 10 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Oral</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Benign gastric and duodenal ulcer</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg once daily for 4 weeks in duodenal ulceration, 8 weeks in gastric ulceration; in severe or recurrent cases, increase to 40 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose for recurrent duodenal ulcer: 20 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For prevention of relapse in duodenal ulcer: 10-20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>NSAID-associated duodenal or gastric ulcer</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continue for another 4 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg once daily is used as prophylaxis in patients with a history of NSAID-associated duodenal or gastric ulcers.</li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continue for 4-8 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">40 mg once daily may be given for 8 weeks in refractory cases.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose: 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Long-term management of acid reflux disease</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10-20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Acid-related dyspepsia</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">10-20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">40 mg the evening before surgery, then 40 mg 2-6 hours before surgery.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initial dose: 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual range: 20-120 mg daily. If the dose exceeds 80 mg, it should be divided into two doses.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication in peptic ulcer disease</strong>:</li><li>20 mg twice daily, along with antimicrobial agents as follows:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Amoxicillin 500 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg, both three times a day for one week, or</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Clarithromycin 250 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg, both twice a day for one week, or</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Amoxicillin 1 g and Clarithromycin 500 mg, both twice a day for one week.</li><li><strong>Pediatric use for severe ulcerating reflux esophagitis</strong> (Child &gt;1 year):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If body weight is 10-20 kg: 10-20 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If body weight is over 20 kg: 20-40 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>IV Injection</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">40 mg administered slowly (over a period of 5 minutes) as an intravenous injection, one hour before surgery.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer, gastric ulcer, or reflux esophagitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In patients where oral medication is inappropriate, 40 mg once daily is recommended.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Initial dose: 60 mg daily intravenously. Higher doses may be required and should be adjusted individually. If the dose exceeds 60 mg daily, it should be divided and given twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Losectil 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Direction for use of IV Injection</strong>:</li><li>Losectil 10 mg lyophilized powder and water for injection should only be administered intravenously; it cannot be administered in any other way. Add 10 ml of water for injection to the vial to create the solution for IV injection. Administer slowly over a period of at least 2 to 5 minutes, at a maximum flow rate of 4 ml/minute. Use only freshly made solutions. After reconstitution, the solution must be used within four hours.</li><li><strong>IV Infusion</strong>:</li><li>The solution should be administered as an intravenous infusion over 20 to 30 minutes. One vial\'s contents should be dissolved in 100 ml of either 5% dextrose or saline for infusion. The solution must be used within 12 hours if dissolved in saline and within 6 hours if dissolved in 5% dextrose. No other medications should be mixed or administered in the same infusion set as the reconstituted solution.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Losectil 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Warfarin, phenytoin, and diazepam may take longer to leave the body when taken with Losectil 10 mg, so a dose reduction of warfarin or phenytoin may be required.</li><li>Theophylline, propranolol, and antacids have not been shown to interact with Losectil 10 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient of the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Losectil 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Most people tolerate Losectil 10 mg well. Rare, mild, and temporary side effects may include nausea, stomach cramping, paresthesia, dizziness, and headache, but these rarely require dosage adjustments.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Losectil 10 mg falls under the US FDA pregnancy category C. While studies show no negative impact on pregnancy or the fetus, its entry into breast milk and effects on the baby are unknown. If Losectil 10 mg use is necessary, breastfeeding should be discontinued.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Before starting therapy, rule out the possibility of gastric cancer as the medication may worsen symptoms and delay diagnosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep the medication dry, away from heat and light, and out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Blocker</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Losectil 10 mg inhibits the production of stomach acid by blocking the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in the gastric parietal cells.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you\'d like any other modifications or further details!</p>', 'active', '397_1742755435.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:43:55', '2025-05-03 23:32:50'),
(4341, '3Bion', 'Vitamin B1 + B6 + B12', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg+200 mg+200 mcg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of 3Bion 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg</strong></p><p>3Bion 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg is indicated for the treatment of Vitamin B1, B6 &amp; B12 deficiency syndrome. It is also indicated for the supportive treatment of neuritis &amp; non-inflammatory diseases of the nerves, e.g., diabetic neuropathy, peripheral neuralgia, lumbago, myalgia, optic neuritis, sciatica, facial neuralgia, intercostal neuralgia, and spinal pain.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate) 100 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride) 200 mg</li><li>Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin) 200 mcg</li></ul><p>Each 3 ml ampoule contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B1) 100 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin B6) 100 mg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) 1000 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>3Bion 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg is a combined preparation of Vitamin B1 (Thiamine Mononitrate), Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine Hydrochloride), and Vitamin B12 (Cyanocobalamin). These vitamins play an essential role as coenzymes in the metabolism of the nervous system. They support the regeneration of nerve fibers and myelin sheath by activating nerve cell metabolism and natural repair mechanisms.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of 3Bion 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablets:</strong> 1 to 3 tablets per day or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly (deep intragluteal).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In severe cases: 1 ampoule daily until the acute symptoms subside.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For milder cases and follow-up therapy: 2 to 3 ampoules per week.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of 3Bion 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong> 1-3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection:</strong>In severe (acute) cases: 1 injection daily until the acute symptoms subside or as advised by the physician.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In mild cases: 1 injection 2-3 times per week.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Ampoules are preferably injected intramuscularly.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in children:</strong> There is no information on the use of this drug in children.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Interaction of 3Bion 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported yet.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vitamin B1, Vitamin B6, and Vitamin B12 are contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Patients on levodopa therapy</li><li>Patients with hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients in the preparation</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of 3Bion 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg</strong></p><p>Generally well tolerated, but allergic reactions may be observed in a few cases.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>No risks have been reported associated with the use of this combination during pregnancy at the recommended dosage.</p><p>Vitamin B1, B6, and B12 are secreted into breast milk, but the risks of overdose for the infant are not known.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Cyanocobalamin should not be given in patients with subacute degeneration of the spinal cord.</li><li>Cyanocobalamin is not suitable for the treatment of optic neuropathies associated with raised plasma concentrations of cyanocobalamin.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of 3Bion 100 mg + 200 mg + 200 mcg</strong></p><p>No overdosage symptoms are expected with the recommended dosage. If an overdose occurs, treatment is symptomatic and supportive.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Store in a cool (below 25°C) and dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin B1</strong>: Converts carbohydrates, fatty acids, and amino acids into energy, promotes healthy nerves, improves mood, and strengthens the heart.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B6</strong>: Forms red blood cells, helps cells to make proteins, manufactures neurotransmitters (e.g., serotonin), releases stored forms of energy, helps to prevent cardiovascular diseases and stroke, lifts depression, and eases insomnia.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B12</strong>: Essential for cell replication, important for red blood cell production, prevents anemia, reduces nerve pain, numbness, tingling, and lowers the risk of heart diseases.</li></ul><p>The vitamin ingredients are absorbed well upon oral administration, widely distributed to most tissues, and appear in breast milk.</p><ul><li>Thiamine is mostly present as diphosphate and is not stored significantly in the body. Excess amounts are excreted in the urine.</li><li>Pyridoxine, pyridoxal, and pyridoxamine are readily absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract, converted to active forms, and stored mainly in the liver. Excess amounts are excreted in urine.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Oral tablet form is recommended, but due to the presence of benzyl alcohol, injection is not recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</p>', 'active', '1735_1742756092.jpg', 63, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:54:52', '2025-05-01 22:12:34'),
(4342, 'Neso', 'Naproxen Sodium + Esomeprazole Magnesium', 13.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '375 mg+20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here’s the provided information in a cleaner, bolded format:</p><p><strong>Indications of Neso 375 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p>It is indicated for the relief of signs and symptoms of osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis, as well as to decrease the risk of developing gastric ulcers in patients at risk of developing NSAID-associated gastric ulcers.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>It is a preparation of Naproxen and Esomeprazole.</p><ul><li><strong>Naproxen</strong>: An NSAID with analgesic and antipyretic activities. It inhibits prostaglandin synthesis.</li><li><strong>Esomeprazole</strong>: A proton pump inhibitor that suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the H+/K+ ATPase in the gastric parietal cell, blocking the final step in acid production and thus reducing gastric acidity.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Neso 375 mg+20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>The recommended dose is one tablet twice daily.</li><li>Do not split, chew, crush, or dissolve the tablet.</li><li>Take the tablet at least 30 minutes before meals.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Neso 375 mg+20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ACE Inhibitors, diuretics, and beta-blockers</strong>: May reduce the antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin</strong>: Increases the risk of nephrotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Lithium</strong>: Increases plasma level of lithium.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: May increase the serum levels of methotrexate.</li><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>: May increase the risk of bleeding complications.</li><li><strong>Digoxin</strong>: May increase digoxin toxicity.</li><li><strong>Clopidogrel</strong>: May decrease the exposure to the active metabolite of clopidogrel.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any components of this product.</li><li>Contraindicated in patients who have experienced asthma, urticaria, or allergic-type reactions after taking aspirin or other NSAIDs.</li><li>Contraindicated for the treatment of per-operative pain in the setting of coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Neso 375 mg+20 mg</strong></p><p>The most common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Gastritis</li><li>Erosive gastritis</li><li>Dyspepsia</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Gastric ulcer</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Nausea</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of this tablet should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Fatal cardiovascular thrombotic events (e.g., myocardial infarction, stroke, hypertension, congestive heart failure, and edema).</li><li>Serious gastrointestinal adverse events.</li><li>Hypomagnesaemia.</li><li>Renal or hepatic insufficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul>', 'active', '1164_1742756261.jpg', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:57:41', '2025-05-04 00:36:30'),
(4343, 'Dipa', 'Rupatadine Fumarate', 11.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is the information about <strong>Dipa 10 mg</strong> in a more structured format with headings in bold:</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Indications of Dipa 10 mg</strong></h3><p>Dipa 10 mg is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of:</p><ul><li>Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li><li>Urticaria</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><p>Dipa 10 mg is a second-generation, non-sedating, long-acting histamine antagonist with selective peripheral H1 receptor antagonist activity. It further blocks the receptors of the platelet-activating factor (PAF) according to in vitro and in vivo studies. Rupatadine possesses anti-allergic properties such as:</p><ul><li>Inhibition of the degranulation of mast cells induced by immunological and non-immunological stimuli</li><li>Inhibition of the release of cytokines, particularly tumor necrosis factor (TNF) in human mast cells and monocytes</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Dipa 10 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Rupatadine Tablet:</strong> The recommended dosage is <strong>10 mg once daily</strong>, with or without food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Rupatadine Oral Solution (Children aged 2 to 11 years):</strong>Children weighing <strong>25 kg or more</strong>: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) of oral solution once daily with or without food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing <strong>equal or more than 10 kg to less than 25 kg</strong>: 1/2 teaspoonful (2.5 ml) oral solution once daily with or without food.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction of Dipa 10 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>With medicine:</strong> The concomitant administration of Rupatadine 20 mg and ketoconazole or erythromycin increases the systemic exposure. Rupatadine should be used with caution when administered with inhibitors of the isozyme CYP3A4, statins, CNS depressants, or alcohol.</li><li><strong>With food:</strong> Grapefruit and grapefruit juice should not be taken simultaneously with Rupatadine.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Rupatadine or to any of the excipients.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Dipa 10 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Asthenia, dizziness, drowsiness.</li><li><strong>Uncommon:</strong> Appetite increased, arthralgia, back pain, concentration impaired, constipation, cough, diarrhea, dry throat, epistaxis, fever, gastrointestinal discomfort, increased risk of infection, irritability, malaise, myalgia, nasal dryness, nausea, oropharyngeal pain, rash, thirst, vomiting, weight increased.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Palpitations, tachycardia.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy category B2</strong> (by TGA, Australia). There is no clinical data available on Rupatadine\'s exposure during pregnancy. Pregnant women should not use Rupatadine unless the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk for the fetus.</li><li>There is no information on whether Rupatadine is excreted in the mother\'s milk. Therefore, it should not be used during lactation unless the potential benefits for the mother justify the potential risk to the infant.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Dipa 10 mg should be used with caution in:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients with known prolongation of the QT interval</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients with uncorrected hypokalemia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients with ongoing proarrhythmic conditions (e.g., bradycardia, acute myocardial ischemia)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Elderly patients (65 years and older)</li><li>There is no clinical experience in patients with impaired kidney or liver function; the use of Rupatadine 10 mg tablets is not recommended in these patients.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Dipa 10 mg</strong></h3><ul><li>The most common adverse reaction is <strong>somnolence</strong>.</li><li>In case of accidental ingestion of very high doses, symptomatic treatment and supportive measures should be given.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly patients. No information is available regarding dose adjustments for this population.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> The safety and efficacy of Rupatadine have not been established in patients less than 12 years of age.</li><li><strong>Patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency:</strong> Use is not recommended in patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency, as no relevant clinical data is available.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><p>Dipa 10 mg is a long-acting, non-sedative antagonist of histamine H1 receptors. It also antagonizes the platelet-activating factor (PAF). Both histamine and PAF contribute to broncho-constriction, increased vascular permeability, and inflammatory processes. Rupatadine’s dual action provides a better therapeutic effect than an isolated antihistamine. It also inhibits mast cell degranulation and cytokine release, particularly TNF-α in human mast cells and monocytes.</p><h3><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h3><p>There is no clinical data available on Rupatadine\'s exposure during pregnancy. Pregnant women should avoid using Rupatadine unless the potential benefits outweigh the potential risks to the fetus. No information is available regarding whether Rupatadine is excreted in breast milk, so it should not be used during lactation unless necessary.</p><h3><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Use with caution in the elderly; no dose adjustment is required.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> Safety and efficacy have not been established for patients under 12 years of age.</li><li><strong>Patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency:</strong> The use of Rupatadine is not recommended in patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency due to lack of clinical data.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need more information!</p>', 'active', '1949_1742756360.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-23 23:59:20', '2025-05-03 08:25:02'),
(4344, 'Arth-A Max', 'Glucosamine Sulfate + Diacerein', 13.25, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '750 mg+50 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Arth-A Max 750 mg+50 mg</strong></p><p>This is primarily indicated in conditions like Osteoarthritis, Rheumatoid arthritis, Bone &amp; Joint injuries.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Stimulation of Cartilage formation</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Glucosamine</strong> is a natural substance produced in the human body and found in certain foods. It stimulates the production of glycosaminoglycan &amp; proteoglycan, two essential building blocks of cartilage. As a supplement, Glucosamine acts as a joint lubricant and shock absorber, necessary to maintain healthy cartilage and joint function.</li><li><strong>Diacerein</strong> is an anti-inflammatory analgesic and antipyretic drug, specifically indicated for the treatment of arthritic conditions. It directly inhibits IL-1 synthesis and release in vitro and down modulates IL-1 induced activities, which helps relieve Osteoarthritis symptoms.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Arth-A Max 750 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children above 12 years</strong>: One tablet (Glucosamine Sulphate 750 mg and Diacerein 50 mg) twice daily for 4-12 weeks, taken with food.</li><li><strong>Creatinine Clearance (CrCl) &lt; 30 ml/min</strong>: The dose of Diacerein should be reduced.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Arth-A Max 750 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>No significant interactions of Glucosamine with antibiotics, antidepressants, antihypertensives, nitrates, antiarrhythmics, anxiolytics, hypoglycemic agents, or antisecretives have been reported.</li><li>Diacerein absorption may be decreased with aluminum and/or magnesium hydroxide antacids.</li><li>Increased risk of diarrhea with laxatives and antibiotics.</li><li>Avoid co-administration with fibers and phytic acids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Proven hypersensitivity to Glucosamine is a contraindication.</li><li>Diabetics should monitor blood glucose levels regularly when taking Glucosamine.</li><li>No special studies were conducted in patients with renal and/or hepatic insufficiency, but administration should be under medical supervision for those with severe hepatic or renal issues.</li><li>Children should not be supplemented with Glucosamine or Diacerein.</li><li>Diacerein is contraindicated in pregnancy, lactation, and hypersensitivity to anthraquinone derivatives.</li><li>Caution in patients with allergies to shellfish (e.g., shrimp, crab), diabetes, asthma, alcohol dependence, or liver disease.</li><li>Regular monitoring of blood count, liver function, and urinalysis is recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Arth-A Max 750 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Diarrhea, constipation, epigastric pain, heartburn, nausea, vomiting, headache, skin rashes, drowsiness, intense yellow coloring of urine.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Diacerein is contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation.</li><li>Glucosamine should not be taken by pregnant women or nursing mothers due to lack of scientific data regarding its effects on the fetus and infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution should be taken when administering this drug to those allergic to Glucosamine, Diacerein, or shellfish (e.g., shrimp, crab).</li><li>Monitor patients with diabetes, asthma, alcohol dependence, or liver disease.</li><li>Regular monitoring of complete blood count, liver function, and urinalysis is advised.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Arth-A Max 750 mg+50 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Diarrhea may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, protect from light and moisture.</li></ul>', 'active', '1123_1742756439.jpg', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:00:39', '2025-05-03 00:00:56');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4345, 'Defrol', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 35.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '40000 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Defrol 40000 IU</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated for the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also used as an adjunct in specific therapies for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcaemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Cholecalciferol, a synthetic form of Vitamin D, is essential for normal bone growth and development and maintaining bone density. It is also necessary for the utilization of calcium and phosphorus. Babies need Vitamin D3 for healthy growth and development, as it acts as a hormone.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. Vitamin D3 is essential for normal bone growth and maintenance of bone density. Additionally, it helps reduce the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, reduces the risk of cancer (e.g., breast, colorectal), and maintains adequate insulin levels for type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Defrol 40000 IU:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Capsule:</strong></li><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><em>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</em> 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><em>Maintenance therapy:</em> 1,400-2,000 IU/day. Measurement of 25-hydroxyvitamin D levels should be done 3-4 months after starting maintenance therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><em>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</em> 20,000 IU every 4 weeks. Higher doses may be required in certain situations.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Osteoporosis adjunctive therapy:</em> 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li><strong>Children (12-18 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><em>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</em> 20,000 IU every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</em> 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>For Film-Coated Tablet:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1,000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily, or as directed by a physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li><li><strong>For Oroflash or Chewable Tablets:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1,000 to 2,000 IU daily, or as directed by a physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li><li><strong>For Syrup:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><em>For risk of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</em>0-1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml)</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml)</blockquote><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><em>For Vitamin D3-deficient patients:</em>0-1 year: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 years: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>For Injection:</strong></li><li><em>Prevention:</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Infants receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk: 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) i.e., 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Nursed infants or infants not receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk, or young children up to 5 years: 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e., 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Adolescents: 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e., 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Pregnancy: 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) i.e., 100,000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Elderly: 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) i.e., 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients with digestive disorders, taking antiepileptics, or other specific conditions: 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) i.e., 100,000 IU or 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e., 200,000 IU every 3-6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><em>Vitamin D deficiency:</em> 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e., 200,000 IU, repeated 1 to 6 months later or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Defrol 40000 IU:</strong></p><ul><li>It interferes with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (such as liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Defrol 40000 IU:</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include hypercalcaemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, and abdominal pain.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Studies show the safe use of doses up to 4,000 IU during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU, but higher doses may be needed for Vitamin D3-deficient women. Breastfeeding mothers should be cautious of the vitamin D3 dose they are taking, as it is excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Defrol 40000 IU:</strong></p><ul><li>Can lead to hypervitaminosis D.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30º C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) helps with the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is essential for bone growth, maintenance of bone density, and also plays a role in reducing bacterial infection severity, improving lung function, reducing cancer risks, and maintaining insulin levels for type 2 diabetes patients.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you\'d like any specific details or clarifications!</p>', 'active', '619_1742756649.jpg', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:04:09', '2025-05-03 07:51:37'),
(4346, 'A-Cal D', 'Elemental Calcium + Vitamin D3', 7.00, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of A-Cal D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>This combination is used for the treatment of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, rickets, tetany, and in parathyroid disease. Calcium supplements are often used to ensure adequate dietary intake in conditions such as pregnancy &amp; lactation, osteogenesis and tooth formation (adjunct with definite treatment), and therapy with anti-seizure medications. It is also used as a routine supplement and phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>A-Cal D 500 mg+200 IU is a preparation of Calcium Carbonate and Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol). Calcium is necessary for many normal functions of our body, especially bone formation and maintenance. Vitamin D3 helps with the absorption &amp; reabsorption of Calcium. Vitamin D3 also stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies show that Calcium and Vitamin D3 together help in bone growth, and in the prevention of osteoporosis &amp; bone fractures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of A-Cal D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><ul><li>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 200 IU Tablet: 2 tablets daily or 1 tablet twice daily. A-Cal D 500 mg+200 IU is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li><li>Calcium 500 mg and Vitamin D3 400 IU Tablet: 1 tablet twice daily. It is best taken with or just after a meal to improve absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of A-Cal D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>It has possible interactions with calcium, aluminium or magnesium-containing antacids &amp; other calcium supplements, calcitriol &amp; other vitamin D3 supplements; digoxin, tetracycline, doxycycline, minocycline, or oxytetracycline.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>A-Cal D 500 mg+200 IU is contraindicated in cases of hypercalcaemia, hyperthyroidism, renal calculi &amp; nephrolithiasis, and Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of A-Cal D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>A-Cal D 500 mg+200 IU is generally well tolerated. If any of the following symptoms occur, consult a physician: nausea, vomiting, stomach cramps, dry mouth, increased thirst, increased urination. Constipation may also occur.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This combination should be used as directed by a physician during pregnancy or while breast-feeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>If there is any pre-existing heart disease or kidney disease, precautions should be taken.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of A-Cal D 500 mg+200 IU</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdosage may include nausea and vomiting, severe drowsiness, dry mouth, loss of appetite, metallic taste, stomach cramps, diarrhea, headache, and constipation.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1263_1742756777.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:06:17', '2025-05-02 22:37:46'),
(4347, 'Reset Plus', 'Paracetamol + Caffeine', 2.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+65 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Reset Plus 500 mg+65 mg</h3><p>Reset Plus 500 mg+65 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Migraine</li><li>Toothache</li><li>Neuralgia</li><li>Feverishness</li><li>Period pain</li><li>Sore throat</li><li>Backache</li><li>Helps to reduce temperature</li><li>Aches and pains from colds and flu</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Reset Plus 500 mg+65 mg is a combination of caffeine and paracetamol (acetaminophen). It has both antipyretic and analgesic properties. Paracetamol works as an analgesic by lowering the pain threshold and as an antipyretic by influencing the hypothalamus to regulate body temperature. Caffeine, a stimulant alkaloid, enhances the effects of paracetamol by increasing its solubility and transmembrane permeability. Caffeine also raises the pain threshold and tolerance, and helps treat headaches and migraines by improving cerebral blood vessel tone.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Reset Plus 500 mg+65 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Adult dose:</strong> 1-2 tablets every 4-6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maximum dose:</strong> 8 tablets daily.</li><li><strong>Child dose:</strong> Not recommended for children below 12 years.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Reset Plus 500 mg+65 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Anticonvulsants</strong> (e.g., phenytoin, barbiturates, carbamazepine) may lower serum levels.</li><li><strong>Warfarin and other coumarins</strong> may have enhanced anticoagulant effects with prolonged use.</li><li><strong>Domperidone absorption</strong> may increase when taken with metoclopramide.</li><li><strong>Probenecid</strong> may increase serum levels.</li><li><strong>Chloramphenicol</strong> may raise serum concentrations.</li><li><strong>Colestyramine</strong> taken within 1 hour of administration may decrease absorption.</li><li><strong>Phenothiazine</strong> may significantly increase hypothermic effects.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Not recommended for individuals with severe renal function impairment or hepatic illness (e.g., Viral Hepatitis).</li><li>Known sensitivity to caffeine or aspirin.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Reset Plus 500 mg+65 mg</h3><p>Although side effects are generally mild, they may include:</p><ul><li>Hematological responses such as thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, pancytopenia, neutropenia, and agranulocytosis.</li><li>Rare occurrences of pancreatitis and skin rashes.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Consult a doctor before using caffeine and paracetamol during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Safe to use while breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients taking additional hepatotoxic medications or those with hepatic or renal impairment.</li><li>Avoid prolonged use without consulting a doctor.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Reset Plus 500 mg+65 mg</h3><ul><li>Initial symptoms within the first 24 hours include pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and stomach discomfort.</li><li>After 12 to 40 hours, liver damage may occur.</li><li>Possible metabolic acidosis and abnormalities in glucose metabolism.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Drug Class</h3><ul><li>Non-opioid analgesics</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Reset Plus 500 mg+65 mg combines the effects of caffeine and paracetamol. The antipyretic and analgesic effects of paracetamol are enhanced by caffeine. Caffeine improves the solubility and permeability of paracetamol, raises the pain threshold, and helps treat migraines and headaches by enhancing cerebral vascular tone.</p><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li>Consult a doctor before using caffeine and paracetamol during pregnancy.</li><li>It is safe to use caffeine and paracetamol during breastfeeding.</li></ul>', 'active', '484_1742756894.png', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:08:14', '2025-05-04 15:37:04'),
(4348, 'V-Plex', 'Multivitamin [Pediatric preparation]', 22.07, 1, 'x 15 ml drop', '15 ml drop', 'Pediatric Drops', 'N/A', 'no', 'no', '<h3>V-Plex (Vitamin-B Complex)</h3><h4>Indications:</h4><p>Vitamin-B complex is used for prophylactic or therapeutic nutritional supplementation in physiologically stressful conditions. It is particularly indicated for:</p><ul><li>Conditions causing depletion, reduced absorption, or bioavailability of essential B-vitamins.</li><li>Symptoms such as glossitis, stomatitis, cheilosis, beriberi, and other manifestations of vitamin B deficiency.</li></ul><h4>Composition:</h4><p>Each dosage form contains the following:</p><ul><li><strong>Tablet/Capsule</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Thiamine Hydrochloride: 5 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Riboflavin: 2 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pyridoxine Hydrochloride: 2 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nicotinamide: 20 mg</li><li><strong>Syrup (per 5 ml)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Thiamine Hydrochloride: 5 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Riboflavin: 2 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pyridoxine Hydrochloride: 2 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nicotinamide: 20 mg</li><li><strong>Injection (per 2 ml)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Thiamine Hydrochloride: 50 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Riboflavin: 4 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pyridoxine Hydrochloride: 10 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nicotinamide: 100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">D-Panthenol: 5 mg</li></ul><h4>Therapeutic Class:</h4><ul><li>Specific combined vitamin preparations</li></ul><h4>Pharmacology:</h4><p>Vitamin-B complex contains the essential members of the vitamin B group, in pure form and therapeutically balanced proportions. These vitamins are components of enzyme systems regulating the metabolism of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins. Deficiency in these vitamins can result in glossitis, stomatitis, cheilosis, polyneuritis, beriberi, pellagra, and corneal vascularization.</p><h4>Dosage &amp; Administration:</h4><ul><li><strong>Tablet/Capsule</strong>: 1-2 tablets/capsules 3 times daily or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Syrup</strong>: 2-3 teaspoonfuls daily or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>: For intramuscular and intravenous administration. The usual recommended dose is 2 ml daily or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><h4>Interaction:</h4><ul><li>Pyridoxine (as little as 5 mg daily) can reduce the efficacy of levodopa in the treatment of parkinsonism. Vitamin-B complex is not recommended for patients undergoing levodopa therapy.</li></ul><h4>Contraindications:</h4><ul><li>Vitamin-B complex is contraindicated in patients who are hypersensitive to any of its components.</li></ul><h4>Side Effects:</h4><ul><li>Adverse reactions are uncommon but can include allergic or idiosyncratic reactions. Iron supplementation, even at recommended levels, has been associated with gastrointestinal intolerance in some patients.</li></ul><h4>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</h4><ul><li>It is safe to use Vitamin-B complex during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><h4>Drug Classes:</h4><ul><li>Specific combined vitamin preparations</li></ul><h4>Mode of Action:</h4><p>The B vitamins in V-Plex are essential for carbohydrate, fat, and protein metabolism. Each vitamin plays a unique biological role, and their combined action is crucial in maintaining health and preventing deficiencies.</p><p><br></p><p>If you need any further clarification on its uses or effects, feel free to ask!</p>', 'active', '1973_1742756972.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:09:32', '2025-05-07 10:31:35'),
(4349, 'Cora-D', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 12.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cora-D</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment and prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, and disorders of osteogenesis. It is also used as a supplement in cases of inadequate calcium intake in childhood diets, rickets, pregnancy, lactation, and elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis and phosphate binding in chronic renal failure, among others.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg (from Coral source), equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p>Or</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg (from Coral source), equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 200 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral and vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate calcium in the blood is crucial for a wide range of bodily functions, and the body will not tolerate a deficiency, even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that calcium is effective in the prevention and treatment of osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is essential for healthy bones as it aids in calcium absorption from the GI tract and stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies show that calcium and vitamin D work synergistically to support bone growth and in the prevention of osteoporosis and fractures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cora-D</strong></p><p>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night, or as directed by the physician.</p><p>Dosage can be one tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water, or as directed by the physician. Taking it with a full stomach ensures better absorption.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Cora-D</strong></p><ul><li>Oral calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline and fluoride preparations; at least 3 hours should be allowed between the ingestion of these medications.</li><li>Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of calcium.</li><li>Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce metabolism of Vitamin D3.</li><li>Concomitant ingestion of certain foods like spinach, cereals, and milk and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of calcium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the components</li><li>Hypocalcaemia resulting from an overdose of Vitamin D3</li><li>Hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Bone metastases</li><li>Severe renal insufficiency</li><li>Severe hypercalciuria</li><li>Renal calculi, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Cora-D</strong></p><p>Rare manifestations include flatulence, diarrhea, constipation, and upper GI discomfort. Hypercalcaemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It should be used as directed by the physician during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with dose reduction may be required.</li><li>Plasma and serum calcium levels should be monitored in mild to moderate renal impairment, especially during long-term use.</li><li>Patients with renal stones or a history of renal stones should also take precautions.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Cora-D</strong></p><p>At high doses, it may result in nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc. Treatment involves cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature below 30°C, protect from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral and vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Cora-D is a calcium and vitamin D3 preparation where calcium carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The calcium carbonate from coral has a chemical structure similar to that of human bone, ensuring better absorption. Vitamin D3 aids in the absorption of calcium from the GI tract and helps maintain calcium balance in the body.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of the physician.</p>', 'active', '1795_1742757091.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:11:31', '2025-05-03 01:36:31'),
(4350, 'Evaglip', 'Empagliflozin + Linagliptin', 30.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg+5 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Evaglip 10 mg + 5 mg</strong></p><p>This is a combination of empagliflozin (a sodium-glucose co-transporter 2 (SGLT2) inhibitor) and linagliptin (a dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitor), indicated as an adjunct to diet and exercise to improve glycemic control in adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus.</p><ul><li>Empagliflozin is also indicated to reduce the risk of cardiovascular death in adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus and established cardiovascular disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Evaglip 10 mg + 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal function assessment:</strong> Assess renal function before initiating and as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Recommended dose:</strong> 10 mg empagliflozin and 5 mg linagliptin once daily, taken in the morning, with or without food.</li><li><strong>Dose adjustment:</strong> Dose may be increased to 25 mg empagliflozin and 5 mg linagliptin once daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients:</strong> The safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Patients:</strong> Higher incidence of adverse reactions related to volume depletion and reduced renal function.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> Higher incidence of adverse reactions related to reduced renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Evaglip 10 mg + 5 mg</strong></p><p>The most common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Urinary tract infection</li><li>Stuffy or runny nose and sore throat</li><li>Upper respiratory tract infection</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pancreatitis:</strong> Reports of acute pancreatitis, including fatal pancreatitis. Discontinue if pancreatitis is suspected.</li><li><strong>Ketoacidosis:</strong> Assess patients presenting with signs and symptoms of metabolic acidosis for ketoacidosis, regardless of blood glucose levels.</li><li><strong>Volume Depletion:</strong> Assess volume status and renal function before initiating and monitor during therapy.</li><li><strong>Urosepsis and Pyelonephritis:</strong> Treat urinary tract infections promptly if indicated.</li><li><strong>Hypoglycemia:</strong> Consider lowering the dose of insulin secretagogue or insulin to reduce the risk of hypoglycemia.</li><li><strong>Necrotizing Fasciitis of the Perineum (Fournier’s Gangrene):</strong> Serious, life-threatening cases have occurred. Discontinue if suspected.</li><li><strong>Genital Mycotic Infections:</strong> Monitor and treat appropriately.</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity Reactions:</strong> Serious reactions like anaphylaxis, angioedema, and exfoliative skin conditions have occurred.</li><li><strong>Arthralgia:</strong> Severe and disabling arthralgia may occur. Discontinue if appropriate.</li><li><strong>Bullous Pemphigoid:</strong> Report any development of blisters or erosions. Discontinue if suspected.</li><li><strong>Heart Failure:</strong> Heart failure has been observed with other DPP-4 inhibitors. Monitor for signs and symptoms in patients with risk factors.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light &amp; moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Empagliflozin:</strong> Inhibits SGLT2, reducing renal reabsorption of glucose, increasing urinary glucose excretion, and lowering the renal threshold for glucose.</li><li><strong>Linagliptin:</strong> Inhibits DPP-4, increasing the concentrations of active incretin hormones (GLP-1 and GIP), which stimulates insulin release in a glucose-dependent manner and reduces glucagon levels, helping regulate glucose homeostasis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Advise females of the potential risk to a fetus, especially during the second and third trimesters.</li><li>This medication is not recommended during breastfeeding.</li></ul>', 'active', '1101_1742757221.jpg', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:13:41', '2025-05-03 10:15:52'),
(4351, 'Corabon-DX', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Corabon-DX 600 mg+400 IU</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment and prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, and disorders of osteogenesis. It is also used as a supplement in case of inadequate intake of calcium in childhood diet, rickets, pregnancy and lactation, and in elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis and as a phosphate binder in chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg (from Coral source), equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li><li>Or</li><li>Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg (from Coral source), equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 200 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate calcium in the blood is vital for a variety of bodily functions, and our internal biochemistry does not tolerate a deficiency even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is also essential for healthy bones as it aids in calcium absorption from the GI tract and stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies show that calcium and vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth and in preventing osteoporosis and fractures.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Corabon-DX 600 mg+400 IU</strong></p><p>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night or as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Corabon-DX 600 mg+400 IU</strong></p><p>One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or as directed by the physician. Taking it with a full stomach ensures better absorption.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Corabon-DX 600 mg+400 IU</strong></p><p>Oral calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline and fluoride preparations, and a minimum of 3 hours should be allowed between ingestion of these medications. Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of calcium. Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce the metabolism of vitamin D3. Concomitant ingestion of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of calcium.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the components</li><li>Hypocalcaemia resulting from an overdose of Vitamin D3</li><li>Hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Bone metastases</li><li>Severe renal insufficiency</li><li>Severe hypercalciuria</li><li>Renal calculi</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Corabon-DX 600 mg+400 IU</strong></p><p>Flatulence, diarrhea, constipation, and upper GI discomfort are rare manifestations. Hypercalcaemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It should be used as directed by the physician during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with reduction of dose may be needed. Plasma and serum calcium levels should be monitored in mild to moderate renal impairment and also during long-term use. Patients with renal stones or a history of such should also take precautions.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Corabon-DX 600 mg+400 IU</strong></p><p>At high doses, it may result in nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc. Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature below 30°C, protect from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This is a calcium and vitamin D3 preparation where calcium carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The calcium carbonate from coral has a chemical structure that is very similar to human bone composition. Coral calcium is similar to other sources but ensures better absorption. Vitamin D3 aids in the absorption of calcium from the GI tract and helps to maintain calcium balance in the body.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of a physician.</p>', 'active', '481_1742757319.webp', 58, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:15:19', '2025-05-03 01:36:58'),
(4352, 'Progesic', 'Naproxen Sodium + Esomeprazole', 15.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Progesic 500 mg</strong></p><p>It is indicated for the relief of signs and symptoms of osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis, and to decrease the risk of developing gastric ulcers in patients at risk of developing NSAID-associated gastric ulcers.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Progesic 500 mg is a preparation of Naproxen and Esomeprazole.</p><ul><li><strong>Naproxen</strong>: A NSAID with analgesic and antipyretic activities. It inhibits prostaglandin synthesis.</li><li><strong>Esomeprazole</strong>: A proton pump inhibitor that suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the H+/K+ ATPase in the gastric parietal cell. By acting specifically on the proton pump, Esomeprazole blocks the final step in acid production, thus reducing gastric acidity.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Progesic 500 mg</strong></p><p>The recommended dose is one tablet twice daily. Do not split, chew, crush, or dissolve the tablet. This tablet is to be taken at least 30 minutes before meals.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Progesic 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ACE Inhibitors, diuretics, and beta-blockers</strong>: May reduce the antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin</strong>: Increases the risk of nephrotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Lithium</strong>: Increases plasma level of lithium.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate</strong>: May increase the serum levels of methotrexate.</li><li><strong>Warfarin</strong>: May increase the risk of bleeding complications.</li><li><strong>Digoxin</strong>: May increase digoxin toxicity.</li><li><strong>Clopidogrel</strong>: May decrease the exposure to the active metabolite of clopidogrel.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any components of this product. It is also contraindicated in patients who have experienced asthma, urticaria, or allergic-type reactions after taking aspirin or other NSAIDs and for the treatment of per-operative pain in the setting of coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Progesic 500 mg</strong></p><p>The most common side effects are gastritis, erosive gastritis, dyspepsia, diarrhea, gastric ulcer, upper abdominal pain, and nausea.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of this tablet should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Fatal cardiovascular thrombotic events like myocardial infarction, stroke, hypertension, congestive heart failure, and edema.</li><li>Serious GI adverse events.</li><li>Hypomagnesaemia.</li><li>Renal or hepatic insufficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1686_1742757477.webp', 58, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:17:57', '2025-05-04 10:31:42'),
(4353, 'Evit', 'Vitamin E [Alpha Tocopherol Acetate]', 6.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '200 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Evit Soft 200 mg</h3><p><strong>As a dietary supplement:</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin E deficiency resulting from impaired absorption</li><li>Increased requirements due to a diet rich in polyunsaturated fats</li><li>For healthy hair &amp; skin</li><li>As an antioxidant</li><li>Hemolytic anemia due to Vitamin E deficiency</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic use:</strong></p><ul><li>Cardiovascular disease</li><li>Heavy metal poisoning</li><li>Hepatotoxin poisoning</li><li>Hemolytic anemia</li><li>Oxygen therapy</li><li>Nutritional deficiency states</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Vitamin-E Preparations</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Vitamin E acts as an antioxidant in the body. It protects polyunsaturated fatty acids (which are components of cellular membranes) and other oxygen-sensitive substances such as vitamin A and vitamin C from oxidation. In premature neonates, irritability, edema, thrombosis, and hemolytic anemia may be caused due to vitamin E deficiency. Creatinuria, ceroid deposition, muscle weakness, decreased erythrocyte survival, or increased in vitro hemolysis by oxidizing agents have been identified in adults and children with low serum tocopherol concentrations.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Evit Soft 200 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Betterment of cardiovascular health:</strong> 400 IU - 800 IU per day</li><li><strong>Deficiency syndrome in adults:</strong> 200 IU - 400 IU per day</li><li><strong>Deficiency syndrome in children:</strong> 200 IU per day</li><li><strong>Thalassemia:</strong> 800 IU per day</li><li><strong>Sickle-cell anemia:</strong> 400 IU per day</li><li><strong>Betterment of skin &amp; hair:</strong> 200 IU - 400 IU per day (Topical use is also established for beautification)</li><li><strong>Chronic cold in adults:</strong> 200 IU per day</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Evit Soft 200 mg</h3><ul><li>Vitamin E may impair the absorption of Vitamin A &amp; the function of Vitamin K.</li><li>Potentiates the effect of Warfarin.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>No known contraindications found.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Evit Soft 200 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Overdosage (&gt;1 gm)</strong> has been associated with minor side effects, including:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypertension</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fatigue</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Diarrhea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Myopathy</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>Vitamin E is safe during pregnancy and lactation when used at recommended doses. Higher doses are not established.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>It may increase the risk of thrombosis in some patients, such as those taking estrogens.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li>Vitamin E is safe for children.</li></ul><p>This format separates the information with clear headings for easy reference and enhances readability. Let me know if you\'d like any further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '1258_1742757613.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:20:13', '2025-05-02 17:09:43'),
(4354, 'Rupex', 'Rupatadine Fumarate', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Rupex 10 mg</strong></p><p>Rupex 10 mg is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis and Urticaria.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rupex 10 mg is a second-generation, non-sedating, long-acting histamine antagonist with selective peripheral H1 receptor antagonist activity. It further blocks the receptors of the platelet-activating factor (PAF) according to in vitro and in vivo studies. Rupatadine possesses anti-allergic properties such as the inhibition of the degranulation of mast cells induced by immunological and non-immunological stimuli, and inhibition of the release of cytokines, particularly the tumor necrosis factors (TNF) in human mast cells and monocytes.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rupex 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Rupatadine Tablet:</strong> The recommended dosage is 10 mg once daily, with or without food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Rupatadine Oral Solution (Children aged 2 to 11 years):</strong>Children weighing 25 kg or more: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) of oral solution once daily with or without food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing 10 kg to less than 25 kg: 1/2 teaspoonful (2.5 ml) oral solution once daily with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Rupex 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (over 12 years):</strong> The recommended dose is 10 mg once a day. Rupatadine may be taken with or without food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children aged 2 to 11 years:</strong>Children weighing 25 kg or more: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) of the oral solution once daily with or without food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing 10 kg to less than 25 kg: 1/2 teaspoonful (2.5 ml) oral solution once daily with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Rupex 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With medicine:</strong> The concomitant administration of Rupatadine 20 mg and ketoconazole or erythromycin increases the systemic exposure. Rupatadine should be used with caution when it is administered concomitantly with these drug substances and other inhibitors of the isozyme CYP3A4. Rupatadine should be used with caution when it is co-administered with statins, CNS depressants, or alcohol.</li><li><strong>With food:</strong> Grapefruit and Grapefruit juice should not be taken simultaneously with Rupatadine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to Rupatadine or to any of the excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Rupex 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> Asthenia, dizziness, drowsiness.</li><li><strong>Uncommon:</strong> Appetite increased, arthralgia, back pain, concentration impaired, constipation, cough, diarrhea, dry throat, epistaxis, fever, gastrointestinal discomfort, increased risk of infection, irritability, malaise, myalgia, nasal dryness, nausea, oropharyngeal pain, rash, thirst, vomiting, weight increased.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Palpitations, tachycardia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy category B2 (by TGA, Australia):</strong> There is no clinical data available on the exposure of Rupatadine during pregnancy. Pregnant women should therefore not use Rupatadine, unless the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk for the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> No information is available on whether Rupatadine is excreted in the mother\'s milk. Therefore, it should not be used during lactation unless the potential benefits for the mother justify the potential risk to the infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Rupex 10 mg should be used with caution in patients with known prolongation of the QT interval, patients with uncorrected hypokalemia, and patients with ongoing proarrhythmic conditions, such as clinically significant bradycardia or acute myocardial ischemia. Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly patients (65 years and older). As there is no clinical experience in patients with impaired kidney or liver function, the use of Rupatadine 10 mg tablets is at present not recommended in these patients.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rupex 10 mg</strong></p><p>The most common adverse reaction was somnolence. If accidental ingestion of very high doses occurs, symptomatic treatment together with the required supportive measures should be given.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool &amp; dry place below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly. No information is available that indicates the requirement of any dose adjustment in this population.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> Neither the safety nor the efficacy of Rupatadine has been established in patients less than 12 years of age.</li><li><strong>Patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency:</strong> Use of Rupatadine is not recommended in patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency as no relevant clinical data is available.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Rupex 10 mg is a long-acting, non-sedative antagonist of histamine H1-receptors. It also antagonizes the platelet-activating factor (PAF). Both histamine and PAF cause bronchoconstriction, leading to an increase in vascular permeability and acting as mediators in the inflammatory process. With the dual mode of action, Rupatadine shows a better therapeutic effect than an isolated antihistamine. Rupatadine possesses other anti-allergic properties, such as the inhibition of the degranulation of mast cells induced by immunological and non-immunological stimuli and inhibition of the release of cytokines, particularly the tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF α) in human mastocytes and monocytes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There is no clinical data available on the exposure of Rupatadine during pregnancy. Pregnant women should therefore not use Rupatadine unless the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk for the infant. No information is available on whether Rupatadine is excreted in the mother\'s milk. Therefore, it should not be used during lactation unless the potential benefits for the mother justify the potential risk to the infant.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly. No information is available that indicates the requirement of any dose adjustment in this population.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> Neither the safety nor the efficacy of Rupatadine has been established in patients less than 12 years of age.</li><li><strong>Patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency:</strong> Use of Rupatadine is not recommended in patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency as no relevant clinical data is available.</li></ul>', 'active', '358_1742757705.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:21:45', '2025-05-04 20:35:42');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4355, 'Rabifast', 'Rabeprazole Sodium', 7.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information you provided about <strong>Rabifast 20 mg</strong> presented in normal bold text as requested:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Rabifast 20 mg</strong></p><p>Gastro-resistant tablets of rabeprazole are recommended for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Active ulcer in the duodenum.</li><li>Benign stomach ulcer that is active.</li><li>Symptomatic gastroesophageal reflux illness that is erosive or ulcerative (GERD).</li><li>Disorder of the Gastro-esophageal Reflux Long-Term Planning (GERD Maintenance).</li><li>Moderate to extremely severe gastroesophageal reflux disease symptoms (symptomatic GERD).</li><li>Syndrome of Zollinger-Ellison.</li><li>Helicobacter pylori removal in patients with peptic ulcer disease in conjunction with suitable antibacterial therapy regimens.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>The gastric H+/K+-ATPase at the secretory surface of the stomach parietal cell is inhibited by rabeprazole, which reduces gastric acid secretion. It has been said that Rabeprazole acts as an inhibitor of the gastric proton pump since this enzyme is thought to be the acid (proton) pump within the parietal cell.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rabifast 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer:</strong> Rabeprazole 20 mg in the morning for 4 weeks. Most patients heal within 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Healing of erosive or ulcerative GERD:</strong> Rabeprazole 20 mg daily in the morning for 4 to 8 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely after 8 weeks, an additional 8-week course of Rabeprazole may be considered.</li><li><strong>Treatment of symptomatic GERD:</strong> The recommended adult oral dose is 20 mg once daily in the morning for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely after 4 weeks, an additional course of treatment may be considered.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of healing of erosive or ulcerative GERD:</strong> The recommended adult oral dose of Rabeprazole is 20 mg once daily. Controlled studies do not extend beyond 12 months.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> The recommended adult oral starting dose is 60 mg once a day. Doses should be adjusted according to individual patient needs and should continue for as long as clinically indicated. Some patients may require divided doses. Doses up to 100 mg once daily and 60 mg twice daily have been administered.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication:</strong> Rabeprazole 20 mg, Amoxicillin 1000 mg, Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily in the morning and evening with meals for 7 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Rabifast 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Active Duodenal Ulcer and Active Benign Gastric Ulcer:</strong> The recommended oral dose for both bioactive duodenal ulcer and active benign gastric ulcer is 20 mg to be taken once daily in the morning. Most patients with active duodenal ulcer heal within four weeks. However, a few patients may require an additional four weeks of therapy to achieve healing. Most patients with active benign gastric ulcer heal within six weeks. However, again a few patients may require an additional six weeks of therapy to achieve healing.</li><li><strong>Erosive or Ulcerative Gastro-Esophageal Reflux Disease (GERD):</strong> The recommended oral dose for this condition is 20 mg to be taken once daily for four to eight weeks.</li><li><strong>Gastro-Esophageal Reflux Disease Long-term Management (GERD Maintenance):</strong> For long-term management, a maintenance dose of Rabifast 20 mg or 10 mg once daily can be used depending upon patient response.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic treatment of moderate to very severe Gastro-Esophageal Reflux Disease (symptomatic GERD):</strong> 10 mg once daily in patients without oesophagitis. If symptom control has not been achieved during four weeks, the patient should be further investigated. Once symptoms have resolved, subsequent symptom control can be achieved using an on-demand regimen taking 10 mg once daily when needed.</li><li><strong>Treatment of GERD in pediatric patients 1 to 11 years of age (Less than 15 kg):</strong> 5 mg once daily for 12 weeks with the option to increase to 10 mg if inadequate response.</li><li><strong>Treatment of GERD in pediatric patients 1 to 11 years of age (15 kg or more):</strong> 10 mg once daily for 12 weeks.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> The recommended adult starting dose is 60 mg once a day. The dose may be titrated upwards to 120 mg/day based on individual patient needs. Single daily doses up to 100 mg/day may be given. 120 mg dose may require divided doses, 60 mg twice daily. Treatment should continue for as long as clinically indicated.</li><li><strong>Eradication of H. pylori:</strong> Patients with H. pylori infection should be treated with eradication therapy. The following combination given for 7 days is recommended. Rabeprazole sodium 20 mg twice daily, clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily, and amoxicillin 1g twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Rabifast 20 mg</strong></p><p>Although neither the time of day nor food intake was demonstrated to have any effect on Rabifast 20 mg activity, taking these pills in the morning before eating will make it easier for patients to adhere to the treatment plan. Patients should be informed that the pills of rabeprazole should not be chewed or mashed but rather consumed whole.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Rabifast 20 mg</strong></p><p>Significant and persistent inhibition of stomach acid output is brought about by respite. A compound whose absorption is pH-dependent may interact with another substance. Itraconazole or ketoconazole co-administration with Rabifast 20 mg may cause a considerable drop in antifungal plasma levels. Consequently, when ketoconazole or itraconazole are used concurrently with Respite, individual patients may need to be watched to determine if a dosage adjustment is warranted. There was no apparent interaction with liquid antacids. Atazanavir is pH-dependently absorbed. As a result, PPIs, such as rabeprazole, shouldn\'t be taken alongside atazanavir.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to any excipient or active ingredient. The use of Rabifast 20 mg during pregnancy and breastfeeding is not advised.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Rabifast 20 mg</strong></p><p>In both short- and long-term studies, Rabifast 20 mg is generally well tolerated. In some cases, rabeprazole may result in headache, nausea, vomiting, constipation, dry mouth, increased or decreased appetite, muscle pain, sleepiness, and dizziness.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>FDA pregnancy category \"C\" in the US. There is no proof that Rabeprazole affects fertility or causes harm to the fetus, according to studies done on animals. Nevertheless, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. Since that rabeprazole is probably excreted in human milk, a choice should be made regarding whether to stop breastfeeding or to stop the medication, taking into account the significance of the medication to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Before starting treatment with Rabifast 20 mg Gastro-resistant Tablets, the risk of malignancy should be ruled out because the presence of stomach or oesophageal malignancy is not precluded by the symptomatic response to therapy with rabeprazole. Long-term patients should be regularly monitored, especially those who have been on medication for more than a year. Proton pump inhibitors, particularly when used in high dosages and for extended periods of time (&gt;1 year), may slightly increase the risk of hip, wrist, and spine fracture, mostly in elderly patients or when other known risk factors are present. Proton pump inhibitors may raise the total risk of fracture by 10% to 40%, according to observational studies. Other risk factors could account for some of this increase. Individuals who are at risk for osteoporosis should be looked after and should consume enough calcium and vitamin D.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rabifast 20 mg</strong></p><p>The highest exposure that has been determined has never gone above 60 mg twice daily or 160 mg once daily. Effects are often mild, typical of recognized adverse event profiles, and reversible without additional medical help. There is no known particular antidote. Due to its high protein binding, rabeprazole cannot be dialyzed. Treatment for overdose should be symptomatic, as with any overdose, and broad supportive techniques should be used.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep away from light and moisture at a temperature below 30°C. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><p>It is unknown whether rabeprazole is safe and effective for use in children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>The gastric H+/K+-ATPase at the secretory surface of the stomach parietal cell is inhibited by rabeprazole, which reduces gastric acid secretion. It has been said that Rabeprazole acts as an inhibitor of the gastric proton pump since this enzyme is thought to be the acid (proton) pump within the parietal cell.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>FDA pregnancy category \"C\" in the US. There is no proof that Rabeprazole affects fertility or causes harm to the fetus, according to studies done on animals. Nevertheless, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. Since that rabeprazole is probably excreted in human milk, a choice should be made regarding whether to stop breastfeeding or to stop the medication, taking into account the significance of the medication to the mother.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Hepatic and renal impairment: Patients who have these conditions don\'t require dosage adjustments. Rabeprazole is not advised for usage in pediatric populations since there are insufficient data on its safety and effectiveness.</p>', 'active', '566_1742757804.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:23:24', '2025-05-04 15:02:36'),
(4356, 'Sandom', 'Domperidone Maleate', 2.02, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Sandom 10 mg</h3><p>Sandom 10 mg is indicated for the treatment of dyspeptic symptom complex, often linked with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis. These symptoms may include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric sensation of fullness</li><li>Abdominal distension</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Belching</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Early satiety</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms can occur in various conditions, such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine. Additionally, in Parkinson\'s disease, it can manifest as dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting. Sandom 10 mg is also used in radiological studies to speed up barium transit during follow-through radiological examinations.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Motility Stimulants</li><li>Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist</li><li>Prokinetic drugs</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Sandom 10 mg functions as a dopamine receptor antagonist. It exerts a gastroprokinetic action by inhibiting dopamine receptors found in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier means it has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sandom 10 mg</h3><p>For adults, the recommended dosage is 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily. Children should take 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily. It is advisable to take this medication 15-30 minutes before a meal.</p><h4>Recommended Dosage:</h4><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Adults:</strong>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The maximum daily dose of Domperidone is 80 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Children:</strong>2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg of body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><h4>For Acute and Sub-acute Conditions:</h4><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Adults:</strong>20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Children:</strong>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><h4>Rectal Administration (Suppositories):</h4><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Adults (including elderly):</strong>30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Children:</strong>The maximum daily rectal dose for children weighing between 10 to 25 kg is 30 mg. The dose may be divided throughout the day if necessary.</li></ul><p>Please note that the maximum treatment period for acute nausea and vomiting should not exceed 12 weeks.</p><h3>Interaction of Sandom 10 mg</h3><ul><li>The simultaneous use of <strong>anticholinergic drugs</strong> may counteract the antidyspeptic effects of domperidone.</li><li><strong>Antacids</strong> and <strong>antisecretory drugs</strong> should not be administered concurrently with domperidone as they may reduce its oral bioavailability.</li><li>Domperidone undergoes metabolism through the <strong>CYP3A4 enzyme</strong>. Co-administration with drugs that significantly inhibit this enzyme (e.g., azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, nefazodone) may lead to elevated plasma levels of domperidone.</li><li>Domperidone may potentially affect the absorption of concurrently orally administered drugs, especially those with sustained release or enteric-coated formulations. However, for drugs like <strong>digoxin</strong> and <strong>paracetamol</strong>, concurrent use with domperidone did not affect their blood levels.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Sandom 10 mg should not be used by:</p><ul><li>Patients with a known hypersensitivity to this medication.</li><li>Neonates.</li><li>Patients with gastrointestinal conditions such as gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation.</li><li>Patients with a <strong>prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma)</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Sandom 10 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Rare side effects</strong>: Transient intestinal cramps.</li><li><strong>Extrapyramidal symptoms</strong>: Rare in young children and exceptionally rare in adults.</li><li>Domperidone can increase <strong>plasma prolactin levels</strong>, which in rare cases may result in neuroendocrinological effects such as galactorrhea (abnormal milk production) and gynecomastia (development of breast tissue in men).</li><li><strong>Allergic reactions</strong>: Rash and urticaria may occur.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>The safety of Sandom 10 mg during pregnancy has not been established, so its use is not recommended during pregnancy.</li><li>Animal studies have shown no teratogenic effects, but it is best to avoid use during pregnancy.</li><li>Domperidone is excreted in breast milk in minimal quantities, which are insufficient to be considered harmful. It may enhance post-natal lactation.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Sandom 10 mg should be used with caution in children, especially due to the potential for increased risk of extrapyramidal reactions.</li><li>Caution should also be exercised when administering to patients with <strong>hepatic impairment</strong> due to extensive metabolism in the liver.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Sandom 10 mg</h3><p>Signs of an overdose may include drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions, particularly in children. In case of an overdose, <strong>activated charcoal</strong> should be administered, and the patient should be closely monitored. The use of <strong>anticholinergic drugs</strong>, <strong>antiparkinson drugs</strong>, or <strong>antihistamines</strong> with anticholinergic properties may help manage extrapyramidal reactions.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Sandom 10 mg acts as a <strong>dopamine antagonist</strong>. It works primarily by blocking dopamine receptors in the <strong>chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ)</strong> and the stomach. This helps in the regulation of gastrointestinal motility, promotes gastric emptying, enhances antral and duodenal peristalsis, and increases esophageal peristalsis while lowering esophageal sphincter pressure.</p><h3>Pediatric Uses</h3><ul><li><strong>Infants</strong>: Caution is required in infants due to the immature blood-brain barrier, increasing the potential for neurological side effects. This medication should be used under strict medical supervision.</li></ul><h3>Use in Liver and Kidney Disorders</h3><ul><li><strong>Liver impairment</strong>: Caution should be exercised in patients with liver disorders as domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver.</li><li><strong>Kidney impairment</strong>: In patients with severe renal insufficiency, the elimination half-life of domperidone increases. For repeated administration, the dosing frequency should be reduced to once or twice daily, depending on the severity of the impairment. Regular reviews are advised for prolonged therapy.</li></ul>', 'active', '1980_1742757897.webp', 45, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:24:57', '2025-05-04 20:48:41'),
(4357, 'D-Cap', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 4.00, 15, 'Capsule', '15 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '800 IU', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of D-Cap 800 IU</h3><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated in the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also used as an adjunct to specific therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcaemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Vitamin D3 is essential for normal bone growth and development and maintaining bone density. It also plays a critical role in the absorption and utilization of calcium and phosphorus.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin-D preparations</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) helps in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is essential for normal bone growth and to maintain bone density. Vitamin D3 also has additional benefits, such as reducing the severity of bacterial infections, improving lung function, preventing certain cancers (breast, colorectal), and helping to maintain adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of D-Cap 800 IU</h3><h4>For Capsules:</h4><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks. Maintenance therapy is 1,400-2,000 IU/day. Confirm the target level of 25-hydroxyvitamin D after 3-4 months of maintenance therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 20,000 IU every 4 weeks. Higher doses may be required in certain situations.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adjunct to specific therapy for osteoporosis:</strong> 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (12-18 years):Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 20,000 IU once every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency:</strong> 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li></ul><h4>For Film-Coated Tablets:</h4><ul><li><strong>1000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily</strong>, or as directed by a physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li></ul><h4>For Chewable Tablets (Oroflash):</h4><ul><li><strong>1000 IU to 2000 IU daily</strong>, or as directed by a physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li></ul><h4>For Syrup:</h4><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For patients at risk of Vitamin D3 deficiency:0-1 year:</strong> 400 IU/day (2 ml)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>&gt;1 year:</strong> 600 IU/day (3 ml)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For Vitamin D3 deficient patients:0-1 year:</strong> 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>1-18 years:</strong> 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks</li></ul><h4>For Injection:</h4><ul><li><strong>Infants (receiving Vitamin D enriched milk):</strong> 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) = 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li><strong>Infants not receiving Vitamin D enriched milk or children up to 5 years:</strong> 1 ampoule (1ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li><strong>Adolescents:</strong> 1 ampoule (1ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) = 100,000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) = 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li><strong>In patients with digestive disorders or on antiepileptics:</strong> 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) = 100,000 IU or 1 ampoule (1ml) = 200,000 IU every 3 or 6 months.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of D-Cap 800 IU</h3><p>Colecalciferol may interfere with medications like phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (e.g., liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</p><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</p><h3>Side Effects of D-Cap 800 IU</h3><ul><li>Common side effects: Hypercalcaemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, abdominal pain.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Studies have shown safe use of doses up to 4,000 IU during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake is 400 IU, but higher doses may be needed for those deficient in Vitamin D3.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. No overdose in infants has been observed, but when prescribing Vitamin D3 to a breastfed child, the practitioner should consider any additional doses given to the mother.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><p>Use with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</p><h3>Overdose Effects of D-Cap 800 IU</h3><p>May lead to hypervitaminosis D.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><p>Store below 30ºC, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><p>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</p><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin-D preparations</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Vitamin D3 helps in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus, essential for normal bone growth, bone density maintenance, and various other physiological processes like reducing bacterial infections, improving lung function, preventing cancer, and maintaining insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p>', 'active', '48_1742758041.webp', 33, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:27:21', '2025-05-01 19:33:36'),
(4358, 'Kindical-DX', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 16.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '600 mg+400 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p>Sure! Here\'s the text with headings in bold:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Kindical-DX</strong></p><p>This is indicated for the treatment &amp; prevention of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, tetany, hypoparathyroidism, disorders of osteogenesis. Also used as a supplement in case of inadequate intake of Calcium in childhood diet, rickets, pregnancy &amp; lactation, elderly patients. Other indications include pancreatitis, phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg, Vitamin D3 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li><li>OR</li><li>Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg, Vitamin D3 200 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate Calcium in the blood is vital to a wide variety of bodily functions that our internal biochemistry cannot tolerate a deficiency, even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that Calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of Osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is also essential for healthy bones as it aids in Calcium absorption from the GI tract. In addition to this, it stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies also show that Calcium and Vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth as well as in Osteoporosis and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Kindical-DX</strong></p><p>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night or as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Kindical-DX</strong></p><p>One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water or as directed by the physician. Taking it with a full stomach ensures better absorption.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Kindical-DX</strong></p><p>Oral Calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline &amp; fluoride preparations and a minimum of 3 hours should be allowed between ingestion of these medications. Thiazide diuretics reduce the renal excretion of Calcium. Phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids may induce metabolism of Vitamin D3. Concomitant ingestion of certain foods like spinach, cereals, milk, and its derivatives may reduce the intestinal uptake of Calcium.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to any of the components, hypocalcaemia resulting from overdose of Vitamin D3, hyperparathyroidism, bone metastases, severe renal insufficiency, severe hypercalciuria, renal calculi, etc.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Kindical-DX</strong></p><p>Flatulence, diarrhoea, constipation, upper GI discomfort, etc., are rare manifestations. Hypercalcaemia due to prolonged use has rarely been reported.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It should be used as directed by the physician during Pregnancy and Lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with a reduction of dose may be needed. Plasma and serum Calcium levels should be monitored in mild to moderate renal impairment and also in case of long-term use. Patients with renal stones or with such previous history should also take precautions.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Kindical-DX</strong></p><p>At high doses, it may result in nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc. Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a temperature of below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This is a Calcium and Vitamin D3 preparation where Calcium Carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The Calcium Carbonate from Coral has a chemical structure that is very similar to the composition of human bone. Coral Calcium is similar to other sources but ensures better absorption. Vitamin D3 aids in the absorption of Calcium from the GI tract and helps to maintain Calcium balance in the body.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the recommendation of the physician.</p><p>Let me know if you\'d like any further changes!</p>', 'active', '1313_1742758139.png', 62, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:28:59', '2025-05-03 23:16:40'),
(4359, 'Caldil', 'Elemental Calcium', 4.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Caldil 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>250 mg or 500 mg tablet</strong>: This is used for the treatment or prevention of calcium depletion in patients where dietary measures are inadequate. Conditions that may be associated with calcium deficiency include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypoparathyroidism</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Achlorhydria</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Chronic diarrhea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Vitamin D deficiency</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Steatorrhea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sprue</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pregnancy and lactation</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Menopause</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pancreatitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Renal failure</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Alkalosis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hyperphosphataemia</li></ul><p>Caldil 500 mg is also increasingly used to treat hyperphosphataemia in chronic renal failure, as well as for patients on continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis (CAPD) and haemodialysis. Many patients may not tolerate sufficient doses for complete phosphate control and may require additional measures, such as dietary phosphate restriction or smaller doses of aluminium hydroxide. Caldil 500 mg can provide short-term relief of dyspeptic symptoms but is no longer recommended for long-term treatment of peptic ulceration.</p><ul><li><strong>1000 mg tablet</strong>: This is indicated for the management of conditions associated with hyperidity and for fast relief of acid indigestion, heartburn, sour stomach, and upset stomach.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Minerals in bone formation, Specific mineral preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Calcium carbonate reacts with gastric acid to produce a salt and water. The chemical reaction for Caldil 500 mg is:</p><p><strong>CaCO3 + 2HCl = CaCl2 + H2O + CO2</strong></p><p>Two grams of Caldil 500 mg can readily bring 100 ml of hydrochloric acid to a pH above 6. This increase in gastric pH reduces the activity of pepsin in gastric secretion. Up to 30% of the oral calcium load may be absorbed.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Caldil 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>250 mg or 500 mg tablet</strong>: Caldil 500 mg is always taken orally. When used as an antacid, the recommended doses for adults range from 540-2000 mg per day. For children, doses are typically half those for adults.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">As a dietary supplement (e.g., for the prevention of osteoporosis), 1250-3750 mg of Caldil 500 mg (500-1500 mg calcium) daily is recommended, but this should be tailored based on the patient’s specific needs.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For pregnancy and lactation, the recommended daily dose of calcium is 1200-1500 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In chronic renal failure, doses may range from 2.5-9.0 gm per day and should be adjusted according to individual needs. To maximize phosphate binding, Caldil 500 mg should be taken with meals.</li><li><strong>1000 mg tablet</strong>: 2000-3000 mg, when symptoms occur; may be repeated hourly if needed or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Caldil 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Caldil 500 mg may enhance the cardiac effects of digoxin and other cardiac glycosides if systemic hypercalcaemia occurs.</li><li>It may interfere with the absorption of concurrently administered tetracycline preparations.</li><li>In chronic renal failure, vitamin D therapy may need modification to prevent hypercalcaemia when Caldil 500 mg is used as the primary phosphate binder.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypercalcaemia and hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Hypercalciuria and nephrolithiasis</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Concomitant digoxin therapy (requires careful monitoring of serum calcium levels)</li><li>Use with caution in patients with sarcoidosis, renal or cardiac disease, and in patients receiving cardiac glycosides.</li></ul><p>When hypercalcaemia occurs, discontinuation of the drug typically restores normal serum calcium levels.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Caldil 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Orally administered Caldil 500 mg may irritate the GI tract and cause constipation.</li><li>Hypercalcaemia is rare but may occur when large doses are given to patients with chronic renal failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Calcium-containing drugs have been widely used in pregnancy for oral calcium supplementation or antacid therapy. Caldil 500 mg can also be used by lactating women.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place at controlled room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children</strong>: Calcium carbonate has been extensively studied in children and infants with chronic renal failure and is both safe and effective.</li><li><strong>Use in the elderly</strong>: In elderly patients with renal failure, constipation may become troublesome. Therefore, serum calcium and phosphate levels should be monitored carefully in this group.</li></ul>', 'active', '1708_1742758243.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:30:43', '2025-05-03 01:10:13'),
(4360, 'Flutide', 'Fluticasone Propionate + Formoterol Fumarate', 895.00, 1, 'x 120 metered doses', '120 metered doses', 'Inhaler', '(250 mcg+10 mcg)/puff', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you provided, formatted with normal bold headings:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Flutide 250 mcg+10 mcg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Asthma:</strong> This fixed-dose combination of Fluticasone Propionate and Formoterol Fumarate is indicated for the regular treatment of asthma where a combination of an inhaled corticosteroid and a long-acting ß2 agonist is appropriate. It is for patients not adequately controlled with inhaled corticosteroids and \"as required\" inhaled short-acting ß2 agonist. Alternatively, it can be used for patients already adequately controlled on both an inhaled corticosteroid and a long-acting ß2 agonist.</li><li><strong>COPD:</strong> Fluticasone Propionate and Formoterol Fumarate is recommended when a long-acting ß2 agonist/Inhaled corticosteroid is indicated &amp; patients are unable to use other long-acting ß2 agonist/Inhaled corticosteroid.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong> Respiratory corticosteroids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> This inhaler contains two active ingredients:</p><ul><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: A corticosteroid that helps reduce swelling and inflammation in the lungs.</li><li><strong>Formoterol Fumarate</strong>: A long-acting beta 2 agonist, which is a bronchodilator that helps keep the airways in the lungs open.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Flutide 250 mcg+10 mcg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Asthma:</strong> For adults and adolescents aged 12 years and above, the recommended dose is two inhalations twice daily (morning and evening). For adults, the dose can be increased if asthma remains poorly controlled. This is the highest strength for adults only and is not recommended for adolescents aged 12 years and above.</li><li><strong>COPD:</strong> The recommended dose for adults is two inhalations twice daily (morning and evening).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Flutide 250 mcg+10 mcg:</strong></p><ul><li>Shake the inhaler well (at least six times) before use.</li><li>If the inhaler is new or hasn\'t been used for a week, release one puff into the air before use to ensure it works.</li><li>Breathe out fully, place the actuator in your mouth, and breathe in deeply while pressing the canister to release the medicine.</li><li>Hold your breath for at least 10 seconds.</li><li>Wait 1 minute between puffs if more than one inhalation is prescribed.</li><li>After use, rinse your mouth with water.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Flutide 250 mcg+10 mcg:</strong></p><ul><li>Caution is advised when using this inhaler with strong CYP 3A4 inhibitors, such as Ritonavir, Atazanavir, Ketoconazole, etc., particularly in long-term treatment.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Not for acute asthma attacks; fast-acting bronchodilators should be used during such events.</li><li>Not for use in patients with acute or significantly worsening asthma.</li><li>Should not be used in patients with pulmonary tuberculosis, fungal, viral infections of the airway, or certain cardiovascular disorders.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Flutide 250 mcg+10 mcg:</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include tremor, headache, dry mouth, and nervousness.</li><li>Serious side effects like paradoxical bronchospasm, hypertension, arrhythmias, and systemic corticosteroid side effects (e.g., Cushing\'s syndrome, growth retardation in children) may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Administration is not recommended unless the benefit to the mother outweighs the possible risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong> It\'s unknown whether the components are excreted in human milk. The decision should weigh the benefits of breastfeeding against the need for therapy.</li><li><strong>Fertility:</strong> No data are available regarding effects on fertility.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Flutide 250 mcg+10 mcg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Formoterol Overdose:</strong> May lead to exaggeration of typical ß2 agonist effects, such as palpitations, tachycardia, muscle cramps, and seizures.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Overdose:</strong> Acute overdose usually does not pose significant clinical problems but may cause suppression of the HPA axis. Continued treatment with the recommended dose should be resumed.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> No dose adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Hepatic/Renal Impairment:</strong> No data available.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Population:</strong> Not recommended for use in children under 12 years of age.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Respiratory corticosteroids</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> This inhaler combines a corticosteroid (Fluticasone Propionate) to reduce inflammation and a long-acting bronchodilator (Formoterol Fumarate) to help open the airways, improving breathing.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses:</strong></p><ul><li>The height of children receiving prolonged treatment with inhaled corticosteroids should be monitored regularly.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need further information or clarification!</p>', 'active', '90_1742758345.jpg', 8, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:32:25', '2025-05-03 08:39:30'),
(4361, 'Solivo', 'Naproxen Sodium + Esomeprazole Magnesium', 13.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '375 mg+20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Solivo 375 mg + 20 mg</strong></p><p>It is indicated for the relief of signs and symptoms of osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis and to decrease the risk of developing gastric ulcers in patients at risk of developing NSAID-associated gastric ulcers.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>It is a preparation of Naproxen and Esomeprazole. Naproxen is an NSAID with analgesic and antipyretic activities. It inhibits prostaglandin synthesis. Esomeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor that suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the H+/K+ ATPase in the gastric parietal cell. By acting specifically on the proton pump, Esomeprazole blocks the final step in acid production, thus reducing gastric acidity.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Solivo 375 mg + 20 mg</strong></p><p>The recommended dose is one tablet twice daily. Do not split, chew, crush, or dissolve the tablet. This tablet should be taken at least 30 minutes before meals.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Solivo 375 mg + 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ACE Inhibitors, diuretics, and beta-blockers:</strong> May reduce the antihypertensive effect.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin:</strong> Increases the risk of nephrotoxicity.</li><li><strong>Lithium:</strong> Increases plasma levels of lithium.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate:</strong> May increase the serum levels of methotrexate.</li><li><strong>Warfarin:</strong> May increase the risk of bleeding complications.</li><li><strong>Digoxin:</strong> May increase digoxin toxicity.</li><li><strong>Clopidogrel:</strong> May decrease the exposure to the active metabolite of clopidogrel.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any components of this product. It is also contraindicated in patients who have experienced asthma, urticaria, or allergic-type reactions after taking aspirin or other NSAIDs and for the treatment of per-operative pain in the setting of coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Solivo 375 mg + 20 mg</strong></p><p>The most common side effects are gastritis, erosive gastritis, dyspepsia, diarrhea, gastric ulcer, upper abdominal pain, and nausea.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The use of this tablet should be avoided during pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised in the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Fatal cardiovascular thrombotic events like myocardial infarction, stroke, hypertension, congestive heart failure, and edema.</li><li>Serious GI adverse events.</li><li>Hypomagnesaemia.</li><li>Renal or hepatic insufficiency.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1140_1742758442.webp', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:34:02', '2025-05-04 21:17:04'),
(4362, 'Xylomet Plus', 'Sodium Cromoglicate + Xylometazoline Hydrochloride', 110.00, 1, 'x 120 metered sprays', '120 metered sprays', 'Nasal Spray', '(2.6 mg+0.0325 mg)/spray', 'no', 'no', '<p>Sure! Here\'s the text with the heading in normal bold:</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Indications of Xylomet Plus Spray</strong></p><p>This Nasal Spray is indicated for the prophylaxis and treatment of allergic rhinitis (such as hay fever and perennial rhinitis) where this is accompanied by nasal congestion.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Mast Cell Stabiliser</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Xylometazoline Hydrochloride is a sympathomimetic agent with alpha-adrenergic activity. It produces vasoconstriction thus reducing nasal congestion. This enables patients suffering from colds to breathe more easily through the nose. Sodium Cromoglicate usually acts through a local effect on the nasal mucosa. It prevents the release of the mediators of type-I allergic reactions, including histamine and slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis (SRS-A) from sensitized mast cells after the antigen-antibody union has taken place. The drug does not inhibit the binding of IgE and the specific antigen; instead, it suppresses the release of substances (e.g., histamine, SRS-A) in response to this reaction. The drug also inhibits Type-III (late allergic, Arther’s) reactions to a lesser extent.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Xylomet Plus Spray</strong></p><p>Adults (including the elderly) and children: One spray each nostril four times daily.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Xylomet Plus Spray</strong></p><p>Adults (including the elderly) and children: One spray each nostril four times daily.</p><p><strong>Administration of Xylomet Plus Spray</strong></p><p>How to use the Nasal Spray: Shake the bottle gently and remove the dust cover. Hold the spray with your forefinger and middle finger on either side of the nozzle and your thumb underneath the bottle. Press down until a fine spray appears. If using for the first time or if you have not used it for a week or more, press the nasal applicator several times until a fine mist comes out from the container. Gently blow the nose to clear the nostrils. Close one nostril and carefully insert the nasal applicator into the open nostril. Tilt your head forward slightly and keep the spray upright. Breathe in through your nose and, while breathing in, press the white collar of the nasal applicator firmly down once to release a spray. Breathe out through your mouth. Repeat the above steps in the same/other nostril for consecutive doses.</p><p><strong>Cleaning</strong></p><p>The nasal spray should be cleaned at least once a week. The procedures are as follows:</p><ul><li>Remove the dust cover.</li><li>Gently pull off the nasal applicator.</li><li>Wash the applicator and dust cover in warm water.</li><li>Shake off the excess water and leave to dry in a normal place. Avoid applying additional heat.</li><li>Gently push the applicator back on the top of the bottle and re-fix the dust cover.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to any ingredients of the preparation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Xylomet Plus Spray</strong></p><p>No serious side effects have been reported. Occasional irritation of the nasal mucosa may occur during the first day of use. In rare cases, with Sodium Cromoglicate solutions alone, wheezing and tightness of the chest have been reported. Because a lower dose of Xylometazoline is employed in this preparation, it causes mild side effects such as nasal irritation, dryness of the nose, sneezing, headache, insomnia, drowsiness, and palpitations.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>As with all medicines, caution should be exercised during pregnancy, especially during the first trimester.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The prolonged use or abuse of decongestants in general may lead to rebound congestion or drug-induced rhinitis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Mast Cell Stabiliser</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Xylometazoline Hydrochloride is a sympathomimetic agent with alpha-adrenergic activity. It produces vasoconstriction thus reducing nasal congestion. This enables patients suffering from colds to breathe more easily through the nose. Sodium Cromoglicate usually acts through a local effect on the nasal mucosa. It prevents the release of the mediators of type-I allergic reactions, including histamine and slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis (SRS-A) from sensitized mast cells after the antigen-antibody union has taken place. The drug does not inhibit the binding of IgE and the specific antigen; instead, it suppresses the release of substances (e.g., histamine, SRS-A) in response to this reaction. The drug also inhibits Type-III (late allergic, Arther’s) reactions to a lesser extent.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>As with all medicines, caution should be exercised during pregnancy, especially during the first trimester.</p><p>Let me know if you need anything else!</p>', 'active', '800_1742758564.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:36:04', '2025-05-05 22:09:42'),
(4363, 'Vitalgin', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral [A-Z syrup preparation]', 190.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '0 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '155_1742758668.jpg', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:37:48', '2025-05-04 23:03:23');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4364, 'Protide', 'Salmeterol + Fluticasone Propionate', 17.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50 mcg+500 mcg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Protide 50 mcg + 250 mcg</strong></p><p>This combination product is indicated in the regular treatment of asthma where the use of a combination of a long-acting β2-agonist and an inhaled corticosteroid is appropriate. It is used in patients who are not adequately controlled with inhaled corticosteroids and \"as needed\" inhaled short-acting β2-agonists or patients who are already adequately controlled on both inhaled corticosteroids and long-acting β2-agonists.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants</li><li>Respiratory corticosteroids</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate</strong>: A selective, long-acting beta-2 agonist used in the treatment of asthma and other forms of diffuse airways obstruction.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: A corticosteroid with mainly glucocorticoid activity. It has a potent anti-inflammatory effect in the lungs, reducing symptoms and preventing exacerbations without the systemic side effects typically seen with corticosteroids.</li><li><strong>Combination Therapy</strong>: Salmeterol provides protection against symptoms, while Fluticasone improves lung function and prevents exacerbations, offering a more convenient therapy for patients already on both β-agonists and inhaled corticosteroids.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Inhalation Aerosol</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults &amp; Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 125 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 250 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4-12 years)</strong>: 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily.</li><li><strong>Inhalation Powder in Capsule (for Asthma)</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults &amp; Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg or 250 µg twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum recommended dosage: Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 500 µg twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric (4-11 years)</strong>: Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg twice daily (morning and evening, approximately 12 hours apart).</li><li><strong>Inhalation Powder in Maxhaler (for Asthma)</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults &amp; Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">50/100 Maxhaler: One inhalation twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">50/250 Maxhaler: One inhalation twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">50/500 Maxhaler: One inhalation twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4 years and older)</strong>: 50/100 Maxhaler: One inhalation twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration</strong></p><p>To use the inhaler properly, follow these steps:</p><ol><li>Shake the inhaler well (at least six times).</li><li>Exhale fully and hold the inhaler upright.</li><li>Place the mouthpiece between your teeth and close lips around it.</li><li>While breathing in deeply, press the canister to release the medicine.</li><li>Hold your breath for at least 10 seconds.</li><li>If more than one puff is prescribed, wait 1 minute between puffs.</li><li>After use, rinse your mouth with water.</li></ol><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>Be cautious when co-administering with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole, ritonavir), as this can increase the systemic exposure to Fluticasone Propionate.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to any ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol</strong>: Tremor, palpitations, headache, arrhythmia, muscle cramps, oropharyngeal irritation.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: Hoarseness, candidiasis (thrush), cutaneous hypersensitivity reactions, and facial oropharyngeal oedema. Gargling after use may alleviate some side effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Administer during pregnancy and lactation only if the expected benefit to the mother outweighs the possible risk to the fetus or child. There is insufficient data on its use during pregnancy or breastfeeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with pulmonary tuberculosis.</li><li>Care should be taken in patients with thyrotoxicosis or infections.</li><li>Long-term corticosteroid use in pediatric patients may cause growth suppression.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C.</li><li>Keep away from heat and sunlight.</li><li>Do not puncture, break, or incinerate the canister. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>No dose adjustment needed for elderly or renal impairment patients.</li><li>No data for use in patients with hepatic impairment.</li><li>Corticosteroids may reduce growth velocity in pediatric patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants</li><li>Respiratory corticosteroids</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol</strong>: Selective, long-acting beta-2 adrenoceptor agonist that binds to the exo-site of the receptor.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: Potent glucocorticoid anti-inflammatory action within the lungs, reducing symptoms and exacerbations of asthma.</li></ul>', 'active', '293_1742758827.webp', 8, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:40:27', '2025-05-04 10:36:54'),
(4365, 'Naxin Plus', 'Naproxen Sodium + Esomeprazole Magnesium', 12.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '369_1742759569.png', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:52:49', '2025-05-04 00:31:27'),
(4366, 'Alneed Plus', 'Iron + Folic Acid + Vitamin B Complex + Vitamine C + Zinc Sulfate', 3.50, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Alneed Plus</strong></h3><p>This is indicated for the treatment and prophylaxis of Zinc, Iron, Folic Acid, B-vitamins, and Vitamin-C deficiency, especially during pregnancy and lactation.</p><h3><strong>Composition</strong></h3><p>Each capsule contains:</p><ul><li><strong>Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate</strong>: 61.8 mg</li><li><strong>Dried Ferrous Sulphate</strong>: 150 mg</li><li><strong>Folic Acid (Vitamin-B9)</strong>: 0.50 mg</li><li><strong>Thiamine Mononitrate (Vitamin-B1)</strong>: 2 mg</li><li><strong>Riboflavin (Vitamin-B2)</strong>: 2 mg</li><li><strong>Nicotinamide (Vitamin-B3)</strong>: 10 mg</li><li><strong>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride (Vitamin-B6)</strong>: 1 mg</li><li><strong>Ascorbic Acid (Vitamin-C)</strong>: 50 mg</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><p>Iron &amp; Vitamin Combined Preparations</p><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Zinc sulfate</strong>: Precipitates protein and is responsible for its astringent and weak antiseptic activity. It also has mild vasodilatory effects and can be used systemically as a zinc supplement.</li><li><strong>Iron</strong>: Essential for the production of red blood cells, which carry oxygen throughout the body. Iron deficiency can lead to anaemia.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong>: Important in the synthesis and maintenance of nucleoproteins in erythropoiesis and promotes WBC and platelet production.</li><li><strong>Nicotinamide (Vitamin B3)</strong>: Incorporated into coenzymes NAD and NADP, which play roles in multiple cellular metabolic pathways.</li><li><strong>Vitamin C</strong>: Necessary for collagen formation, tissue repair, and the synthesis of certain hormones and neurotransmitters.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B Complex</strong>: Vital for maintaining cell health and providing energy, with each type of B vitamin having its specific role.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Alneed Plus</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Recommended Dose</strong>: One capsule daily.</li><li>In more severe cases, 2 capsules a day may be required or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>This product is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Alneed Plus</strong></h3><ul><li>Generally well tolerated. However, allergic reactions may occur.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>Recommended.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Caution in patients who may develop iron overload (e.g., those with haemochromatosis, haemolytic anaemia, or red cell aplasia).</li><li>Iron can interfere with the absorption of tetracycline.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Alneed Plus</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Iron overdose</strong>: A leading cause of fatal poisoning in children under 6. Symptoms include bloody diarrhea, vomiting, acidosis, dark stools, and abdominal pain.</li><li><strong>Riboflavin</strong>: No toxic symptoms reported.</li><li><strong>Nicotinamide</strong>: Overdose may cause vomiting and diarrhea.</li><li><strong>Pyridoxine</strong>: Long-term excessive doses (over 200 mg) may cause sensory neuropathy.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong>: No known overdose cases reported.</li><li><strong>Ascorbic Acid</strong>: Acute ingestion is unlikely to cause significant effects.</li><li><strong>Zinc Toxicity</strong>: Can occur in both acute and chronic forms, with doses over 150 mg/day potentially causing low copper status, altered iron function, and immune issues.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a dry place below 25°C. Protect from light.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><p>Iron &amp; Vitamin Combined Preparations</p><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Zinc sulfate</strong>: Precipitates protein, leading to weak antiseptic activity and mild vasodilation.</li><li><strong>Iron</strong>: Supports the formation of red blood cells.</li><li><strong>Folic Acid</strong>: Involved in coenzyme production, particularly for purine and pyrimidine synthesis.</li><li><strong>Nicotinamide</strong>: Essential for the synthesis of coenzymes involved in cellular metabolism.</li><li><strong>Vitamin C</strong>: Supports collagen formation and tissue repair.</li><li><strong>Vitamin B Complex</strong>: Vital for cell health and energy metabolism.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h3><ul><li>Recommended.</li></ul>', 'active', '1841_1742759680.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:54:40', '2025-05-02 23:01:22'),
(4367, 'Famomax', 'Famotidine 40 mg', 5.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here’s the text you provided, formatted in a clearer and more concise manner with headings in bold:</p><p><br></p><h3>Indications of Famomax 40 mg</h3><p>Famomax 40 mg is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Gastric ulcer</li><li>Duodenal ulcer</li><li>Anastomotic ulcer</li><li>Acute stress ulcer</li><li>Reflux esophagitis</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li></ul><p>Famomax 40 mg is also indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Acute gastritis</li><li>Chronic gastritis in the acute exacerbation stage</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>H2 receptor antagonist</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Famomax 40 mg is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist. It completely inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors of parietal cells. This inhibits basal, overnight, and pentagastrin-stimulated gastric acid secretion. The H2-receptor antagonist activity of Famomax 40 mg is slowly reversible, as the drug dissociates slowly from the H2-receptor.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer</strong>: 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks</li><li><strong>Benign gastric ulcer</strong>: 40 mg at night for 4 to 8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance therapy: 20 mg at night for preventing the recurrence of duodenal ulceration</li><li><strong>Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GERD)</strong>: 20 mg twice daily for 6 to 12 weeks</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: Starting dose of 20 mg every 6 hours, adjusted based on individual response. Doses up to 160 mg every 6 hours may be used.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage for Adults:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gastric ulcer, duodenal ulcer, anastomotic ulcer, upper gastrointestinal hemorrhage, reflux esophagitis, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Famomax 40 mg 20 mg twice daily (after breakfast and supper, or before bedtime) or 40 mg once daily at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Acute gastritis, chronic gastritis (acute exacerbation)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Famomax 40 mg 20 mg twice daily (after breakfast and supper, or before bedtime). Alternatively, 40 mg once a day before bedtime.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance therapy: 20 mg once daily at bedtime.</li></ul><p><strong>Powder for Suspension:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>GERD (1-16 years)</strong>: 1 mg/kg/day, divided twice daily, up to 40 mg twice a day.</li><li><strong>Peptic ulcer, duodenal ulcer, reflux esophagitis, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>: Doses should be adjusted based on age and symptoms.</li></ul><p><strong>Intravenous Injection:</strong></p><ul><li>For hospitalized patients with hypersecretory conditions or intractable ulcers, or those unable to take oral medication, Famomax 40 mg injection may be administered.</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 20 mg intravenously every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients (1-16 years)</strong>: 0.25 mg/kg intravenously (over at least 2 minutes or as a 15-minute infusion) every 12 hours, up to 40 mg/day.</li></ul><h3>Interaction</h3><p>No clinically significant drug interactions have been identified. Famomax 40 mg does not interact with the cytochrome P450-linked drug-metabolizing enzyme system.</p><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to any component of the drug.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li>Eruption</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Dry mouth</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Tachycardia</li><li>High blood pressure</li><li>Headache</li><li>Drowsiness or insomnia (rare)</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Famomax 40 mg is known to cross the placenta. It should only be prescribed if clearly needed, as there are no adequate, well-controlled studies in pregnant women.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is not known whether Famomax 40 mg is secreted into human milk. Nursing mothers should either stop nursing or discontinue the drug.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>The drug should be used in the minimum required amount depending on the disease condition.</li><li>Careful administration is advised for elderly patients, and those with renal or hepatic disorders.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Famomax 40 mg is a histamine H2-receptor antagonist. It inhibits the action of histamine on H2-receptors in parietal cells, leading to a reduction in gastric acid secretion. This activity is slowly reversible due to the slow dissociation from the H2-receptor.</p><h3>Pregnancy Category</h3><ul><li>Category B: Famomax 40 mg should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Caution should be exercised when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><p>Let me know if you need more information or further adjustments!</p>', 'active', '654_1742759850.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:57:30', '2025-05-02 17:26:10'),
(4368, 'Progavi', 'Sodium Alginate + Sodium Bicarbonate + Calcium Carbonate', 300.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '(500 mg+213 mg+325 mg)/10 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Progavi 200 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Indications:</strong> Progavi 200 ml is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Gastric reflux</li><li>Heartburn</li><li>Flatulence associated with gastric reflux</li><li>Heartburn during pregnancy</li><li>Epigastric and retrosternal discomfort where the underlying cause is gastric reflux</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Antacids</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> The mode of action of Progavi 200 ml is physical and does not rely on absorption into the systemic circulation. Upon ingestion, it rapidly reacts with gastric acid, forming a protective Alginic acid gel raft with a nearly neutral pH. This raft floats on the stomach contents, effectively preventing gastro-esophageal reflux for up to 4 hours. In severe cases, the raft may reflux into the esophagus, providing a soothing effect instead of the stomach contents.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children over 12 years:</strong> Take 10-20 ml after meals and at bedtime, up to four times a day.</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 12 years:</strong> Take 5-10 ml after meals and at bedtime, up to four times a day.</li><li><strong>Children under 6 years:</strong> Not recommended.</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> No dosage adjustment is needed.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No dosage modification necessary.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong> Use caution if a highly restricted salt diet is necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction:</strong> A time interval of 2 hours should be observed between taking this medication and other medicinal products, particularly:</p><ul><li>Tetracyclines</li><li>Digoxin</li><li>Fluoroquinolones</li><li>Iron salts</li><li>Ketoconazole</li><li>Neuroleptics</li><li>Thyroid hormones</li><li>Penicillamine</li><li>Beta-blockers (atenolol, metoprolol, propranolol)</li><li>Glucocorticoids</li><li>Chloroquine</li><li>Bisphosphonates (diphosphonates)</li><li>Estramustine</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong> Progavi 200 ml should not be used in patients with a known or suspected hypersensitivity to the active ingredients or any of the excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong> Possible side effects include:</p><ul><li>Constipation</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Stomach cramps</li><li>Belching</li><li>If excessive doses are taken, abdominal distension may occur. If these symptoms occur, consult a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Progavi 200 ml is considered safe during pregnancy when clinically necessary. No evidence of malformation or fetal/neonatal toxicity has been observed in clinical studies.</li><li><strong>Breastfeeding:</strong> Considered safe for use during breastfeeding with no adverse effects noted on breastfed infants.</li><li><strong>Fertility:</strong> No negative effects on fertility have been observed in pre-clinical investigations or clinical data.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>If symptoms do not improve within 7 days, reassess the clinical condition.</li><li>Each 10 ml dose contains 141 mg of sodium (equivalent to 6.2 mmol), which should be considered in patients on a restricted salt diet.</li><li>Each 10 ml dose contains 160 mg of calcium carbonate (equivalent to 1.6 mmol), requiring caution in patients with hypercalcemia, nephrocalcinosis, or recurrent calcium-containing renal calculi.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong> In case of overdose, symptomatic treatment should be given. Patients may experience abdominal distension.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong> Store in a cool (below 30°C), dry place, away from light. Keep out of the reach of children. Do not refrigerate or freeze.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Paediatric Use:</strong> Not recommended for children under 6 years.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> No dose modification required for elderly patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Antacids</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Progavi 200 ml works through a physical mechanism, combining calcium carbonate and sodium bicarbonate with alginate. Upon ingestion, it reacts with gastric acid to form a protective gel raft that floats on the stomach contents, preventing reflux and providing up to 4 hours of relief. The raft can trap bile and pepsin, further protecting the esophagus. Calcium carbonate neutralizes gastric acid, while sodium bicarbonate enhances this neutralizing effect.</p>', 'active', '472_1742759960.webp', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 00:59:20', '2025-05-04 10:31:30'),
(4369, 'Sudokof', 'Dextromethorphan + Phenylephrine + Triprolidine', 110.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '(20 mg+10 mg+2.5 mg)/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sudokof 100 ml</strong></p><p>This preparation temporarily relieves these symptoms due to common cold, hay fever (allergic rhinitis), or other upper respiratory allergies:</p><ul><li>Non-productive cough due to minor throat or bronchial irritation</li><li>Runny Nose</li><li>Sneezing</li><li>Itching of the nose or throat</li><li>Itchy &amp; watery eyes</li><li>Nasal congestion</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each 5 ml syrup contains:</p><ul><li>Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide BP 20 mg</li><li>Phenylephrine Hydrochloride BP 10 mg</li><li>Triprolidine Hydrochloride BP 2.5 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Combined Cough Suppressants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dextromethorphan</strong>: A centrally-acting antitussive agent that depresses the medullary cough center through sigma receptor stimulation, resulting in decreased sensitivity of cough receptors and interruption of cough impulse transmission.</li><li><strong>Phenylephrine</strong>: A decongestant that stimulates α-adrenergic receptors, causing vasoconstriction in the respiratory mucosa, and also stimulates β-adrenergic receptors, which leads to bronchial relaxation, increased heart rate, and contractility.</li><li><strong>Triprolidine</strong>: An antihistamine that binds to the histamine H1 receptor, blocking the action of endogenous histamine and leading to temporary relief of histamine-induced symptoms.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 2 teaspoons three times daily</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Over 12 years: 2 teaspoons three times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6 to 12 years: 1 teaspoon three times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 to 5 years: ½ teaspoon three times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6 months to 2 years: ¼ teaspoon three times daily (or as directed by a physician)</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Sudokof 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years</strong>: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) 4 times a day, every 4-hour interval</li><li><strong>6-12 years</strong>: ½ teaspoonful 4 times a day, every 4-hour interval</li><li><strong>Use in children and adolescents</strong>: A physician\'s advice should be obtained before administering this combination to children less than 6 years</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sudokof 100 ml</strong></p><p>Taking certain MAO inhibitors with this medication may cause a serious (possibly fatal) drug interaction. Avoid taking isocarboxazid, methylene blue, moclobemide, phenelzine, procarbazine, rasagiline, selegiline, or tranylcypromine during treatment with this medication. Most MAO inhibitors should also not be taken for two weeks before treatment with this medication.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It should be avoided in patients with liver disease or asthmatic patients and is contraindicated in patients taking monoamine oxidase inhibitors or within 2 weeks from stopping such treatment.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sudokof 100 ml</strong></p><p>It may cause drowsiness and constipation. Other side effects that may occur include gastrointestinal discomfort. No apparent evidence of physical dependence of the morphine type.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>There are no specific data on the use of this combination during pregnancy. Caution should therefore be exercised by balancing the potential benefits of treatment for the mother against any possible hazards to the developing fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>This preparation may cause drowsiness. If affected, do not drive a motor vehicle or operate machinery.</li><li>Avoid alcoholic drinks while on this medication.</li><li>Use with caution in patients with epilepsy, prostatic hypertrophy, glaucoma, hepatic disease, hypertension, heart disease, diabetes, or hyperthyroidism.</li><li>If symptoms do not improve within one week or are accompanied by high fever, consult a physician before continuing use.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sudokof 100 ml</strong></p><p>In cases of overdose, hospital admission is strongly advised. Overdose may produce respiratory depression, paranoid psychosis, delusions, hallucinations, and convulsions.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place (below 30°C), away from light. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Combined Cough Suppressants</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This preparation is a mixture of antitussive, decongestant, and antihistamine agents.</p><ul><li><strong>Dextromethorphan</strong>: A safe, effective, non-narcotic antitussive agent with central action on the cough center in the medulla. It has no analgesic or habit-forming properties and little sedative activity.</li><li><strong>Phenylephrine</strong>: A decongestant that shrinks blood vessels in the nasal passage, providing temporary relief of stuffy nose, sinus, and ear symptoms caused by the common cold, flu, allergies, sinusitis, and bronchitis.</li><li><strong>Triprolidine</strong>: An antihistamine that relieves symptoms of allergy, hay fever, and the common cold, including watery eyes, itchy eyes/nose/throat/skin, cough, runny nose, and sneezing.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There are no specific data on the use of this combination during pregnancy or lactation.</p>', 'active', '1770_1742760135.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:02:15', '2025-05-04 21:25:24'),
(4370, 'Rupatrol', 'Rupatadine Fumarate', 12.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information with headings in bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Rupatrol 10 mg</strong></p><p>Rupatrol 10 mg is indicated for the symptomatic treatment of Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis and Urticaria.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Rupatrol 10 mg is a second generation, non-sedating, long-acting histamine antagonist with selective peripheral H1 receptor antagonist activity. It further blocks the receptors of the platelet-activating factor (PAF) according to in vitro and in vivo studies. Rupatadine possesses anti-allergic properties such as the inhibition of the degranulation of mast cells induced by immunological and non-immunological stimuli, and inhibition of the release of cytokines, particularly of the tumor necrosis factors (TNF) in human mast cells and monocytes.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rupatrol 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Rupatadine Tablet</strong>: The recommended dosage is 10 mg once daily, with or without food.</li><li><strong>Rupatadine Oral Solution (Children aged 2 to 11 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing 25 kg or more: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) of oral solution once daily with or without food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing equal or more than 10 kg to less than 25 kg: 1/2 teaspoonful (2.5 ml) oral solution once daily with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Rupatrol 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (over 12 years)</strong>: The recommended dose is 10 mg once a day. Rupatadine may be taken with or without food.</li><li><strong>Children aged 2 to 11 years</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing 25 kg or more: 1 teaspoonful (5 ml) of the oral solution once daily with or without food.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children weighing equal or more than 10 kg to less than 25 kg: 1/2 teaspoonful (2.5 ml) oral solution once daily with or without food.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Rupatrol 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With medicine</strong>: The concomitant administration of Rupatadine 20 mg and ketoconazole or erythromycin increases the systemic exposure. Rupatadine should be used with caution when it is administered concomitantly with these drug substances and other inhibitors of the isozyme CYP3A4. Rupatadine should be used with caution when it is co-administered with statins, CNS depressants, or alcohol.</li><li><strong>With food</strong>: Grapefruit and Grapefruit juice should not be taken simultaneously with Rupatadine.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Hypersensitivity to Rupatadine or to any of the excipients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Rupatrol 10 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Asthenia, dizziness, drowsiness.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Appetite increased, arthralgia, back pain, concentration impaired, constipation, cough, diarrhea, dry throat, epistaxis, fever, gastrointestinal discomfort, increased risk of infection, irritability, malaise, myalgia, nasal dryness, nausea, oropharyngeal pain, rash, thirst, vomiting, weight increased.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Palpitations, tachycardia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category B2 (by TGA, Australia). There is no clinical data available on the exposure of Rupatadine during pregnancy. Pregnant women should therefore not use Rupatadine unless the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk for the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: No information is available on whether Rupatadine is excreted in the mother\'s milk. Therefore, it should not be used during lactation unless the potential benefits for the mother justify the potential risk to the infant.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Rupatrol 10 mg should be used with caution in patients with known prolongation of the QT interval, patients with uncorrected hypokalemia, and patients with ongoing proarrhythmic conditions, such as clinically significant bradycardia or acute myocardial ischemia. Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly patients (65 years and older). As there is no clinical experience in patients with impaired kidney or liver function, the use of Rupatadine 10 mg tablets is at present not recommended in these patients.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Rupatrol 10 mg</strong></p><p>The most common adverse reaction was somnolence. If accidental ingestion of very high doses occurs, symptomatic treatment together with the required supportive measures should be given.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool &amp; dry place below 30°C, protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly. No information is available that indicates the requirement of any dose adjustment in this population.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Neither the safety nor the efficacy of Rupatadine has been established in patients less than 12 years of age.</li><li><strong>Patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency</strong>: Use of Rupatadine is not recommended in patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency, as no relevant clinical data is available.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Rupatrol 10 mg is a long-acting, non-sedative antagonist of histamine H1-receptors. It also antagonizes the platelet activating factor (PAF). Both histamine and PAF cause bronchoconstriction, which leads to an increase in vascular permeability and act as mediators in the inflammatory process. With the dual mode of action, Rupatadine shows better therapeutic effect than an isolated antihistamine. Rupatadine possesses other anti-allergic properties such as the inhibition of the degranulation of mast cells induced by immunological and non-immunological stimuli and inhibition of the release of cytokines, particularly of the tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF α) in human mastocytes and monocytes.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>There is no clinical data available on the exposure of Rupatadine during pregnancy. Pregnant women should therefore not use Rupatadine unless the potential benefit outweighs the potential risk for the infant. No information is available on whether Rupatadine is excreted in the mother\'s milk. Therefore, it should not be used during lactation unless the potential benefits for the mother justify the potential risk to the infant.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: Rupatadine should be used with caution in elderly. No information is available that indicates the requirement of any dose adjustment in this population.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Neither the safety nor the efficacy of Rupatadine has been established in patients less than 12 years of age.</li><li><strong>Patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency</strong>: Use of Rupatadine is not recommended in patients with renal or hepatic insufficiency. As no relevant clinical data is available.</li></ul>', 'active', '1376_1742760217.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:03:37', '2025-05-04 20:35:52'),
(4371, 'M-Kast', 'Montelukast Sodium', 8.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Sure! Here\'s the text with the headings in bold, as requested:</p><p><strong>Indications of M-Kast 5 mg</strong></p><p>M-Kast 5 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Prophylaxis and chronic treatment of asthma</li><li>Acute prevention of Exercise-Induced Bronchoconstriction (EIB)</li><li>Relief of symptoms of Allergic Rhinitis (AR): Seasonal &amp; Perennial Allergic Rhinitis</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antibodies to leukotriene receptors</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. Cysteinyl leukotriene receptors (CysLT) are located in the human airway, and these eicosanoids bind to them. The orally active drug M-Kast 5 mg has a strong affinity and specificity for the CysLT1 receptor. Without any agonist activity, it inhibits LTD4\'s physiological effects at the CysLT1 receptor.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of M-Kast 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</strong> The dosage for adults and adolescents 15 years of age and older: Monas 10 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric patients with asthma or seasonal allergic rhinitis:</strong>The dosage for pediatric patients 6 to 14 years of age: Monas 5 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 2 years to 5 years of age: Monas 4 mg tablet once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dosage for pediatric patients 6 months to 5 years of age: Monas 4 mg oral granules once daily. This can be administered either directly in the mouth or mixed with a spoonful of cold water or soft food at room temperature.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in the pediatric patient:</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Monas have been established in adequate and well-controlled studies in pediatric patients with asthma 6 months to 14 years of age. Safety and efficacy profiles in this age group are similar to those seen in adults.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency:</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is required in patients with mild-to-moderate hepatic insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Renal Insufficiency:</strong></p><p>No dosage adjustment is recommended in patients with renal insufficiency.</p><p><strong>Elderly use:</strong></p><p>The pharmacokinetic profile and the oral bioavailability of a single 10-mg oral dose of Monas are similar in elderly and younger adults. The plasma half-life of Monas is slightly longer in the elderly. No dosage adjustment in the elderly is required.</p><p><strong>Interaction of M-Kast 5 mg</strong></p><p>In the prevention and long-term treatment of asthma, M-Kast 5 mg has been provided alongside other commonly used treatments without seeming to increase the risk of negative side effects. According to studies on drug interactions, theophylline, prednisone, prednisolone, oral contraceptives (norethindrone 1mg/ethinyl estradiol 35mcg), terfenadine, digoxin, and warfarin did not exhibit any clinically significant effects on the pharmacokinetics at the recommended clinical dose of it. In clinical investigations, the medication was used concurrently with a variety of routinely prescribed medications without showing any signs of clinically harmful interactions, despite the fact that more detailed interaction studies were not carried out. Thyroid hormones, hypnotic sedatives, benzodiazepines, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and decongestants were among these drugs. Following a single dose of this solution, the hepatic metabolism-inducing drug phenobarbital reduced the drug\'s AUC by about 40%. For this medication, no dosage modification is advised. When powerful cytochrome P450 enzyme inducers, such as phenobarbital or rifampin, are provided alongside this solution, it is fair to use proper clinical monitoring.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those who have an extreme sensitivity to any ingredient in this medication should not take it.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of M-Kast 5 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong> upper respiratory tract infection, diarrhea, fever, gastrointestinal pain, headache, nausea, and vomiting.</li><li><strong>Uncommon:</strong> akathisia, anxiety, arthralgia, asthenia, aberrant behavior, depression, dizziness, drowsiness, dry mouth, hemorrhage, irritability, malaise, muscle complaints, oedema, seizure, odd feeling, and sleep disturbances.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong> Pulmonary eosinophilia, angioedema, decreased attention, disorientation, erythema nodosum, hallucinations, hepatic problems, memory loss, palpitations, suicidal thoughts, and tremor.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>M-Kast 5 mg is classified as pregnancy category B. Although it has been demonstrated that the medication crosses the placenta of pregnant rats and rabbits, there are no records of its use in pregnant humans. Although there is some evidence that it is also secreted in breast milk, little is known about the implications of this discovery. Before starting the therapy in nursing moms, caution should be exercised.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>The reversal of bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks, including status asthmaticus, is not recommended as usage for M-Kast 5 mg. Patients should be told to have access to the proper rescue medication. Asthma exacerbations that are acute can still be treated with the solution. While the dosage of an inhaled corticosteroid may be gradually decreased under medical supervision, the use of this solution instead of an inhaled or oral corticosteroid should not be started suddenly. Exercise-induced bronchospasm should not be treated and managed with a single dose of it. While taking it, patients with documented aspirin sensitivity should continue to refrain from taking aspirin or non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Although aspirin sensitivity in asthmatics is a known side effect of the drug, it has not been demonstrated to shorten the bronchoconstrictor response to aspirin and other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs).</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of M-Kast 5 mg</strong></p><p>The majority of overdose reports did not include any negative experiences. The most frequent side effects, which were in line with M-Kast 5 mg\'s safety profile, were nausea, vomiting, psychomotor hyperactivity, thirst, headache, and stomach pain. In the case of an overdose, it is permissible to take the customary supportive measures, such as removing any unabsorbed material from the digestive tract, using clinical monitoring, and if necessary, instituting supportive therapy.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a cool, dry environment while keeping out light and moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Leukotriene receptor antagonists</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>The cysteinyl leukotriene receptor is inhibited by the selective and orally active leukotriene receptor antagonist known as M-Kast 5 mg (CysLT1). The arachidonic acid metabolism results in the production of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) from a variety of cells, including mast cells and eosinophils. The pathophysiology of asthma and allergic rhinitis, including airway edema, smooth muscle contraction, and altered cellular activity associated with the inflammatory process, have been linked to cysteinyl leukotrienes and leukotriene receptor occupation. These factors all contribute to the signs and symptoms of asthma.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>After oral administration, M-Kast 5 mg passes the placenta in rats and rabbits. Nevertheless, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. Because the human reaction to medications is not usually predicted by research on animal reproduction, pregnant women should only use it if absolutely necessary. When giving the pill to a nursing mother, care should be taken because many medications are secreted in human milk.</p><p>Let me know if you need further assistance!</p>', 'active', '1181_1742760391.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:06:31', '2025-05-03 23:39:53'),
(4372, 'Doxofyl', 'Doxophylline', 5.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Doxofyl 200 mg</strong></p><p>Doxofyl 200 mg is used to treat the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Bronchial asthma</li><li>Bronchospasm</li><li>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</li><li>Pulmonary disease with a spastic bronchial component</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Doxofyl 200 mg is a novel bronchodilator that structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. It selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. Unlike Theophylline, Doxofyl 200 mg has decreased affinities toward adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, potentially accounting for its improved safety profile. It is also reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the generation of leukotriene production.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Doxofyl 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: 200 mg tablet two or three times daily</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 400 mg tablet two or three times daily or as prescribed by the physician</li><li><strong>Children &gt; 12 years of age</strong>: 10 ml syrup or 200 mg tablet two or three times daily</li><li><strong>Children 6-12 years of age</strong>: 6-9 mg/kg body weight two times daily (e.g., for a 10 kg child, 3 ml or 60 mg two times daily) or as prescribed by the physician</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Doxofyl 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Doxofyl 200 mg should not be administered with other xanthine derivatives. Toxic synergism with ephedrine has been documented for xanthines.</li><li>Concomitant therapy with troleandomycin, lincomycin, clindamycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, ranitidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccines may decrease hepatic clearance, leading to an increase in blood levels.</li></ul><p>No evidence of a relationship between Doxofyl 200 mg serum concentrations and toxic events has been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Doxofyl 200 mg is contraindicated in patients with acute myocardial infarction, hypotension, lactating women, and those with hypersensitivity to its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Doxofyl 200 mg</strong></p><p>Doxofyl 200 mg rarely causes serious side effects. However, possible side effects are similar to taking excess amounts of caffeine, including:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Headache</li><li>Upset stomach</li><li>Heartburn</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Animal reproduction studies indicate that Doxofyl 200 mg does not cause fetal harm when administered to pregnant animals. However, due to limited experience in pregnant humans, xanthines should only be given during pregnancy when clearly necessary.</li><li>Doxofyl 200 mg is contraindicated in nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>The half-life of xanthine derivatives may be prolonged in patients with liver disease, congestive heart failure, or those on certain medications (e.g., erythromycin, troleandomycin, lincomycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, propranolol, anti-flu vaccine). In such cases, a lower dose may be needed.</li><li>Phenytoin, anticonvulsants, and smoking may cause increased clearance, potentially requiring higher doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Doxofyl 200 mg</strong></p><p>In case of overdose, severe cardiac arrhythmias and tonic-clonic seizures may occur. These effects may represent the first signs of intoxication. If necessary, treatment can be resumed at lower doses after symptoms of toxicity subside. Symptomatic treatment of cardiovascular collapse should be initiated. There is no specific antidote.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li><li>Doxofyl 200 mg should be used only on the prescription of a specialist physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Bronchodilator, Methyl xanthine derivatives</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Doxofyl 200 mg is a novel bronchodilator that selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, thereby relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. Unlike Theophylline, Doxofyl 200 mg has decreased affinities for adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, possibly contributing to a better safety profile. Additionally, it inhibits platelet-activating factor (PAF) and the production of leukotrienes.</p>', 'active', '1255_1742760486.webp', 17, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:08:06', '2025-05-02 08:46:55');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4373, 'Ticas', 'Fluticasone Propionate', 90.61, 1, 'x 10 gm tube', '10 gm tube', 'Cream', '0.05%', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ticas 0.05%</strong></p><p>Ticas 0.05% is indicated for the relief of inflammatory and pruritic manifestations of corticosteroid-responsive eczema or dermatitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Fluticasone &amp; combined preparations topical</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Fluticasone propionate is a glucocorticoid with high topical anti-inflammatory potency but a low HPA-axis suppressive activity after dermal administration. It, therefore, has a therapeutic index greater than most commonly available steroids. Fluticasone propionate has a high degree of selectivity for the glucocorticoid receptor. In vitro studies show that Ticas 0.05% has a strong affinity for and agonist activity at human glucocorticoid receptors, which are responsible for the anti-inflammatory properties of glucocorticoids. Fluticasone propionate has weak affinity for the progesterone receptor, and virtually no affinity for the mineralocorticoid, estrogen, or androgen receptors. The therapeutic potency of glucocorticoids is related to the half-life of the glucocorticoid-receptor complex. The half-life of the Fluticasone propionate glucocorticoid-receptor complex is approximately 10 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ticas 0.05%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cream:</strong> Apply a thin layer of Fluticasone propionate cream to the affected skin areas once daily.</li><li><strong>Ointment:</strong> Apply a thin layer of Fluticasone propionate ointment to the affected skin areas twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Fluticasone propionate is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Rosacea</li><li>Acne vulgaris</li><li>Peri-oral dermatitis</li><li>Primary cutaneous viral infections (e.g., herpes simplex, chickenpox)</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients</li><li>Perianal and genital pruritus</li><li>Infected skin lesions caused by infection with fungi or bacteria</li><li>Dermatoses in children under one year of age (including dermatitis and napkin eruptions)</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ticas 0.05%</strong></p><p>Ticas 0.05% preparations are usually well tolerated. However, the following side effects have been reported:</p><ul><li>Local burning and pruritus</li><li>Hypersensitivity reactions (discontinue use immediately if signs appear)</li><li>Prolonged and intensive use may cause local atrophic changes in the skin, such as thinning, striae, dilatation of superficial blood vessels, hypertrichosis, and hypopigmentation</li><li>Secondary infections, particularly with occlusive dressings or skin folds</li><li>Allergic contact dermatitis</li><li>Exacerbation of dermatosis symptoms</li><li>Prolonged use of large amounts or extensive area application may cause systemic absorption, leading to hypercorticism, especially in infants and children (and if occlusive dressings are used)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Administration during pregnancy should only be considered if the expected benefit to the mother outweighs any potential risk to the fetus.</li><li>The excretion of Ticas 0.05% into human breast milk has not been studied. Plasma levels in patients following dermal application are likely to be low. When used in breastfeeding mothers, the therapeutic benefits must be weighed against potential hazards to both mother and baby.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Fluticasone propionate has a very low propensity for systemic absorption; however, prolonged high-dose application to large body surface areas, especially in infants and children, might lead to adrenal suppression.</li><li>Children may absorb more topical corticosteroids and be more susceptible to systemic toxicity.</li><li>The face is more likely to show atrophic changes after prolonged treatment with potent topical corticosteroids.</li><li>Appropriate antimicrobial therapy should be used when treating infected inflammatory lesions. Any spread of infection requires discontinuation of topical corticosteroids and systemic antimicrobial therapy.</li><li>Bacterial infections are encouraged by occlusive dressings, which promote warm, moist conditions. Skin should be cleansed before a fresh dressing is applied.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ticas 0.05%</strong></p><p>Acute overdosage is unlikely, but chronic misuse could lead to hypercorticism. If this occurs, treatment should be discontinued. Overdose via ingestion is extremely unlikely due to the very low oral bioavailability of Ticas 0.05%.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30˚C. Do not freeze.</p>', 'active', '29_1742760592.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:09:52', '2025-05-04 22:25:31'),
(4374, 'Pronex', 'Esomeprazole', 6.00, 14, 'tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Pronex 20 mg</strong></h3><p>Pronex 20 mg is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li><strong>To alleviate GERD-related symptoms</strong> such as persistent heartburn and others.</li><li><strong>To treat erosive esophagitis</strong> and aid in its healing.</li><li><strong>To maintain the healing</strong> of erosive esophagitis.</li><li><strong>For the eradication of Helicobacter pylori</strong> infection in individuals with duodenal ulcer disease, when used in conjunction with amoxicillin and clarithromycin.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>.</li><li><strong>Duodenal &amp; gastric ulcers</strong> caused by acid dyspepsia.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage of Pronex 20 mg</strong></h3><ol><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Most patients are healed within 4 to 8 weeks. For patients who don\'t heal, an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Maintenance of Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg once daily (Clinical studies did not extend 6 months).</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Helicobacter Pylori eradication</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li>Triple therapy for reducing the risk of duodenal ulcer recurrence:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily for 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">The dose is 20-80 mg once daily, adjusted individually. Treatment should continue as long as clinically indicated.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Acid-related Dyspepsia</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20-40 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks, based on response.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">20-40 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Injection</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">40 mg Pronex 20 mg once daily via intravenous injection (not less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Esomeprazole IV injection should not be administered with any other medication through the same intravenous site.</li></ul><h3><strong>Administration of Pronex 20 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Oral</strong>: Take one Pronex 20 mg pill or capsule <strong>one hour before a meal</strong> and consume it whole.</li><li><strong>Delayed-Release Oral Suspension</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Mix the contents of the packet in 15 ml of water, stir for 2-3 minutes to thicken, then stir again within 30 minutes and consume.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For nasogastric or gastric tube administration, mix in a syringe and wait for 2-3 minutes to thicken before giving.</li><li><strong>Intravenous</strong>: Administer gradually over a minimum of 3 minutes after reconstitution with 5 ml of solvent.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction of Pronex 20 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>CYP2C19 and CYP3A4</strong> are the main enzymes metabolizing esomeprazole in the liver.</li><li><strong>Inhibits CYP2C19</strong> and can affect drugs metabolized by it like <strong>diazepam</strong> (may increase its plasma levels).</li><li>May <strong>reduce absorption</strong> of medications significantly affected by gastrointestinal pH (e.g., <strong>ketoconazole</strong>, <strong>iron salts</strong>, <strong>digoxin</strong>).</li><li><strong>No significant interaction</strong> with medications like <strong>phenytoin</strong>, <strong>warfarin</strong>, and <strong>quinidine</strong>.</li><li><strong>Esomeprazole + Clarithromycin</strong>: Increased plasma levels of 14-hydroxy clarithromycin.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to esomeprazole or any of its components.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Pronex 20 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Headache, diarrhea, nausea, flatulence, abdominal pain, constipation, dry mouth.</li><li><strong>Long-term use</strong>: No significant differences in side effects between short-term and maintenance therapy.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Stomach Cancer</strong>: Positive symptom response does not rule out stomach cancer.</li><li><strong>Capsules</strong>: For those who have difficulty swallowing, open the capsule and mix the pellets with applesauce. Do not chew or crush the pellets.</li><li><strong>Antacids</strong>: May be used alongside esomeprazole.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects of Pronex 20 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Symptoms</strong>: Decreased motor activity, changes in breathing frequency, tremors, ataxia, clonic convulsions.</li><li><strong>Treatment</strong>: Symptomatic and supportive care. Dialysis is not effective due to protein binding.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>dry place</strong> at temperatures not exceeding <strong>30°C</strong>, and shield from moisture and light.</li><li>Keep out of <strong>children\'s reach</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</strong>: Inhibits H+/K+-ATPase in gastric parietal cells to reduce stomach acid output.</li><li><strong>Pharmacokinetics</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Absorbed rapidly with <strong>peak plasma levels</strong> occurring within 1.5 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Bioavailability</strong> increases with repeated doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Metabolized by <strong>CYP2C19</strong> and <strong>CYP3A4</strong>, and excreted primarily in the urine as inactive metabolites.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy</strong></h3><ul><li>No sufficient studies available for <strong>pregnant women</strong>. Use only if deemed necessary, and breastfeeding should be stopped if use is required.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric and Geriatric Use</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pediatric</strong>: Safety and efficacy not established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric</strong>: No significant difference in safety between elderly and younger individuals.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Insufficiency</strong>: No dosage change for mild to moderate hepatic insufficiency; for severe impairment, the dose should not exceed 20 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Renal Insufficiency</strong>: No significant pharmacokinetic changes expected in renal impairment.</li></ul><p>This concise guide covers the essential information regarding <strong>Pronex 20 mg</strong>. Always consult your healthcare provider before starting any new medication.</p>', 'active', '962_1742760708.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:11:48', '2025-05-04 10:33:08'),
(4375, 'Esonix M', 'Esomeprazole', 14.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Esonix M 40 mg</strong></p><p>Esonix M 40 mg is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Treatment of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</li><li>Healing of erosive esophagitis</li><li>Maintenance of healing of erosive esophagitis</li><li>Symptomatic Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</li><li>Reduction of NSAID-associated gastric ulcer</li><li>H. pylori eradication (Triple therapy)</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Esonix M 40 mg exerts its stomach acid-suppressing effects by preventing the final step in gastric acid production by covalently binding to sulfhydryl groups of cysteines found on the H+/K+-ATPase enzyme at the secretory surface of gastric parietal cells. This effect leads to inhibition of both basal and stimulated gastric acid secretion, irrespective of the stimulus. As the binding of Esonix M 40 mg to the H+/K+-ATPase enzyme is irreversible and new enzyme needs to be expressed to resume acid secretion, Esonix M 40 mg\'s duration of antisecretory effect persists longer than 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Esonix M 40 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet or Capsule</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Healing of erosive esophagitis: 20 mg or 40 mg once daily for 4 to 8 weeks. The majority of patients heal within 4 to 8 weeks. For patients who do not heal after 4-8 weeks, an additional 4-8 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance of healing of erosive esophagitis: 20 mg once daily. Controlled studies did not extend beyond six months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Symptomatic GERD: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks. If symptoms do not resolve completely after 4 weeks, an additional 4 weeks of treatment may be considered.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">H. pylori eradication (Triple therapy): Esonix M 40 mg 20 mg twice daily for 10 days, Amoxicillin 1000 mg twice daily for 10 days, and Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 10 days.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>:</li><li>The recommended adult dose in GERD with Erosive Esophagitis is either 20 or 40 mg Esonix M 40 mg given once daily by intravenous injection (no less than 3 minutes) or intravenous infusion (10 to 30 minutes).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Esonix M 40 mg</strong></p><p>A solution for intravenous infusion is prepared by first reconstituting the contents of one vial with 5 ml of 0.9% Sodium Chloride and further diluting the resulting solution to a final volume of 50 ml. The resultant concentration after diluting to a final volume of 50 ml is 0.8 mg/ml.</p><ul><li><strong>20 mg dose</strong>: Withdraw 25 ml of the final solution and administer as an intravenous infusion over 10 minutes to 30 minutes.</li><li><strong>10 mg dose</strong>: Withdraw 12.5 ml of the final solution and administer as an intravenous infusion over 10 minutes to 30 minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Esonix M 40 mg</strong></p><p>Esonix M 40 mg appears to be a selective inhibitor of the cytochrome P450 mono-oxygenase system, and there may be an effect on hepatic clearance, but there have been no reports to date of clinically relevant interactions. There is some uncertainty over the effect of Esonix M 40 mg on the oral combined contraceptive pill. Physiological changes similar to those found with omeprazole are likely to take place due to the reduction in gastric acid, which may influence bacterial colonization of the stomach and duodenum, and also vitamin B12 absorption.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Esonix M 40 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any other components of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Esonix M 40 mg</strong></p><p>Reported side effects include:</p><ul><li>Headache</li><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Abdominal pain</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Esonix M 40 mg is classified as US FDA pregnancy category C. It should be avoided during pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits outweigh the risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Exclude the possibility of malignancy when gastric ulcer is suspected and before treatment for dyspepsia.</li><li>When using in combination with antibiotics, refer to the prescribing information of the respective antibiotics.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p>Directions for reconstitution of solution:</p><ul><li>Solution for injection is prepared by adding 5 ml of 0.9% Sodium Chloride for intravenous injection into the vial containing the dry powder.</li><li>The reconstituted solution for injection is clear and colorless to very slightly yellow.</li></ul>', 'active', '1666_1742760807.png', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:13:27', '2025-05-02 12:36:25'),
(4376, 'Sanbur', 'Dexlansoprazole', 12.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '30 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sanbur 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: Sanbur 30 mg is indicated for the healing of all grades of erosive esophagitis (EE) for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: Sanbur 30 mg is indicated to maintain healing of EE and relief of heartburn for up to 6 months.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</strong>: Sanbur 30 mg is indicated for the treatment of heartburn associated with symptomatic non-erosive GERD for 4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Sanbur 30 mg delayed-release capsule is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI) that inhibits gastric acid secretion. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole, a racemic mixture of the R- and S-enantiomers. Sanbur is supplied as a Dual Delayed Release (DDR) formulation in a capsule for oral administration, containing two types of enteric-coated granules with different pH-dependent dissolution profiles. Sanbur suppresses gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell, blocking the final step of acid production.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Sanbur 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis and Relief of Heartburn</strong>: 30 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive GERD</strong>: 30 mg once daily for 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 60 mg once daily for up to 8 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Sanbur 30 mg</strong></p><p>Sanbur 30 mg can be taken without regard to food. The capsule should be swallowed whole, or the granules may be sprinkled on one tablespoon of food and swallowed immediately. Do not chew the granules. If a dose is missed, it should be taken as soon as possible, unless it is close to the time for the next dose; in that case, only the prescribed dose should be taken at the appointed time. A double dose should not be taken.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Sanbur 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With Medicine</strong>: Atazanavir, Warfarin, Tacrolimus, Clopidogrel, and Methotrexate.</li><li><strong>With Food &amp; Others</strong>: No data available.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Sanbur 30 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sanbur 30 mg</strong></p><p>Common side effects include diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and flatulence.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>There are no adequate or well-controlled studies in pregnant women with Sanbur 30 mg. It should only be used if the expected benefits outweigh the potential risks.</li><li>It is not known whether Sanbur 30 mg is excreted in human milk. However, it is excreted in the milk of rats, so it should not be used by nursing mothers unless considered necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Gastric Malignancy</li><li>Clostridium difficile-associated Diarrhea</li><li>Bone Fracture</li><li>Hypomagnesemia</li><li>Concomitant use with Methotrexate</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sanbur 30 mg</strong></p><p>There have been no reports of significant overdose. Multiple doses of 120 mg or a single dose of 300 mg did not result in death or severe adverse events.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a dry place, protected from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in patients below 12 years of age have not been established.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No adjustment for mild hepatic impairment. A maximum daily dose of 30 mg may be considered for moderate hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Sanbur 30 mg is a PPI that suppresses gastric acid secretion by inhibiting the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cells, blocking the final step of acid production. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole. The dual delayed-release formulation provides two peaks in plasma concentration over time.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Category B: There are no well-controlled studies in pregnant women.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>Safety and effectiveness in children under 12 years of age have not been established.</p><p><strong>Geriatric Use</strong></p><p>No dose adjustment necessary for elderly patients.</p><p><strong>Renal Impairment</strong></p><p>No dose adjustment necessary for renal impairment.</p><p><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong></p><p>No dose adjustment required for mild hepatic impairment. For moderate hepatic impairment, the maximum daily dose should be 30 mg.</p>', 'active', '930_1742760885.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:14:45', '2025-05-04 20:48:04'),
(4377, 'Ziliron-B', 'Iron Polymaltose Complex + Folic Acid + Zinc + Vitamin B-Complex', 6.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '0 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ziliron B</strong></p><p>Ziliron B is indicated for the treatment and prophylaxis of Zinc, Iron, Folic Acid, and B-vitamins deficiency, especially during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each capsule contains:</p><ul><li>Iron BP 47 mg (as Iron Polymaltose Complex 188 mg)</li><li>Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate USP 61.8 mg (eq. to 22.5 mg of elemental zinc)</li><li>Folic Acid BP 0.50 mg</li><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride USP 5 mg</li><li>Riboflavin BP 2 mg</li><li>Nicotinamide USP 20 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride BP 2 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Iron &amp; Vitamin Combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>In this preparation, Iron is present as Iron Polymaltose Complex, a novel Iron preparation containing nonionic Ferric Iron and Polymaltose in a stable complex. This facilitates a controlled absorption of the Iron when it comes in contact with the mucosal cell surface. Being non-ionic, Iron Polymaltose Complex is more stable than conventional Iron forms. Folic acid helps in the proper development of the baby, while Zinc supports enzymes and protein metabolism. Vitamins are essential for building tissue and energy release from food breakdown.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Standard Dosage</strong>: One capsule daily.</li><li><strong>Severe Cases</strong>: 2 capsules per day may be required or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>This product is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Generally well tolerated. However, a few allergic reactions may be seen.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in Pregnancy</strong>: The use of any drug during the first trimester of pregnancy should be avoided if possible. Administration of Iron during the first trimester requires evidence of Iron deficiency. Prophylaxis for Iron deficiency, where inadequate diet calls for supplementary Zinc and Folic Acid, is justified during the remainder of pregnancy.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Care should be taken in patients who may develop iron overload, such as those with haemochromatosis, haemolytic anaemia, or red cell aplasia. Iron chelates with tetracycline, which may impair absorption.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Accidental overdose of iron-containing products is a leading cause of fatal poisoning in children under 6 years old.</li><li>Avoid higher doses in individuals with liver disease or hemochromatosis; excess intake can cause symptoms like bloody diarrhea, vomiting, acidosis, darkened stools, and abdominal pain. These symptoms may clear in a few hours.</li><li>Riboflavin is reported to be completely safe, with no toxic symptoms reported.</li><li>Higher doses of Nicotinamide may cause vomiting and diarrhea.</li><li>Sensory neuropathy has been observed in individuals consuming more than 200 mg of Pyridoxine over long periods.</li><li>Zinc toxicity can occur in both acute and chronic forms. Ingestion of 150 to 450 mg of zinc per day has been associated with reduced copper status, altered iron function, reduced immune function, and lower levels of high-density lipoproteins. Zinc at its RDA dosage does not cause any significant effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Do not store above 30°C.</li><li>Keep away from light and out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Iron &amp; Vitamin Combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This is a special preparation of Iron Polymaltose Complex, Folic Acid, Zinc, and B vitamins. Iron Polymaltose Complex is a water-soluble, macromolecular complex of poly-nuclear iron (III) hydroxide and partially hydrolyzed dextrin (Polymaltose). It does not interact with food components or other medications, ensuring that the bioavailability of the Iron Polymaltose Complex is not decreased. This ensures that iron is utilized faster in hemoglobin synthesis.</p><p>Folic acid prevents neural tube defects, while Zinc supports many metabolic processes, blood formation, wound healing, and immune function. Thiamine is crucial for the nervous and digestive systems. Riboflavin aids digestion, immune function, and energy production. Pyridoxine plays a role in brain development, red blood cell production, protein metabolism, and reducing morning sickness. Nicotinamide is important for energy, blood pressure regulation, and circulation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Recommended during pregnancy and lactation.</p>', 'active', '582_1742760997.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:16:37', '2025-05-05 22:34:00'),
(4378, 'Z-Lidocaine', 'Lidocaine Hydrochloride', 100.00, 1, 'x 30 gm tube', '30 gm tube', 'Gel', '2%', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>﻿Indications of Z-Lidocaine 2%</strong></p><p>Lidocaine Injection is indicated:</p><ul><li>As a local anesthetic for use in infiltration blockade and intravenous regional analgesia.</li><li>For the prevention and treatment of ventricular tachyarrhythmias.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Local &amp; Surface Anesthesia</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Lidocaine acts mainly by inhibiting sodium influx through sodium-specific ion channels in the neuronal cell membrane, particularly the voltage-gated sodium channels. When the influx of sodium is interrupted, an action potential cannot arise, and signal conduction is inhibited. The receptor site is believed to be located at the cytoplasmic (inner) portion of the sodium channel. Lidocaine binds more readily to sodium channels in the activated state, allowing faster onset of neuronal blockade in neurons that are rapidly firing. This is referred to as state-dependent blockade.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Z-Lidocaine 2%</strong></p><p>The dosage is adjusted according to the patient\'s response and the site of administration.</p><ul><li><strong>Adult</strong>: For healthy adults, the maximum individual dose should not exceed 4.5 mg/kg of body weight. Generally, the maximum total dose does not exceed 300 mg. The recommended doses are as follows:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Infiltration- Percutaneous</strong>: Total dose 5-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intravenous regional</strong>: Total dose 50-300 mg</li><li><strong>Peripheral Nerve Blocks</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Brachial: Total dose 225-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Dental: Total dose 20-100 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Intercostal: Total dose 30 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Paravertebral: Total dose 30-50 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Pudendal (each side):</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Paracervical Obstetrical analgesia: Total dose 100 mg</li><li><strong>Sympathetic Nerve Blocks</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Cervical: Total dose 50 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Lumbar: Total dose 50-100 mg</li><li><strong>Central Neural Blocks</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Epidural: Total dose 200-300 mg (Dose determined by the number of dermatomes to be anesthetized, typically 2-3 mL/dermatome)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Thoracic: Total dose 200-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Lumbar Analgesia: Total dose 250-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Anesthesia: Total dose 200-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Caudal Obstetrical analgesia: Total dose 200-300 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Surgical anesthesia: Total dose 225-300 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Z-Lidocaine 2%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Antiarrhythmic Treatment</strong>: An intravenous dose of 1 mg/kg may be given over 2 minutes and repeated after 5 minutes. Alternatively, an infusion of 2-4 mg/min to produce a plasma level of 2-4 mg/L is more effective. A loading dose of approximately 1 mg/kg is usually given intravenously over 2 minutes to avoid a long delay in achieving a steady state.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Z-Lidocaine 2%</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cimetidine</strong> can impair the metabolism of lidocaine, leading to delayed elimination and an increased risk of adverse reactions.</li><li>Significant increases in plasma lidocaine concentrations have occurred when used concurrently with <strong>beta blockers</strong> such as propranolol, metoprolol, or nadolol.</li><li>It prolongs the duration of action of <strong>suxamethonium</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to anesthetics of the amide type.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Z-Lidocaine 2%</strong></p><p>Common side effects are rare and typically result from excessively high blood concentrations due to inadvertent intravascular injection, excessive dosage, rapid absorption, or hypersensitivity.</p><ul><li><strong>CNS Reactions</strong>: Nervousness, dizziness, blurred vision, tremors, followed by drowsiness, convulsions, unconsciousness, and possibly respiratory arrest.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Reactions</strong>: Hypotension, myocardial depression, bradycardia, and possibly cardiac arrest.</li><li><strong>Allergic Reactions</strong>: Rare and may include cutaneous lesions, urticaria, edema, or anaphylactoid reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Although there is no evidence from animal studies of harm to the fetus, lidocaine should not be given during early pregnancy unless the benefits outweigh the risks. Caution is advised when administering to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Lidocaine for infiltration and nerve block should only be administered by clinicians well-versed in managing dose-related toxicity.</li><li>The safety and effectiveness of lidocaine depend on proper dosage, technique, precautions, and readiness for emergencies. Resuscitative equipment and oxygen should be available for immediate use.</li><li>Use cautiously in patients with <strong>epilepsy</strong>, <strong>impaired cardiac conduction</strong>, <strong>bradycardia</strong>, <strong>impaired respiratory function</strong>, and <strong>impaired hepatic function</strong>, especially if high blood levels are anticipated.</li><li>The effect of local anesthetics may be reduced if an injection is made into an inflamed or infected area.</li><li>Repeated doses may cause significant increases in blood levels due to slow accumulation of the drug or its metabolites.</li><li>Close monitoring of cardiovascular and respiratory vital signs and consciousness level is essential after each injection.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 25°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>Dosages in children should be adjusted according to age, body weight, and physical condition.</li><li>Elderly patients may be more sensitive to systemic effects and may require dose reductions.</li></ul>', 'active', '1899_1742761109.jpg', 27, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:18:29', '2025-05-05 22:14:17'),
(4379, 'Lantidex', 'Dexlansoprazole', 10.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '30 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Lantidex 30 mg</h3><p><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>:</p><p>Lantidex 30 mg is indicated for the healing of all grades of erosive esophagitis (EE) for up to 8 weeks.</p><p><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis</strong>:</p><p>Lantidex 30 mg is indicated to maintain healing of EE and relief of heartburn for up to 6 months.</p><p><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)</strong>:</p><p>Lantidex 30 mg is indicated for the treatment of heartburn associated with symptomatic non-erosive GERD for 4 weeks.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><p><strong>Proton Pump Inhibitor</strong></p><p><br></p><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Lantidex 30 mg delayed-release capsule is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI) that inhibits gastric acid secretion. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole, a racemic mixture of the R- and S-enantiomers. The dual delayed-release formulation contains two types of enteric-coated granules with different pH-dependent dissolution profiles. Lantidex suppresses gastric acid secretion by inhibiting the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell, blocking the final step of acid production.</p><p><br></p><h3>Dosage of Lantidex 30 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis and Relief of Heartburn</strong>: 30 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive GERD</strong>: 30 mg once daily for 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 60 mg once daily for up to 8 weeks.</li></ul><h3>Administration of Lantidex 30 mg</h3><p>Lantidex 30 mg can be taken without regard to food and should be swallowed whole. Alternatively, the capsule can be opened, and the intact granules can be sprinkled on one tablespoon of food and swallowed immediately. Granules should not be chewed. If a dose is missed, take it as soon as possible unless it is close to the time for the next dose. Do not double the dose.</p><p><br></p><h3>Interactions of Lantidex 30 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>With Medicines</strong>: Atazanavir, Warfarin, Tacrolimus, Clopidogrel, and Methotrexate.</li><li><strong>With Food &amp; Others</strong>: No data available.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><p>Lantidex 30 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</p><p><br></p><h3>Side Effects of Lantidex 30 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Common Side Effects</strong>: Diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, flatulence.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><p>There are no adequate or well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Lantidex 30 mg should not be used during pregnancy unless the potential benefits outweigh the risks. It is not known if Lantidex 30 mg is excreted in human milk, but it has been found in rat milk. It should not be used by nursing mothers unless considered essential.</p><p><br></p><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Gastric Malignancy</strong></li><li><strong>Clostridium Difficile Associated Diarrhea</strong></li><li><strong>Bone Fracture</strong></li><li><strong>Hypomagnesemia</strong></li><li><strong>Concomitant use with Methotrexate</strong></li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Lantidex 30 mg</h3><p>There have been no reports of significant overdose. Doses of 120 mg daily and a single dose of 300 mg did not result in death or severe adverse effects.</p><p><br></p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><p>Store below 30°C in a dry place, protected from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><br></p><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics</strong>: No dosage adjustment necessary for elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in patients under 12 years have not been established.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment necessary for renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No adjustment for mild hepatic impairment. A maximum dose of 30 mg should be considered for moderate hepatic impairment.</li></ul><h3>Drug Class</h3><p><strong>Proton Pump Inhibitor</strong></p><p><br></p><h3>Mode of Action</h3><p>Lantidex 30 mg suppresses gastric acid secretion by inhibiting the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the parietal cells, blocking the final step in acid production. It uses a dual delayed-release formulation, resulting in two peaks in plasma concentration: one 1–2 hours after administration and another 4–5 hours later.</p><p><br></p><h3>Pregnancy Category</h3><p>Category B. There are no well-controlled studies in pregnant women.</p><p><br></p><h3>Pediatric Use</h3><p>Safety and effectiveness in children under 12 years have not been established.</p><p><br></p><h3>Geriatric Use</h3><p>No dose adjustment required for elderly patients.</p><p><br></p><h3>Renal Impairment</h3><p>No dose adjustment required for patients with renal impairment.</p><p><br></p><h3>Hepatic Impairment</h3><p>No dose adjustment for mild hepatic impairment. For moderate hepatic impairment, the maximum daily dose should be 30 mg.</p>', 'active', '184_1742761228.png', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:20:28', '2025-05-03 23:17:43'),
(4380, 'Brodil', 'Salbutamol', 250.00, 1, 'x 200 metered doses', '200 metered doses', 'Metered-Dose Inhaler (MDI)', '100 mcg/puff', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Brodil 100 mcg/puff</strong></p><p>Brodil 100 mcg/puff is indicated as a bronchodilator for use in:</p><ul><li>Asthma</li><li>Chronic Bronchitis</li><li>Emphysema</li><li>Other conditions associated with airway obstruction</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Brodil 100 mcg/puff is a selective beta2-adrenoceptor agonist. At therapeutic doses, it acts on the beta 2-adrenoceptors of bronchial smooth muscle, with little or no action on the ß1-adrenoceptors of cardiac muscle. Brodil 100 mcg/puff provides short-acting (4-6 hours) bronchodilatation with a fast onset (within 5 minutes) in reversible airways obstruction. It also has an anti-inflammatory effect on mast cells, causing inhibition of the release of bronchoconstrictor mediators, including histamine, neutrophil chemotactic factor (NCF), and prostaglandin D2.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Brodil 100 mcg/puff</strong></p><p><strong>Oral</strong></p><ul><li>Children:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2-6 years: 2.5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years: 5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Over 12 years: 5-10 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily (2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily)</li><li>Adults: 2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily. Maximum single dose is 8 mg tablet.</li></ul><p><strong>Injection</strong></p><ul><li>Subcutaneous route: 500 mcg (8 mcg/kg), repeated every four hours as required.</li><li>Intramuscular route: 500 mcg (8 mcg/kg), repeated every four hours as required.</li><li>Slow intravenous injection: 250 mcg (4 mcg/kg) injected slowly. If necessary, the dose may be repeated.</li><li>Pediatric Population: The safety and efficacy in children under 12 years have not been established. No recommendation on posology can be made.</li></ul><p><strong>Brodil 100 mcg/puff Syrup or Tablet Dosage</strong></p><ul><li>Children:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2-6 years: 2.5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years: 5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Over 12 years: 5-10 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily (2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily)</li><li>Adults: 2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily. Maximum single dose is 8 mg tablet.</li></ul><p><strong>Brodil 100 mcg/puff Respirator Solution</strong></p><ul><li>To be used with a suitable nebulizer device under the direction of a physician.</li><li><strong>Method-1 (Intermittent Administration)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults (and elderly): 0.5 ml-1.0 ml, up to four times a day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children under 12 years: 0.5 ml of the solution diluted to 2.0-4.0 ml with normal saline. Some children may require higher doses of up to 1.0 ml of the solution.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Intermittent treatment may be repeated up to four times a day.</li></ul><p><strong>Brodil 100 mcg/puff Nebulizer Solution</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Elderly</strong>: 2.5 mg to 5 mg, up to 4 times a day. Up to 40 mg/day can be given under strict medical direction in the hospital.</li><li><strong>Children under 12 years</strong>: 2.5 mg up to 4 times a day, or higher doses of up to 5 mg four times a day may be used if required.</li></ul><p><strong>Brodil 100 mcg/puff Injection (IV)</strong></p><ul><li>The infusion solution is prepared by diluting 5 mL of Brodil 100 mcg/puff infusion solution (1000 mcg/mL) in 500 mL of an intravenous solution to provide a Brodil 100 mcg/puff concentration of 10 mcg/mL.</li><li>Infusion rates of 3 to 20 micrograms/minute are usually adequate.</li></ul><p><strong>Brodil 100 mcg/puff Inhalation Capsule</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: One or two inhalation capsules may be administered as a single dose for the relief of bronchospasm.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: One inhalation capsule for the relief of acute bronchospasm and for the maintenance of episodic asthma.</li></ul><p><strong>Exercise-induced Asthma</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 400 microgram 15-30 minutes before physical exertion.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: 200 microgram 15-30 minutes before physical exertion.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Brodil 100 mcg/puff is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Brodil 100 mcg/puff or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Brodil 100 mcg/puff</strong></p><ul><li>Fine tremor of skeletal muscles (particularly the hands), palpitations, muscle cramps, tachycardia, tenseness, headaches, and peripheral vasodilatation have been reported after large doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Use only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It is unknown whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Discontinue nursing or the drug, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Brodil 100 mcg/puff should be used with caution in patients with:</p><ul><li>Hyperthyroidism</li><li>Cardiovascular disease</li><li>Occlusive vascular disorders</li><li>Hypertension</li><li>Aneurysms</li><li>Diabetes mellitus</li><li>Closed-angle glaucoma</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Brodil 100 mcg/puff</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdose include angina, headache, nausea, vomiting, and tremor. The antidote for overdosage is a cardio-selective beta-blocking agent, but caution should be used, especially in patients with bronchospasm.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, away from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Brodil 100 mcg/puff is a synthetic sympathomimetic agent with predominant beta-2 adrenergic activity, causing bronchodilatation through stimulation of beta-2-adrenergic receptors in bronchial smooth muscles.</p>', 'active', '774_1742761351.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:22:31', '2025-04-23 07:38:58');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4381, 'Domaid', 'Domperidone Maleate', 4.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '10 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Domaid 10 mg</strong></p><p>Dyspeptic symptom complex, often linked with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, may include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric sensation of fullness</li><li>Abdominal distension</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Belching</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Early satiety</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms can occur in various conditions, such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine. Additionally, in Parkinson\'s disease, it can manifest as dopamine-agonist-induced nausea and vomiting. For radiological studies, it can be used to speed up barium transit in follow-through radiological examinations.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Domaid 10 mg functions as a dopamine receptor antagonist. It exerts gastroprokinetic action by inhibiting dopamine receptors found in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier means it has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Domaid 10 mg</strong></p><p>For adults, the recommended dosage is 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily. Children should take 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily. When used for acute nausea and vomiting, the maximum treatment duration should not exceed 12 weeks. It is advisable to take this medication 15-30 minutes before a meal.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Domaid 10 mg</strong></p><p>Domaid 10 mg should be taken 15-30 minutes before meals, and if needed, before bedtime. The typical recommended oral dosage of Domperidone is as follows:</p><p>For Adults:</p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li>The maximum daily dose of Domperidone is 80 mg.</li></ul><p>For Children:</p><ul><li>2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg of body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>In cases of dyspeptic symptoms:</p><p>For Adults:</p><ul><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li>For Children:</li><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>For acute and sub-acute conditions, especially in cases of acute nausea and vomiting:</p><p>For Adults:</p><ul><li>20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li>For Children:</li><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>When administered rectally in the form of suppositories:</p><p>For Adults (including elderly):</p><ul><li>30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li><li>For Children:</li><li>The maximum daily rectal dose for children weighing between 10 to 25 kg is 30 mg. The dose may be divided throughout the day if necessary.</li></ul><p>Please note that the maximum treatment period for acute nausea and vomiting is 12 weeks.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Domaid 10 mg</strong></p><p>The simultaneous use of anticholinergic drugs may counteract the antidyspeptic effects of domperidone. It\'s important to note that antacids and antisecretory drugs should not be administered concurrently with domperidone as they can reduce its oral bioavailability. Domperidone primarily undergoes metabolism through the CYP3A4 enzyme. In vitro data suggest that coadministration with drugs significantly inhibiting this enzyme may lead to elevated plasma levels of domperidone. Examples of CYP3A4 inhibitors include azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, nefazodone, and others.</p><p>Theoretically, as domperidone has gastro-kinetic effects, it could potentially impact the absorption of concurrently orally administered drugs, especially those with sustained release or enteric-coated formulations. However, in patients already stabilized on medications like digoxin or paracetamol, the concurrent use of domperidone did not affect the blood levels of these drugs. Domperidone does not potentiate the action of neuroleptics, and it suppresses the unwanted peripheral effects such as digestive disorders, nausea, and vomiting caused by dopaminergic agonists like bromocriptine and L-dopa without counteracting their central properties.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Domaid 10 mg is not to be used by patients with a known hypersensitivity to this medication or in the case of neonates. It should also be avoided in situations where gastrointestinal stimulation could be unsafe, such as in cases of gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation. Additionally, it is contraindicated in patients with a prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma).</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Domaid 10 mg</strong></p><p>Side effects are uncommon, with occasional reports of transient intestinal cramps. Extrapyramidal symptoms are rare among young children and exceptionally rare in adults. These symptoms typically resolve spontaneously and completely upon discontinuing the treatment. Since the pituitary gland is situated outside the blood-brain barrier, domperidone may lead to an increase in plasma prolactin levels. In rare cases, this hyperprolactinemia can result in neuroendocrinological effects like galactorrhea and gynecomastia. In situations where the blood-brain barrier is immature (as in infants) or compromised, the possibility of neurological side effects cannot be entirely ruled out. Additionally, there have been occasional reports of rare allergic reactions, including rash and urticaria.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of using Domaid 10 mg during pregnancy has not been established; hence, its use is not recommended during pregnancy. Animal studies have not shown any teratogenic effects on the fetus. Domperidone may induce galactorrhea and enhance post-natal lactation. It is excreted in breast milk in minimal quantities, which are insufficient to be considered harmful.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Domaid 10 mg should be employed with utmost care in children due to the potential for an increased risk of extrapyramidal reactions, which can be more pronounced in young children with an incompletely developed blood-brain barrier. Additionally, considering that domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should also be used cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Domaid 10 mg</strong></p><p>Signs of an overdose can manifest as drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions, particularly in children. If an overdose occurs, it is advisable to administer activated charcoal and closely monitor the patient. The use of anticholinergic drugs, antiparkinson drugs, or antihistamines with anticholinergic properties may be beneficial in managing extrapyramidal reactions.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Domaid 10 mg acts as a dopamine antagonist, primarily by blocking dopamine receptors located in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its gastroprokinetic effect is achieved by inhibiting dopamine receptors that influence the motility of the gastrointestinal tract. Because it has limited ability to penetrate the blood-brain barrier, Domperidone has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects. Domperidone restores normal motility and tone in the upper gastrointestinal tract, promotes gastric emptying, enhances antral and duodenal peristalsis, and regulates pylorus contraction. Additionally, it increases esophageal peristalsis and lowers esophageal sphincter pressure, preventing the regurgitation of gastric contents.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in infants</strong>: Given that the metabolic and blood-brain barrier functions are not fully developed during the first months of life, any medication should be administered to infants with great caution and under close medical supervision. Although the typical absence of neurological side effects with domperidone is primarily due to its limited penetration through the blood-brain barrier, the potential occurrence of such effects cannot be entirely ruled out in infants under 1 year of age.</li><li><strong>Use in liver disorders</strong>: Because domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should be employed cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Use in kidney disorders</strong>: In individuals with severe renal insufficiency (serum creatinine &gt;6 mg/100ml, i.e., &gt;0.6 mmol/l), the elimination half-life of domperidone increases from 7.4 to 20.8 hours, although plasma drug levels remain lower than in healthy volunteers. Since very little unchanged drug is excreted through the kidneys, it is unlikely that the dose of a single acute administration needs to be adjusted in patients with renal insufficiency. However, for repeated administration, the dosing frequency should be reduced to once or twice daily, depending on the severity of the impairment, and the dose may need to be lowered. In general, patients on prolonged therapy should undergo regular reviews.</li></ul>', 'active', '695_1742761455.jpg', 68, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:24:15', '2025-05-03 08:25:18'),
(4382, 'Ticamet', 'Salmeterol + Fluticasone Propionate', 6.52, 6, 'capsule', '6 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50 mcg+100 mcg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ticamet 50 mcg+100 mcg:</strong></p><p>Ticamet 50 mcg+100 mcg is indicated in the regular treatment of asthma where the use of a combination product (long-acting β2-agonist and inhaled corticosteroid) is appropriate. It is prescribed for patients who are not adequately controlled with inhaled corticosteroids and \"as needed\" inhaled short-acting β2-agonist or for patients already adequately controlled on both inhaled corticosteroid and long-acting β2-agonist.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants</li><li>Respiratory corticosteroids</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate:</strong> A selective, long-acting beta-2 agonist used in the treatment of asthma and other forms of diffuse airways obstruction. It offers protection against asthma symptoms.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate:</strong> A corticosteroid with mainly glucocorticoid activity. It exerts a topical effect on the lungs without systemic effects at the usual dose. It improves lung function and prevents exacerbations of asthma.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ticamet 50 mcg+100 mcg:</strong></p><p><strong>Inhalation Aerosol:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 125 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 250 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily</li><li><strong>Children (4-12 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate twice daily</li></ul><p><strong>Inhalation Powder in Capsule (For Asthma):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg or Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 250 µg twice daily</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients (4-11 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg twice daily (morning and evening)</li></ul><p><strong>Inhalation Powder in Maxhaler (For Asthma):</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 years and older):</strong>50/100 Maxhaler: One Inhalation twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">50/250 Maxhaler: One Inhalation twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">50/500 Maxhaler: One Inhalation twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4 years and older):</strong>50/100 Maxhaler: One Inhalation twice daily (maximum dose: 100 µg twice daily)</li></ul><p><strong>Inhalation Powder in Maxhaler (For COPD):</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older):</strong>One Inhalation twice daily</li></ul><p><strong>Special Patient Groups:</strong></p><ul><li>No need for dose adjustment in elderly patients or those with renal impairment.</li><li>No data available for use in patients with hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration Instructions for Ticamet 50 mcg+100 mcg:</strong></p><p><strong>Using an Inhaler:</strong></p><ol><li>Remove the cap and shake the inhaler vigorously (at least six times) before each use.</li><li>If the inhaler is new or hasn’t been used for a week or more, shake well and release one puff into the air to ensure it works.</li><li>Exhale as fully as comfortable and hold the inhaler upright.</li><li>Place the actuator between the teeth and close lips around the mouthpiece.</li><li>Inhale deeply and slowly through the mouth while pressing down firmly on the canister to release the medicine.</li><li>Remove the inhaler from the mouth and hold the breath for at least 10 seconds.</li><li>If more than one inhalation is prescribed, wait one minute between puffs, then repeat the process.</li><li>After each treatment, rinse mouth with water to reduce side effects like hoarseness or thrush.</li></ol><p><strong>Cleaning the Inhaler:</strong></p><ol><li>Clean the inhaler at least once a week.</li><li>Remove the canister and rinse the plastic actuator and cap in warm water (do not submerge the metal canister).</li><li>Dry thoroughly and reassemble the inhaler.</li></ol><p><strong>Interaction of Ticamet 50 mcg+100 mcg:</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid the use of non-selective and selective β-blockers in patients with asthma unless necessary.</li><li>Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole, ritonavir) may increase systemic exposure to Fluticasone Propionate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ticamet 50 mcg+100 mcg:</strong></p><p><strong>Salmeterol:</strong></p><ul><li>Tremor, palpitations, headache (usually transient)</li><li>Cardiac arrhythmia (in susceptible individuals)</li><li>Arthralgia, hypersensitivity reactions (rash, oedema, angioedema)</li><li>Oropharyngeal irritation, muscle cramps (rare)</li></ul><p><strong>Fluticasone Propionate:</strong></p><ul><li>Hoarseness, candidiasis (thrush)</li><li>Skin hypersensitivity reactions</li><li>Rare facial or oropharyngeal oedema</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Administration during pregnancy and lactation should be considered only if the benefit outweighs potential risks. Insufficient data is available on its use during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use cautiously in patients with active or quiescent pulmonary tuberculosis or thyrotoxicosis.</li><li>Consider adding corticosteroid therapy or antibiotics if infection is present.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C, away from direct sunlight and heat. Do not puncture or incinerate the pressurised canister.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>Orally inhaled corticosteroids may reduce growth velocity in pediatric patients. The long-term impact on final adult height is unknown.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants</li><li>Respiratory corticosteroids</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate:</strong> Selective long-acting beta-2 agonist providing symptom protection.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate:</strong> Potent glucocorticoid that reduces inflammation in the lungs, improving lung function and preventing exacerbations.</li></ul>', 'active', '351_1742761576.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:26:16', '2025-05-04 22:22:59'),
(4383, 'Diclofen SR', 'Diclofenac Sodium', 3.40, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '100 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Diclofen SR 100 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Rheumatology</strong>:</p><ul><li>Inflammatory and degenerative forms of rheumatism</li><li>Chronic involutive polyarthritis</li><li>Ankylosing spondylarthritis</li><li>Osteoarthritis</li><li>Spondylarthroses</li><li>Acute gout</li><li>Peri-articular rheumatic disorders</li></ul><p><strong>Surgery and Traumatology</strong>:</p><ul><li>Sprains</li><li>Bruises</li><li>Dislocations</li><li>Fractures</li><li>Soft tissue injuries</li><li>Post-surgical pain and inflammation</li></ul><p><strong>Obstetrics and Gynecology</strong>:</p><ul><li>Primary dysmenorrhea</li><li>Episiotomy</li><li>Adnexitis</li><li>Endometritis</li><li>Parametritis</li><li>Salpingitis</li><li>Mastitis</li></ul><p><strong>Otorhinolaryngology</strong>:</p><ul><li>Pre-operative medication for pain, inflammation, and swelling prevention</li></ul><p><strong>Dentistry</strong>:</p><ul><li>Post-operative and post-traumatic pain, inflammation, and swelling</li></ul><p><strong>Other indications</strong>:</p><ul><li>Pain and inflammation prevention in patients operated on in the urogenital tract</li><li>Renal and biliary colic</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Diclofen SR 100 mg is a potent NSAID with anti-inflammatory, analgesic, and antipyretic properties. It works by inhibiting prostaglandin biosynthesis, which is central to inflammation, pain, and fever. Diclofenac is rapidly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract, with peak plasma concentrations reached in about 2 hours. It is 99.7% protein-bound and is metabolized in the liver via first-pass metabolism.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Sustained Release Tablets</strong>: One tablet daily with liquid, preferably during a meal.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Conventional Tablets</strong>: 75-150 mg daily in two or three doses after meals.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppositories</strong>: 75-150 mg daily, divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection</strong>: One ampoule daily, or up to two for severe cases.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Gel</strong>: 2-4 g applied 3-4 times daily to affected area.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Enteric-coated tablets</strong>: 1-3 mg/kg/day in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Sustained-release tablets</strong>: Not recommended.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Suppositories</strong>: 1-3 mg/kg body weight in divided doses.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Injection</strong>: 1-3 mg/kg/day for juvenile arthritis in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Elderly Patients</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Lowest effective dose recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction</strong></p><ul><li>Concomitant use with diuretics may inhibit their action.</li><li>Drugs like digoxin, methotrexate, cyclosporine, or lithium may have increased toxicity risks.</li><li>Concurrent use with other NSAIDs or glucocorticoids may increase the risk of gastrointestinal bleeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Diclofenac or any components.</li><li>Peptic ulcers, asthma, or other NSAID-induced sensitivities.</li><li>Not to be used under occlusive dressings or on mucous membranes.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects: Mild epigastric pain, nausea, dizziness, headache, rash, and skin reactions.</li><li>Rare side effects include peripheral edema and skin rashes.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Avoid unless benefits outweigh the risks. The lowest effective dose should be used, especially in the first trimester.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: A minimal amount may pass into breast milk, but no adverse effects on the infant are expected.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor for peptic ulcers or gastrointestinal bleeding, especially in the elderly.</li><li>Avoid eye and mucous membrane contact when using the gel.</li><li>Wash hands thoroughly after applying the gel.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place, below 30°C, and protect from light.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>NSAIDs, Drugs for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Diclofenac works by inhibiting the synthesis of prostaglandins, key mediators of inflammation and pain. It is highly protein-bound and undergoes first-pass metabolism in the liver, contributing to its efficacy in managing pain and inflammation.</p><p>This provides a detailed overview of Diclofen SR 100 mg, including its indications, usage, interactions, and precautions.</p>', 'active', '1700_1742761681.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:28:01', '2025-05-03 08:24:46'),
(4384, 'Filmet', 'Metronidazole', 1.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Filmet 200 mg</strong></p><p>Filmet 200 mg is indicated for the treatment of the following diseases:</p><ul><li>The prevention of post-operative infections due to anaerobic bacteria (particularly species of <em>Bacteroides</em> and anaerobic streptococci).</li><li>The treatment of septicaemia, bacteraemia, peritonitis, brain abscess, pelvic abscess, pelvic cellulitis, and post-operative wound infections caused by anaerobes.</li><li>Urogenital trichomoniasis.</li><li>Bacterial vaginosis (non-specific vaginitis).</li><li>All forms of amoebiasis (intestinal, extra-intestinal, and symptomless cyst passers).</li><li>Giardiasis.</li><li>Acute ulcerative gingivitis.</li><li>Anaerobically infected leg ulcers and pressure sores.</li><li>Acute dental infections caused by anaerobic organisms.</li><li>Antibiotic-associated pseudomembranous colitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Amoebicides, Anti-diarrhoeal, Antiprotozoal</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong> Filmet 200 mg is a member of the imidazole class of antibacterial drugs and is classified as an antiprotozoal agent. The 5-nitro group of Filmet 200 mg is reduced metabolically by anaerobes. Studies have shown that the reduced form of the drug interacts with DNA, leading to its bactericidal action.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Filmet 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet and Suspension:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Trichomoniasis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs)</strong>: 200 mg tid or 400 mg bid for 7 days. 800 mg in the morning and 1-2 gm at night for 2 days, or 2 gm as a single dose for 1 day.</li><li><strong>Trichomoniasis (Children)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 7-10 yrs: 100 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 3-7 yrs: 100 mg bid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 yrs: 50 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Intestinal Amoebiasis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs)</strong>: 800 mg tid for 5 days.</li><li><strong>Intestinal Amoebiasis (Children)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 7-10 yrs: 400 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 3-7 yrs: 200 mg qid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 yrs: 200 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Extra-intestinal &amp; Asymptomatic Amoebiasis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs)</strong>: 400-800 mg tid for 5-10 days.</li><li><strong>Extra-intestinal &amp; Asymptomatic Amoebiasis (Children)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 7-10 yrs: 200-400 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 3-7 yrs: 100-200 mg qid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 yrs: 100-200 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Giardiasis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs)</strong>: 2 gm once daily for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Giardiasis (Children)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 7-10 yrs: 1 gm once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 3-7 yrs: 600-800 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 yrs: 500 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute Ulcerative Gingivitis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs)</strong>: 200 mg tid for 3 days.</li><li><strong>Acute Ulcerative Gingivitis (Children)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 7-10 yrs: 100 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Children 3-7 yrs: 100 mg bid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children 1-3 yrs: 50 mg tid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute Dental Infections (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs)</strong>: 200 mg tid for 3-7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Bacterial Vaginosis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs)</strong>: 400 mg bid for 7 days or 2 gm as a single dose for 1 day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Leg Ulcers and Pressure Sores (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs)</strong>: 400 mg tid for 7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Anaerobic Infections (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs)</strong>: 800 mg initially, followed by 400 mg tid for 7 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Anaerobic Infections (Children)</strong>: Children 1-10 yrs: 7.5 mg/kg tid.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Surgical Prophylaxis (Adults &amp; Children over 10 yrs)</strong>: 400 mg tid started 24 hours before surgery for 1 day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Surgical Prophylaxis (Children)</strong>: Children 1-10 yrs: 7.5 mg/kg tid.</li><li><strong>Vaginal Gel:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommended dose: One applicator full of Filmet 200 mg gel (approximately 5 grams containing approximately 37.5 mg of Filmet 200 mg) intravaginally once or twice a day for 5 days.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For once a day dosing, Filmet 200 mg gel should be administered at bedtime.</li><li><strong>Suppository:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Anaerobic Infections (Adults)</strong>: 1 gm every 8 hours for 3 days, then 1 gm every 12 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Anaerobic Infections (Children)</strong>: Children 5-10 years: 500 mg every 8 hours for 3 days, then every 12 hours. Over 10 years, adult dose.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Surgical Prophylaxis (Adults)</strong>: 1 gm 2 hours before surgery; up to 3 further doses of 1 gm may be given every 8 hours for high-risk procedures.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Surgical Prophylaxis (Children)</strong>: Children 5-10 years: 500 mg 2 hours before surgery; up to 3 further doses of 500 mg may be given every 8 hours for high-risk procedures.</li><li><strong>IV Infusion:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years</strong>: Infuse 500 mg 8 hourly at a rate of 5 ml/minute. A maximum of 4 gm should not be exceeded during a 24-hour period.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children under 12 years</strong>: 7.5 mg/kg body weight/day every 8 hours at a rate of 5 ml/minute.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Filmet 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Disulfiram</strong>: Psychotic reactions may occur when Filmet 200 mg is used with disulfiram.</li><li><strong>Alcohol</strong>: Alcoholic beverages or alcohol-containing drugs should be avoided during therapy and for at least one day afterward to prevent a disulfiram-like reaction (flushing, vomiting, tachycardia).</li><li><strong>Oral Anticoagulant Therapy (Warfarin-type)</strong>: May increase hemorrhagic risk due to decreased hepatic catabolism. Prothrombin time should be monitored regularly.</li><li><strong>Lithium</strong>: Plasma levels of lithium may increase.</li><li><strong>Cyclosporin</strong>: Serum cyclosporin and creatinine should be monitored closely.</li><li><strong>Phenytoin or Phenobarbital</strong>: May reduce plasma levels of Filmet 200 mg due to increased elimination.</li><li><strong>5-Fluorouracil</strong>: May reduce clearance of 5-fluorouracil, increasing its toxicity.</li><li><strong>Busulfan</strong>: May increase plasma levels of busulfan, leading to toxicity.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong> Filmet 200 mg is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Filmet 200 mg or other Nitroimidazole derivatives.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Filmet 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Metallic taste, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, drowsiness, and rashes may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: US FDA Pregnancy Category B. No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Use only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Filmet 200 mg is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to nursing women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Regular hematological tests should be carried out if Filmet 200 mg is administered longer than usual.</li><li>Monitor for adverse reactions such as peripheral or central neuropathy (e.g., paresthesia, ataxia, dizziness, convulsive seizures).</li><li>Caution in patients with hepatic encephalopathy.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Filmet 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose include vomiting, ataxia, and slight disorientation. No specific antidote exists. Symptomatic and supportive treatment should be given.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C. Protect from light. Keep out of reach of children. Do not use after the expiry date.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Amoebicides, Anti-diarrhoeal, Antiprotozoal</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong> Filmet 200 mg is a member of the imidazole class and interacts with DNA in anaerobic bacteria, producing a bactericidal effect.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Category B (use if clearly needed).</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use</strong></p><ul><li>Caution is advised in patients with hepatic encephalopathy. Adjust doses in patients with severe hepatic impairment.</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Impairment</strong></p><ul><li>No dose adjustment is necessary for renal failure, but metabolites are retained. Re-administration is required after hemodialysis.</li></ul>', 'active', '746_1742761812.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:30:12', '2025-05-02 17:58:17'),
(4385, 'Ketozol', 'Ketoconazole', 230.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Shampoo', '2%', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ketozol 2%</strong></p><p>Ketozol 2% therapeutic shampoo is used for the prevention and treatment of infections caused by the yeast <em>Malassezia furfur</em> (formerly known as <em>Pityrosporum orbiculare</em>), including dandruff, seborrhoeic dermatitis, and tinea (pityriasis) versicolor.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for subcutaneous mycoses</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><ul><li>Ketozol 2% is a broad-spectrum imidazole-dioxolane synthetic antifungal agent that inhibits the growth of common dermatophytes and yeasts by altering the permeability of the cell membrane. It is effective against:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dermatophytes</strong>: <em>Trichophyton rubrum</em>, <em>T. mentagrophytes</em>, <em>T. tonsurans</em>, <em>Microsporum canis</em>, <em>M. audouini</em>, <em>M. gypseum</em>, and <em>Epidermophyton floccosum</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Yeasts</strong>: <em>Candida albicans</em>, <em>Malassezia ovale</em> (<em>Pityrosporum ovale</em>), and <em>C. tropicalis</em></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea versicolor</strong>: <em>Malassezia furfur</em> (<em>Pityrosporum orbiculare</em>)</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ketozol 2%</strong></p><ul><li>For topical use in adults, elderly individuals, and children.</li><li><strong>Treatment</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dandruff and seborrhoeic dermatitis</strong>: Wash hair twice weekly for 2-4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea versicolor</strong>: Use once daily for a maximum of 5 days.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dandruff and seborrhoeic dermatitis</strong>: Use once every 1-2 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea versicolor</strong>: Use once daily for a maximum of 3 days before exposure to sunshine.</li><li>If no clinical improvement is observed after the treatment period, the diagnosis should be re-evaluated.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ketozol 2%</strong></p><ul><li>No specific information is available for the shampoo form of Ketozol 2%.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Ketozol 2% is contraindicated in individuals who have shown hypersensitivity to Ketozol 2% or any of the excipients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ketozol 2%</strong></p><ul><li>In clinical trials, side effects were rare:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Dandruff and seborrhoeic dermatitis</strong>: Less than 1% of patients experienced increased hair loss or irritation.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Tinea versicolor</strong>: Common side effects were pruritus, application site reactions, and dry skin.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Other</strong>: Rare reports of abnormal hair texture, scalp pustules, mild dryness of the skin, and itching. Oiliness and dryness of the hair and scalp have also been reported.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Hair discoloration</strong>: Rare instances have been reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong>: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant or lactating women. However, data on a limited number of exposed pregnancies do not indicate adverse effects on pregnancy or fetal health. No known risks are associated with the use of Ketozol 2% shampoo in pregnancy or lactation, but it should only be used if the potential benefits justify the risks to the fetus.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>For patients on prolonged corticosteroid therapy, the steroid therapy should be gradually withdrawn over 2-3 weeks while using Ketozol 2% shampoo to prevent potential rebound effects.</li><li>Avoid contact with eyes. In case of contact, rinse eyes immediately with plenty of water.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Ketozol 2%</strong></p><ul><li>No specific information is available for overdosage with Ketozol 2% shampoo. In case of accidental ingestion, supportive and symptomatic treatment should be provided. Avoid emesis or gastric lavage to prevent aspiration.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 25°C, away from heat and light.</li><li>Do not store in the bathroom.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children and pets.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness in children have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: Normal adult dosage is appropriate for geriatric patients.</li></ul>', 'active', '544_1742761995.jpg', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:33:15', '2025-05-03 23:07:01'),
(4386, 'B-50 Forte', 'Vitamin B complex', 62.19, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '(5 mg+2 mg+2 mg+20 mg)/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of B-50 Forte 200 ml</strong></p><p>Vitamin-B complex is indicated for prophylactic or therapeutic nutritional supplementation in physiologically stressful conditions. These include conditions causing depletion, or reduced absorption or bioavailability of essential B-vitamins. Symptoms may manifest as glossitis, stomatitis, cheilosis, and beriberi.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each Tablet/Capsule contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride 5 mg</li><li>Riboflavin 2 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride 2 mg</li><li>Nicotinamide 20 mg</li></ul><p>Each 5 ml Syrup contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride 5 mg</li><li>Riboflavin 2 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride 2 mg</li><li>Nicotinamide 20 mg</li></ul><p>Each 2 ml Injection contains:</p><ul><li>Thiamine Hydrochloride 50 mg</li><li>Riboflavin 4 mg</li><li>Pyridoxine Hydrochloride 10 mg</li><li>Nicotinamide 100 mg</li><li>D-Panthenol 5 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Vitamin-B complex contains the most important members of the vitamin B group in pure form and in therapeutically balanced proportions. The members of the vitamin B group are components of enzyme systems that regulate various stages of carbohydrate, fat, and protein metabolism. Each component plays a specific biological role. Deficiency of B vitamins can cause glossitis, stomatitis, cheilosis, polyneuritis, beriberi, pellagra, and vascularisation of the cornea.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of B-50 Forte 200 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet/Capsule:</strong> The usual recommended dose is 1-2 tablets/capsules 3 times daily or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Syrup:</strong> 2-3 teaspoonfuls daily or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> For intramuscular and intravenous administration. The usual recommended dose is 2 ml daily or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of B-50 Forte 200 ml</strong></p><p>As little as 5 mg pyridoxine daily can decrease the efficacy of levodopa in the treatment of parkinsonism. Therefore, Vitamin-B complex is not recommended for patients undergoing such therapy.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Vitamin-B complex is contraindicated in patients hypersensitive to any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of B-50 Forte 200 ml</strong></p><p>Adverse reactions have been reported with specific vitamins and minerals, but generally at levels substantially higher than those in Vitamin-B complex. However, allergic and idiosyncratic reactions are possible at lower levels. Iron, even at the usual recommended level, has been associated with gastrointestinal intolerance in some patients.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It is safe to use Vitamin-B complex during pregnancy and lactation.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific combined vitamin preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Vitamin-B complex contains the most important members of the vitamin B group in pure form and in therapeutically balanced proportions. These vitamins play essential roles in enzyme systems that regulate stages of carbohydrate, fat, and protein metabolism. Deficiency of B vitamins can result in conditions like glossitis, stomatitis, cheilosis, polyneuritis, beriberi, pellagra, and vascularisation of the cornea.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>It is safe to use Vitamin-B complex during pregnancy and lactation.</p>', 'active', '1861_1742762126.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:35:26', '2025-05-03 00:26:21'),
(4387, 'Qfol', '6s-5-Methyltetrahydrofolate [Bio-Active Folate]', 100.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '400 mcg', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '801_1742762208.jpg', 17, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:36:48', '2025-05-04 14:59:52'),
(4388, 'Pantium', 'Pantoprazole Sodium', 7.02, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is your provided text in normal bold format for the headings:</p><p><strong>Indications of Pantium 20 mg</strong></p><p>Pantium 20 mg is recommended when inhibiting acid secretion is therapeutically beneficial, i.e. Stomach ulcer disorders. Illnesses caused by gastroesophageal reflux. Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug-induced ulcer (NSAIDs) Helicobacter pylori eradication (in combination with antibiotics) Syndrome of Zollinger-Ellison</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Blocker.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Pantium 20 mg is a proton pump inhibitor that works by covalently attaching to the H+/K+ATPase enzyme system on the surface of the gastric parietal cell to decrease the last stage of gastric acid generation. Regardless of the stimulus that remains for more than 24 hours, this impact results in a suppression of both basal and induced stomach acid secretion.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Pantium 20 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Oral:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Benign gastric ulcer:</strong> 40 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, continued for further 4 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li><strong>Gastro-esophageal reflux disease:</strong> 20-40 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks, continued for further 4 weeks if not fully healed; maintenance dose is 20 mg daily, which may be increased to 40 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer:</strong> 40 mg daily in the morning for 2 weeks, continued for further 2 weeks if not fully healed.</li><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer associated with Helicobacter pylori:</strong> Pantium 20 mg is recommended at a dose of 40 mg twice daily in association with antimicrobial agents as detailed below:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Amoxycillin 1 g and Clarithromycin 500 mg both twice daily for one week, or</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Clarithromycin 250 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg both twice daily for one week.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of NSAID-associated gastric or duodenal ulcer:</strong> 20 mg daily for those requiring long-term NSAID treatment.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> Initially 80 mg once daily adjusted according to response (elderly max. 40 mg daily); daily doses above 80 mg given in 2 divided doses.</li></ul><p><strong>IV Injection:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Duodenal ulcer and gastric ulcer:</strong> 40 mg once daily for 7-10 days.</li><li><strong>Gastroesophageal reflux disease associated with a history of erosive esophagitis:</strong> 40 mg once daily for 7-10 days.</li><li><strong>Prevention of rebleeding in peptic ulcer:</strong> IV 80 mg, followed by 8 mg/hour infusion for 72 hours.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration:</strong> 80 mg IV every 12 h for 24 h, followed by 40mg every 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Long-term management of Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome and other pathological hypersecretory conditions:</strong> 80 mg IV every 12 hours, may increase to 80 mg every 8 hours if needed, may titrate to higher doses depending on acid output.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Pantium 20 mg</strong></p><p>In clinical investigations, no significant medication interactions were found.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those with known hypersensitivity to any of the formulation\'s ingredients should not take Pantium 20 mg.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Pantium 20 mg</strong></p><p>Both in the short-term and the long-term, Pantium 20 mg is well tolerated. The most frequent adverse effects are headache and diarrhea, while abdominal pain, flatulence, rash, sleeplessness, and hyperglycemia are only rarely reported side effects.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Pantium 20 mg is classified as a B pregnancy drug by the US FDA. Nevertheless, there isn\'t any good, controlled research on pregnant women. This medication should only be used during pregnancy if clearly necessary, as a human response to treatments is not usually predicted by studies on animal reproduction. It has been demonstrated that Pantium 20 mg is excreted in human milk. So, a choice should be taken on whether to stop nursing or stop taking the medication, taking into account the benefit of the medication to the mother.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Patients should be advised not to split, chew, or crush the Pantium 20 mg pill. The use of Pantium 20 mg for an extended period of time may cause malabsorption of cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) or raise the risk of problems linked to osteoporosis.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Pantium 20 mg</strong></p><p>There are no recognized signs of human overdose. Due to its strong protein binding, Pantium 20 mg is not easily dialyzable. There is no specific therapy that is advised, other symptomatic and supportive management.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep dry and away from heat and light. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Reconstitution</strong></p><p><strong>Direction for use of IV injection:</strong> Pantium 20 mg lyophilized powder with 0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection must only be administered intravenously and under no other circumstances. A gradual intravenous dose of Pantium 20 mg should be administered. The powder-containing vial is mixed with 10 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride injection to create the IV injection solution. Upon reconstitution, the injection should be administered gradually over a minimum of 2 to 5 minutes. Use just recently made solutions. The reconstituted solution can be kept for up to 4 hours at room temperature (up to 30° C).</p><p><strong>How to administer an IV infusion:</strong> An intravenous infusion of Pantium 20 mg should be administered over roughly 15 minutes. An IV infusion of Pantium 20 mg needs to be reconstituted with 10 ml of 0.9% sodium chloride injection before being further diluted (admixed) to a final volume of 100 ml using 0.9% sodium chloride injection, 5% dextrose, or lactated ringer\'s injection. Prior to further dilution, the reconstituted solution may be kept at room temperature (up to 30° C) for a maximum of 4 hours. The admixed solution must be used within 24 hours of the initial reconstitution and may be kept at room temperature (up to 30° C).</p>', 'active', '88_1742762293.jpg', 63, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:38:13', '2025-05-04 10:14:40');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4389, 'Dexlan', 'Dexlansoprazole', 18.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '60 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Dexlan 60 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis (EE):</strong> Dexlan 60 mg is indicated for the healing of all grades of erosive esophagitis for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> Dexlan 60 mg is indicated to maintain healing of EE and relief of heartburn for up to 6 months.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD):</strong> Dexlan 60 mg is indicated for the treatment of heartburn associated with symptomatic non-erosive GERD for 4 weeks.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li><strong>Mechanism of Action:</strong> Dexlan 60 mg is a PPI that inhibits gastric acid secretion by specifically blocking the (H+/K+)-ATPase in gastric parietal cells, effectively suppressing the final step of acid production.</li><li><strong>Formulation:</strong> Dexlan 60 mg is a Dual Delayed Release (DDR) formulation that contains two types of enteric-coated granules with different pH-dependent dissolution profiles.</li><li><strong>Enantiomer:</strong> Dexlan 60 mg is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole (a racemic mixture of R- and S-enantiomers).</li></ul><h3>Dosage</h3><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis:</strong> 60 mg once daily for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis and Relief of Heartburn:</strong> 30 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive GERD:</strong> 30 mg once daily for 4 weeks.</li></ul><h3>Administration</h3><ul><li>Dexlan 60 mg can be taken without regard to food and should be swallowed whole.</li><li>Alternatively, the capsule can be opened, and the granules sprinkled on one tablespoon of food, but the granules should not be chewed.</li><li>If a dose is missed, it should be taken as soon as possible, but a double dose should not be taken.</li></ul><h3>Interaction with Other Medicines</h3><ul><li><strong>Medications:</strong> Atazanavir, Warfarin, Tacrolimus, Clopidogrel, Methotrexate.</li><li><strong>Food &amp; Others:</strong> No data available.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>Common side effects:</strong> Diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and flatulence.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B:</strong> No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women.</li><li>Dexlan 60 mg should not be used during pregnancy unless the potential benefits outweigh the risks.</li><li>It is not known whether Dexlan 60 mg is excreted in human milk, but it is excreted in rat milk. It should not be used by nursing mothers unless deemed necessary.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Conditions to be cautious of:</strong> Gastric malignancy, Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea, bone fractures, hypomagnesemia, and concomitant use with Methotrexate.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li>No significant overdose reports. Multiple doses of Dexlan 60 mg (120 mg) and a single dose (300 mg) did not result in death or severe adverse events.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C in a dry place, protected from light.</li><li>Keep all medicines out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics:</strong> No dosage adjustment is necessary for elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics:</strong> Safety and effectiveness in children under 12 years have not been established.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment is necessary for patients with renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustment is necessary for patients with mild hepatic impairment. For patients with moderate hepatic impairment, a maximum daily dose of 30 mg may be considered.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li><strong>Proton Pump Inhibitor</strong></li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>Dexlan 60 mg suppresses gastric acid secretion by inhibiting the proton pump (H+/K+-ATPase) in gastric parietal cells.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li>No adequate or well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Use only if the benefits outweigh the risks.</li></ul><h3>Pediatric Use</h3><ul><li>Safety and effectiveness have not been established for patients under 12 years of age.</li></ul><h3>Geriatric Use</h3><ul><li>No dose adjustment needed for elderly patients.</li></ul><h3>Renal Impairment</h3><ul><li>No dose adjustment necessary for patients with renal impairment.</li></ul><h3>Hepatic Impairment</h3><ul><li>No dose adjustment needed for mild hepatic impairment. For moderate hepatic impairment, a maximum dose of 30 mg daily is recommended.</li></ul>', 'active', '1200_1742762371.png', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:39:31', '2025-05-01 20:45:52'),
(4390, 'Migrex', 'Tolfenamic acid', 10.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Migrex 200 mg:</strong></p><p><strong>Indications:</strong> Migrex 200 mg is primarily used for:</p><ul><li>Relieving the pain of migraine headaches</li><li>Analgesic in post-operative pain and fever</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li><li>Other drugs for migraine</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Migrex 200 mg works by inhibiting the biosynthesis of prostaglandins, exerting its effects through inhibition of COX-1 and COX-2 pathways, which decreases prostaglandin secretion and action. This helps in reducing inflammation and pain. It also has an additional anti-inflammatory mechanism by inhibiting leukotriene B4 chemotaxis in human polymorphonuclear leukocytes.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Acute migraine attacks:Adult:</strong> 200 mg when symptoms first appear, may be repeated once after 1-2 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> Dose adjustments may be necessary; avoid in severe renal impairment.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Mild to moderate pain:Adult:</strong> 100-200 mg three times daily (tid).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal impairment:</strong> Adjust dosage as needed; avoid in severe renal impairment.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> Pediatric dosage has not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Important Notes:</strong></p><ul><li>Migrex 200 mg should be taken with food, ideally with water during or immediately after meals.</li><li>Preclinical data suggests a high therapeutic index, but gastrointestinal ulceration and kidney changes have been seen at doses 6-10 times the maximum recommended dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Precautions:</strong></p><ul><li>Asthma, bronchospasm, bleeding disorders, cardiovascular diseases, history of peptic ulceration, hypertension, liver, cardiac, or renal function impairment.</li><li>Increase water intake or reduce the dose to minimize dysuria.</li><li>Use caution in elderly patients and those who are lactating.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction with Other Drugs:</strong></p><ul><li>Absorption rate increases with <strong>Metoclopramide</strong> and <strong>Magnesium hydroxide</strong> but decreases with <strong>Aluminium hydroxide</strong>.</li><li>Risk of bleeding increases with <strong>anticoagulants</strong> and other NSAIDs.</li><li>Reduces antihypertensive effects of <strong>loop diuretics</strong>, <strong>B-blockers</strong>, and <strong>ACE inhibitors</strong>.</li><li>Co-administration can increase plasma concentrations of <strong>Lithium</strong>, <strong>Methotrexate</strong>, and <strong>cardiac glycosides</strong>.</li><li>Increases the risk of nephrotoxicity with <strong>ACE inhibitors</strong>, <strong>Ciclosporin</strong>, <strong>Tacrolimus</strong>, or <strong>diuretics</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Active peptic ulcer or bleeding in the gut.</li><li>Severe heart, kidney, or liver failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Dysuria (especially in males), diarrhea, nausea, epigastric pain, vomiting, dyspepsia, erythema, headache, tremor, euphoria, fatigue, pulmonary infiltration, hematuria.</li><li>Potentially fatal: Blood dyscrasias and hepatitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Not recommended unless considered essential, particularly in the first and third trimesters.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> NSAIDs may appear in breast milk in very low concentrations, so should be avoided if possible during breastfeeding.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with asthma, bronchospasm, bleeding disorders, cardiovascular diseases, peptic ulceration, hypertension, liver infection, and those with cardiac or renal function impairment.</li><li>Elderly patients require special care. Ensure adequate water intake to reduce the risk of dysuria.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose include headache, nausea, vomiting, epigastric pain, gastrointestinal bleeding, diarrhea, excitation, coma, drowsiness, dizziness, tinnitus, fainting, and convulsions.</li><li>In severe poisoning cases, acute renal failure and liver damage are possible.</li><li>Treatment is symptomatic.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place away from light.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li><li>Other drugs for migraine</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong> Migrex 200 mg belongs to the fenamate group and inhibits the cyclooxygenase enzyme (COX-1 and COX-2), which decreases the production of thromboxane (TX) B2 and prostaglandin (PG) E2. These inflammatory mediators cause pain and inflammation. Migrex also directly antagonizes the receptors for these mediators.</p><p><strong>Pharmacokinetics:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Absorption:</strong> Readily absorbed from the GI tract, peak plasma concentration occurs within 60-90 minutes, and bioavailability is 85%.</li><li><strong>Protein-binding:</strong> 99%</li><li><strong>Plasma half-life:</strong> 2 hours</li><li><strong>Metabolism:</strong> Metabolized in the liver, undergoes enterohepatic circulation.</li><li><strong>Excretion:</strong> 90% excreted in urine and the rest in feces.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></p><ul><li>Not recommended during pregnancy, especially in the third trimester.</li><li>NSAIDs can appear in breast milk in very low concentrations, and breastfeeding while on Migrex 200 mg should be avoided if possible.</li></ul>', 'active', '1375_1742762456.png', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:40:56', '2025-05-04 00:04:12'),
(4391, 'Dermasol Plus', 'Clobetasol Propionate + Salicylic Acid', 150.00, 1, 'x 30 gm tube', '30 gm tube', 'Ointment', '0.05%+3%', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Dermasol Plus 30 gm</h3><p><strong>Indications:</strong> This ointment is used for the relief of inflammatory manifestations of hyperkeratotic and dry corticosteroid-responsive dermatoses, including:</p><ul><li>Psoriasis</li><li>Chronic atopic dermatitis</li><li>Neurodermatitis (Lichen Simplex Chronicus)</li><li>Lichen planus</li><li>Eczema (including nummular eczema, hand eczema, eczematous dermatitis)</li><li>Seborrheic dermatitis of the scalp</li><li>Ichthyosis vulgaris and other ichthyotic conditions</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong>: A high-potency corticosteroid that reduces inflammation through various mechanisms including stabilizing leukocyte lysosomal membranes, inhibiting macrophage accumulation, reducing capillary wall permeability, and antagonizing histamine activity.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid</strong>: A potent keratolytic agent with slight antiseptic and weak antifungal properties. It softens and destroys the stratum corneum by increasing hydration, leading to the desquamation of the skin. It also possesses weak antibacterial activity.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: Apply a thin layer to affected skin areas twice daily, gently rubbing it in. Maintenance therapy may be achieved with less frequent applications. Discontinue once control is achieved. If no improvement is seen within 2 weeks, reassess the diagnosis.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Not recommended for use in pediatric patients under 12 years of age.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration:</strong> For external use only.</p><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>No known interactions with Clobetasol Propionate or Salicylic Acid when used topically.</li><li>Caution should be exercised when using other topical medicines in conjunction with Salicylic Acid as it may increase absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong>: Contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to Clobetasol Propionate, primary infected bacterial or fungal skin lesions (if no anti-infective agent is used), primary cutaneous viral infections (e.g., herpes simplex, varicella), tuberculous skin lesions, and dermatoses in children under 1 year of age.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid</strong>: Contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to salicylates or any other ingredients in the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Prolonged use or large quantities of Clobetasol Propionate may lead to systemic absorption, causing hypercortisolism. This is more common in infants and children, especially if occlusive dressings are used.</li><li>Local side effects may include atrophy after prolonged treatment and irritation or dryness with Salicylic Acid use.</li><li>Rare side effects include pustular psoriasis, which can be triggered by corticosteroid use or withdrawal.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong>: The safety during pregnancy and lactation has not been fully established. Use only if the benefit outweighs the risk. Not recommended for extensive use in pregnant patients.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid</strong>: No known contraindications during pregnancy or lactation, but should be used with caution as safety has not been fully established.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid prolonged use on large areas or high concentrations.</li><li>Use with caution in individuals with impaired peripheral circulation or diabetes.</li><li>Avoid contact with broken skin, mouth, eyes, mucous membranes, and anogenital region.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></p><ul><li>Chronic overdosage may result in hypercortisolism. Discontinue corticosteroids gradually under medical supervision to avoid adrenal suppression.</li><li>Systemic salicylate poisoning symptoms may include tinnitus, dizziness, and deafness, particularly when Salicylic Acid is used extensively or for prolonged periods.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, protected from light.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate</strong>: Reduces inflammation through various pathways like stabilizing leukocyte membranes, reducing capillary permeability, and antagonizing histamine.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid</strong>: Acts as a keratolytic agent, softening and destroying the outer skin layers and aiding in the removal of scales.</li></ul>', 'active', '1924_1742762579.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:42:59', '2025-04-15 12:13:51'),
(4392, 'Regler', 'Dexlansoprazole', 10.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '30 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Regler 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: Regler 30 mg is indicated for the healing of all grades of erosive esophagitis (EE) for up to 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: Regler 30 mg is indicated to maintain healing of EE and relief of heartburn for up to 6 months.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease</strong>: Regler 30 mg is indicated for the treatment of heartburn associated with symptomatic non-erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD) for 4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Regler 30 mg delayed-release capsule is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI), that inhibits gastric acid secretion. It is the R-enantiomer of lansoprazole (A racemic mixture of the R- and S-enantiomers). This is supplied as a Dual Delayed Release (DDR) formulation in a capsule for oral administration. Regler 30 mg capsule contains a mixture of two types of enteric coated granules with different pH-dependent dissolution profiles. Regler 30 mg suppresses gastric acid secretion by inhibiting the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell, thus blocking the final step of acid production.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Regler 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Maintenance of Healed Erosive Esophagitis and Relief of Heartburn</strong>: 30 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Symptomatic Non-Erosive GERD</strong>: 30 mg once daily for 4 weeks</li><li><strong>Healing of Erosive Esophagitis</strong>: 60 mg once daily for up to 8 weeks</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Regler 30 mg</strong></p><p>Regler 30 mg can be taken without regard to food. It should be swallowed whole. Alternatively, the capsule can be opened, and the intact granules can be sprinkled on one tablespoon of food and swallowed immediately. Granules should not be chewed. If a dose is missed, it should be taken as soon as possible, but if it is near the time for the next dose, only the prescribed dose should be taken.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Regler 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With medicines</strong>: Atazanavir, Warfarin, Tacrolimus, Clopidogrel &amp; Methotrexate.</li><li><strong>With food &amp; others</strong>: No data available.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Regler 30 mg is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any component of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Regler 30 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and flatulence.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>There are no adequate or well-controlled studies in pregnant women with Regler 30 mg. It should only be used during pregnancy if the expected benefits outweigh the potential risks.</li><li>It is not known whether Regler 30 mg is excreted in human milk. However, Regler 30 mg and its metabolites are excreted in the milk of rats, so it should not be used in nursing mothers unless deemed essential.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Gastric malignancy</li><li>Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea</li><li>Bone fracture</li><li>Hypomagnesemia</li><li>Concomitant use with Methotrexate</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Regler 30 mg</strong></p><p>No significant overdose events have been reported. Doses up to 120 mg per day or a single dose of 300 mg did not result in death or severe adverse events.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C in a dry place, protected from light. Keep out of reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Geriatrics</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary for elderly patients.</li><li><strong>Pediatrics</strong>: Safety and effectiveness have not been established for patients below 12 years of age.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary for patients with mild hepatic impairment. For moderate hepatic impairment, the maximum daily dose should be 30 mg.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Regler 30 mg is a PPI that inhibits gastric acid secretion by blocking the (H+/K+)-ATPase in the gastric parietal cell, which is the final step in acid production. It is a Dual Delayed Release formulation that provides two peaks in plasma concentration.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Category B: There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Use only if the potential benefits outweigh the risks.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Safety and Effectiveness</strong>: Not established for children below 12 years.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use</strong>: No dose adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: No dose adjustment needed.</li><li><strong>Hepatic Impairment</strong>: For moderate impairment, the maximum dose should be 30 mg per day.</li></ul>', 'active', '592_1742762691.webp', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:44:51', '2025-05-04 15:08:33'),
(4393, 'Calcirol', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 120.40, 1, 'x 1 ml ampoule', '1 ml ampoule', 'Injection', '200000 IU/ml', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Calcirol 200000 IU/ml</h3><p><strong>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3)</strong> is indicated for:</p><ul><li><strong>Treatment and prevention</strong> of Vitamin D3 deficiency.</li><li><strong>Adjunct therapy</strong> for conditions like osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcaemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency.</li><li><strong>Essential for bone growth</strong> and development, maintaining bone density, and aiding in the absorption of calcium and phosphorus.</li><li><strong>Support for overall health</strong>, including reducing bacterial infection severity, improving lung function, preventing certain cancers (e.g., breast and colorectal), and maintaining insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Vitamin in bone formation</strong>, Vitamin D preparations.</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li><strong>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3)</strong> promotes calcium and phosphorus absorption and reabsorption, essential for normal bone growth and maintenance of bone density. It also plays a role in reducing bacterial infection severity and improving overall immune function.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Calcirol 200000 IU/ml</h3><p><strong>Capsules (Adults):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin D3 Deficiency Treatment:</strong> 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks. Maintenance dose: 1,400–2,000 IU/day.</li><li><strong>Prevention of Deficiency:</strong> 20,000 IU every 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Osteoporosis:</strong> 20,000 IU once a month.</li></ul><p><strong>Capsules (Children 12-18 years):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Vitamin D3 Deficiency Treatment:</strong> 20,000 IU once every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Prevention:</strong> 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Film-Coated Tablets:</strong></p><ul><li>1,000 IU to 2,000 IU daily, as directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Syrup:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Risk of Deficiency:</strong>0-1 yr: 400 IU/day (2 ml)</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 yr: 600 IU/day (3 ml)</blockquote><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Deficient Patients:</strong>0-1 yr: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week for 6 weeks)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 yrs: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Injection (Prevention):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Infants on Vitamin D-enriched milk:</strong> 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) every 6 months.</li><li><strong>Other children up to 5 years:</strong> 1 ampoule (1 ml) every 6 months.</li><li><strong>Adolescents:</strong> 1 ampoule (1 ml) every 6 months in winter.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) from the 6th or 7th month.</li><li><strong>Elderly and specific conditions:</strong> 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) every 3 months or 1 ampoule (1 ml) every 6 months.</li></ul><h3>Interactions</h3><ul><li>May interact with <strong>phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives, actinomycin, and imidazole antifungals</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>Common side effects:</strong> Hypercalcaemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, abdominal pain.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Safe use</strong> up to 4,000 IU/day during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Vitamin D3 is excreted in breast milk</strong>, but overdose in infants through nursing mothers has not been observed.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li><strong>Caution</strong> in patients with <strong>impaired renal function</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li>Can lead to <strong>hypervitaminosis D</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store <strong>below 30ºC</strong>, <strong>protected from light and moisture</strong>. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li>Safety and efficacy in <strong>children under 12 years</strong> have not been established.</li></ul><h3>Drug Classes</h3><ul><li>Vitamin in <strong>bone formation</strong>, <strong>Vitamin D preparations</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3)</strong> aids in the absorption of calcium and phosphorus, essential for bone health, immune function, and overall wellness.</li></ul><h3>Pediatric Uses</h3><ul><li><strong>Not established</strong> for use in children under 12 years.</li></ul>', 'active', '804_1742762795.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:46:35', '2025-05-03 01:09:45'),
(4394, 'Tamen', 'Paracetamol', 35.00, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '120 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Tamen 60 ml</strong></p><p>Tamen 60 ml is indicated for the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Fever</li><li>Common cold and influenza</li><li>Headache</li><li>Toothache</li><li>Earache</li><li>Bodyache</li><li>Myalgia</li><li>Neuralgia</li><li>Dysmenorrhoea</li><li>Sprains</li><li>Colic pain</li><li>Back pain</li><li>Post-operative pain</li><li>Postpartum pain</li><li>Inflammatory pain</li><li>Post vaccination pain in children</li><li>Rheumatic &amp; osteoarthritic pain</li><li>Stiffness of joints</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Tamen 60 ml has analgesic and antipyretic properties with weak anti-inflammatory activity. Tamen 60 ml (Acetaminophen) is believed to primarily act in the central nervous system (CNS), increasing the pain threshold by inhibiting both isoforms of cyclooxygenase (COX-1, COX-2) and COX-3 enzymes involved in prostaglandin synthesis. It is a para-aminophenol derivative, commonly used for its analgesic and antipyretic effects and is considered to be safe, fast-acting, and well-tolerated with fewer side effects compared to aspirin.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p><strong>Tablet</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 1-2 tablets every 4 to 6 hours, up to a maximum of 4 gm (8 tablets) daily.</li><li><strong>Children (6-12 years)</strong>: ½ to 1 tablet 3 to 4 times daily. For long-term treatment, it is recommended not to exceed 2.6 gm/day.</li></ul><p><strong>Extended Release Tablet</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Children over 12 years</strong>: 2 tablets, swallowed whole, every 6 to 8 hours (maximum of 6 tablets in 24 hours). The tablet must not be crushed.</li></ul><p><strong>Syrup/Suspension</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children under 3 months</strong>: 10 mg/kg body weight (reduce to 5 mg/kg if jaundiced) 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>3 months to below 1 year</strong>: ½ to 1 teaspoonful 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>1-5 years</strong>: 1-2 teaspoonfuls 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>6-12 years</strong>: 2-3 teaspoonfuls 3 to 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 4-8 teaspoonfuls 3 to 4 times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Suppository</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children 3-12 months</strong>: 60-120 mg, 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children 1-5 years</strong>: 125-250 mg 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6-12 years</strong>: 250-500 mg 4 times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults &amp; children over 12 years</strong>: 0.5-1 gm 4 times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Paediatric Drop</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children Up to 3 months</strong>: 0.5 ml (40 mg)</li><li><strong>4 to 11 months</strong>: 1.0 ml (80 mg)</li><li><strong>1 to 2 years</strong>: 1.5 ml (120 mg). Do not exceed more than 5 doses daily for a maximum of 5 days.</li></ul><p><strong>Tamen 60 ml with Actizorb Technology</strong></p><p>It dissolves up to five times faster than standard tablets and is a fast-acting, safe analgesic with marked antipyretic properties.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults and children (12 years and over)</strong>: 1 to 2 Tablets every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not exceed 8 caplets in 24 hours.</li><li><strong>Children (7 to 11 years)</strong>: ½ to 1 Tablet every 4 to 6 hours as needed. Do not exceed 4 caplets in 24 hours. Not recommended in children under 7 years.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>Barbiturates, tricyclic antidepressants, and alcohol may reduce the ability to metabolize large doses of Tamen 60 ml.</li><li>Alcohol can increase the hepatotoxicity of Tamen 60 ml overdose.</li><li>Chronic use of anticonvulsants or oral steroid contraceptives may alter metabolism, reducing Tamen 60 ml\'s effectiveness.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Tamen 60 ml.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Mild side effects, including thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, pancytopenia, neutropenia, and agranulocytosis.</li><li>Occasional pancreatitis, skin rashes, and other allergic reactions.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong> (USFDA). It should only be used during pregnancy if clearly needed.</li><li>Tamen 60 ml is excreted in breast milk. Maternal ingestion of Tamen 60 ml in therapeutic doses is generally considered safe for nursing infants.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in patients with impaired kidney or liver function.</li><li>Caution for those taking other medications that affect liver function.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Early symptoms within 24 hours include pallor, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and abdominal pain.</li><li>Liver damage may become apparent 12-48 hours after ingestion.</li><li>Abnormal glucose metabolism and metabolic acidosis may occur.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Non-opioid analgesics</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Tamen 60 ml works as an analgesic and antipyretic with weak anti-inflammatory properties by inhibiting COX enzymes, thereby increasing the pain threshold in the CNS. It is widely used for pain relief and fever management with fewer side effects compared to other analgesics like aspirin.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>As mentioned, Tamen 60 ml is categorized under <strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong> by the USFDA. It should be used in pregnancy only if clearly needed.</li></ul>', 'active', '1277_1742762938.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:48:58', '2025-05-04 21:30:49'),
(4395, 'I-Vita', 'Vitamin C + Vitamin E + Lutein + Copper + Zinc', 11.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '60 mg+30 mg+6 mg+2 mg+15 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '272_1742763027.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:50:27', '2025-05-03 20:58:58'),
(4396, 'Lutisone', 'Fluticasone Propionate', 250.00, 1, 'x 120 metered sprays', '120 metered sprays', 'Nasal Spray', '50 mcg/spray', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Lutisone 50 mcg/spray</strong></p><p>Lutisone 50 mcg/spray is indicated for the relief of inflammatory and pruritic manifestations of corticosteroid-responsive eczema or dermatitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Fluticasone &amp; combined preparations topical</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Fluticasone propionate is a glucocorticoid with high topical anti-inflammatory potency but a low HPA-axis suppressive activity after dermal administration. It, therefore, has a therapeutic index greater than most commonly available steroids. Fluticasone propionate has a high degree of selectivity for the glucocorticoid receptor. In vitro studies show that Lutisone 50 mcg/spray has a strong affinity for, and agonist activity at, human glucocorticoid receptors, believed to be responsible for the anti-inflammatory properties of glucocorticoids. It has weak affinity for the progesterone receptor, and virtually no affinity for the mineralocorticoid, estrogen, or androgen receptors. The therapeutic potency of glucocorticoids is related to the half-life of the glucocorticoid-receptor complex, which is approximately 10 hours for Fluticasone propionate.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Lutisone 50 mcg/spray</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cream</strong>: Apply a thin layer of Fluticasone propionate cream to the affected skin areas once daily.</li><li><strong>Ointment</strong>: Apply a thin layer of Fluticasone propionate ointment to the affected skin areas twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Fluticasone propionate is contraindicated in:</p><ul><li>Rosacea</li><li>Acne vulgaris</li><li>Peri-oral dermatitis</li><li>Primary cutaneous viral infections (e.g., herpes simplex, chicken pox)</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients</li><li>Perianal and genital pruritus</li><li>It is also contraindicated in the treatment of primarily infected skin lesions caused by infection with fungi or bacteria and dermatoses in children under one year of age (including dermatitis and napkin eruptions).</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Lutisone 50 mcg/spray</strong></p><p>Lutisone 50 mcg/spray preparations are usually well tolerated. However, some side effects may include:</p><ul><li>Local burning and pruritus</li><li>Hypersensitivity reactions (discontinue if signs appear)</li><li>Prolonged and intensive use of potent corticosteroids may cause local atrophic changes (e.g., thinning, striae, dilatation of superficial blood vessels, hypertrichosis, hypopigmentation)</li><li>Secondary infections (especially with occlusive dressings or skin folds)</li><li>Allergic contact dermatitis</li><li>Exacerbation of the signs and symptoms of dermatoses</li><li>Prolonged use of large amounts of corticosteroids may result in systemic absorption, leading to hypercorticism, particularly in infants and children or with the use of occlusive dressings.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Lutisone 50 mcg/spray should be used during pregnancy only if the expected benefit to the mother is greater than any potential risk to the fetus. The excretion of Lutisone 50 mcg/spray into human breast milk has not been studied. Plasma levels after dermal application are likely to be low, but the therapeutic benefits should be weighed against any potential risks when used by breastfeeding mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Fluticasone propionate has a low propensity for systemic absorption, but prolonged use of high doses, particularly in infants and small children, could lead to adrenal suppression.</li><li>Children may absorb larger amounts of topical corticosteroids and be more susceptible to systemic toxicity.</li><li>The face is particularly prone to atrophic changes after prolonged use of potent topical corticosteroids.</li><li>Appropriate antimicrobial therapy should be used when treating infected inflammatory lesions.</li><li>Any spread of infection requires discontinuation of topical corticosteroid therapy and initiation of systemic antimicrobial agents.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Lutisone 50 mcg/spray</strong></p><p>Acute overdose is very unlikely. Chronic overdose or misuse may result in the features of hypercorticism. In such cases, discontinuation of the application should be done. Overdosage by ingestion is extremely unlikely due to the low oral bioavailability of Lutisone 50 mcg/spray.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30˚ C. Do not freeze.</p>', 'active', '1264_1742763105.jpg', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:51:45', '2025-05-03 23:37:39'),
(4397, 'Antanil Plus', 'Aluminium Hydroxide + Magnesium Hydroxide + Simethicone', 75.00, 1, 'x 200 ml bottle', '200 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '(200 mg+400 mg+30 mg)/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Antanil Plus 200 ml</strong></p><p>This is indicated for symptomatic relief of hyperacidity associated with:</p><ul><li>Peptic ulcer</li><li>Gastritis</li><li>Peptic oesophagitis</li><li>Gastric hyperacidity</li><li>Heartburn</li><li>Sour stomach</li><li>Hiatus hernia</li></ul><p>It is effective in the prevention of stress ulceration and GI bleeding. It acts as an antiflatulent to alleviate the symptoms of gas, including post-operative gas pain. This rapidly relieves acid pain, disperses gastric foam, and facilitates eructation (burping) of gas and air.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Dried Aluminium Hydroxide Gel BP (Al2O3, 47% minimum): 425.53 mg</li><li>Magnesium Hydroxide BP: 400 mg</li><li>Simethicone USP: 30 mg</li></ul><p>Each 5 ml contains:</p><ul><li>Aluminium Oxide (Equivalent amount of Aluminium Hydroxide Gel USP): 200 mg</li><li>Magnesium Hydroxide (Equivalent amount of Magnesium Hydroxide Paste USP): 400 mg</li><li>Simethicone USP: 30 mg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This is a mixture of non-systemic acid neutralizing substances and antiflatulent. The preparation offers reliable, long-lasting action.</p><ul><li><strong>Aluminium Hydroxide and Magnesium Hydroxide</strong> neutralize gastric acid secreted by parietal cells of the stomach, helping relieve ulcers.</li><li><strong>Simethicone</strong> works as an antifoam agent. It reduces the surface tension of foam bubbles, causing them to collapse and allowing gas bubbles surrounded by mucus to coalesce and be expelled.</li></ul><p>It is used for the treatment of flatulence, meteorism, abdominal distension, and dyspepsia. It can also help eliminate gas or foam from the gastrointestinal tract prior to radiography. Simethicone is physiologically inert and does not interfere with gastric secretion or nutrient absorption. After oral administration, it is excreted unchanged in the feces.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong> 1-2 tablets 1-3 hours after meals and at bedtime, or as directed by the physician.</li><li><strong>Suspension:</strong> 1-2 teaspoonfuls 1-3 hours after meals and at bedtime, or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><p>Antacids may affect the rate and extent of absorption of concomitant oral drugs. For example, antacids can decrease the bioavailability of:</p><ul><li>Theophylline, tetracyclines, quinolone antibiotics, isoniazid, ketoconazole, ethambutol, and others.</li><li>Antacids may increase the bioavailability of:</li><li>Sulphonamides, levodopa, valproic acid, enteric-coated aspirin, and others.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Patients with renal failure</li><li>Hypophosphataemia</li><li>Severely debilitated individuals</li><li>Alkalosis or hypermagnesaemia, especially if abdominal distention is caused by intestinal obstruction.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><p>Gastrointestinal side effects are uncommon. However, excessive consumption may result in:</p><ul><li>Diarrhea</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Regurgitation</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>It is advised to avoid antacid preparations in the first trimester of pregnancy.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with kidney disease.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p>Antacids</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Aluminium Hydroxide and Magnesium Hydroxide</strong> neutralize stomach acid to relieve ulcers.</li><li><strong>Simethicone</strong> works as an antifoam agent, promoting the expulsion of gas from the gastrointestinal tract.</li></ul><p>If you need more information or clarification, feel free to ask!</p>', 'active', '228_1742763245.jpg', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:54:05', '2025-05-02 23:41:02'),
(4398, 'Atrizin', 'Cetirizine Hydrochloride', 30.00, 1, 'x 60 ml bottle', '60 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '5 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Atrizin 60 ml</strong></p><p>Atrizin 60 ml is indicated for the relief of symptoms associated with seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis. It is also indicated for the treatment of the uncomplicated skin manifestations of chronic idiopathic urticaria and allergen-induced asthma.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Sedating Anti-histamine</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Atrizin 60 ml is a potent H1 receptor antagonist without any significant anticholinergic and antiserotonic effects. At pharmacologically active dose levels, it has almost no drowsiness effect and does not cause behavioral changes. It inhibits the histamine-mediated early phase of the allergic reaction and also reduces the migration of inflammatory cells and the release of mediators associated with the late phase of the allergic reaction.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Atrizin 60 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults and Children 6 years and older</strong>: 1 tablet or 2 teaspoonfuls daily (or 1 teaspoonful twice daily).</li><li><strong>Children 2-6 years</strong>: 1 teaspoonful once daily or 1/2 teaspoonful twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children 6 months to 2 years</strong>: 1/2 teaspoonful once daily. The dose in children 12-23 months of age can be increased to a maximum dose of 1/2 teaspoonful every 12 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Atrizin 60 ml</strong></p><p>No clinically significant drug interactions have been found with Theophylline, Azithromycin, Pseudoephedrine, Ketoconazole, or Erythromycin and with other drugs.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Cetirizine or hydroxyzine.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Atrizin 60 ml</strong></p><p>The most common side effect that occurred more frequently with Cetirizine is somnolence (drowsiness).</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>US FDA Pregnancy Category of Cetirizine Hydrochloride is B. There are, however, no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Because animal reproduction studies are not always predictive of human response, this drug should be used during pregnancy only if clearly needed. Cetirizine Hydrochloride has been shown to be excreted in human milk. So, caution should be exercised when Cetirizine Hydrochloride is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Caution should be exercised when driving a car or operating heavy machinery.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '782_1742763362.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:56:02', '2025-05-03 00:06:21');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4399, 'Bexitrol F', 'Salmeterol + Fluticasone Propionate', 1220.00, 1, 'x 60 doses maxhaler', '60 doses maxhaler', 'Inhaler', '50 mcg+500 mcg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Bexitrol F 50 mcg + 500 mcg:</strong></p><p>Bexitrol F is used for the regular treatment of asthma when a combination product (long-acting β2-agonist and inhaled corticosteroid) is appropriate. It is indicated for:</p><ul><li>Patients who are not adequately controlled with inhaled corticosteroids and \'as needed\' inhaled short-acting β2-agonists.</li><li>Patients already controlled on both inhaled corticosteroids and long-acting β2-agonists.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants</li><li>Respiratory corticosteroids</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate:</strong> A selective, long-acting β2-agonist (12 hours) used to treat asthma and other forms of airways obstruction.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate:</strong> A corticosteroid with glucocorticoid activity, working topically on the lungs, with minimal systemic effects at recommended doses. It reduces symptoms and prevents exacerbations.</li></ul><p>Salmeterol provides protection against asthma symptoms, while Fluticasone improves lung function and reduces flare-ups. This combination offers a more convenient regimen for patients on both β-agonists and corticosteroids.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Inhalation Aerosol:</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older):</strong>2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol &amp; 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate, or</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol &amp; 125 µg Fluticasone Propionate, or</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol &amp; 250 µg Fluticasone Propionate, <strong>twice daily</strong>.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4-12 years):</strong> 2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol &amp; 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate, <strong>twice daily</strong>.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Inhalation Powder in Capsule (For Asthma):</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older):</strong> Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg or 250 µg twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4-11 years):</strong> Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg twice daily.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Inhalation Powder in Maxhaler (For Asthma &amp; COPD):</strong></li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Adults and adolescents (12 years and older):</strong>50/100 Maxhaler: 1 inhalation twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">50/250 Maxhaler: 1 inhalation twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">50/500 Maxhaler: 1 inhalation twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (4 years and older):</strong> 50/100 Maxhaler: 1 inhalation twice daily (max 100 µg fluticasone).</li></ul><p><strong>Administration Tips:</strong></p><ul><li>Shake the inhaler before use, and check the technique regularly.</li><li>Rinse mouth after each use to reduce side effects like oral thrush.</li><li>Clean inhaler weekly to prevent malfunction.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions:</strong></p><ul><li>Avoid non-selective and selective β-blockers unless essential.</li><li>Care should be taken when co-administering strong CYP3A4 inhibitors like ketoconazole and ritonavir, as they may increase systemic exposure to Fluticasone Propionate.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol:</strong> Tremors, palpitations, headache, arrhythmia, hypersensitivity reactions (rash, angioedema), oropharyngeal irritation, muscle cramps.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate:</strong> Hoarseness, candidiasis (thrush), hypersensitivity reactions, oropharyngeal edema.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Use only if the benefit to the mother outweighs potential risks. Limited data is available for its use during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in patients with active or quiescent pulmonary tuberculosis or thyrotoxicosis.</li><li>Use with care in patients with a history of infections or other comorbid conditions.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage:</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30°C and away from direct sunlight and heat. Keep away from children and eyes. Do not puncture or burn the canister.</li></ul><p><strong>Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li>No dosage adjustment needed for elderly or those with renal impairment. Limited data for hepatic impairment and pediatric use.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes:</strong></p><ul><li>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants, Respiratory corticosteroids</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol:</strong> A long-acting β2-agonist that provides prolonged bronchodilation.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate:</strong> A potent anti-inflammatory corticosteroid that reduces asthma symptoms and exacerbations by acting locally in the lungs.</li></ul>', 'active', '149_1742763537.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 01:58:57', '2025-05-03 00:49:42'),
(4400, 'Deslorin', 'Desloratadine', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '5 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Deslorin 5 mg:</strong></p><p>Deslorin 5 mg is indicated for the relief of symptoms associated with seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis, such as:</p><ul><li>Sneezing</li><li>Nasal discharge and itching</li><li>Congestion or stuffiness</li><li>Ocular itching, tearing, and redness</li><li>Itching of the palate</li><li>Coughing</li></ul><p>Deslorin 5 mg is also indicated for the relief of symptoms associated with chronic idiopathic urticaria, such as:</p><ul><li>Itching</li><li>Reduction in the size and number of hives</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Deslorin 5 mg is a long-acting, tricyclic, non-sedating, selective peripheral histamine H1-receptor antagonist. It works by inhibiting the release of pro-inflammatory mediators from human mast cells and basophils.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Deslorin 5 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Drops:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child 6 - 11 months: 2 ml drops once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child 1 - 2 years: 2.5 ml drops once daily</li><li><strong>Syrup:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child 6 - 11 months: 2 ml once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child 1 - 5 years: 2.5 ml once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Child 6 - 11 years: 5 ml once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults &amp; &gt;12 years: 10 ml once daily</li><li><strong>Tablet:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adults and children ≥12 years: 5 mg daily</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Interactions:</strong></p><p>No clinically relevant drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><p>Deslorin 5 mg is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to this medication or to any of its ingredients, including Loratadine.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Side Effects of Deslorin 5 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Less common side effects may include:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Headache</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nausea</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fatigue</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dizziness</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pharyngitis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dyspepsia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Myalgia</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> There are no adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Deslorin 5 mg should only be used in pregnancy if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Deslorin 5 mg passes into breast milk. A decision should be made whether to discontinue nursing or discontinue Deslorin 5 mg, taking into account the importance of the drug to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>In adult patients with liver or renal impairment:</strong> A starting dose of one 5 mg tablet every other day is recommended.</li><li><strong>Seizure history:</strong> Use with caution in patients with a medical or familial history of seizures.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Deslorin 5 mg:</strong></p><p>No clinically relevant adverse effects have been reported with overdose.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><p>Store in a cool &amp; dry place, protected from light. Keep below 30°C and out of reach of children.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness of Deslorin 5 mg in pediatric patients under 2 years of age have not been established.</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong> Caution should be used when dosing elderly patients, considering possible decreased hepatic, renal, or cardiac function.</li><li><strong>Liver or Renal Impairment:</strong> Starting dose of one 5 mg tablet every other day is recommended.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><p>Non-sedating antihistamines</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><p>Deslorin 5 mg, the active metabolite of Loratadine, is a non-sedating, long-acting tricyclic histamine antagonist with selective H1-receptor activity. It also inhibits histamine release from human mast cells.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pregnancy Category:</strong></p><p>Category C. Deslorin 5 mg is not to be used during pregnancy unless clearly indicated.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong></p><p>Deslorin 5 mg is recommended for use in children and adolescents as indicated.</p>', 'active', '184_1742763630.png', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:00:30', '2025-05-03 08:20:49'),
(4401, 'Coralex', 'Coral Calcium + Vitamin D3', 11.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+200 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Coralex 1250 mg + 200 IU</strong></p><p>This medication is indicated for the treatment and prevention of:</p><ul><li>Osteoporosis</li><li>Osteomalacia</li><li>Tetany</li><li>Hypoparathyroidism</li><li>Disorders of osteogenesis</li></ul><p>It is also used as a supplement in cases of inadequate intake of Calcium in childhood diets, rickets, pregnancy, lactation, and in elderly patients. Other indications include:</p><ul><li>Pancreatitis</li><li>Phosphate binder in chronic renal failure, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Carbonate 1500 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 600 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 400 IU as Cholecalciferol</li><li>Or</li><li>Calcium Carbonate 1250 mg (from Coral source) equivalent to elemental Calcium 500 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 200 IU as Cholecalciferol</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate Calcium in the blood is essential for a variety of bodily functions. Clinical evidence suggests that Calcium is effective for the prevention and treatment of Osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is also necessary for healthy bones as it aids in Calcium absorption from the GI tract and stimulates bone formation. Studies show that Calcium and Vitamin D work synergistically to promote bone growth and prevent Osteoporosis and fractures.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Coralex 1250 mg + 200 IU</strong></p><ul><li>One tablet in the morning and one tablet at night, or as directed by the physician.</li><li>Dosage: One tablet once or twice daily with plenty of water, or as directed by the physician. It is recommended to take it with a full stomach for better absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Coralex 1250 mg + 200 IU</strong></p><ul><li>Oral Calcium can reduce the absorption of tetracycline and fluoride preparations; at least 3 hours should be allowed between these medications.</li><li>Thiazide diuretics can reduce the renal excretion of Calcium.</li><li>Phenytoin, barbiturates, and glucocorticoids may induce metabolism of Vitamin D3.</li><li>Certain foods like spinach, cereals, and milk products may reduce the intestinal uptake of Calcium.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any of the components</li><li>Hypocalcaemia resulting from an overdose of Vitamin D3</li><li>Hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Bone metastases</li><li>Severe renal insufficiency</li><li>Severe hypercalciuria</li><li>Renal calculi, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Coralex 1250 mg + 200 IU</strong></p><ul><li>Rare side effects include flatulence, diarrhoea, constipation, upper GI discomfort.</li><li>Hypercalcaemia due to prolonged use has been rarely reported.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>It should be used during pregnancy and lactation only as directed by the physician.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In the presence of mild hypercalciuria, careful monitoring with dose reduction may be required.</li><li>Plasma and serum Calcium levels should be monitored in mild to moderate renal impairment, especially with long-term use.</li><li>Patients with a history of renal stones should take precautions.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Coralex 1250 mg + 200 IU</strong></p><ul><li>High doses may cause nausea, vomiting, dizziness, anorexia, abdominal cramps, headache, constipation, irritability, etc.</li><li>Treatment includes cessation of therapy and adequate rehydration.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at a temperature below 30°C.</li><li>Protect from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This is a Calcium and Vitamin D3 preparation, where Calcium Carbonate is sourced from coral origin. The Calcium Carbonate from Coral has a chemical structure similar to human bone, ensuring better absorption. Vitamin D3 aids in the absorption of Calcium from the GI tract and helps maintain Calcium balance in the body.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This medication can be given to pregnant and lactating mothers as per the physician\'s recommendation.</p>', 'active', '1227_1742764667.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:17:47', '2025-05-03 01:40:11'),
(4402, 'Procap', 'Omeprazole', 6.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is the provided information in bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Procap 20 mg</strong></p><p>Procap 20 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcer</li><li>Gastric and duodenal ulcers brought on by NSAIDs</li><li>In individuals who have a history of duodenal and stomach ulcers brought on by NSAIDs, as a preventative treatment</li><li>The disease of gastroesophageal reflux</li><li>Care of acid reflux illness throughout the long term</li><li>Dyspepsia brought on by acid</li><li>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers</li><li>Prevention of aspiration of acid while under general anesthesia</li><li>Syndrome of Zollinger-Ellison</li><li>Peptic ulcer brought on by Helicobacter pylori</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Blocker</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>An inhibitor of stomach acid secretion is Procap 20 mg, a benzimidazole replacement. By inhibiting the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in the gastric parietal cell, it prevents the release of gastric acid. Following oral administration, the antisecretory effect starts to take effect in about an hour, peaks in two hours, and the inhibition of secretion lasts up to 72 hours. Secretory activity gradually resumes when the medicine is stopped, over a period of 3 to 5 days.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Procap 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Oral</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Benign gastric and duodenal ulcer: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks in duodenal ulceration, 8 weeks in gastric ulceration; in severe or recurrent cases, dose to be increased to 40 mg daily; maintenance dose for recurrent duodenal ulcer, 20 mg once daily; in prevention of relapse in duodenal ulcer, 10-20 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">NSAID-associated duodenal or gastric ulcer: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continued for further 4 weeks, if not fully healed. 20 mg once daily is used as prophylaxis in patients with a history of NSAID-associated duodenal or gastric ulcers.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gastro-esophageal reflux disease: 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continued for further 4-8 weeks, if not fully healed; 40 mg once daily has been given for 8 weeks in gastro-esophageal reflux disease, refractory to other treatment; maintenance dose is 20 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Long-term management of acid reflux disease: 10-20 mg daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acid-related dyspepsia: 10-20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prophylaxis of acid aspiration: 40 mg on the preceding evening, then 40 mg 2-6 hours before surgery.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Zollinger-Ellison syndrome: Initially 60 mg once daily; usual range 20-120 mg daily (If daily dose is more than 80 mg, 2 divided doses should be used).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Helicobacter pylori eradication regimen in peptic ulcer disease: Procap 20 mg is recommended at a dose of 20 mg twice daily in association with antimicrobial agents as detailed below: Amoxicillin 500 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg both three times a day for one week, or Clarithromycin 250 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg both twice a day for one week, or Amoxicillin 1 g and Clarithromycin 500 mg both twice a day for one week.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Paediatric use in severe ulcerating reflux esophagitis (Child &gt;1 year): If body-weight 10-20 kg, 10-20 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks; if body-weight over 20 kg, 20-40 mg once daily for 4-12 weeks.</li><li><strong>IV Injection</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prophylaxis of acid aspiration: Procap 20 mg 40 mg to be given slowly (over a period of 5 minutes) as an intravenous injection, one hour before surgery.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Duodenal ulcer, gastric ulcer or reflux oesophagitis: In patients with duodenal ulcer, gastric ulcer or reflux oesophagitis where oral medication is inappropriate, Procap 20 mg IV 40 mg once daily is recommended.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (ZES): In patients with Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome the recommended initial dose of Procap 20 mg given intravenously is 60 mg daily. Higher daily doses may be required and the dose should be adjusted individually. When doses exceed 60 mg daily, the dose should be divided &amp; given twice daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Procap 20 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Direction for use of IV Injection:</strong> Procap 20 mg lyophilized powder and water for injection should only be administered intravenously; it cannot be administered in any other way. Slow intravenous injections of Procap 20 mg should be administered. By adding 10 ml of water for injection to the vial containing the powder, the solution for IV injection can be created. The injection should be administered slowly after reconstitution over a period of at least 2 to 5 minutes at a maximum flow rate of 4 ml/minute. Use just recently made solutions. After reconstitution, the solution must be used within four hours. Procap 20 mg IV infusion should be administered as an intravenous infusion over a period of at least 20 to 30 minutes. One vial\'s contents need to be dissolved in 100 ml of either 5% dextrose or saline for infusion. When Procap 20 mg is dissolved in saline, the solution must be used within 12 hours; when it is dissolved in 5% dextrose, it must be used within 6 hours. No other medication should be mixed or administered in the same infusion set as the reconstituted solution.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Procap 20 mg</strong></p><p>Warfarin, phenytoin, and diazepam might all take longer to leave the body when taken with Procap 20 mg. Due to this, when Procap 20 mg is added to the treatment, a reduction in the dose of warfarin or phenytoin may be required. Theophylline, propranolol, or antacids have not been shown to interact with Procap 20 mg.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Those who have a history of recognized hypersensitivity to any of the formulation\'s ingredients should not take Procap 20 mg.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Procap 20 mg</strong></p><p>Most people tolerate Procap 20 mg well. It has been reported that mild and temporary side effects such as nausea, stomach cramping, paresthesia, dizziness, and headache rarely require dosage adjustments.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Procap 20 mg is within the C pregnancy category according to the US FDA. Three prospective epidemiological studies\' findings, however, show that Procap 20 mg has no negative impact on pregnancy or the health of the fetus or baby. Procap 20 mg\'s entry into breast milk and its effects on the developing baby are not known. So, if Procap 20 mg use is deemed necessary, breastfeeding should be stopped.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Before starting therapy with Procap 20 mg for a suspected stomach ulcer, it is important to rule out the possibility of gastric cancer because the medication may make the symptoms worse and postpone a diagnosis.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep dry and away from heat and light. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Blocker</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>A benzimidazole replacement called Procap 20 mg inhibits the production of stomach acid. By inhibiting the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in the gastric parietal cell, it prevents the release of gastric acid. Upon oral administration, the antisecretory effect begins to take effect within an hour, reaches its peak within two hours, and the inhibition of secretion lasts up to 72 hours. After the medicine is stopped, secretory activity gradually resumes over a period of 3 to 5 days.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Procap 20 mg is within the C pregnancy category according to the US FDA. Three prospective epidemiological studies\' findings, however, show that Procap 20 mg has no negative impact on pregnancy or the health of the fetus or baby. Procap 20 mg\'s entry into breast milk and its effects on the developing baby are not known. So, if Procap 20 mg use is deemed necessary, breastfeeding should be stopped.</p>', 'active', '1922_1742764756.jpg', 43, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:19:16', '2025-05-04 10:30:34'),
(4403, 'Fastel', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 13.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '180 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Fastel 180 mg (Fexofenadine) Overview</h3><h4><strong>Indications:</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Allergic Rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children 12 years and older</strong>: For the relief of symptoms associated with seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis (e.g., sneezing, rhinorrhea, itchy eyes, nose, throat, and palate). It enhances productivity and health-related quality of life.</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children 12 years and older</strong>: For the relief of symptoms such as pruritus (itching) and wheals (hives). It significantly decreases the frequency and severity of these symptoms and improves quality of life.</li></ul><h4><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong>:</h4><ul><li>Non-sedating antihistamines</li></ul><h4><strong>Pharmacology</strong>:</h4><ul><li>Fastel 180 mg is a selective peripheral <strong>H1-receptor antagonist</strong>. It is rapidly absorbed and reaches peak plasma concentration in 2–3 hours. The drug does <strong>not</strong> cross the blood-brain barrier, minimizing sedative effects.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong>:</h3><h4><strong>For Allergic Rhinitis</strong>:</h4><ul><li><strong>Adults and children 12 years and older</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 11 years</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Children 2 to 11 years (Suspension)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">30 mg or 5 ml twice daily</li></ul><h4><strong>For Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>:</h4><ul><li><strong>Adults and children 12 years and older</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Children 6 to 11 years</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Children 6 months to &lt;2 years (Suspension)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">15 mg or 2.5 ml twice daily</li><li><strong>Children 2 to 11 years (Suspension)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">30 mg or 5 ml twice daily</li></ul><h4><strong>Renal Impairment Adjustments</strong>:</h4><ul><li><strong>Impaired Renal Function</strong>: Reduce dosage to 60 mg once daily for adults, or 30 mg once daily for children (adjusted per age and weight).</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Erythromycin or Ketoconazole</strong>: These may increase fexofenadine levels by 2-3 times, but there is no effect on the QT interval or increased adverse reactions.</li><li><strong>Antacids (containing aluminum and magnesium hydroxide)</strong>: These may reduce fexofenadine bioavailability. It is recommended to separate doses by at least 2 hours.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to fexofenadine or any of its components.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Common</strong>: Headaches, fatigue, dizziness, nausea, and gastrointestinal discomfort.</li><li><strong>Uncommon</strong>: Fatigue, anxiety, and palpitations.</li><li><strong>Rare</strong>: Hypersensitivity reactions like angioedema, tightness in the chest, systemic anaphylaxis, and severe skin reactions.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy (Category C)</strong>: Not enough data to support safety. Should be avoided unless benefits outweigh risks.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Fexofenadine is excreted in breast milk and may affect breastfeeding infants. It is not recommended for nursing mothers.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Elderly &amp; Renal/Hepatic Impairment</strong>: Use with caution and under careful supervision.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Caution</strong>: Use with caution in patients with a history of cardiovascular disease as antihistamines may cause tachycardia or palpitations.</li><li><strong>Driving &amp; Operating Machinery</strong>: Fexofenadine is unlikely to affect the ability to drive or operate machinery, but individual sensitivity should be considered.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Symptoms may include dry mouth, drowsiness, fatigue, and dizziness. In clinical studies, doses up to 800 mg were well tolerated. In the case of overdose, symptomatic treatment and psychological support are advised. Hemodialysis is not effective in removing fexofenadine.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Store in a cool, dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong>:</h3><ul><li>Fexofenadine is an <strong>inverse agonist</strong> at the H1 histamine receptor. It prevents histamine from activating the receptor, thus reducing allergic responses like itching, sneezing, and watery eyes. It does not significantly affect the central nervous system due to its inability to cross the blood-brain barrier.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Use</strong>:</h3><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Dosing adjustments are necessary, with a recommended dose of 60 mg once daily for impaired renal function.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Fastel 180 mg is safe for use in children older than 6 months with appropriate dosing and adjustments based on age and renal function.</li></ul>', 'active', '1797_1742764848.webp', 10, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:20:48', '2025-05-03 10:28:31'),
(4404, 'Clotinex', 'Enoxaparin Sodium', 451.36, 1, 'Injection', '1 piece in a box', 'Injection', '4000 Anti-Xa IU/0.4 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>Cutin 5000 mcg is indicated for hair loss, weak, brittle, splitting or soft nails, eczema, and dermatitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Herbal and Nutraceuticals</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cutin 5000 mcg is an essential vitamin, also known as vitamin H. It is involved in vital physiological functions like fatty acid synthesis, amino acid catabolism, and gluconeogenesis. It acts as a cofactor in the enzymatic carboxylation of pyruvate and acetyl-CoA. Cutin 5000 mcg influences the growth and differentiation of epidermal cells, making it crucial for the formation and renewal of the skin, hair, and nails. It functions as a coenzyme for mitochondrial carboxylases in hair roots and improves keratin structure. Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency may lead to hair loss and a variety of systemic symptoms such as dermatitis and aciduria. Cutin 5000 mcg is one of the most prescribed nutritional supplements for any kind of hair loss.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>The dosage of Cutin 5000 mcg varies depending on the disease condition and age group. Generally, 1-3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician. In children, Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency affects those between 1 week and 2 years of age, often exhibiting seizures, hypotonia, developmental delay, ataxia, hyperventilation, and coma. In this case, 5-10 tablets daily should be crushed and mixed with water or fruit juice, or as advised by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>There are indications that anticonvulsant drugs may lower the plasma level of Cutin 5000 mcg.</li><li>Antibiotic use may decrease the Cutin 5000 mcg contribution to the body made by the microflora of the large intestine.</li><li>Excessive consumption of raw egg whites interferes with Cutin 5000 mcg absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Sufficient clinical data is not available to use in pregnant women and lactating mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1928_1742764938.webp', 4, 12, 'ajd85485@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:22:18', '2025-03-24 02:22:18'),
(4405, 'Almex', 'Albendazole', 23.00, 1, 'x 10 ml bottle', '10 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '200 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Almex 10 ml</strong></p><p>Almex 10 ml is indicated in single and mixed infestations of:</p><ul><li>Hookworm (Ancylostoma, Necator)</li><li>Roundworm (Ascaris)</li><li>Threadworm (Enterobius)</li><li>Whipworm (Trichuris)</li><li>Strongyloides</li><li>Tapeworm</li><li>Opisthorchi</li><li>Hydatid</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Anthelmintic</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Almex 10 ml is a broad-spectrum benzimidazole anthelminthic drug. In addition to its vermicidal properties, it has both ovicidal and larvicidal activities.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Almex 10 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; children over 2 years</strong>: 400 mg (1 tablet or 10 ml suspension) as a single dose in cases of Enterobius vermicularis, Trichuris trichiura, Ascaris lumbricoides, Ancylostoma duodenale, and Necator americanus.</li><li>In cases of strongyloidiasis or taeniasis, 400 mg (1 tablet or 10 ml suspension) daily should be given for 3 consecutive days. If the patient is not cured on follow-up after three weeks, a second course of treatment is indicated.</li><li><strong>Children of 1-2 years</strong>: Recommended dose is a single dose of 200 mg (5 ml suspension).</li><li><strong>Children under 1 year</strong>: Not recommended.</li><li><strong>In Hydatid disease (Echinococcosis)</strong>:</li><li>Almex 10 ml is given by mouth with meals in a dose of 400 mg twice daily for 28 days for patients weighing over 60 kg. A dose of 15 mg/kg body weight daily in two divided doses (to a maximum total daily dose of 800 mg) is used for patients weighing less than 60 kg. For cystic echinococcosis, the 28-day course may be repeated after 14 days without treatment, to a total of 3 treatment cycles. For alveolar echinococcosis, cycles of 28 days of treatment followed by 14 days without treatment may need to continue for months or years.</li><li><strong>In giardiasis</strong>: 400 mg (1 tablet or 10 ml suspension) once daily for five days.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Almex 10 ml</strong></p><p>No interaction involving Almex 10 ml, either pharmacodynamic or pharmacokinetic, has been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Neonates</strong>: Almex 10 ml is not normally used in neonates.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>: Reduction of the dose from 400 mg to 200 mg may be indicated in children weighing less than 10 kg, but there are no grounds for a general reduction in dosage to children.</li><li><strong>Pregnant women</strong>: Almex 10 ml should not be given during pregnancy or women thought to be pregnant. No information is available on placental transfer.</li><li><strong>Concurrent disease</strong>: There is no evidence to suggest that the dose should be altered in renal, hepatic, or cardiac failure.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Almex 10 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Gastrointestinal disturbances</li><li>Headache</li><li>Dizziness</li><li>Changes in liver enzymes</li><li>Rarely reversible alopecia</li><li>Rash</li><li>Fever</li><li>Blood disorders including leucopenia and pancytopenia reported</li><li>Allergic shock if cyst leakage</li><li>Convulsion and meningism in cerebral disease</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>US FDA Pregnancy category of Almex 10 ml is C. Therefore, Almex 10 ml should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Blood counts and liver function tests before treatment and twice during each cycle.</li><li>Breastfeeding: Exclude pregnancy before starting treatment.</li><li>Almex 10 ml should only be used in the treatment of Echinococcosis if there is constant medical supervision with regular monitoring of serum-transaminase concentrations and of leucocyte and platelet counts.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Anthelmintic</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Almex 10 ml is a broad-spectrum anthelmintic. Almex 10 ml exhibits vermicidal, ovicidal, and larvicidal activities. The drug is thought to exert its anthelmintic effect by blocking glucose uptake in the susceptible helminths, thereby depleting the energy level until it becomes inadequate for survival. Immobilization is followed by the parasite. These events may be a consequence of the binding and subsequent inhibition of parasite tubulin polymerization by Almex 10 ml and its metabolites, although the drug also binds to human tubulin. Almex 10 ml is extensively metabolized, probably in the liver. Almex 10 ml is poorly absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract but rapidly undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism. The principal metabolite Almex 10 ml sulphoxide has anthelmintic activity and a plasma half-life of about 8.5 hrs. It is excreted in the urine together with other metabolites.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>US FDA Pregnancy category of Almex 10 ml is C. Therefore, Almex 10 ml should be avoided in pregnancy and lactation unless the potential benefits to the mother outweigh the possible risks to the fetus.</p>', 'active', '469_1742764946.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:22:26', '2025-05-02 23:01:01'),
(4406, 'Firmvit', 'Multivitamin + L-Lysine', 4.02, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '0 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Firmvit</h3><p>Firmvit is indicated for the following purposes:</p><ul><li><strong>Improves appetite</strong></li><li><strong>Promotes growth in height &amp; weight</strong></li><li><strong>Helps in building muscle protein</strong></li><li><strong>Aids in calcium absorption</strong></li><li><strong>Ensures energy production</strong></li></ul><h3>Composition</h3><p>Each tablet contains the following active ingredients:</p><ul><li>L-Lysine: 50 mg</li><li>Vitamin A: 1500 IU</li><li>Vitamin D3: 100 IU</li><li>Vitamin E: 10 IU</li><li>Vitamin B1: 0.25 mg</li><li>Vitamin B2: 0.25 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6: 0.25 mg</li><li>Vitamin B12: 2 mcg</li><li>Vitamin C: 50 mg</li><li>Nicotinamide: 2.5 mg</li><li>Folic Acid: 0.25 mg</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Specific combined vitamin preparations</strong></li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Firmvit</h3><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 3 tablets daily</li><li><strong>Children (from 1 year of age):</strong> 1-3 tablets daily, depending on age and physical condition</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>The product is contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to any of the active ingredients of the product.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Firmvit</h3><ul><li>The product is usually well tolerated and does not cause untoward effects when taken as per the recommended dosage.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>Specific information regarding the use of Firmvit during pregnancy and lactation is not available.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Keep the container tightly closed after use.</li><li>Store below 25°C.</li><li>Protect from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Drug Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Specific combined vitamin preparations</strong></li></ul><h3>Pregnancy</h3><ul><li>Specific information on usage during pregnancy is not available.</li></ul>', 'active', '1673_1742765057.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:24:17', '2025-05-03 10:41:24'),
(4407, 'Linalit-M', 'Linagliptin + Metformin Hydrochloride', 12.00, 30, 'Tablet', '30 piece in a box', 'Tablet', '2.5 mg+500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>Cutin 5000 mcg is indicated for hair loss, weak, brittle, splitting or soft nails, eczema, and dermatitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Herbal and Nutraceuticals</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cutin 5000 mcg is an essential vitamin, also known as vitamin H. It is involved in vital physiological functions like fatty acid synthesis, amino acid catabolism, and gluconeogenesis. It acts as a cofactor in the enzymatic carboxylation of pyruvate and acetyl-CoA. Cutin 5000 mcg influences the growth and differentiation of epidermal cells, making it crucial for the formation and renewal of the skin, hair, and nails. It functions as a coenzyme for mitochondrial carboxylases in hair roots and improves keratin structure. Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency may lead to hair loss and a variety of systemic symptoms such as dermatitis and aciduria. Cutin 5000 mcg is one of the most prescribed nutritional supplements for any kind of hair loss.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>The dosage of Cutin 5000 mcg varies depending on the disease condition and age group. Generally, 1-3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician. In children, Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency affects those between 1 week and 2 years of age, often exhibiting seizures, hypotonia, developmental delay, ataxia, hyperventilation, and coma. In this case, 5-10 tablets daily should be crushed and mixed with water or fruit juice, or as advised by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>There are indications that anticonvulsant drugs may lower the plasma level of Cutin 5000 mcg.</li><li>Antibiotic use may decrease the Cutin 5000 mcg contribution to the body made by the microflora of the large intestine.</li><li>Excessive consumption of raw egg whites interferes with Cutin 5000 mcg absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Sufficient clinical data is not available to use in pregnant women and lactating mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '682_1742765065.webp', 59, 12, 'ajd85485@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:24:25', '2025-03-24 02:24:25'),
(4408, 'Clamox', 'Amoxicillin + Clavulanic Acid', 33.00, 6, 'Tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+125 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>Cutin 5000 mcg is indicated for hair loss, weak, brittle, splitting or soft nails, eczema, and dermatitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Herbal and Nutraceuticals</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cutin 5000 mcg is an essential vitamin, also known as vitamin H. It is involved in vital physiological functions like fatty acid synthesis, amino acid catabolism, and gluconeogenesis. It acts as a cofactor in the enzymatic carboxylation of pyruvate and acetyl-CoA. Cutin 5000 mcg influences the growth and differentiation of epidermal cells, making it crucial for the formation and renewal of the skin, hair, and nails. It functions as a coenzyme for mitochondrial carboxylases in hair roots and improves keratin structure. Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency may lead to hair loss and a variety of systemic symptoms such as dermatitis and aciduria. Cutin 5000 mcg is one of the most prescribed nutritional supplements for any kind of hair loss.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>The dosage of Cutin 5000 mcg varies depending on the disease condition and age group. Generally, 1-3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician. In children, Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency affects those between 1 week and 2 years of age, often exhibiting seizures, hypotonia, developmental delay, ataxia, hyperventilation, and coma. In this case, 5-10 tablets daily should be crushed and mixed with water or fruit juice, or as advised by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>There are indications that anticonvulsant drugs may lower the plasma level of Cutin 5000 mcg.</li><li>Antibiotic use may decrease the Cutin 5000 mcg contribution to the body made by the microflora of the large intestine.</li><li>Excessive consumption of raw egg whites interferes with Cutin 5000 mcg absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Sufficient clinical data is not available to use in pregnant women and lactating mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '1686_1742765148.webp', 7, 12, 'ajd85485@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:25:48', '2025-03-24 02:25:48');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4409, 'Sulprex', 'Salbutamol + Ipratropium', 250.75, 1, 'x 200 metered doses', '200 metered doses', 'Metered-Dose Inhaler (MDI)', '(100 mcg+20 mcg)/puff', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sulprex (100 mcg+20 mcg)/puff</strong></p><p>This inhaler is indicated for use in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) who are on a regular aerosol bronchodilator but continue to have evidence of bronchospasm and require a second bronchodilator.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Combined bronchodilators</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Sulprex Inhaler contains Ipratropium bromide and Salbutamol:</p><ul><li><strong>Ipratropium Bromide</strong>: An anticholinergic agent that inhibits vagally-mediated reflexes by antagonizing the action of acetylcholine released from the vagus nerve. It prevents the increase in intracellular cGMP (cyclic guanosine monophosphate) caused by acetylcholine interacting with muscarinic receptors on bronchial smooth muscle, leading to bronchodilation.</li><li><strong>Salbutamol</strong>: A selective beta2-adrenoceptor agonist that acts on the beta2-adrenoceptors in bronchial smooth muscle, providing short-acting bronchodilation with a fast onset of action (within 5 minutes) and a duration of 4-6 hours.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sulprex (100 mcg+20 mcg)/puff</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Inhaler puffs</strong>: 2 puffs, 4 times a day. Additional puffs may be taken as required, but the total number should not exceed 12 puffs in 24 hours.</li><li><strong>Inhaler solution</strong>: Inhalation solution in ampoules may be used with a suitable nebulizer or intermittent positive pressure ventilator.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults (including elderly)</strong>: One ampoule as needed for symptom relief, or up to 3-4 ampoules daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Patients should consult a doctor or seek immediate medical attention in case of acute or worsening symptoms if additional inhalations do not provide adequate relief.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sulprex (100 mcg+20 mcg)/puff</strong></p><ul><li>This inhaler has been used alongside other COPD medications, including sympathomimetic bronchodilators, methylxanthines, and steroids, without significant adverse reactions. No formal drug interaction studies have been conducted.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>This inhaler is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to soya lecithin, soybeans, peanuts, or any component of the drug, as well as to atropine or its derivatives.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Sulprex (100 mcg+20 mcg)/puff</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Salbutamol</strong>: Mild tremor and headache (usually resolve with continued treatment), very rare reports of muscle cramps, and hypersensitivity reactions such as angioedema, urticaria, bronchospasm, hypotension, and collapse.</li><li><strong>Ipratropium</strong>: Headache, pain, influenza, chest pain, nausea, bronchitis, dyspnea, coughing, pneumonia, and bronchospasm in the lower airways. Upper airway side effects include pharyngitis, sinusitis, and rhinitis.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Category C. There are no adequate studies, so it should only be used if the potential benefit justifies the risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: It\'s not known whether the drug is excreted in human milk, so breastfeeding mothers should only use it if the benefits outweigh the risks.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Ipratropium Bromide</strong>: Use with caution in patients with narrow-angle glaucoma, prostatic hyperplasia, or bladder-neck obstruction.</li><li><strong>Salbutamol</strong>: Use with caution in patients with convulsive disorders, hyperthyroidism, or diabetes mellitus. It should be avoided in those unusually responsive to sympathomimetic amines.</li><li><strong>Hypokalemia</strong>: Beta-adrenergic agents like Salbutamol may cause significant hypokalemia, which can result in cardiovascular effects.</li><li><strong>Liver &amp; Renal Insufficiency</strong>: The inhaler has not been studied in patients with hepatic or renal insufficiency, so it should be used with caution in these populations.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sulprex (100 mcg+20 mcg)/puff</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symptoms of overdose (primarily related to Salbutamol)</strong>: Tachycardia, anginal pain, hypertension, hypotension, palpitations, tremor, arrhythmia, and flushing.</li><li><strong>Therapy</strong>: Sedatives, tranquillisers, and intensive therapy may be needed. Beta-receptor blockers (preferably beta1-selective) can be used as antidotes, but they must be used cautiously in patients with bronchial asthma.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Safety and efficacy have not been established in the pediatric population.</li></ul>', 'active', '1114_1742765164.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:26:04', '2025-04-23 07:50:48'),
(4410, 'Prostanil MR', 'Tamsulosin Hydrochloride', 10.00, 6, 'Capsul', '6 Capsul in a strip', 'Capsule', '0.4 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>Cutin 5000 mcg is indicated for hair loss, weak, brittle, splitting or soft nails, eczema, and dermatitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Herbal and Nutraceuticals</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cutin 5000 mcg is an essential vitamin, also known as vitamin H. It is involved in vital physiological functions like fatty acid synthesis, amino acid catabolism, and gluconeogenesis. It acts as a cofactor in the enzymatic carboxylation of pyruvate and acetyl-CoA. Cutin 5000 mcg influences the growth and differentiation of epidermal cells, making it crucial for the formation and renewal of the skin, hair, and nails. It functions as a coenzyme for mitochondrial carboxylases in hair roots and improves keratin structure. Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency may lead to hair loss and a variety of systemic symptoms such as dermatitis and aciduria. Cutin 5000 mcg is one of the most prescribed nutritional supplements for any kind of hair loss.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>The dosage of Cutin 5000 mcg varies depending on the disease condition and age group. Generally, 1-3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician. In children, Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency affects those between 1 week and 2 years of age, often exhibiting seizures, hypotonia, developmental delay, ataxia, hyperventilation, and coma. In this case, 5-10 tablets daily should be crushed and mixed with water or fruit juice, or as advised by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>There are indications that anticonvulsant drugs may lower the plasma level of Cutin 5000 mcg.</li><li>Antibiotic use may decrease the Cutin 5000 mcg contribution to the body made by the microflora of the large intestine.</li><li>Excessive consumption of raw egg whites interferes with Cutin 5000 mcg absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Sufficient clinical data is not available to use in pregnant women and lactating mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '523_1742765224.jpg', 29, 12, 'ajd85485@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:27:04', '2025-03-24 02:27:04'),
(4411, 'Century Forte', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral [A-Z gold preparation]', 9.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '0 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Century Forte</strong></p><p>Century Forte is indicated for preventing and treating deficiencies in vitamins and minerals. This comprehensive daily nutritional supplement is also recommended to meet the heightened requirements for vitamins and minerals in various situations, including physical and emotional stress, chronic diseases, infectious illnesses, osteoporosis, injuries or wounds, surgeries, poor digestion, old age, pregnancy and lactation, decreased appetite, excessive dieting, exposure to environmental pollution, and strenuous exercise, among others.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each tablet of Century Forte contains the following essential nutrients:</p><ul><li>Vitamin A: 5000 IU</li><li>Vitamin C: 60 mg</li><li>Vitamin D: 400 IU</li><li>Vitamin E: 30 IU</li><li>Vitamin K: 25 mcg</li><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine): 1.5 mg</li><li>Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin): 1.7 mg</li><li>Vitamin B3 (Niacin): 20 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine): 2 mg</li><li>Folic acid: 400 mcg</li><li>Biotin: 30 mcg</li><li>Pantothenate: 10 mg</li><li>Calcium: 162 mg</li><li>Iron: 18 mg</li><li>Phosphorus: 109 mg</li><li>Iodine: 150 mcg</li><li>Magnesium: 100 mg</li><li>Zinc: 15 mg</li><li>Selenium: 20 mcg</li><li>Copper: 2 mg</li><li>Manganese: 2 mg</li><li>Chromium: 120 mcg</li><li>Molybdenum: 75 mcg</li><li>Chloride: 72 mg</li><li>Potassium: 80 mg</li><li>Boron: 150 mcg</li><li>Nickel: 5 mcg</li><li>Silicon: 2 mg</li><li>Tin: 10 mcg</li><li>Vanadium: 10 mcg</li><li>Lutein: 250 mcg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin: 6 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Multivitamin + Multimineral A-Z Gold Preparation is a supplement that combines most vitamins and minerals in a single tablet. This preparation typically contains 32 high-potency vitamins and minerals. It maintains a healthy body and an active lifestyle by providing essential nutrients that may be lacking in one\'s diet.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Century Forte</strong></p><p>The general dosage for adults is one tablet daily. The tablet can be taken with or without food. If you are pregnant or lactating, you may need to take a higher dosage of Century Forte.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Century Forte</strong></p><p>Century Forte is generally considered safe and has no known drug interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Century Forte is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Century Forte</strong></p><p>In most cases, this preparation is well tolerated. However, there may be occasional occurrences of diarrhea while undergoing beta-carotene treatment, and it\'s possible for the skin to take on a mild yellowish tint. Vitamin C and vitamin E may lead to diarrhea and other gastrointestinal disturbances.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Century Forte is generally safe for pregnant and lactating women, but it is important to speak to your doctor before taking any supplement during pregnancy or lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Long-term intake of high levels of vitamin A (excluding that sourced from beta carotene) may increase the risk of osteoporosis in postmenopausal women.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>This is a film-coated tablet, which combines 32 high-potency vitamins and minerals. This preparation maintains a healthy body and active lifestyle.</p>', 'active', '1826_1742765269.webp', 8, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:27:49', '2025-05-03 01:19:17'),
(4412, 'Sevitan', 'Olmesartan Medoxomil', 18.00, 14, 'Tablet', '14 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '40 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>Cutin 5000 mcg is indicated for hair loss, weak, brittle, splitting or soft nails, eczema, and dermatitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Herbal and Nutraceuticals</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cutin 5000 mcg is an essential vitamin, also known as vitamin H. It is involved in vital physiological functions like fatty acid synthesis, amino acid catabolism, and gluconeogenesis. It acts as a cofactor in the enzymatic carboxylation of pyruvate and acetyl-CoA. Cutin 5000 mcg influences the growth and differentiation of epidermal cells, making it crucial for the formation and renewal of the skin, hair, and nails. It functions as a coenzyme for mitochondrial carboxylases in hair roots and improves keratin structure. Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency may lead to hair loss and a variety of systemic symptoms such as dermatitis and aciduria. Cutin 5000 mcg is one of the most prescribed nutritional supplements for any kind of hair loss.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>The dosage of Cutin 5000 mcg varies depending on the disease condition and age group. Generally, 1-3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician. In children, Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency affects those between 1 week and 2 years of age, often exhibiting seizures, hypotonia, developmental delay, ataxia, hyperventilation, and coma. In this case, 5-10 tablets daily should be crushed and mixed with water or fruit juice, or as advised by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>There are indications that anticonvulsant drugs may lower the plasma level of Cutin 5000 mcg.</li><li>Antibiotic use may decrease the Cutin 5000 mcg contribution to the body made by the microflora of the large intestine.</li><li>Excessive consumption of raw egg whites interferes with Cutin 5000 mcg absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Sufficient clinical data is not available to use in pregnant women and lactating mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '744_1742765319.webp', 10, 12, 'ajd85485@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:28:39', '2025-03-24 02:28:39'),
(4413, 'D-Star', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 35.00, 8, 'capsule', '8 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '40000 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of D-Star 40000 IU</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated in the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also used as an adjunct to specific therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Colecalciferol, a synthetic form of Vitamin D, is essential for normal bone growth and development and helps maintain bone density. It also aids in the utilization of both calcium and phosphorus. Vitamin D3 is necessary for babies\' healthy growth and development. It acts as a hormone.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) promotes the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. Vitamin D3 is vital for normal bone growth and density. It also reduces the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, prevents the risk of certain cancers (breast, colorectal), and helps maintain adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of D-Star 40000 IU</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For Capsule</strong>:</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 40000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Maintenance therapy</strong>: 1400–2000 IU/day. Measure 25-hydroxyvitamin D levels 3-4 months after starting maintenance therapy to confirm target levels.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20000 IU every 4 weeks. Higher doses may be needed in certain cases.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adjunct therapy for osteoporosis</strong>: 20000 IU once a month.</li><li><strong>Children (12-18 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20000 IU every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20000 IU every 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>For Film-Coated Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily or as directed by a physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li><li><strong>For Oroflash or Chewable Tablets</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1000–2000 IU daily or as directed by a physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal. Place the tablet in the mouth and swallow after chewing.</li><li><strong>For Syrup</strong>:</li><li><strong>For patients at risk of Vitamin D deficiency</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">0-1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml)</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-2\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml)</blockquote><ul><li><strong>For Vitamin D deficient patients</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">0-1 year: 2000 IU/day (+50000 IU/week) for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 years: 2000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Injection</strong>:</li><li><strong>Prevention</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Infants receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk: 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) i.e. 100000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Nursed infants or infants not receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk or young children up to 5 years: 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e. 200000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\">Adolescents: 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e. 200000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\"><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) i.e. 100000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-3\"><strong>Elderly</strong>: 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) i.e. 100000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\"><strong>In special conditions (e.g., digestive disorders, anticonvulsant treatment)</strong>: 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) i.e. 100000 IU or 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e. 200000 IU every 3 or 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Vitamin D deficiency</strong>: 1 ampoule (1ml) i.e. 200000 IU, repeat after 1 to 6 months or as directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of D-Star 40000 IU</strong></p><p>It may interfere with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (e.g., liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of D-Star 40000 IU</strong></p><p>Common side effects include hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, and abdominal pain.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Studies have shown that doses up to 4000 IU are safe during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU, but higher doses may be required for those deficient in Vitamin D3. During pregnancy, women should follow the advice of their healthcare provider. Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. Overdose in infants due to nursing mothers has not been observed, but the total dose from the mother’s and child’s intake should be considered when prescribing.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Use with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of D-Star 40000 IU</strong></p><p>Overdose can lead to hypervitaminosis D.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30ºC, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) helps with the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is essential for normal bone growth, density, and overall health. It also helps reduce the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, reduces cancer risks, and maintains insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</p>', 'active', '76_1742765368.webp', 11, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:29:28', '2025-05-01 19:41:24'),
(4414, 'Fixcef Plus', 'Cefuroxime Axetil + Clavulanic Acid', 55.00, 7, 'Tablet', '7 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg+125 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>Cutin 5000 mcg is indicated for hair loss, weak, brittle, splitting or soft nails, eczema, and dermatitis.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Herbal and Nutraceuticals</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Cutin 5000 mcg is an essential vitamin, also known as vitamin H. It is involved in vital physiological functions like fatty acid synthesis, amino acid catabolism, and gluconeogenesis. It acts as a cofactor in the enzymatic carboxylation of pyruvate and acetyl-CoA. Cutin 5000 mcg influences the growth and differentiation of epidermal cells, making it crucial for the formation and renewal of the skin, hair, and nails. It functions as a coenzyme for mitochondrial carboxylases in hair roots and improves keratin structure. Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency may lead to hair loss and a variety of systemic symptoms such as dermatitis and aciduria. Cutin 5000 mcg is one of the most prescribed nutritional supplements for any kind of hair loss.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><p>The dosage of Cutin 5000 mcg varies depending on the disease condition and age group. Generally, 1-3 tablets per day or as advised by the physician. In children, Cutin 5000 mcg deficiency affects those between 1 week and 2 years of age, often exhibiting seizures, hypotonia, developmental delay, ataxia, hyperventilation, and coma. In this case, 5-10 tablets daily should be crushed and mixed with water or fruit juice, or as advised by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Cutin 5000 mcg</strong></p><ul><li>There are indications that anticonvulsant drugs may lower the plasma level of Cutin 5000 mcg.</li><li>Antibiotic use may decrease the Cutin 5000 mcg contribution to the body made by the microflora of the large intestine.</li><li>Excessive consumption of raw egg whites interferes with Cutin 5000 mcg absorption.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Sufficient clinical data is not available to use in pregnant women and lactating mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a cool and dry place, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><br></p>', 'active', '349_1742765412.webp', 62, 12, 'ajd85485@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:30:12', '2025-03-24 02:30:12'),
(4415, 'Dermex', 'Clobetasol Propionate', 70.48, 1, 'x 20 gm tube', '20 gm tube', 'Cream', '0.05%', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Dermex 20 gm</strong></p><p>Clobetasol Propionate is indicated for adults, elderly, and children over 1 year in the following dermatoses:</p><ul><li>Psoriasis (excluding widespread plaque psoriasis)</li><li>Recalcitrant dermatoses</li><li>Lichen planus</li><li>Discoid lupus erythematosus</li><li>Other skin conditions which do not respond satisfactorily to less potent steroids</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Other Topical corticosteroids</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Clobetasol Propionate is a very potent topical corticosteroid. It has anti-inflammatory, antipruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties. It shows anti-inflammatory activity via multiple mechanisms to inhibit late-phase allergic reactions. It decreases the density of mast cells, chemotaxis, and activation of eosinophils. It also reduces cytokine production and inhibits the metabolism of arachidonic acid.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Dermex 20 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cream &amp; Ointment</strong>:</li><li>Adults, elderly, and children over 1 year: Apply a thin layer of Clobetasol Propionate Cream or Ointment to the affected skin areas twice daily and rub in gently and completely. Repeated short courses of Clobetasol Propionate may be used to control exacerbations. In more resistant lesions, especially where there is hyperkeratosis, the effect of Clobetasol can be enhanced, if necessary, by occluding the treatment area with polythene film. Overnight occlusion only is usually adequate to bring about a satisfactory response.</li><li>Treatment should be limited to 2 consecutive weeks. The maximum weekly dose should not exceed 50 gm/week. In children, courses should be limited to five days and reviewed weekly.</li><li><strong>Scalp Solution</strong>:</li><li>Apply the required quantity of spray of Clobetasol Scalp Solution once or twice daily to the affected areas of the scalp and gently rub in. The total dose applied should not exceed 50 ml weekly. Therapy should be discontinued if no response is noted after one week or as soon as the lesion heals.</li><li><strong>Topical Spray</strong>:</li><li>Clobetasol 0.05% topical spray is for topical use only. It should be sprayed directly onto the affected skin areas twice daily and rubbed in gently and completely. The total dosage should not exceed 50 g (59 ml or 2 fluid ounces) per week due to the potential for suppression of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis. Treatment should be limited to 4 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Dermex 20 gm</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Route of Administration</strong>: Cutaneous.</li><li><strong>Creams</strong> are especially appropriate for moist or weeping surfaces.</li><li><strong>Ointments</strong> are especially appropriate for dry, lichenified, or scaly lesions.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Dermex 20 gm</strong></p><p>Co-administered drugs that can inhibit CYP3A4 (e.g., ritonavir, itraconazole) may inhibit the metabolism of corticosteroids, leading to increased systemic exposure.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Clobetasol Propionate is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to any component of the preparation. It should not be used in:</p><ul><li>Rosacea</li><li>Acne vulgaris</li><li>Perioral dermatitis</li><li>Perianal and genital pruritus</li><li>Pruritus without inflammation</li><li>Untreated cutaneous infections</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Dermex 20 gm</strong></p><p>The most reported side effects include:</p><ul><li>Burning and stinging sensation</li><li>Itching</li><li>Skin atrophy</li><li>Cracking and fissuring of the skin</li></ul><p>Less frequent adverse reactions include:</p><ul><li>Cushing syndrome, especially in infants and adults due to prolonged use</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>There are limited data on the use of Clobetasol Propionate cream in pregnant women. Topical corticosteroids may cause abnormalities in fetal development in animal studies, but the relevance to humans is not well established. Its use during pregnancy and lactation should only be considered if the expected benefit to the mother outweighs the potential risks. It is unknown whether this drug is excreted in human milk, so caution should be exercised in nursing women.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>In case of using occlusive dressings, the skin should be cleansed before applying a fresh dressing.</li><li>Caution is needed in psoriasis due to the risk of rebound relapses and systemic toxicity from impaired barrier function.</li><li>When using on the face, treatment should be limited to 5 days.</li><li>Care should be taken to avoid the eyes when applying to eyelids, as cataracts and glaucoma may result from repeated exposure.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Dermex 20 gm</strong></p><p>Acute overdosage is unlikely; however, in case of chronic overdosage or misuse, features of hypercortisolism may occur. In this case, topical steroids should be discontinued.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Do not freeze.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>In infants and children under 12 years of age, long-term continuous topical corticosteroid therapy should be avoided where possible due to the risk of adrenal suppression. Children are more susceptible to atrophic changes from the use of topical corticosteroids.</li></ul>', 'active', '1074_1742765489.webp', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:31:29', '2025-05-03 08:13:57'),
(4416, 'Omep', 'Omeprazole', 8.00, 7, 'capsule', '7 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '40 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Omep 40 mg</strong></p><p>Omep 40 mg is indicated for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and gastric ulcer.</li><li>Gastric and duodenal ulcers caused by NSAIDs.</li><li>Prevention of duodenal and gastric ulcers in individuals with a history of NSAID-induced ulcers.</li><li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD).</li><li>Long-term care of acid reflux disease.</li><li>Acid-related dyspepsia.</li><li>Severe reflux esophagitis with ulcers.</li><li>Prevention of acid aspiration during general anesthesia.</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.</li><li>Peptic ulcer caused by <em>Helicobacter pylori</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Blocker</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong> Omep 40 mg is a benzimidazole derivative that inhibits stomach acid secretion. It works by blocking the hydrogen-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase (H+/K+ ATPase) enzyme system in the gastric parietal cells, thereby preventing the release of gastric acid. The antisecretory effect starts in about one hour after oral administration, peaks at two hours, and can last up to 72 hours. Once the medication is stopped, secretory activity gradually returns to normal over 3 to 5 days.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Omep 40 mg:</strong></p><p><strong>Oral:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Benign gastric and duodenal ulcers:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks in duodenal ulcers and 8 weeks in gastric ulcers; for severe or recurrent cases, increase the dose to 40 mg daily.</li><li><strong>NSAID-associated ulcers:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, continued for 4 more weeks if not fully healed. For prevention, use 20 mg once daily in patients with a history of NSAID-induced ulcers.</li><li><strong>GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks, extended for 4-8 weeks if not healed. If refractory, 40 mg once daily for 8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Long-term management of acid reflux disease:</strong> 10-20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Acid-related dyspepsia:</strong> 10-20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration:</strong> 40 mg the evening before surgery, followed by 40 mg 2-6 hours before surgery.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</strong> Initially 60 mg once daily; usual range is 20-120 mg daily. Doses over 80 mg should be divided into two doses.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori eradication (Peptic ulcer disease):</strong> Omep 40 mg at 20 mg twice daily with antibiotics (Amoxicillin 500 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg, or Clarithromycin 250 mg and Metronidazole 400 mg, for one week).</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Severe ulcerating reflux esophagitis (Child &gt;1 year):</strong> 10-20 mg once daily for children weighing 10-20 kg, and 20-40 mg for those over 20 kg, for 4-12 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>IV Injection:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prophylaxis of acid aspiration:</strong> Omep 40 mg IV 40 mg given slowly over 5 minutes, one hour before surgery.</li><li><strong>Duodenal, gastric ulcer, or reflux esophagitis:</strong> IV 40 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:</strong> Initial dose of 60 mg daily, which can be increased and divided into two doses if over 60 mg daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Omep 40 mg:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>IV Injection:</strong> Add 10 ml of water for injection to the vial containing the powder. Administer the solution slowly (over 2 to 5 minutes) with a maximum flow rate of 4 ml/min.</li><li><strong>IV Infusion:</strong> Dissolve the contents of one vial in 100 ml of either 5% dextrose or saline for infusion. The solution should be used within 6 hours if in 5% dextrose, or within 12 hours if in saline.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Omep 40 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>May increase the plasma concentrations of Warfarin, phenytoin, and diazepam, which may require dosage adjustments.</li><li>No significant interactions with theophylline, propranolol, or antacids.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to any ingredient in the formulation.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Omep 40 mg:</strong></p><ul><li>Most people tolerate Omep 40 mg well, but mild, temporary side effects like nausea, stomach cramps, paresthesia, dizziness, and headaches may occur. These rarely require dosage adjustments.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></p><ul><li>Omep 40 mg is categorized as a C pregnancy drug. Three studies show no adverse effects on pregnancy or fetal health. However, since it’s not known whether it passes into breast milk and affects the baby, breastfeeding should be discontinued if use is deemed necessary.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></p><ul><li>Rule out gastric cancer before starting therapy for a suspected stomach ulcer, as Omep 40 mg can mask symptoms and delay diagnosis.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place, away from heat and light, and out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class:</strong></p><ul><li>Proton Pump Blocker</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></p><ul><li>Omep 40 mg is a benzimidazole derivative that inhibits stomach acid secretion by blocking the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme in gastric parietal cells. The antisecretory effect begins within one hour, peaks in two hours, and can last up to 72 hours.</li></ul>', 'active', '1671_1742765603.jpg', 29, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:33:23', '2025-05-04 01:18:17'),
(4417, 'D-Cap', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 20.00, 10, 'Capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '20000 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here’s a more structured version of the information you provided for <strong>D-Cap 20000 IU</strong>:</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Indications of D-Cap 20000 IU</strong></h3><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is used in:</p><ul><li><strong>Treatment &amp; prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong></li><li>As an adjunct to specific therapy for:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Osteoporosis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Osteomalacia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Hypocalcaemia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tetany</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rickets (in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency)</li><li><strong>Cholecalciferol</strong> is the synthetic form of Vitamin D, essential for:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Normal bone growth &amp; development</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintaining bone density</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Utilization of calcium and phosphorus</li><li><strong>For babies</strong>: Vitamin D3 is crucial for healthy growth and development.</li><li><strong>Vitamin D3 acts as a hormone</strong> in the body.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation</li><li>Vitamin D preparations</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3)</strong> helps with the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus.</li><li>It is essential for normal bone growth and maintaining bone density.</li><li><strong>Additional benefits</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Reduces the severity of bacterial infections</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Improves lung function</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prevents risks of cancer (breast, colorectal)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Helps maintain insulin levels for type 2 diabetes patients.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of D-Cap 20000 IU</strong></h3><h4><strong>Capsule Dosage</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance therapy</strong>: 1400-2000 IU/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Osteoporosis adjunct therapy</strong>: 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li><strong>Children (12-18 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU once every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Film-coated Tablet</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily or as prescribed.</li><li><strong>Chewable Tablets</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1000-2000 IU daily or as prescribed.</li><li><strong>Syrup Dosage</strong>:</li><li><strong>For at-risk children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">0-1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml)</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml)</blockquote><ul><li><strong>For deficient children</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">0-1 year: 2000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 years: 2000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li></ul><h4><strong>Injection Dosage</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>For Infants receiving Vitamin D enriched milk</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e. 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li><strong>For Nursed Infants or children up to 5 years</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e. 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li><strong>Adolescents</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e. 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e. 100,000 IU from 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e. 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li><strong>In certain conditions (e.g., digestive disorders, antiepileptic treatment)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e. 100,000 IU or 1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e. 200,000 IU every 3-6 months.</li><li><strong>Vitamin D deficiency</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e. 200,000 IU which can be repeated in 1-6 months, as prescribed by the doctor.</li></ul><h3><strong>Interactions</strong></h3><ul><li>Interferes with:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Phenytoin</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Barbiturates</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Glucocorticoids</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Certain laxatives (e.g., liquid paraffin)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Actinomycin</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Imidazole antifungal agents.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypercalcaemia</li><li>Hypercalciuria</li><li>Skin rash, pruritus, urticaria</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Abdominal pain.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>Safe use of up to 4000 IU during pregnancy has been shown.</li><li><strong>Recommended daily intake for pregnant women</strong>: 400 IU (higher doses may be required for those deficient in Vitamin D3).</li><li>Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. Overdose in infants from breastfeeding has not been observed, but care should be taken if prescribing additional Vitamin D3.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Can lead to hypervitaminosis D (Vitamin D toxicity).</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below 30º C, protected from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation</li><li>Vitamin D preparations</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li>Helps the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus.</li><li>Essential for normal bone growth and maintaining bone density.</li><li>Reduces severity of bacterial infections and improves lung function.</li><li>Helps prevent cancer and supports type 2 diabetes management.</li></ul><p>This format organizes the information in a clear and easy-to-read manner. Let me know if you need further details!</p>', 'active', '1492_1742765729.jpg', 9, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:35:29', '2025-05-01 19:35:11'),
(4418, 'Purisal', 'Levosalbutamol', 2.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '2 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Purisal 2 mg</strong></p><p>Purisal 2 mg is indicated for the treatment and prevention of bronchospasm in adults, adolescents, and children 6 years of age and older with reversible obstructive airway diseases.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Purisal 2 mg is a single isomer beta-2 agonist that differs from racemic salbutamol by the elimination of (S)-salbutamol. Purisal 2 mg is an effective bronchodilator whose primary mechanism of action is not inhibited by (S)-salbutamol. Thus, when compared with racemic salbutamol, clinically comparable bronchodilation can be achieved. Purisal 2 mg produces bronchodilatation through stimulation of beta-2-adrenergic receptors in bronchial smooth muscles, thereby causing relaxation of bronchial muscle fibers.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Purisal 2 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Children (6 -11 years)</strong>: 5 ml (1 mg), three times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults and adolescents above 12 years</strong>: 5-10 ml (1-2 mg), three times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Purisal 2 mg</strong></p><p>Other short-acting sympathomimetic bronchodilators or epinephrine should be used with caution with Purisal 2 mg. If additional adrenergic drugs are to be administered by any route, they should be used with caution to avoid deleterious cardiovascular effects.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Purisal 2 mg is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Purisal 2 mg or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Purisal 2 mg</strong></p><p>Hypocalcaemia, palpitation, fine tremors of the skeletal muscle, and muscle cramps may occur. Other likely side effects include nausea, vomiting, epigastric pain, and diarrhoea.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The drug should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus. It is not known whether Purisal 2 mg is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when oral Purisal 2 mg is administered to a nursing woman.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Particular caution is advised in acute severe asthma as this effect may be potentiated by hypoxia and by concomitant treatment with xanthine derivatives, steroids, and diuretics. Serum potassium levels should be monitored in such situations.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place and away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p>', 'active', '783_1742765843.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:37:23', '2025-05-04 14:56:49');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4419, 'Ostocal JR', 'Elemental Calcium', 3.50, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Chewable Tablet', '250 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ostocal JR 250 mg</strong></p><p><strong>250 mg or 500 mg tablet</strong>: This is used for the treatment or prevention of calcium depletion in patients in whom dietary measures are inadequate. Conditions that may be associated with calcium deficiency include hypoparathyroidism, achlorhydria, chronic diarrhea, vitamin D deficiency, steatorrhea, sprue, pregnancy and lactation, menopause, pancreatitis, renal failure, alkalosis, and hyperphosphataemia. Ostocal JR 250 mg is being used increasingly often to treat hyperphosphataemia in chronic renal failure as well as those on continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis (CAPD) and haemodialysis. Many patients are unable to tolerate sufficient doses for complete phosphate control and require additional measures such as stringent dietary phosphate restriction or relatively small doses of aluminium hydroxide. Ostocal JR 250 mg containing preparations can provide short-term relief of dyspeptic symptoms but are no longer recommended for long-term treatment of peptic ulceration.</p><p><strong>1000 mg tablet</strong>: This is indicated for the management of conditions associated with hyperacidity and for fast relief of acid indigestion, heartburn, sour stomach, and upset stomach.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Minerals in bone formation, Specific mineral preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Calcium carbonate reacts with gastric acid to produce a salt and water. For Ostocal JR 250 mg, the postulated chemical reaction is:</p><p><strong>CaCO₃ + 2HCl = CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂</strong></p><p>Two grams of Ostocal JR 250 mg will readily bring 100 ml of hydrochloric acid to a pH above 6. The increase in gastric pH diminishes the activity of pepsin in the gastric secretion. Up to 30% of the oral calcium load may be absorbed.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ostocal JR 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>250 mg or 500 mg tablet</strong>:</li><li>Ostocal JR 250 mg is always used orally. When used as an antacid, the recommended doses for adults are equivalent to 540-2000 mg Ostocal JR 250 mg per day, with doses for children being half of those for adults. As a dietary supplement (such as for the prevention of osteoporosis), 1250-3750 mg Ostocal JR 250 mg (500-1500 mg calcium) daily is generally recommended, but this will need to be tailored to the individual patient depending on any specific disease such as calcium deficiency, malabsorption, or parathyroid function. In pregnancy and lactation, the recommended daily dose of calcium is 1200-1500 mg. In chronic renal failure, the doses used vary from 2.5-9.0 gm Ostocal JR 250 mg per day and need to be adjusted according to the individual patient. To maximize effective phosphate binding in this context, Ostocal JR 250 mg should be given with meals.</li><li><strong>1000 mg tablet</strong>:</li><li>2000-3000 mg tablet when symptoms occur; may be repeated hourly if needed or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Ostocal JR 250 mg</strong></p><p>Ostocal JR 250 mg may enhance the cardiac effects of digoxin and other cardiac glycosides if systemic hypercalcaemia occurs. Ostocal JR 250 mg may interfere with the absorption of concomitantly administered tetracycline preparations, and in chronic renal failure, modification of vitamin D therapy may be required to avoid hypercalcaemia when Ostocal JR 250 mg is used as the primary phosphate binder.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypercalcaemia and hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Hypercalciuria and nephrolithiasis</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Concomitant digoxin therapy (requires careful monitoring of serum calcium level)</li><li>When hypercalcaemia occurs, discontinuation of the drug is usually sufficient to return serum calcium concentrations to normal. Calcium salts should be used cautiously in patients with sarcoidosis, renal or cardiac disease, and in patients receiving cardiac glycosides.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ostocal JR 250 mg</strong></p><p>Orally administered Ostocal JR 250 mg may be irritating to the GI tract and may also cause constipation. Hypercalcaemia is rarely produced by administration of calcium alone but may occur when large doses are given to patients with chronic renal failure.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Calcium-containing drugs have been widely used in pregnancy by way of oral calcium supplementation or antacid therapy. Ostocal JR 250 mg can be used in lactating women too.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool, dry place at controlled room temperature.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in children</strong>: Calcium carbonate has been extensively studied in children and infants with chronic renal failure and is both safe and effective.</li><li><strong>Use in elderly</strong>: In elderly patients with renal failure, constipation may be troublesome when Ostocal JR 250 mg is taken. For this reason, monitoring of serum calcium and phosphate is indicated for elderly patients.</li></ul>', 'active', '1413_1742765960.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:39:20', '2025-05-04 01:34:48'),
(4420, 'Alanil', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 9.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '120 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Alanil 120 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Allergic Rhinitis:</strong></li><li>Alanil 120 mg is indicated for the alleviation of seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis symptoms in adults and children aged 12 years and older. This includes symptoms such as sneezing, rhinorrhea, lacrimation, itchy red eyes, and itchy nose, palate, and throat. Fexofenadine, the active ingredient, helps improve work/activity productivity and quality of life.</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria:</strong></li><li>Fexofenadine is recommended for the treatment of chronic idiopathic urticaria in adults and children aged 12 years and older, effectively decreasing wheals and pruritus associated with the condition. It also improves work/activity productivity and overall health-related quality of life.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Non-sedating Antihistamines</strong></li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li>Alanil 120 mg is a selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist (antihistamine). It is quickly absorbed after oral administration, reaching peak plasma concentration in 2 to 3 hours. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier, which minimizes central nervous system effects.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>For Seasonal Allergic Rhinitis:</strong></li><li><strong>Adults and Children 12 years and older:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tablet: 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>In case of impaired renal function:</strong> 60 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Children 6-11 years:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tablet: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>In case of impaired renal function:</strong> 30 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Children 2-11 years:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Suspension: 30 mg or 5 ml twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>In case of impaired renal function:</strong> 30 mg or 5 ml once daily</li><li><strong>For Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria:</strong></li><li><strong>Adults and Children 12 years and older:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tablet: 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>In case of impaired renal function:</strong> 60 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Children 6-11 years:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Tablet: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>In case of impaired renal function:</strong> 30 mg once daily</li><li><strong>Children 6 months to less than 2 years:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Suspension: 15 mg or 2.5 ml (1/2 tsp) twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>In case of impaired renal function:</strong> 15 mg or 2.5 ml (1/2 tsp) once daily</li><li><strong>Children 2-11 years:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Suspension: 30 mg or 5 ml (1 tsp) twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>In case of impaired renal function:</strong> 30 mg or 5 ml (1 tsp) once daily</li></ul><h3><strong>Interaction of Alanil 120 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>With Erythromycin or Ketoconazole:</strong></li><li>Fexofenadine levels in plasma may increase 2 to 3 times, but this does not affect the QT interval or increase adverse reactions.</li><li><strong>With Omeprazole:</strong></li><li>There is no significant interaction.</li><li><strong>With Antacids (Aluminum and Magnesium Hydroxide):</strong></li><li>Decreased bioavailability if taken within 15 minutes. It is recommended to wait at least two hours between administration of Alanil 120 mg and antacids.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li>Alanil 120 mg should not be used in individuals with known hypersensitivity to fexofenadine or any of its components.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Alanil 120 mg</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Common:</strong></li><li>Headache, fatigue, vertigo, nausea, and issues with the administration site.</li><li><strong>Uncommon:</strong></li><li>Exhaustion.</li><li><strong>Rare:</strong></li><li>Hypersensitivity reactions, including angioedema, chest tightness, dyspnea, flushing, and systemic anaphylaxis; mental disturbances such as anxiety, nightmares, and sleep disorders; cardiac issues like palpitations and tachycardia; diarrhea; skin conditions such as rash, urticaria, and pruritus.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy (Category C):</strong></li><li>Should be avoided unless absolutely necessary due to insufficient data on its safety during pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong></li><li>Fexofenadine passes into breast milk, and it is not advised for breastfeeding mothers.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Elderly and Renal or Hepatic Impairment:</strong></li><li>Caution should be taken when administering Alanil 120 mg to these patients.</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular Caution:</strong></li><li>Antihistamines may cause tachycardia and palpitations, so patients with a history of cardiovascular disease should be informed of potential risks.</li><li><strong>Impact on Driving or Operating Machinery:</strong></li><li>Fexofenadine is unlikely to impair the ability to drive or operate machinery, but individuals should evaluate their response before performing such activities.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects</strong></h3><ul><li>Symptoms of overdose include dry mouth, sleepiness, weariness, and dizziness. Doses up to 800 mg have been used without significant adverse effects. Treatment includes symptom management, and fexofenadine cannot be removed effectively by hemodialysis.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Keep dry and away from heat and light. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li>Fexofenadine works by binding to the H1 histamine receptor, preventing histamine activation and alleviating allergic symptoms such as pruritus, rhinorrhea, and watery eyes. It does not have antiserotonergic, anticholinergic, sedative, or adrenergic blocking effects.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pediatric Use &amp; Considerations for Renal and Hepatic Impairment</strong></h3><ul><li>In children with renal impairment, the dose may need adjustment. The pharmacokinetics of fexofenadine are not significantly impacted by hepatic disease.</li><li>For elderly patients, the bioavailability of fexofenadine is enhanced, and they may require careful monitoring.</li></ul><p><strong>Note:</strong> Always follow the prescribed dosage and consult with a healthcare professional if you have any concerns regarding Alanil 120 mg.</p>', 'active', '1491_1742766091.webp', 5, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:41:31', '2025-05-02 22:52:14'),
(4421, 'Vita-D3', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 35.00, 5, 'capsule', '5 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '40000 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vita-D3 40000 IU</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated for the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also used as an adjunct to specific therapy for conditions such as osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Vitamin D3 is crucial for normal bone growth and development, and for maintaining bone density. It is also essential for the proper utilization of calcium and phosphorus. Babies require Vitamin D3 for healthy growth and development. It acts as a hormone in the body.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is essential for normal bone growth and to maintain bone density. Additionally, Vitamin D3 helps reduce the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, prevents certain cancers (breast, colorectal), and helps maintain adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Vita-D3 40000 IU</strong></p><p><strong>Capsule Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 40000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance therapy: 1400-2000 IU/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For confirmation, 25 hydroxyvitamin D levels should be measured 3-4 months after starting maintenance therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 20000 IU every 4 weeks. Higher doses may be needed in certain situations.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Addition to specific therapy for osteoporosis: 20000 IU once a month.</li><li><strong>Children (12-18 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 20000 IU every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency: 20000 IU every 6 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Film-Coated Tablet Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li>1000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily, or as directed by a physician.</li><li>Take the medicine with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li></ul><p><strong>Chewable Tablet or Oroflash Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li>1000 IU to 2000 IU daily, or as directed by a physician.</li><li>Take the medicine with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li></ul><p><strong>Syrup Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For patients at risk of Cholecalciferol deficiency:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0-1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml).</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml).</blockquote><ul><li><strong>For Cholecalciferol deficient patients:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0-1 year: 2000 IU/day (+50000 IU/week) for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 years: 2000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>Injection Dosage:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prevention:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Infants receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk: 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml), 1,00000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nursed infants or infants not receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk, or young children up to 5 years: 1 ampoule (1ml), 2,00000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adolescents: 1 ampoule (1ml), 2,00000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Pregnancy: 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml), 1,00000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Elderly: 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml), 1,00000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Digestive disorders or treatments with antiepileptics: 1/2 ampoule (0.5ml) or 1 ampoule (1ml) every 3 to 6 months.</li><li><strong>Vitamin D deficiency:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 ampoule (1ml), 2,00000 IU, which can be repeated after 1 to 6 months, or as directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions of Vita-D3 40000 IU</strong></p><p>It may interact with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (such as liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Vita-D3 40000 IU</strong></p><p>Common side effects include hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, and abdominal pain.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Studies show that doses up to 4000 IU are safe during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU. However, higher doses may be required for women who are Vitamin D3 deficient. Pregnant women should consult their doctor as requirements may vary. Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. There have been no reported overdoses in infants due to nursing mothers, but the prescribing doctor should consider any additional Vitamin D3 supplementation for the child.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Use with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Vita-D3 40000 IU</strong></p><p>Overdose may lead to hypervitaminosis D.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30ºC, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><p>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is crucial for normal bone growth and the maintenance of bone density. It also helps reduce the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, prevents the risk of certain cancers (breast, colorectal), and helps maintain adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</p><p>Let me know if you need further details!</p>', 'active', '1233_1742766195.jpg', 6, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:43:15', '2025-05-04 23:00:31'),
(4422, 'Fexomin', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 9.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '180 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Fexomin 180 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Allergic Rhinitis</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In adults and children 12 years of age and older, Fexomin 180 mg is recommended for alleviating seasonal and persistent allergic rhinitis symptoms (e.g., sneezing, rhinorrhea, itchy eyes, and throat).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">It helps improve work/productivity and quality of life.</li><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For adults and children 12 years and older, Fexomin is used to treat symptoms like wheals and pruritus in chronic idiopathic urticaria.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fexomin improves both activity levels and quality of life.</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li><strong>Non-sedating Antihistamines</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><ul><li>Fexomin (fexofenadine) is a selective peripheral H1-receptor antagonist.</li><li>It is quickly absorbed after oral administration, reaching peak plasma concentrations within 2-3 hours.</li><li>Does <strong>not</strong> cross the blood-brain barrier.</li></ul><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Fexomin 180 mg</h3><ol><li><strong>Seasonal Allergic Rhinitis</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children 12+ years</strong>: 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6-11 years)</strong>: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (2-11 years, suspension)</strong>: 30 mg (5 ml) twice daily.</li></ul><ol><li><strong>Chronic Idiopathic Urticaria</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults and children 12+ years</strong>: 60 mg twice daily, 120 mg once daily, or 180 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6-11 years)</strong>: 30 mg twice daily or 60 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (6 months to &lt;2 years, suspension)</strong>: 15 mg (2.5 ml) twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children (2-11 years, suspension)</strong>: 30 mg (5 ml) twice daily.</li></ul><ol><li><em>In cases of renal impairment, dose adjustments are required.</em></li></ol><h3>Interaction of Fexomin 180 mg</h3><ul><li>Fexomin does <strong>not</strong> undergo hepatic biotransformation, so hepatic interactions are unlikely.</li><li>Co-administration with erythromycin or ketoconazole may <strong>increase fexofenadine levels</strong> in plasma (2-3 times).</li><li>Avoid taking Fexomin with antacids (containing aluminum and magnesium hydroxide) within 2 hours, as bioavailability may be reduced.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li><strong>Known hypersensitivity</strong> to Fexomin 180 mg or its components.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Fexomin 180 mg</h3><ul><li><strong>Common side effects</strong>: Headache, fatigue, dizziness, nausea.</li><li><strong>Uncommon side effects</strong>: Anxiety, nightmares, palpitations, rash, urticaria, pruritus.</li><li><strong>Serious reactions (rare)</strong>: Angioedema, systemic anaphylaxis.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li><strong>Category C</strong> by the US FDA. Use during pregnancy and lactation should be avoided unless the benefit outweighs the risk to the fetus or infant.</li><li>Fexomin passes into breast milk and should not be used during breastfeeding.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Caution with elderly individuals and patients with renal or hepatic impairment.</li><li>Be aware of potential cardiovascular reactions (tachycardia, palpitations) in individuals with pre-existing heart conditions.</li><li>Generally does <strong>not</strong> affect the ability to drive or operate machinery, but individual responses may vary.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects of Fexomin 180 mg</h3><ul><li>Symptoms: Dry mouth, fatigue, dizziness, and drowsiness.</li><li>No known clinically significant overdose limit. In case of overdose, follow standard medical procedures and monitor for symptoms.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store in a <strong>dry</strong> place, away from heat and light.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li><strong>Inverse Agonist</strong>: Fexomin binds to and stabilizes the inactive H1 receptor, preventing activation and subsequent allergic reactions (e.g., pruritus, rhinorrhea).</li><li>Does <strong>not</strong> have sedative, dopaminergic, or anticholinergic effects.</li></ul><h3>Pediatric Use</h3><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment</strong>: Lower doses may be necessary for children with renal issues.</li><li><strong>Elderly Patients</strong>: Changes in pharmacokinetics may require adjustments.</li></ul>', 'active', '864_1742766306.jpg', 30, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:45:06', '2025-05-02 17:55:55'),
(4423, 'Calbo-D Vita', 'Calcium Lactate Gluconate + Calcium Carbonate + Vitamin D3', 15.05, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '1358.196 mg+600 mg (Elemental calcium)+400 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is your content with headings formatted in bold text:</p><p><strong>Indications of Calbo-D Vita</strong></p><p>Prevention and treatment of calcium and vitamin D deficiency. Calcium and vitamin D supplement as an adjunct to specific therapy in the prevention and treatment of Osteoporosis for patients who are at risk of calcium and vitamin D deficiency.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each effervescent tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Calcium Lactate Gluconate 1358.196 mg</li><li>Calcium Carbonate 600 mg</li><li>Vitamin D3 400 IU</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations.</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Adequate Calcium in the blood is so vital to a wide variety of bodily functions that our internal biochemistry will not tolerate a deficiency even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that Calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of Osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is also essential for healthy bones as it aids in Calcium absorption from the GI tract. In addition to this, it stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies also show that Calcium and Vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth as well as in Osteoporosis and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Calbo-D Vita</strong></p><p>1-2 tablets per day, preferably one tablet each morning and evening or as directed by the physician.</p><p><strong>Administration of Calbo-D Vita</strong></p><p>Pour a tablet in a glass of water. The tablet will be dissolved within 1-2 minutes in the water. Drink the solution immediately.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Calbo-D Vita</strong></p><p>It has possible interaction with Digoxin, Antacids containing Calcium, Aluminum or Magnesium, other Calcium supplements, Calcitriol, Tetracycline, Doxycycline, Aminocycline, or Oxytetracycline, etc. So while taking this supplement with any of these drugs, consultations of the physician are needed.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypercalcemia and hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Hypercalciuria and nephrolithiasis</li><li>Hypersensitivity to the component of this preparation</li><li>Severe renal insufficiencies</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Calbo-D Vita</strong></p><p>Orally administered Calcium Carbonate may be irritating to the GI tract. It may also cause constipation. Hypercalcemia is rarely produced by administration of Calcium alone, but may occur when large doses are given to patients with chronic renal failure. Also, there may be allergic reactions, irregular heartbeats, nausea, vomiting, decreased appetite, dry mouth, drowsiness, and skin rash.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>This can be used during pregnancy in case of a calcium and vitamin D deficiency. However, for supplementation starting during the third trimester of pregnancy, the daily intake should not exceed 1500 mg calcium and 1000 IU vitamin D. This preparation can be used during breast-feeding.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>When hypercalcemia occurs, discontinuation of the drug is usually sufficient to return serum calcium concentrations to normal. Calcium salts should be used cautiously in patients with sarcoidosis, renal or cardiac disease, and in patients receiving cardiac glycosides. Patients with a history of stone formation should also be recommended to increase their fluid intake.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Calbo-D Vita</strong></p><p>Symptoms of overdose may include nausea and vomiting, severe drowsiness, dry mouth, loss of appetite, metallic taste, stomach cramps, unconsciousness, diarrhea, weakness, headache, constipation, dizziness, or irritability. The most serious consequence of acute chronic overdose is hypocalcaemia due to Vitamin D toxicity.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store at a cool and dry place. Protect from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children. Keep the container tightly closed.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Specific mineral &amp; vitamin combined preparations.</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Adequate Calcium in the blood is so vital to a wide variety of bodily functions that our internal biochemistry will not tolerate a deficiency even for short periods. Clinical evidence suggests that Calcium is useful for the prevention and treatment of Osteoporosis and associated fractures. Vitamin D is also essential for healthy bones as it aids in Calcium absorption from the GI tract. In addition to this, it stimulates bone formation. Clinical studies also show that Calcium and Vitamin D have synergistic effects on bone growth as well as in Osteoporosis and fracture prevention.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>This can be used during pregnancy, in case of a calcium and vitamin D deficiency. However, for supplementation starting during the third trimester of pregnancy, the daily intake should not exceed 1500 mg calcium and 1000 IU vitamin D. This preparation can be used during breast-feeding.</p>', 'active', '465_1742766494.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:48:14', '2025-05-03 01:05:40'),
(4424, 'Closalic 10 gm', 'Clobetasol Propionate + Salicylic Acid', 70.00, 1, 'x 10 gm tube', '10 gm tube', 'Ointment', '0.05%+3%', 'no', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Closalic 10 gm 0.05%+3%</strong></h3><p>This ointment is indicated for the relief of inflammatory manifestations of hyperkeratotic and dry corticosteroid-responsive dermatoses such as:</p><ul><li>Psoriasis</li><li>Chronic atopic dermatitis</li><li>Neurodermatitis (Lichen Simplex Chronicus)</li><li>Lichen planus</li><li>Eczema (including nummular eczema, hand eczema, eczematous dermatitis)</li><li>Seborrheic dermatitis of the scalp</li><li>Ichthyosis vulgaris and other ichthyotic conditions</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class:</strong></h3><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology:</strong></h3><p><strong>Clobetasol Propionate:</strong></p><p>A high-potency corticosteroid. It decreases inflammation through multiple mechanisms, including:</p><ul><li>Stabilizing leukocyte lysosomal membranes</li><li>Preventing release of destructive acid hydrolases from leukocytes</li><li>Inhibiting macrophage accumulation in inflamed areas</li><li>Reducing capillary wall permeability and edema formation</li><li>Reducing fibroblast proliferation and collagen deposition</li></ul><p><strong>Salicylic Acid:</strong></p><p>A keratolytic agent with slight antiseptic properties. It softens and destroys the stratum corneum by increasing hydration, causing the skin\'s horny layer to swell, soften, and desquamate. It also has weak antifungal and antibacterial activity.</p><h3><strong>Dosage of Closalic 10 gm 0.05%+3%:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> Apply a thin layer to the affected skin areas twice daily and rub in gently. Maintenance therapy may require less frequent application. Therapy should be discontinued once control is achieved. If no improvement is seen within 2 weeks, reassess the diagnosis.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> Not recommended for use in children under 12 years of age.</li><li><strong>Note:</strong> Treatment should not exceed 50 gm per week, and occlusive dressings should be avoided.</li></ul><h3><strong>Administration:</strong></h3><p>For external use only.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Interaction:</strong></h3><ul><li>No significant interactions reported with Clobetasol Propionate.</li><li>Topical salicylic acid may increase the absorption of other topical medications, so avoid concurrent use on the same skin area.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications:</strong></h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Clobetasol Propionate</li><li>Primary bacterial or fungal skin infections (unless treated with an anti-infective agent simultaneously)</li><li>Primary cutaneous viral infections (e.g., herpes simplex, varicella)</li><li>Tuberculous skin lesions</li><li>Children under 1 year old</li><li>Salicylate hypersensitivity or sensitivity to other ingredients in the preparation</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate:</strong> Prolonged use or excessive application can lead to hypercortisolism, particularly in infants and children. Local atrophy may occur. In rare cases, psoriasis may worsen (pustular form).</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid:</strong> Possible irritation, dryness, and sensitivity.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate:</strong> Topical use during pregnancy is not well-studied. Use only if the benefits to the mother outweigh potential risks to the fetus. Avoid extensive or prolonged use.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid:</strong> Safety during pregnancy and lactation has not been established. Use with caution.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</strong></h3><ul><li>Avoid use on broken skin, mouth, eyes, mucous membranes, and the anogenital region.</li><li>Not for prolonged use on large areas of the body, especially in patients with impaired peripheral circulation or diabetes.</li></ul><h3><strong>Overdose Effects:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate:</strong> Chronic misuse can cause hypercortisolism. Gradual discontinuation is recommended under medical supervision.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid:</strong> Systemic salicylate poisoning (e.g., tinnitus, dizziness) may occur if applied to large skin areas for prolonged periods.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions:</strong></h3><p>Store in a cool, dry place, protected from light.</p><p><br></p><h3><strong>Drug Class:</strong></h3><ul><li>Clobetasol / Clobetasone &amp; Combined Preparations</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action:</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Clobetasol Propionate:</strong> Potent corticosteroid that reduces inflammation.</li><li><strong>Salicylic Acid:</strong> Keratolytic and antiseptic agent that softens and exfoliates the skin.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy:</strong></h3><ul><li>Use with caution during pregnancy and lactation. The benefits must outweigh the risks, and extensive use is not recommended.</li></ul>', 'active', '1331_1742766596.jpg', 27, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:49:56', '2025-05-03 01:25:35'),
(4425, 'Deflux', 'Domperidone Maleate', 50.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Oral Suspension', '5 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here’s the text with the requested bold formatting for the headings:</p><p><strong>Indications of Deflux 100 ml</strong></p><p>Dyspeptic symptom complex, often linked with delayed gastric emptying, gastroesophageal reflux, and esophagitis, may include:</p><ul><li>Epigastric sensation of fullness</li><li>Abdominal distension</li><li>Upper abdominal pain</li><li>Belching</li><li>Flatulence</li><li>Early satiety</li><li>Nausea and vomiting</li><li>Heartburn, with or without regurgitation of gastric contents into the mouth</li></ul><p>These symptoms can occur in various conditions, such as non-ulcer dyspepsia, acute nausea and vomiting of functional, organic, infectious, dietetic origin, or induced by radiotherapy, drug therapy, or migraine. Additionally, in Parkinson\'s disease, it can manifest as dopamine-agonist induced nausea and vomiting. For radiological studies, it can be used to speed up barium transit in follow-through radiological examinations.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Deflux 100 ml functions as a dopamine receptor antagonist. It exerts gastroprokinetic action by inhibiting dopamine receptors found in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its limited ability to cross the blood-brain barrier means it has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Deflux 100 ml</strong></p><p>For adults, the recommended dosage is 10-20 mg every 4-8 hours daily. Children should take 0.2-0.4 mg/kg every 4-8 hours daily. When used for acute nausea and vomiting, the maximum treatment duration should not exceed 12 weeks. It is advisable to take this medication 15-30 minutes before a meal.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Deflux 100 ml</strong></p><p>Deflux 100 ml should be taken 15-30 minutes before meals, and if needed, before bedtime. The typical recommended oral dosage of Domperidone is as follows:</p><ul><li><strong>For Adults:</strong></li><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li>The maximum daily dose of Domperidone is 80 mg.</li><li><strong>For Children:</strong></li><li>2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg of body weight or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight, every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>In cases of dyspeptic symptoms:</p><ul><li><strong>For Adults:</strong></li><li>10-20 mg (1-2 tablets or 10-20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li><strong>For Children:</strong></li><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>For acute and sub-acute conditions, especially in cases of acute nausea and vomiting:</p><ul><li><strong>For Adults:</strong></li><li>20 mg (2 tablets or 20 ml suspension) every 6-8 hours daily.</li><li><strong>For Children:</strong></li><li>0.2-0.4 mg/kg (2-4 ml of suspension per 10 kg or 0.4-0.8 ml of pediatric drops per 10 kg of body weight) every 6-8 hours daily.</li></ul><p>When administered rectally in the form of suppositories:</p><ul><li><strong>For Adults (including elderly):</strong></li><li>30-60 mg every 4-8 hours.</li><li><strong>For Children:</strong></li><li>The maximum daily rectal dose for children weighing between 10 to 25 kg is 30 mg. The dose may be divided throughout the day if necessary.</li></ul><p>Please note that the maximum treatment period for acute nausea and vomiting is 12 weeks.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Deflux 100 ml</strong></p><p>The simultaneous use of anticholinergic drugs may counteract the antidyspeptic effects of domperidone. It\'s important to note that antacids and antisecretory drugs should not be administered concurrently with domperidone as they can reduce its oral bioavailability. Domperidone primarily undergoes metabolism through the CYP3A4 enzyme. In vitro data suggest that coadministration with drugs significantly inhibiting this enzyme may lead to elevated plasma levels of domperidone. Examples of CYP3A4 inhibitors include azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, nefazodone, and others.</p><p>Theoretically, as domperidone has gastro-kinetic effects, it could potentially impact the absorption of concurrently orally administered drugs, especially those with sustained release or enteric-coated formulations. However, in patients already stabilized on medications like digoxin or paracetamol, the concurrent use of domperidone did not affect the blood levels of these drugs. Domperidone does not potentiate the action of neuroleptics, and it suppresses the unwanted peripheral effects such as digestive disorders, nausea, and vomiting caused by dopaminergic agonists like bromocriptine and L-dopa without counteracting their central properties.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Deflux 100 ml is not to be used by patients with a known hypersensitivity to this medication or in the case of neonates. It should also be avoided in situations where gastrointestinal stimulation could be unsafe, such as in cases of gastrointestinal hemorrhage, mechanical obstruction, or perforation. Additionally, it is contraindicated in patients with a prolactin-releasing pituitary tumor (prolactinoma).</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Deflux 100 ml</strong></p><p>Side effects are uncommon, with occasional reports of transient intestinal cramps. Extrapyramidal symptoms are rare among young children and exceptionally rare in adults. These symptoms typically resolve spontaneously and completely upon discontinuing the treatment. Since the pituitary gland is situated outside the blood-brain barrier, domperidone may lead to an increase in plasma prolactin levels. In rare cases, this hyperprolactinemia can result in neuroendocrinological effects like galactorrhea and gynecomastia. In situations where the blood-brain barrier is immature (as in infants) or compromised, the possibility of neurological side effects cannot be entirely ruled out. Additionally, there have been occasional reports of rare allergic reactions, including rash and urticaria.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>The safety of using Deflux 100 ml during pregnancy has not been established; hence, its use is not recommended during pregnancy. Animal studies have not shown any teratogenic effects on the fetus. Domperidone may induce galactorrhea and enhance post-natal lactation. It is excreted in breast milk in minimal quantities, which are insufficient to be considered harmful.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Deflux 100 ml should be employed with utmost care in children due to the potential for an increased risk of extrapyramidal reactions, which can be more pronounced in young children with an incompletely developed blood-brain barrier. Additionally, considering that domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should also be used cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</p><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Deflux 100 ml</strong></p><p>Signs of an overdose can manifest as drowsiness, disorientation, and extrapyramidal reactions, particularly in children. If an overdose occurs, it is advisable to administer activated charcoal and closely monitor the patient. The use of anticholinergic drugs, antiparkinson drugs, or antihistamines with anticholinergic properties may be beneficial in managing extrapyramidal reactions.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store below 30°C, Protected from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of children\'s reach.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Motility Stimulants, Motility stimulants/Dopamine antagonist, Prokinetic drugs</p><p><strong>Mode Of Action</strong></p><p>Deflux 100 ml acts as a dopamine antagonist, primarily by blocking dopamine receptors located in the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone (CTZ) and the stomach. Its gastroprokinetic effect is achieved by inhibiting dopamine receptors that influence the motility of the gastrointestinal tract. Because it has limited ability to penetrate the blood-brain barrier, Domperidone has minimal impact on dopaminergic receptors in the brain, thus avoiding psychotropic and neurological side effects. Domperidone restores normal motility and tone in the upper gastrointestinal tract, promotes gastric emptying, enhances antral and duodenal peristalsis, and regulates pylorus contraction. Additionally, it increases esophageal peristalsis and lowers esophageal sphincter pressure, preventing the regurgitation of gastric contents.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Use in infants:</strong> Given that the metabolic and blood-brain barrier functions are not fully developed during the first months of life, any medication should be administered to infants with great caution and under close medical supervision. Although the typical absence of neurological side effects with domperidone is primarily due to its limited penetration through the blood-brain barrier, the potential occurrence of such effects cannot be entirely ruled out in infants under 1 year of age.</li><li><strong>Use in liver disorders:</strong> Because domperidone undergoes extensive metabolism in the liver, it should be employed cautiously in patients with hepatic impairment.</li><li><strong>Use in kidney disorders:</strong> In individuals with severe renal insufficiency (serum creatinine &gt;6 mg/100ml, i.e., &gt;0.6 mmol/l), the elimination half-life of domperidone increases from 7.4 to 20.8 hours, although plasma drug levels remain lower than in healthy volunteers. Since very little unchanged drug is excreted through the kidneys, it is unlikely that the dose of a single acute administration needs to be adjusted in patients with renal insufficiency. However, for repeated administration, the dosing frequency should be reduced to once or twice daily, depending on the severity of the impairment, and the dose may need to be lowered. In general, patients on prolonged therapy should undergo regular reviews.</li></ul>', 'active', '871_1742766714.jpg', 3, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:51:54', '2025-05-01 20:09:15'),
(4426, 'Doximar', 'Doxophylline', 8.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '200 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here’s a summary of the information provided about Doximar 200 mg, with headings in bold as requested:</p><p><strong>Indications of Doximar 200 mg</strong></p><p>Doximar 200 mg is used to treat the following conditions:</p><ul><li>Bronchial asthma</li><li>Bronchospasm</li><li>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</li><li>Pulmonary disease with a spastic bronchial component</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Bronchodilator</li><li>Methyl xanthine derivatives</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Doximar 200 mg is a novel bronchodilator that structurally differs from Theophylline due to the presence of a dioxolane group in position 7. It selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, which helps relax bronchial smooth muscle. Unlike Theophylline, Doximar 200 mg has decreased affinities for adenosine A1 and A2 receptors, which may contribute to its improved safety profile. Additionally, it is reported to inhibit platelet-activating factor (PAF) and reduce leukotriene production.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Doximar 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</li><li><strong>Adults</strong>: 400 mg tablet two or three times daily or as prescribed by a physician.</li><li><strong>Children</strong>:</li></ul><blockquote>12 years of age: 10 ml syrup or 200 mg tablet two or three times daily.</blockquote><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><br></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years of age: 6-9 mg/kg body weight two times daily. For example, for a 10 kg child, 3 ml (60 mg) twice daily or as prescribed by the physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Doximar 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Doximar 200 mg should not be used with other xanthine derivatives.</li><li>Toxic synergism with ephedrine may occur with xanthines.</li><li>Drugs like troleandomycin, lincomycin, clindamycin, allopurinol, cimetidine, ranitidine, propranolol, and anti-flu vaccines may reduce the hepatic clearance of xanthines, leading to increased blood levels.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Doximar 200 mg is contraindicated in acute myocardial infarction.</li><li>It should not be used in patients with hypotension, lactating women, or individuals with hypersensitivity to its components.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Doximar 200 mg</strong></p><p>Rarely causes serious side effects, but may cause symptoms similar to caffeine overdose, including:</p><ul><li>Nausea</li><li>Vomiting</li><li>Headache</li><li>Upset stomach</li><li>Heartburn</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Animal studies suggest Doximar 200 mg does not harm the fetus, but due to limited human data, it should only be used in pregnant women when clearly necessary.</li><li>Doximar 200 mg is contraindicated in breastfeeding women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>The half-life of Doximar 200 mg may be prolonged in patients with liver disease, congestive heart failure, or those taking certain drugs.</li><li>In these cases, a lower dose may be needed.</li><li>Smoking and drugs like phenytoin may reduce the half-life, requiring higher doses.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Doximar 200 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Severe cardiac arrhythmias and tonic-clonic seizures may occur.</li><li>If these symptoms appear, the treatment may need to be discontinued and reinitiated at lower doses once symptoms subside.</li><li>There is no specific antidote; symptomatic treatment should be given for cardiovascular collapse.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a dry place away from light and heat.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li><li>Doximar 200 mg should only be used under the prescription of a specialist physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Bronchodilator</li><li>Methyl xanthine derivatives</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Doximar 200 mg is a bronchodilator that selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, relaxing bronchial smooth muscle. It has reduced affinity for adenosine A1 and A2 receptors compared to Theophylline, which contributes to its safer profile. Additionally, it inhibits platelet-activating factor (PAF) and reduces leukotriene production.</p>', 'active', '373_1742766805.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:53:25', '2025-05-02 08:44:41');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4427, 'Flumetol', 'Salmeterol + Fluticasone Propionate', 850.00, 1, 'x 120 metered doses', '120 metered doses', 'Inhaler', '(25 mcg+250 mcg)/puff', 'no', 'no', '<h3><strong>Indications of Flumetol (25 mcg + 250 mcg)/puff</strong></h3><p>This combination medication is indicated for the regular treatment of asthma, particularly when the use of a combination product (long-acting β2-agonist and inhaled corticosteroid) is appropriate. Specifically, it is used for:</p><ul><li>Patients who are not adequately controlled with inhaled corticosteroids and \"as-needed\" short-acting β2-agonist.</li><li>Patients who are already adequately controlled on both inhaled corticosteroids and long-acting β2-agonists.</li></ul><h3><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></h3><ul><li>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants</li><li>Respiratory corticosteroids</li></ul><h3><strong>Pharmacology</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate</strong>: A selective, long-acting beta-2 agonist that helps treat asthma and other forms of diffuse airway obstruction. It binds to the exo-site of the β2-receptor, providing relief from symptoms for up to 12 hours.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: A corticosteroid with primarily glucocorticoid activity, exerting a potent anti-inflammatory effect within the lungs. It helps improve lung function and prevent asthma exacerbations, with minimal systemic effects at typical doses.</li></ul><h3><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></h3><h4><strong>Inhalation Aerosol</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate, twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 125 µg Fluticasone Propionate, twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 250 µg Fluticasone Propionate, twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children (4-12 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">2 puffs of 25 µg Salmeterol and 50 µg Fluticasone Propionate, twice daily.</li></ul><h4><strong>Inhalation Powder in Capsule (For Asthma)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg or 250 µg, twice daily (morning and evening, approximately 12 hours apart).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum recommended dosage: Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 500 µg, twice daily.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients (4-11 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Salmeterol 50 µg &amp; Fluticasone 100 µg, twice daily.</li></ul><h4><strong>Maxhaler (For Asthma)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">50/100 Maxhaler: One inhalation, twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">50/250 Maxhaler: One inhalation, twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">50/500 Maxhaler: One inhalation, twice daily.</li><li><strong>Children (4 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">50/100 Maxhaler: One inhalation, twice daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum licensed dose: 100 µg of Fluticasone Propionate, twice daily.</li></ul><h4><strong>Inhalation Powder in Maxhaler (For COPD)</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Adults and Adolescents (12 years and older)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">One inhalation, twice daily.</li></ul><h3><strong>Administration of Flumetol (25 mcg + 250 mcg)/puff</strong></h3><ol><li><strong>Remove the cap</strong> from the inhaler.</li><li><strong>Shake the inhaler</strong> vigorously for at least six times before use.</li><li>If the inhaler is new or hasn\'t been used for a week or more, <strong>release one puff into the air</strong> to ensure it is working.</li><li><strong>Breathe out</strong> fully and comfortably.</li><li><strong>Hold the inhaler upright</strong> and place the mouthpiece in your mouth, closing your lips around it.</li><li><strong>Press down firmly</strong> on the canister while inhaling deeply and slowly through your mouth.</li><li><strong>Hold your breath</strong> for at least 10 seconds, or as long as it is comfortable.</li><li><strong>Wait one minute</strong> between puffs, if prescribed more than one inhalation.</li><li>After use, <strong>replace the cap</strong> on the inhaler and rinse your mouth with water.</li></ol><h3><strong>Interaction of Flumetol (25 mcg + 250 mcg)/puff</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Avoid non-selective and selective β-blockers</strong> in patients with asthma unless absolutely necessary.</li><li><strong>Caution with CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> (e.g., ketoconazole, ritonavir) as they may increase systemic exposure to Fluticasone Propionate.</li></ul><h3><strong>Contraindications</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> to any of the ingredients.</li></ul><h3><strong>Side Effects of Flumetol (25 mcg + 250 mcg)/puff</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol</strong>: Tremors, palpitations, headache, cardiac arrhythmia (atrial fibrillation, tachycardia), rash, oedema, oropharyngeal irritation, muscle cramps.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: Hoarseness, candidiasis (thrush), hypersensitivity reactions, facial and oropharyngeal oedema.</li></ul><h3><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></h3><ul><li>Use during pregnancy and lactation should only be considered if the benefit outweighs potential risks. There is insufficient data on the safety of Salmeterol and Fluticasone Propionate during pregnancy and lactation.</li></ul><h3><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></h3><ul><li>Caution in patients with <strong>active or quiescent pulmonary tuberculosis</strong>.</li><li>Caution in patients with <strong>thyrotoxicosis</strong>.</li></ul><h3><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></h3><ul><li>Store below <strong>30°C</strong>, away from heat and sunlight.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li><li>Do not puncture, break, or incinerate the canister, even when it seems empty.</li></ul><h3><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Paediatric use</strong>: Inhaled corticosteroids may affect growth velocity in children. The long-term effects on final adult height are unknown.</li></ul><h3><strong>Drug Classes</strong></h3><ul><li>Long-acting selective β-adrenoceptor stimulants</li><li>Respiratory corticosteroids</li></ul><h3><strong>Mode of Action</strong></h3><ul><li><strong>Salmeterol Xinafoate</strong>: Long-acting beta-2 agonist that provides sustained relief of asthma symptoms.</li><li><strong>Fluticasone Propionate</strong>: Potent glucocorticoid that exerts anti-inflammatory effects in the lungs, improving lung function and preventing exacerbations.</li></ul>', 'active', '495_1742766949.webp', 1, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:55:49', '2025-05-03 08:22:36'),
(4428, 'Rabigut', 'Rabeprazole Sodium', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Rabigut 20 mg</h3><p>Gastro-resistant tablets of rabeprazole are recommended for the treatment of:</p><ul><li>Active duodenal ulcer</li><li>Active benign stomach ulcer</li><li>Symptomatic gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) that is erosive or ulcerative</li><li>GERD long-term maintenance</li><li>Moderate to severe symptomatic GERD</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Helicobacter pylori eradication in patients with peptic ulcer disease in combination with suitable antibacterial therapy</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class:</h3><ul><li><strong>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</strong></li></ul><h3>Pharmacology:</h3><p>Rabeprazole inhibits the gastric H+/K+-ATPase at the secretory surface of the stomach parietal cell, reducing gastric acid secretion. It acts as an inhibitor of the gastric proton pump.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of Rabigut 20 mg:</h3><ul><li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer:</strong> 20 mg in the morning for 4 weeks; most patients heal within 4 weeks.</li><li><strong>Erosive/Ulcerative GERD:</strong> 20 mg daily in the morning for 4-8 weeks. If no healing after 8 weeks, consider an additional 8-week course.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 20 mg daily in the morning for 4 weeks; if symptoms persist, consider additional treatment.</li><li><strong>GERD Maintenance:</strong> 20 mg once daily (controlled studies do not extend beyond 12 months).</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> 60 mg once daily (adjust based on patient needs); doses up to 100 mg once daily or 60 mg twice daily may be required.</li><li><strong>Helicobacter pylori Eradication:</strong> 20 mg, amoxicillin 1000 mg, and clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 7 days.</li></ul><h3>Dosage for Specific Conditions:</h3><ul><li><strong>Active Duodenal Ulcer &amp; Gastric Ulcer:</strong> 20 mg once daily, typically for 4-6 weeks.</li><li><strong>Erosive/Ulcerative GERD:</strong> 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks.</li><li><strong>Long-term GERD Management:</strong> 10 mg or 20 mg daily.</li><li><strong>Symptomatic GERD:</strong> 10 mg daily, may increase to 20 mg.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pediatric GERD Treatment (1-11 years):</strong>&lt;15 kg: 5 mg once daily.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">≥15 kg: 10 mg once daily.</li><li><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome:</strong> Starting dose 60 mg, titrate up to 120 mg/day.</li><li><strong>H. pylori Eradication:</strong> 20 mg twice daily with amoxicillin (1g) and clarithromycin (500 mg) for 7 days.</li></ul><h3>Administration:</h3><ul><li><strong>Dosage Timing:</strong> Preferably take in the morning before meals. Swallow whole, do not chew or crush.</li><li><strong>Food &amp; Time of Day:</strong> No significant effect on the drug\'s action, but morning dosing enhances treatment adherence.</li></ul><h3>Interaction of Rabigut 20 mg:</h3><ul><li><strong>Antifungal Drugs (Itraconazole, Ketoconazole):</strong> Reduced absorption; dosage adjustment may be needed.</li><li><strong>Atazanavir:</strong> Should not be taken with rabeprazole due to pH-dependent absorption.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications:</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to rabeprazole or any of its excipients.</li><li>Use during pregnancy and breastfeeding is not advised.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects of Rabigut 20 mg:</h3><ul><li>Common: Headache, nausea, vomiting, constipation, dry mouth, muscle pain, sleepiness, dizziness.</li><li>Rare: May cause serious side effects in long-term use.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation:</h3><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category C</strong> (US FDA): No controlled human studies, but animal studies show no harm to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Rabeprazole is excreted in breast milk; a decision should be made whether to discontinue breastfeeding or the drug based on the clinical need.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings:</h3><ul><li>Rule out malignancy before starting treatment for stomach or esophageal conditions.</li><li>Long-term use increases fracture risk, particularly in the elderly or those with osteoporosis risk factors.</li><li>Regular monitoring of long-term users (especially beyond 1 year) is recommended.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects:</h3><ul><li>No known antidote.</li><li>Overdose symptoms are typically mild and reversible.</li><li>Treatment is symptomatic and supportive.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions:</h3><ul><li>Keep away from light and moisture, below 30°C.</li><li>Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Special Populations:</h3><ul><li><strong>Hepatic and Renal Impairment:</strong> No dosage adjustments required.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> Insufficient data on safety and effectiveness; not advised for pediatric use.</li></ul><h3>Drug Class:</h3><ul><li>Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI)</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action:</h3><p>Rabeprazole inhibits the gastric H+/K+-ATPase, reducing gastric acid secretion by acting on the proton pump in the parietal cell.</p><p>This detailed guide provides comprehensive information about the indications, usage, side effects, and precautions associated with Rabigut 20 mg. Always consult a healthcare professional before starting or adjusting any treatment.</p>', 'active', '560_1742767028.webp', 17, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:57:08', '2025-05-04 15:02:50'),
(4429, 'Vital-D', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 35.00, 7, 'capsule', '7 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '40000 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Vital-D 40000 IU</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated in the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also used as an adjunct to specific therapy for osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcaemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Colecalciferol, the synthetic form of Vitamin D, is essential for normal bone growth and development and helps to maintain bone density. It also plays a vital role in the utilization of calcium and phosphorus. Babies require Vitamin D3 for healthy growth and development. It functions as a hormone.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin-D preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is essential for normal bone growth and maintaining bone density. Additionally, Vitamin D3 reduces the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, prevents the risk of cancers (such as breast and colorectal cancers), and helps maintain adequate insulin levels in patients with type 2 diabetes.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Vital-D 40000 IU</strong></p><p>For <strong>capsules</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maintenance therapy</strong>: 1,400–2,000 IU/day.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Addition to therapy for osteoporosis</strong>: 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">To confirm the target level of 25-hydroxyvitamin D, measure it 3-4 months after starting maintenance therapy.</li><li><strong>Children (12-18 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU once every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li></ul><p>For <strong>film-coated tablets</strong>:</p><ul><li>1,000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily or as directed by a physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li></ul><p>For <strong>oroflash or chewable tablets</strong>:</p><ul><li>1,000 IU to 2,000 IU daily or as directed by a physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal. Place the tablet in your mouth, chew, then swallow.</li></ul><p>For <strong>Syrup</strong>:</p><ul><li>For <strong>risk of deficiency</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0-1 yr: 400 IU/day (2 ml).</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 yr: 600 IU/day (3 ml).</blockquote><ul><li>For <strong>deficient patients</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0-1 yr: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 yrs: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li></ul><p>For <strong>Injection</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Prevention</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Infants receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Nursed infants or those not receiving Vitamin D-enriched milk, and children up to 5 years: 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Adolescents: 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 6 months, especially during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly</strong>: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>For conditions like digestive disorders or treatments with antiepileptics</strong>: 1/2 ampoule (0.5 ml) = 100,000 IU or 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU every 3 or 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Vitamin D deficiency</strong>: 1 ampoule (1 ml) = 200,000 IU, repeated after 1-6 months or as directed by a physician.</li></ul><p><strong>Interactions</strong></p><ul><li>May interact with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (e.g., liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Vital-D 40000 IU</strong></p><ul><li>Common side effects include hypercalcaemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, and abdominal pain.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Studies have shown the safe use of doses up to 4,000 IU during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU; however, higher doses may be needed for those who are Vitamin D3 deficient. Pregnant women should follow their healthcare provider\'s advice based on their specific needs.</li><li>Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. Overdose in infants due to breastfeeding has not been observed; however, when prescribing Vitamin D3 to a breastfed child, consider the mother\'s dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Should be used with caution in patients with impaired renal function.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Vital-D 40000 IU</strong></p><ul><li>Can lead to hypervitaminosis D.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store below 30ºC, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations.</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is essential for normal bone growth and maintaining bone density. It also reduces the severity of bacterial infections, improves lung function, prevents the risk of cancer, and helps to maintain adequate insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</li></ul>', 'active', '150_1742767142.jpg', 8, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 02:59:02', '2025-05-04 23:01:10'),
(4430, 'Bonemass D', 'Alendronic Acid + Vitamin D3', 30.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '70 mg+2800 IU', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Bonemass D 70 mg+2800 IU</strong></p><p>Alendronic Acid &amp; Vitamin D3 is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Treatment of osteoporosis in postmenopausal women.</li><li>Treatment of osteoporosis in men.</li><li>Treatment of osteoporosis as it increases the bone mass and reduces the incidence of fractures, including those of the hip and spine.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Combined preparations: Inhibiting bone resorption</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Alendronate Sodium is a bisphosphonate that acts as a specific inhibitor of osteoclast-mediated bone resorption. Alendronate is effective when administered at least 30 minutes before breakfast. It transiently distributes to soft tissues following administration but is then rapidly distributed to bone or excreted in the urine. Protein binding in human plasma is approximately 78%. There is no evidence that Alendronate is metabolized in animals or humans. At the cellular level, Alendronate shows preferential localization to sites of bone resorption, especially under osteoclasts. It inhibits osteoclast activity. In addition, bone formation exceeds bone resorption, leading to progressive gains in bone mass. Vitamin D is required for normal bone formation. Vitamin D insufficiency develops when both sunlight exposure and dietary intake are inadequate. Insufficiency is associated with negative calcium balance, increased parathyroid hormone levels, bone loss, and increased risk of skeletal fracture.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Bonemass D 70 mg+2800 IU</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Treatment of Osteoporosis in Postmenopausal Women</strong>: The recommended dosage is 70 mg alendronate and 2800 IU vitamin D3 or 70 mg alendronate and 5600 IU vitamin D3 tablet once weekly. For most osteoporotic women, the appropriate dose is 70 mg alendronate and 5600 IU vitamin D3 once weekly.</li><li><strong>Treatment to Increase Bone Mass in Men with Osteoporosis</strong>: The recommended dosage is 70 mg alendronate and 2800 IU vitamin D3 or 70 mg alendronate and 5600 IU D3 tablet once weekly. For most osteoporotic men, the appropriate dose is 70 mg alendronate and 5600 IU vitamin D3 once weekly.</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Bonemass D 70 mg+2800 IU</strong></p><p>To permit adequate absorption, Alendronate &amp; Colecalciferol must be taken at least 30 minutes before the first food, beverage, or medication of the day with plain water only. Other beverages (including mineral water), food, and some medications are likely to reduce the absorption of Alendronate. To facilitate delivery to the stomach and thus reduce the potential for esophageal irritation, Alendronate &amp; Colecalciferol tablet should only be swallowed upon rising for the day with a full glass of water. Patients should not lie down for at least 30 minutes after taking Alendronate until after their first food of the day. Alendronate &amp; Colecalciferol should not be taken at bedtime.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Bonemass D 70 mg+2800 IU</strong></p><ul><li>Calcium supplements, antacids, and some oral medications will interfere with the absorption of Alendronate if taken at the same time.</li><li>Intravenous ranitidine makes the bioavailability of oral Alendronate double.</li><li>Incidence of upper gastrointestinal adverse events associated with NSAIDs and aspirin appears to be greater with concomitant administration of Alendronate.</li><li>Mineral oils, orlistat, and bile acid sequestrants (e.g., cholestyramine, colestipol) may impair the absorption of vitamin D.</li><li>Anticonvulsants, cimetidine, and thiazides may increase the catabolism of vitamin D.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Abnormalities of the esophagus which delay esophageal emptying, such as stricture or achalasia.</li><li>Inability to stand or sit upright for at least 30 minutes.</li><li>Hypersensitivity to any component of this product.</li><li>Hypocalcemia.</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Bonemass D 70 mg+2800 IU</strong></p><p>Usually mild and generally do not require discontinuation of therapy. Side effects include esophageal reactions, abdominal pain and distension, diarrhea or constipation, flatulence, musculoskeletal pain, headache, rash, erythema, and transient decreases in serum calcium and phosphate.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Pregnancy category C. Overdoses of vitamin D have shown teratogenic effects in pregnant animals. Alendronic Acid &amp; Vitamin D3 (Colecalciferol) should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the mother and fetus.</li><li>Cholecalciferol and some of its active metabolites pass into breast milk. It is not known whether alendronate is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to lactating women.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Hypocalcemia and other disturbances of mineral metabolism should be corrected before initiation of therapy.</li><li>Alendronate can cause local irritation of the upper gastrointestinal mucosa. Caution should be used when Alendronate is given to patients with active upper gastrointestinal problems such as dysphagia, esophageal disease, gastritis, duodenitis, or ulcers.</li><li>Patients should stop taking medicine and consult their physician if they develop esophageal diseases.</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Bonemass D 70 mg+2800 IU</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Alendronate</strong>: Hypocalcemia, hypophosphatemia, and upper gastrointestinal adverse events, such as upset stomach, heartburn, esophagitis, gastritis, or ulcer.</li><li><strong>Vitamin D</strong>: Signs and symptoms of vitamin D toxicity include hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, anorexia, nausea, vomiting, polyuria, polydipsia, weakness, and lethargy.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool and dry place. Protect from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Use In Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dosing in elderly and renal insufficiency</strong>: No dosage adjustment is necessary for the elderly or for patients with mild-to-moderate renal insufficiency (creatinine clearance 35 to 60 mL/min). Bonemass D 70 mg+2800 IU (Colecalciferol) is not recommended for patients with more severe renal insufficiency (creatinine clearance&lt;35 mL/min) due to lack of experience.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Combined preparations: Inhibiting bone resorption</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Alendronate Sodium is a bisphosphonate that acts as a specific inhibitor of osteoclast-mediated bone resorption. Alendronate is effective when administered at least 30 minutes before breakfast. It transiently distributes to soft tissues following administration but is then rapidly distributed to bone or excreted in the urine. Protein binding in human plasma is approximately 78%. There is no evidence that Alendronate is metabolized in animals or humans. At the cellular level, Alendronate shows preferential localization to sites of bone resorption, especially under osteoclasts. It inhibits osteoclast activity. In addition, bone formation exceeds bone resorption, leading to progressive gains in bone mass. Vitamin D is required for normal bone formation. Vitamin D insufficiency develops when both sunlight exposure and dietary intake are inadequate. Insufficiency is associated with negative calcium balance, increased parathyroid hormone levels, bone loss, and increased risk of skeletal fracture.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Pregnancy category C. Overdoses of vitamin D have shown teratogenic effects in pregnant animals. Alendronic Acid &amp; Vitamin D3 (Colecalciferol) should be used during pregnancy only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the mother and fetus. Cholecalciferol and some of its active metabolites pass into breast milk. It is not known whether alendronate is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to lactating women.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Dosing in elderly and renal insufficiency: No dosage adjustment is necessary for the elderly or for patients with mild-to-moderate renal insufficiency (creatinine clearance 35 to 60 mL/min). Bonemass D 70 mg+2800 IU (Colecalciferol) is not recommended for patients with more severe renal insufficiency (creatinine clearance&lt;35 mL/min) due to lack of experience.</li></ul>', 'active', '1676_1742767223.webp', 2, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 03:00:23', '2025-05-03 00:59:59'),
(4431, 'Calcin', 'Elemental Calcium', 5.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of Calcin 500 mg</h3><p><strong>250 mg or 500 mg Tablet</strong>:</p><p>Calcin 500 mg is used for the treatment or prevention of calcium depletion in patients where dietary measures are inadequate. Conditions that may be associated with calcium deficiency include:</p><ul><li>Hypoparathyroidism</li><li>Achlorhydria</li><li>Chronic diarrhea</li><li>Vitamin D deficiency</li><li>Steatorrhea</li><li>Sprue</li><li>Pregnancy and lactation</li><li>Menopause</li><li>Pancreatitis</li><li>Renal failure</li><li>Alkalosis</li><li>Hyperphosphataemia</li></ul><p>Calcin 500 mg is also commonly used in the management of hyperphosphataemia in chronic renal failure, including patients on continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis (CAPD) and hemodialysis. In some cases, patients may require additional measures such as dietary phosphate restriction or small doses of aluminum hydroxide to control phosphate levels. Calcin 500 mg may provide short-term relief from dyspeptic symptoms, but it is no longer recommended for long-term treatment of peptic ulcers.</p><p><strong>1000 mg Tablet</strong>:</p><p>This dosage is indicated for the management of conditions associated with hyperidity, such as:</p><ul><li>Acid indigestion</li><li>Heartburn</li><li>Sour stomach</li><li>Upset stomach</li></ul><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Minerals in bone formation</li><li>Specific mineral preparations</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Calcium carbonate reacts with gastric acid to produce a salt and water. The postulated chemical reaction is:</p><p>CaCO3+2HCl→CaCl2+H2O+CO2\\text{CaCO}_3 + 2\\text{HCl} \\rightarrow \\text{CaCl}_2 + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} + \\text{CO}_2</p><p>Two grams of Calcin 500 mg can bring 100 mL of hydrochloric acid to a pH above 6. This increases gastric pH and diminishes the activity of pepsin. Up to 30% of the oral calcium load may be absorbed.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration</h3><p><strong>250 mg or 500 mg Tablet</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>As an antacid</strong>: The recommended dose for adults is 540-2000 mg Calcin 500 mg per day. For children, the dose should be half of that for adults.</li><li><strong>As a dietary supplement</strong> (e.g., for osteoporosis prevention): 1250-3750 mg Calcin 500 mg (500-1500 mg calcium) daily. This dose can be tailored based on individual patient needs.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy and Lactation</strong>: The recommended daily dose is 1200-1500 mg of calcium.</li><li><strong>Chronic Renal Failure</strong>: The recommended dose varies from 2.5-9.0 gm Calcin 500 mg per day, adjusted based on the individual patient.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">To maximize phosphate binding in this context, Calcin 500 mg should be taken with meals.</li></ul><p><strong>1000 mg Tablet</strong>:</p><p>2000-3000 mg may be used when symptoms occur, and it may be repeated hourly if necessary or as directed by the physician.</p><h3>Interaction</h3><ul><li><strong>Cardiac Effects</strong>: Calcin 500 mg may enhance the effects of digoxin and other cardiac glycosides, especially if systemic hypercalcaemia occurs.</li><li><strong>Tetracycline Interference</strong>: Calcin 500 mg may interfere with the absorption of tetracycline preparations.</li><li><strong>Chronic Renal Failure</strong>: Modification of vitamin D therapy may be required to avoid hypercalcaemia when Calcin 500 mg is used as the primary phosphate binder.</li></ul><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Hypercalcaemia and hyperparathyroidism</li><li>Hypercalciuria and nephrolithiasis</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</li><li>Concomitant digoxin therapy (requires careful monitoring of serum calcium levels)</li></ul><p><strong>Caution</strong> is needed in patients with sarcoidosis, renal or cardiac disease, and those receiving cardiac glycosides.</p><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal Irritation</strong>: Calcin 500 mg may irritate the GI tract and cause constipation.</li><li><strong>Hypercalcaemia</strong>: Rarely, calcium alone may cause hypercalcaemia, especially when large doses are given to patients with chronic renal failure.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><p>Calcium-containing drugs have been widely used in pregnancy for oral calcium supplementation or antacid therapy. Calcin 500 mg can also be used in lactating women.</p><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><p>Store in a cool, dry place at controlled room temperature.</p><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li><strong>Children</strong>: Calcium carbonate has been studied extensively in children and infants with chronic renal failure and is both safe and effective.</li><li><strong>Elderly</strong>: In elderly patients with renal failure, constipation may be problematic. Monitoring of serum calcium and phosphate is advised for this group.</li></ul>', 'active', '1322_1742767311.jpg', 6, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 03:01:51', '2025-05-03 01:08:35'),
(4432, 'D-Balance', 'Cholecalciferol [Vitamin D3]', 40.00, 10, 'capsule', '10 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '50000 IU', 'no', 'no', '<h3>Indications of D-Balance 50000 IU</h3><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) is indicated in the treatment and prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency. It is also used as an adjunct in the treatment of osteoporosis, osteomalacia, hypocalcemia, tetany, and rickets in patients with Vitamin D3 deficiency. Vitamin D3 is essential for normal bone growth and development, maintenance of bone density, and the proper utilization of calcium and phosphorus. It is particularly important for babies to ensure healthy growth and development. It acts as a hormone in the body.</p><h3>Therapeutic Class</h3><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin D preparations</li></ul><h3>Pharmacology</h3><p>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids in the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is crucial for normal bone growth and bone density maintenance. It has also been shown to reduce the severity of bacterial infections, improve lung function, lower the risk of certain cancers (such as breast and colorectal cancer), and help maintain insulin levels in patients with type 2 diabetes.</p><h3>Dosage &amp; Administration of D-Balance 50000 IU</h3><p><strong>For Capsule:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 40,000 IU once weekly for 7 weeks. Maintenance therapy is 1,400-2,000 IU/day. Confirm the target level of 25 hydroxyvitamin D after 3-4 months of maintenance therapy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 4 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adjunct to specific therapy for osteoporosis</strong>: 20,000 IU once a month.</li><li><strong>Children (12-18 years)</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Treatment of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU once every 2 weeks for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prevention of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>: 20,000 IU every 6 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>For Film-Coated Tablet:</strong></p><ul><li>1,000 IU (1-2 tablets) daily, or as directed by a physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li></ul><p><strong>For Oroflash or Chewable Tablets:</strong></p><ul><li>1,000 IU to 2,000 IU daily, or as directed by a physician. Take with food or within 1 hour after a meal.</li></ul><p><strong>For Syrup:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>For patients at risk of Vitamin D3 deficiency</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0-1 year: 400 IU/day (2 ml)</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">1 year: 600 IU/day (3 ml)</blockquote><ul><li><strong>For Vitamin D3 deficient patients</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">0-1 year: 2,000 IU/day (+50,000 IU/week) for 6 weeks.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">1-18 years: 2,000 IU/day for 6 weeks.</li></ul><p><strong>For Injection:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Prevention</strong>:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Infants receiving Vitamin D enriched milk</strong>: ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e. 100,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Nursed infants or those not receiving Vitamin D enriched milk, or young children up to 5 years</strong>: 1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e. 200,000 IU every 6 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adolescents</strong>: 1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e. 200,000 IU every 6 months during winter.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e. 100,000 IU from the 6th or 7th month of pregnancy.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Elderly</strong>: ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e. 100,000 IU every 3 months.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>In conditions like digestive disorders or concomitant treatment with antiepileptics</strong>: ½ ampoule (0.5 ml) i.e. 100,000 IU or 1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e. 200,000 IU every 3 or 6 months.</li></ul><p><strong>For Vitamin D Deficiency:</strong></p><ul><li>1 ampoule (1 ml) i.e. 200,000 IU, repeat 1-6 months later or as directed by the physician.</li></ul><h3>Interactions</h3><p>Vitamin D3 may interfere with phenytoin, barbiturates, glucocorticoids, certain laxatives (e.g., liquid paraffin), actinomycin, and imidazole antifungal agents.</p><h3>Contraindications</h3><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to Vitamin D3.</li></ul><h3>Side Effects</h3><ul><li><strong>General</strong>: Hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, skin rash, pruritus, urticaria, nausea, and abdominal pain.</li></ul><h3>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</h3><ul><li>Studies have shown the safe use of up to 4,000 IU/day during pregnancy. The recommended daily intake for pregnant women is 400 IU, though higher doses may be needed for those with Vitamin D3 deficiency. During pregnancy, it is important to follow the advice of a healthcare provider, as requirements can vary.</li><li>Vitamin D3 and its metabolites are excreted in breast milk. However, overdose in infants induced by nursing mothers has not been observed. Any additional Vitamin D3 supplementation for a breastfed child should be carefully monitored by the physician.</li></ul><h3>Precautions &amp; Warnings</h3><ul><li>Caution is required when using in patients with impaired renal function.</li></ul><h3>Overdose Effects</h3><ul><li>Overdose can lead to <strong>hypervitaminosis D</strong>.</li></ul><h3>Storage Conditions</h3><ul><li>Store below 30°C, protected from light and moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><h3>Use in Special Populations</h3><ul><li>The safety and efficacy of Vitamin D3 in children under 12 years have not been established.</li></ul><h3>Drug Class</h3><ul><li>Vitamin in bone formation, Vitamin-D preparations.</li></ul><h3>Mode of Action</h3><ul><li>Colecalciferol (Vitamin D3) aids the absorption and reabsorption of calcium and phosphorus. It is crucial for bone growth and density maintenance and has a role in reducing the severity of infections, improving lung function, preventing certain cancers, and managing insulin levels in type 2 diabetes patients.</li></ul>', 'active', '1951_1742767401.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 03:03:21', '2025-05-03 01:44:07'),
(4433, 'Losectil MUPS', 'Omeprazole', 7.00, 10, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '20 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here’s the information formatted with normal bold text for headings:</p><p><strong>Indications of Losectil MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><p>Omeprazole (Multiple-Unit Pellet System) is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Duodenal and Gastric ulcers</li><li>NSAID-induced gastric and duodenal ulcers</li><li>Reflux Oesophagitis</li><li>GERD (Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease)</li><li>Eradication of H. pylori with appropriate antibiotics</li><li>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each film-coated MUPS tablet contains 20 mg Omeprazole enteric-coated micro pellets.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Proton Pump Inhibitor</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Omeprazole belongs to a class of antisecretory compounds, the substituted benzimidazoles, that suppress gastric acid secretion by specific inhibition of the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system at the secretory surface of the gastric parietal cell. Because this enzyme system is regarded as the acid (proton) pump within the gastric mucosa, omeprazole has been characterized as a gastric acid-pump inhibitor, in that it blocks the final step of acid production. This effect is dose-related and leads to inhibition of both basal and stimulated acid secretion irrespective of the stimulus.</p><p><strong>Clinical advantage of omeprazole MUPS tablet compared to conventional modified-release omeprazole tablets and pellet-filled omeprazole capsules:</strong></p><ul><li>Ensures greater bioavailability</li><li>Ensures uniform emptying of micro pellets from stomach into small intestine</li><li>Facilitates rapid dissolution of enteric coating and drug release resulting in early Tmax and Cmax (peak time and peak plasma concentration)</li><li>Ensures lesser possibility of dose dumping</li><li>Is a combination of fast acting and sustained action</li><li>Ensures uniform drug release</li><li>Once daily dosing</li><li>Ensures lesser chance of localized irritation</li><li>Ensures better and more uniform drug absorption</li><li>Better than capsules in reducing the esophageal residence time</li><li>Minimizes fluctuation in plasma concentration of drug</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Losectil MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">GERD (Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease): 20 mg once daily for 4 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Gastric ulcer: 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks; in severe cases, twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Duodenal ulcer: 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks; in severe cases, twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">NSAID-induced ulceration: 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Reflux esophagitis: 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks; in severe cases, twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">H. pylori eradication (Omeprazole MUPS tablet with Amoxicillin and Clarithromycin or Metronidazole): 20 mg twice daily for 1 week</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome: 60 mg once daily; usual maintenance, 20-120 mg daily</li><li><strong>Children:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Acid regurgitation in GERD (Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease): 20 mg once daily for 2-4 weeks</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Reflux esophagitis: 20 mg once daily for 4-8 weeks</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Losectil MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Swallow the tablet whole with a glass of water. The tablet must not be chewed or crushed.</li><li>If the patient has trouble swallowing the tablets, put the tablet into a glass of water (Do not use other liquids). Stir the preparation until the tablets disintegrate. Then drink the liquid within 30 minutes. Stir the mixture just before drinking.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Losectil MUPS 20 mg</strong></p><p>Omeprazole is metabolized through CYP2C19. When starting or stopping treatment with Omeprazole, potential interactions with medicines that are CYP2C19 metabolized should be considered.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Omeprazole is contraindicated in those patients who have known hypersensitivity to any other components of the formulation.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Not known to be harmful. Omeprazole can be used during pregnancy.</li><li>Omeprazole is excreted in breast milk but is not likely to influence the child when therapeutic doses are used.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Omeprazole tablet should be used carefully if the patient has severe liver dysfunction and severe renal impairment.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Store in a cool (below 30°C) and dry place, protected from light and moisture.</p>', 'active', '485_1742767496.webp', 26, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 03:04:56', '2025-05-03 23:33:04'),
(4434, 'Sultolin', 'Salbutamol', 23.00, 1, 'x 100 ml bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Syrup', '2 mg/5 ml', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sultolin 100 ml</strong></p><p>Sultolin 100 ml is indicated as a bronchodilator for use in:</p><ul><li>Asthma</li><li>Chronic Bronchitis</li><li>Emphysema</li><li>Other conditions associated with airway obstruction</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Sultolin 100 ml is a selective beta2-adrenoceptor agonist. At therapeutic doses, it acts on the beta 2-adrenoceptors of bronchial smooth muscle, with little or no action on the ß1-adrenoceptors of cardiac muscle. Sultolin 100 ml provides short-acting (4-6 hours) bronchodilatation with a fast onset (within 5 minutes) in reversible airways obstruction. It also has an anti-inflammatory effect on mast cells, causing inhibition of release of bronchoconstrictor mediators including histamine, neutrophil chemotactic factor (NCF), and prostaglandin D2.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sultolin 100 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Oral:</strong></p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Children:</strong>2-6 years: 2.5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">6-12 years: 5 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Over 12 years: 5-10 ml syrup, 3-4 times daily (or 2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily)</li><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 2-4 mg tablet, 3-4 times daily. Maximum single dose is 8 mg tablet.</li></ul><p><strong>Injection:</strong></p><p>Sultolin 100 ml Injection may be administered by subcutaneous, intramuscular, or intravenous routes under a physician\'s direction.</p><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adults:Subcutaneous or Intramuscular route:</strong> 500 mcg (8 mcg/kg), repeated every four hours as required.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Slow intravenous injection:</strong> 250 mcg (4 mcg/kg), injected slowly, with repeat doses if necessary.</li><li><strong>Pediatric Population:</strong> Safety and efficacy in children under 12 years have not been established. For children aged 12 years and older, doses as per the adult population.</li></ul><p><strong>Sultolin 100 ml Respirator Solution:</strong></p><p>To be used with a nebulizer device under physician supervision.</p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Elderly:</strong> 0.5-1.0 ml up to four times a day.</li><li><strong>Children under 12 years:</strong> 0.5 ml diluted to 2.0-4.0 ml with saline.</li><li>Some children may require doses of up to 1.0 ml, repeated four times daily.</li></ul><p><strong>Sultolin 100 ml Nebulizer Solution:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults &amp; Elderly:</strong> 2.5 mg to 5 mg up to 4 times a day.</li><li><strong>Children under 12 years:</strong> 2.5 mg up to 4 times a day (dose may be increased to 5 mg if necessary).</li></ul><p><strong>Sultolin 100 ml Injection:</strong></p><p>For intravenous infusion, dilution with Sodium Chloride or Dextrose Injection is required. Recommended infusion rates: 3 to 20 micrograms/min.</p><p><strong>Sultolin 100 ml Inhalation Capsule:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> One or two 200 mcg capsules every 4-6 hours for maintenance.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> One 200 mcg capsule for relief of bronchospasm or before exercise, 3-4 times a day.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Sultolin 100 ml is contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to Sultolin 100 ml or any of its components.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sultolin 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li>Fine tremors (especially in hands), palpitations, muscle cramps</li><li>Tachycardia, headaches, peripheral vasodilatation (after large doses)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> Use only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Not known if excreted in human milk. Consider discontinuing nursing or the drug based on the importance to the mother.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Use with caution in:</p><ul><li>Hyperthyroidism</li><li>Cardiovascular disease</li><li>Hypertension</li><li>Aneurysms</li><li>Diabetes</li><li>Closed angle glaucoma</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sultolin 100 ml</strong></p><p>Symptoms include angina, headache, nausea, vomiting, and tremors. The antidote is a cardio-selective beta-blocking agent, but beta-blockers should be used with caution in patients with bronchospasm.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Keep below 30°C, away from light and moisture.</li><li>Keep out of the reach of children.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Short-acting selective β2-adrenoceptor stimulants</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>Sultolin 100 ml is a synthetic sympathomimetic agent with predominant beta-2 adrenergic activity. It causes bronchodilation by stimulating beta-2 adrenergic receptors in bronchial smooth muscles, leading to muscle relaxation and improved pulmonary function.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>As mentioned above, use during pregnancy should only be considered if the potential benefit outweighs the risk to the fetus.</p>', 'active', '1952_1742767596.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 03:06:36', '2025-05-04 21:26:52'),
(4435, 'Eyevi', 'Vitamin C + Vitamin E + Lutein + Copper + Zinc', 10.00, 6, 'capsule', '6 capsule in a strip', 'Capsule', '60 mg+30 mg+6 mg+2 mg+15 mg', 'no', 'no', '<p>description</p>', 'active', '167_1742767695.webp', 4, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 03:08:15', '2025-05-03 10:24:55'),
(4436, 'Fexona', 'Fexofenadine Hydrochloride', 8.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '120 mg', 'no', 'no', NULL, 'active', '663_1742788785.jpg', 64, 1, 'fazlearabbi97@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 08:59:45', '2025-03-24 08:59:45');
INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(4437, 'Vitaminz', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral [A-Z gold preparation]', 7.00, 30, 'Tablet', '30 tablet bottle', 'Tablet', 'N/A mg', 'no', 'no', NULL, 'active', '1244_1742789532.jpg', 28, 1, 'fazlearabbi97@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 09:12:12', '2025-03-24 09:12:12'),
(4438, 'Revital-32', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral [A-Z gold preparation]', 12.00, 30, 'Tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', 'N/A', 'no', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Revital 32</strong></p><p>Revital 32 is indicated for preventing and treating deficiencies in vitamins and minerals. This comprehensive daily nutritional supplement is also recommended to meet the heightened requirements for vitamins and minerals in various situations, including physical and emotional stress, chronic diseases, infectious illnesses, osteoporosis, injuries or wounds, surgeries, poor digestion, old age, pregnancy and lactation, decreased appetite, excessive dieting, exposure to environmental pollution, and strenuous exercise, among others.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each tablet of Revital 32 contains the following essential nutrients:</p><ul><li>Vitamin A: 5000 IU</li><li>Vitamin C: 60 mg</li><li>Vitamin D: 400 IU</li><li>Vitamin E: 30 IU</li><li>Vitamin K: 25 mcg</li><li>Vitamin B1 (Thiamine): 1.5 mg</li><li>Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin): 1.7 mg</li><li>Vitamin B3 (Niacin): 20 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine): 2 mg</li><li>Folic acid: 400 mcg</li><li>Biotin: 30 mcg</li><li>Pantothenate: 10 mg</li><li>Calcium: 162 mg</li><li>Iron: 18 mg</li><li>Phosphorus: 109 mg</li><li>Iodine: 150 mcg</li><li>Magnesium: 100 mg</li><li>Zinc: 15 mg</li><li>Selenium: 20 mcg</li><li>Copper: 2 mg</li><li>Manganese: 2 mg</li><li>Chromium: 120 mcg</li><li>Molybdenum: 75 mcg</li><li>Chloride: 72 mg</li><li>Potassium: 80 mg</li><li>Boron: 150 mcg</li><li>Nickel: 5 mcg</li><li>Silicon: 2 mg</li><li>Tin: 10 mcg</li><li>Vanadium: 10 mcg</li><li>Lutein: 250 mcg</li><li>Cyanocobalamin: 6 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>Multivitamin + Multimineral A-Z Gold Preparation is a supplement that combines most vitamins and minerals in a single tablet. This preparation typically contains 32 high-potency vitamins and minerals. It maintains a healthy body and an active lifestyle by providing essential nutrients that may be lacking in one\'s diet.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Revital 32</strong></p><p>The general dosage for adults is one tablet daily. The tablet can be taken with or without food. If you are pregnant or lactating, you may need to take a higher dosage of Revital 32.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Revital 32</strong></p><p>Revital 32 is generally considered safe and has no known drug interactions.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>Revital 32 is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Revital 32</strong></p><p>In most cases, this preparation is well tolerated. However, there may be occasional occurrences of diarrhea while undergoing beta-carotene treatment, and it\'s possible for the skin to take on a mild yellowish tint. Vitamin C and vitamin E may lead to diarrhea and other gastrointestinal disturbances.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Revital 32 is generally safe for pregnant and lactating women, but it is important to speak to your doctor before taking any supplement during pregnancy or lactation.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Long-term intake of high levels of vitamin A (excluding that sourced from beta carotene) may increase the risk of osteoporosis in postmenopausal women.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep in a dry place, away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This is a film-coated tablet that combines 32 high-potency vitamins and minerals. This preparation maintains a healthy body and an active lifestyle.</p>', 'active', '978_1742791685.webp', 44, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 09:48:05', '2025-04-22 19:10:01'),
(4439, 'Zovia Silver', 'Multivitamin & Multimineral [A-Z silver preparation]', 10.50, 30, 'tablet', '30 tablet in a pot', 'Tablet', '0 mg/ml', 'no', 'no', '<p>Here is the information you requested, with headings in bold:</p><p><strong>Indications of Zovia Silver</strong></p><p>This is specially formulated for the prevention and treatment of vitamin and mineral deficiencies for adults over 45 years of age. This Silver is also indicated to meet the increased demands of vitamins and minerals for adults over 45 years of age.</p><p><strong>Composition</strong></p><p>Each tablet contains:</p><ul><li>Vitamin A 3500 IU</li><li>Vitamin C 60 mg</li><li>Vitamin D 400 IU</li><li>Vitamin E 45 IU</li><li>Vitamin K 10 mcg</li><li>Thiamin 1.5 mg</li><li>Riboflavin 1.7 mg</li><li>Niacin 20 mg</li><li>Vitamin B6 3 mg</li><li>Folic acid 400 mcg</li><li>Vitamin B12 25 mcg</li><li>Biotin 30 mcg</li><li>Pantothenic acid 10 mg</li><li>Calcium 200 mg</li><li>Phosphorous 48 mg</li><li>Iodine 150 mcg</li><li>Magnesium 100 mg</li><li>Zinc 15 mg</li><li>Selenium 20 mcg</li><li>Copper 2 mg</li><li>Manganese 2 mg</li><li>Chromium 150 mcg</li><li>Molybdenum 75 mcg</li><li>Chloride 72 mg</li><li>Potassium 80 mg</li><li>Boron 150 mcg</li><li>Nickel 5 mcg</li><li>Silicon 2 mg</li><li>Vanadium 10 mcg</li><li>Lutein 250 mcg</li><li>Lycopene 300 mcg</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p>This preparation is a comprehensive well-balanced multivitamin and multimineral preparation scientifically adjusted and designed to serve as the complete nutritional program for elderly people. This preparation maintains a healthy body and active lifestyle and keeps proper nutrition covered for elderly people.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Zovia Silver</strong></p><p>One tablet once daily with food or as indicated by the physician.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Zovia Silver</strong></p><p>No drug interactions have been reported.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p>This product is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients. Do not take this product if taking other vitamin A supplements.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Zovia Silver</strong></p><p>Generally, this preparation is well tolerated. Allergic sensitization has been reported following oral administration of folic acid. Vitamin C and vitamin E may cause diarrhea and other gastrointestinal disturbances.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p>Recommended by consultation with a physician.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p>Long-term intake of high levels of vitamin A (excluding that sourced from beta carotene) may increase the risk of osteoporosis in postmenopausal women.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p>Keep below 30°C temperature, away from light &amp; moisture. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p>Multi-vitamin &amp; Multi-mineral combined preparations</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p>This preparation is a comprehensive well-balanced multivitamin and multimineral preparation scientifically adjusted and designed to serve as the complete nutritional program for elderly people. This preparation maintains a healthy body and active lifestyle and keeps proper nutrition covered for elderly people.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Recommended by consultation with a physician.</p>', 'active', '1011_1742791913.jpg', 7, 1, 'rajubinahmed@gmail.com', '2025-03-24 09:51:53', '2025-05-05 22:40:06'),
