INSERT INTO `products` (`id`, `productName`, `genericName`, `retail_max_price`, `cart_qty_inc`, `cart_text`, `unit_in_pack`, `type`, `quantity`, `prescription`, `feature`, `description`, `status`, `coverImage`, `company_id`, `category_id`, `add_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(5760, 'Sefro', 'Cephradine', 6.52, 6, 'Capsule', '6 Tablet in a strip', 'Capsule', '250 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sefro 250 mg</strong></p><p> Sefro 250 mg is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by sensitive Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria. These include:</p><ul><li><strong>Upper respiratory tract infections:</strong> sinusitis, pharyngitis, tonsillitis, laryngo-tracheo bronchitis, and otitis media</li><li><strong>Lower respiratory tract infections:</strong> bronchitis (acute and chronic), lobar pneumonia, and bronchopneumonia</li><li><strong>Urinary tract infections:</strong> cystitis, urethritis, and pyelonephritis</li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections:</strong> abscess, cellulitis, furunculosis, and impetigo</li></ul><p><strong>Susceptible Microorganisms (in vitro):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gram-positive:</strong> Staphylococci (both penicillin sensitive and resistant, including penicillinase-producing species), Streptococci, Streptococcus pyogenes (beta haemolytic), Streptococcus pneumoniae</li><li><strong>Gram-negative:</strong> <em>Escherichia coli, Klebsiella spp, Proteus mirabilis, Haemophilus influenzae, Shigella spp, Salmonella spp</em> (including <em>Salmonella typhi</em>), <em>Neisseria spp</em></li></ul><p>Many strains of <em>E. coli</em> and <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> that are ampicillin-resistant due to penicillinase production are susceptible to Sefro 250 mg.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> First generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sefro 250 mg is a cephalosporin antibiotic with broad-spectrum bactericidal activity against both gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria. It interferes with bacterial cell wall synthesis by inhibiting the transpeptidase enzyme, resulting in a weakened cell wall, swelling, and rupture of the bacterial cell.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sefro 250 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Adults:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Respiratory tract infections (excluding lobar pneumonia)</strong> and <strong>skin infections:</strong> 250 mg every 6 hours or 500 mg every 12 hours</li><li><strong>Lobar pneumonia:</strong> 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 g every 12 hours</li><li><strong>Uncomplicated urinary tract infections:</strong> 500 mg every 12 hours</li><li><strong>Complicated urinary tract infections (e.g., prostatitis):</strong> 500 mg every 6 hours or 1 g every 12 hours</li></ul><p><strong>Children (over 9 months):</strong></p><ul><li>25 to 50 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 6 or 12 hours</li><li><strong>Otitis media (H. influenzae):</strong> 75 to 100 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 6 or 12 hours (Max: 4 g/day)</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Sefro 250 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Oral administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Urinary tract infections:</strong> 500 mg four times daily or 1 g twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Respiratory infections:</strong> 250–500 mg four times daily or 500 mg–1 g twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Skin infections:</strong> 250–500 mg four times daily or 500 mg–1 g twice daily</li><li><strong>Children:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Total daily dose: 25–50 mg/kg/day in 2 or 4 divided doses</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Otitis media:</strong> 75–100 mg/kg/day in divided doses every 6–12 hours (Max: 4 g/day)</li><li><strong>Elderly:</strong> Normal adult dose is suitable</li><li><strong>Renal/hepatic impairment:</strong> Monitor during treatment</li></ul><p><strong>Injectable administration</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 2–4 g/day in 4 divided doses (Max: 8 g/day)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Prophylaxis:</strong> 1–2 g IM/IV as single preoperative dose</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> 50–100 mg/kg/day in 4 divided doses (Max: 300 mg/kg/day)</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sefro 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Increased risk of kidney damage when used with nephrotoxic drugs (e.g., aminoglycosides)</li><li>Enhanced renal toxicity risk with diuretics (e.g., frusemide, ethacrynic acid) and probenecid</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Sefro 250 mg is contraindicated in patients with known or suspected hypersensitivity to cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Sefro 250 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain</li><li><strong>Hypersensitivity:</strong> Skin rashes, urticaria, joint pains, oedema</li><li><strong>Rare/Unknown frequency:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Blood:</strong> Thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, agranulocytosis, aplastic/haemolytic anaemia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Immune:</strong> Fever, serum sickness-like reactions, anaphylaxis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Psychiatric:</strong> Confusion, sleep disturbances</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Nervous system:</strong> Hyperactivity, dizziness, headache</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Liver:</strong> Transient hepatitis, cholestatic jaundice</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Renal:</strong> Reversible interstitial nephritis</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Lab values:</strong> Elevated BUN, creatinine, liver enzymes</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy (FDA Category B):</strong> No adequate studies in humans. Use only if clearly needed.</li><li><strong>Lactation:</strong> Excreted in breast milk. Use with caution in nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Risk of superinfection with prolonged use</li><li>Cross-sensitivity in penicillin-allergic patients</li><li>May cause false-positive Coombs’ test and urine glucose test</li><li>Contains lactose—avoid in galactose intolerance, Lapp lactase deficiency, or glucose-galactose malabsorption</li><li>Adjust dose in renal impairment</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sefro 250 mg</strong></p><p> Symptoms include nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and gastric upset. Treatment is supportive. Gastric lavage may be needed for large overdoses.</p><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Suspension:</strong> Use within 7 days (room temperature) or 14 days (refrigerated)</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong> Use within 2 hours at room temperature or 12 hours at 5°C</li><li><strong>General:</strong> Do not use after expiry. Keep out of children’s reach. Dispense only with a registered physician’s prescription.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> First generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Sefro 250 mg inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by interfering with peptidoglycan cross-linking, resulting in bacterial lysis. Effective against both penicillinase-producing and non-producing staphylococci.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Animal studies show no teratogenicity, but human safety not established. Excreted in breast milk. May cause dizziness—caution with driving or operating machinery.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> <strong>Renal impairment (based on 500 mg every 6 hours):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CrCl &gt;20 ml/min:</strong> 500 mg every 6 hours</li><li><strong>CrCl 5–20 ml/min:</strong> 250 mg every 6 hours</li><li><strong>CrCl &lt;5 ml/min:</strong> 250 mg every 50–70 hours</li></ul><p><strong>For haemodialysis:</strong></p><ul><li>250 mg at start</li><li>250 mg 6–12 hours after</li><li>250 mg 36–48 hours after</li><li>250 mg at next session if &gt;30 hours since last dose</li></ul><p><strong>Additional Information:</strong></p><ul><li>Higher doses (up to 1 g four times daily) may be needed for chronic/severe infections</li><li>Continue for 2–3 days after symptoms resolve or bacteria are eradicated</li><li>For haemolytic streptococci: Treat for at least 10 days</li><li>Monitor during and after therapy for chronic UTI</li><li>Pediatric doses should not exceed adult doses</li><li>In persistent infections, therapy may last several weeks</li><li>Transition from injection to oral therapy may be done at the same dosage</li></ul>', 'active', '1568_1744166558.jpg', 62, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-04-09 06:42:39', '2025-04-09 06:42:39'),
(5761, 'Torped', 'Cefotaxime', 75.50, 1, 'IV injection', '500 mg vial injection', 'IV Injection', '500 mg / 10 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Torped 500 mg/10 ml</strong></p><p> Torped 500 mg/10 ml is indicated for the treatment of the following infections either before the infecting organism has been identified or when caused by bacteria of established sensitivity:</p><ul><li>Septicaemia</li><li>Respiratory Tract Infections: acute or chronic bronchitis, bacterial pneumonia, infected bronchiectasis, lung abscess, postoperative chest infections</li><li>Urinary Tract Infections: acute and chronic pyelonephritis, cystitis, asymptomatic bacteriuria</li><li>Soft-tissue Infections: cellulitis, peritonitis, wound infections</li><li>Bone and Joint Infections: osteomyelitis, septic arthritis</li><li>Obstetric and Gynaecological Infections: pelvic inflammatory disease</li><li>Gonorrhoea: particularly when penicillin has failed or is unsuitable</li><li>Other Bacterial Infections: meningitis and other sensitive infections suitable for parenteral antibiotic therapy</li><li>Prophylaxis: May reduce incidence of post-operative infections in contaminated or potentially contaminated surgeries, or in clean operations where infection would have serious effects</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Torped 500 mg/10 ml binds to penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), inhibiting the final transpeptidation step of peptidoglycan synthesis in the bacterial cell wall. This arrests cell wall assembly and results in bacterial cell death. It is a broad-spectrum, bactericidal, 3rd generation parenteral cephalosporin active against both gram-negative and gram-positive bacteria.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Torped 500 mg/10 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong> 1 gm every 12 hours for mild to moderate infections. In severe infections, up to 12 gm daily in 3 or 4 divided doses. For Pseudomonas spp., &gt;6 gm/day is typically required.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> 100–150 mg/kg/day in 2 to 4 divided doses. Up to 200 mg/kg/day for severe infections.</li><li><strong>Neonates:</strong> 50 mg/kg/day in 2 to 4 divided doses. For severe infections, 150–200 mg/kg/day in divided doses.</li><li><strong>Gonorrhoea:</strong> 500 mg as a single dose.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Torped 500 mg/10 ml</strong></p><p> Same as above (note: this section repeats information from \"Dosage &amp; Administration\").</p><p><strong>Interaction of Torped 500 mg/10 ml</strong></p><p> Concomitant use with aminoglycosides may increase nephrotoxicity.</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Hypersensitivity to Torped 500 mg/10 ml or other cephalosporins.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Torped 500 mg/10 ml</strong></p><p> Adverse effects are usually mild and transient:</p><ul><li>Candidiasis, rashes, fever</li><li>Transient liver enzyme elevations</li><li>Diarrhoea, pseudomembranous colitis (rare)</li><li>Changes in renal function (rare, especially in renal impairment)</li><li>Encephalopathy from high doses</li><li>Hypersensitivity reactions including rashes, drug fever, and rarely anaphylaxis</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><p> Animal studies show no adverse fetal effects. Human data is limited. Avoid in first trimester unless clearly needed. Excreted in breast milk; use with caution in nursing mothers.</p><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in patients with GI diseases, especially colitis</li><li>Adjust dosage in renal impairment to avoid high antibiotic concentration</li><li>Monitor renal function and infection severity to tailor dosage</li><li>No adjustment required in moderate renal impairment</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store below 25°C, protected from light and moisture.</p><p> Use reconstituted solution immediately.</p><p> Stable for up to 24 hours if stored at 2° to 8°C.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong></li><li> In severe renal failure (GFR &lt;5 ml/min), give a 1 gm loading dose, then reduce daily dose by half without changing frequency.</li><li> Adjust further based on infection course and patient condition.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Third generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Torped 500 mg/10 ml inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to PBPs, leading to bacterial lysis and death. It is active against both gram-negative and gram-positive organisms.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> Avoid during first trimester unless benefits outweigh risks. Excreted in breast milk; use with caution.</p><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><p> In renal impairment (GFR &lt;5 ml/min), follow same adjusted dosing as described above.</p>', 'active', '1238_1744166718.jpg', 43, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-04-09 06:45:18', '2025-04-09 06:45:18'),
(5762, 'Torosic', 'Ketorolac Tromethamine', 60.00, 5, 'IV injection', '5 injection', 'IV Injection', '30 mg/ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Torosic 30 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Torosic 30 mg/ml is indicated for the short-term management of moderate to severe acute post-operative pain.</p><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis, Non-Opioid Analgesics</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Torosic 30 mg/ml is a potent analgesic of the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). It acts by inhibiting the cyclooxygenase enzyme system, thereby inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis. It demonstrates minimal anti-inflammatory effect at its analgesic dose.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Torosic 30 mg/ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tablet:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Recommended dose is 10 mg every 4–6 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Use short-term only (up to 7 days).</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum daily dose: 40 mg.</li><li><strong>Injection:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">May be given as single or multiple doses (regularly or as needed) for moderate to severe acute pain, usually post-operative.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV bolus:</strong> Administer over no less than 15 seconds.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IM injection:</strong> Administer slowly and deeply into the muscle.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Onset of effect: within 30 minutes.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maximum effect: 1–2 hours.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Duration: 4–6 hours.</li><li><strong>Single-Dose Treatment (Adults):</strong></li><li><strong>IM:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">&lt;65 years: 60 mg once</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">65 years, renally impaired, or &lt;50 kg: 30 mg once</blockquote><ul><li><strong>IV:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">&lt;65 years: 30 mg once</li></ul><blockquote class=\"ql-indent-1\">65 years, renally impaired, or &lt;50 kg: 15 mg once</blockquote><ul><li><strong>Single-Dose Treatment (Children 2–16 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IM:</strong> 1 mg/kg (max 30 mg)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>IV:</strong> 0.5 mg/kg (max 15 mg)</li><li><strong>Multiple-Dose Treatment (IV or IM):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>&lt;65 years:</strong> 30 mg every 6 hours (Max: 120 mg/day)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>&gt;65 years, renally impaired, &lt;50 kg:</strong> 15 mg every 6 hours (Max: 60 mg/day)</li><li><strong>Breakthrough Pain:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Do not increase dose or frequency.</li><li><strong>Conversion from Parenteral to Oral Therapy:</strong></li><li>Total combined daily dose (oral + parenteral) should not exceed:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">120 mg for adults</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">60 mg for elderly</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Oral dose on subsequent days: max 40 mg/day</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Total duration (injection + tablet): not more than 5 days</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Torosic 30 mg/ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Other NSAIDs or aspirin:</strong> Increase side effects</li><li><strong>Anticoagulants:</strong> Enhance bleeding risk</li><li><strong>Beta blockers:</strong> Reduce antihypertensive effect</li><li><strong>ACE inhibitors:</strong> Increase risk of renal impairment</li><li><strong>Methotrexate:</strong> Enhances toxicity</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to ketorolac or other NSAIDs</li><li>Children under 16 years</li><li>Prophylactic use before surgery</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Torosic 30 mg/ml</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Nausea, vomiting, GI bleeding, melena, peptic ulcer, pancreatitis</li><li>Anxiety, drowsiness, headache, excessive thirst, fatigue</li><li>Bradycardia, hypertension, palpitation, chest pain</li><li>Female infertility, pulmonary edema</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>US FDA Pregnancy Category C</strong></li><li>Avoid use during pregnancy and lactation unless benefits outweigh risks</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Caution in patients over 65 years</li><li>Caution in those with peptic ulcers, GI bleeding, asthma, or liver dysfunction</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a dry place, away from heat and light.</p><p> Keep out of reach of children.</p>', 'active', '1638_1744166956.jpg', 76, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-04-09 06:49:16', '2025-04-09 06:49:16'),
(5763, 'Sovalvir', 'Sofosbuvir', 861.71, 7, 'Tablet', '7 Tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Sovalvir 400 mg</strong></p><p> Sovalvir 400 mg is a hepatitis C virus (HCV) nucleotide analog NS5B polymerase inhibitor indicated for the treatment of chronic hepatitis C (CHC) infection as a component of a combination antiviral treatment regimen.</p><p> Efficacy has been established in patients with HCV genotype 1, 2, 3, or 4 infection, including those:</p><ul><li>with hepatocellular carcinoma meeting Milan criteria (awaiting liver transplantation)</li><li>with HCV/HIV-1 co-infection</li></ul><p><strong>Note:</strong></p><ul><li>Monotherapy with Sovalvir 400 mg is <strong>not recommended</strong>.</li><li>Regimen and duration depend on viral genotype and patient factors.</li><li>Treatment response may vary based on baseline host and viral factors.</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Hepatic viral infections (Hepatitis C)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Sovalvir 400 mg is a nucleotide analog inhibitor that specifically inhibits HCV NS5B RNA-dependent RNA polymerase.</p><p> After intracellular metabolism to the active uridine analog triphosphate (GS-461203), it is incorporated into HCV RNA by NS5B polymerase and terminates the RNA chain.</p><p> It binds to two Mg²⁺ ions in the GDD active site of NS5B, preventing further replication of HCV genetic material.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Sovalvir 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adult Dose:</strong> One 400 mg tablet taken <strong>once daily</strong>, with or without food.</li><li>Should be used in combination with <strong>Ribavirin</strong> or <strong>Pegylated Interferon + Ribavirin</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Recommended Combination Therapy</strong></p><p> <em>(For HCV Mono-infected and HCV/HIV-1 Co-infected patients)</em></p><ul><li><strong>Genotype 1 or 4:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sovalvir 400 mg + Peginterferon alfa + Ribavirin for 12 weeks</li><li><strong>Genotype 2:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sovalvir 400 mg + Ribavirin for 12 weeks</li><li><strong>Genotype 3:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sovalvir 400 mg + Ribavirin for 24 weeks</li><li><strong>Genotype 1 (interferon-ineligible):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sovalvir 400 mg + Ribavirin for 24 weeks (alternative regimen)</li><li><strong>Patients with hepatocellular carcinoma awaiting liver transplant:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Sovalvir 400 mg + Ribavirin up to 48 weeks or until transplant (whichever occurs first)</li></ul><p><strong>Renal Impairment:</strong></p><ul><li>No dose recommendation available for patients with <strong>severe renal impairment</strong> or <strong>end-stage renal disease</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Sovalvir 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Decreased efficacy</strong> with potent P-gp inducers:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Rifampicin, St. John’s Wort, carbamazepine, phenytoin, modafinil, phenobarbital, oxcarbazepine, rifabutin, rifapentine</li><li><strong>Caution:</strong> Risk of <strong>serious bradycardia</strong> with amiodarone when combined with another direct-acting antiviral (DAA)</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Contraindicated in combination therapy with Ribavirin or Peginterferon alfa/Ribavirin in:</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Pregnant women</strong> or those planning pregnancy</li><li><strong>Men</strong> whose female partners are pregnant</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"> (Due to the risk of fetal death and birth defects with Ribavirin)</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Sovalvir 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With Ribavirin:</strong> Fatigue, headache (≥20% incidence)</li><li><strong>With Peginterferon alfa + Ribavirin:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Fatigue, headache, nausea, insomnia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Anemia, rash, pruritus, decreased appetite</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Flu-like symptoms, chills, pyrexia, diarrhea, neutropenia</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Myalgia, irritability, asthenia</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong></li><li>No adequate studies in pregnant women</li><li>Unknown if drug/metabolites are excreted in human breast milk</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Bradycardia with amiodarone + DAA:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Co-administration is <strong>not recommended</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">If necessary, monitor cardiac function, especially in patients on beta blockers or with cardiac/liver conditions</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Sovalvir 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Highest dose tested: 1200 mg</li><li>No specific antidote</li><li>Treat overdose with supportive care and monitoring of vital signs</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store in a <strong>cool, dry place</strong>, away from light</li><li>Keep <strong>out of reach of children</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric Use:</strong> Safety not established in children &lt;18 years</li><li><strong>Geriatric Use:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">No dose adjustment necessary</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Similar response rates as younger adults in clinical trials</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Hepatic viral infections (Hepatitis C)</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Sovalvir 400 mg, a nucleotide analog inhibitor, blocks NS5B RNA polymerase by incorporating into HCV RNA and terminating replication. It also binds Mg²⁺ ions at the polymerase’s active site to stop further RNA synthesis.</p>', 'active', '1808_1744167118.jpg', 1, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-04-09 06:51:58', '2025-04-09 06:51:58'),
(5764, 'Lacomax', 'Lacosamide', 12.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '50 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Lacomax 50 mg</strong></p><p> Lacomax 50 mg is indicated for the treatment of <strong>partial-onset seizures</strong> in patients <strong>4 years of age and older</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>Injection form</strong> is indicated only for <strong>adults (17 years and older)</strong>, as its safety has not been established in pediatric patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Lacomax 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults (≥17 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Monotherapy:</strong> Start with 100 mg twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Adjunctive therapy:</strong> Start with 50 mg twice daily</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Maximum dosage:</strong> 200 mg twice daily for both uses</li><li><strong>Pediatric Patients (4 to &lt;17 years):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Dosage based on <strong>body weight</strong>, given <strong>twice daily orally</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Increase dosage gradually, based on clinical response and tolerability, <strong>no more frequently than once per week</strong></li><li><strong>Injection (IV, adults only):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Use when oral administration is <strong>temporarily not feasible</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Same dosage as oral form</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Administer over <strong>15 to 60 minutes</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">ECG recommended prior to initiation in certain patients</li></ul><p><strong>Interaction of Lacomax 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>CYP3A4 or CYP2C9 Inhibitors:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">In <strong>renal or hepatic impairment</strong>, strong inhibitors may increase Lacomax exposure → <strong>Dose reduction may be needed</strong></li><li><strong>Medications Prolonging PR Interval:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Use with <strong>caution</strong> in patients taking PR-prolonging drugs (e.g., <strong>beta-blockers</strong>, <strong>calcium channel blockers</strong>)</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>ECG monitoring</strong> before and after reaching steady-state is recommended</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Closely monitor when using IV form in such patients</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li><strong>None</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Lacomax 50 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adjunctive therapy (adults):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Most common (≥10% and more than placebo): <strong>Diplopia, headache, dizziness, nausea</strong></li><li><strong>Monotherapy (adults):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Similar adverse reactions as in adjunctive therapy</li><li><strong>Pediatric patients:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Similar adverse reactions as seen in adults</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Monitor for <strong>suicidal thoughts or behavior</strong></li><li>May cause <strong>dizziness</strong> and <strong>ataxia</strong></li><li><strong>Cardiac rhythm abnormalities:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">ECG before starting and after reaching maintenance dose in patients with cardiac conduction issues, PR-prolonging drugs, or severe heart disease</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Close monitoring recommended</li><li>May cause <strong>syncope</strong></li><li>Should be <strong>gradually withdrawn</strong> to avoid increased seizure risk</li><li><strong>DRESS / Multi-organ hypersensitivity:</strong> Discontinue if no alternative cause is found</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store at <strong>20°C to 25°C</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Atypical anti-depressant drugs</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><ul><li>Lacomax 50 mg works primarily by <strong>inhibiting sodium channels</strong>, potentially targeting <strong>depolarized neurons</strong> more selectively</li><li><strong>Pain and hyperexcitability</strong> are associated with depolarized neurons</li><li>Binds to <strong>CRMP-2</strong> (collapsin response mediator protein-2), involved in <strong>neuronal development</strong> and <strong>axon growth</strong></li><li>The exact role of CRMP-2 binding in seizure control is <strong>not fully understood</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Based on animal studies, Lacomax 50 mg <strong>may cause fetal harm</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dose adjustment required</strong> in severe renal impairment</li><li><strong>Mild/moderate hepatic impairment:</strong> Dose adjustment recommended</li><li><strong>Severe hepatic impairment:</strong> Use <strong>not recommended</strong></li></ul>', 'active', '1269_1744167253.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-04-09 06:54:13', '2025-04-09 06:54:13'),
(5765, 'Ticoflex SR', 'Naproxen Sodium', 7.20, 30, 'Tablet', '30\'s pack', 'Tablet', '500 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Ticoflex SR 500 mg</strong></p><p> Naproxen is indicated for the relief of signs and symptoms of:</p><ul><li><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis</strong></li><li><strong>Osteoarthritis</strong></li><li><strong>Ankylosing spondylitis</strong></li><li><strong>Juvenile arthritis</strong></li><li><strong>Tendonitis</strong></li><li><strong>Bursitis</strong></li><li><strong>Acute gout</strong></li><li><strong>Primary dysmenorrhea</strong></li><li><strong>Pain</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Naproxen is a <strong>non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID)</strong> with <strong>anti-inflammatory</strong>, <strong>analgesic</strong>, and <strong>antipyretic</strong> properties. It is <strong>rapidly absorbed</strong> from the gastrointestinal tract and achieves <strong>95% bioavailability</strong>.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Ticoflex SR 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Naproxen Tablet</strong></li><li><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis, osteoarthritis, ankylosing spondylitis:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"> 500–1000 mg daily in two divided doses, taken after meals</li><li><strong>Pain, dysmenorrhea, acute tendonitis &amp; bursitis:</strong></li><li> Starting dose 500 mg, then 500 mg every 12 hours or 250 mg every 6–8 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-2\">Initial daily dose should not exceed 1250 mg</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Maintenance dose should not exceed 1000 mg/day</li><li><strong>Acute gout:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"> Starting dose 750 mg, then 250 mg every 8 hours until symptoms subside</li><li><strong>Naproxen Suspension (Juvenile rheumatoid arthritis):</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Children over 2 years: 10 mg/kg/day in two divided doses every 12 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Not recommended for children under 2 years</li><li><strong>Naproxen Gel:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Apply <strong>2–6 times daily as needed</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Not recommended for use in children</li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Ticoflex SR 500 mg</strong></p><p> (Same as above; may be omitted if this section is duplicated)</p><p><strong>Interaction of Ticoflex SR 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ACE inhibitors:</strong> Reduced antihypertensive effect</li><li><strong>Antacids &amp; Sucralfate:</strong> Delay absorption</li><li><strong>Aspirin:</strong> Increases risk of adverse effects</li><li><strong>Diuretics (e.g., Furosemide, Thiazides):</strong> Reduced natriuretic effect</li><li><strong>Methotrexate:</strong> Increased toxicity</li><li><strong>Warfarin:</strong> Increased risk of GI bleeding</li><li><strong>SSRIs:</strong> Increased risk of GI bleeding</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity to Naproxen</li><li>History of asthma, urticaria, or allergic-type reactions to aspirin or other NSAIDs</li><li>Perioperative pain following <strong>coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Ticoflex SR 500 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Heartburn, abdominal pain, nausea, diarrhea, dyspepsia</li><li><strong>Central Nervous System:</strong> Headache, vertigo, drowsiness</li><li><strong>Dermatological:</strong> Pruritus, purpura</li><li><strong>Cardiovascular:</strong> Edema, palpitations</li><li><strong>Others:</strong> Visual disturbances, hearing disturbances</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>US FDA pregnancy category C</strong></li><li>Should be <strong>avoided</strong> in pregnancy and lactation <strong>unless benefits outweigh risks</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Store <strong>below 30°C</strong>, protect from <strong>light and moisture</strong></li><li>Keep <strong>out of reach of children</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><ul><li>Drugs for Osteoarthritis</li><li>Drugs used for Rheumatoid Arthritis</li><li>Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</li></ul><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Naproxen is an NSAID that works by inhibiting <strong>prostaglandin synthesis</strong>, leading to its <strong>anti-inflammatory</strong>, <strong>analgesic</strong>, and <strong>antipyretic</strong> effects. It is <strong>rapidly absorbed</strong> with <strong>95% bioavailability</strong>.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li>Classified as <strong>FDA Category C</strong></li><li>Should be avoided unless <strong>clinically justified</strong></li></ul>', 'active', '242_1744167538.jpg', 2, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-04-09 06:58:58', '2025-04-09 06:58:58'),
(5766, 'Gored', 'Gliclazide', 8.00, 1, 'tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '80 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><br></p>', 'active', '7951_1744168103.jpg', 37, 12, 'mahidsiam1@gmail.com', '2025-04-09 07:06:46', '2025-04-09 07:08:23'),
(5767, 'Supralex', 'Cephalexin', 69.56, 1, 'Bottle', '100 ml bottle', 'Powder For Suspension', '100 ml', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Supralex 100 ml</strong></p><p> Supralex 100 ml is indicated for the treatment of infections caused by susceptible organisms, including:</p><ul><li><strong>Respiratory tract infections:</strong> Acute and chronic bronchitis, infected bronchiectasis</li><li><strong>Genito-urinary tract infections:</strong> Acute and chronic nephritis, cystitis, urethritis, prostatitis, and prophylaxis of recurrent urinary tract infections</li><li><strong>Skin and soft tissue infections:</strong> Caused by staphylococci and/or streptococci</li><li><strong>Ear, Nose and Throat infections:</strong> Otitis media, mastoiditis, sinusitis, follicular tonsillitis, and pharyngitis</li><li><strong>Bone infections:</strong> Caused by staphylococci and/or <em>Proteus mirabilis</em></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> First generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Supralex 100 ml (Cefalexin) is a <strong>semisynthetic analogue of Cephalosporin C</strong>, active by mouth. It works by interfering with <strong>bacterial cell wall synthesis</strong>. By inhibiting the <strong>transpeptidase enzyme</strong>, it prevents proper cross-linking of peptidoglycan, weakening the bacterial cell wall and leading to cell rupture and death.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Supralex 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Adults:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Usual dose: 250 mg to 500 mg every 6 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">For skin/soft tissue infections, streptococcal pharyngitis, and uncomplicated cystitis (in patients over 15 years): 500 mg every 12 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Severe/deep-seated infections: Up to 3–6 g daily</li><li><strong>Children:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">General range: 25–100 mg/kg/day in divided doses (max 4 g/day)</li><li><strong>Weight-based recommended doses:</strong></li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>10 kg (22 lbs):</strong> 62.5–125 mg qid or 125–250 mg bid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>20 kg (44 lbs):</strong> 125–250 mg qid or 250–500 mg bid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>40 kg (88 lbs):</strong> 250–500 mg qid or 500 mg–1 g bid</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Streptococcal pharyngitis, skin/soft tissue infections (patients &gt;1 year):</strong> Divide total daily dose and give every 12 hours</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>Otitis media:</strong> 75–100 mg/kg/day in 4 divided doses</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\"><strong>β-haemolytic streptococcal infections:</strong> Continue full therapeutic dose for <strong>at least 10 days</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Dosage of Supralex 100 ml</strong></p><p> (Same as above; can be omitted if already presented under Dosage &amp; Administration)</p><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Contraindicated in patients with known <strong>hypersensitivity to cephalosporins</strong>.</p><p><strong>Side Effects of Supralex 100 ml</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Gastrointestinal:</strong> Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal discomfort, pseudomembranous colitis</li><li><strong>Skin:</strong> Rash, angioedema</li><li><strong>Liver &amp; Blood:</strong> Elevated liver enzymes, eosinophilia, neutropenia (rare)</li><li><strong>Others:</strong> Superinfection with resistant organisms (e.g., <em>Candida</em>)</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong></li><li>May be used in pregnancy <strong>if essential</strong></li><li>Found in breast milk—<strong>caution in nursing mothers</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Use with caution in <strong>renal impairment</strong>; monitor clinically and consider dosage adjustment</li><li>May cause <strong>false-positive urine glucose tests</strong> with copper reduction methods</li><li>May give <strong>positive Coombs\' test</strong></li><li>Although no evidence of teratogenicity, use during pregnancy only if clearly needed</li><li>Caution in breastfeeding as drug is excreted in breast milk</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Store in a <strong>cool, dry place</strong></p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> First generation Cephalosporins</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Cefalexin interferes with <strong>bacterial cell wall synthesis</strong> by inhibiting <strong>transpeptidase</strong>, an enzyme involved in peptidoglycan cross-linking. This leads to <strong>cell wall weakening</strong>, swelling, and rupture. It is <strong>orally active</strong> and derived from <strong>Cephalosporin C</strong>.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category B</strong></li><li>Can be used during pregnancy <strong>if essential</strong></li><li>Use with <strong>caution in breastfeeding</strong></li></ul>', 'active', '1124_1744168045.jpg', 69, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-04-09 07:07:25', '2025-04-09 07:07:25'),
(5768, 'Soventa', 'Sofosbuvir', 600.00, 6, 'Tablet', '6 Tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '400 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Soventa 400 mg</strong></p><p> Soventa 400 mg is a hepatitis C virus (HCV) nucleotide analog NS5B polymerase inhibitor indicated for the treatment of <strong>chronic hepatitis C (CHC)</strong> infection as part of a combination antiviral regimen.</p><p> Efficacy has been established in patients with <strong>HCV genotypes 1, 2, 3, or 4</strong>, including:</p><ul><li>Patients with <strong>hepatocellular carcinoma</strong> meeting Milan criteria (awaiting liver transplantation)</li><li>Patients co-infected with <strong>HCV/HIV-1</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Note:</strong></p><ul><li>Monotherapy with Soventa 400 mg is <strong>not recommended</strong></li><li>Treatment regimen and duration depend on <strong>HCV genotype and patient characteristics</strong></li><li>Treatment response varies with <strong>baseline host and viral factors</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Hepatic viral infections (Hepatitis C)</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Soventa 400 mg is a <strong>nucleotide analog inhibitor</strong> of the <strong>HCV NS5B RNA-dependent RNA polymerase</strong>.</p><p> After intracellular metabolism to <strong>GS-461203 (uridine analog triphosphate)</strong>, it incorporates into viral RNA via the NS5B polymerase and acts as a <strong>chain terminator</strong>.</p><p> It works by binding to <strong>Mg²⁺ ions</strong> in the enzyme’s GDD active site motif, <strong>blocking further replication</strong> of viral genetic material.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration of Soventa 400 mg</strong></p><p> <strong>Standard dose:</strong> One 400 mg tablet <strong>once daily</strong>, with or without food.</p><p> <strong>Must be used in combination</strong> with other antivirals (e.g., Ribavirin, Pegylated Interferon).</p><p><strong>Recommended regimens:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Genotype 1 or 4:</strong> Soventa 400 mg + Peginterferon alfa + Ribavirin for <strong>12 weeks</strong></li><li><strong>Genotype 2:</strong> Soventa 400 mg + Ribavirin for <strong>12 weeks</strong></li><li><strong>Genotype 3:</strong> Soventa 400 mg + Ribavirin for <strong>24 weeks</strong></li><li><strong>Genotype 1 (Interferon-ineligible patients):</strong> Soventa 400 mg + Ribavirin for <strong>24 weeks</strong></li><li><strong>Patients awaiting liver transplantation (HCC):</strong> Soventa 400 mg + Ribavirin for <strong>up to 48 weeks</strong> or until transplantation (whichever comes first)</li></ul><p><strong>Note:</strong> No dosage recommendation available for patients with <strong>severe renal impairment or end-stage renal disease</strong>.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Soventa 400 mg</strong></p><p> (Same as above; can be omitted to avoid redundancy)</p><p><strong>Interaction of Soventa 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Avoid with potent intestinal P-gp inducers</strong> (e.g., rifampicin, St. John\'s wort, carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbital, oxcarbazepine, rifabutin, rifapentine, modafinil) – may reduce efficacy</li><li><strong>Caution with P-gp and/or BCRP inhibitors</strong></li><li><strong>Serious bradycardia</strong> may occur when co-administered with <strong>amiodarone</strong> and another direct-acting antiviral</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><ul><li>Any contraindications related to <strong>Ribavirin</strong> or <strong>Peginterferon alfa/Ribavirin</strong> apply to Soventa when used in combination</li><li><strong>Contraindicated in pregnancy</strong> or in men whose female partners are pregnant (due to Ribavirin-related risks of <strong>birth defects and fetal death</strong>)</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Soventa 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>With Ribavirin:</strong> Fatigue, headache (≥20% incidence)</li><li><strong>With Peginterferon alfa + Ribavirin:</strong> Fatigue, headache, nausea, insomnia, anemia, pruritus, asthenia, rash, decreased appetite, chills, flu-like illness, fever, diarrhea, neutropenia, myalgia, irritability</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy Category B</strong></li><li>No adequate studies in pregnant women</li><li><strong>Nursing mothers:</strong> Unknown if Soventa or metabolites are excreted in breast milk</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Bradycardia risk</strong> with <strong>amiodarone</strong> + another DAA: Avoid this combination</li><li>If unavoidable, <strong>monitor cardiac status closely</strong>, especially in patients with cardiac comorbidities or advanced liver disease</li></ul><p><strong>Overdose Effects of Soventa 400 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Maximum tested dose: 1200 mg</li><li>No specific antidote</li><li>Treat with <strong>supportive care</strong>, monitor vital signs and clinical status</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a <strong>cool, dry place</strong>, protected from light.</p><p> <strong>Keep out of reach of children</strong></p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use:</strong> Safety and effectiveness not established for patients under 18</li><li><strong>Geriatric use:</strong> No dose adjustment needed; response similar to younger adults</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Hepatic viral infections (Hepatitis C)</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Soventa 400 mg acts as a <strong>nucleotide analog</strong> that <strong>inhibits HCV replication</strong> by targeting NS5B RNA-dependent RNA polymerase. After being metabolized to <strong>GS-461203</strong>, it incorporates into viral RNA and <strong>terminates chain elongation</strong>, halting replication.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Category B</strong></li><li>Avoid use during pregnancy due to <strong>combination risks</strong> (especially Ribavirin)</li><li>Unknown if excreted in breast milk</li></ul><p><strong>Pediatric Uses</strong></p><ul><li>Not approved for use in children &lt;18 years</li><li>No dosage adjustments required for <strong>geriatric patients</strong></li></ul>', 'active', '1793_1744168337.jpg', 53, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-04-09 07:12:17', '2025-04-09 07:12:17'),
(5769, 'Neurolin CR', 'Pregabalin', 35.00, 6, 'tablet', '6 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '165 mg', 'yes', 'no', NULL, 'active', '1453_1744169972.jpg', 4, 12, 'mahidsiam1@gmail.com', '2025-04-09 07:39:32', '2025-04-09 07:39:32'),
