(6111, 'Relaxaid', 'Bromazepam', 6.00, 10, 'Tablet', '10 tablet in a strip', 'Tablet', '3 mg', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications of Relaxaid 3 mg</strong></p><p> Relaxaid 3 mg is indicated in:</p><ul><li>Emotional disturbances, i.e. acute tension and anxiety states</li><li>Difficulties in interpersonal contact</li><li>Agitation, insomnia, anxious and agitated depressive reactions</li><li>Functional disturbances in the cardiovascular and respiratory systems, i.e. pseudoangina pectoris, pericardial anxiety, tachycardia, emotiogenic hypertension, dyspnea and hyperventilation</li><li>Disturbances in the gastrointestinal tract, i.e. irritable bowel syndrome, epigastric pain, spasm, bloating, diarrhea, etc.</li><li>Disturbances in the urinary tract, i.e. frequency, irritable bladder and dysmenorrhea</li><li>Psychosomatic disorders, i.e. psychogenic headache, asthma, gastric and duodenal ulcer</li><li>Emotional reactions to chronic organic disease</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Benzodiazepine sedatives</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Relaxaid 3 mg is a powerful psychotropic agent. In lower dosage, it selectively reduces tension and anxiety. In higher dosage, it shows sedative and muscle-relaxant properties. Relaxaid 3 mg binds to the GABA-A receptor producing a conformational change and potentiating its inhibitory effects. Other neurotransmitters are not influenced.</p><p><strong>Dosage of Relaxaid 3 mg</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Standard dosage:</strong> Average dosage for outpatient therapy is 1.5–3 mg up to three times daily.</li><li><strong>Severe cases (especially in hospital):</strong> 6–12 mg, 2 or 3 times daily</li><li>Treatment should begin with low doses and gradually increase to the optimum level.</li><li>The overall treatment duration should generally not exceed 8–12 weeks. If extended, re-evaluation is necessary.</li><li><strong>Elderly and debilitated patients:</strong> Require lower doses</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> Usually not indicated. If prescribed, dose should be adjusted to their low body weight (approx. 0.1–0.3 mg/kg)</li></ul><p><strong>Administration of Relaxaid 3 mg</strong></p><p> Relaxaid 3 mg tablets are for oral administration.</p><p><strong>Interaction of Relaxaid 3 mg</strong></p><ul><li>Sedative effects may be enhanced if combined with centrally active drugs like antidepressants, hypnotics, narcotics, antipsychotics, sedatives, antiepileptic drugs, sedative antihistamines, and anesthetics.</li><li>Cimetidine may prolong Relaxaid\'s elimination half-life.</li><li>Alcohol should be avoided due to increased sedative effects.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Relaxaid 3 mg is contraindicated in patients with:</p><ul><li>Known hypersensitivity</li><li>Severe respiratory insufficiency</li><li>Severe hepatic insufficiency</li><li>Sleep apnea syndrome</li></ul><p><strong>Side Effects of Relaxaid 3 mg</strong></p><p> Common side effects include:</p><ul><li>Fatigue</li><li>Drowsiness</li><li>Muscle weakness</li><li>Numbed muscle</li><li>Reduced alertness</li><li>Confusion</li><li>Headache</li><li>Ataxia</li><li> These effects are usually seen at the start of therapy and often resolve with continued use. Therapeutic doses may also cause anterograde amnesia.</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li>Safety during pregnancy has not been established.</li><li>Use should be avoided during lactation as the drug is excreted in breast milk.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><ul><li>Benzodiazepines may lead to physical and psychological dependence, especially with higher doses and longer durations.</li><li>Withdrawal symptoms may include headache, muscle pain, extreme anxiety, tension, confusion, and irritability.</li><li>Gradual dosage reduction is recommended to avoid withdrawal or rebound effects.</li><li>Not recommended for primary treatment of insomnia caused by psychotic illness.</li><li>Caution advised when driving or operating machinery.</li></ul><p><strong>Storage Conditions</strong></p><p> Keep in a dry place away from light and heat. Keep out of the reach of children.</p><p><strong>Drug Classes</strong></p><p> Benzodiazepine sedatives</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Relaxaid 3 mg binds to the GABA-A receptor, inducing a conformational change and enhancing inhibitory effects. It reduces tension and anxiety at low doses and has sedative and muscle-relaxant effects at higher doses. Other neurotransmitters are not affected.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong></p><p> The safety of Relaxaid 3 mg during pregnancy has not been established. As it is excreted in breast milk, it should be avoided during lactation.</p>', 'active', '1179_1744213865.jpg', 68, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-04-09 19:51:05', '2025-04-09 19:51:05'),
