(6227, 'Optafenac', 'Nepafenac', 150.00, 1, 'Drop', '5 ml drop', 'Ophthalmic Suspension', '0.1%', 'yes', 'no', '<p><strong>Indications</strong></p><p> <strong>Optafenac 0.1% 0.1% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension</strong>:</p><ul><li>Treatment of post-operative ocular pain and inflammation including cataract surgery</li><li>Inhibition of surgery-induced miosis</li><li>Prevention of post-operative cystoid macular edema (CME)</li></ul><p><strong>Optafenac 0.1% 0.3% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension</strong>:</p><ul><li>Post-operative pain and inflammation associated with cataract surgery</li></ul><p><strong>Therapeutic Class</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic Non-Steroid drugs</p><p><strong>Pharmacology</strong></p><p> Optafenac 0.1% is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory prodrug (NSAID). After instillation in the eye, it penetrates the cornea and is converted by ocular tissue hydrolase to <strong>Amfenac</strong>, a potent NSAID. Amfenac inhibits the cyclooxygenase enzyme, which is essential for prostaglandin synthesis.</p><p><strong>Dosage &amp; Administration</strong></p><p> <strong>Optafenac 0.1% 0.1% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Post-operative pain &amp; inflammation</strong>: Instill 1 drop 3 times daily, starting 1 day before cataract surgery, on the day of surgery, and continue through the first 2 weeks post-operatively.</li><li><strong>Surgery-induced miosis</strong>: Instill 1 drop 3 times daily, starting 1 day before and on the day of surgery.</li><li><strong>Prevention of post-operative CME</strong>: Instill 1 drop 3 times daily, starting 1 day before surgery, on the day of surgery, and continue through the first 6 weeks post-operatively.</li></ul><p><strong>Optafenac 0.1% 0.3% Sterile Ophthalmic Suspension</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Post-operative pain &amp; inflammation</strong>: Instill 1 drop once daily, starting 1 day before cataract surgery, on the day of surgery, and continue through the first 2 weeks post-operatively.</li><li>An additional drop should be administered 30 to 120 minutes prior to surgery.</li></ul><p><strong>Contraindications</strong></p><p> Contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity to any of the ingredients or other NSAIDs.</p><p><strong>Side Effects</strong></p><ul><li>Foreign body sensation</li><li>Lid margin crusting</li><li>Ocular discomfort</li><li>Ocular hyperemia</li></ul><p><strong>Pregnancy &amp; Lactation</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: No adequate and well-controlled studies in pregnant women. Use only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.</li><li><strong>Lactation</strong>: Unknown whether excreted in human milk; caution advised when administered to nursing mothers.</li></ul><p><strong>Precautions &amp; Warnings</strong></p><p> Use with caution in patients with bleeding tendencies or those on medications that may prolong bleeding time.</p><p><strong>Use in Special Populations</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pediatric use</strong>: Safety and effectiveness not established in patients under 18 years.</li><li><strong>Elderly patients</strong>: No significant differences in safety or efficacy compared to younger patients.</li></ul><p><strong>Drug Class</strong></p><p> Ophthalmic Non-Steroid drugs</p><p><strong>Mode of Action</strong></p><p> Optafenac 0.1% is converted to Amfenac in the eye, which inhibits cyclooxygenase—thereby reducing prostaglandin synthesis and inflammation.</p>', 'active', '722_1744251537.jpg', 17, 12, 'meraj.wdev@gmail.com', '2025-04-10 06:18:57', '2025-04-10 06:18:57');
